// GENERATED by `node tools/grim.js build` — do not edit. Sources: content/**/*.grim, locales/**/*.lang
window.LUMENHOLD_SOURCES = [{"path":"content/core/appointments.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Appointments (docs/grimoire-script.md §9): when the game deals cards.\n#  An appointment deals at an occasion (`at:`), once for each of its subjects (`for:` track kinds you're in, `met`\n#  for everyone you've met, nothing for the game itself), the cards it `deals:` (card <id>, pool <id>, trigger <t>, or\n#  own: the subject track's cards), while its `when` holds and by its `chance:`. At the month's start a card goes to\n#  its `slot:` (else the card's own, else the day's first); at a visit it comes before the drawn cards, or instead of\n#  them with `takes: visit`; at a notice it comes next month, in the track's context.\n#  The ones below are the game's own, in the order they deal; `use:` names the built-in way each one deals. A new\n#  appointment needs no engine code: a market day, a tutoring hour, a named character's café shift.\n#  Older spellings still work and mean the same: `trigger: auto` (forced), `ramp:` (milestones), an arc's `beat:`\n#  (beats), `replaces: visitN` (festivals), `@place always:` (fixed), `trigger: letter` (letters), `@track intro:` and\n#  `start:` (intro, start), `@game exam:` / `appeal:` / `off_shift:` / `spark_cooldown:` (their appointment's card).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- the month's start (after the cards queued for it) ----------------\n\n@appointment forced\ndesc: Every card with `trigger: auto` whose `when` holds, by its priority.\nat: month\ndeals: trigger auto\nuse: auto\n\n@appointment beats\ndesc: The arcs' cards once their arc's clock reaches their `beat:`; at most one plot card a month (two in a month with a beat).\nat: month\ndeals: beats\nlimit: plot\nuse: beats\n\n@appointment milestones\ndesc: Each card with a `ramp:`, for whoever is furthest along: 40% at its low mark, certain at its high mark.\nat: month\nfor: met\ndeals: ramp\nuse: ramp\n\n# ---------------- the day's slots ----------------\n\n@appointment class\ndesc: The course slot: a card from each course you're enrolled in (up to its level) and your thesis; its professor and whoever is there see you.\nat: courses\nfor: course thesis\ndeals: own\nuse: courses\n\n@appointment festivals\ndesc: A card with `replaces: visitN` and a `place:` takes that visit when you go there.\nat: visit\ndeals: replaces\ntakes: visit\nuse: festival\n\n@appointment off_shift\ndesc: Visiting your job's place in term when you aren't working: the hint card comes first.\nat: visit\nfor: job\ndeals: card job_off_shift\nuse: off_shift\n\n@appointment fixed\ndesc: A place's `always:` cards, first on every visit, in order, while their `when` holds.\nat: visit\ndeals: always\nuse: always\n\n@appointment letters\ndesc: Away from campus, a letter each night from the letter pool.\nat: night\ndeals: trigger letter\nuse: letters\n\n# ---------------- notices ----------------\n\n@appointment intro\ndesc: Joining a track whose lead you haven't met: their `intro:` card, before the track's own.\nat: meet lead\ndeals: intro\nuse: intro\n\n@appointment start\ndesc: Enrolling in a course with a `start:` card (the lesson that teaches its spell): it comes the same month.\nat: enrol\nfor: course\ndeals: start\nuse: start\n\n@appointment exam\ndesc: A term's last month: the exam card for each course you're enrolled in.\nat: term end\nfor: course\ndeals: card course_exam\n\n@appointment appeal\ndesc: A failed course: the appeal, next month in its context.\nat: fail\nfor: course\ndeals: card grade_appeal\n\n@appointment warning\ndesc: A club, job or thesis ending a term below 40 performance.\nat: low marks\nfor: club job thesis\ndeals: card track_warning\n\n@appointment review\ndesc: Every job at term end, unless it was the last term: another term, or not.\nat: renewal\nfor: job\ndeals: card job_term_review\n\n@appointment promotion\ndesc: A club, job or thesis ending a term at 70 performance with the rank's experience.\nat: promotion\nfor: club job thesis\ndeals: card track_promotion\n\n@appointment spark_cooldown\ndesc: Spark with someone crossing the cooldown mark (not with a partner): whether you want this.\nat: spark cooldown\nfor: met\ndeals: card spark_cooldown\n"},{"path":"content/core/balance.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Numbers (design §8.4). Tier words in effects (`mood +medium`,\n#  `rel npc -small`) take their amount from `sizes`; challenge\n#  difficulty tiers shift the DC from the expected skill at that\n#  point of the run (`curve`, one value per turn, fitted by\n#  `node tools/grim.js curve`). All values are tunable.\n# ============================================================\n\n@balance\ndesc: Size tiers are tiny, small, medium, large and huge; skills use the xp row.\nsizes: health 3 5 10 16 25 | mood 4 8 13 20 30 | gold 3 8 15 30 60 | renown 2 4 8 13 20 | mana 3 6 10 16 25\n  | alignment 3 6 10 16 25 | xp 2 3 6 10 16 | lore 1 1 3 5 8 | rel 1 2 4 6 9 | spark 4 8 13 20 30\ndifficulty: trivial -15 easy -7 medium 0 hard 8 heroic 16\n# Skill XP (design §8.5): the next point costs a + b × level. Fitted so a balanced player averages about 55-60 at the\n# end of a four-year run (L: with the spell bonus); a focused skill reaches ~65-75 by month 24 (design ~85: an alarm).\nxp_cost: 1 0.04\n# Rank XP needed for each promotion (Member → Officer, Officer → Captain), plus performance ≥ 70.\nrank_xp: 10 25\nweights: health 1 mood 1 gold 0.5 renown 0.8 mana 0.4 alignment 0 xp 1.5 rel 0.6 spark 0.4\n# The combat stub (playtest 2, M39, M5): until the minigame, a fight reads arcana, vigor and guile (weighted), plus\n# condition × (the mean of health, mood and mana, each as % of its max, − 50) and spells × total spell levels (at most\n# +15). Entering costs mana.\ncombat: arcana 1 vigor 1 guile 0.5 condition 0.2 spells 0.75\ncombat_cost: small\n# The negotiation stub (E8): until the minigame, a negotiation reads charm, lore and guile (weighted; `using` doubles one),\n# plus mood and renown × their change since the run's start (as % of max). Known spells of those skills add the usual\n# spell bonus (spell_check).\nnegotiate: charm 1 lore 1 guile 1 mood 0.15 renown 0.15\n# A tonic (world expansion 1, K3): what a `boost:` state adds to the plain checks it names: one rung, about 8 points of odds\n# on a normal check. Tonics share `group: tonic`, so one holds at a time; fights and negotiations never read it.\ntonic: 4\n# A sized boost (threads 1): `boost: kind:size` adds its size here instead of a tonic's rung, and the largest boost held\n# counts. Owl's Ink, the illicit exam help, is `exam:large`: about a full grade (tools/exam-odds.js).\nboost_sizes: small 4 medium 8 large 12\n# How far one skill point moves a check's odds (M14): `wild` checks swing whatever your skill; `steady` ones follow it.\nswing: normal 2.5 wild 1.2 steady 4\n# A shift's pay (M10): `wage` in a job's cards pays the job's `pay:` at your rank × this share.\nwage: short 0.5 full 1 overtime 2\n# Coursework (feedback 2026-10-15): the skill XP an enrolled course gives once a term month, at course level I, II, III.\ncoursework: small small small\n# The range a check's odds are clamped to (L-3): a 5% forced fail and a 5% forced success. Milestone checks (a\n# `class: milestone` card, an exam) have no forced fail.\nodds: min 5 max 95\n# The check ladder (docs/authoring/checks.md, design §8.6): checks written with a rung, odds by nature and a luck word.\n# A rung is the DC: who or what you're up against.\ndc_tiers: novice- 0 novice 4 novice+ 8 apprentice- 12 apprentice 16 apprentice+ 20 journeyman- 24 journeyman 28\n  journeyman+ 32 adept- 36 adept 40 adept+ 44 expert- 48 expert 52 expert+ 56 master- 60 master 64 master+ 68\n  grandmaster- 72 grandmaster 76 grandmaster+ 80 archmage- 84 archmage 88 archmage+ 92\n# Odds by nature: the chance (%) when your skill matches the DC; a circumstance step (`+1 if …`) moves it by odds_step (z).\nlikelihood: certain 97 sure 90 likely 70 even 50 unlikely 30 slim 15 long_shot 5 freak 1\nodds_step: 0.5\n# Luck: the bell's σ. Odds = floor + (ceil − floor) × Φ(z(odds) + (skill − DC) / σ); milestones run 0–100.\nsigma: steady 12 normal 20 wild 32 luck 80\nbell: floor 2 ceil 98\n# Drift: how far the DC is pulled toward the expected skill (`curve`). Defaults: story none; milestone, crisis low; else high.\ndrift: none 0 low 0.15 mid 0.3 high 0.5\n# A course's standard rung at levels I, II, III (a track's `standard:` overrides), and the rungs a course card rises per\n# course level above its own `level:`.\ncourse_standard: apprentice+ adept+ master+\ncourse_step: 5\ncourse_drift: low\n# Exam preparation (L-3): a course card adds `attend` by itself (options add or cost more); each term month's\n# coursework adds `monthly` (feedback 2026-10-15b: it replaced the silent per-card 2, so preparation shows); at most `cap` counts.\n# 2026-10-19: `monthly` only in the three months before the exam (9 a term), and `check` instead when a check in that\n# course's class is won (once a month per course); the library's revision card adds 3 a term.\nstudy: attend 0 cap 15 monthly 3 check 5\n# A course exam's difficulty at course level I, II and III: the course standard ± rungs, with low drift (checks.md).\n# One rung higher since spells v2 (2026-10-17), two at II and III: the course spell, and its skill XP, now come at the\n# start of each level, before its exam, not after the pass. Balanced pass rates stay about 87 / 75 / 80%.\nexam_tiers: standard+2 standard+3 standard+3\n# An exam's grades (feedback 2026-10-15): A from `a` over the bar, B from `b`, a pass from `pass`, each on the bell (no\n# hard A gate), at `sigma` (2026-10-19: 16, was the steady 12, so skill shades the odds more gently). Going for top marks\n# (`sit_exam wild`) lowers the A mark by `ambitious` and is sat at `ambitious_sigma`.\n# Preparation floors (2026-10-19): the pass chance can't fall below a smooth ramp from `pass_from` to certain at\n# `pass_at`, nor B-or-better below one from `b_from` to `b_at`: 12 prepared is a sure pass, 15 a sure B.\nexam_bands: a 15 b 5 pass -5 ambitious 8 ambitious_sigma 20 sigma 16 pass_from 6 pass_at 12 b_from 9 b_at 15\n# Peers grow with the player (feedback 2026-10-15): a character with one of these tags (classmates, and the pen pal at\n# Thornwick) has every skill stat scaled up with E(turn), so that by the last month their best skill is `share` of the\n# final E(turn). Faculty and villagers keep theirs.\nnpc_growth: share 0.9 tags peer thornwick\n# A known spell adds `per` to checks of its skill (`@spell skill:`), at most `cap` (design §9.2; L-3).\nspell_check: per 2 cap 6\n# Learning a spell, or a level of one, gives skill XP in its skill (L-6): one tier per level of a course spell, and\n# `spell_bonus_one` for a one-level spell (spells v2 §B: every other spell large).\nspell_bonus: medium large huge\nspell_bonus_one: large\n# A forbidden spell's cast (feedback 2026-10-19): a third of its `alignment: small`, on a card and in a fight\n# (combat/setup/roster.mjs ALIGNMENT_COST.forbidden keeps the same number).\nforbidden_cast: alignment 2\n# Spark follows friendship (M37): a friendship gain adds spark × (spark / per), at most cap× (partner× for a partner);\n# spark crossing `cooldown` asks whether you want it (@game spark_cooldown). Only from `from` spark (2026-10-20: a romantic\n# moment has to start it), and until that question has come, the spark it carries is applied but not shown.\nspark_follow: per 25 cap 2 partner 3 cooldown 30 from 12\ncurve: 12 13 14 16 18 19 19 21 23 24 26 26 27 28 29 31 33 33 34 35 36 37 39 40 40 41 42 43 46 46 47 48 49 50 52 53 53 54 54 55 57 57 58 58 59 60 61 62\n\n# ---------------- Presets (design §8.4): named groups of effects ----------------\n\n@preset grind\nparams: skill\ndesc: Hard work on one skill.\ndo:\n  $skill +medium\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n\n@preset rest\ndesc: A real rest.\ndo:\n  mood +medium\n  health +small\n\n@preset sleep\ndesc: A night's sleep (bedtime, 2026-10-17): 8 Health and 4 Mood; at home on a break, 20 and 10. Taken when you go to bed, whatever your condition.\ndo:\n  if break and has(plan_home):\n    health += 20\n    mood += 10\n  else:\n    health += 8\n    mood += 4\n\n@preset social\nparams: who\ndesc: Time with someone.\ndo:\n  rel $who +small\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n\n@preset work\ndesc: Paid work.\ndo:\n  gold +medium\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n\n@preset price_dear\ndesc: A dear price (world expansion 1, K3): what a shop asks for a special piece, 90 crowns.\ndo:\n  gold -huge\n  gold -large\n\n@preset cure_dire\ndesc: The infirmary's full cure once you've needed it twice already (feedback 2026-10-25): 120 crowns.\ndo:\n  gold -huge\n  gold -huge\n\n@preset price_fortune\ndesc: A small fortune, 150 crowns: what the rarest pieces cost.\ndo:\n  gold -huge\n  gold -huge\n  gold -large\n\n@preset splurge\ndesc: Spending to feel better.\ndo:\n  mood +large\n  gold -medium\n\n@preset risk\nparams: res\ndesc: A gamble: a large gain or a medium loss.\ndo:\n  chance even:\n    $res +large\n  else:\n    $res -medium\n\n# ---------------- Origins (docs/origin-design.md, 2026-10-16) ----------------\n# Starting packages, applied before the first term's charges. Skills are XP grants through the ordinary XP cost\n# (xp_cost: 1 0.04), calibrated to land on the design's levels from the base values in rules.grim: each grant is\n# the sum of 1 + 0.04 × level from the base to the target (test/origins.test.js recomputes them). Purses are added\n# to the base 30 crowns.\n\n@preset origin_noble_start\ndesc: Charm 25, Lore 12; 60 crowns (90 with the 30 everyone starts with).\ndo:\n  charm += 27.88\n  lore += 5.52\n  gold +huge\n\n@preset origin_scholar_start\ndesc: Lore 28, Arcana 12; 30 crowns (60 with the 30 everyone starts with).\ndo:\n  lore += 34\n  arcana += 5.52\n  gold +large\n\n@preset origin_hedge_start\ndesc: Alchemy 30, Arcana 15, Lore 6; 15 crowns (45 with the 30 everyone starts with).\ndo:\n  alchemy += 40.8\n  arcana += 10.08\n  lore -= 2\n  gold +medium\n\n@preset origin_street_start\ndesc: Guile 30, Vigor 18; 8 crowns (38 with the 30 everyone starts with).\ndo:\n  guile += 40.8\n  vigor += 15\n  gold +small\n\n@preset origin_merchant_start\ndesc: Charm 25, Guile 15; 120 crowns (150 with the 30 everyone starts with).\ndo:\n  charm += 27.88\n  guile += 12.6\n  gold +huge\n  gold +huge\n\n# Support before each later term (terms 2–8), paid by the origin's funding card in the last break month. Cut by\n# about a third from the design's amounts (2026-10-16: supported origins ended runs with several hundred crowns).\n@preset noble_allowance\ndesc: The family's allowance, 38 crowns, and the expectations that come with it.\ndo:\n  gold +large\n  gold +small\n  mood -small\n\n@preset noble_gift\ndesc: A family gift on top, 11 crowns (30% of terms, rolled when the letter comes).\ndo:\n  gold +small\n  gold +tiny\n\n@preset scholarship_stipend\ndesc: By the latest completed term's GPA: 3.5 or higher, 46 crowns (with an 8-crown merit bonus); 3.0 or higher, 38; below, nothing.\ndo:\n  if term_gpa >= 3.5:\n    gold +large\n    gold +small\n    gold +small\n  elif term_gpa >= 3.0:\n    gold +large\n    gold +small\n\n@preset trade_returns\ndesc: The company's share after a good trading period, 78 crowns (70% of terms, rolled when the report comes).\ndo:\n  gold +huge\n  gold +medium\n  gold +tiny\n"},{"path":"content/core/conds.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Named conditions (docs/grimoire-script.md §9): call them in any expression, e.g. courting(lyra).\n# ============================================================\n\n@cond courting\ndesc: Not together and not exes: a romance with them is still open.\nparams: who\nis: bond(who) != \"partner\" and bond(who) != \"ex\"\n\n@cond partner_here\ndesc: Your partner, and around to be met in person now.\nparams: who\nis: bond(who) == \"partner\" and around(who)\n\n@cond spell_known\ndesc: You have learnt this spell (any level).\nparams: s\nis: spell_level(s) >= 1\n\n# Renown's tiers (design §8.2; the numbers match `tiers:` on @resource renown): the spotlight cards read these.\n@cond renown_known\ndesc: The academy knows your face: renown 30 or more.\nis: renown >= 30\n\n@cond renown_famous\ndesc: The academy watches you: renown 60 or more.\nis: renown >= 60\n\n@cond renown_legend\ndesc: You are the academy's own story: renown 85 or more.\nis: renown >= 85\n\n@cond fallen_star\ndesc: Yesterday's news: you were Famous once (the flag is set on crossing into it) and are back under 30.\nis: flag(was_famous) and renown < 30\n\n# Between people (phase 4, content/events/between.grim): two characters, as each other's friend or foe.\n@cond at_odds\ndesc: These two can't stand each other.\nparams: a b\nis: npc_rel(a, b) <= -5\n\n@cond get_along\ndesc: These two get on.\nparams: a b\nis: npc_rel(a, b) >= 5\n\n@cond classmate_here\ndesc: A fellow student you've met, around to be met in person now (not an outsider).\nparams: x\nis: tagged(x, peer) and not tagged(x, outsider) and met(x) and around(x)\n\n@cond fancy_free\ndesc: Free to fall for someone else: not your partner or your ex, and not sweet on you.\nparams: x\nis: bond(x) != \"partner\" and bond(x) != \"ex\" and spark(x) < 30\n\n@cond before_finals\ndesc: The two term months that lead up to each term's finals (3–4, 9–10), when exam help sells.\nis: term and (month == 3 or month == 4 or month == 9 or month == 10)\n"},{"path":"content/core/rules.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Lumenhold Academy — core rules\n#  Resources, skills, calendar, global hooks.\n#  See docs/grimoire-script.md for the language.\n# ============================================================\n\n@game\ntitle: Lumenhold Academy\nsubtitle: Four years. Forty-eight moons. One apprentice.\n# The content format version (design §12). Frozen: v1 may only grow; see test/golden/schema-v1.json.\nschema: 1\n# Four years (design §4.1). The academic year starts in autumn; the run ends in Starfall of year 4 (turn 48).\n# Year 4's last break is the wrap-up: no plans or duties, board on the house; its last month only resolves the run.\nyears: 4\nlast_month: 12\nwrapup: 11-12\nmonths: Harvestmoon, Emberwane, Mistveil, Frostcall, Deepwinter, Thawmoon, Stormbloom, Greenrise, Blossomtide, Highsun, Goldenhaze, Starfall\ncalendar: term=1-4 break=5-6 term=7-10 break=11-12\nseasons: autumn=1-3 winter=4-6 spring=7-9 summer=10-12\nsummer_months: 5, 6, 11, 12\n# Each month is one day (design §4.2). The two visits are picked on the map.\nday_term: morning=courses noon=social:1-2 afternoon=visit evening=visit night=world:1-2 midnight=special:0-1\nday_break: morning=plan afternoon=visit evening=visit night=world:1 midnight=special:0\nday_last: morning=special:0\n# Social and world cards are dealt across the whole day (playtest 2, M43), not only at noon and night; `slot:` requires a\n# slot and `time:` favours one.\nspread: social world\n# The origin is picked in the mirror (choose_origin, a menu whose options are the origins): in the game, during the\n# prologue before month 1 (newGame `prologue`); tools and tests pass it to newGame instead.\norigins: choose_origin\n# The exam, the appeal, the off-shift hint and the spark-cooldown card are dealt by their appointments\n# (content/core/appointments.grim).\n# An option that charges crowns up front is locked when you can't pay (review fixes), unless every option would be.\nafford: gold\n# The term-time map; a break plan's `map_id:` replaces it (home, or the internship host's).\nmap_id: campus\ncards_per_month: 3\nmax_cards_per_month: 7\nsocial_per_month: 1\nmin_random_per_month: 1\nintro: The sky-barge lurches. Below, the lake glitters; above, the seven towers of Lumenhold Academy pierce the clouds. You have a trunk, a borrowed wand and a letter of acceptance with your name spelled wrong. Four years begin now.\n\n# ---------------- Resources ----------------\n# The top bars (design §8.1) are Health · Mood · Mana · Renown · Alignment, in this order;\n# `display: panel` resources are shown in the You panel instead; `display: number` (Crowns, Renown) sits in the top row\n# as a plain number, after the meters, with what next month's start will take or give under it.\n\n@resource health\nname: Health\nicon: heart\nmin: 0\nmax: 100\nstart: 70\ncolor: #d9606a\ndesc: Your body. At zero you collapse.\nwarn_below: 20\non_zero:\n  queue crisis_collapse now\n\n@resource mood\nname: Mood\nicon: sun\nmin: 0\nmax: 100\nstart: 60\ncolor: #e6b84a\ndesc: Your spirits. At zero, despair takes hold.\nwarn_below: 20\non_zero:\n  queue crisis_despair now\n\n@resource mana\nname: Mana\nicon: sparkle\nmin: 0\n# One scale with the combat minigame (combat/INTEGRATION.md §2): the fight's Mana capacity, 43 × (2/3 + weighted skill/150),\n# Arcana counted twice: 32 at the starting skills, 43 at 50, 53 at 80 (`manaCapacity`, combat/setup/roster.mjs).\nmax: round(43 * (2 / 3 + (2 * arcana + lore + alchemy + charm + vigor + guile) / 1050))\nstart: 30\ncolor: #5fa8e8\ndesc: The magic you can spend on spells. Refills each month, faster the stronger your Arcana.\nwarn_below: 8\n\n@resource renown\nname: Renown\nicon: star\nmin: 0\nmax: 100\nstart: 5\ncolor: #b58ae0\n# A plain number beside Crowns (feedback 2026-10-15), with next month's fading shown under it.\ndisplay: number\n# Design §8.2's tiers, shown beside the number (phase 4); the spotlight cards read the same steps.\ntiers: 30 Known, 60 Famous, 85 Legend\ndesc: How much the academy talks about you — for better or worse. Fades a little every month.\n\n@resource alignment\nname: Alignment\nicon: moon\nmin: -100\nmax: 100\nstart: 0\ncolor: #c9b8ff\ndrag: -50 0.5\ndesc: Lumen or Hollow: the light you serve, or the dark that has noticed you. Deep in the Hollow, the way back is slow.\n\n@resource gold\nname: Crowns\nicon: coin\nmin: 0\nmax: 99999\nstart: 30\ncolor: #d7a948\n# Shown in the middle of the top row as a plain number (playtest 2, step 5), not in the You panel.\ndisplay: number\nwarn_below: 6\ndesc: Crowns in your purse. Rent is due every month.\non_zero:\n  flag broke\n\n# ---------------- Skills ----------------\n\n@skill arcana\nname: Arcana\nicon: wand\nstart: 8\nmax: 100\ndesc: Raw spellcraft — power, control, nerve.\n\n@skill lore\nname: Lore\nicon: book\nstart: 8\nmax: 100\ndesc: Scholarship, theory, history, languages of the old world.\n\n@skill alchemy\nname: Alchemy\nicon: potion\nstart: 6\nmax: 100\ndesc: Potions, herbs, reagents and the patience to stir clockwise.\n\n@skill charm\nname: Charm\nicon: mask\nstart: 8\nmax: 100\ndesc: Wit, warmth, persuasion. Making people want to say yes.\n\n@skill vigor\nname: Vigor\nicon: sword\nstart: 8\nmax: 100\ndesc: Stamina, reflexes, dueling and broom-flight.\n\n@skill guile\nname: Guile\nicon: eye\nstart: 6\nmax: 100\ndesc: Sneaking, lying, noticing what others miss.\n\n# ---------------- Global hooks ----------------\n\n# Unpaid board (design §8.2): a hungry month; twice in a row, Grisk settles it for you (grisk_board_debt).\n@hook rent\non: month_start\nlabel: Dormitory & board\nwhen: turn > 1 and term\ndo:\n  if gold >= 8:\n    gold -8\n    board_missed = 0\n  else:\n    gold = 0\n    gain hungry\n    board_missed += 1\n    if board_missed >= 2 and not has(in_debt) and not flag(grisk_done):\n      board_missed = 0\n      queue grisk_board_debt\n  say: {board_missed == 0: {~The bursar takes the board money with a nod and a tick in a very large book.|Board paid: a bed, three meals and the right to complain about both.|You pay the board and count what's left twice.}|{~The board went unpaid. The refectory still feeds you, a little less each day.|No board money this month. Lunch is a pocketed roll.|Board unpaid again. Your name in the bursar's very large book is underlined now.}}\n\n@hook break_rent\non: month_start\nlabel: Board at the castle\nwhen: break and (has(plan_castle) or has(plan_work))\ndo:\n  gold -8\n  say: {~The castle in the holidays still wants its board.|A bed in an empty dormitory costs the same as a full one.}\n\n# Tuition each term (design §8.2): everyone pays it (origin design 2026-10-16). A family's or the Crown's support\n# arrives as cash before the term, on the origin's funding card (content/events/origin_funding.grim).\n@hook tuition\non: term_start\nlabel: Tuition\ndo:\n  gold -16\n  say: {~Term fees, paid at a window with a very small grille.|The bursar's quill scratches. Another term is paid for.}\n\n# Course fees (review fixes): charged with the term, like tuition, not by the enrolment card.\n@hook course_fees\non: term_start\nwhen: term_index <= 6\nlabel: Course fees\ndo:\n  gold -small\n  say: {~Course fees, paid at the registrar's window with the tuition.|The registrar takes the course fees with a sigh, as if it pained her too.}\n\n@hook gpa_probation\non: term_end\nlabel: Academic probation\nwhen: gpa > 0 and (year >= 2 or month >= 10)\ndo:\n  if gpa < 1.5 and not has(probation):\n    gain probation\n  if gpa < 1.0:\n    gpa_low += 1\n  else:\n    gpa_low = 0\n  if gpa_low >= 2:\n    gpa_low = 0\n    queue crisis_probation\n\n# Coursework (feedback 2026-10-15): each enrolled course trains its main skill once a term month through its cards,\n# like a job's wage (engine: courseworkFor, `@balance coursework:`), so there are no coursework hooks.\n\n@hook term_stress\non: month_start\nlabel: Term-time pressure\nwhen: turn > 1 and term\ndo:\n  mood -3\n  if month == 4 or month == 10:\n    mood -6\n  say: {month == 4 or month == 10: {~Exams are coming, and everybody's quill moves faster.|The library stays open late and is full until it closes.|You dream of an exam you haven't revised for, which is most of them.}|{~Essays stack up on your desk like weather coming in.|Lectures, reading, more reading. Term has a weight of its own.|Everyone is behind. Everyone says so in the corridors.}}\n\n@hook exhaustion_drain\non: month_start\nlabel: Running on empty\nwhen: health < 25\ndo:\n  mood -6\n  say: {~You are so tired that the stairs look personal.|Your body has opinions about this month, and none of them are kind.|You fall asleep in a lecture and wake up in a different mood.}\n\n@hook gloom_drain\non: month_start\nlabel: Gloom\nwhen: mood < 20\ndo:\n  health -5\n  say: {~A grey month. You eat little and sleep badly.|Low spirits sit in your chest like a cold.|The gloom gets into your bones; even the stairs feel steeper.}\n\n# Health no longer comes back by itself at month start (bedtime, 2026-10-17): recovery is the night's sleep\n# (`sleep` preset, on night_out and night_sleep in content/events/night.grim).\n\n@hook fame_fades\non: month_start\nlabel: Fading gossip\nwhen: turn > 1 and renown >= 5\n# Fame holds better the more of it there is (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md): a fifth a month below Known, less while\n# Known, less again once Famous, so the spotlight tiers can be reached and kept, and lost by going quiet. Legend\n# keeps Famous's rate: a month begins at Legend only from 97 or more before the fade, so it lasts a month or three.\ndo:\n  if renown >= 60:\n    renown -= floor(renown * 0.12)\n  elif renown >= 30:\n    renown -= floor(renown * 0.15)\n  else:\n    renown -= floor(renown * 0.2)\n  say: {~The corridors find something else to talk about.|Yesterday's gossip goes the way of yesterday's bread.|Fewer heads turn when you pass. It's restful.}\n\n@hook mana_regen\non: month_start\nlabel: Mana returns\nwhen: turn > 1\ndo:\n  mana += 10 + arcana / 10\n  say: {~The magic seeps back into you overnight, like warmth into cold hands.|Your fingertips tingle again by morning.|Whatever you spent, the castle quietly gives some back.}\n\n# ---------------- Game start ----------------\n\n@hook start\non: game_start\ndo:\n  queue intro_arrival now\n  # Picked before the game (tools, tests): the mirror only reflects it. Otherwise the mirror asks (the prologue).\n  if has(origin_noble) or has(origin_scholar) or has(origin_hedge) or has(origin_street) or has(origin_merchant):\n    queue sorting_mirror now\n  else:\n    queue choose_origin now\n"},{"path":"content/core/templates.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Templates (docs/grimoire-script.md §9).\n#  A thread with `params:` is a template: a thread `from:` it fills the params in (`with:`), lists its cards by role\n#  (`members:`), and each member takes its role's pool (<template>.<role>) and may call the template's presets\n#  (<template>.<name>, or just <name>). Pools here that other pools build on (`from:`) hold what a kind of pool shares.\n#  Macros inside a template: $param, $this (the card), $thread, $<role> (a role's only card), a member's own values\n#  ($flag), count(<role>: expr) and `first <role> [except this] [where expr]:`.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- story: a start card, then steps that wait on what it began ----------------\n\n@thread story\ndesc: A bespoke storyline. The start card begins it (it sets what $begun tests); every other card is a step and waits on $begun.\nparams: begun\nroles: start step\ndefault_role: step\n\n@pool story.step\nwhen: $begun\n\n# ---------------- quest: start, preparations in any order, a final check (every club and job capstone) ----------------\n\n@thread quest\ndesc: A start card offers it once ($begun not yet set); the parts come in any order, each until its $flag is set; the final needs $need parts $after month(s) after the start, or comes anyway $giveup months on. `next` (in a part's working option) deals, once a day ($beat), the final when it is ready, else the next part not done.\nparams: begun need=2 after=1 giveup=4 beat=chain_beat\nroles: start part final\n\n@pool quest.start\ntrigger: auto\nrepeat: 3\nwhen: not flag($begun)\n\n@pool quest.part\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 60\nwhen: flag($begun) and not flag($flag)\n\n@pool quest.final\nclass: milestone\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 40\nwhen: flag($begun) and since($start) >= $after and (count(part: flag($flag)) >= $need or since($start) >= $giveup)\n\n@preset quest.next\ndesc: Once a day: the final if enough parts are done, else the next part not done (this one aside).\ndo:\n  if $beat != turn:\n    $beat = turn\n    if since($start) >= $after and count(part: flag($flag)) >= $need:\n      queue $final now\n    else:\n      first part except this where not flag($flag):\n        queue $part now\n\n# ---------------- scene: a run of cards in one sitting ----------------\n\n@thread scene\ndesc: A run of cards in one visit. Entry cards start it; each step that is got past deals the next with `next`, drawn from the scene's queued cards like any pool.\nroles: entry step\ndefault_role: step\n\n@pool scene.step\ntrigger: queued\n\n@preset scene.next\ndesc: The next card of the scene, drawn from its steps.\ndo:\n  queue any in $thread now\n\n# ---------------- pool templates: what every pool of one kind shares ----------------\n\n@pool course_routine\ndesc: What every course pool shares (6 pools).\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\ntags: class\n\n@pool course_routine_repeat4\ndesc: What the course pools whose cards come back four times share (3 pools); the others use course_routine.\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\ntags: class\n"},{"path":"content/endings.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Endings. `kind: early` endings are only reached via `end <id>`\n#  and stay exclusive (game over).\n#  Graduation builds a yearbook (design §10.5) from `slot:`s, each\n#  checked in descending `priority`:\n#    headline (default)  1: the career you signed on the last day\n#                           (`took_<career>`, last_career); none\n#                           signed: still looking or back home;\n#                           `ending_graduate` (no `when`) is the\n#                           fallback\n#    mystery             0-1: the Hollow Star outcome\n#    relationships       0-3: bonds kept on the last day\n#    personal            0-2: academic standing, and the lines you\n#                           let the yearbook keep (last_remembered)\n#  Every line is told, and chosen where there is a choice, by a\n#  card on the last day (last_day.grim, feedback 2026-10-25).\n#  `excludes:` drops other endings once this one is picked.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Early endings ----------------\n\n@ending ending_sent_home\nkind: early\ntitle: Sent Home\nart: cross\ntext: The infirmary writes to your family. A carriage comes for you on a grey morning and you watch the towers shrink in the rear window. Your body gave out before your dreams did. Perhaps, one day, you will come back.\n\n@ending ending_quiet_departure\nkind: early\ntitle: The Quiet Departure\nart: rain\ntext: You take the dawn barge without telling anyone. The academy does not notice right away; then, one by one, people do. Some of them write. You keep the letters in a box you don't open for years. When you finally do, you are someone gentler, and you find you are not alone.\n\n@ending ending_expelled\nkind: early\ntitle: Expelled\nart: scales\ntext: The Board's decision is final. Your wand is not taken — Lumenhold is not cruel — but your name is struck from the register in ink that cannot be unwritten. You walk out of the gates with a trunk and a lesson, and the long road ahead is entirely your own.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_claimed\nkind: early\ntitle: Claimed by the Hollow Star\nart: moon\ntext: You open the door beneath the lake. The Hollow Star looks back. For a moment you understand everything, and then there is no \"you\" left to understand it. On clear nights students swear they see a new star, black against black, right above the seventh tower.\n\n@ending ending_runaway\nkind: early\ntitle: Gone With the Wind\nart: map\ntext: You and Corvin leave Lumenhold on a stolen sky-barge at midnight, laughing so hard you can barely steer. The academy will tell the story for years — usually wrong. You never graduate. You don't regret it, mostly.\n\n# ---------------- Final endings (graduation) ----------------\n\n@ending ending_hollow_keeper\nslot: mystery\npriority: 100\ntitle: Keeper of the Hollow Star\nart: moon\nwhen: flag(hollow_bargain)\ntext: You graduate with a black star on your palm and a voice in the back of your mind that is not entirely yours. The Hollow Star gave you power; it will want something back. For now, the towers bow a little when you pass, and even the headmistress looks away first.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_freed\nslot: mystery\npriority: 98\ntitle: Herald of the Hollow Star\nart: moon\nwhen: flag(hollow_freed)\ntext: The spring the lake froze, the Star went free, and you held the door. It did not swallow you; it remembered who let it out. The Northern families write to you as one of their own now, and wherever you walk, candles burn a little lower, as if bowing. Lumenhold still hands you a diploma. Nobody at the ceremony looks you in the eye.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_failed\nslot: mystery\npriority: 90\ntitle: The Night Was Lost\nart: door\nwhen: flag(hollow_failed)\ntext: The spring the lake froze, you went down beneath it, and the door would not close for you. The headmistress closed it in your place and paid for it with years she didn't have to spare. The Wardens don't blame you. Every spring, when the lake thaws, you do, a little, and then you get up and keep the old doors anyway.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_hero\nslot: mystery\npriority: 95\ntitle: Warden of Lumenhold\nart: shield\nwhen: flag(hollow_sealed)\ntext: The academy will never fully know what you did beneath the lake. The headmistress does. At graduation she gives you a silver key without a lock and a seat among the Wardens, the quiet few who keep the old doors closed. Kael stands in the crowd and, for once, he is smiling.\n\n@ending ending_archmage\npriority: 80\ntitle: The Archmage's Path\nart: wand\nwhen: flag(took_conclave)\ntext: In the autumn you climb the steps of the Seven Towers in a new grey robe, an apprentice of the Conclave, with seven years of wards and failures ahead and a desk that faces a wall of locked books. Professor Thorne shakes your hand at the gate and says, gruffly, \"Don't waste it.\" You won't.\n\n@ending ending_scholar\npriority: 75\ntitle: Scholar of the Spire\nart: book\nwhen: flag(took_fellowship)\ntext: Your thesis is shelved in the Spire Library, in the section students whisper about, and you take the junior fellowship that came with it. You will spend your life among books that bite and secrets that wait — and you could not be happier.\n\n@ending ending_beloved\nslot: relationships\npriority: 70\ntitle: Hand in Hand\nart: heart\nwhen: bonds(partner) > 0\ntext: Graduation is a blur of robes and cheers, but what you remember is a hand finding yours as the hats go up. Whatever comes next, you won't face it alone. Lumenhold taught you magic; it also taught you this.\n\n# A partner ending names them (playtest 2, B31); each replaces the generic one above.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_lyra\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: The Second Flame\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"partner\"\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: When the hats go up, {lyra} catches yours before it lands and hands it back without a word. Later, in front of her whole family, she takes your hand and doesn't let go. For House Vaneshade, that is a declaration. For her it is simpler: she has finally found someone she doesn't need to beat.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_tobias\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Home, Wherever\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(tobias) == \"partner\"\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: {tobias} cries through the whole ceremony and blames the pollen. Afterwards he lifts you off your feet in front of everyone, then shows you a drawing of a farmhouse with two chairs on the porch. \"No pressure,\" he says. \"The goats have already voted.\"\n\n@ending ending_beloved_seraphine\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Already Foreseen\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(seraphine) == \"partner\"\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: \"I saw this,\" {seraphine} says as the hats go up. \"Years ago. You were holding my hand.\" You are. For once she smiles at the present, as if it has finally caught up with her, and asks whether you'd like to know what happens next. You tell her you'd rather find out together.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_corvin\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: No More Jokes\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(corvin) == \"partner\"\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: {corvin} slips a key into your palm during the speeches. It opens nothing at Lumenhold; he has checked. \"It's for wherever we end up,\" he says, and for once he doesn't make a joke of it. Then he does, of course. But his hand stays in yours.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_mir\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Built to Last\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(mir) == \"partner\"\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: {mir} has rigged the hats to fly back down. Most of them do. In the cheerful chaos they press a small brass ring into your hand; it ticks faintly, like a second heart. \"It'll run for fifty years,\" they say. \"Probably. We should check.\"\n\n@ending ending_beloved_kael\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Thaw\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(kael) == \"partner\"\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: {kael} doesn't throw his hat. He stands beside you in the crowd, and when the cheering is loudest he says, very quietly, \"Come north with me. Or I stay. Either.\" It is the longest sentence about feelings he has ever spoken. His hand, in yours, is warm.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_nessa\nslot: relationships\npriority: 72\ntitle: Front Page\nart: heart\nwhen: bond(nessa) == \"partner\"\nexcludes: ending_beloved\ntext: The Ledger's graduation issue runs a small notice at the bottom of page six, set in {nessa}'s own type: two names, one line, no comment. All of Lumenhold reads it before breakfast. {nessa} pretends not to watch them read it, and holds your hand under the table the whole time.\n\n@ending ending_alchemist\npriority: 65\ntitle: Master of the Crucible\nart: cauldron\nwhen: flag(took_shop)\ntext: Your potions are already sold under the counter in three cities. On graduation day you sign the lease on the crooked little shop by the harbour. The sign reads your name, and underneath: NO REFUNDS FOR TRANSFORMATIONS.\n\n@ending ending_celebrity\nslot: personal\npriority: 60\ntitle: The Name on Every Lip\nart: star\nwhen: renown >= 45 and flag(remember_renown)\ntext: The Lumen Ledger runs a special edition about you. First-years queue for your autograph. You graduate as the most talked-about student in a generation, and the world beyond the gates is already waiting to hear what you'll do next.\n\n@ending ending_merchant\npriority: 55\ntitle: Gold in the Vaults\nart: coin\nwhen: flag(took_trading)\ntext: You leave Lumenhold with a diploma, a fortune and an old partner's desk in a harbour shipping house. Magic, you've decided, is good, but compound interest is better. Within five years the house has offices in every port — and an enchanted ledger that never loses a coin.\n\n@ending ending_shadow\npriority: 50\ntitle: The Quiet Hand\nart: mask\nwhen: flag(took_unseen)\ntext: No one quite knows how you graduated with those grades. No one quite remembers the favours you called in. In the autumn you take a desk at the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs, in a building with no sign and no address. You'd found it, of course, long before they told you where it was.\n\n@ending ending_beloved_friends\nslot: relationships\npriority: 45\ntitle: The Ones Who Stayed\nart: group\nwhen: bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3 and flag(kept_friends)\ntext: You may not leave as the brightest or the richest, but you leave surrounded. Your friends throw their hats higher than anyone's, and the photograph from that day — all of you squinting into the sun — will hang in every home you ever have.\n\n@ending ending_barely\nslot: personal\npriority: 65\ntitle: By the Skin of Your Teeth\nart: hourglass\nwhen: (turn > 4 and gpa < 2.0) or flag(thesis_resubmit)\ntext: Your name is called last, after a long pause and some whispering between examiners. But it is called. You graduate. You are a mage of Lumenhold, and nobody ever has to know the details.\n\n@ending ending_bystander\nslot: mystery\npriority: 20\ntitle: Someone Else's Lantern\nart: lantern\nwhen: not (flag(hollow_sealed) or flag(hollow_bargain) or flag(hollow_freed) or flag(hollow_failed) or flag(hollow_fled) or flag(hollow_held_line))\ntext: There was a spring frost, the year you graduated, and a night when the bell under the lake would not stop ringing. You were somewhere else: asleep, or studying, or holding a door shut for the first-years. Someone else went down with the lantern. You never learn their whole story, but every spring, when the lake thaws, you find yourself grateful to them.\n\n@ending ending_graduate\npriority: 0\ntitle: A Mage of Lumenhold\nart: tower\ntext: Robes, hats, a speech that runs long. You walk out of the gates of Lumenhold a qualified mage — not legendary, not disgraced, simply yourself, with four years of stories and a whole world to spend them in.\n\n@ending ending_teacher\npriority: 72\ntitle: The Teacher's Desk\nart: feather\nwhen: flag(took_teaching)\ntext: In the autumn you return to Lumenhold — not on the student barge, but on the faculty one. Your classroom has a window over the lake and a desk with somebody else's initials carved in it. On the first day a terrified first-year asks a question you once asked. You know exactly what to say.\n\n@ending ending_wanderer\npriority: 58\ntitle: The Road of Wonders\nart: tent\nwhen: flag(took_circus)\ntext: You join the Travelling Circus of Wonders as its youngest illusionist. For years you cross the kingdoms in a painted wagon, making dragons out of moonlight for children who have never seen magic. Every winter, a letter arrives from Lumenhold. You always write back.\n\n# ---------------- Relationship lines: Ilse Dumont's chain ----------------\n\n@ending ending_tamsin_friends\nslot: relationships\npriority: 40\ntitle: A Friend Across the Hills\nart: letter\nwhen: flag(tamsin_friends) and rel(tamsin) >= 15 and flag(kept_tamsin)\ntext: Your yearbook entry has a green seal pressed into the corner, which is not allowed. Tamsin Reed of Thornwick sent it, with a note: \"Four years of rivalry, four hundred letters, one friend. Insufferable to the end.\" Every spring, at a town halfway between, there are biscuits.\n\n@ending ending_dumonts_reunited\nslot: relationships\npriority: 50\ntitle: Two Dumonts in the Photograph\nart: snow\nwhen: flag(dumont_reunited) and flag(kept_dumonts)\ntext: In the graduation photograph, at the edge of the crowd, two pale heads lean together: Kael, and the sister who was raised to open the door he refused. Neither of them is smiling at the camera. They are smiling at each other, which is rarer, and which you helped to happen.\n\n@ending ending_ilse_taken\nslot: relationships\npriority: 45\ntitle: A Chair Left Empty\nart: rain\nwhen: flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(dumont_reunited) and flag(kept_ilse_chair)\ntext: The man in grey took Ilse north, and no letter has come. At graduation Kael keeps the seat beside him empty without saying why. You know why. Some evenings, when the lake goes very still, you still look for a girl in white furs on the far shore.\n\n# ---------------- Personal lines (yearbook) ----------------\n\n@ending ending_thesis_hollowstar\nslot: personal\npriority: 35\ntitle: The Thesis They Locked Away\nart: book\nwhen: flag(thesis_hollowstar) and flag(remember_thesis)\ntext: Your thesis on the founders' seal earned a distinction and then disappeared into the headmistress's tower. Its catalogue card in the Spire Library reads only \"Restricted\", in her handwriting. Three people in the world have read it. One of them still writes to you with questions.\n\n@ending ending_lumen\nslot: personal\npriority: 30\ntitle: A Lantern for Others\nart: lantern\nwhen: alignment >= 60 and flag(remember_lumen)\ntext: In the yearbook, under your portrait, someone has written: \"The one you went to when it mattered.\" First-years you barely remember will name their own lanterns after you. Lumenhold has a word for people like that. It is the same word as its name.\n\n@ending ending_hollow_touched\nslot: personal\npriority: 30\ntitle: The Shadow at Your Heel\nart: mask\nwhen: alignment <= -60 and flag(remember_hollow)\ntext: Your portrait in the yearbook is the only one where the candles behind you seem to have gone out. People step aside when you pass, not quite knowing why. The Hollow has learned your name, and you find, more and more often, that you answer to it.\n\n# ---------------- The Night's other outcomes (mystery; feedback 2026-10-25) ----------------\n\n@ending ending_hollow_fled\nslot: mystery\npriority: 85\ntitle: The Door Left Ajar\nart: door\nwhen: flag(hollow_fled)\ntext: You went down beneath the frozen lake, saw the door, and ran. Nobody blames you to your face; the headmistress, whose hair went white that night, never mentions it at all. Somewhere under the water the door still stands ajar. Every spring, when the ice goes out, you dream of the dark between the gap and the frame.\n\n@ending ending_held_the_line\nslot: mystery\npriority: 80\ntitle: The Night Above the Ice\nart: shield\nwhen: flag(hollow_held_line)\ntext: The night the lake froze, you didn't go down. You held the doors of the first-year tower against frost and whispers until dawn, and every child in it lived. Someone else went beneath the lake, or no one did; the first-years never cared which. They carved your initials over that door, and nobody has dared paint them out.\n\n# ---------------- Careers you signed on the last day (headline; feedback 2026-10-25) ----------------\n\n@ending ending_royal_archivist\npriority: 70\ntitle: Keeper of the Folios\nart: book\nwhen: flag(took_royal_library)\ntext: You take the cold room at the Royal Library Archives, the one with the folios that object to being opened. Within a year you have catalogued a shelf nobody had dared touch in a century, and the keeper has stopped checking your gloves. On the staff list, under \"Cataloguer\", your name is in copperplate.\n\n@ending ending_guild\npriority: 64\ntitle: A Bench at the Works\nart: cauldron\nwhen: flag(took_guild)\ntext: You join the Guild of Alchemists' great works as its newest journeyman, in protective clothing you paid for yourself. There are two explosions a month and none of them yours. By the second winter the foreman sends the hardest batches to your bench, and pretends he doesn't.\n\n@ending ending_observatory\npriority: 63\ntitle: Charting the Night\nart: star\nwhen: flag(took_observatory)\ntext: You go back up to the plateau with the Observatory Expedition, salaried now, frostbite remedies in your pack. The nights are long and very cold and you stay awake through every one of them. Two years on, a star on the new charts carries your initials, in very small letters.\n\n@ending ending_field_station\npriority: 62\ntitle: Out on the Moors\nart: paw\nwhen: flag(took_field_station)\ntext: You take the keeper's cottage at the Beastcraft Field Station: a lamp, a kettle, and more creatures than any sane person would count. The winters are long, wet and lonely. You count the creatures anyway, every one, and the foal from your summer grows up following you about like a dog.\n\n@ending ending_thornwick\npriority: 61\ntitle: Across the Hills\nart: mirror\nwhen: flag(took_thornwick)\ntext: You cross the hills to Thornwick's illusion laboratory, where they think glamour is a science and are mostly right. Lumenhold says something cutting about it, as predicted. Within three years it offers to hire you back at twice the salary, as predicted. You haven't answered yet.\n\n@ending ending_librarian\npriority: 57\ntitle: The Stacks Kept You\nart: book\nwhen: flag(took_spire)\ntext: You never quite leave. In the autumn you are back behind the Spire Library's reference desk, a keeper of the stacks now, with a longer ladder and a key to the shelves nobody else may reach. Footnote has not bitten you in months. Students ask you for books, and you find them every time.\n\n@ending ending_newt\npriority: 54\ntitle: Behind the Bar\nart: cup\nwhen: flag(took_newt)\ntext: You stay on at the Gilded Newt, full time, behind the bar and then in charge of it. You remember who ordered the spiced cider, and who's owed a favour, and which of the card games upstairs to look away from. Students who were first-years when you graduated come in to tell you their troubles. You pour, and listen.\n\n@ending ending_pell\npriority: 53\ntitle: Counting Drops\nart: cauldron\nwhen: flag(took_pell)\ntext: You stay on at Pell & Daughters, at the counter by day and among the thousand drawers by night, counting drops while Old Pell counts aloud behind you. In ten years, if you're still careful, they'll show you the drawers that never open. You are still careful.\n\n@ending ending_courier\npriority: 52\ntitle: Over the Lake in All Weathers\nart: broom\nwhen: flag(took_courier)\ntext: You keep the satchel with your name on the flap and fly the lake in all weathers, letters and parcels and the occasional live delivery. The broom still pulls left. You don't. On winter nights the first-years watch your lamp crossing the ice and make wishes on it, as if you were a star.\n\n@ending ending_clerk\npriority: 45\ntitle: A Desk and a Stamp\nart: scroll\nwhen: flag(took_clerk)\ntext: You take the clerk's desk from the registrar's list: stamps, ledgers and a window that looks at a wall. It isn't a dream. It pays the rent, and in the evenings you are free, and one day, quietly, you notice that the wall has a crack in it shaped exactly like a door.\n\n@ending ending_still_searching\npriority: 10\ntitle: Still Looking\nart: map\nwhen: flag(took_none) and (gpa >= 2.5 or renown >= 30)\ntext: You graduate without a post, and on purpose, or near enough. Your grades will open doors and your name has been heard; the world, you've decided, can make you a better offer in its own time. You spend the autumn writing letters from a rented room above a bakery. Some of them get answers.\n\n@ending ending_back_home\npriority: 9\ntitle: Back in Your Old Room\nart: rain\nwhen: flag(took_none) and not (gpa >= 2.5 or renown >= 30)\ntext: You go home with a diploma and no letter. Your old room is smaller than you remember, and the bed is too short, and for a while you lie in it until noon and let the days rot quietly by. Then, one morning, you sit up, and write to the registrar about her list. It was still there, as she said it would be.\n"},{"path":"content/events/alignment.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Alignment (design §8.2): Lumen (+) ↔ Hollow (−).\n#  Thresholds ±30 and ±60 open these events; characters tagged\n#  `virtuous` or `ruthless` (content/world/cast.grim) react to\n#  where you stand. Deep in the Hollow the way back is slow\n#  (the resource's drag), so the way-back events cost real effort.\n#  Hollow Star plot scenes that read alignment live in\n#  chain_hollowstar.grim (hs_grey_invitation, hs_grey_tests,\n#  hs_wardens_notice).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Hollow (alignment <= -30) ----------------\n\n@event align_cold_candles\ntitle: The Candles Lean Away\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: hollow\nscene: A long stone corridor at dusk, every candle flame bending away from one student walking down the middle of it.\ntext: {You notice it on the east stair. As you pass, the candle flames bend away from you, all of them, like grass from a wind. A first-year on the landing notices too.|The candles lean away again. You have started to time it. It is getting faster.}\n> left: Let them lean\n  guile +small\n  alignment -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You walk on without looking. Behind you the first-year says nothing at all, which is its own kind of saying.\n> right: Stop and relight one by hand\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You cup a flame back to life with your palm. It flickers, considers you, and stays. It takes longer than it should.\n\n@pool alignment_routine\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 13\n\n@event align_whispered_answer\ntitle: A Voice Over Your Shoulder\npool: alignment_routine\nslot: night\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment <= -30 and term\nart: eye\ntags: hollow study\nscene: A student bent over a desk by candlelight, revision notes everywhere, and a cold patch of frost forming on the inkwell.\ntext: Revising late, you get stuck on the same question for the third time. Then the answer is simply there, in a voice that is almost yours, from just behind your left ear. The inkwell has frosted over.\n> left: Write it down\n  lore +small\n  alignment -small\n  say: It is right. Of course it is right. You write until dawn and never once get stuck, and in the morning you cannot remember learning any of it.\n> right: Close the book\n  mood -small\n  alignment +small\n  say: You close the book and sit with your hands flat on the cover until the frost on the inkwell melts. The question stays unanswered. It's yours, at least.\n\n@event align_maud_way_back\ntitle: Weeding, Dearie\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: maud\ntrigger: social\nwhen: alignment <= -30 and met(maud)\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 14\nart: leaf\ntags: hollow nature\nscene: An old woman in a straw hat kneeling in a vegetable bed, handing a trowel up to a student who hasn't asked for one.\ntext: Old Maud looks at you for a long moment over her spectacles, the way she looks at a plant that's been kept in the wrong window. \"You've a cold look about you, dearie,\" she says, and holds out a trowel. \"Weeds don't pull themselves. Neither does the rest.\"\n> left: Kneel and start pulling\n  health -small\n  alignment +medium\n  rel maud +small\n  say: You weed until your knees ache and your hands smell of earth instead of frost. Maud hums the whole time. Nothing's fixed. Something has started.\n> right: \"I'm fine.\"\n  rel maud -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Course you are,\" Maud says, and goes back to her beans. The trowel stays in the soil beside her, handle towards you, all afternoon.\n\n@event align_reproach\ntitle: \"I Don't Know You Lately\"\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  friend: any where tagged(friend, virtuous) and rel(friend) >= 6\nper: friend\ntrigger: social\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 12\nart: heartbreak\ntags: social hollow\nscene: Two friends on a cold bench in the cloister, one looking straight ahead, the other looking at the first.\ntext: {friend} finds you in the cloister and sits without saying hello. \"People talk,\" {they} say{s} at last. \"I keep telling them they're wrong about you. I'd like to keep being right.\"\n> left: \"You are. I'll do better.\"\n  alignment +small\n  mood -small\n  rel friend +small\n  say: {friend} nods, slowly, and bumps {their} shoulder against yours. It is an apology and a warning at once.\n> right: \"Mind your own business.\"\n  rel friend -medium\n  alignment -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {friend} gets up and goes. {They} don't look back. That's what stays with you: {they} always used to look back.\n\n@pool alignment_social_routine\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\ntags: social\n\n@event align_ruthless_nod\ntitle: Finally, Someone Who Gets It\npool: alignment_social_routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  other: any where tagged(other, ruthless)\nper: other\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nart: mask\ntags: hollow\nscene: A hard-faced student leaning on a pillar, giving another a slow, approving nod across a busy corridor.\ntext: {rel(other) < 10: {other} has always looked straight through you. Today {they} nod{s} as you pass|{other} catches your eye in the corridor and nod{s} again}, slow and approving, the way people nod at someone who has finally learned the rules of a game.\n> left: Nod back\n  rel other +medium\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The nod comes back to you. It feels like being let into a room. You can't tell whether the room is warm.\n> right: Keep walking\n  rel other -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You keep walking. {other} snorts, as if you'd failed a test you didn't know you were sitting.\n\n@event align_northern_parcel\ntitle: A Parcel for the North\npool: alignment_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nart: pouch\ntags: hollow money\nscene: A hooded courier in a rain-dark alley behind the village, holding out a small parcel wrapped in black oilcloth and a purse.\ntext: {A hooded courier stops you behind the bakery.|The hooded courier is waiting again, same alley, same black oilcloth.} \"For the boathouse, after dark,\" {they say, and hold out the parcel and a purse.|they say. \"The families remember who is reliable.\"} The oilcloth is cold enough to ache.\n> left: Carry it\n  gold +medium\n  alignment -medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: You leave it on the boathouse step at midnight and don't look back. In the morning there's frost on the step in the shape of a hand, and the purse is heavier than it looked.\n> right: Walk away\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You leave the courier holding it. \"Pity,\" they say, without any pity at all, and the alley is empty when you look back.\n\n@event align_cruel_advantage\ntitle: Something You Could Use\npool: alignment_routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  mark: any where tagged(mark, student) and rel(mark) < 12\nper: mark\nwhen: alignment <= -30\nart: scroll\ntags: social hollow\nscene: A crumpled letter on a library floor, a student's name on the envelope, another student bending to pick it up.\ntext: {mark} has dropped a letter in the library: something private, something {they} would hate the corridor to hear. You could hand it back. You could also keep it, and {they} would owe you, quietly, for a long time.\n> left: Keep it\n  guile +small\n  alignment -small\n  rel mark -small\n  say: You fold it into your book. When you mention it, very lightly, {mark} goes pale and agrees to everything. It works. It keeps working.\n> right: Hand it back\n  rel mark +small\n  mood -tiny\n  alignment +small\n  say: You hand it back unread. {mark} stares at you, then at the letter, then says \"thank you\" as if it costs something. It does, for both of you.\n\n@event align_frost_in_the_spell\ntitle: The Cold That Helps\npool: alignment_routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment <= -30 and term\nart: snow\ntags: hollow study\nscene: A student in an empty practice room, a spell flaring unnaturally bright in one hand, frost creeping up the sleeve to the elbow.\ntext: Practising alone, you notice that your spells come easier when you let the cold in, the cold that has been following you lately. It climbs your sleeve like ivy. The candle beside you leans away.\n> left: Let the cold in\n  arcana +small\n  alignment -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: The spell flares bright and clean, brighter than anything you've managed in daylight. It takes an hour for the feeling to come back into your fingers. You don't mind as much as you should.\n> right: Practise the slow way\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You shake the cold out of your hands and do it the slow way, badly, again and again. It is your own bad spell, at least.\n\n# ---------------- Lumen (alignment >= 30) ----------------\n\n@event align_asked_for_help\ntitle: \"You're the One People Ask\"\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: alignment >= 30\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: social lumen\nscene: A nervous first-year with an armful of torn notes hovering at the door of a common room, looking at one particular older student.\ntext: {~A first-year hovers by your table with a torn essay and a face like a wet weekend.|A first-year you've never met asks if you're the one who helps people. Apparently you are.|Someone has written your name on the common-room board under \"ask them\".} \"Somebody said you'd know what to do,\" {~they say, as if that settles it.|they say, and then wait.|they say, pointing back at the board.}\n> left: Sit them down and help\n  health -small\n  renown +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: It takes the whole evening and half your sleep. They leave lighter than they came. So, oddly, do you.\n> right: Point them to a tutor\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You give them a name and a smile and get back to your own work. They look disappointed for a moment, then grateful anyway.\n\n@event align_warm_regard\ntitle: A Good Word\npool: alignment_social_routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  friend: any where tagged(friend, virtuous) and rel(friend) >= 3\nper: friend\nwhen: alignment >= 30\nart: heart\ntags: lumen\nscene: Two students sharing a pot of tea in a sunny window seat, one pushing the last biscuit towards the other.\ntext: {friend} pushes the last biscuit across to you. \"People talk, you know. About the kind things you do when you think nobody's looking,\" {they} say{s}. \"Don't make a face. It's good. Someone should say so.\"\n> left: Accept the biscuit, and the praise\n  rel friend +small\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You eat the biscuit. {friend} looks very pleased. You buy the next pot.\n> right: Say anyone would have done the same\n  rel friend +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Anyone would,\" you say. \"Nobody did,\" says {friend}, and lets you change the subject, but the look {they} give{s} you says it has only been postponed.\n\n@event align_saint_jibe\ntitle: \"Saint\"\npool: alignment_social_routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  other: any where (tagged(other, ruthless) or npc_is(corvin)) and tagged(other, peer)\nper: other\nwhen: alignment >= 30\nart: scales\ntags: lumen\nscene: A smirking student blocking a doorway with one arm, another student standing in front of them with an armful of books.\ntext: {other} blocks the doorway with one arm. \"Here comes the saint,\" {they} say{s}, loud enough for the corridor. \"Tell me, does it get heavy, being better than everyone?\"\n> left: Let it go\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You wait. Eventually {other} gets bored and drops the arm. Two first-years behind you look at you like you've done something brave. You're not sure you have.\n> right: Give as good as you get\n  rel other -small\n  mood +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Your answer is quick and much funnier than theirs. The corridor laughs. {other} goes red. It feels wonderful for about an hour.\n"},{"path":"content/events/between_hearts.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Between people, hearts and tongues (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md, batch P4-02): matchmaking between two\n#  classmates, jealousy around you, and what people say about each other. Uses the pools of between.grim\n#  (`between`, `between_once`). A pair's match can't be carried whole (a queued card keeps one person), so the\n#  aftermaths are about the one you helped, and say no name for the other.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Matchmaking ----------------\n\n@event between_circling\ntitle: Circling\npool: between\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 6 and fancy_free(a)\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 4 and fancy_free(b) and get_along(a, b) and npc_rel(a, b) < 30\nper: a\nart: heart\nscene: Two students reaching for the same door handle at the same moment, both pulling back, a third watching from the stairs with a hand over their eyes.\ntext: {~{a} and {b} have sat next to each other at three lectures running, by accident, all three times on purpose.|{a} asks you, very casually, whether {b} is seeing anyone. Ten minutes later {b} asks you the same about {a}.|{a} and {b} keep arriving at the same doorway at the same moment, and then insisting the other goes first, for some time.} Everyone can see it except, apparently, the two of them.\n> left: Give them a push\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      npc_rel a b +medium\n      rel a +tiny\n      rel b +tiny\n      chance likely:\n        queue between_match_made in 3 with a\n      else:\n        queue between_match_soured in 3 with a\n      say: {~You make sure the only free seat at the next lecture is the one beside {b}, and that {a} knows it. They walk out together.|You tell {a} that {b} asked. You tell {b} that {a} asked. Both of these things are true. That evening they're on the same bench.|You hold the door yourself and wave them both through at once. They collide, apologise, and laugh, and keep laughing all the way down the corridor.}\n    lose:\n      npc_rel a b -tiny\n      rel a -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~Your hint lands like a brick. {a} goes scarlet and sits at the far end of the hall for a week.|{b} asks you, coolly, what exactly you meant by that. You don't have a good answer.|You arrange it all perfectly and both of them see straight through it. They're mortified, and a little annoyed with you, separately.}\n> right: Leave them to it\n  lore +small\n  rel a -tiny\n  say: {~You keep your nose in your book. A month on they're still sitting together by accident.|You say nothing. {a} looks at you as if hoping you would.|You leave them to it. They hold the door for each other for the rest of the term.}\n\n@event between_love_note\ntitle: Sealed With Wax\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 10 and fancy_free(a)\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 4 and fancy_free(b) and not at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\nwhen: seen(between_love_note) == 0\nart: letter\nscene: A student pressing a folded note sealed with a blob of candle wax into a friend's hand, looking over their shoulder.\ntext: {a} presses a folded note into your hand, sealed with candle wax and, by the look of it, a great deal of worry. \"For {b}. Don't read it. Don't let {b.them} open it in front of anyone. Don't say it's from me.\" You point out that {b} will know who it's from. {a} looks stricken.\n> left: Deliver it as asked\n  rel a +small\n  study -tiny\n  chance likely:\n    npc_rel a b +medium\n    queue between_match_made in 3 with a\n    say: You give up the evening you'd meant for your essay, find {b} alone in the library, and hand it over. {b} reads it twice, goes pink to the ears, and asks you, with great dignity, to tell {a} \"yes. Probably. Thursday.\"\n  else:\n    npc_rel a b -tiny\n    say: {b} reads it in the library and goes quiet. \"Tell {a} it's very kind,\" {b.they} say{b.s}, which is the worst thing anyone can say. You carry it back as gently as you can.\n> right: Help {a} write it better\n  study -tiny\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      npc_rel a b +medium\n      rel a +small\n      queue between_match_made in 3 with a\n      say: You sit up with {a} over four drafts, crossing out the poetry. What's left is three plain lines, the best thing {a} has ever written, and {b} answers them the same evening. You find out how it went at breakfast, from the way they arrive together.\n    lose:\n      rel a -small\n      say: By the fourth draft it's a sonnet, and a bad one, and {a} has gone off the whole idea. \"I'll just tell {b.them}. One day.\" The note stays in {their} pocket for the rest of the term. You can see the corner of it.\n\n@event between_find_out\ntitle: Asking for a Friend\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 10 and fancy_free(a)\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 6 and fancy_free(b) and not at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\nwhen: seen(between_find_out) == 0\nart: eye\nscene: A student whispering urgently to a friend in a library alcove, both of them looking across the room at a third student who hasn't noticed.\ntext: \"You know {b},\" says {a}, in a library whisper that carries for three shelves. \"Could you find out if {b.they} ever, you know. Thinks about me. At all. Without saying I asked.\" {a} adds, after a moment, \"Especially without saying I asked.\"\n> left: Ask {b} straight out\n  rel b -tiny\n  chance even:\n    npc_rel a b +small\n    rel a +small\n    say: {b} looks at you for a long moment, then laughs. \"Did {a} put you up to this? Tell {a} the answer's yes, and that {a} can ask me {a.them}self next time.\"\n  else:\n    rel a -tiny\n    say: {b} is kind about it, which makes it harder. \"I like {a}. Just not like that.\" You carry it back, and {a} says \"right, of course\", and talks about the weather for some time.\n> right: Find out sideways\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel a +small\n      npc_rel a b +tiny\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: Three casual questions, one about the Midwinter Ball, and you have your answer: {b} goes slightly pink at {a}'s name. You report back. {a} has to sit down.\n    lose:\n      rel b -small\n      rel a -tiny\n      say: You are not as subtle as you think. \"Is this about {a}?\" says {b}, and the whole table turns round to look.\n\n@event between_setup_supper\ntitle: The Long Supper\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 8 and fancy_free(a)\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 8 and fancy_free(b) and get_along(a, b)\nper: a\ntime: evening\nwhen: seen(between_setup_supper) == 0\nart: meal\nscene: Two students at a small refectory table being very polite over their soup, a third watching anxiously from across the hall.\ntext: It was your idea that {a} and {b} share the little table by the window at supper. Twenty minutes in they have covered the weather, the soup, and the weather again. {a} keeps looking at you across the hall like someone going under for the third time.\n> left: Go over and rescue it\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      npc_rel a b +small\n      rel a +tiny\n      rel b +tiny\n      say: You sit down for five minutes and tell the story about the goat and the Lumen Ledger. Somewhere in the middle they start finishing it for you. You leave, and neither of them notices.\n    lose:\n      npc_rel a b -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You sit down and the silence grows to fit three people. {b} remembers an essay. {a} thanks you for trying, in the voice of someone who wishes you hadn't.\n> right: Let it run its course\n  mood +tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    npc_rel a b +small\n    say: You eat your own supper in peace. By pudding they've found something, possibly cheese, to disagree about cheerfully. It goes on past the candles.\n  else:\n    npc_rel a b -tiny\n    say: You eat your own supper in peace. They part at the door with a handshake, which is the saddest thing you have seen all term.\n\n@event between_match_made\ntitle: Going Well\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nactor: npc\nart: flower\ntags: social between\nscene: A student grinning at a friend on a staircase, trying and failing to look casual about it.\ntext: {npc} finds you {~after class|on the stairs|at breakfast} with the look of someone who has news and has decided you're owed it first. It's going well, with the one you nudged {them} towards. Better than well. \"And it was you, so. Thank you. Don't tell anyone it was you.\"\n> left: Tell just a few people\n  renown +tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  flag matchmaker\n  say: You tell exactly two people. By the end of the week you are the castle's matchmaker, whether you like it or not, and {npc} has forgiven you, mostly.\n> right: \"It was all you.\"\n  rel npc +medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: \"It really wasn't,\" says {npc}, but {they} look{s} pleased anyway, and stand{s} a little taller for the rest of the month.\n\n@event between_match_soured\ntitle: Not Going Well\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nactor: npc\nart: heart\ntags: social between\nscene: A student with folded arms on a cold window seat, refusing to look at the friend who has come to sit beside them.\ntext: It's over, the thing you nudged {npc} into, and it ended badly at a dance nobody enjoyed. {npc} has decided, on reflection, that some of this is your fault.\n> left: Apologise\n  rel npc +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You say sorry for the push, and mean it. {npc} sighs. \"It wasn't the push. It was the part after.\" By the end of the evening {npc} is telling you about it, all of it, twice.\n> right: \"You were keen too.\"\n  rel npc -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: It's true, and {npc} knows it, and that doesn't help at all. {They} leave{s} you with the window seat. It comes back round in a week or two; it isn't quick.\n\n@event between_matchmaker\ntitle: Ask the Matchmaker\npool: between\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 0\nper: a\nwhen: flag(matchmaker)\nart: flower\nscene: A student sitting down opposite a friend at a library table with a folded list of names, looking hopeful.\ntext: {~{a} corners you after class. There's someone in the year below, and {a} has heard you're the person to ask.|There's a note under your door, from {a}: \"I hear you do introductions.\"|{a} sits down next to you at lunch with a list. It is a list of names. {a} would like your opinion on all of them.}\n> left: Take the case\n  rel a +small\n  renown +tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: {~You spend an evening on it, and arrange a chance meeting by the owlery that goes off almost by chance. {a} owes you a cake.|You write back: Thursday, the library, the second window. You'll never know if it worked, but {a} smiles at you in corridors now.|You go through the list, crossing out, with commentary. {a} laughs so much that the list ends up being beside the point.}\n> right: \"I'm retired.\"\n  mood +tiny\n  rel a -tiny\n  say: {~\"Retired,\" says {a}, unconvinced. \"From what, one success?\" You keep your evening.|You write \"retired\" under the note and slip it back. You have your evening to yourself, and you spend it, contentedly, on nobody's love life.|{a} folds the list away with great dignity. You eat your lunch in peace, and you are, you notice, a little sorry.}\n\n@event between_likes_your_suitor\ntitle: Of All People\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 12 and fancy_free(a)\n  b: any where suitor(b) and classmate_here(b) and not at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\nwhen: seen(between_likes_your_suitor) == 0\nart: heart\nscene: Two friends on a window seat, one confiding with a hand over their face, the other going very still.\ntext: {a} has something to confess, and takes a long time getting there. \"It's {b},\" {a} says at last. \"I think I like {b.them}. Is that stupid?\" It isn't stupid. It is complicated, because {b} has spent the last month looking for you in every room.\n> left: Tell {a} the truth\n  alignment +small\n  rel a -small\n  spark b +tiny\n  say: {a} laughs, not very happily. \"Of course. Of course it's you.\" You stay on the window seat until {a} can say \"thank you for telling me\" and mean it, which takes most of the evening.\n> right: Step back for {a}\n  spark b -medium\n  rel a +medium\n  npc_rel a b +small\n  say: You're a little cooler with {b} after that, and {b} notices, and drifts. {a} starts turning up wherever {b} is. You find you don't mind as much as you thought you would.\n\n# ---------------- Jealousy (romance only) ----------------\n\n@event between_partner_meets_ex\ntitle: The Same Fire\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where partner_here(a)\n  b: any where bond(b) == \"ex\" and around(b) and met(b)\nper: a\ntime: evening night\nwhen: seen(between_partner_meets_ex) == 0\nart: candle\nscene: A common room in the evening, two people on opposite sides of the fire turning at the same moment to look at a third coming through the door.\ntext: The common room has a fire, a kettle and, tonight, both {a} and {b}. Your partner and your ex. They have never properly met. By the look on {b}'s face, that is about to change.\n> left: Introduce them properly\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      npc_rel a b +small\n      spark a +tiny\n      say: You make the introductions as if they were the most ordinary thing in the world. By the second cup they've found common ground: you, and a shared low opinion of your singing.\n    lose:\n      spark a -tiny\n      rel b -tiny\n      say: It is very polite and extremely short. Later, walking back, {a} asks one careful question, and then another, and you answer them both.\n> right: Steer {a} away first\n  rel b -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You invent an urgent errand and {a} lets you, which is generous. {b} watches you both go. You feel it on the back of your neck all the way up the stairs.\n\n@event between_admirer_gift\ntitle: In Front of Everyone\npool: between\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where partner_here(a)\n  b: any where suitor(b) and classmate_here(b)\nper: a\nart: flower\nscene: A student holding out a small wrapped gift across a refectory table while the person beside its recipient raises an eyebrow.\ntext: {~{b} turns up with a little bunch of something from the greenhouses, for you, in front of {a}.|{b} has made you a bookmark, painstakingly, and gives it to you at lunch, while {a} is sitting right there.|{b} has brought you a pastry back from the village. One pastry. {a}'s eyebrows say a great deal.}\n> left: Thank {b} warmly\n  spark b +tiny\n  spark a -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You thank {b} and put the flowers in water. {a} is very interested in the window for the rest of the meal.|You use the bookmark that afternoon. {a} doesn't say anything about it, twice.|You eat the pastry. It is excellent. {a} watches you eat it, and the silence afterwards is long.}\n> right: Hand it back, kindly\n  spark b -small\n  rel b -tiny\n  spark a +tiny\n  say: {~\"They're lovely, but I can't.\" {b} takes them back, pink, and leaves quickly. {a} squeezes your hand under the table.|You give the bookmark back. {b} nods, too fast. {a} doesn't gloat, which you love {them} a little more for.|You push the pastry gently back across the table. {b} eats it {b.them}self, defiantly. {a} hides a smile.}\n\n@event between_suitors_compare\ntitle: Comparing Notes\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where suitor(a) and classmate_here(a)\n  b: any where suitor(b) and classmate_here(b)\nper: a\nwhen: seen(between_suitors_compare) == 0\nart: group\nscene: Two students sitting side by side on a staircase, heads together over a list, both looking up guiltily at a third coming round the corner.\ntext: You come round the corner and find {a} and {b}, your two most persistent admirers, sitting together on the stairs with their heads together. Comparing notes. About you. \"Oh,\" says {b}. \"We were just...\" \"Comparing,\" says {a}.\n> left: Sit down and join in\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      npc_rel a b +small\n      rel a +tiny\n      rel b +tiny\n      mood +small\n      say: You sit on the step below and dispute every point. By the end all three of you are laughing, they've agreed you're impossible, and nobody has to choose anything today.\n    lose:\n      spark a -tiny\n      spark b -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: You try to be funny about it and it comes out defensive. They exchange a look that you are not part of. That's new.\n> right: Walk on with dignity\n  npc_rel a b +small\n  spark a -tiny\n  spark b -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You step over them and carry on down the stairs. Behind you, the comparing resumes, louder, and someone says \"the hair, though\", and they both laugh.\n\n# ---------------- What people say ----------------\n\n@event between_secret_about\ntitle: Not Mine to Tell\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 10\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 8 and not at_odds(a, b) and not npc_is(nessa)\nper: a\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(between_secret_about) == 0\nart: eye\nscene: A student leaning close to whisper to a friend in a corridor, a third student visible at the far end, unaware.\ntext: \"You can't tell anyone,\" says {a}, and then tells you anyway: {b} failed last term's practical, and has been resitting it in secret every week, and is terrified of anyone finding out. {a} looks relieved to have put it down. Now you're holding it.\n> left: Tell {a} it wasn't {a.theirs} to tell\n  rel a -small\n  alignment +small\n  queue between_did_they_tell within 1..3 with b\n  say: {a} flushes. \"I only told you.\" You point out that this is what everyone says. {a} doesn't speak to you until supper, and is quieter about other people afterwards.\n> right: Keep it, and help {b} quietly\n  study -small\n  rel b +small\n  queue between_did_they_tell within 1..3 with b\n  say: You start turning up where {b} revises, with your notes, as if by accident. {b} doesn't ask why. The practical gets easier, for both of you.\n\n@event between_did_they_tell\ntitle: Somebody Told You\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nactor: npc\nart: mask\ntags: social between\nscene: A student sitting down across a library table from a friend, hands flat on the wood, clearly having rehearsed something.\ntext: {npc} sits down across from you with the face of someone who has rehearsed this. \"Somebody told you. About the practical. Didn't they?\"\n> left: Tell the truth\n  rel npc +tiny\n  alignment +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {npc} nods slowly. \"I thought so. Thank you for not pretending.\" {npc} doesn't ask who. You suspect {they} know{s}.\n> right: \"Nobody told me anything.\"\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel npc +small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {npc} lets out a breath held for a month. \"Good. Good. Sorry. I've been seeing it in everyone's face.\" You change the subject, and {they} let{s} you.\n    lose:\n      rel npc -small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {npc} doesn't believe a word, and now there are two people {they} can't quite trust. {They} pick{s} up {their} books and leave{s}.\n\n@event between_rumour_from_you\ntitle: How It Grew\npool: between\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 4\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 4 and not at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\nwhen: seen(between_rumour_from_you) < 2\nart: feather\nscene: A cluster of students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, two others at opposite ends of it pointedly not looking at each other.\ntext: {~You mentioned, once, in passing, that {a} had lent {b} a scarf. By supper they're engaged.|You said {a} and {b} were revising together. By morning it's a secret romance, with details.|You told one person that {a} walked {b} back from the library. The story has since acquired a moonlit lake.} Both of them have heard it. Both of them know where it started.\n> left: Set the record straight\n  renown -tiny\n  rel a +tiny\n  rel b +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You spend a day explaining that a scarf is only a scarf. Half the castle is disappointed in you. {a} and {b} are not.|You correct it at every table, which makes it sound truer. {b} thanks you anyway.|You say it was a library, and a corridor, and no lake. People believe you, sadly. {a} buys you a cider.}\n> right: Let it run\n  rel a -small\n  rel b -tiny\n  npc_rel a b +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~It's quite funny, the first week. {a} doesn't find it funny at all. {b} starts wearing the scarf.|The details get better. {a} stops sitting with you. {b} seems, oddly, not to mind.|By the end of the month nobody can remember whether it was true, including, it seems, {a} and {b}.}\n\n@event between_overheard\ntitle: Behind Their Back\npool: between\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a)\n  b: any where tagged(b, peer) and met(b) and rel(b) >= 12 and not get_along(a, b)\nper: a\nwhen: seen(between_overheard) < 2\nart: mask\nscene: A student holding court at a refectory table, mid-impression, the others laughing, a fourth student behind them stopping still with a tray.\ntext: {~Behind the library shelves, {a} is telling a little group exactly what {a.they} think{a.s} of {b}. It isn't kind, and it isn't true.|On the stairs, {a} is doing an impression of {b}. It's a good impression. People are laughing.|{a} is holding court at the refectory table: {b}'s spells, {b}'s family, {b}'s hair.} {b} is a friend of yours, and isn't here.\n> left: Speak up\n  rel a -small\n  rel b +small\n  npc_rel a b -tiny\n  alignment +small\n  say: {~\"That isn't true, and you know it.\" The group goes quiet. {a} looks at you as if you've changed sides in a war you didn't know about. Word gets back to {b}, eventually.|\"Do me next,\" you say. Nobody laughs. {a} stops. It gets back to {b} by the end of the week.|You put your tray down at the table and say {b}'s name kindly, once, and leave it there. {a} finds someone else to talk about.}\n> right: Say nothing\n  mood -small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {~You keep walking. The laughing follows you down the aisle. You sit with {b} at supper and don't mention it, and it sits between you.|You laugh, a little, because everyone is. You think about it for the rest of the day.|You eat your lunch somewhere else. It doesn't help as much as you'd like.}\n\n@event between_betting_book\ntitle: The Book on Them\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 6 and fancy_free(a)\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 6 and fancy_free(b) and get_along(a, b) and npc_rel(a, b) < 40\nper: a\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(between_betting_book) == 0\nart: coin\nscene: A grubby ledger open on a common-room table, students crowding round to write names and coins in its columns.\ntext: There's a book running in the common room, and the odds are on {a} and {b}: together by the end of term, three to one. Somebody you don't much like is taking the money. Everyone is very interested in what you know.\n> left: Put a crown on them\n  gold -small\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance even:\n    gold +medium\n    say: You put your crown on \"together\". Three weeks later they are, and you collect. You never tell either of them, and you feel slightly odd every time they hold hands.\n  else:\n    say: You put your crown on \"together\", and they aren't, quite, and the bookmaker smiles at you every morning for a month.\n> right: Shut the book down\n  rel a +small\n  rel b +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You take the ledger and drop it in the fire, which is not a popular move. The bookmaker sulks. {a} and {b} never find out about it, or if they do they never say.\n\n@event between_couple_bubble\ntitle: Party of Two\npool: between\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 10\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 10 and npc_rel(a, b) >= 25\nper: a\nart: heart\nscene: Two students sharing one cushion on a window seat, a third sitting slightly apart with a sandwich.\ntext: {~{a} and {b} have become inseparable, and now they finish each other's sentences, and you finish your supper.|You've been the third chair at {a} and {b}'s table for a month now. Tonight they forgot to pull it out.|{a} and {b} save you a place on the window seat. When you get there, it's a seat for two and a cushion for you.}\n> left: Say something\n  rel a +tiny\n  rel b +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~\"I'm still here, you know.\" They look at you, horrified, and then they laugh, and the next evening there are three cups.|It comes out more plaintive than you meant. {b} hugs you. {a} saves your chair for a week, pointedly.|You mention the cushion. {a} apologises for some time. It's awkward, and then it's better.}\n> right: Give them room\n  study +tiny\n  mood -small\n  npc_rel a b +tiny\n  say: {~You eat early and read late. You've never got so much done. You miss them.|You leave them to it. Your essays improve. Your evenings don't.|You find other company for a while. They come back round, eventually, the way people do.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/between_known_2.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Between people, one of them someone you know well, part 2 (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md, batch P4-04):\n#  Seraphine, Tobias, and three of the staff (Maud, Thorne, the Headmistress) with a classmate. Each card turns on\n#  its character's own voice or surface facts. Uses the pools of between.grim (`between`, `between_once`).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Seraphine ----------------\n\n@event seraphine_between_warning\ntitle: Not the North Stair\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  seraphine: seraphine where met(seraphine) and around(seraphine)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4\nper: seraphine\nwhen: seen(seraphine_between_warning) == 0\nart: eye\nscene: A dreamy student touching another's sleeve on a staircase landing, saying something quiet, while the other laughs.\ntext: Seraphine stops {o} on the landing, puts a hand on {o.their} sleeve, and says, quite exactly, \"Not the north stair tomorrow. The south one. Before the bell.\" Then she drifts off. {o} laughs, and then stops laughing, and asks you, \"She's joking. Isn't she?\"\n> left: \"Take the south stair.\"\n  rel seraphine +small\n  npc_rel seraphine o +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {o} takes the south stair, feeling silly. Nothing happens on the north stair, as far as anyone can tell. But that evening {o} sits beside Seraphine at supper, and she finishes {o.their} sentence for {o.them}, and {o} doesn't mind.\n> right: \"Nobody knows what she means.\"\n  rel o +small\n  rel seraphine -tiny\n  say: {o} takes the north stair out of principle, and meets a prefect with a notebook coming the other way, and a demerit for running. Seraphine looks faintly apologetic, a day early.\n\n@event seraphine_between_sentences\ntitle: The Other Half\npool: between\ntone: light\ncast:\n  seraphine: seraphine where met(seraphine) and around(seraphine)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 6 and not at_odds(seraphine, o)\nper: seraphine\nart: star\nscene: Two students side by side on a window seat, one talking, the other gazing out of the window and quietly saying the end of the sentence a moment before it comes.\ntext: {~{o} has started a sentence three times today, and Seraphine has finished it three times, correctly, half a second early.|Seraphine answered a question {o} hadn't asked yet. {o} asked it anyway, to be sure. It was the right answer.|{o} told a joke at supper, and Seraphine laughed at the punchline before {o} got to it.} \"It's not that I mind,\" {o} tells you. \"It's that I can't tell if I'm still talking to her, or to next week.\"\n> left: Tell {o} it's how she listens\n  npc_rel seraphine o +small\n  rel o +small\n  say: {~\"She only does it to people she likes,\" you say, which you aren't sure is true, and then find is. {o} starts leaving gaps in sentences, on purpose. Seraphine fills them, delighted.|\"It means she's already enjoying the conversation,\" you say. {o} thinks about that for a whole afternoon and decides it's a compliment.|\"Let her,\" you say. \"She's usually right about the ending.\" {o} lets her. They get on, after that, in a way that skips the middle.}\n> right: Ask Seraphine to stay in today\n  rel seraphine +small\n  rel o -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You tell Seraphine, gently, that {o} likes to say {o.their} own endings. \"Oh,\" she says, \"{o} will. On Thursday.\" She says nothing else all evening, and looks pleased about it.|Seraphine listens to you, nods, and then, very carefully, lets {o} finish every sentence for a week. It's somehow more unnerving.|\"Today,\" Seraphine repeats, as if it were a foreign word. \"I'll try.\" She manages most of a lunch.}\n\n# ---------------- Tobias ----------------\n\n@event tobias_between_sayings\ntitle: Where I'm From\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  tobias: tobias where met(tobias) and around(tobias)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 0 and not get_along(tobias, o)\nper: tobias\nwhen: seen(tobias_between_sayings) == 0\nart: leaf\nscene: A refectory table, a big broad-shouldered student laughing along a little too hard while a classmate across from him does an impression with a pretend pitchfork.\ntext: Tobias said, at supper, that the soup \"wanted more rain on it\", which is what his mother says. {o} has been saying it all week, in a farmer's voice, with a pretend pitchfork. Tobias laughs from the belly every time. He's the only one who doesn't look like he's laughing.\n> left: Tell {o} to drop it\n  rel tobias +small\n  rel o -small\n  npc_rel tobias o -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: \"That's his mum you're doing,\" you say. {o} goes red and stops. Tobias tells you, later, that he didn't mind, really, and apologises for you having to say anything, and then thanks you three times.\n> right: Ask Tobias for the rest of the sayings\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      npc_rel tobias o +small\n      rel tobias +small\n      say: You ask Tobias what else his mother says. He tells you, for an hour, with goats. By the end, {o} is laughing with him instead of at him, and asking what \"a frost in the cream\" means.\n    lose:\n      rel tobias -tiny\n      say: Tobias tries, and {o} does the voice again, and the table laughs again. Tobias laughs too. He eats the rest of his supper very fast and goes to the greenhouses.\n\n@event tobias_between_supper\ntitle: Proper Food\npool: between\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  tobias: tobias where met(tobias) and around(tobias) and rel(tobias) >= 6\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4 and not at_odds(tobias, o)\nper: tobias\ntime: evening\nart: meal\nscene: A greenhouse corner at night with a little stove, a big student stirring a pot, a homesick classmate wrapped in a blanket holding a bowl.\ntext: {~{o} has been homesick all week and not saying so. Tobias noticed. He has borrowed a stove and is cooking, in the corner of the greenhouses, \"proper food\".|Tobias heard {o} say the refectory's bread tastes of nothing. There is now a loaf proving under a cloche in the greenhouse, and an invitation.|{o} missed a letter from home this month. Tobias is making a stew \"big enough for a barn\", and wants you to come and help eat it.} He asks if you'll come.\n> left: Bring something to the table\n  gold -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  rel o +small\n  npc_rel tobias o +small\n  say: {~You bring cheese from the village. Tobias treats it like a holy relic. {o} eats three bowls and tells you about home, all of it, and it's a good evening.|You bring a jar of honey. Tobias's bread, it turns out, was waiting for exactly that. {o} laughs for the first time all week.|You bring the cider. Tobias tells the story of the goat that ate a hat. {o} tells one back. It's past midnight when you leave the greenhouse.}\n> right: Leave them to it\n  study +tiny\n  npc_rel tobias o +small\n  say: {~You plead an essay. In the morning {o} looks better than you've seen {o.them} in weeks, and there's a heel of bread wrapped in a cloth on your desk.|You stay in. You can smell the stew from the dormitory. Tobias saves you a bowl, and apologises that it's cold.|You leave them to it. Somebody plays a fiddle badly in the greenhouses until late, and somebody else sings along.}\n\n@event tobias_between_sulking\ntitle: The Ferns Are Sulking\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  tobias: tobias where met(tobias) and around(tobias)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4 and not at_odds(tobias, o)\nper: tobias\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(tobias_between_sulking) == 0\nart: leaf\nscene: A greenhouse bench of ferns all leaning pointedly away from one embarrassed student, a big student crouching to talk to them.\ntext: {o} knocked over a tray of seedlings in the greenhouse last week, and swore at them. Now every plant on {o}'s bench leans away from {o.them}, and the ferns curl up when {o} comes in. Tobias, very gravely, says they're sulking. \"They'll come round. But somebody's got to say sorry, and it'd better be {o}.\"\n> left: Help {o} say sorry to the ferns\n  mood -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  rel o +small\n  npc_rel tobias o +small\n  say: {o} kneels by the bench, mortified, while you hold the watering can and Tobias coaches in a whisper: \"Lower. They like it lower.\" By the end of the lesson one fern has uncurled. Tobias claps {o} on the back hard enough to rattle the glass.\n> right: Tell {o} it's only plants\n  rel o +small\n  rel tobias -small\n  say: {o} laughs, relieved, and goes back to the bench. The ferns go on leaning away from {o} for the rest of term, and {o} quietly moves benches in the spring. Tobias doesn't say anything, which, for Tobias, is saying a great deal.\n\n# ---------------- Old Maud ----------------\n\n@event maud_between_soup\ntitle: Carry This, Dearie\npool: between_once\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  maud: maud where met(maud) and around(maud)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4\nper: maud\nwhen: seen(maud_between_soup) == 0\nart: cup\nscene: An old woman in a garden apron handing a covered pot to a student at her cottage door, the lamp lit behind her.\ntext: Old Maud catches you on the path with a covered pot. \"{o}'s abed with a chill, dearie, and too proud to say so. Take this up. Hot, mind. And sit with {o.them} till it's eaten. That's the important bit, the sitting. Soup's just the excuse.\"\n> left: Carry it, and sit with {o}\n  study -small\n  rel o +medium\n  rel maud +small\n  say: You carry the pot up three flights without spilling, and sit on the end of {o}'s bed while {o} eats it. {o} talks, eventually, about being ill and far from home. The soup does what Maud's soup does. So, you think, does the sitting.\n> right: Leave it at {o}'s door\n  rel o +tiny\n  rel maud -tiny\n  say: You knock, leave the pot on the step, and hurry off to your essay. The pot comes back to Maud empty. Maud looks at you over her spectacles. \"Soup's only half of it, dearie,\" she says, and you understand what she meant a fortnight later.\n\n@event maud_between_beans\ntitle: The Bean Rows\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  maud: maud where met(maud) and around(maud)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4\nper: maud\nwhen: seen(maud_between_beans) == 0\nart: leaf\nscene: A trampled row of runner beans beside a cottage garden, a goat looking innocent, an old woman with her hands on her hips.\ntext: Someone cut through Maud's garden in the dark and took out a whole row of runner beans. You know it was {o}, chasing a runaway hat. {o} knows you know. Maud is standing at the end of the row with her hands on her hips, saying, to nobody in particular, \"Only, I'd like to know, that's all.\"\n> left: Get {o} to own up\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel maud +small\n      npc_rel maud o +small\n      rel o -tiny\n      say: {o} goes and owns up, scarlet. Maud listens, nods, and hands {o} a trowel. By evening the beans are re-staked and {o} has been given soup, a lecture on beans, and a standing invitation to Sunday weeding.\n    lose:\n      rel o -small\n      say: {o} won't. \"She'll tell the headmistress.\" She wouldn't, you know she wouldn't, but {o} won't hear it. Maud re-stakes the beans herself, slowly, and you feel every pole.\n> right: Re-stake them yourself\n  vigor +tiny\n  study -tiny\n  rel maud +small\n  rel o +small\n  say: You come back at dawn and re-stake the row before Maud's up. She finds you at it. \"Funny,\" she says, \"how a person's bean rows mend themselves.\" She doesn't ask. She gives you a jar of honey, and another one \"for your friend\".\n\n@event maud_between_advice\ntitle: Two Weeks Later\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  maud: maud where met(maud) and around(maud)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 6\nper: maud\nwhen: seen(maud_between_advice) == 0\nart: flower\nscene: Two students on a garden bench puzzling over something, an old woman with a watering can walking away from them, smiling to herself.\ntext: {o} went to Old Maud about a friend who'd stopped speaking to {o.them}, and came back with this: \"Water the roots, dearie, not the leaves.\" {o} has been turning it over for days. \"Is it about plants? It's not about plants. Is it?\"\n> left: Puzzle it out together\n  lore +small\n  rel o +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You sit up half the night on it. Somewhere around midnight you both get it at once: stop arguing about the thing, and go and see the person. {o} does, the next day. It works. Maud, told, says \"did I say that, dearie?\", which you choose to take as modesty.\n> right: \"Give it two weeks.\"\n  rel o +tiny\n  npc_rel maud o +tiny\n  say: \"Maud's advice takes two weeks,\" you say. {o} rolls {o.their} eyes. A fortnight later {o} stops you in a corridor, shining: \"The roots! It was the roots!\" You still don't know what {o} worked out. You decide not to ask.\n\n# ---------------- Professor Thorne ----------------\n\n@event thorne_between_hard\ntitle: Hm.\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  thorne: thorne where met(thorne) and around(thorne)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 6\nper: thorne\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(thorne_between_hard) == 0\nart: scroll\nscene: A severe old professor handing back an essay covered in red ink, a student staring at it, a third student at the next desk.\ntext: Thorne has handed {o}'s essay back for the third week running, more red ink than black. \"Hm,\" he said, which is all he said. {o} is close to tears in the corridor. \"He hates me. He doesn't do this to anyone else. Will you talk to him? He listens to you.\"\n> left: Talk to Thorne\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      rel thorne +tiny\n      npc_rel thorne o +small\n      rel o +small\n      say: Thorne hears you out, unmoved, and then says, \"The student in question is the only one in that class worth correcting.\" He doesn't change a word of the red ink. But the next essay comes back with one line at the bottom, in black: \"Better.\" {o} frames it.\n    lose:\n      rel thorne -tiny\n      say: \"Flattery on another's behalf is still flattery,\" says Thorne, and turns back to his marking. You leave, having made it, possibly, slightly worse.\n> right: Read the red ink with {o}\n  study -tiny\n  rel o +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You go through it line by line with {o}. Half of it, you point out, is Thorne arguing with {o}'s ideas, which he only does with ideas he thinks are worth it. {o} looks at the essay differently after that. So, for a moment, do you.\n\n@event thorne_between_flattery\ntitle: Allergic\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  thorne: thorne where met(thorne) and around(thorne)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4\nper: thorne\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(thorne_between_flattery) == 0\nart: hourglass\nscene: A student bowing slightly too low to a severe old professor in a corridor, holding out a single rose, while a friend behind them winces.\ntext: {o} has a plan for the next practical mark: to be nice to Professor Thorne. Very nice. There's a speech about his \"legendary career\", and a rose. {o} is heading for his office now. You know, as everyone who has met Thorne knows, that he is allergic to flattery, possibly literally.\n> left: Head {o} off\n  rel o +small\n  say: You catch {o} on the stair and explain, quickly, about Thorne. {o} goes pale and gives you the rose. You keep it on your desk. It's a perfectly nice rose; it's just in no danger of reaching Thorne.\n> right: Let {o} find out\n  mood +small\n  rel o -small\n  npc_rel thorne o -tiny\n  say: You hear about it at supper, from four people. Thorne listened to the speech in silence, took the rose, and set {o} an additional essay on \"the uses of sincerity\". {o} doesn't speak to you for a day, and then laughs about it for a month.\n\n# ---------------- The Headmistress ----------------\n\n@event isolde_between_remembers\ntitle: The Second Week\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  isolde: isolde where met(isolde) and around(isolde)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4\nper: isolde\nwhen: seen(isolde_between_remembers) == 0\nart: eye\nscene: A tall pale headmistress pausing in the Great Hall beside a student, smiling pleasantly, while the student turns slowly crimson.\ntext: The Headmistress stops beside {o} in the Great Hall, pleasant and unhurried. \"{o}. How is the potion that turned the stairs blue? The second week, I believe. Nobody's ever managed that shade since.\" She moves on. {o} wants the floor to swallow {o.them}. \"She remembers. She remembers everything.\"\n> left: \"She remembered your name.\"\n  rel o +small\n  npc_rel isolde o +tiny\n  say: \"She remembered your name, and the shade of blue,\" you say. \"That's a compliment, from her.\" {o} doesn't believe you. But {o} walks a little taller past the Headmistress's tower for the rest of the term.\n> right: Confess your own first week\n  mood -tiny\n  rel o +medium\n  say: You confess your own first-week disaster, and that you live in fear of the day she mentions it. {o} laughs until {o} has to sit down. You feel it was worth it, nearly.\n\n@event isolde_between_complaint\ntitle: An Appointment\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  isolde: isolde where met(isolde) and around(isolde)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 8\nper: isolde\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(isolde_between_complaint) == 0\nart: tower\nscene: The door of a tall tower office ajar, a student hesitating on the threshold with a folded letter, a friend at their shoulder.\ntext: {o} has written a complaint about a lecturer who marks unfairly, and has an appointment with the Headmistress to deliver it in person. {o} has rewritten it eleven times. \"Come with me,\" {o} says. \"Just stand there. I can't do it alone.\"\n> left: Go in with {o}\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      rel o +medium\n      npc_rel isolde o +small\n      say: The Headmistress reads the letter slowly, asks {o} two questions, each placed like a chess piece, and then says, \"Thank you. That was not easy.\" The marks are reviewed by the end of the month. {o} doesn't stop shaking until supper.\n    lose:\n      rel o +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: The Headmistress listens pleasantly and says she will \"look into it\". {o} can't tell, afterwards, whether that means yes or no. Neither can you. Nothing seems to change for a month, and then, quietly, something does.\n> right: Help {o} rewrite it once more\n  study -tiny\n  rel o +small\n  say: You sit with {o} and cut the letter down to six lines, all of them true. {o} goes in alone, in the end, and comes out ten minutes later, astonished. \"She read every line. Then she asked about the third one.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/between_known.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Between people, one of them someone you know well (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md, batch P4-03): one named\n#  character and one classmate, and how that character treats them. Each card turns on its character's own voice\n#  or facts, so it couldn't be dealt to anyone else. Uses the pools of between.grim (`between`, `between_once`).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Lyra ----------------\n\n@event lyra_between_drills\ntitle: Again\npool: between\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  lyra: lyra where met(lyra) and around(lyra)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 6\nper: lyra\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nart: flame\nscene: An empty duelling floor at dusk, one student lying flat on their back, another standing over them with a wand, saying something short.\ntext: {~{o} asked Lyra to help {o.them} with {o.their} duelling. Lyra said yes. That was a week ago. {o} now flinches at the word \"again\".|{o} limps into supper with singed cuffs and the look of someone who has been told \"again\" two hundred times.|You find {o} asleep on the library steps, wand still in hand. \"Lyra,\" {o} says, without opening {o.their} eyes, as if that explains it.} \"She won't stop,\" {o} tells you. \"And I can't ask her to. You know what she's like.\"\n> left: Talk to Lyra\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      npc_rel lyra o +small\n      rel o +small\n      rel lyra -tiny\n      say: {~\"Too hard?\" Lyra looks genuinely baffled. \"I'm not being hard. I'm being thorough.\" The next morning there's a flask of tea by the duelling ring, and nobody says who brought it.|Lyra listens with her arms folded. \"Fine,\" she says. That afternoon she lets {o} rest for a full minute, and then, without being asked, shows {o.them} how to fall.|\"{o} said that?\" Lyra is silent for a while. \"{o} should have said so.\" She doesn't apologise. She does turn up with new cuffs.}\n    lose:\n      rel lyra -small\n      say: {~\"If {o} wanted it gentle, {o} should have asked someone gentle.\" Lyra turns back to the ring. \"Again.\"|Lyra hears you out and says, \"No.\" Then, after a moment: \"Tell {o} the footwork is improving.\" You're not sure that helps.|\"Do I tell you how to study?\" says Lyra, who tells you how to study weekly. The drills continue.}\n> right: Tell {o} to stick with it\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  rel o -tiny\n  npc_rel lyra o +tiny\n  say: {~{o} sticks with it. A month later {o} disarms a third-year in practice and looks so astonished that Lyra almost smiles.|{o} groans, and goes back. Lyra doesn't say \"well done\". She says \"acceptable\", which, you explain to {o}, is the same thing.|{o} goes back to the ring with the face of someone going to the gallows. It does get easier. It never gets easy.}\n\n@event lyra_between_furniture\ntitle: You Sit Next to Her\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  lyra: lyra where met(lyra) and around(lyra) and rel(lyra) >= 8\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4 and npc_rel(lyra, o) < 10\nper: lyra\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(lyra_between_furniture) == 0\nart: book\nscene: A lecture bench, a proud student looking straight ahead, the classmate beside her waving a hand slowly in front of her face.\ntext: {o} has sat next to Lyra in lectures since the first week. Today Lyra turns to you, across {o}, and asks, \"Who is this?\" {o} is right there. {o} has lent her ink. Twice.\n> left: Introduce them properly\n  npc_rel lyra o +small\n  rel o +small\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: \"This is {o}, who sits next to you, and lends you ink.\" Lyra looks at {o} as if {o} had just appeared out of the air, then nods, very formally. The next morning, there's a new bottle of ink on {o}'s desk. Expensive ink.\n> right: Laugh it off\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  rel o -small\n  say: You laugh, and Lyra doesn't see the joke, and {o} laughs too, a beat late. {o} moves two seats along the next week. Lyra doesn't notice that either.\n\n@event lyra_between_beaten\ntitle: How Did They Do It\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  lyra: lyra where met(lyra) and around(lyra) and rel(lyra) >= 10\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4\nper: lyra\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(lyra_between_beaten) == 0\nart: duel\nscene: A practice ring after a bout, a winner being clapped on the back, a proud student standing alone at the edge, very still.\ntext: {o} beat Lyra in practice today. Only a practice bout, only a point, and the whole hall saw. Lyra finds you afterwards. She doesn't say please; she has never said please first. \"You were watching. How did {o} do it?\"\n> left: Tell her what you saw\n  rel lyra +small\n  rel o -tiny\n  npc_rel lyra o -tiny\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You walk her through it: {o}'s feint, the half-step back, the moment she overreached. Lyra listens without blinking. \"Again,\" she says to herself, and goes to the ring, and doesn't come to supper.\n> right: \"Ask {o}.\"\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      npc_rel lyra o +medium\n      rel lyra +tiny\n      say: Lyra looks at you as if you've suggested she eat soup with a fork. Then she goes and asks. You watch {o}, startled, show her the feint, and Lyra, stiffly, shows {o} something back.\n    lose:\n      rel lyra -small\n      say: \"I don't ask,\" Lyra says, and leaves. She doesn't come to supper, and the next morning the practice dummies have scorch marks that weren't there before.\n\n# ---------------- Kael ----------------\n\n@event kael_between_kindness\ntitle: He Keeps Leaving\npool: between\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  kael: kael where met(kael) and around(kael) and rel(kael) >= 8\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 6 and not at_odds(kael, o)\nper: kael\nart: snow\nscene: A student holding out a mug of something hot towards a boy with frost on his sleeves, who is already half turned away.\ntext: {~{o} has been trying to make friends with Kael for weeks. Every time {o} sits down, Kael finds somewhere else to be.|{o} saved Kael a seat at supper. Kael looked at it as if it might be a trap, and ate standing up.|{o} gave Kael a scarf. Kael said \"why?\" and meant it, and {o} didn't know what to say.} \"What am I doing wrong?\" {o} asks you.\n> left: Tell {o} to give him time\n  rel o +tiny\n  npc_rel kael o +small\n  say: {~\"He isn't used to it,\" you say. \"Keep sitting down.\" {o} does. In the third week, Kael stays.|\"Kindness makes him nervous,\" you say. {o} looks at you oddly, and then keeps being kind, quietly, until it doesn't.|\"Don't ask why,\" you say. \"Just do it again.\" The scarf turns up round Kael's neck a month later. Nobody mentions it.}\n> right: Bring them both to the boathouse\n  rel kael +small\n  rel o +small\n  npc_rel kael o +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You bring {o} down to the boathouse, where Kael goes in the evenings. Nobody talks much. {o} learns that this is how Kael says yes.|You take the three of you down to the water. Kael shows {o} how to skip a stone, without a word. That is apparently a friendship now.|At the boathouse, Kael is all elbows and silence for an hour. Then he tells {o} the name for snow that falls upward. It is the most you've ever heard him say.}\n\n@event kael_between_steps_in\ntitle: Frost on the Floor\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  kael: kael where met(kael) and around(kael)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4\nper: kael\nwhen: seen(kael_between_steps_in) == 0\nart: snow\nscene: A corridor with frost feathering up the walls around one still student, a group of others backing away from a cornered classmate.\ntext: Some older students have {o} pinned against the corridor wall over something about a borrowed book, and it is turning ugly. Before you can move, the air goes cold enough to sting. Kael is standing at the end of the corridor, very still, and frost is feathering up the walls around him.\n> left: Back him up\n  rel kael +small\n  rel o +small\n  npc_rel kael o +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You step up beside him. The older students look at the walls, at Kael's face, at you, and decide the book wasn't worth it. Kael walks {o} to the infirmary without a word. Later, he asks you, very seriously, whether he did it right.\n> right: Fetch a prefect instead\n  rel o +tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel kael -tiny\n  say: You run for a prefect. By the time you're back the corridor is empty and the walls are dripping, and Kael is gone. {o} tells you he didn't do anything; he just stood there until they left. He doesn't speak of it. He avoids you, a little, for a few days.\n\n# ---------------- Bram ----------------\n\n@event bram_between_writes_up\ntitle: Name in the Ledger\npool: between\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  bram: bram where met(bram) and around(bram)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 10\nper: bram\nart: scroll\nscene: A prefect with a red sash writing in a ledger while a student argues, a third student hurrying over.\ntext: {~Bram has {o} by the sleeve for \"loitering with intent\", which turns out to mean waiting for you.|Bram is writing {o} up for \"an insolent hat\". {o}'s hat is a perfectly ordinary hat.|Bram has caught {o} running on the stairs, and is reading out the rule number with relish.} {o} looks at you over Bram's shoulder: help.\n> left: Plead {o}'s case\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel o +small\n      rel bram -tiny\n      say: {~\"Rule fourteen is about loitering with intent,\" you say. \"{o} had no intent. Look at {o.them}.\" Bram looks. He crosses it out, with ill grace.|You argue that a hat cannot be insolent. Bram argues that it can. You win on a technicality he invented himself last term. He hates that.|You remind Bram that he ran on the same stairs on Tuesday. He goes red, says \"prefect's business\", and lets {o} go.}\n    lose:\n      rel bram -small\n      rel o +tiny\n      say: {~\"And a demerit for you, for obstruction.\" Bram writes it with a flourish. {o} mouths \"sorry\" at you.|Bram writes up {o}, then you, then {o} again for laughing. It's a productive afternoon for the ledger.|\"Noted,\" says Bram, and writes up {o} anyway, and adds a little note in the margin about you.}\n> right: Take the demerit for {o}\n  mood -small\n  rel o +medium\n  rel bram +tiny\n  say: {~\"Write me up instead. It was my fault.\" Bram looks at you for a long moment and writes your name. Afterwards, gruffly, he says, \"That was decent.\" Then he walks off before you can answer.|You say {o} was waiting for you, so it's your demerit. Bram, baffled, agrees. {o} buys you a cake.|You take it. Bram writes it slowly. You catch him, much later, crossing it out when he thinks nobody's looking.}\n\n@event bram_between_prank\ntitle: The Sash\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  bram: bram where met(bram) and around(bram)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 6 and at_odds(bram, o)\nper: bram\nwhen: seen(bram_between_prank) == 0\nart: mask\nscene: Two students crouched behind a pillar with a pot of dye, a prefect's red sash hanging on a peg nearby.\ntext: {o} has a pot of dye, a plan and a grudge. Bram's prefect sash hangs on its peg outside the bathhouse every evening at eight. \"Pink,\" says {o}, glowing. \"Just for one day. Are you in?\"\n> left: Be the lookout\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel o +small\n      rel bram -small\n      renown +tiny\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: Bram wears a pink sash to breakfast before he notices. The refectory goes very quiet, and then very loud. He knows it was {o}. He suspects you. He never proves it.\n    lose:\n      rel bram -small\n      rel o -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: Bram comes out of the bathhouse early, and finds you standing very innocently next to his sash and a pot of pink dye. The demerits take him most of a page.\n> right: Talk {o} out of it\n  rel o -small\n  rel bram +tiny\n  say: \"It's his whole world, that sash,\" you say. \"It wouldn't be funny to him. It would be the end of the world.\" {o} looks at you oddly, and puts the dye away. Bram never finds out how close it was.\n\n@event bram_between_coaching\ntitle: Prefect's Business\npool: between_once\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  bram: bram where met(bram) and around(bram) and rel(bram) >= 0\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4 and not at_odds(bram, o)\nper: bram\nwhen: seen(bram_between_coaching) == 0\nart: duel\nscene: A duelling floor late at night, a prefect patiently correcting a younger student's stance, both freezing as a third student opens the door.\ntext: You go into the duelling hall late and find Bram there, without his sash, patiently correcting {o}'s footwork. {o} is terrible. Bram is being kind about it. They both freeze when they see you. \"Prefect's business,\" says Bram, too quickly.\n> left: Tease him about it\n  rel o +tiny\n  rel bram -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Prefect's business,\" you repeat, delighted. Bram goes the colour of his sash. He's short with you for a week, and keeps coaching {o} anyway, which you suspect was never in question.\n> right: Say you never saw a thing\n  rel bram +small\n  npc_rel bram o +small\n  say: You back out of the door. The next morning Bram passes you in the corridor and says, without looking at you, \"Don't make it weird.\" You don't. {o} wins a practice bout the following week, and Bram claps once, loudly, then stops.\n\n# ---------------- Corvin ----------------\n\n@event corvin_between_odds\ntitle: Corvin Makes a Book\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  corvin: corvin where met(corvin) and around(corvin)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4\n  p: any where classmate_here(p) and at_odds(o, p)\nper: corvin\nwhen: seen(corvin_between_odds) == 0\nart: coin\nscene: A student with a deck of cards tucked behind one ear writing odds in chalk on the back of a door, a small crowd pressing coins into his hand.\ntext: {o} and {p} are going to settle it with wands in the courtyard at sunset, and Corvin is making a book. \"Seven to four on {o}, saint,\" he says, chalking it on the back of a door. \"Want in? It's practically charity. I'm charity.\"\n> left: Put a crown on {o}\n  gold -small\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance even:\n    gold +medium\n    rel corvin +tiny\n    say: {o} wins in a flash of green sparks, and Corvin pays you out with a flourish and a wince. \"Beginner's luck. I'll get it back. I always get it back.\"\n  else:\n    rel corvin +tiny\n    say: {p} wins, with a hex nobody saw coming. Corvin pockets your crown with real sympathy. \"Should've asked me,\" he says. \"I'd have told you. For a fee.\"\n> right: Tip off a prefect\n  alignment +small\n  rel corvin -medium\n  npc_rel o p +tiny\n  say: The prefects break it up before the first spell, and confiscate the chalk, and Corvin's name goes in the book.{knows(corvin.last_warning):  He is one detention from being sent home.|} He knows exactly who told. He doesn't say anything. That's worse than if he had.\n\n@event corvin_between_debt\ntitle: Corvin Owes {o}\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  corvin: corvin where met(corvin) and around(corvin) and rel(corvin) >= 6\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 6\nper: corvin\nwhen: seen(corvin_between_debt) == 0\nart: pouch\nscene: A student waving a scrap of paper at another, who is shrugging with both hands empty and grinning.\ntext: Corvin owes {o} eight crowns, and has owed it since the leaves turned. {o} has an IOU in Corvin's handwriting, with a small drawing of a goat on it. {o} would like you, as Corvin's friend, to collect.\n> left: Pay it yourself\n  gold -small\n  rel o +small\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: You pay {o} and tell Corvin he owes you now. \"Saint,\" he says, appalled and touched in equal measure. \"Who does that?\" He pays you back three weeks later, in coins and a very good story about where he got them.\n> right: Get Corvin to pay\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      npc_rel corvin o +small\n      rel o +small\n      say: You corner Corvin and refuse to laugh at any of his jokes until he pays. It takes four jokes. He pays {o}, all eight, and draws {o} a better goat as interest.\n    lose:\n      rel o -small\n      rel corvin -tiny\n      say: Corvin turns it into a joke, then a card trick, then a story about trolls, and by the end you've somehow agreed it's {o}'s fault for lending. {o} doesn't find that funny.\n\n@event corvin_between_witness\ntitle: Where Corvin Was\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  corvin: corvin where met(corvin) and around(corvin) and rel(corvin) >= 12\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4 and not at_odds(corvin, o)\nper: corvin\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(corvin_between_witness) == 0\nart: key\nscene: A student in a dark stairwell holding up an unlocked padlock, another student beside him shaking their head urgently.\ntext: {o} is up before the headmistress: a silver inkstand gone from the library, and {o} was seen near it. Corvin knows {o} didn't take it; he saw who did, at two in the morning. To say so, he'd have to say where he was at two in the morning. \"One more detention, saint,\" he says quietly, \"and they send me home.\"\n> left: Tell him to speak up\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      rel corvin +small\n      rel o +small\n      npc_rel corvin o +medium\n      alignment +small\n      learn corvin.last_warning\n      say: Corvin goes in, and tells it straight, without a single joke. The headmistress gives him a long look and a warning, not a detention, \"in view of the circumstances\". {o} doesn't know what to say. Corvin says it for both of them: \"Don't make a habit of being innocent near me.\"\n    lose:\n      rel corvin -small\n      learn corvin.last_warning\n      say: Corvin shakes his head. \"I can't, saint. I can't go home.\" You don't push. {o} gets the detention, and the real thief is never named, and Corvin can't look at {o} for weeks.\n> right: Find another way to clear {o}\n  learn corvin.last_warning\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel o +small\n      rel corvin +small\n      study -tiny\n      say: You go through the library ledger until midnight and find that the inkstand was booked out for cleaning to someone else entirely. {o} is cleared. Corvin buys you a drink and doesn't say why.\n    lose:\n      rel o -tiny\n      study -tiny\n      say: You turn the library inside out and find nothing anyone will believe. {o} serves the detention. Corvin sits with {o} at supper every night of it.\n\n# ---------------- Nessa ----------------\n\n@event nessa_between_story\ntitle: The Human Angle\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  nessa: nessa where met(nessa) and around(nessa)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 8 and not npc_is(bram)\nper: nessa\nwhen: seen(nessa_between_story) == 0\nart: paper\nscene: A newspaper office cluttered with proofs, an editor holding up a draft headline while a student in the doorway begs her not to run it.\ntext: {o} failed a practical exam badly enough that the lecturer wrote home, and somehow Nessa has heard. \"It's a story about pressure,\" she says, in headline voice. \"The human angle. I'd change the name. Mostly.\" {o} has found out, and caught you in the corridor first. \"Please. She'll listen to you.\"\n> left: Talk Nessa out of it\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel o +medium\n      rel nessa -tiny\n      npc_rel nessa o +tiny\n      say: You put it to her plainly: it's not a story about pressure, it's a story about {o}. Nessa goes very quiet, the way she does off the record. Then she spikes it. \"You're right. I hate that you're right.\"\n    lose:\n      rel nessa -small\n      rel o -tiny\n      say: \"Everyone's a story,\" Nessa says. \"Some just aren't finished.\" She runs it, with the name changed. Everyone knows who it is. {o} doesn't speak to either of you for a fortnight.\n> right: Offer her a better story\n  renown -tiny\n  rel nessa +small\n  rel o +small\n  say: You give Nessa something of your own instead: a long, unflattering account of your worst week this term. She takes it, delighted. {o}'s story never runs. Yours does, on page two, and people are very kind about it for a week.\n\n@event nessa_between_match\ntitle: Match of the Month\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  nessa: nessa where met(nessa) and around(nessa) and rel(nessa) >= 6\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4 and fancy_free(o) and not npc_is(nessa)\nper: nessa\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(nessa_between_match) == 0\nart: heart\nscene: An editor at a cluttered desk holding out a quill and a blank form titled \"Match of the Month\", grinning.\ntext: The Lumen Ledger is launching \"Match of the Month\", anonymous and entirely scientific, and Nessa needs a first nomination by the printers' deadline. She has heard you know {o}. \"Who's {o} sweet on? You don't have to say. Just write it down. That's not saying.\"\n> left: Nominate {o}\n  renown +tiny\n  rel nessa +small\n  rel o -small\n  say: You write a name down. On Friday the whole castle reads that \"a certain student in the year\" is pining for \"a certain other student\", with details. {o} recognises every detail. So does the other student, it turns out, and the next week they're seen at the Gilded Newt together. {o} is still furious with you.\n> right: Tell Nessa it's not yours to print\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  rel o +small\n  say: \"Off the record?\" says Nessa. \"It's off the record that I'm not telling you,\" you say. She laughs, and nominates herself instead, which sells out the issue.\n\n# ---------------- Mir ----------------\n\n@event mir_between_owl\ntitle: The Owl Has Opinions\npool: between_once\ntone: light\ncast:\n  mir: mir where met(mir) and around(mir) and seen(mir_owl_gift) > 0\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4\nper: mir\nwhen: seen(mir_between_owl) == 0\nart: gear\nscene: A clockwork owl perched on a workshop lamp, glaring with brass eyes at a student who is ducking, while its maker apologises with both hands.\ntext: Mir's clockwork owl, which remembers deadlines, birthdays and, slightly, grudges, has taken against {o}. It hoots when {o} comes in. It drops screws on {o} from the rafters. \"It's not personal!\" says Mir. \"Probably. It might be personal. I didn't build it to be personal.\"\n> left: Help Mir find out why\n  challenge research apprentice:\n    win:\n      npc_rel mir o +small\n      rel mir +small\n      rel o +tiny\n      say: You go through the owl's memory spool with Mir, half the night, and find it: {o} once moved a teacup off the owl's favourite shelf, in the first week. Mir has a long, gentle talk with the owl. The screws stop. Mostly.\n    lose:\n      rel mir +tiny\n      study -tiny\n      say: You and Mir take the owl apart and put it back together and it is still furious with {o}. \"It's a feature now,\" Mir decides. {o} starts wearing a hat.\n> right: Tell {o} to apologise to it\n  rel o +small\n  npc_rel mir o +tiny\n  say: You suggest {o} apologise. {o} stands under the rafters and apologises to the owl, formally, for whatever it was. The owl considers this for a whole minute, then hoots once, and from then on drops screws on {o} only on Tuesdays. Mir is moved almost to tears.\n\n@event mir_between_blunt\ntitle: Probably Fine\npool: between\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  mir: mir where met(mir) and around(mir)\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 6 and not at_odds(mir, o)\nper: mir\nart: gear\nscene: A workshop bench strewn with gears, an artificer talking excitedly to a half-built machine while a classmate stands to one side, looking hurt.\ntext: {~{o} asked to help Mir in the workshop. Mir said, cheerfully, \"No, you'll touch things,\" and then talked to a gearbox for ten minutes.|{o} showed Mir a design. Mir said, \"That'll explode,\" with total delight, and went back to the lathe.|{o} has been holding the same lamp for Mir for an hour. Mir has thanked the lamp twice and {o} not once.} {o} finds you afterwards. \"Do they even like me?\"\n> left: Translate for Mir\n  rel o +small\n  npc_rel mir o +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {~You explain that \"you'll touch things\" is high praise from Mir; it means you'd be interested. {o} goes back. Mir gives {o} a screwdriver of {o.their} very own, which is practically a ring.|\"It'll explode\" means \"I'd want to watch,\" you tell {o}. {o} brings the design back, and Mir spends three hours making it explode properly.|You tell {o} that Mir thanks lamps because lamps can't be hurt. {o} laughs. The next time, Mir, prompted by you, thanks {o}, and then the lamp, in that order.}\n> right: Tell Mir directly\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel mir +tiny\n      npc_rel mir o +small\n      say: {~\"Oh!\" says Mir, stricken. \"Oh no. Probably I should say things to people.\" They go and say several things to {o}, all at once, most of them about gears. It works.|Mir listens, goggles pushed up, and then goes and apologises to {o} with a small clockwork frog. The frog says sorry. Mir says it too.|Mir takes it very seriously and writes \"SAY THANK YOU TO {o}\" on the workshop wall in chalk, where it stays for a year.}\n    lose:\n      rel mir -tiny\n      say: {~Mir doesn't really hear it; they're halfway into a gearbox. \"Hm? Yes. Probably,\" they say. {o} gives up on the workshop.|\"But I do like {o},\" says Mir, baffled, \"I let {o} hold the lamp.\" You don't get further than that.|Mir nods along and then forgets, which is very Mir. {o} decides the workshop isn't for {o.them}.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/between_stories.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Between people, as stories (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md, batch P4-05): three small storylines about how\n#  others get on, each a `story` thread (content/core/templates.grim) with Journal lines. Two follow canon pairs\n#  whose feelings are already set in the cast (Bram and Corvin's feud; Tobias and Mir), so every step knows who it\n#  is about; the study circle grows by whoever joins it. How a pair ends reads their own feelings (`npc_rel`).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Ledger War (Bram and Corvin) ----------------\n\n@thread bram_corvin_feud\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=flag(feud_begun)\nclass: story\nrepeat: 1\ntags: story between\nname: The Ledger War\npremise: Bram had written Corvin up eleven times in one term. Corvin had started writing back.\njournal:\n  flag(feud_begun): Bram and Corvin went to war over the demerit ledger, and you stepped into the middle of it.\n  flag(feud_ledger_back): Bram's ledger vanished. You found it in Corvin's coat and put it back before anyone knew.\n  flag(feud_ledger_gone): Bram's ledger vanished, and you let it stay vanished. The castle held its breath for a week.\n  flag(feud_locked_in): The headmistress set them to clear the confiscation vault together. You locked the door on them for an hour.\n  flag(feud_by_the_rules): The headmistress set them to clear the confiscation vault together, and you watched them by the rules.\n  flag(feud_truce): Bram and Corvin shook hands in the vault doorway. Neither will say who offered first.\n  flag(feud_cold): Bram and Corvin came out of the vault still enemies, if quieter ones.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(feud_truce) or flag(feud_cold)\nmembers:\n  start feud_ledger_war\n\n@event feud_ledger_war\ntitle: Say Please\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\ntone: light\nwhen: met(bram) and met(corvin) and around(bram) and around(corvin) and term and turn >= 4 and not flag(feud_begun)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: scroll\nscene: A courtyard fountain, a prefect shouting at a locked ledger in his hands, a grinning student leaning on the fountain rim with a deck of cards.\ntext: Bram has written Corvin up for the eleventh time this term (\"existing in a corridor\"), and Corvin has answered by charming the lock on Bram's demerit ledger so that it opens only for the word \"please\". Bram has been saying \"OPEN\" at it for ten minutes. Half the courtyard is watching. Both of them see you at the same moment.\n> left: Step in between them\n  flag feud_begun\n  npc_rel bram corvin -small\n  rel corvin +tiny\n  rel bram -tiny\n  queue feud_ledger_gone in 1\n  say: \"Just say it, Bram.\" Bram says it, through his teeth. The ledger opens. Corvin laughs so hard he has to hold on to the fountain, and Bram writes your name on a fresh page, very neatly, and underlines it.\n> right: Keep walking\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You keep walking. Behind you, the courtyard counts along with Bram: \"OPEN. OPEN.\" It's still going when you reach the library.\n\n@event feud_ledger_gone\ntitle: The Missing Ledger\nthread: bram_corvin_feud\ntrigger: queued\ntone: tense\nart: key\nscene: A coat hanging on a dormitory peg with the corner of a red leather ledger sticking out of its pocket.\ntext: Bram's ledger has gone. Not locked: gone. Bram is white with fury and quoting rules at the walls. Corvin is very helpful about looking for it. Then you borrow Corvin's coat on a cold evening, and find the ledger in its inside pocket. {knows(corvin.last_warning): Corvin is one detention from being sent home. If Bram finds it there, that's the end of him.|If Bram finds it there, Corvin is finished.}\n> left: Put it back quietly\n  flag feud_ledger_back\n  alignment +small\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  npc_rel bram corvin +tiny\n  queue feud_vault within 1..2\n  say: You leave it on Bram's desk in the prefects' room, open at today's page. Bram never knows how. Corvin knows exactly how. \"Saint,\" he says, and for once it isn't quite a joke.\n> right: Leave it where it is\n  flag feud_ledger_gone\n  alignment -small\n  rel corvin +small\n  npc_rel bram corvin -small\n  queue feud_vault within 1..2\n  say: You hang the coat back up. For a week nobody in the castle gets a demerit, and Bram stalks the corridors like a man who has lost a hand. Corvin buys everyone cider.\n\n@event feud_vault\ntitle: A Week in the Vault\nthread: bram_corvin_feud\ntrigger: queued\ntone: light\ndefer: not (around(bram) and around(corvin))\nart: key\nscene: A cluttered vault of confiscated oddities, two students glaring at each other over a crate, a third leaning in the doorway with a lantern.\ntext: The headmistress has had enough of both of them. Bram and Corvin will clear out the confiscation vault together, every evening for a week, and you, having been seen in the middle of all of it, are to watch them. Twenty minutes in, Bram is labelling things. Corvin is unlabelling them.\n> left: Lock them in for an hour\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      flag feud_locked_in\n      npc_rel bram corvin +medium\n      rel corvin +tiny\n      queue feud_end in 1\n      say: You step out \"for a lantern\" and turn the key. When you open the door an hour later they're sitting on the same crate, arguing about a confiscated board game, and Bram is winning, and Corvin is letting him.\n    lose:\n      flag feud_locked_in\n      rel bram -small\n      rel corvin -tiny\n      queue feud_end in 1\n      say: Corvin hears the key turn and has the lock open before you've let go of it. \"Nice try, saint.\" Bram writes you up for \"obstruction of a punishment\", which isn't a rule, until he invents it.\n> right: Watch them by the rules\n  flag feud_by_the_rules\n  study +tiny\n  npc_rel bram corvin -tiny\n  queue feud_end in 1\n  say: You sit in the doorway and revise while they snipe. By the end of the week the vault is spotless and they've stopped speaking altogether, which is, the headmistress says, an improvement.\n\n@event feud_end\ntitle: The Vault Door\nthread: bram_corvin_feud\ntrigger: queued\ntone: warm\nart: hourglass\nscene: Two students in a vault doorway at the end of a long week, one in a prefect's sash, one with cards behind his ear, a third watching.\ntext: The last evening in the vault. {npc_rel(bram, corvin) > -20: Something has changed this week. Bram has stopped quoting rules at Corvin. Corvin has stopped quoting Bram back.|Nothing much has changed. They've cleared the vault by working at opposite ends of it.} At the door, Bram stops, and looks at Corvin, and then at you.\n> left: Say something to both of them\n  rel bram +small\n  rel corvin +small\n  if npc_rel(bram, corvin) > -20:\n    flag feud_truce\n    npc_rel bram corvin +small\n    say: You say they make a better team than either would admit. Bram goes red. Corvin grins. Then Bram, stiffly, holds out a hand, and Corvin shakes it, and immediately palms the prefect's pencil. Bram lets him keep it.\n  else:\n    flag feud_cold\n    say: You try. Bram says \"noted\". Corvin says \"saint\". They leave by different stairs. But the ledger, after that, has Corvin's name in it a little less often.\n> right: Leave them to it\n  mood +tiny\n  if npc_rel(bram, corvin) > -20:\n    flag feud_truce\n    say: You go ahead up the stairs. Behind you, a long silence, and then Corvin's laugh, and then, astonishingly, Bram's. At breakfast they nod to each other. Nobody knows what to make of it.\n  else:\n    flag feud_cold\n    say: You leave them to it. Neither of them says goodbye to the other. The war is over, more or less, because both of them are tired of it.\n\n# ---------------- The Watering Engine (Tobias and Mir) ----------------\n\n@thread tobias_mir\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=flag(tm_begun)\nclass: story\nrepeat: 1\ntags: story between\nname: The Watering Engine\npremise: Mir built Tobias a machine to water the greenhouse. Then neither of them could stop talking about it.\njournal:\n  flag(tm_begun): Mir built Tobias a watering engine, and you noticed how long they spent explaining it to each other.\n  flag(tm_planned): Tobias asked how to invite Mir to the orchard \"without it being a thing\". You helped him plan the thing.\n  flag(tm_just_ask): Tobias asked how to invite Mir somewhere. You told him to just ask.\n  flag(tm_told): Mir wondered if Tobias only liked the engine. You told Mir otherwise.\n  flag(tm_ask_yourself): Mir wondered if Tobias only liked the engine. You told Mir to ask him.\n  flag(tm_together): Tobias and Mir turned up at the orchard together. Whatever it is, it's their business, and it's going well.\n  flag(tm_friends): Tobias and Mir are the best of friends, and the greenhouse is the best-watered in the country.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(tm_together) or flag(tm_friends)\nmembers:\n  start tm_watering_engine\n\n@event tm_watering_engine\ntitle: The Watering Engine\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\ntone: light\nwhen: met(tobias) and met(mir) and around(tobias) and around(mir) and fancy_free(tobias) and fancy_free(mir) and turn >= 5 and not flag(tm_begun)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: gear\nscene: A greenhouse with a brass contraption trundling along the benches watering seedlings, a big student and a goggled artificer crouched beside it, deep in talk.\ntext: There's a brass contraption trundling along the greenhouse benches, watering seedlings with a little copper spout. Mir built it. Tobias is explaining to it, gently, which plants like it lower. Mir is listening to Tobias the way Mir usually listens only to machines. Neither of them has noticed you.\n> left: Ask them how it works\n  flag tm_begun\n  npc_rel tobias mir +small\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  rel mir +tiny\n  queue tm_invitation within 1..2\n  say: They tell you at the same time, in different directions: Mir about gears, Tobias about ferns. They keep interrupting each other to agree. You leave an hour later. They don't notice that either.\n> right: Leave them to it\n  study +tiny\n  say: You take your notes to the far end of the greenhouse. The engine squeaks past you twice, followed by two people arguing happily about tomatoes.\n\n@event tm_invitation\ntitle: Not a Thing\nthread: tobias_mir\ntrigger: queued\ntone: warm\nwhen: fancy_free(tobias) and fancy_free(mir)\ndefer: season == \"winter\"\nart: flower\nscene: A big student sitting on an upturned flowerpot, turning his cap round and round in his hands, asking a friend for advice.\ntext: Tobias corners you by the potting benches, turning his cap round and round in his big hands. \"I'd like to ask Mir to the orchard on Sunday,\" he says. \"To see the bees. Only, without it being a thing. Unless it's a thing. How do you do that?\"\n> left: Help him plan it\n  flag tm_planned\n  study -tiny\n  npc_rel tobias mir +small\n  rel tobias +small\n  queue tm_misread within 1..2\n  say: You spend an evening on it: a picnic, a jar of honey, a spare pair of goggles in case of bees. Tobias writes it all down on a seed packet. He apologises for the trouble four times and thanks you seven.\n> right: Tell him to just ask\n  flag tm_just_ask\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  chance likely:\n    npc_rel tobias mir +small\n    queue tm_misread within 1..2\n    say: \"Just ask,\" you say. Tobias takes a breath that lifts his whole frame, and goes and asks. You hear Mir say \"probably!\" from across the greenhouse, which from Mir is a yes.\n  else:\n    queue tm_misread within 1..2\n    say: Tobias goes to ask, and comes back having talked about the engine for twenty minutes instead. \"I'll ask tomorrow,\" he says. He does, in the end. It takes a week.\n\n@event tm_misread\ntitle: Only the Engine?\nthread: tobias_mir\ntrigger: queued\ntone: tense\nart: gear\nscene: A workshop at night, an artificer sitting on a bench with goggles pushed up, staring at a half-built engine, a friend in the doorway.\ntext: Mir finds you, goggles pushed up, unusually quiet. \"Tobias wants to go to the orchard. To see the bees.\" A long pause. \"Is that about the engine? Probably it's about the engine. It's a very good engine. It's fine if it's about the engine.\"\n> left: Tell Mir it isn't\n  flag tm_told\n  rel mir +small\n  npc_rel tobias mir +medium\n  queue tm_end within 1..2\n  say: \"It isn't about the engine,\" you say. Mir looks at you for a long moment, and then goes slowly pink from the collar up. \"Oh,\" says Mir. \"Oh. Probably I should bring something. Not the engine.\" You suggest honey.\n> right: \"Ask him yourself.\"\n  flag tm_ask_yourself\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      npc_rel tobias mir +small\n      rel mir +tiny\n      queue tm_end within 1..2\n      say: Mir marches into the greenhouse and asks him, straight out, in front of a bench of seedlings. You don't hear the answer. You see Mir's face, after. That's enough.\n    lose:\n      rel mir -tiny\n      queue tm_end within 1..2\n      say: Mir doesn't ask. Mir builds Tobias a second engine instead, a smaller one, for the windowsill. It's a kind of asking. You're not sure Tobias reads it.\n\n@event tm_end\ntitle: The Orchard\nthread: tobias_mir\ntrigger: queued\ntone: warm\ndefer: season == \"winter\" or not (around(tobias) and around(mir))\nart: flower\nscene: An orchard on a sunny afternoon, two friends on a blanket among beehives, one in goggles, one with a jar of honey, laughing.\ntext: Sunday, the orchard, the bees. {npc_rel(tobias, mir) >= 30: Tobias and Mir arrive together, carrying a picnic between them and arguing happily about bees.|Tobias and Mir arrive separately, and spend the afternoon talking about the engine.} They've saved you a corner of the blanket, though you weren't sure you were invited.\n> left: Stay for the picnic\n  mood +small\n  rel tobias +small\n  rel mir +small\n  if npc_rel(tobias, mir) >= 30:\n    flag tm_together\n    say: You stay an hour, and then slip away, and neither of them notices you go. Whatever it is, it's their business. It looks like it's going well.\n  else:\n    flag tm_friends\n    say: It's a lovely afternoon. Whatever it might have been, it's a friendship, and a good one: the greenhouse gets watered on time for the rest of the year.\n> right: Leave them the blanket\n  study +tiny\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  rel mir +tiny\n  if npc_rel(tobias, mir) >= 30:\n    flag tm_together\n    say: You say you've an essay, which is true. From the orchard gate you look back once. They're sharing the honey. You don't look back again.\n  else:\n    flag tm_friends\n    say: You leave them to the bees. At supper, they're still talking about the engine. Best friends, then. It's not nothing.\n\n# ---------------- The Thursday Circle (a study circle) ----------------\n\n@thread study_circle\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=flag(circle_begun)\nclass: story\nrepeat: 1\ntags: story between\nname: The Thursday Circle\npremise: A study circle that started with two chairs and a borrowed lamp.\njournal:\n  flag(circle_begun): You and a friend started meeting in the library on Thursdays, with one lamp between you.\n  flag(circle_open): Someone nobody much liked asked to join. You said yes, and the circle got bigger.\n  flag(circle_closed): Someone nobody much liked asked to join. The circle said no, and stayed cosy.\n  flag(circle_held): The night before exams, the circle nearly came apart. You held it together.\n  flag(circle_split): The night before exams, the circle came apart, and put itself back together after.\n  flag(circle_done): The Thursday Circle has its own table now, and a name carved under it.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(circle_done)\nmembers:\n  start circle_founded\n\n@event circle_founded\ntitle: Thursdays\nplace: library\ntime: evening\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 10\nwhen: term and not wrapup and turn >= 3 and not flag(circle_begun)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\nscene: A library alcove in the evening, two students sharing a single lamp over open books, an empty third chair pulled up.\ntext: {o} drops into the chair across from you in the library with a stack of books and a borrowed lamp. \"I can't revise alone. I just read the same page. What if we did this every week? Thursdays. You and me, and whoever else.\"\n> left: \"Thursdays, then.\"\n  flag circle_begun\n  study +small\n  rel o +small\n  queue circle_newcomer within 1..2\n  say: You shake on it over the lamp. The first Thursday you get through a chapter each and an argument about it. The second Thursday someone else asks if the third chair is taken.\n> right: \"I work better alone.\"\n  lore +small\n  rel o -tiny\n  say: {o} nods, and takes the lamp to another alcove. You get a great deal done. Every Thursday after that you see the lamp lit in the far alcove, and sometimes two heads under it.\n\n@event circle_newcomer\ntitle: The Fourth Chair\nthread: study_circle\ntrigger: queued\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  n: any where classmate_here(n) and rel(n) <= 5\nwhen: term\nart: book\nscene: A library table with three students around a lamp, a fourth standing a little way off with books clutched to their chest.\ntext: The circle is four now, sometimes five. Tonight {n} is hovering at the end of the table with an armful of books: {n}, whom nobody in the circle much likes, and who has, it turns out, failed the last two practicals. \"Could I sit in?\" {n} asks. The others look at you.\n> left: \"Pull up a chair.\"\n  flag circle_open\n  rel n +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  queue circle_exam_night within 1..2\n  say: {n} sits down. It's stiff for a week. Then {n} explains, better than any of you, the one thing all of you had wrong, and the circle shifts round to make room.\n> right: \"We're full, sorry.\"\n  flag circle_closed\n  rel n -small\n  study +small\n  queue circle_exam_night within 1..2\n  say: {n} says \"of course\" and goes. The circle stays as it was, cosy and quick. You see {n} revising alone the next Thursday, at the far end of the room.\n\n@event circle_exam_night\ntitle: The Night Before\nthread: study_circle\ntrigger: queued\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  w: any where classmate_here(w) and rel(w) >= 4\nwhen: term\ndefer: not (month == 4 or month == 10)\nart: candle\nscene: A library table late at night strewn with notes and cold tea, students glaring at each other across it, one of them on their feet.\ntext: The night before exams the circle sits till the lamps are trimmed, and it goes wrong. {w} hasn't done the notes {w.they} promised, and somebody says so, loudly, and {w} says some things back. Half the table is on its feet.\n> left: Hold it together\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag circle_held\n      study +small\n      rel w +small\n      queue circle_last within 2..4\n      say: You split {w}'s chapter four ways and start reading it aloud before anyone can leave. By midnight it's done, and {w} brings everyone breakfast the next morning without being asked.\n    lose:\n      flag circle_split\n      study -tiny\n      mood -small\n      queue circle_last within 2..4\n      say: You try, but it's too late and too tired. The circle breaks up at the door. You revise alone till two. It comes back together after the exams, a little shy of itself.\n> right: Take {w}'s chapter yourself\n  flag circle_held\n  study -small\n  health -tiny\n  rel w +medium\n  queue circle_last within 2..4\n  say: \"I'll do it,\" you say, and the table goes quiet. You're up till three. {w} sits up with you, not saying much, and makes the tea.\n\n@event circle_last\ntitle: The Carved Table\nthread: study_circle\ntrigger: queued\ntone: warm\nart: book\nscene: A library table in the evening, a group of students round a lamp, one of them carving something small into the underside of the table with a penknife.\ntext: The Thursday Circle has outlasted the term. It has its own table now, and its own lamp, which the librarian pretends not to know about. Someone has brought a penknife and suggests, in a whisper, that the table should have a name on it. Underneath, where nobody will see.\n> left: Carve it\n  mood +small\n  flag circle_done\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You carve \"THURSDAYS\" on the underside of the table, very small, while the others keep lookout. Years from now somebody will find it and wonder. You hope they start a circle.\n> right: Leave the table be\n  study +tiny\n  flag circle_done\n  say: You talk them out of the penknife. Somebody writes \"Thursdays\" on the inside cover of a library book instead, in pencil, and puts it back on the shelf where it'll be found by the right person.\n"},{"path":"content/events/between.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Between people (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md, batch P4-01): two people you know, and how they treat each\n#  other. Friction and feuds between classmates who can't stand each other (`at_odds`), and the circles that\n#  form between those who get on (`get_along`). Both roles are generic; the first (`a`) carries the cooldown.\n#  Each card moves how the pair feel about each other (`npc_rel`), so the People panel learns it too.\n# ============================================================\n\n# Two classmates and you are the seasoning, not the meal: a run's people come one at a time. Weight 1 against the\n# single-character social cards' 10-12 keeps these to about one every four to six months (phase 4 tracker,\n# \"Between-people cards are rare\"); fresh cards keep full novelty, so their share is set here, not by their count.\n@pool between\ndesc: A card about two classmates and you: social, routine, rare, a long rest per character.\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 1\ntags: social between\n\n@pool between_once\ndesc: A one-off between two classmates, rare; a social card's once is per character, so each card adds `seen(<its id>) == 0`.\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nweight: 1\ntags: social between\n\n# ---------------- Friction and feuds ----------------\n\n@event between_two_seats\ntitle: Two Saved Seats\npool: between\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 8\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 8 and at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\ntime: noon evening\nart: meal\nscene: A long refectory table at mealtime, two students at opposite ends, each with a hand on an empty stretch of bench.\ntext: {~{a} has put a bag on the bench beside {them}, and at the far end {b} has put a book on the bench too. Both are for you.|{a} waves you over to a saved place. So, from the other end of the table, does {b}. They have not looked at each other once.|There are two places kept for you in the refectory, one at each end of the table, and the people keeping them would rather eat soup through a straw than sit together.}\n> left: Sit with {a}\n  rel a +small\n  rel b -small\n  say: {~{a} moves the bag. Down the table, {b} moves the book back onto the bench, slowly, so you see.|{a} has saved you the crust. {b} eats facing the window.|You sit with {a}. {b} leaves before pudding, loudly.}\n> right: Pull them to one table\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      npc_rel a b +small\n      rel a +tiny\n      rel b +tiny\n      say: {~You carry your tray to the middle and wave them both in. They come, sulking, and argue about the gravy, which is at least talking.|You sit dead centre and start a story that needs both of them to finish it. By the end they're correcting each other instead of glaring.|You make them pass you things. Salt, bread, the jug. By the end they're passing each other things, which is how it starts.}\n    lose:\n      npc_rel a b -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {~You sit in the middle and they both stay where they are. You eat alone between two empty places, like a referee nobody asked for.|You try. {a} takes {their} tray to the window, {b} takes {b.theirs} to the door, and you're left with three puddings.|Neither of them moves. The whole table watches you eat. It's the longest meal of the term.}\n\n@event between_messenger\ntitle: Pass It On\npool: between\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 6\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 6 and at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\nart: letter\nscene: A student in a corridor holding a folded note out at arm's length, another student turned pointedly away at the far end.\ntext: {~{a} and {b} aren't speaking, and you've become the post. \"Tell {b} the common-room lamp is NOT {b.theirs},\" says {a}.|{a} hands you a folded note for {b}. It isn't sealed. The first word is underlined three times.|\"Would you let {b} know,\" says {a}, very politely, \"that some of us have to sleep.\"}\n> left: Pass it on word for word\n  rel a +tiny\n  npc_rel a b -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You deliver it exactly. {b} sends one back, worse. By supper you've carried six, and you're tired in a way that has nothing to do with stairs.|You hand it over unread. {b} reads it, writes \"NO\" under it, and hands it back to you to carry.|You say it word for word. {b} says something word for word back. You're not paid enough for this, and you're not paid.}\n> right: Soften it on the way\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      npc_rel a b +small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {~By the time it reaches {b} it's a polite question about the lamp. {b} answers politely. {a} is baffled, and a little disarmed.|You lose the underlining somewhere on the stairs. {b} reads a reasonable note and writes a reasonable answer. Nobody knows why it worked.|\"Would you mind\" becomes \"would you mind, please\". It's enough. They nod at each other at breakfast.}\n    lose:\n      rel a -small\n      rel b -small\n      say: {~They compare notes, literally. Neither recognises what you delivered. For once they agree on something: you.|{b} asks {a} where the underlining went. Both of them turn to look at you.|It comes out at supper. They're united, briefly, in being annoyed with the post.}\n\n@event between_whose_idea\ntitle: Whose Idea Was It\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 5\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 5 and not get_along(a, b)\nper: a\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(between_whose_idea) == 0\nart: scroll\nscene: Two students in front of a lecturer's desk, both pointing at the same page of notes, a third student standing just behind them.\ntext: The lecturer praises the trick that made last week's practical work, and asks whose it was. {a} says \"mine\", and so does {b}, at the same moment. You were there when {a} thought of it, on the back of an envelope. Both of them look at you.\n> left: \"It was {a}'s.\"\n  rel a +small\n  rel b -small\n  npc_rel a b -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {b} goes red to the ears. {a} gets the praise and, later, buys you a cake. {b} doesn't speak to either of you until the end of the week, and is extremely polite when {b.their} silence ends.\n> right: \"We worked it out together.\"\n  rel a -tiny\n  npc_rel a b +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: The lecturer gives the three of you the credit and moves on. {b} looks relieved. {a} looks at you for a long moment, and doesn't mention the envelope again.\n\n@event between_boot_hex\ntitle: Dancing Boots\npool: between\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 4\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 4 and at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\nart: sparkle\nscene: A corridor where one student's boots are jigging along the flagstones on their own, the student in them furious, another student by a pillar looking innocent.\ntext: {~{b}'s boots have started to dance, on their own, in the middle of the corridor.|{b}'s boots squelch at every step, though it hasn't rained for a week.|{b}'s laces have tied themselves together, in a bow, very neatly.} You saw {a} flick a wand at them. So did nobody else. \"Who did this?\" {b} demands, of the corridor, and then of you.\n> left: Tell {b} the truth\n  rel b +small\n  rel a -small\n  npc_rel a b -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {~{b} stalks off to find {a}, still jigging. You hear about the rematch at supper.|{b} squelches off towards {a}. You don't stay to watch.|{b} hops away in search of {a}, still tied. There's a prefect involved by lunchtime.}\n> right: \"I didn't see a thing.\"\n  rel a +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {~{b} dances off to the infirmary. {a} winks at you across the corridor, and you find you don't like being winked at.|{b} squelches away, sure it was someone else. {a} grins. It sits badly at lunch.|{b} sits on the floor and unpicks the bow with dignity. {a} owes you, and you'd rather {they} didn't.}\n\n@event between_not_them\ntitle: Not Them Again\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 10\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 4 and at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\nwhen: seen(between_not_them) == 0\nart: door\nscene: Two students talking low in a common-room doorway, one glancing over the other's shoulder at a third by the fire.\ntext: {a} catches you at the common-room door. \"Every time you sit with me, {b} comes too,\" {they} say{s}. \"I'm not asking you to choose. I'm just asking you to stop bringing {b}.\" Over by the fire, {b} looks up, and smiles at you.\n> left: Keep bringing {b}\n  rel a -small\n  rel b +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: \"Then I'll sit somewhere else,\" says {a}, and does, for a week. {b} never knows it was about {b.them}. You do.\n> right: Quietly leave {b} out\n  rel a +small\n  rel b -small\n  alignment -small\n  say: You start meeting {a} in the library, where {b} never goes. It works. {b} asks once, cheerfully, where you've been. You say \"around\", which is true and isn't.\n\n@event between_peace_offering\ntitle: A Peace Offering\npool: between_once\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 8\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\nwhen: seen(between_peace_offering) == 0\nart: cup\nscene: A student holding out a small wrapped parcel and a folded letter, looking anywhere but at the person they're handing it to.\ntext: {a} has a parcel in one hand and a letter in the other, and the look of someone who's been carrying both for days. \"It's for {b}. I was out of order. I said something I can't take back.\" {They} hold{s} them out. \"Would you give {b.them} these? I can't face it.\"\n> left: Make {a} do it in person\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      npc_rel a b +medium\n      rel a +small\n      say: You walk {a} there yourself, all the way to the door. It takes {them} two goes to say it. {b} takes the parcel, and then, after a long moment, takes {a}'s hand as well. You leave them to it.\n    lose:\n      npc_rel a b -tiny\n      rel a -small\n      say: {a} gets as far as the door, then turns round and walks straight back past you. \"I said I couldn't,\" {they} say{s}. The parcel goes back in a drawer.\n> right: Carry it for {a}\n  npc_rel a b +small\n  rel a +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You spend the evening you meant for revising finding {b}, who reads the letter twice and says \"Tell {a} I'll think about it.\" That's not nothing. You say so.\n\n@event between_seconds\ntitle: Seconds\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 10\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and at_odds(a, b) and npc_rel(a, b) <= -15\nper: a\nwhen: seen(between_seconds) == 0\nart: duel\nscene: Dawn on a dew-wet lawn, two students with wands squared off, a third standing at one of their shoulders in the mist.\ntext: What started between {a} and {b} over a library book has become a matter of honour: a formal challenge, dawn, the far lawn. Neither will back down in front of the other, and {a} has asked you, of all people, to stand at {their} shoulder. \"You only have to be there. And if I lose, don't let me hear about it.\"\n> left: Stand as {a}'s second\n  rel a +medium\n  rel b -small\n  renown +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  npc_rel a b -small\n  say: You stand at {a}'s shoulder {season == \"winter\": with your teeth chattering|in the wet grass}. The duel is short, loud and inconclusive; a stray hex singes your sleeve. {a} shakes your hand afterwards like a general. Half the dormitory heard about it by breakfast.\n> right: Talk them out of it at dawn\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      npc_rel a b +small\n      rel a +tiny\n      rel b +small\n      say: You stand between them on the {season == \"winter\": frosted|wet} grass and talk until the sun is up and both of them are too {season == \"winter\": cold|hungry} to want anything but toast. Nobody wins. Nobody has to.\n    lose:\n      rel a -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: They duel anyway, around you. You're not a very good wall. {a} says you embarrassed {them}, and {b} doesn't say anything, which is worse.\n\n@event between_dorm_vote\ntitle: The Common-Room Vote\npool: between\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 4\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 4 and at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\ntime: evening night\nart: group\nscene: A crowded common room, two students standing on chairs at opposite ends making speeches, everyone else holding up hands.\ntext: {~The common room is voting on whether the window stays open at night. {a} is leading the cold side, {b} the warm one.|Today's vote: who gets the good armchair by the fire on exam weeks. {a} has a rota. {b} has a counter-rota.|The common room votes on the portrait over the fire, which snores: turned to the wall at night, or not. {a} says turn it. {b} would sooner die than agree with {a}.} It's tied. Your hand will decide it.\n> left: Vote with {a}\n  rel a +small\n  rel b -small\n  npc_rel a b -tiny\n  say: {~The window stays open. {b} sleeps in two jumpers and a grievance.|The rota stands. {b} reads in the cold chair for a month and makes a point of it.|The portrait goes to the wall. {b} turns it back every night, on principle, and apologises to it.}\n> right: Propose a middle way\n  mood -tiny\n  npc_rel a b +tiny\n  say: {~You say the window should be open on even nights, and suddenly that's what everyone wants. Nobody's happy. Everyone's slightly warmer.|You say the chair should go to whoever's actually revising. They both look at you as if you've cheated.|You say the portrait can face the wall on exam weeks only. It's a coward's answer. It passes unanimously, and the portrait sulks.}\n\n@event between_lab_partners\ntitle: A Bench for Three\npool: between\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 2\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\nwhen: term and not wrapup and enrolled(alchemy)\nart: cauldron\nscene: A practical-class workbench, three students around one bubbling cauldron, two of them very carefully not passing each other anything.\ntext: {~The alchemy practical comes in threes, and the lecturer has put you at a bench with {a} and {b}.|A bench for three, a cauldron for one, and {a} and {b} have taken the two stools furthest from each other.|You're paired again: you, {a}, {b}, and one set of scales.} By the second step they're working two separate halves of the recipe and arguing through you.\n> left: Do the work yourself\n  study alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  npc_rel a b -tiny\n  say: {~You do the whole thing while they sulk at the ends of the bench. It comes out right, and you're the only one who knows how.|You make the brew. They each claim the other ruined it. It's perfectly fine.|You weigh, stir and bottle it alone. The mark is good. The afternoon was not.}\n> right: Make them work it together\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      npc_rel a b +small\n      study alchemy +tiny\n      say: {~You give {a} the stirring and {b} the timing, and make them call it out to each other. It works. The cauldron doesn't explode, and neither do they.|You hand each of them one half of the recipe. They have to read it out to each other. By the end {b} is laughing at {a}'s pronunciation, and {a} lets {b.them}.|You stand back with your arms folded until they talk. Eventually, they do. The brew is late, and passable.}\n    lose:\n      study alchemy -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~They won't. The brew curdles while they argue about whose fault the curdling is.|The cauldron boils over while {a} and {b} each wait for the other to move it.|You try to referee and the brew goes grey. All three of you get the same bad mark, which is, at least, fair.}\n\n# ---------------- Friends together ----------------\n\n@event between_cipher\ntitle: The Cipher\npool: between_once\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 10 and not npc_is(kael)\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 10 and get_along(a, b) and not npc_is(kael)\nper: a\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(between_cipher) == 0\nart: rune\nscene: Two students passing a scrap of paper covered in little drawn symbols under a lecture-hall desk, a third leaning in to see.\ntext: {a} and {b} have been passing notes all term in a cipher of their own: little drawings that stand for words, a fish for \"boring\", a tower for \"Thorne\". You've watched them laugh at blank-looking scraps for weeks. Today {b} slides one to you. It's the key, and it's a test.\n> left: Learn the cipher\n  lore +tiny\n  rel a +small\n  rel b +small\n  npc_rel a b +tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: You spend a lecture you should have spent listening learning forty little drawings instead. By the end of it you can read \"this lecture is a fish\" from three rows away, and the three of you have a language nobody else in the castle speaks.\n> right: Hand the key back\n  rel b -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"I'd only get you both caught,\" you whisper, and slide it back. {b} shrugs and tucks it away. The notes go on passing in front of you, and you find you'd quite like to know what the fish means.\n\n@event between_inside_joke\ntitle: The Joke You Missed\npool: between\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 8 and not npc_is(kael)\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 4 and get_along(a, b) and not npc_is(kael)\nper: a\nart: group\nscene: Two students doubled over with laughter on a staircase, a third standing a step below them, smiling politely.\ntext: {~{b} says \"the goose\" and {a} has to sit down on the stairs.|{a} hums four notes and {b} snorts tea out of {b.their} nose.|{b} only has to raise one eyebrow and {a} is gone, helpless, crying with laughter.} You have no idea why. They look at you, still laughing, and wait to see what you'll do.\n> left: Laugh along anyway\n  mood +tiny\n  rel a -tiny\n  say: {~You laugh. {a} can tell you've no idea, and it's somehow worse than not laughing.|You laugh along, too late and a bit too loud. {b} pats your shoulder, kindly.|You laugh. They stop. \"You don't know, do you,\" says {a}, delighted.}\n> right: \"All right. What's the joke?\"\n  rel a +small\n  rel b +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~It takes ten minutes to explain, involves a professor and a pond, and is about half as funny told. But now it's yours too, and the next time someone says \"the goose\" you're the one on the stairs.|They explain. It's about you, a little, from your first week. You laugh anyway. It is quite funny.|\"You had to be there,\" says {b}. Then they make you be there, re-enacting the whole thing on the landing. You're in it now.}\n\n@event between_cover_roll\ntitle: Answering for Two\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 10\n  b: any where tagged(b, peer) and not tagged(b, outsider) and met(b) and rel(b) >= 6 and get_along(a, b)\nper: a\ntime: morning noon afternoon\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(between_cover_roll) == 0\nart: paper\nscene: A lecture hall at the start of class, a lecturer reading names from a register, two students whispering in the third row beside an empty seat.\ntext: Roll call. {b}'s seat is empty, and {a} leans over. \"{b}'s not ill, {b}'s just not here. One more absence and it's a letter home. Answer for {b.them}? I can't, the lecturer knows my voice.\" The lecturer is three names away.\n> left: \"Present.\"\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel a +small\n      rel b +small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: You say it in a voice you hope is {b}'s. The lecturer ticks the register without looking up. {b} buys you both breakfast for a week and never tells you where {b.they} went.\n    lose:\n      rel a +tiny\n      rel b +tiny\n      mood -small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: The lecturer looks up. \"Remarkable, how {b} has your face.\" It's a detention for you and the letter home for {b} anyway. {b} is grateful, which almost makes it worse.\n> right: Stay quiet\n  rel a -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: The name hangs in the air and goes down as absent. {a} doesn't look at you for the rest of the lecture. {b} gets the letter home, and turns up the next day, and never asks who didn't answer.\n\n@event between_closed_table\ntitle: Saved, Sorry\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 10\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 8 and get_along(a, b)\nper: a\ntime: noon evening\nwhen: term and not wrapup and seen(between_closed_table) == 0\nart: meal\nscene: A corner table in a busy refectory, three friends crowded round it, a first-year with a full tray hovering at the end.\ntext: You, {a} and {b} have had the corner table for a term now. A first-year with a full tray and nowhere to sit asks if the fourth place is free. {a} and {b} exchange a look you know very well. It means \"no\".\n> left: Make room\n  alignment +small\n  rel a -tiny\n  rel b -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You shove your bag along and the first-year sits down, pink with relief. {a} is cool for the length of the meal. By the end of the week the first-year is the one who saves the table, and {b} has adopted them.\n> right: \"Sorry, it's saved.\"\n  rel a +small\n  rel b +tiny\n  alignment -small\n  say: The first-year says \"oh, of course,\" and walks away very straight. The corner stays yours. {a} relaxes. You find you can't remember what the fourth place was ever saved for.\n\n@event between_night_roof\ntitle: The Roof at Night\npool: between\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 12 and not npc_is(bram)\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 8 and get_along(a, b) and not npc_is(bram)\nper: a\nslot: night\nart: moon\nscene: Two students halfway out of a dormitory window onto a moonlit roof, one of them holding a hand back in for a third.\ntext: {~After lights out, {a} and {b} are halfway out of the window with a blanket and a stolen jar of honey.|{a} taps on your door after lights out. {b} is behind {them}, holding the roof-ladder.|It's a clear night, and {a} and {b} are already on the roof. A hand appears at your window.} \"Come on,\" whispers {a}. \"The stars are out and the prefects aren't.\"\n> left: Climb out with them\n  rel a +small\n  rel b +small\n  npc_rel a b +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You sit on the slates until the honey's gone and you've named three constellations wrongly. Tomorrow will be long. You don't mind yet.|Up on the roof the three of you say the kind of things you only say at that height. You're tired all the next day, and it was worth it.|The slates are freezing and the view is everything. You get in at three, and nobody catches you, this time.}\n> right: Stay in bed\n  health +tiny\n  rel a -tiny\n  say: {~You hear them laughing above your ceiling until late. You sleep well. You're not sure that was the point.|\"Your loss,\" whispers {b}, and the window clicks shut. In the morning they share a look you aren't in.|You stay warm. At breakfast they're yawning and pleased with themselves, and talk about something called \"the honey incident\".}\n\n@event between_falling_out\ntitle: Both Sides\npool: between_once\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 10\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 10 and get_along(a, b)\nper: a\nwhen: seen(between_falling_out) == 0\nart: heartbreak\nscene: Two students on opposite sides of a cloister garden, arms folded, each with their back to the other, a third student on the path between them.\ntext: {a} and {b} have fallen out, properly, over something neither will name. Each has told you their side by lunchtime, and the sides don't match. \"You'll tell {b} I'm right,\" says {a}. An hour later, {b} says exactly the same about {a}.\n> left: Get them in one room\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      npc_rel a b +medium\n      rel a +small\n      rel b +small\n      say: You book the window seat in the library and send each of them a note that says the other one is sorry. Neither was. By the time they've worked that out, they're arguing about you instead, and it's over.\n    lose:\n      npc_rel a b -small\n      rel a -tiny\n      rel b -tiny\n      say: They come, see each other, and leave by different doors. Each of them thinks you took the other's side. For a while you have two friends who don't talk to each other, and both talk to you about it.\n> right: Stay out of it\n  npc_rel a b -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You listen to both, agree with neither, and keep your head down. It takes them a month to start speaking again, and they never quite sit together the way they did. You wonder sometimes if a word from you would have done it.\n"},{"path":"content/events/bonds/friends.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Friendship (world expansion 1, plan §2b): about four cards per peer, each built on a fact found out\n#  (`learn`) or paid off later (`knows`), and the close milestones of Lyra, Bram and Ilse (their own\n#  versions of soc_become_close). No spark here: these are friends' cards.\n#  Plot safety: Kael's family, Ilse's upbringing and the lake are hard canon; these cards don't touch them.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Lyra ----------------\n\n@event lyra_who_beat_her\ntitle: Who Beat Her\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 9 and bond(lyra) != \"rival\" and bond(lyra) != \"nemesis\"\nweight: 9\nart: flame\ntags: social\nscene: A red-haired student on a window seat holding an old folded duelling programme, soft from handling, her thumb over one line.\ntext: Lyra is on a window seat with an old duelling programme, soft from being folded and unfolded. The Winter Trials, the year before she came here. Second place: L. Vaneshade. \"My mother wrote the week after,\" she says, without looking up. \"Not to ask how I was. To ask who beat me.\"\n> left: Ask who did\n  learn lyra.second_place\n  rel lyra +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"A boy from a fishing town who never duelled again,\" she says. \"He was better that day. I've thought about him every day since.\" She folds the programme along its old creases. \"I hope he's happy. I hope he never thinks about it at all.\"\n> right: Ask what she wrote back\n  learn lyra.second_place\n  charm +tiny\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: \"His name,\" Lyra says. \"What else?\" She walks away. Later you see her at the practice ring, alone, long after the torches are out.\n\n@event lyra_well_done\ntitle: Well Done\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: knows(lyra.mother) and rel(lyra) >= 15 and bond(lyra) != \"rival\" and bond(lyra) != \"nemesis\"\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: social\nscene: A practice ring at dusk, a red-haired duellist sitting on the edge with her wand across her knees, another student holding out a cup of water.\ntext: Lyra comes second in an exhibition bout, to a fourth-year twice her size, and comes off the ring with her face set like stone. Nobody says anything to her. They know better. You know her mother has never once said the two words.\n> left: Say them\n  rel lyra +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Well done,\" you say. She looks at you as if you've spoken another language. Then she sits down very suddenly on the edge of the ring, and laughs, and doesn't stop for a while, and when she does her eyes are wet. \"Don't,\" she says. \"Don't you dare tell anyone.\"\n> right: Spar with her until she's won something\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: You don't say anything. You pick up a practice wand. She beats you four times running, harder each time, and after the fourth she's breathing easier. \"Thank you,\" she says, which is as close as she gets.\n\n@event lyra_study_hall_truce\ntitle: The Study Hall Truce\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 9 and term and not wrapup and bond(lyra) != \"rival\" and bond(lyra) != \"nemesis\"\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 9\nart: book\ntags: social study\nscene: Two students at opposite ends of a long library table, a line of books stacked down the middle like a wall, both pretending not to look over.\ntext: Lyra has built a wall of books down the middle of your library table. \"My side,\" she says. \"Your side. No talking.\" {~Twenty minutes later she slides a note over the wall: \"What did Thorne mean by the second law?\"|An hour in, a biscuit appears on your side of the wall, unexplained.}\n> left: Answer over the wall\n  study +small\n  rel lyra +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You write the answer and slide it back. She writes a better one and slides that back. By closing time the wall is gone and you've both written an essay neither of you could have alone.|You pass notes over the wall all evening, half about Thorne and half not. She keeps the notes. You see them later, pinned in her book.}\n  if knows(lyra.burnout):\n    rel lyra +tiny\n    say: Near midnight you slide one more note over the wall: \"Bed.\" She reads it twice, glares at you, and, for once, goes.\n> right: Keep to your side\n  study +medium\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: {~You keep to your side and get more done than in a week. So does she. At closing she looks almost disappointed that you didn't break the truce.|Silence, all evening. Your notes have never been better. Lyra packs up first and doesn't say goodnight, then comes back and says it.}\n\n@event lyra_become_close\ntitle: Let It Run\nspecializes: soc_become_close\nslot: night\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nweight: 18\nart: flame\ntags: social\nscene: Two friends on a castle battlement at 3am, one of them making a small flame dance between her fingers, lighting both their faces.\ntext: It's 3am. You find Lyra on the battlements, making a small flame walk across her knuckles. \"I can't sleep,\" she says. \"I've never told anyone that. Vaneshades sleep exactly eight hours.\" The flame wavers. \"My grandmother had a spell she only taught to people she trusted. I've never had anyone to teach it to.\"\n> left: Tell her she doesn't owe you anything\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: \"I know,\" she says. \"That's why I'd want to.\" But she lets the flame go out, and you sit there until the sky greys, and she doesn't mention the spell again, not yet.\n> right: Stay until sunrise\n  bond lyra close\n  rel lyra +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: You talk until the sun comes up over the lake. About her grandmother, about fire that isn't for showing off, about being tired. When the light comes, she says, \"Wildfire. That's the spell. When you're ready.\" She means it as the biggest thing she has.\n\n# ---------------- Tobias ----------------\n\n@event tobias_moonflower_vigil\ntitle: Every Eleven Years\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 12\ntime: evening night\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: social\nscene: A greenhouse at night lit by one lantern, a broad-shouldered student sitting on an upturned pot beside a bed of tightly closed pale buds.\ntext: Tobias is sitting in the greenhouse at night, beside a bed of tight pale buds, with a flask and a blanket. \"Moonflowers,\" he says. \"Once every eleven years. Could be tonight. Could be next spring.\" He's been coming every night for a month.\n> left: Keep watch with him\n  learn tobias.moonflowers\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +medium\n  say: You keep watch until the lantern runs out. They don't bloom. Tobias isn't disappointed. \"That's the point,\" he says. \"You wait for them. They don't wait for you.\" He tucks the blanket round you both.\n> right: Bring him a proper dinner\n  learn tobias.moonflowers\n  gold -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: You bring a pie from the kitchens. He eats it with real gratitude, and tells you about his mother's moonflowers at home, which never bloomed either, and how she kept the bed weeded for twenty years anyway.\n\n@event tobias_moonflower_bloom\ntitle: Tonight\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: knows(tobias.moonflowers) and rel(tobias) >= 15 and year >= 2\ntime: evening night\nweight: 12\nart: leaf\ntags: social\nscene: A greenhouse at midnight full of pale open blossoms glowing faintly silver, a big student standing among them with his mouth open.\ntext: Tobias bangs on your door after dark. \"Tonight,\" he says. \"They're opening. Come on. Come ON.\" He's already running. In the greenhouse the moonflowers are unfolding, one by one, silver at the edges, and the whole place smells like rain.\n> left: Run with him\n  mood +medium\n  rel tobias +small\n  study -small\n  say: You run. You're in time. Every bud opens while you watch, and the greenhouse glows, and Tobias doesn't say a word for an hour. At the end he says, \"Eleven years,\" and then, \"Thank you for waiting with me,\" as if you'd done the waiting. You'll remember it longer than anything you were meant to study tonight.\n> right: Tell him to go; you'll catch up\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You finish your essay first. By the time you get there, half are open and Tobias is crying, quietly, the happy way. He saves you the best one to watch. It opens just for you, he insists. You don't argue.\n\n@event tobias_plants_vote\ntitle: The Greenhouse Votes\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: knows(tobias.plants) and rel(tobias) >= 9\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: leaf\ntags: social\nscene: A big student in a greenhouse holding his ear close to a row of seedlings, another student watching with folded arms.\ntext: You've got a decision to make, and Tobias offers to ask the greenhouse. \"They're good judges,\" he says. \"Plants don't lie. They just turn towards what's good for them.\" He holds a seedling up to your face. {~It leans toward you, slowly.|It leans away, very slightly, and Tobias frowns.}\n> left: Do what the seedling says\n  mood +small\n  say: {~\"Yes, then,\" Tobias says, satisfied. It turns out to be the right call, though you'll never be sure it was the seedling's.|\"Not that, then,\" he says, and you don't, and you never find out what would have happened, which is how good advice usually works.}\n> right: Ask him what he thinks\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: {~\"Me?\" He's surprised. He thinks it through, slowly, the way he does everything. His answer is better than the seedling's. He doesn't tell the seedling.|He gives you an answer so sensible you're embarrassed you asked a plant. \"Don't tell them I said that,\" he whispers.}\n\n@event tobias_scholarship_review\ntitle: The Turnip Board\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 9 and term and not wrapup and year >= 2\nweight: 9\nart: book\ntags: social study\nscene: A big student at a library table with a letter on top of a pile of open textbooks, head in his hands.\ntext: Tobias has a letter from the agricultural scholarship board: a review of his marks, next week, in person. \"If they take it away I go home,\" he says. \"Not that home's bad. But I'm not done here.\" He's opened four textbooks and read none of them.\n> left: Drill him all week\n  learn tobias.scholarship\n  study -small\n  challenge research journeyman:\n    win:\n      rel tobias +large\n      say: You drill him every night. At the review he answers everything, slowly and right, and one of the board members, a farmer herself, shakes his hand. He brings you a turnip. It's the best turnip you've ever had.\n    lose:\n      rel tobias +medium\n      say: You drill him, but the board is hard. He keeps the scholarship on probation. \"Probation's fine,\" he says. \"Probation means I'm still here.\" He hugs you anyway, which lifts you off the floor.\n> right: Write to the board for him\n  learn tobias.scholarship\n  rel tobias +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You write a letter about the boy who keeps the greenhouse alive and talks the mandrakes through exams. He never knows you sent it. The board renews him with a note about \"character\", and he can't think why.\n  if knows(tobias.moonflowers):\n    mood +tiny\n    say: You put in the moonflowers, eleven years of waiting for one night, and how he would stand in a storm for them. The board, it turns out, has farmers on it.\n\n# ---------------- Seraphine ----------------\n\n@event seraphine_oracle_letter\ntitle: A Letter From Vell\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 15\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A girl in a paint-stained smock holding a letter sealed in silver wax at arm's length, as if it were warm.\ntext: Seraphine has a letter with a silver seal. She hasn't opened it. \"My mother,\" she says. \"She's the Oracle of Vell. She tells kings what they want to hear and they pay her in castles. She wants me home, to learn it.\" She turns it over. \"I already know what it says. I see it, a little, through the paper.\"\n> left: Ask what she wants\n  learn seraphine.oracle\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Nobody has asked her that. She thinks for a long time. \"To be wrong sometimes,\" she says. \"Here, I'm wrong about small things. It's lovely.\" She puts the letter in a drawer, unopened. \"She'll write again. She always knows when I've stopped reading.\"\n> right: Offer to read it to her\n  learn seraphine.oracle\n  lore +tiny\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: You read it out. It's exactly what she said it would be, word for word. \"See,\" she says, quietly. \"Nothing in it I didn't know. That's the worst part. Thank you for saying it in someone else's voice.\"\n\n@event seraphine_what_you_want_to_hear\ntitle: What You Want to Hear\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: knows(seraphine.oracle) and rel(seraphine) >= 12\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 9\nart: eye\ntags: social\nscene: A seer holding both of another student's hands across a table, eyes half-closed, a theatrical candle burning between them.\ntext: \"My mother's trick,\" Seraphine says, taking your hands, eyes half-closed. \"Watch. I'll tell you what you want to hear.\" {~\"You will pass every exam, be loved by everyone and find a small dog.\"|\"Your enemies will grow warts and your friends will grow taller.\"} She opens one eye. \"Now. Do you want the true one?\"\n> left: Keep the pretty one\n  mood +small\n  say: {~\"Wise,\" she says. \"Kings always pick that one too.\" She blows out the candle with a flourish. You do, briefly, believe in the small dog.|\"Good choice.\" She looks relieved. \"The true one has a lot of rain in it.\"}\n> right: Ask for the true one\n  rel seraphine +small\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~\"You'll be tired a lot, and it will be worth it.\" She lets go of your hands. \"That's the only true one I'll give anyone. Nobody else asks for it.\"|\"Something will be hard, and you'll do it anyway, and someone will be proud of you.\" She shrugs. \"It's not much. It's true.\"}\n\n@event seraphine_too_loud\ntitle: Too Loud\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 9\nweight: 9\nart: candle\ntags: social\nscene: A crowded refectory at lunchtime, a girl with her hands pressed over her ears in the middle of it, everyone else oblivious.\ntext: In the middle of a full refectory Seraphine puts both hands over her ears and goes white. Nobody notices but you. \"Everyone's futures,\" she whispers, when you get to her. \"All at once. It's like a hundred people shouting what they'll be.\"\n> left: Get her out of there\n  learn seraphine.too_loud\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  study -tiny\n  say: You walk her out into the cloister, where it's quiet. She breathes. \"Yours is quiet,\" she says, after a while. \"I've always meant to say. It's like a room with the door shut.\" You miss your lecture. You don't mind.\n> right: Talk to her, loudly, about nothing\n  learn seraphine.too_loud\n  charm +tiny\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: You talk about the soup, the weather, a dog you once knew, loudly, right next to her. It works: she can hear the now over the noise. \"Keep going,\" she says, eyes shut. You talk about soup for twenty minutes.\n\n# ---------------- Corvin ----------------\n\n@event corvin_lock_lesson\ntitle: Every Door\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 9\ntime: evening night\nweight: 10\nart: key\ntags: social\nscene: A lanky student crouched at a classroom door after dark, two thin picks in the lock, another student holding the lantern.\ntext: Corvin is crouched at the Alchemy store's door after dark, two picks in the lock. \"Hold that lantern up,\" he says, as if you'd agreed. \"There isn't a locked door in this castle I can't open. I'm not taking anything. I'm keeping in practice.\"\n> left: Ask him to teach you\n  learn corvin.locks\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: He shows you how to feel the pins, one at a time. On your eleventh try the lock clicks. Corvin applauds silently. Then he locks it again, carefully, and leaves it exactly as it was. \"Rule one,\" he says. \"Always leave it how you found it.\"\n> right: Keep watch instead\n  learn corvin.locks\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: You keep watch. He's in and out in a minute, with nothing in his hands but a single sugared almond from the jar on the master's desk. \"Tradition,\" he says. He splits it with you.\n\n@event corvin_takes_the_blame\ntitle: One More Detention\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: knows(corvin.last_warning) and rel(corvin) >= 12 and term\nweight: 10\nart: mask\ntags: social\nscene: A stern professor in a corridor holding up a confiscated set of lock picks, two students in front of her, one of them grinning too widely.\ntext: Professor Vey holds up a set of lock picks found outside the Restricted Section. \"Whose?\" Corvin opens his mouth to make a joke, and you remember: one more detention and he's sent home, and home is not a good place.\n> left: \"They're mine.\"\n  gain detention\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel corvin +large\n  say: Vey looks at you for a long moment, and at Corvin, and writes your name down. Corvin doesn't speak to you all day. That night there's a note under your door. It says \"I owe you\" and, underneath, smaller, \"I'm not joking.\"\n> right: Let him talk his way out\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  chance even:\n    say: He talks. It's a masterpiece: picks, a jackdaw, a misunderstanding about a cupboard. Vey, who is in a good mood today, doesn't believe a word and lets him go anyway, for the performance.\n  else:\n    rel corvin -small\n    say: He talks, and Vey, who is not in a good mood today, isn't having it. She can't prove the picks are his, so there's no detention; she keeps them, and writes his name in a little book. He laughs it off. His hands shake for the rest of the day.\n\n@event corvin_cartographer_hint\ntitle: The Club That Doesn't Exist\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 12 and not member(cartographers)\nweight: 9\nart: map\ntags: social\nscene: A folded map slipped into a book in a library, the margins full of tiny inked doors, a lanky student pretending not to have left it.\ntext: There's a map in your library book that wasn't there before. Corridors you've never seen, inked in a careful hand, and in the corner, very small: \"M.C. — we don't exist. Bring a lantern.\" Corvin, across the table, is reading upside down.\n> left: Ask him outright\n  learn corvin.cartographers\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: \"Never heard of them,\" he says. \"Terrible people. Midnight, the third gargoyle on the left. Don't bring anyone.\" He goes back to his upside-down book. He's grinning at it.\n> right: Follow the map alone\n  learn corvin.cartographers\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You follow it into a passage that smells of old candle wax, and bark your shin on a step that isn't on the map. At the end, someone has chalked \"TOLD YOU\" on the wall. It's in Corvin's handwriting.\n\n# ---------------- Mir ----------------\n\n@event mir_small_fire\ntitle: A Small Fire\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 6\nweight: 9\nart: gear\ntags: social\nscene: A workbench with a tiny cheerful fire burning in the middle of it, an artificer in goggles looking at it with professional interest rather than alarm.\ntext: There's a small fire on Mir's workbench. Mir is looking at it with interest. \"It's fine,\" they say. \"It's a controlled fire. I'm controlling it by watching it.\" The fire grows. \"I'm watching it harder.\"\n> left: Put it out\n  learn mir.artificer\n  health -tiny\n  rel mir +small\n  say: You smother it with a lab coat. Mir sighs, and writes \"fire: yes\" in a notebook, under a long column of \"fire: no\". \"That's useful data,\" they say, sincerely. \"Thank you for ruining it.\"\n> right: Ask what it was meant to be\n  learn mir.artificer\n  arcana +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  rel mir -tiny\n  say: \"A kettle,\" they say, as the fire eats the kettle. \"A self-heating kettle.\" The bucket under the bench, it turns out, is always full for exactly this reason.\n\n@event mir_padlock\ntitle: The Padlock\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: flag(mir_workshop_shut) and rel(mir) >= 12 and bond(mir) != \"partner\"\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: social\nscene: An artificer sitting on the floor outside a padlocked door, back against it, notebooks stacked beside them, a student coming down the corridor with a box.\ntext: Mir's workshop is still padlocked, and whatever they've been making do with isn't working: they've been soldering in the corridor outside the old door, on the floor, for a week. \"It's fine,\" they say, soldering on their knee. \"It's character-building. My character is very built.\"\n> left: Give them your desk\n  rel mir +large\n  study -small\n  say: You clear the desk in your room and carry their boxes up four flights. Mir moves in within the hour. The first thing they build there is a sign: \"Workshop. Fires: probably fine.\" You do your reading on the stairs for a month.\n> right: Ward the workshop and prove it\n  hint: Make the old room safe, and show Thorne.\n  rel mir +small\n  challenge spell journeyman:\n    win:\n      unflag mir_workshop_shut\n      renown +tiny\n      say: You spend a week warding every bench. Thorne lights a match on the worst one, watches it go out on its own, sighs like a dying bellows and hands Mir the key. \"Bucket within reach,\" he says. They keep three.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The ward holds until it doesn't. Thorne puts out the bench with one hand and files the request with the other. But you sit in the corridor with Mir that evening and hand them screws, and it's less fine with company.\n\n@event mir_family_clocks\ntitle: Not a Hobby\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: flag(mir_family_done) and rel(mir) >= 15\nweight: 9\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A letter on a workbench, held down by a small brass clock, the artificer reading it with their goggles pushed up and their jaw tight.\ntext: {flag(mir_family_wrote): Mir's mother has named her prize: the guild medal for young artificers, entries closing in a fortnight. Mir has three inventions half-built and no time. \"Win a prize,\" they mutter, to a clock. \"Win a prize.\"|A letter from Mir's father, the first he's ever written them himself: could they send something they've made, to show the men at the clockworks? Mir has started the reply four times.}\n> left: Help them write it up\n  charm +small\n  rel mir +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {flag(mir_family_wrote): You write up the entry together, all evening: what it does, why it matters, who'll use it. It's three pages and not one apology. \"Whatever the judges say,\" Mir says, \"it's finished.\"|You write it together, all evening: what Mir built this year, what it does, who uses it. Three pages of inventions and not one apology. \"He'll read every word,\" Mir says. \"Twice. He reads instructions twice.\"}\n> right: Tell them to send the owl\n  rel mir +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {flag(mir_family_wrote): They send in the brass owl. It comes second. Mir's mother writes back one line: \"Second is a prize.\" Mir frames it.|Mir looks at the brass owl on the shelf for a long time. Then they wrap it and post it. The letter back is very short: \"Dad cried. He says it's not a hobby. I said I know.\"} You miss the owl on the shelf.\n\n# ---------------- Kael ----------------\n\n@event kael_northern_supper\ntitle: A Northern Supper\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 9\ntime: evening night\nweight: 9\nart: snow\ntags: social\nscene: A pale boy in a fur coat at the kitchen hearth late at night, carefully stirring a pot of dark broth, a second bowl set out without being asked for.\ntext: You find Kael in the castle kitchens after hours, cooking something dark and sour-smelling over the smallest fire. \"Northern,\" he says. \"Fish, berries, and something you don't want to know. Nobody here makes it.\" There's already a second bowl out.\n> left: Eat it all\n  learn kael.north\n  health +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  rel kael +medium\n  say: It's the strangest thing you've ever eaten. You finish the bowl. Kael watches you with an expression you've never seen on him: hope. \"Next time,\" he says, \"I'll tell you what was in it.\" You hope he doesn't.\n> right: Ask him to tell you about home\n  learn kael.north\n  lore +small\n  rel kael +small\n  say: He tells you about winter that lasts eight months and summer that lasts eight days, and singing on the ice to keep the dark back. He doesn't mention his family once. You notice. You don't ask.\n\n@event kael_gloves\ntitle: Gloves\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: knows(kael.cold_hands) and rel(kael) >= 12 and bond(kael) != \"partner\"\nweight: 9\nart: snow\ntags: social\nscene: A pair of thick knitted gloves, slightly lopsided, on a pale boy's hands as he turns them over and over, frost beginning to form on the wool.\ntext: Kael's hands are so cold the ink in his pen keeps freezing in lectures. You've noticed. He's noticed you noticing. Winter's coming, and he still doesn't own a pair of gloves.\n> left: Knit him some\n  study -small\n  rel kael +large\n  say: They take a month and are lopsided. Frost forms on the wool within the hour. He wears them anyway, every day, until the thumbs wear through, and then he darns the thumbs himself, badly, rather than give them up.\n> right: Buy him some in the village\n  gold -small\n  rel kael +medium\n  say: You buy a good warm pair. He holds them as if no one had ever given him a thing just for being cold. \"I don't feel it, mostly,\" he says. He wears them anyway.\n\n@event kael_frost_fern\ntitle: The Frost-Fern Trick\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 12 and season == \"winter\"\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: snow\ntags: social magic\nscene: A frosted window in a common room, a pale boy and another student drawing on it with their fingertips, ferns of ice spreading from each line.\ntext: Kael shows you a children's trick from the North: you draw on a frosted window and the frost finishes the drawing for you. {~He draws a line; the frost turns it into a fern.|You draw a circle; the frost turns it into a snowflake the size of your hand.}\n> left: Draw something silly\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You draw a goat. The frost, after a moment's thought, gives it a magnificent beard. Kael makes a sound you've never heard from him. It's a laugh.|You draw Professor Thorne. The frost gives him icicles for eyebrows. Kael has to sit down.}\n> right: Ask him how it works\n  arcana +small\n  rel kael +tiny\n  say: {~\"You don't make it,\" he says. \"You let it. Frost wants to grow. You show it where.\" He's a better teacher than he thinks.|He explains, slowly, and then guides your hand. The frost follows your finger like a cat. \"There,\" he says. \"You're Northern now. A bit.\"}\n\n# ---------------- Nessa ----------------\n\n@event nessa_interview_prep\ntitle: Twenty Years\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: knows(nessa.interview) and rel(nessa) >= 12 and seen(nessa_headmistress_interview) == 0\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: social\nscene: A girl in an untidy office with a long list of questions pinned to a wall, crossing out most of them, another student reading over her shoulder.\ntext: Nessa has two hundred questions for the headmistress, pinned to her wall. \"Nobody's interviewed her in twenty years,\" she says. \"I'll get one chance. I have to pick the question that matters.\" She's crossed out a hundred and ninety.\n> left: Help her pick\n  lore +small\n  rel nessa +small\n  say: You go through the last ten together until midnight. The one you pick is very simple: \"What do you wish students asked you?\" Nessa stares at it. \"That's either the best question I've ever asked or the worst,\" she says. \"Good.\"\n> right: Play the headmistress for practice\n  charm +small\n  rel nessa +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You play the headmistress, badly, with a pencil for a wand. Nessa grills you for an hour and makes you cry with laughter twice and nearly cry the other way once. \"She'll be harder,\" she says. \"She'll be you, but calmer.\"\n\n@event nessa_fact_check\ntitle: Fact-Check\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 9 and term\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 8\nart: paper\ntags: social study\nscene: A newspaper proof covered in red-ink circles, a girl handing it across a desk to another student with a pencil behind her ear.\ntext: Nessa slaps a proof down in front of you. \"Fact-check it. I don't trust my quill and I don't trust me.\" {~It's an article about the history of the castle's founding.|It's a profile of the Duelling Circle's captain.} There are, she warns you, at least three errors, and one of them is planted, to see if you're paying attention.\n> left: Go through it properly\n  challenge research journeyman:\n    win:\n      rel nessa +small\n      lore +small\n      say: {~You find all three, and a fourth she didn't plant. Nessa looks at you with something close to love, the editorial kind. \"Staff,\" she says. \"You're staff now.\"|You find the planted one first, then the others. Nessa writes \"GOOD\" on your hand in red ink. It takes three days to wash off.}\n    lose:\n      lore +tiny\n      say: {~You find two. The planted one gets past you. Nessa circles it, gleefully, and tells you why it was obvious. You learn more from that than from the article.|You find one, and mark a true fact as wrong. Nessa is delighted: now she has to check it again, and it's still true, and she likes being right twice.}\n> right: Hand it back: \"It's fine.\"\n  mood +tiny\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  say: {~\"Nothing's fine,\" Nessa says. She takes it back and finds all three in five minutes. \"Lazy,\" she says, but she's smiling.|She looks at you over the proof. \"That's what my quill says,\" she says. \"I don't trust it either.\"}\n\n@event nessa_quill_loose\ntitle: The Quill Gets Loose\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: knows(nessa.quill) and rel(nessa) >= 9\nweight: 9\nart: feather\ntags: social\nscene: An enchanted quill flying down a castle corridor trailing a long scroll, a girl chasing it with an inkpot, students ducking.\ntext: Nessa's quill has escaped. It's loose in the castle, writing news. You've seen three of its headlines on the walls already: \"PROFESSOR'S CAT IS SECRETLY A PROFESSOR\", \"LAKE: TOO WET?\", and one about you that isn't true but is, you have to admit, flattering.\n> left: Help her catch it\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel nessa +small\n  say: You corner it in the owlery. It writes \"CAUGHT\" on your sleeve as you grab it. Nessa stuffs it in her inkpot and screws the lid on. \"Thank you,\" she says, breathless. \"It was going to do a horoscope page. Again.\"\n> right: Leave the headline about you up\n  renown +small\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You leave it up. People read it for a week. Nessa takes it down eventually, with a look. \"It made it up,\" she says. \"You know that.\" You do. It's still a lovely headline.\n\n# ---------------- Bram ----------------\n\n@event bram_portrait\ntitle: Head Boy, Forty Years Ago\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: knows(bram.father) and rel(bram) >= 6 and bond(bram) != \"rival\" and bond(bram) != \"nemesis\"\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: social\nscene: A row of old portraits of head boys in a dim corridor, a big student in a prefect's sash standing in front of one, not moving.\ntext: You find Bram in the corridor of head boys' portraits, in front of one in particular: a big man with Bram's jaw and none of his doubt. \"He walks past this every visit,\" Bram says. \"Stops. Looks at it. Then looks at me.\" He doesn't need to say the rest.\n> left: Say the portrait's a bad likeness\n  rel bram +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"It is,\" Bram says, surprised into it. \"Painter made him look kind.\" He laughs, short and rough, and it changes his face. \"Don't tell anyone I laughed at a portrait.\"\n> right: Ask what he wants to be\n  rel bram +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: He's never been asked. \"Not that,\" he says, nodding at the portrait. Then, after a long time: \"A teacher. Small school. Somewhere nobody's ever heard of my father.\" He walks off before you can answer. He looks lighter.\n\n@event bram_owns_up\ntitle: Bram Owns Up\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: knows(bram.cheating) and flag(bram_softened) and rel(bram) >= 9\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: social\nscene: A big student in a prefect's sash standing in front of a professor's desk, cap in hand, another student waiting outside the door.\ntext: Bram finds you. \"That exam,\" he says. \"You saw. You never said.\" He's holding his sash. \"I'm going to tell Thorne. I wanted you to know first. In case you wanted to stop me, I suppose.\" He doesn't look like he wants to be stopped.\n> left: Go in with him\n  rel bram +large\n  study -tiny\n  say: You stand by the door while he tells Thorne everything. Thorne listens, takes the sash, looks at it for a while, and hands it back. \"Resit in a month,\" he says. \"Wear it like you mean it.\" Bram walks out shaking. \"Don't,\" he says. \"Don't make it weird.\" You don't.\n> right: Tell him it's past\n  rel bram +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: \"It's done,\" you say. \"Leave it.\" He hesitates, then puts the sash back on. You can see it sits differently. He doesn't cheat again; you'd bet on it. He doesn't forget, either.\n\n@event bram_become_close\ntitle: Not Weird\nspecializes: soc_become_close\nslot: night\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nweight: 18\nart: shield\ntags: social\nscene: A big student sitting on the stable fence at 3am beside another, a red sash folded in his lap, the hippogriffs asleep behind them.\ntext: It's 3am and you can't sleep. Bram is on the stable fence, sash in his lap. \"Night rounds,\" he says, which is a lie; rounds ended at midnight. \"Sit if you want. Don't talk if you don't want.\" You sit. After a while he says, \"You're the only one who didn't believe I was him.\"\n> left: Leave him be\n  rel bram +small\n  say: You sit a while longer and then go. He says goodnight like it's an order. In the morning your demerits for the week have been crossed out, with no explanation.\n> right: Stay until morning\n  bond bram close\n  rel bram +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: You stay. He talks about his mother, who's kind and doesn't say much, and the dog he had as a boy, and what he's afraid of, which is everything. At sunrise he says, \"This is weird.\" Then: \"It's fine. It's fine if it's weird.\"\n\n# ---------------- Ilse ----------------\n\n@event ilse_frozen_coins\ntitle: Coins That Freeze\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: met(ilse) and rel(ilse) >= 3\nweight: 9\nart: snow\ntags: social\nscene: A teashop counter with a coin frozen to the wood, a girl in white furs glaring at it, a teashop owner holding a kettle over it hopefully.\ntext: Ilse is at Mabel's counter again, glaring at a coin that has frozen to the wood. Mabel is pouring hot water on it. It isn't working. \"It does this,\" Ilse says, through her teeth. \"When I'm cross. I'm cross because it does this.\"\n> left: Pay, and warm the coin\n  learn ilse.furs\n  gold -tiny\n  rel ilse +small\n  say: You pay. Then you take the frozen coin and carry it in your pocket all afternoon, and give it back to her warm. She looks at it for a long time. \"That's stupid,\" she says. She keeps it, separately from the others.\n> right: Make her laugh until it thaws\n  learn ilse.furs\n  charm +small\n  rel ilse +tiny\n  say: You do Thorne's voice. Then Bram's. On Bram, the coin cracks free of the counter with a pop, and Ilse makes a noise she clearly hadn't planned to make. \"Again,\" she says. \"No. Don't. Again.\"\n\n@event ilse_private_storm\ntitle: Her Weather\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: met(ilse) and rel(ilse) >= 9\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: snow\ntags: social\nscene: A girl in white furs walking through the village under a small private snowstorm that follows her, a second student stepping under it with her.\ntext: Ilse's private snowstorm follows her about the village. You've learned to read it. {~Today it's thick and sideways, which means stay away.|Today it's three flakes, slow, which means something's wrong and she won't say what.}\n> left: Step under it with her\n  health -tiny\n  rel ilse +small\n  say: {~You walk beside her, inside the storm, getting snowed on. She doesn't say anything. After a street, the snow slows. After two, it stops. \"Don't read it,\" she says. \"It's rude.\" You read it anyway. It says: better.|You walk with her, getting snow down your collar. At the bridge she says, \"My brother used to do that,\" and the three flakes become one, and then none.}\n> right: Wait it out at a distance\n  mood +tiny\n  rel ilse -tiny\n  say: {~You give her room. The storm passes in an hour. She nods to you later across the square, which, for Ilse, is an apology for the weather.|You let her be. Later, a single snowflake lands on your sleeve, perfectly formed, and doesn't melt until you're indoors. You think it's a thank you.}\n\n@event ilse_frost_craft_trade\ntitle: A Fair Trade\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: knows(ilse.frost_craft) and rel(ilse) >= 12\nweight: 9\nart: snow\ntags: social magic\nscene: A frozen village pond, a girl in white furs teaching another student to throw a ring of frost across the ice, both breathing clouds.\ntext: \"Teach me something,\" Ilse says, at the pond. \"Something of yours. I taught you frost. It's your turn. Something that isn't useful.\" She seems to think that's the hardest kind.\n> left: Teach her a card game\n  charm +tiny\n  rel ilse +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You teach her a card game from home. She's terrible, and then, suddenly, ruthless. She beats you six times running on the ice and laughs out loud, once, before she catches herself. \"That's not useful,\" she says. \"Good.\"\n> right: Teach her to skip stones\n  vigor +tiny\n  rel ilse +small\n  say: There's no open water, so you skip stones across the ice. Hers go further. Of course they do. She counts every bounce out loud, in Northern, and makes you count yours too.\n\n@event ilse_become_close\ntitle: Not a Trick\nspecializes: soc_become_close\nslot: night\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nweight: 18\nart: snow\ntags: social\nscene: A girl in white furs and a student sitting on the frozen lakeshore at 3am under a sky full of stars, snow falling very gently around only the two of them.\ntext: It's 3am. You walk down to the shore, and Ilse is already there, on the ice, under her own small snowfall. \"You keep being kind,\" she says, not looking at you. \"I keep waiting to find out what it's for.\"\n> left: Tell her it isn't for anything, and go\n  rel ilse +small\n  say: \"Everything's for something,\" she says. But she lets you go without an argument, which is new. In the morning there's a small perfect snowflake on your windowsill that doesn't melt.\n> right: Stay, and let her wait\n  bond ilse close\n  rel ilse +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: You stay. Neither of you says much. Around dawn the snow around her changes: it starts falling on you too, gently, the same as on her. She notices. \"Oh,\" she says, very quietly, as if she's just learned a word. \"Oh. It isn't a trick.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/bonds/rivals.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Rivalry (world expansion 1, plan §2b): Lyra and Bram, the two written rivals, get a rivalry worth\n#  having (eight cards each, with the generic truce no longer pre-empting them: migration 0040); Corvin\n#  and Ilse, who are never rivals, get two cards each for when things have soured.\n#  Lyra's way out is lyra_rival_respect; Bram's is bram_turns, opened by bram_crying or bram_steps_in.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Lyra ----------------\n\n@event lyra_rankings_war\ntitle: The Rankings War\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"rival\" and term\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: rival study\nscene: A noticeboard outside the Great Hall with the term's rankings pinned up, two names side by side at the top, a red-haired student tapping one with her wand.\ntext: The rankings are up outside the Great Hall. {~You're one place above Lyra.|Lyra is one place above you.} She's standing in front of the list when you get there, and she doesn't move aside. \"Enjoy it,\" she says. \"It's the last time.\"\n> left: Out-study her this term\n  study +medium\n  health -small\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: {~You take the library table opposite hers and don't leave it for a month. She doesn't either. Thorne remarks that he's never seen the library so tense. The next list has you in the same order. Neither of you is satisfied.|You study every night. So does she; you can see her lamp from your window. It's the best work either of you has done, and you'd both rather die than say so.}\n> right: Leave a note on her desk\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  rel lyra -small\n  say: {~\"Second suits you.\" She finds it in front of the whole class, and goes the colour of her hair. The next morning there's a note on yours: \"Says the person who'll be third.\"|You leave a single word: \"Close.\" It drives her mad for a week. It was meant to.}\n\n@pool rivals_lyra_social_routine\nactor: lyra\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nweight: 10\ntags: rival\n\n@event lyra_sabotage_returned\ntitle: Pink Bubbles, Returned\npool: rivals_lyra_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"rival\" and chosen(lyra_rival_sabotage, left)\nart: potion\nscene: A student's wand producing a cloud of blue smoke shaped like a donkey in the middle of a lecture, a red-haired student three rows away not looking.\ntext: In the middle of Thorne's lecture your wand produces a cloud of blue smoke shaped like a donkey. Three rows away, Lyra is taking very careful notes. It's your move. It's always your move.\n> left: Call a ceasefire\n  rel lyra +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You slip her a note: \"Truce? I'm running out of eyebrows.\" She reads it twice, and writes underneath: \"Truce. For now. The donkey was very good though.\" It was.\n> right: Escalate\n  challenge deceive vs lyra.guile:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel lyra -small\n      say: Her next spell comes out as a choir of tiny frogs singing her family motto. The whole class applauds. Lyra stands there with her wand out, absolutely white, and then, unbelievably, starts to laugh.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel lyra -small\n      say: She sees it coming a mile off and turns it round. You spend detention scrubbing cauldrons. She spends it in the next room, also scrubbing cauldrons, for the donkey.\n\n@event lyra_glove_thrown\ntitle: The Glove Thrown\npool: rivals_lyra_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"rival\" and rel(lyra) <= 0\nart: duel\ntags: danger\nscene: A white duelling glove on the flagstones of a crowded courtyard, a red-haired student standing over it with her wand drawn, a ring of watching students.\ntext: Lyra drops a glove at your feet in the courtyard, in front of everyone. \"The ring. Tonight. No referee but the crowd.\" She's been waiting for this all term. So, if you're honest, have you.\n> left: Leave the glove where it lies\n  renown -small\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: You step over it. The crowd mutters. Lyra picks it up herself, very slowly. \"Next time,\" she says. \"There's always a next time.\" There is.\n> right: Pick it up\n  challenge combat vs lyra.arcana +1:\n    win:\n      renown +large\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: You win, barely, in front of half the school. Lyra gets up off the flagstones and, after a long moment, holds out the glove. \"Keep it,\" she says. \"Until I win it back.\"\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      renown +small\n      say: She wins, and it costs her: she's limping, and she knows you made her work for every point. \"Good,\" she says, breathing hard. \"Good. Again, soon.\"\n\n@event lyra_allies_thornwick\ntitle: Allies, for an Afternoon\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: (bond(lyra) == \"rival\" or rel(lyra) <= -6) and seen(life_rival_academy) > 0 and around(lyra)\nweight: 9\nart: flame\ntags: rival\nscene: Two rival students standing shoulder to shoulder in a corridor facing a group of visitors in green Thornwick colours, both wands half-drawn.\ntext: Three Thornwick students in green are laughing at the Lumenhold crest in the entrance hall. Lyra hears it at the same moment you do. She looks at you. You look at her. For the first time all year, you're on the same side.\n> left: Stand with her\n  rel lyra +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: You stand shoulder to shoulder. The Thornwick students take one look at the two of you and remember an appointment elsewhere, though the last one flicks a jinx at your ankles on the way out, and it stings for an hour. Lyra doesn't thank you. She does walk with you as far as the stairs, which is unheard of.\n> right: Let her handle it\n  study +small\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: You leave her to it and get an hour's reading done in the library. She handles it alone, magnificently, and you hear about it at dinner from four different people. \"You missed it,\" she says, passing. She sounds as if she minds.\n\n@event lyra_mother_watches\ntitle: Her Mother Watches You Win\npool: rivals_lyra_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"rival\" and year >= 2\nart: duel\nscene: An exhibition duelling ring with a gallery above, an elegant woman in black watching a red-haired student who is losing.\ntext: An exhibition bout, and you're paired with Lyra. Up in the gallery, in black, is Lady Vaneshade. Halfway through, you're winning, clearly, and Lyra knows it, and you see her glance up once at the gallery and then not again.\n> left: Win it\n  renown +medium\n  rel lyra -small\n  say: You win cleanly. The gallery applauds. Lady Vaneshade doesn't. She leaves before the bows. Lyra watches her go, and then turns and shakes your hand properly. \"Don't you dare go easy on me,\" she says. \"Ever.\" You won't.\n> right: Let her come back\n  rel lyra +medium\n  renown -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You slow down, just enough. Lyra wins. In the gallery, her mother nods once. Afterwards Lyra corners you, furious. \"You let me,\" she says. Then, so quietly you nearly miss it: \"Thank you. Never again.\"\n\n@event lyra_rival_letter\ntitle: A Rival's Letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: letter\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"rival\" and break\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: rival letter\nscene: A letter with a black Vaneshade seal on a windowsill, inside it a neatly ruled table of marks with one line underlined twice.\ntext: A letter with the Vaneshade seal. Inside: a ruled table of her term marks, beside yours, with every subject she beat you in underlined. {~At the bottom: \"Enjoy your holiday. I'm not taking one.\"|At the bottom: \"You're welcome to check my sums. They're right.\"}\n> left: Write back with corrections\n  lore +small\n  rel lyra +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You find one mistake in her sums, in your favour, and write back about it at length. Her reply is two words: \"Noted. Grrr.\"|You send back her table with your own column added: hours slept. She doesn't answer for a week. Then: \"Fine. I'll sleep. Some.\"}\n> right: Pin it above your desk\n  mood +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: {~You pin it up where you'll see it every morning. It works better than any timetable.|Up it goes, above your desk. Every time you want to stop, there it is, underlined twice.}\n\n@event lyra_last_match\ntitle: The Last Rankings\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"rival\" and final_year and month >= 6\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: rival\nscene: A final rankings list on a noticeboard, two names tied at the top, two students standing in front of it in silence.\ntext: The last rankings you'll ever see at Lumenhold. You and Lyra are tied. Exactly. She stands in front of the list for a long time. \"Four years,\" she says. \"And it comes to that.\" She doesn't sound angry. She sounds almost happy.\n> left: Share it\n  rel lyra +large\n  renown -tiny\n  say: \"Joint,\" you say. She looks at you, and then holds out her hand. You shake. \"It was never going to be anyone else,\" she says. \"I'm glad it was you I didn't beat.\"\n  if knows(lyra.mother):\n    rel lyra +tiny\n    say: Then you say \"well done\", because somebody should, once. She doesn't answer. She holds on to your hand a moment longer.\n> right: Ask Thorne for a decider\n  challenge research expert-:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: Thorne sets you both one question, the hardest he has. You get it by a hair. Lyra reads your answer, and nods, and says, \"That's right. That's annoyingly right.\" She's proud of you. She'll never say it.\n    lose:\n      renown +small\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: Thorne sets you both one question. She gets it. You don't. She doesn't gloat, which is how you know she means it when she says, \"Thank you. That's the only one I wanted.\"\n\n# ---------------- Bram ----------------\n\n@event bram_marked\ntitle: Top of the Ledger\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: year == 1 and month >= 2 and month <= 3 and bond(bram) == \"none\" and rel(bram) <= -12\nweight: 14\nart: shield\ntags: rival\nscene: A big student in a red prefect's sash writing a name at the top of a demerit ledger with great ceremony, another student watching from a doorway.\ntext: Bram opens his demerit ledger in the middle of the corridor and writes your name at the top of a fresh page, slowly, so you can watch him do it. \"A page of your own,\" he says. \"You've earned it.\"\n> left: Keep your head down\n  rel bram +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You walk past without a word. He looks almost disappointed. The page stays empty for a while. It doesn't stay empty for long.\n> right: Meet his eye\n  bond bram rival\n  renown +tiny\n  rel bram -tiny\n  say: You stop, and look at him, and don't look away. \"Good,\" says Bram, and snaps the ledger shut. \"I was hoping you would.\" It's war, and you're both, somehow, pleased about it.\n\n@event bram_demerit_ledger\ntitle: The Demerit Ledger\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: bond(bram) == \"rival\" or bond(bram) == \"nemesis\"\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: rival\nscene: A prefect's ledger open on a windowsill, a column of demerits next to one name, each with a reason in neat block capitals.\ntext: Bram has given you a demerit for {~\"walking in a smug manner\".|\"breathing in a provocative direction\".} It's the fourth this month. The ledger is starting to look like a novel.\n> left: Accept it with dignity\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {~You thank him for his diligence. He has no idea what to do with that. He writes \"THANKED ME\" in the margin, puzzled.|You nod gravely and walk on. He follows you for a corridor, waiting for you to crack. You don't.}\n  if knows(bram.hall_tax):\n    rel bram +tiny\n    say: As you go, you add that at least it isn't a hall tax. He goes red to the ears.\n> right: Appeal it to his face\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      renown +tiny\n      rel bram -tiny\n      say: {~You point out, with diagrams, that it's impossible to walk smugly. A crowd gathers. Bram crosses it out to make you stop.|You argue it so well that two first-years applaud. Bram crosses it out, then gives you a fresh one for \"public speaking\".}\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      say: {~\"Arguing with a prefect,\" Bram says happily, writing. \"That's a detention.\" It is.|You argue, and he writes, and the demerit becomes a detention. \"Thank you,\" he says. \"That's my quota.\"}\n\n@pool rivals_bram_social_routine\nactor: bram\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nweight: 9\ntags: rival\n\n@event bram_inspection\ntitle: Inspection\npool: rivals_bram_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: bond(bram) == \"rival\" or bond(bram) == \"nemesis\"\nart: shield\nscene: A big prefect going through a student's dormitory trunk with exaggerated care, holding up a sock with two fingers, the owner standing in the doorway.\ntext: \"Room inspection,\" Bram announces, at your door, with a clipboard. There is no such thing as room inspection. He's already in. He's already opening your trunk.\n> left: Let him search\n  mood -small\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain detention\n    say: He finds the kitchen's missing jar of honey under your bed. You have no idea how it got there. He does, you suspect. Detention.\n  else:\n    rel bram +tiny\n    say: He finds nothing worse than socks. He's almost disappointed. On his way out he straightens your books, without thinking, and then glares at you as if you made him do it.\n> right: Inspect him back\n  challenge deceive vs bram.guile:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel bram -small\n      say: While he's in your trunk, you inspect his clipboard. It's mostly a drawing of a horse, quite good. You hold it up for the corridor to see. He leaves. Quickly.\n    lose:\n      rel bram -small\n      gain detention\n      say: He catches you reading his clipboard. \"Interfering with an inspection,\" he says. \"Detention.\" He covers the clipboard with his hand as he goes.\n\n@event bram_rumour\ntitle: The Rumour He Started\npool: rivals_bram_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: (bond(bram) == \"rival\" or bond(bram) == \"nemesis\") and renown >= 10\nart: paper\nscene: Students whispering behind their hands in a corridor as one student passes, a big prefect at the far end leaning on a wall, looking innocent.\ntext: There's a rumour going round that you cried at the Sorting Mirror. There's a rumour that you're afraid of owls. People are being very nice to you about the owls. You know exactly where it started.\n> left: Rise above it\n  renown -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You let it run. It dies in a fortnight, as rumours do. Bram looks faintly cheated. You catch him later telling a first-year that the owl one wasn't true, quite firmly.\n> right: Start one back\n  alignment -tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  rel bram -medium\n  say: By Friday the whole school believes Bram sleeps with a night-light shaped like a duck. He doesn't deny it fast enough. It is, it turns out, true.\n\n@event bram_hall_tax_escalated\ntitle: The Hall Tax, Escalated\npool: rivals_bram_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: knows(bram.hall_tax) and rel(bram) < 0 and not flag(bram_softened)\nart: coin\nscene: A big prefect standing at the foot of a second staircase with a collecting tin, a hand-lettered sign beside him reading WEST STAIRS, a queue of first-years.\ntext: The hall tax has spread. There's a tin at the foot of the west stairs now, too, and a hand-lettered sign, and Bram behind it looking enormously pleased with himself. A queue of first-years are emptying their pockets.\n> left: Pay, and the first-years' too\n  gold -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel bram -tiny\n  say: You drop in enough for the whole queue. The first-years look at you like you've slain a dragon. Bram looks at the tin, and at you, and for some reason shuts it. The sign is gone the next day.\n> right: Organise a boycott\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel bram -small\n      say: By lunch, nobody in the castle is using the west stairs. They're using the windows, a laundry chute and, in one case, a broom. Bram stands by his empty tin until dinner. Then he quietly takes the sign down.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      rel bram -tiny\n      say: Nobody joins your boycott. The stairs are the shortest way to lunch. Bram waves at you, cheerfully, from behind his tin.\n\n@event bram_after_curfew\ntitle: Caught After Curfew\npool: rivals_bram_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: bond(bram) == \"rival\" or bond(bram) == \"nemesis\"\ntime: night\nart: candle\nscene: A dark castle corridor at night, a lantern held high by a big prefect, a student caught halfway up a staircase.\ntext: Bram's lantern catches you halfway up the north stairs, an hour after curfew. He smiles, slowly. He's been waiting all year for this. \"Well,\" he says. \"Well, well, well.\"\n> left: Take the detention\n  gain detention\n  rel bram +tiny\n  say: \"Fair,\" you say. It throws him; he'd prepared a speech. He writes you up. Then, as you go, he says, \"Use the east stairs next time. They don't creak.\" You decide not to wonder why he told you.\n> right: Talk your way past\n  challenge deceive vs bram.guile:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      rel bram -small\n      say: You tell him Professor Vey sent you for a book. He doesn't believe you. But he can't prove it, and he's not brave enough to ask Vey. He lets you go, fuming.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel bram -small\n      say: You try a story about sleepwalking. He lets you finish. Then he writes you up for curfew and for \"insulting my intelligence\", which he underlines.\n\n@event bram_rematch\ntitle: The Rematch\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: (bond(bram) == \"rival\" or bond(bram) == \"nemesis\") and flag(beat_bram_duel)\nweight: 10\nart: duel\ntags: rival danger\nscene: The duelling green at dusk, a big student in a prefect's sash pulling on his gloves, a crowd forming a ring around him.\ntext: Bram has been practising. Everyone knows; you've heard him on the duelling green every evening for a month. Tonight he's waiting for you there. \"Last time doesn't count,\" he says. \"Again. Properly.\"\n> left: Not tonight\n  renown -tiny\n  rel bram -tiny\n  say: \"Scared,\" says Bram, but he doesn't sound sure. He practises alone until dark. You can hear him from your window.\n> right: Accept\n  challenge combat vs bram.vigor +1:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      rel bram +small\n      say: He's better. Much better. You win anyway, by one exchange. He lies on the grass looking at the sky for a while. \"A month,\" he says. \"A whole month.\" Then he laughs, and you help him up, and he doesn't let go of your hand at once.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel bram +small\n      say: He wins. He stands over you, breathing hard, and doesn't gloat, and holds out a hand. \"Now we're even,\" he says. \"Now it's interesting.\"\n\n@event bram_steps_in\ntitle: He Steps In\npool: rivals_bram_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: (bond(bram) == \"rival\" or bond(bram) == \"nemesis\") and not flag(bram_softened)\nart: shield\nscene: A big prefect standing between two older students and a frightened first-year at the boathouse, another student arriving at a run.\ntext: Two fourth-years have a first-year backed against the boathouse wall. Before you can move, Bram is there, between them, sash and all. \"Problem?\" he says. There are two of them, and they're bigger. He doesn't step aside.\n> left: Stand beside him\n  flag bram_softened\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel bram +medium\n  say: You step up next to him. One of them shoves you into the wall for form's sake, and then they decide it isn't worth it. Bram watches them go. Then he looks at you as if you've done something he can't file. \"Don't make it weird,\" he says. He walks the first-year back to the castle himself.\n> right: Run for a professor\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel bram +tiny\n  say: You bring Brask at a run. By the time you get there it's over: Bram has a split lip and the first-year is fine. He doesn't say thank you. He doesn't give you a demerit that week, either.\n\n@event bram_fathers_shadow\ntitle: His Father's Shadow\npool: rivals_bram_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: (bond(bram) == \"rival\" or bond(bram) == \"nemesis\") and year >= 2\nart: shield\nscene: A big man in an old-fashioned prefect's pin standing in the entrance hall with a student who looks smaller than usual, another student watching from the stairs.\ntext: There's a big man in the entrance hall with Bram's jaw, talking to Bram in a low steady voice. Bram is standing very straight and getting smaller. Then the man sees you on the stairs. \"Is that the one?\" he says. \"The one beating you?\"\n> left: Walk on as if you didn't hear\n  learn bram.father\n  rel bram +small\n  say: You keep walking. Behind you, Bram says nothing at all. That evening he finds you, and stands there, and says, \"You didn't hear that.\" \"Hear what?\" you say. He nods once and goes.\n> right: Tell his father he's the best prefect here\n  learn bram.father\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel bram +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The man looks at you as if a chair had spoken. Bram looks at you as if you've hit him. Later, he finds you. \"Why,\" he says. Not a question. You shrug. He gives you no demerits for a month, and never says why either.\n\n# ---------------- Corvin, soured ----------------\n\n@event corvin_locked_out\ntitle: Locked Out\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) <= -12\nweight: 9\nart: key\ntags: rival\nscene: A dormitory door with a new padlock on it, a lanky student leaning beside it and spinning a key on one finger.\ntext: There's a new padlock on your door. Corvin is leaning beside it, spinning a key on one finger. \"Funny thing,\" he says. \"Locks. They don't care whose side you're on. Make it worth my while.\"\n> left: Pay him off\n  gold -small\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: He takes the coins, unlocks the door and bows you in. \"Pleasure doing business,\" he says. He sounds tired, not pleased. The padlock is gone in the morning.\n> right: Pick it yourself\n  challenge deceive journeyman:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      rel corvin +tiny\n      say: You pick it in four minutes with two hairpins. Corvin watches, and stops spinning the key. \"Huh,\" he says. \"Huh.\" It's the first thing he's said to you in a month that isn't a joke.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      rel corvin -tiny\n      say: You can't. He lets you struggle for twenty minutes, then unlocks it without a word and walks off. It's worse than if he'd laughed.\n\n@event corvin_bad_odds\ntitle: Bad Odds\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) <= -12 and renown >= 10\nweight: 8\nart: mask\ntags: rival\nscene: A chalked odds board in a dim corner of the common room, a student's name on it with long odds against them, a lanky bookmaker taking coins.\ntext: Corvin is running a book on you: whether you'll fail Friday's mock paper. The odds aren't flattering. Half the common room has a coin on it. He catches your eye across the room and raises the chalk, as if to say: well?\n> left: Bet on yourself\n  hint: A wager on the mock: study for it.\n  gold -small\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  challenge research journeyman:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      study +small\n      say: You put your coin on yourself, at his long odds. Then you study like you've never studied. You pass the mock. Corvin pays out, every coin, and says, \"Fine. I'll stop.\" He doesn't quite, but he shortens the odds.\n    lose:\n      study +tiny\n      say: You put your coin on yourself and study all week. You pass, by one mark under his line. Corvin keeps your coin and chalks the result up with a flourish. \"Close,\" he says. \"Closer than the odds.\"\n> right: Shrug it off\n  mood -small\n  rel corvin +tiny\n  say: You let it go. The book runs for a week and closes. Corvin wipes the board clean himself and doesn't start another. He nods to you after, which is something.\n\n# ---------------- Ilse, soured ----------------\n\n@event ilse_frost_door\ntitle: Frost on the Door\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: met(ilse) and rel(ilse) <= -12\nweight: 8\nart: snow\ntags: rival\nscene: A dormitory door completely sealed in clear ice, a student with a kettle staring at it, a single word scratched into the frost.\ntext: Your door is sealed in ice, a hand thick, clear as glass. Someone has scratched a word into the frost: a Northern word you don't know. It doesn't sound kind. In the village, they say, a girl in white furs bought nothing today, and smiled.\n> left: Thaw it the slow way\n  health -tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: Kettle after kettle. It takes all evening. When the last of it goes, the Northern word goes with it. You never find out what it meant, which might be the point.\n> right: Go and find her\n  rel ilse +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You find her by the frozen pond. \"Your door,\" you say. She looks at you without blinking. \"Your fault,\" she says. Then, after a long time, \"It was a stupid word. I take it back.\" The ice is gone when you get home.\n\n@event ilse_contempt\ntitle: Beneath Her\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\nwhen: met(ilse) and rel(ilse) <= -12\nweight: 8\nart: snow\ntags: rival\nscene: A girl in white furs walking past another student in the village square without looking, a gust of snow following her that soaks the other student's shoes.\ntext: Ilse walks past you in the village square as if you were weather. Her private storm, by accident or not, empties itself into your shoes. Two villagers laugh. She doesn't look back.\n> left: Say her name\n  rel ilse +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: She stops. She doesn't turn round. \"What,\" she says. You don't have an answer. Neither does she. But the storm over her head thins, just a little, before she goes.\n> right: Let it go\n  mood -small\n  say: You squelch home. It's the kind of small cold that lasts all day.\n"},{"path":"content/events/breaks.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Break visits (world expansion 1, plan §B): a break at the castle or at home now has four free visits a day (E2,\n#  the plan states' `day:`), so each castle place gets a card that only happens while the castle is half empty, and\n#  each place at home gets two or three more. Breaks are months 5–6 (winter: snow, Deepwinter, Thawmoon) and 11–12\n#  (summer: Goldenhaze, Starfall). The people who stay are the ones whose `schedule_break` keeps them here.\n#  Each castle card rests six months: once a break at most.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The castle, half empty ----------------\n\n@event brk_one_long_table\ntitle: One Long Table\nplace: great_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: break and not off_campus\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 14\nart: meal\ntags: social break\nscene: A vast empty dining hall with a single long table at one end, a handful of students and staff eating together under one chandelier.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The Great Hall has given up on house tables for the break. Everyone left eats at one long table by the fire: a handful of students, two professors and a cook who sits down with you, which nobody has ever seen.|Half the candles are out for the summer. The few of you left eat at one long table under the one lit chandelier, and the cook brings out whatever she likes.} {~Someone has started a game of passing the salt without asking.|A professor tells a story about the founders that is almost certainly libel.|The cook asks everyone what they'd change about her soup. Only one answer is the right one.}\n> left: Stay for the whole evening\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {~You stay until the fire is embers, and learn more about the staff in one evening than in a term. Your reading waits on the bench, unread.|You stay and talk until the cook throws you all out. It's the best supper of the year, and your essay notes never leave your bag.|You stay, and the table gets louder and warmer, and you forget the work you came down meaning to do.}\n> right: Eat, then go and work\n  study +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You eat fast and go back to your books. Through the floor, later, you can hear them laughing.|You take your bowl up to the library. The laughter follows you up the stairs, then thins.|You thank the cook, slip out, and get a good evening's work done in the quiet. It is very quiet.}\n\n@event brk_kitchen_pie\ntitle: The Cook's Holiday Pie\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: break and not off_campus\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 12\nart: meal\ntags: break\nscene: A steamy castle kitchen, a red-faced cook rolling out pastry the size of a tabletop, a student up to the elbows in flour.\ntext: The kitchens are nearly empty for the break, and the cook has decided to make the pie she never has time for in term: a pie the size of a cartwheel, with {season == \"winter\": a frost-apple filling and pastry stars on top.|a summer-berry filling and a pastry lake on top, with a pastry boat.} She looks at you. \"Hands,\" she says. \"Washed.\"\n> left: Help with the pastry\n  health +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: {~You roll and crimp and get flour in your eyebrows. The pie is enormous and lopsided, and the cook says it's the best she's made. You get the first slice.|Making pastry is just alchemy with butter, you realise. The cook agrees, and gives you a slice to prove it.|You stamp out pastry stars until your arms ache. The pie is glorious. You eat too much of it.}\n> right: Lick the bowl and run\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You take the bowl, a spoon and your leave. The cook shouts after you, without much heat.|The bowl is the best part of any pie, and you're not ashamed. The cook pretends to be furious.|You steal the bowl and eat it on the stairs. It's worth every word she says.}\n\n@event brk_courtyard_quiet\ntitle: The Empty Courtyard\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: break and not off_campus\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 12\nart: tower\ntags: break\nscene: A castle courtyard with no one in it, the fountain still, a single student sitting on its rim.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Snow has filled the courtyard knee-deep and nobody has dug a path. The fountain is a cake of ice with a frozen fish in it, looking surprised.|The courtyard bakes in the sun with nobody to cross it. The fountain is low and warm, and a cat has claimed the rim.} You can hear the castle breathe.\n> left: Make the first tracks\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": You wade across it on purpose, then back, then in a large figure of eight. Tomorrow someone will wonder who did it, and you'll know.|You walk every flagstone you've hurried across all term, slowly, barefoot. The stones are hot, and so are your soles.}\n> right: Sit and do nothing\n  mood +small\n  say: {season == \"winter\": You sit on the fountain's icy rim and watch the snow land on nothing. Your feet go numb. You don't mind.|You sit by the cat and do nothing until the shadows move. The cat permits it.}\n\n@event brk_snowball_bram\ntitle: Bram in the Snow\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: break and not off_campus and season == \"winter\"\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 12\nart: snow\ntags: social break\ncast:\n  bram: bram where around(bram) and met(bram)\nscene: A stern prefect with a red sash brushing snow off his ledger in a snowy courtyard, a snowball clearly having just hit him.\ntext: Bram has stayed for the break, and he is patrolling an empty courtyard because somebody must. He is writing in his ledger. There is nobody to write down. You have a snowball in your hand, and you don't remember making it.\n> left: Throw it\n  rel bram +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: It takes his sash clean off. For a moment his face is outraged. Then he puts down the ledger, very deliberately, and makes a snowball of his own. Twenty minutes later you're both soaked, and he's laughing, and he'll deny it by Monday.\n> right: Drop it and say hello\n  rel bram +tiny\n  say: \"Prefect.\" \"Student.\" He looks at the snowball melting at your feet, and at you. \"Very wise,\" he says, and walks on, and you think, just possibly, he was disappointed.\n\n@event brk_library_alone\ntitle: The Library to Yourself\nplace: library\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: break and not off_campus\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: study break\nscene: A huge empty library with long shafts of light, one student lying on the floor between the stacks with a book held up overhead.\ntext: The library is yours. No queue for the good desk, no whispering, no one shelving loudly in the next row. Even the librarian's cat has gone off duty, and is asleep on the returns cart.\n> left: Read for the exams\n  study +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You work through the term's reading list in a week. It's dull, and your next exam will thank you.|You take the good desk and keep it all break, and the reading gets done.|You read until the light goes, and get further than you have all term.}\n> right: Read whatever you like\n  lore +small\n  say: {~You read a history of the castle's drains, a book of Northern poems and half a novel. None of it is on any syllabus. All of it is wonderful.|You find a shelf of old letters bound into books, and read strangers' lives all afternoon.|You read lying on the floor between the stacks, which you've wanted to do since your first week.}\n\n@event brk_kael_shore\ntitle: Kael at the Water\nplace: lakeshore\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: break and not off_campus\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 12\nart: wave\ntags: social break\ncast:\n  kael: kael where around(kael) and met(kael)\nscene: A pale Northern student sitting alone on a rock at a lake's edge, skimming stones, frost on the stones around him.\ntext: Kael hasn't gone home for the break. He doesn't say why, and you don't ask. He's on a rock by the lake, skimming stones that leave a trail of frost across the water, and he moves up to make room without looking round.\n> left: Sit with him\n  rel kael +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You sit. The rock is cold. Neither of you says much, and after a while he hands you a stone, and you skim it, badly. He almost smiles.|You sit with him until the light goes. Somewhere in the second hour, he starts talking about snow, and doesn't stop for a long time.}\n> right: Leave him his quiet\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You leave him to the stones. From the path you look back: his trail of frost is still going out across the lake, further than seems possible.|He's earned his quiet. You let him have it, and feel the lack of the company all evening.}\n\n@event brk_starfall_observatory\ntitle: Starfall, Almost Alone\nplace: observatory\nslot: night\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: break and not off_campus and (month == 11 or month == 12)\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 14\nart: star\ntags: social break\ncast:\n  seraphine: seraphine where around(seraphine) and met(seraphine)\nscene: An observatory dome open to a sky full of falling stars, a seer in a paint-stained smock lying on the floor looking up.\ntext: Starfall, and almost nobody left to see it. Seraphine is lying on the observatory floor with the dome open, watching the stars fall. \"I painted this,\" she says, without looking at you. \"Last spring. I didn't know you'd be in it.\"\n> left: Lie down beside her\n  rel seraphine +small\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You lie on the cold floor beside her and count stars until you lose count, and then keep going. Somewhere around midnight she says, \"That's the one,\" and doesn't say which.|You lie and watch. She tells you which ones she's already seen fall, last spring, in paint.}\n> right: Ask what else she painted\n  lore +tiny\n  rel seraphine -tiny\n  say: {~\"Nothing you'd want to know,\" she says, too quickly. She goes back to the stars. So do you, but it isn't quite the same.|She shakes her head and points at the sky. \"Watch. Don't ask. That's the whole trick.\"}\n\n@event brk_mir_project\ntitle: The Break Project\nplace: workshop\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: break and not off_campus\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: social break\ncast:\n  mir: mir where around(mir) and met(mir)\nscene: A cluttered workshop with a half-built clockwork machine the size of a wardrobe, an artificer in goggles waving a spanner.\ntext: Mir has had the workshop to themself for a week and it shows. In the middle of the floor is something the size of a wardrobe with too many gears. \"It's a machine,\" Mir says, \"for turning pages. Any book. Hands free.\" It's very loud. \"It's not finished.\"\n> left: Hold the spanner\n  rel mir +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You hold the spanner, then the lamp, then a gear that's hotter than it looks. By midnight the machine turns one page, beautifully, and then eats the book. Mir is ecstatic.|You spend the evening as Mir's assistant, which mostly means holding things and being told about them. It's the best kind of evening.}\n> right: Suggest a smaller machine\n  lore +small\n  rel mir -tiny\n  say: {~\"Smaller,\" Mir repeats, as if you'd said \"duller\". But they write it down. \"Next break,\" they say, \"something the size of a teacup that turns pages very politely.\" You'll believe it when you see it.|Mir sulks for an hour, and then admits you're right, and then sulks about that.}\n\n@event brk_holiday_edition\ntitle: The Holiday Edition\nplace: ledger_office\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: break and not off_campus\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social break\ncast:\n  nessa: nessa where around(nessa) and met(nessa)\nscene: A cluttered newspaper office in an empty castle, an editor with ink on her nose surrounded by blank pages, a quill hovering.\ntext: Nessa is putting out a holiday edition of the Ledger for the eleven people still in the castle. There is no news. Her quill keeps trying to invent some. \"Give me a story,\" Nessa says. \"Anything. Please. Before it writes that the lake has resigned.\"\n> left: Give her a real one\n  rel nessa +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {~You tell her the cook has been baking for a full school out of habit, and feeding the surplus to the gargoyles. She makes it the front page. It sells out, all eleven copies.|You tell her a portrait on the east stair has moved two frames along while nobody was looking. She runs it with a drawing. Bram buys three copies and pretends not to.}\n> right: Let the quill have its way\n  mood +small\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  say: {~The quill writes that the lake has resigned and been replaced by a smaller, more manageable pond. Nessa prints it under protest. It's the most-read edition of the year.|The quill invents a romance between two gargoyles. Nessa refuses to print it, and then prints it.}\n\n@event brk_duel_trophies\ntitle: Polishing the Trophies\nplace: duel_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: break and not off_campus\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 10\nart: trophy\ntags: break\nscene: An empty duelling hall, trophy cabinets standing open, a student polishing a silver cup with a cloth.\ntext: The duelling hall is empty except for the trophy cabinets, which are open, and a bucket of polish with a note on it: \"Volunteers. —Thorne\". Two hundred years of cups and shields stare at you, tarnished.\n> left: Polish them all\n  vigor +tiny\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~It takes all afternoon. Halfway through you find a cup won by a student from your own corner of the country, a hundred years ago. You polish that one twice.|By the end your arms ache and the cabinets shine. Thorne, passing, nods once at the work. It feels like an honour.}\n> right: Read the names instead\n  lore +small\n  say: {~You read every name on every cup instead. Some of them are on portraits in the halls. One of them is carved on the bench you sit on every morning.|You read the names and the years, and the losers' names scratched underneath by someone with a grudge. It's a whole history, written in spite.}\n\n@event brk_amaranth_restock\ntitle: Restocking the Infirmary\nplace: infirmary\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: break and not off_campus\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: break\ncast:\n  amaranth: amaranth where around(amaranth)\nscene: A healer in a white apron counting jars on high shelves in an empty infirmary, a student up a ladder handing them down.\ntext: With the beds empty for the break, Sister Amaranth is restocking the infirmary from the bottom shelf to the top. She hands you a list and a ladder. \"Count everything,\" she says. \"Then count it again. The second count is the true one.\"\n> left: Count everything twice\n  alchemy +small\n  rel amaranth +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You count salves and tinctures until you dream in labels. The second count is different from the first in three places. Amaranth is not surprised.|By the end you know where every remedy in the castle lives. That will be useful, one day, at the worst possible time.}\n> right: Ask her about the strange jars\n  lore +small\n  say: {~She tells you what's in the black jar on the top shelf, and then why it's on the top shelf. You come down the ladder very carefully.|\"That one,\" she says, \"is for a curse we haven't had since my first year. I keep it in case.\" She doesn't say which curse.}\n\n@event brk_stables_round\ntitle: The Feeding Round\nplace: stables\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: break and not off_campus\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 10\nart: paw\ntags: nature break\nscene: A stable in winter or summer with every stall occupied, a professor and a student carrying buckets down the long aisle at dawn.\ntext: Most of the stable hands have gone home, so Professor Brask does the feeding round herself, at dawn, every day. She doesn't ask for help. She hands you a bucket anyway.\n> left: Do the whole round\n  vigor +small\n  rel brask +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You carry buckets until your shoulders burn. The hippogriffs eat out of your hand by the end. Brask says nothing, which from her is a speech.|You do the round, then the next day's round. On the third day a griffon lets you scratch its neck. Brask raises an eyebrow, impressed.}\n> right: Feed the old ones slowly\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You spend the whole round with the oldest horse, who takes a full hour to eat and is worth every minute.|You feed the elderly creatures at the end of the row by hand. Brask lets you. \"They don't get visitors,\" she says.}\n\n@event brk_corridors_echo\ntitle: The Echo\nplace: lecture_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: break and not off_campus\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 10\nart: tower\ntags: break\nscene: An empty spiral staircase in a castle tower, a student halfway up shouting a word and listening to it come back.\ntext: With nobody on the stairs, the lecture tower echoes like a well. You say \"hello\" on the first landing and it comes back, a moment later, from somewhere near the top, in a voice that isn't quite yours.\n> left: Run all the stairs\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You race your own echo to the top and lose. You lie on the top landing, wheezing, while it laughs at you on the way down.|You run up and down until you can't. The echo keeps going a while without you.}\n> right: Talk to the echo\n  lore +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You ask the echo a question. It answers a different one. You spend an hour trying to work out which.|You tell the echo your name. It tells you someone else's, very faintly. You look it up later. A student from a hundred years ago.}\n\n@event brk_corvin_route\ntitle: The Holiday Route\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: break and not off_campus\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 12\nart: map\ntags: social break\ncast:\n  corvin: corvin where around(corvin) and met(corvin)\nscene: A lanky student with a lock-pick behind one ear grinning beside a narrow secret passage behind a gargoyle, holding a lantern.\ntext: Corvin is in the passage behind the third gargoyle with a lantern and a grin. \"Holidays,\" he says, \"are when the castle forgets to lock things. I've found three doors this week. One of them goes to a room full of hats.\" He offers you the lantern.\n> left: Take the lantern\n  rel corvin +small\n  guile +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {~The room full of hats is real. So is a second room, full of umbrellas, and a third you don't talk about. You put everything back. Mostly.|You explore until dawn. Corvin draws a map on the back of his hand and loses half of it washing.}\n> right: Keep him company, not the route\n  rel corvin +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You sit in the passage and talk instead. He tells you about the doors he hasn't tried, and why, and it's more interesting than the doors.|You stay with the lantern while he goes. He comes back with a hat. It suits you.}\n\n# ---------------- Home, a little more ----------------\n\n@event home_cottage_rain\ntitle: Rain on the Roof\npool: home_cottage_routine\ntone: warm\nart: rain\nscene: A cottage kitchen in heavy rain, a family playing cards around the table, a kettle steaming.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Sleet on the roof all day, and nobody going anywhere.|A summer storm, sudden and loud, and the whole house shut in together.} {~Someone gets the old cards out.|Someone starts telling a story you've heard a hundred times.}\n> left: Play every hand\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~You lose three hands, win one, and lose the winnings in the next. Your family cheats outrageously. You remember why you love them.|You play until the rain stops, and then a few more, and pay your debts in coppers.}\n> right: Read by the window\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You read by the rain-streaked window while the cards go on behind you. Every so often someone asks you to settle an argument, and you lose your page.|You get through a whole book while the storm goes over. Somebody saves you the last biscuit, and somebody else eats it.}\n\n@event home_cottage_photograph\ntitle: The Picture on the Mantel\nplace: home_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 8\nart: mirror\ntags: home\nscene: A small framed picture on a cottage mantelpiece of a child in oversized robes, a student holding it and smiling ruefully.\ntext: There's a new picture on the mantel: you, on the day you left for the academy, in robes two sizes too big. {final_year: It looks like someone else's child now.|You look terrified. You were.} Someone has polished the frame.\n> left: Tell them what it's really like\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You tell them about the stairs that move, the professor who throws chalk, the friend who can't make toast. They laugh in the right places, and look worried in the others.|You tell them everything you can, and not the rest. They know there's a rest. They let you keep it.}\n> right: Put it back and say nothing\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You put it back, straighter. Later, you see someone straighten it again.|You put it back. Some things are better on a mantel than said out loud.}\n\n@event home_fields_harvest_help\ntitle: All Hands\npool: home_fields_routine\ntone: warm\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A field at harvest or a frozen field being cleared, neighbours working in a line, a student in academy boots among them.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The neighbours are clearing a fallen tree from the far field before the thaw, and every pair of hands is wanted.|The neighbours are getting in a late crop before the weather turns, and every pair of hands is wanted.} You still remember how. Your back remembers more.\n> left: Work the whole day\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  gold +tiny\n  say: {~By sunset you're filthy and aching, the job is done, and a neighbour presses a coin on you you can't refuse.|You work till dark. Nobody mentions the academy once, and you like that more than you expected.}\n> right: Do a bit of quiet magic\n  arcana +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~A little lift here, a little warmth there. The work goes twice as fast. Nobody says anything, but an old neighbour watches your hands the whole time, and doesn't smile.|You make it easier than it should be. The neighbours thank you warily, as if you'd brought a stranger.}\n\n@event home_fields_old_friend\ntitle: Someone You Grew Up With\npool: home_fields_routine\ntone: warm\nart: hand\ntags: social\nscene: Two young people leaning on a field gate at sunset, one in work clothes, one in academy robes, talking easily.\ntext: {~Someone you grew up with is leaning on the gate at the end of the lane, the way they always did. They're married now.|At the gate, the friend from before. You've both changed; the gate hasn't. They've taken over the mill, and never mean to leave.}\n> left: Talk until it's dark\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You talk until you can't see each other's faces. It's easy, the way it is with people who knew you before you were anyone.|You talk about nothing for two hours. It's the best conversation you've had in months.}\n> right: Tell them about the academy\n  charm +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~They listen politely. Halfway through you notice they've stopped asking questions, and you stop.|You tell them everything. They say \"huh\" a lot. You realise you're talking about a place they'll never see.}\n\n@event home_fields_night_sky\ntitle: The Sky Without Towers\nplace: home_fields\nslot: evening\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 8\nart: star\ntags: home nature\nscene: A student lying on their back in a dark field at night, the sky enormous and full of stars, no buildings anywhere.\ntext: You stay out in the field until the light goes. At home the sky comes right down to the ground on every side. No towers, no observatory dome, just stars from one edge to the other. {month == 11 or month == 12: And Starfall, which looks different out here: closer, somehow, and less like a show.|You'd forgotten how many there are.}\n> left: Name every one you know\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You name them until the dew soaks through. You know more than you did last year. You know which ones are lying.|You name them aloud, softly. A fox somewhere across the field barks, as if correcting you.}\n> right: Just lie there\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You don't name anything. You just lie there until you're cold, and walk home feeling enormous and very small.|You lie in the grass and let the sky be a sky. It's the first time in months nothing has been a lesson.}\n\n@event home_market_stallholder\ntitle: The Stallholder Remembers\npool: home_market_routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: money\nscene: A country market stall piled with cheeses, a cheerful stallholder pointing at a student and laughing.\ntext: The cheese-seller remembers you. \"The one who went to the magic school!\" she tells everyone in earshot. \"Do us a trick, then.\" The whole queue turns round to look.\n> left: Do a small trick\n  renown +tiny\n  mana -tiny\n  say: {~You make a cheese float, very slightly. The queue applauds. The cheese-seller gives you the cheese. Your family is mortified and proud in equal parts.|You light a candle with a look. A child bursts into tears of joy. You get a discount for life.}\n> right: Laugh and buy some cheese\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~\"Next time,\" you say, and buy the cheese. She's disappointed and gives you a bigger piece anyway.|You buy the cheese and refuse the trick. The queue loses interest. You're oddly relieved.}\n\n@event home_market_letter_home\ntitle: A Letter for the Castle\nplace: home_market\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 8\nart: letter\ntags: home social\nscene: A village post counter at a country market, a student writing a letter on the counter with a borrowed pen.\ntext: The market has a post counter, and you find yourself writing a letter to the castle: to someone you miss already. It's strange, missing the castle from home. You didn't expect to.\n> left: Write a long one\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~You write four pages and pay for the postage twice. It'll arrive before you do, and be read aloud, probably, to people you'd rather it wasn't.|You write until the postmaster coughs. It's a good letter. You'll find out how good when you get back.}\n> right: Write two lines and go\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~\"Home is fine. Miss you. See you soon.\" It's enough. It's more than enough.|You write two lines, then a third, then stop before it turns into a confession.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/calendar_2b.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The four-year loop's fixed beats (design §4, §7; phase 2b).\n#  These replace the old calendar menus and exams, which are\n#  retired by tools/migrations/0001-retire-v02-calendar.js.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Enrolment (design §7.3): two courses a term ----------------\n\n@event enrolment\ntitle: Enrolment\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: term and (month == 1 or month == 7)\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 80\ntype: menu\noffers: courses\npick: 2\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: class study\nscene: The registrar's hall at the start of term, students queueing at long tables with course lists that rewrite themselves.\ntext: {term_index == 1: Your first term. The registrar slides a list across the desk: four courses open to first-years.|A new term, and a new list. Courses you've passed are gone from it; new levels have appeared.} {member(thesis): Choose one; the thesis takes your other slot.|Choose two.} Course fees are eight crowns. A notice by the door says the library sets out revision tables before exam month: an afternoon there counts toward every exam.\n> none: There is nothing left for you to take\n  when: offered(courses) == 0\n  say: The registrar checks the list twice. Nothing on it is open to you this term. \"Enjoy the quiet,\" she says, without much conviction.\n\n# ---------------- Club Fair (design §7.4): one club at a time ----------------\n\n@event club_fair_v3\ntitle: The Club Fair\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 2\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 60\ntype: menu\noffers: clubs\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: group\ntags: club festival\nscene: The courtyard full of recruiting tables, banners snapping, a duelling demonstration drawing a crowd.\ntext: The Club Fair fills the courtyard. Every table wants you; every captain swears theirs is the one that matters. {rel(lyra) >= 6: Lyra is at the Duelling Circle's table, pretending not to watch which way you walk.|}\n> none: {member(club): Stay with your club|Not this year}\n  hint: {member(club): Another year with the same table.|You can join at next year's fair.}\n  say: {member(club): {~You stop by your own club's table to help hand out leaflets. Someone asks you if it's worth joining. You say yes and mean it.|You walk past every other table with the ease of someone already spoken for.}|You collect three leaflets and a free biscuit, and sign nothing.}\n> quit: Resign from your club\n  when: member(club)\n  hint: You can join again at next year's fair, as a new member.\n  leave club\n  say: {~You hand back your club pin at the table where you got it. It feels smaller than it did.|You resign. Your evenings are yours again, and nobody will expect you anywhere.}\n\n# ---------------- The Job Board (design §7.4): visiting it is the change event ----------------\n\n@event job_board_visit\ntitle: The Job Board\nplace: job_board\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 30\ntype: menu\noffers: jobs\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: job money\nscene: A cork board crowded with notices, a student running a finger down a column of shift times.\ntext: Notices for work, three layers deep: the library, the Gilded Newt, Pell & Daughters, the courier loft. Each wants someone reliable. Each says so twice.{term and (month == 4 or month == 10):  This week every notice ends the same way: new staff start next term.|}\n> look: Just looking\n  say: You read every notice twice and take none of them down.\n> quit: Hand in your notice\n  when: member(job)\n  hint: You can find work again later.\n  leave job\n  mood +small\n  say: You hand in your notice. Your evenings are yours again; so is your empty purse.\n\n# A term's last month takes on no staff (feedback 2026-10-20); one evening's odd job makes up for it, for someone without work.\n@event job_board_odd_job\ntitle: One Evening's Work\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 40\nart: paper\ntags: job money\nscene: A cork board of notices that all say \"from next term\", and one small card pinned low and crooked beneath them.\nwhen: term and (month == 4 or month == 10) and not member(job)\ntext: Every notice says \"from next term\", except one, pinned low and crooked: {~the refectory wants hands for the end-of-term clear-out|the porters want someone to carry exam desks back to the store rooms|a professor wants her rooms boxed up before the break}. One evening, paid on the night.\n> left: Take the evening's work\n  gold +medium\n  health -small\n  say: {~You scrub, carry and stack until the lamps go out. The pay is counted into your hand, coin by coin, by someone too tired to talk.|It is heavier work than the card let on. Your arms ache all the next day, and your purse is heavier for it.|You finish late, sore and paid. Nobody remembers your name, which is the restful part.}\n> right: Leave it for someone else\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You leave the card where it is. Somebody poorer will want it more, and you want your evening.|You read it twice and walk on. The term is nearly over; so is your patience for work.|You let the card be. The evening goes on a long walk instead, which pays nothing and feels like a lot.}\n\n# ---------------- Break plans (design §4.3) ----------------\n\n@event break_plan\ntitle: The Break\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: break and (month == 5 or month == 11) and not wrapup\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 75\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: sun\ntags: home\nscene: The academy gates thrown open, barges loading trunks, a few students lingering on the steps with nowhere they'd rather be.\ntext: {month == 5: The winter break.|The summer break.} Two months without lectures. The castle empties, mostly.\n> castle: Stay at the castle\n  hint: Quiet corridors and secrets. Board 8 crowns a month.\n  gain plan_castle\n  say: The castle in the holidays is a different building: louder echoes, kinder staff, doors you've never seen open.\n> home: Go home\n  hint: Family, old friends, free board.\n  gain plan_home\n  say: The barge, the coach, the last mile on foot. Home is smaller than you remember, and warmer.\n> work: Work on campus\n  hint: A holiday gig that pays well. Board 8 crowns a month.\n  gain plan_work\n  queue holiday_gigs now\n  say: You trade the holidays for wages. Your feet will remember it.\n> intern: Start your internship\n  when: member(internship) and month == 11\n  hint: A stipend, a supervisor and a project.\n  gain plan_internship\n  say: You pack a trunk of notebooks and set off {member(intern_expedition): up the long road to the high plateau|{member(intern_field_station): across the moors to the field station|{member(intern_thornwick): on the coach to Thornwick, of all places|on the long coach to the capital}}}.\n\n# ---------------- Track notices (pushed by the engine at term end) ----------------\n\n@event course_exam\ntitle: Exam Day\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study class\nscene: A silent exam hall in late light, rows of bent heads, an hourglass on the invigilator's desk.\ntext: Exam day for {track}. The hall is silent except for quills and somebody's knee bouncing under a desk. {~The paper is long.|The first question is one you revised.|The first question is one you didn't.|The invigilator turns the hourglass with a click you feel in your teeth.}\n> left: Answer what you know, carefully\n  sit_exam steady\n  say: {~You write slowly: everything you're sure of, and nothing you aren't.|You check every answer twice and hand in at the bell.|The first question is one you didn't revise, so you leave it and bank the rest.|You pace yourself by the hourglass and finish with sand to spare.}\n> right: Go for top marks\n  sit_exam wild\n  say: {~You take the hardest question first and gamble the essay on an idea you had at breakfast.|You write the answer you'd want to read, not the safe one.|The first question is one you didn't revise. You answer it anyway, boldly, from first principles.|You race the hourglass and write until the last grain, and past it.}\n\n@event track_warning\ntitle: A Word About Your Attendance\ntrigger: queued\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: paper\ntags: job club\nscene: A folded note on official paper, the words \"performance\" and \"concern\" underlined twice.\ntext: {kind_is(track, club): A note about {track}: you haven't shown up once all term, and it has been noticed. Members who never come stop being members.|A note about {track}: your attendance and your efforts have slipped, and it has been noticed. One more term like this and there will be consequences.}\n> left: Promise to do better\n  mood -small\n  say: You write back a promise in your neatest hand. Now you'll have to keep it.\n> right: Let it slide\n  perform track -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You put the note in a drawer and skip the next session to prove a point. The note doesn't stop existing in the drawer.\n\n# Every job at term end (playtest 2, M33): stay on for another term, or resign.\n@event job_term_review\ntitle: Another Term?\ntrigger: queued\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A staff rota pinned to a door, last term's names crossed off, a blank column waiting for next term's.\ntext: The term is over, and so is the rota for {track}. A fresh one goes up for next term, and there's a gap where your name usually goes. {~Nobody would blame you either way.|Your feet have an opinion. So does your purse.|Someone has already pencilled a question mark next to it.}\n> left: Stay on next term\n  say: {~You write your name in the gap. Another term of shifts, another term of wages.|Your name goes back on the rota, in ink this time.|You rub out the question mark and write your name over it.}\n> right: Resign\n  leave track\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You hand in your notice, politely. The evenings are yours again; the purse is lighter for it.|You leave the gap empty. You can always find work again, if you need to.|You leave the question mark where it is. Someone else can answer it.}\n\n@event track_promotion\ntitle: A Step Up\ntrigger: queued\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: trophy\ntags: job club\nscene: A small ceremony in a cramped room, someone pinning a badge to a student's collar while the others applaud.\ntext: {track} wants to promote you. More responsibility, more respect, and in the right places, more pay.\n> left: Accept\n  promote track\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: The badge is a little crooked. You don't straighten it.\n> right: Not yet\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"When you're ready, then.\" They mean it. The offer will come again.\n\n# ---------------- Research internship (design §7.5; the Royal Library is the slice host) ----------------\n\n@event intern_application\ntitle: Applications Open\ntrigger: auto\nrepeat: 2\npriority: 50\ntype: menu\npick: 6\nwhen: month == 7 and year >= 2 and year <= 3\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: job study\nscene: A cluster of notices with wax seals pinned above all the others: summer research posts, each with its own demands.\ntext: Summer research posts are open. Six notices, six seals, six lists of requirements. Apply to as many as you like: the first letter is an evening's work, and every one after it costs another evening and the postage.\non_show:\n  intern_letters = 0\n> royal: The Royal Library\n  when: level(astromancy) >= 2 and not member(intern_royal_library)\n  locked: {member(intern_royal_library): They've already asked you back.|Needs Astromancy II.}\n  hint: GPA 2.5, an interview on lore, a love of indexes.\n  apply intern_royal_library\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  elif intern_letters < 2:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n  else:\n    leave intern_royal_library\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\n> conclave: The Conclave Laboratory\n  when: level(evocation) >= 2 and not member(intern_conclave)\n  locked: {member(intern_conclave): They've already asked you back.|Needs Evocation II.}\n  hint: GPA 3.0, an interview on arcana, and a word from Thorne helps.\n  apply intern_conclave\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  elif intern_letters < 2:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n  else:\n    leave intern_conclave\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\n> alchemists: The Guild of Alchemists' Works\n  when: level(alchemy) >= 2 and not member(intern_alchemists)\n  locked: {member(intern_alchemists): They've already asked you back.|Needs Alchemy II.}\n  hint: GPA 2.0, an interview on alchemy, and a strong stomach.\n  apply intern_alchemists\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  elif intern_letters < 2:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n  else:\n    leave intern_alchemists\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\n> expedition: The Observatory Expedition\n  when: level(astromancy) >= 2 and not member(intern_expedition)\n  locked: {member(intern_expedition): They've already asked you back.|Needs Astromancy II.}\n  hint: An interview on vigor, and the legs for a plateau.\n  apply intern_expedition\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  elif intern_letters < 2:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n  else:\n    leave intern_expedition\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\n> field: The Beastcraft Field Station\n  when: level(beastcraft) >= 2 and not member(intern_field_station)\n  locked: {member(intern_field_station): They've already asked you back.|Needs Beastcraft II.}\n  hint: An interview on vigor. Boots provided, mostly.\n  apply intern_field_station\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  elif intern_letters < 2:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n  else:\n    leave intern_field_station\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\n> thornwick: The Thornwick Exchange\n  when: level(illusion) >= 2 and not member(intern_thornwick)\n  locked: {member(intern_thornwick): They've already asked you back.|Needs Illusion II.}\n  hint: GPA 2.5, an interview on charm. Bring a thick skin.\n  apply intern_thornwick\n  intern_letters += 1\n  if intern_letters >= 2 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\n  elif intern_letters < 2:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\n  else:\n    leave intern_thornwick\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\n> none: Not this year\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You let the notices flap. There's always next year, until there isn't.\n\n@event intern_offer\ntitle: Letters With Seals\ntrigger: auto\ntype: menu\nwhen: month == 9 and year >= 2 and year <= 3 and (applied(internship) or (guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office)))\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 55\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: study\nscene: Heavy envelopes with wax seals on a dormitory desk, one of them plain grey with no seal at all.\ntext: The spring post brings answers. {applied(internship): There are sealed letters on your desk. Which do you open first?|There are no more sealed letters.} {guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office): There is also a plain grey envelope you never asked for.|}\n> royal: Letter from the Royal Library\n  when: applied(intern_royal_library)\n  hint: They asked for a GPA of 2.5. The interview tests lore.\n  if gpa >= 2.5:\n    challenge research adept-:\n      win:\n        join intern_royal_library\n        renown +small\n        leave intern_conclave\n        leave intern_alchemists\n        leave intern_expedition\n        leave intern_field_station\n        leave intern_thornwick\n        say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the oldest archives in the realm.\n      lose:\n        leave intern_royal_library\n        say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n        if applied(internship):\n          queue intern_offer now\n  else:\n    leave intern_royal_library\n    say: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.5, and they say so twice.\n    if applied(internship):\n      queue intern_offer now\n> conclave: Letter from the Conclave\n  when: applied(intern_conclave)\n  hint: They asked for a GPA of 3.0. The interview tests arcana. A word from Thorne would help.\n  if gpa >= 3.0:\n    if rel(thorne) >= 6:\n      challenge research using arcana adept likely:\n        win:\n          join intern_conclave\n          renown +small\n          leave intern_royal_library\n          leave intern_alchemists\n          leave intern_expedition\n          leave intern_field_station\n          leave intern_thornwick\n          say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in spell research under the Seven Towers.\n        lose:\n          leave intern_conclave\n          say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n          if applied(internship):\n            queue intern_offer now\n    else:\n      challenge research using arcana adept unlikely:\n        win:\n          join intern_conclave\n          renown +small\n          leave intern_royal_library\n          leave intern_alchemists\n          leave intern_expedition\n          leave intern_field_station\n          leave intern_thornwick\n          say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in spell research under the Seven Towers.\n        lose:\n          leave intern_conclave\n          say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n          if applied(internship):\n            queue intern_offer now\n  else:\n    leave intern_conclave\n    say: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 3.0, and they say so twice.\n    if applied(internship):\n      queue intern_offer now\n> alchemists: Letter from the Alchemists' Works\n  when: applied(intern_alchemists)\n  hint: They asked for a GPA of 2.0. The interview tests alchemy.\n  if gpa >= 2.0:\n    challenge research using alchemy journeyman+:\n      win:\n        join intern_alchemists\n        renown +small\n        leave intern_royal_library\n        leave intern_conclave\n        leave intern_expedition\n        leave intern_field_station\n        leave intern_thornwick\n        say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the great vats of the Guild.\n      lose:\n        leave intern_alchemists\n        say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n        if applied(internship):\n          queue intern_offer now\n  else:\n    leave intern_alchemists\n    say: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.0, and they say so twice.\n    if applied(internship):\n      queue intern_offer now\n> expedition: Letter from the Expedition\n  when: applied(intern_expedition)\n  hint: The interview tests vigor.\n  challenge research using vigor journeyman+:\n    win:\n      join intern_expedition\n      renown +small\n      leave intern_royal_library\n      leave intern_conclave\n      leave intern_alchemists\n      leave intern_field_station\n      leave intern_thornwick\n      say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer of sky charts on the high plateau.\n    lose:\n      leave intern_expedition\n      say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n      if applied(internship):\n        queue intern_offer now\n> field: Letter from the Field Station\n  when: applied(intern_field_station)\n  hint: The interview tests vigor.\n  challenge research using vigor journeyman+:\n    win:\n      join intern_field_station\n      renown +small\n      leave intern_royal_library\n      leave intern_conclave\n      leave intern_alchemists\n      leave intern_expedition\n      leave intern_thornwick\n      say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the moors, the heather and the creatures in them.\n    lose:\n      leave intern_field_station\n      say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n      if applied(internship):\n        queue intern_offer now\n> thornwick: Letter from Thornwick\n  when: applied(intern_thornwick)\n  hint: They asked for a GPA of 2.5. The interview tests charm.\n  if gpa >= 2.5:\n    challenge research using charm adept-:\n      win:\n        join intern_thornwick\n        renown +small\n        leave intern_royal_library\n        leave intern_conclave\n        leave intern_alchemists\n        leave intern_expedition\n        leave intern_field_station\n        say: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the rival academy's illusion laboratory.\n      lose:\n        leave intern_thornwick\n        say: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\n        if applied(internship):\n          queue intern_offer now\n  else:\n    leave intern_thornwick\n    say: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.5, and they say so twice.\n    if applied(internship):\n      queue intern_offer now\n> unseen: Open the plain envelope\n  when: guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office)\n  join intern_unseen_office\n  guile +small\n  leave intern_royal_library\n  leave intern_conclave\n  leave intern_alchemists\n  leave intern_expedition\n  leave intern_field_station\n  leave intern_thornwick\n  say: Inside: a time, an address and no signature. The paper is blank again by morning. You go anyway.\n> decline: Fold the letters away\n  leave intern_royal_library\n  leave intern_conclave\n  leave intern_alchemists\n  leave intern_expedition\n  leave intern_field_station\n  leave intern_thornwick\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You fold them carefully. Next year, perhaps.\n\n# The end of a placement (feedback 2026-10-15b): the supervisor's report, by performance. A strong one (80+) brings an\n# offer to come back (M16); otherwise the post ends.\n@event intern_assessment\ntitle: The Supervisor's Report\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 90\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: paper\ntags: study job\nscene: A supervisor's desk on the last day of a placement, a written report face down under a pen.\n# A strong placement (80+) is remembered in the final year: the host writes with a post (careers.grim, 2026-10-25).\non_show:\n  if performance(internship) >= 80:\n    if member(intern_royal_library):\n      flag return_royal_library\n    elif member(intern_conclave):\n      flag return_conclave\n    elif member(intern_alchemists):\n      flag return_guild\n    elif member(intern_expedition):\n      flag return_observatory\n    elif member(intern_field_station):\n      flag return_field_station\n    elif member(intern_thornwick):\n      flag return_thornwick\n    else:\n      flag return_unseen\ntext: Your last day at {member(intern_royal_library): the Royal Library Archives|{member(intern_conclave): the Conclave Laboratory|{member(intern_alchemists): the Guild of Alchemists' Works|{member(intern_expedition): the Observatory Expedition|{member(intern_field_station): the Beastcraft Field Station|{member(intern_thornwick): the Thornwick Exchange|the office with no name}}}}}}. Your supervisor turns the report over. {performance(internship) >= 80: \"Exceptional,\" it says at the top, underlined, and the rest is better.|{performance(internship) >= 60: \"Reliable and capable.\" A few notes in the margin, all of them fair.|{performance(internship) >= 40: \"Adequate.\" The word sits on the page by itself.|\"Did not meet expectations.\" Your supervisor doesn't quite look at you.}}}\n> left: Ask for a letter of reference\n  if performance(internship) >= 60:\n    renown +small\n    say: The letter comes a week later, sealed, and the registrar files a copy with a look you haven't had from her before.\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: The letter comes a month later: two lines, your name spelled wrong in one of them. You put it at the bottom of a drawer.\n  if performance(internship) >= 80:\n    queue intern_asked_back\n  else:\n    leave internship\n> right: Ask what you could have done better\n  mood -tiny\n  if member(intern_royal_library) or member(intern_expedition):\n    lore +small\n  elif member(intern_conclave):\n    arcana +small\n  elif member(intern_alchemists):\n    alchemy +small\n  elif member(intern_field_station):\n    lore +small\n  elif member(intern_thornwick):\n    charm +small\n  else:\n    guile +small\n  say: Your supervisor talks for twenty minutes without notes. Some of it stings. You write all of it down on the coach home, and read it twice.\n  if performance(internship) >= 80:\n    queue intern_asked_back\n  else:\n    leave internship\n\n# An internship lasts one summer (M16). The host asks you back only after a strong one.\n@event intern_asked_back\ntitle: Asked Back\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: year <= 3\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: study job\nscene: A letter on good paper with a familiar seal, the words \"next summer\" underlined once, neatly.\ntext: A letter from {member(intern_royal_library): the Royal Library Archives|{member(intern_conclave): the Conclave Laboratory|{member(intern_alchemists): the Guild of Alchemists' Works|{member(intern_expedition): the Observatory Expedition|{member(intern_field_station): the Beastcraft Field Station|{member(intern_thornwick): the Thornwick Exchange|the office with no name}}}}}}. They were pleased with your summer, and the post is yours again next summer if you want it.\n> left: Write back yes\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {~You write back the same evening. Next summer is spoken for, and you find you're glad.|\"Yes,\" you write, and then three more lines to make it sound less eager.}\n> right: Thank them, and decline\n  leave internship\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You thank them warmly and say no. Next summer is yours to spend, or to apply for something new.|A polite no. You keep the letter anyway; it's good to be asked.}\n\n# ---------------- Thesis (design §7.6) ----------------\n\n@event thesis_setup\ntitle: Choosing a Thesis\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: year == 4 and month == 1 and not member(thesis)\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 85\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A professor's study, three topic proposals laid out on the desk like cards.\ntext: The final year opens with a thesis. Before you choose this term's courses, Professor Thorne lays out three topics, at three levels of ambition.\n> safe: A careful survey\n  hint: Easier, and it will read as easier.\n  join thesis\n  flag thesis_safe\n  say: A sensible topic. Thorne says \"Sensible,\" the way other people say \"Disappointing.\"\n> solid: An original study\n  hint: The usual path.\n  join thesis\n  flag thesis_solid\n  say: Thorne nods. \"Good. Bring me something I don't already know.\"\n> ambitious: Something no one has done\n  hint: Harder, and worth more.\n  join thesis\n  flag thesis_ambitious\n  say: Thorne is quiet for a long time. \"Hm,\" he says, and for once it sounds like hope.\n\n@event thesis_crunch\ntitle: The Crunch\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month == 9 and member(thesis) and progress(thesis) < 60\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: hourglass\ntags: study\nscene: A thesis carrel at dawn, a candle burned to a stub, drafts on the floor and a student asleep on the keyboard of a typing-quill.\ntext: The defence is next month and your thesis is not ready. Your advisor's note is three words long: \"Well? Final draft.\"\n> left: Work through the nights\n  progress thesis +large\n  health -medium\n  mood -medium\n  say: You stop seeing daylight. The draft gets better and you get worse, which seems to be how this works.\n> right: Hand in what you have\n  mood +tiny\n  progress thesis +small\n  say: You tidy what's there and accept the gaps. Your advisor sighs, which is not the same as no.\n\n@event thesis_defence\ntitle: The Defence\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month == 10 and member(thesis)\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 65\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study exam\nscene: A semicircular table with three examiners, a student standing at a lectern with a stack of notes.\ntext: Three examiners, one lectern, and a year of work. {progress(thesis) >= 60: You know this material better than anyone in the room.|There are gaps, and you know where they are.} First, you present. Then they ask.\n> left: Defend it\n  hint: A safe topic is easier to defend and earns less; an ambitious one is harder and earns more. A year of thesis work counts.\n  def_score = 0\n  if not (flag(thesis_safe) or flag(thesis_ambitious)):\n    challenge negotiate using lore expert:\n      win:\n        def_score = 2\n        say: Your presentation runs clean from first slide to last. One examiner has stopped taking notes and is just listening.\n      partial:\n        def_score = 1\n        say: A solid presentation with one wobble in the middle. You recover. They noticed the wobble.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: You lose your place twice and your nerve once. The examiners exchange a look you'll remember for years.\n  elif flag(thesis_safe):\n    challenge negotiate using lore expert likely:\n      win:\n        def_score = 2\n        say: A careful survey, carefully presented. Nobody could fault a word of it. Nobody looks thrilled, either.\n      partial:\n        def_score = 1\n        say: Careful, competent and a little dull, and you can see one examiner fighting a yawn.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: Even a safe topic has its gaps, and your presentation walks straight into one of them.\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate using lore expert unlikely:\n      win:\n        def_score = 2\n        if clue(seven_locks) + clue(star_key) + clue(ilse_truth) >= 3:\n          flag thesis_hollowstar\n        say: Something no one has done, laid out so clearly they can't look away. One examiner has put down their pen.\n      partial:\n        def_score = 1\n        say: They follow you most of the way, arguing among themselves about whether it's brilliant or mad. Possibly both.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: You reach too far in the first ten minutes, and spend the rest of the hour trying to get back. They let you.\n  if progress(thesis) >= 70:\n    def_score += 1\n    say: And under the presentation, a year of work they can feel. Every page they turn has been turned before.\n  elif progress(thesis) < 30:\n    def_score -= 1\n    say: Under the presentation, the thin chapters show. One examiner starts counting pages.\n  queue thesis_defence_question now\n> right: Open with your weakest chapter\n  hint: Name the gaps before they find them. Safe, and modest.\n  def_score = 1\n  mood -tiny\n  queue thesis_defence_question now\n  say: You start with what you couldn't prove, and why, and what you'd do next. It takes the wind out of their sails, and a little out of yours.\n  if progress(thesis) >= 70:\n    def_score += 1\n    say: The rest of it stands on a year of real work, and they can tell.\n  elif progress(thesis) < 30:\n    def_score -= 1\n    say: The trouble is how many chapters are weak. They notice that too.\n\n@event thesis_defence_question\ntitle: The Question\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 12\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study exam\nscene: An examiner leaning over a semicircular table, pointing at one line in a thick bound thesis, the student at the lectern very still.\ntext: The examiner who hasn't spoken yet opens your thesis to a page near the end, puts a finger on one line and asks the question you hoped no one would ask.\n> left: Answer it head-on\n  challenge research expert:\n    win:\n      def_score += 1\n      say: You answer it, and then the question behind it, and then the one behind that. The examiner closes the thesis, slowly.\n    lose:\n      def_score -= 1\n      mood -small\n      say: Your answer starts well and ends somewhere you didn't mean to go. The examiner lets the silence do the rest.\n  queue thesis_defence_verdict now\n> right: Admit what you don't know\n  mood -tiny\n  if def_score >= 2:\n    def_score += 1\n    say: \"I don't know,\" you say, \"and here is how I'd find out.\" From someone who has just presented so well, it sounds like mastery. Two examiners nod.\n  else:\n    say: \"I don't know,\" you say, \"and here is how I'd find out.\" It stings a little, and earns you nothing. The examiner moves on.\n  queue thesis_defence_verdict now\n\n@event thesis_defence_verdict\ntitle: The Verdict\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 12\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study exam\nscene: A student waiting alone on a bench in a long stone corridor outside a closed oak door, hands clasped, a clock on the wall.\ntext: They send you out. Twenty minutes on a bench in the corridor, with the clock and your heartbeat. Then the door opens, and they call you back in.\n> ok: Go back in\n  if def_score >= 3:\n    flag thesis_distinction\n    if flag(thesis_ambitious):\n      renown +huge\n    elif flag(thesis_safe):\n      renown +medium\n    else:\n      renown +large\n    say: They stop asking questions and start asking for copies. Distinction. One of them shakes your hand twice, as if the first didn't count.\n  elif def_score >= 1:\n    flag thesis_passed\n    if flag(thesis_ambitious):\n      renown +medium\n    else:\n      renown +small\n    say: A pass, with one examiner's handwriting all over your conclusion. \"Good work,\" says the chair, and means it, mostly.\n  else:\n    flag thesis_resubmit\n    mood -large\n    if flag(thesis_ambitious):\n      renown -small\n    say: They send you away to resubmit. It isn't the end. It feels like it. The idea was good, says the chair. The proof wasn't there yet.\n  # The defence closes the thesis: its lab events stop (B30).\n  leave thesis\n\n# ---------------- Festivals that take a visit (design §4.4) ----------------\n\n@event lantern_making\nslot: noon\ntitle: A Lantern to Launch\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\n# Lantern Night as a festival (feedback 2026-10-16): the lantern first, on the Ball's pattern (ball_robes).\nwhen: month == 2 and term\nrepeat: 4\npriority: 25\nart: lantern\ntags: festival money\nscene: A dormitory table covered in paper, wire and candle stubs, a student folding a lopsided paper lantern.\ntext: {year == 1: Tonight is Lantern Night, and everyone else seems to have known for weeks. You need a lantern.|{final_year: Your last Lantern Night. The lantern should be a good one.|Lantern Night again, and last year's lantern did not survive the trunk.}} {~The village stationer has fine rice paper, at a price.|Your roommate is folding theirs with their tongue between their teeth.|The common room is ankle-deep in paper offcuts.}\n> left: Buy fine paper in the village\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  flag lantern_fine\n  say: {~Rice paper so thin you can read through it, and a proper beeswax candle. It folds like a dream.|The stationer sells you paper the colour of the dawn sky. Your lantern will be the finest on the shore.|Gold-flecked paper, too dear and entirely worth it. You fold it slowly, as if it might mind.}\n> middle: Make your own from scraps\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~Old exam paper, a bent wire and a candle stub. Your fingers smell of wax for a day, and one of them is burnt.|You glue it with porridge and patience. It's lopsided, and it's yours.|You fold it from a letter from home. It feels right to send that out over the water.}\n> right: Glamour a plain one\n  # Only with Glamour known, and it costs its Mana (2026-10-20: it used to be open to anyone).\n  when: spell_level(glamour) >= 1\n  hint: Needs Glamour.\n  mana -small\n  challenge spell using charm apprentice+:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      guile +tiny\n      flag lantern_fine\n      say: {~A plain paper bag becomes a lantern of stained glass. Nobody guesses.|You glamour your lantern into a little silver moon. Three people ask where you bought it.|Your lantern shimmers like mother-of-pearl. You'll have to keep it lit all evening, or it'll show.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {~The glamour slips at dusk, and your stained-glass lantern turns back into a paper bag in front of everyone.|Your silver moon fades to brown paper halfway to the shore. A first-year points.|The pearl sheen peels off like old paint. You carry it lit-side-in, and fool nobody.}\n\n@event lantern_night_v3\ntitle: Lantern Night\nplace: lakeshore\nreplaces: visit2\nwhen: month == 2\nrepeat: 12\ntype: menu\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: lantern\ntags: festival\nscene: The Mirror Lake at night, hundreds of paper lanterns drifting over the water, students crowding the shore.\ntext: Lantern Night. The whole academy gathers on the lakeshore with paper lanterns, each carrying a wish out over the water. {month == 2 and year == 1: Your first one.|{final_year: Your last one, and you know it.|Another year, another wish.}}{flag(lantern_fine):  Yours is one of the finest on the shore.|} Lanterns that wash back up are read by whoever finds them, they say.\n> left: Wish for something selfish\n  unflag lantern_fine\n  mood +medium\n  queue lantern_reeds now chance even\n  say: {year == 1: Your lantern goes out over the water and keeps its secret. You feel lighter for having asked.|{final_year: You wish for what comes after. The lantern climbs high and goes out of sight before anyone else's.|You wish for the same thing as last year, more firmly. The lantern seems to take note.}}\n> right: Wish for someone else\n  hint: Someone close may ask you about it.\n  unflag lantern_fine\n  mood +medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  queue lantern_asked in 1\n  say: {year == 1: You don't tell anyone who. The lantern seems to know; it flies straight.|{final_year: You wish for everyone you'll leave behind here. It's a heavy lantern, and it flies anyway.|You wish for someone you love, and keep their name behind your teeth. The lantern flies straight.}}\n> read: Read other people's wishes\n  hint: Tempting. Not kind.\n  unflag lantern_fine\n  alignment -small\n  guile +small\n  queue lantern_washed_up in 1 chance likely\n  say: {year == 1: You fish a lantern out of the shallows and read it by its own candle. Someone wants to pass Alchemy. Someone wants a kiss. You feel like a thief, and read another.|{final_year: In your last year you finally do what everyone dares: you read the wishes caught on the jetty. Half are about leaving. You put them back very carefully.|You read three wishes caught in the reeds. One of them has your name on it. You put it back, and don't sleep.}}\n\n@event lantern_reeds\ntitle: Caught in the Reeds\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 12\nart: lantern\ntags: festival\nscene: A small paper lantern tangled in lake reeds near the shore, its candle guttering, a tearful first-year on the bank.\ntext: Further along the shore a first-year is crying quietly. Their lantern caught in the reeds a few yards out, and it is guttering, wish and all.\n> left: Wade in and free it\n  alignment +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: The water is shockingly cold. You lift the lantern clear and push it out, and it catches the air and goes. The first-year looks at you as if you'd done magic, which, in a way, you have.\n> right: Tell them wishes don't need lanterns\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You sit with them on the bank and say the true thing: the wish counts when you make it. They stop crying. The lantern goes out in the reeds, and neither of you watches.\n\n@event lantern_washed_up\ntitle: A Lantern Washes Up\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 12\nart: lantern\ntags: festival\nscene: A burnt-out paper lantern lying on the wet stones of a lakeshore at dawn, a folded wish still tucked inside it.\ntext: Weeks after Lantern Night a lantern washes up at your feet on the shore, burnt out, the wish still folded inside it. The handwriting is neat and nervous.\n> left: Read it\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: It's a wish about someone, a student who \"never notices anything\". You recognise the handwriting from the library sign-out book, and say nothing, ever.\n> right: Send it back out unread\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You relight the stub with a snap of your fingers and push it out again. It wobbles off over the water, its secret intact, and you never find out.\n\n@event lantern_asked\ntitle: What Did You Wish For?\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 12\nart: heart\ntags: festival social\ncast:\n  dear: any where (bond(dear) == \"partner\" or bond(dear) == \"close\" or bond(dear) == \"friend\") and around(dear)\nscene: Two students on a window seat with mugs of cocoa, one teasing the other, a paper lantern on the sill between them.\ntext: {dear} drops onto the window seat beside you with two mugs of cocoa. \"So,\" {they} say{s}. \"Lantern Night. What did you wish for? You can tell me. I won't tell the lake.\"\n> left: Tell {them} the truth\n  rel dear +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You tell {them}. {dear} goes quiet for a moment, and then bumps {their} shoulder against yours and doesn't move it away. Saying it out loud was harder than writing it.\n> right: \"If I tell you, it won't come true.\"\n  rel dear +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {dear} groans, and spends the rest of the cocoa guessing, each guess more ridiculous than the last. You laugh so hard you spill yours.\n\n# ---------------- Calendar beats ----------------\n\n@event year_four\ntitle: The Final Year\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 37\npriority: 90\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: The seven towers at dawn from the barge, the same view as the first day, but the student watching it has changed.\ntext: The barge again, the towers again, and a thesis waiting. The first-years on the deck look at you the way you once looked at the fourth-years. At the opening feast the headmistress says, \"Fourth years, this is your last year in these halls. What you become here, you will be for a long time.\" She looks, for a moment, directly at you.\n> ok: Here we go\n  mood +small\n  say: You know which steps creak and which doors stick. The thesis will want evenings of its own each term; something else may have to give, a club or a job.\n\n# ---------------- Appeals (playtest 2, N4): pushed after a failed course, in its context (@game appeal) ----------------\n\n@event grade_appeal\ntitle: The Appeal\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: scales\ntags: study class\nscene: A long table of grey-robed examiners in a panelled room, a single student standing before them holding an exam paper.\ntext: The examiners' list is pinned up, and beside {track} there is an F. The clerk mentions, without looking up, that a student may appeal a grade in person, once, before the board. Most don't. Some of those who do come out with a C.\n> left: Appeal before the board\n  challenge negotiate expert unlikely drift low:\n    win:\n      regrade track C\n      say: You talk for ten minutes about the paper, the question and the answer they marked wrong. The board confers in whispers. The F becomes a C, in red ink, with a sigh.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: The board hears you out, then reads your paper aloud. It's worse aloud. The F stands, and the queue outside the door heard every word.\n> right: Accept it, and read the comments\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The examiner's comments are fair, which is the worst thing about them. You copy out every one. Next time you'll know exactly where it went wrong.\n\n# ---------------- Lead-ins (playtest 2, N1): what's coming, before it comes ----------------\n\n@event registrar_list\ntitle: The Registrar's List\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 1\npriority: 85\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A crowd of new students around a long parchment list pinned in an entrance hall, one of them tracing a line with a finger.\ntext: Before enrolment, the registrar's list goes up in the entrance hall: each course, what it tests, which spells it teaches, and which open only next term. A second-year taps Evocation I. \"If you came here to fight, that one: Flame inside a month.\" She taps Illusion I. \"If you'd rather talk your way out of things, that one. Glamour wins more arguments than Flame ever did.\"\n> ok: Read it twice\n  say: Two courses a term, a fee each time, and an exam at the end of each. Pass a level and you learn its spells.\n\n@event club_fair_flyer\ntitle: Flyers Under the Door\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 1\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: club\nscene: A dormitory floor scattered with colourful club flyers, more sliding under the door as a student watches.\ntext: One flyer slides under your door, then another, then nine. The Club Fair is next month in the courtyard: the Duelling Circle, the Lumen Ledger, the Verdant Circle. One club at a time, says the small print, and each wants a couple of evenings a term.\n> ok: Keep the flyers\n  say: You pin them over your bed and argue with them until you fall asleep.\n\n@event job_board_notice\ntitle: Hands Wanted\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 2 and not member(job)\nslot: noon\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: job money\nscene: A refectory door with a notice pinned to it, three different handwritings added beneath the printed part.\ntext: Pinned to the refectory door: \"Hands wanted. See the Job Board, entrance hall.\" Under it, in pencil: \"The Newt pays best. The library is warmest.\" Under that, in a third hand: \"Pell counts.\"\n> ok: Make a note of it\n  say: One job at a time, a few shifts a term, paid by the shift. The board is up from morning until evening.\n"},{"path":"content/events/calendar.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Calendar: the academy year's fixed beats.\n#  Most are `trigger: auto` — forced into the month's deck\n#  whenever their `when` is true (and `repeat` allows).\n#  Month 1 Harvestmoon ... 12 Starfall. 11–12 are summer recess.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event intro_arrival\ntitle: The Sky-Barge\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: A wooden barge with patched sails drifting between clouds toward seven impossibly tall towers above a mirror-still lake.\ntext: The barge drops through the clouds and there it is: Lumenhold, seven towers stitched together with bridges of light. A first-year beside you is quietly being sick over the side. A prefect with a ledger counts heads and frowns at yours.\n> ok: Step onto the landing\n  say: Your boots touch stone that hums faintly underfoot. Four years. Forty-eight moons.\n\n@event choose_origin\ntitle: Who Were You Before?\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\nart: mirror\ntags: story\nscene: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall, showing a different version of the viewer in each pane.\ntext: The Sorting Mirror in the entrance hall does not sort you into anything. It simply asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came here. It waits.\n> noble: A younger child of minor nobility\n  hint: Charm and a known name. 60 crowns from home now, and an allowance each later term, with strings attached.\n  gain origin_noble\n  say: The mirror shows you a crest you have tried to outgrow. It approves, faintly.\n> scholar: A provincial scholarship student\n  hint: Lore above all. 30 crowns now, and a stipend each later term while your grades hold.\n  gain origin_scholar\n  say: The mirror shows you a candle burning very late in a very small room.\n> hedge: Raised by a marsh hedge-witch\n  hint: Alchemy, some Arcana, good spirits. 15 crowns, and no money from home: you'll need a job.\n  gain origin_hedge\n  say: The mirror smells, briefly, of wet moss and woodsmoke. You feel at home.\n> street: Raised on the streets of Port Calder\n  hint: Guile and grit. 8 crowns, and no one to send more: you'll need a job.\n  gain origin_street\n  say: The mirror shows a pickpocket's quick hands. You check your pockets out of habit.\n> merchant: The heir to a trading company\n  hint: The biggest purse and a nose for bargains: 120 crowns now, and more from the company when trade is good.\n  gain origin_merchant\n  say: The mirror shows ledgers, ships and your mother's disappointed face. Still: gold.\n\n@event sorting_mirror\ntitle: The Sorting Mirror\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: mirror\ntags: story\nscene: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall, showing a different version of the viewer in each pane.\ntext: The Sorting Mirror in the entrance hall does not sort you into anything. It simply asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came here. You tell it.\n> ok: Look into the mirror\n  say: {has(origin_noble): The mirror shows you a crest you have tried to outgrow. It approves, faintly.|{has(origin_scholar): The mirror shows you a candle burning very late in a very small room.|{has(origin_hedge): The mirror smells, briefly, of wet moss and woodsmoke. You feel at home.|{has(origin_street): The mirror shows a pickpocket's quick hands. You check your pockets out of habit.|The mirror shows ledgers, ships and your mother's disappointed face. Still: gold.}}}}\n\n@event wrapup_begins\ntitle: The Last Weeks\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: wrapup and month == 11\npriority: 70\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: An emptying castle in late summer light, trunks stacked in a corridor, a notice pinned to the great hall door.\non_show:\n  if flag(career_offered) or flag(career_accepted):\n    fair_offer\n  if flag(thornwick_offer):\n    flag offer_thornwick\ntext: A notice on the great hall door: classes are over, duties are done, and final-years may stay on until graduation, board on the house. The castle is yours for one last summer.\n> ok: Make the most of it\n  mood +small\n  say: You walk the long way to breakfast, past every door you ever got lost behind.\n\n@event choose_elective\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 1 or month == 6\"); replaced by enrolment (offers: courses, pick: 2)\ntitle: Choosing an Elective\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 5\npriority: 45\ntype: menu\nart: scroll\ntags: study class retired\nscene: A long noticeboard covered in fluttering enchanted parchment, each sheet trying to shoulder the others aside.\ntext: The registrar's noticeboard flaps with sign-up sheets for this term's electives. Core lectures are compulsory; your elective is yours to choose. The sheets jostle for your attention.\n> pyro: Pyromancy\n  hint: +Arcana each month, small burns. Taught by Professor Thorne.\n  gain class_pyromancy\n> tide: Tidecalling\n  hint: +Arcana, +Vigor, lakeside calm.\n  gain class_tidecalling\n> illusion: Illusion & Glamour\n  hint: +Guile, +Charm.\n  gain class_illusion\n> alchemy: Practical Alchemy\n  hint: +Alchemy.\n  gain class_alchemy\n> divination: Divination\n  hint: +Lore, occasional gloomy omens.\n  gain class_divination\n> artifice: Artifice\n  hint: +Alchemy, +Lore.\n  gain class_artifice\n> beast: Beastcraft\n  hint: +Vigor, +Mood. Smells of hay.\n  gain class_beastcraft\n> astro: Astromancy\n  hint: +Lore, late nights.\n  gain class_astromancy\n> healing: Healing Arts\n  hint: +Alchemy, +Lore, a little health.\n  gain class_healing\n> umbral: Umbral Theory (restricted)\n  hint: Requires Lore 20 or second year. +Arcana, dark moods.\n  when: lore >= 20 or year >= 2\n  locked: Needs Lore 20, or your second year.\n  gain class_umbral\n  flag took_umbral\n\n@pool calendar_auto_story\ntrigger: auto\nclass: story\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\n\n@event club_fair\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 2\"); replaced by club_fair_v3 (offers: clubs)\ntitle: The Club Fair\npool: calendar_auto_story\ntone: light\nwhen: false\ntype: menu\nart: group\ntags: social club retired\nscene: The great courtyard filled with banners and stalls: a duelist juggling sparks, a choir floating a foot off the ground, a girl handing out newspapers that shout headlines.\ntext: The courtyard is a riot of banners. Every club wants you, or at least your membership fee. A broom whizzes overhead trailing a SKYRACE TRYOUTS banner; somewhere a choir is harmonising with a cat.\n> duel: Dueling Circle\n  hint: +Vigor +Arcana, bruises. Home of the Spring Tournament.\n  gain club_dueling\n  meet lyra\n> choir: Choir of the Spheres\n  hint: +Charm, +Mood.\n  gain club_choir\n> sky: Skyrace Squad\n  hint: +Vigor, crowd glory, crashes.\n  gain club_skyrace\n> ledger: The Lumen Ledger\n  hint: +Guile +Charm. Nessa Quill's student paper.\n  gain club_ledger\n  meet nessa\n> garden: Verdant Circle\n  hint: +Alchemy +Mood. Greenhouses and gossip.\n  gain club_garden\n  meet tobias\n> stars: Stargazers\n  hint: +Lore +Mood.\n  gain club_stargazers\n> none: Keep your evenings free\n  hint: No club (leaves your current club, if any).\n  lose club_dueling\n  lose club_choir\n  lose club_skyrace\n  lose club_ledger\n  lose club_garden\n  lose club_stargazers\n  mood +tiny\n\n@event job_board\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 3 or month == 9\"); replaced by job_board_visit (the Job Board place)\ntitle: The Job Board\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 5\npriority: 35\ntype: menu\nart: coin\ntags: job money retired\nscene: A cork board outside the bursar's office, papered with notices; one is on fire, one is wriggling.\ntext: Rent is ten crowns a month and your purse is a fragile thing. The board outside the bursar's office offers honest work, and some that is merely work.\n> library: Library Assistant — 10 gold/month\n  hint: +Lore, a little dull.\n  gain job_library\n> tavern: The Gilded Newt — 16 gold/month\n  hint: +Charm, tiring evenings in the village.\n  gain job_tavern\n> apothecary: Apothecary Clerk — 12 gold/month\n  hint: Requires Alchemy 12. +Alchemy.\n  when: alchemy >= 12\n  locked: Needs Alchemy 12.\n  gain job_apothecary\n> stables: Familiar Stables — 11 gold/month\n  hint: +Vigor. Things bite.\n  gain job_stables\n> tutor: First-Year Tutor — 12 gold/month\n  hint: Requires Lore 18. +Standing.\n  when: lore >= 18\n  locked: Needs Lore 18.\n  gain job_tutor\n> courier: Broom Courier — 18 gold/month\n  hint: Requires Vigor 14. Dangerous.\n  when: vigor >= 14\n  locked: Needs Vigor 14.\n  gain job_courier\n> none: No job (quit any current one)\n  lose job_library\n  lose job_tavern\n  lose job_apothecary\n  lose job_stables\n  lose job_tutor\n  lose job_courier\n\n@event lantern_night\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 2\"); replaced by lantern_night_v3 (a festival at the lakeshore)\ntitle: Lantern Night\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 12\nart: lantern\ntags: festival social retired\nscene: Hundreds of paper lanterns rising off the lake at dusk, each with a wish written on it in glowing ink.\ntext: On Lantern Night every student writes a wish on a paper lantern and lets it go over the lake. Rumour says the lanterns that sink were wishes the lake wanted for itself.\n> left: Wish for something selfish\n  chance even:\n    gold +large\n    say: Your lantern rises high. The next morning you find coins in your shoe. The lake is generous, and a little creepy.\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: Your lantern sinks with a hiss. Something under the water now knows what you want.\n    flag lake_knows\n    alignment -small\n> right: Wish for someone else\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +medium\n  renown +small\n  say: Your lantern rises and catches the wind. Across the shore, someone you have never met laughs out loud for no reason.\n\n@event kael_arrives\ntitle: The Boy From the North\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 3\npriority: 30\nactor: kael\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: A pale young man in a fur-lined coat standing at the door of the dining hall, frost spreading from his boots across the floor.\ntext: Mid-term, the dining hall doors open and frost crawls across the floor. A boy in a fur coat walks in, takes the empty seat beside you, and says nothing at all. The headmistress introduces him as Kael Dumont, a transfer. She does not say from where.\n> left: Leave him be\n  meet kael\n  rel kael +tiny\n  say: You eat in silence. When you leave, your cup of tea has frozen solid.\n> right: Offer him the bread\n  meet kael\n  rel kael +medium\n  say: He looks at the bread as if no one has offered him anything in a long time. \"...Thank you.\" His voice sounds like wind over snow.\n  flag kael_kindness\n\n@event winter_trials\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 4\"); replaced by term-end exams (engine, design §7.3)\ntitle: The Winter Trials\npool: calendar_auto_story\ntone: tense\nwhen: false\nart: hourglass\ntags: study exam retired\nscene: A vast examination hall where floating quills hover over a hundred desks and the windows are thick with frost.\ntext: The Winter Trials. A hundred desks, a hundred floating quills, one very old proctor who may be asleep. The first question asks you to explain the Third Law of Correspondence. The second asks you to demonstrate it.\n> left: Lean on theory\n  challenge research journeyman:\n    win:\n      study +large\n      mood +small\n      say: Your answer is so thorough the quill gets a cramp. Top marks.\n    lose:\n      study -large\n      mood -small\n      say: You write three pages and realise halfway that you have been answering a different question.\n> right: Lean on practice\n  challenge spell journeyman:\n    win:\n      study +large\n      renown +small\n      say: Your demonstration is clean and bright. The proctor wakes up and applauds.\n    lose:\n      study -large\n      health -small\n      say: The demonstration backfires. Politely, but with smoke.\n\n@event midwinter_ball\nslot: evening\ntitle: The Midwinter Ball\npool: calendar_auto_story\ntone: warm\nwhen: month == 4\ntype: menu\nart: music\ntags: festival romance social\nscene: The great hall transformed: an ice-crystal chandelier, couples waltzing a foot above the floor, snow falling indoors without ever landing.\ntext: The great hall has been turned to ice and starlight for the Midwinter Ball. Couples float above the floor. Who will you go with? You can only ask someone you know well.\n> lyra: Ask Lyra\n  when: met(lyra) and rel(lyra) >= 9\n  hint: {&She has had six invitations. She hasn't said yes to any.|She has had invitations again. She is pretending not to count them.}\n  challenge appeal vs lyra.charm +1 if spark(lyra) >= 25:\n    win:\n      spark lyra +medium\n      rel lyra +medium\n      mood +medium\n      queue ball_last_dance now with lyra\n      say: {bond(lyra) == \"partner\": \"Obviously,\" she says, before you finish asking. She leads, of course.|{flag(ball_lyra): \"Again? Fine. You've improved.\" She dances like a duel, and this year you nearly keep up.|\"Finally, someone who doesn't stammer.\" She dances like a duel — and wins.}}\n      flag ball_lyra\n    lose:\n      rel lyra -small\n      mood -small\n      say: \"I'm going alone. It's more efficient.\" You watch her dance alone all night, perfectly.\n> tobias: Ask Tobias\n  when: met(tobias) and rel(tobias) >= 9\n  hint: {flag(ball_tobias): He has been practising with a broom again. The broom has improved.|He has been practising with a broom.}\n  spark tobias +medium\n  rel tobias +medium\n  mood +medium\n  queue ball_last_dance now with tobias\n  say: {flag(ball_tobias): He only steps on your feet twice this year, and is so proud of it that he tells everyone. It's the best night of the term.|He steps on your feet nine times and apologises ten. It's the best night of the term.}\n  flag ball_tobias\n> seraphine: Ask Seraphine\n  when: met(seraphine) and rel(seraphine) >= 9\n  hint: She may already know you'll ask.\n  spark seraphine +medium\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  mood +medium\n  queue ball_last_dance now with seraphine\n  say: {flag(ball_seraphine): \"Same as last time,\" Seraphine says, \"but you'll wear blue.\" You hadn't planned to. You do.|\"I said yes three weeks ago,\" she says, \"you just hadn't asked yet.\" She dances a beat ahead of the music.}\n  flag ball_seraphine\n> corvin: Ask Corvin\n  when: met(corvin) and rel(corvin) >= 9\n  hint: Odds he shows up: unclear.\n  chance likely:\n    spark corvin +medium\n    mood +medium\n    queue ball_last_dance now with corvin\n    say: {flag(ball_corvin): He arrives on time, which alarms everyone, in a jacket he swears is borrowed. He is still the best dancer in the room.|He arrives late, in a stolen jacket, and is the best dancer in the room.}\n    flag ball_corvin\n  else:\n    mood -medium\n    say: {flag(ball_corvin_arrested): He never shows. A note arrives at midnight: \"Not arrested this time. Worse. Explain later.\" He never does.|He never shows. A note arrives at midnight: \"Got arrested. Long story. Save me a dance next year.\"}\n    flag ball_corvin_arrested\n> mir: Ask Mir\n  when: met(mir) and rel(mir) >= 9\n  hint: {flag(ball_mir): They promise the violin has been fixed.|They're bringing a self-playing violin.}\n  spark mir +medium\n  rel mir +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: {flag(ball_mir): The violin behaves all night. Mir is almost disappointed, until it plays the last waltz entirely on its own and bows.|Mir's violin plays itself, then plays the chandelier, then plays the windows. You are asked to leave early. Worth it.}\n  flag ball_mir\n> kael: Ask Kael\n  when: met(kael) and rel(kael) >= 12\n  hint: He doesn't seem to feel the cold.\n  spark kael +medium\n  rel kael +medium\n  mood +medium\n  queue ball_last_dance now with kael\n  say: {flag(ball_kael): He takes your hand without asking this time, as if it were already decided. The frost-ferns on the glass grow into a whole winter garden.|He holds your hand like it is something fragile. Where your fingers touch, frost-ferns bloom on the glass.}\n  flag ball_kael\n> nessa: Ask Nessa\n  when: met(nessa) and rel(nessa) >= 9\n  hint: She'll be working. She's always working.\n  spark nessa +medium\n  rel nessa +medium\n  renown +small\n  queue ball_last_dance now with nessa\n  say: {flag(ball_nessa): She leaves her notebook in her bag all night. \"The column can make something up,\" she says. It does.|She takes notes between dances. \"For the society column.\" Then she stops taking notes.}\n  flag ball_nessa\n> friends: Go with friends\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {flag(ball_friends): You go as a pack again, and this year someone has taught everyone the same dance, badly. It is still perfect.|You go as a pack and dance badly together and it is perfect.}\n  flag ball_friends\n> skip: Stay in and study\n  lore +small\n  study +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The music drifts up through the floor while you read. Somewhere, people are happy. You are productive.\n\n@thread tournament\nname: The Spring Tournament\npremise: Duelling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching.\njournal:\n  seen(tournament_semifinal) > 0: You fought your way to the semifinal of the Spring Tournament.\n  seen(tournament_final) > 0: In the final, Lyra was waiting. \"I was hoping it would be you.\"\n  flag(tournament_champion): You won the Spring Tournament, and the lawn erupted.\nstatus:\n  resolved: seen(tournament_final) > 0\n\n@event spring_tournament\ntitle: The Spring Tournament\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 8 and not member(duelling)\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nart: duel\ntags: festival rival\nthread: tournament\nscene: A ring of white stone on the lawn, crowds in the stands, two duelists bowing as their wands spark.\ntext: The Spring Tournament: dueling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching. Entry is open to anyone foolish enough.\n> left: Watch from the stands\n  mood +small\n  say: You eat candied chestnuts and scream for strangers.\n> right: Enter the tournament\n  challenge combat journeyman+ drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: First round: you win, barely, and {flag(tournament_champion): the crowd remembers your name from last year and chants it.|the crowd learns your name.}\n      queue tournament_semifinal now\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      renown +tiny\n      say: First round: you are knocked out of the ring before you've found your footing. The crowd has already turned to the next bout.\n\n@event spring_tournament_circle\ntitle: The Spring Tournament\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 8 and member(duelling)\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 40\nthread: tournament\nart: duel\ntags: rival\nscene: The great lawn ringed with banners, a chalk duelling circle at its centre and the Duelling Circle's names on a board.\ntext: The Spring Tournament: duelling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching. The Duelling Circle's names went up on the board a week ago, yours among them. {rank(duelling) >= 3: You are the captain, and the Circle expects its captain to win something.|The captain wants results, and says so.}\n> left: Fight to make a good showing\n  challenge combat journeyman drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      perform duelling +small\n      say: You win your first bout cleanly and lose the second honourably. {rank(duelling) >= 3: Your second-years cheer their captain louder than anyone in the stands.|The captain ticks your name, which from her is a medal.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You go out in the first round, on your feet at least. {rank(duelling) >= 3: Your second-years are kind about it, which is worse.|The captain says you fought like someone who expected to lose.}\n> right: Go for the title\n  challenge combat journeyman+ drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: First round: you win, barely, and the crowd learns your name.\n      queue tournament_semifinal now\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      renown +tiny\n      perform duelling -tiny\n      say: First round: you are knocked out of the ring before you've found your footing, and the Circle's bench goes very quiet.\n\n@event tournament_semifinal\ntitle: Semifinal\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: duel\ntags: rival\nthread: tournament\nscene: The same ring at sunset, scorch marks on the stone. Your opponent is grinning.\ntext: The semifinal. Your opponent is a fourth-year who has never lost. The crowd is louder now; you can hear your own name in it.\n> left: Fight clean\n  challenge combat expert- drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: You trade spells like questions and answers — and get the last word.\n      queue tournament_final now\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      renown +small\n      say: A good fight. Not good enough. The crowd applauds you off the lawn.\n> right: Fight dirty\n  alignment -small\n  challenge deceive adept drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      say: A feint, a trip, a jinx to the shoelaces. Legal. Mostly.{met(bram):  On the prefects' bench Bram watches the whole thing with his jaw set, and doesn't clap.|}\n      queue tournament_final now\n      if met(bram):\n        rel bram -small\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      say: The referee sees the trick. Disqualified, and booed.\n\n@event tournament_final\ntitle: The Final\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: trophy\ntags: rival festival\nthread: tournament\nscene: The dueling ring under floating lanterns, a silver cup on a plinth, and across from you, a familiar face.\ntext: The final. Across the ring stands {lyra}, wand raised, eyes blazing. \"I was hoping it would be you,\" she says, and she is not being friendly.\n> left: Give everything you have\n  meet lyra\n  challenge combat vs lyra.arcana +3:\n    win:\n      renown +huge\n      mood +large\n      flag tournament_champion\n      gain famous\n      rel lyra -medium\n      if bond(lyra) == \"none\":\n        bond lyra rival\n      say: Your final spell lands like a bell. Silence — then the lawn erupts. Lyra stares at you as if seeing you for the first time, then walks off the lawn without shaking your hand.\n    lose:\n      renown +medium\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: She wins, but only just. Afterwards, she offers her hand. \"Next year,\" she says. It is a promise and a threat.\n> right: Yield with a bow\n  meet lyra\n  rel lyra +large\n  renown +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The crowd boos. Lyra does not cheer her victory. Later she finds you: \"Why?\" You don't have a good answer, and she seems to like that.\n\n@event grand_exams\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 10\"); replaced by term-end exams (engine, design §7.3)\ntitle: Grand Examinations\npool: calendar_auto_story\ntone: tense\nwhen: false\nart: scroll\ntags: study exam retired\nscene: A circular examination chamber with a domed ceiling of moving constellations; examiners in grey robes sit behind a long table.\ntext: Year-end examinations. Three days, five examiners and a practical in front of a panel that includes the headmistress. Your standing for the year rides on this.\n> left: Prepare a dazzling practical\n  challenge spell adept+:\n    win:\n      study +large\n      renown +small\n      say: You finish your practical and the examiners actually lean forward. Distinction.\n    lose:\n      study -huge\n      mood -medium\n      say: The practical sputters. An examiner writes something very short.\n> right: Write a meticulous thesis\n  challenge research adept+:\n    win:\n      study +large\n      mood +small\n      say: Your thesis is cited by one examiner in an argument with another. Distinction.\n    lose:\n      study -huge\n      mood -medium\n      say: Your thesis is described, aloud, as \"ambitious\". It is not a compliment.\n\n@event summer_plans\n# retired in phase 2b (was \"month == 11\"); replaced by break_plan\ntitle: Summer Recess\npool: calendar_auto_story\ntone: warm\nwhen: false\ntype: menu\nart: sun\ntags: home retired\nscene: The academy gates thrown open to green hills; students with trunks waving from departing sky-barges.\ntext: The towers empty for the summer. Two months of freedom. How will you spend them?\n> home: Go home\n  hint: Rest and recover. No rent while away.\n  gain summer_away\n  health +large\n  mood +large\n  if has(origin_noble):\n    say: Your family hosts three dinners in your honour and asks about your marriage prospects at all of them.\n  elif has(origin_street):\n    say: The old crowd in Port Calder treats you like a returning hero, and steals your trunk only once.\n  else:\n    say: Home is smaller than you remember, and warmer. Someone made your favourite pie.\n> work: Take summer work in the village\n  hint: About 50 gold over the summer. Tiring.\n  gain summer_work\n  vigor +small\n  say: You haul crates, serve tables and polish brass until your hands know the shape of every coin.\n> study: Stay and study in the empty library\n  hint: +Lore, +Arcana, +Standing. Lonely.\n  lore +medium\n  arcana +medium\n  study +small\n  mood -small\n  say: Just you, the books and the dust motes. You learn things the term never had time for.\n> travel: Travel to the coast\n  hint: Costs 40 gold. Great for mood.\n  when: gold >= 40\n  locked: Needs 40 crowns.\n  gold -large\n  gain summer_away\n  mood +huge\n  charm +small\n  say: Salt air, shell markets, a sea serpent at sunset. You come back tanned and ten years younger.\n> expedition: Join Old Maud's herb expedition\n  hint: Requires knowing Maud. Adventure.\n  when: met(maud)\n  gain summer_away\n  alchemy +medium\n  vigor +small\n  rel maud +large\n  health +small\n  say: Two months in the Whisperwood with Maud and her goat. You learn which mushrooms sing and which ones lie.\n\n@event summer_letter\ntitle: A Letter From Home\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 12 and not has(summer_away) and not wrapup\nrepeat: 12\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A letter sealed with candle wax lying on a sunlit windowsill.\ntext: A letter arrives in the last month of summer, the handwriting so familiar it hurts. They want to know how you are doing. Really doing.\n> left: Write back honestly\n  mood +small\n  if (turn > 4 and gpa < 2.0):\n    mood -medium\n    say: You tell them about your grades. Their reply is kind, which is worse.\n  else:\n    say: You write six pages. It feels like setting down a heavy bag.\n> right: Write back that everything is perfect\n  mood -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance even:\n    gold +medium\n    say: Proud of you, they write, and enclose a little money{has(origin_noble) or has(origin_merchant): , as if a purse were the right answer to a letter.|  you suspect they couldn't spare.}\n  else:\n    say: They reply with exclamation marks. The lie sits in your stomach all week.\n\n@event starfall\nslot: night\ntitle: Starfall\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 12 and year < 4 and not off_campus\nrepeat: 12\npriority: 30\ntype: menu\nart: star\ntags: festival\nscene: Students lying on the observatory roof under a sky streaked with falling stars.\ntext: {year == 1: The last night of the academic year. Everyone climbs onto the observatory roof, and the rest spill out onto the lawns with blankets, to watch the stars fall. They say if you catch one in a jar, you get a year of luck.|Starfall again. The observatory roof, the lawns full of blankets, the whole academy lying on its back.} {starfall_jar >= 1: Your jar is ready, cork in hand.|} {~The first star falls before anyone is ready.|Somebody has brought a lute, unwisely.|The lake is full of reflected stars.}\n> left: Lie back and just watch\n  mood +medium\n  health +small\n  starfall_jar = 0\n  say: You count forty falling stars and then stop counting. It's enough.\n> right: Try to catch a star\n  hint: Nobody has caught one in years. A good jar from the observatory improves the odds.\n  if starfall_jar >= 1:\n    challenge feat apprentice unlikely luck:\n      win:\n        renown +small\n        mood +small\n        gain inspired\n        queue starfall_caught\n        say: It lands in your jar with a hiss and glows for weeks. Students come to see it.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        say: You lunge, miss, and nearly roll off the roof. A prefect catches you by the collar.\n  else:\n    challenge feat apprentice slim luck:\n      win:\n        renown +small\n        mood +small\n        gain inspired\n        queue starfall_caught\n        say: It lands in your jar with a hiss and glows for weeks. Students come to see it.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        say: You lunge, miss, and nearly roll off the roof. A prefect catches you by the collar.\n  starfall_jar = 0\n> together: Find your sweetheart on the lawn\n  when: bonds_around(partner) > 0\n  starfall_jar = 0\n  queue starfall_together now\n  say: You leave the roof and go looking. You know exactly where to look.\n> wish: Make the Starfall wish\n  starfall_jar = 0\n  queue starfall_wish now\n  say: You climb to the very top of the roof, where the old custom says the wishes go.\n\n@event year_two\ntitle: Second Year\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 13\npriority: 55\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: The seven towers in autumn colours, the first-year barge arriving below, tiny and nervous.\ntext: The barge brings new first-years, and you watch them gawp at the towers. Was that really you a year ago? You know which staircases move now, and which professors you can charm. Second-years, a prefect announces, may apply for summer research posts in Stormbloom.{chosen(break_plan, home):  Your trunk still smells of home, and there's a cake in it you were told to share.|}\n> ok: Begin the second year\n  mood +small\n  renown +small\n  say: You unpack faster than last year, and in the right order. Someone in your year already has a list of the research posts, and the castle's midnight doors, side by side.\n\n@event term_begins\ntitle: The Spring Term\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: term and month == 7\nrepeat: 4\npriority: 85\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: The castle steps after the winter break, trunks stacked by the doors and the first lamps lit in the towers.\ntext: {chosen(break_plan, home): You come back from home with a tin of something your family insists you'll need, and the castle feels enormous after a winter of small rooms.|{chosen(break_plan, work): Your holiday wages are in your purse and your feet still ache from earning them. The castle fills up around you, trunk by trunk.|After a winter of empty corridors, the castle fills up around you again, and the noise of it is almost too much.}} The spring term begins, and the registrar's hall is already queueing.\n> left: Get a head start on the reading\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You find a window seat in the library before the term has properly started. By the time the others arrive, you have a list of questions and a crick in your neck.\n> right: Catch up with everyone first\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You spend the first evening in the common room, then at the Newt, hearing everyone's winter. The reading can wait a day. It waits three.\n\n@event year_three\ntitle: Third Year\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 25\npriority: 55\nart: crown\ntags: story\nscene: The headmistress addressing a hall of third-years, every candle in the room leaning toward her.\ntext: \"Third years,\" the headmistress says, \"you are past the middle of your time here. Next year will ask a great deal of you; spend this one finding out what you want from it.\" She looks, for a moment, directly at you.{chosen(break_plan, home):  You came straight from home, and the summer's quiet is still on you.|}\n> ok: Begin the third year\n  mood +tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: You know the castle well enough now to get lost in it on purpose. This is the last year for a summer research post, and the holiday notices by the gates pay better every year.\n\n# Marrow asks again (feedback 2026-10-20): the answer you gave her at the Welcome (intro_what_is_just), read back on the eve\n# of graduation, and whether the four years kept to it, by where your alignment ended up: mercy is Lumen, fairness the\n# middle, keeping your own safe Hollow. It changes nothing; it only remembers.\n@event isolde_asks_again\nslot: evening\ntitle: Ask Me Again\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns and (flag(just_mercy) or flag(just_fair) or flag(just_strong))\npriority: 65\nart: scales\ntags: story\nscene: A grey-haired headmistress in an evening cloister holding a small worn notebook open, a final-year in graduation robes beside her.\ntext: Headmistress Marrow finds you in the cloister, the same small notebook under her arm, four years more worn. \"I asked you what was just,\" she says, and reads it back: {flag(just_mercy): \"Mercy.\"|{flag(just_fair): \"Fairness.\"|\"Keeping your own safe.\"}} She looks at you over the page. {(flag(just_mercy) and alignment >= 20) or (flag(just_fair) and alignment > -20 and alignment < 20) or (flag(just_strong) and alignment <= -20): \"You kept to it. I watched.\"|\"You changed your answer without telling me. Most people do.\"} \"Well? I'm asking again.\"\n> left: Give the same answer\n  say: {(flag(just_mercy) and alignment >= 20) or (flag(just_fair) and alignment > -20 and alignment < 20) or (flag(just_strong) and alignment <= -20): You say it again, and this time you know what it cost. She writes it under the first, in the same hand. \"Good,\" she says. \"Some answers are worth giving twice.\"|You say it again, though you both know the four years said something else. She writes it down anyway. \"Then go and live up to it,\" she says. \"You've time. Not much, but some.\"}\n> right: Give a new answer\n  say: You tell her what four years taught you instead. She listens to all of it, writes it under the first, and closes the notebook on both. \"That,\" she says, \"is what I hoped the castle would do to you.\"\n\n@event graduation_eve\nslot: night\ntitle: The Eve of Graduation\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 60\nart: crown\ntags: story\nscene: Graduation robes laid out on a dormitory bed, a single candle burning, the towers lit gold outside the window.\ntext: Tomorrow you graduate. Your robes are laid out on the bed, slightly too long. Outside, the towers are lit gold. Four years have gone like a single held breath.\n> left: Spend the night with friends\n  mood +medium\n  say: {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0: Laughter until dawn. Promises to write. Some of them, you'll keep.|You knock on a few doors. Nobody who answers is a close friend, not yet, but they pour you a cup and keep you up laughing till dawn. You trade addresses anyway.}\n> right: Walk the halls alone one last time\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Every corridor holds a memory. At the end of the last one, the castle sighs, as if saying goodbye.\n"},{"path":"content/events/careers.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Careers and offers (design §7.7; feedback 2026-10-25). A job\n#  comes only through a card: an offer is a flag `offer_<career>`\n#  that a letter, an interview or a return offer leaves, and on\n#  the last day (`last_career`, last_day.grim) you sign one of\n#  them (`took_<career>`), which is the yearbook's headline. No\n#  offer by the wrap-up brings the last call (month 47), which\n#  always holds one sure post. The career fair is in\n#  expansion_y3.grim.\n# ============================================================\n\n# Any offer still open (the last call comes only without one).\n@cond has_offer\nis: flag(offer_conclave) or flag(offer_royal_library) or flag(offer_fellowship) or flag(offer_guild) or flag(offer_shop) or flag(offer_unseen) or flag(offer_trading) or flag(offer_teaching) or flag(offer_circus) or flag(offer_observatory) or flag(offer_field_station) or flag(offer_thornwick) or flag(offer_spire) or flag(offer_newt) or flag(offer_pell) or flag(offer_courier) or flag(offer_clerk)\n\n# The career fair's employer, once an interview there ends in an offer (expansion_y3.grim).\n@preset fair_offer\ndesc: Turns the career fair's choice into an offer.\ndo:\n  if flag(career_conclave):\n    flag offer_conclave\n  elif flag(career_library):\n    flag offer_royal_library\n  elif flag(career_guild):\n    flag offer_guild\n  elif flag(career_unseen):\n    flag offer_unseen\n  elif flag(career_circus):\n    flag offer_circus\n  elif flag(career_teaching):\n    flag offer_teaching\n\n# Letters in the final year: at most one a month (career_letter_turn), each once.\n@pool career_letter\ndesc: A letter with a post in it, in the final year: one a month at most.\ntrigger: auto\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: job story\nchain: careers\npriority: 40\nwhen: final_year and month >= 3 and month <= 11 and career_letter_turn < turn\n\n# ---------------- Head-hunted: what you're known for brings a letter ----------------\n\n@event career_letter_conclave\ntitle: A Letter From the Seven Towers\npool: career_letter\nwhen: arcana >= 80 and gpa >= 2.5 and not flag(offer_conclave)\nscene: A letter sealed in silver wax on a dormitory desk, seven small towers pressed into the seal.\ntext: Silver wax, seven little towers in it. The Conclave writes that Professor Thorne has mentioned your name, \"more than once and without being asked\", and offers you an apprenticeship at the Seven Towers after graduation. Your answer is due on the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Write back that you're interested\n  flag offer_conclave\n  mood +small\n  say: You write three drafts and send the shortest. Thorne passes you in the corridor the next day and says \"Hm,\" which from him is a parade.\n> right: Ask what an apprentice actually does\n  flag offer_conclave\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The answer comes by return: long hours, a grey robe, and seven years before anyone lets you near a real ward. It is less glamorous on paper. The offer stands.\n\n@event career_letter_fellowship\ntitle: A Fellowship at the Spire\npool: career_letter\nwhen: (lore >= 98 or (flag(thesis_distinction) and lore >= 85)) and gpa >= 2.5 and not flag(offer_fellowship)\nscene: A folded note tucked inside a returned library book, written in tiny, careful faculty handwriting.\ntext: The note is inside a book you returned last week, which is how the Spire Library's fellows do things. They have a junior fellowship free, a carrel with your name already chalked on it, and a question: would you like to spend your life among books that bite? Answer by the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Chalk \"yes\" under your name\n  flag offer_fellowship\n  mood +small\n  say: You go down to the carrel that evening and chalk a small \"yes\" under your name. In the morning someone has underlined it.\n> right: Ask how many fellows the books have eaten\n  flag offer_fellowship\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Three,\" the reply says, \"in two hundred years, and all of them were careless.\" You are not entirely reassured. The carrel waits.\n\n@event career_letter_shop\ntitle: A Lease by the Harbour\npool: career_letter\nwhen: alchemy >= 72 and not flag(offer_shop)\nscene: A rolled lease tied with string on a dormitory desk, a small brass key on top of it.\ntext: The lease comes with a brass key and a letter from a harbour landlord whose wife swears by your hair tonic. A crooked little shop, two rooms and a still, yours from the autumn if you sign on the last day. Nobody has ever offered you a door of your own before.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Keep the key on a string round your neck\n  flag offer_shop\n  mood +small\n  say: You wear the key under your robes for the rest of the year. It's warm when you touch it, which is probably the hair tonic's doing.\n> right: Write back about the drains\n  flag offer_shop\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The landlord's reply is two pages about the drains, which you read twice. An alchemist's shop lives or dies by its drains. This one, apparently, will live.\n\n@event career_letter_unseen\ntitle: A Letter That Wasn't Posted\npool: career_letter\nwhen: guile >= 75 and not flag(offer_unseen)\nscene: A plain envelope with no stamp and no seal lying on a pillow that was empty a moment ago.\ntext: The envelope is on your pillow, though the door was locked and the window shut. Inside, a single line: the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs has a desk for you, if you want it, from the autumn. There is no address to reply to. They'll know your answer on the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Leave a \"yes\" on the pillow\n  flag offer_unseen\n  mood +small\n  say: You leave one word on the pillow and go to supper. When you come back, the paper is gone and the bed has been made. You did not make it.\n> right: Find out how they got in\n  flag offer_unseen\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You spend a week on it and find the scratch on the window catch, the loose tile, the borrowed key. It's very good work. That's the trouble.\n\n@event career_letter_trading\ntitle: A Partnership Offered\npool: career_letter\nwhen: gold >= 180 and not flag(offer_trading)\nscene: A thick letter on cream paper, stamped with a shipping line's anchor, a tally of figures down its margin.\ntext: A shipping house in the harbour has heard about your purse, or rather about how you filled it. Its old partner wants to retire, and offers you his desk and his share of the ledger from the autumn. \"Bring your crowns,\" he writes. \"Bring your nerve. Answer on the last day.\"\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Write back that you're interested\n  flag offer_trading\n  mood +small\n  say: You reply on the cream paper's own back, which seems businesslike. By return comes a ledger page with a column already headed with your name.\n> right: Ask to see the books first\n  flag offer_trading\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He sends them, all of them, and you find the hole in the third year that he hoped you'd find. \"Good,\" says his next letter. \"The last partner didn't.\"\n\n# ---------------- Asked back: a strong internship's return offer ----------------\n\n@event career_return_royal_library\ntitle: The Archives Write Again\npool: career_letter\nwhen: flag(return_royal_library) and not flag(offer_royal_library)\nscene: A letter in archivist's copperplate, a pressed slip of catalogue card tucked into the fold.\ntext: The Royal Library Archives remember your summer. The keeper writes that a cataloguer's post is free from the autumn, \"for someone who reads the footnotes\", and that your name was the only one proposed. Answer by the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Write back in your best copperplate\n  flag offer_royal_library\n  mood +small\n  say: It takes an evening and four nibs. The keeper replies with a catalogue card made out in your name, under \"Staff\".\n> right: Ask which room you'd be given\n  flag offer_royal_library\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"The cold one,\" the keeper writes, \"with the folios that object to being opened.\" You will need gloves. You will also, you suspect, love it.\n\n@event career_return_conclave\ntitle: The Laboratory Writes Again\npool: career_letter\nwhen: flag(return_conclave) and not flag(offer_conclave)\nscene: A letter on laboratory paper, slightly singed at one corner, sealed with seven small towers.\ntext: The Conclave Laboratory remembers your summer, and the report your supervisor wrote at the end of it. She writes that a place as a junior researcher is yours from the autumn if you'll take it. \"Don't make me ask twice,\" she adds. Answer by the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Write back yes, in principle\n  flag offer_conclave\n  mood +small\n  say: You write back the same night. Her reply is one line: \"Good. Bring your own gloves.\"\n> right: Ask what you'd be testing\n  flag offer_conclave\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Wards that are meant to fail, she writes, so that the ones on the city gates don't. You'd be standing very close to a great many explosions.\n\n@event career_return_guild\ntitle: The Works Write Again\npool: career_letter\nwhen: flag(return_guild) and not flag(offer_guild)\nscene: A grubby letter with a chemical stain on the envelope, stamped with the Guild of Alchemists' retort.\ntext: The Guild of Alchemists' works remember your summer, and how you kept your head at the vats. The foreman writes, in a hand that has clearly been burned more than once, that a journeyman's bench is yours from the autumn. Answer by the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Write back yes, in principle\n  flag offer_guild\n  mood +small\n  say: You write back yes. The foreman's reply is a list of protective clothing you will need to buy yourself, which you take as a welcome.\n> right: Ask about the explosions\n  flag offer_guild\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Two a month,\" he writes, \"and none fatal since my father's day.\" You read it three times. The bench is still yours.\n\n@event career_return_observatory\ntitle: The Expedition Writes Again\npool: career_letter\nwhen: flag(return_observatory) and not flag(offer_observatory)\nscene: A letter with a star chart sketched on the back, the paper creased from a long journey off a plateau.\ntext: The Observatory Expedition remembers your summer on the plateau. Its leader writes that next year's survey needs a chartmaker who can stay awake till dawn, and a salaried place is yours from the autumn. Answer by the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Write back yes, in principle\n  flag offer_observatory\n  mood +small\n  say: You write back under the star chart, adding one they'd missed. The leader's reply circles it twice.\n> right: Ask how cold the plateau gets\n  flag offer_observatory\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Very, is the answer, with a list of the frostbite remedies they carry. The stars, the letter adds, are worth it. You remember that they are.\n\n@event career_return_field_station\ntitle: The Field Station Writes Again\npool: career_letter\nwhen: flag(return_field_station) and not flag(offer_field_station)\nscene: A muddy letter with a feather pressed in it and a paw print smudged across the address.\ntext: The Beastcraft Field Station remembers your summer on the moors. The warden writes that a keeper's post is yours from the autumn, with a cottage, a lamp and more creatures than any sane person would count. Answer by the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Write back yes, in principle\n  flag offer_field_station\n  mood +small\n  say: You write back yes. The next letter has a second paw print on it, which the warden says belongs to whoever was sitting on the desk, and counts as a vote.\n> right: Ask about the winters\n  flag offer_field_station\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Long, wet and lonely, the warden writes, honestly, and the best work there is. You believe the second half.\n\n@event career_return_thornwick\ntitle: Thornwick Writes Again\npool: career_letter\nwhen: (flag(return_thornwick) or flag(thornwick_offer)) and not flag(offer_thornwick)\nscene: A green-sealed letter on lacquered paper, from the rival academy over the hills.\ntext: Thornwick's illusion laboratory remembers your summer{flag(thornwick_offer): and the offer you kept sealed in your trunk|}. The dean writes that a researcher's bench is yours from the autumn, across the hills, among people who think glamour is a science. Answer by the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Write back yes, in principle\n  flag offer_thornwick\n  mood +small\n  say: You write back yes, in green ink, to annoy someone. The dean's reply says the ink was noticed and admired.\n> right: Ask what Lumenhold would say\n  flag offer_thornwick\n  charm +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Something cutting,\" the dean writes, \"and then they'd hire you back at twice the salary.\" You suspect she's right about both.\n\n@event career_return_unseen\ntitle: The Office Writes Again\npool: career_letter\nwhen: flag(return_unseen) and not flag(offer_unseen)\nscene: A blank sheet of paper on a desk that, held to a candle, shows writing in brown ink.\ntext: A blank page, until you hold it to the candle. The Office of Unseen Affairs remembers your summer, and how few questions you asked. A desk is yours from the autumn. No reply is needed; they will know your answer on the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Burn it, as they'd want\n  flag offer_unseen\n  mood +small\n  say: You burn it over the candle and grind the ash into the grate. It feels like the first day of something.\n> right: Keep it, and read it again\n  flag offer_unseen\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You keep it, which they'll know, and read it by candle every few nights. There's a second line you missed the first time: \"We'd have kept it too.\"\n\n# ---------------- Kept on: a part-time job that goes well ----------------\n\n@event career_job_spire\ntitle: The Stacks Want You Back\npool: career_letter\nwhen: member(library_assistant) and (performance(library_assistant) >= 60 or rank(library_assistant) >= 2) and not flag(offer_spire)\nscene: The library's reference desk after hours, a note left under the bell in the head librarian's careful hand.\ntext: A note under the bell on the reference desk, after your shift. The head librarian wants to keep you on after graduation, full time, as a keeper of the stacks. \"Footnote has stopped biting you,\" it says. \"That is a qualification.\" Answer by the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Leave a note under the bell: yes\n  flag offer_spire\n  mood +small\n  say: You leave your answer under the bell. In the morning there's a second note: \"Good. Bring a longer ladder.\"\n> right: Ask about the restricted shelves\n  flag offer_spire\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"In time,\" says the next note, \"and only with gloves.\" It is not a no, and you find you're counting the years.\n\n@event career_job_newt\ntitle: The Landlady's Offer\npool: career_letter\nwhen: member(tavern) and (performance(tavern) >= 60 or rank(tavern) >= 2) and not flag(offer_newt)\nscene: The Gilded Newt after closing, chairs up on the tables, the landlady counting the till with a cloth over her shoulder.\ntext: After closing, the landlady counts the till, then counts you in. She wants to keep you on after graduation, full time, behind the bar and, in a year or two, in charge of it. \"You remember who ordered the spiced cider,\" she says. \"That's rarer than magic.\" Answer by the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Shake on it, in principle\n  flag offer_newt\n  mood +small\n  say: You shake on it over the bar. She pours you a cider on the house, which has never happened to anyone before.\n> right: Ask about the card games upstairs\n  flag offer_newt\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"You'd look the other way,\" she says, \"and so would I.\" The offer stands. So does the question of what you'd be looking away from.\n\n@event career_job_pell\ntitle: The Counter at Pell's\npool: career_letter\nwhen: member(apothecary_clerk) and (performance(apothecary_clerk) >= 60 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 2) and not flag(offer_pell)\nscene: A narrow apothecary counter, a thousand labelled drawers behind it, an old man counting drops aloud at the back.\ntext: Pell's daughters ask you into the back, where Old Pell is counting drops. They want to keep you on after graduation, full time, at the counter and, one day, at the drawers that nobody else may open. \"Father says you count well,\" says the eldest. Answer by the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Say yes, in principle\n  flag offer_pell\n  mood +small\n  say: Old Pell stops counting long enough to nod at you, then starts again from one. His daughters say that's the highest praise there is.\n> right: Ask about the drawers that never open\n  flag offer_pell\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"In ten years,\" says the eldest, \"if you're still careful.\" You realise you'd like to still be careful in ten years.\n\n@event career_job_courier\ntitle: A Satchel of Your Own\npool: career_letter\nwhen: member(broom_courier) and (performance(broom_courier) >= 60 or rank(broom_courier) >= 2) and not flag(offer_courier)\nscene: A courier loft at dusk, brooms racked along the wall, a satchel with a newly stitched name hanging on a peg.\ntext: There's a new satchel on your peg at the loft, with your name stitched on the flap. The chief wants to keep you on after graduation, full time, flying the lake in all weathers. \"The broom still pulls left,\" she says. \"You don't.\" Answer by the last day.\non_show:\n  career_letter_turn = turn\n> left: Sling the satchel over your shoulder\n  flag offer_courier\n  mood +small\n  say: It fits. You fly a lap of the lake that evening for no reason at all, and the loft cheers you down.\n> right: Ask about the winter routes\n  flag offer_courier\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The chief shows you the map of the winter routes, with the crosses where couriers came down. There are not many. There are some.\n\n# ---------------- The last call (month 47): one sure post, always ----------------\n\n@event career_last_call\ntitle: The Last Call\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: wrapup and month == 11 and not has_offer()\npriority: 68\ntype: menu\nart: scroll\ntags: job story\nthread: careers\nscene: The registrar's window in the entrance hall at closing time, a short typed list of posts pinned beside it, one student left in the queue.\ntext: Everyone else seems to have a letter. The registrar, who has seen this a hundred times, pins a short list beside her window: posts still open for this year's graduates. \"Some people find their work late,\" she says. \"Some people find it here. Some people find it in a fortnight's time, in their mother's kitchen. Choose.\"\n> back: Go back to where you worked\n  hint: The people who know your work. A friendly interview.\n  when: performance(intern_royal_library) > 0 or performance(intern_conclave) > 0 or performance(intern_alchemists) > 0 or performance(intern_expedition) > 0 or performance(intern_field_station) > 0 or performance(intern_thornwick) > 0 or performance(intern_unseen_office) > 0 or performance(library_assistant) > 0 or performance(tavern) > 0 or performance(apothecary_clerk) > 0 or performance(broom_courier) > 0\n  challenge appeal apprentice likely drift none:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      if performance(intern_conclave) > 0:\n        flag offer_conclave\n      elif performance(intern_royal_library) > 0:\n        flag offer_royal_library\n      elif performance(intern_alchemists) > 0:\n        flag offer_guild\n      elif performance(intern_unseen_office) > 0:\n        flag offer_unseen\n      elif performance(intern_expedition) > 0:\n        flag offer_observatory\n      elif performance(intern_field_station) > 0:\n        flag offer_field_station\n      elif performance(intern_thornwick) > 0:\n        flag offer_thornwick\n      elif performance(library_assistant) > 0:\n        flag offer_spire\n      elif performance(apothecary_clerk) > 0:\n        flag offer_pell\n      elif performance(broom_courier) > 0:\n        flag offer_courier\n      else:\n        flag offer_newt\n      say: You knock on a door you know. The person behind it remembers you, and remembers your work better than you do. \"We'd been wondering when you'd ask,\" they say, and write your name on a list that matters.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You knock on a door you know. They remember you warmly, and they've filled the post, last week, with someone who asked first. They wish you luck and mean it.\n> registrar: Take the registrar's list\n  hint: A clerk's post, here or in the village. Yours if you want it.\n  flag offer_clerk\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You put your name to the first post on the list: a junior clerk's desk, stamps and ledgers, a window that looks at a wall. It isn't a dream. It is a start date, and today that feels like a great deal.\n> reach: Write to the post you really want\n  hint: A long shot, in your best subject.\n  challenge appeal expert unlikely drift none:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      if arcana >= lore and arcana >= alchemy and arcana >= guile and arcana >= charm and arcana >= vigor:\n        flag offer_conclave\n      elif lore >= alchemy and lore >= guile and lore >= charm and lore >= vigor:\n        flag offer_royal_library\n      elif alchemy >= guile and alchemy >= charm and alchemy >= vigor:\n        flag offer_guild\n      elif guile >= charm and guile >= vigor:\n        flag offer_unseen\n      elif charm >= vigor:\n        flag offer_circus\n      else:\n        flag offer_field_station\n      say: You write the letter you'd been too proud to write at the fair, and send it before you can tear it up. A reply comes by the next owl: come and see us. You go. They say yes.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You write the letter you'd been too proud to write at the fair. The reply is kind and short, and the post went in the autumn to someone who wrote then.\n> wait: Leave it until after graduation\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You thank the registrar and walk away from the window. The world will still be there in a fortnight. So, she says to your back, will her list.\n"},{"path":"content/events/cartographers.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The Midnight Cartographers, expanded (threads 1, docs/threads-1-plan.md Part E).\n#  - The Master Map (a quest thread): Corvin, its new Keeper, shows a member the map's two great blanks. Draw two\n#    lines on it (either blank, or a blank and the shortcut found inside it) and he asks you to keep it with him.\n#    Two of the blanks open the midnight heists: the stair below the chained shelf (`heist_stacks_known`, lead\n#    lead_deep_stacks; content/events/heists/stacks.grim) and the seventh tower's wall stair (`heist_tower_known`,\n#    lead lead_tower_stair; heists/tower.grim). Once a blank is filled, a shortcut inside it can be charted\n#    (`carto_book_lift`, `carto_tower_chimney`). None of it touches the main plot: the tower blank is the wall, and the\n#    chimney comes out among the old classrooms, well below the top room.\n#  - Routine society cards (drawn at the third gargoyle, like the club's others) and two midnight cards there.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Master Map (quest) ----------------\n\n@thread carto_master_map\nfrom: quest\nwith: begun=carto_map_chain\nwhen: turn >= 8\ntrack: cartographers\nclues: lead_master_map lead_deep_stacks lead_tower_stair\nname: The Master Map\npremise: The Cartographers' Master Map has two great blanks nobody has filled in thirty years. Draw two lines there, and Corvin will ask you to keep the map with him.\njournal:\n  flag(carto_map_chain): Corvin unrolled the Master Map and showed you its two great blanks. \"Two lines on it,\" he said, \"and you keep it with me.\"\n  flag(heist_stacks_known): You charted the stair under the last chained shelf, down to the deep stacks and the confiscation vault.\n  flag(heist_tower_known): You found the seventh tower's second stair, inside the wall, from the cellars to the landing of candles.\n  flag(carto_book_lift): You rode the old book-lift down its shaft, from the bindery deep into the stacks, to where the shelves start breathing.\n  flag(carto_tower_chimney): You climbed the seventh tower's old chimney from the cellars and came out in a classroom a floor above the landing of candles.\n  flag(carto_map_keeper): Corvin put the Master Map in your hands. You keep it together now.\n  flag(carto_map_done) and not flag(carto_map_keeper): You told Corvin to keep the map himself. He looked relieved.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(carto_map_done)\nmembers:\n  start carto_map_blanks\n  part carto_deep_stacks flag=heist_stacks_known\n  part carto_tower_stair flag=heist_tower_known\n  part carto_book_lift flag=carto_book_lift\n  part carto_tower_chimney flag=carto_tower_chimney\n  final carto_map_keeper\n\n@event carto_map_blanks\ntitle: The Blanks on the Map\nthread: carto_master_map\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: member(cartographers)\nactor: corvin\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A huge map in shifting silver ink spread on the stone floor of a secret passage, weighted with candle stubs, two regions of it completely blank.\ntext: {seen(carto_map_blanks) > 1: Corvin has the Master Map out again, and the two blanks are still white as snow. \"Still thirty years,\" he says. \"Still yours if you want them.\"|Corvin has the Master Map out on the passage floor, weighted with candle stubs: silver lines that move a little, like breathing, and two great blanks. One is under the library, below the last chained shelf; one is inside the seventh tower's wall. \"The last Keeper left it to me this summer,\" he says. \"Two lines on those, and you keep it with me. Fancy it?\"}\n> left: \"Show me where to start.\"\n  flag carto_map_chain\n  find lead_master_map\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Corvin grins and rolls the map to the library end. \"The chained shelf, then,\" he says. \"Bring chalk. Don't touch the chains.\"\n  chain_beat = turn\n  queue carto_deep_stacks now\n> right: \"Not this year.\"\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {seen(carto_map_blanks) > 1: \"One day,\" says Corvin, and rolls it up without a joke this time.|\"Thirty-one years, then,\" says Corvin, and rolls the map up a little too neatly.}\n\n@event carto_deep_stacks\ntitle: The Stair Under the Chained Shelf\nthread: carto_master_map\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: book\ntags: club mystery danger\nscene: The bottom shelf of a dark library aisle, iron chains running from the books down through holes in the floorboards, a student kneeling with a stub of chalk.\ntext: Below the east stacks' last chained shelf in the Restricted Section, by the book-return hatch, the Master Map simply stops. The chains on that shelf run down through the floor, and at night something below pulls them taut. Corvin's way in is a book-return hatch nobody has locked since it was built. The shelf is yours to chart.\n> left: Find the stair and pace it down\n  challenge research using guile journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag heist_stacks_known\n      find lead_deep_stacks\n      guile +small\n      say: Behind the shelf's kickboard, a stair goes down forty-four steps, between shelves that breathe in their sleep, to stacks no lamp reaches. At the bottom is a door with a prefects' padlock: the confiscation vault. You chart it all, and come up very quietly.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You find the stair, and it turns more times than it has steps. You come up in the same aisle you went down from, with your chalk worn to nothing and the chains above you swinging.\n  next\n> right: Copy what the old margins say\n  lore +small\n  say: The oldest map in the club has a note in the margin by the chained shelf, in brown ink: \"Stair: yes. Down: no. Ask the chains.\" You copy it out neatly. The blank stays blank.\n\n@event carto_tower_stair\ntitle: Two Strides Fatter\nthread: carto_master_map\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: door\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A student in a dark vaulted cellar pressing an ear to a curved stone wall, a candle and a coil of measuring string at their feet.\ntext: The seventh tower's blank on the Master Map isn't its own stair, locked as it is; that's on every old plan. It's the wall. Corvin has measured the tower from outside and from in, and the outside is two strides fatter. \"There's a second stair in there,\" he says. \"A servants' stair, from the cellars up. Find its door.\"\n> left: Sound the cellar walls\n  challenge research using guile journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag heist_tower_known\n      find lead_tower_stair\n      guile +small\n      say: Behind a rack of empty bottles, one stone rings hollow. The door behind it opens on a stair one shoulder wide, curling up inside the wall. You follow it to a loose panel that comes out on the landing of candles, halfway up, and nobody on the main stair any the wiser.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: You knock on stone until your knuckles are raw and every wall in the cellar sounds the same. The tower keeps its two strides to itself, for now.\n  next\n> right: Measure it again from outside\n  lore +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: You walk the tower's foot in the cold with Corvin's string, and get the same answer he did: two strides. Your fingers are numb and the blank is still blank, but now you believe in it.\n\n@event carto_book_lift\ntitle: The Book-Lift\nthread: carto_master_map\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(heist_stacks_known)\nart: book\ntags: club mystery danger\nscene: A dusty wooden box on a rope inside a narrow shaft, an old library bindery around it, a student climbing into the box with a candle.\ntext: Under the library, the bindery has an old book-lift: a wooden box on a rope in a shaft, from the days when the stacks went deeper than anyone now admits. The rope is new. Somebody still uses it.\n> left: Ride it down\n  challenge feat apprentice+:\n    win:\n      flag carto_book_lift\n      guile +tiny\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: The box lowers you in the dark past floor after floor of shelves, and stops where the shelves start breathing, so gently it might know you. A quicker way down than any stair. You ink the shaft onto the map before you've stopped shaking.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The rope slips a yard, then another, then catches. You climb out at the first floor down with skinned palms and a new respect for whoever oils it.\n  next\n> right: Find out who oils the rope\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Nobody in the bindery will say. Footnote, the library cat, sits by the shaft all afternoon, as if waiting for a lift that never comes.\n\n@event carto_tower_chimney\ntitle: Wide as a Coffin\nthread: carto_master_map\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(heist_tower_known)\nart: door\ntags: club mystery danger\nscene: A student wedged inside a narrow soot-black chimney, climbing with back and feet, a lantern hung from their belt lighting the brick above.\ntext: The old classrooms in the seventh tower each had a fireplace, and the fireplaces share one chimney, which starts in the cellar beside your wall stair. A Cartographer fifty years gone wrote in the margin beside it: \"Chimney: wide as a coffin. Climbable. Don't.\"\n> left: Climb it\n  challenge feat apprentice+:\n    win:\n      flag carto_tower_chimney\n      vigor +tiny\n      guile +tiny\n      say: Back against one side, feet against the other, soot to the elbows. You come out of a classroom grate a floor above the landing of candles, past the wall stair's slow part, and sit in a century of dust laughing quietly.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Halfway up the chimney narrows, and so do you, and for a long minute neither of you gives. You come down the way you went up, black from head to foot.\n  next\n> right: Send a lantern up on a cord\n  lore +tiny\n  say: The lantern goes up and up and comes back sooty, swinging. The margin was right about the coffin.\n\n@event carto_map_keeper\ntitle: The Keeping of the Map\nthread: carto_master_map\ntone: warm\nactor: corvin\nart: map\ntags: club\nscene: A circle of students with candles in a secret passage, one of them holding out a rolled map in silver ink to another.\ntext: The Cartographers gather round the Master Map with candles. {(flag(heist_stacks_known) and (flag(heist_tower_known) or flag(carto_book_lift))) or (flag(heist_tower_known) and flag(carto_tower_chimney)): Where the blanks were, your lines are, faintly silver and not quite settled.|The blanks are still mostly blank, but the club has watched you try. \"Close enough is a Cartographer's whole trade,\" says Corvin.} \"The club says you keep it with me. There's a test. There's always a test. Walk from here to the Thursday stair with the map in your head and no candle.\"\n> left: Walk it in the dark\n  challenge research using guile adept- +1 if flag(heist_stacks_known) +1 if flag(heist_tower_known) +1 if flag(carto_book_lift) +1 if flag(carto_tower_chimney):\n    win:\n      flag carto_map_done carto_map_keeper\n      guile +small\n      rel corvin +small\n      mood +small\n      say: You walk it blind, every turning where the map says it is, and at the foot of the Thursday stair the club is waiting with candles. Corvin puts the rolled map in your hands. \"Keepers,\" he says, and for once he doesn't laugh after.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: Three turnings in, the castle moves one. You walk into a wall, very gently, in the dark, and stand there until someone comes with a candle. \"It wants more of you,\" says Corvin kindly. \"Come back.\"\n> right: \"It's yours, Corvin. Keep it.\"\n  flag carto_map_done\n  rel corvin +medium\n  say: Corvin looks at you for a long moment, then rolls the map back up and tucks it into his coat, where it has lived since the summer. He looks relieved, and then embarrassed about looking relieved.\n\n# ---------------- The society's other nights (track: cartographers) ----------------\n\n@event carto_forged_map\ntitle: A Map for Sale\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: map\ntags: club money\nscene: A first-year in a corridor selling folded paper maps from a satchel to a small crowd of younger students.\ntext: A first-year is selling \"the secret map of the castle\" in the corridor for a crown a copy. It is a forgery, and a good one, and every passage on it leads, by a lovely roundabout way, straight to the prefects' office. Corvin swears it wasn't him.\n> left: Let it sell\n  guile +tiny\n  mood +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You get a copy for the club's wall. All week first-years turn up, breathless, at the prefects' door. The prefects are delighted. Nobody looks behind the third gargoyle at all.\n> right: Buy up the stock and burn it\n  gold -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You buy the whole satchel and burn it in the passage grate. The first-year is thrilled with the money. Somewhere, the forger is very annoyed.\n\n@event carto_quiet_prefect\ntitle: The Prefect Who Doesn't Look\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: lantern\ntags: club\nscene: A senior prefect with a lantern on a windy parapet at night, standing beside a gargoyle and not looking at it, talking to a student in the shadows.\ntext: A senior prefect walks the north parapet on her rounds and never once looks at the third gargoyle. This time she stops beside it, still not looking. \"The draught behind this one's got worse,\" she says to the dark. \"Tell whoever needs telling. And somebody's been eating the grapes in the dish. Those were mine.\"\n> left: Trade a shortcut for her rota\n  flag prefect_rota\n  guile +tiny\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  say: You give her the back way from the parapet to the prefects' common room, four minutes quicker. She gives you, without a word, the times of every round on her rota. Corvin, when he hears, says giving routes away is how clubs end.\n> right: Play dumb\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Draught?\" you say. \"What gargoyle?\" She almost smiles, and walks on, swinging the lantern. You keep the club's secrets, and she keeps hers.\n\n@event carto_member_out\ntitle: One Map Fewer\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nart: map\ntags: club\nscene: Two students kneeling by a small grate in a stone passage, a stack of hand-drawn maps between them, one holding a lit match.\ntext: One of the club's seniors has been caught twice this term, and wants out before a third. The club's rule is that leavers burn their maps. She has a year of them in a bundle, and she asks you to sit with her while she does it. \"Or hide them,\" she says. \"I can't watch them go.\"\n> left: Burn them with her\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You feed the grate one map at a time and she reads each one aloud before it goes. At the end she thanks you, and goes up to bed a member of nothing, and lighter.\n> right: Keep them for the club\n  guile +small\n  queue night_suspicion chance slim\n  say: You hide the bundle in the passage where only the club will find it, which means carrying a caught-twice member's maps through the castle after curfew. She doesn't ask where. She'll know, every time she passes the gargoyle, that she didn't really leave.\n\n@event carto_recruit\ntitle: Your Turn to Invite\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  pick: any where met(pick) and tagged(pick, student) and not npc_is(corvin)\nart: map\ntags: club social\nscene: A black card with a silver map drawn on it being slid under a dormitory door in a dark corridor.\ntext: Corvin hands you a black card, blank but for a silver line that wanders. \"Members bring members,\" he says. \"Your turn. Who?\" You think of {pick} before he's finished asking.\n> left: Slip it under {pick}'s door\n  rel pick +small\n  queue night_suspicion chance slim\n  say: You slide the card under {pick}'s door at midnight and run. Three nights later {pick} comes in under the gargoyle with a candle and a look you'll remember: someone finding out the castle is bigger than they thought.\n> right: \"Nobody I'd trust with it.\"\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Corvin takes the card back. \"Careful's a good thing in a Cartographer,\" he says, and doesn't sound as if he means it.\n\n# ---------------- The third gargoyle at midnight ----------------\n\n@event gargoyle_midnight_one_short\ntitle: One Short\npool: gargoyle_passage_routine\nslot: midnight\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: club danger\nscene: A huddle of students with candles in a dark stone passage, one of them counting heads, an empty gap in the circle.\ntext: Midnight under the gargoyle, and the headcount comes up one short. {~Someone went into the passages alone an hour ago, to settle a bet about a stair that only goes down on wet nights.|Someone took a dare about the kitchens' dumbwaiter, and the dare was \"alone\".|A new member, it turns out, sleepwalks, and nodded off on the bench an hour ago, and the bench is empty.} Nobody says anything for a moment.\n> left: Go after them\n  challenge feat using guile journeyman:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {~You find them two floors down, on a step that wasn't there yesterday and can't now be got off. You talk them down one stride at a time.|You find them stuck in the dumbwaiter between two floors, very glad of company. You wind them down by the rope, slowly, and walk them back.|You find them on the parapet, asleep on their feet and walking the edge. You turn them round by the shoulders, gently, and walk them home.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      queue night_suspicion chance unlikely\n      say: {~The passages turn you round twice, and a prefect's lantern turns you round a third time. The missing member gets back on their own at dawn, grinning.|You never find the dumbwaiter's shaft, and a prefect nearly finds you. They come back at dawn, floury and triumphant.|You search every passage but the right one. At dawn they wake in their own bed, with no idea anyone looked.}\n> right: Wait with the others\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You sit in the circle with the gap in it, and at dawn they climb back out of the wall, filthy and triumphant. The waiting was worse than going.|At dawn they come back through the wall, floury to the elbows, with a story nobody believes. The waiting was worse than going.|At dawn somebody finds them asleep in the library. The waiting was worse than going.}\n\n@event gargoyle_midnight_lost_map\ntitle: A Map on the Wrong Table\npool: gargoyle_passage_routine\nslot: midnight\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 8\nart: map\ntags: club danger\nscene: A dark common room with red sashes hung over chairs, a folded hand-drawn map lying on a table in a pool of moonlight.\ntext: {~A second-year Cartographer dropped their copy of the kitchens' passages, and it's turned up on a table in the prefects' common room.|One of the club's old route maps, the one to the boathouse, is lying on the prefects' study desk, where a cleaner left it.|A first-year member lent their map to a friend, and the friend lost it to a prefect's coat pocket, and the coat is hanging in the prefects' common room.} Nobody has opened it yet. It's still there at midnight.\n> left: Steal it back\n  challenge deceive journeyman:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {~In through the door, across the moonlit rug, map into your shirt, out. A prefect turns over in an armchair by the fire and doesn't wake.|You lift it off the desk between two snores. The club has it back by one o'clock.|You go through the coat's pockets in the dark, find the map and a toffee, and take only the map.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      queue night_suspicion now\n      say: {~You get the map, and a prefect gets a very good look at you holding it. You run. The map is safe; you aren't, quite.|A floorboard by the desk squeaks. You get out with the map and a prefect's voice behind you, asking your name.|The coat's owner comes back for it while your hand's in the pocket. You run, map and all, and they know your face.}\n> right: Redraw the routes instead\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {~The club spends the night changing every route to the kitchens: new chalk, new turnings, a passage given up for good.|The club gives up the old boathouse route for good, and spends the night finding a worse one.|The club spends the night redrawing everything the lost map showed. By dawn it's worth nothing to anyone.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_hollowstar_acts.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  THE HOLLOW STAR: the acts' openers, climaxes and confrontations (v2, docs/plots/hollowstar-v2.md).\n#  The wiring (act threads, deadlines, the Night) is in content/events/hollowstar/threads.grim; the newer cards are\n#  in content/events/hollowstar/. The beats are deadlines: each act's climax raises the clock to the next opener.\n#  Act I (omens)            opener hs_lake_breathes (beat 1)\n#  Act II (investigation)   opener hs_ilse_arrives (beat 4), with hs_silver_rings and hs_seraphine_towers; climax\n#                           hs_frost_ring (beat 5); hs_ilse_door is one of its steps\n#  Act III (escalation)     opener hs_dark_tower (beat 7); hs_two_shadows -> hs_choosing_sides; hs_maud_locks\n#  Act IV (convergence)     opener hs_last_winter (beat 10): who stands with you beneath the lake\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Act I: omens ----------------\n\n@event hs_lake_breathes\ntitle: The Lake Breathes\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 1\nslot: evening\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_omens\nscene: The Great Hall at supper, every head turned to the tall windows, the lake beyond them gone white with sudden frost.\ntext: At supper the lake outside the tall windows goes white in one breath, and every candle in the Great Hall leans toward it. Far under the water a bell tolls. Professor Thorne's spoon freezes into his soup. The headmistress rises. \"A cold current,\" she says pleasantly. \"Eat.\" Then the lake breathes out, and is water again. Nobody eats.\n> left: Run down to the shore\n  meet maud\n  find lead_lake_breathed\n  find lead_maud_friend\n  queue hs_dreams_begin within 1..3\n  health -tiny\n  say: The shore is soaked and freezing, and the reeds are white to the root. Old Maud is already there with a lantern, staring at the water. \"Last time it did that, I lost a friend,\" she says, not quite to you. Then she sends you to bed, sharply, and you go.\n> right: Follow Thorne out of the hall\n  find lead_founders_archive\n  queue hs_dreams_begin within 1..3\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Thorne leaves his frozen soup and walks straight to the library archive, fast for a man of his age. You hear the vault door slam. You lie awake wondering what he went down there to read.\n\n# ---------------- Act II: the Wardens stir ----------------\n\n@event hs_silver_rings\ntitle: Silver at Supper\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 4\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: ring\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_investigation\nscene: The Great Hall at supper, candlelight catching three identical silver rings on three very different hands at the high table.\ntext: At supper you notice three identical silver rings at the high table: on the headmistress, on Professor Thorne and on Old Maud's earthy hand. After curfew, from your window, you see all three walking the lakeshore with lanterns, stopping at the foot of each tower in turn.\n> left: Follow them down\n  challenge deceive vs thorne.guile:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: You shadow them from tower to tower. At each one they lay a palm on the stone and wait. At the last, Maud takes her hand away and blows on her fingers as if the wall were ice.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      rel thorne -small\n      say: A lantern swings toward you. \"Hm,\" says Professor Thorne. \"Bed.\" He doesn't report you, which somehow feels worse.\n> right: Draw the curtains\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You sleep well, mostly. In the morning the rings are still there at breakfast, and nobody at the high table looks as if they slept at all.\n\n@event hs_seraphine_towers\ntitle: Seven, Then Six\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 4\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nactor: seraphine\nart: tower\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_investigation\nscene: A seer on the observatory balcony at night, counting the academy's towers on her fingers, one finger held back.\ntext: Seraphine is on the observatory balcony, counting towers on her fingers. \"Seven,\" she says. \"Seven. Six, and then… no. Seven, for now.\" She looks at you as if you had arrived a little early. \"What month is it? I lose track around the… you know.\"\n> left: \"Which one goes out?\"\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  find lead_seventh_tower\n  say: She points, then frowns at her own hand. \"That one. Or the one next to it. Next year, I think. Not the seventh; nobody's lit the seventh for a hundred years.\" She doesn't remember saying it the next morning, but you do.\n> right: \"It's {month}. Evening.\"\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  mood +tiny\n  say: She lets out a long breath and sits down on the cold stone. \"Thank you. I was halfway into a winter.\" You stay until she is sure which one she's in.\n\n@event hs_ilse_arrives\ntitle: A Storm at the Gate\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 4\nslot: noon\nwhen: met(kael)\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nactor: kael\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_investigation\n# Act II's opener (v2): Ilse arrives, and the Dumonts clash in the open.\nscene: A snowstorm falling on one girl in white furs in the middle of a sunny courtyard, a pale young man facing her, students scattering, a cracked fountain between them.\ntext: At noon a snowstorm walks in through the academy gate, with a girl in white furs at the middle of it. Kael drops his books. \"Go home, Ilse.\" \"Make me.\" They shout at each other across the courtyard in Northern, and the fountain between them cracks in half. Half the school is watching. Then she turns on her heel and goes, down the road to the village.\n> left: Go after her\n  flag ilse_in_village\n  find lead_white_furs\n  queue ilse_village in 1\n  rel kael -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Kael catches your sleeve. \"Don't. She's here for the door, not for me.\" He lets go when you don't stop. By the time you reach the road the snow has stopped too, and there's only a line of frozen footprints toward the bakery.\n> right: Stay with Kael\n  flag ilse_in_village\n  find lead_white_furs\n  rel kael +small\n  say: You pick up his books. He stands looking at the broken fountain until the bell goes. \"My sister,\" he says at last. \"She's taken a room above the bakery. She isn't here to visit.\"\n\n@event hs_frost_ring\ntitle: Things Come Ashore\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 5\nslot: night\nwhen: since(hs_ilse_arrives) >= 2\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nthread: hs_investigation\n# Act II's climax (v2): two of the act's truths bring it early (hs_investigation_progress), the deadline otherwise; it\n# opens Act III. The fight is one way through; watching the headmistress and herding the first-years are the others.\nscene: The Mirror Lake on a warm night, a perfect white ring of frost on the water, figures of frost and whispers climbing onto the shore, a tall woman with a lantern facing them.\non_show:\n  advance hollowstar to 7\ntext: {season == \"winter\": A still, starless night|A warm night}, and a ring of frost as wide as the Great Hall has formed in the middle of the lake. Far below, the bell tolls, and tolls. Then things climb out of the ring onto the shore: shapes of frost and whispers, walking like people who have forgotten how. The headmistress stands alone at the waterline with a lantern, talking to the lake.\n> fight: Meet them on the shore\n  hint: Combat. Kael fights beside you, if he's your ally.\n  if flag(kael_ally):\n    challenge combat adept+ drift mid with kael:\n      win:\n        renown +small\n        alignment +tiny\n        find lake_bell\n        say: Kael's frost and your spells meet the first of them on the shingle, and it comes apart into snow and a sound like someone saying your name. The rest turn back to the water. Under the ice, as it closes, you hear the bell's note clearly for the first time.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: It walks through your spell as if it were weather and puts a cold hand on your chest, and you can't breathe for a long moment. Kael drags you back up the shingle. When you can see again, the ring has closed and the shapes are gone.\n  else:\n    challenge combat adept+ drift mid:\n      win:\n        renown +small\n        alignment +tiny\n        find lake_bell\n        say: Your spell meets the first of them on the shingle, and it comes apart into snow and a sound like someone saying your name. The rest turn back to the water. Under the ice, as it closes, you hear the bell's note clearly for the first time.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: It walks through your spell as if it were weather and puts a cold hand on your chest, and you can't breathe for a long moment. When you can see again, the ring has closed, the shapes are gone, and the headmistress is helping you up.\n> watch: Watch what she does\n  hint: Guile. She doesn't like an audience.\n  challenge deceive expert likely:\n    win:\n      find lake_bell\n      lore +small\n      say: She kneels and speaks to the water, slow and patient, the way you'd speak to a sleeping animal. The shapes stop on the shingle and listen. The ring shrinks, cracks, and is gone, and the bell under it rings one long note you'll know again anywhere.\n    lose:\n      rel isolde +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: \"Good evening,\" she says, without turning round, as a shape of frost walks past her into the lake. \"You are the third student this week. The other two asked questions.\" She tells you nothing, and remembers your name.\n> help: Get the first-years inside\n  hint: Nobody will see what happens at the water.\n  renown +small\n  rel isolde +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You run the length of the first-year tower banging on doors, and get forty children into the common room and the shutters closed. Something taps on the glass all night, very politely. In the morning there are wet footprints on the stairs, going up.\n\n# ---------------- Act III: escalation ----------------\n\n@event hs_dark_tower\ntitle: The Tower That Went Dark\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 7\nwhen: since(hs_frost_ring) >= 2\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nart: candle\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nthread: hs_escalation\n# Act III's opener (v2): the cult strikes, in the open.\nscene: An academy tower standing black against the night sky among its lit neighbours, a black star burned into a great wooden door, and below them a lake half-covered in ice.\ntext: Mid-lecture, every lamp in the Lecture Tower goes out at once, and none of them will light again. In the morning there's a black star burned into the door of the Great Hall, still smoking, and half the lake has frozen {season == \"winter\": black and glassy, harder than any winter ice|weeks before any frost}, in a sheet that ends in a clean edge, as if cut. Out on it, at dawn, someone in black furs is walking away.\n> left: Go after the figure on the ice\n  hint: Nerve. The ice is thinner than it looks.\n  challenge feat expert:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      health -small\n      say: You follow the footprints to the middle of the ice, where they simply stop. Under your boots the ice is clear as glass. Far down, something round and dark sits on the lakebed, and a glow runs along its rim. Then the glow turns, slowly, as if to look up.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The ice groans and a crack races past your feet. You scramble back to the shore soaked to the knee, and a prefect writes your name down very slowly. The figure in black furs never once looks back.\n> right: Carry lamps for the prefects\n  renown +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You spend the night carrying lamps up the Lecture Tower stairs. Every one goes out at the doorway. The first-years have started sleeping in their clothes. From her window across the courtyard, the headmistress watches you try.\n\n@event hs_maud_locks\ntitle: Maud Has Seen Ice Before\nwhen: seen(hs_dark_tower) > 0\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nactor: maud\nweight: 16\nart: cup\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_escalation\nscene: An old woman wrapped in a shawl on her cottage step, two mugs steaming beside her, a tall woman in dark robes walking stiffly away down the path, a half-frozen lake beyond.\ntext: At Maud's gate the headmistress is saying, quite pleasantly, \"Keep the young ones out of it, Maud.\" \"No,\" says Maud, \"and you know why.\" The headmistress walks away very straight. Maud pushes a mug of tea at you. \"{season == \"winter\": It doesn't freeze like that, dearie, not even in winter|The lake doesn't freeze this time of year, dearie}. Sixty years ago it did, and three of us went down. Sit. Or ask.\"\n> left: Sit with her\n  rel maud +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You drink your tea and watch the ice. She hums something old. When the mugs are empty she squeezes your hand. \"That was the right one,\" she says, and you'll only understand why much later.\n> right: Ask what's holding it shut\n  find seven_locks\n  find lead_seventh_tower\n  rel maud +small\n  mood -small\n  hs_escalation_progress\n  say: \"Seven towers, seven locks, one in each,\" she says. \"When a tower goes dark, so does its lock.\" She looks at the Lecture Tower. \"Locks can be mended. They need hands, and they need a key, and she knows it.\"\n\n@event hs_two_shadows\ntitle: Two Wands on the Ice\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nweight: 20\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_escalation\n# v2: a confrontation on the page (style.md, \"Conflict and climax\"); it brings the invitations (hs_choosing_sides).\nscene: Dusk on a half-frozen lake, two figures facing each other in the middle of the ice with wands raised, one holding a lantern, the dark tower behind them.\ntext: At dusk the headmistress and Kael walk out onto the frozen half of the lake from opposite shores and meet in the middle. Kael's voice carries all the way to the towers: \"You'll seal it with students standing in the door!\" Hers never rises, and carries anyway: \"Better than your mother's way.\" Both wands come up. For a long moment nobody moves. Then she lowers hers first, and they part in silence.\n> left: Ask Kael what they said\n  rel kael +small\n  mood -tiny\n  queue hs_choosing_sides in 1\n  say: \"She wants the door shut her way. I want it shut mine.\" He stirs his tea until it freezes. \"She asked about you. So did I. Don't make that face. You'll be asked.\"\n> right: Ask the headmistress\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +small\n  rel kael -tiny\n  queue hs_choosing_sides in 1\n  say: \"We disagree about method,\" she says, \"and agree about the door.\" She studies you as if you were a chess problem. \"Expect an invitation. Possibly two. Choose with your eyes open.\" Kael, across the hall, sees you leave her tower.\n\n@event hs_choosing_sides\ntitle: Two Invitations\nwhen: seen(hs_two_shadows) > 0\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nweight: 20\nart: letter\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_escalation\nscene: A crowded refectory gone quiet, a student at a table between a tall woman in dark robes and a pale young man, both waiting for an answer.\ntext: Two invitations, both in front of the whole refectory. The headmistress, passing your table: \"My study, after supper. Come alone. There will be chess.\" And Kael, not ten seconds later, loud enough for her to hear: \"She wants you in her circle. I know the door from the other side. Come with us.\" Every head in the hall turns to you.\n> wardens: Join the headmistress's circle\n  hint: The Wardens: old rules, old wards.\n  flag sided_wardens\n  rel isolde +large\n  rel kael -small\n  lore +small\n  hs_escalation_progress\n  say: She beats you at chess in eleven moves, then tells you about the seal while she resets the board. \"Rings are for people who have stood on the ice,\" she says. \"Until then, you have my door.\"\n> dumonts: Stand with the Dumonts\n  hint: Kael's way, against his own family.\n  flag sided_dumonts\n  rel kael +large\n  rel isolde -small\n  if met(ilse):\n    rel ilse +small\n  arcana +small\n  hs_escalation_progress\n  say: \"Not my family,\" Kael says, to the whole hall. \"Us.\" He teaches you to hold a ward in a wind that wants your fingers. His hands are cold. They are steadier than yours.\n> self: Go my own way\n  hint: Nobody's circle. Nobody's help.\n  flag sided_self\n  guile +small\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  hs_escalation_progress\n  say: You thank them both, out loud, and keep your own notes in your own cipher. It's lonelier than you expected. Both of them, you notice, keep an eye on you anyway.\n> none: Stay out of it\n  hint: It isn't your door.\n  mood +small\n  rel kael -tiny\n  say: You turn both invitations down. Kael nods as if he expected it. The headmistress's note simply says, the next morning, \"Understood. Mind the ice.\"\n\n# ---------------- Act IV: convergence ----------------\n\n@event hs_last_winter\ntitle: The Last Winter Before\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 10\nwhen: final_year and (clues(hollowstar) >= 2 or flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts) or flag(sided_self) or flag(warden_trial))\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_convergence\n# Act IV's opener (v2): each ally says it out loud. Whoever you train with will come down with you on the Night, even\n# if you go early (hs_night_company, night_* in threads.grim).\nscene: Lantern light in a frost-rimed window, the frozen edge of the lake beyond it, a student's desk covered in maps of seven towers.\ntext: The Lecture Tower has been dark for a year, and the lake freezes overnight in circles now. In spring the seal will be at its weakest. In the same week, one after another, they come and say it plainly: they'll stand with you beneath the lake, if you'll have them. You have one winter, and evenings enough for one of them.\n> kael: Train with Kael on the ice\n  hint: He'll come down with you, and fight beside you.\n  flag winter_kael\n  rel kael +large\n  health -small\n  say: He drills you on the ice until you can hold a ward with numb hands. \"Again,\" he says, and then, quietly, \"Good.\" You will remember that he said it.\n> maud: Mend wards with Maud\n  hint: She'll come down with you, and knows the founders' wall.\n  flag winter_maud\n  rel maud +large\n  mood -small\n  say: You walk the towers with her, tracing old wards back into the stone. \"Your hands are steadier than mine were,\" she says, and doesn't say whose hands she means.\n> isolde: Study with the headmistress\n  hint: She'll come down with you; you'll know the founders' words.\n  flag winter_isolde\n  rel isolde +large\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You lose at chess every evening and learn the founders' words between moves. Once you nearly win. Her short, delighted laugh is the warmest sound you hear all winter.\n> ilse: Spar with Ilse at the shore\n  hint: She'll come down with you, and fight beside you.\n  when: met(ilse) and not flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\n  flag winter_ilse\n  rel ilse +large\n  health -small\n  say: She knocks you into the snow eleven times. The twelfth time you don't fall, and she looks at you as if you had done a trick she has never learned the counter to.\n> alone: Rest while you still can\n  hint: A quiet winter. No new training, but rested.\n  rest\n  say: You sleep, you eat, you read novels with no doors in them. It's the kindest winter you've had in years, and you can feel the lake getting colder anyway.\n\n@event hs_ilse_door\ntitle: What Ilse Was Taught\nwhen: met(ilse) and not clue(ilse_truth) and not flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\nactor: ilse\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nart: door\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nweight: 14\nthread: hs_investigation\nscene: A young woman in white furs drawing a round door in the snow with the tip of a wand, a frozen lake behind her.\ntext: Ilse draws a circle in the snow with her wand, then a smaller one inside it. \"This is the door,\" she says. \"I have drawn it every day since I was six. I have never seen it.\" She doesn't look up. \"Ask me what they taught me to do. Or don't. I don't care which.\"\n> left: \"What did they teach you?\"\n  flag ilse_confided\n  find ilse_truth\n  rel ilse +small\n  hs_investigation_progress\n  mood -small\n  say: She tells you, flatly, like a lesson recited: how to stand, what to say, where to put her hands. \"They raised me to open it,\" she says. \"Nobody asked me if I wanted to.\" Then, sharply: \"Now you know. Don't look at me like that.\"\n> right: \"You don't have to tell me.\"\n  rel ilse +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She rubs the circle out with her boot. \"Kindness,\" she says, as if naming a trick. \"I haven't learned the counter to that one yet.\" She doesn't leave, though.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_hollowstar.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  MAIN CHAIN: The Hollow Star (v2, docs/plots/hollowstar-v2.md)\n#\n#  Something sleeps beneath the lake, sealed by Lumenhold's founders. Kael Dumont's family serve it. The wiring is in\n#  content/events/hollowstar/threads.grim: four acts, each a story thread whose climax raises the arc's clock to the\n#  next act's opener (the arc's beats are deadlines), and the Night, a scene in one sitting. The acts' openers and\n#  confrontations are in chain_hollowstar_acts.grim, the newer cards in content/events/hollowstar/.\n#  Here: Act I's finds (the dream, the ledger, the bell) and its climax (hs_grey_stranger); Kael's secret and the man\n#  in grey's offers (Act II); the seventh tower and its key (Act III); the headmistress's summons (Act IV); and the\n#  Night's tunnel, wall and door with their aftermaths.\n#  Alignment (Lumen + / Hollow -) is the moral axis: the Star's gifts pull toward the Hollow; refusing them, toward the\n#  Lumen. Outcomes fill the yearbook's mystery line: sealed / bargained / freed / failed / fled / held the line /\n#  claimed (game over) / bystander.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event hs_dreams_begin\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Black Star Dream\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 5 and not has(hollow_dreams)\nweight: 14\nart: moon\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_omens\nscene: A dreamscape: a frozen lake seen from beneath, a round door glowing at the bottom, and above it a star that is a hole in the sky.\ntext: You dream of the lake from underneath. There is a round door at the bottom, and above the water a star that is not a light but an absence. A voice says your name like it is tasting it. You wake with frost on your pillow.\n> left: Write the dream down\n  lore +small\n  find lead_black_star_dream\n  gain hollow_dreams\n  hs_omens_progress\n  say: Your notes fill four pages. In the morning you don't remember writing the last line: \"It is not asleep. It is waiting.\"\n> right: Ask Seraphine what it means\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine +small\n  find lead_black_star_dream\n  gain hollow_dreams\n  hs_omens_progress\n  say: Seraphine listens, then hands you a sprig of lavender. \"It won't stop the dreams,\" she says. \"But it'll make them smell nicer.\"\n\n@event hs_grey_stranger\ntitle: A Man in Grey\narc: hollowstar\nbeat: 2\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(kael)\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nthread: hs_omens\n# Act I's climax (v2): two of the act's finds bring it early (hs_omens_progress), the deadline otherwise; it opens Act II.\nscene: A frozen fountain in a moonlit courtyard; a tall man in a grey coat facing a pale young man who is shouting, frost spreading from both.\non_show:\n  advance hollowstar to 4\ntext: The courtyard fountain freezes mid-splash with a crack like a whip. Kael, who never raises his voice, is shouting at a tall man in a grey coat: \"Tell her no. I did.\" The man smiles. \"The door under the lake will be opened, by you or by your sister. Your mother doesn't mind which.\" Then he turns his head and says your name, which nobody told him.\n> left: Back into the shadows\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  find lead_man_in_grey\n  say: You back away. He looks at the exact spot where you're hiding, smiles, and walks out through the gate with frost blooming in his footprints. You don't sleep. Neither, you suspect, does Kael.\n> right: Step out beside Kael\n  hint: He'll remember your face.\n  rel kael +large\n  health -tiny\n  find lead_man_in_grey\n  flag confronted_grey\n  say: \"He said no.\" It comes out louder than you meant. The man in grey studies you, inclines his head and leaves; the cold he leaves behind bites through your sleeves. Kael's hands are shaking. \"You shouldn't have done that,\" he says. \"...Thank you.\"\n\n@event hs_founders_records\ntitle: The Founders' Ledger\nplace: library\ntime: morning afternoon\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clue(lead_founders_archive) and not clue(founders_ledger)\nweight: 14\nart: book\ntags: mystery hollowstar study\nthread: hs_omens\nscene: A dusty archive vault, a giant ledger open on a lectern, one page carefully razored out.\ntext: In the archive vault you find the Founders' Ledger, three hundred years old. The entry for the academy's founding year has been cut out with a razor. But the page beneath still holds the impression of the pen.\n> left: Leave it — this feels dangerous\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You close the ledger. The dust settles. Some doors are better left alone. Probably.\n> right: Rub charcoal over the page\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      flag knows_founders_seal\n      find knows_founders_seal\n      find founders_ledger\n      lore +small\n      hs_omens_progress\n      say: Ghostly letters emerge: \"Seven towers, seven locks. The Star sleeps beneath the water. The key is a star that holds no light.\" The founders built the academy as a seal.\n    lose:\n      lore +tiny\n      say: The impression is too faint. You make out only three words: \"...beneath the water...\"\n\n@event hs_restricted_founders\ntitle: The Chained Drafts\nplace: restricted_section\ntime: midnight\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clue(lead_founders_archive) and not clue(founders_ledger)\nweight: 16\nart: lock\ntags: mystery hollowstar study\nthread: hs_omens\nscene: A dark aisle of the Restricted Section at midnight, a bundle of yellowed drafts chained to a shelf, the chain faintly glowing.\ntext: The archive's ledger has its founding page cut out. The Restricted Section keeps the founders' own drafts, chained to a shelf at the very back, and the chain is warm to the touch. Somewhere behind you, a book turns its own page.\n> left: Work the chain loose\n  challenge deceive journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag knows_founders_seal\n      find founders_ledger\n      find knows_founders_seal\n      health -tiny\n      hs_omens_progress\n      say: The links give one by one, and burn your fingers doing it. The drafts say it plainly, in three hands: \"Seven towers, seven locks. The Star sleeps beneath the water. The key is a star that holds no light.\" Lumenhold is a seal.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      mood -small\n      say: The chain shrieks like a ward. You're out of the window before the prefect arrives, but not before he sees your face. Detention, and a long look from the librarian.\n> right: Copy the shelf-list instead\n  lore +small\n  mana -tiny\n  find lead_seventh_tower\n  say: The drafts' shelf-list is less guarded and nearly as strange. One entry reads only \"Key: the seventh tower, top room. Do not lend.\" You copy it out by the light of a reading charm you have to keep alive for an hour, and it leaves you drained.\n\n@event hs_boathouse_bell\ntitle: The Hatch in the Boathouse\nplace: boathouse\ntime: evening night midnight\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clue(lead_lake_breathed) and not clue(lake_bell)\nweight: 16\nart: bell\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_omens\nscene: The inside of an old wooden boathouse at night, a square hatch open in the floorboards, stone steps leading down into dark water.\ntext: Under a coil of rotten rope in the boathouse there's a hatch, and under the hatch, stone steps going down into the lake. The air coming up smells of cold bronze. From somewhere below, very faint, a bell is tolling under the water.\n> left: Go down the steps\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      flag found_lake_tunnel\n      find lake_bell\n      health -tiny\n      hs_omens_progress\n      say: The steps become a tunnel under the lakebed, dry and ice-cold, running toward the sound. You follow it until you can see the bell's green rim in the dark ahead. Then you turn back, frozen to the bone, and remember every turning.\n    lose:\n      find lake_bell\n      health -small\n      hs_omens_progress\n      say: The third step is ice. You go into the water to the chest and come up gasping, but you've seen it: a tunnel under the lake, running toward the bell. You walk home soaked.\n> right: Listen at the waterline\n  find lake_bell\n  mood -small\n  hs_omens_progress\n  say: You lie on the boards with your ear to the gap. The bell tolls seven times, slow, and between the strokes you hear a draught moving under the lake, the way air moves in a corridor. Something down there is a door. You don't sleep well.\n\n@event hs_seraphine_shadow\ntitle: One Star Too Many\nplace: observatory\nslot: evening\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: met(seraphine) and not clue(second_shadow) and (clue(lead_black_star_dream) or seen(hs_shadow_late) > 0)\nweight: 16\nart: star\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_escalation\nscene: An observatory at night, the great telescope tilted down toward the window and the lake below, a seer pointing at the reflection of the stars in the water.\ntext: Seraphine has turned the great telescope down, not at the sky but at the lake, where the stars are reflected. \"Look,\" she says. \"In the water. There's one star too many, and it's dark.\" She counts under her breath. \"It wasn't there in my dream. It was in yours.\"\n> left: Chart it with her\n  meet seraphine\n  find second_shadow\n  lore +small\n  hs_escalation_progress\n  health -tiny\n  say: You chart it till dawn on the cold floor: a small dark shape in the reflection that has no star above it, sitting exactly over the middle of the lake. Your knees never quite forgive you.\n> right: Tell her about your dream\n  meet seraphine\n  find second_shadow\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  hs_escalation_progress\n  mood -small\n  say: She finishes your sentences, three of them, before you've started them. Then she shows you the dark star in the water, right over the middle of the lake. You both stop talking for a long time.\n\n@event hs_kael_truth\ntitle: What Kael Is Running From\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: (clue(lead_man_in_grey) or clues(hollowstar) >= 1) and rel(kael) >= 15 and not flag(kael_told)\nactor: kael\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar social\nthread: hs_investigation\n# v2: a confrontation on the page; you corner him, and it comes out shouted.\nscene: Two students on an observatory roof at night, one shouting with frost racing out from his boots across the leads, the other standing their ground.\ntext: It comes to a head on the observatory roof. You've had a year of his silences and you tell him so, loudly. He shouts back, for the first time ever: \"My family serve the thing under this lake! They sent me here to open its door!\" Frost races out from his boots across the roof. Then, quieter: \"I came to stop them. Some nights I'm not sure which I'll do.\"\n> left: \"Then we stop them together.\"\n  flag kael_told kael_ally\n  rel kael +large\n  spark kael +tiny\n  alignment +small\n  find kael_family\n  hs_investigation_progress\n  if bond(kael) == \"none\":\n    bond kael friend\n  say: He looks at you for a long moment, and the frost on the roof melts a little. \"Together,\" he repeats, as if trying out a word from another language.\n> right: Tell the headmistress\n  flag kael_told reported_kael\n  rel kael -huge\n  rel isolde +medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  find kael_family\n  hs_investigation_progress\n  say: The headmistress thanks you gravely. The next day Kael is moved to a room in the headmistress's tower. He doesn't look at you anymore.\n\n@event hs_star_mark\ntitle: The Cold Coin Burns\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: (flag(star_coin) or flag(bell_fragment)) and not has(star_mark) and not flag(threw_star)\nweight: 12\nart: star\ntags: mystery hollowstar magic\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: A student's open palm, a pale star-shaped mark glowing on it, a blackened coin smoking on the floor beside them.\ntext: The black-starred relic you've been carrying burns suddenly, so cold it feels hot. When you drop it, there's a pale star-shaped mark on your palm. It hums when you're near the lake.\n> left: Throw the relic into the lake\n  flag threw_star\n  unflag star_coin bell_fragment\n  alignment +medium\n  mood +tiny\n  say: It sinks without a ripple. The mark on your hand fades — almost entirely. Something under the water sighs.\n> right: Keep it. Feel the power.\n  gain star_mark\n  arcana +medium\n  alignment -medium\n  say: The mark pulses with every heartbeat. Spells come easier now. Much easier. That should probably worry you more than it does.\n\n@event hs_grey_offer\ntitle: An Offer in Grey\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 18 and (clues(hollowstar) >= 2 or alignment <= -30) and not flag(grey_offer_seen)\nweight: 12\nart: mask\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nthread: hs_investigation\nscene: A grey-coated man sitting uninvited at a library table, a black velvet box open in front of him, every candle around him burning blue.\ntext: Every candle in the room goes blue at once. The man in grey is sitting at your table, uninvited, a black velvet box open in front of him. \"You've been asking about the Star,\" he says. \"It has started asking about you. It could make you extraordinary. All it wants is a door opened.\" He turns the box toward you. Inside it you see yourself, burning.\n> left: \"Get out.\"\n  hint: He leaves. He remembers.\n  alignment +small\n  flag grey_offer_seen\n  mood +tiny\n  gain grey_watching\n  queue hs_grey_return within 2..4\n  hs_investigation_progress\n  say: He inclines his head and leaves. The candles go back to yellow. You realise your hands are trembling, and that he looked at you on the way out the way a man looks at a lock.\n> right: \"...What would it give me?\"\n  hint: Everything, a little. The nights turn cold.\n  flag grey_offer_seen hollow_tempted\n  arcana +small\n  lore +small\n  alchemy +small\n  charm +small\n  vigor +small\n  guile +small\n  alignment -medium\n  gain star_taste\n  queue hs_grey_return within 2..4\n  hs_investigation_progress\n  say: He shows you, just for a moment. Power, endless and cold, in everything you can do at once. When he's gone you can still taste it, and that night you dream of water closing over your head.\n\n@event hs_grey_return\ntitle: A Small Service\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: mask\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nthread: hs_investigation\n# hs_grey_offer's follow-up (feedback 2026-10-16): the man in grey comes back with a first, small ask.\nscene: A grey-coated man on a frozen garden bench, a sealed letter held out between two gloved fingers, the fountain beside him iced over.\ntext: {flag(hollow_tempted): The man in grey is on a bench in the winter garden, and the fountain beside him has frozen mid-splash. \"You liked what you tasted,\" he says. \"Here is how it continues.\" He holds out a sealed letter. \"Leave this under the third plank of the boathouse jetty. Nothing more.\"|The man in grey is on a bench in the winter garden, and the fountain beside him has frozen mid-splash. \"You told me to get out,\" he says, pleasantly. \"I did. Now a small thing, and I'll stay out.\" He holds out a sealed letter. \"The third plank of the boathouse jetty. Nothing more.\"}\n> left: Take the letter to the jetty\n  hint: Hollow. He pays in what he promised.\n  alignment -small\n  flag hollow_tempted grey_errand\n  gold +medium\n  gain star_taste\n  say: You do it at midnight, with the lake breathing under the planks. In the morning there's a purse on your pillow and the cold dreams are back, sweeter than before.\n  # Threads 1: the main plot teaches Eclipse (the forest's Outcast no longer does).\n  if spell_level(eclipse) < 1:\n    learn_spell eclipse\n    say: In the purse, under the coins, a slip of black paper with a few lines on it. You read them once, and a bruise on your wrist stops hurting and starts to hum. Pain taken, power held: Eclipse.\n> right: Drop it in the fountain unread\n  hint: Lumen. He won't thank you.\n  alignment +small\n  mood -small\n  lose star_taste\n  gain grey_watching\n  say: You break the ice and push the letter under. The ink runs blue. He watches it sink, then you, and then he isn't there, and the fountain starts to splash again as if nothing had happened.\n\n@event hs_grey_invitation\ntime: evening night\ntitle: A Seat at the Northern Table\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment <= -60 and turn >= 12 and not flag(hollow_courted)\nweight: 16\nart: letter\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: A black-edged letter on a dormitory pillow, its wax seal pressed with a star that has no light in it.\ntext: There is a letter on your pillow, sealed with a black star. The handwriting is beautiful. \"The families have watched you with great interest,\" it says. \"There is a seat for you at our table, when the door is opened. Come to the boathouse at midnight, or burn this. Either is an answer.\"\n> left: Go to the boathouse\n  flag hollow_courted hollow_tempted\n  alignment -small\n  guile +small\n  health -small\n  say: The man in grey is waiting with two cups of something hot. He talks to you as an equal for an hour. You come back with frost in your hair and the feeling of having been measured, and found to fit.\n  if spell_level(eclipse) < 1:\n    learn_spell eclipse\n    say: Before you go he takes your cold hand in his and shows you how to keep what hurts: pain taken, power held. Eclipse. Your fingers ache all the way back, and hum.\n> right: Burn it\n  alignment +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: The paper burns blue and slow, and smells of the lake. It takes a long time to go out. Climbing back from where you've been is slower still, but this is a step.\n\n@event hs_grey_tests\ntitle: The Good Argument\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: alignment >= 30 and turn >= 14 and not flag(grey_tested)\nweight: 12\nart: mask\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: A grey-coated man standing beside a student at the infirmary window, both looking down at a first-year with a bandaged arm.\ntext: The man in grey falls into step beside you outside the infirmary. \"You help people,\" he says pleasantly. \"I admire it. But think how many more you could help with real power. The Star doesn't care what it's used for. Only that someone uses it.\"\n> left: \"No.\"\n  flag grey_tested\n  alignment +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"No,\" you say. He smiles as if you'd said something charming, and leaves. The first-year in the window waves at you. You wave back, and your hand is shaking.\n> right: \"...How many more?\"\n  flag grey_tested hollow_tempted\n  alignment -medium\n  lore +small\n  say: He tells you. The numbers are very large and very cold. That night, you dream of the lake from underneath, and you are not frightened in the dream.\n\n@event hs_wardens_notice\ntitle: The Headmistress Takes Note\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nactor: isolde\nwhen: alignment >= 60 and turn >= 12 and not flag(wardens_noticed)\nweight: 16\nart: crown\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: A tall woman in dark robes pausing on a stair to look down at one student among many, a silver ring glinting on her hand.\ntext: On the grand stair the headmistress stops beside you. \"Several people have told me about you,\" she says, \"and not one of them was complaining. That is rare.\" Her silver ring catches the light. \"I keep a list of students I would trust with a door. Don't let it go to your head.\"\n> left: \"I'd be honoured.\"\n  meet isolde\n  flag wardens_noticed\n  rel isolde +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Good,\" she says. \"Honour is a heavy thing to carry. Practise.\" She walks on. You stand on the stair for some time.\n> right: \"What kind of door?\"\n  meet isolde\n  flag wardens_noticed\n  rel isolde +small\n  lore +small\n  say: Her short, delighted laugh. \"The kind that ought to stay shut,\" she says. \"Keep asking questions like that. Just not on the stairs.\"\n\n@event hs_seventh_tower\ntitle: The Seventh Tower\nplace: seventh_tower\nslot: evening\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clue(lead_seventh_tower) and not flag(star_key) and not flag(gave_star_key) and not flag(key_taken)\nweight: 16\nart: tower\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_escalation\nscene: The top of a narrow, abandoned tower, cobwebbed and moonlit, with a wall bearing a star-shaped recess.\ntext: The seventh tower has been locked for a century, and tonight you mean to see what it keeps. You go up two steps at a time to the very top: a single room of dust and moonlight, and a star-shaped hollow in the wall, holding something that does not reflect any light. The wards are woven round the hollow itself. They hum as you come near, and they will shriek the moment it's touched.\n> left: Take it quietly\n  challenge deceive journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag star_key\n      find star_key\n      mood -small\n      hs_escalation_progress\n      queue hs_key_breakfast in 1\n      say: A key shaped like a star, black as a hole in the world. As you lift it, every candle in the academy goes out for one breath. You walk back in the dark with it cold against your ribs, and you don't sleep.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      mood -small\n      say: A ward shrieks. A prefect finds you on the stairs. You get detention and a very long look from the headmistress.\n> right: Take it boldly\n  challenge spell journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag star_key\n      find star_key\n      mana -small\n      hs_escalation_progress\n      queue hs_key_breakfast in 1\n      say: You tear through the ward with brute force, and it drains you to the bone. The key comes free. Every candle in the academy goes out for one breath, and somewhere below, a door hears it.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      say: The ward throws you down two flights of stairs.\n\n@event hs_key_breakfast\ntitle: Something of Mine\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus\nactor: isolde\nart: key\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: The high table at breakfast, the headmistress setting down her cup and looking along the hall at one student, the candles behind her newly lit.\ntext: At breakfast the headmistress sets down her cup and looks straight at you along the whole length of the hall. Afterwards she is waiting at the door. \"Every candle in my academy went out,\" she says pleasantly, \"and you have something of mine.\"\n> left: Give her the key\n  meet isolde\n  unflag star_key\n  flag gave_star_key wardens_noticed\n  rel isolde +medium\n  alignment +small\n  say: She takes it in a gloved hand without looking at it. \"Thank you. It will be where it is needed, on the night. So, I hope, will you.\" You feel lighter, and a little robbed.\n> right: Keep it\n  meet isolde\n  flag kept_star_key\n  rel isolde -medium\n  say: A long pause. \"Then keep it close,\" she says, \"and never let it near the water before its time. People will come asking for it. Not all of them will ask.\" From then on, the man in grey always seems to know where you are.\n\n@event hs_isolde_summons\ntitle: The Headmistress Knows\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and month <= 8 and not flag(hs_early) and (clues(hollowstar) >= 3 or flag(kael_ally) or flag(wardens_noticed) or flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts) or flag(sided_self))\npriority: 45\nactor: isolde\nart: crown\ntags: mystery hollowstar story\nthread: hs_convergence\n# v2: no longer pinned to months 3-7; it waits on Act IV. If the man in grey took the key, the Wardens fetch it back\n# here (one of the ways back that isn't a fight, decision 2026-10-26).\nscene: A circular study at the top of a tower, walls lined with sleeping portraits, the headmistress standing by a window over the lake, Thorne and Maud beside her, a black-sealed letter on the desk.\ntext: The headmistress summons you to her tower. Thorne is there, and Maud, and on the desk between them is a letter with a black-star seal, opened. \"The Dumonts' mother is coming south for the Blossom moon,\" Isolde says. \"She means to open the door herself. I need people I trust beneath the lake. Or you may walk away, and no one in this room will blame you.\"\n> left: Walk away\n  meet isolde\n  flag hollow_out\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Wise,\" she says, and you can't tell if she means it. Maud looks at her tea. \"Then stay above,\" says the headmistress, \"and keep the first-years indoors.\"\n> right: \"I'll be there.\"\n  alignment +small\n  meet isolde\n  flag warden_trial\n  find star_key\n  rel isolde +large\n  arcana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: She presses a silver ward-ring into your hand. \"{flag(star_key): The founders' key is with you. Keep it close.|{flag(gave_star_key) or flag(key_taken): The founders' key is in my desk; the Wardens saw to that.|The founders' key is kept at the top of the seventh tower.}} On the night, it comes down with us. Learn quickly. You have until Blossomtide.\"\n  if not flag(star_key):\n    unflag key_taken\n    flag gave_star_key\n\n@event hs_the_night\ntitle: The Night the Lake Froze\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and clock(hollowstar) >= 12 and hs_called()\npriority: 70\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nthread: hs_night\n# The Night's entry (v2) at the deadline (the clock reaches 12 at y4m9); hs_go_down_early starts the Night at its\n# second card instead, having frozen the lake itself.\nscene: The whole lake frozen white in the middle of spring, cracks glowing from beneath, a ring of lanterns out on the ice, students crowding the towers' windows.\ntext: In the middle of spring, the lake freezes in a single breath, from shore to shore. Cracks glow beneath the ice. Every candle in the academy leans toward the water. Out on the ice, lanterns are moving in a ring: someone is already there. Under it all the bell is tolling, {clue(lake_bell): the note you followed once|a note you've never heard and somehow know}.\n> left: Go down beneath the lake\n  find lake_bell\n  queue hs_night_company now\n  say: You run for the boathouse. Behind you, someone is shouting your name. You don't stop.\n> right: Stay above and protect the students\n  close hollowstar\n  alignment +small\n  health -medium\n  renown +large\n  rel isolde +small\n  flag hollow_held_line\n  say: You hold the doors of the first-year tower all night against things made of frost and whispers. By dawn the lake thaws. Something below is still asleep, for now, and forty children are alive who might not have been.\n\n@event hs_tunnel\ntitle: The Man in the Tunnel\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\npriority: 95\nart: mask\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nthread: hs_night\n# v2: whoever came down with you rides along (`with npc`: Kael's or Ilse's Hoarfrost in the fight). The talk pays off\n# ilse_truth and kael_family; slipping past, lake_bell. Then Ilse (if she was taken), then your shadow.\nscene: A slick stone tunnel under the ice, lit blue from above, a tall man in a grey coat standing in the middle of it with his hands folded.\ntext: The tunnel runs down under the ice, slick and glowing blue. Halfway down, the man in grey is waiting, alone, as if he has all night. {flag(night_kael): \"Kael. Your mother is disappointed.\" \"She always is,\" says Kael, and the walls frost over.|{flag(night_ilse): \"Ilse. You were meant to open it.\" \"I know what I was meant for,\" she says.|{flag(night_isolde): \"Headmistress. You're late by sixty years.\" \"Then I'll be thorough,\" she says.|\"Go back up,\" he says pleasantly. \"This isn't your door.\"}}}\n> fight: Break through him\n  hint: Combat. Whoever came down with you fights beside you.\n  if flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north):\n    queue hs_night_ilse now with npc\n  else:\n    queue hs_night_shadow now with npc\n  challenge combat master- unlikely with npc:\n    win:\n      flag hs_tunnel_clear\n      say: You hit him {flag(night_kael): with Kael's frost at the same moment|square}, and he slides back along the ice, {flag(grey_beaten): and this time he isn't surprised; he's angry|surprised for the first time in four years}. He bows, and is simply not there any more. The road down is clear.\n    lose:\n      night_hurt += 1\n      say: He takes your last spell on his own gloved hand and closes his fist on it, then steps back to let you pass, smiling. \"Go on, then. She's waiting.\" You go down bruised, and late.\n> talk: Talk your way past\n  hint: Charm. What the Dumonts were raised for is your best argument.\n  if flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north):\n    queue hs_night_ilse now with npc\n  else:\n    queue hs_night_shadow now with npc\n  challenge negotiate master- unlikely +1 if clue(ilse_truth) +1 if flag(ilse_saved) +1 if flag(mother_answered):\n    win:\n      flag hs_tunnel_clear\n      find ilse_truth\n      find kael_family\n      say: You tell him {flag(ilse_taken): that Kael has chosen, and Ilse will|that the Dumont children have chosen, both of them}, and that you know exactly what they were raised for and what it cost them. For a moment he looks old. Then he steps aside. It costs you something, seeing that look.\n    lose:\n      night_hurt += 1\n      find ilse_truth\n      find kael_family\n      say: He listens courteously, then tells you, kindly, everything the Dumonts did to their children and why. It takes a long time. He lets you pass only once you've heard every word. You arrive shaken, and late.\n> slip: Find another way down\n  hint: Guile. Easier if you know the old tunnels, or the bell's note.\n  if flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north):\n    queue hs_night_ilse now with npc\n  else:\n    queue hs_night_shadow now with npc\n  challenge deceive master- unlikely +1 if flag(found_lake_tunnel) +1 if clue(lake_bell):\n    win:\n      flag hs_tunnel_clear\n      say: You follow the bell's note into a side passage he doesn't know, the one its rope once ran through. You come out below him, breathless, and he never sees you go.\n    lose:\n      night_hurt += 1\n      say: The side passage is half flooded. You come out soaked and freezing below him, and late, and he calls a polite good evening after you down the tunnel.\n\n@event hs_threshold\ntitle: The Founders' Words\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\npriority: 95\nart: book\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nthread: hs_night\n# v2: every clue about the seal opens a lock here (seven_locks, the ledger, the founders' seal, Edmund's wand), and the\n# wall pays them off for anyone who never found them.\nscene: A wall of black stone at the end of a tunnel, carved with lines of script that drift when not looked at, seven round locks set in it, a faint bell-tone hanging in the air.\ntext: The tunnel ends at a wall of black stone, carved with the founders' words in a script that drifts when you look away, and set with seven round locks, one for each tower. {chosen(hs_maud_locks, right): You know them. Maud told you.|{clue(seven_locks): You know them.|You count them twice.}} Behind it the bell is tolling. {flag(night_maud): Maud lays a hand on the stone and says, \"Hello again.\"|}\n> read: Read the founders' words\n  hint: Lore. Everything you learned about the seal makes it easier.\n  queue hs_beneath now with npc\n  challenge research master- unlikely +1 if clue(knows_founders_seal) +1 if clue(seven_locks) +1 if clue(founders_ledger) +1 if flag(winter_isolde) +1 if flag(night_isolde):\n    win:\n      say: {clue(knows_founders_seal): You've read these words before, in pieces.|You've never read them, but they read themselves to you, line by line.} Said whole, they turn the seven locks one after another, and the wall opens like a door. \"Seven towers, seven locks,\" you hear yourself say. \"The key is a star that holds no light.\"\n      flag hs_threshold_read\n      find seven_locks\n      find knows_founders_seal\n    lose:\n      night_hurt += 1\n      find seven_locks\n      find knows_founders_seal\n      say: You stumble on the last line, and only six of the locks turn. The wall opens a crack. You squeeze through it, and it takes skin off your shoulder on the way, and the words follow you down the dark like a rhyme.\n> force: Force the locks\n  hint: Arcana, and it hurts. Maud's wards and Edmund's wand help.\n  queue hs_beneath now with npc\n  challenge spell master- unlikely +1 if flag(winter_maud) +1 if has_item(edmunds_silver_wand) +1 if flag(night_maud):\n    win:\n      flag hs_threshold_read\n      find seven_locks\n      find knows_founders_seal\n      say: {has_item(edmunds_silver_wand): Edmund's wand hums the bell's note against the stone, and the locks turn to it one by one.|You trace the old wards backwards into the stone, {flag(winter_maud): the way Maud showed you|the way the stone seems to want}, and the locks turn one by one.} The wall cracks down the middle and opens. Your hands ache to the elbow.\n    lose:\n      night_hurt += 1\n      find seven_locks\n      find knows_founders_seal\n      say: The stone fights back. Six locks give; the seventh you break, and it breaks something in your hands too. You get through in the end, raw and shaking.\n\n@event hs_beneath\ntitle: The Door Beneath the Lake\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\npriority: 95\nart: door\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nthread: hs_night\n# v2: the seal's odds add up the whole plot and the whole Night (one check, a step per threshold of hs_seal). Each\n# outcome closes the arc and is told in its own aftermath card (which belongs to no arc, so it still comes).\nscene: A vast domed cavern under the lake, a round door of black stone standing open a crack, and through it a star that is pure absence.\ntext: Beyond the wall, the round door, open a crack, and through the crack the Hollow Star looks at you. It is beautiful, and it is hungry, and it speaks in your own voice: \"You came all this way. Open me. Or take what I'm offering. Or run.\" {flag(night_kael): Kael is beside you, frost spreading from his feet.|{flag(night_ilse): Ilse is beside you, breathing steady for once.|{flag(night_maud): Maud is beside you{has_item(edmunds_silver_wand): , and Edmund's wand in your hand is humming the bell's note|, humming the bell's note under her breath}.|{flag(night_isolde): The headmistress stands beside you, her ring lit like a lamp.|You are alone.}}}}\n> seal: Seal the door\n  hint: Needs the star key, the founders' secret or a ward-ring. Arcana; everything you did tonight counts. It can fail.\n  when: flag(star_key) or flag(knows_founders_seal) or clue(knows_founders_seal) or flag(warden_trial) or flag(gave_star_key)\n  alignment +small\n  hs_prep = clue(seven_locks) + clue(star_key) + clue(ilse_truth) + (rel(kael) >= 18) + (rel(ilse) >= 18) + (rel(maud) >= 18) + (rel(isolde) >= 18) + 2 * (flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts)) + flag(sided_self) + (alignment >= 30) + (alignment >= 60) + (spell_level(starlight) >= 1) + (spell_level(ward) >= 1) + flag(wardens_noticed)\n  hs_seal = hs_prep + 2 * flag(hs_tunnel_clear) + 2 * flag(hs_threshold_read) + 2 * flag(cult_broken) + 2 * flag(shadow_beaten) + 2 * (flag(star_key) or flag(gave_star_key)) + 2 * flag(night_isolde) + flag(night_maud) - night_hurt\n  challenge spell master slim +1 if hs_seal >= 4 +1 if hs_seal >= 8 +1 if hs_seal >= 12 +1 if hs_seal >= 16:\n    win:\n      flag hollow_sealed\n      close hollowstar\n      renown +huge\n      mood +large\n      lose star_mark\n      lose hollow_dreams\n      if flag(night_kael) or (met(kael) and rel(kael) >= 0 and not flag(night_ilse) and not flag(night_maud) and not flag(night_isolde)):\n        queue hs_aftermath in 1\n      else:\n        queue hs_aftermath_quiet in 1\n      say: {flag(star_key): The star key turns in the round door, and you|{flag(gave_star_key) and flag(night_isolde): The headmistress turns the star key in the round door, and you|You}} speak the founders' words with everything you have, and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above you, the ice cracks from shore to shore, and the lake breathes again.\n    lose:\n      flag hollow_failed\n      close hollowstar\n      lose hollow_dreams\n      queue hs_aftermath_lost in 1\n      if flag(night_kael):\n        rel kael +large\n        say: Your seal falters. Kael throws every ounce of frost in him into it, and it still isn't enough: the door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\n      else:\n        say: Your seal falters and breaks, and takes most of you with it. The door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\n> bargain: Accept the Star's bargain\n  hint: Its power, now, and a voice in your head forever. Only for those it has already tempted.\n  when: alignment <= -30 or flag(hollow_tempted)\n  flag hollow_bargain\n  close hollowstar\n  alignment -large\n  gold +huge\n  mana +large\n  learn_spell eclipse\n  if not has(star_mark):\n    gain star_mark\n  gain star_crowned\n  lose hollow_dreams\n  queue hs_aftermath_bargain in 1\n  if alignment <= -30:\n    mood +medium\n    say: You take what it offers, and it feels like coming home. The door closes on its own, for now. The Star settles into the back of your mind like a cat finding a warm lap, and pours itself into your hands.\n  else:\n    mood -large\n    say: You take what it offers. The door closes on its own, for now. The Star settles into the back of your mind like a cat finding a warm lap, and pours itself into your hands, and you can't stop shaking.\n> open: Open the door\n  hint: It wants out. Only one already deep in the Hollow could walk through beside it.\n  when: alignment <= -60 or flag(hollow_tempted)\n  if alignment <= -60:\n    flag hollow_freed\n    close hollowstar\n    alignment -large\n    arcana +large\n    gain star_mark\n    lose hollow_dreams\n    renown -medium\n    queue hs_aftermath_freed in 1\n    say: You set your palm on the black stone and push. The door swings wide. The Star rises past you like a breath held for three hundred years, and on its way out it touches your shoulder, the way a lord touches a sword. Above, every candle in Lumenhold goes out at once.\n  else:\n    end ending_hollow_claimed\n> flee: Run\n  close hollowstar\n  mood -large\n  flag hollow_fled\n  queue hs_aftermath_fled in 1\n  say: You run. You run until the tunnel spits you out onto the shore, and you don't look back once. Behind you, under the ice, the door is still ajar.\n\n@event hs_aftermath\ntitle: After the Thaw\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: sun\ntags: hollowstar story\n# No arc (v2): it comes after the arc is closed.\nscene: The lake glittering in spring sunshine, students skimming stones, a bandaged figure sitting on the jetty.\non_show:\n  close hollowstar\n  if night_hurt >= 2:\n    health -medium\n  elif night_hurt >= 1:\n    health -small\ntext: The lake glitters as if nothing happened. Most of the academy believes there was a strange spring frost. The headmistress knows better. So does Kael, who finds you on the jetty with two cups of tea. {flag(ilse_taken): He doesn't say his sister's name. She went north before dawn, with the man in grey.|{flag(dumont_reunited) and not flag(ilse_went_north): Ilse is further down the jetty, pretending not to watch.|}}\n> left: Sit with him in silence\n  rel kael +large\n  mood +medium\n  say: You watch the water. It's just water, now. Kael's tea, for once, doesn't freeze.\n> right: Tell him how you feel\n  if spark(kael) >= 25 and rel(kael) >= 0:\n    spark kael +large\n    bond kael partner\n    say: He kisses you, and for the first time since you've known him, his hands are warm.\n  else:\n    rel kael +large\n    if bond(kael) == \"none\":\n      bond kael close\n    say: \"I owe you everything,\" he says. \"I'm not good at owing people. I'll learn.\"\n\n@event hs_aftermath_lost\ntitle: The Headmistress Closed It\nclass: story\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\nactor: isolde\nart: door\ntags: hollowstar story\n# No arc (v2): it comes after the arc is closed. The seal's cost is paid here.\nscene: An infirmary bed by a window, the lake thawed outside, a tall woman with newly white hair sitting beside it without her silver ring.\non_show:\n  close hollowstar\n  health -large\ntext: You wake in the infirmary. The lake has thawed, and the door is shut: the headmistress came down after you and closed it with her own hands. She is sitting by your bed. Her hair has gone white overnight, and her silver ring is gone. {flag(ilse_taken): Ilse went north before dawn.|}\n> left: \"I'm sorry.\"\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +medium\n  mood -medium\n  say: \"You went down,\" she says. \"Most don't. The door is shut, and I am old, and that was always the price for someone.\" She pats your hand once, like a chess move. You can't stop thinking about it for weeks.\n> right: \"What did it cost you?\"\n  meet isolde\n  lore +small\n  mood -large\n  say: She tells you, plainly, and you wish she hadn't. The seal will hold for another lifetime. She won't. She asks you not to tell the others, and you don't, and it weighs on you.\n\n@event hs_kael_sister\ntitle: The Sister\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: flag(kael_ally) and turn >= 22 and not flag(met_sister) and not flag(ilse_in_village) and not met(ilse)\nweight: 10\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hs_investigation\nscene: A girl in white furs standing at the academy gates in a snowstorm that is falling only on her.\ntext: A girl in white furs is waiting at the gates in a private snowstorm. Kael's younger sister, Ilse. \"If my brother won't open it,\" she says, \"I will. Unless you can give me a reason not to.\"\n> left: Convince her\n  flag met_sister\n  find lead_white_furs\n  find ilse_truth\n  hs_investigation_progress\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      rel kael +large\n      say: You talk for an hour. Then she's crying, and Kael is holding her, and the snow stops. She stays in the village to be near him.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: She listens politely and leaves. \"We'll see each other again,\" she says. \"Beneath the water.\"\n> right: Warn the headmistress\n  flag met_sister\n  find lead_white_furs\n  find ilse_truth\n  rel isolde +small\n  hs_investigation_progress\n  rel kael -small\n  say: Ilse is escorted from the grounds. Kael understands. He says he understands. He's quiet for a long time.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_ilse.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  CHAIN: Ilse Dumont (expansion)\n#  Kael's younger sister, raised by the Hollow cult. She takes a room\n#  above the bakery in year 2 (hs_ilse_arrives, X11); you meet her\n#  there if you follow the lead, or at the gates (hs_kael_sister).\n#    ilse_village -> ilse_frost_lessons -> ilse_grey_returns\n#      -> (ilse_saved) ilse_kael_reunion / ilse_northern_letter / ilse_farewell\n#      -> (ilse_taken) she is gone north\n# ============================================================\n\n@thread ilse\nactor: ilse\nname: Ilse's winter\npremise: Kael's sister, in white furs, in a village that has never seen anyone like her.\njournal:\n  seen(ilse_village) > 0: In the village bakery a girl in white furs froze her coin to the counter. She has Kael's eyes. Her name is Ilse.\n  seen(ilse_frost_lessons) > 0: Ilse traded you Northern frost-craft for news of her brother.\n  flag(ilse_taken) and seen(ilse_grey_returns) > 0: When the man in grey came for her in the lane, Ilse went with him.\n  flag(ilse_saved): When the man in grey came for her, she stepped out from under his hand. You were there.\n  flag(dumont_reunited): Kael and Ilse talked at last: a careful arm's length apart, then less.\n  flag(ilse_went_north): Ilse took the dawn barge north, to tell her family it's over.\n  flag(ilse_decided) and not flag(ilse_went_north): Ilse stayed, and sewed a sliver of winter into your collar in case they come.\n  flag(ilse_goodbye): Under one snowing tree in blossom, Ilse said goodbye.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(ilse_goodbye) or flag(ilse_went_north) or flag(dumont_reunited)\n  missed: flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_saved)\n\n@event ilse_village\ntitle: The Girl at the Bakery\nplace: village\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not met(ilse) and (flag(ilse_in_village) or flag(met_sister))\nweight: 20\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: ilse\nscene: A village bakery counter, a pale girl in white furs pushing a coin that is visibly frosted across the wood, a baffled baker.\ntext: In the village bakery a girl in white furs is trying to pay for a bun with a coin so cold it has frozen to the counter. The baker is losing patience. You recognise those eyes: they're Kael's. \"Ilse,\" she says, before you ask. \"Don't.\"\n> left: Pay for her bun\n  meet ilse\n  gold -tiny\n  rel ilse +medium\n  say: She stares at the bun, then at you, as if kindness is a trick she hasn't learned the counter to yet. \"Fine,\" she says. \"Thank you. Fine.\"\n> right: Leave her to it\n  meet ilse\n  rel ilse -tiny\n  say: You leave. Through the window you see the coin crack, and the baker's breath freeze in the air. Ilse walks out with the bun. Nobody stops her.\n\n@event ilse_frost_lessons\ntitle: A Trade in Winter\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nwhen: rel(ilse) >= 3 and not flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\nweight: 14\nart: snow\ntags: magic hollowstar\nthread: ilse\nscene: A frozen pond at the edge of the woods, a girl in white furs drawing a perfect hexagon of frost in the air, a student copying her clumsily.\ntext: Ilse finds you by the frozen pond. \"My brother won't talk to me. You will. Tell me how he really is, and I'll teach you Northern frost-craft. It's better than anything your professors know.\"\n> left: Tell her the truth about Kael\n  rel ilse +large\n  arcana +medium\n  say: You tell her about the cold hands, {seen(astro_kael_roof): the roof at three in the morning|the nights he doesn't sleep}, the bread. She listens without moving. Then she teaches you to freeze a falling leaf mid-air.\n> right: Protect Kael's privacy\n  rel ilse -small\n  rel kael +small\n  arcana +small\n  say: \"He can tell you himself,\" you say. Ilse's eyes go flat. But the next day, she's at the pond again. \"Fine. I'll teach you anyway. You'll need it.\" She teaches you badly on purpose for an hour, and then, despite herself, well.\n\n@event ilse_grey_returns\ntime: night\ntitle: The Man in Grey Comes for Ilse\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nwhen: rel(ilse) >= 7 and turn >= 20 and not flag(ilse_saved) and not flag(ilse_taken) and not closed(hollowstar)\nweight: 18\nart: mask\ntags: danger hollowstar\nthread: ilse\n# Feedback 2026-10-25: his fight was a fixed level 23 (journeyman, no drift); now expert with drift mid, so he keeps up\n# with you. v2: three ways to stand between them, one of them a fight; losing any of them, he takes her (and the\n# Night gives you one more chance, hs_night_ilse).\nscene: A moonlit village lane with every window frosted over, a man in a grey coat with one hand on a girl's shoulder, frost and shadow coiling around them both.\ntext: A lane behind the bakery, after dark, every window on it frosted over. The man in grey has a hand on Ilse's shoulder and is talking to her softly: \"Home. Your mother is waiting. The door is waiting.\" Ilse's jaw is set and her eyes are wet. She sees you over his shoulder and doesn't call out. She just looks.\n> left: Step between them\n  hint: Combat. He fights like winter. Lose, and he takes her.\n  challenge combat expert drift mid:\n    win:\n      flag ilse_saved grey_beaten\n      rel ilse +large\n      renown +small\n      alignment +small\n      find ilse_truth\n      hs_investigation_progress\n      say: Your spell breaks his grip. He looks at his frosted glove with mild surprise, bows, and walks into the dark. Ilse is shaking. \"Nobody's ever...\" she says, and stops, and shouts after him in Northern instead, every word of it a door slammed.\n    lose:\n      flag ilse_taken\n      alignment +small\n      find ilse_truth\n      hs_investigation_progress\n      say: He's breathing hard when it ends, which is somehow worse. When you get up, the lane is empty except for a single white glove in the snow.\n> talk: \"She's not yours to take.\"\n  hint: Charm. Better if you know what she was raised for.\n  challenge negotiate expert +1 if clue(ilse_truth) +1 if rel(ilse) >= 15:\n    win:\n      flag ilse_saved\n      rel ilse +large\n      alignment +small\n      find ilse_truth\n      hs_investigation_progress\n      say: You tell him, out loud, in the street, what they raised her for and what she's chosen instead. Ilse says \"Yes,\" very clearly, and steps out from under his hand. He looks at the two of you for a long moment, then bows and walks into the dark.\n    lose:\n      flag ilse_taken\n      mood -medium\n      find ilse_truth\n      hs_investigation_progress\n      say: He agrees with every word, gently, and Ilse's shoulders drop as he talks. When he turns her toward the road, she goes. At the corner she looks back once. In the snow there's a single white glove.\n> right: Run for Kael\n  hint: Sure, if Kael is here to fetch. She'll know who you went for.\n  if met(kael):\n    flag ilse_saved\n    rel kael +large\n    rel ilse -small\n    find ilse_truth\n    hs_investigation_progress\n    say: Kael arrives like a blizzard, shouting her name. The man in grey takes one look at him and leaves without a word. Brother and sister stand in the snow, not touching, not speaking, until Ilse punches him in the arm and the snow stops. \"You went for him,\" she tells you later. \"Not for me.\"\n  else:\n    flag ilse_taken\n    mood -medium\n    find ilse_truth\n    hs_investigation_progress\n    say: By the time you're back with help, the lane is empty. There's frost on the cobbles in the shape of a closed door.\n\n@event ilse_kael_reunion\ntitle: Brother and Sister\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nwhen: flag(ilse_saved) and met(kael) and not flag(dumont_reunited) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: social hollowstar\nthread: ilse\nscene: Two pale siblings on opposite ends of a frozen jetty, a third person between them holding three cups of tea.\ntext: Kael and Ilse have been in the same village for weeks and have spoken eleven words to each other. You have three cups of tea, a frozen jetty and a very bad idea.\n> left: Sit them both down\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      flag dumont_reunited\n      rel kael +large\n      rel ilse +large\n      say: It takes an hour and two cups of tea are frozen solid. Then Ilse says \"I was scared,\" and Kael says \"So was I,\" and you quietly leave them to it.\n    lose:\n      rel kael +small\n      rel ilse +small\n      say: They argue in a language you don't speak, at a temperature you can't endure. But they argue — which, you suspect, is a start.\n> right: Let them find their own way\n  flag dumont_reunited\n  rel kael +small\n  rel ilse +small\n  say: You leave the tea on the jetty. A week later you see them walking by the lake together, a careful arm's length apart. Then less.\n\n@event ilse_northern_letter\ntitle: A Summons From the North\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nwhen: flag(ilse_saved) and final_year and not flag(ilse_decided)\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: hollowstar\nthread: ilse\nscene: A girl holding a letter sealed with a black star, standing at a window that looks north toward far white mountains.\ntext: A letter with a black-star seal. The Dumont family summons Ilse home. \"If I don't go, they'll send someone worse than the man in grey,\" she says. \"If I do go, I could tell them to their faces that it's over. Or they could keep me.\"\n> left: \"Go. Tell them it's over.\"\n  flag ilse_decided ilse_went_north\n  rel ilse +medium\n  say: She leaves on the dawn barge, chin up. Before she goes, she presses her forehead to yours, a Northern farewell. \"If I'm not back by graduation,\" she says, \"come and get me.\"\n> right: \"Stay. Let them come.\"\n  flag ilse_decided\n  rel ilse +large\n  gain frost_ward\n  say: She stays. She sews a sliver of winter into your collar \"in case they do come\". Nobody does. Not yet.\n\n@event ilse_farewell\ntitle: Snow in Blossomtide\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nwhen: flag(ilse_saved) and final_year and month >= 7 and month <= 9 and not flag(ilse_goodbye) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\nweight: 14\nart: snow\ntags: social\nthread: ilse\nscene: A single snowflake falling in a spring orchard full of blossom, a girl in white furs catching it on her glove and holding it out.\ntext: It's spring, the orchard is in full blossom, and it is snowing — gently, over one tree. Ilse is underneath it. \"Graduation's soon,\" she says. \"Everyone's leaving. I wanted to give you something before you forgot me.\"\n> left: \"I won't forget you.\"\n  flag ilse_goodbye\n  rel ilse +large\n  gain frost_ward\n  mood +medium\n  say: She doesn't believe you, but she smiles anyway — the first real smile you've seen on her. She gives you a snowflake that never melts.\n> right: Invite her to the graduation\n  flag ilse_goodbye\n  rel ilse +large\n  rel kael +small\n  say: \"Me? At a graduation?\" She looks horrified, and then, slowly, delighted. On the day, she sits next to Kael in the second row and cries more than anyone.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_thornwick.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The Thornwick rivalry (playtest 2, X9): four years of a rival\n#  school and one friend in it, apart from the Hollow Star.\n#    the pen pal: life_pen_pal (y1) -> Tamsin's letters (social,\n#      actor tamsin) -> asked for secrets (y2) / offered theirs\n#      (y3) -> what they were sent to do (tamsin_confession)\n#    the fixture: life_rival_academy (y1, they visit) ->\n#      rival_return_visit (y2) -> rival_contest (y3, combat) ->\n#      rival_last_match (y4) -> tamsin_farewell\n#  Ties: the Thornwick internship, Lyra's opinion, a yearbook line.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The letters ----------------\n\n@event tamsin_second_letter\ntitle: Insufferable AND Rude\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A second letter with a green Thornwick seal on a dormitory pillow, the word \"NOTED\" underlined three times.\ntext: A second letter from Thornwick, in the same quick hand. \"Insufferable AND rude. Noted. I'm giving you one more chance, because I'm a generous person and because my roommate bet me you'd never answer. Prove her wrong. T. Reed.\"\n> left: Write back, finally\n  meet tamsin\n  rel tamsin +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write back, grudgingly at first and then not at all grudgingly. Four pages. The answer comes within a week, with a postscript: \"My roommate owes me a pie. Thank you.\"\n> right: Let it lie\n  say: You leave it on the pillow. Somewhere across the hills, a roommate wins a pie. You'll never know what you missed, which is how it should be, probably.\n\n@event tamsin_letter_gossip\ntitle: News From Thornwick\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social letter\nscene: A letter with a green seal open on a desk, its margins full of tiny unflattering sketches of Thornwick professors.\ntext: {Tamsin has written again, six pages of Thornwick gossip with sketches of the professors in the margins.|Another letter from Tamsin, signed \"your rival, nemesis and only friend west of the hills\".|Tamsin's letter is mostly a drawing of their illusion professor as a goat.} {Their head of house has banned whistling.|Someone at Thornwick set the library on fire, a little.|They want to know if Lumenhold's food is really as bad as everyone says.} Answering properly would take an evening.\n> left: Write back at length\n  rel tamsin +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You write till the candle gutters, with sketches of your own professors. Tamsin's reply calls your drawing of Thorne \"libellous and correct\".|Five pages back, with the castle's gossip and your worst week. They answer the worst week first.|You answer the goat with your own worst professor as a hen, and four pages on the refectory food. Tamsin replies that you've undersold the food.}\n> right: A few lines and a sketch\n  rel tamsin +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {A short note and a drawing of a gargoyle. Tamsin writes back that it's \"the most Lumenhold thing ever sent through the post\".|Three lines. Tamsin replies with three pages, offended and delighted.|A few lines about the food and a goat of your own in the margin. Tamsin replies with a whole menagerie.}\n\n@event tamsin_letter_questions\ntitle: Twenty Questions\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: social letter\nscene: A letter numbered down the margin from one to twenty, each line a question, a student chewing a quill over the answers.\ntext: {Tamsin's letter is a numbered list of twenty questions about life at Lumenhold, from \"Is the lake haunted?\" to \"What do you eat for breakfast?\"|Another list of questions. Number seven is \"Who is the most annoying person at your school?\"|Questions again. \"Be honest,\" says number one. \"Number twenty can be a lie.\"} {Number twelve asks about your exams.|Number fifteen asks about the lake.|Number three asks about the food.} Number nineteen just says \"Are you happy?\"\n> left: Answer every one honestly\n  rel tamsin +small\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Twenty honest answers, even number nineteen. It's harder than it looks. Their reply begins, \"Thank you for nineteen.\"|You answer the lot. Writing it down, you learn a few things about yourself.|You answer all twenty honestly, even number three, which is about the food and unkind. Number twenty you leave as a lie, as permitted.}\n> right: Make the answers outrageous\n  charm +tiny\n  rel tamsin +tiny\n  say: {The lake is haunted by a choir of geese. Breakfast is boiled bricks. Tamsin's reply is two pages of laughter and a request for \"the true answers, eventually\".|You invent a Lumenhold of dragons and duels before dawn. Tamsin believes none of it and asks for more.|Every answer is outrageous except number twenty, which is the true one. Tamsin works out which, and writes back only \"I knew it.\"}\n\n@event tamsin_letter_trouble\ntitle: A Short Letter\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: rel(tamsin) >= 4\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social letter\nscene: A single short page with a green seal, the handwriting cramped and unsteady, a blot where a sentence was crossed out hard.\ntext: Tamsin's letter is one page, which has never happened. They failed their illusion examination, the one thing they were good at, and their grandmother's theatre has been sold. There's a sentence crossed out so hard the nib went through the paper.\n> left: Write back that night\n  rel tamsin +medium\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You write until two in the morning: the time you failed, what helped, a list of everything they're good at that isn't illusion. Their next letter is six pages again, and the first line is \"You idiot, you made me cry in the refectory.\"\n> right: Send them something instead\n  gold -small\n  rel tamsin +small\n  say: You send a parcel: Mabel's best biscuits, a book of plays, a drawing of a gargoyle holding a sign that says \"RETAKE\". Tamsin writes back that the gargoyle is on their wall now, and that they passed.\n\n@event tamsin_letter_asks_secret\ntitle: A Small Favour\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: year >= 2 and rel(tamsin) >= 6\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social letter rival\nscene: A letter half-read on a desk, a single paragraph underlined twice, a student staring out of a window at the hills.\ntext: Tamsin's letter is cheerful until the last paragraph. \"Small favour. Thornwick's duelling captain wants to know Lumenhold's tournament order. Just the order. Everyone does it, honestly. I'd owe you.\" The underline is very neat, as if they did it twice.\n> left: Send the real order\n  alignment -small\n  rel tamsin +small\n  flag told_thornwick\n  say: You write it out: who fights first, who last. It feels like nothing at all until you post it, and then it feels like a stone. Tamsin's reply thanks you, too warmly.\n> right: Send a false one\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel tamsin -tiny\n  say: You invent an order, plausible and wrong. At the tournament, Thornwick's captain looks baffled for the whole first round. Tamsin's next letter is short and doesn't mention it, which is how you know they know.\n> refuse: Say no, and say why\n  rel tamsin -small\n  alignment +small\n  say: \"Ask me anything else,\" you write. Their reply is a week late and very polite. Then, a fortnight after: \"You were right. Sorry. Still friends?\" You write back yes before you've finished reading.\n\n@event tamsin_letter_offers_secret\ntitle: Their Turn\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: year == 3 and month <= 7 and seen(rival_contest) == 0 and rel(tamsin) >= 8 and not flag(thornwick_secrets)\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social letter rival\nscene: A folded paper inside a letter, covered in diagrams of duelling circles and arrows, a student holding it at arm's length as if it were hot.\ntext: Folded inside Tamsin's letter is a second paper: Thornwick's whole plan for this year's contest, every pairing, every trick. \"Fair's fair,\" they've written. \"Burn it or use it. I won't ask which.\"\n> left: Use it\n  flag thornwick_secrets\n  alignment -small\n  rel tamsin +small\n  say: You keep the plan and study it until you know it better than Thornwick does. It will help. You can't quite look at your own reflection while you read it.\n> right: Burn it unread\n  alignment +small\n  rel tamsin +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You burn it in the candle without unfolding it, and write back that you did. Their reply has one line: \"I knew you would. That's why I sent it.\" You're still not sure if it was a test.\n\n@event tamsin_confession\ntitle: What I Was Sent to Do\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nwhen: year >= 2 and rel(tamsin) >= 10\nramp: rel(tamsin) 18..30\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: social letter rival\nscene: A long letter in a shaky hand spread across a desk, a green seal broken beside it, a student reading with one hand over their mouth.\ntext: Tamsin's letter is long and the hand is unsteady. \"I have to tell you. The first letter wasn't random. Thornwick set some of us to write to Lumenhold students and report back. I reported for two letters. Then I liked you, and I stopped, and I've been ashamed ever since. You can stop writing. I'd understand.\"\n> left: Forgive them\n  rel tamsin +large\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write back one line: \"Third letter onwards, then. That's when I started liking you too.\" Their reply is the longest yet, and the first not signed with an insult.\n> right: Ask what they reported\n  rel tamsin +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: They tell you, exactly: the food, the weather, your opinion of Professor Thorne. Nothing that mattered. \"I made it boring on purpose,\" they write. You laugh, and then you forgive them anyway.\n\n@event tamsin_letter_after\ntitle: After Graduation\nactor: tamsin\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: final_year and month >= 7 and rel(tamsin) >= 6\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: social letter\nscene: A letter with a green seal and a pressed flower, a map of the realm sketched on the back with two towns circled.\ntext: Tamsin's letter has a map on the back with two towns circled: Lumenhold and Thornwick, and a line between them. \"After graduation, there'll be no school to be rivals for. What are we, then?\" Under it, smaller: \"Don't answer that. Answer it.\"\n> left: \"Friends. Obviously.\"\n  rel tamsin +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You write it in letters an inch high. Their reply has the map again, with a third town circled halfway between: \"Meeting point. Every spring. Bring biscuits.\"\n> right: \"Still rivals. Obviously.\"\n  rel tamsin +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: \"Excellent,\" they write back. \"I was worried you'd go soft.\" Under it, very small: \"Friends too, though. Obviously.\"\n\n# ---------------- The yearly fixture ----------------\n\n@pool chain_thornwick_auto_story\ntrigger: auto\nclass: story\ntags: rival\n\n@event rival_return_visit\ntitle: To Thornwick\npool: chain_thornwick_auto_story\nwhen: year == 2 and month == 8 and not off_campus\ntype: menu\ntone: light\nart: tower\ntags: social\nscene: A barge full of Lumenhold students arriving at a rival academy of green-roofed towers, a crowd of green-robed students waiting on the jetty.\ntext: This year it's Lumenhold's turn to visit. A barge leaves for Thornwick at dawn with a delegation of students who have been told to behave. {met(tamsin): Tamsin has written three letters about it. The last one just says \"COME\".|Thornwick has, by all accounts, better plumbing.}\n> go: Join the delegation\n  hint: Two days across the hills. The barge fare is yours.\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  if met(tamsin):\n    rel tamsin +medium\n    say: Tamsin is on the jetty, smaller than their letters and louder, holding a sign with your name spelled wrong on purpose. They show you every corner of Thornwick and argue that each one beats Lumenhold. They're mostly wrong.\n  else:\n    charm +tiny\n    say: Thornwick's towers are green-roofed and smug, and their plumbing really is better. A student in green follows you around the library, asking about the lake.\n> stay: Stay at the castle\n  hint: A quiet weekend with half the school gone.\n  rest\n  if met(tamsin):\n    rel tamsin -small\n    say: You stay. The castle is wonderfully quiet. Tamsin's next letter is very short, and sulks, and then forgives you on the last line.\n  else:\n    say: The castle is half empty and wholly peaceful. You read in the window seat all weekend and don't miss Thornwick at all.\n\n@event rival_contest\ntitle: The Inter-School Contest\npool: chain_thornwick_auto_story\nwhen: year == 3 and month == 8 and not off_campus\ntone: tense\nart: trophy\ntags: duel\nscene: A packed duelling arena under banners of blue and green, two duellists facing each other in the chalk ring, a crowd on its feet.\ntext: The inter-school contest, at Lumenhold this year, before both schools and half the village. You've made the team. {met(tamsin): In the final, the name opposite yours is Tamsin Reed. They wave from across the ring, apologetically, and then stop smiling.|In the final, a Thornwick illusionist faces you, smiling far too much.}{flag(thornwick_secrets): You know every trick they've planned.|}\n> left: Fight to win\n  if flag(thornwick_secrets):\n    challenge combat adept likely:\n      win:\n        flag thornwick_contest_won\n        renown +large\n        health -small\n        say: You counter every planned trick before it's cast. Lumenhold wins the contest, and the castle roars. {met(tamsin): Tamsin shakes your hand afterwards and holds on too long. \"You read the plan,\" they say quietly. \"Good.\" You can't tell if they mean it.|}\n      lose:\n        renown -tiny\n        health -medium\n        say: Knowing the plan isn't enough: they change it in the ring. Thornwick takes the cup. The plan, in your pocket, feels heavier than ever.\n  else:\n    challenge combat adept:\n      win:\n        flag thornwick_contest_won\n        renown +large\n        health -small\n        say: An honest win, hard and close. Lumenhold takes the cup and carries you round the arena. {met(tamsin): Tamsin is on the ground laughing. \"Next year,\" they gasp. \"Next year I'll flatten you.\"|}\n      lose:\n        renown -tiny\n        health -medium\n        say: You lose by a breath. Thornwick sings all the way back to their barge. {met(tamsin): Tamsin helps you up and doesn't gloat, not once, which you'll never forget.|}\n> right: Fight clean and generous\n  renown +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  if met(tamsin):\n    rel tamsin +small\n  say: You fight well and fairly, and when you have them down you let them stand before the next exchange. You lose on points. The crowd stands for both of you anyway, and Lyra, in the stands, actually claps.\n\n@event rival_last_match\ntitle: The Last Match\npool: chain_thornwick_auto_story\nwhen: year == 4 and month == 8 and not off_campus\ntone: warm\nart: trophy\ntags: duel\nscene: An evening exhibition match on the lawn between two academies, lanterns strung between the trees, final-year students in blue and green laughing in the ring.\ntext: The last match of your year against Thornwick's: an exhibition on the lawn, lanterns in the trees, nothing at stake but four years of grudges. {met(tamsin): Tamsin has asked to be your opponent. \"For the record,\" they say. \"Ours.\"|Thornwick's final-years have brought cake, which feels like a trick.}\n> left: Give it everything\n  challenge combat adept+:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      queue tamsin_farewell in 1\n      say: You win the last exchange of your schooling against Thornwick, and the lawn erupts. {met(tamsin): Tamsin, flat on the grass, laughs up at the lanterns. \"Fine,\" they say. \"Fine. Record's yours.\"|}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      queue tamsin_farewell in 1\n      say: You lose, gloriously, and lie on the grass looking at the lanterns. {met(tamsin): Tamsin lies down next to you. \"Record's mine,\" they say, and then, quieter, \"I'll miss this.\"|}\n> right: Turn it into a show\n  charm +small\n  mood +small\n  queue tamsin_farewell in 1\n  say: You turn the match into a performance, illusions and flourishes and a duel that never lands a blow. {met(tamsin): Tamsin catches on at once and plays along. The two schools applaud the same thing for the first time in a century.|Both schools applaud the same thing for the first time in a century.}\n\n@event tamsin_farewell\ntitle: Across the Hills\ntrigger: queued\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(tamsin)\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: Two students at a barge landing at dusk, one in Lumenhold blue and one in Thornwick green, exchanging small wrapped parcels.\ntext: At the barge landing, before Thornwick's delegation leaves for the last time, Tamsin gives you a small parcel. Inside: every letter you ever sent them, tied with string. \"So you can read how insufferable you were,\" they say. \"I kept copies. Obviously.\"\n> left: Give them yours\n  rel tamsin +medium\n  mood +small\n  flag tamsin_friends\n  say: You hand over the box you've kept under your bed for four years: every letter they sent, every insult, every postscript. They read the first one on the barge steps and laugh, and the barge leaves with them still laughing.\n> right: \"Write to me. Same as ever.\"\n  rel tamsin +small\n  flag tamsin_friends\n  say: \"Every week,\" they promise. \"Insufferably.\" The barge pulls away. They're still waving when it rounds the bend, and so are you.\n\n# ---------------- Ties ----------------\n\n@event intern_thornwick_tamsin\ntitle: A Familiar Hand\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(tamsin)\nweight: 16\nart: mask\ntags: study social\nscene: A laboratory at a rival academy, a student in Thornwick green looking up from a glowing illusion on a bench, mid-grin, at a visitor in the doorway.\ntext: At the Thornwick lab, the student at the next bench, back from a week away, looks up from a glowing illusion and grins. It's Tamsin. \"They said a Lumenhold intern had come,\" they say. \"I told them it had better be you, or I'd sabotage everything.\"\n> left: Work beside them from now on\n  wage\n  perform intern_thornwick +tiny\n  rel tamsin +medium\n  say: The rest of the summer at the next bench. They show you every trick in Thornwick's book, and you show them three Lumenhold ones they swear aren't legal. Your supervisor calls you \"the inseparables\", not kindly.\n> right: Keep it professional\n  wage\n  perform intern_thornwick +medium\n  rel tamsin -tiny\n  say: You work at your own bench and keep your mind on the work. The lab notices; Tamsin notices more. \"Fine,\" they say at the end. \"You were right. But I missed you, at the next bench.\"\n\n@event lyra_on_thornwick\ntitle: Lyra on Thornwick\nactor: lyra\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: seen(rival_contest) > 0 and not off_campus and around(lyra)\nweight: 12\nart: duel\ntags: social rival\nscene: A proud red-haired student sitting on a bench by the duelling hall, a newspaper folded to the contest results across her knee.\ntext: Lyra has the Ledger folded to the contest results. \"Thornwick,\" she says, as if it were a mild disease. {met(tamsin): \"And you write letters to one of them. Every week. I've seen the green seals.\"|\"I hope you enjoyed humiliating them. Or being humiliated. I was too angry to watch closely.\"}\n> left: Defend Thornwick\n  rel lyra -small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You tell her Thornwick's people are people. She listens with enormous scepticism. A month later she asks, very casually, whether {met(tamsin): your Thornwick friend|any of Thornwick's duellists} would ever want a practice duel. \"For research,\" she says.\n> right: Agree they're insufferable\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: \"Insufferable,\" you agree. She looks almost happy. \"Exactly,\" she says. \"Thank you.\" {met(tamsin): You don't tell Tamsin, who would agree with her.|You keep to yourself that the Thornwick illusionist was rather good.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/chain_timeloop.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The time loop (playtest 2, X8 with L21): the cards each loop\n#  can lead to. The start, the hub and the fix are in\n#  expansion_life.grim (life_timeloop_1/2/3). Every card here ends\n#  by looping again (loop_n += 1), and the ninth loop breaks it.\n#  Nothing done in an experiment lasts; what you exploit does,\n#  and costs more each time (loop_exploit).\n# ============================================================\n\n@thread timeloop\nname: Tuesday\npremise: It's Tuesday. It was Tuesday yesterday, too.\njournal:\n  flag(timeloop_started): You woke to eggs, and Thorne tripping on the third step, and it was Tuesday again.\n  seen(timeloop_noticed) > 0: Seraphine saw the creases in the day. Tomorrow she won't remember saying so.\n  seen(timeloop_clue_bells) > 0: Every bell in the castle strikes on time except the astronomy tower's.\n  seen(timeloop_clue_light) > 0: One lamp burns in the astronomy tower before dawn, and goes out the moment the morning begins.\n  seen(timeloop_clue_firstyear) > 0: A first-year who dreads Wednesday has written \"one more day\" in his margin.\n  flag(loop_perfect_day): You gave a lonely stranger one perfect Tuesday. Only you remember it.\n  flag(timeloop_done): The great clock in the astronomy tower burst its springs, and it was Wednesday.\n  seen(timeloop_after) > 0: Weeks later, the Tuesdays still visit you in dreams.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(timeloop_done)\n\n@event timeloop_exploit\ntitle: Every Bet, Every Answer\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 8\nart: coin\ntags: mystery money\nthread: timeloop\nscene: A student at the centre of a crowd in the courtyard collecting coins from groaning classmates, a quiz paper marked full marks in their other hand.\ntext: {loop_exploit == 0: You know which way every coin will fall, and what's on this afternoon's quiz.|{loop_exploit == 1: The bets again. Your classmates are starting to look at you oddly when you win.|The bets, the quiz, the same faces losing. Their groans have stopped being funny.}} {~The quiz is on the founders.|A fourth-year bets you can't name every tower.|Someone bets on which way the prefect will fall.}\n> left: Take everything you can\n  gold +medium\n  study +tiny\n  alignment -small\n  loop_exploit += 1\n  if loop_exploit >= 3:\n    mood -medium\n    health -small\n    queue timeloop_noticed now\n    say: You win it all again, and the winning has a taste like tin. The loop tightens: the morning is shorter now, the edges closer. And someone is watching you across the courtyard as if they know.\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    loop_n += 1\n    if loop_n >= 9:\n      queue timeloop_cap now\n    else:\n      queue life_timeloop_2 now\n    say: {~The coins are real when you wake, and the marks stay in the ledger. So does the hollow feeling, a little deeper each time.|You win again. The crowns carry over into the next Tuesday. So does the weight of them.|You win the bet on the prefect, who falls exactly where you knew. The coins carry over; so does the look on the prefect's face.}\n> right: Just the quiz, then stop\n  study +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {~Full marks, and nothing else. It's a small cheat, and it still sits badly at supper.|You ace the quiz and leave the bets alone. A small mercy, to yourself mostly.|You take the quiz and leave the bet on the prefect alone. You don't even watch the fall.}\n\n@event timeloop_noticed\ntitle: \"You've Done This Before\"\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nactor: seraphine\nrepeat: 2\nart: eye\ntags: mystery social\nthread: timeloop\nscene: A seer catching a student's sleeve in a crowded corridor, her eyes very clear, the other students blurred around them as if moving too fast.\ntext: Seraphine catches your sleeve in the corridor. Her eyes are very clear. \"You've done this before,\" she says. \"Today. Many times. I can see the creases in it.\" She doesn't let go. \"Whatever you're taking from it, it's taking from you too.\"\n> left: Tell her everything\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  loop_clues += 1\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You tell her, all of it, in a window seat. She listens and then says, \"Ask the clocks in the astronomy tower.\" Tomorrow she won't remember. Today, she holds your hand like she will.\n> right: Pull away\n  meet seraphine\n  mood -small\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You pull free and walk fast. She doesn't follow. \"I'll forget this,\" she calls after you. \"You won't.\" She's right, and that's the worst of it.\n\n@event timeloop_dare_confess\ntitle: Things You'd Never Say\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 3\nart: heart\ntags: mystery romance\nthread: timeloop\nscene: Two students in an empty corridor at dusk, one mid-sentence with their heart on their sleeve, the other's face caught between surprise and joy.\ntext: {Nothing today will last. So you find the person you've never told, and you tell them.|Another Tuesday, another confession nobody will remember.|You've confessed to them three times now, three different ways.} {They look at you as if you've handed them something breakable.|They laugh, kindly, then don't.|They take a long time to answer.}\n> left: Say it all, properly\n  mood +small\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {You say all of it. They say something back that you'll keep for the rest of your life, and they'll have forgotten by breakfast. It's the most bittersweet thing you've ever done.|They kiss you, or hug you, or cry; it's different each time. By morning it never happened. Only you know.|You say it a fourth way. They take a long time to answer, and when they do it's the best answer yet. Gone by morning.}\n> right: Say it badly, on purpose\n  mood +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {You make a terrible speech, as a rehearsal. They're baffled. You learn exactly which words you'd use if this were real, and file them away.|You stumble through it, and practise, and stumble less. One Tuesday you'll say it on a Wednesday.|You say it badly on purpose, and they take a long time to answer anyway, as if they heard what you meant.}\n\n@event timeloop_dare_fall\ntitle: Off the Observatory Roof\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 3\nart: feather\ntags: mystery\nthread: timeloop\nscene: A student standing on the observatory parapet with arms wide, the lake far below, the morning sky enormous.\ntext: {If nothing lasts, then nothing can hurt for long. You climb the observatory parapet with the whole lake below you.|The parapet again. You know exactly how the wind feels at the top.|You've done this before. You still can't quite believe you do it.} {The lake is very blue.|Gulls wheel below you.|The bell in the tower is about to ring.}\n> left: Jump\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {You fall for a long, bright moment, and the lake rushes up, and you wake in your bed to the smell of eggs. Your heart is still pounding. It was glorious, and you'll never do it again. You'll do it again.|You hit the water like a bell. Then: eggs, sunlight, your own bed. You laugh out loud at breakfast and the prefect stares.|You jump as the bell rings, and fall in time with it. Eggs, sunlight, bed, and the bell still in your ears.}\n> right: Just look\n  mood +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {You sit on the parapet and watch the whole valley wake, the same as yesterday, the same as tomorrow. From up here the loop looks almost peaceful.|You don't jump. You count every roof in the village instead, and find one more than there should be.|The bell rings while you're sitting on the edge. You count the strokes, the same as yesterday, and climb down.}\n\n@event timeloop_dare_kindness\ntitle: One Perfect Day for a Stranger\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 3\nart: sun\ntags: mystery social\nthread: timeloop\nscene: A shy first-year in the refectory looking astonished at a slice of cake, a note and a flower appearing one after another in front of them.\ntext: {There's a shy first-year who eats alone every Tuesday. Today you know everything that will go wrong for them, and you can stop all of it.|The same first-year, the same table. You know their day by heart now.|{flag(loop_perfect_day): You've made this stranger's Tuesday perfect before. You do it again anyway.|You've sat with this stranger before. Today you could do more.}} {They get the juice in the face at ten.|They fail the quiz by one mark.|Someone laughs at their hat.}\n> left: Make it perfect\n  flag loop_perfect_day\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {You move the juice, whisper the quiz answer, compliment the hat and buy them a slice of cake at tea. By evening they're laughing with three new friends. Tomorrow none of it will have happened, except in you.|A perfect day, built carefully, down to the cake you paid for, gone by morning. You don't mind as much as you thought you would.|You get to the hat before the laughter does, and by lunch it's the fashion. By evening three people are wearing one. None of them will remember.}\n> right: Just sit with them\n  mood +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: {You sit with them at lunch and talk about nothing. They smile once. That one, somehow, you're sure they keep.|An hour of company for someone lonely. It doesn't last. It isn't nothing.|Someone laughs at their hat, and you laugh too, kindly, with them. They decide to keep it on.}\n\n@event timeloop_clue_bells\ntitle: The Bells Are Wrong\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 2\nart: bell\ntags: mystery study\nthread: timeloop\nscene: A student counting chimes in a castle stairwell, one hand raised, the great clock face above them stopped between two hours.\ntext: You spend this Tuesday listening. Every bell in the castle strikes on time, except the astronomy tower's, which strikes a beat late, and then a beat early, as if it's arguing with itself.\n> left: Follow the sound up the tower\n  lore +small\n  loop_clues += 1\n  health -tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: Four hundred steps. At the top the door is locked and humming. Through the keyhole, gears turning the wrong way. The morning folds before you can open it.\n> right: Ask the clockmaster\n  lore +tiny\n  loop_clues += 1\n  mood -tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: The clockmaster swears the tower clock is perfect. \"The big chronometer up there? Not wound for fifty years.\" He looks at you oddly. \"Unless someone's been at it.\"\n\n@event timeloop_clue_light\ntitle: A Light in the Astronomy Tower\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 2\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery study\nthread: timeloop\nscene: A dark astronomy tower at dawn, a single lamp burning in its highest window, a student watching from a courtyard far below.\ntext: You wake before the eggs this Tuesday and look out of the window. One lamp is burning in the astronomy tower, the only light in the castle. It goes out at the exact moment the morning begins.\n> left: Be there when it goes out\n  loop_clues += 1\n  health -small\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You run the stairs in the dark and reach the top as the lamp dies. Someone small is curled by a great brass clock, asleep. Then the morning folds, and you're in bed again, breathless.\n> right: Watch the window all night\n  loop_clues += 1\n  mood -small\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You don't sleep. At three a shadow passes the window: someone small, with a wand. At four the lamp goes out, and so do you.\n\n@event timeloop_clue_firstyear\ntitle: The Boy Who Dreads Wednesday\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 4\nart: book\ntags: mystery social\nthread: timeloop\nscene: A small first-year hunched over a textbook at breakfast, not eating, a note in the margin reading \"one more day\".\ntext: {There's a first-year who doesn't eat breakfast on Tuesdays. He reads the same page of a textbook over and over. In the margin he's written \"one more day\".|The boy again, the page again. He's in your dreams now.|You sit down opposite the boy before he can leave.} Tomorrow is his first exam.\n> left: Ask him about the exam\n  loop_clues += 1\n  charm +tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: He's terrified of it: if he fails, he goes home. \"I wished for one more day,\" he says, and then looks frightened. \"I mean, I just wished.\" He glances at the astronomy tower without meaning to.\n> right: Help him study instead\n  loop_clues += 1\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  loop_n += 1\n  if loop_n >= 9:\n    queue timeloop_cap now\n  else:\n    queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You teach him the chapter he's been failing to read. He nearly gets it. He mentions, offhand, the big clock in the astronomy tower, \"the one that grants wishes, people say\".\n\n@event timeloop_cap\ntitle: Wednesday, Whether You Like It or Not\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery magic\nthread: timeloop\nscene: A shattered brass chronometer in a tower room at dawn, springs and gears scattered across the floor, a first-year and a student staring at the wreck.\ntext: The loop pulls tight one last time and snaps. Somewhere above, a great clock bursts its springs, and the morning comes in like a wave. It's Wednesday. You feel as if you've run every Tuesday at once.\n> left: Climb the tower to see\n  flag timeloop_done\n  health -small\n  mood -small\n  queue timeloop_after in 1\n  say: The astronomy tower's chronometer lies in pieces, and a first-year sits among them, white-faced. \"I just wanted one more day,\" he whispers. You sit with him until the clockmaster comes. Your bones ache for a week.\n> right: Go back to bed\n  flag timeloop_done\n  mood -medium\n  queue timeloop_after in 1\n  say: You sleep through Wednesday's breakfast, and it is the best sleep of your life. When you wake, nobody remembers anything, and you remember all of it.\n\n@event timeloop_after\ntitle: Only You Remember\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery\nthread: timeloop\nscene: A student alone at a window seat on an ordinary morning, a cup of tea going cold, a clock on the wall ticking forward at last.\ntext: Weeks later, the Tuesdays still visit you in dreams. {loop_exploit >= 2: People remember your winning streak, though not why, and some of them have stopped betting with you.|Nobody else remembers anything.} You remember the eggs, the bells, the same morning over and over. You could tell someone, if you wanted.\n> left: Tell one person the truth\n  mood +small\n  if loop_exploit >= 2:\n    renown -tiny\n    say: You tell a friend everything over tea. They don't believe a word and believe all of it. At the end they ask, gently, whether you gave back the winnings. You didn't. It weighs, a little.\n  else:\n    say: You tell a friend everything over tea. They don't believe a word and believe all of it. {seen(timeloop_dare_kindness) > 0: At the end they say, \"So you were kind to a stranger, again and again, and nobody knew.\" You nod. \"Sounds like you,\" they say.|At the end they say, \"So you lived one day over and over, and nobody knew.\" You nod. \"Sounds exhausting,\" they say.}\n> right: Keep it to yourself\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You keep it. Some mornings you catch a phrase you once said in a loop, and the person you say it to looks at you as if they almost remember. They never quite do.\n"},{"path":"content/events/chains_minor.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Minor chains: short cause-and-effect stories.\n#    forbidden  — the Restricted Section key (from umbral_forbidden_notes)\n#    curse      — curing the Umbral Curse\n#    debt       — Grisk the moneylender (see state in_debt)\n#    familiar   — Ember the fox-spirit\n#    ghost      — the haunted dormitory\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Restricted Section ----------------\n# Feedback 2026-10-16: the key (umbral_forbidden_notes) is a lead, and the chain is played by going there: each step is\n# a card at the Restricted Section after hours (evening or midnight), waiting on the key and the step before.\n\n@thread forbidden\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=flag(forbidden_key)\nclues: lead_codex_open lead_restricted_shelves lead_silver_key\nname: The silver key\npremise: A graduating fourth-year's notebook, in three colours of ink, and a key to the Restricted Section.\njournal:\n  flag(forbidden_key): Odile Varn left you her notebook, and a silver key to the Restricted Section.\n  flag(forbidden_rounds): You learned the prefects' rounds from Odile's green ink.\n  flag(forbidden_shelfmarks): Odile's red ink, underlined twice: never with bare hands.\n  flag(forbidden_abandoned): You locked the door again and hung the key in your wardrobe. Some nights it rattles.\n  flag(forbidden_inside): You slipped into the Restricted Section after hours and read by candle-stub.\n  flag(forbidden_warned): Odile's last advice: if a book falls open at your feet, don't let it choose you.\n  flag(forbidden_read): A book fell open at your feet, and you read it until dawn.\n  seen(forbidden_section_3) > 0: The headmistress herself caught you with that book in your hands.\nstatus:\n  resolved: seen(forbidden_section_3) > 0\n  let_go: flag(forbidden_abandoned)\nmembers:\n  start umbral_forbidden_notes\n\n@event forbidden_annotations\ntitle: Three Colours of Ink\nplace: library\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: seen(forbidden_section_1) == 0 and not flag(forbidden_abandoned)\nweight: 14\nart: book\ntags: mystery study\nthread: forbidden\nscene: A battered notebook open on a library desk, its margins crowded with notes in red, green and black ink, a silver key lying across the page.\ntext: You read Odile's notebook properly at last, at a desk within sight of the locked door. Black ink for the shelves, green for the prefects' rounds, red for warnings. Half the red is underlined twice.\n> left: Learn the prefects' rounds\n  hint: Easier to slip in unseen.\n  guile +small\n  flag forbidden_rounds\n  say: Green ink: the prefect on library duty walks the east stacks at nine and the west at ten, and stops for tea in between. You learn it like a timetable.\n> right: Learn the shelf marks\n  hint: Know what not to touch.\n  lore +small\n  flag forbidden_shelfmarks\n  say: Black ink and red: which shelves are safe, which books are bound in something that isn't leather, and, underlined twice, \"never with bare hands\".\n\n@event forbidden_section_1\ntitle: The Silver Key\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nplace: restricted_section\ntime: evening midnight\nwhen: not flag(forbidden_inside) and not flag(forbidden_abandoned)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 16\nart: key\ntags: mystery\nthread: forbidden\nscene: A heavy iron door at the end of a library aisle, a silver key turning in its lock, a candle held low.\ntext: After hours. The silver key fits the Restricted Section door perfectly. Beyond it, shelves of black-bound books breathe softly in the dark. Somewhere, a prefect's footsteps.\n> left: Turn back\n  mood -tiny\n  flag forbidden_abandoned\n  say: You lock the door again and hang the key on a nail in your wardrobe. Some nights you hear it rattling.\n> right: Slip inside\n  challenge deceive apprentice+ +1 if flag(forbidden_rounds):\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      flag forbidden_inside\n      find lead_restricted_shelves\n      say: You spend two hours reading by candle-stub. You leave with a head full of things no student should know, and the certainty that you'll come back.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      gain watched\n      rel bram -medium\n      say: Bram Hollis is waiting by the door when you come out. He is smiling. It's the worst smile you've ever seen.\n\n@event forbidden_bram_watch\ntitle: The Prefect at the Door\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nplace: restricted_section\ntime: evening midnight\nwhen: seen(forbidden_section_1) > 0 and not flag(forbidden_read) and not flag(forbidden_abandoned)\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 12\nactor: bram\nart: lock\ntags: mystery danger\nthread: forbidden\nscene: A broad prefect leaning against an iron library door with folded arms, a lantern at his feet, a student watching from behind a bookcase.\ntext: {Bram Hollis is leaning on the Restricted Section door, lantern at his feet, as if he has all night. Somebody has told him about the key, or he has guessed.|Bram is at the door again. He has brought a sandwich this time.|Bram's watch on the door has become a habit. He hums while he waits, badly.}\n> left: Wait him out in the stacks\n  hint: Cold and cramped, but unseen.\n  health -tiny\n  lore +small\n  say: {You crouch behind the history shelves for an hour, reading by the light of his lantern, until he gives up and goes to bed.|You wait him out, and he eats the whole sandwich first. Your knees will never forgive you.|You know his humming now. When it stops, he's asleep on his feet, and you slip past.}\n> right: Bluff your way past\n  hint: Guile against a prefect who wants to catch you.\n  challenge deceive vs bram.guile:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: {You tell him a professor is looking for him in the east tower. He goes. You are inside before his lantern reaches the stair.|\"Thorne wants you,\" you say, and Bram's face goes pale. Nobody checks whether Thorne wants them.|You tell him there's a fight in the courtyard. There isn't. He's gone before you finish the sentence.}\n    lose:\n      gain watched\n      rel bram -small\n      say: {\"Nice try,\" says Bram, and writes your name in his little book with great pleasure.|He laughs, actually laughs, and walks you back to your dormitory by the ear.|Bram has heard that one before, from you. He takes your name, and his time over it.}\n\n@event forbidden_section_2\ntitle: The Codex of Hollow Names\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nplace: restricted_section\ntime: evening midnight\nwhen: flag(forbidden_inside) and not flag(forbidden_read) and since(forbidden_section_1) >= 1 and not closed(hollowstar)\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 16\nart: book\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: forbidden\nscene: A book bound in something that is not leather, open on a reading stand, its letters slowly rearranging themselves.\ntext: You keep going back. One night, a book finds you. It falls from a high shelf and lands open at your feet: the Codex of Hollow Names. The first page is a list of names. The last one is still being written. It's yours.{flag(forbidden_warned):  Odile's warning comes back to you: don't let a book choose you.|}\n> left: Put it back\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You shelve it. When you look back, it has fallen again, open at the same page.\n> right: Read it\n  lore +medium\n  arcana +medium\n  alignment -tiny\n  find codex_names\n  flag forbidden_read\n  find lead_codex_open\n  chance even -1 if flag(forbidden_shelfmarks):\n    gain umbral_curse\n    say: You read until dawn. You learn about the thing under the lake. When you finally close the book, your fingertips are grey.\n  else:\n    say: You read until dawn and close the book with shaking hands. Some things you can't unread.\n\n@event forbidden_fourth_year\ntitle: Odile's Last Week\nplace: library\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nwhen: seen(forbidden_section_1) > 0 and seen(forbidden_section_3) == 0\nweight: 14\nart: book\ntags: mystery social\nthread: forbidden\nscene: A fourth-year with a packed satchel leaning on a library shelf, talking quietly to a younger student between the stacks.\ntext: Odile Varn finds you between the stacks with her satchel already packed. She leaves at the end of term. \"You used the key,\" she says. It isn't a question. \"I won't ask what you found. Unless you want to tell me.\"\n> left: Tell her what you've seen\n  hint: She has a warning for you.\n  mood +small\n  flag forbidden_warned\n  say: She listens to all of it, then puts a hand on your notebook. \"Some books want readers,\" she says. \"If one falls open at your feet, don't let it choose you.\" Then she hugs you, briefly, and is gone.\n> right: Keep it to yourself\n  hint: Your secret stays yours.\n  guile +small\n  say: She smiles, as if that were the right answer. \"Good,\" she says. \"That's how I kept it too.\" She leaves you her spare candle-stubs and a list of the quietest desks.\n\n@event forbidden_section_3\ntitle: Caught\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nplace: restricted_section\ntime: evening midnight\nwhen: flag(forbidden_read) and since(forbidden_section_2) >= 1 and not closed(hollowstar)\nweight: 16\nactor: isolde\nart: lock\ntags: mystery\nthread: forbidden\nscene: A tall silhouette in the doorway of the Restricted Section, holding a lantern that casts long shadows over a startled student.\ntext: A lantern blazes in the doorway. Not a prefect this time: the headmistress herself. She looks at the book in your hands, then at you, for a very long time. \"Most students who find that book,\" she says, \"don't put it down.\"\n> left: Apologise and accept punishment\n  alignment +small\n  meet isolde\n  study -medium\n  gain detention\n  rel isolde +small\n  unflag forbidden_key\n  say: She takes the key. \"Detention, every evening this month; your coursework can wait. And come to my tower on Thursdays. If you're going to learn dangerous things, you'll learn them properly.\"\n> right: Ask her what it means\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +medium\n  lore +medium\n  find codex_names\n  unflag forbidden_key\n  say: A long pause. \"It means something is waking, and it is looking for help. Make sure it doesn't find yours.\" She takes the key, leaves you the candle, and forgets, or chooses to forget, the detention.\n\n# ---------------- The Umbral Curse ----------------\n\n@thread curse\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=flag(moonpetal_sought)\nwhen: has(umbral_curse)\nclues: lead_moonpetal\nname: The cold that won't leave\npremise: A cold sits under your skin, and Sister Amaranth's tonics do nothing for it.\njournal:\n  seen(curse_cure_1) > 0: The cold under your skin isn't an illness, Sister Amaranth said. It's a hunger.\n  flag(moonpetal_sought): Old Maud told you the cure: moonpetal from the Whisperwood, picked with your own hand, after dark.\n  seen(curse_cure_2) > 0: In the Whisperwood after dark, a stag made of shadow stood between you and the flower.\n  flag(moonpetal_sought) and not has(umbral_curse): Maud's brew drove the cold out. It tasted of moonlight and burnt sugar.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(moonpetal_sought) and not has(umbral_curse)\nmembers:\n  start curse_cure_1\n\n@event curse_cure_1\ntitle: A Cold That Won't Leave\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nwhen: not flag(moonpetal_sought)\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 30\nart: skull\ntags: crisis mystery\nthread: curse\nscene: A student wrapped in three blankets beside a roaring fire, breath still fogging the air, fingertips grey.\ntext: {&The cold under your skin is getting worse.|The cold is back, worse than before.} Sister Amaranth's tonics do nothing. She looks troubled. \"This isn't an illness. It's a hunger.\" She lowers her voice. \"Old Maud might know something. Don't tell anyone I said so.\"\n> left: Endure it\n  mood -tiny\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You pile on blankets and keep going. The cold whispers sometimes. You pretend not to hear. Maud, you suppose, will still be there if it gets worse.\n> right: Go to Old Maud\n  meet maud\n  rel maud +small\n  flag moonpetal_sought\n  find lead_moonpetal\n  say: Maud takes one look at your fingertips and puts the kettle on. \"Moonpetal,\" she says. \"Grows in the Whisperwood. Only blooms after dark. You'll fetch it yourself — the cure has to come from the cursed.\"\n\n@event curse_cure_2\ntitle: Moonpetal\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nplace: whisperwood\nslot: evening\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 60\nart: flower\ntags: nature danger\nthread: curse\nscene: A dark forest at nightfall, a single silver flower glowing in a clearing, eyes watching from between the trees.\ntext: The Whisperwood after dark. Things move between the trees. In a clearing, a single silver flower is opening, and between you and it stands a stag made of shadow.\n> left: Sneak around it\n  challenge deceive journeyman:\n    win:\n      lose umbral_curse\n      rel maud +medium\n      say: You creep through the bracken and pluck the moonpetal. Maud's brew tastes like moonlight and burnt sugar. The cold is gone.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      say: The stag sees you. You run. No moonpetal tonight; you'll have to come back.\n> right: Face the stag\n  challenge combat adept-:\n    win:\n      lose umbral_curse\n      vigor +small\n      rel maud +medium\n      say: You stand your ground. The stag lowers its head — and steps aside. Maud's brew drives the cold out of your bones.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      say: The stag charges. You wake up in the bracken at dawn. Maud bandages you. \"Another night,\" she says.\n\n# ---------------- Grisk the Moneylender ----------------\n\n@thread debt\nname: Mister Grisk's ledger\npremise: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat: friend to the temporarily embarrassed.\njournal:\n  chosen(grisk_offer, right) or chosen(grisk_board_debt, left): Grisk wrote your name in his ledger, with a flourish that had teeth in it.\n  chosen(grisk_board_debt, right): You begged the bursar for time instead, and got a month's grace and a long look over spectacles.\n  seen(grisk_reminder) > 0: A note on your door: Mister Grisk charges interest on his patience.\n  seen(grisk_collectors) > 0: Two trolls in waistcoats came to say that Mister Grisk was disappointed.\n  flag(grisk_favour): You cut a page from the Founders' Ledger for Grisk's client. It had a black star on it.\n  flag(grisk_contact): Grisk told you how his parcels travel: the jetty at midnight, oilcloth, a cobbler's back door.\n  flag(grisk_gone): You told Grisk you were done with him. You never saw him again.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(grisk_gone) or (flag(grisk_done) and not has(in_debt) and not flag(grisk_contact))\n\n@event grisk_offer\ntitle: A Helpful Goblin\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: gold < 15 and turn >= 3 and not has(in_debt) and not flag(grisk_done)\nweight: 30\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nthread: debt\nscene: A small goblin in a velvet waistcoat and pince-nez, sitting on a crate in an alley, a ledger in one hand and a quill behind his ear.\ntext: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat falls into step beside you. \"Grisk,\" he says, \"friend to the temporarily embarrassed. Sixty crowns, today. Twelve a month for six months. Very reasonable.\"\n> left: No thank you\n  meet grisk\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"My door is always open,\" Grisk says. You suspect it is actually always locked.\n> right: Take the loan\n  meet grisk\n  gold +huge\n  gain in_debt\n  debt_paid = 0\n  debt_missed = 0\n  flag grisk_done\n  say: He counts out sixty crowns, writes your name in the ledger and smiles with a great many teeth.\n\n@event grisk_board_debt\ntitle: Settled, With Interest\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 70\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nthread: debt\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat waiting outside the bursar's office, waving a receipt like a small flag.\ntext: The bursar's office has stopped sending reminders. Instead, Grisk is waiting outside it with a receipt. \"Two months' board, paid in full,\" he beams. \"By me. A loan, naturally, with a little extra for your pocket. Twelve a month for six months. You can sign here.\"\n> left: Sign his ledger\n  meet grisk\n  gold +large\n  gold +medium\n  gain in_debt\n  debt_paid = 0\n  debt_missed = 0\n  flag grisk_done\n  say: You sign. He has already paid the board out of it; he counts the rest into your palm and writes your name in the ledger with a flourish that has teeth in it.\n> right: Beg the bursar for time\n  meet grisk\n  flag grisk_done\n  mood -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You spend an afternoon on a hard bench outside the bursar's office and leave with a month's grace and the feeling of having been looked at over spectacles for a very long time.\n\n@event grisk_favour\ntitle: Grisk's Proposition\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: has(in_debt)\nweight: 14\nart: chain\ntags: money mystery\nthread: debt\nscene: The goblin moneylender leaning close in a library alcove, pushing a slip of paper across a table.\ntext: Grisk finds you in the library. \"I could cancel your debt,\" he murmurs. \"A small favour. There's a book in the archive — the Founders' Ledger. A client wants a page. Just one page.\"\n> left: Refuse\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: \"Pity.\" He makes a note in his ledger. It looks expensive.\n> right: Do the favour\n  alignment -small\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      lose in_debt\n      flag grisk_favour\n      say: You razor a page from the oldest ledger in the archive. Grisk tears up your debt. You notice the page had a black star on it.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      study -medium\n      say: The archivist catches you with a razor in your hand. Detention. Grisk is nowhere to be found, and the debt stands.\n\n@event grisk_smuggling\ntime: afternoon evening\ntitle: One More Job\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(grisk_favour) and not has(in_debt)\nweight: 10\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nthread: debt\nscene: A crate of clinking bottles hidden under a tarp on a dark boathouse dock, a small goblin silhouetted against the lake.\ntext: Grisk again. \"You were so good last time. I have parcels that need carrying: collected at the boathouse at midnight, delivered to the village by day. Thirty crowns a parcel. Steady work, for steady hands.\"\n> left: You're done with him\n  unflag grisk_favour\n  flag grisk_gone\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Everyone's done with me eventually,\" Grisk says, without rancour. You never see him again.\n> right: Hear him out\n  flag grisk_contact\n  find lead_grisk_jetty\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He explains the arrangement: the jetty, midnight, oilcloth, a cobbler's back door. \"Any month you like,\" he says. \"I'm very flexible.\" You walk away with the arrangement in your head, and it won't leave.\n\n# ---------------- Ember the Fox-Spirit ----------------\n\n@thread familiar\nname: Ember\npremise: A fox with a smouldering tail, caught in a snare at the forest's edge.\njournal:\n  flag(fox_met): You freed a fox from a snare at the forest's edge. She bit you, and let you bandage her paw.\n  seen(fox_returns) > 0: Two months later she was on your windowsill, with a dusty ruby at her feet.\n  has(familiar_fox): She has decided she's yours. Her name is Ember.\nstatus:\n  resolved: has(familiar_fox)\n\n@event fox_injured\ntitle: Smoke in the Woods\nclass: story\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 4 and not flag(fox_met)\nweight: 6 + flag(familiar_affinity) * 14\nart: fox\ntags: nature\nthread: familiar\nscene: A small red fox lying in the snow at the forest edge, its tail smouldering like a dying campfire, one paw caught in a rusted snare.\ntext: At the edge of the forest, a thin plume of smoke. A small fox is caught in a rusted snare, its tail smouldering like an ember. It snarls at you, then whimpers.\n> left: Leave it — it's wild\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You walk away. That night you dream of smoke.\n> right: Free it and bandage the paw\n  flag fox_met\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  queue fox_returns in 2\n  say: It bites you, of course. But it lets you wrap the paw. Then it limps into the trees, glancing back once with eyes like embers.\n\n@event fox_returns\ntitle: A Visitor at the Window\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: fox\ntags: nature\nthread: familiar\nscene: A fox with a softly glowing tail sitting on a stone windowsill at dawn, a ruby glinting at its feet.\ntext: Two months later, a fox with a glowing tail is sitting on your windowsill. Its paw has healed. At its feet: a small, dusty ruby.\n> left: Shoo it away\n  gold +medium\n  say: It leaves the ruby and vanishes. You sell the ruby. Somehow you feel you've failed a test.\n> right: Open the window\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  queue fox_bond in 1\n  say: It hops in, inspects your room thoroughly and falls asleep on your pillow. Your pillow is now slightly scorched. You don't mind.\n\n@event fox_bond\ntitle: Ember\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: fox\ntags: nature magic\nthread: familiar\nscene: A student and a small fire-tailed fox nose to nose, a soft golden light connecting them.\ntext: The fox has decided. {term: It follows you to class|It follows you through the half-empty corridors}, sleeps on your feet and growls at Bram. Students aren't allowed unregistered familiars. Registering one costs fifteen crowns.\n> left: Hide her\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      gain familiar_fox\n      say: You name her Ember. Nobody notices a fox that doesn't want to be noticed.\n    lose:\n      gain familiar_fox\n      gold -medium\n      say: A professor spots her at once. You pay what you can of the fee — and Ember is yours, officially.\n> right: Register her properly\n  gold -medium\n  gain familiar_fox\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  say: The registrar stamps a form. \"Name?\" \"Ember,\" you say. The fox looks smug.\n\n@event fox_helps\ntitle: Ember Finds Something\nclass: story\ntone: light\nwhen: has(familiar_fox)\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 8\nart: fox\ntags: nature\nthread: familiar\nscene: A fox nosing through dry leaves under a hedge and emerging with a purse in its jaws.\ntext: {~Ember drags you, by the sleeve, through three corridors and a hedge. She's found something. She's very proud of it.|Ember sits on your book until you look at her. She has found something, and she has buried it, and now she wants you to dig.|Ember comes back from the lake with her tail steaming and something in her jaws. She drops it at your feet like a gift.}\n> left: Let her keep it\n  mood +small\n  say: {~It's a sock. Ember is thrilled. You are thrilled for her.|It's a glove that smells of the stables. Ember sleeps on it for a week.|It's a pine cone, slightly charred. She guards it from Bram personally.}\n> right: See what it is\n  chance even:\n    gold +medium\n    say: A lost purse. You return it to its owner, who insists you keep the reward.\n  else:\n    lore +small\n    say: A lost page from a professor's lecture notes. Enlightening.\n\n# ---------------- The Haunted Dormitory ----------------\n\n@thread ghost\nname: Prudence\npremise: Somebody has been rearranging your socks.\njournal:\n  flag(ghost_met): Your socks spelled out HELP. You stayed up, and at three in the morning the drawer opened by itself.\n  seen(ghost_reveal) > 0: The ghost is Prudence Ashworth: very polite, and two hundred years late with a letter.\n  chosen(ghost_reveal, left): You spoke the banishing rite. She faded with a small, hurt look.\n  chosen(ghost_reveal, right): You promised to deliver her letter to the bakery. She clapped her translucent hands.\nstatus:\n  resolved: seen(ghost_reveal) > 0\n\n@event ghost_socks\ntime: morning\ntitle: Rearranged Socks\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 3 and not flag(ghost_met)\nweight: 8\nart: ghost\ntags: mystery\nthread: ghost\nscene: A dormitory drawer open in candlelight, socks arranged in a careful spiral, a faint shimmer in the air above them.\ntext: Every morning, your socks have been rearranged. By colour. Then alphabetically by colour. Last night they spelled out \"HELP\".\n> left: Ignore it\n  gain haunted\n  say: You buy new socks. The next morning they're arranged by mood.\n> right: Stay up and watch\n  flag ghost_met\n  gain haunted\n  queue ghost_reveal now\n  say: At three in the morning, the drawer slides open by itself.\n\n@event ghost_reveal\ntitle: Prudence\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: ghost\ntags: mystery\nthread: ghost\nscene: A translucent girl in old-fashioned school robes, hovering by a window, holding a faded letter.\ntext: A girl in old-fashioned robes, translucent and very polite. \"Prudence Ashworth, class of 1793. Terribly sorry about the socks. I never finished my letter. Could you deliver it? The village. The bakery. Please.\"\n> left: Exorcise her\n  lose haunted\n  alignment -tiny\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You speak the banishing rite. She fades with a small, hurt look. Your socks stay put after that. You feel a bit rotten.\n> right: Promise to deliver it\n  queue ghost_letter in 1\n  say: She claps her translucent hands. \"Oh, thank you! It's only taken two hundred years.\"\n\n@event ghost_letter\ntitle: A Letter Two Hundred Years Late\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: letter\ntags: mystery social\nscene: A cosy village bakery, an old baker reading a yellowed letter with tears on her cheeks, a ghostly figure smiling in the doorway.\ntext: The bakery has been in the same family for two hundred years. The old baker reads Prudence's letter at the counter. \"My great-great-great-grandmother waited for this,\" she says. \"She never stopped.\"\n> left: Stay and listen to the story\n  lose haunted\n  mood +large\n  lore +small\n  say: Prudence was in love with the baker's daughter. When you return, the ghost is waiting, glowing like a sunrise. Then she's gone, at peace.\n> right: Leave quietly\n  lose haunted\n  mood +medium\n  renown +small\n  say: The baker presses a free loaf into your hands. That night, your socks are arranged into a heart. Then never again.\n"},{"path":"content/events/classes.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Elective events — only drawn while the matching class state\n#  is active (see states/states.grim, group: elective).\n#  Convention: weight 16 so your class shows up most months.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Pyromancy ----------------\n\n@pool evocation_class_weight16\ntrack: evocation\nclass: routine\nweight: 16\ntags: class\n\n@event pyro_candle\ntitle: Light the Candle\npool: evocation_class_weight16\ntone: light\nart: candle\ntags: magic\nscene: A dark classroom, a single candle on a stool at the far end, Professor Thorne's silhouette with folded arms.\ntext: Professor Thorne places a candle at the far end of the hall. \"Light it. From there.\" The class holds its breath. Last week a boy lit his own sleeve.\n> left: A careful spark\n  challenge spell standard steady:\n    win:\n      arcana +tiny\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: The wick catches with a polite little flame. Thorne grunts. From him, that is applause.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Nothing. Then a wisp of smoke from your fingertips. A few people giggle.\n> right: Show them real fire\n  challenge spell standard+2:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      renown +small\n      rel thorne +tiny\n      say: The candle erupts in a pillar of white flame and then settles, obedient. The room goes quiet.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      study evocation -small\n      rel thorne -tiny\n      say: The candle lights. So do the curtains. And, briefly, your eyebrows.\n\n@event pyro_ember_heart\ntitle: The Ember Within\npool: evocation_class_weight16\ntone: light\nart: flame\ntags: magic\nscene: Students sitting cross-legged in a stone circle, each with a faint glow behind their ribs.\ntext: Today's exercise: hold a spark inside your chest and let it grow. \"Fire is fed by will,\" says Thorne. \"And by you, if you are careless.\" It burns. It is supposed to burn.\n> left: Stop when it hurts\n  health -tiny\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You let the spark gutter out. Thorne says nothing. The silence is a grade.\n> right: Hold it longer than anyone\n  arcana +medium\n  health -medium\n  say: Your vision goes white at the edges. When you open your eyes, your breath is steaming and the stones around you are warm.\n\n@event pyro_lyra_spar\ntitle: A Challenge in Class\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(lyra)\ntrack: evocation\nactor: lyra\nweight: 14\nart: flame\ntags: class rival\nscene: Two students facing each other across a scorched practice ring; one is a tall girl with crimson hair, flames curling around her wrists.\ntext: \"Professor, I'd like a demonstration partner.\" Lyra Vaneshade points her wand at you without looking. \"That one.\"\n> left: Politely decline\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"As expected,\" Lyra says, and turns away. It stings worse than a burn would.\n> right: Step into the ring\n  challenge combat vs lyra.arcana:\n    win:\n      rel lyra +medium\n      renown +small\n      if bond(lyra) == \"none\":\n        bond lyra rival\n      say: Your fire meets hers and holds. When Thorne calls time, Lyra is breathing hard. \"Again. Next week,\" she says.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: She wins in under a minute — but she looks at you afterwards like she's filing you away for later.\n\n# ---------------- Tidecalling ----------------\n\n@event tide_lake_lesson\ntitle: The Lake Answers\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 16\nart: wave\ntags: class magic nature\nscene: Students standing waist-deep in a silver lake at dawn, ripples spiralling out from their outstretched hands.\ntext: Waist-deep in the freezing lake at dawn, Professor Marisol tells you to ask the water to rise. \"Ask,\" she says. \"Never demand. The lake remembers rudeness.\"\n> left: Command it anyway\n  challenge spell apprentice+ unlikely:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      say: The water rises in a shivering column. Marisol raises an eyebrow. \"Effective. Rude.\"\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: The lake declines, and expresses its displeasure by knocking you flat.\n> right: Listen first\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You stand still until the cold stops hurting. The lake murmurs — and for a moment you could swear it said your name.\n  chance unlikely:\n    flag lake_knows\n\n@event tide_storm_drill\ntitle: Storm Drill\npool: evocation_class_weight16\ntone: light\nart: rain\ntags: nature\nscene: A rocky shore under black clouds, a few figures in soaked robes holding back a curtain of rain with raised hands.\ntext: A storm rolls over the lake and Marisol is delighted. \"Perfect conditions! Hold back the rain for as long as you can.\" Most of the class has already fled.\n> left: Shelter with the sensible ones\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You watch from the boathouse, dry and slightly ashamed.\n> right: Stay out in it\n  vigor +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  chance unlikely -1 if health >= 60:\n    gain sick\n    say: You hold the rain back for almost a minute. You also catch Lake Fever.\n  else:\n    say: For a full minute, the rain parts around you. Marisol whoops like a child.\n\n@event tide_drowned_bell\ntitle: The Drowned Bell\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(bell_fragment) and seen(hs_lake_breathes) and not closed(hollowstar)\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 12\nart: bell\ntags: class mystery hollowstar\nscene: Murky green water, a shaft of light catching the bronze curve of an enormous bell half-buried in silt.\ntext: Ever since the lake held its breath in Mistveil, you listen for it. During a breathing-charm lesson you hear it: a bell, deep under the lake, tolling slow and soft. Nobody else seems to notice.\n> left: Mention it to Professor Marisol\n  lore +tiny\n  say: Her face goes very still. \"There is no bell,\" she says. \"There hasn't been a bell for three hundred years.\"\n  flag heard_bell\n  find lead_lake_breathed\n> right: Dive down to find it\n  challenge feat adept- wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      flag bell_fragment\n      find lead_lake_breathed\n      say: Deep in the silt you find a broken piece of bronze, warm to the touch, engraved with a black star.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The water closes over you, dark and cold. You surface gasping, empty-handed, with the bell still ringing in your teeth.\n\n# ---------------- Illusion ----------------\n\n@event illusion_mirror_self\ntitle: A Second You\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: illusion\nweight: 16\nart: mirror\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student facing a shimmering duplicate of themselves; the duplicate's smile is a little too wide.\ntext: Assignment: conjure a convincing illusion of yourself. Professor Vey circles the room. \"Most students make themselves taller. Interesting, what people choose.\"\n> left: Make it flattering\n  charm +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Your double is radiant, confident, slightly better-looking. Vey writes something down and smiles thinly.\n> right: Make it accurate\n  guile +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: Your double slouches the way you slouch and chews its lip the way you do. It is deeply unsettling. Vey awards top marks.\n  study illusion +tiny\n\n@event illusion_professor_prank\ntitle: The Professor's Face\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: illusion\nweight: 14\nart: mask\ntags: class social\nscene: A student wearing a shimmering glamour of a stern old professor, classmates stifling laughter behind a door.\ntext: Your classmates dare you: wear a glamour of Professor Thorne and cancel tomorrow's Arcana lecture. \"Nobody will know,\" says someone who will definitely tell.\n> left: Absolutely not\n  study illusion +tiny\n  say: Wise. Someone else tries it the next day and spends a week scrubbing gargoyles.\n> right: Do it\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive using charm journeyman+ wild:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: \"Class is cancelled,\" you announce in Thorne's gravel voice. The cheering is audible from three towers away.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      study illusion -small\n      rel thorne -small\n      say: Halfway through, the glamour flickers. The real Thorne is standing in the doorway. He is not laughing.\n\n@event illusion_dragon\ntitle: The Courtyard Dragon\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: arcana >= 12\ntrack: illusion\nweight: 12\nart: dragon\ntags: class magic\nscene: A vast translucent dragon coiled around the academy's central fountain, students frozen mid-step beneath it.\ntext: For your term project you must create an illusion that fools a crowd. You could make something small and flawless. Or you could put a dragon in the courtyard at lunchtime.\n> left: Small and flawless\n  study illusion +small\n  say: A single butterfly that nobody can tell is fake. Vey taps it with her wand and it bursts into light. \"Perfect.\"\n> right: Dragon. Obviously.\n  challenge spell using charm standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      study illusion +tiny\n      say: The dragon uncurls around the fountain and yawns. Forty students scream. Vey laughs until she cries.\n    lose:\n      renown +small\n      study illusion -small\n      rel isolde -tiny\n      say: The dragon is magnificent for four seconds and then sneezes into a cloud of glitter. The headmistress was eating lunch below it.\n\n# ---------------- Practical Alchemy ----------------\n\n@event alch_first_brew\ntitle: Draught of Mild Courage\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 16\nart: cauldron\ntags: class\nscene: Rows of bubbling cauldrons in a low stone room, each giving off a different coloured steam.\ntext: The recipe is on the board: Draught of Mild Courage. Seven ingredients, three stirs clockwise, one counter-clockwise, and never — never — add the newt eye early.\n> left: Follow the recipe exactly\n  alchemy +tiny\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  say: A clear, honey-coloured brew. The professor sniffs it and nods. Safe, solid, unremarkable.\n> right: Improvise a little\n  challenge brew standard wild:\n    win:\n      alchemy +medium\n      gold +small\n      say: Your variant tastes like cinnamon and makes people brave enough to ask for things. A fourth-year buys the whole flask.\n    lose:\n      gain frog_voice\n      say: You taste-test. Mistake. For the next month, everything you say comes out as a deep, wet croak.\n\n@event alch_unstable_cauldron\ntitle: Unstable Cauldron\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 14\nart: potion\ntags: class danger\nscene: A cauldron shuddering on its iron legs, violet bubbles swelling over the rim, a student frozen beside it.\ntext: The cauldron next to yours starts to shudder. Its owner, a terrified first-year, has added the newt eye early. It is going to blow.\n> left: Dive for cover\n  say: The explosion paints the ceiling violet. Nobody is hurt. Everybody is purple.\n> right: Try to neutralise it\n  challenge brew standard+1:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      alchemy +small\n      say: A pinch of salt, a drop of moonwater, a quick counter-stir. The bubbling dies. The first-year bursts into grateful tears.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The cauldron goes off in your face. You spend a week with violet eyebrows and a ringing in your ears.\n\n@pool alchemy_class_weight12\ntrack: alchemy\nclass: routine\nweight: 12\ntags: class\n\n@event alch_corvin_market\ntitle: A Side Business\npool: alchemy_class_weight12\ntone: light\nwhen: met(corvin)\nactor: corvin\nart: bottle\ntags: money\nscene: A young man leaning on a cauldron table, holding up a vial against the light with a merchant's grin.\ntext: Corvin turns your leftover potions over in his hands. \"These would sell for real crowns in the village. I know a buyer. I take a third. Nobody needs to know they came from a classroom.\"\n> left: Decline\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  say: \"Your loss, saint.\" He tosses the vial back. You fumble it.\n> right: Shake on it\n  alignment -tiny\n  gold +large\n  rel corvin +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain detention\n    study alchemy -small\n    say: It goes well until a vial turns up in a professor's tea. You and Corvin share a detention and an alibi.\n  else:\n    say: Thirty crowns, no questions asked. Corvin winks. \"Pleasure doing crime with you.\"\n\n# ---------------- Divination ----------------\n\n@event div_tea_leaves\ntitle: Reading the Leaves\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 16\nart: cup\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A delicate teacup tipped on its saucer, the dregs forming a shape like a skull — or possibly a mushroom.\ntext: You read your partner's tea leaves. The shape at the bottom of the cup is unmistakably a skull. Your partner is looking at you hopefully.\n> left: Tell the truth\n  lore +small\n  chance even:\n    say: \"A skull?\" They laugh. \"Professor says a skull means change.\" They're relieved. You aren't sure you should be.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: They go pale and leave early. The next day they trip down the stairs. Nothing serious. Still.\n> right: Lie kindly\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: \"A mushroom,\" you say. \"Means good fortune.\" They beam. The professor, passing behind you, says nothing at all.\n> up[omen]: Foretell what it really means\n  lore +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You say, out loud and quite certain, that the skull means your partner will lose a bet before Friday. They lose a bet on Thursday, to you, and are thrilled the skull wasn't worse.\n\n@event div_next_tuesday\ntitle: The Mirror of Next Tuesday\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 14\nart: mirror\ntags: class mystery\nscene: An ornate standing mirror whose reflection shows the room on a different day — the light at a different angle, a different person sitting in your chair.\ntext: The Mirror of Next Tuesday shows exactly what it promises. Students take turns looking. Some come away smiling. One comes away and goes straight to the infirmary.\n> left: Take a look\n  chance even:\n    study astromancy +small\n    alignment -tiny\n    say: Next Tuesday you are sitting an Astromancy quiz. You memorise the questions. Is that cheating? It's at least ambiguous.\n  else:\n    mood -medium\n    say: Next Tuesday you are crying in the rain outside the observatory. You don't know why. You spend the week dreading it.\n> right: Decline\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"I'd rather be surprised.\" The professor smiles as if you have passed a different test entirely.\n\n@event div_seraphine_vision\ntitle: She Grabs Your Hand\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nactor: seraphine\nweight: 14\nart: eye\ntags: class mystery hollowstar\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: A dreamy-eyed girl gripping a classmate's wrist across a table of crystal balls, her pupils wide and silver.\ntext: In the middle of crystal-gazing, Seraphine Hale seizes your wrist. Her eyes have gone silver. \"The door under the lake,\" she whispers. \"Don't let him open it. Or do. I can't see which.\"\n> left: Pull away\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She blinks and lets go. \"Sorry. That happens.\" She goes back to her crystal as if nothing occurred.\n> right: Ask who she means\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  flag heard_prophecy\n  say: \"Someone cold,\" she says. \"Someone kind.\" Then she shakes her head, smiles vaguely, and asks if you'd like a biscuit.\n\n# ---------------- Artifice ----------------\n\n@event art_clockwork_beetle\ntitle: The Clockwork Beetle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: artifice\nweight: 16\nart: gear\ntags: class\nscene: A brass beetle the size of a fist on a workbench, tiny gears visible through its translucent wing-cases.\ntext: Your project: a brass beetle that walks. The gears are in. The spring is wound. You could make it efficient, or you could give it a little personality.\n> left: Make it efficient\n  study artifice +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: It walks in a perfect straight line until it hits a wall. Full marks.\n> right: Give it personality\n  mood +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    renown +small\n    say: It escapes during the presentation and lives in the walls for a month, occasionally stealing biscuits. It becomes a minor legend.\n  else:\n    say: It refuses to walk unless you compliment it. The professor is torn between marking you down and asking how you did it.\n> up[animate]: Animate it instead of winding it\n  study artifice +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You skip the spring and give the beetle a want instead: to find the warmest spot on the bench. It does, and sits there, content, and nothing in the class is wound tighter or happier.\n\n@event art_mir_collab\ntitle: A Joint Project\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: artifice\nactor: mir\nweight: 14\nart: gear\ntags: class social\nscene: A cluttered workbench covered in tools, sketches and one smoking teapot; a grinning student with goggles pushed up into wild hair.\ntext: Mir Ten drops onto the bench beside you with a sketch of something that is either a self-stirring cauldron or a small war machine. \"Partners? Your hands, my brain. Or the other way round. Both, actually.\"\n> left: Work alone\n  meet mir\n  study artifice +tiny\n  say: \"Fair,\" says Mir, with no hard feelings, and is immediately on fire. Not badly.\n> right: Partner with Mir\n  meet mir\n  challenge brew standard wild:\n    win:\n      study artifice +small\n      rel mir +medium\n      say: The cauldron stirs itself, hums a tune and only threatens the professor once. Mir hugs you with oily hands.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      rel mir +small\n      say: The prototype explodes. Mir laughs so hard they cry. \"Again! We were so close!\" You find you're laughing too.\n> up[contraption]: Build it on the spot\n  meet mir\n  study artifice +tiny\n  rel mir +small\n  say: You don't sketch it. You ask the bits what they want to be, and they become a cauldron that stirs and occasionally salutes. Mir makes a noise you've never heard a person make. \"Partners,\" Mir says. \"Forever. Probably.\"\n\n@event art_enchanted_quill\ntitle: The Self-Writing Quill\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: artifice\nweight: 14\nart: feather\ntags: class\nscene: A quill writing busily on its own over a sheaf of paper while its owner sleeps on folded arms.\ntext: You've enchanted a quill to take dictation. It works beautifully. A thought occurs: what if it wrote your essays, too?\n> left: Just use it for notes\n  lore +small\n  say: Your notes are flawless. Even the professor's jokes are transcribed, with a small marginal comment: \"(not funny).\"\n> right: Let it write your essays\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance even:\n    study artifice +small\n    mood +small\n    say: Your essays arrive on time, in impeccable penmanship. A little too impeccable. Nobody notices. Yet.\n  else:\n    study artifice -medium\n    gain detention\n    say: The quill develops opinions and writes a scathing review of the professor. It signs your name.\n\n# ---------------- Beastcraft ----------------\n\n@event beast_griffon_chick\ntitle: Hatching Day\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: beastcraft\nweight: 16\nart: feather\ntags: class nature\nscene: A straw nest under a heat lamp, a cracked golden egg, and a damp fluffy griffon chick blinking at the world.\ntext: The griffon egg is cracking. Professor Brask whispers that the first face a chick sees, it trusts forever — or attacks forever. Hard to predict.\n> left: Hang back\n  lore +tiny\n  say: The chick sees Brask first and bites her. She seems delighted.\n> right: Offer your hand\n  challenge feat apprentice+ luck:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: The chick peeps, nuzzles your palm and falls asleep. You are, apparently, its mother now.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: It bites. Hard. Griffon beaks are sharper than they look, and they look extremely sharp.\n\n@pool beastcraft_class_weight12\ntrack: beastcraft\nclass: routine\nweight: 12\ntags: class\n\n@event beast_manticore\ntitle: The Manticore Is Loose\npool: beastcraft_class_weight12\ntone: tense\nart: paw\ntags: danger\nscene: A shadowy lion-shape with a scorpion's tail prowling between the greenhouse benches, students pressed against the glass.\ntext: Someone left the manticore pen open. It's in the greenhouse, tail twitching, between you and the only exit.\n> left: Back away slowly\n  say: You edge out. Professor Brask arrives with a bucket of meat and a lullaby and the crisis is over in minutes.\n> right: Try to calm it\n  challenge soothe adept wild:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: You hum the lullaby Brask taught you. The manticore yawns, sits, and lets you scratch behind its ear. The whole class saw.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      gain injured\n      say: The manticore does not like your humming. The tail is quicker than you are.\n\n@event beast_familiar_call\ntitle: Calling a Familiar\npool: beastcraft_class_weight12\ntone: light\nwhen: not has(familiar_fox)\nart: fox\ntags: magic\nscene: A circle of salt and candles in a forest clearing at dusk, shapes of small animals watching from the edge of the trees.\ntext: Brask teaches the summoning circle. \"It doesn't summon,\" she says. \"It invites. Most of the time, nothing comes. That's fine. Familiars choose you.\"\n> left: Just watch the others\n  lore +tiny\n  say: A first-year is chosen by a very large toad. The toad looks smug about it.\n> right: Sit in the circle\n  mood +small\n  flag familiar_affinity\n  say: Nothing comes. But for a long moment, something at the edge of the trees watches you with eyes like embers. Then it is gone.\n\n# ---------------- Astromancy ----------------\n\n@event astro_comet\ntitle: A New Comet\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 16\nart: star\ntags: class\nscene: An observatory dome open to the night, a long-tailed comet smeared across the sky, students crowded at the telescopes.\ntext: A comet appears that isn't in any of the charts. The observatory is in uproar. Everyone wants to be the one to name it.\n> left: Sketch it carefully\n  lore +small\n  say: Your drawing is lovely. Someone else's name goes on the comet. You keep the drawing.\n> right: Calculate its path\n  challenge research standard+1:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      lore +small\n      say: Your math is right. The comet is named after you, or at least after a slight misspelling of you.\n    lose:\n      study astromancy -tiny\n      say: Your calculation suggests the comet will hit the academy on Thursday. It does not. You are teased about it for weeks.\n\n@event astro_kael_roof\ntitle: Up All Night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: met(kael)\ntrack: astromancy\nactor: kael\nweight: 14\nart: moon\ntags: class mystery hollowstar\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: A young man in a fur coat sitting alone on the observatory roof, frost on the tiles around him, staring at one spot in the stars.\ntext: On your way to the morning lecture you find Kael alone on the observatory roof. He has been there all night. Frost has spread across the tiles around him. He is staring at a patch of sky where there are no stars at all.\n> left: Leave him be\n  say: You go down to class. By evening there's a perfect circle of frost on the roof where he sat, and no Kael.\n> right: Sit beside him\n  rel kael +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  flag saw_hollow_star\n  say: \"There,\" he says, pointing at the dark. \"Can you see it? A star that eats light.\" You look for a long time. You think you can.\n\n@event astro_sleepless\ntitle: Sleepless Weeks\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 14\nart: bed\ntags: class\nscene: A student asleep on an open star atlas at a library desk, a guttering candle beside them, dawn light in the window.\ntext: Midnight lectures, dawn homework, morning classes. You have started seeing constellations in your porridge.\n> left: Skip a few lectures\n  study track -tiny\n  gain slacking\n  health +small\n  say: You sleep for fourteen hours and wake up feeling like a person.\n> right: Power through\n  health -small\n  lore +small\n  say: You learn the names of forty stars and forget the names of two friends. Temporarily.\n\n# ---------------- Healing Arts ----------------\n\n@event heal_first_patient\ntitle: Your First Patient\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: alchemy\nweight: 16\nart: cross\ntags: class\nscene: A pale first-year sitting on an infirmary cot holding a wrist at a strange angle, a nervous student healer kneeling beside them.\ntext: A first-year stumbles into the practice ward with a wrist that bends the wrong way. Sister Amaranth is busy. The first-year looks at you with enormous, trusting eyes.\n> left: Fetch Sister Amaranth\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  say: Amaranth fixes it in seconds and nods at you. \"Knowing your limits is the first lesson.\"\n> right: Heal it yourself\n  challenge brew standard+1:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      renown +small\n      say: The bone slides back with a soft click and a glow of warm light. The first-year tells everyone you're a genius.\n    lose:\n      study alchemy -small\n      say: The wrist is now straight. The fingers, however, are pointing backwards. Amaranth sighs and fixes both.\n\n@event heal_fever_ward\ntitle: Fever in the Village\npool: alchemy_class_weight12\ntone: light\nart: cross\ntags: nature\nscene: A crowded village hall lined with cots, lanterns hanging from the rafters, student healers moving between patients.\ntext: Lake Fever is spreading through the village below the academy. Sister Amaranth asks for student volunteers. It's hard, unpaid, and you might catch it.\n> left: Decline\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You watch the volunteers board the barge in the morning. You do your homework. It feels like the wrong homework.\n> right: Volunteer\n  renown +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  chance unlikely -1 if health >= 60:\n    gain sick\n    say: You save lives, and catch the fever yourself. The village sends a basket of honey cakes to your sickbed.\n  else:\n    say: A week of long nights and cool cloths. When you leave, the baker's daughter gives you her best loaf.\n\n@event heal_tobias_sting\ntitle: Stung\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: met(tobias) and rel(tobias) >= 0\ntrack: alchemy\nactor: tobias\nweight: 12\nart: flower\ntags: class social\nscene: A broad-shouldered boy sitting on a greenhouse bench, grimacing, holding up a hand swollen to twice its size.\ntext: Tobias wanders into the practice ward holding up a hand swollen like a pumpkin. \"Mandrake,\" he says. \"She didn't like my singing.\" He looks hopefully at you.\n> left: Send him to the infirmary\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  say: \"Right. Proper healer. Good idea.\" He goes, a little disappointed.\n> right: Treat him yourself\n  alchemy +tiny\n  rel tobias +medium\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: Your hands are steady. The swelling goes down. Tobias stares at his hand, then at you. \"You're really good at this,\" he says softly.\n\n# ---------------- Umbral Theory ----------------\n\n@event umbral_whisper\ntitle: The Shadow Speaks\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: umbral\nweight: 16\nart: skull\ntags: class mystery danger\nscene: A student at a desk in a dim lecture hall, their shadow on the wall behind them turned the wrong way, leaning close to whisper.\ntext: In the middle of a lecture on the Nature of Absence, your shadow leans away from the lamp, toward your ear, and says your name.\n> left: Banish it\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You speak the warding phrase. Your shadow snaps back into place, sulking. It does not speak again. You think.\n> right: Answer it\n  lore +medium\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain umbral_curse\n    say: It tells you something about death you can't unlearn. The cold starts the next morning.\n  else:\n    say: It tells you where a lost book is shelved in the library. It is right. You decide not to wonder how it knew.\n\n@event umbral_bone_mouse\ntitle: The Bone Mouse\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: umbral\nweight: 14\nart: skull\ntags: class magic\nscene: A tiny mouse skeleton on a silver tray, one bony paw twitching, students leaning away from it.\ntext: Practical exercise: animate the skeleton of a field mouse. \"Purely educational,\" the professor says, with a smile nobody trusts.\n> left: Refuse on principle\n  study umbral -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You write a short essay on why instead. The professor reads it with surprising attention.\n> right: Do the exercise\n  challenge spell standard:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      study umbral +tiny\n      say: The little skeleton stands up, washes its non-existent whiskers, and lies down again. Perfect control.\n    lose:\n      gain haunted\n      say: It gets up. It does not lie down again. It follows you back to your dormitory and sits on your pillow every night.\n\n@event umbral_forbidden_notes\ntitle: Forbidden Notes\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: umbral\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: class mystery\nthread: forbidden\nscene: A battered notebook with a cracked leather cover being slid across a library table, a silver key tucked into its spine.\ntext: A fourth-year, Odile Varn, slides a battered notebook across the table. \"My notes on the Restricted Section. I'm graduating. You look like you'd use them.\" A silver key is tucked in the spine.\n> left: Decline\n  say: The fourth-year shrugs and slides the notebook to the next student. You watch it go.\n> right: Take the notebook\n  alignment -tiny\n  lore +medium\n  flag forbidden_key\n  find lead_silver_key\n  discover restricted_section\n  say: The notes are brilliant, half-mad and annotated in three colours of ink. The key is cold in your pocket: it opens the Restricted Section, the locked door at the back of the library, after hours.\n"},{"path":"content/events/club_spell_chains.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Club and job spells (spells v2, docs/spells-v2-list.md §G).\n#  Every chain has the same shape:\n#   - a start card, pushed once you reach the track's second rank (and again later if you turn it down): it sets\n#     <spell>_chain and leaves a lead;\n#   - three preparation cards, each a check or a resource spent, each leaving a flag (skipped, they come back);\n#   - the final milestone check on the spell's skill (once two preparations are done and a month has passed, or four\n#     months on), one step\n#     easier per preparation done (`even` plus three steps is\n#     near certain), with a paid route at better odds for the underprepared. On a loss it comes back later, and the\n#     preparation is kept.\n#  Measured 2026-10-17: a club meets about twice a term and a track pool is ~20 cards, so with §G's weight ~8 for a\n#  drawn start card most rank-2 members never saw the chain. The start is pushed instead, and the chain's cards\n#  outweigh the pool while it runs (preparation 60, final 40).\n#  Two beats a visit (feedback 2026-10-19, migration 0035): the start's accepting option queues the first preparation\n#  `now`, and a preparation's working option queues, once a visit (`chain_beat`), the final when it is ready or else\n#  the next preparation not done. Measured over 90 games: learned 60% of chains started (was 43%), median 6 months\n#  from start to spell (was 8).\n#  Each chain is a @thread from the quest template (content/core/templates.grim, migration 0047): the template's\n#  pools give each role its trigger, weights and gates, and `next` is the routing above. Bramble's later\n#  preparations wait on the first, so its thread is plain and its cards keep their own wiring.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Duelling Circle → Brace (vigor) ----------------\n\n@thread brace_quest\nfrom: quest\nwith: begun=brace_chain\nwhen: spell_level(brace) == 0\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclues: lead_brace\nname: The captain's charm\npremise: An old captain's charm in the duelling dummy empties only if someone holds firm while it fires.\njournal:\n  flag(brace_chain): You said you'd try to empty the old captain's charm. Someone drew a stretcher under your name.\n  flag(brace_notes): The captain's notes: don't stop the first blow, let it land on something that isn't you.\n  flag(brace_circle): Bram threw hexes at you in the chalk circle until he ran out.\n  flag(brace_footwork): Lyra drilled your stance until dark. \"Better,\" she said.\n  seen(duel_brace_final) > 0 and spell_level(brace) < 1: The first time, the charm lifted you clean out of the circle.\n  flag(brace_chain) and spell_level(brace) >= 1: You held the circle until the charm sputtered out. Lyra nodded, once.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(brace_chain) and spell_level(brace) >= 1\nmembers:\n  start duel_dummy_hexes\n  part duel_captains_notes flag=brace_notes\n  part duel_bram_barrage flag=brace_circle\n  part duel_footwork_lyra flag=brace_footwork\n  final duel_brace_final\n\n@event duel_dummy_hexes\ntitle: The Dummy Hits Back\nthread: brace_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: rank(duelling) >= 2 and turn <= 44\nart: shield\ntags: club duel\nscene: A duel hall's practice corner, an eyepatched straw dummy flinging a crackling hex back at a startled student, stuffing spilling from a split seam.\ntext: The eyepatched dummy has started throwing hexes back. Lyra splits its seam and pulls out an old captain's charm, sewn in decades ago and still humming. \"It empties if someone holds firm in the circle while it fires,\" she says. \"Nobody's managed it. Nobody's tried properly.\"\n> left: \"I'll try properly.\"\n  flag brace_chain\n  find lead_brace\n  perform duelling +tiny\n  say: Lyra sews the charm back in, looks at you, and writes your name on the board under the dummy. Underneath, someone adds a small drawing of a stretcher.\n  chain_beat = turn\n  queue duel_captains_notes now\n> right: Leave it to the seniors\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You go back to your bout, and the dummy goes back to hexing whoever passes. A senior limps past at the end of practice, still smoking slightly.\n\n@event duel_captains_notes\ntitle: The Captain's Notes\nthread: brace_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: club study\nscene: A dusty cupboard in the duel hall, a student reading a cracked leather notebook by the light of a single lamp.\ntext: The captain who made the charm left notes, in a cupboard under the trophy shelf, in a hand like barbed wire. Half of it is about footwork and the other half is insults addressed to people long graduated.\n> left: Read them properly\n  challenge research journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag brace_notes\n      lore +small\n      say: Between the insults is the trick: you don't stop the first blow, you let it land on something that isn't you. You copy the page out and put the notebook back where the captain would have wanted it.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You read until the lamp gutters and learn a great deal about a student called Pim, who could not parry. The trick is in there somewhere. You'll have to come back for it.\n  next\n> right: Spar instead tonight\n  perform duelling +small\n  say: You leave the notes for another evening and take a bout instead. You win it, which is the other kind of useful.\n\n@event duel_bram_barrage\ntitle: Inside the Chalk Circle\nthread: brace_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: bolt\ntags: club duel\nscene: A student standing in a chalk circle on a scorched floor while a broad prefect hurls a stream of stinging hexes from the edge.\ntext: Word reaches Bram that you mean to empty the captain's charm. He turns up with his sleeves rolled. \"Regulation hazing,\" he announces, \"section four. You stand in the circle. I throw. You don't step out. Ready?\"\n> left: Hold the circle against him\n  challenge duel vs bram.vigor:\n    win:\n      flag brace_circle\n      vigor +small\n      say: You don't step out. By the twentieth hex Bram is red in the face and you are still in the circle, scorched and planted. \"Adequate,\" he says, gruffly, and doesn't put you on report.\n    lose:\n      say: The ninth hex knocks you clean out of the circle. Bram notes it in his book. \"Section four, failed,\" he says. \"Again next week.\"\n  next\n> right: Take it all on your shield arm\n  flag brace_circle\n  health -medium\n  say: You don't try to be clever. You just stand there and take it, every hex, on your arm, until Bram runs out. Your arm is purple for a week. Bram looks almost impressed.\n  next\n> up[item:duelling_coat]: Shrug it off in the coat\n  flag brace_circle\n  health -small\n  perform duelling +tiny\n  say: You button the quilted coat to the chin and plant your feet. The hexes thud into the padding like snowballs into a wall. Bram runs out first. \"That's cheating,\" he says. \"Section four doesn't mention coats,\" you say.\n  next\n\n@event duel_footwork_lyra\ntitle: Footwork Until Dark\nthread: brace_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: wand\ntags: club duel\nscene: An empty duel hall at dusk, two students drilling footwork across chalk marks, one of them pointing sharply at the other's feet.\ntext: Lyra finds you after practice. \"The captain's notes say the stance is everything,\" she says. \"Your stance is a disgrace. Three hours. Now.\" She has already chalked the marks on the floor.\n> left: Drill until dark\n  flag brace_footwork\n  mood -small\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  say: She is merciless and exact. By dark you can set your feet without thinking, and you never want to see a chalk mark again. \"Better,\" she says, which from Lyra is a sonnet.\n  next\n> right: \"Tomorrow. I have an essay.\"\n  study +tiny\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: She looks at you as if you'd chosen to lose. You write the essay. It is a fine essay, and Lyra mentions your stance at breakfast.\n\n@event duel_brace_final\ntitle: Hold Firm\nthread: brace_quest\ntone: tense\nart: spell_brace\ntags: club duel magic\nscene: A student standing firm in a chalk circle in a crowded duel hall as an eyepatched dummy fires hex after hex, the club watching from the benches.\ntext: The club gathers on the benches. You step into the chalk circle, and the eyepatched dummy turns its head toward you with an audible creak. The captain's charm begins to fire. {flag(brace_notes) and flag(brace_circle) and flag(brace_footwork): You know how to stand, and why.|You have done some of the work. Not all of it.}\n> left: Hold firm\n  challenge feat adept- +1 if flag(brace_notes) +1 if flag(brace_circle) +1 if flag(brace_footwork):\n    win:\n      learn_spell brace\n      perform duelling +small\n      say: The hexes break on you one after another, and each lands softer than the last. When the charm finally sputters out, the club is on its feet. Lyra doesn't clap. She nods, once, which is louder.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The fourteenth hex lifts you out of the circle and into the benches. The dummy turns its head back with a creak. It will be there at practice, waiting.\n> right: Borrow the captain's old gauntlets\n  gold -small\n  challenge feat adept- likely +1 if flag(brace_notes) +1 if flag(brace_circle) +1 if flag(brace_footwork):\n    win:\n      learn_spell brace\n      perform duelling +small\n      say: The club quartermaster rents you the captain's own gauntlets for the night. They take the first blow for you, and the rest you take yourself. When the charm sputters out, the benches roar.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: Even in the gauntlets, the fourteenth hex lifts you out of the circle. The rental is not refunded. The dummy creaks its head back and waits.\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger → Quickquill (guile) ----------------\n\n@thread quickquill_quest\nfrom: quest\nwith: begun=quickquill_chain\nwhen: spell_level(quickquill) == 0\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 2\nclues: lead_quickquill\nname: The quill that talks back\npremise: A spare quill at the Ledger has started inventing your sentences.\njournal:\n  flag(quickquill_chain): You took the Ledger's runaway quill home in a handkerchief. It wrote \"kidnapped\" on it.\n  flag(quickquill_soup): You kept the quill to the facts on forty letters about the soup.\n  flag(quickquill_ink): You bought it proper ink, black as a well. It underlines the bits you'll need.\n  flag(quickquill_deadline): You and the quill wrote until four on deadline night. Nessa put your name on the page.\n  seen(ledger_quill_interview) > 0 and spell_level(quickquill) < 1: The first interview got away from you, and from the quill.\n  flag(quickquill_chain) and spell_level(quickquill) >= 1: The chair denied everything, word for word. \"Front page,\" said Nessa.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(quickquill_chain) and spell_level(quickquill) >= 1\nmembers:\n  start ledger_spare_quill\n  part ledger_soup_complaint flag=quickquill_soup\n  part ledger_proper_ink flag=quickquill_ink\n  part ledger_deadline_night flag=quickquill_deadline\n  final ledger_quill_interview\n\n@event ledger_spare_quill\ntitle: The Spare Quill\nthread: quickquill_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: rank(lumen_ledger) >= 2 and turn <= 44\nart: paper\ntags: club\nscene: A newspaper office with proofs pegged on a drying line, a lone quill hanging among them scribbling on a proof by itself.\ntext: A spare quill on the drying line has been finishing your sentences. Today it started inventing them: your proof now says the refectory serves dragon. \"Oh, it's woken up,\" says Nessa, delighted. \"Tame it and it's yours. Nobody's tamed one since I've been editor.\"\n> left: Take the quill home\n  flag quickquill_chain\n  find lead_quickquill\n  perform lumen_ledger +tiny\n  say: You wrap it in a handkerchief. It writes \"kidnapped\" on the handkerchief. Nessa writes your name on the office board under QUILL, with a question mark.\n  chain_beat = turn\n  queue ledger_soup_complaint now\n> right: Peg it back on the line\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You peg it back. It writes a small, hurt editorial about you on the next proof, which Nessa cuts, laughing.\n\n@event ledger_soup_complaint\ntitle: The Soup Complaint\nthread: quickquill_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: club\nscene: A desk buried in angry letters about soup, a student dictating firmly to a quill that keeps trying to add flourishes.\ntext: Forty furious letters about the refectory soup have arrived. Nessa wants one calm reply, printed. You decide to dictate it to the quill, which wants very badly to make it more exciting.\n> left: Keep the quill to the facts\n  challenge spell using guile journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag quickquill_soup\n      guile +small\n      say: Every time it reaches for a flourish, you catch it. The reply comes out calm, true and very dull. Nessa prints it. The quill sulks, and then, grudgingly, writes the next one straight.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You blink and the quill has written that the soup contains a mild curse. It prints. You spend Thursday apologising to the cooks.\n  next\n> right: Write the reply by hand\n  perform lumen_ledger +tiny\n  say: You write it yourself, slowly. It's a good reply. The quill watches from the inkwell, offended at being left out.\n\n@event ledger_proper_ink\ntitle: Proper Ink\nthread: quickquill_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: club\nscene: A stationer's counter in the village, a student holding a small dark bottle of ink up to the light while a quill pokes out of their pocket.\ntext: The quill refuses the school ink. It blots, it sulks, it writes \"no\" in large letters. The stationer in the village has proper ink, black as a well and priced like it.\n> left: Buy the proper ink\n  gold -small\n  flag quickquill_ink\n  say: The quill drinks the new ink and writes a whole page of your notes, neatly, in a better hand than yours. It underlines the bits you'll need. You feel slightly managed.\n  next\n> right: Make it use the school ink\n  mood -tiny\n  say: It uses the school ink under protest and blots every third word, including, pointedly, your name.\n\n@event ledger_deadline_night\ntitle: Deadline Night\nthread: quickquill_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: candle\ntags: club\nscene: A newspaper office after midnight, cold tea and candle stubs everywhere, a student and a quill working side by side while an editor paces.\ntext: Deadline night. The front page fell through at nine and Nessa is pacing. \"We need eight hundred words by four,\" she says. \"Anybody with a quill that writes fast, now is the time.\"\n> left: Sit it out with the quill\n  flag quickquill_deadline\n  perform lumen_ledger +small\n  mood -medium\n  say: You and the quill write until four. By the end it finishes your sentences only when you want it to. You are both too tired to argue. Nessa puts your name on the page, small.\n  next\n> right: Go to bed\n  health +tiny\n  say: You sleep. In the morning the front page is late and Nessa is hoarse, and the quill has written a bitter little poem about you on the pillowcase.\n\n@event ledger_quill_interview\ntitle: Deny Everything\nthread: quickquill_quest\ntone: tense\nart: spell_quickquill\ntags: club magic\nscene: A stuffy committee room, a smooth official smiling across a table at a student whose quill writes furiously on its own.\ntext: Nessa sends you to interview the chair of the refectory committee, who will deny everything, including the soup. \"Get it word for word,\" she says. \"Every word. They'll deny the words too.\" {flag(quickquill_soup) and flag(quickquill_ink) and flag(quickquill_deadline): Your quill sits up in your pocket, ready.|Your quill is half-tamed at best.}\n> left: Interview them with the quill\n  challenge negotiate using guile adept- +1 if flag(quickquill_soup) +1 if flag(quickquill_ink) +1 if flag(quickquill_deadline):\n    win:\n      learn_spell quickquill\n      perform lumen_ledger +small\n      say: The chair denies everything, and the quill takes every denial down word for word, including the one that contradicts the other three. Nessa reads it and goes very quiet. \"Front page,\" she says.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: The quill gets bored halfway and starts improving the chair's answers. Nothing is printable. The quill sulks in the WILL NOT PRINT basket until your next front page.\n> right: Prepare all night first\n  mood -small\n  challenge negotiate using guile adept- likely +1 if flag(quickquill_soup) +1 if flag(quickquill_ink) +1 if flag(quickquill_deadline):\n    win:\n      learn_spell quickquill\n      perform lumen_ledger +small\n      say: You go in tired and over-prepared, with every question written out. The quill follows your lead all the way through. Nessa reads the transcript twice. \"Front page.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: All night's preparation, and the chair simply doesn't turn up. The quill sulks in the WILL NOT PRINT basket until your next front page.\n\n# ---------------- Verdant Circle → Bramble (alchemy) ----------------\n\n@thread bramble_quest\nclues: lead_bramble\nname: The seed in your pocket\npremise: A hedge-seed that chose you, and has already put out a shoot.\njournal:\n  flag(bramble_chain): \"That's a hedge-seed,\" Tobias said. \"They choose people.\" This one chose you.\n  flag(bramble_planted): You found your seedling a bed of its own.\n  flag(bramble_fed): You sat up with it through the first frost, feeding it warmth. It grew a thorn.\n  flag(bramble_choir): You quieted the mandrake choir, and your seedling put out a vine.\n  seen(verdant_bramble_bolts) > 0 and spell_level(bramble) < 1: The night it bolted, the hedge didn't know you yet.\n  flag(bramble_chain) and spell_level(bramble) >= 1: The night it bolted across the greenhouse, it stopped for you.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(bramble_chain) and spell_level(bramble) >= 1\n\n@event verdant_far_bed\ntitle: The Far Bed Answers\nthread: bramble_quest\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: rank(verdant) >= 2 and spell_level(bramble) == 0 and not flag(bramble_chain) and turn <= 44\nrepeat: 3\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: The far end of a greenhouse, a tangle of vines reaching toward a student who has just found a green shoot curling out of their coat pocket.\ntext: The far bed answers back today: every vine in it turns to follow you. On the way out you find a seed in your coat pocket that wasn't there this morning, and it has already put out a shoot.\n> left: Keep it and grow it\n  flag bramble_chain\n  find lead_bramble\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You cup it in your palm. The shoot curls round your finger, firmly. Tobias, when you show him, goes pale and then delighted. \"That's a hedge-seed,\" he says. \"They choose people.\"\n  chain_beat = turn\n  queue verdant_plant_seed now\n> right: Give it to Tobias\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  say: Tobias takes it gently and puts it in a pot by the door. \"It'll want you back,\" he says. \"They usually do.\"\n\n@event verdant_plant_seed\ntitle: Somewhere to Grow\nthread: bramble_quest\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: flag(bramble_chain) and not flag(bramble_planted) and spell_level(bramble) == 0\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 60\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: Two students kneeling in a greenhouse bed planting a seedling, a lantern between them and earth up to their elbows.\ntext: The seed has outgrown your pocket. It needs a bed. Tobias offers the corner of his own in the greenhouse. Old Maud, passing, says she still keeps a bed at the back of the same greenhouse, by the old pipes, that nobody else is allowed to touch, \"if you can keep a secret, dearie.\"\n> left: Plant it with Tobias\n  flag bramble_planted\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: You plant it together by lantern light, and Tobias tells it a long story about a goat to help it settle. It settles. So does Tobias, eventually.\n  if chain_beat != turn:\n    chain_beat = turn\n    if since(verdant_far_bed) >= 1 and flag(bramble_planted) + flag(bramble_fed) + flag(bramble_choir) >= 2:\n      queue verdant_bramble_bolts now\n    elif not flag(bramble_fed):\n      queue verdant_cold_snap now\n    elif not flag(bramble_choir):\n      queue verdant_mandrake_choir now\n> right: Plant it in Maud's corner\n  flag bramble_planted\n  rel maud +small\n  say: Maud leads you to the back of the greenhouse, behind a stack of broken frames you'd never have looked past, to a bed she has kept for forty years. You plant it there. \"It'll be happy,\" she says, \"for a while.\"\n  if chain_beat != turn:\n    chain_beat = turn\n    if since(verdant_far_bed) >= 1 and flag(bramble_planted) + flag(bramble_fed) + flag(bramble_choir) >= 2:\n      queue verdant_bramble_bolts now\n    elif not flag(bramble_fed):\n      queue verdant_cold_snap now\n    elif not flag(bramble_choir):\n      queue verdant_mandrake_choir now\n\n@event verdant_cold_snap\ntime: night\ntitle: The Cold Snap\nthread: bramble_quest\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(bramble_planted) and not flag(bramble_fed) and spell_level(bramble) == 0\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 60\nart: snow\ntags: club nature\nscene: Frost creeping over a greenhouse bed at night, a student wrapped in a blanket sitting beside a shivering seedling with a lantern.\ntext: A hard frost is coming tonight, the first of the season. Your seedling is too young for it. Somebody would have to sit with it till dawn, feeding it warmth.\n> left: Sit up with it all night\n  flag bramble_fed\n  health -small\n  say: You sit wrapped round the lantern till dawn, feeding it what warmth you have. By morning you're stiff and coughing, and the seedling has grown a thorn, which it seems proud of.\n  if chain_beat != turn:\n    chain_beat = turn\n    if since(verdant_far_bed) >= 1 and flag(bramble_planted) + flag(bramble_fed) + flag(bramble_choir) >= 2:\n      queue verdant_bramble_bolts now\n    elif not flag(bramble_planted):\n      queue verdant_plant_seed now\n    elif not flag(bramble_choir):\n      queue verdant_mandrake_choir now\n> right: Cover it and hope\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You cover it with sacking and go to bed. It survives, sulking, and loses a leaf. It is noticeably cooler towards you.\n\n@event verdant_mandrake_choir\ntitle: The Mandrake Choir\nthread: bramble_quest\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: flag(bramble_planted) and not flag(bramble_choir) and spell_level(bramble) == 0\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 60\nart: music\ntags: club nature\nscene: A row of potted mandrakes in knitted hats screaming in chorus at a nervous seedling, a student with earmuffs approaching with a watering can.\ntext: The mandrakes in the next bed have taken against your seedling and scream at it in chorus every evening. It's wilting from the noise. Gerald is conducting.\n> left: Quiet the choir\n  challenge enchant journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag bramble_choir\n      alchemy +small\n      say: A drop of valerian in each pot and a firm word with Gerald, and the choir settles into a low hum. Your seedling straightens up and puts out a vine.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: The choir screams at you instead. Your ears ring till supper. Gerald looks unrepentant in his hat.\n  if chain_beat != turn:\n    chain_beat = turn\n    if since(verdant_far_bed) >= 1 and flag(bramble_planted) + flag(bramble_fed) + flag(bramble_choir) >= 2:\n      queue verdant_bramble_bolts now\n    elif not flag(bramble_planted):\n      queue verdant_plant_seed now\n    elif not flag(bramble_fed):\n      queue verdant_cold_snap now\n> right: Move the seedling's pot\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You move it to the quiet end. It perks up, but it's away from the warm pipes now, and it isn't growing.\n\n@event verdant_bramble_bolts\ntime: night\ntitle: The Night It Bolts\nthread: bramble_quest\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(bramble_chain) and spell_level(bramble) == 0 and since(verdant_far_bed) >= 1 and (flag(bramble_planted) + flag(bramble_fed) + flag(bramble_choir) >= 2 or since(verdant_far_bed) >= 4)\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 40\nart: spell_bramble\ntags: club nature magic\nscene: A greenhouse at night, a thorny hedge surging across the beds toward a row of pale flowers, a student with arms outstretched trying to hold it back.\ntext: Tobias wakes you at midnight. Your plant has bolted: a hedge of thorns is surging across the greenhouse toward the moonflower beds, and it is not stopping for anyone but you. {flag(bramble_fed) and flag(bramble_choir): It knows your voice.|You're not sure it knows you at all.}\n> left: Call it to heel\n  challenge enchant adept- +1 if flag(bramble_planted) +1 if flag(bramble_fed) +1 if flag(bramble_choir):\n    win:\n      learn_spell bramble\n      perform verdant +small\n      say: You put out your hand and the hedge stops a finger's width from the moonflowers, then folds itself back into your palm like a sleeping cat. Tobias sits down on a bucket and laughs from the belly.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The thorns take your sleeve and some of your arm before Tobias shears it back. Maud keeps a cutting, and says she'll offer it to you again in Stormbloom.\n> right: Wade in and grab the root\n  health -small\n  challenge enchant adept- likely +1 if flag(bramble_planted) +1 if flag(bramble_fed) +1 if flag(bramble_choir):\n    win:\n      learn_spell bramble\n      perform verdant +small\n      say: You wade through thorns to the root and hold it. The hedge goes still, then curls round you instead, gently. You're scratched to ribbons and it's yours.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: You get the root and it gets you. Tobias shears the hedge back by dawn. Maud keeps a cutting for Stormbloom.\n\n# ---------------- Library Assistant → Index (lore) ----------------\n\n@thread index_quest\nfrom: quest\nwith: begun=index_chain\nwhen: spell_level(index) == 0\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 2\nclues: lead_index\nname: Footnote's drawer\npremise: The library cat guards a card for a book eleven years overdue.\njournal:\n  flag(index_chain): Footnote was guarding the card of a book on wards, borrowed by Professor Thorne eleven years ago.\n  flag(index_fish): A proper herring bought you the card, and apparently the cat.\n  flag(index_hush): You walked the night hush with the old lamp and marked which shelf went quiet last.\n  flag(index_string): You followed the wandering section on a string for six hours.\n  seen(library_overdue_book) > 0 and spell_level(index) < 1: The first night the shelves moved, the book stayed lost.\n  flag(index_chain) and spell_level(index) >= 1: You found the book, eleven years overdue. Every book falls open at the right page for you now.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(index_chain) and spell_level(index) >= 1\nmembers:\n  start library_footnote_drawer\n  part library_fish_footnote flag=index_fish\n  part library_hush_lamp flag=index_hush\n  part library_wandering_section flag=index_string\n  final library_overdue_book\n\n@event library_footnote_drawer\ntitle: Footnote's Drawer\nthread: index_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: rank(library_assistant) >= 2 and turn <= 44\nart: cat\ntags: job study\nscene: A long library catalogue cabinet, a large cat sitting immovably on one drawer, a student crouching to read its label.\ntext: Footnote has sat on one catalogue drawer for a week and hisses at anyone who comes near it. The label underneath him says OVERDUE, ELEVEN YEARS. The head librarian says whoever finds that book is welcome to the trick of finding books.\n> left: Find out what he's guarding\n  flag index_chain\n  find lead_index\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You read the card around his tail. A book on wards, borrowed by a Professor Thorne, eleven years ago. Footnote looks at you as if you've finally asked the right question.\n  chain_beat = turn\n  queue library_fish_footnote now\n> right: Shelve around him\n  perform library_assistant +tiny\n  say: You shelve around Footnote for the rest of the shift. He watches every book you put away, as if marking them.\n\n@event library_fish_footnote\ntitle: Bribing the Cat\nthread: index_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: cat\ntags: job\nscene: A student offering a small paper parcel of fish to a large, unimpressed cat on a library cabinet.\ntext: Footnote won't move from the drawer for less than fish. Good fish, from the village, not the refectory's. The head librarian says this is \"between you and the cat\".\n> left: Buy him a proper herring\n  gold -small\n  flag index_fish\n  say: Footnote eats the herring slowly, looking at you the whole time, then steps off the drawer and onto your lap. The card is yours. So, apparently, is the cat.\n  next\n> right: Wait him out\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You wait. Footnote waits better. At closing he yawns, stretches, and lies back down on the drawer.\n\n@event library_hush_lamp\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Night Hush\nthread: index_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: lantern\ntags: job study\nscene: A dark library reading room at night, a student walking the shelves with a lamp held high while the books seem to lean toward the light.\ntext: The overdue book will only be on the shelves the night hush sends it to. The head librarian lends you the old lamp. \"Walk the hush,\" she says, \"and listen to which shelf goes quiet last.\"\n> left: Walk the hush and listen\n  challenge research journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag index_hush\n      lore +small\n      say: Three laps, and on the third, the shelves on the north side go quiet a heartbeat after the rest. You mark it on your map. The lamp seems pleased with you.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: Every shelf goes quiet at once, which is either very helpful or a joke at your expense. You walk until midnight and mark nothing.\n  next\n> right: Ask the head librarian outright\n  perform library_assistant -tiny\n  say: \"If I told you,\" she says, \"you wouldn't have found it.\" She gives you extra shelving for asking.\n\n@event library_wandering_section\ntitle: The Wandering Section\nthread: index_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: job\nscene: A student trailing a long ball of string through a maze of library shelves that have visibly rearranged themselves behind them.\ntext: The section the book belongs to wanders. It was by the stairs on Monday and in the map room on Wednesday. The only way to follow it is to tie a string to the end shelf and walk where it goes, however long that takes.\n> left: Follow it with string\n  flag index_string\n  mood -small\n  say: You follow it for six hours, through rooms you didn't know the library had. At the end you know its route by heart and never want to see a ball of string again.\n  next\n> right: Wait for it to come back\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You read at the desk until the section wanders past again, a day later. You learn nothing about its route, and a little about medieval ward-lattices.\n\n@event library_overdue_book\ntitle: Eleven Years Overdue\nthread: index_quest\ntone: eerie\nart: spell_index\ntags: job study magic\nscene: A student high on a rolling ladder in a library where the shelves are visibly moving, a cat on their shoulder, one dusty book glowing faintly.\ntext: Tonight the shelves are moving. Somewhere in the wandering section is the book, eleven years overdue, and every book you touch tries to fall open at the wrong page. {flag(index_fish): Footnote is on your shoulder.|You're on your own.}\n> left: Find the page\n  challenge research adept- +1 if flag(index_fish) +1 if flag(index_hush) +1 if flag(index_string):\n    win:\n      learn_spell index\n      perform library_assistant +small\n      say: Your hand finds the spine before your eyes do, and the book falls open at exactly the page it was borrowed for. Every book you touch afterwards does the same. The head librarian stamps the card, eleven years late, without a word.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You get lost on the seventh floor among shelves that don't exist by day. Footnote leads you out at dawn, looking smug. The card is still on the drawer.\n> right: Pay a senior clerk for their map\n  gold -small\n  challenge research adept- likely +1 if flag(index_fish) +1 if flag(index_hush) +1 if flag(index_string):\n    win:\n      learn_spell index\n      perform library_assistant +small\n      say: The senior clerk's map is ten years out of date and exactly right where it matters. You find the book, and it falls open at the page it was borrowed for. Every book does, after that.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The map is ten years out of date and mostly wrong. Footnote leads you out at dawn. The book stays lost, for now.\n\n# ---------------- The Gilded Newt → Rapport (charm) ----------------\n\n@thread rapport_quest\nfrom: quest\nwith: begun=rapport_chain\nwhen: spell_level(rapport) == 0\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 2\nclues: lead_rapport\nname: Two taps on the bar\npremise: The Newt's landlady stops fights with two taps and one sentence.\njournal:\n  flag(rapport_chain): \"Can't teach it,\" the landlady said. \"You earn it. Learn the room, buy the room, work the room.\"\n  flag(rapport_eel): You learned the old barge-man's eel story and told it back.\n  flag(rapport_round): You stood the regulars' corner a round. They know your name now.\n  flag(rapport_shift): Fourteen hours on your feet: now you can read the room from across it.\n  seen(tavern_festival_room) > 0 and spell_level(rapport) < 1: The first night the room was yours, it got away from you.\n  flag(rapport_chain) and spell_level(rapport) >= 1: You held the Newt on a packed night. The landlady knew at once, and nodded.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(rapport_chain) and spell_level(rapport) >= 1\nmembers:\n  start tavern_two_taps\n  part tavern_eel_story flag=rapport_eel\n  part tavern_regulars_round flag=rapport_round\n  part tavern_double_shift flag=rapport_shift\n  final tavern_festival_room\n\n@event tavern_two_taps\ntime: evening\ntitle: Two Taps on the Bar\nthread: rapport_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: rank(tavern) >= 2 and turn <= 44\nart: cup\ntags: job\nscene: A crowded tavern on the edge of a brawl, a landlady tapping the bar twice and the whole room suddenly laughing instead.\ntext: Two barge-men are halfway out of their chairs when the landlady taps the bar twice, says one sentence you don't catch, and the whole room laughs instead. The fight is over before it began. She catches you staring. \"Want to know how?\"\n> left: \"Yes. Teach me.\"\n  flag rapport_chain\n  find lead_rapport\n  charm +tiny\n  say: \"Can't teach it,\" she says. \"You earn it. Learn the room, buy the room, work the room.\" She taps the bar twice at you, and you laugh, and you don't know why.\n  chain_beat = turn\n  queue tavern_eel_story now\n> right: Keep pouring\n  perform tavern +tiny\n  say: You keep pouring. The barge-men buy each other a drink and sing. The landlady watches you, thoughtfully, for the rest of the shift.\n\n@event tavern_eel_story\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Eel Story\nthread: rapport_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: wave\ntags: job\nscene: A barge-man at a tavern table with his arms spread wide, telling a story to a student taking notes on a bar napkin.\ntext: The old barge-man's eel story is the most famous story at the Newt. Regulars mouth along. The landlady says if you can tell it as well as he does, the room will be half yours. He's willing to teach it, slowly, for an evening.\n> left: Learn it and tell it back\n  challenge perform journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      flag rapport_eel\n      charm +small\n      say: You tell it back that same night, to the same corner, and get the laugh at the eel's second escape. The barge-man wipes his eyes and buys you a cider.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You get the eel's name wrong and the ending backwards. The regulars are very kind about it, which is worse.\n  next\n> right: Just enjoy the story\n  mood +small\n  say: You sit and listen to the eel story twice through, and laugh both times. You couldn't tell it, but you've rarely had a better evening.\n\n@event tavern_regulars_round\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Regulars' Corner\nthread: rapport_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: job\nscene: A student setting down a tray of mugs at a crowded corner table in a tavern, the regulars raising them in a toast.\ntext: The regulars' corner has never had a round bought by staff. The landlady says that's exactly why you should. \"Nobody listens to a story from someone who's never stood them a drink.\"\n> left: Stand the corner a round\n  gold -small\n  flag rapport_round\n  say: The corner raises the mugs to you, by name, which you didn't know they knew. For the rest of the night they call you over to settle every argument.\n  next\n> right: Keep your tips\n  gold +tiny\n  say: You pocket your tips. The corner doesn't notice either way, which is rather the point.\n\n@event tavern_double_shift\ntitle: The Double Shift\nthread: rapport_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: cup\ntags: job\nscene: A tired student wiping down a bar at closing time after a very long day, the landlady counting the till beside them.\ntext: Two servers are down with Lake Fever, and the landlady needs a double shift: lunch through closing. \"You'll learn more about a room in fourteen hours,\" she says, \"than in a term of evenings.\"\n> left: Work the double\n  wage\n  flag rapport_shift\n  health -small\n  say: Fourteen hours on your feet. By closing you can tell who wants to talk and who wants to be left alone from across the room. Your feet are a separate, angrier person.\n  next\n> right: Turn it down\n  perform tavern -tiny\n  say: You say you can't. The landlady works it herself, and is short with you for a week.\n\n@event tavern_festival_room\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Room Is Yours\nthread: rapport_quest\ntone: tense\nart: spell_rapport\ntags: job magic\nscene: A packed tavern on a festival night, a student standing on a chair telling a story while the crowd hangs on every word.\ntext: The landlady is away for the night and the Newt is packed to the rafters, two barges in and a fight brewing by the fire. It's yours to hold. {flag(rapport_eel) and flag(rapport_round) and flag(rapport_shift): You know this room now.|You know some of this room.}\n> left: Tap the bar twice\n  challenge negotiate adept- +1 if flag(rapport_eel) +1 if flag(rapport_round) +1 if flag(rapport_shift):\n    win:\n      learn_spell rapport\n      renown +small\n      say: You tap the bar twice, say one sentence about an eel, and the room laughs. The fight by the fire turns into a song. When the landlady comes back, she knows at once. She just nods.\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      say: The tap comes out as a knock and the laugh never comes. A chair breaks, and so do some glasses, and the fine comes out of your wages. Next festival night, she says, you can try again.\n> right: Buy the first round, then talk\n  gold -small\n  challenge negotiate adept- likely +1 if flag(rapport_eel) +1 if flag(rapport_round) +1 if flag(rapport_shift):\n    win:\n      learn_spell rapport\n      renown +small\n      say: A round on the house first, then a story while they drink it. By the time the mugs are empty, nobody remembers wanting to fight. The landlady hears about it before she's through the door.\n    lose:\n      gold -tiny\n      say: They drink the round and fight anyway. You mop up with the rest of the staff. Next festival night, the landlady says.\n\n# ---------------- Apothecary Clerk → Distill (alchemy) ----------------\n\n@thread distil_quest\nfrom: quest\nwith: begun=distil_chain\nwhen: spell_level(purify) == 0\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 2\nclues: lead_distil\nname: Pell's oldest drawer\npremise: A yellowed card in Pell's oldest drawer: a draught \"of your own strength\".\njournal:\n  flag(distil_chain): You copied out the old draught's recipe. Pell corrected one of your measures, which is permission.\n  flag(distil_drops): You counted four hundred and twelve drops with Pell and didn't lose count. \"Fair,\" he said.\n  flag(distil_moonwort): You bought the good moonwort and carried it home without humming.\n  flag(distil_shortcut): You tasted the draught first, the daughters' way, and spent a day in bed learning where it pulls from.\n  seen(pell_draught) > 0 and spell_level(purify) < 1: The first brew boiled over and took more out of you than it should.\n  flag(distil_chain) and spell_level(purify) >= 1: The draught came down to one shining drop. Pell stopped counting. \"Fair.\"\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(distil_chain) and spell_level(purify) >= 1\nmembers:\n  start pell_oldest_drawer\n  part pell_counting_drops flag=distil_drops\n  part pell_buy_moonwort flag=distil_moonwort\n  part pell_daughters_shortcut flag=distil_shortcut\n  final pell_draught\n\n@event pell_oldest_drawer\ntitle: The Oldest Drawer\nthread: distil_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 2 and turn <= 44\nart: potion\ntags: job\nscene: An old apothecary's wall of tiny drawers, one at the very bottom pulled open to reveal a yellowed recipe card, a gruff old man watching from the counter.\ntext: Restocking, you open the oldest drawer at the bottom of Pell's wall. Inside is one yellowed card: a draught \"of your own strength\", in a hand older than Pell's. Pell sees it and counts to three under his breath. \"That one costs the brewer,\" he says. \"Measure twice.\"\n> left: Copy the recipe\n  flag distil_chain\n  find lead_distil\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You copy it out carefully. Pell watches you do it and says nothing, then corrects one of your measures in your copy. That is the closest he comes to permission.\n  chain_beat = turn\n  queue pell_counting_drops now\n> right: Close the drawer\n  rel pell +tiny\n  say: You close it. Pell nods once and goes back to his scales. Later you notice he has relabelled the drawer, clearly, \"Not yet.\"\n\n@event pell_counting_drops\ntime: night\ntitle: Counting Drops\nthread: distil_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: potion\ntags: job\nscene: A dim apothecary at night, an old man and a student bent over a slowly dripping still, both counting under their breath.\ntext: Pell distills by the drop, all night, counting aloud. \"If you mean to try the old draught,\" he says, \"you'll count with me first. Lose count once and we start again.\"\n> left: Count all night with him\n  challenge brew journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag distil_drops\n      alchemy +small\n      say: Four hundred and twelve drops, and you don't lose count once. At dawn Pell says \"Fair,\" which is once a decade, and lets you stopper the bottle.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You lose count at three hundred and something. Pell starts again without comment. So do you. You lose it again.\n  next\n> right: Go home and sleep\n  health +tiny\n  say: You go home. Pell counts on alone, steadily, through the night. In the morning the bottle is full and labelled, and he says nothing about it.\n\n@event pell_buy_moonwort\ntitle: Moonwort\nthread: distil_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: leaf\ntags: job\nscene: An apothecary counter with a small jar labelled \"Moonwort. Do not hum near.\", a student counting out coins beside it.\ntext: The old draught needs moonwort, and the only good moonwort in the village is the jar on Pell's top shelf, labelled \"Do not hum near.\" Pell sells it to staff at cost. The cost is not small.\n> left: Buy the moonwort\n  gold -small\n  flag distil_moonwort\n  say: Pell weighs it twice and wraps it in waxed paper. You carry it home without humming, which is harder than it sounds.\n  next\n> right: Try the cheap substitute\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The substitute smells wrong the moment you open it. Pell takes it from you without a word and puts it in the bin.\n\n@event pell_daughters_shortcut\ntitle: The Daughters' Shortcut\nthread: distil_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: potion\ntags: job\nscene: The back room of an apothecary, a student drinking a small cup of something dark while two women watch with professional interest.\ntext: Pell's daughters say there's a shortcut: learn what the draught takes out of a brewer by tasting a little of it first. \"Father hates it,\" says one. \"It works,\" says the other. \"You'll be ill for a day.\"\n> left: Try it on yourself\n  flag distil_shortcut\n  health -small\n  say: It takes the strength out of your knees and then, slowly, gives it back. You spend a day in bed. When you get up, you know exactly where the draught will pull from.\n  next\n> right: Do it Pell's way\n  rel pell +tiny\n  say: You decline. The daughters shrug. Pell, who was listening, corrects a label more firmly than it needed.\n\n@event pell_draught\ntitle: Of Your Own Strength\nthread: distil_quest\ntone: eerie\nart: spell_distil\ntags: job magic\nscene: An apothecary's still at night, a student drawing a single shining drop out of a dark mixture while an old man holds the lamp.\ntext: Pell closes the shop early, sets the old still on the bench and holds the lamp himself. \"Brew it,\" he says. \"I'll count.\" The recipe card lies beside you. {flag(distil_drops) and flag(distil_moonwort) and flag(distil_shortcut): You know every step of it.|There are steps you haven't practised.}\n> left: Brew the draught\n  challenge brew adept- +1 if flag(distil_drops) +1 if flag(distil_moonwort) +1 if flag(distil_shortcut):\n    win:\n      learn_spell purify\n      perform apothecary_clerk +small\n      say: The draught comes down to one shining drop, and as it falls you feel exactly what it took from you, and how to take it again. Pell stops counting. \"Fair,\" he says, twice in one year.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The mixture boils over and takes more out of you than it should. Pell puts the card back in the drawer and writes \"Don't.\" on the front. Next term, perhaps.\n> right: Use Pell's own moonwort stock\n  gold -small\n  challenge brew adept- likely +1 if flag(distil_drops) +1 if flag(distil_moonwort) +1 if flag(distil_shortcut):\n    win:\n      learn_spell purify\n      perform apothecary_clerk +small\n      say: With the good stock, it comes down clean to one drop. You feel it pull, and you feel where it pulls from. Pell writes the cost in his ledger, and your name beside the recipe.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: Even with his best stock it boils over. Pell writes \"Don't.\" on the drawer and the cost in the ledger. Next term, perhaps.\n\n# ---------------- Broom Courier → Tailwind (vigor) ----------------\n\n@thread tailwind_quest\nfrom: quest\nwith: begun=tailwind_chain\nwhen: spell_level(tailwind) == 0\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 2\nclues: lead_tailwind\nname: The jar of wind\npremise: A stoppered jar on the dead-letter shelf: FOR WHOEVER IS FAST ENOUGH.\njournal:\n  flag(tailwind_chain): You took the wind jar down. The loft master wrote your name under one too faded to read.\n  flag(tailwind_fisherman): The old fisherman flew the jar once. Never stop, he said. Stopping's what kills it.\n  flag(tailwind_gulls): You beat the gulls from the jetty to the far bank, and the wind in the jar turned over once.\n  flag(tailwind_broom): You had your broom trued. It flies straight for the first time in living memory.\n  seen(courier_storm_delivery) > 0 and spell_level(tailwind) < 1: The first storm run, a gust took you into the shallows.\n  flag(tailwind_chain) and spell_level(tailwind) >= 1: You crossed the lake in a storm with the wind behind you. It stays when you call it.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(tailwind_chain) and spell_level(tailwind) >= 1\nmembers:\n  start courier_wind_jar\n  part courier_fisherman_waves flag=tailwind_fisherman\n  part courier_race_gulls flag=tailwind_gulls\n  part courier_fix_broom flag=tailwind_broom\n  final courier_storm_delivery\n\n@event courier_wind_jar\ntitle: Wind in a Jar\nthread: tailwind_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: rank(broom_courier) >= 2 and turn <= 44\nart: broom\ntags: job\nscene: A courier loft with a dusty shelf of unclaimed letters, a student holding up a stoppered jar in which a small wind is visibly whirling.\ntext: On the dead-letter shelf, behind a parcel addressed to nobody, is a stoppered jar with a wind in it, going round and round. The label says FOR WHOEVER IS FAST ENOUGH. The loft master says someone flew with it once, before her time.\n> left: Take the jar down\n  flag tailwind_chain\n  find lead_tailwind\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: The jar hums in your hands, and every broom in the loft leans toward you. You strap it on for one loop of the yard, just to see, and come back windblown and breathless. The loft master writes your name on the jar's label, under a name too faded to read.\n  chain_beat = turn\n  queue courier_fisherman_waves now\n> right: Leave it on the shelf\n  perform broom_courier +tiny\n  say: You leave it for whoever is fast enough. You fly your round. The jar's wind stirs the dead letters as you go.\n\n@event courier_fisherman_waves\ntitle: The Fisherman Waves\nthread: tailwind_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: wave\ntags: job\nscene: An old fisherman in a small boat on a grey lake waving up at a student on a broom flying low overhead.\ntext: The old fisherman in the reed beds has never waved at a courier. Today, seeing the jar strapped to your broom, he waves, and beckons you down. \"That jar,\" he says. \"I flew it once. Fastest year of my life.\"\n> left: Land and ask him about it\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag tailwind_fisherman\n      charm +small\n      say: He tells you, slowly, between casts: you don't fly the wind, you let it get behind you and keep going. Never stop. \"Stopping's what kills it,\" he says, and goes back to his line.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: He talks about fish for an hour, then says the light's going, and rows off. You'll have to come back.\n  next\n> right: Wave back and fly on\n  perform broom_courier +small\n  say: You wave and fly on with your round, on time. He watches you go, and doesn't wave again.\n\n@event courier_race_gulls\ntitle: Racing the Gulls\nthread: tailwind_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: broom\ntags: job\nscene: A student on a broom flying flat out over a lake in a cloud of wheeling gulls, letters fluttering in a satchel.\ntext: The gulls over the lake race couriers for fun. {flag(tailwind_fisherman): The fisherman says the jar's wind won't follow you until you've beaten them once, flat out, from the jetty to the far bank.|The jar's label has grown a second line, in the same faded hand: BEAT THE GULLS FIRST. Jetty to far bank, flat out.}\n> left: Race them flat out\n  challenge feat journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      flag tailwind_gulls\n      vigor +small\n      say: You hit the far bank a beak ahead of the lead gull. The wind in the jar turns over once, like a cat settling. The gulls follow you home, offended.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You clip a wave at full speed and skip across the lake like a stone. The gulls are delighted. You are wet to the bone.\n  next\n> right: Fly your round steady\n  perform broom_courier +tiny\n  say: You fly the round steady and deliver everything dry. The gulls lose interest. So does the jar.\n\n@event courier_fix_broom\ntitle: The Broom That Pulls Left\nthread: tailwind_quest\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: broom\ntags: job\nscene: A broom-maker's bench in the village, a craftsman sighting along a broom handle while a student waits with their satchel.\ntext: Your loft broom pulls left, always has. With the jar's wind behind it, it would pull you into the lake. The broom-maker in the village can true it, for a price.\n> left: Pay to have it trued\n  gold -small\n  flag tailwind_broom\n  say: The broom-maker sights along it, tuts, and spends an afternoon on it. It flies straight for the first time in living memory. You miss the pull, a little.\n  next\n> right: Learn to fly it crooked\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You learn to lean right all the time. Your back complains, and the broom keeps pulling left out of habit.\n\n@event courier_storm_delivery\ntitle: The Storm Run\nthread: tailwind_quest\ntone: tense\nart: spell_tailwind\ntags: job magic\nscene: A student on a broom flying through a storm over a dark lake, a jar of wind unstoppered at their belt, lightning behind them.\ntext: A storm is down on the lake, and a parcel must cross it tonight. The loft master looks at the jar at your belt, then at you. \"Nobody else will go,\" she says. {flag(tailwind_fisherman) and flag(tailwind_gulls) and flag(tailwind_broom): You know how to let the wind get behind you.|You've never flown it in weather.}\n> left: Open the jar and go\n  challenge feat adept- +1 if flag(tailwind_fisherman) +1 if flag(tailwind_gulls) +1 if flag(tailwind_broom):\n    win:\n      learn_spell tailwind\n      perform broom_courier +small\n      say: You pull the stopper and the wind gets behind you and doesn't stop. You cross the lake faster than the lightning can find you. The parcel arrives dry. The wind stays with you after, when you call it.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: Halfway across, a gust takes you and you ditch in the shallows. The parcel survives. You barely do. The next storm season, the loft master says, you can try again.\n> right: Wait for the lull, then go\n  mood -small\n  challenge feat adept- likely +1 if flag(tailwind_fisherman) +1 if flag(tailwind_gulls) +1 if flag(tailwind_broom):\n    win:\n      learn_spell tailwind\n      perform broom_courier +small\n      say: You wait, nerves screaming, for the gap in the storm, then open the jar and go. The wind gets behind you and stays. The parcel arrives an hour late and dry, and the wind is yours.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: The lull never comes, and you go anyway and ditch in the shallows. The next storm season, the loft master says.\n"},{"path":"content/events/clubs.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Club events — drawn while the club state is active.\n#  Clubs are joined at the Club Fair (calendar.grim) except the\n#  Midnight Cartographers, who find you.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Dueling Circle ----------------\n\n@event duel_practice_bout\ntitle: Practice Bout\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: duelling\nweight: 14\nart: duel\ntags: club\nscene: A chalk circle on a stone floor under high windows, two students mid-duel, a ring of members cheering.\ntext: Wednesday practice. The captain pairs you with a fourth-year who has a reputation for ending bouts quickly. \"Defence or attack,\" the captain says. \"Pick one and commit.\"\n> left: Hold your ground\n  challenge combat apprentice+ steady:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      renown +small\n      say: You weather everything she throws until she runs out of breath. The captain nods slowly.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You hold your ground very bravely, right up until you're on the floor.\n> right: Go on the attack\n  challenge combat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      renown +small\n      say: Your opening hex catches her off guard. Point to you. The circle whoops.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: She sidesteps and you hex the wall. The wall wins.\n> up[brace]: Brace and let them come\n  vigor +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: You plant your feet and the fourth-year's whole attack breaks on you, each blow softer than the last, until they run out of ideas. The captain marks the bout a draw and looks at you for a long moment.\n\n@event duel_bram_hazing\ntitle: Initiation, Prefect-Style\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: rank(duelling) == 1\ntrack: duelling\nactor: bram\nweight: 12\nart: shield\ntags: club rival\nscene: A burly student in a prefect's sash blocking a corridor, wand twirling, a crowd of members watching nervously.\ntext: Bram Hollis, prefect and the Circle's loudest member, has decided new members need \"toughening up\". He's made you hold a shield-charm against him for an hour. The hour is not up.\n> left: Endure it\n  meet bram\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  rel bram +small\n  say: Your arms shake. You don't drop the charm. Bram grunts, almost approvingly, and finds someone else to torment.\n> right: Drop the shield and duel him\n  meet bram\n  challenge combat vs bram.vigor:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      rel bram -large\n      bond bram rival\n      say: You disarm him in front of everyone. His wand clatters across the floor. The silence is delicious. He will remember this.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel bram -small\n      say: Bram flattens you with a single bored flick. \"Toughening up,\" he reminds the room.\n> up[brace]: Brace and outlast him\n  meet bram\n  vigor +small\n  rel bram +small\n  say: You brace, and the hour stops hurting. At the end Bram is out of breath and you are still standing. \"Section nine,\" he mutters. \"Unusual fortitude.\" He writes it down, which from Bram is a medal.\n\n@event duel_lyra_training\ntitle: Training Partners\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(lyra) and month == 6 and rel(lyra) >= 0\ntrack: duelling\nactor: lyra\nweight: 14\nart: duel\ntags: club rival social\nscene: A misty lawn at dawn, two figures practising wand forms side by side, frost on the grass.\ntext: Lyra finds you after practice. \"The tournament is in two months. You're the only person in this club who doesn't bore me. Train with me. Dawn. Every day. Don't be late.\"\n> left: Decline — you need sleep\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: \"Sleep,\" she repeats, as if it's a foreign word. \"Fine.\"\n> right: Agree\n  health -tiny\n  gain training\n  rel lyra +medium\n  say: She's a merciless coach. Somewhere around the third week, you notice she's started bringing two cups of tea.\n  if knows(lyra.toast):\n    rel lyra +tiny\n    say: Since she can't make toast, you bring it. She eats it at the edge of the training ground without a word, and the next morning there are two slices waiting for you.\n> up[wildfire]: Train at her fire, not her side\n  arcana +small\n  rel lyra +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You tell her you'll come at dawn, but to spar, not to drill. Lyra's eyes narrow, then light up. Every morning for a week you both let the fire run and come in scorched to breakfast.\n\n# ---------------- Choir of the Spheres ----------------\n\n@event choir_solo\ntitle: Solo Auditions\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: great_hall\nweight: 14\nart: music\ntags: club\nscene: A circular choir loft under a painted dome, a single student standing in a pool of light, the rest of the choir floating gently above their seats.\ntext: Auditions for the Midwinter solo. The choirmaster's eyebrows are famously hard to impress. Your throat is dry. Your hands are wet.\n> left: Stay in the back row\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You harmonise from safety, and it's lovely. Somebody else gets the solo and cries with relief.\n> right: Audition\n  challenge perform apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: You hit the high note and the candles in the loft all flare at once. The choirmaster's eyebrows rise one millimetre. You got it.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: Your voice cracks on the high note and a candle goes out in protest.\n\n@event choir_levitation\ntitle: Off-Key Altitude\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: great_hall\nweight: 14\nart: music\ntags: club magic\nscene: A choir hovering a few feet off the floor, all in harmony — except one singer, who has dropped back to the ground with a thump.\ntext: The Choir of the Spheres literally floats when it harmonises. You keep falling out of the air on the third verse. People are starting to notice the thumps.\n> left: Mime the hard bits\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Nobody notices. You float serenely, mouthing words. It's a little bit like fraud.\n> right: Practise every night\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: A week of late nights and you float through the third verse like a feather. The choirmaster gives you a tiny nod.\n\n@event choir_concert\ntitle: The Winter Concert\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: (month == 4 or month == 5)\nplace: great_hall\nweight: 16\nart: bell\ntags: club festival\nscene: The great hall at night, a floating choir lit by candles, the audience below with faces tilted up.\ntext: The winter concert. The great hall is packed. Somewhere in the audience, the Ledger's critic is sharpening her quill.\n> left: Sing for the audience\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You sing and the hall sways. The review calls the choir \"unearthly\". You cut it out and keep it.\n> right: Sing for yourself\n  mood +medium\n  say: You close your eyes and forget anyone is listening. When you open them, you are higher than everyone else.\n\n# ---------------- Skyrace Squad ----------------\n\n@event sky_tryouts\ntitle: Tryouts\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: not flag(sky_team)\nplace: flight_field\nweight: 18\nart: broom\ntags: club\nscene: Brooms streaking around the towers, hoops of light hanging in the sky, a captain with a whistle on the battlements.\ntext: Skyrace tryouts: three laps around the towers, through the hoops, without dying. The captain has a stopwatch and no visible sense of humour.\n> left: Fly clean and steady\n  challenge feat apprentice+ steady:\n    win:\n      flag sky_team\n      find lead_sky_championship\n      renown +small\n      say: You're not the fastest, but you hit every hoop. \"Reserve,\" says the captain. Then: \"...no. Team.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You miss two hoops and nearly a gargoyle. \"Reserves,\" says the captain. \"Keep practising.\"\n> right: Go full speed\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      flag sky_team\n      find lead_sky_championship\n      renown +medium\n      say: You scream through the hoops so fast the stopwatch has to be checked. You're on the team. Obviously.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You go full speed into the north tower. The tower is fine.\n> up[tailwind]: Surge round the towers\n  flag sky_team\n  find lead_sky_championship\n  renown +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: The wind gets behind you on the first lap and you never let it go. Three laps, every hoop, faster each time, and your hands are raw on the handle. The captain looks at the stopwatch, then at you, and writes your name down.\n\n@event sky_crash\ntitle: A Bad Landing\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nplace: flight_field\nweight: 12\nart: broom\ntags: club danger\nscene: A broom snapped in two on the grass, a student sitting up dazed, teammates landing around them.\ntext: Practice. A gust catches you mid-turn, and the ground arrives rather sooner than expected. Your ankle is making an interesting shape.\n> left: Walk it off\n  vigor +tiny\n  chance even:\n    gain injured\n    say: You do not walk it off. You limp it off, for a month.\n  else:\n    say: Tough as old boots. The team cheers when you get back on your broom.\n> right: Straight to the infirmary\n  gold -tiny\n  health +small\n  say: Sister Amaranth sets it in a minute and charges you three crowns for \"carelessness\". Fair.\n\n@event sky_championship\ntitle: Lumenhold vs. Thornwick\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: flag(sky_team) and month >= 6\nplace: flight_field\nweight: 16\nart: trophy\ntags: club festival\nscene: Two teams of broom-riders in blue and green above a packed stadium, the golden finishing hoop glowing at the top of the highest tower.\ntext: The championship against Thornwick Academy. Final lap. You're neck and neck with their captain, and the golden hoop is at the top of the tallest tower.\n> left: Play it safe\n  renown +small\n  say: Thornwick takes the hoop by a whisker. Still: second place in the region. The team carries you off the field anyway.\n> right: The suicide dive\n  challenge feat adept- wild:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      gain famous\n      mood +large\n      say: You dive straight down the tower face and pull up at the last second, through the hoop. The stadium explodes.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      gain injured\n      renown +small\n      say: You dive. You miss. The crowd gasps, then cheers you anyway when you stagger up waving.\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger ----------------\n\n@event ledger_pie_scoop\ntitle: The Case of the Missing Pies\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nactor: nessa\nweight: 14\nart: paper\ntags: club social\nscene: A cramped newspaper office in a tower attic, a printing press clanking, a girl with an ink-stained grin pointing at a map pinned with string.\ntext: Nessa slams a file on your desk. \"Pies. Forty-two of them, gone from the kitchens in a single night. I want the culprit, I want a photo and I want it by Friday.\"\n> left: Write a light-hearted piece\n  charm +tiny\n  say: \"Pie-rates Strike Again.\" Nessa groans at the headline and prints it anyway.\n> right: Actually investigate\n  challenge deceive journeyman-:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel nessa +medium\n      say: The culprit: a family of brownies living in the chimney. Your story runs on the front page. Nessa frames it.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      rel nessa +tiny\n      say: You stake out the kitchens for three nights and catch nothing but a cold. Nessa pats your head.\n> up[quickquill]: Scribble every alibi down\n  rel nessa +small\n  guile +small\n  say: Your quill takes down every alibi in the kitchens word for word. Laid side by side, two of them describe the same pie differently. Nessa reads your notes and goes very quiet, which means it's good.\n\n@event ledger_bram_story\ntitle: The Prefect's Bribes\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(bram)\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nactor: nessa\nweight: 12\nart: scales\ntags: club rival\nscene: An anonymous note spread on a desk under a lamp, next to a sketch of a prefect taking a coin purse from a first-year.\ntext: An anonymous tip: Bram Hollis has been taking bribes from first-years to skip their demerits. You have a witness. Nessa wants your byline on it. \"This is real journalism,\" she says, eyes shining.\n> left: Kill the story\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel nessa -small\n  rel bram +small\n  say: Nessa is disappointed in you. Somehow, Bram finds out you buried it. He nods at you in the corridor. You feel slightly dirty.\n> right: Publish it\n  alignment +tiny\n  renown +medium\n  rel nessa +medium\n  rel bram -large\n  gain watched\n  if rel(bram) < -18:\n    bond bram nemesis\n  say: The story runs. Bram is stripped of his sash for a month. He gets it back. And now he knows exactly who you are.\n> up[quickquill]: Take the witness down word for word\n  renown +small\n  rel nessa +medium\n  rel bram -medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: Your quill takes the witness down word for word, every hesitation included, and the story runs on what was said and nothing more. Bram can't deny a word of it. He tries, at length, in the corridor.\n\n@event ledger_deadline\ntitle: Deadline Night\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nactor: nessa\nweight: 12\nart: candle\ntags: club\nscene: The newspaper office at 3am, crumpled drafts on the floor, two students slumped over typesetting trays.\ntext: The press runs at dawn and half the paper is blank. Nessa is chewing her quill. \"I need you tonight. All night. I'll owe you.\"\n> left: Say you have revision to do\n  study +tiny\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  say: You sleep. The paper comes out with a large drawing of a duck on page four. Nessa doesn't speak to you for a day.\n> right: Pull an all-nighter\n  health -small\n  study -tiny\n  rel nessa +medium\n  say: You write three articles and a horoscope. At dawn Nessa falls asleep on your shoulder, ink on her nose.\n  if knows(nessa.quill):\n    rel nessa +tiny\n    say: You keep an eye on her quill all night, too, and twice catch it inventing a fire in the east wing. The paper is, for once, entirely true.\n\n# ---------------- Verdant Circle ----------------\n\n@event garden_mandrakes\ntitle: The Mandrake Choir\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: verdant\nweight: 14\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: Rows of potted mandrakes in a steamy greenhouse, their little leafy heads opened in song.\ntext: The greenhouse mandrakes have started singing again. Off-key and enthusiastic. The earmuffs are on the hook by the door. Tobias isn't wearing his; he's harmonising.\n> left: Earmuffs on\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You repot six mandrakes in blissful silence. Good honest work.\n> right: Sing along\n  mood +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    health -tiny\n    say: You sing along and promptly faint. Mandrake song is strong stuff. You wake up very relaxed.\n  else:\n    say: The mandrakes adore you. The greenhouse sounds like a pub at closing time.\n\n@event garden_moonflower\ntitle: The Moonflower\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(tobias) and rel(tobias) >= 0\ntrack: verdant\nactor: tobias\nweight: 12\nart: flower\ntags: club romance nature\nscene: A single silver flower opening in the dark of a glass greenhouse, two faces lit by its glow.\ntext: \"The moonflower's going to bloom tonight,\" Tobias says, a little breathless. \"Once every eleven years. I'm staying up to see it. You... could stay too. If you want.\"\n> left: You need sleep\n  rel tobias -tiny\n  say: \"Course. Yeah.\" He tells you about it at breakfast, in detail, with gestures.\n> right: Stay up with him\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +medium\n  spark tobias +small\n  mood +small\n  say: At 2am the flower opens and fills the greenhouse with silver light. Tobias doesn't look at the flower. He looks at you.\n> up[bramble]: Grow a hedge against the wind\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +medium\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: You stay, and when the wind gets up you grow a thorny screen round the bed. The moonflower opens at midnight in perfect stillness. Tobias doesn't say anything for a long time. Then he says, \"Thank you,\" and means all of it.\n\n@event garden_maud_secret\ntitle: The Secret Garden\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: member(verdant) or (month >= 7 and month <= 9)\nactor: maud\nweight: 5\nart: mushroom\ntags: club nature\nscene: An old woman in a battered straw hat holding open a hidden door in an ivy-covered wall; beyond, impossible flowers glow.\ntext: The old groundskeeper beckons you behind the potting shed, to a door in the wall you've walked past a hundred times without seeing. \"You've got the hands for it,\" Old Maud says. \"Want to see what grows where nobody's looking?\"\n> left: Thank her and decline\n  meet maud\n  rel maud +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Suit yourself.\" The door is gone when you look back. You make your class with a minute to spare, oddly pleased with yourself.\n> right: Follow her in\n  meet maud\n  rel maud +medium\n  alchemy +small\n  gain blessed\n  study -tiny\n  say: Inside: glowing ferns, silver bees, a pear tree that hums. Maud tucks a sprig of rosemary into your pocket. \"For luck. Don't lose it.\" You lose the whole afternoon in there, and a class with it.\n\n# ---------------- Stargazers ----------------\n\n@event stars_cocoa_night\ntime: evening night\ntitle: Cocoa on the Roof\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: observatory\nweight: 14\nart: cup\ntags: club social\nscene: Students wrapped in blankets on a flat roof, steaming mugs in hand, telescopes pointed at a velvet sky.\ntext: Stargazers meet on the roof with blankets, telescopes and an illegal quantity of cocoa. Tonight is cloudy. Nobody cares.\n> left: Talk until dawn\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You talk about home, about the future, about whether stars are lonely. Someone falls asleep on your shoulder.\n> right: Do your homework by starlight\n  lore +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: You finish your essay by the light of the cocoa-warmer charm. Productive. A little lonely.\n\n@event stars_meteor_bet\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Meteor Wager\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: observatory\nweight: 12\nart: dice\ntags: club money\nscene: A chalkboard on an observatory wall covered in tally marks and names, a pile of coins on a stool beneath it.\ntext: The club is running a betting pool on tonight's meteor count. The current favourite is 40. The charts suggest something else. Or do they?\n> left: Bet on the charts\n  when: gold >= 8\n  challenge research journeyman-:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      say: Fifty-three meteors. You called fifty-two. Close enough to win the pot.\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      say: The charts were wrong, or you read them wrong. Eight crowns gone.\n> right: Stay out of it\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You watch the meteors for their own sake. Turns out that's the best seat in the house.\n> up[index]: Recall the old meteor charts\n  gold +small\n  say: The observatory's oldest chart book falls open at this exact night a century ago: sixty-one meteors. You bet on sixty. You win, and the club demands to know what you know. You don't tell them.\n\n@event stars_constellation\ntitle: An Unnamed Shape\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nplace: observatory\nweight: 12\nart: star\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A star chart spread on a table, a student's finger tracing a new pattern between unmarked stars.\ntext: Plotting the autumn sky, you notice seven faint stars that form a ring around nothing. The charts call that patch of sky \"empty\". It doesn't look empty.\n> left: Ignore it\n  say: You plot the usual constellations. Your chart gets top marks.\n  study astromancy +tiny\n> right: Investigate\n  lore +medium\n  flag saw_hollow_star\n  say: You spend a week in the archives. Three hundred years ago, the patch was called the Hollow. Then someone scraped the name off every chart.\n\n# ---------------- Midnight Cartographers ----------------\n\n@event carto_invite\ntime: evening night\ntitle: An Invitation at Midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: met(corvin) and rel(corvin) >= 6 and not member(cartographers) and turn >= 4\nactor: corvin\nweight: 20\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A slip of black paper pushed under a dormitory door, a silver ink map on it that rearranges itself as you watch.\ntext: A black card under your door. On it, a map in silver ink that redraws itself while you watch. Corvin's handwriting: \"Midnight. Under the third gargoyle. Bring a candle. Tell no one.\"\n> left: Ignore it\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  say: The card blanks itself the next morning. Corvin doesn't mention it, but he's a little cooler for a week.\n> right: Go\n  health -tiny\n  join cartographers\n  rel corvin +medium\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Under the gargoyle: six students, a stack of maps and a passage you'd never seen. \"Welcome,\" says Corvin, \"to the club that doesn't exist.\"\n\n@event carto_thursday_stairs\ntitle: The Thursday Staircase\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nslot: midnight\ntrack: cartographers\nweight: 14\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A spiral staircase emerging from a blank wall in moonlight, its steps unfolding one by one.\ntext: The Cartographers have heard of a staircase that only exists on Thursdays between midnight and one. Tonight is Thursday. It is 11:58.\n> left: Map it carefully\n  health -tiny\n  lore +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You chart forty-one steps and a landing with a window onto a sky that isn't this one. The map is the club's pride.\n> right: Climb it to the top\n  challenge deceive journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      say: At the top, a forgotten storeroom. Inside: a chest of old academy coins. The club splits it. Corvin cries a little.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: At one o'clock the staircase stops existing. You were on it. You land in a laundry chute.\n\n@event carto_lake_tunnel\ntitle: The Tunnel Under the Lake\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(found_lake_tunnel)\ntrack: cartographers\nweight: 12\nart: door\ntags: club mystery hollowstar\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: A narrow brick tunnel dripping with water, lit by a single candle, ending at a round door carved with a black star.\ntext: A loose stone in the boathouse cellar opens on a tunnel running out under the lake. The bricks drip. At the far end, a round door carved with a black star. It is warm to the touch.\n> left: Mark it and leave\n  flag found_lake_tunnel\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You add the tunnel to the club map with a skull beside it. Corvin adds a second skull, to be safe.\n> right: Press your palm to the star\n  flag found_lake_tunnel\n  alignment -small\n  arcana +small\n  mood -tiny\n  gain hollow_dreams\n  say: The star drinks the warmth out of your hand. Behind the door, something very large shifts in its sleep.\n"},{"path":"content/events/contraband.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Contraband (threads 1, docs/threads-1-plan.md): two banned elixirs (lore brightwater, owls_ink). A bottle waits in\n#  your trunk (`*_bottle`, one of each at most) until you drink, pour or sell it; a dose lasts two months; when it wears\n#  off the craving may take hold (`*_want`, content/states/contraband.grim), more likely with each dose. Every dose risks a\n#  search at dawn. Sources: a classmate's offer, a bag, a first-year's tonic, a classmate's spare before finals, and\n#  Grisk (met at the Newt, then a bottle for a favour at the boathouse at midnight); later the confiscation vault (the\n#  Restricted heist). `elixir_kept` marks a player who has kept or bought a bottle; only they hear Grisk's offer.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The bottle in your trunk ----------------\n\n@event elixir_trunk\ntitle: The Bottle in Your Trunk\ntrigger: auto\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: (has(brightwater_bottle) and not has(brightwater)) or (has(owls_ink_bottle) and not has(owls_ink))\nrepeat: 1\nart: bottle\ntags: danger\nscene: A student kneeling at an open school trunk at night, a small stoppered bottle in a sock in one hand, a candle on the floor beside them.\ntext: {has(brightwater_bottle) and has(owls_ink_bottle): Under your spare robes, wrapped in two socks, the bottles wait, one gold and one black.|{Under your spare robes, wrapped in a sock, the bottle waits.|The bottle is still in your trunk. You have checked on it more often than on your homework.|You know exactly where the bottle is without looking.}} {has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want): Since the last dose wore off, it has been very easy to think about.|{before_finals(): Finals are close.|Nobody knows it's there, you're nearly sure.}}\n> bright: Drink the Brightwater\n  hint: Two good months. After that, maybe not.\n  when: has(brightwater_bottle) and not has(brightwater)\n  lose brightwater_bottle\n  gain brightwater\n  alignment -tiny\n  queue contraband_caught chance slim\n  say: {It tastes of honey and pennies. Within the hour you're warm to your fingertips, and the day ahead looks easy.|You drink it at the window. The tiredness lifts like a lid. You know it's borrowed, and you don't mind yet.|One swallow, and everything that ached stops. You hide the empty bottle at the bottom of the trunk.}\n> ink: Drink the Owl's Ink\n  hint: Two sharp months, exams included. After that, maybe not.\n  when: has(owls_ink_bottle) and not has(owls_ink)\n  lose owls_ink_bottle\n  gain owls_ink\n  alignment -tiny\n  queue contraband_caught chance slim\n  say: {It's bitter as a burnt quill. By morning your head is a lamp-lit room, every shelf in order, and magic hums in your fingers.|You drink it standing up. The words on your desk sharpen, line by line, like a page coming into focus.|Black, bitter, and then clear. You read three chapters before breakfast and remember every word of them.}\n> pour_bright: Pour the Brightwater away\n  when: has(brightwater_bottle)\n  lose brightwater_bottle\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You tip the gold out of the window into the ivy. The ivy, next morning, is a remarkable colour.|You pour the gold down the washbasin and run the tap for a long time afterwards. You feel lighter, and worse.|Out of the window, every gold drop. You watch it go and want it back the whole time.}\n> pour_ink: Pour the Owl's Ink away\n  when: has(owls_ink_bottle)\n  lose owls_ink_bottle\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You tip the black out of the window into the ivy. By morning the leaves it touched have curled up tight.|You pour the black down the washbasin, and it stains the porcelain grey. You scrub until it's gone.|Out of the window, every black drop. Finals will be exactly as hard as they are.}\n> wait: Not yet\n  say: {You wrap it up again and close the lid.|Not tonight. You put the trunk back under the bed and lie awake knowing where it is.|You leave it where it is, for now. For a better reason, later.}\n\n# ---------------- Where bottles come from ----------------\n\n@event elixir_offered_bright\ntitle: Something for the Grey Days\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 4 and not flag(elixir_known) and not off_campus\nweight: 8\ncast:\n  other: any where met(other) and tagged(other, student)\nart: bottle\ntags: danger social\nscene: A school corridor, one student pressing a small cloudy-gold bottle into another student's hand, glancing down the corridor for prefects.\ntext: {other} has been glowing for a week: sleeping well, laughing loudly, top of every drill. In the corridor {they} put{s} a small cloudy-gold bottle in your hand. \"Brightwater. From the village. It's only banned because it works.\"\n> left: Keep it, for a grey day\n  flag elixir_known elixir_kept\n  gain brightwater_bottle\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You wrap it in a sock and bury it in your trunk. It feels heavier there than it did in your hand.\n> right: Give it back\n  flag elixir_known\n  rel other -tiny\n  say: \"Suit yourself,\" says {other}, and pockets it, a little stung. For a day or two {they} look{s} at you as if you'd said something unkind.\n\n@event elixir_borrowed_bag\ntitle: Not Your Bottle\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 6 and not off_campus\nweight: 6\ncast:\n  other: any where met(other) and tagged(other, student)\nart: bottle\ntags: danger social\nscene: A student kneeling over a borrowed satchel, holding up a small black bottle wrapped in a sock, with an uneasy face.\ntext: You borrow {other}'s satchel to carry your books to a lecture, and at the bottom of it, wrapped in a sock, is a small black bottle with a village label. Owl's Ink. Everyone knows what it is, and that it's banned.\n> left: Keep it\n  flag elixir_known elixir_kept\n  gain owls_ink_bottle\n  alignment -small\n  say: You slip it into your own pocket and give the satchel back with a smile. {other} doesn't say a word. Neither, it turns out, do you, for a week.\n> right: Give it back, and say why\n  flag elixir_known\n  rel other +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You hand it back in private. {other} goes red, then quiet, then thanks you, which is the hardest part to listen to.\n\n@event elixir_first_year\ntitle: A Tonic from Home\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: turn >= 8 and not off_campus\nweight: 6\nart: bottle\ntags: danger social\nscene: A first-year's dormitory windowsill with a cloudy-gold bottle on it, the first-year asleep on top of the covers at midday.\ntext: A first-year on your corridor has been dancing on the stairs at midnight and sleeping through breakfast. On their windowsill is their \"tonic from home\": cloudy gold, a village label. You know exactly what it is, and so, by the look of the stairs, does everyone else.\n> left: Buy it off them, quietly\n  flag elixir_known elixir_kept\n  gold -small\n  gain brightwater_bottle\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You pay a fair price and tell them where it really comes from. They look relieved to be rid of it. Now it's in your trunk instead of theirs.\n> right: Walk them to the infirmary\n  flag elixir_known\n  meet amaranth\n  rel amaranth +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Sister Amaranth takes the bottle in one hand and the first-year in the other. \"Good. Next time, come sooner,\" she tells you. You spend the evening on a hard bench, worrying, until you hear they're all right.\n\n@event elixir_finals_spare\ntitle: Two Bottles Too Many\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: before_finals() and not has(owls_ink_bottle) and not off_campus\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 6\ncast:\n  other: any where met(other) and tagged(other, student)\nart: bottle\ntags: danger study money\nscene: A student in a dormitory doorway holding out a small black bottle in an open palm, a second identical bottle in the other hand, looking embarrassed.\ntext: {~{other} catches you on the dormitory stairs, pale and sheepish. In a panic about finals {they} bought two bottles of Owl's Ink from someone behind the refectory, and now want{s} rid of the second. \"At cost,\" {they} say{s}. \"Please. I can't have both in my trunk.\"|{other} sits down next to you in the library and slides a black bottle across the desk behind a book. A cousin in the village sent two. \"I only need one to pass,\" {they} whisper{s}. \"At cost. Please.\"|{other} finds you after supper with a black bottle in each coat pocket, bought off a fifth-year at the Newt at a terrible price. \"I panicked,\" {they} say{s}. \"Take one off me at cost? Please?\"}\n> left: Take it off {their} hands\n  flag elixir_known elixir_kept\n  gold -small\n  gain owls_ink_bottle\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {~The coins go one way and the bottle the other, on the stairs, both of you looking at your shoes. {other} looks lighter for it. You don't.|You slip the bottle inside your book and the coins under {other}'s. Neither of you reads another word that evening.|You take the bottle from {other}'s pocket yourself, so nobody sees it change hands. The price was terrible; you pay it anyway.}\n> right: Tell {them} to pour them both away\n  flag elixir_known\n  rel other -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {~\"Both?\" says {other}, as if you'd asked {them} to pour away a term's work. {other} goes off to think about it, and won't quite meet your eye for a week.|{other} puts the bottle back behind the book without a word, and spends the rest of the evening reading the same page.|\"At that price?\" says {other}, appalled, and goes off with a bottle in each pocket, and looks at you sideways at breakfast.}\n\n@event elixir_finals_buyers\ntitle: Customers Before Finals\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: before_finals() and has(owls_ink_bottle) and not off_campus\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\ncast:\n  buyer: any where met(buyer) and tagged(buyer, student)\nart: coin\ntags: danger money\nscene: A student cornered on a stair landing by a desperate classmate holding out a purse.\ntext: {Word has got round that you have a bottle of Owl's Ink. {buyer} corners you on the stairs, purse already open. \"Name your price. I'm going to fail otherwise.\"|{buyer} has heard about your bottle too, and doesn't haggle.|Finals, and {buyer} on the stairs, purse in hand. Somehow they always know.}\n> left: Sell it\n  lose owls_ink_bottle\n  gold +medium\n  alignment -small\n  queue contraband_caught chance slim\n  say: {You sell it. {buyer} holds the bottle like a holy relic and runs. You count the coins twice and don't feel richer.|The bottle changes hands on the landing. Your purse is heavy, and so, for a day or two, is something else.|You sell, and {buyer} thanks you as if you'd done something kind. You haven't.}\n> right: Keep it for your own exams\n  rel buyer -tiny\n  say: {\"It's spoken for,\" you say. {buyer}'s face falls, and stays fallen for a week.|You keep it. {buyer} calls you something on the way down the stairs, not quite quietly enough.|No sale. {buyer} doesn't believe you need it more, and says so.}\n\n# ---------------- Grisk's supply: met at the Newt, then the boathouse at midnight ----------------\n\n@event grisk_elixir_offer\ntitle: Grisk's Better Bottles\nplace: gilded_newt\nslot: evening\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: grisk\nwhen: flag(elixir_kept) and not flag(grisk_supply) and not flag(grisk_gone)\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 12\nart: bottle\ntags: money danger\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat at a corner table of a candlelit tavern, a gold bottle and a black one standing on the table beside his ledger.\ntext: {Grisk beckons you to his corner table, where a gold bottle and a black one stand beside his ledger. \"I hear you've a taste for the village's better remedies,\" he purrs. \"I can keep you in them. Not only for money. Between friends, favours keep better. The boathouse jetty, any midnight.\"|Grisk's corner table, and the two bottles again. \"The offer keeps,\" he says, \"like the bottles.\"|The corner table, the bottles, the little bow from his chair. \"Still a friend in need of friends?\" he asks.}\n> left: Shake his hand\n  meet grisk\n  flag grisk_supply\n  find lead_grisk_bottles\n  alignment -small\n  gain brightwater_bottle\n  say: {His hand is dry and very warm. He slides the gold bottle across the table. \"A sample,\" he says. \"Between friends, the first is a gift. The rest, at the jetty.\"|You shake. Grisk writes something in his ledger with a flourish, and hands you the gold bottle \"for goodwill\". \"Midnight, the jetty,\" he says. \"I keep.\"|You shake on it. He tucks the black bottle away and gives you the gold one. \"The black costs a favour,\" he says. \"Come down to the jetty when you want one.\"}\n> right: Not interested\n  meet grisk\n  say: {\"The offer keeps,\" says Grisk, unoffended, and turns back to his ledger.|He bows you away from the table without getting up. \"Another night, then. I'm a patient friend.\"|\"Of course,\" says Grisk. He doesn't believe you, and he lets you see it.}\n\n@event grisk_elixir_favour\ntitle: A Bottle for a Favour\nplace: boathouse\nslot: midnight\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: grisk\nwhen: flag(grisk_supply) and not flag(grisk_gone)\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 12\nart: bottle\ntags: money danger\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat on a midnight jetty holding up a small bottle between two fingers, a ledger open on a crate beside him.\ntext: Grisk has a bottle and a favour, and they come as a pair. {&\"Watch the jetty for an hour, and see nothing.\"|\"Keep a crate under your bed till Thursday.\"|\"A kind word to a prefect, about a friend of mine.\"} {before_finals() or has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want): \"And a few crowns on delivery,\" he adds. \"{before_finals(): Ink is dear this close to finals.|You look like someone who needs a friend, and friends are dear.}\"|He smiles. \"Small things.\"}\n> ink: Owl's Ink, for the favour\n  hint: A few crowns more before finals, and when he knows you need it.\n  when: not has(owls_ink_bottle)\n  meet grisk\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      gain owls_ink_bottle\n      if before_finals() or has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want):\n        gold -small\n      say: {&You stand on the jetty for an hour and see nothing, very carefully. At dawn there's a black bottle on your windowsill, with a note that says \"Friends.\"|The crate sits under your bed till Thursday, humming faintly. Then it's gone, and a black bottle is where it stood.|You put in Grisk's word with a prefect, and it lands. The black bottle arrives in your shoe, which you don't like thinking about.}\n    lose:\n      gain watched\n      mood -small\n      say: {&A prefect's lantern comes down the lawn while you're watching the jetty. You're in their notebook before you've thought of a reason. No bottle; Grisk doesn't pay for failures.|A prefect doing rounds finds the crate under your bed and asks what's in it. You don't know, which is the worst answer. The crate goes, and no bottle comes.|The prefect hears you out, then asks who put you up to it. You don't say, and your name goes in the notebook anyway. Grisk sends only his regrets.}\n> bright: Brightwater, for the favour\n  hint: A few crowns more before finals, and when he knows you need it.\n  when: not has(brightwater_bottle)\n  meet grisk\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      gain brightwater_bottle\n      if before_finals() or has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want):\n        gold -small\n      say: {&You stand on the jetty for an hour and see nothing, very carefully. At dawn there's a gold bottle on your windowsill, with a note that says \"Friends.\"|The crate sits under your bed till Thursday, humming faintly. Then it's gone, and a gold bottle is where it stood.|You put in Grisk's word with a prefect, and it lands. The gold bottle arrives in your shoe, which you don't like thinking about.}\n    lose:\n      gain watched\n      mood -small\n      say: {&A prefect's lantern comes down the lawn while you're watching the jetty. You're in their notebook before you've thought of a reason. No bottle; Grisk doesn't pay for failures.|A prefect doing rounds finds the crate under your bed and asks what's in it. You don't know, which is the worst answer. The crate goes, and no bottle comes.|The prefect hears you out, then asks who put you up to it. You don't say, and your name goes in the notebook anyway. Grisk sends only his regrets.}\n> done: Tell him you're done\n  meet grisk\n  unflag grisk_supply\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {&\"Everyone is, eventually,\" says Grisk pleasantly, and closes his ledger. \"Until they aren't.\" You walk back up the lawn without a bottle, and it's the longest walk you've had.|Grisk shrugs. \"My door doesn't lock,\" he says. You go back to bed shaking, and proud of it.|\"Done,\" you say. Grisk bows, as if you'd paid him a compliment, and turns the lamp down.}\n> none: Not this month\n  say: {&Grisk tucks the bottle away. \"Next month, then.\"|You shake your head. Grisk makes a small note in his ledger, as if you'd ordered something.|\"Of course,\" says Grisk. \"I keep.\"}\n\n# ---------------- Caught, and noticed ----------------\n\n@event contraband_caught\ntitle: A Search at Dawn\ntrigger: queued\nslot: morning\ndefer: off_campus\nrepeat: 1\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: eye\ntags: crisis\nscene: A dormitory corridor at dawn, two prefects in red sashes going through an open school trunk, students in nightclothes watching from their doors.\ntext: The prefects are going down your corridor at dawn, trunk by trunk, sniffing bottles. {has(brightwater_bottle) or has(owls_ink_bottle): There is a bottle in yours.|Your trunk is clean, nearly, but you know what has been through your hands lately, and the prefect sniffing at the lid looks as if they know it too.}\n> left: Bluff it out\n  challenge deceive journeyman:\n    win:\n      guile +tiny\n      say: You're half asleep and mildly offended, and they believe both. The prefects move on to the next trunk. You sit on the end of your bed until your heart slows down.\n    lose:\n      gain watched\n      mood -small\n      if has(brightwater_bottle) or has(owls_ink_bottle):\n        flag elixir_confiscated\n        if has(owls_ink_bottle):\n          flag confiscated_owls_ink\n        if has(brightwater_bottle):\n          flag confiscated_brightwater\n        gain detention\n        say: They find it in under a minute. The bottle goes into a bag labelled for the confiscation vault, your name goes into a notebook, and the whole corridor watches you go red.\n      else:\n        say: There's nothing in the trunk to find, but your face gives you away. You get a warning, a line in the prefect's notebook, and a long look from the whole corridor.\n      lose brightwater_bottle\n      lose owls_ink_bottle\n> right: Own up before they look\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood -small\n  if has(brightwater_bottle) or has(owls_ink_bottle):\n    flag elixir_confiscated\n    if has(owls_ink_bottle):\n      flag confiscated_owls_ink\n    if has(brightwater_bottle):\n      flag confiscated_brightwater\n    say: You hand it over before they reach your trunk. The prefect bags it for the confiscation vault, looks at you for a long moment, and writes only a warning. It still stings.\n  else:\n    say: You tell them before they ask. With nothing to confiscate, the prefect writes only a warning, and gives you a long look. It still stings.\n  lose brightwater_bottle\n  lose owls_ink_bottle\n\n@event contraband_friend_notices\ntitle: You Don't Look Right\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: has(brightwater) or has(owls_ink) or has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want)\nrepeat: 4\ncast:\n  friend: any where met(friend) and rel(friend) >= 15 and tagged(friend, student)\nper: friend\nart: eye\ntags: social danger\nscene: Two students on a stone bench in an empty corridor, one holding the other's hand and looking hard at their face.\ntext: {has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want): {~{friend} catches your wrist at supper and turns your hand over. \"You're shaking,\" {they} say{s}. \"What is it?\"|{friend} sits down beside you without a word and looks at your hands until you put them in your lap. \"What is it?\"}|{~\"You've been too bright for weeks,\" {friend} says at supper. \"Nobody's that well. What is it?\"|{friend} watches you finish a week's reading in an evening and says, quietly, \"That isn't you. What is it?\"}}\n> left: Tell {them} the truth\n  rel friend +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You tell {friend} about the bottle. {they} listen{s} without interrupting, and at the end only say{s}, \"All right. Now I know. Sister Amaranth sees this every year, you know.\"|You tell {them} all of it. {friend} doesn't lecture, and doesn't leave, and mentions the infirmary only once.}\n> right: Laugh it off\n  rel friend -small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {~\"Exams,\" you say, and laugh. {friend} lets the subject go, slowly, and doesn't believe you.|\"I'm fine.\" {friend} looks at you for a long moment. \"All right,\" {they} say{s}. It isn't.}\n\n# ---------------- Withdrawal: the occasional bad day (queued by the craving states; never on a break or away) ----------------\n\n@pool withdrawal_brightwater\ntrigger: queued\nclass: incident\nwhen: has(brightwater_want)\ndefer: off_campus or break\nrepeat: 2\n\n@pool withdrawal_owls_ink\ntrigger: queued\nclass: incident\nwhen: has(owls_ink_want)\ndefer: off_campus or break\nrepeat: 2\n\n@event wd_bright_grey\ntitle: The First Thought\npool: withdrawal_brightwater\ntype: menu\ntone: grim\nart: rain\ntags: danger\nscene: A student lying awake in bed at dawn, eyes on the school trunk at the foot of the bed, one hand already reaching for the lid.\ntext: Your first thought on waking is the gold bottle, and so is your second. The Brightwater wore off weeks ago. Everything since has needed a little more pushing than it should.\n> rise: Get up anyway\n  mood -small\n  study +tiny\n  say: You get up, dress, and go to every lecture, thinking about the trunk the whole way. It counts. It doesn't feel like it counts.\n> bed: Stay in bed\n  health +small\n  study -small\n  say: You sleep through the morning and most of the afternoon. You miss two lectures and feel no better for it, only rested.\n> dose: Drink the bottle in your trunk\n  when: has(brightwater_bottle)\n  lose brightwater_bottle\n  gain brightwater\n  alignment -tiny\n  queue contraband_caught chance slim\n  say: One swallow, and the pushing stops. Everything is easy again at once. You hate how easy that was.\n\n@event wd_bright_shakes\ntitle: Hands That Won't Hold Still\npool: withdrawal_brightwater\ntype: menu\ntone: tense\nart: hand\ntags: danger study\nscene: A student at a laboratory bench, their hand trembling so that the liquid in a glass vial shivers.\ntext: In the practical your hands won't hold still. The vial shivers; the measure goes over the line, twice. The student at the next bench pretends not to see.\n> push: Push through it\n  health -small\n  study +tiny\n  say: You grip your wrist with your other hand and finish the measure, badly. Afterwards you sit in the corridor until the shaking stops.\n> sit: Ask to sit it out\n  mood -small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You ask to sit out. The lecturer looks at your hands, then at your face, and says yes too quickly.\n> dose: Drink the bottle in your trunk\n  when: has(brightwater_bottle)\n  lose brightwater_bottle\n  gain brightwater\n  alignment -tiny\n  queue contraband_caught chance slim\n  say: You leave the practical \"for some air\" and drink it in the washroom. Your hands go still. Your face in the mirror doesn't look like a winner's.\n\n@event wd_bright_grisk\ntitle: Grisk Knows\npool: withdrawal_brightwater\nslot: night\ntype: menu\ntone: grim\nactor: grisk\nwhen: met(grisk) and not flag(grisk_gone) and not has(brightwater_bottle)\nart: bottle\ntags: danger money\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting uninvited on a student's bed at night, a gold bottle held up to the candle.\ntext: Grisk is sitting on your bed. You didn't leave the window open. He holds a gold bottle up to the candle and turns it. \"You look peaky,\" he says kindly. \"I have just the thing.\" {flag(grisk_supply): \"The price has gone up, of course. Everything has.\"|\"Not a friend's price, I'm afraid. You never did come to see me.\"}\n> buy: Pay what he asks\n  meet grisk\n  gold -medium\n  gain brightwater_bottle\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You pay. He counts it twice, sets the bottle on your pillow like a mint, and leaves by the window.\n> no: Show him the window\n  meet grisk\n  mood -small\n  say: \"Another night,\" says Grisk, quite unbothered, and goes out the way he came. You don't sleep, but you didn't buy it.\n\n@event wd_ink_blank\ntitle: A Blank Page\npool: withdrawal_owls_ink\ntype: menu\ntone: grim\nart: paper\ntags: danger study\nscene: A student in a lecture hall staring at a completely blank page, quill hovering, the lecturer's words filling the board.\ntext: In the lecture, the words go past you like carriages. Two months ago you could have recited them back. Now your page is blank, and the lecturer is already on the next board.\n> stare: Sit it out, blank\n  study -small\n  say: You sit through the hour writing nothing. At the end you have the date at the top of the page, and that's all.\n> ask: Admit you can't follow\n  mood -small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You put your hand up and say you've lost the thread. The lecturer is kind about it, which is worse, and goes back over it slowly, for you.\n> dose: Drink the bottle in your trunk\n  when: has(owls_ink_bottle)\n  lose owls_ink_bottle\n  gain owls_ink\n  alignment -tiny\n  queue contraband_caught chance slim\n  say: You drink it behind the lecture hall and go back in. The board snaps into focus mid-sentence. You copy it all, and your hand doesn't feel like yours.\n\n@event wd_ink_empty_well\ntitle: The Well Runs Dry\npool: withdrawal_owls_ink\ntype: menu\ntone: grim\nart: spell_moonwell\ntags: danger magic\nscene: A student in a practice room, hand outstretched, a spell fizzling out into a few weak sparks.\ntext: You reach for your magic in the practice room, and there's nothing there: not empty, exactly, but dry, like a well at the end of a long summer. It used to be so easy, on the ink.\n> force: Force it\n  mana -medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: You wring the spell out of yourself. It comes, thin and late. You sit down on the floor afterwards without meaning to.\n> wait: Leave it for today\n  study -small\n  say: You put your hands in your pockets and leave the practice for another day. The day after, it's still dry.\n> dose: Drink the bottle in your trunk\n  when: has(owls_ink_bottle)\n  lose owls_ink_bottle\n  gain owls_ink\n  alignment -tiny\n  queue contraband_caught chance slim\n  say: One dose and the well is full again, brimming, easy. You stand there with sparks on your fingers, ashamed of how good it feels.\n\n@event wd_ink_dreams\ntitle: Ink in Your Sleep\npool: withdrawal_owls_ink\nslot: night\ntype: menu\ntone: eerie\nart: moon\ntags: danger\nscene: A student sitting up in bed in the dark, sheets tangled, staring at their hands as if expecting ink on them.\ntext: You dream of black ink rising in a well, over your feet, your knees, your mouth, and you wake with the taste of a burnt quill. The candle has burned down, and the castle is very quiet.\n> lie: Lie awake till dawn\n  health -small\n  say: You lie and watch the window go from black to grey. By breakfast you feel scraped thin.\n> walk: Walk the corridors till it passes\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  queue night_suspicion chance slim\n  say: You walk the dark corridors in your socks until your head clears. A lantern turns a far corner; you don't wait to see whose.\n> dose: Drink the bottle in your trunk\n  when: has(owls_ink_bottle)\n  lose owls_ink_bottle\n  gain owls_ink\n  alignment -tiny\n  queue contraband_caught chance slim\n  say: You drink it in the dark. The dream doesn't come back. In the morning you can't decide whether that was rescue or surrender.\n\n# ---------------- The way back (signposted in the craving states' desc and by a friend) ----------------\n\n@event amaranth_quit\ntitle: Sister Amaranth's Remedy\nplace: infirmary\nclass: incident\ntone: warm\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 20\nart: cup\ntags: danger\nscene: A stern healer in an infirmary looking at a student over a rolled bandage, a bitter-looking cup of medicine on the table between them.\ntext: Sister Amaranth looks at you once, the way she looks at a fever chart. \"Sit,\" she says, still rolling a bandage. \"I've seen that face before. Brightwater, or the ink? Don't answer. Drink this every morning for a week. It's foul. It works.\"\n> left: Take the remedy\n  meet amaranth\n  rel amaranth +small\n  lose brightwater_want\n  lose owls_ink_want\n  lose brightwater_bottle\n  lose owls_ink_bottle\n  health -small\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: A week of bitter mornings, and her hand held out each time for any bottle you still have. On the eighth day the world comes back in colour. \"Drink it all,\" she says, \"and don't come back for the same thing.\"\n> right: \"It's just a cold, Sister.\"\n  meet amaranth\n  rel amaranth -tiny\n  say: \"Hm,\" says Sister Amaranth, and goes back to her bandage. \"The door's open. It always is.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/crisis.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Crises: queued by resources hitting zero (see core/rules.grim)\n#  and by debt. These are the \"game over\" gates — each gives the\n#  player one way out, at a price, before ending the run.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event crisis_collapse\ntitle: Collapse\nclass: crisis\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 90\nart: cross\ntags: crisis\nscene: A narrow infirmary bed under a high arched window, a healer's lamp floating beside it.\ntext: {collapse_cures == 0: The world tilts. When it rights itself you are in the infirmary, and Sister Amaranth is looking at you over her spectacles. \"You have been running your body like a borrowed broom,\" she says.|{collapse_cures == 1: The world tilts again, harder. You wake in the infirmary bed you know too well, and Sister Amaranth isn't looking over her spectacles this time; she's taken them off. \"Twice,\" she says. \"The body keeps count, even when you don't. The cure's dearer now, because you are worse.\"|You come round in the infirmary with no memory of falling. Sister Amaranth is already mixing the black tonic, the one she keeps on the top shelf. \"Again,\" she says, flatly. \"Each time it takes more to bring you back, and costs more. One of these times it won't.\"}}\n> left: {collapse_cures == 0: Pay for the full cure (30 crowns)|{collapse_cures == 1: Pay for the dearer cure (60 crowns)|Pay for the black tonic (120 crowns)}}\n  if collapse_cures == 0:\n    gold -large\n    say: Three foul tonics and two days' sleep later, you feel almost human.\n  elif collapse_cures == 1:\n    gold -huge\n    say: Six foul tonics and four days' sleep later, you feel almost human, and you can still taste the third one.\n  else:\n    cure_dire\n    say: The black tonic tastes of tar and lightning. It takes a week to work and you remember none of it, but your body comes back to you, sullenly, like a dog that's been kicked.\n  collapse_cures += 1\n  health +huge\n> right: Ask to rest instead\n  if collapses >= 1:\n    end ending_sent_home\n  else:\n    health +huge\n    study -large\n    mood -small\n    collapses += 1\n    lose sick\n    say: A long, dull fortnight in bed. Sister Amaranth warns you: \"Next time, I write to your family.\"\n\n@event crisis_despair\ntitle: The Grey\nclass: crisis\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 90\nart: rain\ntags: crisis\n# Mood at zero (rules.grim: mood on_zero). Feedback 2026-10-16: the card says so, and reaching out is no longer a free\n# cure: it brings Mood back only after a breakdown that costs something else (one of four, at random).\nscene: A student sitting on the edge of a dormitory bed, rain on the window, a packed trunk by the door.\ntext: Your spirits have hit bottom. Everything has gone grey: food tastes of nothing, lectures are noise, and you have packed your trunk twice this week and unpacked it once. It would be so easy to take the next barge and simply stop.\n> left: Reach out to someone\n  hint: Spirits come back, after a night that breaks something else.\n  if bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0 or met(maud) or despairs < 1:\n    chance even:\n      chance even:\n        study -small\n        mood -tiny\n        say: First you cry, properly, the way you haven't since you were small, until the window goes pale. Then you sleep straight through the morning's lectures, and wake ashamed of it.\n      else:\n        mana -large\n        say: You walk out to the empty practice field and cast until there is nothing left to cast: fire, wind, light, anything. You come back hollow and shaking, and lighter.\n    else:\n      chance even:\n        health -small\n        renown -small\n        say: You walk the grounds in the rain all night without a cloak, round and round the lawns. A prefect finds you at dawn, soaked to the skin, and the story is round the dormitory by breakfast.\n      else:\n        gold -medium\n        mood -tiny\n        say: You throw your inkwell at the wall, then the chair, then the little mirror. Afterwards you sit among the pieces, oddly calm. Replacing it all costs a small fortune.\n    if bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0:\n      mood +huge\n      health +small\n      say: In the morning you knock on a friend's door. They don't ask questions. They just make tea, and stay.\n    elif met(maud):\n      mood +huge\n      health +small\n      rel maud +small\n      say: Old Maud finds you in the gardens and puts a trowel in your hand. \"Dig,\" she says. You dig. It helps more than it should.\n    else:\n      mood +huge\n      health +small\n      despairs += 1\n      say: Afterwards you talk to the night porter, of all people. He tells you about his own first year. It's enough, for now.\n  else:\n    end ending_quiet_departure\n> right: Take the next barge\n  hint: Spirits back for now; a second time, you go.\n  if despairs >= 1:\n    end ending_quiet_departure\n  else:\n    mood +large\n    despairs += 1\n    say: You get as far as the landing. The barge is late. By the time it arrives, you have decided to stay one more month.\n\n@event crisis_probation\ntitle: The Disciplinary Board\nclass: crisis\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 90\nart: scales\ntags: crisis\nscene: Three professors behind a high bench in a dim chamber, the headmistress in the centre, hands folded.\ntext: Two terms of failing marks. You are summoned before the Disciplinary Board: three professors and the headmistress, who looks at you with an expression of deep, patient disappointment. {flag(board_reprieve): They have seen you here before.|}\n> left: Plead your case\n  if flag(board_reprieve):\n    end ending_expelled\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate journeyman likely:\n      win:\n        flag board_reprieve\n        study +large\n        gain probation\n        say: You speak honestly and well. They grant you one reprieve, and a study plan that fills every evening. \"There will not be a second,\" says the headmistress, pleasantly.\n      lose:\n        end ending_expelled\n> right: Promise to work harder\n  if flag(board_reprieve):\n    end ending_expelled\n  else:\n    flag board_reprieve\n    study +large\n    gain probation\n    mood -medium\n    say: \"Promises are cheap,\" says Professor Thorne. \"We'll take this one. Only this one.\" A reprieve, a study plan in his own hand, and a very long term ahead.\n\n# The first missed payment (M22): a reminder, with interest.\n@event grisk_reminder\ntitle: A Friendly Reminder\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 70\nart: letter\ntags: money\nthread: debt\nscene: A small, very neat note pinned to a dormitory door with a brass tack, the handwriting tiny and precise.\ntext: A note on your door, in a tidy goblin hand: \"A payment was missed. Mister Grisk is a patient businessman. Mister Grisk charges interest on his patience.\" Underneath, smaller: \"Pay something, or another month will be added.\"\n> left: Scrape together what you can\n  if gold > 0:\n    gold = 0\n    debt_missed = 0\n    mood -small\n    say: You empty your purse into an envelope and slide it under his door. It isn't twelve crowns. It is, says the note in reply, \"a gesture. Noted.\"\n  else:\n    debt_paid -= 1\n    mood -small\n    say: There is nothing to put in the envelope. You write \"next month, I promise\" on a scrap and slide that under his door instead. The reply is a single line, \"Promises are added to the ledger,\" and another month is.\n> right: Put it out of your mind\n  debt_paid -= 1\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You put the note in a drawer. Another month goes on the ledger, and next month the knock on your door will be louder.\n\n@event grisk_collectors\ntitle: Grisk's Collectors\nclass: crisis\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\npriority: 80\nart: chain\ntags: crisis money\nthread: debt\nscene: Two large trolls in ill-fitting waistcoats looming in a dormitory doorway, one holding a ledger.\ntext: Two trolls in waistcoats are waiting outside your dormitory. \"Mister Grisk,\" says the larger one, \"is disappointed.\" The smaller one cracks his knuckles, which sounds like a rockslide.\n> left: Hand over everything you have\n  gold = 0\n  debt_missed = 0\n  mood -medium\n  if debt_paid >= 3:\n    lose in_debt\n    say: They count your purse twice and declare the debt settled. \"Pleasure,\" says the large one, meaning it.\n  else:\n    debt_paid += 1\n    renown -small\n    say: They take every coin and your good boots, in front of half the corridor. \"Partial,\" they say, and leave.\n> right: Run\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      debt_missed = 1\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You escape across the rooftops. They'll be back, but not this month.\n    lose:\n      health -huge\n      debt_missed = 0\n      say: You do not escape. The trolls are surprisingly gentle about breaking only one rib.\n      gain injured\n"},{"path":"content/events/detention.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Detention (threads 1, docs/threads-1-plan.md): while you have detention, a term month's two visits are served here\n#  instead of on the map (`@state detention`: afternoon=detention evening=detention). Canon (lore demerits): detention\n#  is scrubbing pans or cataloguing contraband. Only those two slots draw these cards; the punishment is the visits\n#  lost, and each card is still a choice.\n# ============================================================\n\n@pool detention\nclass: routine\nwhen: has(detention)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\n\n@event det_vault_catalogue\ntitle: Cataloguing the Vault\npool: detention\ntone: light\nart: lock\ntags: mystery study\nscene: A cramped stone vault crammed with confiscated student contraband, a student with a ledger and a candle, a goblin-shaped alarm glaring from above the door.\ntext: {Detention in the confiscation vault, among three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student, and a ledger nobody has kept properly in decades.|Today's shelf is the cursed dice. The goblin alarm over the door watches you take down the first box.|The goblin alarm over the door remembers you, and says so, loudly, until the prefect hushes it.} {A fanged flying disc snaps at your sleeve.|A goblet of endless soup is still, after all these years, gently simmering.|A box marked DO NOT OPEN is rattling politely.}\n> left: Catalogue it shelf by shelf\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  flag vault_catalogued\n  say: {You write up forty things by candlelight, and by the end you know where the vault keeps what. Your eyes feel sandpapered.|Shelf by shelf. You find where this year's confiscations go, by the door, and where nothing has been touched since before Maud was a student. Your wrist aches.|Today it's the love potions, shelf after shelf of them. It's dull work, and you now know the vault better than the prefects do.}\n> right: Read what students left behind\n  mood +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {Some of the confiscated things are diaries. You read one instead of cataloguing, and laugh until the goblin coughs, and feel bad about it after.|A whole folder of notes passed in class, taken and never given back. \"Meet me after Astromancy.\" You read them all. The ledger doesn't move an inch.|A love letter, taken in a lecture and never given back. You read it twice, feel like a thief, and put it back exactly where it was.}\n\n@event det_prefects_room\ntitle: Clerk to the Prefects\npool: detention\ntone: tense\nart: paper\ntags: social study\ncast:\n  other: any where met(other) and tagged(other, student)\nscene: A cramped prefects' room with red sashes on pegs, a student copying names from a battered ledger into a clean one, a duty rota pinned to the wall above the desk.\ntext: {Your detention is clerking. The prefects' demerit ledger has to be copied out fair, every name and every crime, in a hand they can read.|The rota on the wall has a new prefect's initials on it, and the week's demerits are waiting to be copied.|The ledger, the clean copy, and a prefect at the window who hums while you work.} {{other} has three demerits this week, one for \"insolent humming\".|{other} is in this week, for \"a tone of voice\".|{other}'s name comes up twice, both times in the same prefect's hand.} The rota above the desk says who walks which corridor tonight.\n> left: Copy it, and the rota too\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  flag prefect_rota\n  say: {You copy the ledger faithfully, and the rota into your head while you're at it. Now you know who walks the east stair, and when the library goes unwatched. Your hand cramps by the end.|Every name, every crime, and in the back of your mind the rota, quarter-hour by quarter-hour. It's the dullest useful {slot == \"evening\": evening|afternoon} you've had.|You copy the week out neatly. A fresh rota is pinned up; you learn it without moving your lips.}\n> right: Leave {other}'s name out\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel other +small\n      say: {You copy everything but one line. Nobody checks. {other} never knows why their week was so quiet, and you never say.|One name fewer in the clean copy. The prefect signs it without reading. {other} buys you a cup of tea a week later, for no reason they can name.|You leave out {other}'s line. The ledger is lighter by one insolence, and you feel lighter by none.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The prefect checks your copy against the old one, finds the gap, and writes {other}'s name back in, and yours underneath.|\"You missed a line,\" says the prefect, and reads it aloud. You copy it in, in your best hand, while they watch.|A prefect reads over your shoulder. \"Friend of yours?\" They put the name back in red, and stay to watch you finish.}\n\n@event det_apology\ntitle: Four Hundred Words of Sorry\npool: detention\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: study\nscene: An empty classroom in fading light, a student frowning at a nearly blank sheet of parchment, a professor's note pinned to the desk.\ntext: {Detention, and a note pinned to the desk. A letter of apology, four hundred words, sincere, to be read by the professor concerned.|The professor's note adds, this time, that spelling counts.|This time the professor has left two good apologies as examples, both by the same student, ten years apart.} {The note underlines \"sincere\" twice.|Someone has carved SORRY into the desk, very deep.|The quill provided is the scratchiest in the castle.}\n> left: Mean every word\n  alignment +tiny\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You write it plainly, and it's harder than lying would have been. The professor reads it, folds it, and says \"thank you\", and means it.|You find you are sorry, about two hundred words in. The rest come slowly. The professor keeps the letter, which feels worse than if they'd burned it.|It doesn't get easier to mean it; you only get better at it. The professor nods at the last line and doesn't make you read it aloud.}\n> right: Write a magnificent one\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive adept- wild:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {Four hundred words of polished regret, with a quotation from the founders. The professor reads it twice and looks almost moved.|You write the apology of the century. The professor calls it \"very thorough\" and files it with the others, which are apparently a collection.|A masterpiece of remorse, and you enjoy writing it more than you should.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {\"This is a performance,\" says the professor, and hands it back. \"Again. Sincerely, this time.\" You're there until the lamps go out.|The professor reads one line aloud, flatly, and the whole thing sounds as hollow as it is. You start again on a fresh sheet, until your hand cramps and the room goes cold.|\"Very pretty,\" says the professor. \"Now the real one.\" You write the real one, late, with cold feet.}\n\n@event det_candle_wax\ntitle: Wax on the Long Tables\npool: detention\ntone: light\nart: candle\ntags: magic\nscene: The great hall emptied after a meal, floating candles drifting overhead, a student scraping hardened wax off a long wooden table.\ntext: {The floating candles over the Great Hall drip all through every meal, and between meals someone must scrape the long tables clean. Today it's you.|The long tables, the scraper, and a meal's worth of wax, with an extra puddle under the high table.|The kitchens have left you a better scraper, which is either a kindness or a hint.} {One candle hangs lower than the others, dripping with enthusiasm.|The house banners rustle above you.|The ceiling shows tomorrow's weather: {season == \"winter\": snow|rain}.}\n> left: Scrape every table clean\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Five long tables, one scraper, and a sore back. By the end the oak shines and you can see your tired face in it.|You scrape until your shoulders burn, and the tables come up like new. Somewhere above you a candle drips, in the middle of the first one.|You know which tables drip worst now, and start there. It still takes until your arms give out.}\n> right: Coax the candles down to trim them\n  challenge spell apprentice:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      say: {You call the candles down one by one and trim their wicks. The dripping stops. Tomorrow's breakfast will be the cleanest in a year.|The candles come down to your hand like tame birds. You trim them, and they bob back up looking smart.|Down they come, all forty. You trim them neatly, and one of them, you'd swear, bows.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {A candle comes down too fast and drips a perfect circle of hot wax onto your head. The prefect laughs for a full minute.|The candles scatter like startled pigeons and drip on everything, you included. You scrape for twice as long.|Three candles come down; one comes down on your hand. You finish with a scraper and a blister.}\n\n@event det_great_lens\ntitle: The Great Lens\npool: detention\ntone: warm\nart: planet\ntags: study magic\nscene: The inside of a brass observatory dome, a huge telescope, a student on a ladder rubbing its lens with a chamois cloth.\ntext: {Detention in the observatory. The great telescope's brass and its lens are to be polished by hand, with a chamois and a bucket of warm water that goes cold at once.|Someone has written a rude word in the dust on the great lens, backwards, so it reads right from the eyepiece.|The telescope has turned itself to face the door, as if it were expecting you.} {{slot == \"evening\": The dome is open, and the stars are coming out over your shoulder.|The dome is half open on a pale sky.}|The brass is green at the joints.|A moth is asleep on the eyepiece.}\n> left: Polish every inch of it\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You polish the brass until it gleams and the lens until it disappears. Your fingers are numb. The keeper of the dome checks it and says nothing, which here is praise.|You learn the telescope's whole body by polishing it, every screw and gear. You also learn how cold a brass dome can get.|An hour of small circles. When you climb down, the lens is so clean the sky looks closer.}\n> right: Look through it instead\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {{slot == \"evening\": You put your eye to the lens and the first stars come up out of the dark. The keeper finds you an hour later, the brass still dull.|It swings, as it likes, to the far shore and the mountains past it. The keeper finds you an hour later, the brass still dull.} You owe your revision an evening.|You look instead of polishing. The sky, close up, is worth an essay you won't now write.|You look, and the lens is so smeared it's like looking through honey, and still it's beautiful. The reading you'd planned waits for another night.}\n\n@event det_flight_divots\ntitle: Filling the Divots\npool: detention\ntone: light\nart: broom\ntags: nature\nscene: A grassy flight field pocked with crater-like divots, a student with a spade and a barrow of turf, brooms racked by the fence.\ntext: {Detention on the Flight Field. Every bad landing of the week has left a divot, and every divot needs filling before the next practice.|The weekend's race has left a crater the exact shape of a broom-handle, and plenty of its friends.|Somebody from the race team has left a bag of turf by the fence, with a note: SORRY.} {One crater is large enough to sit in.|The brooms in the rack by the fence are unwatched.|{season == \"winter\": The turf is frozen into bricks.|The grass smells of rain.}}\n> left: Fill every last one\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You dig and stamp and dig until the field is level and your blisters have blisters.|By the last divot you can lift a full barrow one-handed. You mean to be proud of that once your arms stop shaking.|You fill the field flat. The next practice lands on it and makes new divots before you've left the fence.}\n> right: Borrow a broom while nobody's looking\n  challenge deceive apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: {One lap of the field, low and fast, and back on the ground before the prefect looks up. The divots can wait for the wind.|You take a broom from the rack and fly the length of the field and back. Nobody saw. It was the best part of the week.|A lap, then another. You land beside your spade and pick it up, breathless, as the prefect turns round.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {The prefect turns round just as you take off. The whole race team, arriving for practice, sees you marched back to your barrow.|You're spotted halfway down the field. The prefect makes you fill the divot you made landing.|You get as far as the fence. The prefect is waiting there with your spade, and the team cheers, sarcastically.}\n\n@event det_inkwells\ntitle: Three Hundred Inkwells\npool: detention\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A tiered lecture hall emptied for the night, rows of desks with brass inkwells, a student walking the rows with a big jug of ink.\ntext: {Detention in the Lecture Tower. Every inkwell in every hall, three hundred of them, to be filled before morning from one heavy jug.|Somebody has been drinking from one of the inkwells, and carved a confession into the desk beside it.|Three hundred inkwells, and a new jug, heavier than the last one.} {The lecturer's own well at the front is empty.|Someone has left their spectacles on a desk.|A paper bird has been left on a desk.}\n> left: Fill them all, neatly\n  study +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Row by row, hall by hall, with the jug getting heavier. In the quiet you plan your next essay from first line to last.|Three hundred wells, not one spilled. Your arms ache, and somewhere around the hundredth well the problem you were stuck on came loose.|You fill the wells in the order you'd answer an exam, and mutter the answers as you go. The halls don't mind.}\n> right: Doctor the lecturer's well\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {A drop of something that turns blue in the light, in the lecturer's well only. By noon tomorrow the whole year knows why the lecturer's hands are blue.|You add a pinch of sneezing powder to the front well. The next morning's lecture is punctuated, and nobody can prove a thing.|A drop of colour-change ink in the lecturer's well. The notes come out a shy pink. The class will talk of nothing else for a week.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The prefect sees the little bottle in your hand. You refill all three hundred wells again, from fresh ink, until midnight.|The lecturer finds the blue ink before the lecture, and you in front of it. You stay until every well is clean and refilled.|Caught with the bottle. You empty and refill every well in the hall while the prefect reads aloud from the rules.}\n\n@event det_gargoyle_scrub\ntitle: The Gargoyles' Bath\npool: detention\ntone: light\nart: rain\ntags: mystery\nscene: A windy castle parapet lined with stone gargoyles, a student with a bucket and a stiff brush scrubbing pigeon mess off a grinning stone face, the lake far below.\ntext: {Detention on the north parapet. The gargoyles, a bucket and a brush, and a year of pigeons to scrub off them. The third one along has a draught behind it.|A pigeon has moved into the mouth of the cleanest gargoyle on the parapet, the third one along.|The wind is up, and the third gargoyle along is humming in it, very faintly.} {The wind tries to take the bucket.|The lake below is very still.|{season == \"winter\": The water in the bucket keeps freezing.|A pigeon watches you clean its work, offended.}}\n> left: Scrub them till they grin\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You scrub until every face along the parapet is clean and grinning. Your knuckles are raw and you smell of pigeon.|A whole row of clean gargoyles. The pigeons are already lining up to undo it.|You scrub hard and fast in the wind. By the end you're freezing and the gargoyles look delighted.}\n> right: Linger at the third gargoyle\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {member(cartographers): {You check the chalk mark behind the third gargoyle is still fresh while you scrub, and smudge it a little, so it looks old.|You give the third gargoyle a very thorough wash, and the passage behind it a very thorough look. All clear.|You scrub the third gargoyle last and slowest, and leave it shining as if it had nothing to hide.}|{You scrub around the draught slowly, and it comes from no wall. You feel for the edge of something.|The stone behind the third one is warmer than it should be. You don't find a door. You're nearly sure there is one.|You spend so long on the third gargoyle that the prefect asks if you're in love with it. You learn nothing, and suspect a great deal.}}\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_classes.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — one more event per elective, plus a core-class\n#  event everyone can get.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event core_flying_lesson\nslot: morning\ntitle: Compulsory Broom Flight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: year == 1 and month <= 6 and term\nweight: 12\nart: broom\ntags: class danger\nscene: A row of nervous first-years standing beside brooms on the lawn, one broom already rising on its own, an instructor with a whistle.\ntext: Compulsory first-year broom flight. The instructor blows her whistle. Your broom rises, hesitates, and then — clearly having taken a dislike to you — begins drifting toward the lake.\n> left: Hold on and steer it back\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      renown +small\n      say: You wrestle it round and land, wobbling, exactly on the mark. The instructor nods. The broom grudgingly accepts you.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The broom wins. You land in the shallows. Everyone sees. Everyone remembers.\n> right: Jump off while it's still low\n  mood -tiny\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: You drop four feet onto the grass. Undignified, but dry. The broom carries on to the lake alone and sinks with a sulky gurgle.\n\n@event pyro_dragon_breath\ntitle: A Sample of Dragonfire\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 12\nart: dragon\ntags: class magic\nscene: A glass jar containing a single writhing flame of deep blue, held in tongs by a stern professor, students leaning back from it.\ntext: Thorne holds up a sealed jar containing one flame of genuine dragonfire, blue-white and furious. \"Who would like to try holding it?\" he asks. Nobody moves. He looks at you.\n> left: Hold it\n  challenge spell standard+1:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      renown +small\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: It sears and struggles and then, astonishingly, settles in your palm like a small warm animal. The class stares. Thorne almost smiles.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      say: The dragonfire disagrees. You spend a week with bandaged hands and a new respect for dragons.\n> right: Watch someone else\n  lore +tiny\n  say: A fourth-year volunteers and loses a sleeve. You take careful notes on what not to do.\n\n@event tide_water_spirit_race\ntitle: The Water Spirit Race\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: evocation\nweight: 12\nart: wave\ntags: class nature\nscene: Students swimming across a lake at dawn, translucent water-spirits racing alongside them like silver dolphins.\ntext: Professor Marisol's final challenge: swim the lake while the water spirits race you. \"They cheat,\" she says cheerfully. \"Ask them nicely not to.\"\n> left: Race them flat out\n  challenge feat journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You touch the far shore a hand's length ahead of the fastest spirit. It sulks, then laughs like a fountain.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The spirits tow you in circles. You arrive last, but very clean.\n> right: Ask them nicely\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: You ask. They carry you across on their backs like a raft. Marisol says that counts. Marisol says it's the whole point.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: They pretend not to hear. Water spirits are terrible at manners.\n\n@event illusion_mirror_maze\ntitle: The Mirror Maze Exam\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: illusion\nweight: 12\nart: mirror\ntags: class magic\nscene: A maze of tall mirrors in a dark hall, each reflecting a different version of the same student, one reflection grinning.\ntext: Professor Vey's exam: a maze of enchanted mirrors. Find the exit. Some reflections lie. One of them is — she promises — the real you.\n> left: Trust your own eyes\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +medium\n      study illusion +tiny\n      say: You spot the reflection that blinks late, and follow it out. Vey awards top marks and says, \"Most people trust the one that looks happiest.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You follow the happiest reflection for an hour. It leads you in a circle and waves goodbye.\n> right: Shatter the illusion\n  challenge spell standard:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study illusion +tiny\n      say: Every mirror goes dark at once. Exit found. Vey writes, \"Effective. Inelegant. Pass.\"\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You shatter one mirror. It was real. Seven years of bad luck, the caretaker says, or three crowns for the glass.\n      gold -tiny\n\n@event alch_love_potion\ntitle: The Love Potion Assignment\npool: alchemy_class_weight12\ntone: warm\nart: potion\ntags: romance\nscene: A pink, glittering potion in a heart-shaped flask on a workbench, a student holding it up to the light with a doubtful expression.\ntext: This week's assignment: brew a love potion — \"for analytical purposes only\". Your flask glows a suspicious pink. Someone at the next bench asks if they can buy it. Several people ask.\n> left: Brew it perfectly, then pour it away\n  alchemy +medium\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  say: Perfect colour, perfect scent. You pour it down the sink. The pipes are affectionate for a week.\n> right: Sell it\n  alignment -small\n  gold +medium\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain detention\n    say: A prefect falls in love with the headmistress's portrait. It's traced back to you. Worth it, almost.\n  else:\n    say: Fifteen crowns. You never ask what it was used for. You see a lot of unusual couples at dinner.\n\n@event div_prophecy_exam\ntitle: The Prophecy Exam\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 12\nart: eye\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A dim divination classroom, a single student staring into a crystal ball, the examiner's quill hovering expectantly over a blank page.\ntext: The mock Divination exam: gaze into the crystal and make one prophecy about the coming month. The examiner will check. \"Vague prophecies,\" she warns, \"fail.\"\n> left: Make a bold, specific prophecy\n  chance even:\n    study astromancy +medium\n    renown +small\n    say: \"On the eighth, the kitchen cat will have kittens in the headmistress's hat.\" On the eighth, she does. Top marks in the mock.\n  else:\n    study astromancy -small\n    say: \"On the eighth, it will snow indoors.\" It does not snow indoors. The examiner writes \"ambitious\".\n> right: Make a careful, safe prophecy\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: \"Someone will be late to breakfast.\" Someone is. The examiner sighs and gives you a bare pass: it was vague, but it was checkable.\n> up[omen]: Foretell it, out loud, so it lands\n  study astromancy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You say it plainly, in that flat certain voice: the examiner's kettle will boil dry on Friday. It does. She gives you full marks and looks at you as if you'd done it on purpose. You aren't sure you didn't.\n\n@event art_golem_helper\ntitle: The Little Golem\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: artifice\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: class magic\nscene: A knee-high clay golem carrying a teetering stack of books across a classroom, a student watching anxiously as it nears a staircase.\ntext: Your term project: a knee-high clay golem that carries books. It works! It works so well that it has started carrying everyone's books, whether they want it to or not.\n> left: Add a \"stop\" command\n  challenge research standard-2:\n    win:\n      study artifice +small\n      lore +small\n      say: The golem stops. It also waits politely. The professor asks if she can borrow it for her office.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Now it stops randomly, mid-staircase, holding forty books. Nobody can get past it. It looks very pleased.\n> right: Let it roam free\n  chance likely:\n    renown +small\n    mood +small\n    say: The golem becomes a beloved castle fixture, delivering books to whoever looks sad. It signs every delivery with your initials, in clay.\n  else:\n    renown -tiny\n    say: It also delivers a Restricted Section book to a first-year. The librarian follows the clay smudges straight to your door.\n\n@event beast_hippocampus\ntitle: The Hippocampus Foal\npool: beastcraft_class_weight12\ntone: light\nart: wave\ntags: nature\nscene: A tiny sea-horse foal with a fish's tail struggling in a rock pool on the lake shore, a student kneeling beside the water.\ntext: A hippocampus foal — half pony, half fish — has been stranded in a shrinking rock pool on the lakeshore. Professor Brask is on the other side of the grounds. The foal is panicking.\n> left: Carry it to the lake\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: It thrashes, then trusts you. You wade out and let it go. It circles you three times before diving deep.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood +small\n      say: It kicks you in the stomach with surprising force — but you get it to the water. Brask says that's how they say thank you.\n> right: Run for Professor Brask\n  lore +small\n  say: Brask arrives with a bucket and a lullaby. She makes you hold the bucket. You learn more in ten minutes than in a whole term.\n\n@event astro_eclipse\nwhen: not closed(hollowstar)\ntitle: The Eclipse\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: astromancy\nweight: 12\nart: moon\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A black disc sliding across the sun above the observatory dome, students on the roof with smoked-glass lenses, a halo of light around the darkness.\ntext: An eclipse, the first in forty years. The astromancy class is on the roof with smoked-glass lenses. At the moment of totality, you notice something the charts don't show: a second shadow, smaller, moving the wrong way.\n> left: Record it precisely\n  lore +medium\n  find second_shadow\n  say: You chart the second shadow's path exactly. It crosses the sky and stops — directly above the lake.\n> right: Just watch the eclipse\n  mood +medium\n  say: For four minutes the world holds its breath. You'll remember the cold, and the silence, and the strange birdsong, for the rest of your life.\n\n@event heal_triage_drill\ntitle: Triage\npool: alchemy_class_weight12\ntone: tense\nart: cross\ntags: danger\nscene: A practice ward full of students acting injured, groaning theatrically on cots, a single student healer in the middle with a clipboard.\ntext: Sister Amaranth's triage drill: twelve \"injured\" students, one healer — you — and ten minutes. Some are faking worse than others. One, you realise, is genuinely hurt.\n> left: Treat the genuine injury first\n  challenge brew standard-1:\n    win:\n      alchemy +medium\n      study alchemy +tiny\n      say: You spot the real injury in the first minute. Amaranth nods, once, which is the highest honour in the infirmary.\n    lose:\n      study alchemy +tiny\n      say: You find the real injury in minute nine. Late, but not too late. Amaranth's lips thin. \"Again tomorrow.\"\n> right: Work systematically down the line\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: Methodical, thorough and slow. The real patient waits seven minutes. Amaranth makes you write an essay on why.\n\n@event umbral_last_lecture\ntitle: The Last Lecture on Absence\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: year >= 2\ntrack: umbral\nweight: 12\nart: skull\ntags: class mystery hollowstar\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: An Umbral Theory lecture hall lit only by a black candle, the professor drawing a star with a hollow centre on the board.\ntext: The Umbral professor draws a star with a hollow centre on the board. \"This is not on the syllabus,\" she says. \"Three hundred years ago this academy imprisoned an absence that thinks. I believe you should know that before you graduate.\"\n> left: Take careful notes\n  lore +medium\n  flag knows_founders_seal\n  find knows_founders_seal\n  hs_omens_progress\n  find seven_locks\n  say: She speaks for an hour about seals and locks and the founders' fear. At the end she wipes the board clean and says, \"This lecture never happened.\"\n> right: Ask why she's telling you\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  say: \"Because something is stirring,\" she says, \"and the headmistress asked me to find out which of you would ask that question.\" She writes your name down.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_faculty.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — short arcs for Professor Thorne, Old Maud and\n#  Headmistress Marrow. All `trigger: social` (need met + rel).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Professor Thorne ----------------\n\n@event thorne_old_rival\ntitle: An Old Rival\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 9 and year >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: duel\ntags: rival study\nscene: Two elderly mages facing each other across a dueling ring at dusk, one stern and grey, one flamboyant in purple, a nervous student holding a towel at the side.\ntext: A flamboyant old wizard in purple has arrived to settle \"a forty-year-old matter\" with Professor Thorne. A formal duel needs a second. Thorne looks around the courtyard and, to everyone's astonishment, points at you.\n> left: Stand as his second\n  mood -tiny\n  rel thorne +medium\n  renown +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: The duel lasts four seconds, the longest of your life. Thorne wins. Then both old men sit on the grass and laugh until they wheeze. You've never seen him laugh. Neither has anyone else.\n> right: Politely decline\n  rel thorne -tiny\n  say: \"Hm,\" says Thorne, and asks the caretaker instead. He wins anyway. He does not mention it again, which is somehow worse.\n\n@event thorne_roses\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Professor's Roses\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 6 and month >= 7 and month <= 10 and met(maud)\nweight: 10\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: A severe old professor kneeling in a hidden rose garden at dawn, sleeves rolled up, murmuring encouragement to a single struggling rosebush.\ntext: At dawn, behind the observatory, you find Professor Thorne on his knees in a tiny walled rose garden, murmuring encouragement to a sickly bush. He sees you. Neither of you breathes.\n> left: Back away and never speak of it\n  rel thorne +medium\n  say: You leave. The next day in class he calls on you for the hardest question — and then, very quietly, gives you full marks for a so-so answer.\n> right: Offer to help with the sick bush\n  challenge brew apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel thorne +large\n      alchemy +small\n      say: You know the blight; Maud taught you. Within a month the bush is covered in red roses. Thorne leaves one, without comment, on your desk.\n    lose:\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: Your remedy does nothing. But Thorne lets you kneel beside him for an hour, saying nothing, which you suspect is a great honour.\n\n@event thorne_shaking_hands\ntime: noon afternoon\ntitle: Thorne's Hands\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 15 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: hand\ntags: social\nscene: An old professor gripping the edge of a lectern with both hands, a dropped wand on the floor, a single student in the doorway.\ntext: After class you see Thorne's wand slip from fingers that won't stop trembling. He picks it up, slowly. \"Age,\" he says, not looking at you. \"It comes for every storm eventually. You'll say nothing.\"\n> left: Say nothing, as he asks\n  mood -tiny\n  rel thorne +medium\n  say: You never mention it. But you start arriving early to set up his demonstrations. He notices. He lets you.\n> right: Tell Sister Amaranth\n  rel thorne -medium\n  say: Amaranth's tonic steadies his hands. Thorne is furious with you for a week. Then he stops being furious, and starts, grudgingly, teaching you his hardest spells — \"before I can't.\"\n\n# ---------------- Old Maud ----------------\n\n@event maud_goat\ntitle: Bartholomew in the Library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 3\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: nature\nscene: A shaggy goat standing on a library table chewing a book, an apoplectic librarian below, an old woman in a straw hat hurrying through the stacks.\ntext: Maud's goat, Bartholomew, has got into the library and is eating the Restricted Section. The librarian is apoplectic. Maud, hurrying in, grabs your arm. \"He likes you. Get him out before he eats anything with teeth.\"\n> left: Lure him out with an apple\n  challenge soothe apprentice- wild:\n    win:\n      rel maud +medium\n      say: Bartholomew follows the apple, and you, out into the sunlight. He has eaten one-third of \"Curses of the Northern Wastes\". He seems fine. Mostly.\n    lose:\n      rel maud +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: Bartholomew eats the apple, then your sleeve, then headbutts you into a shelf. Maud gets him out in the end, laughing.\n> right: Pick him up and carry him out\n  health -tiny\n  vigor +small\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: He is heavier than he looks and struggles like a sack of angry cats. You carry him all the way to the paddock. The librarian applauds.\n\n@event maud_recipe\ntitle: Maud's Soup\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 12\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: nature home\nscene: A small cottage kitchen, a black iron pot over the fire, an old woman guiding a student's hand as they stir.\ntext: \"I'm going to teach you my soup,\" says Maud, rolling up her sleeves. \"Cures colds, broken hearts and most curses. Don't write it down. You'll remember it if you need it.\"\n> left: Learn it properly\n  alchemy +small\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: It takes all afternoon. Some of the ingredients are herbs; some, as far as you can tell, are just stories. You remember every step.\n> right: Mostly eat it\n  health +tiny\n  rel maud +tiny\n  say: You learn about half of it and eat three bowls. Maud declares that the most important part of the recipe anyway.\n\n@event maud_old_wand\ntitle: A Wand From Sixty Years Ago\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 18 and flag(knows_founders_seal)\nweight: 14\nart: wand\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: An old woman unwrapping a slender silver wand from a faded silk scarf, a photograph of three young students on the table beside it.\ntext: Maud unwraps something from a faded scarf: a slender silver wand. \"Edmund's,\" she says. \"He was the one who didn't come back. He'd have wanted it to go beneath the lake again, with someone who meant to close the door.\"\n> left: Accept it\n  arcana +medium\n  flag edmunds_wand\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: It fits your hand like it was waiting. When you hold it near the lake, it hums the same note as the drowned bell.\n> right: Tell her to keep it\n  rel maud +large\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Keep it for him,\" you say. She holds it for a long time. \"Sixty years,\" she says, \"and nobody ever said that.\" She makes tea, and you sit with her until dark.\n\n# ---------------- Headmistress Marrow ----------------\n\n@event isolde_chess\ntitle: A Game of Tower Chess\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: rel(isolde) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: crown\ntags: study\nscene: A chessboard of carved crystal towers on a small table by a tower window, two players, one candle, the lake silver below.\ntext: The headmistress keeps a board of crystal towers by her window. \"Do you play?\" she asks. It is clearly not a question. The pieces are already moving into position by themselves.\n> left: Play to win\n  challenge research vs isolde.lore wild:\n    win:\n      rel isolde +medium\n      lore +small\n      say: You win. She looks at the board for a long moment, then laughs — a short, delighted sound. \"Again,\" she says. \"Thursday.\"\n    lose:\n      rel isolde +small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: She wins in nine moves and spends the next hour showing you exactly why. It is the most useful lesson you've had all year.\n  if knows(isolde.portrait):\n    rel isolde +tiny\n    say: Between games you mention, very innocently, the observatory. She looks at you over the towers. \"Twice,\" she says. \"And I would do it again.\"\n> right: Play to learn how she thinks\n  mood -tiny\n  rel isolde +medium\n  guile +small\n  say: You lose, slowly, watching. She sacrifices pieces she seems to love. By the end, you understand a great deal about how the academy is run.\n\n@event isolde_portrait\ntitle: The Young Headmistress\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: rel(isolde) >= 3\nweight: 8\nart: mirror\ntags: mystery\nscene: A portrait in a forgotten corridor of a laughing young woman in student robes with a wand behind her ear, frame dusty, eyes unmistakably the headmistress's.\ntext: In a forgotten corridor you find a portrait of a laughing girl with a wand stuck behind her ear and ink on her nose. The plaque has been scratched off. The eyes are unmistakably the headmistress's. The portrait winks.\n> left: Ask the portrait about her\n  chance likely:\n    guile +tiny\n    lore +tiny\n    say: \"Detention record of the decade,\" whispers the portrait. \"Set the observatory on fire twice. Best student this castle ever had.\" It winks again.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: The portrait pretends to be asleep, loudly. By the time you give up, the corridor has rearranged itself, and it takes you half the evening to find your way out.\n> right: Tell the headmistress you found it\n  rel isolde +small\n  say: She is quiet for a moment. \"I thought they'd burned it.\" She almost smiles. \"Leave it where it is. Someone should remember her.\"\n\n@event isolde_judgement\ntitle: A Question of Mercy\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: rel(isolde) >= 12 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: story\nscene: The headmistress at her desk with a single file open, a student seated opposite, rain on the window behind them.\ntext: The headmistress slides a file across her desk. A second-year stole from the apothecary to pay for a sick mother's medicine. The rules say expulsion. \"You're a student,\" she says. \"Tell me what a student thinks is just.\"\n> left: Argue for mercy\n  rel isolde +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You argue for an hour. She listens to all of it. The second-year is given a job in the infirmary and a stern lecture. The mother recovers.\n> right: The rules exist for a reason\n  rel isolde +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: \"Yes,\" she says, \"they do.\" She closes the file. You never learn what she decided, but the second-year is still in class the next week, looking grateful at nobody in particular.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_life.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — more academy life, plus a short chain:\n#    timeloop: life_timeloop_1 -> life_timeloop_2 -> life_timeloop_3\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Tuesday That Wouldn't End ----------------\n\n# The time loop (playtest 2, X8 with L21): one Tuesday, lived over and over in a single day of the game. The hub\n# (`life_timeloop_2`) counts the loops in `loop_n`; each loop you exploit it, experiment or investigate, and each\n# card ends by looping again. Clues (`loop_clues`) open the fix (`life_timeloop_3`), which gets easier every loop;\n# at the ninth loop it breaks by itself (`timeloop_cap`), so it can never soft-lock. More cards: chain_timeloop.grim.\n@event life_timeloop_1\nslot: morning\ntitle: Haven't We Done This?\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 5 and not flag(timeloop_started)\nweight: 6\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery magic\nthread: timeloop\nscene: A student at breakfast staring at a plate of eggs with intense déjà vu, a clock on the wall with its hands spinning backward.\ntext: Breakfast. Eggs. Thorne trips on the third step. A first-year spills juice on a prefect. You know all of this is about to happen, because it already happened. Yesterday. Which was also Tuesday.\n> left: Ignore it, you're probably just tired\n  flag timeloop_started\n  loop_n = 1\n  loop_clues = 0\n  loop_exploit = 0\n  mood -tiny\n  queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: You eat the eggs. They taste exactly as they did yesterday. Thorne trips on the third step. You go to bed early, uneasy, and wake to eggs.\n> right: Warn Thorne about the step\n  flag timeloop_started\n  loop_n = 1\n  loop_clues = 0\n  loop_exploit = 0\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  queue life_timeloop_2 now\n  say: \"The third step, Professor.\" He looks at you strangely and steps over it. Somewhere, a clock skips. You go to bed, and wake, and it's still Tuesday.\n\n@event life_timeloop_2\ntitle: Tuesday, Again\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\nrepeat: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery magic\nthread: timeloop\nscene: The same breakfast scene repeated, but the student is now standing on the table, holding up a sign that reads \"IT'S STILL TUESDAY\".\ntext: {loop_n <= 1: It's Tuesday again. Eggs, the third step, the juice, the prefect. Nobody else notices anything.|{loop_n <= 3: Tuesday. You could recite breakfast. You could recite the prefect's yelp.|{loop_n <= 5: Tuesday, again. The days are starting to feel thin, like a page read too often.|{loop_n <= 7: Tuesday. The eggs taste of nothing now. The clock in the hall ticks a beat late.|Tuesday. The edges of the morning are fraying, like a loop pulled too tight. It can't hold much longer.}}}} {loop_clues >= 3: You know where it comes from now.|{loop_clues >= 1: You have a thread to pull.|You have no idea why.}}\n> exploit: Use it: win every bet, ace the quiz\n  hint: Crowns and marks carry over. It wears on you, and someone may notice.\n  queue timeloop_exploit now\n  say: You know every answer and every coin-toss. Why not?\n> experiment: Do something you'd never dare\n  hint: Nothing you do today will last. Almost nothing.\n  chance unlikely:\n    queue timeloop_dare_confess now\n  else:\n    chance even:\n      queue timeloop_dare_fall now\n    else:\n      queue timeloop_dare_kindness now\n  say: Nothing today can stick. You might as well find out who you are when nothing sticks.\n> investigate: Look for the cause\n  hint: Each loop, a clue. Clues make the fix possible.\n  if loop_clues == 0:\n    queue timeloop_clue_bells now\n  elif loop_clues == 1:\n    queue timeloop_clue_light now\n  else:\n    queue timeloop_clue_firstyear now\n  say: Something is doing this. Clocks don't stop on their own.\n> fix: Try to break the loop\n  hint: A spell against the clock. Hard at first; easier every loop, and with what you've found.\n  when: loop_clues >= 1\n  queue life_timeloop_3 now\n  say: You go up the astronomy tower before breakfast, while the morning is still new.\n\n@event life_timeloop_3\ntitle: The Broken Chronometer\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 8\nart: gear\ntags: mystery magic\nthread: timeloop\nscene: An astronomy tower at night, a giant brass chronometer with its gears spinning in reverse, a sleeping first-year slumped beside it with a wand still in hand.\ntext: In the astronomy tower, a first-year is asleep beside an enormous brass chronometer, wand still stuck in its winding key. \"I just wanted one more day before my exam,\" says the note on his chest. {loop_clues >= 3: You know exactly which gear, and why.|You know roughly where to start.}\n> left: Wrench the clock forward\n  loop_tier = loop_n + 2 * (loop_clues >= 3)\n  if loop_tier >= 7:\n    challenge spell adept likely:\n      win:\n        flag timeloop_done\n        arcana +small\n        queue timeloop_after in 1\n        say: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        loop_n += 1\n        if loop_n >= 9:\n          queue timeloop_cap now\n        else:\n          queue life_timeloop_2 now\n        say: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\n  elif loop_tier >= 5:\n    challenge spell adept:\n      win:\n        flag timeloop_done\n        arcana +small\n        queue timeloop_after in 1\n        say: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        loop_n += 1\n        if loop_n >= 9:\n          queue timeloop_cap now\n        else:\n          queue life_timeloop_2 now\n        say: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\n  elif loop_tier >= 3:\n    challenge spell adept unlikely:\n      win:\n        flag timeloop_done\n        arcana +small\n        queue timeloop_after in 1\n        say: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        loop_n += 1\n        if loop_n >= 9:\n          queue timeloop_cap now\n        else:\n          queue life_timeloop_2 now\n        say: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\n  else:\n    challenge spell adept slim:\n      win:\n        flag timeloop_done\n        arcana +small\n        queue timeloop_after in 1\n        say: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\n      lose:\n        health -small\n        loop_n += 1\n        if loop_n >= 9:\n          queue timeloop_cap now\n        else:\n          queue life_timeloop_2 now\n        say: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\n> right: Wake the first-year and fix it together\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  if loop_clues >= 2:\n    flag timeloop_done\n    queue timeloop_after in 1\n    say: You shake him awake. He's horrified, then helpful. Between your clues and his guilt you find the gear together, and the clock strikes Wednesday. He passes his exam. You never tell him how many Tuesdays it was.\n  else:\n    loop_n += 1\n    if loop_n >= 9:\n      queue timeloop_cap now\n    else:\n      queue life_timeloop_2 now\n    say: You shake him awake, and he panics, and the wand snaps in the key. The morning folds up. You wake to eggs. It's Tuesday, and you know a little more about the boy.\n\n# ---------------- Around the castle ----------------\n\n@pool expansion_life_routine_weight10\nclass: routine\nweight: 10\n\n@event life_portrait_escape\ntitle: The Escaped Portrait\npool: expansion_life_routine_weight10\ntone: eerie\nart: mirror\ntags: magic mystery\nscene: An empty gilded picture frame on a gallery wall, and further down the corridor a painted duchess hiding inside a landscape of a picnic.\ntext: An empty frame in the long gallery. The Duchess of Ambry has escaped her portrait and is hiding in a landscape three corridors away, refusing to come back. \"That frame,\" she declares, \"is hideous.\"\n> left: Persuade her back\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      say: You promise to have her frame regilded. She returns, mollified. The caretaker regilds it. You've acquired a duchess who now considers you a personal friend.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: She calls you a peasant and moves to a painting of a shipwreck. She's still there. She says she likes the sea air.\n> right: Leave her in the picnic\n  mood +tiny\n  say: She seems happier among the sandwiches. So does the picnic. Some arrangements simply work.\n\n@event life_library_cat\ntitle: The Library Cat\nwhen: term and not wrapup\npool: expansion_life_routine_weight10\ntone: warm\nart: cat\ntags: home\nscene: An enormous fluffy cat asleep on the exact book a student needs, on a high library shelf, one eye half-open.\ntext: The library cat — a vast, ancient beast called Footnote — is asleep on the only copy of the book you need. You have an essay due tomorrow. Footnote has no deadlines.\n> left: Move the cat\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      study +tiny\n      say: You lift Footnote like a sack of warm flour. He allows it, with contempt. You get the book. He gets your chair.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: Footnote does not consent to being moved. You now have three long scratches and still no book.\n> right: Bribe the cat\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: One sardine. Footnote rises like a sultan, stretches, and moves exactly one book to the left. It's enough.\n\n@event life_invent_spell\ntime: night\ntitle: A Spell of Your Own\npool: expansion_life_routine_weight10\ntone: light\nwhen: arcana >= 18\nart: sparkle\ntags: magic study\nscene: A student at a desk covered in crossed-out incantations, a tiny spiral of light hovering over the page, an expression of dawning triumph.\ntext: Late at night you realise there's a gap — a spell nobody has written yet, just a small one, to make ink dry instantly. You could try to invent it. Inventing spells, Thorne says, is \"how most of my former students lost their eyebrows\".\n> left: Invent it carefully\n  challenge research journeyman steady:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      alchemy +small\n      say: Three weeks of theory and one perfect evening. The ink dries, and so does a paper cut on your thumb: what you've written is a cousin of Mend. Thorne grunts and puts your name on next year's lecture slide.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Your spell dries ink instantly. It also dries the paper, the desk and your eyes. You shelve it for now.\n> right: Just try things until something works\n  chance unlikely:\n    arcana +medium\n    renown +small\n    say: Somehow — through chaos and luck — it works. You couldn't explain why under oath. It still counts.\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: Your eyebrows survive. Your desk does not.\n\n@pool expansion_life_routine\nclass: routine\nweight: 8\n\n@event life_cursed_umbrella\ntitle: The Cursed Umbrella\npool: expansion_life_routine\ntone: light\nwhen: month >= 3 and month <= 7\nart: rain\ntags: magic\nscene: A student holding a black umbrella under which a tiny private rainstorm is pouring, while the sky outside is perfectly clear.\ntext: You borrowed an umbrella from lost property. It's black, elegant and cursed: underneath it, it is always raining. The rest of the world is sunny. You are very wet.\n> left: Break the curse\n  challenge spell apprentice:\n    win:\n      arcana +tiny\n      gold +tiny\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: The curse lifts with a soft hiss. The umbrella is now just a very nice umbrella. It isn't yours, but you sell it to Corvin anyway; he wants it \"for drama\".\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: Now it rains sideways.\n> right: Return it to lost property\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: The caretaker takes it back, sighs, and hangs it on a hook labelled \"DO NOT\". There are six other things on that hook.\n\n@event life_exam_dream\ntime: night\ntitle: The Exam Dream\npool: expansion_life_routine_weight10\ntone: warm\nwhen: month == 3 or month == 4 or month == 9 or month == 10\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A dream-image: a student in an exam hall in their pyjamas, the exam paper written in a language that keeps changing, a clock with no hands.\ntext: You dream you're in the exam hall in your pyjamas. The paper is in a language that keeps changing. The proctor is Bartholomew the goat. You wake at 4am, heart pounding.\n> left: Get up and revise\n  study +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: You revise until breakfast. The real exam paper is written in a normal language. You're absurdly relieved.\n> right: Go back to sleep\n  health +tiny\n  say: You dream again. This time the goat gives you full marks. You choose to take this as an omen.\n\n@event life_garden_plot\ntitle: A Patch of Earth\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: not has(garden_plot) and turn >= 4\nweight: 5\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A small square of freshly turned earth behind the greenhouses, a wooden stake with a student's name painted on it, a watering can beside it.\ntext: Old Maud is giving out the abandoned plots behind the greenhouses. There's one left: small, weedy and full of rocks. \"It's yours if you want it,\" she says. \"Gardens are a lot of work. Most of the work is noticing.\"\n> left: Take the plot\n  gain garden_plot\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You spend a whole weekend clearing rocks and earn a back that complains for a week. By spring, you have lettuces, three violent mandrakes and a strange purple flower nobody can name.\n> right: Leave it for someone else\n  mood +tiny\n  say: A shy first-year takes it and grows giant pumpkins. You feel oddly proud of them.\n\n# The pen pal's first letter (playtest 2, X9): Tamsin Reed, the start of chain_thornwick.grim.\n@event life_pen_pal\ntitle: A Letter From Thornwick\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: year == 1 and month == 2 and not met(tamsin) and not off_campus\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A letter with a green Thornwick seal open on a desk, beside a reply half-written in Lumenhold blue ink.\ntext: A letter arrives from a stranger at Thornwick Academy, the rival school. \"Dear Lumenhold student chosen at random,\" it begins. \"I'm told you're all insufferable. Prove me wrong? T. Reed (illusion, first year, not insufferable).\"\n> left: Write back\n  meet tamsin\n  rel tamsin +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Your reply is four pages long and only slightly insufferable, and it takes the whole evening. The answer arrives within a week, signed \"Tamsin\". You've made a friend in enemy territory.\n> right: Ignore it\n  mood +tiny\n  queue tamsin_second_letter in 3\n  say: You ignore it, and enjoy the feeling of being unbothered for about a day. Some letters, you suspect, don't take no for an answer.\n\n@event life_three_headed_puppy\ntitle: A Very Lost Puppy\npool: expansion_life_routine\ntone: tense\nart: paw\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A puppy with three heads sitting in a corridor, one head chewing a shoe, one licking a wall, one howling mournfully.\ntext: There is a puppy in the dormitory corridor. It has three heads. One is eating a shoe, one is licking the wall, one is howling. It is very lost and, all three heads agree, very hungry.\n> left: Feed it and find its owner\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +medium\n  say: You buy it a string of sausages from the kitchens, then follow its owner's frantic shouting to Professor Brask's office. She weeps with relief. The puppy's three heads each lick you once, in strict order.\n> right: Call Professor Brask\n  lore +tiny\n  say: Brask arrives at a run with a lead and three biscuits, and explains to you, at length, how to tell which head is in charge. \"Bad boys,\" she says fondly. All three heads look deeply ashamed.\n\n@event life_bell_tower\ntime: night\ntitle: The Midnight Bell\npool: expansion_life_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 4\nart: bell\ntags: danger social\nscene: A dark bell tower interior, ropes hanging down, a group of students at the bottom daring one of their number to climb.\ntext: Tradition: once in your time at Lumenhold, you must ring the old bell at midnight without getting caught. Your dorm has decided tonight is your night.\n> left: Climb and ring it\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: BONG. The whole castle wakes. By the time the prefects reach the tower you're back in bed, innocent and smug.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      renown +small\n      say: BONG — and Bram is at the bottom of the stairs when you come down. Detention. But you rang it, and everybody heard.\n> right: Decline the dare\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Someone else rings it instead and becomes a legend. You'll always wonder.\n\n@event life_fog_maze\ntitle: The Fog Maze\npool: expansion_life_routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: month == 3 or month == 4\nart: compass\ntags: nature mystery\nscene: The academy grounds swallowed by thick white fog, hedges looming like walls, a lantern glowing faintly ahead.\ntext: Fog has come up off the lake so thick you can't see your own feet. The path to the greenhouses has become a maze. Somewhere ahead, someone is calling for help, faintly.\n> left: Follow the voice\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: You find a first-year sitting on a bench, completely lost, sixty feet from the door. You walk them in. They tell everyone you saved their life.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: You walk in circles for an hour and end up exactly where you started. The voice was a very clever crow.\n> right: Cast a light and wait\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You raise a floating light and wait. One by one, three lost students find their way to it. You feel like a lighthouse.\n\n@event life_wishing_well\ntitle: The Wishing Well\npool: expansion_life_routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: magic money\nscene: An old stone well in the corner of a cloister, coins glinting at the bottom, a carved inscription half-worn away.\ntext: In the old cloister there's a wishing well. The inscription says: \"ONE COIN, ONE WISH, ONE PRICE.\" The bottom glitters with three hundred years of coins.\n> left: Throw in a coin and wish\n  gold -tiny\n  chance even:\n    mood +medium\n    say: You wish for a good month. It is, mostly. Coincidence, probably.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    gold +tiny\n    say: You wish. The well hiccups and spits your coin back out. Apparently, you can't afford that wish.\n> right: Fish out a few old coins\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      say: Fifteen crowns' worth of antique coins. The well is silent. Disapprovingly silent.\n    lose:\n      gold -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The well bites. You lose your own purse in it. ONE PRICE indeed.\n\n@event life_borrowed_broom\ntitle: The Borrowed Broom\npool: expansion_life_routine\ntone: tense\nart: broom\ntags: danger\nscene: A student clinging to a bucking broom high above the castle, its bristles on fire, a smaller student on the ground waving frantically.\ntext: A second-year lends you their broom for an errand. Midway across the lake, you discover why they were so keen to lend it: it is extremely ticklish, and it hates you.\n> left: Wrestle it home\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      say: You land in a heap on the lawn, triumphant and windswept. The broom sulks. The second-year owes you a favour.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You land in the lake. The broom flies home without you, very pleased with itself.\n> right: Soothe it\n  challenge soothe apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      charm +tiny\n      mood +small\n      say: You talk to it gently all the way across. It purrs. Brooms shouldn't purr. Now it follows you around.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: It is not soothed. It is, if anything, more ticklish, and it tips you into the shallows on the way home.\n\n@event life_double_trouble\ntitle: Your Double Is Loose\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: (enrolled(illusion) or passed(illusion, 1)) or seen(illusion_mirror_self) > 0\nweight: 6\nart: mask\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A student staring in horror across a crowded corridor at a perfect copy of themselves, cheerfully chatting up a professor.\ntext: An illusion of you — one you thought you'd dismissed — is still wandering the castle. It has been to three of your classes, flirted with a prefect, and volunteered you for the choir.\n> left: Catch it\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: You corner it in a broom cupboard. It looks at you, shrugs and pops like a bubble. The prefect, however, still expects a date.\n    lose:\n      renown +small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: It escapes, and spends a week as the most charming version of you. People are disappointed when you come back.\n> right: Let it run its course\n  renown +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: It attends your lectures and takes better notes than you do. When it fades after a week, you're almost sorry.\n\n@event life_auction\ntitle: The Lost-Property Auction\npool: expansion_life_routine\ntone: light\nwhen: gold >= 10\nart: dice\ntags: money\nscene: A crowded hall, a caretaker with a gavel at a lectern, tables piled with lost scarves, wands, boots and one suspiciously humming box.\ntext: Once a year the caretaker auctions off unclaimed lost property. There's a box nobody wants that hums faintly. The bidding starts at eight crowns. Nobody's bidding.\n> left: Bid on the humming box\n  gold -small\n  chance even:\n    gain inspired\n    say: Inside: a music box that plays a different song every time you open it. Each one makes you think of something new.\n  else:\n    gain hiccup_hex\n    say: Inside: a very old, very annoyed hiccup curse, which immediately transfers itself to you.\n> right: Just watch the bidding\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Nobody bids on the box. A first-year buys a single left boot for a crown and looks thrilled. You leave with nothing and a good story.\n\n@event life_village_busking\ntitle: Busking in the Village\npool: expansion_life_routine\ntone: light\nwhen: visits_to(great_hall) >= 3 or charm >= 16\nart: music\ntags: money social\nscene: A cobbled village square, a student singing beside a fountain with an upturned hat at their feet, villagers pausing to listen.\ntext: You're short on crowns, and the village square has a fountain, an empty hat and a crowd of shoppers. You do know a few songs.\n> left: Sing your heart out\n  challenge perform apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: A small crowd gathers. Someone requests an encore. The hat fills with copper and one silver coin from a weeping old man.\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Three crowns and a sympathetic look from a dog. Busking is harder than it looks.\n> right: Play the street-magic act instead\n  challenge spell apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: You make doves out of the fountain spray. Children scream with joy. The hat overflows.\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      say: Your doves come out as damp sparrows. People are kind about it.\n\n@event life_moths\ntitle: A Storm of Moths\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: month >= 7 and month <= 10\nweight: 5\nart: moon\ntags: nature mystery\nscene: A dormitory room filled with thousands of pale moths circling a single candle, a student standing in the doorway, astonished.\ntext: You open your door to find your room full of moths — thousands of them, pale as moonlight, circling your candle in silent spirals. It's eerie. It's also, you can't help noticing, beautiful.\n> left: Open the window and let them go\n  mood +small\n  say: They pour out into the night in a silver river. One stays behind, on your pillow, for a week. You name it Gregory.\n> right: Study them\n  lore +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: They're luna moths, migrating — and drawn to something in your room. You find it: a crumb of moonpetal on your sleeve. Mystery solved. Mostly.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_lightrag_2026_10.grim","text":"# Five adopted cards from the LightRAG exploratory authoring batch.\n\n@event stables_hippogriff_grooming\ntitle: Feathers the Wrong Way\npool: stables_routine\ntone: warm\nart: feather\nscene: A student brushing the wing feathers of a lying-down hippogriff in a straw-filled stall, loose feathers drifting in a shaft of light.\ntext: {A hippogriff has rolled in the mud and lies in her stall with every feather pointing the wrong way, waiting to be groomed.|The muddy hippogriff again. She lies down the moment she sees you with a brush.|The hippogriff has kept her muddiest wing for you, and holds it out like a gift.} {season == \"autumn\": Leaves are stuck in her feathers.|{The stable cat supervises from a beam.|The owls are watching to see if you get it wrong.|Brask is somewhere nearby, humming to a feed sack.}}\n> left: Groom her properly, feather by feather\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {It takes the whole afternoon and both your arms. She falls asleep with her head on your boot, which is the highest mark a hippogriff gives.|You do it the slow way, with the grain. She clicks her beak at you every time you're right, and once, gently, when you're wrong.|You know her feathers now. She lets you do the hard bit under the wing, and Brask, passing, says nothing at all.}\n> right: Read Brask's grooming notes on the wall\n  lore +small\n  rel brask -tiny\n  say: {The notes are long, opinionated and illustrated. You learn a great deal about feathers. The hippogriff stays muddy and sighs at you.|You read the notes. Brask catches you at it and says, \"She doesn't need a scholar, she needs a brush.\"|You finish the notes, which you could recite by now, and leave the brush for someone else. The hippogriff looks away.}\n\n@event stables_tack_room\ntitle: The Tack Room\npool: stables_routine\ntone: light\nart: paw\nscene: A cramped tack room hung with harnesses and saddles, a student sitting on an upturned bucket oiling a strap, a griffon chick peering in at the door.\ntext: {The tack room is a mess of harnesses, and the oil pot is out. Someone has to do the straps before Beastcraft tomorrow.|The tack room again. The griffon chick has been at the buckles.|The tack room has your bucket in it now, the one you sit on.} {season == \"winter\": It's the warmest room in the stables, and it smells of leather and oil.|{One strap has teeth marks all along it.|A saddle is missing; nobody wants to say whose.|There is a nest in the old harness, and it is occupied.}}\n> left: Oil and mend the straps\n  vigor +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You oil straps until your hands are black and the room smells like a cobbler's. Every buckle works. Nobody thanks you, which is how you know it's done.|You mend the bitten strap and replace the buckle the chick swallowed. It will reappear, Brask says darkly.|You do the straps by touch now, talking to the nest in the old harness. Its owner answers.}\n> right: Slip out before anyone hands you the pot\n  mood +small\n  rel brask -tiny\n  say: {You leave the straps for the morning. In the morning Brask hands you the pot, and a look.|You're out of the door before the oil pot is. Behind you the griffon chick starts on another buckle.|You slip away. You hear Brask say your name to the hippogriffs, in a tone.}\n\n@pool courtyard_routine\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event courtyard_fountain_wishes\ntime: afternoon\ntitle: Coins in the Fountain\npool: courtyard_routine\ntone: light\nart: sun\ntags: money\nscene: A courtyard fountain with coins glinting on the bottom, a student crouching at the rim with a sleeve rolled up, a groundskeeper's rake leaning nearby.\ntext: {Students throw coins in the courtyard fountain for luck before exams. The coins are still there, glinting.|The fountain is full of exam-luck again.|You know exactly how many coins are in the fountain this week, and that is a bad sign.} {season == \"winter\": The water is cold enough to make your teeth ache by looking at it.|{A sign says the coins go to the infirmary fund.|A first-year is throwing in a coin with a long, whispered wish.|The fountain is freezing at one edge, as it does when it shouldn't.}}\n> left: Fish out a few coins for yourself\n  gold +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {Nobody's watching. Your sleeve is soaked to the shoulder and your pocket is heavier. The sign about the infirmary fund keeps looking at you.|You fish quickly and walk away fast. A first-year's wish is in your pocket now, and the coin is warm.|You've become good at this, which is not a compliment.}\n> right: Throw one in yourself\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You throw a coin and wish for something you won't admit to. It's the best you've felt all week.|You throw your coin in after the first-year's. They grin at you, as if you're in on something together.|You throw a coin in. It lands on top of an old one of yours, which you take to mean something.}\n\n@event courtyard_lost_cat\ntitle: Footnote on the Wall\npool: courtyard_routine\ntone: light\nart: paw\ntags: social\nscene: A grey library cat sitting on top of a high courtyard wall, a small crowd of students below calling up to it, one holding out a sardine.\ntext: {Footnote, the library cat, is sitting on top of the courtyard wall and refusing to come down. A small crowd has gathered.|Footnote is on the courtyard wall again, being worshipped.|Footnote is on the wall. A few faces in the crowd know you from last time, and move up to make room.} {season == \"spring\": Blossom is blowing over the wall onto his head.|{The library will not reopen until he is found, apparently.|Someone has a sardine. Someone else has a ladder.|A first-year is trying to bribe him with a sock.}}\n> left: Climb up after him\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You climb the ivy, take the cat and come down to applause. Footnote looks as if he arranged the whole thing.|You get him on the second try. He purrs, for the crowd only.|You're up the wall before the crowd can offer the ladder. Footnote walks into your arms as if he's been waiting all afternoon.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {The ivy gives. You land in the flowerbed. Footnote walks down the ladder someone else brought.|You get halfway, and Footnote jumps down past you and walks off. The crowd's sympathy is mostly laughter.|You slip at the top. He watches you fall with professional interest, then comes down by himself.}\n> right: Wait him out with the crowd\n  charm +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You stand with the crowd and trade news while Footnote ignores you all. He comes down when he's bored, which takes an hour.|You chat with the others until the cat gives up. Someone shares a pastry. Footnote takes half.|You spend the hour in good company under the wall. Footnote comes down and sits on your foot, to make a point.}\n\n@event company_card_widow\ntitle: The Widow's Game\nthread: company_night\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nart: cup\ntags: money company_night\nscene: A tavern's upstairs room after midnight, an elegant old woman in black dealing cards at a small table, a student in a borrowed jacket sitting opposite her.\ntext: {Tonight's guest is a widow in black lace who has come up from the river towns to play cards with somebody young. She deals before you've sat down.|The widow is back, and asked for you by description: \"the one who loses nicely.\"|The widow again. She has brought her own deck this time, and a flask she says is cough syrup.} The landlady murmurs: she tips the ones who make her laugh, and she always knows who's letting her win.\n> left: Play her honestly, and well\n  hint: She tips well for a good game; a dull one gets a small tip and an early night.\n  challenge appeal journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      mood +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You play your best and lose by a hair, and she laughs until she has to take her spectacles off. She tips you twice what you'd have won.|You beat her, once, and she's so pleased she tells you about her late husband's three duels. You go home at four, paid and full of stories.|You keep it close all night. When she leaves she pinches your cheek, presses a fold of notes on you, and says, \"Next time, cheat a little. It's more fun.\"}\n    lose:\n      gold +small\n      mood -small\n      say: {She sees through you in three hands, and is bored by the fourth. A small tip and an early night.|She plays you off the table, kindly, and tips like a woman who has won. It is not much.|You try too hard to amuse her. She pats your hand. \"Never mind, dear,\" she says, and leaves early.}\n> right: Let her win, gracefully\n  hint: Steady pay, and you know what she thinks of it.\n  gold +medium\n  say: {You lose with care all night. She knows, and tips you anyway, the way you'd tip a waiter for a bad soup.|You let her win. She thanks you very politely, which stings more than you expected.|You lose gracefully, as the landlady asked. The widow gives you exactly the going rate, and not a coin more.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_people.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — more character events (social slot).\n#  Three each for Lyra, Tobias, Seraphine, Corvin, Mir, Nessa,\n#  Kael; two for Bram.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Lyra ----------------\n\n@event lyra_toast\ntitle: Lyra Cannot Cook\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 9\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: social\nscene: A dormitory kitchenette full of smoke, a crimson-haired girl holding a blackened slice of toast at arm's length, a fire-charm still sparking in her other hand.\ntext: Smoke pours from the kitchenette. Lyra Vaneshade, pyromancy prodigy, is holding a piece of bread that is now mostly charcoal. \"I can incinerate a target at forty paces,\" she says. \"I cannot make toast. Don't. Say. Anything.\"\n> left: Say absolutely nothing\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: You open a window, take the bread, and make two perfect slices with a toasting fork. She eats hers in dignified silence. Then she asks for another.\n> right: Teach her — gently\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: \"Less fire,\" you say. \"No, less. Less than that.\" It takes eleven slices. On the twelfth, she makes perfect golden toast and looks prouder than after any duel.\n\n@event lyra_mother_visit\ntitle: Lady Vaneshade\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 15 and year >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: crown\ntags: social rival\nscene: A tall woman in crimson and black walking through the courtyard like a queen inspecting troops, Lyra two steps behind her, very straight.\ntext: Lady Vaneshade has come to inspect the academy, and her daughter. She stops in front of you. \"You must be the one my daughter mentions,\" she says. \"Constantly. I'd like to know why.\"\n> left: Answer with perfect manners\n  challenge negotiate journeyman +1 if has(origin_noble):\n    win:\n      rel lyra +large\n      renown +small\n      say: You are polite, precise and unimpressed. Lady Vaneshade studies you, then says, \"Hm. Adequate.\" Lyra looks at you like you've slain a dragon.\n    lose:\n      rel lyra +small\n      mood -small\n      say: You stumble over a title. Lady Vaneshade's eyebrow says everything. Later, Lyra finds you. \"She does that to everyone,\" she says. \"You lasted longer than most.\"\n> right: Answer honestly\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  if spark(lyra) >= 15:\n    spark lyra +small\n  say: \"Because she's brilliant,\" you say, \"and nobody tells her.\" Lady Vaneshade is silent. Lyra is redder than her hair. The visit ends early.\n\n@event lyra_rival_respect\ntitle: Respect, Grudgingly\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"rival\" and rel(lyra) >= 9\nweight: 16\nart: flame\ntags: rival social\nscene: Two rivals sitting on the edge of a scorched practice ring, wands laid down between them, sharing a flask of water.\ntext: After another draw, Lyra drops onto the edge of the ring beside you. \"You're the only person here who makes me better,\" she says, as if confessing a crime. \"I hate it. I don't want it to stop.\"\n> left: \"Then let's keep it a rivalry.\"\n  rel lyra +medium\n  say: \"Good,\" she says, relieved. \"Rivalry I understand.\" She shoves you, lightly. You shove her back. The rivalry continues, warmer.\n> right: \"Or we could just be friends.\"\n  bond lyra friend\n  rel lyra +large\n  say: She considers the word like an unfamiliar spell. \"Friends,\" she says. \"Fine. But I'm still going to beat you.\" You'd be disappointed if she didn't.\n\n# ---------------- Tobias ----------------\n\n@event tobias_scarecrow\ntitle: The Scarecrow Project\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: social nature\nscene: A scarecrow in a straw hat waving its arms at crows in a vegetable patch, a big farm boy and a student watching it proudly.\ntext: Tobias has built a scarecrow for the greenhouse plots and wants to enchant it to scare crows. \"Just a little life,\" he says. \"Just enough to wave. Nothing creepy.\"\n> left: Help enchant it\n  challenge spell apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel tobias +medium\n      say: The scarecrow waves. It also learns to wave hello, and to tip its hat to the headmistress. The crows are terrified. The first-years adore it.\n    lose:\n      rel tobias +small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The scarecrow comes to life, looks at you both, and walks off into the forest. You see it sometimes, at dusk, waving.\n> right: Suggest a cat instead\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: You borrow the greenhouse cat. The crows leave immediately. Tobias is a little sad about the scarecrow, so you help him make it a hat.\n\n@event tobias_dance_lessons\ntitle: Two Left Feet\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 9 and (month == 3 or month == 4)\nweight: 12\nart: music\ntags: social romance\nscene: An empty classroom with desks pushed to the walls, a big boy counting steps aloud, a broom propped against the wall as his former partner.\ntext: Tobias has pushed the desks against the walls of an empty classroom. \"The Ball's {month == 4: tonight|next month},\" he says, mortified. \"I've been practising with a broom. The broom has complained.\"\n> left: Teach him to waltz\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +medium\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: One-two-three, one-two-three. He steps on you exactly fifty times. On the fifty-first bar, something clicks, and for a moment he's graceful. He looks astonished.\n> right: Tell him nobody cares about footwork\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Just enjoy it,\" you say. He thinks about this, nods, and dances like a delighted bear. It's terrible. It's wonderful.\n\n@event tobias_storm_greenhouse\ntime: night\ntitle: Storm Over the Greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 12 and month >= 6 and month <= 8\nweight: 12\nart: bolt\ntags: nature danger\nscene: Rain lashing a glass greenhouse at night, panes cracking, a big boy holding a door shut against the wind while plants rattle inside.\ntext: A storm hits at midnight. Glass is cracking in the greenhouses. Tobias is out there in his nightshirt, trying to hold a door shut against the wind with his whole body. \"The moonflowers!\" he shouts. \"Eleven years!\"\n> left: Run out and help\n  health -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel tobias +large\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: You hold the panes together with every shielding charm you know until dawn. Every moonflower survives. Tobias hugs you, soaked, laughing, half crying.\n> right: Fetch Old Maud\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  rel maud +small\n  say: Maud arrives in her dressing gown, mutters three words at the sky, and the wind politely goes elsewhere. Tobias stares at her. \"Teach me that,\" he says.\n> up[heartwood]: Grow a tree to shelter the glass\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +large\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: You kneel in the mud and a tree comes up between the wind and the greenhouse, and spreads its branches over the glass. The storm breaks on it all night. In the morning Tobias sits under it with you and doesn't say anything at all.\n\n# ---------------- Seraphine ----------------\n\n@event seraphine_lost_day\ntitle: Seraphine Is Tomorrow\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: mystery social\nscene: A girl sitting at the wrong desk in an empty classroom, a teacup in her hands, the calendar on the wall showing the next day's date.\ntext: Seraphine is sitting in an empty classroom, waiting for a lesson that's happening tomorrow. \"I've slipped,\" she says calmly. \"I'm a day ahead. It happens. I need someone to remind me what today is.\"\n> left: Talk her back into today\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You tell her what you had for breakfast, what the weather's like, what Thorne said this morning. Slowly her eyes stop looking through you. \"Oh,\" she says. \"Hello. Today.\"\n> right: Ask her what tomorrow holds\n  chance even:\n    study +tiny\n    say: \"There's a surprise quiz in every class,\" she says. \"They planned it together. Question three is always a trick.\" It is, every time. You get them all right.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: \"It rains,\" she says. \"You get wet. You'll be fine.\" You are not fine. You get very wet.\n\n@event seraphine_painting\ntitle: A Painting of Somewhere Else\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 12\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: social mystery\nscene: A small canvas on an easel showing a stranger's cottage by the sea, a girl with paint on her fingers watching someone look at it.\ntext: Seraphine has painted a small cottage by the sea, with a crooked chimney and a blue door. \"It's yours,\" she says. \"In twelve years. I thought you should see it now. In case you want a different door.\"\n> left: Keep the painting\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    rel seraphine +medium\n    say: You hang it over your bed. Some nights you swear the smoke from the chimney moves. You decide, privately, that you like the blue door.\n  else:\n    rel seraphine +small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You hang it over your bed. Some mornings the door is a different colour, and you lie there wondering which one you chose.\n> right: Ask her to paint her own future\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  spark seraphine +tiny\n  say: She looks at the blank canvas for a long time. \"I never can,\" she says. \"Seers can't see themselves.\" Then, quietly: \"Unless someone's standing next to us.\"\n\n@event seraphine_mother\ntitle: The Oracle's Summons\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 15 and year >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A girl holding a letter that glows faintly silver, standing at the top of a tower stair, looking down as if about to fall or fly.\ntext: Seraphine's mother, the Oracle of Vell, wants her home to take up the family seat. \"I'd spend my life telling kings what they want to hear,\" Seraphine says. \"But she's already seen me say yes. She always sees me say yes.\"\n> left: \"Then surprise her.\"\n  mood -tiny\n  rel seraphine +large\n  say: Seraphine writes back: no. For a whole day she's terrified, and so are you. Then she's giddy. \"I didn't see that coming,\" she keeps saying. \"I didn't see it coming!\"\n> right: \"Maybe she's right.\"\n  rel seraphine -medium\n  say: She nods slowly. She doesn't go — not yet. But for a long while she stops telling you her visions, and you miss them more than you expected.\n\n# ---------------- Corvin ----------------\n\n@event corvin_confiscation_vault\ntitle: The Confiscation Vault\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 12\nweight: 12\nart: lock\ntags: social danger\nscene: A dark vault crammed with confiscated student contraband — fanged frisbees, cursed dice, a goblet of endless soup — two figures with a lantern at the door.\ntext: \"The confiscation vault,\" Corvin breathes. \"Three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student. My grandfather's lucky deck is in there. I just want it back. Lookout?\"\n> left: Keep lookout\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      flag corvin_deck_back\n      rel corvin +large\n      gold +small\n      say: He comes out with the deck and a handful of confiscated coins \"for your trouble\". The deck is, it turns out, genuinely lucky. He wins at cards for a month.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel corvin +medium\n      say: The vault alarm is a very loud goblin. You both spend a week cataloguing contraband — which Corvin considers a victory.\n> right: Talk him out of it\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  challenge negotiate vs corvin.guile:\n    win:\n      flag corvin_deck_back\n      rel corvin +medium\n      say: You convince him to just ask Thorne. Thorne, astonishingly, gives it back. \"Your grandfather cheated me with it in '41,\" he says. \"Tell him I said hello.\"\n    lose:\n      flag corvin_deck_back\n      say: He goes anyway, alone. He comes back with the deck and a black eye from the goblin. Worth it, he says.\n\n@event corvin_brother\ntitle: Corvin's Brother\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 15 and year >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: mask\ntags: social money\nscene: A rough-looking young man leaning on the academy gate, cap pulled low, Corvin facing him with his hands in his pockets.\ntext: There's a rough-looking man at the gates, and Corvin has gone very still. \"My brother,\" he says. \"He wants money. He always wants money. If I don't pay, he tells the headmistress where I learned to pick locks.\"\n> left: Help Corvin face him down\n  alignment +small\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      rel corvin +large\n      say: You stand beside Corvin while he says no — the first time, he tells you later, that he's ever said it. His brother leaves. Corvin sits down on the steps and laughs and shakes.\n    lose:\n      rel corvin +medium\n      mood -small\n      say: It turns ugly. In the end you empty your purse into his hand, just to make him leave. Corvin presses every coin back into yours that evening, with a strange new seriousness.\n> right: Stay out of family business\n  rel corvin -small\n  say: Corvin pays. You see him counting out every coin he has. He's quiet for a long while after.\n\n@event corvin_cards\ntitle: How to Cheat at Cards\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 6\nweight: 10\nart: cards\ntags: social money\nscene: A candlelit table in a corner of the common room, a boy fanning a deck of cards with a flourish, a student watching his hands very closely.\ntext: \"Rule one: never cheat,\" says Corvin, shuffling so fast the cards blur. \"Rule two: if you're going to cheat, never get caught. Rule three: I'm about to teach you how they cheat, so you never lose to them.\"\n> left: Learn the tricks\n  guile +small\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: By midnight you can spot a marked card, a bottom deal and a mirror charm. You will never be fleeced at Wizard's Hand again.\n> right: Learn to play honestly — and well\n  when: gold >= 3\n  chance likely:\n    lore +tiny\n    rel corvin +small\n    gold +small\n    say: He teaches you odds and nerve instead. That weekend you win eight crowns at the common-room game without cheating. Corvin is weirdly proud.\n  else:\n    gold -tiny\n    lore +tiny\n    rel corvin +small\n    say: He teaches you odds and nerve instead. That weekend the odds take three crowns off you. \"Also a lesson,\" says Corvin.\n  if knows(corvin.sundays):\n    rel corvin +tiny\n    say: Near the end he shuffles the slow way, the rainy-Sunday way, and you say you know whose shuffle it is. He doesn't answer. He deals you in anyway, for good.\n\n# ---------------- Mir ----------------\n\n@event mir_ornithopter\ntitle: The Test Flight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 9\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: social danger\nscene: A rickety winged contraption balanced on the edge of a tower roof, a goggled artificer strapping into it, a friend holding a rope.\ntext: Mir has built a flying machine with canvas wings and seven propellers. It is balanced on the edge of the north tower. \"Brooms are so last century,\" they say, strapping in. \"I need someone on the rope. Probably. Possibly.\"\n> left: Hold the rope\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      rel mir +large\n      renown +small\n      say: The machine flies — wobbly, loud, glorious — twice around the tower before landing in the lake. Mir surfaces whooping. You're both legends by dinner.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel mir +medium\n      say: The rope wins. You are dragged eight feet along the roof before letting go. The machine lands in a tree. Mir waves from it, delighted.\n> right: Insist on a safety net\n  rel mir +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You spend a day building a net. The machine flies four feet and drops into it. \"Data!\" says Mir happily. Nobody dies. It's a good day.\n\n@event mir_lab_fire\ntitle: Fire in the Workshop\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 12\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: danger\nscene: A workshop full of smoke and orange light, notebooks scattered across a burning bench, a figure in goggles coughing in the doorway.\ntext: The workshop is on fire. Properly this time. Mir is in the doorway, coughing — and trying to go back in. \"My notebooks! Three years of notes!\"\n> left: Grab the notebooks\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      rel mir +large\n      health -small\n      say: You come out singed, smoking, with an armful of notebooks. Mir stares at you, then at the notebooks, then bursts into tears and hugs you, both of you coughing.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      rel mir +medium\n      say: You get halfway before the smoke drives you back. You save one notebook. Mir says it's the most important one. You both know it isn't. They mean it anyway.\n> right: Drag Mir away\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel mir +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: You haul Mir back from the door. The notebooks burn. Mir is silent for a week. Then they start a new notebook. The first page just says: \"Thank you.\"\n  if knows(mir.corridor):\n    rel mir +tiny\n    say: The next morning you find them soldering on the corridor floor outside the burnt door, and bring them a cushion, and tea.\n> up[hoarfrost]: Freeze the fire out\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel mir +large\n  health -tiny\n  say: You throw frost at the doorway until the flames grey over and give up with a hiss. The notebooks are wet, cold and whole. Mir hugs them, then you, then the notebooks again, shivering.\n\n@event mir_name_day\ntitle: Mir's Name Day\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 15\nweight: 10\nart: gem\ntags: social\nscene: A cluttered workshop decorated with paper chains and one small cake with a single gear-shaped candle, an artificer looking embarrassed and pleased.\ntext: It's Mir's name day. They mention it casually, as if it doesn't matter. It clearly matters. Nobody else seems to know.\n> left: Throw a surprise party\n  gold -small\n  rel mir +large\n  mood +small\n  say: Six friends, one cake and a banner you painted badly. Mir walks in, stops, and has to sit down. \"Nobody's ever...\" they start, and can't finish.\n> right: Give them a handmade gift\n  rel mir +medium\n  spark mir +tiny\n  say: A little brass key that fits nothing. \"For whatever you build next,\" you say. Mir wears it on a chain, every day, for years.\n\n# ---------------- Nessa ----------------\n\n@event nessa_headmistress_interview\ntitle: The Impossible Interview\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 9 and (met(isolde) and rel(isolde) >= 6)\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A newspaper editor and a student waiting outside the headmistress's tower door, the editor clutching a list of questions three pages long.\ntext: \"Nobody has interviewed the headmistress in twenty years,\" Nessa says. \"But she likes you. Probably. Get me in the door and I'll name my firstborn after you.\"\n> left: Ask the headmistress\n  rel isolde -tiny\n  challenge negotiate journeyman +1 if renown >= 25:\n    win:\n      rel nessa +large\n      renown +medium\n      say: The headmistress agrees to ten minutes. She gives Nessa forty, and gives you a look that says you have spent a favour. The interview runs on the front page and sells out three printings.\n    lose:\n      rel nessa +small\n      say: \"No,\" says the headmistress, pleasantly. \"But tell Miss Quill her piece on the kitchens was excellent.\" Nessa frames the rejection. The headmistress is a shade cooler with you in the corridors after that.\n> right: Suggest someone overlooked instead\n  rel nessa +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Nessa interviews the night porter instead. It's the most-read piece of the year. Half the academy cries over breakfast.\n\n@event nessa_typo\ntime: morning\ntitle: A Catastrophic Typo\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 6 and met(thorne)\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A newspaper with a single word circled in red ink, an editor with her head in her hands, a stack of papers still warm from the press.\ntext: The Ledger has gone to print. In the profile of Professor Thorne, a single wrong letter has turned \"Thorne: Still Lively\" into \"Thorne: Still Lovely\". By breakfast the whole academy will be calling the most frightening man in the castle lovely. Five hundred copies.\n> left: Help her fix every copy overnight\n  health -tiny\n  rel nessa +medium\n  say: You correct five hundred copies with a scraping charm and a lot of tea. At dawn, Nessa falls asleep on the press. Thorne never knows.\n  if knows(nessa.planted):\n    rel nessa +tiny\n    say: At dawn you ask whether this was one of her planted errors. \"No,\" she says, horrified, and then laughs until she cries.\n> right: Tell her to run a correction and own it\n  mood -tiny\n  rel nessa +small\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: The correction is gracious. Thorne, astonishingly, cuts it out and pins it to his door. \"Precision,\" he says in class, \"is a virtue. So is apology.\"\n\n@event nessa_burnout\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Editor Sleeps\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 12 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: social\nscene: A girl asleep at a desk in a newspaper office, her self-writing quill still scratching on its own, the candle burned down to a stub.\ntext: Nessa hasn't slept properly in a week. She's asleep at her desk now, and her quill — unsupervised — is still writing next week's paper. It has started inventing news.\n> left: Put her to bed and run the paper yourself\n  health -tiny\n  rel nessa +large\n  study -small\n  say: You edit, typeset and print one issue, and miss a day of classes doing it. It's adequate. When Nessa wakes she reads it, winces at your headlines, and hugs you for a long time.\n> right: Wake her and make her rest\n  mood -tiny\n  rel nessa +medium\n  say: She argues for ten minutes, then sleeps for fourteen hours. The quill's invented news about a dragon in the kitchens turns out to be true.\n\n# ---------------- Kael ----------------\n\n@event kael_northern_lights\ntime: night\ntitle: The Lights of the North\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 12 and (month <= 5 or month >= 12)\nweight: 12\nart: star\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two figures on a frozen hillside under ribbons of green and violet light, one pointing upward, frost forming a circle around them.\ntext: Kael wakes you at midnight. \"Come,\" he says. On the hill above the lake, the sky is moving — green and violet ribbons, the lights of his home, here, for him. \"I called them,\" he says. \"I wanted you to see.\"\n> left: Watch in silence\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: You watch until the lights fade. Neither of you speaks. On the walk down, he takes your hand without seeming to notice he's done it.\n> right: Ask him about home\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +medium\n  lore +small\n  say: He tells you about ice palaces and singing glaciers and a mother who never once said she was proud. Then he says: \"This place is more home now.\" He means the hill. He means you.\n  spark kael +tiny\n\n@event kael_fever\ntitle: Kael Burns\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 9\nweight: 10\nart: rain\ntags: danger social\nscene: A pale boy in a narrow bed, sweating, frost forming and melting on the pillow around his head in a constant cycle.\ntext: Kael has a fever — and for someone made of frost, a fever is dangerous. The infirmary's warming charms are making it worse. Sister Amaranth looks frightened. \"He needs cold,\" she says. \"Real cold. I don't know where to get it.\"\n> left: Bring him snow from the mountain\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge feat journeyman+:\n    win:\n      rel kael +large\n      health -medium\n      say: You fly to the peaks on a borrowed broom and come back with a sack of ancient snow. By morning his fever breaks. He doesn't let go of your sleeve all day.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      rel kael +medium\n      say: The mountain wins. You come back half-frozen and empty-handed. Kael recovers anyway, slowly — and visits you in the next bed.\n  if knows(kael.cold_hands):\n    rel kael +tiny\n    say: Later you remember that his hands were warm when you left, for the first time since you've known him, and how much that frightened you.\n> right: Send word to Ilse\n  if met(ilse) and not flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north):\n    rel kael +medium\n    rel ilse +medium\n    say: Ilse arrives in a whirl of snow, puts her hand on his forehead and says something sharp in Northern. The fever breaks. Kael stares at his sister for a long time.\n  else:\n    rel kael +small\n    say: You can't find her. Kael recovers slowly, on his own, the way he's always done everything.\n\n@event kael_letter_home\ntitle: A Letter He Won't Send\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 15 and flag(kael_told)\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A boy at a library desk, a letter half-written in sharp Northern script, a candle beside it, ink frozen in the pen.\ntext: Kael is writing a letter. He's rewritten the first line eleven times. \"To my mother,\" he says. \"I'm telling her I won't come home. I won't open the door. I'm telling her I've found...\" He stops.\n> left: \"Found what?\"\n  rel kael +medium\n  spark kael +tiny\n  say: He looks at you for a long time. Then he writes the last word of the sentence without showing you, seals it, and sends it. He's lighter for days.\n> right: Help him find the words\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You sit together until the candle's gone. The letter, when it's finished, is short. It doesn't apologise once. He sends it at dawn.\n\n# ---------------- Bram ----------------\n\n@event bram_father_visit\ntitle: Bram's Father\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: flag(bram_softened) and rel(bram) >= 9\nweight: 14\nart: shield\ntags: social\nscene: A towering grey-haired man in an old head-boy's sash speaking down to a red-faced prefect in the entrance hall, students hurrying past pretending not to look.\ntext: A tall man in an old head-boy's sash is speaking to Bram in the entrance hall, quietly and terribly. Bram has shrunk two sizes. \"...never amount to anything,\" the man is saying.\n> left: Interrupt — say something good\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel bram +large\n      say: You tell his father {seen(bram_turns): about the night at the boathouse|what Bram is like when nobody's keeping score}. The man falls silent. Bram looks at you like you've handed him a sword.\n    lose:\n      rel bram +medium\n      say: His father looks through you as if you're furniture. But afterwards, Bram finds you and says, roughly, \"Nobody's ever stood there before. Thanks.\"\n> right: Find Bram afterwards\n  mood -tiny\n  rel bram +medium\n  say: He's behind the stables, again. You sit on the bucket next to his. You don't talk. After a while he says, \"Same time next year, probably,\" and almost laughs.\n  if knows(bram.portrait):\n    rel bram +tiny\n    say: On the way back you take him the long way round, so you don't pass the corridor of head boys. He notices, and says nothing, which from Bram is thanks.\n\n@event bram_cheating\ntitle: Bram Cheats\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: met(bram) and (month == 4 or month == 10)\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: rival study\nscene: A prefect in an exam hall slipping a tiny scroll out of his sleeve, a student two desks away watching.\ntext: In the exam hall, two desks away, you see Bram Hollis — prefect, enforcer of rules — slip a tiny scroll out of his sleeve. He sees you seeing him. His face goes white.\n> left: Report him\n  rel bram -huge\n  rel isolde +small\n  renown +small\n  alignment +small\n  say: Bram loses his sash for the rest of the year. Some students cheer. You don't feel like cheering. Neither, later, does he.\n> right: Say nothing — and let him know you saw\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel bram +medium\n  guile +small\n  say: After the exam you just look at him. He nods, once. You never mention it. But his demerits stop, for you and for everyone you know.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_social.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — more generic relationship events (actor: any).\n# ============================================================\n\n@pool expansion_social_routine\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 8\ntags: social\n\n@event soc_lost_notes\ntitle: The Borrowed Notes\npool: expansion_social_routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 3 and not npc_is(lyra)\nper: npc\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A student holding a single soggy page of notes, the rest of the stack floating away down a stream behind them.\ntext: {npc} borrowed all your lecture notes last week. {They} {are} now standing in front of you holding a single soggy page. \"There was a stream,\" {they} say{s}. \"And a duck. Mostly a duck.\"\n> left: Laugh it off\n  rel npc +medium\n  study -tiny\n  say: You rewrite your notes from memory. {npc} brings you a cake every day for a week, by way of apology. The cakes are better than the notes were.\n> right: Make {them} rewrite them\n  rel npc -tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: {npc} copies out forty pages from a friend's notes, grumbling. The new notes are neater than yours ever were.\n\n@pool expansion_social_routine_repeat12\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 12\n\n@event soc_birthday\ntitle: A Birthday\npool: expansion_social_routine_repeat12\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 9\nper: npc\nart: gem\ntags: social festival\nscene: A small cake with a single candle in a dark common room, a group of students crouched behind a sofa, a surprised face in the doorway.\ntext: {&You find out — by accident — that it's {npc}'s birthday. {They} haven't told anyone. {They} {are} spending it in the library, alone, on purpose.|It's {npc}'s birthday again, and again {they} haven't told anyone. {They} {are} in the library, alone, as if you might have forgotten.}\n> left: Organise a surprise\n  gold -small\n  rel npc +large\n  mood +small\n  say: {&Seven people jump out from behind a sofa. {npc} screams, then laughs, then has to sit down. \"How did you know?\" You'll never tell.|This year it's a cake shaped, badly, like the library. {npc} pretends to be annoyed for nearly a minute.}\n> right: Join {them} in the library\n  rel npc +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {&You sit down opposite {them} with your own book.|You take the same seat as last year, with your own book.} Neither of you says happy birthday. At midnight {they} slide{s} a biscuit across the table, and you both grin.\n\n@event soc_bad_news\ntitle: Bad News From Home\npool: expansion_social_routine_repeat12\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 12 and not npc_is(kael)\nper: npc\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A student sitting on a stone staircase with a crumpled letter, head bowed, a friend sitting down beside them.\ntext: {npc} is sitting on the stairs with a letter in {their} lap, not moving. {Their} grandmother is ill. The barge home takes two days. \"I don't know whether to go,\" {they} say{s}.\n> left: \"Go. We'll cover your classes.\"\n  rel npc +large\n  study -tiny\n  say: {npc} catches the next barge. Every evening you copy out the day's notes for {them} from {their} classmates. When {npc} comes back, {their} grandmother is better, and {they} hug{s} you on the landing.\n> right: Just sit with {them}\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You sit there until the stairs move under you. You don't fix anything. That's not what {they} needed.\n\n@event soc_stargazing\ntitle: Counting Stars\npool: expansion_social_routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and rel(npc) >= 12 and courting(npc)\nper: npc\nart: star\ntags: romance\nscene: Two students lying on their backs on a flat roof, close but not quite touching, a sky full of stars above.\ntext: {&{npc} has dragged a blanket up to the roof.|{npc} is on the roof again with the blanket, and a flask this time.|{npc} has found a better spot on the roof, out of the wind, and a better blanket.} \"There's a meteor shower,\" {they} say{s}. \"Supposedly.\" There's room on the blanket for two, just about.\n> left: Count meteors\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You count thirty-one. {npc} counts thirty-four and accuses you of not looking properly. You weren't, entirely. You go in when the shower's over, warm under the blanket all the way down the stairs.\n> right: Talk about everything else, till dawn\n  spark npc +small\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You don't see a single meteor. You talk about home and fear and what you'd do if you could do anything. When you look up, the sky's gone grey with dawn.\n  chance unlikely:\n    health -tiny\n    say: The roof's cold has got into your bones. You sneeze for a week, and {npc} sends you a scarf with no note.\n\n@pool expansion_social_routine_repeat10\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 10\n\n# retired 2026-10-20 (was \"known() >= 4\"): jealousy is for romance only (social.grim's jealousy_* cards)\n@event soc_jealous_friend\ntitle: Jealousy\npool: expansion_social_routine_repeat10\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"friend\" or bond(npc) == \"close\")\nper: npc\nwhen: false\nart: mask\ntags: social retired\nscene: A student standing alone at the edge of a laughing group, arms folded, watching someone else take what used to be their seat.\ntext: {~{npc} has been odd all week. Finally it comes out.|{npc} has been short with you at every meal. Finally, on the stairs, it comes out.|{npc} cancels on you twice and then turns up at your door. It comes out in a rush.} \"You've got all these new friends now. I just — I don't know where I fit anymore.\"\n> left: Reassure {them}\n  rel npc +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You tell {them} exactly where {they} fit: right here, first. {npc} rolls {their} eyes, but {they}'re smiling. The oddness is gone by dinner.\n> right: Tell {them} friendship isn't a contest\n  rel npc -tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You're right, technically. {npc} knows it. It still lands badly. It takes a few weeks for things to feel easy again. You spend the evenings you'd have spent soothing {them} in the library instead.\n\n@event soc_teach_spell\ntitle: A Trick Worth Learning\npool: expansion_social_routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 7\nper: npc\nart: wand\ntags: magic\nscene: Two students in an empty classroom, one demonstrating a wand movement, the other copying it with a tiny spark of light.\ntext: {npc} has a spell {they} invented — a little thing, half-useful, half-ridiculous — and wants to teach it to someone. {&\"It makes your tea exactly the right temperature,\" {they} say{s}. \"Forever.\"|\"It finds the page you were reading,\" {they} say{s}. \"Any book, anywhere.\"|\"It keeps your socks dry,\" {they} say{s}. \"Lake, rain, puddles. Dry.\"}\n> left: Learn it properly\n  chance likely:\n    arcana +small\n    rel npc +small\n    say: It takes an hour. {&Your tea is now perfect, always.|You will never lose your page again.|Your socks are dry. Your socks will always be dry.} You'll use this spell more than anything you learn in class.\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    rel npc +small\n    say: {&The first attempt freezes your tea solid, and your eyebrows with it. The second works. Your eyebrows take a day to thaw.|The first attempt finds every page in the library at once. The second works. The librarian is not amused.|The first attempt dries your socks, your shoes and most of your feet. The second works. Your skin flakes for a day.}\n> right: Teach {them} one of yours in return\n  rel npc +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You trade spells like secrets. By midnight you've both got a new trick and an inside joke that will last three years.\n\n@event soc_secret_admirer\ntitle: A Secret Admirer\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and spark(npc) >= 15 and bond(npc) != \"partner\" and rel(npc) >= 0\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nart: letter\ntags: romance\nscene: A folded note tucked into a library book with a pressed flower, unsigned, the handwriting careful and slightly shaky.\ntext: Folded notes keep appearing in your library books. Unsigned. They're funny, and a little nervous, and they know things about you that only someone paying attention would know. The handwriting looks familiar.\n> left: Leave a note back\n  spark npc +small\n  say: You write back. The notes get longer. One day the last line just says: \"It's {npc}. You knew, didn't you?\" You did.\n> right: Stake out the library\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      spark npc +medium\n      rel npc +small\n      say: You catch {npc} red-handed, mid-tuck. {They} turn{s} the colour of a pyromancy exam. You both laugh until the librarian throws you out.\n    lose:\n      spark npc +tiny\n      say: You wait three nights and catch nobody. On the fourth morning the note says: \"Nice try.\"\n\n@event soc_injured_friend\ntitle: Carried\npool: expansion_social_routine_repeat10\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 6 and not npc_is(seraphine)\nper: npc\nart: cross\ntags: social danger\nscene: A student carrying another piggyback across a muddy lawn, the injured one's ankle wrapped in a scarf.\ntext: {npc} has twisted an ankle on the far side of the grounds, a long way from the infirmary, and it's getting dark. {They} {are} trying very hard to pretend it doesn't hurt.\n> left: Carry {them} on your back\n  vigor +small\n  rel npc +medium\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: It's a long way. {npc} narrates the whole journey like a horse race. By the time you reach the infirmary you're both laughing too hard to breathe.\n> right: Run for help\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You run for Sister Amaranth, who arrives with a floating stretcher and a lot of opinions. {npc} is fine. {They} send{s} you a thank-you sweet.\n\n@event soc_rival_decency\ntitle: An Unexpected Kindness\npool: expansion_social_routine_repeat10\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"rival\" or bond(npc) == \"nemesis\" or rel(npc) <= -9)\nper: npc\nart: hand\ntags: rival social\nscene: A student picking up a rival's scattered books from a wet floor, the rival frozen mid-step, not sure what to say.\ntext: {&You drop your books in the rain, and the first person to stoop and help pick them up is {npc}.|You slip on the wet stairs, and the hand that catches your elbow is {npc}'s.|The wind off the lake takes your scarf, and {npc} is the one who runs after it.} {They} hand{s} it back without a word, looking as surprised as you are.\n> left: Thank {them} properly\n  rel npc +large\n  say: \"Don't make it a thing,\" {npc} mutters. But the next day {they} nod{s} to you in the corridor. {&Something has thawed.|It's getting harder to stay enemies.|You're not sure what you are to each other anymore.}\n> right: Suspect a trick\n  rel npc -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You check for hexes. There aren't any. {npc} saw you checking. You'll both remember that.\n\n@event soc_partner_anniversary\ntitle: An Anniversary\npool: expansion_social_routine_repeat12\n# On the day itself (feedback 2026-10-25: \"make it a real anniversary\"): a whole number of years since you got together.\ntrigger: auto\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\" and bond_months(npc) >= 12 and bond_months(npc) % 12 == 0 and spark(npc) >= 40\nper: npc\nart: ring\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people on a bench by the lake at sunset, one holding out a small wrapped box, the other's hands over their mouth.\ntext: {bond_months(npc) >= 36: Three years|{bond_months(npc) >= 24: Two years|A year}} to the month since you and {npc} stopped pretending — long enough that {npc} calls things \"ours\" now. Today {they} hand{s} you a small wrapped box and won't meet your eyes.\n> left: Open it now\n  spark npc +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: Inside: a pebble from the lakeshore, polished to a shine, from the day you first really talked. \"It's stupid,\" {npc} says. It isn't. You carry it everywhere, and spend the next day hunting for something half as good to give back.\n> right: Give {them} yours first\n  gold -small\n  spark npc +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: You'd had the same idea, and spent most of your purse on it. You both open your boxes at once. They're nearly the same gift. You laugh until the sun goes down.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_summer.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — Summer recess (months 11–12).\n#  `has(summer_away)` = went home / travelled; otherwise you're\n#  at the near-empty academy or working in the village.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event sum_empty_castle\ntitle: The Empty Castle\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: month >= 11\nplan: castle\nweight: 16\nart: tower\ntags: mystery home\nscene: A vast empty dining hall in summer, chairs upturned on the tables, a single student eating alone in a shaft of dusty sunlight.\ntext: In summer the castle breathes differently. Corridors you've walked a thousand times are silent; doors that are always locked stand slightly open. A portrait yawns. Nobody is watching.\n> left: Explore the open doors\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      gold +small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: Behind one door: a forgotten trophy room. Behind another: a professor's stash of very old toffee and a purse of lost-property coins nobody will miss. You pocket it anyway.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Behind the first door: the caretaker, in a dressing gown, very surprised. You both agree this never happened.\n> right: Enjoy the quiet\n  health +small\n  say: You read in window seats, nap on the great lawn and eat breakfast at noon. For two weeks the most famous academy in the kingdom is just a big, peaceful house.\n\n@event sum_home_chores\ntitle: Help Around the House\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: month >= 11 and not has(origin_noble)\nplan: home\nweight: 14\nart: house\ntags: home\nscene: A farmhouse kitchen in high summer, a basket of laundry, a list of chores pinned to the door, a textbook hidden under a tea towel.\ntext: Home is lovely for about three days. Then the list appears on the kitchen door: the roof, the goats, the fence, the cousin's wedding. Your summer reading list is hidden under a tea towel.\n> left: Do the chores — with magic\n  challenge spell apprentice-:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      renown +small\n      say: The fence mends itself, the goats are briefly levitated, the roof is better than new. The whole street comes to watch. Your mother pretends not to be proud.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: The goats are briefly levitated. Unfortunately so is the fence. Your family is very patient about it.\n> right: Do them the slow way, and talk\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  vigor +small\n  say: You fix the fence by hand with your father, saying very little and then, suddenly, a lot. It's the best conversation you've had all year.\n\n@event sum_old_friend\ntitle: An Old Friend\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: month >= 11\nplan: home\nweight: 12\nart: group\ntags: social home\nscene: Two young people sitting on a stone wall above a village, one in academy robes, one in work clothes, a little space between them.\ntext: Your oldest friend from home is waiting at the crossroads, like always. But something's different. \"So,\" they say, not quite looking at you, \"I suppose you're too grand for us now.\"\n> left: Prove you're not\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You spend the summer exactly as you used to — orchard raids, river swims, terrible jokes. By Starfall, the space between you on the wall is gone.\n> right: Admit you've changed\n  lore +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"I have,\" you say. \"So have you.\" They laugh, surprised. You talk all night about who you're both becoming. It's different from before. It might be better.\n\n@event sum_midsummer_fair\ntime: afternoon\ntitle: The Midsummer Fair\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: (month == 11 or month == 12) and gold >= 3\nweight: 12\nart: tent\ntags: festival money\nscene: A village green full of striped tents, a carousel of enchanted wooden animals, lanterns strung between trees, a strongman lifting a cow.\ntext: The village Midsummer Fair: enchanted carousel, ring-toss, a strongman, and a booth promising \"GUESS YOUR WEIGHT IN CHEESE\". You have a few crowns in your pocket and a whole afternoon.\n> left: Win something at the ring-toss\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      mood +small\n      say: Three rings, three hits. You win a stuffed griffon the size of a sheep and sell it to a crying child's father for fifteen crowns. Everyone wins.\n    lose:\n      gold -tiny\n      mood +tiny\n      say: Every ring bounces off. The ring-toss is definitely enchanted. You don't care; you're eating candied apples.\n> right: Ride the carousel\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +medium\n  say: Your wooden hippogriff takes off, very slightly, at the top of every turn. You ride it nine times.\n\n@event sum_letter_from_friend\ntitle: A Summer Letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: letter\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 12\nper: npc\nwhen: month >= 11\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A thick letter with a doodle on the envelope arriving by owl at a summer window.\ntext: An owl arrives with a thick, slightly crumpled letter from {npc}. Six pages of summer news, two doodles, one pressed flower, and at the end, in smaller writing: \"I miss the castle. I miss you, actually.\"\n> left: Write a short reply\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You write a page. It's warm, if brief. {npc} keeps it anyway.\n> right: Write back for hours\n  health -tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You write until the candle gutters. Twelve pages. You can't remember the last time you wrote that much that wasn't an essay.\n\n@event sum_rain_week\ntitle: A Week of Rain\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: month >= 11\nweight: 10\nart: rain\ntags: home\nscene: Rain streaming down a tall window, a student curled in an armchair with a fat novel, a teapot steaming on the sill.\ntext: It rains for a solid week. Not dramatic rain: just steady, grey, endless rain. Every plan you made for the summer is quietly drowning.\n> left: Read a very long novel\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You buy an eight-hundred-page novel about a family of sea-witches, read every word and cry at the end. It's the best week of the summer.\n> right: Practise spells indoors\n  arcana +small\n  say: You practise until you can light every candle in the room with a flick. You also set fire to one curtain. It's raining; it goes out quickly.\n\n@event sum_sea_serpent\ntitle: Fishing With an Uncle\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: month >= 11\nplan: home\nweight: 8\nart: wave\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A small fishing boat on a grey-green sea, a fishing line drawn taut, something very long and scaled rising beside the hull.\ntext: Your uncle takes you fishing. Something takes the bait. Something very large. The line sings. The boat tilts. A long scaled neck rises out of the water and looks at you with polite interest.\n> left: Cut the line\n  mood +tiny\n  say: The serpent sinks back with a sigh like a disappointed whale. Your uncle tells the story for the rest of his life, and it gets bigger every time.\n> right: Try to talk to it\n  challenge soothe adept- wild:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You greet it in the old courtesies {met(brask): Professor Brask taught you|from a book you once read and never expected to need}. It bows its great head, leaves a pearl on the deck and swims away. Your uncle faints.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The serpent is not interested in conversation. It is interested in the boat. You swim home.\n\n@event sum_reading_list\ntitle: The Summer Reading List\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: month == 11 and not wrapup\nweight: 12\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A long scroll unrolling off a table and across the floor, a list of titles in tiny handwriting, a student staring at it in despair.\ntext: The summer reading list arrives by owl. It is a scroll. It unrolls across the floor, out of the door and partway down the stairs. Thorne has added a note at the bottom: \"Optional. (It is not optional.)\"\n> left: Read all of it\n  lore +medium\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You read everything. When term starts, Thorne asks a question about the last book and you're the only one who can answer. His eyebrow rises half a millimetre.\n> right: Read the first and last chapters of each\n  guile +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: A time-honoured strategy. You can talk confidently about the beginning and end of forty books. The middle remains a mystery, like most of life.\n\n@event sum_night_market\nslot: night\ntitle: The Night Market\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: month >= 11 and gold >= 15\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: money mystery\nscene: A market that only appears after midnight, stalls lit with coloured flames, hooded sellers, a bottle that seems to contain a small thundercloud.\ntext: A night market appears in a field outside the village — it wasn't there yesterday and won't be tomorrow. A hooded stall-keeper offers you a stoppered bottle with a tiny thundercloud inside. \"Fifteen crowns. What it does is a surprise.\"\n> left: Buy the bottle\n  gold -medium\n  chance even:\n    gain inspired\n    arcana +small\n    say: You uncork it in your room. The little storm rains ideas on you for a fortnight. Your notebooks overflow.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    gain sick\n    say: You uncork it in your room. The little storm rains on you. Just rain. For a fortnight. You catch a cold.\n> right: Just look around\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You wander until dawn, buying nothing, watching everything. When the sun rises the field is empty except for a single glowing feather. You keep it.\n\n@event sum_end_of_summer\ntitle: The Last Week of Summer\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: month == 12\nweight: 5\nart: sun\ntags: home\nscene: A student packing a trunk in golden late-summer light, a half-finished jar of jam, a pressed flower and a new textbook on the bed.\ntext: The last week of summer has that particular light — golden, heavy, already nostalgic. Your trunk is half-packed. Part of you is desperate to get back. Part of you wants this week to last forever.\n> left: Squeeze every drop out of it\n  mood +medium\n  health +tiny\n  say: Late swims, long dinners, one final sunset from the hill. You arrive at the barge sunburnt and happy and only slightly late.\n> right: Get ready for the new year\n  gain inspired\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You re-read your notes, sharpen your quills and polish your wand. When term starts you are, for once, ahead. It's an unfamiliar feeling.\n"},{"path":"content/events/expansion_y3.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Expansion — Final year: careers, last times, farewells.\n#  The Career Fair sets career_<employer> and its interview an\n#  offer (fair_offer, careers.grim); the last day signs one.\n# ============================================================\n\n@thread careers\nname: What comes next\npremise: The final year, and the question everyone asks at supper.\njournal:\n  seen(y3_career_fair) > 0: You walked the career fair and left your name at the stalls that mattered.\n  seen(y3_interview) > 0: You sat an interview, and waited for the letter.\n  flag(career_offered): They wrote back: they'd have you.\n  seen(y3_rejection) > 0: A polite letter said no.\n  has_offer(): An offer waits on your desk with your name on it. The answer is due on the last day.\n  seen(last_career) > 0 and not flag(took_none): On the last day, you said yes to what comes next.\n  flag(took_none): On the last day you said yes to nothing yet, and that was an answer too.\nstatus:\n  resolved: seen(last_career) > 0\n\n@event y3_career_fair\ntitle: The Career Fair\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: final_year and (month == 2 or (month == 3 and flag(fair_later)))\npriority: 42\ntype: menu\nart: scroll\ntags: study story\nthread: careers\nscene: The great hall lined with recruiters' booths — a Conclave mage in silver, a librarian behind a wall of books, a circus ringmaster juggling fire.\ntext: {month == 3: The fair's second week, and the great hall is half packed: the booths that are left have stopped being polite and started being keen. This is the last chance to leave your name.|Every guild, order and institution in the kingdom has sent someone to the great hall. They are all very polite. They are all, very clearly, shopping. Where will you leave your name?}\n> conclave: The Conclave of the Seven Towers\n  hint: Apprentice archmages. The interview tests your Arcana.\n  flag career_conclave\n  queue y3_interview now\n  say: You leave your name with the mage in silver, who writes it down without looking up. The interview is set.\n> library: The Royal Library\n  hint: Keepers of every book ever written. The interview tests your Lore.\n  flag career_library\n  queue y3_interview now\n  say: The librarian takes your name and asks what you're reading at the moment. You answer. The interview is set.\n> guild: The Guild of Alchemists\n  hint: Potions, profit, explosions. The interview tests your Alchemy.\n  flag career_guild\n  queue y3_interview now\n  say: The alchemist at the guild's booth shakes your hand and checks it for burns. The interview is set.\n> unseen: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs\n  hint: They found you, actually. The interview tests your Guile.\n  flag career_unseen\n  queue y3_interview now\n  say: A woman nobody else seems to notice takes your name and tells you where to be. The interview is set.\n> circus: The Travelling Circus of Wonders\n  hint: Wagons, crowds, illusions. The interview tests your Charm.\n  flag career_circus\n  queue y3_interview now\n  say: The ringmaster juggles your name into the air, catches it and writes it down. The interview is set.\n> teaching: Stay at Lumenhold as a junior teacher\n  hint: Needs a faculty member who trusts you: Thorne, Maud or the Headmistress.\n  when: rel(thorne) >= 15 or rel(maud) >= 15 or rel(isolde) >= 15\n  locked: Nobody on the faculty knows you well enough to vouch for you.\n  flag career_teaching\n  queue y3_interview now\n  say: Your old professor puts your name forward to the faculty, with a few words you don't hear. The interview is set.\n> none: Not yet — you'd rather not decide\n  mood +tiny\n  if month == 2:\n    flag fair_later\n  say: You collect a tote bag, three pamphlets and a free quill. {month == 2: The future can wait one more month; the fair's second week is next month.|The fair packs up for good. The future will have to come and find you, by letter or at the registrar's window.}\n\n@event y3_interview\ntitle: The Interview\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: scales\ntags: study story\nthread: careers\nscene: A small panelled room, a long table, three interviewers and one empty chair facing them.\ntext: Three people behind a long table. One takes notes; one smiles too much; one says nothing at all, which is clearly the one who decides. \"Tell us,\" says the smiling one, \"why you.\"\n> left: Let your record speak\n  iv_score = 0\n  challenge research adept:\n    win:\n      iv_score += 1\n      queue y3_interview_practical now\n      say: You talk about what you've learned and, more importantly, what you got wrong and fixed. The silent one writes a single word. You suspect it's \"yes\".\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      queue y3_interview_practical now\n      say: You recite your achievements like a shopping list. The silent one looks at the clock. \"Now,\" says the smiling one, \"a small practical.\"\n> right: Tell them a story\n  iv_score = 0\n  challenge negotiate adept wild:\n    win:\n      iv_score += 1\n      queue y3_interview_practical now\n      say: You tell them about the night the lake froze, or the night you caught a star, or the night you were wrong. The silent one laughs. Out loud.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      queue y3_interview_practical now\n      say: The story goes on too long and ends in the wrong place. Somewhere in the middle you lost them, and yourself. \"Now,\" says the smiling one, \"a small practical.\"\n\n@event y3_interview_practical\ntitle: The Practical\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: scales\ntags: study story\nthread: careers\nscene: A plain table in a panelled room set with a single task, three interviewers watching a student roll up their sleeves.\ntext: The note-taker sets a task on the table. {flag(career_conclave): A ward with a flaw in it, somewhere.|{flag(career_library): A crumbling manuscript in three hands and no catalogue.|{flag(career_guild): A cauldron, a recipe with one ingredient missing, and a clock.|{flag(career_unseen): A sealed letter, and the request to read it without breaking the seal.|{flag(career_circus): An empty stage, a sceptical crowd of three, and five minutes.|A class of imaginary first-years, and a lesson to give them.}}}}} \"In your own time,\" says the smiling one, and turns over an hourglass.\n> left: Do it properly\n  if flag(career_conclave):\n    challenge spell expert drift low:\n      win:\n        iv_score += 1\n        say: You find the flaw, name it and close it, and the ward rings like a struck glass. The silent one leans forward for the first time.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: The flaw finds you first. The ward fizzes out in your hands, and the note-taker writes for a long time.\n  elif flag(career_library):\n    challenge research expert drift low:\n      win:\n        iv_score += 1\n        say: Three hands, three centuries, one lost chapter. You date them all before the sand runs out. The silent one nods, once.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: You date the second hand a century wrong, and know it the moment you say it. The note-taker writes for a long time.\n  elif flag(career_guild):\n    challenge brew expert drift low:\n      win:\n        iv_score += 1\n        say: You find the missing ingredient by its absence and the cauldron settles to a perfect simmer. Someone behind the table sniffs, approvingly.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: The cauldron spits and turns a colour no recipe asks for. The note-taker writes for a long time.\n  elif flag(career_unseen):\n    challenge deceive expert drift low:\n      win:\n        iv_score += 1\n        say: Steam, a thin blade, a little patience. You read it, reseal it and hand it back. The silent one smiles, which is somehow worse.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: The seal cracks with a sound like a shot. The silent one does not look surprised. The note-taker writes for a long time.\n  elif flag(career_circus):\n    challenge negotiate expert drift low:\n      win:\n        iv_score += 1\n        say: Five minutes of nonsense and wonder, and the crowd of three are on their feet. One of them is the silent one.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: The crowd of three stays seated, arms folded, for all five minutes. The note-taker writes for a long time.\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate using lore expert drift low:\n      win:\n        iv_score += 1\n        say: You teach the empty chairs the thing you most wish someone had taught you, and forget for a while that they're empty. The silent one nods.\n      lose:\n        mood -small\n        say: You lose the imaginary class in the first minute, which you hadn't known was possible. The note-taker writes for a long time.\n  queue y3_interview_question now\n> right: Ask questions before you start\n  mood -tiny\n  iv_score += 1\n  if iv_score >= 2:\n    say: You ask three good questions, then do the task carefully and well. It's slower than they'd like. The silent one doesn't seem to mind.\n  else:\n    iv_score -= 1\n    say: You ask three good questions and run out of sand before you've finished. \"Careful,\" says the smiling one, in a tone that isn't quite praise.\n  queue y3_interview_question now\n\n@event y3_interview_question\ntitle: The Silent One's Question\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: scales\ntags: study story\nthread: careers\nscene: A small panelled room, an interviewer who hasn't spoken leaning forward over folded hands, a student on the other side of the table very still.\ntext: At last the silent one speaks. \"Tell me about a time you did something you weren't proud of,\" they say, \"and whether you'd do it again.\"\n> left: Tell the truth\n  alignment +tiny\n  if alignment >= 20:\n    iv_score += 1\n    say: You tell them the truth, all of it, and what you learned, and that you wouldn't. The silent one is quiet for a long moment. \"Thank you,\" they say, and it sounds like the end of a search.\n  else:\n    say: You tell them the truth, and halfway through you realise you might do it again. So does the silent one. \"Honest,\" they say. It isn't clear whether that's good.\n  queue y3_interview_verdict now\n> right: Tell them what they want to hear\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive expert drift low:\n    win:\n      iv_score += 1\n      say: A small mistake, gracefully regretted, neatly learned from. It isn't quite true. The silent one writes something down and underlines it.\n    lose:\n      iv_score -= 1\n      mood -small\n      say: The story is too neat, and the silent one has heard it before, from better liars. \"I see,\" they say, and you hear the door closing.\n  queue y3_interview_verdict now\n\n@event y3_interview_verdict\ntitle: We'll Be in Touch\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: letter\ntags: study story\nthread: careers\nscene: A student walking out of a panelled room into a bright corridor, loosening their collar, a closed door behind them.\ntext: \"We'll be in touch,\" says the smiling one, and stands, and so you stand too. In the corridor your legs feel like someone else's. {iv_score >= 3: You've never been so sure of anything.|{iv_score >= 2: You think it went well. You think.|{iv_score >= 1: It could have gone either way.|You know exactly how it went.}}}\n> ok: Wait for the letter\n  if iv_score >= 2:\n    flag career_offered\n    fair_offer\n    queue y3_offer_letter in 2\n    say: You go back to your room and lie on the bed and don't move for an hour. Somewhere, three people are deciding.\n  elif iv_score >= 1:\n    queue y3_waitlist in 2\n    say: You go back to your room and replay every answer. The better ones get better each time; the bad ones get worse.\n  else:\n    queue y3_rejection in 2\n    say: You go back to your room and don't replay anything at all. Some things you just know.\n\n@event y3_waitlist\ntitle: The Waiting List\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: letter\ntags: story\nthread: careers\nscene: A thin envelope on a dormitory desk, a single page beside it, a student reading it a second time with their chin on their fist.\ntext: A thin letter. You are \"a strong candidate\" and on \"a short list\", and they \"hope to write again\". It's not a no. It's not anything.\n> left: Write back and make your case\n  challenge appeal journeyman+ drift low:\n    win:\n      flag career_offered\n      fair_offer\n      queue y3_offer_letter in 1\n      say: Two pages, plain and sure: why you, why them, why now. A fortnight later, a heavier envelope.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: You write two pages, then four, then a fifth you shouldn't send, and do. No heavier envelope comes.\n> right: Let it lie and see\n  mood -tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    flag career_offered\n    fair_offer\n    queue y3_offer_letter in 2\n    say: You let it lie. Two months later, when you'd almost stopped looking, the heavier envelope comes after all.\n  else:\n    say: You let it lie. It stays lying. Other paths open; you try not to look back at this one.\n\n@event y3_rejection\ntitle: A Polite Letter\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: queued\nart: letter\ntags: story\nthread: careers\nscene: A crumpled letter on a windowsill, a student looking out at the lake with their arms folded.\ntext: A short letter, very politely written. They were \"impressed by your enthusiasm\". They regret. They wish you every success.\n> left: Ask them what went wrong\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You write and ask. To your surprise they answer, frankly, two pages of it. It stings, and it's the most useful thing anyone's told you all year.\n> right: Throw it in the lake\n  mood +small\n  say: You fold it into a boat and launch it from the jetty. It sinks with great dignity. Some doors close; there are a great many doors.\n\n@event y3_offer_letter\ntitle: The Offer\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: flag(career_offered)\nart: letter\ntags: story\nthread: careers\nscene: An envelope with a heavy wax seal on a dormitory desk, unopened, a hand hovering over it.\ntext: A heavy envelope with a seal you recognise from the fair. Inside, a single page: an offer. A place. A future with a start date. {iv_score >= 3: There is a signing purse, too, which they call \"a token\".|} The contract itself is signed on the last day, with any other you've had.\n> left: Say yes today\n  flag career_accepted\n  mood +medium\n  if iv_score >= 3:\n    gold +medium\n  say: You write \"yes\" so hard the nib goes through. The contract will wait for the last day, they write back, but the place is held for you. You pin the letter above your bed and look at it every night.\n> right: Ask them to wait\n  mood -tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You write back asking for time. They grant it, coolly: the offer stands until the last day, and not an hour after.\n\n@event y3_first_year_mentee\ntitle: Your First-Year\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month <= 4\nweight: 5\nart: feather\ntags: social\nscene: A tiny first-year trotting beside a fourth-year across the courtyard, firing questions, clutching an enormous timetable.\ntext: Fourth-years are assigned a first-year to mentor. Yours is called Pim, is about the size of a large cat, and has already asked forty-one questions, including \"are the stairs alive\" and \"are YOU alive\".\n> left: Answer every question honestly\n  mood +small\n  charm +small\n  say: Yes, the stairs are alive. Yes, you are alive. No, the lake is not \"friendly\". Pim takes notes. By spring, Pim is explaining things to other first-years, wrongly but with total confidence.\n> right: Teach Pim to find answers alone\n  lore +small\n  say: You hand Pim a library card and a map. Pim is furious for a week, then unstoppable. The librarian now knows Pim by name. And fears Pim, slightly.\n\n@event y3_last_lanterns\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Last Lantern Night\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month == 2\nweight: 40\nart: lantern\ntags: festival\nscene: A lone student on the shore at night holding an unlit lantern, hundreds of others already drifting over the dark lake.\ntext: Your last Lantern Night. You stand on the shore with an unlit lantern and a pen and realise you have no idea what to wish for. Three years ago it was obvious. Now it isn't.\n> left: Wish for the people you'll leave behind\n  mood +medium\n  say: You write all their names. It takes the whole lantern. It rises slowly, heavily, and you watch it until it is just a star among stars.\n> right: Leave it blank\n  lore +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You let it go empty. Strangely, it rises higher than all the others. Maybe the lake likes not knowing either.\n\n@event y3_thesis\ntitle: The Last Chapter\nclass: story\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month >= 5 and month <= 8 and member(thesis)\nweight: 16\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A desk buried in drafts, a wall of pinned notes connected by string, a single candle and a single exhausted student.\ntext: Your thesis needs one more chapter. You could write something safe and solid, or chase the question your sources keep circling and nobody has answered in three hundred years: why the academy was built above a lake.\n> left: Safe and solid\n  progress thesis +medium\n  say: Forty pages on the thermodynamics of hovering charms. Impeccable. Unremarkable. Your examiners will sleep well.\n> right: The question nobody answered\n  challenge research expert wild:\n    win:\n      progress thesis +large\n      renown +medium\n      say: The chapter is so good that your supervisor shows it round the staff room, and the headmistress asks for the only copy — and then, very politely, does not give it back.\n    lose:\n      progress thesis +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: You chase the question until it runs out of archive. The chapter ends mid-sentence, like the founders' ledger. The examiners are not amused.\n\n@event y3_portrait\ntitle: Sitting for the Portrait\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month >= 7\nweight: 5\nart: mirror\ntags: story\nscene: An old painter at an easel in a sunlit studio, a student posing stiffly in robes, half-finished portraits of past graduates watching from the walls.\ntext: Every graduate's portrait hangs in the long gallery — and every portrait, eventually, starts talking. You have one sitting to decide who will be gossiping on that wall for the next three hundred years.\n> left: Pose solemn and dignified\n  renown +small\n  say: The painter captures you looking wise and distant. Your portrait will be dignified. It will also be dreadfully bored.\n> right: Pose as yourself, mid-laugh\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Your portrait is laughing. Three days after it's hung, it starts telling the other portraits jokes. The gallery has never been louder.\n\n@event y3_letter_to_self\ntitle: A Letter to Your First-Year Self\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: final_year and month >= 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A fourth-year writing by candlelight, a box of keepsakes open beside them: a pressed flower, a ticket stub, a borrowed wand returned.\ntext: The Divination department runs a tradition: write a letter to your first-year self. They swear it will be delivered. Nobody knows how. Some fourth-years swear they received theirs.\n> left: Tell them it'll be all right\n  chance likely:\n    mood +medium\n    say: You write: \"It's going to be all right. Not easy. All right.\" The next morning the letter is gone, and you remember, very faintly, reading it once on a sky-barge.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You try to write something comforting, and it keeps coming out as a list of warnings. You seal it anyway. The next morning it is gone.\n> right: Warn them about everything\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write eleven pages of warnings. The letter is returned unopened with a note in Divination ink: \"Some things must be learned the hard way.\"\n\n@event y3_maud_seeds\ntitle: Seeds for Later\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month >= 9 and met(maud) and rel(maud) >= 9\nactor: maud\nweight: 5\nart: flower\ntags: nature social\nscene: An old woman pressing a small paper packet of seeds into a student's hands at the garden gate, bees drifting in the evening light.\ntext: Maud presses a paper packet into your hands. \"Seeds,\" she says. \"From the secret garden. Plant them wherever you end up. They'll grow anywhere someone is a bit kind to them.\" She sniffs. \"Like you did.\"\n> left: Hug her\n  rel maud +large\n  mood +medium\n  say: She protests for exactly one second and then hugs back, fierce and small and smelling of rosemary. \"Go on,\" she says. \"Before I get soppy.\"\n> right: Promise to come back and visit\n  rel maud +medium\n  flag promised_maud\n  say: \"Every summer,\" you promise. \"We'll see,\" she says, but she writes it on her calendar, in ink, which for Maud is a blood oath.\n\n@event y3_last_duel\ntime: morning\ntitle: One Last Duel\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: final_year and month >= 6 and (member(duelling) or flag(tournament_champion)) and met(lyra)\nactor: lyra\nweight: 16\nart: duel\ntags: rival\nscene: Two duelists bowing in an empty dueling ring at dawn, no crowd, no referee, just the mist and the lake beyond.\ntext: Dawn, the empty dueling ring, no crowd. Lyra is waiting. \"No referee,\" she says. \"No points. Just one last duel, for us. So we know.\"\n> left: Duel her with everything\n  challenge combat vs lyra.arcana +2:\n    win:\n      rel lyra +medium\n      vigor +small\n      say: It lasts eleven minutes. Nobody wins. You both end up flat on your backs, laughing at the sky. \"Now we know,\" she says.\n    lose:\n      rel lyra +medium\n      health -medium\n      say: She wins, cleanly. Then she helps you up and doesn't let go of your hand for a moment longer than necessary. \"Now we know.\"\n> right: Lower your wand and just talk\n  study -tiny\n  rel lyra +large\n  if spark(lyra) >= 20 and rel(lyra) >= 0:\n    spark lyra +small\n  say: You talk until the mist burns off. About fire, and mothers, and what comes after. You both miss the morning lecture. It turns out you didn't need to duel to know.\n\n@event y3_time_capsule\ntitle: The Time Capsule\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nwhen: final_year and month >= 8 and bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) >= 2\nweight: 5\nart: key\ntags: social\nscene: A group of students kneeling around a hole under an old oak, lowering in a battered tin box sealed with wax.\ntext: Your friends have a battered tin box, a shovel and a plan: bury a time capsule under the old oak, to be opened in twenty years. Everyone puts something in. What will you give?\n> left: Something precious\n  mood +medium\n  say: You put in the first thing you ever enchanted. It's ugly and crooked and it still hums. Someone cries, and blames the dirt.\n> right: Something ridiculous\n  mood +large\n  say: You put in a single sock, labelled \"EVIDENCE\". In twenty years, nobody will remember why. That's the point.\n\n@event y3_speech\ntitle: The Graduation Speech\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns and (renown >= 40 or gpa >= 3.0)\npriority: 66\nactor: isolde\nart: crown\ntags: story\nscene: A headmistress handing a scroll to a student in an empty great hall, rows of chairs set out for a ceremony that hasn't happened yet.\ntext: The headmistress catches you in the great hall, where chairs are already being set out. \"Each year one graduate speaks for their class,\" she says. \"This year, the faculty would like it to be you.\"\n> left: Accept and write something honest\n  renown +large\n  mood +medium\n  rel isolde +small\n  say: You write about fear, and friends, and the lake, and the things you didn't know you'd miss. When you practise it alone, your voice cracks in the same place every time. You leave it in.\n> right: Decline — someone else deserves it\n  rel isolde +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: \"Interesting,\" says the headmistress, which from her is a medal. The speech goes to a nervous first-generation student who absolutely brings the house down.\n"},{"path":"content/events/festivals.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Festivals as chains (playtest 2, X6 and X16).\n#  Starfall (month 12, years 1-3): star jars at the observatory\n#  as the afternoon's festival visit, the night itself (`starfall`\n#  in calendar.grim) with its follow-ups here, and the same night\n#  for those at home or on an internship.\n#  The Midwinter Ball (month 4): something to wear at noon, the\n#  ice garden as the afternoon's festival visit, the ball itself\n#  (`midwinter_ball` in calendar.grim), the last waltz with\n#  whoever you went with, and the snowball war after.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Starfall ----------------\n\n@event starfall_jars\ntitle: Star Jars\nplace: observatory\nreplaces: visit1\nwhen: month == 12 and year < 4 and not off_campus\nrepeat: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: star\ntags: festival\nscene: Long trestle tables under the observatory dome covered in glass jars, students painting stars on them, one of the Stargazers handing out corks.\ntext: {The Stargazers have set up tables under the dome, and everyone is making a jar to catch a falling star in tonight. Nobody has caught one in years. Everyone tries.|The jar tables are out under the dome again, and the first-years are making theirs all wrong.|Star jars again. You know which glass holds and which cracks in the cold.} {The Stargazers are arguing about the forecast.|Somebody has painted a jar entirely gold.|The telescope keeps swinging toward the lake.}\n> left: Make a proper jar\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  starfall_jar = 1\n  say: {Thick glass, a cork sealed with wax, a rune for luck scratched into the lid. \"Better odds tonight,\" says one of the Stargazers. \"Theoretically.\"|You buy the good glass and take your time over it. It's the best jar on the table, and you know it.|You pick the glass that holds in the cold without thinking, and seal it twice. It's the jar the first-years copy.}\n> right: Help the Stargazers set up the roof\n  renown +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  starfall_jar = 0\n  say: {You haul blankets and telescopes up the observatory stairs all afternoon. Your arms ache. The Stargazers save you the best spot on the roof.|Blankets, cushions, a kettle that has to be carried up four hundred steps. The Stargazers know your name by the end of it.|You haul the blankets up and turn the telescope back from the lake three times. It keeps swinging back. The Stargazers give up and save you a spot.}\n\n@event starfall_together\ntitle: A Blanket for Two\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 3\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nart: star\ntags: festival romance\nscene: Two students lying on a blanket on the lawn at night, wrapped in one shawl, a falling star streaking over the castle towers.\ntext: You find {partner} on the lawn below the observatory with a blanket already spread for two and a flask of something hot. \"I saved you the sky,\" {they} say{s}. The first star falls before you've sat down.\n> left: Stay out until dawn\n  spark partner +small\n  mood +medium\n  health -small\n  say: You stay out until the sky turns grey, counting stars and then not counting anything. You're both frozen and hoarse and late for breakfast, and it was worth all of it.\n> right: Tell {them} what you wished for\n  spark partner +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You say it out loud, which you're told ruins a wish. {partner} goes very quiet and then tells you {their} own. Neither of you minds if it's ruined.\n\n@event starfall_wish\ntitle: A Wish on a Falling Star\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 3\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: star\ntags: festival\nscene: A student kneeling on the observatory roof with eyes closed, hands clasped, a bright star falling over the dark lake below.\ntext: The oldest Starfall custom: pick one star, the brightest you'll see all night, and give it one wish. The Stargazers say a wish you spend on someone else falls slower. Nobody knows what that means. A very bright one is coming down over the lake.\n> left: Wish for your own year\n  gain inspired\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You wish hard for your own year, and the star burns out over the water. It feels like a promise you've made to yourself, which is heavier than you expected.\n> right: Wish for someone else\n  mood +small\n  say: You don't say who. The star falls slowly, or you imagine it does, all the way down to the lake.\n\n@event starfall_caught\ntitle: The Star in the Jar\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 3\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntype: menu\nart: star\ntags: festival\nscene: A glass jar on a dormitory windowsill holding a tiny glowing star, a pale light on the curtains, frost on the outside of the glass.\ntext: The star you caught at Starfall still glows in its jar on your windowsill, a little dimmer each night. A collector in the village has offered good money for it. One of the Stargazers says it would like to go back up, if stars like anything.\n> keep: Keep it by your bed\n  mood +medium\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You keep it, and buy oil to keep the window frost off it. It fades by spring, slowly, like a coal. The last night it glows, you sit up with it.\n> sell: Sell it to the collector\n  gold +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: The collector holds it up to the light and pays without haggling. The windowsill looks very dark that night.\n> release: Let it go from the roof\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: You climb the observatory at midnight and open the jar. It hangs there a moment, then rises, slow and sure, until you can't tell it from the others. A few people saw. The story goes round by breakfast.\n\n@event starfall_at_home\nslot: night\ntitle: Starfall at Home\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 12 and year < 4 and has(plan_home)\nrepeat: 3\npriority: 30\nart: star\ntags: festival home\nscene: A family on the roof of a cottage at night, wrapped in quilts, a grandmother pointing at a streak of light across the sky.\ntext: {Starfall at home. There's no observatory here, only the cottage roof and the whole family on it in quilts, as when you were small.|Starfall at home again. Someone has saved you the warm patch of roof by the chimney.|The roof, the quilts, the chimney. Someone asks what the academy does for Starfall, and you find you'd rather be here.} {The village dogs howl at every star.|Somebody's brought cocoa.|A neighbour is telling the one about the star that fell in the duck pond.}\n> left: Tell them about the academy\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You tell them about the towers and the lake and the staircase that moves, and they ask questions until the stars run out. It's past three when you climb down, cold to the bone and glad.|Everyone wants a story. You give them the best ones, slightly improved, until your feet have gone numb on the slates. Nobody minds.|You tell them about the academy, and then the neighbour tells the one about the duck pond, which is better. You laugh until the slates are cold.}\n> right: Slip off to the hill alone\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: {From the hill behind the cottage you name every star the way they taught you, and a few the way you learned as a child. Both names are right.|Alone on the hill, you watch the sky the way an academy student does now, and miss, a little, the way you used to.|From the hill you can still hear the neighbour telling the one about the duck pond. You find you'd rather be on the roof, and walk back before the end.}\n\n@event starfall_on_internship\nslot: night\ntitle: Starfall Far From Home\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 12 and year < 4 and has(plan_internship)\nrepeat: 3\npriority: 30\nart: star\ntags: festival\nscene: A group of interns on a flat roof far from the academy, wrapped in coats, watching falling stars over unfamiliar rooftops.\ntext: Starfall, and you're far from the observatory roof. {member(intern_expedition): Up at the ridge camp the sky is enormous, and the expedition's astronomers are working through it, counting every streak.|{member(intern_thornwick): Thornwick marks Starfall with illusions, and the lawn is full of false stars falling among the real ones. {met(tamsin): Tamsin finds you and points out which ones are theirs.|}|{member(intern_field_station): At the field station the creatures are restless under the falling stars, and someone has to sit up with them.|{member(intern_royal_library) or member(intern_unseen_office): The city has no lawns, only rooftops, and every one of them has people on it tonight.|The other interns have found a flat roof and a crate of something fizzy, and your supervisor is pretending not to know.}}}}\n> left: Join the others on the roof\n  mood +medium\n  perform internship -tiny\n  say: {~You lie on a strange roof among people you met two months ago and count stars until they feel like old friends. Tomorrow's work goes slowly.|Coats, a borrowed blanket, someone's terrible song. It's not the observatory roof, and it's good anyway, though the morning's work comes out half asleep.}\n> right: Keep working through the night\n  perform internship +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~You work through it with the window open, glancing up now and then. Your supervisor finds the finished work on the desk at dawn and raises an eyebrow, which is a great deal from them.|The stars fall outside while you finish the job. You see a few. Your supervisor sees all of it.}\n\n# ---------------- The Midwinter Ball ----------------\n\n@event ball_robes\nslot: noon\ntitle: Something to Wear\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 4 and term\nrepeat: 4\npriority: 25\nart: mirror\ntags: festival money\nscene: A dormitory room strewn with robes, a student holding up a moth-eaten formal robe against themselves before a cracked mirror.\ntext: {year == 1: The Midwinter Ball is tonight, and you have nothing to wear that isn't a uniform or a nightshirt.|{final_year: The last ball of your life as a student. The old robes look very tired.|{~Ball night again, and last year's robes have shrunk, somehow, in the trunk.|Ball night again. Your robes have a stain on the cuff you'd forgotten about.|Ball night again. The robes fit, but you're tired of the sight of them.}}} {~The village tailor is open till three.|Your roommate is already dressed and gloating.|The mirror has opinions.}\n> left: Buy new robes in the village\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {~Deep blue, with a lining like the night sky. In the tailor's mirror you look, for once, like someone in a portrait.|New robes, a sensible colour and an insensible price. They fit as if they were waiting for you.|Something bold, chosen in a hurry. The mirror's opinions, for once, are all good ones.}\n> right: Glamour your old ones\n  challenge spell apprentice+:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      guile +tiny\n      say: The old robes shimmer into silk. Nobody guesses, and three people ask where you had them made.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: At the stroke of nine your glamour slips, and the mended elbow shows through the silk like a confession. You keep your arm by your side all night.\n\n@event ball_ice_garden\ntitle: The Ice Garden\nplace: courtyard\nreplaces: visit1\nwhen: month == 4 and term\nrepeat: 4\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: snow\ntags: festival\nscene: A castle courtyard turned into a garden of ice, frozen roses along glittering paths, a fountain stopped mid-splash, students walking under lanterns.\ntext: {The frost-mages have turned the courtyard into an ice garden for the ball. Frozen roses line the paths, and the fountain has stopped mid-splash.|The ice garden again, bigger this year, with a hedge maze of frost.|The ice garden, and you know its paths by heart now.} {Couples are walking it before the music has started.|A first-year has got their tongue stuck to a rose.|The lanterns make the ice glow blue.}\n> left: Walk it slowly\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You walk every path twice, until your toes are numb and your head is clear. The fountain's frozen spray catches the light like a held breath.|You find the centre of the maze, where someone has left a frozen bench and a frozen apple. You sit a while, until the cold comes up through your shoes.|You know the paths by heart, so you take the ones nobody else finds. Under the blue lanterns you have the garden to yourself for a while.}\n> right: Help the frost-mages finish it\n  arcana +tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {They show you how to hold the cold in a petal until it sets. Your hands ache for an hour. One of the roses on the path is yours.|You shape a whole archway of ice under instruction, fingers stiff with cold, and it holds. The frost-mages nod, which is how they applaud.|This year they let you shape a lantern of ice. It glows blue on the path, and nobody knows it's yours but you.}\n> up[item:midnight_robes]: Wear the midnight-blue\n  renown +small\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {In the midnight-blue among the frozen roses, people turn to look, and keep looking. You walk the paths slowly on purpose.|Someone asks where you had your robes made. Someone else asks who you are. Your toes are frozen and you don't care.|The ice catches the silver at your hem. A first-year whispers that you look like the portrait in the east stair.}\n\n@event ball_last_dance\ntitle: The Last Waltz\ntrigger: queued\nrepeat: 4\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nactor: npc\nart: music\ntags: festival romance\nscene: The great hall near midnight, candles burning low, a single couple still turning slowly beneath a dripping ice-crystal chandelier.\ntext: Near midnight the musicians play the last waltz, slow and a little sad. {npc} holds out a hand without saying anything. Half the hall has gone to bed. The chandelier is dripping.\n> left: Dance until the music stops\n  spark npc +small\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You dance it slowly, and then once more after the musicians have packed up, to no music at all. Nobody else is left to see, which is the point.\n> right: Slip out onto the frozen terrace\n  rel npc +small\n  spark npc +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: Outside it's bitter, and the stars are out over the lake. You talk until you can't feel your fingers, about nothing and then about everything, and go back in blue-lipped and grinning.\n\n@event ball_snowball_war\nslot: night\ntitle: The Snowball War\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 4 and term\nrepeat: 4\npriority: 20\nart: snow\ntags: festival social\nscene: A snowy courtyard at night full of students in ruined ball robes hurling snowballs from behind snow forts, a prefect ducking.\ntext: {After the ball, someone throws the first snowball in the courtyard, and within a minute every house is at war, in ball robes.|The snowball war, as much a tradition as the ball. The forts went up during the last waltz.|The snowball war again. The first-years have built a fort that is frankly impressive.} {A prefect is trying to restore order and failing.|Someone has enchanted their snowballs, which is cheating.|The robes are already ruined.}\n> left: Charge the enemy fort\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: You take the fort with a war cry and a single perfect throw. Your house carries you round the fountain on its shoulders. Your robes will never recover.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood +small\n      say: A snowball, then fourteen. You go down laughing under a drift, and someone has to dig you out. Worth it.\n> right: Watch from the cloister with cocoa\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  say: {You watch from the cloister, warm and dry, and cheer for whoever's losing. Nobody throws at you. Mostly.|You hold the cocoa and the coats, and you're the only one who goes to bed warm.|You watch the first-years' fort hold against all comers. Yours are the only robes in the courtyard that survive the night.}\n> up[item:ward_cloak]: Pull the ward-stitched hood up\n  renown +tiny\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Snowballs slide off the ward thread like rain off a window. You walk through the whole war untouched, and both sides declare you a neutral country.|You stroll into the enemy fort with your hood up. Twelve snowballs later they surrender out of sheer confusion.|The cloak turns every snowball aside. By the end the first-years are following you around for cover.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/fixtures.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Fixtures (design §5.5): the people of a place or a track,\n#  promoted from the background. Lighter ladders than the core\n#  cast: an introduction where they work (or through their course\n#  or job, via `lead:`), two or three repeatable cards that come\n#  when you're there or when they look you up (rarely: `seeks:`),\n#  and one milestone. No romance; nothing touches the mystery.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Sister Amaranth (infirmary; Alchemy & Healing) ----------------\n\n@event amaranth_intro\ntitle: No Excuses\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: infirmary\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: not met(amaranth)\nweight: 30\nart: cross\ntags: social\nscene: A stern healer in a white apron holding a student's wrist up to the window light, a row of tonic bottles behind her.\ntext: You came in for a plaster. Sister Amaranth takes your hand, turns it to the light and asks how it happened. You tell her. She waits. You tell her the true version. \"Better,\" she says, and reaches for a bottle that is not a plaster.\n> left: Drink whatever it is\n  meet amaranth\n  health +small\n  mood -small\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: It tastes of iron and regret. The hand stops throbbing before you reach the door. \"Come back if it swells,\" she calls. \"Don't come back if it doesn't.\"\n> right: Just the plaster, thanks\n  meet amaranth\n  gold -tiny\n  rel amaranth -tiny\n  say: She gives you the plaster, and a look you will remember the next time you lie to a healer. \"Three crowns,\" she says. \"The look is free.\"\n\n@event amaranth_rounds\ntime: evening night\ntitle: Evening Rounds\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: rel(amaranth) >= 0\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: potion\ntags: social nature\nscene: A healer and a student walking a long infirmary ward at dusk, the student carrying a tray of small glass bottles, lamps being lit along the beds.\ntext: {Sister Amaranth catches you in the corridor and hands you a tray of tonics without a word of explanation.|Amaranth holds out the tonic tray as you pass. It seems you are on the rota now.|The tray is waiting for you on the ward table, labelled YOURS in Amaranth's square hand.} {Six beds on the ward today.|A first-year with hiccups that light up.|Someone has charmed their own eyebrows off.}\n> left: Do the rounds with her\n  study -tiny\n  alchemy +small\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: {She tells you what each tonic does and why it tastes like that. You remember all of it, to your surprise.|You learn which patients are brave and which are loud, and that they are rarely the same ones.|At the last bed she lets you measure the dose. \"Again,\" she says. You do it again. \"Fine.\"}\n> right: You have an essay due\n  rel amaranth -tiny\n  say: {\"So does everyone,\" she says, and takes the tray back. \"Off you go.\"|She nods, which is somehow worse than a lecture.|\"Next time,\" she says, as a fact, not a question.}\n> up[mend]: Mend the worst one yourself\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: {You close a split knuckle with a whisper of light. Amaranth inspects it for a long time. \"Neat,\" she says, the highest word she owns.|The sprain knits under your hands. The patient thanks Amaranth. Amaranth points at you.|The student with no eyebrows is the worst case. You coax them halfway back. Amaranth finishes the job, and writes your name on tomorrow's tray as well.}\n\n@event amaranth_orders\ntitle: Doctor's Orders\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: rel(amaranth) >= 6 and health < 50\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 6\nart: bed\ntags: social\nscene: A healer standing in a dormitory doorway with her arms folded, a pale student halfway out of bed with a stack of books.\ntext: {Sister Amaranth has come to find you, which has never happened to anyone. \"You look like a draught of bad decisions,\" she says. \"Bed.\"|Amaranth again, at your door, arms folded. \"I can hear you coughing from the infirmary.\"|\"Bed,\" says Amaranth, before you've opened the door all the way.}\n> left: Go to bed as ordered\n  health +medium\n  mood -small\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: {You sleep fourteen hours. She checks on you twice and leaves a tonic on the sill both times.|Two days of broth and boredom. You come out of it a person again.|You argue for one more chapter. You lose, and sleep.}\n> right: \"I'm fine, honestly.\"\n  rel amaranth -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {\"Nobody who says 'honestly' is,\" she says, and leaves. You are not fine by Thursday.|She writes something on a card and walks off. It turns out to be your name, on her list.|\"Bed,\" she says again, as if you hadn't spoken. You don't go. By the weekend you wish you had.}\n\n@event amaranth_trust\ntitle: The Cupboard Key\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: rel(amaranth) >= 20\nweight: 20\nart: key\ntags: social magic\nscene: An infirmary storeroom at night lit by a single lamp, a healer pressing a small brass key into a student's palm, shelves of labelled bottles all around.\ntext: Sister Amaranth closes the storeroom door behind you both. \"I'm away for the solstice and the junior healer faints at blood,\" she says. \"You don't.\" She holds out the key to the tonic cupboard, and with it a page of her own notes on the mending charm.\n> left: Take the key and the notes\n  rel amaranth +small\n  alchemy +medium\n  say: \"Mend what you can, send the rest to me, and lie to nobody,\" she says. The notes are written for someone she expects to be good at this. You read them twice and try very hard to be.\n> right: \"I'm not ready for that.\"\n  rel amaranth +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She looks at you for a long moment. \"Good answer. Wrong, but good.\" She keeps the key. She gives you the notes anyway.\n\n# ---------------- Professor Brask (stables; Beastcraft & Flight) ----------------\n\n@event brask_shovel\ntitle: Hands Wanted\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: brask\nwhen: rel(brask) >= 0\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: paw\ntags: nature social\nscene: A weathered professor in a leather apron holding out a shovel to a student at a stable door, a hippogriff watching suspiciously from its stall.\ntext: {Professor Brask holds out a shovel as you pass the stables. She doesn't say anything. The hippogriff behind her does, loudly.|Brask hands you the shovel before you're through the door.|\"Ah,\" says Brask, and the shovel is already in your hands.} {The griffon chick is in a mood.|The owls have opinions about the new straw.|A hippogriff has eaten a glove. Possibly a professor's.}\n> left: Muck out the stalls\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel brask +small\n  say: {An hour of straw and silence. At the end Brask says, \"The grey one likes you,\" which is more than she has said to anyone this term.|She tells you a story about a manticore, a bucket and a bishop. It ends with a bite and a moral. You miss the moral.|The hippogriff bows to you on your way out. Brask pretends not to see.}\n> right: Decline, politely\n  rel brask -tiny\n  say: {\"Suit yourself,\" she says, and takes the shovel back. \"The straw won't mind.\"|\"The hippogriff doesn't want to either,\" says Brask. \"It helps anyway.\"|You hand the shovel back. Brask takes it, and the hippogriff looks at your gloves with interest. You leave quickly.}\n> up[haste]: Hasten through the stalls\n  rel brask +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: {You clear six stalls before the hippogriff has finished glaring at you. Brask watches with her arms folded and something almost like a smile.|You muck out, refill and re-straw in a blur. The griffon chick follows your hands, dizzy. \"Show-off,\" says Brask, approving.|You're done before Brask has found her own shovel. The hippogriff, outpaced, gives back the glove. Brask takes it, damp, and says nothing, which from her is thanks.}\n\n@event brask_night_owl\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Sick Owl\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: brask\nwhen: rel(brask) >= 10\nweight: 12\nart: owl\ntags: nature\nscene: A dim stable loft at night, a student sitting cross-legged beside a straw nest where a sick barn owl sleeps under a blanket, a lantern turned low.\ntext: Brask stops you in the corridor and falls into step without asking, which is a first. \"The old barn owl won't eat,\" she says. \"Somebody should sit with her tonight. Somebody quiet.\" She is looking at you.\n> left: Sit up with the owl\n  health -small\n  rel brask +medium\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: You sit in the loft till dawn, dripping water onto the owl's beak. At four she takes a mouse from your fingers. In the morning Brask looks at the empty dish for a long time, then claps you once on the shoulder, hard.\n> right: Send her to Sister Amaranth\n  rel brask -tiny\n  rel amaranth +tiny\n  say: Amaranth looks at the owl, looks at you, and says she doesn't do owls. Then she sits with it all night. Brask is not sure how to feel about this, and neither are you.\n\n@event brask_trust\ntime: evening\ntitle: Old Courtesies\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: brask\nwhen: rel(brask) >= 20\nweight: 20\nart: feather\ntags: nature magic\nscene: A professor and a student at the edge of a forest at dusk, both bowing low to a great antlered stag half-hidden in the trees.\ntext: Brask takes you to the edge of the Whisperwood one afternoon. \"Most people talk at animals,\" she says. \"A few talk to them. Fewer listen.\" She bows, very low, to something in the trees. \"Do as I do. Keep still, and keep your eyes down.\"\n> left: Bow, and learn\n  vigor +medium\n  rel brask +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: It takes until full dark, bent double until your back aches, and something huge breathes on your neck for most of it. Then the stag bows back, very slightly. Brask laughs once, loudly, the way she does once a term.\n> right: Keep your distance\n  rel brask -tiny\n  vigor +small\n  say: You stay by the path and watch. She doesn't press you. On the walk back she talks about hoof care for an hour, which you suspect is a kindness.\n\n# ---------------- Professor Vey (lecture tower; Illusion & Glamour) ----------------\n\n@event vey_pop_quiz\ntime: morning\ntitle: Is It There?\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: vey\nwhen: rel(vey) >= 0 and around(vey)\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: mask\ntags: magic study\nscene: A smiling professor in a violet scarf leaning against a corridor wall, a cup of tea in her hand, a second, slightly too perfect cup on the windowsill beside her.\ntext: {Professor Vey is in the corridor with two cups of tea. She offers you one. \"Only one of them is real,\" she says pleasantly.|Vey stops you on the stairs. \"There's a coin behind your ear. Or there isn't.\"|\"Good morning,\" says Vey, and the door you were about to open is, possibly, a wall.} {She looks delighted already.|A student behind you walks into something.|She is humming.}\n> left: Look closely\n  challenge deceive adept:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      rel vey +small\n      say: {You pick the real one. Vey raises an eyebrow. \"Insufferable,\" she says, beaming.|You catch the shimmer at the edge. \"Ah,\" says Vey. \"You've been paying attention. Dreadful habit. Keep it.\"|You lay your hand flat on it first. It's a door. Vey stops humming. \"Pity,\" she says, delighted.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You reach for the real one and close your fingers on air. Vey enjoys this very much.|Wrong. \"It's usually the other one,\" she says, kindly, which is worse.|You walk confidently into what turns out to be a wall. Vey hums on, very pleased, while you hold your nose.}\n> right: Play along and get it wrong\n  guile +small\n  rel vey +small\n  say: {You choose the false one on purpose, with feeling. Vey narrows her eyes. \"Either you're bad at this, or very good,\" she says. \"I'll find out.\"|You let her win. She knows. She lets you know she knows.|You walk into it on purpose, braced for a wall. It's a door, and you fall through it. Vey hums all the way down the corridor.}\n\n@event vey_revue\ntitle: The Faculty Revue\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: vey\nwhen: rel(vey) >= 10 and (month == 3 or month == 9)\nweight: 12\nart: music\ntags: social festival\nscene: A small stage in the Great Hall hung with violet curtains, a professor directing a nervous student holding a paper crown, chairs being set out below.\ntext: Professor Vey is producing the faculty revue, in which professors make fools of themselves for charity. \"I need someone to play a king who isn't there,\" she says. \"You'd be wonderful. You'll be terrible. Either is fine.\"\n> left: Take the part\n  challenge feat using charm journeyman+ wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel vey +medium\n      say: You are a king who isn't there, magnificently. Thorne, in the front row, says \"Hm\" twice, which the faculty agree is a standing ovation.\n    lose:\n      renown +tiny\n      mood -small\n      rel vey +small\n      say: You forget your lines, invent new ones and are, by general agreement, the best thing in it for the wrong reasons. Vey keeps the paper crown on her desk.\n  if knows(vey.tests):\n    rel vey +tiny\n    say: At the curtain call you hand her a cup of tea that isn't there. She drinks it without blinking, and gives you full marks.\n> right: Hide in the wings\n  rel vey -tiny\n  say: You run the curtain. The revue goes ahead without a king, which Vey says was the point all along.\n\n@event vey_trust\ntime: noon afternoon\ntitle: The Mirror Trick\nclass: milestone\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: vey\nwhen: rel(vey) >= 20 and around(vey)\nweight: 20\nart: mirror\ntags: magic\nscene: An empty classroom at twilight, two identical students standing either side of a tall mirror, a professor seated on a desk between them, clapping softly.\ntext: Vey keeps you after class and tilts a tall mirror towards you. Your reflection steps out of it and sits on a desk. \"The trick isn't making a copy,\" she says. \"It's knowing which of you to send. Want to see how it's done?\"\n> left: Study the trick with her\n  charm +medium\n  rel vey +small\n  mood -small\n  say: It takes a week of evenings, and you never manage the copy. On the last night your reflection refuses to go back for her. Vey talks it round over tea, then says, \"Now you know why I don't do this in public.\" You know a great deal more about faces.\n> right: Keep your own face, thanks\n  rel vey +tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: \"Sensible,\" she says, disappointed. She teaches you instead how to tell when someone else is using it. It turns out a lot of people are.\n\n# ---------------- Professor Marisol (lakeshore; Evocation's tidecalling) ----------------\n\n@event marisol_intro\ntitle: A Dry Patch of Air\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nplace: lakeshore\nactor: marisol\nwhen: not met(marisol)\nweight: 30\nart: rain\ntags: nature magic\nscene: Heavy rain on a lake shore, a woman reading on the boathouse steps beneath a perfectly dry circle of air, raindrops sliding around it as if around glass.\ntext: It is raining on the whole lake except for one circle on the boathouse steps, where a woman in a soaked hat is reading poetry, perfectly dry. She looks up. \"It's not hard,\" says Professor Marisol. \"You just ask the water to go round. Sit, if you like.\"\n> left: Sit and ask how\n  meet marisol\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel marisol +small\n  say: She explains it as a conversation. The water, apparently, is willing. By the end of the poem the rain is going round you too, for almost a minute, and then it remembers you. \"There,\" she says, and goes back to the poem.\n> right: Leave her to her book\n  meet marisol\n  say: You nod and run for cover. Behind you she reads aloud, to nobody, a poem about a storm that comes back every year to see if you've changed.\n\n@event marisol_cold_water\ntime: morning\ntitle: Cold Water Is Good for You\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: marisol\nwhen: rel(marisol) >= 0\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: wave\ntags: nature magic\nscene: Dawn mist on a still lake, a professor standing waist-deep in the water waving cheerfully to a shivering student on the jetty.\ntext: {Professor Marisol is in the lake, waist-deep, and waves at you as though this were normal.|Marisol again, in the lake. \"It's lovely once you're in,\" she calls. It is not lovely.|\"You came!\" calls Marisol from the water. You had been walking past.} {There is ice at the edges.|A heron is watching her with professional respect.|She has a book in one hand, held perfectly dry.}\n> left: Wade in\n  health -tiny\n  arcana +small\n  rel marisol +small\n  say: {She teaches you to feel the lake breathing. You cannot feel your feet. Both are, she says, normal.|You ask the water to rise and it rises, a little, around your knees. Marisol whoops like a child.|By the time you climb out you've stopped shivering, which she says is a good sign and Amaranth will say is a bad one.}\n> right: Wave back from the jetty\n  rel marisol -tiny\n  say: {She waves again, unoffended, and goes under.|\"Coward,\" she calls cheerfully. You are fine with that.|\"Next time, then,\" she calls, and goes back to her book, held perfectly dry above the water.}\n> up[flame]: Burn the chill off the shallows\n  rel marisol +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: {You hold a flame under the surface until the shallows steam. Marisol swims over to sit in your warm patch, delighted.|Your flame warms the water around your ankles and, briefly, a very surprised heron. Marisol laughs so hard she sinks.|The shallows steam around you like a bath. \"Cheating,\" calls Marisol, and paddles over to share it.}\n\n@event marisol_storm\ntitle: Weather\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: marisol\nwhen: rel(marisol) >= 12 and (season == \"autumn\" or season == \"spring\")\nweight: 12\nart: bolt\ntags: nature magic danger\nscene: A wild storm over a dark lake, a professor and a student standing on a rocky point with their arms raised, lightning splitting the sky behind them.\ntext: There's a knock at your window during the worst storm of the year. Professor Marisol is outside, hair everywhere, grinning. \"Nobody learns tides on a calm day,\" she shouts over the wind. \"Coming?\"\n> left: Go out into it\n  challenge feat using arcana adept- wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      rel marisol +medium\n      health -small\n      say: On the point, in the roar, you hold back one wave for three whole seconds. Marisol screams with joy. You go home soaked to the bone and lit up like a lamp.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel marisol +small\n      say: The lake throws you on your back. Marisol hauls you out by the collar, laughing and apologising in equal parts, and walks you all the way to the infirmary.\n> right: Close the window\n  rel marisol -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She shrugs, cheerful, and runs off into the rain. You lie awake listening to the lake and wondering what you missed.\n  if knows(marisol.poems):\n    rel marisol +tiny\n    say: In the morning there's a poem about rain under your door, in ink that's still wet. It has no last line.\n> up[heartwood]: Root yourselves a shelter\n  arcana +small\n  rel marisol +medium\n  say: On the point you grow a tree out of bare rock, roots and all, and the two of you watch the storm from under it. Marisol is delighted and soaked. \"That's not tidecalling,\" she shouts, \"but I'll allow it!\"\n\n@event marisol_trust\ntitle: The Last Line\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: marisol\nwhen: rel(marisol) >= 20\nweight: 20\nart: scroll\ntags: magic\nscene: A quiet lakeside at sunset, a professor handing a student a worn book of poems with a ribbon in it, the water perfectly still before them.\ntext: Marisol presses her old book of water poems into your hands. \"I never remember the last lines,\" she says. \"You will. My notes are in the margins; I wrote them there when I was your age. Read it on the shore. It works better on the shore.\"\n> left: Read it on the shore\n  arcana +medium\n  rel marisol +small\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You read until dark, ankle-deep. On the last page the lake answers you, softly, all the way out to the far bank. You remember the last line. You write it in, for her.\n> right: Give it back; it's hers\n  rel marisol +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She looks at the book and then at you. \"Nobody's ever given it back,\" she says, and reads you the poem herself. She forgets the last line. You tell her.\n\n# ---------------- Mabel (the tea shop) ----------------\n\n@event mabel_intro\ntitle: The Way You Open a Door\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nplace: mabels\nactor: mabel\nwhen: not met(mabel)\nweight: 30\nart: cup\ntags: social home\nscene: A cosy village tea shop with a bell over the door, a woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin of tea as a student steps in from the cold.\ntext: The bell over the door rings. The woman behind the counter looks up, considers how you came in, and reaches for a tin before you've said a word. \"Homesick,\" says Mabel, \"with a side of exam. Sit by the window, petal.\"\n> left: Sit by the window\n  meet mabel\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  rel mabel +small\n  say: The tea is exactly right and you can't say why. She brings a slice of cake you didn't order and doesn't charge for it. \"First visit,\" she says. \"After that I charge double.\"\n> right: Say you'll come back another time\n  meet mabel\n  rel mabel +tiny\n  say: She looks at you over the counter. \"Come back when you've got time to sit,\" she says. \"You'll know when.\"\n\n@event mabel_parcel\ntitle: A Tin From Mabel\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: mabel\nwhen: rel(mabel) >= 2\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 6\nart: cup\ntags: social home\nscene: A small tin of tea wrapped in brown paper and string on a dormitory pillow, a handwritten label on top and a pressed flower tucked under the string.\ntext: {A small tin arrives on the barge with your name on it, in Mabel's round hand. The label says \"FOR THE COUGH I HEARD THROUGH THE DOOR.\"|Another tin from Mabel. The label says \"FOR WHATEVER THIS WEEK IS.\"|Mabel's tins have a shelf of their own now. Today's label says only \"YOU KNOW.\"} {There's a pressed violet under the string.|It smells of honey and woodsmoke.|There's a biscuit taped to the lid.}\n> left: Bring her something back\n  gold -tiny\n  health +small\n  rel mabel +small\n  say: {You bring her a jar of honey from the market. She calls it bribery and puts it on the shelf behind the counter.|You bring her flowers from the village stall. \"Don't,\" she says. \"Drink it.\" You do. It works.|You bring her a tin of your own, labelled \"YOU KNOW\". She laughs until she has to sit down, and keeps it.}\n> right: Send it back with a note\n  rel mabel -tiny\n  say: {She sends it back again with a longer note. You give up and drink it.|The tin comes back on the next barge. The label now says \"STUBBORN.\"|You send it back with a note that says \"I KNOW\". It comes back with two biscuits taped to the lid.}\n\n@event mabel_cup\ntitle: Your Cup on the Hook\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: mabel\nwhen: rel(mabel) >= 15\nweight: 18\nart: cup\ntags: social home\nscene: A row of mismatched cups hanging on hooks behind a tea-shop counter, a woman hanging a new one with a student's initial painted on it.\ntext: Behind Mabel's counter is a row of hooks with cups on them, each painted with a letter. You've never asked whose they are. Today, while you watch, Mabel hangs a new one with your initial on it. \"Regulars,\" she says, as if that settled it.\n> left: Ask whose the others are\n  rel mabel +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: She tells you, cup by cup: a fisherman, a dead headmaster, a girl who became a duchess and still writes. \"And you,\" she says. Something in your chest unknots.\n> right: Say thank you and sit down\n  mood +small\n  health +small\n  say: You sit. She brings your tea in your cup. You don't need to say anything else, and she doesn't either.\n\n# ---------------- Mr Grimsby (the bookshop) ----------------\n\n@event grimsby_intro\ntitle: Not That One\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: grimsbys_books\nactor: grimsby\nwhen: not met(grimsby)\nweight: 30\nart: book\ntags: study money\nscene: A cramped bookshop with leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller on a ladder holding out a book to a student below, the student holding a list.\ntext: You ask for a textbook. An elderly man on a ladder says \"Ah!\" with enormous enthusiasm and hands you a history of lighthouses. \"Not what you asked for,\" says Mr Grimsby. \"What you'll need. You'll see.\"\n> left: Buy the lighthouse book\n  meet grimsby\n  gold -small\n  lore +small\n  rel grimsby +small\n  say: You read it on the barge home. Chapter four has the exact proof your essay was missing. When you go back to say so, he only nods. \"They always come back,\" he says. \"Lighthouses.\"\n> right: Say you'll think about it\n  meet grimsby\n  rel grimsby -tiny\n  say: He puts the lighthouses back on the shelf, sadly, like a doctor whose prescription has been refused. \"It'll keep,\" he says. \"So will you.\"\n\n@event grimsby_parcel\ntitle: The Wrong Book, Posted\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: grimsby\nwhen: rel(grimsby) >= 2\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 6\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A brown-paper parcel tied with string on a library desk, a student unwrapping an odd old book while a cat watches from the next chair.\ntext: {A parcel arrives from Grimsby's. Inside is a book you didn't order and a note that says \"Thought of you. Pay when convenient. A. G.\"|Another parcel from Mr Grimsby. This one is about eels.|Grimsby's parcels come monthly now, like weather.} {It smells of rain and pipe smoke.|There's a bus ticket from another century inside the cover.|The note is written on the back of a recipe.}\n> left: Keep it and pay him\n  gold -small\n  lore +small\n  rel grimsby +small\n  say: {It's the wrong book, exactly as promised, and somewhere in it is the right page. It always is.|You read it out of politeness and finish it out of something else.|You pay him, and try the recipe on the back of the note. The book and the cake are both strange, and both good.}\n> right: Send it back with the carrier\n  rel grimsby -tiny\n  say: {The carrier takes it back for nothing, grumbling. Grimsby sends a cheerful note that says \"Next time, then.\"|He sends it straight back with a second, wronger book. You keep both.|You send it back. Next month's parcel arrives anyway, as if nothing had happened, with a new recipe on the back of the note.}\n\n@event grimsby_mind_the_shop\ntitle: Mind the Shop\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: grimsby\nwhen: rel(grimsby) >= 15\nweight: 18\nart: key\ntags: study money\nscene: A student alone behind the counter of a crowded old bookshop at dusk, a ring of keys on the counter and a customer peering in through the dusty window.\ntext: Mr Grimsby has a ladder accident (a mild one; he lands on the poetry) and needs someone to mind the shop for a week. \"You know where everything is,\" he says, which is untrue. \"Better than me,\" he adds, which is.\n> left: Mind the shop\n  lore +medium\n  gold +small\n  mood -small\n  rel grimsby +medium\n  say: You sell eleven books, all of them the wrong ones, and every customer comes back to thank you. Grimsby, on crutches, says you have the gift. He means it as a warning.\n> right: \"I can't, I'm sorry.\"\n  rel grimsby -small\n  say: He closes for the week instead, with a sign that says GONE TO LIGHTHOUSE. Nobody knows which one.\n\n# ---------------- Old Pell (Pell & Daughters; the apothecary job) ----------------\n\n@event pell_intro\ntitle: A Thousand Drawers\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: pell_apothecary\nactor: pell\nwhen: not met(pell)\nweight: 30\nart: bottle\ntags: job money\nscene: An old apothecary on a library ladder in front of a wall of tiny labelled drawers, peering down over his spectacles at a student at the counter.\ntext: You ask for something for a headache. An old man on a ladder stops, counts the drawers under his breath and comes down with exactly one twist of paper. \"Willow and feverfew,\" says Old Pell. \"Three pinches. Not four. Four is for people who don't listen.\"\n> left: Ask why not four\n  meet pell\n  alchemy +small\n  rel pell +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He tells you, at length, with diagrams drawn in the counter dust. He's so pleased someone asked that he forgets to charge you. You leave with the cure, a headache from the lecture and the dosage for nine other things.\n> right: Pay and go\n  meet pell\n  gold -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  say: He nods at the door as you leave, as if adding you to a ledger of people who will be back. The headache is gone before the quay, and you are back within a month.\n\n@event pell_count_drops\ntitle: Count Them Aloud\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: pell\nwhen: rel(pell) >= 0 and member(apothecary_clerk)\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 5\nart: bottle\ntags: job study\nscene: An old apothecary at a wooden counter holding a glass dropper over a vial, a student beside him counting on their fingers, a wall of tiny labelled drawers behind them.\ntext: {Old Pell sets a dropper in your hand. \"Forty. Aloud.\" He watches your lips, not the vial.|\"Forty,\" says Pell, and stands behind you. You can hear him counting along under his breath.|Pell has started leaving you the hard tinctures. That is how he says he trusts you.} {A daughter rolls her eyes at the counter.|The drawers rattle when a cart goes by.|Something in drawer three hundred is humming.}\n> left: Count, aloud, every drop\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  rel pell +small\n  say: {Forty, exactly. Pell says nothing for a full minute, then \"Again.\" You are fairly sure that was praise.|You lose count at thirty-one and start over without being told. Pell grunts approvingly.|Forty, of a tincture that fights back. Pell holds the vial to the light and puts it in drawer three hundred. The humming stops.}\n> right: Eyeball it; it's close enough\n  challenge brew apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      alchemy +tiny\n      say: {It's right. Pell checks it three times and is visibly annoyed that it's right.|Close enough is, today, exactly right. \"Luck,\" says Pell. \"Don't.\"|The hard tincture forgives you, today. Pell sniffs it and puts it away without comment, which is worse than comment.}\n    lose:\n      rel pell -small\n      perform apothecary_clerk -small\n      say: {Forty-three. Pell pours it out without a word and hands you the dropper again. The silence lasts all shift.|Too many. A daughter fixes it before he sees. He sees.|Too many, of the hard one. Drawer three hundred hums louder, and Pell keeps the dropper for the rest of the week.}\n\n@event pell_daughters\ntitle: Pell & Daughters\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: pell\nwhen: rel(pell) >= 20\nweight: 18\nart: scales\ntags: job home\nscene: An old apothecary and his two grown daughters arguing across a counter over a new painted shop sign, a student standing awkwardly between them.\ntext: Pell's daughters want a new sign, brighter labels and a counter that faces the door. Pell wants it to be fifty years ago. All three of them turn to you, the only person in the shop who isn't family. \"Well?\" says Pell.\n> left: Side with the daughters\n  rel pell -small\n  mood +small\n  say: Pell stamps into the back room. A week later the new sign goes up. Underneath it, in small old letters, it still says PELL. He shows you, gruffly, as though you'd asked.\n> right: \"The drawers have never been wrong.\"\n  rel pell +medium\n  if member(apothecary_clerk):\n    gold +small\n    say: Pell glows. The daughters glare. Pell gives you a bonus and the daughters give you all the worst stocktakes. Everyone feels this is fair.\n  else:\n    health +small\n    say: Pell glows. The daughters glare. Pell presses a bottle of his best tonic on you, and the daughters give you a look that lasts a month.\n\n# ---------------- Grisk (the moneylender) ----------------\n\n@event grisk_pleasantries\ntitle: Pleasantries\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: grisk\nwhen: rel(grisk) >= -10 and not flag(grisk_gone)\nweight: 8\nrepeat: 6\nart: pouch\ntags: money social\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting at a corner table in a low-beamed tavern, raising a small glass to a student, a fat ledger at his elbow.\ntext: {Grisk raises a small glass from the corner table of the Gilded Newt. \"My friend,\" he says. \"Do sit. I'm not working.\"|Grisk, at the corner table again. \"How is the purse?\" he asks, with what sounds like real concern.|{has(in_debt) or flag(grisk_done): \"Ah, my favourite client,\" purrs Grisk.|\"Ah, my favourite non-client,\" purrs Grisk.}} {His ledger stays closed, which feels deliberate.|He is eating a single olive very slowly.|The landlady gives you a warning look.}\n> left: Sit for one drink\n  renown -tiny\n  guile +small\n  rel grisk +small\n  say: {He talks about money the way other people talk about weather, and you learn more in an hour than in a term of Umbral Theory.|He tells you which of the village shops are in trouble and why. It feels like gossip. It is a lesson.|He pays for the drinks and writes nothing down, which is the most unsettling thing he has ever done.}\n> right: Keep walking\n  rel grisk -tiny\n  say: {\"Another time,\" he calls after you. \"I'm very patient.\"|He raises the glass anyway. You feel it between your shoulder blades all the way out.|You keep walking. The landlady nods to you on your way out, very slightly.}\n\n@event grisk_tip\ntitle: A Tip, Between Friends\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: grisk\nwhen: rel(grisk) >= 4 and not has(in_debt) and not flag(grisk_gone)\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: money\nscene: A goblin leaning across a tavern table to murmur into a student's ear, sliding a single gold coin across the wood with one finger.\ntext: Grisk slides a coin across the table. \"Between friends,\" he murmurs. \"The barge company is about to buy a second barge. The ferryman's cousin will sell his share this week, cheap, because he doesn't know. You could know.\"\n> left: Buy the share\n  hint: The share costs a fair few crowns.\n  alignment -tiny\n  gold -medium\n  chance likely:\n    gold +large\n    gold +medium\n    rel grisk +small\n    say: The second barge arrives. The share triples. Grisk lifts his glass across the Newt. You feel rich and slightly unwell.\n  else:\n    say: The second barge sinks on its first crossing. Grisk is sympathetic. \"Risk,\" he says. \"Delicious, and sometimes poison.\"\n> right: Leave the cousin his share\n  rel grisk -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: Grisk pockets the coin. \"Honest,\" he says, as if diagnosing something. \"It'll pass. Or it won't, and you'll be poor and admired.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/full_picture.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The whole picture (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md, batch P4-11): when you know every fact about someone close\n#  (content/world/facts/<id>.grim, the People panel), a quiet milestone in which they notice. One for each main\n#  character whose facts don't wait on the main plot (Kael's and Nessa's do, so they have none; nor the headmistress\n#  or Ilse). A card's `when` lists that character's facts: add a fact to their file, add it here.\n# ============================================================\n\n@pool full_picture\ndesc: What every whole-picture milestone shares: once, when you know someone completely and they're near.\ntrigger: auto\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\ntags: people facts\n\n@event picture_lyra\ntitle: Unbearable\npool: full_picture\ncast:\n  lyra: lyra where around(lyra) and rel(lyra) >= 30\nwhen: knows(lyra.prodigy) and knows(lyra.toast) and knows(lyra.mother) and knows(lyra.second_place) and knows(lyra.burnout) and knows(lyra.rival) and knows(lyra.inheritance) and knows(lyra.wildfire) and knows(lyra.flourish)\nart: flame\nscene: A duelling hall after dark, one student sitting on the floor against the wall, another handing her a plate of slightly burnt toast.\ntext: You bring Lyra toast after practice, because she can't make it, and set it down without a word, because she won't ask. She looks at it, and at you. \"You know about the toast,\" she says. \"And my mother. And the Trials, and the letter, and the flourish.\" A pause. \"You know everything. It's unbearable.\" She takes a slice anyway.\n> left: \"I'd like to know more.\"\n  rel lyra +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: \"There isn't any more,\" she says, and then, after a long time, tells you one more thing: the name of her grandmother's horse. It doesn't matter at all. It's the first thing she has ever told you that she didn't have to.\n> right: \"I'll forget half of it, if you like.\"\n  rel lyra +small\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Don't you dare,\" she says, too quickly, and then goes red. She eats the toast. It's burnt. She says it's adequate, which from Lyra is a bouquet, and doesn't let you leave until it's finished.\n\n@event picture_corvin\ntitle: No Jokes\npool: full_picture\ncast:\n  corvin: corvin where around(corvin) and rel(corvin) >= 30\nwhen: knows(corvin.locks) and knows(corvin.last_warning) and knows(corvin.brother) and knows(corvin.grandfather) and knows(corvin.cartographers) and knows(corvin.trolls) and knows(corvin.sundays)\nart: cards\nscene: A gargoyle passage at night, two students sitting on the parapet, one shuffling a worn old deck of cards very slowly.\ntext: Up on the parapet, Corvin shuffles his grandfather's deck and doesn't deal. \"You know about the trolls,\" he says. \"And my brother. And the warning. And the Sundays.\" He laughs, but only a little. \"Nobody knows all of it, saint. I don't usually let anyone have more than two.\" The jokes have stopped. So has he.\n> left: Tell him something of yours\n  rel corvin +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You tell him the thing you don't tell anyone. He listens, the way his grandfather probably did, and then deals you a hand. \"Now we're even,\" he says. \"Don't you dare tell anyone I said something nice.\" You don't.\n> right: Let him deal, and say nothing\n  rel corvin +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You don't say anything, which turns out to be right. He deals. You play badly, and he lets you win once, which you both pretend not to notice. Somewhere around midnight he starts joking again, and it sounds different.\n\n@event picture_tobias\ntitle: A Saying for It\npool: full_picture\ncast:\n  tobias: tobias where around(tobias) and rel(tobias) >= 30\nwhen: knows(tobias.scholarship) and knows(tobias.plants) and knows(tobias.ma) and knows(tobias.gerald) and knows(tobias.moonflowers) and knows(tobias.blight) and knows(tobias.dancing) and knows(tobias.after)\nart: leaf\nscene: A greenhouse in the evening, two students repotting seedlings side by side, a small mandrake in a knitted hat watching from a shelf.\ntext: Repotting seedlings, Tobias goes quiet for so long that Gerald, on his shelf, stops screaming to look. \"You know about my ma, and the blight, and the broom,\" he says. \"And about after. I haven't told anyone about after.\" He wipes his hands on his apron. \"Back home there's a saying for someone who knows you like that. I'll get it wrong.\"\n> left: Ask him to say it anyway\n  rel tobias +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: He says it, and gets it wrong, and then tells you the long version, which is about a goat. By the end you're both laughing so hard you knock over a tray of seedlings. They don't seem to mind. Gerald does.\n> right: Promise to help, whatever he decides\n  rel tobias +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: He looks at you for a long moment, and then laughs from the belly, the way he does, and apologises for laughing, the way he does. \"That's the saying,\" he says. \"Near enough.\" He gives you the best seedling to keep.\n\n@event picture_seraphine\ntitle: Forty-One Todays\npool: full_picture\ncast:\n  seraphine: seraphine where around(seraphine) and rel(seraphine) >= 30\nwhen: knows(seraphine.seer) and knows(seraphine.slips) and knows(seraphine.oracle) and knows(seraphine.self) and knows(seraphine.lake) and knows(seraphine.too_loud) and knows(seraphine.paintings)\nart: eye\nscene: A seer at the lakeshore in the afternoon holding out a small hand-stitched calendar, dozens of its days marked with the same tiny drawn star.\ntext: Seraphine gives you a little calendar she has stitched herself. Forty-one of its days have a tiny star on them. \"The days you told me what today was,\" she says. \"I slip, and you always know. And you know the rest: my mother, the lake, what gets too loud.\" She frowns, the way she does at a future. \"Nobody's ever kept me in today before. I wanted you to have the count.\"\n> left: Promise to keep counting\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Forty-two,\" you say, because it's today and she's here. She laughs, a real one, finished. For the rest of the afternoon she doesn't slip once, and when you part she says \"see you tomorrow\", and means tomorrow.\n> right: Teach her a trick to find today herself\n  rel seraphine +small\n  study +small\n  say: You teach her to count the bells: the castle rings the day in a pattern, if you listen. She practises all afternoon, half a sentence at a time. The next week she slips, and finds her own way back, and comes to tell you, exact and pleased.\n\n@event picture_mir\ntitle: Documented\npool: full_picture\ncast:\n  mir: mir where around(mir) and rel(mir) >= 30\nwhen: knows(mir.artificer) and knows(mir.owl) and knows(mir.notebooks) and knows(mir.family) and knows(mir.name_day) and knows(mir.workshop) and knows(mir.corridor)\nart: gear\nscene: A cluttered workshop at night, an artificer holding out a battered notebook open at a page with someone's name at the top.\ntext: Mir holds out a notebook, the newest one, open at a page with your name at the top. \"I've been documenting you,\" they say. \"Fair's fair. You know about the owl and the family and the name day and the fires. Probably all the fires. So I've been writing down what I know about you, to see if it's even.\" They look worried. \"It isn't even. You're winning.\"\n> left: Help them fill in the page\n  rel mir +medium\n  study -small\n  say: You sit on the bench and answer every question, fast, the way they ask them, with exclamation marks. It takes until midnight. When it's done they read it through twice and say \"huh\", which from Mir means something has been mended. They file it with the precious ones.\n> right: Tell them it doesn't have to be even\n  rel mir +small\n  mood +small\n  say: \"It doesn't have to be even,\" you say. Mir thinks about it, visibly, like a machine running a sum. \"That's very inefficient,\" they say. \"I like it.\" They close the notebook. The owl on the shelf blinks twice, which is how it says \"noted\".\n\n@event picture_bram\ntitle: Don't Make It Weird\npool: full_picture\ncast:\n  bram: bram where around(bram) and rel(bram) >= 30\nwhen: knows(bram.prefect) and knows(bram.father) and knows(bram.hall_tax) and knows(bram.cheating) and knows(bram.ally) and knows(bram.portrait) and knows(bram.rematch)\nart: shield\nscene: A corridor of old portraits of head boys, a prefect in a red sash standing in front of one of them with a student beside him.\ntext: You find Bram in the corridor of head boys, in front of his father's portrait. He doesn't move when you come up. \"You know all of it,\" he says, to the portrait. \"The exam. The hall tax. Him. Every rematch.\" He clears his throat. \"Most people know the sash. You know the rest, and you never once used it.\" He's very red. \"Don't make it weird.\"\n> left: Make it a little weird\n  rel bram +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You put your arm round his shoulders and tell the portrait, firmly, that its son is going to be a better prefect than it ever was. Bram makes a noise like a kettle. Then he laughs, really laughs, and gives you a demerit for \"insolence to a painting\".\n> right: Stand there with him, and don't\n  rel bram +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You stand there with him and say nothing at all, which is the kindest thing you've ever done for him. After a while he says \"right\" and walks off, briskly. The next day there's a note in your bag in block capitals: THANK YOU. DON'T.\n\n@event picture_maud\ntitle: Over Tea, Dearie\npool: full_picture\ncast:\n  maud: maud where around(maud) and rel(maud) >= 30\nwhen: knows(maud.groundskeeper) and knows(maud.student) and knows(maud.edmund) and knows(maud.soup) and knows(maud.garden) and knows(maud.age) and knows(maud.tuppence) and knows(maud.blazes)\nart: cup\nscene: A cottage kitchen, a pot of soup on the stove, an old woman pouring two cups of tea at a scrubbed table, a goat looking in at the window.\ntext: Maud pours two cups and doesn't sit. \"You know about Edmund,\" she says. \"And the garden, and Tuppence, and my knees, and the soup. You've heard all my sad stories, dearie, and you've never once looked sorry for me.\" She sits, finally. \"Sixty years since anyone knew me all the way through. I'd forgotten what it was like.\"\n> left: Ask her to teach you the soup\n  rel maud +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: \"You're not allowed to write it down,\" she says, and teaches you anyway, slowly, with her hands over yours on the spoon. You understand, two weeks later, that she wasn't teaching you the soup at all.\n> right: Tell her a sad story of yours\n  rel maud +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You tell her one, as if it were a recipe. She listens, and nods, and at the end says, \"Needs salt.\" It's exactly right. Bartholomew bleats at the window. She gives him a biscuit, and you another.\n\n@event picture_thorne\ntitle: Hm\npool: full_picture\ncast:\n  thorne: thorne where around(thorne) and rel(thorne) >= 30\nwhen: knows(thorne.storm) and knows(thorne.lost) and knows(thorne.roses) and knows(thorne.hands) and knows(thorne.rival) and knows(thorne.letters) and knows(thorne.accusation)\nart: rose\nscene: A tiny walled rose garden behind an observatory at dawn, an old professor with secateurs, his hands not quite steady, a student holding the basket.\ntext: You hold the basket while Thorne cuts roses at dawn, and his hands shake, and he lets you see them shake. \"You know about the storm,\" he says. \"The real version. And the roses. And these.\" He holds up his hands. \"And the old fool in purple.\" A long pause. \"Hm.\" From Thorne, it's a speech.\n> left: Ask him to tell you about the storm again\n  rel thorne +medium\n  study +small\n  say: He tells you, the whole of it, the way he never tells it in lectures: how badly he lost, and how. At the end he hands you the secateurs. \"Your turn,\" he says, meaning the roses, meaning more than the roses. \"Badly is fine. Badly is the point.\"\n> right: Say nothing, and cut the next rose for him\n  rel thorne +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You take the secateurs and cut the next rose, carefully, and the one after that. He watches, and doesn't correct you, which has never happened. At the end he says nothing at all, and doesn't take the secateurs back. You'll be proud of that for years.\n\n@event picture_brask\ntitle: Half a Sentence More\npool: full_picture\ncast:\n  brask: brask where around(brask) and rel(brask) >= 30\nwhen: knows(brask.beasts) and knows(brask.stories) and knows(brask.owl) and knows(brask.courtesies) and knows(brask.feather) and knows(brask.blackthorn) and knows(brask.asking)\nart: owl\nscene: A stable loft at dusk, an old barn owl on a beam, a professor in a leather apron sitting on a hay bale beside a student.\ntext: In the loft, Brask feeds the old owl and, for once, talks to you in a whole sentence. \"You know about the owl, and the feather, and the woman with the blackthorn, and that I bow to the wood.\" She feeds the owl another mouse. \"Students don't usually stay long enough to learn that much. The owl's noticed. So have I.\"\n> left: Ask her which loss hurt most\n  rel brask +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She's quiet for a long time. Then she tells you, in a whole sentence, then another, and you understand why she never would before. At the end the owl hoots once, softly, as if it had been waiting. \"There,\" she says. \"That's the moral.\"\n> right: Ask the owl instead\n  rel brask +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You turn to the owl and ask it, very politely, what it thinks of you. The owl regards you with contempt. Brask laughs, loudly, the once-a-term laugh, a month early. \"It likes you,\" she says. \"That's its liking face.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/heists/shared.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Midnight heists (threads 1, docs/threads-1-plan.md Part C): two stealing mini-delves, the Restricted Stacks\n#  (heists/stacks.grim) and the seventh tower (heists/tower.grim). Like the Whisperwood delve (whisperwood/delve.grim),\n#  but quiet: guile checks (deceive past people, feats using guile on locks, shelves and boards), now and then a\n#  research or a spell check, never a fight or a negotiation; midnight only. The Cartographers' Master Map opens them\n#  (heist_stacks_known, heist_tower_known; content/events/cartographers.grim).\n#\n#  Counters, reset on entry: heist_where (1 the stacks, 2 the tower), heist_depth (how deep or high), heist_loot (what\n#  you carry), heist_cover (slips you can survive: 1, +1 with a prefect's round times, +1 more for the place's own\n#  advantage). heist_stacks_deepest and heist_tower_highest persist.\n#  Each step: left slips away with what you carry (heist_way_out); right goes on (heist_depth += 1, maybe loot, next).\n#  A lost check queues heist_slip while cover lasts (cover, health or loot pay for it; a word may reach a prefect),\n#  else heist_caught (the loot is taken, a month of detention, and the prefects' notice escalates as night_suspicion's\n#  does: watched, then locked out; outrunning them leaves only the morning's word). A hazard marked ⚠ goes straight to\n#  heist_caught. Stealing is a little Hollow, paid at the way out.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event heist_way_out\ntitle: Out Before Dawn\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 1\nart: pouch\ntags: danger money\nscene: A student slipping out of a dark doorway at the grey end of night, a bundle held under one arm, looking both ways down a castle corridor.\ntext: {heist_loot == 0: {heist_deck_tonight == 1: You come out with nothing to sell but a tin of cards that was never yours to sell, and a grin.|You come out with nothing but dust on your knees and your heart going like a drum.}|{heist_loot <= 2: You come out with a few small, old things under your shirt, each worth more than it looks.|{heist_loot <= 4: You come out with a real bundle: {heist_where == 1: pages, and something heavier that sighs when you move|papers, and something heavier that clinks when you move}.|You come out with more than your shirt will hold, and walk very carefully.}}} {heist_where == 1: The library is grey with dawn behind you.|The tower's slits are going grey above you.}\n> left: Sell it to the collector\n  hint: Crowns, by what you carry. A little Hollow.\n  if heist_loot >= 1:\n    alignment -tiny\n  if heist_loot >= 6:\n    gold +huge\n  elif heist_loot >= 4:\n    gold +large\n  elif heist_loot >= 2:\n    gold +medium\n  elif heist_loot >= 1:\n    gold +small\n  say: {heist_loot == 0: {heist_deck_tonight == 1: The tin goes to Corvin, not the collector. You go to bed, and lie awake grinning.|There's nothing to sell. You go to bed, and lie awake anyway.}|{~The collector in the village back room weighs each thing in a gloved hand and pays in old crowns, without a question.|The collector's man meets you by the village pump at noon. He counts your things, and then his coins, and both counts are fair.|The collector holds your best piece up to the window and is quiet for a long time. Then he pays more than you'd have asked.}}\n  if heist_where == 1 and heist_depth > heist_stacks_deepest:\n    heist_stacks_deepest = heist_depth\n  if heist_where == 2 and heist_depth > heist_tower_highest:\n    heist_tower_highest = heist_depth\n  heist_depth = 0\n  heist_loot = 0\n  heist_deck_tonight = 0\n> right: Keep it, and read it\n  hint: Lore, by what you carry. A little Hollow.\n  if heist_loot >= 1:\n    alignment -tiny\n  if heist_loot >= 6:\n    lore +large\n    guile +small\n  elif heist_loot >= 4:\n    lore +medium\n  elif heist_loot >= 2:\n    lore +small\n  elif heist_loot >= 1:\n    lore +tiny\n  say: {heist_loot == 0: There's nothing to keep but the night, and you keep that.|{~You spread it all on your bed behind drawn curtains and read until breakfast. Half of it you shouldn't know. You know it now.|You hide it under a loose floorboard and take it out a piece at a time, over weeks, like a box of sweets.|You read it all twice and burn the worst of it. The rest you keep, and it keeps you up.}}\n  if heist_where == 1 and heist_depth > heist_stacks_deepest:\n    heist_stacks_deepest = heist_depth\n  if heist_where == 2 and heist_depth > heist_tower_highest:\n    heist_tower_highest = heist_depth\n  heist_depth = 0\n  heist_loot = 0\n  heist_deck_tonight = 0\n\n@event heist_slip\ntitle: A Near Thing\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 1\nart: lantern\ntags: danger\nscene: A student flattened into a dark alcove, holding their breath, as the glow of a lantern slides past along the floor.\non_show:\n  heist_cover -= 1\n  health -tiny\n  heist_dropped = 0\n  if heist_loot > 0:\n    heist_loot -= 1\n    heist_dropped = 1\ntext: {heist_dropped == 1: {~You get away, just, and something you carried doesn't: it's lying out there in the dark where you dropped it.|You end up flat in an alcove with your heart in your mouth and one thing fewer in your shirt.|You're clear, scraped and shaking, and you've left something behind for somebody to find.}|{~You get away, just, with nothing to lose but your nerve, and some of that.|You end up flat in an alcove with your heart in your mouth, glad your shirt was empty.|You're clear, scraped and shaking, and you've left nothing behind but a scuff.}} {heist_cover > 0: You've one more near thing in you, maybe.|That was your last near thing.}\n> left: That's enough for tonight\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You take what's left and go, by the long way.|You decide the night has made its point.|You go while you can.}\n> right: Wait, and go on\n  hint: On: the next slip may not be a near thing.\n  queue night_suspicion chance unlikely\n  if heist_where == 1:\n    queue any in heist_stacks now\n  else:\n    queue any in heist_tower now\n  say: {~You wait till the dark is quiet again, and go on.|You count to five hundred, and go on.|You go on. Somewhere behind you, someone is looking at the place where you were.}\n\n@event heist_caught\ntitle: Caught\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 1\nart: eye\ntags: danger crisis\nscene: A prefect's lantern held high in a dark doorway, lighting up a student caught with a bundle of stolen things in their arms.\non_show:\n  if heist_where == 1 and heist_depth > heist_stacks_deepest:\n    heist_stacks_deepest = heist_depth\n  if heist_where == 2 and heist_depth > heist_tower_highest:\n    heist_tower_highest = heist_depth\n  heist_depth = 0\n  heist_deck_tonight = 0\n  heist_warned = 0\n  if has(watched):\n    heist_warned = 1\ntext: A lantern comes up full in your face. {heist_where == 1: Down in the stacks, at night,|Halfway up the seventh tower, at night,} {heist_loot == 0: and empty-handed, which convinces nobody.|with your arms full of things that aren't yours. The prefect takes them all, into a sack for the confiscation vault.} The prefect behind the lantern looks less angry than tired.\n> left: Go quietly\n  gain detention\n  mood -small\n  if has(watched):\n    lose watched\n    gain locked_out\n  else:\n    gain watched\n  heist_loot = 0\n  say: {heist_warned == 1: You go quietly. You'd been warned: a month of detention, and three of a door that is checked at midnight.|You go quietly. It doesn't help much: a month of detention, starting with the first free afternoon, and your name on the prefects' list.}\n> right: Run for it\n  challenge feat using guile journeyman:\n    win:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      heist_loot = 0\n      queue night_suspicion now\n      say: {heist_loot == 0: You run, and know every turn better than they do. You get away. You also know they saw your face.|You leave the sack to the prefect and run, and know every turn better than they do. You get away. You also know they saw your face.}\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      if has(watched):\n        lose watched\n        gain locked_out\n      else:\n        gain watched\n      heist_loot = 0\n      say: {heist_warned == 1: You run two corridors and into a second prefect. You'd been warned: a month of detention, three of a locked door at midnight, and a bruised shin.|You run two corridors and into a second prefect. A month of detention, a bruised shin, and your name on the prefects' list.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/heists/stacks.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The Restricted Stacks heist (threads 1; the shape is in heists/shared.grim). Down the stair under the east stacks'\n#  last chained shelf, by the book-return hatch (lore deep_stacks): band 1 the chained shelves (depth 1–2, apprentice), band 2 the breathing stacks (3–4,\n#  journeyman), band 3 the deep stacks (5–6, adept), and at depth 7 the confiscation vault. Teaches Torment (a book\n#  that charges its reader) and Reflect (the mirror desk). Cover: +1 with `prefect_rota`, +1 with `forbidden_rounds`\n#  (Odile's notes on the library's rounds). The bindery's book-lift (`carto_book_lift`) starts you at depth 3, and\n#  `vault_catalogued` (a detention card) makes the vault's lock easier.\n#  Plot safety: the heist goes down from the east stacks, never to the very back where the plot's books are (the Codex,\n#  the founders' drafts); it reveals nothing of the main mystery. It replaces the section's old midnight cards\n#  (midnight_restricted_stacks, restricted_page_buyer, retired; the collector is now the heist's fence).\n# ============================================================\n\n@pool restricted_heist_entry\nplace: restricted_section\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 1\norder: last\n\n@event heist_stacks_enter\ntitle: The Stair Under the Chains\npool: restricted_heist_entry\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(heist_stacks_known)\nart: book\ntags: danger mystery\nscene: A dark library aisle at midnight, the last shelf's kickboard swung open on a narrow stair going down, a student with a shaded candle at the top.\ntext: {heist_stacks_deepest == 0: Midnight in the Restricted Section. By the book-return hatch, the kickboard under the east stacks' last chained shelf swings open where your map says it will, on the stair going down. Below, the deep stacks: books nobody has signed out in a century, and the confiscation vault at the bottom.|{~The kickboard, the stair, the chains above you pulled taut by something below.|The stair under the chained shelf, and the cold breath coming up it.|Midnight, the chained shelf, and the dark going down.}} {flag(carto_book_lift): Or there's the bindery's book-lift, a floor below, which goes down as far as the breathing shelves.|}\n> left: Not tonight\n  say: {~You put the kickboard back and go to bed with the stair in your head.|Not tonight. The chains settle as you leave, as if disappointed.|You listen at the top of the stair for a while, and go no further.}\n> right: Go down\n  hint: A heist: press on card by card, slip away with what you carry any time.\n  flag heist_stacks_tried\n  heist_where = 1\n  heist_loot = 0\n  heist_cover = 1\n  if flag(prefect_rota):\n    heist_cover += 1\n  if flag(forbidden_rounds):\n    heist_cover += 1\n  if flag(carto_book_lift):\n    heist_depth = 3\n  else:\n    heist_depth = 1\n  queue any in heist_stacks now\n  say: {flag(carto_book_lift): You go down to the bindery, climb into the book-lift's box and let the rope down hand over hand, until its knots stop you where the shelves start breathing.|{~You pull the kickboard shut behind you and go down by your map.|You shade your candle and go down, counting steps.|You go down quietly, one hand on the cold wall, the chains creaking overhead.}}\n\n@event heist_stacks_unmapped\ntitle: Chains Below\npool: restricted_heist_entry\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(heist_stacks_known)\nrepeat: 4\nart: book\ntags: mystery\nscene: A student in a dark library aisle at midnight with an ear to the bottom shelf, iron chains running down through holes in the floor and pulled taut.\ntext: {Midnight in the Restricted Section. The chains on the east stacks' last shelf run down through the floor, and something below pulls them taut, then lets them go, like breathing.|The chains on the last shelf again, taut, then slack. There's a stair down there somewhere. You've no map for it.} {seen(carto_map_blanks) > 0: The Cartographers have a blank on their map just here.|Someone in the castle must have a map of what's below.}\n> left: Listen at the shelf\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You lie on the cold floor with an ear to the boards. Far down, pages turn, a great many of them, all at once.|You listen until your ear is numb. Something down there is very large and very quiet, and it's a library.}\n> right: Go back to bed\n  say: {You leave the chains to whatever is pulling them.|Not without a map. You go back up to bed.}\n\n@thread heist_stacks\nfrom: scene\nclues: lead_deep_stacks\nname: The Restricted Stacks\npremise: Below the last chained shelf, a stair goes down to the deep stacks and the confiscation vault. The Cartographers go at midnight.\njournal:\n  flag(heist_stacks_tried): You went down the stair under the chained shelf at midnight.\n  flag(heist_stacks_torment): Down in the stacks you read a book that charged you a drop for every page, and learned Torment from it.\n  flag(heist_stacks_reflect): At the mirror desk your reflection read the book back to you, and you learned Reflect.\n  flag(heist_vault_opened): You picked the prefects' padlock on the confiscation vault.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(heist_vault_opened)\n\n# The way shut: a step for any depth, drawn rarely (weight 1), and always when nothing else is left at this depth\n# (a slip at the vault, or a band's cards used up), so a night never ends without the way out.\n@event st_dead_end\ntitle: The Way Shut\nthread: heist_stacks\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: heist_depth >= 1 and (heist_depth <= 6 or (seen(st_vault) > 0 and since(st_vault) == 0))\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 1\nart: book\ntags: heist\nscene: A narrow library aisle in near darkness, two tall bookcases leaned together overhead to close it like a door, a student's candle at the bottom.\ntext: {heist_depth >= 7: The goblin over the vault door is still muttering, and the padlock won't take another pick tonight. The only way left is up.|{~The shelves ahead have breathed in and not breathed out. The aisle is shut, and the only way that's still a way is back.|Ahead, the stacks have closed ranks, spine to spine, like a door. Whatever's further down is not for tonight.|A shelf has come down across the way, gently, as if it meant to. Behind it, the dark goes on without you.}}\n> left: Go back up with what you carry\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {heist_depth >= 7: You leave the goblin to its muttering and climb, all forty-four steps, with what you have.|{~You turn round and go up by the way you know.|You take the hint, and the stair.|You go, and the shelves let out their breath behind you.}}\n> right: Search where you stand\n  challenge research apprentice:\n    win:\n      heist_loot += 1\n      say: {heist_depth >= 7: Among the crates stacked outside the vault door, one lid is loose. Something small and old comes with you.|{~The shelf that closed the aisle has a gap in it, and in the gap a small book nobody's missed. You take it up with you.|You run your hand along the closed shelf and find one spine that's loose. It comes with you.|Behind the fallen shelf, a sheaf of loose pages, very old. You roll them into your sleeve.}}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: {heist_depth >= 7: The goblin catches you at the crates and says so. You leave them be.|{~The shelf leans on you, very gently, until you stop.|You find nothing but dust, and a splinter.|The shelf breathes in, and you jump back, and leave it be.}}\n  queue heist_way_out now\n\n# ---------------- Band 1: the chained shelves (depth 1–2, apprentice) ----------------\n\n@event st1_taut_chains\ntitle: The Chains Pull Taut\nthread: heist_stacks\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: heist_depth >= 1 and heist_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: book\ntags: heist danger\nscene: A narrow dark aisle between library shelves strung with iron chains, the chains drawn tight across the aisle at different heights, a student ducking between them.\ntext: {seen(st1_taut_chains) > 1: The chains know you now. They draw taut across the aisle at knee and throat as you come, slower than they used to, as if to see whether you've got careless.|{heist_depth == 1: At the foot of the stair the chains from the shelf above come down through the ceiling and run off between the shelves, and as you step off the last stair they draw taut across the aisle, one at the knee, one at the throat.|Round the next bookcase the chains from the shelf above run between the shelves, and as you come they draw taut across the aisle, one at the knee, one at the throat.} Something is pulling them, slowly, to see what you'll do.}\n> left: Slip back the way you came\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You go back the way you came, and the chains go slack behind you, satisfied.|You decide the chains have made their point.}\n> right: Thread between them\n  challenge feat using guile apprentice:\n    win:\n      heist_loot += 1\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~Under, over, sideways, holding your breath. On the far side a page has fallen from a chained book, gold-leafed. You take it.|You go through the chains like a cat through railings. One of them twangs, very softly, as you pass, and a loose illuminated page drifts down. You catch it.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~A chain catches your ankle and rings like a bell all the way up to the shelf.|You misjudge one and it takes you across the shoulder, hard, and every chain in the aisle shivers.}\n\n@event st1_library_rounds\ntitle: The Night Lamp\nthread: heist_stacks\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: heist_depth >= 1 and heist_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: lantern\ntags: heist danger\nscene: A lamp's glow moving along the top of a library bookcase, seen from below in a dark aisle, a student pressed flat against the books.\ntext: {seen(st1_library_rounds) > 1: The night lamp again, along the gallery overhead, on the same slow round, looking everywhere.|A lamp comes along the gallery overhead: a prefect walking the library's night round, who has no reason to look down the stair and every reason to look everywhere.} {flag(forbidden_rounds): By Odile's green ink, this is the late round, and it turns back at the reading room.|The glow moves slowly. You have no idea where it goes next.}\n> left: Back up the stair while you can\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You go back up behind the lamp, step for step, and out.|You let the round have the library tonight.}\n> right: Stand still in the shelves\n  challenge deceive apprentice +1 if flag(forbidden_rounds):\n    win:\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~The lamp passes over you, over the stair, over the dark, and goes on. You breathe out for what feels like a minute.|You stand so still among the books you could be one. The lamp moves on to the reading room.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~The lamp stops. \"Hello?\" says the prefect, to the dark. You run.|You shift your weight, and a board answers. The lamp swings down the stair.}\n\n@event st1_catalogue\ntitle: The Catalogue Below\nthread: heist_stacks\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: heist_depth >= 1 and heist_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: paper\ntags: heist study\nscene: An old wooden card catalogue in a dark library basement, one long drawer pulled out, cards written in faded brown ink, a candle on top.\ntext: {seen(st1_catalogue) > 1: The catalogue at the bend again. WHAT IS WHERE has a new card in it since last time, in fresher ink.|An old card catalogue stands at the bend of the aisle, for the books down here that the library upstairs pretends not to own. One drawer is labelled in a dead librarian's hand: WHAT IS WHERE, AND WHAT MUST NOT BE MOVED.}\n> left: Leave it, and slip away\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You leave the drawer shut and the dead librarian's secrets with it.|You go, and take nothing, not even a look.}\n> right: Read the drawer\n  challenge research apprentice:\n    win:\n      heist_loot += 1\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~WHAT IS WHERE turns out to be a map in cards. You pocket the three that matter and go on knowing where you're going.|You read fast by candle and pocket the card for the deep stacks. Half of it is a warning. You take the other half.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~The drawer jams, then comes out all at once, and four hundred cards go across the floor.|You read too long. The candle gutters, and in the dark you knock the whole catalogue a foot sideways, loudly.}\n\n# ---------------- Band 2: the breathing stacks (depth 3–4, journeyman) ----------------\n\n@event st2_breathing\ntitle: Between Breaths\nthread: heist_stacks\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: heist_depth >= 3 and heist_depth <= 4\nrepeat: 1\nart: book\ntags: heist danger\nscene: Tall black bookcases in deep darkness, leaning very slightly inward and outward as if breathing, a narrow gap closing between them, a student waiting to slip through.\ntext: {seen(st2_breathing) > 1: The breathing shelves again. You know their rhythm now, mostly, and they seem to know yours: tonight they breathe a little faster.|Down here the shelves breathe, and they're close enough together that when they breathe in, the aisle closes. You'd have to go through on the out-breath, all the way, before the next in.}\n> left: Leave the breathing to them\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You go back the way you came, and the shelves breathe easier behind you.|You decide you don't want to be between them on an in-breath.}\n> right: Go on the out-breath\n  challenge feat using guile journeyman:\n    win:\n      heist_loot += 1\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You go on the out-breath, fast and flat, and the shelves sigh shut behind your heels. A book has fallen in the gap. It's yours now.|You time it, and go, and come out the far side with a black-bound book that fell into your arms on the way.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~You're late by a breath. The shelves hold you, gently, all over, for a long minute, and let go.|The aisle closes on your satchel and keeps it a while. Its idea of a while.}\n\n@event st2_charging_book\ntitle: The Book That Charges\nthread: heist_stacks\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nwhen: heist_depth >= 3 and heist_depth <= 4 and spell_level(torment) < 1\nrepeat: 1\nart: book\ntags: heist magic danger\nscene: An open black book on a lectern in a dark library stack, a single drop of blood on the corner of its page, a student's pricked finger held above it.\ntext: {seen(st2_charging_book) > 1: The book that charges is open on its lectern again, at the page where you stopped, and it hasn't forgotten what you owe.|On a lectern in the dark is a book with a duellist's hand on the cover, a forbidden manual from some war nobody teaches. When you turn its page, the corner pricks your finger and takes a drop. The next page will cost another. It charges its reader for everything it teaches.}\n> left: Close it, and slip away\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You close it. It lets you, this time. You suck your finger all the way back up the stair.|You leave the book on its lectern, owed nothing.}\n> right: Read it to the end, and pay\n  hint: Torment, at a price.\n  challenge research journeyman:\n    win:\n      learn_spell torment\n      flag heist_stacks_torment\n      health -small\n      alignment -small\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~Page by page, drop by drop. By the end your fingertips are sore and you understand the trick of it: make every spell the other side casts cost them, and hurt. Torment. You feel the shape of it in your hands, and wish you didn't.|You pay to the last page. The book closes itself, satisfied. You know Torment now, and how it feels to be charged for every step.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~You read too fast, and it charges too much. You come to on the floor with the book shut and the room very loud.|It takes more than a drop. You stumble back from the lectern into a shelf, which tells everyone.}\n\n@event st2_other_lamp\ntitle: Someone Else's Lamp\nthread: heist_stacks\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: heist_depth >= 3 and heist_depth <= 4\nrepeat: 1\nart: lantern\ntags: heist danger\nscene: A hooded student at a desk deep in dark library stacks, copying from a black book by a small shaded lamp, not yet aware of a second student watching from the aisle.\ntext: {seen(st2_other_lamp) > 1: Another lamp ahead, shaded low. Someone else is copying down here again, a different hood, the same hurry. They haven't seen you.|There's a lamp ahead, shaded low. Someone else is down here: a hooded student copying out of a black book, very fast, with a satchel of copied pages at their feet. They haven't seen you. They will in a minute.}\n> left: Back away before they look up\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You back away into the dark, and leave them to their copying and their luck.|You go. One thief in the stacks is plenty.}\n> right: Slip past them\n  challenge deceive journeyman:\n    win:\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You go past behind their back, close enough to read their page. They never look up. You never ask who.|You wait till they bend to the book, and go by like a draught.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~They look up, and you both freeze, and then you both run, in opposite directions, loudly.|They see you and blow out their lamp, and in the dark you go straight into a shelf.}\n> take: Take a sheaf of their pages\n  challenge deceive journeyman+:\n    win:\n      heist_loot += 2\n      alignment -tiny\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You lift a sheaf from the top of their satchel while they turn a page, and are three aisles away before they reach for it.|You take their copied pages, which is stealing from a thief, which still counts.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~They catch your wrist, and shout. Somewhere above, a lamp comes on.|The satchel's buckle rings on the floor, and they're up and shouting before you've got a page.}\n\n# ---------------- Band 3: the deep stacks (depth 5–6, adept) ----------------\n\n@event st3_mirror_desk\ntitle: The Mirror Desk\nthread: heist_stacks\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: heist_depth >= 5 and heist_depth <= 6 and spell_level(reflect) < 1\nrepeat: 1\nart: star\ntags: heist magic\nscene: A reading desk of black mirrored glass deep in a dark library, a book open on it, the student's reflection in the glass reading the book with its lips moving.\ntext: {seen(st3_mirror_desk) > 1: The mirror desk again. Your reflection is already there, a line further on than where you stopped.|Deep in the stacks there's a reading desk of black mirror-glass with a book open on it. When you lean over, your reflection is already reading, a line ahead of you, and its lips are moving. Whatever you read, it reads back.}\n> left: Look away, and slip off\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You look away first. For a week your reflection in windows is a line ahead.|You leave the desk to its reader.}\n> right: Read along with it\n  hint: Reflect, if you can keep up.\n  challenge research adept:\n    win:\n      learn_spell reflect\n      flag heist_stacks_reflect\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You read with your reflection until you're reading together, and then it reads you, and gives it all back. That's the trick: give it all back. Reflect.|By the last line you understand the desk. Everything put to it comes back, every word. You take the lesson up the stairs with you.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~Your reflection gets ahead, then a page ahead, then shuts the book on you. The glass rings like a struck glass.|You read too slowly, and the desk reads your face back at you, all of it, very loud.}\n\n@event st3_no_lamp\ntitle: Where No Lamp Reaches\nthread: heist_stacks\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: heist_depth == 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: book\ntags: heist danger\nscene: Pitch darkness in a vast library, a single hand on the spine of a book, the faintest outline of endless shelves.\ntext: {seen(st3_no_lamp) > 1: The dark at the bottom again, where your candle gives up. You know the first twenty spines by touch now; it's the rest that worry you.|Down here no lamp reaches, and your candle won't stay lit; it leans over and gives up. The last stretch to the vault is by touch: spines under your fingers, and the floor going down by inches.} ⚠ Lose your way here, and there's no near thing.\n> left: Turn back while you know the way\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You turn round in the dark and walk back by the spines you know.|You decide that the dark here is someone's, and not yours.}\n> right: Go on by touch\n  challenge feat using guile adept-:\n    win:\n      heist_loot += 1\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~Spine by spine, in a dark like deep water. Your hand closes on a small book lying loose on a shelf, and you take it, and keep going.|You count the shelves by touch, and at the end of the count, a door.}\n    lose:\n      queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~You lose the spines, then the floor, then the way. When the lantern finds you, you're sitting in the dark holding a book you can't see, nowhere near anywhere.|Something in the dark takes your hand and leads you, kindly, all the way to the prefects' door.}\n\n@event st3_glass_case\ntitle: The Glass Case\nthread: heist_stacks\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: heist_depth >= 5 and heist_depth <= 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: book\ntags: heist magic money\nscene: A small glass display case in a dark library, full of tiny leather-bound books no bigger than a thumb, a faint shimmer of a charm over its lock.\ntext: {seen(st3_glass_case) > 1: {flag(heist_glass_robbed): The glass case again, a few books fewer, and the charm on its lock rewoven tighter than before. Someone has noticed.|The glass case again, every little book where it was, the charm humming as before.}|In an alcove stands a glass case of grimoires no bigger than your thumb, each with a clasp of gold, a hundred spells to a book. The lock has no keyhole, only a charm laid over it that hums when your breath fogs the glass. Any collector would sell their mother for one of them.}\n> left: Leave them under glass\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You leave the little books to their glass and go back up the way you came.|You fog the glass once more, for the pleasure of it, and go.}\n> right: Unpick the charm\n  challenge spell adept-:\n    win:\n      flag heist_glass_robbed\n      heist_loot += 2\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~The charm comes apart under your fingers like wet paper. You take two of the little books, and the case closes itself behind you as if offended.|You unpick the charm thread by thread and lift out the smallest book. Then, because you're here, a second.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~The charm bites back, a white flash, and the glass rings like a struck bell all through the stacks.|You pull the wrong thread, and the whole charm goes off in your face with a noise like a slammed book.}\n\n# ---------------- Depth 7: the confiscation vault ----------------\n\n@event st_vault\ntitle: The Confiscation Vault\nthread: heist_stacks\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: heist_depth >= 7\nrepeat: 1\nart: door\ntags: heist money danger\nscene: An iron-banded oak door in a dark library basement, a heavy prefects' padlock on it, crates labelled for confiscation stacked outside, a student kneeling at the lock with picks.\ntext: At the bottom of the deep stacks, a door with a prefects' padlock and crates outside labelled for the confiscation vault. Behind it, three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student. {flag(vault_catalogued): You catalogued half of it yourself, in detention. You know which shelf is which.|You've no idea what's where.} {flag(elixir_confiscated): Somewhere in there is the bottle the prefects took from your trunk.|}\n> leave: Leave it locked, and go\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You leave the vault to its padlock. Getting here was the heist.|You touch the padlock once, for luck, and go back up.}\n> crates: Pick the lock for the crates\n  challenge feat using guile adept- +2 if flag(vault_catalogued):\n    win:\n      flag heist_vault_opened\n      heist_loot += 3\n      heist_depth += 1\n      queue heist_way_out now\n      say: {~The padlock gives with a click like a knuckle. Inside, a century of contraband on shelves: charmed dice, love letters, a stuffed owl that blinks. You fill your shirt with the small valuable things and lock it again behind you.|You go straight to the shelf you catalogued in detention and take the three best things on it. The padlock clicks shut after you as if nothing happened.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~The pick snaps in the lock, with a sound like a shot down here.|The vault's alarm, a very loud goblin, wakes up and says so.}\n> bottle: Pick the lock for your own bottle back\n  when: flag(elixir_confiscated)\n  challenge feat using guile adept- +2 if flag(vault_catalogued):\n    win:\n      flag heist_vault_opened\n      unflag elixir_confiscated\n      if flag(confiscated_owls_ink):\n        unflag confiscated_owls_ink\n        gain owls_ink_bottle\n      if flag(confiscated_brightwater):\n        unflag confiscated_brightwater\n        gain brightwater_bottle\n      if not has(owls_ink_bottle) and not has(brightwater_bottle):\n        gain brightwater_bottle\n      heist_loot += 1\n      heist_depth += 1\n      queue heist_way_out now\n      say: {~The padlock gives. Your things are on a shelf with a label in a prefect's neat hand, your name spelled right. You take them back, and one small thing more for the trouble.|Inside, a shelf of confiscated elixirs, gold and black, each labelled with a name. You find what's yours, and leave the rest to their owners' consciences.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~The pick snaps in the lock, with a sound like a shot down here.|The vault's alarm, a very loud goblin, wakes up and says so.}\n> deck: Fetch Corvin's grandfather's deck\n  when: seen(corvin_confiscation_vault) > 0 and not flag(corvin_deck_back)\n  challenge feat using guile adept- +2 if flag(vault_catalogued):\n    win:\n      flag heist_vault_opened corvin_deck_back\n      heist_deck_tonight = 1\n      meet corvin\n      rel corvin +medium\n      heist_depth += 1\n      queue heist_way_out now\n      say: {~The deck is in a tin on the third shelf, labelled in faded ink with a date and a very rude word. You give it to Corvin at breakfast. He doesn't say anything for a whole minute, which is a record.|You find the deck, and the faculty's note on it: \"Marked. Obviously.\" Corvin, when you give it to him, laughs until he has to sit down, and then holds it very carefully.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~The pick snaps in the lock, with a sound like a shot down here.|The vault's alarm, a very loud goblin, wakes up and says so.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/heists/tower.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The seventh tower heist (threads 1; the shape is in heists/shared.grim). Up the servants' stair inside the wall\n#  (lead lead_tower_stair): band 1 the wall stair (depth 1–2, apprentice), band 2 the old classrooms above the landing\n#  of candles (3–4, journeyman), band 3 the upper classrooms (5–6, adept), and at depth 7 the old duelling master's\n#  study, the heist's end. Teaches Extraction. Cover: +1 with `prefect_rota`, +1 if you know the tower by day (three\n#  visits). The chimney (`carto_tower_chimney`) starts you at depth 3.\n#  Plot safety: the tower's top room holds the star key (hs_seventh_tower). The heist ends at the study beside the desks\n#  two turns below the top, and never goes past them to the top room or the hollow; it names no key, grants no clue and\n#  gives no reason for the tower's locking (canon: nobody living remembers). After the key has gone it says only that\n#  the tower above is quiet.\n# ============================================================\n\n@pool tower_heist_entry\nplace: seventh_tower\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 1\norder: last\n\n@event heist_tower_enter\ntitle: The Stair in the Wall\npool: tower_heist_entry\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(heist_tower_known)\nart: door\ntags: danger mystery\nscene: A dark vaulted cellar at midnight, a rack of empty bottles swung aside from a curved stone wall, a narrow stair curling up inside it, a student with a shaded candle.\ntext: {heist_tower_highest == 0: Midnight in the seventh tower's cellar. Behind the rack of empty bottles, your stair curls up inside the wall, one shoulder wide. Above the landing of candles are the old classrooms, shut for a century, with everything their teachers left behind.|{~The bottle rack, the hollow stone, the stair in the wall.|The cellar at midnight, and the cold coming down the wall stair.|Your stair, curling up into the dark inside the stone.}} {flag(carto_tower_chimney): The chimney's soot-black mouth gapes beside it.|}\n> left: Not tonight\n  say: {~You put the rack back and go to bed with the stair in your head.|Not tonight. The tower can keep its classrooms a while longer.|You stand at the foot of the stair and listen to the tower settle, and go no further.}\n> right: Go up\n  hint: A heist: press on card by card, slip away with what you carry any time.\n  flag heist_tower_tried\n  heist_where = 2\n  heist_loot = 0\n  heist_cover = 1\n  if flag(prefect_rota):\n    heist_cover += 1\n  if visits_to(seventh_tower) >= 3:\n    heist_cover += 1\n  if flag(carto_tower_chimney):\n    heist_depth = 3\n  else:\n    heist_depth = 1\n  queue any in heist_tower now\n  say: {flag(carto_tower_chimney): You go up the chimney, back to one side and feet to the other, and come out in a classroom grate, sooty and grinning.|{~You turn sideways and go up into the wall.|You shade your candle and climb, counting the turns.|You go up quietly, one shoulder to the stone, the tower breathing round you.}}\n\n@event heist_tower_unmapped\ntitle: The Tower at Midnight\npool: tower_heist_entry\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(heist_tower_known)\nrepeat: 4\nart: door\ntags: mystery\nscene: The foot of a dark tower stair at midnight, the door locked, a student with a candle looking up at arrow-slits full of moonlight.\ntext: {~Midnight at the foot of the seventh tower. The stair is locked, as it has been for a century, and the arrow-slits above are full of moon.|The seventh tower at midnight, locked, and very quiet. Somewhere up in it, the wind finds a slit and plays one note.} {member(cartographers): The Cartographers swear the tower is fatter outside than in.|Somebody in the castle must know another way up.}\n> left: Listen to the tower\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You sit on the cold bottom step and listen. The tower creaks and settles, floor by floor, and then on one floor it stops, as if something up there is listening back.|You listen until the candle gutters. High up, a door that nobody opens bangs once in the wind.}\n> right: Go back to bed\n  say: {~You leave the tower to its century.|Not without a way up. You go back to bed.}\n\n@thread heist_tower\nfrom: scene\nclues: lead_tower_stair\nname: The Seventh Tower by Night\npremise: A servants' stair climbs inside the seventh tower's wall to the old classrooms, shut for a century. The Cartographers go at midnight.\njournal:\n  flag(heist_tower_tried): You climbed the stair inside the seventh tower's wall at midnight.\n  flag(heist_tower_extraction): In the old duelling master's study you read how to take another's magic, and learned Extraction.\n  flag(heist_tower_study): You reached the old duelling master's study, at the top of the classrooms.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(heist_tower_study)\n\n# The way shut: as in the stacks, a rare step for any depth that ends the night, and the one left when nothing else is.\n@event tw_dead_end\ntitle: A Door That Won't\nthread: heist_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: heist_depth >= 1 and (heist_depth <= 6 or (seen(tw_masters_study) > 0 and since(tw_masters_study) == 0))\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 1\nart: door\ntags: heist\nscene: A narrow tower landing in darkness, an old classroom door swollen shut in its frame, a student's candle at its foot.\ntext: {heist_depth >= 7: The study's lock won't take another try tonight, and every board on the landing has heard you. The only way left is down.|{~The door to the next floor has swollen shut in a century of damp, and it isn't going to open quietly tonight.|The stair turns, and turns, and comes back to the same landing. The tower has decided you've climbed enough.|A whole flight of the old stair has rotted away above you. The rest of the tower is out of reach tonight.}}\n> left: Go back down with what you carry\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {heist_depth >= 7: You leave the master's door to its century and go down, all the way, with what you have.|{~You go back down by the way you know.|You take the hint, and the stair.|You go down, and the tower settles behind you.}}\n> right: Search where you stand\n  challenge research apprentice:\n    win:\n      heist_loot += 1\n      say: {heist_depth >= 7: Behind the stacked desks by the study door, a drawer has come loose. Something small and old comes with you.|{~Under a loose board on the landing, a tin of old marbles and a note that just says \"Sorry about the frog\". You take the tin.|In a niche in the wall, a brass bell-pull handle, very fine. It comes down with you.|On the window ledge, a pair of spectacles with gold rims, left a century ago. You take them.}}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: {heist_depth >= 7: The desks shift as you reach in, and the whole heap groans. You leave them be.|{~You find a splinter, and dust, and nothing.|A board gives under your knee, and you leave the landing alone.|The ledge is crumbling, and so, briefly, are you.}}\n  queue heist_way_out now\n\n# ---------------- Band 1: the wall stair (depth 1–2, apprentice) ----------------\n\n@event tw1_one_shoulder\ntitle: One Shoulder Wide\nthread: heist_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: heist_depth >= 1 and heist_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: door\ntags: heist danger\nscene: A student squeezing sideways up an impossibly narrow spiral stair inside a stone wall, candle held up, cobwebs in their hair.\ntext: {seen(tw1_one_shoulder) > 1: The pinch in the wall stair again. You know exactly how far to breathe out now, and the stone still takes a button for its trouble.|Halfway up the wall stair narrows from one shoulder to less. The servants who built it must have been very thin, or very determined. Somewhere above the pinch, a draught; somewhere below it, everything you've climbed.}\n> left: Back down while you can turn round\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You back down into the cellar and the stair lets you go.|You decide the pinch is the tower's way of saying no.}\n> right: Breathe out and squeeze\n  challenge feat using guile apprentice:\n    win:\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You breathe out, turn sideways and go up the pinch an inch at a time. On the far side, the stair widens like a sigh.|You squeeze through with your candle held over your head and a button left behind on the stone.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~You stick, halfway, for a very long minute, and come loose with a scrape that the whole tower hears.|Your candle catches the cobwebs, and for a moment the stair is full of light and smoke and you.}\n\n@event tw1_someone_else\ntitle: Someone Else's Climb\nthread: heist_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: heist_depth >= 1 and heist_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: lantern\ntags: heist danger\nscene: A narrow stone stair in darkness, a lantern's glow coming down it from above, a student pressed into a tiny niche in the wall.\ntext: {seen(tw1_someone_else) > 1: A light coming down the wall stair again, and footsteps. You're not the only one who uses it, and never were.|A light is coming down the wall stair towards you, and footsteps. Someone else knows this stair.} The only place to go is a niche in the stone, barely deep enough for a candle, let alone you.\n> left: Go back down before they reach you\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You go back down faster than you came up, and out through the cellar, and never see who it was.|You retreat. The light stops at the top of the pinch, as if listening, and goes back up.}\n> right: Press into the niche\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      heist_loot += 1\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~They pass so close their sleeve brushes yours: a student in a house scarf, out of bounds with a lantern and a sack, who never looks sideways. On the step where they paused, a dropped coin. You take it and climb on.|A student with Cartographer chalk on their sleeve goes down past you, humming. On the step above, they've dropped a silver thimble. It's yours now.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~The lantern swings round into your face, and you bolt before the prefect behind it can find your name.|You sneeze. It's the dust. The lantern stops.}\n\n@event tw1_spyhole\ntitle: The Spyhole\nthread: heist_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: heist_depth >= 1 and heist_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: eye\ntags: heist\nscene: A tiny spyhole in a stone wall, a student's eye at it, beyond it a broad tower staircase with a prefect's lantern moving down it.\ntext: {seen(tw1_spyhole) > 1: The spyhole again, and through it the round's lantern on the main stair, on time to the minute.|The wall stair has a spyhole onto the tower's main stair, so the servants could see who was coming. Through it, a lantern: a prefect on the tower's round, slow, thorough, and heading up the way you're going.}\n> left: Wait for the round to pass, then go down\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You watch the prefect all the way up and all the way down, and then you go back to bed.|You take the round as the tower's answer, and go.}\n> right: Time your climb to the round\n  challenge research using guile apprentice:\n    win:\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You count the prefect's steps through the spyhole, and climb when they climb, so their footsteps cover yours.|You learn the round in one pass, and go up behind it, a floor behind, like a shadow it hasn't noticed.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~You climb a beat too late. Your step lands in a silence, and the prefect's lantern stops.|You lose count, and come out on the landing face to face with the round.}\n\n# ---------------- Band 2: the old classrooms (depth 3–4, journeyman) ----------------\n\n@event tw2_exam_cupboard\ntitle: The Examiner's Cupboard\nthread: heist_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: heist_depth >= 3 and heist_depth <= 4\nrepeat: 1\nart: paper\ntags: heist money\nscene: A dusty old classroom at night, a tall locked cupboard with its doors open a crack, bundles of yellowed exam papers tied with ribbon inside.\ntext: {seen(tw2_exam_cupboard) > 1: {flag(heist_papers_taken): The examiner's cupboard again, its lock scratched now, a bundle or two lighter than it was.|The examiner's cupboard again, locked as ever, the ribbons still tied.}|The first classroom off the landing still has its examiner's cupboard, locked, and through the crack in the doors, bundles of exam papers tied with faded ribbon, a century of them, answers and all. Collectors pay for old papers. Historians pay more.}\n> left: Leave the papers, and go\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You leave the past's exams to the past.|You go back down the wall stair with clean hands and nothing in them.}\n> right: Pick the cupboard's lock\n  challenge feat using guile journeyman:\n    win:\n      heist_loot += 2\n      heist_depth += 1\n      flag heist_papers_taken\n      next\n      say: {~The old lock gives in a puff of dust. You take the two bundles with the best seals, a century of sweat in faded ink.|Inside, the papers, and on top, a marked script with a teacher's note: \"See me.\" You take that one, and two bundles more.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~The lock gives, and so does the cupboard, slowly, forward, onto the floor.|The pick snaps, and the cupboard door swings out and bangs like a shot.}\n\n@event tw2_telling_floor\ntitle: The Telling Floor\nthread: heist_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: heist_depth >= 3 and heist_depth <= 4\nrepeat: 1\nart: door\ntags: heist danger\nscene: A long dark classroom with old desks in rows, moonlight in stripes across worn floorboards, a student stepping carefully from board to board.\ntext: {seen(tw2_telling_floor) > 1: The telling floor again. You know half the chalked crosses by heart now, and the other half by bruises.|The old classrooms are a run of rooms, one into the next, and their floorboards have had a century to learn to talk. Every one creaks a different note. Someone, a hundred years ago, chalked a few of them with a cross.}\n> left: Go back the way you know\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You go back over the boards you've already crossed, and down.|You leave the floor to its talking.}\n> right: Cross on the chalked boards\n  challenge feat using guile journeyman:\n    win:\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You go from cross to cross like stepping stones, and the floor keeps its mouth shut all the way across.|You cross in a silence so careful the floor seems to hold its breath with you.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~One cross was a joke. The board under it shrieks like a gull.|You miss a chalk mark by an inch, and the floor tells the whole tower.}\n\n@event tw2_class_register\ntitle: The Last Register\nthread: heist_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: heist_depth >= 3 and heist_depth <= 4\nrepeat: 1\nart: book\ntags: heist mystery\nscene: A teacher's desk in an abandoned classroom, an old leather register open on it, the last page of names with a date a century old, moonlight on it.\ntext: {seen(tw2_class_register) > 1: The register still lies open at its last day, everyone present, its silver clasp catching the moon.|On the teacher's desk, a class register lies open at its last day, a century ago. Every name is ticked present. After the last name, in the same hand: \"Tower closed. Lessons moved.\" Nothing about why. The register is bound in good leather, with a silver clasp.}\n> left: Leave it open at its last day\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You leave the register as it was, everyone present.|You don't take it. It seems rude to the class.}\n> right: Take the register\n  challenge feat using guile journeyman:\n    win:\n      heist_loot += 1\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You slip it into your shirt, silver clasp cold against your ribs, and go on as quietly as a class that has been told to.|You take it. Somewhere a door bangs in the wind, and nobody comes.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~The silver clasp snaps shut on your finger with a click that carries.|As you lift it, the whole desk shifts on the boards, and the tower answers.}\n\n# ---------------- Band 3: the upper classrooms (depth 5–6, adept) ----------------\n\n@event tw3_no_wind\ntitle: When the Wind Stops\nthread: heist_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: heist_depth >= 5 and heist_depth <= 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: moon\ntags: heist danger\nscene: A dark tower corridor lined with arrow-slits, moonlight falling through them in bars, utterly still air, a student frozen mid-step.\ntext: {seen(tw3_no_wind) > 1: The corridor of slits again, and again the wind drops as you reach it, as if it knows your step.|Up here the arrow-slits have been playing the wind all night, covering your steps. Now, between one note and the next, the wind stops, and the tower is so quiet you can hear your candle burn.} ⚠ One wrong sound here, and there's no near thing.\n> left: Wait for the wind, then go back down\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You wait, not breathing, until the wind comes back, and go down under its cover.|You stand like a statue until the slits start singing again, and take the hint.}\n> right: Cross in the silence\n  challenge feat using guile adept-:\n    win:\n      heist_loot += 1\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You cross the whole corridor in the silence, step by soundless step. At the end, on a sill, a silver pencil someone left in a hurry. You take it.|You go through the quiet like a cat through a sleeping house, and find on the far side a dropped purse, very old.}\n    lose:\n      queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~Your shoe squeaks. In that silence, it's a shout.|You drop the candle. It rolls, very loudly, all the way down the corridor and the stair, to a prefect's feet.}\n\n@event tw3_orrery\ntitle: The Orrery\nthread: heist_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: heist_depth >= 5 and heist_depth <= 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: star\ntags: heist money\nscene: An abandoned astronomy classroom at night, a large brass orrery of planets on arms still slowly turning in the dark, a student reaching carefully toward one small planet.\ntext: {seen(tw3_orrery) > 1: {flag(heist_pearl_taken): The orrery is still turning, one planet short where you took the pearl.|The orrery is still turning, the pearl still riding its little arm.}|The astronomy classroom's brass orrery is still turning, a century after anyone wound it, its planets on long arms going round and round in the dark. The smallest planet is a pearl. It would come off its arm, if you were quick, and the orrery wouldn't stop.}\n> left: Leave it turning\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You watch it go round once more and leave it to its century.|You leave the planets where they are. Somebody should.}\n> right: Take the pearl as it passes\n  challenge feat using guile adept-:\n    win:\n      flag heist_pearl_taken\n      heist_loot += 2\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You time the arm, and the pearl is in your palm before it's gone round. The orrery doesn't notice. It goes on turning, one planet short.|Quick as a pickpocket, you lift the pearl as it passes. The little arm swings on, empty, keeping time.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~You catch the arm instead of the pearl, and the whole orrery stops with a groan of brass that wakes the tower.|You're too slow, and the arm takes your knuckles going round, and you yelp.}\n\n@event tw3_portrait_eyes\ntitle: The Portrait on the Landing\nthread: heist_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: heist_depth >= 5 and heist_depth <= 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: eye\ntags: heist danger\nscene: A dim tower corridor with a large stern painted portrait of a robed professor, a student creeping past beneath it, the painted eyes turned toward them.\ntext: {seen(tw3_portrait_eyes) > 1: The portrait at the top of the classrooms opens its painted eyes before you've reached it. \"You again,\" it says.|At the top of the classrooms hangs a portrait of a professor a century dead, in a frame worth more than your trunk. The painted eyes follow you, and the painted mouth is opening, as portraits here do, to ask what you think you're doing.}\n> left: Apologise to him, and go\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You bow, and say you were just leaving, and he says \"Quite,\" and you were.|You tell the portrait you're lost. \"Down,\" he says, and you go down.}\n> right: Talk your way past him\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      heist_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You tell him you're the new caretaker, sent to check the damp. He's been bored for a century, and believes you because he wants to.|You tell him the lessons are moving back up. He's so pleased he forgets to ask your name.}\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~\"Intruder,\" says the portrait, in a voice built for a full hall, and doesn't stop saying it.|He doesn't believe a word, and he has a century of saved-up shouting.}\n\n# ---------------- Depth 7: the duelling master's study (the heist's end) ----------------\n\n@event tw_masters_study\ntitle: The Duelling Master's Study\nthread: heist_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nwhen: heist_depth >= 7\nrepeat: 1\nart: book\ntags: heist magic danger\nscene: A small locked study at the top of an old tower's classrooms, a desk under a dust sheet, a leather notebook in a glass case, a student kneeling at the lock with picks.\ntext: At the top of the old classrooms, the stair is choked with stacked desks, and beside them is a locked study with a brass plate: MASTER OF DUELLING. It was locked with the rest of the tower, and nobody has had its key since. {flag(star_key) or flag(gave_star_key) or flag(key_taken): Up past the desks, the tower is quiet.|Up past the desks, something in the tower hums at midnight, and every hair on your arms stands up. You won't be going higher.}\n> left: Leave the master to his study\n  flag heist_tower_study\n  queue heist_way_out now\n  say: {~You touch the brass plate once and go back down. You got this far.|You don't try the door. Some rooms are closed for a reason.}\n> right: Pick the lock and search the study\n  challenge feat using guile adept-:\n    win:\n      flag heist_tower_study\n      heist_loot += 2\n      heist_depth += 1\n      if spell_level(extraction) < 1:\n        learn_spell extraction\n        flag heist_tower_extraction\n        alignment -small\n        say: In a glass case lies a notebook the master confiscated from a student duellist long ago, its ink faded brown. In a neat and terrible hand it tells how to reach into someone and take their magic for yours. You read it through, and close it with a strength in your hands that isn't yours. Extraction.\n      else:\n        say: The confiscated notebook is in its glass case, and you know already what it says. You leave it, and take the master's silver inkstand and a box of old duelling medals instead.\n      queue heist_way_out now\n    lose:\n      if heist_cover > 0:\n        queue heist_slip now\n      else:\n        queue heist_caught now\n      say: {~The old lock is warded, and it rings like a struck sword.|The door gives, and so does its top hinge, and it comes down on the desks with a crash that wakes everything.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/hollowstar/convergence.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  THE HOLLOW STAR, v2, Act IV: convergence (docs/plots/hollowstar-v2.md).\n#  New cards; hs_last_winter (the opener) and hs_isolde_summons keep their ids where they were.\n#    hs_dumont_letter  the Dumonts' mother is coming south for the Blossom moon\n#    hs_go_down_early  go down before the deadline (in spring): the cult's circle is half-formed, and only those who promised come\n# ============================================================\n\n@event hs_dumont_letter\ntitle: A Letter From the North\nthread: hs_convergence\nactor: kael\nwhen: met(kael) and rel(kael) >= 5\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nweight: 16\nart: letter\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nscene: A pale young man holding a letter sealed with a black star at arm's length, as if it might bite, frost creeping up the paper from his fingers.\ntext: Kael finds you and puts a letter on the table between you without a word. Black-star seal, broken. One line, in a beautiful hand: \"I will be at the lake for the Blossom moon. Be there, my son, and bring your friend.\" It's signed \"Mother\". {seen(kael_letter_home) > 0: It's her answer to the one he rewrote eleven times. |}His hands are shaking. \"She has never come south,\" he says. \"She has never once called me that.\"\n> left: Burn it together\n  hint: He'll remember who held the match.\n  rel kael +medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You hold it over a candle between you until the black star curls. The flame burns blue, then white, then out, and leaves frost on the candlestick. \"She'll know,\" Kael says. \"Good.\" It's the most like his sister you've ever heard him sound.\n> right: Keep it. Know your enemy.\n  hint: Useful. He won't like it.\n  flag kept_dumont_letter\n  lore +small\n  rel kael -tiny\n  say: You fold it into your notes. Kael watches you do it and says nothing, which is worse than if he had. That night you read the line again by lamplight. The handwriting is beautiful, and patient, and very sure of itself.\n\n@event hs_go_down_early\ntitle: Before They're Ready\nthread: hs_convergence\nplace: lakeshore\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: final_year and month >= 7 and month <= 8 and flag(star_key) and (clue(seven_locks) or clue(ilse_truth)) and hs_called()\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 30\nart: door\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\n# The early descent (v2, decision 2026-10-26): go down before the Blossom moon, in spring (Stormbloom or Greenrise, so\n# \"the spring the lake froze\" stays true). The Night starts at once, at its second card (this one is its first): the\n# cult's circle is half-formed (easier odds against it), but only the allies who promised (winter_*) come with you.\nscene: A frozen lakeshore at night, a student holding a black star-shaped key up against the moon, a boathouse door ajar behind them.\ntext: The lake is freezing in circles tonight, and the key in your pocket is so cold it burns. {seen(hs_dumont_letter) > 0 or seen(hs_isolde_summons) > 0: The Dumonts' mother is coming for the Blossom moon.|Someone is coming for the Blossom moon; the lake knows it.} Her circle isn't ready yet, and neither is the headmistress's. If you went down tonight you'd catch them all unready, and you'd have only the ones who promised you beside you.\n> left: Go down tonight\n  hint: The Night, now. Only the allies who promised come.\n  flag hs_early\n  advance hollowstar to 12\n  find lake_bell\n  queue hs_night_company now\n  say: You walk down to the boathouse without telling anyone. As you lift the latch, the whole lake behind you goes white in one breath, from shore to shore, and somewhere under it the bell begins to toll.\n> right: Wait for the Blossom moon\n  hint: Everyone who'd stand with you will be there. So will everyone against you.\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You stand on the shore until your feet are numb, then go back up to bed. The ice creaks after you all the way. Not yet.\n"},{"path":"content/events/hollowstar/escalation.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  THE HOLLOW STAR, v2, Act III: escalation (docs/plots/hollowstar-v2.md).\n#  New cards; the act's older ones keep their ids where they were (chain_hollowstar*.grim).\n#    hs_shadow_late   your shadow moves a moment late (it opens the way to Seraphine's second shadow)\n#    hs_grey_daylight the climax: the man in grey strikes in daylight, for the key or for Kael\n#    hs_key_recover   the key taken, and four ways to win it back, only one of them a check you might lose badly\n# ============================================================\n\n@event hs_shadow_late\ntitle: A Moment Late\nthread: hs_escalation\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntime: evening night\nweight: 14\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nscene: A student on a lamplit staircase looking at their own shadow on the wall, which is still raising its hand after theirs has dropped.\ntext: On the stairs by lamplight you wave to a friend and drop your hand, and your shadow on the wall goes on waving a moment longer. Then it lowers its hand, slowly, as if it had only just noticed you were watching. You stand on the stair for a long time. It doesn't move again. Neither do you.\n> left: Find Seraphine, now\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine +small\n  mood -small\n  say: She opens her door before you knock, already in her coat. \"Yours too,\" she says. \"I painted it last winter. I didn't know whose it was.\" She won't show you the painting. She does walk you back to your room and makes you leave the lamp lit.\n> right: Blow the lamp out\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: In the dark there are no shadows, and you go up to bed without looking at the walls. You sleep badly. In the morning the frost on your window has melted in the shape of a hand, on the inside of the glass.\n\n@event hs_grey_daylight\ntitle: In Broad Daylight\nbeat: 8\nslot: noon\ntype: menu\nwhen: met(kael) and since(hs_dark_tower) >= 2\nthread: hs_escalation\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: mask\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\n# Act III's climax (v2): two of the act's finds bring it early (hs_escalation_progress), the deadline otherwise; it\n# opens Act IV. He comes for the star key if you hold it, else for Kael. Losing the key is recoverable, with or without\n# a fight (hs_key_recover; the headmistress's summons).\nscene: A sunlit courtyard at noon gone silent and frozen, students pressed against the walls, a tall man in grey walking straight across it toward one student.\non_show:\n  advance hollowstar to 10\ntext: Noon, the courtyard full, and the man in grey walks in through the gate as if he had an appointment. The fountain freezes, then the flagstones, then the air. Students scramble back against the walls. He stops in front of you. {flag(star_key): \"The key,\" he says pleasantly, holding out a gloved hand. \"It was never yours.\"|\"Where is Kael Dumont?\" he says pleasantly. \"His mother would like a word.\"}\n> fight: Draw on him, here\n  hint: Combat, in front of everyone. Lose, and he takes what he came for.\n  if flag(kael_ally):\n    challenge combat expert drift mid with kael:\n      win:\n        flag grey_beaten\n        renown +medium\n        alignment +tiny\n        say: Kael's frost and your spell hit him together, and the man in grey goes skidding back across the ice he made. For the first time he looks surprised. He straightens his coat, bows to the whole courtyard, and walks out of the gate. Then the cheering starts.\n      lose:\n        if flag(star_key):\n          unflag star_key\n          flag key_taken\n        else:\n          gain grey_watching\n        renown -tiny\n        say: It goes on for a long minute, in front of everyone, and then your last spell is still in the air when the flagstones come up to meet you both. {flag(key_taken): When you get up, the key is gone from your pocket, and so is he.|When you get up he is gone, and Kael is bleeding from a cut over his eye that frosts over as you watch.}\n  else:\n    challenge combat expert drift mid:\n      win:\n        flag grey_beaten\n        renown +medium\n        alignment +tiny\n        say: Your spell catches him square and he goes skidding back across the ice he made. For the first time he looks surprised. He straightens his coat, bows to the whole courtyard, and walks out of the gate. Then the cheering starts.\n      lose:\n        if flag(star_key):\n          unflag star_key\n          flag key_taken\n        else:\n          gain grey_watching\n        renown -tiny\n        say: You trade spells with him across the frozen fountain for longer than anyone thought you could, and then the flagstones come up to meet you. {flag(key_taken): When you get up, the key is gone from your pocket, and so is he.|When you get up he is gone, and the frost on the flagstones spells out a name: Kael's.}\n> hide: Run, and take what he wants with you\n  hint: Guile. Half the school is between you and the gate{flag(confronted_grey): , and he knows your face|}.\n  challenge deceive expert -1 if flag(confronted_grey):\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: You duck into the crowd and the crowd closes behind you; a dozen first-years suddenly need to cross the courtyard all at once. By the time he has walked through them, you're three corridors away {flag(star_key): with the key|with Kael}, and the bell for lessons is ringing.\n    lose:\n      if flag(star_key):\n        unflag star_key\n        flag key_taken\n      else:\n        gain grey_watching\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: He doesn't chase you. He simply arrives, at the end of the corridor, ahead of you. {flag(key_taken): He holds out his hand, and the key leaves your pocket on its own and goes to him.|\"Tell him she's coming,\" he says, and leaves you shaking.}\n> call: Shout for the headmistress\n  hint: Sure. She'll want to know why he came for you.\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +small\n  if flag(star_key):\n    unflag star_key\n    flag gave_star_key wardens_noticed\n    say: She is there before you finish her name, and the courtyard thaws around her feet. He bows to her and goes. Then she holds out her own hand. \"The key,\" she says, exactly as he did. \"For safekeeping.\" You give it to her.\n  else:\n    rel kael -tiny\n    say: She is there before you finish her name, and the courtyard thaws around her feet. He bows to her and goes. Kael, who heard the whole thing from a window, doesn't speak to you for a week: \"You told her he was looking for me.\"\n\n@event hs_key_recover\ntitle: Getting It Back\ntype: menu\nthread: hs_escalation\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(key_taken) and not flag(star_key) and not flag(gave_star_key)\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 18\nart: key\ntags: mystery hollowstar\n# The star key taken (hs_grey_daylight): four ways back, three of them without a fight or a hard check, and \"not yet\",\n# which brings this card back after its two-month cooldown (repeat: 2) (decision 2026-10-26: losing the key is harsh but never final).\nscene: A narrow upstairs corridor of a village inn at night, a door with frost on its handle, a student with a candle hesitating outside it.\ntext: The man in grey has the star key, and by now the whole academy knows it. He has taken the top room at the Gilded Newt, and the landlord says he never seems to sleep. The headmistress has said nothing to you. Kael hasn't stopped saying things. There are ways to get it back, and none of them involve asking him nicely.\n> isolde: Go to the headmistress\n  hint: She'll get it back, and keep it until the night.\n  when: flag(wardens_noticed) or flag(sided_wardens) or flag(warden_trial) or rel(isolde) >= 15\n  unflag key_taken\n  flag gave_star_key\n  rel isolde +small\n  mood -small\n  say: She listens without a word, then puts on her coat. In the morning the key is on her desk, and there is frost on her sleeve up to the elbow. \"He was polite about it,\" she says. \"They always are.\" She doesn't give it back to you.\n> steal: Steal it back from the Newt\n  hint: Guile. He never seems to sleep.\n  challenge deceive expert:\n    win:\n      unflag key_taken\n      flag star_key\n      find star_key\n      say: He is sitting up in the dark with his eyes open, and he doesn't see you. The key is on the windowsill, in a dish of snow. You take it, and back out, and don't breathe until you're on the road.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: The landing creaks. He says \"Good evening\" through the closed door, and the cold comes under it like water. You run, and slip on the stairs, and get back to the castle with a sprained wrist and nothing else.\n> family: Ask a Dumont where he'd keep it\n  hint: Charm. Kael or Ilse know how the families hide things.\n  when: flag(kael_ally) or flag(ilse_saved)\n  challenge negotiate adept likely:\n    win:\n      unflag key_taken\n      flag star_key\n      find star_key\n      say: \"Snow,\" says {flag(ilse_saved): Ilse|Kael}, at once. \"He'll keep it in snow, so it can't call to anyone.\" It's on his windowsill, in a dish of it. The Dumont who told you stands lookout in the lane while you climb, and doesn't say a word on the way home.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: \"I don't know,\" {flag(ilse_saved): Ilse|Kael} says, too quickly, and won't look at you. Whatever the families do with what they take, it isn't something they want to tell you. Not yet.\n> wand: Let Edmund's wand find it\n  hint: Arcana. The wand hums near anything the founders made.\n  when: has_item(edmunds_silver_wand)\n  challenge spell adept likely:\n    win:\n      unflag key_taken\n      flag star_key\n      find star_key\n      mana -small\n      say: The silver wand drags at your hand like a dog on a lead, all the way to the Newt and up the back stairs, and goes quiet beside a dish of snow on a windowsill. The man in grey is out. The key is not.\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      say: The wand hums, and wavers, and points at the lake every time. Wherever he's keeping it, it's near the water, and you can't follow it there. Not tonight.\n> wait: Not yet\n  hint: It'll keep. He will too.\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You leave it for now. Every night you can feel it from across the village, cold, the way you feel a draught from a door left open.\n"},{"path":"content/events/hollowstar/night.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  THE HOLLOW STAR, v2: the Night (docs/plots/hollowstar-v2.md), played in one sitting.\n#    hs_the_night (chain_hollowstar.grim) -> hs_night_company -> hs_night_cult -> hs_tunnel -> [hs_night_ilse]\n#      -> hs_night_shadow -> hs_threshold -> hs_beneath -> one aftermath per outcome\n#  Whoever comes down with you rides along as each card's actor (`queue … with kael`); a fight `with npc` has them\n#  throw their Hoarfrost in now and then (combat/HANDOFF.md, \"Allies\"). Every fight sits beside a way that isn't one.\n#  A clue you never found is still paid off here, at worse odds. Losses that aren't fights add to night_hurt, which\n#  the aftermath settles in Health, so nothing interrupts the Night. The outcome closes the arc; the aftermaths\n#  belong to no arc, so they still come after it is closed (sealed: hs_aftermath with Kael, hs_aftermath_quiet with\n#  whoever else came down).\n#  A player the Night never called gets hs_the_night_above: the lake freezes without them.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event hs_the_night_above\ntitle: Someone Else's Night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: auto\narc: hollowstar\nwhen: final_year and clock(hollowstar) >= 12 and not hs_called()\npriority: 70\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nscene: A dormitory window at night, a lake frozen white below, lanterns moving out on the ice, a student watching from behind the glass.\ntext: You wake in the night to the bell under the lake, tolling and tolling. From your window the whole lake is white, and out on the ice there are lanterns moving in a ring, and figures you can't make out. Somebody runs past your door. Nobody knocks on it.\n> left: Keep the first-years in their beds\n  close hollowstar\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You sit on the stairs of the first-year tower until dawn with a lamp, saying \"go back to bed\" forty times. By morning the lake has thawed. Someone else went down with a lantern. You never learn the whole of it.\n> right: Watch from the window\n  close hollowstar\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: You watch until the lanterns go out, one by one, and then something under the ice glows and goes dark. By morning the lake is blue. At breakfast the headmistress's hair is a shade whiter, and she smiles at nobody.\n\n@event hs_night_company\ntitle: Who Comes Down With You\nthread: hs_night\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\npriority: 95\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nscene: The boathouse at night, its hatch open on stone steps going down into blue light, a student at the top with a lantern and figures hurrying in behind.\ntext: The boathouse hatch is open, and the steps go down into blue light. {night_kael() or night_ilse() or night_maud() or night_isolde(): {flag(hs_early): You came without telling anyone, and only the ones who promised have followed you down the shore.|Behind you, people are running across the frozen lawn: the ones who said they'd stand with you, and meant it.} The tunnel down is narrow. There's room for two of you, and no more. Who comes?|Nobody has followed you down the shore. The tunnel is narrow, and the dark at the bottom is very quiet.}\n> kael: Kael\n  hint: Hoarfrost beside you in every fight. His mother is on the ice.\n  when: night_kael()\n  flag night_kael\n  queue hs_night_cult now with kael\n  say: Kael takes the lantern out of your hand without asking. \"My family,\" he says. \"My door.\" Frost runs down the steps ahead of you, so you don't slip.\n> ilse: Ilse\n  hint: Her frost beside you in every fight. Her mother is on the ice.\n  when: night_ilse()\n  flag night_ilse\n  queue hs_night_cult now with ilse\n  say: Ilse is already on the top step, white furs and a wand like an icicle. \"I was raised for this door,\" she says. \"I'd like to see their faces when I close it.\"\n> maud: Old Maud\n  hint: No help in a fight. The founders' wall knows her.\n  when: night_maud()\n  flag night_maud\n  queue hs_night_cult now\n  say: Maud comes down the lawn at a pace that should be impossible at her age. \"I lost him to that door,\" she says. \"I'm not losing you, dearie.\" {has_item(edmunds_silver_wand): She taps the wand in your hand. \"Keep his wand up. It knows the way.\"|}\n> isolde: The headmistress\n  hint: No help in a fight. Her seal beside yours at the door.\n  when: night_isolde()\n  flag night_isolde\n  queue hs_night_cult now\n  say: The headmistress walks into the boathouse as if into her own study, her ring lit like a lamp. \"After you,\" she says. \"I've read about this door all my life.\"\n> alone: No one. Keep them safe above.\n  hint: Every fight is yours alone. Nobody you love goes under the lake.\n  alignment +tiny\n  queue hs_night_cult now\n  say: You tell them to guard the towers, and close the hatch on their protests. The steps are slick and the dark is very quiet, and it's the bravest and loneliest thing you've ever done.\n\n@event hs_night_cult\ntitle: The Circle on the Ice\nthread: hs_night\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\npriority: 95\nart: moon\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\n# The Dumonts' mother leads the cult's circle (decision 2026-10-26): the family's conflict, out loud on the ice. It pays\n# off kael_family (said to your face) and codex_names (the names, spoken or read off the furs).\nscene: A ring of figures in black furs with lanterns on a frozen lake at night, and at its centre a tall woman in white furs with a pale, proud face, her wand raised.\ntext: The tunnel comes up through the ice inside a ring of lanterns: twelve figures in black furs, chanting{flag(hs_early): , half the lanterns not yet lit|}. At the centre stands a tall woman in white with Kael's face, thirty years on{flag(hs_early): , snow from the North still on her shoulders|}. Madame Dumont. {flag(night_kael): \"My son. You came after all.\" \"You never came for me,\" Kael shouts. \"You came for the door!\"|{flag(night_ilse): \"Ilse,\" she says, the way you'd call a dog. \"I'm not yours to call,\" says Ilse, white to the lips.|{flag(night_isolde): \"Headmistress,\" she says. \"Still guarding a hole in the ground.\" \"Still trying to open one, Madame,\" says the headmistress.|\"Kael's little friend,\" she says. \"Step aside.\"}}}\n> fight: Break the circle by force\n  hint: Combat against her{flag(hs_early): . Half her circle isn't here.|.}\n  queue hs_tunnel now with npc\n  challenge combat master- unlikely with npc +1 if flag(hs_early):\n    win:\n      flag cult_broken\n      find kael_family\n      say: Her ward breaks and she goes down on one knee on the ice, and the chant stops dead. She looks up at you with Kael's eyes. \"So this is what he chose,\" she says. The circle scatters into the dark, carrying her.\n    lose:\n      night_hurt += 1\n      find kael_family\n      say: Her last word lands before your last spell does, and the ice throws you down. \"Go down, then,\" she says. \"The door will have you either way.\" The chant goes on, louder, and the glow under the ring grows.\n> names: Speak the names from the Codex\n  hint: Lore. {clue(codex_names): You've read them.|You'll have to read them off their furs.}\n  queue hs_tunnel now with npc\n  if clue(codex_names):\n    challenge research expert likely +1 if flag(hs_early):\n      win:\n        flag cult_broken\n        find kael_family\n        say: You say their names aloud, the ones from the Codex's first page, one for each of them. Each name you say stops a mouth. When you reach \"Dumont\", the woman in white stops chanting, and the ring of lanterns goes out.\n      lose:\n        night_hurt += 1\n        find kael_family\n        say: You lose your place halfway down the list, and the ones you haven't named say yours back. Your fingertips go grey with cold. The circle closes again behind you as you run for the tunnel.\n  else:\n    challenge research master- unlikely +1 if flag(hs_early):\n      win:\n        flag cult_broken\n        find codex_names\n        find kael_family\n        say: Their names are stitched into the hems of their furs, the way the Codex lists them. You read them off one by one as they turn, and each name you say stops a mouth. At \"Dumont\", the woman in white falls silent.\n      lose:\n        night_hurt += 1\n        find codex_names\n        find kael_family\n        say: You can read the names stitched in their furs, but not fast enough. Half of them say yours back, and your fingertips go grey with cold. You run for the tunnel with the chant at your back.\n> family: Let them answer their mother\n  hint: Charm. Say what she sent them for, and what they chose.\n  when: flag(night_kael) or flag(night_ilse)\n  queue hs_tunnel now with npc\n  challenge negotiate master- unlikely +1 if clue(kael_family) +1 if clue(ilse_truth) +1 if flag(hs_early) +1 if flag(kept_dumont_letter):\n    win:\n      flag cult_broken mother_answered\n      if flag(night_kael):\n        rel kael +large\n      else:\n        rel ilse +large\n      find kael_family\n      say: You start it, and then you stop, because {flag(night_kael): Kael|Ilse} is shouting, at last, everything a whole life kept in: \"You never once said well done. Not once!\" The circle wavers. The chant breaks. Madame Dumont lowers her wand and says nothing at all.\n    lose:\n      night_hurt += 1\n      find kael_family\n      say: She lets them shout, and then says, quite gently, \"Are you finished?\" {flag(night_kael): Kael|Ilse} is shaking. The circle goes on chanting as if nobody had spoken, and you drag your friend down the tunnel before the ice opens.\n> round: Go round the circle\n  hint: Guile. You'll get past. They'll still be chanting.\n  queue hs_tunnel now with npc\n  challenge deceive master- +1 if flag(hs_early) +1 if flag(kept_dumont_letter):\n    win:\n      find kael_family\n      say: You go round the outside of the ring, low, in the dark between the lanterns, and nobody turns. At the tunnel mouth you look back. She is looking straight at you, and she smiles, and doesn't stop chanting.\n    lose:\n      night_hurt += 1\n      find kael_family\n      say: A lantern swings your way, and the woman in white says a word, and the ice bucks under your feet. You reach the tunnel on your hands and knees, with the cold all the way into your chest.\n\n@event hs_night_ilse\ntitle: Ilse in the Dark\nthread: hs_night\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: tense\npriority: 95\nart: snow\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\n# Ilse taken (ilse_grey_returns, v2): she stands in the way under the man in grey's compulsion. Reach her, or break it\n# in a fight, and she is saved on screen (with Kael there, the Dumonts are reunited).\nscene: A young woman in white furs standing in a dark tunnel under ice, eyes empty, frost falling from her hands like flour.\ntext: Where the tunnel opens into the dark, Ilse is standing in the way. Her eyes are on nothing, and frost is falling from her hands like flour. \"Go back,\" she says, in the man in grey's pleasant voice, not her own. \"This is my door. I was raised for it.\" {flag(night_kael): Kael says her name. Then he shouts it, and it echoes all the way down.|}\n> reach: Reach her\n  hint: Charm. What she was raised for, and who she is, are your words.\n  queue hs_night_shadow now with npc\n  challenge negotiate master- unlikely +1 if clue(ilse_truth) +1 if flag(night_kael) +1 if rel(ilse) >= 15:\n    win:\n      unflag ilse_taken\n      flag ilse_saved\n      if flag(night_kael):\n        flag dumont_reunited\n      rel ilse +large\n      alignment +small\n      find ilse_truth\n      say: You tell her {flag(ilse_confided): what she told you once in the snow: that nobody asked her|that nobody ever asked her what she wanted}. Her eyes find yours. Then she's crying, furiously, and the frost stops falling. \"I can hear him,\" she says. \"I can't hear him any more.\" {flag(night_kael): Kael gets to her first.|}\n    lose:\n      night_hurt += 1\n      find ilse_truth\n      say: She listens to every word with the man in grey's polite face, and steps aside to let you pass. When you look back, she is walking up the tunnel toward {flag(cult_broken): the empty ice where her mother's circle was|her mother's circle}, and doesn't turn round.\n> fight: Break his hold on her\n  hint: Combat, against her. Win, and the compulsion breaks.\n  queue hs_night_shadow now with npc\n  challenge combat vs ilse.arcana with npc:\n    win:\n      unflag ilse_taken\n      flag ilse_saved\n      if flag(night_kael):\n        flag dumont_reunited\n      rel ilse +small\n      find ilse_truth\n      say: Your last spell hits the frost in her hands, not her, and it shatters, and so does whatever was holding her. She sits down hard on the tunnel floor. \"That hurt,\" she says, in her own voice, and then, \"Thank you,\" and then she gets up.\n    lose:\n      night_hurt += 1\n      find ilse_truth\n      say: She fights like what she was raised to be. When you get up, she's gone, up the tunnel toward the ice, and there's a single white glove on the stone.\n> past: Go past her\n  hint: She won't stop you. She won't come with you.\n  queue hs_night_shadow now with npc\n  mood -medium\n  find ilse_truth\n  say: You step round her. She doesn't stop you; she doesn't see you. {flag(night_kael): Kael stands with her a moment longer, then comes after you with his jaw set and doesn't speak again until the wall.|You don't look back. You'll think about not looking back for a long time.}\n\n@event hs_night_shadow\ntitle: Your Own Shadow\nthread: hs_night\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\npriority: 95\nart: mirror\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\n# The second shadow (v2): the Star's champion is you. Forewarned (second_shadow), the odds are better; letting it in\n# opens the bargain and the door.\nscene: A narrow stone tunnel under the ice, a student's shadow standing out from the wet wall on its own, with the student's face and eyes like holes.\ntext: Where the tunnel narrows, your lantern throws your shadow onto the wet stone, and it stays there when you stop. Then it turns round. It has your face, and the Star's eyes. {clue(second_shadow): Seraphine painted this. You knew it was coming.|Nobody warned you. Nobody could have.} \"Let me in,\" it says, in your voice. \"We'd be so much stronger.\"\n> fight: Fight it\n  hint: Combat, against everything you know.\n  queue hs_threshold now with npc\n  challenge combat master- unlikely with npc +1 if clue(second_shadow):\n    win:\n      flag shadow_beaten\n      find second_shadow\n      say: It fights with your spells, in your order, and it is a beat late, every time, the way it was on the stairs. You use the beat. It breaks against the wall like water, and your own shadow lies down on the floor where it belongs.\n    lose:\n      night_hurt += 1\n      find second_shadow\n      say: It knows every spell you're going to cast, because you do. It puts you on the floor, then sinks back into the wall and waits there, smiling with your mouth.\n> light: Hold up a star's light\n  hint: Arcana. Light the Star can't swallow; Starlight helps.\n  queue hs_threshold now with npc\n  challenge spell master- unlikely +1 if spell_level(starlight) >= 1 +1 if clue(second_shadow):\n    win:\n      flag shadow_beaten\n      find second_shadow\n      mana -small\n      say: You call down the light of a named star into your hand, and the shadow has nowhere to stand. It shrinks back into your outline on the floor with a sound like a sigh, and stays there.\n    lose:\n      night_hurt += 1\n      find second_shadow\n      mana -small\n      say: The light comes, and wavers, and the shadow simply steps into the dark behind it. It is still there on the wall when you go on, a moment late behind you, all the way to the door.\n> let: Let it back in\n  hint: It fits. That's the trouble.\n  queue hs_threshold now with npc\n  flag hollow_tempted\n  alignment -medium\n  mood +small\n  find second_shadow\n  say: You open your arms and it steps into you, and it fits, it fits so well. You've never felt so strong, or so certain, or so cold. {flag(night_kael): Kael takes a step back from you, and doesn't know he's done it.|}\n\n# ---------------- After the Night: one card per outcome (sealed and failed in chain_hollowstar.grim) ----------------\n\n@event hs_aftermath_bargain\ntitle: The Star's Gift\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: star\ntags: hollowstar story\n# The bargain pays at once (feedback 2026-10-25): what it gave is said here, and none of it sits at a cap.\nscene: A student at a dormitory mirror at dawn, a crowned black star glowing on their palm, a purse spilling coins on the bed behind them.\non_show:\n  if night_hurt >= 2:\n    health -medium\n  else:\n    health -small\ntext: You wake with the Star's gift already in you. Eclipse, a spell you never studied, sits in your hands like an old habit. There's a purse on your pillow heavy with coins nobody gave you. The mark on your palm has grown a crown of points, and a voice at the back of your mind says, pleasantly, \"Good morning.\" {flag(ilse_taken): Ilse went north before dawn, with the man in grey.|}\n> left: Use it\n  hint: The Star likes to be used.\n  alignment -small\n  mood +small\n  say: You light every candle in your room with a look, and then put them all out with another. It's easy. It's so easy. Somewhere under the lake, something settles in to wait for what it's owed.\n> right: Hide the mark\n  hint: Gloves, in spring. People will ask.\n  guile +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You wear gloves from that day on. People ask, and you say it's a burn, and it is, in a way. At night the crown on your palm glows through the leather, and you lie awake watching it.\n\n@event hs_aftermath_quiet\ntitle: Only Water Now\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: sun\ntags: hollowstar story\n# The seal held, for a player Kael isn't with (never met, or turned against): hs_aftermath is his jetty and his tea.\nscene: The lake glittering in spring sunshine, students skimming stones off the jetty, one student sitting a little apart on the shore.\non_show:\n  close hollowstar\n  if night_hurt >= 2:\n    health -medium\n  elif night_hurt >= 1:\n    health -small\ntext: The lake glitters as if nothing happened. Most of the academy believes there was a strange spring frost. {flag(night_ilse): Ilse is at the end of the jetty, boots off, feet in water that is only water now.|{flag(night_maud): Maud waves from her step, two mugs of tea beside her.|{flag(night_isolde): Across the courtyard, the headmistress inclines her head to you, once.|Nobody knows where you were last night. Nobody thanks you.}}} {flag(ilse_taken): Ilse went north before dawn, with the man in grey.|}\n> left: Go and sit with someone\n  mood +medium\n  if flag(night_ilse):\n    rel ilse +medium\n  elif flag(night_maud):\n    rel maud +medium\n  elif flag(night_isolde):\n    rel isolde +medium\n  say: {flag(night_ilse): You put your feet in beside hers. It's freezing. She laughs, properly, for the first time you've ever heard.|{flag(night_maud): She pours you a cup without asking. \"Edmund would have liked you,\" she says, and that's all she says about it.|{flag(night_isolde): She beats you at chess that evening in nine moves, and lets you see her smile.|You find friends who never knew, and let them talk about nothing all afternoon. It's the best afternoon of the year.}}}\n> right: Write it all down\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You walk the shore alone and write down every word of the founders' wall before it fades. Nobody will ever read it. You need it to have happened somewhere.\n\n@event hs_aftermath_freed\ntitle: Every Candle Out\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: moon\ntags: hollowstar story\nscene: An academy at dawn with every window dark, students on the lawns staring at an empty sky, a black-sealed letter in one student's hand.\non_show:\n  if night_hurt >= 2:\n    health -medium\ntext: Every candle in Lumenhold went out at midnight and didn't come back until dawn. The lake is blue and empty and very deep. On your pillow there's a letter with a black-star seal: \"The families thank you. Your seat at our table is waiting.\" At breakfast the headmistress looks at you once, and then never again. {flag(ilse_taken): The room above the bakery is empty; Ilse went north before dawn.|}\n> left: Answer the letter\n  hint: They'd be glad of you.\n  alignment -small\n  gold +medium\n  say: You write back, and a purse comes by the next barge, and after that a letter every month in that beautiful hand. They call you \"herald\". You find, a little more each time, that you answer to it.\n> right: Burn it\n  hint: What's done stays done.\n  alignment +small\n  mood -small\n  say: The letter burns blue. It doesn't change anything, and you knew it wouldn't, and you burn the next one too, and the one after that. They keep coming.\n\n@event hs_aftermath_fled\ntitle: The Door Left Ajar\nclass: story\ntone: grim\ntrigger: queued\nactor: isolde\nart: door\ntags: hollowstar story\nscene: A tall woman with newly white hair standing at a window above a lake, a student in the doorway behind her, neither speaking.\non_show:\n  if night_hurt >= 2:\n    health -medium\n  else:\n    health -small\ntext: The headmistress {flag(night_isolde): stayed at the door when you ran|went down after you ran}, and came back up at dawn with her hair gone white. The door is still ajar, she says, but it will hold, for now, with the Wardens sitting up beside it in shifts. She doesn't ask why you ran. That's worse. {flag(ilse_taken): Ilse went north before dawn.|}\n> left: \"I'm sorry.\"\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +small\n  mood -medium\n  say: \"Everyone runs, the first time,\" she says. \"Most don't come back down. You may yet.\" She gives you a silver ring without a word, and a rota with your name on it, and you take both.\n> right: Say nothing\n  meet isolde\n  mood -small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You stand in her doorway until she turns back to the window. Every night after that, you dream of the gap between the door and the frame, and the dark looking out of it, and what you'd say to it if you went back.\n"},{"path":"content/events/hollowstar/threads.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  THE HOLLOW STAR, v2 (docs/plots/hollowstar-v2.md): how the plot is wired.\n#\n#  The arc's clock (content/world/arcs.grim) still runs on its schedule, but the beats are deadlines: an act's\n#  climax raises the clock to the next act's opener (`advance hollowstar to N`), so a player who pushes the\n#  investigation opens the next act early; one who doesn't is reached by the deadline anyway.\n#    Act I    omens          opener clock 1  hs_lake_breathes   climax hs_grey_stranger (beat 2)  -> 4\n#    Act II   investigation  opener clock 4  hs_ilse_arrives    climax hs_frost_ring (beat 5)     -> 7\n#    Act III  escalation     opener clock 7  hs_dark_tower      climax hs_grey_daylight (beat 8)  -> 10\n#    Act IV   convergence    opener clock 10 hs_last_winter     the Night at 12 (y4m9), or earlier by hs_go_down_early\n#  Each act's cards are a story thread (its steps wait on the act being open, not on a month); the Night is a\n#  scene, played in one sitting, and its outcome cards close the arc.\n#  Every fight in the plot sits beside at least one way that isn't a fight, and losing one never closes the\n#  story off (2026-10-26).\n# ============================================================\n\n@cond hs_act\ndesc: The Hollow Star's act n (1 omens … 4 convergence) has opened: the clock has reached its opener.\nparams: n\nis: clock(hollowstar) >= 3 * n - 2\n\n@thread hs_omens\ndesc: Act I: the lake breathes; the bell, the founders' ledger and the black-star dream, in any order.\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=hs_act(1)\narc: hollowstar\nmembers:\n  start hs_lake_breathes\n\n@thread hs_investigation\ndesc: Act II: Ilse arrives; Kael's secret, Ilse's, and the man in grey's offer.\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=hs_act(2)\narc: hollowstar\nmembers:\n  start hs_ilse_arrives\n\n@thread hs_escalation\ndesc: Act III: the cult strikes; sides are taken; the locks, the key and your shadow.\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=hs_act(3)\narc: hollowstar\nmembers:\n  start hs_dark_tower\n\n@thread hs_convergence\ndesc: Act IV: the last winter; who stands with you; going down early.\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=hs_act(4)\narc: hollowstar\nmembers:\n  start hs_last_winter\n\n@thread hs_night\ndesc: The Night the lake froze, in one sitting: who comes down with you, the cult on the ice, the man in grey, Ilse, your shadow, the founders' wall and the door.\nfrom: scene\narc: hollowstar\nmembers:\n  entry hs_the_night\n\n# ---------------- Progress: each act's finds raise the clock toward its climax ----------------\n\n@preset hs_omens_progress\ndesc: Two of Act I's three finds (the bell; the founders' seal, from the ledger or from Maud; the black-star dream) bring its climax (the clock to 2).\ndo:\n  if clue(lake_bell) + clue(knows_founders_seal) + has(hollow_dreams) >= 2:\n    advance hollowstar to 2\n\n@preset hs_investigation_progress\ndesc: Two of Act II's truths (Kael's family, what Ilse was raised for, the Codex's names, the man in grey's offer) bring its climax (the clock to 5).\ndo:\n  if clue(kael_family) + clue(ilse_truth) + clue(codex_names) + flag(grey_offer_seen) >= 2:\n    advance hollowstar to 5\n\n@preset hs_escalation_progress\ndesc: Two of Act III's (the seven locks, the star key, your second shadow, a side taken) bring its climax (the clock to 8).\ndo:\n  if clue(seven_locks) + clue(star_key) + clue(second_shadow) + (flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts) or flag(sided_self)) >= 2:\n    advance hollowstar to 8\n\n# ---------------- The Night: who is called, and who comes ----------------\n\n@cond hs_called\ndesc: The Night calls you down: you followed the plot far enough, or the Star counts you as its own, and you didn't walk away.\nis: (not flag(hollow_out) or flag(hollow_tempted) or flag(hollow_courted)) and (clues(hollowstar) >= 3 or flag(warden_trial) or flag(kael_ally) or flag(sided_wardens) or flag(sided_dumonts) or flag(sided_self) or flag(wardens_noticed) or flag(hollow_courted) or alignment <= -60)\n\n@cond night_kael\ndesc: Kael would come down with you: an ally, or he trained with you; if you go early, only if he promised.\nis: met(kael) and (flag(winter_kael) or (flag(kael_ally) and not flag(hs_early)))\n\n@cond night_ilse\ndesc: Ilse would come down with you: saved, still here, and (if you go early) promised.\nis: met(ilse) and flag(ilse_saved) and not flag(ilse_went_north) and (flag(winter_ilse) or (rel(ilse) >= 15 and not flag(hs_early)))\n\n@cond night_maud\ndesc: Maud would come down with you: she mended wards with you, or gave you Edmund's wand.\nis: met(maud) and (flag(winter_maud) or (has_item(edmunds_silver_wand) and not flag(hs_early)))\n\n@cond night_isolde\ndesc: The headmistress would come down with you, her Wardens behind.\nis: met(isolde) and (flag(winter_isolde) or ((flag(warden_trial) or flag(sided_wardens)) and not flag(hs_early)))\n"},{"path":"content/events/incidents.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Incidents and money sinks (design §8.2).\n#  A) forced sinks: setbacks that cost crowns or health, always\n#     with a way to soften them. Gated on gold so they drain\n#     savings rather than break poor players.\n#  B) voluntary sinks: patronage and donations for the well-off.\n#  C) year three: a term of Thornwick exchange students.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- A) Forced sinks ----------------\n\n@pool incidents_incident\nclass: incident\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 6\n\n@event inc_fever_bill\ntime: morning\ntitle: A Fever and a Bill\npool: incidents_incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 30 and seen(inc_fever_bill) < 2 and since(inc_fever_bill) >= 12\nart: potion\ntags: life money\nscene: A pale student in an infirmary bed, a stern nurse holding out a small blue bottle and a folded bill.\ntext: You wake in the infirmary with a fever that makes the ceiling breathe. Sister Amaranth holds up a small blue bottle. \"This clears it by morning. It is also not free,\" she says. \"Or you can sweat it out, like your ancestors.\"\n> left: Buy the tonic\n  gold -large\n  health -small\n  say: {~It tastes of tin and regret.|It tastes like a pond that has given up.} By morning the ceiling is a ceiling again, and your purse is noticeably lighter.\n> right: Sweat it out\n  health -medium\n  study -small\n  say: Three days of damp sheets and strange dreams about stairs. You miss a week of notes, and come back feeling like a wrung-out dishcloth.\n\n@pool incidents_incident_repeat1\nclass: incident\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 6\n\n@event inc_broken_wand\ntitle: Snapped on the Stairs\npool: incidents_incident_repeat1\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 30 and seen(inc_broken_wand) == 0\nart: wand\ntags: magic money\nscene: A student kneeling on a stone staircase, holding the two halves of a wand that still fizzes faintly at the break.\ntext: The moving stairs shift without warning. You catch yourself; your wand doesn't. It lies in two pieces on the landing, still fizzing at the break like a sulking firework.\n> left: Have it properly rebound\n  gold -large\n  say: The repairer in the village tuts, splints it with silver wire and charges you for every tut. It works better than before, which is somehow worse.\n> right: Bind it with string and hope\n  challenge spell apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: It holds. It spits a little when you cast, like a cat that forgives you but wants it noted.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      study -small\n      say: It holds for three days, then sneezes a spell backwards. Your eyebrows grow back within the week; your marks take longer.\n\n@event inc_library_fine\ntitle: The Overdue Notice\npool: incidents_incident\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 30 and seen(inc_library_fine) < 2 and since(inc_library_fine) >= 12\nart: paper\ntags: study money\nscene: A prefect in a red sash holding out a long paper notice, a book with teeth marks on the desk between them.\ntext: A prefect in a red sash slides a notice across your desk. The library book you borrowed has come back late, damp and, somehow, with teeth marks. \"Damages,\" she says. \"Or you can argue. People do. It never goes well.\"\n> left: Pay the fine\n  gold -medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She stamps your receipt with great satisfaction. The book is reshelved in a cage, which feels like the right outcome for everyone.\n> right: Argue that the book bit first\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+ unlikely:\n    win:\n      mood +tiny\n      say: You produce the bite on your thumb as evidence. The prefect inspects it, then the book, and waives the fine. \"Fair,\" she says. \"It has a history.\"\n    lose:\n      gold -large\n      study -tiny\n      say: The prefect writes down every word you say, then doubles the fine for \"contempt of librarian\". You pay it, and lose an evening's revision to the demerit hearing besides.\n\n@event inc_letter_home\ntitle: Bad Weather at Home\npool: incidents_incident_repeat1\ntone: grim\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 30 and seen(inc_letter_home) == 0\nart: letter\ntags: home money\nscene: A student at a window reading a rain-spotted letter, a small pile of coins on the sill beside them.\ntext: A letter from home, written in a hurry. A storm took half the roof, and the repairs will cost more than anyone has. Nobody asks you for anything. That's how you know it's bad.\n> left: Send crowns home\n  gold -large\n  alignment +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You send everything you can spare and a little you can't. The reply comes a month later: a drawing of a roof, finished, and a pressed flower.\n> right: Write back with love, not money\n  mood -medium\n  say: You write four pages and keep your purse. The reply is kind. It doesn't mention the roof. That night you lie awake and listen to the rain.\n\n@event inc_stolen_purse\ntitle: Market Day Fingers\npool: incidents_incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 30 and seen(inc_stolen_purse) < 2 and since(inc_stolen_purse) >= 12\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nscene: A crowded village square on market day, a figure in a grey hood slipping away between the stalls with a small purse.\ntext: Somewhere between the cheese stall and the man selling enchanted socks, your purse stops being yours. You catch a grey hood slipping between the stalls, already three stalls ahead.\n> left: Run after the hood\n  challenge feat apprentice+:\n    win:\n      gold -tiny\n      health -tiny\n      say: You corner the thief behind the bakery. The purse comes back a little lighter; they'd already spent some on a bun. You let them keep the bun.\n    lose:\n      gold -large\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: You trip over a goose, then a second goose. When you get up, the hood is gone, and so are your crowns.\n> right: Let it go\n  gold -large\n  mood -small\n  say: You tell yourself it's only money. You tell yourself that all the way back up the hill, and it never quite takes.\n\n@event inc_hiccuping_robe\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Robe That Hiccups\npool: incidents_incident_repeat1\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 20 and seen(inc_hiccuping_robe) == 0\nart: cauldron\ntags: magic money\nscene: A student in potion-stained robes jolting mid-step as their robe gives a visible hiccup, classmates hiding smiles.\ntext: A cauldron two benches down boils over, and a whole batch of Draught of Unending Hiccups soaks your best robe. The stain fades by supper. The hiccups don't. Every few minutes, the robe jumps.\n> left: Buy a new robe\n  gold -medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The tailor in the village fits you in an afternoon and charges you for a week. The old robe now hiccups quietly in the bottom of your trunk.\n> right: Wear it with dignity\n  renown -small\n  mood -small\n  say: {~It hiccups through a lecture on silence.|It hiccups through the headmistress's announcements.} Someone starts a tally on the common-room wall. You are briefly famous for the wrong reasons.\n\n# ---------------- B) Voluntary sinks: patronage ----------------\n\n@event patron_tournament_purse\ntitle: A Patron's Purse\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: (month == 7 or month == 8) and gold >= 80\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 12\nart: trophy\ntags: festival money\nscene: A festival committee table on a lawn, a thin purse on a velvet cushion and a list of patrons with plenty of space left.\ntext: The Spring Tournament committee has a velvet cushion, a very thin prize purse and a list of patrons with a lot of room left on it. Patrons are read out before the first bout, in a loud voice, to the whole academy.\n> left: Fund the champion's purse\n  gold -large\n  renown +medium\n  say: {~Your name is read out first, and mispronounced only slightly.|Your name rings across the lawn before the first bout. Two first-years bow to you, unsure why.} The champion thanks you from the podium.\n> right: Cheer from the stands instead\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Other names are read out, among them a professor's cat and \"a well-wisher\". You cheer for all of them, and loudest for the champion.\n\n@event patron_ledger_print\ntitle: Paper for the Press\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 6 and gold >= 80\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social money\nscene: A cramped office under a staircase, a groaning printing press, and a young editor holding out an empty paper tray.\ntext: Nessa Quill corners you by the library stairs, holding an empty paper tray like evidence. \"Special edition. Biggest story of the term. No paper. You,\" she says, pointing, \"look solvent. Patrons get their name on the masthead.\"\n> left: Pay for the whole print run\n  gold -large\n  rel nessa +medium\n  renown +medium\n  say: \"Headline: generous student saves free press,\" Nessa says, already writing it. The masthead lists you in tiny capitals, which she says is the most honour anyone gets.\n> right: Help her fold the edition instead\n  rel nessa +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You'd rather give her your time than your crowns. Nessa scrounges paper from the Ledger's scrap bin, and you fold until midnight, fingers grey with ink. Nessa credits you on the back page as \"the folding department\".\n\n# ---------------- B) Voluntary sinks: donations ----------------\n\n@event donate_greenhouse_glass\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Broken Panes\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 80 and not discovered(greenhouses)\nweight: 8\nrepeat: 3\nart: leaf\ntags: nature money\nscene: A collection tin on a garden gate, broken glass glittering in the grass behind it and steam escaping from a damaged greenhouse.\ntext: Last night's hail went through the greenhouse glass like a hammer through toffee. A collection tin hangs on the gate with a hand-lettered sign: every pane paid for keeps the mandrakes from catching cold.\n> left: Pay for a whole pane\n  gold -large\n  mood +small\n  discover greenhouses\n  say: A gardener with mud to the elbows insists on showing you your pane. Inside it is warm, green and loud with opinions. You are welcome, she says, any morning.\n> right: Walk on\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You walk past the tin. Behind the gate, something small and leafy sneezes. You feel, obscurely, that you've been judged.\n\n@event donate_duel_floor\ntitle: The Scorched Floor\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 3 and gold >= 80 and not discovered(duel_hall)\nweight: 8\nrepeat: 3\nart: sword\ntags: club money\nscene: A sweaty duellist with a collection box at the foot of a stair, scorched floorboards stacked behind them.\ntext: A duellist with a bandaged hand rattles a box at the foot of the east stair. \"The hall floor,\" he says. \"Someone set it on fire again. Every board you buy, we carve your initials on the underside. Honour, and splinters.\"\n> left: Buy a row of floorboards\n  gold -large\n  renown +small\n  discover duel_hall\n  say: He drags you upstairs to see. The hall smells of smoke and wax, and the practice dummies glare at you. Your initials go on the underside of the board by the door.\n> right: Wish him luck with the bandage\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He thanks you with exaggerated courtesy and goes back to rattling. Somewhere above, steel rings on steel, and nobody invites you up.\n\n# ---------------- C) Year three: the Thornwick exchange ----------------\n\n@event xchg_arrival\ntitle: Guests in Green\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: year == 3 and month <= 2 and seen(xchg_arrival) == 0\nweight: 12\nart: mask\ntags: social rival\nscene: Four students in green Thornwick robes standing in a grand entrance hall, the portraits on the walls all turned to stare at them.\ntext: Four students in Thornwick green arrive for a term, with trunks and very polite smiles. Within an hour every portrait in the entrance hall is winking at the wrong people. A prefect looks around for someone, anyone, to show them the castle.\n> left: Offer to show them round\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      charm +small\n      say: You give the full tour, moving stairs included. By the end the boy with the sketchbook admits Lumenhold's towers are \"not as crooked as reported\".\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: Halfway round, they convince you a wall is a door. It isn't. They are very sorry, and laughing too hard to say so.\n> right: Watch them from a distance\n  guile +small\n  say: You watch them all week. They watch back. You learn that the quiet one counts doorways, and that they know you're watching.\n\n@event xchg_ink_cuffs\ntitle: The Ink-Stained Cuffs\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: year == 3 and month <= 4 and seen(xchg_arrival) > 0 and seen(xchg_farewell) == 0\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: study rival\nscene: A library at dusk, a student in green robes with ink-stained cuffs copying from a book chained to a desk.\ntext: Late in the library, you find the Thornwick girl with the ink-stained cuffs copying from a chained book she shouldn't have reached. She sees you see her. \"It's only history,\" she says. \"Unless you want it to be a scandal.\"\n> left: Report her to the librarian\n  renown +small\n  mood -small\n  say: The librarian thanks you, coldly. The Thornwick girl is confined to the guest wing for a week. She nods to you at dinner afterwards, as if you had both played well.\n> right: Keep quiet, for a favour\n  alignment -tiny\n  guile +small\n  say: She tears out the page she copied and hands you the other half of her notes instead. \"We're even,\" she says. You're fairly sure you aren't.\n\n@event xchg_farewell\ntitle: A Thornwick Farewell\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nwhen: year == 3 and month <= 4 and seen(xchg_arrival) > 0 and since(xchg_arrival) >= 2 and seen(xchg_farewell) == 0\nweight: 12\nart: group\ntags: social rival\nscene: Students in green and blue robes on a jetty at dawn, a barge waiting, one of them holding out a striped scarf.\ntext: The Thornwick students leave on today's barge. The boy with the sketchbook finds you on the jetty and holds out his scarf. \"It's a custom,\" he says. \"Probably. I've just decided.\" Behind him his friends pretend not to listen.\n> left: Swap scarves\n  mood +medium\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You trade scarves on the jetty. Someone from your own house mutters \"traitor\" at breakfast. You wear it anyway, and get a letter from across the hills by the end of the month.\n> right: Challenge him to one last duel\n  challenge combat adept- wild:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: You win, narrowly, on the wet boards. He laughs, bows, and sketches you mid-spell on the barge as it pulls away. The sketch arrives by post, flattering.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood +small\n      say: He wins with an illusion of a second you. You both end up in the lake. It is, somehow, the best goodbye you've had.\n"},{"path":"content/events/intro.grim","text":"# The intro sequence (world expansion 1, plan section 0). Every card is `class: story`, tagged `intro`: left is always\n# the quick way (a line, nothing else); right engages, with at most a line, a fact or a tiny warm-up. Never a cost.\n#   The orientation (feedback 2026-10-20): one condensed run of cards at the very start, before the registrar's list and\n#   enrolment. The Welcome asks first: left skips it, right plays along, and then each card leads straight to the next\n#   whichever side you take: Marrow's question (what is just? asked again on the eve of graduation, isolde_asks_again),\n#   the tour (where you meet Bram; following him shows you the places that matter, intro_places), the wrong tower (a\n#   negotiation lesson), lunch, Vey's tea (a check lesson), your room, the safety talk (slice_ward_lesson, so\n#   you know Ward before the practice fight), the practice circle (a fight lesson, coached exchange by exchange) and your\n#   kit. Skipping never skips Ward: the safety talk then comes in its usual place, after enrolment.\n#   The lessons' checks are fair (even odds, pitched at a first-year's skill: feedback 2026-10-20), and their only\n#   consequence is a tiny change of Mood.\n#   Just-in-time cards: once each, the first time a system matters; off with the title screen's \"Skip the introductions\"\n#   or the Settings row (flag skip_intros).\n#   The kit card (intro_kit) is Thorne's, not Mir's (the plan said Mir): it would pre-empt Mir's own first meeting.\n\n# ---------------- The orientation ----------------\n\n@event intro_welcome\ntitle: The Welcome\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == 1\npriority: 90\nslot: morning\nart: crown\ntags: intro story\nscene: The Great Hall full of new first-years under floating candles, a tall headmistress at the lectern.\ntext: Floating candles, two hundred first-years trying not to look up. Headmistress Marrow speaks for four minutes: four years of twelve moons, a name that travels, and a castle that remembers who you were in it. \"Before you choose your courses,\" she ends, \"the prefects will show you how things work here. Those who think they know already may go straight to the registrar.\"\n> left: Skip the orientation\n  say: You slip out ahead of the crowd to find the registrar's list. Whatever the castle has to teach you, you'll learn it the hard way. (The safety talk is compulsory: you'll still have that.)\n> right: Stay for the orientation\n  queue intro_what_is_just now\n  say: You stay. The hall empties toward the prefects, and the headmistress comes down from the lectern, straight toward you.\n\n# Marrow's question (feedback 2026-10-20): what you answer is remembered, and nothing else. On the eve of graduation she\n# asks again (isolde_asks_again, calendar.grim) and says whether your four years kept to it.\n@event intro_what_is_just\ntitle: What Is Just?\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\nart: scales\ntags: intro story\nscene: A tall headmistress with a small notebook stopping in front of one first-year in an emptying hall, candles guttering overhead.\ntext: Headmistress Marrow stops in front of you, notebook open, as if she'd been looking for you in particular. \"One question before the prefects have you,\" she says. \"I ask it of everyone. Very few remember. What do you think is just?\"\n> mercy: \"Mercy.\"\n  hint: Giving people more than they've earned.\n  flag just_mercy\n  learn isolde.mercy\n  queue intro_tour now\n  say: \"Mercy,\" she repeats, and writes it down. \"Expensive. Most people who say it haven't been asked to pay for it yet.\" She closes the notebook on her finger. \"Ask me again in four years.\"\n> fair: \"Fairness.\"\n  hint: One rule, the same for everyone.\n  flag just_fair\n  learn isolde.mercy\n  queue intro_tour now\n  say: \"Fairness,\" she repeats, and writes it down. \"The same rule for the duke and the pickpocket. Harder than it sounds, when you know one of them.\" She closes the notebook on her finger. \"Ask me again in four years.\"\n> strong: \"Keeping your own safe.\"\n  hint: Whatever it takes, for the people who are yours.\n  flag just_strong\n  learn isolde.mercy\n  queue intro_tour now\n  say: \"Keeping your own safe,\" she repeats, and writes it down. \"An honest answer. Most people dress it up.\" She closes the notebook on her finger. \"Ask me again in four years. I'd like to know who you decided was yours.\"\n\n@event intro_tour\ntitle: The Prefect's Tour\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: map\ntags: intro story\nactor: bram\non_show:\n  meet bram\nscene: A prefect in a red sash leading a straggle of first-years through a castle corridor, holding up a ledger like a flag.\ntext: A burly prefect in a red sash gathers the first-years with a ledger. \"Bram Hollis. Prefect. Tour. Each day you get a few free hours, and you spend them on the map: one place an hour. Some doors open only once you've earned them. Some never open for you. Keep up.\"\n> left: Skip the tour\n  queue intro_wrong_tower now\n  say: You slip away and find your own way round. You get lost twice and find the kitchens once, which is a fair trade.\n> right: Follow him round\n  queue intro_places now\n  say: Bram walks you past every door and names its rules. At a blank stretch of wall, a boy with a lockpick behind his ear winks at you, as if the wall were a joke you'd both get later.\n\n# The tour's second half (feedback 2026-10-20): the places with the most uses, named once, by the prefect who'd rather\n# not. Every one is open from the first day.\n@event intro_places\ntitle: Where Things Are\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nactor: bram\nart: map\ntags: intro story\nscene: A prefect reading from a ledger at the top of a castle stair, pointing down corridors with his quill, first-years craning to follow.\ntext: Bram reads the castle to you off his ledger. \"Job Board, by the stairs: work and wages. Library: revision tables before every exam. Infirmary, on the Alchemy floor: Sister Amaranth, for when you break something, yourself included. The village, down the hill: robes and wands on market day, tea at Mabel's, tonics at Pell's.\" He snaps the ledger shut. \"Questions? No. Good.\"\n> left: \"No questions.\"\n  queue intro_wrong_tower now\n  say: \"Excellent,\" says Bram, and means it. He leaves you at the foot of the stairs with a map in your head and a strong sense of having been processed.\n> right: Ask what he left out\n  queue intro_wrong_tower now\n  say: He looks pained. \"The Gilded Newt, in the village, after dark: supper, cider and people you shouldn't be seen with. The Lakeshore, for thinking. That's all.\" He writes something in the ledger. It might be your name.\n\n@event intro_wrong_tower\ntitle: The Wrong Tower\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: scales\ntags: intro story\nscene: A harassed house steward behind a desk heaped with lists, a student's trunk visible through a doorway in the wrong tower.\ntext: Your trunk has gone to the wrong tower. The house steward has a list, and the list says it hasn't. \"If it's on the list,\" she says, \"it's where it should be.\" Behind her, through a door, you can see your trunk with your name on it.\n> left: Carry it over yourself\n  queue intro_lunch now\n  queue intro_lunch_alone now\n  say: You haul it across two bridges and up ninety stairs. The steward ticks something on the list as you pass, satisfied that it was always right.\n> right: Talk her round\n  queue intro_lunch now\n  queue intro_lunch_alone now\n  challenge negotiate novice+:\n    win:\n      mood +tiny\n      say: You ask to see the list. Then the line. Then the tower on the line. The steward goes pink, corrects it in red ink and sends two porters. \"Clerical,\" she says.\n    partial:\n      say: She won't change the list, but she lends you a porter \"as a courtesy\". The porter and the list never speak again.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: She has an answer for everything, most of it the list. You carry the trunk yourself in the end, but you've learned how she argues, which is worth a tower.\n\n@event intro_lunch\ntitle: The Lunch Table\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nart: meal\ntags: intro story\ncast:\n  friend: any where met(friend) and tagged(friend, peer) and not npc_is(bram)\nscene: Long refectory tables at noon, first-years spread along the benches, one empty place beside a familiar face.\ntext: Lunch in the Great Hall, under the gallery where the refectory tables run. You can sit anywhere. At noon people find you, or you find them; either way, who you sit with tends to become who you know.\n> left: Eat with whoever's there\n  queue intro_vey_tea now\n  say: You squeeze in between two strangers who are arguing about soup. By pudding they're not strangers, quite.\n> right: Sit with {friend}\n  queue intro_vey_tea now\n  rel friend +tiny\n  say: {friend} shifts along the bench to make room. You talk about nothing much, which is how most friendships start.\n\n# Lunch for a player who has met no fellow student yet (usual at this point): the card above needs someone to sit with,\n# and every peer has a first meeting of their own that lunch mustn't take. Both are queued; exactly one plays. Bram, met\n# on the tour, is a prefect, not a lunch companion: he counts for neither.\n@event intro_lunch_alone\ntitle: The Lunch Table\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: not met(lyra) and not met(tobias) and not met(seraphine) and not met(corvin) and not met(mir) and not met(kael) and not met(nessa) and not met(ilse)\nart: meal\ntags: intro story\nscene: Long refectory tables at noon, first-years spread along the benches, a student with a tray looking for a place.\ntext: Lunch in the Great Hall, under the gallery where the refectory tables run. You can sit anywhere, and you don't know anyone yet. At noon people find you, or you find them; either way, who you sit with tends to become who you know.\n> left: Eat with whoever's there\n  queue intro_vey_tea now\n  say: You squeeze in between two strangers who are arguing about soup. By pudding they're not strangers, quite.\n> right: Sit at the end and listen\n  queue intro_vey_tea now\n  say: You take the end of a bench and listen. By the end of the meal you know who runs the Ledger, who duels, who talks to plants, and that the girl with the floating quill will find you whether you like it or not.\n\n@event intro_vey_tea\ntitle: Tea That Isn't There\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: cup\ntags: intro story\nscene: A corridor alcove, a sharp-eyed professor holding out an empty hand as if it held a teacup, a student hesitating.\ntext: Professor Vey stops you in a corridor and offers you a cup of tea. There is no cup. There is no tea. She waits to see what you'll do. \"Every attempt has its odds,\" she says pleasantly. \"Skill moves them. Luck moves them less. Drink up.\"\n> left: Decline the tea\n  queue intro_room now\n  say: \"No, thank you,\" you say. Vey sighs, drinks the tea that isn't there, and walks on. You feel as though you've failed something small.\n> right: Pretend to drink it\n  queue intro_room now\n  challenge deceive novice-:\n    win:\n      learn vey.tests\n      mood +tiny\n      say: You sip, wince at the heat and compliment the blend. Vey laughs, delighted, and writes something down. \"Points,\" she says. \"Nobody compliments the blend.\"\n    partial:\n      learn vey.tests\n      say: You drink it convincingly and then try to hand back the cup. There is no cup. Vey smiles. \"Half points,\" she says.\n    lose:\n      learn vey.tests\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You laugh halfway through the sip. Vey sighs, theatrically. \"Most do,\" she says. \"I keep score, you know. Everyone starts at nought.\"\n\n@event intro_room\ntitle: Your Room\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nart: bed\ntags: intro story\nscene: A narrow tower room with a bed, a trunk at its foot and a window full of lake, a folded note on the pillow.\ntext: Your room in the tower: a bed, a trunk, a window with half the lake in it. On the pillow, a note from the bursar, folded twice.\n> left: Unpack later\n  queue slice_ward_lesson now\n  queue intro_practice_circle now\n  say: You fall onto the bed in your robes and sleep like a dropped stone. The note is still under your cheek in the morning.\n> right: Read the bursar's note\n  queue slice_ward_lesson now\n  queue intro_practice_circle now\n  say: Board is paid each month, it says, or you go hungry. Sleep mends the body and the mood; a bad night shows. Mana comes back on its own. And please do not feed the gargoyles.\n\n@event intro_practice_circle\ntitle: The Practice Circle\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nart: duel\ntags: intro story\nscene: A chalk circle on a scorched duelling-hall floor, a straw mannequin standing in it, a grizzled professor watching from the edge.\ntext: Professor Thorne has chalked a circle and stood a straw mannequin in it. \"Every first-year fights it once,\" he says. \"It moves first, and it shows you what it means to do. Watch it. Lose to it if you must. Losing well is the only thing worth teaching.\"\n> left: Watch from the bench\n  queue intro_kit now\n  say: You watch three first-years go down to the mannequin, then one beat it. Thorne says the same thing to all four: \"Good. Again.\"\n> right: Step into the circle\n  queue intro_kit now\n  challenge combat novice certain:\n    win:\n      mood +tiny\n      say: The mannequin topples in a puff of straw. Thorne nods. \"Now lose one sometime,\" he says. \"Then you'll know what you're doing.\"\n    partial:\n      say: You trade blows until the bell. Thorne looks pleased. \"A draw is a fight you both learned from,\" he says.\n    lose:\n      learn thorne.lost\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The mannequin wins, which is humiliating and educational. Thorne helps you up. \"I lost to a storm once,\" he says. \"Badly. Losing well is the whole art.\"\n\n@event intro_kit\ntitle: What You Carry\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nart: wand\ntags: intro story\nscene: A grizzled professor tapping a student's battered borrowed wand, a robe and a small pouch laid out on a bench.\ntext: Afterwards Thorne taps your borrowed wand with one finger. \"Held together with hope,\" he says. \"What you carry matters in a fight, and only there: a wand, a robe, one thing in your pocket. What you own opens other doors. Keep your kit in order.\"\n> left: Later\n  say: You nod and put the wand away. It sparks once, sideways, as if it had heard.\n> right: Show me\n  say: He shows you: what you wear only counts when you fight; what you own lets you do things on the right day, like a spell does. Your kit is on its own tab, beside the others. \"And trade that wand in,\" he says, \"when you can.\"\n\n# ---------------- Just in time (off with skip_intros) ----------------\n\n@pool intro_auto_story\ntrigger: auto\nclass: story\npriority: 30\ntags: intro\n\n@event jit_after_curfew\ntitle: After Curfew\npool: intro_auto_story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn == 3 and not flag(skip_intros)\nslot: night\nart: lantern\nscene: A dark castle corridor after curfew, a prefect's lantern far off at one end, a student pressed into a doorway.\ntext: After curfew the castle changes hands. The corridors belong to prefects and to whatever else walks them. You can stay in and sleep, or slip out at midnight to wherever is open then. Get caught often enough and people start to notice.\n> left: Stay in tonight\n  say: You listen to the castle settle and decide midnight can wait.\n> right: Watch the prefect's lantern go by\n  say: The lantern passes your door, pauses and moves on. You learn the round by heart. It feels like knowing a secret.\n\n@event jit_red_string\ntitle: Red String\npool: intro_auto_story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clues(hollowstar) >= 1 and not flag(skip_intros)\nart: paper\nscene: A student's desk by candlelight, scraps of paper pinned to the wall above it, joined by lengths of red string.\ntext: You've started keeping what you notice: a scrap of paper here, a remembered word there. Tonight you pin them to the wall above your desk and join them with red string. It doesn't make sense yet. It isn't supposed to.\n> left: Go to bed\n  say: You leave the string where it is. In the morning it looks like a constellation you don't know the name of.\n> right: Stare at it a while\n  say: The things you've found are all there in your notes now, and the leads you haven't followed. Nobody will make you follow them. That's the frightening part.\n\n@event jit_exam_nerves\ntitle: Exam Nerves\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 4 and term and year == 1 and not flag(skip_intros)\npriority: 95\nslot: morning\nart: hourglass\ntags: intro\nscene: A queue of pale first-years outside a closed examination hall door, one of them reciting under their breath.\ntext: Exams. Outside the hall someone is reciting the stirring count under their breath. Every class you went to and every night you revised is in your head somewhere, and how much of it is there is most of what decides the paper.\n> left: Walk straight in\n  say: You walk in before the nerves can catch you. The door shuts like a held breath.\n> right: Go over your notes once more\n  say: Preparation counts more than talent here, a fourth-year once told you. You hope she was right. She usually is.\n\n@event jit_long_break\ntitle: The Long Break\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: break and year == 1 and not flag(skip_intros)\npriority: 90\nslot: morning\nart: house\ntags: intro\nscene: An emptying castle courtyard, trunks being loaded onto a barge, a few students waving and a few staying behind.\ntext: The term is over and the castle empties. {off_campus: You watch it go from the barge rail, your trunk at your feet.|} A break is your own: no lectures, and four free visits a day if you stay at the castle or go home, three if you take on paid work. Some friends leave; some stay. Letters come instead.\n> left: Make plans later\n  say: {off_campus: You put your feet up for an afternoon first. Home is very quiet after the castle, and nobody rings a bell at you.|You put your feet up for an afternoon first. The castle is very quiet, and very much yours.}\n> right: {off_campus: Walk round home|Walk the empty halls}\n  say: {off_campus: Everything is smaller than you remember. Your old room is full of apples now, and someone has kept your bed made anyway.|Your footsteps echo. The kitchens are open all day on a break, the cook says, and the cook is lonely.}\n\n@event jit_counter\ntitle: Across the Counter\nplace: village\nclass: story\ntone: light\nwhen: not flag(skip_intros)\nweight: 40\nart: coin\ntags: intro shop\nscene: A village shop counter seen from a student's side, a price slate, a few goods and a shopkeeper waiting patiently.\ntext: The village sells what the castle doesn't. Every shop has its counter, with its prices on the slate; some things are only offered once you've proved something, or know someone, or can pay a great deal. And the pawnbroker buys back what you're tired of.\n> left: Browse another day\n  say: You walk the square with your hands in your pockets. Everything in the windows looks expensive and possible.\n> right: Read every slate\n  say: Buns, robes, wands, tea, tonics, one at a time. You make a list of what you'd buy if you had money. It's a long list.\n\n@event jit_real_fight\ntitle: A Real Fight\npool: intro_auto_story\ntone: tense\nwhen: turn >= 6 and seen(intro_practice_circle) > 0 and not flag(skip_intros)\nslot: evening\nart: sword\nscene: A duelling hall with a real duel under way, two students circling, a third nursing a split lip on the bench.\ntext: The mannequin was a lesson. Real fights aren't. Whoever you face fights to win, the way you would; Health lost below the line stays lost, and anything you'd rather not be seen casting, someone may see. You can always refuse a fight, at a price of its own.\n> left: Nod and move on\n  say: You file it away. Somewhere in the hall, someone is losing well. Thorne would be pleased.\n> right: Watch a bout to the end\n  say: You watch two third-years fight to a standstill, bow and go to supper together. It looked like the opposite of a lesson. It was the same thing.\n\n@event jit_real_negotiation\ntitle: A Real Negotiation\npool: intro_auto_story\ntone: light\nwhen: turn == 3 and seen(intro_wrong_tower) > 0 and not flag(skip_intros)\nslot: afternoon\nart: scales\nscene: A student and an official facing each other across a desk heaped with papers, both leaning forward.\ntext: Not every talk is a quick word. Some are negotiations: someone across the table with reasons of their own, and something at stake. Charm, lore and guile all count, and so does how you come across today. The steward and her list were practice.\n> left: Fair enough\n  say: You resolve to argue only with people who aren't holding a ledger.\n> right: Remember the steward and her list\n  say: However it went with the steward, the lesson was the same: the list was her whole argument. You make a note: always ask to see the line.\n\n@event jit_infirmary\ntitle: A Word From the Infirmary\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: health <= 30 and not flag(skip_intros)\npriority: 70\nart: potion\ntags: intro\nscene: A stern healer in a white apron writing on a chart at the foot of a student's bed.\ntext: Sister Amaranth catches you on the stairs, looks at you once and steers you to a bed. \"Low Health,\" she says. \"Under thirty, the body starts to lose things it doesn't get back easily. Sleep. Eat. Stop fighting things.\" She doesn't believe you'll listen.\n> left: Promise to rest\n  say: You promise. She writes it on your chart, which feels binding in a way promises usually don't.\n> right: Ask how low is too low\n  say: \"Lower than you are,\" she says, \"is too low.\" She gives you a tonic that tastes of iron and disapproval.\n\n@event jit_something_more\ntitle: Something More\npool: intro_auto_story\ntone: warm\nwhen: not flag(skip_intros)\nart: heart\ntags: romance\ncast:\n  x: any where romanceable(x) and spark(x) >= 9\nscene: Two students in a crowded corridor, one of them looking back over their shoulder a moment longer than they meant to.\ntext: You catch yourself looking for {x} in every room. That's new. Friendship grows on its own; this is something else, and it grows only when you let it. It can also go cold if you leave it too long.\n> left: Think about something else\n  say: You think about something else, with great determination, for almost an hour.\n> right: Admit it, to yourself at least\n  say: You admit it to your pillow, which keeps it. Tomorrow you'll be braver. Possibly.\n\n@event jit_rivals\ntitle: Rivals\npool: intro_auto_story\ntone: tense\nwhen: bonds(rival) >= 1 and not flag(skip_intros)\nart: duel\nscene: Two students passing on a staircase, neither giving way, both pretending not to notice the other.\ntext: Somebody here has decided you're the one to beat. A rival watches what you do and does it better, or tries to, and you'll find yourself doing the same. Rivals can soften into something else. They can also sharpen.\n> left: Ignore it\n  say: You resolve to ignore it. You fail almost at once, which is how rivalry works.\n> right: Get better\n  say: You stay late in the library out of pure spite. It is extraordinarily motivating.\n\n@event jit_mirror\ntitle: The Mirror Remembers\npool: intro_auto_story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: (alignment >= 15 or alignment <= -15) and not flag(skip_intros)\nart: mirror\nscene: A student passing the tall sorting mirror in the entrance hall, their reflection a half-step behind them.\ntext: You pass the Sorting Mirror on the way to breakfast and your reflection is a half-step slow. {alignment >= 15: It looks brighter than you feel.|It looks colder than you feel.} What you do when it costs you something is being remembered, somewhere, and it leans you one way or the other.\n> left: Walk faster\n  say: Your reflection catches up by the end of the hall. You decide not to wonder how.\n> right: Look back at it\n  say: It looks back. Then it straightens its robes, which you hadn't, and you straighten yours.\n\n@event jit_noticed\ntitle: Noticed\npool: intro_auto_story\ntone: light\nwhen: renown >= 30 and not flag(skip_intros)\nart: crown\nscene: A student walking into a crowded common room as a few heads turn, someone whispering behind a hand.\ntext: Heads turn when you come into the common room. A first-year whispers your name to another. Being known opens doors and invitations; it also fades if you stop giving people something to talk about.\n> left: Pretend not to notice\n  say: You pretend not to notice. Everyone notices you pretending.\n> right: Wave\n  say: You wave. Three people wave back, and one of them has no idea who you are.\n\n@event jit_first_shift\ntitle: First Shift\npool: intro_auto_story\ntone: light\nwhen: member(job) and not flag(skip_intros)\nart: coin\ntags: job\nscene: A student tying on an apron in a back room, a list of duties pinned on the wall beside them.\ntext: A job is a few shifts a term, shown on the map when they're due; each one pays, and a missed one costs the boss's good opinion. Work well and you're promoted. Turn up on a break and it's just a visit.\n> left: Tie the apron and go\n  say: You tie the apron backwards. Nobody tells you for an hour.\n> right: Read the duty list twice\n  say: You read it twice. The second time, you notice the line at the bottom: \"Ask if unsure.\" You decide to be sure instead.\n\n@event jit_applications\ntitle: Applications\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: month == 7 and year == 2 and not flag(skip_intros)\npriority: 52\nart: letter\ntags: intro\nscene: A noticeboard plastered with internship notices, a student copying deadlines into a notebook.\ntext: The internship notices are up. A summer's placement means a summer away doing the work, and a letter from a supervisor that follows you after. Each wants a course passed, or more, and asks once a year.\n> left: Look later\n  say: You walk past the board. The notices will still be there tomorrow, mostly.\n> right: Copy the deadlines down\n  say: You copy every deadline into your notebook in your best hand, and underline the ones you could actually meet.\n"},{"path":"content/events/jobs.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Job events — drawn while a job state is active.\n#  Jobs are taken at the Job Board (calendar.grim).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Library Assistant ----------------\n\n@event lib_biting_book\ntitle: The Book That Bites\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: library_assistant\nweight: 14\nart: book\ntags: job\nscene: A thick leather book with tiny teeth along its pages snapping at a hand reaching for it on a high shelf.\ntext: Reshelving in the zoology section, a fat leather book clamps onto your finger and refuses to let go. The head librarian, without looking up, says: \"Stroke the spine.\"\n> left: Muzzle it with a belt\n  wage\n  health -tiny\n  vigor +small\n  say: You wrestle it into submission and shelve it. It growls at you every time you pass for the rest of the term.\n> right: Stroke the spine and read it\n  wage\n  lore +medium\n  health -tiny\n  perform track -tiny\n  say: It purrs, opens and turns out to be a fascinating treatise on carnivorous plants. It only bites twice more. The reshelving waits until tomorrow.\n\n@event lib_overdue_professor\ntitle: Overdue\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: met(thorne)\ntrack: library_assistant\nweight: 12\nart: hourglass\ntags: job\nscene: A severe professor towering over a library desk, a stack of books under one arm, a very small student assistant on the other side holding a fines ledger.\ntext: Professor Thorne returns a book that is eleven years overdue. The fine, by library rules, is forty crowns. He looks at you. You look at the ledger.\n> left: Waive the fine\n  wage\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  say: \"Hm,\" says Thorne. You can't tell if you've earned his respect or lost it.\n> right: Enforce the rules\n  wage\n  rel thorne +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: A long silence. Then he pays, in exact change. \"Good,\" he says, and you could swear he almost smiles.\n> up[index]: Recall the amnesty clause\n  wage\n  rel thorne +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: The ledger falls open at a rule from three hundred years ago: books returned by a professor on a wet Tuesday are forgiven. It is a wet Tuesday. Thorne reads the page twice and says, \"Hm,\" which in this case means you'll go far.\n\n@event lib_restricted_cart\ntitle: The Restricted Cart\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: library_assistant\nweight: 12\nart: lock\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A wheeled library cart chained shut, piled with black-bound books, parked alone in a candlelit aisle.\ntext: You are asked to wheel the Restricted Section returns to the vault. The cart's chain has come loose. One of the books has fallen open on a page that is writing itself.\n> left: Close it without looking\n  wage\n  perform track +tiny\n  say: The book snaps shut with a sound like a disappointed sigh. The vault door clangs. The head librarian notes the time, approvingly.\n> right: Read a few lines\n  wage\n  lore +medium\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain umbral_curse\n    say: The words crawl off the page and up your sleeve. That night, you can't get warm.\n  else:\n    say: A spell for walking through mirrors. You will never try it. Probably.\n> up[seal]: Seal the book shut\n  wage\n  perform track +small\n  say: You seal the book shut before you've read a line. It stops writing. The vault keeper takes it from you, tries the clasp, and gives you a look you can't read. \"Good,\" he says, eventually.\n\n# ---------------- The Gilded Newt ----------------\n\n@event tavern_brawl\ntitle: Brawl at the Newt\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: tavern\nweight: 14\nart: cup\ntags: job danger\nscene: A low-beamed tavern mid-brawl: a chair in mid-air, a dwarf on a table, cider sloshing everywhere.\ntext: A dwarf and a troll disagree about the price of turnips, and the Gilded Newt erupts. A chair flies past your head. The owner is hiding behind the bar, gesturing at you to do something.\n> left: Duck behind the bar too\n  wage\n  say: You crouch beside the owner and share a bag of crisps while the room destroys itself.\n> right: Break it up\n  wage\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You slam a tray on the bar and bellow. Silence. The troll apologises. The dwarf tips you fifteen crowns.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You step in. Someone's elbow steps into your face.\n> up[rapport]: Tap the bar twice\n  wage\n  renown +small\n  say: You tap the bar twice and say the one thing about turnips that makes a dwarf and a troll both laugh. The chair comes down. The owner comes out from behind the bar and pours you a cider, staring.\n\n@event tavern_star_coin\ntitle: A Generous Stranger\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: tavern\nweight: 12\nart: coin\ntags: job mystery hollowstar\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: A hooded figure at a corner table sliding a black coin across the wood, its surface stamped with a dark star.\ntext: A hooded stranger nurses a cider all evening and leaves a tip: a black coin stamped with a hollow star. \"For the student,\" they say. \"Tell Kael Dumont his family sends regards.\"\n> left: Refuse the coin\n  wage\n  say: The stranger shrugs and pockets it. When you look again, the corner table is empty and the cider is frozen solid.\n> right: Take it\n  wage\n  gold +tiny\n  flag star_coin\n  alignment -small\n  say: The coin is cold as ice and heavier than it should be. You find yourself turning it over in your pocket for days.\n\n@event tavern_extra_shifts\ntitle: More Shifts\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: tavern\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A ruddy tavern owner leaning on the bar, wiping a mug, a rota pinned behind him covered in crossed-out names.\ntext: The owner is short-handed. \"Double shifts for a month,\" he says. \"Double pay. I know you've got studies, but you've also got rent.\"\n> left: Decline\n  wage\n  study +tiny\n  say: \"Fair enough,\" he says, and asks again next week.\n> right: Take the extra shifts\n  wage overtime\n  health -medium\n  study -small\n  say: A month of late nights and early lectures. Your purse is heavy. So are your eyelids.\n\n# Hazards of the job (L-1): minor incidents that show why the Newt's pay is higher.\n\n@event tavern_broken_glass\ntitle: Broken Glass\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrack: tavern\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: cup\ntags: job danger\nscene: A tavern floor glittering with broken tankards, a server crouching with a cloth, a dog padding closer.\ntext: {A tray of tankards meets a regular's elbow, and the floor is suddenly all glass.|Another tray, another floor of glass. The Newt goes through tankards the way other places go through napkins.|Glass on the floor again, and the same regular's elbow to blame.} {The regular is very sorry, loudly.|Everyone lifts their feet.|The dog is already heading for it.}\n> left: Clear it by hand, fast\n  wage\n  health -small\n  perform track +tiny\n  say: {You have it cleared before the dog gets there, with a cut across your palm to show for it. The landlady binds it with a bar towel.|Fast and thorough, apart from the sliver you find in your thumb at midnight.|You beat the dog to it by a whisker and a nicked finger. The landlady gives the dog a sausage for its trouble.}\n> right: Fetch the broom and do it properly\n  wage\n  perform track -tiny\n  say: {By the time you're back with the broom, three customers have left with their cider unfinished. The landlady counts them.|You sweep slowly and safely while the bar goes dry. The landlady taps the clock.|You go for the broom, and the landlady has to hold the dog back by the collar while the customers' cider goes warm.}\n\n@event tavern_after_closing\ntitle: After Closing\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrack: tavern\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: lantern\ntags: job danger\nscene: A lantern-lit shore path at night, a tired server walking alone beside black water, the castle's towers far off.\ntext: {Your shift tonight runs until the Newt closes, long after the last barge.|Another late shift. By closing time the lake will be dark.|Late shift again. You've stopped expecting to see the last barge.} {season == \"winter\": These nights the shore path is ice under snow.|{Fog comes off the water most nights now.|The shore path has no lamps.|Somebody always sings, badly, on the way home.}} It's a long walk round the lake to the castle.\n> left: Walk the shore path\n  wage\n  health -small\n  say: {Two hours round the lake, and every noise in the reeds is something. You reach the gates with wet boots and a turned ankle.|You walk home in the dark and slip twice. You fall into bed as the first bell rings.|You walk home with the other late workers, singing badly to keep the dark off. You're hoarse by the gates, and late to bed.}\n> right: Sleep by the Newt's fire\n  wage\n  study -tiny\n  say: {The landlady lends you a blanket and the settle by the fire. You wake at dawn smelling of woodsmoke and miss the first lecture.|You sleep on the settle under somebody's coat and catch the morning barge, too late for your first class.|You hear the others singing their way home along the shore and choose the fire. The settle is lumpy, and you miss the first lecture anyway.}\n\n# ---------------- Apothecary Clerk ----------------\n\n@event apoth_wrong_label\ntitle: A Mislabelled Jar\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nweight: 14\nart: bottle\ntags: job\nscene: Shelves of glass jars in a narrow shop, one jar labelled SLEEPING DRAUGHT glowing an ominous green.\ntext: The jar labelled \"Sleeping Draught\" is green. Sleeping Draught is blue. You're fairly sure the green stuff is Draught of Unending Hiccups. Old Pell is very proud of his labelling.\n> left: Quietly fix the label\n  wage\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: Nobody ever knows. Somewhere, a customer sleeps soundly instead of hiccupping for a week.\n> right: Point it out to Pell\n  wage\n  gold +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: Pell goes red, then thanks you gruffly and slips you three crowns \"for your eyes\".\n\n@event apoth_poor_mother\ntitle: Can't Afford It\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nweight: 12\nart: potion\ntags: job\nscene: A tired woman at a shop counter counting copper coins, a small child coughing beside her.\ntext: A woman counts out coppers for fever tonic. She's fifteen crowns short. Her son coughs into her coat. Pell is in the back room.\n> left: Charge the full price\n  wage\n  if gold >= 15:\n    alignment -small\n  mood -small\n  say: She leaves without it. The bell on the door rings for a very long time.\n> right: Pay the difference yourself\n  wage\n  alignment +small\n  gold -medium\n  mood +medium\n  renown +small\n  say: You slip the tonic into her basket. Next week a pie appears on the counter with your name on it.\n\n@event apoth_nightshade_order\ntitle: The Nightshade Order\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nweight: 10\nart: skull\ntags: job mystery\nscene: An order slip on a counter listing a dozen deadly reagents, signed with an elegant, illegible flourish.\ntext: A note arrives with a sealed purse: a large order of nightshade, grave-moss and powdered moonstone. Legal, technically. Ingredients for something very old and very forbidden, also technically.\n> left: Fill the order\n  wage\n  gold +medium\n  flag filled_dark_order\n  say: The courier who collects it never shows his face. The purse is very generous.\n> right: Refuse and report it\n  wage\n  rel isolde +small\n  renown +small\n  say: The headmistress listens to your report in silence. \"Thank you,\" she says. \"Tell no one else.\" She looks worried.\n> up[seal]: Seal the order for the wardens\n  wage\n  rel isolde +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You seal the note and the purse together, so nobody can change a word, and send them up to the castle. The headmistress writes back the same day: one line, thanking you for the seal, which is how she knows it's true.\n\n# ---------------- Familiar Stables ----------------\n\n@event stables_hippogriff\ntitle: Bow to the Hippogriff\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: stables\nweight: 14\nart: feather\ntags: job nature\nscene: A proud hippogriff in a straw-strewn stall, head lowered, regarding a stable hand who is bowing very deeply.\ntext: The new hippogriff won't let anyone near the feed trough. The stablemaster says to bow, keep eye contact and not blink. \"Whatever you do, don't blink.\"\n> left: Leave the feed and run\n  say: You toss the feed bucket and flee. The hippogriff eats it, and the bucket.\n> right: Bow and hold its gaze\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: It bows back. After that it follows you around the stables like a large, feathered dog.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You blinked. It knocks you flat against the trough, and you spend the evening in the infirmary with your arm in a sling.\n\n@event stables_dragon_egg\ntitle: An Egg in the Hay\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(dragon_egg)\nplace: stables\nweight: 10\nart: dragon\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A speckled egg the size of a melon nestled in hay, steam rising from it, a stable hand's lantern held over it.\ntext: Mucking out the far stall, you find an egg the size of a melon, nestled in the hay and steaming gently. It's very warm. It's very definitely not a chicken's.\n> left: Report it to Professor Brask\n  renown +small\n  rel brask +tiny\n  say: Brask nearly faints. \"Smugglers! A firedrake egg!\" It's taken to the menagerie. You get a commendation.\n> right: Keep it\n  alignment -tiny\n  flag dragon_egg\n  queue egg_hatches in 3\n  say: You smuggle it back to your dormitory wrapped in a scarf. It purrs. Eggs shouldn't purr.\n\n@event egg_hatches\ntitle: It Hatches\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nart: dragon\ntags: danger mystery\nscene: A tiny red dragon perched on a scorched dormitory pillow, a puff of smoke rising from its nostrils, a horrified roommate in the doorway.\ntext: At 3am your pillow catches fire. The egg has hatched: a firedrake the size of a kitten, delighted with itself and setting fire to everything it loves. It loves you very much.\n> left: Hand it over to the menagerie\n  mood -tiny\n  renown +small\n  say: Brask takes it gently. \"You'll be able to visit.\" You do, every week. It always tries to set you on fire, affectionately.\n> right: Hide it as long as you can\n  challenge deceive apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      gold -medium\n      say: You keep it secret for a month — buying fireproof sheets, raw meat, an asbestos sock — before releasing it in the mountains. It circles once, roaring.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      study -medium\n      health -small\n      say: The smoke alarm charm goes off in three towers at once. Discovered, singed, and in very deep trouble.\n\n@event stables_night_shift\ntitle: The Sick Pegasus\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: stables\nweight: 12\nart: moon\ntags: job nature\nscene: A white pegasus lying in a moonlit stall, wings folded, a student sitting beside it with a lantern and a blanket.\ntext: The oldest pegasus in the stables is sick. Someone has to sit with her overnight, keep her warm, and make sure she drinks. It's unpaid. Old Maud says she'll do it if no one else will. Maud is eighty.\n> left: Let Maud do it\n  health +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You sleep well. Maud is a little slower the next day.\n> right: Take the night shift\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +small\n  rel maud +small\n  say: She drinks. You talk to her all night about home. At dawn she stands, shakes her wings, and nuzzles your hair.\n\n# ---------------- First-Year Tutor ----------------\n\n@event tutor_hopeless\ntitle: The Hopeless Case\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: courtyard\nweight: 14\nart: wand\ntags: job\nscene: A tiny first-year in oversized robes pointing a wand at a feather, which is stubbornly not moving, while a tutor watches with infinite patience.\ntext: Your tutee has been trying to levitate a feather for three weeks. The feather has not moved. The tutee is starting to cry. You have one more session before their exam.\n> left: Drill the basics, again\n  arcana +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Wrist, word, will. Wrist, word, will. On the exam, the feather twitches. It's enough to pass. Your own levitation has never been cleaner.\n> right: Try something unorthodox\n  challenge appeal apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You tell them to imagine the feather is in love with the ceiling. It shoots upward and sticks there. They pass with distinction.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You tell them to imagine the feather is in love with the ceiling. They are confused. So is the feather.\n> up[contraption]: Build them a feather-lifter\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You build a little machine of springs and a fan that lifts the feather on a puff. Your tutee laughs so hard they lift the next one themselves, by accident, out of sheer surprise. Neither of you is sure what the lesson was.\n\n@event tutor_bribe\ntitle: An Indecent Proposal\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: courtyard\nweight: 12\nart: pouch\ntags: job money\nscene: A rich student in tailored robes sliding a heavy coin purse across a study table, smiling smugly.\ntext: A rich second-year doesn't want tutoring. He wants an essay. \"Thirty crowns,\" he says, dropping a purse on the table. \"Nobody needs to know.\"\n> left: Refuse\n  study +tiny\n  say: He sneers and goes elsewhere. You spend the evening on your own essays. Later you see him in detention, scrubbing a cauldron.\n> right: Write the essay\n  alignment -small\n  gold +large\n  chance unlikely:\n    study -large\n    gain detention\n    say: His professor recognises your style. You both end up in detention. He doesn't speak to you. Fine.\n  else:\n    guile +tiny\n    say: He gets a decent mark. You get thirty crowns and a slightly sour feeling.\n\n@event tutor_prodigy\ntitle: Surpassed\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nplace: courtyard\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: job\nscene: A small first-year casting a dazzling spell that fills a classroom with light, their tutor watching with complicated feelings.\ntext: Your newest tutee, a first-year barely a month in, casts in a week what took you a year. Then she asks you a question you can't answer.\n> left: Bluff an answer\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: She nods politely. You both know.\n> right: Admit you don't know and find out together\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You spend an evening in the library together. She thanks you as if you've taught her something. Maybe you have.\n\n# ---------------- Broom Courier ----------------\n\n@event courier_storm\ntitle: Delivery in a Storm\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: broom_courier\nweight: 14\nart: bolt\ntags: job danger\nscene: A courier on a broom bent low against a lightning-lit sky, a parcel strapped to their back, rain sheeting sideways.\ntext: An urgent parcel needs to cross the lake tonight. There's a storm. The dispatcher offers double pay to whoever's fool enough. Everyone looks at you.\n> left: Wait for the storm to pass\n  wage\n  gold -tiny\n  say: The parcel arrives late. The client deducts it from your wages. Rude, but survivable.\n> right: Fly through it\n  challenge feat adept- wild:\n    win:\n      wage overtime\n      vigor +small\n      say: You thread the lightning like a needle and land soaked, laughing, with the parcel dry.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      gain injured\n      say: Lightning strikes close enough to stand your hair on end. You lose the broom, the parcel and the pay, and gain a limp.\n> up[tailwind]: Surge across before the worst\n  wage overtime\n  health -tiny\n  say: You call the wind behind you and go flat out, under the worst of the storm, and never stop. You land on the far bank soaked and shaking, with the parcel dry. The dispatcher pays double without a word.\n\n@event courier_whispering_parcel\ntitle: The Whispering Parcel\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrack: broom_courier\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A brown paper parcel tied with black string on a courier's satchel, frost forming on its corners, faint whispers rising from it.\ntext: Your parcel is addressed to \"K. Dumont, Stillwater House\". It is ice cold and it whispers when you aren't looking at it.\n> left: Deliver it unopened\n  wage\n  rel kael +small\n  meet kael\n  say: Kael takes it without a word. His hands shake. The next day, he looks like he hasn't slept.\n> right: Peek inside\n  wage\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      flag read_kael_parcel\n      lore +small\n      say: Inside: a letter in a cold, beautiful hand. \"The Star wakes. Come home, or open the door. Your family.\" You reseal it perfectly.\n    lose:\n      rel kael -medium\n      meet kael\n      say: The seal hisses as it breaks. Kael is waiting at the door when you arrive. He saw. He says nothing, which is worse.\n\n@event courier_sky_pirates\ntitle: Sky Pirates\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrack: broom_courier\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: job danger\nscene: Three ragged figures on patched brooms surrounding a courier above the clouds, cutlasses glinting.\ntext: Three figures on patched brooms drop out of a cloud and box you in. \"Toll,\" says their leader, a woman with a parrot that is also on a tiny broom. \"Eight crowns or the parcel.\"\n> left: Pay the toll\n  wage\n  gold -small\n  say: They let you pass with a salute. The parrot salutes too.\n> right: Outfly them\n  wage\n  challenge feat journeyman+:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: You dive through a cloudbank and lose them over the lake. The client tips extra for the excitement.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      gold -small\n      say: They catch you, take the toll, the parcel and your hat. The parrot wears it.\n\n# Hazards of the job (L-1): minor incidents that show why the courier's pay is higher.\n\n@event broom_courier_bad_landing\ntitle: A Bad Landing\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrack: broom_courier\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: broom\ntags: job danger\nscene: A courier sprawled on wet flagstones beside a broom, parcels scattered across a rain-slick courtyard.\ntext: {The courtyard flagstones are wet, and your broom has opinions about wet flagstones.|Another wet landing. Your knees remember the last one.|Wet flagstones again. You've learned to dread this courtyard.} {You come in too fast.|A gust takes the tail as you drop.|A pigeon chooses this moment to exist.} You hit the ground rolling, and your ankle says no.\n> left: Finish the round anyway\n  wage\n  health -small\n  perform track +tiny\n  say: {You limp the last three parcels to their doors. The dispatcher notes your time and, later, your ankle.|You finish the round on one good leg and a great deal of stubbornness. The bruise is spectacular by evening.|You finish the round with a limp and a grudge against pigeons. The dispatcher writes down both.}\n> right: Hand your sack to another courier\n  wage short\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Another courier takes your sack with a sympathetic wince. Half a shift's pay, and an evening with your foot up.|You sit on the loft steps with a cold cloth on your ankle while someone else flies your round. They keep half the pay.|You hand over your sack and spend the evening on the loft steps, foot up, glaring at pigeons.}\n\n@event broom_courier_lost_parcel\ntitle: One Parcel Short\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ntrack: broom_courier\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: letter\ntags: job danger\nscene: A courier on a windy tower top turning an empty mail sack inside out, the lake far below.\ntext: {Somewhere between the loft and the lecture tower, a parcel has left your sack.|One parcel short, again. You've started counting at every landing.|Another parcel gone. You count at every landing, and still you lose one.} {It was marked \"Fragile\".|It was marked \"Urgent\", in red, twice.|It was addressed to a professor.}\n> left: Retrace your route\n  challenge research using guile apprentice:\n    win:\n      wage\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You find it in a hedge behind the greenhouses, whole and slightly damp. You deliver it late, with a story nobody believes.|It's on the boathouse roof. You climb up for it and deliver it with moss on your knees.|You find it in a gutter of the lecture tower and deliver it to the professor, who signs for it without looking up.}\n    lose:\n      wage short\n      perform track -small\n      say: {You search until dark. The loft takes the parcel's worth from your pay, and the dispatcher stops calling you reliable.|Nothing. The client complains twice, in writing, and the loss comes out of your wages.|The professor's parcel never turns up. The professor does, at the loft, and the loss comes out of your wages.}\n> right: Report it lost at once\n  wage short\n  say: {The dispatcher writes it in the ledger and the loss in your pay. \"Happens,\" they say, in a voice that means it shouldn't.|You own up straight away. The loft takes it out of your wages, but the dispatcher seems to think better of you for it.|You report it at once. The dispatcher winces at the address, a professor's. It comes out of your pay, with an apology to write.}\n\n# ---------------- Off shift (playtest 2, I1) ----------------\n\n# `@game off_shift`: pushed on the first visit to a job's place after the term's last shift, once a run for every\n# job together. A hint not to work overtime; the visit then draws the place's ordinary cards.\n@event job_off_shift\ntitle: Not Your Shift\ntrigger: queued\nclass: incident\ntone: light\nart: hourglass\ntags: job\nscene: A duty roster pinned by a staff door, every one of a student's shifts for the term already ticked off.\ntext: You turn up for work out of habit. The roster by the door says your shifts this term are done, and whoever runs the place says so too. \"Nobody pays you to be here today. Go and have a life.\"\n> left: Take the time for yourself\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You leave before anyone can change their mind, and spend the hours on nothing in particular. It's wonderful.|You take the advice. The place gets on without you, and so, it turns out, do you.}\n> right: Stay and help anyway\n  renown +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You stay, unpaid. Someone notices. Nobody thanks you, and you're tired the next morning.|You lend a hand for an hour. It's not a shift and it doesn't pay, but people remember who stayed.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/journal_cards.grim","text":"# The Journal's own cards (phase 4, P4-14): at the end of each of the first three years, a look back through it; and\n# gentle nudges about a lead left unfollowed for half a year. Nudges stay with side threads (everyday leads), never\n# the main mystery's.\n\n@pool year_recap\ndesc: At the end of a year, you read it back in your journal. Once each, and only the year it is for.\ntrigger: auto\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\npriority: 40\nart: book\ntags: journal\n\n@pool nudge\ndesc: Someone asks about a lead you found half a year ago and haven't followed. Once each, while it still can be followed.\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 1\ntags: journal\n\n# ---------------- the year in your journal ----------------\n\n@event recap_year_one\ntitle: The First Year, Read Back\npool: year_recap\nwhen: turn == 12\nscene: A journal open on a windowsill in summer light, its pages crowded with a year of small handwriting, a pressed leaf between two of them.\ntext: Before the summer swallows it, you read your first year back in your journal. You can't believe how lost you were in the autumn. {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3: By spring there are more friends in it than fit at one table.|{bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 1: By spring there's a friend in it, or two, who would notice if you went missing.|By spring there are still more meals alone in it than you'd like.}} {gpa >= 3: Your marks were better than you feared.|Your marks were what they were.} {clues(hollowstar) >= 1: And on the last page there's a question you can't put down.|}\n> left: Read it all again, slowly\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You read it twice, the second time for the parts you skimmed: the wrong tower, the first spell, the first person who saved you a seat. Then you buy a second journal, because this one is full.\n> right: Write down what you want next year\n  study +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You turn to a clean page and write a list for next year, honest and slightly embarrassing. You read it again before term, and cross one thing off already.\n\n@event recap_year_two\ntitle: The Second Year, Read Back\npool: year_recap\nwhen: turn == 24\nscene: Two worn journals side by side on a summer desk, the newer one thicker, a quill resting across them.\ntext: The second journal is fuller than the first. You read the year back over a long summer evening. {bonds(partner) > 0: There's a name in it that wasn't there last summer, written more often than any other.|{bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3: The same names come up on nearly every page now, which is what having friends looks like, written down.|There are new names in it, and some old ones have gone quiet.}} {renown_known(): Somewhere in the spring, people you've never met started to know your face.|Hardly anyone outside your year knows your name yet, which has its uses.} {clues(hollowstar) >= 2: The question at the back has grown pages of its own.|}\n> left: Mark the pages worth keeping\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You go through with a ribbon and mark the pages you'd save from a fire. There are more of them than you expected, and fewer of them are about exams than you'd have guessed a year ago.\n> right: Count what's left to do\n  study +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Two years left. You count the courses, the doors you haven't opened and the people you haven't thanked, and make a list for each. The third list is the longest.\n\n@event recap_year_three\ntitle: The Third Year, Read Back\npool: year_recap\nwhen: turn == 36\nscene: A stack of three journals on a summer windowsill, the top one open, a student sitting with their knees up reading it, the towers far off through the glass.\ntext: Three journals now. Next year is the last, and the castle will want everything. Before it does, you read the third year back. {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) >= 3: The people in it would walk into the lake for you, and you're fairly sure you'd do the same.|{bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) >= 1: There are one or two people in it you'll write to for the rest of your life.|It's a quieter book than you'd once hoped, and some of the quiet you chose.}} {renown_famous(): Half the castle knows your name now, and some of what they say is even true.|{renown_known(): People know your face in the corridors, and nod.|You've stayed mostly unnoticed, and done a great deal there.}}\n> left: Read the first one again\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You go back to the first journal, the one from the autumn you were lost in. You laugh at most of it. One page, about the first person who saved you a seat, you read three times.\n> right: Start the fourth one today\n  study +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You open a fresh journal and write the date, and under it the one thing you most want from the last year. You don't show it to anyone. You'll know in a year whether you kept it.\n\n# ---------------- nudges about old leads ----------------\n\n@event nudge_quickquill\ntitle: How's My Quill?\npool: nudge\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nactor: nessa\nwhen: clue(lead_quickquill) and spell_level(quickquill) == 0 and since(ledger_spare_quill) >= 6 and member(lumen_ledger) and turn <= 44\nart: feather\nscene: A newspaper office, an editor leaning over a student's shoulder, a quill on the desk writing something rude in the margin all by itself.\ntext: \"How's my quill?\" says Nessa, reading over your shoulder. On Tuesday the spare quill announced the engagement of two of the gargoyles and slipped it into a proof. \"You took it home half a year ago. Have you tamed it yet, or has it tamed you?\"\n> left: \"I'm working on it.\"\n  rel nessa +tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: She gives you the good ink from her own drawer. \"Proper ink first,\" she says, \"then sit a deadline night with it. That's where quills learn manners. Mine did.\"\n> right: \"It's a lost cause.\"\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Nothing's a lost cause,\" says Nessa, \"except the gargoyles.\" She doesn't take the quill back. She tells you to give it until the end of term, and goes off to cut the engagement.\n\n@event nudge_footnote\ntitle: Still Guarding It\npool: nudge\ntrack: library_assistant\nwhen: clue(lead_index) and spell_level(index) == 0 and since(library_footnote_drawer) >= 6 and member(library_assistant) and turn <= 44\nart: cat\nscene: A library catalogue cabinet, a large grey cat sitting on one drawer, glaring at a student with an armful of books.\ntext: Footnote is still on the overdue drawer, eleven years and half a year now, and the head librarian has started leaving his saucer there. When you pass, he stops washing and stares at you, as if you'd promised him something and he has been very patient about it.\n> left: Go looking for the book again\n  study -small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You spend the evening on the shelves that wander, with the card in your hand. They move twice while you're in them. You don't find the book, but you find where it was last week, which Footnote seems to accept as a start.\n> right: Tell him you're still looking\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You tell Footnote, solemnly, that you're still looking. He considers this for a long time, then goes back to washing, which seems to mean he'll allow it for now.\n\n@event nudge_two_taps\ntitle: How Far Have You Got?\npool: nudge\ntrack: tavern\nwhen: clue(lead_rapport) and spell_level(rapport) == 0 and since(tavern_two_taps) >= 6 and member(tavern) and turn <= 44\nart: cup\nscene: A crowded tavern at night, a landlady behind the bar polishing a glass, tapping the counter twice with one finger, looking across at a student.\ntext: The landlady catches your eye across the Newt and taps the bar twice, gently, like a question. It's half a year since she said you could earn it. \"Learn the room, buy the room, work the room,\" she says, polishing a glass. \"How far have you got?\"\n> left: Ask where to start\n  study -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"The corner table,\" she says. \"He's told everyone his story but you. Then the round, then a double shift. In that order, or the room knows.\" She taps the bar twice at you, and you catch half the sentence.\n> right: Say you'd rather just pour\n  gold +tiny\n  say: \"Pouring's honest,\" she says, and leaves it there. The tips are good that night. She doesn't tap the bar at you again that term.\n\n@event nudge_brace\ntitle: Your Name on the Board\npool: nudge\ntrack: duelling\nactor: lyra\nwhen: met(lyra) and clue(lead_brace) and spell_level(brace) == 0 and since(duel_dummy_hexes) >= 6 and member(duelling) and turn <= 44\nart: duel\nscene: A duelling hall doorway, a chalk board on the wall with a name under a drawing of a practice dummy and two drawings of stretchers, a student with folded arms beside it.\ntext: Lyra stops you outside the duelling hall. \"Your name's been under that dummy for half a year,\" she says. \"Someone's drawn a second stretcher.\" She folds her arms. \"Either try properly, or let me rub it out. I'm not having it look like you're scared.\"\n> left: \"Leave it. I'll try.\"\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: \"Good,\" says Lyra, and rubs out the second stretcher herself. \"Read the old captain's notes first. Nobody does. Then come and stand my barrage, and Bram's, and stop flinching.\"\n> right: \"Rub it out.\"\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: She rubs it out, slowly, so that you have to watch. The dummy goes on hexing whoever passes. Lyra says nothing more about it, which is worse.\n\n@event nudge_red_ink\ntitle: Still Waiting\npool: nudge\nwhen: clue(lead_red_ink) and not flag(red_ink_finished) and since(lecture_tower_red_ink) >= 6 and term\nart: paper\nscene: A narrow stone stair in a lecture tower, a folded note in red ink tucked into a crack in the wall at eye level.\ntext: There's a note in red ink tucked into the crack in the lecture tower stair, in the small round hand you know. It says only: \"Still waiting for the next draft. Paragraph four is still the best thing you've written. Don't make it the last.\"\n> left: Write the next draft tonight\n  study -small\n  lore +small\n  say: You write it that night and leave it on the stair in the morning. By evening it's gone, and someone has drawn a single small red tick on the step where it lay.\n> right: Put the note in a drawer\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You put the note in a drawer, under the other pages. It doesn't get any easier to forget, and the stair is on your way to everything.\n\n@event nudge_wind_jar\ntitle: The Jar Is Bored\npool: nudge\ntrack: broom_courier\nwhen: clue(lead_tailwind) and spell_level(tailwind) == 0 and since(courier_wind_jar) >= 6 and member(broom_courier) and turn <= 44\nart: broom\nscene: A courier loft full of brooms on pegs, all leaning the same way, toward a stoppered glass jar with a small wind going round inside it.\ntext: The jar of wind has moved. It was on the dead-letter shelf; this morning it's on your peg, going round and round, and every broom in the loft is leaning toward it. The loft master doesn't look up from her ledger. \"It does that,\" she says, \"when it's bored of waiting.\"\n> left: Ask who flew with it\n  study -tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: \"Before my time,\" she says, \"but the old logbooks are in the trunk.\" You read them until dark. The faded name on the jar's label turns up three times, always on the worst weather days.\n> right: Put it back on the shelf\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You put it back on the dead-letter shelf. In the morning it's on your peg again, and the brooms look at you reproachfully.\n"},{"path":"content/events/kit/item_stories.grim","text":"# Item stories (world expansion 1, K4): small cards of their own for the things you carry, so owning one is felt. All\n# plot-safe: the black-star coin's and the frost-ward's stay ambient (nothing confirmed, no clue, no clock). The items\n# found in the Whisperwood get theirs with the delve.\n\n# ---------------- The borrowed wand ----------------\n\n@pool grimsbys_books_routine_weight8\nplace: grimsbys_books\nclass: routine\nweight: 8\n\n@event item_borrowed_initials\ntitle: Initials on the Handle\npool: grimsbys_books_routine_weight8\ntone: warm\nwhen: has_item(borrowed_wand) and month >= 2\nart: wand\ntags: item study\nscene: An old bookseller peering through half-moon glasses at the scratched initials on a battered student wand.\ntext: Grimsby sees your borrowed wand and asks to hold it, very carefully. Someone scratched initials into the handle years ago, and he turns it to the light. \"Oh,\" he says. \"Oh, I know this hand. She used to buy maps from me. She went off to keep a lighthouse.\"\n> left: Ask him about her\n  lore +tiny\n  learn grimsby.lighthouses\n  rel grimsby +tiny\n  say: He talks about her for an hour, and then about lighthouses for another. You leave knowing a great deal about lamps, and that the wand once belonged to someone who wanted to see a long way.\n> right: Ask if it's worth anything\n  rel grimsby -tiny\n  gold +small\n  say: \"Worth?\" He hands it back as if you'd spoken ill of the dead. \"It's worth four years of somebody. Mind it.\" Then, because he can't help himself, he buys the old maps you've been using as bookmarks, at a fair price, and doesn't quite forgive you.\n\n@event item_borrowed_sideways\ntitle: It Sparks Sideways\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has_item(borrowed_wand) and worn(borrowed_wand)\nweight: 8\nart: wand\ntags: item class\nscene: A classroom full of students casting at targets, one student's spark flying off at a right angle into a hat stand.\ntext: Your borrowed wand chooses today, of all days, to spark sideways. The flame you aimed at the target lands in the hat stand, which takes it personally. The class is delighted. The professor less so.\n> left: Laugh it off and try again\n  renown -tiny\n  study evocation +tiny\n  say: You laugh, the class laughs, and on the second try the spark goes where you send it. The hat stand is put outside to recover.\n> right: Blame the wand, loudly\n  mood +tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  say: \"It's borrowed!\" you protest. Someone at the back says \"Mir trades them in at the market, you know.\" You file that away and the hat stand forgives no one.\n\n# ---------------- The hazelwood wand ----------------\n\n@event item_hazel_tuned\ntitle: Mir Tunes It\nplace: workshop\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: has_item(hazel_wand) and met(mir)\nweight: 8\nart: gear\ntags: item\ncast:\n  mir: mir where around(mir)\nscene: A cluttered workshop bench, an artificer with a jeweller's loupe bent over a hazel wand clamped in a tiny vice.\ntext: Mir spots your hazel wand and makes a noise like a kettle. \"Can I? I can. Two minutes.\" They clamp it in a vice, screw a loupe into one eye and start talking to the wand much more kindly than they talk to people.\n> left: Let them tinker\n  rel mir +small\n  mana -tiny\n  say: Two minutes is two hours, and Mir has you hold the wand the whole time while it drinks from your hand. Mir hands it back humming, and you swear it's quicker. \"Tuned,\" they say. \"Probably. Don't cast anything near a cabbage for a day.\"\n> right: Ask about the notebooks on the shelf\n  rel mir +tiny\n  learn mir.notebooks\n  say: Mir goes pink and pulls one down: three years of sketches, wands and owls and things with wings. \"Don't spill on them,\" they say, and talk you through every page.\n\n@event item_hazel_water\ntitle: Finding Water\nplace: maud_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: has_item(hazel_wand) and met(maud)\nweight: 8\nart: wand\ntags: item nature\nscene: An old woman in a garden holding a forked twig of hazel loosely in two hands, a student copying her with a wand.\ntext: Maud sees your hazel wand and chuckles. \"A hazel one, is it? My gran found three wells with a hazel twig and a bad temper. Hold it loose, dearie. Let it pull.\"\n> left: Learn the trick\n  vigor +tiny\n  rel maud +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: You walk her garden in circles for an hour with the wand held loose. Then it dips, hard, over the old pump. Maud cackles. \"There. You'll never be thirsty.\"\n> right: Thank her and leave the trick\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Suit yourself,\" says Maud, and gives you a jar of something pickled for the road. It is very good, and you never find out what it was.\n\n# ---------------- Robes ----------------\n\n@event item_robes_singed\ntitle: Singed\nplace: duel_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has_item(duelling_coat) or has_item(midnight_robes)\nweight: 8\nart: flame\ntags: item\nscene: A duelling hall with a scorch mark on the floor and a student patting out a smouldering sleeve.\ntext: A stray hex from the next circle catches your sleeve. There's a smell of hot wool and a small, sad hole. Somewhere in the village, a tailor feels a disturbance.\n> left: Take it down to the tailor\n  gold -tiny\n  say: The tailor tuts, darns it in five minutes, and charges you for ten. \"Duelling hall,\" she says. \"It's always the duelling hall.\"\n> right: Wear the hole with pride\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You wear it singed. By the end of the week two first-years have singed their own sleeves on purpose. You feel responsible, and slightly famous.\n\n@event item_robes_copied\ntitle: A First-Year Copies Your Robes\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has_item(midnight_robes) and year >= 2\nweight: 8\nart: mirror\ntags: item social\nscene: A courtyard where a first-year in brand-new midnight-blue robes is trying very hard to walk exactly like an older student.\ntext: A first-year has turned up in midnight-blue robes exactly like yours, down to the silver at the hem. They have also, you notice, started walking the way you do.\n> left: Tell them they look good\n  renown +tiny\n  say: They go scarlet with joy. By supper the whole first-year corridor is wearing blue. The tailor sends you a thank-you note and no discount.\n> right: Walk faster\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You lose them on the east stair. They find you again at dinner, still walking like you, slightly out of breath.\n\n# ---------------- The ashwood duelling wand ----------------\n\n@event item_ashwood_lyra\ntitle: Lyra Notices\nplace: duel_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: has_item(ashwood_wand) and met(lyra)\nweight: 10\nart: duel\ntags: item rival\ncast:\n  lyra: lyra where around(lyra)\nscene: A duelling hall, a proud student with a pale family wand looking narrowly at the dark ashwood wand in another student's hand.\ntext: Lyra's eyes go straight to the ashwood wand in your hand. \"Ashwood,\" she says. \"Duellist's wood. Mother says it's for people who can't win on talent.\" She draws her family's pale wand. \"One bout. Ashwood against Vaneshade. Unless you're shy.\"\n> left: Decline, with a bow\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Another time,\" you say. Lyra looks almost disappointed, then annoyed at herself for looking it. She practises alone, very loudly, for an hour.\n> right: Accept the bout\n  challenge duel vs lyra.arcana:\n    win:\n      rel lyra +small\n      renown +small\n      say: Her ward goes up and the ashwood bites straight through it. Lyra stares at the scorch on her sleeve. \"Again,\" she says. It's the most respectful thing she's ever said to you.\n    partial:\n      rel lyra +tiny\n      say: You trade hex for hex until Thorne wanders in and sends you both to supper. Lyra says \"Adequate\" to your wand, not to you.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: She's faster than the ashwood, and she knows it. \"Talent,\" she says, and walks off. The wand hums in your hand, sulking.\n\n# ---------------- Ilse's frost-ward ----------------\n\n@pool item_stories_routine\nclass: routine\nweight: 8\ntags: item\n\n@event item_frost_ward_cold\ntitle: Cold Things Think Twice\npool: item_stories_routine\ntone: eerie\nslot: night\nwhen: has_item(ilse_frost_ward) and season == \"winter\"\nart: snow\nscene: A dark castle corridor at night, frost spreading across the windows, a student walking through it untouched.\ntext: The cold comes into the castle tonight like something looking for someone. Frost runs along the corridor windows ahead of you, then stops at your feet and goes round, as if it had remembered its manners. Your collar, where Ilse's ward is sewn, is warm.\n> left: Walk the long corridor anyway\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You walk the whole east wing in the frost and it parts for you every time. You get back to bed cold-footed and oddly proud.\n> right: Go straight to bed\n  health +tiny\n  say: You don't push your luck. In the morning the frost on your window has drawn a careful circle round your handprint.\n\n@event item_frost_ward_thread\ntitle: A Northern Thread\npool: item_stories_routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: has_item(ilse_frost_ward) and met(kael)\nart: snow\ncast:\n  kael: kael where around(kael)\nscene: A pale northern student touching the stitched collar of another student's robe, his face unreadable.\ntext: Kael stops beside you in the corridor and, without asking, touches your collar where Ilse's ward is sewn. \"That's my mother's stitch,\" he says. \"Ilse learned it from her.\" He takes his hand back as if the thread were hot.\n> left: Ask him about his mother\n  rel kael +small\n  say: He says three sentences, which for Kael is a speech. She stitched wards into everything, even the dogs' collars. Then he goes, but he walks slower than usual.\n> right: Let it be\n  rel kael +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You say nothing, and he seems grateful. At supper he passes you the bread without being asked.\n\n# ---------------- The black-star coin ----------------\n\n@event item_star_coin_turning\ntitle: Turning It Over\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nslot: night\nwhen: has_item(black_star_coin) and not has(star_mark)\nweight: 6\nart: coin\ntags: item mystery\nscene: A student awake in a dark dormitory, turning a black coin over and over in their fingers by the window.\ntext: You've been turning the black coin over in your fingers again without noticing. It's cold enough to ache. You dreamed last night of cold, dark water, and woke with it in your fist.\n> left: Put it at the bottom of your trunk\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You bury it under your winter socks and sleep well. In the morning it's in your pocket. You don't remember putting it there.\n> right: Keep turning it\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You turn it until dawn. It never warms. By morning you know every scratch on it, and you couldn't say why that seemed important.\n\n@event item_star_coin_kael\ntitle: Kael Sees It\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: has_item(black_star_coin) and met(kael) and not has(star_mark)\nweight: 6\nart: coin\ntags: item mystery\ncast:\n  kael: kael where around(kael)\nscene: A northern student going very still at the sight of a black coin on a library table.\ntext: The black coin slides out of your pocket onto the library table. Kael, across from you, goes completely still. He doesn't touch it. He doesn't look at you. \"Put that away,\" he says quietly.\n> left: Put it away\n  rel kael +tiny\n  say: You put it away. After a long while he goes back to his book, but he doesn't turn a page for the rest of the hour.\n> right: Ask him what it is\n  rel kael -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: \"Nothing you want,\" he says, and closes his book, and leaves. You notice his hands were shaking. The coin is very cold in your pocket on the way back.\n\n# ---------------- The pocket almanac ----------------\n\n@event item_almanac_lighthouse\ntitle: The Lighthouse Page\npool: item_stories_routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: has_item(pocket_almanac)\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A student reading a tiny thick almanac by candlelight, a page of pencil notes about a lighthouse open under their thumb.\ntext: Page forty of the almanac is all Grimsby: the lighthouse at the point, its lamp times for every season, and in the margin, very small, \"best on a clear night in Frostcall, if one goes alone\".\n> left: Go down to the point one clear night\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You find the lighthouse where the page said, turning its long arm over the water. You stand there until your ears freeze. On the way back you understand Grimsby much better.\n> right: Copy his notes into your own book\n  lore +small\n  say: You copy all of it, the lamp times and the tides and the margin note. Some of it turns up, oddly, on your next astronomy paper.\n\n# ---------------- Pell's measuring spoon ----------------\n\n@event item_spoon_daughters\ntitle: The Daughters Want It Back\nplace: pell_apothecary\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has_item(measuring_spoon) and not on_shift()\nweight: 8\nart: bottle\ntags: item\nscene: Two women behind an apothecary counter, arms folded, looking pointedly at a brass spoon in a student's hand.\ntext: Pell's daughters are waiting at the counter with their arms folded. \"That's our spoon,\" says the elder. \"We learned to measure on it. Dad had no right.\" Behind them, Old Pell counts drops loudly and pretends not to hear.\n> left: Give the spoon back\n  take measuring_spoon\n  rel pell +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: They take it, and the younger one cries a little, and Pell stops counting for a moment. On your way out he catches your sleeve. \"Measure by eye, then,\" he says. \"You've the eye for it.\"\n> right: Keep it; he gave it to you\n  rel pell -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"He gave it to me,\" you say. The daughters look at their father. Pell keeps counting. Later the younger one says, not unkindly, \"Then measure well with it.\"\n\n# ---------------- The honey tin ----------------\n\n@event item_honey_bees\ntitle: Silver Bees Follow You\npool: item_stories_routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has_item(honey_tin) and season != \"winter\"\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: A student walking across a castle courtyard trailed by a small glittering cloud of silver bees.\ntext: A small cloud of silver bees has followed you up from Maud's cottage and through the castle gates. They hum round your bag, where the honey tin is. A prefect looks at them, then at you, and visibly decides it isn't his problem.\n> left: Walk them back to Maud\n  rel maud +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You walk the long way down with the bees in tow. Maud laughs until she has to sit down. \"They're checking on their honey, dearie. Nosy things.\"\n> right: Let them come along\n  renown +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {term: The bees sit in a row on the windowsill through all of Astromancy. The lecturer pauses, nods to them, and carries on.|The bees follow you round the half-empty castle and sit in a row on the library windowsill while you read. The librarian pauses, nods to them, and carries on.} Everyone talks about it for a week.\n"},{"path":"content/events/kit/shops.grim","text":"# The village shops (world expansion 1, K3). Each shop's counter is its place's `always:` card: a menu of what it sells,\n# priced in tier words (an option you can't pay for is locked), and a way out with no `when`. Shops that are also job\n# places skip the counter on a shift (`not on_shift()`). Special pieces are offers (tag `offer`): cards in the shop's\n# pool that ask for more than money.\n#   The earlier buying cards (life_new_robes, ball_robes) stay as they are; the tailor takes those robes in exchange.\n\n@event shop_square\ntitle: Market Day\nplace: village\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 10\nart: house\ntags: money shop\nscene: A cobbled market square, a bakery stall steaming, a tailor's window full of robes, a pawnbroker's sign and a student's stall of odd wands.\ntext: {The square is all stalls today, with the bakery's trestle, the tailor's rail of robes, the pawnbroker's sign creaking over his door.|Market day again. The baker's girl is shouting the price of buns as if it were news.|The tailor has put a new robe in the window and is standing behind it, watching people's faces.} {met(mir): {Mir has a stall by the well, under a sign that says WANDS, IMPROVED (PROBABLY).|Mir's stall has grown a canopy and a small, worried sign: PLEASE DO NOT TEST ON THE BAKERY.|Mir is at the stall, rewinding something that should not need rewinding.}|{A pigeon is eating the display.|Somebody is haggling over a cabbage with real feeling.|A barge horn sounds from the jetty.}}\n> buns: A bag of warm buns\n  hint: A little Health.\n  gold -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  say: {The buns are still hot enough to hurt. You eat two on the walk back and save one, nearly.|The baker's girl throws in a crooked one for free. It's the best of the lot.|Sugar on your fingers, sugar on your robes. Worth it.}\n> coat: The quilted duelling coat\n  hint: A robe for fights: more Health, and Brace costs less.\n  when: not has_item(duelling_coat) and not member(duelling)\n  gold -medium\n  give duelling_coat\n  say: {The tailor buttons you into it and thumps you on the chest. \"There. Now you're a wall.\"|It's heavier than it looks and smells faintly of the duelling hall. You feel braver at once, which may be the padding.|\"Singed in the duelling hall? Bring it back and I'll patch it,\" says the tailor, which is not reassuring.}\n> coat_circle: The duelling coat, at the Circle's price\n  hint: A robe for fights: more Health, and Brace costs less.\n  when: not has_item(duelling_coat) and member(duelling)\n  gold -small\n  give duelling_coat\n  say: {\"Circle members pay what the Circle pays,\" says the tailor, and charges you half. \"Don't tell the others.\"|The tailor knows the Circle's crest and knocks the price down without being asked.|\"Half price for the Circle. Your captain sent three of you last week. Two came back singed.\"}\n> robes: Midnight-blue robes\n  hint: A good robe: a little more Health in a fight, and people stand up straighter.\n  when: not has_item(midnight_robes) and not (chosen(life_new_robes, right) or chosen(ball_robes, left))\n  gold -large\n  give midnight_robes\n  say: {Deep blue wool, silver at the hem, a lining like the night sky. In the tailor's mirror you look like someone in a portrait.|The tailor pins the hem with her mouth full of pins and still manages to say \"Lovely\".|It costs more than you meant to spend. It also fits as if it had been waiting for you.}\n> robes_swap: Midnight-blue robes, for your old ones\n  hint: Bring the robes you bought before; the tailor takes them off your hands at half price.\n  when: not has_item(midnight_robes) and (chosen(life_new_robes, right) or chosen(ball_robes, left))\n  gold -small\n  give midnight_robes\n  say: {\"Stained at the Ball? Bring them here.\" The tailor takes your old robes, sniffs them, and fits you properly at half price.|She holds your old robes up to the light, tuts at a stain, and takes them off your hands. The new ones are the same blue, properly cut.|\"Half price for the old ones back. I'll turn them into cushions.\" You don't ask whose cushions.}\n> hazel: A hazelwood wand from Mir's stall\n  hint: A light, quick wand: a little more Mana in a fight.\n  when: met(mir) and not has_item(hazel_wand) and not has_item(borrowed_wand)\n  gold -medium\n  give hazel_wand\n  say: {Mir wraps it in a page of their own notes. \"This one likes to be used. Probably.\"|\"I re-cored it twice,\" Mir says. \"The second time on purpose.\"|Mir waves it once over the stall and a cabbage rolls politely out of the way. \"See? Improved.\"}\n> hazel_swap: Trade the borrowed wand to Mir for hazel\n  hint: Mir takes the academy's loaner in part-exchange: a little more Mana in a fight.\n  when: met(mir) and not has_item(hazel_wand) and has_item(borrowed_wand)\n  gold -small\n  take borrowed_wand\n  give hazel_wand\n  say: {\"That borrowed wand's held together with hope,\" says Mir, taking it gently. \"I'll give it a better one. Hope, I mean.\"|Mir weighs the old wand, winces at the chipped tip and hands you a hazel one, warm from the sun.|\"I'll fix this one up for next year's first-years,\" Mir says. \"Someone will need it. Probably you, in spirit.\"}\n> pawn: Sell something at the pawnbroker's\n  hint: He buys what you no longer want, at less than you paid.\n  when: has_item(hazel_wand) or has_item(ashwood_wand) or has_item(heartwood_wand) or has_item(duelling_coat) or has_item(ward_cloak) or has_item(midnight_robes) or has_item(spinner_silk_robe) or has_item(lodestone_pendant) or has_item(stag_tine) or has_item(owl_quill) or has_item(moonpetal_locket) or has_item(pocket_almanac) or has_item(measuring_spoon) or has_item(honey_tin)\n  queue shop_pawn now\n  say: {The pawnbroker's bell rings like a sigh.|The stuffed owl in his window watches you go in.|\"Ah,\" says the pawnbroker, who says \"ah\" to everyone.}\n> look: Just looking\n  say: {You walk the stalls with your hands in your pockets. The smell of the bakery is free.|You admire the tailor's window from a safe, inexpensive distance.|You buy nothing and enjoy the noise. Market days are good for that.}\n\n@event shop_pawn\ntitle: The Pawnbroker's Counter\nplace: village\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 1\nart: scales\ntags: money shop\nscene: A narrow shop crammed with other people's treasures, a stuffed owl on a shelf and a pawnbroker with a loupe in one eye.\ntext: {The pawnbroker turns each thing over twice, as if it might be hiding a second, cheaper thing underneath.|\"Ah,\" says the pawnbroker, screwing in his loupe. \"Let's see what you're tired of.\"|Behind him, the stuffed owl. In front of him, a ledger. Neither looks sentimental.} He pays fair, he says, for what is no longer loved.\n> hazel: The hazelwood wand\n  when: has_item(hazel_wand)\n  take hazel_wand\n  gold +small\n  say: He tests it on a candle and nods. \"Light. Some student will like it.\"\n> ashwood: The ashwood duelling wand\n  when: has_item(ashwood_wand)\n  take ashwood_wand\n  gold +medium\n  say: He holds it at arm's length, as if it might bite. \"Duellists come in here sometimes. Usually limping.\"\n> heartwood: The heartwood wand\n  when: has_item(heartwood_wand)\n  take heartwood_wand\n  gold +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: He goes quiet over the grain. \"That's not from any wood I sell.\" He pays well. You feel it go, the whole way home.\n> coat: The quilted duelling coat\n  when: has_item(duelling_coat)\n  take duelling_coat\n  gold +small\n  say: He pokes a finger through a singed patch and docks the price for it.\n> cloak: The ward-stitched cloak\n  when: has_item(ward_cloak)\n  take ward_cloak\n  gold +medium\n  say: He holds the hem to the lamp and the ward thread flickers. \"Sister Amaranth's work. I'll keep it out of the window.\"\n> robes: The midnight-blue robes\n  when: has_item(midnight_robes)\n  take midnight_robes\n  gold +small\n  say: \"Lovely cut,\" he says, and pays you a third of it.\n> silk: The Spinner-silk robe\n  when: has_item(spinner_silk_robe)\n  take spinner_silk_robe\n  gold +large\n  say: He touches the silk and takes his hand back very quickly. Then he pays, a great deal, without haggling.\n> lodestone: The lodestone pendant\n  when: has_item(lodestone_pendant)\n  take lodestone_pendant\n  gold +small\n  say: He sets it on the counter and it turns, slowly, to the north wall. \"Huh,\" he says, and pays.\n> tine: The stag's tine\n  when: has_item(stag_tine)\n  take stag_tine\n  gold +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: He doesn't ask where you got it. The stuffed owl seems to.\n> quill: The owl-feather quill\n  when: has_item(owl_quill)\n  take owl_quill\n  gold +tiny\n  say: \"A quill's a quill,\" he says. It writes \"a quill's a quill\" on his ledger by itself, and he pays a little more.\n> locket: The moonpetal locket\n  when: has_item(moonpetal_locket)\n  take moonpetal_locket\n  gold +small\n  say: He opens it, sees the petal, closes it again. \"Pretty,\" he says. It's the kindest word he's used all day.\n> almanac: The pocket almanac\n  when: has_item(pocket_almanac)\n  take pocket_almanac\n  gold +tiny\n  say: He flicks through Grimsby's pencil notes and snorts. \"He'll buy it back off me by Tuesday.\"\n> spoon: Pell's measuring spoon\n  when: has_item(measuring_spoon)\n  take measuring_spoon\n  gold +tiny\n  say: \"Brass,\" he says, \"worn thin.\" He pays for the brass.\n> honey: The honey tin\n  when: has_item(honey_tin)\n  take honey_tin\n  gold +tiny\n  say: He shakes it, sniffs it and pays a few coins. A silver bee follows you all the way to the jetty, looking disappointed.\n> keep: Keep it all\n  say: {You put everything back in your bag. The pawnbroker shrugs. \"Ah.\"|Not today. The owl watches you go.|You find you're fond of it all after all.}\n\n@event shop_mabels\ntitle: Mabel's Counter\nplace: mabels\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: money shop\nscene: A small warm tea shop with forty tins on the shelves, three cakes under glass and a woman behind the counter already reaching for a pot.\ntext: {Mabel looks at you once as you come in and is already reaching for a tin. \"Sit, petal. I know what you want; let's see if you do.\"|\"There's my petal,\" says Mabel. \"You've the look of someone who needs something steeped.\"|The bell over the door. Mabel doesn't turn round. \"Ginger, by the sound of that door,\" she says.} {The courage blend is in the green tin; the calm blend in the blue.|A sign on the counter reads: TONICS — ONE AT A TIME. MABEL.|The cups on the hooks behind her catch the light.}\n> tea: A pot of tea\n  hint: A little Mood.\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {She brings it before you've sat down. It's exactly right, which is worrying.|A pot, a cup and a slice of cake you didn't order and don't pay for. \"Crumbs,\" she says. \"Day-old.\"|You drink it by the window and watch the barge come in. The day gets a little easier.}\n> courage: The courage blend\n  hint: A tonic for a month: asking for things and performing come easier.\n  when: not has(courage_blend)\n  gold -small\n  gain courage_blend\n  say: {\"Ginger, honey and something I don't tell,\" says Mabel, screwing the lid on. \"One cup a morning. Speak up, petal.\"|She weighs it out on her little scales. \"For the nerve,\" she says. \"Not for being rude. Different tin.\"|You drink a cup there and then. Your voice comes out a size bigger when you thank her.}\n> calm: The calm blend\n  hint: A tonic for a month: exams and frightened animals come easier.\n  when: not has(calm_blend)\n  gold -small\n  gain calm_blend\n  say: {\"Chamomile, lavender, a pinch of moonwort,\" says Mabel. \"For exams. And for your hands, petal. They're shaking.\"|She hands you the blue tin. \"Breathe before you open the paper. The tea only does half.\"|\"Good for nerves and nervous beasts,\" says Mabel. \"Don't ask me why. It just is.\"}\n> chat: Just say hello\n  say: {Mabel tells you three things about the village and one about yourself. The last one is right.|You lean on the counter and let her talk. It costs nothing and helps more than tea.|\"Off you go, then,\" she says, \"and come back with better news.\"}\n\n@event shop_grimsbys\ntitle: Grimsby's Counter\nplace: grimsbys_books\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: money shop study\nscene: A bookshop crowded to the rafters, shelves leaning on each other, an old man in half-moon glasses sniffing the spine of a book.\ntext: {Mr Grimsby is up a ladder, sniffing a spine. \"Ah! Not that one,\" he says, and hands you a different one.|Grimsby greets you like an old customer, by the title of a book you've never read. He has no idea what you came in for today.|The shop smells of dust and pipe smoke and one shelf, somewhere, of the sea.} {A stack of exam primers leans on the counter like a tired student.|A box by the door says BOOKS BOUGHT BACK, FAIR PRICES, NO QUESTIONS (SOME QUESTIONS).|A lighthouse print hangs crooked behind the till.}\n> pick: Let him choose you a book\n  hint: Skill XP, in whatever he thinks you need.\n  gold -small\n  chance even:\n    lore +small\n    say: He sniffs three books and hands you the fourth. It's on a subject you'd never have picked, and you can't put it down.\n  else:\n    chance even:\n      arcana +small\n      say: \"This one,\" he says, holding out a battered spellbook. \"It smells of thunder.\" It does, and it's very good.\n    else:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: He gives you a herbal with someone's pressed flowers still inside. \"The flowers were extra. I mean free. I mean extra.\"\n> primer: An exam primer\n  hint: A little exam preparation for every course you're taking.\n  when: term\n  gold -small\n  study +tiny\n  say: {The primer is dog-eared in all the right places. Someone passed with this book before you.|Grimsby hands it over reluctantly. \"Exams,\" he says, as if naming a sad illness.|You read the first chapter on the barge home and, for once, the questions look like ones you could answer.}\n> sell: Sell a book back\n  hint: A few crowns, and a book fewer.\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {He pays for it and puts it on a shelf you'll never find again. You miss it at once.|\"Fair price,\" he says, and it is. You miss the book anyway.|He sniffs it, approves, and pays. Your shelf at the castle looks a little emptier tonight.}\n> browse: Browse the shelves\n  say: {You spend an hour reading the first pages of twenty books. It's the best hour of the week.|You find a book you want and can't afford, and read a chapter of it standing up.|Grimsby recommends a book by its smell. You leave with nothing and an appetite.}\n\n@event shop_pell\ntitle: Pell's Counter\nplace: pell_apothecary\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 10\nwhen: not on_shift()\nart: bottle\ntags: money shop\nscene: An apothecary of a thousand labelled drawers, a daughter behind the counter, an old man counting drops at the back.\ntext: {One of Pell's daughters is at the counter. Behind her, Old Pell counts drops aloud and doesn't look up.|\"Tonic or draught?\" asks the daughter on the counter. \"Dad will say draught. Dad always says draught.\"|The drawers rattle as you come in, as if they were sorting themselves.} {A card on the till reads MEASURED TWICE, SOLD ONCE.|Old Pell is arguing with a label.|The smell of mint and something sterner.}\n> tonic: Pell's tonic\n  hint: A little Health.\n  gold -small\n  health +small\n  say: {It tastes of mint and iron. Halfway back to the castle you realise you've stopped yawning.|The daughter pours it; Pell checks the measure from across the room. \"Right,\" he says. It is.|\"Two spoons at bedtime,\" says the daughter. \"Not three. Dad will know.\"}\n> draught: The steadying draught\n  hint: A tonic for a month: research, brewing and enchanting come easier.\n  when: not has(steadying_draught)\n  gold -small\n  gain steadying_draught\n  say: {Old Pell measures it himself, counting aloud. \"Steady hands,\" he says. \"That's all it does. That's all anyone needs.\"|The draught tastes of nothing at all, which the daughter says is the expensive part.|\"For the fine work,\" says Pell, corking it. \"Don't waste it on the coarse work.\"}\n> look: Just looking\n  say: {You read labels until the daughter asks if you're lost. You are, a little.|You leave with nothing but the smell of mint in your robes.|Pell glances up once, decides you're not buying, and goes back to his drops.}\n\n@event shop_newt\ntitle: The Newt's Counter\nplace: gilded_newt\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 10\nwhen: not on_shift()\nart: cup\ntags: money shop social\nscene: A low-beamed tavern with small windows, a fire that never goes out and a landlady polishing glasses behind the bar.\ntext: {{slot == \"afternoon\": By day the Gilded Newt is quiet, just a fire, a few regulars and the landlady with a cloth over her shoulder.|At this hour the Gilded Newt is all elbows and cider, and the landlady works the bar with a cloth over her shoulder.}|{slot == \"afternoon\": The Newt in the afternoon smells of bread and last night's cider. Someone is asleep by the fire.|The Newt smells of cider and wet coats tonight. Someone is singing by the fire, badly.}|The landlady nods at you over the bar. \"Supper's on. Cider's cold. Table's free.\"} {The slate says HOT SUPPER, A ROUND, CIDER.|A cat is asleep in the bread basket.|A barge crew is arguing about weather.}\n> supper: A hot supper\n  hint: Health and Mood; it ends a hungry month.\n  gold -small\n  health +small\n  mood +tiny\n  lose hungry\n  say: {Stew, bread, an apple you didn't ask for. You leave warm all the way through.|The landlady watches you eat as if she'd been worried about you. Perhaps she had.|You finish the bowl and she fills it again without asking. \"Growing,\" she says, though you're not.}\n> round: Buy a round for the table\n  hint: Renown; the regulars will remember.\n  gold -small\n  renown +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {The regulars lift their cups to you. One of them, very drunk, makes a speech about students.|\"To the castle!\" someone shouts, and the whole room drinks to it, and to you.|The barge crew drink your health and then argue about whether you're a good sort. They decide you are.}\n> cider: A cup of cider\n  hint: A little Mood.\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {Cold, sharp and tasting of last autumn's apples.|You drink it by the window and watch the lake turn gold.|The cat in the bread basket opens one eye at you and closes it again.}\n> look: Just warm your hands at the fire\n  say: {Nobody minds. The fire never goes out; that's the point of it.|You sit by the fire until your hands remember they're hands.|The landlady leaves you be. Sometimes that's the service.}\n\n# ---------------- Special offers ----------------\n\n@event offer_ashwood_wand\ntitle: Prove It\nplace: village\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nweight: 8\nwhen: met(mir) and year >= 2 and not has_item(ashwood_wand)\nart: wand\ntags: money shop offer\nscene: A market stall with a single dark wand on a velvet cloth, an artificer student holding a hand over it protectively.\ntext: Under a cloth at the back of Mir's stall is an ashwood duelling wand, dense and dark. \"It bites anything that blocks it,\" says Mir. \"I'm not selling it to just anyone. Show me you can hold it, and it's yours, for what the ash cost me.\"\n> left: Leave it under its cloth\n  say: Mir covers it again, relieved. \"It'd probably bite me too.\"\n> right: Pay up, and prove it\n  hint: A dear price, handed back if it won't answer you. A wand for fights: anything that blocks it takes a jolt.\n  price_dear\n  challenge spell adept-:\n    win:\n      give ashwood_wand\n      say: The wand jumps in your hand like a live thing and a spark cracks off the well. Mir whoops. \"It likes you! Probably.\"\n    lose:\n      gold +huge\n      gold +large\n      mood -small\n      say: The wand sulks in your hand and won't so much as spark. Mir gives you your money back, kindly, which is worse.\n\n@event offer_ward_cloak\ntitle: Ward Thread\nplace: village\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 8\nwhen: (rel(amaranth) >= 12 or spell_level(ward) >= 2) and not has_item(ward_cloak)\nart: shield\ntags: money shop offer\nscene: A tailor's back room, a spool of faintly shining thread on the table, a grey cloak half-hemmed beside it.\ntext: The tailor beckons you into her back room. On the table is a spool of thread that shines faintly when you aren't looking at it. \"Sister Amaranth sent it down. Ward thread. She says you're to have a cloak with it, if you can pay for the hemming. Fire and frost slide off it like rain.\"\n> left: You can't afford a cloak\n  say: The tailor puts the spool away in a drawer marked YOURS. \"It'll keep,\" she says.\n> right: Have the cloak made\n  hint: A robe for fights: more Health, and spells of fire, frost and starlight hit softer.\n  gold -large\n  give ward_cloak\n  say: She hems it in an afternoon, muttering. When you put it on, the thread warms, and the draught from the door stops at your collar.\n\n@event offer_pocket_almanac\ntitle: The Lighthouse Page\npool: grimsbys_books_routine_weight8\ntone: light\nwhen: not has_item(pocket_almanac)\nart: book\ntags: money shop offer\nscene: An old bookseller holding a tiny thick book out of reach, its margins crowded with pencil notes.\ntext: On Grimsby's counter is a pocket almanac: fifty years of tides and moons, every margin full of his own pencil notes. You reach for it and he moves it, gently, out of reach. \"Not for sale,\" he says. \"Well. Not for sale to someone who doesn't know what it's for.\"\n> left: Leave him his almanac\n  say: He tucks it back in his waistcoat pocket and pats it. \"Thank you,\" he says, oddly formal.\n> right: {knows(grimsby.lighthouses): Talk to him about lighthouses|Argue that you'd use it}\n  hint: {knows(grimsby.lighthouses): You know what he loves.|A negotiation.} A pocket almanac: tides, moons and weather on the right cards.\n  if knows(grimsby.lighthouses):\n    gold -tiny\n    give pocket_almanac\n    rel grimsby +small\n    say: You ask him about the lighthouse at the point and he talks for an hour. At the end he presses the almanac into your hands for a few coins. \"Page forty,\" he says. \"That's the good one.\"\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate journeyman:\n      win:\n        gold -small\n        give pocket_almanac\n        say: You tell him what you'd look up first: the next spring tide, the next new moon. He considers this for a long time. \"Yes,\" he says at last, and sells it to you.\n      lose:\n        rel grimsby -tiny\n        say: \"No, no,\" he says kindly, \"you'd look up the weather and nothing else.\" He's right, and that's the worst of it.\n\n@event offer_measuring_spoon\ntitle: Measure Twice\nplace: pell_apothecary\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 8\nwhen: not on_shift() and not has_item(measuring_spoon) and year >= 2\nart: bottle\ntags: money shop offer\nscene: An old apothecary holding a thin brass spoon up to the light, a row of empty vials in front of a student.\ntext: Old Pell is holding up a brass spoon worn thin on one side. \"Forty years,\" he says. \"It's never been wrong. I'm getting a new one.\" He sets six vials in front of you. \"Measure these. If you count right, it's yours, for what the brass is worth.\"\n> left: Not today\n  say: He shrugs and puts the spoon in his waistcoat pocket. \"It'll wait. Spoons are patient.\"\n> right: Count the drops aloud\n  hint: Money down, handed back if you miscount. A spoon for brewing cards.\n  gold -medium\n  challenge brew journeyman steady:\n    win:\n      give measuring_spoon\n      rel pell +small\n      say: You count aloud, every drop, and every vial is right. Pell says nothing for a long moment. Then: \"Measure twice,\" and hands you the spoon.\n    lose:\n      gold +medium\n      say: You lose count in the fifth vial. Pell hands your money back. \"Count aloud,\" he says. \"Every time. Then come back.\"\n\n@event offer_honey_tin\ntitle: A Tin of Silver\nplace: maud_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 10\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 12 and not has_item(honey_tin)\nart: flower\ntags: offer\nscene: A thatched cottage's garden full of beehives, an old woman in a veil lifting a frame of silver comb.\ntext: Maud is at the hives, veil down, lifting out a frame of comb that shines like poured silver. \"Silver bees,\" she says. \"They don't take to everyone. They've taken to you, dearie. Help me with the frames and there's a tin in it.\"\n> left: Keep well back from the bees\n  say: Maud laughs and does it herself. A silver bee follows you to the gate anyway, curious.\n> right: Help with the frames\n  hint: A tin of honey: a way to sweeten someone on the right cards.\n  health -tiny\n  give honey_tin\n  rel maud +small\n  say: You get stung once, politely, on the thumb. Maud fills a dented tin with silver honey and presses it into your hands. \"Share it,\" she says. \"That's what it's for.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/knowing_2.grim","text":"# Use what you know, part 2 (phase 4, P4-13): moments where knowing someone, or what someone is like, changes\n# what you can do. Wiring is the `knowing` pool's (knowing.grim). Each card has an option that shows locked until\n# you know the fact it names.\n\n@event know_grisk_collectors\ntitle: Running Late\npool: knowing\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  x: any where classmate_here(x) and rel(x) >= 10\nper: x\nwhen: met(grisk) and term\nart: coin\nscene: A common room at night, a student counting a few coins on a table and coming up short, a friend watching from the doorway.\ntext: {x} is counting coins on the common-room table, and coming up short. {They} borrowed from Grisk in the village to get through the month, and the payment was due on Friday. \"He was so nice about it,\" {x} says. \"He called me his friend. He'll give me another week, surely.\"\n> left: \"Ask him for more time, then.\"\n  rel x -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {x} goes down to the village to ask. The next evening there are two large, polite visitors at the castle gate asking for {x} by name, and {x} spends a fortnight's supper money making them go away. Nobody hears {x} call Grisk a friend after that.\n> right: \"Pay him tonight. Trust me.\"\n  when: knows(grisk.collectors)\n  gold -small\n  rel x +small\n  say: You tell {x} about the waistcoats, and the trolls inside them, and how polite and thorough they are. {x} goes pale, and you lend {x.them} the rest. Grisk takes the coins at the Newt that night with real regret. \"Such a pity,\" he says. \"They were looking forward to it.\"\n\n@event know_grisk_newt\ntitle: Paid in Advance\npool: knowing\ntone: light\nplace: village\nwhen: met(grisk) and term\nart: coin\nscene: A village lane, a long queue of students outside a small shop with a hand-painted sign in the window offering half price.\ntext: A stationer's in the village is selling a whole term of ink, paper and candles at half price, paid in advance. Half the castle is queuing down the lane. It's a real bargain, as long as the shop is still there at the end of term.\n> buy: Buy a term's worth\n  gold -small\n  chance even:\n    mood -small\n    say: Three weeks later the shutters are down and there's a notice on the door about creditors. Half the castle stands in the lane reading it. So do you.\n  else:\n    gold +medium\n    say: The shop lasts the term, just. You don't buy ink again until spring, and feel very clever every time you don't.\n> pass: Walk past the queue\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You walk past. You'll never know whether it was a bargain. That bothers you more than it should.\n> ask: Ask Grisk at the Newt first\n  when: knows(grisk.newt_table)\n  gold -tiny\n  rel grisk +tiny\n  say: You buy Grisk a drink at his corner table and mention the shop. He sighs at the ceiling like a man discussing rain. \"Three weeks,\" he says. \"Four, if the landlord is sentimental.\" It's gone in three, and you were not in the queue.\n\n@event know_grisk_favours\ntitle: Just a Small Favour\npool: knowing\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  x: any where classmate_here(x) and rel(x) >= 10\nper: x\nwhen: met(grisk) and term\nart: pouch\nscene: A castle stairwell, a relieved-looking student holding a small parcel wrapped in oilcloth, a friend frowning at it.\ntext: {x} looks relieved, which worries you. Grisk has offered to forget {x.their} whole debt for one small favour: carrying a parcel across the village after dark, for a client he didn't name. \"It's nothing,\" {x} says. \"It's a parcel. What's in a parcel?\"\n> left: \"A parcel's a parcel.\"\n  rel x +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {x} carries the parcel, and the debt is gone, just like that. {They} won't tell you where {they} took it, and for the rest of term {they} won't go down to the village alone.\n> right: \"Pay in coin. I'll help.\"\n  when: knows(grisk.favours)\n  gold -small\n  rel x +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You know about Grisk's favours, and the clients he never names. You and {x} scrape the coins together between you instead. Grisk takes them with a little bow. \"Prudent,\" he says, and sounds almost disappointed.\n\n@event know_amaranth_book\ntitle: Peppermint and a Charm\npool: knowing\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  x: any where classmate_here(x) and rel(x) >= 8\nper: x\nwhen: met(amaranth) and season == \"winter\"\nart: potion\nscene: A dormitory window seat in winter, a student holding a steaming mug of something green, a friend sniffing it doubtfully.\ntext: {x} has had a cold for a week and has cured it, {they} say{s}, with a remedy of {x.their} own: peppermint, honey, a pinch of something from the alchemy lab and a charm {they} won't explain. It works. {They} {are} too embarrassed to show anyone. \"Amaranth would laugh,\" {they} say{s}.\n> left: Ask for a cup yourself\n  mood +small\n  rel x +tiny\n  say: {x} makes you a cup. It's dreadful, and it works. {They} make{s} you promise to tell no one, and you never do, which seems a pity.\n> right: \"She keeps a book of these.\"\n  when: knows(amaranth.book)\n  rel x +small\n  rel amaranth +tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: You tell {x} about Sister Amaranth's book of her students' remedies. It takes a week of persuading. When {x} finally shows her, Amaranth tries it, says nothing at all, and copies it out on a fresh page in her square hand. {x} doesn't stop smiling for a day.\n\n@event know_amaranth_rounds\ntitle: Who Comes at Night\npool: knowing\ntone: warm\nplace: infirmary\ncast:\n  x: any where classmate_here(x) and rel(x) >= 10\nper: x\nwhen: met(amaranth) and seen(infirmary_missed_round) == 0\nart: bed\nscene: An infirmary ward at dusk, a feverish student in a bed by the window, a friend on the chair beside it, the lamps being lit.\ntext: {x} is in the infirmary overnight with a fever, and frightened, the way people are when they're ill and it's getting dark. \"What if something happens,\" {they} say{s}, \"and nobody comes?\" Visiting hours are nearly over.\n> left: Stay until they send you out\n  rel x +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You stay, talking about nothing, until you're sent out with a scolding. {x} is asleep by then. You aren't, for hours.\n> right: \"She does every round herself.\"\n  when: knows(amaranth.rounds)\n  rel x +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You tell {x} about the rounds: every bed, every night, by Amaranth herself, and never once missed. {x} doesn't quite believe you. In the morning {they} tell{s} you {they} woke at two, and there she was with her lamp. \"She nodded at me,\" {they} say{s}, amazed.\n\n@event know_pell_not_yet\ntitle: Not Yet\npool: knowing\ntone: warm\nplace: pell_apothecary\nwhen: met(pell) and rel(pell) >= 10 and year >= 3 and seen(pell_draught) == 0\nart: bottle\nscene: An apothecary's wall of a thousand small drawers, one very old drawer open near the floor, an old man kneeling beside it with a yellowed card.\ntext: Pell is restocking the bottom of the wall when you come in, and his oldest drawer is open beside him, the one with the yellowed card in it. He looks at you over the drawer for longer than usual, as if measuring something twice.\n> left: Wait for him to finish\n  mood +tiny\n  say: He finishes, shuts the drawer and serves you as if nothing happened, counting aloud, as always. On your way out you notice he's watching you go.\n> right: Ask about the card\n  when: knows(pell.oldest_drawer)\n  rel pell +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You ask about the card, the draught \"of your own strength\". Pell takes it out, reads it, and looks at you again. Then he puts it back, and under \"Not yet.\" he adds one word in his precise hand: \"Nearly.\" He doesn't explain. He doesn't need to.\n\n@event know_vey_own_face\ntitle: It Slides Off\npool: knowing\ntone: light\ncast:\n  x: any where classmate_here(x) and rel(x) >= 8\nper: x\nwhen: met(vey) and term\nart: mirror\nscene: A dormitory mirror, a frustrated student pointing a wand at a roommate's hair, which is flickering between two colours.\ntext: {x} has been trying all week to glamour {x.their} roommate's hair green, for a dare, and it won't hold: it slides off within the hour, every time. {They} {are} about ready to give up on illusion altogether.\n> left: Practise with {x.them} all evening\n  rel x +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: You practise all evening. By midnight the green holds for two whole hours. {x} says that's progress, and goes to bed looking like someone who doesn't believe it.\n> right: \"Try it on your own face first.\"\n  when: knows(vey.own_face)\n  rel x +small\n  rel vey +tiny\n  say: You tell {x} what Vey says: a glamour you can't hold on yourself won't hold on anyone. {x} tries it on {x.their} own nose, swears at the mirror for an hour, and then it holds. The roommate is green for a week. Vey hears of it, awards nobody any points, and smiles.\n\n@event know_marisol_margins\ntitle: The Book in the Margins\npool: knowing\ntone: warm\nplace: library\ncast:\n  x: any where classmate_here(x) and rel(x) >= 8\nper: x\nwhen: met(marisol) and term\nart: book\nscene: A library table, a student staring at the same open page of a tidecalling textbook, a friend sitting down across from them.\ntext: {x} has the tidecalling textbook open at the same page {they} had last week, and the week before. \"It makes sense when I read it,\" {they} say{s}, \"and no sense at all in the lake.\" The exam is close. {They} {are} starting to talk about dropping the course.\n> left: Drill {x.them} at dawn\n  study -small\n  rel x +small\n  say: You spend three cold dawns at the lake with {x}, going through it line by line. It helps, a little. {x} passes narrowly, and is grateful, and still hates the book.\n> right: \"Ask Marisol about her poems.\"\n  when: knows(marisol.margins)\n  rel x +small\n  rel marisol +tiny\n  say: You tell {x} about the book of poems Marisol wrote her own tidecalling into as a student. {x} asks her, mortified. Marisol lends it on the spot, dripping. The margins make sense of everything. {x} passes well, and says the poems were awful but the margins were wonderful.\n\n@event know_mabel_tins\ntitle: A Tin on the Barge\npool: knowing\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  x: any where classmate_here(x) and rel(x) >= 10\nper: x\nwhen: met(mabel) and term\nart: cup\nscene: A refectory table at supper, a quiet student with an unopened letter beside the plate, a friend sliding onto the bench next to them.\ntext: {x} has gone quiet this month. {They} {are} homesick, though {they} would never say so. A letter from home came on Tuesday, and it's still unopened beside {x.their} plate.\n> left: Sit with {x.them} at supper\n  rel x +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You sit with {x} at supper every night that week and talk about nothing much. It doesn't fix anything. On Sunday {they} open{s} the letter, with you there.\n> right: Write to Mabel\n  when: knows(mabel.tins)\n  rel x +small\n  rel mabel +tiny\n  say: You send a note down to Mabel in the village. Two days later a tin comes up on the barge, addressed to {x} in round handwriting: tea, a slab of cake, and no note. {x} never finds out who sent it, and keeps the tin on {x.their} windowsill all year.\n\n@event know_mabel_fourth_cake\ntitle: A Cake With a Name\npool: knowing\ntone: light\nplace: mabels\ncast:\n  x: any where classmate_here(x) and rel(x) >= 15\nper: x\nwhen: met(mabel)\nweight: 20\nart: meal\nscene: A village tea shop, three cakes under glass domes on the counter, two students leaning over them, a woman in an apron watching.\ntext: It's {x}'s birthday, and you've brought {x.them} to Mabel's, because there's nowhere better. {x} looks at the three cakes on the counter as if choosing between them were the hardest decision of the year.\n> left: Let {x.them} choose\n  rel x +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {x} chooses the lemon after ten minutes, and says it was the right choice, and then eats half of yours.\n> right: Ask Mabel for a fourth cake\n  when: knows(mabel.fourth_cake)\n  rel x +small\n  rel mabel +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: You ask, quietly, whether there's a fourth. Mabel looks at {x}, vanishes into the back and comes out with something pink and alarming. She writes its card while you watch: {x}'s name, and the date. {x} eats it, survives, and is very proud to be on the card.\n\n@event know_isolde_mercy\ntitle: A Witness to Character\npool: knowing\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  x: any where classmate_here(x) and rel(x) >= 10\nper: x\nwhen: met(isolde) and term\nart: scales\nscene: A tall tower study, a headmistress behind her desk with her hands folded, two students standing before it, one of them very pale.\ntext: {x} has been sent to the headmistress over a prank that went further than {they} meant, and has asked you along as a witness to {x.their} good character. Headmistress Marrow hears all of it without a word. Then she turns to you.\n> left: Vouch for {x.them}, warmly\n  rel x +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You vouch for {x}, warmly and at length. The headmistress thanks you, and gives {x} a month of detentions anyway. \"Character,\" she says, \"is what one has left after the detention.\"\n> right: Wait for her question\n  when: knows(isolde.mercy)\n  rel isolde +small\n  rel x +tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You say nothing, and wait. Sure enough, she asks what you think would be just. You tell her honestly: not nothing, but less than a month, and the damage put right by {x}'s own hands. She considers it, and, to {x}'s astonishment, she listens.\n\n@event know_grimsby_upper_floor\ntitle: Out of Print\npool: knowing\ntone: light\nplace: grimsbys_books\nwhen: met(grimsby) and year >= 2\nweight: 20\nart: book\nscene: A crowded bookshop, an old bookseller holding out a book about lighthouses, a student behind the counter's ladder looking upward.\ntext: You need a book for an essay: the one every reading list cites and nobody has seen in thirty years. The library's copy went missing before you arrived. Grimsby, asked, gives you a book about lighthouses instead and says it's the next best thing. He may even be right.\n> left: Take the lighthouse book\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You read the lighthouse book. There's a chapter on lenses that turns out, absurdly, to help with your essay. It's still not the book, and Grimsby is unbearable about it.\n> right: Climb to the upper floor\n  when: knows(grimsby.upper_floor)\n  lore +small\n  gold -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: You climb the ladder behind the shop, skipping the third and fourth rungs, and spend an hour in the dust among the remainders. The book is there, under a stack of almanacs. Grimsby, below, says he had no idea, and charges you half price for knowing about the ladder.\n\n@event know_grimsby_parcels\ntitle: On Credit\npool: knowing\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  x: any where classmate_here(x) and rel(x) >= 8\nper: x\nwhen: met(grimsby)\nart: book\nscene: A castle corridor window looking down toward the village, a student turning away from it, a friend catching their sleeve.\ntext: {x} has been avoiding the village for a month. It comes out eventually: Grimsby posted {x.them} three books on credit, exactly the right ones, and {they} can't pay, and {they} {are} too ashamed to walk past the shop. \"He'll have my name on a list,\" {they} say{s}.\n> left: Help {x.them} save up\n  rel x +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You help {x} put coins aside, week by week. It takes the rest of term. When {x} finally goes in to pay, Grimsby looks puzzled: he'd forgotten. {x} hadn't, for a single day.\n> right: \"He's never chased a debt.\"\n  when: knows(grimsby.parcels)\n  rel x +small\n  rel grimsby +tiny\n  say: You tell {x} that Grimsby posts books on credit and has never chased a debt in his life. {x} goes down that afternoon to say sorry, and comes back with a fourth book and a cup of tea inside {x.them}. \"He said to pay when I can,\" {they} say{s}, \"and to stop being silly.\"\n\n@event know_ilse_frost\ntitle: Frost on the Inside\npool: knowing\ntone: light\ncast:\n  ilse: ilse where around(ilse) and met(ilse) and rel(ilse) >= 3\nwhen: season == \"winter\" and not off_campus and not flag(ilse_taken) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\nart: snow\nscene: A dormitory corridor in deep winter, windows frosted over on the inside, students in coats and hats, a girl in white furs looking unimpressed at a cracked ward.\ntext: Winter has got into the dormitories. The windows frost over on the inside every night, and the warming ward the professors put up keeps cracking. Everyone is sleeping in their coats, and the porters say it will be mended next week, as they said last week.\n> left: Patch the ward with the others\n  study -small\n  health +tiny\n  say: You and half the corridor patch it, badly, out of a textbook. It holds most nights. You get very good at sleeping in a hat.\n> right: Ask Ilse\n  when: knows(ilse.frost_craft)\n  rel ilse +small\n  health +small\n  say: You go to Ilse, who knows Northern frost-craft better than anything the professors teach. She looks at the ward for less than a minute, says something rude about it in Northern, and reworks it with two fingers. The windows are clear by morning. She refuses all thanks, and accepts a scarf.\n\n@event know_tamsin_backdrop\ntitle: A Forest by Post\npool: knowing\ntone: light\nwhen: met(tamsin) and term and (month == 4 or month == 10)\nart: letter\nscene: A hall stage after supper, a huge blank canvas propped against the wall, a student with a bucket of grey paint and a letter full of sketches.\ntext: The first-years' end-of-term play needs a backdrop, a forest at night, and you, somehow, have been volunteered to paint it. You have a week, a bucket of grey and no idea at all.\n> left: Paint the best forest you can\n  study -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You paint a forest. It looks like a forest from the back of the hall, if the lamps are low. The first-years are very kind about it.\n> right: Write to Tamsin for help\n  when: knows(tamsin.theatre)\n  rel tamsin +small\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You write to Tamsin, who learned illusion painting their grandmother's backdrops. The reply is six pages of sketches and insults about your brushwork. You follow it to the letter. On the night, the forest stirs in a wind that isn't there, and the hall gasps. You send Tamsin the programme.\n"},{"path":"content/events/knowing.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Use what you know (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md, batch P4-12): scenes where a fact you've learned about someone\n#  (content/world/facts/, the People panel) opens a better way through: an option that waits, locked, until you\n#  know it (`when: knows(...)`), or an outcome that turns on it (`if knows(...)`). Then second-hand facts (one person\n#  tells you about another) and a secret you could sell. Nothing here touches the main plot's facts.\n# ============================================================\n\n@pool knowing\ndesc: What every use-what-you-know card shares: once, a routine moment where knowing someone matters.\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 1\ntags: people facts\n\n@pool second_hand\ndesc: One person tells you something about another: you learn the fact whatever you choose. Once each.\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 1\ntags: people facts\n\n# ---------------- use what you know ----------------\n\n@event know_kael_thaw\ntitle: Before the Break\npool: knowing\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(kael) and rel(kael) >= 12 and month == 4\nart: snow\nscene: A barge landing in the snow at the end of term, students with trunks waiting to leave, two of them standing face to face at the edge of the jetty.\ntext: The last barge before the winter break is loading, and Kael is at the landing with his trunk, not quite looking at you. He's been strange all week. Back home, he told you once, saying goodbye is bad luck. The barge horn sounds. He's waiting, as if there were something you might say.\n> left: Shake his hand\n  rel kael +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You shake his hand, and wish him a good break. He says \"and you\", and climbs aboard, and doesn't look back. You realise, as the barge rounds the bend, that he was waiting for something else.\n> right: \"Until the thaw.\"\n  when: knows(kael.thaw)\n  rel kael +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Until the thaw,\" you say, and touch your forehead to his. He goes very still. Then he laughs, a short surprised breath, and says it back, so the cold knows you're coming back. He waves from the barge all the way round the bend.\n\n@event know_ilse_useless\ntitle: Ilse, Losing\npool: knowing\ntrigger: social\nactor: ilse\ntone: light\nwhen: met(ilse) and rel(ilse) >= 8\nart: snow\nscene: A frozen pond at dusk, a girl in white furs sitting alone on the ice with a ruined frost-sculpture beside her, snow falling only on her.\ntext: Ilse's frost-work has gone wrong for the first time anyone has seen. She's sitting on the frozen pond beside a sculpture that has cracked down the middle, and the private snowstorm over her head has turned to sleet. \"Go away,\" she says. \"Or teach me something. I don't care which.\"\n> left: Help her mend the sculpture\n  challenge spell adept-:\n    win:\n      rel ilse +small\n      say: You hold the crack while she seals it, and it takes, and the sculpture stands. She looks at it critically. \"Useful,\" she says, as if that were an insult. The sleet eases, a little.\n    lose:\n      rel ilse -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: You make it worse. The sculpture falls in two. Ilse looks at the pieces, then at you. \"Well,\" she says. \"Now we're both bad at it.\" It isn't quite a joke.\n> right: Teach her to skim stones across the ice\n  when: knows(ilse.useless)\n  rel ilse +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You teach her something completely useless: skimming stones across the ice, as far as they'll go. She counts every bounce out loud, in Northern. The sleet turns back to snow. When you leave, she's still at it, alone, counting, and very nearly smiling.\n\n@event know_ilse_stitch\ntitle: A Torn Ward\npool: knowing\ntone: warm\nplace: courtyard\nwhen: met(ilse) and met(kael) and rel(ilse) >= 10\nart: snow\nscene: A courtyard bench in winter, a torn white frost-ward charm lying in a student's hands, a needle and silver thread beside it.\ntext: You find a frost-ward charm in the snow by the courtyard fountain, torn half through, the kind Ilse makes. Without it, whoever lost it will feel the cold all winter. The stitching is unusual, a looping Northern pattern. You have a needle and some silver thread.\n> left: Take it to the lost-property box\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You hand it in. Somebody claims it the next day: a second-year who has been shivering for a week. Ilse, you hear later, made her a new one, without being asked, and without saying anything.\n> right: Mend it with her mother's stitch\n  when: knows(ilse.stitch)\n  rel ilse +small\n  rel kael +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You mend it slowly, in the looping stitch, the one that was her mother's. When you hand it to Ilse, she turns it over, finds the mend, and goes very quiet. Kael, beside her, touches it once. \"Mother's,\" he says. Neither of them says anything else, for a long time.\n\n@event know_isolde_chess\ntitle: Called to the Tower\npool: knowing\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: met(isolde) and term\nart: tower\nscene: A tall tower study, a chessboard of crystal towers by the window mid-game, a headmistress behind a desk with a letter of complaint open in front of her.\ntext: You've been sent to the headmistress over a complaint from a lecturer: a broken window, a misunderstanding, a lot of shouting. Headmistress Marrow reads the letter, then looks at you over it. Behind her, the tower chess game by the window is half finished, and one of her towers is in trouble.\n> left: Explain, very carefully\n  challenge appeal adept:\n    win:\n      rel isolde +tiny\n      say: You explain, carefully and truthfully, and she listens without interrupting. At the end she folds the letter. \"Pay for the glass,\" she says. \"And apologise properly.\" It could have gone a great deal worse.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      gold -small\n      say: You explain, and it comes out sounding like an excuse even to you. She hears you out, and fines you for the window, and makes you write the apology twice.\n> right: Offer to finish her game\n  when: knows(isolde.chess)\n  rel isolde +small\n  mood +small\n  say: \"Your tower's in trouble,\" you say. She looks at you, and then at the board, and says \"Is it?\" You play it out together. She loses, and laughs, short and delighted. Then she folds the letter. \"Pay for the glass,\" she says. \"Come back and lose to me properly, sometime.\"\n\n@event know_nessa_off_record\ntitle: Asking About a Friend\npool: knowing\ntone: tense\nplace: ledger_office\nwhen: met(nessa) and rel(nessa) >= 6\nart: paper\nscene: A newspaper office, an editor at her desk, a self-writing quill poised over a notebook, a student in the visitor's chair with arms folded.\ntext: Nessa wants to ask you about a friend of yours, who's in trouble with the prefects and who you'd rather nobody wrote about. \"I'll print it either way,\" she says. \"I'd rather print it right.\" Her quill is already moving, taking down everything you say, including your sighs.\n> left: Give her the friendly version\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You give her the kindest true version you can, and the quill takes it down. The piece runs, fair and short. Your friend is grateful, mostly. The quill added an adjective you didn't say.\n> right: \"This is off the record.\"\n  when: knows(nessa.off_record)\n  rel nessa +small\n  alignment +small\n  say: \"Off the record,\" you say, and the quill stops mid-word and lies down on the desk. Nessa looks at it, and at you, and something in her face changes. You tell her the whole of it. She never prints a line, and the next week she helps your friend with the prefects instead.\n\n@event know_nessa_planted\ntitle: The Proofs\npool: knowing\ntone: light\nplace: ledger_office\nwhen: met(nessa) and rel(nessa) >= 4\nart: paper\nscene: A cluttered newspaper office at night, galley proofs spread over a desk under a lamp, an editor watching a student from across the room.\ntext: Nessa drops a stack of proofs on the desk in front of you and goes to make tea, very casually. \"Errors,\" she says from the doorway. \"Find them before the printers come. There might be one.\" There are, you can see already, at least four.\n> left: Fix everything you can find\n  study +small\n  rel nessa +tiny\n  say: You find four errors and fix them, and a fifth that turns out to be the editor's style. Nessa reads your corrections, nods, and points to a sixth, in the headline, that you missed. \"Close,\" she says.\n> right: Find hers first\n  if knows(nessa.planted):\n    rel nessa +medium\n    renown +tiny\n    say: You find the planted one first: a date that couldn't be true, set in the middle of the page like a trap. You circle it, and only then fix the others. Nessa comes back with the tea, sees the circle, and laughs out loud. \"Nobody's ever gone for that one first,\" she says. \"You're hired. Unpaid, obviously.\"\n  else:\n    rel nessa -tiny\n    mood +small\n    say: You hunt for whichever error she meant, and can't tell which it is, and run out of time before the printers come. Nessa takes the proofs back with a small, satisfied smile. \"There's always one,\" she says, and doesn't say which. The hunt, you have to admit, was fun.\n\n@event know_seraphine_too_loud\ntitle: Lost at Lantern Night\npool: knowing\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(seraphine) and rel(seraphine) >= 10 and month == 2\nart: lantern\nscene: A crowded lakeshore at night strung with floating lanterns, a press of students and villagers, one dark boathouse at the edge with its door ajar.\ntext: Lantern Night, and the whole academy and half the village are on the lakeshore, laughing, singing, all their futures at once. Somebody says Seraphine was here a minute ago and now she isn't. Her friends are searching the crowd, calling her name over the music.\n> left: Search the crowd with them\n  health -tiny\n  rel seraphine +tiny\n  say: You push through the crowd for an hour, calling her name. Someone finds her in the end, sitting very still in the dark of the boathouse, and she says she's fine, and isn't, and goes back to bed early.\n> right: Look for her somewhere dark\n  when: knows(seraphine.too_loud)\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You don't look in the crowd. You walk away from it, to the dark boathouse at the end, and she's there on an upturned boat with her eyes shut. You sit down beside her without a word. After a while she says, \"Quiet.\" Then: \"Yours.\" You stay till the lanterns are all out.\n\n@event know_thorne_hands\ntitle: The Demonstration\npool: knowing\ntone: tense\nplace: lecture_tower\nwhen: met(thorne) and term\nart: wand\nscene: A lecture hall, an old professor at the front with his wand raised for a demonstration, its tip trembling very slightly, a student in the front row rising from their seat.\ntext: Thorne is demonstrating a duelling stance to the whole year, wand raised, and halfway through the demonstration his wand hand begins to shake. It's small. Most of the hall hasn't noticed yet. He has. You can see him deciding whether to stop.\n> left: Look away, and let him finish\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  say: You look down at your notes, and so, after a moment, does the rest of the front row. Thorne finishes the demonstration with his other hand, briskly, and dismisses the class early. You're fairly sure that was the right thing.\n> right: Ask to be his demonstration partner\n  when: knows(thorne.hands)\n  rel thorne +medium\n  renown +tiny\n  say: \"Could you show it on someone, sir?\" you ask, and step up before he can answer. He takes your wrist instead of his wand, and shows the stance with your arm, steady, the whole year watching. Afterwards he says, \"Hm.\" It's the warmest thing he's ever said to you.\n\n@event know_tamsin_failed\ntitle: Exam Nerves, by Post\npool: knowing\ntrigger: letter\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(tamsin) and rel(tamsin) >= 6 and year >= 2\nart: letter\nscene: A letter on a dormitory desk, its handwriting cramped and blotted, a student holding a quill over a blank reply.\ntext: Tamsin's letter is short again. They're sitting their advanced illusion examination next week, and they can't sleep, and every insult in the letter is half-hearted, which is how you know. \"Say something,\" the postscript says. \"Anything. I'm not asking. Obviously.\"\n> left: Send them luck and a sketch\n  rel tamsin +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You send a sketch of a goat in a mortarboard, labelled \"your examiner\", and a great deal of luck. Their reply comes a fortnight later: \"Passed. The goat helped. Insufferable of you.\"\n> right: Remind them of the last time\n  if knows(tamsin.failed):\n    rel tamsin +medium\n    mood +small\n    say: You write about the last time: the failed exam, the sold theatre, the retake, and how they passed it anyway with everything gone. \"You've done harder,\" you write. Their reply comes the day after the exam, one line: \"You remembered. Top marks. I cried in the refectory again.\"\n  else:\n    rel tamsin +small\n    say: You write that they'll be fine, and that you're sure they've done harder things. Their reply is cheerful and vague. They passed, but they don't say much else, and you have the feeling you missed something.\n\n@event know_grimsby_nose\ntitle: The Night Before the Essay\npool: knowing\ntone: light\nplace: grimsbys_books\nwhen: met(grimsby) and term\nart: book\nscene: A village bookshop at closing time, a student at the counter out of breath, the bookseller holding a lamp up to a wall of shelves.\ntext: Your essay is due tomorrow, and the one book you need isn't in the library. You reach Grimsby's as he's turning the sign to \"closed\". He lets you in, holds up the lamp to a wall of a thousand spines, and says, \"Well? What does it look like?\"\n> left: Describe the title and the author\n  study -tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: You tell him the title and the author. He brightens and goes off into the shelves, and comes back an hour later with a book about lighthouses, and then, at last, the right one, as the clock in the square strikes ten.\n> right: Describe how it smells\n  when: knows(grimsby.nose)\n  study +small\n  rel grimsby +small\n  say: \"Old paper and rain,\" you say, \"and something like cloves.\" Grimsby's face lights up. He closes his eyes, takes three steps, and pulls it off the shelf without looking. \"Nobody ever asks properly,\" he says, and won't take your money.\n\n@event know_pell_counting\ntitle: The Good Stock\npool: knowing\ntone: light\nplace: pell_apothecary\nwhen: met(pell) and term\nart: potion\nscene: An apothecary's counter lined with brass scales and a wall of tiny drawers, an old man in spectacles measuring drops into a vial while a student watches.\ntext: You need a strong remedy for a friend with a fever, and Pell has the good stock, in a dark bottle on the top shelf he sells only to people he trusts. Everyone else gets the shop's ordinary tincture. Pell looks at you over his spectacles and starts to measure out the ordinary one.\n> left: Go to the infirmary instead\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You thank him and take your friend to the infirmary instead, where Sister Amaranth's ordinary tonic works, slowly. Your friend is up in a week, and grateful, and you wonder whether the dark bottle would have done it in a day.\n> right: Count the drops aloud with him\n  when: knows(pell.counting)\n  gold -small\n  rel pell +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You count the drops aloud as he measures, quietly, one to nine. Pell stops at four and looks at you. Then he puts the ordinary bottle away and reaches for the top shelf. \"Measure twice,\" he says, counting with you. Your friend is up the next morning.\n\n@event know_amaranth_truth\ntitle: Patched Up\npool: knowing\ntone: tense\nplace: infirmary\nwhen: met(amaranth) and health < 70\nart: potion\nscene: An infirmary bench, a student holding a bruised arm, a healer in a grey apron turning it to the light from the window.\ntext: You come into the infirmary with an arm that's swelling fast, from something you'd rather not explain. Sister Amaranth turns it to the light and asks, as she always does, how it happened. Behind you there's a queue. Your story is ready: a fall on the stairs.\n> left: Tell her about the stairs\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You tell her about the stairs. She listens, and waits, and treats it, slowly, with a salve that stings. \"Come back in two days,\" she says. \"Bring the truth with you next time. It heals faster.\"\n> right: Tell her the true version, first\n  when: knows(amaranth.truth)\n  health +medium\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: You skip the stairs and tell her exactly what happened, embarrassing as it is. She nods once. \"Better,\" she says, and reaches for a bottle that isn't a salve. The swelling's gone before you reach the door.\n\n@event know_marisol_spirits\ntitle: The Storm Crossing\npool: knowing\ntone: tense\nplace: lakeshore\nwhen: met(marisol) and term and season != \"winter\"\nart: wave\nscene: A small boat on a choppy grey lake under a storm sky, a student at the oars, shapes like galloping horses moving in the waves around the boat.\ntext: You're halfway across the lake in a borrowed boat when the storm comes in, fast and black. The waves are full of shapes like galloping horses: the water spirits, racing the boat and, by the look of it, enjoying themselves. The shore is a long way off.\n> left: Row for it\n  challenge feat adept-:\n    win:\n      health -small\n      say: You row until your hands are raw, and the spirits race you all the way, and lose, just. You land on the shingle soaked to the skin, and lie there laughing at the sky.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      mood -small\n      say: The spirits win. They tip the boat over, cheerfully, ten yards from shore, and you swim the rest in the cold. You arrive half drowned, and they gallop off, delighted with themselves.\n> right: Ask them nicely not to cheat\n  when: knows(marisol.spirits)\n  mood +small\n  rel marisol +tiny\n  say: You ship the oars, lean over the side and ask the spirits, very politely, not to cheat. There's a pause. Then they gather under the boat like a raft and carry you to shore at a gallop. Marisol, on the steps with her book, laughs so hard she drops it.\n\n@event know_vey_smell\ntitle: The Feast That Wasn't\npool: knowing\ntone: light\nplace: refectory\nwhen: met(vey) and term\nart: meal\nscene: A refectory table laden with a glorious feast, students reaching hungrily for it, one student pausing with a fork in mid-air and sniffing.\ntext: Some third-years have laid on a feast at the end of the long table: roast fowl, pies, a cake with candles. The whole table is reaching for it. It's beautiful. It's a little too beautiful. Somewhere at the far end, the third-years are trying very hard not to laugh.\n> left: Dig in with everyone\n  mood -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You reach for the pie with everyone else, and your fork goes through it and into the table, and the whole feast dissolves into air and a few crumbs of real bread. The third-years cheer. Everyone laughs, you included, eventually.\n> right: Smell it first\n  when: knows(vey.smell)\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: You lean in and sniff. Nothing: not roast, not pastry, not a crumb. \"It doesn't smell of anything,\" you say, loudly. The table stops. The third-years groan. Somewhere, you feel, Professor Vey would be proud, and would never say so.\n\n@event know_brask_asking\ntitle: Out of Your Depth\npool: knowing\ntone: tense\nplace: stables\nwhen: met(brask) and term\nart: paw\nscene: A stable yard, a large and irritable griffin pulling against a rope held by a nervous student, a professor in a leather apron watching from the fence.\ntext: Brask has handed you a lead rope and a griffin with opinions, and walked away to the fence. The griffin has decided it would like to be somewhere else, and it's bigger than you. Everyone else in the class seems to be managing. Brask is watching, arms folded, saying nothing.\n> left: Manage it yourself\n  challenge soothe using vigor adept-:\n    win:\n      rel brask +tiny\n      say: You dig your heels in and talk to it in a low voice, the way Brask talks to everything but students, and slowly the griffin comes round. Brask nods, once. From her that's a medal.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: The griffin wins. It drags you halfway across the yard and into a water trough before Brask takes the rope from your hand with two fingers. \"Hm,\" she says. \"Again tomorrow.\"\n> right: Ask Brask for help\n  when: knows(brask.asking)\n  rel brask +small\n  study +small\n  say: \"I can't,\" you say. \"Show me.\" Brask comes over from the fence, takes your hands and puts them on the rope the right way, and the griffin goes quiet as a cat. \"Good,\" she says. \"Most never ask.\" You realise she's remembering it.\n\n# ---------------- second-hand: one person about another ----------------\n\n@event heard_bram_corvin\ntitle: Bram on Corvin\npool: second_hand\nactor: bram\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(bram) and met(corvin) and rel(bram) >= 6 and rel(corvin) >= 4 and not knows(corvin.last_warning)\nart: shield\nscene: A courtyard at noon, a prefect in a red sash speaking low to a student, glancing across at another student lounging by the fountain.\ntext: Bram takes you aside, which he never does. \"You're friendly with Corvin,\" he says, not quite a question. \"Then you should know. He's on his last warning. One more detention and they send him home.\" He looks at the fountain, where Corvin is laughing at something. \"I shouldn't have told you. I'm telling you anyway.\"\n> left: Thank Bram, and keep it quiet\n  learn corvin.last_warning\n  rel bram +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You thank him, and don't tell anyone. Over the next month you notice yourself steering Corvin away from trouble, without saying why. Bram notices too, and pretends not to.\n> right: Ask Bram why he cares\n  learn corvin.last_warning\n  rel bram +tiny\n  rel corvin +tiny\n  say: \"Why do you care?\" you ask. Bram goes red. \"Somebody has to,\" he says, and walks off. You don't tell Corvin what Bram said. You do sit with him at lunch more, and he notices, and makes a joke of it.\n\n@event heard_corvin_bram\ntitle: Corvin on Bram\npool: second_hand\nactor: corvin\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(bram) and met(corvin) and rel(corvin) >= 8 and not knows(bram.father)\nart: cards\nscene: A gargoyle passage at night, a student shuffling cards on a parapet, telling a story to another in a low voice.\ntext: Corvin is shuffling his deck on the parapet. \"You know why Bram's like he is?\" he says. \"His father was head boy. Tells him every holiday he'll never amount to anything. I heard it myself, on the stairs, one parents' day.\" He deals a card and looks at it. \"Don't tell him I told you. He'd hate that more than anything I've ever done to him.\"\n> left: Keep it to yourself\n  learn bram.father\n  rel corvin +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You keep it to yourself. But the next time Bram gives you a demerit, you hear his father's voice under it, and you take it a little more gently than before. Bram notices, and doesn't know why.\n> right: Ask Corvin why he told you\n  learn bram.father\n  rel corvin +tiny\n  say: \"Why tell me?\" you ask. Corvin shuffles. \"So someone's kind to him,\" he says, \"and it isn't me.\" Then he deals you a hand, and talks about something else very fast.\n\n@event heard_maud_thorne\ntitle: Maud on Thorne\npool: second_hand\nactor: maud\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(maud) and met(thorne) and rel(maud) >= 8 and not knows(thorne.roses)\nart: rose\nscene: A cottage garden at dusk, an old woman pruning a hedge with shears, nodding toward the dark shape of an observatory on the hill.\ntext: Maud is cutting back the hedge. \"You know old Thorne?\" she says. \"Every dawn, dearie, he's up behind the observatory in that little walled garden of his, talking to his roses. Thinks nobody knows. I've known for thirty years.\" She snips. \"He's kinder to them than to any of you. Don't tell him I said.\"\n> left: Promise not to tell\n  learn thorne.roses\n  rel maud +small\n  say: You promise. You don't tell. But the next time Thorne is terrible to you in a lecture, you imagine him at dawn, talking to a rose, and it helps more than you'd have thought.\n> right: Ask whether she'd show you\n  learn thorne.roses\n  rel maud +tiny\n  rel thorne -tiny\n  say: \"Show you? Certainly not,\" says Maud, delighted. She does anyway, from behind the hedge one dawn, both of you crouching. Thorne is pruning and murmuring. He looks up, just once, at the hedge. You both hold your breath.\n\n@event heard_mabel_grimsby\ntitle: Mabel on Grimsby\npool: second_hand\ntrigger: random\nplace: mabels\nweight: 25\nactor: mabel\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(mabel) and seen(mabel_intro) > 0 and not knows(grimsby.lighthouses)\nart: cup\nscene: A village tea shop, a woman behind the counter wiping cups, nodding through the window at a bookshop across the lane with its sign turned to closed.\ntext: \"Shut again,\" says Mabel, nodding at Grimsby's across the lane. \"You know where he goes, petal? Lighthouses. Walks out to look at one, any one, whenever the shop gets too much. Forty years I've watched him go.\" She pours your tea. \"He thinks it's a secret. Everyone in the village knows.\"\n> left: Keep his secret, such as it is\n  learn grimsby.lighthouses\n  rel mabel +small\n  say: You keep it, such as it is. Next time you're in the shop, you notice the little brass lighthouse on his counter for the first time, and the picture of one above the till, and you don't say anything.\n> right: Ask which lighthouse he likes best\n  learn grimsby.lighthouses\n  rel mabel +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: \"The one at the point,\" says Mabel at once. \"It's the ugliest. He says it tries the hardest.\" She thinks about it. \"That's the nicest thing anyone's ever said about anything round here, and he said it about a lighthouse.\"\n\n@event heard_marisol_vey\ntitle: Marisol on Vey\npool: second_hand\nactor: marisol\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(marisol) and met(vey) and rel(marisol) >= 6 and not knows(vey.mirror)\nart: mirror\nscene: A rainy lakeshore, a professor reading under a dry patch of air on the steps, talking quietly to a student beside her.\ntext: Marisol closes her book of poems. \"Has Vey shown you the mirror trick yet?\" she asks. \"She won't, in public. Once, years ago, her reflection didn't come back for a week. Walked around the castle as her. Taught two of her classes.\" She looks at the rain. \"Vey still won't talk about it. I think she was frightened. I think she still is.\"\n> left: Promise never to mention it to Vey\n  learn vey.mirror\n  rel marisol +small\n  say: You promise. You never mention it. But the next time Vey passes the long mirror on the stairs, you see her glance at it, just once, and look away quickly, and you understand.\n> right: Ask who noticed it wasn't her\n  learn vey.mirror\n  rel marisol +tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: \"Nobody,\" says Marisol. \"That's what frightened her. Nobody noticed for a week.\" She opens her book again. \"The poem I can't remember the end of is about that. I've just realised.\"\n\n@event heard_nessa_isolde\ntitle: Nessa on the Headmistress\npool: second_hand\nactor: nessa\ntone: light\nwhen: met(nessa) and met(isolde) and rel(nessa) >= 8 and not knows(isolde.chess)\nart: tower\nscene: A newspaper office, an editor pointing at a sketch of a tower window pinned to a cluttered wall, a student leaning in to look.\ntext: Nessa taps a sketch on her wall: the headmistress's tower, one window circled. \"Tower chess,\" she says. \"Every evening. She plays both sides, and when she loses to herself she laughs out loud. I've heard it from the courtyard.\" She lowers her voice. \"That's my way in, one day. Nobody interviews a woman who's winning.\"\n> left: Wish her luck with the interview\n  learn isolde.chess\n  rel nessa +small\n  say: You wish her luck. She says she doesn't need luck, she needs a chessboard, and spends the evening learning tower chess from a library book with a great deal of muttering. She's terrible at it.\n> right: Ask her not to use it as a trick\n  learn isolde.chess\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  alignment +small\n  say: \"Don't make it a trick,\" you say. Nessa looks at you for a long moment. \"It isn't a trick,\" she says. \"It's a door.\" But she takes the sketch down off the wall, and puts it in a drawer.\n\n@event heard_tobias_mir\ntitle: Tobias on Mir\npool: second_hand\nactor: tobias\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(tobias) and met(mir) and rel(tobias) >= 6 and not knows(mir.family)\nart: gear\nscene: A greenhouse in the afternoon, a broad farm boy repotting seedlings and talking over his shoulder to a student holding a watering can.\ntext: \"Mir's family,\" Tobias says, repotting, \"run a clockworks. Big one. And they think all of it, the inventing, is a hobby.\" He presses the soil down very gently. \"Mir told me that like it was nothing. It wasn't nothing. On the farm we'd say a calf that isn't let run never learns its legs.\"\n> left: Say you'll keep it between you\n  learn mir.family\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: \"Between us,\" you say. Tobias nods, relieved. Later you notice that every time someone calls one of Mir's machines \"a nice little toy\", Tobias, very politely, changes the subject.\n> right: Say Mir should hear that saying\n  learn mir.family\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  rel mir +tiny\n  say: \"Tell Mir that,\" you say. Tobias goes pink. He does, a week later, at supper, and gets it slightly wrong, and Mir laughs, and then goes quiet, and then says it back to him, correctly, as if writing it down.\n\n@event heard_grimsby_mabel\ntitle: Grimsby on Mabel\npool: second_hand\ntrigger: random\nplace: grimsbys_books\nweight: 25\nactor: grimsby\ntone: warm\nwhen: met(grimsby) and seen(grimsby_intro) > 0 and not knows(mabel.cups)\nart: book\nscene: A bookshop window, an old bookseller pointing across the lane at a tea shop with cups hanging on hooks behind its counter.\ntext: Grimsby nods across the lane at Mabel's. \"Those cups behind her counter,\" he says. \"Every one belongs to a regular. Living or dead. She never takes one down.\" He turns a page without reading it. \"Mine's the blue one, third from the left. She hung it my first winter in the village, when I knew nobody. I've never told her I know why.\"\n> left: Keep it between you and him\n  learn mabel.cups\n  rel grimsby +small\n  say: You keep it. The next time you're at Mabel's, you look for the blue cup, third from the left, and find it, and say nothing. Mabel sees you looking, and smiles, and pours your tea.\n> right: Ask whether he'll tell her\n  learn mabel.cups\n  rel grimsby +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Will you tell her?\" you ask. Grimsby considers it, then shakes his head. \"She knows I know,\" he says. \"That's the whole arrangement.\" He sells you a book about lighthouses you didn't ask for. You buy it.\n\n# ---------------- a secret you could sell ----------------\n\n@event sell_a_secret\ntitle: What's It Worth?\npool: knowing\ntone: tense\nplace: village\ncast:\n  x: any where tagged(x, peer) and met(x) and rel(x) >= 15 and ((npc_is(bram) and knows(bram.cheating)) or (npc_is(tobias) and knows(tobias.blight)) or (npc_is(lyra) and knows(lyra.burnout)) or (npc_is(corvin) and knows(corvin.brother)))\nper: x\nwhen: renown_known() and term\nart: coin\nscene: A village lane, a sly man in a printer's apron leaning in a doorway, holding up a purse, a student stopped in front of him.\ntext: The man who prints the gossip sheets in the village stops you in the lane. He's heard you're close to {x}, he says, and that you know something about {x.them} nobody else does. \"Whatever it is,\" he says, weighing a purse, \"from someone like you, it would sell out by noon. Your name on it, if you like. Or not.\"\n> left: Sell it\n  gold +medium\n  renown +medium\n  alignment -medium\n  rel x -large\n  queue scandal_secret_out within 2..4 with x chance likely\n  say: You tell him. The sheet sells out by noon. {x} comes to breakfast, reads it, puts down {x.their} fork, and looks across the hall at you, and doesn't say anything at all. That's the worst part.\n> right: Say you know nothing\n  alignment +small\n  rel x +tiny\n  say: \"Never heard of it,\" you say, and walk on. The man shrugs and pockets his purse. That evening {x} sits down beside you at supper for no reason {x.they} could name, and stays longer than usual.\n\n@event scandal_secret_out\ntitle: Who Sold It\npool: scandal\nactor: npc\nwhen: renown_known()\nart: paper\nscene: A refectory at breakfast, a gossip sheet passed hand to hand, a whole table turning to look at one student, another student sitting very straight at the next table.\ntext: It's out: everyone knows who sold the gossip sheet the story about {npc}. The printer told the Newt, and the Newt told the castle. People who never cared for {npc} are disgusted with you anyway: nobody likes someone who sells a friend. {npc} hasn't said a word. Everyone is waiting to see what you'll do.\n> own: Apologise to {npc}, in front of everyone\n  renown -medium\n  alignment +small\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You stand up at breakfast, cross the hall and apologise to {npc}, out loud, with everyone watching. {They} look{s} at you for a long time, and then nod{s}, once. It doesn't mend it. It's a start.\n> deny: Say the printer is lying\n  challenge deceive adept:\n    win:\n      renown -tiny\n      rel npc -tiny\n      say: You say the printer is a liar who'd sell his own mother, which is true. People decide to believe you, more or less. {npc} doesn't, and says nothing, and doesn't need to.\n    lose:\n      renown -large\n      alignment -small\n      rel npc -small\n      say: You say the printer is lying, and the printer produces the receipt with your initial on it. The castle has its verdict. {npc} walks past you in the corridor as if you were a draught.\n> spin: Say it was true, and someone had to\n  alignment -medium\n  rel npc -large\n  renown +tiny\n  say: You say, coolly, that it was true, and someone had to say it. Some people agree with you. {npc} hears it. {They} stop{s} coming to meals for a week, and the castle forgets who started it, and you never will.\n"},{"path":"content/events/last_day.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The last day (playtest 2, X10; fills M21's stub). Month 48 is\n#  Starfall of the final year and holds only pushed cards, in\n#  priority order: the morning, the results, the career, the\n#  lake, the people, the speech (expansion_y3.grim), the last\n#  Starfall, what the yearbook keeps, and the eve of graduation\n#  (calendar.grim). Every line of the yearbook is told here, and\n#  where it is a choice the card makes it (feedback 2026-10-25):\n#  the career you sign (took_<career>), whether you stay with a\n#  partner, keep your friends, stand in the Dumonts' photograph,\n#  write to Tamsin, and which personal lines the yearbook keeps.\n#  Replaying this month alone reaches every ending the run can.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event last_morning\ntitle: The Last Morning\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 95\nart: tower\ntags: story\nscene: A dormitory room in early light, trunks packed and strapped, graduation robes hanging on the wardrobe door, the towers gold through the window.\ntext: You wake before the bell, out of habit, and there's no bell. Your trunk is packed and strapped. The robes hang on the wardrobe door. It's Starfall, the last day of the year, and tomorrow you graduate. The castle is very quiet, as if it's listening.\n> ok: Get up and begin the last day\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {has(origin_noble): You dress with care, the way you were raised to.|{has(origin_street): You check your pockets out of habit, then laugh at yourself.|You dress slowly, wanting the morning to last.}} The staircase that moves waits for you at the top, for once, as if it knows.\n\n@event last_results\ntitle: The Final Lists\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 90\nart: scroll\ntags: story study\nscene: A crowd of final-year students around a great oak door in the entrance hall, long lists of names pinned to it, one student reaching up to touch a line.\ntext: The final lists are pinned to the great hall door, and the whole year is crowded in front of them. {gpa >= 3.5: Your name is near the top, and someone has drawn a small star beside it.|{gpa >= 2.5: Your name sits in the middle of the list, solid and unremarkable, exactly where you hoped.|{gpa >= 2.0: Your name is near the bottom, but it's there.|Your name is on the list, just. Someone has pencilled \"phew\" next to it, possibly you.}}} {flag(thesis_distinction): In the thesis column, one word in gold ink, Distinction.|{flag(thesis_passed): In the thesis column, a plain pass.|{flag(thesis_resubmit): In the thesis column, in red, To be resubmitted.|}}}\n> ok: Touch your name, for luck\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {gpa >= 3.0: People you barely know shake your hand on the way out. You let them.|{gpa >= 2.0: You run a finger along the line. Four years, one line. It's enough.|You stand there a while after the crowd has gone. It's on the list. It counts.}}\n\n@event last_career\ntitle: What Comes Next\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 85\ntype: menu\nart: letter\ntags: story job\nthread: careers\nscene: Letters with heavy seals spread across the lid of a packed trunk, a student sitting cross-legged among them with a pen.\ntext: {has_offer(): Every letter you've had this year is spread across the lid of your trunk. Tomorrow you graduate, and today each of them wants its answer. You can sign one. You can, if you're brave or foolish, sign none.|No letter, no start date. Everyone else seems to know where they're going, or says so. The registrar's list is still pinned by her window, but you didn't put your name to it.}\n> conclave: Sign with the Conclave\n  hint: An apprentice at the Seven Towers.\n  when: flag(offer_conclave)\n  flag took_conclave\n  mood +small\n  say: You sign the Conclave's contract with the silver-sealed pen they sent. In the autumn you climb the Seven Towers in grey, an apprentice with seven years of wards ahead of you.\n> royal_library: Sign with the Royal Library\n  hint: A cataloguer in the old archives.\n  when: flag(offer_royal_library)\n  flag took_royal_library\n  mood +small\n  say: You sign for the cold room at the Royal Library Archives and the folios that object to being opened. You'll need gloves. You've bought two pairs already.\n> fellowship: Take the Spire fellowship\n  hint: A junior fellow among books that bite.\n  when: flag(offer_fellowship)\n  flag took_fellowship\n  mood +small\n  say: You chalk your name properly over the carrel at the Spire Library. A junior fellow, from the autumn: a life among books that bite, and secrets that wait.\n> guild: Sign with the Guild's works\n  hint: A journeyman's bench, two explosions a month.\n  when: flag(offer_guild)\n  flag took_guild\n  mood +small\n  say: You sign for the journeyman's bench at the Guild of Alchemists' works, and order the protective clothing on the same day.\n> shop: Sign the harbour lease\n  hint: A crooked shop and a still of your own.\n  when: flag(offer_shop)\n  flag took_shop\n  mood +small\n  say: You sign the lease on the crooked little shop by the harbour and hang the brass key back round your neck. In the autumn, the sign will read your name.\n> unseen: Answer the Office of Unseen Affairs\n  hint: A desk at an address that doesn't exist.\n  when: flag(offer_unseen)\n  flag took_unseen\n  mood +small\n  say: You write one word on a slip of paper and leave it under your pillow. In the morning it's gone. In the autumn, a desk at the Office of Unseen Affairs will be waiting.\n> trading: Take the shipping house's desk\n  hint: A partner's share of a harbour ledger.\n  when: flag(offer_trading)\n  flag took_trading\n  mood +small\n  say: You sign for the old partner's desk and his share of the ledger. The shipping house's anchor is pressed into the wax beside your name.\n> teaching: Stay at Lumenhold to teach\n  hint: The faculty barge, on the wrong side of the lectern.\n  when: flag(offer_teaching)\n  flag took_teaching\n  mood +small\n  say: You sign the faculty's contract in the staff room, at a desk with somebody else's initials carved in it. In the autumn you'll be on the faculty barge.\n> circus: Join the Circus of Wonders\n  hint: Wagons, crowds and illusions.\n  when: flag(offer_circus)\n  flag took_circus\n  mood +small\n  say: You sign the ringmaster's contract. In a week the circus wagons pass the village, and they'll slow down for you, and this time they'll stop.\n> observatory: Go back to the plateau\n  hint: Salaried chartmaker for the Observatory Expedition.\n  when: flag(offer_observatory)\n  flag took_observatory\n  mood +small\n  say: You sign for a salaried place with the Observatory Expedition and pack the frostbite remedies first. The plateau, and its nights, are yours from the autumn.\n> field_station: Take the keeper's cottage\n  hint: The Beastcraft Field Station, on the moors.\n  when: flag(offer_field_station)\n  flag took_field_station\n  mood +small\n  say: You sign for the keeper's post at the Beastcraft Field Station: a cottage, a lamp and more creatures than anyone could count.\n> thornwick: Cross the hills to Thornwick\n  hint: A researcher's bench at the rival academy.\n  when: flag(offer_thornwick)\n  flag took_thornwick\n  mood +small\n  say: You sign Thornwick's contract in green ink. In the autumn you'll cross the hills, and Lumenhold will say something cutting, and you'll go anyway.\n> spire: Stay on in the Spire's stacks\n  hint: A keeper of the stacks, full time.\n  when: flag(offer_spire)\n  flag took_spire\n  mood +small\n  say: You leave your signature under the bell on the reference desk. In the autumn you'll be back behind it, with a longer ladder.\n> newt: Stay on at the Gilded Newt\n  hint: Behind the bar, then in charge of it.\n  when: flag(offer_newt)\n  flag took_newt\n  mood +small\n  say: You shake on it over the bar with the landlady, properly this time. Full time from the autumn, and in charge in a year or two.\n> pell: Stay on at Pell & Daughters\n  hint: The counter, and one day the drawers.\n  when: flag(offer_pell)\n  flag took_pell\n  mood +small\n  say: You sign Pell's ledger under his daughters' names. Old Pell stops counting long enough to watch you do it, then starts again from one.\n> courier: Keep your courier's satchel\n  hint: Flying the lake in all weathers.\n  when: flag(offer_courier)\n  flag took_courier\n  mood +small\n  say: You hang the satchel with your name on it back on its peg, where it will be in the autumn, and the autumn after that.\n> clerk: Take the registrar's clerk's desk\n  hint: Stamps, ledgers and a window on a wall.\n  when: flag(offer_clerk)\n  flag took_clerk\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You sign for the clerk's desk at the registrar's window. It pays the rent. It is a start date. Today that is enough.\n> look: {has_offer(): Sign none of them, and keep looking|Keep looking}\n  hint: {gpa >= 2.5 or renown >= 30: Your grades and your name will open doors, in time.|Without a post, it's home for a while.}\n  flag took_none\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {gpa >= 2.5 or renown >= 30: You put the pen down. Your grades are good and people know your name; the world can make you a better offer in its own time. You'll rent a room and write letters.|You put the pen down, and the letter you'll write home is the hardest you've ever had to write. There's a room there, and a bed that's too short, and time.}\n\n@event last_lake\ntitle: The Lake, One Last Time\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 80\nart: wave\ntags: story mystery\nscene: A student standing alone at the end of a wooden jetty at dawn, the Mirror Lake perfectly still, the towers reflected upside down in it.\ntext: You walk down to the lake before breakfast, the way you did your first morning. {flag(hollow_sealed): It's clearer than you've ever seen it, clear all the way down, and nothing looks back. You did that. Almost nobody knows.|{flag(hollow_freed): It has been different since the spring it froze, warmer somehow, and the candles along the shore lean toward you as you pass. You did that too.|{flag(hollow_bargain): The water is still. The mark on your palm is not. It hums as you come near, like something greeting something.|{flag(hollow_fled): The water looks ordinary. It isn't. Somewhere under it a door stands ajar, because you ran, and the headmistress's hair has been white since that night.|{flag(hollow_held_line): The first-years you held the tower for are skimming stones off the jetty. One of them waves at you. Whatever happened under the ice that night, it happened without you, and they are alive.|{flag(hollow_failed): It's calm this morning. You know what lies beneath it, and who paid to keep it closed. You stand on the jetty a long time.|{clues(hollowstar) >= 1: There were stories about this lake, and you followed some of them a little way. You never learned what they meant, or whether they meant anything. The lake isn't telling.|It's flat and blue and ordinary, and the towers stand upside down in it. Four years, and it never once did anything strange. That you saw.}}}}}}}\n> ok: Say goodbye to the lake\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You skim one stone. It skips seven times, which is your record, and nobody saw.|You crouch and put your hand in the water. It's cold, the way it always was.} Then you walk back up to the castle without looking round.\n\n@event last_friends\ntitle: The Last Ordinary Morning\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 75\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 15 and not tagged(friend, outsider)\nart: cup\ntags: story social\nscene: Two students sitting on the rim of a courtyard fountain with mugs of tea, packed trunks stacked by the doors behind them.\ntext: {friend} finds you in the courtyard with two mugs of tea and no particular reason. \"Last ordinary morning,\" {they} say{s}. \"I thought it should be spent properly.\" {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3: The others drift over one by one, the friends who stayed, until the fountain's rim is full.|You sit on the rim of the fountain and don't talk about tomorrow.}\n> left: Promise to visit, and mean it\n  hint: {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3: The yearbook will remember the ones who stayed.|A date, a place and a time.}\n  flag kept_friends\n  rel friend +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You set a date, a real one, with a place and a time, and write it in every notebook round the fountain. {friend} underlines it twice. You all know which of you will be late.\n> right: Say goodbye properly, here\n  hint: A clean farewell, and no promises to break.\n  rel friend +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You give {friend} the thing on your desk you'd most miss: a cracked mug, a dog-eared book, the stone from the lake. No promises; you've both seen what happens to promises. {They} turn{s} it over and over and say{s} nothing, which says enough.\n\n@event last_partner\ntitle: After Tomorrow\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 72\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\"\nper: partner\nart: heart\ntags: story romance\nscene: Two students sitting on a castle battlement at noon, legs dangling over the edge, a map of the realm spread between them, weighted with an apple.\ntext: {partner} has a map, an apple and the whole afternoon. \"Tomorrow we're not students,\" {they} say{s}. {flag(after_together): \"We said we'd go together. I still mean it. Do you?\"|\"So I thought we'd better have a plan. Or decide that we don't have one.\"} The map is spread on the battlement between you, weighted at one corner with the apple.\n> left: Plan the first year together\n  hint: Whatever comes, together.\n  spark partner +small\n  mood +small\n  queue last_partner now\n  say: You plan it badly and in detail: a town on the map, a room above a shop, whose turn it is to cook. {partner} draws a circle round the town in ink, so it can't be rubbed out.\n> right: Part at the gate tomorrow\n  hint: Kindly, and for good.\n  bond partner ex\n  mood -medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  queue last_partner now\n  say: You tell {partner} the truth: that tomorrow you'll walk out of the gates in different directions, and that it's right. {They} fold{s} the map very carefully and give{s} you the apple. Neither of you eats it.\n\n@event last_rival\ntitle: One Last Word\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 70\ncast:\n  rival: any where bond(rival) == \"rival\" or bond(rival) == \"nemesis\"\nart: wand\ntags: story social\nscene: Two students facing each other in an empty duelling hall, wands holstered, packed trunks visible through the open door behind them.\ntext: {rival} is waiting in the empty duelling hall, as if by appointment. Four years of grudges, and tomorrow there won't be a school to have them in. \"Well,\" {they} say{s}. \"That's that, then.\"\n> left: Offer your hand\n  rel rival +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: {rival} looks at your hand for a long moment, then takes it, hard. \"You were worth beating,\" {they} say{s}, and then, almost too quietly to hear, \"and worth losing to.\"\n> right: \"Rematch, in ten years.\"\n  rel rival +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: \"Ten years,\" says {rival}, \"and I'll still win.\" You shake on it in front of the last of the Circle, who spread it round the year by supper. It's the closest either of you will come to saying anything kind.\n\n@event last_starfall\ntitle: The Last Starfall\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns\npriority: 62\nart: star\ntags: festival story\nscene: Final-year students in a row on the observatory roof under a sky full of falling stars, graduation robes thrown over their shoulders like blankets.\ntext: Tonight is Starfall, and by tradition the final-years have the observatory roof to themselves. The first-years are down on the lawns with their jars. Up here, everyone is pretending not to be sad.\n> left: Catch one for the road\n  challenge feat apprentice slim luck:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: It drops into your hands with a hiss, the last thing Lumenhold gives you. The whole roof cheers. You'll keep it in its jar on a windowsill somewhere you haven't seen yet.\n    lose:\n      mood +tiny\n      say: You lunge, miss, and sit down hard, and the whole roof laughs, and you laugh too. Some things you're supposed to leave behind.\n> right: Lie back with everyone\n  mood +medium\n  say: You lie in a row with your year, shoulder to shoulder, and count the stars aloud until the counting falls apart. Somebody starts to cry, and then everybody is laughing.\n\n@event last_dumonts\ntitle: Two Pale Heads\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns and met(kael) and (flag(dumont_reunited) or flag(ilse_taken)) and not flag(ilse_went_north)\npriority: 71\nart: snow\ntags: story social\nscene: A photographer's tripod on the great hall steps, graduates jostling into rows, a pale young man at the end of a row beside an empty space.\ntext: {flag(dumont_reunited): The photographer is herding the year onto the great hall steps. At the end of a row, two pale heads lean together, Kael and Ilse, who was raised to open the door he refused. Kael sees you and moves up, which leaves a space between them and the edge.|The photographer is herding the year onto the great hall steps. At the end of a row Kael stands beside a space nobody is allowed to fill. Ilse should be in it. The man in grey took her north, and no letter has come.}\n> left: {flag(dumont_reunited): Squeeze into the photograph with them|Leave the space empty with him}\n  hint: The yearbook will keep this.\n  rel kael +small\n  if flag(dumont_reunited):\n    flag kept_dumonts\n    say: You squeeze in beside them, and Ilse elbows you, and Kael, for once, laughs out loud just as the shutter goes. It's the only photograph of the three of you, and it hangs in two houses for the rest of your lives.\n  else:\n    flag kept_ilse_chair\n    say: You stand at the edge of the space, so it stays a space, and Kael's hand finds your shoulder for a moment. In the photograph there's a gap in the row the size of a girl in white furs.\n> right: {flag(dumont_reunited): Take the photograph yourself|Talk Kael into closing the gap}\n  hint: Today, and not the yearbook.\n  mood +small\n  if flag(dumont_reunited):\n    say: You take the camera from the photographer and make them stand still for once. Two Dumonts, squinting at the sun, in nobody's photograph but yours.\n  else:\n    rel kael +small\n    say: \"She'd hate being a gap,\" you tell him, and he almost smiles, and moves up. In the photograph the row closes. Neither of you forgets why it was open.\n\n@event last_tamsin\ntitle: A Page for Thornwick\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns and flag(tamsin_friends) and rel(tamsin) >= 15\npriority: 69\nart: letter\ntags: story letter\nscene: A yearbook page and a sheet of green-sealed paper side by side on a desk, a student holding a pen over both.\ntext: There's a space in the corner of your yearbook page where a friend's message should go, and a green-sealed letter on your desk that came this morning: \"Four years of rivalry, four hundred letters. Leave me the corner. Insufferable to the end, T.\"\n> left: Leave Tamsin the corner\n  hint: The yearbook will keep this.\n  flag kept_tamsin\n  rel tamsin +small\n  say: You send the page over the hills by the evening owl. It comes back by morning, a green seal pressed into the corner, which is not allowed, and two lines you'll know by heart for the rest of your life.\n> right: Save it for the spring meeting\n  hint: Biscuits, halfway between, every spring.\n  mood +small\n  say: You write back that the corner can wait: you'll fill it in person, at the town halfway between, in the spring, with biscuits. Tamsin's reply says \"Coward\" and \"Yes\" in the same line.\n\n@event last_remembered\ntitle: What the Yearbook Keeps\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: turn == max_turns and ((renown >= 45) or (alignment >= 60) or (alignment <= -60) or flag(thesis_hollowstar))\npriority: 61\ntype: menu\npick: 2\nart: book\ntags: story\nscene: A yearbook committee's table in the library, proofs of portrait pages spread out, a student reading their own page upside down.\ntext: The yearbook committee has your page in proof, with the lines they mean to print under your portrait. You may let them stand or strike them out. \"Two at most,\" says the editor, a second-year with ink on her nose. \"We're short of room.\"\n> renown: Let them print your fame\n  hint: The most talked-about student in a generation.\n  when: renown >= 45\n  flag remember_renown\n  say: You leave the line about the Ledger's special edition. The editor looks relieved; it was her favourite.\n> lumen: Let them print the line about you\n  hint: \"The one you went to when it mattered.\"\n  when: alignment >= 60\n  flag remember_lumen\n  say: Someone has written \"The one you went to when it mattered.\" You don't know who. You leave it.\n> hollow: Let the shadow stand\n  hint: The candles behind your portrait went out.\n  when: alignment <= -60\n  flag remember_hollow\n  say: In the proof, the candles behind your portrait have gone out. The editor offers to retouch it. You tell her to leave it as it is.\n> thesis: Keep the line about your thesis\n  hint: The thesis they locked away.\n  when: flag(thesis_hollowstar)\n  flag remember_thesis\n  say: The line about your thesis on the founders' seal has been half struck through already, in the headmistress's hand. You un-strike it.\n> plain: Strike the rest\n  hint: Only the line you keep; on its own, just your name and the year.\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You strike out everything else on the page. The editor sighs and reaches for the red pen. Less, it turns out, suits you.\n"},{"path":"content/events/life.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Academy life — the random pool. Small moments that fill the\n#  month: weather, food, money, mischief, mishaps.\n#  Seasonal ones use `month` (1 Harvestmoon ... 12 Starfall).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Around the castle ----------------\n\n@event life_staircase\ntitle: The Staircase Moves\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: tower\ntags: magic\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nscene: A grand spiral staircase in mid-rotation, a student clinging to the banister as the steps swing toward a different floor.\ntext: Halfway up the west staircase, it decides to be somewhere else. You're now heading toward a floor you've never seen, at speed, and you're late for class.\n> left: Hold on and see where it goes\n  lore +tiny\n  chance even:\n    gold +small\n    say: It deposits you in a dusty forgotten classroom. In a desk: eight old crowns and a love letter from 1802.\n  else:\n    study -tiny\n    say: It takes you to the roof. Then the cellar. Then the roof again. You miss your morning lectures entirely.\n> right: Jump for the landing\n  challenge feat novice+ wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: You leap and land neatly. A passing portrait applauds.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You leap and land, but not neatly. A passing portrait winces.\n\n@event life_mystery_stew\ntime: evening\ntitle: Mystery Stew\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A huge cauldron of grey-green stew in the dining hall, a ladle standing upright in it unaided.\ntext: Tonight's dinner is described on the board only as \"Stew\". It is grey-green. The ladle is standing up by itself. The cook is watching you with hope.\n> left: Eat it bravely\n  chance likely:\n    health +small\n    mood +small\n    say: It's... delicious? Rich, peppery, faintly glowing. You go back for seconds. The cook beams.\n  else:\n    health -medium\n    say: It's not delicious. It's not anything a stomach should know about. You spend the night regretting it.\n> right: Toast and jam again\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You eat toast. The cook looks hurt. You feel guilty all evening.\n\n@event life_lost_wand\ntitle: Where Is Your Wand?\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: wand\ntags: magic\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nscene: A dormitory turned upside down, clothes everywhere, a student on hands and knees peering under a bed.\ntext: Your wand is gone. It was on your desk. Now it isn't. Classes start in twenty minutes.\n> left: Tear the room apart\n  chance likely:\n    say: It's in your boot. Why is it in your boot? You make it to class with seconds to spare.\n  else:\n    study -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You find it at lunch, inside the pillowcase. You've missed a whole morning.\n> right: Try a summoning charm\n  challenge spell novice+:\n    win:\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: It flies into your hand from inside the wardrobe, offended at having been lost.\n    lose:\n      mana -tiny\n      say: Every pencil in the dormitory flies at your face. The wand stays hidden out of spite.\n\n@event life_snoring_roommate\ntitle: The Snorer\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A dark dormitory, one bed shaking with snores so loud the curtains flutter, another occupant glaring with a pillow over their head.\ntext: Someone in your dormitory snores like a dragon with a head cold, and nobody will admit to it. You haven't slept properly in a week.\n> left: Brew a silencing draught\n  challenge brew apprentice-:\n    win:\n      health +small\n      alchemy +tiny\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: Perfect silence. Too perfect. The culprit, the quiet one by the window, is now silent even when awake. It wears off by Tuesday. You never quite ask their permission.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: The draught finds the wrong bed. Now two people snore, in harmony.\n> right: Move to the common room sofa\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You sleep on the sofa and meet everyone who comes down at 3am. Some of them are very interesting.\n\n@event life_portrait_gossip\ntitle: The Portraits Talk\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: mirror\ntags: mystery\nscene: A long gallery of gilded portraits, painted figures leaning out of their frames toward each other, whispering behind painted hands.\ntext: Two portraits in the east gallery are gossiping about a student. Then one of them says your name and they both go suspiciously quiet.\n> left: Bribe them with flattery\n  challenge appeal novice+:\n    win:\n      guile +tiny\n      lore +tiny\n      say: Ten minutes of compliments on their brushwork, and they tell you everything. Gossip about half the faculty, too.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: \"Flattery,\" sniffs the duchess, \"is so common.\" They turn their backs. Literally.\n> right: Pretend you didn't hear\n  say: You walk on. Behind you, the whispering resumes, louder.\n\n@event life_pixies\ntitle: Pixie Infestation\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: sparkle\ntags: danger\nscene: A dormitory full of tiny blue winged creatures swinging from curtains, stealing socks and pulling hair.\ntext: Someone left a window open and the dormitory is now full of pixies. They're stealing socks, unscrewing bedposts and laughing in very high voices.\n> left: Fight them off\n  challenge feat apprentice- wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +tiny\n      renown +small\n      say: You catch nine with a laundry bag. The dorm cheers. The pixies swear at you, colourfully.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You lose. The pixies hang you from the chandelier by your robe. It takes an hour to get down.\n> right: Bribe them with sugar\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You lay a trail of sugar cubes out of the window. The pixies follow it, munching, and leave one sock as a tip.\n\n@pool life_windfall\nclass: windfall\nweight: 5\n\n@event life_gargoyle\ntitle: A Talkative Gargoyle\npool: life_windfall\ntone: light\nart: tower\ntags: magic\nscene: A weathered stone gargoyle on a parapet, one eye open, mouth moving, a student sitting beside it with a sandwich.\ntext: The gargoyle above the east door clears its throat as you pass. \"You,\" it rumbles. \"You look like someone who'd listen. Nobody listens to gargoyles.\"\n> left: Hurry along\n  say: \"Typical,\" says the gargoyle. It sulks until spring.\n> right: Sit and listen\n  lore +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: It tells you three hundred years of academy gossip, including where the founders hid the good biscuits. You find the biscuits.\n\n@event life_brownies\ntime: morning\ntitle: Help in the Night\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: house\ntags: magic home\nscene: Tiny bearded figures in aprons tidying a cluttered student desk by moonlight, one darning a sock.\ntext: You wake to find your desk tidied, your socks darned and your essay proofread. Brownies. They expect something in return. Traditionally: cream.\n> left: Leave out a bowl of cream\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: Your room stays spotless all term. Your essays have suspiciously good spelling.\n> right: Leave them nothing\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The next morning, every one of your socks is tied in knots. Brownies keep score.\n\n@event life_midnight_snack\ntime: night\ntitle: Midnight Hunger\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A student in pyjamas tiptoeing past a sleeping prefect toward a door marked KITCHENS, a candle cupped in their hand.\ntext: It's midnight and you're starving. The kitchens are three floors down, past a prefect's post.\n> left: Go back to bed hungry\n  health -tiny\n  say: You dream of sandwiches. Long, detailed, beautiful sandwiches.\n> right: Sneak down\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      health +small\n      mood +small\n      say: Cold pie, a pot of custard, a heel of bread. Feast of kings.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      say: The prefect is awake. You spend a week washing the pots you were trying to raid.\n\n@event life_insomnia\ntime: night\ntitle: The Ceiling at 3am\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: moon\ntags: home\nscene: A student lying awake staring at a ceiling where faint painted stars have started slowly moving.\ntext: You can't sleep. Your brain has decided to replay every embarrassing thing you've said since you were six.\n> left: Get up and study\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: By dawn you've read three chapters and remembered one of them.\n> right: Chamomile, and count the stars\n  gold -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Halfway through counting, the stars start counting back. Then you're asleep.\n\n@pool life_routine\nclass: routine\nweight: 5\n\n@event life_morning_run\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Dawn Run\npool: life_routine\ntone: light\nart: sun\ntags: nature\nwhen: month <= 3 or month >= 7\nscene: The lakeshore path at dawn, mist on the water, a lone runner's breath steaming, the towers catching the first gold light.\ntext: A group of students runs the lakeshore every dawn. They've invited you. It's very early. It's very cold.\n> left: Sleep in\n  mood +small\n  say: You hear them come back, glowing and smug. You are warm and unrepentant.\n> right: Run with them\n  vigor +small\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You nearly die at the first mile and feel immortal by the third. The lake shines like a coin. You feel every step of it all through your lectures.\n\n@event life_homework_mountain\ntitle: The Pile\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nscene: A desk buried under a precarious tower of scrolls and books, a student peeking hopelessly over the top.\ntext: Three essays, a potion log, a star chart and a reading list the length of your arm. All due this week. The pile has achieved its own weather.\n> left: Grind through it\n  study +tiny\n  health -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You finish at 4am on the last day. Every piece is handed in. You sleep through breakfast.\n> right: Pick what matters, skip the rest\n  study -tiny\n  gain slacking\n  mood +small\n  say: You hand in two of five and go for a walk. The professors are not pleased. You feel great, temporarily.\n\n@event life_stolen_answers\ntitle: The Answers\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study danger\nwhen: month == 3 or month == 4 or month == 9 or month == 10\nscene: A folded paper being passed under a library table, the faint glow of an examiner's seal on it.\ntext: A third-year is selling copies of next week's exam papers. Eight crowns for the set. \"Guaranteed real,\" he whispers. \"Straight from the examiner's desk.\"\n> left: Walk away\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Two weeks later, five students who bought copies are suspended. The answers were fake.\n> right: Buy a copy\n  alignment -small\n  gold -small\n  chance unlikely:\n    study +medium\n    guile +tiny\n    say: They're real. You know every question before you sit down. You also can't quite look the examiner in the eye.\n  else:\n    study -medium\n    gain detention\n    say: They're fake, and planted. You're caught with a copy. Detention, and a very cold letter home.\n\n@event life_backfire\ntitle: Spell Backfire\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: sparkle\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A student mid-hiccup, a small firework bursting from their mouth, classmates ducking.\ntext: You mispronounce a minor cheering charm in the corridor. Something goes wrong. Every time you hiccup, a tiny firework comes out.\n> left: Go to the infirmary\n  gold -tiny\n  say: Sister Amaranth cures it in a minute and charges you three crowns. \"For the ceiling,\" she says.\n> right: Live with it\n  gain hiccup_hex\n  renown +small\n  say: You become briefly famous as \"the sparkler\". It's not the fame you wanted.\n\n@event life_lake_swim\ntitle: A Swim in the Lake\nclass: story\ntone: light\nart: wave\ntags: nature\nwhen: month >= 9 or month <= 1\nscene: Students leaping off a wooden jetty into a sparkling lake on a hot afternoon, something large and dark moving far beneath the surface.\ntext: It's hot and the whole year has gone down to the jetty. Something large is rumoured to live in the deep part. People are swimming anyway.\n> left: Stay on the jetty\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You dangle your feet and eat cherries. Something brushes your toe. You keep your feet up after that.\n> right: Dive in\n  health +small\n  mood +medium\n  chance slim:\n    flag lake_knows\n    say: In the deep part, you open your eyes underwater and see a round door, far below, glowing. Then you need air.\n  else:\n    say: The water's perfect. Something large and friendly swims under you, and you decide not to panic.\n\n@event life_stray_cat\ntitle: A Cat Moves In\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: cat\ntags: home\nscene: A scruffy black cat sitting on a dormitory pillow as if it has always lived there, one torn ear, very green eyes.\ntext: A black cat with one torn ear is sitting on your pillow. It looks at you as though you are the one trespassing.\n> left: Shoo it out\n  say: It leaves with enormous dignity and moves in with your neighbour, who spoils it rotten.\n> right: Let it stay\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: It's called Professor now. It sleeps on your homework. You've never been happier.\n\n@event life_storm_night\ntitle: The Lights Go Out\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: bolt\ntags: mystery\nwhen: month >= 6 and month <= 8\nscene: A dark common room lit only by lightning, students huddled under blankets, one telling a ghost story.\ntext: A storm knocks out every light-crystal in the dormitory. The common room fills with students, blankets and somebody's terrible ghost story.\n> left: Tell an even scarier story\n  challenge perform apprentice-:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: Your story is so good that three people sleep with the lights on for a week. You're a legend.\n    lose:\n      mood +tiny\n      say: Your story is not scary. It is, however, accidentally hilarious.\n> right: Light the room with a spell\n  challenge spell novice+:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: You fill the room with soft floating lights. Someone starts singing. It turns into the best night of term.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: You light the sofa. Briefly. The storm seems less frightening after that.\n\n@event life_mirror_talks\ntime: morning\ntitle: The Honest Mirror\npool: life_routine\ntone: light\nart: mirror\ntags: magic\nscene: A bathroom mirror with a stern painted face in its silvering, frowning at a student mid-toothbrush.\ntext: The bathroom mirror has started commenting on your appearance. \"Hair,\" it says this morning. Just: \"Hair.\"\n> left: Ask for its advice\n  charm +small\n  say: It turns out to be an excellent stylist. You look great. You hate that it's right.\n> right: Cover it with a towel\n  mood +tiny\n  say: It sulks under the towel. Muffled: \"Hair.\"\n\n@event life_prank_war\ntitle: Prank War\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: mask\ntags: social\nscene: A corridor ankle-deep in enchanted soap bubbles, two dormitories' worth of students hurling them at each other.\ntext: The dormitory across the corridor turned your door into a goat. The goat is fine. Honour, however, demands retaliation.\n> left: Rise above it\n  study -tiny\n  say: You take the high road. The goat eats your homework. You let it.\n> right: Retaliate\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: You fill their dorm with a thousand singing bubbles. They concede by dawn.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      renown +tiny\n      say: You're caught carrying a crate of bubbles. Detention, but the goat seems proud of you.\n> up[glamour]: Glamour their door into a goat\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You return the compliment with a glamour: their door becomes a goat, an extremely convincing one, which bleats when knocked. It lasts three days. Nobody can prove a thing.\n\n@event life_mail_owl\ntitle: The Wrong Letter\npool: life_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: letter\ntags: mystery\nscene: A ruffled delivery owl dropping a scented pink envelope onto a breakfast plate, a student looking up in confusion.\ntext: A delivery owl drops a scented pink letter in your porridge. It's addressed to someone else. It's a love letter. It's very, very passionate.\n> left: Deliver it to its owner\n  charm +tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  say: The recipient goes crimson and thanks you. Two weeks later, they're holding hands with the sender in the dining hall.\n> right: Read it first\n  alignment -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You read it. You will never be able to look the potions professor in the eye again.\n\n# ---------------- Money ----------------\n\n@event life_money_from_home\ntitle: A Parcel From Home\npool: life_windfall\ntone: warm\nart: pouch\ntags: home money\nwhen: turn >= 2\nscene: A brown paper parcel tied with string on a dormitory bed, a knitted scarf, a jar of jam and a small coin purse spilling out.\ntext: A parcel from home: a knitted scarf, a jar of jam, a note telling you to eat vegetables, and a small purse of coins.\n> left: Save the money\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You tuck it away. The jam lasts six days.\n> right: Spend it on a treat for your friends\n  gold +tiny\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Cake from the village for everyone. You're popular for an entire afternoon.\n\n@event life_found_purse\ntitle: A Purse in the Grass\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: pouch\ntags: money\nscene: A velvet purse lying in the long grass beside a path, coins glinting at its open mouth, initials embroidered on it.\ntext: A fat velvet purse lies in the grass by the path. Thirty crowns at least. Embroidered initials: A.T.\n> left: Return it to its owner\n  alignment +small\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: A.T. turns out to be Aldric Thorne. He takes the purse and stares at you a long moment. \"Honest,\" he says. It sounds like a diagnosis.\n> right: Keep it\n  alignment -small\n  gold +large\n  guile +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Thirty crowns richer. A few days later, Thorne mentions in class that he lost something. He looks at no one in particular.\n\n@event life_peddler\ntitle: The Travelling Peddler\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: lantern\ntags: money magic\nscene: A gaudy painted wagon at the academy gates, hung with lanterns and trinkets, a peddler with a gold tooth grinning.\ntext: A peddler's wagon has parked by the gates. \"Lucky charms! Genuine article! Thirty crowns, and fortune follows you for a season!\"\n> left: Buy a lucky charm\n  hint: 30 gold\n  gold -large\n  chance even:\n    gain inspired\n    mood +small\n    say: It's a real one. Things go right all month. You start talking to it.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: It turns your neck green. Fortune does not follow you. The peddler is long gone.\n> right: Just browse\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You buy nothing and enjoy a sugared apple and the peddler's outrageous stories.\n\n@event life_bookshop\ntitle: Sale at Grimsby's\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: money study\nscene: A crooked bookshop with books stacked to the ceiling, a hand-painted sign in the window reading HALF OFF, MOSTLY.\ntext: Grimsby's Books is having a sale. An old copy of \"Advanced Arcane Theory\" for fifteen crowns. Normally forty. It's annotated, apparently, by a famous wizard.\n> left: Buy it\n  hint: 15 gold\n  gold -medium\n  lore +medium\n  arcana +small\n  say: The annotations are brilliant, rude and occasionally about the author's divorce. You learn a great deal.\n> right: Leave it\n  say: You walk away. You think about it for weeks.\n\n@event life_new_robes\ntitle: Shabby Robes\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: crown\ntags: money social\nwhen: renown >= 10\nscene: A student in a hall mirror holding up a fraying sleeve, a tailor's flyer in their other hand.\ntext: Your robes are fraying at every hem. Someone at dinner called them \"vintage\". It was not a compliment.\n> left: Patch them yourself\n  charm +tiny\n  say: Your stitching is crooked but cheerful. Some people start copying it.\n> right: Buy new robes (30 gold)\n  when: gold >= 30\n  hint: 30 gold\n  gold -large\n  renown +small\n  charm +small\n  say: Midnight-blue wool, silver trim. People actually turn to look. You walk differently all week.\n\n@event life_card_game\ntime: night\ntitle: A Game of Wizard's Hand\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: cards\ntags: money social\nscene: A smoky corner of the common room, students around a low table with cards and coins, one card floating and changing face.\ntext: A late-night game of Wizard's Hand. The cards change faces when no one's looking. The pot is thirty crowns. The upper-years look hungry.\n> left: Play\n  when: gold >= 15\n  challenge deceive apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      say: You read every bluff and keep a straight face. Thirty crowns and a reputation.\n    lose:\n      gold -medium\n      say: You lose fifteen crowns and the upper-years' respect, in that order.\n> right: Watch and learn\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You learn more watching than you would have playing. Mostly about who cheats.\n\n@event life_essay_prize\ntitle: The Essay Prize\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: feather\ntags: study money\nscene: A brass plaque on a noticeboard announcing THE LUMEN ESSAY PRIZE — 30 CROWNS, a quill pinned beneath it.\ntext: The Lumen Essay Prize: thirty crowns for the best essay on \"Magic and Duty\". The deadline is Friday. You have an idea already.\n> left: Enter\n  study -tiny\n  challenge research adept-:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      renown +medium\n      say: The essay eats your week, and wins. Your essay is read aloud at dinner. You don't know where to look.\n    lose:\n      lore +tiny\n      say: The essay eats your week, and doesn't win, but the judges' notes are glowing. Next year, maybe.\n> right: Skip it\n  mood +tiny\n  say: The winner reads their essay aloud at dinner. Yours would have been better. Probably. You had a very pleasant week instead.\n\n@event life_charity\ntitle: The Winter Fund\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: money social\nwhen: month >= 3 and month <= 5\nscene: A collection jar shaped like a lantern on the dining hall table, half full of coins, a hand-lettered sign: FOR THE VILLAGE WINTER FUND.\ntext: The village winter fund is collecting for families who can't afford fuel this winter. The jar is half full.\n> left: Donate fifteen crowns\n  hint: 15 gold\n  alignment +small\n  gold -medium\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Your name goes on a little list by the jar. You feel warm, which is the point.\n> right: Walk past\n  if gold >= 15:\n    alignment -tiny\n  say: {gold >= 15: You walk past. The jar fills anyway. You think about it on cold nights.|You have nothing to give this year. The jar fills anyway. You think about it on cold nights.}\n\n@event life_shady_offer\ntime: afternoon evening\ntitle: A Quiet Job\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: mask\ntags: money danger\nscene: A hooded figure in an alley behind the village tavern holding out a sealed envelope, a purse visible in their other hand.\ntext: A hooded figure behind the tavern: \"Deliver this envelope to the boathouse tonight. Don't open it. Thirty crowns.\" The envelope is warm. Under the hood, a glint of pince-nez, and teeth: this is one of Grisk's errands.\n> left: Refuse\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The figure melts into the dark. The next morning, someone's boat is missing, and you wonder what was in the envelope, and whether you could have stopped it.\n> right: Do the job\n  gold +large\n  alignment -small\n  flag grisk_contact\n  find lead_grisk_jetty\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain watched\n    say: You make the drop. Bram is watching from the boathouse window and writes something down. On the jetty, a note in a neat hand: \"Good work. Midnight, any month. G.\"\n  else:\n    guile +tiny\n    say: You make the drop. Nobody sees. Thirty crowns, a cold feeling in your stomach, and a note on the jetty: \"Good work. Midnight, any month. G.\"\n\n# ---------------- Body & mind ----------------\n\n@event life_food_poisoning\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Fish Was Off\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\nart: meal\ntags: crisis\nscene: A student curled on a dormitory bed clutching their stomach, a half-eaten fish pie on the bedside table.\ntext: The fish pie at dinner was a mistake. Your stomach is staging a revolution.\n> left: Ride it out\n  health -medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: A miserable night. You never look at fish pie again.\n> right: Get a tonic from the infirmary\n  gold -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: The tonic tastes like old pennies, but it works.\n\n@event life_self_doubt\ntitle: Imposter\npool: life_windfall\ntone: warm\nart: mirror\ntags: home\nscene: A student sitting alone on the edge of a fountain at night, staring at their reflection broken up by ripples.\ntext: Everyone around you seems to belong here more than you do. They cast faster, learn quicker, laugh easier. Maybe the letter was a mistake.\n> left: Write down everything you've learned\n  mood +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: The list is longer than you expected. Much longer. You pin it above your bed.\n> right: Talk to someone about it\n  if bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0:\n    mood +large\n    say: Your friend laughs — not at you. \"You? Everyone thinks you're the one who belongs.\" It helps more than you can say.\n  else:\n    mood +small\n    say: You talk to the night porter. He tells you everyone feels this way. You decide to believe him.\n\n@event life_sleepwalking\ntime: night\ntitle: Sleepwalking\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: moon\ntags: mystery\nscene: A student in pyjamas standing on a moonlit battlement, eyes closed, one foot raised over the edge, an owl watching.\ntext: You wake up on the battlements in your pyjamas, at the very edge, with no memory of how you got there. Your feet are wet with lake water.\n> left: Tell Sister Amaranth\n  gold -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  say: A sleeping draught and a stern warning. It doesn't happen again.\n> right: Keep it to yourself\n  mood -tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    flag lake_knows\n    say: It happens twice more. Each time you're facing the lake. Each time, you're a little closer to the water.\n  else:\n    say: It doesn't happen again. You start locking your door from the inside, just in case.\n\n@event life_quiet_afternoon\nslot: afternoon\ntitle: A Quiet Afternoon\npool: life_windfall\ntone: light\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A student lying in the long grass under an oak tree, a book open on their chest, clouds drifting above the towers.\ntext: For once, nothing is due, nobody needs you, and the sun is out. A whole afternoon, unclaimed.\n> left: Nap under the oak tree\n  health +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You wake to find a bird sitting on your book. It seems content. So are you.\n> right: Practise spells in the meadow\n  arcana +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You make dandelion seeds dance in spirals for an hour. Nobody sees. It's perfect.\n\n@event life_injury_stairs\ntitle: The Trick Step\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: cross\ntags: danger\nscene: A stone staircase with one step glowing faintly, a student mid-fall, books flying everywhere.\ntext: The ninth step on the north stair is a trick step. Everyone knows. You forgot. Your leg sinks to the knee and the stair holds on.\n> left: Wait for someone to help\n  study -tiny\n  say: Forty minutes later, a first-year pulls you out. You're late for everything.\n> right: Wrench yourself free\n  challenge feat apprentice-:\n    win:\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: You pull free with a pop. Your shoe stays in the stair forever.\n    lose:\n      gain injured\n      health -small\n      say: Something in your knee goes click. Not a good click.\n\n@event life_lake_fever\ntitle: Lake Fever Season\npool: life_routine\ntone: grim\nart: rain\ntags: crisis\nwhen: month >= 3 and month <= 6\nscene: A dormitory where half the beds hold sniffling students with faintly blue-glowing noses, tissues everywhere.\ntext: Half your dormitory has Lake Fever. Noses are glowing blue. You feel a tickle in your throat.\n> left: Dose up on Maud's tea\n  if met(maud):\n    health +small\n    say: Maud's lemon-and-thunder tea keeps you upright through the whole outbreak.\n  else:\n    chance even:\n      gain sick\n      say: The tea from the kitchens isn't the same. Your nose starts glowing on Thursday.\n    else:\n      say: You dodge it. Somehow.\n> right: Keep going as normal\n  chance likely:\n    gain sick\n    say: You keep going as normal for exactly two days.\n  else:\n    vigor +tiny\n    say: Iron constitution. You sail through. Everyone hates you a little.\n\n# ---------------- Seasons ----------------\n\n@event life_first_snow\ntitle: First Snow\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: snow\ntags: nature festival\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 5\nscene: The academy courtyard blanketed in fresh snow, students mid-snowball fight, a snowman wearing a professor's hat.\ntext: The first snow of winter falls overnight and the whole academy wakes up giddy. Classes are optional, officially. Unofficially, they're cancelled by snowball.\n> left: Join the snowball war\n  mood +medium\n  vigor +tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    health -tiny\n    say: You take a snowball to the face from the headmistress herself. Her aim is terrifying.\n  else:\n    say: Your side wins. You build a snow-fort that lasts until Thawmoon.\n> right: Hot chocolate by the fire\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  say: You watch from the window, warm and smug, while the snowballs fly.\n\n@event life_spring_cleaning\ntitle: Spring Cleaning\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: broom\ntags: home\nwhen: month == 7 or month == 8\nscene: A dormitory with every window thrown open, brooms sweeping by themselves, dust swirling in sunbeams.\ntext: Spring cleaning. The brooms are enchanted, but the enchantment needs supervising. Your dorm's brooms have formed a union.\n> left: Negotiate with the brooms\n  challenge soothe novice+ wild:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: You agree to one day off a week and a polish. The dorm sparkles.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      say: The brooms do not accept your terms. They sweep you into a cupboard.\n> right: Clean by hand\n  vigor +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: An afternoon of scrubbing. It's oddly satisfying. You find three lost quills and a sandwich from Thawmoon.\n\n@event life_summer_heat\ntitle: The Heat\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: sun\ntags: nature\nwhen: month == 10 or month == 11 or month == 12\nscene: Students lying flat on the cool stone floor of a shaded colloquy, fanning themselves with exam papers.\ntext: The heat is unbearable. Even the gargoyles are sweating. The lake is crowded, the library stifling, the dormitories ovens.\n> left: Cast a cooling charm on your room\n  challenge spell apprentice-:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      renown +small\n      say: Your room becomes an icebox. People pay to visit. You let them for free. Mostly.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You make it colder. Briefly. Then hotter. Then it rains inside.\n> right: Sleep on the roof\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You sleep under the stars with half the dormitory. It's the best idea anyone's had all year.\n\n@event life_harvest_festival\nslot: evening\ntitle: The Harvest Feast\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: auto\nrepeat: 12\nart: meal\ntags: festival\nwhen: month == 1 and turn > 1\nscene: Long tables groaning under pies and roast vegetables, candles floating above, a giant pumpkin carved like the headmistress.\ntext: The Harvest Feast: pies the size of cartwheels, cider, and {&a pumpkin carved to look like the headmistress, which she pretends not to notice.|a pumpkin carved to look like Professor Thorne this year, which he pretends not to notice, badly.|a turnip carved to look like the headmistress, by popular demand. She is seen smiling at it.}\n> left: Eat until you can't move\n  health +small\n  mood +medium\n  say: {&You eat three helpings of everything. You're carried back to the dorm by friends. Worth it.|You pace yourself this year, and still lose to the pies. Worth it.|You win the cider-tasting by not falling over. Worth it.}\n> right: Help in the kitchens for extra pay\n  gold +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {&You serve pie to the entire academy. The cooks slip you coins and a whole pie of your own.|The cooks remember you and put you on gravy, a promotion. Coins, and a pie of your own.|You carve the pumpkin yourself this year. The cooks pay you double in pie and the usual in coins.}\n\n# The rivalry's first fixture (playtest 2, X9): Thornwick visits in year 1's Greenrise.\n@event life_rival_academy\ntitle: Visitors From Thornwick\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: year == 1 and month == 8 and not off_campus\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: shield\ntags: rival social\nscene: A delegation of students in green robes marching through the gates, noses raised, a Lumenhold crowd watching suspiciously.\ntext: A delegation from Thornwick Academy is visiting. Their students are smug, polished and very loudly unimpressed with Lumenhold's plumbing. {met(tamsin): One of them is scanning the crowd with a letter in their hand, and you know the handwriting.|}\n> left: Be a gracious host\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +small\n  renown +tiny\n  if met(tamsin):\n    rel tamsin +small\n    say: Tamsin finds you before you find them, and is exactly like their letters, only faster. You show them the observatory. They admit, grudgingly, that it's better than theirs.\n  else:\n    say: You show them the observatory. One of them admits, grudgingly, that it's better than theirs.\n  if knows(tamsin.sketches):\n    rel tamsin +tiny\n    say: Their illusion professor has come with them, and you recognise him at once, from a hundred goats in a hundred margins, and have to look at the floor. Tamsin is no help at all.\n> right: Defend Lumenhold's honour\n  challenge combat apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      say: You challenge their best to a friendly duel and win. The Thornwick students are quieter at dinner.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      health -small\n      say: You challenge their best to a friendly duel and lose, quite unfriendly. Thornwick will be insufferable.\n\n@event life_lost_firstyear\ntitle: A Lost First-Year\npool: life_routine\ntone: light\nart: compass\ntags: social\nwhen: month <= 3\nscene: A tiny first-year in enormous robes standing in the middle of a junction of five identical corridors, clutching a timetable upside down.\ntext: A tiny first-year is standing in the middle of the corridor, clutching an upside-down timetable and trying very hard not to cry.\n> left: Point the way and hurry on\n  say: You point. They go the wrong way anyway. You hope they're all right.\n> right: Walk them to class\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You walk them all the way and they talk the entire time. You're late, but they've decided you're their hero.\n\n@event life_frog_escape\ntitle: The Frogs Are Out\nclass: routine\ntrack: alchemy\ntone: tense\nweight: 5\nart: frog\ntags: danger\nscene: A potions classroom overrun by dozens of hopping green frogs, one sitting smugly on the professor's desk.\ntext: Someone knocked over the frog crate in Potions. Thirty frogs are loose. One has climbed into the professor's hat. He has not noticed yet.\n> left: Help round them up\n  vigor +tiny\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  say: You catch eleven, a personal best. The professor finds the hat frog at dinner. You were not involved.\n> right: Quietly let a few escape out the window\n  mood +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: Five frogs hop to freedom in the lake. You feel like a liberator.\n\n@event life_fortune_teller\ntitle: The Village Fortune-Teller\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: eye\ntags: mystery\nscene: A beaded doorway into a tiny dim tent, an old woman with a crystal ball and too many rings beckoning.\ntext: In the village, an old fortune-teller beckons you into her tent. \"Eight crowns for a good future, dearie. The bad ones I tell for free.\"\n> left: Decline politely\n  chance even:\n    mood +tiny\n    say: \"Suit yourself,\" she calls after you. \"You will meet a tall stranger.\" You already have. Several. It's an academy.\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: \"Beware of water,\" she calls after you, free of charge. She won't say more. You think about the lake a lot.\n> right: Pay eight crowns\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  say: Your future is wonderful. She's very specific. You are fairly sure she's making it up, and you don't care.\n\n@event life_volunteer\ntitle: Clean-Up Duty\npool: life_routine\ntone: warm\nart: broom\ntags: home\nscene: Students in rolled-up sleeves raking leaves on the great lawn, a groundskeeper directing them with a pitchfork.\ntext: After the storm, the grounds are a wreck. Volunteers needed to help clear branches and rebuild the hen-house. No pay. Lots of mud.\n> left: Volunteer\n  vigor +tiny\n  mood +small\n  rel maud +small\n  say: A long day of mud and laughter. An old woman in a straw hat brings everyone cocoa and remembers your name.\n> right: Stay in and study\n  study +tiny\n  say: You study through the shouting and hammering outside. The hen-house is very nice when it's done.\n"},{"path":"content/events/miniplots_2026_10.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Mini-plots (2026-10-25): short storylines of four to six cards, at the places whose visits most often came up\n#  empty (grim report --gaps: the infirmary, the lecture tower, the home town). Each is a @thread from the story\n#  template (docs/grimoire-script.md §9): its start card begins it, and every other card waits on that without saying\n#  so. Plot-safe: none of them touches the main mystery.\n#    missed_round  — the night Sister Amaranth missed her round (infirmary)\n#    red_ink       — someone marks your essays in red ink (lecture tower)\n#    late_bloomer  — the sweet shop's daughter and her late magic (home, across two breaks)\n#  Reworked after retrieval (build/batches/miniplots_2026_10.notes.md): each premise was searched and each card checked\n#  with `retrieve related --draft` against the existing cards it must not repeat.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The missed round ----------------\n\n@thread missed_round\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=flag(rounds_begun)\nclass: story\ndefer: not term\nrepeat: 1\ntags: story\nclues: lead_missed_round\nname: The missed round\npremise: Sister Amaranth never misses a round. One night her lamp stayed dark.\njournal:\n  flag(rounds_begun): You went looking for Sister Amaranth when her lamp stayed dark, and found her grey with Lake Fever.\n  flag(rounds_reported): You fetched the headmistress. Sister Amaranth didn't speak to you for a week, then told you to run faster next time.\n  flag(rounds_secret): You kept her secret and did the rounds she called out from her chair.\n  flag(rounds_covered): You covered eight beds on your own, one remedy at a time.\n  flag(bed_six_truth): Bed six owned up: a flat rock, a dare, and three friends coughing in secret. You brought them all down.\n  flag(bed_six_done) and not flag(bed_six_truth): You treated bed six for the chill she claimed, and her fever took its time.\n  flag(rounds_finished): Sister Amaranth came back a week too soon. You did the round with her, and she let you.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(rounds_finished)\nmembers:\n  start infirmary_missed_round\n\n@event infirmary_missed_round\ntitle: The Lamp That Went Out\nplace: infirmary\ntime: evening\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(amaranth) and term and turn >= 6 and not flag(rounds_begun)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 14\nart: lantern\nscene: A dim infirmary ward at evening, a fever charm over the far bed glowing a dull red, a healer's desk lamp unlit.\ntext: The fever charm over the far bed has burned down to a red glow. It should have been turned an hour ago. Sister Amaranth does every round herself, and has never once missed one. Her lamp is dark.\n> left: Go and find her\n  health -tiny\n  flag rounds_begun\n  find lead_missed_round\n  queue infirmary_sister_fevered now\n  say: You go looking through the cold back corridor in your shirtsleeves. The evening tray is still on the ward table, every bottle full. Her office door, at the back, is open a crack, and dark.\n> right: It isn't your ward\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You tell yourself she's only late. On your way out, the patient in the far bed is still awake, watching the dark.\n\n@event infirmary_sister_fevered\ntitle: The Healer's Own Fever\nthread: missed_round\ntrigger: queued\ntone: tense\nart: potion\nscene: A small office behind a hospital ward, a grey-faced healer upright in her chair with a compress on her neck and a list of patients in her lap.\ntext: You find her in the little office behind the ward, upright in her chair, a cold compress on her own neck and the patient list in her lap. Her face is grey. \"Lake Fever,\" she says. \"From bed six. It's nothing. Go to bed.\"\n> left: Fetch the Headmistress\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel amaranth -small\n  flag rounds_reported\n  queue infirmary_covering_rounds in 1\n  say: The headmistress comes in her dressing gown, and two healers from the village by morning. Sister Amaranth doesn't speak to you for a week. Then she thanks you, as an instruction: \"Next time, run faster.\"\n> right: Do the rounds she dictates\n  health -small\n  rel amaranth +small\n  flag rounds_secret\n  queue infirmary_covering_rounds in 1\n  say: \"Bed two, the blue bottle, half a spoon. Bed four, turn the charm. Bed six…\" She stops. \"Bed six will lie to you. Don't believe her.\" You do the rounds until midnight.\n\n@event infirmary_covering_rounds\ntitle: Eight Beds, One of You\nthread: missed_round\ntrigger: queued\nslot: evening\ntone: tense\nwhen: not flag(rounds_covered)\nart: cauldron\nscene: A row of infirmary beds at evening, a student in rolled-up sleeves measuring a remedy while a healer gives orders from a chair.\ntext: {flag(rounds_secret): Sister Amaranth still can't stand, and is still giving orders from her chair.|The village healers are run off their feet, and Sister Amaranth, propped up in her chair, is giving them orders too.} Eight beds, eight remedies, one of you. A second-year with a burned hand wants to know if it will scar.\n> left: Do every bed properly\n  study -tiny\n  challenge brew apprentice+:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      rel amaranth +small\n      flag rounds_covered\n      say: Every bed, every spoon, every charm on the hour. The burned hand won't scar. At midnight the chair says, \"Bed three. Well done,\" and then you hear her fall asleep sitting up.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      flag rounds_covered\n      say: You mix up beds three and five. Nobody notices but her. She makes you recite every dose on the ward back to her, with your eyes shut, before she lets you sleep.\n  queue infirmary_bed_six now\n> right: Do the urgent ones, then revise\n  rel amaranth -tiny\n  flag rounds_covered\n  queue infirmary_bed_six now\n  say: You see to the burns and the fevers and leave the rest for the morning. From the chair comes a small, disappointed silence.\n\n@event infirmary_bed_six\ntitle: Bed Six's Story\nthread: missed_round\ntrigger: queued\nslot: evening\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(rounds_covered) and not flag(bed_six_done)\nart: potion\nscene: A shivering second-year in an infirmary bed with blue-tinged ears, insisting on something, while a healer watches sceptically from a chair across the ward.\ntext: Bed six is a second-year with Lake Fever, blue to the ears, who swears she never went near the lake. \"I fell asleep in the bath,\" she says. From her chair, Sister Amaranth raises one eyebrow and keeps it there. The fever isn't breaking. A cure for a bath is not a cure for the lake.\n> left: Get the true story out of her\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      flag bed_six_truth\n      say: It comes out in a rush: the flat rock, a dare, four of them. The other three are in her dormitory, coughing into their scarves so nobody will know. You fetch them down one by one. By midnight the ward is full, and all four fevers are breaking.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: She sticks to the bath. You treat her for the lake anyway, on Sister Amaranth's say-so, and on Thursday three more second-years turn up blue to the ears.\n  flag bed_six_done\n  queue infirmary_own_prescription in 2\n> right: Treat what she tells you\n  health +tiny\n  rel amaranth -small\n  flag bed_six_done\n  queue infirmary_own_prescription in 2\n  say: You dose her for a chill and get an early night. The fever takes three days longer than it should, and the chair across the ward watches every one of them.\n\n@event infirmary_own_prescription\ntitle: Her Own Prescription\nthread: missed_round\ntrigger: queued\nslot: evening\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nwhen: flag(rounds_covered) and flag(bed_six_done)\nart: potion\nscene: A grey-faced healer in her apron holding a tray of tonics at an infirmary bedside, swaying slightly, while a student reaches to take the tray.\ntext: Sister Amaranth is up a week too soon, grey at the edges, taking the tray out for the evening round as if nothing had happened. At bed two she sways and puts a hand on the rail. {flag(bed_six_truth): Four second-years sit up in their beds to watch.|The whole ward goes quiet.} Everyone looks at you.\n> left: \"Bed.\"\n  health -small\n  rel amaranth +medium\n  flag rounds_finished\n  say: You take the tray out of her hands and say it the way she says it to everyone else. She looks at you for a long moment. Then she goes to bed. You do the round. In the morning there's a card on the tray, in her square hand: \"Bed four needs less.\"\n> right: Walk the round beside her\n  mood -tiny\n  rel amaranth +small\n  alchemy +small\n  flag rounds_finished\n  say: You don't argue. You carry the tray and have each bottle ready before she reaches for it. At the last bed she sits down on the end of it, and lets you measure out her own dose. \"Half a spoon,\" she says. \"I'm not a horse.\"\n\n# ---------------- Red ink ----------------\n\n@thread red_ink\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=flag(red_ink_begun)\nclass: story\ndefer: not term\nrepeat: 1\ntags: study\nclues: lead_red_ink\nname: Red ink\npremise: Someone unsigned keeps marking your essays in red, and they're right.\njournal:\n  flag(red_ink_begun): Someone marked your essay in red ink, unsigned, and was right. You rewrote it and left it on the stair.\n  flag(red_ink_answered): The ink asked why you want to be good at this. You answered in the margin.\n  flag(red_ink_glimpse): You hid on the landing and saw who took the essay: a first-year in robes two sizes too big.\n  flag(red_ink_met): The red ink is hers: a first-year who can't say a word in front of a room. She has a seminar on Thursday.\n  flag(red_ink_finished) and not flag(red_ink_silent): On Thursday she spoke, all the way to the end.\n  flag(red_ink_silent): On Thursday she sat down without reading. Her red ink is still the best marking you've ever had.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(red_ink_finished)\nmembers:\n  start lecture_tower_red_ink\n\n@event lecture_tower_red_ink\ntitle: Two Sets of Marks\nplace: lecture_tower\ntime: morning afternoon\ntone: light\nwhen: year >= 2 and not flag(red_ink_begun)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 14\nart: scroll\nscene: A student on a spiral stair reading an essay covered in neat pencil and, beneath it, furious corrections in red ink.\ntext: Your returned essay has two sets of marks. The marker's, in polite pencil: \"Good.\" And another, in red ink and unsigned, in a small round hand, savaging every paragraph and fixing three of them. The last line reads: \"You can do better than good. Paragraph four proves it.\"\n> left: Rewrite it properly\n  mood -tiny\n  lore +medium\n  study +tiny\n  flag red_ink_begun\n  find lead_red_ink\n  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_reply in 1\n  say: You rewrite it twice. On the third morning you leave it on the second-floor stair, where you found the first. By evening it's gone.\n> right: Bin it\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Good is good. You drop the red pages in the scorched-room bin, and feel a little foolish about how much you want to fish them out.\n\n@event lecture_tower_red_ink_reply\ntitle: A Question in the Margin\nthread: red_ink\ntrigger: queued\ntone: light\nwhen: not flag(red_ink_answered) and not flag(red_ink_glimpse) and not flag(red_ink_waited)\nart: paper\nscene: An essay left on a stone stair, its pages bleeding red ink, a single question written small at the bottom of the last page.\ntext: The essay is back on the stair, bleeding red. Most of it is about your argument. At the bottom, smaller: \"Why do you want to be good at this?\" It's the first question the ink has asked.\n> left: Answer it honestly in the margin\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  flag red_ink_answered\n  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_front_row in 1\n  say: You write three lines you'd never say aloud, and leave the essay on the stair. It's gone by noon. You spend the afternoon wishing you'd written two.\n> right: Wait on the stair to see who takes it\n  challenge deceive apprentice likely:\n    win:\n      flag red_ink_glimpse\n      guile +small\n      say: You hide on the landing above. A first-year in robes two sizes too big gathers up the pages, reads the first line right there on the step, and makes a small, furious noise.\n    lose:\n      flag red_ink_waited\n      health -tiny\n      say: You fall asleep on the stair. When you wake the essay is gone, and there's a note on your chest in red: \"Paragraph six. Also, you snore.\"\n  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_front_row in 1\n\n@event lecture_tower_red_ink_front_row\ntitle: The Front Row\nthread: red_ink\ntrigger: queued\nslot: morning\ntone: warm\nwhen: (flag(red_ink_answered) or flag(red_ink_glimpse) or flag(red_ink_waited)) and not flag(red_ink_met)\nart: book\nscene: A lecture hall of first-years, one small student alone in the front row with red ink on her fingers, looking down at a page of perfect notes while the lecturer waits.\ntext: You cut through the first-years' lecture on your way somewhere else. In the front row, alone, sits a first-year with red ink on her fingers. The lecturer asks her a question. She opens her mouth, and nothing comes out, and the hall waits until she looks at the floor. {flag(red_ink_glimpse): The robes are the ones from the stair, two sizes too big.|Her notes, you can see from the aisle, are perfect.}\n> left: Sit by her afterwards, and write\n  study -tiny\n  lore +small\n  flag red_ink_met\n  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_seminar in 1\n  say: You sit down beside her after the hall empties and slide your last essay across, without a word. She writes on it for twenty minutes without looking up. At the bottom: \"Seminar on Thursday. I have to speak. I can't.\"\n> right: Leave her the secret\n  lore +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  flag red_ink_met\n  queue lecture_tower_red_ink_seminar in 1\n  say: You walk on, and leave your next essay on the stair as usual. It comes back with the usual red, and one line more at the bottom: \"Seminar on Thursday. I have to speak. Please don't come.\"\n\n@event lecture_tower_red_ink_seminar\ntitle: Thursday's Seminar\nthread: red_ink\ntrigger: queued\nslot: afternoon\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nwhen: flag(red_ink_met)\nart: scroll\nscene: A small seminar room, a first-year frozen at a lectern holding her paper in both hands, a dozen students waiting, one older student at the back half out of their seat.\ntext: Thursday. The first-year stands at the seminar lectern with her paper in both hands and no voice at all. A dozen students wait. The lecturer looks at the clock. Her eyes find you at the back of the room.\n> left: Stand up and argue with her paper\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      renown +small\n      say: You stand and say her fourth paragraph is wrong. It isn't, and she knows it, and she's so annoyed she forgets to be frightened. She takes your objection apart for ten minutes. The room applauds, mostly for her.\n    lose:\n      flag red_ink_silent\n      mood -small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: You stand and object, and your objection is bad, and she's too polite to say so out loud. She sits down without reading. Your essay comes back that week with one line of red: \"Good try. Bad argument.\"\n  flag red_ink_finished\n> right: Offer to read it for her\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  flag red_ink_finished\n  say: You walk to the front and hold out your hand. She shakes her head, then gives you the first page. You read the first paragraph aloud. She takes the second page back, and reads the rest herself, quietly, all the way to the end.\n\n# ---------------- The late bloomer ----------------\n\n@thread late_bloomer\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=flag(bloomer_begun)\nclass: story\nrepeat: 1\ntags: magic\nclues: lead_late_bloomer\nname: The sweet shop's daughter\npremise: At home, the sweet-shop daughter has fire in her hands and nobody to teach her.\njournal:\n  flag(bloomer_begun): The sweet-shop daughter's sugar pan boiled on a cold stove. You taught her to breathe the heat down.\n  flag(bloomer_steady): Out past the fields, she learned to hold a flame the size of a fingernail.\n  flag(bloomer_promise): You promised her father you'd ask at the castle.\n  flag(bloomer_father_seen) and not flag(bloomer_promise): Her father asked what all that fire was for. You told him you didn't know.\n  flag(bloomer_damped): She bought the charm seller's grey glass bead, and the pans stayed cold.\n  flag(bloomer_charm_done) and not flag(bloomer_damped): You told her the charm was only glass. She kept her coins.\n  flag(bloomer_ice_done): Frost ran from her feet across the square and caught a carter's arm in the trough. You got him out.\n  flag(bloomer_letter): You wrote to the castle about her.\n  flag(bloomer_finished) and not flag(bloomer_letter): You left her your first practice candle, burned down to a stub.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(bloomer_finished)\nmembers:\n  start home_market_late_bloomer\n\n@event home_market_late_bloomer\ntitle: The Cold Stove\nplace: home_market\ntone: warm\nwhen: not flag(bloomer_begun)\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 16\nart: cauldron\nscene: The back of a small sweet shop, a copper sugar pan boiling over into dark caramel on a stove with no fire in it, a young woman staring at her own hands.\ntext: The sweet shop is the one thing in town that hasn't changed. Then the owner's daughter, a year or two older than you, bangs down a ladle and swears, and the sugar pan on the cold stove boils over into caramel. There is no fire under it. Her father looks at you, then at your castle robes. \"You're the one who'd know,\" he says quietly.\n> left: Show her how to breathe it down\n  mana -small\n  arcana +small\n  flag bloomer_begun\n  find lead_late_bloomer\n  say: You show her the first thing anyone at the castle learns: breathe in for four, hold the heat, let it go for four. The pan stops seething. She looks at her hands as if they belonged to someone else.\n> right: \"It happens. It'll settle.\"\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Her father nods, glad to be told so. Behind him she scrapes the burned sugar out of the pan, very carefully, and doesn't go near the stove again.\n\n@event home_fields_bloomer_lessons\ntitle: Where Nothing Can Catch\nthread: late_bloomer\nplace: home_fields\ntone: light\nwhen: not flag(bloomer_steady)\nweight: 16\nart: flame\nscene: A young woman and a student in castle robes on a bare, stony patch past the last field row at sunset, the young woman holding a candle stub at arm's length, its flame far too tall.\ntext: She's waiting past the last of the long rows, where the ground is all stones and nothing can catch, with a candle stub in each pocket. \"Teach me something,\" she says. \"Properly. Before you go back.\"\n> left: Teach her to keep a flame small\n  challenge spell apprentice+:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mood +small\n      flag bloomer_steady\n      queue home_cottage_bloomer_father in 1\n      say: By sunset she can hold a flame the size of a fingernail for ten breaths. She counts every one of them out loud.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The candle goes off like a firework. You both lie in the grass laughing until the farmer shouts. \"Next time,\" she says.\n> right: Spend the afternoon at home\n  health +small\n  say: You promise her next time. She shrugs, the way people do when they've stopped expecting things.\n\n@event home_cottage_bloomer_father\ntitle: Sweets on the Step\nthread: late_bloomer\ntrigger: queued\ntone: warm\nwhen: flag(bloomer_steady) and not flag(bloomer_father_seen)\ndefer: not has(plan_home)\nart: door\nscene: A worried shopkeeper at a cottage door holding a small paper bag of sweets, not stepping inside.\ntext: Her father is at the door with a paper bag of the sweets you liked when you were small. He doesn't come in. \"They take them young, at the castle, don't they,\" he says. \"She's past it. So what is it for, all that fire, in a sweet shop?\"\n> left: Tell him the truth\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  flag bloomer_father_seen\n  queue home_market_bloomer_charm in 1\n  say: \"I don't know what it's for,\" you tell him. \"I don't think anyone does, at her age.\" He thanks you for not lying, and leaves the sweets on the step.\n> right: Promise to ask at the castle\n  mood +small\n  flag bloomer_father_seen\n  flag bloomer_promise\n  queue home_market_bloomer_charm in 1\n  say: His face does something complicated. \"Would you,\" he says, not quite a question. You hear yourself promise. The sweets taste exactly as they used to.\n\n@event home_market_bloomer_charm\ntitle: A Lid on the Pot\nthread: late_bloomer\ntrigger: queued\nslot: afternoon\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(bloomer_father_seen) and not flag(bloomer_charm_done)\ndefer: not has(plan_home)\nart: gem\nscene: A market charm seller holding up a grey glass bead on a cord to a young woman counting coins from her apron.\ntext: In the market you find her at the charm seller's stall. He's holding up a grey glass bead on a cord. \"Lumenhold-blessed,\" he's saying. \"Smothers it, like a lid on a pot. No more flares, no more fuss.\" It's plain glass; you can see that from here. She's counting out her savings.\n> left: Let her believe in it\n  mood -small\n  flag bloomer_damped\n  flag bloomer_charm_done\n  queue home_cottage_bloomer_letter in 1\n  say: You say nothing. She buys it and puts it on. It's only glass, but she believes in it, and the sugar pans stay cold all week. She looks lighter, and smaller.\n> right: Tell her it's only glass\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      queue home_market_bloomer_ice now\n      say: \"It's glass,\" you tell her. \"You did the hard part yourself, out past the fields.\" The charm seller gives you the look of a man revising his prices. She puts her coins back in her apron, slowly.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      flag bloomer_damped\n      queue home_cottage_bloomer_letter in 1\n      say: She buys it anyway. \"Easy for you,\" she says, not unkindly. \"You get to go back.\"\n  flag bloomer_charm_done\n\n@event home_market_bloomer_ice\ntitle: Ice in the Square\nthread: late_bloomer\ntrigger: queued\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(bloomer_charm_done) and not flag(bloomer_damped) and not flag(bloomer_ice_done)\nart: snow\nscene: A market-square horse trough frozen solid around a carter's arm, a panicking young woman beside it and frost spreading across the cobbles.\ntext: Not ten minutes later, a shout from the square. The horse trough has frozen solid, with a carter's arm in it to the elbow, and she's standing beside it white with panic. Frost is creeping across the cobbles from her feet.\n> left: Break the ice by hand\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      health -small\n      vigor +small\n      say: You hammer at the ice with a cobble until it gives with a crack like a door. The carter pulls his arm free, swearing, mostly at the trough.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You get him free in the end, but the ice takes the skin off your knuckles doing it. You'll feel that trough for days.\n  flag bloomer_ice_done\n  queue home_cottage_bloomer_letter in 1\n> right: Talk her down so it melts\n  challenge appeal adept-:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +small\n      say: \"Four in. Hold. Four out.\" Her breath slows, and the ice goes grey, then soft, then water. The carter stares at his arm, and then at her.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: It takes an hour, three kettles from the inn carried at a run, and your own hands scalded red holding the carter's arm in the steam. He will tell this story for years.\n  flag bloomer_ice_done\n  queue home_cottage_bloomer_letter in 1\n\n@event home_cottage_bloomer_letter\ntitle: A Flame That Stays\nthread: late_bloomer\ntrigger: queued\nslot: evening\ndefer: not has(plan_home)\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nwhen: flag(bloomer_charm_done) and (flag(bloomer_ice_done) or flag(bloomer_damped)) and not flag(bloomer_finished)\nart: candle\nscene: A young woman at a cottage table at evening, a small steady candle flame burning on the tip of her finger.\ntext: {flag(bloomer_damped): She comes by one evening, the grey bead on its cord. \"I know it's only glass,\" she says. \"I keep it in my pocket anyway.\" She lights a candle with her finger, small and steady, to show you.|She comes by one evening with a candle she lit at noon and has kept burning, small and steady, all day. \"Look,\" she says. \"It stays.\"}\n> left: Write to the castle for her\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +medium\n  flag bloomer_letter\n  flag bloomer_finished\n  say: {flag(bloomer_promise): You keep your promise to her father and write to the castle that night|You write to the castle that night}: what she can do, what she learned with no teacher but you, and that some people come late to things. You pay the postage; she walks it to the post office herself.\n> right: Leave her your practice candle\n  study -tiny\n  mood +medium\n  flag bloomer_finished\n  say: {flag(bloomer_promise): You never did ask at the castle. Instead you leave her|You leave her} the practice candle you've had since your first week, the one you learned on and still revise by, burned down to a stub. You'll be reading by the window for a while. A month later a letter comes with a drawing of the stub, still lit.\n"},{"path":"content/events/night.grim","text":"# The midnight turn (playtest 2, M41; stub — X13 fills it): after a lead-in in year 1 Stormbloom, a night card each\n# on-campus month, first in the midnight slot (after every night card), so going out leads straight to the pick. \"Go out\" opens the midnight pick (`visit midnight`); six places are open then. Going out in term\n# queues `night_suspicion` at 33%, and risky outcomes queue it at their own odds (I4); two copies bring at most one\n# card the next morning (or the first month back on campus), the only way to a lockout. The cards never say whether\n# you were seen.\n\n@event night_lead_in\ntitle: An Unsigned Note\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: year == 1 and month == 7 and not off_campus\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: map\ntags: mystery\nscene: A folded note slipped under a dormitory door at night, unsigned, a tiny silver map drawn in one corner.\ntext: A note under your door, unsigned: \"The castle has a second timetable. It starts at midnight. Curfew is a suggestion made by people who go to bed early.\" In the corner, a map no bigger than a thumbnail, showing six doors that are open when they shouldn't be.\n> left: Burn it\n  flag night_unlocked\n  find lead_second_timetable\n  say: You hold it to the candle. The map in the corner is the last thing to burn, and you could swear it rearranged itself first.\n> right: Keep it\n  flag night_unlocked\n  find lead_second_timetable\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You fold it into your book. Some nights you take it out and look at the six doors.\n\n@event night_out\ntitle: After Curfew\ntrigger: auto\nslot: midnight\nwhen: flag(night_unlocked) and not off_campus and not has(locked_out) and turn < max_turns\nrepeat: 1\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: moon\ntags: mystery\nscene: A dormitory window at midnight, the castle dark beyond it, one lantern moving along a far battlement.\ntext: {month == 11: Starfall is close, and the sky over the towers is already restless.|{month == 12: The last of Starfall is still falling over the towers, slow and silver.|{month == 4 or month == 10: Exams soon. Half the windows on your corridor are still lit.|{season == \"winter\": {~Frost on the window, and the corridors are colder than the snow.|Snow on the sills; the tower creaks like a ship.|Ice in the water jug, and the tower silent with snow.}|{season == \"summer\": {~The night is warm and the castle's windows are all open.|Moths at the window, and somebody laughing on the lawn.|The night is short and never quite dark.}|{~The castle settles for the night, beam by beam.|Rain on the leads, and a gargoyle gargling it.|The wind finds every keyhole in the tower.}}}}}} {has(watched): Bram does his rounds earlier these nights. You've learned the sound of his boots.|{health < 30: Every bone you own would like to stay in bed.|{mood < 25: You can't sleep anyway.|{gold < 10: Your purse is light, and some doors after midnight pay.|{bonds(partner) > 0: {~A note under your door, in a hand you know: \"Roof?\"|You could be on the observatory roof with someone right now.|Somewhere across the castle, someone you love is also awake.}|{final_year: {~Your last year. The castle at night won't be yours much longer.|The doors you know by heart will open for someone else next year.|Next year someone else will lie awake in this bed.}|{~Somewhere a door that should be locked isn't.|Down in the courtyard, a lantern goes out on purpose.|Someone has left the servants' stair unbolted again.}}}}}}}\n> left: Sleep\n  hint: A night's rest.\n  sleep\n  say: {~You blow out the candle and let the castle keep its secrets for one more night.|Sleep. The night can wait. It always does.|You pull the blanket up and listen to the castle until you're asleep.}\n> right: Go out\n  hint: No rest tonight.\n  visit midnight\n  if term:\n    queue night_suspicion chance unlikely\n  say: {~You wait for the last footsteps to fade, then ease the door open.|Boots in hand, you take the servants' stair.|You slip out while the castle is still settling, and nobody hears.}\n\n# Bedtime (2026-10-17): every ordinary month ends with a night's sleep, the only steady way back to health and\n# mood. On campus once the night is open, night_out offers it (\"Sleep\"); this card is the bedtime everywhere else:\n# before the unlock, at home, on an internship or other plan away, while locked out, or when the night card dropped\n# out of a day already scheduled. It steps aside once night_out has shown this month, so going out never also rests.\n@event night_sleep\ntitle: Lights Out\ntrigger: auto\nslot: midnight\nwhen: since(night_out) > 0 and turn < max_turns\nrepeat: 1\npriority: 10\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: bed\ntags: life home\nscene: A narrow bed under a slanting ceiling, a candle just blown out, its thread of smoke curling up in the moonlight.\ntext: {has(locked_out): {~The lock on your door turns at curfew now, from the outside. The castle has decided where you sleep.|Bram's boots go past your door twice before midnight. There's nowhere to go, so you go to bed.|Locked in for the night. You can hear the castle's second timetable starting without you.}|{break and has(plan_home): {~Your old bed at home, too short now, and the smell of the kitchen still in the walls.|At home the night is so quiet you can hear the kettle cooling downstairs.|Your own pillow, your own ceiling, and nobody's footsteps but family's.}|{has(plan_internship): {~Your lodgings are small, the bed is narrow, and you're too tired to mind.|The city doesn't stop at night, but you do.|Tomorrow's work is already laid out on the chair. The bed is the only other furniture.}|{off_campus: {~A borrowed bed, a strange ceiling, and the day finally over.|The night is quiet here, far from the towers.|You fall asleep before you've finished deciding to.}|{season == \"winter\": {~Frost on the window, and the blankets pulled up to your ears.|Snow on the sills; the tower creaks like a ship.|Ice in the water jug, and the warmest place in the castle is your bed.}|{~The castle settles for the night, beam by beam.|Rain on the leads, and a gargoyle gargling it.|The day is done, and the candle is low.}}}}}}\n> ok: Sleep\n  hint: A night's rest.\n  sleep\n  say: {break and has(plan_home): {~You sleep the way you only sleep at home: all night, without a single dream you remember.|Somebody looks in on you before they go to bed, as if you were still small. You pretend to be asleep, and then you are.|You wake late, to bread and the smell of the garden. Home mends things.}|{~You blow out the candle, and the day goes with it.|You're asleep before the candle has stopped smoking.|You lie awake a little, listening, and then you don't.}}\n\n# The morning after you were noticed (queued by the night's cards, I4): the only source of lockouts. Caught while\n# watched: three months confined after dark and a month of detention (threads 1: it takes next month's visits).\n@event night_suspicion\ntitle: A Word in the Morning\ntrigger: queued\nslot: morning\n# Caught in a term's last month and off home or to an internship (I6): it waits until you're back on campus; on a\n# castle break it waits for term too, so the month of detention it gives is served (threads 1).\ndefer: off_campus or break\nrepeat: 1\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: eye\ntags: crisis\nscene: A prefect waiting at the foot of a dormitory stair at breakfast time, arms folded, a small notebook open.\ntext: {has(watched): Bram is waiting at the foot of the stair, and this time he has a notebook open. \"Twice,\" he says. \"I did warn you.\"|Bram is waiting at the foot of the stair. \"Someone was out last night,\" he says, looking at nobody in particular, which is to say at you.}\n> left: Deny everything\n  guile +tiny\n  if has(watched):\n    lose watched\n    gain locked_out\n    gain detention\n    mood -medium\n    say: A month of detention, and three of a door that is checked at midnight. Bram writes it all down, neatly.\n  else:\n    gain watched\n    say: \"I'll be keeping an eye on you.\" He means it literally.\n> right: Own up\n  alignment +tiny\n  if has(watched):\n    lose watched\n    gain locked_out\n    gain detention\n    mood -medium\n    say: A month of detention, and three of a door that is checked at midnight. Bram writes it all down, neatly.\n  else:\n    gain watched\n    say: \"I'll be keeping an eye on you.\" He means it literally.\n\n# ---------------- One night at each open place (X13 adds more) ----------------\n\n@event midnight_newt_cards\ntitle: Upstairs at the Newt\nplace: gilded_newt\nslot: midnight\n# Retired (feedback 2026-10-16): the Newt's nights are the company offer, then the high table (`@place always:`).\nwhen: false\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: money danger\nscene: A smoky upstairs room at a tavern after hours, a card table under one lamp, coins stacked high.\ntext: Upstairs at the Gilded Newt, after hours, the real game starts. The stakes are higher than anything downstairs, and nobody asks your name.\n> left: Play a few hands\n  alignment -tiny\n  gold -medium\n  challenge deceive adept- unlikely luck:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      gold +medium\n      say: {~You leave with more than you came with and a few new enemies who smile too much.|Luck, nerve and one well-timed sigh. The pot is yours.}\n    lose:\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {~The cards go cold, and so does your purse. On the stair down, a face from the castle turns away a little too fast.|You lose your stake and walk back in the dark, poorer, wiser and not sure you went unseen.}\n> right: Watch, then leave\n  say: {~You watch one hand, see someone lose a familiar on a bluff, and leave before you're tempted.|You stay long enough to learn the rules, and leave before they learn you.}\n\n@event midnight_boathouse_ring\ntitle: The Ring Under the Boathouse\nplace: boathouse\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\n# The ring (feedback 2026-10-16): one of the two entry cards opens every boathouse night (`@place always:`). A win\n# leads to the next bout (ring_bout_2 … 4), each harder and better paid. Some nights the house runs last-standing\n# rules, rolled when the card is drawn and said before you choose: once you step in, you fight until you lose or\n# have won four. Declining sends you to the side bets. Every opponent is a wizard (feedback 2026-10-25); nobody down\n# here reports a forbidden cast (the fights' witnesses: false), but its alignment cost stands, and the text says both.\n# After a won bout a character who'd fight here may step in out of turn (ring_cameo, content/events/pools/night.grim).\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: sword\ntags: danger money\nscene: A cellar under a boathouse, lanterns hung from the beams, a crowd around two wizards in a chalk ring, sparks in the air, coins changing hands.\non_show:\n  unflag ring_last_standing ring_down\n  chance unlikely:\n    flag ring_last_standing\ntext: {Under the boathouse someone has chalked a ring on the flagstones. Inside it, wands out, anything goes, and nobody here reports a forbidden spell. What it does to you is your own affair.|The chalk ring under the boathouse again, scorched from last time. The student holding the money knows your face.|The ring is busier than ever, the air sharp with spent spells, and the money-holder nods to you like a regular.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Tonight a slate by the steps reads LAST STANDING: whoever steps in fights until they drop or have beaten four.|\"Next,\" says the student holding the money, looking at you. Win, and you can fight on for a bigger purse.}\n> left: Step into the ring\n  hint: Four bouts at most, each harder and richer. Nothing cast here is reported.\n  challenge combat adept-:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +tiny\n      queue any in ring_cameo now chance unlikely\n      queue ring_bout_2 now\n      say: {Your opponent's wand skitters out of the chalk, and the ring's respect comes your way, which is worth less and lasts longer.|Your opponent yields, smoking at the cuffs. Somebody whistles, and the money-holder looks at you: another?|You put your opponent down in the first exchange, and the crowd starts calling your name.}\n    lose:\n      unflag ring_last_standing\n      health -medium\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {You lose, and limp home singed past a window where a light is still burning.|A bruising loss, and a lantern somewhere on the path home. Maybe it saw you; maybe not.|You go down to a first-year with a very fast wand. The crowd enjoys it more than you do.}\n> right: Not tonight\n  hint: Watch, and bet if you like.\n  unflag ring_last_standing\n  queue ring_side_bets now\n  say: {You stay at the edge of the crowd and watch someone else's turn.|You shake your head. The money-holder shrugs and looks for somebody braver.|\"Not tonight,\" you say, and find a place on the cellar steps to watch.}\n\n@event midnight_kitchen_raid\ntitle: The Kitchens at Midnight\nplace: refectory\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: danger\nscene: A dark castle kitchen at midnight, a student with a candle reaching into a larder, a cat watching from the hearth.\ntext: The refectory kitchens at midnight: banked fires, a larder that isn't locked, and the smell of tomorrow's bread already rising.\n> left: Fill your pockets\n  alignment -tiny\n  lose hungry\n  health +small\n  queue night_suspicion chance even\n  say: {~Bread, cheese and half a pie. Nobody counts the pies, you hope, and the scullery door creaks as you leave.|You eat by the banked fire with the kitchen cat. On your way out, a kitchen boy is up early. Did he see you?}\n> right: Just warm yourself by the fire\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You sit by the embers until your hands stop hurting, and take nothing. The walk back is cold.|The kitchen cat sits with you. You leave the larder alone, feel strangely proud of it, and shiver all the way back to bed.}\n\n@event midnight_restricted_stacks\ntitle: The Restricted Stacks\nplace: restricted_section\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\n# Retired (threads 1): the Restricted Stacks heist replaces the section's midnight cards (content/events/heists/).\nwhen: false\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A chained library section at midnight, a student reading by a stolen candle, shelves leaning in like listeners.\ntext: The Restricted Section at midnight. The chains on the shelves clink when nobody touches them. One book has been left open, as if for you.\n> left: Read the open book\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: {~Three pages before the book closes itself. You remember every word, which is worrying.|The book shows you a diagram you're not supposed to understand. You understand it.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {~The book slams shut and a lamp comes on two floors down. You're gone before it reaches the stair.|The pages scream, briefly. You leave quickly, and hope nobody heard.}\n> right: Leave it open and go\n  say: {~You back away from the book. It seems disappointed.|You leave it open for whoever it was waiting for. It wasn't you, you decide.}\n\n@event midnight_observatory_stars\ntitle: Stars at Midnight\nplace: observatory\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: nature\nscene: The observatory roof at midnight, a student lying on the leads, the sky thick with stars.\ntext: The observatory roof at midnight is the quietest place in the castle. {bonds(partner) > 0: You've brought someone.|The stars are very close tonight.} Nobody comes up here after curfew, mostly.\n> left: Stay until the sky turns\n  mood +medium\n  health -small\n  say: {~You count stars until you lose count, and then until the sky goes grey. You'll be tired. It was worth it.|A meteor, then another. You stay until the first bell.}\n> right: An hour, then bed\n  mood +small\n  queue night_suspicion chance slim\n  say: {~An hour of stars, and you're back in bed before anyone stirs, you think.|You slip back down the stair, the sky still in your eyes. A lantern turns a far corner, and you don't wait to see whose.}\n\n@event midnight_passage_mapping\ntitle: The Passage After Dark\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A secret passage behind a gargoyle at midnight, chalk marks on the walls, a candle guttering in a draught from nowhere.\ntext: Behind the third gargoyle the passage is busier at midnight than at noon. {met(corvin): Corvin's chalk marks are fresh.|Someone's chalk marks are fresh.} A draught comes from a direction that has no wall.\n> left: Follow the draught\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {~The draught leads you to a door you've never seen, and then, politely, closes it.|A new turning, a new stair. You chalk it for the map.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {~After an hour of cold stone, the passage lets you out somewhere unexpected: the prefects' corridor, empty, you hope.|You get lost for an hour and come out in the wrong tower, cold and dusty, and not alone on the stair.}\n> right: Mark it and go back\n  say: {~You chalk an arrow at the turning and leave the rest for another night.|You mark it on your copy of the map and go back to bed, a little sorry.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/origin_funding.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Origin funding (docs/origin-design.md, 2026-10-16): the money a family, the Crown or a company sends before each\n#  later term (terms 2–8), as one card in the last break month (turns 6, 12 … 42), wherever you spend the break.\n#  The amounts and odds are presets in content/core/balance.grim. A roll happens once, when the card is drawn\n#  (`on_show`), so the text matches what arrives and a reload doesn't change it. Payment needs no choice.\n# ============================================================\n\n@pool origin_funding_auto_story\ntrigger: auto\nclass: story\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 60\n\n@event noble_letter\ntitle: A Letter from Home\npool: origin_funding_auto_story\ntone: warm\nwhen: has(origin_noble) and (month == 6 or month == 12) and term_index <= 7\nart: letter\ntags: home money\nscene: A heavy envelope with a wax family seal on a writing desk, a bank draft half out of it.\non_show:\n  unflag family_gift\n  chance unlikely:\n    flag family_gift\ntext: {month == 6: Before the spring term|Before the new year} a letter comes with your family's seal and a draft on the family bank. {flag(family_gift): {&Folded inside is a smaller note from your grandmother, and a little more money \"for something nice\".|There's an extra sum this time, marked \"a gift\", which in your family means it will be mentioned at dinner for a year.|Your father has added something on top, with a line about how proud he would be of better marks.}|{&The allowance, to the crown, and three pages about cousins who are doing very well.|The usual sum, and a reminder that the family name goes wherever you go.|The allowance, and a postscript asking whether you have met anyone suitable.}}\n> ok: Fold the letter away\n  noble_allowance\n  if flag(family_gift):\n    noble_gift\n  say: {&You read it twice: once for the money, once for what it wants from you.|You put the draft in your purse and the letter in a drawer, and the letter is heavier.|You write back the same night: thank you, yes, of course, you'll try.}\n\n@event scholarship_award\ntitle: The Bursary List\npool: origin_funding_auto_story\ntone: tense\nwhen: has(origin_scholar) and (month == 6 or month == 12) and term_index <= 7\nart: scroll\ntags: study money\nscene: A list of names pinned outside the registrar's office, a clerk's red ink beside some of them.\ntext: The bursary list {month == 6: for the spring term|for the new year} goes up outside the registrar's office. {term_gpa >= 3.5: {&Your name is near the top with a small star beside it: the full stipend, and a merit bonus.|The clerk hands you the envelope herself. The full stipend, and a little extra \"for distinction\".|Your name, the full sum, and the word \"Merit\" in red ink, which the clerk says she rarely gets to use.}|{term_gpa >= 3.0: {&Your name is there: the stipend is renewed for another term.|\"Renewed,\" says the list, in the clerk's tidy hand. You breathe out.|Your stipend stands for another term. The marks held, if only just.}|{term_gpa > 0: {&Your name is there with a line through the amount. Last term's marks fell below the 3.0 the Crown pays for, and the stipend waits until they come back up.|The clerk is apologetic: the Crown pays for marks of 3.0 and above, and last term yours were not. Next term, perhaps.|No stipend this term. Your marks dipped below what the Crown pays for, and the list says so in plain ink.}|{&Your name isn't on the list. No marks were recorded for you last term, and the Crown pays for marks.|The clerk checks twice. No grades last term means no stipend this one.|An empty line where your amount should be: the Crown pays for marks, and last term you had none.}}}}\n> ok: Read your line again\n  scholarship_stipend\n  say: {term_gpa >= 3.0: {&You fold the envelope into your book and walk a little taller to breakfast.|Tuition, board and something left over. You allow yourself one good dinner.|You write home that the stipend held. Your family will read the letter aloud to the neighbours.}|{&You do the sums on the back of a lecture note, and they come out short. Work, then, or thinner meals.|You walk away from the list without looking back at it. Next term the marks will be better; they have to be.|You go straight to the library, which is free, and stay until it closes.}}\n\n@event trading_report\ntitle: The Company's Report\npool: origin_funding_auto_story\ntone: tense\nwhen: has(origin_merchant) and (month == 6 or month == 12) and term_index <= 7\nart: scales\ntags: home money\nscene: A merchant company's ledger report on thick paper, columns of figures, a bank draft clipped to the first page or not.\non_show:\n  unflag trade_good\n  chance likely:\n    flag trade_good\ntext: {month == 6: The company's half-year report|The company's year-end report} arrives with a clerk's note on top. {flag(trade_good): {&The spice fleet came in early and whole. Your share is paid in full.|A good season: the northern warehouses sold out by midsummer. Your share comes with your mother's compliments.|Profit on every route but one. Your share is enclosed, and so is a list of what it cost.}|{&A ship lost off the southern coast, and a cargo of silk ruined by rain. There is no share this term.|The markets turned. The report is long and the envelope is empty: no share until trade recovers.|Pirates, tariffs and a warehouse fire, in that order. Your mother writes that the company cannot spare anything this term.}}\n> ok: Fold the report away\n  if flag(trade_good):\n    trade_returns\n  say: {flag(trade_good): {&You count it twice and lock it away. Next term's tuition is safe.|You read the figures like a story with a happy ending, and put the draft somewhere safe.|You think about what the good season cost someone else, and then about tuition, and put the draft away.}|{&You count what's in your purse instead. It will have to do.|You read the figures twice, as if they might change. They don't.|You write back that you understand, and then go and look at the job board.}}\n"},{"path":"content/events/people_others.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Character events — Kael, Bram, faculty and staff.\n#  (Kael's main arc lives in chain_hollowstar.grim.)\n# ============================================================\n\n# ================= KAEL DUMONT =================\n\n@event kael_snowball\ntitle: Snow in Thawmoon\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 6 and month >= 5 and month <= 7\nweight: 10\nart: snow\ntags: social\nscene: A courtyard where it is snowing in one small circle only, a boy in a fur coat standing at its centre holding a perfect snowball.\ntext: It's snowing in the courtyard. Only in a small circle, around Kael, who is holding a snowball and looking at it as though he's never been allowed to throw one.\n> left: Leave him to it\n  say: When you pass again, the circle of snow is gone, and there's a small, lopsided snowman in its place.\n> right: Throw the first one\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +small\n  mood +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: Your snowball hits him in the ear. He stares. Then he laughs — a real laugh, startled out of him — and the snow triples.\n  if knows(kael.cold_hands):\n    rel kael +tiny\n    say: Afterwards you take his hands to warm them, out of habit, and for once he doesn't tell you they're always cold. He just lets you.\n\n@event kael_cold_hands\ntime: evening night\ntitle: Cold Hands\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 12 and spark(kael) < 35\nweight: 10\nart: heart\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two students sitting close in a library alcove, one holding the other's frost-white hand between both of theirs.\ntext: Kael's hands are always cold. In the library tonight they're so cold the ink in his pen has frozen. He's trying to thaw it with his breath, embarrassed.\n> left: Lend him your gloves\n  health -tiny\n  rel kael +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: He wears them for the rest of the term. They're far too small. He doesn't care. Your own fingers go numb on the walk back.\n> right: Warm his hands in yours\n  rel kael +medium\n  spark kael +small\n  say: He goes very still. Then, slowly, the frost on his fingertips melts. \"Nobody's ever been warm enough,\" he says quietly. \"Before.\"\n\n@event kael_confession\nramp: spark(kael) 25..40\ntitle: Thaw\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: spark(kael) >= 25 and rel(kael) >= 18 and courting(kael)\nweight: 30\nart: snow\ntags: romance\nscene: Frost-ferns spreading across a dark window behind a pale boy who is, for once, looking directly at someone.\ntext: \"I was taught not to want things,\" Kael says. \"Wanting things is how they control you.\" Frost blooms across the window behind him. \"I want you. I'm sorry. I don't know what to do with it.\"\n> left: Gently say no\n  spark kael -large\n  rel kael +tiny\n  say: He nods, as if he expected it. \"Thank you for being honest. Nobody is.\" The frost on the window fades slowly.\n> right: Show him what to do with it\n  bond kael partner\n  spark kael +large\n  mood +large\n  say: You kiss him. Every window in the corridor frosts over at once, and then, very slowly, melts.\n\n# ================= BRAM HOLLIS =================\n\n@event bram_intro\ntitle: Running in the Halls\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: bram\nwhen: (not met(bram) or seen(intro_tour) > 0) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: scroll\ntags: rival\nscene: A burly prefect with a red sash blocking a corridor, a demerit ledger open in one hand and a quill poised.\ntext: \"Running in the halls.\" {met(bram): Bram Hollis, the prefect from the tour,|A burly prefect with a red sash} steps into your path, ledger already open. \"Name?\" {met(bram): He knows it. |}You weren't running. You were walking briskly.\n> left: Accept the demerit\n  meet bram\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He writes your name very slowly, enjoying it. Bram Hollis. You will learn to recognise his footsteps.\n> right: Argue\n  meet bram\n  rel bram -medium\n  renown +small\n  say: \"Walking briskly,\" you insist. Two demerits: one for running, one for \"lip\". Some students nearby clap quietly.\n\n@event bram_hall_tax\ntitle: The Hall Tax\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: rel(bram) < 6 and not (flag(bram_sash_lost) and since(corvin_framed) < 10)\nweight: 12\nart: coin\ntags: rival money\nscene: A prefect leaning against a stone archway, palm out, a line of nervous first-years shuffling past him.\ntext: Bram has invented a \"hall tax\" for using the east staircase. Eight crowns. \"Maintenance,\" he says. The first-years are paying. He looks at you expectantly.\n> left: Pay it\n  gold -small\n  rel bram +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He pockets the coins. \"Pleasure.\" You take the west staircase forever after.\n> right: Refuse loudly\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel bram -medium\n  renown +small\n  chance even:\n    gain watched\n    say: The first-years stop paying too. Bram is furious. He'll find a way to make you pay later. He always does.\n  else:\n    say: Your refusal spreads. Within a week, nobody pays the hall tax. Bram drops it, scowling.\n\n@event bram_sabotage\ntitle: Missing Notes\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: rel(bram) <= -12 and (month == 4 or month == 10)\nweight: 14\nart: scroll\ntags: rival study\nscene: An empty desk drawer, a few scattered pages on the floor, a red prefect's sash glimpsed disappearing around a corner.\ntext: Two days before exams, your revision notes vanish from your desk. All of them. You saw a red sash in the corridor.\n> left: Rewrite them from memory\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: You rewrite everything in one furious night. Your new notes are better than the old ones. Take that, Bram.\n    lose:\n      study -medium\n      health -tiny\n      say: You rewrite what you can. It isn't enough. Exams go worse than they should.\n> right: Search his room\n  challenge deceive vs bram.guile:\n    win:\n      rel bram -medium\n      guile +tiny\n      say: You find your notes under his mattress, and also a teddy bear. You take your notes and leave the bear exactly where it was, slightly straightened. He will know.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel bram -small\n      say: He catches you in his room. That's two weeks of detention, and he gets to supervise.\n\n@event bram_crying\ntitle: Behind the Stables\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: met(bram) and turn >= 8 and not flag(bram_softened)\nweight: 8\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A big prefect sitting on an upturned bucket behind the stables, a crumpled letter in his fist, shoulders shaking.\ntext: Behind the stables, you find Bram Hollis sitting on a bucket, crying into a crumpled letter. He sees you and freezes. \"If you tell anyone,\" he begins, but his voice cracks.\n> left: Walk away and never mention it\n  rel bram +medium\n  say: You leave. You never tell anyone. Somehow, he knows you didn't. His demerits become fewer.\n> right: Sit down and ask what happened\n  mood -tiny\n  flag bram_softened\n  rel bram +huge\n  say: His father. A letter saying he's a disappointment, again. \"He was a prefect too,\" Bram says. \"Head boy. I'm just... loud.\" He wipes his face. \"Don't be nice to me. I don't know what to do with it.\"\n\n@event bram_turns\nramp: rel(bram) 6..15\ntitle: An Unexpected Ally\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: flag(bram_softened) and rel(bram) >= 6 and (bond(bram) == \"none\" or bond(bram) == \"rival\" or bond(bram) == \"nemesis\")\nweight: 12\nart: shield\ntags: social\nscene: A prefect stepping between a cornered student and a pair of older bullies, arms crossed.\ntext: Two fourth-years corner you by the boathouse, looking for trouble. Then a red sash steps between you. \"Problem?\" says Bram, cracking his knuckles. They leave fast.\n> left: Thank him awkwardly\n  rel bram +medium\n  say: \"Don't make it weird,\" he says, and walks off. He's smiling, slightly.\n> right: Offer your hand\n  rel bram +large\n  bond bram friend\n  say: He stares at your hand. Then he shakes it, hard. \"Fine. Friends. But I'm still giving you demerits.\" He does. Fewer.\n\n@event bram_duel\ntitle: A Formal Challenge\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: bram\nwhen: bond(bram) == \"rival\" or bond(bram) == \"nemesis\"\nweight: 12\nart: duel\ntags: rival danger\nscene: A white glove lying on a dormitory floor, a red sash folded beside it, a crowd peering through the doorway.\ntext: Bram throws a glove at your feet in front of half the dormitory. \"Formal challenge. The dueling green, right now. Unless you're a coward.\" Everyone is watching.\n> left: Decline\n  renown -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Coward,\" he says, delighted. It sticks for a while. Then people forget. Mostly.\n> right: Accept\n  challenge combat vs bram.vigor:\n    win:\n      flag beat_bram_duel\n      renown +large\n      rel bram +small\n      say: You disarm him in three moves. The crowd goes wild. Bram, on the grass, starts laughing. \"Fine,\" he says. \"Fine.\"\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      renown +small\n      say: He wins. But it takes him a full ten minutes, and he's limping too. He nods to you after. Grudging respect.\n\n# ================= PROFESSOR THORNE =================\n\n@event thorne_intro\ntitle: The First Question\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: thorne\nwhen: not met(thorne) and enrolled(evocation)\nweight: 16\nart: wand\ntags: study\nscene: A steep lecture theatre, a stern grey-bearded professor pointing a long finger at one student in the middle row.\ntext: First Evocation lecture. Professor Thorne surveys the room like a hawk over a field of mice. His finger lands on you. \"You. What is the first law of the Arcane?\"\n> left: Admit you don't know\n  meet thorne\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: \"Correct answer,\" he says. \"The first law is: know what you don't know. The rest of you, write that down.\"\n> right: Take a guess\n  meet thorne\n  challenge research apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      rel thorne +small\n      study evocation +tiny\n      say: \"Energy follows intention.\" His eyebrows twitch. \"Acceptable.\" The room exhales.\n    lose:\n      rel thorne -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: Your guess is so wrong the student next to you gasps. Thorne simply says, \"No,\" and moves on.\n\n@event thorne_office_hours\ntime: noon afternoon\ntitle: Office Hours\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 6 and arcana >= 14\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: A cramped office full of scorched books and a single lit candle, a stern professor leaning back in his chair, fingers steepled.\ntext: Thorne stops you after class. \"Your casting is sloppy and your instincts are good. That's an annoying combination. Thursdays. Seven o'clock. My office. Don't be late.\"\n> left: Politely decline — you're stretched thin\n  rel thorne -tiny\n  say: \"Hm.\" He seems neither surprised nor pleased. Thorne is rarely either.\n> right: Be there at seven\n  arcana +small\n  rel thorne +medium\n  health -tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: Two hours of brutal drills. At the end, he hands you a book from his own shelf. \"Read it. Return it unburnt.\"\n\n@event thorne_storm_story\ntitle: The Storm Duel\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 12\nweight: 8\nart: bolt\ntags: study\nscene: An old professor standing at a rain-lashed window, lightning illuminating a long scar along his jaw.\ntext: During a thunderstorm, Thorne stops mid-lecture and watches the lightning. \"They say I out-dueled a storm once,\" he says, to nobody in particular. \"Would you like to know what actually happened?\"\n> left: \"Yes, sir.\"\n  gain inspired\n  rel thorne +small\n  study evocation -tiny\n  say: He lost, he says. Badly. He spent a year learning why. \"Losing well,\" he says, \"is the only thing worth teaching.\" The class is silent. The lecture never resumes.\n> right: Stay quiet\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  study evocation +tiny\n  say: He shakes his head and returns to the lecture, which is a good one. You'll always wonder.\n\n@event thorne_recommendation\ntitle: A Letter of Recommendation\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: rel(thorne) >= 18 and final_year\nweight: 16\nart: letter\ntags: study\nscene: A wax-sealed envelope on a professor's desk with a student's name written on it in sharp, angular handwriting.\ntext: Thorne calls you to his office and slides an envelope across the desk. \"The Conclave asked for my recommendations. I wrote one. I don't write many.\" He pauses. \"Don't make me regret it.\"\n> left: Thank him sincerely\n  flag thorne_letter\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Go away,\" he says gruffly. \"I have marking.\" You see him smile at the window as you leave.\n  if knows(thorne.roses):\n    rel thorne +tiny\n    say: On your way out you leave a single rose on the corner of his desk, from the village, not from his garden. He pretends not to see it. The next day it's in a glass of water.\n> right: Ask why you\n  mood -tiny\n  flag thorne_letter\n  rel thorne +medium\n  say: \"Because you ask questions like that,\" he says. \"Most students don't. Go.\" You leave unsure whether you were praised or dismissed.\n\n@event thorne_disappointed\ntitle: A Word in Private\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ntrigger: social\nactor: thorne\nwhen: (turn > 4 and gpa < 2.0)\nweight: 14\nart: scales\ntags: study crisis\nscene: A professor standing by his desk with a sheaf of red-marked papers, a student standing awkwardly before him.\ntext: Thorne holds up your last three Evocation essays. All red. \"You're better than this,\" he says. \"That's not a compliment. It's an accusation.\"\n> left: Make excuses\n  rel thorne -small\n  mood -tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: He listens to them all and says nothing. The silence is the worst grade yet.\n> right: Ask for help\n  mood -small\n  rel thorne +medium\n  study evocation +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: He sighs and pulls out a chair. \"Sit. We'll start with the first essay, and we'll stay until it's right.\" You stay until midnight.\n\n# ================= OLD MAUD =================\n\n@event maud_chickens\ntitle: The Great Chicken Escape\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: maud\nwhen: not met(maud) and turn >= 2\nweight: 12\nart: feather\ntags: nature social\nscene: Chickens fleeing across a lawn in every direction, an old woman in a straw hat waving a broom, students diving after birds.\ntext: Forty chickens are loose on the main lawn, pursued by an old woman in a straw hat. \"Don't just stand there, dearie!\" she shouts at you. \"Grab the speckled one, she's the ringleader!\"\n> left: Keep walking\n  say: You hear a crash, a squawk and a triumphant cackle behind you. The chickens, it seems, are caught.\n> right: Grab the speckled one\n  meet maud\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      rel maud +medium\n      say: You tackle the ringleader. The rest give up. \"Good hands,\" says Old Maud, pressing a warm egg into your palm. \"Come for tea.\"\n    lose:\n      rel maud +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: The ringleader escapes and pecks you, but Maud is impressed by your enthusiasm. \"Come for tea. Bring plasters.\"\n\n@event maud_honey\ntitle: Honey for a Cold\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 3 and (has(sick) or health < 40)\nweight: 18\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: A small cottage kitchen with bundles of herbs hanging from the beams, an old woman pouring golden honey into a steaming mug.\ntext: Maud spots you sniffling across the lawn and drags you into her cottage. \"Sit,\" she orders, pouring honey into something steaming that smells of lemon and thunder.\n> left: Drink it all\n  health +medium\n  lose sick\n  rel maud +small\n  say: It tastes like summer and lightning. You sneeze sparks for the rest of the day. By morning you're cured and slightly glowing.\n> right: Drink it and help with chores\n  health +small\n  lose sick\n  rel maud +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You sip and sort herbs while she tells you the names of every plant in the room. You remember most of them.\n\n@event maud_old_student\ntitle: Maud Was a Student Once\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: (rel(maud) >= 9 or (clue(lead_maud_friend) and rel(maud) >= 4)) and clock(hollowstar) >= 1\nweight: 14\nart: moon\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: An old woman sitting on a bench by the lake at dusk, looking at the water as if it might look back.\ntext: \"I was a student here, you know,\" Maud says, watching the lake. \"Sixty years ago. The lake froze one spring. Three of my friends went down to the boathouse that night. Only two came back.\" She doesn't look at you. \"Ask me what I know.\"\n> left: \"You don't have to tell me.\"\n  rel maud +medium\n  queue maud_tea_tells in 1\n  say: She pats your hand. \"Kind. But time's short, dearie.\" She tells you anyway, a little at a time, over many cups of tea.\n> right: \"What do you know?\"\n  rel maud +small\n  mood -small\n  flag knows_founders_seal\n  find knows_founders_seal\n  hs_omens_progress\n  find lead_seventh_tower\n  say: \"The founders built this place as a lid. Seven towers, and the key kept in the one nobody climbs.\" She finally looks at you. \"The thing below loves a lonely heart. Don't be lonely.\" It's a long walk back in the dark.\n\n@event maud_bees\ntitle: The Silver Bees\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 6 and month >= 7 and month <= 10\nweight: 10\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: Wooden hives in a wildflower meadow, an old woman in a veiled hat lifting a frame dripping with silver honey, bees glinting in the sun.\ntext: \"The silver bees need moving to the summer meadow,\" Maud says, handing you a veiled hat. \"They're friendly. Mostly. Don't make any sudden thoughts.\"\n> left: Help with the hives\n  challenge feat apprentice- wild:\n    win:\n      health +small\n      rel maud +medium\n      gold +small\n      say: The bees hum around you like a blessing. Maud gives you a jar of silver honey that sells for eight crowns. You eat half first.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel maud +small\n      say: You have a sudden thought. The bees know. You spend the evening with vinegar on your stings.\n> right: Watch from a safe distance\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You watch Maud move hives barehanded, singing to them. It's the calmest you've felt all month.\n\n# Review fixes (G): the kind answer to maud_old_student; she tells you anyway, over tea.\n@event maud_tea_tells\ntitle: Over Many Cups of Tea\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nactor: maud\nart: cup\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: A cluttered cottage kitchen at dusk, two teacups and a pot between an old woman and a student, the lake grey in the window.\ntext: Maud keeps her word, a little at a time. Over a month of afternoons and more tea than is good for anyone, the story of that spring comes out in pieces: the boathouse, the cold, the friend who didn't come back.\n> left: Listen to the end\n  rel maud +small\n  mood -small\n  flag knows_founders_seal\n  find knows_founders_seal\n  hs_omens_progress\n  find lead_seventh_tower\n  say: \"The founders built this place as a lid,\" she says on the last afternoon. \"Seven towers, and the key kept in the one nobody climbs. The thing below loves a lonely heart. Don't be lonely.\" She pours another cup.\n> right: Tell her it can wait\n  rel maud +medium\n  queue maud_tea_tells in 2\n  say: \"It can't, dearie,\" she says, \"but I like you for saying so.\" She tells you about her chickens instead. You'll come back for the rest.\n\n@event maud_ill\ntitle: Maud Is Unwell\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: maud\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 12 and year >= 2 and not flag(maud_ill_done)\nweight: 12\nart: house\ntags: social\nscene: A small cottage bedroom with a patchwork quilt, an old woman propped up on pillows, a student reading aloud by candlelight.\ntext: Maud's chickens are running wild, unfed. You find her in bed, grey-faced and coughing. \"Just a chill,\" she wheezes. \"I'm eighty-one. I'm allowed.\"\n> left: Fetch Sister Amaranth\n  flag maud_ill_done\n  rel maud +medium\n  say: Amaranth brings tonics and a stern lecture. Maud is back on her feet in a week, grumbling about fuss.\n> right: Look after her yourself for a week\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  flag maud_ill_done\n  rel maud +large\n  study -small\n  gain blessed\n  say: You feed chickens, brew teas, read her the newspaper. When she's well, she gives you a sprig of rosemary. \"You'll do,\" she says. \"You'll do.\"\n  if knows(maud.soup):\n    rel maud +tiny\n    say: On the third day you make her own soup, from memory, since you're not allowed to write it down. She tastes it, narrows her eyes and says it wants more pepper. It doesn't.\n\n# ================= HEADMISTRESS ISOLDE MARROW =================\n\n@event isolde_intro\ntime: evening\ntitle: She Knows Your Name\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nactor: isolde\nwhen: not met(isolde) and turn >= 3 and year == 1 and term\nweight: 5\nart: crown\ntags: story\nscene: A tall pale woman in dark robes pausing behind a student's chair in a candlelit dining hall, a hand resting lightly on the chair back.\ntext: At dinner, a cool hand rests on the back of your chair. The headmistress. \"{player},\" she says, though you've never met. \"Your marks in Arcana are interesting. Your marks in punctuality, less so.\" She moves on.\n> left: Stammer an apology\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  say: She's already three tables away. Every student between you sits a little straighter.\n> right: \"Interesting how, Headmistress?\"\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +small\n  renown +small\n  say: She pauses. Turns. Almost smiles. \"Ask me again at the end of your first year,\" she says. The table stares at you.\n\n@event isolde_tea\ntitle: Tea in the Tower\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: renown >= 25 and rel(isolde) >= 0\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social\nscene: A round tower study at twilight, two delicate teacups on a small table, a headmistress pouring tea with a perfectly steady hand.\ntext: An invitation to tea with the headmistress. Students talk about these the way soldiers talk about battles. She pours. \"Tell me, {player}: what do you want to become?\"\n> left: Give an ambitious answer\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel isolde +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: She listens without interrupting, then says, \"Good. Ambition without arrogance. Rarer than you'd think.\"\n    lose:\n      rel isolde -tiny\n      say: She nods politely. \"Ambition is a fine servant,\" she says, \"and a poor master.\" You're dismissed shortly after.\n> right: Admit you don't know yet\n  rel isolde +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: \"Neither did I, at your age,\" she says. \"I still don't, some days. That's the point of an academy.\" She refills your cup.\n\n@event isolde_warning\ntitle: A Quiet Warning\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ntrigger: social\nactor: isolde\nwhen: has(detention) or has(watched) or has(probation)\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: crisis\nscene: A headmistress walking alongside a student in a long colonnade, hands clasped behind her back, speaking without looking at them.\ntext: The headmistress falls into step beside you in the colonnade. \"I hear your name more often than I'd like,\" she says pleasantly. \"In the wrong rooms. I'd hate to hear it in mine.\"\n> left: Promise to do better\n  rel isolde +small\n  mood -tiny\n  lose watched\n  say: \"Good.\" She stops walking. You keep going. When you look back, she's gone.\n> right: Defend yourself\n  challenge negotiate journeyman +1 if renown >= 25:\n    win:\n      rel isolde +small\n      lose watched\n      say: You lay it out plainly. She listens, then nods once. \"Fair. I'll have a word with certain prefects.\"\n    lose:\n      rel isolde -small\n      say: \"Hm,\" she says. \"Let's hope you're right.\" It doesn't feel like hope.\n\n# ---------------- Track leads met on a card of their own (playtest 2, N1): queued by enrolling or joining ----------------\n\n@event vey_intro\ntitle: Tea That Isn't There\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: vey\nwhen: not met(vey) and enrolled(illusion)\nweight: 30\nart: mask\ntags: study magic\nscene: A lecture room hung with velvet curtains, a woman in a violet coat pouring from an empty teapot into a student's empty cup.\ntext: First Illusion lecture. Professor Vey pours you a cup of tea from a pot you would swear is empty, and waits. \"Milk?\" she asks. The whole row turns to see what you'll do with a cup of nothing.\n> left: Drink it, and thank her\n  meet vey\n  challenge deceive using charm apprentice+:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      rel vey +small\n      say: You sip, sigh and ask where she buys it. Vey's mouth twitches. \"Lapsang. There was never any tea, of course. Sit down; you'll do.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You raise the cup with a flourish and tip it a little too far. Nothing pours out, and the whole row sees nothing pour out. Vey writes something down, and smiles at it.\n> right: Say the cup is empty\n  meet vey\n  lore +small\n  say: \"It's empty,\" you say. \"So it is,\" says Vey, pleased and disappointed at once. \"Honest eyes. We shall have to do something about those.\"\n\n@event brask_intro\ntitle: Shovel. There. Go.\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: brask\nwhen: not met(brask) and enrolled(beastcraft)\nweight: 30\nart: paw\ntags: study nature\nscene: A long stable in morning light, a grey-haired professor talking earnestly to a hippogriff while a student stands holding a shovel.\ntext: First Beastcraft lesson, at the stables. Professor Brask is explaining to a hippogriff, in full and courteous sentences, that the new students will be clumsy and she is to be patient with them. Then she turns to you. \"Shovel. There. Go.\"\n> left: Muck out without a word\n  meet brask\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel brask +small\n  say: You shovel until your arms shake. Brask watches, then tells the hippogriff, loud enough for you to hear, \"This one might do.\" To you she says nothing, which you later learn is praise.\n> right: Ask what today's lesson is\n  meet brask\n  lore +small\n  rel brask -tiny\n  say: Brask tells the hippogriff a story about a student who asked questions before shovelling. It ends with a bite. \"Moral: shovel.\" You shovel, a little behind the others.\n\n@event pell_first_shift\ntitle: Count Aloud\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: pell\nwhen: not met(pell) and member(apothecary_clerk)\nweight: 30\nart: bottle\ntags: job\nscene: A narrow apothecary lined with tiny labelled drawers, an old man on a ladder holding out a glass dropper to a nervous new clerk.\ntext: Your first shift at Pell & Daughters. Old Pell climbs down from his ladder, hands you a dropper and a bottle of tincture, and points at a waiting customer. \"Twelve drops,\" he says. \"Aloud. Everyone counts aloud in this shop. The drawers like it.\"\n> left: Count every drop aloud\n  meet pell\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"One. Two. Three.\" Your voice fills the shop; the customer joins in at nine. Pell nods once. \"Twelve. Not eleven. Not thirteen. You'll count like that until they bury you.\"\n> right: Count in your head, quickly\n  meet pell\n  challenge brew apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel pell -tiny\n      renown +tiny\n      say: Twelve drops, done in a breath; the customer tells the whole queue how quick the new clerk is. Pell counts the level against the light. \"Correct. Next time, aloud. I like to hear a thing being right.\"\n    lose:\n      rel pell -small\n      say: You lose count at eight and guess. Pell tips the bottle out and starts it again, aloud, without a word to you. The customer leaves with the right dose and a look at you.\n"},{"path":"content/events/people_students.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Character events — fellow students.\n#  Intros are `trigger: random` (they introduce the NPC).\n#  Everything else is `trigger: social`: drawn from the monthly\n#  social slot, only once the NPC has been met.\n#  Romance arc per character: spark (generic) -> love >= 35 ->\n#  confession -> partner events. See social.grim for generic ones.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ================= LYRA VANESHADE =================\n\n@event lyra_intro\ntitle: The Last Copy\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: lyra\nwhen: not met(lyra) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: book\ntags: social rival\nscene: Two hands reaching for the same red-bound book on a library shelf; one hand wears a signet ring with a flame crest.\ntext: You reach for the last copy of \"Foundations of Flame\" and a ringed hand gets there first. \"I need it more,\" says a tall girl with crimson hair, not looking at you. \"You'll only underline the wrong bits.\"\n> left: Let her have it\n  meet lyra\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  say: \"At least you know your place,\" she says, which is somehow worse than rudeness. Lyra Vaneshade. Everyone knows the name.\n> right: Argue for it\n  meet lyra\n  challenge appeal vs lyra.charm:\n    win:\n      rel lyra +medium\n      say: You quote chapter three at her. She raises an eyebrow, then hands you the book. \"Fine. Return it underlined correctly.\"\n    lose:\n      rel lyra -small\n      say: She wins the argument in four sentences and leaves with the book. You think of the perfect comeback that night.\n\n@pool people_students_lyra_social_routine\nactor: lyra\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nweight: 12\n\n@event lyra_letter\ntitle: A Letter With a Black Seal\npool: people_students_lyra_social_routine\ntone: light\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 6 and (year > 1 or month > 4)\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A girl sitting on a window seat in an empty corridor, crushing a letter with a black wax seal in her fist, face turned away.\ntext: You find Lyra on a window seat with a crumpled letter. Black seal. Her family's. She's not crying; Vaneshades don't. But she's not quite not crying either.\n> left: Pretend you didn't see\n  say: You walk past. Later, she's flawless again, as if nothing happened. Maybe nothing did.\n> right: Sit down beside her\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +large\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  flag lyra_vulnerable\n  say: \"Second place,\" she says eventually. \"I came second in the Winter Trials. My mother wants to know who beat me.\" She almost laughs. \"It's always someone.\"\n\n@event lyra_study_rivalry\ntime: morning noon\ntitle: Top of the Class\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) > -24 and term and not wrapup\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 4\nart: scroll\ntags: social study rival\nscene: Two students at opposite ends of a long library table, surrounded by towers of books, glaring over the tops of them.\ntext: The professors post this week's rankings, with an open paper this afternoon for anyone who wants to improve their place. Lyra is first{&. You're close enough to see the back of her head.|, again. You're close enough to read her handwriting.|. Of course she is. You're one name below her, which is somehow worse than three.} She notices you noticing.\n> left: Suggest studying together\n  mood -tiny\n  lore +small\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: {&\"Together?\" She considers this like a foreign custom. Then she pulls out a chair. She's an excellent, merciless study partner.|She already has two cups of tea and your chair pulled out. \"You're late,\" she says. You aren't.|\"Fine. But I'm quizzing you first.\" She is merciless again, and you're better for it.}\n> right: Beat her outright\n  challenge research vs lyra.lore:\n    win:\n      study +small\n      rel lyra +small\n      renown +small\n      say: The afternoon paper puts you first. Lyra stares at the list for a long time. Then she finds you and says, \"Good.\" She means it.\n    lose:\n      study +tiny\n      rel lyra -tiny\n      say: {&You cram through lunch and come third. Lyra is still first. She doesn't gloat. That's worse.|Second, by one mark. Lyra leaves a note on your desk: \"Close.\" You keep it.|You come fourth. Lyra looks almost disappointed on your behalf, which is unbearable.}\n\n@event lyra_family_gala\ntitle: The Vaneshade Winter Gala\npool: people_students_lyra_social_routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 18 and month >= 3 and month <= 6\nart: crown\ntags: social romance\nscene: A glittering ballroom in a noble house, candles floating in mid-air, a tall woman in crimson watching the crowd like a hawk.\ntext: \"My family's winter gala,\" Lyra says, too casually. \"I need a guest who won't embarrass me. You'll do. Probably. You'll need proper clothes.\"\n> left: Decline\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: \"Of course.\" A pause. \"It doesn't matter.\" It clearly does.\n> right: Go with her (15 gold for clothes)\n  gold -medium\n  charm +tiny\n  renown +small\n  spark lyra +small\n  rel lyra +small\n  chance unlikely -1 if has(origin_noble):\n    mood -small\n    say: Lyra's mother asks who your parents are, and then asks no further questions at all. Lyra squeezes your hand under the table, hard.\n  else:\n    mood +medium\n    say: You dance, you charm a duke, you don't spill anything. In the carriage home, Lyra falls asleep on your shoulder.\n\n@event lyra_confession\nramp: spark(lyra) 25..40\ntitle: Lyra Doesn't Lose\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: spark(lyra) >= 25 and rel(lyra) >= 18 and courting(lyra)\nweight: 30\nart: flame\ntags: romance\nscene: A girl with crimson hair standing very close in a practice ring at dusk, sparks still drifting from her wand, cheeks flushed.\ntext: After practice, Lyra doesn't leave. \"I don't lose,\" she says, staring at the scorch marks. \"I don't. But I think — \" She stops. Starts again. \"I think I've lost to you. Say something before I set something on fire.\"\n> left: Let her down gently\n  spark lyra -large\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  say: She nods, sharp and quick. \"Understood.\" She practises alone for a month. Then one day she nods to you in the corridor, and it's almost all right.\n> right: Kiss her\n  bond lyra partner\n  spark lyra +large\n  mood +large\n  say: Nothing catches fire. Well. Something does, but it's only the practice dummy, and neither of you notices for a while.\n\n@event lyra_family_ultimatum\ntitle: The Ultimatum\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"partner\" and turn >= 14\nweight: 18\nart: crown\ntags: romance crisis\nscene: Lyra holding a letter with a black seal, her other hand clenched around a ring she has just pulled off.\ntext: Another black-sealed letter. Lyra reads it twice and goes white. \"My mother has found out about us. End it, she says, or she ends my inheritance.\" She looks at you. \"Tell me what to do.\"\n> left: \"Choose yourself, not them.\"\n  if spark(lyra) >= 50:\n    challenge negotiate vs lyra.charm likely:\n      win:\n        rel lyra +large\n        spark lyra +medium\n        flag lyra_free\n        say: She tears the letter in half. Then in quarters. Then she sets the quarters on fire and laughs like she hasn't in years.\n      lose:\n        rel lyra -small\n        spark lyra -medium\n        mood -medium\n        say: \"Easy for you to say,\" she snaps. She doesn't mean it. She also doesn't take it back.\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate vs lyra.charm:\n      win:\n        rel lyra +large\n        spark lyra +medium\n        flag lyra_free\n        say: She tears the letter in half. Then in quarters. Then she sets the quarters on fire and laughs like she hasn't in years.\n      lose:\n        rel lyra -small\n        spark lyra -medium\n        mood -medium\n        say: \"Easy for you to say,\" she snaps. She doesn't mean it. She also doesn't take it back.\n> right: Step back so she doesn't lose everything\n  bond lyra ex\n  gain heartbroken\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: You end it before she has to. She doesn't argue. That's how you know how much it costs her.\n\n@event lyra_rival_sabotage\ntitle: Sabotage in Class\npool: people_students_lyra_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"rival\" and rel(lyra) <= -4\nart: potion\ntags: rival\nscene: A cauldron spitting black smoke, a crimson-haired student walking away with a tiny vial hidden in her sleeve.\ntext: Your perfect potion turns black and starts screaming. Across the room, Lyra is very carefully not looking at you.\n> left: Retaliate\n  challenge deceive vs lyra.guile:\n    win:\n      rel lyra -small\n      renown +small\n      say: Next lesson, her wand produces only bubbles. Pink ones. She knows. You both know. It's war. It's sort of fun.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel lyra -medium\n      say: You're caught slipping something into her bag. She looks genuinely delighted.\n> right: Confront her directly\n  mood -tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  say: \"That was beneath you.\" She flushes. \"...Yes. It was.\" The next day there's a perfect replacement potion on your desk.\n\n@event lyra_burnout\ntime: morning\ntitle: Lyra Falls\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 12 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: cross\ntags: social\nscene: A girl collapsed across a library desk among scattered notes, quill still in hand, candle burned down to nothing.\ntext: You find Lyra face-down on a library desk at dawn, not asleep — out cold. She's been studying for three days straight. \"Don't tell anyone,\" she mumbles when she comes round. \"Mother can't know.\"\n> left: Take her to the infirmary anyway\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  say: She's furious. Then she sleeps for nineteen hours. When she wakes up, she says, quietly, \"Thank you.\"\n> right: Cover for her and make her rest\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  study -tiny\n  say: You tell her professors she has a cold and sit by her door with your homework. She sleeps. You keep watch.\n\n# ================= TOBIAS FENNICK =================\n\n@event tobias_intro\ntitle: The Lost Boy\nclass: windfall\ntone: light\nactor: tobias\nwhen: not met(tobias) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: compass\ntags: social\nscene: A large, gentle-faced boy leading a crying first-year by the hand down the wrong corridor, both very lost.\ntext: A big, broad-shouldered boy is leading a crying first-year through the corridors. \"Don't worry, I know exactly where the infirmary is,\" he says, and walks confidently into a broom cupboard.\n> left: Point them the right way\n  meet tobias\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: \"Ah. Left, you say. Right. I mean, correct.\" He grins, enormous and sheepish. \"Tobias. Turnip scholarship. Thanks.\"\n> right: Walk with them\n  meet tobias\n  rel tobias +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: You end up taking the scenic route, and the first-year stops crying because Tobias is telling a very long story about a goat.\n\n@event tobias_homesick\ntitle: Homesick\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 6\nweight: 12\nart: leaf\ntags: social nature\nscene: A dormitory windowsill crowded with clay pots of seedlings, a big boy watering them with a tiny can.\ntext: Tobias has gone quiet. He's planted seedlings in every cup in the common room. \"Harvest at home,\" he says. \"First one I've missed. Ma's doing it alone.\"\n> left: Cheer him up with a joke\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: Your joke is terrible. He laughs anyway, the big rumbling laugh, and that seems to help.\n> right: Help him build a window garden\n  gold -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You buy seeds and a stack of pots. By the end of the week the common room smells of tomato leaves. Tobias whistles while he waters them.\n\n@event tobias_bad_harvest\ntitle: The Harvest Failed\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 15 and turn >= 8 and not flag(tobias_harvest_done)\nweight: 14\nart: rain\ntags: social money\nscene: A boy packing a battered trunk in an empty dormitory, a letter with smudged handwriting open on the bed.\ntext: Tobias is packing. Blight took the harvest. His family needs money, and hands. \"Thirty crowns would get them through winter,\" he says, not asking. \"But I haven't got thirty crowns. I've got two arms.\"\n> left: Persuade him to stay and find work here\n  flag tobias_harvest_done\n  challenge negotiate vs tobias.charm:\n    win:\n      rel tobias +medium\n      say: You walk him to the job board. He signs up for double shifts at the stables and sends every crown home. He stays.\n    lose:\n      rel tobias +tiny\n      mood -small\n      flag tobias_went_home\n      say: He listens, nods, and takes the barge in the morning. He's back in two months, thinner and quieter.\n> right: Give him thirty crowns\n  when: gold >= 30\n  flag tobias_harvest_done\n  gold -large\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel tobias +huge\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: He tries to refuse three times. Then he hugs you so hard your spine cracks. \"I'll pay you back. Every crown. Every one.\"\n  queue tobias_repays in 6\n\n@event tobias_repays\ntitle: Every Crown\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nactor: tobias\nart: pouch\ntags: social money\nscene: A clumsy hand-stitched pouch of coins and a jar of honey left on a dormitory pillow.\ntext: A lumpy, hand-stitched pouch on your pillow: thirty crowns exactly, in small coins. And a jar of honey from home. A note in big, careful letters: \"TOLD YOU. — T.\"\n> ok: Smile\n  gold +large\n  rel tobias +small\n  mood +small\n  say: The honey tastes of a farm you have never seen and somehow already miss.\n\n@event tobias_godparent\ntitle: The Mandrake Baby\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 9\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: social nature\nscene: A tiny mandrake in a pot wearing a knitted hat, a beaming boy presenting it like a newborn.\ntext: Tobias presents a flowerpot containing a very small mandrake in a knitted hat. \"This is Gerald,\" he says. \"I'd like you to be his godparent.\" Gerald screams softly.\n> left: Politely decline\n  rel tobias -tiny\n  say: \"No pressure,\" says Tobias, visibly pressured. Gerald glares at you from under his hat.\n> right: Accept with honour\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  mood +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: You are now Gerald's godparent. Gerald bites you. Tobias says this means Gerald loves you.\n\n@event tobias_bullied\ntitle: Tobias and the Prefect\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: met(bram) and rel(tobias) >= 3\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: social rival\nscene: A burly prefect mocking a big farm boy in a crowded corridor, students sniggering, the boy's ears bright red.\ntext: Bram is doing an impression of Tobias's accent in the corridor. People are laughing. Tobias is pretending not to hear, ears burning red.\n> left: Stay out of it\n  rel tobias -small\n  say: Tobias doesn't mention it. He doesn't sit with you at dinner, either.\n> right: Step in\n  alignment +small\n  rel tobias +large\n  rel bram -medium\n  challenge negotiate vs bram.charm wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      say: \"Funny,\" you say. \"Do your father next.\" The laughter turns on Bram. He stalks off. Tobias stares at you in awe.\n    lose:\n      gain watched\n      say: Bram turns his attention to you instead, with interest. Tobias brings you a pie later. A whole pie.\n\n@event tobias_confession\nramp: spark(tobias) 25..40\ntitle: A Confession, With Vegetables\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: spark(tobias) >= 25 and rel(tobias) >= 18 and courting(tobias)\nweight: 30\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: A big boy holding out a perfect pumpkin like an offering in a greenhouse, cheeks scarlet.\ntext: Tobias corners you in the greenhouse holding a very large pumpkin. \"I grew this,\" he says. \"For you. It's the best one. I didn't know how else to — I like you. Like that. I've practised this on the pumpkin. Sorry.\"\n> left: Let him down gently\n  spark tobias -large\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: He nods a lot. \"That's all right. That's fine. You can still have the pumpkin.\" You keep it until it rots, out of loyalty.\n> right: Say yes\n  bond tobias partner\n  spark tobias +large\n  mood +large\n  say: He drops the pumpkin. It's fine; pumpkins are sturdy. So, it turns out, are his hugs.\n\n@event tobias_picnic\ntitle: A Picnic in the Orchard\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: bond(tobias) == \"partner\" and month >= 7 and month <= 10\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 10\nart: tree\ntags: romance nature\nscene: A checked blanket under blossoming apple trees, a basket of bread and cheese, two students lying in the dappled light.\ntext: Tobias has packed a picnic: {~bread he baked, cheese he begged from the kitchens, apples from the orchard he has befriended|a pie with your initials in the crust, slightly burnt, and a jar of honey from someone he helped|plums, hard cheese and something he calls a salad, which is mostly flowers}. \"No {~exams|essays|professors} for six hours,\" he announces. \"I checked.\"\n> left: Bring your books anyway\n  lore +small\n  spark tobias -tiny\n  say: You study on the blanket. He eyes the books, sighs a very small sigh, and falls asleep with his head in your lap. It's still lovely.\n> right: Leave everything behind\n  mood +medium\n  health +small\n  spark tobias +small\n  say: The afternoon goes golden and slow. You can't remember the last time you didn't think about grades.\n\n# ================= SERAPHINE HALE =================\n\n@event seraphine_intro\ntitle: Your Left Shoe\nclass: windfall\ntone: eerie\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: not met(seraphine) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: eye\ntags: social mystery\nscene: A slight girl with silver-grey eyes and a scarf of stars passing in a corridor, pointing absently at someone's feet.\ntext: A girl drifts past you in the corridor and says, without stopping, \"You're going to lose your left shoe today. Don't worry, it's under the stairs in the east wing.\"\n> left: Ignore her\n  meet seraphine\n  say: That afternoon a staircase moves and eats your left shoe. It is, eventually, under the stairs in the east wing.\n> right: Ask how she knows\n  meet seraphine\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  say: \"I don't know things,\" she says. \"Things know me.\" Then: \"Seraphine. You'll want the shoe back by four.\" You get it back at 3:58.\n\n@event seraphine_tarot\ntitle: The Cards\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 3\nweight: 12\nart: cards\ntags: social mystery\nscene: A tarot spread on a velvet cloth in candlelight, a girl's pale fingers turning over a card showing a lightning-struck tower.\ntext: Seraphine shuffles a battered tarot deck. \"One card,\" she says. \"For you. Only if you want it. They're quite rude sometimes.\"\n> left: Draw a card\n  chance even:\n    mood +small\n    say: The Star. \"Hope,\" she says, delighted. \"And someone's going to tell you a secret. Oh — it's me. I like you.\" She blushes.\n    rel seraphine +small\n    spark seraphine +small\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: The Tower. She looks at it a long time. \"It's fine,\" she says. \"Towers fall. That's what they're for.\"\n    rel seraphine +small\n> right: Decline\n  rel seraphine +tiny\n  say: \"Good,\" she says, putting the deck away. \"Knowing is overrated.\"\n\n@event seraphine_lake_vision\ntitle: She Walks Into the Lake\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 6 and month >= 3 and month <= 9\nweight: 10\nart: wave\ntags: social danger\nscene: A girl in a nightgown wading into a misty lake at night, eyes glowing silver, arms outstretched toward something under the water.\ntext: From your window you see Seraphine walking into the lake in her nightgown, eyes shining silver, already up to her waist.\n> left: Wake a prefect\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: The prefect drags her out, coughing. She doesn't remember anything. She looks at you strangely for days.\n> right: Run down and pull her out\n  alignment +small\n  challenge feat apprentice likely:\n    win:\n      rel seraphine +large\n      spark seraphine +small\n      flag seraphine_lake_told\n      say: You reach her just as she goes under. On the shore she wakes and whispers, \"It was calling me. It called you too.\" Then she holds on very tight.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      gain sick\n      rel seraphine +large\n      say: You both nearly drown. You both come down with Lake Fever. She brings you soup that tastes faintly of the future.\n\n@event seraphine_future\ntitle: I Saw You Old\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 15\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: social mystery\nscene: Two students sitting on the rim of a dry fountain, one looking up at the stars, the other looking at the first with a soft smile.\ntext: \"I saw you old,\" Seraphine says suddenly. \"Last night. You were happy. Really happy. There was someone with you.\" She pauses. \"Do you want to know who?\"\n> left: Yes, tell me\n  chance even:\n    spark seraphine +small\n    say: She looks at you for a long time. \"I'll tell you when you're old,\" she says, and goes pink.\n  else:\n    mood +small\n    say: \"Someone kind,\" she says. \"That's all I could see. Their hands were kind.\"\n> right: No, let it be a surprise\n  mood -tiny\n  rel seraphine +medium\n  say: She smiles like you've given her a present. \"That's the right answer. Nobody ever gives the right answer.\" Not knowing nags at you for days.\n  if knows(seraphine.self):\n    rel seraphine +tiny\n    say: Later you wonder whether she could see the someone only because you were standing next to her when she looked. You don't ask. She goes pink anyway, as if you had.\n\n@event seraphine_overwhelmed\ntitle: Too Many Tomorrows\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 9 and year >= 2 and term\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: social\nscene: A girl curled in a dark corner of an observatory with her hands over her eyes, a teacup gone cold beside her.\ntext: Seraphine hasn't come to class in a week. You find her in a dark storeroom with her hands over her eyes. \"It's too loud,\" she whispers. \"Everyone's futures. All at once.\"\n> left: Fetch Sister Amaranth\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: Amaranth gives her a sleeping draught and a dark room. She recovers. She doesn't say much about it.\n> right: Sit with her in the dark\n  rel seraphine +large\n  spark seraphine +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You don't speak. After an hour she takes her hands from her eyes. \"Yours is quiet,\" she says. \"Your future. It's very quiet. I like it.\"\n\n@event seraphine_confession\nramp: spark(seraphine) 25..40\ntitle: She Already Knew\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: spark(seraphine) >= 25 and rel(seraphine) >= 18 and courting(seraphine)\nweight: 30\nart: star\ntags: romance\nscene: A girl with silver eyes holding out a single falling-star seed in her palm on a dark observatory balcony.\ntext: \"I've seen this conversation forty-one times,\" Seraphine says. \"In twenty-nine of them, you say yes. I've been very nervous about the other twelve.\" She holds out her hand. \"I like you. That part was always the same.\"\n> left: Gently say no\n  spark seraphine -large\n  rel seraphine +tiny\n  say: She nods. \"Twelve,\" she says softly. \"I knew it might be twelve.\" She's kind to you afterwards. Somehow that hurts more.\n> right: Take her hand\n  bond seraphine partner\n  spark seraphine +large\n  mood +large\n  say: \"Twenty-nine,\" she whispers, and laughs, and for once she's entirely in the present.\n\n# ================= CORVIN ASHBY =================\n\n@event corvin_intro\ntitle: Light Fingers\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: corvin\nwhen: not met(corvin) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: mask\ntags: social\nscene: A dark-haired student with a crooked grin holding up a purse between two fingers, mid-bow.\ntext: Someone bumps you in the crowd. A moment later a dark-haired boy is bowing and holding up your purse. \"You dropped this,\" he says. \"Into my pocket. Terrible habit of yours. Corvin Ashby.\"\n> left: Snatch it back and report him\n  meet corvin\n  rel corvin -small\n  say: He's gone before the prefect arrives. Your purse is all there, plus an IOU you don't remember receiving.\n> right: Laugh\n  gold -tiny\n  meet corvin\n  rel corvin +medium\n  guile +tiny\n  say: \"Oh, I like you,\" he says, and teaches you the trick on the spot. You practise it on him. He lets you win. Probably. Later, you are three crowns short. Probably a coincidence.\n\n@event corvin_kitchen_raid\ntime: night\ntitle: The Kitchen Raid\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 3\nweight: 12\nart: meal\ntags: social\nscene: Two students creeping through a cavernous castle kitchen at night, a mountain of tarts on a table, a sleeping cook snoring by the hearth.\ntext: \"Midnight,\" Corvin whispers. \"The cooks have made forty treacle tarts for the faculty dinner. Forty. Nobody needs forty.\"\n> left: Not tonight\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  say: He goes alone. The next morning there's a treacle tart on your pillow. You decide not to ask.\n  if knows(corvin.last_warning):\n    rel corvin +tiny\n    say: He only took the one, he tells you later, because you'd reminded him about his last warning. It's the closest he comes to saying thank you.\n> right: Lead the raid\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +medium\n      rel corvin +medium\n      say: You get out with nine tarts and a bottle of elderflower cordial. You eat them on the roof until sunrise.\n    lose:\n      gain detention\n      rel corvin +medium\n      say: The cook wakes up. You both spend a week scrubbing pans, laughing too hard to work.\n> up[shadowstep]: Step past the cook\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood +medium\n  rel corvin +medium\n  say: You step from shadow to shadow across the kitchen, past the sleeping cook, and back with an armful of tarts before Corvin has finished picking the lock. He stares at you. \"Saint,\" he says, \"where did you learn that?\"\n\n@event corvin_debt\ntitle: Corvin's Little Problem\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 12 and not flag(corvin_owes) and not flag(corvin_debt_refused)\nweight: 12\nart: chain\ntags: social money\nscene: A boy with a black eye sitting on the dormitory stairs, trying very hard to look casual.\ntext: Corvin has a black eye and a very casual expression. \"Small misunderstanding with some trolls,\" he says. \"Thirty crowns would clear it right up. I'll pay you back. Probably with interest. Possibly with a goat.\"\n> left: Refuse\n  flag corvin_debt_refused\n  rel corvin -medium\n  say: \"Course. No problem.\" He has another black eye the next week. He's still friendly. Mostly.\n> right: Give him thirty crowns\n  when: gold >= 30\n  gold -large\n  alignment +tiny\n  flag corvin_owes\n  rel corvin +large\n  queue corvin_repays in 4\n  say: He stares at the coins, then at you. \"No one's ever just... done that.\" He's quiet for a whole minute. A record.\n\n@event corvin_repays\ntitle: Interest\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nactor: corvin\nart: coin\ntags: social money\nscene: A small brass key and a fat purse left on a desk with a playing card, the jack of spades, tucked under them.\ntext: There's a fat purse on your desk and a jack of spades tucked under it. It's heavy, and it clinks.\n> left: Count it\n  chance likely:\n    gold +huge\n    rel corvin +medium\n    say: Sixty crowns. A note: \"Interest. Don't ask. — C.\" You don't ask.\n  else:\n    gold +tiny\n    rel corvin +small\n    say: Three crowns and a very convincing IOU for the other twenty-seven, with a doodle of a goat.\n> right: Find him and ask where it came from\n  rel corvin +large\n  gold +large\n  say: \"I won it,\" he says. \"Fairly. Mostly.\" He pushes the exact thirty into your hand. \"The rest's mine. I've got another debt to pay.\"\n\n@event corvin_rooftop\nslot: night\ntitle: Up on the Roof\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 18\nweight: 10\nart: moon\ntags: social\nscene: Two students sitting on a steep slate roof under a full moon, legs dangling over a dizzying drop, sharing a bottle of something fizzy.\ntext: On the roof at 2am, Corvin goes quiet. \"I'm on my last warning,\" he says. \"One more detention and they send me home. And home is... not a good place.\" He laughs, badly.\n> left: Tell him to keep his head down\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: \"Head down. Right. I'll try anything once.\" He keeps his head down for almost a month.\n> right: Promise you'll cover for him\n  rel corvin +large\n  spark corvin +tiny\n  flag covering_corvin\n  say: He looks at you like nobody has ever promised him anything. \"You're an idiot,\" he says. \"Thank you.\"\n\n@event corvin_framed\ntitle: Corvin Is Framed\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 9 and met(bram) and turn >= 10\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: social rival\nscene: A boy standing before a disciplinary desk, a stolen silver goblet on it, a smug prefect at his shoulder.\ntext: The headmistress's silver goblet has been found in Corvin's trunk. He swears he didn't take it. Bram, who found it, looks very pleased. Corvin is one detention from expulsion.\n> left: Stay out of it\n  rel corvin -large\n  chance even:\n    say: Corvin talks his way out, barely. He doesn't look at you for a month.\n  else:\n    rel corvin -large\n    say: Corvin is suspended for a month. When he comes back, he's different. Quieter.\n> right: Find proof he was framed\n  challenge deceive vs bram.guile:\n    win:\n      rel corvin +large\n      rel bram -large\n      renown +small\n      flag bram_sash_lost\n      say: You find the bill of sale: Bram bought a fake. Bram is stripped of his sash for the rest of the year. Corvin buys you a goat. An actual goat.\n    lose:\n      rel corvin +medium\n      gain watched\n      say: You can't prove it. But you stood up for him in front of everyone, and he'll never forget it. Neither will Bram.\n\n@event corvin_confession\nramp: spark(corvin) 25..40\ntitle: Corvin, Serious for Once\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: spark(corvin) >= 25 and rel(corvin) >= 18 and courting(corvin)\nweight: 30\nart: cards\ntags: romance\nscene: A boy holding out a playing card — the queen of hearts — with an unsteady hand, no grin for once.\ntext: Corvin holds out a playing card. The queen of hearts. His hand isn't quite steady. \"No trick,\" he says. \"No angle. I just — I like you more than I like anything, and I'm terrified, and I wanted you to have this.\"\n> left: Gently decline\n  spark corvin -large\n  rel corvin +tiny\n  say: He laughs, too quickly. \"Worth a shot.\" He keeps the card. You see it sometimes, tucked in his sleeve.\n> right: Take the card\n  bond corvin partner\n  spark corvin +large\n  mood +large\n  say: For once, Corvin Ashby has nothing to say. He just kisses you, and then grins, and then can't stop grinning.\n\n@event corvin_runaway\nslot: night\ntitle: Let's Leave Tonight\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: bond(corvin) == \"partner\" and final_year\nweight: 16\nart: map\ntags: romance danger\nscene: A small sky-barge tied to the academy dock at midnight, lanterns lit, a boy at the tiller holding out his hand.\ntext: Corvin's at your door at midnight, bag over his shoulder. \"There's a sky-barge at the dock and nobody guarding it. Let's go. Tonight. The world's huge and we're wasting it in classrooms.\"\n> left: Stay and finish what you started\n  rel corvin -small\n  spark corvin +tiny\n  say: He looks at you for a long time. Then he drops the bag. \"Fine. But after graduation, we steal a boat. A big one.\"\n> right: Take his hand\n  end ending_runaway\n\n# ================= MIR TEN =================\n\n@event mir_intro\ntitle: An Explosion in the West Wing\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: mir\nwhen: not met(mir) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: gear\ntags: social\nscene: A cloud of purple smoke billowing from a workshop door, a soot-covered student emerging with goggles and a thumbs-up.\ntext: The west wing explodes. Not badly. A figure in goggles stumbles out of the purple smoke, coughing, and gives you a thumbs-up. \"Don't worry! That was intentional! Mostly! Can you hold this?\" They hand you a ticking teapot.\n> left: Put the teapot down and back away\n  meet mir\n  rel mir +tiny\n  say: The teapot stops ticking and starts humming the academy anthem. \"Mir,\" they say. \"They/them. Thanks for not throwing it.\"\n> right: Hold the teapot steady\n  meet mir\n  challenge feat apprentice- wild:\n    win:\n      rel mir +medium\n      say: You keep it level while they twist a valve. It stops ticking. \"You've got good hands,\" Mir says. \"I'm going to need those.\"\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      rel mir +medium\n      say: The teapot goes off. Your eyebrows survive, mostly. Mir is apologising and laughing at the same time.\n\n@event mir_owl_gift\ntitle: A Present With Gears\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 18 and not has(familiar_owl)\nweight: 14\nart: owl\ntags: social magic\nscene: A small brass owl with glowing amber eyes perched on a student's wrist, its clockwork feathers fluffing.\ntext: Mir presents a brass owl with amber glass eyes. It blinks, hoots the hour and says \"HOMEWORK\" in a tinny voice. \"Made it for you. It remembers deadlines. And birthdays. And grudges, slightly.\"\n> left: It's too much\n  rel mir +small\n  say: Mir looks crushed, then rallies. \"I'll give it to Thorne. He needs reminding to smile.\" The owl, you hear, refuses to work for him.\n> right: Accept it gratefully\n  mood -tiny\n  gain familiar_owl\n  rel mir +medium\n  spark mir +tiny\n  say: The owl settles on your shoulder and says, \"WELCOME.\" Mir pretends to have something in their eye. At six the next morning, the owl says \"HOMEWORK.\" It says it every morning for the rest of the year.\n\n@event mir_fair\ntitle: The Artificers' Fair\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 9 and month >= 6 and month <= 9\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: social festival\nscene: A hall crammed with bizarre inventions — walking teapots, singing clocks — and one enormous brass machine draped in a sheet.\ntext: The Artificers' Fair. Mir's entry is under a sheet and occasionally growls. \"I need an assistant to stand next to it and look confident,\" they say. \"And possibly run.\"\n> left: Watch from a safe distance\n  mood +tiny\n  rel mir +tiny\n  say: The machine turns out to be a mechanical bear that bakes bread. It wins second place. Mir is ecstatic and flour-covered.\n  if knows(mir.family):\n    rel mir +tiny\n    say: You tell Mir, while they're still covered in flour, that nobody wins second prize at a hobby. They stop in the middle of a sentence.\n> right: Be the confident assistant\n  challenge perform apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      rel mir +medium\n      say: You present it like a showman. The bear bakes a perfect loaf and bows. First prize. Mir kisses the trophy, then, in a fluster, your cheek.\n      spark mir +tiny\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      renown +small\n      rel mir +small\n      say: The bear eats your sleeve and swats you into the flour bins on its way to the judges. The crowd loves it. Third prize and a very good story.\n\n@pool people_students_mir_social_routine\nactor: mir\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nweight: 10\ntags: social\n\n@event mir_workshop_closed\ntitle: The Workshop Is Shut\npool: people_students_mir_social_routine\ntone: light\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 12 and year >= 2\nart: lock\nscene: A workshop door chained shut with a faculty notice nailed to it, a forlorn student sitting on a toolbox outside.\ntext: After the third fire this term, the faculty has padlocked Mir's workshop. Mir is sitting on a toolbox outside it, looking like someone took their heart and filed it under \"hazards\".\n> left: Plead their case to Professor Thorne\n  challenge negotiate master-:\n    win:\n      rel mir +large\n      rel thorne +tiny\n      say: Thorne agrees to reopen it — with fire buckets and a weekly inspection. Mir cries on you. Oil and all.\n    lose:\n      rel mir +small\n      rel thorne -tiny\n      flag mir_workshop_shut\n      say: Thorne refuses. But Mir heard you try. \"Nobody's ever argued for me before,\" they say.\n> right: Help them build a secret workshop\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      rel mir +large\n      guile +tiny\n      flag mir_workshop_shut\n      say: An abandoned classroom behind a tapestry. You bring candles; Mir brings everything else. It's perfect. It's almost entirely fireproof.\n    lose:\n      rel mir +medium\n      gain detention\n      flag mir_workshop_shut\n      say: Bram finds the secret workshop on day two. Worth it, says Mir, scrubbing cauldrons beside you.\n\n@event mir_dance_machine\ntitle: The Dancing Machine\npool: people_students_mir_social_routine\ntone: light\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 6\nart: music\nscene: A spindly brass contraption with too many legs attempting a waltz in an empty classroom, a student dancing with it.\ntext: Mir has built a machine that teaches dancing. It has six legs and opinions. \"It needs a test subject,\" they say. \"It's mostly safe. It only kicked me twice.\"\n> left: Make Mir test it\n  rel mir -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You watch Mir and the machine waltz, badly, across the room. It kicks them twice; Mir gives you a look. It's the funniest thing you've ever seen.\n> right: Dance with the machine\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel mir +medium\n  say: It kicks you twice too. But by the end you can waltz, and Mir is clapping with oily hands.\n\n@event mir_confession\nramp: spark(mir) 25..40\ntitle: Blueprint\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: spark(mir) >= 25 and rel(mir) >= 18 and courting(mir)\nweight: 30\nart: gear\ntags: romance\nscene: A rolled-out blueprint on a workbench showing a heart-shaped mechanism labelled with tiny careful notes, a nervous artificer beside it.\ntext: Mir unrolls a blueprint. It's a mechanical heart, labelled in tiny careful handwriting: \"Does not work without the other part.\" They clear their throat. \"I'm bad at saying things. So I drew it. You're the other part. If you want.\"\n> left: Kindly say no\n  spark mir -large\n  rel mir +small\n  say: Mir rolls up the blueprint carefully. \"Good to know. Data is data.\" They're fine. They're mostly fine.\n> right: \"I want.\"\n  bond mir partner\n  spark mir +large\n  mood +large\n  say: The heart doesn't need to work. But Mir builds it anyway, and it ticks on your windowsill, in time with your own.\n\n@event mir_family_business\ntitle: The Family Clockworks\npool: people_students_mir_social_routine\ntone: light\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 15 and year >= 2 and not flag(mir_family_done)\nart: gear\nscene: An artificer holding a stiff letter embossed with a gear crest, a train ticket tucked inside, staring at it blankly.\ntext: A letter from Mir's parents: the family clockworks needs them home. A ticket is enclosed. \"They think this is a hobby,\" Mir says. \"This. Is. Not. A. Hobby.\"\n> left: Tell them family comes first\n  flag mir_family_done\n  rel mir +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Mir goes home for a month and comes back with a new scar and a clearer jaw. \"They know now,\" they say. \"It's not a hobby. You were right to send me.\"\n> right: Help them write back\n  flag mir_family_done mir_family_wrote\n  rel mir +large\n  charm +tiny\n  study -small\n  say: Together you spend three evenings, and most of your coursework, on a letter so persuasive it should be framed. Mir's mother replies: \"Fine. Win a prize.\" Mir intends to win all of them.\n\n# ================= NESSA QUILL =================\n\n@event nessa_intro\ntitle: First Impressions\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: nessa\nwhen: not met(nessa) and turn >= 2\nweight: 30\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A girl with ink-stained fingers and a self-writing quill floating at her shoulder, holding a notebook out expectantly.\ntext: A girl with a quill floating at her shoulder plants herself in front of you. \"Nessa Quill, Lumen Ledger. First impressions of Lumenhold, go. Be interesting, please. The last six were boring.\"\n> left: Give a safe answer\n  meet nessa\n  rel nessa +tiny\n  say: The quill writes \"SAFE\" in the margin and underlines it twice. Nessa sighs and moves on.\n> right: Say something outrageous\n  meet nessa\n  challenge perform apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel nessa +medium\n      say: You're quoted on the front page. Half the academy now thinks you're hilarious. The other half thinks you're dangerous. Nessa thinks both.\n    lose:\n      renown +small\n      rel nessa +tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You are quoted. Out of context. Extremely out of context.\n\n@event nessa_gossip_column\ntitle: You're in the Gossip Column\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: renown >= 15\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A copy of the Lumen Ledger pinned to a noticeboard, a gossip column circled in red ink, students gathered round giggling.\ntext: This week's Ledger gossip column features you, prominently, under the headline \"WHO IS THE MYSTERY STUDENT ALWAYS IN THE LIBRARY AT MIDNIGHT?\" Nessa swears she didn't write it.\n> left: Confront Nessa\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  challenge negotiate vs nessa.charm:\n    win:\n      say: She prints a correction. Sort of. It's a much more flattering paragraph about you.\n      renown +small\n    lose:\n      say: \"Freedom of the press,\" says Nessa, sweetly. You both know she wrote it.\n> right: Lean into the mystery\n  chance likely:\n    renown +small\n    mood +small\n    rel nessa +small\n    say: You start leaving cryptic notes in the library. The column runs for six more weeks. Nessa is delighted.\n  else:\n    renown +tiny\n    mood -small\n    say: The column runs for six more weeks. By the fourth it has decided you are haunting the library, and people move tables when you sit down.\n\n@event nessa_source\ntitle: The Source\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 12 and (member(library_assistant) or rel(thorne) >= 9)\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: social\nscene: Two students in a dark corner of the library, one whispering, the other writing furiously in a notebook.\ntext: Nessa leans in. \"You've got faculty access. The professors are planning to change the exam format and they're hiding it. I just need a quote. Anonymous. Please?\"\n> left: Refuse — that's a breach of trust\n  rel nessa -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Nessa sulks for a day. Then she respects you for it, which she hates. You sleep well.\n> right: Give her the scoop\n  rel nessa +large\n  chance unlikely:\n    rel thorne -large\n    say: The story runs. Thorne knows exactly who talked. His glare could curdle milk.\n  else:\n    say: The story runs. Students are grateful. Nobody knows it was you. Nessa buys you cake.\n\n@event nessa_hollow_story\ntitle: Nessa Smells a Story\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: clues(hollowstar) >= 1 and rel(nessa) >= 6\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: social mystery hollowstar\nscene: A wall in the newspaper office covered in pinned clippings and red string connecting a frozen lake, a black star and a fur-coated boy.\ntext: Nessa's office wall is covered in red string. In the middle: a sketch of a black star. \"Frozen fountains. Missing pages. A transfer from the North. Something's going on and you know about it. Spill.\"\n> left: Warn her off — it's dangerous\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  say: \"That's what people say right before the best stories.\" But she takes the string down. Mostly.\n> right: Share what you know\n  rel nessa +medium\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain watched\n    say: Nessa's article runs with half the facts. The man in grey reads it. So does Bram, who starts following you around.\n  else:\n    say: Nessa's notes are scarily thorough. She's found a record of three students who vanished in 1723. All on Blossom moons.\n\n@event nessa_paper_threatened\ntitle: Stop the Presses\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 9 and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A printing press with a faculty seal stamped across it, a defiant student standing in front of it with arms crossed.\ntext: The headmistress has threatened to shut down the Ledger after an article about faculty salaries. Nessa is organising a protest. \"I need people on the steps. Visible people. You.\"\n> left: Stay out of it\n  rel nessa -small\n  say: The protest is small. The Ledger survives, barely. Nessa notices who wasn't there.\n> right: Stand on the steps\n  rel nessa +large\n  rel isolde -small\n  renown +medium\n  say: Forty students on the steps, chanting. The headmistress comes out and, astonishingly, listens, looking straight at you the whole time, and not warmly. The Ledger stays.\n\n@event nessa_confession\nramp: spark(nessa) 25..40\ntitle: Off the Record\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: spark(nessa) >= 25 and rel(nessa) >= 18 and courting(nessa)\nweight: 30\nart: feather\ntags: romance\nscene: A girl with an inkpot in one hand, her self-writing quill hovering above a blank page that she has commanded to stay blank.\ntext: Nessa puts down her quill. Then she tells it to stop writing. It's the first time you've seen it still. \"This is off the record,\" she says. \"Completely. I like you. I've drafted this eleven times. None of the drafts were good enough.\"\n> left: Gently say no\n  spark nessa -large\n  rel nessa +tiny\n  say: \"Noted,\" she says crisply. She keeps her quill still a moment longer. Then it starts writing again, and so does she.\n> right: Kiss her\n  bond nessa partner\n  spark nessa +large\n  mood +large\n  say: Her quill, unattended, writes a single word in the margin: \"FINALLY.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/castle_halls.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): castle_halls.\n#  Repeatable routine events for map visits to the castle's\n#  public halls: courtyard, library, refectory, lecture tower,\n#  great hall, infirmary, alchemy laboratory and job board.\n#  Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Courtyard ----------------\n\n@event courtyard_sundial_bench\ntime: afternoon\ntitle: The Slow Sundial\npool: courtyard_routine\ntone: warm\nart: sun\ntags: study\nscene: A stone bench beside a weathered sundial in a flagstone courtyard, a student reading with their back to the dial.\ntext: {There's a bench by the old sundial, which runs eleven minutes slow.|Someone has scratched a tiny \"+11\" by the dial's noon. A fellow sufferer.|The sundial bench is yours by custom. A first-year asked if you came with it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost fills the hour lines, and the shadow skates across them.|{Pigeons are holding a meeting on the gnomon.|A first-year checks a watch against it, horrified.|Someone has chalked a face where noon should be.}}\n> left: Read until the shadow moves\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {Your fingers go numb by page two. The chapter is worth it.|You read in gloves, turning pages with your teeth.|You read in your own bench's frost-free corner. A first-year brings you a hot chestnut, as a tribute to the bench.}|{You finish a chapter in the minutes the sundial owes you, squinting into the glare.|A pigeon reads over your shoulder, unimpressed, and your neck has a crick in it by the end.|The shadow crawls; you outpace it by three pages and a stiff back.}}\n> right: Close your eyes a while\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {You wake with a chalk moustache and no suspects.|You wake to a prefect clearing his throat an inch from your ear.|You wake rested and late, eleven minutes late by the dial.}\n\n@event courtyard_saved_seat\ntime: afternoon\ntitle: A Seat on the Rim\npool: courtyard_routine\ntone: light\nart: group\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer) and rel(here) >= 0\nscene: Two students sitting on the dry edge of a courtyard fountain, bags between them, pigeons circling overhead.\ntext: {{here} has claimed the dry stretch of the fountain rim and waves you over.|{here} is on your favourite stretch of the fountain rim, the one the sun reaches first, and waves you over.|Your stretch of the fountain rim is known now. {here} is sitting on it and shifts over without being asked.} {Two towers' bells are arguing about the hour.|Pigeons circle like creditors.|{season == \"winter\": Your breath hangs between you.|Someone's lunch smells better than yours.}}\n> left: Sit down and catch up\n  social here\n  if npc_is(corvin):\n    say: {Corvin tells you three stories, one of them true, and calls you \"saint\" when you don't ask which.|Corvin spots a prefect, palms something off the rim, and goes on talking as if nothing happened.|Corvin shifts over and hands you half of something he shouldn't have. \"Your seat, saint. I kept it warm. Illegally.\"}\n  elif npc_is(nessa):\n    say: {Nessa interviews you about the weather, the soup and your love life, in that order, and writes down all three.|Nessa reads you tomorrow's headline and asks if it's too cruel. It is. She keeps it.|Nessa shifts over without looking up from her notes. \"You're in tomorrow's column,\" she says. \"Nicely, for once.\"}\n  elif npc_is(bram):\n    say: {Bram lists every rule broken in the courtyard while you sit. Then, gruffly, he asks how your exams went.|Bram writes a demerit for a passing first-year, then tears it up when he thinks you aren't looking.|Bram shifts over with great dignity and informs you the seat is technically unassigned. Then he guards it while you fetch a cider.}\n  else:\n    say: {You trade the castle's news for an hour. {here} does most of the trading.|You talk until the shadows cross the fountain. Neither of you can remember about what.|You sit on your stretch of the rim and catch up. {here} already knows half your news and makes you tell it anyway.}\n  say: The cider from the cart by the gate is on you.\n> right: Wave, and keep walking\n  lore +small\n  rel here -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You promise next time and take your book to the library instead. {here} looks as if {they} {are} counting.|You spend the hour with a book instead. It is a good book; it is not much company.|You wave and keep walking. {here} stays on your stretch of the rim, which feels like a small theft.}\n\n@event courtyard_seasonal_game\ntitle: The Courtyard Game\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: Students playing a rowdy game across a flagstone courtyard while others crowd the edges shouting bets.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": A snowball war between Emberhall and Stillwater has reached the treaty stage.|{season == \"spring\": A charmed ball is loose, dodging everyone who kicks at it.|{season == \"summer\": It's too hot for sport, so it's catch with a charmed apple that bites.|It's conker season. The champion conker is said to be older than the fountain.}}} {Someone shouts for one more player.|They shout for you by name.|Both sides shout for you, and start bidding.}\n> left: Join in\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {Your side wins, and the result is disputed until supper.|You score the deciding point and are carried three whole steps before they drop you.|You join the side that bid more. They win, and the other side says you were bought, which you were.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You are flattened, politely, by both sides at once.|You spend the rest of the game as the thing the other side aims at.|Both sides think you're on theirs. Both sides find out, at once, in the middle of the courtyard.}\n> right: Hold the coats and the bets\n  chance even:\n    gold +small\n    say: {The favourite loses. You are the only person in the courtyard who is pleased about it.|The underdog wins by a whisker, and the coats you are holding are suddenly lighter on your conscience.|You hold the coats and both sides' bids. The underdog wins. You are, briefly, the richest person in the courtyard.}\n  else:\n    if gold > 0:\n      gold -small\n      say: {The favourite wins, and so does everyone who backed them, out of your pocket.|Everyone bet the same way, and everyone was right. You pay out in a hail of small coins.|The favourite wins, and both sides had bet on it through you. You pay out twice, which shouldn't be possible.}\n    else:\n      mood -small\n      say: The favourite wins. With nothing in your pocket to pay out, you're chased twice round the fountain and lose a coat button to the crowd.\n\n# ---------------- The Library ----------------\n\n@pool library_routine\nplace: library\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event library_wandering_book\ntitle: The Book That Moved\npool: library_routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: Tall library shelves leaning very slightly, a gap where one book should be, a student on a ladder peering into it.\ntext: {Yesterday your book was on the third floor. Today the third floor has never heard of it.|Your book has moved again, leaving a bookmark in the gap, like a note.|You keep a map of where the shelves hide your books. They've started hiding the map.} {In its place is a shelf of cookery spells.|In its place are bound weather reports.|In its place is one slim volume, face out, waiting.} {final_year: Soon they'll play these games with someone else.|}\n> left: Hunt it down\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You find it on the sixth floor, filed under \"regret\".|You find it in the stairwell, halfway between floors, as if it had been trying to leave.|You find it at once. Either you are learning the shelves, or they are getting lazy.}\n> right: Read what's in front of you\n  mood +small\n  say: {You read the wrong book with great conviction, and quote it at supper.|You learn a great deal about something nobody will ever ask you about.|The wrong book turns out to be a very good novel. You read all of it, and none of it will be on any exam.}\n\n@event library_shared_table\ntitle: The Last Free Chair\npool: library_routine\ntone: warm\nart: candle\ntags: social study\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer) and rel(here) >= 0\nscene: A crowded library reading room, every table full but one, a single empty chair facing a student absorbed in a book.\ntext: {The library is full. The only free chair is opposite {here}.|The only free chair is opposite {here}. A shelf has leaned over it, as if keeping it for you.|The library knows your habits now. The free chair, opposite {here}, has a book you wanted waiting on it.|The chair opposite {here} is so reliably free that you suspect the library of arranging it.} {Rain ticks on the tall windows.|A shelf nearby is quietly rearranging itself.|Footnote is asleep in the returns basket.|{season == \"winter\": The radiators clank like a ghost with a grudge.|Dust turns gold in the light.}}\n> left: Take the chair and work\n  rel here +small\n  lore +small\n  if npc_is(kael):\n    say: {Kael doesn't look up. By the end of the hour there is frost on your inkwell and a tiny, careful correction in your margin.|Kael pushes a page of his notes across to you, then looks away as if it had moved on its own.|Kael moves his inkwell to make room for yours. By the end, there's frost on both, and a note in his hand: \"Your third line.\"|Kael says one sentence in the whole hour. It's the answer to the question you were stuck on. He goes back to reading.}\n  elif npc_is(lyra):\n    say: {Lyra slides the candle an inch toward your side without looking up. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra finishes before you, and waits, and says she wasn't waiting.|Lyra corrects your notation without asking and is right. You correct hers and are also right. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra works at your pace for once, and you finish together. She says it was coincidence, twice.}\n  elif npc_is(seraphine):\n    say: {Seraphine writes a word in your notes that you won't understand until next week.|Seraphine reads the same page for an hour, smiling at something that isn't on it.|Seraphine slides you a book you hadn't asked for. You'll need it on Thursday, it turns out.|Seraphine works in silence, then says your name once, softly, as if checking it was still there.}\n  elif npc_is(bram):\n    say: {Bram informs you that you breathe too loudly. Then he lends you his ruler.|Bram underlines every rule in his book twice. Once, he asks your opinion of one.|Bram shares his lamp and lectures you on its proper use. The lecture is long. The lamp is very good.|Bram reads your essay upside down from across the table and finds the mistake. He's insufferable about it, and right.}\n  else:\n    say: {You work in silence for an hour, which turns out to be a kind of company.|Neither of you speaks. Both of you get further than you would have alone.|You work opposite {here} as if you'd planned to. By the end you've both got further, and nodded once.|You share the table for the hour and the cat for the last ten minutes. It's a good arrangement.}\n> right: Whisper a question\n  rel here +small\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {The conversation is worth it. The look from the front desk has a long memory.|You get three sentences in before a shush arrives from two floors up.|You whisper your question to {here} and get a whispered answer. The front desk lets it pass, this once.|Your whisper wakes the cat in the returns basket, who complains loudly. You are shushed for the cat's noise.}\n\n@event library_overdue\ntitle: Overdue\npool: library_routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: study money\nscene: A student holding a book behind their back near a wicker returns basket, a cat on the front desk watching them.\ntext: {The book you borrowed in a hurry is overdue, and the library knows it. The spine has started to sigh.|Another overdue book. The front desk has begun keeping a little card with your name on it.|Your overdue books sigh in chorus now, and the card at the front desk has a second side.} {Footnote watches you approach the returns basket with professional interest.|The front desk is, for once, unattended.|A queue of first-years is paying fines in small, sad coins.}\n> left: Own up and pay the fine\n  alignment +tiny\n  lore +small\n  if gold > 0:\n    gold -small\n    say: {The fine is paid; the desk, mollified, lends you the sequel.|You pay, and are lent another book, which you suspect is a trap.|The desk sighs, takes your coins and hands you a book it says you'll like. It is always right, which is the annoying part.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: You own up with empty pockets. The desk lets you work the fine off reshelving, which takes all evening, and lends you the sequel anyway.\n> right: Slip it into the basket\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {The book goes in without a sound. It stops sighing at once, which feels like forgiveness.|You drop it in under cover of a sneeze. Footnote saw. Footnote will keep it to himself, for a price.|You slip it in while the first-years pay their fines. Nobody notices one more sad sound.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      if gold > 0:\n        gold -medium\n        say: {The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. You pay double.|It lands with a groan. The front desk adds a mark to your card, and the fine, doubled.|The book sighs in harmony with your other overdue books. The desk adds a third side to your card.}\n      else:\n        say: The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. With nothing to pay, you're put on reshelving until the fine is worked off twice over.\n\n# ---------------- The Refectory ----------------\n\n@event refectory_late_supper\nslot: evening\ntitle: Late Supper\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: The far end of a long refectory table at night, most benches empty, a single student with a bowl and a half-written letter.\ntext: {You come in late. The benches are empty and the cooks are wiping the far tables.|The cook leaves a lamp lit at the end of the bench now, where the draught can't find you.|Your late bowl is waiting under a plate, with a spoon laid beside it. Nobody admits to it.} {year == 1: Home feels very far away tonight.|{final_year: You count the suppers you have left, and stop.|{The candles are down to stubs.|Someone has left a half-done puzzle on the next table.|The cook is humming in the kitchen behind the hatch.}}}\n> left: Write a letter home\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {You write two pages and post them with the last of your coppers.|You write about the food. You do not write about anything else, but they'll know.|You write a letter that is mostly questions. You post it before you can make it braver.}\n> right: Revise over the bowl\n  lore +small\n  say: {You learn three dates and a spill of gravy across all of them.|The cook reads over your shoulder and corrects one of your answers. She is right.|You revise over your bowl. The cook takes the plate away when you've finished and leaves a second helping.}\n\n@event refectory_house_tables\ntime: evening\ntitle: Where to Sit\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: A crowded refectory at suppertime, a student standing with a tray between a loud house table and a quieter one of strangers.\ntext: {Supper, and the question of where to sit.|Your house table has started saving a gap on the bench, roughly your width.|The gap is a named seat now, and the quiet table nods at you too.} Your house table is {loud with a story about a goat|in the middle of a pudding treaty|singing, badly}. At the far end, {season == \"winter\": some second-years from Rootward huddle round a warming charm.|some second-years from Duskgate are arguing quietly about a map.}\n> left: Squeeze in with your house\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {You eat with an elbow in your ribs and laugh the whole way through.|Somebody's story gets better every time you hear it. So does the pudding.|You squeeze into your gap and join the singing, badly, to fit in. It works.}\n> right: Sit with the strangers\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {They are polite, then curious, then actually rather good company.|It is awkward for exactly one course. By pudding, they have told you their secret map route.|The quiet table makes room as if you'd always sat there. They ask about your house's singing, with sympathy.}\n\n@event refectory_pudding_tax\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Pudding Tax\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and (tagged(friend, student) or tagged(friend, prefect)) and around(friend)\nscene: Two friends at a refectory table, one guarding a bowl of treacle pudding, the other holding a spoon at the ready.\ntext: {{friend} sits down opposite you and looks at your pudding the way a cat looks at a fish.|Your pudding has a reputation now. {friend} sits down opposite you with a spoon already in hand.|Word of your pudding tax has got round the refectory. Tonight {friend} comes to collect, with a little receipt.} {season == \"winter\": Tonight it's treacle sponge, steaming.|{Tonight it's apple crumble.|Tonight it's a custard that wobbles with intent.|Tonight it's jam roly-poly, much defended.}}\n> left: Pay the pudding tax\n  rel friend +small\n  mood -small\n  if npc_is(tobias):\n    say: {Tobias apologises four times, eats it all, then tells you a long story about a goat who did the same. It helps, somehow.|Tobias eats half, then pushes the bowl back with a sprig of mint from his pocket on top, as an apology.|Tobias pays you back with a receipt of his own: half a jar of honey from home. You call it even.}\n  elif npc_is(lyra):\n    say: {\"Efficient,\" Lyra says, finishing it. The next day a whole pudding appears on your tray. She doesn't mention it.|Lyra eats it in four exact bites and critiques the custard. She does not give it back.|Lyra presents the receipt, takes exactly the tax and not a crumb more. She is, you have to admit, scrupulous.}\n  elif npc_is(mir):\n    say: {Mir eats it while explaining a pudding-defence device that would probably work. Probably.|Mir eats it with a spoon they built themselves, which hums. They say the humming is probably normal.|Mir hands you a receipt they've engraved on brass, collects, and promises a refund mechanism by spring.}\n  else:\n    say: {{friend} eats exactly half and leaves you the burnt corner.|{friend} eats it slowly, savouring your defeat.|{friend} collects the tax and writes \"paid\" on the receipt. You're starting to suspect the receipts are the point.}\n> right: Defend your pudding\n  challenge deceive vs friend.guile:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      rel friend -tiny\n      say: {You hide it under a napkin and point at the window. It works. {friend} will remember this.|You claim it's been sneezed on. {friend} retreats in disgust, and you eat in peace.|You refuse to recognise the receipt. {friend} appeals to the table, which rules in your favour, narrowly.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      rel friend +tiny\n      say: {{friend} sees straight through you and takes the whole thing as a penalty. It is hard to stay cross.|{friend} distracts you with a story and the bowl is empty before it ends.|You dispute the receipt. {friend} produces a second receipt for the dispute, and takes the pudding as costs.}\n\n# ---------------- The Lecture Tower ----------------\n\n@pool lecture_tower_routine\nplace: lecture_tower\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event lecture_tower_old_board\ntitle: Someone Else's Lecture\npool: lecture_tower_routine\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: An empty spiral lecture hall, a blackboard still dense with chalk diagrams from a class that has long gone.\ntext: {term: {The fourth-floor hall is empty, but someone else's lecture is still on the board.|In the empty hall, a stub of chalk waits on the ledge, as if for you.|The board has a corner fenced off in chalk, labelled \"answers here\".}|In the break the boards are wiped, except one nobody dares touch.} {Today it's a ward diagram, labelled \"wrong, but how?\"|Today it's half a proof, ending in a thumbprint.|Today it's a list headed \"on the exam\", crossed out.}\n> left: Copy it all down\n  arcana +small\n  say: {Your hand cramps. Your head, for once, does not.|You copy it all and understand most of it. The rest you'll grow into.|You copy the board, fenced corner and all. Your answer from last time is still there, with a tick beside it.}\n> right: Add your own answer\n  chance likely:\n    renown +small\n    say: {Next time, someone has added \"yes!\" under your line.|Someone has answered your answer. The board has become a correspondence.|By the afternoon someone has drawn a box around your line, to keep it safe.}\n  else:\n    renown -tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {The professor whose board it was finds you holding the chalk. \"Hm,\" says the look.|Your answer is wiped by the time you reach the door, by someone who knew better.|You write in the \"answers here\" corner. The next morning there's a line through it, and \"nearly\", underlined.}\n\n@event lecture_tower_stair_question\ntitle: One Question a Flight\npool: lecture_tower_routine\ntone: warm\nart: tower\ntags: study\ncast:\n  prof: thorne\nscene: A narrow spiral stair inside a stone tower, a severe professor climbing ahead with a stack of papers, a student a step behind.\ntext: {You meet Professor Thorne on the spiral stair. He allows one question per flight, and climbs fast.|Professor Thorne on the stair. He has slowed down by about one step in seven, which is how you know he expects you.|Thorne hands you half his stack of papers at the foot of the stair, without a word, and starts climbing.} {term: |The tower is half empty for the break; your footsteps echo twice.}{final_year: There are not many of these climbs left.|}\n> left: Ask a hard question\n  challenge research using arcana adept-:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      rel prof +tiny\n      say: {He answers in four words, and the four words are enough.|\"Hm,\" he says, then answers it properly, all the way to the seventh floor.|You carry half his papers and ask your question on the fourth flight. He answers on the seventh, and it was worth the wait.}\n    lose:\n      arcana +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {He answers with a harder question and leaves you on the landing.|\"Wrong question,\" he says. \"Find the right one by Thursday.\"|He takes his papers back on the landing. \"Better question next time,\" he says, which means he'll be expecting one.}\n> right: Climb in silence beside him\n  rel prof +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {He climbs faster than you. At the top he says, \"Adequate,\" and you are fairly sure he means the climbing.|Neither of you says a word for seven flights. It is oddly restful, apart from your legs.|You carry his papers in silence. At the top he takes them back and says, \"Thursday,\" which is an invitation.}\n\n@event lecture_tower_scorched_room\ntitle: The Scorched Room\npool: lecture_tower_routine\ntone: tense\nart: flame\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A practice room on a lower floor of a stone tower, walls streaked with scorch marks, a sign by the door forbidding unsupervised casting.\ntext: {The practice room on the Evocation floor is empty. A sign forbids unsupervised casting.|The scorched room is empty. Since your last visit, someone has underlined the sign.|The scorched room. The sign is underlined three times now, and signed by the headmistress.} {A fresh scorch mark is shaped like a surprised face.|Someone has left a bucket of sand by the door.|{season == \"winter\": The walls are the only warm thing here.|The air smells faintly of singed eyebrow.}}\n> left: Practise anyway\n  mana -small\n  chance likely:\n    arcana +small\n    say: {Your spell lands clean in the centre of the oldest scorch mark. You leave no evidence.|You practise until the flame does what you mean, not what you say.|You practise under the headmistress's signature. Your spell is very good. You leave before it's tempted to be better.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    renown -tiny\n    say: {A new scorch mark joins the wall, shaped very like you.|The spell comes back at you. You leave with your eyebrows, mostly.|Your scorch mark lands right under the headmistress's signature. You'd swear the signature looks disappointed.}\n> right: Work through the theory\n  lore +small\n  say: {You draw the spell on paper fifty times. Paper is very forgiving.|You learn exactly why the walls look like that, and resolve not to add to them.|You work the theory under the thrice-underlined sign. You'll cast it properly, supervised, and it will be perfect.}\n\n# ---------------- The Great Hall ----------------\n\n@pool great_hall_routine\nplace: great_hall\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event great_hall_tomorrow_sky\ntitle: Tomorrow's Weather\npool: great_hall_routine\ntone: warm\nart: candle\ntags: magic\nscene: An empty great hall in the evening, floating candles drifting under a vaulted ceiling that shows a different sky from the windows.\ntext: {The great hall is empty but for the candles. The ceiling shows tomorrow.|One candle drifts over to you and hovers at reading height.|Your candle meets you at the door now and leads you to your bench.} {season == \"winter\": Tomorrow, it says, it will snow.|{season == \"spring\": Tomorrow, a storm with bad manners.|{season == \"summer\": Tomorrow is cloudless, stars and all.|Tomorrow is fog, rolling across the vaults.}}} {final_year: There are fewer tomorrows here now.|}\n> left: Lie on a bench and watch\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You watch tomorrow until your neck stiffens. It is worth it.|A candle drifts down to see if you are all right, and stays.|You fall half asleep and dream tomorrow a little early.}\n> right: Read by the floating candles\n  lore +small\n  say: {A candle drips wax onto the best paragraph. You read it anyway, through the wax.|The candles follow your page like curious moths. It is the best light in the castle.|Your candle sits on the bench beside you like a friend. The light is steady, and you read further than you meant to.}\n\n@event great_hall_choir_rehearsal\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Choir Rehearses\npool: great_hall_routine\ntone: warm\nart: music\ntags: social\nscene: A choir rehearsing on the stage of a candlelit great hall, a few singers floating a foot off the boards, the benches mostly empty.\ntext: {The choir is rehearsing on the stage, a foot off the floor, and one voice short.|Still a voice short. The conductor has left a spare songbook on your bench.|The spare songbook has your name pencilled in it, and the alto line marked in red.} {season == \"winter\": They are learning the Midwinter music.|{Tonight it's a round that nobody can find the end of.|Tonight it's a lament, sung far too cheerfully.|Tonight it's a song for the lake, sung very softly.}}\n> left: Listen from the back\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {The collection hat comes round. It is worth every coin.|You listen until the candles gutter. The hat comes round twice.|You listen from the back with your name pencilled into a book you're not singing from. It's the best of both.}\n> right: Fill the missing voice\n  challenge perform apprentice:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      charm +tiny\n      say: {You hit every note but one. The conductor says it was the best one.|You float a little off the floor on the last chord. Nobody mentions it, which is how you know.|You sing your marked alto line and the choir is whole. The conductor doesn't say anything, and doesn't need to.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You come in confidently, in the wrong key, in the wrong song. The choir is very kind about it, which is worse.|You sing the round in a straight line and finish alone. The conductor thanks you for your enthusiasm.|You sing the red line, which is the soprano line, it turns out. The choir carries you, out of kindness.}\n\n@event great_hall_high_table_dare\ntitle: The High Table Dare\npool: great_hall_routine\ntone: tense\nart: crown\ntags: social danger\nscene: An empty high table on a dais at the end of a dark great hall, a carved chair at its centre, students daring each other from the doorway.\ntext: {Someone dares you to sit in the headmistress's chair at the empty high table.|The dare comes round again, and someone has started keeping a tally in chalk on the doorframe.|The tally on the doorframe has your column and everyone else's. First-years dare each other to dare you.} {The chair is still warm.|A cushion embroidered with an owl sits on it.|Her teacup has been left behind, still steaming, which is worse.}\n> left: Take the dare\n  challenge deceive apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You sit, cross your legs, and sip the air. The doorway erupts.|You sit for a full minute. The chair is surprisingly comfortable, and very slightly warm.|You take the dare, and lift the steaming teacup, and drink. Nobody in the doorway breathes. It's very good tea.}\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      say: {As you sit, a quiet, delighted laugh comes from the dark beyond the dais. You leave very fast.|A portrait above the table clears its throat. You have never moved so fast in your life.|You reach for the steaming teacup and a voice behind you says, \"That's mine.\" You don't look round. You simply leave.}\n> right: Tell them you value your life\n  renown -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {They call you a coward, fondly. You keep your dignity, and your place at the academy.|\"Coward,\" they chant. You bow. It seems the safest thing to do in that hall.|You decline, and point out the steaming cup. The first-years go very quiet, and so does the dare.}\n\n# ---------------- The Infirmary ----------------\n\n@pool infirmary_routine\nplace: infirmary\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event infirmary_spare_bed\ntitle: The Spare Bed\npool: infirmary_routine\ntone: warm\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A row of white infirmary beds under a high arched window, one turned down, a stern healer pointing at it.\ntext: {You only came in for a plaster.|Your name is on the slate by the end bed, in pencil.|Your name on the slate is in ink now.} {health < 40: Sister Amaranth takes one look at you and points at the spare bed.|Sister Amaranth decides, over her spectacles, that you'll live.} {It smells of lavender and singed wool.|Two beds down, someone hiccups in a minor key.|{season == \"winter\": A warming charm hums under every blanket.|The curtains breathe.}}\n> left: Lie down for an hour\n  mood +small\n  health +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {You sleep the deep, dreamless sleep of the infirmary. The tonic afterwards costs a few crowns and tastes of regret.|You wake under a blanket you didn't pull up. There is a tonic on the side, and a bill under it.|Your name is on the slate in ink, so you take the end bed without asking. The tonic is waiting, and so is the bill.}\n> right: Help roll bandages\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {Sister Amaranth teaches you the knot she uses. You will never tie your boots any other way.|You roll bandages and listen to her opinion of duelling, which is long and specific.|You roll bandages as if you work here. Sister Amaranth says that if you're going to be here this often you may as well.}\n\n@event infirmary_visiting_hour\ntitle: Visiting Hour\npool: infirmary_routine\ntone: warm\nart: cross\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and (tagged(friend, student) or tagged(friend, prefect)) and around(friend)\nscene: A friend propped up in an infirmary bed by a window, a visitor on a stool beside them with a paper bag of grapes.\ntext: {Visiting hour. {friend} is in the bed by the window,|Visiting hour. Sister Amaranth waves you to the stool you used last time. Today it's {friend},|Visiting hour. The stool by the window has worn to your shape. Today it's {friend},} {nursing a duelling bruise the colour of plums.|recovering from a draught of hiccups that hiccups back.|wrapped in three blankets against a lake chill.} {They} brighten{s} when you come in.\n> left: Sit and keep {them} company\n  social friend\n  say: {You bring grapes and eat most of them yourself. {friend} doesn't mind.|You stay until Sister Amaranth sweeps you out. {friend} waves from the pillows.|You sit on your worn stool and tell {friend} everything they've missed. {They} fall{s} asleep halfway, smiling.}\n> right: Smuggle in a pastry\n  chance likely:\n    rel friend +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {The pastry makes it past the desk. {friend} eats it under the blanket like a crime.|You hide the pastry in a hollowed-out book. {friend} is delighted by both.|You smuggle in a hot pastry under your coat. {friend} eats it wrapped in all three blankets and declares you a hero.}\n  else:\n    rel friend +tiny\n    renown -tiny\n    say: {Sister Amaranth confiscates the pastry and eats it herself, slowly, while looking at you.|Sister Amaranth smells the pastry from across the ward and holds out her hand without a word.|Sister Amaranth takes the pastry, cuts it in three, and gives you, {friend} and herself a piece each. Then she lectures you both.}\n\n@event infirmary_tonic_cupboard\ntitle: The Tonic Cupboard\npool: infirmary_routine\ntone: tense\nart: potion\ntags: danger\nscene: A tall glass-fronted cupboard of coloured tonics in an empty infirmary, its door ajar, a teacup steaming on the healer's desk.\ntext: {The tonic cupboard is open, and Sister Amaranth is in the back having her tea.|The tonic cupboard is open at teatime. A new label on the door reads \"counted daily\".|At teatime the cupboard is open, and the label now reads \"counted daily, by name\". The bright blue bottles have moved to the front.} {mana < 20: Your head aches with the dry ache of spent magic.|{The bottles clink in the draught.|A label reads, in her handwriting, \"absolutely not\".|The bright blue bottles have a new lock.}}\n> left: Help yourself to a tonic\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      mana +small\n      health +tiny\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {You drink it in the corridor. It tastes of peppermint and getting away with things.|You pocket the bottle, rinse it later, and put it back full of water. You feel slightly worse about that.|You take a blue one from the front, since that's where they've been put. You suspect that was the point.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {\"That one is for goats,\" says Sister Amaranth from the doorway. She makes you drink the right one, which is worse.|Sister Amaranth's teacup appears at your elbow. \"Put it back,\" she says, \"and sit down.\"|The blue bottles were put at the front to see who'd take one. Sister Amaranth writes your name in the \"counted daily\" book.}\n> right: Ask for one properly\n  health +small\n  mana +tiny\n  if gold > 0:\n    gold -small\n    say: {She charges you, lectures you, and gives you the good one.|She charges you, lectures you a little less, and gives you the good one.|\"You again,\" she says, and hands it over before you ask.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: She looks at your empty purse, gives you the good one anyway, and lectures you twice as long.\n\n# ---------------- The Alchemy Laboratory ----------------\n\n@pool alchemy_lab_routine\nplace: alchemy_lab\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event alchemy_lab_spare_cauldron\ntitle: A Spare Cauldron\npool: alchemy_lab_routine\ntone: light\nart: cauldron\ntags: study magic\nscene: A long laboratory bench in the afternoon light, a small cauldron free among larger ones, jars of ingredients lined up beside it.\ntext: {There is a spare cauldron on the second bench, unclaimed.|The spare cauldron has a chalk mark on its rim like your initial.|Your cauldron, everyone calls it. Someone has scratched your initial into the handle.} {Someone has left a ladle standing upright in something.|The bench smells of mint and burnt sugar.|{season == \"summer\": The heat makes every brew twice as eager.|The windows have steamed over with other people's experiments.}}\n> left: Brew a practice batch\n  challenge brew apprentice:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {The brew turns the right colour at the right moment. It feels like being trusted.|It comes out clear and smells of nothing, which is exactly right.|You brew in your cauldron, by your initial. It knows your hand by now and doesn't argue.}\n    lose:\n      alchemy +tiny\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The brew turns a colour you have never seen. So do your eyebrows.|It foams over and sets, firmly, around your sleeve.|Even your own cauldron turns on you today. The brew sets, and your initial is the only part of the handle you can see.}\n> right: Scrub the cauldrons for crowns\n  gold +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You learn more about failed potions from their residue than from any lecture.|You scrub until the bench is clean. Then you scratch your own initials into it, small, under the rim.|You scrub every cauldron and yours last, with care. It shines. Someone else uses it the next morning, and you mind.}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_third_floor\ntitle: The Third-Floor Fumes\npool: alchemy_lab_routine\ntone: light\nart: bottle\ntags: social magic\nscene: A crowded upper floor of an alchemy laboratory, coloured vapour hanging under the ceiling, students working with handkerchiefs tied over their faces.\ntext: {On the third floor the ventilation charm has given up, and the fumes have taken charge.|Someone has hung a sign by the stairs giving the fumes' mood of the day.|The fumes' sign has a column for forecasts now, in several hands.} {Everyone is speaking in rhyme and trying to hide it.|Everyone is laughing at nothing and cannot stop.|Everyone is being unusually honest about everyone else's hair.} {season == \"winter\": The windows are frozen shut.|}\n> left: Stay and work in the fug\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You get a great deal done and agree with everyone about everything.|You leave with good notes and a headache that hums.|You work through the honesty fumes and hear what everyone thinks of your hair. Your notes are excellent. Your hair is fine.}\n> right: Force the windows open\n  vigor +tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {The ice cracks. Half the floor cheers; half complains of the cold.|The frozen latch gives, and so does your thumbnail.|The window cracks open and the cold rushes in. Everyone stops being honest at once, with relief.}|{The windows give. The fumes leave, sulking, and the floor applauds.|You wedge the window with a textbook. The fumes drift out, and nearly the textbook too.|You open the window and the honest fumes leave. The silence that follows is extremely polite.}}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_unlabelled_jar\ntitle: The Unlabelled Jar\npool: alchemy_lab_routine\ntone: tense\nart: potion\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A dusty shelf of ingredient jars in an alchemy laboratory, one jar with no label glowing faintly, a student reaching for it.\ntext: {Someone has left an unlabelled jar on the shared shelf. It glows, faintly.|Another unlabelled jar on the shared shelf, beside the cautious label someone wrote for the last one.|The shared shelf now has a row of careful labels, and one new jar at the end of it, waiting its turn.} {It smells of thunder.|Something inside it turns over when you look.|It is warm to the touch.} {gpa >= 3: You could probably identify it, given time.|You have no idea what it is.}\n> left: Add a pinch to your brew\n  mana -tiny\n  chance even:\n    mana +medium\n    alchemy +tiny\n    say: {The brew turns gold and so do you, briefly. You feel enormous.|The brew sings a single note and leaves you humming with power.|A pinch of the warm stuff and your brew purrs like a cat. You don't know what it did, but it's better.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: {The brew ignites, politely, and then impolitely.|Your cauldron sneezes. So, for the rest of the afternoon, do you.|The warm stuff makes your brew too warm, and then your bench, and then your sleeve.}\n> right: Label it, carefully\n  lore +small\n  say: {An hour of tests later, you write a cautious label. Someone adds a skull to it.|You work out what it is. Then you wash your hands three times.|You label it and add it to the row. Yours is the most careful label on the shelf, and the most honest: \"Warm. Unsure.\"}\n\n# ---------------- The Job Board ----------------\n\n@pool job_board_routine\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\n\n@event job_board_odd_jobs\ntitle: Odd Jobs\npool: job_board_routine\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: job money\nscene: A cork board in an entrance hall, a small notice pinned at an angle under the official job postings, a student reading it with a frown.\ntext: {Under the proper postings, someone has pinned an odd job.|Under the proper postings is an odd job, and a second pin someone has left beside it, for answers.|The odd-jobs corner has a card addressed to you by name, in a hand you're starting to recognise.} {Someone wants a homesick portrait read to.|Someone wants a ladder held, no questions asked.|Someone wants a reluctant gargoyle walked.} {term: |In the break the board is half bare, and the odd jobs pay a little better.}\n> left: Take the job\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {It takes all afternoon and pays exactly what it said, which is rarer than it should be.|It is stranger than the notice suggested, and pays slightly less.|You take the gargoyle for its walk. It doesn't want to go, and then it doesn't want to come back. The pay is fair.}\n> right: Haggle first\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      gold +tiny\n      charm +tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You haggle the fee up by a third. Your employer seems to respect you more for it.|You agree a price and a biscuit. The biscuit turns out to be the better half of the deal.|You haggle with the hand you recognise. They pay what you ask, and leave a note: \"Worth it.\"}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {Your employer takes the notice down and pins up a new one, asking for \"someone less difficult\".|The job goes to a first-year who asked for nothing. They seem very happy with it.|You haggle, and the next card in that hand is addressed to someone else. You've been dropped, politely.}\n\n@event job_board_lost_familiars\ntitle: Lost and Found\npool: job_board_routine\ntone: warm\nart: cat\ntags: social\nscene: The corner of a cork board crowded with hand-drawn posters of lost familiars and a ransom note for a sock.\ntext: {The corner of the board for lost things is busier than the jobs.|An old poster in the lost-things corner now reads \"found, thanks to a student\".|The lost-things corner has thank-you notes now. One draws the rescuer with a halo.} {A toad called \"Sir\" is missing, last seen being brave.|A ransom note demands three biscuits for one sock.|A small owl is lost, answers to nothing, and bites.} {season == \"winter\": Half the familiars have simply gone somewhere warm.|}\n> left: Go looking for it\n  chance even:\n    gold +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {You find it behind a radiator in the east tower, deeply unrepentant. The reward is small and grateful.|You find it, and the owner cries, and the reward is mostly biscuits.|You find the owl in the rafters of the owlery, pretending to belong. It bites you. The owner pays double, for the bite.}\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {You search for an hour and find only a different, much angrier creature.|You don't find it. The next day the poster says \"Found! Thanks anyway.\"|You find a small owl. It is not the small owl. It also bites.}\n> right: Read every notice twice\n  guile +small\n  say: {The board is the castle's diary. You learn who lost what, and who owes whom.|You learn three secrets from the small print, and who has terrible handwriting.|You read every notice, including the thank-you notes. One of them is about you. You read that one three times.}\n\n# Job board stories (playtest 2, N13): small, everyday, plot-safe.\n\n@pool job_board_routine_2\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\nweight: 10\ntags: job\n\n@event job_board_yellow_notice\ntitle: The Oldest Notice\npool: job_board_routine_2\ntone: warm\nart: paper\ntags: social\nscene: A corner of a cork board where one notice, yellowed and curling, is pinned beneath years of newer ones.\ntext: In the bottom corner of the board, under years of newer notices, is one gone yellow and soft: \"Wanted, Thursdays: someone to read the newssheet aloud to the portrait on the west stair. He likes the sport and the obituaries. Small pay.\" Someone has renewed the pin.\n> left: Answer it, this Thursday\n  gold +tiny\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: The portrait is a bald man in an old-fashioned collar who hasn't had the news read to him in years. He cheers the duelling results and sighs at the obituaries. You lose your Thursday evening, and he asks you to come back.\n> right: Ask the porter who pins it\n  lore +small\n  say: The porter says the notice was there when he started, and that a few crowns appear on the stair every Thursday whether anyone reads or not. He has never asked who leaves it. He suggests you don't either.\n\n@event job_board_torn_notice\ntitle: Half a Notice\npool: job_board_routine_2\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: money\nscene: Two students tugging at either end of a single job notice in front of a crowded cork board, the paper starting to tear.\ntext: A well-paid notice (\"one evening's portering, generous rates\") has drawn two students, who have both got hold of it. It tears down the middle as you arrive. One holds the half with the pay; the other holds the half with the address. Both look at you.\n> left: Settle it between them\n  charm +small\n  say: You suggest they share the evening and split the fee. They both hate the idea, then both agree to it, then both thank you. You get nothing but a reputation for being reasonable.\n> right: Read the address and go yourself\n  gold +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You read the address over a shoulder and get there first. The job pays well. By supper both of them have told everyone what you did.\n\n@event job_board_monthly_letter\ntitle: A Letter a Month\npool: job_board_routine_2\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A small handwritten notice pinned beside a sealed letter on a cork board, a student reaching to unpin both.\ntext: A small notice in a careful, shaky hand: \"Wanted: a student to post a letter for me in the village, first of each month. A few crowns. I can't manage the hill any more.\" The letter is pinned beside it, sealed and addressed to someone in the village.\n> left: Take the letter down the hill\n  gold +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: You post it, then the next, then the next. In the third month the letter-writer leaves a note: the replies have started coming. You never learn who either of them is. You keep the crowns in a jar.\n> right: Leave it for someone else\n  say: You leave it on the board. The next morning it's gone, and someone has drawn a small heart beside the notice in pencil.\n\n# Odd gigs (feedback 2026-10-15): the Job Board shows its job menu on every visit and one of these after it, so a\n# visit always has something small to take or leave. Everyday and plot-safe; each repeats every few months.\n\n@event job_board_copying\ntitle: Fair Copies Wanted\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: job money\nscene: A cork board with a notice in a sick student's shaky hand, a stack of borrowed lecture notes tied with string beneath it.\ntext: {A notice in a shaky hand. A student down with fever needs a fortnight of lecture notes copied out fair, and will pay for neatness.|Another fever, another fortnight of notes to copy fair. The notice says \"neat hand preferred\", and someone has written your name beside it.|The copying notice again. By now you can copy a page without reading a word of it, if you want to.}\n> left: Copy them for the pay\n  gold +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Two evenings of careful letters. The pay arrives with a note that says your sevens are beautiful.|You copy every page, blot nothing and are paid exactly. Your wrist aches until the weekend.|You copy without reading, as fast as the ink allows. The pay is fair and the evening is gone.}\n> right: Copy them slowly, and learn them too\n  gold +tiny\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You read as you copy. Half the pay goes on candles, and you learn a course you aren't even taking.|You copy slowly and read every line. The notes are better than your own, and you keep a few of the ideas.|You read it all, slowly. The student pays a little less for the delay, and you know a little more for it.}\n\n@event job_board_message_run\ntitle: Messages Across the Castle\npool: job_board_routine\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: job money\nscene: A student with a satchel of folded notes running across a castle bridge between towers, a staircase swinging away behind them.\ntext: {A professor wants a runner for the afternoon, with notes for every tower before the bell. A small fee, and no reading them.|The runner's notice is back, with a longer list of towers.|The runner's notice, and a list you could walk in your sleep. The staircases have learned your face.}\n> left: Run the round yourself\n  gold +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You reach the last tower as the bell goes. Your legs are jelly and the fee is honest.|Seven towers, two moving staircases and one argument with a portrait. Paid in full.|You take the shortcuts the staircases allow you now. Paid, and back in time for supper.}\n> right: Send the owls instead\n  gold +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You pay the owlery out of the fee and keep the rest, and the afternoon. The professor never knows.|The owls do it faster than you would have. You pocket what's left and feel only slightly guilty.|The owls know the round now as well as you. You keep a little of the fee and all of your afternoon.}\n\n@event job_board_market_stall\ntitle: Mind the Stall\npool: job_board_routine\ntone: warm\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A village market stall heaped with jars and woollens, a student behind it with a cash tin, the owner hobbling away towards a chair in the sun.\ntext: {A villager with a bad knee wants someone to mind her market stall on Saturday morning, for a share of the takings.|The stall again: jars, woollens, the bad knee and the share of the takings.|The stall owner pins the notice up with your name already on it, and a question mark, to be polite.}\n> left: Mind it as she asked\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You sell three jars and a scarf and count the tin twice. She pays you a fair share and a jar.|A slow morning, honestly counted. She is pleased with the tin and gives you your share.|You know the prices by heart now. She hands you your share without counting it, which is a kind of compliment.}\n> right: Talk up the wares\n  gold +tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: {You sell like a pedlar and the takings are up. Most of the extra goes back to her, as it should, and you've learned a patter.|You talk a farmer into two scarves he didn't know he needed. Your share is small and your voice is gone.|You could sell her jars to anyone now. She jokes about leaving you the stall.}\n\n@event job_board_lost_property\ntitle: The Lost-Property Box\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: key\ntags: job money\nscene: The porter's lodge, a vast wooden box of odd gloves, scarves, books and one boot, a student sorting them into piles.\ntext: {The porter wants the lost-property box sorted before it starts to smell, and pays by the hour.|The lost-property box is full again, the porter says, mostly of the same gloves.|The porter slides the box across without a word. You know where the odd boot goes now.}\n> left: Sort it quickly for the pay\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Gloves, scarves, books and one boot, in piles by dusk. The porter pays and keeps the boot for himself.|You sort it in an hour. The porter pays for two, out of habit.|You sort it fast. The porter pays, and then tries the odd boot on again, hopefully.}\n> right: Read what people lose\n  gold +tiny\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {You learn who carries love letters and who never returns library books. The porter pays you less for taking so long.|You read the names in the books and the notes in the pockets, and put everything back exactly. A slow hour, and a useful one.|You know the castle by what it drops. The porter pays a little less, and you put the gossip away for later.}\n\n@event job_board_sick_familiar\ntitle: A Sick Familiar\npool: job_board_routine\ntone: warm\nart: cat\ntags: job money\nscene: A student sitting up by lamplight beside a basket where a small sick familiar is curled in a blanket, a bottle of tonic on the table.\ntext: {A student going home for a funeral needs someone to sit up with their sick familiar for two nights. They will pay.|Another notice for a night sitter, for a sick toad this time, and the pay is better.|The familiars' night-sitter: that's you, apparently. A cat this time, and a worried owner.}\n> left: Sit up with it, both nights\n  gold +small\n  health -small\n  say: {Two nights of tonic every hour and very little sleep. It recovers. So, eventually, do you.|The toad recovers and you nearly don't. The owner pays double and calls you a saint.|The cat is ungrateful and lives. You're paid, and spend the next day asleep in lectures.}\n> right: Sit up one night, and find a second sitter\n  gold +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You take the first night and talk a friend into the second. The pay splits; the sleep doesn't.|One night each, you and a sleepy first-year. The familiar is fine. Half the pay is still pay.|You know who will sit up for a little money now. You split the nights and the pay, and sleep.}\n\n@event job_board_porters_crates\ntitle: Crates for the Porter\nplace: job_board\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: house\ntags: job money\nscene: A cart of wooden crates at the foot of a long castle stair, a student lifting one while the porter points upward.\ntext: {The porter needs crates carried up from the barge, a great many of them, up a great many stairs.|Crates again, the porter says, pointing at the barge. More of them than last time.|You hear the barge bell and the porter waves before you're even down the stairs. Crates.}\n> left: Carry them all\n  gold +small\n  health -small\n  say: {Forty crates up seventy stairs. The porter pays you and a little extra for not dropping the one that clinked.|Your back will remember every crate. The pay is good.|You carry the lot and don't count them any more. Paid, and sore.}\n> right: Carry what you can, and train with it\n  gold +tiny\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You take them slowly, two steps at a time, and count it as exercise. The porter pays for the crates you managed.|Half the crates, carried properly. Your legs are stronger and your purse a little lighter than it could be.|You carry what you can and stop before your back does. Paid for half, and stronger for it.}\n\n@event job_board_tutor_hour\ntitle: An Hour of Tutoring\npool: job_board_routine\ntone: warm\nart: book\ntags: job money\nscene: An empty classroom, a nervous first-year with an essay full of crossings-out and an older student pointing at a line.\ntext: {A first-year's notice, written with great care, asks for an hour's help with an essay. They can pay a little.|The same first-year's careful handwriting: another essay, another hour.|The first-year has stopped writing notices. They just pin up the essay, with a coin taped to the corner.}\n> left: Take the hour, and the coin\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {An hour of commas and confusion. The essay improves; so does your purse, slightly.|You take them through it line by line. They pay, and thank you twice.|You know their mistakes before they make them now. The hour goes quickly, and they pay.}\n> right: Help, and leave the coin pinned\n  mood +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {You leave the coin taped to the board. The essay comes back with a good mark, and the first-year tells everyone.|You help for nothing. They try to pay you anyway, and you won't let them.|You leave the coin where it is, as always. Somebody has drawn a small sun beside the notice.}\n\n@event job_board_greenhouse_pots\ntitle: Pots for Old Maud\npool: job_board_routine\ntone: warm\nart: leaf\ntags: job money\nscene: A greenhouse doorway stacked with muddy clay pots, an old gardener on a stool directing a student with a scrubbing brush.\ntext: {Old Maud's notice asks for clay pots scrubbed before planting, for a small wage and a cup of tea.|Maud's notice again. More pots, the same tea.|Maud has given up pinning notices. The pots are just waiting at the greenhouse door, with your brush on top.}\n> left: Scrub them for the wage\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {A hundred pots, scrubbed and stacked. Maud pays you and says you're slower than her and twice as thorough.|You scrub them all and drink the tea. The wage is small and honestly earned.|You scrub in silence, and so does she. She pays you and lets you take a cutting home.}\n> right: Scrub them and ask about the plants\n  gold +tiny\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {Maud talks while you scrub, about which leaf cures what and which leaf kills. She pays a little less, because of the talking.|You learn three remedies and a poison you should never brew. The wage is smaller; the lesson isn't.|Maud tests you on last month's lesson while you scrub. You pass, mostly. The wage is small and the tea is good.}\n\n# Exam revision (2026-10-19): once a term, before exam month, the library's first card. Studying adds `study +small`\n# (3) to every course: with the three months of class (9), one afternoon here makes the 12 that is a sure pass.\n@event library_revision\ntitle: The Revision Tables\nplace: library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A long library table under green-shaded lamps, stacked with past exam papers, students bent over them and a cat asleep at the far end.\nwhen: term and month != 4 and month != 10 and revised_term != term_index\ntext: The librarians have set out the revision tables early this term: past papers, model answers, and a sign promising the lamps stay lit until the bell. {~Footnote is asleep on last year's Evocation paper.|A shelf has rearranged itself into exam order, which it has never done for anyone before.|Someone has left a full set of notes behind, with a note of their own: \"Take them. I passed.\"}\n> left: Sit down and revise\n  study +small\n  revised_term = term_index\n  say: {~You work through two past papers and most of a third. The questions start to look like ones you have seen before, which is the point.|You copy out the model answers until your hand aches, then close the book and write them again from memory. Mostly right.|By the bell you have a page of everything you didn't know this morning. It is a shorter page than you feared.}\n> right: Not today\n  mood +small\n  revised_term = term_index\n  say: {~You leave the tables to the people who look more worried than you. The afternoon is yours.|You borrow a novel instead and read it in the window seat. The exams can wait for another day, you hope.|You nod at the revision tables on your way past, like an acquaintance you mean to call on.}\n> up[item:owl_quill]: Take notes with the owl quill\n  study +small\n  lore +tiny\n  revised_term = term_index\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~The owl quill takes your notes tidier than your hand could, and then underlines the bits you'll be asked. You argue with it for an hour.|The quill writes faster than you think. By the bell you have a page of everything, in a hand you could almost be proud of.|You let the quill copy out the model answers. It corrects your spelling, then your reasoning, then your posture.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/castle_more.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools, second pass (2026-10-24): castle_more.\n#  Repeatable routine events for the castle places that had the\n#  thinnest pools: library, lecture tower, great hall, infirmary,\n#  alchemy laboratory, Mir's workshop, the Ledger office, the\n#  headmistress's tower, the seventh tower and the thesis lab.\n#  Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Library ----------------\n\n@event library_whispering_dome\ntitle: The Whispering Dome\npool: library_routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A gallery railing under a painted dome at the top of the library, a student leaning over it with one ear cocked, seven floors of shelves below.\ntext: {Under the dome on the sixth floor the echoes are wrong in a useful way. A whisper anywhere in the library arrives here, clear as a bell.|The dome is carrying voices again. Someone has chalked a small ear on the railing.|You know the dome's geography now: by the pillar you hear the front desk; by the window, the stacks.} {Two voices below are discussing a test that hasn't been set yet.|Someone below is rehearsing a proposal of marriage, badly.|{season == \"winter\": The radiators hiss over half of it.|A prefect is reading out demerits to himself, with relish.}}\n> left: Lean on the rail and listen\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {You learn the third question of next week's test, probably. Eavesdroppers, it turns out, don't feel as clever as they'd hoped.|The rehearsal ends with a yes, said in the same voice as the question. You feel like a thief who has stolen a wedding that hasn't happened yet.|You learn exactly what the front desk thinks of you. It's fair, which is worse.}\n> right: Cough loudly, and read\n  lore +small\n  say: {Your cough rolls down the dome like thunder. Seven floors stop whispering at once, and you read in a silence you made.|You cough. Somewhere below, the proposal stops mid-sentence. You read a whole chapter before you stop wondering how it ended.|You cough on purpose now. Below, every whisper stops, and the dome is yours, and so is the chapter.}\n\n@event library_after_closing\nslot: evening\ntitle: After the Closing Bell\npool: library_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: lantern\ntags: study\nscene: A library at closing time, lamps going out floor by floor from the top down, a student half-hidden between two shelves with a book held to their chest.\ntext: {The bell rings to close the upper galleries, and their lamps begin to go out from the top floor down, one floor a minute. Students drift down to the reading room, which keeps its lamps for the night.|The galleries' closing bell. You've noticed the shelves up here lean together a little after hours, as if to talk.|The bell, the lamps, the drift down the stairs. You know the alcove on the fourth floor where the last lamp dies.} {Footnote is doing his rounds, tail up.|Rain is loud on the high windows.|{final_year: You wonder who will hide in your alcove next year.|Somewhere a book closes itself with a snap.}}\n> left: Stay hidden past closing\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You read by a stolen candle until the small hours. The library is a different animal at night, and it lets you be.|The shelves lean together and whisper while you read. You catch one word in three, and none of them are kind about the history section.|You read in your alcove till the candle drowns. Walking back across the bridges at two, you feel like a burglar who stole only sentences.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {A shelf shuffles sideways and nudges you, firmly, toward the stairs. A prefect on the stair watches you evicted by furniture.|The shelves close the aisle behind you like a mouth and walk you down to the reading room. The story is round the house by breakfast.|Footnote finds you, sits on your book and yowls until the porter comes. The porter tells everyone. Footnote is unrepentant.}\n> right: Leave with the crowd\n  health +small\n  say: {You go to bed at a decent hour, which feels almost illicit.|You leave with everyone else and sleep like a shelved book.|You walk out under the dying lamps and sleep well. The alcove can wait for you.}\n\n# ---------------- The Lecture Tower ----------------\n\n@event lecture_tower_faculty_basket\ntitle: The Faculty Basket\npool: lecture_tower_routine\ntone: light\nart: tower\ntags: class\nwhen: term\nscene: A wicker basket on a rope rising up the hollow core of a spiral tower, a sign on it reading FACULTY ONLY, a student eyeing it from the foot of the stairs.\ntext: {Up the hollow middle of the Lecture Tower runs a wicker basket on a rope, for faculty knees. A sign says FACULTY ONLY. It's two hundred and twelve steps to the top.|The basket is waiting at the bottom again. Someone has added \"AND THEIR LUGGAGE\" to the sign.|The basket. You know its creak, its sway, and the exact landing where it likes to stop.} {Professor Thorne's satchel is riding up in it, alone.|It still smells faintly of Professor Vey's perfume.|{season == \"summer\": The stairwell is an oven by the fifth landing.|A draught howls up the stairwell like a chimney.}}\n> left: Ride it up, quietly\n  challenge deceive apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      health +tiny\n      say: {You ride up beside the satchel, which seems to disapprove, and step off at the top fresh as morning.|You glide past six landings and step out at the top as if you'd walked. Your knees send their thanks.|The basket stops where it likes to stop; you know the trick of the rope now, and arrive at the top unwinded and unseen.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {The satchel's owner is waiting at the top. Thorne looks at you, then at the sign. \"Hm.\" You walk back down, then up.|The basket stops between floors. Professor Vey leans over the rail above. \"Faculty,\" she says, \"are generally taller.\" It's ten minutes before anyone winds you down.|It sticks at its favourite landing, and a whole lecture hall files past, one by one, to look at you.}\n> right: Climb all two hundred steps\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You arrive at the top red-faced and righteous, legs like wet bread.|You count the steps aloud. Somewhere beside you the basket creaks past, empty, smug.|You take the steps two at a time now, and only stop to breathe at the landing where the basket stops.}\n\n@event lecture_tower_office_hours\ntitle: The Office-Hours Queue\npool: lecture_tower_routine\ntone: warm\nart: scroll\ntags: study class\ncast:\n  prof: present(place) where tagged(prof, teacher) and met(prof)\nscene: A queue of students on a stone landing outside a half-open office door, one nervous first-year clutching an essay at the very back.\ntext: {{prof} keeps office hours on the landing, and the queue already reaches the stairs.|Office hours. {prof}'s queue has its etiquette now: no pushing, no crying, no essays read aloud.|The office-hours queue. You know the landing's cold step and the warm one.} At the very back a first-year is holding an essay like a shield{season == \"winter\": , breath fogging|}.\n> left: Wait your turn and ask\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  if npc_is(thorne):\n    say: {Forty minutes on the stair for four minutes of Thorne. \"Hm. Wrong, but interestingly.\" You'll take it.|Thorne reads your question, crosses out half of it, and answers the half that's left. It was the half that mattered.|Thorne lets you in, answers you in three sentences and says \"Next.\" You carry the three sentences around for a week.}\n  elif npc_is(vey):\n    say: {Vey answers your question with a question, and then another, until you answer it yourself. It takes an hour you didn't have.|Vey asks how you think you did. You lie. She smiles and tells you exactly how you did.|Vey listens, then asks what you actually came to ask. You hadn't known, until then.}\n  elif npc_is(marisol):\n    say: {Marisol answers with a poem about rivers, forgets the last line, and somehow the forgetting answers your question.|Marisol talks you through it while watering a fern on the sill. It takes an hour. It's a very good hour.|Marisol hears you out, then opens the window to let the weather in. \"There. Now ask me again.\"}\n  else:\n    say: {The wait is long, the answer short, and worth it.|You reach the door just before it shuts and get your answer standing on the threshold.|You get your answer, and a reading list longer than the queue.}\n> right: Give the first-year your place\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {The first-year goes in white and comes out pink and grateful. You never do get to ask your question.|You swap places. The first-year comes out an hour later having been told their essay \"has a pulse\". They take it as a triumph. So do you.|You give up your place again. The first-year waves the essay at you on the way out, like a flag.}\n\n# ---------------- The Great Hall ----------------\n\n@event great_hall_house_banners\ntitle: The House Banners\npool: great_hall_routine\ntone: light\nart: crown\ntags: social\nscene: Five long house banners hanging over the tables of an empty great hall, one of them conspicuously wrong.\ntext: {The five house banners over the long tables move with their houses' moods. Today Duskgate's hangs upside down, and is furious about it.|Someone has been at the banners again: Brasswick's has been embroidered with a small, elegant moustache.|Another banner prank: Rootward's has been swapped for a tablecloth with a turnip painted on it. The real one is sulking in a corner.} {The tables are empty; supper is an hour off.|A ladder leans against the wall, still warm from the culprit.|{season == \"winter\": The banners shiver in the draught from the high windows.|Emberhall's banner is smouldering slightly, in sympathy or envy.}}\n> left: Put it right before supper\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Twenty minutes up a wobbling ladder. Duskgate's banner, righted, gives a grudging flap. Your palms are rope-burned.|You unpick the moustache stitch by stitch from the top of the ladder. Brasswick's banner shudders with relief; your neck doesn't.|You haul the real banner up and the tablecloth down. Rootward's banner settles over its table like a hen on eggs. Your arms ache till morning.}\n> right: Improve the prank\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You add a matching upside-down candle to the table below. At supper Duskgate laughs hardest, which is how you know it was them.|You give the moustache a monocle. Brasswick declares it an improvement and votes to keep it.|You paint a crown on the turnip. Rootward is outraged, then proud, then outraged again.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {A prefect finds you on the ladder with an upside-down candle, and the whole prank is pinned on you by supper.|You're halfway through a monocle when Brasswick's entire table walks in. You are now the moustache person.|You're caught with paint on your hands and a crown half done. Rootward sends a strongly worded turnip to your table.}\n\n@event great_hall_stagehands\ntitle: Stagehands Wanted\nplace: great_hall\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: music\ntags: festival social\nscene: Students on ladders painting a huge backdrop on the great hall's stage while a cook carries in a tray of tasting plates.\ntext: {season == \"autumn\": Scenery for the Harvest Feast is going up on the stage: a painted moon the size of a barn.|{season == \"winter\": They're building the Midwinter Ball's ice arch out of painted canvas and optimism.|{season == \"spring\": The Spring Tournament's banners are being hemmed on the stage, in five house colours.|They're painting the Starfall backdrop, a whole sky, star by star.}}} {A sign by the stage says STAGEHANDS WANTED, NO EXPERIENCE NECESSARY.|The sign now says SOME EXPERIENCE PREFERRED. Nobody is checking.|Someone hands you a brush as you come in, just in case.} Meanwhile the kitchens are testing the feast menu on anyone who sits still.\n> left: Paint, hammer and hold ladders\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You paint for three hours and charm the paint to shimmer at the edges. It's the best thing on the stage. Your shoulders disagree.|You hold ladders, hammer, paint, hit your thumb twice and get paint in your hair that won't come out till the festival. Someone stands back and says \"oh\", the good kind.|They give you the tricky bit, the part that has to move when the candles do, because nobody else will. It does. You get splinters, and applause.}\n> right: Taste-test for the kitchens\n  health +small\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {You eat seven tasting plates and give honest marks. The pear tart wins. Your revision notes stay shut.|You taste and judge and taste again. The cook writes down everything you say, and your afternoon of reading is gone.|The cook saves you the corner seat and the first spoonful. The menu is better for you; your reading pile is taller.}\n\n# ---------------- The Infirmary ----------------\n\n@event infirmary_night_watch\nslot: evening\ntitle: Turning the Fever Charm\npool: infirmary_routine\ntone: warm\nart: candle\ntags: social\nscene: A dim infirmary ward at night, one lamp lit by a bed where a sleeping student lies under a slowly turning ring of pale light.\ntext: {A first-year in the end bed has a fever, and the cooling charm over the bed has to be turned by hand every hour until dawn. Sister Amaranth looks at you, then at the chair.|Another fever, another chair. The cooling charm's brass ring is worn smooth where hands turn it.|The chair by the end bed. Sister Amaranth has left a cushion on it, which is as close as she comes to asking.} {The ward is quiet except for the clock.|Rain is drumming on the infirmary roof.|{season == \"winter\": Frost feathers the window behind the bed.|Moths keep the lamp company.}}\n> left: Take the night watch\n  alchemy +small\n  health -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {You turn the ring every hour and watch the fever ebb by inches. At dawn Sister Amaranth says \"Keep doing that,\" and takes the chair.|You doze between turns and wake on the hour, every hour, as if the ring calls you. The patient wakes clear-eyed. You don't.|You know the charm's weight now and turn it half asleep. At dawn the patient asks your name. You're too tired to remember it.}\n> right: Promise next time, and sleep\n  health +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Sister Amaranth turns the charm herself, without comment, and that's worse than a comment.|You go to bed and lie awake a while, counting hours you're not turning.|You leave the cushion on the chair for someone else. You sleep, mostly.}\n\n@event infirmary_curious_case\ntitle: A Curious Case\npool: infirmary_routine\ntone: light\nart: potion\ntags: magic\nscene: A student sitting up in an infirmary bed with a strange magical symptom while a stern healer frowns at a thick reference book.\ntext: {A second-year sits in bed four hiccuping soap bubbles.|In bed four today: a student whose hair has turned to feathers, and keeps trying to migrate.|Bed four has a student who can only speak in rhyme, and is running out of rhymes.} {Sister Amaranth has three books open and no patience left.|Sister Amaranth glances at you. \"You. Useful or scarce. Pick.\"|{season == \"winter\": Half the ward has the sniffles, and the hiccups have gone round.|Sister Amaranth is writing a stern note to whoever did this.}}\n> left: Help look it up\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      lore +small\n      say: {You find it on page four hundred. A soap-tongue jinx, cured by mint and silence. Sister Amaranth says \"Good,\" without looking up.|You trace the feathers to a half-learned transformation and the cure to a bowl of warm salt water. The student moults gratefully.|You find the rhyming hex and the counter-word. The student's first plain sentence is \"thank you\", which is a relief to everyone.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      lore +tiny\n      say: {You read for an hour and find three cures for things nobody has. Sister Amaranth solves it with a cup of tea and a look.|You recommend plucking. Everyone stares at you. Sister Amaranth sends you to fetch towels instead.|You suggest a cure that makes the rhymes worse and slightly operatic. Sister Amaranth fixes it, and you, with a glance.}\n> right: Keep the patient company\n  charm +small\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You see who can blow the biggest bubble. You lose, but you laugh so hard your ribs ache.|You help the student hold on to the bedframe whenever a flock goes over. Feathers get everywhere, including your lungs.|You rhyme back. You run out of rhymes together, and by then you're both crying with laughter and very sore.}\n\n# ---------------- The Alchemy Laboratory ----------------\n\n@event alchemy_lab_crystal_sill\ntitle: The Crystal on the Sill\npool: alchemy_lab_routine\ntone: light\nart: gem\ntags: magic\nscene: A blue crystal growth spilling out of a jar on a laboratory windowsill and creeping slowly down the wall.\ntext: {On the windowsill sits somebody's abandoned crystal experiment, a jar of blue spikes labelled DO NOT FEED.|The crystal has grown out of its jar and halfway down the wall. The label now reads DO NOT FEED (WE MEAN IT).|The crystal reaches the floor now and has started on the next window. Someone has given it a name and a little hat.} {It chimes faintly when the door slams.|It has grown a new spike toward the warmest cauldron.|{season == \"winter\": Frost and crystal are racing each other up the glass.|In the afternoon light it throws blue all over the benches.}}\n> left: Feed it a pinch of salt\n  chance even:\n    alchemy +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {It drinks the salt with a sound like a sigh and grows one perfect new spike. You understand, suddenly, how it's built.|A new spike unfolds slowly, like a fern. You sketch the growth, and it makes sense of a whole chapter.|It grows a spike in your exact direction, which is either affection or aim. You take notes either way.}\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {It shoots out a spike the size of a finger and catches your sleeve and your wrist. The label, you concede, was right.|It grows a spike straight through your notes and into your thumb. You are not the first; there's a list.|It sneezes crystal dust all over you. You sparkle blue until bedtime and itch till morning.}\n> right: Snap off a spike for Pell's\n  gold +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {Pell & Daughters pays well for clean crystal, no questions asked. The jar seems to notice the gap.|You snap off a spike while it's busy growing toward the cauldron. It chimes once, sharply, as if taking your name. Pell's pays anyway.|You break off a piece from the bottom where nobody will notice and sell it in the village. The little hat tilts at you when you come back.}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_good_scales\ntitle: The Good Scales\npool: alchemy_lab_routine\ntone: light\nart: scales\ntags: study\nscene: A queue of students with jars and papers waiting at a single fine brass balance in a cluttered laboratory, while battered scales sit unused along the bench.\ntext: {The laboratory has twelve sets of scales and one that's accurate. The queue for it reaches the door.|The queue for the good scales has rules now, chalked on the bench: one weighing each, no hovering, no charms.|The good scales. You can tell from the door how long the wait is by how many people are sighing.} {A third-year at the front is weighing powdered moonstone grain by grain.|The other scales are off by a hair, a whisker or a small mouse.|{season == \"summer\": It's hot, and every powder in the room is clumping.|The windows have steamed over and nobody can read their numbers.}}\n> left: Charm your way to the front\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You admire the third-year's technique until they wave you ahead out of sheer flattered confusion.|You quote the chalked rules back at the queue in such a reasonable voice that they let you through to weigh \"just one thing\".|You've learned the queue's weak points. A compliment, a promise and a borrowed spatula, and you're at the scales in a minute.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {The queue turns as one and points at the back. You go to the back.|Someone reads out rule two, NO HOVERING, and looks at you while they do it. The whole queue laughs.|The queue closes ranks like a wall of elbows. You spend the wait at the back, flushed.}\n> right: Use the bad scales and correct\n  alchemy +small\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You weigh everything twice on two bad scales and average the errors. Your measurements are right; your head hurts.|You work out exactly how wrong the mouse-scale is and correct for it. It's tedious, and the best arithmetic you've done all week.|You've mapped all eleven bad scales' errors on a card in your pocket. It doesn't make the work less dull.}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_mind_my_cauldron\nslot: evening\ntitle: Mind My Cauldron?\npool: alchemy_lab_routine\ntone: warm\nart: cauldron\ntags: social\ncast:\n  mate: any where (tagged(mate, student) or tagged(mate, prefect)) and rel(mate) >= 0 and around(mate)\nscene: A student hurrying out of an evening laboratory, pointing back at a bubbling cauldron and an hourglass, while another student is left holding the spoon.\ntext: {{mate} has a six-hour brew going and a supper {they} can't miss. \"Stir it widdershins when the sand runs out. Twice. I owe you.\"|{mate} spots you across the lab and is already holding out the spoon. \"Hourglass. Twice. Widdershins. You're a lifesaver.\"|{mate} hands you the spoon with the air of someone who knows you'll say yes. The hourglass is half run.} {The brew smells of oranges and thunder.|The brew is the colour of a bruise, and humming.|{season == \"winter\": The lab windows are black and the cauldrons the only warmth.|Somebody's cauldron at the far end goes off like a cork.}}\n> left: Stay and mind it\n  alchemy +small\n  rel mate +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You miss supper and stir twice, exactly. {mate} comes back to a perfect brew, and you to a rumbling stomach.|You mind it, and pick up three of {mate}'s tricks just by watching the sand. Your own supper is cold bread.|The brew behaves for you. {mate} comes back, sniffs, and says your wrist is better than {theirs}. Your stomach disagrees with the praise.}\n> right: Sorry, I've plans\n  mood +small\n  rel mate -tiny\n  say: {You go to supper. {mate} goes to supper late, and the brew turns slightly orange in revenge.|You make your excuses. {mate} finds someone else, and mentions it, twice.|You say no this time. {mate} looks at the spoon, then at you, and sighs the sigh of the betrayed.}\n\n# ---------------- Mir's Workshop ----------------\n\n@event workshop_after_the_bang\ntitle: After the Bang\npool: workshop_routine\ntone: light\nart: gear\ntags: magic\nscene: A workshop wreathed in drifting smoke, a soot-blackened inventor waving cheerfully through it, springs and gears scattered across the floor.\ntext: {You hear the bang from the corridor. Inside, the smoke is thinning, and Mir waves through it, eyebrows mostly intact. \"That was the good kind of bang! Probably!\"|Another bang. Mir has chalked a tally by the door: GOOD BANGS and BAD BANGS. Today's goes in a third column, MUSICAL.|You don't even flinch at the bang now. Mir hands you a broom before the smoke clears.} {Gears are still rolling across the floor.|A small cog is spinning on its edge, refusing to fall.|{season == \"winter\": The blast has blown the window open and snow is coming in.|The fire bucket by the door is already empty.}}\n> left: Help sweep up and salvage\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You sort springs from gears from bits of the ceiling. A spring bites your finger; Mir says that one's always been like that.|You salvage a box of parts and a burnt sleeve. Mir promises to name the next bang after you.|You sweep like a professional now, and pocket a cog that wouldn't fall. Mir pretends not to see. Your fingers are cut in two places.}\n> right: Read the singed blueprint\n  arcana +small\n  say: {You find the mistake, a rune drawn backwards, and tell Mir, who is delighted, then mortified, then delighted. \"Probably that's what it was!\"|You study the scorched blueprint while Mir sweeps around you, and learn more from the burn marks than from the lines.|You can read Mir's blueprints now, even singed. You show them where today's bang came from. They want to do it again, on purpose.}\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger Office ----------------\n\n@event ledger_office_crossword\ntitle: The Crossword Is Late\npool: ledger_office_routine\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: club\nscene: A half-finished crossword grid pinned above a cluttered desk in a newspaper office, a pencil dangling from a string beside it.\ntext: {The Ledger's crossword setter has vanished before the deadline, leaving half a grid pinned over the desk and a pencil on a string.|The crossword is late again. The setter's note says \"back soon\", and has said it since Tuesday.|The crossword grid again, half done. The pencil on its string swings toward you as if it knows.} {Seven across is blank. Nine down is a rude word, crossed out.|A furious letter about last week's puzzle is pinned beside it, in a professor's hand.|{season == \"winter\": The ink in the pot has a skin of ice.|The press is warming up, and so is everyone's temper.}}\n> left: Finish setting it, properly\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You fill the grid with honest clues and one fiendish one. Your eyes ache; it goes to print with your pencil marks still on it.|You set it so carefully that the professor who complained will have nothing to complain about. It takes all evening.|You set the whole grid by lamplight. Nine down is no longer rude. You're a little sorry.}\n> right: Hide a message in the clues\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      renown +small\n      say: {The first letters of the across clues spell SOUP IS A CRIME. By lunch the whole refectory has solved it, and your name is going round with it.|You hide a riddle in the down clues. Half the castle spends a week on it, the professor writes in again, delighted, and somebody tells him who set it.|Your hidden message is the talk of breakfast, and the Ledger lets slip who set it.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {Nessa reads the proof, finds SOUP IS A CRIME in the first letters and circles it in red, then circles you.|The message comes out garbled: the clues spell SOUP IS A CRIMP. Nobody understands it. Somehow that's worse.|Nessa spots it at the drying line and pulls the proof. \"Clever,\" she says. \"Not printed. Next.\"}\n\n# ---------------- The Headmistress's Tower ----------------\n\n@event headmistress_tower_window_boxes\ntitle: The Window Boxes\npool: headmistress_tower_routine\ntone: warm\nart: flower\ntags: nature\ncast:\n  host: isolde\nscene: A high tower window open over a dizzying drop to the lake, a row of window boxes of night-flowering herbs along the sill, a calm woman writing at a desk behind.\ntext: {\"Would you see to the window boxes while I finish this?\" {host} says, without looking up. The boxes run along the outside of the tower window, high over the lake.|The window boxes again. {host} has left the watering can on the sill, which counts as an invitation.|You reach for the watering can before {host} asks. She smiles at her letter.} {The far box is just out of reach without leaning.|One plant in the far box has opened a single silver flower.|{season == \"winter\": Frost has nipped the herbs; they need covering.|Bees from somewhere have found the boxes, this high up.}}\n> left: Lean out to the far box\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You lean out over the drop with one hand on the frame and tend the far box. When you pull back in, {host} is watching. \"Steady,\" she says. It's praise.|You reach the far box and the silver flower opens a second bloom as you water it. You stay leaning out a moment longer than you need to, for the view.|You know the reach now: knee on the sill, hand on the frame. The far box is tended in a minute, and the lake glitters far below.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {Your foot slips on the sill and the watering can drops a very long way. {host} doesn't look up. \"There's another in the cupboard,\" she says. Your shin is skinned.|You bang your head on the frame coming back in. {host}'s quill pauses, then goes on. The silver flower doesn't get its water.|You reach, wobble, and decide against it with your heart pounding and an elbow scraped. {host} tends the far box herself afterwards, without comment.}\n> right: Tend the near boxes and talk\n  lore +small\n  say: {You water the near herbs while {host} tells you their names, moonmint, star-sorrel, a thyme that only opens after dark. She knows each one's story.|{host} asks what you're reading this term and listens to the answer as she writes. You leave having said more than you meant to, and learned the names of six plants.|You tend the near boxes while {host} reads you a line from her letter and asks what you'd reply. You think about it on the stairs all the way down.}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_waiting_bench\ntitle: The Waiting Bench\npool: headmistress_tower_routine\ntone: light\nart: hourglass\ntags: social\nscene: A narrow stone bench at the foot of a spiral stair, a pale first-year sitting on it clutching a summons, a stone owl watching from above.\ntext: {On the bench at the foot of the stair sits a first-year with a summons in one hand and doom on their face. \"What's she like?\" they whisper.|Another summoned first-year on the bench, another whisper: \"Is it true she can read minds?\"|The waiting bench has a first-year on it again; you suspect it always will. \"Have you been in? What happens?\"} {The stone owl watches you both.|Somewhere above, a door opens and closes.|{final_year: By next year you'll be gone, and the bench will still have someone on it.|The summons is smudged from being held so hard.}}\n> left: Tell them the kind truth\n  charm +small\n  say: {\"She'll listen,\" you say, \"and it will be worse than shouting, and then it will be better.\" The first-year breathes for the first time in an hour.|\"She can't read minds,\" you say. \"She just doesn't need to.\" The first-year laughs, shakily, and goes up a little taller.|\"She'll pour you tea,\" you say. \"Drink it. Tell her the truth.\" The first-year nods and goes up the stair like someone visiting a friend.}\n> right: Tell them a horror story\n  guile +small\n  mood +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {You describe the trapdoor, the pit and the crocodiles in loving detail. The first-year goes up grey. You feel wonderful, then less wonderful.|\"Only on Thursdays,\" you say gravely. It's Thursday. The first-year's face is a work of art.|You tell them about the owl that eats liars. The owl, above you, opens one eye and looks at you for a long time.}\n\n# ---------------- The Seventh Tower ----------------\n\n@pool seventh_tower_routine\nplace: seventh_tower\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event seventh_tower_candle_landing\ntitle: Other People's Candles\npool: seventh_tower_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: candle\ntags: mystery\nscene: A narrow stone landing crowded with old candle stubs of every colour, initials scratched into the wall above them, one stub still faintly warm.\ntext: {Halfway up the seventh tower is a landing crowded with candle stubs, a century of them, with initials scratched in the wall above. Whoever climbed here, climbed with a light.|The candle landing. You recognise some of the initials now, the way you recognise faces in a corridor.|Back at the landing of candles. You've found your own favourite set of initials, deep and careful, and wonder who cut them.} {One stub is still faintly warm.|{season == \"winter\": The wax is cold as stone.|A draught from nowhere leans every wick the same way.}|One stub has been lit and blown out since your last climb.}\n> left: Leave a candle of your own\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You light the candle you bought in the village and set it among the others. For a moment the landing is bright, and the initials look almost cheerful.|You light a new candle and sit with it while it burns down. It feels like adding your name to a very long, very quiet register.|You leave a fresh candle beside the careful initials. When you look back from the stair, its flame is leaning toward them.}\n> right: Read the initials by lamplight\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You copy down the initials and the dates. Most of them are a century old or more; one is from last year. Nobody you ask admits to it.|You find two sets of initials cut in the same hand, forty years apart. You leave without deciding what that means.|You read the wall until your lamp gutters. All those people, climbing up here with a light, alone. It sits with you all evening.}\n\n@event seventh_tower_wind_song\ntitle: The Wind in the Slits\npool: seventh_tower_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: music\ntags: mystery\nscene: Thin arrow-slit windows in the curved wall of a dusty tower stair, wind whistling through them, a student with one hand pressed to the cold stone.\ntext: {The wind plays the seventh tower's arrow-slits like a flute, a different note at every window.|The tower is playing again. You'd swear the tune has more notes than there are windows.|The wind's song. You can hum the first part now; nobody else in the castle knows it.} {Today it sounds almost like a lullaby.|Today it is quick and fretful, as if late for something.|{season == \"winter\": It plays low and slow, like something under snow.|It plays in fits, between gusts.}}\n> left: Hum along and listen\n  mood +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {You hum back. The tower answers in harmony, and you feel it drawing your magic out through the tune, thin and bright. You come away drained and happy, as after a long song.|You hum, and one window falls silent as if to listen. The tune pulls at your magic like a tide; it costs you something to keep going. You keep going.|You hum the part you know and the tower plays the rest, on your magic as much as the wind. When it's done your fingertips are cold and empty, and you're smiling.}\n> right: Write the tune down\n  lore +small\n  say: {You mark each window's note in your notebook. It's a scale nobody uses any more; you find it later in a very old songbook.|You write the notes down and get a different tune each time you read them back. That seems important, and you can't think why.|Your page of notes is filling up. The tune has the same shape as the stair, you realise; you climb it, more or less.}\n\n@event seventh_tower_outside_ledge\ntitle: The Desks on the Stair\npool: seventh_tower_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: tower\ntags: danger\nscene: A narrow tower stair choked with a heap of old wooden classroom desks stacked legs-up, a student with a candle looking for a way through.\ntext: {Two turns below the top, the stair is choked with old classroom desks, stacked legs-up to the ceiling, the way somebody left them a century ago. There's a gap, just.|The desks on the stair have shifted again; the gap is somewhere else this time.|You know the heap now: the desk with the cracked lid, the one that wobbles, the one you never lean on.} {A draught whistles through the legs.|{season == \"winter\": Frost has furred the old varnish.|A mouse watches you from an inkwell.}|Your candle finds a carved date, and loses it.}\n> left: Shift the desks and clear the way\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You haul the desks off the heap one by one and stand them against the wall, legs down, properly. When you're done the stair is a stair again, and your arms are lead.|You lift, slide and wedge until the gap is wide enough to walk through upright. Someone has been at the heap since; you put it right again.|Cracked lid, wobbler, the one you never lean on: in that order. You're through in ten minutes and up at the top with the dust and the moon.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {The heap shifts the wrong way and a desk comes down the stair after you. You get out of its way, mostly. Your shin does not.|You pull the wrong desk and the whole heap groans. You back down the stair with a splinter the size of a pen in your palm.|The wobbler wobbles. You catch it with your shoulder, hard, and decide the top of the tower can wait.}\n> right: Look inside the desks\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You lift one cracked lid. Inside are a quill, a ruler and a page of conjugations copied out a hundred years ago, stopping halfway down. You finish it, for them, and feel oddly sad.|Another lid, another desk: a note passed in class, \"meet me by the lake\", never answered. Underneath it is the lesson, and you read that instead.|You've been through half the desks now. Every one stops in the middle of something, as if the bell rang and nobody came back.}\n\n# ---------------- The Thesis Laboratory ----------------\n\n@pool thesis_lab_routine\nplace: thesis_lab\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event thesis_lab_tea_urn\ntitle: The Tea Urn\npool: thesis_lab_routine\ntone: warm\nart: cup\ntags: social study\nscene: A great dented copper tea urn on a trolley at the end of a row of study carrels, final-year students clustered round it holding mugs and complaints.\ntext: {The thesis lab runs on the tea urn, a dented copper monster that has outlived forty years of final-years. Round it, people confess things.|At the urn today someone is crying quietly into their mug about chapter three.|The urn. Someone has taped a list to it: STAGES OF THESIS GRIEF. Everyone has initialled the stage they're at.} {The urn is making the noise that means it's about to boil over.|Someone has hidden a tin of biscuits behind it, labelled NOT BISCUITS.|{season == \"spring\": The windows are open, and blossom keeps landing in the mugs.|The lamps are lit already, though it's barely evening.}}\n> left: Stay and listen\n  charm +small\n  mood +small\n  say: {You hear about an advisor who writes only in question marks, a source that turned out to be a forgery and a cat that ate a bibliography. You say almost nothing, and everyone thanks you.|You sit with the one crying about chapter three until they're laughing about chapter three.|You find your stage on the list and initial it. Then you listen to everyone else's, which is better than any advice.}\n> right: Fill your mug and get back to work\n  lore +small\n  say: {You fill your mug and walk away from the confessions. Your carrel is quiet and your notes go well, and you feel slightly like a deserter.|You pat the crying student's shoulder on your way past and go back to work. It's the best you can do today.|You take your tea back to your carrel. Behind you, the list of stages grows another set of initials.}\n\n@event thesis_lab_old_theses\ntitle: The Shelf of Old Theses\npool: thesis_lab_routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A long shelf of bound theses in faded cloth, a student pulling one out and blowing dust off a spine with a ridiculous title.\ntext: {Along the back wall run three centuries of finished theses, bound in cloth that has faded to the same brown.|The thesis shelf. You've started a list of the best titles. Today's find: a study of the emotional lives of doorknobs.|The old theses. You can find the good ones by feel now: the spines are worn where people keep taking them down.} {One near the end has a pressed flower for a bookmark.|One has a note on the flyleaf: \"Passed. Barely. Worth it.\"|{season == \"spring\": Blossom drifts in through the open window and settles on the spines.|A spider has made its home between two famous ones.}}\n> left: Read a famous one\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {It's brilliant, and it was written by someone younger than you. You put it back very carefully and go and sit somewhere quiet.|It's the one everybody cites. Reading it in full is like seeing a mountain from the bottom.|You read the most-worn one cover to cover. It's so good it hurts, which you suppose is the point.}\n> right: Read a gloriously bad one\n  mood +small\n  say: {It argues for forty pages that rain is a kind of apology. It passed. You feel enormously better about everything.|You read the doorknob one. It is three hundred pages long and completely serious, and you laugh until the carrels shush you.|You find one with footnotes longer than its chapters, and footnotes to the footnotes. Your own thesis suddenly seems sane.}\n\n@event thesis_lab_mock_defence\ntitle: The Mock Defence\npool: thesis_lab_routine\ntone: tense\nart: scales\ntags: study\nscene: A chalkboard at the end of the thesis lab, three final-year students sitting as stern pretend examiners in borrowed gowns while a fourth stands to defend.\ntext: {Final-years run mock defences at the end of the lab. Three in borrowed gowns play the committee, and they play it cruel.|Mock defence day. The pretend committee has added a fourth chair and a tiny gavel.|The mock committee nods at you as you come in. They've started keeping score on the board.} {Tonight's chief examiner is doing an impression of Thorne that is unkindly accurate.|The chair has a bell, which they ring for every \"um\".|{season == \"winter\": Everyone defends in scarves; the lab's fire is out.|Someone has borrowed a skeleton from the infirmary to sit in the audience.}}\n> left: Take the hot seat\n  challenge research adept-:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {You field every question, even the cruel one about your sources. The chief examiner says \"Hm,\" in Thorne's voice, and means it.|They ring the bell for \"um\" only twice. That's a record, and the committee applauds with the tiny gavel.|You hold your ground for an hour. Your score on the board is the highest of the term, and people come to ask how.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: {The third question undoes you. You stand at the board saying \"well\" while the chief examiner does Thorne's eyebrow at you.|They find the hole in your argument in four minutes, and the bell rings for every \"um\" after that. Better here than in front of the real thing.|You lose your thread under the gavel. The committee is merciful afterwards, which is worse.}\n> right: Sit on the committee\n  guile +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You ask one question, quietly, and watch the defender's face as they see the hole. You feel like a cat. A kind cat.|You learn to find the weak joint in an argument by watching three of them crack. It's educational and a little shameful.|You're the committee's favourite examiner now: gentle voice, terrible questions.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/castle_towers.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools: the castle towers (phase 3, step 1b).\n#  Repeatable routine events for the observatory, Mir's workshop,\n#  the Ledger office, the headmistress's tower, the Restricted\n#  Section, the third gargoyle and the seventh tower, plus the\n#  hidden-course hints for artifice, starcalling and hollow studies.\n#  No clues, no arc moves: these places stay mysterious.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Observatory ----------------\n\n@pool observatory_routine\nplace: observatory\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event observatory_night_log\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Night Log\npool: observatory_routine\ntone: warm\nart: book\ntags: study magic\nscene: A brass observatory dome at night, one student writing in a huge logbook by lamplight while the great telescope swings round on its own.\ntext: {The observatory keeps a night log, and anyone may write in it.|{flag(wrote_night_log): Since your last entry|Since you were last here}, someone has drawn a moustache on the moon.|You know the log's hands by now: the careful one, the frantic one, the one who only ever writes \"cloudy\".} {season == \"winter\": Frost feathers the inside of the dome.|{The telescope turns, unasked, towards the north.|A draught turns the pages for you.|The lamp is low on oil, as always.}}\n> left: Log what the telescope finds\n  flag wrote_night_log\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You chart a star that wasn't there last month. The log already has it, in a hand forty years older.|Three hours, two pages and one sneeze that smudges a constellation.|The telescope settles on a quiet blue star and waits until you've written it down properly.}\n> right: Read the old entries instead\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {A winter entry from long ago says \"Saw nothing. Stayed anyway. Worth it.\"|A love letter written across a page of moon sightings. Very bad. Very sweet.|{final_year and flag(wrote_night_log): You find your own first entry and barely know the hand.|A student from your year logged your star a week before you did, with a small drawn smile.}}\n> up[item:pocket_almanac]: Check the almanac first\n  flag wrote_night_log\n  lore +small\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The almanac says the moon sets at eleven, so you log the faint stars after it, and get three the log has never had.|Grimsby's pencil in the margin says \"watch the east at two\". You do. Something small and bright crosses it, and you write it down.|You check the tides and the moon before you start, and for once the telescope and you agree on where to look.}\n\n@event observatory_one_star_each\ntime: evening night\ntitle: One Star Each\npool: observatory_routine\ntone: warm\nart: star\ntags: social magic\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)\nscene: Two students at the eyepiece of a great brass telescope, one stepping aside to let the other look.\ntext: {The rule chalked on the observatory door says one star each, then pass it on. {here} has been at the eyepiece for some time.|Someone has added \"two if they're small\" under the chalk rule. {here} is at the eyepiece.|Someone is always at the telescope when you arrive; tonight it's {here}.} {season == \"summer\": The nights are short and the queue is impatient.|{The dome is cold enough to see your breath.|The telescope creaks round to follow something.|Below, the bridges of light are going out one by one.}}\n> left: Share the eyepiece\n  rel here +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {\"That one,\" Seraphine says, \"is going to matter in, oh.\" She doesn't finish.|\"Left of it,\" Seraphine says. \"Next spring's left.\" You look anyway.|\"There,\" Seraphine says, and moves your hand an inch. \"That's the one you'll want. Don't ask me when.\"}|{cast_as(here, kael): {Kael steps aside and points. When your hands get cold, he stands closer.|Kael says the star's name in a Northern word, once, and doesn't explain.|Kael shares the eyepiece without being asked. Below, the bridges go out one by one, and neither of you counts them.}|You take turns until neither of you remembers whose star it was.}}\n> right: Wait your turn properly\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {Seraphine leaves mid-sentence. \"You'll want it,\" she says. \"Later. Now, I mean.\"|\"Yours is the next one,\" Seraphine says, and it is.|Seraphine steps back as you arrive, as if she'd been holding your place. \"Right on time,\" she says, puzzling you.}|{cast_as(here, kael): {Kael yields with a formal nod and goes out onto the roof. Frost follows.|Kael waits behind you. When you turn round, he's gone.|Kael gives up the eyepiece the moment you arrive and stays to watch the bridges go out with you.}|By your turn the star has set. The next one is nearly as good.}}\n\n@event observatory_roof_ledge\ntitle: The Roof Ledge\npool: observatory_routine\ntone: tense\nart: moon\ntags: life danger\nscene: A student sitting on a narrow ledge outside a brass dome, legs dangling over the castle roofs, the lake far below.\ntext: {A window in the dome opens onto the roof, and onto a ledge that everyone swears they have never sat on.|Someone else who never sits there has oiled the latch.|The ledge. You know where the loose tile is now.} {season == \"winter\": The tiles are white with frost.|{The lake below is a second sky.|A warm wind comes up from the village.|Someone's scarf is snagged on the gutter.}}\n> left: Climb out onto the ledge\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: {You sit with the whole sky to yourself until the stars begin to wheel.|The castle looks small from here, and kind, and very old.|You step over the loose tile without looking. Someone's scarf is still on the gutter; you leave it, for whoever comes next.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The loose tile is loose in a new place. You slide, grab, and hang on, and climb back in with skinned palms.|A gust takes your balance and most of your dignity. The gutter keeps the rest.|You reach for the snagged scarf and the gutter reaches for you. You get back in with the scarf and a bruise.}\n> right: Watch through the glass\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {You see half the sky and all of the smudges.|Through the glass the stars look like they're behind a shop window. You can look; you can't have.|You watch through the glass. On the ledge outside, someone else is sitting where you'd have sat, and they wave.}\n\n@event observatory_scraped_word\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Scraped-Out Word\nplace: observatory\nwhen: not enrolled(starcalling) and not passed(starcalling, 1) and not closed(hollowstar)\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 6\nart: star\ntags: study mystery\nscene: An old observatory logbook open by lamplight, the same word scraped out of line after line.\ntext: {In the night log's oldest volume, every entry by a student who had finished Advanced Astromancy uses the same word, and every time it has been scraped out.|You've worked out that the missing word was something you did to a star, not something you saw.|You know these pages now. The word is gone; the shape of the work around it is not.} {In one margin, \"Ask. Be told.\"|A pressed flower falls out.|The lamp gutters as you turn the page.}\n> left: Trace the gaps by lamplight\n  find heard_of_starcalling\n  lore +small\n  mana -small\n  say: You feed a thread of witchlight into the gaps, and it drinks more than you meant to give. {The words around the gaps say \"called\", \"named\", \"down\". Whoever taught it taught it aloud, to people who had finished Advanced Astromancy.|\"...and the light came down when I said its name...\" Then the gap. You will need someone to tell you the word.|The shape around the gap is a verb. Whatever it was, it was done to a star, and it was taught, not written.}\n> right: Ask the telescope, half joking\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {The telescope swings to one bright star and stays there, patient as a teacher who knows you aren't ready.|It turns to a named star and holds it. You get the feeling you have to finish Advanced Astromancy before it explains.|The telescope turns, very slowly, to the same star as always. You're beginning to think it's a reminder, not an answer.}\n\n# ---------------- Mir's Workshop ----------------\n\n@pool workshop_routine\nplace: workshop\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event workshop_spare_bench\ntitle: The Spare Bench\npool: workshop_routine\ntone: light\nart: gear\ntags: magic life\nscene: A cluttered workshop bench under a hand-painted sign, a music box in pieces beside a jar of springs, fire buckets by the door.\ntext: {Mir keeps a spare bench for visitors, under a sign that says TOUCH THINGS (CAREFULLY).|The sign over the spare bench has grown a second line, (MORE CAREFULLY).|Your bench, as Mir has started calling it, has a jar of springs waiting.} {Mir waves a spanner at you without looking up.|Something on a high shelf is ticking.|{season == \"winter\": The forge-lamp makes it the warmest room in the west wing.|The window is open to let the smoke out.}}\n> left: Mend a jammed music box\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: A loose spring bites your thumb on the way in. {It plays again, a little too fast, like it's late for something.|You fix the tune and break the lid. Mir says that's probably progress.|It plays a waltz nobody has heard in years. You leave it on the sill to finish.}\n> right: Just watch Mir work\n  mood +small\n  rel mir +tiny\n  say: {Mir explains a gear train for an hour. You follow about a third, which they say is probably a record.|You sit by the fire buckets and watch the sparks. Your eyes sting. It's a good afternoon.|Mir hands you things to hold and forgets you're holding them. It's oddly restful.}\n\n@event workshop_volunteer\ntitle: A Volunteer With Hands\npool: workshop_routine\ntone: light\nart: gear\ntags: social magic\ncast:\n  maker: mir\nscene: An artificer in soot-streaked goggles holding out a strange brass contraption, a fire bucket waiting hopefully on the floor.\ntext: {\"Perfect timing! I need a volunteer and you have hands!\" {maker} holds up {a pair of boots that walk on their own|a hat that remembers where you left things|goggles that see round corners}.|{maker} has a new invention and a familiar look. Today: {boots that walk on their own|a hat with a memory|goggles for corners}.|You don't ask anymore. You just look at what {maker} is holding, then at the fire bucket.}\n> left: Try it on\n  chance likely:\n    alchemy +small\n    rel maker +tiny\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {It works! For eleven glorious minutes, it works, and {maker} writes down every one of them.|It does exactly what it should. {maker} looks almost suspicious and walks round it three times.|It works. You still don't know what it was for, and {maker} is too delighted to tell you.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    rel maker +small\n    say: {It works in the wrong direction. You spend a while upside down. {maker} apologises at length, with diagrams.|There's a bang, a smell of singed eyebrow, and {maker} saying \"Interesting!\" in a voice that means sorry.|It goes off with a pop and a smell of hot brass. {maker} writes \"promising\" and hands you the bucket.}\n> right: Hold the fire bucket\n  rel maker +tiny\n  say: {You hold the bucket. You don't need the bucket. {maker} looks faintly disappointed.|You need the bucket. Briefly, splendidly, you need the bucket.|You hold the bucket. You need it twice, which {maker} calls an improvement.}\n\n@event workshop_stirring_cauldron\ntitle: The Self-Stirring Cauldron\npool: workshop_routine\ntone: light\nart: cauldron\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A small cauldron in a workshop corner stirring itself with a wooden spoon, a stern paper note pinned to its rim.\ntext: {The self-stirring cauldron in the corner wears a note. DO NOT ADJUST. IT KNOWS WHAT IT DID.|Someone has underlined the cauldron's note twice and added the word STILL.|The cauldron's spoon pauses when you come in, as if it remembers you.} {It's going anticlockwise, which it isn't supposed to.|It smells of toffee and lightning.|{season == \"spring\": Someone has put flowers in it. It is stirring them.|It's stirring soup for nobody.}}\n> left: Adjust the enchantment\n  challenge enchant apprentice+:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {It settles into a slow, contented clockwise. Mir, passing, says \"Oh!\" and then \"Huh,\" and then \"Teach me.\"|The spoon taps the rim twice, like a thank-you, and gets on with it.|You adjust it, gently, the way it seems to want. It stirs the soup for nobody a little more cheerfully.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {It stirs your sleeve, then you, then your sleeve again.|It speeds up instead. The ceiling now has toffee on it, and so do you.|It recognises you, and holds a grudge. The spoon flicks soup at you until you back away.}\n> right: Take notes from a safe distance\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {You write down everything it's doing wrong. The list is long and slightly envious.|You sketch the rune on its base. The cauldron splashes your page, on purpose you think.|You take notes from the doorway. The spoon pauses, as if waiting for you to finish the sentence.}\n\n@event workshop_glass_cabinet\ntitle: The Glass Cabinet\nplace: workshop\nwhen: not enrolled(artifice) and not passed(artifice, 1)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 6\nart: gear\ntags: study magic\nscene: A tall glass cabinet at the back of a workshop, clockwork animals inside it, a faded card propped against the glass.\ntext: {At the back of the workshop is a glass cabinet that isn't Mir's, full of faculty machines, very still.|A clockwork mouse in the glass cabinet has moved since your last visit, and now faces the door.|The cabinet. Mir says the machines in it think, probably, and are waiting for the right student.} A card in old ink: \"Artifice. By invitation, to those who have passed Alchemy II and Evocation II.\"\n> left: Study the machines through the glass\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {Nothing moves while you watch. The moment you look away, something ticks.|You count the gears in a brass beetle and lose count. Somebody made this who could make things and make them burn.|The machines don't move, but you're sure they're thinking. One of them, very slightly, nods.}\n> right: Try the cabinet lock\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive journeyman-:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {The lock gives. The mouse winds itself up, bows, and goes back to sleep. You close the door very quietly.|The door swings open an inch, and every machine inside turns its head to look at you. You close it again, politely.|The lock opens at a touch, as if it had been waiting. The machines look at you, and then, satisfied, at each other.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {The cabinet shrieks like a kettle. Mir is delighted. The corridor is not.|The lock bites your pick and keeps it. Mir says it does that, probably, to people it likes.|The lock doesn't bite this time. It simply refuses, the way a cat refuses, and Mir says that's progress.}\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger Office ----------------\n\n@pool ledger_office_routine\nplace: ledger_office\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event ledger_office_unprintable\ntitle: Letters We Will Not Print\npool: ledger_office_routine\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: life\nscene: A wicker basket overflowing with letters by the door of a cramped newspaper office, front pages papering the walls.\ntext: {By the Ledger's door sits a basket marked LETTERS WE WILL NOT PRINT. Visitors may read them.|The unprintable basket has overflowed into a second basket, which is also full.|You are a connoisseur of the unprintable basket now.} {On top is a complaint about the soup, in verse.|One is just a drawing of a prefect, unflattering and accurate.|{season == \"winter\": Someone demands that snow be cancelled.|Someone asks who keeps moving the stairs.}}\n> left: Read the whole basket\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {The best one is an apology to a goat.|One is from a first-year who misses home. You put it back gently.|{final_year and flag(ledger_letter_sent): A letter in your own first-year hand. You don't remember sending it. You were right.|Three letters about the same broken window, all blaming different people.}}\n> right: Write one of your own\n  flag ledger_letter_sent\n  chance unlikely:\n    renown +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {They print it. Two people quote it at you at breakfast; one of them disagrees with it loudly.|It runs on the back page, under your initials. You feel oddly famous for a day.|They print it, with a footnote from the editor disagreeing. It's the most-read thing in the issue.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {It goes in the basket. By next week, someone is reading it aloud.|It's rejected with a note: \"Too sensible.\" You aren't sure that's a criticism.|It goes in the basket. You find it there next week, with someone else's reply pinned to it.}\n\n@event ledger_office_deadline_night\ntitle: Deadline Night\npool: ledger_office_routine\ntone: warm\nart: paper\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)\nscene: A cramped office lit by one lamp, a groaning printing press, two students folding newspapers into tall stacks.\ntext: {The press is groaning like a dragon on deadline, and {here} eyes you like a captain eyes a spare oar.|{here} hands you a stack before you've said hello, and a rag for your fingers.|You take a stack and start folding. {here} doesn't look up, which is a compliment.} {The ink on the front page is still wet.|A headline has a spelling mistake nobody will admit to.|{final_year and flag(ledger_folded): You've folded this paper for years now.|The press shudders as if it's thinking of stopping.}}\n> left: Fold until the press stops\n  flag ledger_folded\n  rel here +small\n  health -small\n  say: {cast_as(here, nessa): {\"Good. Fast. Again,\" says Nessa. From her, that's a sonnet.|Nessa folds twice as fast as you and reads every page as she goes.|Nessa doesn't say anything. She just slides the next stack to you, which is the highest honour on deadline night.}|{By dawn your fingers are grey with ink, and you know tomorrow's news before anyone.|You fold until the stacks are taller than you. Then you fold the ones that fell over.|You fold in rhythm with the press. When it stops, you keep going for a moment, out of habit.}}\n> right: Make your excuses\n  mood +small\n  rel here -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {cast_as(here, nessa): {\"Deserter,\" Nessa calls. \"Noted. Quoted. Forgiven by Thursday.\"|Nessa doesn't look up. \"Coward,\" she says, almost fondly, and writes something down.|Nessa looks up for once. \"Fine. Go. You owe me a Thursday.\" You do, and she collects.}|{You slip away before the second stack. The press groans after you, reproachfully.|You plead an essay. Nobody believes you. Tomorrow's paper mentions a certain absent volunteer.|You leave, and the press shudders to a stop as you go. Somebody blames you, and it goes in the paper.}}\n\n@event ledger_office_drying_line\ntitle: The Drying Line\npool: ledger_office_routine\ntone: tense\nart: paper\ntags: life\nscene: Freshly printed pages pegged on a line across a cramped office to dry, one page hanging slightly crooked.\ntext: {Tomorrow's gossip column is pegged on a line to dry, and nobody is watching it.|The proofs hang on the line, and the peg on the gossip column is a new red one.|The drying line. You know which peg holds the gossip column.} {One item names your house.|One item begins \"Which second-year was seen...\"|{final_year: One item is about the graduating year. Possibly you.|A whole paragraph is about a broken window.}}\n> left: Read ahead\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You read it all and hang it back exactly as crooked as it was.|You know tomorrow's scandal tonight. It is much smaller than it will look in print.|You read the item under the red peg. It's about you, and it's true, and it's very short. You leave it exactly as it was.}\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {Caught. The next column has an item beginning \"Which nosy visitor...\"|A peg snaps. The page lands face-down in the ink tray, and everyone turns round.|You lift the red peg and the whole line comes down. Tomorrow's column has a new item, and it's about you.}\n> right: Leave the line alone\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You spend the night wondering. That's the price of manners.|You find out with everyone else, at breakfast. It was about you anyway.|You leave it. You know which peg it is, and that's almost the same as knowing.}\n\n# ---------------- The Headmistress's Tower ----------------\n\n@pool headmistress_tower_routine\nplace: headmistress_tower\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event headmistress_tower_stone_owl\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Stone Owl\npool: headmistress_tower_routine\ntone: tense\nart: owl\ntags: magic\nscene: A stone owl on the newel post of a spiral stair, one eye open, a student standing very still before it.\ntext: {The stone owl on the stair opens one eye. \"A question first,\" it says, in a voice like a door closing.|The owl's other eye is half open tonight. It has been waiting all day for someone to ask.|The owl opens both eyes for you now, which may or may not be an honour.} {Today's question is about the founders.|Today's question is a riddle with no right answer.|{season == \"winter\": There is snow on its head, and it is not amused.|A moth has landed on its beak. It is pretending not to notice.}}\n> left: Answer the question\n  challenge research journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      rel isolde +tiny\n      say: {The owl closes its eyes and the stair is yours. Upstairs, someone laughs, short and pleased.|\"Adequate,\" says the owl, which from a stone owl is a standing ovation.|You answer before it finishes asking. The owl looks at you with both eyes for a long time, then closes them.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {\"No,\" says the owl, and closes both eyes. You climb anyway, feeling marked down.|The owl repeats your answer back to you, slowly. It sounds worse.|Your answer is right and the owl says \"No\" anyway, to see what you'll do. You climb on. It seems satisfied.}\n> right: Climb past without a word\n  guile +small\n  rel isolde -tiny\n  say: {The owl says nothing. At the top, the door is shut. You have the strong feeling you've been noted.|You get past. The owl's eye follows you all the way up, and all the way down again.|You climb past and nod to it, as you'd nod to a porter. It nods back, very slightly.}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_tea\ntitle: Two Cups Poured\npool: headmistress_tower_routine\ntone: warm\nart: cup\ntags: social\ncast:\n  host: isolde\nscene: A tower study full of books, a tall pale woman pouring two cups of tea beside a board of crystal towers by the window.\ntext: {The headmistress pours two cups unasked. \"Sit. Tell me how your term is going, and then how it is really going.\"|Two cups, already poured. \"Well,\" says {host}, \"how is it really going?\"|You sit before she asks. She looks pleased, and doesn't say so.} {The crystal towers on her board have moved.|Rain ticks on the high windows.|{final_year: \"Your last year,\" she says. \"I remember your first.\"|Footnote, the library cat, sleeps on her papers.}}\n> left: Tell her the truth\n  rel host +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {She listens without interrupting, which is worse than any lecture and better than most comfort.|She hears all of it and says only, \"Yes. I thought so.\" Somehow that helps, later.|You tell her before she asks. She hears it all, then tells you something true about her own first years. It doesn't help, and then it does.}\n> right: Give her the polished version\n  challenge deceive vs isolde.guile -2:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {She laughs, short and delighted, and lets you get away with it. You aren't sure that's a good sign.|\"Very good,\" she says. \"Next time, the true one.\" She means both halves.|She lets the polished version stand and pours more tea. \"You've got better at it,\" she says. You can't tell if that's praise.}\n    lose:\n      rel host -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {\"Mm,\" she says, and pours you more tea, and waits. You end up telling her anyway.|She lets the silence go on until it says everything you didn't.|She hears the polished version, then repeats the one sentence of it that was true. You talk for an hour after that.}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_window_seat\ntitle: The Window Seat\npool: headmistress_tower_routine\ntone: warm\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A deep window seat high in a tower, a student reading with the lake far below, a headmistress writing letters at a desk behind.\ntext: {The headmistress waves you to the window seat without looking up from her letters. \"Read anything. Break nothing.\"|A book has been left on the window seat, open, as if for you.|Your window seat, the headmistress calls it now, as though it always was.} {Below, the lake is perfectly still.|Her quill scratches; the clock does not tick.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves blow past at the height of the clouds.|The bridges of light come on one by one below.}}\n> left: Read what she left out\n  lore +small\n  say: {A history of the seven towers, annotated in her hand. One note just says \"No.\"|A book of old law, with a dried rose marking a page about second chances.|A treatise on wards so dull it must be important. You get through it. She notices.}\n> right: Doze on the window seat\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  rel isolde -tiny\n  say: {You wake to find a blanket over you and no comment at all, which is its own kind of comment.|You sleep through most of the visit. When you wake, she is still writing, and gives you a look over her spectacles that says she had hoped you would read.|You doze in your window seat. You wake to find she's moved her desk lamp so it doesn't shine in your eyes.}\n\n# ---------------- The Restricted Section ----------------\n\n@pool restricted_section_routine\nplace: restricted_section\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event restricted_section_breathing_shelves\ntitle: The Breathing Shelves\npool: restricted_section_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: book\ntags: study mystery\nscene: Black-bound books on tall shelves in the dark, a single candle on a reading table, the spines faintly rising and falling.\ntext: {The black-bound books breathe in the dark, slow as sleepers. You find a chair and light a candle.|You've learned to breathe with the shelves; it helps.|Your chair is where you left it. The books shift, as if making room.} {One spine is warm to the touch.|Somewhere a page turns by itself.|{season == \"winter\": Your breath fogs. Theirs doesn't.|The candle burns low and blue.}}\n> left: Read until the candle gutters\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You read about wards that were old when the towers were new. Afterwards you can't sleep, and don't quite want to.|You read a whole chapter before you notice the book has been reading along, a line ahead.|You read in your chair by a low blue candle. The shelves breathe with you, and the chapter goes in like a held breath.}\n> right: Read only the spines\n  lore +tiny\n  mana -tiny\n  say: {Even the titles take something out of you. One of them is just your house's name.|You walk the shelves by candlelight. The spines lean towards you, and then politely away.|You walk the spines, and they make room for you as you pass. One spine you haven't seen before has no title at all.}\n\n@event restricted_section_lantern\ntitle: Holding the Lantern\npool: restricted_section_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: lantern\ntags: social mystery\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student)\nscene: Two students in a dark library aisle, one holding a lantern high, the other reading a black book, shadows stretching behind them.\ntext: {{friend} came with you, for courage, and is holding the lantern in both hands.|You bring a second candle this time; {friend} takes the lantern without being asked.|You know which aisles creak now. {friend} holds the lantern where you point.} {\"Did that book just sigh?\" {they} whisper{s}.|A shelf creaks behind you both.|{final_year: \"I'll miss this,\" {they} say{s}, not sounding sure.|The lantern makes the shadows walk.}}\n> left: Read while they keep watch\n  lore +small\n  rel friend -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You read; {friend} keeps watch and keeps count of every noise, out loud.|You lose an hour in a book. {friend} loses an hour staring at a shelf that keeps staring back.|You read; {friend} holds the light where you point, and the shadows walk where {they} hold{s} it. Neither of you says much on the way back.}\n> right: Take the lantern for a while\n  rel friend +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You hold the light so {friend} can read. Something in the dark rustles the whole time, and you don't let it show.|{friend} reads aloud in a whisper. The books seem to lean in to listen.|You take the lantern and {friend} reads to you, a page you'll both remember. The shadows stop walking to listen.}\n\n@event restricted_section_chained_book\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Chained Book\npool: restricted_section_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: chain\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A heavy book chained to a library shelf, lying open on its own under a dim candle, the chain hanging slack.\ntext: {One book here is chained to its shelf, and tonight it has opened itself to a page.|The chained book lies open at a different page, and the chain is a link longer.|The chained book has been expecting you. That's the worst part.} {The page is in a script you almost know.|The page shows a lock with no keyhole.|The page is blank until you look away.}\n> left: Read the open page\n  challenge research adept-:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      mana +small\n      say: A thread of stored power runs up your arm from the page. {The words make sense for exactly as long as you're reading them. Afterwards you only remember that they did.|You read it, and the book turns its own page, satisfied, as if you'd passed something.|The page stays blank while you look and fills when you blink. You read it in blinks. It takes all night.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The words read you back. For a day afterwards you keep feeling underlined.|The candle goes out mid-line. In the dark, the page keeps going.|You look away and the page fills; you look back and it's blank, and smug. Your eyes ache for a day.}\n> right: Close it and step back\n  mood -tiny\n  mana +tiny\n  say: As the cover shuts, something that had been quietly drawing on your magic lets go. {The chain rattles as the cover shuts, like a sigh. You stand with your back to it until your hands stop shaking.|You close it gently, the way you'd close a door on someone sleeping.|You close it before it can finish expecting you. The chain lies still, a link shorter.}\n\n@event restricted_section_handleless_door\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Door Without a Handle\nplace: restricted_section\nwhen: not enrolled(hollow_studies) and not passed(hollow_studies, 1)\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 8\nweight: 6\nart: door\ntags: study mystery\nscene: A plain door with no handle at the back of a dark library, chalk writing on it that has not smudged in years.\ntext: {At the very back is a door with no handle and no window. On it, in chalk that never smudges, HOLLOW STUDIES, BY LEAVE OF THE HEADMISTRESS.|Tonight the door without a handle is warm, like the outer door.|You've read its chalk so often you could recite it.} Underneath, smaller: \"Umbral Theory II. Astromancy II. And know what the founders made, and why.\"\n> left: Knock\n  rel isolde +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {Nobody answers. Then, very far away, a pleasant voice says, \"Not yet.\"|Your knock doesn't echo. It just stops, as if something on the other side caught it. Next day the headmistress nods to you in the corridor, as if she heard.|You knock, as you always do. This time, from far away, someone knocks back, once, as if to say they've noted it.}\n> right: Copy the chalk into your notes\n  lore +small\n  mana -small\n  say: Copying the chalk draws on your magic like a cold draught. {The chalk makes your pencil cold. Two courses and a question, and the question is the hard part.|You copy it word for word. Back in your room, your notes have the same words, and one more underline than you made.|You copy it from memory and check it against the door. The chalk agrees with you, which feels like being agreed with.}\n\n# ---------------- The Third Gargoyle ----------------\n\n@pool gargoyle_passage_routine\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_old_marks\ntitle: Marks on the Wall\npool: gargoyle_passage_routine\ntone: warm\nart: map\ntags: life mystery\nscene: A narrow stone passage behind a gargoyle, its walls covered in scratched initials, dates and tiny arrows, lit by a stub of candle.\ntext: {The passage walls are covered in initials, dates and tiny arrows, scratched by generations who were never here.|Among the marks on the wall is a new one, still sharp.|You know these walls now: the arrows that lie, the dates that can't be right, the initials that keep coming back.} {The wind sings through the gargoyle's teeth.|Below, a door closes that shouldn't be there.|{season == \"winter\": Frost picks out every letter.|Moths sleep in the deeper scratches.}}\n> left: Add your own mark\n  renown +tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You scratch your initials small and low, with a tiny arrow pointing nowhere. It feels like signing something.|{final_year and flag(left_wall_mark): You add the year beside your old mark. It looks so much younger than you.|A knuckle pays for it. The mark is crooked and entirely yours.}|You add your mark beside the initials that keep coming back. Next time, there's a tiny arrow pointing to yours.}\n  flag left_wall_mark\n> right: Follow an old arrow\n  lore +small\n  say: {The arrow leads to another arrow, which leads to a wall, which has a joke scratched on it.|The arrow leads to a slit window with a view of the lake no plan admits to. Your knees disagree with the route.|You follow the arrow you know lies, to see how it lies. It lies beautifully, all the way to a view of the stars.}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_offering_dish\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Gargoyle's Dish\npool: gargoyle_passage_routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: magic life\nscene: A weathered gargoyle on a moonlit parapet with a dish worn into its stone paw, holding a button, a biscuit and a bent key.\ntext: {The third gargoyle has a dish worn into its paw, and in the dish are offerings, a button, a biscuit, a bent key.|Someone has left the gargoyle a single grape, peeled.|The gargoyle looks at you the way a landlord looks at the first of the month.} {Tonight it looks hungry.|Tonight it looks smug.|{season == \"summer\": The stone is still warm from the day.|Rain runs out of its mouth in a steady complaint.}}\n> left: Leave a few crowns in the dish\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {By morning the crowns are gone, and so is the biscuit. Nobody admits to either.|The crowns clink in. The gargoyle's expression doesn't change, but the wind drops.|You pay the gargoyle its crowns, like rent. The night is calm, and the stair doesn't move once on your way back.}\n> right: Borrow the biscuit\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance even:\n    mood +small\n    health +tiny\n    say: {It's a very good biscuit. Somebody's mother made it. You feel a little bad, and then less.|Shortbread, still crisp. The gargoyle allows it, this once.|You borrow the peeled grape. The gargoyle's look follows you down the stair, but the grape is excellent.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: {It's made of stone. The gargoyle's joke, and it's been waiting a long time to tell it.|The wind gets up the moment you take it, and stays up all night, rattling your window.|You take the grape, and the gargoyle collects. Your boots are full of rainwater by the bottom of the stair.}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_parapet_cards\ntitle: Cards on the Parapet\npool: gargoyle_passage_routine\nslot: midnight\ntone: light\nart: cards\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)\nscene: Two students playing cards by lantern-light on a high castle parapet, a pot of buttons between them and the lake far below.\ntext: {On the parapet, by lantern-light, {here} is dealing cards for buttons, and crowns for anyone who dares.|{here} has a new deck, and the pot of buttons has grown.|{here} deals you in before you've sat down.} {The wind keeps trying to take the jack.|A pigeon watches the game with too much interest.|{final_year: \"One more for the road,\" {they} say{s}.|The lake below holds the lantern perfectly still.}}\n> left: Play a hand\n  when: gold >= 8\n  challenge deceive apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      gold +tiny\n      mood +small\n      say: {cast_as(here, corvin): {\"Well, look at you,\" Corvin says, paying out. \"Who taught you that? Don't say me.\"|\"Remind me never to owe you money,\" Corvin tells the pigeon, and pays you in buttons and, grudgingly, crowns.|Corvin deals you in, loses, and laughs. \"You're getting good. I'll have to start cheating properly.\"}|{You win the pot, buttons and a few actual crowns.|You bluff with nothing and the wind turns your card over at the right moment. You take the buttons, and the crowns under them, with dignity.|You're dealt in before you sit and win before you've warmed up. The pot is mostly buttons, and some of it is crowns.}}\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {cast_as(here, corvin): {Corvin scoops the pot and winces. \"Saint, you're the worst liar I love playing.\" He gives you your buttons back, at least.|\"Never bluff a man who owes three people money,\" Corvin says, kindly.|Corvin deals from the bottom and you know it and can't prove it. You lose with a smile, which annoys him.}|{You lose your buttons, then some crowns, then the will to bluff.|The wind takes your best card over the parapet. It was the only one you had.|You lose the hand to a card the pigeon was standing on. The pigeon will not be questioned.}}\n> right: Watch the lake instead\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  rel here +tiny\n  say: {cast_as(here, corvin): {\"Saint,\" Corvin says, not looking up, \"you'll never be rich.\" He passes you his scarf.|Corvin deals you out without a word and tosses you his scarf when the wind picks up.|Corvin deals you out without asking and hands you his scarf before the wind does. \"Lake's nice tonight,\" he says. It is.}|{You sit with your back to the stone and watch the dark water while the cards slap behind you. It's cold, and peaceful.|You count the lights in the village until the game's over.|You watch the lake hold the lantern still. Behind you, someone wins, and someone else blames the pigeon.}}\n\n# ---------------- The Seventh Tower ----------------\n\n@event seventh_tower_stair_count\ntitle: The Stair That Changes\npool: seventh_tower_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: tower\ntags: mystery\nscene: A narrow spiral stair inside a dusty stone tower, a single candle climbing it, moonlight falling through slit windows.\ntext: {The seventh tower's stair never has the same number of steps twice. Scholars count it to learn how the castle bends; everyone else says it lets you up faster if you don't look.|Back on the stair that won't keep its count. You've started chalking each total on the wall; no two agree.|You've stopped expecting the stair to agree with you. You count anyway; it's a kind of manners.} {Dust lies thick on every step but yours.|The moon follows you from window to window.|{season == \"winter\": The cold comes up through your shoes.|Your candle leans towards the top, like a dog on a lead.}}\n> left: Count every step\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You lose count twice and get two answers, both certain, both wrong.|Up, one number; down, one more. You don't stay to find the extra step.|{final_year and flag(counted_seventh_stair): The count matches your very first climb. You can't say why that frightens you.|You count a step that isn't there, and your foot believes you.}}\n  flag counted_seventh_stair\n> right: Climb without counting\n  mana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You climb by feel, in the dark between windows. The stair seems to like that better.|You stop counting and the climb is suddenly short, as if the tower was in a hurry too.|You climb without counting, candle leaning ahead. You're at the top before you've thought about it, and it feels allowed.}\n\n@event seventh_tower_dust_room\ntime: evening\ntitle: Dust and Moonlight\npool: seventh_tower_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: moon\ntags: mystery\nscene: A bare round room at the top of a tower, thick dust on the floor marked by footprints, moonlight slanting through one window onto a star-shaped hollow in the wall.\ntext: {At the top is one room of dust and moonlight, with a star-shaped hollow in the wall. The footprints in the dust aren't yours.|More footprints in the dust; some are yours now.|The room keeps your footprints like a guest book.} {The moon sits in the window as if it lives here.|The hollow in the wall is darker than the night.|A moth beats at the glass from outside, trying to get in.}\n> left: Sit in the moonlight\n  mana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {The light is cold and kind. You sit until your shadow forgets which way to fall.|You sit where the moon is brightest. It feels like being looked at by someone very old and very tired.|You sit with the moth tapping at the glass and the moon in the window. Nothing happens, very deeply, for an hour.}\n> right: Sweep away your footprints\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You sweep your way out backwards with your sleeve. The dust settles behind you, and then, you'd swear, rearranges itself.|You cough your way to the door. The other footprints you leave alone. They seem to want to stay.|You sweep your prints away and leave the others. By the next visit, yours are back, in the same places.}\n\n@event seventh_tower_lake_window\ntitle: The Window Over the Lake\npool: seventh_tower_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: wave\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A small arched window high in a tower, its latch half open, the Mirror Lake lying still and silver far below.\ntext: {The tower's one window looks down on the Mirror Lake, and the latch isn't locked. They say if you open it, you can hear the lake.|The latch moves more easily than last time, as if someone else has been practising.|The window. You've stood here often enough that your hand finds the latch before you decide anything.} {The lake is so still it looks painted.|There's a light on the water that isn't the moon's.|{season == \"winter\": The lake is grey and hard.|Mist lies on the water.}}\n> left: Open it and listen\n  challenge research using arcana journeyman- wild:\n    win:\n      mana +small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: {You hear water, and under it a sound too low to be wind. You decide it was the pipes, and don't quite believe yourself.|Nothing but wind and water. You stay until you're sure, and you're never quite sure.|You open it without deciding to. The lake is quiet, and then not, and then quiet again, as if it changed its mind about you.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The cold comes in and won't leave. For days you hear dripping in dry rooms.|The lake is silent, entirely, the way a room goes silent when you walk in on a conversation.|The window opens on its own before your hand gets there. You shut it fast, and the latch is cold for a week.}\n> right: Leave it latched\n  mood -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  say: {You leave the latch alone and go down to bed, and lie awake wondering what you didn't hear.|You put your palm flat on the cold glass instead. The lake does nothing. It's very good at that.|You leave it latched and rest your hand on the latch a while. It feels like keeping a promise to someone.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/clubs_jobs.grim","text":"# Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): club practice and job shifts.\n# Clubs: duelling, lumen_ledger, verdant, cartographers. Jobs: library_assistant, tavern, apothecary_clerk, broom_courier.\n# Each track: three repeatable routines (two at rank 1, one at rank 2) and one rank-2 milestone.\n\n# ---------------- Duelling Circle (track: duelling) ----------------\n\n@event duelling_ward_line\ntitle: The Ward Line\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: shield\ntags: club duel\nscene: A row of students holding shimmering shields across a scorched hall while seniors lob sparks at them.\ntext: {Ward drill. Members hold shields in a line while the seniors lob stinging hexes down the hall.|You know by now which senior aims low, and which one apologises.|You could hold a shield in your sleep, and one night you did.} {A dummy at the end of the line has a shield of its own, out of pity.|Someone has chalked a tally of scorched floorboards. It is long.|A first-year holds the shield upside down and does surprisingly well.}\n> left: Take the front of the line\n  challenge spell apprentice+:\n    win:\n      perform duelling +small\n      vigor +small\n      say: {Your shield takes everything and hums about it afterwards.|Hexes splash off your ward like rain off a good hat.|You hold until the bell. {around(lyra): Lyra|The senior on the whistle} writes something down, which is either praise or a list of your faults.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      perform duelling +tiny\n      say: {A hex finds the gap by your elbow. Your arm fizzes until supper.|Your ward folds like wet paper. The senior apologises, mostly to the floor.|You hold the front half asleep, as usual, and a hex wakes you. Your arm fizzes until supper, and the first-year beside you holds.}\n> right: Hold the back row tonight\n  arcana +small\n  perform duelling -tiny\n  say: {From the back you watch how the good shields bend instead of break. Nobody watches you.|You spend the drill studying the front row's mistakes, which feels useful and looks like hiding.|Safe at the back, you learn a great deal about the ceiling.}\n> up[ward]: Hold the line with a real ward\n  perform duelling +small\n  arcana +small\n  say: {You raise a proper ward, the one from your first week, and the seniors' hexes slide off it like rain off glass. {around(lyra): Lyra stops the drill to look.|The seniors stop the drill to look.}|Your ward holds the whole line's flank. Your arm aches from the mana, not the hexes.|You raise the real ward without thinking, the way you hold a shield in your sleep. The seniors give up hexing your end of the line.}\n\n@event duelling_dummy_mending\ntitle: Mending the Dummies\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: duel\ntags: club\nscene: A student alone in a duelling hall at night, stitching straw back into a battered practice dummy.\ntext: {After practice someone has to mend the dummies, and tonight the rota says it's you.|Mending night again, and the rota has your name on it.|You know every dummy here by its scars. They look at you with something like trust.} {The tall dummy is missing an arm. Someone always takes the arm.|One dummy is now mostly scorch mark.|Someone has painted a new name on a dummy's chest, and a grudge beneath it.}\n> left: Stay late and stitch them up\n  mood -small\n  perform duelling +small\n  rank_xp duelling +tiny\n  say: {By midnight every dummy is whole and you have straw in places straw should not be.|You sew one dummy's arm on slightly backwards. It looks more dangerous for it.|The hall is quiet, the needle is steady, and for an hour nobody is shouting about footwork.}\n> right: Leave them for the morning\n  mood +tiny\n  perform duelling -tiny\n  say: {In the morning the dummies are still in pieces, and so is the seniors' patience.|You slip out early. Someone finds the dummies and puts your name on next week's rota, underlined twice.|You leave them. In the morning the dummy with the new name has been mended by someone else, with your name painted under the grudge.}\n\n@event duelling_refereeing_lyra\ntitle: Refereeing Lyra\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nactor: lyra\nwhen: around(lyra)\nart: duel\ntags: club duel\nscene: A student with a whistle watching a noble duellist in a chalk circle, her opponent's shield just failing.\ntext: {Officers referee, so tonight you hold the whistle while Lyra duels a second-year.|You know the circle's chalk by heart now, and Lyra's footwork better.|{rel(lyra) >= 12: Lyra no longer asks who is refereeing. She looks for you.|The whistle has a dent in it from some earlier, unrecorded argument.}} {Her last spell landed a breath after the bell.|She stepped out of the circle by a boot's width, and knows it.|Her last hex went in after her opponent had called the yield.} {final_year: One of the last bouts you will call for her.|}\n> left: Call it against her\n  rel lyra -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  perform duelling +small\n  rank_xp duelling +small\n  say: {\"Noted,\" Lyra says, in a voice that could chill wine. Next day her feet are perfect.|Lyra stares at you, then shakes her opponent's hand. Later a cup of tea appears on your bench.|The hall goes silent. Lyra bows to the second-year, very correctly, and to you, slightly less.}\n  if knows(lyra.rival):\n    rel lyra +tiny\n    say: She would never have forgiven you for letting it stand, and you both know why: you're the one person here who makes her better.\n> right: Let it stand\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  perform duelling -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Lyra wins. The second-year looks at you, and you find something interesting about your whistle.|Nobody objects. Lyra does not thank you; if anything, she looks a little disappointed in you.|The bout goes in the book as hers. It sits there all week like a stone in your shoe.}\n\n@event duelling_running_practice\ntitle: Running Practice\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: duel\ntags: club duel\nscene: A note pinned to a duelling hall door, a crowd of students waiting inside in chalk circles.\ntext: A note is pinned to the hall door in Lyra's sharp hand: \"Called home. You are running practice. Footwork first. Do not let anyone set the ceiling on fire again.\" The whole Circle turns to see what you will say.\n> left: Run it exactly her way\n  perform duelling +small\n  rank_xp duelling +medium\n  rel lyra +small\n  say: Footwork, then shields, then footwork again. Nobody sets the ceiling on fire. Lyra reads your practice log on her return and says \"adequate\", then reads it again when she thinks you've gone.\n> right: Run it your own way\n  rank_xp duelling +medium\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You turn the footwork drill into a game of tag with stinging hexes. The ceiling only smoulders. A week later Lyra runs the same game and calls it her idea, which is the highest praise she has.\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger (track: lumen_ledger) ----------------\n\n@event lumen_ledger_letters_box\ntitle: The Letters Box\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: club\nscene: A battered wooden box under a library staircase, overflowing with folded letters and one sock.\ntext: {The letters box is full. Stringers sort it, Nessa says, having no dignity yet to lose.|Sorting day. A regular correspondent has started numbering their letters.|Under the stairs the box has burst its lid. You know the regulars by their handwriting now.} {Porridge complaints, a poem about the lake and a sock.|A furious letter about the crossword, in a professor's hand.|An anonymous tip in lemon juice, noticed only because it smells nice.}\n> left: Print the angriest letter\n  perform lumen_ledger +small\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {It runs on page four. By breakfast several people are angry about the angry letter, which Nessa calls \"engagement\".|The letter runs. Its author glares at you over the porridge, as if you had written it.|You print the lemon-juice tip, warmed over a candle. Half the castle holds the page to the fire. Nessa calls it the best-selling issue of term.}\n> right: Answer them all by hand\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  perform lumen_ledger +tiny\n  say: {You write back to every one of them, even the ones that weren't really letters. Your hand cramps; somewhere in the castle a lonely first-year gets a reply.|A pile of replies, all polite. Nessa calls it \"a waste of a perfectly good grudge\", then borrows your phrasing.|You write back to every regular, by number. The numbering correspondent replies with a number of their own, and a thank-you.}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_courtyard_quotes\ntitle: A Word from the Courtyard\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: club social\nscene: A student with a notebook stopping passers-by in a busy courtyard.\ntext: {Every issue needs a word from the courtyard. One question, many opinions.|Word has got round the courtyard that the Ledger is asking questions.|Half the courtyard now takes the long way round your notebook.} This week: {is the soup getting thinner?|who will win the Spring Tournament?|is the lake louder lately?} {season == \"winter\": Everyone is hurrying indoors.|{Some second-years pretend not to see you.|A passing professor looks very quotable.|A prefect makes a detour to avoid you, which is a quote of a sort.}}\n> left: Stop strangers and ask\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      perform lumen_ledger +small\n      charm +small\n      say: {A gardener, a prefect and a very small first-year give you the best quotes of the term. The first-year's is unprintable, and perfect.|You get a quote so good Nessa moves it to the front page and pretends it was her idea to ask.|You ask the question to the people who take the long way round. They answer, to get rid of you, and the answers are the best yet.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform lumen_ledger +tiny\n      say: {Everyone is busy, cold or suddenly deaf. You come back with \"no comment\" in five different tones of voice.|A fourth-year answers at length, entirely about himself.|Everyone takes the long way round you. You get one quote, from the prefect, and it is \"No.\"}\n> right: Just quote your friends\n  mood +tiny\n  perform lumen_ledger -tiny\n  say: {Every opinion comes from someone you had lunch with. Nessa reads them and says, \"Interesting. Your friends are very consistent.\"|It's quick. It's also the courtyard as represented by one person in several scarves.|You quote your friends again. Nessa runs it under a headline about the same four people, and it's somehow popular.}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_red_ink\ntitle: Red Ink\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nactor: nessa\nart: scroll\ntags: club\nscene: A cramped office papered with old front pages, an editor slashing a manuscript with a red quill.\ntext: {Nessa edits your story with a red quill and no mercy.|Nessa has your draft, and this time the red quill hesitates once before it moves.|Edit night. You have learned to bring two copies, one for Nessa to kill.} \"{Kill your darlings. Bury them. Page six.|Lead's buried. Dig it up. Headline it.|Good. Too long. Shorter. Shorter than that.}\" She has cut your favourite paragraph.\n> left: Fight for the paragraph\n  challenge negotiate vs nessa.charm:\n    win:\n      perform lumen_ledger +small\n      rank_xp lumen_ledger +small\n      say: {Nessa reads it again, out loud, and puts it back. \"Fine. Stays. Don't get used to it.\"|You win the paragraph. Nessa makes you cut a different one, as a tax.|You fight for it, and it gets shorter, and shorter than that, and it stays. Nessa calls it \"a paragraph now\".}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      rel nessa -tiny\n      say: {\"No,\" says Nessa, and that is the whole argument.|She listens to your case with great interest, then cuts the paragraph anyway, plus the one you defended it with.|You fight with your second copy ready. Nessa cuts the paragraph from both.}\n> right: Let her cut it\n  perform lumen_ledger +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {It reads better without it. You will never forgive her.|The story runs, tighter and less yours. Nessa scribbles \"good\" in the margin, a word Nessa hands out like gold.|You let her cut it, and slip it into next week's story instead. She notices, and lets it stand.}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_front_page\ntitle: Front Page\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: paper\ntags: club\nscene: A creaking printing press at dawn spitting out newspapers, a student holding the first copy up to the light.\ntext: The press groans like a dragon and spits out the dawn edition. Your story is on the front page, under your own name. Nessa hands you a tack without looking up. \"Wall,\" she says. \"That's where front pages go.\"\n> left: Pin it to the wall yourself\n  rank_xp lumen_ledger +medium\n  perform lumen_ledger +small\n  rel nessa +small\n  say: You find a gap between a scandal from before you were born and a recipe someone printed by mistake. It looks small up there, and permanent. Nessa pretends not to watch you step back and admire it.\n> right: Send the first copy home\n  rank_xp lumen_ledger +medium\n  mood +medium\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You post it home with a note that says nothing about how long it took. The reply comes within the week: they have framed it, slightly crooked, over the kitchen fire.\n\n# ---------------- Verdant Circle (track: verdant) ----------------\n\n@event verdant_watering_rota\ntitle: The Watering Rota\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: A student with a watering can and a huge chart in a steaming greenhouse, ferns leaning in hungrily.\ntext: {You're on the watering rota, a chart the size of a door.|The ferns have learned your footsteps and lean in.|You no longer need the chart, luckily, since a fern ate it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost outside the glass, drips down your collar inside.|{season == \"summer\": It's so hot the cacti look smug.|{The biting ferns are in a mood.|Something in the far bed grew overnight.|The moonflowers do nothing, beautifully, as they will for years.}}}\n> left: Follow the chart exactly\n  perform verdant +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {Warm rainwater for the ferns, cold tea for the moss, nothing at all for the cactus that bites. It takes all afternoon.|By the chart, to the drop. {around(tobias): Tobias says the plants look \"right pleased\", and they do, in a leafy way.|The plants look pleased, in a leafy way.}|You follow the chart from memory, since the fern ate it. You're right to the drop, and the fern looks unrepentant.}\n> right: Water by feel\n  challenge research using alchemy apprentice:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      perform verdant +small\n      say: {You read the leaves instead of the chart. Everything perks up, and one fern makes a sound suspiciously like purring.|You guess right on every bed. {around(tobias): Tobias asks how you knew. You honestly don't.|You couldn't say how you knew.}|You water by feel and talk to the moonflowers. They still do nothing, beautifully, and the rest of the house thrives.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      perform verdant -tiny\n      say: {You drown a pot of sundew, and a biting fern takes a polite nip of your thumb.|The moss wanted tea, not water. It sulks visibly, and the cactus you reach past bites.|You water the moonflowers by feel and drown them by feel. They do nothing, as usual, but wetly.}\n\n@event verdant_repotting_day\ntitle: Repotting Day\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: flower\ntags: club nature\nscene: Students in earmuffs wrestling squalling mandrakes into bigger pots on a crowded greenhouse bench.\ntext: {Repotting day. The mandrakes have outgrown their pots and have opinions about it.|Someone always takes the good earmuffs, so you bring your own now.|You know which mandrakes kick, and which only pretend to.} {Tobias's own mandrake watches from a shelf in a knitted hat, superior.|A seed tray of something blue has started to hum.|{season == \"spring\": Everything is trying to grow at once.|The glass is fogged and the benches crowded.}}\n> left: Take on the mandrakes\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  perform verdant +small\n  rank_xp verdant +tiny\n  say: {One mandrake has rooted into the bench itself. It takes a trowel, a lullaby and both hands to talk it out.|Mandrake after mandrake, each into a bigger pot. Your ears ring all the way to supper.|The biggest one goes in headfirst and comes out, somehow, the right way up. You are not sure who won.}\n> right: Take the quiet seed trays\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  perform verdant +tiny\n  say: {Hundreds of seedlings, pricked out one by one. Your back will remember this tomorrow.|The seed trays are peaceful and endless. By dusk you have names for most of them.|You do the seed trays with your own earmuffs on, for the peace. One tray hums, and you hum back.}\n\n@event verdant_tobias_cuttings\ntitle: The Cuttings Question\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nactor: tobias\nwhen: around(tobias)\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: A big gentle student cradling a tray of leggy cuttings, eyeing a full compost heap with dread.\ntext: {The benches are full and someone must choose which cuttings stay. Tobias can't. \"It's like choosing between goats,\" he says.|Bench day. Tobias hovers over a tray of cuttings, apologising to them in advance and glancing at you.|Tobias hands you the tray before he can change his mind. He is grateful in a way that looks like grief.} {season == \"autumn\": The frost will take the weakest soon anyway.|}\n> left: Choose, and compost the rest\n  perform verdant +small\n  rank_xp verdant +small\n  rel tobias -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You keep the strongest and compost the rest. Tobias says \"fair's fair\", then goes very quiet over the heap.|The benches breathe again. Tobias thanks you, then tells you a long story about a goat he couldn't sell. You understand it is about the cuttings.|You choose quickly, so Tobias doesn't have to watch. He takes the compost bucket himself, and you let him.}\n> right: Find room for every one\n  rel tobias +small\n  perform verdant -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You find room on windowsills, under benches and in a teacup. Nothing grows well, but everything grows. Tobias laughs from the belly.|\"Ma always said a crowded field still feeds you,\" Tobias says, beaming. The benches groan. So does Old Maud, when she sees.|You find room again, on the last windowsill nobody had thought of. Tobias names that one after you.}\n\n@event verdant_greenhouse_keys\ntitle: The Greenhouse Keys\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nactor: tobias\nwhen: around(tobias)\nart: key\ntags: club nature\nscene: A heavy ring of old iron keys handed over in a steaming glasshouse at dusk.\ntext: At dusk Tobias holds out the iron key ring, warm from his pocket. \"Ma says you give the keys to whoever shuts the gate without being asked,\" he says. \"You always shut the gate.\" From now on, opening up and locking up are yours.\n> left: Open up at dawn\n  rank_xp verdant +medium\n  perform verdant +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: Dawn, the glass silver with dew, every plant turning to the light you let in. You had never noticed they all turn at once. For a moment you are the only person in the world.\n> right: Walk the night round with a lamp\n  rank_xp verdant +medium\n  alchemy +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You walk the benches with a lamp, checking latches and saying goodnight, because Tobias does and it feels rude not to. Something in the far bed says it back. You decide not to mention it.\n\n# ---------------- Midnight Cartographers (track: cartographers) ----------------\n\n@event cartographers_survey_run\ntitle: A Survey Run\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: A student pacing a dark stone passage by candlelight, chalking marks on the wall.\ntext: {Initiates do the pacing. Corvin hands you chalk and a passage to measure before the castle changes its mind.|Your chalk is a stub now, so you mark the turns with notches.|Your stride is a unit of measurement on the master map now, and the castle seems to know it.} {The passage smells of oranges, which nobody can explain.|Behind the wall, a clock ticks that no one has ever found.|Your candle leans, very slightly, toward the left-hand turning.}\n> left: Pace it properly\n  challenge research using guile apprentice:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: {Every turning chalked and counted. The passage is exactly where it was last month, which on the master map counts as a scandal.|The passage is two strides longer from this end than from the other. Corvin is delighted.|You pace it with the stride the map uses. The left turning, where your candle leans, wasn't there last week. Corvin inks it in.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {Your chalk marks lead neatly into a wall that wasn't there when you started.|You lose count somewhere in the dark and have to admit it, which in this club is rarer than a map.|You follow the leaning candle into the left-hand turning and come out a floor below, with no stairs in between.}\n> right: Copy last month's figures\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You copy the old figures and sit in an alcove eating toffee. The castle, as if offended, moves the passage a foot.|The figures match last month's exactly. Corvin looks at them, then at you. \"Forger,\" he says, approvingly.|You copy the figures with your own stride as the unit. The castle, offended, changes your stride.}\n\n@event cartographers_lantern_patrol\ntitle: The Prefect's Lantern\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: club mystery\nscene: Students pressed into a stone alcove as a lantern's glow sweeps along a dark corridor.\ntext: {Every expedition has the moment a lantern comes round the corner. Tonight's has a red sash behind it.|A lantern, a red sash, footsteps. You know this part.|You can tell the prefects on patrol apart by their footsteps now.} {The alcove beside you is just big enough for one.|A loose flagstone ahead clanks if you step on it.|The tapestry here already has feet showing under it, and they aren't yours.}\n> left: Freeze in the shadows\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {The light slides over you like water off a duck. The footsteps go on.|You don't breathe for a very long time. The prefect sneezes, apologises to nobody and walks on.|You freeze beside the tapestry with the feet. The prefect sees the feet, not yours, and someone else spends Saturday scrubbing pans.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The lantern stops on your face. You spend Saturday scrubbing pans and inventing a reason you were inside a wall.|You talk your way out with a story about a lost cat. It costs you the night's mapping and most of your dignity.|The feet under the tapestry turn out to be the prefect's own. You both have some explaining to do.}\n> right: Go back the long way\n  guile +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The long way is four flights of freezing stairs. You learn every one of them by heart.|You double back through the kitchens and arrive late, cold and unseen. Corvin raises a mug of milk he certainly didn't pay for.|You know these footsteps. You take the long way at exactly the prefect's pace, one corridor behind.}\n\n@event cartographers_inking_night\ntitle: Inking Night\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nactor: corvin\nart: map\ntags: club\nscene: Two students bent over a huge map drawn in silver ink that shifts as they watch, in a hidden candlelit room.\ntext: {Mappers ink the master map. Corvin unrolls it, silver lines shifting like breath, and passes you the pen.|Inking night. The map has redrawn a stair since your last visit, out of spite.|The map has started leaving a blank wherever you hesitate.} {\"Leave the one behind the kitchens off,\" he says. \"Not for long. For me.\"|\"The stair under the clock. Don't draw it. Don't ask why.\"|He taps a corridor. \"That one stays off the map. Humour me.\"}\n> left: Ink it anyway\n  rel corvin -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {You draw it, line by honest line. Corvin watches, then laughs, a little too late. \"Saint,\" he says, without the usual smile.|It goes on the map. Corvin is quiet for the rest of the night, which on him is very loud.|You ink it, and the map leaves a blank there anyway, as if it agreed with Corvin. He looks at you and says nothing.}\n> right: Leave it off for him\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: {You leave a careful blank. Corvin squeezes your shoulder and doesn't make a joke, which is how you know it matters.|The map gets a gap and Corvin keeps his secret. You wonder, later, what it's for.|You leave the corridor off. The map closes over the gap so smoothly you'd never know. Neither would anyone else.}\n\n@event cartographers_silver_pen\ntitle: A Pen of Your Own\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nactor: corvin\nart: map\ntags: club\nscene: A silver-nibbed pen laid on a folded map in a candlelit stone alcove.\ntext: Corvin presses a pen into your hand: silver nib, black barrel, warm from his pocket. \"Mappers carry their own,\" he says. \"And the first passage you find, you name. Club rule. I made it up, but it's a rule.\" Tonight you found one.\n> left: Name it after yourself\n  guile +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Your name goes on the master map in your own silver hand, at the mouth of a passage that smells faintly of rain. The ink shivers, then settles, as if the castle had agreed.\n> right: Name it for a club joke\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: You name it after the night's worst joke. Corvin laughs so hard a prefect two floors down stops to listen. \"Keeper material,\" he whispers, and for once he doesn't laugh after.\n\n# ---------------- Library Assistant (track: library_assistant) ----------------\n\n@event library_assistant_wandering_shelves\ntitle: The Wandering Section\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: job study\nscene: A library aisle with an empty gap where a whole bookcase should be, a student holding a catalogue.\ntext: {The shelves rearranged themselves overnight. History is missing, and a queue is forming for it.|The shelves have wandered, this time the {poetry|herbals|atlases}. You have brought string to mark the way.|The head librarian simply hands you the catalogue now and points upward.} {Footnote sits where the section used to be.|A first-year swears she saw it go upstairs.|There is a faint trail in the dust, as if something heavy had tiptoed.}\n> left: Hunt it down by catalogue\n  wage\n  challenge research apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      perform library_assistant +small\n      say: {You find it on the fifth floor, wedged between the cookery books, looking innocent.|You track it by its shelfmarks to a landing that wasn't there yesterday. The queue cheers, quietly, because it's a library.|You follow the tiptoe trail to the atlases, three floors up. They are pretending to be geography.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform library_assistant -tiny\n      say: {You search until closing. In the morning the section is back where it started, faintly smug.|You find the wrong section, twice. The queue disperses with sighs you will hear in your sleep.|You follow the trail in the dust to a blank wall. The trail continues on the other side, presumably.}\n> right: Put up a sign and wait\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform library_assistant -tiny\n  say: {\"Back shortly,\" says your sign. The section returns in the afternoon. The queue does not.|You sit at the desk and wait. A student asks whether the sign means you or the books.|You put up the sign and the catalogue, open at the right page. The section comes back to see what you wrote about it.}\n\n@event library_assistant_hush_round\ntitle: The Hush Round\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: job\nscene: A student assistant with a lamp walking between study tables in a dim library, one finger to her lips.\ntext: {Hush round. One lap of the reading room, a lamp, a finger to your lips.|The reading room has learned the sound of your lamp. Whispers stop a table ahead of you.|The regulars hear your step and straighten up before you reach them.} {A table of second-years is giggling.|An old scholar snores on his notes, in a scholarly key.|Somebody's quill is scratching so loudly it echoes.} {final_year: Soon this will be someone else's lap.|}\n> left: Hush them firmly\n  wage\n  mood -tiny\n  perform library_assistant +small\n  rank_xp library_assistant +tiny\n  say: {One look and the room falls silent. Somewhere a bookcase seems to approve.|You hush them. They glare. In the morning one of them leaves a note on the desk: \"sorry\", in very small writing.|You hush the scratching quill. Its owner hears your step before you arrive and is already writing quietly.}\n> right: Let them be\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform library_assistant -tiny\n  say: {You let it go. The noise rises, the head librarian appears from nowhere, and looks at you, not at them.|You leave them to it. For a while the murmur is the nicest sound in the building.|You let them be. The regulars straighten up anyway when you pass, out of habit, and the noise sorts itself out.}\n\n@event library_assistant_reference_desk\ntitle: The Reference Desk\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: job study\nscene: A student behind a reference desk facing a line of readers, one of them holding up a green ribbon.\ntext: {Clerks take the reference desk alone. The first question arrives before you sit down.|The desk has a worn patch now, exactly where your elbows go.|You keep a list of the strangest questions now, and it is long.} {A professor wants \"the green book, about this thick, I read it in my youth\".|A first-year wants the book their professor \"definitely mentioned\".|A visiting scholar wants the one book that explains why bees hum in thirds, by tonight.}\n> left: Dig until you find it\n  wage\n  challenge research journeyman+:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      perform library_assistant +small\n      rank_xp library_assistant +tiny\n      say: {You find it in the end, in the wrong section, under a cat. The reader looks at you as if you had worked a miracle, and you have.|Three ladders, two catalogues and an educated guess. It is the right book.|There's no such book. You find three that explain it between them, by tonight. The scholar adds your name to the acknowledgements.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {Hours later you admit defeat. The book, it turns out, was on the desk all along, propping up the inkpot.|You never find it. You dream of card catalogues all night.|The bees remain unexplained. You add the question to your list, at the top.}\n> right: Send them off with a good guess\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform library_assistant -tiny\n  say: {You point confidently at a staircase. They come back an hour later with a different book, which they say they liked better.|\"Try the fourth floor.\" They do. You hope the fourth floor helps.|You send them to the apiary. They come back with a bee and no book, delighted.}\n\n@event library_assistant_stack_keys\ntitle: The Stack Keys\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: key\ntags: job study\nscene: An old librarian handing a brass ring of keys to a student among towering shelves at closing time.\ntext: At closing the head librarian presses a brass ring of keys into your hand: the upper stacks, the map drawers, the ladder chains. Not the Restricted Section. \"Lock up. The shelves settle after dark. Let them.\"\n> left: Do the closing round alone\n  wage\n  rank_xp library_assistant +medium\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You walk every floor with the keys chiming. The shelves shift and creak and settle, like an old house going to sleep, and this time you are the one who gets to hear it.\n> right: Let Footnote come along\n  wage\n  rank_xp library_assistant +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: Footnote pads ahead of you up every stair, checking each lock before you do. At the top he sits in the window and lets you scratch his ears, once. You suspect that was the real promotion.\n\n# ---------------- Server at the Gilded Newt (track: tavern) ----------------\n\n@event tavern_cider_rush\ntitle: The Cider Rush\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: job money\nscene: A crowded low-beamed tavern, a server balancing a tray of steaming mugs through the press of bodies.\ntext: {The rush at the Gilded Newt. A dozen orders shouted at once, and a tray that holds fewer.|The door won't stay shut. You have started remembering faces by their drink, which works until two people want spiced cider.|The regulars just point now, and you know what the points mean.} {A barge crew has come in soaked and thirsty.|The card table is arguing about the rules again.|{season == \"winter\": Snow is melting off every coat in the room.|Half the village is on the step.}}\n> left: Keep every order in your head\n  wage\n  challenge research apprentice:\n    win:\n      gold +tiny\n      perform tavern +small\n      say: {Every mug to the right hand. The whole room tips like it's payday.|You don't miss once. The landlady, who remembers every tab to the penny, gives you a look of professional respect.|The regulars point, and you deliver. The door won't stay shut, and nobody waits more than a minute.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      perform tavern -tiny\n      say: {The spiced cider goes to the wrong table. The right table has opinions. The wrong table drinks it anyway.|You mix up two orders and three tempers.|Half the village points at once. You misread two points and a wave.}\n> right: Write every order down\n  wage\n  perform tavern +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Slow but sure. Nobody gets the wrong drink; nobody tips the slow server either.|Your order pad fills with a shorthand only you can read. By closing, you can't either.|You write every order, even the points. By closing you have a full record of the village's habits.}\n\n@event tavern_regulars_corner\ntitle: The Regulars' Corner\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: job social\nscene: An old barge-man, a herb-wife from the lane and a quiet traveller at a fireside table, beckoning a server over.\ntext: {The regulars' corner by the fire wants you to sit for a minute. The fire is warm, and the minute is tempting.|The regulars' corner has saved you a stool now, with a cushion on it.|The regulars wave you over by name. They want to know how your exams went, all of them, in order.} {The old barge-man is telling the eel story again.|The herb-wife from the lane wants her pie warmed \"exactly as before\".|A quiet traveller is listening to everything.}\n> left: Sit with them a while\n  wage\n  charm +small\n  gold +tiny\n  perform tavern -tiny\n  say: {You hear the latest instalment of everyone's story. They tip you for listening, which is a better rate than serving.|You sit for one minute and stay for ten. The landlady clears her throat across the room like a falling tree.|You tell them about your exams, all of them, in order. The quiet traveller listens hardest and tips best.}\n> right: Keep the trays moving\n  wage\n  perform tavern +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You wave and keep going. The corner toasts your back. The eel story will keep.|You work the room like weather. The corner calls you \"the brisk one\" now, which is not a compliment.|You keep moving. The quiet traveller watches you all evening, and leaves without a word, and a large tip.}\n\n@event tavern_tab_book\ntitle: The Tab Book\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\ncast:\n  regular: present(place) where not tagged(regular, faculty)\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A ledger open on a tavern bar, a long column of figures, a hopeful customer at the far end.\ntext: {Barkeeps keep the tab book, and the tab book says {regular} owes the Newt rather a lot.|The tab book has grown a second volume, and {regular} has a page in it.|{regular} is at the end of the bar, and so, in the book, is a long column of figures.} {{They} smile{s} at you hopefully.|{They} {are} pretending to be fascinated by the fire.|{They} {are} counting coins that clearly won't be enough.}\n> left: Call the tab in\n  wage\n  gold +tiny\n  perform tavern +small\n  rel regular -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {{regular} pays, slowly, coin by coin, as if each one were a friend leaving. The landlady slides you a few crowns for the collecting.|You call it in. {regular} pays half and an apology. The landlady counts both, and gives you a few crowns of it for your trouble.|You call it in. {regular} pays in coins and a promise, and the landlady accepts the coins. You get a share of those.}\n> right: Let it ride another week\n  wage\n  rel regular +small\n  perform tavern -tiny\n  say: {{regular} looks at you as if you had hung the moon. The landlady looks at you as if you had lost it.|Another week. The column grows, and so, oddly, does {regular}'s loyalty.|You let it ride and add a line to the long column. {regular} buys you a drink with money {they} clearly owe the Newt.}\n\n@event tavern_behind_the_bar\ntitle: Behind the Bar\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: cup\ntags: job\nscene: A landlady setting a heavy cellar key on a polished tavern bar, the hearth fire glowing behind her.\ntext: The landlady sets the cellar key on the bar between you. \"You pour from tonight,\" she says. \"Barkeeps feed the fire, too. It hasn't gone out in my lifetime, nor my mother's. Don't be the one.\" The hearth crackles, as if listening.\n> left: Bank the fire at closing\n  wage\n  rank_xp tavern +medium\n  perform tavern +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You stay after the last song, banking the coals the way she showed you, slow and careful as tucking in a child. In the morning it is still burning. You check twice anyway.\n> right: Pour the staff a round\n  wage\n  rank_xp tavern +medium\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You pour your first round for the cooks and the pot-boy. The landlady takes hers without a word, then taps the bar twice, which apparently means \"well poured\" in a language older than the Newt.\n\n# ---------------- Apothecary Clerk (track: apothecary_clerk) ----------------\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_counting_drops\ntitle: Counting Drops\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: job\nscene: A student squinting at a glass dropper over a tiny vial at an apothecary counter, a wall of labelled drawers behind.\ntext: {Old Pell's first rule is to count every drop aloud. Today the counter is lined with tinctures waiting to be measured.|Drop-counting. Your tongue knows the numbers before your eyes do.|Counting drops. Old Pell says you count like a metronome now, which from him is a sonnet.} {A customer is waiting, tapping a coin on the glass.|One of the daughters is humming, which throws off your count.|Today's tincture is the colour of a bruise and smells of violets.}\n> left: Count aloud, every drop\n  wage\n  perform apothecary_clerk +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {Every vial exact. Anyone in the shop has now heard you count higher than anyone should.|Aloud, drop by drop. Old Pell, passing, nods once without breaking stride.|You count the violet one aloud in perfect time. Pell, passing, nods in time too.}\n> right: Count by eye, faster\n  wage\n  challenge brew apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      perform apothecary_clerk +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {Quick and true. You clear the counter before the tea goes cold, and a satisfied customer leaves a coin.|By eye, and spot on. One of the daughters raises an eyebrow and says nothing to her father.|You count by eye in your metronome rhythm. Pell checks one vial, finds it perfect, and stops checking.}\n    lose:\n      perform apothecary_clerk -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {One drop over. Old Pell can tell from across the shop. He tips the vial out and makes you count the next one aloud, twice.|You overshoot. The tincture turns from violet to a colour with no name, and Pell sighs like a closing door.|You go by eye and the violet one goes the colour of a worse bruise. Pell makes you count the rest aloud, as punishment and as lesson.}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_drawer_check\ntitle: The Thousand Drawers\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A wall of tiny labelled wooden drawers, one of them rattling on its own.\ntext: {Each shift a clerk checks a row of drawers against the stock book. Today's row starts with one that is stuck.|A different row today, and the same feeling that something in it is awake.|Your row for the week. You have learned that the wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.} {One is labelled in a hand that isn't Pell's.|One is rattling, very gently, as if politely.|One smells of snow, in {season == \"winter\": a shop this warm|the wrong season entirely}.}\n> left: Open the odd drawer\n  wage\n  challenge research using alchemy apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      perform apothecary_clerk +small\n      say: {Inside, a nest of dried moonpetals that belong on the top row. You move them, and the drawer stops sulking.|You coax it open and find the missing powdered newt. Pell crosses something off a list he has kept for a year.|Inside is a little snow that hasn't melted, and a note in Pell's hand: \"Don't.\" You close it. Pell thanks you, oddly sincerely.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The drawer opens with a sneeze of pepperwort. You sneeze until closing.|Something small and grey bolts out of the drawer and into another, and you never find out which.|The snow-smelling drawer is full of cold. It gets out, and the shop is frosted until noon.}\n> right: Sign it off unchecked\n  wage\n  alignment -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  perform apothecary_clerk -tiny\n  say: {You tick the row and move on. Behind you a drawer rattles, as if to say it noticed.|\"Checked,\" you write. Pell reads it, opens one drawer at random, and closes it again with a look.|You sign off the row, cold drawer and all. Pell checks that one himself, as if he'd expected you to.}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_counter_advice\ntitle: Asking the Dispenser\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: potion\ntags: job\nscene: A worried customer leaning over an apothecary counter, a student behind it consulting a heavy recipe book.\ntext: {Dispensers advise, and today a customer asks you instead of Pell.|Pell has moved your stool to the end of the counter, where the customers come.|You know the regular coughs by sound now, before anyone opens the door.} {A farmer has a cough that sounds like a gate.|A nervous student wants something for courage before exams.|A barge-hand has a back that hasn't straightened since autumn.}\n> left: Prescribe it yourself\n  wage\n  challenge brew journeyman:\n    win:\n      perform apothecary_clerk +small\n      rank_xp apothecary_clerk +small\n      say: {You mix it, label it and explain the dose twice. A week later they come back just to say it worked.|Your remedy, your writing on the bottle. Pell reads the label, reaches for his pen, and leaves it as it is.|You know the barge-hand's back from the sound of the door. The liniment is ready before they reach the counter.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform apothecary_clerk -tiny\n      say: {It helps a little, then not at all. They come back, and this time they ask for Pell.|Pell takes the bottle from your hand, adds one thing and gives it back. He doesn't say a word, which is worse.|You recommend the liniment for the barge-hand's back. It's the wrong one, and the barge-hand smells of it for a week.}\n> right: Fetch one of the daughters\n  wage\n  alchemy +small\n  perform apothecary_clerk -tiny\n  say: {The elder daughter takes over and talks the customer down like a nervous horse. You watch and write it all down.|The younger daughter mixes it in half the time you would have. She lets you hold the funnel.|Both daughters come, and argue the case across you. You learn twice as much and get the cure from neither.}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_labelling_pen\ntitle: The Labelling Pen\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: feather\ntags: job\nscene: An old apothecary handing over a well-worn pen beside a stack of fresh blank labels.\ntext: Old Pell is very proud of his labelling. So it means something when he puts his own pen in your hand, its nib worn to his slant, and slides a stack of blank labels across the counter. \"New stock,\" he says. \"Your hand from now on. Neat, mind.\"\n> left: Label the new drawers yourself\n  wage\n  rank_xp apothecary_clerk +medium\n  alchemy +small\n  perform apothecary_clerk +small\n  say: You write every label slowly, in your best hand. Pell inspects them through his spectacles one by one and changes not a single letter. His daughters notice before you do.\n> right: Ask him to watch the first one\n  wage\n  rank_xp apothecary_clerk +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: He watches. Your first label wobbles at the end. Pell takes the pen, adds a tiny flourish to your wobble so that it looks deliberate, and returns the pen for good.\n\n# ---------------- Broom Courier (track: broom_courier) ----------------\n\n@event broom_courier_morning_crossing\ntitle: The Lake Crossing\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: job\nscene: A courier on a broom skimming low over a mirror-still lake at dawn, a mail sack over one shoulder.\ntext: {The dispatch bell rings. The village sack is ready, and the quickest way is over the lake.|You have started timing yourself against the gulls.|The lake knows your shadow by now.} {season == \"winter\": Ice fog sits on the water like a lid.|{season == \"summer\": The water is so still you fly over your own reflection.|{A headwind is coming off the hills.|Gulls are holding a meeting on your route.|The far shore is lost in mist.}}}\n> left: Fly straight across\n  wage\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      perform broom_courier +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {Straight as a thrown stone. You're early, and the village postmistress tips early couriers.|You skim the water so low your boots leave a wake. Nobody sees, which is a shame.|You fly straight into the mist, by memory. The far shore appears exactly where you expected it.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      perform broom_courier -tiny\n      say: {A gust drops you into the shallows. The sack stays dry; you do not.|The far shore vanishes and you fly a slow circle round a buoy. You arrive late and very damp.|The mist takes the far shore and your sense of direction with it. You arrive at the right village from the wrong side.}\n> right: Take the long way round\n  wage\n  perform track -tiny\n  say: {Round the shore, safe and slow. The dispatcher checks the clock and sighs.|The long way. You wave to a fisherman, who does not wave back.|The long way, round the mist. The fisherman waves, for the first time. You're so surprised you nearly fall off.}\n\n@event broom_courier_broom_scramble\ntitle: The Broom Scramble\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: broom\ntags: job\nscene: A draughty loft rack of battered brooms, couriers jostling to grab the least battered one.\ntext: {Only one loft broom is in a good state, and every courier on shift wants it.|You have started arriving early for the brooms, and so has everyone else.|You could pick the good broom blindfold, by the smell of its varnish.} {What's left is one that pulls left.|What's left sheds twigs when nervous.|What's left only flies if you're polite to it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has got into all their bristles.|}\n> left: Shoulder in for the good broom\n  wage\n  perform broom_courier +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {You get there first, by a whisker and an elbow. The second-year you elbowed will remember.|You shoulder a second-year aside and the good broom is yours. It flies like a dream; theirs flies like a complaint.|You pick out the good broom by its varnish while the others are still arguing. You're over the lake before they notice.}\n> right: Take the difficult one\n  wage\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  perform broom_courier +tiny\n  say: {It fights you the whole way across the lake. Your arms will remember this, and so will you.|It takes the whole route to come to an understanding with it. You win, narrowly.|You're polite to the difficult one, very polite. It flies well, and everyone else is too embarrassed to try it.}\n\n@event broom_courier_dead_letters\ntitle: The Dead-Letter Shelf\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: job mystery\nscene: A dusty shelf of undeliverable parcels in a courier loft, one tied with a faded ribbon.\ntext: {Couriers sort the dead letters, post with no proper address, waiting on a dusty shelf.|The dead-letter shelf has grown a second row.|You know which parcels have waited longest now, and which have stopped expecting anyone.} Today, a parcel addressed only to {\"the tall one in the red scarf, third tower\"|\"whoever plays the flute at dawn\"|\"my brother, the one who never writes\"}.\n> left: Track down the recipient\n  wage\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      perform broom_courier +small\n      rank_xp broom_courier +tiny\n      say: {It takes three towers and a dozen questions, but the parcel reaches the right hands. They open it at once and cry a little, in a good way.|Found them. The look on their face is worth every stair.|You find the brother. He's a porter in the next village and he does write; the letters just never arrive. Now one has.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {You knock on every door you can think of. The parcel goes back on the shelf, a little more crumpled, a little more sad.|Wrong door. Wrong tower. Wrong week.|There are a great many brothers who never write. None of them is this one.}\n> right: Leave it on the shelf\n  wage\n  mood +tiny\n  perform broom_courier -tiny\n  say: {It goes back with the others. Some of them have been waiting longer than you have been alive.|You leave it. That night you dream of a parcel on a high tower, waiting.|You leave it, but you dust the shelf. The parcels that have stopped expecting anyone look a little less forgotten.}\n\n@event broom_courier_own_pigeonhole\ntitle: Your Own Pigeonhole\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 20\nart: bell\ntags: job\nscene: A courier loft wall of pigeonholes, one freshly painted with a student's name, a brass bell above.\ntext: The dispatcher has painted your name over a pigeonhole, in letters that will outlast the loft's roof. \"Your route from now on,\" the dispatcher says. \"Village, barge landing, the high towers. When the bell rings for you, you answer.\" The dispatch bell rings. It's for you.\n> left: Fly the route at once\n  wage\n  rank_xp broom_courier +medium\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You fly your own route for the first time, every turning yours. At the barge landing someone calls you by name to hand over a letter. The wind is cold, and you don't mind at all.\n> right: Stand the runners a round\n  wage\n  rank_xp broom_courier +medium\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: The runners toast your pigeonhole with mugs of loft tea. Somebody chalks a little crown above your name. It is still there at the end of the year, smudged, the most permanent thing in the loft.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_a.grim","text":"# Baseline course-morning pools, group courses_a (docs/authoring/pools.md):\n# Evocation, Astromancy & Divination, Alchemy & Healing. Repeatable routine events, three at level I and two each at II and III.\n\n# ---------------- Evocation (track: evocation, skill arcana) ----------------\n\n@pool evocation_class\nfrom: course_routine\ntrack: evocation\n\n@event evo_snuffing_drill\ntitle: The Snuffing Drill\npool: evocation_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: light\nart: candle\ntags: magic\nscene: A row of iron braziers burning in a scorched lecture hall, students standing before them with their hands raised as if to calm a horse.\ntext: {Evocation I opens with a lesson nobody expected. \"Anyone can start a fire,\" says the lecturer. \"Stopping one is the course.\"|The braziers are lit before you arrive. You know which one smokes.|Your brazier goes out when you walk in now, out of habit. The lecturer relights it, unamused.} {The front row smells of singed eyebrow.|Someone's brazier has gone out from fright.|The lecturer is timing you with an egg-timer.}\n> left: Call it back slowly\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The flames shrink into embers, then into a warm ache in your palm.|It takes the whole hour. Your brazier goes out like a sigh.|You call it back while the egg-timer runs. It goes out with a second to spare, and the lecturer turns the timer over again.} {season == \"winter\": The hall is colder afterwards, and it's your fault.|The lecturer nods once.}\n> right: Snuff it in one breath\n  challenge exam standard-1:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +tiny\n      say: {The fire folds itself into your hand all at once.|One breath, and the brazier is dark.|You snuff it before the egg-timer has turned.} The lecturer writes something down without looking surprised, which is the compliment.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The fire goes out. It goes out up your sleeve first.|The brazier flares, then sulks, then goes out on its own terms.|You rush it for the timer, and the fire rushes back.} You spend the rest of the hour holding your wrist in a bucket.\n\n@event evo_bucket_duty\ntitle: Bucket Duty\npool: evocation_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: light\nart: wave\ntags: study\nscene: A student standing at the edge of a busy lecture hall beside a bucket of sand and a bucket of water, watching sparks fly.\ntext: Every Evocation I lesson needs someone on the buckets: sand for the pyromancers, water for everyone else. {&Today it's your name on the board.|It's your turn again. The rota has a sense of humour.|Your name is on the board. You suspect the rota is personal.} {&The water bucket has a frog in it.|Someone has labelled the sand bucket \"Hope\".|The water bucket is half empty, because the tidecallers borrowed it.}\n> left: Mind the buckets, watch closely\n  study evocation +tiny\n  say: {&You put out four sleeves and a textbook, and learn more from their mistakes than from your own.|Watching from the side, you finally see what the lecturer means by \"breathe first\".|You keep the buckets full and borrowed back from the tidecallers, and watch the whole lesson from the best seat in the hall.} {month == 4 or month == 10: It's the best revision you've done all term.|Nobody thanks the bucket monitor.}\n> right: Swap for a turn up front\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {&You trade your duty for a turn at the practice line. Nobody is minding the buckets when your spark jumps the desk.|A first-year takes your buckets for a promise of help with their essay. Your practice goes well; your thumb goes pink.|You swap, and the tidecallers have the water bucket. Your spark finds the one dry curtain in the hall.}\n\n@event evo_steady_cup\ntitle: The Steady Cup\npool: evocation_class\nlevel: 1\nlesson: mirror_lake\ntone: light\nart: cup\ntags: magic\nscene: A row of students on a stone terrace holding brimming cups at arm's length while a professor reads aloud from a slim book of poems.\ntext: {Professor Marisol's Tidecalling mornings begin with a full cup held at arm's length. Keep the water level by will alone while she reads poetry aloud.|Marisol has brought a new book of poems, thicker than the last.|You can hold the cup half asleep now, and often are.} {season == \"winter\": The water has a skin of ice, which she says is cheating.|{The poem is very long.|The poem is about the sea, and so are your sleeves.|A gull is watching your cup with intent.}}\n> left: Hold it to the last line\n  arcana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Your arm burns by the third stanza. The water doesn't move.|By the end your arm is shaking and the water isn't.|The gull lands on your wrist in the last verse. The water doesn't notice, and neither, quite, do you.} The poem goes on for ever, and so, it feels, do you. Marisol closes the book at last. \"There. The lake would approve.\"\n> right: Spill, and study her hands\n  study evocation +tiny\n  say: {Your cup tips over in the second verse. You spend the rest watching how Marisol turns her wrist, and write it down.|You give up on the cup and watch Marisol instead. She holds a whole pond steady while turning pages.|The gull takes your cup the moment you spill it. You watch Marisol hold hers steady through the gull's attempt on hers too.} {final_year: You'll miss her terrible poems.|Your shoes are wet.}\n\n@event evo_shaping_hour\ntitle: Shaping Hour\npool: evocation_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: light\nart: flame\ntags: magic\nscene: A lecture hall full of small fires shaped like birds, keys and letters, one of them visibly trying to escape through a window.\ntext: Evocation II is shaping: flame made into a sphere, a key, a bird. The lecturer keeps a ledger of every scorch mark in the hall and makes you sign for yours. {flag(scorched_ledger): Your page in the ledger has a few signatures on it already.|Your page in the ledger is still blank.} {&Today's shape is a bird.|Today's shape is a key that turns.|Today's shape is your own initial, which is harder than it sounds.}\n> left: A hundred plain spheres\n  arcana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {&Sphere after sphere, until your hands do it without asking you.|It's dull, honest work. Your spheres come out round, every one.|Spheres until your fingers know the curve. Your initial would come easier after this, you suspect.} {season == \"summer\": The hall is an oven by noon.|The lecturer says nothing, which is approval.}\n> right: Try the hard shape\n  challenge exam standard+1:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +tiny\n      say: {&Your bird flaps twice, circles the lectern and goes out on a perch.|Your key turns in the air, with a click you feel more than hear.|Your initial hangs in the air, crisp at every edge.} The lecturer turns the ledger round to show it hasn't moved.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {&Your bird has opinions, and leaves through the window.|Your key comes out as a fork, and a hot one.|Your initial comes out as somebody else's, and singes your cuff.} You sign the ledger. The lecturer blots it for you, almost kindly.\n      flag scorched_ledger\n> up[flame]: Shape it at a whisper\n  study evocation +tiny\n  say: {&You call the flame softly and it comes, small and obedient as a candle. The lecturer leans in to watch.|The shape comes out right first time, at a whisper. Your page in the ledger stays clean.|The flame stays the size you asked for, which is the whole lesson.}\n\n@event evo_kettle_pairs\ntitle: Fire Meets Water\npool: evocation_class\nlevel: 2\nlesson: mirror_lake\ntone: tense\nart: cauldron\ntags: study\nscene: Pairs of students on either side of copper kettles, one with a palm of flame and one with a palm of water, steam curling between them.\ntext: {This is why Pyromancy and Tidecalling are taught together. One of you heats the kettle, one cools it, and it must simmer and never boil.|The kettles come out of the cupboard, every dent a story.|You know every kettle by its dents now. Two dents are yours.} {Your partner is a nervous second-year from Stillwater.|Your partner is very confident, which is worrying.|Your partner yawns hugely.}\n> left: Take the fire side\n  challenge exam standard:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +tiny\n      say: {You and your partner find the rhythm together, and the kettle hums all hour.|Push, give, push. The kettle simmers like a contented cat.|Your partner yawns through the whole hour, and the kettle simmers anyway. You've been doing both halves.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The kettle boils, then whistles, then lifts its lid at you. You both get a faceful of steam.|You push too hard. Your partner panics and freezes the spout, and the trapped steam bursts out of the lid at you.|Your partner falls asleep mid-lesson and the kettle boils. You both wake up very suddenly.}\n> right: Take the water side\n  study evocation +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {Cooling is the patient half. You hold the kettle back all hour and your partner gets the credit.|You lend the kettle your cold for an hour and feel drained by noon.|You hold the water side alone while your partner yawns. The lecturer notices who did the work.} {final_year: The kettle has been here longer than you, and will stay after you've gone.|Your fingertips stay damp until lunch.}\n\n@event evo_deep_call\ntitle: Calling From the Deep\npool: evocation_class\nlevel: 3\nlesson: mirror_lake\ntone: eerie\nart: wave\ntags: danger\nscene: Advanced students on a jetty with their arms raised, a column of dark, old lake water rising toward them out of the depths.\ntext: {Advanced Evocation calls water from the lake bed, where it is cold and old and in no hurry.|The jetty is slick at this hour.|The jetty boards know your boots by now.} The lecturer paces behind the line. The water comes up carrying things. {Today, a bottle with no message in it.|Today, a single boot, laced.|Today, nothing at all, which is somehow worse.} {season == \"winter\": Ice grinds at the edges of the column.|Mist sits on the water.}\n> left: Reach deeper than the rest\n  challenge exam standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      say: {You call water up from somewhere it has been still for a century. It rises like a held breath and comes down gently.|The water obeys, then lingers, as if it wanted to talk.|You call from deeper than anyone, and the water brings nothing up at all. It feels like being refused.} The lecturer stops pacing.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mana -small\n      say: {The lake pulls back. You're on your knees on the jetty, cold to the bone.|The water rises, considers you, and drops onto your head.|You reach for the deep water and something reaches back, gently, and lets go. You sit down on the jetty for a while.} The lecturer pulls you up with no comment, which is a comment.\n> right: Keep to the shallows\n  study evocation +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You keep to safer water and log what the others bring up. It's useful and it's dull.|You hold your column at a sensible depth and watch someone else go white with the cold.|You keep to the shallows. The others bring up nothing today, and look at the water a long time.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says you have nothing to prove. You'd like to.|You'll go deeper next time, perhaps.}\n\n@event evo_signature_flame\ntitle: A Fire of Your Own\npool: evocation_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: warm\nart: flame\ntags: magic\nscene: A student alone at a stone bench in a dim hall, coaxing a flame of an unusual colour above their palm.\ntext: Advanced Evocation asks every student for a signature flame: its own colour, its own heat, its own temper. {Yours is a sulky orange.|Yours has turned a gold that you're quietly proud of.|Yours knows you now. It leans toward you when you come in.} {Somebody's flame across the hall is green and smells of pine.|Somebody's flame is silver and hums.|The lecturer's own flame is lit on the lectern, blue and tiny, never discussed.}\n> left: Push it somewhere new\n  challenge exam standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      say: {Your flame slides into a colour nobody has a name for. The lecturer asks you to do it again, slowly.|For a moment your flame burns without smoke and without heat, just light.|Your flame leans toward the lecturer's blue one and takes a little of its colour. The lecturer covers hers.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {Your flame flares white, takes a bite of your hand and goes back to orange as if nothing happened.|The new colour lasts a heartbeat. The blister lasts a week.|Your flame reaches for something new and finds your sleeve. It knows you, apparently, and still bites.}\n> right: Refine what it already is\n  study evocation +tiny\n  say: {You spend the morning making it steadier, not stranger. It's a better flame by noon, and a less exciting one.|You tune the heat by a hair, then another hair.|You refine it until it leans toward you on its own. That's all it does, and it's enough.} {final_year: You'll carry this fire out of Lumenhold with you.|It's yours, at least.}\n\n# ---------------- Astromancy & Divination (track: astromancy, skill lore) ----------------\n\n@pool astromancy_class\nfrom: course_routine\ntrack: astromancy\n\n@event astro_tea_rota\ntitle: Another Pot of Tea\npool: astromancy_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: light\nart: cup\ntags: study\nscene: A round classroom under a brass dome, every desk crowded with teacups, a huge brown teapot steaming on the lectern.\ntext: Astromancy I has a tea-leaf morning every week, and tea leaves require tea. {The class gets through three pots before ten.|The tea rota has your name on it again.|The teapot greets you like an old colleague.} {Your cup shows something that is either a ship or a boot.|Your cup shows a shape the textbook calls the ladder. It looks like a spoon.|Your cup shows rain. Your cup always shows rain.}\n> left: Drink the pot and read\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {By the fourth cup your hands are shaking and the leaves are making more sense.|You read six cups in a row and see the pattern underneath the shapes.|Your cup shows rain again. It rains. You're starting to think that's the reading.} {season == \"winter\": At least the tea is warm.|You will not sleep tonight.}\n> right: Brew the next pot\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  say: {You take the rota and brew for the room. Watching leaves settle in a whole pot teaches you more than any one cup.|You brew it strong, the way the lecturer likes it, and learn the shapes as they sink.|You brew the pot as the teapot likes it, which is the lecturer's way plus a pinch. The room reads better for it.} {season == \"summer\": The classroom is a steam bath by ten.|Nobody thanks the brewer, but everyone notices weak tea.}\n\n@event astro_star_names\ntitle: Naming the Stars\npool: astromancy_class\nlevel: 1\nlesson: observatory\ntone: light\nart: star\ntags: study\nscene: Students craning their necks under a painted dome of constellations while a lecturer points upward with a very long pole.\ntext: {Astromancy I begins with names. Three hundred stars painted on the dome, to be recited on request.|The long pole comes out. The lecturer points; you name.|You dream the star names some nights now, and wake reciting.} {Today it's the winter constellations.|Today it's the faint stars nobody can see without squinting.|Today the lecturer points at a patch of bare plaster and waits to see who invents a name.}\n> left: Recite them until perfect\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You chant star names under your breath all morning, like a prayer you don't quite believe in.|By noon you can name the whole southern sky backwards.|You recite them in your sleep, apparently, and again at breakfast. Your roommate can name the southern sky now too.} {month == 4 or month == 10: The exam is close, and the names are finally sticking.|Your neck aches from looking up.}\n> right: Volunteer to go first\n  challenge exam standard-1:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {You name every star the pole touches, including the bare plaster, which you correctly call \"nothing\".|The lecturer runs out of stars before you run out of names.|The lecturer points at bare plaster. You invent a name so plausible that two people write it down.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      lore +tiny\n      say: {You call a bright star by the name of a lesser one. The lesser one's champion in the back row is offended.|You blank on the fourth star, and a whole constellation falls out of your head.|The lecturer points at bare plaster and you name it confidently, with a star that doesn't exist. It's in three people's notes by lunch.}\n\n@event astro_cold_dome\ntitle: The Open Dome\npool: astromancy_class\nlevel: 1\nlesson: observatory\ntone: warm\nart: moon\ntags: study\nscene: The observatory's brass dome cranked open to a pale dawn sky, one student at the great telescope and the rest huddled round an iron stove.\ntext: {Astromancy mornings start before the sun, under a dome cranked open to the sky.|The dome creaks open. You've learned which side of the stove is warmest.|The telescope's eyepiece has the shape of your eye by now.} {season == \"winter\": It is bitterly cold, and the stove is the most popular student in the class.|{season == \"summer\": The air is soft and the sky is already going pale.|There's dew on the telescope and on you.}} Somebody has to take the telescope.\n> left: Take the telescope turn\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You track a planet across the eyepiece until {season == \"winter\": your eyelashes stick to it|your eyes water and your neck gives up}.|The great telescope wanders, as it does. You coax it back to the sky you were assigned.|The eyepiece fits your eye. You find the planet before the lecturer has finished saying where it is.} {final_year: You've looked through it more times than you can count, and you'll still miss it.|{season == \"winter\": Your fingers take an hour to thaw.|You yawn through the whole of breakfast.}}\n> right: Stay by the stove with charts\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The stove students form a little society of the warm and the guilty. You copy charts and hear every complaint.|You annotate the charts while the others shiver. The lecturer notices who is warm.|You keep the stove and the charts. Someone else takes the telescope and complains it's shaped wrong.}\n\n@event astro_foresight_drill\ntitle: Catch the Chalk\npool: astromancy_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: light\nart: hourglass\ntags: magic\nscene: A lecturer letting a stick of chalk fall from an outstretched hand while a student lunges to catch it a moment before it drops.\ntext: {Foresight at Astromancy II is practised on chalk. The lecturer drops a stick without warning; you catch it before it falls, by seeing it a heartbeat early.|The lecturer has opened a new box of chalk, which is a threat.|The lecturer's drops have got sneakier, and so have your hands.} {The floor is white with failures.|A student from Stillwater has caught four in a row and is unbearable about it.|The lecturer is dropping two at once today.}\n> left: Catch it before it falls\n  challenge exam standard wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      study astromancy +tiny\n      say: {Your hand is there before the chalk is. The lecturer looks at it, then at you, then drops another.|You catch it a breath before the drop.|Two sticks at once. You catch one in each hand, and the lecturer drops a third out of spite.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {You see it falling, clearly, half a second after it lands.|You catch a stick of chalk that wasn't dropped. The real one hits your shoe.|Two sticks at once, and you catch the one in the past.}\n> right: Time the drops instead\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {You keep a tally of every drop and every catch and start to see the lecturer's rhythm.|You work out that the lecturer always blinks first. It's not foresight, but it helps.|Your tally shows the lecturer drops the second stick a beat after the first, always. It's not foresight. It works.} Holding a little seeing-charm open the whole hour to check your tally leaves your head aching. {gpa >= 3: The lecturer asks to see your tally.|You'd rather have caught one.}\n> up[foresight]: Let foresight do it\n  lore +small\n  say: {You stop trying and let the spell look ahead for you. Your hand opens, and the chalk drops into it like a tame bird.|It's a two-chalk day. The spell shows you both, and you catch both.|The lecturer stops mid-drop and just looks at you. You catch it anyway.}\n\n@event astro_starlight_jars\ntitle: Bottling Starlight\npool: astromancy_class\nlevel: 2\nlesson: observatory\ntone: warm\nart: bottle\ntags: magic\nscene: Students on the observatory roof before dawn, holding glass jars up to the sky, each jar glowing faintly with captured light.\ntext: {Starlight is gathered before dawn, one star at a time, in glass jars on the observatory roof.|The roof is slippery with frost or dew, depending.|The starlight jars. Yours has a chip in the rim you've grown fond of.} {Most of the class is aiming for the brightest stars.|The lecturer says faint light keeps longer.|There's a slip of cloud coming in from the lake.}\n> left: Wait for the faint stars\n  challenge research standard:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      study astromancy +tiny\n      say: {Your jar fills slowly with a cool, pale light that lasts all day in your pocket.|The faint stars come one at a time.|You wait out the cloud with your chipped jar. The faint stars come back for you, one by one.} The lecturer holds your jar up and says, \"That will keep.\"\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {The cloud comes in before your jar is half full, and you're cold for nothing.|You wait so long the sun rises on you and burns the jar to plain glass.|The cloud from the lake settles in for the morning. Your chipped jar stays empty, and you stay cold.}\n> right: Take the bright, easy ones\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  say: {Your jar is full in ten minutes and glows like a lamp. The lecturer calls it a lamp.|Easy light, quickly gathered and gone by noon.|You fill your chipped jar with the bright ones before the cloud arrives. It's gone by lunch, as usual.} {season == \"summer\": The short nights are no help.|You're back in bed before the bell.}\n\n@event astro_moving_charts\ntitle: The Charts Move\npool: astromancy_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: eerie\nart: map\ntags: study\nscene: A long table covered in star charts whose inked constellations are visibly sliding across the parchment, students pinning corners down.\ntext: {Advanced Astromancy uses charts that move. The inked stars drift to where they will be, not where they are.|The charts are livelier this morning, and one has crept off the table.|You've stopped flinching when the stars move. You still check twice.} {Your chart keeps rearranging itself into a shape like a doorway.|Your chart is sulking in one corner.|Your chart is going faster than the others.} Pin it and read it, or read it as it goes.\n> left: Pin it and trace the drift\n  challenge research standard+1:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      say: {You pin each star and trace its path, and for a moment you can see the whole season ahead laid out in ink.|The drift makes sense all at once.|You pin the fast chart and trace a season so quickly it makes you dizzy. The lecturer asks to keep the tracing.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {You pour magic into the pins to hold it, and the chart slides out from under them anyway, leaving ink on your sleeves.|You hold the chart down with a binding until your magic runs thin, then trace the drift for an hour. It goes back to where it started, laughing at you in its own way.|The fast chart drags the pins with it across the table and onto the floor. You chase it on your knees.}\n> right: Sketch every shape it makes\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You fill four pages with the shapes the chart passes through. Most are nothing. One is almost something.|You draw it as it moves, like a portrait of someone who won't sit still.|You sketch the fast chart at its own pace. Your drawing hand is exhausted. The shapes are beautiful.} {final_year: Soon the stars will move without you watching.|The lecturer collects your pages without a word.}\n\n@event astro_far_glimpse\ntitle: Looking Too Far\npool: astromancy_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: tense\nart: eye\ntags: magic\nscene: A student staring into a dark mirror at the centre of a curtained room, a lecturer standing close with an hourglass.\ntext: {At this level Foresight reaches a season ahead. The lecturer allows one glimpse each, timed by an hourglass. People who look longer come back strange.|The dark mirror waits behind its curtain.|The hourglass is turned before you sit down. The lecturer knows your pace.} {final_year: This is your last year of looking ahead from this room, which makes the mirror harder to look at.|{The curtains are drawn against the morning.|Someone before you came out very quiet.|The mirror is very clean today, which is somehow worse.}}\n> left: Look a season ahead\n  challenge exam standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      study astromancy +tiny\n      say: {You see a lit window and a cup going cold, and you know exactly where and when it will be. You keep it to yourself.|You see something small and true. You'll remember it when it happens.|You see a season ahead before the hourglass is half gone. You stop yourself, which the lecturer notes.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {The mirror shows you too much at once. You come out with a nosebleed and a headache that lasts till dinner.|You see nothing, and then you see everything.|You look until the sand runs out, and a little after. The lecturer pulls you back by the shoulder.}\n> right: Look only as far as tomorrow\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {Tomorrow, says the mirror, it will rain at noon. It does. It's not much, but it's right.|You look only one day ahead. Lunch will be soup.|You look only to tomorrow, in your timed turn. It will be cold. It is.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says caution is a talent too.|It's safe, and it feels small.}\n\n# ---------------- Alchemy & Healing (track: alchemy, skill alchemy) ----------------\n\n@pool alchemy_class\nfrom: course_routine\ntrack: alchemy\n\n@event alch_stir_count\ntitle: Clockwise\npool: alchemy_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: light\nart: cauldron\ntags: study\nscene: A laboratory of identical cauldrons, a whole class stirring in unison while the lecturer counts aloud from the front.\ntext: {Alchemy I is mostly stirring, and the stirring is counted aloud by the whole class, in unison, like a choir with spoons.|The chorus is getting good. Someone has started harmonising.|You count stirs in your sleep now, and sometimes on the stairs.} {Today's draught is a simple tonic.|Today's draught is meant to turn blue at forty.|Today the lecturer keeps changing the count to see who's listening.}\n> left: Count every stir aloud\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {By the hundredth stir your voice is gone and your tonic is perfect.|Your count is right and your draught is dull. Both are what was asked.|The lecturer changes the count; you change with it, aloud. Your tonic is the only one that's right.} {month == 4 or month == 10: The exam recipes come back to you in rhythm.|Your wrist aches.}\n> right: Stir by the colour instead\n  challenge brew standard-1:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {You stop counting and watch the colour. It turns blue exactly when you expect it to.|You lose the count and keep the draught. The lecturer can tell, and allows it.|The count changes and you don't care, because you're watching the colour. The lecturer asks how you knew.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You watch the colour too closely and miss it changing. The fumes make your eyes water.|Your draught turns a colour the lecturer has never seen. It's not a good sign.|You watch the colour and miss the count changing. Your draught is right for a different recipe.}\n> up[item:measuring_spoon]: Measure twice with Pell's spoon\n  challenge brew standard-1 steady:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {You measure every reagent twice with the worn brass spoon. The tonic turns blue on the fortieth stir, exactly.|The spoon makes you slow, and slow makes you right. The lecturer holds your vial up to the window.|Pell's spoon has never been wrong, and today, neither are you.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You measure twice and stir once too many. The spoon was right; you weren't.|The measures are perfect. Your stirring arm gives out at sixty.|You get every measure right and the fumes wrong, and your eyes water all afternoon.}\n\n@event alch_chopping_board\ntitle: Prep Work\npool: alchemy_class\nlevel: 1\nlesson: pell_and_daughters\ntone: light\nart: leaf\ntags: study\nscene: A long scrubbed bench heaped with roots, berries and dried beetles, students bent over chopping boards with small sharp knives.\ntext: {&Half of alchemy, says the lecturer, is cutting things up correctly. The ingredients come from Pell & Daughters in the village, packed in straw, every one labelled in a careful hand.|The crate from the village is heavier today.|Your knife knows the way, and your thumb knows where not to be.} {&Today it's roots, to be sliced paper-thin.|Today it's beetles, to be crushed and not thought about.|Today it's berries, which stain everything.}\n> left: Prep for the whole bench\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {&You cut enough roots for six. Your bench-mates bring you tea, which is fair.|You crush beetles for an hour and think firmly about other things.|You prepare enough berries for six, and your hands stay purple till dinner.} The crate is empty when you're done.\n> right: Prep yours, read ahead\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: You rush your share and get to the reading, plus a small cut on your thumb you'll be explaining all day.\n\n@event alch_bandage_round\ntitle: Bandage Round\npool: alchemy_class\nlevel: 1\nlesson: infirmary\ntone: warm\nart: cross\ntags: study\nscene: A sunny infirmary ward where students roll bandages at a long table while a nun in a starched apron checks their work.\ntext: {&The healing half of Alchemy I is taught in the infirmary, where Sister Amaranth has the class roll bandages and watch her work.|Sister Amaranth inspects every roll, and unrolls one of yours.|Sister Amaranth hands you a roll without looking up.} {&A third-year is in with a burnt hand from Evocation.|A first-year is in with a scraped knee and a great deal to say about it.|A second-year is in with a sprained wrist from the flight field.}\n> left: Roll bandages and watch\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  say: {&You roll, and watch Sister Amaranth clean a cut in about three movements.|Your bandages pass inspection.|You roll, and watch Sister Amaranth strap a wrist so it can't move and doesn't hurt. You try it on your own wrist later.} {season == \"winter\": The ward fills with sniffling students all morning.|She says nothing, which is high praise.}\n> right: Offer to treat a patient\n  challenge exam standard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {&You treat the burn properly. Sister Amaranth says, \"Hm.\" From her, that is a speech.|You clean and bind the scraped knee neatly. The patient doesn't even wince. Sister Amaranth adjusts one knot.|You strap the wrist firmly and neatly. The patient flexes it and looks impressed.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {&You bandage the wrong place entirely. The patient is too polite to say so; Sister Amaranth is not.|Your bandage unrolls itself and the patient has to wait while you start again.|You strap the wrist so tightly the patient's fingers go white. Sister Amaranth loosens it, and looks at you.}\n\n@event alch_purify_samples\ntitle: Lake Water Samples\npool: alchemy_class\nlevel: 2\nlesson: mirror_lake\ntone: light\nart: bottle\ntags: magic\nscene: A bench lined with jars of murky lake water, a lecturer at the end raising one now-clear jar to drink it.\ntext: {Alchemy II hands out jars of murky lake water, to be made fit to drink. The lecturer has a habit of drinking the results.|The lecturer looks thirsty, which is never a good sign.|The lecturer has brought a favourite cup for this, chipped at the rim.} {Your jar has a small weed in it.|Your jar is suspiciously green.|Your jar has a tiny snail in it, which you set free.}\n> left: Purify it thoroughly\n  challenge brew standard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study alchemy +tiny\n      say: {Your water comes out clear and sweet. The lecturer drinks it, pauses and nods.|Clean as rainwater.|Your water goes into the lecturer's chipped cup, and the lecturer drinks it to the bottom.} {flag(purified_well): The lecturer drinks a second glass, and pretends not to.|The lecturer drinks a second glass, which nobody has seen before.}\n      flag purified_well\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {You test it first, just a sip, as the lecturer always does. You spend the afternoon close to the washroom.|Your water clears, then clouds again. You taste it anyway, to be sure, and regret it all afternoon.|You purify it so hard the snail you set free comes back to complain. The water tastes of snail anyway.}\n> right: Filter it the slow way\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {Cloth, sand and charcoal, the old way. It takes the whole lesson.|You filter it by hand and write every step down. The lecturer drinks it and says, \"Patient.\"|You filter it slowly and the snail goes back to the lake in a cup of its own. The lecturer approves of both.} {final_year: You'll be filtering water like this for the rest of your life, probably.|Your hands are grey with charcoal.}\n\n@event alch_long_simmer\ntitle: The Long Simmer\npool: alchemy_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: tense\nart: hourglass\ntags: study\nscene: A bubbling cauldron in an empty laboratory, a single student slumped on a stool beside it with an hourglass and a book.\ntext: {Some potions simmer for hours and must be watched. Whoever is on watch when the colour turns gets the credit, or the blame.|The class takes turns at the cauldron, and the rota has come round.|Your turn at the simmer. You've brought a cushion this time.} {The rest of the class has gone to lunch.|The potion is making a noise like a contented cat.|The lecturer has left a note that says \"Do not leave it.\"}\n> left: Sit the whole watch\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Three hours, one cauldron, one hourglass. You see it turn and catch it at the right moment.|You read the same paragraph forty times and stir at every bubble.|You sit the watch on your cushion, as the note says. The potion turns, and you're there.} {season == \"summer\": The laboratory is stifling.|By the end, you can tell its mood from the bubbles.}\n> right: Set a charm to watch it\n  challenge enchant standard:\n    win:\n      study alchemy +tiny\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {Your charm rings like a bell when the colour turns. You come back from lunch in time to take it off the fire.|The charm works and you get to eat.|You set the charm and leave a note of your own under the lecturer's: \"I didn't, technically.\" It works.}\n    lose:\n      alchemy +tiny\n      health -small\n      say: {Your charm falls asleep. The potion boils over and you breathe in a lot of steam getting it off the fire.|The charm rings for no reason, three times, and the fourth time you ignore it. That was the real one: you get a faceful of steam pulling the cauldron off the fire.|The charm watches it perfectly until the lecturer's note blows onto it. Then it watches the note.}\n\n@event alch_volatile_reagents\ntitle: Volatile Reagents\npool: alchemy_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: tense\nart: potion\ntags: danger\nscene: A hushed laboratory, a student holding a glass vial of glowing liquid at arm's length, everyone else very still behind a screen.\ntext: {Advanced Alchemy handles reagents that react to being looked at the wrong way. The lecturer has one rule. One person speaks at a time, and that person is the one holding the vial.|Nobody is speaking. Somebody is breathing too loudly.|You've stopped holding your breath, mostly.} {Today's vial is pale yellow and fizzing.|Today's vial is dark red and perfectly still, which is worse.|Today's vial is labelled in the lecturer's hand: \"Gently.\"}\n> left: Handle it yourself\n  challenge brew standard+1 steady:\n    win:\n      alchemy +medium\n      say: {You measure it drop by drop, speaking only the numbers. It settles exactly as it should.|Your hands don't shake.|You handle it gently, as labelled, and it behaves for you. The lecturer takes the label off and keeps it.} The lecturer lets out a breath.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The vial pops like a cork. Your eyebrows survive; your gloves do not.|A drop too many. The flask hisses and coughs out blue smoke, and you spend the hour in the infirmary.|You handle it gently, which turns out not to be gently enough. The label lands in your hair.}\n> right: Pass it to the steadiest hand\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You hand the vial to someone with steadier hands and take careful notes. It stings a little.|You watch someone else do it right. You'll know how next time.|You pass the \"Gently\" vial on. The steady hand drops it, very gently, into the sink. Everyone breathes again.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says you're allowed to be cautious.|You note every step.}\n\n@event alch_original_remedy\ntitle: A Remedy of Your Own\npool: alchemy_class\nlevel: 3\nlesson: infirmary\ntone: warm\nart: potion\ntags: study\nscene: A student at a cluttered bench writing a recipe in a notebook, a small flask of their own remedy steaming beside it.\ntext: {Advanced Alchemy asks for an original remedy, invented, written up, and tested under Sister Amaranth's eye.|Another revision. The notebook has more crossings-out than words.|Your remedy is nearly finished. It has been nearly finished for weeks.} {This version is for headaches.|This version is for the common cold, which has resisted remedies for centuries.|This version is for exam nerves, which you need.}\n> left: Test it on yourself\n  challenge brew standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      alchemy +medium\n      say: {It works. Your headache lifts and your mood with it.|It works better than you expected.|It works. Your hands stop shaking, and for an afternoon so does your dread of exams.} {flag(remedy_in_book): Sister Amaranth adds a note to your page in her book.|Sister Amaranth writes your recipe in her own book.}\n      flag remedy_in_book\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {It does something, but not what you wanted. Your ears are warm for the rest of the day.|It cures the sniffles and replaces them with hiccups.|It calms your nerves so thoroughly that you fall asleep at the bench.}\n> right: Ask Sister Amaranth to review it\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {She crosses out three ingredients and one adjective.|She reads it twice, then hands it back. \"Sound,\" she says. \"Dull.\"|She reads it and takes one dose herself, before the exam she's invigilating. \"Adequate,\" she says, calmly.} {final_year: You'll keep this recipe long after you leave Lumenhold.|It's better already, and you hate it a little.}\n> up[purify]: Distill the base first\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You distill the base to its strength before you start, and pay for it with a yawn. The remedy comes out clear and gentle.|The base comes down to one clean drop, and you feel the drop in your knees. Sister Amaranth smells it and says nothing, which is approval.|With the base distilled, the fault in your recipe finally shows. You fix it, a little light-headed.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_b.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): course mornings for\n#  Illusion & Glamour, Beastcraft & Flight and Umbral Theory.\n#  Repeatable routine events, drawn once per enrolled course a day.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Illusion & Glamour (track: illusion) ----------------\n\n@pool illusion_class\nfrom: course_routine_repeat4\ntrack: illusion\n\n@event illu_candle_drill\ntitle: The Candle Drill\npool: illusion_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: light\nart: candle\ntags: magic\nscene: A row of unlit candles on a lecture bench, each crowned with a flame that casts no shadow, a professor holding her palm over one.\ntext: {Illusion I opens with a row of cold candles. Make them burn, Professor Vey says, without burning.|Vey's drawer of candles is open. Every one is older than you.|The candle drill. Some mornings you could do it asleep.} {Vey passes her hand through each flame and says \"cold\" in the same flat voice.|Someone's flame leans in a draught only it can feel.|The student beside you has made a lovely candle and forgotten the flame.}\n> left: Hold the flame until it's right\n  study illusion +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {Your flame steadies, then flickers in time with your breathing, which Vey says is the point.|Vey's palm hovers over your flame a moment longer than the others. \"Almost warm,\" she says. From her, that's a sonnet.|By the bell your flame has a proper blue heart. You feel oddly proud of something that isn't there.}\n> right: Make it gutter and smoke\n  challenge deceive using charm standard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You add a thread of smoke and a little gutter. The student behind you reaches to pinch it out.|Your candle drips wax that isn't there onto a desk that is. Vey watches it fall and says nothing, which means yes.|Your candle gutters and smokes so well that the student beside you lends it their flame. Vey writes \"two for one\".}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The smoke goes up and the flame goes out, as though embarrassed by its own ambition.|Vey waves a hand through the whole performance. \"A candle doesn't try this hard,\" she says.|You add so much smoke the drill becomes a fog. Vey opens a window that isn't there, and the fog obligingly leaves.}\n\n@event illu_one_small_change\ntitle: One Small Change\npool: illusion_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: warm\nart: mirror\ntags: magic\nscene: Students bent over little hand mirrors, one changing the colour of her own eyes, another squinting at a nose that isn't quite his.\ntext: {Hand mirrors all round. Glamour your own face, Vey says; change one small thing and hold it.|Out come the mirrors. A glamour you can't hold on yourself, Vey says, won't hold on anyone.|Mirror practice. You know your own face too well by now.} {Vey says people always change their eyes first.|A second-year from Rootward is giving himself a better chin, slowly.|The mirrors fog, as they do {season == \"winter\": in the cold|when the room warms}.}\n> left: Change something trivial\n  study illusion +tiny\n  say: {You move a freckle half an inch. It takes the whole hour. Vey initials your page without looking up.|You darken one eyebrow and keep it dark. Dull, careful work, and the glamour holds.|A small scar vanishes and comes back, vanishes and comes back. By the end it stays gone until you let it.}\n> right: Change what you'd really change\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {It holds beautifully. You look at it longer than the exercise requires, and letting it go is the hard part. The mirror hands back the face you came in with.|Vey glances over your shoulder. \"Ah,\" she says, kindly enough. \"That one. Everybody has one.\" When the hour ends you give it back, and the old face comes back with the old feeling.|You hold it all hour. Afterwards your real face seems slightly unfamiliar, like a coat you've lent out, and you don't like it any better for the rest.}\n\n@event illu_spot_the_coin\ntitle: Spot the Coin\npool: illusion_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: social\ncast:\n  partner: any where tagged(partner, student)\nscene: Two students at a bench with three coins between them, one coin shimmering very slightly at the edge.\ntext: {Partner work. {partner} lays out three crowns, and one of them is an illusion. Say which.|Three crowns from {partner}. One isn't there, and {they} look{s} pleased about it.|You and {partner} have started keeping score.} {Vey walks the room flicking real coins, to confuse everyone.|The windows are bright and the illusions show it.|The next bench is arguing whether a coin can be \"partly fake\".}\n> left: Study the coins honestly\n  study illusion +tiny\n  rel partner +tiny\n  say: {It's the one with no tarnish. Illusions always come out cleaner than money does.|You stare until your eyes water and pick the middle one. Wrong. {partner} pockets the real two, laughing.|You get it on the third try, by the shine that doesn't move when the light does.}\n> right: Watch their face, not the coins\n  challenge deceive using charm standard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      rel partner +tiny\n      say: {{partner} glances at the left coin a fraction too fondly. You point. {They} groan{s}.|You keep smiling until {partner} gives it away by smiling back. Vey notes that this isn't the exercise, and that it worked.|You know {partner}'s face by now. {They} know{s} you know, and pick{s} a coin with a straight face to beat you. It doesn't work.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {{partner} has a better face than you'd thought. Your wrong guess buys {them} a cup of tea.|You watch {partner} so hard you miss the coin blinking out of existence entirely.|{partner} has been practising a blank face in the mirror. It shows. You lose a point and buy the tea.}\n\n@event illu_bread_smell\ntitle: The Smell of Bread\npool: illusion_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: light\nart: meal\ntags: magic\nscene: A lecture bench with an illusory loaf of bread on a board, steam rising from it, and several students sniffing it doubtfully.\ntext: {Illusion II adds the other senses. Today, a loaf that looks, smells and sounds fresh when broken.|More bread. Sight is easy, Vey says; smell is where liars are caught.|The bread lesson. The floor smells of loaves that never existed.} {Somebody's loaf smells strongly of wet dog.|One loaf crackles beautifully and smells of nothing.|{gpa >= 3: Vey asks you to go first.|Vey asks you to go last, which feels pointed.}}\n> left: Get the smell right\n  study illusion +tiny\n  say: {You spend an hour on the smell of a crust. By the end it's nearly convincing and you're desperately hungry.|You think of a bakery on a cold street and the smell arrives with it. Vey sniffs, and nods once.|The smell is right. The sound of breaking is wrong. Vey says that's the usual order.}\n> right: Add the warmth of the oven\n  challenge enchant using charm standard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {Vey picks your loaf up and puts it down hastily, as if it had burnt her. The class actually applauds.|The bench beside you swears the room got warmer. It didn't. That's the point.|You add the warmth of the oven and the smell of the morning after. Vey takes your loaf home, she claims, by mistake.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The warmth arrives and the loaf leaves. You're left holding a hot, empty board.|Your bread steams, then smokes, then smells of a cupboard. Vey writes something short.|You add warmth, and the smell goes with it. The loaf is perfect and hot and smells of absolutely nothing.}\n\n@event illu_find_vey\ntitle: Find the Professor\npool: illusion_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: light\nart: mask\ntags: magic\nscene: A lecture hall where the professor's chair is empty and everyone is looking suspiciously at everyone else, including a pigeon on the sill.\ntext: {Professor Vey is rarely at the lectern in Illusion II. She is somewhere in the room, wearing someone else, and the first to name her wins.|Vey's chair is empty. Everyone eyes the porter with the mop.|The lectern is empty. You trust nothing in this room.} {There's a pigeon on the sill.|A student nobody remembers has taken the back row.|One of the portraits seems unusually attentive.}\n> left: Watch everyone, all lesson\n  study illusion +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You don't find her, but you notice who blinks late. Vey turns out to have been the pigeon.|At the end she steps out of the porter's coat. You'd suspected the mop. The mop was real.|You learn three classmates' nervous habits and nothing about Vey, who was the attentive portrait all along.}\n> right: Name her early\n  challenge deceive using charm standard+2:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You name her before the bell has finished ringing. She says, \"Hm,\" which from Vey means a great deal.|\"Professor,\" you say to the new student in the back row. The glamour falls away like a dropped shawl. Vey looks almost pleased.|You name the attentive portrait. The paint slides off Vey's face like a curtain. \"Hm,\" she says.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {You accuse a perfectly ordinary classmate. He will remember it. So will Vey, who was standing behind you.|You accuse the quiet student in the back row, who turns out to be a quiet student in the back row. Vey was the mop.|You point at the portrait. The portrait points back. Vey, at the lectern after all, makes a note.}\n\n@event illu_courtyard_hour\ntitle: Holding the Courtyard\npool: illusion_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: tense\nart: sparkle\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A student standing very still in a busy courtyard, sweat on their brow, while a flock of illusory swallows circles above the crowd.\ntext: {Advanced Illusion leaves the classroom. Hold an illusion over the busy courtyard for an hour, and don't let it fray.|Another courtyard hour. The porters step round you now.|The courtyard. {final_year: One of the last times you'll hold this sky.|Your hands know the weight of it.}} {A flock of swallows today.|Rain that doesn't wet anyone.|{season == \"winter\": Snow that never lands.|{season == \"spring\": Blossom that never lands.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves that never land.|Thistledown that never lands.}}}}\n> left: Hold it the full hour\n  charm +small\n  health -small\n  say: {By the end your head rings like a struck bell. Nobody looked up once, which is how you know it worked.|A first-year holds out a hand to catch something you made. You keep it falling for her until the bell.|You hold it, then sit on the fountain's edge for longer than you'd like anyone to see.}\n> right: Keep it small and steady\n  study illusion +tiny\n  say: {You keep only a corner of it, perfect, for an hour. Vey says ambition is cheap and an hour is not.|You shrink it to a patch of weather over one bench. It never frays, and nobody notices, which is the point and the pity.|You keep it small: a few flakes over one bench, falling for the hour. A first-year sits under it and doesn't know why she's happy.}\n\n@event illu_original_work\ntitle: An Illusion of Your Own\npool: illusion_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: warm\nart: eye\ntags: magic\nscene: A darkened studio room, a student alone at the centre, and around them the half-formed image of a lit window at dusk.\ntext: {Original work, an illusion nobody taught you. Vey asks one question of each piece. \"What does it make people feel?\"|More original work. Vey's question hasn't changed; your answers get longer.|Studio morning. {final_year: Next year, Vey says, she'll ask someone else.|Vey goes round the room asking her question.}} {A classmate has made a door that sighs.|Someone has made the smell of rain on hot stone.|Someone's illusion is humming.}\n> left: Make something true\n  study illusion +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You make the window of a house you grew up near, at dusk. Vey stands in front of it a long time and writes nothing down.|You make a corridor that remembers who walked down it. It's sadder than you meant. Vey says that's usually the good sign.|You make the hum of a kitchen at night, kettle cooling. Vey sits down in front of it, which she never does.}\n> right: Make something dazzling\n  challenge enchant using charm standard+1 steady:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      renown +small\n      say: {A staircase of light climbs into the ceiling and keeps going. The class goes quiet. \"Yes,\" says Vey. \"But what does it feel like?\"|Your piece fills the room with lanterns, one for each person watching. Vey counts them, and finds one for herself.|Your piece hums and blazes and then, at the end, goes very quiet and small. Vey writes \"that last part\" in her notes.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {It's dazzling for a breath and then empty, like a fair after the fair has gone.|Vey watches it all, politely. \"Very bright,\" she says. You'd have preferred anything else.|It hums, and blazes, and Vey waits politely for it to stop. \"And when it's over?\" she asks. You don't have an answer.}\n\n# ---------------- Beastcraft & Flight (track: beastcraft) ----------------\n\n@pool beastcraft_class\nfrom: course_routine_repeat4\ntrack: beastcraft\n\n@event beast_mucking_out\ntitle: Mucking Out\npool: beastcraft_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: light\nart: paw\ntags: nature\nscene: A student with a pitchfork in a straw-strewn stable, a hippogriff watching from its stall with a look of deep judgement.\ntext: {Beastcraft I begins in the stables, with a pitchfork. Professor Brask says you don't touch a creature until you've cleaned up after it.|Pitchforks. Brask still hasn't said when that rule ends.|The stables, early. You know which stall to start with.} {The hippogriff in the end stall is watching you.|Owls shift in the rafters, unimpressed.|{season == \"summer\": The heat doubles the smell.|A cold wind finds every gap.}}\n> left: Muck out properly\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Two stalls, a barrow and a blister. Brask inspects your work and calls the straw \"nearly decent\".|You finish before anyone else. The hippogriff treads straight through your clean straw, deliberately.|Brask says nothing, which you've learned is the best review she gives humans.}\n> right: Bow to the hippogriff first\n  challenge feat standard:\n    win:\n      study beastcraft +tiny\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You bow low and hold it. The hippogriff considers, then bows back. Brask writes your name on the board, without comment.|The hippogriff lets you near enough to see where its feathers turn to fur. You'll remember the exact line.|You bow to the hippogriff in the end stall, as you always do now. It bows back before you've finished.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {You bow too fast. The hippogriff takes it as a challenge and accepts.|The hippogriff looks at your bow, then at your hat, and shoves you into the barrow to get at it. It eats the hat.|You bow and the hippogriff ignores you entirely, which is worse than the hat. You muck out under its scorn.}\n\n@event beast_hover_line\ntitle: The Hover Line\npool: beastcraft_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: light\nart: broom\ntags: danger\nscene: A line of students hovering a foot above a lawn on brooms, one broom drifting out of line, a stern professor counting slowly.\ntext: {Flight is half the course. Brask lines you up on the lawn to hover a foot up and hold still while she counts.|The hover line. Brask counts slowly on purpose, you're sure.|Hover line. You and your broom have an understanding, of sorts.} {Somebody's broom keeps edging toward the lake.|An owl has landed on the end of a broom, and its rider is pretending not to notice.|Brask says, as always, that brooms are harder to reason with than griffons.}\n> left: Hold still until the count\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You hold it until forty. Your legs remember it for a day.|The broom bucks once at thirty-one. You hold on. Brask gets to fifty and nods.|Steady all the way. Brask says it's nice to see a broom that knows who's in charge, and she means the broom.}\n> right: Study how the others fall off\n  study beastcraft +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You stand at the end and watch. You learn a great deal about balance, and nobody lets you forget you didn't fly.|You take notes on every wobble. Brask reads them and says you should consider a career in inquests.|You watch the owl on the broom-end win the hover line. Brask writes down the owl's name.}\n\n@event beast_feeding_round\ntitle: Feeding Round\npool: beastcraft_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: warm\nart: feather\ntags: nature\ncast:\n  partner: any where tagged(partner, student)\nscene: Two students carrying buckets between stalls, a sulky griffon chick in a nest of straw, owls leaning down from the rafters.\ntext: {Feeding round, in pairs. You and {partner} have two buckets and a list, and the griffon chick is first on it.|Feeding round with {partner}. The griffon chick has heard the buckets.|You and {partner} could do the round blindfolded now. The chick would still sulk.} {Brask has written \"DO NOT HURRY\" at the top of the list.|The owls lean down from the rafters to watch.|{partner} is humming to the buckets.}\n> left: Take the griffon chick\n  challenge feat standard wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      rel partner +tiny\n      say: {The chick eats from the bucket, then from your hand, then tries your sleeve. {partner} applauds quietly.|It sulks, then eats, then sulks about having eaten. Brask says that's progress for a griffon.|You and {partner} feed the chick in turns, humming. It sulks, but eats in rhythm.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {The chick bites the bucket, then the hand holding it. {partner} finishes the round alone, kindly.|It knocks the bucket over, and you with it, and sits in it, glaring. You spend the hour, bruised and soaked, fishing out its breakfast.|{partner}'s humming puts the chick in a mood. It takes the mood out on your hand.}\n> right: Take the owls instead\n  study beastcraft +tiny\n  rel partner +tiny\n  say: {You learn the owls' names from the tags on their perches, and what a pellet is, in detail.|Up and down the ladder for an hour. {partner} survives the chick and is grateful in a quiet way.|The owls take their mice with great ceremony. One of them seems to be counting yours.}\n\n@event beast_hare_paddock\ntitle: Running with Hares\npool: beastcraft_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: light\nart: bolt\ntags: magic\nscene: Students sprinting across a paddock alongside a scattering of brown hares, one student's feet suddenly much faster than the rest of them.\ntext: {Beastcraft II teaches haste the old way. Run with the hares until their speed rubs off. \"Borrow it,\" Brask says. \"Don't steal it.\"|The hare paddock. The hares remember you.|Hares. You dream in sudden right angles now.} {The grass is wet and the hares don't care.|Brask times you with a crooked hourglass.|{season == \"summer\": It's hot and the hares are lazy.|The paddock smells of rain.}}\n> left: Run until it catches\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  say: {For three strides you're faster than you've any right to be. Then you're in the hedge.|You catch it on the fourth lap, a lightness behind the knees. You pay for it with a nap you can't refuse.|The hares let you keep up for a moment, out of curiosity. It's the best moment of your week.}\n> right: Watch the hares and take notes\n  study beastcraft +tiny\n  say: {You sketch the way a hare turns, everything at once, no warning. Brask says your sketch is better than your running.|You note how the hares rest between bursts. Brask reads it and says, \"Yes. That's the half everyone forgets.\"|You sketch the hares in sudden right angles. Brask pins your page up by the paddock gate.}\n\n@event beast_complaints\ntitle: The Complaints Hour\npool: beastcraft_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: light\nart: owl\ntags: nature\nscene: A student sitting on an upturned bucket in a stable, surrounded by animals who all seem to be talking at once.\ntext: {Beast-speech practice. You sit on a bucket in the stables and listen. Every creature has been waiting to complain, and the owls start with the draught.|Another hour on the bucket. The owls open, as always, with the draught.|The bucket, the owls, the draught. Brask calls it fieldwork.} {The hippogriff wants different hay. It is very specific.|The griffon chick has a list.|{gpa >= 3: Brask leaves you alone with them, which feels like trust.|Brask stays near the door, which feels like supervision.}}\n> left: Hear every complaint out\n  study beastcraft +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {The owls talk for forty minutes about the draught. You understand every word now, which is the trouble.|The hippogriff explains its views on hay at length. You come away fluent and exhausted.|A mouse you didn't know was there wants to be on the record. You put it on the record.}\n> right: Fix the draught yourself\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You climb into the rafters with a hammer and a plank. The owls supervise. The draught stops, and so do the complaints, briefly.|You patch the gap with straw and a board. The owls say thank you, then mention the window.|You fix the draught. The owls thank you, then move on to the window, the hay and the mouse, in order.}\n\n@event beast_lake_flight\ntitle: Over the Lake\npool: beastcraft_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: tense\nlesson: mirror_lake\nart: feather\ntags: danger\nscene: A student on a hippogriff's back skimming low over a mirror-still lake, their reflection racing along beneath them.\ntext: {Advanced Beastcraft. A hippogriff each, over the lake, no saddle unless you ask. Brask won't think less of you for asking. She'll just remember.|The lake flight. Your hippogriff knows the way.|Over the lake. {final_year: You catch yourself memorising the view.|Your reflection keeps pace.}} {The water is perfectly still.|A barge below, its passengers waving.|{season == \"summer\": The air is full of midges.|The wind off the water stings.}}\n> left: Ride bareback, low over the water\n  challenge feat standard+1:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You skim so low a wingtip draws a line across the lake. For one breath you and your reflection touch hands.|The hippogriff banks and you go with it, as if you'd always known how. Brask says nothing at all, then grins.|You fly low enough for your reflection to keep pace, wingtip to wingtip. You don't want to land.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The hippogriff dips and you don't. The lake is colder than it looks. It always is.|You slide off on the turn and hang on by a fistful of feathers, all the way back to shore.|You race your reflection and your reflection wins. You surface laughing, and very cold.}\n> right: Take the saddle and the high circuit\n  study beastcraft +tiny\n  say: {Saddled, high and steady. You see all seven towers at once, and nobody sees you.|The safe circuit, twice. Brask remembers, as promised, and nods at you all the same.|You take the saddle and the high circuit, and look down at the lake as if memorising it. Brask lets you take a second lap.}\n\n@event beast_manticore_rounds\ntitle: Manticore Rounds\npool: beastcraft_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: tense\nart: paw\ntags: danger\nscene: A manticore lying in straw with its eyes half-closed, one student gently holding its scorpion tail while another hums nearby.\ntext: {Advanced students check the manticore once a month. Someone holds the tail; someone hums the lullaby. Brask lets you choose.|Manticore rounds. It knows the lullaby better than you do.|The manticore yawns at you, which is either a welcome or a threat.} {Its tail twitches in its sleep.|It has been off its food, Brask says, and is in a mood.|{final_year: You wonder who'll hold the tail after you.|Brask has brought a bucket of meat and a smaller one of patience.}}\n> left: Hold the tail\n  challenge feat standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {You hold the tail still while Brask checks the sting. It's warm and heavy and does not, at any point, stab you.|The manticore lets you hold its tail the way a cat lets you hold a paw. Under protest, briefly, as a favour.|You hold the tail while Brask feeds it from the bucket. It eats from her hand and watches you the whole time.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {The tail flicks. You let go just in time, mostly. Sister Amaranth asks no questions; she's seen this course before.|It rolls over, tail and all, onto you. Nothing breaks, but you're bruised in the exact shape of a manticore.|It's in a mood, and the tail knows. You let go a moment too late and spend an hour with Sister Amaranth.}\n> right: Hum the lullaby\n  study beastcraft +tiny\n  say: {You hum the whole lullaby twice, off-key on the high bit. The manticore sleeps through its own check-up.|You hum. A classmate holds the tail. Brask says the humming is the harder job, and nobody believes her.|You hum while Brask brings the meat. The manticore eats, slowly, and for once the mood lifts.}\n\n# ---------------- Umbral Theory (track: umbral) ----------------\n\n@pool umbral_class\nfrom: course_routine_repeat4\ntrack: umbral\n\n@event umbral_dim_lamps\ntitle: Reading by Shadow\npool: umbral_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: eerie\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery\nscene: A dim lecture room at the top of a tower, objects hidden under cloths on a table, their shadows thrown long on the wall behind.\ntext: {Umbral Theory I, top of the tower, lamps low on purpose. Name what's under each cloth by its shadow alone.|The cloths are back. The lamps are as low as ever.|Shadow reading. The dark no longer seems strange, which the professor says is the first lesson.} {One shadow is slightly larger than its object.|The professor moves the lamp an inch, and every answer changes.|{season == \"summer\": It's warm outside, never up here.|Rain on the windows makes the shadows shiver.}}\n> left: Read them patiently\n  study umbral +tiny\n  say: {A teapot, a skull, a folded letter addressed to you. You open it later. It's blank.|You name every shadow but one. Under that cloth there is nothing at all. The professor doesn't explain.|Slowly, you get them all. The shadows told you, the professor says; you only had to stop interrupting.}\n> right: Slip in a false shadow\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: {You add the shadow of a cat to the wall. Three classmates write \"cat\". The professor writes your name, and underlines it.|Your false shadow passes all morning. The professor only catches it when it doesn't move with the lamp.|Your false shadow shivers with the rain like the real ones. Nobody finds it. The professor shakes your hand, which is cold.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {Your false shadow points the wrong way. The professor turns it round gently, like straightening a picture.|Your shadow-cat sits up and leaves. It wasn't meant to do that. The professor doesn't seem surprised.|The rain makes every shadow shiver except yours. The professor notices the one that keeps still.}\n\n@event umbral_chalk_ward\ntitle: Pale Shells\npool: umbral_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: light\nart: shield\ntags: magic\nscene: Students behind faint, pale bubbles of light as a professor flicks pieces of chalk at them from the front of a dim room.\ntext: {Ward drill, in pairs. One of you raises a pale shell over the other, and the professor lets the lamp-shadows loose along the floor to find the gap.|Pairs again. The professor's shadows have learned new tricks since last time.|The paired drill. You know how the shadows like to come: low, and from behind.} {Your partner's shell keeps flickering.|Your partner is a first-year who has never held a ward for more than a minute.|{gpa >= 3: The professor pairs you with the weakest student, on purpose.|The professor pairs you with someone as nervous as you are.}}\n> left: Hold the shell over both of you\n  study umbral +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {You stretch your shell over your partner as well. The shadows nose along its edge for an hour and find nothing. You feel every touch in your teeth.|Two people under one ward is twice the weight. Yours holds until the bell, just.|The shadows give up on your shell and go after the next pair. The professor nods at you, once.}\n> right: Coach your partner through theirs\n  guile +small\n  say: {You talk your partner through it, breath by breath. Their shell holds for nine minutes, then a shadow slips under it and makes you both jump. \"Teaching,\" says the professor, \"is also a ward.\"|You keep your own shell down and watch for the gaps in theirs. By the end, they've stopped flickering. You've learned where shadows like to get in.|You coach your nervous partner, and you're nervous too. The shadows come low and from behind; you both turn in time.}\n\n@event umbral_quiet_minutes\ntitle: The Quiet Minutes\npool: umbral_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: eerie\nart: candle\ntags: mystery\nscene: A circle of students sitting perfectly still in near darkness, a single candle in the middle, everyone listening very hard.\ntext: {Each lesson opens with ten minutes of silence. \"Listen to what's hidden,\" the professor says, and puts out every lamp but one.|The quiet minutes. You can hear the tower settle, stone by stone.|Ten minutes of silence. You look forward to it, and also not.} {Someone's stomach rumbles at the worst moment.|The candle leans toward the door, though nothing comes in.|{season == \"summer\": A bee is lost in the room somewhere.|The wind hums in the stairwell.}}\n> left: Listen properly\n  study umbral +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You hear the tower breathing, which it shouldn't, and a sigh on the stair that nobody makes.|You hear the lake, which is impossible from up here. The professor says that's normal for this room. It is not reassuring.|In the silence you hear someone you miss, very faintly. When the lamps come up, you don't mention it.}\n> right: Quietly finish other homework\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: {You finish a whole essay by feel in the dark. The professor never notices, or never says.|Not a scratch of the quill. When the lamps come up, your essay is done and your face is innocent.|You write in the dark with the wind humming the rhythm. The essay is done when the lamps come up.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {When the lamps come up the professor is behind you, reading your essay. \"Your third paragraph is weak,\" she says.|Your quill squeaks. In that silence it sounds like a scream. Everyone turns.|A gust slams the stairwell door and you drop your quill. It rolls, loudly, to the professor's feet.}\n\n@event umbral_short_distances\ntitle: Short Distances\npool: umbral_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: tense\nart: ghost\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A student stepping into the shadow under one desk and halfway out of the shadow under another, the room's lamps burning low.\ntext: {Umbral Theory II brings the shadowstep, desk to desk. \"The shadows in between are not friendly,\" the professor says, \"so don't linger.\"|Shadowstep drill. Three desks this time, then four.|The desks. You know the way between them, and the cold that lives there.} {Someone comes out under the wrong desk.|The lamps are lower than usual.|{gpa >= 3: The professor sets you the long way round.|The professor sets you the short way, and watches.}}\n> left: Step across\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      say: {In, through, out. It takes no time and lasts forever. You come out under the far desk with frost on your sleeves.|Four desks, clean. Something in between brushes your ankle, curious, and lets you pass.|You take your set route and come out exactly where the professor pointed. The cold in between nods at you, almost.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You come out one desk short and somewhat upside down. The cold stays in your fingers until lunch.|You linger, just for a moment. It's enough. You come back pale, and the professor sits you by the one warm lamp.|The professor's route is longer than you'd like. You come out at the end, but so does the cold, with you.}\n> right: Map the route on paper first\n  study umbral +tiny\n  say: {You draw every shadow in the room, and where it goes at each hour. The professor keeps your map.|You work out the route in ink and walk it once, slowly, in the light. It's dull, and correct.|You map the set route desk by desk. The professor checks your map and adds one shadow you'd missed.}\n\n@event umbral_hidden_object\ntitle: Present but Unseen\npool: umbral_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: eerie\nart: key\ntags: mystery\nscene: A dim classroom being searched by several students at once while one of them sits calmly at a desk with a small brass key in plain sight.\ntext: {Hide a small key in this room, present but unseen. \"Hiding is not removal,\" the professor says. \"Removal is for amateurs.\"|The key, and somewhere to hide it. Your classmates are better at finding things.|The key exercise. Half the class pats their pockets out of habit.} {The key is brass today.|The key is silver and very cold.|Last time a key hid in a shadow for two days.}\n> left: Hide it, and stay calm\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You leave the key on the desk in plain sight, in your own shadow. Nobody sees it. The professor smiles, which is rarer than a key.|You hide it in the dark behind your own ear. They search the room twice and never once look at you.|You hide the key in a shadow and leave it there for two days, like the last one. Nobody finds it. The professor does, and approves.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {A first-rate hiding place, found in under a minute by someone who simply asked the shadow.|You hide it so well you can't find it yourself. The professor retrieves it from the wall with a sigh.|You hide it in a shadow, and the shadow moves with the afternoon. By the bell the key is in the corridor.}\n> right: Search for everyone else's\n  study umbral +tiny\n  say: {You find four keys, one in a hat, one in a hinge, one in a shadow that didn't want to let go.|You find one key and a pencil nobody has missed for years. The professor counts both.|You find the key that hid for two days. It's still in its shadow, and you have to ask it nicely to come out.}\n\n@event umbral_stair_wards\ntitle: The Old Stair Wards\npool: umbral_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: eerie\nart: shield\ntags: mystery\nscene: A student kneeling on an ancient spiral stair, hands pressed to a faint ring of light set into the stone.\ntext: {Advanced students renew the small wards on the tower's old stair. Some are newer than the stone. Some, the professor says, are much older.|The stair wards. You know which step hums.|The stair. {final_year: Someone else will kneel here next year.|Your knees know every step.}} {One ward is warm, as if someone's just left.|Something beyond a ward leans back when you touch it.|The stair lamps are mostly a suggestion.}\n> left: Renew them carefully\n  study umbral +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {Step by step, ring by ring. Each ward takes a little of you and hums.|You renew eleven and pause at the twelfth. Leave that one, the professor says, and doesn't say whose it is.|At the top you're light-headed and the wards are bright. Something on the other side has gone quiet.}\n> right: Study the oldest ward instead\n  guile +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You trace the oldest ring with one finger. The pattern folds in on itself like a knot nobody was meant to untie, and you feel watched for noticing even that.|The old ward is cold, careful work, made by someone who was afraid. You can feel the fear still in the stone.|You study the oldest ward by your hands alone, the lamps being no help. It's older than the stair. You're sure of that much.}\n\n@event umbral_errand\ntitle: The Shadow's Errand\npool: umbral_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: eerie\nart: moon\ntags: magic mystery\nscene: A student standing in lamplight with no shadow at all, while across the room their shadow lifts a book from a shelf.\ntext: {Original work. Send your own shadow on an errand across the room, the professor says, and have it come back. \"It will want to wander. They all do.\"|Shadow errands. Yours picks its own route now.|Errand day. {final_year: Does your shadow know you're leaving soon?|Your shadow seems eager, which worries you.}} {Someone's shadow is still under a desk from last week.|The professor's own shadow is missing today.|The lamps are low and the shadows long.}\n> left: Send it, and trust it\n  challenge deceive standard+1:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {Your shadow crosses the room, lifts a book, and brings it back open at a page you needed. You didn't ask it to.|It comes back with a feather from the rafters, which wasn't the errand. The professor counts it as initiative.|Your shadow goes out long and comes back short, carrying a stub of candle. You don't know where it got it.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {It comes back an hour late and a little the wrong shape. For a day it lags behind you on stairs, sulking, and you can't get warm.|It doesn't come back until dusk. When it does, it smells of the lake. The professor makes you sit with the lamp until your hands stop shaking with cold.|It stretches out under the low lamps and doesn't want to come back. When it does, it's longer than you for a day.}\n> right: Keep it on a short leash\n  study umbral +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {Your shadow goes as far as the window and no further. It looks out a long time before it comes back.|Two yards and home. Dull, safe and precise. The professor says precision is how shadow-workers get old.|You keep it close under the low lamps. It strains toward the long shadows and comes back to you, reluctantly.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_hidden.grim","text":"# Baseline course-morning pools, group courses_hidden (docs/authoring/pools.md):\n# the three hidden courses, Artifice, Starcalling and Hollow Studies. Repeatable routine events, two each at level I.\n\n# ---------------- Artifice (track: artifice, skills alchemy arcana; Mir's Workshop) ----------------\n\n@event art_escapement_drill\ntitle: The Ticking Hour\ntrack: artifice\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: class study\nscene: A cluttered workshop bench covered in tiny brass gears, files and loupes, a row of half-built clockwork hearts ticking out of time with one another.\ntext: {Artifice begins with a heart, a fist of clockwork that must tick evenly before it is allowed to think.|Your clockwork heart is waiting where you left it, sulking at the wrong speed.|You can hear your own heart on the bench from the doorway now. Its tick is almost yours.} {The bench beside you ticks like hail.|One heart near the door is running backwards, proudly.|The lecturer listens to each bench with eyes closed.} {season == \"winter\": The brass is cold enough to bite.|The workshop smells of solder.}\n> left: File every tooth by hand\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Tooth by tooth, under a loupe, until your eyes water. The tick evens out by the bell.|You file all morning and burn one thumb on the solder. The heart ticks steady and slightly smug.|It's slow, fiddly work, and your hands learn it faster than your head does.}\n> right: Charm the spring to keep time\n  challenge enchant standard:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study artifice +tiny\n      say: {The charm settles into the spring like a cat into a basket. Your heart ticks like a metronome.|You whisper the rhythm into the coil and it keeps it. The lecturer opens one eye, then closes it again.|The charm takes your own pulse as its pattern. The lecturer listens with eyes closed and nods: your heart ticks in time with you.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The spring takes the charm too literally and unwinds all at once, across the room.|Your heart ticks perfectly for a minute, then very fast, then not at all. Someone hands you a fire bucket just in case.|The charm copies your pulse a little too well, and when you get nervous, so does the heart. The lecturer listens, eyes closed, and winces.}\n\n@event art_stirring_cauldron\ntitle: The Cauldron That Stirs\ntrack: artifice\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: class magic\nscene: A copper cauldron on iron legs stirring itself with a long brass arm while students crowd round with notebooks and a wary distance.\ntext: {The class project is a cauldron that stirs itself. It does, mostly, in whatever direction it pleases.|The cauldron has been rebuilt again. It stirs with a new brass arm and the same old temper.|The cauldron recognises you, or behaves as if it does.} {Today it stirs clockwise, then argues.|Today it stirs so fast the porridge in it is climbing out.|Today the arm keeps tapping the rim like an impatient guest.} {final_year: You'll miss its moods, a little.|The fire buckets by the door are full, for once.}\n> left: Recut the runes on the arm\n  study artifice +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You spend the morning with a graver and a magnifier, recutting the stirring runes one stroke at a time. Your fingers are nicked; the arm moves sweetly.|Fine work in brass, the whole lesson long. The arm stirs evenly, and you have solder burns in a neat little row.|The arm taps the rim the whole time you work, as if hurrying you. When the runes are recut it stops tapping and stirs, which may be thanks.}\n> right: Test it on a live brew\n  challenge brew standard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study artifice +tiny\n      say: {The cauldron stirs your brew exactly as the recipe asks, and you take the credit for both.|The brew turns the right colour at the right minute. The cauldron seems to know it did well.|The arm stops tapping the moment your brew goes in, and stirs it perfectly. It seems to like you.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The arm speeds up, the brew climbs the walls, and the whole class steps back as one.|The cauldron stirs your brew into something with the texture of an argument. You scrub it out until the bell.|The arm taps the rim, impatient, then stirs your brew in a temper. You clean the ceiling until the bell.}\n\n# ---------------- Starcalling (track: starcalling, skills lore arcana; the Observatory, at night) ----------------\n\n@event star_names_in_the_dark\ntitle: Names in the Dark\ntrack: starcalling\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: star\ntags: class study\nscene: A dark brass dome open to a sky full of stars, a student at the eyepiece of a great telescope while another recites from memory, breath steaming.\ntext: {Starcalling is taught by appointment, at night, and this morning's note gives you tonight's hour. It begins with names. \"You cannot call what you cannot name.\"|Another note at breakfast, another night appointment under the dome. The list of names is longer than it was.|Tonight's appointment under the dome again. You know where the draught comes in.} {The note warns that the great telescope has been swinging round on its own.|Meteors are expected tonight.|The note is written on the back of a star chart.} {season == \"winter\": Tonight your breath will freeze on the eyepiece.|Tonight the air will smell of cut grass from the lawns below.}\n> left: Recite the names until perfect\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You say them over and over into the cold until they stop being words and start being places.|Name after name, in order and then backwards. By the end you could find them blindfold, and nearly have to.|You stumble on the same name four times. The fifth time it comes out right, and you feel it land somewhere.}\n> right: Let the telescope choose a star\n  challenge research standard-1:\n    win:\n      study starcalling +tiny\n      say: {The telescope settles on a star you've never heard of. You find its name in the old charts before the lecturer can tell you.|Wherever it points, you follow, and the charts agree with it. That's a whole lesson learned the other way round.|You let the telescope choose and check it against the chart on the back of the note. It's right, and so are you.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The telescope picks a star and then changes its mind every ten minutes. You chase it round the dome until midnight.|You spend the night at the eyepiece and come down with a stiff neck and one name you aren't sure of.|The telescope chooses a star that isn't on the chart on the back of the note. You spend the night in the draught, trying to prove one of them wrong.}\n\n@event star_thread_of_light\ntitle: A Thread of Light\ntrack: starcalling\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: sparkle\ntags: class magic\nscene: A student in a dark observatory with hands cupped upward, a single silver thread of starlight running down through the open dome into their palms.\ntext: {Tonight's lesson, says the morning note, is to call down a single thread of a named star's light, no thicker than a hair.|Tonight's lesson is the same as ever: one thread, one star, held as long as you can bear it.|You've called down threads before. The morning note says tonight the lecturer wants it held longer.} {The morning sky is clear, and the night promises to be.|Thin cloud is forecast to cross the star you've chosen.|At breakfast the others are already practising the hand positions.}\n> left: Call the thread down yourself\n  study starcalling +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {The thread comes down cold and bright and pours into your palms. You hold it until your hands shake.|It takes three tries. On the third, the light drops into your hands like water from a great height.|A hair of light, the length of the whole tower. When it fades, your fingers glow for an hour.}\n> right: Keep the night log instead\n  arcana +small\n  say: {You write down every thread the others call, with the star, the minute and how long it held. It's dull, cold work, and you start to see the pattern.|You hold the lamp and the log while the others call. Watching from outside, you see exactly where their hands go wrong.|Tonight the others hold their threads longer, and you note to the second when each one breaks. The pattern is in the breaking.} {season == \"winter\": Your ink keeps freezing.|The lecturer reads your log twice.}\n> up[starlight]: Hold your own starlight up to it\n  study starcalling +tiny\n  say: {You light your own starlight and the thread finds it, like calling to like. It holds twice as long.|Your starlight steadies the thread, and the lecturer has you show the others how.|The thread drops straight into the light you're already holding. For a moment the dome is bright as noon.}\n\n# ---------------- Hollow Studies (track: hollow_studies, skills guile lore; the headmistress, a room without windows) ----------------\n\n@event hollow_sealed_boxes\ntitle: How It Was Sealed\ntrack: hollow_studies\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: lock\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A windowless room lined with black-bound books, a single lamp on a table, a tall calm woman setting a small locked box in front of a student.\ntext: {Each lesson, the headmistress sets a sealed thing in front of you. \"Tell me how it was closed,\" she says. \"Not what is inside. How.\"|Another sealed thing on the table. Today it is {a letter with a wax seal|a small lacquered box|a jar with its lid charmed shut}.|The headmistress places today's sealed thing with a little smile. She has started choosing harder ones.} {The lamp hisses.|The books on the shelves breathe slowly in the dark.|She sits with her hands folded, unhurried.}\n> left: Study the seal for the hour\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You trace every line of it without touching. At the end you can name each step of the sealing, in order. She nods once.|It takes the whole hour and a headache, but you work it out backwards, layer by layer.|You get two layers right and one wrong. She tells you which, kindly, and you'll remember.}\n> right: Open it without leaving a trace\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study hollow_studies +tiny\n      say: {You open it and close it again, and nothing shows. She looks at it, then at you, and laughs, short and delighted.|Open, closed, not a mark. \"Good,\" she says. \"Now never do that to a door.\"|It's a harder one, and it opens anyway, and closes, and shows nothing. She unfolds her hands for the first time all lesson.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The seal cracks with a tiny, final sound. The headmistress writes something in her book. She remembers every mistake, and now there is one more.|It opens, and it shows. \"Everyone breaks one,\" she says pleasantly. \"Rarely so loudly.\"|The harder seals are harder for a reason. It opens with a mark that won't go, and she lets you sit with it, unhurried.}\n\n@event hollow_reading_the_blank\ntitle: Reading the Blank Page\ntrack: hollow_studies\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A student bent over an apparently blank page by lamplight, faint lines of ink surfacing under their fingertips, black-bound books leaning in from the shelves.\ntext: {\"Most things that are hidden are not gone,\" the headmistress says, and gives you a blank page. \"Find what was written. Gently.\"|A new blank page today. It feels warmer than paper should.|You know the weight of hidden writing in a page by now, like a pocket with something in it.} {The books on the nearest shelf seem to lean closer.|The lamp burns low and blue.|Somewhere in the stacks, a book turns its own page.} {final_year: She asks where you'll go after graduation, and listens.|She is reading while you work, and doesn't look up.}\n> left: Coax the ink up slowly\n  study hollow_studies +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {You warm the page with a thread of power, a breath at a time. The writing surfaces slowly, safe and dull. It's someone's laundry list, very carefully hidden.|Line by line, the ink comes up. It's an old timetable. The lesson was in the finding.|It takes most of your strength. The page gives up a single sentence about the weather, decades ago.}\n> right: Read it bare-handed\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The ink comes up fast under your fingers, and so does a chill that stays in your hand until morning.|You read it quickly and well. Your fingertips ache for an hour, and the headmistress hands you a cup of tea without comment.|You find the writing the way you'd find a coin in a pocket, and read it bare-handed. Somewhere in the stacks a page turns, and your fingers are cold until supper.}\n> up[ward]: Let your ward do the reading\n  lore +small\n  say: {Your ward settles over the page like a held breath, and the writing rises into it. The headmistress raises an eyebrow, pleased.|You read through the ward as if through glass. It costs you, but not your hands.|The hidden lines surface inside your shell, one by one, in no hurry.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_more.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools, second pass (2026-10-24): courses_more.\n#  One more repeatable class morning for every course, at the\n#  level its pool was thinnest. Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Evocation II ----------------\n\n@event evo_fog_to_the_line\ntitle: Fog to the Line\npool: evocation_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nart: wave\ntags: magic\nscene: A lecture hall filling slowly with white fog from students' hands, a chalk line drawn on the wall at shoulder height, a stern old professor holding a measuring stick.\ntext: {Professor Thorne chalks a line on the wall at shoulder height. \"Water and fire together. Make fog. To the line, and not a hair over.\"|Fog day. Thorne's chalk line is lower than last time.|The fog line again. Thorne no longer explains; he just draws it and folds his arms.} {The windows have been shut.|Somebody's fog smells of burnt toast.|{season == \"winter\": The fog freezes on the windows in ferns.|The hall is warm, and the fog comes too easily.}}\n> left: Balance fire and water exactly\n  challenge spell standard:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +tiny\n      say: {Your fog rises, thick and white, and stops at the line as if it can read. Thorne looks at it a long time. \"Hm.\"|Your fog fills the hall to the chalk precisely. Thorne makes a small mark in his book; you'd give a crown to know what.|You hold the balance so finely that your fog comes up the wall like a tide and stops. Thorne nods, once. From him, a standing ovation.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {Too much fire. Your fog turns to scalding steam and the front row leaves. Thorne opens a window without comment.|Too much water. A small rain falls on your desk, and only your desk, and on your hands, which are cold for an hour. Thorne writes something down.|You overshoot the line by a head and scald your wrist doing it. Thorne measures the fog with his stick, twice, in silence.}\n> right: Hold the water; let a partner burn\n  mana -small\n  study evocation +tiny\n  say: {You call the water and your partner warms it. Half the credit, half the effort, and a good share of your mana.|You hold a ball of water steady for twenty minutes while your partner fusses with the flame. Your arms shake; your fog is fine.|You're the best water-holder in the class now, which Thorne calls \"a respectable specialism\" in a tone that isn't sure.}\n\n# ---------------- Astromancy I ----------------\n\n@event astro_dream_diary\ntitle: The Dream Diary\npool: astromancy_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nart: moon\ntags: study\nscene: A circle of yawning students under an observatory dome, each holding a small notebook, one standing to read aloud while the others try not to laugh.\ntext: {Every first-year astromancer keeps a dream diary, and once a month you read an entry aloud for the class to interpret. Today it's your turn.|Dream diaries again. The lecturer reminds you that dreams are evidence; the class reminds you they're also very funny.|Your turn with the dream diary. The class leans in; your entries have a following.} {Last night you dreamed about a goat in a crown.|Last night you dreamed your teeth were made of keys.|{season == \"winter\": Last night you dreamed of snow falling upward.|Last night you dreamed of an exam in a language made of birds.}}\n> left: Read the real one aloud\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You read about the goat. The class interprets it as ambition, appetite and possibly a farm. You go pink, and learn more than they do.|You read the dream of key-teeth. The lecturer finds three meanings in it; the class finds four, one of them rude.|You read it straight. The interpretations are thoughtful and humiliating, in equal parts.}\n> right: Invent something impressive\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You describe a dream of a silver hare on a staircase. The lecturer is deeply moved. You must remember to have it someday.|You invent a prophetic dream so convincing that half the class writes it down. The lecturer gives it full marks and a worried look.|Your invented dream gets a round of applause. You almost wish it were true.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {You get the silver hare's symbolism backwards. The lecturer, who keeps a book of real dream symbols, notices, and says so, gently, in front of everyone.|You overdo it. Nobody dreams in footnotes. The class is delighted to say so.|Halfway through, you forget what the hare was doing. The class finishes your dream for you, badly.}\n\n# ---------------- Advanced Alchemy ----------------\n\n@event alch_antidote_race\ntitle: The Antidote Race\npool: alchemy_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nart: potion\nscene: A laboratory bench lined with beakers of clear liquid, each pair of students racing to identify and counter a mild poison while a healer watches an hourglass.\ntext: {Sister Amaranth sets a beaker of clear liquid in front of each pair. \"A mild poison. Identify it. Counter it. Then drink both. Hourglass.\"|Antidote day. Sister Amaranth has made the poisons milder and the hourglass shorter.|The antidote race. You can smell three of Sister Amaranth's poisons from the door now; she has started using a fourth.} {The last pair to finish washes everyone's beakers.|One pair's tongues are already blue.|{season == \"summer\": The heat makes everything evaporate twice as fast.|Rain hammers the windows and nobody can hear the hourglass.}}\n> left: Identify it by testing\n  challenge research standard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study alchemy +tiny\n      say: {Three reagents and one colour change say hiccup-root. You counter it with mint and pepper and drink both before the sand's half gone. \"Keep doing that,\" says Sister Amaranth.|You name it in two tests and brew the counter nearly blindfold. Sister Amaranth watches you drink without a word, which is praise.|You identify the fourth poison, the one she thought nobody would get. She looks at you over the hourglass and adds a fifth to her list.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You guess wrong and drink a counter to the wrong thing. Your tongue is blue until supper.|You run out of sand, drink your best guess and hiccup in iambic rhythm for an hour.|Your antidote works on everything except the poison. Sister Amaranth hands you a second one, the right one, without comment.}\n> right: Brew a broad remedy instead\n  alchemy +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {You skip the testing and brew a remedy that counters most mild poisons, expensively. It works; it takes a lot out of you.|A broad remedy, slow and draining, and very safe. Sister Amaranth notes it as \"a cautious answer\", which is not praise.|Your broad remedy is so reliable now that the other pairs come to borrow it. Brewing it still leaves you hollow.}\n\n# ---------------- Illusion II ----------------\n\n@event illu_turnip_market\ntitle: The Turnip Market\npool: illusion_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: light\nart: mask\ntags: magic\nscene: Students at makeshift market stalls in a lecture hall holding up dazzling jewels that flicker, now and then, into turnips, while a theatrical professor drifts between them with a purse.\ntext: {Professor Vey gives everyone a turnip and a stall. \"Glamour it into something I'll pay for. I have a purse of play-crowns, and no mercy.\"|Turnip market. Vey's purse of play-crowns is fatter this time, and so is her suspicion.|The turnip market again. Vey walks the stalls with her purse and the expression of a duchess at a bad auction.} {Your neighbour's turnip is already a very convincing tiara.|Somebody's ruby is dripping slightly.|{season == \"autumn\": The turnips are fresh from Old Maud's patch and smell of earth.|The hall smells overwhelmingly of turnip.}}\n> left: Make it dazzle\n  challenge spell using charm standard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      study illusion +tiny\n      say: {Your turnip becomes a sapphire the size of an egg. Vey turns it over, sniffs it, and pays three play-crowns. \"It smells of turnip,\" she says. \"I'll allow it.\"|You turn it into a golden pear. Vey bites it, which is cheating, then pays anyway, laughing.|Your turnip becomes a pocket watch that ticks. Vey listens to it and pays you double. \"You made me want it,\" she says. \"That's the whole art.\"}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {Your jewel flickers back into a turnip as Vey reaches for it. The whole hall sees. Vey pays you one play-crown, out of pity.|Your sapphire has roots. Vey points this out, delighted, to everyone.|Your watch ticks, then sprouts leaves. \"Charming,\" says Vey, and moves on.}\n> right: Buy, and catch the fakes\n  guile +small\n  say: {You're given a few play-crowns and set loose as a customer. You catch three turnips in disguise by their smell. Vey nods. \"The other half of the art.\"|You haggle your way down the stalls and catch the dripping ruby out. Its maker sulks.|You can tell a glamoured turnip at ten paces now. Vey makes you her assistant buyer for the rest of the hour.}\n\n# ---------------- Beastcraft I ----------------\n\n@event beast_bristle_trim\ntitle: The Bristle Trim\npool: beastcraft_class\nlevel: 1\ntone: light\nart: broom\nscene: A row of school brooms clamped to a bench outside the stables, students with shears trimming and binding the twigs while a stern professor inspects each tail.\ntext: {Professor Brask has the class trimming broom tails. \"Broom's a creature. Sulky, stupid creature. Groom it, or it grooms you. Into a hedge.\"|Bristle day again. Brask inspects each broom tail like a hoof, muttering to the brooms more kindly than to you.|Broom trimming. Your school broom recognises you and shivers.} {A hippogriff watches from the paddock, judging.|One broom keeps flicking its twigs at whoever's trimming it.|{season == \"winter\": The twigs are brittle with frost and snap if you look at them.|The twigs are green and springy, and fight back.}}\n> left: Trim it twig by twig\n  study beastcraft +tiny\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You bind every twig and trim the tail to a perfect curve. Brask looks it over. \"Adequate. Broom thinks so too.\" Your fingers are numb, and the hour felt like three.|You trim for an hour, slowly, and your broom flies straighter for it. It's mindless and it isn't; your back aches.|You trim as Brask taught you, twig by twig. The broom purrs, if a broom can purr. Brask says it can. It's still the dullest hour of your week.}\n> right: Test-fly it untrimmed\n  challenge feat standard wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You fly it rough and it flies well anyway, the way some ugly ponies do. Brask watches, grunts, and lets you off.|The untrimmed broom bucks once and then settles under you like an old dog. Brask, drily: \"Lucky. Don't count on it.\"|You know your broom's moods now; you fly it shaggy and it goes like a hawk. Brask almost smiles.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The shaggy tail catches the wind and you go into the hedge, as Brask predicted. She says so, to the hippogriff.|The broom sheds twigs in mid-air and spins you into the paddock. The hippogriff is unimpressed. So is Brask.|It bucks you off at head height. Brask tells the broom it did well, and tells you to get up.}\n\n# ---------------- Umbral Theory II ----------------\n\n@event umbral_shadow_witness\ntitle: The Shadow Witness\npool: umbral_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: eerie\nart: eye\ntags: mystery\nscene: A dim lecture room at the top of a tower, an empty desk with an inkpot-shaped gap in the dust, students crouching to question the long shadows in the corner.\ntext: {An inkpot has been taken from the professor's desk, on purpose, for the lesson. \"The shadows in this room saw who took it,\" the professor says. \"Ask them.\"|Another staged theft, a quill this time. The professor turns the lamps down. \"Shadows remember. They just don't like being asked.\"|The shadow witness lesson. You know which corner's shadow gossips and which one sulks.} {The lamps burn low and blue.|The shadow under the window is longer than it should be.|{season == \"winter\": It's dark by mid-afternoon, and the shadows are bold.|A moth keeps throwing huge shadows across the ceiling, confusing everyone.}}\n> left: Question the long shadow\n  challenge research using guile standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study umbral +tiny\n      say: {The long shadow leans toward the door, then toward a third-year's bag. The inkpot is in it. The professor nods. \"Good. Shadows point. People lie.\"|You coax the shadow into showing a hand, a sleeve, a stride. You name the thief; the thief applauds.|The gossiping corner tells you everything before you've finished asking. The professor marks you down for leading the witness, and up for results.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The shadow stretches toward you instead, and the cold of it settles in your hands. You name the wrong student, confidently.|The shadow shows you a thief who looks a great deal like the professor. The professor finds this very funny; you're freezing.|The sulking corner gives you nothing but chill. You spend the hour with numb fingers and no answer.}\n> right: Watch the doorway, not the dark\n  lore +small\n  say: {You ignore the shadows and work out who left the room and when. You get the right answer by the wrong method. The professor sighs, but marks it.|You reason it out from the dust on the desk. Correct, and entirely unmagical.|You solve it from the doorway again. The professor has started calling you \"the magistrate\", and doesn't mean it as a compliment.}\n\n# ---------------- Artifice ----------------\n\n@event art_fair_entry\ntitle: Something for the Fair\ntrack: artifice\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: class magic\nscene: A workshop bench crowded with half-built clockwork entries for the Artificers' Fair, a student holding a tiny screwdriver over a machine that twitches.\ntext: {Every Artifice student is expected to have something for the Artificers' Fair. Yours is a clockwork wren that sings a little flat.|Fair entries again. Your wren has started to hop, all by itself, which nobody built into it.|Your fair entry has a name, a shelf and a temperament. The class has opinions about it.} {Your neighbour's entry, a self-pouring teapot, is pouring onto the floor.|Someone's entry has escaped and is hiding under the bench.|{season == \"spring\": The fair is weeks away and nobody is ready.|The fire buckets have been moved closer to the benches, on the faculty's orders.}}\n> left: Tinker with your own\n  challenge enchant standard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study artifice +tiny\n      say: {You adjust the wren's voice-reed by a hair and it sings a whole scale. The workshop goes quiet to listen.|The wren hops, sings and flutters, and nothing catches fire. That's the bar, and you've cleared it.|You get the wren to sing something nobody built into it. You don't know how. You write it down very fast.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The wren sings one perfect note and then smokes. Your fingers are singed from catching it.|You fix the flat note and break the hop. The wren lurches round the bench like a drunk sparrow. You burn a thumb on the spring.|The wren sings, beautifully, and then won't stop. You have to pull a gear with smoking pliers to get some peace, and burn yourself doing it.}\n> right: Rescue the neighbour's entry\n  arcana +small\n  study artifice -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {You stop the teapot pouring, rebind its spout-charm and dry the floor. Your own wren waits, unfinished, all hour. Your neighbour will owe you for life.|You coax the escaped entry out from under the bench and fix its leg. Your wren sulks on its shelf, untouched.|You spend the hour on someone else's machine again. Theirs works now. Yours sings flat.}\n\n# ---------------- Starcalling ----------------\n\n@event star_cloudy_appointment\ntitle: A Cloudy Appointment\ntrack: starcalling\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: star\ntags: class magic\nscene: A brass observatory dome open to a heavy, clouded night sky, a student reaching up with both hands toward a faint gleam behind the clouds.\ntext: {Tonight's appointment, says this morning's note, will go ahead \"whatever the sky thinks\". The sky thinks clouds, wall to wall.|Another clouded appointment tonight. The note is unbothered. \"Starlight comes through cloud. It simply has to want to.\"|Cloud again tonight. You know the dome's cold corners and which way the gaps will drift.} {Somewhere behind the murk, the star you know best will be up.|The note says a thin gap may open in the west.|{season == \"winter\": It will be snow-cloud, heavy and low; your breath will freeze on the rail.|It will be warm cloud, with rain coming.}}\n> left: Call through the cloud\n  challenge spell standard+1:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study starcalling +tiny\n      say: {The starlight comes down through the cloud like a rope through water, slow and then all at once. Your palms glow till dawn.|You call, and the cloud thins over your hands alone, a little round window in the sky. The lecturer writes for a long time.|You call the star by name and it answers, cloud or no cloud. You walk back down the stairs with light still in your fingers.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {You call until your hands shake. The cloud doesn't care. You go to bed drained and dark.|A thread of light comes halfway and frays in the murk. It takes something out of you as it goes.|You reach up for an hour into nothing. The lecturer says even nothing is a lesson. It doesn't feel like one.}\n> right: Chart the gaps in the cloud\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You sit by the rail all night logging the gaps, when and where and which stars peeked through. Cold to the bone; good data.|You chart the drift of the cloud like a sailor and predict the next gap to the minute. It comes; you're too frozen to cheer.|Your cloud log is pages long now. You know the sky's habits even when you can't see it, and your chest aches with cold.}\n\n# ---------------- Hollow Studies ----------------\n\n@event hollow_jarred_silences\ntitle: Silences in Jars\ntrack: hollow_studies\nlevel: 1\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: lock\ntags: class mystery\nscene: A windowless room lined with black-bound books and a shelf of small stoppered jars, each holding nothing visible, a student holding one up to the lamplight.\ntext: {Today the headmistress hands you an empty jar. \"Seal a silence in it. Not quiet; silence. You will know the difference by the end.\"|Another empty jar. Behind the headmistress, a shelf of stoppered jars, all apparently empty, all labelled in faded hands.|Silence jars. You know the weight of a well-sealed one now, heavier than it looks.} {The lamp hisses.|The books on the shelves have stopped breathing, as if to help.|{final_year: She asks what silence you'd keep, if you could keep only one.|She works at her own jar, unhurried, while you work at yours.}}\n> left: Seal the room's silence\n  study hollow_studies +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {You draw the room's silence into the jar a breath at a time and stopper it. When you shake it, nothing happens, very heavily. She nods.|The silence fights the stopper like a cat fights a bath. You win, eventually, emptied out.|You seal a silence so clean that the lamp seems louder when you're done. The headmistress takes the jar and shelves it with the old ones.}\n> right: Unseal one of the old jars\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You unstopper a jar dated a century ago. Out comes the silence of a room just after someone laughed. It's unbearably sad, and you don't know why.|You open an old jar and the room fills with the silence of a held breath before a goodbye. You close it again quickly. She says nothing, kindly.|You open one more old jar. A silence after snow. You sit in it a long while before you can speak.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/courses_upper.grim","text":"# Course-morning pools, group courses_upper (docs/authoring/pools.md):\n# two more events each at levels II and III for the six core courses. Repeatable routine events.\n\n# ---------------- Evocation (track: evocation, skill arcana) ----------------\n\n@event evo_rain_barrel\ntitle: Rain on Demand\npool: evocation_class\nlevel: 2\nlesson: mirror_lake\ntone: light\nart: rain\ntags: magic\nscene: A row of students on a lakeside terrace, each with a small grey cloud above a wooden barrel, one cloud raining sideways onto a neighbour.\ntext: {Tidecalling at Evocation II. Pull a cloud off the lake, park it over a barrel, and fill the barrel to the chalk line. Not over, not under.|The barrels are out on the terrace again, each with a chalk line.|You know your barrel now, the one with the iron band that's slipped.} {Professor Marisol is reading on the steps, under a perfectly dry patch of air.|The wind keeps trying to borrow the clouds.|{season == \"winter\": The clouds come off the lake as sleet.|A heron watches the barrels with professional interest.}}\n> left: Wring it out to the line\n  challenge spell standard steady:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      study evocation +tiny\n      say: {The last drop lands on the chalk. Marisol looks up from her book and back down, which counts.|You stop the rain a breath early and let the drip finish the job. Exactly to the line.|You know how much your barrel leaks at the slipped band, and you rain that much extra. Exactly to the line.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {Your cloud overshoots, fills the barrel, and keeps going down your collar.|The cloud drifts left and rains on the next barrel, which is not yours, and on its owner, who is very not yours. The splashback finds your collar anyway.|The heron moves at the wrong moment and so does your cloud. It empties itself over the heron, who leaves offended.}\n> right: Drizzle it in, slowly\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {It takes the whole lesson. Your cloud is the size of a pillow by the end, and very tired.|You fill it a thimble at a time. The others have gone in for lunch. The line is met.|You drizzle it in while your barrel leaks it out at the band. It's a long lesson, but you win in the end.}\n\n@event evo_ember_stair\ntitle: Carrying the Ember\npool: evocation_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: tense\nart: flame\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A student climbing a dim spiral stair, a single glowing ember resting in their bare open palm.\ntext: {Pyromancy at Evocation II. Carry a live ember in your bare palm from the ground floor to the top of the Lecture Tower. Keep it alight. Keep your hand.|The ember drill again. The stair hasn't got any shorter.|Ember day. You've counted the stair by now, and you know where the draught is.} {A door on the third floor opens just as you pass.|Someone ahead of you is climbing very fast and swearing quietly.|The lamps on the stair are out, and your ember is the only light.}\n> left: Feed it, and climb fast\n  challenge feat using arcana standard:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      say: {You keep the ember bright with small breaths of heat and take the stairs two at a time. It arrives fat and orange.|You reach the top with the ember glowing and your palm only warm. The lecturer tips it into a brazier without a word.|Your ember lights the dark stair all the way up. People wait on the landings to follow you.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {The ember flares on the landing and you learn exactly how hot a palm can get. You arrive with it alight, and with a blister.|You feed it too much at the draughty window. It bites. You carry it up the last flight in your other hand.|You feed it in the dark and misjudge it. It bites, and you finish the climb by its light and your own swearing.}\n> right: Shield it, and climb slow\n  study evocation +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You climb at a crawl with the ember cupped against the draughts. It is alight at the top, just. So are your nerves.|Step by step, a held breath at each window. Last up, but the ember's still glowing.|You shield it from the draught you know is coming. It reaches the top small and steady, and so do you.}\n\n@event evo_firebreak\ntitle: The Reed Burn\npool: evocation_class\nlevel: 3\nlesson: mirror_lake\ntone: tense\nart: flame\ntags: magic danger\nscene: Advanced students along a lake shore in a line, half of them calling a low wall of fire through dry reeds, half of them holding back a curtain of lake water behind it.\ntext: {Advanced Evocation does real work, the controlled burn of the dead reeds along the lake shore. Pyromancers lead the fire, tidecallers hold the line behind it.|Burn day. The shore smells of smoke for a week afterwards.|The reed burn. You can tell a fire's mood from its smoke now.} {The wind is steady from the lake.|The wind can't decide.|The reeds are drier than anyone would like.} {season == \"summer\": The air is hot already.|Your breath steams.}\n> left: Walk at the fire's head\n  challenge spell standard+1:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      say: {The fire follows your hands down the shore like a dog on a lead. The lecturer lets you take it all the way to the boathouse.|You lead the fire low and even, and it goes out where you tell it. Behind you there's nothing but clean black ground.|You lead the fire through the dry reeds slowly and keep it on a short lead. Not a spark gets away.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {A gust lifts the fire over your head. The tidecallers drop it in a wall of lake water, and you with it.|The fire jumps the line and runs. It's caught in a minute, but your eyebrows weren't.|The dry reeds go up faster than you can lead them. The tidecallers save the boathouse; you save some of your sleeve.}\n> right: Hold the water line\n  study evocation +tiny\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You stand in the shallows holding a curtain of lake water for an hour. Nothing gets past you. Nobody notices, which is the point.|Your arms ache from keeping the water up. It's the least glamorous job on the shore and the one the lecturer checks first.|You hold the water line through the worst of it. When the dry reeds flare, your curtain is the one that holds.}\n\n@event evo_drowned_flame\ntitle: Fire Under Water\npool: evocation_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: eerie\nart: candle\ntags: magic\nscene: A glass tank of water on a scorched bench, a small blue flame burning steadily at the bottom of it.\ntext: {Original work in Advanced Evocation, a fire that burns underwater. Every student gets a tank. Most tanks get a new pane every week.|Your tank is waiting, with a fresh crack along one pane.|The tank again. You talk to the water now, and to the fire, separately.} {Someone's tank has boiled dry and they're staring at it.|A flame at the next bench burns green beneath the water, quite calm.|The lecturer is standing well back today.}\n> left: Light it at the bottom\n  challenge spell standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {A small blue flame takes hold under the water and stays. The bubbles go up in a thin silver line.|It catches, gutters, and settles. The water around it doesn't even warm. You don't know how you did it.|You light it while the lecturer stands well back. It burns, small and stubborn, and the lecturer comes a step closer.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {The water boils in a heartbeat and the pane splits. You wear the steam on your face for the rest of the day.|The flame goes up instead of in. The tank cracks, and your sleeve is wet and scorched at once.|You learn why the lecturer is standing back. The pane goes, and so does your eyebrow.}\n> right: Wrap it in a bubble first\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You hold a skin of air around the flame and sink them both. It's half a cheat, and the lecturer says so. It burns all hour.|Your flame sits in a bubble at the bottom like a moth in a jar. Not the assignment, but not nothing.|You wrap it in a bubble, as you always do now. The lecturer says it's time to try without, and doesn't make you.}\n\n# ---------------- Astromancy & Divination (track: astromancy, skill lore) ----------------\n\n@event astro_sealed_forecast\ntitle: Tomorrow's Weather\npool: astromancy_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: light\nart: rain\ntags: study\nscene: A row of sealed envelopes pinned to a corkboard in the observatory, each labelled with a student's name and tomorrow's date.\ntext: {Foresight at Astromancy II. Write down tomorrow's weather to the hour, seal it, pin it to the board. The lecturer opens the envelopes a day later, in public.|The envelopes come out again.|You've written so many forecasts you dream in drizzle.} {Outside the dome the sky is giving nothing away.|Last week's best forecast just said \"Wet\", and was right.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the dome, and snow somewhere in the future.|Clouds over the lake, moving with intent.}}\n> left: Commit to the hour\n  challenge research standard wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      study astromancy +tiny\n      say: {\"Rain at the third bell, stopping at the fourth.\" It does. The lecturer reads it out twice.|You forecast a fog to the hour. When the envelope is opened, the fog is pressing on the dome.|You write \"Sleet at the second bell, from the lake.\" It comes off the lake at the second bell. The lecturer stops reading the others.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {\"Clear by noon.\" It rains till evening. Your envelope is read out to great enjoyment.|You predicted wind. Tomorrow turns out so still that a feather on the sill doesn't move.|You forecast with great confidence. Tomorrow is the opposite of everything you wrote, in order.}\n> right: Hedge, and learn from it\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  say: {\"Some weather, at some point.\" The lecturer reads it out flatly and moves on. But you spent the hour watching how the good forecasters work.|You write a careful forecast with three possibilities. It's right, technically. The lecturer calls it \"an umbrella of a prophecy\".|You hedge, and read the others' envelopes after. You can tell the good ones by how short they are.}\n\n@event astro_stubborn_telescope\ntitle: The Stubborn Telescope\ntrack: astromancy\nlevel: 2\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: planet\ntags: class study\nscene: A student tugging at a huge brass telescope in a dome, while it points stubbornly at a different patch of sky.\ntext: {Astromancy II needs exact positions, and that means a night on the great telescope. At the morning lecture your name goes up on tonight's rota. The great telescope, everyone warns you, points wherever it likes.|Your name is on tonight's telescope rota again. The lecturer warns you it is already pointing at something else.|Your night on the telescope again. The lecturer only raises an eyebrow at you as the rota goes up.} {This week it wants the moon.|This week it's fixed on the lake, which is not in the sky.|This week it keeps drifting east, slowly, like a sigh.}\n> left: Argue it round\n  challenge research standard:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: {You coax it, inch by inch, and it settles on your star with a creak like a shrug.|You find the right thing to say to an old brass instrument. It points where you ask. You write the position fast.|You coax it off its eastward drift with a gentle hand and a sharp word. It sighs back to your star.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {It spends the night pointing where it likes. So do you, in the end, cold and annoyed.|You lean on it. It leans back. You go to bed at the fourth bell with nothing written down.|It drifts east all night, slowly, and takes you with it. You write down a very good position for the wrong star.}\n> right: Work it out from tables\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You leave the telescope to its moods and work the positions out by candlelight from the old tables. It takes until dawn.|Pages of sums by candle. The telescope watches over your shoulder, pointedly.|You use the tables and let the telescope drift where it likes. Your positions are good, and you're in bed by midnight.}\n\n@event astro_long_watch\ntitle: Keeping the Light\npool: astromancy_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: warm\nart: lantern\ntags: magic\nscene: A student on the observatory roof before dawn, a glass lantern in their lap holding a single cold point of starlight.\ntext: {Advanced Starlight. At the morning lecture you're given tonight's task. Hold one star's light in a lantern through a whole night on the roof. It will want to go home before dawn. Your job is to keep it.|The night watch on the roof comes round again. After the morning lecture you collect a lantern and a blanket.|Your lantern, your star, your corner of the roof, booked for tonight.} {The star you've drawn is pale and blue.|The star you've drawn is small and stubborn.|The star you've drawn is very old and very faint.} {season == \"winter\": There will be frost on the slates.|The night will be warm and full of moths.}\n> left: Keep it all night\n  lore +small\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  health -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {At dawn your star is still in the lantern, blinking at the sun it was never meant to meet. You are blinking too.|You keep it till the lecturer comes up at sunrise. Your eyes are full of sand and your lantern is full of light.|Your old faint star almost goes out at the fourth bell. You talk to it until dawn, and it stays.}\n> right: Let it go at midnight\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {You open the lantern and the light goes up like a kite let loose. You watch it all the way home and then go to bed.|You let it go early. The lecturer's note just says \"Released at midnight.\" You suspect it's not a compliment.|You let the old star go at midnight. It goes slowly, as if it isn't sure of the way. You watch it until you can't.}\n> up[starlight]: Feed it your own starlight\n  lore +small\n  say: {You pour a little of your own starlight into the lantern. The star brightens as if it had been lonely.|Your light and the star's together are enough to read by. You read until dawn.|You give the old star some of your light. It burns brighter than it has in a long time, and you feel it go out of you.}\n\n@event astro_two_futures\ntitle: Both Futures\npool: astromancy_class\nlevel: 3\ntone: eerie\nart: dice\ntags: magic\nscene: A coin balanced on edge on a dark table in the observatory, with two faint ghostly outcomes hovering above it.\ntext: {Advanced Foresight. The lecturer balances a coin on its edge. Read both futures at once, the one where it falls heads and the one where it falls tails, and hold them both.|The coin on its edge again. You know how it feels to be two places at once now.|The coin. People come out of this lesson pressing their temples.} {Someone in the front row says \"heads\" in two voices.|The lecturer is drinking tea and looking at nobody.|The candles burn straight up, very still.}\n> left: Hold both to the end\n  challenge exam standard+1:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      say: {For a long moment you live both futures, side by side, and neither is frightening. Then the coin falls, and one of them goes out.|You hold both until the lecturer flicks the coin. One future folds away, neat as a letter.|You hold both in the still candlelight until the lecturer stops the coin with a finger. Both futures are still there. \"Interesting,\" the lecturer says.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {The futures tangle and you get a headache that lasts till supper. For an hour you keep reaching for the wrong door handle.|You lose track of which future is which, and of which way is down. The lecturer sits you by an open window.|You hold both too long. For the rest of the day you hear things a moment before they happen, and you hate it.}\n> right: Read one, then the other\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {You read heads, then tails, carefully, one after the other. It's not the exercise, but you get both right.|One future at a time. Slower, safer, and the lecturer says you'll get there.|One future, then the other, and you know both now before you read them. The lecturer says that's the start of holding them.}\n\n# ---------------- Alchemy & Healing (track: alchemy, skill alchemy) ----------------\n\n@event alch_unlabelled_vial\ntitle: The Unlabelled Vial\npool: alchemy_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: light\nart: bottle\ntags: study\nscene: A vial of clear liquid on a lab bench beside a strip of paper turning slowly purple, a student squinting at both.\ntext: {Alchemy II hands out an unlabelled vial each, and an hour to say what's in it. Paper strips, a candle, a nose and a notebook are allowed.|Another unlabelled vial. The lecturer enjoys these.|The vial is waiting on your bench. You've started recognising the corks.} {Yours is perfectly clear and smells faintly of pears.|Yours is cloudy and keeps settling into layers.|Yours is warm to the touch.} {The ventilation charm is wheezing again.|The lab is quiet except for scratching quills.|The lecturer is humming, which means the vials are harder this week.}\n> left: Test it the long way\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Strip, flame, cold, filter. By the end of the hour you've a name and three reasons for it.|Every test, in order, written up neatly. Your notes are dull and your answer is right.|The warm one takes the whole hour, test by test. It's something you've never met, and you name it anyway, correctly.}\n> right: Trust your nose\n  challenge brew standard wild:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study alchemy +tiny\n      say: {One sniff and you know it. You write the name and use the spare hour to check yourself.|You tip the vial under your nose and the answer arrives before the smell does. The lecturer raises an eyebrow and a tick.|It's warm, and that tells you most of it. You sniff to confirm. The lecturer stops humming.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {One sniff and your eyes stream for the rest of the morning. You guessed wrong, too.|It's not what you thought, and it is very much up your nose. You spend the hour at the window.|You sniff the warm one. It sniffs back, in a way, and you spend the hour with a damp cloth on your face.}\n\n@event alch_cracked_tray\ntitle: The Cracked Tray\npool: alchemy_class\nlevel: 2\nlesson: infirmary\ntone: warm\nart: heart\ntags: magic\nscene: A tray in the infirmary laid with a cracked teacup, a cracked hand mirror and a cracked bone from a teaching skeleton, a student's hands hovering over them.\ntext: {Mend at Alchemy II. Sister Amaranth sets out a tray of cracked things, cup, mirror and bone. \"Mend those,\" she says, \"and then we'll see about people.\"|The tray again. Sister Amaranth has found new things to crack.|Sister Amaranth puts the tray down and nods at it. You know the drill.} {A second-year is waiting on a cot with a sprained wrist from broom practice.|A third-year with a scalded hand from Evocation is waiting.|A first-year is in with a split lip and a long story about a door.}\n> left: Mend the tray, perfectly\n  challenge enchant standard:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {The cracks close like seams being sewn shut. Sister Amaranth runs a thumbnail along the cup and finds nothing.|Cup, mirror, bone, whole. Sister Amaranth says \"Fine,\" which is her highest word.|You mend the tray in half the time and Sister Amaranth sends you to the first-year with the split lip. It mends cleanly.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The cup mends with a ridge in it, the mirror with a ripple. Sister Amaranth sets them on the shelf of shame.|You mend the bone a little crooked. Sister Amaranth says that's how you learn and puts it back in the skeleton anyway.|The mirror mends with your face in it, slightly wrong. Sister Amaranth turns it to the wall.}\n> right: Mend the patient instead\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You skip the tray and take the patient, with Sister Amaranth's hand on your shoulder. It goes slowly. It works.|Sister Amaranth lets you try on the patient while she watches. You're shaky and slow, and you'll never mend a cup the same way again.|You mend the first-year's lip and hear the whole story about the door. It wasn't a door. You keep that to yourself.}\n\n@event alch_lake_fever\ntitle: Lake Fever Season\npool: alchemy_class\nlevel: 3\nlesson: infirmary\ntone: warm\nart: potion\ntags: magic\nscene: A row of infirmary cots, students under blankets looking flushed and sorry for themselves, an advanced student drawing a faint grey haze out of one patient's brow.\ntext: {Advanced Alchemy treats real patients. The infirmary has Lake Fever cases, from swimming where they shouldn't. Sister Amaranth wants the fever drawn out, not just cooled.|Lake Fever again. Students never learn, Sister Amaranth says, and neither do lakes.|Fever duty. Sister Amaranth hands you a list without looking up.} {Your patient says they only went in up to the knees.|Your patient is a prefect, very embarrassed.|Your patient insists they were pushed.}\n> left: Draw the fever out by hand\n  challenge enchant standard+1:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      study alchemy +tiny\n      say: {The fever comes out like a grey thread pulled from wool. Your patient sleeps. You'd like to.|You draw it out slowly and the flush leaves their face. Sister Amaranth checks your work and doesn't check twice.|You draw out the fever and the story with it. They were pushed. Sister Amaranth writes down the name.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You draw out half of it and the half you drew comes with you. You spend the evening shivering in the next cot.|The cure has to come from somewhere. You wake up two hours later on the floor, with a blanket on you.|The fever comes out angry and goes into you. You spend the night in the next cot, insisting you weren't pushed.}\n> right: Brew the slow tonic\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Three hours of stirring willow bark and lake moss. The tonic works by morning, which is slower and safer.|You brew the old remedy by the book. Sister Amaranth tastes it, grimaces, and approves.|You brew the tonic without the book now. Sister Amaranth tastes it and doesn't grimace, which worries you more.}\n> up[purify]: Distill the fever out in one pass\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You distill the fever into a single grey drop and stopper it. The drop takes some of your strength with it, and leaves you yawning.|One pass of Distill and the patient sits up asking for soup. The tiredness lands on you an hour later.|You distill it out in one pass, and your patient sits up and says who pushed them. You're asleep before Sister Amaranth asks.}\n\n@event alch_third_floor_still\ntitle: The Still Cracks\ntrack: alchemy\nlevel: 3\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: class danger\nscene: A copper still on a long bench, hissing from a fresh crack, a thin green vapour creeping across the laboratory floor.\ntext: {Advanced Alchemy distils essences that would rather be gas. The ventilation charm gave up on the third floor years ago. Today your still cracks.|Distilling day, and a hiss from your still that you know.|Your still has cracked again. You're getting fast at noticing.} {The vapour is green and smells of mint.|The vapour is pale and smells of nothing, which is worse.|The vapour is curling toward the next bench.}\n> left: Mend the crack, still hot\n  challenge enchant standard+1:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {You close the copper under your palms while it's still singing. The hiss stops. The class breathes out.|The crack seals with a sound like a sigh, and the batch is saved. The lecturer opens a window, for form's sake.|You seal it before the vapour reaches the next bench. Its owner never knows how close it came.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {The crack closes, then reopens with a pop. You breathe in a mouthful and spend the afternoon seeing double.|You burn your fingertips on the copper and the vapour gets out anyway. The lab is cleared for an hour.|The vapour reaches the next bench before you do. Two of you spend the afternoon seeing double.}\n> right: Shout, and clear the bench\n  study alchemy -tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You call the warning and clear the bench. The batch is lost, nobody is hurt, and you start again next week.|Everyone is out in the corridor in a minute flat. The lecturer goes back in, a scarf over the mouth, and comes out carrying your ruined batch.|You clear the bench and the one next to it. The lecturer notes that you thought of the neighbours, and the batch is still lost.}\n\n# ---------------- Illusion & Glamour (track: illusion, skill charm) ----------------\n\n@pool illusion_class_repeat5\nfrom: course_routine\ntrack: illusion\n\n@event illu_walking_double\ntitle: The Walking Double\npool: illusion_class_repeat5\nlevel: 2\ntone: light\nart: mirror\ntags: magic\nscene: Two identical students walking side by side across a lecture hall, one of them leaving no footprints in the chalk dust.\ntext: {Mirror at Illusion II. Make a double of yourself and walk it across the room. \"Standing doubles are furniture,\" says Professor Vey. \"Make it walk.\"|Doubles day. Vey watches feet, not faces.|Your double's walk is improving. It has your slouch now.} {The floor has been dusted with chalk, to show footprints.|Someone's double is walking backwards.|Vey is sitting in the front row, which is unsettling.}\n> left: Walk it right across\n  challenge enchant using charm standard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      say: {Your double crosses the room at your pace and even scuffs the chalk. Vey watches its feet the whole way and says nothing, which is high praise.|It walks, it turns, it nearly sits down. Vey says, \"Better.\"|Your double slouches across the room, Vey in the front row watching every step. \"That's you,\" Vey says. It isn't a compliment, quite.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {Halfway across, your double's legs forget to move and it glides. Vey says it's a ghost, not a person, and moves on.|Your double walks into the wall and keeps walking. The class watches it vanish into the plaster.|Your double sees Vey in the front row and freezes, exactly as you would. It's very lifelike, and it doesn't walk.}\n> right: Keep it still and perfect\n  study illusion +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {Your double stands at your elbow, perfect to the last button. It doesn't move. Vey calls it a very good wardrobe.|You make it flawless and keep it standing. Vey taps its nose, and frowns when it holds.|Your double stands perfectly still in front of Vey, slouch and all. Vey reaches out and adjusts its collar, which holds.}\n\n@event illu_good_news\ntitle: Good News, Read Aloud\npool: illusion_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: light\nart: mask\ntags: magic\nscene: A student standing at a lectern reading from a dull notice while the listening class smiles as though hearing wonderful news.\ntext: {Glamour at Illusion II, and today it's the voice. Vey hands you a dull notice and asks you to read it so the class hears good news.|Another notice from Vey's drawer of very dull notices.|Vey has saved the dullest notice for you.} {Today's is about the drains.|Today's is about a change to the laundry rota.|Today's is a list of overdue library books, in full.}\n> left: Glamour every word\n  challenge deceive using charm standard:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      say: {By the second line the room is beaming. Someone claps. Vey waits for the clapping to stop and asks what the notice said. Nobody knows.|You read it warm and low, and the class leans in as if you were reading their letters from home.|You read out the overdue books like a list of prize-winners. Three students go to the library that afternoon.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {Your glamour slips on a long word and the room hears exactly what it is, a notice. Someone yawns on purpose.|You overdo it. The class looks alarmed, as if the notice were about to propose.|The glamour holds for twenty titles and drops on the twenty-first. The class hears the rest exactly as they are.}\n> right: Read it plain, watch others\n  study illusion +tiny\n  say: {You read it straight and sit down. Then you watch the others, and notice the best of them barely change their voices at all.|Plain reading, polite applause. You spend the hour writing down where the good ones breathe.|You read the list plain. It takes a long time. You learn more watching Vey's face than you would have by trying.}\n> up[glamour]: Glamour the notice itself\n  charm +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {You don't touch your voice; you glamour the paper. Every student who reads it over your shoulder sees good news in their own hand. Vey applauds, twice, slowly.|You glamour the ink so it shimmers like a wedding invitation. The class fights over the notice afterwards. It is still, underneath, a very dull notice.|You glamour the list into an honours roll. Half the class goes to look for their names, and finds their overdue books.}\n\n@event illu_false_room\ntitle: The Room That Isn't\npool: illusion_class_repeat5\nlevel: 3\ntone: tense\nart: door\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A student walking confidently into a room that is partly painted light, a real stone wall standing where the illusion shows an open doorway.\ntext: {Advanced Illusion asks for a double of a whole room. Hang it over the real one and walk a classmate through it. Vey's rule is simple. If anyone walks into a wall, it's your fault.|The room exercise. Somebody always walks into something.|You hang your room. You've learned where the real walls are.} {Vey's volunteer today is a porter with a tray of cups.|Vey's volunteer today is Vey.|The real room has been rearranged overnight.}\n> left: Hang the whole room\n  challenge enchant using charm standard+1:\n    win:\n      charm +medium\n      say: {Your walker crosses the false room and sits down in a false chair, which you hold up for them. Vey writes for a long time.|The room holds. Nobody touches a real wall. Vey walks it twice to be sure.|You hang your room over the rearranged one and nobody notices which is which, including, briefly, Vey.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {Your walker steps through a door you painted onto solid stone. You catch them as they fall, take the edge of the stone on your own elbow, and walk them to Sister Amaranth with an apology.|Halfway across the room flickers, and the walker freezes. So do you, and you walk hip-first into a real chair.|You hang your room over where the walls were yesterday. The walker finds today's walls with their nose.}\n> right: Chalk the real walls first\n  study illusion +tiny\n  mana -small\n  say: {You hang half a room, with the real walls marked out in chalk so nobody could miss them. Vey calls it \"a room with a safety rope\".|Safe and visible, chalk lines everywhere. Vey sighs, and then copies down how you did the window.|You chalk the new walls first, all of them. Vey says that's the only correct answer to a rearranged room.}\n\n@event illu_peeling_layers\ntitle: Seeing Through\npool: illusion_class_repeat5\nlevel: 3\ntone: eerie\nart: eye\ntags: magic\nscene: A lecture hall in layers like peeled paper: one layer with velvet curtains, one bare stone, one darker one beneath, and a student standing at its centre.\ntext: {Advanced Illusion. To make illusions you must see through them. Vey layers the hall in false rooms, one on another, and you peel them back one at a time.|Layer day. The hall looks normal. That's never a good sign.|You count the layers when you come in. You're usually wrong.} {The top layer has velvet curtains.|The top layer is a summer garden.|The top layer is the hall itself, just slightly too tidy.}\n> left: Peel down to the stone\n  challenge exam standard+1:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      study illusion +tiny\n      say: {Seven layers, then bare stone. Vey asks how many. You say seven. She smiles and says eight, and points at herself.|You reach the stone and stay there, sure of it. Vey nods slowly. \"Hold on to that,\" she says.|The too-tidy hall comes off last, and under it is the real hall, untidy and wonderful. Vey says you're the first today.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You peel one too many and the stone underneath looks false. For the rest of the day you knock on walls, with a headache behind your eyes.|After the fourth layer you stop being sure which way is up. Vey walks you to a chair, which is real.|You peel the tidy hall and find another tidy hall. And another. Vey stops you at the sixth, gently.}\n> right: Stop at the third layer\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Three layers and you stop, dizzy. Vey says knowing when to stop is the other half of the skill.|You get through three and sit down. The hall still looks too tidy, and you decide not to ask.|You stop at three, and you're right to: the third was the real one. Vey says so, with some surprise.}\n\n# ---------------- Beastcraft & Flight (track: beastcraft, skill vigor) ----------------\n\n@pool beastcraft_class_repeat5\nfrom: course_routine\ntrack: beastcraft\n\n@event beast_hedge_run\ntitle: The Hedge Run\npool: beastcraft_class_repeat5\nlevel: 2\ntone: tense\nart: broom\ntags: magic danger\nscene: Students on brooms skimming low over a line of hedges in a field, one broom bucking as its rider leans forward.\ntext: {Beastcraft II puts haste on a broom, low over the hedges, as fast as you dare. Professor Brask says brooms are harder to reason with than hippogriffs, and harder to apologise to.|The hedge run. Your broom remembers last time.|Hedges. You've learned to read a broom's sulk through the handle.} {Brask is timing with a crooked hourglass.|The hedges have been trimmed, unevenly.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves come up behind every broom.|{season == \"spring\": The hedges are full of nesting birds who object.|The ground is hard and unforgiving.}}}\n> left: Haste at the last hedge\n  challenge feat standard wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      study beastcraft +tiny\n      say: {You borrow a hawk's speed for the last hedge and clear it by a hand. Brask stops the hourglass and nods.|The broom flinches, then commits. You come in fastest of the morning, with hedge in your hair.|You feel the broom's sulk through the handle and haste it anyway, kindly. It clears the last hedge and forgives you.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {The broom bucks at the haste and you go over the hedge without it.|You're fast. The broom is faster, briefly, and then upside down. You land in a bramble.|The broom has been sulking all morning and saves it for the last hedge. You go over; it stays.}\n> right: Fly it clean and steady\n  vigor +small\n  say: {No haste, just a clean low line over every hedge. Brask says it's the best boring flying she's seen all week.|You fly it slow and tidy. The broom approves. Brask is less impressed.|You fly it steady and let it sulk. Brask says the broom will remember you were patient, and so will she.}\n\n@event beast_owl_post\ntitle: Teaching the Owls\npool: beastcraft_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: warm\nart: owl\ntags: nature\nscene: A student in a stable loft with a young owl on their arm, pointing out of a high window toward a distant tower.\ntext: {Beast-speech at Beastcraft II. The young owls must learn the post routes. Brask hands you one and says, \"Explain the castle to it.\"|A new young owl, and the same castle to explain.|Another owl. You've got a speech now, and a map scratched on a beam.} {Your owl wants to know why there are so many towers.|Your owl has strong opinions about the library.|Your owl keeps falling asleep, standing up.}\n> left: Explain it in owl\n  challenge soothe using vigor standard:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      study beastcraft +tiny\n      say: {You describe the castle in winds, perches and mice. The owl blinks, and goes and comes back with Brask's letter.|It takes a while to get \"left\" across to a bird. When it does, the owl does the route twice to show off.|You give your speech, map and all. The sleepy owl wakes for the important part and does the route without a mistake.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {The owl listens closely, then flies to the kitchens. Every time.|The owl makes you explain \"tower\" seven times and then goes to sleep on your head.|Your owl sleeps through the whole speech, then wakes and flies to the kitchens. You're starting to take it personally.}\n> right: Walk the route with it\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You walk the whole post route with the owl on your arm, up every stair. Your legs ache and the owl has learned it.|Up and down the castle with an owl on your wrist. People stare. The owl stares back.|You walk the route with the owl asleep on your arm. It learns it anyway, somehow. Brask says they do that.}\n\n@event beast_hooves\ntitle: Hoof Day\npool: beastcraft_class_repeat5\nlevel: 3\ntone: tense\nart: paw\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A student crouched beside a hippogriff in a stall, a hoof trimming knife in hand, the hippogriff looking down with one sceptical eye.\ntext: {Advanced Beastcraft does the work nobody else wants. Today it's hooves. A hippogriff's need trimming each month, and the hippogriff disagrees.|Hoof day. The stall is the same; the hippogriff's opinion is too.|Hooves. You go in with a bucket of apples and a plan.} {Today's hippogriff is grey and patient.|Today's is young and very sure of itself.|Today's is old and wants to tell you about it.}\n> left: Talk it into lifting a foot\n  challenge soothe using vigor standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      study beastcraft +tiny\n      say: {It listens to your case and lifts a hoof with enormous dignity. Brask watches from the door and says nothing.|You talk it through every hoof. It sighs at you between each one, like a patient uncle.|The old hippogriff tells you about its youth for every hoof. You listen to all of it, and it lifts each foot when the story ends.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {It lets you get all the way to the last hoof before stepping on your foot.|It hears you out, then sits on you. Brask lifts it off with one hand and a word.|The old one talks and talks and doesn't lift a foot. You've been had, and Brask is laughing at the door.}\n> right: Rope, and Brask holding\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  say: {The old way, then. A rope, a strong arm, Brask at its head. It's sweaty work and nobody enjoys it, including the hippogriff.|With Brask holding the head and you holding on, you finish every hoof. Your shoulders will remember it.|Apples first, then rope, then Brask. The plan works, and the hippogriff eats the plan.}\n\n@event beast_night_stable\ntitle: Sitting Up With the Sick\npool: beastcraft_class_repeat5\nlevel: 3\ntone: warm\nart: lantern\ntags: nature\nscene: A student sitting in straw by lantern light, a sick creature curled beside them, the rest of the stables asleep.\ntext: {Advanced students take turns sitting up in the stables when a creature is ill. At the morning lecture Brask hands you a lantern, a blanket and a list for tonight.|Your name is on Brask's night-watch list again, and the list is shorter.|Night watch again tonight. Brask hands over the lantern without a word; the owls will only mutter.} {This time it's the griffon chick, off its food.|This time it's an old owl with a cough.|This time it's a hippogriff who ate something it shouldn't.} {season == \"winter\": The stable will be cold enough to see your breath.|At least it's warm in the straw.}\n> left: Sit up all night\n  vigor +small\n  study beastcraft +tiny\n  health -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You don't sleep, and by dawn it's breathing easier. Brask finds you asleep in the straw and leaves you there.|You watch it all night, changing the water and the straw. In the morning it eats, and so do you, enormously.|You sit up with the hippogriff while it groans about its mistake. By dawn it's eating again, and you're asleep against its side.}\n> right: Ask it where it hurts\n  challenge soothe using vigor standard+1:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      say: {It tells you, in its own way, exactly where it hurts. You write it on Brask's list and both of you sleep.|It points with its whole body. You bring the right tonic and it drops off before the lantern does.|It tells you exactly what it ate, with some pride. You fetch the purge and it forgives you by morning.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {It tells you a great deal, about its neighbours, the hay and the moon, and nothing about where it hurts.|It won't talk to you. It talks to the lantern. You stay up anyway, which is the point.|It won't say what it ate, out of embarrassment. You guess, and Brask corrects the guess in the morning.}\n> up[mend]: Mend what hurts\n  study beastcraft +tiny\n  say: {You find the sore place with your hands and mend it. By midnight it's breathing easier, and so are you.|You mend the cough out of its chest, slowly, a breath at a time. It sleeps before the lantern does, and so do you.|You mend the ache in its belly. It looks at you, ashamed and grateful, and Brask's lecture in the morning is short.}\n\n# ---------------- Umbral Theory (track: umbral, skill guile) ----------------\n\n@event umbral_ward_candle\ntitle: Ward the Candle\npool: umbral_class\nlevel: 2\ntone: tense\nart: candle\ntags: magic social\ncast:\n  partner: any where tagged(partner, student)\nscene: Two students at a dim bench under low lamps, a candle between them inside a faint pale shell, one student trying to blow it out.\ntext: {Ward at Umbral Theory II. Ward a candle while your partner tries to put it out. Today that's {partner}.|Candle wards again, and the lamps are lower. {partner} is across the bench.|The candle exercise. You're paired with {partner}, who rolls up {their} sleeves.} {The professor walks between the benches with a hand over the flames.|The room smells of wax and burnt thread.|Someone's ward has shrunk to the size of a thimble.}\n> left: A thick, honest shell\n  guile +small\n  mana -small\n  rel partner +tiny\n  say: {Your ward is plain and solid. {partner} blows and blows and the candle doesn't even lean.|Strong and dull. {partner} gives up and the two of you watch the flame burn down.|Your shell is thick and calm. {partner}'s sleeves are rolled for nothing; the flame doesn't even notice {them}.}\n> right: A ward that turns them aside\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study umbral +tiny\n      say: {Your ward turns {partner}'s breath round, and it blows {their} own hair into {their} eyes. The professor stops and says, \"Ah.\"|{partner} reaches for your candle and finds {their} hand on the wrong side of the bench. The professor approves.|Your ward turns {partner} aside so gently that {they} try{s} three times before working out what happened.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      rel partner -tiny\n      say: {Your clever ward turns aside the wrong thing, and the candle goes out with a smug little puff. {partner} smirks.|The ward bends {partner}'s breath straight into your face. You sneeze and the candle goes out.|Your ward shrinks to a thimble, like your neighbour's, and {partner} pinches the flame out with two fingers.}\n> up[ward]: A true ward\n  guile +small\n  study umbral +tiny\n  rel partner +tiny\n  say: {You cast the real thing, the spell itself, and the candle sits inside a pale shell that hums. {partner} blows, then laughs, then asks you to show {them} how.|You raise the true ward. The professor stops at your bench and holds a hand over it, and for once says nothing at all.|The true ward comes easily now. {partner} tries everything, a breath, a book, a wet finger, and the flame burns on, untroubled.}\n\n@pool umbral_class_repeat5\nfrom: course_routine\ntrack: umbral\n\n@event umbral_dark_roll\ntitle: Who Moved\npool: umbral_class_repeat5\nlevel: 2\ntone: eerie\nart: moon\ntags: magic\nscene: A pitch-dark classroom at the top of a tower, a few vague shapes of students sitting perfectly still in the gloom.\ntext: {Umbral Theory II. The professor puts out every lamp, and in the dark one student moves. Say who, and where to.|Lamps out. You've learned to hear a sleeve.|The dark. You know the room's sounds, and which floorboard is a liar.} {Rain on the tower roof makes it harder.|It's so quiet you can hear the stair breathing.|Somebody's stomach rumbles, which helps no one.}\n> left: Listen, and name them\n  guile +small\n  say: {\"Third bench, to the window.\" Right. The professor relights a lamp and moves on.|You get it on the second try, by the creak of a bench and a faint smell of soap.|\"Second bench, toward the door, stepping over the liar.\" The professor laughs in the dark, once.}\n> right: Be the one who moves\n  challenge deceive standard:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study umbral +tiny\n      say: {You cross the whole room in the dark and nobody names you. When the lamps come up you're behind the professor.|Nobody hears you. The professor says, \"Someone moved,\" and seems pleased not to know who.|You step over the floorboard that lies and cross the room in silence. Even the professor guesses wrong.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {You catch a desk with your shin. \"Window, limping,\" says everyone at once.|Your shoe squeaks on the second step, you turn your ankle stopping, and the whole class names you, with feeling.|You avoid the liar and find a new one. The whole room names you before you've reached the wall.}\n\n@event umbral_corridor_step\ntitle: Across the Corridor\npool: umbral_class_repeat5\nlevel: 3\ntone: eerie\nart: ghost\ntags: magic danger\nscene: A dim stone corridor at the top of a tower, a student half stepping out of the shadow of one doorway while their own shadow lingers in another across the hall.\ntext: {Advanced Umbral Theory. Shadowstep across the corridor, door to door, the farthest the professor allows. \"Short distances only,\" the professor says. \"This is as short as long gets.\"|The corridor. The cold in between is colder than last time.|You stand at the door with your heels on the line. You know the cold that's coming.} {The lamps are out on the landing.|A draught is moving along the floor.|Someone came back from it with frost on one shoulder.}\n> left: Step across\n  challenge deceive standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {One shadow, cold and long, and then the other. You come out on the far side shaking and grinning.|You step in by one door and out by the other, with a breath of frost on your collar. The professor writes your name, and a time.|You step into the cold you knew was coming and out the far door with nothing on your shoulder. The professor writes a shorter time.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {The cold in between is longer than you thought. You come out in the right doorway and can't feel your hands for an hour.|You step and come out in the same doorway you went in by, with the feeling that something looked at you on the way.|You come back with frost on one shoulder, like the others. It takes until supper to melt, and it isn't only cold.}\n> right: Walk it, counting shadows\n  study umbral +tiny\n  say: {You walk the corridor and count every shadow and every lamp. The professor calls this \"mapping your road\".|You pace it out and write down each shadow's depth. Next time you'll step it. Probably.|You walk it and count the shadows, again. They're the same as last time, which you now realise is the lesson.}\n> up[shadowstep]: Step with a held breath\n  guile +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You step without thinking about it, the way the spell knows. The cold barely touches you.|The spell takes you over quick and clean. Your fingers are cold for a minute.|You hold your breath and step. No frost, no cold, no time at all. The professor stops writing.}\n\n@event umbral_unpick_ward\ntitle: Unpicking the Box\npool: umbral_class_repeat5\nlevel: 3\ntone: tense\nart: lock\ntags: magic\nscene: A small iron-bound box on a bench under a dim lamp, faint threads of a pale ward wrapped around it, a student leaning close with a needle.\ntext: {Advanced Umbral Theory. The professor wards a small box and you unpick the ward without it noticing. \"A ward that notices you,\" the professor says, \"tells someone.\"|Box day. The professor has a new box, and a new ward on it.|The box. You've got a favourite needle for this now.} {This ward is fine as a cobweb.|This ward is thick and patient.|This ward hums when you breathe on it.}\n> left: Thread by thread\n  guile +small\n  mana -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {An hour with a needle and a steady breath, and the box opens. The professor checks the ward and it has no idea.|You undo it strand by strand. It takes the whole lesson, and you don't look up once.|Your favourite needle and a slow breath. The humming ward goes quiet one strand at a time, and never notices it's gone.}\n> right: Cut it in one stroke\n  challenge deceive standard+1:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      study umbral +tiny\n      say: {You find the knot and cut it clean. The ward never knows. The professor raises a brow and opens the box.|One cut and the threads fall away. The professor spends a moment looking at the box and then at you.|You cut it between hums. The ward stops mid-note and never starts again. The professor writes something down.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The ward notices, snaps across your knuckles and rings like a little bell. The professor says, \"And now someone knows.\" Everyone looks at you.|The ward snaps back at your hand and stings. The box stays shut.|You cut it mid-hum. It shrieks instead, and the professor has to open the window to let the sound out.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/dating.grim","text":"# Dating (playtest 2, X4): more time together once you're a couple. Generic cards for any partner (`cast: partner`,\n# `per: partner`), then one date per romanceable character that only they would plan. They come to you as social\n# cards; a partner's warmth decides how often (design §5.5).\n\n# ---------------- Any partner ----------------\n\n@event date_market_day\ntitle: Market Day\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nper: partner\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 12\nart: coin\ntags: romance\nscene: Two students wandering a village market arm in arm, stalls of ribbons and pies, one holding up a ridiculous hat.\ntext: {Market day in the village, and {partner} has decided you're going together.|Market day again. {partner} knows which stall has the good pies now.|{partner} has a list for the market, and you're on it.} {A stall sells hats nobody should wear.|There's a juggler with one ball too many.|{season == \"winter\": Roasted chestnuts, paper cones, cold fingers.|Strawberries, a whole cart of them.}}\n> left: Buy {them} something silly\n  gold -small\n  spark partner +small\n  say: {You buy {partner} the worst hat at the market. {They} wear{s} it all day, with enormous dignity.|A tin ring from a pedlar, too big for any finger. {partner} threads it on a string and doesn't take it off.|Halfway down {partner}'s list, between flour and string, you find \"something silly\" in your own handwriting. A paper crown. {They} wear{s} it home.}\n> right: Just walk and talk\n  rel partner +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You walk the whole market twice and buy nothing at all. You talk the entire time and remember none of it, only the voice.|An afternoon of nothing in particular, which is exactly what you both needed.|You ignore the list together and just walk. {partner} crosses things off anyway, one by one, as if you'd bought them.}\n\n@event date_study_together\ntitle: Studying Together\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nper: partner\nwhen: term\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: romance study\nscene: Two students at a library table, books piled between them, one pretending to read while watching the other.\ntext: {{partner} suggests studying together. In theory, this is efficient.|Another study evening with {partner}. The theory remains a theory.|{partner} has saved you a seat in the library and stacked your books for you.} {The exam is in a fortnight.|The library is very quiet.|Your knees keep touching under the table.}\n> left: Actually study\n  study +tiny\n  spark partner -tiny\n  say: {You study, properly, in companionable silence. {partner} sighs once, theatrically. You both pass the next quiz.|Two hours of real work. {partner} quizzes you on the walk back, a little wistful that it was only studying, and you get them all right.|You keep your knees to yourself and your eyes on the page, mostly. {partner} stacked the books in the right order, and you get through all of them.}\n> right: Don't study at all\n  spark partner +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {You pass notes, then drawings, then you're both thrown out of the library for laughing. Worth it.|Not a page is turned. The librarian moves you apart, which only makes it worse.|Your knees keep touching and neither of you moves. The books stay stacked exactly as {partner} left them.}\n\n@event date_first_quarrel\ntitle: A Quarrel\ntrigger: social\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and spark(partner) >= 30 and around(partner)\nper: partner\nweight: 10\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: Two students on opposite ends of a cold courtyard bench, not looking at each other, a dropped scarf between them.\ntext: Your first real quarrel with {partner}. It started about something small, a missed supper, and became about something large before either of you noticed. Now you're on opposite ends of a bench and neither of you will speak first.\n> left: Speak first\n  rel partner +small\n  mood -small\n  say: \"I'm sorry,\" you say, and it costs you. {partner} looks at you, then shuffles along the bench. \"Me too.\" You talk it through until the bell. It's better after, and you both know how it's done now.\n> right: Wait for {them}\n  spark partner -small\n  rel partner -tiny\n  say: You wait. So does {partner}. You leave separately. The next day you both pretend it didn't happen, and it lies between you like the scarf on the bench.\n\n@event date_meet_friends\ntitle: Meeting the Friends\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 12 and bond(friend) != \"partner\" and tagged(friend, student) and not tagged(friend, outsider) and around(friend)\nper: partner\nweight: 10\nart: group\ntags: romance social\nscene: Three students around a refectory table, two of them sizing each other up over the bread basket, one in the middle smiling too hard.\ntext: {friend} and {partner} have heard a great deal about each other, all from you, and now they're at the same supper table. {friend} is being very polite. {partner} is being very polite back. You are dying.\n> left: Steer the talk to something they share\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      rel friend +small\n      rel partner +small\n      say: You find it on the third try: a shared loathing of Thursday's soup. By pudding they're ganging up on you, which is exactly what you wanted.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: Every subject dies. They're kind about it, both of them, and the silence is very long. \"Well,\" says {friend}, afterwards. \"They're nice.\" It's not quite a compliment.\n> right: Let them find their own way\n  rel friend +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You eat your supper and let them circle each other. By the end they've agreed on nothing except that you talk in your sleep. You never find out how {partner} knew that and {friend} did.\n\n@event date_rainy_afternoon\ntitle: Rained In\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner) and not npc_is(tobias)\nper: partner\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: romance\nscene: Rain streaming down a tall window seat, two students under one blanket with a board game between them, cups of tea steaming.\ntext: {Rain all afternoon, and {partner} has found the window seat in the west tower, a blanket and an old board game with half the pieces.|Rain again, and {partner} has claimed the window seat for the two of you.|Rain, a blanket, and a game whose missing pieces someone has replaced with ones carved from soap.} {The rain is loud on the leads.|The tea is too hot.|The game's rules are mostly invented.}\n> left: Play to win\n  spark partner +tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {You win, ruthlessly. {partner} demands a rematch and wins that. Neither of you will ever forget the score.|You cheat; {partner} cheats better. The game ends in a pillow fight and a broken teacup.|You invent a rule; {partner} invents a better one. A soap piece wins in the end, and you both agree it earned it.}\n> right: Forget the game\n  spark partner +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The game is abandoned by the second move. You talk about your families until the rain stops and the room is dark and you're late for supper.|You fall asleep under the blanket and wake up cold and late and very happy.|You carve another soap piece together while the rain goes on. Nobody can say what it was meant to be, and {partner} keeps it anyway.}\n\n@event date_forgotten_day\ntitle: The Day You Forgot\ntrigger: social\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nper: partner\nweight: 9\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: A student holding a small wrapped present out to another student, whose hands are empty and whose face has just fallen.\ntext: {partner} hands you a small parcel, wrapped carefully. \"It's our day,\" {they} say{s}. Apparently there is a day. You didn't know. You have nothing. {partner} sees you realise it.\n> left: Confess and make it up\n  gold -small\n  spark partner +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: \"I forgot,\" you say. {They} laugh{s}, a little hurt. That evening you turn up with flowers from the village and a terrible poem, and the poem does it.\n> right: Pretend your gift is \"coming\"\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance even:\n    gold -medium\n    say: You run to the village before the shops shut and spend more than you meant on something you hope looks planned. {partner} believes you, or kindly pretends to.\n  else:\n    spark partner -small\n    say: The shops are shut. Your gift \"is delayed\" for a week. {partner} doesn't ask about it again, and that is worse than asking.\n\n@event date_dance_lesson\ntitle: Learning to Dance\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 12\nper: partner\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: music\ntags: romance\nscene: Two students practising dance steps in an empty classroom, chairs pushed back, one counting aloud and the other looking at their feet.\ntext: With the {month == 4: Midwinter Ball|Starfall dance} coming, {partner} has decided you're going to learn properly, in an empty classroom, with the chairs pushed back. {~You start on the dance with the spin.|\"Again,\" says {partner}. \"From the turn.\"|Somebody is watching from the door.|Your feet have opinions.|There's no music, only counting.}\n> left: Practise until it's right\n  vigor +tiny\n  spark partner +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~By the end of the hour you can do the turn without looking. {partner} says \"There,\" very softly, and doesn't let go straight away.|Your feet ache, and you can dance. Nearly.|Whoever is watching from the door claps when you finally get the turn. {partner} bows to them, and then to you.|Your feet stop arguing about halfway through the hour. By the end you can do the turn, and {partner} is insufferably pleased.|You count until the counting turns into a tune, and the turn comes out right on the last beat.}\n> right: Make it a joke\n  mood +small\n  rel partner +small\n  say: {~You invent a new dance, mostly flailing. {partner} laughs so hard {they} ha{s} to sit down, then joins in.|You never learn the turn. You learn that {partner} snorts when {they} laugh{s}, which is better.|You play it up for whoever is watching at the door. They laugh, {partner} laughs, and nobody learns the turn.|You let your feet do what they like. {partner} copies them exactly, and it becomes a dance of its own.|You count in the wrong order on purpose. {partner} loses count laughing, and that's the end of the lesson.}\n\n@event date_after_graduation\ntitle: After\ntrigger: social\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and spark(partner) >= 60 and around(partner)\nwhen: final_year\nper: partner\nweight: 16\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: Two students sitting on the castle wall at sunset, legs dangling, the whole valley below them gold.\ntext: On the castle wall, with the light going long over the valley, {partner} asks the question neither of you has said out loud: \"After graduation. What happens to us?\" The valley below is gold. {They} {are} not looking at you, very deliberately.\n> left: \"We go together.\"\n  spark partner +medium\n  flag after_together\n  mood +small\n  say: {They} let{s} out a breath {they}'d been holding for months. You plan it there and then, badly: a city, a cottage, a list of rules for the kitchen. It doesn't matter that it's badly. It's yours.\n> right: \"Let's not decide yet.\"\n  rel partner +small\n  spark partner -tiny\n  say: \"Not yet,\" {they} agree{s}, too quickly, and takes your hand. You watch the gold go grey. It's honest, and it's lonelier than the other answer.\n\n# ---------------- One date only they would plan ----------------\n\n@event lyra_date_dawn_duel\ntitle: A Duel for Two\nactor: lyra\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: partner_here(lyra)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: duel\ntags: romance duel\nscene: Two duellists bowing in a misty courtyard at dawn, wands lowered, both trying not to smile.\ntext: {Lyra's idea of a romantic afternoon is a duel in the empty courtyard, \"to keep you sharp\".|Another courtyard duel. Lyra has started calling them \"our afternoons\".|Lyra is already in the courtyard, wand out, sleeves rolled.} {She's brought coffee for after.|She's in a terrible mood, which means she slept well.|\"No holding back,\" she says. \"I'll know.\"}\n> left: Duel her properly\n  challenge duel vs lyra.arcana:\n    win:\n      spark lyra +small\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: {You win. Lyra stares at you as if you'd proposed. \"Again,\" she says, breathless, and then, \"Tomorrow.\" It's the best thing anyone has ever said to you.|A clean win. She shakes your hand, then doesn't let go of it.|No holding back, so you don't, and you win. She grins as if she'd won herself, and demands you show her how.}\n    lose:\n      spark lyra +tiny\n      health -small\n      say: {She wins, because she always does, and then sits beside you on the cold steps with the coffee and explains your mistake, very gently, for her.|You lose. She bandages your knuckle herself and pretends it's an inspection.|No holding back on either side. She wins, just, and admits on the steps that it was closer than she'll ever tell anyone else.}\n> right: Let her win, and tell her so\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {She knows the moment you drop your guard. \"Don't you dare,\" she says, and then kisses you, furious, and then makes you do it again properly.|She's outraged. She's also, for the rest of the day, unusually soft.|\"I said I'd know,\" she says, the moment you drop your guard. She makes you start again, and this time you don't dare.}\n\n@event tobias_date_greenhouse_supper\ntitle: Lunch Among the Tomatoes\nactor: tobias\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: partner_here(tobias)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: flower\ntags: romance nature\nscene: A candlelit table set up between rows of tomato plants in a warm greenhouse at night, a big student serving soup from a pot.\ntext: {Tobias has cooked lunch in the greenhouse, at a table between the tomato rows, with everything he grew himself.|Lunch in the greenhouse again. The tomatoes have names now.|Tobias has added a second candle and a jar of wildflowers.} {The soup is his grandmother's recipe.|A tomato plant is leaning in to listen.|There is far too much bread.}\n> left: Eat everything, and ask for seconds\n  spark tobias +small\n  health +tiny\n  say: {You eat three bowls and he beams like the sun. He tells you about the farm, the goats, the year the river froze. You could listen for ever, and nearly do.|Seconds, thirds, a slice of pie. Tobias watches you eat with an expression you want to keep in a jar.|You eat until the bread is gone, which takes some doing. Tobias lights the second candle off the first and says he'll bake less next time. He won't.}\n> right: Help him in the beds after\n  rel tobias +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You weed the beds in the afternoon sun, side by side. Your back aches; your hands smell of tomato leaves. He hums the whole time.|An hour of planting. He shows you how to tell a seedling it's doing well. You feel ridiculous, and do it anyway, until your knees ache from the kneeling.|You work off the bread in the beds after. He picks you a wildflower for the jar and pretends it was a weed.}\n\n@event seraphine_date_a_future\ntitle: A Future She Saw\nactor: seraphine\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: partner_here(seraphine)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: eye\ntags: romance magic\nscene: A seer and a student lying on the observatory floor beneath the open dome, a scatter of tarot cards between them.\ntext: {Seraphine says she's seen a future with the two of you in it, and wants to tell you about it on the observatory floor, under the shuttered dome.|Another future, another hour on the observatory floor.|\"I saw us again,\" Seraphine says, lying down beside you. \"Older.\"} {She's shuffling the cards without looking.|The dome's painted stars have faded to grey.|She is, for once, entirely in the present.}\n> left: Ask her to tell you\n  spark seraphine +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {She tells you. A kitchen, a cat, a terrible argument about curtains, a garden. It's so ordinary you could cry. She says it's her favourite one.|A future with snow in it, and an old friend who comes to stay and never leaves. She stops halfway and holds your hand.|She tells you about the older two of you: slower, quieter, still arguing about curtains. She laughs at her own future and holds your hand all the way through it.}\n> right: Ask her not to\n  rel seraphine +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {\"Let it surprise us,\" you say. She looks at you as if no one has ever asked her that. Then she puts the cards away, all of them.|She's quiet a while, then laughs. \"You're the only person I can't read,\" she says. \"It's very restful.\"|\"Then don't tell me,\" you say. She doesn't. For a whole hour she's only here, with you, and that's the best future yet.}\n\n@event corvin_date_rooftop_supper\ntitle: Supper on the Roof\nactor: corvin\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: partner_here(corvin)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: map\ntags: romance\nscene: A tablecloth spread on a castle rooftop between chimneys, a candle in a jar, two students eating pastries above the sleeping towers.\ntext: {Corvin leads you through three passages and a window to a rooftop between chimneys, where a tablecloth and supper are waiting.|Another rooftop, another tablecloth. You don't ask where he finds them.|\"New roof,\" says Corvin. \"Better view. Mostly legal.\"} {There are pastries you don't recognise.|There's a candle in a jam jar.|He's brought cushions, which is unlike him.}\n> left: Ask where the supper came from\n  guile +tiny\n  spark corvin +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {\"Borrowed,\" he says. From the headmistress's kitchen. You eat it anyway, and it tastes of getting away with something. He grins all evening.|\"Don't ask, saint.\" You don't. The pastries are perfect.|\"Mostly legal,\" he repeats, when you ask. The supper is from the prefects' pantry. You eat it on his cushions and laugh until a window opens below.}\n> right: Just enjoy it\n  spark corvin +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You eat supper on the roof until the candle gutters and the cold comes up through the slates. He doesn't make a single joke for a whole hour, and when you notice, he kisses you to stop you saying so.|The chimneys are warm, the air is cold, and you climb down at midnight scraped and happy.|You lean back on the cushions and watch the towers go dark one by one. He says nothing clever all evening, and you like him better for it.}\n\n@event mir_date_invention\ntitle: They Built You Something\nactor: mir\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: partner_here(mir)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: romance\nscene: A cluttered workshop bench, a small brass contraption with wings sitting on it, a grinning inventor and a nervous student leaning away.\ntext: {Mir has built you a present, a brass bird that sings your name, mostly.|Another present from Mir's bench, ticking ominously.|Mir presents their newest invention with a flourish and a fire bucket.} {It's warm to the touch.|It has seven moving parts and one they don't explain.|\"It's probably safe,\" they say.}\n> left: Wind it up at once\n  chance likely:\n    spark mir +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {It sings your name, slightly wrong, all the way round the workshop, and lands in your hand. Mir bursts into happy tears and pretends it's soot.|It works. It works! They kiss you on the cheek and immediately start on version two.|It's probably safe, and for once it is. It hums a tune you both know and settles on your shoulder. Mir puts the fire bucket down, astonished.}\n  else:\n    spark mir +tiny\n    health -small\n    say: {It sings for three seconds and then explodes, gently, into springs. Mir is mortified. You laugh until they do too, and help them pick up every piece.|It sets your sleeve on fire. Mir has the bucket ready. \"Version two,\" they say grimly.|It's probably not safe after all. The fire bucket earns its keep, and Mir chalks \"probably\" on the bench as a warning to themself.}\n> right: Ask them how it works first\n  rel mir +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: {They explain for an hour, with diagrams. You understand a third of it and love all of it. You wind it up together, very carefully, and it works.|Two hours of explanation and three cups of tea. The bird sings at the end. You've never been so proud of a thing you didn't make.|They explain why it's probably safe, at length, with the fire bucket close by. It is. You wind it up together, and it works.}\n\n@event kael_date_snow\ntitle: Snow, Just for You\nactor: kael\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: partner_here(kael)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: snow\ntags: romance magic\nscene: Snow falling over one small patch of a spring lawn, two students standing in it, one holding out his hand to catch the flakes.\ntext: {Kael leads you to a corner of the lawn and makes it snow, only there, only on the two of you.|Another private snowfall. He's getting better at the shapes.|Kael has made it snow again. This time the flakes are stars.} {season == \"summer\": It's a hot day; the snow steams as it falls.|{The flakes are as big as coins.|Somewhere a bird is very confused.|It's the most he's said with his hands in weeks.}}\n> left: Catch the flakes\n  spark kael +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You catch them on your tongue until your teeth ache with cold, and laugh, and Kael, astonishingly, laughs too, out loud. The snow falls harder when he's happy, it turns out.|You're both soaked and frozen. He warms your hands in his, which have warmed, against all habit.|You catch a star-shaped flake on your glove and it doesn't melt for a whole minute. Kael watches it more than you do, and smiles when it goes.}\n> right: Ask him what it costs\n  rel kael +small\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {\"Nothing,\" he says. Then, honestly, \"A little cold in the chest, for a day.\" You make him stop. He looks at you as if no one had ever asked him to.|He tells you about Northern frost-craft and what it takes. Then he makes one last snowflake, small, and lets you keep it.|\"Less, now,\" he says, which isn't an answer. You make him stop anyway, and he lets the last star land in your hair.}\n\n@event nessa_date_off_record\ntitle: Off the Record\nactor: nessa\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: partner_here(nessa)\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: romance\nscene: A newspaper office late at night, an enchanted quill lying still on a desk for once, two students sharing a pot of tea among the proofs.\ntext: {Nessa locks the Ledger office, puts her quill in a drawer and says, \"Off the record. For as long as you like.\"|Another hour off the record. The quill sulks in its drawer.|Nessa has started saying \"off the record\" when she means \"I love you\". You've noticed.} {There's a pot of tea and a plate of toast.|The proofs are stacked and, for once, ignored.|It's raining on the skylight.}\n> left: Ask her about herself\n  spark nessa +small\n  say: {She talks about her childhood for an hour, which she never does. A printing shop, a father who read the news aloud. You don't take a single note. She notices.|Nessa, off the record, is quieter and funnier. You stay until the lamp runs out.|You ask about her, and she says \"off the record\" first, and then, quietly, a great deal. The rain on the skylight keeps it between the two of you.}\n> right: Tell her a secret of your own\n  rel nessa +small\n  say: {You tell her something nobody knows. She holds it like it's the most valuable story she's ever had, and never writes it down.|She listens, and then tells you one back. Off the record. It feels like a promise.|You tell her your secret. She says \"off the record\" back, and you both know what she means.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/grounds_more.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools, second pass (2026-10-24): grounds_more.\n#  Repeatable routine events for the grounds places with thin\n#  pools: the duelling hall, greenhouses, flight field, secret\n#  garden and courier loft. Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Duelling Hall ----------------\n\n@event duel_hall_reading_the_floor\ntitle: Reading the Floor\npool: duel_hall_routine\ntone: warm\nart: duel\ntags: magic\nscene: Scorched and scarred floorboards in an empty duelling hall, a student crouched over them tracing a burn mark with one finger.\ntext: {Every famous bout in the Duelling Hall left its mark on the floor, a scorch or a frost-crack or a gouge. Old duellists say you can read a fight in the boards.|The floor again. You've learned which scorch belongs to which year's champion, from the plaques on the wall.|You can read the boards now, more or less: here someone parried late; here someone lost their nerve and their eyebrows.} {Today a fresh burn sits on top of the old ones.|The cleaners have been, and the boards shine like a map under glass.|{season == \"winter\": The hall is freezing and the boards creak under every step.|Dust hangs in the long light from the windows.}}\n> left: Trace an old bout through\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You follow a duel from the first scorch to the final frost-crack, step by step, until you know where the loser went wrong. Your knees ache from crawling.|You walk a champion's footwork across the burns, slowly, like learning a dance from its footprints. It's tiring, careful work.|You read a whole tournament final in the floor and find the moment it turned, a single mark the size of a coin. You stand up stiff and certain.}\n> right: Scrub the floor for the club\n  gold +tiny\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The club pays a crown or two for scrubbing. You scrub until the boards gleam and your arms burn.|You scrub, and the old marks stay, as they always do; the dirt goes. You're paid in coins and a sore back.|You know which boards to go easy on: the famous ones. The club treasurer pays you, and adds a nod.}\n\n@event duel_hall_second_wanted\ntitle: A Second Wanted\npool: duel_hall_routine\ntone: light\nart: sword\ntags: rival social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer) and met(here)\nscene: Two students facing each other on a scorched duelling floor, wands lowered, while a third stands at the edge holding a coat and a handkerchief.\ntext: {{here} has a formal practice bout today and no second. \"Hold my coat, count the paces, drop the handkerchief. You can count, I assume.\"|{here} is looking for a second again, and spots you at the door before you can leave.|{here} tosses you the coat as you come in, as if it's been decided.} {The opponent is an upper-year with a terrible moustache.|The opponent bows too low, which is a kind of insult.|{season == \"winter\": The hall is so cold the wands spark on their own.|The gallery has a dozen spectators and a bookmaker.}}\n> left: Stand as the second\n  rel here +small\n  if npc_is(lyra):\n    say: {You count, she wins, she doesn't thank you. On the way out she hands you back your scarf, which you hadn't noticed she'd picked up.|She loses on a technicality and blames the handkerchief. Then she buys you a cider, without a word.|You count; she wins in nine seconds. \"Adequate,\" she says, which from Lyra is a medal.}\n  elif npc_is(bram):\n    say: {Bram makes you recite the rules of seconding before he lets you hold his coat. He wins, and gives you a demerit for dropping the handkerchief early, then rescinds it.|Bram loses and stands very straight. Afterwards, gruffly, \"Fair count. Thanks.\"|Bram wins, and for a moment looks up at the gallery as if for someone who isn't there.}\n  else:\n    say: {You count the paces, drop the handkerchief and hold the coat through an hour of shouting. {here} owes you one, and knows it.|You stand on the line through a long, sweaty bout. {here} thanks you warmly afterwards.|You second with the calm of long practice. {here} wins, and claps you on the shoulder like an old comrade.}\n> right: Offer to referee instead\n  arcana +small\n  renown +tiny\n  rel here -tiny\n  say: {You referee straight down the middle and call a foul against {here}. The gallery approves. {here} does not.|You referee, fairly, and learn more watching both wands than you would holding a coat. {here} feels a little abandoned.|Your call decides the bout. It's the right call; {here} won't admit it till next week.}\n\n# ---------------- The Greenhouses ----------------\n\n@pool greenhouses_routine\nplace: greenhouses\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\ntags: nature\n\n@event greenhouses_gerald_is_out\ntitle: Gerald Is Out\npool: greenhouses_routine\ntone: light\nart: leaf\nwhen: met(tobias)\nscene: A small mandrake in a lopsided knitted hat sitting in the wrong flowerpot among the seedlings, screaming very softly at a watering can.\ntext: {Gerald, Tobias's mandrake, has escaped his pot again and planted himself among the seedlings. His knitted hat has come unravelled.|Gerald is out again. This time he's in a teacup, screaming softly at a watering can.|Gerald has moved into the pot of the tallest fern, with an air of permanence. His hat is half unravelled; the wool trails back to his own pot.} {Tobias is nowhere to be seen.|A note on Gerald's empty pot says \"BACK SOON, DO NOT LET HIM NEAR THE TOMATOES.\"|{season == \"winter\": He's shivering, defiantly.|The seedlings around him are wilting with alarm.}}\n> left: Fix his hat and carry him home\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You re-knit the hat with two pencils and great patience. Gerald lets you, then bites your thumb, which Tobias says means he loves you.|You lift him gently out of the teacup. He screams softly the whole way home and bites you once at the end, fondly.|You tie off the trailing wool and carry him home in your cupped hands. The bite is affectionate. It still stings.}\n> right: Re-pot the seedlings he trampled\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {You leave Gerald where he is and save the seedlings he kicked out. He watches you work, screaming softly with approval.|You re-pot a row of uprooted seedlings and leave the teacup alone. Mandrakes are Tobias's department.|You patch up the damage around the fern and let Gerald keep his new home. It seems kinder than arguing with a mandrake.}\n\n@event greenhouses_grafting_bench\ntitle: The Grafting Bench\npool: greenhouses_routine\ntone: warm\nart: tree\nscene: A potting bench crowded with small grafted saplings bound in twine and wax, one of them bearing two different kinds of fruit on two different branches.\ntext: {On the grafting bench is the club's oldest experiment, a potted tree that bears pears on one branch and lemons on the other. Beside it, knives, twine, wax and an open invitation.|The grafting bench. Somebody's new graft has taken: a rose that grows on a gooseberry, looking pleased with itself.|The grafting bench. The two-fruit tree has a third branch now, and nobody will admit to it.} {The wax pot is warm and smells of honey.|A row of failed grafts stands along the back, each with a small apologetic label.|{season == \"spring\": Every graft on the bench is in bud.|The saplings are bare, and the twine shows.}}\n> left: Try a graft of your own\n  challenge brew apprentice+:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You splice a cutting of mint onto a lavender root and wax it shut. A week later it's growing; it smells like a summer evening.|Your graft takes. Someone pins a label to it with your name and a small drawn star.|You graft a cutting onto the two-fruit tree itself. It takes, and the tree now has one branch that is entirely yours.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The knife slips; so does the cutting. It joins the row of failures with its own small label, and you join the infirmary queue with a cut thumb.|Your graft sulks, browns and drops off within the week. Your thumb has a nick that takes longer to heal.|The twine won't hold and the wax burns your fingers. Your cutting goes to the failures' shelf.}\n> right: Label the old grafts\n  lore +small\n  say: {You work out from the bark scars which graft went on when, and write it all down. The two-fruit tree is older than anyone thought.|You label the rose on the gooseberry, which turns out to be harder than growing it.|You map the two-fruit tree branch by branch. The third branch has no record at all. You write \"unknown, but welcome\".}\n\n@event greenhouses_seed_tin\ntitle: The Seed Tin\npool: greenhouses_routine\ntone: warm\nart: leaf\nscene: An old biscuit tin of paper seed packets on a potting bench, every packet labelled in a different hand, one of them rattling on its own.\ntext: {On the potting bench is the club's seed tin. Take a packet, leave a packet, write what it is; most people write what it is.|The seed tin is fuller than last time. Someone has left a packet labelled simply \"SORRY\".|The seed tin. You know the regulars' handwriting now, and the one who only draws pictures.} {One packet is rattling on its own.|One packet is warm to the touch.|{season == \"spring\": Half the packets have started sprouting through the paper.|One packet hums a single note when you lift it.}}\n> left: Take the strangest packet\n  chance even:\n    alchemy +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {You plant it in a spare pot. By the end of the hour there's a tiny silver bell-flower that rings when you breathe on it.|You plant the warm one. It comes up in minutes, a fern that curls round your finger like a cat's tail.|You plant it and it grows into something nobody in the club can name. You get to name it. You choose well.}\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {It sprouts at once, straight up your nose. You sneeze pollen for the rest of the day.|It grows a single angry thorn and nothing else, and the thorn finds your thumb.|It's a nettle that stings in rhymes. You'll be hearing them in your sleep.}\n> right: Sort the tin properly\n  lore +small\n  say: {You sort the packets by family, season and handwriting. The club will never know it was you, which is the nice part.|You sort the lot and label the unlabelled ones. \"SORRY\" turns out to be turnips, which explains a great deal.|You file the picture-packets by guessing from the drawings. You're right about most of them, you think.}\n\n# ---------------- The Flight Field ----------------\n\n@event flight_field_kite_day\ntitle: Kite Day\npool: flight_field_routine\ntone: light\nart: feather\ntags: social\nscene: Enchanted paper kites shaped like dragons and fish wheeling and diving over a windswept green field, one tangled in a hoop at the far end.\ntext: {The wind is too wild for brooms, so the first-years are out with charmed kites, paper dragons and fish and one very angry swan.|Kite day again. The kites have learned to fight; a paper dragon has a fish in a headlock.|Whenever the wind is too wild for brooms, the kites come out. You know the swan by now, and the swan knows you.} One kite is stuck in the far hoop, {flapping like a trapped bird|and its first-year is close to tears|and the other kites are jeering at it}.\n> left: Climb the hoop and free it\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      renown +small\n      say: {You shin up the hoop pole and free the kite. The first-years cheer as if you'd won a race.|You climb, untangle, slide down. The first-year announces your name to the whole field.|You know the hoop pole's footholds by now. The kite goes back to its owner, who tells everyone, loudly.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You get halfway up the pole and the wind takes your hat and your grip. You land in the long grass, bruised.|The kite comes free and so do you, from rather high up.|You slip on the wet pole and slide down it, slowly and painfully, to applause.}\n> right: Borrow a kite and fly it\n  arcana +small\n  mood +small\n  say: {You borrow a fish kite and steer it with a whispered charm. It bites the swan. Everyone is delighted.|You fly a paper dragon in loops until your arms ache with the pull and your face aches with grinning.|You fly your borrowed kite higher than the rest, holding the charm steady against the gusts.}\n\n@event flight_field_mowing_day\ntitle: Mowing Day\npool: flight_field_routine\ntone: light\nart: broom\ntags: nature\nscene: A row of enchanted scythes swinging themselves across a green field in neat lines while students dart between them and others rake the cut grass.\ntext: {Today the enchanted scythes are mowing the Flight Field, swinging themselves in neat lines while the groundskeeper sleeps under a tree.|Mowing day. A game has sprung up among the scythes: run the line without breaking stride. The groundskeeper is asleep again.|The scythes are out. You know their rhythm now: swish, step, swish, a pause on every seventh.} {The smell of cut grass is everywhere.|Somebody's hat has already been mown in half.|{season == \"summer\": Heat shimmers over the stubble.|A light drizzle makes the grass shine.}}\n> left: Run the scythe line\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You dart through the line and out the other side without a scratch. The watching first-years make you do it again.|Swish, step, swish. You run the whole line and arrive at the end breathless and laughing.|You run the line on the seventh-beat pause and it feels like dancing. Nobody else has timed it that well.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {A scythe catches your robe and neatly removes a sleeve. You trip on the stump of it. The groundskeeper wakes, looks, and goes back to sleep.|You trip on a tussock and fall flat as the scythes pass politely overhead. The first-years cheer, the wrong way.|You mistime the seventh beat and go sprawling. Your hat joins the other hat. Your pride follows.}\n> right: Rake hay for the stables\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  gold +tiny\n  say: {You rake the cut grass into rows for the stables. The groundskeeper wakes long enough to give you a coin and a nod. Your palms blister.|You rake until the field is striped and your arms are lead. The stables send a crown, and a hippogriff's thanks.|You rake with the scythes' rhythm in your arms. The groundskeeper pays you without waking, which is a skill in itself.}\n\n# ---------------- The Secret Garden ----------------\n\n@pool secret_garden_routine\nplace: secret_garden\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\ntags: nature\n\n@event secret_garden_two_cups\ntitle: Two Cups on the Bench\npool: secret_garden_routine\ntone: warm\nart: cup\nscene: A mossy stone bench in a hidden garden, an old tartan flask and two chipped cups left on it, glowing ferns all around.\ntext: {On the bench by the humming pear there's a flask and two chipped cups, and a note in Old Maud's hand. \"One for you, dearie, one for whoever comes.\"|The flask on the bench again, still warm, still with two cups. Maud is never here when you are; the tea always is.|The flask, the cups, the note, and today a biscuit on a saucer beside them, for whoever comes.} {The silver bees are busy.|The ferns are glowing even in daylight.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the bench, steam from the flask.|Blossom has settled in one of the cups.}}\n> left: Sit and drink the tea\n  health +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {The tea tastes of honey and something green. You sit till the cup is cold and feel mended in some small, specific way.|You drink your cup and leave the second for whoever comes. Nobody does, but the pear hums as if someone had.|You drink, eat the biscuit, and wash both cups at the spring. The garden seems to approve of good manners.}\n> right: Weed the beds for her\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You leave the tea and weed the beds by the wall until your knees are green and your back is stiff. Maud will know, somehow.|You pull weeds that pull back, and one of them draws blood. The ferns lean away from the worst of them, gratefully.|You weed the far beds until your palms blister, and find a trowel Maud must have lost years ago. You leave it on the bench, by the cups.}\n\n@event secret_garden_remembering_moss\ntitle: The Moss That Remembers\npool: secret_garden_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: leaf\ntags: magic\nscene: A deep green bed of moss under the humming pear tree, holding the faint pressed shape of someone who lay there and is gone.\ntext: {Under the humming pear is a bed of moss so thick it keeps the shape of whoever last lay in it. Today it holds a small curled shape, like someone napping.|The moss again. Today's shape is long and straight, arms folded, as if someone lay there watching the leaves.|You know the moss's habits now. Today it holds two shapes, side by side, close.} {The shapes fade slowly, over an hour or so.|One silver bee is asleep in the hollow of an elbow.|{season == \"winter\": Frost rims the shape like a chalk outline.|The moss is warm where the shape is.}}\n> left: Lie down in the moss yourself\n  health +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You lie down beside the curled shape, not in it. The moss is warm and soft, and you sleep a while without dreaming.|You lie down and look up at the pear tree's leaves for an hour. When you get up, the moss keeps your shape too.|You lie down between the two shapes, careful not to spoil either. You wake rested, and a little lonely.}\n> right: Study the shapes, and wonder\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You kneel and read the shape. Small hands, knees drawn up, a hat left behind in the dent of a head. Someone came here to feel safe. It's a strange thing to know about a stranger.|You trace the long shape's folded arms. It looks like someone thinking very hard about something they couldn't say. You leave quietly.|Two shapes, close. You don't know whose, and you decide you shouldn't. It stays with you all evening.}\n\n@event secret_garden_snail_post\ntitle: The Snail Post\npool: secret_garden_routine\ntone: warm\nart: letter\nscene: A garden snail with a tiny folded paper note tucked under its shell, making its way slowly along a mossy wall.\ntext: {A snail is crossing the garden wall with a folded scrap of paper tucked under its shell. Maud's garden, it seems, has a postal service.|The snail post again. Three snails today, each with a note, all heading in different directions.|Snail post. You know the route now, along the wall and round the pear, about a day each way.} {The note within reach is folded twice and sealed with a blob of resin.|One note has been answered in a different hand.|{season == \"winter\": The snails are sluggish with cold, the notes damp.|The snail nearest you is wearing a dewdrop like a hat.}}\n> left: Write a note and send it on\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {You write something kind for a stranger and tuck it under a shell. The snail sets off, at snail speed, with your secret.|You answer a note left by someone you'll never meet. It feels like talking across a very slow, very gentle river.|You write back to whoever's been writing. The snail takes the note, and an age, and you don't mind.}\n> right: Read the notes on the way\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {You ease a note out from under a shell. It's a confession about a stolen apple, decades old. You put it back, a little ashamed.|You read three notes before the snails notice. One of them is about someone exactly like you.|You read the snail post like a gossip column. The snails take it personally; one refuses to move till you leave.}\n\n# ---------------- The Courier Loft ----------------\n\n@event courier_loft_record_board\ntitle: The Record Board\npool: courier_loft_routine\ntone: light\nart: broom\ntags: job\nscene: A chalkboard in a draughty loft listing delivery records to every tower and the village, some times rubbed out and rewritten, a student squinting at the fastest one.\ntext: {On the loft wall is the record board, the fastest delivery ever flown to every tower and the village, in chalk. The village record is eleven minutes. It has stood for twelve years.|The record board. Someone has shaved four seconds off the observatory run and written the new time very large.|The record board. You know every time on it now, and which ones the couriers say were cheated.} {A courier is idly offering a parcel to anyone who fancies a go.|The wind outside is behind you, for once.|{season == \"winter\": There's ice on the landing platform.|The air is still and the lake is glass.}}\n> left: Make a run at a record\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      renown +small\n      say: {You fly the lecture-tower run flat out and land four seconds inside the record. A courier chalks your name on the board, grumbling.|You take the observatory run, cut the corner by the bell tower and beat the new time by a breath. The loft cheers; the old record holder sulks.|You know the shortcuts now. Your name goes up on the board for the library run, in very large chalk.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You take the corner by the bell tower too fast and meet a gutter. The parcel arrives; you arrive limping.|You're on pace until a gust shoves you into a flagpole. Your time goes on the board anyway, under the heading SLOWEST.|You misjudge the landing platform and skid the length of it on your knees. The couriers award you a record for distance.}\n> right: Study the routes instead\n  lore +small\n  say: {You copy down every route on the board and the wind notes beside them. You won't break a record today, but you'll know how.|You ask the couriers how each record was flown. Three of the stories are lies; you can tell which.|You sit under the board working out a new route to the village. It might be a minute faster. Might.}\n\n@event courier_loft_pigeon_strike\ntitle: The Pigeons Are on Strike\npool: courier_loft_routine\ntone: light\nart: feather\ntags: job\nscene: A row of indignant pigeons sitting in a line on a loft rafter beside a scrap of paper listing their demands in tiny claw-marks.\ntext: {The loft's message pigeons are on strike. They sit on the rafter in a row, puffed and silent, beside a list of demands scratched in claw-marks.|The pigeons have struck again. The demands have grown: better grain, a window that shuts, and the removal of a particular courier.|The pigeons are on strike. They look at you as you come in, as if you've been appointed to negotiate.} {The undelivered notes are piling up.|One pigeon is plainly the ringleader; it has a bald patch and a stare.|{season == \"winter\": The window that doesn't shut is letting the snow in, which seems fair.|Every courier in the loft is pretending not to be afraid of the ringleader.}}\n> left: Talk the ringleader round\n  challenge soothe apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You offer seed, flattery and a promise about the window. The ringleader considers, coos once, and the strike is over.|You talk to the ringleader as you'd talk to a magistrate. It accepts your terms and a raisin.|The ringleader hears you out, eye to eye. The pigeons rise in a clatter and the backlog flies.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The ringleader hears your offer and pecks your knuckle, hard. Talks have broken down.|The whole rafter turns its back on you, in unison, which is very hard to recover from. One of them nips your ear on the way.|They accept the raisin and refuse the terms. One of them sits on your head, claws first, to make the point.}\n> right: Carry their notes by hand\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You run the undelivered notes round the castle yourself, all seven towers, and come back with sore legs and the pigeons' quiet contempt.|You deliver the notes on foot. The pigeons watch you go, smug as landladies. Your feet ache by the last tower.|You run the round on foot again. You're faster than a pigeon on the stairs, if not in the air, and your calves know it.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/grounds.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools: the grounds (phase 3, step 1b).\n#  Repeatable routine events for map visits to the lakeshore,\n#  duelling hall, greenhouses, flight field, stables, boathouse,\n#  orchard, Maud's cottage, the secret garden, the courier loft\n#  and the Whisperwood. Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Lakeshore ----------------\n\n@pool lakeshore_routine\nplace: lakeshore\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event lakeshore_shore_walk\ntitle: The Long Way Round\npool: lakeshore_routine\ntone: warm\nart: moon\ntags: romance nature\ncast:\n  date: any where romanceable(date) and rel(date) >= 12 and around(date)\nscene: Two figures walking a lakeside path at dusk, the towers reflected upside down in the still water beside them.\ntext: {You came out to walk alone. {date} finds you on the shore path and falls into step without asking.|The shore path is where you go to think. Today {date} has the same idea.|You know every stone of the shore path by now. Today {date} walks it with you.} {season == \"winter\": The lake is frozen at the edges and your breath walks ahead of you.|{season == \"summer\": The water is warm and full of sky.|{Mist lies on the water like a sheet someone forgot to fold.|The towers stand upside down in the lake, lit window by lit window.|The water is so still that a dropped leaf makes rings for a minute.}}}\n> left: Walk the long way round\n  spark date +small\n  rel date +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {final_year: Halfway round, {date} says \"next year\" and stops, because there won't be one here. You walk the rest closer together.|{season == \"winter\": {You walk until your toes go numb. {date} lends you {their} scarf and pretends not to be cold.|The ice sings under your boots. {date} takes your arm on the slippery stretch and forgets to let go.|You walk the frozen edge in step. {date} counts the herons out loud, and you let {them} lose count.}|{You walk until the far lamps go out. Neither of you mentions the time.|{date} points out a lit tower window and tells you who lives behind it. Half of it is invented. You don't mind which half.|You stand at the water's edge in silence, and the silence is the good kind.}}}\n> right: Talk about anything but us\n  rel date +small\n  spark date -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You talk about exams, the cook and the weather. It's easy. It's also not what either of you came for.|You argue cheerfully about whether the lake has a bottom. It's a good walk. It's only a walk.|You talk about the lake, the cook and everything except the thing. It gets easier to leave it unsaid, which is not a good sign.}\n\n@event lakeshore_cold_swim\ntitle: The Cold Swim\npool: lakeshore_routine\ntone: light\nart: wave\ntags: nature life\nscene: Students in towels on a lakeshore, one wading in up to the knees and shrieking, the towers reflected behind them.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Students in towels are daring each other into the lake through a hole in the ice.|{season == \"summer\": Half the academy is in the lake; the other half pretends not to want to be.|A few hardy students are swimming out to the flat rock, shrieking.}} {The water looks inviting. It always does.|You know how cold the Mirror Lake really is. You're tempted anyway.|You and the lake have an understanding. It doesn't help.}\n> left: Brave the water\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {season == \"winter\": You go under, come up roaring, and are hauled out a hero.|{You slap the flat rock, whoop, and swim back to applause from people with dry hair.|You reach the rock first. The cold reaches you second, but you were already winning.|You swim out to the rock and back without thinking about it. That's the trick, it turns out.}}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You come back blue to the lips and sneezing. Sister Amaranth sighs before you've even sat down.|Halfway out, the lake reminds you who is in charge. You turn back with what dignity you have left.|The lake wins again. Sister Amaranth has a blanket ready before you've reached the shore.}\n> right: Hold the towels and heckle\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You announce every dive like a herald. You're dry, popular and a tiny bit jealous.|You keep the towels warm and the commentary warmer. Someone throws a wet sock at you, which is fair.|You keep the towels and the score. The swimmers argue with your score, which means they trust it.}\n\n# ---------------- The Duelling Hall ----------------\n\n@pool duel_hall_routine\nplace: duel_hall\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event duel_hall_dummies\ntitle: The Practice Dummies\npool: duel_hall_routine\ntone: light\nart: shield\ntags: magic\nscene: An empty duelling hall with scorched floorboards and a row of battered straw practice dummies, one with a painted moustache.\ntext: {The near end of the hall belongs to the practice dummies, who watch you come in. The one with the painted moustache looks especially unforgiving.|The dummy with the moustache is still here, with a new scorch mark and, you'd swear, a new grudge.|The moustached dummy greets you with its usual silence. Someone has given it an eyepatch.} {The floorboards are still warm from the last session.|A singed glove lies abandoned on the rack.|Rain drums on the high windows.}\n> left: Drill your hexes on it\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {Hex after hex until your wrists ache. The dummy loses a sleeve and none of its contempt.|You land a clean one at last. A wisp of straw floats down, slowly, like applause.|You knock the eyepatch clean off. The dummy looks more menacing without it, which you didn't plan for.}\n> right: Restuff the battered ones\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You haul a bale of straw over from the stables and restuff three dummies. They look almost grateful, which is unsettling.|You restuff the moustached one with special care. It still looks like it's planning something.|You restuff the moustached one and straighten its eyepatch. It's the best-dressed thing in the hall.}\n> up[item:ashwood_wand]: Draw the ashwood\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The moustached dummy blocks, as dummies do, and the ashwood answers with a crack that singes its straw. Your wrist buzzes for an hour.|Every time a dummy's ward goes up, the ashwood bites through it with a spark. You stop when the hall smells of toast.|You duel the whole row. The ashwood jolts each block back at you, a little, and you learn to like it.}\n\n@event duel_hall_gallery\ntitle: From the Gallery\npool: duel_hall_routine\ntone: tense\nart: duel\ntags: magic social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where tagged(here, peer)\nscene: A narrow wooden gallery above a duelling floor, one student watching another drill spells at a target below.\ntext: {From the gallery above the duelling floor you can watch whoever is practising. Today it's {here}.|You've learned to read duels from up here. Today {here} is drilling.|The gallery rail has a groove where you lean. Below, {here} is drilling.} {Sparks drift up to the gallery rail.|Somebody's familiar is asleep on the scoreboard.|The ceiling has acquired a new scorch mark.}\n> left: Offer to spar\n  challenge combat vs here.arcana:\n    win:\n      rel here +small\n      arcana +tiny\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You take a round off {here}. {They} stare{s} at you for a long second, then ask{s} for another.|You win with a feint you stole from watching {here}. {They} notice{s}, and look{s} annoyed and pleased at once.|You spar with {here} as if you'd done it for years, because from the gallery, you have. {They} win{s} narrowly and look{s} at you differently.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      rel here +tiny\n      say: {{here} puts you on the floorboards, politely. From down there the ceiling's scorch marks make a sort of map.|{here} disarms you so neatly that the dummies seem to wince. Your wand skids under the gallery stairs.|{here} lands a spell that singes the ceiling and you with it. You add your own mark to the map.}\n> right: Watch and take notes\n  lore +small\n  say: {You fill a page with what {here} does with {their} feet. You'll never get to try it, and you know it.|You sketch {here}'s guard from above. On paper it has a gap. On the floor it doesn't.|You take notes from your groove at the rail. Your notes on {here} could fill a book now, and might.}\n\n@event duel_hall_humming_circle\ntime: evening\ntitle: The Humming Circle\npool: duel_hall_routine\nwhen: slot == \"evening\"\ntone: tense\nart: rune\ntags: magic\nscene: A dark duelling hall lit only by a ring of faintly glowing runes on the floor.\ntext: {The evening bouts are over. At the near end of the hall the warding circle is still humming.|The warding circle is humming. Whoever's drilling at the far end isn't looking your way.|The circle hums. You know its tune by now.} {final_year: Soon someone else will be sneaking in here after hours.|{A practice wand lies on the rack, still warm.|The steward's broom leans by the door, watching.|Tonight the ceiling stain looks like a dragon.}}\n> left: Step in and try a hard one\n  challenge spell adept-:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mana -small\n      say: {The spell comes out clean and the circle drinks it. Somewhere a dummy loses its head and nobody ever finds out how.|You try the thing you saw a fourth-year do. It works. You tell no one, and walk back grinning.|You cast the hard one and the circle hums the note back to you, a little higher. You think that's approval.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mana -small\n      say: {The circle hands your spell straight back to you. Your eyebrows will regrow.|The circle flares, the dummies rattle, and you leave very quickly before the steward arrives.|The circle swallows half your spell and spits out the other half in your direction. The steward's broom falls over, laughing.}\n> right: Sweep up for the steward\n  gold +tiny\n  say: {You sweep up a winter's worth of ash. The steward pays you a few crowns and a grunt that might be thanks.|You sweep. The circle hums at you the whole time, like a cat that wants to be let out.|You sweep in time with the hum. The steward says you're the only one who does it properly, and pays you for the compliment.}\n\n# ---------------- The Greenhouses ----------------\n\n@event greenhouses_warm_glass\ntitle: The Warm Glass\npool: greenhouses_routine\ntone: warm\nart: leaf\nscene: A student reading on an upturned pot among tall ferns inside a steamy glass greenhouse, snow on the panes outside.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Outside it's snowing. Inside, the glass runs with warm water and everything smells of earth.|{season == \"summer\": The greenhouses are sweltering, and everyone has retreated to the far end where the vents are.|The greenhouses steam gently. Someone is singing to the mandrakes at the far end.}} {You find a dry corner by the ferns, out of everyone's way.|Someone has left a cushion in your corner by the ferns.|The ferns lean toward you when you come in, as they do for you now.}\n> left: Do your reading in the warm\n  lore +small\n  say: {You get through a whole chapter. The pages curl in the damp and you come out smelling of moss.|You read until the steam makes the ink run. What's left of the chapter sticks.|You read with the ferns leaning in to listen. The chapter goes in easily, and the moss smell stays a day.}\n> right: Nap on the potting bench\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {You wake with a seed tray for a pillow and a first-year staring at you. By supper it's a story.|You wake to a mandrake humming a lullaby. You choose not to tell anyone. Someone tells everyone.|You nap on your cushion among the ferns. Someone has put a sign by your feet. It says \"Do not water\".}\n> up[item:hazel_wand]: Let the hazel find water\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The hazel dips over a bed of wilting seedlings. Under it you find a cracked pipe, and an hour of mud later the bed is drinking.|The wand tugs you to the far corner, where a rain barrel has silted up. You clear it with your sleeves rolled and come out filthy and pleased.|The hazel points at the mandrakes, firmly. They have been overwatered. You move them; they sulk; the wand seems satisfied.}\n\n@event greenhouses_repotting\ntitle: Repotting Day\npool: greenhouses_routine\ntone: warm\nart: flower\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place)\nscene: A student elbow-deep in compost at a greenhouse bench, handing a trowel to someone arriving.\ntext: {here} is up to the elbows in compost, repotting {season == \"spring\": seedlings that have outgrown everything|{season == \"autumn\": bulbs for the winter|something with more teeth than leaves}}. {\"Hands?\" {they} ask{s}.|{They} hand{s} you a trowel before you've said hello.|There's a trowel on the hook that has come to be yours. {here} nods at it.}\n> left: Get your hands dirty\n  rel here +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You repot a whole row. Something nips your thumb in thanks.|You're filthy, nipped and oddly happy. {here} says you have good hands for roots.|You take your trowel off its hook and repot the difficult ones. Nothing bites you, which {here} says is a first.}\n> right: Ask questions, stay clean\n  lore +small\n  rel here -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You learn a great deal about soil. {here} learns that you're afraid of it.|You take notes. {here} gives you a look over the trowel that says notes are not hands.|You ask your questions with your trowel in your hand, which {here} counts as progress.}\n\n@event greenhouses_cuttings\ntitle: The Cuttings Bench\npool: greenhouses_routine\ntone: tense\nart: potion\nscene: A greenhouse bench of labelled cuttings drying in rows, a row of snapdragons with actual teeth watching from their pots.\ntext: {A row of silver-leaf cuttings is drying on the bench|A tray of night-orchid cuttings sits by the door|A bundle of moonpetal cuttings is wrapped in damp cloth}, and Pell & Daughters pays well for that sort of thing. {Nobody is watching. The snapdragons are.|The snapdragons have seen you here before. They remember.|The snapdragons snap at you on principle now.}\n> left: Pocket a cutting\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {Pell's counts the leaves twice and pays without a question. You don't look at the snapdragons on your next visit.|It sells for more than you expected, which somehow makes it worse.|Moonpetal sells for twice the others. Pell's doesn't ask where it came from, which you notice.}\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {A snapdragon shrieks. Three Verdant students turn around at once. You put it back with a cough that convinces no one.|Your pocket rustles at the wrong moment. You spend the rest of the visit apologising to a fern.|The snapdragons have been waiting for this. They raise the alarm before your hand reaches the cloth.}\n> right: Feed the snapdragons\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {They take the food, then a little of your finger. You learn which end to hold the tongs by.|Fed, the snapdragons purr like kettles. One of them still bites you, out of habit.|You feed the snapdragons and they snap anyway, gently, out of principle. You've come to find it friendly.}\n\n# ---------------- The Flight Field ----------------\n\n@pool flight_field_routine\nplace: flight_field\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event flight_field_cheating_wind\ntitle: The Cheating Wind\npool: flight_field_routine\ntone: light\nart: broom\ntags: life\nscene: A long green field with tall hoops at either end, one student wobbling on a school broom against a gusting wind.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The flight field is white, the hoops wearing caps of snow.|{season == \"spring\": The field is soft with new grass and the wind is playful.|{The field is empty but for the wind, practising on its own.|A lone first-year is flying very careful circles.|A pair of fourth-years are racing low over the grass.}}} {The school brooms in the shed are, at best, opinionated.|Your usual broom is in the shed, the one that pulls left.|The broom that pulls left now pulls right, out of spite.}\n> left: Practise your landings\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  say: {A dozen landings. A few of them on purpose. The grass keeps a careful record of the rest.|The wind waits until you're nearly down, then shoves. You land in a hoop instead. Technically, a goal.|Your broom pulls right; you lean left. The landings come out straight, for once.}\n> right: Hire a decent broom\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: {The good broom goes where you look, not where it feels like. For an hour, flying is easy and the field is yours.|You fly the length of the field and back with the wind on your side for once. It costs a week's sweets.|You hire the good broom and leave the spiteful one in the shed. It will be waiting next time, and it will remember.}\n\n@event flight_field_pickup_race\ntitle: The Pick-Up Race\npool: flight_field_routine\ntone: light\nart: trophy\ntags: social life\nscene: Three students on brooms jostling at a crooked painted start line, a fourth holding up a scarf as a starting flag.\ntext: {A pick-up race is forming at the far hoops. Three brooms, two rules, no referee.|The pick-up racers are back. The rules have grown to four.|The pick-up race is a fixture now. Someone has painted a finish line, crookedly.} {A second-year from Emberhall is taking bets.|A Brasswick student has bolted a lantern to their broom-tail.|A Rootward student is flying barefoot, for grip.}\n> left: Borrow a broom and race\n  challenge feat journeyman+ wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You win by a broom's length and a lot of shouting. The second-year taking bets has to pay out and never forgives you.|You take the last hoop sideways and cross first. Someone adds a new rule, about you.|You cross the crooked finish line first, barefoot Rootward student close behind. Your name goes up on the post in chalk.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You finish last, and partly in a hedge.|The wind takes your side, then changes its mind. You walk the broom back to the start.|You lose to someone flying barefoot. You consider the lesson in this and decide against it.}\n> right: Bet a few crowns on the outsider\n  gold -tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    gold +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {The outsider wins on a gust nobody else saw coming. You are suddenly very good at betting.|The outsider comes in first by a nose. You toast the nose.|You bet on the barefoot flyer. Grip, it turns out, is everything.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {The outsider stops halfway to admire the view. Your crowns go with them.|Your flyer is disqualified for a rule invented on the spot. You don't argue. It's a good rule.|Your outsider's broom throws them at the first hoop. The crowns stay where they landed, which is not with you.}\n\n@event flight_field_long_grass\ntitle: The Long Grass\npool: flight_field_routine\ntone: light\nart: pouch\ntags: life\nscene: The long grass at the edge of a flight field, scattered with things dropped from brooms, a student crouching to look.\ntext: {The long grass at the edge of the flight field catches everything that falls off a broom.|Someone has planted a little flag in the long grass, to mark where things land.|You've come to think of the long grass as the academy's real lost-and-found.|Somebody has started a ledger of the long grass, pinned to the fence. Today's entry is yours to write.} Today it's {a velvet glove|a letter with no envelope|a single boot, laced|someone's notes for an exam}. {season == \"autumn\": The leaves are trying to bury it.|{season == \"winter\": It's half under frost.|A grasshopper is sitting on it.}}\n> left: Keep what you find\n  guile +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    renown -small\n    say: {The owner lands right beside you while you're still holding it. There's a short, very long silence.|A second-year marches over the grass shouting \"Thief!\" at the sky in general and you in particular.|A boy hops across the field on one foot, shouting. He wants his boot back, and he wants to know why you're holding it.|The owner of the notes finds you reading them. Their exam is tomorrow. It turns out yours is too.}\n  else:\n    say: {Finders, keepers. The grass doesn't argue, and neither does your conscience, much.|Nobody comes looking. You tell yourself that settles it.|It's a good boot. You'll need another one, but it's a start.|The notes are better than yours. You tell yourself that settles it.}\n> right: Carry it to the porter's lodge\n  alignment +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {It's a long walk round the field. The porter writes it in a book and gives you a look of mild surprise.|The porter thanks you with a peppermint from a jar that has clearly been open since the founders.|The porter puts the boot on a shelf of single boots, all left feet. This one is a right. He looks almost moved.|The porter reads the first page of the notes, says \"Hm,\" and files them under urgent.}\n\n# ---------------- The Familiar Stables ----------------\n\n@pool stables_routine\nplace: stables\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\ntags: nature\n\n@event stables_owl_loft\ntitle: The Owl Loft\npool: stables_routine\ntone: warm\nart: owl\nscene: A hay loft in old stables, rows of sleepy owls on the rafters above a student reading by a lantern.\ntext: {Up the ladder, above the hippogriffs, is a hay loft full of owls and quiet.|The owls open one eye each, decide you're furniture, and go back to sleep.|The owls have stopped opening their eyes for you. You've been accepted.} {season == \"winter\": The warmth of the animals rises through the boards.|{Rain ticks on the roof.|A stray letter has fallen into the hay.|The griffon chick is complaining somewhere below.}}\n> left: Read up among the owls\n  lore +small\n  say: {You read until the light goes. The hay gets in everywhere, and the chapter gets in too.|An owl reads over your shoulder for a whole page, then hoots, pointedly, at a footnote.|You read until the griffon below stops complaining. The owls don't stir. You belong up here now.}\n> right: Help sort the owl post\n  charm +small\n  say: {You sort a dropped bundle of post by hand. One owl seems to know which letters you're going to misfile.|You deliver three soggy letters to their owners on the way back. One of them hugs you, which you weren't ready for.|You sort the owl post by the light of the hatch. An owl drops a letter in your lap; it's for you.}\n\n@event stables_brask_rounds\ntitle: Brask's Rounds\npool: stables_routine\ntone: light\nart: paw\nscene: A burly professor stroking a hippogriff's beak in a stable aisle, ignoring a student holding two feed buckets.\ntext: {Professor Brask is on her rounds, telling each hippogriff she's a very good girl. She tells you to mind the bucket.|Brask is on her rounds. She nods at you, which is more than most get.|Brask greets you by name now, somewhere after the owls.} {season == \"spring\": There are new hippogriff foals, all legs and beak.|{A hippogriff is sulking about its feathers.|The feed is late and everyone is cross.|The stable cat is asleep in the oats.}}\n> left: Ask Brask a question\n  lore +small\n  say: {She answers it at length, to the hippogriff. You take notes anyway.|\"Good question,\" she says, to the owl. You decide that counts.|She answers you directly, which has never happened. The stable cat wakes up to see what's going on.}\n> right: Carry the feed buckets\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Every stall, two buckets each. Brask says nothing, which is her way of saying thank you.|A hippogriff bows to you over its bucket. Brask looks almost jealous.|You carry the buckets and work around the cat in the oats. Brask hands you the last bucket without being asked.}\n\n@event stables_griffon_sulk\ntitle: The Sulking Griffon\npool: stables_routine\ntone: tense\nart: feather\nscene: A griffon chick with puffed-up feathers glaring from the corner of a stall, a student holding out a morsel at arm's length.\ntext: {The griffon chick is sulking in the corner stall, feathers puffed, refusing breakfast.|The griffon chick is sulking again. It's bigger this time, and so is the sulk.|The griffon is hardly a chick any more, and still sulks like one. It watches you come in.} {year == 1: The sign on the stall says not to hand-feed it, underlined twice.|{The sign on the stall now has a drawing of a bitten hand.|The sign on the stall has been underlined until the paper tore.|Someone has added \"I mean it\" to the sign, in Brask's hand.}}\n> left: Offer it breakfast by hand\n  challenge soothe apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {It considers you for a long time, then takes the morsel with great delicacy. You feel chosen.|It eats from your hand and then, briefly, leans on you. You don't move for ten minutes.|It takes the morsel and then nudges your hand for another. Brask, passing, crosses out \"I mean it\".}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {It takes the morsel, a corner of your sleeve and a nip of your thumb. The sign was right.|It turns its back on you so pointedly that a passing owl looks embarrassed, and its tail catches you hard across the shins on the way round.|It takes the morsel and your glove, and then the sign agrees with it. Brask adds a second underline.}\n> right: Leave it to its sulk\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {You read the notice about griffon moods instead. It is mostly a list of things that will not help.|You leave it alone. It looks, as you go, a little disappointed you gave up.|You leave it to sulk. It follows you along the bars, sulking at a closer range.}\n\n# ---------------- The Boathouse ----------------\n\n@pool boathouse_routine\nplace: boathouse\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event boathouse_jetty_end\ntitle: The End of the Jetty\npool: boathouse_routine\ntone: warm\nart: lantern\ntags: romance\ncast:\n  date: any where romanceable(date) and rel(date) >= 12 and around(date)\nscene: Two people sitting side by side at the end of a wooden jetty, boots over dark water, a lantern between them.\ntext: {{date} is at the end of the jetty, boots over the water. {They} move{s} over without a word.|The end of the jetty has become your place for saying things. Today {date} is already there.|You know which plank creaks. {date} doesn't, and finds it.} {season == \"winter\": The lake has frozen round the posts and creaks like an old house.|{The rowing boats knock gently together.|A heron fishes in the shallows, pretending not to listen.|The village lamps blink on across the water.}}\n> left: Hire a boat and row out\n  gold -small\n  spark date +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": You can't row on ice, so you sit in a beached boat and pretend. {date} calls it a fine voyage.|{You row badly, {date} laughs, and then neither of you is rowing at all.|Out in the middle the shore goes quiet. {date} says your name like the end of a sentence.|You row out until the village lamps are behind you. {date} trails a hand in the water and doesn't say anything for a long time.}}\n> right: Stay on the jetty and talk\n  rel date +small\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {final_year: You talk about where you'll both be next year. Neither of you says \"together\", and both of you hear it.|{You talk until the lantern gutters. The jetty is cold and you don't notice until you stand.|You say one of the things you couldn't say indoors. {date} listens all the way to the end.|You stay on the jetty, on the plank that creaks. {date} says it sounds like you, and won't explain.}}\n> up[item:edmunds_silver_wand]: Let Edmund's wand hum\n  spark date +small\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Over the water the silver wand hums one low note. {date} goes quiet to listen, and so do you, until the lantern gutters.|The wand hums, and a ring spreads on the still water as if something answered. {date} takes your hand without asking why.|You hold the wand out over the lake and it hums its one note. {date} says it sounds like someone remembering. You think so too.}\n\n@event boathouse_green_boat\ntitle: The Leaky Green Boat\npool: boathouse_routine\ntone: warm\nart: wave\ntags: life\nscene: Inside an old wooden boathouse, an upturned green rowing boat on trestles, initials carved into every beam above.\ntext: {The boathouse smells of tar and old rope. Its rowing boats are mostly leaks held together by paint, the green one worst of all.|Someone has patched the green boat since your last visit, badly, with what looks like a cake tin.|The boathouse knows you. The green boat still leaks.} {Initials are carved into every beam, some so old they've gone soft.|Rain patters on the roof.|A spider has strung a web across the oars, and seems proud of it.}\n> left: Patch the green boat\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You spend an hour with tar and a mallet. The green boat floats, briefly, with an air of surprise.|Your patch holds for a whole circuit of the boathouse. You have tar in your eyebrows and a certain pride.|You patch the green boat again. It leaks again. You're starting to think that's what it's for.}\n> right: Carve your initials in a beam\n  hint: The boatman charges a few crowns a beam.\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {final_year: You pay the boatman and carve them next to a pair from sixty years ago. Somebody, one day, will run a thumb over yours and wonder.|{You pay the boatman, and your penknife snaps on the last letter. The initials stay, slightly wrong, which feels right.|You pay the boatman, find a carving that could almost be yours from long ago, and add your own beneath it.|You pay the boatman and carve your initials under the spider's web. The spider doesn't object.}}\n\n@event boathouse_overheard\ntitle: Things Said Outside\npool: boathouse_routine\ntone: tense\nart: eye\ntags: social\nscene: Two silhouettes talking quietly on a jetty at night, seen through the slats of a boathouse wall.\ntext: {Two voices out on the jetty, low and urgent. People come to the boathouse to say what they can't say indoors.|You've found the gap in the boathouse planks where every word on the jetty comes through.|Another quiet conversation on the jetty. You're starting to know the academy by its secrets.} {It sounds like a break-up.|It sounds like an apology that isn't going well.|It sounds like someone confessing to a pudding.}\n> left: Listen a little longer\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    renown -small\n    say: {A plank creaks under you. The voices stop. Two very red faces come round the door, and now they know your face too.|An oar slides off its peg with a clatter. Footsteps; a hiss of \"Who's there?\"; you are, unfortunately.|You lean on the plank you know creaks. The pudding confession stops mid-sentence, and two faces you know appear at the door.}\n  else:\n    say: {You learn more than you wanted to. You'll carry it carefully, and never tell.|It ends well, as it happens. You slip away before anyone has to know you were there.|It's about a pudding, honestly. You leave knowing more than any academy should about one pudding.}\n> right: Cough loudly and leave\n  alignment +tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {They wait for you to pass, hushed and grateful. You'll never know how it ended, and that's the price.|You make a great show of looking for an oar. By the time you've found one, they've gone.|You cough and leave. On the path you meet the pudding's owner, and decide not to say anything.}\n\n# ---------------- The Orchard ----------------\n\n@pool maud_cottage_routine\nplace: maud_cottage\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event orchard_under_the_trees\ntitle: Under the Old Trees\npool: maud_cottage_routine\ntone: warm\nart: tree\ntags: romance nature\ncast:\n  date: any where romanceable(date) and rel(date) >= 12 and around(date)\nscene: Two people beneath an ancient apple tree, blossom drifting down around them like snow.\ntext: {season == \"spring\": Blossom is coming down like snow, and {date} has some in {their} hair.|{season == \"autumn\": The trees are heavy with apples and wasps, and {date} is reaching for one.|{season == \"winter\": The orchard is bare and silver, and {date} is walking between the trees.|{date} has claimed the best of the orchard shade.}}} {{They} wave{s} you over.|{They} saw you coming before you saw {them}.|The old trees seem to expect you by now. {date} only half does.}\n> left: Stay until the light goes\n  spark date +small\n  health -small\n  say: {season == \"spring\": {You stay until the blossom buries your shoes. You miss supper, and you'd do it again.|{date} picks blossom out of your hair, one petal at a time, and takes far too long about it.|You lie in the blossom until you're both freckled with it. {date} says it suits you, and keeps a petal.}|{season == \"winter\": {Your fingers go white, and {date} takes your hands to warm them, and doesn't give them back.|You lean against the same cold trunk until the stars come out over the bare branches.|You walk the bare rows until the frost comes up. {date} breathes on your hands and says it's for practical reasons.}|{You stay until the shadows outgrow the trees. You miss supper. Neither of you mentions it.|The light goes gold, then blue. {date} says something quiet you'll turn over for days.|You stay until the first star. {date} names it after something you said, and won't tell you which thing.}}}\n> right: Help in the orchard together\n  rel date +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {season == \"autumn\": {You fill two baskets and earn one wasp sting each. The cook gives you a pie, which you share on the steps.|{date} holds the ladder and says nothing useful, and you don't want {them} to.|You pick from the top branches while {date} sorts the basket below. Every good apple goes to you, which you notice.}|{season == \"winter\": {You prune dead wood side by side until your hands ache. {date} stacks it with great seriousness.|You wrap the young trees against the frost. {date} ties every knot twice.|You mend the orchard fence together, one post each. {date}'s post is straighter, and {they} won't stop mentioning it.}|{season == \"spring\": {You carry hives along the rows for the blossom. One bee follows {date} all afternoon.|You clear the grass under the blossom. {date} keeps stopping to look up.|You graft a branch onto an old tree. {date} says you'll both have to come back to see if it takes.}|{You water the young trees from a bucket chain of two. {date} is soaked by the end, and so are you.|You prop up a branch bowed with early apples. {date} holds it, and holds still, and you notice.|You rake the cut grass into rows. {date} lies down in the first row, and the work goes no further.}}}}\n\n@event orchard_windfalls\ntitle: Windfalls\npool: maud_cottage_routine\ntone: warm\nart: pouch\ntags: nature life\nscene: A student gathering fallen fruit into a basket under gnarled orchard trees, older than the towers behind them.\ntext: {The orchard trees are older than the towers, and never tidy.|The oldest tree has dropped its fruit in a ring, like a table laid for guests.|You know which tree drops what by now, and which one throws.} {season == \"autumn\": Pears and apples lie bruised in the grass, attended by wasps.|{season == \"winter\": Last year's apples lie frozen, hard as stones.|{season == \"spring\": It's mostly blossom, and the odd stubborn pear.|Early apples lie in the shade, small and sharp.}}}\n> left: Gather windfalls to sell\n  gold +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The cook buys your basket for the cider vats and finds {season == \"winter\": a frozen beetle|a wasp} in it. You both pretend that's normal.|A full basket, {season == \"winter\": numb fingers|two stings} and a crick in your back. The crowns feel earned.|You go straight to the trees that drop the good ones. The cook pays for a full basket and says you have an eye.}\n> right: Eat one under the oldest tree\n  mood +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    health -small\n    say: {{season == \"winter\": It's frozen solid. Your teeth will remember this.|There was a wasp in it. There was, briefly, a wasp in you.}|It's sour enough to turn your face inside out, and your stomach complains about it until supper. A passing first-year laughs, and so, eventually, do you.|The one that throws gets you on the shoulder. You eat its apple out of spite, and it's {season == \"winter\": frozen through|full of wasp}, and so is your mood.}\n  else:\n    health +tiny\n    say: {It's the best thing you've eaten all month. The old tree creaks, as if pleased.|You eat it slowly. Above you, the oldest tree drops one more, gently, beside your hand.|You eat it under the oldest tree, as you always do now. It drops a leaf on your knee, and you keep it.}\n\n@event orchard_cider_shed\ntitle: The Cider Shed\npool: maud_cottage_routine\ntone: light\nart: cup\ntags: social life\nscene: A tumbledown orchard shed with a wooden cider press inside, three students turning the screw and a fourth keeping watch at the door.\ntext: {In the orchard shed, a few students are running an unofficial cider press.|The lookout at the cider shed has started nodding you in.|The cider shed is a fixture now. Even the lookout seems bored.} {season == \"autumn\": The press groans with this year's apples.|The shed smells of last autumn and wet straw.} {A goat is watching from the hedge, very carefully saying nothing.|Someone has carved a menu into the door.|A prefect's scarf hangs on a nail, unexplained.}\n> left: Buy a cup\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    renown -tiny\n    say: {A prefect turns up as you raise the cup. Everyone is suddenly very interested in apples.|The lookout whistles too late. A prefect takes your name, then, after some thought, your cup.|The prefect whose scarf it is comes back for it. Everyone looks at the scarf, then at you, holding a cup.}\n  else:\n    say: {It's sharp, cold and a little bit fizzy. The day goes golden at the edges.|You drink it on an upturned bucket while someone tells a story about a goose. Excellent cider. Better goose.|You drink it under the prefect's scarf. Nobody asks, and the cider is the best yet.}\n> right: Help turn the press, for a share\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You turn the screw until your shoulders burn. The second-year in charge teaches you why some apples are better bruised.|You're sticky to the elbows and you've learned more about fermentation than in a term of class.|You turn the press with the regulars now. The second-year gives you a jar of your own to take home.}\n\n# ---------------- Old Maud's Cottage ----------------\n\n@event maud_cottage_kettle\ntitle: Tea at Maud's\npool: maud_cottage_routine\nwhen: slot == \"evening\"\ntone: warm\nart: cup\ntags: home\nscene: A low cottage kitchen, a kettle on the hob, an old woman pouring tea into a chipped cup, a goat's head poking through the open door.\ntext: {Old Maud's kettle is nearly boiling, as it always is. \"Sit down, dearie,\" she says. \"You look like a puddle with a worry.\"|The kettle is nearly boiling. Maud has already put out your cup, the chipped one.|Your chipped cup is waiting. Maud says it's been asking after you.|Maud pours before you've sat down. \"Same as ever, dearie,\" she says, and it is.} {Bartholomew is eating a tea towel.|Rain drums on the thatch.|A hen is asleep on the windowsill.|Bees drone in the eaves.}\n> left: Stay for a second cup\n  mood +small\n  rel maud +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Maud tells you a sad story the way other people give a recipe. You walk home late and cold, thinking about it.|\"Never trust a dry spring, dearie,\" she says. You won't understand it for a fortnight.|The second cup becomes a third. It's dark when you leave, and you've only half noticed.|You stay for a second cup and the bees in the eaves put Maud to sleep mid-story. You wash the cups and let yourself out.}\n> right: Ask her for a remedy\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {She brews you something that tastes of ditches. \"Down in one, dearie.\" You feel better at once, and resent it.|\"For what ails you,\" she says, and doesn't say what she thinks that is. It tastes of nettles and of being right.|She hands you a remedy before you've said what's wrong. It works, which is the annoying part.|\"Honey and bee-balm, dearie, and stop worrying.\" It tastes of summer. You stop worrying, briefly.}\n\n@event maud_cottage_bartholomew\ntitle: Bartholomew Is Out\npool: maud_cottage_routine\ntone: light\nart: house\ntags: home\nscene: A goat standing triumphantly on the roof of a henhouse, chickens scattering, a student approaching with an apple held out.\ntext: {Bartholomew the goat has got out.|Bartholomew is out again, and has learned to lift the gate latch with his teeth.|Bartholomew has escaped so often that the hens no longer scatter.} {slot == \"evening\": Maud doesn't even look up from her knitting.|Maud's note on the door says she's at the greenhouses, and to mind the goat.} {He has eaten half a washing line.|He is standing on the roof of the henhouse, looking pleased.|He is in the cabbages, conducting a slow and thorough war.}\n> left: Chase him down\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      rel maud +small\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: {You corner him by the water butt. He gives up with the air of someone who allowed it. {slot == \"evening\": \"Ta, dearie,\" calls Maud.|You tie him to the gate and leave Maud a note.}|You catch him on the second lap. He chews your sleeve all the way back, as a souvenir.|You chase him out of the cabbages with the ease of long practice. The hens don't even look up.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {He leads you twice round the orchard and once through the hen run, then trots home on his own.|You end up in the cabbages. Bartholomew watches you from the roof, chewing, unhurried.|He leads you through the cabbages and leaves you there. The war continues without you.}\n> right: Bribe him with your lunch\n  rel maud +tiny\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {He takes your sandwich and follows you home like a gentleman. You go hungry until supper.|Your lunch disappears in three bites. Bartholomew looks at you as if you might be worth knowing.|He takes your lunch and walks home ahead of you. He knows the way better than you do by now.}\n\n@event maud_cottage_top_shelf\ntitle: The Top Shelf\npool: maud_cottage_routine\ntone: tense\nart: bottle\ntags: magic home\nscene: A high cottage shelf crowded with stoppered jars and handwritten labels, one jar glowing faintly blue.\ntext: {{slot == \"evening\": Maud has stepped out to the hens.|Maud is up at the greenhouses.} The top shelf of jars is right there, every label in her spidery hand.|Maud has stepped out, and the top shelf has gained a jar since you were last here.|You know the top shelf by heart, except for whichever jar Maud added last.} One label reads {\"not for you, dearie\"|\"only in emergencies\"|\"do not shake\"}, and the jar is {glowing faintly|humming|warm to the touch}.\n> left: Open it, just a little\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance even:\n    mana +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {Something sweet and blue drifts out and settles in your chest. You feel, briefly, enormous.|A breath of summer rain escapes. You put the lid back on smiling.|It's warm, and so is the air that comes out. You stand in it like a cat in a sunbeam until you hear Maud's step.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {Your eyebrows turn the colour of the label. When Maud sees them, she says nothing whatsoever.|It sneezes. Then you sneeze, for an hour. \"Told you, dearie,\" says Maud later, not unkindly.|You shook it, a little. The label was right. Your hair stands on end until supper.}\n> right: Dust the shelf instead\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {You dust every jar and read every label. Maud's handwriting is a whole education, if a strange one.|Maud finds a clean shelf. \"Somebody's been learning,\" she says, which isn't quite thanks.|You dust the new jar first and read its label twice. Maud finds you there and gives you a look that's almost proud.}\n\n# ---------------- The Secret Garden ----------------\n\n@event secret_garden_humming_pear\ntitle: The Humming Pear\npool: secret_garden_routine\ntone: warm\nart: tree\ntags: magic\nscene: A student lying in the grass under a pear tree in a hidden walled garden, ferns glowing softly around them.\ntext: {The pear tree in Maud's secret garden is humming to itself.|The pear tree has changed its tune since you were last here, as if it's been practising.|The pear tree hums when you come in now. You'd like to think it's for you.} {season == \"winter\": Its tune is low and slow, like something asleep.|{season == \"spring\": Its tune has gone quick and bright with blossom.|{Its tune is one you almost know.|Its tune has a sad turn in the middle.|Its tune is one you've heard Maud whistle.}}}\n> left: Hum along with it\n  mana +small\n  say: {You find the harmony on the third try. The tree corrects you twice, kindly.|The two of you hum until the ferns glow brighter. Afterwards you can't get the tune out of your head for days.|You hum along without thinking now. The pear tree drops a pear in your lap at the end, which feels like applause.}\n> right: Nap in its shade\n  mood +small\n  health +small\n  if gold > 0:\n    gold -tiny\n    say: {You wake rested, a few crowns lighter, with a fresh root curled over the spot where they fell.|You sleep like a seed. A few crowns slip out of your pocket and the roots close over them. Rent, perhaps.|You sleep in your usual hollow between the roots. A few crowns are gone when you wake, and the tree looks very innocent.}\n  else:\n    say: You sleep like a seed and wake with your empty pocket turned out, as if the roots had checked.\n\n@event secret_garden_silver_bees\ntitle: The Silver Bees\npool: secret_garden_routine\ntone: light\nart: sparkle\ntags: magic\nscene: Bees made of silver light hovering around a hive in a hollow stump, a student reaching carefully towards a dripping comb.\ntext: {The silver bees are working the ferns; their hive in the old stump drips honey the colour of moonlight.|The silver bees have built a second comb, lopsided, as if in a hurry.|The silver bees know you now, more or less.} {season == \"winter\": Even in winter they fly, slowly, like snow going the wrong way.|{The comb is fuller than you've ever seen it.|One of them lands on your sleeve and stays.|They're dancing a direction you can't read.}}\n> left: Take a little comb\n  alchemy +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    health -small\n    say: {A silver sting feels like a cold spark. Your hand glows for an hour and aches for two.|The bees file a complaint, in the form of your thumb.|They know your hand by now, and sting it anyway, more out of habit than anger.}\n  else:\n    say: {You take a thumb's width of comb. It tastes like starlight and burns a little going down.|The bees let you take a little, and watch to make sure it's only a little.|They let you take a little and dance something at you afterwards. You'd give a lot to know what it meant.}\n> right: Follow a bee home\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The bee leads you through brambles to a fern you've never seen, and then leaves you there, scratched and curious.|You follow it all the way round the garden and back to the stump. It seems to find this funny.|You follow a bee that's dancing the strange direction. It leads you to the garden wall, and over it, and you can't follow.}\n\n@event secret_garden_glowing_ferns\ntitle: The Glowing Ferns\npool: secret_garden_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: leaf\ntags: magic\nscene: A hidden garden at dusk, waist-high ferns glowing pale green, one student crouched with fingers near a frond.\ntext: {The ferns in the secret garden glow when the light goes. One frond would light your dormitory for a week. Maud said not to pick them.|Since your last visit a new fern has unfurled by the gate, already glowing.|The ferns glow. You know exactly what a frond is worth, and what Maud said.} {One has grown over the path, right at your hand.|A broken frond lies on the ground already, still glowing.|They're brighter than you've ever seen them.}\n> left: Pick a single frond\n  mood +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    mood -small\n    say: {The frond goes dark before you reach the door. The garden feels a little colder to you the next time.|The frond curls up in your hand like a woodlouse and goes out. The silver bees follow you to the gate, accusingly.|You pick the brightest one. It goes out in your hand like a sigh, and the rest dim a little as you pass.}\n  else:\n    say: {It lights your dormitory green for a week. You read by it, and feel very slightly watched.|You keep it in a jar by your bed. It glows softer every night, like something being polite.|It's the brightest frond you've ever seen, and it lights your room for a fortnight. You don't tell Maud.}\n> right: Leave them glowing\n  lore +small\n  say: {You sit and watch the glow instead, and learn the way it moves with your breath. Maud would approve, if she knew.|You leave them be. On the way out, the ferns lean after you, like a dog that wanted to come.|You sit among them at their brightest. They glow brighter while you stay, and that's enough.}\n\n# ---------------- The Courier Loft ----------------\n\n@pool courier_loft_routine\nplace: courier_loft\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event courier_loft_letter_home\ntitle: A Letter Home\npool: courier_loft_routine\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A draughty loft full of pigeonholes, a student writing a letter on a parcel by the light of a high window.\ntext: {The courier loft will take a letter home for next to nothing. You've meant to write for weeks.|This time the letter has been half-written in your head since breakfast.|Writing home is a habit now, and the loft knows your handwriting.} {final_year: There aren't many letters left to send from here.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the window; the pigeonholes stuffed with cards.|{The dispatch bell rings twice while you write.|A broken broom leans against your elbow.|A courier is reading over your shoulder, and apologises.}}}\n> left: Write it all down and send it\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You write three pages and post them before you can cross anything out. You feel lighter, and a little homesick.|You tell them about your classes, and not about the hard parts. Then, in a postscript, you tell them about the hard parts.|You write the letter you've written before, with this month's news in it. The courier says your family must look forward to them.}\n> right: Write it, then don't send it\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You read it over and fold it into your pocket instead. Some letters are only for writing.|You keep this one. The courier asks if you're sure. You aren't.|You put this one with the others you didn't send. It's getting to be quite a stack.}\n\n@event courier_loft_dispatch_bell\ntitle: The Dispatch Bell\npool: courier_loft_routine\ntone: light\nart: bell\ntags: life\nscene: A chaotic loft as a dispatch bell clangs, couriers grabbing brooms and parcels, one holding a parcel out to a visitor.\ntext: {The dispatch bell clangs and the loft explodes into brooms. A courier shoves a parcel at you. \"You've got legs. Lecture tower!\"|The couriers have started eyeing the ladder whenever someone climbs it.|The bell. A parcel. A look. You know the drill.} {The parcel is ticking.|The parcel is addressed to \"Whoever Is On Duty\".|The parcel is very cold.}\n> left: Run the parcel across campus\n  gold +tiny\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You get it there out of breath and in time. The tip is a few crowns and a very strange look.|You run it up four flights of stairs. The recipient signs for it without looking up, as if you were the post.|You run the cold parcel across campus with your hands going numb. The tip is generous, and given quickly.}\n> right: Hand it back, politely\n  mood +tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {\"Fine, fine,\" says the courier, already on a broom. You hear about it, loudly, from the rafters.|The courier takes it back with a sigh that follows you down the ladder.|You hand the cold parcel back. The courier holds it at arm's length and doesn't argue.}\n\n@event courier_loft_broom_rack\ntitle: The Fast Broom\npool: courier_loft_routine\nwhen: not member(broom_courier)\ntone: tense\nart: broom\ntags: life\nscene: A rack of courier brooms in a loft, one sleek black broom set apart from the others with a red tag on its handle.\ntext: {At the end of the rack is the courier loft's fast broom, tagged in red. Couriers only. Nobody's watching.|The fast broom is back on the rack, red tag and all.|The red-tagged broom. You've thought about it every time you've come up here.} {season == \"winter\": The loft doors are open on a sky full of snow.|{The loft doors stand open on a blue sky.|Someone has polished it recently.|The red tag has a fresh scorch mark.}}\n> left: Take it for one lap\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge feat journeyman+ wild:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {One lap of the towers, fast enough to make your ears sing. You put it back with the bristles barely warm.|You fly it under the bridges of light and come back laughing. Nobody saw, except, possibly, everybody.|You take the fast broom for one lap at last. It flies like it's been waiting for you. You put it back and can't stop smiling.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {It knows you aren't a courier. It shows you the courtyard, very close up.|It bucks you off into a haystack outside the stables. The stable hand laughs about it for a week.|The broom with the scorched tag has a scorched temper. It puts you through the stable roof.}\n> right: Tighten its bristles instead\n  lore +small\n  say: {You spend an hour learning how a good broom is bound. It's more satisfying than you'd admit.|You retie the bristles properly. Some courier will fly a little faster tomorrow and never know why.|You rebind the bristles and the scorched tag. The head courier notices and says nothing, which is promising.}\n\n# ---------------- The Whisperwood ----------------\n\n@pool whisperwood_routine\nplace: whisperwood\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\ntags: nature\n\n@event whisperwood_sleeptalk\ntitle: The Forest Talks\npool: whisperwood_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: tree\ntags: mystery\nscene: A student standing very still in a misty old forest, head tilted, as if listening to the trees.\ntext: {The Whisperwood is talking in its sleep. You stand very still to make out the words.|You've started to catch whole words in the forest's mumbling.|You've learned to stand still and let the forest talk.} Today it's {listing trees that were felled|arguing about the rain|saying a name, over and over, that isn't yours}. {season == \"winter\": Snow muffles half of it.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves fall with every word.|Fog moves between the trunks.}}\n> left: Listen closely\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You catch a whole sentence. It's about a winter long ago, and it's sad, and you walk home with it.|It notices you listening and goes quiet, the way a sleeper does. You leave feeling rude.|You listen to the name until you could say it in your sleep. You don't know whose it is. You'll find out, one day.}\n> right: Walk on, humming\n  vigor +small\n  say: {You walk the long loop humming loudly. The forest grumbles and turns over. You come back with muddy boots and a clear head.|You hum all the way round. Twice, the forest hums back.|You hum over the name it keeps saying. The forest falls quiet, as if it had been waiting for someone to answer.}\n\n@event whisperwood_foragers\ntitle: Foraging Season\npool: whisperwood_routine\ntone: light\nart: mushroom\ntags: social\nscene: A handful of students with baskets crouching in a forest clearing, one holding up a mushroom for the others to inspect.\ntext: {season == \"autumn\": The Whisperwood is full of mushrooms and students with baskets.|{season == \"winter\": A few hardy foragers are scraping lichen off the oaks.|{season == \"spring\": The clearings are thick with wild garlic and foragers.|The brambles are heavy with berries, and so are the foragers.}}} {A second-year from Rootward waves you over.|The Rootward forager is back, and saves you a spot.|The foragers treat you as one of their own now.}\n> left: Forage for the apothecary\n  gold +small\n  health -small\n  say: {Pell & Daughters buys your basket and throws out a third of it. You've still got nettle rash.|A good basket, a fair price, and brambles in places you'd rather not discuss.|The foragers show you their own patch. Pell & Daughters keeps the whole basket, which has never happened.}\n> right: Learn the plants from the expert\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The second-year names everything, twice, with its uses and its poisons. You come away full of names and short of patience.|You learn which mushroom is supper and which is a funeral. It takes until supper, and you'll need it.|You teach a first-year what the Rootward student taught you, and get two of the names wrong. They're corrected for you, kindly.}\n\n@event whisperwood_off_path\ntitle: The Path That Wasn't\npool: whisperwood_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: compass\ntags: mystery\nscene: A narrow path branching off into fog between old trees, a student pausing at the fork with a hand on a marked trunk.\ntext: {A path opens to your left that wasn't there last time.|Another path that wasn't there before. The Whisperwood does this.|The wood has offered you a new path again. You're starting to think it likes you.} {It's lined with pale stones.|It smells of woodsmoke where there's no fire.|It goes downhill, which is wrong, because you're at the bottom of the hill.}\n> left: Follow it a little way\n  challenge research journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      mana +small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {It leads to a clearing you'll never find again, full of moonpetal buds. You come back with a pocketful of quiet.|It loops you round to an old stone with words on it. You copy them down. They mean nothing yet.|You follow it downhill, though there's no down left. It brings you out above the lake, which is impossible, and lovely.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You walk in circles until dusk and come out at the lake, with no idea how.|The path ends at a wall of fog. Turning back takes much longer than going in did.|You follow it downhill for an hour and come out exactly where you started. The wood seems pleased with itself.}\n> right: Mark a tree and turn back\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {You carve an arrow in the bark. The next time you come, the arrow points somewhere else.|You turn back. The path watches you go, and quietly closes behind you.|You mark a tree. The next morning there's a mark on the tree beside it, in the same hand.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/internships.grim","text":"# Internships (playtest 2, X12; with M16): a shared backbone every placement draws (`plan: internship`; its\n# performance through `perform internship`, whichever host you are with), and for each host one card that changes\n# something beyond the summer. The everyday cards per host are in pools/plans_thesis.grim.\n\n# ---------------- The backbone ----------------\n\n@event intern_midsummer_review\ntitle: The Midsummer Review\nplan: internship\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nwhen: month == 12\nweight: 14\nart: scroll\ntags: study job\nscene: A supervisor's desk with a single sheet of notes face down, a student sitting opposite with hands folded too tightly.\ntext: {Halfway through the placement, your supervisor asks to see you. There's a single sheet of notes on the desk, face down.|The midsummer review again: the desk, the sheet, the tea nobody drinks.|You know the review's shape by now. You still don't know what's on the sheet.} {performance(internship) >= 60: Your supervisor is almost smiling.|{performance(internship) < 40: Your supervisor is not smiling.|Your supervisor's face gives nothing away.}}\n> left: Ask for honest criticism\n  perform internship +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You get it, three things you do badly, in order. You fix two of them by the end of the summer. Your supervisor notices both.|\"Most interns ask for praise,\" your supervisor says, and turns the sheet over. It's a long list. You're grateful for every line, eventually.|This time you ask to see the sheet itself. It's shorter than you feared, and every line is fair.}\n> right: Ask what you've done well\n  mood +small\n  perform internship -tiny\n  say: {Your supervisor reads the good half aloud. You leave glowing, and never hear the other half.|\"Your handwriting,\" your supervisor says, after a pause, \"is excellent.\" You decide to take it.|Your supervisor reads you the top of the sheet and folds the rest away. You'll wonder what's under the fold.}\n\n@event intern_credit_taken\ntitle: Someone Else's Name\nplan: internship\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: study job\nscene: A report pinned on a wall, a student staring at the name at the bottom of it, which is not their own.\ntext: {The report you spent a week on is pinned up in the corridor, praised by everyone, with another intern's name at the bottom.|Another piece of your work, another name on it.|It's happened again, and this time the other intern doesn't even look away.} {Your supervisor is reading it with approval.|The other intern is being congratulated.|Your notes are still on your desk, in your hand.}\n> left: Take it to your supervisor\n  challenge negotiate journeyman+:\n    win:\n      perform internship +medium\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You bring your notes, your drafts, your dates. Your supervisor reads them in silence and changes the name in red ink. The corridor goes very quiet around you.|You make your case calmly. The name is changed. The other intern doesn't speak to you again, which is fine.|You put your notes on your supervisor's desk, in your hand and dated. The name is changed, and this time the other intern looks away.}\n    lose:\n      perform internship -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {Your supervisor hears you out and says it's \"a matter for the two of you\". It isn't. You go back to your desk with your face burning.|Without proof enough, you sound petty, and you know it. The name stays.|Your notes are in your hand, but so, it turns out, is a draft in theirs. Your supervisor can't tell which came first, and the name stays.}\n> right: Let it go and work faster\n  perform internship +tiny\n  mood -medium\n  say: {You say nothing and do the next one twice as well, in ink that's hard to copy. It doesn't feel like justice. It feels like work.|You let it go. You write your name on every page from then on, small, in the corner.|You take your notes home. The next piece of work never leaves your sight until it's on the supervisor's desk.}\n\n@pool internship_routine\nplan: internship\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\n\n@event intern_city_evening\ntitle: An Evening Off\npool: internship_routine\ntone: warm\nart: lantern\ntags: social\nscene: A lamplit street far from the academy, interns spilling out of a busy inn, one looking back at the lit window of the workplace.\ntext: {{member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): The other interns are going out tonight, three miles to a village dance, with a fiddler and a supper laid on at a few coins a head.|The other interns are going out tonight, to an inn with music and a stall that sells fried bread at midnight.}|Another evening off, and the others are already at the door.|\"Coming?\" says the intern with the biscuits. \"Last time you said next time.\"} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {The track is dark, but somebody has a lantern.|It's the only dance for thirty miles.|Someone has brought a fiddle of their own.}|{The streets are bright.|There's a fair on the river.|Somebody knows a band.}} Your work for tomorrow isn't done.\n> left: Go out with them\n  mood +medium\n  perform internship -tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {You pay your share of the supper, dance badly, laugh well and walk home at two in the morning, arm in arm with people you met last month. Tomorrow's work is a little worse for it.|You go, and stand your round. You learn more about the place from the other interns in one evening than from a month of briefings, and remember half of it.|You keep last time's promise and go. The intern with the biscuits shares them on the walk home, and tomorrow's work is a little worse for it.}\n> right: Finish tomorrow's work\n  perform internship +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You finish the work by lamplight. You hear them come home, singing. In the morning your work is the best on the table.|You stay in. The work is good. The evening is long.|\"Next time,\" you say again, and mean it less. Your work is excellent, and the biscuits are gone by morning.}\n\n@event intern_letter_from_campus\ntitle: A Letter From the Castle\npool: internship_routine\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 8 and tagged(friend, student)\nscene: A letter open on a desk far from the academy, its margins full of little drawings of the castle, a student smiling at it.\ntext: {A letter from {friend}, forwarded twice, with the castle drawn in the margins.|Another letter from campus, this one from {friend}.|{friend}'s handwriting on the envelope. You open it before your coat is off.} {The letter misses you, in a roundabout way.|{They} {are} bored without you, {they} say{s}.|There's gossip, and a pressed flower.} Answering it properly would take the evening.\n> left: Write back at length\n  rel friend +small\n  perform internship -tiny\n  say: {Six pages, with drawings of your own. You post it at dawn and are late for the briefing, and don't mind.|You write back everything: the work, the place, the interns. It's like talking to {them}.|You answer the gossip with gossip of your own, and press a flower from here to send back. It's like being in two places.}\n> right: Send a quick note\n  perform internship +tiny\n  rel friend -tiny\n  say: {\"Busy. Miss you. More soon.\" It's true, and it's thin, and you know it.|Three lines and a promise. You mean to write more. The summer is very full.|A quick note, and the pressed flower kept in your notebook. You'll write properly when the summer's less full.}\n\n# ---------------- One card per host that reaches past the summer ----------------\n\n@event intern_conclave_your_ward\ntitle: A Ward of Your Own\ntrack: intern_conclave\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nweight: 18\nart: shield\ntags: study magic\nscene: A circular test chamber, a student at the centre holding up a shimmering ward against a bolt of light, researchers behind thick glass on their feet.\ntext: The Conclave is testing a new ward, and the researcher who designed it is ill. \"You've read every note,\" says your supervisor. \"Cast it. If it holds, it's yours to keep, and your name goes in the paper.\" Behind the glass, half the Conclave has come to watch.\n> left: Cast it\n  wage\n  challenge spell expert:\n    win:\n      perform intern_conclave +large\n      arcana +small\n      renown +small\n      say: The bolt hits the ward and stops, humming, an inch from your nose. Behind the glass, someone starts clapping. Your name goes in the paper, third of four, and the ward is yours for good.\n    lose:\n      perform intern_conclave -small\n      health -medium\n      say: The ward holds for half a heartbeat and then doesn't. You wake on the floor with your hair standing up and a researcher fanning you with your own notes.\n> right: Ask them to wait for the designer\n  wage\n  perform intern_conclave -tiny\n  lore +small\n  say: \"Sensible,\" says your supervisor, in a voice that means \"disappointing\". The designer recovers, casts it, and thanks you in the paper's footnotes for the notes you kept.\n> up[ward]: Layer your own Ward under theirs\n  wage\n  perform intern_conclave +large\n  renown +small\n  say: You cast the new ward, then your own beneath it, the one you've cast a hundred times. The bolt goes through the first like paper and stops dead on the second. Behind the glass there's an argument about whose ward held. Your supervisor settles it: \"Both. Write it up.\" The paper has your name second.\n\n@event intern_alchemists_safety\ntitle: The Cracked Vat\ntrack: intern_alchemists\nclass: milestone\ntone: grim\nweight: 18\nart: cauldron\ntags: job danger\nscene: A huge iron vat in an alchemical works, a hairline crack weeping green steam, workers walking past it without looking.\ntext: The seventh vat at the Guild works has a crack in it. Everyone knows. The foreman says it'll last the season, and the season's quota depends on it. Workers walk past it without looking. You've seen what the steam does to brass.\n> left: Report it to the Guild masters\n  wage\n  alignment +small\n  perform intern_alchemists -small\n  mood -small\n  say: The masters close the seventh vat for a week and the foreman doesn't speak to you again. In the third week the replacement vat arrives, and an old worker shakes your hand without a word.\n> right: Mend it yourself, quietly\n  wage\n  challenge brew expert:\n    win:\n      perform intern_alchemists +medium\n      alchemy +medium\n      say: You seal it from the inside in the night, with a sealant of your own. It holds through the season. The foreman never knows. The old worker does, somehow, and leaves an apple on your bench every morning.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      perform intern_alchemists -small\n      say: The steam gets you as the seal gives. You wake in the works infirmary with blistered hands, and the vat is closed anyway.\n\n@event intern_expedition_new_star\ntitle: A Star Nobody Charted\ntrack: intern_expedition\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 18\nart: star\ntags: study magic\nscene: A telescope on a high plateau at night, a student with an eye to the eyepiece and a chart on their knee, a single new star circled in red.\ntext: The lead gives you tonight's watch at the eyepiece, alone. At three in the morning there's a star in it that isn't on any chart: small, steady, blue. The expedition lead is asleep. By custom, whoever records a new star first may name it.\n> left: Wake the lead and share it\n  wage\n  perform intern_expedition +medium\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: The lead comes in a nightshirt and stares until dawn. The star goes in the record under the expedition's name and yours, and the whole camp toasts it at breakfast.\n> right: Record it in your own name\n  wage\n  renown +medium\n  perform intern_expedition +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write it into the log: the time, the place, a name you choose. It's yours for as long as there are charts. At breakfast the lead reads the log twice, and is polite to you all week, which is worse.\n\n@event intern_field_station_foal\ntitle: The Orphaned Foal\ntrack: intern_field_station\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 18\nart: paw\ntags: nature\nscene: A newborn hippogriff foal shivering in the heather on the moor, a student kneeling with a blanket, the field station lamps far off.\ntext: On the moor, a hippogriff foal, newborn and alone. The station's rule is plain: you count wild creatures, you don't raise them. The station chief says it won't last the night. It looks at you as if you were the only warm thing on the moor, which you are.\n> left: Take it back and raise it\n  wage\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  alignment +small\n  perform intern_field_station -small\n  rel brask +small\n  say: You feed it every two hours for a month and break the rule every one of them. By summer's end it flies. Back at Lumenhold, Professor Brask hears the story and invites you to supper. \"Rules,\" she says, \"are for people who've never held a foal.\"\n> right: Count it and leave it\n  wage\n  perform intern_field_station +small\n  mood -medium\n  say: You write it down: one foal, alone, the date. You don't sleep. In the morning the chief tells you its mother came back in the night. You never find out if that's true.\n\n@event intern_thornwick_offer\ntitle: An Offer From Thornwick\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nweight: 18\nart: letter\nwhen: month == 12\ntags: study\nscene: A lacquered office at a rival academy, a smiling dean sliding a sealed offer across a desk, a window full of unfamiliar towers.\ntext: Thornwick's dean asks to see you on your last week. \"Transfer to us,\" she says, sliding a sealed offer across the desk. \"Full scholarship, your own lab bench, a seat in our illusion faculty in time. Lumenhold never valued glamour. We do.\" She's sincere, and that's the danger.\n> left: Decline, warmly\n  wage\n  perform intern_thornwick +small\n  rel vey +small\n  say: \"Lumenhold is home,\" you say. She smiles as if she expected it. \"The offer stands.\" Back at the castle, Professor Vey hears about it somehow and gives you an approving look that lasts all term.\n> right: Keep the offer, unopened\n  wage\n  gold +medium\n  renown +small\n  flag thornwick_offer\n  mood -small\n  say: You keep it, sealed, in the bottom of your trunk. Thornwick sends a stipend \"as a courtesy\". Knowing there's a door out makes some evenings easier, and some harder.\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_file\nwhen: not closed(hollowstar)\ntitle: The File With Your Name\ntrack: intern_unseen_office\nclass: milestone\ntone: eerie\nweight: 18\nart: eye\ntags: mystery\nscene: A windowless office lined with drawers, a student holding open a thin file with their own name on the label, a clerk's empty chair behind them.\ntext: Sorting files for the Office, you find one with your name on it. It's thin. The clerk has gone to lunch. The last page is about Lumenhold, and the lake, and a tower nobody has opened in a century.\n> left: Read it all\n  wage\n  guile +small\n  find lead_seventh_tower\n  perform intern_unseen_office -small\n  say: They know where you sleep and what you eat, which is unsettling. The last page is mostly blacked out. What's left says the seventh tower \"is to remain closed\", and nothing about why. When the clerk comes back you are filing something else. The clerk looks at you a little too long.\n> right: Put it back unread\n  wage\n  perform intern_unseen_office +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: You put it back. The clerk returns, checks the drawer and smiles for the first time all summer. \"The Office notices who doesn't look,\" the clerk says. You didn't look. You'll wonder for years.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/letters.grim","text":"# Letters (playtest 2, M38, M19; stub — N14 fills them): off campus a letter comes each night, and anyone who\n# reaches you while one of you is away writes instead of calling. `trigger: letter` keeps them out of every other\n# pool. Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.\n\n@event letter_from_a_friend\ntitle: A Letter From {friend}\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  friend: any where met(friend) and rel(friend) >= 6 and not npc_is(tamsin)\nper: friend\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 20\nart: letter\ntags: social letter\nscene: A folded letter on a windowsill, the handwriting crowded to the very edge of the page, a pressed leaf inside.\ntext: {friend} has written. {~Three pages, mostly about nothing, which is the best kind.|Half of it is crossed out and the other half underlined.|There's a drawing in the margin that is probably meant to be you.} {off_campus: The castle, they say, is too quiet without you.|They're away, and they say the quiet there is the wrong kind.}\n> left: Write back at length\n  rel friend +small\n  gold -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You write until the candle gutters and spend the postage you meant for something else. It's worth it.|Four pages back, and a pressed flower of your own. You pay the extra stamp.|You write back pages and draw them in the margin, just as badly. The postage costs more than you meant it to.}\n> right: Send a short note\n  rel friend +tiny\n  chance likely:\n    say: {~A few lines, warm enough. They'll know you read it.|\"Missing you. More soon.\" It says enough.|A few lines, and a drawing of them in return. They'll laugh.}\n  else:\n    rel friend -tiny\n    say: {~Your note is so short it reads as cross. You meant it kindly.|Two lines. You can picture their face reading it, and it isn't pleased.|A short note, and no drawing. They'll notice the missing drawing more than the words.}\n\n@event letter_castle_news\ntitle: News From the Castle\ntrigger: letter\nwhen: has(plan_home)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: letter\nscene: A copy of the academy newssheet, forwarded in an envelope, its corners soft from travelling.\ntext: Someone has forwarded the Lumen Ledger to you at home. {~A professor's hat has gone missing.|The lake froze, or thawed, depending on which column you believe.|There's a letter to the editor about the refectory soup, and it is furious.} It's strange to read about the castle from your old bedroom.\n> left: Read it cover to cover\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You read every word, even the notices. By the end you're homesick for a place you're not at.|The whole paper, twice. Home feels very far from the castle tonight, and the castle very far from home.|You read every word, down to the furious letter about the soup, and find you miss even the soup.}\n> right: Just the gossip page\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    say: {~The gossip is excellent. You laugh out loud and have to explain it to everyone at the table.|Someone you know is in it, for something ridiculous. Perfect.|The gossip is good, but the furious letter about the soup is better. You read it aloud at the table.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~The gossip is about someone you like, and it's unkind.|There's nothing in it you didn't already know. Somehow that's worse.|The gossip is all about a quarrel you'd rather not hear of. Even the letter about the soup can't save it.}\n\n@event letter_writing_home\ntitle: Writing Home\ntrigger: letter\nwhen: has(plan_internship)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: letter home\nscene: A borrowed desk in a strange room, a half-written letter under a lamp, a map of somewhere else pinned on the wall.\ntext: The placement keeps you busy, and home feels further away than the miles. {~There's ink, paper and a quiet hour.|The lamp in your lodgings is good for writing.|You owe home a letter, and you know it.}\n> left: Write a proper letter home\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~Four pages about the work, the food and the people. The postage is dear from here. You send it anyway.|You tell them everything, and in telling it you find out how much you like it here.|You owe them a letter, so you write a long one, and the debt feels paid with interest. The postage hurts.}\n> right: A few lines and back to work\n  chance likely:\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {~Short and cheerful. It'll do, and they'll be glad of it.|\"All well, more soon.\" True, mostly.|A few lines to settle what you owe. They'll be glad of it, and you'll owe them a longer one.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~You write three lines and feel guilty for a week.|The letter is so brief it reads like a receipt.|You pay what you owe with three lines. It doesn't feel paid.}\n\n# ---------------- More letters (playtest 2, N14): general ones, then two for each romanceable character ----------------\n\n@event letter_reading_list\ntitle: A Reading List\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: letter study\nscene: A long handwritten list of book titles on academy paper, a small inkblot at the bottom where the writer paused.\ntext: A professor has written over the break, as some of them do, with a reading list for next term: eleven titles, three of them underlined, one with \"(if you dare)\" beside it. {~The handwriting slopes like a staircase.|There's a coffee ring on the corner.}\n> left: Start on the list now\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You read the first two by candlelight while everyone else is resting. The third is the one marked \"if you dare\". You don't, yet.|You make real progress and lose two afternoons of holiday to it. Next term will be easier for it.}\n> right: Put it under the bed until term\n  mood +small\n  say: {~The list goes under the bed with your winter boots. The holiday stays a holiday.|You don't read a word, and you don't feel a bit sorry.}\n\n@event letter_parcel_from_home\ntitle: A Parcel From Home\ntrigger: letter\nwhen: not has(plan_home)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 10\nart: pouch\ntags: letter home\nscene: A brown paper parcel tied with string, opened to show a squashed cake, a pair of knitted socks and a short letter.\ntext: A parcel from home, squashed by the post: {~a cake that has become a different shape of cake|a tin of biscuits and a jar of jam|knitted socks and a fruit loaf}, and a letter that says, in four different hands, that everyone is well and the cat is not.\n> left: Share it with everyone around you\n  charm +small\n  say: {~You pass it round and it vanishes in minutes. People you barely know thank you by name the next day.|You share it out to the last crumb. Someone gives you a biscuit back, out of pity.|You cut the fruit loaf into as many slices as there are hands. The socks you keep.}\n> right: Keep it for yourself\n  health +small\n  say: {~You eat it slowly, over a week, a slice a night. It tastes like your kitchen table.|You hide it under your bed and ration it. It lasts, and so does the feeling of home.|You keep the loaf for the cold nights and wear the socks to bed. Both last till the end of the month.}\n\n@pool letters_routine\ntrigger: letter\nclass: routine\nweight: 10\ntags: letter\n\n@event letter_wrong_address\ntitle: Not For You\npool: letters_routine\ntone: light\nart: letter\nscene: A sealed envelope addressed in an unfamiliar hand to a name that isn't the reader's, lying on a windowsill beside a stamp.\ntext: A letter arrives that isn't yours: the right house, the wrong name, a perfumed envelope sealed with green wax. It's addressed to someone you've never heard of. The seal is very thin.\n> left: Forward it unopened\n  gold -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You write \"not known here, please try again\" on the back, add a stamp of your own and send it on its way. You'll never know what it said, which is how it should be.\n> right: Open it, just to see\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: It's a love letter, and a bad one, full of rhymes on \"moon\". You reseal it carefully and send it on, feeling worse than you expected.\n\n@event letter_bookshop_bill\ntitle: A Bill You Don't Remember\npool: letters_routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: money\nscene: A crisp bill from a bookshop, an itemised list with one title circled, held up beside a student's puzzled face.\ntext: A bill from the village bookshop for a book called \"On Small Explosions\", which you have never seen, never borrowed and never wanted. The bookseller writes that you signed for it. The signature looks a little like yours.\n> left: Pay it and be done\n  gold -small\n  say: You pay it. A week later the book arrives, and it's actually quite good. You still don't know who signed for it.\n> right: Write back and dispute it\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      say: You write a polite, firm letter. The bookseller writes back: wrong student, same name, profuse apologies, and a bookmark by way of sorry.\n    lose:\n      if gold > 0:\n        gold -small\n        mood -tiny\n        say: The bookseller replies with a copy of the signature and a note that she has \"dealt with your sort before\". You pay, and add a stiff note of your own.\n      else:\n        mood -small\n        health -tiny\n        say: The bookseller replies with a copy of the signature and a note that she has \"dealt with your sort before\". Your purse is empty, so you work the bill off, shelving her stock for a long, dusty afternoon.\n\n@event letter_old_friend\ntitle: From Before\npool: letters_routine\nwhen: not has(plan_home)\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: home\nscene: A letter in a familiar childhood hand, with a small drawing of a village street in the margin.\ntext: A letter from someone you grew up with, from before Lumenhold. {~They're apprenticed to a cooper now.|They're getting married in the spring.|They've moved to the city and hate it.} The letter asks what magic is like. It's a good question, and you don't know how to answer it.\n> left: Write back pages and pages\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You try to explain magic, and the castle, and what it's like to fail an exam in front of a ghost. It takes four pages and an extra stamp. It's the best letter you've written.\n> right: Send a postcard of the towers\n  say: A postcard of the seven towers, and \"It's hard to explain. Come and see?\" You both know they won't. It's a kind postcard all the same.\n\n@event letter_ledger_competition\ntitle: The Holiday Essay Prize\npool: letters_routine\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: study\nscene: A folded copy of a student newspaper with a competition notice circled in red ink.\ntext: The Lumen Ledger's holiday issue has found you: an essay prize, \"What I Did Not Do This Holiday\", five hundred words, the winner printed on the front page and paid in crowns.\n> left: Write an entry\n  challenge research journeyman+ slim:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      gold +small\n      say: Your essay about the lake you didn't swim in wins. It's printed on the front page, a little cut, under your name spelled right for once.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You write a good essay that doesn't win. The winner wrote about a goat. Everyone agrees the goat was the right choice.\n> right: Let someone else win\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You don't write a word of it, and spend the afternoon doing one of the things you didn't do.\n\n@event letter_from_a_firstyear\ntitle: Advice, Please\npool: letters_routine\nwhen: year >= 2\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A short anxious letter on cheap paper, several words crossed out, a small blot where the writer pressed too hard.\ntext: A first-year you once showed the way to the library has written to you over the break. They're thinking of leaving. The letter is short, crossed out in places, and ends: \"Did you ever feel like this?\"\n> left: Write back honestly, at length\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You tell them about your own worst month, which isn't easy to write. Next term they find you at breakfast and say they stayed because of it.\n> right: Send a few kind lines\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Yes. It passes. Come back.\" It's short, and it's true. You hope it's enough. At the start of term you see them in the queue for the barge.\n\n@event letter_registrar_forms\ntitle: Forms in Triplicate\npool: letters_routine\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A thick envelope of forms with the academy's seal, three identical copies of everything fanned out on a table.\ntext: The registrar's office has sent forms, in triplicate, about next term: your courses, your board, your \"intentions regarding extracurricular activities\". There is a box marked \"Other (explain)\". It is very small.\n> left: Fill them in properly now\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: An afternoon of copying your own name three times. At the start of term, your enrolment takes four minutes while everyone else queues. Smug is a feeling too.\n> right: Leave them until the barge\n  mood +small\n  say: You fill them in on the barge, on your knee, in the wind. One copy goes over the side. The registrar sighs at you all the way through enrolment.\n\n# Two letters each from the romanceable characters: one friendly, one when there's a spark.\n\n@event letter_lyra_drills\ntitle: A List of Drills\ntrigger: letter\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: letter social duel\nscene: A letter of numbered duelling drills in crisp handwriting, a scratched-out postscript at the bottom.\ntext: Lyra has written. It's a list of drills, numbered, with a note at the bottom: \"Do these. You're too slow. Everyone is.\" There's a postscript under that, scratched out and rewritten three times and then scratched out for good.\n> left: Do every drill, and send your times\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  say: You do them in the cold every morning and post her your times. Her reply is one word: \"Adequate.\" It's underlined, which from Lyra is a bouquet.\n> right: Ask what the postscript said\n  chance even +1 if rel(lyra) >= 30:\n    rel lyra +small\n    say: Two weeks later, one line: \"It said: write back.\" Then, smaller: \"You did. Good.\"\n  else:\n    rel lyra -tiny\n    say: She ignores the question entirely and sends a second list, longer, with no postscript at all.\n\n@event letter_lyra_book\ntitle: Foundations of Flame\ntrigger: letter\nactor: lyra\nwhen: spark(lyra) >= 20 and rel(lyra) >= 0\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: book\ntags: letter romance\nscene: A worn copy of a spellbook unwrapped from brown paper, every margin full of small, fierce annotations in red ink.\ntext: A parcel from Lyra: her own copy of \"Foundations of Flame\", every margin crowded with notes in red ink. On the flyleaf: \"You'll only underline the wrong bits. So I've done it for you.\" The handwriting goes a little uneven at the end.\n> left: Read every note in the margins\n  lore +small\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  say: Her notes are sharp, funny and occasionally rude about the author. On page ninety there's a small flame drawn beside a paragraph, and your initials.\n> right: Annotate it back and return it\n  spark lyra +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You add your own notes in green and post it back at your own expense. Her reply says you're wrong about chapter four. She keeps the book, and your notes, and doesn't cross them out.\n\n@event letter_tobias_goats\ntitle: Four Pages of Goats\ntrigger: letter\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: letter social\nscene: A long letter in a big, careful hand with a drawing of a goat in the corner and a recipe card tucked into the fold.\ntext: Tobias has written four pages, mostly about goats: one has escaped, one has eaten a hat, and one is, he writes, \"going through something\". Tucked in the fold is a recipe for his gran's barley soup, \"for when the castle food gets you down\".\n> left: Make the soup\n  health +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You buy the barley and borrow a pot. The soup is thick and plain and exactly what you needed. You write and tell him so; he writes back that his gran says you're welcome.\n> right: Write back with a story of your own\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: You send him the story of the ghost in the bathtub, at length. His reply says he read it to the goats, and that the one going through something seemed to feel better.\n\n@event letter_tobias_seeds\ntitle: Seeds for a Windowsill\ntrigger: letter\nactor: tobias\nwhen: spark(tobias) >= 20 and rel(tobias) >= 0\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: flower\ntags: letter romance nature\nscene: A small paper packet of seeds tied with garden twine, a note in big careful handwriting beside it.\ntext: A packet of seeds from Tobias, tied with garden twine. \"For your windowsill. They'll keep you company. Water them Tuesdays.\" Then, squashed in at the bottom: \"Sorry if that's soppy.\"\n> left: Plant them straight away\n  mood +small\n  spark tobias +tiny\n  say: Three shoots by the end of the month, leaning toward the light. You water them on Tuesdays and think of him on the other days too.\n> right: Write back that it isn't soppy\n  spark tobias +small\n  say: You write that it isn't soppy, and then a whole page about why. His reply is two lines long and you can tell he went red writing it.\n\n@event letter_seraphine_card\ntitle: Half a Sentence\ntrigger: letter\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 14\nart: cards\ntags: letter social\nscene: A single tarot card in an envelope, with half a sentence written across the back in silver ink.\ntext: An envelope from Seraphine with a single tarot card inside, the Wheel, and half a sentence on the back in silver ink: \"Not this week but the one after, don't take the\"\n> left: Try to work out the rest\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You spend an evening with the card and every book on tarot you can find, and spend the following week avoiding stairs, carts and soup. Nothing happens. Probably.\n> right: Write and ask her to finish it\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: Her reply comes ten days later, and finishes the sentence: \"…long way round. Or do. It's lovely either way.\" It is, when you take it.\n  if knows(seraphine.slips):\n    rel seraphine +tiny\n    say: You date your letter clearly at the top and underline it, the way she needs. Her reply begins: \"Thank you for the today.\"\n\n@event letter_seraphine_painting\ntitle: A Painting of You\ntrigger: letter\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: spark(seraphine) >= 20 and rel(seraphine) >= 0\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: rose\ntags: letter romance\nscene: A small watercolour of a student reading under a tree in blossom, unwrapped from tissue paper on a desk.\ntext: Seraphine has sent a small watercolour: you, reading under a tree in blossom, lit from one side. It is very good. You have never sat under that tree, and it isn't spring.\n> left: Hang it by your bed\n  mood +small\n  spark seraphine +tiny\n  say: It's the first thing you see each morning. It makes you look kinder than you feel, and you start, a little, to feel it.\n> right: Write and ask when she saw this\n  spark seraphine +small\n  say: She writes back, \"Next spring,\" and nothing else, and then a second letter the same day: \"I hope that's all right.\"\n\n@event letter_corvin_bakery\ntitle: The Best Bakery in the Kingdom\ntrigger: letter\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 14\nart: map\ntags: letter social\nscene: A hand-drawn map of an unfamiliar town with a cross in ink over one corner, a postscript crammed into the margin.\ntext: A letter from Corvin, with a map of a town you've never heard of and a cross on one corner. \"Found the best bakery in the kingdom. Can't afford it. Thought you should know where it is, saint, in case you're ever richer than me. Low bar.\"\n> left: Send him a few crowns for a pastry\n  gold -small\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: You fold a few crowns into the reply. His next letter is mostly about the pastry, in loving detail, and then, at the very end and without a joke: \"Thank you.\"\n> right: Write back a map of your own\n  guile +small\n  say: You draw him a map of every place you've found that he hasn't, with crosses and a key. He writes back that two of them are wrong and one of them is brilliant.\n\n@event letter_corvin_no_jokes\ntitle: A Letter With No Jokes\ntrigger: letter\nactor: corvin\nwhen: spark(corvin) >= 20 and rel(corvin) >= 0\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: letter romance\nscene: A single short page in quick handwriting, with no doodles and no crossings-out, folded very small.\ntext: Corvin's letter has no jokes in it. You read it twice to be sure. It says the place is too quiet, that he keeps turning to say something to you, and that he isn't used to that. It isn't signed \"your favourite thief\". It's just signed.\n> left: Write back just as plainly\n  spark corvin +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write back with no jokes either, and it's harder than you thought. His reply comes by return of post, and the first line is a joke, and the second line isn't.\n> right: Send a joke to make it easier\n  rel corvin +small\n  spark corvin -tiny\n  say: You send him the worst joke you know. He replies with a worse one, and you're both on safer ground. You wonder, later, if you missed something.\n\n@event letter_mir_blueprint\ntitle: A Letter That Folds Itself\ntrigger: letter\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 14\nart: gear\ntags: letter social magic\nscene: A letter folding itself back up on a desk like a paper bird, a blueprint with arrows and exclamation marks spread out underneath.\ntext: Mir's letter starts folding itself back up while you're still reading it. Inside is a blueprint for a self-folding letter (\"PROBABLY works!!\") and a page of excited tangents about hinges.\n> left: Build one yourself\n  arcana +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: Wire, wax paper and one small enchantment. Your letter folds itself into a perfect swan and then, unprompted, into a slightly worse frog. Mir's reply is all exclamation marks.\n> right: Write back with improvements\n  rel mir +small\n  say: You suggest a stiffer hinge and a slower fold. Mir writes back that you are a genius and that they've already tried it and it exploded, but in a promising way.\n\n@event letter_mir_ticking\ntitle: Something That Ticks\ntrigger: letter\nactor: mir\nwhen: spark(mir) >= 20 and rel(mir) >= 0\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: gear\ntags: letter romance\nscene: A tiny brass mechanism the size of a walnut ticking softly on a pillow, a note in hurried handwriting beside it.\ntext: A small brass thing the size of a walnut, ticking softly, with a note in Mir's hurried hand: \"It's a metronome. Probably. It's set to a resting heart rate. Mine, actually, it took ages. Anyway!!\"\n> left: Keep it on your pillow\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: It ticks you to sleep every night, slightly faster than a resting heart rate. You don't mention that. Some nights it keeps you awake.\n> right: Write back how much you like it\n  spark mir +small\n  say: You write that you like it very much. Mir's reply is three pages about escapements, and then at the very end, very small: \"good. i'm glad. good.\"\n\n@event letter_kael_three_lines\ntitle: Three Lines\ntrigger: letter\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: snow\ntags: letter social\nscene: A sheet of good paper with only three short lines on it in careful handwriting, a great deal of white space below.\ntext: A letter from Kael. Three lines, in careful handwriting, with a great deal of white paper below them: the weather, a star he saw, and \"I hope you are well. That is the whole letter.\"\n> left: Write back three lines\n  rel kael +small\n  say: You write back three lines: your weather, a star of your own, and that you're well. His reply comes promptly. It has four lines. You count them twice.\n> right: Write back three pages\n  charm +small\n  rel kael +tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: You write three pages and pay the extra postage. His reply takes a long time and says only: \"Thank you. I read it four times. I do not know how to answer so much.\" He keeps it, though.\n\n@event letter_kael_star_chart\ntitle: The Sky on Your Birthday\ntrigger: letter\nactor: kael\nwhen: spark(kael) >= 20 and rel(kael) >= 0\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: star\ntags: letter romance\nscene: A hand-inked star chart unfolded on a bed, one small cluster of stars circled in pencil, a short note pinned to the corner.\ntext: Kael has sent a star chart, inked by hand, with one cluster circled in pencil. The note says: \"The sky on the night you were born, from here. I checked the tables twice. It is a good sky.\"\n> left: Find those stars yourself tonight\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  spark kael +tiny\n  say: You stand outside in the cold until you find them, low over the roofs. Your feet go numb. It is a good sky; he was right.\n> right: Thank him plainly\n  spark kael +small\n  say: You write that it's the kindest thing anyone has sent you. His reply says \"You are welcome.\" Below that, smaller, as if added later: \"Very.\"\n  if knows(kael.thaw):\n    rel kael +tiny\n    say: You sign it \"until the thaw\". His next letter is the first he signs the same way.\n\n@event letter_nessa_deadline\ntitle: URGENT\ntrigger: letter\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 10\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 14\nart: paper\ntags: letter social\nscene: A letter with URGENT written across the top in capitals and underlined three times, a deadline circled at the bottom.\ntext: A letter from Nessa, URGENT underlined three times: the Ledger needs three hundred words on holidays for the first issue of term, \"interesting ones please, the last six were about weather\". Deadline circled. Twice.\n> left: Write the piece\n  renown +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You spend an evening on three hundred words and post them. Nessa cuts eighty, prints the rest on page four and writes back \"Good. Rewrite the ending next time.\"\n> right: Write back off the record\n  rel nessa +small\n  say: You write back with no piece at all, just how she is. Her reply has no capitals in it. It's the longest thing she's ever written that she isn't going to print.\n\n@event letter_nessa_off_record\ntitle: Off the Record\ntrigger: letter\nactor: nessa\nwhen: spark(nessa) >= 20 and rel(nessa) >= 0\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: letter romance\nscene: A letter with OFF THE RECORD written at the top and then, for once, a page of short, unhurried sentences.\ntext: Nessa's letter has OFF THE RECORD at the top, and then, for once, short sentences with nothing to sell. She's tired. The holidays are long. She keeps writing headlines about you in her head and crossing them out.\n> left: Reply off the record too\n  spark nessa +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write back slowly and honestly, which costs you more than you expected. Her answer is posted the day your letter must have reached her, which means she wrote it the moment she read yours.\n> right: Ask to see the headlines\n  charm +small\n  spark nessa +tiny\n  say: She sends three. One is unprintable, one is very sweet and one is \"LOCAL STUDENT STILL NOT REPLYING\", which is fair. You reply at once.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/money.grim","text":"# Money outside jobs (playtest 2, X14; D4/D7): financial aid, the bursar's loan, favours for the faculty and\n# upper-years, and the pawnbroker. Hinted at low gold, and sooner for those with no family money; never obligations.\n# The illicit routes (company, the ring, the high table, Grisk's parcels) are the night's (pools/night.grim).\n\n@event aid_notice\ntitle: The Hardship Notice\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: term and turn > 2 and not has(hardship_grant) and not has(bursar_loan) and since(aid_interview) > 10 and (gold < 6 or (gold < 15 and (has(origin_hedge) or has(origin_street) or has(origin_scholar))))\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 14\nart: scales\ntags: money\nscene: A notice pinned beside the bursar's office door, half covered by a newer notice about lost owls, a queue of three students pretending not to queue.\ntext: {A small notice by the bursar's door reads \"Hardship fund. Applications in writing. All circumstances considered.\"|The hardship notice again, under a new one about lost owls.|Someone has underlined \"all circumstances\" on the hardship notice.} {Three students are pretending not to queue.|The ink on the forms is still wet.|The bursar's door is ajar.}\n> left: Fill in the forms\n  mood -tiny\n  queue aid_interview in 1\n  say: {Four pages. One asks you to list everything you own. It doesn't take long, and that is the hard part.|You fill in the forms at a library table with your arm around them so nobody can see.|The bursar's door is open, so you hand the forms in yourself. \"All circumstances,\" the bursar says, reading. \"Yes.\"}\n> right: Not yet\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You walk past. Your pride is intact, and so is your empty purse.|Another month, you tell yourself. You have a button left.|You read \"all circumstances\" twice and walk past the open door. Not yet.}\n\n@event aid_interview\ntitle: The Bursar Will See You\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: scales\ntags: money\nscene: A small cluttered office, a grey-haired bursar behind a ledger as large as a tombstone, a student sitting very upright on a hard chair.\ntext: The bursar has your forms open under her hand and a ledger the size of a tombstone at her elbow. \"The fund is small,\" she says, \"and the hands are many. Tell me why it should be yours. Or, if you'd rather not beg, I can lend you sixty crowns against your future, at no interest.\"\n> left: Make your case\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      gain hardship_grant\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You tell her plainly, and she listens without pity, which helps. \"One term,\" she says, and stamps it. \"Come back if it's still hard.\" Walking out, you feel both lighter and smaller.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: She hears you out, and then reads you the list of people ahead of you. Some of their circumstances are worse. \"Next year,\" she says, kindly. You almost wish she hadn't been kind.\n> right: Take the loan\n  gold +huge\n  gain bursar_loan\n  loan_paid = 0\n  say: Sixty crowns, counted out in old coins, and your name in the tombstone ledger. \"Eight a month, when you can, and a little less at the end,\" she says. \"I'm a patient woman. The ledger is more patient still.\" It sits on your shoulder from then on.\n\n@event favour_vey_letter\ntitle: A Discreet Errand\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: vey\nwhen: met(vey) and gold < 25 and term\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: money social\nscene: A professor in a violet coat holding out a sealed letter and a silver coin between two gloved fingers, in an empty theatre corridor.\ntext: Professor Vey stops you in an empty corridor with a sealed letter and a silver coin. \"A letter for the village. The house with the blue shutters. Into the hand of whoever opens the door, and not a word.\" Her smile is magnificent and gives nothing away.\n> left: Deliver it, unread\n  meet vey\n  gold +small\n  rel vey +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: The house with the blue shutters is at the far end of the village, in the rain. An old man opens the door, reads the name on the letter and starts to cry. You walk back soaked and never ask.\n> right: Hold it up to the light first\n  meet vey\n  gold +small\n  guile +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel vey -small\n  say: You make out one line: \"...I have never stopped...\" Then you deliver it. The next day Vey says, without looking at you, \"The light in that corridor is very good, isn't it?\"\n\n@event favour_thorne_marking\ntitle: Forty Essays\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: thorne\nwhen: met(thorne) and gold < 25 and year >= 2 and term\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: money study\nscene: A stack of forty first-year essays on a desk under a lamp, a red pen, a student with an expression of growing despair.\ntext: Professor Thorne puts forty first-year essays on your desk. \"Mark them. A crown for every five. Be honest. Honesty is a kindness they will not thank you for.\" He leaves. The first essay begins, \"Fire is hot.\"\n> left: Mark them honestly\n  meet thorne\n  gold +small\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: Forty essays, eleven of them good, one brilliant and the rest about how fire is hot. Thorne reads your marks and says \"Hm.\" You've learned that \"Hm\" is pay too.\n> right: Mark them kindly\n  meet thorne\n  gold +small\n  mood +tiny\n  rel thorne -small\n  say: You give every first-year a word of encouragement. Thorne raises your marks on three and lowers them on twenty. \"Kindness,\" he says, \"is not the same as a pass.\" He pays you anyway.\n\n@event favour_amaranth_shift\ntitle: The Night Shift\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: amaranth\nwhen: met(amaranth) and gold < 25 and term\nweight: 10\nart: cross\ntags: money\nscene: A dim infirmary ward at night, a student in an apron carrying a basin between rows of beds, a stern healer writing by a single lamp.\ntext: Sister Amaranth needs a pair of hands for the infirmary's night shift. \"Basins, bandages, and sitting with whoever can't sleep. Eight crowns. Don't faint. If you faint, you're a patient, and patients don't get paid.\"\n> left: Take the shift\n  meet amaranth\n  gold +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: A first-year with a fever, a prefect with a broken wrist, a boy who just wants someone awake. By dawn you're grey with tiredness. Amaranth says, \"Keep doing that,\" and pays you.\n> right: Offer to roll bandages by day instead\n  meet amaranth\n  gold +tiny\n  rel amaranth +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She looks at you over her spectacles and hands you a mountain of linen. \"Bandages it is. Less pay, all of the tedium.\" By the end you could roll them in your sleep.\n\n@event upper_year_errands\ntitle: Errands for Upper-Years\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: term and year <= 2 and gold < 30\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 8\nart: pouch\ntags: money social\nscene: A crowded common room, a lounging upper-year holding out a coin and a list, a younger student taking the list with a sigh.\ntext: {Upper-years pay for errands, a book fetched or a queue stood in or a letter carried to the owlery.|An upper-year waves a list and a coin at you. Word has got round.|\"You again,\" says the upper-year, pleased. \"Good. The usual, plus my laundry.\"} {The list is long.|The coin is small.|One item on the list just says \"find my cat\".}\n> left: Run the errands\n  gold +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You run up and down the towers all evening and are paid in coins and condescension.|You fetch, carry and queue. Your legs are tired; your purse is less light.|The usual, plus the laundry, plus the cat, which is on the owlery roof. You're paid for two of the three.}\n> right: Name your price\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      say: {\"More, or find your own cat.\" They pay. You find the cat anyway; it was in the laundry.|You haggle like a merchant's child. The upper-year pays with grudging respect.|\"The laundry's extra,\" you say. They pay it, and add the cat for free. You find it in the library.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {\"Never mind,\" says the upper-year, and gives the list to a first-year behind you. Word gets round of that too.|They laugh, and find someone cheaper. You keep your dignity and nothing else.|\"The laundry's extra,\" you say. The upper-year gives the list to someone who doesn't think so.}\n\n@event pawnbroker_window\ntitle: The Pawnbroker's Window\nplace: village\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: gold < 10\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: scales\ntags: money\nscene: A dusty village shop window crowded with pawned treasures, wands, rings and a stuffed owl, a student looking in with their hands in empty pockets.\ntext: {The pawnbroker's window is full of other people's treasures, wands and rings and a stuffed owl. The sign says \"Fair prices. Redemption within the year.\"|{flag(pawned_something): The pawnbroker's window again. Something of yours is in it now.|The pawnbroker's window again, the same treasures in new places.}|The pawnbroker nods at you through the glass like an acquaintance.} {A first-year's silver locket is in the front row.|There's a familiar's collar, empty.|The stuffed owl seems to judge you.}\n> left: Pawn something you'd miss\n  flag pawned_something\n  gold +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: {Your good boots, or your grandmother's ring, or the wand-case with your name burned in it. The money is fair; the ticket says \"within the year\". You put the ticket somewhere safe and try not to look at it.|You hand over something you love, and take the money, and walk home faster than usual.|The pawnbroker greets you by name and writes the ticket without asking what it is. The owl watches the whole transaction.}\n> right: Walk on\n  say: {You walk on with your hands in your pockets. They're empty, but they're yours.|Not today. The owl watches you go.|The pawnbroker nods through the glass; you nod back, and walk on. The owl looks unconvinced.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/night.grim","text":"# The midnight pools (playtest 2, X13): what the six open places hold after curfew, and who is about. Most of the\n# castle is sealed; what is open feels shady, and nights are riskier than days: a risky outcome queues\n# `night_suspicion` at its own odds (M41). Illicit money (the ring, the high table, the stores, Grisk's parcels,\n# stolen pages) is a third way to pay and pulls alignment toward the Hollow; hosting upstairs is legal, only risky. Harmless nights exist too.\n\n# ---------------- The Gilded Newt, upstairs ----------------\n\n@event newt_company\ntitle: Company for the Evening\nplace: gilded_newt\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\n# The first offer (feedback 2026-10-16): first on every Newt night (`@place always:`) until you take it; turning it\n# down rests it for a few months. Once you are a regular, `newt_company_regular` takes its place.\nwhen: not flag(newt_regular) and not (chosen(newt_company, right) and since(newt_company) < 3)\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: money danger\nscene: An upstairs room at a tavern after hours, card tables under low lamps, well-dressed guests laughing, a student in borrowed finery refilling a glass.\ntext: The woman who runs the upstairs room looks you over. \"The guests like good company. Laugh at the jokes, keep the glasses full, lose gracefully at cards. Nothing more: guests who want more are shown the stair. Every night is a different party, and every party pays.\"\n> left: Take a seat upstairs\n  hint: Paid company, a different guest each night.\n  flag newt_regular\n  find lead_newt_upstairs\n  queue any in company_night now\n  queue company_late_hour now\n  if bonds(partner) > 0:\n    queue night_partner_asks chance unlikely\n  say: She hands you a borrowed jacket that almost fits. \"Smile with your eyes,\" she says. \"The guests can tell.\"\n> right: Not tonight\n  say: \"Another night, then,\" says the woman, writing something in a small book. You wonder what.\n\n@event newt_company_regular\ntitle: The Upstairs Room\nplace: gilded_newt\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\n# The regular's offer (feedback 2026-10-16): first on every Newt night once you've taken the work; a refusal rests it.\nwhen: flag(newt_regular) and not (chosen(newt_company_regular, right) and since(newt_company_regular) < 3)\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: money danger\nscene: An upstairs room at a tavern after hours, a landlady holding out a borrowed jacket to a student at the top of the stair.\ntext: {The woman who runs the upstairs room knows your face now. \"There's a party tonight,\" she says, already holding out the jacket.|The landlady has your jacket on a peg with your name chalked above it. \"Your table's waiting,\" she says.|\"Your regulars asked after you,\" says the landlady, which is either flattering or a warning. Upstairs the lamps are lit.}\n> left: Work the room\n  hint: Paid company; tonight's guests decide the rest.\n  queue any in company_night now\n  queue company_late_hour now\n  if bonds(partner) > 0:\n    queue night_partner_asks chance unlikely\n  say: {You button the jacket and go up. It fits a little better than last time.|You take your jacket from its peg and climb the stair into the lamplight.|Your regulars wave you over before you've reached the top of the stair.}\n> right: Not tonight\n  say: {\"Next week, then,\" she says, and the jacket goes back on its peg.|She shrugs and chalks a line through tonight beside your name.|\"They'll miss you,\" she says, and it sounds like the truth and the bill at once.}\n\n@event newt_high_table\ntitle: The High Table\nplace: gilded_newt\nslot: midnight\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\n# Every Newt night ends here, after the company (feedback 2026-10-16: `@place always:`, no cooldown).\nwhen: not flag(grisk_gone)\nweight: 9\nart: cards\ntags: money danger\nscene: A round table in a back room under a single green lamp, piles of coins, a goblin in a velvet waistcoat keeping the bank.\ntext: {Behind the upstairs room there's a back room, and in it the high table. Grisk keeps the bank. Nobody here plays for crowns alone.|The high table again. Grisk nods to you as if you were a regular. You are becoming one.|Grisk has kept your seat warm, he says. It is not a comforting thought.} {A first-year's familiar sits in a cage beside the pot.|Someone has staked a silver wand and is sweating about it.|Somebody's signed promise is in the pot, folded small.}\n> purse: Stake your purse\n  hint: Guile against the table.\n  meet grisk\n  alignment -tiny\n  gold -medium\n  challenge deceive adept unlikely wild:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      gold +medium\n      mood +small\n      queue newt_high_table_raise now\n      say: {You read the table like a book you've already finished. The pot slides over, and Grisk applauds politely with two fingers.|Your nerve holds and theirs doesn't. You walk home heavy with silver and hollow with nerves.|Somebody's folded promise is in the pot. You win it, and give it back to the owner unread. Grisk thinks that's hilarious.}\n    lose:\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {The cards turn on you, then the room does. You leave lighter, and a prefect's lamp is going round the courtyard.|You lose the pot and your temper with it. Grisk sympathises, with interest.|You lose, and Grisk offers to take a signed promise instead of the coin you're short. You pay in coin, all of it.}\n> favour: Stake a favour to Grisk\n  hint: Win big, or owe him a job.\n  when: not flag(owes_grisk)\n  meet grisk\n  alignment -small\n  challenge deceive adept- unlikely wild:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      say: You stake \"one favour, to be named\" and win. Grisk smiles, tears up the promise, and keeps the pieces. \"Next time,\" he says.\n    lose:\n      flag owes_grisk\n      mood -small\n      queue grisk_names_favour in 1\n      say: You lose, and Grisk folds your promise into his waistcoat pocket like a love letter. \"I'll be in touch,\" he says. \"Soon.\"\n> health: Play the bone-dice\n  hint: The old northern game. Win or bleed.\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge feat adept- wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +tiny\n      say: The bone-dice game is played with a knife between the fingers. You keep all ten and win the pot. Grisk calls you \"dear heart\" for the rest of the night.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      gold +small\n      say: You win the pot and lose a fair amount of skin from your left hand. Amaranth, in the morning, asks no questions, very loudly.\n> leave: Watch one hand, then go\n  say: {You watch a student lose a familiar on a bluff, and go home and hug yours, or your pillow.|You watch, learn and leave. Grisk raises his glass to your back.|You watch a signed promise change hands across the table, and decide never to sign anything in this room.}\n\n@event night_partner_asks\ntitle: Where Were You?\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: heart\ntags: romance\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nscene: Two students on a cold stone stair in the morning, one holding a tavern's silver token found in the other's coat.\ntext: {partner} is waiting on the stair with a silver token from the Gilded Newt in {their} palm. It fell out of your coat. \"The upstairs room,\" {they} say{s}. \"People talk. I'd rather hear it from you.\"\n> left: Tell {them} everything\n  spark partner -tiny\n  rel partner +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You tell the truth: the glasses, the cards, the guests, the stair. {partner} is quiet a long time. \"Thank you for telling me,\" {they} say{s} at last, and means it, and it still costs you both.\n> right: \"It's nothing. A card game.\"\n  spark partner -small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {partner} looks at the token, then at you, and puts it in your hand. \"All right,\" {they} say{s}. It isn't. For a week {they} sit{s} a little further away at supper.\n\n@event grisk_names_favour\ntitle: The Favour, Named\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus\nwhen: not flag(grisk_gone)\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nactor: grisk\nart: chain\ntags: money danger\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat waiting in a library alcove, a folded promise held up between two fingers.\ntext: Grisk is waiting in the library alcove with your promise between two fingers. \"The favour,\" he says. \"A small one. A crate goes from the boathouse to the village. Tonight. Or, if you prefer, the price of the crate.\" He smiles. \"I always offer a choice.\"\n> left: Move the crate\n  unflag owes_grisk\n  flag grisk_contact\n  find lead_grisk_jetty\n  alignment -small\n  challenge deceive vs bram.guile +1:\n    win:\n      say: The crate clinks and, faintly, sings. You don't look inside. Grisk tears up your promise. \"You're good at this,\" he says, which is the worst thing he could say.\n    lose:\n      gain watched\n      mood -small\n      say: Bram stops you on the jetty and lifts the tarp. Charm-wine. The crate is gone, the favour counts as paid, and Bram has your name.\n> right: Pay him off\n  unflag owes_grisk\n  gold -large\n  mood -small\n  say: You count out the crowns and Grisk gives you back the promise with a small bow. It feels like buying your own name back. It is, more or less.\n\n# ---------------- Company nights (feedback 2026-10-16) ----------------\n# The pool behind `queue any tagged company_night`: each night upstairs is one party, drawn by the usual score. The\n# pay is better than any shift, and so is the price: late nights, guests who push, and the castle finding out.\n\n@thread company_night\nfrom: scene\n\n@event company_birthday\ntitle: A Merchant's Birthday\nthread: company_night\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 3\nart: cup\ntags: money company_night\nscene: A loud birthday party in a tavern's upstairs room, a stout merchant in a paper crown, a cake shaped like a ship.\ntext: {A cloth merchant from the capital is sixty tonight and wants a room full of young people laughing at his stories.|The cloth merchant is back with another party. He says it's his birthday again, and nobody dares to count.|Another of the merchant's birthdays. He has started inviting his rivals, so that they can watch him be adored.} The cake is shaped like one of his ships.\n> left: Be the life of the party\n  hint: Charm the room; big tips, a long night.\n  challenge perform journeyman+:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      health -small\n      say: {You laugh, sing, and dance with his aunt. He tips like a man buying a ship. You get home with the dawn and a sore throat.|He tells the story about the storm off the southern coast again, and you gasp in the right place. The tip is heavy; so are your feet.|You toast him in front of his rivals and he nearly cries. The purse he presses on you is shaped, faintly, like a ship.}\n    lose:\n      gold +small\n      mood -small\n      queue night_suspicion chance unlikely\n      say: {You laugh a moment too late at the wrong story, and he notices. A thin tip, and a long walk home past the castle's lit windows.|You call his ship by his rival's ship's name. The room goes quiet. The landlady pays you off early.|His rivals are funnier than he is, and you show it. He doesn't forgive it, and the tip says so.}\n> right: Keep the glasses full\n  hint: A quiet night's pay, on your feet.\n  gold +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You pour, smile and stay out of the stories. The pay is fair, and the floor is very hard.|You keep the glasses full and your mouth shut. The merchant never learns your name, which suits you.|You pour for him and his rivals alike. Nobody notices you, and you go home with honest money and sore feet.}\n\n@event company_magistrate\ntitle: The Magistrate's Card Party\nthread: company_night\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nart: cards\ntags: money company_night\nscene: A card table under a green lamp, a stern magistrate in a fur collar studying her hand, a student opposite her looking at the ceiling.\ntext: {A magistrate from the county court is holding a card party, and she likes to win, but not too easily.|The magistrate again. She deals you in without asking, and her eyes say she hasn't forgotten the last game.|The magistrate's party, and a new rule: whoever lets her win too obviously pays for the wine.} The landlady's look says: lose, gracefully.\n> left: Lose exactly enough\n  hint: Throw the game without being seen to.\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You lose by a hair after a brave fight. She is delighted, and tips accordingly. You feel faintly like a forger.|You let her catch your bluff in the last hand. She laughs for the first time all night, and pays you twice over.|You lose so well she thinks she beat you fairly. The money is good. Lying with cards is still lying.}\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      renown -small\n      queue night_suspicion chance unlikely\n      say: {She catches you discarding an ace and puts her cards down. \"Don't patronise me.\" Word gets about, as word from a magistrate does.|You lose too obviously, and pay for the wine, and the story of it is round the village by noon.|She sees through you in three hands and says so to the whole table. The landlady doesn't look at you on the stair.}\n> right: Deal and keep score\n  hint: Honest work at the table, smaller pay.\n  gold +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You deal, count and say nothing. It is the dullest money you've ever earned, and it's clean.|You keep the score, honestly, and she loses honestly, and doesn't tip the scorer.|You deal for four hours. The magistrate wins without help, and nods at you as if you'd had something to do with it.}\n\n@event company_masked\ntitle: The Masked Night\nthread: company_night\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 3\nart: mask\ntags: money company_night\nscene: A candlelit upstairs room full of guests in feathered and lacquered masks, one masked figure watching a student without moving.\ntext: {Tonight every guest wears a mask, and nobody says why. The landlady hands you one too, plain black, no feathers.|The masks again. One guest, in lacquered silver, asks for you by the colour of your mask.|The masked night. The silver mask is here, and has saved you the seat beside it.} Without faces the room is easier to read, and harder.\n> left: Sit with the masks\n  hint: The best tips, and nobody's name.\n  challenge negotiate adept- wild:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      guile +small\n      mood -small\n      say: {You learn to read a room by its hands. You go home with heavy pockets, no names, and a feeling you can't put down.|The silver mask asks you nothing at all, and tips like a duke. You lie awake wondering who that was.|Behind the masks the guests say what they mean. You come home rich and a little colder.}\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      mood -medium\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {A masked guest won't take no for an answer, and nobody can see who it is. You leave by the back stair with a few coins and a very bad feeling.|The silver mask leans too close, and nobody else in the room seems to notice. You go home by the long way.|A guest takes your mask off, laughing, in front of everyone. Someone at the far table looks very like a prefect.}\n> right: Work the door\n  hint: Fair pay in the cold stairwell.\n  gold +medium\n  health -small\n  say: {You take coats at the door all night. The stairwell is freezing, and every coat smells of a different perfume.|You keep the door, and turn away a guest the landlady points out. The draught gets into your bones.|You work the door and watch the masks arrive one by one. The silver one bows to you, which is worse than being spoken to.}\n\n@event company_patron\ntitle: A Guest Who Offers Patronage\nthread: company_night\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 3\nart: book\ntags: money study company_night\nscene: An elderly scholar in a moth-eaten velvet coat at a tavern table, a pile of rare books beside a purse, talking earnestly to a student.\ntext: {An old collector of books has asked for \"the student who reads\". He wants company and argument, and he says he funds promising young minds.|The old collector is back with a new pile of books and a new argument. He has been thinking about what you said, for a month.|The collector again. He calls you his protégé to the whole room now, which is flattering and a little alarming.}\n> left: Take the patron's purse\n  hint: Generous, and he'll want your evenings.\n  gold +large\n  study -small\n  say: {He funds your books for the term, and wants you at his table every week. You argue till two, and sleep through a lecture.|The purse is generous and so is the argument, which runs until the candles do. Your coursework waits.|He pays you to disagree with him. You are good at it, and your exam reading is not getting done.}\n> right: Ask to borrow his books\n  hint: Learning instead of money.\n  lore +medium\n  gold +small\n  say: {He beams, and lends you three books older than the castle. You read them on the walk home by moonlight.|He lends you his annotated copy, full of furious notes in the margin. They are better than the book.|\"Finally,\" he says, \"someone who wants the books more than the money.\" He gives you a small purse anyway.}\n\n@event company_castle\ntitle: Someone from the Castle\nthread: company_night\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nart: eye\ntags: money danger company_night\ncast:\n  guest: any where tagged(guest, teacher) and met(guest)\nscene: A professor out of academic dress at a tavern card table, a glass of wine halfway to their mouth, looking up at a student holding a tray.\ntext: {Halfway up the stair you see {guest} at the corner table, out of robes, with a glass of wine and the air of someone who would rather not be seen either.|{guest} is at the corner table tonight. You both pretend this is a coincidence.|Tonight it is {guest} in the corner. Half the faculty seem to drink here, and there is an arrangement: nobody has seen anybody.}\n> left: Serve {them} as if you'd never met\n  hint: Discretion pays; a slip gets talked about.\n  challenge deceive adept:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      rel guest +tiny\n      say: {You serve {guest} like any guest, and {they} tip{s} like one, too generously. At breakfast {they} nod{s} to you as if nothing ever happened.|You keep a straight face all night. On the way out {guest} leaves a coin under {their} glass and, beside it, a very small nod.|Neither of you blinks. In class next week {guest} calls on you with something almost like respect.}\n    lose:\n      gold +small\n      rel guest -small\n      queue night_suspicion chance likely\n      say: {You drop a glass when {guest} looks up. Everyone turns. {guest} leaves early, and is cold to you all week.|You say \"Professor\" before you can stop yourself. The table laughs. {guest} does not.|{guest} catches your eye and you both flinch, in front of everyone. By morning half the village knows who was where.}\n> right: Slip back downstairs\n  hint: Give up the night's pay to stay unseen.\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You give the landlady an excuse and go down the back stair. It's a lost night's pay, and the walk home is sulky.|You hand your tray to another student and leave. {guest} never sees you; your purse notices.|You go home early, and lie awake doing the sum of what you didn't earn.}\n\n@event company_wants_more\ntitle: A Guest Who Wants More\nthread: company_night\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 3\nart: mask\ntags: money danger company_night\nscene: A well-dressed guest at a tavern table holding out a heavy purse, a student standing back from the table with arms folded, a landlady watching from the door.\ntext: {A guest with rings on every finger has been generous all night, and now pushes a heavy purse across the table. \"Come back to my inn,\" he says. \"Just to talk.\" The house rule is clear.|Another guest, another heavy purse, another invitation \"just to talk\". The landlady is busy across the room.|You know the look now: the generous hour, then the purse, then the invitation. Tonight it comes from a woman in pearls.}\n> left: Call the landlady\n  hint: The house rule; the tip goes with the guest.\n  alignment +tiny\n  gold +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {The landlady has the guest down the stair in a minute, purse and all. You're paid for the hours, and shaky for the rest of the night.|\"The stair,\" says the landlady, and the guest takes it. You keep your wage and lose the tip, and your hands won't stop shaking.|The guest is shown out. The landlady pours you something strong and pays you for the night. It doesn't feel like enough.}\n> right: Keep the purse, promise nothing\n  hint: Talk them round and keep it; it can go wrong.\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive adept:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      mood -small\n      say: {You let him believe tomorrow, and then the day after. He leaves the purse. You feel like a pickpocket with good manners.|You laugh, and keep the purse, and see the guest into a carriage, alone. It's the most you've earned in a night. You don't sleep.|You promise nothing in a way that sounds like something. She leaves happy and you leave rich, and a little ashamed.}\n    lose:\n      mood -medium\n      health -tiny\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {He sees through you, and takes his purse back with a hand that grips too hard. The landlady throws him out, too late.|The guest makes a scene on the stair, loudly calling you a cheat. A prefect on the stair hears every word.|She wants the purse back, loudly, and the landlady gives it to her. You go home with nothing but a bruise on your wrist.}\n\n# ---------------- The high table, round after round (feedback 2026-10-20) ----------------\n# A win with your purse at the high table doesn't end the night: Grisk offers the next hand at once (`queue … now`),\n# each round dearer and harder than the last, three in all. Walking away is always open; a loss ends the run.\n\n@event newt_high_table_raise\ntitle: Let It Ride\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 6\nart: cards\ntags: money danger\nscene: A goblin banker in a velvet waistcoat pushing a tall stack of coins back into the middle of a green-lamped card table.\ntext: {Grisk sweeps your winnings into one neat stack and pushes it back into the middle of the table. \"Let it ride,\" he says. \"The second hand pays double. Most people stop here. Most people are dull.\"|Grisk is already shuffling. \"Again?\" he says, as if it were your idea. Your winnings sit in the middle of the table, waiting.|\"Second hand,\" says Grisk, and the players at the next table stop pretending not to watch.} The new dealer is a woman who hasn't smiled once tonight.\n> left: Let it ride\n  hint: Your winnings on one hand; the table plays harder.\n  alignment -tiny\n  gold -large\n  challenge deceive adept+ unlikely wild:\n    win:\n      gold +huge\n      queue newt_high_table_last now\n      say: {The unsmiling dealer turns her last card and looks at you properly for the first time. The pot is yours, and it's heavy.|You bluff on nothing and the whole table folds. Grisk laughs so hard he has to sit down.|You win the second hand by a single card. Somewhere behind you somebody says your name, which is not ideal.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {The dealer takes the hand without a flicker. Your winnings go back to the bank, and the walk home is very long.|You read her wrong, and she lets you know it. Grisk pats your shoulder. \"That's the second hand,\" he says. \"It's always the second hand.\"|The pot goes the other way. On the stair down, a lamp moves in the courtyard and you have to wait in the dark for it to pass.}\n> right: Cash out\n  hint: Walk away with what you won.\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You stand, pocket your winnings and bow to the table. Grisk looks almost disappointed in you.|\"Dull,\" says Grisk fondly, as you go. Your purse doesn't think so.|You leave while the leaving is good, and whistle all the way back to the castle, very quietly.}\n\n@event newt_high_table_last\ntitle: Everything on One Hand\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nart: cards\ntags: money danger\nscene: A crowd of gamblers standing around one card table in a tavern back room, a goblin banker taking off his rings one by one.\ntext: {The room has gone quiet. Players from every other table have drifted over to stand around yours. Grisk takes off his rings, one by one, and sets them on the pot. \"Everything,\" he says. \"One hand. Then we see who goes home.\"|Grisk deals this one himself. Nobody has done that in years, someone whispers.|The whole back room is standing around your table now. Grisk rolls up his velvet sleeves.} Whatever is in the pot tonight is in it again, and more besides.\n> left: Everything on one hand\n  hint: The biggest pot in the room; Grisk deals.\n  alignment -small\n  gold -large\n  challenge deceive expert- unlikely wild:\n    win:\n      gold +huge\n      gold +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: {Grisk turns his last card, looks at yours, and starts to laugh. He puts his rings back on, one by one, and pushes the pot to you. \"Don't come back for a month,\" he says. \"I mean it kindly.\"|You win, and the back room applauds. By morning the village has three versions of the story, and in all of them you are taller.|The whole room breathes out at once. Grisk shakes your hand with both of his. \"A real player,\" he says, and it sounds like a warning.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      queue night_suspicion chance likely\n      say: {Grisk turns a card you didn't think was left in the deck. Your stake goes back to him, a fat slice of the night's winnings with it. He's very kind about it, which is worse.|You lose the hand on one card. The crowd drifts back to their own tables as if nothing happened. On the stair, a prefect's lamp is waiting.|The pot goes to Grisk. He counts it slowly, in front of you, and offers to call you a carriage. There are no carriages.}\n> right: Leave on a winning streak\n  hint: Two hands won, and a story to tell.\n  renown +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You stand up in the middle of the hush. \"Another night,\" you say, and leave with two pots in your coat. Grisk applauds, alone, and then everyone does.|You refuse the last hand. The back room is torn between booing and admiration. Your purse settles it.|\"Not tonight,\" you say. Grisk grins all his teeth. \"That,\" he says, \"is how you get to play again.\"}\n\n# ---------------- Company nights that run late (feedback 2026-10-20) ----------------\n\n@event company_late_hour\ntitle: The Second Party\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 8\nart: cup\ntags: money danger\nscene: A landlady catching a tired student by the sleeve on a tavern stair long after midnight, a second party arriving noisily below.\ntext: {Past two, the landlady catches your sleeve on the stair. \"Another party's just come up, and I'm a pair of hands short. Same pay again.\"|A second party is coming up the stair as yours goes down it. The landlady looks at you and raises an eyebrow.|\"One more,\" says the landlady, \"and I'll owe you.\" The castle will be waking in four hours.}\n> left: Stay till dawn\n  hint: Another party's pay; no sleep, and a later walk home.\n  health -small\n  queue any in company_night now\n  queue night_suspicion chance unlikely\n  say: {You take your jacket back off the peg. The second party is louder than the first.|You splash cold water on your face and go back up. The lamps are still lit, and so are the guests.|You stay. It will be light when you get home, and the castle's early risers will see you come in.}\n> right: Go home to bed\n  hint: One party is enough for one night.\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You hand back the jacket and walk home under the last of the stars. Your bed has never been so welcome.|\"Next time,\" you tell the landlady. You're asleep before the castle clock strikes three.|You leave the second party to someone else and sleep the four hours you have left like a stone.}\n\n@event company_wedding\ntitle: A Wedding Upstairs\nthread: company_night\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 3\nart: music\ntags: money company_night\nscene: A noisy wedding party in a tavern's upstairs room, a fiddler on a table, a bride in a crooked flower crown dancing with her grandmother.\ntext: {A miller's daughter from the next valley married this afternoon, and the party has moved upstairs at the Newt to keep going.|Another wedding upstairs: a carter and a dressmaker, and both families determined to out-dance the other.|A wedding again, and the fiddler from last time, who waves at you with his bow.} The landlady wants the dancing kept going and the families kept apart.\n> left: Keep everyone dancing\n  hint: Dance with every aunt; big tips if the families mix.\n  challenge perform journeyman:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      health -small\n      say: {You dance with every aunt, two uncles and the bride. By midnight the families are singing the same song. Both fathers tip you.|You get the two grandmothers dancing together, and after that nobody can remember the feud. The tips are enormous; your feet are not.|The fiddler and you keep it going until the candles go out. You walk home with a pocket of coins and a flower in your hair.}\n    lose:\n      gold +small\n      mood -small\n      say: {You ask the wrong cousin to dance, and the families end up on opposite sides of the room. A thin tip, and a long night.|The groom's brother picks a fight with the bride's, and you're the one standing between them. The landlady pays you off early.|Nobody wants to dance with the hired help tonight. You stand by the wall for four hours.}\n> right: Mind the food and the fire\n  hint: Steady pay, no dancing.\n  gold +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You keep the platters full and the fire fed. Nobody notices you, which at a wedding is a kind of success.|You carry cake until your arms ache. The bride kisses your cheek on her way out, and you go home sticky and paid.|You mind the fire and watch the dancing. It's honest money, and the music is good.}\n\n@event company_poets\ntitle: The Poets' Supper\nthread: company_night\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 3\nart: book\ntags: money study company_night\nscene: A long tavern table covered in wine bottles and loose manuscript pages, ink-stained poets arguing, one standing on a chair to recite.\ntext: {A society of village poets has taken the upstairs room for its supper, and needs someone to judge the contest.|{chosen(company_poets, left): The poets are back. Their president remembers your last verdict and has not forgiven it.|The poets are back. Their president remembers you pouring the wine, and asks whether you'd dare to judge this time.}|The poets' supper again, and tonight the contest is in rhyming couplets only, including the toasts.} The landlady says: whoever wins, the wine bill gets paid.\n> left: Judge the contest\n  hint: A clever verdict pleases everyone; a bad one pleases nobody.\n  challenge appeal journeyman-:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      lore +tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You give a prize to everyone, each for something different, and each of them thinks theirs is the real one. The wine bill gets paid with a flourish.|Your verdict, in rhyming couplets, gets a standing ovation. The president pays you himself and asks for a copy.|You find something true to say about every poem, even the long one about turnips. They tip in verse and in coin.}\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {You give the prize to the poem about the president's late wife. It was, it turns out, written by her rival. The supper ends early.|Your verdict starts an argument that becomes a fight that becomes a poem about you. It is not a kind poem.|Nobody agrees with you, loudly, in couplets.}\n> right: Pour and listen\n  hint: Plain pay, and a night of poems.\n  gold +medium\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {You pour wine and listen to forty poems. Three of them are good, and you remember one for weeks.|You keep the glasses full and the poets talking. You go home with fair pay and a head full of rhymes.|You pour and say nothing, and learn more about metre than a term of lectures taught you.}\n\n# ---------------- The boathouse ----------------\n\n@event ring_on_yourself\ntitle: Bet on Yourself\nplace: boathouse\nslot: midnight\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\n# The ring's other door (feedback 2026-10-16): the same chain as midnight_boathouse_ring, with a bookmaker.\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 9\nart: sword\ntags: danger money\nscene: A lantern-lit cellar under a boathouse, a scorched chalk ring on wet flagstones, a bookmaker holding up a slate of odds, a crowd in scarves.\non_show:\n  unflag ring_last_standing ring_down\n  chance unlikely:\n    flag ring_last_standing\ntext: {The ring under the boathouse has a bookmaker now, with a slate. \"Fancy yourself?\" he says. \"Put your money where your wand is. Cast what you like. Nobody down here tells.\"|The bookmaker knows your name and your odds, and they've shortened.|Your name is on the slate tonight before you've said you'll fight.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Across the top of his slate he has chalked LAST STANDING: step in tonight and you fight until you drop or have beaten four, or you pay the house a forfeit to walk out.|Win, and the next bout pays more.}\n> left: Bet your purse on yourself\n  alignment -tiny\n  gold -medium\n  challenge combat adept-:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      gold +medium\n      renown +tiny\n      health -small\n      queue any in ring_cameo now chance unlikely\n      queue ring_bout_2 now\n      say: {You win, singed and grinning, and the bookmaker pays out through his teeth.|The crowd roars. You collect your bet and your opponent's grudging handshake.|Your name was on the slate before you agreed, and the crowd is bigger for it. You win, and the bookmaker pays you in front of all of them.}\n    lose:\n      unflag ring_last_standing\n      health -medium\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {You go down in the third exchange, and your purse goes with you. On the way home a light is on in the prefects' corridor.|A bad loss: the bet, your pride and both eyebrows.|The bigger crowd came to see you lose, it turns out. You oblige, and your purse goes with your pride.}\n  if met(lyra):\n    queue lyra_ring_word chance unlikely\n> right: Fight for the purse only\n  challenge combat adept-:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      health -small\n      queue any in ring_cameo now chance unlikely\n      queue ring_bout_2 now\n      say: {A clean win for a small purse. The money-holder asks if you'll go again.|You win and bow to your opponent. The bookmaker is already chalking your name for the next bout.|You win the purse in front of the biggest crowd yet. The bookmaker sighs at the money he didn't make on you.}\n    lose:\n      unflag ring_last_standing\n      health -medium\n      say: {You lose, bow, and spend the walk home finding new bruises.|A loss, and the loser's purse, which is nothing at all.|You lose in front of the biggest crowd yet. At least it was only the purse.}\n> no: Not tonight\n  hint: Watch, and bet if you like.\n  unflag ring_last_standing\n  queue ring_side_bets now\n  say: {The bookmaker shrugs and rubs your name off the slate.|\"Your loss,\" says the bookmaker. \"Or someone else's gain. Care to back one?\"|You leave your name on the slate and your wand in your pocket.}\n\n# Walking out of a last-standing night (the critic, 2026-10-28): every fight down here keeps a way out. The house keeps\n# a forfeit from the purse and the crowd remembers it.\n@preset ring_forfeit\ndesc: Leaving the ring on a last-standing night.\ndo:\n  unflag ring_last_standing\n  gold -medium\n  renown -small\n  mood -small\n\n@event ring_bout_2\ntitle: The Second Bout\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: sword\ntags: danger money\nscene: A chalk ring under a boathouse, a fresh opponent twirling a wand, a crowd pressing in with coins in their fists.\nwhen: not flag(ring_down)\ntext: The money-holder points at the chalk again. {~A lanky girl steps in who quit the academy last spring. She kept the wand, and the duelling stance.|A fairground conjurer steps in, still in spangles, rolling a spark across his knuckles. The crowd knows him.|A hedge-hexer from past the village steps in, a cord of knotted charms at her wrist. Some of those knots aren't legal anywhere.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: stepping out now costs a forfeit.|The purse has doubled. Or you can take what you've won and go.}\n> left: Fight on\n  hint: A harder bout, a bigger purse.\n  challenge combat adept:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      queue ring_bout_3 now\n      say: {~She duels by the book she walked out on. You've read the later chapters. Her wand ends up in the crowd, and the purse ends up heavier.|The conjurer's sparks are all show. You aim at the hand that isn't waving, and he sits down in a shower of spangles.|You cut her charm-cord with your first spell. Whatever the knots were for, it doesn't happen, and she spits and yields.}\n    lose:\n      unflag ring_last_standing\n      health -medium\n      mood -small\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {~She was the best in her year before she left, and she still is. She helps you up off the flagstones, which is worse.|He palms a spark, and while you watch it, the other hand ends the bout. You limp up the steps with the night's winnings and singed sleeves.|One knot comes undone, and something cold walks through your head. You come round outside, with your purse, and a headache that lasts a day.}\n> right: {flag(ring_last_standing): Forfeit, and walk out|Take the purse and go}\n  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: the house keeps a forfeit, and the crowd remembers.|Keep what you've won.}\n  if not flag(ring_last_standing):\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {~You bow out with the purse in your fist. The dropout shrugs and finds someone else.|You leave the conjurer to the next fool, and count your winnings on the walk home.|The hexer laughs as you go, and ties another knot.}\n  else:\n    ring_forfeit\n    say: You raise your hand and step out of the chalk. The bookmaker chalks FORFEIT across your name, takes the house's share of your purse in front of everyone, and the cellar hisses you up the stairs.\n\n@event ring_bout_3\ntitle: The Third Bout\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: sword\ntags: danger money\nscene: A packed boathouse cellar roaring at a chalk ring, a wizard in a long coat stepping in, lantern light swinging.\nwhen: not flag(ring_down)\ntext: Two down. The cellar is packed now and the betting is loud. {~The house sends in a Conclave clerk in ink-stained cuffs, moonlighting for the purse. He reads your stance like a form.|The house sends in a prefect who lost the sash last year, and has been angry about it ever since.|The house sends in someone in a hood whose wand hand is grey to the wrist. The steps go quiet.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: the chalk, or the forfeit.|One more win and the purse is the biggest yet. Or walk away a winner.}\n> left: Fight on\n  hint: Harder still, and worth it.\n  challenge combat adept+:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      gold +small\n      renown +small\n      queue ring_bout_4 now\n      say: {~He foresees every spell you planned. So you stop planning. The clerk goes down muttering about irregular procedure, and the cellar shakes.|The ex-prefect fights like a rulebook with a grudge. You break a rule he didn't know, and the purse is fat.|The hood falls back as they go down: nobody you know, and a face older than the hands. The crowd chants your name to drown the quiet.}\n    lose:\n      unflag ring_last_standing\n      health -large\n      queue night_suspicion chance even\n      say: {~The clerk dismantles you, politely, by the numbers. You wake on the steps with the purse still in your pocket and a ringing ear.|The ex-prefect's ward doesn't move, and neither, after a while, do you. Your ribs remember it all week.|The hooded caster drinks something out of you that isn't blood. You go down and stay down, and someone carries you to the door.}\n> right: {flag(ring_last_standing): Forfeit, and walk out|Take the purse and go}\n  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: the house keeps a forfeit, and the crowd remembers.|Leave a winner.}\n  if not flag(ring_last_standing):\n    mood +small\n    say: {~You leave the clerk waiting in the chalk with his forms. The crowd boos, and your purse doesn't care.|The ex-prefect calls you a coward. Nobody else does; you're holding too much money.|You bow to the hood and climb the steps. Some fights are better not known.}\n  else:\n    ring_forfeit\n    say: Two down, and you step out. It's a forfeit under the rules: the house takes its cut of your winnings, and the crowd, which had money on you, lets you know about it all the way to the door.\n\n@event ring_bout_4\ntitle: The Last Bout\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: trophy\ntags: danger money\nscene: A boathouse cellar gone silent around a chalk ring, the night's champion stepping in, wand raised, every lantern turned toward the fighters.\nwhen: not flag(ring_down)\ntext: Three down, and the cellar has gone quiet. {~The ring's own champion steps in: a duellist the academy expelled, who hasn't lost down here in a year.|The bookmaker himself takes off his coat. His wand was in it all along, and the old hands step back from the chalk.|A girl in a borrowed cloak steps in, a runaway from Thornwick. Her shadow moves a moment after she does, and the old hands start betting against you.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: win this and the night is yours.|Win, and the cellar will talk about you for a term. Or go while you can still walk.}\n> left: Fight the last bout\n  hint: Expert opposition; the night's whole purse.\n  challenge combat expert-:\n    win:\n      gold +huge\n      renown +small\n      health -small\n      if flag(ring_last_standing):\n        gold +large\n        renown +small\n      unflag ring_last_standing\n      say: {~The champion's ward breaks at last, and the cellar stamps until dust comes down from the boathouse floor. You go home with a purse you can barely lift.|He reads your next spell out of your head every time, so you cast the one you hadn't thought of. He goes down laughing and pays you from his own coat.|You hit the shadow, not the girl, and she yields, grinning, and buys you a drink you can't hold. You walk home sore and rich and famous in one cellar.}\n    lose:\n      unflag ring_last_standing\n      health -large\n      queue night_suspicion chance likely\n      say: {~The champion ends it in one spell, and you understand why the academy wanted them gone. You wake on the steps with your night's winnings and a long walk.|The bookmaker casts things the library keeps chained, and wins clean. You crawl up the steps holding your purse and your ribs.|You can't tell the girl from her shadow, and the wrong one hits you. The crowd is so loud the prefects must hear it.}\n> right: {flag(ring_last_standing): Forfeit, and walk out|Take the purse and go}\n  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: the house keeps a forfeit, and the crowd remembers.|Three wins is a fine night.}\n  if not flag(ring_last_standing):\n    mood +small\n    say: {~You bow out with three wins and a heavy purse. The champion nods: next time.|You don't fight the man who sets the odds, and knows your thoughts. You take your winnings before he can change his mind.|You look at the runaway's shadow and decide three is plenty.}\n  else:\n    ring_forfeit\n    say: You step out before the last bout. The bookmaker counts out the forfeit from your purse with great ceremony, and the cellar boos you, and a few people, quietly, look relieved for you.\n\n@event ring_side_bets\ntitle: Side Bets\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: dice\ntags: money danger\nscene: Students on the steps of a boathouse cellar passing coins to a bookmaker while two fighters circle in a chalk ring below.\ntext: {You find a place on the cellar steps. The bookmaker works his way along them, slate in hand.|From the steps you can see the fighters' wand hands, which tell you more than their faces. The bookmaker is coming round.|You know the regulars on the steps now, and who bets with their heart. The bookmaker holds out his slate.}\n> left: Back the favourite\n  hint: A small stake on the likely winner.\n  gold -small\n  challenge research using guile journeyman luck:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      say: {Your fighter wins. You collect quietly and leave before anyone remembers your face.|You read the wand hands right. The bookmaker pays you as if it hurt.|The favourite wins, as favourites do. You pocket a small, sensible profit.}\n    lose:\n      say: {Your fighter goes down in the first exchange. So does your stake.|You picked the wand hand, not the wizard. The bookmaker smiles.|The favourite slips on the wet stone, and your crowns slip with them.}\n> under: Back the underdog\n  hint: A bigger stake against the odds.\n  gold -medium\n  challenge research using guile adept unlikely luck:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      gold +medium\n      say: {The underdog wins, and you're the only one on the steps who backed them. The bookmaker counts it out very slowly.|Your long shot lands one spell, and it's enough. The steps turn round to look at you.|You saw what nobody else did, the limp in the favourite's step. You walk home heavy with silver.}\n    lose:\n      say: {The underdog is an underdog for a reason. Your stake is gone in a minute.|A brave fight, a long fall, and your crowns go with it.|You backed your heart. Your purse pays for it.}\n> home: Go home\n  say: {You watch one bout and go home with your purse unopened.|You leave before the bookmaker reaches you.|You climb the steps into the cold and leave the ring to its noise.}\n\n# A character who would fight in an illegal ring steps into the chalk out of turn (feedback 2026-10-25): drawn by chance\n# after a won first or second bout (`queue any in ring_cameo now chance unlikely`), before the next one, never the last.\n# Only those whose cast fits a cellar ring have a card here: reckless Corvin, proud Lyra, Bram out of his sash, fierce\n# Ilse. Losing (or walking out) ends the night: the next bout's `when` reads ring_down.\n@pool ring_cameo\ndesc: Someone you know steps into the boathouse ring out of turn, between bouts.\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 12\nart: duel\ntags: danger money\n\n@event ring_cameo_corvin\ntitle: A Familiar Grin\npool: ring_cameo\nactor: corvin\nwhen: met(corvin) and around(corvin)\nscene: A grinning young man vaulting a rope into a chalk ring in a lantern-lit cellar, sleeves rolled, a wand spinning in his fingers.\ntext: Before the next bout someone vaults the rope into the chalk: Corvin, sleeves rolled, wand spinning. He sees you and his grin slips. \"Saint. Of course it's you.\" Then, low: \"I owe three people money, and one of them has started describing what happens next. The house pays double for a grudge match. So hate me. Loudly.\"\n> left: Fight him for real\n  hint: He fights dirty, and fast.\n  challenge combat vs corvin.guile:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +tiny\n      rel corvin -tiny\n      say: You knock his wand into the crowd on the fifth exchange. \"Rude,\" he says from the flagstones, and laughs, but his eyes go to the bookmaker's slate and stay there a moment too long.\n    lose:\n      flag ring_down\n      unflag ring_last_standing\n      health -small\n      rel corvin +tiny\n      say: He was never where your spell landed. He hauls you up by the collar, collects his purse, and presses a few coins of it into your hand. \"Your cut, for the hating. You were very convincing.\"\n> right: Put on a show, and let him win\n  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: this counts as a forfeit.|Your night ends; his debt doesn't.}\n  flag ring_down\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel corvin +medium\n  say: You shout, he shouts, sparks fly everywhere but at each other, and you go down with a groan the back row will talk about all week. The bettors who backed you never know. Corvin does. \"I owe you,\" he says on the steps. \"Add it to the list.\"\n  if flag(ring_last_standing):\n    ring_forfeit\n    say: On a last-standing night it counts as a forfeit: the bookmaker takes the house's share of your winnings on the way out.\n\n@event ring_cameo_lyra\ntitle: Someone Worth Losing To\npool: ring_cameo\nactor: lyra\nwhen: met(lyra) and around(lyra)\nscene: A proud red-haired student in a chalk ring, gloves off, hair tied back, a ribbon of flame curling from her wand, a cellar crowd roaring.\ntext: The crowd parts. Lyra Vaneshade is in the chalk, gloves off, hair tied back. \"So it's true.\" Her voice carries over the whole cellar. \"You're throwing your talent away down here, in front of drunks. Fine. Then you'll lose it to someone worth losing to.\" Somebody is already shouting odds on House Vaneshade.\n> left: Fight her\n  hint: In front of everyone.\n  challenge combat vs lyra.arcana:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +small\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: Her flame breaks on your last spell and she goes down on one knee in the chalk, in front of all of them. She stands, white to the lips. \"Again,\" she says. \"In the hall. Where it counts.\" The crowd will be talking about it for a term.\n    lose:\n      flag ring_down\n      unflag ring_last_standing\n      health -small\n      rel lyra +tiny\n      say: She takes you apart in four exchanges, beautifully. Then she walks you up the steps herself, holding your elbow. \"Now you know,\" she says, \"where you belong. It isn't here.\"\n> right: Walk out with her\n  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: this counts as a forfeit.|The crowd won't like it.}\n  flag ring_down\n  renown -tiny\n  rel lyra +medium\n  say: You step out of the chalk and offer her your arm. The cellar boos you both up the steps. Outside, in the cold, Lyra lets go of your arm and doesn't say thank you. She does walk you all the way back.\n  if flag(ring_last_standing):\n    ring_forfeit\n    say: On a last-standing night it counts as a forfeit: the bookmaker takes the house's share of your winnings on the way out.\n\n@event ring_cameo_bram\ntitle: Out of Uniform\npool: ring_cameo\nactor: bram\nwhen: met(bram) and around(bram)\nscene: A broad young man pulling back a hood in a chalk ring, no prefect's sash, wand gripped tight, staring at someone he didn't expect.\ntext: The next challenger steps in hooded, and drops the hood: Bram Hollis, without his red sash. He sees you and goes the colour of it. \"You didn't see me,\" he says, fast and low. \"I didn't see you. Nobody down here writes anything down. That's the point.\" Then, loud, for the crowd: \"Well? Get in the chalk!\"\n> left: Fight him\n  hint: He hits like a wall.\n  challenge combat vs bram.vigor:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +tiny\n      rel bram +small\n      say: His ward finally gives, and he sits down hard in the chalk. Then something odd happens: he laughs, a real one, out loud. \"Nobody's ever beaten me where my father couldn't hear about it,\" he says. He offers you his hand.\n    lose:\n      flag ring_down\n      unflag ring_last_standing\n      health -small\n      rel bram +tiny\n      say: He walks through your spells like rain and puts you on the flagstones. On the steps he stops. \"Not a word,\" he says. \"And not a demerit either. We were never here.\"\n> right: Let him have the bout\n  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: this counts as a forfeit.|Leave him his secret, and the purse.}\n  flag ring_down\n  rel bram +medium\n  say: You step back out of the chalk and bow to him. He stares at you as if it were a trick. On the stairs to the dormitory next morning, your name isn't in his ledger, and he looks away first.\n  if flag(ring_last_standing):\n    ring_forfeit\n    say: On a last-standing night it counts as a forfeit: the bookmaker takes the house's share of your winnings on the way out.\n\n@event ring_cameo_ilse\ntitle: Frost in the Chalk\npool: ring_cameo\nactor: ilse\nwhen: met(ilse) and around(ilse)\nscene: A girl in white furs in a chalk ring, frost creeping from her boots across the flagstones, lantern flames shrinking, a crowd stepping back.\ntext: The cellar goes cold. The lanterns shrink. A girl in white furs steps into the chalk and frost creeps out from her boots across the flagstones: Ilse Dumont. \"They said the academy's best come down here,\" she says. She looks you over. \"They lied. But you'll do.\"\n> left: Fight her\n  hint: She was raised to fight.\n  challenge combat vs ilse.arcana:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      renown +small\n      rel ilse +small\n      say: She slips on her own ice and your spell catches her before she's up. She stays down a moment, then laughs, a short Northern sound. \"Again,\" she says. \"Not tonight. But again.\" She takes her purse and leaves the cellar colder.\n    lose:\n      flag ring_down\n      unflag ring_last_standing\n      health -small\n      rel ilse +tiny\n      say: Rime closes over your wand hand and you can't feel it to cast. She stands over you, unimpressed. \"You fight like someone taught you,\" she says. \"Find someone who didn't.\" Your fingers ache till morning.\n> right: Ask her why she's down here\n  hint: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: this counts as a forfeit.|Talk, and give up the night's next bout.}\n  flag ring_down\n  rel ilse +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: She doesn't answer. Instead she tells you your stance is wrong, then fixes it, roughly, with her boot. \"Southerners,\" she says, as if that explained everything. The crowd boos you both, and she doesn't seem to hear.\n  if flag(ring_last_standing):\n    ring_forfeit\n    say: On a last-standing night it counts as a forfeit: the bookmaker takes the house's share of your winnings on the way out.\n\n@event lyra_ring_word\ntitle: Lyra Has Heard\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus or not around(lyra)\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nactor: lyra\nart: sword\ntags: social\nscene: A proud red-haired student blocking a corridor with folded arms, a duelling glove tucked in her belt.\ntext: Lyra blocks the corridor. \"The ring under the boathouse.\" She says it as if it were a smell. \"Duelling for coins in a cellar, in front of drunks, with spells nobody will report. You can actually duel. I've seen you. This is beneath you.\" A pause. \"I'd hate to lose to someone who does it.\"\n> left: \"Then spar with me instead.\"\n  rel lyra +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: She stares, then almost smiles. \"Tomorrow, the Duelling Hall, six sharp.\" She beats you, and then shows you why, which from Lyra is close to an apology for the corridor.\n> right: \"It pays. You wouldn't know.\"\n  rel lyra -medium\n  gold +tiny\n  say: Her face shuts like a door. \"No,\" she says. \"I wouldn't.\" She walks off. Later the Newt's landlady hands back the deposit you left on your bar tab: Lyra has paid it off. That is somehow worse.\n\n@event grisk_pickup\ntitle: A Parcel at the Boathouse\nplace: boathouse\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 12\nactor: grisk\nwhen: flag(grisk_contact) and not flag(grisk_parcel) and not flag(grisk_gone)\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nscene: A dark boathouse jetty at midnight, a small goblin holding out a parcel wrapped in oilcloth, the lake black behind him.\ntext: {Grisk is waiting on the jetty, a parcel in oilcloth under his arm. \"Into the village by tomorrow afternoon. A cobbler's back door. Don't open it, don't drop it, don't be seen.\"|The jetty, the oilcloth, Grisk's teeth in the dark. \"Same arrangement,\" he says.|Grisk again, and a heavier parcel than last time.} {It ticks faintly.|It's cold enough to frost your gloves.|It smells of oranges and something under the oranges.}\n> left: Take the parcel\n  meet grisk\n  alignment -tiny\n  flag grisk_parcel\n  queue grisk_delivery in 1\n  say: {You take it. It is heavier than it looks, and the walk back to your room is very long.|You hide it under your bed. You don't sleep well with it there.|You take the heavier parcel. It smells of oranges, and under the oranges of something you decide not to name.}\n> right: Not this month\n  meet grisk\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {\"Next month, then,\" says Grisk, as if it were already arranged. He tucks the parcel away. You feel watched all the way back up the lawn.|Grisk shrugs. \"There's always someone.\" You don't like how true it sounds.|You turn down the heavier parcel. Grisk weighs it in his hand, then in his mind. \"Your loss,\" he says. \"Or not.\"}\n\n@event grisk_delivery\ntitle: The Cobbler's Back Door\ntrigger: queued\nslot: afternoon\ndefer: off_campus\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: pouch\ntags: money danger\nscene: A narrow village lane in daylight, a student with an oilcloth parcel under one arm walking past a prefect who is buying apples.\ntext: {~You carry Grisk's parcel down to the village in broad daylight.|Grisk's parcel, the village lane, and too many people about.|The parcel is in your satchel and the satchel is suddenly the heaviest thing you own.} Bram is at the apple stall. The cobbler's back door is past him.\n> left: Walk past as if you belong\n  unflag grisk_parcel\n  challenge deceive vs bram.guile +1:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {~You nod to Bram, buy an apple, and knock twice at the cobbler's door. A hand takes the parcel; another gives you a purse. Your heart takes an hour to slow down.|The door opens before you knock. Coins, a nod, done. You eat the apple on the way home and taste none of it.|The satchel feels heavier with every step past Bram. He waves. You wave. The cobbler pays, and your hands shake until supper.}\n    lose:\n      gain watched\n      flag grisk_angry\n      mood -small\n      queue grisk_cross in 1\n      say: {~Bram calls your name. You drop the parcel in a horse trough and walk on, very calmly. Bram follows you halfway home.|You panic at the corner and leave the parcel behind a water butt. When you go back, it's gone, and Bram is writing in his notebook.|Bram asks to see inside your satchel, kindly. You show him your books, and he never looks in the other pocket. You walk home sick.}\n> right: Leave it at the door and run\n  unflag grisk_parcel\n  gold +small\n  say: {~You go the long way round, drop it on the cobbler's step and run. The cobbler pays you, short, for the lack of manners.|You don't knock, and you don't wait. Short pay, slipped under your door that night.|You drop the satchel's weight on the cobbler's step and run. He pays you short, and you're glad to be rid of it.}\n\n@event grisk_cross\ntitle: Grisk Is Not Pleased\ntrigger: queued\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\nactor: grisk\nart: chain\ntags: money danger\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting on a student's bed uninvited, a ledger open on his knee, the dormitory window open behind him.\ntext: Grisk is sitting on your bed. You didn't leave the window open. \"A parcel was lost,\" he says pleasantly, turning a page in his ledger. \"Lost things have a price. I've written it down. Would you like to see?\"\n> left: Put it on your account\n  meet grisk\n  unflag grisk_angry\n  if has(in_debt):\n    debt_paid = debt_paid - 2\n    mood -small\n    say: \"Two more months,\" he says, and writes it neatly. \"Such a good customer.\" He leaves by the window. You don't sleep with it open again.\n  else:\n    gain in_debt\n    debt_paid = 0\n    debt_missed = 0\n    mood -small\n    say: He writes your name in the ledger under a new line: one parcel, six payments. \"Twelve a month,\" he says, \"as usual.\" He leaves by the window. You don't sleep with it open again.\n> right: Tell him you're done\n  meet grisk\n  unflag grisk_angry grisk_contact\n  flag grisk_gone\n  health -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: \"Everyone is, eventually,\" Grisk says. On your way to breakfast, the stair you always take collapses three steps under you. Nobody saw. Everybody heard. You never see him again.\n\n# ---------------- The refectory kitchens ----------------\n\n@event kitchen_stores\ntitle: The Stores\nplace: refectory\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 9\nart: meal\ntags: money danger\nscene: A dark storeroom behind a castle kitchen, sacks of sugar and crates of oranges stacked high, a student with an empty satchel.\ntext: {Behind the kitchens is the storeroom, full of sugar, spices and oranges from the south. The village pays well for oranges in winter, and nobody counts the crates.|The storeroom again. You know which crate is at the back and never counted.|The stores have a new padlock, which is mostly decorative.} {A mouse watches you without judgement.|The kitchen cat does judge you.|Somewhere a pot is still dripping.}\n> left: Fill a satchel to sell\n  alignment -small\n  gold +small\n  queue night_suspicion chance even\n  say: {Oranges, a cone of sugar, a jar of saffron. The village grocer asks no questions and pays in coppers. Somebody in the castle will be short of saffron.|A full satchel and an empty conscience, or nearly. On the way out, the scullery door bangs behind you.|The new padlock opens with a hairpin. You fill the satchel, and the grocer pays, and you wonder who'll be blamed for the lock.}\n> right: Take one orange and go\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You eat it in the dark by the banked fire. It's the best orange you have ever eaten, and you know exactly why.|One orange. You leave the peel in the fire so nobody finds it.|You take one orange past the new padlock and feel worse about it than about all the crates.}\n\n@event kitchen_night_baker\ntitle: The Night Baker\nplace: refectory\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 9\nart: meal\ntags: nature\nscene: A warm castle bakehouse at midnight, a flour-dusted old baker kneading dough by the light of an open oven, a student leaning in the doorway.\ntext: {The bakehouse behind the kitchens is lit. The night baker, flour to the elbows, looks up. \"Hungry or helpful?\" she says. \"Both is allowed.\"|The night baker waves you in without looking up. \"Dough's waiting.\"|The bakehouse smells of tomorrow's bread. The baker has saved you a heel of yesterday's.} {The oven sighs.|A tray of rolls is cooling by the door.|She is humming something old.}\n> left: Knead until the oven's hot\n  lose hungry\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You knead until your arms shake. She gives you the first roll out of the oven and no lecture at all.|An hour of flour and warmth, and a hot roll for your trouble. You go back to bed with your hands smelling of bread, and sleep like a stone.|You knead while she hums something old. When the loaves go in, she toasts yesterday's heel for you, and you eat it on the step.}\n> right: Just sit by the oven\n  lose hungry\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You sit by the open oven with your knees pulled up. She works, talks to the dough, and lets you be.|You sit by the oven with a roll from the cooling tray while she works. She doesn't ask why you're up. You don't ask why she is.|You eat the heel of bread and warm your feet. Upstairs, the castle is cold; down here, it never is.}\n\n# ---------------- The Restricted Section ----------------\n\n@event restricted_page_buyer\ntitle: A Buyer for Old Pages\nplace: restricted_section\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 8\n# Retired (threads 1): the Restricted Stacks heist replaces the section's midnight cards; the collector is its fence.\nwhen: false\nart: book\ntags: money danger mystery\nscene: A dim Restricted Section aisle at midnight, a student holding a razor over an illuminated page, a black book watching from the shelf above.\ntext: {Grisk mentioned, once, that a collector pays for illuminated pages \"of a certain age\". The Restricted Section is full of them.|{flag(sold_page): There's a gap on the shelf where the last page you sold came from.|The illuminated book you read last time is back on its shelf, untouched.}|The illuminated books lean away from you as you pass.} {A gold-leaf dragon glints on one page.|One margin is full of tiny stars, all black.|A page here is worth a month's board.}\n> left: Cut a page\n  flag sold_page\n  alignment -small\n  gold +medium\n  queue night_suspicion chance even\n  say: {One clean cut. The book doesn't cry out, which is somehow worse. The collector pays well, and never asks where.|You sell the page and keep the guilt, which no one will buy.|The books lean away, and you cut a page anyway. A month's board, in one stroke. You don't come back to this aisle for a long time.}\n> right: Read it instead\n  lore +small\n  mana -tiny\n  say: {You read the illuminated page by moonlight. It's a recipe for honey cakes, in exquisite gold leaf, and the gilding draws at your mana the whole time you read. You leave it where it is.|The page is a list of stars, by name, and each name pulls a little magic out of you as you read it. You learn three, and put the book back.|The books lean away from you, then back, as you read instead of cut. The page is a map of the castle as it was. It costs you a little magic to look at it.}\n\n# ---------------- The observatory roof ----------------\n\n@event midnight_roof_partner\ntitle: The Roof, Together\nplace: observatory\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\ncast:\n  partner: any where bond(partner) == \"partner\" and around(partner)\nart: star\ntags: romance nature\nscene: Two students lying side by side on an observatory roof at midnight under a sky full of stars, one blanket between them.\ntext: {You've brought {partner} up to the observatory roof, with a stolen blanket and two cups of cocoa going cold.|{partner} knows the way up now, and goes first.|{partner} has brought the blanket this time.} {The stars are very close.|A meteor, then another.|The castle below is all asleep.}\n> left: Stay until the sky turns\n  spark partner +small\n  health -small\n  queue night_suspicion chance unlikely\n  say: {You name the stars between you, wrong, and argue about it, and stay until the sky goes grey. The stair down is lit by a prefect's lamp you just avoid.|You fall asleep up there and wake to the first bell, frozen and happy.|{partner} wraps you both in the blanket {they} brought and talks you to sleep. The castle wakes up around you.}\n> right: An hour, then back\n  rel partner +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {One hour, one blanket, back in bed before anyone stirs. You can still feel where {partner} was leaning on you.|You go down early. {partner} squeezes your hand on the stair, which is better than any star.|One hour under {partner}'s blanket. {They} fold{s} it and give{s} it to you at the bottom of the stair: \"For next time.\"}\n\n@event kael_midnight_roof\ntitle: Kael at Midnight\nplace: observatory\nactor: kael\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 12\nart: snow\ntags: social\nscene: A northern student sitting on the observatory parapet at midnight with frost spreading from his hands, the lake far below, the sky thick with stars.\ntext: {Kael is on the observatory parapet, which he shouldn't be, with frost spreading from his fingers into the stone. He moves over to make room.|Kael again, on the parapet, the night's cold not touching him.|Kael has left room on the parapet before you arrive.} {\"I can't sleep south of the mountains,\" he says. \"Too warm.\"|He is watching the lake, not the stars.|\"Northern stars,\" he says, pointing at nothing you can see.}\n> left: Sit with him\n  meet kael\n  rel kael +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You sit until your fingers ache from the cold. He names northern constellations in a language that sounds like snow falling. It's the most he's ever said.|He doesn't talk. Neither do you. When you go, he says \"Goodnight,\" as if it were difficult, and it was.|He's left room, so you take it. He points out a northern star you can't see, and you pretend you can. He knows, and is pleased.}\n> right: Ask what he's watching for\n  meet kael\n  lore +tiny\n  rel kael -tiny\n  say: {\"For it to wake,\" he says, and then shuts up like a book. You've pressed too hard. But you know which way he faces now.|\"Nothing,\" he says, too fast. He's watching the middle of the lake. You leave him to it, colder than when you came.|\"Home,\" he says, about the northern stars, and nothing more. It's the most honest thing he's told you, and he looks away after.}\n\n# ---------------- The gargoyle passage ----------------\n\n@event corvin_note_was_mine\ntitle: Second Timetable\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nactor: corvin\nslot: midnight\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nwhen: seen(night_lead_in) > 0 and rel(corvin) >= 6\nart: map\ntags: social mystery\nscene: A secret passage behind a gargoyle at midnight, a grinning dark-haired student holding up a thumbnail-sized silver map, chalk marks on the wall behind him.\ntext: In the gargoyle passage, Corvin holds up a map no bigger than a thumbnail. \"Recognise it? The castle has a second timetable. It starts at midnight.\" He grins. \"Who did you think writes notes like that? Prefects?\"\n> left: \"Why me?\"\n  meet corvin\n  rel corvin +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Because you looked at that door at the back of the library the way I do.\" He shrugs, and doesn't joke. \"And because it's lonely, knowing all the doors.\" You don't sleep much, after.\n> right: \"You could have signed it.\"\n  meet corvin\n  rel corvin +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: \"And spoil it?\" He tucks the map into your pocket. \"Keep it. The doors move. The map knows.\" You check it in the morning, and one of the six doors has moved.\n\n@event corvin_midnight_watch\ntitle: Corvin Keeps Watch\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nactor: corvin\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: lantern\ntags: social\nscene: A dark corridor at midnight, a dark-haired student leaning out of a niche to wave someone past, a prefect's lantern turning a far corner.\ntext: {Corvin leans out of a niche as you pass. \"Bram's on the east stair. Take the west. You owe me nothing, I'm a saint.\"|Corvin's in his niche again with an apple and a list of where every prefect is.|\"West stair,\" says Corvin, before you ask.} {He looks tired.|He has a new scratch on his cheek.|He's whistling, which he shouldn't.}\n> left: Keep him company\n  meet corvin\n  rel corvin +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You share his apple and his niche. He tells you which tapestry hides a cupboard full of confiscated wands. You're up till the bells.|You keep watch with him for an hour. He's funnier at midnight, and sadder, and pretends to be neither.|He whistles until you shush him, then whistles quieter. You keep watch till the bell and learn three new routes.}\n> right: Take the west stair\n  meet corvin\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {The west stair is dark and empty, as promised. Corvin's map is never wrong. You wonder who taught him to be so careful.|You slip past Bram's lamp with a corridor to spare.|West stair, before you've asked. Corvin's whistling follows you down it, far too loud, and nobody catches either of you.}\n\n# ---------------- The lakeshore ----------------\n\n@event midnight_swim\ntitle: The Lake at Midnight\npool: lakeshore_routine\nslot: midnight\ntone: warm\nart: wave\ntags: nature\nscene: A moonlit lakeshore at midnight, clothes folded on a rock, a swimmer's head and a ring of ripples in the silver water.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The lake at midnight has a skin of ice at the edges and stars in the middle.|{~The lake at midnight is black and silver and perfectly still.|Nobody swims after curfew, which is why you do.|The water is warm from the day and the air is not.}} {clock(hollowstar) >= 5: Far out, a ring of frost forms and fades.|{~An owl crosses the moon.|Somewhere on the far shore, a lantern.|A fish jumps, very loudly.}}\n> left: Swim out to the middle\n  mood +medium\n  health -small\n  say: {~You swim until the castle is a toy and the stars are all around you. The cold gets into your bones and stays till breakfast.|You float on your back in the middle of the lake. Something far below hums, once. You swim back faster than you swam out.|You swim out and turn on your back. The stars and the water are the same colour, and you lose track of which is which. You swim back slowly.}\n> right: Paddle, then get dry\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~Knee-deep, then waist-deep, then out, gasping and laughing at yourself. Your teeth chatter all the way up the lawn.|A quick, cold paddle, and back to bed glowing.|You paddle until the fish jumps right beside you. You're out of the water before you've decided to be.}\n\n@event grey_on_the_shore\ntitle: Someone Else Who Can't Sleep\nplace: lakeshore\nslot: midnight\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: clock(hollowstar) >= 2 and seen(hs_grey_stranger) > 0 and not flag(grey_shore_seen)\nweight: 14\nart: mask\ntags: mystery hollowstar danger\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: A moonlit lakeshore at midnight, a tall man in a grey coat sitting on an upturned boat, frost spreading from his boots across the pebbles.\ntext: The man in grey is sitting on an upturned boat at the water's edge, as if he'd been waiting for a ferry. \"You can't sleep either,\" he says pleasantly. \"Nobody sleeps well near this lake. Sit. I won't keep you long. I only want to tell you what the headmistress won't.\"\n> left: Sit and listen\n  flag grey_shore_seen\n  alignment -tiny\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: He tells you, kindly, that the founders were frightened people who buried what they couldn't understand, and that it has been lonely down there for three hundred years. It sounds true. You walk back with it, and can't put it down.\n> right: Walk away\n  flag grey_shore_seen\n  alignment +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: You turn your back and walk, and the cold follows you all the way up the lawn like a dog. At the door you look back. The boat is empty, and frosted white.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/placeholders.grim","text":"# Placeholders (playtest 2, I7): places whose ordinary visits could come up empty get one quiet card each, so a\n# visit always shows something. Low weight, so real cards win when they're available. Tagged `placeholder`: the\n# budget lint lists them instead of judging them, and X replaces them with real content.\n\n@event courier_loft_quiet_hour\ntitle: A Quiet Hour at the Loft\nplace: courier_loft\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 2\nart: letter\ntags: placeholder job\nscene: An empty courier loft in slanting light, parcels stacked neatly, a single broom asleep in its rack.\ntext: The Courier Loft is quiet today. The dispatch bell hangs still, the pigeonholes are full and nobody needs anything flown anywhere. It's a nice place when it isn't in a hurry.\n> left: Sit by the window a while\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You watch the brooms doze in their rack and the dust turn in the light. Nothing happens, pleasantly.|An hour among the parcels with nobody shouting your name. You leave in a better mood.}\n> right: Head off\n  say: {~You leave the loft to its quiet.|Nothing to fly today. You go and find something else to do.}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_quiet_hour\ntitle: An Empty Study\nplace: headmistress_tower\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 2\nart: book\ntags: placeholder\nscene: A tower study with nobody in it, tea going cold on a tray, a board of crystal towers catching the afternoon sun.\ntext: The study at the top of the tower is empty. The headmistress is elsewhere, the tea has gone cold and the crystal board by the window throws little rainbows on the books. It's a nice room when nobody is deciding your future in it.\n> left: Enjoy the view a moment\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You stand at the window and look down at the whole castle, small and busy. Then you go, before anyone asks why you're here.|The rainbows move slowly across the shelves. It's very peaceful, and a little like trespassing.}\n> right: Leave before anyone comes\n  say: {~You close the door softly behind you.|Nobody here today. You take the long stair down.}\n\n@event ledger_office_quiet_hour\ntitle: A Slow News Day\nplace: ledger_office\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 2\nart: paper\ntags: placeholder club\nscene: A cramped newspaper office under a staircase, the press silent, old front pages curling on the walls.\ntext: It's a slow news day at the Lumen Ledger. The press is cold, the editor's chair is empty and the only sound is the library above, creaking. It's a nice room when nobody is on deadline.\n> left: Read the old front pages\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You read headlines from before you were born. Half of them are about the refectory soup.|An hour with the old front pages. The castle has always been like this, it turns out.}\n> right: Come back on deadline night\n  say: {~Nothing to write today. You leave the press to its nap.|You tidy a stack of proofs and go.}\n\n@event flight_field_quiet_hour\ntitle: An Empty Field\nplace: flight_field\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 2\nart: leaf\ntags: placeholder\nscene: A long green flying field with nobody on it, hoops at either end, grass flattened by the wind.\ntext: Nobody is flying today. The Flight Field is all grass and wind, the hoops at either end swinging a little on their own. It's a nice place when nobody is crashing into it.\n> left: Lie in the grass a while\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You watch the clouds race the hoops. The clouds win.|The wind does the flying today. You just watch it.}\n> right: Walk on\n  say: {~You cut across the empty field and go on your way.|No lessons, no races. You leave the field to the wind.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/plans_gigs.grim","text":"# Holiday gigs (playtest 2, M6; X15): working on campus over a break, you take one of two gigs open to anyone\n# (the grounds, the kitchens), or, at a term job's top rank, its holiday version (`gig_library`, `gig_newt`,\n# `gig_pell`, `gig_post`), which pays a little better. A gig pays through its cards; board is still paid.\n# Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.\n\n@event holiday_gigs\ntitle: Holiday Work\ntrigger: queued\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A noticeboard by the castle gates in the emptying holidays, two handwritten notices pinned side by side.\ntext: Two notices are still pinned by the gates. {season == \"winter\": The snow has got under one of them.|The sun has curled the corners of both.} Old Maud wants hands for the grounds. The Refectory kitchens want someone who can peel fast and not complain about it. {rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: A third notice has your name on it.|}\n> grounds: Work the grounds for Old Maud\n  hint: Outdoor work, heavy and honest. Good pay; hard on the body.\n  gain gig_grounds\n  say: Maud looks you up and down, hands you a pair of gloves two sizes too big, and says, \"You'll grow into them.\"\n> kitchens: Work the Refectory kitchens\n  hint: Long hours by the ovens. Good pay; hard on the temper.\n  gain gig_kitchens\n  say: The head cook gives you an apron, a knife and a sack of onions, and doesn't ask your name until the sack is empty.\n> library: Run the library's holiday stocktake\n  when: rank(library_assistant) >= 3\n  hint: For senior clerks. Better pay; long days in the stacks.\n  gain gig_library\n  say: The head librarian hands you the stocktake ledger, a lamp and a pair of gauntlets. \"Count everything,\" she says. \"Twice, if it moves.\"\n> newt: Run the Newt through the holiday rush\n  when: rank(tavern) >= 3\n  hint: For the Newt's managers. The best tips of the year; the longest nights.\n  gain gig_newt\n  say: The landlady gives you the keys and goes to visit her sister. \"Don't let the fire go out,\" she says, \"or the village.\"\n> pell: Mix the season's remedies at Pell's\n  when: rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3\n  hint: For assistant apothecaries. Good pay; the village depends on you.\n  gain gig_pell\n  say: Old Pell ties an apron on you himself. \"Half the valley will be coughing by next week,\" he says, delighted. \"We'll be rich.\"\n> post: Fly the holiday post\n  when: rank(broom_courier) >= 3\n  hint: For chief couriers. Every parcel in the valley; the weather against you.\n  gain gig_post\n  say: The loft is stacked to the rafters with parcels in brown paper. Your name is chalked on the board above the busiest route.\n\n# The notices go up in a term's last month, so a player can plan the break (X15).\n@event holiday_notices\ntitle: Notices by the Gate\ntrigger: auto\nslot: afternoon\nwhen: term and (month == 4 or month == 10) and not (final_year and month == 10)\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 30\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: paper\ntags: job money\nscene: A noticeboard by the castle gates near the end of term, fresh notices for holiday work pinned over the old ones, students stopping to read.\ntext: {Holiday work notices are going up by the gates, for the grounds and the kitchens, both paying better than any term job.|The holiday notices again, the same two, in fresher ink.|The notices by the gate. You know them by heart.} {rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: One is addressed to you by name, from your own job.|{Someone has written \"and board!\" under one.|A first-year is copying them out.|The wind is trying to take them.}}\n> left: Pin them up for the cook\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The head cook pays you a coin to pin the new notices straight. Working the break would mean no lie-ins, and real money.|You pin them up, get a coin for it, and read the hours. It's tempting, and tiring just to read.|You pin them up against the wind for the head cook, who pays you a coin and asks, again, whether you'll be staying.}\n> right: Walk past\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {The break is for sleeping, you decide, and walk on. The notices flap behind you.|Not this time. You'll choose when the break comes.|You walk past. You know them by heart, and the break is still for sleeping.}\n\n# ---------------- The grounds (gig_grounds) ----------------\n\n@pool work_routine\nplan: work\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 30\ntags: job\n\n@event gig_grounds_hard_ground\ntitle: Hard Ground\npool: work_routine\nwhen: has(gig_grounds)\ntone: light\nart: leaf\ntags: nature money\nscene: A student and an old woman working a frozen garden bed at dawn, breath steaming, a wheelbarrow between them.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The ground is frozen two fingers deep and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.|The ground is baked hard and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.} \"The soil doesn't take holidays,\" she says. {~She hands you the heavier spade.|The bees watch you from their hives with professional interest.|She is already on her third bed.}\n> left: Dig until it's done\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  vigor +small\n  say: {~By noon your hands have blisters on their blisters. Maud counts your wages into them anyway.|You turn every bed. Maud inspects them, says \"Hm,\" and pays you a little over.|You dig until Maud's third bed is matched by your third. She pays you, and gives you the lighter spade next time.}\n> right: Work at Maud's pace\n  gold +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~Maud works slowly and never stops, and by evening you understand why. Less done, a smaller purse, an easier back.|You keep to her pace and her stories. She pays for the beds you turned, not the ones she did.|You work at her pace, and she still finishes three beds to your one. She pays for yours, and laughs about it.}\n\n@pool work_routine_repeat4\nplan: work\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 30\ntags: job\n\n@event gig_grounds_storm_damage\ntitle: After the Storm\npool: work_routine_repeat4\nwhen: has(gig_grounds)\ntone: tense\nart: tree\ntags: nature money\nscene: A fallen branch across an orchard path after a storm, a student with a saw, an old woman pointing at something higher up.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Last night's storm brought down half a pear tree.|A summer storm in the night has stripped the orchard.} Maud wants it cleared before the stay-behinds go walking. A cracked limb still hangs over the path.\n> left: Climb up and cut it down\n  gold +large\n  challenge feat apprentice+:\n    win:\n      say: {~The limb comes down where you meant it to. Maud pays you extra for not falling.|You lower it on a rope like a professional. Maud pretends not to be impressed.}\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: {~The limb comes down where it wanted to. So do you, more or less. Maud pays you and sends you to the infirmary.|You get the limb and the limb gets you.}\n> right: Rope it off and clear the rest\n  gold +medium\n  say: {~You clear the path and rope off the danger. Maud nods: she'll ask someone with a ladder. The pay is for the path.|Safe and sensible, and a smaller purse for it.}\n\n@event gig_grounds_hive_check\ntitle: Checking the Hives\npool: work_routine_repeat4\nwhen: has(gig_grounds)\ntone: warm\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A student in a veiled hat leaning over an open beehive beside an old woman, who isn't wearing one.\ntext: Maud's hives sit at the end of the orchard and she checks them {season == \"winter\": by ear in winter, knocking and listening|by hand in summer, slow and bare-armed}. Today she wants you to do it. \"Tell them who you are,\" she says. \"They like manners.\"\n> left: Introduce yourself properly\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You tell the bees your name and your house. Only one stings you, on principle. Maud pays you and calls you a natural.|You feel foolish for about a minute, and then you don't. The hive hums back, and one bee makes a point of it.}\n> right: Get it done quickly\n  gold +large\n  chance even:\n    say: {~Quick and quiet. The bees let it pass this once.|You're done before the bees notice. Maud notices.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: {~The bees take your hurry personally. Maud pays you and pulls out the stings with no sympathy at all.|Three stings and a lesson about manners.}\n\n# ---------------- The Refectory kitchens (gig_kitchens) ----------------\n\n@event gig_kitchens_onions\ntitle: The Onion Sack\npool: work_routine\nwhen: has(gig_kitchens)\ntone: light\nart: meal\ntags: money\nscene: A student at a scrubbed kitchen table behind a mountain of onions, eyes streaming, a huge cook stirring a cauldron behind them.\ntext: {The holiday kitchens feed fewer mouths and work no fewer hours.|Back at the long table, back to the sack.|The head cook has started saving the worst sack for you, as a compliment.} {season == \"winter\": The Midwinter stock needs a hundred onions.|The summer stew needs a hundred onions.} Nobody has ever needed fewer.\n> left: Peel the whole sack\n  gold +large\n  mood -small\n  say: {A hundred onions and not one tear left in you. The head cook pays you and adds a heel of fresh bread.|You finish the sack. The head cook counts the onions, then your wages, both correctly.|You peel the worst sack in the kitchen and don't cry once. The head cook calls it a personal best, and pays it.}\n> right: Peel, then help at the stove\n  gold +medium\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: {Half a sack and an hour at the stove. Less pay, but now you know why the soup tastes like home.|The head cook lets you stir. Somehow the stew tastes of your grandmother's. You get paid for the onions.|You peel half the worst sack and stir the rest of the morning. The head cook pays for the onions and teaches you a stew.}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_burnt_pan\ntitle: The Burnt Pan\npool: work_routine_repeat4\nwhen: has(gig_kitchens)\ntone: tense\nart: meal\ntags: money\nscene: A smoking pan on a range, a student holding it at arm's length, a cook turning round with a ladle raised.\ntext: You turn away for a moment and the porridge for the stay-behinds catches. The smell is already halfway to the dining hall. The head cook hasn't noticed. Yet.\n> left: Own up and start again\n  gold +medium\n  mood -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {~The head cook sighs the sigh of thirty years in kitchens and docks you for the oats. You make it again, properly.|\"Everyone burns one,\" says the head cook. \"Once.\" You are paid, minus the oats.}\n> right: Scrape it and serve it\n  gold +large\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance likely:\n    say: {~Nobody notices. The stay-behinds eat it and ask for more. You don't feel great about it.|A little smoky. \"Rustic,\" says someone. You get the full day's pay.}\n  else:\n    mood -medium\n    say: {~A professor sends it back with a note. The head cook reads it aloud, slowly, to the whole kitchen.|The smell gives you away. You're paid in full and in disapproval.}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_feast_night\ntitle: Feast Night\npool: work_routine_repeat4\nwhen: has(gig_kitchens)\ntone: warm\nart: cup\ntags: money social\nscene: A steaming kitchen on a feast night, trays going out in a stream, a student in an apron grinning through the chaos.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": Tonight the castle keeps a small Midwinter table for those who stayed, and the kitchens will cook as if for the whole school.|Tonight the stay-behinds hold a summer supper in the courtyard, and the kitchens will cook as if for the whole school.} At breakfast the head cook chalks up the rota: somebody carries trays, somebody plates.\n> left: Volunteer to carry trays\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  say: {~You carry until your arms forget being arms. The head cook pays you double for the night, in coins and leftovers.|Up and down the stairs until midnight. Somebody toasts the kitchens and you hear it through the door.}\n> right: Ask to plate in the back\n  gold +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You plate by the ovens and listen to the feast through the wall. Smaller pay, a scorched wrist, and the best seat in the house.|Quiet hands in a hot room. The head cook gives you the last slice of pie, which is its own wage.}\n\n@event gig_grounds_goat\ntitle: Bartholomew Escapes\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_grounds)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 26\nart: paw\ntags: job nature money\nscene: A goat standing triumphantly on the roof of a garden shed, a student with a rope below, an old woman laughing behind them.\ntext: {Bartholomew the goat has got out again and is standing on the shed roof, eating the thatch.|Bartholomew is on the shed roof. He has made this a habit.|The goat is on the roof. You don't even ask how any more.} Maud is laughing too hard to help. {The thatch is going fast.|He has a cabbage in his mouth.|He looks at you like a duke.}\n> left: Climb up after him\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: {You get him down after an hour and a fall. Maud pays you double for the entertainment, she says, and a little for the bruises.|Up the ladder, along the ridge, one hand on his horn. He comes down when he's ready, which is later than you are.|You climb up and bow to him. He considers it, then lets himself be led down like a duke leaving a ball. Maud pays you for the bow.}\n> right: Bribe him down with an apple\n  gold +medium\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {One apple, held very still. He considers it for a long time, like a merchant, and comes down. Maud pays for the hour you saved.|He takes the apple and stays where he is. Then, at his own pace, he comes down. Maud pays you for patience.|He takes the apple like tribute and descends. Maud pays you, and says he'll expect two next time.}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_staff_supper\ntitle: The Staff Supper\nplan: work\nwhen: has(gig_kitchens)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 26\nart: meal\ntags: job money\nscene: Cooks and kitchen hands crowded round a scrubbed table late at night, eating the day's leftovers, one student serving.\ntext: {At the morning briefing the head cook announces a staff supper tonight, after the last service. The whole kitchen eats at the scrubbed table, on leftovers, bread and the head cook's opinions.|Staff supper again tonight. They're saving you a place now.|The head cook bangs a ladle at breakfast. \"Staff supper tonight. Sit, or serve.\"} {Somebody is already promising a story about a swan.|The pot-boy is asleep on his feet.|There's a cake cooling that nobody will admit to making.}\n> left: Serve, then wash up\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  say: {You finish the washing-up at midnight. The head cook pays you extra and calls you \"the useful one\".|You serve, wash up, and wake the pot-boy at midnight to send him to bed. The head cook pays you for both jobs.|You serve forty bowls, wash forty bowls, and are paid for the extra hour in coins and a slice of the mystery cake.}\n> right: Sit and eat with them\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: {You eat, and laugh at the swan story, and leave earlier with a smaller purse and a full stomach.|You sit. The cooks tell you which professors send back their soup. It's worth more than the wages.|You sit. Nobody admits to the cake, so everyone eats it. You leave full and a little poorer.}\n\n# ---------------- The holiday versions of term jobs (X15), at their top rank ----------------\n\n@event gig_library_stocktake\ntitle: The Stocktake\npool: work_routine\nwhen: has(gig_library)\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: study money\nscene: A deserted library in the holidays, a student on a tall ladder with a ledger, books stacked on every table, dust in the lamplight.\ntext: {The library in the holidays is all yours and all to be counted.|The stocktake ledger is half full; the shelves are not half counted.|You've stopped counting books and started counting floors.} {A shelf of atlases has migrated to the poetry section.|One book is not on any list and seems pleased about it.|The lamp in the east wing keeps going out, politely.}\n> left: Count the restricted shelves too\n  gold +large\n  lore +small\n  health -small\n  say: {The restricted shelves count back. You finish with the right number and a bite on the thumb. The librarian dresses the bite and writes it in the ledger, under \"damages\".|You count every chained volume. One of them counts you. The pay is better than term-time's by a good margin.|You count the restricted floor by the lamp that keeps going out. The books count you in the dark. The pay is the best of the break.}\n> right: Keep to the open stacks\n  gold +large\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Open stacks only, every book counted, the ledger perfect. The librarian pays you in full and says nothing about the shelves you left.|A long, dusty, honest day. You go to bed counting.|You count the open floors and leave the east wing to its lamp. The librarian pays in full and asks no questions.}\n\n@pool work_routine_repeat4_2\nplan: work\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 24\ntags: job\n\n@event gig_library_lost_shelf\ntitle: The Shelf That Wasn't There\npool: work_routine_repeat4_2\nwhen: has(gig_library)\ntone: eerie\nart: book\ntags: mystery money\nscene: A narrow library aisle that seems to go on further than the building, a student holding a lamp up to an unfamiliar shelf.\ntext: {The stocktake finds a shelf that isn't in any ledger.|The extra shelf again. It has grown a book since yesterday.|The shelf is longer than the room it's in, now.} {The books on it have no titles.|One is warm.|One is labelled with the date of tomorrow.}\n> left: Catalogue it\n  gold +large\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You list every book. The librarian reads your list, goes pale and pays you to forget it. You don't.|You catalogue the shelf by lamplight. In the morning, it's gone, and your list is the only proof.|You catalogue the book labelled with tomorrow's date. Tomorrow, the label has changed to the day after. The librarian pays you and takes your list.}\n> right: Leave it off the ledger\n  gold +medium\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {You don't write it down. Some shelves don't want to be counted. The librarian, passing, nods as if you'd passed a test.|You skip it. The ledger balances. The shelf, you'd swear, looks relieved.|You don't write down the book with tomorrow's date. Tomorrow, it isn't there. The librarian thanks you, oddly sincerely.}\n\n@pool work_routine_repeat3\nplan: work\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 20\ntags: job\n\n@event gig_library_last_day\ntitle: The Ledger Balances\npool: work_routine_repeat3\nwhen: has(gig_library)\ntone: warm\nart: scroll\ntags: money\nscene: A head librarian signing a thick stocktake ledger at a desk under a window, a tired student waiting with ink on every finger.\ntext: {The last page of the stocktake. The librarian reads it slowly, all of it.|Another stocktake done, another ledger signed.|The librarian signs, and blots, and signs again for luck.} {\"Not one missing,\" she says. \"That has never happened.\"|\"Three missing, and one extra. Normal.\"|She closes the ledger with both hands.}\n> left: Ask for a reference\n  gold +large\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {She writes one on the spot, three lines, and the third makes you blush. The pay is good and the reference is better.|She writes you a reference in a hand like engraving. It's worth more than the wages, someone tells you later.|She signs the ledger and then, without being asked, a reference. \"You'll want it,\" she says. You will.}\n> right: Ask for the afternoon off\n  gold +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: {She gives you the afternoon and a little less pay. You spend it asleep in the reading room, which is the best use of it.|An afternoon off, and a library to yourself. You read for pleasure for the first time in months.|She closes the ledger and gives you the afternoon before you ask. You spend it in the window seat, doing nothing whatsoever.}\n\n@event gig_newt_rush\ntitle: The Holiday Rush\npool: work_routine\nwhen: has(gig_newt)\ntone: tense\nart: cup\ntags: money\nscene: A packed village tavern at the holidays, garlands over the bar, every table full, a student manager behind the bar with three jugs in hand.\ntext: {The landlady hands you the keys over breakfast. Tonight, she warns, the Newt will be full to the rafters with farmers, barge crews and families home for the season.|Another holiday night ahead, and the Newt is already promised to a full house.|By morning, half the valley has said it's coming to the Newt tonight.} {A wedding party has sent word.|The fiddler has promised a song with forty verses.|The firewood is stacked to the ceiling.}\n> left: Keep the bar open till the last song\n  gold +large\n  charm +small\n  health -small\n  say: {You pour till the song ends, then pour for the encore. The till is the heaviest you've ever counted, and your feet will never forgive you.|Closing at three, the till heavy, the floor sticky. The landlady's note says \"Well done\" in two places.|Half the valley comes, and you keep pouring until the firewood runs low. The till is heavy, and so is your sleep.}\n> right: Close at the proper hour\n  gold +large\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You call time at midnight, firmly and politely. Some grumble. The till is lighter, and you sleep.|Last orders on the stroke. The till loses some takings; you keep your temper.|You close on the stroke, half the valley or not. They go home singing, which counts as a good night.}\n\n@event gig_newt_brawl\ntitle: A Fight Over a Song\npool: work_routine_repeat4_2\nwhen: has(gig_newt)\ntone: tense\nart: cup\ntags: danger money\nscene: Two barge crews squaring up in the middle of a crowded tavern, a student manager stepping between them with a tray.\ntext: {Two barge crews disagree about the last verse of a song, loudly.|The barge crews again, and the song again, and the same verse.|This time it's a farmer and a fisherman, and the song is a different one.} {A chair is already in the air.|Somebody has taken their coat off.|The fiddler has stopped playing.}\n> left: Step between them\n  challenge feat apprentice+:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {You stop the first punch with a tray and the second with a look. Both crews buy a round to make up. You take the tips.|You walk between them and stay there. Eventually somebody laughs, and the song goes on.|The fiddler stops; you start the next verse yourself, badly. Both sides correct you, together, and forget to fight.}\n    lose:\n      gold +medium\n      health -medium\n      say: {The tray takes one punch and your jaw takes the other. They pay for the chair, at least.|You get a black eye and a free drink, which you can't drink, being on duty.|The fiddler stops, and so does the fight, just as you step in. Everyone laughs at your timing. You take the bruise for nothing.}\n> right: Bribe them with a free round\n  gold +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {One round on the house, and they're singing together by the second verse. The house is a little poorer.|A free round fixes most things. The landlady's ledger will notice.|A free round, and the fiddler starts again. The farmer and the fisherman sing a duet, and the landlady's ledger winces.}\n\n@event gig_newt_fire\ntitle: The Fire Must Not Go Out\npool: work_routine_repeat3\nwhen: has(gig_newt)\ntone: warm\nart: candle\ntags: money\nscene: The great hearth of a village tavern late at night, embers banked low, a student kneeling with tongs and a bucket of ash.\ntext: {The landlady's fire has never gone out in her lifetime or her mother's. She's away tonight, and this morning the wind is already in the chimney.|The landlady is away again tonight, and the fire is already low. The wind is from the north.|The fire flickered last night as you locked up, as if to test you. Tonight it's yours again.} {The wood is damp.|There's frost on the inside of the windows.|The cat is sitting on the warmest stone, unhelpfully.}\n> left: Sit up with it all night\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  say: {You feed it one stick at a time until dawn. When the landlady comes back, she puts a hand on the stones and nods at you without a word.|You sleep in the chair with the tongs in your lap. The fire lives. So, nearly, do you.|You sit up with it, cat on the warmest stone. At dawn it's still burning, and the cat has not moved once.}\n> right: Bank it and trust it\n  gold +large\n  mood -small\n  say: {You bank it the way she showed you and go to bed. You dream of ash. In the morning, it's alive, and you're shaking.|You trust it. It holds. You don't sleep a wink anyway.|You bank it and move the cat. The cat moves back. In the morning the fire's alive, and the cat is very warm.}\n\n@event gig_pell_remedy_rush\ntitle: Cough Season\npool: work_routine\nwhen: has(gig_pell)\ntone: light\nart: bottle\ntags: alchemy money\nscene: A crowded village apothecary in winter, a queue of coughing villagers, a student and an old man filling bottles as fast as they can.\ntext: {The whole valley is coughing, and Pell's queue is out of the door.|Day three of the cough. Pell is humming. You are not.|The queue at Pell's has its own gossip by now.} {A carter wants the cherry one.|A farmer wants something for his cow.|A professor in disguise wants something \"for a friend\".}\n> left: Mix to order, carefully\n  gold +large\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Every bottle mixed for the person in front of you. It's slow, and Pell pays you for every one of them.|You learn sixty coughs by ear. Pell says you're wasted on students.|You mix the disguised professor's remedy \"for a friend\" with great discretion. Pell charges double for the discretion and gives you half.}\n> right: Mix in big batches\n  gold +large\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {One cauldron, one recipe, three hundred bottles. It works for nearly everyone. Pell pays you and looks at the cauldron for a long time.|Fast and efficient. A farmer comes back the next day about the cow.|The professor in disguise takes a bottle from the batch and looks offended to be treated like everyone else.}\n\n@event gig_pell_night_knock\ntitle: A Knock After Closing\npool: work_routine_repeat4_2\nwhen: has(gig_pell)\ntone: tense\nart: bottle\ntags: alchemy money\nscene: An apothecary's door at night, a lantern held up to a frightened villager in the doorway, a student in a nightcap behind the counter.\ntext: {Pell asks you over breakfast to sleep above the shop tonight. There's a fever about, and the knocks come after closing.|Another night above the shop, Pell asks. The knocks keep coming after dark.|You're sleeping above the shop again tonight. You've stopped bothering to unlace your boots.} {Pell sleeps like a stone.|The fire goes out at ten.|The ledger says not to open after dark.}\n> left: Mix any remedy yourself\n  challenge brew adept-:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {The knock comes at midnight. A shepherd, his wife burning with fever. You mix the remedy by candlelight from memory. She's better by morning; he pays in eggs and coins, and Pell pays you for the night.|The remedy is right. Pell, in the morning, checks your work and says nothing, which from him is a medal.|You sleep in your boots and mix the remedy before you're properly awake. It's right. Pell finds the mortar warm in the morning, and nods.}\n    lose:\n      gold +medium\n      mood -small\n      say: {The first batch is wrong. You wake Pell, who fixes it in a minute and pays you anyway, for trying.|You get it right on the third try, at dawn, and you shake all the next day.|You mix it half asleep and half right. The knock comes back an hour later, and Pell comes down.}\n> right: Wake Pell for anything\n  gold +medium\n  say: {When the knock comes, Pell comes down grumbling, mixes it in two minutes and goes back to bed. You learn more watching than mixing.|You wake him. He's not cross. \"That's what I'm for,\" he says.|You wake Pell, who's already pulling his boots on. He's started sleeping in them too.}\n\n@event gig_pell_ledger\ntitle: Pell's Books\npool: work_routine_repeat3\nwhen: has(gig_pell)\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: money\nscene: A cluttered apothecary back room, an old man and a student bent over a ledger full of crossings-out, jars all around them.\ntext: {Pell's account books have not been balanced since his daughters left home.|The account books again. Pell has found another drawer of receipts.|You're nearly at the bottom of the receipts. Pell is nearly at the bottom of his patience.} {Half the debts are owed to him.|Half the debts are owed by him.|Some debts are in a currency that no longer exists.}\n> left: Balance them properly\n  gold +large\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {Three days of receipts. At the end, Pell is owed more than he thought. He pays you out of it, generously.|You balance every page. Pell signs it as if it were a treaty.|You convert the vanished currency at an old rate you find in a drawer. Pell is owed a goat, a few crowns and an apology. He pays you from the crowns.}\n> right: Forgive the small debts\n  gold +medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {You strike out every small debt owed to Pell, everything under a crown. Pell reads the list and nods. \"They'd never have paid,\" he says. \"Now they'll come back.\"|Small debts forgiven, the book is simpler and the village is kinder to Pell. The pay is a little less.|You forgive the debts in the vanished currency, since nobody could pay them anyway. Pell laughs for the first time in a week.}\n\n@event gig_post_storm_run\ntitle: The Storm Run\npool: work_routine\nwhen: has(gig_post)\ntone: tense\nart: feather\ntags: danger money\nscene: A courier on a broom flying low over snowy fields in a storm, a sack of parcels strapped behind them, a farmhouse light ahead.\ntext: {The holiday post doesn't wait for weather. Tonight's route crosses the ridge in a storm.|The ridge again, and the wind again.|You've flown this route so often the farm dogs know your broom.} {The sack is heavier than the broom likes.|One parcel is labelled \"fragile\" in four languages.|The light at the last farm is always on for you.}\n> left: Fly straight over the ridge\n  challenge feat adept wild:\n    win:\n      gold +large\n      vigor +small\n      say: {Over the ridge and down to the farms in half the time. The bonus for early delivery is very good.|The wind does its worst and you do better. Every parcel delivered, dry.|You fly the ridge with the farm dogs barking you home. The light at the last farm is on, and the bonus is too.}\n    lose:\n      gold +medium\n      health -medium\n      say: {The wind takes you into a hedge. The parcels survive. You are less sure about yourself.|You deliver everything, late and frozen, with a sprained wrist.|The storm hides the light at the last farm. You find it by the dogs, an hour late and very wet.}\n> right: Take the valley road\n  gold +large\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The long way round, low and slow. Late, but every parcel's in one piece, and so are you.|The valley road adds two hours and no danger. The pay is the pay.|You take the valley road. The light at the last farm is still on when you get there, late. Somebody waited.}\n\n@event gig_post_wrong_parcel\ntitle: The Wrong Parcel\npool: work_routine_repeat4_2\nwhen: has(gig_post)\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: money\nscene: A farmhouse doorway in the snow, an old woman holding up a parcel that is clearly ticking, a courier on the step looking worried.\ntext: {A parcel you delivered yesterday has come back. The old woman says it isn't hers, and it's ticking.|Another wrong address. The holiday post is chaos.|The labels are getting worse. One just says \"Aunt\".} {The ticking is getting faster.|It smells of oranges.|It's addressed to someone who died years ago.}\n> left: Find the right house tonight\n  gold +large\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You fly the valley till midnight and find the right house. The ticking was a clock, a present for a grandfather. He gives you a slice of cake and a coin.|You find the owner three farms over. The bonus for \"lost and found\" is surprisingly good.|The person it's addressed to died years ago. You find their granddaughter instead, who opens it in the doorway and laughs and cries at once. The bonus is good.}\n> right: Return it to the loft\n  gold +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You take it back to the loft. Someone else will fly it tomorrow, and get the bonus.|The loft-master sighs and puts it on the shelf of the lost. It ticks there all week.|You take it back. The loft-master reads the name, goes quiet, and says he'll deliver this one himself.}\n\n@event gig_post_last_parcel\ntitle: The Last Parcel\npool: work_routine_repeat3\nwhen: has(gig_post)\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: money\nscene: An empty courier loft at the end of a holiday shift, one small parcel left on the shelf, addressed in shaky handwriting.\ntext: {The last parcel of the day is small, and addressed in a shaky hand to a cottage on the far moor.|One parcel left again. Always one.|The last parcel, and the last daylight.} {It's light as a feather.|It's warm.|It rattles like a box of buttons.}\n> left: Fly it out now\n  gold +large\n  health -small\n  say: {The cottage is an hour into the dark. An old man opens the door and holds the parcel as if it were alive. \"From my son,\" he says. The pay feels like the least of it.|You deliver it at dusk to a woman who cries. You don't ask why. You fly home slowly.|It rattles all the way across the moor. Buttons, the woman says at the door, from her mother's coat. She gives you one.}\n> right: Leave it for the morning\n  gold +large\n  mood -small\n  say: {It waits on the shelf all night and you think about it all night. You fly it at dawn.|In the morning you deliver it, a day late. Nobody minds but you.|You leave it till morning, last light being gone. It rattles on the shelf all night, as if impatient.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/plans_more.grim","text":"# More break-plan pool events (castle, home, work) and the \"regular\" milestones.\n# Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.\n\n# ---------------- Break: at the castle (plan: castle) ----------------\n\n@pool castle_routine\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\ntags: home\n\n@event break_castle_holiday_repairs\ntitle: Holiday Repairs\npool: castle_routine\ntone: light\nart: house\nscene: A caretaker with a long list on a clipboard in an empty castle corridor, a student trying to look busy nearby.\ntext: {The caretaker has a list of jobs that can only be done when the castle is empty, and an eye for idle students.|The caretaker's list is out again. Your name is pencilled in at the bottom, provisionally.|The caretaker hands you the list and a mug of tea, in that order.} {season == \"winter\": Today it's snow on the battlements, heavy enough to worry the roof leads.|Today it's the dormitory mattresses, which must be carried out and beaten in the sun.}\n> left: Lend a hand\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {You shovel snow off the battlements until you can't feel your ears. The view is worth one of them.|You and the caretaker clear a whole parapet in silence. At the end you're handed a biscuit, which is how the caretaker says thank you.|You drink the tea, then shovel the battlements till noon. The caretaker ticks you off the list, which feels like a medal.}|{You beat forty mattresses. Two of them contained socks, one a love letter, and one a very surprised mouse.|You carry mattresses down six flights of moving stairs. One flight moves halfway through, with you and the mattress on it.|Tea first, then mattresses until the sun goes down. The caretaker ticks you off the list with a flourish.}}\n> right: Make myself scarce\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: {You remember an urgent appointment with a book and vanish round a corner. The caretaker's sigh follows you a long way.|You hide in a window seat until the list has moved on to someone else.|You drink the tea, hand back the list and disappear before the mug is cold.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: {The caretaker finds you behind a tapestry and hands you a shovel without comment.|You get as far as the stairs, which choose that moment to move you straight back to the caretaker.|You finish the tea and try to slip off. The caretaker taps the list, and you work down it until supper.}\n\n@pool castle_routine_repeat4\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\ntags: home\n\n@event break_castle_flight_field\ntitle: The Field to Yourself\npool: castle_routine_repeat4\ntone: light\nart: broom\nscene: An empty flight field in the holidays, hoops at either end, a single student on a broom high above the grass.\ntext: {With the flying lessons over for the break, the flight field is empty and nobody has locked the broom shed.|The broom shed is unlocked again. You suspect it's on purpose now.|The flight field in the break. You know which broom in the shed pulls left.} {season == \"winter\": The hoops are rimed with frost and the wind has teeth.|The air shimmers with heat, and the wind is lazy for once.} {A crow is using the far hoop as a perch.|Somebody's lost scarf is snagged on the near hoop.|The grass is printed with a fox's tracks.}\n> left: Fly the hoops, fast\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You thread every hoop and pull up so late that the grass brushes your boots. Nobody saw. That's almost a shame.|You lap the field until the hoops blur. Landing, you find you're grinning like a first-year.|You take the broom that pulls left and fly the hoops leaning right. Below you, the fox's tracks blur into one long line.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The wind cheats, as it always does, and you meet the far hoop with your shoulder.|You land in the long grass, which has caught a great many students before you. It does not catch gently.|You forget which broom pulls left until the far hoop reminds you. You land in the fox's tracks, hard.}\n> right: Practise slow, careful turns\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Left, right, left, at walking pace, for an hour. It is deeply dull and, annoyingly, it works.|You practise the turn that always throws you until it doesn't. Your arms ache all evening.|You turn against the broom that pulls left until it gives up and flies straight. You land beside the fox's tracks, pleased with you both.}\n\n@event break_castle_bored_stairs\ntitle: The Bored Staircases\npool: castle_routine\ntone: light\nart: tower\nscene: A grand castle stairwell with several staircases swinging slowly through the air, a lone student waiting on a landing.\ntext: {With nobody to carry up and down, the castle's staircases have grown restless.|The staircases are bored again, and they've noticed you.|The stairs know you're one of the few left. One swings over the moment you reach the landing.} {season == \"winter\": The stairwell is cold enough to see your breath.|Warm air drifts up the stairwell with the smell of cut grass.} {A flight is drifting past with a lost shoe on its top step.|Two staircases are sulking against the wall.|One flight keeps nudging the landing, like a dog with a stick.}\n> left: Ride whichever one comes\n  chance likely:\n    guile +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {It carries you to a corridor you've never seen, full of portraits who are thrilled to have a visitor.|It takes you the long way, through three towers, and sets you down exactly where you wanted to be.|The eager flight carries you off like a dog with a stick, the long way round, and brings you back very pleased with itself. It was a good ride.}\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    mood -small\n    say: {It drops you on a landing in the east wing and refuses to come back. You walk for an hour.|It swings out over the stairwell and stops. You wait there, cramped and cold, with nothing to read, until it gets bored again.|The eager flight carries you off like a dog with a stick and drops you somewhere it thinks you'll like. You don't. It takes an hour to walk back.}\n> right: Take the long way, on foot\n  vigor +small\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You take the servants' stair, all two hundred steps of it. The staircases watch you go, disappointed.|You walk round by the bridges. It takes all morning, and your legs will remember it.|You walk past the eager flight. It follows you the length of the stairwell, nudging the banister, and you don't give in.}\n\n@event break_castle_night_rounds\ntitle: The Lone Prefect\npool: castle_routine_repeat4\ntone: tense\nart: lantern\nscene: A single lantern bobbing along a bridge between dark towers at night, a student watching it from a window.\ntext: {Only one prefect has stayed for the break, and at breakfast they sigh that seven towers are a lot to patrol tonight.|The lone prefect is pinning tonight's rounds to the noticeboard again.|You know the lone prefect's rounds by now, to the quarter-hour, and tonight's are on the noticeboard.} {season == \"winter\": The corridors are cold enough to frost the portraits.|The nights are warm, and every window in the castle stands open after dark.} {A staircase shifts somewhere below with a long stone sigh.|An owl has got into the east wing.|Somewhere a door bangs in a draught.}\n> left: Wander the towers tonight\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You have the castle to yourself for an hour. Moonlit bridges, sleeping portraits, the whole lake laid out below.|You slip past the lantern twice and watch the lake from a window nobody uses. It feels like owning the place.|You know the rounds to the quarter-hour, and you're never where the lantern is. The banging door covers your footsteps all the way to the roof.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {The lantern swings round and finds you. The prefect writes your name in the demerit ledger with visible relief at having something to write.|You walk straight into the prefect on a dark landing. You both scream, and then you get a demerit.|The prefect changed the rounds after pinning them up. The lantern is exactly where you are, and a moment later so is your name, in the ledger.}\n> right: Offer to walk the rounds\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The prefect is glad of the company and tells you which portraits snore. You go to bed very late.|You carry the lantern while the prefect checks doors. Somewhere in the fourth tower you become friends, a little.|You walk the rounds you already know by heart. The prefect is impressed, then suspicious, and you shut the banging door together.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: at home (plan: home) ----------------\n\n@pool home_routine\nplan: home\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\ntags: home\n\n@event break_home_family_recipe\ntitle: The Family Recipe\npool: home_routine\nslot: morning\ntone: warm\nart: cauldron\nscene: A warm family kitchen, an older relative guiding a student's hands over a steaming pot, jars and bowls everywhere.\ntext: {Someone at home has decided you're old enough for the family recipe, and ties an apron on you before breakfast.|The recipe lessons resume. You're trusted with the knife now, if not the salt.|You know the first half of the recipe by heart. The second half is still guarded like a treaty.} {season == \"winter\": It's the stew for the cold months, which takes all day.|It's the summer preserve, and every fruit in the house is involved.} {Nobody measures anything.|A pinch turns out to mean a handful.|The pot is older than you are.}\n> left: Write it all down\n  alchemy +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You take notes like it's a potions practical. You're corrected on every line, and then on the corrections.|Your notes are thorough, exact and, you're told, missing the point.|You write down the first half. When the second half begins, your notebook is gently confiscated.}\n> right: Learn it by hand, the old way\n  mood +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You burn a finger and get a nod. In this kitchen, that's a diploma.|You get it wrong in a way that turns out to be how your grandmother made it. Everyone goes quiet, and then they eat it all.|Your hands learn a step of the second half before anyone means to teach it. Nobody says a word, but you're the one left stirring the old pot.}\n\n@pool home_routine_repeat4\nplan: home\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\ntags: home\n\n@event break_home_own_bed\ntitle: Your Own Bed\npool: home_routine_repeat4\nslot: morning\ntone: warm\nart: bed\nscene: A narrow bed under a cottage window in early light, a student buried in blankets while the house bustles below.\ntext: {Your own bed, after a term in the dormitory. You wake at the academy's hour anyway.|The house wakes at dawn. It always has; you'd forgotten.|You know which floorboard creaks on the way down, and that everyone else learned it first.} {season == \"winter\": There's frost on the window and the blankets weigh a ton.|Sunlight is coming through the curtains, and someone is already whistling in the yard.} Downstairs, {the kettle is going.|someone is calling your name.|the door bangs for the third time.}\n> left: Sleep until noon\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You sleep like a stone. At lunch you're called \"the scholar\" with a new and pointed emphasis.|You wake at noon, rested and ashamed. The whole house has done a morning's work, and mentions it.|You sleep through the door banging a third time. When you finally come down, you miss the creaky floorboard, and nobody hears how late you are.}\n> right: Get up with everyone\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {You break the ice on the water butt and carry wood in before breakfast. Your hands sting all morning.|You're out in the dark with a lantern before the cockerel. The cockerel resents the competition.|You step over the creaking floorboard and are at the woodpile before anyone knows you're up. Your fingers sting till noon.}|{You're in the yard by sunrise, hauling and fetching. Breakfast, when it comes, is the best of the year.|You work the cool hours with everyone else and see why they swear by them.|You step over the creaking floorboard and are in the yard before the door bangs again. By breakfast the work is half done.}}\n\n@event break_home_mending_chair\ntitle: The Mending Chair\npool: home_routine\ntone: light\nart: wand\nscene: A chair by a cottage door piled with broken household things, a student in academy robes eyeing them with a wand.\ntext: {Word goes round the house that the scholar can fix things.|The things that need fixing have been waiting for you on a chair by the door.|The mending chair fills up between breaks, like a well.} {season == \"winter\": Today it's a stove door that won't stay shut.|Today it's a window that sticks in the heat.} {Also a clock that strikes thirteen.|Also a doll with a missing eye.|Also a teapot nobody admits to breaking.}\n> left: Mend it with a spell\n  challenge spell apprentice:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {It mends with a click and a little puff of light. The family applauds, then brings out three more things.|You fix it so well that it works better than new. Now everyone wants their things to work better than new.|The teapot mends without a crack showing. Somebody looks very relieved, and the chair fills up again by supper.}\n    lose:\n      mana -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {It's mended, technically. It also hums now, and nobody can make it stop.|The spell fixes it and then, for good measure, everything else on the chair. Most of it did not need fixing that way.|The teapot mends, and now pours only for whoever broke it. Nobody will pick it up.}\n> right: Mend it by hand\n  vigor +small\n  say: {Glue, string, a borrowed hammer and the whole morning. It holds. You don't trust it, but it holds.|You fix it the way your family always has: slowly, with a lot of opinions from the doorway.|You glue the teapot together crack by crack. Nobody admits to anything, but someone brings you tea in it.}\n\n@event break_home_the_trunk\ntitle: The Trunk in the Corner\npool: home_routine_repeat4\ntone: warm\nart: house\nscene: An open travelling trunk in the corner of a small bedroom, overflowing with jars, socks and parcels in cloth.\ntext: {Your trunk stands open in the corner, and the family keeps putting things in it.|The trunk is open again, and it's filling up again.|The trunk is a family project now.} {season == \"winter\": Woollen socks, a jar of something pickled, a cake wrapped in three cloths.|Dried fruit, a straw hat nobody asked for, a cake wrapped in three cloths.} {final_year: Someone says \"for next term\", then stops.|{The lid won't close.|Someone sits on the lid.|A note is tucked into a sock, not to be read till later.}}\n> left: Take everything\n  health +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {The bargeman charges you double for the weight, but you eat well for a month.|You pay the barge extra for the weight and haul it all back across the lake. Half the corridor smells of pickles until spring.|You take everything. The bargeman charges for the weight, and somewhere in the third cloth you find a note from home.}\n> right: Sneak half of it back\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You smuggle the jars back into the pantry at night. In the morning they're in the trunk again, wrapped tighter.|You leave the cake on a shelf by the door. You feel worse about it than you expected.|You manage to leave one jar behind. In its place, someone has tucked a note.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: working full-time (plan: work) ----------------\n\n@pool work_routine_weight10\nplan: work\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\ntags: job\n\n@event break_work_stocktake\ntitle: Stocktaking\npool: work_routine_weight10\ntone: light\nart: scales\nscene: A student with a ledger and pencil counting rows of stock in a quiet workplace, the owner reading over their shoulder.\ntext: {The quiet days of the break are, says whoever is in charge, the time to count everything.|Stocktaking again. You have opinions about how to count things now.|Stocktaking. You count faster than your boss, and slower than you'd like.} {has(gig_newt): At the Gilded Newt that means every tankard, keg and spoon.|{has(gig_pell): At Pell & Daughters that means a thousand drawers.|{has(gig_post): At the loft that means every parcel, pigeonhole and broom.|{has(gig_library): In the library that means books that won't hold still to be counted.|{has(gig_kitchens): In the kitchens that means every ladle, pot and sack of flour.|{has(gig_grounds): In the sheds that means every trowel, rake and seed packet.|That means everything, twice.}}}}}} The tally comes out one short.\n> left: Track down the missing one\n  challenge research using guile apprentice:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {You find it wedged behind the counter, where it has been since the last stocktake. You're tipped for your thoroughness.|You find the mistake in the ledger itself: someone counted a box as a crate. You're paid a little extra, and nothing else is said.|You count faster than your boss and find it first, under a sack nobody has moved in years. You're tipped, a little grudgingly.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {You search until dusk, on your knees in the dust. It stays missing, and your boss looks at you as if you might have eaten it.|You count everything again and get a different number. Then a third.|You count faster than your boss, and wrong. The missing one stays missing, and now so does another.}\n> right: Write it off and move on\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {\"Breakage,\" you write. A coin is docked from your pay, on principle.|You write it off, and your boss sighs and takes it from your wages.|You write it off before your boss has finished counting. It comes out of your wages all the same.}\n\n@event break_work_complaint\ntitle: The Complaint\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: hand\ntags: job\nscene: An irate customer leaning over a counter, finger raised, while a young worker stands very still behind it.\ntext: {Someone has come back with a complaint and a voice built for it.|Another complaint, another raised voice. You've learned to breathe out first.|This one has been saving the complaint all week. You can tell.} {has(gig_newt): The cider was too warm, or too cold, or too cider.|{has(gig_pell): The tonic did nothing, which is worse, they say, than doing something.|{has(gig_post): The parcel arrived wet, late and somehow smug.|{has(gig_library): The book they want is out, and they want it in.|{has(gig_kitchens): The stew had a button in it, and they want to know whose.|{has(gig_grounds): A visitor's boots are ruined, and the mud on the path is apparently your mud.|The complaint has several parts, all of them loud.}}}}}} Whoever's in charge is out.\n> left: Talk them down\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      charm +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {You agree with everything until there's nothing left to agree with. They leave calm, and they leave a tip.|You listen, nod and offer them a chair. By the end they're telling you about their sister.|You let them say a week's worth of complaint without interrupting once. By the end they're almost apologetic, and they tip.}\n    lose:\n      mood -medium\n      say: {It gets louder, and then it leaves, still loud, to tell everyone in the valley about you.|They demand your boss, then you, then your boss again, and you spend the rest of the day hearing about it.|A week's worth of complaint, and you interrupt it once. That's once too many.}\n> right: Give them what they want\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You hand over a refund out of your wages. They leave. The quiet afterwards is worth a coin.|A free one, on you. They take it without thanks, and the next one is lovely.|You give in before they're halfway through a week's worth of complaint. They seem almost disappointed.}\n\n@event break_work_half_hour\ntitle: Half an Hour Off\npool: work_routine_weight10\ntone: warm\nart: cup\nscene: A tired student in a work apron standing in a back doorway, looking out at the weather with a cup in hand.\ntext: {Half an hour off in the middle of a long holiday shift.|Your half hour again. You know exactly how far it goes now.|Half an hour. You spend the first minute deciding.} {season == \"winter\": Outside, the snow is coming down sideways.|Outside, the sun is bright enough to hurt.} {The back room has one chair and one draught.|A colleague has left half a pie, possibly for sharing.|A clock somewhere strikes the wrong hour.}\n> left: Walk out into the weather\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {You buy roast chestnuts and eat them walking, snow in your collar. You come back soaked and much happier.|You buy a hot pie for the walk to the lake's edge and back. Your nose will not forgive you.|You spend a minute deciding and the rest in the snow, to the end of the lane and back. A clock strikes the wrong hour as you come in.}|{You buy a cold drink and sit in the sun until your neck burns. Worth it.|You buy a lemonade and walk to the water and stand there. Half an hour is longer outside.|You spend a minute deciding and the rest in the sun. A clock strikes the wrong hour, and you nearly believe it.}}\n> right: Nap in the back room\n  health +small\n  say: {You sleep sitting up and wake with the shape of a crate on your cheek.|You doze off and dream of work, which seems unfair. The bell wakes you mid-shift in the dream.|You spend a minute deciding, and wake to a clock striking the wrong hour. You're late, or early. Nobody can say.}\n\n@event break_work_short_wages\ntitle: A Coin Short\npool: work_routine_weight10\ntone: tense\nart: coin\ntags: money\nscene: A student counting a small pile of coins on a counter for the second time, frowning, an employer pretending not to watch.\ntext: {Wages, counted out into your hand. You count them again.|Wage day. You count your coins at the counter, in front of everyone, on principle.|Wage day. Your boss counts slowly, and so do you.} {It's short by one.|It's short, and there's a button where a coin should be.|It's short by just enough to notice.} {has(gig_newt): The landlady remembers every tab to the penny, and has somehow forgotten this one.|{has(gig_pell): Old Pell counted it himself, drop by drop.|{has(gig_post): The dispatch ledger says otherwise, in smudged ink.|{has(gig_library): The pay envelope says otherwise, in very small writing.|{has(gig_kitchens): The head cook's tally is written in gravy.|{has(gig_grounds): Maud's pay book says otherwise, in pencil.|Someone's sums say otherwise.}}}}}}\n> left: Point it out\n  challenge negotiate apprentice:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      charm +tiny\n      say: {You show your sums. Your boss checks them, coughs, and pays you the difference with a little extra for the embarrassment.|You point it out politely and firmly. The missing coin appears, with an apology and a pastry.|You count as slowly as your boss did, aloud, coin by coin. The difference is paid before you reach the end.}\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {Your boss recounts, gets the same wrong answer, and looks hurt that you asked.|You're told it's for the breakage. You don't remember any breakage. Nobody else does either.|You count aloud, slowly. Your boss counts back, slower, and reaches a different number. You give up before supper.}\n> right: Let it go this once\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {You pocket it without a word. Your boss sees you notice, and you're given the easy shift next week.|One coin. You decide you can afford to be the bigger person, just about.|Short by just enough to notice, and you notice, and say nothing. Your boss counts faster next time.}\n\n# ---------------- Regulars (one-time milestones at public places) ----------------\n\n@event refectory_cooks_taster\ntitle: The Cook's Taster\nplace: refectory\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(refectory) >= 6\nart: meal\ntags: life\nscene: A refectory cook holding out a small spoon over the counter to a student, a new pot steaming behind her.\ntext: The cook stops you before you reach the bench and holds out a spoon. \"You're in here more than the cats,\" she says. \"So you can taste the new pot first. Regulars do.\" Behind her a dozen steaming pots wait their turn, and the other students in the queue look on with naked envy.\n> left: Give an honest verdict\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You say it wants salt and less of the grey thing. The cook frowns, tastes, and nods. From then on your bowl comes from the bottom of the pot, where the good bits are.\n> right: Praise it to the rafters\n  mood +small\n  say: You declare it the best soup in the kingdom. The cook doesn't believe a word of it, and gives you a second spoonful anyway.\n\n@event library_footnote_bag\ntitle: Footnote's Approval\nplace: library\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(library) >= 6\nart: cat\ntags: life\nscene: A huge old library cat curled asleep on a student's satchel under a reading desk, a student standing over it, uncertain.\ntext: You come back from the shelves to find Footnote asleep on your bag, curled tight, one paw over the strap. Footnote sleeps on the reference desk, as a rule, and almost never on anyone's belongings; everybody knows that. A passing librarian stops, looks from the cat to you, and nods, as if something official has happened.\n> left: Wait for him to wake\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You read standing up until the lamps are lit. Footnote wakes, stretches, and walks off without a glance. Your bag smells of warm cat for a week, and you find you don't mind.\n> right: Ease the bag out, inch by inch\n  guile +small\n  say: You slide it free a fraction at a time. Footnote opens one eye, judges you, and allows it. The librarian says she's never seen him allow anything.\n\n@event mabels_your_usual\ntitle: Your Usual\nplace: mabels\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(mabels) >= 6\nart: cup\ntags: life\nscene: A small tea shop counter with a cup already poured and a slice of cake on a plate, the shopkeeper smiling at a student in the doorway.\ntext: You haven't even reached the counter. Mabel already has your cup out, the right tea steeping and the cake you always end up choosing on a plate. \"Your usual,\" she says, and it isn't a question. On the slate behind the till, among the regulars, there's a new line in chalk.\n> left: Have the usual\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: It's exactly right, as it always is. Mabel watches you take the first sip and turns back to the shelves, satisfied.\n> right: Ask for something new\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Mabel's eyebrows rise. Then she smiles like someone handed a puzzle and reaches for a tin from the very top shelf. It's odd and smoky, and you don't like it, and you're glad you asked.\n\n@event bakery_bun_set_aside\ntitle: The Bun Set Aside\nplace: village\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(village) >= 6\nart: meal\ntags: life\nscene: A baker's son handing a paper-wrapped bun across a flour-dusted counter, a letter written on the paper in flour.\ntext: The baker's son reaches under the counter before you've said a word and brings out a bun wrapped in paper, with your initial on it in flour. \"Saved from the first batch,\" he says. \"Regulars get the corner ones.\" Behind him, by the ovens, the oldest baker nods as if she arranged it all herself.\n> left: Insist on paying for it\n  gold -tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: He refuses twice and accepts the third time. The coin goes in a jar by the till marked, in the oldest baker's hand, \"for the post\".\n> right: Thank him and eat it warm\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: It's still warm and far too big, and you eat it on the step in four bites and burn your mouth. You'd do it again tomorrow.\n\n@event grimsbys_right_book\ntitle: The Right Book, First Time\nplace: grimsbys_books\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(grimsbys_books) >= 6\nart: book\ntags: life\nscene: A cramped bookshop of leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller holding out a book to a student who has only just stepped inside.\ntext: You step into Grimsby's and open your mouth, and Mr Grimsby is already holding out a book. It is exactly the one you came for. He looks as astonished as you are. \"Must be getting to know you,\" he says, and then, worried, \"Don't tell anyone.\"\n> left: Buy it before it changes\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You pay at once, in case it turns into a book on beekeeping. It doesn't. You carry it all the way back to the castle, checking.\n> right: Ask what else he'd pick for me\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Restored to form, Mr Grimsby brings you a history of eels and a book of riddles, and lets you read the riddles in the window seat until closing. They're very good, and no help at all.\n\n@event courtyard_in_the_know\ntitle: In the Know\nplace: courtyard\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nweight: 30\nwhen: visits_to(courtyard) >= 6\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: A knot of students gathered on the rim of a courtyard fountain, leaning in, one waving a newcomer over.\ntext: The knot of students by the fountain goes quiet as you pass, and then one of them waves you over. \"You're out here every day,\" says a second-year from Duskgate. \"So. What have you heard?\" It seems you've been counted among the courtyard's regulars, and regulars are expected to bring news.\n> left: Share something harmless\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You offer up a rumour about the refectory pudding. It's received with grave interest and is all over the castle by supper, much improved.\n> right: Say I've heard nothing\n  guile +small\n  say: They take your silence as proof that you know everything. By the end of the week, people are asking you for the news.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/plans_thesis.grim","text":"# Baseline pools (phase 3, step 1b): break plans, thesis and internship hosts.\n# Repeatable routine events; see docs/authoring/pools.md.\n\n# ---------------- Break: at the castle (plan: castle) ----------------\n\n@event break_castle_one_fire\ntitle: The One Lit Fire\npool: castle_routine_repeat4\ntone: warm\nart: flame\nscene: A cavernous common room in the holidays, a handful of students in blankets crowded around {season == \"winter\": one roaring hearth|one open window}.\ntext: {With the houses empty, the stewards light one common room and let the rest go dark.|Someone has brought a kettle, and now there's a rota for it.|The break common room. You know everyone's blanket by now.} {season == \"winter\": The best armchair is right by the fire.|The best armchair is by the only breeze.} {A first-year from Stillwater is asleep in it.|Footnote is in it. Footnote moves for no one.|The steward's dog is in it, and the steward is on the dog's side.}\n> left: Talk my way into the armchair\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      guile +tiny\n      say: {You mention that the kitchens are giving out free toffee. The chair empties. You feel only slightly bad.|You announce, with great authority, that the armchair is due to be re-stuffed. It is, now, by you.|You offer the dog your toast. It's a fair trade, and the chair is still warm.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {Nobody moves. Someone says, \"Nice try,\" without looking up from their book.|The whole room watches you fail to dislodge the armchair's occupant. It's the most entertainment anyone has had all break.|The dog growls, the steward sides with the dog, and you take the floor.}\n> right: Take the draughty corner\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {The corner is cold enough to see your breath, but you read three chapters.|You read in gloves. Turning pages becomes a skill.|You find a blanket nobody claims and read until your nose goes numb.}|{It smells of old ink and warm dust. You read three chapters.|A wasp shares your corner. You reach an understanding.|The breeze turns your pages for you, mostly the right way. You read three chapters anyway.}}\n\n@event break_castle_kitchen_rota\ntitle: The Holiday Kitchen\npool: castle_routine\ntone: light\nart: meal\nscene: A steaming castle kitchen with half its ovens cold, the cook handing aprons to two reluctant students.\ntext: {Half the kitchen staff have gone home for the break, and the cook has started drafting whoever wanders past.|The cook spots you in the corridor and holds up an apron.|The cook holds out an apron without a word. It has your name stitched on it now.} {season == \"winter\": There are puddings to steam for the few who stayed, and a goose that refuses to fit the oven.|The orchard has dropped everything at once, and every pan in the kitchen is full of jam.}\n> left: Roll up your sleeves\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {season == \"winter\": {You stir puddings till your arm aches. \"Just like potion work,\" says the cook. It isn't.|You baste the goose. The goose wins, narrowly.|The goose fits at last. The cook says it was her idea.}|{A morning of jam teaches you more about setting points than a term of alchemy.|You label forty jars. The cook re-spells \"gooseberry\" on all of them.|You stone plums all morning. Your fingers are purple until Tuesday.}}\n> right: Discover urgent business elsewhere\n  guile +tiny\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {You escape to the village and buy a pie you could have had for free. The cook sees you come back with the crumbs.|You hide in the village bakery until lunch is over and buy what you could have eaten for free. The cook gives you a meaningful look all evening.|You claim a library emergency. The cook, who knows the library is shut, says nothing and saves you nothing.}\n\n@event break_castle_empty_library\ntitle: The Library on Holiday\npool: castle_routine_repeat4\ntone: warm\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A great library with no one at the desks, a cat asleep on the reference desk and a single student choosing a book.\ntext: {The librarians have gone home, and the library has been left to you and the cat.|Footnote has moved from the reference desk to your chair, and opens one eye.|Your break-morning library. One window seat is yours now.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has drawn ferns on the tall windows.|Dust turns slowly in the long summer light.} {Every desk is free.|Someone has left half an essay on the theory of hiccups.|The reshelving cart is full, and nobody is coming for it.}\n> left: Read whatever I like\n  mood +small\n  lore +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You read a book about the history of spoons and forget to eat lunch. It was a very good book about spoons.|You read until the light goes, then a little after. Your eyes will mention it tomorrow.|You finish the book you started last break. The bookmark was still where you left it.}\n> right: Get ahead on next term\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {final_year: You make notes for a term you'll only half get to use. It still feels like the right thing to do.|{You make a neat set of notes for next term. Future you will be grateful. Present you is bored stiff.|You read ahead three chapters and find the professor's favourite mistake waiting in the fourth.|You copy out next term's reading list and get through half of it. The other half looks smug.}}\n\n@event break_castle_post_day\ntitle: Post for the Stay-Behinds\npool: castle_routine\ntone: warm\nart: letter\nscene: A porter sorting a thin pile of letters on a long empty table, a few students waiting hopefully.\ntext: {Post day. The porter lays the letters out on one table in the Great Hall.|The porter now sorts the stay-behinds' letters into a pile of their own.|The porter nods to you. He keeps your letters on top now.} {season == \"winter\": {Most of it is Midwinter cards for people who went home.|One parcel is exactly the shape of a scarf.|One envelope has your name on it, in handwriting you know.}|{Most of it is postcards from people's travels.|A postcard says \"Wish you were here\", and nobody knows whose it is.|One envelope has your name on it and smells of the sea.}}\n> left: Write a long letter home\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {final_year: You write that this is your last break at the castle, and then look at the sentence for a long time.|{You write four pages and pay for the heavy stamp. It's the closest thing to a conversation you've had all week.|You describe the empty castle so well that you feel lonelier writing it than living it.|You write about the porter, the biscuit and the empty hall. It reads like a good life, which it is.}}\n> right: Help the porter sort it all\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The porter tells you who writes to whom, in strict confidence. You know far too much about the second-years now.|You sort the post into houses. There's nothing for you this time. The porter gives you a biscuit, which is nearly as good.|You sort the pile in half the time it took you once. The porter calls you his deputy, and nobody argues.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: at home (plan: home) ----------------\n\n@event break_home_kitchen_table\ntitle: Homework at the Kitchen Table\npool: home_routine_repeat4\nslot: morning\ntone: warm\nart: house\ntags: study\nscene: A family kitchen in the morning, a student's books spread among the breakfast plates, someone reaching across them for the butter.\ntext: {You spread your books across the kitchen table, where you did your sums as a child.|Your books have been moved for the bread, and one is being used as a trivet.|The family works around your books now. Mostly.} {season == \"winter\": The stove is the only warm place, so everyone is in the kitchen.|The door is open, and everyone passing asks what the book is about.} {A cousin has drawn a dragon in your margin.|The kettle boils every quarter-hour, on principle.|Someone has put a book of yours under a wobbly table leg, and it works.}\n> left: Study through the noise\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You read the same paragraph nine times and learn it perfectly, along with the price of eggs.|You get through a chapter. Your notes now have a jam stain, a crumb and the shopping list on them.|You learn to read with one hand and pass the salt with the other. A whole chapter goes in.}\n> right: Give up and help with lunch\n  mood +small\n  say: {You're sent to the shop for the one thing everyone forgot, and you buy sweets with the change.|You peel, chop and get told about everyone in the village. Your book stays shut, and it's the best morning of the break.|You're on potatoes again. You're told you peel like a scholar, which isn't a compliment.}\n\n@event break_home_the_box\ntitle: The Box Under the Bed\npool: home_routine\ntone: warm\nart: key\nscene: A narrow childhood bedroom with a bed far too short for its occupant, an old box of keepsakes pulled out onto the floor.\ntext: {Your old room is smaller than you remember. Under the bed is the box where you kept everything that mattered when you were nine.|Someone has dusted the box since last time and pretended not to look inside.|You've almost reached the bottom of the box.} {This time it's a wand you made from a stick and a lot of string.|This time it's the letter you wrote to the academy at age ten, asking to be let in early.|This time it's a drawing of seven towers, each with a little flag.}\n> left: Pack it in your trunk\n  mood +small\n  say: {It comes back to Lumenhold with you. The trunk is heavier, and so, somehow, are you.|You pack it between your robes. Every time you open the trunk this term, it will be the first thing you see.|You pack it carefully. There's not much left in the box now, and that feels like something ending.}\n> right: Give it to your cousin\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {Your cousin, who is applying to the academy next autumn, takes it with enormous seriousness. You feel very old.|Your cousin promises to keep it safe. By supper it has been traded to a neighbour for a jar of honey.|Your cousin says thank you and puts it in a box of their own, under their own bed. You feel very old and quite happy.}\n\n@event break_home_family_debate\ntitle: The Family Debate\npool: home_routine\nslot: morning\ntone: light\nart: meal\nscene: A breakfast table mid-argument, several relatives pointing at a student as if they were the deciding vote.\ntext: {At breakfast the family is arguing, and someone says, \"Ask the scholar.\"|The argument has started without you. Everyone turns as you come down the stairs.|\"The scholar is up,\" someone announces. They've saved today's argument for you.} {season == \"winter\": {Is it colder than the year of the big snow?|Can you freeze a pond by thinking at it?|Can a wizard make the hens lay in winter?}|{Do swallows sleep at the bottom of rivers?|Will it rain on the fair, and can you stop it?|Is the old well really haunted, and should it be?}}\n> left: Settle it with what I've learned\n  challenge research apprentice likely:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You explain it so clearly that the argument ends. There is a short, respectful silence, and then a new argument starts.|You settle it. An uncle demands to see it in writing. You write it on a napkin, and he keeps the napkin.|You settle it with a diagram on the back of the almanac. It's still pinned by the stove the next time you're home.}\n    lose:\n      lore +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {You explain it perfectly, and they all decide the academy has taught you nonsense.|Halfway through you realise you're wrong. So does everyone else.|You're right, and you prove it, and nobody believes you, because the almanac says otherwise.}\n> right: Refuse to take sides\n  charm +small\n  say: {You butter your toast with great care and say, \"Hm.\" It does not stop anyone, but it does save you.|You change the subject to the neighbour's fence. It works, but now there are two arguments.|You say both sides have a point. Both sides turn on you. It's the most united the family has been all year.}\n\n@event break_home_village_children\ntitle: The Wizard Is Home\npool: home_routine_repeat4\nslot: morning\ntone: light\nart: sparkle\nscene: A gaggle of villagers at a cottage gate in the morning, staring hopefully at a student in academy robes.\ntext: {Word has spread that you're home from the wizard school. Half the village has found a reason to pass the gate.|The neighbours are back at the gate, with a cousin from the next village to show you off to.|The gate committee is in session. They've drawn up a list.} {season == \"winter\": They want more snow for the sledging.|They want it to stop being so hot.} {The baker has brought a jar, in case you make something worth keeping.|The miller's wife asks if you've ever met a dragon.|The blacksmith has a stick that might be a wand.}\n> left: Show them some sparks\n  mana -small\n  mood +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: {You make a little burst of light over the gate. The neighbours cheer and demand it again, and again.|You make a spark dance across the gatepost. The baker tries to catch it in her apron, or thinks she does, which is the same thing.|You work through the committee's list of sparks, in order. They tick each one off and ask for the list again tomorrow.}\n> right: Teach them something real\n  charm +small\n  say: {You explain how the light gets into a spell. They listen politely and then ask for sparks.|You show them how to sit still and listen to a candle. The old miller really hears it. You can tell.|You make the blacksmith's stick glow for a minute. It isn't a wand, but he'll never believe that now.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: working full-time (plan: work) ----------------\n\n@event break_work_holiday_rush\ntitle: The Holiday Rush\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A holiday rush at a busy workplace, a student in the thick of it wearing the fixed smile of someone on their fourth hour of the same question.\ntext: {The holidays bring the crowds.|This time you've learned to eat breakfast before the rush, not during it.|You can feel the rush coming an hour before it arrives.} {has(gig_newt): The Gilded Newt is packed to the beams.|{has(gig_pell): The queue at Pell & Daughters is out of the door.|{has(gig_post): The loft's shelves are stacked to the rafters with parcels.|{has(gig_library): The library is full of last-minute readers.|{has(gig_kitchens): The Refectory is feeding everyone who stayed, and all their visiting families.|{has(gig_grounds): The grounds are full of visitors walking off their holiday dinners, and every one of them has a question.|Everyone wants something, and now.}}}}}} {season == \"winter\": {Everyone wants it before Midwinter.|Wet boots, short tempers.|Everyone is in a hurry to be somewhere warm.}|{Everyone is sunburnt.|Travellers keep asking the way.|The heat slows everyone down except the queue.}}\n> left: Smile through it\n  charm +small\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You smile until your face aches. Somebody tips you a coin and a peppermint.|You smile through the fourth complaint about the weather, as if it were your fault. Somehow the tips are better than ever.|You have a smile for every complaint now, sorted by type. The tips follow.}\n> right: Take the heavy jobs instead\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You haul the heaviest loads all morning and talk to no one. Your arms complain, and nobody else does.|You take the dull, heavy work nobody wants. It's tiring and blissfully quiet.|You know where every heavy thing goes by now. You're done by noon, and nobody has spoken to you. Perfect.}\n\n@event break_work_opening_up\ntitle: Opening Up\npool: work_routine_weight10\nwhen: has(gig_newt) or has(gig_pell)\ntone: light\nart: key\nscene: A student unlocking a shop door in the half-light of early morning, breath steaming, the street empty behind them.\ntext: {In the holidays you're trusted with the keys. You open up at dawn, alone.|You've learned to lift the door as you turn the key.|You could open up in your sleep, and once you nearly did.} {season == \"winter\": It's still dark, and the stove has gone out.|The sun is up and the street smells of warm stone.} {There's a note under the door from someone who wanted to come in at midnight.|A cat waits on the step as if it has an appointment.|The place is yours for half an hour.}\n> left: Do it all by the book\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {Swept, stocked and opened to the minute. The owner notices and slips you a little extra.|Everything is ready when the first customer arrives. Nobody notices but you. Still, the coin is good.|You open up to the minute, as always. The owner has stopped checking.}\n> right: Steal a quiet half hour\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    say: {You drink tea by the window and watch the street wake up. It's the best part of the job.|You read a chapter with your feet up. Nobody ever knows.|There's a chair by the window that's yours now. Half an hour of nothing, and nobody the wiser.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    gold -small\n    say: {The owner arrives early and finds you with your feet up. The half hour comes out of your wages.|The first customer arrives early and finds the door locked. They leave a note. So does the owner.|The owner has taken to arriving early just to check, and today it pays off. The half hour comes out of your wages.}\n\n@event break_work_regular\ntitle: The Holiday Regular\npool: work_routine_weight10\nwhen: has(gig_newt) or has(gig_pell)\ntone: warm\nart: cup\nscene: An elderly customer settling in for a long chat at a counter, a student behind it glancing at the queue.\ntext: {Every holiday has its regular. This one comes every morning and wants to talk.|The regular is back. They wave to you as if you were old friends.|The regular has started calling you by your first name.} {Today it's about their late husband's beard.|Today it's about a dragon they swear they saw as a girl.|Today it's about the year the lake froze, which may or may not have happened.} The queue behind them is getting longer.\n> left: Let them finish the story\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You hear the whole story, standing, while the queue thins out to somewhere else. Your feet ache by the end. The regular leaves happy, and so, oddly, are you.|The story has an ending you didn't expect, and you'll think about it all day. You stood through all of it, and your back says so.|You know how their stories end by now, and you let them tell it anyway. The queue waits. Your feet don't forgive you.}\n> right: Hurry them gently along\n  gold +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You serve the queue and the regular leaves, mid-sentence, with a little wave. It shouldn't bother you as much as it does.|The queue is grateful. The regular says, \"Another time, then,\" and you both know they mean it.|You keep the queue moving and promise the regular the rest tomorrow. They hold you to it, every time.}\n\n@event break_work_new_hire\ntitle: The Holiday Hire\npool: work_routine_weight10\nwhen: not has(gig_grounds) and not has(gig_post)\ntone: light\nart: hand\nscene: A nervous new worker holding a tray at the wrong angle, a student leaning over to correct them.\ntext: {The boss has taken on a holiday hire and put them next to you.|Another holiday hire, another nervous face. You're the old hand now.|This break's new hire looks at you the way you once looked at everyone.} {They've already dropped something.|They ask whether the job is always like this. You don't answer.|They're very keen and very fast, and very nearly right.}\n> left: Show them the ropes\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {By noon they know where everything is and that the boss is all bark. You've lost a morning and gained a friend.|You teach them the trick with the till. They teach you a song. You both get told off for the song.|You show them everything you wish someone had shown you. By the end of the day they're showing the next one.}\n> right: Let them learn the hard way\n  mood +tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {They learn. Unfortunately, they learn by breaking a jar that the boss puts on your account.|You let them flounder. It takes them all day to find the stockroom, and you end up covering their shift anyway. The boss takes the stock they spoiled out of your pay.|They learn fast, mostly from your mistakes, which they found in the ledger. The boss bills you for the latest one.}\n\n# ---------------- Break: internship (plan: internship) ----------------\n\n@pool internship_routine_repeat5\nplan: internship\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event break_internship_lodgings\ntitle: Breakfast at the Lodgings\npool: internship_routine_repeat5\nslot: morning\ntone: warm\nart: bed\ntags: home\nscene: A borrowed bed at dawn, boots by the door, a bowl of breakfast waiting and a notebook open on the blanket.\ntext: {{member(intern_expedition): Your lodging for the placement is a tent that flaps all night.|{member(intern_field_station): Your lodging is a bunk in the station cabin, under a shelf of jars.|Your lodging is one small room and a breakfast you pay for.}}|By now you can make any lodging home in an evening, a book on the sill and your boots by the door.|You've slept in enough borrowed beds to know breakfast matters more than the mattress.} {season == \"winter\": {Your breath steams.|The water jug has frozen.|Ice on the inside of the window.}|{It's warm before sunrise.|A bird is shouting outside.|The window is open, and someone's breakfast is frying.}} {Breakfast is porridge.|Breakfast is porridge with opinions.|Breakfast is unnamed.}\n> left: Eat a proper breakfast\n  mood +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {You eat slowly and listen to everyone's gossip. You arrive late enough to be noticed.|Fed, warm and five minutes late. Your supervisor looks at the clock and says nothing at all.|You eat everything and ask for the recipe. You're late, and your supervisor has started to expect it.}\n> right: Skip it and go in early\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You're the first one in. You get through a pile of work before anyone else arrives, and your stomach growls through every meeting after.|You read the day's notes on the way. By lunchtime you're faint and well informed.|You go in early with an apple in your pocket. It's gone by the first meeting, and the hunger stays until supper.}\n\n@event break_internship_briefing\ntitle: The Morning Briefing\npool: internship_routine_repeat5\nslot: morning\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A supervisor reading a list of tasks aloud to a row of interns, one of whom has stopped writing.\ntext: {Your supervisor reads the day's tasks aloud, once, very fast.|You bring two pens now, and a spare for the intern beside you.|You've learned to read the briefing from the way your supervisor holds the list.} {One task makes no sense at all.|One task seems to mean the opposite of what it says.|The last task is mumbled into a cup of tea.} {final_year: It's your last placement, and you want it to count.|The others all write as if they understood.}\n> left: Ask what it means\n  charm +tiny\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Your supervisor explains it again, slowly, while the other interns smirk. Two of them come to you at lunch to ask what it meant.|\"Good question,\" says your supervisor, in a tone that suggests it wasn't. You get the task right, anyway.|You ask before anyone else can pretend. Your supervisor answers, and three pens around you start writing.}\n> right: Guess and get on with it\n  challenge research apprentice even:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You guess right. Your supervisor nods as if they never doubted you.|It turns out the odd task was \"take the rest of the morning off\". Lucky. You spend it reading in the archive.|You guess, and you've guessed this one before. The task is done before anyone else has finished asking.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {You guess wrong and spend the morning carefully doing the opposite of what was wanted.|You did the right task to the wrong thing. The wrong thing will never be the same again.|Your guess is confident, thorough and wrong. Your supervisor reads the result twice and asks you, kindly, to ask next time.}\n\n@event break_internship_other_interns\ntitle: The Other Interns\npool: internship_routine_repeat5\nslot: evening\ntone: light\nart: group\ntags: study\nscene: A small group of interns eating supper together, one sitting slightly apart with a notebook.\ntext: {There are other interns here, and they've already formed a supper table.|You've learned to find the supper table on the first day, and which seat isn't anyone's.|By now you can tell within a morning which intern is the one to sit beside.} {One has a cousin on the Conclave and mentions it hourly.|One is better than all of you, and very nice about it.|One has been here before and knows where the biscuits are.} {season == \"winter\": Supper is eaten in coats.|Supper is eaten in the last of the sun.}\n> left: Join them for supper\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {You chip in for the shared food and find out which of the supervisors are kind. Useful, and fun.|You talk shop until your supper is cold. It turns out everyone else is as lost as you are, which helps.|You sit beside the one who knows where the biscuits are. By pudding you know where everything else is too.}\n> right: Eat alone with your notes\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You finish the reading for tomorrow. You hear laughter from the table, and you don't look up.|You get ahead of everyone. Nobody saves you a biscuit.|You eat with your notes again. Someone leaves a biscuit by your elbow without a word.}\n\n@event break_internship_stipend\ntitle: Stipend Day\npool: internship_routine\ntone: warm\nart: pouch\ntags: money\nscene: A small envelope of coins on a desk, a student looking at it with the expression of someone doing sums.\ntext: {Stipend day. A small envelope, your name spelled almost right.|You've learned to budget the envelope before you open it.|Stipend day. You know what it's for before it arrives, and it still feels like a gift.} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {The supply cart has come, with a pedlar riding on top.|A pedlar has walked a very long way to sell you socks.|The pedlar is back, and has heard about you.}|{season == \"winter\": {There's a warm scarf in a shop window you pass.|A bookshop nearby has a sale on almanacs.|The baker on the corner has started putting one aside on stipend day.}|{A cart is selling lemon ices on the corner.|A bookshop nearby has put its sale out in the sun.|The baker on the corner sells the last of the cherry buns at noon.}}}\n> left: Treat yourself\n  mood +small\n  gold -small\n  say: {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {You buy the pedlar's thickest socks. Your feet have never been so grateful.|You buy a jar of honey from the pedlar and eat it with a spoon, in triumph.|You buy the pedlar's whole stock of liquorice. He says you're his best customer, and you probably are.}|{season == \"winter\": {You buy it. It's quite unnecessary, and the warmest you've felt all week.|You spend the lot before lunch and regret none of it.|You spend it on something warm and something sweet, and keep a single coin back for luck.}|{You spend it before the corner and don't regret a crown.|You take your purchase to a bench in the sun and stay until your nose burns.|You buy the last cherry bun and a book you don't need, and eat one while reading the other.}}}\n> right: Save it and borrow the book\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You put the envelope away and borrow the book instead. It's the dullest thing you've ever read, and the most useful. You copy out half of it the first night.|The envelope goes under your mattress. You read the borrowed book, and the next time your supervisor mentions it you nod in the right places.|The envelope joins the others under the mattress. The pile is getting interesting, and so is the book.}\n\n# ---------------- Thesis (track: thesis) ----------------\n\n@event thesis_margin_notes\ntitle: Notes in the Margin\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A thesis draft returned with a professor's spiky handwriting crawling up every margin.\ntext: {Professor Thorne returns your chapter. In the margin of page one he has written \"No.\"|Another chapter back. The margins say \"Why?\", \"Source?\" and, once, \"Hm.\"|Thorne's notes are getting shorter. You can't decide whether that's good.} {Page six has a single word underlined three times.|One note just says \"See me.\"|There's a tick on page nine. You stare at it for a while.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: The argument is nearly there, and he knows it.|There's a long way to go.}\n> left: Rewrite to his notes\n  progress thesis +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You rewrite every page he marked. The chapter is shorter and better, and you hate that he was right.|You take every note. When he reads it again he crosses out only one thing, and you could cry.|You rewrite to his notes without arguing now. It saves a week, and he's usually right, which still annoys you.}\n> right: Defend my version\n  challenge negotiate using lore vs thorne.lore -2:\n    win:\n      progress thesis +small\n      rel thorne +tiny\n      say: {You make your case at his office door. He listens, says \"Hm,\" and crosses out his own note.|You argue for your paragraph and he lets you keep it. \"Then make it say so,\" he says, which is almost praise.|You defend the paragraph with a source he hasn't read. He reads it overnight and gives you the paragraph.}\n    lose:\n      progress thesis -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {You argue. Thorne waits until you've finished, then points at the sentence that proves him right.|He hears you out, then writes a new note in the margin. It says \"No.\" again, in smaller letters.|You argue. He agrees with every point and still writes \"No.\" You've begun to find this comforting.}\n\n@event thesis_carrel_neighbour\ntitle: The Next Carrel\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: Two thesis carrels side by side, the partition between them covered in pinned notes and a shared candle.\ntext: {Your carrel neighbour, a final-year from Rootward, reads aloud under their breath.|Your carrel neighbour now reads your chapter titles aloud too.|You and your carrel neighbour now speak a language of sighs.} {They're stuck on chapter three.|They've found a source that disagrees with you both.|They pass you half a sandwich over the partition.} {season == \"winter\": The thesis lab is cold enough for gloves.|Someone's notes are escaping through the window.}\n> left: Swap drafts for an hour\n  chance likely:\n    progress thesis +small\n    charm +tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {They find the hole in your argument in ten minutes. You find three in theirs. Everyone feels better and worse.|They read your chapter and say, \"Oh, I see.\" You're not sure you did, until now.|You swap drafts over the partition, sandwich crumbs and all. Both chapters come back better.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: {They love your chapter, which helps nobody. You spend the hour fixing their commas.|They ask so many questions that you start doubting your title.|Their notes on your chapter are longer than your chapter. You lose the afternoon reading them.}\n> right: Hunt a source in the stacks\n  lore +small\n  progress thesis +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You find it on the bottom shelf, under a dictionary and a sleeping cat. It says exactly what you hoped, in a footnote.|Three hours of climbing ladders for one paragraph. It's a very good paragraph.|You find the source and a better one beside it. You bring both back and share the second.}\n\n@event thesis_is_it_good\ntitle: Is It Any Good?\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: hourglass\ntags: study\nscene: A student at a carrel in early light, holding a thick draft at arm's length as if it might bite.\ntext: {Halfway through rereading your thesis, you can't tell if it's brilliant or obvious.|The doubt has learned your timetable. It comes the morning after good work.|You know this doubt now. It reaches your carrel first.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: Most of it is written, which almost makes it worse.|So much of it is still unwritten.} {final_year and season == \"spring\": After this term there are no more terms.|{First-years laugh in the courtyard below.|Last night's candle is a puddle.|Someone two carrels down sighs the same sigh.}}\n> left: Push on regardless\n  progress thesis +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You write a page you don't believe in. By lunch you believe in about half of it, which is a start.|You keep going. The doubt keeps you company, and gets bored by evening.|You write through it, as you have before. By evening the doubt has gone quiet, and the page is good.}\n> right: Walk by the lake instead\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  progress thesis -tiny\n  say: {You walk around the lake and come back with no new pages and one good idea. You'll find out tomorrow if it's good.|The lake is mirror-still and has no opinion on your thesis. It's a relief.|You walk the long way round the lake. The doubt can't keep up, and you come back with a sentence that works.}\n\n# ---------------- The Thesis Laboratory (place: thesis_lab) ----------------\n\n@event thesis_lab_spare_carrel\ntitle: The Spare Carrel\nplace: thesis_lab\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: A long room of wooden carrels, one at the end empty but for a candle stub and a stray page of footnotes.\ntext: {There's an empty carrel at the end of the row, with a candle stub and nobody's name on it.|The spare carrel is still unclaimed. You've started thinking of its candle stub as yours.|You know the carrels by their scratches now. The spare one has a new scratch, and you suspect it's yours.} {season == \"winter\": Everyone writes in fingerless gloves.|The windows are open, and a footnote blows off someone's desk.} {Somewhere a quill scratches without stopping.|Someone is whispering \"however\" over and over.|A candle gutters out three carrels down, and someone groans.}\n> left: Work until the candle dies\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You write until the stub drowns in its own wax. Your notes are good, and your neck will remember them.|You read one book and half of another. The room's silence does half the work for you.|You work until the stub gives out and finish the paragraph by feel. It's better than the ones you could see.}\n> right: Just sit in the quiet\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    say: {You sit with your hands in your lap and listen to everyone else think. It's oddly restful.|You doze off with your cheek on someone's old footnotes. Nobody minds.|You sit in the quiet and the argument rearranges itself while you're not looking. You write it down before it changes its mind.}\n  else:\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {An advisor looks into the carrel and asks what you're working on. \"Thinking,\" you say. He writes that down.|A final-year asks if you'd mind the corner. Their own candle has given out.|An advisor finds you sitting still and lectures you on idleness for exactly as long as you'd have sat.}\n\n@event thesis_lab_footnote_war\ntitle: The Footnote War\npool: thesis_lab_routine\ntone: light\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: Two advisors arguing across a row of carrels, final-year students peering over the partitions to watch.\ntext: {Two advisors are arguing about a footnote across three carrels, and every final-year in the room has stopped pretending to work.|The footnote war has broken out again. Someone has drawn up sides on a scrap of paper.|By now the footnote war has rules, a referee and a running score chalked on the door.} {Today it's about whether an old founders' letter counts as a source.|Today it's about a comma.|Today it's about a footnote to a footnote.}\n> left: Weigh in with a source\n  challenge research expert-:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {You produce the source, open at the right page. Both advisors fall silent, and one of them asks for your name.|Your citation settles it. The loser shakes your hand and then rereads the page for an hour.|Your source wins the round. The referee chalks a point on the door under a new column, which has your name on it.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {Your source turns out to be the one they were both arguing against. They unite, briefly, to explain why.|You cite the wrong edition. The room winces as one.|Your source is ruled out of order by the referee, on a technicality you'll be studying for weeks.}\n> right: Keep my head down and listen\n  lore +small\n  say: {You learn more about sources from ten minutes of the war than from a term of lectures, and you never have to say a word.|You take notes on both sides. Neither of them would like how fair your notes are.|You keep your head down and your notebook open. The war is the best seminar in the building, and it's free.}\n\n@event thesis_lab_borrowed_candle\ntime: evening\ntitle: Borrowing a Candle\npool: thesis_lab_routine\ntone: tense\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: A dark carrel lit by one dying candle, a full box of candles on the empty desk next door.\ntext: {The candles here give out at the worst moment. Yours just has, and the carrel next door has a full box and nobody in it.|Your candle gives out again. Next door, the usual box of spares and nobody in the chair.|The lab's candles have outlasted no one's patience tonight, least of all yours. There's a box next door.} {A draft lies open beside the box, face up.|A note on the box says \"Help yourself\" in one hand and \"Don't\" in another.|Someone has drawn a small eye on the box.}\n> left: Borrow one and keep working\n  alignment -tiny\n  chance likely:\n    lore +small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {You light it and work until the bell. You leave a note that says \"IOU one candle\", unsigned.|One candle, two good hours. You put the stub back, which is either honest or worse.|You borrow one and replace it the next morning with two. The box owner has started leaving the lid open.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    renown -tiny\n    say: {The owner comes back for a forgotten scarf and finds you lit by their candle. They say nothing, which is worse.|By morning the whole lab knows who borrowed the candle. Someone leaves a box on your desk, with a ribbon.|The box is counted now, and the count is short. A note appears asking the borrower to come forward. You don't.}\n> right: Go back in the dark\n  mood +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You walk back across the bridges in the dark, your argument going round and round. It is better by the time you reach your bed.|You stub your toe twice on the way out. The idea you had on the stairs was worth both.|You know the way back in the dark by now. The argument walks with you and arrives a step ahead.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Royal Library Archives ----------------\n\n@event intern_royal_library_slips\ntitle: Request Slips\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: study\nscene: A brass chute dropping paper slips into a basket at the top of a deep stairwell lined with shelves.\ntext: {Scholars upstairs send their requests down a brass chute. Your job is to fetch them from the stacks, which go down for miles.|You've worn a path in the dust of the third landing.|You can read the chute's rattle now. A long rattle means someone wants something from the very bottom.} {One slip is in a hand so old the ink has gone brown.|One slip asks for \"the blue one\".|One slip is a love letter, sent down by mistake.}\n> left: Take the deep requests\n  wage\n  perform intern_royal_library +small\n  lore +small\n  health -small\n  say: {Four hundred steps down, the air is cold enough to keep books for ever. You come back up with the book and a lot of opinions about stairs.|You find the volume wedged behind a pipe. The scholar who asked for it nearly weeps.|You take the bottom slip without looking at it. Down there, you know the shelves better than the catalogue does.}\n> right: Take the near shelves\n  wage\n  perform intern_royal_library -tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You fetch the easy ones and leave the deep slips for someone else. Someone else notices.|You're fast and pleasant, and today the third landing is as deep as you go.|You clear the near shelves twice over. The long rattles go to someone else, who looks at you as they pass.}\n\n@event intern_royal_library_mending\ntitle: The Mending Table\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A bright worktable under a lamp, a crumbling manuscript laid out in pieces and a student holding fine tweezers.\ntext: {Today you're at the mending table, where crumbling records are put back together one flake at a time.|The binder hands you the tweezers without a word, which counts as a greeting.|The binder hands you one of the older pages today, which are the worst.} {The page is a royal decree on the correct length of goose necks.|The page is a recipe, half eaten by something.|The page is a list of names, one crossed out very hard.}\n> left: Mend it myself\n  wage\n  challenge research journeyman+:\n    win:\n      perform intern_royal_library +small\n      lore +small\n      say: {Every flake goes back where it belongs. The binder holds it up to the lamp and says, \"Acceptable,\" which here is a medal.|You save the page and read it while the glue dries. It's better than you expected.|You put the list back together, crossed-out name and all. The binder reads the name and says nothing for a while.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_royal_library -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {A sneeze. The binder looks at the scattered flakes and then at you. You both get the tweezers.|One flake ends up in the wrong century. Nobody can prove it was you, but everybody knows.|The oldest flake crumbles at your touch. The binder writes \"lost in repair\" in the ledger, in your hand.}\n> right: Watch the binder work\n  wage\n  lore +small\n  perform intern_royal_library -tiny\n  say: {You watch for an hour and learn more than a book could tell you. The binder says you're slow. You were.|The binder works without speaking and you learn by watching. It's very peaceful and very little gets done.|You watch the binder mend the page you'd have ruined. Next time, you'll know how. That's the idea.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Conclave Laboratory ----------------\n\n@event intern_conclave_test_chamber\ntitle: The Test Chamber\ntrack: intern_conclave\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: wand\ntags: study magic\nscene: A circular chamber with scorch-marked walls, a student at the centre with a wand raised and researchers watching through thick glass.\ntext: {The Conclave's researchers need someone to cast their newest spell for the first time. The walls are thick for a reason.|Someone has chalked a new circle on the floor. The old one is scorched.|You know every scorch mark in the chamber by now, and who made each one.} {Today's spell is meant to make a candle burn backwards.|Today's spell is meant to tidy a room. It has opinions about what tidy means.|Today's spell is unnamed. Nobody will say why.}\n> left: Step into the chamber\n  wage\n  challenge enchant using arcana adept:\n    win:\n      perform intern_conclave +small\n      arcana +small\n      say: {The spell works on the first try. Behind the glass, someone writes your name in the margin of their notes.|It works, more or less, for one glorious minute before the spell remembers itself.|The spell works, and nobody behind the glass will say what it was for. They thank you very politely.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      perform intern_conclave -tiny\n      say: {The spell goes off sideways. Your eyebrows are fine. Your fringe has views of its own now, and the backlash leaves your hands stinging for a day.|The spell goes off sideways, and the jolt of it leaves you dizzy until supper. Your robes will never fold the same way again.|The spell goes off sideways, you take the edge of the blast on your forearm, and it adds a new scorch mark to the wall. Someone labels it with your name.}\n> right: Take readings behind the glass\n  wage\n  arcana +tiny\n  perform intern_conclave +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You write down numbers for three hours while someone else gets to be brave. They're very good numbers.|You note every flicker. A senior researcher reads your page and asks for your notes next time.|You take the readings for a spell nobody will name. The numbers are strange, and you're asked to forget them.}\n\n@event intern_conclave_minutes\ntitle: Taking the Minutes\ntrack: intern_conclave\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: scroll\ntags: study magic\nscene: A long table of elderly mages in heavy robes, all talking at once, a young intern writing furiously at the end.\ntext: {You've been asked to take the minutes of a Conclave research committee. There are nine members, and none of them ever agrees.|The committee is still arguing about last time's question, and now about the minutes too.|You could take these minutes in your sleep, and some of the committee do attend them that way.} {Two of them are not speaking to each other, and want it recorded.|One of them asks what you think.|One of them falls asleep, and wakes up to vote.}\n> left: Offer an opinion when asked\n  wage\n  challenge research journeyman even:\n    win:\n      perform intern_conclave +small\n      renown +tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The table goes silent. Then the oldest mage says, \"The intern is right,\" and the rest pretend they agreed all along.|Your opinion settles the argument. It also starts two new ones.|You give your opinion and the sleeper wakes up to agree with it. It carries, seven to two.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_conclave -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {Nine heads turn towards you, slowly. You say something about wands. It isn't minuted.|You give your opinion and it is recorded in full, with the note \"(intern)\".|Your opinion is noted, discussed, voted down and then adopted next meeting without credit.}\n> right: Keep my head down and write\n  wage\n  perform intern_conclave +small\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {Your minutes are perfect and so dull that nobody reads them, which in a committee is the highest praise.|You record every word of the argument. Your hand aches, and you understand the Conclave much better than you wanted to.|You write it all down, including the vote of the member who slept through it. The minutes are accurate, which nobody wanted.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Guild of Alchemists' Works ----------------\n\n@event intern_alchemists_vat_watch\ntitle: Vat Watch\ntrack: intern_alchemists\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cauldron\ntags: study\nscene: A huge copper vat in a steaming hall, a student on a gantry staring into it with a colour chart in hand.\ntext: {Your task at the works is to watch one great vat and call the foreman if it changes colour.|You've brought your own colour chart now, with notes in the margins.|You and the vat understand each other now. It's an unsettling feeling.} {The amber is drifting, very slowly.|There's a bubble that keeps coming back, like a thought.|The vat smells faintly of cinnamon, which it never has before.} The foreman is all the way at the other end of the hall.\n> left: Adjust it myself\n  wage\n  challenge brew adept-:\n    win:\n      perform intern_alchemists +small\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {A pinch of salt and a slow stir, and the vat settles back to its proper self. The foreman never knew, but the vat did.|You correct it before it goes wrong. When the foreman passes, he gives the vat a satisfied look as if he did it.|You trace the cinnamon to a cracked spice valve and close it. The vat settles, and the foreman finds the valve next week.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_alchemists -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {The vat turns a brilliant violet and hisses. The foreman arrives at a run, and you'll be hearing about it for weeks.|You add too much salt. The vat sulks a muddy brown until the foreman coaxes it back, at length, with you watching.|You make it worse. A lot worse. The whole hall smells of cinnamon until the end of the week.}\n> right: Run for the foreman\n  wage\n  perform intern_alchemists +tiny\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood -small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You run the length of the hall. The foreman fixes it in a second and explains how, at length, as you catch your breath.|By the time you're back with the foreman the colour has corrected itself. He stares at you, then at the vat.|You run for the foreman, who sniffs the air, says \"Cinnamon,\" and goes very pale. He fixes it, and doesn't explain.}\n\n@event intern_alchemists_no_flame\ntitle: The Rule About Flames\ntrack: intern_alchemists\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: study\nscene: A break room in an alchemical works, workers gathered around a kettle sitting on a glowing stone that is not, technically, on fire.\ntext: {There's a strict rule at the works against naked flames, and against any heating reaction the foreman hasn't approved. The break room has spent forty years looking for one he will.|Break time. The kettle sits on its warm stone, and the workers look at you hopefully.|By now the break room asks the academy intern first, whatever the problem.} {Today the warm stone has gone cold.|Today someone wants toast, which is a much bigger question.|Today a new inspector is touring the works.}\n> left: Help brew the flameless tea\n  wage\n  challenge brew apprentice likely:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You heat the kettle with a reaction you learned in your first year. The break room calls it \"the academy method\" and adopts it on the spot.|It works, though everything boiled on it comes out a little purple.|You brew the tea with the new inspector in the room, and explain the method so confidently that he takes notes.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_alchemists -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {Your method works so well that someone in charge notices the steam from a reaction nobody approved. There's a meeting about it, with you in it.|The kettle boils, and so, briefly, does the table.|The inspector walks in mid-reaction. There's a form for it, and you fill it in.}\n> right: Stick to the rules\n  wage\n  perform intern_alchemists +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You drink cold water while everyone else drinks tea brewed on an unapproved reaction. The foreman notes your conduct, and the break room notes it too.|You remind everyone of the rule. You are absolutely right and nobody offers you a biscuit for the rest of the week.|You keep to the rules with the inspector watching. He commends you by name, and the break room never forgives it.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Observatory Expedition ----------------\n\n@event intern_expedition_thin_air\ntitle: Up the Ridge\ntrack: intern_expedition\nslot: evening\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: compass\ntags: study\nscene: A line of expedition members climbing a rocky ridge at dusk, carrying brass instruments wrapped in blankets.\ntext: {The instruments have to go up to the ridge before dark, and the air on the plateau is very thin.|The path up the ridge again. The thin air is still negotiating with everybody's lungs.|The ridge, and the goats on it, who have long since stopped looking at anyone from the station.} {season == \"winter\": {Snow has drifted against the path.|The wind cuts through every layer you own.|Ice has glazed the steps cut into the rock.}|{The rocks are still hot from the afternoon.|The sky is clear all the way to the top of the world.|The evening wind smells of thyme and stone.}}\n> left: Carry the great lens\n  wage\n  perform intern_expedition +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {You carry the great lens to the top without dropping it, then sit very still for a long time. The leader nods at you, once.|Your arms are numb by the top, and the lens is perfect. It's worth it when the first star shows in it.|You carry the lens up steadily, without fuss. Nobody watches you, which up here is the compliment.}\n> right: Do the sums in the tent\n  wage\n  lore +small\n  perform intern_expedition -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You work the night's sums by lamplight, warm and quiet. The lamp gutters twice. Your sums don't.|You have the charts ready when they come down. Nobody thanks you, because the charts are always ready.|You do the sums in the tent, as usual. The numbers are good, and so is the stove.}\n\n@event intern_expedition_chart_dispute\ntitle: A Star Out of Place\ntrack: intern_expedition\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: star\ntags: study\nscene: Two expedition members bent over a star chart by lamplight, one pointing firmly at a dot.\ntext: {Your chart and the senior astronomer's disagree about where one faint star is.|The senior astronomer's charts and yours have a running disagreement now, kept in red ink.|The senior astronomer now checks your charts first. A compliment, possibly.} {Probably your hand shook.|Probably their eyesight.|Probably the thin air.} {season == \"winter\": The nights are long, with time to recheck everything.|The nights are short, and every hour counts.}\n> left: Recheck it all night\n  wage\n  lore +small\n  perform intern_expedition +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {You recheck it until dawn. You were right by a hair, and nobody will ever care but you.|You were wrong by a hair. You fix it, and know the sky better than you did.|You recheck by lamplight and find the air was bending the star. You note it, and the astronomer copies your note.}\n> right: Raise it with the astronomer\n  wage\n  challenge appeal journeyman even:\n    win:\n      perform intern_expedition +small\n      charm +tiny\n      say: {The astronomer checks, grumbles and corrects their chart. Then they buy you a cup of the camp's terrible coffee.|\"Well spotted,\" says the astronomer, through their teeth.|You raise it, and the astronomer has already checked it. \"You first, this time,\" they say.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_expedition -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The astronomer checks and shows you, in front of everyone, exactly where you went wrong.|They don't check. They just look at you over their spectacles until you go away.|The astronomer blames the thin air and refuses to discuss it further. The star stays where they put it.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Beastcraft Field Station ----------------\n\n@event intern_field_station_count\ntitle: The Dawn Count\ntrack: intern_field_station\nslot: morning\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: study nature\nscene: A student lying flat in the heather at dawn, a tally board in hand, watching nesting towers on the moor.\ntext: {Every morning someone has to lie in the heather and count what's living on the moor. Today it's you.|You've learned which tussock is the least wet, and you guard it.|The dawn count again. The station's best counter, the warden says, is whoever minds the damp least.} {season == \"winter\": {Snow hares are out, white on white.|Frost has made the heather crackle under you.|A fox is counting the same hares.}|{The skylarks are going up one after another.|The midges have found you.|The heather is warm, and so is your tussock.}}\n> left: Count every last one\n  wage\n  perform intern_field_station +small\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {You count until your fingers are numb and the tally is perfect. The warden reads it twice and pins it on the wall.|You stay out until the very last nest is counted. You come back soaked and very proud of the number.|You count them all from your tussock. The warden copies your tally into the station book without checking it.}\n> right: Make a sensible estimate\n  wage\n  challenge research apprentice likely:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You mark it \"estimated\" in the margin, the way the station counts the far nests anyway. The warden accepts it, and you spend the extra hour by the stove with a cup of tea.|The warden checks your estimate against his own count and nods. He does the same on wet mornings.|Your estimate is close enough that the warden uses it for the far nests too. You get the stove first.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_field_station -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The warden counted too. His number is not your number. He makes a note.|Your estimate includes a flock that left last week. The warden is very nice about it, which is worse.|Your estimate misses a new colony on the far slope. The warden found it yesterday and was waiting to see if you would.}\n\n@event intern_field_station_patient\ntitle: The Patient in Paddock Three\ntrack: intern_field_station\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: paw\ntags: study nature\nscene: A muddy paddock with a splinted creature glaring from a straw nest, a student holding out a bucket at arm's length.\ntext: {The station's latest patient is a moor-drake with a splinted wing and no gratitude at all.|Feeding time in paddock three. The patient sees you coming and lowers its head.|The drake in paddock three recognises you now. This hasn't made it any nicer.} {It has eaten a glove.|It is pretending to sleep, which it does very badly.|It has learned to open the bucket by itself.} {season == \"winter\": Its breath steams in the frosty air.|It is lying in the one patch of shade.}\n> left: Feed it by hand\n  wage\n  challenge soothe apprentice likely:\n    win:\n      perform intern_field_station +small\n      vigor +small\n      say: {It takes the food from your hand, very gently. Then it bites the bucket, to make a point.|It lets you check the splint, grumbling the whole time like an old man at the barber's.|It eats from your hand and then opens the bucket to check you haven't kept any back.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      vigor +tiny\n      say: {It takes the food, a bit of your sleeve and a bruise's worth of your arm.|It butts you into the mud and then looks pleased with itself.|It opens the bucket itself and eats the lot, then bites you for being slow.}\n> right: Use the long tongs\n  wage\n  perform intern_field_station +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You feed it from a safe distance. It eats, and looks at you with deep contempt.|The tongs work perfectly. You feel you've let the patient down, somehow.|It takes the tongs off you and drops them in the mud. You feed it by hand after all.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Thornwick Exchange Lab ----------------\n\n@event intern_thornwick_pastries\ntitle: Thornwick Hospitality\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nslot: morning\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: mask\ntags: study\nscene: A polished laboratory table at a rival academy, a plate of perfect pastries set out for a visiting student.\ntext: {Each morning your Thornwick hosts leave pastries on your desk, with a polite note.|The notes have grown longer and politer, which is worrying.|The pastries are a game now, and both sides know it.} {They look perfect, which in an illusion lab is a warning.|One is steaming slightly, which it shouldn't be.|Today there's one plain biscuit, which is somehow worse.} {season == \"winter\": The pastries look very warm.|The icing hasn't melted in the heat.}\n> left: Eat one and smile\n  wage\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    charm +tiny\n    say: {It's real, and very good. The Thornwick interns look almost disappointed.|It's real. You eat it slowly, in front of everyone, and thank them warmly.|You eat the plain biscuit with every sign of delight. Nobody in the lab can tell if it was real, including you.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    charm +tiny\n    say: {It's a glamour over something dry and joyless. You eat the whole thing with a smile, and the lab respects that.|It tastes of nothing at all. You compliment the baker, and someone in the corner laughs.|The biscuit turns to air in your mouth. You keep chewing anyway, and the lab gives you a small round of applause.}\n> right: Bring my own breakfast\n  wage\n  guile +small\n  perform intern_thornwick -tiny\n  say: {You eat your own bread, very pointedly. The Thornwick students write something down.|You leave the plate untouched. Your supervisor here calls it \"a very Lumenhold choice\", and doesn't mean it nicely.|You eat your own bread and offer them some. They check it for glamours, which is the friendliest thing they've done.}\n\n@event intern_thornwick_rivalry\ntitle: The Old Grudge\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: duel\ntags: study\nscene: A group of rival students around a lab bench, one of them leaning in with a pointed smile towards a visitor in different robes.\ntext: {The Thornwick students have questions about Lumenhold. Most of them are about the last time the two schools met on the duelling field.|The Thornwick students have found a new angle on the old grudge.|You and the Thornwick students have a routine now. It's almost friendly.} {Today they want to know if Lumenhold's towers still lean.|Today they ask, sweetly, if Lumenhold teaches illusion yet.|Today one of them pretends to be you, very well.}\n> left: Out-charm them\n  wage\n  challenge negotiate adept-:\n    win:\n      perform intern_thornwick +small\n      charm +small\n      say: {You answer every barb with a better one. By lunch they're laughing with you rather than at you.|You make the room laugh at their joke, and then at them. Their supervisor smiles behind her notes.|You do a better impression of them than they did of you. The lab agrees, and so, grudgingly, do they.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      perform intern_thornwick -tiny\n      say: {You try to be witty. It comes out as a speech about the founders. They let you finish, kindly.|You lose the exchange so badly that one of them takes a bow on your behalf.|Their impression of you is so good that you forget your answer. They finish it for you, in your voice.}\n> right: Let it slide\n  wage\n  perform intern_thornwick +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You smile and go back to work. Your supervisor here notices, and approves, and you feel like you lost anyway.|You say nothing. It takes them all morning to realise you've won.|You let it go, as usual. They seem almost disappointed, and one of them saves you a seat at lunch.}\n\n# ---------------- Internship: the Office of Unseen Affairs ----------------\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_coffee\ntitle: The Coffee Machine\ntrack: intern_unseen_office\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: study mystery\nscene: A plain room with no windows and a gleaming coffee machine, quiet people in plain coats standing near it, not quite talking.\ntext: {Nobody at the Office talks about anything, except the coffee machine. It's very good.|A queue forms around the machine each evening, though nobody seems to join it.|The coffee queue, you've learned, is where the Office's real work gets done.} {Two people beside you discuss the weather in a way that isn't about the weather.|A cup has a name on it that was there yesterday, under another name.|The machine hisses, and everyone falls silent until it stops.}\n> left: Linger at the machine\n  wage\n  challenge deceive apprentice likely:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      perform intern_unseen_office +tiny\n      say: {You learn three things nobody told you, and one thing nobody knows you know.|You drink your coffee slowly and listen. By the second cup you understand what nobody is saying.|You stand in the queue long enough to be part of it. Someone tells you something, and you understand it was meant for you.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {Someone notices you listening and asks, very kindly, how your day is going. You don't sleep that night.|You overhear something you very much wish you hadn't. It can't be unheard.|The machine hisses mid-sentence and the room goes silent. When it stops, everyone is looking at you.}\n> right: Take my cup back to my desk\n  wage\n  perform intern_unseen_office +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You work through the evening with your coffee. The work is dull and your supervisor approves, which is a little chilling.|Your desk is very quiet. It's the most anyone at the Office will say about you, and here that's high praise.|You take your cup to your desk. When you come back for the next one, the queue has saved your place.}\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_wrong_file\ntitle: The Wrong File\ntrack: intern_unseen_office\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: study mystery\nscene: A plain folder with no label lying on an empty desk under a single lamp.\ntext: {A file has been left on your desk. It isn't yours, and it isn't labelled.|A second unlabelled file. You're starting to think it's a test.|The unlabelled files keep arriving. You've stopped asking where they come from.} This one is tied shut with a red ribbon. {It's very thin.|It's very thick, with a coffee ring on the cover.|The knot is one you don't recognise.}\n> left: Read it before returning it\n  wage\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      guile +small\n      perform intern_unseen_office +small\n      say: {You read it, retie the ribbon perfectly and hand it in. Your supervisor checks the knot and smiles, very slightly.|You read every page. It's about a man who sells hats. Nothing has ever made you more nervous.|You read it, retie the unfamiliar knot from memory and hand it in. Your supervisor checks it twice and then teaches you a new one.}\n    lose:\n      perform intern_unseen_office -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {Your supervisor takes the file back and glances at the ribbon. \"Interesting knot,\" she says.|You put the pages back in the wrong order. Somehow, everyone knows by morning.|You can't retie the knot. You hand it in with an ordinary bow, and your supervisor looks at it for a long time.}\n> right: Return it unopened\n  wage\n  alignment +tiny\n  perform intern_unseen_office +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You hand it back without looking. Your supervisor says nothing, which here is approval. You'll wonder about it for weeks.|You return it with the ribbon untouched. The next day there's a slightly better coffee waiting on your desk.|You return it unopened, as always. The files stop coming for a week, and then there are two.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/renown.grim","text":"# Renown (playtest 2, X3; L22: renown was hard to raise): twice a year a chance to be noticed is pushed\n# (`renown_chance`), and a handful of cards in the pools stake renown on a hard challenge: much to gain, a real\n# price to fail. Some open only once you're known.\n\n@event renown_chance\ntitle: A Chance to Be Noticed\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: term and (month == 2 or month == 8) and not final_year\nrepeat: 6\npriority: 25\ntype: menu\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: trophy\ntags: renown\nscene: A noticeboard in the entrance hall crowded with announcements, one gold-bordered notice pinned dead centre, students jostling to read it.\ntext: {A gold-bordered notice goes up in the entrance hall. Twice a year the academy puts students on show.|The gold notice is up again, as it is twice a year, and the crowd around it is bigger.|The gold notice again. People glance at you as they read it now.} Three stages: a spell before the whole school, the great debate, or the village fair. Each is a hard test in public. {Win, and the school knows your name; lose, and it remembers that instead.|The village is invited, and the faculty sit in the front row: a win is talked about for months, and so is a loss.|There's a prize, and there's the shame of losing in public.}\n> demonstrate: Cast a spell before the school\n  hint: Arcana, hard. Fame or embarrassment.\n  queue renown_demonstration now\n  say: You write your name on the list before you can think better of it.\n> debate: Enter the great debate\n  hint: Lore, hard. The faculty will be listening.\n  queue renown_debate now\n  say: You sign up for the debate. Your hands are shaking already.\n> fair: Perform at the village fair\n  hint: Charm and a spell. The village pays and remembers.\n  queue renown_village_fair now\n  say: You put your name down for the village fair. Somebody will have to teach you to bow.\n> pass: Keep your head down\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You read the notice and walk on. Fame can find someone else this term.|Not this time. You've got enough to do without an audience.|People glance at you as you walk past the notice. Let them. You'd rather not lose in public this term.}\n\n@event renown_demonstration\ntitle: Before the Whole School\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: wand\ntags: renown magic\nscene: The Great Hall packed to the rafters, every house at its table, a single student standing in the middle of the floor with a wand raised.\ntext: The whole school is in the Great Hall, and so is the faculty, and so, at the end, is the headmistress. You're in the middle of the floor, and everyone will remember what happens next, good or bad. There's a long silence while they wait for you to begin.\n> left: Cast the hardest thing you know\n  challenge spell adept drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +large\n      arcana +small\n      say: It works, beautifully, better than ever before. The hall is silent and then very loud. For a month, first-years point at you in corridors.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      mood -medium\n      say: It fizzles in front of everyone, with a noise like a sad goose. The hall is very kind about it, which is unbearable. You'll be \"the goose\" until someone else fails.\n> right: Cast something safe, perfectly\n  renown +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Something simple, done flawlessly. Polite applause. Nobody will remember it, and nobody will laugh at you either.\n\n@event renown_debate\ntitle: The Great Debate\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: renown study\nscene: Two lecterns on a stage under banners, a student at one pointing emphatically, an older student at the other frowning, a packed hall of faces in the dark.\ntext: The great debate: \"That magic should be taught to anyone who asks.\" You've drawn the side you disagree with, against a fourth-year who has never lost. The faculty are in the front row, and Thorne is taking notes: win and they'll remember you, lose and so will everyone.\n> left: Argue your side brilliantly anyway\n  challenge research expert drift mid:\n    win:\n      renown +large\n      lore +small\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: You argue the side you don't believe so well that you half-believe it. The fourth-year concedes. Thorne, afterwards, says \"Hm,\" twice, which has never happened.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      mood -small\n      say: The fourth-year takes you apart, politely and completely. The hall applauds you for trying, which is the cruellest kind of applause.\n> right: Argue what you actually believe\n  renown +tiny\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You throw away the brief and argue your own side, which is against the rules. You lose on points and win the hall. Thorne writes something down, and you'll never know what.\n\n@event renown_village_fair\ntitle: Magic at the Fair\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: sparkle\ntags: renown money\nscene: A small stage at a village fair under bunting, a student conjuring coloured lights over a crowd of apprentices and farmers.\ntext: The village fair has a stage, bunting and a crowd of farmers who have seen every trick there is. The village apprentices sit at the front, cross-legged, unimpressed in advance. Win them over and the village talks of you for a year; flop, and it talks of that.\n> left: Put on a real show\n  challenge perform journeyman:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      gold +small\n      say: Lights, a dancing hat, a dove that turns into a shower of petals. The apprentices whoop. The farmers pay for the hat trick twice. The village talks about it until harvest.\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: The dove refuses to turn into anything and sits on your head. The apprentices love it; the farmers don't pay.\n> right: Do card tricks for the front row\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You sit on the edge of the stage and do card tricks for the apprentices until your voice gives out. No fame to speak of. Half of them walk you to the gate.\n> up[sleight]: Lift the mayor's watch for the finale\n  renown +small\n  gold +small\n  say: For the finale you lift the mayor's watch, his spectacles and, for one bad moment, his purse. You give back the watch and the spectacles, to applause. The purse you forget to mention.\n\n@event renown_ice_rescue\ntitle: A First-Year on the Ice\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: season == \"winter\" and term\nweight: 5\nart: snow\ntags: renown danger\nscene: A small figure clinging to the edge of a broken patch of ice in the middle of a frozen lake, students shouting on the shore, a student running out across the ice.\ntext: A first-year has gone through the ice on the lake. A crowd is shouting on the shore, and nobody is moving. You're nearest.\n> left: Go out on the ice\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge feat adept:\n    win:\n      renown +large\n      health -small\n      say: You crawl out flat on the ice and pull them free. By supper the whole school knows your name, and the first-year's mother writes you a letter you keep for ever.\n    lose:\n      health -large\n      renown +small\n      say: The ice gives under you too. The prefects pull you both out with a ladder. You spend a week in the infirmary, a little famous, mostly frozen.\n> right: Run for the prefects\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: You run for help, and the prefects get there with ladders in time. Someone else is the hero. You were right, and you feel wrong about it all winter.\n\n@event renown_portrait\ntitle: A Portrait for the Hall\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: renown >= 30\nweight: 8\nart: mirror\ntags: renown money\nscene: A painter's easel in a sunlit corridor, a half-finished portrait of a student in formal robes, the sitter trying very hard not to scratch their nose.\ntext: The academy hangs a portrait of its most talked-about students in the long gallery each year, and someone has suggested you. The painter's fee is yours to pay, as is the custom. The other portraits are already watching you.\n> left: Sit for it\n  gold -medium\n  renown +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Six sittings, an itchy collar and a painter who hums. The portrait is good, if a little more heroic than you are. Passing it, first-years straighten their backs.\n> right: Decline politely\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You decline. Your purse is grateful. The portrait that goes up instead is of someone who looks very pleased with themselves, and you find you don't mind at all.\n\n@event renown_rumour\ntitle: A Rumour About You\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nwhen: renown >= 40\nweight: 5\nart: mask\ntags: renown social\nscene: Two students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, a third walking past them with a stiff back, a crumpled gossip sheet on the floor.\ntext: Being known has a price: there's a rumour about you going round the castle, untrue and a little bit funny, and it's in Nessa's gossip column by Friday. People stop talking when you walk in.\n> left: Answer it in public\n  challenge perform journeyman unlikely wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      say: You laugh it off at supper, loudly and well, with a better story of your own. By Monday everyone's repeating yours instead.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      mood -small\n      say: You deny it too hard, and it sounds true. The rumour grows a second chapter. It'll pass. It takes a month.\n> right: Ignore it\n  renown -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You say nothing. It burns for a fortnight and then dies of boredom. You learn who repeated it, and remember.\n\n@event renown_patron_dinner\ntitle: Dinner With a Patron\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: renown >= 25 and term\nweight: 8\nart: meal\ntags: renown money\nscene: A long candlelit dining table in a grand townhouse, a student in borrowed formal robes seated between a wealthy patron and a portrait of the patron.\ntext: A wealthy patron of the academy has asked to meet \"the students everyone's talking about\", and you're one of them. Dinner is nine courses. The patron likes clever talk and has been known to fund a promising student's whole final year.\n> left: Be dazzling\n  challenge negotiate expert:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      gold +medium\n      say: You're witty through eight courses and wise on the ninth. The patron sends you home with a purse \"for books\" and an invitation to come back next year.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      mood -small\n      say: You use the wrong fork, then the wrong joke. The patron is kind and bored. You're not asked back.\n> right: Be honest about your struggles\n  renown +small\n  gold +small\n  say: You talk plainly about money and work and exams. The patron listens, the only one at the table who does. A small purse arrives the next week with no letter, just a crest.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/spell_lessons.grim","text":"# Spell lessons (playtest 2, N3): once a spell is learned, the next term month shows where and how it's really taught,\n# and by whom. One card per spell, once a run. Written for the current twelve spells; reworked if the minigames change them.\n\n@event lesson_flame\ntitle: Putting Fire Out\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(flame) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: thorne\nart: flame\ntags: magic study\nscene: A stone practice room with scorched walls, a stern grey-bearded professor beside a roaring brazier, a bucket of water at his feet.\ntext: Flame is yours now. Professor Thorne gathers the year's new Flame casters in the stone practice room and lights a brazier. \"Any fool can start a fire,\" he says. \"You are here to learn to stop one.\" There is a bucket of water at his feet, and he forbids you to touch it.\n> left: Pull the fire back into your hand\n  meet thorne\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  say: You call the flame back like a sleeve pulled over a wrist. It stings, and the brazier flares and sinks. \"Again,\" says Thorne. He says it eleven times.\n> right: Ask what the bucket is for\n  meet thorne\n  lore +small\n  rel thorne +small\n  say: \"For the student who doesn't ask,\" says Thorne, and empties it over the brazier of the boy beside you. Then he explains, for an hour, how every great fire he has known began.\n\n@event lesson_tide\n# retired by spells v2 (was \"spell_level(tide) >= 1 and term\"): tide is cut\ntitle: Waist-Deep at Dawn\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: marisol\nart: wave\ntags: magic study retired\nscene: A misty lake at dawn, a professor standing waist-deep in the water with arms raised, students hesitating on the pebbled shore.\ntext: Professor Marisol teaches Tide where it lives: waist-deep in the lake at dawn. \"Cold water,\" she says happily, \"is good for everyone.\" She recites a poem about waves and stops before the last line, frowning. The lake waits for you.\n> left: Wade in and call a wave\n  meet marisol\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: The water is so cold it feels like an opinion. Your wave rises knee-high and slaps Marisol full in the chest, and she whoops. \"The last line!\" she shouts. \"It was about this!\"\n> right: Call it from the shore\n  meet marisol\n  lore +small\n  rel marisol -tiny\n  say: You work the spell from the dry pebbles and raise a neat little swell. Marisol looks at your dry boots with real sorrow, as if at a poem with no ending.\n\n@pool spell_lessons_auto_routine\ntrigger: auto\nclass: routine\ntags: magic\n\n@event lesson_foresight\ntitle: The Bottom of the Cup\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(foresight) >= 1 and term\ntone: warm\nart: eye\ntags: study\nscene: An observatory room with tea cups on every desk, a lecturer in three scarves peering into a student's cup with a magnifying glass.\ntext: The astromancy lecturer, who wears three scarves in every season, sits the class at the observatory with cups of tea and no instructions. Foresight, it turns out, begins at the bottom of the cup: what you think you see, and what you want to.\n> left: Read your own cup\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The leaves say a hard week is coming. It does, from Tuesday. The lecturer says that's the trouble with reading your own: you start making it true.\n> right: Read your neighbour's\n  charm +small\n  say: You tell your neighbour a letter is on its way. A letter arrives at supper. Now half the dormitory wants their leaves read, and you have to learn to say \"unclear\" kindly.\n\n@event lesson_starlight\ntitle: A Jar on the Roof\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(starlight) >= 1 and term\ntone: warm\nslot: night\nart: star\ntags: study\nscene: A flat observatory roof at night, a student holding up a glass jar toward a sky crowded with stars, breath steaming.\ntext: Starlight is practised after midnight on the observatory roof, where your Starcalling tutor hands you a glass jar and tells you to hold it up \"until something falls in\". The stars are very far away. The roof is very cold.\n> left: Hold the jar until it fills\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: At three in the morning a thread of light settles in the jar like milk in tea. You carry it down the stairs in both hands. You can't feel your fingers until breakfast.\n> right: Lie back and watch the sky\n  mood +small\n  say: You lie on the roof and name the constellations with whoever is beside you. Your jar stays empty. The lecturer, passing, says that's the first lesson too, and the one most people skip.\n\n@event lesson_mend\ntitle: Teacups First\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(mend) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: amaranth\nart: cup\ntags: magic study\nscene: An infirmary storeroom, a stern healer dropping a teacup to the stone floor while a student kneels over the pieces with a wand.\ntext: Sister Amaranth teaches Mend with teacups. She drops one on the infirmary floor without looking. \"Mend it.\" When it's whole she drops another. \"Bones are harder,\" she says, rolling bandages. \"Cups first.\"\n> left: Mend cup after cup\n  meet amaranth\n  alchemy +small\n  mana -small\n  say: By the twentieth cup you can hear the crack close before you see it. Amaranth says \"Keep doing that,\" and drops a teapot.\n> right: Ask her about the bones\n  meet amaranth\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She tells you, still rolling bandages, about the worst break she ever set. It was a student's; that student is a professor now. You go a little pale, and listen to every word.\n\n@event lesson_purify\ntitle: Make It Stronger\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(purify) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: amaranth\nart: cauldron\ntags: magic study\nscene: An alchemy lab bench with a cauldron of thick green liquid, a healer standing over a student who holds a wand above it.\ntext: Sister Amaranth has heard that Pell taught you to Distill. She sets a cauldron of something green in front of you that she will not name. \"Pell teaches it fast,\" she says. \"I teach it so you're still standing afterwards. Make it stronger.\" Then she goes back to her own work.\n> left: Distill it drop by drop\n  meet amaranth\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: Drop by drop, the green comes down to one dark spoonful, and each drop costs you a little. You sit down halfway, as she said you would. Amaranth tastes the result, nods and puts it on the high shelf.\n> right: Ask what it was\n  meet amaranth\n  rel amaranth +small\n  say: \"Last term's soup,\" she says. You decide not to believe her, then decide you do. She shows you where to stop before it takes too much from you, and you distill half a cup, badly.\n\n@pool spell_lessons_vey_auto_routine\nactor: vey\ntrigger: auto\nclass: routine\ntags: magic\n\n@event lesson_glamour\ntitle: Pick a Card\npool: spell_lessons_vey_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(glamour) >= 1 and term\ntone: light\nart: cards\ntags: study\nscene: A velvet-curtained classroom, a professor in a violet coat fanning out a hand of playing cards toward a row of students.\ntext: Professor Vey's glamour lesson is a card game. Each student glamours a plain card to look like the queen of stars, and Vey picks out the true queen from the pile. Nobody, she says, has ever made her pick wrong. \"Fool me, and you may skip the essay.\"\n> left: Glamour your card\n  meet vey\n  challenge deceive master- unlikely +1 if spell_level(glamour) >= 2:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      rel vey +medium\n      charm +small\n      if enrolled(illusion):\n        study illusion +large\n      else:\n        charm +small\n      say: Vey picks your card, turns it over and finds a plain two. She laughs out loud, a real laugh, and the class goes silent. \"Well,\" she says. \"Well, well. No essay for you.\" By supper the whole tower knows, and Vey has started calling you \"my card sharp\".\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      guile +small\n      say: Vey passes over your card without a glance. \"The queen of stars,\" she says kindly, \"does not blush.\" But you saw how she chose: by the hands, not the cards.\n> right: Watch how she catches them\n  meet vey\n  guile +small\n  say: You watch her eyes instead of the cards. She never looks at the faces, only at the hands holding them. You write that down, and she sees you write it, and smiles.\n\n@event lesson_mirror\n# retired by spells v2 (was \"spell_level(mirror) >= 1 and term\"): mirror is cut\ntitle: Gently\npool: spell_lessons_vey_auto_routine\nwhen: false\ntone: warm\nart: mirror\ntags: study retired\nscene: A dim classroom with the curtains drawn, a hand mirror on every desk, a professor standing well to one side of the glass.\ntext: Professor Vey teaches Mirror in an empty classroom with the curtains drawn, which is not like her. \"A mirror charm shows people themselves,\" she says. \"Do it gently. Most people have not asked to see.\" She stands to one side of every glass in the room.\n> left: Practise on yourself first\n  meet vey\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The charm shows you yourself a moment too honestly. You practise until you can look at it without flinching. Mostly.\n> right: Ask why she avoids the glass\n  meet vey\n  rel vey +small\n  say: \"Vanity,\" says Vey, with a magnificent smile that answers nothing at all. But she lets you stay after the others leave, and shows you how to soften the charm's edges.\n\n@event lesson_haste\ntitle: Race the Hippogriff\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(haste) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: brask\nart: feather\ntags: magic study\nscene: A windy flight field marked with flags, a grey-haired professor holding a stopwatch, a student sprinting beside a hippogriff.\ntext: Professor Brask teaches Haste on the flight field, with a stopwatch and a hippogriff that is faster than the spell. \"Beat her,\" she says to you. To the hippogriff she explains, at length and politely, that she is not to bite you if you win.\n> left: Race the hippogriff\n  meet brask\n  challenge feat adept-:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      rel brask +tiny\n      say: You cross the flag a beak's length ahead. The hippogriff looks at Brask; Brask looks at the stopwatch, then at you. \"Hm,\" she says. From her, it's a medal.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The hippogriff wins by a length and takes your hat as her prize. Brask tells a story about a student who lost to a goose. It ends with a bite. \"Moral: faster.\"\n> right: Time the others first\n  meet brask\n  lore +small\n  say: You hold the stopwatch and watch the fast ones. They aren't running harder; they start the spell a step before they need it. Brask sees you notice, and hands you the next race.\n  if knows(brask.asking):\n    rel brask +tiny\n    say: Afterwards you ask her how to start the spell a step early yourself. She shows you, and remembers that you asked.\n\n@event lesson_beast_speech\n# retired by spells v2 (was \"spell_level(beast_speech) >= 1 and term\"): beast_speech is cut\ntitle: The Mule Has Views\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: false\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: brask\nart: paw\ntags: magic study retired\nscene: A warm stall with straw on the floor, a student sitting cross-legged opposite a grey old mule who stares back without blinking.\ntext: Beast-speech, says Professor Brask, is mostly listening. She sits you in a stall beside an old mule who has, she says, \"views\". You are to hear them. The mule looks at you as if you're the one being taught.\n> left: Sit with him all afternoon\n  meet brask\n  vigor +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: In the fourth hour the mule's thoughts come through: mostly apples, and one long grievance about a farrier. Brask says \"Good,\" to the mule, not to you.\n> right: Try the spell straight away\n  meet brask\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  say: You cast before you've listened and get one word back, very clearly: \"Rude.\" Brask laughs once, loudly. It's the laugh of the term, and everyone in the stables heard it.\n\n@event lesson_ward\ntitle: Chalk\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(ward) >= 1 and term\ntone: light\nart: shield\ntags: study\nscene: A dim room at the top of a tower, low lamps, a lecturer mid-throw with a piece of chalk while students hold up shimmering wards.\ntext: At the top of the lecture tower, where the lamps burn dim on purpose, the umbral lecturer drills first-years in their Ward by throwing chalk. Hard, and without warning. \"A ward that only works when you're ready,\" says the lecturer, \"is a decoration.\"\n> left: Hold the ward all hour\n  guile +small\n  mana -small\n  say: The first chalk hits your forehead. The fifth bounces off the ward with a sound like a bell. By the end of the hour your ward is up before you know you've raised it.\n> right: Watch the lecturer's wrist\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You let the ward drop and watch how the throws begin. Three pieces of chalk find your forehead while you do. But you see it now: the shoulder moves first.\n\n@event lesson_shadowstep\ntitle: Where the Voice Was\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(shadowstep) >= 1 and term\ntone: tense\ntime: night\nart: moon\ntags: study\nscene: A pitch-dark tower room with one dim lamp guttering out, the outline of a student stepping between deep shadows.\ntext: Shadowstep is taught in the dark. The umbral lecturer puts out the last dim lamp and calls your name from somewhere else in the room. You have to arrive where the voice was before it moves. Somewhere, a chair is waiting for your shins.\n> left: Step into the dark\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You step, and the dark folds, and you're across the room with a bruise from the chair. The voice is right beside you. \"Better,\" it says, and moves away again.\n> right: Wait for your eyes to adjust\n  lore +small\n  say: You stand still until the dark has edges. The lecturer, somewhere, says that's cheating, and that cheating is half of umbral magic. You step only once, but you step exactly.\n\n# ---------------- Spells v2: practice for the new spells (one each, after you learn it) ----------------\n\n@event lesson_animate\ntitle: The Chair That Follows\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(animate) >= 1 and term\ntone: light\nart: spell_animate\ntags: study\nscene: A workshop corridor, a wooden chair trotting after a student like a dog, a teapot waddling behind it.\ntext: The Artifice homework is to animate something and live with it for a day. You pick a chair, which follows you to every lecture. The teapot is the other option. It has opinions about being carried.\n> left: Live with the chair\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: The chair follows you everywhere and offers itself whenever you stop, including on the stairs, at speed. By evening you have bruised shins and a very loyal chair.\n> right: Animate the teapot instead\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  say: The teapot waddles after you all day, pouring when you sit. It takes more out of you than the chair would, but your friends have never been so well looked after.\n\n@event lesson_seal\ntitle: Only for the Addressee\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(seal) >= 1 and term\ntone: eerie\nart: spell_seal\ntags: study\nscene: A windowless room, a student pressing their thumb to a folded letter while a headmistress watches with her hands folded.\ntext: Today's Hollow Studies exercise: seal a letter so that only the person it's addressed to can open it. The headmistress will try to open it first. \"If I can,\" she says mildly, \"you haven't.\"\n> left: Seal it against her\n  guile +small\n  mana -small\n  say: She tries the letter for a long minute with one finger, then hands it back unopened. \"Better than I was at your age,\" she says, which doesn't sound like a compliment. You feel hollowed out till supper.\n> right: Ask how she'd break it\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She shows you, kindly and very quickly, how she'd break yours. You leave the room knowing a great deal more about seals and rather less sure of your own.\n\n@event lesson_wildfire\ntitle: Longer Each Night\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(wildfire) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: lyra\nart: spell_wildfire\ntags: magic duel\nscene: An empty duel hall at night, two students each holding a growing fire, one watching the other's with narrowed eyes.\ntext: Lyra wants you in the duel hall after hours. \"You let it go too early,\" she says, and lights her own. \"Hold it. Longer each night. It will bite you; let it.\"\n> left: Hold it until it bites\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You hold it until the heat is in your teeth and then a breath longer. When you let it go it runs to the rafters. Lyra looks at your blistered palm and says nothing, which is approval.\n> right: Ask her how she stands it\n  rel lyra +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She doesn't answer at first. Then she shows you her palms, scarred and old, and tells you about the first time, and her mother. You don't practise much. It's still the right night.\n\n@event lesson_heartwood\ntitle: A Tree by the Window\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(heartwood) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: tobias\nart: spell_heartwood\ntags: magic nature\nscene: A dormitory courtyard at dusk, a young tree that wasn't there that morning, two students sitting under it looking pleased with themselves.\ntext: Tobias wants you to grow a heartwood every evening for a week, \"so your hands remember\". Today he suggests the courtyard under your dormitory window, where nothing has grown in years.\n> left: Grow it properly, roots and all\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: It takes everything you've got and a blister. By dusk there's a young tree under your window, and your back aches like a harvest. Tobias says it's the best one he's seen, and apologises for saying so.\n> right: Sit under his instead\n  mood +small\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  say: You let him grow it and sit under it with him, talking about nothing. Your hands don't remember much. The rest of you does.\n\n@event lesson_omen\ntitle: The Spoon Will Fall\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(omen) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nactor: seraphine\nart: spell_omen\ntags: magic study\nscene: A refectory table at breakfast, a pale student pointing at a spoon balanced on the edge of a bowl, a second student staring at it.\ntext: At breakfast Seraphine says, without looking up, \"Practise on small things. Spoons. Doors. The weather. Never people, not yet.\" Then she looks at you, quite clearly, for once.\n> left: Foretell small things all day\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The spoon falls. The door sticks. It rains at four. By evening it unnerves you how well it works, and you catch yourself foretelling the soup.\n> right: Ask what \"not yet\" means\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: \"Later,\" she says, then, \"I mean that exactly.\" She doesn't explain. She does pass you the salt before you've asked for it.\n\n@event lesson_sleight\ntitle: Pockets and Decks\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(sleight) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: corvin\nart: spell_sleight\ntags: magic\nscene: A dim corner of a common room, a grinning student fanning a deck of cards while another student palms a coin under the table.\ntext: Corvin is teaching you to keep your hand in. \"Decks are practice,\" he says, shuffling. \"Pockets are performance.\" Bram is asleep in an armchair nearby, his whistle on a ribbon round his neck.\n> left: Practise on the deck\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Two hours of coins and cards until your fingers cramp. It's dull, and by the end the coin vanishes so cleanly even Corvin blinks. \"Saint,\" he says, \"with very quick hands.\"\n> right: Lift Bram's whistle and return it\n  guile +medium\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: The whistle comes off its ribbon without a sound and goes back on it the same way. Bram snores through all of it. Corvin is delighted and slightly worried about you.\n\n@event lesson_contraption\ntitle: The Breakfast Engine\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(contraption) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: mir\nart: spell_contraption\ntags: magic\nscene: A breakfast table where a clattering machine made of a toast rack, a spoon and a teapot is buttering toast at alarming speed.\ntext: Mir slides a breakfast tray across to you. \"Practice! Make it do breakfast. Toast, butter, tea. It's the hardest machine there is, probably.\" Half the refectory has already turned to watch.\n> left: Build the breakfast engine\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: Your engine butters four slices, pours one cup and singes your eyebrow before it falls apart in a shower of crumbs. The refectory applauds. Mir writes something down, beaming.\n> right: Build it with Mir, slowly\n  rel mir +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You build it together, Mir explaining every joint. It makes one perfect slice of toast and then retires. Mir says it's the best machine of the year, probably.\n\n@event lesson_hoarfrost\ntitle: Putting Out Fires\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(hoarfrost) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nactor: kael\nart: spell_hoarfrost\ntags: magic duel\nscene: A duel hall where one student's flame is frosting over and going out, its caster glaring, a quiet student standing nearby.\ntext: \"It's for putting fires out,\" Kael says again, and nods across the duel hall to where Lyra is practising, very loudly, with a very large flame. He waits to see whether you'll take the hint.\n> left: Put out Lyra's flame\n  arcana +small\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: Your frost meets her flame and they cancel with a hiss and a cloud of steam. Lyra's face, when it clears, is magnificent. Kael makes a sound you think is a laugh.\n> right: Practise on the braziers instead\n  arcana +tiny\n  rel kael +tiny\n  say: You frost the braziers one by one. It's useful, and dull, and Kael stays with you for all of it without saying anything. You suspect that was the point.\n\n@event lesson_candour\ntitle: Questions People Want to Answer\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(candour) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: nessa\nart: spell_candour\ntags: magic social\nscene: A busy refectory, a bossy editor pointing out students at random tables to another student holding a notebook.\ntext: Nessa hands you a notebook in the refectory. \"Three people, three true answers, by the end of lunch,\" she says. \"Ask the question they want to answer. Use the spell only when you have to.\"\n> left: Use the spell on all three\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Three truths by the pudding course, and one of them is about you, and not kind. Nessa reads your notes and says, \"Now you know why I don't use it much.\"\n> right: Ask good questions instead\n  rel nessa +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You only manage two truths without the spell, but they're good ones, and nobody minded telling you. Nessa says that's the better trick, and that she won't print either.\n\n@event lesson_brace\ntitle: Planted\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(brace) >= 1 and term\ntone: light\nart: spell_brace\ntags: duel\nscene: A duel hall's practice corner, an eyepatched dummy firing weak hexes at a student who stands planted and unmoving.\ntext: The eyepatched dummy is empty now, but it still throws a hex or two out of habit. The Circle uses it to practise bracing. Today the seniors are taking bets on how long you'll stand.\n> left: Stand until it gives up\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You stand there for an hour while the dummy throws everything it has left. The seniors pay out to whoever bet longest. Your shins are a mess and you are unmovable.\n> right: Coach the first-years instead\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You stand behind a row of first-years and fix their feet, one by one, while they get hexed. They're grateful. You're bored. The dummy throws one at you on the way out, out of spite.\n\n@event lesson_quickquill\ntitle: Lecture Notes\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(quickquill) >= 1 and term\ntone: light\nart: spell_quickquill\ntags: study\nscene: A lecture hall, a quill writing busily by itself in a notebook while its owner sits back with folded arms, the lecturer squinting at them.\ntext: Your quill wants to take your lecture notes for you. It writes everything down, word for word, faster than the lecturer can talk. It also writes what it thinks of the lecturer, in the margins.\n> left: Let it take the notes\n  study +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You have perfect notes, every word, and a margin full of the quill's views on the lecturer's waistcoat. You spend the evening inking them out before anyone sees.\n> right: Make it take only what you say\n  guile +small\n  mana -tiny\n  say: It sulks, and then takes down exactly what you tell it, no more. It's work keeping it honest. You come out with half the notes and the full trust of your quill.\n\n@event lesson_bramble\ntitle: A Hedge on Demand\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(bramble) >= 1 and term\ntone: light\nart: spell_bramble\ntags: nature\nscene: Old Maud's cottage garden, a groundskeeper pointing at a gap in her fence while a student coaxes a thorny hedge up out of the ground to fill it.\ntext: Old Maud has heard about your hedge. \"There's a gap in my fence the rabbits use, dearie,\" she says. \"Grow me one there. Thorns on the outside, mind.\" The rabbits are watching from the cabbages.\n> left: Grow it thorns and all\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: The hedge comes up thick and thorny, and takes a strip off your knuckles on the way. Maud inspects it and says it'll do. The rabbits look betrayed.\n> right: Grow it gently, for the cabbages\n  rel maud +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You grow it soft and plant a row of cabbages beyond it for the rabbits. Maud laughs until she has to sit down. \"You'll understand that in two weeks,\" she says.\n\n@event lesson_index\ntitle: The Right Page\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(index) >= 1 and term\ntone: light\nart: spell_index\ntags: study\nscene: A library table piled with heavy books, every one falling open at a different marked page as a student touches it, a cat watching from the top of the pile.\ntext: Every book you touch falls open at the page you need, now. The trouble is the library knows it, and books are leaving their shelves to come and find you. Footnote is sitting on the biggest.\n> left: Read every page they offer\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You read every page that offers itself until the lamps go out. You learn a great deal, most of it useful, and none of it what you came in for.\n> right: Find one thing for your exams\n  study +tiny\n  say: You ask for one thing, firmly. Twelve books bring it. You take your page and leave the others, who sulk on the table until a clerk shelves them.\n\n@event lesson_rapport\ntitle: A Room That Was Turning\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(rapport) >= 1 and term\ntone: light\nart: spell_rapport\ntags: social\nscene: A refectory at supper where two tables are on the edge of an argument, a student stepping between them with a raised mug and a grin.\ntext: Two tables in the refectory are on the edge of a food fight, and every head is turning. It's the Newt all over again, with worse gravy.\n> left: Step in and turn it\n  renown +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: One joke about the gravy, at exactly the right moment, and both tables laugh instead of throwing it. The prefects look relieved and slightly suspicious. Your heart doesn't slow down until bed.\n> right: Let it happen\n  mood +small\n  say: You let it go, and the food fight is magnificent. You watch from under the table, laughing. Nobody remembers it was nearly stopped.\n\n@event lesson_tailwind\ntitle: Never Stop\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(tailwind) >= 1 and term\ntone: light\nart: spell_tailwind\nscene: A student on a broom skimming low over a lake at tremendous speed, a wake of spray behind them and a fisherman waving from a boat.\ntext: The fisherman's rule for the wind is \"never stop\". You take the long way round the lake on your free afternoon to practise it, and the wind gets behind you as soon as you're off the jetty.\n> left: Never stop\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You go round the lake three times without stopping, faster each lap, until your hands are numb on the handle. The fisherman waves every lap now.\n> right: Land and talk to him\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You land by his boat and tell him about the storm run. He listens, nods, and says, \"Never stop,\" again, and hands you a fish to take home.\n\n# ---------------- The Whisperwood's spells (world expansion 1) ----------------\n# Four hidden spells found in the wood, each recognised by someone at the academy, and six forbidden ones nobody\n# will teach: their \"lesson\" is the first quiet moment you could use one, and whether you do.\n\n@event lesson_lodestar\ntitle: The Lighthouse Light\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(lodestar) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: grimsby\nart: spell_lodestar\ntags: magic\nscene: An old bookseller in a dark shop watching a soft guiding light gather around a student's shoulders, his spectacles reflecting it.\ntext: The light gathers round your shoulders in Grimsby's shop without your meaning it to, and the old man puts down his pen. \"Lodestar,\" he says softly. \"A lighthouse keeper's spell. She used it to walk the lamp-stair in storms.\" He doesn't say who she was. You think you know.\n> left: Ask him to watch you cast it\n  meet grimsby\n  arcana +small\n  mana -tiny\n  say: You let the light build, breath by breath, until it stands round you like a lantern. Grimsby nods along to your breathing, as if to a tune he knows. \"Slower,\" he says. \"She was always slower.\"\n> right: Ask about the lighthouse\n  meet grimsby\n  lore +small\n  rel grimsby +tiny\n  say: He tells you about the lamp, the stair, the keeper who wanted to see a long way, and the books she bought from him for forty years. The light round your shoulders stays lit the whole time, listening.\n\n@event lesson_moonwell\ntitle: Sitting Still\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(moonwell) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: maud\nart: spell_moonwell\ntags: magic\nscene: An old woman and a student sitting on a garden bench under the moon, eyes closed, a faint silver light pooling in the student's cupped hands.\ntext: Old Maud finds you on her garden bench at dusk, trying to make Moonwell go faster. She sits down beside you. \"That's your trouble,\" she says. \"It's a sitting-still spell, dearie. You can't hurry a basin.\"\n> left: Sit still with her\n  meet maud\n  arcana +small\n  health +tiny\n  say: You sit until the moon is up and your feet are cold. Somewhere in the second hour, the light starts pooling, and you stop trying, and it fills. Maud is asleep. You don't wake her.\n> right: Ask how she knows the spell\n  meet maud\n  lore +small\n  rel maud +tiny\n  say: \"Learned it in the wood,\" says Maud, \"same as you, sixty years back. Everybody who goes deep enough learns it eventually. The ones who come back, anyway.\" She pats your knee as if that were reassuring.\n\n@event lesson_reflect\ntitle: Hall of Mirrors\npool: spell_lessons_vey_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(reflect) >= 1 and term\ntone: light\nart: spell_reflect\nscene: A theatrical professor in a corridor of mirrors throwing a small spell at a student, who sends it bouncing back down the corridor.\ntext: Professor Vey hears you've learned Reflect and appears in the mirror corridor with a bag of very small, very harmless hexes. \"Show me,\" Vey says, and throws one at your head.\n> left: Send it back\n  meet vey\n  arcana +small\n  mana -tiny\n  say: You catch the hex and return it. Vey ducks, delighted, and throws three. You return two. The third gives you rabbit ears for an hour, which Vey says is the lesson.\n> right: Send it somewhere else\n  meet vey\n  guile +small\n  rel vey +tiny\n  say: You catch the hex and bounce it off a mirror into a suit of armour, which sneezes. Vey laughs so hard the mirrors ring. \"Oh, you're going to be trouble.\"\n\n@event lesson_rime\ntitle: Cold Enough\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(rime) >= 1 and term and met(kael)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: kael\nart: spell_rime\ntags: magic\nscene: A pale Northern student and another student standing in a frost-whitened courtyard, frost spreading from their collars, breath steaming.\ntext: Kael sees the frost at your collar and goes still. \"Rime,\" he says. \"Where did you... no. Don't tell me.\" He looks at {season == \"winter\": the snow|the courtyard, and frost runs out from his boots across the flagstones}. \"In the North we learn it as children. You don't feel the cold less. You just stop letting it decide things.\"\n> left: Let him show you\n  meet kael\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: He makes you stand in {season == \"winter\": the snow|the cold he's laid on the flagstones} until it hurts, and then until it doesn't, and then a while longer. When you go in, he's almost smiling. \"Now it's yours,\" he says.\n> right: Ask what else they learn as children\n  meet kael\n  rel kael +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Not to want things,\" he says, and then looks as if he wishes he hadn't. He leaves before you can ask anything else. The frost at your collar feels colder.\n\n@event lesson_siphon\ntitle: The Fern on the Sill\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(siphon) >= 1 and term\nslot: night\ntone: grim\nart: spell_siphon\ntags: forbidden\nscene: A student at a dormitory window at night with a hand hovering over a potted fern, the fern's leaves beginning to curl.\ntext: You're tired, and there are lectures at eight, and there's a fern on your windowsill, green and full of life it isn't using. Siphon is right there in your hands. Nobody would ever know but the fern.\n> left: Just a little\n  alignment -small\n  health +small\n  say: The fern greys from the tips inward. You feel wonderful. You throw the fern out in the morning, before anyone sees it.\n> right: Go to bed tired\n  alignment +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: You go to bed tired, and wake tired, and the fern is green. You water it, which feels like an apology to something.\n\n@event lesson_compel\ntitle: \"Again\"\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(compel) >= 1 and term\ntone: grim\nart: spell_compel\ntags: forbidden\nscene: A student in a castle corridor watching another student drop a stack of books, a word forming on their lips.\ntext: A first-year drops a stack of books in the corridor, and laughs at themselves, and you could say the word. \"Again.\" They'd drop them again, and again, and never know why. It would be funny. That's the worst part: it would be funny.\n> left: Say it, once\n  alignment -small\n  guile +small\n  say: They drop the books again, and stare at their hands. You walk on quickly. It wasn't funny.\n> right: Help pick them up\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You pick up half the books and the first-year thanks you twice. The word stays in your mouth, unused, like a stone you could throw.\n\n@event lesson_torment\ntitle: The Shape of It\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(torment) >= 1 and term\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nactor: thorne\nart: spell_torment\ntags: magic forbidden\nscene: A stern grey-bearded professor in a duelling hall looking hard at a student whose wand hand has frozen mid-cast.\ntext: Sparring in the duelling hall, you feel Torment rise in your hand like a reflex. You stop it. Professor Thorne, across the hall, saw something: he's looking at you the way he looks at a fire in the wrong place.\n> left: Tell him\n  meet thorne\n  alignment +small\n  rel thorne +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: You tell him. He listens without a word, then says, \"Then you know what it costs. Most people learn that afterwards.\" He gives you extra drills for a month, which you suspect is kindness.\n  if knows(thorne.lost):\n    rel thorne +tiny\n    say: As you leave he adds that he lost to the storm because he didn't stop in time. You knew already. You've never heard him say it like that.\n> right: Say it was nothing\n  meet thorne\n  guile +tiny\n  rel thorne -tiny\n  say: \"Nothing, sir.\" He doesn't believe you. He doesn't say so. He watches you spar for the rest of the term.\n\n@event lesson_extraction\ntitle: Someone Else's Spark\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(extraction) >= 1 and term\ntone: grim\nart: spell_extraction\ntags: forbidden\nscene: A study group around a library table at night, one student's quill glowing with magic, another student staring at it hungrily.\ntext: In a study group, the student beside you is casting a little light to read by, and you can feel their magic like warmth from a stove. Extraction would take it. They'd feel tired, that's all. You've got so much to do.\n> left: Take a little\n  alignment -small\n  mana +small\n  say: Their light gutters. They yawn, rub their eyes, and go to bed early. You study until dawn on someone else's magic, and remember none of it.\n> right: Lend them yours instead\n  alignment +tiny\n  mana -tiny\n  say: You light the table yourself so they can rest their hand. They grin at you. The hunger goes quiet, for now.\n\n@event lesson_eclipse\ntitle: The Burn\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(eclipse) >= 1 and term\ntone: grim\nart: spell_eclipse\ntags: forbidden\nscene: A student in an alchemy lab clutching a burnt hand, a faint dark glow gathering around the injury.\ntext: You burn your hand on a cauldron in the lab, badly. And Eclipse wakes up: the pain could be power, it says, all of it, held and ready. You'd still feel the burn. You'd just have something to show for it.\n> left: Hold the pain\n  alignment -small\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You let the burn become power and carry it all day like a coal in your pocket. It's very strong. It still hurts. Both of those stay true.\n> right: Go to the infirmary\n  alignment +tiny\n  health +tiny\n  say: Sister Amaranth dresses it and tells you off for daydreaming. The power you didn't take aches a little, like a tooth.\n\n@event lesson_heartprice\ntitle: Paying in Yourself\npool: spell_lessons_auto_routine\nwhen: spell_level(heartprice) >= 1 and term\nslot: night\ntone: grim\nart: spell_heartprice\ntags: forbidden\nscene: An exhausted student at a desk late at night, a faint red glow in their chest feeding a spell, candles burnt down.\ntext: It's late, {mana < 15: your Mana is spent|your Mana is low and tomorrow has a practical}, and the essay isn't done. Heartprice says you don't need Mana. You have yourself. You could pay in that, for as long as you can stand it.\n> left: Pay in yourself\n  alignment -tiny\n  study +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: You finish the essay on your own strength. In the morning your hands shake and the essay is very good. You don't feel proud of it.\n> right: Leave it for tomorrow\n  study -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You go to bed with the essay unfinished. It's worse than it would have been. You're better than you would have been.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/statuses.grim","text":"# Statuses in play (playtest 2, N2, N5, N8): cards that show a state you're in, so it is more than a number at month end.\n\n# ---------------- Hungry ----------------\n\n@pool statuses_routine\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 20\n\n@event hungry_leftovers\ntitle: The Refectory's Leftovers\npool: statuses_routine\ntone: warm\nslot: night\nwhen: has(hungry) and term\nart: meal\ntags: food money\nscene: A refectory kitchen door at closing time, a cook scraping the last of a stew into a bowl for a student waiting in the doorway.\ntext: {The refectory is closing, and the kitchens are scraping the pots. A cook sees you hovering in the doorway and holds up a ladle, eyebrows raised.|The same cook, the same ladle. She doesn't ask any more; she just raises her eyebrows.|You know the kitchens' closing time to the minute now.} {A prefect is still at the end table, finishing an essay.|Two first-years are sweeping, and watching.|The cook is humming, which means there's plenty.}\n> left: Take the bowl\n  health +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {It's the burnt bit at the bottom of the stew and it is the best thing you've ever eaten. The prefect looks up from her essay and remembers your face.|The cook fills the bowl to the brim. The first-years see, and by breakfast half your year knows why.|You know the closing time to the minute, and so does the cook. She fills the bowl before you're through the door, humming.}\n> right: Shake your head and leave\n  health -tiny\n  say: {\"Not hungry,\" you say, to nobody in particular, and go upstairs on an empty stomach that disagrees.|You walk out with your pride and nothing else. Your stomach complains all night.|You know the closing time, and leave a minute before it. The humming follows you up the stairs, and so does the smell.}\n\n@event hungry_pocketed_rolls\ntitle: Rolls for Later\npool: statuses_routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has(hungry) and term\nart: meal\ntags: food money\nscene: A breakfast table heaped with bread rolls, a student's hand hovering near a pocket while a prefect patrols the aisle.\ntext: {The refectory's bread rolls are piled high. Board-payers only, says the sign; it doesn't say how many.|The rolls again, and the sign again, and your empty pockets.|Another meal. You've got good at looking as if you've paid.} {The prefect on duty is reading a letter.|The prefect on duty is looking straight at the bread.|The prefect on duty has gone for more tea.}\n> left: Pocket a few for later\n  health +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {Three rolls in your robe, still warm. They last a day, and so does the guilt.|You fill your pockets and walk out at an even pace, crumbs and all.|Two rolls and an apple. Nobody stops you. You eat them on the stairs, a little ashamed.}\n> right: Eat what's on your plate\n  say: {One roll, eaten slowly, and nothing taken that isn't yours. By the next meal you're light-headed and honest.|You eat what you're given, and the hunger keeps you company for hours.|One roll, slowly, with the look of someone who has paid. The hunger knows better.}\n\n@event hungry_maud_soup\ntitle: Soup at the Cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nactor: maud\nwhen: has(hungry) and met(maud) and term\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 20\nart: cauldron\ntags: food social\nscene: A crooked cottage kitchen full of hanging herbs, an old woman ladling soup from a black pot into a chipped bowl.\ntext: {Old Maud catches you on the path by her cottage. \"You've a hungry look, dearie. Come and eat.\" She has a pot on already, as if she knew.|Maud's pot is on again. She doesn't ask, and you don't explain.} {The soup is leek and something she won't name.|A bee sits on the windowsill, waiting for its share.}\n> left: Stay for soup and a story\n  health +small\n  mood +small\n  rel maud +tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: {Two bowls, bread, and a sad story about her sister told like a recipe. Your reading waits until morning, and it'll be late.|She talks about the winter the lake froze twice while you eat. The essay you meant to write tonight stays unwritten.}\n> right: Take a jar back to your room\n  health +small\n  say: {She fills a jar and wraps it in a cloth. \"Bring the jar back,\" she says. \"That's the price.\" You eat at your desk, studying.|You carry the warm jar up the hill and eat it over your books. Maud waves from the door, a little disappointed.}\n\n# ---------------- Detention ----------------\n\n@event detention_cauldrons\ntitle: Cauldron Duty\n# Served in a detention slot (threads 1): the pool that the detention day draws from.\npool: detention\ntone: light\nart: cauldron\ntags: study\nscene: A dim potions storeroom, a student up to the elbows in a crusted cauldron while a prefect reads by a lamp at the door.\ntext: {Detention, and a row of crusted cauldrons and a prefect by the door with a book.|Detention again. The cauldrons are the same; the crust is worse.|You know these cauldrons by name now. One of them, you suspect, knows you.} {Something in the third one is still bubbling.|The prefect is pretending not to be bored.|The prefect has brought a second book this time.}\n> left: Scrub them properly\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You scrub until your knuckles are raw, and learn which residues bite back and which only sulk.|By the last cauldron you can tell a week-old draught from a month-old one by the smell alone. Your hands sting for days.|You scrub your old acquaintances one by one. The one that knows you gives up its crust almost gently.}\n> right: Talk your way out early\n  challenge appeal apprentice+:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: {You talk about the prefect's book until the prefect is talking about it too, and an hour early they wave you off. \"Same time next week. Possibly.\"|You find out you're both from the same county. The cauldrons can wait for someone less charming.|The prefect is on the second book and glad of the interruption. You talk until they send you off early, and the cauldrons stay crusted another week.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {\"Nice try,\" says the prefect, and hands you the worst cauldron in the row.|The prefect hears you out, then points at the third cauldron, the bubbling one. \"For the speech.\"|The prefect doesn't look up from the second book. \"The one that knows you,\" they say. \"Start there.\"}\n\n@event detention_lines\ntitle: Lines, With Company\npool: detention\ntone: light\ncast:\n  other: any where met(other) and tagged(other, student)\nart: book\ntags: study social\nscene: An empty classroom at dusk, two students at separate desks writing the same sentence over and over, a quill scratching on its own at the front.\ntext: Detention means lines, a hundred of them, and {other} is at the next desk doing the same. {~The sentence is \"I will not bring livestock into the library.\"|The sentence is \"Curfew is not a suggestion.\"|The sentence is \"Stairs are for walking.\"} An enchanted quill at the front of the room writes down everything anyone says.\n> left: Do your homework under the desk\n  challenge deceive apprentice likely:\n    win:\n      study +tiny\n      say: {~A hundred lines and an evening's revision for every course. The quill at the front records nothing but scratching.|You alternate a line and a page of notes. Nobody checks. Your whole week's reading is the better for it.|You write \"Stairs are for walking\" with one hand and a week's notes with the other. The quill at the front records only the stairs.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      study -tiny\n      say: {~The prefect lifts your lines, finds your notes underneath and confiscates them until the end of term.|Your notes fall on the floor at exactly the wrong moment. The prefect keeps them, and makes you start the lines again.|Between the stairs and the notes you lose track. The prefect finds forty lines of notes among the stairs, and you start again.}\n> right: Talk to {other}\n  rel other +small\n  say: {~By the fortieth line you know why {other} is here, and it's a better story than yours. The quill at the front writes it all down, word for word.|You compare crimes. {other} laughs so hard the quill at the front writes \"LAUGHTER\" in capitals.|{other} tells you which staircase is to blame. You laugh until the quill at the front writes \"STAIRS?\" in the margin.}\n\n# ---------------- Probation ----------------\n\n@pool statuses_routine_repeat2\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 20\n\n@event probation_advisor\ntitle: A Word With Your Advisor\npool: statuses_routine_repeat2\ntone: tense\nwhen: has(probation) and term\nart: scales\ntags: study\nscene: A cluttered faculty office with a ticking clock, an advisor and a student either side of a desk with a marks list between them.\ntext: {A summons to your advisor's office. Your marks lie on the desk between you, upside down for you, right way up for them.|Your advisor again, and the same marks, a little better or a little worse.} \"Two terms under the line and the Disciplinary Board sits,\" they say. \"Shall we make sure it doesn't?\"\n> left: Make a study plan together\n  study +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {An hour later you have a timetable that colours every evening grey, and you start on the first evening of it that night. It is a very good timetable. You hate it.|The plan has a column for sleep, and it is narrow. You work through the first week of it with your advisor checking in.}\n> right: Talk about what's going wrong\n  mood +small\n  say: {You say aloud the thing you hadn't said to anyone. Your advisor listens and makes no plan at all. You leave lighter, and no better prepared.|It turns out your advisor failed a year, once. You talk about that instead of your marks.}\n\n@event probation_letter\ntitle: The Warning Letter\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: has(probation) and term\nweight: 20\nart: letter\ntags: study\nscene: A stiff envelope with the academy's seal lying open on a desk, a student holding the letter at arm's length.\ntext: A letter from the registrar, in the stiff envelope everyone dreads: Academic Probation, your name spelled correctly for once, and a line about \"consequences\" underlined twice. A copy, it says, has gone to your family.\n> left: Pin it above your desk\n  study +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: The letter glares at you every time you look up. It works, in the way a headache works: you study, and you don't enjoy a minute of it.\n> right: Put it in a drawer\n  say: Out of sight, mostly out of mind. Mostly. Some nights you open the drawer, look at it and close the drawer again.\n\n# ---------------- Sick or injured ----------------\n\n@event sick_in_the_infirmary\ntitle: A Bed by the Window\npool: statuses_routine_repeat2\ntone: warm\nwhen: has(sick) and term\nart: bed\ntags: health\nscene: A row of white infirmary beds, a student wrapped in blankets with a faintly glowing blue nose and a steaming mug beside them.\ntext: {Lake fever. Your nose glows faintly blue, and Sister Amaranth says \"Bed,\" in the voice that means it.|The fever again, and the same bed by the window.} {Lectures are going on without you.|Someone has left a stack of notes on the chair.}\n> left: Stay in bed all week\n  health +medium\n  study -small\n  say: {You sleep, drink something bitter and sleep again. When you go back, the class is two chapters ahead.|A week of blankets and broth. You feel much better and know much less.}\n> right: Go to lectures in a scarf\n  study +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: {You sniffle through Arcana in three scarves. The notes are good; the coughing fit in the middle is not.|You make every lecture, and every lecture makes you worse. Sister Amaranth sees you on the stairs and says nothing, very loudly.}\n\n@event injured_on_the_stairs\ntitle: The Moving Stairs, Limping\npool: statuses_routine_repeat2\ntone: light\nwhen: has(injured) and term\nart: door\ntags: health\nscene: A grand staircase swinging slowly between landings, a student with a bandaged ankle clinging to the banister as it moves.\ntext: {Your ankle is bandaged and the stairs are moving, as stairs here do. The lecture is three landings up and starts in five minutes.|The stairs again, the ankle again. The lecture, as ever, is at the top.}\n> left: Take the long way, slowly\n  health +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {You limp round by the back corridor and arrive as everyone is packing up. The ankle thanks you; your notes don't.|Slow, careful and very late. The ankle mends a little faster for it.}\n> right: Hop the gap as it swings\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +tiny\n      say: {You time it perfectly and land on one foot. Somebody on the landing applauds.|One hop, one wobble, one bow. You're on time and briefly famous.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {You land on the wrong foot, the bad one. The stairs swing on without you, and you sit on the step for a while.|The gap is wider than it looked. You arrive on time and limping twice as badly.}\n\n# ---------------- An empty purse (N5: a hint, never an obligation) ----------------\n\n@event purse_and_the_board\ntitle: A Light Purse\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: term and gold < 10 and not member(job) and turn > 2\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 16\nart: pouch\ntags: money\nscene: A student emptying a coin purse on a dormitory bed, three coins and a button rolling out across the blanket.\ntext: {You tip out your purse on the bed. Three coins and a button.|The purse again. The button is still there; the coins are fewer.|The purse, the button, and fewer coins than ever. You've stopped counting the button.} Board is due at the start of every month. {On the way to breakfast you passed the Job Board in the entrance hall, and someone was taking down a notice.|Someone in your year says the Newt is hiring again.|There's a hardship notice by the bursar's door, someone says, and professors who pay for errands.}\n> left: Sell your second-best cloak\n  gold +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {A second-hand stall in the village gives you a fair price and an unfair look. The castle is draughty without it.|You sell the cloak. It was your grandmother's. You're nearly sure she'd understand.|The second-hand stall knows you by now. The price is fair, and the look is kinder than last time.}\n> right: Look at the Job Board later\n  say: {You make a note. The Job Board, entrance hall, morning to evening. One job at a time, paid by the shift.|You'll go and read the notices. Probably. Board is due either way.|You'll look at the Job Board, and at the hardship notice by the bursar's door. Board is due either way.}\n\n# ---------------- Familiars (N8): cards instead of a monthly tick ----------------\n\n@pool statuses_routine_weight12\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\n\n@event ember_on_the_essay\ntitle: A Fox on the Essay\npool: statuses_routine_weight12\ntone: light\nwhen: has(familiar_fox) and term\nart: fox\ntags: nature study\nscene: A fox-spirit curled asleep on a student's half-written essay, her tail smouldering gently at one corner of the page.\ntext: {Ember has chosen your week's coursework to sleep on, and her tail is smouldering, as it does when she dreams.|Ember is asleep on your notes again. The corner of every page is already brown.|Ember has a new favourite sleeping place, and all of it is due on Monday.}\n> left: Rescue the essay\n  study +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You slide the pile out from under her. She opens one eye, says something rude in fox and sulks under the bed until supper.|You rescue the pages and lose a fox: she spends the evening on your pillow with her back to you.|You lift her off Monday's work in one piece. She lets you, which is worse: she's planning something.}\n> right: Let her sleep and write around her\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {You write the conclusion in the margin, round a sleeping fox. Your essays come back scorched at one corner and marked down for it.|She's warm, she's purring in her own way, and your notes have a hole in them now. It's worth it.|You write Monday's work round her, a paragraph at a time, while her tail smoulders in the margin. It smells of smoke, and it's marked down for it, and it's in.}\n\n@event ember_night_hunt\ntitle: Ember Goes Hunting\npool: statuses_routine_weight12\ntone: light\nslot: night\nwhen: has(familiar_fox)\nart: fox\ntags: nature mystery\nscene: A dark castle corridor lit only by the glow of a fox's tail, a student in a nightgown following at a distance.\ntext: {Ember tugs at your blanket. Then at your sleeve. Then at your ear. She wants you to come.|Ember is at the door again, tail glowing, waiting.} {Somewhere below, something is scratching.|The corridors are cold and very quiet.}\n> left: Follow her through the castle\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {She leads you down three staircases to a loose stone, and sits by it, very pleased. Behind it, nothing but a draught. You're back at dawn, frozen.|A mouse, two corridors and a door you'd never noticed. You get back to bed cold and much better at walking quietly.}\n> right: Pull the blanket over your head\n  health +tiny\n  say: {You sleep. Ember goes alone and comes back at dawn smelling of the kitchens, enormously smug.|She sighs at you, a fox's sigh, and goes without you. You sleep like a stone.}\n\n@event ember_and_the_sandwich\ntitle: The Prefect's Sandwich\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has(familiar_fox) and term\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 12\nart: fox\ntags: nature social\nscene: A crowded refectory, a small fire-tailed fox trotting off with a sandwich while a prefect stares at an empty plate.\ntext: Ember trots through the refectory with a prefect's sandwich in her jaws, past forty witnesses. The prefect looks at the empty plate, then at the fox, then at the room. \"Whose,\" the prefect says, \"is that?\"\n> left: Own up and buy a new one\n  gold -tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You raise your hand. The room laughs; you pay for a sandwich at the counter and hand it over. The prefect accepts it with dignity. Ember eats the first one under your chair.\n> right: Look as puzzled as everyone else\n  guile +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You look around the room, as baffled as anybody. Ember sits on your foot under the table, chewing. The prefect never finds out, and Ember never lets you forget it.\n\n@event owl_six_oclock\ntitle: \"HOMEWORK\"\npool: statuses_routine_weight12\ntone: light\nslot: night\nwhen: has(familiar_owl) and term\nart: owl\ntags: study\nscene: A brass clockwork owl on a bedpost at dawn, its amber eyes glowing, a student groaning under a pillow.\ntext: {Bedtime. \"HOMEWORK,\" says the clockwork owl, and then, because you haven't moved, \"HOMEWORK. DUE. TOMORROW.\"|\"HOMEWORK,\" says the owl, at bedtime, as ever. This time it's right. A week's exercises are due tomorrow, in every course, and you'd forgotten.|The owl's bedtime hoot has a new word in it: \"LATE.\"}\n> left: Stay up and write it now\n  study +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You write it all by candlelight with your eyes half shut. It's not your best work, but it's in. The owl says \"GOOD,\" which you didn't know it could.|Two hours of frantic quill-work by the last of the candle. You hand it in on time and nod off in the next lecture.|You write until the owl stops saying \"LATE\" and says nothing at all. It goes in on time, just.}\n> right: Hand it in late\n  health +tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: {You go to sleep. The owl says \"DISAPPOINTED\" twice and goes quiet. Your work goes in a day late and is marked accordingly.|You sleep until eight. The owl doesn't speak to you until lunch, and then only to say \"LATE.\"|\"LATE,\" says the owl, and it is. You hand the work in a day after, and the owl says it again at breakfast.}\n\n@event owl_birthday\ntitle: \"BIRTHDAY\"\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  friend: any where met(friend) and rel(friend) >= 10 and tagged(friend, student) and around(friend)\nwhen: has(familiar_owl)\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 12\nart: owl\ntags: social\nscene: A clockwork owl fluttering in front of a student, its beak open mid-hoot, a calendar on the wall behind.\ntext: \"BIRTHDAY,\" says the owl, flapping in front of your face, and then, louder, \"BIRTHDAY.\" It turns its head toward the window, toward the dormitory where {friend} lives. Mir built it to remember birthdays. You had not.\n> left: Buy a proper present\n  gold -small\n  rel friend +small\n  say: You run down to the village before the shops close and come back with something {friend} actually wanted. {They} look{s} at you as if you've always remembered.\n> right: Write a card, quickly\n  rel friend +tiny\n  say: A card, a drawing of the owl and \"Happy birthday\" in your best hand. {friend} pins it up anyway, and asks how you knew.\n\n@event owl_wound_down\ntitle: The Owl Winds Down\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: has(familiar_owl) and met(mir)\nweight: 12\nart: owl\ntags: magic social\nscene: A brass owl slumped on a desk with one wing drooping, a student peering into a small open panel in its chest with a magnifying glass.\ntext: The clockwork owl has started saying \"HOME… WORK…\" very slowly, like a music box running down. One wing droops. There is a tiny panel in its chest, and a tinier key, and absolutely no instructions.\n> left: Take it back to Mir\n  rel mir +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: Mir lights up and spends the whole evening inside the owl with tweezers, explaining every spring. You miss your reading entirely. The owl comes back saying \"HOMEWORK\" faster than ever.\n> right: Fix it yourself\n  challenge enchant apprentice+:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      say: A wound spring, a drop of oil and a charm to keep it turning. The owl shakes itself and says \"THANK YOU,\" which it has never said before.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You fix it. Now it hoots on the half hour instead of the hour, all night, and says \"HOMEWORK\" in a voice like a kettle.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/upper_ranks.grim","text":"# Top-rank pools (playtest 2, X2): at a club's or job's highest rank (`rank: 3`), its cards carry more risk and more\n# reward instead of a quiet stat bump. They join the lower ranks' cards (pools.md); two per track.\n\n# ---------------- Duelling Circle: Captain ----------------\n\n@event duelling_captain_challenge\ntitle: The Captain's Challenge\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: duel\ntags: club duel\nscene: A captain of a duelling circle in a chalk ring facing a visiting champion, the whole hall packed to the rafters.\ntext: {A visiting champion has challenged the Circle's captain, by name, in front of everyone. The captain is you.|Another challenger at the door, asking for the captain.|They come for the captain now, from three valleys away.} {The hall is packed to the rafters.|{around(lyra): Lyra is in the front row with her arms folded.|The old captains are in the front row with their arms folded.}|Somebody is taking bets in the corridor.}\n> left: Accept in front of everyone\n  challenge combat expert drift mid:\n    win:\n      perform duelling +medium\n      renown +medium\n      health -small\n      say: {You win the third exchange and the hall comes apart. The champion bows, properly. The Circle will talk about it for a year.|A long, hard bout, and yours at the end. Your name goes on the board in gold paint.|You accept, with bets being taken in the corridor. You win, and somebody in the corridor wins a great deal more.}\n    lose:\n      perform duelling -small\n      renown -small\n      health -small\n      say: {You lose, in front of everyone, by a breath. The Circle carries you to the infirmary anyway, which is its own verdict.|Beaten, cleanly. The champion is gracious. That's the worst part.|You lose to someone from three valleys away, and the corridor pays out. The Circle buys you a drink with the winnings it didn't make.}\n> right: Send your best officer instead\n  perform duelling +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {Your officer wins, narrowly, and the hall cheers for the Circle. A few people look at you and wonder why you didn't step up.|You coach from the edge. The officer loses but fights well. It was the right call, you think, mostly.|You send your officer. They win, and the corridor's bookmaker grumbles that the captain was the better bet.}\n\n@event duelling_captain_injury\ntitle: The Captain's Call\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 9\nart: duel\ntags: club duel danger\nscene: A duellist kneeling in a chalk circle clutching a wrist, a captain crouching beside them, a referee waiting with a whistle.\ntext: {A first-year in the Circle has twisted a wrist in the final of the house cup, and insists on finishing.|Another final, another stubborn first-year with an injury.|The house cup final again, and again someone hurt who won't stop.} {The house's honour is riding on it.|Their parents are in the gallery.|The referee looks at you: the captain decides.}\n> left: Let them finish\n  chance even:\n    perform duelling +medium\n    renown +small\n    say: {They finish, and win, with one hand. The gallery stands. You stood beside them, and you'll remember the fear more than the win.|They finish and win. Amaranth bandages them afterwards and gives you a look that could strip paint.|The referee looks at you, and you nod. They finish, and win, and you hold your breath through every exchange.}\n  else:\n    perform duelling -medium\n    mood -small\n    say: {They finish and lose, and the wrist is worse. Sister Amaranth sends for you specifically, to explain.|The bout ends with a second injury. You let it happen. You don't sleep that night.|The referee looks at you, you nod, and you're wrong. The wrist goes. You walk them to the infirmary yourself.}\n> right: Stop the bout\n  alignment +tiny\n  perform duelling -small\n  say: {You call it. The house loses the cup and the first-year cries and calls you a coward. A week later, in the corridor, they thank you.|You stop it. The gallery boos. {around(lyra): Lyra, in the front row, nods once.|The referee, pocketing the whistle, nods once.}|The referee looks at you, and you stop it. The first-year doesn't speak to you for a week, then asks you to coach them.}\n\n# ---------------- The Lumen Ledger: Editor ----------------\n\n@event lumen_ledger_editor_scoop\ntitle: The Story That Could Close Us\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: club\nscene: A cramped newspaper office at night, a student editor reading a draft under a lamp, a stack of evidence beside it and the clock showing late.\ntext: {A reporter brings you a story about a governor of the academy, apparently true and well sourced, and dangerous.|Another story with teeth, on your desk with a dawn deadline.|Nessa calls it \"the kind of story that closes papers\". She says it admiringly.} {The evidence is a stack of letters.|The source wants to stay unnamed.|The printer needs the pages by dawn.}\n> left: Run it on the front page\n  challenge research expert:\n    win:\n      perform lumen_ledger +large\n      renown +medium\n      alignment +tiny\n      say: {Every fact holds. The Ledger sells out by breakfast; the governor resigns by supper. Nessa frames the front page.|It runs, it holds, and the castle talks of nothing else for a week.|You run it with the printer waiting, and every fact holds. Nessa says it's the story that closes papers, and it closes someone else's career instead.}\n    lose:\n      perform lumen_ledger -medium\n      renown -small\n      gain detention\n      say: {One fact was wrong, and it's the one they sue over. The Ledger prints a retraction; you write it. Detention, too, for good measure.|It runs, with a mistake in it. The governor's lawyers are very polite, and very thorough.|You run it by dawn, and the dawn is too soon. One fact needed another day. The retraction is yours to write.}\n> right: Hold it until it's airtight\n  perform lumen_ledger +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {You hold it. Another paper in the village runs a thinner version first. Nessa doesn't speak to you for two days, then admits you were right.|You hold it for a month and check every line. When it finally runs, it's bulletproof, and old news.|You hold the presses until it's airtight. The printer charges you for the dawn, and Nessa pays it, grumbling.}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_editor_threat\ntitle: A Letter to the Editor\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nrank: 3\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 8\nart: letter\ntags: club danger\nscene: A black-edged letter open on a newspaper desk, a student editor reading it with a cold face, the window behind them dark.\ntext: {A letter to the editor, unsigned, about a story you ran. It knows where you sleep.|Another unsigned letter, the same careful hand.|The letters have stopped being polite.} {It smells of expensive ink.|The seal has been scraped off.|It's been slipped under the office door, not posted.}\n> left: Print it, with a reply\n  renown +small\n  perform lumen_ledger +small\n  mood -medium\n  say: {You print it in full on page two, with three lines of your own beneath. The castle loves it. You check your door twice every night for a month.|The letter goes in the paper. The next one takes a month to come. The waiting for it is worse.|You print the impolite one, impolitely answered. The letters stop. You don't know whether that's good.}\n> right: Take it to the headmistress\n  meet isolde\n  rel isolde +small\n  perform lumen_ledger -tiny\n  say: {She reads it once and puts it in a drawer. \"Leave it with me.\" For a long while, you don't get another. Nessa says that's censorship. It's also sleep.|The headmistress handles it. You don't ask how. The Ledger is quieter for a while.|The headmistress reads the one slipped under the door and asks who has a key to the office. The locks are changed by evening.}\n\n# ---------------- Verdant Circle: Head Gardener ----------------\n\n@event verdant_head_gardener_blight\ntitle: The Blight\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: club nature\nscene: A greenhouse full of wilting plants, black spots on every leaf, a head gardener holding a sickly seedling up to the light.\ntext: {Blight in the greenhouses, black spots spreading by the hour. The Head Gardener decides what to burn.|The blight is back, in a different house.|Black spots again. You know the smell of it now.} {{around(tobias): Tobias is close to tears.|The Circle's youngest member is close to tears.}|The rarest plant in the collection is at the edge of it.|Maud has come up from the cottage to look.}\n> left: Try an untested cure on everything\n  challenge brew expert unlikely:\n    win:\n      perform verdant +large\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {The cure works, overnight. Every plant lives, even the rare one. Maud says she's never seen it done, and asks for the recipe.|By morning the spots are fading. The Circle sings, badly, in the greenhouse.|Maud watches you try the cure and says nothing until it works. Then she asks for the recipe, and you give it.}\n    lose:\n      perform verdant -medium\n      mood -medium\n      say: {The cure kills what the blight hadn't. You burn the whole house. {around(tobias): Tobias helps|The Circle helps}, and nobody blames you, which is worse.|It fails. You lose half the collection, and all of your confidence for a week.|Maud watches the cure fail and helps you burn what's left. \"Everyone loses a house once,\" she says. \"Once.\"}\n> right: Burn the sick plants now\n  perform verdant +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You burn a third of the house and save the rest. It's the right call and it smells of grief.|You burn the sick ones before it spreads. The rare plant is among them.|Maud nods at what you burn and points at one plant you missed. You burn that too. She was right.}\n\n@event verdant_head_gardener_show\ntitle: The Valley Fair\ntrack: verdant\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 9\nart: flower\ntags: club nature\nscene: A crowded show tent in a village field, prize vegetables on trestle tables, a student head gardener carrying an enormous potted plant toward the judges.\ntext: {The valley's flower show, and the Verdant Circle's entry is yours to choose.|The flower show again; the judges remember you.|The show tent smells of crushed grass and rivalry.} {A farmer's pumpkin is the size of a cart.|Thornwick has sent a team.|The judges are three old women who have seen everything.}\n> left: Enter the night-blooming lily\n  challenge brew adept- unlikely wild:\n    win:\n      perform verdant +medium\n      renown +small\n      gold +small\n      say: {It opens at dusk in front of the judges, as if on cue. First prize, and a purse, and a drawing in the village paper.|The lily wins, narrowly, over a cabbage. Nobody will forget the cabbage's grower's face.|The three old judges watch the lily open and say nothing. Then all three write \"first\" at once.}\n    lose:\n      perform verdant -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {It refuses to open. The cabbage wins. The lily blooms at midnight, for nobody, in your arms.|Second place, to Thornwick. {around(tobias): Tobias says|The Circle says} second is good. It isn't.|The three old judges have seen night-blooming lilies before. They give it a polite second and the cabbage a ribbon.}\n> right: Enter the reliable roses\n  perform verdant +small\n  say: {Third prize for the roses. Solid, respectable, forgettable.|The roses place. The Circle is pleased; the Circle is also bored.|The three old judges approve of roses. Second prize, and a long talk about pruning you'll actually use.}\n\n# ---------------- Library Assistant: Senior Clerk ----------------\n\n@event library_senior_banned_book\ntitle: A Request for a Banned Book\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: job study\nscene: A library desk at closing time, a nervous first-year holding a request slip for a chained book, a senior clerk with the keys looking down at it.\ntext: {A first-year brings you a request slip for a chained book. Senior clerks hold the keys, and the rules say no.|Another request for a chained book, from a student who looks desperate.|They've heard you hold the keys. The slips keep coming.} {The first-year's essay depends on it.|The slip is signed by a professor, perhaps.|The book in question bites.}\n> left: Fetch it, against the rules\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      wage\n      gold +small\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: {You fetch it, watch them read it, and chain it back before closing. Nobody knows. The first-year passes with honours and leaves you a cake with crowns folded under it.|The book goes out and comes back, with a grateful envelope. The rules bent, and didn't break.|You fetch it in gloves. It bites the glove and not the reader. The essay is excellent, and the glove is a loss.}\n    lose:\n      wage short\n      perform library_assistant -medium\n      say: {The head librarian finds the chain unlocked. Half your pay docked and a long, quiet talk.|Caught, with the key in your hand, and docked for it. You're still Senior Clerk; nobody quite knows how.|It bites the reader, and the head librarian sees the bandage. Your pay is docked, and the reader's thanks are muffled.}\n> right: Refuse\n  wage\n  perform library_assistant +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You say no. The first-year leaves in tears. The head librarian, who heard, nods.|The rules hold. So does your conscience, more or less.|You say no, and show them the book from a safe distance. They see it snap at the air and stop asking.}\n\n@event library_senior_night_flood\ntitle: Water in the Stacks\ntrack: library_assistant\nrank: 3\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 8\nart: wave\ntags: job danger\nscene: A flooded lower library level at night, water lapping at the bottom shelves, a senior clerk wading with an armful of books.\ntext: {A pipe bursts in the lower stacks at the end of your shift, and the senior clerk on duty is you.|Water in the stacks again. The pipes are older than the books.|The lower level floods every spring now.} {The rarest books are on the bottom shelf.|The water is rising.|Someone is shouting for help two floors up.}\n> left: Save the rare books first\n  wage overtime\n  perform library_assistant +medium\n  health -medium\n  say: {You carry armful after armful up the stair in freezing water until long after closing. Every rare book saved. Your boots are not.|You save the rarest and lose the common ones, and the head librarian, arriving at last, hugs you, which nobody has ever seen.|You save the rare books and then go up for the shouting. It's a first-year stuck on a ladder. You save them too.}\n> right: Run for the head librarian\n  wage\n  perform library_assistant -tiny\n  say: {By the time she comes, a shelf is lost. \"You did right to fetch me,\" she says, and looks at the shelf.|She takes charge. You carry what she points at.|You run for the head librarian, past the shouting two floors up. She sends you back for the shouter first. She was right.}\n\n# ---------------- The Gilded Newt: Manager ----------------\n\n@event tavern_manager_inspector\ntitle: The Inspector Calls\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: cup\ntags: job money\nscene: A village tavern in the morning, a stern inspector in a long coat running a finger along a bar, a student manager following with a ledger.\ntext: {The licensing inspector is here, unannounced, and the landlady is away. The manager is you.|The inspector again, with the same long coat and longer list.|The inspector nods at you like an old enemy.} {The cellar has a leak.|The ledger has a page you'd rather they didn't read.|There's a goblin asleep in the back booth.}\n> left: Show everything, honestly\n  wage\n  challenge negotiate adept:\n    win:\n      perform tavern +large\n      gold +small\n      say: {The inspector finds the leak, notes it and passes the Newt with a warning. The landlady, back, gives you a bonus and her late mother's tankard.|A clean bill, almost. The inspector shakes your hand.|The inspector finds the goblin in the back booth, who turns out to be licensed. A clean bill, and a very long afternoon.}\n    lose:\n      perform tavern -medium\n      gold -small\n      say: {The fine is steep, the landlady takes a share out of your wages, and her face is steeper.|Three infractions. The till covers most of the fine. Your own wages cover the rest.|The inspector wakes the goblin in the back booth, who has opinions about inspectors. The fine is for the opinions.}\n> right: Steer them away from the cellar\n  wage\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive adept:\n    win:\n      perform tavern +medium\n      say: {The inspector never goes downstairs. You fix the leak that night with your own hands, guiltily.|A clean bill, by omission. You don't tell the landlady how.|You steer the inspector past the back booth and the goblin sleeps on. A clean bill, by the narrowest of margins.}\n    lose:\n      perform tavern -medium\n      gain watched\n      say: {The inspector goes straight to the cellar. The fine doubles for \"obstruction\". Bram hears of it somehow.|Caught steering. The inspector writes your name down in a separate book.|The goblin in the back booth wakes and gives the inspector a tour. Of the cellar.}\n\n@event tavern_manager_grisk_offer\ntitle: An Investor\ntrack: tavern\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 8\nwhen: met(grisk) and not flag(grisk_gone)\nart: pouch\ntags: job money danger\nscene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting at a tavern's best table, a bag of coin and a contract in front of him, a student manager standing opposite.\ntext: {Grisk would like to invest in the Newt, with new chairs and a second fire, \"and a little room upstairs for my associates\".|Grisk again, with a bigger bag of coin.|\"My offer stands,\" says Grisk, \"and grows.\"} {The chairs really are falling apart.|The landlady is away.|The bag of coin is very heavy.}\n> left: Take his money for the chairs\n  wage\n  gold +medium\n  alignment -small\n  perform tavern +small\n  flag grisk_contact\n  find lead_grisk_jetty\n  say: {New chairs, a second fire, a fat \"manager's commission\" in your pocket, and Grisk's associates upstairs on Thursdays. Trade is up. So is the noise, at midnight.|Grisk pays you a finder's cut in a sealed envelope. The Newt has never looked better. The upstairs room has never been busier, and you don't go up there.|You take the heavy bag. Grisk's offer grows into the upstairs, then the cellar, then your evenings. The commission is excellent.}\n> right: Turn him down\n  wage\n  perform tavern -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {\"Your loss,\" says Grisk, and means it as a threat. The chairs stay wobbly.|You say no. Grisk tips his hat and drinks somewhere else for a month, which hurts trade.|You turn the heavier bag down too. Grisk laughs, and says he likes a manager with standards. He'll be back.}\n\n# ---------------- Apothecary Clerk: Assistant Apothecary ----------------\n\n@event apothecary_assistant_poison\ntitle: A Dangerous Prescription\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: bottle\ntags: job alchemy danger\nscene: An apothecary counter, a prescription slip with a dose underlined in red, a young assistant alone behind the counter frowning at it.\ntext: {A prescription comes in from a village healer with a dose ten times too strong. Pell is out. Assistant apothecaries decide.|Another slip with a strange dose. You've learned to read the healer's handwriting, which doesn't help.|A dose that would fell a horse, for an old shepherd.} {The patient's grown son is waiting.|The healer is a day's ride away.|It might be a slip of the pen. It might not.}\n> left: Mix it at a safe dose\n  wage\n  challenge brew adept:\n    win:\n      perform apothecary_clerk +large\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {You work out what the healer meant and mix that. The patient recovers. Pell, back, reads the slip and goes white, then pats your shoulder for a long time.|Right dose, right remedy. The healer writes later to thank you, embarrassed.|You mix the shepherd a dose that wouldn't fell a sparrow. It works. The healer, told, sends Pell a very apologetic letter.}\n    lose:\n      perform apothecary_clerk -medium\n      mood -medium\n      say: {Your safe dose is too weak. The patient recovers slowly, and the son tells the whole village.|You get it nearly right. Nearly is a word you stop using for a while.|You cut the dose so far that it does nothing. The shepherd comes back, unwell, and Pell has to sort it out.}\n> right: Refuse until the healer confirms\n  wage short\n  perform apothecary_clerk +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: {A day's wait. The patient's son shouts at you. The healer confirms the slip was wrong. You were right, and it was a long day.|You send a rider. The answer comes at midnight. You stay up for it.|It was a slip of the pen. The healer confirms by midnight, and you mix the right dose by one.}\n\n@event apothecary_assistant_rival_shop\ntitle: A Shop Across the Square\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 8\nactor: pell\nart: bottle\ntags: job money\nscene: A gleaming new apothecary shop across a village square, its window full of bright bottles, an old apothecary peering at it from his own doorway.\ntext: {A new apothecary has opened across the square, all brass and bright bottles and half Pell's prices.|The shop across the square has a queue now.|Pell has started calling the new shop \"that brass insult\".} {Their remedies are mostly coloured water.|They've hired a singer for the doorway.|Pell's daughters have written to ask what's wrong.}\n> left: Expose their coloured water\n  wage\n  challenge research adept-:\n    win:\n      perform apothecary_clerk +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: {You buy a bottle of everything and test them in Pell's back room. The results go on a board in the window. The brass shop closes by the season's end.|Your tests are fair and public. The village comes back to Pell, a little sheepish.|You write to Pell's daughters with your test results. They come home for a week and help you nail the board to the window.}\n    lose:\n      perform apothecary_clerk -small\n      mood -small\n      say: {Their remedies turn out to work, annoyingly. You look petty. Pell says, \"Well, now we know.\"|Inconclusive, and everyone saw you try.|Pell's daughters write back that the brass shop's remedies are fine and that you should worry about Pell's. You do.}\n> right: Lower Pell's prices\n  wage short\n  perform apothecary_clerk +small\n  say: {Cheaper remedies, longer hours, a full shop. Pell grumbles and is secretly delighted.|You match them on price and beat them on care. It's slow, and it works.|You lower the prices and write to the daughters that all is well. It is, nearly. Pell reads your letter over your shoulder and doesn't object.}\n\n# ---------------- Broom Courier: Chief Courier ----------------\n\n@event courier_chief_urgent_crossing\ntitle: The Urgent Crossing\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: job danger\nscene: A courier on a broom flying through a mountain pass in a gale, a red-sealed package clutched to their chest, lightning on the peaks.\ntext: {A red-sealed package for the far side of the mountains, by tomorrow, and only the chief courier flies red seals.|Another red seal, another gale over the pass.|The red seals come to you by name now.} {The pass is in cloud.|A storm is coming in from the west.|The package is humming.}\n> left: Fly the pass tonight\n  wage overtime\n  challenge feat expert wild:\n    win:\n      perform broom_courier +large\n      renown +small\n      health -small\n      say: {Through the pass in the dark, lightning on both sides. You deliver at dawn to a pale woman who breaks the seal and weeps with relief. The bonus is the biggest of your life.|You make it, frozen, at dawn. The recipient signs with shaking hands.|The humming package hums louder in the pass and the wind stays off you. You deliver at dawn and don't ask what it was.}\n    lose:\n      perform broom_courier -medium\n      health -large\n      say: {The gale takes you into a snowfield. You walk the last mile with the package and a broken wrist, and arrive late.|You crash short of the pass and are found by a goatherd. The package arrives a day late, via goat.|The humming package hums a note the lightning likes. You land hard and walk it in, late, with the hair on your arms standing up.}\n> right: Wait for the storm to pass\n  wage\n  perform broom_courier -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You wait out the storm and fly at dawn. A day late. The recipient is gracious, and the loft-master isn't.|Late but whole. You'll always wonder what was in it.|You wait out the storm with the humming package on the table. It hums all night. You don't sleep.}\n\n@event courier_chief_new_runner\ntitle: The New Runner\ntrack: broom_courier\nrank: 3\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 9\nart: feather\ntags: job\nscene: A courier loft at dawn, a nervous young runner holding a broom the wrong way round, a chief courier showing them the grip.\ntext: {The loft has a new runner, younger than you were, who holds the broom like a mop.|Another new runner, another bad grip.|The new runners all ask for you now.} {Their first route is across the lake.|They've never flown in rain.|Their parents are watching from the gate.}\n> left: Fly their first route with them\n  wage short\n  perform broom_courier +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You fly beside them across the lake, calling the wind. They land shaking and grinning. They'll name a broom after you, they say.|A slow route, flown twice. They'll be good, one day.|You fly their first route with their parents watching from the gate. They land perfectly and pretend it was nothing.}\n> right: Let them learn on their own\n  wage\n  perform broom_courier +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {They come back soaked and late, with every parcel. You were the same.|They learn the hard way. So did you.|You let them go alone with their parents watching. They wobble, recover, and wave to the gate. You breathe again.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/village_home.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pool: the village below the castle and the family\n#  home (break plan \"home\"). Repeatable routine events.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Village ----------------\n\n@pool village_routine\nplace: village\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event village_barge_crossing\ntitle: The Barge Across\npool: village_routine\ntone: warm\nart: wave\ntags: life\nscene: A flat-bottomed barge crossing a mirror-still lake, the seven towers shrinking behind it.\ntext: {The village barge is slower than walking, if you could walk on water.|The bargeman has started nodding at you like a regular.|The bargeman saves you the dry end of the bench.} {season == \"winter\": Ice ticks against the hull.|{season == \"summer\": Dragonflies ride the bow for free.|{A first-year is being sick, very politely.|Two prefects are pretending not to hold hands.|Someone is reading aloud to a crate of chickens.}}}\n> left: Read on the crossing\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You finish a chapter and miss the towers turning gold behind you.|You read the same paragraph four times; the lake keeps interrupting.|You look up only when the hull bumps the quay, and feel you've skipped something.}\n> right: Chat with the bargeman\n  charm +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {He tells you the lake was rougher in his father's day, and expects a tip for the history.|He has opinions about every tower and charges for none of them, except in tips.|He asks after your exams as if they were a sick relative. You tip him for the sympathy.}\n\n@event village_square_errand\ntitle: One Thing in the Square\npool: village_routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student) and around(friend)\nscene: A crooked village market square, two students weaving between stalls, one already carrying too many parcels.\ntext: {friend} has come down to the village with you \"for one thing\", which turns out to be several. {First, a hat.|You've learned that \"one thing\" means at least three.|You've stopped asking what the one thing is.} {A stallholder is selling candied chestnuts in paper twists.|A tinker is mending a cauldron with a great deal of swearing.|The market cat has chosen {friend} and will not be moved.}\n> left: Carry the parcels\n  rel friend +small\n  health -tiny\n  queue village_errand_parcel in 2 with friend chance unlikely\n  say: {You come back up the hill with an armful of string-tied parcels and sore wrists.|{friend} buys you a chestnut as payment. It is not enough payment.|One parcel clinks suspiciously. You decide not to ask.}\n> right: Talk {them} out of it\n  charm +small\n  rel friend -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  chance likely:\n    queue village_errand_third_thing in 1 with friend chance unlikely\n    say: {You talk {friend} out of the third thing. {They} sulk{s} the whole way back to the barge.|You win. {friend} says you'd make a terrible shopkeeper and a worse friend, then takes your arm anyway.|You talk {friend} out of the third thing. The market cat follows {them} home anyway, which is a sort of purchase.}\n  else:\n    say: Your argument is excellent. {friend} buys the thing anyway, and now you've been wrong in public.\n\n@event village_last_barge\ntitle: The Last Barge\npool: village_routine\nslot: evening\ntone: tense\nart: lantern\ntags: life\nscene: A lamplit village quay at dusk, the last barge pulling at its rope, a student hesitating at the edge of the square.\ntext: {The last barge back leaves at the bell, and the bell is ringing.|The last-barge bell, and you're nowhere near the quay. The bargeman knows your running by now.|The bell. Of course the bell.} {season == \"winter\": Snow is coming down thick over the square.|{The inn has started a song with too many verses.|The lamps are only half lit.|The lake has gone the colour of pewter.}} {final_year: Not many evenings like this are left.|You could run. You could stay.}\n> left: Run for it\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You make it with a leap and one wet boot.|You make it; your scarf does not.|The bargeman holds the rope a heartbeat longer than he should. You owe him.}\n> right: Stay a little longer\n  mood +small\n  chance even:\n    if gold > 0:\n      gold -small\n      say: {A fisherman rows you back for a ruinous fare and a story you didn't ask for.|You pay the night ferryman far too much. He rows like he has somewhere better to be.|The fisherman who rows you back knows you by now and charges you the regular's rate, which is still a great deal.}\n    else:\n      health -small\n      say: You have no fare for the night ferryman, so you sleep on a bench under the Newt's eaves and wake with a cat on your feet.\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: {You sleep on a bench under the Newt's eaves and wake up with a cat on your feet.|The first barge of morning finds you yawning on the quay.|You sleep under the Newt's eaves again. The cat has started saving you the warm end of the bench.}\n\n# ---------------- The Gilded Newt ----------------\n\n@pool gilded_newt_routine\nplace: gilded_newt\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event gilded_newt_fireside\ntitle: By the Newt's Fire\npool: gilded_newt_routine\ntone: warm\nart: flame\ntags: life\nscene: A low-beamed tavern with a roaring hearth, a student tucked into the corner seat beside it with a steaming cup.\ntext: {The Gilded Newt's fire has never gone out, and the landlady says it never will while she lives.|The landlady chalks your name on her slate before you've ordered.|Your corner by the fire is free. The regulars leave it for you now.} {season == \"winter\": Snow hisses off the boots of everyone who comes in.|{A dog is asleep with its paws in the hearth.|Someone has hung wet socks on the fire-irons.|A farmer is explaining turnips to nobody.}}\n> left: Order a cider and stay\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {The cider is warm and spiced and costs exactly what the landlady says it does.|You stay until your cheeks are red and your worries are smaller.|The landlady brings the cider without being asked, and charges for it all the same.}\n> right: Work by the firelight\n  lore +small\n  say: {You get two pages done between three toasts and a fiddle.|The noise is terrible, and somehow you concentrate better than in the library.|A regular reads over your shoulder and corrects your spelling of \"wyvern\".}\n\n@event gilded_newt_card_game\ntime: evening night\ntitle: Cards at the Newt\npool: gilded_newt_routine\ntone: tense\nart: cards\ntags: money\nscene: A smoky back room in a tavern, a card game around a scarred table and one empty chair.\ntext: {There's a card game in the back room of the Gilded Newt, and an empty chair.|The empty chair in the back room has a cushion on it now, as if it's been waiting.|{flag(newt_cards_played): The card players greet you by the name of your worst hand.|The card players nod you towards the chair as if you were a regular.}} {Tonight it's a tinker, a widow and a man who shuffles like a conjuror.|Tonight the stakes are copper and pride.|Tonight a second-year from Duskgate is losing very cheerfully.}\n> left: Take the empty chair\n  hint: The stake is a few crowns a hand.\n  gold -small\n  flag newt_cards_played\n  challenge deceive apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      gold +medium\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {You win a handful of crowns and a reputation for an honest face.|You win, and the widow asks where you learned to bluff like that.|The Duskgate second-year loses to you as cheerfully as to everyone. You win a few crowns and an invitation to lose them back.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You lose your crowns slowly, and then all at once.|The tinker takes your last coin and buys you a drink with it, which somehow makes it worse.|The Duskgate second-year wins for once, delighted. You lose your stake and keep your good humour, mostly.}\n> right: Watch for the tells\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You spend the evening watching. The man who shuffles like a conjuror scratches his ear when he lies.|You learn a great deal about bluffing and nothing about having fun.|Someone asks if you're playing or haunting. You leave before you have to answer.}\n\n@event gilded_newt_corner_table\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Corner Table\npool: gilded_newt_routine\ntone: light\nart: cup\ntags: social\ncast:\n  here: present(place) where met(here) and tagged(here, student)\nscene: A tavern corner table with a half-empty jug of cider, someone familiar waving a student over.\ntext: {here} is at the corner table with {a jug of cider and no plans to leave.|the house jug, which the landlady refills for whoever sits there.|the table's jug. The landlady nods at you as you come in, as if you're expected.} \"{Sit down, you're blocking the fire.|You look like you need a drink. I look like I need company.|Don't tell anyone you saw me here.}\" {season == \"winter\": Snow is piling up against the window.|The window is open to the night.}\n> left: Sit down for one drink\n  rel here +small\n  gold -tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {One drink becomes three stories and a long walk to the barge.|{here} tells you something true halfway down the jug, then pretends not to have.|You stay later than you meant to. It's worth the morning.}\n> right: Wave and keep walking\n  lore +small\n  rel here -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You wave and get back to your book. {here} toasts your back, a little hurt.|{term: You plead an early lecture. It's even true, and you read for it before bed.|You plead an early start, and read in your room instead.}|You wave. {here} raises the jug at you as if you'll be back. You will.}\n\n# ---------------- Mabel's Tea Shop ----------------\n\n@pool mabels_routine\nplace: mabels\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event mabels_the_door\ntitle: Mabel Reads the Door\npool: mabels_routine\ntone: light\nart: cup\ntags: life\nscene: A tiny tea shop crammed with tins, the shop-bell over the door still swinging, a shrewd woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin.\ntext: {Mabel looks up as you open the door and is already reaching for a tin.|Mabel watches you open the door the way a doctor watches a cough.|You've tried opening Mabel's door every way you can think of. She still knows.} {The bell over the door rings a little flat today.|There's a queue of two, which for Mabel's is a crowd.|A cake is cooling on the counter, out of reach and smug about it.}\n> left: Let Mabel choose your tea\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {She brings you something smoky and says, \"For the week you've had.\" You hadn't told her about the week.|She brings you something with rose in it and won't explain why. It helps.|It's mint. It's always mint when you've been lying to yourself, apparently.}\n> right: Fake a cheerful entrance\n  challenge deceive journeyman:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: {She brings you the cheerful tea and waves your coins away, \"for the performance\". It's so good you actually cheer up, which may have been her plan all along.|Mabel looks fooled, and doesn't charge you. Then she adds a biscuit, which means she wasn't.|You've practised the door. Mabel gives you the cheerful tea and a long look, and charges you for neither.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {She brings you a pot labelled, in her own hand, \"for people pretending\". She doesn't charge for it, which is somehow worse.|\"Nice try, dear,\" says Mabel, and brings the strong one, on the house.|Mabel watches your cheerful entrance the way a doctor watches a cough, and brings the strong one, free.}\n\n@event mabels_window_seat\ntitle: The Window Seat\npool: mabels_routine\ntone: warm\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A window seat in a tea shop looking across a still lake to seven distant towers, a teapot and open notes on the sill.\ntext: {Mabel's window seat looks out over the lake to the castle, and it's free.|Mabel has stopped asking if you're waiting for someone at the window seat.|Your window seat. Mabel puts a cushion on it when she sees you on the quay.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has drawn ferns on the glass.|{season == \"spring\": Blossom keeps landing on the sill.|{The towers look small from here, almost friendly.|Rain streaks the glass and blurs the towers.|Below, the barges come and go, slow as thoughts.}}}\n> left: Make one pot last all day\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You get through a whole chapter on the one free pot Mabel gives window-seat readers, under her increasingly pointed looks.|By the third hour the tea is cold and Mabel is dusting very close to your table.|You finish your notes. Mabel says nothing, which is worse.}\n> right: Order cake with it\n  mood +small\n  gold -small\n  say: {flag(mabel_lemon): {Mabel has started cutting the lemon cake before you sit down.|Mabel brings the lemon without asking. You eat it slowly while the barges come and go.|The lemon cake, the window, the lake. The work can wait.}|Mabel has three cakes. You pick the lemon, and it's worth every crown.}\n  flag mabel_lemon\n\n@event mabels_forty_teas\ntitle: Forty Kinds of Tea\npool: mabels_routine\ntone: warm\nart: cup\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student) and around(friend)\nscene: Two students at a small tea-shop table crowded with mismatched teapots, one writing in a notebook.\ntext: {friend} wants to try one of Mabel's forty teas, and Mabel insists on choosing. {Today she starts a chart of who has tried what.|Mabel's chart of your friends' teas is pinned behind the counter now; {friend}'s column gets a tick.|Mabel's chart runs to a second sheet. {friend}'s column is the shortest, and Mabel looks at it pointedly.} {Mabel pretends not to be interested.|Mabel has opinions about the order.|Mabel insists on the order, and on the chart.}\n> left: Pay for both pots\n  gold -small\n  rel friend +small\n  say: {{friend} pronounces today's tea \"bracing\", which means awful. You drink yours anyway.|You pay. Mabel marks you on the chart as a patron of the arts.|{friend} declares it the best tea in the village, then sniffs yours to be sure.}\n> right: Let {friend} pay this time\n  mood +tiny\n  rel friend -tiny\n  say: {{friend} pays, and calls you unadventurous for ordering your usual. Your tea is perfect, thank you.|\"Coward,\" says {friend} fondly, paying for your usual and their something purple.|{friend} pays, grumbling, and drinks something that smells of bonfires and looks betrayed by it.}\n\n# ---------------- Grimsby's Books ----------------\n\n@pool grimsbys_books_routine\nplace: grimsbys_books\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event grimsbys_wrong_book\ntitle: Not the Book You Wanted\npool: grimsbys_books_routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean together, an elderly bookseller on a ladder triumphantly holding up the wrong book.\ntext: {You ask Mr Grimsby for a textbook. He vanishes into the shelves and comes back with something else entirely.|You ask Mr Grimsby for one thing. He brings you another, with great confidence.|Mr Grimsby sees you coming and is already halfway up a ladder, looking for the wrong book.} {Today's is a cookery book with a whole chapter on eels.|Today's is a history of a town that may not exist.|Today's is a guide to the moods of weather.}\n> left: Buy what he found\n  gold -small\n  lore +small\n  say: {It's useless and wonderful. You read half of it on the barge.|You learn more than you meant to about something you never meant to learn.|There's a pressed flower in it, and a name you don't know on the flyleaf.}\n> right: Search the shelves yourself\n  challenge research apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: {You find it behind a stack of almanacs, exactly where he said it wasn't, and read the chapter you need at the counter.|You find it. Mr Grimsby looks wounded and lets you read it at the counter for free, since you found it yourself.|You find it yourself, above the weather guides. Mr Grimsby insists it's a weather guide too, in a way.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {A shelf leans on you for support. You hold it up for a long time.|An afternoon, a sneezing fit, and no book.|You search and find only more guides to the moods of weather. The weather today is a sulk.}\n\n@event grimsbys_damp_barrow\ntitle: The Damp Barrow\npool: grimsbys_books_routine\ntone: warm\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A wooden barrow of damp secondhand books outside a crooked bookshop, a student reading one in the drizzle.\ntext: {Outside Grimsby's there's a barrow of secondhand books, a coin apiece, slightly damp.|The barrow has a new sign: \"NO READING WITHOUT BUYING\". Nobody obeys it.|The barrow. You know its smell now: rain, glue and other people's thoughts.} {season == \"winter\": Someone has thrown a sack over it against the sleet.|{The top book is about knots.|A cat is asleep on the poetry.|A card in the corner says \"NOT RUDE, JUST OLD\".}}\n> left: Buy a damp book\n  gold -tiny\n  lore +small\n  say: {It dries out wavy and full of someone else's underlinings.|The margins are better than the book. Someone argued with every page.|The last page is a shopping list in faded ink. You find it oddly moving.}\n> right: Read it standing in the rain\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You read until your feet are cold and Mr Grimsby taps on the window.|You read a whole chapter in the drizzle and don't buy it. You feel like a thief and a scholar at once.|A previous owner has written \"Don't believe this bit\" in the margin. You don't.}\n\n@event grimsbys_last_copy\ntitle: The Last Copy\npool: grimsbys_books_routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: social\ncast:\n  friend: any where rel(friend) >= 6 and tagged(friend, student) and around(friend)\nscene: Two students in a cramped bookshop, both holding the same book, neither letting go.\ntext: There's one copy left of the book you need, and {friend} has {their} hand on it too. {You both laugh. Neither of you lets go.|This keeps happening to you at Grimsby's. You're starting to suspect the shop.|Mr Grimsby swears he ordered two. It never arrives as two.} {\"I saw it first,\" {they} say{s}.|\"We could share,\" {they} offer{s}, unconvincingly.|Mr Grimsby says there might be another in the cellar. There won't.}\n> left: Let {them} have it\n  rel friend +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {{friend} promises to lend it to you \"very soon\". You know what that means.|You let go. {friend} buys you a bun on the way back, which nearly makes up for it.|{friend} reads it on the barge and tells you all the best parts, in the wrong order.}\n> right: Haggle for it\n  gold -tiny\n  challenge negotiate vs friend.charm:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      rel friend -tiny\n      say: {Mr Grimsby takes your side for reasons of his own. {friend} won't speak to you until the quay.|You win it fair and square, and {friend} says so in a voice that means the opposite.|You haggle, and win. Mr Grimsby promises {friend} the second copy he ordered. You both know it won't come.}\n    lose:\n      gold +tiny\n      rel friend -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {{friend} outbids you, and Mr Grimsby hands your crowns back with a sympathetic look.|You argue so long that a third-year buys it while you're both talking. Mr Grimsby gives your crowns back.|{friend} wins, and Mr Grimsby promises you the second copy. It never arrives as two.}\n\n# ---------------- Pell & Daughters ----------------\n\n@pool pell_apothecary_routine\nplace: pell_apothecary\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event pell_apothecary_drawers\ntitle: A Thousand Drawers\npool: pell_apothecary_routine\ntone: light\nart: bottle\ntags: study\nscene: An apothecary wall of a thousand tiny labelled drawers, brass handles gleaming, a student reading the labels on tiptoe.\ntext: {Pell & Daughters has a thousand drawers, each labelled in a different hand.|You find a drawer you'd swear wasn't there last time.|You've read half the labels in the shop. The other half seem to be reading you.} Today it's {\"Moonwort. Do not hum near.\"|\"Dried courage, low quality.\"|\"Owl pellets (sorted).\"} {season == \"winter\": The shop smells of camphor and wet wool.|The shop smells of mint and something burnt.}\n> left: Buy a pinch of it\n  gold -tiny\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {One of the daughters weighs it on scales finer than an eyelash and tells you what it's really for.|It smells of the shop all the way back across the lake.|Old Pell counts the grains himself and warns you about three things you hadn't planned to do.}\n> right: Just read the labels\n  lore +small\n  say: {Old Pell watches you over his spectacles the whole time, as if labels could be stolen.|You copy a dozen labels into your notebook. A daughter asks if you're planning to open a rival shop.|You learn a great deal. Old Pell sighs every time you touch a handle.}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_queue\ntitle: Symptoms in the Queue\npool: pell_apothecary_routine\ntone: light\nart: cross\ntags: social\nscene: A queue of villagers at an apothecary counter, all turning to a student in academy robes.\ntext: {The queue at Pell & Daughters notices your academy robes. A woman shows you her rash.|Word has got round the queue at Pell's that you're \"the young doctor\".|The queue parts for you as if you were a physician. You are not a physician.} {A farmer has a cough like a door hinge.|A boy has a frog stuck to his hand, and the frog seems content.|An old man wants something for \"the usual\", and won't say what the usual is.}\n> left: Offer some advice\n  challenge brew apprentice:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {Your advice is sound. One of the daughters nods, which in this shop is a medal.|The farmer tries your remedy and comes back to thank you. Old Pell grumbles about lost trade.|You guess the old man's \"usual\" from the way he walks. Old Pell grunts, which is agreement.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {Old Pell corrects you in front of the whole queue, kindly, which is worse.|Your advice would have worked on a horse. A daughter says so.|You guess the old man's \"usual\" wrong, out loud. He tells the queue what it actually is. Everyone wishes he hadn't.}\n> right: Study a drawer intently\n  guile +small\n  say: {You pretend to be fascinated by a drawer of buttons until the queue moves on.|You say \"Hm\" like Professor Thorne and leave quickly.|You become very interested in the floor. The woman with the rash tells her friend you were rude.}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_tonic\ntitle: The Daughters' Tonic\npool: pell_apothecary_routine\ntone: light\nart: potion\ntags: life\nscene: Two apothecary's daughters leaning over a counter, offering a student a small bottle of something that fizzes.\ntext: {One of Pell's daughters has a new tonic and needs someone to try it. Her father must not know.|The daughters have another tonic, and a notebook with your name at the top of a column.|The daughters see you and reach under the counter before you've said hello.} {This one is green and fizzes.|This one smells of pears and thunder.|This one is clear, which they say is the worrying kind.}\n> left: Drink it down\n  chance likely:\n    health +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {You feel wonderful, and briefly taller. The daughters take notes.|Your cold vanishes. So, for an hour, does your sense of smell.|The clear one does nothing at all, then everything at once. You feel marvellous. The daughters look nervous.}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    say: {You hiccup bubbles all the way back to the barge.|Your ears go warm and stay warm. The daughters take very interested notes.|The clear one turns your tongue blue. The daughters write \"clear: not clear\" in the notebook.}\n> right: Buy a proper remedy\n  gold -small\n  health +small\n  say: {Old Pell sells you something brown and reliable. The daughters look disappointed in you.|You buy the dull, tested thing. It works, dully.|A daughter wraps it and murmurs that you'll be sorry you missed the green one.}\n\n# ---------------- The Bakery ----------------\n\n@event bakery_bun_queue\ntitle: The Bun Queue\npool: village_routine\ntone: warm\nart: meal\ntags: life\nscene: A queue of villagers and students outside a little bakery at dawn, steam and the smell of cinnamon pouring out of the door.\ntext: {There's a queue outside the bakery for the afternoon batch, and it smells like a promise.|The baker's son counts heads in the bun queue and knows you by your scarf.|The bun queue. You know everyone in it by their order.} {season == \"winter\": Breath steams in the cold.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves skitter round everyone's ankles.|{The baker's son is singing off-key.|A goat has joined the queue and nobody has the heart to send it away.|The queue is swapping recipes it will never use.}}}\n> left: Buy a bag for your corridor\n  gold -small\n  renown +small\n  say: {You come back with a warm bag and are, for one evening, the most popular person in your house.|The buns are gone before you've taken your coat off. Someone leaves you a thank-you note on a napkin.|A prefect takes one \"to check it for contraband\". It passes.}\n> right: Take a broken bun from the free tray\n  mood +small\n  say: {The baker's son gives you one that came out lopsided. You eat it on the quay while it's still too hot. Worth every burnt finger.|A broken bun, free: butter, cinnamon and the lake in the afternoon. Nothing has ever been better.|The free tray has one left. You eat it slowly and think about nothing at all.}\n\n@event bakery_oldest_baker\ntitle: The Oldest Baker\npool: village_routine\ntone: warm\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A very old woman in a flour-dusted apron sitting by warm bread ovens, looking up hopefully as the shop door opens.\ntext: The oldest of the bakers sits by the ovens, too old to knead and too stubborn to leave. {She asks, as she asks every student, whether the post at the castle is reliable these days.|She asks about the castle post. Her daughter gives you a look that says: just answer.|\"Is the post reliable?\" she asks. You've learned the answer she likes.} {The ovens tick as they heat.|Flour hangs in the light.|The bell over the door rings, and she looks up, every time.}\n> left: Sit with her a while\n  lore +small\n  say: {She tells you about her grandmother's grandmother, who waited for a letter. She doesn't say how it ended.|She tells you the family has baked here for two hundred years. She makes it sound like a promise kept.|She falls asleep mid-story. Her daughter tucks a bun into your pocket and says thank you.}\n> right: Help knead the dough\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {The dough fights back. Your arms ache by the time the trays go in.|The baker shows you the turn of the wrist. You don't have it yet.|You knead until your shoulders burn. The first loaf out has your thumbprint on it.}\n\n@event bakery_cooling_rack\ntitle: The Cooling Rack\npool: village_routine\ntone: light\nart: door\ntags: life\nscene: A tray of steaming buns cooling on the sill of an open bakery window, a crow and a student both eyeing it.\ntext: {Behind the bakery a window stands open, and on the sill a tray of buns is cooling, unwatched.|The cooling tray is back on the sill, nearer the edge this time. The bakers either trust everyone or are testing you.|The tray, the sill, the open window. It's practically an invitation.} {A crow is eyeing it too.|Two first-years are pretending not to look at it.|Steam curls off the tray into the cold air.}\n> left: Lift one off the sill\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive apprentice:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: {You get away with it. It tastes slightly of guilt and mostly of cinnamon.|Clean away. The crow watches you with professional respect.|You lift one through the steam. Nobody sees except the crow, who takes the next.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      if gold > 0:\n        gold -small\n        say: {A floury hand appears from the window, palm up. You pay double.|Caught by the baker's son, who makes you pay double and tells everyone in the queue.|The steam gives you away. A floury hand takes your coins, twice over.}\n      else:\n        mood -small\n        say: A floury hand appears from the window, palm up. Your pockets are empty, so you spend an hour scrubbing trays instead.\n> right: Ask for yesterday's\n  charm +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The baker gives you a bag of yesterday's for nothing. They're as hard as roof slates.|You get the broken ones free. You eat one and lose a little faith in bread.|\"For the student who asks,\" says the baker, and hands you a stale one with a wink.}\n\n# ---------------- Home: the family cottage ----------------\n\n@pool home_cottage_routine\nplace: home_cottage\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\ntags: home\n\n@event home_cottage_old_room\ntitle: Your Old Room\npool: home_cottage_routine\ntone: warm\nart: bed\nscene: A small childhood bedroom under the eaves, a too-short bed, a window looking out over fields.\ntext: {Your old room is exactly as you left it, except smaller.|Someone has been storing apples in your old room.|Your old room has started to feel like a guest room, and you like it less for that.} {season == \"winter\": There's frost on the inside of the window, as always.|The window won't shut properly in the heat, as always.} Downstairs, the house is busy without you.\n> left: Nap the afternoon away\n  health +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You sleep like a stone. Downstairs, someone has done all your chores, loudly.|You wake to the smell of supper and a pointed silence.|You sleep through the afternoon. You feel human, and slightly ashamed.}\n> right: Go down and help\n  mood +small\n  say: {final_year: {You go down and realise there won't be many more afternoons like this.|You memorise the kitchen without meaning to.|You go down and help, and try to learn how every sound in the house goes, for later.}|{You help with the fire. Nobody says much. Nobody needs to.|The creaky stair announces you. Someone hands you a cup before you've asked.|You go down and are handed the job you always had. Your hands know it before you do.}}\n\n@event home_cottage_list\ntitle: The List on the Table\npool: home_cottage_routine\ntone: light\nart: paper\nscene: A kitchen table with a long handwritten list of chores weighted down by a teapot, a student in academy robes reading it with dismay.\ntext: {&Home has remembered that you know where everything is.|A day home, and already a list.|The list. Your name, underlined.} {has(origin_noble): {&The steward wants the ledgers checked.|The steward wants the tenants' letters answered.|The steward wants you to sit in on a dispute about a fence.}|{has(origin_merchant): {&The shop's stock needs counting.|The shop sign needs repainting.|The shop's ledger has a column nobody can explain.}|{has(origin_hedge): {&Your grandmother wants herbs hung to dry.|Your grandmother wants every jar labelled.|Your grandmother wants the bees told the news.}|{&The woodpile needs splitting.|The hen house door argues.|The well rope has frayed.}}}}\n> left: Work through the whole list\n  if has(origin_noble):\n    lore +tiny\n  else:\n    vigor +tiny\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {&By supper the list is done, and someone you love says \"Well.\" It's the highest praise in the house.|It takes all day. You sleep better than you have all term.|You finish, and the list grows a second page in a different hand.}\n> right: Do it with magic\n  challenge enchant using arcana apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: {&The work does itself while you supervise from a chair. The family is impressed and slightly unnerved.|It works. Your mother says it's cheating, then asks if you can do the gutters.|It works so well that a neighbour asks to borrow you.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {&The spell goes slightly wrong. Something is now upside down that wasn't before.|Soot. Everywhere. Someone laughs until they cry, then hands you a broom.|The spell does the list twice, including the things that were already done.}\n\n@event home_cottage_after_supper\ntitle: The Same Question\npool: home_cottage_routine\ntone: warm\nart: candle\nscene: A family kitchen after supper by candlelight, dishes drying, everyone turned towards one student.\ntext: After supper, when the dishes are done, someone always asks it. {\"So what will you do, after?\"|\"Still don't know what you'll do after?\" An aunt, this time.|By now you could ask it yourself, in any of their voices, and someone does.} {final_year: This time the answer is close enough to touch.|{The fire pops.|Nobody pretends not to be listening.|Someone pours more tea to give you time.}}\n> left: Tell them the truth\n  charm +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You say you don't know. It's quiet for a moment, then someone says that's allowed.|You tell them what you're afraid of. It sounds smaller said aloud at this table.|The truth makes your mother go quiet, and then hug you, which you weren't ready for.}\n> right: Give the polished answer\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You give the answer you'd give a professor. They're proud. It sits oddly in your chest all night.|You make it sound like a plan. Everyone relaxes except you.|\"Very sensible,\" says an aunt. You feel as if you've told her a story.}\n\n# ---------------- Home: the fields ----------------\n\n@pool home_fields_routine\nplace: home_fields\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\ntags: home\n\n@event home_fields_long_rows\ntitle: The Long Rows\npool: home_fields_routine\ntone: warm\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A student walking alone between long furrowed rows towards a low wall at the edge of the fields, the horizon wide and empty.\ntext: {You walk the long rows to the field's end, where you used to imagine towers.|You've seen the towers now. The horizon at the end of the rows still pulls.|The walk to the field's end is a habit now. Your feet know it by heart.} {season == \"winter\": The furrows crunch with frost.|The wheat is high and loud with insects.} {A hare bolts.|A crow follows you at a respectful distance.|Smoke rises from a chimney you can't see.}\n> left: Walk the whole boundary\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You come back muddy to the knees and hungry as a first-year.|You walk until your legs ache and your head is finally quiet.|You sit on the low wall where you always sat, and the wind does the thinking.}\n> right: Practise where no one watches\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {You set a scarecrow's hat spinning. Nobody sees. It's glorious.|You practise until your hands tingle and the crows give you a wide berth.|You try the spell you keep failing at school. Out here, with nobody watching, it nearly works.}\n\n@event home_fields_neighbours\ntitle: Neighbours' Work\npool: home_fields_routine\ntone: warm\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: Neighbours working together at the edge of a field, one waving a student in academy robes over to help.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The neighbours are hauling wood before the next snow.|The neighbours are bringing in the hay before the weather turns.} {They wave you over without asking if you're busy.|This time somebody has saved you a pair of gloves.|They've stopped waving. They just hand you the gloves.} {The neighbour's boy wants to be turned into a frog.|A farmer calls you \"the scholar\", mostly kindly.|Someone has brought a jug of mostly cider.}\n> left: Lend a hand\n  vigor +small\n  health -small\n  say: {Blisters by noon, and a supper invitation from three households.|You work till dusk. Nobody mentions the academy once, which is restful.|Your back reminds you that books are lighter than anything out here.}\n> right: Bring them all a drink\n  gold -tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: {You come back from the market with a basket and become the most popular person in the field.|They drink to the academy, then to you, then to the weather.|The neighbour's boy asks for a frog again. You give him a biscuit; it's a compromise.}\n\n@event home_fields_pond\ntitle: The Old Pond\npool: home_fields_routine\ntone: light\nart: wave\ntags: nature\nscene: A pond at the bottom of a field under an old willow, local youngsters daring each other at its edge.\ntext: {season == \"winter\": The pond at the bottom of the fields has frozen over, and the young farmhands are daring each other onto the ice.|The pond at the bottom of the fields is warm and green, and the young farmhands are daring each other off the willow.} {You used to be one of them.|This time they dare you too.|They've started daring you first.} {A dog barks at the whole business.|The blacksmith's apprentice is keeping score.|Your old initials are still carved in the willow.}\n> left: Take the dare\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    vigor +tiny\n    say: {season == \"winter\": {You skate across and back to roaring applause from three farmhands and a dog.|You slide the whole length on your back. It counts.|You skate past your old initials frozen into the willow's roots. The apprentice gives you full marks for nostalgia.}|{You drop off the willow into the green. It's colder than memory, and better.|A perfect splash. The scorekeeper gives it top marks.|You drop off the branch beside your old initials. The score is one you'll be proud of for a week.}}\n  else:\n    health -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {season == \"winter\": {You go through the ice up to your knees and limp home to a lecture and a fire.|The ice cracks under you and you scramble back, soaked and laughed at.|You stop to look at your initials, and the ice stops holding you.}|{You belly-flop. The sound carries across three fields. Everyone heard.|You slip off the willow early and land in the reeds, muddy to the ears.|You climb to your old initials and slip on them. The splash is not dignified.}}\n> right: Hold the coats\n  charm +small\n  say: {You hold the coats and referee the arguments. Someone has to be the grown-up now.|You keep score and are accused of bias by every side.|Holding the coats, you feel very old and quite content.}\n\n# ---------------- Home: the market town ----------------\n\n@pool home_market_routine\nplace: home_market\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\ntags: home\n\n@event home_market_inn_crowd\ntitle: Outside the Inn\npool: home_market_routine\ntone: light\nart: cup\ntags: social\nscene: A knot of young locals outside a country inn, raising their cups as a student in academy robes walks up.\ntext: {The old crowd is outside the inn, exactly where you left them. \"Look who it is,\" someone says. \"The wizard.\"|The old crowd have been rehearsing the wizard joke. It has a second part now.|The old crowd outside the inn. Fewer of them every break: somebody married, somebody moved away.} {season == \"winter\": They're stamping their feet against the cold.|They're sitting on the inn steps in the long light.}\n> left: Buy a round\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {They drink your health and ask if you can make the beer stronger. You can't. You're asked again.|For an hour it's as if you never left.|Someone tells the story of the time you fell in the duck pond. It has grown since you left.}\n> right: Stay for the gossip only\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You learn who's courting, who's feuding and whose goat ate whose washing. Somebody mutters that academy folk are tight with a coin.|You keep your crowns in your pocket and get very well informed.|They notice you didn't buy a round. They notice everything.}\n\n@event home_market_charm_seller\ntitle: The Charm Seller\npool: home_market_routine\ntone: light\nart: gem\ntags: money\nscene: A market stall hung with ribbons and trinkets under a hand-painted sign about academy blessings, a grandmother reaching for her purse.\ntext: {A stall in the market is selling \"genuine Lumenhold charms, blessed in the seven towers\".|The charm seller has new charms and a bigger sign. The claims have grown to match.|The charm seller sees your robes and starts packing up, then thinks better of it.} {has(origin_merchant): You know that markup. You were raised on it.|{A grandmother is buying one for luck.|The charms are bent nails tied with ribbon.|One of them is, worryingly, glowing.}}\n> left: Call out the fakes\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge negotiate apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The crowd turns on him. A grandmother gets her coin back and gives you a cake. The seller gives you a look.|He folds, and offers you a share of the trade. You decline. He seems genuinely surprised.|You point out the glowing one. The seller goes pale and hands it to you to take away, free. The crowd cheers you anyway.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {The crowd takes his side. \"Academy snob,\" someone says, buying two.|He out-talks you in front of everyone. You'd admire it if it were happening to someone else.|You call out the glowing one, and it glows brighter, as if insulted. The crowd decides it's genuine and buys the lot.}\n> right: Buy one for an aunt\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {Your aunt will love it. It's a bent nail. She will love it anyway.|You buy one. It's useless, and the old woman beside you beams at you with approval.|You buy one that glows a little. You decide not to think about why.}\n\n@event home_market_post_office\ntitle: Letters to the Castle\npool: home_market_routine\ntone: warm\nart: letter\nscene: A post office the size of a cupboard, a clerk ceremoniously weighing a stack of letters addressed to a castle on a lake.\ntext: {The town's post office is the size of a cupboard, and its clerk has never posted anything to a magic academy.|The clerk has started weighing your letters with ceremony.|The clerk reaches for the castle stamp before you speak.} {season == \"winter\": The window is fogged; it smells of sealing wax.|Flies wander in to read the notices.} {final_year: Soon you'll write to the castle from elsewhere.|You think about who at the castle you miss.}\n> left: Write to everyone\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You write until your hand cramps. The clerk sighs at the stack.|You draw the cottage for someone who has never seen one.|You start one letter with \"I miss\" and decide to leave it that way.}\n> right: Write the essay you owe\n  lore +small\n  say: {You write the essay on the clerk's counter, between parcels. It's not bad.|You post an essay to a professor. The clerk asks if it'll get you into trouble. Probably.|You finish it and post it. It's the dullest thing the clerk has ever weighed.}\n\n# Follow-ups to the errand (playtest 2, N10), queued by its options with the same friend.\n\n@event village_errand_parcel\ntitle: The Parcel That Clinked\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus or not around(npc)\nactor: npc\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: pouch\ntags: social\nscene: A student unwrapping a string-tied parcel on a dormitory bed, a small glass jar of honey and a note inside.\ntext: {npc} turns up at your door with one of the parcels you carried up the hill: the one that clinked. \"This one was for you,\" {they} say{s}. \"I needed someone to carry it without guessing.\" Inside is a jar of village honey and a note: For the porter.\n> left: Open the honey now and share it\n  rel npc +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You eat half the jar with {npc} on the dormitory floor, with spoons borrowed from the kitchens, and talk until your reading is a lost cause.\n> right: Save it for exam month\n  health +small\n  say: You put it on the shelf, labelled. In exam month, a spoonful at midnight gets you through two chapters. {npc} asks, later, if you liked it. You did.\n\n@event village_errand_third_thing\ntitle: The Third Thing, After All\ntrigger: queued\ndefer: off_campus or not around(npc)\nactor: npc\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: social\nscene: A village stall with a single extravagant hat on a stand, two students looking at it, one with a purse already open.\ntext: {npc} has gone back down to the village for the third thing, the one you talked {them} out of, and has come back with it. \"You were right,\" {they} say{s}. \"I bought it anyway. Now I need you to say it suits me.\"\n> left: Say it suits {them}\n  rel npc +small\n  say: \"It suits you,\" you say. It doesn't, at all. {npc} wears it to breakfast every day for a week, beaming.\n> right: Tell {them} the truth, kindly\n  charm +small\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: You say it's a brave choice. {npc} hears what you mean, laughs and gives it to the porter, who wears it all winter with great dignity.\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/village_more.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools, second pass (2026-10-24): village_more.\n#  Repeatable routine events for the village shops and the home\n#  places with thin pools: the Gilded Newt, Mabel's, Grimsby's,\n#  Pell & Daughters, the family cottage and the market town.\n#  Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Gilded Newt ----------------\n\n@event gilded_newt_ballad\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Newt's Ballad\npool: gilded_newt_routine\ntone: warm\nart: music\ntags: social\nscene: A crowded low-beamed tavern, an old regular standing on a bench by the fire with a tankard raised, singing, while the room bangs time on the tables.\ntext: {An old regular is standing on a bench singing the ballad of the Newt, about the fire that never goes out. It has forty verses. He knows most of them.|The ballad again. Tonight it has forty-one verses; the new one is about a student who fell into the cellar.|The ballad has a verse about the castle now, and the regulars look at you when they reach it.} {The landlady pretends to hate it.|The whole room bangs time on the tables.|{season == \"winter\": Snow is coming down outside, and nobody is going anywhere.|The fire flares at the chorus, every time.}}\n> left: Stand up and add a verse\n  challenge perform apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You make up a verse about the landlady's slate and the room roars. She pours you a cider on the house, scowling.|You rhyme \"Newt\" with \"absolute\" and the whole tavern sings it back. You'll hear it for weeks.|Your verse about the castle gets a chorus of its own. The old regular claps you on the back and says you'll go in the ballad.}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {You stand up, open your mouth and forget every word in the language. The old regular finishes your verse for you, kindly, and badly.|Your rhyme for \"Newt\" is \"Newt\". The room is silent, then merciless.|You sing your verse to the wrong tune. Half the room follows you and half the right one, and it ends in a brawl of harmonies.}\n> right: Buy the singer a drink\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You buy the old regular a cider, and he tells you the verses that aren't allowed in the ballad any more. They're the best ones.|You buy him a drink between verses. He toasts you in verse forty-two, which is new.|You buy the singer his usual. He tells you which verses are true. It's fewer than you'd hoped.}\n\n# ---------------- Mabel's Tea Shop ----------------\n\n@event mabels_fourth_cake\ntitle: The Fourth Cake\npool: mabels_routine\ntone: light\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A tea-shop counter with three familiar cakes under glass domes and a fourth, mysterious one on a plate beside them, a woman in an apron watching expectantly.\ntext: {Mabel's has three kinds of cake. Today, on a plate beside them, there is a fourth. \"Experimental,\" says Mabel. \"Free, if you're brave, petal.\"|Another fourth cake. Mabel has started a little card for each one: name, date, survivors.|The fourth cake. Mabel slides it across before you've asked, in case today is the day you're brave.} {This one is blue.|This one is suspiciously plain.|{season == \"winter\": It smells of cloves and something like snow.|It's still warm and very slightly humming.}}\n> left: Be brave and try it\n  chance likely:\n    mood +small\n    health +tiny\n    say: {It tastes exactly like the first warm day of spring. Mabel writes \"a triumph\" on her card.|It tastes of your grandmother's kitchen, or a kitchen you wish had been hers. Mabel watches your face and nods, satisfied.|It tastes like good news. Mabel says this one's going on the menu, and names it after the face you made.}\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    say: {It tastes of a memory of being scolded. Mabel takes it back, apologising, and writes \"no\" on her card.|It tastes of nothing at all, so completely that it's alarming. Mabel looks crestfallen.|It tastes of rainy afternoons in a waiting room. You can't explain it and never want to again.}\n> right: Stick with your usual\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You order your usual. Mabel sighs, and cuts it a little larger than usual anyway, as if to make up for your cowardice.|You stick with the lemon slice. Mabel feeds the experiment to a delighted first-year, who turns briefly blue.|\"Your usual, petal?\" Mabel already has it on a plate. The fourth cake waits for someone braver.}\n\n@event mabels_regulars_table\ntitle: The Regulars' Table\npool: mabels_routine\ntone: warm\nart: group\ntags: social home\nscene: A big round table in a tea shop crowded with elderly villagers playing dominoes and arguing, one of them waving a student over.\ntext: {The big round table at Mabel's belongs to the village regulars, four old friends, a box of dominoes and fifty years of arguments. They wave you over. \"Settle something for us.\"|The regulars wave you over again. Today's dispute is about the academy in their day, when, they say, the stairs moved twice as often.|The regulars have saved you a chair. They've started calling you \"the referee\".} {The dominoes are clicking.|One of them was a student at the castle sixty years ago, and won't let anyone forget it.|{season == \"winter\": The window behind them is fogged from the teapots.|Mabel brings them more tea without being asked.}}\n> left: Join the domino game\n  challenge research journeyman wild:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You keep count of every tile played and win a whole crown off a retired miller who swears he let you. He didn't.|You win, and the old student declares that the academy has gone soft and been lucky. You'll take it.|You win, and the regulars demand a rematch next time. \"The referee's been practising.\"}\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The retired miller cleans you out with a smile like a closing door.|You lose to a woman of ninety who plays with her eyes shut.|You lose, and the regulars toast your generosity. Mabel brings you a consolation biscuit.}\n> right: Listen to their stories\n  lore +small\n  say: {You hear about the year a professor turned the refectory into a pond by accident, and the year someone taught the stairs to waltz. Half of it might be true.|The old student describes the castle as it was: the same stairs, different shoes. It's strange to hear your life told back sixty years early.|You referee three arguments and hear four stories. Your tea goes cold. It's worth it.}\n\n# ---------------- Grimsby's Books ----------------\n\n@event grimsbys_by_the_nose\ntitle: Chosen by the Nose\npool: grimsbys_books_routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: An elderly bookseller holding closed books under a student's nose one at a time, eyes shut in rapture, shelves leaning overhead.\ntext: {Mr Grimsby holds three books under your nose, one after another, eyes closed. \"Don't look. Smell. The right book smells right.\"|Mr Grimsby greets you with a book already held out at nose height. \"Vanilla and rain,\" he says. \"Trust me.\"|Mr Grimsby hands you a book by its smell before you've closed the door. He hasn't asked what you came in for. He never does.} {One smells of pipe smoke and the sea.|One smells of cut grass and arguments.|{season == \"winter\": One smells of woodsmoke, and the shop is freezing.|One smells, faintly, of you.}}\n> left: Buy the one that smells right\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You buy the one that smells of the sea. It's a book of storms, beautifully written and of no use whatsoever. You love it.|Vanilla and rain is a slim book of poems about lighthouses. Mr Grimsby wraps it like a gift and takes your coin as an afterthought.|You buy the one that smells like you. It's a novel about someone your age on the night before they leave home. You read it on the barge, slowly.}\n> right: Insist on the one you came for\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      say: {You describe the book four times. On the fifth, Mr Grimsby lights up and produces it from under a cat-sized stack. He seems a little disappointed in you.|You stand firm. Mr Grimsby sighs, sniffs the air and goes straight to the right shelf. He could do this all along.|You've learned to describe a book by its smell. Mr Grimsby finds yours in a minute, beaming at your progress.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: {You ask for a textbook and leave, somehow, with nothing at all, after an hour's lecture on the smell of old maps.|Mr Grimsby goes into the shelves for your book and is gone so long you leave a note.|Mr Grimsby brings you a cookbook, with great confidence, and is so hurt when you decline it that you stay an hour to make up for it.}\n\n@event grimsbys_upper_floor\ntitle: The Upper Floor\npool: grimsbys_books_routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: study money\nscene: A rickety ladder rising through a hatch in a bookshop ceiling into a dim upper room where shelves lean at alarming angles.\ntext: {A ladder at the back of Grimsby's climbs through a hatch to an upper floor nobody mentions. \"The remainders,\" says Mr Grimsby vaguely. \"Mind the third rung. And the fourth.\"|The ladder to the upper floor. Someone has tied a ribbon round the third rung, as a warning or a memorial.|The upper floor. You know its geography now: poetry slumps against history, and history has given up.} {Dust swirls in the light from the hatch.|Somewhere up there, something slides and thumps.|{season == \"summer\": It's stifling under the roof.|Rain drums on the roof right above your head.}}\n> left: Climb up and rummage\n  challenge feat novice+ wild:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      gold +tiny\n      say: {You find a first edition priced at a coin, with a pressed love letter inside. Mr Grimsby buys the book back at twice the price without noticing; the letter you keep.|You dig out a box of old exam papers from the castle, decades old. Mr Grimsby gives them to you for nothing and then pays you to take the box away.|You find a book of spells nobody casts any more, wedged under a shelf to stop it falling. Mr Grimsby gives you a crown for straightening the shelf.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The third rung breaks, as advertised. You land in a heap of atlases and see stars, and some constellations.|A shelf leans on you, gently, and then less gently. You climb down under a small avalanche of poetry.|The fourth rung, this time. Mr Grimsby looks up from his counter, says \"Ah,\" and goes back to his reading.}\n> right: Browse the window display\n  mood +small\n  say: {The window display is arranged by colour, not subject. You find three books you didn't know you wanted, and want none of them enough to buy.|You browse the window, where the books have faded through thirty summers. It's a lovely, useless hour.|You browse the window and watch the village go by through the gaps between the spines.}\n\n# ---------------- Pell & Daughters ----------------\n\n@event pell_apothecary_back_door\ntitle: Foragers at the Back Door\npool: pell_apothecary_routine\ntone: tense\nart: mushroom\ntags: money nature\nscene: A muddy forager with a basket of wild roots and mushrooms lingering at the back door of an apothecary, beckoning to a student in academy robes.\ntext: {At Pell's back door a forager in muddy boots is waiting to sell the morning's haul from the Whisperwood. She catches your eye. \"Cheaper than the counter, love. No questions.\"|The forager at the back door again, basket heavier, prices lower. She remembers you.|The back-door forager nods at you like an old business partner. Today's basket has something in it that's still moving.} {The basket smells of moss and rain.|There's moonpetal in it, wrapped in a dock leaf.|{season == \"winter\": Her fingers are blue with cold.|Old Pell can be heard counting aloud inside.}}\n> left: Buy straight from her basket\n  gold -small\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {You buy a bundle of roots for half the counter price and spend the evening sorting the useful from the merely muddy.|You buy the moonpetal, dock leaf and all. It's the real thing; you can tell by the way it doesn't wilt.|You buy the moving thing, which turns out to be a root that wriggles toward water. You learn a great deal keeping it alive.}\n> right: Ask where she found it all\n  lore +small\n  say: {She tells you about a clearing west of the stream where the moss glows. You write it down and do not go there, yet.|She tells you which mushrooms lie about being safe. It's an education, and it's free.|She tells you, lowering her voice, about a stream that runs uphill on nights of the full moon. Then she laughs and says it's probably nothing.}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_humming_drawer\ntitle: The Drawer That Hums\npool: pell_apothecary_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: bottle\ntags: money\nscene: A wall of a thousand small labelled drawers in an apothecary, one of them faintly rattling and humming, a student's hand hovering near its brass knob.\ntext: {One of Pell's thousand drawers is humming, low and steady, like a kettle thinking about it. Its label has worn away.|A humming drawer again. Somebody has taped a fresh label to it: ASK FIRST.|The humming drawer has a new label in Old Pell's precise hand: NOT TODAY. It's a different drawer from last time.} {Both daughters are busy with a queue.|Old Pell is counting aloud in the back, loudly.|{season == \"winter\": The shop is cold, and the drawer is the only warm thing in it.|The bell over the door has just rung, and nobody has come in.}}\n> left: Open it, just a crack\n  chance even:\n    mood +small\n    alchemy +small\n    say: {Inside is a nest of dried bee-balm, humming with old summer. The smell follows you all day, and you know it now by heart.|Inside, a single seed pod is vibrating like a plucked string. You close it gently and remember its name.|Inside is a bottle of something that sings when the cork is loose. You tighten the cork and the shop goes quiet with relief.}\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {It's sneezing powder, enchanted to share itself. You sneeze until the queue applauds.|A puff of sleep-dust comes out. You wake on the floor with a daughter fanning you and Old Pell counting your breaths, aloud.|It's itching-seed, and it's in your sleeves. A daughter hands you a jar of ointment and a look.}\n> right: Ask a daughter what it is\n  lore +small\n  say: {\"Bee-balm from a summer that didn't end,\" says the elder daughter, not looking up. \"Leave it.\" You look it up later. She's right, and it's fascinating.|The younger daughter tells you it's a seed pod that hums before rain, and has never been wrong. You check the sky. She's right.|The daughters argue over what's in it for ten minutes. You learn more from the argument than from either answer.}\n\n# ---------------- Home ----------------\n\n@event home_cottage_neighbour_calls\ntitle: A Neighbour Calls\npool: home_cottage_routine\ntone: warm\nart: house\nscene: A neighbour in a muddy coat standing at a cottage kitchen door holding a stopped clock, while the family pretends not to listen from the table.\ntext: {A neighbour is at the kitchen door with a stopped clock, a hopeful face and the air of someone who has been saving it for you since harvest.|Another neighbour, another hopeful face. This one has a hen that's laying eggs the wrong colour.|The neighbours have a list now, you suspect. Today, a pie that won't rise and won't explain why.} {Your family pretends not to listen.|Someone at the table says, \"Go on, then,\" with pride.|{season == \"winter\": There's snow on the neighbour's hat; they've walked a long way.|The neighbour has brought a basket of plums, as payment in advance.}}\n> left: Have a go at it\n  challenge spell apprentice:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You coax the clock's spring awake with a whisper. It chimes, a bit sheepishly. The neighbour tells the whole lane.|You charm the hen's eggs back to brown, more or less. The neighbour insists on giving you a dozen. They're mostly brown.|You find the trouble with the pie: it was sulking. A warm word and it rises. The neighbour's face rises with it.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: {The clock starts running backwards, cheerfully. The neighbour leaves delighted, which is somehow worse, and tells the whole lane.|The eggs come out tartan. The neighbour is polite about it, and tells everyone.|The pie rises, and keeps rising, and leaves by the chimney. The whole lane will talk about it for years.}\n> right: Say you're on holiday\n  health +small\n  say: {You smile and say magic is for term time. The neighbour goes away disappointed, and you have the afternoon to yourself by the fire.|You send them to the tinker, who will charge them, but kindly. You put your feet up.|You say no, gently. Your family exchange a look. You spend the afternoon asleep in your old chair, guilty and rested.}\n\n@event home_market_schoolmistress\ntitle: The Old Schoolmistress\npool: home_market_routine\ntone: warm\nart: scroll\ntags: social\nscene: A small elderly woman with a satchel of slates stopping a student in academy robes in the middle of a busy market street.\ntext: {In the market you're stopped by the old schoolmistress who taught half the town its letters. \"Come and tell my little ones about the academy,\" she says. \"They won't believe me.\"|The schoolmistress again, with her satchel of slates. \"The offer stands. They still don't believe me.\"|The schoolmistress waves from across the square. The children at her heels are wearing paper wizard hats, badly folded.} {She looks smaller than she used to.|She smells of chalk, as she always did.|{season == \"winter\": Her breath steams; she's been out in the cold looking for you.|A market dog is following her for the biscuits in her pocket.}}\n> left: Visit the schoolroom\n  charm +small\n  mood +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You tell twenty children about the moving stairs and the floating candles. One asks if you've met a dragon. You lie a little. They swarm you like bees.|You show them a single small flame in your palm. Twenty faces light up. You're exhausted after an hour, the good way.|You answer questions until your voice gives out. One of them wants to go to the academy now, very badly. It's your fault, and you're glad.}\n> right: Buy her tea and listen\n  gold -tiny\n  lore +small\n  say: {You buy her a pot of tea and she tells you which of your old classmates married whom, and which ran off to sea. You'd forgotten you missed them.|She asks what you're studying and actually listens. Then she corrects your grammar, out of habit, and you let her.|You buy her tea and she tells you what you were like at seven. It's alarming how little you've changed.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/pools/world_more.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Baseline pools, second pass (2026-10-24): world_more.\n#  Repeatable routine events with no place: the castle between\n#  visits, drawn in the day's world slots (design §5, §6c).\n#  Most older place-less cards happen once a run; these recur.\n#  Spec: docs/authoring/pools.md.\n# ============================================================\n\n@pool world_more_routine\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\nweight: 10\n\n@event world_stairs_ate_a_shoe\ntitle: The Stairs Ate a Shoe\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: light\nart: tower\ntags: magic\nwhen: term\nscene: A great pile of odd shoes heaped in a dusty alcove beneath a castle staircase, a student in one sock digging through it.\ntext: {Halfway down the east stair the steps shift, and when they settle you are one shoe lighter. Everyone knows where the stairs put shoes, in a heap under the east wing.|The stairs have taken another shoe. You suspect they like the taste of yours.|Shoe gone again. You've come to think of the heap under the east wing as a kind of shoe library.} {You have somewhere to be.|It's raining, and the castle floors are cold.|{season == \"winter\": Your sock is soaked through in a minute.|The stairs grind smugly behind you.}}\n> left: Go and dig through the heap\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You dig through three centuries of shoes and find yours under a buckled pair that must be older than the towers. You're filthy, late and oddly triumphant.|You find your shoe, and a slipper with a name sewn into it from fifty years ago. You leave the slipper on top, in case.|You know the heap's layers now, like a geologist. Your shoe is on top, almost politely. You stub your toe on the way out anyway.}\n> right: Hop on, one shoe down\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {You hop to your next lecture and spend it with one foot in your bag. You take excellent notes, out of spite.|You go about your day in one shoe and one sock. People stare; you make the most of it by reading on the move.|You wear one shoe all day and read between lectures to keep your mind off the cold foot. A first-year asks if it's a fashion. You say yes.}\n\n@event world_duchess_has_news\ntitle: The Duchess Has News\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: light\nart: mirror\ntags: social\nscene: An ornate gilt frame in a castle corridor where a powdered portrait duchess has squeezed in beside a placid painted cow, leaning out to whisper.\ntext: {The Duchess of Ambry, who refuses to stay in her own frame, has squeezed into a pastoral scene by your corridor and is beckoning to you with her fan.|The Duchess again, this time in a portrait of a stern admiral, who looks resigned. \"Darling. News.\"|The Duchess has taken to waiting for you in whatever frame is nearest your door. Today she's sharing a still life with a pear.} {A painted cow is chewing at her elbow.|Her wig is slightly askew from travel.|{season == \"winter\": She's wearing a painted fur borrowed from a neighbouring frame.|She's fanning herself vigorously.}}\n> left: Listen to her gossip\n  guile +small\n  mood +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {She tells you which professor has a secret allergy to cats and which prefect cries at the opera. You'll never look at either the same way.|She whispers who is courting whom on the third floor, in scandalous detail. You feel like a spy and enjoy it more than you should.|She hands you the castle's secrets like sweets. Some of them are about people you like. You take them anyway.}\n> right: Offer her some news instead\n  charm +small\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You tell her about your week, a little improved. By supper every portrait in the castle knows a much grander version, and so does half your house, not all of it flattering.|You give her a harmless story about yourself. It comes back to you three days later as a romance, a duel and a minor tragedy, and people keep asking which bit is true.|You feed her an anecdote; she gasps in all the right places. By morning it's a legend with you in it, not entirely flatteringly.}\n\n@event world_laundry_day\ntitle: Laundry Day\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: light\nart: bottle\ntags: home\nscene: Enchanted wash-tubs churning by themselves in a steamy castle laundry, clothes flapping on lines overhead, a student holding up a robe that has turned an alarming colour.\ntext: {The castle laundry has charmed tubs that wash by themselves, mostly. Your robes are due.|Laundry day. Last month the tubs turned a whole corridor's robes a gentle pink, and nobody has let the tubs forget it.|The laundry. You know which tub shrinks things and which one sings while it spins.} {Someone's socks are trying to escape over the edge.|The steam is so thick you can barely see the lines.|{season == \"winter\": Everything on the lines is frozen stiff as boards.|The windows are open, and the shirts are trying to fly.}}\n> left: Charm the tub yourself\n  challenge spell apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You coax the tub into a perfect wash. Your robes come out clean, warm and smelling faintly of thunderstorms.|Your robes come out their own colour, which, after the pink month, feels like a victory parade.|You charm the singing tub, and it sings your favourite song while it spins. Everything comes out spotless.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      gold -tiny\n      say: {Your robes come out three sizes smaller. You buy a cheap replacement from the village tailor.|Your robes come out pink, like everyone's last month. The tubs have a sense of humour. You pay for a dye to put it right.|The tub spins so hard your robes leave by the window. You find most of them. The rest cost you a crown at the tailor's.}\n> right: Wash them by hand\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You scrub everything at the sink till your knuckles are raw. Clean, at least, and still the right size.|You wash by hand, wring by hand, hang by hand. Your arms ache; your robes stay black.|You wash by hand again. It's slow and cold, and you've come to find it soothing, in a chapped sort of way.}\n\n@event world_bridge_in_a_gale\ntitle: The Bridge in a Gale\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: tense\nart: tower\ntags: danger\nscene: A shimmering bridge of light slung between two castle towers, swaying in a howling wind, a student gripping the rail halfway across.\ntext: {The wind is up, and the bridge of light between the towers is swaying like a hammock. The long way round is ten minutes and four staircases.|Gale day. The bridges of light sway and ring faintly, like glass in a cupboard.|The bridge in the wind. You know its rhythm now: the sway, the pause, the shudder.} {Two first-years are halfway across, frozen.|A prefect stands at the end shouting safety rules nobody can hear.|{season == \"winter\": Sleet is coming sideways off the lake.|Leaves are blowing straight through the bridge, as if it isn't there.}}\n> left: Cross it anyway\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      mood +small\n      say: {You walk the swaying bridge like a sailor on a deck and step off the far side with your heart singing.|You cross in the pauses between gusts. The bridge hums under your feet like something alive.|You cross on the rhythm, sway, pause, shudder, step, and arrive before anyone else. The wind claps you on the back.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {A gust drops you to your knees halfway across. You crawl the rest, which nobody who saw will ever let you forget.|You freeze in the middle like the first-years and stand there for a long, windy minute. You make it, bruised and shaken.|You misjudge the shudder and the rail catches you in the ribs. You arrive, eventually, aching.}\n> right: Take the long way round\n  lore +small\n  say: {You take the four staircases and read on the way. You arrive late and better informed.|You take the long way and find a corridor you'd never noticed, with a portrait that winks. You arrive late and curious.|The long way round has become a habit on windy days. You've read most of a book on those four staircases.}\n\n@event world_common_room_kettle\nslot: night\ntitle: The Common-Room Kettle\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: warm\nart: cup\ntags: social home\nscene: A crowded house common room late in the evening, students in dressing gowns round a fire, a battered kettle starting to whistle on the hob.\ntext: {Your house common room has one kettle, older than anyone in the room, and whoever makes the tea hears everything.|The common-room kettle is whistling. Somebody shouts \"Whoever's nearest!\" and it's you.|The kettle. You know its temper now: two taps on the lid and it behaves.} {The fire is roaring and people are in their dressing gowns.|Someone has a letter from home and is reading it aloud.|{season == \"winter\": Snow has drifted against the windows.|The windows are open to the warm night.}}\n> left: Make tea for everyone\n  gold -tiny\n  charm +small\n  mood +small\n  say: {You make a pot for twelve with the last of your good leaves. In return you hear who's courting whom, who's failing what, and one secret you'll keep.|You make the tea from your own tin, and everyone moves up to make room for you by the fire. It's the warmest you've been all week.|You make it the way the room likes it now, with the good leaves you bought. Someone says \"Thank you\" exactly the way your family says it, and your throat goes tight.}\n> right: Take your cup up to bed\n  health +small\n  say: {You take your tea up to bed and fall asleep before it's cold. The common room laughs below you half the night.|You slip away early with your cup. You miss the best story of the term, you hear in the morning.|You go up early, as you've started to. The noise from below is a nice thing to fall asleep to.}\n\n@event world_the_mirror_asks\ntitle: The Mirror Asks Again\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 6\nart: mirror\ntags: mystery\nwhen: seen(sorting_mirror) > 0\nscene: A tall enchanted mirror in a stone entrance hall, its surface rippling like cold water, a student pausing before it with a bag on one shoulder.\ntext: {Once, on your first day, the Sorting Mirror asked who you were before you came. Today, as you cross the entrance hall, it ripples and asks the follow-up, in its voice like cold water. \"And now?\"|The Sorting Mirror asks its follow-up again: \"And now?\" It seems to expect a different answer each time.|The Mirror catches you mid-stride. \"And now?\" You've answered it so often that the question has started to sound like a greeting.} {year == 1: In it, you look younger than you feel.|{final_year: In it, you look like someone about to leave.|In it, your reflection waits for an answer too.}}\n> left: Answer it honestly\n  mood +small\n  say: {You tell it plainly what's changed since your first day. The surface stills, and you walk on lighter, as if you'd set something down.|You give it a true answer, with one new thing in it that wasn't there on your first day. The Mirror seems to notice the new thing.|You answer without stopping, plainly, the way you'd answer a friend. It steadies you.}\n> right: Tell it a pretty lie\n  guile +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You tell it you're secretly a prince in exile. The Mirror ripples, says nothing, and the silence follows you down the corridor.|You tell it something grander than the truth. Your reflection, you'd swear, rolls its eyes.|You lie to it, easily. It doesn't argue. It never argues. That's the worst part.}\n\n@event world_something_in_your_bag\ntitle: A Stowaway in Your Satchel\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: light\nart: paw\ntags: nature\nscene: A student opening a satchel to find a small magical creature curled up among the books, blinking up at them.\ntext: {~A hedge-sprite has moved into your bag and is lining it with your notes.|A small cave newt, glowing faintly orange, is asleep in your inkwell.|A baby owl, still mostly fluff, has climbed into your hood and refuses to leave.} {~Unregistered creatures are tolerated, so long as nobody notices them.|It looks at you as though this was your idea.|It has clearly been there since breakfast.}\n> left: Keep it quiet a while\n  guile +small\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You feed it crumbs and hide it from the prefects all day. By evening it's built a nest out of your essay, and you don't mind.|You keep it warm in your pocket all day. Nobody notices; it glows when you laugh.|You carry it about in your hood all day. It hoots once in the middle of a lecture, and you cough to cover it, heroically.}\n> right: Take it to the stables\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You walk it down to the stables. Professor Brask tells it, very kindly, where hedge-sprites belong, and tells you, less kindly, to check your bag.|Professor Brask takes the newt in her palm and tells you three things about cave newts you'll never forget. You miss it a bit on the walk back.|Professor Brask tucks the owlet into a nest box and tells you where its mother is likely to be. You find yourself checking your hood all week.}\n\n@event world_study_group\nslot: night\ntitle: The Study Group\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 4\nart: candle\ntags: study social\nwhen: term and not wrapup\ncast:\n  friend: any where (tagged(friend, student) or tagged(friend, prefect)) and rel(friend) >= 5\nscene: A huddle of students round a table piled with books and cold mugs, one drawing diagrams in the air with a glowing wand-tip, late at night.\ntext: {{friend} has a study group going in an empty classroom and a spare seat. \"We're doing the hard chapter. Bring your brain, and biscuits.\"|{friend}'s study group is still going, and still short of biscuits. The chalkboard is a battlefield.|{friend} waves you into the study group. Someone has started a list of who brings what; you're down for biscuits.} {Someone is drawing diagrams in the air with a glowing wand-tip.|Two of them are arguing about a footnote.|{season == \"winter\": It's freezing; everyone's in scarves.|The window's open, and moths are attending too.}}\n> left: Join and work late\n  study +tiny\n  rel friend +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You work through the hard chapter together until midnight. It makes sense in the end, mostly because {friend} drew it as a sandwich. You're shattered.|You argue the footnote to a draw and finish the chapter. {friend} walks you back across the bridges, yawning, long past midnight.|You bring the biscuits and an answer. {friend} says you're the group's secret weapon. You go to bed far too late.}\n> right: Go to bed instead\n  health +small\n  rel friend -tiny\n  say: {You go to bed early. {friend} tells you at breakfast how good the group was, at length.|You turn in. Through the wall you hear the study group laughing until one, and sleep anyway.|You skip it. {friend} saves you a biscuit, which is somehow worse than a reproach.}\n\n@event world_weather_following_you\ntitle: Weather Following You\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: light\nart: rain\ntags: magic\nscene: A student walking down a castle corridor with a small personal weather charm hovering over their head while everyone else keeps clear.\ntext: {~Somebody's weather charm has escaped and attached itself to you. A small rain cloud is following you at head height, drizzling.|A runaway weather charm has found you. A tiny snow flurry is falling on you, and only you.|An escaped weather charm has chosen you. A single warm sunbeam follows you everywhere, even indoors.} {~People step politely out of your way.|A first-year is following you around to see what happens.|It seems pleased with itself.}\n> left: Try to dispel it\n  challenge spell apprentice:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      say: {~You unpick the charm thread by thread and the cloud pops like a soap bubble, leaving a small rainbow.|You dispel it; the last snowflake lands on your nose like a goodbye.|You unpick the sunbeam's charm and it fades, regretfully. You feel the cold at once.}\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      health -tiny\n      say: {~Your spell makes it a thundercloud. It follows you for the rest of the day, rumbling at anyone who looks at you, and soaks you to the skin.|Your spell doubles it. You spend the day in a small private blizzard and catch a chill.|Your spell turns the sunbeam into a heatwave. You spend the afternoon sweating in a cold castle, red as a lobster.}\n> right: Let it be, and enjoy it\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {~You go about your day in your own private drizzle. By evening it's a castle joke, and you're in on it.|You let it snow on you all day and catch flakes on your tongue in lectures. People talk about it.|You go about your day sunlit and warm. People keep finding reasons to stand next to you.}\n\n@event world_lamplighters_round\nslot: evening\ntitle: The Lamplighter's Round\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: warm\nart: lantern\ntags: social\nscene: An old castle porter with a long brass lighting pole lighting the corridor lamps one by one at dusk, a student walking beside him carrying a second pole.\ntext: {At dusk the old porter makes his round with a long brass pole, lighting the corridor lamps one by one. He's slow these days, and the west corridor is long.|The lamplighter again, at dusk. He nods at the spare pole leaning by the door, as if it had been left there for someone.|The lamplighter's round. The spare pole has a notch carved in it now, about where your hand goes.} {He hums an old song as he goes.|His knees click on every stair.|{season == \"winter\": It's been dark for an hour already, and every lamp matters.|The evening is long and gold, and he's in no hurry.}}\n> left: Light the west corridor for him\n  arcana +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {You light them with a flick of fire, one after another, all the way to the end, and feel each one in your fingertips. The porter says the corridor has never looked so cheerful, and he's been doing it forty years.|You take the far end with the spare pole and a little flame. He takes the near end and tells you about the castle when he was a boy. You're drained, and lit up.|You light the west corridor in a single walk now, a flame at every lamp, and you're light-headed by the end. The porter lights his pipe off the last one.}\n> right: Walk with him and listen\n  lore +small\n  say: {He tells you which lamps never stay lit and why, and which ghost likes the dark. You walk the whole round and forget you had anywhere to be.|He tells you about a headmistress, long ago, who lit every lamp in the castle with one snap of her fingers. \"Showing off,\" he says fondly.|He tells you a story for every lamp. You've heard some before. You don't mind.}\n\n@event world_the_warm_flue\ntitle: The Warm Flue\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: light\nart: bed\ntags: home\nwhen: term\nscene: A sunny stone window niche in a castle corridor beside a warm chimney flue, a student curled up asleep in it with a book over their face.\ntext: {Behind the kitchens there's a window niche where the chimney flue runs warm through the stone. It's the best nap in the castle, and it's free.|The warm niche by the flue is free again. Someone has left a cushion, and a note: \"FIRST COME\".|The niche by the flue. You've learned when the kitchens fire the ovens, and when it's warmest.} {The corridor is quiet.|A cat is already asleep in one corner.|{season == \"winter\": Frost is on the window; the stone is toasty.|The window is open, and a breeze plays over it.}}\n> left: Curl up for an hour\n  health +small\n  mood +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {You sleep like a loaf in an oven. You wake to find you've missed the reading you meant to do, and don't regret it as much as you should.|You nap on the cushion and wake an hour later, rosy and behind on everything.|You nap through the oven-firing, the warmest hour. Your notes stay shut; your soul is restored.}\n> right: Read in it instead\n  lore +small\n  health +tiny\n  say: {You read in the warm with your back to the flue and finish a whole chapter without once nodding off. Nearly.|You read with the cushion behind you and the warmth seeping in. It's the most comfortable studying you've ever done.|You read through the warmest hour and manage not to sleep. It's an act of will, and a pleasant one.}\n\n@event world_margin_argument\ntitle: The Argument in the Margins\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: warm\nart: book\ntags: study\nwhen: term\nscene: An open second-hand textbook covered in handwritten margin notes in three different inks, arrows and crossings-out running between them, a student's pencil hovering.\ntext: {Your second-hand textbook came with an argument in the margins. Two former owners, decades apart, have been disagreeing about chapter four in ink.|You've reached chapter six, and the margin argument has followed. A third hand has joined in, in green, mostly to tell them both to calm down.|The margins again. You know the three hands now: the furious one, the patient one, the green one who draws small owls.} {Today it's about whether a spell can be \"polite\".|Today one of them has written \"SEE ME AFTER\" to the other, forty years late.|{final_year: You wonder who'll own this book after you.|Someone has pressed a flower at the page where they finally agree.}}\n> left: Add your own note to the argument\n  lore +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {You write a careful note siding with the patient one and citing the page. It feels like joining a very old, very slow conversation.|You add a fourth hand, in pencil, and settle the question of polite spells for good. You're sure someone will disagree in thirty years.|You write a small note to the green one, thanking them for the owls. The book feels more yours for it.}\n> right: Ignore them and read the text\n  study +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You cover the margins with your hand and read the actual chapter. It's duller than the argument, and you remember more of it.|You read the printed text and resolutely ignore three people shouting in ink. You lose the thread twice anyway.|You read straight through without once looking at the margins, which takes willpower. The chapter goes in; the fun stays out.}\n\n@event world_door_that_wasnt_there\ntitle: A Door That Wasn't There\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: eerie\nart: door\ntags: mystery\nscene: A small arched wooden door set into a castle corridor wall where there was only bare stone yesterday, its brass handle faintly warm.\ntext: {~In a corridor you walk every day there is a door that wasn't there yesterday, small and arched, with a brass handle worn by hands that aren't yours.|A door has appeared in the bare wall by the stairs, painted a cheerful blue, with no keyhole.|There's a new door at the end of a corridor that used to just end. Behind it, something is ticking.} {~The castle does this. It always puts them back.|Nobody else seems to notice it.|It's slightly ajar.}\n> left: Open it and look inside\n  chance even:\n    lore +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {~Behind it is a tiny library of books about doors, all open at the same page. You read for an hour, and when you come out, it's gone.|Behind it is a cupboard full of preserved pears, labelled in a hand from two centuries ago. You take one. It's delicious, and the door is gone by supper.|Behind it is a room full of clocks, every one of them right. You set your watch by them and leave, and the door seals behind you.}\n  else:\n    health -tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {~Behind it is a spiral stair up to nowhere. Halfway, the steps get narrower than your foot, and you come down with a turned ankle and the door gone.|Behind it is a broom cupboard, and the brooms are not pleased to see you. You leave with bruises.|Behind it is a room full of clocks, all of them striking at once, right in your ears. You stagger out with a headache that lasts till bedtime.}\n> right: Chalk a cross on it, walk on\n  guile +small\n  say: {~You chalk a neat cross on it, like the old marks you've seen on other walls. By tomorrow the door is gone, but your cross is still there.|You mark it and keep walking. Some doors are invitations; some are tests. You don't feel like being tested today.|You leave your mark and a note of the date. You're keeping a map of where the doors appear. The castle, you suspect, is keeping one of you.}\n\n@event world_paper_bird\ntitle: The Paper-Bird Jam\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: light\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: Dozens of folded paper birds crowding and flapping against a dormitory window, a student trying to catch them in both hands.\ntext: {Somebody's routing charm has gone wrong, and every paper bird in your tower is turning in at your window instead of its own. There are eleven on the sill, and more coming.|The paper-bird jam again. Someone has pinned a sign to your door that says SORTING OFFICE.|The birds know your window now. They queue on the sill like regulars at a counter.} {One is pecking at the glass with real urgency.|Two of them are fighting over the latch.|{season == \"winter\": They're dusted with frost and shivering.|Rain has smudged half the names on their wings.}}\n> left: Sort them and send them on\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You read wings, untangle a knot of six and send every bird to its proper window. An hour gone, and your eyes ache from the handwriting.|You sort them by tower and floor on your bed and launch them in order. It's dull work, and every one of them arrives.|You sort the queue at speed now, like a postmaster. It's still an hour you meant to spend on something else.}\n> right: Open the window and shoo them\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {You fling the window wide and wave your arms. The birds scatter like startled pigeons, and half of them land at the wrong doors. By supper it's been traced to you.|You shoo them out in a great flapping cloud. It's glorious. Two notes end up in the headmistress's tower, and people know whose window they came from.|You open the window and let the flock go where it likes. Wrong doors all over the tower, and a reputation as the leak.}\n\n@event world_practice_next_door\nslot: night\ntitle: The Practice Next Door\npool: world_more_routine\ntone: warm\nart: music\ntags: social\nscene: A student at a desk with their head in their hands, a thin wall between them and an unseen musician, music drifting under the door.\ntext: {Someone in the room next door is learning a piece on a charmed fiddle. They play the same eight bars over and over, and the fiddle sighs at every wrong note.|The fiddle next door has got as far as the sixteenth bar. It still sighs, but less.|The piece next door is almost a piece now. You know it as well as the player does, wrong notes included.} {You were trying to read.|It's late, and the wall is thin.|{season == \"winter\": The radiator carries it into your room like a second instrument.|The windows are open, and half the corridor can hear it.}}\n> left: Knock and offer to help\n  charm +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You knock. The player is a mortified first-year who has never had a lesson. You hum the eight bars properly until they have them, which is well past midnight.|You knock, and stay to count the beat for an hour. The sixteenth bar comes right for the first time; you both cheer, and the neighbours bang on the wall.|The door's already open when you knock. You tap out the tricky bit on the tabletop while they follow. Bed is very late.}\n> right: Pillow over your head\n  lore +small\n  mood -small\n  say: {You read with a pillow pressed to one ear. You get through the chapter; the eight bars get into your dreams.|You work through it while the fiddle sighs at the sixteenth bar every four minutes, regular as a clock. You finish your reading through gritted teeth.|You study to the sound of it, grudgingly. Annoyingly, it helps; you'll be humming it in the exam.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/romance/kael_nessa.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Romance sets (world expansion 1, plan §2a): Kael and Nessa. See lyra_tobias.grim for the shape.\n#  Kael's family and the lake are hard canon: nothing here touches them. His sets read cold hands, his mother, the North.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Kael ----------------\n\n@event kael_warm_enough\ntitle: Warm Enough\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 9 and courting(kael) and spark(kael) < 40\nrepeat: 4\ntime: evening night\nweight: 10\nart: snow\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two students on a cold stone bench under a lamp, one with frost on his sleeves holding his bare hands near the other's mug of tea.\ntext: Kael sits down beside you on the cold bench outside the library. {~Frost creeps from his sleeves onto the stone.|Your tea steams; his breath doesn't.} He holds his bare hands near your mug, not quite touching it, the way you'd hold them near a fire you weren't sure you were allowed to use.\n> left: Wrap his hands round the mug\n  learn kael.cold_hands\n  spark kael +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You fold his fingers round the mug, your hands over his. The tea goes cold in a minute. He doesn't let go. \"Warm enough,\" he says, as if it's a thing he's never said.|You give him the mug and keep your hands on his. Your fingers ache with cold. His, slowly, don't. \"Warm enough,\" he says, testing the words.}\n> right: Fetch him a second cup\n  rel kael +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~You bring him a cup of his own. It freezes at the rim. He drinks it anyway, solemnly, as if it were a ceremony.|You fetch him a cup from the kitchens. He holds it like something alive. \"Nobody brings me hot things,\" he says. \"They assume.\"}\n\n@pool kael_nessa_kael_social_routine\nactor: kael\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nweight: 10\n\n@event kael_frost_drawings\ntitle: Frost Drawings\npool: kael_nessa_kael_social_routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 9 and spark(kael) >= 5 and courting(kael)\nart: snow\ntags: social romance\nscene: A tall classroom window covered in delicate frost-ferns that, looked at closely, make the shape of a face; a pale boy wiping at it with his sleeve.\ntext: The window by your seat in the lecture tower is frosted over, in a lecture hall that's too warm. Looked at closely, the frost-ferns make a face. Yours. Kael, two rows back, has gone very red, which on him is a faint pink.\n> left: Breathe on the glass and draw one back\n  spark kael +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You breathe on the glass and draw, badly, a boy in a fur coat with a speech bubble that says \"...\". By the end of the lecture the frost has added a smile to your drawing. Neither of you mentions it afterwards.\n> right: Let him wipe it off\n  rel kael +small\n  say: He leans over and wipes it away with his sleeve, mortified. \"It does that,\" he says. \"When I'm not paying attention.\" He paid attention to the rest of the lecture very hard.\n\n@event kael_until_the_thaw\ntitle: Until the Thaw\npool: kael_nessa_kael_social_routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 12 and spark(kael) >= 10 and courting(kael)\nart: star\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two students parting at the foot of a spiral staircase at night, one of them touching his forehead briefly to the other's.\ntext: At the foot of your staircase, Kael stops. \"In the North we don't say goodbye,\" he says. \"It's unlucky. You say 'until the thaw', and touch foreheads, so the cold knows you're coming back.\" He hasn't said it to anyone here. He's waiting to see if you'll laugh.\n> left: Touch foreheads\n  learn kael.north\n  spark kael +small\n  say: You bend your head to his. His forehead is cold as a windowpane. \"Until the thaw,\" you say. He stands there after you've gone up. You can hear him not moving.\n> right: Say it back, carefully\n  learn kael.north\n  rel kael +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: \"Until the thaw,\" you say, getting the vowels wrong. He corrects them, gently, three times, until you sound like you were born to it. He looks absurdly pleased.\n\n@event kael_bakery_window\ntitle: Wanting\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: rel(kael) >= 9 and courting(kael) and spark(kael) < 25\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 9\nart: house\ntags: social romance\nscene: A pale boy standing outside a village bakery window in the cold, looking at a tray of iced buns, hands in his pockets.\ntext: You find Kael standing outside the bakery window in the village, looking at the iced buns. He's been there, by the {season == \"winter\": snow on his shoulders|rain on his collar}, a while. \"I'm not hungry,\" he says, before you ask. He doesn't move.\n> left: Buy him one\n  gold -tiny\n  spark kael +small\n  say: {~You buy two. He holds his like an unexploded charm. \"I wasn't going to,\" he says. \"We weren't — I wasn't supposed to want things.\" Then he eats it in four bites and looks at you as if you've done something dangerous.|You put a bun in his hands. He looks at it, and at you, and says nothing for a long time. Then: \"Thank you for not asking.\" He eats it slowly, as if someone might take it.}\n> right: Ask which one he'd pick\n  rel kael +small\n  say: {~\"The one with the cherry,\" he says, at once, and then looks shocked at himself. You don't buy it. You just let him have said it, which seems to matter more.|He thinks about it seriously. \"None,\" he says. \"All of them.\" Then, quietly: \"Is that allowed?\" You tell him it is.}\n\n@event kael_lets_himself_want\ntitle: One Thing a Month\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: bond(kael) == \"partner\"\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: A boy and his partner on the castle roof at dusk, him counting something on his cold fingers, very seriously.\ntext: Kael has a practice now: once a month, he tells you one thing he wants, out loud. {~This month he's been working up to it for days.|He has it ready. He's written it down so he won't back out.} \"I want,\" he says, and stops, and starts again. \"I want to see the sea. With you. Not for a reason.\"\n> left: \"Then we'll go.\"\n  spark kael +small\n  mood +small\n  say: {~\"Just like that?\" he says. \"Just like that,\" you say. He laughs, out loud, and frost blooms on every slate of the roof at once.|He nods slowly, as if you've signed something. \"Next month,\" he says, \"I'll want something bigger.\" You find you're looking forward to it.}\n> right: Tell him one of yours\n  rel kael +small\n  spark kael +tiny\n  say: {~You tell him something you want. He listens like it's a lesson, and then says, \"Yes. That too. I want that for you,\" and seems surprised that he can.|You trade wants with him until the stars come out. His are small and precise. Yours make him laugh. It's the best lesson either of you has had.}\n\n@event kael_dont_protect_me\ntitle: Don't Protect Me\npool: kael_nessa_kael_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: bond(kael) == \"partner\"\nart: bolt\ntags: romance\nscene: A corridor floor glazed with ice around another student's boots, a pale boy standing between them and his partner, frost in the air.\ntext: An older student says something sharp to you in the corridor, and the floor under his boots freezes solid. He goes down hard. Kael steps between you, frost in the air around him. \"Where I'm from,\" he says, very quietly, \"you guard what's yours.\"\n> left: \"I'm not yours to guard.\"\n  spark kael -tiny\n  rel kael +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: The frost stops. He stares at you. \"I didn't — \" he says, and then, slowly, \"No. You're not.\" He helps the other boy up himself. Later he says, \"Tell me when I do it again,\" and you do, twice, and then he stops.\n> right: Let him, this once\n  spark kael +small\n  renown +tiny\n  rel kael -tiny\n  say: The corridor clears around you both. People step aside for you for a week. Kael walks you to every lesson, and you start to understand that he doesn't know another way to say it.\n\n@event kael_proud_of_you\ntitle: Proud of You\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: bond(kael) == \"partner\" and knows(kael.mother)\nweight: 12\nart: letter\ntags: romance\nscene: A pale boy on the edge of a bed with an unopened letter in Northern script, his partner sitting down beside him.\ntext: Kael has done something hard, a practical he wasn't sure he'd pass, and passed it well. There's a letter from home on his bed. He hasn't opened it. \"It won't say it,\" he tells you. \"It never does. I don't know why I keep hoping.\"\n> left: Say it for her\n  spark kael +medium\n  if flag(kael_kindness):\n    say: \"I'm proud of you,\" you say. He looks at you the way he did his first night here, at a piece of bread nobody had to offer. Then he puts the letter in a drawer, unopened, and doesn't take it out again.\n  else:\n    say: \"I'm proud of you,\" you say. He doesn't move for a long time. Then he puts the letter in a drawer, unopened, and asks you to say it again, just once, so he'll know he didn't imagine it.\n> right: Open it together\n  rel kael +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You open it together. It's about the weather and his uncle's knee. At the bottom, after the signature, in a different, hurried hand: \"Well done.\" He reads it eleven times. It's more than he's ever had.\n\n@event kael_ex\ntitle: Frost Recedes\nspecializes: soc_ex_encounter\nwhen: seen(kael_ex) == 0\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nart: snow\ntags: romance\nscene: A long corridor of tall windows, frost on every pane except the ones nearest a student walking past alone.\ntext: You notice it on a cold morning: the corridor windows are frosted over, every one, except the panes you walk past. Kael is at the far end. When he sees you, the frost on his side of the hall draws back, carefully, the way you'd step out of someone's road.\n> left: Walk on\n  mood -small\n  say: You walk on. The frost closes behind you. It's a kindness, you think, of a kind: he's keeping out of your way so precisely that it must take all his attention.\n> right: Walk up to him\n  rel kael +small\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      bond kael friend\n      say: You say his name. He flinches, and then doesn't. \"Until the thaw?\" you ask, and he almost smiles. \"Until the thaw,\" he says. The frost comes back across the windows, slowly, all of them, yours too.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: \"Don't,\" he says, very gently. \"Please. I'm not good at this part. I wasn't taught it.\" He goes. The windows stay clear where you stand.\n\n@event kael_gift_given\ntitle: A Snowflake Under Glass\nspecializes: soc_gift_given\nwhen: seen(kael_gift_given) == 0\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nweight: 10\nart: snow\ntags: social\nscene: A tiny glass dome in a student's palm, a single perfect snowflake inside it that has not melted.\ntext: There's a parcel in your pigeonhole, cold to the touch. Inside, wrapped in a scrap of fur, is a tiny glass dome with a single snowflake in it. It hasn't melted. A note in sharp Northern handwriting: \"It won't. I asked it not to.\"\n> left: Keep it on your windowsill\n  rel kael +small\n  mood +small\n  say: It catches the light every morning. On the hottest day of the year you check it, worried. The snowflake is still there, perfect. So, you think, is he: careful, and very stubborn about what lasts.\n> right: Give him something warm back\n  gold -small\n  rel kael +medium\n  say: You find him a scarf, thick and red and absurd. He wears it at once, even indoors. Frost forms on it and he picks it off, flake by flake, as if he's protecting it.\n\n# ---------------- Nessa ----------------\n\n@event nessa_the_one_phrase\ntitle: The One Phrase\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 9 and courting(nessa) and spark(nessa) < 40\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: feather\ntags: social romance\nscene: A girl at a cluttered desk with a self-writing quill scratching beside her, glaring at it, while another student leans on the desk.\ntext: You tell Nessa something about your day and her quill writes it down. {~It always does.|You're used to it by now.} \"Sorry,\" she says. \"It takes everything. There's one phrase that stops it, you know. I've never used it.\" She doesn't say what it is. She looks at you as if she's deciding.\n> left: Don't ask; let her decide\n  learn nessa.off_record\n  spark nessa +small\n  say: {~You say nothing. She keeps looking, and the quill keeps writing, and then she tells you what the phrase is, which is not the same as using it. \"Off the record,\" she says. \"Not yet.\" The quill writes that down too.|You wait. \"Off the record,\" she says at last, as a definition, not a command. \"That's the phrase. Someday I'll say it to somebody.\" The quill underlines \"someday\" twice.}\n> right: Guess the phrase\n  rel nessa +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {~You guess \"please stop\". The quill writes \"PLEASE STOP\" in capitals and keeps going. Nessa laughs so hard she has to sit down.|You guess \"the end\". The quill writes \"THE END\" and then, to make a point, a sequel. Nessa gives you a mark out of ten. It's a four.}\n\n@pool kael_nessa_nessa_social_routine\nactor: nessa\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nweight: 10\n\n@event nessa_quill_draft\ntitle: The Quill's Draft\npool: kael_nessa_nessa_social_routine\ntone: light\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 9 and spark(nessa) >= 5 and courting(nessa)\nart: paper\ntags: social romance\nscene: A sheet of newsprint on a desk with an over-written headline about two students, a mortified girl snatching for it.\ntext: There's a draft on Nessa's desk in the quill's handwriting, headed \"EXCLUSIVE: THE EDITOR AND THE SOURCE.\" It's about you. And her. And a great many moonlit walks that never happened. Nessa comes in, sees you reading it, and turns the colour of red ink.\n> left: Hand it back unread\n  learn nessa.quill\n  spark nessa +small\n  say: You fold it and hand it back. \"I didn't read past the headline,\" you lie. \"It invents things,\" she says, too fast. \"Left alone. It makes up the news.\" She folds it smaller. She doesn't throw it away.\n> right: Read the best line aloud\n  learn nessa.quill\n  rel nessa +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You read out \"their eyes met over the proofs like two scandals\". Nessa puts her face in her hands. \"It makes things up,\" she says, through her fingers. \"When I'm not watching it.\" Then, muffled: \"That line's not even good.\"\n\n@event nessa_deadline_night\ntitle: Deadline Night\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 9 and bond(nessa) != \"ex\"\nrepeat: 3\ntime: evening night\nweight: 10\nart: paper\ntags: social romance\nscene: A newspaper office at night, proofs everywhere, a girl with ink on her face, a printing press waiting under a dust sheet.\ntext: The Ledger goes to the printer at midnight and Nessa is three columns short. {~Ink on her face, ink in her hair.|The press is waiting under its dust sheet.} \"I don't need help,\" she says. \"I need a miracle, or a typesetter who doesn't sleep.\"\n> left: Set type until midnight\n  spark nessa +small\n  health -tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: {~You set type with her until your fingers are black. At two minutes to midnight she slots in the last line, and kisses the press, and then, quickly, as if it were the same thing, your cheek.|You read proofs aloud while she sets them. You catch four errors. She catches you yawning, and calls it the fifth. The paper goes out on time.}\n> right: Write the third column yourself\n  challenge research journeyman:\n    win:\n      renown +tiny\n      rel nessa +small\n      say: {~You write it in an hour. She cuts a third and calls it \"almost good\", which from Nessa is a medal. It runs under \"Staff\".|Your column runs. Nessa edits it so hard it bleeds, and then pins the proof on her wall.}\n    lose:\n      rel nessa +tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {~She reads it, says \"no\", kindly, and writes the column herself in twenty minutes. She keeps your draft anyway.|It's not good. She tells you so, and why, for ten minutes, and you learn more about writing than in a year of essays.}\n\n@event nessa_shared_byline\ntitle: A Shared Byline\npool: kael_nessa_nessa_social_routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: rel(nessa) >= 12 and spark(nessa) >= 10 and courting(nessa)\nart: paper\ntags: social romance\nscene: A newspaper proof with two names printed side by side under a headline, one student pointing at them, the other pretending not to care.\ntext: Nessa shows you the proof of next week's lead story. You helped with it, a little: one interview, one fact you checked. Under the headline it says \"by Nessa Quill and\" — and then your name. \"It's accurate,\" she says, too casually. \"I only print what's accurate.\"\n> left: Keep your name on it\n  renown +small\n  spark nessa +small\n  say: The story runs with both your names. People stop you in the corridor about it. Nessa cuts the byline out of the proof copy and keeps it in her notebook, where you're not supposed to see.\n> right: \"It's your story. Take it.\"\n  rel nessa +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: She looks almost hurt, then very much not. \"You're the first person who's ever tried to give me credit back,\" she says. \"Fine. But you're in the acknowledgements. In large type.\"\n\n@event nessa_corrections_column\ntitle: Corrections\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: bond(nessa) == \"partner\"\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 12\nart: paper\ntags: romance\nscene: A student reading the back page of a school newspaper over breakfast, smiling at a tiny paragraph in the corrections box.\ntext: Every week the Ledger's corrections column has a paragraph only you can read. {~This week: \"Correction: last Tuesday we reported the weather as fair. It was, in fact, lovely, for reasons the editor declines to give.\"|This week: \"Correction: the editor did not, as reported, sleep at her desk. She slept on someone's shoulder, which is not a desk.\"}\n> left: Write one back\n  spark nessa +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {~You slip a correction under her door: \"Correction: the reader was not 'mildly pleased'. The reader was delighted.\" It runs the next week, verbatim, in a box with a border.|You send in a correction of the correction. She prints it with a correction of that. It goes on for a month. The rest of the school thinks the Ledger's lost its mind.}\n> right: Read it aloud at breakfast\n  rel nessa +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {~You read it aloud. Nobody else understands it. Nessa, three tables away, puts her head down on her toast.|You read it aloud with feeling. Half the table works it out. Nessa runs no corrections the following week, which is its own sort of correction.}\n\n@event nessa_not_a_story\ntitle: Not a Story\npool: kael_nessa_nessa_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: bond(nessa) == \"partner\"\nart: feather\ntags: romance\nscene: A girl holding out a typed draft to her partner across a desk, eager, the partner not taking it, the quill between them frozen mid-word.\ntext: Nessa has written a feature. It's beautiful. It's about you: where you came from, what you told her late at night, what you're afraid of. Names changed. \"Nobody would know,\" she says. \"It's the best thing I've ever written.\"\n> left: \"I'm not a story.\"\n  hint: She means it kindly; make her hear you.\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      spark nessa +medium\n      rel nessa +small\n      say: It takes an hour. She argues like an editor; you argue like someone who's been edited. At the end she stops, and looks at the draft, and puts it in the drawer with the quill. \"Off the record,\" she says. \"All of it. Always. I'm sorry.\"\n    lose:\n      spark nessa -small\n      rel nessa -tiny\n      say: She doesn't print it. She doesn't understand why, either, and she says so, at length, and you both go to bed angry. In the morning the draft is gone from her desk. You don't know where.\n> right: Let her print it\n  renown +small\n  spark nessa -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: It runs. People cry over it in the refectory. Nobody guesses it's you, quite. Nessa is radiant all week, and you feel, oddly, like a photograph of yourself.\n\n@event nessa_press_breaks\ntitle: The Press Breaks\npool: kael_nessa_nessa_social_routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: bond(nessa) == \"partner\" and year >= 2\nart: gear\ntags: romance\nscene: An old iron printing press with a cracked frame, a girl sitting on the floor beside it with her knees drawn up, ink on her hands.\ntext: The Ledger's press has cracked down the middle, a hundred years old and done. Nessa is sitting on the floor beside it. \"Twenty years we haven't missed an issue,\" she says, very steady. \"I'll copy it out by hand. Every copy. I'll chain myself to it if I have to.\" She isn't joking.\n> left: Copy it out with her, all night\n  learn nessa.press\n  spark nessa +small\n  health -small\n  say: You copy out three hundred Ledgers by hand, by candlelight, with half the club. At dawn the last one dries. Nessa falls asleep on the stack, ink on her nose, and the issue goes out on time, every copy in a different hand.\n> right: Find Mir\n  if met(mir):\n    learn nessa.press\n    rel nessa +small\n    rel mir +small\n    say: Mir looks at the press for a long minute, says \"oh, you poor old thing\", and spends three nights with a welding charm. It prints again, louder than before. Nessa hugs Mir so hard their goggles come off.\n  else:\n    learn nessa.press\n    rel nessa +small\n    gold -small\n    say: You don't know anyone who can mend it, so you hire someone from the village. It costs. It prints. Nessa says she'll pay you back; you both know she'll pay you back in column inches.\n\n@event nessa_ex\ntitle: Retraction\nspecializes: soc_ex_encounter\nwhen: seen(nessa_ex) == 0\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nart: paper\ntags: romance\nscene: A tiny boxed paragraph on the back page of a school newspaper, a student reading it alone in a window seat.\ntext: In this week's Ledger, at the very bottom of the back page, in the smallest type the press has: \"Retraction. The editor regrets an earlier statement, made off the record, that she was certain. She was not. She is sorry.\" Nessa is in the doorway, watching you read it.\n> left: Fold the paper and go\n  mood -small\n  say: You fold the Ledger and put it in your bag. You don't look back. Weeks later you find it again, the little paragraph worn soft where you've folded it, over and over.\n> right: Go and talk to her\n  rel nessa +small\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      bond nessa friend\n      say: \"On the record,\" you say, \"I'd like to be friends.\" Her quill, at her shoulder, writes it down, and she lets it. \"Printed,\" she says. \"No retractions.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: She starts to say something, and then her quill starts writing, and she looks at it, and at you, and puts it away. \"Not yet,\" she says. \"I'm still drafting.\"\n\n@event nessa_argument\ntitle: On the Record\nspecializes: soc_argument\nwhen: seen(nessa_argument) == 0\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nweight: 8\nart: bolt\ntags: social\nscene: Two students arguing in a corridor, one of them with a notebook out, a quill floating at her shoulder taking it all down.\ntext: You and Nessa are arguing about something small, and it's getting less small, and halfway through you realise her quill is taking it down. Verbatim. With stage directions. \"Ignore it,\" she says. \"Go on. You were saying I'm 'impossible'?\"\n> left: Make her put the quill away\n  rel nessa +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Not with that thing listening.\" She glares, and then, astonishingly, puts it in her pocket. The argument dies without an audience. \"You're right,\" she says. \"Don't quote me.\"\n> right: Argue for the record\n  mood -tiny\n  challenge appeal vs nessa.charm:\n    win:\n      rel nessa +medium\n      charm +small\n      say: You argue it out for the record, both of you playing to the quill, until you're laughing too hard to finish. The transcript is pinned on her wall under \"best interview of the year\".\n    lose:\n      rel nessa -small\n      say: You argue for the record. She wins on points, and prints a column the next week about \"people who say 'impossible' when they mean 'right'\". You aren't named. Everyone knows.\n"},{"path":"content/events/romance/lyra_tobias.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Romance sets (world expansion 1, plan §2a): Lyra and Tobias. Each set: spark beats before the confession (two of\n#  them repeatable from friendship 9), three partner beats (a tradition, a quarrel in their own voice, a crisis), the\n#  ex card (a specialization of soc_ex_encounter), and their versions of generic social cards (`specializes:`).\n#  Spark beats need friendship ≥ 9 and no partner or ex bond with them; the confessions themselves are unchanged.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Lyra ----------------\n\n@event lyra_second_place\ntitle: Second Place, Again\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 9 and courting(lyra) and term\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: trophy\ntags: social romance\nscene: A posted list of class rankings on a stone wall, two names at the top, a red-haired girl staring at the second one.\ntext: The rankings are up. {~Your name is above hers, by one mark. Lyra stands in front of the list with her arms folded. \"One mark,\" she says. \"I'll have it back by Frostcall.\"|Her name is above yours, by one mark, and she's not looking at it. She's looking at you. \"One mark,\" she says. \"I'll make it two by Frostcall.\"|Your names are level. The registrar has had to put you side by side, alphabetically, and she hates it. \"A tie,\" she says, arms folded. \"I'll break it by Frostcall.\"}\n> left: \"You can try.\"\n  rel lyra +small\n  spark lyra +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: {~Her eyes narrow, and something in them lights. \"Oh, I will,\" she says, and walks off already planning. You find yourself studying harder too, which you suspect was the point.|\"Good,\" she says, with relish. \"I'd hate to win against someone who wasn't trying.\" She's almost smiling.|\"Try,\" she repeats, as if tasting the word. She's in the library before you the next morning. So are you.}\n> right: \"It's just a list.\"\n  mood +tiny\n  rel lyra -tiny\n  say: {~\"It's never just a list,\" she says, and for a second she sounds about twelve. Then the Vaneshade face comes back. \"But you wouldn't know that.\"|She looks at you as though you'd said the sun was optional. Then she walks away to study.|\"Lucky you,\" she says, and means something you don't catch until later.}\n\n@event lyra_glove\ntitle: The Glove\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 9 and spark(lyra) >= 8 and courting(lyra)\nweight: 10\nart: hand\ntags: social romance\nscene: A red-haired girl dropping a leather duelling glove at a student's feet in an empty corridor, her chin very high.\ntext: Lyra drops a glove at your feet in an empty corridor. An actual glove. \"Vaneshades settle things properly,\" she says. \"You've been in my head all term. It's insufferable. Dawn, the practice circle. If I win, you stop.\" She doesn't say stop what. She's gone red to the ears.\n> left: Pick it up\n  rel lyra +small\n  spark lyra +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: You meet her at dawn. Neither of you casts a single spell. You stand in the frost and talk for an hour instead, about nothing, and she forgets to win, and so do you.\n> right: Hand it back, politely\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  spark lyra -small\n  say: \"You dropped this.\" Her face does something complicated and then goes perfectly smooth. \"So I did,\" she says, and puts it on, and that's the end of it. Almost.\n\n@event lyra_after_hours\ntitle: After the Torches\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 9 and courting(lyra) and spark(lyra) < 40\nrepeat: 4\ntime: evening night\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: social romance\nscene: An empty practice hall at night, the torches out, a red-haired girl lighting a single flame in her palm so two people can see.\ntext: {~The duelling hall's torches go out at ten. Lyra lights one flame in her palm and keeps practising.|Lyra is still in the practice hall after the torches are out, working one form over and over.|The torches are out again. Lyra's palm flame is the only light, and she's waiting, as if she knew you'd come.} \"You're late,\" she says. You didn't say you were coming.\n> left: Spar with her\n  arcana +small\n  spark lyra +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You spar until your arms shake. She says nothing nice, but she shows you, without explaining, how she shapes the turn of a flame. Then she blows it out, and you walk back in the dark, close enough to bump shoulders.|You spar in the light of her hand. She's better than you. She tells you exactly how, and stays until you've fixed it.|You fight until you're both laughing at a missed block. Lyra doesn't laugh, usually. She stops, surprised at herself.}\n> right: Make her stop\n  rel lyra +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {~\"Enough,\" you say, and take her wrist. The flame goes out. \"You'll be useless tomorrow.\" She glares at you in the dark, and then lets you walk her back, which she almost never does.|You sit on the bench and refuse to move until she does. It takes an hour. Your own reading doesn't get done.|You make her eat something. She eats it as if it's an insult, and then eats yours.}\n\n@event lyra_partner_watch\ntitle: Burnout Watch\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"partner\"\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 12\nart: candle\ntags: romance\nscene: A candlelit desk buried in books, a red-haired girl asleep on her notes, someone gently taking the quill out of her hand.\ntext: You've an arrangement now, the two of you: at midnight, you go and find her, wherever she is, and make her stop. Tonight it's the library. She's on chapter nine of a book with eleven. {~\"Two more,\" she says, without looking up.|\"Ten minutes,\" she says, which means two hours.}\n> left: Make her stop\n  spark lyra +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {~You close the book on her fingers, gently. She lets you. On the way back she falls asleep against your shoulder, standing up, which you didn't know was possible.|You take the quill. She says something cutting. Then she says thank you, very quietly, as if it costs her a mark.}\n> right: Stay and finish it with her\n  study +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You pull up a chair and read chapter ten aloud while she takes notes. By two you're both asleep on the table, and the librarian covers you with a cloak.|You stay. She finishes. She says finishing a book with someone there is a different thing entirely.}\n\n@event lyra_not_second\ntitle: Not Second to Anyone\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"partner\"\nweight: 10\nart: flame\ntags: romance\nscene: A red-haired girl standing very straight in a corridor, eyes bright with anger, a crumpled tournament list in her fist.\ntext: Lyra has found out you skipped her match for {~a study group|a club meeting|a friend's crisis}. She is very calm, which is worse than shouting. \"I have been second to my mother's ambitions my whole life,\" she says. {~\"I won't be second to a study group.\"|\"I won't be second to a club.\"|\"I know it was a crisis. I know. I still wanted you there.\"}\n> left: Talk it through, properly\n  hint: She wants to be argued with, not soothed.\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      spark lyra +medium\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: You don't apologise first; you argue. You tell her she's not second, she's the one you'd have fought for a seat beside. She listens. Then she kisses you, angrily, and says next time you'll be in the front row.\n    lose:\n      spark lyra -small\n      rel lyra -small\n      say: {~Everything you say sounds like an excuse, because it is.|Everything you say sounds like an excuse, because it is.|You were right to go, and you both know it, and somehow that makes it harder to say.} She nods, very correctly, and walks away. She's at your next lecture. She doesn't sit next to you.\n> right: Apologise, and mean it\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  mood -small\n  say: \"I'm sorry.\" She looks at you a long time. \"Vaneshades don't accept apologies,\" she says. \"But I'm not a Vaneshade with you.\" It's forgiveness. It still stings, for both of you.\n\n@event lyra_empty_chair\ntitle: Her Mother's Empty Chair\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: bond(lyra) == \"partner\" and knows(lyra.mother)\nweight: 14\nart: duel\ntags: romance\nscene: A crowded duelling gallery with one empty velvet chair in the front row, a red-haired duellist glancing at it before her bout.\ntext: Lyra's final bout of the year. In the front row of the gallery is a velvet chair with a card on it: \"Reserved — Lady Vaneshade.\" It stays empty. Lyra looks at it once, just before she bows, and then at you.\n> left: Sit in the chair\n  spark lyra +medium\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You walk down to the front and sit in Lady Vaneshade's chair. People stare. Lyra sees, and something in her shoulders lets go. She wins in under a minute. Afterwards she says, \"That was a terrible breach of etiquette,\" and doesn't let go of your hand.\n> right: Cheer louder than anyone\n  spark lyra +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You shout her name until the gallery joins in. She doesn't look at the chair again. She wins, and turns, and looks for you, only you, the way she used to look for the scoreboard.\n\n@event lyra_ex\ntitle: Cold Hearth\nspecializes: soc_ex_encounter\nwhen: seen(lyra_ex) == 0\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nart: flame\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people passing in a corridor, one a red-haired girl who keeps her chin high and her eyes forward.\ntext: Lyra passes you in the corridor with her chin high and her eyes forward. She's perfectly polite now, which is how you know. A Vaneshade's coldest weapon is good manners.\n> left: Let her pass\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She passes. You both keep walking. Behind you, a torch on the wall flares, briefly, as if someone's temper had slipped.\n> right: Say her name\n  rel lyra +small\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      bond lyra friend\n      say: She stops. Turns. \"What,\" she says. You say you miss her as a rival, at least. She almost laughs. \"Library,\" she says. \"Thursday. I'll destroy you.\" It's an olive branch, the Vaneshade way.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: \"Yes?\" she says, and waits, and you don't have the words. \"I thought not,\" she says, not unkindly, and goes.\n\n@event lyra_partner_neglect\ntitle: \"I Don't Wait\"\nspecializes: soc_partner_neglect\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 5\nart: hourglass\ntags: romance\nscene: A red-haired girl in a duelling coat standing in a doorway with her arms folded, two cups of cold tea on the table behind her.\ntext: Lyra is in your doorway with her arms folded. \"I don't wait for people,\" she says. \"I've waited for you three times this month. I counted. I'm a Vaneshade; I count everything.\" She doesn't say she missed you. She doesn't need to.\n> left: \"Tomorrow. Dawn. I'll be there first.\"\n  spark lyra +small\n  health -tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: You're there first, at dawn, with two cups of tea that are actually hot. She takes one and says nothing, which from her is an embrace.\n> right: \"You know what term's like.\"\n  spark lyra -medium\n  rel lyra -small\n  say: \"I do,\" she says. \"I manage.\" She walks away, perfectly upright. It's the most hurt you've ever seen her look.\n\n@event lyra_lends\ntitle: A Loan, Vaneshade Style\nspecializes: soc_borrow_money\nwhen: seen(lyra_lends) == 0\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nweight: 10\nart: pouch\ntags: social money\nscene: A red-haired girl holding out a heavy purse at arm's length, not looking at it, as if it were beneath her notice.\ntext: Lyra would sooner die than borrow. She has noticed, though, that your purse is thin, and she is holding out hers at arm's length without looking at it. \"Take what you need,\" she says. \"Don't thank me. Don't ever mention it.\"\n> left: Take a little\n  gold +medium\n  rel lyra +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You take a few crowns. She never mentions it again. Neither do you, though you pay her back in study notes she pretends not to read.\n> right: Refuse, kindly\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  say: \"I'm fine.\" She puts the purse away. \"Of course you are,\" she says, and the next day there's a meal paid for at the village inn in your name.\n\n# ---------------- Tobias ----------------\n\n@event tobias_gerald_approves\ntitle: Gerald Approves\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 9 and courting(tobias)\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: leaf\ntags: social romance\nscene: A large gentle student holding a potted mandrake in a tiny knitted hat up to another student's face, both very solemn.\ntext: Tobias holds Gerald up to your face. Gerald is wearing {~a new hat|his winter hat}. \"He's deciding about you,\" Tobias says seriously. \"He doesn't like everyone. He bit the Verdant Circle's treasurer.\" Gerald considers you with his small dark eyes.\n> left: Bow to Gerald\n  learn tobias.gerald\n  rel tobias +small\n  spark tobias +small\n  say: {~You bow. Gerald screams very softly, which Tobias says is approval. Tobias looks at you as if you'd done something wonderful. You think maybe you have.|You bow, and Gerald pats your nose with a leaf. Tobias goes pink. \"He hardly ever does that,\" he says. \"Not even to me.\"}\n> right: Offer Gerald a finger\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {~Gerald bites it. Tobias is mortified and laughs so hard he has to sit down. You're both still laughing when the greenhouse bell goes.|Gerald bites it, then lets go and looks sorry. \"He's shy,\" says Tobias. Your finger disagrees.}\n\n@event tobias_letter_aloud\ntitle: Read It to Me\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 9 and spark(tobias) >= 5 and courting(tobias)\nweight: 10\nart: letter\ntags: social romance\nscene: A large student sitting on a bench handing another a letter on farm paper, his eyes red-rimmed and tired.\ntext: Tobias has a letter from home and he hasn't opened it. \"My eyes are tired,\" he says, which isn't true. \"Would you read it? Out loud?\" It's from his mother. She writes the way people talk on farms: weather first, goats second, love last and briefly.\n> left: Read it, voices and all\n  learn tobias.ma\n  spark tobias +small\n  rel tobias +small\n  say: You read it in his mother's voice, as near as you can guess. He laughs at the goats and goes quiet at the end. \"She says she's proud,\" he says. \"She never says that.\" You read that line twice, because he asks.\n> right: Read it plainly, then talk\n  rel tobias +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You read it plainly. Then he talks about home for an hour, the farm, the frost, his mother alone at harvest, and you listen. It's a lot to carry. You carry some of it back with you.\n  if knows(tobias.after):\n    rel tobias +tiny\n    say: When he gets to coming home after graduation, you ask what he wants, not what was planned. He doesn't know yet. He says nobody has asked him before.\n\n@event tobias_umbrella\ntitle: One Umbrella\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: rel(tobias) >= 9 and courting(tobias) and spark(tobias) < 40\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: rain\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two students walking close under one enormous patched umbrella across a rainy castle courtyard, one much bigger than the other.\ntext: It's pouring, and Tobias has the biggest umbrella you've ever seen: patched, green, and older than both of you. {~\"Ma's,\" he says. \"It's seen worse.\"|\"Room for two,\" he says, \"or four, if they're small.\"}\n> left: Walk close under it\n  spark tobias +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You walk back under it, very close, because the umbrella insists, and your outside shoulder is soaked through by the door anyway. He tells you a long story about a goat that fell in a well, and you don't hear the end of it, and don't mind.|Halfway across the courtyard he stops talking. So do you. The rain does the talking, and runs down the back of your neck.}\n> right: Race him to the door\n  vigor +small\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You run. He runs after you with the umbrella and arrives soaked anyway, laughing from the belly.|You make it first, drenched. He arrives dry and very pleased with himself.}\n\n@event tobias_seed_month\ntitle: A Seed a Month\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: bond(tobias) == \"partner\"\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 12\nart: flower\ntags: romance\nscene: A large student pressing a single seed into a pot on a windowsill crowded with sprouting pots, another student watching.\ntext: Tobias gives you one seed a month, every month, in a twist of paper with its name on it. Your windowsill is crowded now. {~This month's is a sunflower. \"For when it's grey,\" he says.|This month's is a herb you've never heard of. \"For your cough,\" he says. You don't have a cough. \"Yet,\" he says.}\n> left: Plant it together\n  spark tobias +small\n  alchemy +tiny\n  say: {~You plant it together, his big hands over yours, pressing the soil down gently. He waters it. You'll remember this month by it.|He shows you how deep, how much water, where the light falls. The windowsill is a calendar now.}\n> right: Give him one back\n  rel tobias +small\n  gold -tiny\n  say: {~You buy a seed in the village and give it to him in a twist of paper. He holds it as if it were a jewel, and plants it that day.|You give him a seed back. He laughs from the belly and plants it in his best pot.}\n\n@event tobias_home_or_here\ntitle: Home or Here\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: bond(tobias) == \"partner\" and year >= 2\nweight: 10\nart: house\ntags: romance\nscene: A large student sitting on a farm gate in a dream-like vision, then on a castle wall, torn between the two.\ntext: Tobias has been quiet all week. Finally: \"Ma wants me home after. The farm. It's always been the plan.\" He doesn't look at you. \"I always thought I wanted that. I'm sorry. I'm sorry for saying it like this. I'm sorry.\"\n> left: \"Then I'll come and see the goats.\"\n  spark tobias +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He looks up so fast he nearly falls off the wall. \"You'd like the goats,\" he says. \"They'd like you.\" Neither of you knows what comes after. It's enough, for now, that you'd come.\n> right: \"Do you still want it?\"\n  rel tobias +small\n  spark tobias -tiny\n  say: He thinks for a long time, the way he thinks about seeds. \"I don't know,\" he says at last. \"I didn't know I was allowed not to know.\" You sit with him until he stops apologising.\n\n@event tobias_gerald_wilts\ntitle: Gerald Is Ill\nclass: story\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: bond(tobias) == \"partner\" and knows(tobias.gerald)\nweight: 14\nart: leaf\ntags: romance\nscene: A large student cradling a drooping potted mandrake in a knitted hat, sitting on a greenhouse floor, another student kneeling beside him.\ntext: Gerald is wilting. His hat has slipped, his leaves are grey, and he hasn't screamed in two days. Tobias is sitting on the greenhouse floor holding the pot, and he isn't apologising, which is how you know it's bad.\n> left: Brew him a tonic\n  challenge brew journeyman:\n    win:\n      spark tobias +medium\n      alchemy +small\n      say: You brew all night. At dawn Gerald drinks it, shudders, and screams, loudly, furiously, beautifully. Tobias laughs and cries at once and holds you so hard your ribs creak.\n    lose:\n      spark tobias +small\n      mood -small\n      say: The tonic does nothing. But in the morning Gerald is a little greener anyway, maybe because Tobias didn't sleep and neither did you. \"It's not your fault,\" Tobias says. \"It's nobody's. You stayed.\"\n> right: Sit with him all night\n  spark tobias +small\n  health -small\n  say: You sit on the greenhouse floor all night, Tobias holding Gerald, you holding Tobias. At dawn Gerald stirs, and screams, very softly. It's the best sound you've ever heard.\n\n@event tobias_ex\ntitle: He Still Waters Your Plant\nspecializes: soc_ex_encounter\nwhen: seen(tobias_ex) == 0\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nart: flower\ntags: romance\nscene: A large student watering a single pot on a windowsill that isn't his, glancing guiltily at the door.\ntext: You find Tobias at your window, watering the plant on your sill. He jumps. \"Sorry,\" he says. \"Sorry. It looked dry. I'll go. I'm sorry.\"\n> left: Let him go\n  mood -tiny\n  say: He goes. The plant is very well watered. You don't know whether to be grateful or sad, so you're both.\n> right: Ask him to stay a minute\n  rel tobias +small\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      bond tobias friend\n      say: He stays. You talk about the plant, and then about goats, and it's almost easy. \"Friends,\" he says, at the door, testing the word, \"is a good word. I like it.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: He stays, and stands, and apologises four times, and goes. The plant turns toward the door after him.\n\n@event tobias_dance_specialized\ntitle: The Broom Waltz, Again\nspecializes: date_dance_lesson\nwhen: seen(tobias_dance_specialized) == 0\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nweight: 10\nart: music\ntags: romance\nscene: A large student counting steps under his breath in a moonlit greenhouse, guiding another student between rows of plants.\ntext: Tobias has been practising again, he admits, with the broom. \"It complained,\" he says. \"But I'm better now. I think. Will you?\" He holds out a hand the size of a spade, very carefully.\n> left: Dance with him\n  spark tobias +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: He's better. He steps on your foot only twice, and apologises five times, and between the apologies he's a wonderful dancer, slow and sure, like a tide.\n> right: Teach him a new step\n  charm +small\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  say: You teach him a step from the Midwinter Ball. He gets it on the ninth try and is so proud he dances it alone, between the tomato plants, until the gardener throws him out.\n\n@event tobias_friend_sick\ntitle: Too Big for the Bed\nspecializes: soc_friend_sick\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 8\nart: rain\ntags: social\nscene: A very large student squeezed into a small infirmary bed, feet hanging off the end, a potted mandrake on the bedside table.\ntext: Tobias has Lake Fever, and the infirmary bed is a foot too short for him. His feet stick out at the end under a blanket. He isn't worried about himself. \"Gerald,\" he croaks. \"Who's watering Gerald?\"\n> left: Send a card, and a promise\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  say: You send a card with a drawing of Gerald in a sickbed hat. Tobias keeps it propped against the water jug and shows it to Sister Amaranth twice a day.\n> right: Water Gerald, and sit with him\n  rel tobias +medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain sick\n    say: You water Gerald, then sit with Tobias every evening until he's better. Then you're in the next bed. Tobias waters Gerald for both of you, apologising to everyone.\n  else:\n    say: {~You bring Gerald to the infirmary in his pot. Amaranth objects. Gerald screams at her. Tobias recovers mostly from laughing.|You water Gerald every morning and report on him every evening, leaf by leaf. Tobias gets better, he says, out of relief.|You read him farming almanacs aloud until he falls asleep, which takes four pages. He recovers in three days.}\n\n@event lyra_teach_spell\ntitle: A Trick Worth Learning\nspecializes: soc_teach_spell\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 8\nart: wand\ntags: social magic\nscene: A duelling hall after hours, a red-haired girl correcting another student's wand arm with two fingers, a scorched practice target behind them.\ntext: Lyra doesn't invent little spells. \"Half-useful is useless,\" she says. But she'll teach you the flourish her family has used to open a duel for two hundred years, if you swear not to tell anyone she taught it to someone outside the family. She looks as if she might regret the offer.\n> left: Learn it her way\n  challenge spell apprentice+ likely:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: She corrects your wrist eleven times. On the twelfth the flourish leaves a ribbon of flame in the air, exactly where it should be. Lyra nods once. \"Adequate,\" she says, which from her is a medal.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: The ribbon of flame goes where it shouldn't, which is your sleeve. Lyra puts it out with a flick and doesn't laugh, which is worse. \"Again,\" she says. The second time it works.\n> right: Teach her to make toast\n  rel lyra +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  if knows(lyra.toast):\n    say: You hand her a slice of bread and a toasting fork. She looks at it the way she looks at a duelling opponent. Four slices are cinders. The fifth is golden. She eats it standing up, in silence, and then says, \"Don't tell anyone that either.\"\n  else:\n    say: You offer to trade: her flourish for something useful, like toast. She goes very still. \"I can incinerate a target at forty paces,\" she says. \"I cannot make toast.\" By the fifth slice she can. She makes you swear about that too.\n"},{"path":"content/events/romance/seraphine_corvin_mir.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Romance sets (world expansion 1, plan §2a): Seraphine, Corvin and Mir. See lyra_tobias.grim for the shape.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Seraphine ----------------\n\n@event seraphine_stand_next\ntitle: Stand Next to Me\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 9 and courting(seraphine)\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: mirror\ntags: social romance\nscene: A girl in a paint-stained smock standing before a tall mirror, pulling another student by the sleeve to stand beside her.\ntext: Seraphine pulls you in front of the long mirror in the east corridor. \"Stand there,\" she says. \"No, closer.\" {~She stares at her own reflection, as if at a stranger.|She looks at herself, then at you, then at herself, and lets out a breath.} \"Seers can't see themselves,\" she says. \"Not ahead. Not unless someone's standing next to them.\"\n> left: Stay as long as she needs\n  learn seraphine.self\n  spark seraphine +small\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: {~You stand there until the candles gutter. She looks at herself the whole time, very seriously, and when you finally go, she says, \"Thank you. I was in it. A long way on, and I was in it.\"|You stand beside her a long time. At the end she reaches out and touches the mirror where your shoulders almost meet. \"There,\" she says. \"That's where I'll be.\"}\n> right: Ask what she sees\n  lore +small\n  rel seraphine -tiny\n  say: {~\"A girl,\" she says. \"Next to someone.\" She doesn't elaborate. She lets go of your sleeve.|\"Half a sentence,\" she says, and laughs, and won't finish it.}\n\n@event seraphine_tomorrow_answer\ntitle: Tomorrow's Answer\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 9 and spark(seraphine) >= 5 and courting(seraphine)\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: social romance\nscene: A seer answering a question nobody has asked yet, in a corridor, the other student frozen mid-word.\ntext: \"Yes,\" Seraphine says, as you pass her in the corridor. You haven't said anything. \"Oh,\" she says. \"You haven't asked yet. Tomorrow, then.\" She looks embarrassed, which is rare. \"I slip, sometimes. A day ahead. Someone has to tell me what today is.\"\n> left: Tell her what today is\n  learn seraphine.slips\n  spark seraphine +small\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: You tell her: the date, the weather, what's for lunch, what she did yesterday. She listens like it's poetry. \"Thursday,\" she says. \"I like Thursday. I'll stay.\" You wonder what you're going to ask her tomorrow.\n> right: Ask what you'll ask\n  mood +tiny\n  rel seraphine -tiny\n  say: \"If I tell you,\" she says, \"you won't ask it, and then I'll have answered nothing.\" She drifts off down the corridor, a day ahead of everyone.\n\n@event seraphine_dark_room\ntitle: The Dark Room\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: rel(seraphine) >= 9 and seen(seraphine_overwhelmed) > 0 and courting(seraphine) and spark(seraphine) < 40\nrepeat: 4\ntime: evening night\nweight: 10\nart: candle\ntags: social romance\nscene: A storeroom with no windows, a girl sitting on the floor in the dark among pots of paint, a crack of light from the door falling across her face.\ntext: The storeroom is a habit now. On loud days Seraphine goes there, and lately she's moved her paints in. You find her in the dark, sorting colours by touch. \"Loud again,\" she says. \"Yours is still quiet. Help? You hand me one, I guess it.\"\n> left: Sort paints in the dark\n  spark seraphine +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {~You hand her pots in the dark for an hour. She names each by its smell: ochre, viridian, a blue she says tastes of Tuesdays. She gets eleven of twelve. \"You're the quietest person I know,\" she says. It sounds like the best compliment she has.|You sort paints by feel until the bell. Your notes don't get written. At some point she leans her head on your shoulder, and the pot in her hand is the right colour, and she doesn't explain.}\n> right: Bring her a candle\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: {~You bring a single candle and set it by the door. \"That's better,\" she says. \"One small light. It doesn't shout.\"|You bring a candle. She watches it as if it were a friend, and the noise goes away, she says, a little.}\n\n@event seraphine_already_happened\ntitle: She Tells You the Ending\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: bond(seraphine) == \"partner\"\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 12\nart: book\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people curled in a window seat with one book between them, one of them covering the page with her hand.\ntext: You read together now, the two of you, one book between you in the window seat. Seraphine always knows the ending. {~Today she's trying very hard not to say it.|Today she's covering the last page with her hand.} \"Don't look ahead,\" she says. \"It's better not knowing. I'm told.\"\n> left: Read it slowly, together\n  spark seraphine +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {~You read aloud, slowly, one page at a time. She doesn't say the ending. At the end she says, \"I was wrong. It ends happier than I saw.\" She is always glad to be wrong about endings.|You read and she listens, and twice she starts to say something and stops. When you finish, she's smiling. \"I didn't know,\" she says. \"I didn't let myself.\"}\n> right: Ask her to tell you\n  rel seraphine +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~She tells you the ending, delighted, in half a sentence. You finish the other half together, wrong, and laugh.|She tells you. It's terrible. You read it anyway, and she was right, and you both throw the book across the room.}\n\n@event seraphine_dont_look\ntitle: Don't Look Ahead\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: bond(seraphine) == \"partner\"\nweight: 10\nart: eye\ntags: romance\nscene: A seer with paint on her hands looking guilty, another student holding up a canvas that shows them both, older.\ntext: You find a painting under Seraphine's bed: the two of you, older, somewhere you've never been. \"I didn't mean to look,\" she says. \"It just came. I'm sorry. I know you didn't want to know.\"\n> left: \"Tell me about it, then.\"\n  spark seraphine +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: She tells you, half a sentence at a time: a house by water, a dog with one ear up, a garden that does badly. \"That's all,\" she says. \"That's all I let myself see.\" You hang the painting where you'll both see it.\n> right: \"Don't look again. Promise.\"\n  spark seraphine -tiny\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: She promises. You can see what it costs her: it's like asking someone not to hear. But she keeps it. The painting goes back under the bed, face down.\n\n@event seraphine_vision_hurt\ntitle: She Won't Let You Go\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: bond(seraphine) == \"partner\" and term\nweight: 14\nart: hand\ntags: romance\nscene: A girl in a paint-stained smock gripping another student's wrist in a doorway, her face white, refusing to let go.\ntext: Seraphine catches your wrist in the doorway and won't let go. Her face is white. \"Not today,\" she says. \"Don't go out today. I saw it. I saw you hurt.\" She can't say how, or where. Her hands are shaking.\n> left: Stay in, for her\n  spark seraphine +medium\n  study -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You stay in. Nothing happens, all day. At sunset she lets go of your wrist and sits down, very suddenly, and cries, and doesn't explain whether it was because you stayed or because it was never going to happen. You'll never know. She says that's the point.\n> right: Go, and come back to prove it\n  rel seraphine +tiny\n  health -small\n  say: You go. You come back with a twisted ankle from a stair that wasn't where it should be. Seraphine looks at it and laughs, horribly relieved. \"That's all?\" she says. \"That's all it was?\" She bandages it herself, shaking.\n\n@event seraphine_ex\ntitle: She Painted the Ending\nspecializes: soc_ex_encounter\nwhen: seen(seraphine_ex) == 0\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nart: mirror\ntags: romance\nscene: A girl in a paint-stained smock handing a small canvas to a former lover in a corridor, both careful not to touch hands.\ntext: Seraphine stops you in the corridor and gives you a small canvas. It's the two of you, walking away from each other down a long hall, both looking back. \"I painted it last spring,\" she says. \"Before. I'm sorry. I knew.\"\n> left: Take it, and go\n  mood -small\n  say: You take it. You don't hang it. You don't throw it away, either. Some nights you look at it, the two figures looking back.\n> right: Ask if she painted after\n  rel seraphine +small\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      bond seraphine friend\n      say: \"After?\" She smiles, for the first time in a while. \"Two people having tea. Arguing about a book. I don't know when.\" She tucks the painting under your arm. \"Friends, I think. Yes.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: \"I don't look after,\" she says. \"Not with you. Not anymore.\" She walks away, a day ahead, already gone.\n\n@event seraphine_partner_neglect\ntitle: You Were in a Different Week\nspecializes: soc_partner_neglect\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 5\nart: hourglass\ntags: romance\nscene: A seer waiting on a window seat with two cups of tea gone cold, looking up as another student finally arrives.\ntext: Seraphine is on the window seat with two cold cups of tea. \"I waited,\" she says. \"I thought you were coming on Tuesday. Then I thought I'd slipped a day. Then I thought you'd slipped a week.\" She almost smiles. \"It's hard to tell, with you, lately.\"\n> left: Sit, and stay all evening\n  spark seraphine +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You sit with her and drink the cold tea. She tells you the date three times, to be sure. You stay until the candle's out. It's Thursday. She's sure of it now.\n> right: \"I'm here now, isn't that enough?\"\n  spark seraphine -medium\n  rel seraphine -small\n  say: \"Now,\" she says, as if it were a word in a foreign language. She pours out the cold tea and goes, a day ahead of you.\n\n@event seraphine_confess_generic\ntitle: She Already Knew\nspecializes: soc_confess_generic\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nweight: 20\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: A seer listening to a confession with a small patient smile, a sketchbook open on her knee showing the very scene.\ntext: You've rehearsed this. You start to say it. Seraphine holds up her sketchbook: it's this moment, the two of you, drawn weeks ago. \"I know,\" she says gently. \"I've known since spring. I just didn't know what I'd say.\"\n> left: \"Well? What do you say?\"\n  bond seraphine partner\n  spark seraphine +large\n  mood +medium\n  say: She closes the sketchbook. \"I didn't let myself look,\" she says. \"I wanted to hear it.\" Then she kisses you, and for once, she's as surprised as anyone.\n> right: Let it rest, for now\n  spark seraphine -small\n  rel seraphine +small\n  say: \"Then let's not,\" she says, with relief or disappointment, you can't tell. She tears the drawing out and gives it to you. \"For later,\" she says. \"If there's a later.\"\n\n# ---------------- Corvin ----------------\n\n@event corvin_lucky_deck\ntitle: The Lucky Deck\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 9 and flag(corvin_deck_back) and courting(corvin)\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: cards\ntags: social romance\nscene: A lanky student shuffling an old, soft-cornered deck of cards with impossible speed, grinning across a dormitory table.\ntext: Corvin deals you a hand from an old deck, soft at the corners and smelling of pipe smoke. {~\"Grandfather's,\" he says. \"It really is lucky. Watch.\"|\"Cut,\" he says. \"Any card. It'll be the queen. It's always the queen.\"} You win, which shouldn't be possible against Corvin.\n> left: Ask about his grandfather\n  learn corvin.grandfather\n  spark corvin +small\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: {~The jokes stop. He tells you about a man who could find a lost thing in any house, and who taught him to shuffle on rainy Sundays. \"He'd have liked you, saint,\" he says. \"He liked people who lost honestly.\"|He talks for an hour, and none of it is a joke. Then he deals again, and cheats outrageously, so you'll know he's fine.}\n> right: Play for real stakes\n  gold +tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {~You play for coppers. You win, because the deck likes you. Corvin is delighted and furious and asks for a rematch.|You play until midnight and come out four coppers up. \"Beginner's luck,\" he says. \"It's the deck. It's cheating for you.\"}\n\n@event corvin_last_warning\ntitle: Last Warning\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 9 and spark(corvin) >= 5 and courting(corvin)\nweight: 10\nart: lock\ntags: social romance\nscene: A lanky student sitting on a staircase holding an official letter with a red seal, no grin at all.\ntext: Corvin is sitting on the stairs with a letter with a red seal. For once he isn't grinning. \"Last warning,\" he says. \"One more detention and they send me home.\" He folds it very small. \"Home isn't a good place, saint. Don't ask.\"\n> left: Promise to keep him out of trouble\n  learn corvin.last_warning\n  spark corvin +small\n  rel corvin +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"You?\" he says. \"Keep me out of trouble?\" But he looks at you a long time, and the grin comes back, smaller and realer. \"Fine. Deal. You're my conscience now. Terrible job. No pay.\" That night you lie awake counting his detentions.\n> right: Tell him to stop being stupid\n  learn corvin.last_warning\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: \"Wow,\" he says. \"Thanks, saint.\" But he doesn't pick a single lock all month. You know, because you check.\n\n@event corvin_route_named\ntitle: A Route Named After You\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: rel(corvin) >= 9 and courting(corvin) and spark(corvin) < 40\nrepeat: 4\ntime: evening night\nweight: 10\nart: map\ntags: social romance\nscene: A lanky student unrolling a hand-drawn map of the castle's hidden passages on a dormitory floor, one route inked in a different colour.\ntext: Corvin unrolls a map of the castle's passages on your floor. One route is in green ink, new. {~\"Found it last week,\" he says. \"Kitchens to the roof, no stairs.\"|\"Library to the lake,\" he says, \"in four minutes, if you don't mind a ladder.\"} At the bottom, he's labelled it with your name. \"Every route needs a name,\" he says, not looking at you.\n> left: Walk it with him tonight\n  spark corvin +small\n  guile +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You walk it at midnight. It comes out on the roof, under more stars than you knew the castle had. Corvin doesn't say anything clever. He doesn't need to.|You walk the whole route, bumping shoulders in the narrow parts. At the end he says, \"Well? Is it yours?\" It is.}\n> right: Ask why your name\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: {~\"Because,\" he says, and then nothing, which from Corvin is a whole speech. He rolls the map up very carefully.|\"It's a good name for a route,\" he says. \"Doesn't give anything away.\" He means the opposite.}\n\n@event corvin_goat_clause\ntitle: The Goat Clause\nspecializes: soc_borrow_money\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 6\nart: pouch\ntags: social money\nscene: A grinning student holding out a scrawled contract with a drawing of a goat at the bottom, an empty purse in his other hand.\ntext: Corvin needs fifteen crowns. He's drawn up a contract, very official, with a clause at the bottom: \"In the event of non-payment, the lender shall receive one (1) goat.\" He's drawn the goat. It's a good goat.\n> left: Lend it\n  gold -medium\n  rel corvin +medium\n  learn corvin.trolls\n  queue soc_loan_repaid in 2 with corvin\n  say: You sign. He swears he'll pay you back in three weeks, which would be a personal record, and tells you the goat story anyway, because you asked about the clause. The trolls still have it. It runs them now.\n> right: Refuse, for the goat's sake\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: \"The goat deserves better,\" you say. He laughs so hard he has to sit down. Then he finds the fifteen crowns somewhere else, and you decide not to ask where.\n\n@event corvin_lock_left_open\ntitle: A Lock Left Open\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: bond(corvin) == \"partner\"\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 12\nart: key\ntags: romance\nscene: A door in a castle corridor standing slightly ajar at midnight, a note pinned to it in a messy hand.\ntext: You've a tradition now: once a month Corvin leaves a lock open somewhere in the castle, a door that's always shut, and a note on it. {~Tonight it's the clock tower: \"Up. Bring cake.\"|Tonight it's the music room: \"Play me something bad.\"} You have to find it first.\n> left: Find it, follow it\n  spark corvin +small\n  guile +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You find it at midnight. He's waiting at the top with a blanket and a cake he definitely didn't buy. You eat it watching the clock's hands move from the wrong side.|You find the door. He's inside, in the dark, playing a terrible tune on a borrowed piano, just for you.}\n> right: Leave a lock open for him\n  rel corvin +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {~You pick a lock, badly, and leave a note: \"Your turn.\" He finds it in ten minutes and is so proud of you he can't speak.|You leave the observatory door open with a note. He finds it, and you, and says you're a terrible influence.}\n\n@event corvin_honest_once\ntitle: Honest for Once\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: bond(corvin) == \"partner\"\nweight: 10\nart: mask\ntags: romance\nscene: A lanky student turning away from another, joking with his back turned, his shoulders tense.\ntext: Corvin has been joking all evening, and it's getting thin. Something's wrong. Every time you ask, he makes another joke, quicker. \"It's nothing, saint,\" he says. \"Nothing's ever anything. That's the trick.\"\n> left: Make him tell you\n  hint: He deflects; get past the jokes.\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      spark corvin +medium\n      rel corvin +small\n      say: You don't let him off. Eventually the jokes run out, and he tells you: his brother wrote. Wants money. Threatened to come. You sit with him while he says it all, plainly, no jokes. {seen(corvin_brother) > 0: \"You knew already,\" he says. \"I still had to say it out loud.\"|\"That's the first time I've said that to anyone,\" he says.}\n    lose:\n      spark corvin -small\n      say: Every question gets a better joke. You end up laughing despite yourself, and he looks relieved and sad at once, and you know you lost.\n> right: Let him joke\n  mood +tiny\n  rel corvin +tiny\n  say: You let him joke. You laugh at the good ones. At the end of the night he says, very quietly, \"Thanks for not making me.\" It's honest. For once.\n\n@event corvin_brother_again\ntitle: The Brother, Again\nclass: story\ntone: grim\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: bond(corvin) == \"partner\" and knows(corvin.brother)\nweight: 14\nart: hand\ntags: romance\nscene: A tough older man in a worn coat looming at the castle gate, a lanky student standing in front of another student protectively.\ntext: Corvin's brother is at the castle gate again. This time he isn't asking Corvin for money. He's looking at you. \"So this is who you've been spending it on,\" he says. Corvin steps in front of you without a joke anywhere in him.\n> left: Stand beside Corvin, not behind\n  spark corvin +medium\n  rel corvin +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You step up beside him. The brother looks at the two of you a long time, then spits, and goes. Corvin shakes for an hour afterwards. {chosen(corvin_brother, left): \"Twice now,\" he says. \"You keep standing next to me.\"|\"Nobody's ever done that,\" he says. \"Stood next to me.\"}\n> right: Pay him to go\n  gold -large\n  spark corvin +small\n  say: You pay the brother what he asks, which is a great deal. He goes. Corvin is furious with you, and grateful, and furious about being grateful. \"Never again,\" he says. \"Promise me.\" You promise. You'd do it again.\n\n@event corvin_ex\ntitle: New Locks\nspecializes: soc_ex_encounter\nwhen: seen(corvin_ex) == 0\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nart: lock\ntags: romance\nscene: A lanky student kneeling at a dormitory door fitting a new lock, looking up at his ex with a grin that doesn't reach his eyes.\ntext: Corvin is changing the lock on his door. It's a very good lock. \"Don't take it personally, saint,\" he says, with a grin that stops before his eyes. \"Old habits. Somebody once had a key.\"\n> left: Leave him to it\n  mood -small\n  say: You leave him to it. You hear the lock click behind you, very final, and very well made.\n> right: \"It's a good lock.\"\n  rel corvin +small\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      bond corvin friend\n      say: He looks up. Then he laughs, a real one. \"It's the best lock in the castle,\" he says. \"I'll show you how it works. You'll never get in.\" It's an invitation, of a kind.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: \"It is,\" he says. \"Best I've got.\" He turns back to it. That's the end of the conversation.\n\n@event corvin_partner_date\ntitle: Supper on Someone Else's Roof\nspecializes: soc_partner_date\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 4\nart: meal\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people sitting on a high castle roof with a stolen picnic between them, the kitchens' chimney smoking behind them.\ntext: Corvin's idea of a date is supper on a roof you're not allowed on, made of food from a kitchen you're not allowed in. \"It's not stealing,\" he says. \"It's redistribution. The cook knows. She likes me.\"\n> left: Climb up\n  spark corvin +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You eat stolen pie on the roof and watch the lake go dark. He points out every window he's ever climbed through. There are a lot.\n> right: Bring the cook along\n  rel corvin +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You talk the cook into joining you. She climbs the roof better than either of you and brings better pie. Corvin is thrilled and slightly outclassed.\n\n@event corvin_saint_jibe\ntitle: \"Saint\"\nspecializes: align_saint_jibe\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nrepeat: 12\nweight: 10\nart: scales\ntags: social lumen\nscene: A lanky student leaning in a doorway with one arm across it, grinning, while another student stands in front of him with an armful of books.\ntext: Corvin leans across the doorway with one arm. \"Here comes the saint,\" he says, loud enough for the corridor, and then, lower, grinning: \"Does it get heavy? Being better than me? Asking for a friend. The friend is me.\"\n> left: \"It does, actually.\"\n  rel corvin +small\n  mood -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: The grin goes. He looks at you properly for a second, then takes half your books without asking and carries them to the library. \"There,\" he says. \"Lighter.\" He doesn't call you saint again that week.\n> right: \"You'd know, sinner.\"\n  mood +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: He laughs so hard he has to let go of the doorway. \"Sinner,\" he repeats, delighted, as if you've given him a title. By dinner he's signing his notes with it.\n\n# ---------------- Mir ----------------\n\n@event mir_owl_remembers\ntitle: The Owl Remembers\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 9 and courting(mir)\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: owl\ntags: social romance\nscene: A clockwork owl landing on a student's shoulder and hooting a reminder, its creator running after it in goggles.\ntext: Mir's clockwork owl lands on your shoulder and hoots. {~\"Reminder,\" it says, in Mir's voice. \"Birthday. Yours. In nine days. Do not tell them I remembered.\"|\"Reminder,\" it says. \"Lunch. With them. Wear the green coat.\" Mir arrives, scarlet, and grabs it.} Mir is mortified. \"It's not supposed to say the part in brackets,\" they say. \"Probably. It's a feature.\"\n> left: Ask what else it remembers\n  learn mir.owl\n  spark mir +small\n  rel mir +small\n  say: {~Mir tells you, very fast: deadlines, birthdays, and, slightly, grudges. \"And you,\" the owl adds, unhelpfully. Mir puts it in a bag. The bag hoots your name.|\"Everything important,\" says Mir, and then hears themself, and goes red to the goggles.}\n> right: Offer to oil its wings\n  arcana +tiny\n  rel mir +small\n  say: {~You oil its wings while Mir explains every gear, with exclamation marks. By the end the owl prefers you, which Mir says is a betrayal and a compliment.|You oil the wings and the owl hoots a perfect C. Mir writes it down: \"they fixed the pitch!!!\"}\n\n@event mir_notebooks\ntitle: The Notebooks\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 9 and spark(mir) >= 5 and not knows(mir.notebooks) and courting(mir)\nweight: 10\nart: book\ntags: social romance\nscene: An artificer holding out a battered notebook bristling with paper slips, hesitating, to another student in a workshop.\ntext: Mir holds out a notebook, one of a stack, battered and bristling with slips of paper. \"Three years,\" they say. \"Every idea. Some of them work. Probably. Nobody's read them. You can, if you want. Carefully. Very carefully. With clean hands.\"\n> left: Read it with them\n  learn mir.notebooks\n  spark mir +small\n  lore +small\n  say: You read it at the workbench with Mir hovering, explaining every page before you get to it. Halfway through there's a sketch of you, labelled \"test subject (willing?)\". Mir pretends it isn't there. You pretend too, badly.\n> right: Ask which idea is their favourite\n  rel mir +small\n  say: They flip to a page with a drawing of a tiny lantern that remembers who lit it. \"This one,\" they say. \"It doesn't work yet. It will.\" They don't let go of the notebook for the rest of the evening.\n\n@event mir_blueprints\ntitle: Blueprints\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: rel(mir) >= 9 and courting(mir) and spark(mir) < 40\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: social romance\nscene: An artificer pinning a blueprint to a workshop wall covered in drawings, all of them labelled with another student's name.\ntext: Mir has drawn a blueprint for something for you. {~\"A lamp that turns itself off when you fall asleep reading,\" they say. \"Probably.\"|\"A pocket that's bigger inside,\" they say. \"In theory. In practice, it eats keys.\"} They don't say why they keep making things for you. They hold the blueprint like a confession.\n> left: Help build it\n  spark mir +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {~You build it together, Mir talking the whole time, both of you singed at the fingertips. It works. Mostly. Mir hugs it, then you, then looks horrified at themself.|You build it until midnight. It doesn't work. Mir says that's the best part: now you have to build it again, together.}\n> right: Pin it up, for later\n  rel mir +small\n  study +tiny\n  say: {~You pin it on your wall, next to your timetable, and go back to the essay it interrupted. Mir sees it there the next time they visit and goes very quiet and very pleased.|\"Later,\" you say, and go back to your revision. Mir nods fast. \"Later is good. Later is a plan.\" They say \"later\" several times on the way out.}\n\n@event mir_tinkering_evenings\ntitle: Tinkering Evenings\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: bond(mir) == \"partner\"\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 12\nart: gear\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people at a crowded workbench late at night, one soldering, one reading aloud, a clockwork owl asleep between them.\ntext: Thursdays are tinkering evenings now. Mir works; you sit on the bench with your feet up, reading or helping or just there. {~Tonight it's a music box that plays backwards.|Tonight it's a kettle that refuses to boil unless asked nicely.} The owl is asleep on a pile of bolts.\n> left: Hand them the tools\n  spark mir +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: {~You fetch, hold, steady. The music box plays a waltz backwards and you dance to it, badly, among the bolts.|You hand over each tool before they ask. By midnight you know what they need before they do. \"You're the best assistant,\" Mir says, \"and the worst distraction.\" They mean both kindly.}\n> right: Read aloud while they work\n  rel mir +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {~You read aloud. Mir works slower, listening, and doesn't seem to mind.|You read them a terrible novel. They laugh at the wrong parts and burn a finger. The kettle boils, eventually, because you asked it nicely.}\n\n@event mir_not_a_hobby\ntitle: Not a Hobby\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: bond(mir) == \"partner\"\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: romance\nscene: An artificer furiously packing tools into a case in a workshop, another student standing in the doorway.\ntext: You said it lightly: \"your hobby\". Mir has gone very still. \"My family calls it that,\" they say. \"A hobby. Something to grow out of. I didn't think you would.\" They're packing their tools away, carefully, which is how you know they're furious.\n> left: Take it back, properly\n  spark mir +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You take it back, not with \"sorry\" but with every thing they've ever made, one by one, and what each one means. By the fifth they've stopped packing. By the tenth they're crying into their goggles. \"It's not a hobby,\" they say. \"It's not,\" you agree.\n> right: Ask them to show you what it is, then\n  rel mir +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: They stop packing. Then they show you, for three hours, everything, and it's not a hobby. It's a whole life. You never use the word again.\n\n@event mir_family_summons\ntitle: The Family Wants to Meet You\nclass: story\ntone: tense\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: bond(mir) == \"partner\" and knows(mir.family)\nweight: 14\nart: letter\ntags: romance\nscene: An artificer holding a formal letter on clockworks-company paper, sitting on the floor of a workshop among half-finished machines, looking up at someone.\ntext: A letter from Mir's family, on company paper. They have heard there is someone. They would like to meet them, at the clockworks, over the next break. Mir is sitting on the workshop floor, very small among their machines. \"You don't have to,\" they say. \"They'll ask about your prospects. And your hobbies.\"\n> left: Go and meet them\n  spark mir +medium\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You go. Mir's mother asks about your prospects, twice. Their father shows you every clock in the building, and you ask about every one. On the barge back Mir holds your hand the whole way. \"He showed you the escapement,\" they say. \"He never shows anyone the escapement.\"\n> right: Write to them first\n  rel mir +small\n  charm +tiny\n  spark mir -tiny\n  say: \"Not yet,\" you say. \"Let me write first.\" You write a careful letter to two people you've never met. Mir reads it over your shoulder and doesn't correct a single thing, which, for Mir, is a declaration.\n\n@event mir_ex\ntitle: The Owl Holds a Grudge\nspecializes: soc_ex_encounter\nwhen: seen(mir_ex) == 0\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nart: owl\ntags: romance\nscene: A clockwork owl glaring from a high shelf at a former partner of its maker, ruffling its brass feathers.\ntext: Mir's clockwork owl sees you across the courtyard, lands on a gargoyle and glares. \"Grudge,\" it says, in Mir's voice. \"Ongoing.\" Mir, chasing it, sees you, and stops. \"It's not me,\" they say. \"It's the owl. Probably.\"\n> left: Leave them to the owl\n  mood -small\n  say: You go. The owl follows you to the library, hooting \"grudge\" at intervals, until Mir catches it.\n> right: Offer the owl a gear\n  rel mir +small\n  challenge appeal journeyman:\n    win:\n      bond mir friend\n      say: The owl takes the gear and considers it. Then it hoots, quite differently. Mir laughs, surprised. \"That's 'acquaintance, improving',\" they say. \"I didn't know it had that one. I didn't build it in.\" Maybe it built itself.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: The owl takes the gear and drops it on your head. \"Grudge,\" it says. \"Ongoing.\" Mir apologises and doesn't.\n\n@event mir_gift_given\ntitle: A Gift With Gears\nspecializes: soc_gift_given\nwhen: seen(mir_gift_given) == 0\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nweight: 10\nart: gear\ntags: social\nscene: An artificer turning a small gift over and over in their hands, peering at it through goggles as if checking for gears.\ntext: You give Mir a gift. They turn it over three times, then hold it to their ear, then take their goggles off to look at it properly. \"It doesn't do anything,\" they say, wondering. \"It's just... for me?\"\n> left: \"It's just for you.\"\n  rel mir +medium\n  mood +tiny\n  say: They hold it very carefully for the rest of the day, as if it might break, or tick. The next morning there's a small brass gear on your pillow. It doesn't do anything either.\n> right: \"It could do something, if you made it.\"\n  rel mir +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: Their eyes light. By Friday it hums, glows, and plays one note when you're nearby. It's not what you gave them. It's better.\n\n@event mir_birthday\ntitle: Not a Birthday\nspecializes: soc_birthday\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nweight: 10\nrepeat: 12\nart: gear\ntags: social festival\nscene: An artificer at a cluttered workbench, goggles pushed up, looking blankly at a small cake with a single candle set among the springs.\ntext: You find out it's Mir's birthday, and turn up at the workshop with a cake. Mir looks at the candle, puzzled. \"We don't do birthdays at home,\" they say. \"We do name days. Nobody does mine, but that's the one we do.\"\n> left: Light the candle anyway\n  gold -tiny\n  rel mir +medium\n  if knows(mir.name_day):\n    say: \"Two in a year,\" Mir says, as you light it. \"That's excessive.\" They blow it out, very carefully, as if it might be a fuse, and then cut the cake with a pair of calipers into exactly equal slices.\n  else:\n    say: You light it anyway. Mir watches the flame for a while, then blows it out with great precision. \"It's a stupid custom,\" they say, and eat three slices, and save the candle in a drawer of springs.\n> right: Ask when the name day is\n  rel mir +small\n  lore +tiny\n  say: They tell you, then make you write it down, then check you wrote it down correctly. \"You won't remember,\" they say. \"People don't.\" They say it lightly. They watch you put the note in your pocket.\n"},{"path":"content/events/scandals.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Scandals (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md, batch P4-09; design §8.2): the crash that fame risks. Each is a menu with\n#  three ways out: own it (renown falls, a step toward Lumen, people keep faith), fight it (a check: the story dies, or\n#  it gets much worse) or spin it (the story moves onto someone else: a step toward Hollow, and it costs). Half are\n#  callbacks, queued by a choice on a spotlight card; the rest come at random while you're Famous, by the two\n#  appointments below (slim, and slim again once you're a Legend: about the design's (renown − 50)%).\n# ============================================================\n\n@pool scandal\ndesc: What every scandal shares: an incident, a menu of three ways out (own, deny, spin), never drawn at random.\ntrigger: queued\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 1\ntags: renown scandal\n\n@pool scandal_any\ndesc: The scandals that can find any Famous student, dealt by the scandal appointments.\nfrom: scandal\nwhen: renown_famous()\n\n@appointment scandal\ndesc: While you're Famous, now and then a month brings a scandal.\nat: month\nwhen: renown_famous() and term and not wrapup\nchance: slim\ndeals: pool scandal_any\n\n@appointment scandal_legend\ndesc: A Legend has twice the chance: a second roll each month.\nat: month\nwhen: renown_legend() and term and not wrapup\nchance: slim\ndeals: pool scandal_any\n\n# ---------------- callbacks: what an earlier choice brings home ----------------\n\n@event scandal_ledger_lie\ntitle: The Dragon, Checked\npool: scandal\nwhen: renown_known()\nart: paper\nscene: A newspaper spread out on a refectory table, a small boxed headline circled in red ink, a ring of students reading over each other's shoulders.\ntext: Somebody has checked the Ledger's profile of you, the one with the better version of yourself in it. A boxed item on page two lists, very politely, the four things in it that never happened, with dates. The refectory has read it. Everybody is waiting to see what you'll say.\n> own: Own every word of it\n  renown -medium\n  alignment +small\n  rel nessa +tiny\n  say: You write to the Ledger: yes, you embellished; yes, it was vanity; no, there was no dragon. Nessa prints it in full. People are unkind about it for a week, and then, slowly, they start to trust what you say again.\n> deny: Insist it was all true\n  challenge deceive adept:\n    win:\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You insist, calmly and in detail, that every word was true, and you tell it so well that the boxed item starts to look petty. By Friday people have decided the checker had a grudge. You don't feel as good about it as you'd hoped.\n    lose:\n      renown -large\n      alignment -small\n      rel nessa -small\n      say: You insist it was all true, and the checker produces a fifth thing, the one you'd forgotten. It's in the next issue, with your denial printed beside it. Nessa doesn't speak to you for a fortnight.\n> spin: Say Nessa twisted your words\n  alignment -medium\n  rel nessa -medium\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You tell people the Ledger must have muddled your interview. It works, mostly: the story becomes one about sloppy journalism. Nessa reads it in silence, folds the paper very small, and doesn't ask you for another interview.\n\n@event scandal_candle_smoke\ntitle: The Study Candle Fire\npool: scandal\nwhen: renown_known()\nart: candle\nscene: A dormitory window with a scorched curtain, a smoking candle stub on the sill with a little painted label of a student's face, a prefect writing in a notebook.\ntext: One of the candles with your face on it set a dormitory curtain smouldering last night. Nobody was hurt, but the whole tower was woken and stood in the courtyard in their nightclothes for an hour. By breakfast the castle is calling them \"the {player} candles\", and it isn't meant kindly.\n> own: Pay for the damage and stop the sales\n  gold -medium\n  renown -small\n  alignment +small\n  say: You go to the chandler and tell him to stop selling them, and you pay for the curtain out of your own cut. It costs you most of what you made. The tower grumbles, then forgives you, and someone leaves a fire bucket outside your door as a joke.\n> deny: Prove the candle wasn't the cause\n  challenge research adept-:\n    win:\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You take the stub to the alchemy laboratory and prove, with a great deal of smoke, that it was knocked over by a cat, not faulty. The cat belongs to a prefect. The story changes very fast.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      mood -small\n      say: You set out to prove the candle wasn't to blame, and your test sets a second curtain alight, in the laboratory, in front of the lecturer. The story is now much better than it was. You are not in it as the hero.\n> spin: Blame the chandler's wicks\n  alignment -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You say, loudly and often, that the chandler must have changed his wicks without telling you. The castle turns on the chandler, who loses his academy trade for a term. You keep your name. You don't buy candles in the village for a long while.\n\n@event scandal_memoir_book\ntitle: Chapter Nine\npool: scandal\nwhen: renown_known()\nart: book\nscene: A thin, glossy book lying open on a library table, one page heavily underlined, a group of students crowded around it whispering.\ntext: The memoir has come out in full, and chapter nine, which you never read, says that you once copied a whole essay from a friend in your first year. It isn't true. It's very well written. Half the castle has a copy, and the other half is borrowing one.\n> own: Own that you never read it\n  renown -medium\n  alignment +small\n  say: You admit, publicly, that you signed a book you never read, and that chapter nine is invented. People laugh at you for it, and then believe you. The publisher's next printing quietly leaves the chapter out.\n> deny: Make the publisher retract it\n  challenge appeal adept:\n    win:\n      renown -tiny\n      gold +tiny\n      say: You write to the publisher, then go to the city and sit in his office until he agrees to print a correction and pay you for the trouble. The correction is small. The money is not. The castle decides you were wronged.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      gold -small\n      say: The publisher points to the contract you signed, the clause about \"creative licence\", and offers you a free copy. You pay for the journey home yourself. The chapter stays in.\n> spin: Hint that someone else copied\n  alignment -medium\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You say, with a sad look, that it must have been confused with somebody else's story. Within a day, the castle has decided whose. It wasn't them either. They don't speak to you again.\n\n@event scandal_flourish_hurt\ntitle: The {player}, Done Badly\npool: scandal\nwhen: renown_known()\nart: wand\nscene: An infirmary bed with a first-year sitting up, both eyebrows missing and one sleeve singed, a healer in a grey apron frowning, a crowd of first-years peering round the door.\ntext: A first-year doing \"the {player}\", the flourish that does nothing, did it so hard that the spell went off sideways. Nobody is badly hurt: the first-year has lost both eyebrows and is enjoying the attention. But a professor has written to the headmistress, and the word going round is \"reckless\".\n> own: Visit them and teach the real one\n  renown -small\n  alignment +small\n  study -small\n  say: You go to the infirmary and sit with the first-year, then teach the whole yard the real flourish, the one that steadies a cast, slowly, for a week. The eyebrows grow back. The professor's letter, you hear, ends with \"and the student took it in hand.\"\n> deny: Say it was never your flourish\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You point out, reasonably, that you have never once done the flourish and that it isn't yours to teach or stop. People see the sense in it. The first-year, eyebrowless, agrees cheerfully that it was \"more of a tribute\".\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: You say it was never your flourish, and somebody produces a sketch of you doing it, solemnly, in the yard, on the day the whole yard cheered. You had forgotten that. Nobody else has.\n> spin: Blame the lecturer who never stopped it\n  alignment -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You ask, in the right ears, why the practice-yard lecturer let a dangerous flourish go on for a month. The lecturer is spoken to. The flourish is banned. You were never mentioned, and you never quite look the lecturer in the eye again.\n\n@event scandal_wrong_notes\ntitle: The {player} Method, Failed\npool: scandal\nwhen: renown_known()\nart: scroll\nscene: A results list pinned to a board, a first-year in tears beside it clutching a sheaf of copied notes, older students reading the notes and shaking their heads.\ntext: The copies of your notes that were sold in the common room had a mistake in them, in a page you never corrected, and a first-year who learned it word for word has failed a paper on it. The first-year's friends are telling everyone that \"the {player} method\" is a fraud, and that you took money for it.\n> own: Refund it and tutor the first-year\n  gold -small\n  renown -small\n  alignment +small\n  study -small\n  say: You give back every crown you made, and spend three evenings with the first-year going over the paper properly. They pass the resit. The first-year's friends, grudgingly, stop telling the story, and start telling a different one.\n> deny: Point out the page wasn't yours\n  challenge research apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You find the original and show, page by page, that the mistake was added by the copyist. It's true, and it's convincing. The seller has to refund everyone. You still feel bad about the first-year.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      say: You go looking for your original, and find the mistake in your own hand. There's no arguing with it. The story gets worse for the week you spent pretending it wasn't there.\n> spin: Blame the seller\n  alignment -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You tell everyone the third-year with the cash tin had been altering the notes for profit. The third-year is hauled in front of a prefect and loses their place in two clubs. It isn't quite true. It's close enough that you sleep, most nights.\n\n@event scandal_thornwick_cheat\ntitle: Thornwick Cries Foul\npool: scandal\nwhen: renown_known()\nart: duel\nscene: A rival school's newspaper held up in a courtyard, its front page showing a cartoon of a student cheating at a stone circle, a crowd jeering and laughing.\ntext: Thornwick's student paper says you cheated at the boundary stones: that the last spell, the one that won it, used a ward Thornwick rules forbid. It's half true. Lumenhold's rules allow it; Thornwick's don't, and nobody agreed whose rules it was. The cartoon on their front page is very good.\n> own: Offer them a rematch, their rules\n  challenge spell adept:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      alignment +small\n      say: You write and offer a rematch under Thornwick rules, and win that too, by a hair. Thornwick's paper prints a short, sour correction. The cartoon goes up on the Lumenhold common-room wall, framed, with a new caption.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      alignment +small\n      say: You offer a rematch under Thornwick rules, and lose it, fairly. It hurts. But the story Thornwick tells now is about a Lumenhold student who came back to lose properly, and it isn't an unkind one.\n> deny: Say the rules were always Lumenhold's\n  renown -small\n  mood -small\n  say: You say flatly that the contest was at Lumenhold's boundary and under Lumenhold's rules. It's a fair point. It makes no difference to anybody at Thornwick, and it makes you sound, even to yourself, like someone who won on a technicality.\n> spin: Leak a Thornwick scandal of your own\n  alignment -medium\n  renown +tiny\n  say: You find out something unflattering about the Thornwick student who sent the challenge, and make sure it reaches their paper's rival. Within a week, nobody at Thornwick is talking about the boundary stones. You won. It doesn't taste of much.\n\n@event scandal_society_dare\ntitle: In Your Name\npool: scandal\nwhen: renown_known()\nart: group\nscene: A castle rooftop at dawn, a small first-year clinging to a gargoyle, a crowd of prefects and a professor staring up from the courtyard below.\ntext: The {player} Society, the club of first-years with ribbons, held an initiation last night, and the dare was to climb to the third gargoyle \"like {player} would\". One of them got stuck there till dawn. The castle is saying the society is dangerous, and that you never told them to stop.\n> own: Take responsibility for the society\n  renown -small\n  alignment +small\n  say: You go to the headmistress yourself and say it was your name, so it's your society, and you should have been paying attention. You write the society some rules. They are thrilled to have rules. Nobody climbs anything again.\n> deny: Say you never asked for a society\n  challenge appeal adept-:\n    win:\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You point out, gently, that you never asked for a society and have been to exactly one meeting. People agree that that's fair. The first-years are mortified. The first-year from the gargoyle sends you a note: \"SORRY. Still worth it.\"\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      mood -small\n      say: You say you had nothing to do with it, and a first-year, very upset, shows everyone the minutes from the meeting you attended, with your signature. \"We thought you'd want us to be brave,\" the minutes say. It's in the Ledger by Friday.\n> spin: Blame the society's president\n  alignment -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You let it be known that the president had been running the society badly for months. The president resigns in tears. The society carries on without them. You don't go to the next meeting, or any of them.\n\n# ---------------- any Famous student ----------------\n\n@event scandal_leaked_letter\ntitle: Private Correspondence\npool: scandal_any\nart: letter\nscene: A gossip sheet with a letter printed across its centre, a student snatching a copy from a crowd, another reading it aloud on a bench.\ntext: A private letter of yours, a long, frank one written to someone back home, has been printed in a gossip sheet. It isn't the Ledger: it's one of those single pages that appear on the boards overnight. In it you say what you really think of several people. They have all read it.\n> own: Apologise to each of them, in person\n  renown -small\n  alignment +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You go round, one by one, and apologise, and say the letter was unkind and you were tired and homesick when you wrote it. Most of them accept it. One of them says you were right about their singing. It takes a week, and it's the worst week of the term.\n> deny: Find out who stole it\n  challenge research adept-:\n    win:\n      renown +tiny\n      say: You trace the paper and the ink back to a second-year with a grudge and a key to the post room. Once it's known the letter was stolen, the story becomes the thief's. People forgive you faster than you expected.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      say: You spend a week hunting the thief and find nothing, and meanwhile three more pages of the letter appear. Everybody has noticed that you never said it wasn't true.\n> spin: Say it was forged\n  alignment -medium\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You tell everyone the letter was forged, by someone who hates you, and you have a guess who. It works. Somebody else is now the most disliked student in the castle. You can't quite meet the eyes of the people in the letter.\n\n@event scandal_cheating_rumour\ntitle: Nobody Gets Marks Like That\npool: scandal_any\nwhen: gpa >= 3.0\nart: scroll\nscene: A corridor outside an examiners' office, students muttering behind their hands as one student passes with a results slip.\ntext: Somebody has started saying that nobody gets marks like yours honestly, and that someone famous could easily get hold of the papers. It's nonsense. But the examiners have heard it, and you've been asked, very politely, to come and answer some questions on Thursday.\n> own: Ask them to examine you again\n  renown -small\n  study +small\n  alignment +small\n  say: You ask the examiners to set you a fresh paper, there and then, with a proctor. They do. You sit it in a cold room with an hourglass and a lecturer watching, and you do well. The rumour dies in a week, and nobody apologises.\n> deny: Answer their questions, and theirs alone\n  challenge appeal adept:\n    win:\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You answer every question on Thursday plainly and well, and the examiners thank you for your time. One of them says, at the door, that they'd never really doubted it. By the next week the rumour has moved on.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      mood -small\n      say: You get flustered on Thursday, and the examiners, who had only half believed the story, start to believe it a little more. Nothing comes of it in the end. But it takes the whole term to come to nothing.\n> spin: Say who really had the papers\n  alignment -medium\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You let slip a name, someone you once saw near the examiners' office. The rumour moves on to them at once. They are questioned for a week and cleared. They know where it came from. So do you.\n\n@event scandal_ex_tells_all\ntitle: Their Side of It\npool: scandal_any\ncast:\n  x: any where met(x) and around(x) and bond(x) == \"ex\"\nper: x\nart: heartbreak\nscene: A crowded common room, a student holding court with a cup in hand, telling a story to a rapt circle, another student frozen in the doorway.\ntext: {x} has been telling the story of the two of you to anyone who'll listen: the way it started, the way it ended, and a great many things in between that you'd thought were private. The common room goes quiet when you come in. Somebody coughs.\n> own: Tell your own side, kindly\n  renown -small\n  alignment +small\n  rel x +small\n  say: You don't argue. You say that it ended badly and you're sorry for your part in it, and that {x} isn't wrong about everything. It takes the air out of the story. A week later {x} stops you in a corridor and says, stiffly, that {they} had gone too far.\n> deny: Say it's all made up\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      renown -tiny\n      rel x -small\n      say: You laugh it off and say {x} always did have an imagination. People decide to believe you, mostly. {x} hears about it and goes very quiet, and the story stops, and so does anything else between you.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      rel x -medium\n      say: You say it's all made up, and {x} produces your letters. All of them. The common room reads them aloud, with feeling. You have to leave by the window.\n> spin: Tell a worse story about {x}\n  alignment -medium\n  rel x -large\n  renown +tiny\n  say: You tell a story about {x}, a worse one, and some of it is true. The common room switches sides by supper. {x} doesn't come down to meals for a week. You win, in the sense that nobody's talking about you.\n\n@event scandal_curfew_column\ntitle: Seen Where You Shouldn't Be\npool: scandal_any\nart: moon\nscene: A gossip column pinned to a noticeboard with one paragraph circled, a sketch beside it of a student creeping past a lantern at night.\ntext: The gossip column has a paragraph about a \"certain famous student\" seen creeping back past the night porter at two in the morning, \"from the direction of the boathouse\". Nobody needs to guess who. A prefect has torn it down twice. It keeps going back up.\n> own: Own up to the prefects\n  renown -small\n  alignment +small\n  say: You go to the prefects' room and say yes, it was you, and you were out walking because you couldn't sleep. They give you a week of detention polishing the trophy cabinet. It's dull. The column moves on, because there's nothing left to whisper.\n> deny: Prove you were somewhere else\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You produce three friends who swear you were playing cards in the common room till one. The column prints a short, sniffy correction. Your friends make you buy them supper for a month.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      say: Your alibi lasts until somebody points out that the common room was locked at midnight. The next column runs the whole story, with the bit about the alibi. The prefects are not amused.\n> spin: Hint it was someone in a borrowed coat\n  alignment -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You let it be known you'd lent your coat that night to a friend. It's half true. The column hunts for the friend, who doesn't thank you for it, and the story slowly turns into one about the coat.\n\n@event scandal_snubbed\ntitle: The Snub\npool: scandal_any\nart: mask\nscene: A first-year sitting alone on a stone bench, face in hands, a group of first-years glaring across a courtyard at an older student walking away.\ntext: The story is that a first-year waited two hours in the rain outside your lecture to ask you something, and you walked straight past without a word. You don't remember it. You might have been thinking about something else. The first-year cried, apparently, and the first-years have decided you're a monster.\n> own: Find the first-year and apologise\n  renown -small\n  alignment +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You find the first-year and apologise, properly, and ask what they'd wanted to ask. It was about the library. You take them there. By the end of the week, the first-years' monster has become the first-years' favourite again, mostly.\n> deny: Say you were never there that day\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You say you were in the infirmary that afternoon with a cold, and Sister Amaranth, when asked, can't say you weren't. Neither can you, quite. The story decides the first-year must have mistaken somebody else for you.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      alignment -tiny\n      say: You say you were never there, and three first-years say they saw you. The first-years are now sure you're a monster and a liar. It takes the rest of the term.\n> spin: Say the first-year has been stalking you\n  alignment -medium\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You tell people, sadly, that the first-year has been following you everywhere, and you didn't know what to do. People are sympathetic. The first-year stops coming to meals for a while. You never find out what they'd wanted to ask.\n\n@event scandal_thornwick_defection\ntitle: Off to Thornwick?\npool: scandal_any\nart: paper\nscene: A newspaper from a rival school pinned up in a courtyard, the headline in large red letters, Lumenhold students tearing it down while others stare.\ntext: Thornwick's student paper has run a story that you're leaving Lumenhold for Thornwick next year, with a quote, apparently from you, about Lumenhold's \"draughty towers and draughtier lecturers\". You've never said it. Half the castle believes it, and the other half is hurt that you'd joke about it.\n> own: Say you'd never leave\n  renown -tiny\n  alignment +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You stand up at supper and say, plainly, that you've never been asked and you'd never go, and that the towers are draughty but they're yours. The Great Hall bangs the tables. It's embarrassing. It works.\n> deny: Prove the quote is fake\n  challenge research adept-:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      say: You find that the \"quote\" is lifted, word for word, from a Thornwick student's own letter home, which their paper printed last year. Lumenhold reprints both side by side. Thornwick's paper is very quiet for a month.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      say: You try to prove the quote was invented, and can't, and the more you protest, the more people suspect you did say it, once, in a bad mood, somewhere. You probably did. You can't remember.\n> spin: Hint you've had a better offer\n  alignment -small\n  renown +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You let it be known you've had offers from all sorts of places, and Thornwick isn't the best of them. People are impressed. They are also, now, a little bit wary of you, and stop telling you things.\n\n@event scandal_forged_love_letter\ntitle: A Letter You Didn't Write\npool: scandal_any\ncast:\n  y: any where classmate_here(y) and rel(y) >= 0\nper: y\nart: letter\nscene: A pink letter being passed hand to hand along a library table, a student at one end turning bright red, another at the far end staring in horror.\ntext: A love letter is going round the library, in what looks exactly like your handwriting, addressed to {y}. It is very passionate and very badly spelled. You didn't write it. {y} has read it, and is now going very carefully out of every room you come into.\n> own: Go and talk to {y} first\n  rel y +small\n  renown -small\n  say: You find {y} before anybody else can, and say you didn't write it and you're sorry {they} had to read it. {y} laughs, from relief. \"I did wonder about the spelling.\" The story fizzles, and you and {y} are, oddly, closer for it.\n> deny: Find the forger\n  challenge research adept-:\n    win:\n      renown +tiny\n      rel y +tiny\n      say: You compare the hand with every exercise book in your year, and find the forger: a second-year who thought it was the funniest thing ever done. The library agrees, a little. The second-year apologises, mostly to {y}.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      rel y -small\n      say: You can't find the forger, and the longer you look, the more people think you're covering for yourself. {y} starts taking the long way round the library. It's a month before you can laugh about it.\n> spin: Say it was meant for someone else\n  alignment -small\n  rel y -small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You say, with a wink, that it was meant for somebody else entirely, and wasn't it obvious? The castle spends a week guessing who. {y} is relieved, and then, for some reason, hurt, and you never quite work out why.\n\n@event scandal_credit_taken\ntitle: An Idea Over Supper\npool: scandal_any\ncast:\n  f: any where classmate_here(f) and rel(f) >= 15\nper: f\nart: book\nscene: A lecture hall with a diagram on the board, a professor pointing to one student, another student in the row behind sitting with arms folded and jaw set.\ntext: The idea everyone's praising you for, the one the lecturer called \"the best thing this year\", started as something {f} said to you over supper. You'd forgotten. {f} hadn't. Now {f}'s friends are telling everyone that the famous {player} steals ideas from people who aren't famous.\n> own: Give {f} the credit, out loud\n  rel f +medium\n  renown -small\n  alignment +small\n  say: You go to the lecturer, with {f}, and say whose idea it was first. The lecturer puts both names on the board. {f} goes red and says it wasn't much of an idea, really. It was. People notice that you said so.\n> deny: Say you'd had it too\n  alignment -tiny\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      renown -tiny\n      rel f -small\n      say: You say you'd been thinking along the same lines for weeks, and you have notes to prove it, nearly. People accept it. {f} doesn't, and things between you are cool for a long while.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      rel f -medium\n      say: You say you'd had the idea too, and {f} produces the napkin from supper with the idea sketched on it, and the date. The story is now much worse, and it has a napkin in it.\n> spin: Say {f} is jealous\n  alignment -medium\n  rel f -large\n  renown +tiny\n  say: You say, sorrowfully, that {f} has always been a little jealous of you. People believe it, because you're famous and {f} isn't. {f} stops sitting with you at supper, and you start to wonder whose ideas you're going to have now.\n"},{"path":"content/events/slice_2a.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Phase 2a vertical slice (design §13): ten events that use every\n#  construct of the new event format — classes and tones, size\n#  tiers and presets, challenges with partial results and `vs`,\n#  spells and the swipe-up option, inline text variation, facts,\n#  memories and relations between characters. Since spells v2\n#  only the safety talk teaches a spell (Ward); Flame is Evocation's.\n# ============================================================\n\n@event slice_ward_lesson\n# Spells v2: the orientation safety talk (docs/spells-v2-list.md §C, Ward). Every first-year learns Ward in the first\n# week, whichever side they take; the id is the old Thorne lesson's.\ntitle: The Safety Talk\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: auto\nwhen: spell_level(ward) == 0\nart: shield\ntags: class study magic\nscene: The Great Hall in the first week of term, rows of first-years on benches holding up wobbling pale shells, a healer walking the aisle with a roll of bandages.\ntext: First week, the Great Hall: the safety talk. Sister Amaranth gives it while re-bandaging a prefect. \"You will be hexed,\" she says, \"by accident and on purpose. One spell before any other: a ward. It stops one blow.\" She looks up. \"Practise in pairs. Aim low.\"\n> left: Pair up and take the hexes\n  learn_spell ward\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: Your partner's practice hex stings your knuckles twice before your shell holds. The third one splashes off it like rain off glass. Amaranth, passing, says \"Keep doing that,\" and keeps walking.\n> right: Copy her notes and practise alone\n  learn_spell ward\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You take down every word and practise in your room that night. The shell comes on the eleventh try, lopsided, while your roommate watches. It holds, which is the part that counts.\n\n@event slice_candle_flame\ntitle: Borrowed Fire\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\nactor: lyra\nwhen: met(lyra) and turn >= 4 and spell_level(flame) == 0 and rel(lyra) >= 0\nweight: 24\nart: flame\ntags: class magic rival\nscene: A crimson-haired student balancing a flame on one fingertip while another student squints at a sputtering candle.\ntext: Lyra watches you fail to light a candle for the third time. \"You're asking it,\" she says. \"Fire doesn't want to be asked. {rel(lyra) >= 12: Here — watch my hand.|Watch, if you can keep up.}\"\n> left: Watch her hand\n  arcana +small\n  rel lyra +small\n  spark lyra +tiny\n  learn lyra.prodigy\n  say: She doesn't explain. She just does it, slowly, twice. On the third try your candle catches, and so, very briefly, does your sleeve.\n> right: Challenge her to teach you properly\n  challenge duel vs lyra.arcana:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      rel lyra +medium\n      say: You hold your own long enough that she stops and actually explains. \"Fine,\" she says. \"You'll do.\"\n    partial:\n      arcana +tiny\n      health -small\n      say: She wins, and you hit the floor hard enough to feel it tomorrow, but she shows you the trick on the way out, as if it were nothing.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: You end up on the floor with singed eyebrows. Lyra steps over you. \"Ask better,\" she says.\n\n@event slice_stray_cold\ntitle: The Drift\nclass: incident\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 5\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A corridor at night where frost spreads across the flagstones toward a student holding a candle.\ntext: Cold drifts down the corridor like a tide coming in, and the torches lean away from it. {spell_level(ward) > 0: Your ward prickles at your fingertips.|You have nothing to hold it back.}\n> left: Run for the stairs\n  health -small\n  mood -small\n  say: You make the stairs with frost in your hair. Behind you, the corridor sighs and is just a corridor again.\n> right: Stand and study it\n  challenge research adept- wild:\n    win:\n      lore +medium\n      say: It isn't weather. It feels like being looked at, from somewhere far below. You write down everything before your hands warm up, and none of it makes sense yet.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: You learn exactly one thing, which is how cold cold can be. Sister Amaranth wraps you in four blankets.\n> up[ward]: Raise a ward\n  mood +small\n  say: Your ward flares, pale and steady. The cold breaks against it and slides away, the way a wave gives up on a rock.\n\n@event slice_late_lamps\ntime: evening night\ntitle: The Last Lamp\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 2\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 6\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: The library at closing time, one desk still lit, a cat asleep on an open book.\ntext: {~The library is emptying.|Rain on the high windows, and the library is emptying early.|Exam fever has filled the library all day; now, finally, it is emptying.} {~Footnote, the library cat, is asleep on the book you need|The book you need is on the top shelf, and the ladder squeaks|Someone has left a half-finished essay on your table, and a biscuit}, and the last lamp is yours if you want it.\n> left: Keep the lamp\n  grind lore\n  say: {~You read until Footnote leaves in protest.|You read until the words swim.|You read until the night porter coughs pointedly.}\n> right: Go to bed\n  health +small\n  say: {~You sleep like a stone. The book will still be there. So, probably, will Footnote.|You fall asleep before your head finds the pillow. You wake up knowing one thing you didn't, somehow.|You sleep well and dream of shelves. The lamp goes to someone who needed it more.}\n\n@event slice_quarrel\ntitle: A Quarrel in the Courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: kael\nwhen: met(kael) and met(lyra) and turn >= 6 and around(lyra) and around(kael)\nweight: 16\nart: snow\ntags: social rival\nscene: Two students facing each other in a snowy courtyard, one breathing frost, the other with sparks at her fingertips.\ntext: Kael and Lyra are facing each other in the courtyard, and the {season == \"winter\": snow|grass} between them is half {season == \"winter\": melted|scorched}, half frozen. \"Say it again,\" Lyra says. Kael doesn't. He just looks at you.\n> left: Step between them\n  alignment +tiny\n  npc_rel lyra kael +small\n  rel kael +small\n  health -tiny\n  memory: protected kael\n  say: Lyra's sparks catch your sleeve, Kael's frost puts it out. They both stare at you. The fight, somehow, is over.\n> right: Walk past\n  npc_rel lyra kael -medium\n  rel kael -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Behind you, something cracks: ice, or temper. At dinner they sit at opposite ends of the hall.\n\n@event slice_burned_letter\ntitle: Ashes in the Grate\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nactor: lyra\nwhen: rel(lyra) >= 6 and not knows(lyra.mother)\nweight: 18\nart: letter\ntags: social\nscene: A fireplace where the corner of a letter with a black seal is curling into flame.\ntext: Lyra is feeding a letter into the common-room fire, a corner at a time. Black seal. She doesn't look round. \"It says 'well done',\" she says. \"It doesn't. I'm checking whether it burns better if I pretend.\"\n> left: Sit with her\n  learn lyra.mother\n  rel lyra +medium\n  mood -small\n  memory: confided lyra\n  say: You watch it burn together. \"She's never said it,\" Lyra says, to the fire. \"Not once.\" You don't say anything, and that seems to be right.\n> right: Make a joke\n  rel lyra -small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: She almost smiles. Then she puts the rest of the letter in at once and leaves you with the smoke.\n\n@event slice_hollow_whisper\ntitle: A Voice in the Water\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: turn >= 7\nweight: 12\nart: wave\ntags: mystery hollowstar\nthread: hollowstar\nscene: A student kneeling at the edge of a still lake at night, their reflection smiling when they are not.\ntext: At the lakeshore your reflection speaks before you do. \"You're running low,\" it says, kindly. \"{mana < 15: I could fill you up.|I could make you stronger.} Just say yes. Saying yes costs nothing.\"\n> left: Say yes\n  mana +large\n  arcana +medium\n  alignment -medium\n  say: Cold pours into you, generous and endless. When you stand, your reflection is a little slower to stand with you.\n> right: Walk away\n  alignment +small\n  mood -small\n  say: The reflection's smile stays on the water after you've gone. You don't look back. You can feel it not minding.\n\n@event slice_market_haggle\ntitle: The Spice Stall\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: turn >= 3\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 8\nart: coin\ntags: money\nscene: A crowded village market stall heaped with jars of glowing spices and a stall-holder with an enormous moustache.\ntext: The stall-holder in the village market wants thirty crowns for {~a jar of starpepper|a twist of dragon-chilli|a pot of cliff-bee honey}. Mabel swears by it for colds. The stall-holder swears by his prices.\n> left: Haggle\n  when: gold >= 30\n  challenge negotiate journeyman-:\n    win:\n      gold -small\n      health +small\n      say: Eight crowns and a compliment on your persistence. Mabel says you've been robbed, but only slightly.\n    partial:\n      gold -medium\n      health +small\n      say: Fifteen crowns. He throws in a free sneeze's worth of pepper, which you did not want.\n    lose:\n      gold -large\n      mood -tiny\n      say: You pay full price and somehow promise to come back for more. He waves you off like a nephew.\n> right: Leave it\n  health -tiny\n  say: You walk on. You catch a cold the next week, out of spite.\n\n@event slice_wager\ntitle: The Tower Wager\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: renown >= 3 and turn >= 4\nweight: 5\nart: dice\ntags: social\nscene: A knot of students around a table where coins and a pocket watch are being stacked as bets.\ntext: Half the common room is betting on whether you can climb the clock tower before the bell. Corvin is holding the purse, which is either reassuring or not.\n> left: Climb\n  vigor +tiny\n  chance even:\n    renown +large\n    say: {~You make it with a heartbeat to spare.|The bell rings as your hand touches the top.} They carry you round the common room on a chair.\n  else:\n    renown -medium\n    say: The bell rings when you're two-thirds up, and the caretaker has to talk you down with a ladder. They'll tell it for years.\n> right: Decline politely\n  renown -tiny\n  say: The crowd groans. Corvin pockets the purse thoughtfully and you decide not to ask.\n\n@event slice_frost_duel\ntitle: Frost Against Fire\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nactor: kael\nwhen: met(kael) and rel(kael) >= 9 and turn >= 8\nweight: 20\nart: duel\ntags: duel social\nscene: A duelling ring at dawn, frost spreading from one side and a faint warmth glowing from the other.\ntext: Kael asks for a practice duel, which for him is an astonishing number of words. \"Not to win,\" he adds. \"I want to see what you do when it's cold.\"\n> left: Duel him straight\n  memory: fought kael\n  challenge combat vs kael.arcana +1:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel kael +medium\n      spark kael +small\n      say: You win by a whisker, breathing steam. Kael bows, and his hands, for once, are warm when he shakes yours. Half the duelling club, it turns out, was watching from the doorway.\n    partial:\n      rel kael +small\n      health -small\n      say: A draw, of sorts: you're both on the ground. He laughs, a short surprised sound, and helps you up.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel kael +small\n      say: He wins, gently. \"You stopped when it got cold,\" he says. \"Don't.\"\n> right: Decline; offer him tea instead\n  rel kael +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: He thinks about this for a long time. \"Tea,\" he says. It freezes in his cup. You make him another.\n> up[flame]: Answer with fire\n  rel kael +medium\n  spark kael +small\n  renown +small\n  memory: fought kael\n  say: Your flame meets his frost in a column of steam that fills the ring and brings half the corridor running. When it clears, Kael is smiling at you, properly, for the first time.\n"},{"path":"content/events/slice_2b.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Phase 2b vertical slice (design §13): the world loop in the new\n#  format. The Evocation course (3 levels), the Duelling Circle, the\n#  Library Assistant job, five campus places, break plans, the\n#  Royal Library internship, the thesis, and a four-event arc\n#  (The Bell Under the Water). Uses cast roles, say[npc],\n#  present(place), queue within … and unqueue.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Evocation (track: evocation) ----------------\n\n@pool evocation_class_2\ntrack: evocation\nclass: routine\ntags: class\n\n@event evo_first_spark\ntitle: The First Spark\npool: evocation_class_2\nlevel: 1\nlesson: lumenhold\ntone: light\nart: flame\ntags: magic\nscene: A lecture hall with scorch marks on every desk, students holding their palms up as if testing for rain.\ntext: Evocation I begins with a single instruction from the lectern: \"Make heat. Not fire. Heat.\" Half the class makes nothing. One student makes a small, apologetic fire.\n> left: Aim for heat, carefully\n  arcana +small\n  say: Your palm warms like a stone left in the sun. The lecturer nods, which is better than most get.\n> right: Aim for fire, boldly\n  challenge exam standard-1 wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      renown +tiny\n      say: A neat little flame, exactly where you meant it. The lecturer writes your name down. Approvingly, you hope.\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      arcana +tiny\n      say: Your sleeve learns more about fire than you do. The lecture continues around the smell.\n\n@event evo_puddle_drill\ntitle: Puddle Drill\npool: evocation_class_2\nlevel: 1\nlesson: mirror_lake\ntone: light\nart: wave\ntags: magic\nscene: Students crouched over puddles in a stone courtyard, trying to make the water stand up.\ntext: Tidecalling half of the course: a puddle each, and the task of making it stand up and stay up. Yours trembles, considers it, and lies back down.\n> left: Practise until it stands\n  grind arcana\n  say: By the end of the hour your puddle stands like a sulky child. It counts.\n> right: Watch the student next to you\n  arcana +tiny\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You learn their trick, and also that they hum while they cast. You hum too. It works, inexplicably.\n\n@event evo_misfire\ntitle: Misfire\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 1\nclass: incident\ntone: tense\nart: bolt\ntags: class danger\nscene: A lecture hall filling with steam after a spell meets a bucket of water at the wrong moment.\ntext: Someone's tidecalling meets someone else's flame in the middle of the room. The hall fills with scalding steam and twenty students who all have opinions.\n> left: Shield the front row\n  health -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  renown +small\n  say: You take the worst of it on your sleeves. The front row looks at you the way people look at lifeboats.\n> right: Get out and open the windows\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The steam pours out into the courtyard and becomes, briefly, a very small cloud with a grudge.\n\n@event evo_tide_wall\ntitle: The Tide Wall\npool: evocation_class_2\nlevel: 2\nlesson: mirror_lake\ntone: tense\nart: wave\ntags: magic\nscene: A student holding back a wall of lake water on the shore while classmates watch from behind them.\ntext: Evocation II takes the class to the lakeshore. The exercise: raise a wall of water and hold it while a classmate walks past behind it. Your classmate looks nervous. So do you.\n> left: Hold it steady\n  challenge exam standard steady:\n    win:\n      arcana +medium\n      say: The wall holds. Your classmate walks past dry and amazed. You let it down gently, which is the hard part.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: The wall holds until it doesn't, and comes down on both of you. Your classmate is very wet and very gracious about it. You shiver until dinner.\n> right: Ask to go second\n  arcana +small\n  say: You watch three people do it first and learn from all of their mistakes.\n\n@event evo_lattice\ntitle: The Flame Lattice\npool: evocation_class_2\nlevel: 2\nlesson: founders_seal\ntone: light\nart: flame\ntags: study\nscene: Interlocking threads of fire hanging in the air of a lecture hall like a glowing net.\ntext: Fire woven into a lattice, each strand holding the others up. The lecturer says the founders built wards like this. The lecturer also says you have twenty minutes.\n> left: Build it slowly\n  lore +small\n  arcana +small\n  mood -small\n  say: Your lattice is small and ugly and holds perfectly. The lecturer says \"Founder-ish,\" which you choose to take as praise.\n> right: Build it big\n  chance even:\n    arcana +large\n    say: {~It's magnificent for nine seconds.|It holds just long enough for everyone to see it.} Then the strands find each other and the whole thing sighs into sparks, and you understand exactly why.\n  else:\n    mood -small\n    health -tiny\n    say: The strands find each other in the first second, and the whole thing goes up in your face. You spend the rest of the lesson picking sparks out of your hair.\n\n@event evo_storm_pairing\ntitle: Storm Pairing\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: bolt\ntags: class duel\ncast:\n  partner: any where (tagged(partner, student) or tagged(partner, prefect))\nscene: Two students on a windswept tower top, one calling rain, the other calling lightning, the sky between them churning.\ntext: Advanced Evocation pairs students for a storm: one calls the rain, the other the lightning, and neither is allowed to let go first. You're paired with {partner}.\n> left: Call the lightning\n  challenge exam standard+1 wild:\n    win:\n      arcana +large\n      rel partner +small\n      say: The storm answers both of you at once. {partner} laughs into the wind, soaked and astonished.\n      say[lyra]: The storm answers both of you at once. Lyra looks at you as though you've finally said something worth hearing.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      rel partner +tiny\n      say: The lightning goes where it likes. You both spend an afternoon in the infirmary, comparing burns.\n> right: Call the rain, and let them lead\n  arcana +medium\n  rel partner +small\n  say: You hold the rain steady while {partner} draws the lightning down. Afterwards {they} thank{s} you for not trying to steal it.\n\n@event evo_final_practical\ntitle: The Final Practical\ntrack: evocation\nlevel: 3\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 10\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: flame\ntags: class exam\nscene: An examination hall cleared of desks, a single student facing three examiners and a target dummy made of ice.\ntext: The last practical of Advanced Evocation: melt an ice dummy without touching the floor around it. Professor Thorne is on the panel, and he is not blinking.\n> left: Fire, precisely\n  challenge exam standard+1 steady:\n    win:\n      arcana +large\n      study evocation +small\n      rel thorne +small\n      say: The ice runs off the dummy and not a drop touches the floor. Thorne says \"Hm,\" and writes something underlined.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: The dummy melts, and so does a fair part of the floor. Thorne writes something with a question mark.\n> right: Water, patiently\n  arcana +medium\n  lore +small\n  say: You draw the cold out of the ice with water instead of fire. It takes twice as long. The panel watches every minute.\n\n# ---------------- Duelling Circle (track: duelling) ----------------\n\n@event duel_first_practice\ntitle: Footwork\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 1\nwhen: around(lyra)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: duel\ntags: club duel\nscene: A duelling hall with chalk circles on the floor and practice dummies with names painted on their chests.\ntext: Lyra runs practice like a general. \"Footwork,\" she says. \"If your feet are wrong, your spell is a rumour.\" She pairs you with a dummy called Gerald the Second.\n> left: Drill footwork\n  vigor +small\n  perform track +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Two hours of stepping in circles. Gerald the Second wins every round. Your feet, however, are now correct.\n> right: Spar for real\n  challenge combat vs lyra.vigor -1:\n    win:\n      vigor +medium\n      rel lyra +small\n      say: You land a clean disarm on your partner. Across the hall, Lyra raises an eyebrow, which is her version of a standing ovation.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: You spend the evening relearning which way is up. Lyra says that's the first lesson anyway.\n\n@event duel_footwork_feud\ntitle: The Footwork Feud\ntrack: duelling\nwhen: around(lyra) and around(bram)\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: duel\ntags: club rival\nscene: Two students arguing in the middle of a duelling circle while the rest of the club pretends not to listen.\ntext: Bram says the Circle's footwork is \"Emberhall nonsense\" and Lyra says Bram's footwork is \"a hazard to shipping\". Both of them look at you to settle it.\n> left: Side with Lyra\n  rel lyra +small\n  rel bram -small\n  npc_rel lyra bram -tiny\n  say: Lyra nods as if this were obvious. Bram takes a demerit book out of his pocket, looks at it, and puts it back. Progress.\n> right: Side with Bram\n  rel bram +small\n  rel lyra -small\n  say: Bram looks startled to be agreed with. Lyra looks at you like a puzzle she will solve later, with fire.\n\n@event duel_fixture\ntitle: Fixture Against Thornwick\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: trophy\ntags: club duel festival\nscene: A duelling green with rival banners, students from both academies packed onto benches.\ntext: Thornwick Academy's duelling team has crossed the hills for a fixture. The Circle needs one more duellist in the line-up, and the captain is looking at you.\n> left: Duel for the Circle\n  challenge combat adept-:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      perform track +medium\n      say: You win your bout, and the Circle wins the fixture by a whisker. Someone carries you a few steps before remembering they don't like you.\n    partial:\n      renown +small\n      perform track +small\n      say: A draw. The Circle loses by one bout, but nobody blames you, and {around(lyra): Lyra|the captain} says \"acceptable\" twice.\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      mood -small\n      say: The Thornwick duellist is very good and very polite about it, and every bruise you collect is a polite one. You hate both.\n> right: Offer to be the reserve\n  perform track +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You hold coats and bring water. It turns out reserves see everything, including how Thornwick cheats.\n> up[wildfire]: Let the fire run\n  renown +medium\n  perform track +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You hold the fire until it bites, and then let it go. It runs across the hall and through the Thornwick duellist's wards like paper. The Circle goes mad. Your sleeve is on fire, which you notice afterwards.\n\n@event duel_captain_call\ntitle: The Captain's Question\ntrack: duelling\nrank: 2\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: duel\ntags: club\nscene: An empty duelling hall at night, two students sitting on the edge of a chalk circle.\ntext: After practice, the captain sits beside you. \"When I graduate, someone has to run this place,\" she says. \"Someone who turns up. You turn up.\"\n> left: Say you'll think about it\n  perform track +small\n  rank_xp track +medium\n  say: \"Think fast,\" she says. \"Feet first, then spells.\" You're fairly sure that's a yes.\n> right: Say you're not the type\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Nobody's the type,\" she says. \"That's why it has to be someone who turns up.\"\n\n# ---------------- Library Assistant (track: library_assistant) ----------------\n\n@event lib_shelving_rush\ntitle: The Shelving Rush\ntrack: library_assistant\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 10\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: job study\nscene: A library cart overflowing with returned books, a student pushing it at a run between towering shelves.\ntext: Exam week, and every book in the library has been borrowed, read and returned at once. The cart is taller than you are. Footnote is sitting on top of it.\n> left: Shelve properly\n  wage\n  lore +small\n  perform track +small\n  mood -small\n  say: Every book in its place by closing. Footnote has moved to the top shelf to supervise.\n> right: Shelve quickly\n  wage\n  perform track -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: The books are on shelves. Most of them are even on the right shelves. Someone will find out in a decade.\n> up[animate]: Animate the cart\n  wage\n  perform track +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The cart trundles off by itself, putting books back in roughly the right places, and you follow behind it fixing the rough parts. It takes everything you've got to keep it from shelving Footnote.\n\n@event lib_overdue_mage\ntitle: The Overdue Mage\ntrack: library_assistant\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: hourglass\ntags: job\nscene: An elderly mage at the library desk clutching a book that is sixty years overdue.\ntext: An old mage returns a book sixty years overdue and asks, with great dignity, what the fine is. The ledger says the fine is more than the library.\n> left: Waive it\n  wage\n  perform track -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: The old mage thanks you and leaves a sweet on the desk. Later you find out he taught Professor Thorne.\n> right: Charge it, by the book\n  wage\n  gold +small\n  perform track +small\n  say: The mage pays in coins so old the bank has to look them up. The head librarian is delighted, and gives you the collector's share of the fine, as is tradition.\n\n@event lib_footnote_strike\ntitle: Footnote Goes on Strike\ntrack: library_assistant\nclass: incident\ntone: light\nart: cat\ntags: job\nscene: A large library cat lying across a reference desk, refusing to move, while students queue in front of it.\ntext: Footnote has lain down across the reference desk and will not move. Students are queueing. The cat has made its demands clear: none.\n> left: Bribe him with fish\n  wage\n  gold -tiny\n  perform track +small\n  say: Footnote accepts the fish, the apology and the chair you bring him. The queue resumes.\n> right: Work around him\n  wage\n  perform track -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You stamp books on the far end of the desk for an hour. Footnote watches with the calm of a cat who has won.\n\n# ---------------- Places ----------------\n\n@event court_fountain_freeze\ntitle: The Frozen Fountain\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: snow\ntags: mystery\nscene: A courtyard fountain frozen mid-splash, the water hanging in the air like glass.\ntext: The courtyard fountain has frozen in the middle of a splash. The water hangs in the air like glass. A first-year is trying to lick it.\n> left: Stop the first-year\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +small\n  say: You pull the first-year away just as the ice cracks. For a moment you'd swear you saw a black star reflected in it.\n> right: Study the ice\n  lore +small\n  say: The ice is colder than ice should be. Your notes say \"colder than ice should be\" three times.\n> up[hoarfrost]: Freeze the first-year free\n  alignment +tiny\n  arcana +small\n  say: You put a hand on the ice and pull the cold back into yourself, just round the first-year's tongue. They come free with a pop and a look of betrayal at the fountain. Your hand aches with cold all afternoon.\n\n@event court_rumour_mill\ntitle: The Rumour Mill\nplace: courtyard\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: group\ntags: social\ncast:\n  gossip: present(place) where tagged(gossip, peer)\nscene: Students huddled around the courtyard fountain trading whispers, one of them clearly enjoying it most.\ntext: {gossip} has news, which is to say gossip, which is to say three different stories about the same broken window. \"You'll want to hear this,\" {they} say{s}.\n> left: Listen to all of it\n  rel gossip +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You hear all three stories. Two are true. One is about you.\n  say[nessa]: You hear all three stories. Nessa is already writing the fourth.\n> right: Make up a better one\n  guile +small\n  rel gossip +tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: Your story is better than all three. By dinner it's the official version.\n> up[candour]: Ask what really happened\n  rel gossip -tiny\n  lore +small\n  say: You ask the right question and {gossip} tells you the truth, startled: the window broke because someone sneezed during a levitation. It is the least interesting story of the four. {They} look{s} at you as if you'd cheated.\n\n@pool lakeshore_routine_2\nplace: lakeshore\nclass: routine\n\n@event lake_skimming\ntitle: Skimming Stones\npool: lakeshore_routine_2\ntone: warm\nart: wave\ntags: nature\ncast:\n  friend: any where tagged(friend, peer) and rel(friend) >= 6\nscene: Two students skimming stones across a perfectly still lake at dusk.\ntext: {friend} is skimming stones on the Mirror Lake. The water is so still each stone leaves rings that go on forever. \"Seven,\" {they} say{s}. \"Beat that.\"\n> left: Try to beat it\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      rel friend +small\n      mood +small\n      say: Nine skips. {friend} demands a rematch and gets one. The afternoon disappears into stones.\n    lose:\n      rel friend +small\n      mood +tiny\n      say: Your stone sinks with great dignity. {friend} laughs until {they} have to sit down.\n> right: Just watch the rings\n  mood +small\n  rel friend +tiny\n  say: You sit and watch the rings spread until the light goes. Neither of you says much. It's one of the good afternoons.\n\n@event lake_reflection_bet\ntitle: The Reflection Bet\npool: lakeshore_routine_2\ntone: eerie\nart: mirror\ntags: mystery\nscene: A student staring into a still lake where their reflection appears to be holding a coin.\ntext: Your reflection in the Mirror Lake is holding a coin you don't have. It flips it. It catches it. It offers it to you.\n> left: Take the coin\n  gold +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: The coin is real, and cold, and you can't remember putting it in your pocket.\n> right: Shake your head\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: Your reflection shrugs, pockets the coin and walks out of the frame before you do.\n> up[sleight]: Lift it before it offers\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You lift the coin from your reflection's hand before it can offer it. The reflection looks at its empty hand, then at you, deeply affronted. For a week you can't quite meet your own eye in the washroom mirror.\n\n@event ref_food_fight\ntitle: Pudding Diplomacy\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: meal\ntags: social\nscene: A refectory mid-food-fight, puddings in the air, a prefect rising slowly from behind a table.\ntext: A pudding sails across the refectory and lands in Bram's lap. The hall goes silent. Every eye turns to the table it came from, which is yours.\n> left: Own up\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel bram +small\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Bram writes you a demerit, then pauses. \"Good throw,\" he says, almost without wanting to.\n> right: Throw another\n  renown +small\n  rel bram -small\n  mood +small\n  say: The second pudding starts a war that the refectory will talk about for years. Historians will argue about who won. You know.\n> up[rapport]: Turn the hall with a joke\n  renown +small\n  rel bram +tiny\n  say: You stand up, bow to Bram, and say the pudding was meant as a tribute. The hall laughs, and so, against his will, does Bram. He fishes the cherry out of his lap and eats it.\n\n@event ref_cook_secret\ntitle: The Cook's Secret\nplace: refectory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: cup\ntags: home\nscene: A huge kitchen behind the refectory, an enormous cook stirring a pot with a spoon the size of an oar.\ntext: The cook catches you lingering by the kitchen door. \"You look like someone who misses proper soup,\" she says, and hands you a bowl before you can deny it.\n> left: Eat it slowly\n  health +small\n  mood +small\n  say: It tastes like the kitchen at home. You don't say so. She can tell anyway.\n> right: Ask for the recipe\n  alchemy +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: \"Ha,\" says the cook, which means no, which means you'll have to keep coming back.\n> up[candour]: Ask for the honest recipe\n  alchemy +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: The cook tells you the truth before she can stop herself: the secret is a spoon of the faculty's good sherry. She laughs, then makes you promise. You promise, and get a second bowl.\n\n@event obs_comet_watch\nslot: evening\ntitle: The Comet Watch\nplace: observatory\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: planet\ntags: magic\nscene: The brass dome of an observatory open to the night, a student at the eyepiece, another beside them with a notebook.\ntext: A comet is due tonight and the observatory is full of students pretending they came for the science. The telescope has a queue. The roof has a view.\n> left: Queue for the telescope\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Your turn at the eyepiece lasts thirty seconds. The comet's tail is green. You'll remember it longer than most lectures.\n> right: Watch from the roof\n  mood +small\n  rel kael +tiny\n  say: The comet crosses the sky slowly, like it's looking for something. Kael is already on the roof. He moves over to make room.\n> up[foresight]: Foresee where it will cross\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You look a moment ahead and point at an empty patch of sky. Everyone on the roof looks, and the comet crosses exactly there. Half the roof claps. The other half accuses you of reading the charts.\n\n@event obs_star_chart\ntitle: The Missing Star\nplace: observatory\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: star\ntags: mystery magic\nscene: An old star chart spread on a desk, one constellation with a careful empty circle drawn where a star should be.\ntext: An old star chart in the observatory archive has a circle drawn where a star should be, with a note in faded ink: \"It eats the light. Do not chart it.\"\n> left: Copy the chart\n  lore +small\n  say: You copy it carefully. That night you dream of a circle with nothing inside it, waiting.\n> right: Put it back\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You return it to the drawer. The drawer feels colder when you close it.\n\n@event green_mandrake_choir\ntitle: The Mandrake Choir\nplace: greenhouses\nwhen: around(tobias)\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: leaf\ntags: nature\nscene: A greenhouse bench of potted mandrakes wearing tiny earmuffs, a student conducting them with a pencil.\ntext: Tobias has taught the greenhouse mandrakes to scream in harmony. He would like your opinion. The mandrakes would like an audience.\n> left: Conduct a verse\n  rel tobias +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You conduct one verse. Your ears ring for a day. Tobias says it's the best performance they've given.\n> right: Wear earmuffs and listen\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: Through the earmuffs it sounds almost beautiful. Tobias is so proud he cries a little.\n\n@event green_moonflower_sprout\ntitle: A Moonflower Seedling\nplace: greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: flower\ntags: nature\nscene: A tiny silver-leaved seedling in a clay pot, glowing faintly in a dark greenhouse.\ntext: In a forgotten corner of the greenhouse, a moonflower seed has sprouted on its own, eleven years early. It glows faintly when you lean over it.\n> left: Pot it and look after it\n  alchemy +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You give it a pot, a label and a name. It glows a little brighter when you visit.\n> right: Tell Tobias\n  when: around(tobias)\n  rel tobias +medium\n  say: Tobias is speechless, then very loud. The seedling gets a sign, a fence and a rota.\n> up[bramble]: Entangle a hedge round it\n  alchemy +small\n  say: A ring of thorns comes up round the seedling, knee-high and very sure of itself. Nobody is treading on this one by accident. The seedling glows a little brighter, or you imagine it does.\n\n# ---------------- Break days ----------------\n\n@event castle_empty_halls\ntitle: Empty Halls\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: tower\ntags: home\nscene: A long castle corridor in the holidays, dust in sunbeams, one student walking alone.\ntext: The castle in the holidays is enormous and quiet. Your footsteps echo down corridors you've never been alone in. A door you've never noticed is standing open.\n> left: Go through the door\n  guile +small\n  mood +tiny\n  discover seventh_tower\n  say: It leads to a stair that leads to a locked door that leads, you're almost sure, to the seventh tower.\n> right: Read in the window seat\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You read all day in a window seat with the whole lake below you. The best kind of nothing.\n\n@event castle_skeleton_crew\ntitle: The Skeleton Crew\nplan: castle\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A handful of staff and students sharing a long table in a nearly empty great hall.\ntext: Supper in the holidays is one table in the Great Hall: you, three other students, the cook, two prefects and Old Maud, who has brought her goat.\n> left: Sit next to Maud\n  rel maud +small\n  mood +small\n  say: Maud tells a story about the frozen spring that she's never told the term-time students. Bartholomew eats your napkin.\n> right: Play cards with the prefects\n  gold +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: The prefects play cards like they enforce rules: badly and with great confidence. You win dessert and most of their pocket money.\n\n@event home_family_supper\ntitle: Supper at Home\nplace: home_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: meal\ntags: home\nscene: A crowded kitchen table, a family passing dishes, a student back from the academy being asked too many questions.\ntext: Supper at home. Everyone wants to know what you've learned. You try to explain evocation to an aunt who thinks it's a kind of cheese.\n> left: Show them a spell\n  mood +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  say: You light every candle in the room at once. Your aunt applauds and asks if you can do it with cheese.\n> right: Just listen\n  mood +medium\n  health +tiny\n  say: You let them talk. You'd forgotten how loud and how good it is.\n\n@event home_old_friend\ntitle: An Old Friend\nplace: home_fields\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: leaf\ntags: home\nscene: Two old friends sitting on a field wall at sunset, one in academy robes, the other in work clothes.\ntext: Your oldest friend is sitting on the wall at the edge of the fields, exactly where you left them. \"So,\" they say. \"Are you a wizard now, or what?\"\n> left: Tell them everything\n  mood +medium\n  charm +tiny\n  say: You talk until the stars come out. They want to hear about the lake most of all.\n> right: Ask about their life instead\n  mood +small\n  charm +small\n  say: They've been busy too: a farm, a fiancé, a goat that bites. Your life isn't the only one that grew.\n\n@event home_market_day\ntitle: Market Day\nplace: home_market\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: coin\ntags: home money\nscene: A small country market, a student in academy robes being stared at by stallholders.\ntext: Market day in town. A stallholder recognises your academy robes and asks if you can do anything about his leaky roof. For a price.\n> left: Fix it with magic\n  gold +small\n  arcana +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: The roof is dry, the stallholder is thrilled and you are slightly scorched. He pays in apples and coins.\n> right: Just buy sweets\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: The sweet shop still sells your favourites. You eat them on the bridge like you used to.\n\n@event work_double_shift\ntitle: The Double Shift\nplan: work\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: coin\ntags: job money\nscene: A student asleep on their feet behind a counter, a clock on the wall showing a very late hour.\ntext: Holiday shifts pay double and feel triple. It's your second shift today and the evening rush hasn't started.\n> left: Push through\n  gold +medium\n  health -small\n  mood -small\n  say: You push through. The coins are heavy in your pocket and your feet are heavy everywhere else.\n> right: Swap the shift\n  mood +small\n  say: A colleague takes it for a favour you'll owe later. You sleep for eleven hours.\n\n# ---------------- The Royal Library internship (plan: internship) ----------------\n\n@event intern_first_assignment\ntitle: The First Assignment\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nart: book\ntags: study\nscene: A vast underground archive, a supervisor handing a student a list longer than their arm.\ntext: Your supervisor at the Royal Library hands you a list of forty uncatalogued boxes. \"Find out what's in them,\" she says. \"Carefully. One of them is probably alive.\"\n> left: Work through the boxes\n  wage\n  lore +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: Box thirty-one is alive. It's a very small, very old dictionary that bites. You catalogue it.\n> right: Ask the other interns\n  wage\n  charm +small\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The other interns have a system, a rota and a song. You learn all three.\n\n@event intern_index_maze\ntitle: The Index Maze\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nart: map\ntags: study mystery\nscene: A student lost between endless shelves underground, following an index card trail by lamplight.\ntext: A cross-reference in the archive leads to another, and another, and another, deeper under the city. The last card is about the founders of Lumenhold.\n> left: Follow it to the end\n  wage\n  challenge research adept:\n    win:\n      lore +large\n      say: At the end is a letter from a founder about \"the seventh lock\". You copy it with shaking hands.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: The trail ends at a missing card. Somebody took it. Recently.\n> right: Report it to your supervisor\n  wage\n  lore +small\n  perform track +small\n  say: Your supervisor reads the card, goes pale, thanks you for your care, and writes a letter to the headmistress of Lumenhold.\n\n# ---------------- Thesis (track: thesis) ----------------\n\n@event thesis_proposal\ntitle: The Proposal\ntrack: thesis\nwhen: progress(thesis) < 30\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A desk covered in drafts, one page with a single sentence circled many times.\ntext: Your thesis proposal is due. Professor Thorne wants one sentence that says what you'll find out. You have eleven sentences and none of them is it.\n> left: Rewrite it all night\n  progress track +medium\n  health -small\n  say: At dawn you have one sentence. Thorne reads it twice. \"Yes,\" he says. \"That.\"\n> right: Hand in what you have\n  progress track +small\n  rel thorne -tiny\n  say: Thorne reads all eleven sentences and circles the fourth. \"That one,\" he says. \"Next time, only that one.\"\n\n@event thesis_lab_night\ntitle: Late in the Lab\ntrack: thesis\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nart: candle\ntags: study\nscene: A thesis laboratory at midnight, one carrel lit, a student surrounded by open books.\ntext: The thesis lab, long after everyone else has gone home: and your argument has a hole in it the size of the seventh tower.\n> left: Find the hole\n  progress track +medium\n  mood -small\n  say: You find it, fill it and find two more. That's progress, apparently.\n> right: Go to bed\n  rest\n  progress track -small\n  say: You sleep. In the morning the hole is still there, but you can see the edges of it.\n\n# ---------------- The Bell Under the Water (arc: the_bell) ----------------\n\n@event bell_first_ring\ntitle: A Bell Under the Water\narc: the_bell\nbeat: 1\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: bell\ntags: mystery\nscene: A dark lakeshore at night, ripples spreading from the middle of the lake though nothing has fallen in.\ntext: In the middle of the night you hear a bell, low and slow, from the middle of the lake. Nobody else in your dormitory wakes up.\n> left: Go down to the shore\n  find bell_heard\n  health -tiny\n  say: The water is perfectly still. Then, once, a ring spreads out from the centre, as if the lake had been struck from below.\n> right: Pull the blanket over your head\n  find bell_heard\n  mood -tiny\n  say: It rings seven times. You count every one. In the morning you are the only one who remembers.\n\n@event bell_maud_rope\ntitle: Maud's Rope\narc: the_bell\nbeat: 2\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: bell\ntags: mystery\ncast:\n  keeper: maud\nscene: An old woman in a garden shed holding up a frayed, waterlogged bell-rope.\ntext: {keeper} shows you a rope, frayed and black with lake water, that she's kept in her shed for sixty years. \"From the bell,\" she says. \"It came up the night the lake froze. Someone cut it.\"\n> left: Ask who cut it\n  find bell_rope\n  rel keeper +small\n  flag bell_asked\n  find lead_maud_answer\n  say: \"That,\" says {keeper}, \"is the question I've been asking for sixty years.\" She promises to tell you what she knows, when she's ready. \"Come by the cottage, dearie. Not tonight.\"\n> right: Leave her to her memories\n  find bell_rope\n  rel keeper +tiny\n  say: You leave her with the rope in her lap. She doesn't notice you go.\n\n@event bell_answer\ntitle: What Maud Remembers\n# Feedback 2026-10-16: no longer on a timer. Asking about the rope leaves a lead; she tells you at her cottage.\nplace: maud_cottage\nwhen: flag(bell_asked) and met(maud)\nweight: 18\nactor: maud\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: lantern\ntags: mystery\nscene: An old woman and a student sitting by a cottage fire, a waterlogged rope coiled between them.\ntext: {npc} has the kettle on before you knock. \"The rope,\" she says, once the tea is poured. \"Edmund cut it. To stop the bell ringing the thing awake. It went on ringing anyway.\"\n> ok: Listen\n  rel npc +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She talks until the light goes. When she's finished, she seems lighter, and you seem to be carrying something.\n\n@event bell_drowned\ntitle: The Diving Bell\narc: the_bell\nbeat: 3\nslot: night\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: wave\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: A student in a borrowed diving helmet standing at the end of a jetty over black water.\ntext: The bell has rung every night for a week. The boathouse has an old diving helmet and a rope. The lake is very dark and very still.\n> left: Dive\n  challenge research adept- wild:\n    win:\n      find bell_name\n      lore +medium\n      say: Far down, the bell lies on its side, and around its rim, letters: a name you don't know. You surface gasping, and you keep the name.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      find lead_bell_dive\n      say: The cold drives you back up before you see anything. Old Maud is waiting on the jetty with a blanket and a look. The helmet stays in the boathouse, if you want to try again.\n> right: Wait for the bell to stop\n  mood -small\n  lore +small\n  find lead_bell_dive\n  say: You wait for a week, listening from the shore, until you know its rhythm by heart. It doesn't stop. Something in you decides it will have to be you, next time: the helmet is still in the boathouse.\n\n@event bell_silence\ntitle: The Bell Calls\narc: the_bell\nbeat: 4\nslot: night\nwhen: clue(bell_name) or clue(bell_rope)\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nart: bell\ntags: mystery\n# Feedback 2026-10-16: beat 4 no longer ends the arc by itself. It names where to go; the silencing is played at the\n# lakeshore after hours (bell_laid_to_rest), with the name or the rope.\nscene: A dormitory window at night, the Mirror Lake beyond it silver under the moon, rings spreading across the water.\ntext: The bell is ringing every night now, louder, and tonight it rings your way: you can feel it in your teeth. {clue(bell_name): You have the name from its rim.|You have Maud's rope.} You know, the way you know your own handwriting, that it wants you at the lakeshore after dark.\n> ok: Tonight, then\n  find lead_bell_shore\n  say: You lie awake counting the rings. Seven, always seven. Whenever you go down to the shore after dark, it will be waiting.\n\n@event bell_laid_to_rest\ntitle: The Bell Falls Silent\narc: the_bell\nplace: lakeshore\ntime: evening midnight\nwhen: seen(bell_silence) > 0 and (clue(bell_name) or clue(bell_rope)) and not flag(bell_silenced)\nrepeat: 1\nweight: 20\ntype: menu\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: bell\ntags: mystery\nscene: The Mirror Lake at dusk, perfectly still, a student standing at the water's edge with a lantern.\ntext: The shore is empty and the lake is glass. Then the bell begins, slow and low, from the very middle. {clue(bell_name) and clue(bell_rope): You have the name from its rim and Maud's rope in your hands.|{clue(bell_name): You have the name from its rim.|You have Maud's rope, cut sixty years ago.}}\n> name: Say the name over the water\n  when: clue(bell_name)\n  flag bell_silenced\n  mood +medium\n  alignment +small\n  rel maud +small\n  say: You say the name from the bell's rim out loud. The bell rings once more, as if answering to it, and then the lake is just a lake. Somewhere in her cottage, you think, Maud sleeps through the night.\n> rope: Give the rope back to the lake\n  when: clue(bell_rope)\n  flag bell_silenced\n  mood +small\n  alignment +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You wade in to your knees and let the rope sink. It goes down straight, as if something below were pulling it home. The bell rings one last time, muffled, and stops. The cold stays in your legs all night.\n> wait: Not tonight\n  say: You stand at the shore until the ringing stops on its own, and go back up the lawn. It will ring again tomorrow.\n\n@event bell_dive_again\ntitle: The Helmet in the Boathouse\narc: the_bell\nplace: boathouse\ntime: afternoon evening\nwhen: clue(lead_bell_dive) and not clue(bell_name)\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 16\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nart: wave\ntags: mystery danger\nscene: An old brass diving helmet on a boathouse workbench beside a coil of rope, the lake glinting through the open doors.\ntext: The old diving helmet is where you left it, on the workbench by the doors. Somebody has oiled the rope since last time. The lake is still, and under it the bell is waiting.\n> left: Dive again\n  hint: You know the cold now.\n  challenge research adept- wild +1 if clue(lead_bell_dive):\n    win:\n      find bell_name\n      lore +medium\n      say: You go down slower this time, and further. The bell lies on its side, and around its rim, letters: a name you don't know. You surface gasping, and you keep the name.\n    lose:\n      health -medium\n      say: The cold finds you again, halfway down. You come up blue and shaking, with nothing but the certainty that it's down there.\n> right: Not today\n  say: You put the helmet back on the bench. The bell can wait one more day. It has waited sixty years.\n\n@event bell_echo_lakeshore\ntitle: Rings on Still Water\npool: lakeshore_routine_2\nwhen: clue(bell_heard) and not flag(bell_silenced)\ntone: eerie\nart: wave\ntags: mystery\nscene: A calm lake at dusk, perfect rings spreading from its centre although nothing has fallen in, a student crouched at the water's edge.\ntext: The lake is flat calm, and still rings spread from its middle, slow and even, like the ripples from a bell that nobody hears. A heron on the shallows lifts its head at each one.\n> left: Count the rings\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: Seven, then a pause, then seven. The heron counts them too, you'd swear. You walk back up the lawn with the number stuck in your head.\n> right: Skim a stone across them\n  mood +small\n  say: Your stone skips four times and sinks in the middle of a ring. For a moment the rings stop. Then they start again, patient as anything.\n\n@event bell_echo_boathouse\ntitle: The Boats Keep Time\nplace: boathouse\nwhen: clue(bell_heard) and not flag(bell_silenced)\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nart: wave\ntags: mystery\nscene: Rowing boats tied in an old boathouse, all knocking gently against their posts in the same rhythm, a student listening in the doorway.\ntext: Inside the boathouse every boat is knocking against its post in the same slow rhythm, though there's no wind and the water outside is still. Knock, knock, knock: seven, then a pause.\n> left: Steady the boats\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You go from boat to boat with wet sleeves, holding each one still. As soon as you let go, they take up the rhythm again.\n> right: Sit and listen\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You sit on an upturned hull and listen until you can hear the bell under the knocking. It is very far down, and very patient.\n\n@event bell_echo_cottage\ntitle: Maud's Teacups\nplace: maud_cottage\nwhen: clue(bell_heard) and met(maud) and not flag(bell_silenced)\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nart: cup\ntags: mystery\nscene: A cottage dresser full of mismatched teacups, all trembling and chiming together, an old woman pretending not to notice.\ntext: In Maud's cottage the teacups on the dresser are chiming against each other, all together, though nobody has touched them. Maud pours your tea as if she can't hear a thing. \"Biscuit, dearie?\"\n> left: Ask her about the cups\n  rel maud +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Old cups,\" says Maud. \"Old lake.\" She looks at the window a long time. \"They've done that since I was a girl, on the nights it rings. You get used to it. You don't.\"\n> right: Help her wrap them in cloth\n  rel maud +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You wrap every cup in an old tea towel. The chiming stops, and Maud's shoulders come down an inch. \"There,\" she says. \"Now we can hear ourselves think.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/social.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Generic social events — `actor: any`.\n#  The engine binds `npc` to one met character for whom `when`\n#  is true. Use rel(npc), love(npc), bond(npc), romanceable(npc),\n#  tagged(npc, faculty) in conditions and {npc}, {they}, {them},\n#  {their}, {They} in text. These drive the relationship system:\n#  friendships form, romances spark, rivalries flare, bonds decay.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Friendship ----------------\n\n@event soc_study_session\ntitle: Study Session\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 0\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nrepeat: 4\nart: book\ntags: social study\nscene: Two students at a candlelit table piled with books, one explaining something with wild hand gestures.\ntext: {~{npc} drops into the seat across from you with an armful of books. \"Exams are coming for us all. Misery loves company. Study with me?\"|{npc} slides a stack of notes onto your table. \"I've read this chapter four times and it's still in a foreign language. Help?\"|{npc} finds you in the library and sits down without asking. \"If I study alone I'll just reorganise my quills. Keep me honest?\"}\n> left: You work better alone\n  lore +tiny\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: You get a lot done. {~{npc} sits at the next table and sighs loudly every few minutes.|{npc} finds someone else, and their laughter carries across the library all evening.|{npc} leaves you a biscuit on the way out, which somehow makes it worse.}\n> right: Pull up a chair\n  mood -tiny\n  lore +small\n  study +tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You get half as much done and remember twice as much of it. {~{npc} explains the Third Law with a biscuit diagram.|You quiz each other until the candle gutters.|{npc} makes up a rude rhyme for every date, and you will never forget a single one.}\n\n@pool social_routine\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 6\n\n@event soc_borrow_money\ntitle: A Small Loan\npool: social_routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 3\nper: npc\nwhen: gold >= 20\nart: pouch\ntags: social money\nscene: A student holding out an empty purse upside down with a sheepish grin.\ntext: {npc} turns out an empty purse. \"I'm fifteen crowns short {~for rent|for the term's ink and parchment|for the wand-mender, and it's my only wand}. I'll pay you back. I swear on my wand.\"\n> left: Sorry, you're tight too\n  rel npc -small\n  say: \"No, of course, I understand.\" {They} {are} quiet at dinner.\n> right: Lend fifteen crowns\n  gold -medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  chance likely:\n    queue soc_loan_repaid in 2 with npc\n    say: {npc} hugs you with the empty purse still in {their} hand.\n  else:\n    say: {npc} hugs you. You suspect you will never see those fifteen crowns again.\n\n@event soc_loan_repaid\ntitle: Paid Back\nclass: story\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nactor: npc\nart: coin\ntags: social money\nscene: A small stack of coins and a sweet bun left on a desk with a thank-you note.\ntext: {~{npc} finds you after class and presses a little stack of coins into your hand, plus a sticky bun. \"Fifteen crowns. And interest.\" The interest is the bun.|{npc} turns up at breakfast with a small purse and a very straight face. \"Fifteen crowns. I said I would.\"|{npc} slides fifteen crowns across the library table, one coin at a time. The last one has a note under it: \"Thank you. Really.\"}\n> left: Count it\n  gold +medium\n  rel npc +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You count it, because {they}'d be offended if you didn't. Fifteen exactly. {npc} nods, satisfied, as if a debt of honour has been settled.\n> right: Pocket it without counting\n  gold +medium\n  rel npc +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You put it away uncounted. {npc} looks quietly pleased to be trusted, which was worth more than the crowns.\n\n@event soc_become_friends\nramp: rel(npc) 18..30\ntitle: Something Like Friendship\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and bond(npc) == \"none\" and rel(npc) >= 18 and not (npc_is(bram) and not flag(bram_softened))\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 20\nart: group\ntags: social\nscene: Two students walking across the lawn at sunset, laughing at the same joke, shadows long behind them.\ntext: {npc} saves you a seat without asking now, and you save {them} one. You trade notes, complaints and the last biscuit without keeping score. Somewhere along the way, this became a friendship.\n> left: Keep a little distance\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: You stay friendly. Just not too close. Some walls are load-bearing.\n> right: Call it what it is\n  bond npc friend\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood +small\n  say: \"So we're friends now,\" {npc} says. \"Officially.\" You shake on it, solemnly, then ruin it by laughing.\n\n@event soc_become_close\nramp: rel(npc) 36..50\nslot: night\ntitle: The Kind of Friend You Call at 3am\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and bond(npc) == \"friend\" and rel(npc) >= 36\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 18\nart: heart\ntags: social\nscene: Two friends sitting on a dormitory floor at 3am wrapped in one blanket, empty mugs between them.\ntext: It's 3am. You can't sleep, and you've walked to {npc}'s door before you've even decided to. {They} open{s} it before you knock.\n> left: Apologise and go back to bed\n  rel npc +small\n  say: \"Don't be daft,\" {npc} says, but lets you go. In the morning there's tea outside your door.\n> right: Talk until sunrise\n  bond npc close\n  rel npc +medium\n  mood +medium\n  say: You tell {them} things you haven't told anyone. {They} tell you things back. The sun comes up and neither of you notices.\n\n@pool social_routine_repeat8\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 8\ntags: social\n\n@event soc_secret\ntitle: A Secret\npool: social_routine_repeat8\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 15\nper: npc\nart: lock\nscene: Two students huddled under a staircase, one whispering into the other's ear, a finger to their lips.\ntext: {~{npc} pulls you under the stairs. \"I need to tell someone or I'll burst. Swear you won't tell anyone. Not even the portraits.\"|{npc} closes the dormitory door, checks it, and checks it again. \"I have to tell someone. Swear you'll keep it.\"|{npc} walks you to the far end of the lake path, where nobody goes. \"Can you keep something? Really keep it?\"}\n> left: \"I'd rather not carry it.\"\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: {npc} looks hurt, then relieved. \"Maybe that's better.\" You'll never know.\n> right: \"I swear.\"\n  mood -tiny\n  rel npc +medium\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {~It's not a big secret. It's the kind that's only big to the person holding it. You hold it with {them}.|It's about a letter from home, and it's heavier than {they} let on. You carry half of it now.|It's ridiculous, and {they} know it's ridiculous, and {they} needed someone else to know anyway. You do.}\n\n@event soc_argument\ntitle: Words Exchanged\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 6 and bond(npc) != \"partner\" and not npc_is(tobias) and not npc_is(seraphine) and not npc_is(kael)\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 6\nweight: 8\nart: bolt\ntags: social\nscene: Two students facing off in an empty corridor, one with arms crossed, the other mid-gesture, both flushed.\ntext: It starts with something small — {~a borrowed book, a joke that landed wrong|whose turn it was to fetch the tea|a seat someone saved and someone else took} — and suddenly you and {npc} are arguing {~in the corridor|on the stairs|in the middle of the refectory}, louder than either of you intended.\n> left: Apologise first\n  rel npc +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~\"I'm sorry.\" {npc} deflates. \"...Me too.\" It's over. It feels better than winning would have.|You say sorry first. {npc} looks almost disappointed not to get to say it. You share a bun about it later.|\"I'm sorry.\" {npc} blinks, then laughs. \"We were both being absurd.\" You were.}\n> right: Stand your ground\n  mood -tiny\n  chance even:\n    rel npc -medium\n    say: {npc} storms off. You were right. It doesn't feel as good as you'd hoped.\n  else:\n    rel npc +medium\n    charm +small\n    say: You argue it out until you're both laughing at how ridiculous it got. Somehow you're closer after.\n\n@event soc_village_trip\ntitle: A Day in the Village\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 6\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: gold >= 5\nrepeat: 5\nart: house\ntags: social\nscene: A cobbled village street with crooked shopfronts, two students sharing a paper cone of roasted nuts.\ntext: \"Barge to the village? Tea at Mabel's, a wander round the bookshops, back before curfew?\" {npc} is already wearing {their} coat.\n> left: Not this week\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: {~{They} bring{s} you back a cinnamon bun anyway.|{They} bring{s} you back a postcard of the village, which is three streets long.|{They} come{s} back with a story about a goat and tell{s} it badly.}\n> right: Grab your coat\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +medium\n  rel npc +small\n  say: {~Tea, buns, a bookshop cat that sits on {npc}'s feet. You almost miss the last barge, laughing.|Mabel's, then the market, then a long argument about which bun is best. You get the last barge home with sticky fingers.|Rain, so you spend the whole afternoon in the bookshop. {npc} buys a book about knots and explains every one on the barge home.}\n\n@event soc_friend_sick\ntitle: Sick Friend\npool: social_routine_repeat8\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"friend\" or bond(npc) == \"close\" or bond(npc) == \"partner\") and not npc_is(kael)\nper: npc\nart: rain\nscene: A student tucked into an infirmary bed under a mountain of blankets, a friend sitting beside them with a bowl of soup.\ntext: {npc} has Lake Fever and is miserable in the infirmary. Sister Amaranth says visitors are allowed, but she won't be responsible.\n> left: Send a card\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: You send a card with a drawing of Sister Amaranth as a dragon. {npc} keeps it on {their} bedside table.\n> right: Sit with them every evening\n  rel npc +medium\n  alignment +tiny\n  chance unlikely:\n    gain sick\n    say: You keep {them} company for a week. Then you're in the next bed. You both find this hilarious.\n  else:\n    say: {~You read aloud, badly, doing voices. {npc} recovers and claims it was the voices.|You teach {them} every card game you know and lose all of them. {npc} recovers, insufferably.|You bring soup from the kitchens every evening and tell {them} all the gossip. {npc} recovers mostly out of curiosity.}\n> up[item:honey_tin]: Bring a spoon of Maud's honey\n  rel npc +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {~You stir silver honey into {npc}'s tea. {They} sit{s} up, sniff, and ask for more. Sister Amaranth pretends not to see.|{npc} takes one spoonful of the honey and colour comes back into {their} face. Amaranth asks where you got it, then asks for some.|The honey tastes of summer. {npc} is up and complaining by the end of the week, which Amaranth calls a full recovery.}\n\n@event soc_gift_given\ntitle: A Small Gift\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 9\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 7\nart: gem\ntags: social\nscene: A small brown-paper parcel tied with string, wedged into a student's pigeonhole in the porters' lodge.\ntext: There's a small parcel wedged into your pigeonhole, tied with string. A note in {npc}'s handwriting: \"Saw this and thought of you. Don't make it weird.\"\n> left: Thank {them} later\n  mood +tiny\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: {~It's a tiny brass compass that always points to the nearest teapot. You use it constantly.|It's a bookmark that hums when you stop reading. Annoying. You love it.|It's a pair of gloves that stay warm all winter. You wear them until they're threadbare.}\n> right: Get {them} something back (8 gold)\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  rel npc +medium\n  say: You find {them} {~a book {they}'d wanted for months|a tin of the good biscuits from the village|a sketchbook with good paper}. {npc} grins and calls you a menace, which is worth twice the price.\n\n@event soc_drift\ntitle: Drifting Apart\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and rel(npc) >= 6 and rel(npc) < 24 and apart(npc) >= 2 and not npc_is(lyra) and not npc_is(kael)\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: since(soc_drift) > 3\nweight: 6\nrepeat: 6\nart: hourglass\ntags: social\nscene: A student glancing back across a busy courtyard at another who hasn't seen them, the space between them full of strangers.\ntext: You realise you haven't really talked to {npc} in weeks. You see {them} {~across the courtyard|at the far end of the refectory|on the library steps}, laughing with people you don't know.\n> left: Let it be\n  rel npc -small\n  say: People drift. It's nobody's fault. You still smile when you pass in the halls.\n> right: Go over and say hello\n  chance likely:\n    rel npc +medium\n    mood +tiny\n    say: {~\"There you are!\" {npc} says, as if you'd been the one missing. Maybe you had.|{npc} introduces you to everyone as \"the one I told you about\". You wonder what {they} told them. It seems to have been good.|{npc} shifts over to make room on the bench without a word, as if your seat had been waiting.}\n  else:\n    rel npc +tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: You go over. It is friendly, and short, and you both say \"we should catch up\" in a way that means you won't.\n\n@event soc_rumour\ntitle: Rumours About {npc}\npool: social_routine_repeat8\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where rel(npc) >= 9 and not tagged(npc, villager) and not npc_is(nessa)\nper: npc\nart: paper\nscene: A cluster of students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, glancing toward someone just out of view.\ntext: A nasty rumour about {npc} is going round {~the common room|the refectory|the library steps}: {~that {they} cheated on an exam|that {they} {are} leaving at the end of term in disgrace|that {they} said something cruel about a friend}. It's not true. You know it's not true. People are laughing.\n> left: Let it blow over\n  mood -tiny\n  say: It blows over. {npc} never finds out you heard it. You find out you don't like yourself for it, a little.\n> right: Shut it down\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge appeal apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel npc +medium\n      renown +small\n      say: You make the rumour-monger look so foolish that the story dies on the spot. {npc} hears about it later and just squeezes your arm.\n    lose:\n      rel npc +small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You make a scene and now there are rumours about you too. {npc} thinks you're an idiot. A loyal idiot.\n\n@event soc_homework_help\ntitle: Desperate Homework\nclass: routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and rel(npc) >= 0 and not npc_is(lyra) and not npc_is(mir)\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nrepeat: 5\nart: feather\ntags: social study\nscene: A student with ink on their face clutching a half-finished essay, another student leaning over to read it.\ntext: {npc} waves a half-finished {~essay|lab report|star chart} at you. \"It's due in an hour and I've written the title twice. Help.\"\n> left: Explain the concept\n  challenge research apprentice:\n    win:\n      rel npc +medium\n      lore +tiny\n      say: You explain it so clearly that {npc} finishes the essay with time to spare. {They} insist{s} you're a genius.\n    lose:\n      rel npc +tiny\n      say: You explain it so confusingly that you both end up more lost. You hand it in together, two confused people against the world.\n> right: \"Copy mine.\"\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  chance unlikely:\n    study -small\n    rel npc -tiny\n    say: The professor notices two identical pieces of work. You both get detention, a lecture, and a note on your record that every one of your professors reads.\n    gain detention\n  else:\n    say: {npc} hands it in and gets a better mark than you did. Unbelievable.\n\n@event soc_faculty_advice\ntitle: A Word of Advice\npool: social_routine\ntone: light\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, teacher) and rel(npc) >= 6\nper: npc\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nart: scroll\ntags: study\nscene: A professor walking with a student along a covered walkway, pointing at something in an open book.\ntext: {npc} catches you after a lecture. \"You've been working hard. Or hardly working. I can never tell with your year. Some advice, if you'll take it?\"\n> left: Politely decline\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: \"Suit yourself.\" {They} walk{s} off. Faculty have long memories.\n> right: Listen carefully\n  mood -tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  study +tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: It's good advice about how to study, {~annoyingly good|delivered at great length|illustrated with a story from {their} own disastrous first year}. Every piece of work you hand in that month is better for it.\n\n# ---------------- Romance ----------------\n\n@event soc_spark\ntitle: A Spark\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and rel(npc) >= 9 and spark(npc) < 12 and bond(npc) != \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: bonds(partner) == 0\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 6\nart: sparkle\ntags: social romance\nscene: Two hands brushing as they reach for the same quill, both students freezing for a heartbeat.\ntext: You and {npc} reach for {&the same quill|the last biscuit on the plate|the same library book} at the same moment. Your hands touch. Neither of you lets go immediately. {&It's a perfectly ordinary quill.|It's a perfectly ordinary biscuit.|It's a perfectly ordinary book.}\n> left: Laugh it off\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: \"All yours,\" you say. {npc} laughs. The moment passes. Mostly.\n> right: Hold on a moment longer\n  chance likely:\n    spark npc +medium\n    rel npc +tiny\n    say: {npc} looks up. Something in {their} face shifts. Then {they} let{s} go, a little pink, and {&the quill rolls under the desk|the biscuit breaks neatly in half|the book falls open on the floor between you}.\n  else:\n    rel npc +tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {npc} pulls back as if burned, and the rest of the {&lesson|meal|afternoon} is very, very awkward.\n\n@event soc_date\ntitle: An Invitation\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and spark(npc) >= 12 and spark(npc) < 40 and bond(npc) != \"partner\" and rel(npc) >= 9\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 12\nrepeat: 4\nart: heart\ntags: social romance\nscene: A student nervously holding out a hand-drawn ticket to the village's floating lantern show.\ntext: You could ask {npc} to {&the lantern show in the village|the travelling players at the Newt|the music on the quay} this weekend. As friends. Or, you know. Not as friends.\n> left: Ask as friends\n  gold -tiny\n  rel npc +small\n  mood +small\n  say: You pay for both tickets and the hot cider. It's a lovely evening. You're both very careful about elbows.\n> right: Ask as more than friends\n  if spark(npc) >= 25:\n    challenge appeal apprentice likely wild:\n      win:\n        spark npc +medium\n        mood +medium\n        say: {npc} says yes before you finish the sentence. You hold hands through the whole {&show and miss every lantern|play and miss every line|evening and miss every song}.\n      lose:\n        spark npc -tiny\n        mood -small\n        say: {npc} hesitates, then says {they}'d rather go as friends. It's a nice evening. It's a long evening.\n  else:\n    challenge appeal apprentice wild:\n      win:\n        spark npc +medium\n        mood +medium\n        say: {npc} says yes before you finish the sentence. You hold hands through the whole {&show and miss every lantern|play and miss every line|evening and miss every song}.\n      lose:\n        spark npc -tiny\n        mood -small\n        say: {npc} hesitates, then says {they}'d rather go as friends. It's a nice evening. It's a long evening.\n\n@event soc_confess_generic\nramp: spark(npc) 30..45\ntitle: Feelings, Spoken\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where romanceable(npc) and spark(npc) >= 30 and rel(npc) >= 18 and bond(npc) != \"partner\" and bond(npc) != \"ex\" and not npc_is(lyra)\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 10\nart: heart\ntags: romance\nscene: Two students standing very close on a windswept bridge between towers, one with a hand over their heart.\ntext: On the bridge between the towers, the wind snatching at your robes, you realise you can't keep it in anymore. {npc} is looking at you like {they} know what you're about to say.\n> left: Say nothing\n  spark npc -small\n  say: The moment passes. You walk back together, a careful inch apart.\n> right: Tell {them}\n  challenge appeal apprentice likely +1 if spark(npc) >= 60:\n    win:\n      bond npc partner\n      spark npc +medium\n      mood +large\n      say: \"Finally,\" {npc} says, and kisses you, and the wind steals your breath anyway.\n    lose:\n      spark npc -large\n      mood -medium\n      say: {npc} takes a long breath. \"I care about you,\" {they} say{s}. \"Just not like that. I'm sorry.\" The wind is very cold.\n\n@event soc_partner_date\ntitle: Date Night\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 3\nweight: 14\nart: candle\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people at a tiny candlelit table in a window alcove of a village tea shop, snow falling outside.\ntext: {npc} has booked the window table at Mabel's. Candles. {season == \"winter\": Snow outside.|The lake going gold outside.} \"We're splitting the bill,\" {they} say{s}. \"Don't argue.\"\n> left: Talk about the future\n  gold -tiny\n  spark npc +small\n  mood +small\n  say: {&You talk about where you'll be in ten years. In every version, you're both there.|You talk about after graduation, carefully, then not carefully at all.|You make a list of places you'll go together. It is already too long.}\n> right: Talk about absolutely nothing\n  spark npc +tiny\n  mood +medium\n  say: You argue for an hour about {&whether soup is a drink|whether owls are smart or just look it|which professor would win in a duel}, and {npc} pays the bill while you're making your closing argument. {&It is the best date of your life.|It might be better than the last one.|You lose the argument and don't mind.}\n\n@event soc_partner_neglect\ntitle: \"You're Always Busy\"\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\" and apart(npc) >= 3\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 14\nrepeat: 5\nart: hourglass\ntags: romance\nscene: A person waiting alone at a table set for two, a candle burned low, the other chair empty.\ntext: {npc} is waiting outside your door. \"I feel like I only see you in corridors,\" {they} say{s}. \"Do you even want this?\"\n> left: \"I'm sorry. I'll make time.\"\n  spark npc +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: You skip a study session for a long walk by the lake. It's the right call.\n> right: \"You know how busy I am.\"\n  spark npc -medium\n  rel npc -medium\n  say: {npc} nods slowly. Something between you cools a degree.\n\n@event soc_breakup\ntitle: The End of Something\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\" and spark(npc) < 25\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 30\nart: heartbreak\ntags: romance crisis\nscene: Two people sitting on opposite ends of a bench in a grey garden, not looking at each other, rain beginning.\ntext: {npc} sits down at the far end of the bench. \"I think we both know,\" {they} say{s} quietly. \"This isn't working anymore.\"\n> left: Fight for it\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      spark npc +large\n      say: You talk for hours. It's raw and honest and it hurts. And at the end, {npc} takes your hand again.\n    lose:\n      bond npc ex\n      gain heartbroken\n      rel npc -large\n      say: You try. It only makes it harder. In the end you're both crying, and it's over anyway.\n> right: Let {them} go\n  bond npc ex\n  gain heartbroken\n  rel npc +small\n  say: You agree. It's terrible and gentle and right. {npc} kisses your forehead once before {they} leave{s}.\n\n@event soc_two_timing\ntitle: Found Out\nclass: incident\ntone: grim\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"partner\" and not npc_is(seraphine)\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nwhen: bonds(partner) >= 2\nweight: 40\nart: heartbreak\ntags: romance crisis\nscene: A person holding up two crumpled love letters in a crowded corridor, the crowd parting around them.\ntext: {npc} is holding two letters. One is to {them}. The other isn't. \"I found it in your bag,\" {they} say{s}, very quietly. Half the corridor has stopped to watch.\n> left: Beg forgiveness\n  challenge negotiate journeyman- unlikely:\n    win:\n      spark npc -large\n      rel npc -large\n      say: {npc} doesn't forgive you. But {they} don't leave. Not yet. You're on borrowed time.\n    lose:\n      bond npc ex\n      rel npc -huge\n      renown -medium\n      gain heartbroken\n      say: {npc} tears both letters in half and walks away. Everyone knows by dinner.\n> right: Say nothing\n  bond npc ex\n  rel npc -huge\n  renown -medium\n  say: Your silence is an answer. {npc} drops the letters at your feet and leaves. The story is all over the academy.\n\n@event soc_ex_encounter\ntitle: Awkward\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  npc: any where bond(npc) == \"ex\"\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 6\nart: mirror\ntags: romance\nscene: Two people coming face to face in a narrow library aisle, both holding books, neither moving.\ntext: {~You turn into the library aisle and there's {npc}. Your ex. Holding the same book you came for.|The only free seat in the refectory is opposite {npc}. Your ex. {They} look{s} up at the same moment you do.|You open the door to the owlery and there's {npc}. Your ex. Holding a letter, halfway to an owl.} Neither of you moves.\n> left: Turn around and leave\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You find a different book in a different aisle and read the same paragraph six times.\n> right: Say hello like a person\n  rel npc +medium\n  challenge appeal apprentice-:\n    win:\n      bond npc friend\n      say: It's awkward for one minute, then it isn't. You end up laughing about the worst of the dates you went on. Friends, maybe. Really.\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: \"Hello.\" \"Hello.\" You both stand there. Then you both leave. It's a start.\n\n# ---------------- Rivalry ----------------\n\n@event soc_rival_taunt\ntitle: A Public Taunt\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"rival\" or bond(npc) == \"nemesis\" or rel(npc) <= -12) and (npc_is(lyra) or npc_is(bram))\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nrepeat: 4\nart: mask\ntags: rival\nscene: A smirking student surrounded by a small audience, pointing at someone across a crowded dining hall.\ntext: In the middle of a crowded {~meal|common room|library queue}, {npc} loudly recounts {~your most embarrassing moment of the term|the time you walked into a door that was a painting|your worst spell in front of the whole class}. People laugh. {They} look{s} very pleased.\n> left: Ignore {them}\n  mood -small\n  say: The laughter dies down. {~You carry on with as much dignity as you can find.|You wait it out, and stare at a fixed point on the wall.|Someone nearby passes you a biscuit in silent solidarity.}\n> right: Fire back\n  challenge appeal vs npc.charm:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      rel npc -small\n      say: {~Your comeback is so good that someone at the far table applauds.|Your comeback lands so well that people repeat it for a week.|Your comeback is quiet, exact and devastating.} {npc} goes scarlet.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      rel npc -tiny\n      say: Your comeback comes out as a squeak. The laughter doubles.\n\n@event soc_make_peace\nramp: rel(npc) 3..15\ntime: noon afternoon\ntitle: Burying the Hatchet\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where tagged(npc, peer) and (bond(npc) == \"rival\" or bond(npc) == \"nemesis\") and rel(npc) >= 3 and not npc_is(lyra) and not npc_is(bram)\nper: npc\ntrigger: social\nweight: 18\nart: hand\ntags: rival social\nscene: Two former rivals shaking hands on a dueling green at sunset, wands tucked away.\ntext: {npc} finds you after class, scuffing {their} shoes. \"This whole rivalry thing. It's getting tiring. Truce?\"\n> left: Keep the edge\n  rel npc -small\n  say: \"Thought so,\" {npc} says, half-smiling. The game goes on.\n> right: Shake on it\n  bond npc friend\n  rel npc +large\n  mood +small\n  say: You shake. It's strange — you know {them} better than most of your friends. Maybe that's the point.\n\n@event soc_rival_bet\ntitle: The Wager\npool: social_routine\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  npc: any where (tagged(npc, student) or tagged(npc, prefect)) and bond(npc) == \"rival\"\nper: npc\nwhen: term and not wrapup\nart: dice\ntags: rival\nscene: Two students slapping coins onto a table between them, a crowd gathering, a chalked tally on the wall.\ntext: \"Eight crowns says I {~outscore you in the next practical|light more candles in one breath than you|hold a ward longer than you},\" {npc} says, slapping coins on the table. A crowd forms.\n> left: Decline the bet\n  rel npc -tiny\n  say: \"Thought so.\" {npc} pockets the coins. You decide not to care. You care.\n> right: Take the bet\n  when: gold >= 8\n  challenge spell vs npc.arcana wild:\n    win:\n      gold +small\n      renown +small\n      rel npc +small\n      say: You win by a hair. {npc} pays up with a theatrical bow. \"Next time.\"\n    lose:\n      gold -small\n      rel npc +tiny\n      say: You lose by a hair. \"Next time,\" you say. {npc} grins. \"Next time.\"\n\n# Spark crossing @balance spark_follow's cooldown mark (playtest 2, M37): the engine asks once whether you want it.\n@event spark_cooldown\ntitle: Something Between You\ntrigger: queued\nactor: npc\ndefer: off_campus or not around(npc)\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: rose\ntags: romance social\nscene: Two students on a bench a little closer than friends usually sit, both pretending to read the same page.\ntext: Something has shifted between you and {npc}. {~They hold your gaze a moment too long.|They've started saving you a seat, and blushing about it.|Your names keep turning up next to each other in other people's gossip.} You could let it grow, or gently let it cool.\n> left: Let it grow\n  say: {~You don't say anything. You don't move away either.|You hold their gaze back, for exactly as long.|Let them gossip. You sit beside them anyway, in front of everyone.}\n> right: Let it cool, kindly\n  cool npc\n  rel npc +tiny\n  say: {~You find a gentle way to say you value them as a friend. They look relieved, or almost.|You keep it light and friendly, and the moment passes. The friendship stays.|You give the gossip nothing to work with, gently, and in a week it moves on. The friendship doesn't.}\n\n# ---------------- Jealousy (playtest 2, N7; design §8.2): two people with a spark for you, neither your partner yet ----------------\n\n@pool social_routine_repeat2\ntrigger: social\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 2\nweight: 20\ntags: romance\n\n# Someone you've a spark with but aren't with, on campus: the two sides of jealousy's cards. An ex isn't one\n# (feedback 2026-10-25: Lyra as an ex walked in on you).\n@cond suitor\nparams: who\nis: romanceable(who) and spark(who) >= 30 and courting(who) and rel(who) >= 0 and around(who)\n\n@event jealousy_two_invitations\ntitle: Two Invitations\npool: social_routine_repeat2\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  suitor: any where suitor(suitor)\n  other: any where suitor(other)\nper: suitor\nart: heart\ntags: social\nscene: A student holding two folded notes, one in each hand, in a corridor where two people wait at opposite ends.\ntext: Two notes in one morning. {suitor} asks if you're free on Friday evening, in neat handwriting, crossed out once. {other} asks the same, for the same evening, in a hurry. They both know the other asked. The whole corridor seems to know.\n> left: Say yes to {suitor}\n  spark suitor +small\n  spark other -small\n  rel other -tiny\n  say: Friday with {suitor} is everything the note promised. On Saturday {other} passes you on the stairs with a smile that stops a little short of the eyes.\n> right: Say yes to {other}\n  spark other +small\n  spark suitor -small\n  rel suitor -tiny\n  say: Friday with {other} runs late and warm. On Saturday {suitor} is very polite to you at breakfast, which is worse than if they'd been rude.\n\n@event jealousy_seen_together\ntitle: \"Who Was That?\"\npool: social_routine_repeat2\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  suitor: any where suitor(suitor)\n  other: any where suitor(other)\nper: suitor\nart: heartbreak\ntags: social\nscene: Two students talking by a window in a crowded common room, one of them asking a question too casually while looking at someone across the room.\ntext: {suitor} saw you laughing with {other} in the courtyard yesterday, and is now asking about it too casually, in the voice people use when they've rehearsed. \"You two looked… close. Not that it's any of my business.\"\n> left: Tell the truth: you like {other}\n  spark suitor -small\n  rel suitor +tiny\n  spark other +tiny\n  say: It lands like a dropped plate. {suitor} nods, and swallows, and thanks you for not pretending. It's the kindest thing you've done all week, and it doesn't feel kind at all.\n> right: \"Oh, we're just friends\"\n  chance likely:\n    spark suitor +small\n    say: {suitor}'s shoulders come down an inch. You spend the rest of the evening together, and nobody mentions the courtyard again.\n  else:\n    spark suitor +tiny\n    spark other -small\n    say: It goes well, until word gets back to {other}, who hears \"just friends\" and decides to believe it.\n\n@event jealousy_cold_supper\ntitle: A Quiet Supper\npool: social_routine_repeat2\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  suitor: any where suitor(suitor)\n  other: any where suitor(other)\nper: suitor\nart: meal\ntags: social\nscene: A long refectory table at supper, one student eating in pointed silence beside another, a third person waving from the far end of the hall.\ntext: {suitor} has gone quiet at supper, and answers in single words. From the far end of the hall, {other} is waving you over to an empty seat. You know exactly why {suitor} is quiet. So, by the look of it, does {other}.\n> left: Stay and talk it through\n  spark suitor +small\n  spark other -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: A long walk after supper and a careful, clumsy conversation. {suitor} laughs at the end of it, which is something. At the far end of the hall, the empty seat stays empty.\n> right: Take the seat {other} saved\n  spark other +small\n  spark suitor -small\n  say: The seat is warm and so is {other}. Behind you, {suitor} finishes supper alone and leaves without the pudding, which everyone notices.\n"},{"path":"content/events/society.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  High society and fame among friends (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md, batch P4-10; design §8.2). The Season: once\n#  the patrons have noticed you (a patron's dinner, or being Famous), a round of suppers, Lady Vaneshade, a rival and\n#  a ball, as a `story` thread with Journal lines; built on existing canon (renown_patron_dinner, the Vaneshades).\n#  Then cross-system cards: what your fame does to the people around you (pool `fame_between`).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Season ----------------\n\n@thread the_season\nfrom: story\nwith: begun=flag(season_begun)\nclass: story\nrepeat: 1\ntags: story renown society\nname: The Season\npremise: The patrons had noticed you. Their round of suppers and their ball had a place laid for you, if you wanted it.\njournal:\n  flag(season_begun): An invitation came with a crest on it: the patrons' Season had a place laid for you.\n  flag(season_charmed): At the first supper you were the talk of the table.\n  flag(season_plain): At the first supper you were plainly yourself, and some of the table liked it.\n  flag(season_sponsored): Lady Vaneshade offered to sponsor you, and you let her.\n  flag(season_unsponsored): Lady Vaneshade offered to sponsor you, and you thanked her and said no.\n  flag(season_ball_star): At the patrons' ball, you outshone the Season's other darling.\n  flag(season_ball_shared): At the patrons' ball, you shared the floor with the Season's other darling.\n  flag(season_price_paid): A patron asked a favour in return, and you did it.\n  flag(season_price_refused): A patron asked a favour in return, and you refused.\n  flag(season_in): The Season ended with a standing invitation. You belong among them now, a little.\n  flag(season_out): The Season ended, and you went back up the hill and didn't look back.\nstatus:\n  resolved: flag(season_in) or flag(season_out)\nmembers:\n  start season_invitation\n\n@event season_invitation\ntitle: A Card With a Crest\ntrigger: auto\ntone: warm\nwhen: term and not wrapup and month <= 3 and (renown_famous() or (renown_known() and seen(renown_patron_dinner) > 0)) and not flag(season_begun) and seen(season_invitation) == 0\npriority: 40\nart: letter\nscene: A heavy cream card with a gilt crest lying on a dormitory bed beside a pile of textbooks, a student holding it up to the light.\ntext: The card is heavy, cream and gilt-edged, and it has your name on it in a hand that has written a great many names. The patrons of the academy, it says, would be delighted to see you at the Season: a round of suppers in the town houses below the hill, and a ball at the end of it. People who go to the Season come back different. People say so as if it were a warning.\n> left: Accept\n  flag season_begun\n  gold -small\n  renown +small\n  queue season_first_supper in 1\n  say: You write back in your best hand and spend more than you meant on a decent coat. Word gets round the castle that you're going. Your friends are impressed and a little wary. \"Don't come back talking about wine,\" one of them says.\n> right: Send your regrets\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You send a polite note saying you're afraid your studies won't allow it. It's half true. The card goes in a drawer, and now and then you take it out and look at the crest.\n\n@event season_first_supper\ntitle: The First Supper\nthread: the_season\ntrigger: queued\ntone: tense\ndefer: off_campus\nart: meal\nscene: A long candlelit table in a townhouse dining room, silver and crystal everywhere, a student in a new coat seated between two elderly patrons in jewels.\ntext: The first supper is twelve courses in a town house with a ceiling painted with clouds. You're seated between a retired magistrate and a woman who owns half the orchards in the valley. Both of them have read about you. Both of them want to see whether you're as interesting as the Ledger said. The magistrate has an opinion on every course, and is plainly waiting for somebody to disagree with him.\n> left: Argue with the magistrate\n  challenge appeal adept-:\n    win:\n      flag season_charmed\n      renown +small\n      queue season_lady_vaneshade in 1\n      say: You tell the magistrate he's wrong about the soup, and then about a famous old trial, and he's delighted: nobody has disagreed with him since he retired. By the ninth course the whole table is listening to the two of you. Someone at the far end asks your name twice, to remember it.\n    lose:\n      flag season_plain\n      mood -small\n      queue season_lady_vaneshade in 1\n      say: You take the magistrate on over an old case, and turn out to have the facts backwards, in front of the man who heard it. The orchard woman changes the subject, kindly, which is worse. You spend the last three courses very interested in your soup.\n> right: Ask about her orchards\n  flag season_plain\n  mood +tiny\n  queue season_lady_vaneshade in 1\n  say: You leave the magistrate to the soup. You ask the orchard woman about her orchards, and she talks for an hour, delighted, about apple blight. The table thinks you're a little dull. She thinks you're the best guest she's had in years.\n\n@event season_lady_vaneshade\ntitle: Lady Vaneshade's Offer\nthread: the_season\ntrigger: queued\ntone: tense\ndefer: off_campus\nart: crown\nscene: A tall woman in crimson and black in a drawing room by a fire, holding a glass she isn't drinking from, a student standing very straight before her.\ntext: At the second supper, Lady Vaneshade{met(lyra): , Lyra's mother,|} takes you aside into a drawing room. \"You'll want a sponsor,\" she says. \"Someone to tell you which fork, and which people. I could be that. In return, you'll be mine: seen with my family, and not with anyone I'd rather you weren't.\" She doesn't say who that is. She doesn't have to.\n> left: Accept her sponsorship\n  flag season_sponsored\n  gold +medium\n  renown +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  queue season_rival in 1\n  say: \"Good,\" she says, and from then on doors open before you reach them. She tells you which forks. She also tells you, once, very quietly, who not to be seen with, and you find that you've done as she says before you've thought about it.\n> right: Thank her, and say no\n  flag season_unsponsored\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  queue season_rival in 1\n  say: \"I'd rather find my own forks,\" you say. Lady Vaneshade looks at you for a long moment, then smiles, not warmly. \"Most people do,\" she says. \"Most people find the wrong ones.\" The doors stay shut a little longer after that.\n\n@event season_rival\ntitle: The Season's Other Darling\nthread: the_season\ntrigger: queued\ntone: tense\ndefer: off_campus\nart: duel\nscene: A crowded salon, a dashing young duelling champion holding court by the piano, a ring of admirers, a student watching from the doorway.\ntext: The Season always has a darling, and this one had one before you came: a young duelling champion from the city, handsome, charming and very used to being the most interesting person in the room. At the third supper they find you by the piano. \"So you're the student,\" they say. \"Shall we find out which of us they'll remember?\"\n> left: Take them on\n  challenge perform adept:\n    win:\n      flag season_ball_star\n      renown +medium\n      queue season_ball in 1\n      say: You don't compete. You just tell the room a better story than they do, and the room turns, slowly, like a ship. The champion laughs, and bows, and says, \"Well. Until the ball, then.\" The whole Season hears about it by morning.\n    lose:\n      flag season_ball_shared\n      mood -small\n      queue season_ball in 1\n      say: You try, and the champion is better at this than you, much better, and the room stays with them. Afterwards they find you on the terrace. \"You were good,\" they say. \"I've just had more practice.\" It's oddly comforting.\n> right: Make a friend of them\n  flag season_ball_shared\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  queue season_ball in 1\n  say: \"Why don't we not?\" you say, and the champion looks surprised, then delighted. You spend the rest of the evening on the terrace, laughing at everyone else. The Season is a little disappointed. You've never had a better supper.\n\n@event season_ball\ntitle: The Patrons' Ball\nthread: the_season\ntrigger: queued\ntone: warm\ndefer: off_campus\nart: music\nscene: A grand townhouse ballroom, chandeliers blazing, couples whirling, a student pausing at the top of a staircase as the room turns to look.\ntext: The Season ends with the patrons' ball. {flag(season_ball_star): Everyone knows by now that you're the one to watch, and the room turns when you come down the stairs.|You and the champion come down the stairs together, by arrangement, and the room can't decide which of you to look at.} The patrons are watching who you dance with, who you talk to, and how long you stay.\n> left: Stay till dawn, and dance with everyone\n  renown +medium\n  health -small\n  gold -small\n  queue season_friends in 1\n  say: You dance with the magistrate, the orchard woman, the champion and a very old duke who treads on your feet with great dignity. You leave at dawn with your shoes in your hand. By noon every patron in the valley knows your name.\n> right: Leave at midnight, while it's still lovely\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  queue season_friends in 1\n  say: You dance three dances, eat one perfect pastry and slip out at midnight into the cold. Walking up the hill under the stars, you think it was the loveliest night of your life, and you're glad you didn't stay long enough to spoil it.\n\n@event season_friends\ntitle: Back Up the Hill\nthread: the_season\ntrigger: queued\ntone: tense\ndefer: off_campus\ncast:\n  f: any where classmate_here(f) and rel(f) >= 25\nart: group\nscene: A common room on an ordinary evening, a group of friends around a table mid-laugh, one student standing in the doorway still in a good coat.\ntext: You come back to the common room after the Season, still in the good coat, and {f} and the others are in the middle of a joke you don't understand, about something that happened while you were away. {f} looks up. \"Oh,\" {they} say{s}. \"You're back. How were the patrons?\" It's not unkind. It's not quite warm either.\n> left: Tell them everything\n  renown +tiny\n  rel f -small\n  queue season_price in 1\n  say: You tell them about the ceiling with the clouds and the duke who trod on your feet. They listen, politely. Somewhere in the middle, you notice {f} looking at the coat. Later, you hear the joke you missed. It was very good. You weren't there.\n> right: Take off the coat and sit down\n  rel f +small\n  renown -tiny\n  queue season_price in 1\n  say: You hang the coat on the back of the door and sit down and ask what the joke was. It takes three people to explain it and by the end you're all crying with laughter. {f} passes you the last of the biscuits, as if you'd never gone.\n\n@event season_price\ntitle: One Word From You\nthread: the_season\ntrigger: queued\ntone: tense\ndefer: off_campus\nart: scales\nscene: A letter with a crest open on a desk beside a quill and an inkpot, a student sitting back with arms crossed, frowning at it.\ntext: A letter from one of the patrons, very warm, very grateful for your company at the Season. And a small favour, between friends: the patron's grandson is applying to the Duelling Circle, and a word from you would settle it. You've seen the grandson duel. He's dreadful, and someone better will lose the place.\n> left: Write the word\n  flag season_price_paid\n  alignment -small\n  gold +small\n  renown +tiny\n  queue season_end now\n  say: You write two warm sentences and seal them. The grandson gets his place. A purse arrives with the patron's thanks. A second-year who should have had the place stops you in the corridor to ask if you know why they didn't. You say no.\n> right: Refuse, politely\n  flag season_price_refused\n  alignment +small\n  renown -tiny\n  queue season_end now\n  say: You write back that the Circle chooses its own, and you wouldn't presume. The patron's reply is short and cool. The second-year gets the place, and never knows you had anything to do with it. That seems right.\n\n@event season_end\ntitle: The Season Ends\nthread: the_season\ntrigger: queued\ntone: warm\ndefer: off_campus\nart: letter\nscene: A student at a window with a heavy cream card in one hand, looking down at the lights of the town houses below the hill.\ntext: Another card with a crest: the patrons hope you'll come again next Season, and the one after. {flag(season_sponsored): Lady Vaneshade has added a line in her own hand: \"You did well. Do better.\"|flag(season_price_refused): One patron's name, you notice, is missing from the bottom.|It is signed by every patron in the valley.} You could have a place among them, if you wanted one, for good.\n> left: Take your place among them\n  flag season_in\n  renown +small\n  gold +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You write back that you'd be honoured. It's a door you can walk through now whenever you like, and you suspect you'll walk through it for the rest of your life. You're not sure, some evenings, whether that's a good thing.\n> right: Let the Season go\n  flag season_out\n  mood +small\n  renown -small\n  say: You write back, kindly, that you've loved it and won't come again. Then you put the card in the drawer with the first one, and go down to the common room, where somebody has saved you a seat by the fire.\n\n# ---------------- fame among friends ----------------\n\n@pool fame_between\ndesc: What your fame does to the people around you: dealt with the other spotlight cards while you're Known.\nfrom: spotlight\nwhen: renown_known()\nweight: 8\n\n@event fame_rivals_for_you\ntitle: Both Want You Tonight\npool: fame_between\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 15\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 15 and at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\nart: cup\nscene: A courtyard at dusk, two students on either side of another, each holding an invitation and pointedly not looking at the other.\ntext: {a} and {b} can't stand each other, and both of them have asked you to the Newt tonight, separately, each making very sure the other heard. It isn't really about the Newt. It's about who gets to be seen walking in with you. They're both waiting for your answer, in front of half the courtyard.\n> left: Go with {a}\n  rel a +small\n  rel b -small\n  npc_rel a b -tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  say: You go with {a}. {a} walks into the Newt a little taller than usual. {b} hears about it by breakfast, and greets you all week with a small, bright, terrible smile.\n> right: Turn both down, kindly\n  rel a -tiny\n  rel b -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You tell them both you're staying in, and do, and read in the common room till late. Each of them assumes you've gone with the other. By the time they work out you haven't, they've both forgiven you.\n\n@event fame_wedge\ntitle: Using You\npool: fame_between\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 25\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 15 and get_along(a, b)\nper: a\nart: mask\nscene: Two friends arguing in low voices in an alcove of a corridor, one of them gesturing back the way they came, where another student is waiting.\ntext: {a} takes you aside. \"I don't like how {b} is with you lately,\" {they} say{s}. \"{b} never sat with us before you were famous. Now {b}'s everywhere you are, telling people you're close.\" {a} and {b} have been friends for years. You can see it costing {a} to say it.\n> left: Tell {a} {b} is a friend\n  rel a -small\n  rel b +small\n  npc_rel a b -tiny\n  say: \"{b}'s always been kind to me,\" you say. {a} looks at you for a moment, then nods, stiffly. A week later you see {a} and {b} at opposite ends of the refectory. You're fairly sure that was you.\n> right: Thank {a}, and watch\n  rel a +small\n  npc_rel a b +tiny\n  study -tiny\n  say: You thank {a}, and say you'll keep an eye out, and you do. {b} is, you notice, a lot more interested in where you're going than in you. You start sitting with {a} again. {a} doesn't say \"I told you so\", which is generous.\n\n@event fame_lend_your_name\ntitle: Your Name on It\npool: fame_between\ntone: light\ncast:\n  f: any where classmate_here(f) and rel(f) >= 20\nper: f\nart: paper\nscene: A student holding a hand-painted sign for a stall at a fair, a pot of paint and a brush ready, looking hopefully at a friend.\ntext: {f} is running a stall at the next fair, selling enchanted bookmarks to pay for next year's books, and has had an idea: what if the sign said \"as used by {player}\"? You have, in fact, used one. Once. \"It'd sell out in an hour,\" {f} says. \"Go on. I can't afford the set texts otherwise.\"\n> left: Let {f} use your name\n  chance even +1 if renown >= 60:\n    rel f +small\n    renown +small\n    say: The stall sells out in forty minutes, and {f} can buy every book on next year's list, with enough over for a new quill. {f} is giddy all evening. You're asked to sign three bookmarks, and you do, and one of them ends up in the library, where it marks a page for years.\n  else:\n    rel f +tiny\n    renown -small\n    say: The sign draws a crowd, and then the bookmarks start marking the wrong pages, all of them, at once. People bring them back in handfuls, saying your name in a tone you don't like. {f} is mortified. You help pack up the stall.\n> right: Help at the stall instead\n  rel f +small\n  study -small\n  say: You say your name stays off the sign, but you'll spend the afternoon behind the stall. You sell twenty bookmarks, badly, and {f} sells two hundred, very well. It's one of the nicest afternoons of the term.\n\n@event fame_best_friend_claims\ntitle: Your Best Friend, Twice\npool: fame_between\ntone: light\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 10\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 10 and at_odds(a, b)\nper: a\nart: paper\nscene: A gossip column on a corridor board with two paragraphs circled in different inks, two students on opposite sides of the corridor, arms folded.\ntext: The gossip column this week has two letters. In one, {a} writes that {a} is \"{player}'s closest friend, as everyone knows\". In the other, {b} writes exactly the same thing about {b}. They can't stand each other. Everyone has read both, and everyone is waiting to see what you'll say.\n> left: Write to the column yourself\n  challenge perform adept-:\n    win:\n      npc_rel a b +small\n      rel a +tiny\n      rel b +tiny\n      say: You write one line: that your closest friends are the two people who wrote in, and that they should compare notes, since they have so much in common. The column prints it. {a} and {b} are furious with you, together, which is a start.\n    lose:\n      rel a -tiny\n      rel b -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: Your letter is meant to be funny and reads as if you were choosing. Each of them decides you meant the other. The column runs a follow-up, with diagrams.\n> right: Say nothing at all\n  rel a -tiny\n  rel b -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You say nothing, to anyone. The column decides your closest friend must be someone else entirely, and spends a fortnight guessing who. {a} and {b}, united at last, both think it's very unfair.\n\n@event fame_after_you\ntitle: What They Found Instead\npool: fallen\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 10\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 10 and npc_rel(a, b) >= 20\nper: a\nart: heart\nscene: A quiet lakeshore path in the evening, two students walking close together ahead, laughing, a third student watching them from a bench.\ntext: When you were famous, {a} and {b} used to argue over who got to walk with you. Now nobody fights over you, and you see the two of them on the lakeshore path in the evening, walking together, very close, laughing at something. They haven't noticed you on the bench. They don't need to.\n> left: Call out to them\n  rel a +small\n  rel b +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You call out, and they turn, and wave, and come over, and the three of you sit on the bench until it's dark. They're kind. You can see, though, that they'd have been just as happy walking on.\n> right: Let them walk on\n  mood +small\n  npc_rel a b +tiny\n  say: You let them walk on, and watch them go round the curve of the shore until you can't see them. It's a strange feeling, being the reason two people met, and not being needed any more. Mostly it's a good one.\n"},{"path":"content/events/spell_sources.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Where spells come from (spells v2, docs/spells-v2-list.md §B, §F).\n#  Course spells: each course's `start:` card comes in the month you\n#  enrol and teaches that level of the course's spell (`level(x)` is\n#  the levels passed, so each card has a scene per level). Passing\n#  the level would teach it too (`teaches:`), but by then it is known. Ward is the\n#  orientation safety talk (slice_ward_lesson). Club and job spells\n#  are in club_spell_chains.grim.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Course spells (3 levels) ----------------\n\n@event evo_first_flame\ntitle: Flame, Properly\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: enrolled(evocation) and spell_level(flame) <= level(evocation)\nart: spell_flame\ntags: class magic\nscene: A lecture hall with one word chalked on the board, students holding small flames on their palms, a grey-bearded professor watching from the lectern.\ntext: {level(evocation) == 0: Professor Thorne writes one word on the board: FLAME. \"Every duellist you will ever face has this ready before their wand is out,\" he says. \"So will you. By Friday.\"|{level(evocation) == 1: Evocation II meets at the duel hall, not the lectern. Thorne has set a straw target forty paces off. \"Last year you lit candles. This year the fire goes where you send it, and arrives.\"|Advanced Evocation opens with Thorne standing in a ring of fire he did not light. \"This is what a duel looks like when it goes wrong,\" he says. \"Your flame must hold while you are hit.\" He has hired a senior to hit you.}}\n> left: Drill it until it comes\n  learn_spell flame\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {level(evocation) == 0: By Friday the flame comes when you call it, and your fingertips are pink and tender. Thorne looks at your palm for a long moment. \"Hm,\" he says.|{level(evocation) == 1: You throw until your sleeves smoke. On the sixtieth try the straw target goes up with a satisfying whump. Thorne writes something down, without comment.|The senior hits you nine times. On the tenth your flame stays lit through it, and the senior, singed, asks for a rest. Thorne says \"Better,\" which is new.}}\n> right: Learn the shape of it first\n  learn_spell flame\n  study evocation +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {level(evocation) == 0: You work it out on paper while the room fills with small fires. Yours comes last, on Thursday night, alone in your room. It's a very correct flame.|{level(evocation) == 1: You chart the target's distance and the arc a flame takes. Your fire lands late but dead centre. Nobody saw, because they'd gone to lunch.|You watch how Thorne's ring keeps burning when the senior hits him. It takes a week of evenings to copy it, and the class teases you for every one.}}\n\n@event astro_first_foresight\ntitle: A Moment Ahead\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: enrolled(astromancy) and spell_level(foresight) <= level(astromancy)\nart: spell_foresight\ntags: class magic\nscene: An observatory classroom by day, a coin spinning on a brass desk, students leaning in with their eyes half-closed.\ntext: {level(astromancy) == 0: Astromancy I begins with a coin. The lecturer spins it on the brass desk and asks which way it will fall. \"Don't guess,\" the lecturer says. \"Look a moment ahead and read it.\"|{level(astromancy) == 1: Astromancy II, and Foresight must reach further. The lecturer deals star cards face down. \"The next three, in order, before I turn them.\"|Advanced Astromancy: the lecturer sits opposite you and says nothing at all. \"Foresee what I am about to say,\" the lecturer says at last, \"and say it first.\"}}\n> left: Look ahead until you see it\n  learn_spell foresight\n  lore +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {level(astromancy) == 0: The coin falls heads eleven times before you see it fall heads first. Then you see it every time, and your head aches with how far you're leaning.|{level(astromancy) == 1: You see the third card before the first, which is the wrong order and still counts as a start. By the bell you read all three, drained to the soles of your feet.|You say \"Tuesday\" a heartbeat before the lecturer does. It costs you an afternoon of feeling hollowed out, and a small, rare nod.}}\n> right: Ask how far ahead to look\n  learn_spell foresight\n  study astromancy +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {level(astromancy) == 0: \"Less far than you think,\" says the lecturer, and makes you read the rules of it twice. You get the coin right on the last try of the hour, with the class watching.|{level(astromancy) == 1: The answer is a chart, and the chart is long. You copy it all and read the cards the next week, after everyone else.|\"A breath,\" says the lecturer. \"Anything past a breath is fortune-telling.\" You learn the breath slowly, and are the last in the room to get it.}}\n\n@event alch_first_mend\ntitle: The Mending Practical\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: enrolled(alchemy) and spell_level(mend) <= level(alchemy)\nart: spell_mend\ntags: class magic\nscene: An alchemy bench with a row of cracked teacups, a healer setting down the last one, students holding their hands above them.\ntext: {level(alchemy) == 0: Alchemy I's first practical is a cracked teacup on every bench. Sister Amaranth sets them down without looking up. \"Mend it,\" she says. \"Then I'll let you near a person.\"|{level(alchemy) == 1: Alchemy II moves into the infirmary. Sister Amaranth hands you a first-year with a split lip from the Duelling Circle. \"Skin this time,\" she says. \"It argues more than china.\"|Advanced Alchemy: Mend for what's deep. Sister Amaranth gives you a sprained wrist, its unhappy owner and her own pocket watch. \"Bone takes patience,\" she says. \"Patients don't.\"}}\n> left: Mend it until it holds\n  learn_spell mend\n  alchemy +small\n  mana -small\n  say: {level(alchemy) == 0: Your cup holds tea on the fifth try and leaks on the sixth, and you've spent everything getting there. Sister Amaranth pours some anyway and drinks it. \"Keep doing that,\" she says.|{level(alchemy) == 1: The lip closes under your hand, slowly, and you feel every bit of it leave you. The first-year says it tickles. Sister Amaranth says that means it worked.|The sprain knits with the watch ticking beside you. You're wrung out by the end, and the wrist bends like a wrist. Sister Amaranth puts the watch away. \"Again tomorrow,\" she says, which is praise.}}\n> right: Watch her do one first\n  learn_spell mend\n  study alchemy +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {level(alchemy) == 0: She mends hers without stopping her conversation with the next bench. You copy her hands. Yours takes the whole hour, and the bench beside you finishes first.|{level(alchemy) == 1: You watch her close a cut from the inside out. When your turn comes it's slower than hers by a mile, and the first-year sighs the whole time.|You watch her set a wrist and mend it in one quiet minute. Yours takes twenty, and the patient tells you about every one.}}\n\n@event illu_first_glamour\ntitle: Lighting, Not Lying\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: enrolled(illusion) and spell_level(glamour) <= level(illusion)\nart: spell_glamour\ntags: class magic\nscene: A bright classroom where a smiling professor in a violet scarf sits on her own desk, students across from her trying very hard to look likeable.\ntext: {level(illusion) == 0: Illusion I begins where Professor Vey always begins: every student glamours their own face first, one small change held for a minute. Then she sits on her desk. \"Now make me like you more, without a word. Glamour is not lying. It's lighting.\"|{level(illusion) == 1: Illusion II: glamour that lasts past the door. Vey sends you to the refectory at lunch with one rule. \"Be remembered for something you didn't do.\"|Advanced Illusion opens with Vey wearing your face. She teaches the first ten minutes in your voice, quite well. \"Now take it back,\" she says.}}\n> left: Try it on Vey herself\n  learn_spell glamour\n  charm +small\n  mana -small\n  if level(illusion) == 1:\n    alignment -tiny\n  say: {level(illusion) == 0: Vey likes you a great deal for almost a minute, then shakes it off and applauds. You feel wrung out, as if you'd smiled all day.|{level(illusion) == 1: By supper three people remember you saving the cook from a falling ladle. There was no ladle. You're too tired to enjoy it.|You take your face back from her a feature at a time. It costs you everything you had. Vey hands over the last one, your frown, with regret.}}\n> right: Practise on a mirror first\n  learn_spell glamour\n  study illusion +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {level(illusion) == 0: You glamour your reflection, which likes you and is no help at all. Vey catches you at it. \"Start with a harder audience,\" she says, and the class laughs.|{level(illusion) == 1: You rehearse in the washroom mirror until a prefect walks in on you being charming at nobody. The glamour works on the second day.|You practise your own face for a week before you try to take it back. By then everyone has met Vey-as-you, and they liked her better.}}\n\n@event beast_first_haste\ntitle: Borrowed Speed\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: enrolled(beastcraft) and spell_level(haste) <= level(beastcraft)\nart: spell_haste\ntags: class magic nature\nscene: A muddy paddock at the stables, a weathered professor pointing at a hare while a student crouches to sprint.\ntext: {level(beastcraft) == 0: Beastcraft I's first spell is taught at a run. Professor Brask points at a hare in the paddock. \"Catch it. You won't, on your legs.\" She teaches you Haste in four words and lets the hare go.|{level(beastcraft) == 1: Beastcraft II: Haste in the air. Brask has a hawk on her glove and a broom against the fence. \"Keep up with her,\" she says, \"to the far tower and back.\"|Advanced Beastcraft: Brask's test is to keep pace with a hippogriff at full gallop, the length of the flight field, and still be able to talk at the end.}}\n> left: Run it down\n  learn_spell haste\n  vigor +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {level(beastcraft) == 0: You catch the hare on the fourth go, briefly, and it bites you. You sleep eleven hours. Brask says that's the price, and the hare agrees.|{level(beastcraft) == 1: You reach the far tower a wingbeat behind the hawk, and the hawk looks smug about it. Your legs shake all through supper.|You run the field at the hippogriff's shoulder and say \"good afternoon\" at the end, gasping. Brask laughs once. You're stiff for three days.}}\n> right: Study how it moves first\n  learn_spell haste\n  study beastcraft +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {level(beastcraft) == 0: You watch the hare for an hour, then borrow its speed properly, once, at the end. Everyone else is already muddy and proud of it.|{level(beastcraft) == 1: You watch the hawk's turns from the fence and only fly the course at dusk, alone. It's tidy. Nobody sees.|You study the gallop from the rail until you can feel it in your knees. You run it last, slower than the rest, and very sure.}}\n\n@event umbral_first_shadowstep\ntitle: The Shadows in Between\nclass: milestone\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: enrolled(umbral) and spell_level(shadowstep) <= level(umbral)\nart: spell_shadowstep\ntags: class magic\nscene: The top of the lecture tower in near darkness, two pools of lamplight on the floor and a long shadow between them, a student stepping into it.\ntext: {level(umbral) == 0: Umbral Theory I. The lecturer snuffs every lamp but two and stands in the dark between them. \"Shadowstep,\" says the voice. \"In at one shadow, out at another. Don't stop in the middle.\"|{level(umbral) == 1: Umbral Theory II: Shadowstep under watch. The lecturer sits a prefect in the doorway with a lantern. \"Out of the room,\" says the lecturer, \"without being seen leaving it.\"|Advanced Umbral Theory: step, and keep stepping. At dusk the lecturer lays a path of shadows across the tower roof, chimney to chimney. \"To the far side. Without the floor.\"}}\n> left: Step through and trust it\n  learn_spell shadowstep\n  guile +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: {level(umbral) == 0: You step in, and the dark is cold, and you come out against a desk with your shin. It counts. The lecturer's voice says, \"Again,\" from somewhere new.|{level(umbral) == 1: You leave by the prefect's own shadow and arrive in the corridor with your elbow in a coat rack. The prefect is still watching the door.|Chimney to chimney, six steps, and on the fifth the shadow is thinner than you thought. You land on the far side with grazed palms and a grin.}}\n> right: Map the shadows first\n  learn_spell shadowstep\n  study umbral +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {level(umbral) == 0: You sketch where every shadow falls before you trust one. The class has stepped twice by then and is bored of watching you. Your step, when it comes, is clean.|{level(umbral) == 1: You learn where the lantern can't reach before you move, and the bell goes first. You get out the next day, unseen and unhurried.|You chart the roof's shadows as the sun goes down and miss the light for it. You cross at full dark, alone, to no applause.}}\n\n# ---------------- Hidden courses (1 level) ----------------\n\n@event art_first_animate\ntitle: Make It Want One Thing\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: enrolled(artifice) and spell_level(animate) == 0\nart: spell_animate\ntags: class magic\nscene: A cluttered workshop with a broom sweeping the floor by itself in uneven circles, a student dodging it, brass gears on every bench.\ntext: Artifice begins with a broom. The lecturer says one word and the broom sweeps the workshop by itself, badly and with enthusiasm. \"Animate,\" the lecturer says. \"Give a made thing one want, and only one. Yours next.\"\n> left: Give your broom its want\n  learn_spell animate\n  alchemy +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: Your broom wants to sweep, and sweeps. It also wants to sweep you, and does, out of the door and halfway down the stairs. The lecturer marks it a pass.\n> right: Read how the old ones were made\n  learn_spell animate\n  study artifice +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You read the workshop's oldest notes on wanting things until your broom is the last one still lying down. When it rises, it sweeps one perfect circle and stops, satisfied.\n\n@event star_first_starlight\ntitle: A Name to Call\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: enrolled(starcalling) and spell_level(starlight) == 0\nart: spell_starlight\ntags: class magic\nscene: The observatory dome open to a black sky, a tutor pointing out one faint star to a student whose hand is raised toward it.\ntext: The first appointment is under the open dome, past midnight. Your tutor says a star's name aloud, the old name, the one on no chart, and points. \"Ask it for a thread of its light,\" the tutor says. \"Ask properly. It has been asked rudely before.\"\n> left: Call until it answers\n  learn_spell starlight\n  lore +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You call until your voice goes and your hands are numb on the rail. At four the thread comes down, no thicker than a hair, and rests in your palm, warm. You don't feel the cold again until breakfast.\n> right: Learn whose name it is first\n  learn_spell starlight\n  study starcalling +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You ask about the name instead, and get an hour of history you'll need later. The thread comes for you at dawn, the last in the dome, with the tutor already yawning.\n\n@event hollow_first_seal\ntitle: Closed, Even to Me\nclass: milestone\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: queued\nwhen: enrolled(hollow_studies) and spell_level(seal) == 0\nart: spell_seal\ntags: class magic\nscene: A room without windows lined with black-bound books, a headmistress setting an open, empty wooden box on the table in front of a student.\ntext: The headmistress sets a small wooden box on the table and opens it. It is empty. \"Close it,\" she says, \"so that it stays closed. To anyone. Even to me.\" She sits down across from you with her hands folded. \"That is Seal.\"\n> left: Seal it as tight as you can\n  learn_spell seal\n  guile +small\n  mana -small\n  say: It takes everything you have and the rest of the hour. The headmistress tries the lid with one finger, then two, then not at all. \"Good,\" she says. You feel scraped hollow until supper.\n> right: Ask what she keeps sealed\n  learn_spell seal\n  study hollow_studies +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Many things,\" she says, \"for good reasons.\" She doesn't say more, and you seal the box with that sitting in your stomach. It holds. She doesn't try it.\n\n# ---------------- Peer capstones (1 level) ----------------\n# stub: each is a placeholder milestone at the \"close\" rung (or as partner), one teaching beat, to be replaced by a\n# 5–7 card chain (docs/spells-v2-list.md §F). Turning it down keeps the offer open: it comes back after `repeat`.\n\n@event lyra_teaches_wildfire\ntitle: Let It Run\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: lyra\nwhen: (bond(lyra) == \"close\" or bond(lyra) == \"partner\") and spell_level(wildfire) == 0\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 20\nart: spell_wildfire\ntags: social magic\nscene: An empty duel hall at night, a crimson-haired student holding a fire that keeps growing in her cupped hands, its light up to the rafters.\ntext: Lyra lets you into the duel hall after it has closed. She cups her hands and the fire in them grows, and grows, until it lights the rafters. \"They taught me to keep it small,\" she says. \"This is what it does when I stop. {I've never shown anyone.\"|You've seen it once already. The offer's still open.\"}\n> left: Ask her to teach you\n  learn_spell wildfire\n  rel lyra +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: She teaches it the way she does everything, without explaining, twice. Your fire runs on the third try and bites your wrist on the way out. \"It does that,\" she says. \"You stop apologising for it eventually.\"\n> right: Just watch her let it run\n  arcana +small\n  rel lyra +tiny\n  say: You sit on the bench and watch her let it run to the rafters and back. She doesn't offer again tonight. On the way out she says, \"Another time,\" which from Lyra is a promise.\n\n@event tobias_teaches_heartwood\ntitle: Something to Stand Under\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: tobias\nwhen: (bond(tobias) == \"close\" or bond(tobias) == \"partner\") and spell_level(heartwood) == 0\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 20\nart: spell_heartwood\ntags: social magic nature\nscene: A greenhouse at dusk, a broad young man kneeling over bare soil as a sapling shoots up into a sheltering tree between two students.\ntext: Tobias kneels in an empty bed at the back of the greenhouse. \"Ma used to say a farm's only as good as its shelter,\" he says, and presses his hands into the soil. A sapling comes up between you, then a trunk, then branches overhead. \"Took me a year. I'll show you in an evening, if you like.\"\n> left: Kneel down beside him\n  learn_spell heartwood\n  rel tobias +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You kneel in the cold soil until your knees ache and your back complains. Near midnight a little tree stands up out of your bed, wobbling, and spreads one branch over you. Tobias laughs from the belly and apologises for the mud.\n> right: Sit under his tree instead\n  mood +small\n  rel tobias +tiny\n  say: You sit under his tree and he tells you a long story about a goat. It is very peaceful. \"Another evening, then,\" he says, and means it.\n\n@event seraphine_teaches_omen\ntitle: Say It So It Lands\nclass: milestone\ntone: eerie\ntrigger: social\nactor: seraphine\nwhen: (bond(seraphine) == \"close\" or bond(seraphine) == \"partner\") and spell_level(omen) == 0\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 20\nart: spell_omen\ntags: social magic\nscene: The observatory at night, a pale student with distant eyes speaking softly while a candle across the room gutters out exactly as she finishes.\ntext: Seraphine stops halfway through a sentence and looks at the candle across the observatory. \"That will go out,\" she says, quite clearly, \"when I've finished the next word.\" It does. \"Saying it is the spell. Seeing it is just… I can teach you the saying part.\"\n> left: Learn to say it out loud\n  learn_spell omen\n  rel seraphine +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: It takes the whole night, and it's unsettling to hear your own voice go flat and certain. Near dawn you foretell a dropped spoon and the spoon drops. Seraphine smiles at something next week.\n> right: Ask what she sees for you\n  lore +small\n  rel seraphine +tiny\n  say: \"Quiet,\" she says. \"Yours is always quiet.\" She doesn't teach you tonight. She says you'll ask again, and you know she's right, which is the strange part.\n\n@event corvin_teaches_sleight\ntitle: Watch the Other Hand\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: corvin\nwhen: (bond(corvin) == \"close\" or bond(corvin) == \"partner\") and spell_level(sleight) == 0\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 20\nart: spell_sleight\ntags: social magic\nscene: A corner table at a tavern in the evening, a grinning student holding up a coin that is clearly not where it was a moment ago.\ntext: At the Gilded Newt, Corvin rolls a coin across his knuckles, and then it's in your pocket, and then it's back in his. \"Grandfather's trick,\" he says. \"I'd teach it to a saint, if the saint promised to only use it on people who deserve it.\" The jokes stop. \"Do you promise?\"\n> left: Promise, and learn it\n  learn_spell sleight\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: You promise. He teaches you over three drinks you don't pay for, and by the last one you lift his coin without him feeling it. He looks at you for a long moment. \"Don't make me sorry,\" he says, quietly.\n> right: \"Not that one, Corvin.\"\n  rel corvin +tiny\n  say: \"Saint,\" he says, fondly, and the coin goes back in his own pocket. He buys the next round anyway. You suspect he's relieved.\n\n@event mir_teaches_contraption\ntitle: It Probably Works\nclass: milestone\ntone: light\ntrigger: social\nactor: mir\nwhen: (bond(mir) == \"close\" or bond(mir) == \"partner\") and spell_level(contraption) == 0\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 20\nart: spell_contraption\ntags: social magic\nscene: A cluttered workshop, an artificer flinging together a whirring machine from a kettle, a clock and a spoon while another student ducks.\ntext: Mir sweeps a bench clear with one arm. \"Watch!\" A kettle, a mantel clock and a spoon jump together into a whirring thing that pours tea, mostly into the cup. \"No plans, no drawings, just ask the bits what they want to be! It's my own. It probably won't explode. Want it?\"\n> left: Yes, show me how\n  learn_spell contraption\n  rel mir +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: Your first one is a teapot that walks and the second one sparks and singes your eyebrows. The third pours tea. Mir writes your name in a notebook, which you understand is the highest honour Mir has.\n> right: Help them clean up instead\n  alchemy +small\n  rel mir +tiny\n  say: You help sort the bits back into their drawers while Mir explains every one. \"Next time,\" Mir says, \"you're building. Probably.\"\n\n@event kael_teaches_hoarfrost\ntitle: Cold Hands\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: kael\nwhen: (bond(kael) == \"close\" or bond(kael) == \"partner\") and spell_level(hoarfrost) == 0\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 20\nart: spell_hoarfrost\ntags: social magic\nscene: A frosty courtyard at night, a quiet student holding a hand over a brazier as rime creeps across its coals and the fire dies down.\ntext: Kael holds his hand over a brazier in the courtyard, and the coals grey over with frost until the fire gives up. \"Where I come from, everyone learns this before they learn to read,\" he says. A pause. \"It's for putting fires out. Mostly. I could teach you, if you wanted it.\"\n> left: \"I want it.\"\n  learn_spell hoarfrost\n  rel kael +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: He seems startled that you said it out loud. He takes your hands to show you, and his are as cold as ever. By the end your fingers ache and the brazier is frosted grey. \"Good,\" he says, and doesn't let go at once.\n> right: Warm his hands instead\n  mood +small\n  rel kael +tiny\n  say: You take his hands and hold them over the last of the fire. He doesn't say anything for a long time. \"Another night,\" he says, eventually, about the spell or the hands.\n\n@event nessa_teaches_candour\ntitle: Off the Record\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\ntrigger: social\nactor: nessa\nwhen: (bond(nessa) == \"close\" or bond(nessa) == \"partner\") and spell_level(candour) == 0\nrepeat: 4\nweight: 20\nart: spell_candour\ntags: social magic\nscene: A newspaper office after midnight, an editor setting down her quill which goes still, two students talking quietly over cold tea.\ntext: Nessa puts her quill down, says \"off the record\", and it goes still. \"Here's the real trick of interviews,\" she says. \"Ask the question they want to answer. And then this.\" She says a word, and you find yourself telling her the truth about your exams. \"That's Candour. Want it?\"\n> left: Learn it from her\n  learn_spell candour\n  rel nessa +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: She practises on you until you've told her three embarrassing things, then lets you practise on her. She tells you one true thing, quietly, and asks you never to print it. You won't.\n> right: Ask it on the record\n  charm +small\n  rel nessa +tiny\n  say: She laughs, picks up the quill and interviews you about why you won't learn it. It runs on page four. \"Another night,\" she says. \"Off the record.\"\n"},{"path":"content/events/spell_uses.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Everyday uses of the basic curriculum spells (2026-10-25): three routine cards for each of the six first-year\n#  course spells and Ward, at places across the campus. Every card resolves without the spell; once you know it,\n#  its `up[spell]` option opens, the card is drawn more often (its pool's weight), and now and then a visit to its\n#  place opens on it (its spell's `@appointment`, rolled only at a place that has one of its cards).\n#  Wiring (docs/grimoire-script.md §9): `spell_known(s)` in conds.grim; `spell_use` is what every card here shares;\n#  each spell's pool comes from it and adds the weight; each spell's appointment deals from its pool.\n# ============================================================\n\n@pool spell_use\ndesc: What every everyday spell-use card shares.\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 5\ntags: magic\n\n# ---------------- Flame ----------------\n\n@pool flame_uses\nfrom: spell_use\nweight: 10 + spell_known(flame) * 15\n\n@appointment flame_in_use\ndesc: Once you know Flame, a visit now and then opens on one of its everyday uses.\nat: visit\nwhen: spell_known(flame)\nchance: unlikely\ndeals: pool flame_uses\n\n@event mabels_cold_range\ntitle: The Cold Range\npool: flame_uses\nplace: mabels\ntime: morning noon afternoon\ntone: light\nart: cup\nscene: A village tea shop on a cold day, its iron range out, a queue of students in scarves, a woman on her knees at the grate.\ntext: {No steam on Mabel's windows, and no smell of toast. The range behind the counter has gone out, and a queue of students in scarves is stamping its feet. \"No tea till it's lit, petal,\" says Mabel, rattling the grate.|The range is out again: the flue sulks in a north wind. Mabel looks at the queue, then at you, then at the coal bucket.|Mabel's range, cold again, and Mabel in front of it with a twist of paper. \"It knows you now,\" she says. \"Talk to it.\"}\n> left: Fetch coal from the yard\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You haul two heavy buckets in from the coal yard. When the kettle finally sings, Mabel pours yours first and waves your coins away.|Two buckets, one dropped on your foot. The range catches on the third try, and Mabel puts a scone by your cup, \"for the foot\".|You fetch the coal without being asked. Mabel tells the queue you're \"staff, near enough\", and makes you carry the milk.}\n> right: Read in the cold till it's lit\n  health -tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {You read a chapter in your coat while the queue grumbles. By the time the tea comes your fingers can't turn the page.|You read by the cold window. The chapter is good; the draught under the door is better at its job.|Coat on, book open, breath fogging. You learn a great deal and feel your toes rather less.}\n> up[flame]: Light it with a finger\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {You lean over the counter and touch the coals. The range roars, the queue cheers, and Mabel calls you \"a useful sort of petal\" for the rest of the term.|A flick, and the flue draws like a chimney in a storybook. Mabel pours your cup first and doesn't charge, \"for services to tea\".|{spell_level(flame) >= 2: You light the range and the lamps and the kettle's underside, one, two, three. Mabel applauds with the tea towel.|You light it before Mabel has finished asking. She gives you a look that's half thanks, half \"show-off\".}}\n\n@event boathouse_damp_stove\ntitle: The Damp Stove\npool: flame_uses\nplace: boathouse\ntime: evening night\ntone: light\nart: flame\nscene: A wooden boathouse at dusk, a rowing crew dripping and shivering around a cold iron stove, wet kindling steaming in a basket.\ntext: {A rowing crew comes in off the lake soaked to the skin, laughing through chattering teeth. The boathouse stove is cold, and the kindling basket is wet through.|The crew again, wetter than last time: someone caught a crab and took the boat over. The kindling has been left out in the rain, as usual.|Another soaking. The crew have started looking at you when they come in, and then at the stove.}\n> left: Run to the boatman's loft for dry wood\n  health -tiny\n  vigor +tiny\n  say: {You run up to the loft and back with an armful of dry wood. By the time the stove's going you're as wet as they are, from the sweat.|The loft ladder, in the dark, with an armful of wood. You make it. The crew make room for you on the bench.|The loft ladder is the worst part. The crew cheer you down it like a returning hero.}\n> right: Lend your scarf and leave them to it\n  health -tiny\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {You give your scarf to the one with blue lips and walk back up to the castle with a cold neck. You'll get the scarf back. Probably.|You hand round your scarf and your sympathy and leave them to it. The scarf comes back a week later, smelling of lake.|Scarf, a joke, and goodnight. They wave you off, still shivering, still laughing.}\n> up[flame]: Dry the kindling with a flame\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {You hold a flame under the wet kindling until it steams, then catches. The crew crowd the stove and somebody's lake-cold hand finds your shoulder in thanks.|A flame, coaxed small and patient, and the kindling catches in a fizz of steam. The crew name the stove after you. It's not a compliment, exactly.|You dry the whole basket in one long breath of fire. The crew offer you a seat in the boat next week. You decline, warmly.}\n\n@event secret_garden_late_frost\ntitle: A Hard Frost\npool: flame_uses\nplace: secret_garden\ntime: evening\ntone: warm\nwhen: season != \"summer\"\nart: flower\nscene: A hidden garden on a clear, freezing evening, the humming pear tree, a row of unlit clay pots and an old woman with a tinderbox.\ntext: {A hard frost is coming down out of a clear sky. Old Maud is going along the row of clay smudge pots under the humming pear with a tinderbox that won't strike. \"Every one, dearie,\" she says, \"or the old pear sulks for a month.\"|Frost again. The pear has gone quiet, which Maud says is how it shivers.|The smudge pots are set out before you arrive. Maud has left a second tinderbox on the wall, for you.}\n> left: Light the pots with a tinderbox\n  health -small\n  mood +small\n  say: {Forty pots, a tinderbox and numb fingers. The last one catches as the frost comes down, and the pear starts humming again, low and grateful.|You light pot after pot on your knees in the frost. Maud brings you something hot in a chipped cup and doesn't say what it is.|You light your half of the row before Maud lights hers, and she pretends to be cross about it.}\n> right: Wrap the low branches in sacking\n  vigor +tiny\n  health -tiny\n  say: {You climb into the lower branches and wrap what you can reach. It isn't all of it. Maud says some is a lot more than none.|Sacking, string and a ladder that leans the wrong way. You save the low branches; the top will have to take its chances.|You wrap the branches like presents. Maud says the pear will remember, and she says it as if pears do.}\n> up[flame]: Light every pot at once\n  mood +small\n  rel maud +small\n  say: {You hold out your hand and every pot in the row blooms at once, a line of small suns in the frost. Maud laughs, which you've never heard her do so loudly.|One breath, forty flames. The pear hums so loudly the bees come out to see. \"Well,\" says Maud. \"Aren't you handy.\"|{spell_level(flame) >= 3: You light the pots and hold the warmth over the whole tree like a hand. Not one bud browns. Maud says she'll name a pear after you, and does.|You light the row in two breaths. Maud says it took her forty years to learn to do it with a tinderbox, and she isn't sure which of you to be proud of.}}\n\n# ---------------- Foresight ----------------\n\n@pool foresight_uses\nfrom: spell_use\nweight: 10 + spell_known(foresight) * 15\n\n@appointment foresight_in_use\ndesc: Once you know Foresight, a visit now and then opens on one of its everyday uses.\nat: visit\nwhen: spell_known(foresight)\nchance: unlikely\ndeals: pool foresight_uses\n\n@event refectory_tipping_tray\ntitle: The Tipping Tray\npool: foresight_uses\nplace: refectory\ntime: noon evening\ntone: light\nart: meal\nscene: A crowded dining hall, a first-year carrying a towering tray of soup bowls between long benches, one bench leg sticking out into the aisle.\ntext: {A first-year is coming down the aisle with a tray of eight soup bowls stacked like a tower, and a bench leg is sticking out right where they'll step.|The same first-year, a bigger tray, a different bench leg. Some people never learn; some benches don't either.|The tray, the first-year, the bench. You can almost hear the crash before it happens.}\n> left: Lunge for the tray\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      say: {You catch the tray an inch from disaster with soup up to your wrists. The first-year looks at you as if you'd stopped the sun.|You get a hand under it and one bowl of eight goes over. The first-year counts it as a miracle.|Tray, bowls, first-year, all upright, and not a drop on your sleeve. The table nearest applauds with spoons.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You get there just in time to wear the soup. All eight bowls. It's leek.|You catch the first-year and miss the tray. The crash is heard in the kitchens; the cook comes out to see.|You lunge, the bench catches you too, and you both go down in a lake of soup.}\n> right: Move your own bowl out of range\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You lift your bowl to safety and watch it happen. The leg, the trip, the crash. Nobody blames you. You blame you, a little.|You move your bowl. The first-year doesn't move theirs. Somebody else catches the tray, and the first-year thanks them with shining eyes.|Out of range, and dry. The crash is spectacular. You help pick up the bowls afterwards, which is something.}\n> up[foresight]: Kick the bench leg in first\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  say: {You see it a heartbeat early, the leg and the step and the tray, and nudge the bench in with your heel. The first-year sails past and never knows.|A glimpse ahead and a small shove. The tray arrives whole. The first-year doesn't notice; you do, and it's lovely.|You move the bench before the first-year has even turned into the aisle. The cook, watching from the hatch, raises her ladle at you like a salute.}\n\n@event library_crumbs\ntitle: Crumbs\npool: foresight_uses\nplace: library\ntime: afternoon evening night\ntone: light\nart: book\nscene: A quiet library stack three floors up, a student hunched over notes with a paper bag of biscuits, a grey cat watching, a lamp moving somewhere among the shelves.\ntext: {You're three floors up in the library with a paper bag of ginger biscuits from the village, which is against every rule painted on the stairs. Somewhere in the stacks the head librarian is doing her rounds, and her rounds make no sound at all.|Biscuits again, and the head librarian again, somewhere in the shelves. Footnote has smelt the ginger and is watching you from the next desk with enormous patience.|The bag rustles. Two stacks away, a floorboard doesn't.}\n> left: Put the bag away and work hungry\n  health -tiny\n  lore +tiny\n  say: {You fold the bag into your satchel and work on an empty stomach. You get a great deal done, all of it about food.|The bag goes away. Your stomach complains loudly enough to echo down the stacks.|You work hungry. When the head librarian passes, she nods at your empty desk, as if she knows exactly what's in the satchel.}\n> right: Eat them and risk it\n  chance even:\n    mood +small\n    say: {You eat the whole bag a crumb at a time. Nobody comes. It's the best chapter you've ever read.|You and Footnote share the bag, one for you, one for the cat. The rounds pass two stacks away and keep going.|You eat them all and brush the crumbs into your sleeve. Nobody ever knows except the cat, who is not telling.}\n  else:\n    renown -tiny\n    mood -tiny\n    say: {The head librarian is behind you, holding out her hand for the bag. She takes it without a word. You hear her eating them, later, at the desk.|A crunch, and the lamp is suddenly at the end of your row. Your name goes in a book with a black cover.|Footnote knocks the bag off the desk at the worst possible moment. The head librarian arrives as the biscuits finish rolling.}\n> up[foresight]: See her coming\n  mood +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {You glimpse the lamp turning into your row a breath before it does, and when it arrives there is nothing on your desk but notes and a very innocent face.|A moment ahead, every time. The bag goes under the book, the book comes out of the bag, and the head librarian passes and never knows.|You see her coming, and seeing her coming, offer her a biscuit. She looks at you for a long time. Then she takes one.}\n\n@event gilded_newt_knucklebones\ntitle: Knucklebones\npool: foresight_uses\nplace: gilded_newt\ntime: evening night\ntone: light\nart: dice\nscene: A low-beamed village tavern, regulars round a table throwing knucklebones for copper stakes by the fire.\ntext: {At the fireside table the regulars are throwing knucklebones for coppers. There's a space on the bench, and a copper on the table with your name on it, if you want it.|Knucklebones again. The old carter who always wins is winning. He pats the bench beside him.|The regulars wave you over before you've shut the door. The landlady says the game's been dull without you, and charges you for it.}\n> left: Play it straight\n  chance even:\n    gold +small\n    mood +small\n    say: {The bones fall your way three throws running. The carter says it's beginner's luck, then that it's been going on too long for that.|You win the pot, a handful of coppers and a lot of goodwill. The landlady takes a cut for the fire.|You play badly and win anyway. The table says that's the worst kind of player.}\n  else:\n    gold -small\n    say: {The bones roll every way but yours. The carter buys you a cider out of your own losses, with great ceremony.|You lose, cheerfully and steadily. The regulars like you better for it.|Down a few coppers by the end. The carter says you've got the hands for it, just not the luck.}\n> right: Buy the round and just watch\n  gold -small\n  charm +small\n  say: {You buy the round and watch. By the end you know everyone's name and the carter's whole family tree.|A round for the table, and a seat by the fire with no stakes at all. It's the best evening you've had all month.|You buy the round. The landlady writes it on your tab, to the penny, and the table drinks your health.}\n> up[foresight]: See how the bones will fall\n  gold +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {You glimpse each throw before it leaves the hand and bet on what you've seen. You win, and win, and the carter starts to look at you with great interest.|A moment ahead every time. You win the pot and buy the next round with it, which helps your conscience a little.|You see the bones fall before they're thrown. You take the pot, and leave before the carter works out how, which he is getting close to.}\n\n# ---------------- Mend ----------------\n\n@pool mend_uses\nfrom: spell_use\nweight: 10 + spell_known(mend) * 15\n\n@appointment mend_in_use\ndesc: Once you know Mend, a visit now and then opens on one of its everyday uses.\nat: visit\nwhen: spell_known(mend)\nchance: unlikely\ndeals: pool mend_uses\n\n@event workshop_cracked_lens\ntitle: The Cracked Lens\npool: mend_uses\nplace: workshop\ntime: afternoon evening\ntone: light\nart: gear\nscene: A cluttered workshop, an artificer holding up a large glass lens with a crack running across it, a half-built machine waiting on the bench.\ntext: {Mir is holding their best lens up to the window, and there's a crack across it like a hair. \"Demonstration's tomorrow,\" they say. \"For the faculty. It's probably fine. It's not fine.\"|Another lens, another crack. Mir has started keeping the good ones in a sock.|Mir doesn't say anything; they just hold up the lens. The crack is worse than last time.}\n> left: Grind a spare by hand\n  health -tiny\n  alchemy +small\n  say: {You grind a spare lens with Mir until midnight. It's slightly wrong in a way only Mir can see, and they say it's the best wrong lens they've ever had.|Two hours of grit and polish. The new lens works. Mir writes your name on its rim in tiny letters.|Mir grinds one side, you the other. The lens is done by ten; Mir makes tea that tastes of solder.}\n> right: Tape it and hope\n  rel mir -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You tape the crack and tell Mir it'll hold. It holds for the demonstration and splits in half at the applause, which Mir calls \"dramatic timing\".|Tape, and a promise to come back. Mir says \"probably\" four times in a row, which is a lot even for Mir.|You tape it. Mir looks at the tape for a long time, then at you, then says it's fine in a voice that means it isn't.}\n> up[mend]: Mend it, seamless\n  rel mir +small\n  mood +small\n  say: {You close the crack with a breath of Mend, and the glass runs together like water. Mir holds it to the light and can't find where it was. \"Probably magic,\" they say, and hug you.|The lens mends clear as the day it was poured. Mir tests it on a moth, which is now the best-studied moth in the castle.|{spell_level(mend) >= 2: You mend the lens and the hairline crack in its brass ring. Mir makes you promise to come to the demonstration, \"in case\".|You mend it on the second try. Mir says the first try was \"very educational\", and keeps the slightly wobbly result for a different machine.}}\n\n@event lakeshore_torn_net\ntitle: The Torn Net\npool: mend_uses\nplace: lakeshore\ntime: morning noon afternoon\ntone: warm\nart: wave\nscene: An old fisher sitting on an upturned boat on a still lakeshore, a long net across their knees with a ragged hole torn through it.\ntext: {An old fisher is sitting on an upturned boat, mending a net with a hole in it the size of a door. \"The old pike,\" they say, not looking up. \"Bigger every year.\"|The fisher again, the net again, a different hole. \"Same pike,\" they say.|The fisher has kept a space on the boat for you, and the net has kept a hole.}\n> left: Help knot the net\n  health -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {You knot until your fingers go white in the cold. The fisher tells you about every fish they ever lost, and none they caught.|An hour of knots, badly, then less badly. The fisher gives you a fish wrapped in a dock leaf, still flapping.|The knot comes to your fingers more easily than you expected. The fisher says you'd have made a fisher, in a tone that means it's the highest thing they can say.}\n> right: Buy a fish and leave them to it\n  gold -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  say: {You buy the best of the morning's catch. The fisher wraps it in yesterday's Ledger and doesn't look up from the net.|A fish, a nod, a coin. You eat it grilled on the shore, and the net doesn't get any smaller.|You buy a fish. The fisher throws in a second, small and ugly, \"for luck\". It's the tastier of the two.}\n> up[mend]: Mend the whole net\n  mood +small\n  health +tiny\n  say: {You lay a hand on the net and the hole knits shut, knot by knot, from the edges in. The fisher stares at it, then at you, then gives you their lunch.|The net mends in one breath. The fisher says nothing for a long time, then tells you about the pike, which by the end of the story is the length of a rowing boat.|You mend the net and two old patches you hadn't noticed. The fisher says the pike will be furious, and grins with three teeth.}\n\n@event great_hall_cracked_sky\ntitle: A Crack in Tomorrow\npool: mend_uses\nplace: great_hall\ntime: noon afternoon evening\ntone: light\nart: rain\nscene: A vaulted hall whose ceiling shows tomorrow's weather, a thin crack across the painted sky, rain dripping through it onto the end of a long table.\ntext: {The great hall's ceiling shows tomorrow, and tomorrow, it seems, is wet. There's a crack across the sky above the end of the long table, and tomorrow's rain is dripping through it into somebody's soup.|The crack is back, over a different table. Tomorrow is sleet.|A crack again, and a first-year holding a bowl under it with the air of someone who's been there a while.}\n> left: Fetch buckets from the kitchens\n  health -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You fetch two buckets and a mop. By supper there are buckets all down the table, and the hall sounds like a clock shop.|You set out buckets under the drips. They ring all through supper in different notes, and somebody starts conducting.|Buckets, and the mop, and the first-year freed from duty. They follow you about for a week.}\n> right: Move to a dry bench and eat\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You move up the table and eat with tomorrow dripping steadily behind you. Someone else will fix it. Nobody does until Thursday.|You move. The first-year with the bowl watches you go with the look of a soldier left at the gate.|A dry bench, and supper. The drip keeps time with your spoon all evening, which is somehow worse.}\n> up[mend]: Mend the sky\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {You reach up with Mend and the crack in tomorrow knits shut, cloud to cloud. The drip stops mid-fall. Half the hall claps; the other half didn't notice, which is the better compliment.|The crack draws together from both ends and meets in the middle with a small click. Tomorrow's rain will have to wait for tomorrow, as it should.|You mend the sky over the long table. The first-year with the bowl pours it, solemnly, into a plant pot.}\n\n# ---------------- Glamour ----------------\n\n@pool glamour_uses\nfrom: spell_use\nweight: 10 + spell_known(glamour) * 15\n\n@appointment glamour_in_use\ndesc: Once you know Glamour, a visit now and then opens on one of its everyday uses.\nat: visit\nwhen: spell_known(glamour)\nchance: unlikely\ndeals: pool glamour_uses\n\n@event boathouse_good_boat\ntitle: The Good Boat\npool: glamour_uses\nplace: boathouse\ntime: afternoon evening\ntone: light\nart: wave\nscene: A boathouse jetty, a row of patched rowing boats and one sound blue one, a weathered boatman sitting on it with his arms folded.\ntext: {The boathouse has six boats that leak and one, the blue one, that doesn't. The boatman keeps it for himself and sits on it to make the point.|The blue boat, the boatman, the folded arms. You've started to think of them as one animal.|The boatman sees you coming and settles more firmly onto the blue boat. \"No,\" he says, before you've asked.}\n> left: Pay his price for an hour\n  gold -small\n  mood +small\n  say: {He takes your crowns, counts them twice and lets you have the blue boat for exactly an hour. It's the stillest hour you've spent all term.|An hour on the lake in a boat that doesn't sink. You row out to the middle and lie back and watch the towers upside down.|You pay. He watches you the whole hour, from the jetty, as if you'd borrowed his dog.}\n> right: Take the green one, bailing\n  health -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You take the green boat and a bailer. You row half the time and bail the other half. It's still the lake, and still lovely.|The green boat, which takes on water like a sponge. You come back with wet boots and a better opinion of yourself.|You bail all the way out and all the way back. The boatman says you've got a rower's arms now, and charges you nothing for the joke.}\n> up[glamour]: Charm him off the blue boat\n  mood +small\n  charm +tiny\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {You turn on a little more charm than you have, and he finds himself telling you about his late wife, and then lending you the blue boat, and then wondering why.|A smile with Glamour in it. The boatman gets up, holds the boat steady for you, and calls you \"young sir or madam\" like a courtier.|He remembers liking you and not quite why. You get the blue boat for the rest of the day and a cup of his terrible tea after.}\n\n@event courtyard_prefects_notebook\ntitle: The Prefect's Notebook\npool: glamour_uses\nplace: courtyard\ntime: morning noon afternoon evening\ntone: light\nart: paper\nscene: A sunny castle courtyard, a red-sashed prefect with a notebook open and pencil poised, a student standing on a forbidden patch of lawn.\ntext: {You have, it turns out, been walking on the lawn, which is for looking at. Bram Hollis has his notebook open and his pencil licked. \"Rule nine,\" he says. \"Demerit.\"|Bram again, and the notebook again, and the lawn again. \"Rule nine,\" he says, with real joy.|Bram's pencil is already moving when you look up. He has a page with your name at the top now.}\n> left: Argue the rule\n  challenge appeal vs bram.charm:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      say: {You point out that rule nine was repealed in a year when the lawn caught fire. Bram checks. It was. He closes the notebook with great dignity.|You argue that you were looking at the lawn, very closely. Bram can't find the rule against that. He'll have one by Thursday.|You quote rule nine back at him, with the bit he left out. Bram goes pink and lets you off \"this once, as a courtesy\".}\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {Bram reads rule nine out loud to the whole courtyard, twice, and writes your name in capitals.|You argue; Bram quotes rule ten, which is about arguing. Two demerits.|Bram listens to your whole case, then writes it down as a separate offence.}\n> right: Take the demerit and move on\n  renown -tiny\n  say: {You take it. Bram writes slowly, so everyone can see. \"Next time,\" he says, and means it kindly, in his way.|Demerit. You walk off the lawn with your hands in your pockets. A first-year on a bench gives you a sympathetic look.|You take the demerit with a bow. Bram doesn't know what to do with a bow, and writes that down too.}\n> up[glamour]: Remind him he likes you\n  mood +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel bram +tiny\n  say: {You say his name the way people say a friend's, with a little Glamour in it. Bram's pencil stops. He forgets, quite visibly, what he was writing.|A smile with a spell behind it. Bram closes the notebook, tells you to \"watch the edges\", and walks off frowning at himself.|Bram remembers liking you and not why. He lets you off, then follows you to the cloister to explain rule nine properly, which is its own punishment.}\n\n@event grimsbys_book_behind_counter\ntitle: The Book Behind the Counter\npool: glamour_uses\nplace: grimsbys_books\ntime: noon afternoon evening\ntone: warm\nart: book\nscene: A cramped bookshop, an elderly bookseller behind his counter with one wrapped book kept apart from the rest, a student leaning in hopefully.\ntext: {Behind Mr Grimsby's counter there's a book wrapped in brown paper, kept apart. \"For someone who'll need it,\" he says vaguely. \"Not you. Or not yet.\"|The wrapped book is still there. Mr Grimsby moves it a little further from you when you come in.|The brown-paper book has a name pencilled on it now, but he keeps his thumb over the name.}\n> left: Wait to be the someone\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You browse for an hour in plain sight, being the sort of person who'd need a book. Mr Grimsby sells you a history of lighthouses instead.|You come back twice in the week. On the second visit he lets you hold it, then takes it back. \"Soon,\" he says.|You don't ask. Mr Grimsby notices you not asking, and approves, and doesn't give you the book.}\n> right: Buy what he recommends instead\n  gold -tiny\n  lore +small\n  say: {He hands you something about tides that smells of seaweed. Chapter two is exactly what your essay needed, as usual, annoyingly.|You buy his recommendation without arguing. He looks almost disappointed you didn't fight for the other one.|A slim book on candle-making, for reasons he won't explain. You'll need it on Thursday.}\n> up[glamour]: Charm the parcel out of him\n  lore +small\n  mood +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {You smile at him with a little more charm than you own, and he finds himself handing you the parcel. \"Ah,\" he says, puzzled. \"It was you all along. Was it?\"|Glamour, and a compliment about his shelving. He unwraps the book for you himself. It's the one you came in for last month and couldn't find.|He beams, decides you've been a customer for years, and wraps the parcel again for you, more nicely, with string.}\n\n# ---------------- Haste ----------------\n\n@pool haste_uses\nfrom: spell_use\nweight: 10 + spell_known(haste) * 15\n\n@appointment haste_in_use\ndesc: Once you know Haste, a visit now and then opens on one of its everyday uses.\nat: visit\nwhen: spell_known(haste)\nchance: unlikely\ndeals: pool haste_uses\n\n@event lecture_tower_far_bell\ntitle: The Far Bell\npool: haste_uses\nplace: lecture_tower\nwhen: term\ntime: morning noon afternoon\ntone: light\nart: bell\nscene: A bridge of light between two towers, a student running across it with an armful of books while a bell rings on the far side.\ntext: {The bell for your lecture is ringing in the other tower, and you are on the wrong side of the bridge with all your books.|The bell again, and you on the wrong end of the bridge again. You're starting to suspect the bridge.|You can hear the bell before you see the bridge. Seven strokes; it's on the third.}\n> left: Run for it\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      say: {You slide into your seat on the last stroke with your books in a heap. The lecturer looks at the clock, then at you, then lets it go.|You make it, red-faced and triumphant. Your neighbour pushes a quill at you without a word.|You know the run now: left foot on the second plank, duck the lamp. In with a stroke to spare.}\n    lose:\n      study -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {You drop your books halfway across the bridge and arrive five minutes late with half of them.|You arrive as the door closes, and spend the hour listening through it. It's not the same.|You trip on the top step and the whole lecture turns to watch you pick up your inkwell.}\n> right: Arrive late, quietly\n  study -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You walk, unhurried, and slip in at the back ten minutes late, breathing easy. You miss the one thing that'll be on the exam.|You arrive late and sit on the end of the back row, where the notes from the front arrive late too.|Late, and calm, and behind for the rest of the hour. Someone lends you their notes afterwards, in a hand you can't read.}\n> up[haste]: Borrow a hare's legs\n  mood +small\n  say: {You cross the bridge in four strides with the wind whistling, and sit down before the bell has finished. Your books arrive a moment later, in your arms.|Haste, and the bridge goes by in a blur of light. You're in your seat, notes open, looking as if you've been there an hour.|{spell_level(haste) >= 2: You're early. You've time to sharpen a quill and help a first-year find the room.|You arrive breathless but on the stroke. The lecturer says \"Prompt,\" which from them is warm.}}\n\n@event courtyard_loose_pages\ntitle: Loose Pages\npool: haste_uses\nplace: courtyard\ntime: afternoon evening\ntone: light\nart: paper\nscene: A castle courtyard on a windy day, a first-year clutching an empty folder while the pages of an essay blow across the flagstones toward a fountain.\ntext: {A gust comes over the wall and takes a first-year's essay out of their hands, every page of it, and scatters it across the courtyard towards the fountain. They stand there holding the empty folder.|Wind, a folder, a first-year, and pages everywhere. The fountain looks hungry.|The pages are already in the air when you come through the arch. You know exactly where the fountain is.}\n> left: Chase them down\n  challenge feat apprentice wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      say: {You run them down one by one, off the flagstones, out of a hedge, one off a prefect's back. Every page, and only one of them damp.|You stamp on three, catch two and dive for the last at the fountain's rim. The first-year hugs the folder like a lost dog.|You read the wind and get to the fountain before the pages do, and catch them as they come.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You get most of them. The last three go into the fountain, and you go in after the last one, and it's a long cold walk to the dormitory.|You trip on the fountain step and the pages sail on without you. The first-year helps you up, which isn't how it was meant to go.|The wind wins. You bring back half an essay and a leaf.}\n> right: Lend them your own notes to start again\n  study -tiny\n  charm +small\n  say: {You sit with them on the cloister step while they work it up again from your notes. It comes out shorter and, honestly, better.|You lend them your notes and your afternoon. It takes all of it, and they tell their whole year you did.|You lend them your quill and your evening, and they hand it in on time.}\n> up[haste]: Gather every page before it lands\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {Haste, and the courtyard slows around you. You pluck every page out of the air before the gust has finished, and hand back the folder neatly stacked.|You go round the courtyard like a whirl of wind yourself, and come back with the essay in order. The first-year counts the pages twice, then stares at you.|{spell_level(haste) >= 2: You catch the pages and the first-year's hat, which they hadn't noticed had gone.|You get every page but one, and that one you snatch off the fountain's surface, barely wet.}}\n\n@event refectory_supper_hatch\ntitle: The Supper Hatch\npool: haste_uses\nplace: refectory\ntime: evening\ntone: light\nart: meal\nscene: A long dining hall at evening, the kitchen hatch at the far end with its shutter starting to come down, a student at the wrong end of the hall.\ntext: {The kitchen hatch shuts at the bell, and the bell is going, and you are at the wrong end of the longest hall in the castle.|The hatch is coming down again. The cook leaves it open an inch less for you every week, on principle.|You hear the hatch rattle before you're through the door. Stew night, too.}\n> left: Run the length of the hall\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      health +small\n      say: {You get a bowl under the hatch as it comes down. The cook gives you the last of the stew and a look of grudging respect.|You make it with a slide that would win prizes. The hatch stops for you. The cook looks almost impressed.|In under the shutter by a hand's breadth. The cook says, \"You again,\" and adds a dumpling, as a prize.}\n    lose:\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {The hatch closes in your face, gently, with finality. Bread and cheese it is.|You slip on the flagstones halfway and arrive on your back. The cook leans over the hatch to check you're alive, and closes it.|Too slow. You hear the stew being scraped into a bucket for the pigs, which is a sound that stays with you.}\n> right: Take bread and cheese from the board\n  mood -tiny\n  health +tiny\n  say: {You take bread and cheese from the board by the door and eat them on the steps. It's fine. It isn't stew.|Bread, cheese and an apple, eaten walking. You catch the smell of the stew all the way upstairs.|You take the board's bread and somebody's abandoned pudding. You're not proud. You're full.}\n> up[haste]: Cross the hall in a heartbeat\n  health +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: {You're at the hatch before the bell's first echo. The cook stares at the door, then at you, then ladles. \"Well,\" she says. \"Somebody's hungry.\"|Haste down the long aisle, between the benches, round a prefect, and your bowl is under the hatch with time to spare.|You arrive so fast you have to wait for the hatch to finish opening. The cook gives you seconds out of sheer surprise.}\n\n# ---------------- Shadowstep ----------------\n\n@pool shadowstep_uses\nfrom: spell_use\nweight: 10 + spell_known(shadowstep) * 15\n\n@appointment shadowstep_in_use\ndesc: Once you know Shadowstep, a visit now and then opens on one of its everyday uses.\nat: visit\nwhen: spell_known(shadowstep)\nchance: unlikely\ndeals: pool shadowstep_uses\n\n@event lecture_tower_volunteer\ntitle: A Volunteer\npool: shadowstep_uses\nplace: lecture_tower\nwhen: term\ntime: morning noon afternoon\ntone: light\nart: wand\nscene: A tiered lecture hall, a lecturer at the front with a crackling demonstration charm, scanning the back row for a volunteer.\ntext: {\"I need a volunteer,\" says the lecturer, holding up a charm that crackles, \"for a hex that is only mildly unpleasant.\" Their eyes are travelling along the back row towards you.|The volunteer again. The back row has learned to look at its shoes; the lecturer has learned to look at the back row.|\"Volunteer,\" says the lecturer, and you can feel the word coming for you like weather.}\n> left: Put your hand up\n  arcana +small\n  health -tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {You volunteer. The hex is exactly as mildly unpleasant as promised, and your hair stays upright until supper. You learn more than anyone else in the room.|You go down and take it on the chin, literally. The class applauds; your chin doesn't.|You volunteer before they ask. The lecturer, delighted, gives you the slightly worse hex, \"as a treat\".}\n> right: Sink into your seat\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You sink. The lecturer picks the student beside you, who gives you a look you'll be paying for until the end of term.|You become very interested in your notes. It works; the person in front is less lucky and less interested in theirs.|You sink until you're more desk than student. The lecturer says, \"I can see you,\" and picks someone else anyway.}\n> up[shadowstep]: Step into the pillar's shadow\n  mood +small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: {You step into the shadow of the pillar and out of the shadow by the door, and spend the demonstration watching from the corridor, very innocently.|A step into the dark under your desk and out under the window. When the lecturer's eyes reach your seat, your seat is empty, and they look puzzled for the rest of the hour.|You're gone before the word \"volunteer\" lands. From the doorway you watch someone else's hair stand on end, and feel only slightly guilty.}\n\n@event observatory_shutter_rope\ntitle: The Shutter Rope\npool: shadowstep_uses\nplace: observatory\ntime: evening night\ntone: light\nart: star\nscene: A brass observatory dome at dusk, its shutter still closed, a crank rope dangling out of reach up in the dark of the dome's ribs, students with star charts waiting below.\ntext: {The stars are coming out, and the dome is still shut. The crank rope has jumped its pulley and is hanging up in the dark of the ribs, well out of anyone's reach. A dozen students with star charts look up at it.|The shutter rope has jumped its pulley again. The caretaker says it does it on clear nights only, out of spite.|Up in the ribs of the dome, in the dark, the rope again. Somebody has chalked an arrow on the floor pointing at it.}\n> left: Climb the brass ribs\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      say: {You climb the ribs like a ladder and hook the rope with one foot. The shutter grinds open on a sky full of stars, and the room cheers.|Up the ribs, out along the curve, a grab. The rope comes free and the dome opens with a groan like a waking giant.|The handholds are just where you'd hope. Up, grab, down, and the sky is open before the charts are unrolled.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {Halfway up a rib turns slippery with dew and you come down a lot faster than you went up. The rope stays where it is.|You get a hand to the rope and the pulley bites your fingers. You let go of both.|You climb, reach, miss and hang there by your elbows while someone runs for the caretaker.}\n> right: Read the charts by lamplight instead\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {The caretaker comes up an hour later with a hook on a pole. By then you've learned the charts by heart, and the best of the sky has gone behind the hill.|You spread the charts on the floor and plot tonight's route across them instead. It isn't the same; it's not nothing.|You read by lamplight under the shut dome. When it finally opens, the moon is up and drowning half of what you came for.}\n> up[shadowstep]: Step into the dark under the dome\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {You step into the shadow by the door and out of the shadow in the ribs, grab the rope, and step back down. The dome opens. A dozen star charts lower slowly as their owners stare at you.|Shadow to shadow, up into the dark of the dome and back. The rope slots onto its pulley with a clunk. Someone asks if you can do that for the bell tower.|You don't even put your charts down. A step into the dark, a tug, and the shutter rolls back on the whole sky.}\n\n@event infirmary_slip_out\ntitle: One More Night\npool: shadowstep_uses\nplace: infirmary\ntime: evening night\ntone: light\nart: bed\nscene: An infirmary ward at night, a student sitting up in a white bed fully dressed, a healer's lamp moving between beds at the far end.\ntext: {Sister Amaranth wants you in for \"one more night, for observation\". You feel completely fine and have three things due tomorrow.|One more night again. Sister Amaranth has started leaving your boots on the far side of the ward.|The bed by the window has your name on the slate in ink. \"One more night,\" says Sister Amaranth, not as a question.}\n> left: Stay the night\n  health +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: {You stay. You sleep through two bells and the breakfast trolley and wake up much better than \"completely fine\" turned out to be. Your three things are now late.|You stay. She was right, irritatingly; you sleep like a dropped stone. Your essay, unwritten, waits.|You stay without arguing. Sister Amaranth notices, and gives you the good tonic, and a look that might be respect.}\n> right: Argue your case\n  challenge appeal vs amaranth.charm +1:\n    win:\n      say: {You make a short, true, convincing case. She looks at you over her spectacles. \"Go,\" she says. \"Come back if it swells. Don't come back if it doesn't.\"|You tell her the true version of how fine you are, with the limp left in. She respects that and lets you go.|She lets you go with a tonic in your pocket and instructions you'll follow later, when you understand them.}\n    lose:\n      health +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: {She listens to every word, then points at the bed. You lie down. You don't remember falling asleep.|\"No,\" says Sister Amaranth, which was always going to be the answer, and she says it while doing something else.|She lets you argue to the end, then writes \"argued\" on your slate, under your name.}\n> up[shadowstep]: Step out through the dark\n  study +tiny\n  rel amaranth -tiny\n  say: {When her lamp turns, you step into the shadow behind the screen and out of the shadow by the door. In the morning there's a note on your desk in square handwriting. It says \"Next time, ask.\"|You step out of the ward and into your own room's corner in one breath. You get your three things done. Sister Amaranth, of course, knows.|Shadow to shadow, past the beds, out. Your essay is finished by midnight. At breakfast Sister Amaranth puts a tonic by your plate without a word.}\n\n# ---------------- Ward ----------------\n\n@pool ward_uses\nfrom: spell_use\nweight: 10 + spell_known(ward) * 15\n\n@appointment ward_in_use\ndesc: Once you know Ward, a visit now and then opens on one of its everyday uses.\nat: visit\nwhen: spell_known(ward)\nchance: unlikely\ndeals: pool ward_uses\n\n@event alchemy_lab_do_not_drop\ntitle: Do Not Drop\npool: ward_uses\nplace: alchemy_lab\ntime: morning afternoon evening\ntone: tense\nart: bottle\nscene: A laboratory with tall shelves, a first-year on a ladder reaching for a bottle labelled DO NOT DROP, the bottle tilting off their fingertips over a crowded bench.\ntext: {A first-year at the top of the shelf ladder is reaching for a bottle labelled, in large letters, DO NOT DROP. It's sliding off their fingertips, over a bench crowded with people.|The same shelf, a different first-year, the same bottle. Someone has added AGAIN to the label.|The bottle is tilting before you've put your bag down. You'd swear it waits for you.}\n> left: Dive and catch it\n  challenge feat apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      alchemy +small\n      say: {You catch it an inch above the bench. Everyone breathes out at once. The first-year comes down the ladder very carefully and shakes your hand for a long time.|Caught, with both hands, against your chest. You put it back on the shelf yourself, at the bottom.|You catch it one-handed without looking, which surprises you more than anyone. Somebody starts a sweepstake on next time.}\n    lose:\n      health -tiny\n      mood -tiny\n      say: {You touch it and it skips off your fingers and bursts on the floor. Everyone's eyebrows go blue, yours most.|You get a hand to it and slow it down. It still breaks, but only on your boots, which smoke politely for an hour.|You catch the first-year instead of the bottle. Both of you are blue until Thursday.}\n> right: Shout everyone back from the bench\n  charm +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {\"Back!\" Everyone backs. The bottle hits the bench and the cloud goes up blue and harmless and smelling of pear drops. The bench is ruined; nobody is.|You clear the bench in a word. The bottle breaks; the first-year cries; the lecturer says it was the right call, in a voice that says it was an expensive one.|You shout before it's fallen. The bench clears; the bottle, perversely, lands upright.}\n> up[ward]: Throw a ward over the bench\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {The bottle bursts on a pale shell an arm's length above everyone's heads. The cloud rolls off the ward like rain off a window. The first-year, still on the ladder, starts to clap.|A ward, flat over the bench like a tabletop. The bottle breaks on it and the cloud slides off into the sink. The lecturer writes your name down, in the good column.|{spell_level(ward) >= 2: You ward the bench and the first-year at once. Nothing touches anyone. Somebody asks you to teach them; you do, after.|You ward the bench, a little late; the cloud catches your sleeve. Your sleeve is blue. Nobody else is.}}\n\n@event courtyard_hail\ntitle: Hail in the Courtyard\npool: ward_uses\nplace: courtyard\ntime: morning noon afternoon evening\ntone: light\nart: snow\nscene: A castle courtyard in a sudden hailstorm, first-years caught in the open with their arms over their heads, white stones bouncing off the flagstones.\ntext: {Hail comes out of a clear sky, stones the size of peas, then the size of marbles. A knot of first-years is caught in the middle of the courtyard with their books over their heads.|Hail again, the courtyard's speciality. The first-years know to run now; they just don't know where.|The sky goes white and the hail comes down like a dropped tray of beads. {season == \"summer\": In summer, which is insulting.|The fountain rings with it.}}\n> left: Herd them into the cloister\n  health -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {You wave them into the cloister and take the last of the hail on your own back. One of them gives you a sugar mouse from their pocket, warm and slightly squashed.|You get them all in and count them twice. Your ears ring for an hour from the stones.|You herd them like ducks into the cloister, the stones bouncing off your shoulders. One of them says thank you in a very small voice.}\n> right: Run for cover yourself\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {You make the cloister in six strides and watch them work it out for themselves. They do, eventually, damply.|You run. A prefect gets them in. You feel you were quicker than you should have been.|Cover first. From the cloister you shout directions, which helps a little and makes you feel better a lot.}\n> up[ward]: Raise a ward over them\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {You throw up a ward like a roof and the hail bounces off it in a white roar. The first-years stand under it, dry, looking up at you as if you'd hung the sky.|A pale shell over the knot of them. The hail drums on it like rain on a tent. Somebody under it starts to sing.|You walk them across the courtyard under a ward, slowly, like a procession. Half the castle is watching from the windows. You don't hurry.}\n\n@event lakeshore_swan\ntitle: The Swan\npool: ward_uses\nplace: lakeshore\ntime: morning noon afternoon evening\ntone: tense\nart: feather\nscene: A reedy lakeshore, a huge white swan with wings raised and neck lowered, hissing at a first-year who has wandered too close to its nest.\ntext: {A first-year has wandered into the reeds too close to the swans' nest. The cob is coming out of the water with his wings up, hissing like a kettle.|The swans are nesting again. The cob has grown, or your memory of him has, and there's a first-year in the reeds.|The cob sees you from across the lake and starts hissing in advance.}\n> left: Step between them\n  alignment +tiny\n  challenge soothe using vigor apprentice+ wild:\n    win:\n      vigor +small\n      say: {You step between them with your arms out wide and hiss back. The cob considers this, and decides you are the bigger swan. The first-year runs.|You stand your ground, slowly, talking nonsense in a low voice. He lowers his wings an inch, then another, then goes back to the nest.|He knows you by now, and you know him. You both posture, politely, until the first-year is clear.}\n    lose:\n      health -small\n      say: {He hits you with a wing like a flung door. The first-year gets away; you get a bruise the size of a plate.|You step in and the cob takes it personally. You retreat across the reeds at speed, honked at.|You take one wing and then a bite on the ankle. The first-year helps you limp home, which seems backwards.}\n> right: Shout for them to run\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {\"Run!\" The first-year runs, the cob chases them twenty yards, and everyone ends up in the lake except you.|You shout, they run, the cob gets a mouthful of their scarf. They don't get the scarf back.|You shout from a safe distance. It works, mostly. The first-year limps into the infirmary with a story they'll tell for years.}\n> up[ward]: Put a ward between them\n  mood +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: {A pale shell springs up between the cob and the first-year. He beats his wings on it twice, offended, then sails back to his nest with enormous dignity.|You ward the first-year and walk them out of the reeds inside it. The cob hisses at the ward all the way, and the ward doesn't mind.|A ward, and a slow walk, and the swans left in peace. The cob watches you go, and you'd swear he nods.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/spotlight_famous.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The spotlight, Famous (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md, batch P4-07; design §8.2): renown 60 or more, when the\n#  academy watches what you do. The pools and appointments are in spotlight.grim. A bold choice here pays big, and\n#  carries a scandal whose odds grow with renown (`chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85`: about 15%,\n#  25%, 37%, the design's (renown − 50)%).\n# ============================================================\n\n@event spot_endorsement\ntitle: Your Face on a Candle\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: light\nart: candle\nscene: A village chandler's shop window full of candles, each with a little painted label of a student's face, the chandler beaming in the doorway.\ntext: The village chandler wants to sell \"{player}'s study candles: they burn all night, like genius\". Your face on every label, a crown or two from every box. He's already had the labels painted. They look almost like you.\n> left: Lend your name\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85:\n    renown -medium\n    gold +small\n    mood -small\n    say: The candles burn all night, as promised, and give off a smell like a wet dog. Half the dormitory sleeps with the windows open. Someone writes \"GENIUS\" under your painted face, with an arrow, in soot.\n  else:\n    gold +medium\n    renown +small\n    queue scandal_candle_smoke within 3..6 chance unlikely\n    say: The candles sell out twice. They're perfectly ordinary candles, but people study by them with great seriousness, and the chandler sends a box up to you every month, with your own face looking back from the label.\n> right: Turn him down\n  mood +tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You tell him, kindly, that your face isn't for sale. He sighs and paints over the labels. The next week they say \"a famous student's study candles\", and sell almost as well.\n\n@event spot_memoir\ntitle: As Told To\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: light\nart: book\nscene: A smart stranger in a travelling coat at a refectory table, a contract and a quill laid out in front of a student.\ntext: A man from a city publisher has come all the way up the hill to offer you a sum for your memoir. You won't have to write a word: \"Our writer does all that. You just tell her a few stories, and sign.\" He has a draft title already. It has an exclamation mark.\n> left: Sign\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85:\n    gold +medium\n    renown -medium\n    say: The first chapter is printed in the Ledger as a teaser. In it you wrestle a lake serpent, win a duel blindfold and kiss three people on one page. You have done none of these things. The whole castle reads it aloud at breakfast, in voices.\n  else:\n    gold +medium\n    renown +small\n    queue scandal_memoir_book within 2..5 chance even\n    say: The book is short and breathless and only slightly made up. It sells in the city, and copies come back up the hill, where first-years read it under the desks. You sign one, once, and then hide.\n> right: Decline\n  study +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You tell him you're still in the middle of it, and you'd like to find out how it ends first. He laughs, leaves his card, and takes the long way down the hill.\n\n@event spot_impostor\ntitle: The Other {player}\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: tense\nart: mask\nscene: A village bakery counter, a slip of paper pinned up reading \"on account\", a hooded figure in academy-coloured scarf ducking out of the back door.\ntext: The baker asks, very politely, when you're going to settle your account. You haven't got one. It turns out somebody in an academy scarf has been eating very well in the village for a month and signing your name, with a flourish, at the bakery, the tearoom and the Newt.\n> left: Catch them in the act\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85:\n    renown -medium\n    alignment -tiny\n    say: You catch the impostor at the bakery on market day, in front of a crowd. The impostor is a thin first-year who hadn't eaten properly in weeks. The crowd looks at you as if you'd kicked a puppy. You pay the account. It doesn't help much.\n  else:\n    renown +small\n    gold +tiny\n    say: You catch the impostor in the act at the Newt: a grinning fourth-year who's done it for a bet. The landlady makes them pay every crown back, and you get a free supper and a story that does the rounds for weeks.\n> right: Pay the bills, and leave a note\n  gold -small\n  alignment +small\n  say: You pay what's owed and leave a note with the baker: \"If they come back, feed them, and tell them to come and see me.\" A week later, a thin first-year turns up at your door, and you find them a job at the Newt.\n\n@event spot_fan_circle\ntitle: The {player} Society\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: light\nart: group\nscene: A disused classroom with a hand-painted banner, a dozen younger students in matching ribbons sitting in a circle, a portrait of one student propped on a chair.\ntext: There is a society. It meets on Tuesdays in the disused classroom at the top of the lecture tower. It has a banner, ribbons, minutes and a portrait of you, done in crayon. Its president has written asking whether you would honour them with a visit.\n> left: Visit, and make a fuss of them\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85:\n    renown -medium\n    mood -tiny\n    say: The society is thrilled, and sets out to honour you properly. The next morning your name is written across the observatory dome in luminous paint, misspelled. The headmistress asks to see you. So does the observatory.\n  else:\n    renown +medium\n    mood +small\n    queue scandal_society_dare within 2..5 chance unlikely\n    say: You visit, sign the minutes, admire the portrait, and answer their questions for an hour. The society is beside itself. You have, from then on, a dozen first-years who would walk into the lake for you, and who very kindly don't.\n> right: Ask them to do something useful\n  renown -tiny\n  alignment +small\n  say: You visit, and suggest that a society in your name might run a study table for first-years who are struggling. They look crestfallen, and then very determined. By spring, the study table is the best thing about Tuesdays.\n\n@event spot_thornwick_challenge\ntitle: A Challenge From Across the Hills\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: tense\nart: duel\nscene: A pinned notice on the courtyard board with a red Thornwick seal, a crowd of students reading it and turning to look at one student.\ntext: A Thornwick student has sent a challenge, very publicly, by way of a notice in a red seal on the courtyard board: \"We hear Lumenhold's famous {player} is the best you've got. A contest of spells at the boundary stones, at the month's end. Bring a crowd.\" Half the castle has already promised to come.\n> left: Accept\n  challenge spell adept:\n    win:\n      renown +large\n      mood +small\n      queue scandal_thornwick_cheat within 1..3 chance unlikely\n      say: The contest at the boundary stones lasts four minutes. You win it on the last spell, with the whole of Lumenhold roaring behind you. The Thornwick student shakes your hand, says \"next year\", and means it.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      mood -small\n      say: You lose, fairly and very publicly, to a Thornwick student who is simply better on the day. The walk back up the hill is long and quiet. Thornwick's paper prints the result in very large letters.\n> right: Answer with a letter\n  renown -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You pin a reply under the challenge: \"Lumenhold doesn't perform on request. Do come for the Spring Tournament.\" The castle is a little disappointed, and a little proud. Thornwick's reply, if there is one, never arrives.\n\n@event spot_settle_dispute\ntitle: Your Word on It\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: tense\nart: scales\nscene: Two groups of students in different club colours glaring at each other across a courtyard, one student standing between them with a hand raised.\ntext: The Duelling Circle and the Skyrace Squad have both booked the flight field for the same Saturday, and it has come close to blows. Somebody suggests they let you decide, since everybody listens to you. Both captains agree. Both are glaring at you, and both are certain you'll pick them.\n> left: Decide it, there and then\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85:\n    renown -small\n    mood -small\n    say: You give the field to the Squad, who had booked first. By supper the Circle is telling everyone you were bought, and someone remembers you once had a drink with the Squad's captain. It's nonsense. It sticks for a month.\n  else:\n    renown +medium\n    say: You give the field to the Squad, who had booked first, and the Circle the Sunday, with the good side of the hall for practice. Both captains grumble, and both shake on it. People start bringing you their arguments.\n> right: Make them settle it themselves\n  challenge appeal adept-:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      alignment +tiny\n      say: You sit both captains down with a pot of tea and refuse to leave until they've worked it out. It takes three hours and the whole pot. They share the field, half the day each, and claim it was their idea.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      say: You sit them down, and they argue for three hours, and in the end neither gets the field, because the groundskeeper has booked it for mowing. Both clubs blame you, slightly, forever.\n\n@event spot_charity_auction\ntitle: An Afternoon With {player}\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: warm\nart: coin\nscene: The Great Hall set out for an auction, a student on a stage holding up a card, a forest of raised hands, an auctioneer with a gavel.\ntext: The infirmary fund is holding an auction, and the top lot, the committee tells you, has to be \"an afternoon with {player}\": tea, a walk, whatever the winner likes. They think it will raise more than everything else put together. They are probably right.\n> left: Agree to be auctioned\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85:\n    alignment +small\n    renown -small\n    mood -small\n    say: It raises a fortune. The winner turns out to be the Ledger's worst gossip, who spends the afternoon asking about your love life and prints every word. The infirmary gets new beds. You get a column you'd rather not have read.\n  else:\n    alignment +small\n    renown +small\n    mood +small\n    say: It raises a fortune. The winner is an old bookbinder from the village, who has saved for a year and only wants to walk round the lake with somebody famous and tell them about his garden. It's one of the best afternoons you have.\n> right: Give the fund a donation instead\n  gold -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You give the committee what you can, and ask them to auction something more useful than you. They auction the headmistress's old hat, which raises almost as much and is far less trouble.\n\n@event spot_newt_drink\ntitle: Whatever You're Having\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: light\nart: cup\nscene: A tavern bar, a landlady leaning over the counter with a dusty bottle of pale fizz, the whole room turned to watch one student order.\ntext: Whatever you order at the Gilded Newt, half the bar orders next, and the landlady has noticed. She leans over the counter. \"Do me a favour, love. Order the elderflower fizz. I've forty bottles of it in the cellar and nobody's touched one since midsummer. There's a cut in it for you.\" The whole bar is waiting to see what you'll have.\n> left: \"One elderflower fizz, please.\"\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85:\n    renown -small\n    gold +tiny\n    mood -small\n    say: The fizz has been in the cellar since midsummer for a reason. Half the bar orders one after you, and half the bar spends the rest of the evening outside. The landlady gives you your cut anyway. By morning everyone knows whose idea it was.\n  else:\n    gold +small\n    renown +small\n    say: You raise a glass of fizz to the bar, and the bar raises forty back. It is far too sweet. For the rest of the term it's what people drink at the Newt, and every few weeks the landlady presses a small purse of coppers into your hand.\n> right: Order what you always have\n  renown -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: You order your usual. So does half the bar. The landlady sighs, says \"Traitor\" quite fondly, and goes down to the cellar for more of it. The fizz stays where it is.\n\n@event spot_lecture_crowds\ntitle: Standing Room Only\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\nscene: A lecture hall crammed with students sitting in the aisles and on the windowsills, a lecturer at the front glaring over spectacles at one student in the third row.\ntext: Your lectures have filled up. Not with people who take the course: with people who want to sit near you. They sit in the aisles. They sit on the windowsills. After class, the lecturer asks you to stay behind, and says, without warmth, \"This is a lecture, not a menagerie. Do something about it.\"\n> left: Hold a question time after class\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85:\n    renown -small\n    study -small\n    say: You offer a half-hour of questions after class, for anyone. The first question is about your love life. The second is about your hair. The lecturer comes back for a book, sees the circus, and gives you an essay to write on decorum.\n  else:\n    renown +small\n    study +small\n    say: You offer a half-hour of questions after class, and to your surprise most of them are about the lecture. By the end of the month you know the course better than ever, and the aisles are clear: people come to your half-hour instead.\n> right: Sit at the back, in a hood\n  study +small\n  renown -small\n  say: You sit at the back with your hood up, take careful notes and leave by the side door. The crowds drift away to look at something else. The lecturer gives you, at the end of term, the very smallest nod.\n\n@event spot_luck_sleeve\ntitle: For Luck\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: light\nwhen: month == 3 or month == 4 or month == 9 or month == 10\nart: hand\nscene: A corridor outside an exam hall, a nervous queue of students each reaching out to touch one student's sleeve as they pass.\ntext: Somebody has decided that touching your sleeve before an exam brings luck. The queue outside the exam hall reaches out as you pass, one after another, very solemnly. A first-year asks if you'd mind breathing on their quill.\n> left: Bless them all, grandly\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85:\n    renown -small\n    alignment -tiny\n    say: You bless every quill and sleeve with great ceremony. When the results come out, a first-year who failed writes to the Ledger to say that your luck \"is a fraud, and so are you\". The letter is printed. Several people agree with it loudly.\n  else:\n    renown +small\n    mood +small\n    say: You bless every quill with great ceremony, and the queue goes in laughing, which is better for them than any luck. Afterwards three of them find you to say they passed. You don't point out that most people do.\n> right: Tell them to trust their work\n  alignment +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You tell the queue, kindly, that your sleeve has never passed an exam, and that they've done the work. Some of them look let down. One of them, a first-year with very cold hands, says thank you and goes in steadier.\n\n@event spot_routine_known\ntitle: Where to Find {player}\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: tense\nart: paper\nscene: A gossip sheet pinned to a board with a timetable printed on it, a crowd gathering on the library steps, a student watching from a corner with books held to their chest.\ntext: Someone has printed your whole week in a gossip sheet: which mornings you're in the library, where you sit for lunch, which way you walk back. By Tuesday there's a crowd on the library steps at nine o'clock, waiting, and the librarians are not pleased.\n> left: Hold court on the steps\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85:\n    study -medium\n    renown -small\n    say: You stop on the steps and chat, and the crowd grows, and grows, until it blocks the library door. The librarians ban you, very politely, for a week. The crowd moves on to the Newt. You fall behind on everything.\n  else:\n    renown +medium\n    study -small\n    say: You stop on the steps each morning for ten minutes, answer whatever people ask, and then go inside. The crowd shrinks to a friendly handful who bring you tea. The librarians decide they can live with it.\n> right: Change everything\n  study +tiny\n  mood -small\n  renown -small\n  say: You change your mornings, your lunch table, your way back. It takes a week to get it right and you keep getting lost. But you find a window seat in a disused stair where nobody comes, and you work better there than you have all year.\n\n@event spot_guest_flyer\ntitle: A Guest Lap\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: tense\nart: broom\nscene: A flight field ringed by a packed crowd, banners snapping, one student on a borrowed broom at the starting line, the lake glittering beyond.\ntext: The Skyrace Squad wants you to fly a guest lap at the season's opening race: just once round the course, before the real race starts, for the crowd. \"It'll double the gate,\" the captain says. \"Nobody's going to watch you race. They'll just watch you fly.\" The course goes out over the lake.\n> left: Fly it\n  challenge feat adept-:\n    win:\n      renown +large\n      mood +small\n      say: You fly the lap clean and a little faster than you should, and the crowd stands up to roar as you come round the last marker. The Squad captain is grinning. For the rest of the season, small children run about the field with brooms, being you.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      health -small\n      say: You fly it beautifully, right up to the last marker over the lake, where you misjudge the wind and go into the water in front of the whole academy. The crowd's gasp is louder than any cheer. Your own laughter, when you surface, saves a little of it.\n> right: Present the trophy instead\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You stay on the ground and present the trophy at the end, which takes a speech and no risk at all. The crowd is perfectly happy. The captain looks at your boots, and then the sky, and doesn't say anything.\n\n@event spot_petition\ntitle: Your Name at the Top\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: light\nart: paper\nscene: A student holding out a long scroll petition with dozens of signatures, a quill in the other hand, the top line left blank.\ntext: A petition is going round to keep the library open all night in exam weeks. It has a hundred and twelve names and a blank line at the top, which the second-year carrying it would like you to fill. \"If your name's on it, they'll have to listen.\" The scroll goes on for quite a long way below the main request.\n> left: Sign at the top\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85:\n    renown -small\n    mood -small\n    say: The headmistress reads it out at supper, all of it, including clause nine (puddings twice a day) and clause twelve (the abolition of Thursdays), \"proposed by {player}\". The hall laughs for a full minute. The library stays shut.\n  else:\n    renown +small\n    study +small\n    say: The library stays open all night in exam weeks. The petition is framed and hung by the door, with your name at the top, and in the small hours there are students at every desk, and someone always brings you a cup of tea.\n> right: Read it all first\n  study -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You read it all, which takes a while. Clause nine asks for puddings twice a day. Clause twelve abolishes Thursdays. You hand it back with a note on how to make it shorter. The second-year looks very let down, and then, the next week, comes back with one clause, which you sign.\n\n@event spot_party_host\ntitle: The Party, If You Come\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: tense\nart: music\nscene: A student at a dormitory door holding out a handwritten invitation, behind them a room strung with lanterns and stacked with crates.\ntext: A second-year is throwing a party in the old music room on Saturday, and says frankly that it'll either be the party of the term or a sad evening with twelve people and a fiddle, depending entirely on whether you come. Everybody knows already that you've been asked. Everybody is waiting to see.\n> left: Go\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 75 +1 if renown >= 85:\n    renown -small\n    health -small\n    mood -tiny\n    say: Everyone comes. Everyone. At one o'clock the prefects come too, and the report that goes to the headmistress names one guest by name, at the top. You spend a week of evenings in detention polishing the trophy cabinet, and the castle talks about it for a month.\n  else:\n    renown +small\n    mood +medium\n    health -small\n    say: It's the party of the term. You dance badly, talk to everyone, and leave before it gets out of hand. On Monday a dozen people you'd never met say hello to you in the corridors, and the second-year is a legend.\n> right: Stay in, and wish them well\n  study +small\n  mood -tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You send a note saying you can't, and a cake. The party is small and very nice, and the twelve people and the fiddle have a lovely time. The second-year sends back a slice.\n\n@event spot_ball_opening\ntitle: The First Dance\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: warm\nwhen: month == 4\nart: music\nscene: The Great Hall turned to ice and starlight, an empty dance floor ringed by the whole school, one couple stepping out alone into the middle.\ntext: The Midwinter Ball committee has asked you to open the Ball: the first dance, alone on the floor, in front of the whole school, before anybody else may step out. {bonds(partner) > 0: Your partner has gone very pale and is pretending not to have heard.|You'd need to choose someone, and everyone knows it.}\n> left: Open the Ball\n  challenge perform adept-:\n    win:\n      renown +large\n      mood +small\n      say: The music starts and, for once, your feet know what to do. You turn once round the whole floor under the floating candles, and when the music ends, the hall applauds before it remembers to dance. People talk about it till spring.\n    lose:\n      renown -medium\n      mood -small\n      say: You start well. Then the ice-floor, the candles and the whole school watching all happen at once, and you go down in a heap at the exact centre of the hall. You get up and finish the dance. The hall cheers more for that than it would have for the steps.\n> right: Let someone else open it\n  renown -small\n  mood +small\n  say: You thank the committee and suggest the two first-years who've been practising for weeks in the boathouse. They open the Ball, nervously and beautifully. You dance later, in the crowd, where nobody's watching, and enjoy every minute.\n\n@event spot_stable_name\ntitle: A Name for the Foal\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: light\nart: paw\nscene: A stable stall with a gangly winged foal tottering on long legs, a professor in a leather apron holding out a slate and chalk to a student.\ntext: A winged foal was born at the Familiar Stables last night, and Professor Brask, who claims to have no opinion on the matter, says the first-years will only stop arguing about its name if you choose it. She hands you a slate. The foal looks at you and sneezes.\n> left: Give it a grand name\n  renown +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You chalk up something grand and ancient and very hard to spell. The first-years approve. The foal grows into it slowly, and in the meantime everybody calls it something else, behind Brask's back, that you suspect is ruder.\n> right: Let the first-years vote\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You hand the slate back and say the first-years should vote. They vote for \"Biscuit\". Brask writes it in the stable book with the face of a woman enduring something, and the foal, as it turns out, loves it.\n\n@event spot_friend_strain\ntitle: Just {player}'s Friend\npool: spotlight_famous\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  f: any where classmate_here(f) and rel(f) >= 40\nper: f\nart: heart\nscene: Two students sitting on a cold stone step outside the castle at dusk, one with their hands in their pockets, the other turned toward them.\ntext: {f} sits down beside you on the step and says, without looking at you, \"Someone called me '{player}'s friend' today. Not my name. Just that. It's the fourth time this week.\" {They} laugh{s}, but not much. \"I don't mind. I just wanted you to know.\"\n> left: Make time for {f}, away from it all\n  rel f +medium\n  renown -small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You start taking {f} to the places no one goes: the boathouse at dusk, the end of the orchard. You let the invitations wait. One evening {f} says something so funny you fall off a jetty, and nobody else is there to see it, and that's the point.\n> right: Bring {f} into the spotlight with you\n  chance even +1 if rel(f) >= 60:\n    rel f +small\n    renown +tiny\n    say: You start bringing {f} everywhere with you, and making sure people hear {f.their} name. After a month, people in corridors start saying \"{f} and {player}\", in that order. {f} pretends not to notice, and is pleased.\n  else:\n    rel f -small\n    renown +tiny\n    say: You bring {f} everywhere, and introduce {f.them} to everyone, and {f} goes quieter and quieter. \"I didn't want to be famous,\" {f} says at last. \"I wanted you to notice.\" It takes a while to put right.\n"},{"path":"content/events/spotlight_legend.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The spotlight, Legend, and the fall (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md, batch P4-08; design §8.2). Legend (renown 85\n#  or more): the academy tells stories about you. The fall: you were Famous (`flag was_famous`, set when you cross\n#  into it) and are back under 30 (`fallen_star` in conds.grim). The pools and appointments are in spotlight.grim.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Legend: the academy's own story ----------------\n\n@event spot_tavern_song\ntitle: The Ballad of {player}\npool: spotlight_legend\ntone: light\nart: music\nscene: A packed tavern, a fiddler standing on a bench, the whole room singing along with raised mugs, one student by the door with their face in their hands.\ntext: The Newt has a new song, and it's about you. It has nine verses and a chorus everybody knows. In verse three you fight a frost giant on the lake. In verse six you marry a mermaid. The fiddler sees you in the doorway, bows, and starts again from the top, louder.\n> left: Teach them the true verses\n  alignment +small\n  renown -small\n  mood +small\n  say: You climb on a bench and sing them what really happened, which rhymes much worse. The room listens politely, then goes back to the mermaid. But the fiddler keeps one of your verses, the true one about the night you got lost, and it's the one that makes people quiet.\n> right: Let the legend grow\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 90:\n    renown -small\n    mood -small\n    say: By the next week the song has a tenth verse, in which you are cruel to a friend, by name. It isn't true. It is very catchy. The friend hears it at breakfast, and you spend a long evening putting it right.\n  else:\n    renown +small\n    mood +tiny\n    say: You buy the fiddler a drink and let it grow. By spring there are fourteen verses, two dances and a version for children. You have never fought a frost giant. Half the village is sure you did.\n\n@event spot_witness_promise\ntitle: Stand Witness\npool: spotlight_legend\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  a: any where classmate_here(a) and rel(a) >= 20\n  b: any where classmate_here(b) and rel(b) >= 20 and npc_rel(a, b) >= 40\nper: a\nart: heart\nscene: A lakeshore at dawn, two students holding hands on the shingle, a third student standing before them holding a lantern, mist on the water.\ntext: {a} and {b} find you together, very nervous, holding hands. They've decided to make each other a promise, a proper one, at the lakeshore at dawn, the old way. They'd like a witness. \"It has to be you,\" {a} says. \"If you saw it, everyone will know it happened.\"\n> left: Make it a ceremony\n  challenge perform adept-:\n    win:\n      rel a +small\n      rel b +small\n      npc_rel a b +small\n      renown +tiny\n      say: You find the right words, short and true, and say them over the water as the sun comes up. {a} cries. {b} pretends not to. By breakfast the whole castle knows, and it's the happiest thing anyone has talked about all term.\n    lose:\n      rel a +tiny\n      rel b +tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: You'd prepared a speech, and halfway through it you lose your place, and a duck lands in the shallows and starts shouting. {a} and {b} laugh so hard they have to hold each other up. It's perfect, they tell you. It is not what you planned.\n> right: Keep it quiet and simple\n  rel a +small\n  rel b +small\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: \"Just us, then,\" you say, and it is: three people and a lantern on the shingle, and the promise said plainly. Nobody else knows for a week. When it comes out, {a} and {b} only smile, because it was theirs first.\n\n@event spot_flourish_named\ntitle: The {player}\npool: spotlight_legend\ntone: light\nart: wand\nscene: A practice yard full of students all flicking their wands in the same showy twist, one older student watching with arms folded.\ntext: There's a wand flourish going round the practice yards, a sort of showy twist before the cast, and everybody calls it \"the {player}\". You've never done it in your life. It does nothing at all for the spell. Every first-year in the castle is doing it.\n> left: Teach them a flourish that helps\n  study -small\n  alignment +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You spend three afternoons in the practice yard showing them a real one, a small turn of the wrist that steadies the cast. It's much less showy. The first-years are disappointed, and then their spells start landing, and they call that one \"the {player}\" instead.\n> right: Let them have it\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 90:\n    renown -small\n    say: A first-year doing \"the {player}\" too enthusiastically puts the twist through the greenhouse glass. Professor Thorne asks, in a voice like frost, whose name it is. Everybody looks at you.\n  else:\n    renown +small\n    mood +tiny\n    queue scandal_flourish_hurt within 2..4 chance unlikely\n    say: You let them have it. It's harmless, and it makes the practice yards look like a dance. Once, passing, you do it yourself, very solemnly, and the whole yard cheers.\n\n@event spot_named_child\ntitle: A Baby Called {player}\npool: spotlight_legend\ntone: warm\nart: heart\nscene: A cottage doorway in the village, a young couple holding a swaddled baby, an academy student on the step holding a small wrapped gift.\ntext: A letter from the village: the blacksmith's daughter has had a baby, and they've named it after you, and would you, if it's not too much, come to the naming? There'll be cake. The letter is spelled very carefully, as if it was copied out three times.\n> left: Go to the naming\n  mood +medium\n  study -small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: You go down the hill with a silver spoon you can't really afford. The baby is red and furious and has your name. You hold it for the whole naming and it falls asleep on you, which the village takes as a very good sign.\n> right: Send a gift and a letter\n  gold -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You can't get away, so you send a silver spoon and a long letter, to be read to the child when it's older, about the hill and the lake and the castle. The blacksmith's daughter writes back to say it's in a drawer, waiting.\n\n@event spot_disguise_day\ntitle: An Ordinary Afternoon\npool: spotlight_legend\ntone: light\nart: mask\nscene: A student in a borrowed hat and a scarf pulled up to the nose walking through a busy village market, glancing sideways at the stalls.\ntext: You haven't bought a loaf of bread in a month without someone asking you to sign it. Today you borrow a hat, a scarf and somebody's terrible coat, pull them all up, and walk down to the village to see if you can have one ordinary afternoon.\n> left: Try it\n  challenge deceive adept-:\n    win:\n      mood +large\n      say: Nobody looks twice. You buy bread, argue with a stallholder over apples, sit on the quay and eat them, and walk back up the hill unrecognised, humming. It's the best afternoon you've had in a year.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You get as far as the bakery, where a small child points and says, very loudly, \"That's {player} in a silly hat.\" The whole street turns. You sign eleven loaves. The coat, for some reason, becomes fashionable.\n> right: Stay in the castle\n  study +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You stay in, and read, and look out of the window at the village, very small and far away. Somebody brings you bread from it, which is kind of them. It isn't the same.\n\n@event spot_higher_bar\ntitle: Because It's You\npool: spotlight_legend\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\nscene: A returned essay on a desk covered in red ink, a lecturer's note at the bottom, a student staring at it in disbelief beside a classmate's cleaner page.\ntext: Your essay comes back covered in red, with a mark lower than your neighbour's, though your neighbour's essay was, honestly, not as good. At the bottom the lecturer has written: \"From anyone else, excellent. From you, I expect more. Everybody is watching what you do.\"\n> left: Rise to it\n  study +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You rewrite it from the first line, then rewrite the next one before it's due. It is the hardest you've worked all year. The lecturer's next note is one word, \"Better\", and you find you care about it more than any cheer in the Great Hall.\n> right: Ask to be marked like anyone else\n  challenge appeal adept-:\n    win:\n      mood +small\n      alignment +tiny\n      say: You go to the lecturer's office and say, politely, that you'd like to be marked like everyone else. The lecturer looks at you over the spectacles, and then laughs, and changes the mark. \"Fair,\" the lecturer says. \"But I'll still be reading closely.\"\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You make your case. The lecturer hears you out and says that's exactly what a famous student would say. The mark stands. Worse, the story gets out, and for a week people call you \"the one who argued about the mark\".\n\n@event spot_history_essay\ntitle: A Living Figure\npool: spotlight_legend\ntone: light\nart: scroll\nscene: A corridor outside a lecture hall, a queue of students holding notebooks and quills, one student at the front of it looking trapped.\ntext: A lecturer has set the second-years an essay on \"a living figure in the academy's history\", and forty of them have chosen you. They want interviews. There is a queue outside your door, with notebooks, and one of them has brought a list of a hundred questions.\n> left: Answer them all\n  study -small\n  renown +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: It takes a week of evenings. You answer every question, including the one about your favourite pudding and the one about whether you're afraid of dying. The essays are pinned up in the corridor. Two of them are better than anything you've written.\n> right: \"I'm not history yet\"\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You tell them, cheerfully, that you're not history yet and might still do something stupid, and send them to the library to write about someone safely dead. Most of them laugh. The one with the hundred questions writes about you anyway.\n\n@event spot_villain_play\ntitle: The Villain of Thornwick\npool: spotlight_legend\ntone: light\nart: mask\nscene: A makeshift stage in a village hall, a student actor in a black cape and a cardboard crown hissing at the audience, another student in the back row laughing into a scarf.\ntext: Some visiting Thornwick students are putting on a play in the village hall, and it's about you. In their version you are the villain: you cheat at duels, steal Thornwick's glory and have a moustache. The actor playing you hisses very well. A Lumenhold crowd has come along to boo.\n> left: Go, and laugh loudest\n  chance slim +1 if renown >= 90:\n    renown -small\n    mood -small\n    say: You laugh loudest, and the actor, spotting you, improvises a whole scene about your real lost duels, which turns out to be well researched. The village laughs at you, not with you. Thornwick's paper prints the best lines.\n  else:\n    renown +small\n    mood +small\n    say: You sit in the front row and laugh loudest, and at the curtain the actor bows to you, and you bow back, and the hall cheers both of you. Afterwards you buy the cast supper. The moustache, you admit, is a fair cop.\n> right: Stay away\n  mood -tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: You stay away, which turns out to be the right thing, because the play without you is merely silly. Your friends come back with a programme and a very good impression of your hiss, which you have never once made.\n\n@event spot_rival_lucky\ntitle: Just Lucky\npool: spotlight_legend\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  r: any where classmate_here(r) and rel(r) <= 0\nper: r\nart: duel\nscene: A quiet corridor late in the day, two students facing each other, one leaning against the wall with arms folded, the other holding a stack of books.\ntext: {r} stops you in the corridor. \"Everyone says you're a legend,\" {they} say{s}. \"I think you're lucky. You were in the right place, at the right time, a lot. Someone should say it to your face.\" {r} doesn't look angry. {They} look{s} as if {they} had been waiting a long time to say it.\n> left: Agree that a lot of it was luck\n  rel r +medium\n  renown -tiny\n  say: \"A lot of it was,\" you say. {r} blinks. You tell {r.them} which bits, honestly. By the end {r} is half laughing. \"That's worse,\" {they} say{s}. \"That's so much worse.\" It's the start of something like respect, on both sides.\n> right: Tell {r} to try being lucky\n  renown +tiny\n  rel r -small\n  say: \"Try it,\" you say. \"See how lucky it feels.\" {r} looks at you for a long moment and walks off. You win the argument. It doesn't feel like it, for the rest of the day.\n\n@event spot_guest_lecture\ntitle: Take the Class\npool: spotlight_legend\ntone: tense\nart: book\nscene: A lecture hall full of first-years in rows, an older student at the lectern holding chalk, a professor sitting in the back row with a notebook.\ntext: A lecturer has asked you to take the first-years' class for an hour: \"They listen to you. They certainly don't listen to me.\" Sixty first-years, the chalk, the lectern. The lecturer will be in the back row, taking notes on you.\n> left: Teach it properly\n  challenge perform adept-:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      study +small\n      say: You teach them the thing you found hardest, the way you wish someone had taught you, and they get it. All sixty. The lecturer comes down at the end, looks at the board for a long time, and copies it into the notebook.\n    lose:\n      renown -small\n      mood -small\n      say: You start well, and lose them at the second diagram, and never get them back. By the end the first-years are passing notes about your hair. The lecturer takes the last ten minutes and says, kindly, that teaching is harder than it looks.\n> right: Tell stories instead\n  renown +small\n  study -tiny\n  say: You put the chalk down and tell them stories: the lake, the stairs, the first time a spell went right. They hang on every word. They learn almost nothing about the syllabus. A few of them, you suspect, will stay up late tonight practising.\n\n@event spot_legend_burden\ntitle: A Legend Can't Miss\npool: spotlight_legend\ntone: tense\nart: wand\nscene: A practice room, a student's spell fizzling into smoke, a whole class turned to stare in shocked silence.\ntext: In practice, in front of the class, an easy spell goes wrong in your hands. It just fizzles, the way spells do for everyone, now and then. But the whole room gasps as if the sky had fallen, and you can feel them all waiting to see what you'll do.\n> left: Laugh, and try again\n  mood +small\n  renown -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You laugh, say \"well, that's a relief\", and try again, and get it. The room laughs with you. Afterwards a first-year says it was the best thing they'd seen all year: that even you miss. You decide that's worth something.\n> right: Practise until it never happens again\n  study +small\n  mood -small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: You stay behind and cast it a hundred times, until your arm aches and it couldn't fail if it tried. It never happens again, in front of anyone. Sometimes you wish it would, so you could stop being so careful.\n\n# ---------------- the fall: yesterday's news ----------------\n\n@event renown_fallen_entry\ntitle: Yesterday's News\ntrigger: auto\nclass: milestone\ntone: tense\nwhen: fallen_star() and seen(renown_fallen_entry) == 0\nart: paper\nscene: A student walking through a busy corridor that doesn't part for them, a crumpled old copy of the Ledger with their face on it lying in a bin.\ntext: You notice it on a Tuesday. You walk the length of the long corridor and nobody turns. In the common room, a copy of the Ledger with your face on the front is being used to light the fire. Not long ago you couldn't buy bread without signing it. Now you're just somebody who used to be somebody.\n> left: Enjoy the quiet\n  mood +small\n  study +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: It's wonderful, mostly. You eat your lunch where you like. You walk to the lake without an escort. Now and then somebody squints at you and says \"weren't you...?\", and you say \"no\", and they believe you.\n> right: Plan the comeback\n  renown +small\n  mood -small\n  say: You sit down with a sheet of paper and write a list of everything that made people talk. Some of it you'd do again. Some of it you can't believe you did. You fold the paper very small and keep it in your pocket, just in case.\n\n@event fallen_old_fan\ntitle: The Fan Who Stayed\npool: fallen\ntone: warm\nart: pouch\nscene: A first-year holding a frayed charm on a ribbon, standing a little awkwardly in front of an older student in a quiet courtyard.\ntext: Most of the first-years who used to queue for you have found somebody else. But one of them, older now, stops you in the courtyard and says, a bit shyly, \"I still think you were the best one. For what it's worth.\" {chosen(spot_fan_charm, left): You recognise the blue thread on their wrist.|They've still got your name written inside the cover of a book.}\n> left: Ask how they're getting on\n  mood +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You ask about their lectures, their friends, the spell they couldn't get. They tell you everything. It turns out they've been doing very well, and nobody has asked. You walk them to supper and they talk the whole way.\n> right: Thank them, and keep walking\n  mood -tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: \"That's very kind,\" you say, and mean it, and keep walking, because you're late. It's only later that you realise they'd wanted to say more. The next time you look for them, they're with a crowd of their own.\n\n@event fallen_where_now\ntitle: Where Are They Now?\npool: fallen\ntone: tense\nwhen: met(nessa)\nart: paper\nscene: A newspaper office at dusk, an editor with a quill leaning across a desk toward a student who has their arms folded.\ntext: Nessa wants to run a piece: \"Where Are They Now?\", a look back at the academy's brightest stars, starring you. \"It's not unkind,\" she says. \"It's nostalgic. People love a comeback. Or a cautionary tale. You can choose which.\"\n> left: Give her the comeback\n  challenge perform adept-:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      rel nessa +small\n      say: You give her a new plan and an old smile, and the piece runs on the front page: \"{player}: not done yet\". By the end of the week people are turning in corridors again, cautiously, as if they'd forgotten how.\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: You try to sound like someone with a plan. It comes out sounding like someone with a list. The piece runs on page six, between the weather and the crossword, under the heading \"fading gracefully?\"\n> right: Ask her to leave you out\n  rel nessa +tiny\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: \"Leave me out of it,\" you say. \"I like it here, out of the paper.\" Nessa looks at you for a long moment and puts her quill down. \"That,\" she says, \"is the most interesting thing you've ever told me. Fine.\"\n\n@event fallen_unrecognised\ntitle: Just Another Student\npool: fallen\ntone: light\nart: meal\nscene: A village bakery counter, a baker handing a loaf to a student without a second glance, a short queue behind.\ntext: In the village, the baker who used to lean out of the window and shout your name sells you a loaf, gives you your change and says \"next, please\" without looking up. Outside, the children who used to follow you are following somebody else, over by the fountain.\n> left: Laugh about it\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You laugh, out loud, in the street, and a dog barks at you. You eat the end of the loaf on the quay and watch the boats, and nobody comes to ask you anything at all, and it's lovely.\n> right: Remind the baker who you are\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: \"It's me,\" you say. \"From the Ledger.\" The baker squints, says \"oh, yes\", and gives you a bun, out of pity. You eat it on the way back up the hill, slowly. It tastes of nothing much.\n\n@event fallen_mocked\ntitle: Weren't You Somebody?\npool: fallen\ntone: tense\nart: mask\nscene: A crowded refectory, a student at the next table pointing with a fork, a ripple of laughter, one student sitting very straight with their tray.\ntext: At lunch, someone at the next table says, just loud enough, \"Weren't you somebody once?\" The table laughs. A couple of people at your own table laugh too, then look as if they wish they hadn't. Everyone waits to see whether you'll answer.\n> left: Answer with a joke\n  challenge perform apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: \"I was,\" you say. \"Then I got hungry.\" You hold up your pudding. The whole refectory laughs, at the right person this time, and somebody at the next table actually applauds.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      say: Your joke comes out a beat too late and a little too sharp. Nobody laughs. The next table goes back to its lunch, satisfied. You eat yours very quickly.\n> right: Say nothing, and eat your lunch\n  mood -tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You say nothing and eat your lunch, and in a minute they've moved on to something else. A friend at your table kicks you gently under it and passes you their pudding. You take it.\n\n@event fallen_friends\ntitle: The Ones Who Stayed\npool: fallen\ntone: warm\ncast:\n  f: any where classmate_here(f) and rel(f) >= 40\nper: f\nart: group\nscene: A small group of students around a table in a quiet corner of a common room late at night, cards scattered, cups of cocoa, one chair pulled out for someone arriving.\ntext: The invitations have stopped. The crowd has gone. But on Thursday night, as always, {f} has pulled out the chair in the corner of the common room, and the cards are dealt, and the cocoa is going cold. \"You're late,\" {f} says. \"We waited.\"\n> left: Sit down and stay late\n  rel f +medium\n  study -small\n  say: You sit down and stay until the fire goes out. Nobody asks you about anything you did. They ask what you think, about everything, and you realise you'd almost forgotten anybody wanted to know.\n> right: Say you've been a poor friend\n  rel f +small\n  alignment +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"I've been a poor friend,\" you say. {f} shrugs. \"You were busy being famous.\" Then, more quietly: \"You're back now.\" {f} deals you in, and you lose, as usual, and it's the best evening in months.\n"},{"path":"content/events/spotlight.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The spotlight (phase 4, docs/phase4-plan.md, batches P4-06 to P4-08; design §8.2): what being known is like. Each\n#  renown tier (`renown_known`, `renown_famous`, `renown_legend` in conds.grim) has its own pool of spotlight cards;\n#  they are never drawn at random (`trigger: queued`), only dealt by the appointment below at the month's start, at\n#  most one a month, from the highest tier you've reached more often than the ones below (each tier's `weight:`).\n#  Crossing a tier is a milestone: Known's is `jit_noticed` (content/events/intro.grim); Famous and Legend have\n#  theirs here.\n# ============================================================\n\n@pool spotlight\ndesc: What every spotlight card shares: a routine moment of being known, dealt only by the spotlight appointment.\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 1\ntags: renown spotlight\n\n@pool spotlight_known\ndesc: Spotlight cards for the Known tier (renown 30 or more).\nfrom: spotlight\nwhen: renown_known()\n\n@pool spotlight_famous\ndesc: Spotlight cards for the Famous tier (renown 60 or more): bigger stakes, and a scandal that grows likelier with renown.\nfrom: spotlight\nwhen: renown_famous()\nweight: 20\n\n@pool spotlight_legend\ndesc: Spotlight cards for the Legend tier (renown 85 or more): the academy telling stories about you.\nfrom: spotlight\nwhen: renown_legend()\nweight: 30\n\n@pool fallen\ndesc: Yesterday's news: you were Famous and are back under Known. Dealt only by the `fallen` appointment.\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 1\ntags: renown fallen\nwhen: fallen_star()\n\n@appointment spotlight\ndesc: While you're Known, now and then a month opens on being recognised: a card from any tier you've reached.\nat: month\nwhen: renown_known() and term and not wrapup\nchance: even\ndeals: pool spotlight\n\n@appointment fallen\ndesc: After a fall from Famous, now and then a month brings it home.\nat: month\nwhen: fallen_star() and term and not wrapup\nchance: even\ndeals: pool fallen\n\n# ---------------- crossing a tier ----------------\n\n@event renown_famous_entry\ntitle: Your Name in Every Mouth\ntrigger: auto\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nwhen: renown_famous() and seen(renown_famous_entry) == 0\nart: star\nscene: A crowded corridor parting around a student, heads turning in a wave, a first-year pointing and being shushed.\ntext: Somewhere this month it stopped being \"have you heard of {player}\" and became just \"{player}\". Corridors go quiet and then loud as you pass. The Ledger has stopped explaining who you are. You are, it seems, famous: the academy watches what you do now, and remembers.\n> left: Enjoy it\n  mood +small\n  renown +tiny\n  flag was_famous\n  say: You let yourself enjoy it, for a week. The seats are better. The queues are shorter. Someone gives you the last slice of pudding without being asked. It is, you admit, very nice.\n> right: Keep your head down\n  mood -tiny\n  study +small\n  flag was_famous\n  say: You keep to the library and the back row. It doesn't make much difference: people find you anyway. But you get a great deal of reading done, and they come away saying you're modest, which only makes it worse.\n\n@event renown_legend_entry\ntitle: The Academy's Own Story\ntrigger: auto\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nwhen: renown_legend() and seen(renown_legend_entry) == 0\nart: star\nscene: A great hall at supper, every head at every table turned toward one student coming in through the doors, as if a story had walked in.\ntext: First-years tell stories about you that you don't recognise. A third-year swears you once did something you have never done, and the version where you did it is better. When you come into the Great Hall, it isn't quiet and it isn't loud: it simply watches. You've become part of what Lumenhold says about itself.\n> left: Live up to it\n  renown +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You start getting up earlier, working later, being kinder in corridors, because people are watching and you'd like the story to be true. It mostly is. It's exhausting. It's worth it.\n> right: Correct the stories\n  alignment +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You start telling people, cheerfully, which bits never happened. It makes no difference whatever to the stories. It makes a great deal of difference to the people who hear you say it.\n\n# ---------------- Known: people start to know your face ----------------\n\n@event spot_ledger_profile\ntitle: A Profile in the Ledger\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: light\nwhen: met(nessa) and seen(spot_ledger_profile) == 0\nart: paper\nscene: A cluttered newspaper office, an editor with a self-writing quill hovering over a notebook, a student in the visitor's chair looking uneasy.\ntext: Nessa wants you for the Ledger: a whole column, \"Who Is {player}?\", with an interview. Her quill is already hovering. \"Tell me everything,\" she says. \"Or tell me something better than everything. Readers can't tell the difference. I can.\"\n> left: Be candid\n  renown +small\n  rel nessa +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You tell her the truth, including the dull parts and one embarrassing one. The column is warm and true and a little bit unflattering. People like you more for it. You wish they'd stop quoting the embarrassing part.\n> right: Polish the story\n  challenge deceive apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +medium\n      rel nessa -tiny\n      alignment -tiny\n      flag ledger_embellished\n      queue scandal_ledger_lie within 3..6\n      say: You give her a better version of yourself: braver, wittier, slightly taller. Nessa writes it all down with a look you can't read. The column is a sensation. Some of it is even true.\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      rel nessa -small\n      say: Nessa stops writing halfway through the story about the dragon. \"There was no dragon,\" she says. The column runs anyway, short and faintly amused. \"We checked,\" it says, twice.\n\n@event spot_judge_exhibition\ntitle: A Judge for the First-Years\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: light\nart: duel\nscene: A duelling floor ringed by nervous first-years in practice robes, a student at a small table with a scorecard and a bell.\ntext: The first-years' duelling exhibition needs a judge, and they've asked for you by name. Twelve bouts, a scorecard, a little brass bell. Every first-year in the hall wants to impress you. Every one of their friends is in the gallery.\n> left: Judge it strictly\n  arcana +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You score every bout to the rules, including the one where the favourite lost on a foot fault. The gallery boos. The first-year who won on the foot fault looks at you as if you'd hung the moon.\n> right: Play to the crowd\n  renown +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You ring the bell with a flourish, give a speech between bouts, and somehow every first-year comes away with a prize for something. The hall loves it. Two of the duellists who deserved to win know they were robbed.\n\n@event spot_village_recognised\ntitle: Is It You?\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: light\nart: meal\nscene: A village square, a baker leaning out of a shop window with a tray of buns, children gathering round a student in academy robes.\ntext: In the village, a baker leans out of the window and says, \"It's you, isn't it? From the Ledger?\" By the time you've said yes, there are children, a dog, and an old man who wants to tell you what the academy was like in his day.\n> left: Stay and talk\n  renown +small\n  mood +small\n  study -small\n  say: You stay the afternoon. You eat a free bun, admire the dog, and hear three versions of the old man's story. You walk back up the hill much later than you meant to, with flour on your sleeve.\n> right: Smile and slip away\n  mood -tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: You thank the baker, buy a bun like anyone else, and slip away before the crowd thickens. Behind you, the old man is telling the story to the dog.\n\n@event spot_fan_charm\ntitle: A Charm From a Stranger\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: warm\nart: pouch\nscene: A first-year holding out a small knotted charm on a ribbon at arm's length, looking at the floor, an older student bending to take it.\ntext: A first-year you've never spoken to waits outside your lecture to give you a charm: a little knot of blue thread on a ribbon, clearly made by hand, clearly not very well. \"For luck,\" the first-year says, to the floor. \"I made it. You don't have to wear it.\"\n> left: Wear it where people can see\n  renown +small\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You tie it to your bag strap. Within a week every first-year in the castle is knotting blue thread, and the one who made yours walks past you every morning, very straight, very proud.\n> right: Thank them, and keep it in a pocket\n  alignment +tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: \"I'll keep it close,\" you say, and you do, in the inside pocket nobody sees. The first-year goes pink and runs. It's still there at the end of the year, a little frayed.\n\n@event spot_signature\ntitle: Sign This?\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: light\nart: book\nscene: A library corridor, a younger student holding out an open textbook and a quill, an older student hesitating over the page.\ntext: A first-year has their warding textbook open at the chapter on circles, and a quill held out. \"Could you sign it? Next to the diagram. Everyone says you got it in your first week.\"\n> left: Sign it with a flourish\n  renown +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You sign it, with a little drawn circle for good measure. The textbook goes round the first-year dormitory by supper. By the end of the week three more appear, and a library book, which you decline.\n> right: Show them the rune instead\n  study -tiny\n  alignment +small\n  say: You don't sign it. You take the quill and draw the circle properly in the margin, slowly, and make the first-year draw it back. \"There. Now it's yours.\" The first-year looks disappointed, and then, by the third try, astonished.\n\n@event spot_house_speech\ntitle: A Few Words\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: tense\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 10\nart: candle\nscene: A long table at supper, candles lit, a student halfway to their feet with a cup in hand, every face along the table turned their way.\ntext: At the end-of-term supper, a final-year at the top of your table taps a glass and says that {player}, who has done the table such credit this term, will now say a few words. Nobody told you about the few words. The whole table turns.\n> left: Make a speech\n  challenge perform apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: You say something short and true about the people at this table, and sit down before you can spoil it. They bang the table for you. A third-year you barely know shakes your hand after, hard.\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      mood -small\n      say: You start well and lose the thread somewhere around \"and in conclusion\". Your table cheers anyway, loyally and a bit too loudly. You eat your pudding very fast.\n> right: Raise your cup and sit\n  renown -tiny\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You raise your cup, say \"to all of you\", and sit down. It turns out that's all anybody wanted. The table drinks, and goes back to arguing about the pudding.\n\n@event spot_resentful\ntitle: All Anyone Talks About\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4\nper: o\nart: mask\nscene: A common room, a student sitting stiffly apart with a book they're not reading, glancing at a crowd gathered around someone else.\ntext: {o} has been short with you all week. Tonight, when the common room gathers round you for the third time to hear about something you did, {o} shuts {o.their} book hard and says, to nobody, \"Some of us did things this term too.\"\n> left: Turn the room to {o}\n  renown -tiny\n  rel o +medium\n  say: \"{o} did, actually,\" you say, and tell them about it, the thing {o} did that nobody noticed. The room turns. {o} goes red, and then, slowly, starts telling it properly. Later {o} says thank you, gruffly.\n> right: Let it pass\n  rel o -small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You let it pass, and the room moves on, back to you. {o} goes up to bed early. It sits between you for a while, like a coat on a chair nobody will move.\n\n@event spot_letters_page\ntitle: Letters to the Editor\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: light\nart: letter\nscene: A newspaper's letters page pinned to a corridor board, students crowding round to read it, one of them laughing behind their hand.\ntext: The Ledger's letters page is about you this week. \"Sir, I write to complain about {player}'s conduct in the library,\" says one. \"Sir, I write to defend {player}, who is a credit to us all,\" says another, in what is very clearly the same handwriting. Half the castle is reading it.\n> left: Write in yourself\n  challenge perform apprentice:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: You write a short, grave letter apologising for your conduct in the library, which you say was \"a sneeze of uncommon violence\". It runs on the front page. The library gives you a round of applause.\n    lose:\n      renown -tiny\n      say: Your letter tries to be funny and comes out pompous. The next issue has three letters about your letter. You resolve never to write to a newspaper again.\n> right: Ignore it entirely\n  mood -tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: You don't read it, which is harder than it sounds when everyone keeps reading it to you. By the next week there's a new scandal: someone has been taking two puddings. You are forgotten, gratefully.\n\n@event spot_saved_seats\ntitle: The Seat Problem\npool: spotlight_known\nrepeat: 3\ntone: light\nart: meal\nscene: A refectory at lunch, three different tables each with a student waving at an empty seat, a fourth student standing in the middle with a tray.\ntext: {~Three different tables have saved you a seat at lunch, and all three are waving.|You can't sit down in the refectory any more without someone having saved the spot \"for you\", whether you wanted it or not.|Somebody you have never met has saved you a seat, and is offended you haven't taken it.} Your own friends are at the corner table, where they always are, watching to see where you'll go.\n> left: Sit with your friends\n  renown -tiny\n  mood +small\n  say: {~You walk past all three waving tables to the corner. Your friends make a great show of not caring, and then shift up to make room.|You sit where you always sit. One table looks hurt. Your friends look smug.|You sit down at the corner. \"Oh, we're good enough for you, are we?\" says someone, delighted.}\n> right: Take a saved seat\n  renown +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You sit at the nearest table and are asked forty questions. It's flattering. At the corner table, your friends are laughing at something without you.|You take the saved seat, and the table is very kind, and you can't remember any of their names afterwards.|You sit with the stranger, who is thrilled, and tells you about their aunt. Your friends wave from across the hall.}\n\n@event spot_shop_window\ntitle: The Window Table\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: light\nart: cup\nscene: A village tearoom window with a reserved card on its best table, a shopkeeper beckoning a student in from the street.\ntext: A tearoom in the village has noticed that when you sit in its window, people come in. The owner makes you an offer: your tea is free, for the rest of the term, so long as you drink it in the window, where it can be seen.\n> left: Take the window\n  gold +small\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You drink a great deal of free tea in the window, smiling at strangers who press their noses to the glass. The tearoom is full every afternoon. You can never quite read your book.\n> right: Pay for your tea\n  gold -tiny\n  mood +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You thank the owner and pay, and sit at the back, where nobody sees you. You read four chapters in peace. The owner, a little put out, brings you extra biscuits anyway.\n\n@event spot_impression\ntitle: Somebody Doing You\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: light\nwhen: month == 4 or month == 10\nart: mask\nscene: An end-of-term skit in a common room, a student on a chair doing an exaggerated impression, the room in stitches, one student in the audience watching their own walk being copied.\ntext: At the end-of-term skits in the common room, a second-year gets up on a chair and does you. The walk, the face you make in lectures, the thing you say when you lose at cards. It is very, very accurate. The whole room is looking at you to see how you'll take it.\n> left: Get up and do them back\n  challenge perform apprentice:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      mood +small\n      say: You climb up on the next chair and do them doing you. The room goes wild. The second-year laughs so hard they fall off the chair. It becomes, for a term, a double act.\n    lose:\n      mood -small\n      renown -tiny\n      say: You climb up, and freeze, and the second-year does you freezing, which gets the biggest laugh of the night. You laugh too, eventually. It takes a while.\n> right: Laugh along\n  renown +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You laugh, properly, because it is funny. The second-year bows to you at the end. You notice, for the rest of the week, that you've stopped making the face in lectures.\n\n@event spot_name_dropped\ntitle: A Friend of {player}'s\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: tense\ncast:\n  o: any where classmate_here(o) and rel(o) >= 4 and rel(o) < 20\nper: o\nart: group\nscene: A student talking grandly to a group of younger students in a corridor, an arm flung out, the person they're talking about rounding the corner behind them.\ntext: You come round a corner and hear {o} telling a little crowd of second-years that {o.they} and {player} are \"very close, actually\", and that you \"always ask {o.their} advice\". You've spoken to {o} perhaps four times. {o} turns, and sees you.\n> left: Play along\n  rel o +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  say: \"We are,\" you say. \"Terrible advice, mostly.\" The second-years laugh. {o} looks at you with such gratitude that you find, over the next month, that you do start asking {o.their} advice. Some of it is quite good.\n> right: Set it straight, kindly\n  rel o -small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: \"We've met,\" you say pleasantly, \"a few times.\" {o} goes scarlet. The second-years drift away. {o} avoids you for a while, and then, one day, sits down and talks to you properly, about nothing much.\n\n@event spot_professor_example\ntitle: As {player} Showed Us\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\nscene: A lecture hall, a professor gesturing toward one student in the third row, the rest of the class turning to look.\ntext: In the middle of a lecture, the lecturer says, \"As {player} showed us last week,\" and describes something you did, slightly wrong, as the right way to do it. Forty heads turn. You know, and you're fairly sure the lecturer doesn't, that it only worked by accident.\n> left: Say it was an accident\n  alignment +small\n  renown -tiny\n  study +tiny\n  say: You put your hand up and say it was mostly luck. The lecturer looks at you for a long moment, then laughs, and makes the class work out why it shouldn't have worked. It's the best lecture of the term.\n> right: Let it stand\n  renown +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You say nothing. The class writes it down. For the rest of the term people ask you to show them how, and you have to work out, in secret and in a hurry, how you did it.\n\n@event spot_three_invitations\ntitle: Three Invitations, One Evening\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: light\nart: letter\nscene: A student at a desk with three different invitation cards fanned out in one hand, a calendar with one evening circled three times.\ntext: Three invitations for the same evening: a supper at the Gilded Newt with people who matter, a stargazing party on the observatory roof, and your oldest friend here, who just wants to play cards in the common room like you used to.\n> left: Go where you'll be seen\n  renown +small\n  gold -tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: The Newt supper is loud and full of people who know your name. You make three useful acquaintances and laugh a great deal. Walking back, you think of the card game, and wonder who won.\n> right: Play cards with your friend\n  mood +small\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You send two polite notes and go down to the common room. Your friend looks up, surprised, and deals you in without a word. You lose every hand. It's the best evening of the month.\n\n@event spot_family_tour\ntitle: Show Them Round\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: light\nart: tower\nscene: A student leading a nervous family through a castle courtyard, a small child staring up at the towers, a parent clutching a prospectus.\ntext: A note comes down from the Headmistress's Tower: a family is visiting, their eldest starts next autumn, and they've asked, having read about you, whether you might show them round. The eldest is pale with nerves. The parents have a list of questions. The youngest wants to see a dragon.\n> left: Show them the polished academy\n  renown +small\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You take them the long way: the Great Hall at its brightest, the library from the good end, the observatory with the sun on it. You skip the draughty stairs and the lecture with the smell. The parents are dazzled. The eldest looks more frightened than ever.\n> right: Show them the real one\n  alignment +small\n  mood +tiny\n  renown -tiny\n  say: You show them the draughty stairs, the bench where everyone cries before exams, and the kitchen door where the kind cook leaves buns. The parents look a little doubtful. The eldest, for the first time, smiles. \"So people get lost here too?\" Constantly, you tell them.\n\n@event spot_owl_mail\ntitle: Letters From Strangers\npool: spotlight_known\nrepeat: 3\ntone: warm\nart: letter\nscene: A dormitory windowsill heaped with letters, a tired delivery owl sitting on the pile, a student reading one with a candle.\ntext: {~The owls have started bringing you letters from people you've never met: a farmer's child who wants to come to Lumenhold, a retired professor with opinions, someone who wants to know if you're single.|There are eleven letters on your windowsill this morning, and an owl asleep on top of them, looking put-upon.|Another heap of letters from strangers. One has a pressed flower in it. One is just a drawing of you, with very large hands.}\n> left: Answer every one\n  mood +small\n  study -small\n  renown +tiny\n  say: {~You spend the evening writing back, one after another, until your hand aches. The farmer's child writes again within a week, in very careful capitals.|You answer every letter, even the one with the drawing, and thank its artist for the hands.|It takes two evenings. You sleep badly and dream of owls. Somewhere out there, eleven people are very pleased.}\n> right: Keep them, answer none\n  study +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: {~You put them in a box under the bed and get on with your reading. Now and then you take one out and read it again. You never quite manage to write back.|The box under the bed is getting full. The owl gives you a long, reproachful look on its way out.|You read them all, at least. The pressed flower goes in a book. You mean to answer that one. You don't.}\n\n@event spot_notes_for_sale\ntitle: Your Notes, For Sale\npool: spotlight_known\ntone: tense\nart: scroll\nscene: A common room table covered in copied pages, a student with a cash tin selling sheaves of notes, a queue of younger students waiting.\ntext: Somebody has been copying your lecture notes and selling them in the common room as \"the {player} method\", a few coppers a copy, and there's a queue. The seller, a cheerful third-year with a cash tin, sees you and doesn't even look guilty. \"You could have a cut,\" they offer.\n> left: Take a cut\n  gold +small\n  alignment -small\n  renown +tiny\n  queue scandal_wrong_notes within 2..4 chance even\n  say: You take your cut and correct a few pages so the copies are at least right. The money is very welcome. A first-year thanks you, earnestly, for the notes they paid for. It sits a little wrong with you all week.\n> right: Give them away free\n  gold -tiny\n  renown +small\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You buy the last copy yourself and pin it to the common-room board, free to anyone. The seller sighs and closes the tin. By supper there's a queue at the board instead, and someone has added a note at the bottom: \"thank you\".\n"},{"path":"content/events/whisperwood/around.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Around the Whisperwood (world expansion 1, plan §1 and K4): the talking trees (delve cards in bands 2–3, one per\n#  peer, each a plot-safe personal fact), the clues that the Hush keeps midnight hours (ww_midnight_known), Old\n#  Maud's cards about Tuppence and her old trail map, the delve items' own stories, and the two offers the delve's\n#  finds open (the heartwood splinter for Mir, the Spinner's silk for the tailor).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The talking trees (delve, bands 2–3) ----------------\n# Right listens (the fact) and goes on; a month later, `ww_tree_tell` asks whether you tell them you heard it.\n\n@event ww_tree_tobias\ntitle: The Trees Gossip\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 6\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature social\ncast:\n  peer: tobias where met(tobias) and not knows(tobias.gerald)\nscene: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, as if whispering, a student listening beneath.\ntext: Two old trees have their crowns together, whispering. You catch your own name, and then Tobias's. The trees know him: he talks to plants, and plants, it turns out, talk back about him. Something about a hat.\n> left: Leave them to it; turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: You leave the trees to their gossip and go home. Whatever it was about the hat, it can wait.\n> right: Listen in\n  learn tobias.gerald\n  queue ww_tree_tell in 1 with peer\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: Tobias, say the trees, is raising a mandrake called Gerald, who wears a knitted hat and screams softly. They approve of Gerald. They're less sure about the hat.\n\n@event ww_tree_lyra\ntitle: The Trees Gossip\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 6\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature social\ncast:\n  peer: lyra where met(lyra) and not knows(lyra.toast)\nscene: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, as if whispering, a student listening beneath.\ntext: Two old trees have their crowns together, whispering. The name that comes up is Lyra's, and the trees sound amused. One of them has a scorch mark low on its trunk, and it's still a little sore about it.\n> left: Leave them to it; turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: You leave the trees to their gossip. Some things about Lyra you'd rather hear from Lyra.\n> right: Listen in\n  learn lyra.toast\n  queue ww_tree_tell in 1 with peer\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: Lyra Vaneshade, say the trees, can incinerate a target at forty paces and cannot make toast. She tried, out here, alone, at dawn. The scorched tree was the toast.\n\n@event ww_tree_bram\ntitle: The Trees Gossip\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 6\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature social\ncast:\n  peer: bram where met(bram) and not knows(bram.hall_tax)\nscene: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, as if whispering, a student listening beneath.\ntext: Two old trees have their crowns together, whispering, and the word you catch is \"prefect\", said with relish. They know Bram: a red sash comes through here on patrol, they say, and talks to itself about money.\n> left: Leave them to it; turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: You leave the trees to their gossip. Bram has enough people talking about him.\n> right: Listen in\n  learn bram.hall_tax\n  queue ww_tree_tell in 1 with peer\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: Bram, say the trees, once invented a hall tax for the east staircase, and collected it for a week before anyone checked. They think it was very enterprising. They would.\n\n@event ww_tree_mir\ntitle: The Trees Gossip\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 6\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature social\ncast:\n  peer: mir where met(mir) and not knows(mir.owl)\nscene: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, as if whispering, a student listening beneath.\ntext: Two old trees have their crowns together, whispering about a bird that isn't a bird. It ticks, they say. It flew through here once with Mir chasing it and shouting about a deadline.\n> left: Leave them to it; turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: You leave the trees to their gossip, and go home listening for ticking.\n> right: Listen in\n  learn mir.owl\n  queue ww_tree_tell in 1 with peer\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: Mir, say the trees, built a clockwork owl that remembers deadlines, birthdays, and grudges, slightly. The owl landed here and told an oak it was overdue. The oak is still upset.\n\n@event ww_tree_kael\ntitle: The Trees Gossip\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 6\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature social\ncast:\n  peer: kael where met(kael) and not knows(kael.cold_hands)\nscene: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, frost on one side of their trunks, a student listening.\ntext: Two old trees are whispering, and one side of each trunk is frosted, as if someone leaned there. The Northern boy, they say. He walks out here when he can't sleep, and puts his hands on their bark.\n> left: Leave them to it; turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: You leave the trees to their whispering. It feels like his to tell.\n> right: Listen in\n  learn kael.cold_hands\n  queue ww_tree_tell in 1 with peer\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: His hands are always cold, the trees say. He puts them on the bark to see if anything is ever warm enough. Nothing has been, yet. The trees sound sorry about it.\n\n@event ww_tree_nessa\ntitle: The Trees Gossip\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 6\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature social\ncast:\n  peer: nessa where met(nessa) and not knows(nessa.quill)\nscene: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, a quill-scratched message on one trunk, a student listening.\ntext: Two old trees are whispering about a quill. Somebody's quill was left out here overnight, they say, and by morning it had written a headline on a birch. The birch would like a retraction.\n> left: Leave them to it; turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: You leave the trees to their gossip, which is at least partly about the press.\n> right: Listen in\n  learn nessa.quill\n  queue ww_tree_tell in 1 with peer\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: Nessa's quill, say the trees, starts inventing the news if she leaves it unsupervised. The birch's headline is about the birch. It's very unflattering, and, the trees admit, mostly true.\n\n@event ww_tree_corvin\ntitle: The Trees Gossip\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 6\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature social\ncast:\n  peer: corvin where met(corvin) and not knows(corvin.trolls)\nscene: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, a goat grazing beneath, a student listening.\ntext: Two old trees are whispering, and laughing, in the creaking way trees laugh. It's about Corvin. It's about a debt. It's about, as far as you can make out, a goat.\n> left: Leave them to it; turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: You leave the trees laughing. You'll hear about the goat eventually. Everyone does.\n> right: Listen in\n  learn corvin.trolls\n  queue ww_tree_tell in 1 with peer\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: Corvin, say the trees, once owed thirty crowns to trolls, and offered to pay them back with a goat. The trolls took the goat. The goat now runs the trolls.\n\n@event ww_tree_tell\ntitle: Something the Trees Said\ntrigger: queued\nactor: npc\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nrepeat: 1\nart: tree\ntags: social nature\nscene: Two students talking in a castle corridor, one looking surprised and caught out, the other trying not to smile.\ntext: {npc} is right there, and you know something about {them} that {they} didn't tell you. The Whisperwood did. It's on the tip of your tongue.\n> left: Keep it to yourself\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You keep it. Whatever the trees say, it's {npc}'s to tell. It sits in you like a small warm stone.\n> right: Tell {them} you heard it\n  if npc_is(tobias) or npc_is(mir) or npc_is(corvin) or npc_is(kael):\n    rel npc +small\n  else:\n    rel npc -tiny\n  say: {npc_is(tobias): Tobias goes pink to the ears. \"They told you about Gerald?\" Then, proudly: \"Do you want to meet him?\"|{npc_is(mir): \"The owl went to the WOOD?\" Mir is delighted. \"It's famous. It's going to be unbearable.\"|{npc_is(corvin): Corvin laughs until he has to lean on the wall. \"The goat,\" he says. \"Everyone always hears about the goat.\"|{npc_is(kael): Kael is quiet for a long moment. Then he holds out a hand, and you take it. \"Not yet,\" he says. \"But warmer.\"|{npc_is(lyra): Lyra turns the colour of her own fire. \"Trees,\" she says, \"are liars,\" and stalks off, and doesn't speak to you until Thursday.|{npc_is(bram): Bram goes rigid. \"That was a legitimate levy,\" he says, and writes something down that is probably your name.|Nessa narrows her eyes. \"Who's your source? A tree. A TREE.\" She writes it down anyway.}}}}}}\n\n# ---------------- The midnight clues ----------------\n\n@event jit_maud_warning\ntitle: Maud's Warning\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nart: tree\ntags: intro nature\nscene: An old woman in a shawl with a basket of mushrooms catching a student's sleeve at the edge of a misty forest.\ntext: Old Maud is at the edge of the wood with a basket, and catches your sleeve. \"Off the path, are we? Listen, dearie. Press on as far as you like, turn back whenever you like, and what you bring out, Pell will buy. Fall once and you'll get up. Twice, and someone'll have to come and find you.\"\n> left: Thank her\n  meet maud\n  say: \"And mind the Hush,\" she adds, letting go. \"It keeps the castle's hours. It wakes when you're meant to be asleep.\"\n> right: Ask about the deep part\n  meet maud\n  flag ww_midnight_known\n  find lead_the_hush\n  say: \"The Hush,\" she says. \"Past the heartwood. It keeps the castle's hours, dearie: it wakes at midnight, when you're meant to be asleep.\" She looks at the wood a long moment. \"Don't go down there without a friend.\"\n\n@pool stables_routine_weight8\nplace: stables\nclass: routine\nweight: 8\n\n@event brask_deep_hours\ntitle: Midnight Hours\npool: stables_routine_weight8\ntone: eerie\nwhen: met(brask) and not flag(ww_midnight_known) and visits_to(whisperwood) > 0\nart: owl\ntags: nature mystery\ncast:\n  brask: brask where around(brask)\nscene: A professor in a riding coat feeding a barn owl in a dim stable loft at dusk, the forest dark through the window.\ntext: Professor Brask is feeding the old barn owl in the loft. \"You've been in the wood,\" she says, without turning round. \"By day, I hope. The deep things keep midnight hours. Most of them would rather you didn't.\"\n> left: Ask her what deep things\n  flag ww_midnight_known\n  find lead_the_hush\n  lore +tiny\n  rel brask -tiny\n  say: \"Old ones,\" says Brask. \"In the Hush, past the heartwood. Wakes at midnight. Go down by day and it's just quiet.\" She feeds the owl another mouse. \"That was a warning, not an invitation.\" It didn't sound like either.\n> right: Help with the owl instead\n  rel brask +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You hold the bucket and the owl regards you with deep contempt. Brask says nothing more about the wood, and you leave with a peck on the thumb and her good opinion.\n  if knows(brask.owl):\n    rel brask +tiny\n    say: You don't ask which colleague the owl outlived last. She tells you anyway, in four words, and that's all.\n\n@event carto_hush_margin\ntitle: A Note in the Margin\ntrack: cartographers\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(ww_midnight_known)\nweight: 10\nart: map\ntags: club mystery\nscene: An old hand-drawn map of a forest spread on a table by candlelight, one margin crowded with tiny ink notes.\ntext: The Cartographers' oldest map of the grounds has a margin crowded with notes in a dozen hands. Over the Whisperwood someone has written, very small: \"W. wood: HUSH. Twelfth bell only.\" Corvin taps it. \"Never checked that one,\" he says. \"Nobody's that brave. Or that stupid.\"\n> left: Ink it onto the new map\n  flag ww_midnight_known\n  find lead_the_hush\n  lore +tiny\n  say: You copy the note onto the new map in your neatest hand, and the Cartographers watch you do it with the particular silence of people placing bets.\n> right: Leave the margin alone\n  guile +tiny\n  say: You leave the old note where it is. The Cartographers move on to the third floor's missing corridor. The margin stays in your head anyway.\n\n@event grimsbys_field_guide\ntitle: A Field Guide\npool: grimsbys_books_routine_weight8\ntone: light\nwhen: not flag(ww_midnight_known) and visits_to(whisperwood) > 0\nart: book\ntags: nature shop\nscene: A small battered green field guide open on a bookshop counter, a long footnote circled in pencil.\ntext: Grimsby has put a field guide to the western woods on the counter, open at moonpetal. There's a long footnote, in tiny print, and someone has circled it. \"I think,\" says Grimsby mildly, \"that you should read the footnote.\"\n> left: Buy the book\n  gold -small\n  lore +small\n  flag ww_midnight_known\n  find lead_the_hush\n  say: The footnote says moonpetal in the deep wood opens only \"in the Hush, at the twelfth bell\". You buy the book and read the rest on the way home. It's mostly about mushrooms, and very good.\n> right: Read the footnote and go\n  flag ww_midnight_known\n  find lead_the_hush\n  rel grimsby -tiny\n  say: You read the footnote standing up: moonpetal in the deep wood opens only \"in the Hush, at the twelfth bell\". Grimsby closes the book with a sigh that says browsers are a cross he bears.\n\n# ---------------- Old Maud and Tuppence ----------------\n\n@event maud_tuppence\ntitle: My Girl in the Hush\nplace: maud_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: rel(maud) >= 12 and not knows(maud.tuppence)\nweight: 10\nart: spider\ntags: social nature\ncast:\n  maud: maud where around(maud)\nscene: An old woman pouring tea in a cluttered cottage kitchen, an old drawing of a tiny spider pinned above the stove.\ntext: Over tea, Maud nods at a drawing pinned over the stove: a spider, no bigger than a hand. \"My Tuppence,\" she says. \"Found her in the greenhouses, my first winter as groundskeeper. Kept her in the potting shed till she got too big for it. She's in the Hush now, the deep part of the wood. Keeps the worse things out.\" She sips. \"Only wakes at midnight, the Hush.\"\n> left: Ask how big she is now\n  learn maud.tuppence\n  flag ww_midnight_known\n  find lead_the_hush\n  rel maud +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Oh, carriage-sized,\" says Maud fondly. \"Bit more.\" She pours more tea. You find you don't want any.\n> right: Ask if she misses her\n  learn maud.tuppence\n  flag ww_midnight_known\n  find lead_the_hush\n  rel maud +small\n  say: \"Every day,\" says Maud. \"But you can't keep a thing in a potting shed because you'd miss it. That's not love, that's a jar.\" She looks at you over the cup. \"Tell her I said hello, if you're ever that foolish.\"\n\n@event maud_worse_out\ntitle: She Kept Worse Out\nplace: maud_cottage\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: flag(ww_spinner_beaten) and met(maud)\nweight: 14\nart: spider\ntags: social nature\ncast:\n  maud: maud where around(maud)\nscene: An old woman standing at her cottage door at dusk, looking toward a dark forest with an expression half proud, half afraid.\ntext: Maud knows. Of course she knows: the bees told her, or the wood did. \"You beat my Tuppence,\" she says, and she's proud, you can hear it, and frightened too. \"Good for you, dearie. Only she kept worse things out. Now she's let one in. Sits by a lantern, I hear. Polite.\" She doesn't say his name.\n> left: Promise to be careful\n  rel maud +small\n  say: \"Careful,\" says Maud, \"is what people say instead of 'not going'.\" But she pats your hand, and gives you a jar of honey for the road you haven't said you'll take.\n> right: Ask who he is\n  lore +small\n  mood -tiny\n  say: \"Somebody they sent away,\" says Maud, \"before my time, almost. He was clever. They always are.\" She shuts the door very firmly, as if on a draught.\n\n@event ww_mastery_map\ntitle: Maud's Old Map\nplace: maud_cottage\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nwhen: (flag(ww_spinner_beaten) or ww_descents >= 3) and not flag(ww_mastery) and met(maud)\nweight: 16\nart: map\ntags: nature social\ncast:\n  maud: maud where around(maud)\nscene: An old woman handing a creased, hand-drawn map of a forest to a student across a cottage table.\ntext: Maud slides a creased old map across the table. The Whisperwood, drawn in her young hand, every carved bee marked, every path to the heartwood, and a careful line down into the Hush. \"Mine,\" she says, \"from when I used to go down to visit. My knees won't take it now. Yours will.\"\n> left: Take it, gratefully\n  flag ww_mastery\n  rel maud +small\n  say: You fold it into your coat. From now on, at midnight, you can go straight down past the heartwood. \"Bring it back,\" says Maud, \"when you're old.\"\n> right: Ask her to come with you, once\n  flag ww_mastery\n  rel maud +medium\n  health -tiny\n  say: She laughs till she coughs. \"Once,\" she says. And one night she does, slowly, on your arm, as far as the heartwood, and stands there smiling at the dark. Then she gives you the map.\n\n# ---------------- The delve's finds: offers ----------------\n\n@event offer_heartwood_wand\ntitle: Mir Carves the Heartwood\nplace: village\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nweight: 14\nwhen: flag(ww_heartwood) and not has_item(heartwood_wand) and met(mir)\nart: wand\ntags: money shop offer item\nscene: An artificer at a market stall turning a red-gold splinter of wood in their fingers, a carving knife ready.\ntext: Mir is at the market stall, mending a clockwork hen, when you put the heartwood splinter on the counter. They stop. They pick it up as if it might bite, then as if it might sing. \"Where,\" they say, \"did you get THIS?\" Then: \"I can carve it. It's work. Not much money.\"\n> left: Not yet\n  say: Mir hands it back with real reluctance. \"Don't lose it,\" they say. \"Don't use it to stir anything. Don't...\" You leave while they're still listing.\n> right: Have Mir carve it\n  hint: A wand for fights: more Mana.\n  gold -small\n  give heartwood_wand\n  rel mir +small\n  say: Mir carves it in an evening, talking to it the whole time. The grain moves under their knife as if it's helping. When they hand it over, it's warm, and so are their hands. \"Don't tell anyone I made a thing that isn't a machine,\" they say.\n\n@event offer_spinner_silk_robe\ntitle: Spinner Silk\nplace: village\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nweight: 14\nwhen: flag(ww_spinner_silk) and not has_item(spinner_silk_robe)\nart: spider\ntags: money shop offer item\nscene: A village tailor holding up a length of shimmering grey silk to the window light, her mouth open in astonishment.\ntext: The village tailor unrolls the Spinner's silk across her table and goes very quiet. She holds it to the window. Light goes into it and doesn't come out. \"I can make you a robe of this,\" she says at last. \"It will cost you. Not for the silk. For my nerves.\"\n> left: Keep the silk for now\n  say: She rolls it up again, very carefully, as if it might roll her up. \"Come back,\" she says. \"Please come back. I want to have made it.\"\n> right: Have the robe made\n  hint: A robe for fights: much more Health, quicker steps.\n  gold -large\n  give spinner_silk_robe\n  say: It takes her a week and two sleepless nights. When you put it on, you move like a rumour. The tailor sits down. \"I'll never make anything like it again,\" she says, happily.\n\n# ---------------- The delve's items: their own stories ----------------\n\n@event item_quill_brask\ntitle: Brask Knows That Owl\npool: stables_routine_weight8\ntone: warm\nwhen: has_item(owl_quill) and met(brask)\nart: feather\ntags: item nature\ncast:\n  brask: brask where around(brask)\nscene: A professor in a riding coat holding up a white owl-feather quill to the light, an old barn owl in the rafters above.\ntext: Professor Brask sees your quill and takes it out of your hand without asking. She turns it to the light. Above you, in the rafters, the old barn owl shifts. \"That's hers,\" says Brask. \"From the winter she moulted in the wood. I looked for that feather for a month.\"\n> left: Offer it back\n  rel brask +small\n  learn brask.owl\n  say: Brask looks at the quill a long time, then hands it back. \"Keep it. She'd want it used.\" Then, gruffly, she tells you how old the owl is, and how many colleagues it has outlived, and doesn't say which loss hurt most.\n> right: Ask her about the owl\n  lore +tiny\n  learn brask.owl\n  say: \"Older than the beastcraft syllabus,\" says Brask. \"Outlived three professors.\" She hands back the quill. \"Wouldn't say which one I miss most,\" she says, and then doesn't.\n\n@event item_quill_nessa\ntitle: The Quill Writes Back\nplace: ledger_office\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has_item(owl_quill) and met(nessa)\nweight: 8\nart: feather\ntags: item social\ncast:\n  nessa: nessa where around(nessa)\nscene: Two quills scratching furiously at each other on a sheet of paper in a cluttered newspaper office, a student and an editor watching aghast.\ntext: You leave your owl quill on Nessa's desk for one minute. When you come back, her enchanted quill and yours are writing at each other on a sheet of foolscap: hers in headlines, yours in very tidy footnotes. It is, as far as you can tell, an argument about accuracy.\n> left: Let them finish\n  rel nessa +small\n  learn nessa.quill\n  say: Nessa reads the finished sheet aloud. Her quill invented a scandal; yours corrected it, line by line. \"I'm printing this,\" says Nessa, \"as a warning to my quill.\"\n> right: Separate them\n  lore +tiny\n  rel nessa -tiny\n  say: You pull your quill away mid-footnote. Nessa's quill sulks for a week and invents nothing, which Nessa says is worse.\n\n@pool around_routine\nclass: routine\nweight: 8\ntags: item\n\n@event item_lodestone_kael\ntitle: North, Always North\npool: around_routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: has_item(lodestone_pendant) and met(kael)\nart: compass\ntags: social\ncast:\n  kael: kael where around(kael)\nscene: A pale Northern student looking at a dark pendant turning slowly on a cord, his frosted hand held out beneath it.\ntext: Kael notices the lodestone turning on its cord, slowly, to face north. He watches it for a long moment. \"It points home,\" he says. \"My home.\" He doesn't sound as if he's decided how he feels about that.\n> left: Let him hold it\n  rel kael +small\n  learn kael.north\n  say: He holds it in his cold palm and the stone keeps turning north, steady as a promise. \"It doesn't know I left,\" he says, and gives it back.\n> right: Ask about the North\n  lore +tiny\n  rel kael -tiny\n  say: \"Cold,\" says Kael, which is the whole answer, and the subject is closed. The pendant keeps pointing at it.\n\n@event item_lodestone_stacks\ntitle: Lost in the Stacks\nplace: library\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has_item(lodestone_pendant)\nweight: 8\nart: compass\ntags: item study\nscene: A student following a turning pendant between tall dim library shelves, a misshelved book glowing faintly at the end of a row.\ntext: In the deep stacks the lodestone stops pointing north and starts pointing at a shelf. Then at a book, misshelved forty years ago between two treatises on drainage. It's very old, and very interesting, and nobody has missed it.\n> left: Read it here and now\n  lore +small\n  study +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  say: You read it on the floor of the stacks until the lamps are put out. It's about the wood. Most of it you already knew, the hard way.\n> right: Return it to its shelf\n  rel grimsby +tiny\n  alignment +tiny\n  say: You find where it belongs and put it there. Grimsby, when you tell him, is so pleased he gives you tea.\n\n@event item_tine_brask\ntitle: Brask Bows to It\npool: stables_routine_weight8\ntone: warm\nwhen: has_item(stag_tine) and met(brask)\nart: paw\ntags: item nature\ncast:\n  brask: brask where around(brask)\nscene: A sturdy professor bowing low to a dark antler tine held by a student, horses watching from their stalls.\ntext: Professor Brask sees the stag's tine at your belt and stops in the middle of a sentence. Then she bows to it, deeply, the way she bows in the bracken. Every horse in the stable goes quiet.\n> left: Bow back\n  rel brask +small\n  say: You bow to the tine too, which feels silly, and then doesn't. Brask straightens and nods. \"You'll do,\" she says, which from her is a sonnet.\n> right: Ask why she bowed\n  lore +small\n  say: \"It's not yours,\" she says. \"It's lent. The stag lends it to people it means to see again.\" She looks at you hard. \"Don't make it regret that.\"\n\n@event item_heartwood_tobias\ntitle: It Hums\nplace: greenhouses\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: has_item(heartwood_wand) and met(tobias)\nweight: 8\nart: leaf\ntags: item social\ncast:\n  tobias: tobias where around(tobias)\nscene: A large gentle student in a greenhouse surrounded by potted plants all leaning toward a red-gold wand in another student's hand.\ntext: You walk into the greenhouses with the heartwood wand, and every plant in the place leans toward it. Tobias looks up from his potting. \"Oh,\" he says. \"Oh, they like that. It's humming. Can you hear it? It's humming to them.\"\n> left: Let him listen\n  rel tobias +small\n  learn tobias.plants\n  say: Tobias holds the wand to his ear with his eyes shut and smiles. \"It says it's far from home,\" he says. \"But it doesn't mind.\" The plants agree. You almost hear it.\n> right: Get on with your work\n  study +tiny\n  rel tobias -tiny\n  say: You put the wand away and get on with your herbology. The plants lean back to their own business, disappointed, and so does Tobias.\n\n@event item_thorn_wants\ntitle: It Wants to Be Used\nslot: night\npool: around_routine\ntone: grim\nwhen: has_item(thorn_wand)\nart: rose\ntags: magic\nscene: A blackthorn wand held in a student's hand in a dark dormitory, faintly green at its heart, its thorns bristling toward the wall and the room beyond it.\ntext: You wake at night with the thorn wand in your hand and no memory of picking it up. It's warm. It's telling you, in the way wands tell you things, about the student who laughed at you at supper, asleep two doors down, and how much life a sleeper doesn't need until morning.\n> left: Put it away in a drawer\n  mood -small\n  say: You shut it in the drawer and lie awake listening to it not be quiet. At breakfast the student who laughed is fine, and as rude as ever. You're not, quite.\n> right: Just a little of theirs\n  alignment -small\n  mana +small\n  say: You point it at the wall between you, and something comes through, warm and green. You feel wonderful. At breakfast they're yawning into their porridge, puzzled, and you watch them yawn. The thorn wand is cool again, satisfied, for now.\n\n@event item_thorn_brask\ntitle: Brask Smells It\nplace: stables\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: has_item(thorn_wand) and met(brask)\nweight: 10\nart: rose\ntags: item nature\ncast:\n  brask: brask where around(brask)\nscene: A stern professor in a stable holding out her hand for a blackthorn wand, a student hesitating.\ntext: Professor Brask stops beside you and sniffs, like a horse. \"Thorn,\" she says. \"Blackthorn. Where did you get it?\" She holds out her hand. \"I know that wand. I know the woman who cuts them. Give it to me, and I'll snap it, and we'll say no more.\"\n> left: Hand it over\n  take thorn_wand\n  alignment +small\n  rel brask +small\n  say: Brask snaps it over her knee. It screams, very faintly. \"Good,\" she says, and drops the pieces in the stove, and gives you a hippogriff to muck out, as a kindness.\n> right: Argue you can handle it\n  rel brask -small\n  guile +tiny\n  say: \"Can you,\" says Brask flatly. She doesn't take it. She doesn't say anything else, either, and she doesn't need to.\n\n@event item_thorn_snap\ntitle: Snap It\nslot: midnight\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: has_item(thorn_wand)\nweight: 6\nart: rose\ntags: item magic\nscene: A student alone on a castle rampart at midnight, holding a blackthorn wand bent nearly to breaking.\ntext: Alone on the ramparts, at midnight, you take out the thorn wand and look at it. It knows what you're thinking. It's very still, the way a cat is still.\n> left: Snap it\n  take thorn_wand\n  alignment +small\n  mood +small\n  say: It takes both hands. It doesn't want to go, and then it goes, with a crack like ice. You drop the pieces over the parapet and feel lighter than you have in months.\n> right: Not yet\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: You put it away. It's warm against your side all the way back to bed, purring, if wands purr.\n\n@event item_locket_midnight\ntitle: The Locket Opens\nslot: midnight\npool: around_routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: has_item(moonpetal_locket)\nart: moon\ntags: nature\nscene: A plain silver locket opening on its own in a student's palm at midnight, a pressed silver flower glowing inside.\ntext: At the twelfth bell the moonpetal locket opens in your hand on its own. The pressed petal inside glows, very faintly, and for a moment you smell the Hush: cold stone, moonlight, silence.\n> left: Close it gently\n  mood +tiny\n  say: You close it. The glow stays on your fingers for a while, like pollen.\n> right: Let it glow\n  mana +small\n  health -tiny\n  say: You hold it open until the glow fades, and you're full of quiet, and very cold, and you sleep badly and well.\n\n@event item_silk_corvin\ntitle: Corvin Is Jealous\npool: around_routine\ntone: light\nwhen: has_item(spinner_silk_robe) and met(corvin)\nart: spider\ntags: social\ncast:\n  corvin: corvin where around(corvin)\nscene: A lanky student in a patched coat circling another student in a shimmering grey silk robe, looking deeply envious.\ntext: Corvin walks round you twice. \"That,\" he says, \"is Spinner silk. You can't buy Spinner silk. You have to...\" He stops. \"You didn't.\" He looks at you with a whole new kind of respect, and also as if calculating what the robe would fetch.\n> left: Let him touch the sleeve\n  rel corvin +small\n  say: He touches the sleeve with one finger, reverently. \"I would sell my grandfather's deck for this,\" he says. \"Don't tell him I said that. He's dead. He'd still mind.\"\n> right: Keep it out of his reach\n  guile +tiny\n  rel corvin -tiny\n  say: You step back. Corvin grins. \"Smart,\" he says. \"I'd have had it off you by Thursday.\"\n\n@event item_grimoire_isolde\ntitle: The Headmistress Asks\nactor: isolde\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nwhen: has_item(outcast_grimoire) and flag(outcast_beaten)\nweight: 20\nart: book\ntags: item magic\nscene: A silver-haired headmistress on a castle stair, looking at a black-bound book in a student's hands, her face very still.\ntext: The headmistress stops you on the stair. She doesn't look at you. She looks at the black grimoire. \"Absalom Vrell,\" she says. \"Expelled long before my time. His name is still in the ledger, crossed out. I wondered when he'd find a way to give that away.\" Now she looks at you. \"What do you mean to do with it?\"\n> left: Offer it to her\n  take outcast_grimoire\n  alignment +small\n  rel isolde +small\n  say: She takes it without touching the pages and locks it in a drawer. \"Thank you,\" she says. \"Some things are safest where people can see them not being used.\" She means it as praise. You think.\n> right: Keep it, and say so\n  alignment -tiny\n  rel isolde -tiny\n  renown +tiny\n  say: She looks at you for a long time. \"Then use it as he never did,\" she says, \"carefully.\" She doesn't ask for it again. That's somehow worse.\n"},{"path":"content/events/whisperwood/delve.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The Whisperwood delve (world expansion 1, plan §1): step off the path and go card by card into the wood, deeper\n#  with each one you get past, and turn back whenever you like. What you carry out (the haul) is paid at the edge.\n#\n#  Counters, reset on each entry: ww_depth (how deep), ww_haul (what you carry), ww_grace (falls you can survive:\n#  1, +1 with a close friend or partner around, +1 with the fox guide), ww_hush (1 on a midnight delve).\n#  ww_deepest persists. Bands: 1 at depth 1–2 (apprentice), 2 at 3–4 (journeyman), 3 at 5–6 (adept); depth 7 is\n#  dusk by day or the Hush's gate at midnight; band 4 at 8–9 (expert-, midnight only); the Hush's heart at 10.\n#\n#  Every delve card is `trigger: queued`, tagged `ww`, and gates its band in `when`. Getting past one runs\n#  `ww_depth += 1` and `queue any tagged ww now`. Left always turns back (`ww_way_home`, the payout). A graced loss\n#  queues `ww_stumble` (grace, health and haul pay for it; you go on at the same depth); without grace, or on a\n#  hazard marked ⚠ (no second chances), it's `ww_lost`. Things that talk get a negotiation first; a fight follows\n#  only if the talk goes badly. Fights and negotiations are registered in combat/setup/foes.mjs and\n#  src/negotiation.js. Hollow Star premonitions here are eerie asides only: no `find` of an arc clue, no clock.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The edge, the way home, and falls ----------------\n\n# The delve's start cards: on a visit they come after the forest's routine cards (order: last), so the delve they start\n# is the visit's last thing. How often they come up is unchanged.\n@pool whisperwood_routine_repeat1\nplace: whisperwood\nclass: routine\nrepeat: 1\ntags: nature\norder: last\n\n@event ww_edge\ntitle: Off the Path\npool: whisperwood_routine_repeat1\ntone: eerie\nart: tree\ntags: danger\nscene: A narrow forest path at the edge of an old wood, fog between the trunks, a student looking off the path into the green dark.\ntext: {ww_deepest == 0: The path through the Whisperwood is safe, everyone says. Off it, the wood goes down and down, and things grow there that Pell pays good money for. So do other things.|{ww_deepest >= 10: The wood knows you now. The path bends politely toward the deep places, as if to save you the trouble.|{~The path, and off it, the deep green dark you've been into before.|Fog between the trunks, and the deep wood breathing behind it.|The wood is quiet at the edge. Further in, it never is.}}} {flag(ww_stag_friend): The shadow stag will meet you halfway now.|{flag(ww_trail): You know where the bee-marked trail begins.|}}\n> left: Stay on the path\n  say: {~You keep to the path, where the wood only mutters.|Not today. The path is quite interesting enough.|You stay on the path. The dark between the trees watches you not go in.}\n> right: Step off the path\n  hint: A delve: press on card by card, turn back any time.\n  ww_haul = 0\n  ww_hush = 0\n  ww_grace = 1\n  if bonds_around(close) + bonds_around(partner) > 0:\n    ww_grace += 1\n  if flag(ww_fox_guide):\n    ww_grace += 1\n  if flag(ww_stag_friend):\n    ww_depth = 5\n  elif flag(ww_trail):\n    ww_depth = 3\n  else:\n    ww_depth = 1\n  if seen(jit_maud_warning) == 0 and not flag(skip_intros):\n    queue jit_maud_warning now\n  queue any in ww_delve now\n  say: {flag(ww_stag_friend): A shadow moves between the trunks and waits. You follow it down past the easy places, into the heartwood.|{flag(ww_trail): You find the first little carved bee on a birch, and follow the trail straight into the thicket.|{bonds_around(partner) + bonds_around(close) > 0: You step off the path, and someone you trust steps off after you.|You step off the path. The mutter of the wood drops to a whisper.}}}\n\n@event ww_edge_night\ntitle: The Hush Is Awake\npool: whisperwood_routine_repeat1\nslot: midnight\ntone: eerie\nwhen: flag(ww_midnight_known)\nart: moon\ntags: danger\nscene: A forest at midnight under a pale moon, the trees silvered, a deep silence pooling between them like water.\ntext: {flag(ww_hush_seen): The wood at midnight, and deep in it the Hush, awake and listening.|Midnight in the Whisperwood. You were told the deep part keeps the castle's hours. Somewhere ahead, the forest has gone quiet in a way it never is by day.} {flag(ww_mastery): Maud's old map is in your pocket.|}\n> left: Not tonight\n  say: {~You stand at the edge until the cold gets in, and go no further.|You listen to the silence for a while, and keep to the path.|Not tonight. The Hush can wait; it's good at it.}\n> right: Go down into the dark\n  hint: A midnight delve: the Hush lies past the heartwood.\n  ww_haul = 0\n  ww_hush = 1\n  ww_grace = 1\n  if bonds_around(close) + bonds_around(partner) > 0:\n    ww_grace += 1\n  if flag(ww_fox_guide):\n    ww_grace += 1\n  if flag(ww_stag_friend) or flag(ww_mastery):\n    ww_depth = 5\n  elif flag(ww_trail):\n    ww_depth = 3\n  else:\n    ww_depth = 1\n  queue any in ww_delve now\n  say: {flag(ww_mastery): You follow Maud's map down by moonlight, past everything that would have stopped you once.|{flag(ww_stag_friend): The shadow stag is waiting by the path, darker than the dark. It leads you down.|You step off the path. At midnight the wood is a different wood, and it knows you came.}}\n\n@event ww_night_first\ntitle: The Wood After Midnight\npool: whisperwood_routine_repeat1\nslot: midnight\ntone: eerie\nwhen: not flag(ww_midnight_known)\nart: moon\ntags: mystery\nscene: The edge of a forest at midnight, the path pale under the moon, and beyond the first trees a darkness so still it looks solid.\ntext: The Whisperwood at midnight is not the wood you know. The path is the same, pale under the moon, but past the first trees the usual mutter of leaves and small feet stops, as if someone had shut a door on it. Deep in, the silence is listening.\n> left: Go back to bed\n  flag ww_midnight_known\n  find lead_the_hush\n  say: {~You back away from the edge, and the night noises start again behind you, all at once, as if released. You'll come back when you know what keeps those hours.|You don't go in. On the walk back the castle windows look very warm, and the silence behind you feels like something that will wait.}\n> right: Go towards the silence\n  hint: A midnight delve: the deep wood keeps the castle's hours.\n  flag ww_midnight_known\n  find lead_the_hush\n  queue ww_edge_night now\n  say: {~You walk until the silence presses on your ears. This is what Maud meant, or would have, if you'd asked: the deep wood keeps the castle's hours.|You follow the path to where the quiet starts. It isn't empty. It's awake.}\n\n@event ww_way_home\ntitle: The Way Home\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\nart: pouch\ntags: nature money\nscene: A tired student coming out of a dark forest into evening light, a lumpy foraging bag over one shoulder.\ntext: {ww_haul == 0: You come out of the trees with nothing but burrs and the feeling you were watched.|{ww_haul <= 2: You come out of the trees with a light bag: a few good mushrooms, a root Pell will want.|{ww_haul <= 4: You come out of the trees with a bag that clinks and rustles: roots, caps, a shed thing or two.|{ww_haul <= 6: You come out of the trees bent under a heavy bag. Pell will have to sit down.|You come out of the trees with more than you can carry, and carry it anyway.}}}} {ww_depth >= 8: The moon is low.|{ww_depth >= 5: Your hands still smell of heartwood.|}}\n> left: Sell it to Pell\n  hint: Crowns, by what you carry.\n  if ww_haul >= 7:\n    gold +huge\n  elif ww_haul >= 5:\n    gold +large\n  elif ww_haul >= 3:\n    gold +medium\n  elif ww_haul >= 1:\n    gold +small\n  say: {ww_haul == 0: Pell looks at your empty bag and offers you a biscuit, which is worse than nothing.|{~Pell weighs it all on the brass scale and pays without haggling, which means you should have asked for more.|Pell's daughters sort your bag in a minute and argue about it for ten. The crowns are fair.|Pell holds a cap up to the lamp, sniffs it, and pays you double for that one alone.}}\n  if ww_depth > ww_deepest:\n    ww_deepest = ww_depth\n  ww_depth = 0\n  ww_haul = 0\n> right: Keep it for the lab\n  hint: Alchemy and lore, by what you carry.\n  if ww_haul >= 7:\n    alchemy +large\n    lore +medium\n  elif ww_haul >= 5:\n    alchemy +medium\n    lore +small\n  elif ww_haul >= 3:\n    alchemy +medium\n  elif ww_haul >= 1:\n    alchemy +small\n  say: {ww_haul == 0: There's nothing to keep but the walk, and you keep that.|{~You spread it all out on a lab bench and spend the evening learning what each thing is, and what it's for, and what it does to your fingers.|You press, dry, label and brew until midnight. Half of it was worth more to Pell. The other half you'd never have understood.|You brew from the wood's own stock and the lab smells of forest for a week.}}\n  if ww_depth > ww_deepest:\n    ww_deepest = ww_depth\n  ww_depth = 0\n  ww_haul = 0\n\n@event ww_stumble\ntitle: Back on Your Feet\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 1\nart: hand\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A student sitting in wet bracken in a dark forest, catching their breath, a spilled foraging bag beside them.\non_show:\n  ww_grace -= 1\n  health -small\n  if ww_haul > 0:\n    ww_haul -= 1\ntext: {~You go down hard, and something spills out of your bag into the dark. When you get up, the wood is waiting to see what you do.|You come to your senses in the bracken, bruised and short of breath and short of something from your bag.|A hand, or a branch, or luck, catches you before the worst. You lose a little of what you carried getting free.} {ww_grace > 0: You've got one more fall in you, maybe.|That was your last easy fall.}\n> left: That's enough for today\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You pick up what's left and limp back toward the light.|You decide the wood has made its point.|You turn back, and the wood lets you, this time.}\n> right: Get up and go on\n  hint: Deeper: the next fall may not be kind.\n  queue any in ww_delve now\n  say: {~You brush off the leaves and go on, a little slower.|You get up. The wood seems surprised, and then interested.|You go on. Somewhere behind you, something stops following.}\n\n@event ww_lost\ntitle: Found at Dawn\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 1\nart: lantern\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A grey dawn in a forest, an old woman with a lantern helping a muddy, shivering student to their feet.\non_show:\n  health -large\n  ww_haul = 0\n  if ww_depth > ww_deepest:\n    ww_deepest = ww_depth\n  ww_depth = 0\ntext: {ww_hush == 1: You don't remember the rest of the night. At grey dawn a lantern bobs between the trees, and Old Maud is there with a blanket and a flask of something hot. \"There you are, dearie,\" she says, as if you'd been late for tea.|You don't remember the rest of the day. At dusk Professor Brask finds you in the ferns, cold and scratched and missing your bag. She looks you over the way she looks over a hurt foal.}\n> left: Thank her and go quietly\n  if ww_hush == 1:\n    meet maud\n    rel maud +small\n  else:\n    meet brask\n    rel brask +small\n  say: {ww_hush == 1: Maud walks you back to her cottage and doesn't ask a single question, which is worse than a lecture.|Brask walks you to the stables in silence. At the door she says, \"The wood doesn't hate you. It just doesn't notice you yet.\" It's the kindest thing she's ever said.}\n> right: Ask how she knew to look\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  if ww_hush == 1:\n    meet maud\n  else:\n    meet brask\n  say: {ww_hush == 1: \"The bees were cross,\" says Maud. \"They're always cross when someone's lost.\" She doesn't seem to be joking.|\"The owls were talking about you,\" says Brask. \"Unflatteringly.\" She doesn't seem to be joking.}\n\n# ---------------- Band 1: the Fringe (depth 1–2, apprentice) ----------------\n\n@thread ww_delve\nfrom: scene\n# The wood's leads join its Journal story (phase 4).\nclues: lead_heartwood lead_the_hush lead_spinner_silk\nname: The Whisperwood\npremise: The wood past the village goes deeper every time you go in.\njournal:\n  flag(ww_trail): You followed little carved bees a mile into the Whisperwood. You know whose bees they are.\n  flag(ww_bowed): You bowed in the bracken beside Professor Brask. Now the wood knows your face.\n  flag(ww_fox_guide): Ember's kin decided you'd do. A small smouldering tail walks with you off the path now.\n  flag(ww_stag_friend): The Shadow Stag lowered its great head to you.\n  flag(ww_heartwood): You asked the Elder Oak for its heartwood.\n  flag(ww_midnight_known): Deep in the wood there's a clearing where it's always midnight, its moonpetal waiting.\n  flag(ww_hush_seen): At midnight the silence opened, and you saw the Hush.\n  flag(ww_spinner_beaten): You fought the Spinner, queen of the Hush, and won.\n  flag(outcast_beaten): You faced Absalom Vrell by his lantern, and walked away.\n  seen(ww_hush_heart) > 0: You reached the heart of the Hush. The wood is yours, as much as a wood is ever anyone's.\nstatus:\n  resolved: seen(ww_hush_heart) > 0\n\n@event ww1_forage\ntitle: The Mushroom Ring\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: ww_depth >= 1 and ww_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: mushroom\ntags: ww nature\nscene: A perfect ring of pale mushrooms in a mossy forest clearing, a student kneeling with a knife and a basket.\ntext: {season == \"autumn\": A fairy ring in full flush, caps as big as saucers, more than you could carry.|A ring of pale caps in the moss, and among them the frilled ones Pell pays for.} Never step inside a ring, Maud says. She never says why.\n> left: Leave it and turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You leave the ring to whoever made it and head back to the path.|You count the caps, don't take any, and go home feeling oddly polite.}\n> right: Fill your bag from outside\n  health -tiny\n  ww_haul += 1\n  if season == \"autumn\":\n    ww_haul += 1\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You pick from the outside, kneeling in cold moss. Your knees are soaked and your bag is heavier.|You reach in for the best cap without stepping in. Something in the ring sighs. You move on, briskly.}\n\n@event ww1_bramble_ferret\ntitle: Something in Your Bag\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: ww_depth >= 1 and ww_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: paw\ntags: ww nature danger\nscene: A small bristly ferret with brambles tangled in its fur, half inside a student's foraging bag, glaring out.\ntext: A bramble ferret has got into your bag, and is eating {ww_haul > 0: your best find|your lunch}. It's all bristles and bad temper, and it fights like a pin cushion that has opinions.\n> left: Let it have it; turn back\n  if ww_haul > 0:\n    ww_haul -= 1\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You tip the bag out and let it keep what it wanted. You go home lighter and wiser.|You leave it to its meal and walk back, picking bristles out of your sleeve.}\n> right: Fight it for the bag\n  challenge combat apprentice:\n    win:\n      ww_haul += 1\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~It bolts, dropping what it stole and something shiny it stole earlier. You take both.|It gives up with a furious chitter and leaves behind its own hoard of nuts and one silver button.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~It nips, dodges, nips, and is gone with your bag's best corner. You're bleeding from places you didn't know could.|You never land a thing on it. It leaves you in the bracken, panting and full of small holes.}\n> up[item:honey_tin]: Bribe it with honey\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You open Maud's honey tin and hold out a fingerful. The ferret forgets the bag entirely, eats the honey, and follows you a mile, hoping.|One sniff of the silver-bee honey and the ferret is your friend for life, or at least until the honey runs out.}\n\n@event ww1_bristleback\ntitle: The Bristleback\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: ww_depth >= 1 and ww_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: paw\ntags: ww nature danger\nscene: A huge shaggy bear-like creature with a ridge of bristles along its back rising on its hind legs in a forest clearing.\ntext: A bristleback rears up out of the blackberries: a bear with a ridge of quills down its spine and a voice like a cellar door. It isn't hungry. It's offended. It swings slowly, and you can see each blow coming a long way off.\n> left: Back away slowly\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You back away the way Brask taught you: slowly, eyes down, apologising. It lets you go and goes back to its berries.|You leave the clearing to the bristleback, who seems to think that's the least you could do.}\n> right: Stand your ground\n  hint: It telegraphs its blows: brace on the swing.\n  challenge combat apprentice+:\n    win:\n      renown +tiny\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~It decides you're not worth the trouble and lumbers off with great dignity. Word gets round the foragers that you saw off a bristleback.|You weather three swings and land enough to make it think again. It goes, grumbling, and you go on, shaking.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~You see the swing coming the whole way and still don't move fast enough.|It knocks you into the blackberries, which is somehow the worst part.}\n> up[flame]: Wave fire at it\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~One flare of flame and the bristleback remembers an appointment elsewhere.|You light the air between you. It snorts at the smoke, deeply offended, and leaves.}\n\n@event ww1_bog\ntitle: The Sucking Bog\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: ww_depth >= 1 and ww_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: frog\ntags: ww nature danger\nscene: A student knee-deep in black bog water among reeds, reaching for a low branch, frogs watching from a log.\ntext: The moss looks solid until you're on it. Then you're knee-deep in black water that pulls, gently and with great patience, like a grandmother who wants you to stay for supper.\n> left: Haul yourself back out\n  health -tiny\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You haul yourself back the way you came, losing a boot and gaining a smell.|You get out backwards and go home with the bog all the way up to your knees.}\n> right: Wade on across\n  challenge feat apprentice:\n    win:\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You wade, haul, wade and come out the far side, black to the thighs and still going.|You find the tussocks under the water by feel, and cross like a heron. A muddy heron.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~The bog takes you to the waist, then to the chest. You get out, eventually, much colder and much humbler.|You lose your footing and go under. You come up spitting moss.}\n> up[tailwind]: Skim across\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~A gust under your feet and you skim the bog like a skipped stone. The frogs are very impressed.|You call a wind and run on it. Your boots barely get damp.}\n\n@event ww1_pixie_toll\ntitle: The Toll Stone\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: ww_depth >= 1 and ww_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: sparkle\ntags: ww nature\nscene: Several tiny winged pixies perched on a mossy stone across a forest path, one holding out a tiny hand for payment.\ntext: Three pixies sit on a mossy stone across the path. \"Toll,\" says the smallest. \"Toll,\" agrees the middle one. The third is eating a beetle and nods. Nobody has ever established what the toll is, only that it isn't what you offer.\n> left: Turn round\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You turn back. The pixies jeer, delighted, in tiny voices.|You don't have time to be fleeced by beetles' cousins. You go home.}\n> right: Haggle your way past\n  challenge negotiate apprentice:\n    win:\n      ww_haul += 1\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You argue them down to a compliment and a button, and they're so pleased they throw in a dropped cap from the ring.|You convince them that you are the toll, coming the other way. They pay you, confused, and wave you on.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~They take your offer, your next offer, and your bootlaces. Then they trip you anyway, on principle.|They don't care for your terms. They express this with pinecones, at speed.}\n> up[glamour]: Pay in fairy gold\n  alignment -tiny\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You pay in coins that will be leaves by morning. They bite them, nod, and let you by. You feel a little guilty, and a little proud.|You glamour a pebble into a crown. The smallest pixie looks at you for a long moment, then pockets it. Professional courtesy.}\n\n@event ww1_lost_firstyear\ntitle: A First-Year in the Ferns\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: ww_depth >= 1 and ww_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 2\nart: hand\ntags: ww nature social\nscene: A small tearful first-year student sitting in tall ferns in a forest, clutching a library book, looking up hopefully.\ntext: A first-year is sitting in the ferns, holding a library book on edible fungi and trying very hard not to cry. They've been lost for an hour. They have eaten nothing from the book, which is the first sensible thing they've done.\n> left: Walk them back out\n  alignment +small\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You walk them all the way back to the path and point them at the castle. They thank you four times. Your delve is over, and you don't mind much.|You take them home. They ask forty questions on the way, and you answer most of them honestly.}\n> right: Point the way and press on\n  mood -tiny\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You point them back toward the path and go on. You look back once. They're walking the right way. Probably.|You draw them a map on the flyleaf of the library book and go on. It's a good map. You keep thinking about it anyway.}\n\n@event ww1_hollow_whisper\ntitle: The Riddling Hollow\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 1 and ww_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature mystery\nscene: A hollow in an ancient oak trunk with a faint glow inside, a student leaning close to listen.\ntext: A hollow oak whispers as you pass. It's asking a riddle, the same one over and over, and it sounds as if it has been waiting a long time for anyone to try. The answer is not \"a tree\". You can tell that from the tone.\n> left: Leave it riddling; turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You leave the riddle unanswered. It follows you all the way to the path.|You go home. Halfway there you think of the answer, and it's too late, and it's probably wrong.}\n> right: Try an answer\n  challenge negotiate apprentice:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You answer, and argue the point when it quibbles. The oak goes quiet, then tells you something about the wood that you'll understand in a fortnight.|\"Close enough,\" it sighs, and opens a path you hadn't seen.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~Wrong, says the oak, and the path rearranges itself so that you walk into a ditch.|It laughs, a terrible creaking laugh, and you find you've been walking in circles for an hour.}\n\n@event ww1_quiet_path\ntitle: The Path Runs On\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: ww_depth >= 1 and ww_depth <= 2\nrepeat: 1\nart: leaf\ntags: ww nature\nscene: A deer path winding between moss-covered trees, dappled light, a student walking alone with a foraging bag.\ntext: {~A deer path, winding and quiet, with the castle bells faint behind you.|Birdsong, and moss, and nothing at all trying to eat you.|A stream to cross on stepping stones, and wild garlic along the bank.} For a while the wood is only a wood.\n> left: Turn back while it's easy\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You turn round while the going is good, and are home before the light changes.|You take the quiet as a hint and head back.|You turn back at the stream, with dry feet and a clear head.}\n> right: Go on\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You walk on, deeper, and the birdsong thins.|You go on, deeper, and the wood stops being only a wood.|You go on. The bells behind you stop being audible somewhere around the stream.}\n\n@event ww1_maud_blaze\ntitle: The Little Carved Bees\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: ww_depth >= 1 and ww_depth <= 2 and not flag(ww_trail)\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature mystery\nscene: A tiny bee carved into the bark of a birch tree, worn smooth by decades, a student tracing it with a finger.\ntext: On a birch, at knee height, someone has carved a tiny bee. There's another a few trees on, and another. They're old, smoothed by sixty years of bark growing round them, and they lead somewhere on purpose.\n> left: Note it and turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You remember the birch and go home. The bees will still be there.|You touch the first bee for luck and head back.}\n> right: Follow the bees\n  challenge research apprentice:\n    win:\n      flag ww_trail\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~Bee to bee, a mile in, the trail drops straight past the fringe to the thicket's edge. Next time you can start here. You know exactly whose bees these are.|You lose the trail twice and find it twice. By the end you could walk it blind.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~You lose the bees in a hollow full of brambles and spend an hour getting out.|The trail runs out at a fallen tree. Climbing round it, you fall in a ditch.}\n\n@event ww1_owl_feather\ntitle: The Barn Owl's Tree\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: ww_depth >= 1 and ww_depth <= 2 and not has_item(owl_quill)\nart: feather\ntags: ww nature item\nscene: A tall dead oak with a single white barn-owl feather caught on a high branch, a student looking up at it.\ntext: High in a dead oak, a single barn-owl feather is caught on a twig, white as paper and long as your hand. Grimsby once told you an owl's flight feather makes the best quill there is. He didn't say anything about the climb.\n> left: Leave it to the wind; turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You leave the feather where it is. When you look back, it's gone.|It's a long way up and a long way home. You choose home.}\n> right: Climb for it\n  challenge feat apprentice+:\n    win:\n      give owl_quill\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You climb, reach, nearly fall, reach again. You come down with the feather and a fine view of the castle. Cut to a nib, it will make a quill worth having.|The branch breaks just as your fingers close. You land in leaves, feather intact.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~The dead wood gives under your boot halfway up. You come down a good deal faster than you went up.|You get high enough to see the feather is just out of reach. Then the branch has opinions.}\n\n@event ww1_brask_bows\ntitle: Brask in the Bracken\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: ww_depth >= 1 and ww_depth <= 2 and not flag(ww_bowed) and term\nart: paw\ntags: ww nature social\nscene: A sturdy older professor in a riding coat bowing low to something unseen in tall bracken, in a misty forest.\ntext: Professor Brask is standing in the bracken, bowing deeply to nothing you can see. \"Don't stare,\" she says without straightening. \"Bow. It's watching you.\" You can't see what it is. You're fairly sure that's the point.\n> left: Back away and go home\n  meet brask\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You back away. Brask doesn't watch you go, but the bracken does.|You retreat to the path. Next lesson, Brask gives you a look that says she noticed.}\n> right: Bow with her\n  meet brask\n  rel brask +small\n  flag ww_bowed\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You bow, low, and hold it until Brask straightens. Something in the bracken moves away, satisfied. \"Good,\" says Brask. \"Do that to everything down there. Most of it's older than manners.\"|You bow. Brask nods once. \"Now the wood knows your face,\" she says, and walks off, which is apparently the lesson.}\n\n# ---------------- Band 2: the Thicket (depth 3–4, journeyman) ----------------\n\n@event ww2_grumpy_oak\ntitle: The Oak You Kicked\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 4\nrepeat: 1\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature\nscene: A gnarled oak with a scowling face in its bark leaning over a forest path, a student holding a stubbed foot.\ntext: You stub your toe on a root, and say something about it. The root's oak takes this badly. It has a face in its bark now, and the face is scowling, and the path ahead is suddenly a great deal of oak.\n> left: Retreat with dignity\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You back away, apologising to the general direction of the oak, and limp home.|You decide the oak has won this one and turn back.}\n> right: Apologise properly\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      ww_haul += 1\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You apologise to the oak at length, with references. It harrumphs, drops you an acorn the size of a fist as a mark of favour, and lets you by.|Your apology is so formal that the oak is moved. It lifts its root for you, and a hoard of truffles under it.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww2_grumpy_oak_fight now\n      say: {~The apology goes badly. It turns out you also stood on its cousin.|The oak hears you out and then creaks something that is very clearly \"no\".}\n> up[item:heartwood_wand]: Show the heartwood\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You hold up the heartwood wand. The oak's scowl softens into something almost grandfatherly, and it lifts a root to let you by.|The oak sees the heartwood and goes quiet. Then it bows, creaking, and steps aside.}\n\n@event ww2_grumpy_oak_fight\ntitle: The Oak Swings\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 1\nart: tree\ntags: nature danger\nscene: An angry oak tree swinging a great bough down at a student, roots erupting from the ground around their feet.\ntext: The oak's roots come up out of the path and close round your ankles, and a bough the size of a boat lifts overhead. Oaks are slow. This one has been practising.\n> left: Tear free and run\n  health -tiny\n  if ww_haul > 0:\n    ww_haul -= 1\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You leave a boot in the roots and run. The oak keeps the boot.|You wrench free and run, shedding half your bag. The oak looks satisfied.}\n> right: Fight it\n  hint: It roots you, then swings: guard early.\n  challenge combat journeyman+:\n    win:\n      ww_depth += 1\n      queue any in ww_delve now\n      say: {~You burn, hack and dodge until the oak decides it's had enough exercise for one century, and goes back to scowling at nothing.|The bough comes down where you were. The oak stops, as if surprised that you're still here, and lets you go on.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~The roots hold, the bough falls, and the oak goes back to being a tree, slightly smug.|You never get your feet free. The bough finds you twice.}\n\n@event ww2_briar_maze\ntitle: The Thorn Maze\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 4\nrepeat: 1\nart: rose\ntags: ww nature danger\nscene: A maze of tall thorny briars in a dim forest, narrow green corridors branching off in every direction.\ntext: The briars here have grown into walls taller than you, with corridors between them that turn and fork and double back. Somebody planted this, a long time ago, on purpose, and they weren't fond of visitors.\n> left: Back out the way you came\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You retrace your steps while you still can, and lose only your sleeve.|You find the way out, and decide that's an achievement in itself.}\n> right: Find the way through\n  challenge research journeyman:\n    win:\n      lore +tiny\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~Left at every fork, then right at the one with the dead rose: the old gardener's rule, and now you know it. You come out the far side scratched but triumphant.|You notice that the moss grows on the side the old gardener wanted you to see, and follow it through.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~The corridors turn back on themselves until you're dizzy. You come out bleeding from a hundred tiny cuts.|You take a wrong turn into a dead end so full of thorns it has its own weather.}\n> up[item:lodestone_pendant]: Follow the lodestone\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~The lodestone turns, steady and patient, and you follow it through every fork without a scratch.|You let the pendant lead. It knows north, and north is through.}\n\n@event ww2_stag_beetle\ntitle: The Stag Beetle\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 4\nrepeat: 1\nart: paw\ntags: ww nature danger\nscene: An enormous armoured stag beetle the size of a pony lowering its antler-like jaws in a forest clearing.\ntext: A stag beetle the size of a pony is guarding a fallen log. Its jaws are as long as your arm and its shell would turn a sword. It lowers its head and starts to back up, which, you realise, is a run-up.\n> left: Leave it the log; turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You leave the beetle its log. It watches you all the way out of the clearing, revving.|You go home. Some fights aren't yours, and that one was very much the beetle's.}\n> right: Take it on\n  hint: It charges in two beats: step aside on the second.\n  challenge combat journeyman+:\n    win:\n      ww_haul += 2\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~It charges, misses, charges, misses, and gives up in disgust, shedding a wing-case like polished jet. Pell will weep.|You catch it mid-charge. It retreats under its log, leaving a wing-case and some dignity behind.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~The second charge catches you square. You fly further than you ever have on a broom.|It charges. You dodge the wrong way. You see a great many leaves, very fast.}\n> up[shadowstep]: Step out of its way\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You step sideways into shadow just as it charges. It thunders past into a tree and stays there, thinking about it.|You blink out of its path and it charges straight through where you were. You leave while it's still confused.}\n\n@event ww2_fog_wall\ntitle: The Fog Wall\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 4\nrepeat: 1\nart: rain\ntags: ww nature mystery\nscene: A solid-looking wall of white fog blocking a forest path, tree branches vanishing into it, a student holding out a hand.\ntext: The fog in front of you isn't drifting. It's standing, like a wall, and when you put out a hand it's cold and faintly springy. Behind it the wood goes on. Through it, perhaps, if you can talk it into letting you.\n> left: Turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You don't argue with fog. You go home.|You leave the wall to its standing and walk back toward the light.}\n> right: Part it with a spell\n  challenge spell journeyman:\n    win:\n      arcana +tiny\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You push, and the fog parts like a curtain, sulkily, and lets you through. You'll remember how it gave.|You find the knot at the heart of the fog and pick it. The wall unravels into ordinary mist.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww_way_home now\n      say: {~The fog swallows you, turns you round three times and puts you out at the edge of the wood, exactly where you started. It wasn't unkind about it.|You walk into the fog and out of it again, a mile back toward the castle. The wood is done with you for today.}\n> up[item:stag_tine]: Raise the tine\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You hold up the stag's tine and the fog draws back from it as from a lantern.|The fog sees the tine and parts, politely, the way a crowd parts for a duchess.}\n\n@event ww2_waystone\ntitle: The Waystone\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 4\nrepeat: 1\nart: rune\ntags: ww nature mystery\nscene: A weathered standing stone in a forest, little offerings of nuts, coins and ribbons piled at its base.\ntext: A standing stone, worn smooth, with offerings heaped at its foot: hazelnuts, a ribbon, a copper crown older than the castle. Foragers leave something here on the way in. Nobody you've asked will say what for.\n> left: Turn back here\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You leave the stone and its offerings alone and turn back.|You decide the stone is a sign, and the sign says home.}\n> right: Leave an offering\n  hint: {ww_haul > 0: Something from your bag, for a little luck.|Your bag is empty: a coin from your purse, then.}\n  say: {ww_haul > 0: {~You leave the best thing in your bag. Walking on, you feel watched over, which is a nicer feeling than watched.|You set down your offering with the others. A wind goes through the trees, approving.}|{~Your bag is empty, so you leave a crown from your purse. Walking on, you feel watched over, which is a nicer feeling than watched.|You set a coin down with the others, warm from your pocket. A wind goes through the trees, approving.}}\n  if ww_haul > 0:\n    ww_haul -= 1\n  else:\n    gold -tiny\n  ww_grace += 1\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n> up[sleight]: Palm a few offerings\n  alignment -small\n  ww_haul += 2\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~Nobody's looking. A copper, a ribbon, a handful of nuts slip into your bag. The wood goes very quiet as you walk on.|You palm the old crown. It's cold in your pocket for the rest of the day, and something keeps pace with you, just out of sight.}\n\n@event ww2_lodestone\ntitle: The Iron Hollow\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: light\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 4 and not has_item(lodestone_pendant)\nart: compass\ntags: ww nature item\nscene: A forest hollow where every fallen leaf points the same direction, a dark stone half-buried at its centre.\ntext: In this hollow every fallen leaf lies pointing the same way, and your boots feel heavier. At the bottom, half-buried in roots, is a dark stone the size of an egg, and every leaf points at it.\n> left: Leave it buried\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~Some things are buried for a reason. You go home.|You leave the stone to its leaves and turn back.}\n> right: Dig it out\n  challenge feat journeyman:\n    win:\n      give lodestone_pendant\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You dig until your nails are black and work it free. It turns, slowly, in your palm, to face north. A bit of cord and it's a pendant.|It comes up with a sucking sound and a lot of roots. In your hand it turns, gently, like a compass waking.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~The roots won't let go, and neither will you, until the hollow wall comes down on you.|You dig too deep and the hollow fills with black water.}\n\n@event ww2_stone_words\ntitle: Words on a Stone\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 4 and spell_level(lodestar) < 1\nart: rune\ntags: ww nature magic\nscene: A moss-covered flat stone in a forest clearing carved with faint glowing letters, a student kneeling to read.\ntext: A flat stone, mossed over, with letters carved deep in it. When you scrape the moss away, the letters are a spell: a way to gather light around you, breath by breath, until it stands between you and harm. Whoever left it here wanted it found by someone patient.\n> left: Copy nothing; go home\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You cover the words again with moss and go home. They'll keep.|You leave the stone its secret.}\n> right: Work out the spell\n  challenge research journeyman+:\n    win:\n      learn_spell lodestar\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You read it three times and the light begins to gather at your shoulders. Lodestar. It feels like being walked home.|It takes an hour on your knees in the moss. By the end, light gathers when you breathe in, and stays.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~You get the first line and the second line, and the third line is wrong in a way that knocks you flat.|The light gathers, then goes off like a lamp dropped on stone.}\n\n@event ww2_thornwitch\ntitle: The Woman Among the Thorns\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 4\nrepeat: 2\nart: rose\ntags: ww danger magic\nscene: A thin woman in a thorn-trimmed cloak with her hand on a withering birch tree, a sickly green light flowing from the tree into her palm.\ntext: A thin woman in a cloak sewn with thorns has her palm on a birch, and the birch is dying: its leaves curl and fall as you watch, and the woman looks younger with every leaf. She sees you. She doesn't stop. \"Siphon,\" she says. \"They'd never teach it to you up there.\"\n> left: Leave her to it\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You back away and go home, and the dying birch stays in your head all evening.|You leave. She laughs, softly, without turning round.}\n> right: Talk to her\n  challenge negotiate journeyman+:\n    win:\n      queue ww2_thornwitch_offer now\n      say: {~You talk. She listens, and stops, and takes her hand off the birch to look at you properly.|She's lonely, it turns out, and proud, and she likes being argued with. The birch gets a reprieve.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww2_thornwitch_fight now\n      say: {~She tires of you halfway through a sentence and turns her hand toward you instead.|\"Enough,\" she says, and the thorns on her cloak lift like hackles.}\n> up[flame]: Stop her\n  challenge combat journeyman+:\n    win:\n      alignment +small\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You drive her off the birch and into the thorns. She goes, cursing, and the birch puts out one green leaf. You'll tell Brask.|She runs. The birch is too far gone, but the next one won't be. Brask will want to know.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~She turns your fire on you, and then turns to you, and then you don't remember.|She takes from you what she was taking from the birch. You wake much later, very cold.}\n\n@event ww2_thornwitch_fight\ntitle: Thorns\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 1\nart: rose\ntags: danger magic\nscene: A woman in a thorn cloak raising both hands as brambles burst from the forest floor toward a student.\ntext: Brambles come up out of the ground at her gesture. She's going to try Siphon on you next, and you have seen what it does to a birch. ⚠ Down here, nobody comes to catch you.\n> left: Run for it\n  health -small\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You run, and the brambles tear at you all the way to the path. She doesn't follow. She doesn't need to.|You run. Her laugh follows you further than she does.}\n> right: Fight her\n  hint: She heals by holding Siphon: break the hold.\n  challenge combat journeyman+:\n    win:\n      alignment +small\n      ww_depth += 1\n      queue any in ww_delve now\n      say: {~You break her hold and she breaks, fleeing into the thorns. The birch behind her is grey, but you'll tell Brask, and it won't happen here again.|She goes down into the brambles and doesn't come up. When you look, she's gone, and so are the thorns.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~Her hand finds your arm, and the cold comes in, and you don't remember the rest.|She drinks from you until you can't stand. Then she leaves you, generously, alive.}\n\n@event ww2_thornwitch_offer\ntitle: Her Lesson\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 2\nart: spell_siphon\ntags: danger magic\nscene: A thin woman in a thorn cloak holding out a blackthorn wand to a student, a grey birch behind her.\ntext: \"You have nerve,\" the thornwitch says. \"I could use an apprentice with nerve.\" She holds out a wand of blackthorn, still bristling. \"I'll teach you Siphon. It's only life. There's so much of it about.\" Behind her the birch is grey to the root.\n> left: Tell her to go\n  alignment +small\n  ww_depth += 1\n  queue any in ww_delve now\n  say: {~\"Go,\" you say, \"and don't come back to this wood.\" She shrugs, as if you'd refused a biscuit, and walks off into the thorns. You'll tell Brask.|She looks at you for a long time. Then she goes, and the thorns go with her.}\n> right: Take the lesson\n  hint: A forbidden spell, and a wand that wants using.\n  alignment -medium\n  if spell_level(siphon) < 1:\n    learn_spell siphon\n  if not has_item(thorn_wand):\n    give thorn_wand\n  ww_depth += 1\n  queue any in ww_delve now\n  say: {~She shows you. It's easy, which is the worst part. You walk on with the thorn wand in your belt and a dead birch behind you.|You learn it on a fern, which curls and greys. \"There,\" she says. \"Now you know something they don't.\" The thorn wand pricks your palm.}\n\n@event ww2_embers_kin\ntitle: Ember's Kin\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 4 and not flag(ww_fox_guide)\nrepeat: 1\nart: fox\ntags: ww nature\nscene: Several small foxes with glowing ember-like tails sitting in a ring in a dark forest, watching a student.\ntext: A ring of small foxes is sitting in the dark, tails glowing like banked coals. {has(familiar_fox): Your own fox goes forward and touches noses with the eldest.|The eldest looks at you as if it has heard about you.} Foxes like these, Maud says, will walk anyone through the wood who can hold a conversation.\n> left: Bow and turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You bow to the foxes and go home. Their tails glow in the dark behind you all the way.|You leave them their ring. One follows you as far as the path, then vanishes.}\n> right: Hold a conversation\n  challenge negotiate journeyman:\n    win:\n      flag ww_fox_guide\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You talk, they yip, and the eldest decides you'll do. From now on, a small smouldering tail will turn up whenever you step off the path, and steer you out of the worst of it.|You trade news for news. The foxes are terrible gossips. One of them will walk with you from now on.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~You say the wrong thing, whatever it was. The foxes vanish, and so does the path.|The eldest yawns at you. A tail flicks, and you're lost.}\n> up[rapport]: Meet them as equals\n  flag ww_fox_guide\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You sit down in their ring as if you'd been invited, and talk to them like old friends. They're delighted. From now on, one of them walks with you whenever you step off the path.|The eldest laughs, a fox's laugh. \"Manners,\" it says. \"At last.\" One of them will walk with you now.}\n\n@event ww2_stag_tine\ntitle: The Shed Tine\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 4 and flag(ww_bowed) and not has_item(stag_tine)\nart: paw\ntags: ww nature item\nscene: A single dark antler tine lying on moss inside a ring of stones, faint shadow curling off it like smoke.\ntext: In a ring of stones lies a single tine from the shadow stag's antlers, shed and left as if for someone. It's heavy, and darker than its shadow. You bowed once, in the bracken. Perhaps that was noticed.\n> left: Leave it be\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You bow to the tine, and leave it, and go home. That feels right too.|You don't touch it. The ring of stones seems to approve.}\n> right: Lift it\n  challenge feat journeyman+:\n    win:\n      give stag_tine\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~It weighs as much as a sack of flour and as little as a feather, depending on how you hold it. You find the right way. Wild things will look at it before they look at you.|You lift it. Somewhere in the wood, something very large breathes out, satisfied.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~It's too heavy, and then suddenly not, and you fall backwards over the ring of stones.|You lift it and it lifts you, and sets you down a long way off, bruised.}\n\n@event ww2_deer_path\ntitle: Deeper\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 3 and ww_depth <= 4\nrepeat: 1\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature\nscene: A dim forest thicket with dense old trees, a narrow path between them, pale fungus glowing faintly on the trunks.\ntext: {~The thicket closes overhead and the daylight goes green, then grey.|Pale fungus glows on the trunks in rings, like lamps somebody keeps lit.|A path of old flagstones, sunk in moss, going down.} The wood is quieter here, and it's listening.\n> left: Turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You turn round. The light comes back a shade at a time.|You go home while you still remember the way.|You climb back up the flagstones toward the day.}\n> right: Go deeper\n  ww_haul += 1\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You pick glowing caps from the trunks as you go, and your fingers glow for an hour.|You gather pale fungus from the lamp-rings, and the trees let you.|Down the flagstones, gathering what grows between them.}\n\n# ---------------- Band 3: the Heartwood (depth 5–6, adept) ----------------\n\n@event ww3_shadow_stag\ntitle: The Shadow Stag\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 5 and ww_depth <= 6 and not flag(ww_stag_friend)\nrepeat: 1\nart: paw\ntags: ww nature danger\nscene: A huge stag made of living shadow standing between ancient trees, its antlers lost in the dark canopy.\ntext: The shadow stag stands across the way, a stag-shaped hole in the light with antlers that go up into the canopy and don't stop. It doesn't move. It's waiting to see what you are.\n> left: Back away\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You back away, step by step. It watches until you're out of sight, and then a while longer.|You leave the heartwood to the stag. It seems to think that's correct.}\n> right: Bow, and hold your nerve\n  hint: {flag(ww_bowed): You learned to bow from Brask.|A test of nerve.}\n  if flag(ww_bowed):\n    flag ww_stag_friend\n    ww_depth += 1\n    next\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate adept:\n      win:\n        flag ww_stag_friend\n        ww_depth += 1\n        next\n      lose:\n        queue ww3_shadow_stag_fight now\n  say: {flag(ww_stag_friend): You bow and don't look away, and the stag lowers its great head. From now on it'll meet you at the edge and walk you down past the easy places.|You bow and hold its gaze too long. The stag lowers its antlers, not in greeting.}\n\n@event ww3_shadow_stag_fight\ntitle: Antlers\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 1\nart: paw\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A shadow stag charging between dark trees, antlers lowered, a student raising a wand.\ntext: The stag lowers its antlers. Brask would tell you to run. Brask would also tell you, afterwards, that running from a shadow stag is how you teach it to chase.\n> left: Run for the path\n  health -small\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You run. It doesn't chase. Somehow that's worse.|You run until the trees thin. When you look back, there's nothing but shadow.}\n> right: Stand and fight\n  challenge combat adept+:\n    win:\n      alignment -small\n      ww_depth += 1\n      queue any in ww_delve now\n      say: {~It turns away at last, wounded more in its pride than its shadow. You go on, but the wood feels colder, as if you'd broken something it liked.|You drive it off. Nothing about that feels like winning.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~The antlers lift you and set you down a long way off, almost gently.|It charges through you like a cold wind. You lie in the leaves for a long time.}\n\n@event ww3_elder_oak\ntitle: The Elder Oak\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 5 and ww_depth <= 6 and not flag(ww_heartwood)\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature mystery\nscene: An immense ancient oak in a forest clearing, its bark like a face with closed eyes, a student standing small before it.\ntext: The elder oak is so old the other trees grow round it like courtiers. Its bark is a face with its eyes shut. You ask, politely, how old it is, and it opens a split in its trunk to show you its rings. Sixty rings in from the bark, one ring is pale as frost. \"The cold spring,\" it says, and nothing more.\n> left: Bow out and go home\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You bow, and leave the old thing to its long thoughts.|You go home thinking about one pale ring among a thousand brown ones.}\n> right: Ask it for heartwood\n  hint: A splinter of it would make a wand.\n  challenge negotiate adept:\n    win:\n      flag ww_heartwood\n      find lead_heartwood\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~It considers you for so long you think it's fallen asleep. Then a splinter of heartwood, red-gold and warm, drops into your hand. Mir could carve this.|\"Use it well,\" it creaks, \"or not at all.\" The splinter in your palm is warm as a living thing.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww3_elder_oak_fight now\n      say: {~It closes its split, and its eyes open instead.|\"Take,\" it says, \"is not a word for trees.\" Its roots begin to move.}\n\n@event ww3_elder_oak_fight\ntitle: The Slow Fall\ntrigger: queued\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 1\nart: tree\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A colossal oak bough slowly descending toward a student in a forest clearing, roots coiling across the ground.\ntext: The elder oak doesn't hurry. It lifts one great bough, slowly, and you have all the time in the world to watch it come down. Its roots are moving too. The bough will take three breaths to land.\n> left: Get out from under it\n  health -tiny\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You run out from under the falling bough and keep running. The ground shakes behind you like a door slammed in a cellar.|You leave. The oak goes back to sleep before the bough has finished falling.}\n> right: Hold your ground\n  hint: Answer each slow fall; its roots chip at you all along.\n  challenge combat adept+:\n    win:\n      ww_depth += 1\n      queue any in ww_delve now\n      say: {~You weather every fall, and the oak stops, surprised. It doesn't give you any heartwood. It doesn't stop you going on, either.|The oak lets the last bough rest. You've won nothing but the right to pass.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~The third fall finds you. The roots carry you, gently, to the edge of the clearing and set you down.|You misjudge the third breath.}\n\n@event ww3_masked_circle\ntitle: The Masked Circle\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: grim\nwhen: ww_depth >= 5 and ww_depth <= 6\nrepeat: 2\nart: mask\ntags: ww danger magic\nscene: Three figures in plain white masks standing in a chalk circle in a dark forest clearing, one pointing at a cringing hare.\ntext: Three figures in plain white masks stand in a chalk circle, practising on a hare. One says \"again,\" and the hare does what it just did, again, against its will. The chalk marks round the circle are copied from somewhere, badly. They haven't seen you yet.\n> left: Slip away\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You slip back into the dark before they turn. The hare's eyes stay with you.|You leave. Behind you, someone says \"again\", and you walk faster.}\n> right: Walk in as one of them\n  challenge deceive adept:\n    win:\n      queue ww3_masked_offer now\n      say: {~You stride in as if you were late. They're amateurs: they make room for you at once.|\"You're late,\" says one. \"Sorry,\" you say, and they believe you.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~\"That's not one of us,\" says the tallest, and says the word at you instead.|They know their own. Three wands turn your way, and a voice says \"again\", and again.}\n> up[flame]: Break up the circle\n  challenge combat adept+:\n    win:\n      renown +small\n      alignment +small\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You scatter them. They run, masks askew, into the trees. The hare is gone before you can look. You'll tell the headmistress.|One loses a mask as they run. You don't see the face. You do see the chalk, and scuff it out.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~They make you do it again: whatever you did last, until you can't stand. Then they leave you.|Three against one, and they've practised this. You don't remember the end.}\n\n@event ww3_masked_offer\ntitle: \"Again\"\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 2\nart: spell_compel\ntags: danger magic\nscene: Three masked figures in a chalk circle offering a white mask to a student, a hare frozen in the middle.\ntext: Inside the circle, the masked three are friendly, which is worse. \"It's called Compel,\" the tallest says. \"Say the word and they do it again. Anyone. Anything.\" They hold out a spare mask. The hare in the middle is shaking.\n> left: Let the hare go and leave\n  alignment +small\n  ww_depth += 1\n  queue any in ww_delve now\n  say: {~You scoop up the hare on your way out of the circle, and nobody stops you, because you walk like you're allowed. Behind you they argue about who you were.|You let the hare go and walk on. They don't follow. They don't know who you are, which is the only thing they've got right.}\n> right: Learn the word\n  hint: A forbidden spell.\n  alignment -medium\n  if spell_level(compel) < 1:\n    learn_spell compel\n  ww_depth += 1\n  queue any in ww_delve now\n  say: {~You learn it on the hare. It works the first time. You walk on with the word in your mouth like a stone.|\"Again,\" you say, and the hare does. The masks nod. You leave before they can see your face, or you theirs.}\n\n@event ww3_always_midnight\ntitle: Where It's Always Midnight\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 5 and ww_depth <= 6 and ww_hush == 0 and spell_level(moonwell) < 1\nart: moon\ntags: ww nature magic\nscene: A forest clearing under a night sky in broad daytime, moonlight pooling in a stone basin full of still water.\ntext: You step into a clearing and it's midnight: stars overhead, though it's day behind you, and moonlight pooling in a stone basin in the middle. The moonpetal round the edge is closed. It's waiting, you'd swear, for a real midnight.\n> left: Step back into the day\n  flag ww_midnight_known\n  find lead_the_hush\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You step back and it's afternoon again. You note the place, and the closed flowers, and the hour they're waiting for.|You leave the clearing to its private night, and remember the moonpetal waiting.}\n> right: Drink from the basin\n  hint: It wants a careful hand; a brewer's.\n  challenge brew adept:\n    win:\n      learn_spell moonwell\n      flag ww_midnight_known\n      find lead_the_hush\n      ww_haul += 2\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You skim the moonlight off the water like cream, the way you'd skim a potion. It settles in you, slow and free. Moonwell. The flowers stay shut: not their hour. Midnight, they seem to say. Come back at midnight.|You drink the light, not the water. It fills you slowly, like a basin. Around you the moonpetal waits for midnight.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~You drink the water, not the light. It's very cold, and so are you, for the rest of the day.|The basin tips. Moonlight goes everywhere, and you go down in it.}\n\n@event ww3_other_sky\ntitle: The Other Sky\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 5 and ww_depth <= 6\nart: star\ntags: ww nature magic\nscene: A still forest pool reflecting a completely different sky from the one above, autumn constellations in a summer noon.\ntext: A pool so still it's a mirror, except that it reflects the wrong sky: another season's stars at noon, as if the pool is looking somewhere else. When you lean over, your reflection is a moment late. Then it's a moment early.\n> left: Look away and go home\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You look away first. All the way home your reflection in puddles is very slightly late.|You leave the pool to its other sky.}\n> right: Work out how it does that\n  challenge research adept+:\n    win:\n      lore +small\n      ww_haul += 1\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~It's throwing back what's cast at it, all of it, from wherever it comes. You can't do it yourself, but you understand it, and you fill a jar with the water for Pell.|You watch until your reflection catches up. Then you understand: it gives everything back. You take a jar of the pool with you, carefully.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~You lean too far, and your reflection leans further, and you're in the water and very cold.|The pool throws your own curiosity back at you, hard.}\n> up[starlight]: Answer it with starlight\n  challenge spell adept:\n    win:\n      arcana +small\n      ww_haul += 1\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You cast starlight at the pool and it throws it back, all of it, and you catch it, and a little of the other sky with it, in a jar.|The pool returns your starlight with interest. You learn something about your own light by being on the wrong end of it, and bottle a little of the pool.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~The pool returns your starlight, all of it, and you weren't ready.|It throws the light back. You see stars for some time.}\n\n@event ww3_spiderlings\ntitle: Spiderlings\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: ww_depth >= 5 and ww_depth <= 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: spider\ntags: ww nature danger\nscene: Hundreds of small grey spiders pouring down silk threads from dark branches toward a startled student.\ntext: The branches overhead are thick with grey silk, and the silk is moving. Spiderlings, hundreds, pouring down their threads toward you, each the size of a coin. Somewhere deeper, something laid all these. You'd rather not meet it today.\n> left: Run back the way you came\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You run, brushing at your hair for the next three days.|You go home and shake out every piece of clothing you own.}\n> right: Fight through them\n  hint: Many small bites: a single brace won't stop them.\n  challenge combat adept-:\n    win:\n      ww_haul += 1\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You fight through, wand flailing, until the silk thins and the bites stop. You come out with a bag full of silk-wrapped seed-cases Pell will pay for.|They scatter. You gather a handful of their silk, which is stronger than rope and lighter than breath.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~They're everywhere at once. You go down in a grey storm of tiny bites.|You stop a dozen and a hundred get through.}\n> up[flame]: Burn the webs\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~The silk goes up like paper and the spiderlings flee in every direction. The path ahead is clear, and smells of burnt sugar.|You burn a road through the silk. Something deeper in the wood notices the smoke.}\n\n@event ww3_wisps\ntitle: Wisps\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 5 and ww_depth <= 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: lantern\ntags: ww nature mystery\nscene: Several small floating blue lights drifting between dark trees, one hovering close to a student's face.\ntext: Wisps: little blue lights that bob between the trees and ask riddles, and lead you into bogs if you answer wrong. They know where the best things grow. When something startles them, they scatter every way but one: toward the lake.\n> left: Ignore them; turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You ignore them, which wisps find very rude. They follow you to the edge, sulking.|You turn back. The wisps scatter, and you notice, again, the direction they don't go.}\n> right: Play their game\n  challenge negotiate adept:\n    win:\n      ww_haul += 3\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You out-riddle the riddlers. Furious and delighted, they lead you to a hollow full of things Pell has only read about.|You argue every answer and they love it. They lead you somewhere good, and then somewhere better.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~They lead you, laughing, into a bog. A deep one.|You answer wrong. They go out like candles, and you're alone in the dark.}\n\n@event ww3_sinkhole\ntitle: The Sinkhole\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: ww_depth >= 5 and ww_depth <= 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: skull\ntags: ww nature danger\nscene: The edge of a collapsing sinkhole in a dark forest floor, roots dangling into darkness, a student leaping.\ntext: The ground opens. A sinkhole the size of a cottage, roots hanging down into a dark that doesn't end, and the edge crumbling under your boots. ⚠ Nobody comes to catch you down here.\n> left: Scramble back\n  health -tiny\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You throw yourself backwards and the edge goes without you. You go home very carefully.|You scramble back from the edge and decide you've seen enough of the wood today.}\n> right: Jump for the far side\n  challenge feat adept:\n    win:\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You jump. For a moment there's nothing under you at all. Then there's moss, and you're through.|You land on the far edge as it crumbles, and run. Behind you the hole sighs.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~You don't make it. Roots catch you, and hold you, and you hang there for a long time.|The far edge gives under you.}\n> up[tailwind]: Ride a gust over\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~A gust lifts you over the hole like a leaf, and sets you down on the far side, dizzy and laughing.|You float across. Something at the bottom watches you go.}\n\n@event ww3_heartwood_hush\ntitle: The Heartwood\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 5 and ww_depth <= 6\nrepeat: 1\nart: tree\ntags: ww nature\nscene: Enormous red-barked trees in a dim forest heart, their roots like walls, a single shaft of light reaching the floor.\ntext: {~The heartwood: trees so old their roots are walls, and the light comes down in single shafts like rope.|The trees here are red-barked and warm to the touch, as if they had blood.|Silence, and the slow creak of trees that were old when the castle was a quarry.} You're deeper than most foragers ever go.\n> left: Turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You turn round. It's a long way back up.|You head home while you still have legs.|You turn back, and the warm red trees watch you go.}\n> right: Gather and go on\n  ww_haul += 2\n  health -small\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You gather red bark and root-caps in the long light, and go on, slower.|You fill your bag with warm red bark, and go on.|You climb between the roots, gathering as you go, and come out lower and more tired.}\n\n# ---------------- Depth 7: dusk by day, the gate at midnight ----------------\n\n@event ww_dusk\ntitle: The Silence Ahead\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 7 and ww_hush == 0\nrepeat: 1\nart: moon\ntags: ww nature mystery\nscene: The end of a forest path where the trees go silent and dark, a single closed silver flower at the edge of the gloom.\ntext: The path runs on into a silence so thick it presses on your ears. No birds, no wind, no mutter of trees. At its edge a single moonpetal is closed tight, waiting for something. {flag(ww_midnight_known): Midnight. You know that now.|Midnight, by the look of the moon.}\n> left: Turn back\n  flag ww_midnight_known\n  find lead_the_hush\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You turn back, and the birds start again behind you, as if released.|You leave the silence to itself, and to whatever keeps the castle's hours down there.}\n> right: Listen a while\n  flag ww_midnight_known\n  find lead_the_hush\n  lore +tiny\n  mood -tiny\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You stand at the edge of the silence and listen to nothing for a long time. It listens back. You go home before it's dark.|You listen until you can hear your own blood. Somewhere in the silence, very far down, something turns over in its sleep.}\n\n@event ww_hush_gate\ntitle: The Hush\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth == 7 and ww_hush == 1\nrepeat: 1\nart: moon\ntags: ww nature mystery\nscene: A silent moonlit forest where every silver flower has opened at once, the trees perfectly still, a student at the threshold.\ntext: At midnight the silence has opened. Every moonpetal along the edge is wide and shining, and the path goes on between them into the Hush, where the wood is so quiet you can hear the moon. {flag(ww_hush_seen): You've been here before. The Hush remembers.|Nobody told you it would be beautiful.}\n> left: Turn back\n  flag ww_hush_seen\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You look, and remember, and turn back while you can.|You turn back. The moonpetal close behind you one by one.}\n> right: Step into the Hush\n  hint: Deep magic: every check here is hard.\n  flag ww_hush_seen\n  ww_descents += 1\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You step in. The sound of your own boots goes away.|You walk into the Hush. It doesn't welcome you. It doesn't stop you, either.}\n\n# ---------------- Band 4: the Hush (depth 8–9, midnight only, expert-) ----------------\n\n@event ww4_silence\ntitle: The Silence\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 8 and ww_depth <= 9 and ww_hush == 1\nrepeat: 1\nart: ghost\ntags: ww nature danger mystery\nscene: A student standing in a perfectly still moonlit forest, hands over their ears, frost forming on the leaves around them.\ntext: The silence here is a thing. It presses, it leans, it wants you to stop: breathing, thinking, being. It feels like the stillness over the lake on exam mornings. Under it, very faintly, something hums one low note. ⚠ Nobody comes to catch you down here.\n> left: Back out of it\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You back out of the silence and it lets you go, reluctantly, like a hand.|You turn back. It takes the whole walk home for your ears to stop ringing.}\n> right: Hold your own sound\n  challenge spell expert-:\n    win:\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You hold a small spell in your mouth like a lozenge, and the silence can't get in. You walk through it humming.|You keep one spark lit inside you the whole way through. The silence presses and finds nothing to press.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~The silence gets in. You stop, and stand, and the next thing you know is dawn.|You can't hear your own thoughts. Then you can't have them.}\n\n@event ww4_rime_hollow\ntitle: The Rime Hollow\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 8 and ww_depth <= 9 and ww_hush == 1 and spell_level(rime) < 1\nart: snow\ntags: ww nature magic\nscene: A hollow in a moonlit forest where everything is coated in thick white frost, a single icicle hanging from a branch, glowing.\ntext: A hollow where it's always winter. Frost thick as fur on every twig, and in the middle, hanging from nothing, a single sliver of ice that glows. It's a spell, or the shape of one: the blow that should fell you finds you frozen instead.\n> left: Leave the winter here\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You leave the hollow to its winter, and go home with frost in your eyelashes.|You turn back. Your breath steams all the way out of the wood.}\n> right: Take the sliver\n  challenge spell expert-:\n    win:\n      learn_spell rime\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You take it in your bare hand. It doesn't melt; it goes in. Rime. You'll never quite be warm again, and you'll never quite be felled.|The sliver goes into your collar like a needle and stays there. Rime. The hollow thaws a little, as if relieved.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~The sliver bites. The cold goes all the way in, and you go down.|Frost climbs your arm faster than you can let go.}\n\n@event ww4_web_halls\ntitle: The Web Halls\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: tense\nwhen: ww_depth >= 8 and ww_depth <= 9 and ww_hush == 1 and not flag(ww_spinner_beaten)\nrepeat: 1\nart: spider\ntags: ww nature danger\nscene: Vast silver webs strung between moonlit trees like cathedral halls, a cocooned hippogriff struggling in one of them.\ntext: Webs strung between the trees like halls, silver in the moon, each thread as thick as a finger. Hanging in one, wrapped to the neck, is a hippogriff from the stables, its eyes white with fright. It's Brask's. You'd know that grey anywhere.\n> left: Leave it; turn back\n  mood -small\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You can't. You do. You go home and can't look at the stables for a week.|You turn back. Its eyes follow you, and then you can't see them.}\n> right: Cut it free\n  challenge feat expert-:\n    win:\n      meet brask\n      rel brask +medium\n      flag ww_spinner_angry\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~You saw through the silk thread by thread and the hippogriff bolts, free, for home. Brask will find it at dawn on the stable roof. Something deeper in the Hush has noticed you took it.|It comes free all at once and kicks you, gratefully, and flies. Far off, a web shivers.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~The silk takes your knife, then your sleeve, then you.|You get it half free and the web gets you the rest of the way.}\n> up[flame]: Burn it loose\n  meet brask\n  rel brask +medium\n  flag ww_spinner_angry\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You burn the silk away from its wings and it bolts for home. The whole web hall shudders. Something deeper is very, very awake now.|Fire goes up the silk like a fuse. The hippogriff drops, rights itself, and flies for the castle.}\n\n@event ww4_last_witch\ntitle: The Last Witch of the Wood\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 8 and ww_depth <= 9 and ww_hush == 1 and not flag(ww_spinner_secret) and not flag(ww_spinner_beaten)\nrepeat: 1\nart: ghost\ntags: ww nature mystery\nscene: The faint silver ghost of an old woman in a shawl sitting on a fallen log in a moonlit forest, knitting.\ntext: A ghost sits on a fallen log, knitting something that isn't there. She was the wood's witch, she says, before the castle, before the lake was a lake. She left when the Spinner came, and still can't decide if that was cowardice or manners. Then she asks what you want.\n> left: Nothing; turn back\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~\"Wise,\" says the ghost, and goes on knitting the nothing.|You leave her on her log, still asking her question of the dark.}\n> right: Ask about the Spinner\n  challenge negotiate expert-:\n    win:\n      flag ww_spinner_secret\n      ww_depth += 1\n      next\n      say: {~\"She has a blind side,\" the ghost says. \"The left. Something took an eye a long time ago, and she's never let anyone see. Stand on her left and talk to her like a lady, and you'll have a fair fight.\"|She tells you about the Spinner's left side, and laughs about it for a long time.}\n    lose:\n      if ww_grace > 0:\n        queue ww_stumble now\n      else:\n        queue ww_lost now\n      say: {~\"No,\" says the ghost, and she isn't there, and neither is the path.|She doesn't like your question. The cold comes down like a curtain.}\n\n@event ww4_moonpetal_bloom\ntitle: Moonpetal in Bloom\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: warm\nwhen: ww_depth >= 8 and ww_depth <= 9 and ww_hush == 1\nrepeat: 1\nart: flower\ntags: ww nature\nscene: A moonlit clearing carpeted with glowing silver flowers in full bloom, a student kneeling among them.\ntext: A whole clearing of moonpetal, open to the moon, shining like a lake. You've only ever seen one bloom at a time. {has_item(moonpetal_locket): Your locket opens on its own.|{flag(ww_locket_found): You look for the root where the locket was, and can't find it.|In the middle, caught on a root, something glints.}}\n> left: Leave them be\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You sit and look until the moon moves, and pick nothing, and go home.|You leave them all to bloom. You'll remember this one.}\n> right: Gather a few\n  ww_haul += 3\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  if not has_item(moonpetal_locket) and not flag(ww_locket_found):\n    give moonpetal_locket\n    flag ww_locket_found\n    say: You gather moonpetal by the armful, and the clearing hardly notices. Caught on a root you find a silver locket, old and plain. When you open it, there's a pressed moonpetal inside already.\n  else:\n    say: {~You fill your bag with moonlight and go on.|You gather by the armful, and the clearing hardly notices.}\n\n@event ww4_spinner_court\ntitle: An Audience\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: routine\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 8 and ww_depth <= 9 and ww_hush == 1 and flag(ww_spinner_beaten)\nrepeat: 1\nart: spider\ntags: ww nature\nscene: A great grey spider the size of a carriage resting in a silver web canopy, bowing her forelegs to a student below.\ntext: The Spinner receives you in her halls, on the left side as is proper. {knows(maud.tuppence): \"You smell of her bees,\" she says, as she always does. |}\"The trees grow away from the water,\" she says. \"So do I. I've never liked what's down there.\" She waits to see if you've brought her anything.\n> left: Bow and take your leave\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You bow. She bows her forelegs back. You go home with the strange warm feeling of having been to court.|\"Mind the left,\" she says, as you leave. It may be a joke.}\n> right: Sit with her a while\n  ww_haul += 2\n  lore +tiny\n  ww_depth += 1\n  next\n  say: {~You sit with her and she tells you about the wood before the castle. She gives you a bolt of silk as you go, for your trouble.|She shows you her oldest webs, which are a kind of map. You leave with silk and a better sense of the Hush.}\n\n# ---------------- Depth 10: the Hush's heart ----------------\n\n@event ww_hush_heart\ntitle: The Heart of the Hush\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nwhen: ww_depth >= 10 and ww_hush == 1 and flag(ww_spinner_beaten) and flag(outcast_beaten)\nrepeat: 1\nart: moon\ntags: ww nature\nscene: The silent moonlit heart of a forest, a ring of silver trees around a still pool, a student alone and unafraid.\ntext: The heart of the Hush is a ring of silvered trees round a pool that reflects nothing but the moon. Nobody is waiting here any more. The wood is yours, as much as a wood is ever anyone's.\n> ok: Take a little, and go home\n  ww_haul += 3\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You fill your bag at the edge of the pool and walk home under the moon, unhurried, unafraid.|You sit by the pool until the moon moves, and take a little, and go home.}\n"},{"path":"content/events/whisperwood/hush.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The Hush's heart (world expansion 1, plan §1): the Spinner, and after her the Outcast. Both wait at depth 10 of a\n#  midnight delve. The Spinner was Old Maud's hatchling once (Tuppence, her soft fact maud.tuppence); she keeps the\n#  Hush closed to worse things. The Outcast, Absalom Vrell, is a human practitioner expelled long ago: an optional\n#  bonus fight with nothing at stake (`stakes: none`), never in the way. Neither is the cult, the man in grey or the\n#  Star; their lines about the lake are eerie asides only.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Spinner ----------------\n\n@event ww_spinner_meet\ntitle: The Spinner\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 10 and ww_hush == 1 and not flag(ww_spinner_beaten)\nrepeat: 1\nart: spider\ntags: ww nature danger\nscene: A great grey spider the size of a carriage descending on a silver thread in a moonlit forest, many eyes gleaming.\non_show:\n  unflag ww_spinner_weak\n  unflag ww_spinner_offended\ntext: Silk across the path, then silk everywhere, and then the Spinner comes down her thread: grey, vast, queen of the Hush, with more eyes than you'd like and one of them milky. {knows(maud.tuppence): She lowers her head to you. \"You smell of her bees,\" she says.|\"A student,\" she says, in a voice like a cello. \"It's been a while.\"} {flag(ww_spinner_angry): \"You took my hippogriff.\"|}\n> left: Bow and back away\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You bow, very low, and back away. She lets you go. \"Another night,\" she says. It sounds like a promise.|You bow and retreat. Her eyes follow you a long way, all but the milky one.}\n> right: Ask for a fair fight\n  hint: {flag(ww_spinner_secret): You know her blind side.|She's proud: bargain well and she'll fight fair.}\n  if flag(ww_spinner_angry):\n    flag ww_spinner_offended\n    queue ww_spinner_fight now\n  elif flag(ww_spinner_secret):\n    flag ww_spinner_weak\n    queue ww_spinner_fight_weak now\n  else:\n    challenge negotiate expert:\n      win:\n        flag ww_spinner_weak\n        queue ww_spinner_fight_weak now\n      lose:\n        flag ww_spinner_offended\n        queue ww_spinner_fight now\n  say: {flag(ww_spinner_weak): You step to her left and speak to her like a lady. She hesitates, then laughs. \"Fair, then,\" she says, and sends her brood away.|{flag(ww_spinner_angry): \"You stole from my larder,\" she says. \"There's no bargaining after that.\"|She doesn't care for your terms. Her brood comes down the threads behind her.}}\n\n@event ww_spinner_fight\ntitle: The Long Wait\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 1\nart: spider\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A giant grey spider rearing in a moonlit web hall, venom glistening, a student braced below with a wand raised.\ntext: She comes down at you with all her brood. Venom, then web, then the long wait: she'll bind you, and then she'll strike when you can't move. ⚠ Nobody comes to catch you down here. {flag(ww_spinner_offended): She won't let you leave now.|}\n> left: Yield\n  when: not flag(ww_spinner_offended)\n  if ww_haul >= 2:\n    ww_haul -= 2\n  else:\n    ww_haul = 0\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You lower your wand. She takes her toll from your bag and lets you go. \"Another night,\" she says.|You yield. She considers this, and takes her share of your haul, and lets you walk away.}\n> right: Fight her\n  hint: She binds, then strikes: keep a way out.\n  challenge combat master-:\n    win:\n      queue ww_spinner_hoard now\n      say: {~She falls back, legs folded, and bows her head to the ground. \"Well,\" she says. \"Well. It's been a long time since anyone did that.\"|Her last strike misses. She stops, and lowers herself, and the web halls go still.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww_cocooned now\n      say: {~The web holds you. The long wait ends. Silk, everywhere, warm and tight, and then nothing.|She binds you and you can't answer the blow. The silk comes up over your eyes.}\n\n@event ww_spinner_fight_weak\ntitle: A Fair Fight\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 1\nart: spider\ntags: nature danger\nscene: A giant grey spider facing a student alone in a moonlit clearing, the student standing on her blind left side.\ntext: She's sent the brood away. You stand on her left, where the milky eye can't see you, and she knows that you know. She's still the biggest thing in the wood. ⚠ Nobody comes to catch you down here.\n> left: Yield\n  if ww_haul >= 2:\n    ww_haul -= 2\n  else:\n    ww_haul = 0\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~You lower your wand. \"Sensible,\" she says, and takes her toll from your bag, and lets you go.|You yield. She bows, a little mocking, and takes a fee from your bag.}\n> right: Fight her\n  hint: Stay on her blind side.\n  challenge combat master- likely:\n    win:\n      queue ww_spinner_hoard now\n      say: {~You stay on her blind side and she can't find you in time. At last she folds her legs and bows. \"Fairly done,\" she says.|She turns and turns, and you're always on her left. In the end she laughs and stops.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww_cocooned now\n      say: {~Even half-blind, she's faster. The silk comes up warm and tight.|She finds you by your breathing. The web closes.}\n\n@event ww_spinner_hoard\ntitle: The Spinner's Hoard\ntrigger: queued\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: spider\ntags: nature money\nscene: A great grey spider bowing her forelegs to a student amid a hoard of glittering trinkets and bolts of silver silk.\ntext: She bows her forelegs to you, the way the old courts did. Behind her, in the oldest web, is a hoard: lost coins, a christening spoon, sixty years of things that fell out of foragers' pockets, and bolts of her own grey silk, which a tailor could make into anything. \"Take what you like,\" she says. \"You've earned the left side.\"\n> ok: Take silk and silver\n  flag ww_spinner_beaten\n  flag ww_spinner_silk\n  find lead_spinner_silk\n  gold +large\n  renown +small\n  ww_haul += 2\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: You take a bolt of silk and as much silver as you can carry. She watches you go. \"Tell the girl I'm well,\" she says. {knows(maud.tuppence): She means Maud, who raised her in the potting shed, and you'll tell her.|You don't yet know who she means.} Behind you, the Hush feels emptier, and something else in it shifts, interested.\n\n@event ww_cocooned\ntitle: Cut Free\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: grim\nrepeat: 1\nart: lantern\ntags: nature danger\nscene: An old woman with a pruning knife cutting a silk cocoon open in a moonlit forest, a giant spider watching quietly above.\non_show:\n  health -huge\n  ww_haul = 0\n  if ww_depth > ww_deepest:\n    ww_deepest = ww_depth\n  ww_depth = 0\ntext: Silk, warm and tight and everywhere. Then a voice outside it: \"Tuppence. Let go, dearie. That one's ours.\" A pruning knife slits the silk, and Old Maud's face is there, lit by a lantern, very cross. Above her the Spinner is waiting, quite still, as if she'd been told off.\n> left: Let Maud take you home\n  meet maud\n  rel maud +small\n  learn maud.tuppence\n  say: Maud walks you all the way home without a word. At the door she says, \"She was the size of my hand, once. I kept her in the potting shed.\" Then she goes back into the dark, toward the wood.\n> right: Ask her who Tuppence is\n  meet maud\n  lore +tiny\n  learn maud.tuppence\n  say: \"Mine,\" says Maud. \"Once. She outgrew the potting shed, and I let her go.\" She looks back at the trees. \"She keeps worse things out, you know. Don't you go thinking she's the worst thing down there.\"\n\n# ---------------- The Outcast (optional; nothing at stake) ----------------\n\n@event ww_outcast_meet\ntitle: A Lantern Left Burning\nthread: ww_delve\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nwhen: ww_depth >= 10 and ww_hush == 1 and flag(ww_spinner_beaten) and not flag(outcast_beaten)\nrepeat: 1\nart: lantern\ntags: ww nature magic\nscene: A gaunt elegant old man in a frayed academy robe sitting on a tree root beside a lantern in a ring of silver trees.\ntext: Where the Spinner's webs were, there's a ring of silvered trees and a lantern left burning. An old man in a frayed academy robe, decades out of date, is sitting on a root beside it. \"Absalom Vrell,\" he says. \"Expelled, before your parents were born. I've been waiting for her to fall. The lake hums at night, down here. I don't ask what about. Nobody sensible does.\"\n> left: Not tonight\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~\"Another night, then,\" he says, amused, and turns the lantern down.|He waves you off without getting up. \"I'll be here. I'm always here.\"}\n> right: Sit down with him\n  queue ww_outcast_table now\n  say: {~You sit on the other side of the lantern. He looks pleased, and then hungry, and then polite again.|You sit. He offers you tea from a flask. It's very good, and you drink it carefully.}\n\n@event ww_outcast_table\ntitle: Absalom Vrell\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: eerie\nrepeat: 1\nart: book\ntags: nature magic\nscene: An old man and a student sitting across a lantern in a moonlit forest, a black book open on his knee.\ntext: \"I taught the thornwitch,\" he says. \"And those children in masks, after a fashion, though they never paid attention. I know every spell they burned me for. I'd teach you one. Or,\" and his eyes light, \"you could try me. Nobody has in forty years. Nothing would be at stake but my pride.\"\n> left: Bargain for a lesson\n  hint: Forbidden spells; he drives a hard bargain.\n  challenge negotiate grandmaster-:\n    win:\n      alignment -small\n      # Threads 1: Torment and Reflect are the Restricted Stacks', Extraction the seventh tower's, Eclipse the man in\n      # grey's; the Outcast keeps Heartprice.\n      if spell_level(heartprice) < 1:\n        learn_spell heartprice\n      else:\n        lore +medium\n      queue ww_way_home now\n      say: {~He teaches you, there by the lantern, with a patience that's almost kind. It's a terrible thing, beautifully explained. You go home with it under your tongue.|\"Good,\" he says. \"You argue like we used to.\" He teaches you one thing, and makes you promise nothing.}\n    partial:\n      queue ww_way_home now\n      say: {~\"Almost,\" he says, delighted. \"Another night.\" He turns the lantern down.|He laughs and shakes his head. \"Not tonight. But you're close.\"}\n    lose:\n      queue ww_way_home now\n      say: {~He shrugs. \"Another night.\" He doesn't seem disappointed. He has a great many nights.|He listens to your terms, and then to the Hush, and then says no, kindly.}\n> right: Challenge him to a duel\n  hint: Nothing at stake but pride.\n  queue ww_outcast_fight now\n  say: {~He stands, and his old robe straightens itself. \"Oh,\" he says. \"Oh, lovely.\"|He puts down his tea. For the first time, he looks young.}\n\n@event ww_outcast_fight\ntitle: Every Forbidden Thing\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: tense\nrepeat: 1\nart: duel\ntags: nature magic\nscene: An old wizard in a frayed robe casting dark spells in a ring of silver trees, a student dodging, a lantern on the ground between them.\ntext: He fights with everything they expelled him for: Heartprice and Siphon, Compel and Eclipse, all at once, at a grandmaster's speed, and he's laughing. There's nothing at stake. He promised. It doesn't feel like nothing.\n> left: Walk away\n  queue ww_outcast_loss now\n  say: {~You lower your wand. He bows. \"Another night,\" he says. \"Come back when you're bored of winning.\"|You step back out of the ring. He's not offended. He's already pouring more tea.}\n> right: Fight him\n  hint: A grandmaster; you lose nothing by losing.\n  challenge combat grandmaster+:\n    win:\n      queue ww_outcast_victory now\n      say: {~His last spell fizzles. He looks at his hand, then at you, and laughs until he has to sit down.|You catch him between Heartprice and Siphon, with nothing left to pay. He bows, very low.}\n    partial:\n      queue ww_outcast_loss now\n      say: {~You give him the fight of his life, nearly. He stops you, gently, a breath from winning.|You come close. He knows it. So do you.}\n    lose:\n      queue ww_outcast_loss now\n      say: {~He takes you apart with great courtesy. Then he helps you up.|You don't touch him. He doesn't hurt you either, much.}\n\n@event ww_outcast_victory\ntitle: The Hush Is Yours\ntrigger: queued\nclass: milestone\ntone: warm\nart: book\ntags: nature magic\nscene: An old wizard bowing deeply to a student in a moonlit forest, then dissolving into silver frost, a black book left on the root.\ntext: Absalom Vrell bows to you as deep as his old back allows. \"Forty years,\" he says. \"Well. The Hush is yours, then. Mind the lake.\" He sets his black grimoire on the root, and laughs, and is gone in a breath of silver frost. The lantern goes out on its own.\n> ok: Take the grimoire\n  flag outcast_beaten\n  give outcast_grimoire\n  renown +large\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: You pick up the grimoire. It's cold, and some of its pages are blank, and they won't stay blank. Somewhere in the castle, someone will hear of this by breakfast. The headmistress, probably.\n\n@event ww_outcast_loss\ntitle: Another Night\ntrigger: queued\nclass: story\ntone: warm\nrepeat: 1\nart: lantern\ntags: nature magic\nscene: An old wizard helping a student up off the forest floor beside a lantern, both smiling.\ntext: Absalom helps you up, brushes the leaves off your shoulder, and pours you a cup of tea from his flask. \"Come back when you're bored of winning,\" he says. \"I'm not going anywhere. That's rather the point of me.\"\n> ok: Drink the tea, and go home\n  queue ww_way_home now\n  say: {~The tea is very good. You walk home under the moon, beaten and oddly cheerful.|You drink it, thank him, and go home. Next time, you think.}\n"},{"path":"content/states/contraband.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Contraband (threads 1, docs/threads-1-plan.md): two banned elixirs (lore brightwater, owls_ink).\n#  - A bottle waits in your trunk (`*_bottle`, one of each at most) until you drink, pour or sell it\n#    (content/events/contraband.grim); every dose risks a search at dawn.\n#  - A dose lasts two months. When it wears off the craving may take hold (`*_want`), more likely with each dose\n#    taken: four months of a little lost each month and an occasional bad day (the withdrawal pools), ended early by\n#    another dose or by Sister Amaranth's remedy.\n#  - Owl's Ink is the illicit exam help: a large boost to exams (`@balance boost_sizes`), well past a tonic's rung.\n# ============================================================\n\n@state brightwater_bottle\nname: A Bottle of Brightwater\nkind: neutral\nart: bottle\ndesc: Hidden in your trunk, wrapped in a sock: one dose of a banned elixir, cloudy gold.\n\n@state owls_ink_bottle\nname: A Bottle of Owl's Ink\nkind: neutral\nart: bottle\ndesc: Hidden in your trunk, wrapped in a sock: one dose of a banned elixir, black and bitter.\n\n@state brightwater\nname: Brightwater\nkind: buff\nart: bottle\nduration: 2\ndesc: You sleep like a stone and wake like a bell. It's lovely. It's banned.\non_add:\n  lose brightwater_want\n  brightwater_doses += 1\ntick:\n  health +4\n  mood +4\non_expire:\n  if brightwater_doses >= 3:\n    chance likely:\n      gain brightwater_want\n  elif brightwater_doses == 2:\n    chance even:\n      gain brightwater_want\n  else:\n    chance unlikely:\n      gain brightwater_want\n\n@state owls_ink\nname: Owl's Ink\nkind: buff\nart: bottle\nduration: 2\nboost: exam:large\ndesc: Your mind is a new nib and your magic a full well. Exams look small from up here.\non_add:\n  lose owls_ink_want\n  owls_ink_doses += 1\n  mana +10\ntick:\n  mana +6\non_expire:\n  if owls_ink_doses >= 3:\n    chance likely:\n      gain owls_ink_want\n  elif owls_ink_doses == 2:\n    chance even:\n      gain owls_ink_want\n  else:\n    chance unlikely:\n      gain owls_ink_want\n\n@state brightwater_want\nname: Wanting Brightwater\nkind: debuff\nart: rain\nduration: 4\ndesc: The world has gone a shade greyer since the Brightwater wore off, and you know exactly what would fix it. Sister Amaranth, they say, has seen this before.\ntick:\n  mood -2\n  queue any in withdrawal_brightwater in 0 chance unlikely\n\n@state owls_ink_want\nname: Wanting Owl's Ink\nkind: debuff\nart: rain\nduration: 4\ndesc: Since the ink wore off your magic comes slow and the page swims, and you know exactly what would fix it. Sister Amaranth, they say, has seen this before.\ntick:\n  mana -3\n  queue any in withdrawal_owls_ink in 0 chance unlikely\n"},{"path":"content/states/states.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Persistent states. A state is something that sticks to the\n#  player across months: an origin, a class, a club, a job, an\n#  illness, a curse. States can:\n#    tick:      run every month start while active\n#    duration:  default length in months (omit = until removed)\n#    group:     only one state of the same group at a time\n#    on_add / on_expire / on_remove: effects\n#  Events check them with has(state_id) and left(state_id).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Origins (chosen at the start) ----------------\n\n@state origin_noble\nname: Minor Nobility\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: crown\ndesc: A family name that opens doors, and letters from home that bring money and expectations in the same envelope.\nmoney: Comfortable: 60 crowns from home to start, and 38 from the family before each later term, now and then with a gift. Every letter weighs on you a little. Tuition is yours to pay.\non_add:\n  origin_noble_start\n  renown +6\n\n@state origin_scholar\nname: Scholarship Student\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: scroll\ndesc: The Crown pays for clever students, for as long as they stay clever.\nmoney: Tight: 30 crowns to start. Before each later term, 38 crowns if last term's GPA was 3.0 or better, 46 at 3.5 or better, and nothing below. Tuition is yours to pay.\non_add:\n  origin_scholar_start\n\n@state origin_hedge\nname: Hedge-Witch's Grandchild\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: mushroom\ndesc: Raised among drying herbs and muttered charms: a practical hand with potions, and not much patience for theory.\nmoney: Poor: 15 crowns and no money from home. You will need a job from the first term.\ntags: poor\non_add:\n  origin_hedge_start\n  mood +6\n\n@state origin_street\nname: Street-Raised\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: mask\ndesc: You learned to read crowds before you learned to read runes.\nmoney: Poor: 8 crowns and no one to send more. You will need a job from the first term.\ntags: poor\non_add:\n  origin_street_start\n\n@state origin_merchant\nname: Merchant's Child\nkind: origin\ngroup: origin\nart: scales\ndesc: Everything has a price, and you were taught to haggle it down.\nmoney: Rich but unsteady: 120 crowns to start, and 78 from the company before each later term when trade goes well, about two terms in three; nothing when it doesn't. Tuition is yours to pay.\non_add:\n  origin_merchant_start\n\n# ---------------- Electives (one per term) ----------------\n\n@state class_pyromancy\nname: Elective: Pyromancy\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: flame\ntags: class\ndesc: Fire answers to will, not wishes. Singed eyebrows are considered a rite of passage.\ntick:\n  arcana +2\n  health -1\n\n@state class_tidecalling\nname: Elective: Tidecalling\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: wave\ntags: class\ndesc: Lessons on the lake shore, bending water and patience in equal measure.\ntick:\n  arcana +1\n  vigor +1\n  mood +1\n\n@state class_illusion\nname: Elective: Illusion & Glamour\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: mask\ntags: class\ndesc: The art of making people see what you want. Professor Vey insists it is not lying.\ntick:\n  guile +1\n  charm +1\n\n@state class_alchemy\nname: Elective: Practical Alchemy\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: cauldron\ntags: class\ndesc: Brewing, distilling and the occasional controlled explosion.\ntick:\n  alchemy +2\n\n@state class_divination\nname: Elective: Divination\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: eye\ntags: class\ndesc: Tea leaves, star charts and the Mirror of Next Tuesday.\ntick:\n  lore +1\n  if rand(1, 3) == 1:\n    mood -2\n\n@state class_artifice\nname: Elective: Artifice\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: gear\ntags: class\ndesc: Enchanting objects to do what they were never meant to do.\ntick:\n  alchemy +1\n  lore +1\n\n@state class_beastcraft\nname: Elective: Beastcraft\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: paw\ntags: class\ndesc: Familiars, griffon husbandry and dodging the manticore pen.\ntick:\n  vigor +1\n  mood +2\n\n@state class_astromancy\nname: Elective: Astromancy\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: planet\ntags: class\ndesc: Midnight lectures on the observatory roof. You will learn the stars; you will lose sleep.\ntick:\n  lore +2\n  health -1\n\n@state class_healing\nname: Elective: Healing Arts\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: cross\ntags: class\ndesc: Mending flesh with light and needle. Sister Amaranth expects perfection.\ntick:\n  alchemy +1\n  lore +1\n  health +1\n\n@state class_umbral\nname: Elective: Umbral Theory\nkind: class\ngroup: elective\nart: skull\ntags: class\ndesc: Restricted. The study of shadow, death and what lies behind both. Strictly theoretical. Mostly.\ntick:\n  arcana +2\n  lore +1\n  mood -2\n\n# ---------------- Clubs (one at a time) ----------------\n\n@state club_dueling\nname: Club: Dueling Circle\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: duel\ndesc: Bruises, glory and the Spring Tournament.\ntick:\n  vigor +1\n  arcana +1\n  health -1\n\n@state club_choir\nname: Club: Choir of the Spheres\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: music\ndesc: Harmonies that make the candles flicker in time.\ntick:\n  charm +1\n  mood +2\n\n@state club_skyrace\nname: Club: Skyrace Squad\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: broom\ndesc: Brooms, hoops and screaming crowds.\ntick:\n  vigor +2\n  health -1\n\n@state club_ledger\nname: Club: The Lumen Ledger\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: paper\ndesc: The student paper. Deadlines, scoops and enemies.\ntick:\n  guile +1\n  charm +1\n\n@state club_garden\nname: Club: Verdant Circle\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: leaf\ndesc: Greenhouse afternoons with mandrakes that sing off-key.\ntick:\n  alchemy +1\n  mood +1\n\n@state club_stargazers\nname: Club: Stargazers\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: star\ndesc: Telescopes, cocoa and constellations nobody else can name.\ntick:\n  lore +1\n  mood +1\n\n@state club_cartographers\nname: Club: Midnight Cartographers\nkind: club\ngroup: club\nart: map\ndesc: A club that officially does not exist, mapping corridors that officially do not either.\ntick:\n  guile +1\n  lore +1\n\n# ---------------- Jobs (one at a time) ----------------\n\n@state job_library\nname: Job: Library Assistant\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: book\ndesc: Reshelving books that bite. Quiet hours, modest pay.\ntick:\n  gold +10\n  lore +1\n  mood -1\n\n@state job_tavern\nname: Job: The Gilded Newt\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: cup\ndesc: Serving cider in the village tavern. Good tips, sore feet.\ntick:\n  gold +16\n  charm +1\n  health -2\n\n@state job_apothecary\nname: Job: Apothecary Clerk\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: bottle\ndesc: Labelling jars at Pell & Daughters. Do not lick anything.\ntick:\n  gold +12\n  alchemy +1\n\n@state job_stables\nname: Job: Familiar Stables\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: paw\ndesc: Feeding hippogriffs and mucking out things that breathe smoke.\ntick:\n  gold +11\n  vigor +1\n  health -1\n\n@state job_tutor\nname: Job: First-Year Tutor\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: feather\ndesc: Explaining the difference between a hex and a jinx, again.\ntick:\n  gold +12\n  study +tiny\n  mood -1\n\n@state job_courier\nname: Job: Broom Courier\nkind: job\ngroup: job\nart: letter\ndesc: Parcels across the lake at full speed. Pays well. Hurts often.\ntick:\n  gold +18\n  vigor +1\n  health -3\n\n# ---------------- Conditions ----------------\n\n@state hungry\nname: Hungry\nkind: debuff\nart: meal\nduration: 1\ndesc: The board went unpaid this month. Bread rolls pocketed from the refectory only go so far.\ntick:\n  health -3\n  mood -4\n  say: {~Your stomach growls through every lecture.|Bread rolls in your pockets, and not enough of them.|You learn exactly how long a bowl of porridge lasts.}\n\n@state sick\nname: Lake Fever\nkind: debuff\nart: rain\nduration: 2\ndesc: Shivers, sniffles and a nose that glows faintly blue.\ntick:\n  health -4\n  mood -2\n  say: {~You sniffle through the month, nose glowing faintly blue.|Shivers, tea and far too many handkerchiefs.}\n\n@state injured\nname: Injured\nkind: debuff\nart: cross\nduration: 2\ndesc: Something is bruised, sprained or bent the wrong way.\ntick:\n  health -2\n  vigor -1\n  say: {~Every staircase reminds you where it hurts.|You limp, and the moving stairs show no sympathy.}\n\n@state exhausted\nname: Exhausted\nkind: debuff\nart: bed\nduration: 1\ndesc: You could sleep standing up. You might have.\ntick:\n  health -3\n  mood -3\n  say: {~You yawn through a whole lecture without ever closing your mouth.|You could sleep standing up, and once you do.}\n\n@state inspired\nname: Inspired\nkind: buff\nart: sparkle\nduration: 2\ndesc: Ideas arrive faster than you can write them down.\ntick:\n  lore +1\n  arcana +1\n  mood +2\n  say: {~Ideas arrive faster than you can write them down.|You fill a notebook and start another.}\n\n@state heartbroken\nname: Heartbroken\nkind: debuff\nart: heartbreak\nduration: 3\ndesc: Songs are suddenly about you. All of them.\ntick:\n  mood -4\n  say: {~Every song in the common room is about you.|You walk the long way round, to miss a certain corridor.}\n\n@state slacking\nname: Skipping Classes\nkind: debuff\nart: bed\nduration: 2\ndesc: Freedom! Also: falling behind.\ntick:\n  mood +2\n  say: {~A free morning, a lie-in, a twinge of guilt.|You skip a lecture, and nobody seems to mind yet.}\n\n# Detention (threads 1): a term month's two visits are served in detention instead of on the map (the `detention`\n# pool, content/events/detention.grim). Its duration counts the term months it has taken; a break clears it.\n@state detention\nname: Detention\nkind: debuff\nart: lock\nduration: 1\ndesc: Your afternoons and evenings belong to a prefect, a bucket and a long list of chores.\nday: morning=courses noon=social:1-2 afternoon=detention evening=detention night=world:1-2 midnight=special:0-1\ntick:\n  if break:\n    lose detention\n\n@state probation\nname: Academic Probation\nkind: debuff\nart: scales\ndesc: Your marks are below the line. Two terms under a 1.0 and the Disciplinary Board sits. A GPA of 2.0 clears it.\ntick:\n  mood -2\n  if gpa >= 2.0:\n    lose probation\n  say: {~Probation sits on your shoulder through every lecture.|Your name is on a list you never wanted to be on.}\n\n# Financial aid (playtest 2, X14): a term's grant from the bursar's hardship fund, or the bursar's own loan, which is\n# gentler than Grisk's (no interest, no collectors; a missed month costs only mood and waits).\n@state hardship_grant\nname: Hardship Grant\nkind: buff\nart: scales\nduration: 4\ndesc: The bursar's hardship fund pays 8 crowns a month for a term. The paperwork asked how poor you were, in detail.\ntick:\n  gold +8\n  say: {~The hardship grant arrives in a brown envelope with your name spelled wrong.|Eight crowns from the fund, and a form to say you received them.}\n\n@state bursar_loan\nname: Bursar's Loan\nkind: debuff\nart: scales\ndesc: The academy lent you sixty crowns. It takes 8 a month when you can pay, seven times, then a last 4, with no interest and no collectors: only a very patient ledger.\ntick:\n  if loan_paid >= 7 and gold >= 4:\n    gold -4\n    loan_paid += 1\n  elif loan_paid < 7 and gold >= 8:\n    gold -8\n    loan_paid += 1\n  else:\n    mood -1\n    say: {~No loan payment this month. The bursar notes it without comment, which is its own comment.|The loan waits. The ledger is patient; you are less so.}\n  if loan_paid >= 8:\n    lose bursar_loan\n    say: The bursar's loan is paid off. She stamps the ledger with something like a smile.\n\n@state in_debt\nname: Debt to Grisk\nkind: debuff\nart: chain\ndesc: The goblin moneylender takes 12 crowns a month until the debt is cleared. Ignore a missed payment and he adds another.\n# Unpaid, the debt escalates (playtest 2, M22): the first miss brings a reminder (ignore it and a month is added),\n# the next the collectors.\ntick:\n  if gold >= 12:\n    gold -12\n    debt_paid += 1\n  else:\n    mood -4\n    debt_missed += 1\n    if debt_missed == 1:\n      queue grisk_reminder now\n  if debt_paid >= 6:\n    lose in_debt\n  if debt_missed >= 2:\n    queue grisk_collectors now\n\n@state famous\nname: Talk of the Academy\nkind: buff\nart: star\nduration: 3\ndesc: Strangers know your name. Some of them even say it nicely.\ntick:\n  renown +2\n  mood +1\n\n@state hiccup_hex\nname: Hiccup Hex\nkind: debuff\nart: sparkle\nduration: 2\ndesc: Every hiccup releases a small, harmless, extremely embarrassing firework.\ntick:\n  mood -2\n  renown +1\n\n@state blessed\nname: Maud's Blessing\nkind: buff\nart: flower\nduration: 3\ndesc: A sprig of rosemary in your pocket that never wilts.\ntick:\n  health +3\n\n@state familiar_fox\nname: Familiar: Ember\nkind: buff\nart: fox\ndesc: A fox-spirit with a smouldering tail who has decided you are hers.\n\n@state familiar_owl\nname: Familiar: Clockwork Owl\nkind: buff\nart: owl\ndesc: Mir's gift. It hoots the hour, mostly correctly, and remembers your homework.\n\n@state training\nname: Tournament Training\nkind: buff\nart: duel\nduration: 2\ndesc: Dawn drills on the dueling green.\ntick:\n  vigor +2\n  arcana +1\n  health -1\n\n@state wakeful\nname: Draught of Wakefulness\nkind: buff\nart: potion\nduration: 1\ndesc: You haven't blinked in eleven hours. Your notes are immaculate.\ntick:\n  study +small\n  health -3\n\n@state haunted\nname: Haunted Dormitory\nkind: debuff\nart: ghost\nduration: 2\ndesc: Something rearranges your socks at night.\ntick:\n  mood -2\n\n@state watched\nname: Under Bram's Eye\nkind: debuff\nart: eye\nduration: 3\ndesc: The prefect is watching you. Specifically, and with relish.\ntick:\n  mood -1\n\n@state umbral_curse\nname: Umbral Curse\nkind: debuff\nart: skull\ndesc: A cold that lives under your skin. Something in the restricted section followed you home.\ntick:\n  health -3\n  mood -1\n  arcana +1\n\n@state hollow_dreams\nname: Hollow Dreams\nkind: debuff\nart: moon\ndesc: Every night: a black star, a door beneath the lake, a voice that knows your name.\ntick:\n  mood -1\n  lore +1\n\n@state star_mark\nname: The Star-Mark\nkind: buff\nart: star\ndesc: A pale, star-shaped mark on your palm that hums near old magic.\ntick:\n  arcana +1\n\n@state star_crowned\nname: The Star's Crown\nkind: buff\nart: star\n# hs_beneath's bargain (v2, feedback 2026-10-25: the bargain gave nothing immediate): the gift that deepens the\n# star-mark, with an edge and a price that both last.\ndesc: The star on your palm has grown a crown of points. Spells come at a thought, and a voice at the back of your mind agrees with you a little too often.\ntick:\n  arcana +1\n  lore +1\n  alignment -1\n\n@state star_taste\nname: A Taste of the Star\nkind: buff\nart: star\nduration: 3\n# hs_grey_offer (feedback 2026-10-16): what the man in grey showed you. Every skill comes easier; the nights are cold.\ndesc: Since the man in grey showed you, everything comes easier, and every night you dream of cold water.\ntick:\n  arcana +1\n  lore +1\n  alchemy +1\n  charm +1\n  vigor +1\n  guile +1\n  mood -4\n  say: {~Spells come easier, and the dreams come colder.|You wake with frost on the inside of the window, and the day's work goes like a song.}\n\n@state grey_watching\nname: He Remembers\nkind: debuff\nart: mask\nduration: 3\n# hs_grey_offer (feedback 2026-10-16): you told the man in grey to get out, and he remembers.\ndesc: You told the man in grey to get out. Candles burn blue when you're alone in the library, and you've learned to look over your shoulder.\ntick:\n  mood -2\n  guile +1\n\n@state summer_away\nname: Summer Away\nkind: neutral\nart: house\nduration: 2\ndesc: Away from the academy for the summer recess. No rent, no classes.\n\n@state summer_work\nname: Summer Work\nkind: job\nart: coin\nduration: 2\ndesc: A summer of honest (and occasionally dishonest) labour.\ntick:\n  gold +25\n  health -2\n\n@state frog_voice\nname: Croaking\nkind: debuff\nart: frog\nduration: 1\ndesc: A botched transfiguration left you with the voice of a bullfrog.\ntick:\n  charm -1\n  mood -2\n\n@state garden_plot\nname: Garden Plot\nkind: buff\nart: leaf\ndesc: A little square of earth behind the greenhouses that is yours. Things grow; so do you.\ntick:\n  alchemy +1\n  if rand(1, 3) == 1:\n    mood +2\n\n@state pen_pal\nname: Pen Pal in Thornwick\nkind: buff\nart: letter\ndesc: A student at the rival academy who writes you long, funny letters full of terrible gossip.\n# Legacy (playtest 2, X9): the pen pal is now Tamsin (@npc tamsin) and their letters are cards; nothing grants this.\n\n@state frost_ward\nname: Ilse's Frost-Ward\nkind: buff\nart: snow\n# Now an item (world expansion 1, E4): @item ilse_frost_ward, granted while you have this state, carries its tick.\ndeprecated: true\ndesc: A sliver of Northern winter that Ilse sewed into your collar. Cold things think twice about you.\ntick:\n  arcana +1\n  health +1\n\n# ---------------- Break plans (design §4.3; phase 2b) ----------------\n# Chosen at the start of each break (calendar_2b.grim: break_plan). A plan lasts the break.\n\n@state plan_castle\nname: Break: at the castle\nkind: plan\ngroup: plan\nduration: 2\nart: tower\n# Four visits a day (E2): the castle is yours to wander.\nday: morning=plan:0-1 noon=visit afternoon=visit evening=visit night=visit midnight=special:0\ndesc: A skeleton crew, empty corridors and secrets. Board costs 6 crowns a month.\n\n@state plan_home\nname: Break: at home\nkind: plan\ngroup: plan\nduration: 2\nart: house\ntags: away\nmap_id: home\n# Four visits a day at home (E2).\nday: morning=plan:0-1 noon=visit afternoon=visit evening=visit night=visit midnight=special:0\ndesc: Family, old friends and free board. The days are slower here.\non_add:\n  gain summer_away 2\n\n@state gig_grounds\nname: Holiday gig: the grounds\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: leaf\ndesc: Working the grounds for Old Maud this break. Good pay, sore hands.\n\n@state gig_kitchens\nname: Holiday gig: the kitchens\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: meal\ndesc: Working the Refectory kitchens this break. Good pay, short temper.\n\n# The holiday version of a term job (X15): for those at its top rank; its own pay, through its cards.\n@state gig_library\nname: Holiday gig: the stocktake\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: book\ndesc: The library's holiday stocktake, for its senior clerks. Every book counted, every bite noted. Better pay than the baseline gigs.\n\n@state gig_newt\nname: Holiday gig: the Newt's holiday rush\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: cup\ndesc: Running the Gilded Newt through the holiday rush, for its managers. The best tips of the year, and the longest nights.\n\n@state gig_pell\nname: Holiday gig: the remedy rush\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: bottle\ndesc: Mixing the season's remedies at Pell & Daughters, for its assistant apothecaries. Good pay, and the village depends on it.\n\n@state gig_post\nname: Holiday gig: the holiday post\ngroup: gig\nduration: 2\nart: letter\ndesc: Flying the holiday post for the courier loft, for its chief couriers. Every parcel in the valley, and the weather against you.\n\n@state plan_work\nname: Break: working full-time\nkind: plan\ngroup: plan\nduration: 2\nart: coin\n# Three visits around the gig (E2): its shift card comes in the morning.\nday: morning=plan noon=visit afternoon=visit evening=visit night=world:1 midnight=special:0\ndesc: Working on campus through the break: a holiday gig, and board as usual. Tiring.\n\n@state plan_internship\nname: Break: internship\nkind: plan\ngroup: plan\nduration: 2\nart: book\ntags: away\nmap_id: internship\ndesc: A research placement away from the castle, with a stipend and a supervisor.\ntick: gold +6\n# One summer each (M16): the supervisor's report (feedback 2026-10-15b) comes first, and a strong one brings an offer\n# to come back; otherwise the post ends.\non_expire:\n  queue intern_assessment\n\n# ---------------- The midnight turn (playtest 2, M41) ----------------\n\n@state locked_out\nname: Confined After Dark\nkind: debuff\nduration: 3\nart: lock\ndesc: Caught once too often. Your door is checked at midnight, and the night is closed to you.\n"},{"path":"content/states/tonics.grim","text":"# Tonics (world expansion 1, K3): bought in the village, one at a time (`group: tonic`), for a month. While one holds,\n# the plain checks its `boost:` names come one rung easier (`@balance tonic`). Fights and negotiations never read it.\n\n@state courage_blend\nname: Courage Blend\nkind: buff\ngroup: tonic\nduration: 1\nart: cup\nboost: appeal perform\ndesc: Mabel's courage blend: ginger, honey and something she won't name. Asking for things and getting up in front of people come a little easier.\n\n@state calm_blend\nname: Calm Blend\nkind: buff\ngroup: tonic\nduration: 1\nart: cup\nboost: exam soothe\ndesc: Chamomile, lavender and a pinch of moonwort, in a tin Mabel labelled \"for nerves\". Exam halls and frightened animals both seem quieter.\n\n@state steadying_draught\nname: Steadying Draught\nkind: buff\ngroup: tonic\nduration: 1\nart: bottle\nboost: research brew enchant\ndesc: Pell's steadying draught, measured to the drop. Your hands stop shaking over the fine work, and your eyes stop skipping lines.\n"},{"path":"content/world/archetypes.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Archetypes (design §10.4): templates for side characters spawned\n#  in phase 4. Names come from per-language pools\n#  (locales/<lang>/names.lang). Every character is an adult.\n# ============================================================\n\n@archetype student\nnames: pool students\nage: 18..22\ntraits: pick 2\npronouns: any\nhouse: any\nrole: student\ndesc: A fellow student: a face in the corridor who might become a friend, a rival or a story.\n\n@archetype professor\nnames: pool professors\nage: 35..72\ntraits: pick 2\npronouns: any\nhouse: Faculty\nrole: professor\ndesc: A visiting or junior member of the faculty: an advisor, an examiner or an internship supervisor.\n\n@archetype villager\nnames: pool villagers\nage: 18..80\ntraits: pick 1\npronouns: any\nhouse: none\nrole: outsider\ndesc: Someone from the village across the lake: a shopkeeper, a bargeman, a customer at the Gilded Newt.\n\n@archetype northerner\nnames: pool northerners\nage: 18..40\ntraits: pick 2\npronouns: any\nhouse: none\nrole: outsider\ndesc: A traveller from the frozen North, with frost in their eyelashes and loyalties that are hard to read.\n"},{"path":"content/world/arcs.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Arcs (design §10.6): the main plot and character arcs as data.\n#  acts: name@year · clock: range · beats: clock@yNmM (the clock\n#  advances on schedule whatever the player does) · clues: what can\n#  be found · key_clues: needed for the finale, each with ≥ 2\n#  sources. Mechanics arrive in phase 2b; the structure in phase 3.\n# ============================================================\n\n@arc hollowstar\nname: The Hollow Star\ncanon: hard\nacts: omens@y1 investigation@y2 escalation@y3 convergence@y4\nact_names: Omens, Investigation, Escalation, Convergence\n# Each act opens at its opener's clock (content/events/hollowstar/threads.grim), so pushing opens it early.\nact_clock: 1 4 7 10\nclock: 0..12\nbeats: 1@y1m3 2@y1m9 4@y2m2 5@y2m9 7@y3m2 8@y3m7 10@y4m3 12@y4m9\nclues: founders_ledger lake_bell second_shadow seven_locks star_key kael_family ilse_truth knows_founders_seal codex_names\nkey_clues: seven_locks star_key ilse_truth\ncast: kael ilse isolde maud seraphine\nact_rumours:\n  They say the lake breathed on the first frost, and the bell under it rang with no one near.\n  There's talk of a girl in white furs in the village, and of a man in grey who asks after the Dumonts.\n  A tower went dark. Nobody says the word \"cult\" at supper, which is how you know everyone is thinking it.\n  The lake has frozen early, and the headmistress has stopped pretending it hasn't.\nepilogue: The Night is over. Whatever you chose beneath the lake, the castle wakes every morning around it now.\ndesc: Something that eats light sleeps beneath the Mirror Lake, behind a door the founders sealed with seven locks. Someone wants it open.\n\n@arc lyra_and_her_mother\nname: Second Place\ncanon: hard\nacts: rivals@y1 cracks@y2 choice@y3\nact_names: Rivals, Cracks, Choice\nclock: 0..6\nclues: black_seal_letters\ncast: lyra\nact_rumours:\n  Everyone knows Lyra Vaneshade means to come first. Fewer know who she means to beat.\n  Her mother's letters come with a black seal, and Lyra reads them alone.\n  House Vaneshade expects an answer from its heir before she graduates.\ndesc: Lyra Vaneshade has never once heard \"well done\". Her rivalry with you can become respect, friendship or more, and her mother will have something to say about all three.\n\n@arc kael_thaw\nname: Thaw\ncanon: hard\nacts: frost@y1 trust@y2 thaw@y3\nact_names: Frost, Trust, Thaw\nclock: 0..6\nclues: kael_family\ncast: kael\nact_rumours:\n  The transfer from the North doesn't feel the cold, they say, and doesn't sit with anyone.\n  Kael has started sitting somewhere. People have noticed where.\n  Letters come from the North more often now, and Kael answers fewer of them.\ndesc: Kael Dumont was sent to open a door and came to stop it. Whether he manages depends on whether anyone is warm enough.\n\n@arc ilse_dumont\nname: The Girl in White Furs\ncanon: hard\nacts: arrival@y2 the_man_in_grey@y3 north@y4\nact_names: Arrival, The Man in Grey, North\nclock: 0..6\nclues: ilse_truth\ncast: ilse kael\nact_rumours:\n  A girl in white furs paid at the bakery with a coin that froze to the counter.\n  The man in grey has been seen in the lanes after dark, asking after the girl in white.\n  Letters with a black-star seal keep coming for Ilse.\ndesc: Kael's sister was raised by the Hollow cult to do what he wouldn't. The man in grey wants her home. She might let him.\n\n@arc the_bell\nname: The Bell Under the Water\ncanon: soft\nacts: echo@y1 rope@y2\nact_names: Echo, Rope\nclock: 0..4\nbeats: 1@y1m2 2@y1m8 3@y2m3 4@y2m9\nclues: bell_heard bell_rope bell_name\ncast: maud\nact_rumours:\n  Some nights the drowned bell rings, and the fishermen won't say so in daylight.\n  Old Maud has kept something of the bell for sixty years, they say, and she's starting to talk.\ndesc: The drowned bell under the Mirror Lake rings when no one is near it. Someone wants it found.\n"},{"path":"content/world/cast.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  The cast. Each NPC has a relationship record:\n#    rel  (-100..100)  how they feel about you\n#    love (0..100)     romantic interest (romance: true only)\n#    bond             none | friend | close | rival | nemesis | partner | ex\n#  Characters are introduced with `meet <id>` (or any rel/love/bond change).\n# ============================================================\n\n@npc lyra\nname: Lyra Vaneshade\ntitle: Heir of House Vaneshade, pyromancy prodigy\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Emberhall\ncolor: #b0383c\nromance: true\nlook: hair:7 skin:5 style:1\ntags: noble ambitious fire peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: proud ambitious lonely stubborn\ninterests: duelling fire books\nstats: arcana 34 lore 22 alchemy 8 charm 20 vigor 28 guile 12 wealth 5\nschedule: duel_hall@afternoon,evening refectory@noon library@night:60\n# Breaks at the Vaneshade estate (X7): reachable only by letter.\nschedule_break: away\nrelations: kael:-10 bram:5 nessa:-5\nvoice: Clipped and formal; sarcastic when hurt. Never says \"please\" first, never admits to being tired, apologises by doing something useful.\ndesc: Brilliant, impatient and used to winning. She treats everyone as either a rival or furniture — and is quietly lonely about it.\n\n@npc tobias\nname: Tobias Fennick\ntitle: Farmer's son on a turnip scholarship\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Rootward\ncolor: #4f8a3c\nromance: true\nlook: hair:2 skin:0 style:2\ntags: commoner kind herbs virtuous peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: kind gentle homesick loyal\ninterests: plants animals cooking dancing\nstats: arcana 12 lore 14 alchemy 26 charm 18 vigor 30 guile 4 wealth 1\nschedule: greenhouses@morning,afternoon refectory@noon,evening stables@evening:60\ngone_when: flag(tobias_went_home) and since(tobias_bad_harvest) < 2\n# Breaks on the family farm (X7): reachable only by letter.\nschedule_break: away\nrelations: maud:20 bram:-15 mir:10\nvoice: Warm, slow and earnest, with farm sayings and long stories about goats. Apologises too much; laughs from the belly.\ndesc: Big hands, bigger heart. Talks to plants and, worryingly, the plants seem to answer.\n\n@npc seraphine\nname: Seraphine Hale\ntitle: Seer who never quite looks at the present\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Stillwater\ncolor: #6a4aa8\nromance: true\nlook: hair:4 skin:3 style:4\ntags: seer quiet mystery peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: dreamy distant perceptive kind\ninterests: prophecy painting tarot stars\nstats: arcana 20 lore 32 alchemy 10 charm 16 vigor 8 guile 14 wealth 3\nschedule: observatory@evening,night lakeshore@afternoon library@morning:50\nschedule_break: observatory@evening,night lakeshore@afternoon\nrelations: kael:10 lyra:5\nvoice: Half-sentences, finished in another week. Calm, vague, then suddenly exact about something that hasn't happened yet.\ndesc: Speaks in half-sentences, as if finishing the other half in a different week. Often right. Unsettlingly often.\n\n@npc corvin\nname: Corvin Ashby\ntitle: Charming liability\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Duskgate\ncolor: #3a3a4a\nromance: true\nlook: hair:0 skin:1 style:3\ntags: rogue guile trouble peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: charming reckless loyal secretive\ninterests: maps locks cards money\nstats: arcana 16 lore 12 alchemy 14 charm 30 vigor 18 guile 38 wealth 1\nschedule: courtyard@noon,afternoon gargoyle_passage@night gargoyle_passage@midnight:40 gilded_newt@evening\n# Nowhere he'd go back to (X7): the Newt and the passages over breaks.\nschedule_break: gilded_newt@afternoon,evening gargoyle_passage@night gargoyle_passage@midnight:50\nrelations: bram:-30 nessa:10\nvoice: Quick, teasing, deflecting with jokes; calls you \"saint\" when you're being good. When he means something, the jokes stop and so does he.\ndesc: Knows every locked door in the academy and has opinions about all of them. Owes money to at least three people.\n\n# Renamed on 2026-10-15: this character was \"Mira\" (id `mira`, Chinese 米拉) and is now Mir (id `mir`, 米尔), they/them.\n# Every id was renamed with it (`mir_*`, `letter_mir_*`, `art_mir_collab`, `ending_beloved_mir`, `mir.*` facts).\n# Older records (tools/migrations/, past reviews) still use the old name; never reintroduce it in content.\n@npc mir\nname: Mir Ten\ntitle: Artificer of questionable inventions\npronouns: they/them\nhouse: Brasswick\ncolor: #2a8a8a\nromance: true\nlook: hair:5 skin:2 style:0\ntags: artificer chaotic peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: chaotic curious stubborn funny\ninterests: machines clocks flying\nstats: arcana 24 lore 20 alchemy 30 charm 14 vigor 10 guile 12 wealth 3\nschedule: workshop@afternoon,evening,night refectory@noon:50 lecture_tower@morning\nschedule_break: workshop@afternoon,evening,night\nrelations: tobias:10\nvoice: Fast, enthusiastic, full of technical tangents and exclamation marks. Talks to machines more gently than to people. Says \"probably\" a lot.\ndesc: Smells permanently of solder and ozone. Has built a clockwork owl, a self-stirring cauldron and at least one small fire.\n\n@npc kael\nname: Kael Dumont\ntitle: Transfer student from the frozen North\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Stillwater\ncolor: #3a5a7a\nromance: true\nlook: hair:4 skin:0 style:0\ntags: mystery north hollowstar peer\nage: 20\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: guarded cold lonely protective\ninterests: stars snow books\nstats: arcana 36 lore 26 alchemy 8 charm 10 vigor 22 guile 18 wealth 2\nschedule: observatory@night observatory@midnight:30 library@afternoon:60 boathouse@evening\n# Can't go home (X7): the lake and the observatory all break long.\nschedule_break: lakeshore@afternoon boathouse@evening observatory@night observatory@midnight:40\nrelations: ilse:30 lyra:-10 seraphine:5\nvoice: Few words, quiet, as if each one costs something. Northern formality; literal-minded about feelings, startled by kindness.\ndesc: Arrived mid-term with no luggage and frost in his eyelashes. Doesn't talk about where he came from. Watches the sky at night.\n\n@npc nessa\nname: Nessa Quill\ntitle: Editor of the Lumen Ledger\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Duskgate\ncolor: #a86a2a\nromance: true\nlook: hair:0 skin:4 style:3\ntags: gossip journalist virtuous peer\nage: 19\nrole: student\nyear: 1\ntraits: nosy idealistic overworked bold\ninterests: writing gossip justice\nstats: arcana 14 lore 26 alchemy 8 charm 30 vigor 12 guile 24 wealth 2\nschedule: ledger_office@afternoon,evening,night courtyard@noon gilded_newt@evening:25\nschedule_break: ledger_office@afternoon village@evening:50\nrelations: bram:-20 corvin:10 lyra:-5\nvoice: Rapid, bossy, headline-shaped. Treats every conversation as an interview; goes very quiet when it's off the record.\ndesc: Runs the student paper with a quill that writes on its own. Everyone is a story; some are just not finished yet.\n\n@npc bram\nname: Bram Hollis\ntitle: Prefect, enforcer, menace\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Emberhall\ncolor: #7a4a20\nromance: false\nstart_rel: -10\nlook: hair:3 skin:1 style:2\ntags: prefect bully rival ruthless peer\nage: 19\nrole: prefect\nyear: 1\ntraits: bullying insecure proud loyal\ninterests: rules duelling\nstats: arcana 26 lore 18 alchemy 10 charm 14 vigor 32 guile 20 wealth 4\nschedule: courtyard@morning,noon duel_hall@evening library@afternoon:50\n# A prefect who stays on campus through every break (X7).\nschedule_break: courtyard@afternoon duel_hall@evening\nrelations: tobias:-15 corvin:-30 nessa:-20 lyra:5\nvoice: Loud, officious, rule-quoting; demerits as punctuation. Under it, his father's voice. When he's honest, he's gruff and brief.\ndesc: A prefect of your own year, with a ledger of demerits and a fondness for writing your name in it.\n\n@npc thorne\nname: Professor Aldric Thorne\nshort: Professor Thorne\ntitle: Master of Arcana\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #444466\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:0 style:0\ntags: faculty mentor strict teacher\nage: 68\nrole: professor\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: severe exacting dry warm\ninterests: duelling roses teaching chess\nstats: arcana 88 lore 74 alchemy 30 charm 22 vigor 40 guile 36 wealth 4\nschedule: lecture_tower@morning,afternoon great_hall@noon library@evening:50\nschedule_break: library@afternoon,evening\nrelations: isolde:30 maud:20\nvoice: Terse, precise, allergic to flattery. \"Hm.\" is a full sentence. Praise arrives disguised as criticism, and very rarely.\ndesc: Severe, exacting, allergic to flattery. Rumoured to have once out-dueled a storm.\n\n@npc maud\nname: Old Maud\nshort: Old Maud\ntitle: Groundskeeper and hedge-witch\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #6a7a3a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:2 style:4\ntags: faculty kind herbs virtuous\nage: 81\nrole: staff\nseeks: sometimes\ntraits: kind eccentric patient warm\ninterests: plants bees soup animals history\nstats: arcana 46 lore 58 alchemy 72 charm 30 vigor 20 guile 34 wealth 2\nschedule: maud_cottage@morning,noon,evening greenhouses@afternoon\nschedule_break: maud_cottage@morning,evening greenhouses@afternoon\nrelations: thorne:20 isolde:25 tobias:20\nvoice: Folksy and sharp; calls everyone \"dearie\". Gives advice you only understand two weeks later; tells sad stories over tea as if they were recipes.\ndesc: Keeps bees, chickens and a rather large secret garden. Gives advice you only understand two weeks later.\n\n@npc isolde\nname: Headmistress Isolde Marrow\nshort: Headmistress Marrow\ntitle: Headmistress of Lumenhold\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #5a2a4a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:7 skin:5 style:1\ntags: faculty authority\nage: 57\nrole: headmistress\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: calm watchful formidable perceptive\ninterests: chess history justice\nstats: arcana 84 lore 90 alchemy 50 charm 60 vigor 30 guile 70 wealth 4\nschedule: headmistress_tower@morning,afternoon,evening great_hall@noon\nschedule_break: headmistress_tower@afternoon,evening\nrelations: thorne:30 maud:25\nvoice: Pleasant, unhurried, every word placed like a chess piece. Never raises her voice; never needs to. A short, delighted laugh when surprised.\ndesc: Tall, pale and impossibly calm. She remembers every student's name — and every student's mistakes.\n\n@npc ilse\nname: Ilse Dumont\ntitle: Kael's younger sister, a storm in white furs\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: none\ncolor: #8aa8c8\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:5 style:1\ntags: north hollowstar ruthless peer\nage: 18\nrole: outsider\ntraits: fierce lonely proud wary\ninterests: snow duelling\nstats: arcana 40 lore 18 alchemy 6 charm 12 vigor 26 guile 24 wealth 2\nschedule: village@afternoon,evening lakeshore@night\ngone_when: flag(ilse_taken) or flag(ilse_went_north)\nrelations: kael:20\nvoice: Sharp, curt, older than she is; Northern words when upset. Treats kindness like a trick she hasn't learned the counter to.\ndesc: Raised by the Hollow cult to open a door she has never seen. Sharp as an icicle, lonely as a frozen lake, and much younger than she pretends to be.\n\n# The pen pal at Thornwick (playtest 2, X9): never on campus but for the yearly fixture; their cards are letters.\n@npc tamsin\nname: Tamsin Reed\nshort: Tamsin\ntitle: Illusionist at Thornwick, your pen pal\npronouns: they/them\nhouse: none\ncolor: #3f7a4f\nromance: false\nlook: hair:3 skin:3 style:2\ntags: thornwick student\nage: 19\nrole: outsider\nseeks: sometimes\ntraits: funny curious ambitious loyal\ninterests: painting gossip theatre cards\nstats: arcana 26 lore 20 alchemy 10 charm 30 vigor 12 guile 28 wealth 2\nschedule_break: away\n# A pen pal (feedback 2026-10-15): reached only by letter, and never cast in other people's cards.\ndistant: true\nvoice: Writes the way they talk: fast, sly, all asides and crossings-out. Signs every letter with a new insult. Means every kind word, and hides them in postscripts.\ndesc: A Thornwick illusionist who answered a letter to \"a Lumenhold student chosen at random\" and kept writing. Insufferable, by their own account, and the best correspondent you have.\n\n# ---------------- Fixtures (design §5.5): people of a place or a track, with lighter ladders ----------------\n# They come looking for you rarely, and only once you've been coming to them (`seeks:`). No romance.\n\n@npc amaranth\nname: Sister Amaranth\nshort: Sister Amaranth\ntitle: Healer of the infirmary\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #9a3b4a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:2 skin:3 style:0\ntags: faculty healer teacher virtuous\nage: 54\nrole: staff\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: severe honest patient warm\ninterests: plants soup history\nstats: arcana 40 lore 52 alchemy 80 charm 24 vigor 34 guile 30 wealth 2\nschedule: alchemy_lab@morning infirmary@afternoon,evening,night great_hall@noon\nschedule_break: infirmary@afternoon,evening,night\nrelations: maud:25 thorne:10 brask:15\nvoice: Short sentences, delivered while doing something else. Believes no excuse, forgives most things anyway. Praise comes as an instruction (\"Keep doing that.\").\ndesc: Runs the infirmary with warming charms, tonics and no patience for excuses. Teaches the healing half of Alchemy & Healing.\n\n@npc brask\nname: Professor Hedda Brask\nshort: Professor Brask\ntitle: Professor of Beastcraft & Flight\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #7a5a2a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:3 skin:4 style:4\ntags: faculty teacher animals\nage: 61\nrole: professor\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: dry gentle stubborn brave\ninterests: animals flying history\nstats: arcana 52 lore 48 alchemy 30 charm 16 vigor 70 guile 28 wealth 2\nschedule: stables@morning,afternoon,evening great_hall@noon\nschedule_break: stables@morning,afternoon,evening\nrelations: amaranth:15 maud:20 thorne:5\nvoice: Speaks to creatures in full sentences and to students in half ones. Every story ends with a bite and a moral. Laughs once a term, loudly.\ndesc: Teaches Beastcraft & Flight and is kinder to animals than to students, though the gap closes for anyone who mucks out without being asked.\n\n@npc vey\nname: Professor Ottilie Vey\nshort: Professor Vey\ntitle: Professor of Illusion & Glamour\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #6a4a8a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:6 skin:1 style:1\ntags: faculty teacher theatre\nage: 46\nrole: professor\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: charming perceptive sly funny\ninterests: theatre painting cards\nstats: arcana 60 lore 50 alchemy 20 charm 78 guile 70 vigor 22 wealth 3\nschedule: lecture_tower@morning,afternoon great_hall@noon\n# Breaks with the theatre season in the capital (X7).\nschedule_break: away\nrelations: thorne:5 marisol:20\nvoice: Amused, theatrical, a little too observant. Asks questions she already knows the answer to, to see what you'll do. Delighted by anyone who fools her.\ndesc: Teaches Illusion & Glamour and grades on whether you fooled her. She is very hard to fool, and very generous to the few who manage it.\n\n@npc marisol\nname: Professor Ines Marisol\nshort: Professor Marisol\ntitle: Professor of Tidecalling\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: Faculty\ncolor: #2a6a8a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:1 skin:3 style:4\ntags: faculty teacher tides\nage: 39\nrole: professor\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: dreamy warm reckless curious\ninterests: tides music writing\nstats: arcana 70 lore 56 alchemy 18 charm 40 guile 20 vigor 52 wealth 2\nschedule: lakeshore@morning,evening lecture_tower@afternoon\nschedule_break: lakeshore@morning,evening\nrelations: vey:20 thorne:15\nvoice: Soft and unhurried until there's weather; then she whoops. Quotes poems about water and forgets the last line. Believes cold water is good for everyone.\ndesc: Teaches the tidecalling half of Evocation, waist-deep in the lake at dawn. Keeps the lake calm on exam days and reads poetry under a dry patch of air when it rains.\n\n@npc mabel\nname: Mabel Thistlewood\nshort: Mabel\ntitle: Keeper of the tea shop\npronouns: she/her\nhouse: none\ncolor: #a0703a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:2 style:4\ntags: village kind\nage: 63\nrole: villager\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: perceptive warm nosy patient\ninterests: cooking gossip music\nstats: arcana 14 lore 30 alchemy 36 charm 58 vigor 20 guile 40 wealth 3\nschedule: mabels@morning,noon,afternoon village@evening\nrelations: grimsby:20 pell:15\nvoice: Warm and brisk; finishes your order for you. Reads moods like weather and says so. Calls students \"petal\" and means something different every time.\ndesc: Forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake, and a talent for reading your mood from the way you open her door.\n\n@npc grimsby\nname: Mr Alder Grimsby\nshort: Mr Grimsby\ntitle: Bookseller\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: none\ncolor: #5a4a2a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:1 style:0\ntags: village books\nage: 72\nrole: villager\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: eccentric kind distant curious\ninterests: books history maps\nstats: arcana 20 lore 74 alchemy 12 charm 30 vigor 10 guile 22 wealth 2\nschedule: grimsbys_books@noon,afternoon village@evening\nrelations: mabel:20\nvoice: Vague, courteous, enthusiastic about the wrong thing. Recommends books by their smell. Remembers what you read three years ago and nothing about what you asked for today.\ndesc: Keeps a bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other. He will find anything except what you came in for, which is often what you needed.\n\n@npc pell\nname: Old Pell\nshort: Old Pell\ntitle: Apothecary of Pell & Daughters\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: none\ncolor: #3a5a3a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:4 skin:2 style:0\ntags: village\nage: 77\nrole: villager\nseeks: rarely\ntraits: exacting proud loyal stubborn\ninterests: plants rules money\nstats: arcana 22 lore 46 alchemy 76 charm 14 vigor 16 guile 30 wealth 3\nschedule: pell_apothecary@morning,afternoon village@evening\nrelations: mabel:15 grisk:-20\nvoice: Gruff, precise, counts aloud. Never apologises; corrects a label instead. Trusts people who measure twice, and says so once a decade.\ndesc: Taught three generations of the village to count drops. His daughters run the counter now; he runs the drawers, and the drawers are never wrong.\n\n@npc grisk\nname: Grisk\nshort: Grisk\ntitle: Moneylender, friend to the temporarily embarrassed\npronouns: he/him\nhouse: none\ncolor: #4a6a3a\nromance: false\nlook: hair:0 skin:0 style:1 body:1\ntags: village goblin money\nage: 58\nrole: villager\nseeks: sometimes\ntraits: sly charming patient guarded\ninterests: money cards gossip\nstats: arcana 18 lore 40 alchemy 20 charm 46 vigor 14 guile 82 wealth 4\nschedule: gilded_newt@evening village@afternoon\ngone_when: flag(grisk_gone)\nrelations: pell:-10\nvoice: Courtly, purring, every sentence an invoice. Calls debts \"friendships\". Never threatens; describes, pleasantly, what will happen.\ndesc: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat who lends to students: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six. He keeps a ledger, and sometimes asks for favours instead of coin.\n"},{"path":"content/world/clues.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Clues and leads (playtest 2, X11) as the Journal shows them.\n#  kind: clue is one of its arc's clues: and counts in clues(arc);\n#  kind: lead points somewhere to look, never counts, and is\n#  struck through once its followed: holds. Only hard-canon story\n#  cards find clues; a lead may come from any story beat.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- The Hollow Star: leads ----------------\n\n@clue lead_lake_breathed\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: The lake held its breath\ntext: In Mistveil the lake froze for one breath and the drowned bell tolled. Whoever keeps the boathouse has heard that shore for years.\nfollowed: clue(lake_bell)\n\n@clue lead_founders_archive\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: What the founders wrote\ntext: The headmistress told the hall it was nothing. The founders' records in the library archive may disagree, and the Restricted Section keeps what the archive won't.\nfollowed: clue(founders_ledger)\n\n@clue lead_maud_friend\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: Maud lost a friend\ntext: Old Maud was a student the last time the lake froze, and lost a friend to it. She might tell you more, if she trusts you.\nfollowed: clue(knows_founders_seal)\n\n@clue lead_man_in_grey\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: A man in grey\ntext: A man in a grey coat told Kael to open something, or his sister would. Kael knows what. He'll tell someone he trusts.\nfollowed: clue(kael_family)\n\n@clue lead_black_star_dream\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: A dream of a black star\ntext: You dreamt of the lake from underneath. Seraphine dreams too, and the observatory sees further than any dream.\nfollowed: clue(second_shadow)\n\n@clue lead_seventh_tower\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: The seventh tower\ntext: Seven towers, seven locks. One tower has been locked for a century, and something is kept at the top of it.\nfollowed: clue(star_key)\n\n@clue lead_white_furs\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: A girl in white furs\ntext: A Northern girl has taken a room above the village bakery. Kael went pale when he heard.\nfollowed: met(ilse)\n\n@clue heard_of_starcalling\narc: hollowstar\nkind: lead\nname: A scraped-out word\ntext: One word has been scraped out of the observatory's oldest logs. Students who finished Advanced Astromancy were taught it aloud.\nfollowed: enrolled(starcalling) or passed(starcalling, 1)\n\n# ---------------- The Hollow Star: clues ----------------\n\n@clue founders_ledger\narc: hollowstar\nname: The razored page\ntext: The founding year was cut out of the Founders' Ledger with a razor, but the pen's impression survived on the page beneath.\n\n@clue knows_founders_seal\narc: hollowstar\nname: The founders' seal\ntext: Lumenhold was built as a seal: seven towers, seven locks, and a key shaped like a star. Something sleeps beneath the water.\n\n@clue lake_bell\narc: hollowstar\nname: The bell under the water\ntext: A drowned bell lies under the lake and tolls when the thing below stirs. A tunnel runs down from the boathouse toward it.\n\n@clue second_shadow\narc: hollowstar\nname: A second shadow\ntext: Something small and dark crossed the sky the wrong way and stopped directly above the lake.\n\n@clue seven_locks\narc: hollowstar\nname: Seven locks\ntext: One lock in each tower; when a tower goes dark, so does its lock. Locks can be mended. They need hands, and a key.\n\n@clue star_key\narc: hollowstar\nname: The star key\ntext: A key shaped like a star that holds no light, kept at the top of the seventh tower. The seal can't be renewed without it, or without the founders' words.\n\n@clue kael_family\narc: hollowstar\nname: The Dumonts\ntext: Kael's family serve the thing under the lake. They sent him to open its door. He came to stop them.\n\n@clue ilse_truth\narc: hollowstar\nname: What Ilse was raised for\ntext: The Dumonts raised Kael's sister to open the door if he wouldn't. Nobody ever asked her if she wanted to.\n\n@clue codex_names\narc: hollowstar\nname: The Codex of Hollow Names\ntext: A book in the Restricted Section lists everyone the Star has noticed. The last name is still being written, and it is yours.\n\n# ---------------- The Bell Under the Water ----------------\n\n@clue bell_heard\narc: the_bell\nname: A bell where none is\ntext: The drowned bell rang in the night with no one near it, and nobody else woke.\n\n@clue bell_rope\narc: the_bell\nname: The cut rope\ntext: Maud has kept the bell's rope for sixty years. It came up the night the lake froze, cut clean.\n\n@clue bell_name\narc: the_bell\nname: The name on the rim\ntext: Letters run around the bell's rim on the lakebed: a name you don't know, and now keep.\n\n@clue lead_maud_answer\narc: the_bell\nkind: lead\nname: What Maud knows\ntext: Maud has kept the bell's rope for sixty years, and she has promised to tell you who cut it. Go and see her at her cottage.\nfollowed: seen(bell_answer) > 0\n\n@clue lead_bell_dive\narc: the_bell\nkind: lead\nname: The diving helmet\ntext: There's an old diving helmet in the boathouse. Whatever is written on the bell, you'll only read it from the lakebed.\nfollowed: clue(bell_name)\n\n@clue lead_bell_shore\narc: the_bell\nkind: lead\nname: The lakeshore after dark\ntext: The bell wants you at the lakeshore after dark, in the evening or at midnight, with what you have: the name from its rim, or Maud's rope.\nfollowed: flag(bell_silenced)\n\n# ---------------- Second Place ----------------\n\n@clue black_seal_letters\narc: lyra_and_her_mother\nname: Letters under a black seal\ntext: House Vaneshade writes to Lyra under its black seal. Her mother asks who beat her, never how she is.\n\n# ---------------- Side threads (feedback 2026-10-15): hinted now, to be followed up later ----------------\n# A side thread's lead names no arc; the Clues panel groups it under its thread:. They never touch the mystery.\n\n@clue lead_moonpetal\nthread: The cold that won't leave\nkind: lead\nname: Moonpetal\ntext: Old Maud says the cure is a moonpetal from the Whisperwood, which blooms only after dark, and that you have to fetch it yourself.\nfollowed: not has(umbral_curse)\n\n@clue lead_second_timetable\nthread: The unsigned note\nkind: lead\nname: A second timetable\ntext: Someone slipped a note under your door: the castle has a second timetable, and it starts at midnight. Whoever wrote it knows the passages.\nfollowed: seen(corvin_note_was_mine) > 0\n\n@clue lead_grisk_jetty\nthread: Grisk's parcels\nkind: lead\nname: The jetty at midnight\ntext: Grisk's arrangement: a parcel collected at the boathouse jetty at midnight, carried to a cobbler's back door in the village by day.\nfollowed: seen(grisk_pickup) > 0 or flag(grisk_gone)\n\n@clue lead_grisk_bottles\nthread: Grisk's bottles\nkind: lead\nname: Bottles at the jetty\ntext: Grisk's arrangement: a bottle of Brightwater or Owl's Ink for a favour, at the boathouse jetty at midnight.\nfollowed: seen(grisk_elixir_favour) > 0 or flag(grisk_gone) or not flag(grisk_supply)\n\n@clue lead_silver_key\nthread: The silver key\nkind: lead\nname: The Restricted Section\ntext: Odile Varn's notebook came with a silver key to the Restricted Section, the locked door at the back of the library. It's yours to use after hours, in the evening or at midnight.\nfollowed: seen(forbidden_section_1) > 0 or flag(forbidden_abandoned)\n\n@clue lead_restricted_shelves\nthread: The silver key\nkind: lead\nname: Deeper in the stacks\ntext: You've been inside the Restricted Section once, and there are shelves you didn't reach. The key still works after hours.\nfollowed: flag(forbidden_read) or seen(forbidden_section_3) > 0\n\n@clue lead_codex_open\nthread: The silver key\nkind: lead\nname: The Codex, left open\ntext: The Codex of Hollow Names is still lying where it fell in the Restricted Section, open at your name. Sooner or later, someone will notice you've been there.\nfollowed: seen(forbidden_section_3) > 0\n\n@clue lead_newt_upstairs\nthread: The upstairs room\nkind: lead\nname: A regular at the Newt\ntext: The landlady of the Gilded Newt's upstairs room keeps a jacket for you now. Any night out at the Newt, there's a party upstairs that pays.\nfollowed: seen(newt_company_regular) > 0\n\n@clue lead_sky_championship\nthread: The Skyrace team\nkind: lead\nname: The championship\ntext: You made the team. The championship against Thornwick is flown at the flight field later in the year.\nfollowed: seen(sky_championship) > 0\n\n# Spells v2 club and job chains (docs/spells-v2-list.md §G; content/events/club_spell_chains.grim): each start card\n# leaves a lead, followed once the spell is learned.\n\n@clue lead_brace\nthread: The captain's charm\nkind: lead\nname: The dummy that hexes back\ntext: An old duelling captain's charm is sewn into the practice dummy, and it throws hexes back. Read the captain's notes, stand Bram's barrage, drill your footwork: then hold firm inside the circle while it empties.\nfollowed: spell_level(brace) >= 1\n\n@clue lead_quickquill\nthread: The quill that talks back\nkind: lead\nname: A quill of your own\ntext: A spare quill from the Ledger's drying line finishes your sentences, and then invents them. Teach it, give it proper ink and sit a deadline night with it, before the interview where everyone denies everything.\nfollowed: spell_level(quickquill) >= 1\n\n@clue lead_bramble\nthread: The seed in your pocket\nkind: lead\nname: A seed that answers back\ntext: A seed from the Verdant Circle's far bed sprouted in your pocket. Plant it, see it through a cold snap, and quiet the mandrakes around it before the night it bolts.\nfollowed: spell_level(bramble) >= 1\n\n@clue lead_index\nthread: Footnote's drawer\nkind: lead\nname: The drawer Footnote guards\ntext: Footnote sits on one catalogue drawer in the library and won't move. Under him is the card for a book eleven years overdue, somewhere on shelves that wander.\nfollowed: spell_level(index) >= 1\n\n@clue lead_rapport\nthread: Two taps on the bar\nkind: lead\nname: How the landlady does it\ntext: The Newt's landlady taps the bar twice and a fight turns into a laugh. Learn the regulars' stories, stand them a round and work a double shift, and one festival night the room will be yours to hold.\nfollowed: spell_level(rapport) >= 1\n\n@clue lead_distil\nthread: Pell's oldest drawer\nkind: lead\nname: A draught of your own strength\ntext: Pell's oldest drawer holds a recipe for a draught \"of your own strength\". Count drops with Pell, buy moonwort, try the daughters' shortcut, and then brew it.\nfollowed: spell_level(purify) >= 1\n\n@clue lead_tailwind\nthread: The jar of wind\nkind: lead\nname: Wind in a jar\ntext: A jar of wind sits on the courier loft's dead-letter shelf, and someone once flew with it. Find out who, race the gulls and fix the broom that pulls left, then fly a storm delivery across the lake.\nfollowed: spell_level(tailwind) >= 1\n\n# World expansion 1: the Whisperwood delve\n\n@clue lead_the_hush\nthread: The Hush\nkind: lead\nname: The Hush\ntext: The deepest part of the Whisperwood, past the heartwood, keeps the castle's hours: it wakes only at midnight.\nfollowed: flag(ww_hush_seen)\n\n@clue lead_heartwood\nthread: The elder oak's gift\nkind: lead\nname: A splinter of heartwood\ntext: The elder oak gave you a splinter of its heartwood. Mir, at the market stall, could carve it into a wand.\nfollowed: has_item(heartwood_wand)\n\n@clue lead_spinner_silk\nthread: The Spinner's silk\nkind: lead\nname: A bolt of grey silk\ntext: The Spinner gave you a bolt of her silk. The village tailor could make a robe of it.\nfollowed: has_item(spinner_silk_robe)\n\n@clue lead_missed_round\nthread: The missed round\nkind: lead\nname: The lamp that went out\ntext: Sister Amaranth missed her evening round, the first time anyone can remember. Someone has to keep the infirmary going until she's on her feet, in the evenings, bed by bed.\nfollowed: flag(rounds_finished)\n\n@clue lead_red_ink\nthread: Red ink\nkind: lead\nname: Two sets of marks\ntext: Someone is marking your essays a second time, in red ink and unsigned, and leaving them on the lecture tower stair. They expect the next draft there too.\nfollowed: flag(red_ink_finished)\n\n@clue lead_late_bloomer\nthread: The sweet shop's daughter\nkind: lead\nname: The cold stove\ntext: The sweet shop owner's grown daughter boils the sugar on a cold stove when she's upset, and nobody at home knows what to do about it. You're the one from the castle, and she'll be waiting when you're next home.\nfollowed: flag(bloomer_finished)\n\n# New threads 1: the Master Map's blanks open the midnight heists (content/events/cartographers.grim)\n\n@clue lead_master_map\nthread: The Master Map\nkind: lead\nname: The blanks on the map\ntext: The Cartographers' Master Map has two great blanks: below the Restricted Section's chained shelf, and inside the seventh tower's wall. Inside each, once it's charted, there's a shortcut to find. Two lines on the map, and Corvin will ask you to keep it with him. The club's work turns up at the third gargoyle.\nfollowed: flag(carto_map_done)\n\n@clue lead_deep_stacks\nthread: The deep stacks\nkind: lead\nname: The stair under the chained shelf\ntext: Below the last chained shelf of the Restricted Section's east stacks, by the book-return hatch, a stair goes down to stacks no lamp reaches, and to the confiscation vault. The Cartographers go at midnight.\nfollowed: flag(heist_stacks_tried)\n\n@clue lead_tower_stair\nthread: The tower's wall stair\nkind: lead\nname: The stair inside the wall\ntext: A servants' stair, one shoulder wide, climbs inside the seventh tower's wall from the cellars to the landing of candles. The Cartographers go at midnight.\nfollowed: flag(heist_tower_tried)\n"},{"path":"content/world/factions.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Factions (design §3.2): who wants what. alignment: lean on the\n#  Lumen (+) ↔ Hollow (−) axis · stance: toward the player at the\n#  start of a run.\n# ============================================================\n\n@faction wardens\nname: The Wardens\nmembers: isolde thorne maud\nalignment: 60\nstance: neutral\ndesc: The headmistress's quiet circle, sworn to keep the founders' seal. They recruit students only when the seal grows thin.\n\n@faction hollow_cult\nname: The Hollow Cult\nmembers: ilse\nalignment: -80\nstance: hostile\ndesc: Northern families, the Dumonts first among them, who serve the star beneath the lake. Their messenger is the man in grey.\n\n@faction midnight_cartographers\nname: The Midnight Cartographers\nmembers: corvin\nalignment: 0\nstance: neutral\ndesc: The club that doesn't exist: students who map the castle's secret passages and share them with almost no one.\n\n@faction faculty\nname: The Faculty\nmembers: isolde thorne maud\nalignment: 30\nstance: neutral\ndesc: The professors and staff of Lumenhold: strict, eccentric, and mostly on your side whether you like it or not.\n\n@faction conclave\nname: The Conclave of the Seven Towers\nalignment: 20\nstance: neutral\ndesc: The governing body of magic in the realm, headquartered in the capital. Hands out research posts, licences and very long letters.\n\n@faction thornwick\nname: Thornwick Academy\nalignment: 0\nstance: hostile\ndesc: The rival school across the hills. Famous for illusionists, tournament grudges and spies who think they're subtle.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/amaranth.grim","text":"# Facts about Sister Amaranth.\n\n@fact amaranth.healer\ntier: surface\ntext: Runs the infirmary and teaches the healing half of Alchemy & Healing. Believes no excuse.\n\n@fact amaranth.truth\ntier: personal\nhint: Tell her how you really got hurt, and see.\nunlock: seen(amaranth_intro) > 0\ntext: She treats the true version of an injury faster than the story you tell her, and she always knows which one you told.\n\n@fact amaranth.rounds\ntier: personal\nhint: Watch the infirmary at evening.\nunlock: seen(amaranth_rounds) > 0\ntext: She does evening rounds of every bed herself, and has never once missed one.\n\n@fact amaranth.key\ntier: personal\nhint: Some students are trusted with more than tonics.\nunlock: seen(amaranth_trust) > 0\ntext: Only a handful of students have ever been trusted with the key to her tonic cupboard.\n\n@fact amaranth.count\ntier: personal\nhint: Help her restock over a break.\nunlock: seen(brk_amaranth_restock) > 0\ntext: She counts every remedy twice, because the second count is the true one, and keeps a black jar on the top shelf for a curse not seen since her first year.\n\n@fact amaranth.cupboard\ntier: personal\nhint: Look in the tonic cupboard at teatime.\nunlock: seen(infirmary_tonic_cupboard) > 0\ntext: She leaves the tonic cupboard open at teatime on purpose, to see who will help themselves, and writes their names in a book.\n\n@fact amaranth.book\ntier: personal\nhint: Invent a remedy under her eye.\nunlock: seen(alch_original_remedy) > 0\ntext: She keeps a book of the remedies her students invented, written out in her own square hand.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/bram.grim","text":"# Facts about Bram Hollis.\n\n@fact bram.prefect\ntier: surface\ntext: A prefect of your own year, with a red sash, a ledger of demerits and a fondness for your name.\n\n@fact bram.father\ntier: secret\nhint: Someone's opinion weighs on him more than any rule.\nunlock: seen(bram_crying) > 0 or seen(bram_father_visit) > 0\ntext: His father was head boy, and has told Bram all his life that he will never amount to anything.\n\n@fact bram.hall_tax\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask about the east staircase.\nunlock: seen(bram_hall_tax) > 0\ntext: He once invented a \"hall tax\" for the east staircase.\n\n@fact bram.cheating\ntier: secret\nhint: Prefects sit exams too.\nunlock: seen(bram_cheating) > 0\ntext: You saw him cheat in an exam. He knows you saw.\n\n@fact bram.ally\ntier: personal\nhint: Under the sash, there may be someone else.\nunlock: seen(bram_turns) > 0\ntext: Under the sash, he will step between you and trouble. Then he tells you not to make it weird.\n\n@fact bram.portrait\ntier: personal\nhint: Walk the corridor of head boys with him.\nunlock: seen(bram_portrait) > 0\ntext: His father's portrait hangs in the corridor of head boys. Every visit, his father stops in front of it, and then looks at Bram.\n\n@fact bram.rematch\ntier: personal\nhint: Beat him at something he cares about.\nunlock: seen(bram_rematch) > 0\ntext: When he loses, he practises alone on the duelling green every evening for a month, and then asks for a rematch.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/brask.grim","text":"# Facts about Professor Brask.\n\n@fact brask.beasts\ntier: surface\ntext: Teaches Beastcraft & Flight from the stables, and talks to every creature in them more kindly than to students.\n\n@fact brask.stories\ntier: personal\nhint: Let her tell you a story. Wait for the end.\nunlock: seen(brask_shovel) > 0\ntext: Every one of her stories ends with a bite and a moral. She laughs out loud about once a term.\n\n@fact brask.owl\ntier: personal\nhint: Visit the stable loft at night.\nunlock: seen(brask_night_owl) > 0\ntext: The old barn owl in the stable loft has outlived three of her colleagues. She would not say which loss hurt most.\n\n@fact brask.courtesies\ntier: personal\nhint: She has manners for things that live in the wood.\nunlock: seen(brask_trust) > 0\ntext: She bows to the things that live in the Whisperwood, and believes it's why they have never bitten her. Much.\n\n@fact brask.feather\ntier: personal\nhint: She lost something in the Whisperwood one winter.\nunlock: seen(item_quill_brask) > 0\ntext: Her barn owl moulted in the Whisperwood one winter, and she looked for the lost flight feather for a month.\n\n@fact brask.blackthorn\ntier: secret\nhint: She knows wands by more than their look.\nunlock: seen(item_thorn_brask) > 0\ntext: She knows a blackthorn wand by its smell, and she knows the woman in the wood who cuts them.\n\n@fact brask.asking\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask her for a saddle.\nunlock: seen(beast_lake_flight) > 0\ntext: She never thinks less of a student for asking for help. She just remembers that they asked.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/corvin.grim","text":"# Facts about Corvin Ashby.\n\n@fact corvin.locks\ntier: surface\ntext: Knows every locked door in the academy and owes money to at least three people.\n\n@fact corvin.last_warning\ntier: personal\nhint: One more detention would cost him more than an evening.\nunlock: seen(corvin_rooftop) > 0\ntext: He is on his last warning. One more detention and they send him home, and home is not a good place.\n\n@fact corvin.brother\ntier: secret\nhint: Not all of his family writes kindly.\nunlock: seen(corvin_brother) > 0\ntext: His brother turns up for money and threatens to tell the headmistress where Corvin learned to pick locks.\n\n@fact corvin.grandfather\ntier: personal\nhint: The confiscation vault keeps more than contraband.\nunlock: seen(corvin_confiscation_vault) > 0\ntext: His grandfather's lucky deck sat in the confiscation vault for decades. It really is lucky.\n\n@fact corvin.cartographers\ntier: secret\nhint: Some clubs don't put up posters.\nunlock: member(cartographers)\ntext: He runs the Midnight Cartographers, the club that doesn't exist.\n\n@fact corvin.trolls\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask him about money, if you dare.\nunlock: seen(corvin_debt) > 0\ntext: He once owed thirty crowns to trolls and offered to pay you back with a goat.\n\n@fact corvin.sundays\ntier: personal\nhint: There's one person he talks about without a joke.\nunlock: seen(corvin_lucky_deck) > 0\ntext: His grandfather could find a lost thing in any house, and taught him to shuffle on rainy Sundays. He's the one person Corvin talks about without a joke.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/grimsby.grim","text":"# Facts about Mr Grimsby.\n\n@fact grimsby.books\ntier: surface\ntext: Keeps the village bookshop. He will find anything except what you came in for.\n\n@fact grimsby.wrong\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask him for a book, and take what he gives you.\nunlock: seen(grimsby_intro) > 0\ntext: The book he gives you instead is, more often than not, the one you needed.\n\n@fact grimsby.parcels\ntier: personal\nhint: Some students get post from the bookshop.\nunlock: seen(grimsby_parcel) > 0\ntext: He posts books to students he thinks need them, on credit, and has never once chased a debt.\n\n@fact grimsby.lighthouses\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask where he goes when the shop is shut.\nunlock: seen(grimsby_mind_the_shop) > 0\ntext: He has a weakness for lighthouses, and when he closes the shop, it is usually to go and look at one.\n\n@fact grimsby.nose\ntier: personal\nhint: Let him choose for you, eyes shut.\nunlock: seen(grimsbys_by_the_nose) > 0\ntext: He finds a book by its smell. The right book, he says, smells right.\n\n@fact grimsby.upper_floor\ntier: personal\nhint: Climb the ladder at the back of the shop.\nunlock: seen(grimsbys_upper_floor) > 0\ntext: Above the shop is a floor of remainders nobody mentions, up a ladder whose third and fourth rungs break.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/grisk.grim","text":"# Facts about Grisk.\n\n@fact grisk.lender\ntier: surface\ntext: A goblin moneylender in a velvet waistcoat: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six months.\n\n@fact grisk.ledger\ntier: personal\nhint: Pass the time of day with him.\nunlock: met(grisk) and seen(grisk_pleasantries) > 0\ntext: He calls debts \"friendships\", and keeps all of them in one ledger he never lets out of reach.\n\n@fact grisk.favours\ntier: personal\nhint: He doesn't always want coin.\nunlock: seen(grisk_favour) > 0\ntext: He sometimes asks for favours instead of coin, and the favours are for clients he never names.\n\n@fact grisk.newt_table\ntier: personal\nhint: Sit with him at the Newt more than once.\nunlock: rel(grisk) >= 4 and seen(grisk_pleasantries) > 0\ntext: He keeps the corner table at the Gilded Newt, and talks about money the way other people talk about weather: which shops are in trouble, and why.\n\n@fact grisk.collectors\ntier: personal\nhint: Fall behind on a payment.\nunlock: seen(grisk_collectors) > 0\ntext: When a debt runs late, two trolls in waistcoats come to collect. They are polite, and very thorough.\n\n@fact grisk.parcels\ntier: secret\nhint: He has work that only happens at midnight.\nunlock: seen(grisk_pickup) > 0\ntext: He runs parcels in oilcloth from the boathouse jetty at midnight to a cobbler's back door in the village. Nobody opens them.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/ilse.grim","text":"# Facts about Ilse Dumont.\n\n@fact ilse.furs\ntier: surface\ntext: Kael's younger sister: white furs, a private snowstorm and coins that freeze to counters.\n\n@fact ilse.raised\ntier: personal\nhint: Earn her trust, and she may tell you what she was raised for.\nunlock: rel(ilse) >= 12\ntext: The Hollow cult raised her to open a door she has never seen.\n\n@fact ilse.frost_craft\ntier: personal\nhint: She might teach you, if you trade.\nunlock: seen(ilse_frost_lessons) > 0\ntext: She knows Northern frost-craft better than anything the professors teach.\n\n@fact ilse.grey\ntier: secret\nhint: Someone has come looking for her.\nunlock: seen(ilse_grey_returns) > 0\ntext: The man in grey came to take her home. She didn't call for help; she only looked.\n\n@fact ilse.scared\ntier: secret\nhint: Brother and sister still haven't really talked.\nunlock: seen(ilse_kael_reunion) > 0\ntext: She admits, to her brother, that she was scared. So was he.\n\n@fact ilse.stitch\ntier: personal\nhint: Look closely at a frost-ward's thread.\nunlock: seen(item_frost_ward_thread) > 0\ntext: The stitch in her frost-wards is her mother's. Ilse learned it from her; Kael knows it at a touch.\n\n@fact ilse.useless\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask her to teach you something.\nunlock: seen(ilse_frost_craft_trade) > 0\ntext: She thinks the hardest thing to learn is something that isn't useful. She counts out loud in Northern when she plays.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/isolde.grim","text":"# Facts about Headmistress Isolde Marrow.\n\n@fact isolde.names\ntier: surface\ntext: She remembers every student's name, and every student's mistakes.\n\n@fact isolde.portrait\ntier: secret\nhint: Look for the corridor nobody uses.\nunlock: seen(isolde_portrait) > 0\ntext: As a student she set the observatory on fire twice. Her portrait from those days is hidden in a forgotten corridor.\n\n@fact isolde.chess\ntier: personal\nhint: Visit her tower when she's at her window.\nunlock: seen(isolde_chess) > 0\ntext: She plays tower chess by her window and laughs, short and delighted, when she loses.\n\n@fact isolde.thread\ntier: secret\nhint: She is following something too.\nunlock: flag(warden_trial) or seen(hs_isolde_summons) > 0\ntext: She has been following the same thread you have: the seal beneath the lake is weakening.\n\n@fact isolde.mercy\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask her what she thinks is just.\nunlock: seen(isolde_judgement) > 0\ntext: She asks students what they think is just, and sometimes she listens.\n\n@fact isolde.vrell\ntier: personal\nhint: An old name is crossed out in the academy's ledger.\nunlock: seen(item_grimoire_isolde) > 0\ntext: She knows the name Absalom Vrell: expelled long before her time, and still in the ledger, crossed out.\n\n@fact isolde.really\ntier: personal\nhint: Take tea with her in her tower.\nunlock: seen(headmistress_tower_tea) > 0\ntext: She asks how your term is going, and then how it is really going, and waits for the second answer.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/kael.grim","text":"# Facts about Kael Dumont.\n\n@fact kael.north\ntier: surface\ntext: A transfer from the frozen North who arrived mid-term with frost in his eyelashes.\n\n@fact kael.cold_hands\ntier: personal\nhint: Notice his hands.\nunlock: seen(kael_cold_hands) > 0\ntext: His hands are always cold. Nobody has ever been warm enough before.\n\n@fact kael.sky\ntier: personal\nhint: Find him on the roof at night.\nunlock: seen(astro_kael_roof) > 0\ntext: He watches a patch of sky with no stars in it: a star that eats light.\n\n@fact kael.family\ntier: secret\nhint: Earn his trust, and ask what he's running from.\nunlock: flag(kael_told)\ntext: His family serve something under the lake. They sent him to open its door; he came to stop them.\n\n@fact kael.sister\ntier: secret\nhint: He has family he doesn't mention.\nunlock: flag(met_sister) or seen(ilse_village) > 0\ntext: He has a younger sister, Ilse, raised to do what he wouldn't.\n\n@fact kael.mother\ntier: personal\nhint: Under the northern lights, he might talk.\nunlock: seen(kael_northern_lights) > 0\ntext: His mother never once said she was proud of him.\n\n@fact kael.wanting\ntier: secret\nhint: Some things he was taught not to say.\nunlock: seen(kael_confession) > 0\ntext: He was taught not to want things, because wanting is how they control you.\n\n@fact kael.thaw\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask how the North says goodbye.\nunlock: seen(kael_until_the_thaw) > 0\ntext: In the North nobody says goodbye; it's unlucky. You say \"until the thaw\" and touch foreheads, so the cold knows you're coming back.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/lyra.grim","text":"# Facts about Lyra Vaneshade (design §10.1). Tiers: surface (known on meeting),\n# personal (closeness), secret (specific events). Shown in the People panel in phase 4.\n\n@fact lyra.prodigy\ntier: surface\ntext: The pyromancy prodigy of House Vaneshade. Everyone knows the name, and she makes sure of it.\n\n@fact lyra.toast\ntier: personal\nhint: Catch her at breakfast.\nunlock: seen(lyra_toast) > 0\ntext: She can incinerate a target at forty paces. She cannot make toast.\n\n@fact lyra.mother\ntier: personal\nhint: Get closer, and ask about her letters home.\nunlock: rel(lyra) >= 18\ntext: Her mother has never once told her \"well done\".\n\n@fact lyra.second_place\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask her about the Winter Trials.\nunlock: seen(lyra_letter) > 0\ntext: She came second in the Winter Trials once. Her mother wrote to ask who beat her.\n\n@fact lyra.burnout\ntier: secret\nhint: Watch how late her lamp burns.\nunlock: seen(lyra_burnout) > 0\ntext: She studies until she collapses, and begs you not to let her mother find out.\n\n@fact lyra.rival\ntier: personal\nhint: Be the rival she can't shake.\nunlock: bond(lyra) == \"rival\"\ntext: You are the only person here who makes her better. She hates it. She doesn't want it to stop.\n\n@fact lyra.inheritance\ntier: secret\nhint: Her mother may have something to say about you.\nunlock: flag(lyra_free)\ntext: Her mother threatened her inheritance over you. She set the letter on fire.\n\n@fact lyra.wildfire\ntier: personal\nhint: Get truly close to her.\nunlock: seen(lyra_become_close) > 0\ntext: Vaneshades sleep exactly eight hours; she can't sleep. Her grandmother's spell, Wildfire, was only for people she trusted, and Lyra has never had anyone to teach it to.\n\n@fact lyra.flourish\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask her to teach you something of her family's.\nunlock: seen(lyra_teach_spell) > 0\ntext: Her family has opened its duels with the same ribbon-of-flame flourish for two hundred years. Teaching it outside the family is not done.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/mabel.grim","text":"# Facts about Mabel.\n\n@fact mabel.teashop\ntier: surface\ntext: Keeps the tea shop in the village: forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake.\n\n@fact mabel.door\ntier: personal\nhint: Open her door in a mood.\nunlock: seen(mabel_intro) > 0\ntext: She reads your mood from the way you open her door, and is rarely wrong.\n\n@fact mabel.tins\ntier: personal\nhint: Some students get tea on the barge.\nunlock: seen(mabel_parcel) > 0\ntext: She sends tins of tea up to the castle on the barge for students she's worried about, and refuses payment for them.\n\n@fact mabel.cups\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask about the cups behind her counter.\nunlock: seen(mabel_cup) > 0\ntext: The cups on the hooks behind her counter belong to her regulars, living and dead. She remembers every one of them.\n\n@fact mabel.petal\ntier: surface\ntext: Calls everyone \"petal\", and means it more for some than others.\n\n@fact mabel.fourth_cake\ntier: personal\nhint: Be brave about the cake.\nunlock: seen(mabels_fourth_cake) > 0\ntext: Beside her three cakes there is sometimes a fourth, experimental and free, and she keeps a card on each: its name, the date, and the survivors.\n\n@fact mabel.range\ntier: personal\nhint: Come in on a cold morning.\nunlock: seen(mabels_cold_range) > 0\ntext: Her range goes out whenever the wind is in the north. Anyone who helps her light it is \"staff, near enough\", and carries the milk.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/marisol.grim","text":"# Facts about Professor Marisol.\n\n@fact marisol.tides\ntier: surface\ntext: Teaches the tidecalling half of Evocation, waist-deep in the lake at dawn, and keeps the lake calm on exam days.\n\n@fact marisol.poems\ntier: personal\nhint: Walk with her in the rain.\nunlock: seen(marisol_intro) > 0\ntext: She reads poems about water in the rain, under a dry patch of air, and never remembers their last lines.\n\n@fact marisol.storms\ntier: personal\nhint: Find her on the worst day of the year.\nunlock: seen(marisol_storm) > 0\ntext: She goes out into the worst storms of the year on purpose. She says nobody learns tides on a calm day.\n\n@fact marisol.margins\ntier: personal\nhint: Her teaching comes from an old book.\nunlock: seen(marisol_trust) > 0\ntext: She wrote her own tidecalling into the margins of a book of poems when she was a student, and still teaches from it.\n\n@fact marisol.ice\ntier: personal\nhint: Pass by when she is in the lake.\nunlock: seen(marisol_cold_water) > 0\ntext: She stays in the lake when there is ice at the edges, and whoops like a child whenever a student's water rises.\n\n@fact marisol.spirits\ntier: personal\nhint: Swim her last test.\nunlock: seen(tide_water_spirit_race) > 0\ntext: She says the lake's water spirits always cheat, and that asking them nicely not to is the whole point of tidecalling.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/maud.grim","text":"# Facts about Old Maud.\n\n@fact maud.groundskeeper\ntier: surface\ntext: Groundskeeper and hedge-witch, keeper of bees, chickens and a goat called Bartholomew.\n\n@fact maud.student\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask her what the lake was like, long ago.\nunlock: seen(maud_old_student) > 0\ntext: She was a student here sixty years ago, the spring the lake froze.\n\n@fact maud.edmund\ntier: secret\nhint: She keeps a wand that isn't hers.\nunlock: seen(maud_old_wand) > 0\ntext: Edmund went down beneath the lake that night and didn't come back. She kept his silver wand.\n\n@fact maud.soup\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask for her recipe. You won't get it.\nunlock: seen(maud_recipe) > 0\ntext: Her soup cures colds, broken hearts and most curses. You're not allowed to write it down.\n\n@fact maud.garden\ntier: personal\nhint: Look behind the potting shed.\nunlock: seen(garden_maud_secret) > 0\ntext: Behind the potting shed there is a door to a garden that only exists for people she shows it to.\n\n@fact maud.age\ntier: personal\nhint: Visit when she's under the weather.\nunlock: seen(maud_ill) > 0\ntext: She is eighty-one, and says she's allowed the occasional chill.\n\n@fact maud.tuppence\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask about her first winter as groundskeeper.\nunlock: seen(maud_tuppence) > 0 or seen(ww_cocooned) > 0\ntext: In her first winter as groundskeeper she raised a hatchling spider called Tuppence in the potting shed. Tuppence is the Spinner of the Hush now.\n\n@fact maud.blazes\ntier: personal\nhint: Someone marked the old way into the Whisperwood.\nunlock: seen(ww_mastery_map) > 0\ntext: The little bees carved on the Whisperwood's birches are hers, from when she used to go down to the Hush to visit Tuppence. Her knees won't take it now.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/mir.grim","text":"# Facts about Mir Ten (they/them).\n# Formerly \"Mira\" (id `mira`); renamed 2026-10-15. See the note on `@npc mir` in cast.grim.\n\n@fact mir.artificer\ntier: surface\ntext: An artificer who smells of solder and ozone and has started at least one small fire.\n\n@fact mir.owl\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask what their clockwork owl remembers.\nunlock: seen(mir_owl_gift) > 0\ntext: Built a clockwork owl that remembers deadlines, birthdays and, slightly, grudges.\n\n@fact mir.notebooks\ntier: personal\nhint: Get close enough to be shown the notebooks.\nunlock: rel(mir) >= 12\ntext: Three years of notebooks are the most precious things they own.\n\n@fact mir.family\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask about the family business.\nunlock: seen(mir_family_business) > 0\ntext: Their family runs a clockworks and thinks invention is a hobby. It is not a hobby.\n\n@fact mir.name_day\ntier: secret\nhint: Find out when their name day is.\nunlock: seen(mir_name_day) > 0\ntext: Nobody ever remembered their name day before.\n\n@fact mir.workshop\ntier: personal\nhint: Count the fires this term.\nunlock: seen(mir_workshop_closed) > 0\ntext: After the third fire in a term, the faculty padlocked their workshop.\n\n@fact mir.corridor\ntier: personal\nhint: See what they do when the door is locked.\nunlock: seen(mir_padlock) > 0\ntext: Locked out of a workshop, they will solder on the corridor floor for a week rather than stop. It's character-building, they say.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/nessa.grim","text":"# Facts about Nessa Quill.\n\n@fact nessa.editor\ntier: surface\ntext: Editor of the Lumen Ledger, with a quill that writes on its own.\n\n@fact nessa.quill\ntier: personal\nhint: Watch her quill when she isn't.\nunlock: seen(nessa_burnout) > 0\ntext: Left unsupervised, her quill starts inventing the news.\n\n@fact nessa.interview\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask her what story she wants most.\nunlock: rel(nessa) >= 12\ntext: Nobody has interviewed the headmistress in twenty years. She means to be the first.\n\n@fact nessa.string\ntier: secret\nhint: See what's pinned to her office wall.\nunlock: seen(nessa_hollow_story) > 0\ntext: Her office wall is covered in red string around a sketch of a black star.\n\n@fact nessa.press\ntier: personal\nhint: See what happens if someone threatens the Ledger.\nunlock: seen(nessa_paper_threatened) > 0\ntext: She would chain herself to the printing press before letting anyone shut the Ledger.\n\n@fact nessa.off_record\ntier: secret\nhint: There's one phrase that stops her quill.\nunlock: seen(nessa_confession) > 0\ntext: The only time her quill stops is when she tells it the conversation is off the record.\n\n@fact nessa.planted\ntier: personal\nhint: Check her proofs for her.\nunlock: seen(nessa_fact_check) > 0\ntext: She plants one error in every proof she asks you to check, to see whether you'll find it.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/pell.grim","text":"# Facts about Old Pell.\n\n@fact pell.apothecary\ntier: surface\ntext: Runs the drawers at Pell & Daughters. He taught three generations of the village to count drops.\n\n@fact pell.counting\ntier: personal\nhint: Count with him.\nunlock: seen(pell_count_drops) > 0\ntext: He makes every new clerk count aloud. He trusts people who measure twice.\n\n@fact pell.daughters\ntier: personal\nhint: Meet the daughters behind the counter.\nunlock: seen(pell_daughters) > 0\ntext: His daughters run the counter and want the shop to change; he wants it to be fifty years ago. They're all secretly proud of each other.\n\n@fact pell.spoon\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask about the brass spoon.\nunlock: seen(offer_measuring_spoon) > 0\ntext: His brass measuring spoon hasn't been wrong in forty years. His daughters learned to measure on it, and say it's theirs.\n\n@fact pell.oldest_drawer\ntier: personal\nhint: Restock the bottom of his wall.\nunlock: seen(pell_oldest_drawer) > 0\ntext: His oldest drawer holds a yellowed card for a draught \"of your own strength\", in a hand older than his. He has labelled it \"Not yet.\"\n\n@fact pell.humming\ntier: personal\nhint: Listen to his drawers.\nunlock: seen(pell_apothecary_humming_drawer) > 0\ntext: Some of his thousand drawers hum. He relabels them himself, in a precise hand: ASK FIRST, NOT TODAY.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/seraphine.grim","text":"# Facts about Seraphine Hale.\n\n@fact seraphine.seer\ntier: surface\ntext: A seer who speaks in half-sentences and is unsettlingly often right.\n\n@fact seraphine.slips\ntier: personal\nhint: Sometimes she isn't quite in today.\nunlock: seen(seraphine_lost_day) > 0\ntext: Sometimes she slips a day ahead and needs someone to tell her what today is.\n\n@fact seraphine.oracle\ntier: personal\nhint: Get close, and ask about home.\nunlock: rel(seraphine) >= 15\ntext: Her mother is the Oracle of Vell, who wants her home to tell kings what they want to hear.\n\n@fact seraphine.self\ntier: personal\nhint: Stand next to her while she paints.\nunlock: seen(seraphine_painting) > 0\ntext: Seers can't see themselves, unless someone is standing next to them.\n\n@fact seraphine.lake\ntier: secret\nhint: Ask what she hears at night.\nunlock: flag(seraphine_lake_told)\ntext: The lake calls to her at night. She says it calls to you too.\n\n@fact seraphine.too_loud\ntier: secret\nhint: Find out where she hides.\nunlock: seen(seraphine_overwhelmed) > 0\ntext: When everyone's futures get too loud she hides in the dark. Yours, she says, is quiet.\n\n@fact seraphine.paintings\ntier: personal\nhint: Look under her bed. Or ask.\nunlock: seen(seraphine_dont_look) > 0 or seen(brk_starfall_observatory) > 0\ntext: What she sees ahead comes out in her paintings, sometimes months early. She hides the ones she didn't mean to paint under her bed.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/tamsin.grim","text":"# Facts about Tamsin Reed (playtest 2, X9).\n\n@fact tamsin.thornwick\ntier: surface\ntext: An illusion student at Thornwick, in your year, who writes to you.\n\n@fact tamsin.theatre\ntier: personal\nhint: Keep writing back.\nunlock: rel(tamsin) >= 8\ntext: Their grandmother runs a travelling theatre; they learned illusion painting her backdrops.\n\n@fact tamsin.assigned\ntier: secret\nhint: Ask why they wrote to you in the first place.\nunlock: seen(tamsin_confession) > 0\ntext: Thornwick told them to write to a Lumenhold student and report back. They stopped reporting by the third letter.\n\n@fact tamsin.pie\ntier: personal\nhint: Answer their second letter.\nunlock: seen(tamsin_second_letter) > 0\ntext: Their roommate bet them a pie that you'd never write back. They won the pie.\n\n@fact tamsin.sketches\ntier: personal\nhint: Let them tell you the Thornwick gossip.\nunlock: seen(tamsin_letter_gossip) > 0\ntext: They fill their letters with sketches of Thornwick's professors in the margins. Their illusion professor is usually a goat.\n\n@fact tamsin.failed\ntier: personal\nhint: Read the short letter.\nunlock: seen(tamsin_letter_trouble) > 0\ntext: They once failed their illusion examination, the one thing they were good at, in the same term their grandmother's theatre was sold.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/thorne.grim","text":"# Facts about Professor Aldric Thorne.\n\n@fact thorne.storm\ntier: surface\ntext: Rumoured to have once out-duelled a storm.\n\n@fact thorne.lost\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask about the storm he fought.\nunlock: seen(thorne_storm_story) > 0\ntext: He lost to the storm, badly. Losing well, he says, is the only thing worth teaching.\n\n@fact thorne.roses\ntier: secret\nhint: Walk past the observatory at dawn.\nunlock: seen(thorne_roses) > 0\ntext: He tends a tiny walled rose garden behind the observatory at dawn and talks to the roses.\n\n@fact thorne.hands\ntier: secret\nhint: Watch his hands in class.\nunlock: seen(thorne_shaking_hands) > 0\ntext: His hands have started to shake. Age comes for every storm eventually.\n\n@fact thorne.rival\ntier: personal\nhint: Someone has wanted a rematch for forty years.\nunlock: seen(thorne_old_rival) > 0\ntext: A flamboyant old wizard in purple has wanted a rematch with him for forty years.\n\n@fact thorne.letters\ntier: personal\nhint: Earn his respect. He writes very few letters.\nunlock: rel(thorne) >= 18\ntext: He writes very few letters of recommendation, and the Conclave reads every one.\n\n@fact thorne.accusation\ntier: personal\nhint: Disappoint him.\nunlock: seen(thorne_disappointed) > 0\ntext: \"You're better than this\" is, from him, not a compliment. He says so himself: it's an accusation.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/tobias.grim","text":"# Facts about Tobias Fennick.\n\n@fact tobias.scholarship\ntier: surface\ntext: A farmer's son on a turnip scholarship, built like a barn door and gentle as a lamb.\n\n@fact tobias.plants\ntier: surface\ntext: He talks to plants. Worryingly, they seem to answer.\n\n@fact tobias.ma\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask about harvest at home.\nunlock: seen(tobias_homesick) > 0\ntext: His mother runs the farm alone at harvest. It's the first harvest he has ever missed.\n\n@fact tobias.gerald\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask about the mandrake in the hat.\nunlock: seen(tobias_godparent) > 0\ntext: He is raising a mandrake called Gerald, who wears a knitted hat and screams softly.\n\n@fact tobias.moonflowers\ntier: personal\nhint: Look in the greenhouse at night.\nunlock: rel(tobias) >= 12\ntext: The greenhouse moonflowers bloom once every eleven years. He would stand in a storm for them.\n\n@fact tobias.blight\ntier: secret\nhint: Home may be having a bad year.\nunlock: seen(tobias_bad_harvest) > 0\ntext: Blight took his family's harvest. Thirty crowns stood between them and a hungry winter.\n\n@fact tobias.dancing\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask him about the Midwinter Ball.\nunlock: seen(tobias_dance_lessons) > 0\ntext: He practised waltzing with a broom before the Midwinter Ball. The broom complained.\n\n@fact tobias.after\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask what he'll do after graduation.\nunlock: seen(tobias_home_or_here) > 0\ntext: His mother expects him home on the farm after graduation. It was always the plan; he isn't sure any more that it's his.\n"},{"path":"content/world/facts/vey.grim","text":"# Facts about Professor Vey.\n\n@fact vey.illusion\ntier: surface\ntext: Teaches Illusion & Glamour and grades on whether you fooled her.\n\n@fact vey.tests\ntier: personal\nhint: Expect a question in a corridor.\nunlock: seen(vey_pop_quiz) > 0\ntext: She tests students in corridors, unannounced, with tea that isn't there. She keeps score.\n\n@fact vey.revue\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask about the faculty revue.\nunlock: seen(vey_revue) > 0\ntext: She produces the faculty revue every year, and has never once let Professor Thorne refuse a part.\n\n@fact vey.mirror\ntier: personal\nhint: Ask why she never uses the mirror charm.\nunlock: seen(vey_trust) > 0\ntext: She doesn't use the mirror charm in public. Once, her reflection didn't come back for a week.\n\n@fact vey.smell\ntier: personal\nhint: Bake something false in her class.\nunlock: seen(illu_bread_smell) > 0\ntext: She teaches that sight is easy to fool; smell is where liars are caught.\n\n@fact vey.own_face\ntier: personal\nhint: Change one small thing about yourself, in her class.\nunlock: seen(illu_one_small_change) > 0\ntext: She has every student glamour their own face first: a glamour you can't hold on yourself, she says, won't hold on anyone.\n"},{"path":"content/world/items.grim","text":"# ============================================================================\n#  The kit (world expansion 1, K2): what a student can own and wear.\n#  One wand, one robe and one trinket are worn; worn effects act in fights only, as flat numbers. Owning an item\n#  adds options to cards where it fits (`> up[item:x]:`). Amounts in `price:` are what the pawnbroker pays.\n#  Format: docs/grimoire-script.md (@item). Where each comes from: the plan's K2 table and the events that give them.\n# ============================================================================\n\n# ---------------- Wands ----------------\n\n@item borrowed_wand\nname: Borrowed Wand\nslot: wand\nart: wand\nstart: true\ndesc: The academy's loan to every first-year: scuffed beech, a chipped tip and somebody else's initials on the handle. It works. Mostly.\n\n@item hazel_wand\nname: Hazelwood Wand\nslot: wand\nart: wand\nprice: small\nworn: mana +1\ndesc: Mir's work: light, springy hazel with a copper band. It warms up before you do.\n\n@item ashwood_wand\nname: Ashwood Duelling Wand\nslot: wand\nart: wand\nprice: medium\nworn: on_defend strike 2\ndesc: A duellist's wand, dense and unforgiving. Anything that tries to block it gets a shock for its trouble.\n\n@item heartwood_wand\nname: Heartwood Wand\nslot: wand\nart: wand\nprice: medium\nworn: mana +2\ndesc: Carved by Mir from a splinter the elder oak gave you. The grain moves when no one is looking.\n\n@item edmunds_silver_wand\nname: Edmund's Silver Wand\nslot: wand\nart: wand\nlegacy: flag(edmunds_wand)\nworn: cost ward -2\ndesc: Slender silver, sixty years old, wrapped for decades in a faded scarf. Near the lake it hums one low note.\n\n@item thorn_wand\nname: Thorn Wand\nslot: wand\nart: wand\nworn: on_cast forbidden spell 2\ndesc: Blackthorn, still bristling, still faintly green at the core. It wants to be used, and it does not much care for what.\ntick:\n  alignment -tiny\n  say: {~The thorn wand pricks your palm when you pick it up, as if it were keeping count.|You dream of brambles growing over a door. The thorn wand is warm in the morning.}\n\n# ---------------- Robes ----------------\n\n@item school_robes\nname: School Robes\nslot: robe\nart: mask\nstart: true\nprice: tiny\ndesc: Regulation black with the house trim, a size too big in the sleeves. Everyone has a pair.\n\n@item duelling_coat\nname: Quilted Duelling Coat\nslot: robe\nart: shield\nprice: small\nworn: hp +3, cost brace -1\ndesc: Padded, stitched in diamonds, smelling faintly of the duelling hall. Bracing in it feels like leaning on a wall.\n\n@item ward_cloak\nname: Ward-Stitched Cloak\nslot: robe\nart: shield\nprice: medium\nworn: hp +2, taken -2 from flame hoarfrost starlight\ndesc: Amaranth's ward thread runs through the hem in a pattern too fine to see. Fire and frost slide off it like rain.\n\n@item midnight_robes\nname: Midnight-Blue Robes\nslot: robe\nart: crown\nprice: small\nworn: hp +2\ndesc: Deep blue wool with silver trim and a lining like the night sky. People stand up a little straighter when you walk in.\n\n@item spinner_silk_robe\nname: Spinner-Silk Robe\nslot: robe\nart: spider\nprice: large\nworn: hp +6, cost shadowstep -1, cost haste -1\ndesc: Grey silk that drinks the light, sewn by the village tailor from what the Spinner left you. You move in it like a rumour.\n\n# ---------------- Trinkets ----------------\n\n@item ilse_frost_ward\nname: Ilse's Frost-Ward\nslot: trinket\nart: snow\nlegacy: has(frost_ward)\nworn: taken -2 from flame wildfire\ndesc: A sliver of Northern winter that Ilse sewed into your collar. Cold things think twice about you.\ntick:\n  arcana +1\n  health +1\n\n@item black_star_coin\nname: Black-Star Coin\nslot: trinket\nart: coin\nlegacy: flag(star_coin)\nbound: true\nworn: cost umbral -1, cost forbidden -1\ndesc: Black, cold as ice and heavier than it should be, stamped with a hollow star. You keep meaning to throw it away.\n\n@item lodestone_pendant\nname: Lodestone Pendant\nslot: trinket\nart: compass\nprice: small\ndesc: A dark stone on a leather cord that turns, very slowly, to face north. In the Whisperwood it is never wrong.\n\n@item stag_tine\nname: Stag's Tine\nslot: trinket\nart: paw\nprice: small\nworn: taken -2 from nonhuman\ndesc: A tine shed by the shadow stag, smooth as old ivory. Wild things look at it before they look at you.\n\n@item owl_quill\nname: Owl-Feather Quill\nslot: trinket\nart: feather\nprice: tiny\ndesc: A barn-owl feather cut to a nib. Notes taken with it are tidier than the hand that wrote them.\n\n@item moonpetal_locket\nname: Moonpetal Locket\nslot: trinket\nart: moon\nprice: medium\nworn: mana +2\ndesc: A plain silver locket with one pressed moonpetal inside. It opens on its own at midnight.\n\n@item pocket_almanac\nname: Pocket Almanac\nslot: trinket\nart: book\nprice: tiny\ndesc: Tides, moons and weather for fifty years, in print you need a lens for. Grimsby's pencil notes run down every margin.\n\n@item measuring_spoon\nname: Pell's Measuring Spoon\nslot: trinket\nart: potion\ndesc: A brass spoon worn thin on one side by forty years of exact quarters. Pell's daughters say it is theirs.\n\n@item honey_tin\nname: Honey Tin\nslot: trinket\nart: flower\ndesc: A dented tin of Maud's silver-bee honey. Shared, it opens doors; the bees seem to know it is yours.\n\n@item outcast_grimoire\nname: The Outcast's Grimoire\nslot: trinket\nart: book\nworn: cost forbidden -2, on_cast forbidden spell 2\ndesc: A black-bound book of forbidden practice in a cramped, elegant hand. Its pages are cold, and some of them are blank until midnight.\n"},{"path":"content/world/lore/soft/academy.grim","text":"# Lore drafts (canon: soft): the academy and its customs.\n\n@lore lumenhold\nkeys: Lumenhold, academy, seven towers, bridges of light\ncanon: soft\ntext: Lumenhold Academy is seven towers joined by bridges of light, standing above a mirror-still lake. It has trained mages for three hundred years and has been a seal for exactly as long.\n\n@lore mirror_lake\nkeys: Mirror Lake, lake\ncanon: soft\ntext: The lake is so still it shows the towers upside down. Students swim in it in summer and catch Lake Fever when they shouldn't. Some nights it calls to seers.\n\n@lore houses\nkeys: houses, Emberhall, Rootward, Stillwater, Duskgate, Brasswick\ncanon: soft\ntext: Students belong to five houses: Emberhall (fire and ambition), Rootward (growing things), Stillwater (quiet and the far-sighted), Duskgate (shadows and secrets) and Brasswick (makers and tinkerers). Faculty belong to none.\n\n@lore crowns\nkeys: crowns, money, currency\ncanon: soft\ntext: The currency is the crown. Rent and board at the academy cost about ten crowns a month; a good meal in the village, one; a registered familiar, twenty.\n\n@lore calendar\nkeys: Harvestmoon, Emberwane, Mistveil, Frostcall, Deepwinter, Thawmoon, Stormbloom, Greenrise, Blossomtide, Highsun, Goldenhaze, Starfall\ncanon: soft\ntext: The academic year starts in autumn with Harvestmoon, then Emberwane, Mistveil, Frostcall, Deepwinter, Thawmoon, Stormbloom, Greenrise, Blossomtide, Highsun, Goldenhaze and Starfall.\n\n@lore festivals\nkeys: Harvest Feast, Lantern Night, Midwinter Ball, Spring Tournament, Starfall festival\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Harvest Feast opens the year; on Lantern Night lanterns carry wishes over the lake; the Midwinter Ball fills the Great Hall; the Spring Tournament crowns a duelling champion; and at Starfall the whole academy sleeps on the lawns to watch the meteors.\n\n@lore exams\nkeys: Winter Trials, Grand Examinations, exams\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Winter Trials test the term's work in Frostcall. The Grand Examinations close the year: three days, five examiners and a practical before a panel that includes the headmistress.\n\n@lore restricted_section\nkeys: Restricted Section, silver key\ncanon: soft\ntext: A locked section at the back of the library, opened by a silver key, where black-bound books breathe softly. Bartholomew the goat once ate a third of \"Curses of the Northern Wastes\" there.\n\n@lore confiscation_vault\nkeys: confiscation vault, contraband\ncanon: soft\ntext: Three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student. Its alarm is a very loud goblin.\n\n@lore gargoyles\nkeys: gargoyle, third gargoyle, passages\ncanon: soft\ntext: The castle is riddled with passages that appear on no official plan. The way into the Midnight Cartographers' world is behind the third gargoyle on the north parapet.\n\n@lore moving_stairs\nkeys: staircases, moving stairs, east wing\ncanon: soft\ntext: Lumenhold's staircases move when they feel like it and occasionally eat shoes, which turn up under the stairs in the east wing.\n\n@lore demerits\nkeys: demerits, prefects, detention, sash\ncanon: soft\ntext: Prefects wear red sashes and keep ledgers of demerits. Detention is usually scrubbing pans or cataloguing contraband. Being stripped of a sash is the worst thing that can happen to a prefect short of expulsion.\n\n@lore familiars\nkeys: familiars, registration, Ember\ncanon: soft\ntext: Students may keep a familiar if it is registered: twenty crowns and a stamp from the registrar. Unregistered familiars are tolerated if nobody notices them, and some don't want to be noticed.\n\n@lore lumen_ledger\nkeys: Lumen Ledger, newspaper, press\ncanon: soft\ntext: The student newspaper, printed at dawn on a press that groans like a dragon. Its gossip column is feared; its editor, Nessa Quill, has a quill that writes on its own.\n\n@lore infirmary\nkeys: infirmary, Sister Amaranth, Lake Fever\ncanon: soft\ntext: Sister Amaranth runs the infirmary with warming charms, tonics and no patience for excuses. Lake Fever comes from swimming where you shouldn't; curse-cold comes from worse.\n\n@lore graduation\nkeys: graduation, speech, yearbook\ncanon: soft\ntext: Each year one graduate speaks for their class in the Great Hall, chosen by the faculty. Graduates plant something, keep something, or leave something behind.\n\n@lore fairs\nkeys: Club Fair, Job Board, Artificers' Fair, Midsummer Fair\ncanon: soft\ntext: In Emberwane the Club Fair fills the courtyard with recruiting tables; the Job Board in the entrance hall posts term-time work. The Artificers' Fair shows off students' machines, and in summer the village holds its Midsummer Fair with an enchanted carousel.\n\n@lore disciplinary_board\nkeys: Disciplinary Board, probation, expulsion\ncanon: soft\ntext: When a student's standing collapses, the Disciplinary Board meets: three professors, a long table and very little mercy. Probation comes first; expulsion second.\n\n@lore lumen_essay_prize\nkeys: Lumen Essay Prize, essay prize\ncanon: soft\ntext: An annual prize of thirty crowns for the best student essay on a set theme, judged by the faculty. Past themes include \"Magic and Duty\".\n\n@lore sorting_mirror\nkeys: Sorting Mirror, entrance hall\ncanon: soft\ntext: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall. It does not sort anyone into anything; it asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came.\n\n@lore spire_library\nkeys: Spire Library, the Spire\ncanon: soft\ntext: The academy's research library at the top of the tallest tower, where theses of distinction are shelved in a section students whisper about.\n\n@lore the_season\nkeys: Season, the Season, patrons\ncanon: soft\ntext: The academy's patrons keep a Season: a round of suppers in their town houses in the village below the hill, closed by the patrons' ball. Students the castle is talking about are sometimes sent a card with a crest. It is an honour, and an inspection.\n\n@lore brightwater\nkeys: Brightwater\ncanon: soft\ntext: A cloudy gold elixir from the village's back rooms, banned at Lumenhold. A dose keeps the body rested and the spirits high for weeks; the weeks after can drag. Grisk deals it.\n\n@lore owls_ink\nkeys: Owl's Ink, owls ink\ncanon: soft\ntext: A bitter black elixir, banned at Lumenhold, that fills a tired student with magic and sharpens the mind like a new nib. Before finals it is worth more than gold; afterwards, for a while, nothing reads right. Grisk deals it, and before finals so do some of the older students.\n\n@lore master_map\nkeys: Master Map, Keeper of the Map, Cartographer\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Midnight Cartographers' Master Map: a great sheet of silver ink that shifts as the castle does. A Keeper looks after it and hands the keeping on when the club decides. For thirty years it had two great blanks: the stair below the Restricted Section's chained shelf, and a stair inside the seventh tower's wall.\n\n@lore deep_stacks\nkeys: deep stacks\ncanon: soft\ntext: Below the last chained shelf of the Restricted Section's east stacks, by the book-return hatch, a stair goes down to the deep stacks: books nobody has signed out in a century, shelves that breathe close enough to close an aisle, and at the bottom, where no lamp reaches, the confiscation vault, behind a prefects' padlock and an alarm that is a very loud goblin.\n"},{"path":"content/world/lore/soft/hollowstar.grim","text":"# Lore drafts (canon: soft), distilled from the existing events in phase 1.\n# Curate: review, correct, then move entries to lore/hard/ with canon: hard (design §3.6).\n# The Hollow Star and the founders' seal.\n\n@lore hollow_star\nkeys: Hollow Star, star, light-eating star, the thing under the lake\ncanon: soft\ntext: A star that is not a light but an absence: it eats light. It sleeps beneath the Mirror Lake, behind a round door. It is not asleep so much as waiting, and it learns the names of those who look for it.\n\n@lore founders_seal\nkeys: founders, seal, seven locks, seven towers\ncanon: soft\ntext: The founders built Lumenhold as a seal over the Hollow Star: seven towers, one lock in each, and a key shaped like a star that holds no light. The seal weakens over generations and must be renewed.\n\n@lore star_key\nkeys: star key, key, seventh tower\ncanon: soft\ntext: The key to the founders' seal is shaped like a star and black as a hole in the world. It was kept in a star-shaped hollow at the top of the seventh tower, behind wards that shriek.\n\n@lore seventh_tower\nkeys: seventh tower, locked stair\ncanon: soft\ntext: One of the seven towers has been locked for a century. At its top is a single room of dust and moonlight. When the star key is lifted, every candle in the academy goes out at once.\n\n@lore round_door\nkeys: door, round door, beneath the lake, tunnel\ncanon: soft\ntext: A tunnel from the boathouse runs down to a round door at the bottom of the lake. On the night the seal weakens, it stands open a crack, and the Star looks through.\n\n@lore drowned_bell\nkeys: drowned bell, bell, lake bell\ncanon: soft\ntext: A bell lies under the Mirror Lake. It tolls when the Star stirs, and once on the night the lake freezes. Edmund's silver wand hums the same note.\n\n@lore lake_freezes\nkeys: frozen lake, the Night, Blossom moon, freeze\ncanon: soft\ntext: When the seal is at its weakest, on the night of the Blossom moon, the lake freezes in a single breath in the middle of spring. Cracks glow beneath the ice and things made of frost and whispers come ashore.\n\n@lore founders_ledger\nkeys: Founders' Ledger, ledger, archive vault\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Founders' Ledger in the archive vault is three hundred years old. The entry for the founding year was cut out with a razor, but the impression of the pen remains on the page beneath. A client of Grisk's once paid to have another page removed.\n\n@lore codex_hollow_names\nkeys: Codex of Hollow Names, codex, Restricted Section\ncanon: soft\ntext: A book in the Restricted Section that finds its readers. Its first page is a list of names; the last is still being written. Reading it turns the fingertips grey.\n\n@lore star_mark\nkeys: star mark, mark, cold coin\ncanon: soft\ntext: Relics of the Star burn so cold they feel hot and can leave a pale star-shaped mark on the palm. The mark hums near the lake and makes spells come easier, which should worry its bearer more than it does.\n\n@lore hollow_dreams\nkeys: dreams, black star dream\ncanon: soft\ntext: Those the Star notices dream of the lake from underneath and wake with frost on their pillows. Lavender doesn't stop the dreams, but it makes them smell nicer.\n\n@lore man_in_grey\nkeys: man in grey, grey coat\ncanon: soft\ntext: A tall, polite man in a grey coat who serves the Star on behalf of the Northern families. Fountains freeze when he talks. He offers power to those who ask questions, and he has come for both Dumont children.\n\n@lore hollow_cult\nkeys: Hollow cult, cult, Northern families\ncanon: soft\ntext: Northern families who serve the Star beneath the lake and send their children south to open its door. Their letters carry a black-star seal.\n\n@lore dumont_family\nkeys: Dumont, Dumonts, Kael, Ilse\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Dumonts are the foremost of the Hollow families. They sent Kael to open the door; when he refused, they raised his younger sister Ilse to do it instead. Their mother has never told either child she was proud.\n\n@lore wardens\nkeys: Wardens, ward-ring, headmistress\ncanon: soft\ntext: The headmistress keeps a quiet circle sworn to the founders' seal. When the seal grows thin she recruits students she trusts and gives them silver ward-rings.\n\n@lore edmund\nkeys: Edmund, silver wand, frozen spring\ncanon: soft\ntext: Sixty years ago, the spring the lake froze, Maud and two friends went down to the boathouse. Edmund was the one who didn't come back. His slender silver wand was meant to go beneath the lake again, with someone who means to close the door.\n\n@lore starcalling\nkeys: Starcalling, named star, starlight\ncanon: soft\ntext: An old discipline of calling down the light of a named star. Light that the Hollow Star cannot swallow is one of the few weapons the founders left.\n\n@lore lumen_and_hollow\nkeys: Lumen, Hollow, light and dark, alignment\ncanon: soft\ntext: Lumenhold talks about people the way it talks about candles: some give light (Lumen), some swallow it (the Hollow). It is not a house or a club, only what people notice. Those who lean toward the Hollow find the Star noticing them back; the way out of it is slow, and usually starts with someone handing you something to do.\n\n@lore star_herald\nkeys: herald, Herald of the Hollow Star, freed\ncanon: soft\ntext: If the round door is opened by one the Star already counts as its own, it goes free instead of swallowing them, and remembers who let it out. The Northern families call such a person its herald.\n\n@lore dumont_mother\nkeys: Madame Dumont, the Dumonts' mother\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Dumonts' mother heads the foremost of the Hollow families. She has never come south, until the Blossom moon of the year the seal is weakest, when she leads the cult's circle on the ice herself. She has never told either of her children she was proud of them.\n"},{"path":"content/world/lore/soft/people.grim","text":"# Lore drafts (canon: soft): minor figures and families.\n\n@lore vaneshade\nkeys: Vaneshade, Lady Vaneshade, black seal, winter gala\ncanon: soft\ntext: House Vaneshade is old pyromancer nobility. Its letters carry a black seal; its winter gala is an inspection disguised as a party; its duellists carry pale family wands and open every duel with the same flame flourish they have used for two hundred years. Vaneshades sleep exactly eight hours. Lady Vaneshade's highest praise is \"Adequate\"; she has a velvet chair reserved at her daughter's bouts, and it stays empty.\n\n@lore oracle_of_vell\nkeys: Oracle of Vell, Vell, family seat\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Oracle of Vell, Seraphine's mother, tells kings what they want to hear and has already seen her daughter say yes to taking the family seat.\n\n@lore fennick_farm\nkeys: Fennick, farm, harvest, blight, turnip scholarship, scholarship board\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Fennick farm grows turnips, tomatoes and stubborn children. Tobias's mother runs it alone at harvest, writes weather first and love last, and expects him home after graduation; one year blight nearly ruined them. Tobias studies on an agricultural scholarship whose board reviews his marks in person.\n\n@lore ten_clockworks\nkeys: Ten family, clockworks, guild medal, name day\ncanon: soft\ntext: Mir's family runs a clockworks, calls invention a hobby and expects Mir home. After a persuasive letter, Mir's mother agreed: \"Fine. Win a prize\": the guild medal for young artificers. The family keeps name days, not birthdays; Mir's father shows almost nobody the escapement of his clocks.\n\n@lore ashby_family\nkeys: Ashby, Corvin's brother, Corvin's grandfather\ncanon: soft\ntext: Corvin's grandfather could find a lost thing in any house, taught Corvin to shuffle on rainy Sundays, and owned a genuinely lucky deck of cards that later sat in the confiscation vault. Corvin's brother turns up at the gates wanting money. Home, Corvin says, is not a good place.\n\n@lore hollis_family\nkeys: Hollis, Bram's father, head boy, head boys' portraits\ncanon: soft\ntext: Bram's father was a prefect and head boy at Lumenhold; his portrait hangs in the corridor of head boys, and he stops in front of it on every visit. He has never stopped telling his son he will not amount to anything.\n\n@lore grisk\nkeys: Grisk, goblin, moneylender, debt\ncanon: soft\ntext: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat, friend to the temporarily embarrassed: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six months. He keeps a ledger and occasionally asks for favours instead of coin.\n\n@lore prudence_ashworth\nkeys: Prudence Ashworth, ghost, socks\ncanon: soft\ntext: A polite ghost from two hundred years ago who rearranges socks to get attention. She was in love with the baker's daughter and never delivered her letter.\n\n@lore footnote\nkeys: Footnote, library cat\ncanon: soft\ntext: The library cat, who sleeps on the reference desk and decides which books are worth reading.\n\n@lore bartholomew\nkeys: Bartholomew, goat\ncanon: soft\ntext: Maud's goat, who has eaten parts of the Restricted Section and most of a sleeve. He likes some students more than others.\n\n@lore gerald\nkeys: Gerald, mandrake\ncanon: soft\ntext: Tobias's mandrake, who wears a knitted hat, screams softly and bites the people he loves.\n\n@lore ember_fox\nkeys: Ember, fox, ember tail\ncanon: soft\ntext: A small fox with a tail that smoulders like an ember. If you free her from a snare she may come back with a ruby, or stay.\n\n@lore pim\nkeys: Pim, first-year, mentee\ncanon: soft\ntext: A nervous first-year who attaches to final-year students in need of a mentor.\n\n@lore duchess_of_ambry\nkeys: Duchess of Ambry, portrait\ncanon: soft\ntext: An escaped portrait who wanders the castle's frames, gossiping and refusing to return to her own.\n\n@lore professors\nkeys: Professor Marisol, Professor Vey, Professor Brask\ncanon: soft\ntext: Professor Marisol teaches Tidecalling and keeps the lake calm on exam days. Professor Vey teaches Illusion and grades on whether you fooled her. Professor Brask teaches Beastcraft and is kinder to animals than to students.\n\n@lore clockwork_owl\nkeys: clockwork owl, brass owl\ncanon: soft\ntext: Mir's brass owl with amber eyes: it hoots the hour, remembers deadlines and birthdays, and refuses to work for Professor Thorne.\n\n@lore thornes_rival\nkeys: wizard in purple, rematch\ncanon: soft\ntext: A flamboyant old wizard in purple who has wanted to settle a forty-year-old matter with Thorne. The rematch lasts four seconds; afterwards both old men laugh until they wheeze.\n\n@lore isolde_youth\nkeys: young headmistress, portrait, observatory fire\ncanon: soft\ntext: Before she was headmistress, Isolde Marrow was the student with the detention record of the decade, who set the observatory on fire twice.\n\n@lore tamsin_reed\nkeys: Tamsin Reed, Tamsin, pen pal\ncanon: soft\ntext: A Thornwick illusionist in the player's year, a pen pal since their first term. Raised in a travelling theatre; sly in letters, loyal in person. Thornwick set them to spy on Lumenhold through their letters, and they gave it up for the friendship.\n\n@lore odile_varn\nkeys: Odile Varn, Odile, the fourth-year's notebook\ncanon: soft\ntext: A fourth-year in Umbral Theory, graduating, who kept a notebook on the Restricted Section in three colours of ink and passed it on, with a silver key to its door, to a student she thought would use it. What she read there frightened her into finishing early.\n"},{"path":"content/world/lore/soft/places.grim","text":"# Lore drafts (canon: soft): places in and beyond the academy.\n\n@lore the_village\nkeys: village, barge, market square, market day, tailor, pawnbroker\ncanon: soft\ntext: The village lies across the lake, reached by barge. On its square stand the bakery's trestle, the tailor's rail of robes and the pawnbroker's creaking sign; on market days Mir keeps a stall there. Round the square are the Gilded Newt, Mabel's tea shop, Grimsby's Books and Pell & Daughters, and their shopkeepers have seen every kind of student.\n\n@lore gilded_newt\nkeys: Gilded Newt, tavern, landlady\ncanon: soft\ntext: The village tavern, with low beams and a fire that never goes out. By day it's a few regulars and the landlady with a cloth over her shoulder; by night it's all elbows and cider. Students work shifts there and card games run late.\n\n@lore mabels\nkeys: Mabel's, tea shop\ncanon: soft\ntext: Forty kinds of tea and three kinds of cake. Mabel reads your mood from the way you open the door.\n\n@lore grimsbys\nkeys: Grimsby's Books, bookshop\ncanon: soft\ntext: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other. Mr Grimsby will find anything except what you came in for.\n\n@lore pell_and_daughters\nkeys: Pell & Daughters, apothecary, Old Pell\ncanon: soft\ntext: The village apothecary, with a thousand labelled drawers. Old Pell taught three generations to count drops; his daughters keep the counter now, and he counts aloud behind them. It buys what foragers bring out of the Whisperwood.\n\n@lore bakery\nkeys: bakery, baker's daughter\ncanon: soft\ntext: The same family has baked in the village for two hundred years. A great-great-great-grandmother waited all her life for a letter from a student who died.\n\n@lore whisperwood\nkeys: Whisperwood, forest, moonpetal, shadow stag, heartwood, waystone\ncanon: soft\ntext: An old forest west of the lake that talks in its sleep. The path through it is safe; off it, the wood goes down and down: the fringe, the thicket, the red-barked heartwood, and at the bottom the Hush. Foragers leave offerings at a worn waystone on the way in, and Pell buys what they bring out. A stag made of shadow walks the deep wood and steps aside only for the brave; Professor Brask bows to it in the bracken.\n\n@lore secret_garden\nkeys: secret garden, silver bees, humming pear tree\ncanon: soft\ntext: Behind the potting shed, a door in the wall leads to Maud's secret garden: glowing ferns, silver bees and a pear tree that hums. It only exists for people Maud has shown it to.\n\n@lore greenhouses\nkeys: greenhouses, moonflower\ncanon: soft\ntext: Glass houses that steam even in winter. The moonflowers there bloom once every eleven years and fill the greenhouse with silver light.\n\n@lore observatory\nkeys: observatory, telescope, east tower\ncanon: soft\ntext: A brass dome atop the east tower. The great telescope points wherever it likes. On cold nights Kael sits on the roof outside it, and frost spreads around him.\n\n@lore boathouse\nkeys: boathouse, jetty\ncanon: soft\ntext: An old boathouse with a jetty over the water, where people say the things they can't say indoors. The tunnel to the round door starts here.\n\n@lore thornwick\nkeys: Thornwick Academy, rival school\ncanon: soft\ntext: The rival academy across the hills, famous for illusionists and tournament grudges. Exchange students come and go with suspicious politeness.\n\n@lore the_north\nkeys: the North, Northern, ice palaces, singing glaciers, northern lights\ncanon: soft\ntext: The frozen North of ice palaces, singing glaciers and green-and-violet lights in the sky. Northerners touch foreheads in farewell and practise a frost-craft older than anything taught at Lumenhold.\n\n@lore port_calder\nkeys: Port Calder, coast, street origin\ncanon: soft\ntext: A rough harbour town on the coast, where students of the street origin learned their trade and where trunks get stolen only once per visit.\n\n@lore conclave\nkeys: Conclave, Conclave of the Seven Towers\ncanon: soft\ntext: The governing body of magic, in the capital. It asks professors for recommendations and runs laboratories where new spells are tested behind thick walls.\n\n@lore capital_institutions\nkeys: Royal Library, Guild of Alchemists, Office of Unseen Affairs, Travelling Circus of Wonders\ncanon: soft\ntext: Beyond the academy lie the Royal Library's archives, the Guild of Alchemists' works, the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs, which has no sign and no address, and the Travelling Circus of Wonders, which recruits graduates who want to wander.\n\n# World expansion 1: the Whisperwood delve (content/events/whisperwood/)\n\n@lore the_hush\nkeys: the Hush, Hush\ncanon: soft\ntext: The deepest part of the Whisperwood, past the heartwood, which wakes only at midnight. Its moonpetal blooms by the clearing-full, and its silence is a thing you can walk into.\n\n@lore the_spinner\nkeys: the Spinner, Tuppence, Spinner, Spinner silk\ncanon: soft\ntext: A spider as big as a carriage, grey, with a voice like a cello, who rules the Hush and keeps worse things out of it. Old Maud found her as a hand-sized hatchling in the greenhouses in her first winter as groundskeeper, called her Tuppence, and let her go into the wood when she outgrew the potting shed. She has a blind left eye and receives guests on her left side, as is proper. Her oldest web holds sixty years of things foragers dropped, and her grey silk drinks the light.\n\n@lore absalom_vrell\nkeys: Absalom Vrell, the Outcast, Vrell\ncanon: soft\ntext: A wizard expelled from Lumenhold for forbidden practice before the current students' parents were born; his name is still in the school ledger, crossed out. He waits in the Hush, by a lantern, with a flask of good tea, for the Spinner to fall. He taught the thornwitch and the masked dabblers in the wood. A grandmaster of every forbidden spell, courteous and bored; nobody has challenged him in forty years. He is not part of any plot but his own.\n\n@lore thornwitch\nkeys: thornwitch, thorn wand\ncanon: soft\ntext: A hedge-practitioner in a cloak sewn with thorns who drinks the life of trees in the Whisperwood's thicket with Siphon, and offers apprentices a blackthorn wand.\n\n@lore whisperwood_creatures\nkeys: bramble ferret, bristleback, stag beetle, wisps, pixies, ember foxes, grumpy oak, spiderlings, talking trees\ncanon: soft\ntext: The Whisperwood's lesser folk: bristly bramble ferrets that rob foragers' bags, bristlebacks (quilled bears, slow and easily offended), stag beetles the size of ponies, pixies who keep a toll stone, riddling wisps and hollow oaks, oaks that take a stubbed toe personally, coin-sized spiderlings by the hundred in the deep wood, and foxes with ember tails who will walk a polite student through. The old trees gossip about the students who walk under them.\n\n@lore elder_oak\nkeys: elder oak, pale ring, the cold spring\ncanon: soft\ntext: The oldest tree in the Whisperwood, in the heartwood, with the other trees grown round it like courtiers and a face in its bark with its eyes shut. Asked politely, it shows its rings: sixty in from the bark, one is pale as frost, for \"the cold spring\". It gives a splinter of heartwood to someone it judges worthy.\n\n@lore masked_circle\nkeys: masked circle, white masks, chalk circle, Compel\ncanon: soft\ntext: Three hedge-dabblers in plain white masks who practise Compel in a chalk circle in the heartwood. Their chalk marks are copied, badly, from somewhere; they don't know what the marks are for. Absalom Vrell taught them, after a fashion.\n\n@lore wood_witch\nkeys: wood's witch, ghost on the log, last witch\ncanon: soft\ntext: A ghost who knits nothing on a fallen log in the Hush. She was the wood's witch before the castle, before the lake was a lake, and left when the Spinner came; she still can't decide if that was cowardice or manners. She knows the Spinner's weakness.\n\n@lore village_tailor\nkeys: village tailor, ward thread, ward-stitched cloak\ncanon: soft\ntext: The tailor on the village square, who exchanges students' ruined robes at half price and, it's said, feels it whenever a sleeve is singed. She sews Sister Amaranth's ward thread into cloaks, and will make a robe of Spinner silk, at a price \"for my nerves\".\n\n@lore pawnbroker\nkeys: pawnbroker, stuffed owl, loupe\ncanon: soft\ntext: The village pawnbroker, under a stuffed owl, turns each thing twice through his loupe as if it hid a second, cheaper thing underneath. He buys back what students are tired of.\n"},{"path":"content/world/places.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Places (design §6.1). Positions are on the campus map\n#  (960 × 680, see docs/map-concept.svg): the castle stands on the\n#  north shore of the Mirror Lake, the village to the south-east,\n#  the Whisperwood to the west. Data only until phase 2b.\n#\n#  open:      the slots a place can be visited in\n#  discover:  when it becomes visible (omit for places open from the start)\n#  hint:      teaser shown while hinted (hinted_when) but not yet discovered\n#  draws:     tags of older events (no place: yet) that may happen here (compat, phase 2b)\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Public from the start ----------------\n\n@place courtyard\nname: The Courtyard\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 585,278\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night\nart: tower\ndraws: social festival rival story\ndesc: Flagstones worn smooth by three centuries of students, a fountain that freezes when it shouldn't, and the best gossip in the castle.\n\n@place library\nname: The Library\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 380,275\nkind: public\ntrack: library_assistant\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night\nart: book\ndraws: study\n# The revision card comes first on a term visit, once a term (2026-10-19).\nalways: library_revision\ndesc: Seven floors of shelves that rearrange themselves when nobody is looking. Footnote the cat keeps order.\n\n@place refectory\nname: The Refectory\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 470,278\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening midnight\nart: meal\ndraws: social home money\ndesc: Long tables, longer benches, and soup that tastes faintly of whatever you're homesick for.\n\n@place lakeshore\nname: The Lakeshore\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 480,505\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night midnight\nart: wave\ndraws: nature mystery romance\ndesc: The Mirror Lake lies so still it shows the towers upside down. On some nights it shows other things.\n\n@place village\nname: The Village Square\nregion: village\nmap: campus 720,520\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: house\ndraws: money home\n# The shop counter (world expansion 1, K3): what it sells, first on every visit.\nalways: shop_square\ndesc: A barge ride across the lake: crooked roofs, a market square, a bakery that has fed students for two hundred years and shopkeepers who have seen every kind of student.\n\n# ---------------- Castle ----------------\n\n@place lecture_tower\nname: The Lecture Tower\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 420,180\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: scroll\ndraws: class study exam\ndesc: Spiral lecture halls stacked one above another. Evocation on the lower floors, where scorch marks are expected; Umbral Theory at the top, where the lamps burn dim.\n\n@place observatory\nname: The Observatory\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 600,175\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening night midnight\nart: planet\ndraws: magic mystery\ndesc: A brass dome at the top of the east tower. The great telescope points wherever it likes; usually that is the right place.\n\n@place great_hall\nname: The Great Hall\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 455,215\nkind: public\nopen: noon afternoon evening\nart: crown\ndraws: festival social\ndesc: Floating candles, a vaulted ceiling that shows tomorrow's weather, and the stage where the choir rehearses and the Midwinter Ball is held.\n\n@place infirmary\nname: The Infirmary\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 530,200\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night midnight\nart: potion\ndraws: crisis danger\ndesc: Clean white beds, a cupboard of tonics and Sister Amaranth, who has seen every kind of injury and believes none of the excuses.\n\n@place alchemy_lab\nname: The Alchemy Laboratory\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 555,240\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: cauldron\ndraws: class magic\ndesc: Long benches, cauldrons in every size, and a ventilation charm that has given up on the third floor.\n\n@place workshop\nname: Mir's Workshop\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 615,235\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening night\nclosed_when: flag(mir_workshop_shut) and since(mir_workshop_closed) < 3\ndiscover: met(mir)\nhint: Something clanks and hums behind the west-wing tapestry.\nhinted_when: turn >= 2\nart: gear\ndraws: magic\ndesc: A former classroom full of springs, gears, half-finished machines and the smell of solder. Fire buckets by the door, on the faculty's orders.\n\n@place job_board\nname: The Job Board\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 520,262\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: paper\ndraws: job money\n# The job menu first on every visit, then one odd gig (feedback 2026-10-15).\nalways: job_board_visit\ndesc: A cork board in the entrance hall layered with notices: jobs, lost familiars, club recruitment and the occasional ransom note for a sock.\n\n@place ledger_office\nname: The Lumen Ledger Office\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 395,230\nkind: club\ntrack: lumen_ledger\nopen: afternoon evening night\ndiscover: member(lumen_ledger) or met(nessa)\nart: paper\ndraws: club\ndesc: A cramped room under the library stairs, walls papered with old front pages. The printing press groans like a dragon on deadline night.\n\n@place headmistress_tower\nname: The Headmistress's Tower\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 500,130\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening\ndiscover: rel(isolde) >= 12 or flag(warden_trial)\nhint: A spiral stair guarded by a stone owl that only opens its eyes for some students.\nhinted_when: met(isolde)\nart: crown\ndraws: story\ndesc: Books, tea and a board of crystal towers by the window. You are only here because she wants you here.\n\n@place restricted_section\nname: The Restricted Section\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 352,245\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening midnight\ndiscover: flag(forbidden_key) or seen(forbidden_section_1) > 0 or flag(heist_stacks_known)\nhint: A door at the back of the library that is always locked, and always slightly warm.\nhinted_when: turn >= 2\nart: lock\ndraws: mystery danger\ndesc: Black-bound books that breathe in the dark. Some of them read you back.\n\n@place gargoyle_passage\nname: The Third Gargoyle\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 418,135\nkind: society\ntrack: cartographers\nopen: afternoon evening midnight\ndiscover: member(cartographers)\nhint: Corvin keeps glancing at a particular gargoyle.\nhinted_when: met(corvin)\nart: map\ndraws: club mystery\ndesc: Behind the third gargoyle on the north parapet, a passage that isn't on any map. Except theirs.\n\n@place seventh_tower\nname: The Seventh Tower\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 500,72\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening midnight\ndiscover: clue(lead_seventh_tower) or clues(hollowstar) >= 4 or flag(heist_tower_known)\nhint: A locked stair that leads up into a tower nobody admits is there.\nhinted_when: clock(hollowstar) >= 1\nart: key\ndraws: mystery story\ndesc: Locked for a century. At the very top, one room of dust and moonlight, and a star-shaped hollow in the wall.\n\n@place thesis_lab\nname: The Thesis Laboratory\nregion: castle\nmap: campus 565,160\nkind: thesis\ntrack: thesis\nopen: morning afternoon evening\ndiscover: year >= 4\nart: book\ndraws: study\ndesc: Rows of carrels where final-year students and their advisors argue about footnotes until the candles give out.\n\n# ---------------- Grounds ----------------\n\n@place duel_hall\nname: The Duelling Hall\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 660,150\nkind: club\ntrack: duelling\nopen: afternoon evening\ndiscover: member(duelling) or rel(lyra) >= 9\nhint: Steel rings somewhere above the east stair.\nhinted_when: met(lyra)\nart: duel\ndraws: rival\ndesc: Scorched floorboards and a ceiling that has seen things. The practice dummies have names, and grudges.\n\n@place greenhouses\nname: The Greenhouses\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 740,235\nkind: club\ntrack: verdant\nopen: morning afternoon evening\ndiscover: member(verdant) or met(tobias) or met(maud)\nart: leaf\ndraws: nature\ndesc: Glass houses that steam even in winter. The moonflowers bloom once every eleven years; the mandrakes have opinions every day.\n\n@place flight_field\nname: The Flight Field\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 850,300\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: broom\ndraws: club\ndesc: A long green field with hoops at either end and a wind that cheats. Broom lessons, races and spectacular landings.\n\n@place stables\nname: The Familiar Stables\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 845,385\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: paw\ndraws: nature\ndesc: Hippogriffs, a sulky griffon chick, owls in the rafters and Professor Brask, who talks to all of them more kindly than to students.\n\n@place boathouse\nname: The Boathouse\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 610,440\nkind: public\nopen: afternoon evening night midnight\nart: wave\ndraws: social mystery romance\n# A boathouse night (feedback 2026-10-16): the ring first (either door), then one drawn card.\nalways: midnight_boathouse_ring|ring_on_yourself\ndesc: An old wooden boathouse and a jetty out over the water. People come here to say the things they can't say indoors.\n\n@place orchard\ndeprecated: maud_cottage\nname: The Orchard\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 790,165\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: tree\ndraws: nature romance\ndesc: Apple and pear trees older than the towers. In spring the blossom falls like snow; once, it was snow.\n\n@place maud_cottage\nname: Old Maud's Cottage\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 780,400\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\ndiscover: met(maud)\nart: house\ndraws: nature home romance\ndesc: A thatched cottage with chickens on the doorstep, bees in the eaves, a goat called Bartholomew and a kettle that is always nearly boiling. Behind it, apple and pear trees older than the towers.\n\n@place secret_garden\nname: The Secret Garden\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 815,425\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening\ndiscover: seen(garden_maud_secret) > 0\nhint: A door in the wall behind the potting shed that you keep walking past.\nhinted_when: met(maud)\nart: flower\ndraws: nature magic\ndesc: Glowing ferns, silver bees and a pear tree that hums. It only exists for people Maud has shown it to.\n\n@place courier_loft\nname: The Courier Loft\nregion: grounds\nmap: campus 880,230\nkind: job\ntrack: broom_courier\nopen: morning afternoon evening\ndiscover: turn >= 3\nart: letter\ndraws: job\ndesc: A draughty loft full of parcels, pigeonholes and brooms in various states of repair. The dispatch bell rings at all hours.\n\n# ---------------- Village ----------------\n\n@place gilded_newt\nname: The Gilded Newt\nregion: village\nmap: campus 800,480\nkind: job\ntrack: tavern\nopen: afternoon evening night midnight\nart: cup\ndraws: job money social\n# A Newt night (feedback 2026-10-16): the company offer first, then the high table.\nalways: newt_company newt_company_regular newt_high_table shop_newt\ndesc: The village tavern: low beams, a fire that never goes out, and a landlady who remembers every student's tab to the penny.\n\n@place mabels\nname: Mabel's Tea Shop\nregion: village\nmap: campus 725,565\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening\nart: cup\ndraws: social home\n# The shop counter (world expansion 1, K3): what it sells, first on every visit.\nalways: shop_mabels\ndesc: Forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake and Mabel, who reads your mood from the way you open the door.\n\n@place grimsbys_books\nname: Grimsby's Books\nregion: village\nmap: campus 680,600\nkind: public\nopen: noon afternoon evening\nart: book\ndraws: study money\n# The shop counter (world expansion 1, K3): what it sells, first on every visit.\nalways: shop_grimsbys\ndesc: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other for support. Mr Grimsby will find anything except what you came in for.\n\n@place pell_apothecary\nname: Pell & Daughters\nregion: village\nmap: campus 770,600\nkind: job\ntrack: apothecary_clerk\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: bottle\ndraws: job money\n# The shop counter (world expansion 1, K3): what it sells, first on every visit.\nalways: shop_pell\ndesc: An apothecary of a thousand labelled drawers. Old Pell taught three generations to count drops; his daughters taught him to smile at customers.\n\n@place bakery\ndeprecated: village\nname: The Bakery\nregion: village\nmap: campus 640,555\nkind: public\nopen: morning noon afternoon\nart: meal\ndraws: home money\ndesc: The same family has baked here for two hundred years. The buns are famous; so, in certain circles, is a letter that arrived very late.\n\n# ---------------- Wilds ----------------\n\n@place whisperwood\nname: The Whisperwood\nregion: wilds\nmap: campus 185,340\nkind: public\nopen: morning afternoon evening night midnight\ndiscover: met(maud) or flag(fox_met)\nhint: Fog sits on the western woods even at noon.\nhinted_when: turn >= 2\nart: tree\ndraws: nature danger mystery\nalways: ww_edge ww_edge_night|ww_night_first\ndesc: An old forest that talks in its sleep. Moonpetal grows in its clearings; so do things that are better left there.\n\n# ---------------- Internship hosts (one map each; design §7.5) ----------------\n\n@place royal_library_archives\nname: The Royal Library Archives\nregion: internship:royal_library\nmap: royal_library 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_royal_library\nopen: morning afternoon\nart: book\ndesc: Miles of stacks beneath the capital, where the oldest records of the realm are kept in temperature-controlled silence.\n\n@place conclave_laboratory\nname: The Conclave Laboratory\nregion: internship:conclave\nmap: conclave 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_conclave\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: wand\ndesc: The research laboratory of the Conclave of the Seven Towers, where new spells are tested behind very thick walls.\n\n@place alchemists_works\nname: The Guild Works\nregion: internship:alchemists\nmap: alchemists 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_alchemists\nopen: morning afternoon\nart: cauldron\ndesc: The Guild of Alchemists' great works by the river: vats, chimneys and a strict rule about naked flames.\n\n@place observatory_camp\nname: The Expedition Camp\nregion: internship:expedition\nmap: expedition 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_expedition\nopen: afternoon evening night\nart: planet\ndesc: Tents on a high plateau where the air is thin and the stars are close enough to argue with.\n\n@place field_station\nname: The Beastcraft Field Station\nregion: internship:field_station\nmap: field_station 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_field_station\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: paw\ndesc: A cabin on the moors, ringed with paddocks and nesting towers, where injured creatures are mended and wild ones are counted.\n\n@place thornwick_lab\nname: The Thornwick Exchange Lab\nregion: internship:thornwick\nmap: thornwick 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_thornwick\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: mask\ndesc: A guest laboratory at Thornwick Academy, the rival school, where the illusionists are excellent and the hospitality is suspicious.\n\n@place unseen_office\nname: The Office of Unseen Affairs\nregion: internship:unseen_office\nmap: unseen_office 480,300\nkind: internship\ntrack: intern_unseen_office\nopen: evening night\nart: eye\ndesc: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs. It has no sign, no address, and a very good coffee machine.\n\n# ---------------- Home (the \"go home\" break plan; map: home) ----------------\n\n@place home_cottage\nname: The Family Cottage\nregion: home\nmap: home 485,320\nkind: plan\nopen: morning noon afternoon evening night\nart: house\ndraws: home\ndesc: The kitchen table, the creaky stair, the smell of whatever is in the oven. Everyone asks how school is.\n\n@place home_fields\nname: The Fields\nregion: home\nmap: home 310,320\nkind: plan\nopen: morning afternoon evening\nart: leaf\ndraws: nature home\ndesc: Long rows, a low wall to sit on, and the same view you used to stare at when you dreamed of towers.\n\n@place home_market\nname: The Market Town\nregion: home\nmap: home 770,240\nkind: plan\nopen: noon afternoon evening\nart: coin\ndraws: money social\ndesc: Market stalls, the old crowd outside the inn, and the one shop that still sells your favourite sweets.\n"},{"path":"content/world/spells.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Spells (design §9.1; docs/spells-v2-list.md, revision 2).\n#  Ids are the combat minigame's (combat/setup/roster.mjs, SPELLS).\n#  skill: the skill it trains when learned and adds to on checks · verb: the one verb its cards use\n#  cost: Mana per level (three levels for a course spell, else one), the same in a card's up[] option and in a\n#  fight: the roster's SPELLS[id].cost (one Mana scale; test/combat-bridge.test.js keeps them equal)\n#  pool: hidden | forbidden, no source yet · alignment: taken off per cast.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Course spells (3 levels) and Ward ----------------\n\n@spell flame\nname: Flame\ndiscipline: evocation\nskill: arcana\nverb: Burn\ncost: 10, 9, 8\nart: spell_flame\ndesc: Fire from the fingertips: a candle's worth at first, a bonfire's later. Every duellist you'll ever face has it ready, and so should you.\n\n@spell foresight\nname: Foresight\ndiscipline: astromancy\nskill: lore\nverb: Foresee\ncost: 7, 6, 5\nart: spell_foresight\ndesc: A glimpse of the next few moments. Enough to know which way the dice will fall, sometimes.\n\n@spell mend\nname: Mend\ndiscipline: alchemy\nskill: alchemy\nverb: Mend\ncost: 10, 9, 8\nart: spell_mend\ndesc: Knits cuts, cracks and broken teacups. Hearts take longer.\n\n@spell glamour\nname: Glamour\ndiscipline: illusion\nskill: charm\nverb: Glamour\ncost: 9, 7, 5\nart: spell_glamour\ndesc: A little more charm than you really have. People remember liking you and not quite why.\n\n@spell haste\nname: Haste\ndiscipline: beastcraft\nskill: vigor\nverb: Hasten\ncost: 7, 7, 7\nart: spell_haste\ndesc: Speed borrowed from hares and hawks: a blow slipped in between your spells, a parry in between everything else. It wears off quickly.\n\n@spell shadowstep\nname: Shadowstep\ndiscipline: umbral\nskill: guile\nverb: Step\ncost: 8, 10, 12\nart: spell_shadowstep\ndesc: Step into one shadow and out of another. Short distances only; the shadows in between are not friendly.\n\n@spell ward\nname: Ward\nverb: Ward\ncost: 7\nart: spell_ward\ndesc: A pale shell that turns aside the next harm. Every first-year is taught it in the first week, before anything else, and told to keep it close.\n\n# ---------------- Hidden courses (1 level) ----------------\n\n@spell animate\nname: Animate\ndiscipline: artifice\nskill: alchemy arcana\nverb: Animate\ncost: 14\nart: spell_animate\ndesc: A made thing gets up and moves by itself: a broom that sweeps, a chair that follows. It does one thing, and keeps doing it.\n\n@spell starlight\nname: Starlight\ndiscipline: starcalling\nskill: lore\nverb: Illuminate\ncost: 9\nart: spell_starlight\ndesc: The light of a named star, held in the hand. Darkness that eats light finds it hard to swallow.\n\n@spell seal\nname: Seal\ndiscipline: hollow_studies\nskill: guile\nverb: Seal\ncost: 8\nart: spell_seal\ndesc: Closes what is open and locks it away: a box, a door, a spell half-spent. What's sealed stays where you put it.\n\n# ---------------- Peer capstones (1 level) ----------------\n\n@spell wildfire\nname: Wildfire\nskill: arcana\nverb: Burn\ncost: 8\nart: spell_wildfire\ndesc: Fire you stop holding small. It grows while you hold it and runs when you let go, and it never quite forgets you're there.\n\n@spell heartwood\nname: Heartwood\nskill: vigor\nverb: Root\ncost: 14\nart: spell_heartwood\ndesc: A young tree out of bare ground, grown in a breath to stand over you. It shelters whoever stands under it.\n\n@spell omen\nname: Omen\nskill: lore\nverb: Foretell\ncost: 8\nart: spell_omen\ndesc: Say what will happen, out loud, and it lands. Not soon; but it lands.\n\n@spell sleight\nname: Sleight\nskill: guile\nverb: Lift\ncost: 4\nalignment: tiny\nart: spell_sleight\ndesc: Palm, swap, pocket. A coin walks out of a purse and into yours, and nobody saw a thing. It is still theft.\n\n@spell contraption\nname: Contraption\nskill: alchemy\nverb: Build\ncost: 10\nart: spell_contraption\ndesc: A machine out of whatever is in reach, that works at once and mostly as meant. Stand back when it stops.\n\n@spell hoarfrost\nname: Hoarfrost\nskill: arcana\nverb: Freeze\ncost: 8\nart: spell_hoarfrost\ndesc: Cold called down hard: it freezes, cools and quenches. Set against a flame, the two of them cancel out.\n\n@spell candour\nname: Candour\nskill: charm\nverb: Confess\ncost: 6\nart: spell_candour\ndesc: Ask the right question and get the honest answer, sometimes more honest than anyone wanted.\n\n# ---------------- Club and job spells (1 level) ----------------\n\n@spell brace\nname: Brace\nskill: vigor\nverb: Brace\ncost: 10\nart: spell_brace\ndesc: Plant your feet and hold. For a breath or two, blows that should fell you land soft.\n\n@spell quickquill\nname: Quickquill\nskill: guile\nverb: Scribble\ncost: 4\nart: spell_quickquill\ndesc: A quill that takes it all down, word for word, as fast as anyone can talk. It has opinions about your handwriting.\n\n@spell bramble\nname: Bramble\nskill: alchemy\nverb: Entangle\ncost: 12\nart: spell_bramble\ndesc: A hedge or a vine, now, where there wasn't one. The thorns are not for decoration.\n\n@spell index\nname: Index\nskill: lore\nverb: Recall\ncost: 4\nart: spell_index\ndesc: Find the page. Any book you've held falls open where you need it, and a library starts to make sense.\n\n@spell rapport\nname: Rapport\nskill: charm\nverb: Befriend\ncost: 4\nart: spell_rapport\ndesc: The right joke at the right moment. A room that was about to turn on you laughs instead.\n\n@spell purify\nname: Distill\nskill: alchemy\nverb: Distill\ncost: 0\nart: spell_distil\ndesc: Draws out and concentrates: the strength from a root, the magic from yourself. It costs no Mana; it costs you.\n\n@spell tailwind\nname: Tailwind\nskill: vigor\nverb: Surge\ncost: 4\nart: spell_tailwind\ndesc: Go fast, then faster. The wind gets behind you and keeps pushing as long as you don't stop.\n\n# ---------------- Hidden pool (no source yet) ----------------\n\n@spell rime\nname: Rime\nskill: arcana\nverb: Freeze\ncost: 4\npool: hidden\nart: spell_rime\ndesc: A sliver of winter sewn into your collar. The blow that should fell you finds you frozen instead, and you thaw.\n\n@spell reflect\nname: Reflect\nskill: lore\nverb: Reflect\ncost: 8\npool: hidden\nart: spell_reflect\ndesc: What is thrown at you goes back where it came from, all of it.\n\n@spell moonwell\nname: Moonwell\nskill: alchemy\nverb: Gather\ncost: 0\npool: hidden\nart: spell_moonwell\ndesc: Sit still and let magic pool in you, like moonlight in a basin. It is slow, and it is free.\n\n@spell lodestar\nname: Lodestar\nskill: lore\nverb: Gather\ncost: 4\npool: hidden\nart: spell_lodestar\ndesc: Gather a guiding light around you, a little more each breath, until it is ready to stand between you and harm.\n\n@spell toll\nname: Toll\nverb: Toll\ncost: 0\npool: hidden\nart: spell_toll\ndesc: One low note, held, like a bell heard under water. Nobody teaches it.\n\n# ---------------- Forbidden pool (no source yet; alignment per cast) ----------------\n\n@spell torment\nname: Torment\nskill: guile\nverb: Torment\ncost: 14\npool: forbidden\nalignment: small\nart: spell_torment\ndesc: Every spell your opponent pays for hurts them. It is forbidden for good reason.\n\n@spell extraction\nname: Extraction\nskill: arcana\nverb: Extract\ncost: 10\npool: forbidden\nalignment: small\nart: spell_extraction\ndesc: Reach into someone and take their magic for yours. Forbidden, and everyone who has felt it remembers.\n\n@spell siphon\nname: Siphon\nskill: alchemy\nverb: Siphon\ncost: 12\npool: forbidden\nalignment: small\nart: spell_siphon\ndesc: Draw the life out of someone, slowly, and keep it. Forbidden.\n\n@spell compel\nname: Compel\nskill: guile\nverb: Compel\ncost: 18\npool: forbidden\nalignment: small\nart: spell_compel\ndesc: Make someone do what they just did again, and again, whether they want to or not. Forbidden.\n\n@spell eclipse\nname: Eclipse\nskill: arcana\nverb: Convert\ncost: 6\npool: forbidden\nalignment: small\nart: spell_eclipse\ndesc: Pain taken becomes power held. You still feel all of it. Forbidden.\n\n@spell heartprice\nname: Heartprice\nskill: vigor\nverb: Sacrifice\ncost: 0\npool: forbidden\nalignment: small\nart: spell_heartprice\ndesc: Pay for magic with your own strength instead of Mana, for as long as you can stand it. Forbidden.\n"},{"path":"content/world/timeline.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Timeline (design §3.2, §4.4). `history` entries are dated canon\n#  (years before the player's first year, as negative numbers; the\n#  first year is 0). `beat` entries are the in-run calendar of fixed\n#  beats (yNmM; m1 = Harvestmoon); in phase 2b they drive the dated\n#  `auto` events. Context for authors; not shown in the game.\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- History ----------------\n\n@timeline founding\ndate: -300\nkind: history\ntext: The founders raise seven towers above the Mirror Lake as a seal over the Hollow Star, with one lock in each tower and a star-shaped key. The entry for the founding year in the Founders' Ledger is later cut out with a razor.\n\n@timeline prudence_ashworth\ndate: -200\nkind: history\ntext: Prudence Ashworth, a student, dies before she can deliver a letter to the baker's daughter in the village. Her ghost stays in the dormitories, rearranging socks.\n\n@timeline seventh_tower_locked\ndate: -100\nkind: history\ntext: The seventh tower is locked. Nobody living remembers why, and the faculty prefer it that way.\n\n@timeline the_frozen_spring\ndate: -60\nkind: history\ntext: The lake freezes one spring night. Three students go down to the boathouse; only two come back. Maud is one of them; Edmund is the one who doesn't return. His silver wand is never buried.\n\n@timeline tuppence_hatched\ndate: -59\nkind: history\ntext: Maud's first winter as groundskeeper. She finds a hand-sized hatchling spider in the greenhouses, calls her Tuppence and keeps her in the potting shed; when Tuppence outgrows it, Maud lets her go into the Whisperwood, and visits her in the Hush, marking the way with carved bees. Tuppence becomes the Spinner.\n\n@timeline vrell_expelled\ndate: -50\nkind: history\ntext: Absalom Vrell is expelled from Lumenhold for forbidden practice. His name stays in the ledger, crossed out. Later, in the Whisperwood, he teaches what he was expelled for to anyone who'll learn it.\n\n@timeline thorne_and_the_storm\ndate: -40\nkind: history\ntext: Aldric Thorne duels a storm, loses badly, and spends a year learning why. The rumour says he won. A rival from those years still wants a rematch.\n\n@timeline isolde_appointed\ndate: -20\nkind: history\ntext: Isolde Marrow, once the student who set the observatory on fire twice, becomes headmistress. Her student portrait is hidden in a forgotten corridor.\n\n@timeline ledger_founded\ndate: -20\nkind: history\ntext: The Lumen Ledger prints its first issue. Nobody has interviewed the headmistress since she took office.\n\n@timeline kael_arrives\ndate: y1m3\nkind: beat\nevent: kael_arrives\ntext: Kael Dumont arrives mid-term from the North, with frost on the dining-hall floor behind him.\n\n# ---------------- The year's fixed beats (today's calendar; re-paced in phase 3) ----------------\n\n@timeline harvest_feast\ndate: y1m1\nkind: beat\nevent: choose_elective\ntext: The year begins: electives are chosen and the Harvest Feast is held.\n\n@timeline club_fair\ndate: y1m2\nkind: beat\nevent: club_fair\ntext: The Club Fair, and Lantern Night over the lake.\n\n@timeline lantern_night\ndate: y1m2\nkind: beat\nevent: lantern_night\ntext: Lantern Night: lanterns released over the Mirror Lake, each carrying a wish.\n\n@timeline job_board\ndate: y1m3\nkind: beat\nevent: job_board\ntext: The Job Board fills with term-time work.\n\n@timeline winter_trials\ndate: y1m4\nkind: beat\nevent: winter_trials\ntext: The Winter Trials, the first exams of the year.\n\n@timeline midwinter_ball\ndate: y1m5\nkind: beat\nevent: midwinter_ball\ntext: The Midwinter Ball in the Great Hall.\n\n@timeline spring_tournament\ndate: y1m8\nkind: beat\nevent: spring_tournament\ntext: The Spring Tournament on the duelling green.\n\n@timeline grand_examinations\ndate: y1m10\nkind: beat\nevent: grand_exams\ntext: The Grand Examinations close the academic year.\n\n@timeline summer_recess\ndate: y1m11\nkind: beat\nevent: summer_plans\ntext: Summer recess: the towers empty.\n\n@timeline starfall\ndate: y1m12\nkind: beat\nevent: starfall\ntext: Starfall, when the meteor showers come and the whole academy sleeps on the lawns.\n\n@timeline the_night\ndate: y3m9\nkind: beat\nevent: hs_the_night\ntext: The night the lake freezes: the Blossom moon of year 4 in the v0.3 calendar, or earlier that spring for one who goes down before the cult is ready (v2). The Dumonts' mother leads the cult's circle on the ice.\n"},{"path":"content/world/tracks.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Tracks (design §7): courses, clubs, jobs, internships and the\n#  thesis as one progression system. The reduced list of §7.1\n#  (decision D4). Data only until phase 2b; today's electives,\n#  clubs and jobs are still the `class_*`, `club_*` and `job_*`\n#  states in content/states/states.grim.\n#  legacy: the old states a track stands for (phase 2b compat: joining\n#  grants them, so their events keep firing) · opens: the first term a\n#  course is offered (default 1).\n# ============================================================\n\n# ---------------- Courses: 6 disciplines × 3 levels ----------------\n\n@track evocation\nkind: course\nname: Evocation\nskill: arcana\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Evocation I, Evocation II, Advanced Evocation\nplace: lecture_tower\nteaches: flame@1 flame@2 flame@3\nstart: evo_first_flame\nlead: thorne\nintro: thorne_intro\ndesc: Fire and water, called up and held. Pyromancy and Tidecalling, taught together because they keep putting each other out.\npicked: You sign up for Evocation. The reading list comes with a note about singed eyebrows.\n\n@track astromancy\nkind: course\nname: Astromancy & Divination\nskill: lore\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Astromancy I, Astromancy II, Advanced Astromancy\nplace: observatory\nteaches: foresight@1 foresight@2 foresight@3\nstart: astro_first_foresight\ndesc: Reading the sky and the time that hasn't happened yet. Tea leaves in the first year; star charts that move in the third.\npicked: You sign up for Astromancy. Someone hands you a teacup and tells you not to drink from it.\n\n@track alchemy\nkind: course\nname: Alchemy & Healing\nskill: alchemy\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Alchemy I, Alchemy II, Advanced Alchemy\nplace: alchemy_lab\nteaches: mend@1 mend@2 mend@3\nstart: alch_first_mend\nlead: amaranth\nintro: amaranth_intro\ndesc: Potions, remedies and the patience to stir clockwise. Half of it happens in the laboratory and half in the infirmary.\npicked: You sign up for Alchemy. Your first lesson is how to stir clockwise, and why.\n\n@track illusion\nkind: course\nname: Illusion & Glamour\nskill: charm\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Illusion I, Illusion II, Advanced Illusion\nplace: lecture_tower\nteaches: glamour@1 glamour@2 glamour@3\nstart: illu_first_glamour\nlead: vey\nintro: vey_intro\ndesc: Making people see what you choose. Professor Vey grades on whether you fooled her, and she is very hard to fool.\npicked: You sign up for Illusion. Professor Vey looks at you as if you might already be one.\n\n@track beastcraft\nkind: course\nname: Beastcraft & Flight\nskill: vigor\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Beastcraft I, Beastcraft II, Advanced Beastcraft\nplace: stables\nteaches: haste@1 haste@2 haste@3\nstart: beast_first_haste\nopens: 2\nlead: brask\nintro: brask_intro\ndesc: Creatures, familiars and brooms. Professor Brask believes the first two are easier to reason with than the third.\npicked: You sign up for Beastcraft. Your name goes on the list beside a broom and a hippogriff.\n\n@track umbral\nkind: course\nname: Umbral Theory\nskill: guile\nlevels: 3\nlevel_names: Umbral Theory I, Umbral Theory II, Advanced Umbral Theory\nplace: lecture_tower\nteaches: shadowstep@1 shadowstep@2 shadowstep@3\nstart: umbral_first_shadowstep\nopens: 2\ndesc: Shadow, wards and the magic of what is hidden. Taught at the top of the tower, where the lamps burn dim on purpose.\npicked: You sign up for Umbral Theory. The lamps at the top of the tower are dim on purpose.\n\n# ---------------- Hidden courses ----------------\n\n@track artifice\nkind: course\nname: Artifice\nskill: alchemy arcana\nlevels: 1\nteaches: animate@1\nstart: art_first_animate\n# taken late, after level II–III elsewhere: its classwork is pitched at expert (docs/authoring/checks.md)\nstandard: expert\nlevel_names: Artifice\nplace: workshop\nhidden: true\nprereq: passed(alchemy, 2) and passed(evocation, 2)\ndesc: Enchanted machinery: clockwork that thinks, cauldrons that stir themselves. Offered to students who already know how to make things and how to make them burn.\npicked: You take the place in Artifice. Someone shows you where the fire buckets are before anything else.\n\n@track starcalling\nkind: course\nname: Starcalling\nskill: lore arcana\nlevels: 1\nteaches: starlight@1\nstart: star_first_starlight\n# taken late, after level II–III elsewhere: its classwork is pitched at expert (docs/authoring/checks.md)\nstandard: expert\nlevel_names: Starcalling\nplace: observatory\nhidden: true\nprereq: passed(astromancy, 3) and clue(heard_of_starcalling)\ndesc: Calling down the light of a named star. Not on any syllabus; taught by appointment, at night.\npicked: You accept the appointment for Starcalling. The first one is tonight.\n\n@track hollow_studies\nkind: course\nname: Hollow Studies\nskill: guile lore\nlevels: 1\nteaches: seal@1\nstart: hollow_first_seal\n# taken late, after level II–III elsewhere: its classwork is pitched at expert (docs/authoring/checks.md)\nstandard: expert\nlevel_names: Hollow Studies\nplace: restricted_section\nhidden: true\nprereq: passed(umbral, 2) and passed(astromancy, 2) and clue(knows_founders_seal)\ndesc: What the founders sealed, and how. The headmistress teaches it herself, to very few students, in a room without windows.\npicked: You accept a seat in Hollow Studies. The headmistress writes your name in a book with a black cover.\n\n# ---------------- Clubs (one at a time) ----------------\n\n@track duelling\nkind: club\nname: Duelling Circle\nskill: arcana vigor\nplace: duel_hall\nranks: Member, Officer, Captain\ndue: 1\nlead: lyra\nintro: lyra_intro\ndesc: Practice bouts, the Spring Tournament and a great deal of shouting about footwork.\npicked: You join the Duelling Circle. Someone is already explaining footwork to you, loudly.\n\n@track lumen_ledger\nkind: club\nname: The Lumen Ledger\nskill: charm lore\nplace: ledger_office\nranks: Stringer, Reporter, Editor\ndue: 1\nlead: nessa\nintro: nessa_intro\ndesc: The student newspaper. Scoops, deadlines, gossip columns and the occasional duty to the truth.\npicked: You join the Lumen Ledger. Nessa hands you a notebook and your first deadline.\n\n@track verdant\nkind: club\nname: Verdant Circle\nskill: alchemy\nplace: greenhouses\nranks: Member, Gardener, Head Gardener\ndue: 1\nlead: tobias\nintro: tobias_intro\ndesc: The greenhouse club: moonflowers, mandrakes and long talks with plants that occasionally answer.\npicked: You join the Verdant Circle. Tobias introduces you to the plants, one by one.\n\n# A society (playtest 2, M40): a hidden flavour group with no dues, performance or ranks, beside your club.\n@track cartographers\nkind: society\nname: Midnight Cartographers\nskill: guile\nplace: gargoyle_passage\nhidden: true\nlead: corvin\nintro: corvin_intro\ndesc: The club that doesn't exist, mapping the passages the castle would prefer to keep. Never offered at the Club Fair; you are invited, or you aren't.\n\n# ---------------- Jobs (one at a time) ----------------\n# Pay per full shift by rank (L-1): one base, 15 / 21 / 27, with the hazardous jobs (the Newt's nights, the courier's\n# flights) a quarter above it and the quiet ones (the library, the apothecary's counter) a fifth below.\n\n@track library_assistant\nkind: job\nname: Library Assistant\nskill: lore\nplace: library\nranks: Apprentice, Clerk, Senior Clerk\npay: 12, 17, 22\ndue: 3\ndesc: Shelving books that don't want to be shelved, and keeping Footnote off the reference desk.\npicked: You sign on at the library. Your first shift is next week, and Footnote is already watching you.\n\n@track tavern\nkind: job\nname: Server at the Gilded Newt\nskill: charm\nplace: gilded_newt\nranks: Server, Barkeep, Manager\npay: 19, 26, 34\ndue: 3\ndesc: Evening shifts behind the bar. Tips depend on charm; survival depends on remembering who ordered the spiced cider.\npicked: You sign on at the Gilded Newt. The landlady shows you the cellar and the back door.\n\n@track apothecary_clerk\nkind: job\nname: Apothecary Clerk\nskill: alchemy\nplace: pell_apothecary\nranks: Clerk, Dispenser, Assistant Apothecary\npay: 12, 17, 22\ndue: 3\nlead: pell\nintro: pell_first_shift\ndesc: Counting drops and reading labels at Pell & Daughters. The wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.\npicked: You sign on at Pell & Daughters. Pell shows you which drawers never to open.\n\n@track broom_courier\nkind: job\nname: Broom Courier\nskill: vigor\nplace: courier_loft\nranks: Runner, Courier, Chief Courier\npay: 19, 26, 34\ndue: 3\ndesc: Parcels, letters and the occasional live delivery, flown across the lake in all weathers.\npicked: You sign on at the courier loft. They give you a satchel and a broom that pulls left.\n\n# ---------------- Research internships (by application; design §7.5) ----------------\n\n@track intern_royal_library\nkind: internship\nname: Royal Library Archives\nskill: lore\npay: 18\nplace: royal_library_archives\ndesc: Cataloguing and research in the oldest archives of the realm.\n\n@track intern_conclave\nkind: internship\nname: Conclave Laboratory\nskill: arcana\npay: 18\nplace: conclave_laboratory\ndesc: Spell research with the Conclave of the Seven Towers. Prestigious; a faculty recommendation is expected.\n\n@track intern_alchemists\nkind: internship\nname: Guild of Alchemists' Works\nskill: alchemy\npay: 18\nplace: alchemists_works\ndesc: Industrial alchemy at the Guild's great works.\n\n@track intern_expedition\nkind: internship\nname: Observatory Expedition\nskill: lore\npay: 18\nplace: observatory_camp\ndesc: A summer of sky charts on a high plateau.\n\n@track intern_field_station\nkind: internship\nname: Beastcraft Field Station\nskill: vigor\npay: 18\nplace: field_station\ndesc: Tracking, tending and counting creatures on the moors.\n\n@track intern_thornwick\nkind: internship\nname: Thornwick Exchange Lab\nskill: charm\npay: 18\nplace: thornwick_lab\ndesc: An exchange placement at the rival academy's illusion laboratory.\n\n@track intern_unseen_office\nkind: internship\nname: Office of Unseen Affairs\nskill: guile\npay: 18\nplace: unseen_office\nhidden: true\ndesc: By invitation only. Nobody applies to the Office of Unseen Affairs.\n\n# ---------------- Thesis (year 4; design §7.6) ----------------\n\n@track thesis\nkind: thesis\nname: Thesis\nplace: thesis_lab\ndue: 2\ndesc: A year-long research project with a faculty advisor, ending in a defence before a committee of three.\n"},{"path":"content/world/vocabulary.grim","text":"# ============================================================\n#  Controlled vocabularies (design §3.1). Character traits and\n#  interests, roles, tones and memory kinds must come from these\n#  lists; lint rejects anything else. Add a value here first.\n# ============================================================\n\n@vocab traits\ndesc: Personality traits for characters and archetypes.\nvalues: proud ambitious lonely kind gentle homesick dreamy distant perceptive charming reckless loyal secretive chaotic curious stubborn guarded cold protective bold nosy idealistic overworked insecure bullying severe exacting dry warm eccentric patient calm watchful formidable fierce wary funny anxious honest sly brave shy\n\n@vocab interests\ndesc: Things characters care about; used by casting and briefs.\nvalues: duelling fire tides plants animals cooking prophecy painting tarot maps locks cards machines clocks flying stars snow writing gossip justice rules chess roses bees soup history teaching books music dancing money theatre\n\n@vocab roles\ndesc: A character's place in the academy.\nvalues: student prefect professor staff headmistress outsider villager\n\n@vocab seeks\ndesc: How readily a character comes looking for you (design §5.5): often (the default; friends and rivals), sometimes, rarely (only once you've sought them out), never.\nvalues: often sometimes rarely never\n\n@vocab tones\ndesc: The mood of an event (design §5.2 variety, §12 `tone:`).\nvalues: light warm tense grim eerie\n\n@vocab memory_kinds\ndesc: What an option can leave behind in a character's memory (design §10.2).\nvalues: helped hurt laughed fought confided kissed betrayed rescued protected abandoned\n\n@vocab regions\ndesc: Where a place is (design §6.1). Internship hosts use internship:<host>.\nvalues: castle grounds village wilds home travel internship\n\n@vocab slots\ndesc: The parts of a day (design §4.2).\nvalues: morning noon afternoon evening night midnight\n\n@vocab houses\ndesc: Student houses.\nvalues: Emberhall Rootward Stillwater Duskgate Brasswick Faculty\n\n@vocab canon_allowlist\ndesc: Proper nouns that canon lint accepts without a worldbook entry: book and essay titles, potion names, headlines and other small props. Anything that matters to the world belongs in @lore instead.\nvalues: Advanced Arcane Theory, Arcane, Curses of the Northern Wastes, Draught of Mild Courage, Draught of Unending Hiccups, Sleeping Draught, Magic and Duty, Nature of Absence, Potions, Pie-rates, Strike Again, Third Law of Correspondence, Brilliant Lectures, Brilliant Lecturers, Foundations of Flame, F, On Small Explosions\n"}];
window.LUMENHOLD_LOCALES = {"zh-CN":[{"path":"locales/zh-CN/combat.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of combat.lang\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@combat\n# en: Deal {dmg} Impact damage before defenses; {guard}.\nd.strike [610b34] = 在防御前造成 {dmg} 点冲击伤害；{guard}。\n# en: Parry stops all of it, Ward half\nd.strike.guard [d9dcb4] = 招架可全部挡下，护界挡下一半\n# en: Parry stops all of it (half, from a Parry straight after a Parry), Ward half\nd.strike.guardLeak [3eed8c] = 招架可全部挡下（紧接着上一次招架再招架，只挡一半），护界挡下一半\n# en: Turn aside all Impact damage and half of spell damage this exchange.\nd.guard [4ae8a9] = 本回合拨开全部冲击伤害和一半的法术伤害。\n# en: Still parrying, and tiring: stop {pct}% of Impact damage and half of spell damage this exchange.\nd.guard.tired [10839a] = 仍在招架，但已疲惫：本回合挡下 {pct}% 的冲击伤害和一半的法术伤害。\n# en: Turn aside all Impact damage and half of spell damage this exchange. Parrying again {when} stops only {pct}% of Impact damage.\nd.guard.fresh [833e3a] = 本回合拨开全部冲击伤害和一半的法术伤害。{when}再次招架，只能挡下 {pct}% 的冲击伤害。\n# en: straight after\nd.guard.straightAfter [e541a9] = 紧接着\n# en: within {n} exchanges\nd.guard.within [af17d4] = {n} 个回合内\n# en: Recover up to {n} flux after attacks, capped at capacity; recovering Mana breaks Shadowstep before this exchange's attacks.\nd.reclaim [bb6d42] = 在攻击结算后回收至多 {n} 点游离法力，不超过上限；回收法力会在本回合的攻击之前打破影步。\n# en: Bound: struggle free. Your only action this exchange; no Bind can take hold of you while you break free.\nd.breakFree [91153c] = 被缚：奋力挣脱。这是你本回合唯一的行动；挣脱期间，任何束缚都无法套住你。\n# en: {action} {spell}. {text}\nd.channelAction [0f2284] = {action}{spell}。{text}\n# en: Cannot act this exchange, and ordinary damage can't touch you (poison and Torment still can); then thaw.\nd.frozen [9f6883] = 本回合无法行动，寻常伤害也伤不到你（毒和折磨依然可以）；之后解冻。\n# en: Deal {dmg} spell damage.\nd.dealSpell [7e711a] = 造成 {dmg} 点法术伤害。\n# en: Deal {dmg} spell damage{pierce}.\nd.starlight.release [ba5f1e] = 造成 {dmg} 点法术伤害{pierce}。\n# en: ; Ward doesn't stop it\nd.starlight.releasePierce [7d8227] = ；护界挡不住\n# en: Charge the release to {dmg} spell damage{pierce}. Further Holds add no damage.\nd.starlight.hold [e6beb4] = 将释放蓄至 {dmg} 点法术伤害{pierce}。继续蓄持不再增加伤害。\n# en:  that Ward doesn't stop\nd.starlight.holdPierce [e63892] = ，且护界挡不住\n# en: Deal {dmg} spell damage as one hit ({bank} of it banked); Ward stops only half of it.\nd.wildfire.release [bd3848] = 以一次攻击造成 {dmg} 点法术伤害（其中 {bank} 点为积攒）；护界只能挡下一半。\n# en: Deal {dmg} spell damage to them and {self} to yourself, and bank {more} more: Release would then deal {release}.\nd.wildfire.hold [961927] = 对对方造成 {dmg} 点法术伤害，对自己造成 {self} 点，并再积攒 {more} 点：届时释放将造成 {release} 点。\n# en: Heal {n} HP from damage dealt.\nd.siphon.release [6444ac] = 按造成的伤害治疗 {n} 点生命。\n# en: Deal {dmg} spell damage; bank actual damage for healing.\nd.siphon.hold [c836c7] = 造成 {dmg} 点法术伤害；实际造成的伤害存起来用于治疗。\n# en: Gain up to {n} ready Mana, capped at capacity.\nd.moonwell.release [ddd74e] = 获得至多 {n} 点可用法力，不超过上限。\n# en: Bank {n} more Mana; Release makes it ready.\nd.moonwell.hold [2b54fa] = 再存入 {n} 点法力；释放后即可使用。\n# en: {effect} at the end of this exchange and of each exchange after, {life} in all. Each opposing hit on you loses {reduce}; it breaks once you have taken {threshold} damage. A new summon replaces it.{burst}\nd.construct [5b08d7] = 本回合结束时及之后每回合结束时{effect}，共 {life}。对方每次击中你都会被减免 {reduce} 点；你累计承受 {threshold} 点伤害后它便破碎。新的召唤会取代它。{burst}\n# en: Heal {n} HP\nd.construct.heal [3e1ba5] = 治疗 {n} 点生命\n# en: Deal {n} Impact damage before defenses\nd.construct.strike [4953f0] = 在防御前造成 {n} 点冲击伤害\n# en: Deal {n} spell damage before defenses\nd.construct.spell [6aecba] = 在防御前造成 {n} 点法术伤害\n# en:  When it ends (broken, run out or replaced): {n} spell damage to both sides before defenses.\nd.construct.burst [c0bb83] = 它消失时（被打碎、时效耗尽或被取代）：在防御前对双方各造成 {n} 点法术伤害。\n# en: {text} Forbidden: alignment −2 each cast; witnessed casts are reported.\nd.forbidden [7be822] = {text}禁咒：每次施放，立场向空洞偏移 2；被人目击的施放会遭举报。\n# en: Deal {dmg} spell damage before defenses. Parry halves it; Ward stops it.\nd.spellHit [09e04b] = 在防御前造成 {dmg} 点法术伤害。招架减半；护界完全挡下。\n# en: Deal {dmg} Impact damage before defenses. Parry stops it; Ward halves it.\nd.physicalHit [606357] = 在防御前造成 {dmg} 点冲击伤害。招架完全挡下；护界减半。\n# en: Your opponent commits first for the next {n}: you see their move before you choose.\nd.foresight [ca9354] = 接下来 {n}，对手必须先出招：你能先看到对方的行动再做选择。\n# en: At the end of the exchange, heal {n} HP, unless you take any damage this exchange.{exposes}\nd.mend [6a05ec] = 回合结束时治疗 {n} 点生命，前提是本回合没有受到任何伤害。{exposes}\n# en:  Breaks your own Shadowstep.\nd.mend.exposes [33ddae] = 会打破你自己的影步。\n# en:  The first cast that pays gives {full}, the second {half}, later casts nothing.\nd.reward.tranches [57495b] = 第一次有收获的施放得 {full}，第二次得 {half}，之后再施放一无所获。\n# en: With observers: earn {n} Renown at settlement.{tranches}\nd.glamour [97a9e3] = 有观众时：结算时获得 {n} 点声望。{tranches}\n# en: With observers: pay {crowns} Crowns to earn {n} Renown at settlement (no Crowns when it would earn nothing).{tranches}\nd.rapport [f52013] = 有观众时：支付 {crowns} 克朗，结算时获得 {n} 点声望（若毫无收获，则不花克朗）。{tranches}\n# en: Steal up to {n} Crowns; alignment −tiny. Reflect turns it on you.{tranches}\nd.sleight [85c698] = 偷取至多 {n} 克朗；立场略微偏向空洞。反照会让它落回你身上。{tranches}\n# en: nothing more this fight\nd.reward.none [0afa8b] = 本场不会再有收获\n# en: For the next {n}, each spell you cast that deals no damage of its own and isn't a channel also throws an Impulse ({dmg} Impact damage): an attack, so it breaks your Shadowstep. Every other action you take also parries.\nd.haste [c9a817] = 接下来 {n}，你施放的每个本身不造成伤害、也不是引导的法术，都会附带一次冲击（{dmg} 点冲击伤害）：这算攻击，因此会破除你的影步。你的其他每个行动都会同时招架。\n# en: Evade attacks and every opposing spell effect for {n} including this one; breaks before the hits land on any exchange you attack or recover Mana.\nd.shadowstep [a8385a] = 包括本回合在内的 {n} 中，闪避攻击和对方的一切法术效果；在你攻击或回收法力的回合，影步会在攻击命中之前破除。\n# en: This exchange: stop every opposing spell (its damage, and Bind, poison, Torment, Omen, Compel, Extraction, Seal and the like) and half of each Impact hit. A charged Starlight gets through, and half of Wildfire's Release.\nd.ward [3962e1] = 本回合：挡下对方的一切法术（其伤害，以及束缚、毒、折磨、预兆、强令、汲取、封缄等）和每次冲击的一半。蓄满的星光能穿透，野火的释放也有一半能穿透。\n# en: Start channel. Release for {dmg} spell damage, or {charged} after a Hold{pierce}. Holds cost {cost} Mana.\nd.starlight [88ab27] = 开始引导。释放可造成 {dmg} 点法术伤害，蓄持一次后为 {charged} 点{pierce}。每次蓄持消耗 {cost} 点法力。\n# en: , which Ward doesn't stop\nd.starlight.pierce [4202b2] = ，且护界挡不住\n# en: Erase all their flux (loose Mana not yet recovered). Ward or stepping aside stops it; Reflect turns it on you.\nd.seal [2e0e23] = 抹去对方全部游离法力（尚未回收的散逸法力）。护界或闪避可以挡下；反照会让它落回你身上。\n# en: Start channel: deal {hit} spell damage now, burn yourself for {self} and bank {hit} for the Release. Each Hold does the same, its heat on you growing by {self} a Hold. Release: {hit} plus the bank as one spell hit, of which Ward stops only half. Holds cost {cost} Mana.\nd.wildfire [9b8bf7] = 开始引导：现在造成 {hit} 点法术伤害，灼伤自己 {self} 点，并为释放积攒 {hit} 点。每次蓄持都如此，烧向你的热度每次蓄持增加 {self} 点。释放：以一次法术攻击造成 {hit} 点加上积攒的伤害，护界只能挡下一半。每次蓄持消耗 {cost} 点法力。\n# en: Bind them for {n} from the next exchange: Breaking Free is their only action. Ward, stepping aside or Breaking Free keeps it off; Reflect turns it on you. Parry and Brace don't stop it.\nd.bind [b197b3] = 从下一回合起束缚对方 {n}：挣脱是对方唯一的行动。护界、闪避或挣脱可以挡下；反照会让它落回你身上。招架与坚守挡不住。\n# en: Deal {dmg} spell damage before defenses (Parry halves it) and Bind them for {n} from the next exchange: Breaking Free is their only action. Ward or stepping aside keeps both off; Reflect turns both on you.\nd.bramble [8e6616] = 在防御前造成 {dmg} 点法术伤害（招架减半），并从下一回合起束缚对方 {n}：挣脱是对方唯一的行动。护界或闪避两者都能挡下；反照会让两者都落回你身上。\n# en: Poison them: {dmg} damage at the end of this exchange and of the next, each before Mend, past every defense but a mature Lodestar. Nothing cures it; a new bite starts it over. Ward or stepping aside keeps it off; Reflect turns it on you.\nd.poison [d67f3e] = 让对方中毒：本回合结束时和下一回合结束时各受 {dmg} 点伤害，都在愈合之前结算，除升至顶点的引星外无视一切防御。无法解毒；再咬一口则重新计时。护界或闪避可以挡下；反照会让它落回你身上。\n# en: Poison them: {dmg} damage at the end of each of {n} from this one, past every defense but a mature Lodestar. Nothing cures it. Ward or stepping aside keeps it off; Reflect turns it on you.\nd.poison.n [16f3ff] = 让对方中毒：从本回合起的 {n} 里，每回合结束时受 {dmg} 点伤害，除升至顶点的引星外无视一切防御。无法解毒。护界或闪避可以挡下；反照会让它落回你身上。\n# en: Scatter up to {n} of their ready Mana. Ward or stepping aside stops it; Reflect turns it on you.\nd.drain [1fa26a] = 驱散对方至多 {n} 点可用法力。护界或闪避可以挡下；反照会让它落回你身上。\n# en: Take up to {n} of their ready Mana as your own flux. Ward or stepping aside stops it; Reflect turns it on you.\nd.drain.keep [9c4ed7] = 夺取对方至多 {n} 点可用法力，化为你自己的游离法力。护界或闪避可以挡下；反照会让它落回你身上。\n# en: Deal {dmg} spell damage before defenses and scatter up to {n} of their ready Mana. Ward or stepping aside stops both; Reflect turns both on you.\nd.roar [6defcc] = 在防御前造成 {dmg} 点法术伤害，并驱散对方至多 {n} 点可用法力。护界或闪避两者都能挡下；反照会让两者都落回你身上。\n# en: Deal {dmg} spell damage as {n} hits of {each}; only the first meets Parry and Brace.\nd.hits [d007d0] = 以 {n} 次、每次 {each} 点造成共 {dmg} 点法术伤害；只有第一次会受到招架和坚守的影响。\n# en: Deal {dmg} Impact damage as {n} hits of {each}; only the first meets Parry and Brace.\nd.hits.physical [4e6b1e] = 以 {n} 次、每次 {each} 点造成共 {dmg} 点冲击伤害；只有第一次会受到招架和坚守的影响。\n# en: Deal {dmg} spell damage now and {next} more at the end of the next exchange; each hit meets the defenses of its own exchange.\nd.twoHits [dab509] = 现在造成 {dmg} 点法术伤害，下一回合结束时再造成 {next} 点；每一击都由当回合的防御应对。\n# en: In the open: {n} {kind} lands {when}, before defenses. {stops}\nd.delayed [cae5b4] = 公开出招：{n} 点{kind}将于{when}落下（防御前）。{stops}\n# en: In the open: {n} spell damage and {m} Impact damage land together {when}, before defenses. {stops}\nd.delayed.both [88a14d] = 公开出招：{n} 点法术伤害和 {m} 点冲击伤害将于{when}一同落下（防御前）。{stops}\n# en: spell damage\nd.kind.spell [b8504a] = 法术伤害\n# en: Impact damage\nd.kind.impact [9f67f0] = 冲击伤害\n# en: next exchange\nd.delayed.next [7dd225] = 下一回合\n# en: in {n} exchanges\nd.delayed.later [2f98d8] = {n} 个回合后\n# en: Ward stops it, Parry halves it, Brace softens it and stepping aside dodges it.\nd.stops.spell [2005fb] = 护界能挡下，招架减半，坚守削弱，闪避可以躲开。\n# en: Parry stops it, Ward halves it, Brace softens it and stepping aside dodges it.\nd.stops.impact [77d457] = 招架能挡下，护界减半，坚守削弱，闪避可以躲开。\n# en: Ward halves it, Brace softens it and stepping aside dodges it; Parry doesn't touch it.\nd.stops.impactNoGuard [16543a] = 护界减半，坚守削弱，闪避可以躲开；招架对它无效。\n# en: Parry stops it, Ward halves it and stepping aside dodges it; Brace doesn't touch it.\nd.stops.impactNoBrace [6e2ec7] = 招架能挡下，护界减半，闪避可以躲开；坚守对它无效。\n# en: Ward stops the spell damage and halves the Impact, Brace softens both, stepping aside dodges both and Reflect returns the spell damage; Parry touches neither.\nd.stops.bothNoGuard [edffee] = 护界挡下法术伤害、将冲击减半，坚守削弱两者，闪避两者都能躲开，反照能把法术伤害反弹回去；招架对两者都无效。\n# en: The defenses of that exchange meet it.\nd.stops.generic [dbc17e] = 由那一回合的防御来应对。\n# en: Foretell two misfortunes: if it takes hold, {n} spell damage lands in two exchanges and {n} more in three. Ward or stepping aside keeps it off; Reflect turns it on you. Each hit meets the defenses of its exchange, but Brace doesn't soften it; Omens due the same exchange land as one hit.\nd.omen [e0989e] = 预言两桩厄运：若应验，两个回合后降下 {n} 点法术伤害，三个回合后再降下 {n} 点。护界或闪避可以挡下；反照会让它落回你身上。每一击都由当回合的防御应对，但坚守无法削弱它；同一回合到期的预兆合为一击。\n# en: Deal {dmg} cold spell damage before defenses; opposing Flame or Wildfire damage this exchange cancels with it.\nd.hoarfrost [28c6b6] = 在防御前造成 {dmg} 点寒冰法术伤害；对方本回合的火焰或野火伤害会与之相互抵消。\n# en: No combat effect.\nd.noEffect [5d3a9f] = 在战斗中没有效果。\n# en: For {n} including this one, take {pct} off all opposing damage, except Omen's. Hostile spell effects still land.\nd.brace [e0e7dc] = 包括本回合在内的 {n} 中，削减对方全部伤害的 {pct}，预兆除外。敌对的法术效果照常生效。\n# en: Pay {hp} HP; gain up to {n} ready Mana, capped at capacity.\nd.purify [0850c9] = 支付 {hp} 点生命；获得至多 {n} 点可用法力，不超过上限。\n# en: Deal {dmg} Impact damage before defenses (Parry stops it, Ward halves it). Each Tailwind straight after a Tailwind deals {step} more and costs {more} more Mana; any other move starts it over.\nd.tailwind [a7012b] = 在防御前造成 {dmg} 点冲击伤害（招架能挡下，护界减半）。紧接着上一次顺风再用顺风，每次多造成 {step} 点，也多耗 {more} 点法力；做任何别的行动都会重新计起。\n# en: Once a fight: arm a rescue. The first time you would fall, you freeze instead with {n} HP, through the next exchange: ordinary damage can't touch you then, but poison and Torment still can.\nd.rime [7d8ca0] = 每场一次：布下保命术。你第一次将要倒下时，会改为以 {n} 点生命被冻住，直到下一回合结束：期间寻常伤害伤不到你，但毒和折磨依然可以。\n# en: This exchange, return incoming hostile spells and spell damage to their source, including scheduled spell damage. Does not reflect Impact damage, poison ticks or Torment tolls. Nothing bounces twice.\nd.reflect [1518d2] = 本回合，将来袭的敌对法术与法术伤害反弹回施法者，包括预定落下的法术伤害。不反弹冲击伤害、毒伤和折磨的代价。任何东西都不会被反弹两次。\n# en: Start channel: bank {n} Mana now and {more} per free Hold; Release makes it ready.\nd.moonwell [521190] = 开始引导：现在存入 {n} 点法力，之后每次蓄持再存 {more} 点；释放后即可使用。\n# en: Kindle a star that rises for {n} exchanges. When it matures it negates all damage to you that exchange (your own included) and wards you, then heals {heal} HP. One rising at a time.\nd.lodestar [cb49c5] = 点亮一颗星辰，它将上升 {n} 个回合。升至顶点的那一回合，它抵消你受到的一切伤害（包括你自己造成的），并为你筑起护界，然后为你恢复 {heal} 点生命。同一时间只能有一颗在上升。\n# en: Cards/story only; not a combat cast.\nd.toll [12e20a] = 仅用于卡牌与剧情；战斗中无法施放。\n# en: For three exchanges including this one, each of their moves costs them {n}× the ready Mana they pay for it, in HP, past every defense but a mature Lodestar. Ward or stepping aside keeps it off; Reflect turns it on you.\nd.torment [169be7] = 包括本回合在内的三个回合里，对方每做一个行动，都要按其支付的可用法力的 {n} 倍付出生命，除升至顶点的引星外无视一切防御。护界或闪避可以挡下；反照会让它落回你身上。\n# en: Move up to {n} of their ready Mana into your flux; your Mana plus flux can't pass capacity. Ward or stepping aside stops it; Reflect turns it on you.\nd.extraction [586b0b] = 把对方至多 {n} 点可用法力转为你的游离法力；你的法力加游离法力不能超过上限。护界或闪避可以挡下；反照会让它落回你身上。\n# en: Start and each Hold deal {n} spell damage before defenses. Release heals actual damage dealt; Holds cost {cost} Mana.\nd.siphon [10e812] = 开始时及每次蓄持，在防御前造成 {n} 点法术伤害。释放时按实际造成的伤害治疗；每次蓄持消耗 {cost} 点法力。\n# en: Seize their move of this exchange: it plays as usual, then they must repeat it for the next {n}, shown in advance; a turn they can't repeat it is lost. Ward or stepping aside keeps it off; Reflect turns it on you. Not in the first exchange.\nd.compel [e0026b] = 夺下对方本回合的行动：它照常生效，之后接下来 {n} 里对方必须重复它，并提前公开；无法重复的回合作废。护界或闪避可以挡下；反照会让它落回你身上。第一回合不能施放。\n# en: For {n} including this one, damage you actually take also gives you that much ready Mana, capped at capacity.\nd.eclipse [72ee0f] = 包括本回合在内的 {n} 中，你实际受到的伤害会等量转化为可用法力，不超过上限。\n# en: Turn all your ready Mana into HP now (up to your max). Then, for the rest of the fight, nothing heals you but Rime's rescue, and your spells cost HP at 1 HP per 2 Mana (healing spells still cost Mana). Once a fight.\nd.heartprice [867c95] = 现在把全部可用法力转化为生命（不超过上限）。之后在本场战斗余下的时间里，除雾凇的保命术外没有任何东西能治疗你，你的法术改以生命支付，每 2 点法力折 1 点生命（治疗法术仍需法力）。每场一次。\n# en: one\nw.one [2719ef] = 1 个回合\n# en: exchange\nw.exchange [e3eb60] = 1 个回合\n# en: 1 exchange\nw.exchange1 [3812e1] = 1 个回合\n# en: {n} exchanges\nw.exchanges [673775] = {n} 个回合\n# en: , \nw.listSep [d000f1] = 、\n# en: Impulse\nlabel.strike [de94f4] = 冲击\n# en: Parry\nlabel.guard [ae2255] = 招架\n# en: Refocus\nlabel.reclaim [c64fc8] = 凝神\n# en: Hold\nlabel.hold [f295d8] = 蓄持\n# en: Release\nlabel.release [1e9b1e] = 释放\n# en: Frozen\nlabel.frozen [c114a3] = 冰封\n# en: Cancelled\nlabel.cancel [2b09bc] = 作废\n# en: Breaking Free\nlabel.break_free [2329fe] = 挣脱\n# en: Gore\nlabel.gore [c0939a] = 冲撞\n# en: Nip\nlabel.nip [2eb008] = 轻咬\n# en: Filch\nlabel.filch [2c5687] = 顺手牵魔\n# en: Roar\nlabel.roar [4c08fd] = 咆哮\n# en: Swarm\nlabel.swarm [a2aa3f] = 蜂拥\n# en: Root\nlabel.root [295a65] = 缠根\n# en: Web\nlabel.web [645431] = 织网\n# en: Threads\nlabel.threads [244ec6] = 蛛丝\n# en: Venom\nlabel.venom [6698aa] = 毒牙\n# en: Pinch\nlabel.pinch [993927] = 钳夹\n# en: Acorns\nlabel.acorns [59150f] = 橡果\n# en: Flame\nlabel.beetle_flame [1551a0] = 火焰\n# en: Skitter\nlabel.skitter [bc97f1] = 窜逃\n# en: Fade\nlabel.fade [15cc33] = 隐没\n# en: Burrow\nlabel.burrow [967eda] = 钻地\n# en: Hide\nlabel.hide [0c7bcd] = 厚皮\n# en: Bark\nlabel.bark [ba1003] = 树皮\n# en: Carapace\nlabel.carapace [77995f] = 甲壳\n# en: Maul\nlabel.maul [674f92] = 重掌\n# en: Charge\nlabel.charge [90e891] = 冲锋\n# en: Bough\nlabel.bough [67bf3e] = 枝击\n# en: Slow Fall\nlabel.slow_fall [0ae971] = 缓坠\n# en: The Long Wait\nlabel.the_long_wait [0171eb] = 漫长的等待\n# en: Brood\nlabel.brood [7c8635] = 幼群\n# en: Thicket\nlabel.thicket [0021bf] = 灌丛\n# en: Frozen this exchange\nunavailable.frozen [178ede] = 本回合被冰封\n# en: Not an available action\nunavailable.notAction [271b7c] = 不是可用的行动\n# en: Hold or Release while channeling\nunavailable.channeling [564846] = 引导中只能蓄持或释放\n# en: Not channeling\nunavailable.notChanneling [28f077] = 没有在引导\n# en: Not learned\nunavailable.notLearned [ee3631] = 尚未学会\n# en: Cards/story only\nunavailable.cardsOnly [5be244] = 仅限卡牌与剧情\n# en: Not enough ready Mana\nunavailable.mana [2918ec] = 可用法力不足\n# en: Not enough HP\nunavailable.hp [96d38a] = 生命不足\n# en: Bound: Breaking Free is the only action\nunavailable.bound [663a09] = 被缚：挣脱是唯一的行动\n# en: Compelled: only the captured move\nunavailable.compelled [5746a8] = 受强令所迫：只能做被夺下的那一招\n# en: Not in the first exchange\nunavailable.firstExchange [f21ea5] = 第一回合不能施放\n# en: A blow is already on its way\nunavailable.blowOnWay [7dc380] = 已有一击正在袭来\n# en: Once a fight\nunavailable.once [dbd899] = 每场一次\n# en: Already paid\nunavailable.paid [156137] = 已经付过了\n# en: A Lodestar is already rising\nunavailable.lodestar [07d1d4] = 已有一颗引星正在上升\n# en: Bank\nbrief.bank [74c5b7] = 存入\n# en: Mana\nbrief.mana [78ad2a] = 法力\n# en: Heal\nbrief.heal [97377b] = 治疗\n# en: Charge\nbrief.charge [90e891] = 蓄力\n# en: Release\nbrief.release [1e9b1e] = 释放\n# en: Hold\nbrief.hold [f295d8] = 蓄持\n# en: Impact\nbrief.physical [852cb7] = 冲击\n# en: Parry\nbrief.block [ae2255] = 招架\n# en: Ward\nbrief.ward [a11053] = 护界\n# en: Flux\nbrief.flux [351e82] = 游离\n# en: If unhit\nbrief.ifUnhit [c9a466] = 未受击时\n# en: Each turn\nbrief.eachTurn [485277] = 每回合\n# en: Channel\nbrief.channel [5d8a44] = 引导\n# en: In 2 turns\nbrief.in2 [da11dc] = 两回合后\n# en: Evade\nbrief.evade [676b06] = 闪避\n# en: Brace\nbrief.brace [3cb458] = 坚守\n# en: Reflect\nbrief.reflect [70544a] = 反弹\n# en: Rescue\nbrief.rescue [ff23ac] = 保命\n# en: Reveal\nbrief.reveal [a2a3f0] = 看穿\n# en: Haste\nbrief.haste [2f6010] = 疾速\n# en: +Impulse/Parry\nbrief.impulses [6c75f0] = +冲击/招架\n# en: Snuff flux\nbrief.snuffFlux [353286] = 抹去游离\n# en: Steal\nbrief.steal [407a00] = 汲取\n# en: × Mana paid\nbrief.perAction [559a11] = ×所付法力\n# en: Force\nbrief.force [64fa24] = 强令\n# en: Hurt → Mana\nbrief.hurtToMana [370425] = 受伤→法力\n# en: Mana → HP\nbrief.hpForMana [b507b4] = 法力→生命\n# en: Renown\nbrief.renown [996fb2] = 声望\n# en: Steal Crowns\nbrief.stealCrowns [efbab8] = 偷克朗\n# en: Drain\nbrief.drain [103bb9] = 吸取\n# en: Spell\nbrief.spell [58ec69] = 法术\n# en: No effect\nbrief.noEffect [4a05e3] = 无效果\n# en: Break free\nbrief.free [255696] = 挣脱\n# en: In {n} turns\nbrief.star [fdf96e] = {n} 回合后\n# en: Chain\nbrief.chain [ec1176] = 连击\n# en: Next turn\nbrief.nextTurn [c22eb7] = 下回合\n# en: In {n} turns\nbrief.inTurns [fdf96e] = {n} 回合后\n# en: Bind\nbrief.bind [ad5f4e] = 束缚\n# en: Per turn\nbrief.perTurn [b881d3] = 每回合\n# en: {n} hits\nbrief.hits [623cc7] = {n} 次\n# en: Scatter\nbrief.scatter [607ee1] = 驱散\n# en: Twice\nbrief.twice [634793] = 两次\n# en: {name} {v:drives|drive} {pos} wand forward; a blunt pulse of force strikes toward {foe}.\nflavor.strike.cast.0 [03b694] = {name}把魔杖向前一推，一股钝重的冲击力朝{foe}撞去。\n# en: {name} {v:thrusts|thrust} {pos} wand at {foe}, and a punch of raw force follows it.\nflavor.strike.cast.1 [4f983b] = {name}将魔杖刺向{foe}，一记纯粹的力量紧随其后。\n# en: {name} {v:snaps|snap} {pos} wand forward, throwing a hard shove of force at {foe}.\nflavor.strike.cast.2 [caf458] = {name}猛地向前一挥魔杖，朝{foe}推出一股强劲的力道。\n# en: {name} {v:lunges|lunge} in and {v:strikes|strike} at {foe}.\nflavor.strike.beast.0 [ad0643] = {name}猛扑上前，朝{foe}攻去。\n# en: {name} {v:closes|close} the distance and {v:strikes|strike} at {foe}.\nflavor.strike.beast.1 [4acd82] = {name}拉近距离，向{foe}出击。\n# en: {name} {v:throws|throw} {pos} weight at {foe}.\nflavor.strike.beast.2 [6315ac] = {name}用全身的重量撞向{foe}。\n# en: {foe} {fv:yields|yield}, beaten back by {name’s} force.\nflavor.strike.ko [6ff1a1] = {foe}被{name’s}力量逼退，认输了。\n# en: {name} {v:sets|set} {pos} feet and {v:turns|turn} the force aside with a sharp motion of {pos} wand.\nflavor.guard.cast.0 [5e178f] = {name}站稳脚跟，魔杖利落一挥，把力道拨到一旁。\n# en: {name} {v:sweeps|sweep} {pos} wand across, ready to turn {foe’s} force aside.\nflavor.guard.cast.1 [9c92ae] = {name}将魔杖横扫，准备拨开{foe’s}攻势。\n# en: {name} {v:hunches|hunch} low, ready to shrug off {foe’s} force.\nflavor.guard.beast.0 [599c5d] = {name}伏低身子，准备硬扛{foe’s}攻势。\n# en: {name’s} parry slows, and {foe’s} force starts to get through.\nflavor.guard.tired [0a1485] = {name’s}招架慢了下来，{foe’s}力道开始透了进来。\n# en: {name} {v:turns|turn} {pos} wand inward; the loose light gathers at its tip.\nflavor.reclaim.cast.0 [b6cce2] = {name}把魔杖转向自己，散逸的光芒汇聚到杖尖。\n# en: {name} {v:draws|draw} the loose sparks of magic in the air back to {pos} wand.\nflavor.reclaim.cast.1 [b3c89c] = {name}把空中零星的魔力火花收回到魔杖上。\n# en: {name} {v:strains|strain} against the bonds, working free.\nflavor.break_free.cast.0 [ceb60a] = {name}奋力挣扎，一点点挣脱束缚。\n# en: {name} {v:twists|twist} and {v:tears|tear} at the bonds.\nflavor.break_free.cast.1 [c05853] = {name}扭动着撕扯身上的束缚。\n# en: {name} {v:is|are} frozen solid in rime and can’t move.\nflavor.frozen.cast.0 [18af5e] = {name}被雾凇冻得结结实实，动弹不得。\n# en: The rime around {name} cracks, and {name} {v:moves|move} again.\nflavor.frozen.end [6b740f] = {name}身上的雾凇裂开了，{name}又能动了。\n# en: A burst of flame leaps from {name’s} wand at {foe}.\nflavor.flame.cast.0 [483601] = 一团火焰从{name’s}魔杖上蹿出，扑向{foe}。\n# en: {name} {v:hurls|hurl} a ball of fire at {foe}.\nflavor.flame.cast.1 [95e20d] = {name}朝{foe}掷出一颗火球。\n# en: {foe} {fv:yields|yield} before {name’s} fire.\nflavor.flame.ko [53fab0] = {foe}在{name’s}烈火面前认输了。\n# en: {name’s} eyes glaze over, reading {foe’s} next moves before they happen.\nflavor.foresight.cast.0 [b38fa3] = {name’s}眼神变得空茫，提前读出了{foe’s}后招。\n# en: {name} can no longer read {foe’s} moves ahead.\nflavor.foresight.end [a08669] = {name}再也读不出{foe’s}后招了。\n# en: {name} {v:lays|lay} a glowing hand on {pos} wounds and {v:starts|start} to knit them shut.\nflavor.mend.cast.0 [1acb94] = {name}把发光的手按在{pos}伤口上，开始将它们缝合。\n# en: {name’s} wounds close up.\nflavor.mend.end [7f51c1] = {name’s}伤口愈合了。\n# en: {foe’s} attack breaks {name’s} mending before it can take hold.\nflavor.mend.broken [45c97c] = {foe’s}攻击打断了{name’s}愈合，法术还没来得及生效。\n# en: {name} {v:strikes|strike} a dazzling pose for the onlookers, but it does nothing to {foe}.\nflavor.glamour.cast.0 [7203b1] = {name}为围观的人摆出一个耀眼的姿势，可这对{foe}毫无作用。\n# en: {name} {v:quickens|quicken}, ready to slip a blow in between spells.\nflavor.haste.cast.0 [7a393c] = {name}的动作骤然加快，随时准备在法术之间插进一击。\n# en: {name} {v:slows|slow} back to a normal pace.\nflavor.haste.end [fbc506] = {name}慢了下来，恢复了平常的速度。\n# en: {name} {v:steps|step} into the shadows, out of {foe’s} reach.\nflavor.shadowstep.cast.0 [7451e1] = {name}踏入阴影，脱离了{foe’s}攻击范围。\n# en: {name} {v:steps|step} back out of the shadows.\nflavor.shadowstep.end [7f8b87] = {name}从阴影中走了出来。\n# en: A translucent ward appears before {name}, blocking off {foe’s} spells.\nflavor.ward.cast.0 [deb340] = 一道半透明的护界在{name}面前展开，挡住了{foe’s}法术。\n# en: A chair, a coat stand and a bucket lurch to life and lumber at {foe} on {name’s} command.\nflavor.animate.cast.0 [c0e6e8] = 在{name’s}命令下，一把椅子、一个衣帽架和一只水桶摇摇晃晃地活了过来，笨重地朝{foe}冲去。\n# en: {name’s} animated furniture clatters apart.\nflavor.animate.end [08b605] = {name’s}活化家具哐啷一声散了架。\n# en: {foe} {fv:goes|go} down under {name’s} animated furniture.\nflavor.animate.ko [35d4ff] = {foe}被{name’s}活化家具压倒在地。\n# en: {name} {v:raises|raise} {pos} wand to the sky, and starlight begins to gather high above.\nflavor.starlight.cast.0 [5a101a] = {name}把魔杖举向天空，星光开始在高处汇聚。\n# en: The starlight gathered above {name} grows brighter.\nflavor.starlight.hold [5b7dbf] = {name}上方汇聚的星光越来越亮。\n# en: A piercing beam of starlight falls from the sky at {foe}.\nflavor.starlight.release [a12bb8] = 一道穿透一切的星光从天而降，射向{foe}。\n# en: {foe} {fv:yields|yield} in a flood of {name’s} starlight.\nflavor.starlight.ko [a07cb6] = {foe}在{name’s}倾泻而下的星光里认输了。\n# en: {name} {v:twists|twist} a hand, snuffing out the loose magic around {foe}.\nflavor.seal.cast.0 [81ea08] = {name}一拧手腕，掐灭了{foe}周围的游离魔力。\n# en: Flames spring up before {name} and lash out at {foe}; {name} {v:flinches|flinch} from their heat.\nflavor.wildfire.cast.0 [4dbd81] = 火焰在{name}面前腾起，向{foe}猛扑过去；{name}被那热浪逼得一缩。\n# en: The wildfire grows fiercer and harder to hold, and its heat scorches {name} too.\nflavor.wildfire.hold [f396ab] = 野火越烧越猛，也越来越难以驾驭，它的热浪连{name}也一并灼伤。\n# en: The wildfire breaks loose from {name} and sweeps at {foe}.\nflavor.wildfire.release [87f00a] = 野火从{name}身边挣脱，朝{foe}席卷而去。\n# en: {foe} {fv:yields|yield} before {name’s} wildfire.\nflavor.wildfire.ko [6eb778] = {foe}在{name’s}野火面前认输了。\n# en: A sapling bursts from the floor beside {name}, ready to shelter and mend.\nflavor.heartwood.cast.0 [771791] = 一株树苗从{name}身旁的地板里钻出，随时准备遮护与疗愈。\n# en: {name’s} sapling withers back into the floor.\nflavor.heartwood.end [a3c488] = {name’s}树苗枯萎了，缩回地板里。\n# en: {name} {v:speaks|speak} of two small misfortunes; the light around {foe} falters.\nflavor.omen.cast.0 [f6ccb2] = {name}说起两桩小小的厄运；{foe}周围的光芒黯淡了一瞬。\n# en: A misfortune {name} foretold comes due for {foe}.\nflavor.omen.end [c4bede] = {name}预言的一桩厄运到期，找上了{foe}。\n# en: {name} {v:slips|slip} a hand into {foe’s} purse in passing.\nflavor.sleight.cast.0 [b81c62] = {name}擦身而过时，顺手把手探进了{foe’s}钱袋。\n# en: {name} {v:snaps|snap} together a whirring contraption and {v:aims|aim} it at {foe}.\nflavor.contraption.cast.0 [1a1b7b] = {name}三两下拼出一台嗡嗡作响的奇械，对准了{foe}。\n# en: {name’s} contraption bursts in a shower of sparks and cogs.\nflavor.contraption.end [9f7d70] = {name’s}奇械炸开了，迸出一阵火花和齿轮。\n# en: A lance of frost flies from {name} at {foe}.\nflavor.hoarfrost.cast.0 [4396d3] = 一支冰枪从{name}手中飞向{foe}。\n# en: {foe} {fv:falls|fall}, frozen stiff by {name’s} frost.\nflavor.hoarfrost.ko [f72a68] = {foe}被{name’s}寒霜冻得僵直，倒了下去。\n# en: {name} {v:asks|ask} {foe} a disarming question, but it does little here.\nflavor.candour.cast.0 [dd1743] = {name}向{foe}抛出一个叫人卸下心防的问题，可在这里没什么用。\n# en: {name} {v:plants|plant} both feet, braced to take {foe’s} hits.\nflavor.brace.cast.0 [31bae1] = {name}双脚扎稳，准备硬接{foe’s}攻击。\n# en: {name} {v:eases|ease} out of the braced stance.\nflavor.brace.end [e7b394] = {name}放松了坚守的架势。\n# en: A quill flies around {name}, but it does little in this situation.\nflavor.quickquill.cast.0 [9640fa] = 一支羽毛笔绕着{name}飞舞，可眼下派不上什么用场。\n# en: Thorny vines lash out at {foe} at {name’s} call and snatch at {fpos} ankles.\nflavor.bramble.cast.0 [01836e] = 在{name’s}召唤下，带刺的藤蔓抽向{foe}，缠住{fpos}脚踝。\n# en: {name} {v:leafs|leaf} through a conjured index, but it does little here.\nflavor.index.cast.0 [595226] = {name}翻阅一本变出来的索引，可在这里没什么用。\n# en: {name} {v:flaunts|flaunt} a handful of crowns, and the crowd cheers.\nflavor.rapport.cast.0 [47a830] = {name}亮出一把克朗，人群欢呼起来。\n# en: {name} {v:draws|draw} some of {pos} own strength out and {v:distills|distill} it into magic.\nflavor.purify.cast.0 [2fb01b] = {name}从自己身上抽出一点力气，萃取成魔力。\n# en: {name} {v:lowers|lower} its antlers and {v:charges|charge} at {foe}.\nflavor.gore.cast.0 [0a65f1] = {name}低下鹿角，朝{foe}冲了过去。\n# en: {name} {v:paws|paw} the ground, then {v:drives|drive} its antlers at {foe}.\nflavor.gore.cast.1 [8cb2b5] = {name}刨了刨地，随即用鹿角顶向{foe}。\n# en: {foe} {fv:is|are} flung down by {name’s} charge.\nflavor.gore.ko [69a96d] = {foe}被{name’s}冲撞掀翻在地。\n# en: A gust rises at {name’s} back and hurls {name} at {foe}.\nflavor.tailwind.cast.0 [2a8a7f] = 一阵风在{name}背后升起，把{name}推向{foe}。\n# en: The wind at {name’s} back gusts again, stronger, and drives {name} at {foe}.\nflavor.tailwind.cast.1 [341bb7] = {name}背后的风又起，更加猛烈，推着{name}冲向{foe}。\n# en: The wind at {name’s} back dies down.\nflavor.tailwind.end [da9550] = {name}背后的风渐渐停了。\n# en: {foe} {fv:is|are} bowled over on {name’s} wind.\nflavor.tailwind.ko [ea7cea] = {foe}被{name’s}风掀翻在地。\n# en: {name} {v:darts|dart} in and {v:nips|nip} at {foe}.\nflavor.nip.cast.0 [364a9b] = {name}猛地窜上前，在{foe}身上咬了一口。\n# en: {name} {v:snaps|snap} at {foe’s} ankles and {v:darts|dart} away.\nflavor.nip.cast.1 [3e8a10] = {name}冲着{foe’s}脚踝一口咬下，又飞快地窜开。\n# en: {foe} {fv:gives|give} up, nipped one time too many by {name}.\nflavor.nip.ko [f5effb] = {foe}被{name}咬了太多口，终于认输了。\n# en: {name} {v:skitters|skitter} aside, out of reach.\nflavor.skitter.cast.0 [205a21] = {name}一窜身躲到一旁，够不着了。\n# en: {name} {v:whisks|whisk} out of sight.\nflavor.skitter.cast.1 [cc1349] = {name}嗖地一下没了踪影。\n# en: {name} {v:edges|edge} back into view.\nflavor.skitter.end [abe1bb] = {name}又慢慢露出了身影。\n# en: {name} {v:digs|dig} in low, braced.\nflavor.burrow.cast.0 [8b05bd] = {name}伏低身子钻进地里，稳住阵脚。\n# en: {name} {v:wriggles|wriggle} back up out of {pos} hollow.\nflavor.burrow.end [28a195] = {name}从坑里扭着身子钻了出来。\n# en: {name} {v:snatches|snatch} at the loose sparks around {foe} and {v:gulps|gulp} them down.\nflavor.filch.cast.0 [2cf1a5] = {name}一口叼住{foe}身边飘散的魔力火花，囫囵吞了下去。\n# en: {name} {v:hunches|hunch} down behind {pos} thick, quilled hide.\nflavor.hide.cast.0 [7e2a63] = {name}缩在一身长满硬刺的厚皮后面。\n# en: {name} {v:uncurls|uncurl}, bristling.\nflavor.hide.end [b6c23b] = {name}舒展开身子，浑身的刺都竖了起来。\n# en: {name’s} bark creaks and thickens, closing over like a shield.\nflavor.bark.cast.0 [b4042f] = {name’s}树皮嘎吱作响，越长越厚，像盾牌一样合拢起来。\n# en: {name} {v:draws|draw} in under {pos} carapace.\nflavor.carapace.cast.0 [2f9b7a] = {name}缩进了甲壳里。\n# en: {name} {v:rears|rear} up and {v:roars|roar}: the blast of it rolls at {foe}, and loose magic scatters like startled birds.\nflavor.roar.cast.0 [6c8599] = {name}人立而起，一声咆哮：声浪滚向{foe}，散逸的魔力像受惊的鸟群一样四散。\n# en: {name} {v:rears|rear} up on {pos} hind legs, towering over {foe}: the maul comes down next exchange.\nflavor.maul.cast.0 [9ca82d] = {name}用后腿人立起来，高高耸立在{foe}面前：重掌将在下一回合落下。\n# en: {name} {v:comes|come} down on {foe} with a crushing maul.\nflavor.maul.end [4e6697] = {name}一记重掌朝{foe}砸了下来。\n# en: {foe} {fv:goes|go} down under {name’s} maul.\nflavor.maul.ko [3a2fb3] = {foe}倒在了{name’s}重掌之下。\n# en: {name} {v:lowers|lower} {pos} horns and {v:scrapes|scrape} the ground: the charge comes next exchange.\nflavor.charge.cast.0 [531fd7] = {name}压低犄角，刨着地面：下一回合就要冲过来了。\n# en: {name} {v:thunders|thunder} into {foe}.\nflavor.charge.end [7c8043] = {name}轰隆隆地朝{foe}冲了过去。\n# en: {foe} {fv:is|are} bowled over by {name’s} charge.\nflavor.charge.ko [919988] = {foe}被{name’s}冲锋撞翻在地。\n# en: {name} {v:snaps|snap} {pos} great mandibles at {foe}.\nflavor.pinch.cast.0 [a39770] = {name}张开巨大的上颚朝{foe}咬去。\n# en: {name} {v:scissors|scissor} {pos} pincers at {foe}.\nflavor.pinch.cast.1 [7f573e] = {name}挥动钳子朝{foe}一夹。\n# en: {foe} {fv:staggers|stagger} back from {name’s} mandibles and {fv:goes|go} no further.\nflavor.pinch.ko [5b744b] = {foe}从{name’s}上颚前踉跄后退，再也无力向前。\n# en: Sparks crackle between {name’s} mandibles and leap at {foe} as a spurt of flame.\nflavor.beetle_flame.cast.0 [28af7f] = 火花在{name’s}上颚之间噼啪作响，化作一股火焰喷向{foe}。\n# en: {foe} {fv:backs|back} away from {name’s} sparking mandibles.\nflavor.beetle_flame.ko [154c75] = {foe}从{name’s}火花四溅的上颚前退开了。\n# en: {name} {v:draws|draw} back a heavy bough, creaking: it swings in two exchanges.\nflavor.bough.cast.0 [2bb20e] = {name}吱呀作响地向后扳起一根粗枝：两个回合后它将横扫而来。\n# en: {name’s} bough sweeps into {foe}.\nflavor.bough.end [d33cf6] = {name’s}粗枝朝{foe}横扫过去。\n# en: {foe} {fv:is|are} swept off {fpos} feet by {name’s} bough.\nflavor.bough.ko [bc69b4] = {foe}被{name’s}粗枝扫倒在地。\n# en: {name} {v:shakes|shake} {pos} crown: a glowing acorn flies at {foe}, and a second hangs, waiting to drop.\nflavor.acorns.cast.0 [b361dd] = {name}摇动树冠：一颗发光的橡果飞向{foe}，另一颗悬在那里，等着落下。\n# en: The second enchanted acorn drops at {foe}.\nflavor.acorns.end [0a945a] = 第二颗附魔的橡果朝{foe}落了下来。\n# en: {name} {v:begins|begin}, very slowly, to lean: a bough the size of a house comes down in two exchanges.\nflavor.slow_fall.cast.0 [3445d2] = {name}开始非常、非常缓慢地倾斜：两个回合后，一根房子那么大的树枝就要落下来了。\n# en: {name’s} bough comes down on {foe} like the sky falling.\nflavor.slow_fall.end [77f779] = {name’s}树枝如天塌一般朝{foe}压了下来。\n# en: {foe} {fv:is|are} pinned beneath {name’s} fallen bough.\nflavor.slow_fall.ko [d32e1c] = {foe}被压在{name’s}落枝底下，动弹不得。\n# en: Saplings and brambles shoulder up out of the earth around {name}, thorns turned on {foe}.\nflavor.thicket.cast.0 [4a8db3] = 树苗和荆棘从{name}周围的土里拱了出来，尖刺对准了{foe}。\n# en: {name’s} thicket sinks back into the ground.\nflavor.thicket.end [d9d35b] = {name’s}灌丛缩回了地里。\n# en: {name} {v:goes|go} utterly still, and a strand of glowing silk around {foe} begins, very gently, to tighten: in three exchanges it closes.\nflavor.the_long_wait.cast.0 [fd9053] = {name}一动不动，{foe}周围一缕发光的蛛丝开始非常轻柔地收紧：三个回合后它就会合拢。\n# en: The enchanted silk snaps taut, and {name} {v:lunges|lunge} with all eight legs behind it.\nflavor.the_long_wait.end [f9cfa5] = 附魔的蛛丝猛然绷紧，{name}八条腿一齐发力扑了过去。\n# en: {foe} {fv:hangs|hang} in {name’s} silk, finished.\nflavor.the_long_wait.ko [096369] = {foe}挂在{name’s}蛛丝里，再也动不了了。\n# en: A scuttle of little spiders pours out from under {name} and swarms over {foe}.\nflavor.brood.cast.0 [b40be7] = 一窝小蜘蛛从{name}身下涌出，爬满了{foe}全身。\n# en: {name’s} brood scatters into the dark.\nflavor.brood.end [54c0a8] = {name’s}幼蛛四散，钻进了黑暗里。\n# en: Roots heave out of the ground and coil around {foe’s} legs at {name’s} call.\nflavor.root.cast.0 [b81e66] = 随着{name’s}召唤，树根破土而出，缠住了{foe’s}双腿。\n# en: {name} {v:flings|fling} a sheet of sticky silk at {foe}.\nflavor.web.cast.0 [5c3fd9] = {name}朝{foe}甩出一张黏糊糊的蛛网。\n# en: {name} {v:trails|trail} a mesh of sticky threads around {foe’s} feet.\nflavor.threads.cast.0 [9d9183] = {name}在{foe’s}脚边拖出一圈黏稠的蛛丝。\n# en: {name} {v:darts|dart} in to bite, fangs dripping.\nflavor.venom.cast.0 [6855cb] = {name}倏地扑上来要咬，毒牙滴着毒液。\n# en: {name} {v:steps|step} back into the shadows and {v:is|are} gone.\nflavor.fade.cast.0 [a5ef0a] = {name}退入阴影，消失不见。\n# en: {name} {v:steps|step} out of the dark again.\nflavor.fade.end [e2d0db] = {name}又从黑暗中走了出来。\n# en: {name} {v:pours|pour} over {foe} in a biting, scuttling wave.\nflavor.swarm.cast.0 [7e4098] = {name}像一股又咬又爬的潮水，涌过{foe}全身。\n# en: {foe} {fv:staggers|stagger} out of {name}, beaten.\nflavor.swarm.ko [57b91f] = {foe}踉跄着从{name}里挣出来，败了。\n# en: {name} {v:sews|sew} a sliver of rime into {pos} collar, against a fall.\nflavor.rime.cast.0 [eb7223] = {name}把一缕雾凇缝进衣领，以防跌倒。\n# en: A mirror-bright sheen settles over {name}, ready to throw {foe’s} spells back.\nflavor.reflect.cast.0 [3ee8fc] = 一层镜面般的光泽覆上{name}全身，随时准备把{foe’s}法术弹回去。\n# en: The mirror sheen around {name} fades.\nflavor.reflect.end [218e9f] = {name}身上的镜面光泽褪去了。\n# en: A pool of moonlight opens at {name’s} feet.\nflavor.moonwell.cast.0 [a0a7dd] = 一汪月光在{name}脚下铺开。\n# en: The moonwell at {name’s} feet fills higher.\nflavor.moonwell.hold [fb7658] = {name}脚下的月泉越积越满。\n# en: {name} {v:drinks|drink} the moonwell down and {v:fills|fill} with magic.\nflavor.moonwell.release [9896a8] = {name}将月泉一饮而尽，周身充盈着魔力。\n# en: A small star kindles above {name} and begins, slowly, to rise.\nflavor.lodestar.cast.0 [e2bd32] = 一颗小小的星辰在{name}头顶点亮，开始缓缓上升。\n# en: The star above {name} reaches its height, and its light settles over {pos} wounds.\nflavor.lodestar.end [6d9a1b] = {name}头顶的星辰升到了顶点，它的光芒落在{pos}伤口上。\n# en: A single bell note sounds from nowhere.\nflavor.toll.cast.0 [9e6185] = 不知从哪里传来一声钟鸣。\n# en: {name} {v:lays|lay} a curse on {foe}: every move will hurt.\nflavor.torment.cast.0 [182c9e] = {name}对{foe}下了诅咒：每动一下都会痛。\n# en: {name’s} curse on {foe} wears off.\nflavor.torment.end [32b03c] = {name}施在{foe}身上的诅咒消退了。\n# en: {name} {v:pulls|pull} the ready magic straight out of {foe}.\nflavor.extraction.cast.0 [2a0ddd] = {name}把{foe}体内可用的魔力直接抽了出来。\n# en: A dark thread links {name} to {foe} and starts to drink.\nflavor.siphon.cast.0 [971a5c] = 一缕暗线连起{name}与{foe}，开始吸取。\n# en: The dark thread drinks more from {foe}.\nflavor.siphon.hold [beadda] = 暗线从{foe}身上吸走了更多。\n# en: {name} {v:snaps|snap} the thread and {v:takes|take} in everything it drank.\nflavor.siphon.release [9d894f] = {name}掐断暗线，把它吸来的一切尽数纳入体内。\n# en: {foe} {fv:sags|sag}, drained by {name’s} dark thread.\nflavor.siphon.ko [ea9177] = {foe}瘫软下去，被{name’s}暗线吸干了力气。\n# en: {name} {v:speaks|speak} a word of command, binding {foe} to the move {fv:is|are} making now.\nflavor.compel.cast.0 [1d4489] = {name}说出一个命令的字眼，把{foe}牢牢钉在{fpos}此刻的这一招上。\n# en: {name} {v:shakes|shake} off the compulsion.\nflavor.compel.end [1eba16] = {name}挣脱了强令。\n# en: {name} {v:is|are} compelled to repeat the seized move: {action}.\nflavor.compel.forced [376905] = {name}被迫重复被夺下的那一招：{action}。\n# en: {foe’s} defences shut out {name’s} command.\nflavor.compel.shut [6e0dbc] = {foe’s}防御挡住了{name’s}命令。\n# en: A dark disc slides over {name}, turning the next blows into magic.\nflavor.eclipse.cast.0 [746799] = 一轮暗盘滑过{name}上空，把接下来的打击化为魔力。\n# en: The eclipse over {name} passes.\nflavor.eclipse.end [d4053f] = {name}上空的蚀过去了。\n# en: {name} {v:strikes|strike} a bargain: from now on, spells cost life instead of magic.\nflavor.heartprice.cast.0 [f963af] = {name}立下一笔交易：从今往后，法术要付出的是生命，而不是魔力。\n# en: {name’s} force drives into the shadows, but {foe} {fv:is|are} not there.\nattack.strike.evaded [696cfc] = {name’s}力道冲进阴影里，可{foe}并不在那儿。\n# en: {foe} {fv:turns|turn} {name’s} force aside with a parry.\nattack.strike.guarded [fdab91] = {foe}一记招架，把{name’s}力道拨到了一旁。\n# en: {foe’s} tiring parry gives, and part of {name’s} force gets through.\nattack.strike.tiredGuard [c5c509] = {foe’s}招架渐渐乏力，{name’s}力道有一部分透了进去。\n# en: {name’s} force thuds into {foe’s} ward, losing half its push.\nattack.strike.warded [ac32fc] = {name’s}力道砸在{foe’s}护界上，冲劲减了一半。\n# en: {foe} {fv:takes|take} {name’s} force braced, barely moving.\nattack.strike.braced [78b8aa] = {foe}稳稳扎住，硬接下{name’s}力道，几乎纹丝不动。\n# en: {name’s} fireball passes through the shadows where {foe} stood.\nattack.flame.evaded [a43bf5] = {name’s}火球穿过了{foe}方才所在的阴影。\n# en: {name’s} fireball splashes against {foe’s} ward and fizzles out.\nattack.flame.warded [72ce9d] = {name’s}火球撞上{foe’s}护界，噼啪几声熄灭了。\n# en: {foe’s} parry turns half of {name’s} fireball aside.\nattack.flame.guarded [d16039] = {foe’s}招架把{name’s}火球拨开了一半。\n# en: {foe} {fv:plants|plant} {fpos} feet and {fv:weathers|weather} {name’s} fireball.\nattack.flame.braced [776120] = {foe}扎稳脚跟，扛住了{name’s}火球。\n# en: {name’s} fireball bounces off {foe’s} mirror sheen and flies back at {name}.\nattack.flame.reflected [fc7d70] = {name’s}火球从{foe’s}镜面光泽上弹开，飞回了{name}这边。\n# en: {name’s} frost lance flies through the shadows where {foe} stood.\nattack.hoarfrost.evaded [8e49af] = {name’s}冰枪穿过了{foe}方才所在的阴影。\n# en: {name’s} frost lance shatters on {foe’s} ward.\nattack.hoarfrost.warded [671a16] = {name’s}冰枪在{foe’s}护界上碎裂。\n# en: {foe} {fv:knocks|knock} {name’s} frost lance aside with a parry, taking half the chill.\nattack.hoarfrost.guarded [3a340b] = {foe}一记招架把{name’s}冰枪拨到一旁，只承受了一半的寒意。\n# en: {name’s} frost lance glances off {foe’s} mirror sheen and turns back on {name}.\nattack.hoarfrost.reflected [02a971] = {name’s}冰枪擦过{foe’s}镜面光泽，掉头刺回了{name}。\n# en: {name} {v:charges|charge} into the shadows and finds no one there.\nattack.gore.evaded [54858b] = {name}冲进阴影，却扑了个空。\n# en: {foe} {fv:turns|turn} {name’s} antlers aside with a parry.\nattack.gore.guarded [91973f] = {foe}一记招架，把{name’s}鹿角拨到了一旁。\n# en: {name’s} antlers drive into {foe’s} ward, which takes half the charge.\nattack.gore.warded [6e3eda] = {name’s}鹿角顶上{foe’s}护界，冲势被挡下一半。\n# en: {foe} {fv:meets|meet} {name’s} charge braced and {fv:holds|hold} firm.\nattack.gore.braced [d2497c] = {foe}稳稳扎住，迎上{name’s}冲撞，寸步不让。\n# en: {name’s} wildfire sweeps through the shadows where {foe} stood.\nattack.wildfire.evaded [502664] = {name’s}野火扫过了{foe}方才所在的阴影。\n# en: {name’s} wildfire breaks against {foe’s} ward, and part of it burns on through.\nattack.wildfire.warded [3a50ab] = {name’s}野火撞上{foe’s}护界，仍有一部分烧了过去。\n# en: {foe’s} parry turns half of {name’s} wildfire aside.\nattack.wildfire.guarded [024e8a] = {foe’s}招架把{name’s}野火拨开了一半。\n# en: {name’s} wildfire rolls off {foe’s} mirror sheen and back over {name}.\nattack.wildfire.reflected [73b19a] = {name’s}野火从{foe’s}镜面光泽上翻卷回去，扑向了{name}。\n# en: {name’s} beam of starlight lights up empty shadows.\nattack.starlight.evaded [b42374] = {name’s}星光照亮了空荡荡的阴影。\n# en: {name’s} beam of starlight scatters on {foe’s} ward.\nattack.starlight.warded [ba2620] = {name’s}星光在{foe’s}护界上四散开来。\n# en: {foe’s} parry turns half of {name’s} starlight aside.\nattack.starlight.guarded [ad1d56] = {foe’s}招架把{name’s}星光拨开了一半。\n# en: {name’s} starlight bounces off {foe’s} mirror sheen straight back at {name}.\nattack.starlight.reflected [651e01] = {name’s}星光从{foe’s}镜面光泽上弹开，直直射回了{name}。\n# en: {foe} {fv:is|are} in the shadows: {name’s} {noun} passes straight through.\ndefended.evaded [1ed393] = {foe}隐在阴影里：{name’s}{noun}径直穿了过去。\n# en: {foe’s} ward meets {name’s} {noun}.\ndefended.warded [c2e256] = {foe’s}护界迎上了{name’s}{noun}。\n# en: {foe} {fv:parries|parry} {name’s} {noun}.\ndefended.guarded [705716] = {foe}招架住了{name’s}{noun}。\n# en: {foe’s} tiring parry lets part of {name’s} {noun} through.\ndefended.tiredGuard [b30846] = {foe’s}招架渐渐乏力，{name’s}{noun}有一部分透了进来。\n# en: {foe} {fv:weathers|weather} {name’s} {noun} in a braced stance.\ndefended.braced [bc4bd8] = {foe}以坚守的架势扛住了{name’s}{noun}。\n# en: {foe’s} mirror sheen throws {name’s} {noun} back at {name}.\ndefended.reflected [9a85de] = {foe’s}镜面光泽把{name’s}{noun}弹回给了{name}。\n# en: {foe’s} summoning soaks up part of {name’s} {noun}.\ndefended.absorbed [f4f54f] = {foe’s}召唤物吸收了{name’s}{noun}的一部分。\n# en: {foe’s} risen star swallows {name’s} {noun} whole.\ndefended.negated [31b040] = {foe’s}升起的星辰把{name’s}{noun}整个吞没了。\n# en: {foe} {fv:is|are} frozen in rime, and {name’s} {noun} can’t touch {foe}.\ndefended.frozen [6ae96f] = {foe}被冻在雾凇里，{name’s}{noun}碰不到{foe}。\n# en: nip\nnoun.nip [a41868] = 轻咬\n# en: maul\nnoun.maul [862572] = 重掌\n# en: charge\nnoun.charge [ca2371] = 冲锋\n# en: mandibles\nnoun.pinch [24954e] = 上颚\n# en: spurt of flame\nnoun.beetle_flame [9437d3] = 火焰\n# en: bough\nnoun.bough [6d3c9e] = 粗枝\n# en: acorn\nnoun.acorns [f04ac6] = 橡果\n# en: thicket\nnoun.thicket [18351f] = 灌丛\n# en: falling bough\nnoun.slow_fall [93a7a5] = 落枝\n# en: silk and strike\nnoun.the_long_wait [2eaafd] = 蛛丝与扑击\n# en: swarm\nnoun.swarm [e7389f] = 虫群\n# en: venom\nnoun.venom [a9940a] = 毒\n# en: brood\nnoun.brood [820395] = 幼蛛\n# en: force\nnoun.strike [a98884] = 力道\n# en: fireball\nnoun.flame [bad9b8] = 火球\n# en: frost lance\nnoun.hoarfrost [1f14ca] = 冰枪\n# en: charge\nnoun.gore [ca2371] = 冲撞\n# en: wind-driven rush\nnoun.tailwind [0be856] = 乘风一冲\n# en: roar\nnoun.roar [1fbcdd] = 咆哮\n# en: wildfire\nnoun.wildfire [98f3e9] = 野火\n# en: beam of starlight\nnoun.starlight [0d15b3] = 星光\n# en: dark thread\nnoun.siphon [66ac8f] = 暗线\n# en: thorns\nnoun.bramble [b8a203] = 荆棘\n# en: furniture\nnoun.animate [500965] = 活化家具\n# en: contraption\nnoun.contraption [3efede] = 奇械\n# en: misfortune\nnoun.omen [8da279] = 厄运\n# en: curse\nnoun.torment [4041f5] = 诅咒\n# en: sapling\nnoun.heartwood [c9115b] = 树苗\n# en: attack\nnoun.attack [95b8fd] = 攻击\n# en: {name} can’t be hit for {n}, until {name} {v:attacks|attack} or {v:gathers|gather} Mana.\neffect.shadowstep [e42435] = 接下来 {n} 里，{name}无法被击中，直到{name}发动攻击或回收法力。\n# en: For {n}, {name’s} spells without damage of their own also throw an Impulse, and every other action also parries.\neffect.haste [79e09c] = 接下来 {n} 里，{name’s}本身不造成伤害的法术也会附带一次冲击，其他每个行动都会同时招架。\n# en: {name} {v:shrugs|shrug} off most of the damage for {n}.\neffect.brace [3ec0e3] = 接下来 {n} 里，{name}能扛下大部分伤害。\n# en: Spells cast at {name} bounce back for {n}.\neffect.reflect [0f9e07] = 接下来 {n} 里，对{name}施放的法术都会被弹回。\n# en: Every move {foe} {fv:makes|make} costs {fobj} Health for {n}.\neffect.torment [b2285a] = 接下来 {n} 里，{foe}每做一个动作都要付出生命。\n# en: Blows that hit {name} turn into Mana for {n}.\neffect.eclipse [ab0c02] = 接下来 {n} 里，击中{name}的打击会化为法力。\n# en: {foe} must repeat that move for {n}.\neffect.compelled [978124] = 接下来 {n} 里，{foe}必须重复这一招。\n# en: {name’s} magic becomes Health, and {name’s} spells cost Health from now on.\neffect.heartprice [921f90] = {name’s}魔力化作了生命，从现在起，法术改用生命支付。\n# en: The star rises over {name}: in {n} it shields and heals.\neffect.lodestar [7f4295] = 星辰在{name}上方升起：{n}后它将带来庇护与治疗。\n# en: {foe} must break free for {n}.\neffect.bound [95e3a2] = 接下来 {n} 里，{foe}必须奋力挣脱。\n# en: The poison works in {foe} for {n}.\neffect.poison [f939b4] = 接下来 {n} 里，毒在{foe}体内发作。\n# en: {name’s} {noun} hits {foe}.\nline.hits [fe6048] = {name’s}{noun}击中了{foe}。\n# en: {ally} throws {noun} at {foe} beside {name}.\nline.assist [b01aa4] = {ally}在{name}身旁向{foe}掷出{noun}。\n# en: {name} {v:takes|take} {n} damage from {pos} own {noun}.\nline.reflectedBack [08ea34] = {name}被自己的{noun}反伤了 {n} 点。\n# en: It does no damage.\nline.noDamage [4cca69] = 没有造成伤害。\n# en: {foe} {fv:takes|take} {n} damage.\nline.takes [542453] = {foe}受到 {n} 点伤害。\n# en: {foe} {fv:takes|take} {n} damage from {hits} hits.\nline.takesHits [b04be9] = {foe}挨了 {hits} 下，共受到 {n} 点伤害。\n# en: {name} {v:regains|regain} {n} Mana.\nline.regains [3f633e] = {name}恢复了 {n} 点法力。\n# en: There is no loose magic left to gather.\nline.noLooseMagic [bbc322] = 空气中已没有可以收拢的游离魔力。\n# en: {name} {v:heals|heal} {n} HP.\nline.heals [7048df] = {name}恢复了 {n} 点生命。\n# en: The mending breaks: no healing.\nline.mendBreaks [9e71cf] = 愈合被打断了：没有治疗。\n# en: {foe’s} defences keep {fpos} Mana in place: it takes nothing.\nline.extractionBlocked [bd3e16] = {foe’s}防御护住了{fpos}法力：什么也没抽到。\n# en: {n} of {foe’s} Mana becomes {pos} loose magic.\nline.extraction [44202a] = {foe’s} {n} 点法力变成了{pos}游离魔力。\n# en: There is no room for more magic: it takes nothing.\nline.extractionFull [87ddb4] = 已经装不下更多魔力：什么也没抽到。\n# en: {foe’s} defences keep {fpos} loose magic safe.\nline.sealBlocked [07a63f] = {foe’s}防御护住了{fpos}游离魔力。\n# en: {foe} {fv:loses|lose} {n} loose magic.\nline.seal [fa0f33] = {foe}失去了 {n} 点游离魔力。\n# en: There was no loose magic to snuff out.\nline.sealNothing [77b307] = 没有可以掐灭的游离魔力。\n# en: {name} {v:pays|pay} Health for {n} Mana.\nline.purify [803aef] = {name}以生命换取了 {n} 点法力。\n# en: {foe} must commit first for the next {ex}.\nline.commitFirst [006f83] = 接下来 {ex} 里，{foe}必须先出招。\n# en: {foe} must repeat it for the next three exchanges.\nline.repeatAgain [68c56f] = 接下来三个回合，{foe}必须重复这一招。\n# en: {foe} must repeat it for the next three exchanges: {action}.\nline.repeatAgainAction [260a07] = 接下来三个回合，{foe}必须重复这一招：{action}。\n# en: Compelled, {name} {v:repeats|repeat} the seized move.\nline.compelledRepeat [2220f0] = 受强令所迫，{name}重复了被夺下的那一招。\n# en: {foe’s} defences keep {name’s} {spell} off.\nline.applyBlocked [5cc04f] = {foe’s}防御挡下了{name’s}{spell}。\n# en: {foe’s} mirror sheen turns {name’s} {spell} back on {name}.\nline.applyReflected [ced87d] = {foe’s}镜面光泽把{name’s}{spell}反照回了{name}身上。\n# en: {foe’s} mirror sheen turns {name’s} {spell} back, and {name’s} own defences keep it off.\nline.applyReflectedBlocked [39268d] = {foe’s}镜面光泽把{name’s}{spell}反照了回去，而{name’s}自己的防御挡下了它。\n# en: {foe} can fight no more, and {name} {v:wins|win}.\nending.knockout [c16566] = {foe}再也打不动了，{name}获胜。\n# en: Neither falls; {name} {v:wins|win} on points.\nending.decision [119097] = 谁也没有倒下；{name}以点数获胜。\n# en: Neither {name} nor {foe} can finish it: a draw.\nending.stalemate [179269] = {name}和{foe}谁也拿不下对方：平局。\n# en: {foe} {fv:yields|yield} to {name}.\nending.conceded [c6f4c2] = {foe}向{name}认输。\n# en: {foe} {fv:lowers|lower} its head and {fv:steps|step} aside for {name}.\ndefeat.shadow_stag [b81b70] = {foe}低下头，退到一旁，为{name}让开了路。\n# en: {foe} {fv:topples|topple} over in a puff of straw, and Thorne nods at {name}.\ndefeat.practice_mannequin [f00aca] = {foe}扑地倒下，扬起一阵稻草，索恩教授冲{name}点了点头。\n# en: {foe} {fv:chatters|chatter} something rude and {fv:vanishes|vanish} into the brambles.\ndefeat.bramble_ferret [e18f42] = {foe}吱吱骂了句难听的，钻进荆棘丛不见了。\n# en: {foe} {fv:huffs|huff}, {fv:turns|turn} and {fv:lumbers|lumber} off into the trees.\ndefeat.bristleback [a57678] = {foe}喷了口粗气，转过身，慢吞吞地走进了林子。\n# en: {foe} {fv:clicks|click} {fpos} horns once and {fv:trundles|trundle} away under a log.\ndefeat.stag_beetle [43d718] = {foe}咔哒碰了一下犄角，慢悠悠地爬进了一根倒木底下。\n# en: {foe} {fv:goes|go} still and creaky, the way trees are meant to be.\ndefeat.grumpy_oak [99b05b] = {foe}安静下来，只剩嘎吱声——树本来就该是这样。\n# en: {foe} {fv:sighs|sigh} through every leaf and {fv:goes|go} still.\ndefeat.elder_oak [7d8333] = {foe}的每一片叶子都叹了口气，然后静止了。\n# en: {foe} {fv:scatters|scatter} into a thousand cracks.\ndefeat.spiderling_swarm [b27432] = {foe}四散钻进了千百道缝隙。\n# en: {foe} {fv:bows|bow} {fpos} forelegs to {name}.\ndefeat.the_spinner [116c69] = {foe}向{name}屈下了前腿。\n# en: {foe} {fv:bows|bow} {fpos} forelegs to {name}.\ndefeat.the_spinner_weakened [116c69] = {foe}向{name}屈下了前腿。\n# en: {foe} {fv:laughs|laugh}, {fv:bows|bow}, and {fv:vanishes|vanish} into silver frost.\ndefeat.the_outcast [10efe7] = {foe}大笑一声，欠身行礼，化作一片银霜消失了。\n# en: you\ngrammar.you [b8d09c] = 你\n# en: your\ngrammar.your [f0e6aa] = 你的\n# en: {n}’s\ngrammar.possessive [71a797] = {n}的\n# en: , then \ngrammar.then [43c04e] = ，然后\n# en: 1 exchange\ngrammar.exchange1 [3812e1] = 1 个回合\n# en: {n} exchanges\ngrammar.exchanges [673775] = {n} 个回合\n# en: the rest of the fight\ngrammar.restOfFight [d8f962] = 本场战斗余下的时间\n# en: two\ngrammar.two [248829] = 两\n# en: three\ngrammar.three [8603c3] = 三\n# en: An ally\ngrammar.anAlly [b11df0] = 一位盟友\n# en: your\ngrammar.you.pos [f0e6aa] = 你的\n# en: you\ngrammar.you.obj [b8d09c] = 你\n# en: their\ngrammar.they.pos [fd3c67] = TA的\n# en: them\ngrammar.they.obj [22e7e3] = TA\n# en: her\ngrammar.she.pos [b48d8c] = 她的\n# en: her\ngrammar.she.obj [b48d8c] = 她\n# en: his\ngrammar.he.pos [d2b0cb] = 他的\n# en: him\ngrammar.he.obj [d2c6d5] = 他\n# en: its\ngrammar.it.pos [f867d9] = 它的\n# en: it\ngrammar.it.obj [388410] = 它\n# en:  \ngrammar.sentenceSep [0c8f7f] = ​\n# en: your opponent\nfoe.tournament_entrant [124943] = 你的对手\n# en: the unbeaten fourth-year\nfoe.tournament_unbeaten [50a17e] = 那位不败的四年级生\n# en: the fourth-year\nfoe.quick_fourth_year [53e4ee] = 那位四年级生\n# en: your sparring partner\nfoe.club_partner [e1feaa] = 你的陪练\n# en: the visiting champion\nfoe.visiting_champion [6ca4e8] = 来访的冠军\n# en: Thornwick’s best\nfoe.thornwick_best [10ffbe] = 荆木学院的头号好手\n# en: the Thornwick duellist\nfoe.thornwick_duellist [a62f33] = 荆木学院的决斗手\n# en: the Thornwick illusionist\nfoe.thornwick_illusionist [6033f2] = 荆木学院的幻术师\n# en: the boy with the sketchbook\nfoe.thornwick_sketcher [3452a2] = 拿速写本的男孩\n# en: the Thornwick final-year\nfoe.thornwick_final_year [e1bc13] = 荆木学院的毕业班学生\n# en: your opponent\nfoe.ring_opener [124943] = 你的对手\n# en: the dropout\nfoe.ring_dropout [25d1bb] = 那个退学生\n# en: the fairground conjurer\nfoe.ring_conjurer [2b85e9] = 集市上的变戏法的\n# en: the hedge-hexer\nfoe.ring_hexer [d6f892] = 那个野巫\n# en: the Conclave clerk\nfoe.ring_clerk [5ec80e] = 议会的文书\n# en: the disgraced prefect\nfoe.ring_prefect [5c9ceb] = 被摘了绶带的级长\n# en: the hooded caster\nfoe.ring_hooded [47ca95] = 戴兜帽的施法者\n# en: the ring’s champion\nfoe.ring_champion [08ac99] = 擂台的冠军\n# en: the bookmaker\nfoe.ring_bookmaker [6075d3] = 那个庄家\n# en: the Thornwick runaway\nfoe.ring_thornwick [081e80] = 荆木学院的逃学生\n# en: the man in grey\nfoe.man_in_grey [47b3be] = 灰衣人\n# en: the thing of frost\nfoe.frost_wraith [03d2af] = 那个霜之物\n# en: Madame Dumont\nfoe.hollow_matriarch [99855b] = 杜蒙夫人\n# en: your shadow\nfoe.your_shadow [d25e9e] = 你的影子\n# en: the shadow stag\nfoe.shadow_stag [c41cbf] = 影鹿\n# en: the practice mannequin\nfoe.practice_mannequin [d5963d] = 练习用的木人\n# en: the bramble ferret\nfoe.bramble_ferret [7f60ed] = 荆棘雪貂\n# en: the bristleback\nfoe.bristleback [1c0010] = 刚鬃熊\n# en: the stag beetle\nfoe.stag_beetle [3c3a52] = 锹形甲虫\n# en: the grumpy oak\nfoe.grumpy_oak [10d6bd] = 脾气古怪的橡树\n# en: the elder oak\nfoe.elder_oak [b8b6e5] = 古橡\n# en: the spiderling swarm\nfoe.spiderling_swarm [fdbbd1] = 小蜘蛛群\n# en: the Spinner\nfoe.the_spinner [ed44d9] = 织网者\n# en: the Spinner\nfoe.the_spinner_weak [ed44d9] = 织网者\n# en: the thornwitch\nfoe.thornwitch [21e2a4] = 荆棘女巫\n# en: the masked circle\nfoe.masked_circle [1992b7] = 假面法阵会\n# en: the Outcast\nfoe.the_outcast [073f07] = 放逐者\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/core/rules.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/core/rules.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@game\n# en: Lumenhold Academy\ntitle [fceae1] = 流明堡学院\n# en: Four years. Forty-eight moons. One apprentice.\nsubtitle [e1da8c] = 四年。四十八轮月。一名学徒。\n# en: The sky-barge lurches. Below, the lake glitters; above, the seven towers of Lumenhold Academy pierce the clouds. You have a trunk, a borrowed wand and a letter of acceptance with your name spelled wrong. Four years begin now.\nintro [2dd4dc] = 天空驳船猛地一晃。脚下，湖面波光粼粼；头顶，流明堡学院的七座高塔刺破云层。你有一只行李箱、一根借来的魔杖，还有一封把你名字拼错的录取信。四年，就从此刻开始。\n# en: Harvestmoon\nmonth.1 [39637f] = 丰收月\n# en: Emberwane\nmonth.2 [293c65] = 烬落月\n# en: Mistveil\nmonth.3 [82c3ac] = 雾纱月\n# en: Frostcall\nmonth.4 [dbae91] = 霜唤月\n# en: Deepwinter\nmonth.5 [668c02] = 深冬月\n# en: Thawmoon\nmonth.6 [0fe880] = 融雪月\n# en: Stormbloom\nmonth.7 [58f0f1] = 风暴月\n# en: Greenrise\nmonth.8 [d3ee01] = 青苏月\n# en: Blossomtide\nmonth.9 [dab8ca] = 花潮月\n# en: Highsun\nmonth.10 [c6ab59] = 盛阳月\n# en: Goldenhaze\nmonth.11 [886cae] = 金霭月\n# en: Starfall\nmonth.12 [ab87b4] = 星坠月\n\n@resource health\n# en: Health\nname [6145af] = 健康\n# en: Your body. At zero you collapse.\ndesc [a87509] = 你的身体。归零时，你会倒下。\n\n@resource mood\n# en: Mood\nname [64551e] = 心情\n# en: Your spirits. At zero, despair takes hold.\ndesc [ca3154] = 你的精神。归零时，绝望会将你吞没。\n\n@resource mana\n# en: Mana\nname [78ad2a] = 法力\n# en: The magic you can spend on spells. Refills each month, faster the stronger your Arcana.\ndesc [738cd1] = 你能用来施咒的魔法。每月恢复，奥术越强恢复越快。\n\n@resource renown\n# en: Renown\nname [996fb2] = 声望\n# en: How much the academy talks about you — for better or worse. Fades a little every month.\ndesc [e64a8d] = 学院里有多少人在谈论你——无论好坏。每个月都会淡去一点。\n# en: 30 Known, 60 Famous, 85 Legend\ntiers [e0e400] = 30 小有名气、60 声名鹊起、85 传奇\n\n@resource alignment\n# en: Alignment\nname [6abafe] = 立场\n# en: Lumen or Hollow: the light you serve, or the dark that has noticed you. Deep in the Hollow, the way back is slow.\ndesc [6e8e70] = 光明或空洞：你所侍奉的光，或已经注意到你的黑暗。在空洞中陷得越深，回来的路就越慢。\n\n@resource gold\n# en: Crowns\nname [a78479] = 金币\n# en: Crowns in your purse. Rent is due every month.\ndesc [cf79f6] = 钱袋里的克朗。每个月都要交房租。\n\n@skill arcana\n# en: Arcana\nname [6f1c33] = 奥术\n# en: Raw spellcraft — power, control, nerve.\ndesc [0428ad] = 纯粹的施法功力——力量、控制与胆量。\n\n@skill lore\n# en: Lore\nname [d907ad] = 学识\n# en: Scholarship, theory, history, languages of the old world.\ndesc [5aadaa] = 学问、理论、历史，以及古老世界的语言。\n\n@skill alchemy\n# en: Alchemy\nname [5d01fc] = 炼金\n# en: Potions, herbs, reagents and the patience to stir clockwise.\ndesc [dbde44] = 药剂、草药、试剂，以及顺时针搅拌的耐心。\n\n@skill charm\n# en: Charm\nname [df43f0] = 魅力\n# en: Wit, warmth, persuasion. Making people want to say yes.\ndesc [e1c3b8] = 机智、温暖与说服力。让别人心甘情愿地点头。\n\n@skill vigor\n# en: Vigor\nname [6c0ee4] = 体魄\n# en: Stamina, reflexes, dueling and broom-flight.\ndesc [9f1acb] = 耐力、反应、决斗和飞帚。\n\n@skill guile\n# en: Guile\nname [987273] = 诡计\n# en: Sneaking, lying, noticing what others miss.\ndesc [fe3ade] = 潜行、撒谎，以及注意到别人忽略的东西。\n\n@hook rent\n# en: Dormitory & board\nlabel [547aba] = 宿舍与膳食\n# en: {board_missed == 0: {~The bursar takes the board money with a nod and a tick in a very large book.|Board paid: a bed, three meals and the right to complain about both.|You pay the board and count what's left twice.}|{~The board went unpaid. The refectory still feeds you, a little less each day.|No board money this month. Lunch is a pocketed roll.|Board unpaid again. Your name in the bursar's very large book is underlined now.}}\ndo.say.1 [4a967f] = {board_missed == 0: {~财务官收下食宿费，点一点头，在一本非常厚的账簿上打了个勾。|食宿费付清了：一张床、一日三餐，外加抱怨这两样的权利。|你付了食宿费，把剩下的钱数了两遍。}|{~食宿费没交上。膳堂照样给你饭吃，只是一天比一天少。|这个月交不起食宿费。午饭是一个揣在兜里的面包。|食宿费又没交。财务主管那本大得出奇的账簿上，你的名字底下画了线。}}\n\n@hook break_rent\n# en: Board at the castle\nlabel [0b2949] = 城堡食宿\n# en: {~The castle in the holidays still wants its board.|A bed in an empty dormitory costs the same as a full one.}\ndo.say.1 [5ac98d] = {~假期里的城堡，照样要收食宿费。|空荡荡的宿舍里一张床，和住满时一个价。}\n\n@hook tuition\n# en: Tuition\nlabel [7ee27b] = 学费\n# en: {~Term fees, paid at a window with a very small grille.|The bursar's quill scratches. Another term is paid for.}\ndo.say.1 [4595be] = {~学期费交在一个铁栅格小得可怜的窗口。|财务官的羽毛笔沙沙作响。又一个学期付清了。}\n\n@hook course_fees\n# en: Course fees\nlabel [f462c1] = 课程费\n# en: {~Course fees, paid at the registrar's window with the tuition.|The registrar takes the course fees with a sigh, as if it pained her too.}\ndo.say.1 [f8b592] = {~课程费和学费一起，在教务处的窗口交清。|教务员收下课程费时叹了口气，仿佛她也心疼。}\n\n@hook gpa_probation\n# en: Academic probation\nlabel [5ecefe] = 学业察看\n\n@hook term_stress\n# en: Term-time pressure\nlabel [0e2de2] = 学期压力\n# en: {month == 4 or month == 10: {~Exams are coming, and everybody's quill moves faster.|The library stays open late and is full until it closes.|You dream of an exam you haven't revised for, which is most of them.}|{~Essays stack up on your desk like weather coming in.|Lectures, reading, more reading. Term has a weight of its own.|Everyone is behind. Everyone says so in the corridors.}}\ndo.say.1 [a08f53] = {month == 4 or month == 10: {~考试要来了，人人的羽毛笔都写得更快。|图书馆开到很晚，一直坐满到关门。|你梦见一场没复习过的考试，而没复习过的考试占了大多数。}|{~论文在你桌上越堆越高，像远处压过来的天气。|上课、阅读、更多的阅读。学期自有它的分量。|人人都落后了。走廊里人人都这么说。}}\n\n@hook exhaustion_drain\n# en: Running on empty\nlabel [069bc4] = 筋疲力尽\n# en: {~You are so tired that the stairs look personal.|Your body has opinions about this month, and none of them are kind.|You fall asleep in a lecture and wake up in a different mood.}\ndo.say.1 [76f080] = {~你累得连楼梯都像在跟你作对。|你的身体对这个月颇有意见，而且没一条是好话。|你在课上睡着了，醒来时换了一副心情。}\n\n@hook gloom_drain\n# en: Gloom\nlabel [0e658b] = 阴郁\n# en: {~A grey month. You eat little and sleep badly.|Low spirits sit in your chest like a cold.|The gloom gets into your bones; even the stairs feel steeper.}\ndo.say.1 [f3af29] = {~灰蒙蒙的一个月。你吃得少，睡得差。|低落的情绪像一场感冒，压在你胸口。|阴郁钻进了骨头里，连楼梯都显得更陡了。}\n\n@hook fame_fades\n# en: Fading gossip\nlabel [06d4e9] = 流言消散\n# en: {~The corridors find something else to talk about.|Yesterday's gossip goes the way of yesterday's bread.|Fewer heads turn when you pass. It's restful.}\ndo.say.1 [5662c0] = {~走廊里找到了别的话题。|昨天的闲话，和昨天的面包一样，没人要了。|你经过时回头的人少了。挺清静的。}\n\n@hook mana_regen\n# en: Mana returns\nlabel [d61671] = 法力恢复\n# en: {~The magic seeps back into you overnight, like warmth into cold hands.|Your fingertips tingle again by morning.|Whatever you spent, the castle quietly gives some back.}\ndo.say.1 [27eb51] = {~魔力在夜里一点点渗回你身上，像暖意回到冻僵的手。|到了早上，你的指尖又开始发麻了。|你花掉的，城堡悄悄还回来一些。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/endings.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/endings.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@ending ending_sent_home\n# en: Sent Home\ntitle [a6af26] = 被送回家\n# en: The infirmary writes to your family. A carriage comes for you on a grey morning and you watch the towers shrink in the rear window. Your body gave out before your dreams did. Perhaps, one day, you will come back.\ntext [9741e7] = 医务室给你家里写了信。一个灰蒙蒙的早晨，马车来接你，你从后窗看着高塔渐渐缩小。你的身体先于你的梦想垮掉了。也许有一天，你还会回来。\n\n@ending ending_quiet_departure\n# en: The Quiet Departure\ntitle [b17cb0] = 悄然离去\n# en: You take the dawn barge without telling anyone. The academy does not notice right away; then, one by one, people do. Some of them write. You keep the letters in a box you don't open for years. When you finally do, you are someone gentler, and you find you are not alone.\ntext [e6ed08] = 你没告诉任何人，搭上了黎明的驳船。学院起初并没察觉；后来，人们一个接一个地发现了。有些人给你写了信。你把信收进一只盒子，好多年都没打开。等你终于打开时，你已变得更温柔，而且发现自己并不孤单。\n\n@ending ending_expelled\n# en: Expelled\ntitle [a01536] = 开除学籍\n# en: The Board's decision is final. Your wand is not taken — Lumenhold is not cruel — but your name is struck from the register in ink that cannot be unwritten. You walk out of the gates with a trunk and a lesson, and the long road ahead is entirely your own.\ntext [27607d] = 委员会的决定无法更改。你的魔杖没有被收走——流明堡并不残忍——但你的名字被一种无法抹去的墨水从名册上划掉了。你提着行李箱、带着一个教训走出校门，前方的漫漫长路，完全属于你自己。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_claimed\n# en: Claimed by the Hollow Star\ntitle [83514d] = 被空洞之星吞噬\n# en: You open the door beneath the lake. The Hollow Star looks back. For a moment you understand everything, and then there is no \"you\" left to understand it. On clear nights students swear they see a new star, black against black, right above the seventh tower.\ntext [205ca2] = 你打开了湖底之门。空洞之星回望着你。有那么一瞬间，你明白了一切；随后，再也没有一个“你”能去明白什么了。晴朗的夜里，学生们发誓看见了一颗新星——黑上之黑——就在第七塔的正上方。\n\n@ending ending_runaway\n# en: Gone With the Wind\ntitle [d9dde5] = 随风而去\n# en: You and Corvin leave Lumenhold on a stolen sky-barge at midnight, laughing so hard you can barely steer. The academy will tell the story for years — usually wrong. You never graduate. You don't regret it, mostly.\ntext [c5d68b] = 午夜时分，你和科尔文驾着一艘偷来的天空驳船离开了流明堡，笑得几乎掌不稳舵。学院会把这个故事讲上好多年——而且通常讲错。你始终没有毕业。你并不后悔，大体上。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_keeper\n# en: Keeper of the Hollow Star\ntitle [3ac5e6] = 空洞之星的守护者\n# en: You graduate with a black star on your palm and a voice in the back of your mind that is not entirely yours. The Hollow Star gave you power; it will want something back. For now, the towers bow a little when you pass, and even the headmistress looks away first.\ntext [8bdf91] = 你毕业时，掌心带着一颗黑色的星，脑海深处还有一个不完全属于你的声音。空洞之星给了你力量；它总有一天会要回些什么。至于现在，你经过时高塔都会微微低头，连院长都会先移开目光。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_freed\n# en: Herald of the Hollow Star\ntitle [5e0dc6] = 空洞之星的使者\n# en: The spring the lake froze, the Star went free, and you held the door. It did not swallow you; it remembered who let it out. The Northern families write to you as one of their own now, and wherever you walk, candles burn a little lower, as if bowing. Lumenhold still hands you a diploma. Nobody at the ceremony looks you in the eye.\ntext [0ea418] = 湖面冰封的那个春天，星挣脱而去，而为它扶住门的是你。它没有吞掉你；它记得是谁放它出来的。如今北境的世家把你当作自家人来写信，你走到哪里，烛火便低下去一点，像在鞠躬。流明堡照样发给你毕业证书。典礼上没有一个人敢直视你的眼睛。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_failed\n# en: The Night Was Lost\ntitle [3fa69b] = 失落之夜\n# en: The spring the lake froze, you went down beneath it, and the door would not close for you. The headmistress closed it in your place and paid for it with years she didn't have to spare. The Wardens don't blame you. Every spring, when the lake thaws, you do, a little, and then you get up and keep the old doors anyway.\ntext [41dc15] = 湖面封冻的那个春天，你下到了湖底，而那扇门不肯为你合上。校长替你关上了它，付出的是她本已所剩无多的岁月。守望者们不怪你。每年春天湖水解冻时，你会怪自己一点点，然后起身，照旧守着那些古老的门。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_hero\n# en: Warden of Lumenhold\ntitle [1eb489] = 流明堡的守望者\n# en: The academy will never fully know what you did beneath the lake. The headmistress does. At graduation she gives you a silver key without a lock and a seat among the Wardens, the quiet few who keep the old doors closed. Kael stands in the crowd and, for once, he is smiling.\ntext [fff990] = 学院永远不会完全知道你在湖底做了什么。院长知道。毕业典礼上，她交给你一把没有锁的银钥匙，以及守望者中的一席之地——那是守护古老之门、默默无闻的少数人。凯尔站在人群里，难得地，他在微笑。\n\n@ending ending_archmage\n# en: The Archmage's Path\ntitle [fea2fb] = 大法师之路\n# en: In the autumn you climb the steps of the Seven Towers in a new grey robe, an apprentice of the Conclave, with seven years of wards and failures ahead and a desk that faces a wall of locked books. Professor Thorne shakes your hand at the gate and says, gruffly, \"Don't waste it.\" You won't.\ntext [a36d7c] = 秋天，你穿着崭新的灰袍登上七塔的台阶，成为七塔议会的学徒。前面是七年的护界与失败，还有一张对着一整面上锁书墙的书桌。索恩教授在门口和你握手，粗声说：“别浪费了。”你不会的。\n\n@ending ending_scholar\n# en: Scholar of the Spire\ntitle [02e34a] = 尖塔学者\n# en: Your thesis is shelved in the Spire Library, in the section students whisper about, and you take the junior fellowship that came with it. You will spend your life among books that bite and secrets that wait — and you could not be happier.\ntext [5472ac] = 你的毕业论文被收进尖塔图书馆，放在学生们低声谈论的那一区；随它而来的初级研究员职位，你也接下了。你将在会咬人的书和静静等待的秘密之间度过一生——而你再快乐不过。\n\n@ending ending_beloved\n# en: Hand in Hand\ntitle [b32cc8] = 携手同行\n# en: Graduation is a blur of robes and cheers, but what you remember is a hand finding yours as the hats go up. Whatever comes next, you won't face it alone. Lumenhold taught you magic; it also taught you this.\ntext [ad5713] = 毕业典礼是长袍与欢呼交织的一片模糊，但你记得的，是帽子抛向空中时，一只手找到了你的手。无论接下来是什么，你都不会独自面对。流明堡教会了你魔法；也教会了你这个。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_lyra\n# en: The Second Flame\ntitle [c90153] = 第二簇火焰\n# en: When the hats go up, {lyra} catches yours before it lands and hands it back without a word. Later, in front of her whole family, she takes your hand and doesn't let go. For House Vaneshade, that is a declaration. For her it is simpler: she has finally found someone she doesn't need to beat.\ntext [1b430a] = 帽子抛向天空时，{lyra} 在你的帽子落地前接住了它，一言不发地递还给你。之后，当着她全家人的面，她握住你的手，再也没有松开。对凡妮希德家族来说，这是一份宣言；对她来说，事情更简单：她终于找到了一个不必去赢的人。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_tobias\n# en: Home, Wherever\ntitle [4a14ae] = 无论何处皆是家\n# en: {tobias} cries through the whole ceremony and blames the pollen. Afterwards he lifts you off your feet in front of everyone, then shows you a drawing of a farmhouse with two chairs on the porch. \"No pressure,\" he says. \"The goats have already voted.\"\ntext [f06768] = {tobias} 哭完了整场典礼，坚称是花粉过敏。之后他当着所有人的面把你抱离地面，又给你看一张画：一座农舍，门廊上摆着两把椅子。“不着急，”他说，“山羊们已经投过票了。”\n\n@ending ending_beloved_seraphine\n# en: Already Foreseen\ntitle [350c84] = 早已预见\n# en: \"I saw this,\" {seraphine} says as the hats go up. \"Years ago. You were holding my hand.\" You are. For once she smiles at the present, as if it has finally caught up with her, and asks whether you'd like to know what happens next. You tell her you'd rather find out together.\ntext [c966c4] = “我见过这一幕，”帽子抛起时 {seraphine} 说，“好几年前。你握着我的手。”你确实握着。她难得地对着此刻微笑，仿佛现在终于追上了她，然后问你想不想知道接下来会发生什么。你说你宁愿和她一起去发现。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_corvin\n# en: No More Jokes\ntitle [ffc267] = 不再说笑\n# en: {corvin} slips a key into your palm during the speeches. It opens nothing at Lumenhold; he has checked. \"It's for wherever we end up,\" he says, and for once he doesn't make a joke of it. Then he does, of course. But his hand stays in yours.\ntext [83042c] = 致辞的时候，{corvin} 把一把钥匙塞进你掌心。它在流明堡打不开任何一扇门——他检查过了。“这是给我们将来落脚的地方用的，”他说，难得没有拿它开玩笑。当然，过了一会儿他还是开了个玩笑。但他的手一直握着你的。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_mir\n# en: Built to Last\ntitle [66dc88] = 经久耐用\n# en: {mir} has rigged the hats to fly back down. Most of them do. In the cheerful chaos they press a small brass ring into your hand; it ticks faintly, like a second heart. \"It'll run for fifty years,\" they say. \"Probably. We should check.\"\ntext [36f87a] = {mir} 给帽子装了机关，让它们自己飞回来。大部分确实飞回来了。在一片欢乐的混乱里，TA 把一枚小小的黄铜戒指按进你手心：它轻轻地滴答作响，像第二颗心脏。“能走五十年，”TA 说，“大概吧。我们得一起验证一下。”\n\n@ending ending_beloved_kael\n# en: Thaw\ntitle [448405] = 解冻\n# en: {kael} doesn't throw his hat. He stands beside you in the crowd, and when the cheering is loudest he says, very quietly, \"Come north with me. Or I stay. Either.\" It is the longest sentence about feelings he has ever spoken. His hand, in yours, is warm.\ntext [edf40c] = {kael} 没有抛帽子。他站在人群里你的身边，在欢呼声最响的时候，非常轻地说：“跟我去北方吧。或者我留下。都行。”这是他说过的最长的一句关于感情的话。他握在你手里的手，是温暖的。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_nessa\n# en: Front Page\ntitle [c50df3] = 头版\n# en: The Ledger's graduation issue runs a small notice at the bottom of page six, set in {nessa}'s own type: two names, one line, no comment. All of Lumenhold reads it before breakfast. {nessa} pretends not to watch them read it, and holds your hand under the table the whole time.\ntext [ed9fec] = 《流明纪事报》的毕业特刊在第六版底部登了一则小启事，用的是 {nessa} 自己的铅字：两个名字，一行字，不加评论。全流明堡的人都在早饭前读到了它。{nessa} 假装没在看大家读它，却在桌子底下一直握着你的手。\n\n@ending ending_alchemist\n# en: Master of the Crucible\ntitle [cd1134] = 坩埚大师\n# en: Your potions are already sold under the counter in three cities. On graduation day you sign the lease on the crooked little shop by the harbour. The sign reads your name, and underneath: NO REFUNDS FOR TRANSFORMATIONS.\ntext [133fe4] = 你的药剂早已在三座城市的柜台底下卖开了。毕业那天，你签下了港口边那间歪歪扭扭的小铺子的租约。招牌上写着你的名字，下面一行：变形概不退款。\n\n@ending ending_celebrity\n# en: The Name on Every Lip\ntitle [264e3e] = 人人口中的名字\n# en: The Lumen Ledger runs a special edition about you. First-years queue for your autograph. You graduate as the most talked-about student in a generation, and the world beyond the gates is already waiting to hear what you'll do next.\ntext [208c47] = 《流明纪事报》为你出了一期特刊。一年级新生排队找你签名。你作为一代人中最受热议的学生毕业，而校门外的世界，早已等着听你接下来要做什么。\n\n@ending ending_merchant\n# en: Gold in the Vaults\ntitle [2332ee] = 金库满盈\n# en: You leave Lumenhold with a diploma, a fortune and an old partner's desk in a harbour shipping house. Magic, you've decided, is good, but compound interest is better. Within five years the house has offices in every port — and an enchanted ledger that never loses a coin.\ntext [a52912] = 你离开流明堡时，带着一张文凭、一笔财富，和港口一家航运商行里一位老合伙人的书桌。你认定魔法固然好，复利更好。不出五年，商行在每座港口都有了办事处——还有一本从不丢一枚硬币的附魔账簿。\n\n@ending ending_shadow\n# en: The Quiet Hand\ntitle [9eb5a5] = 无声之手\n# en: No one quite knows how you graduated with those grades. No one quite remembers the favours you called in. In the autumn you take a desk at the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs, in a building with no sign and no address. You'd found it, of course, long before they told you where it was.\ntext [930717] = 没人说得清你是怎么凭那样的成绩毕业的，也没人记得你讨回了哪些人情。秋天，你在王室隐秘事务署有了一张书桌，那栋楼没有招牌，也没有地址。当然，早在他们告诉你之前，你就已经找到了它。\n\n@ending ending_beloved_friends\n# en: The Ones Who Stayed\ntitle [5337c3] = 留下来的人\n# en: You may not leave as the brightest or the richest, but you leave surrounded. Your friends throw their hats higher than anyone's, and the photograph from that day — all of you squinting into the sun — will hang in every home you ever have.\ntext [8d3051] = 你离开时也许不是最聪明的，也不是最富有的，但你被大家簇拥着。你的朋友们把帽子抛得比谁都高；那天的合影——大家都在阳光里眯着眼——会挂在你今后住过的每一个家里。\n\n@ending ending_barely\n# en: By the Skin of Your Teeth\ntitle [434e96] = 惊险过关\n# en: Your name is called last, after a long pause and some whispering between examiners. But it is called. You graduate. You are a mage of Lumenhold, and nobody ever has to know the details.\ntext [7c93a5] = 在一段长长的停顿和考官之间的窃窃私语之后，你的名字最后一个被念到。但终究被念到了。你毕业了。你是流明堡的法师，没人需要知道其中的细节。\n\n@ending ending_bystander\n# en: Someone Else's Lantern\ntitle [93af7a] = 别人的灯笼\n# en: There was a spring frost, the year you graduated, and a night when the bell under the lake would not stop ringing. You were somewhere else: asleep, or studying, or holding a door shut for the first-years. Someone else went down with the lantern. You never learn their whole story, but every spring, when the lake thaws, you find yourself grateful to them.\ntext [737b03] = 你毕业那年下过一场春霜，还有一个夜晚，湖底的钟响个不停。那时你在别处：在睡觉，在读书，或是替一年级生死死顶住一扇门。提着灯笼下去的是别人。你始终没能知道他们的全部故事，但每年春天湖水解冻时，你总会发现自己在心里默默感谢他们。\n\n@ending ending_graduate\n# en: A Mage of Lumenhold\ntitle [691b6b] = 流明堡的法师\n# en: Robes, hats, a speech that runs long. You walk out of the gates of Lumenhold a qualified mage — not legendary, not disgraced, simply yourself, with four years of stories and a whole world to spend them in.\ntext [7b1e39] = 长袍、学位帽，一场拖得太长的致辞。你作为一名合格的法师走出流明堡的大门——不算传奇，也不丢人，只是你自己，带着四年的故事，和一整个可以挥霍它们的世界。\n\n@ending ending_teacher\n# en: The Teacher's Desk\ntitle [b21115] = 讲台之后\n# en: In the autumn you return to Lumenhold — not on the student barge, but on the faculty one. Your classroom has a window over the lake and a desk with somebody else's initials carved in it. On the first day a terrified first-year asks a question you once asked. You know exactly what to say.\ntext [dce2bc] = 秋天，你回到了流明堡——不是坐学生的驳船，而是坐教职员的那一艘。你的教室有一扇面朝湖水的窗，讲桌上刻着别人的名字缩写。开学第一天，一个吓坏了的一年级生问了一个你当年也问过的问题。你完全知道该怎么回答。\n\n@ending ending_wanderer\n# en: The Road of Wonders\ntitle [055eab] = 奇迹之路\n# en: You join the Travelling Circus of Wonders as its youngest illusionist. For years you cross the kingdoms in a painted wagon, making dragons out of moonlight for children who have never seen magic. Every winter, a letter arrives from Lumenhold. You always write back.\ntext [31dcb2] = 你加入了奇迹巡回马戏团，成了团里最年轻的幻术师。多年来，你坐着彩绘马车穿越一个又一个王国，用月光为从未见过魔法的孩子们变出巨龙。每年冬天，都会有一封来自流明堡的信。你每一封都回。\n\n@ending ending_tamsin_friends\n# en: A Friend Across the Hills\ntitle [c7176e] = 山那边的朋友\n# en: Your yearbook entry has a green seal pressed into the corner, which is not allowed. Tamsin Reed of Thornwick sent it, with a note: \"Four years of rivalry, four hundred letters, one friend. Insufferable to the end.\" Every spring, at a town halfway between, there are biscuits.\ntext [fb273b] = 你的毕业纪念册角落里压着一枚绿色的封蜡，这是不允许的。是荆木学院的塔姆辛·里德寄来的，附了张字条：“四年的较劲，四百封信，一个朋友。到最后都让人受不了。”每年春天，在两校中间的一座小镇上，都有饼干。\n\n@ending ending_dumonts_reunited\n# en: Two Dumonts in the Photograph\ntitle [51d393] = 合影里的两个杜蒙\n# en: In the graduation photograph, at the edge of the crowd, two pale heads lean together: Kael, and the sister who was raised to open the door he refused. Neither of them is smiling at the camera. They are smiling at each other, which is rarer, and which you helped to happen.\ntext [d79ff1] = 毕业合影里，人群边上，两颗浅色的脑袋靠在一起：凯尔，和那个被养大来打开他拒绝打开的那扇门的妹妹。他们谁都没有对着镜头笑。他们是在对彼此笑——这更难得，而你帮了这个忙。\n\n@ending ending_ilse_taken\n# en: A Chair Left Empty\ntitle [c33d8b] = 一把空着的椅子\n# en: The man in grey took Ilse north, and no letter has come. At graduation Kael keeps the seat beside him empty without saying why. You know why. Some evenings, when the lake goes very still, you still look for a girl in white furs on the far shore.\ntext [9df500] = 灰衣人把伊尔泽带去了北境，至今没有一封信。毕业典礼上，凯尔身旁的座位一直空着，他没说为什么。你知道为什么。有些傍晚，湖面静得出奇的时候，你仍会在对岸寻找一个穿白裘的女孩。\n\n@ending ending_thesis_hollowstar\n# en: The Thesis They Locked Away\ntitle [dad732] = 被锁起来的论文\n# en: Your thesis on the founders' seal earned a distinction and then disappeared into the headmistress's tower. Its catalogue card in the Spire Library reads only \"Restricted\", in her handwriting. Three people in the world have read it. One of them still writes to you with questions.\ntext [b15104] = 你关于创始者封印的毕业论文拿到了优等，随后便消失在院长塔里。尖塔图书馆里它的目录卡上只写着\"禁阅\"二字，是她的笔迹。世上读过它的只有三个人，其中一个至今还写信来向你请教。\n\n@ending ending_lumen\n# en: A Lantern for Others\ntitle [217ba6] = 为他人提灯的人\n# en: In the yearbook, under your portrait, someone has written: \"The one you went to when it mattered.\" First-years you barely remember will name their own lanterns after you. Lumenhold has a word for people like that. It is the same word as its name.\ntext [2de9cf] = 年鉴上，你的肖像下面有人写了一句：“要紧的时候，大家去找的那个人。”你几乎记不得的一年级生，日后会用你的名字给自己的灯笼命名。流明堡对这样的人有个说法——就是它自己的名字。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_touched\n# en: The Shadow at Your Heel\ntitle [b9eb7a] = 跟在脚边的影子\n# en: Your portrait in the yearbook is the only one where the candles behind you seem to have gone out. People step aside when you pass, not quite knowing why. The Hollow has learned your name, and you find, more and more often, that you answer to it.\ntext [7dd587] = 整本年鉴里，只有你的肖像身后的烛火像是熄了。人们在你经过时让开路，却说不清为什么。空洞已经知道了你的名字，而你越来越常发现，自己会应声。\n\n@ending ending_hollow_fled\n# en: The Door Left Ajar\ntitle [8b2193] = 虚掩的门\n# en: You went down beneath the frozen lake, saw the door, and ran. Nobody blames you to your face; the headmistress, whose hair went white that night, never mentions it at all. Somewhere under the water the door still stands ajar. Every spring, when the ice goes out, you dream of the dark between the gap and the frame.\ntext [7942e9] = 你下到冰封的湖底，看见了那扇门，然后逃了。没人当面责怪你；院长的头发在那一夜白了，她从此只字不提。在水下某处，那扇门仍然虚掩着。每年春天冰融的时候，你都会梦见门缝与门框之间的那片黑暗。\n\n@ending ending_held_the_line\n# en: The Night Above the Ice\ntitle [f8ba13] = 冰面之上的夜\n# en: The night the lake froze, you didn't go down. You held the doors of the first-year tower against frost and whispers until dawn, and every child in it lived. Someone else went beneath the lake, or no one did; the first-years never cared which. They carved your initials over that door, and nobody has dared paint them out.\ntext [b536e5] = 湖面冰封的那一夜，你没有下去。你守住一年级塔楼的门，抵挡寒霜与低语，一直到天亮，楼里的每个孩子都活了下来。有人下到了湖底，又或者谁也没有；一年级生们从不在意是哪种。他们把你名字的缩写刻在那扇门上方，没人敢把它涂掉。\n\n@ending ending_royal_archivist\n# en: Keeper of the Folios\ntitle [17c7cf] = 对开本的守护人\n# en: You take the cold room at the Royal Library Archives, the one with the folios that object to being opened. Within a year you have catalogued a shelf nobody had dared touch in a century, and the keeper has stopped checking your gloves. On the staff list, under \"Cataloguer\", your name is in copperplate.\ntext [60f61d] = 你接下了皇家图书馆档案馆那间冷房间，里面的对开本都不愿被翻开。不到一年，你就编好了一整架百年来没人敢碰的书，馆长也不再检查你的手套。员工名册的“编目员”一栏下，你的名字用铜版体写着。\n\n@ending ending_guild\n# en: A Bench at the Works\ntitle [e6bbfa] = 工坊里的一张工作台\n# en: You join the Guild of Alchemists' great works as its newest journeyman, in protective clothing you paid for yourself. There are two explosions a month and none of them yours. By the second winter the foreman sends the hardest batches to your bench, and pretends he doesn't.\ntext [60508c] = 你作为最新的熟练工加入了炼金师公会的大工坊，穿着自己花钱买的防护服。那里每月炸两次，没有一次是你。到第二个冬天，工头把最难的那批料都送到你的工作台，还装作没这回事。\n\n@ending ending_observatory\n# en: Charting the Night\ntitle [3efc38] = 绘制夜空\n# en: You go back up to the plateau with the Observatory Expedition, salaried now, frostbite remedies in your pack. The nights are long and very cold and you stay awake through every one of them. Two years on, a star on the new charts carries your initials, in very small letters.\ntext [a7f87f] = 你随观星远征回到高原，这次领着薪水，背包里装着冻疮药。夜晚漫长而寒冷，你每一夜都醒着熬到天亮。两年后，新星图上有一颗星标着你名字的缩写，字写得很小很小。\n\n@ending ending_field_station\n# en: Out on the Moors\ntitle [6c6ba9] = 荒原之上\n# en: You take the keeper's cottage at the Beastcraft Field Station: a lamp, a kettle, and more creatures than any sane person would count. The winters are long, wet and lonely. You count the creatures anyway, every one, and the foal from your summer grows up following you about like a dog.\ntext [651754] = 你住进了驭兽术野外站的看守小屋：一盏灯、一把水壶，还有正常人根本数不过来的生灵。冬天又长又湿又孤单。你还是把它们一只只数过来，而你那个夏天的小马驹长大了，像小狗一样跟着你转。\n\n@ending ending_thornwick\n# en: Across the Hills\ntitle [d009b3] = 翻过山去\n# en: You cross the hills to Thornwick's illusion laboratory, where they think glamour is a science and are mostly right. Lumenhold says something cutting about it, as predicted. Within three years it offers to hire you back at twice the salary, as predicted. You haven't answered yet.\ntext [9bb7da] = 你翻过山，去了荆木学院的幻术实验室，那里的人认为魅影是一门科学，而且大多是对的。流明堡对此说了些尖刻的话，正如所料。不到三年，它又开出双倍的薪水想把你请回来，也正如所料。你还没回信。\n\n@ending ending_librarian\n# en: The Stacks Kept You\ntitle [06374c] = 书库留下了你\n# en: You never quite leave. In the autumn you are back behind the Spire Library's reference desk, a keeper of the stacks now, with a longer ladder and a key to the shelves nobody else may reach. Footnote has not bitten you in months. Students ask you for books, and you find them every time.\ntext [5a506b] = 你从没真正离开。秋天，你又回到尖塔图书馆的咨询台后，如今是书库的看守人，有了一架更长的梯子，和一把别人够不着的书架的钥匙。脚注已经好几个月没咬过你了。学生们来找书，你每次都能找到。\n\n@ending ending_newt\n# en: Behind the Bar\ntitle [7962ab] = 吧台后面\n# en: You stay on at the Gilded Newt, full time, behind the bar and then in charge of it. You remember who ordered the spiced cider, and who's owed a favour, and which of the card games upstairs to look away from. Students who were first-years when you graduated come in to tell you their troubles. You pour, and listen.\ntext [cc89de] = 你留在了镀金蝾螈酒馆，全职，先是站吧台，后来管整个店。你记得谁点了香料苹果酒，谁欠着人情，楼上哪桌牌局该当没看见。你毕业时还是一年级的学生，如今也会来跟你倒苦水。你倒酒，然后听。\n\n@ending ending_pell\n# en: Counting Drops\ntitle [77984e] = 数着药滴\n# en: You stay on at Pell & Daughters, at the counter by day and among the thousand drawers by night, counting drops while Old Pell counts aloud behind you. In ten years, if you're still careful, they'll show you the drawers that never open. You are still careful.\ntext [e4d757] = 你留在了佩尔父女药铺，白天守柜台，夜里在上千个抽屉之间，数着药滴，老佩尔在你身后大声数着。十年后，如果你还够仔细，他们会让你看那些从不打开的抽屉。你一直很仔细。\n\n@ending ending_courier\n# en: Over the Lake in All Weathers\ntitle [08a192] = 风雨无阻飞越湖面\n# en: You keep the satchel with your name on the flap and fly the lake in all weathers, letters and parcels and the occasional live delivery. The broom still pulls left. You don't. On winter nights the first-years watch your lamp crossing the ice and make wishes on it, as if you were a star.\ntext [e98015] = 你留着那个翻盖上绣着你名字的挎包，风雨无阻地飞越湖面，送信件、包裹，偶尔还有活物。扫帚还是往左偏，你不会。冬夜里，一年级生们看着你的灯越过冰面，对它许愿，仿佛你是一颗星。\n\n@ending ending_clerk\n# en: A Desk and a Stamp\ntitle [4ec5f0] = 一张书桌和一枚印章\n# en: You take the clerk's desk from the registrar's list: stamps, ledgers and a window that looks at a wall. It isn't a dream. It pays the rent, and in the evenings you are free, and one day, quietly, you notice that the wall has a crack in it shaped exactly like a door.\ntext [32fd73] = 你接下了教务员清单上那张书记员的桌子：印章、账簿，还有一扇对着墙的窗。这不是梦想。它付得起房租，晚上你是自由的；有一天，你悄悄发现那面墙上有一道裂缝，形状恰好像一扇门。\n\n@ending ending_still_searching\n# en: Still Looking\ntitle [4c93f8] = 还在寻找\n# en: You graduate without a post, and on purpose, or near enough. Your grades will open doors and your name has been heard; the world, you've decided, can make you a better offer in its own time. You spend the autumn writing letters from a rented room above a bakery. Some of them get answers.\ntext [db163a] = 你没有带着职位毕业，而且几乎是故意的。你的成绩会打开门，你的名字也有人听说过；你认定这个世界可以在它自己的时候给你开个更好的价。整个秋天，你在面包房楼上租来的房间里写信。有一些得到了回音。\n\n@ending ending_back_home\n# en: Back in Your Old Room\ntitle [800d40] = 回到你的旧房间\n# en: You go home with a diploma and no letter. Your old room is smaller than you remember, and the bed is too short, and for a while you lie in it until noon and let the days rot quietly by. Then, one morning, you sit up, and write to the registrar about her list. It was still there, as she said it would be.\ntext [3d707b] = 你带着一张文凭、没有一封聘书回了家。旧房间比你记忆里小，床也太短；有一阵子，你躺在上面睡到中午，任日子悄悄烂掉。后来某个早晨，你坐起身，给教务员写信，问起她那张清单。它还在那儿，就像她说过的那样。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/alignment.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/alignment.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event align_cold_candles\n# en: The Candles Lean Away\ntitle [1845e0] = 烛火避让\n# en: {You notice it on the east stair. As you pass, the candle flames bend away from you, all of them, like grass from a wind. A first-year on the landing notices too.|The candles lean away again. You have started to time it. It is getting faster.}\ntext [8f1ab2] = {你是在东楼梯上注意到的。你经过时，烛焰全都朝远离你的方向弯去，像风吹过草地。楼梯平台上的一个一年级生也看见了。|烛火又避开了。你开始给它计时。它越来越快了。}\n# en: A long stone corridor at dusk, every candle flame bending away from one student walking down the middle of it.\nscene [48ce03] = 黄昏时分一条长长的石廊，每一簇烛焰都朝远离走在中间那名学生的方向弯去。\n# en: Let them lean\nopt.left.label [131e40] = 由它们去吧\n# en: You walk on without looking. Behind you the first-year says nothing at all, which is its own kind of saying.\nopt.left.say.1 [93806d] = 你目不斜视地走过去。身后那个一年级生一句话也没说——这本身就是一种表态。\n# en: Stop and relight one by hand\nopt.right.label [87e0d1] = 停下，亲手点亮一盏\n# en: You cup a flame back to life with your palm. It flickers, considers you, and stays. It takes longer than it should.\nopt.right.say.1 [4fde72] = 你用掌心护着一簇火苗让它重新燃起。它摇曳着，打量着你，然后留了下来。这比本该花的时间要久。\n\n@event align_whispered_answer\n# en: A Voice Over Your Shoulder\ntitle [be6019] = 肩后的声音\n# en: Revising late, you get stuck on the same question for the third time. Then the answer is simply there, in a voice that is almost yours, from just behind your left ear. The inkwell has frosted over.\ntext [b8b636] = 深夜复习时，你第三次卡在同一道题上。然后答案就那样出现了——一个几乎是你自己的声音，从你左耳后方传来。墨水瓶上结了一层霜。\n# en: A student bent over a desk by candlelight, revision notes everywhere, and a cold patch of frost forming on the inkwell.\nscene [6d785b] = 烛光下伏案的学生，复习笔记摊得到处都是，墨水瓶上正凝出一片冰霜。\n# en: Write it down\nopt.left.label [f20ae7] = 把它写下来\n# en: It is right. Of course it is right. You write until dawn and never once get stuck, and in the morning you cannot remember learning any of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [d6cb97] = 它是对的。当然是对的。你一直写到天亮，一次也没卡住；可到了早上，你完全不记得自己学过这些。\n# en: Close the book\nopt.right.label [7a6dbd] = 合上书\n# en: You close the book and sit with your hands flat on the cover until the frost on the inkwell melts. The question stays unanswered. It's yours, at least.\nopt.right.say.1 [88c4b2] = 你合上书，双手平放在封面上，一直坐到墨水瓶上的霜化掉。那道题仍然没有答案。但至少它是你自己的。\n\n@event align_maud_way_back\n# en: Weeding, Dearie\ntitle [b9db32] = 来拔草吧，孩子\n# en: Old Maud looks at you for a long moment over her spectacles, the way she looks at a plant that's been kept in the wrong window. \"You've a cold look about you, dearie,\" she says, and holds out a trowel. \"Weeds don't pull themselves. Neither does the rest.\"\ntext [602ce6] = 老莫德从眼镜上方久久地看着你，就像在看一株被放错了窗台的植物。“你身上有股冷气，孩子，”她说着，递过来一把小铲子，“杂草不会自己拔掉。别的事也一样。”\n# en: An old woman in a straw hat kneeling in a vegetable bed, handing a trowel up to a student who hasn't asked for one.\nscene [557087] = 一位戴草帽的老妇人跪在菜畦里，把小铲子递给一个并没有开口要的学生。\n# en: Kneel and start pulling\nopt.left.label [2be082] = 跪下来开始拔\n# en: You weed until your knees ache and your hands smell of earth instead of frost. Maud hums the whole time. Nothing's fixed. Something has started.\nopt.left.say.1 [8ff3f1] = 你拔草拔到膝盖发酸，双手闻起来是泥土味而不是霜味。莫德一直在哼歌。什么都还没好起来。但有些事已经开始了。\n# en: \"I'm fine.\"\nopt.right.label [bdda04] = “我没事。”\n# en: \"Course you are,\" Maud says, and goes back to her beans. The trowel stays in the soil beside her, handle towards you, all afternoon.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bc997] = “当然没事，”莫德说着，回头去照料她的豆子。那把小铲子整个下午都插在她身边的土里，把手朝着你。\n\n@event align_reproach\n# en: \"I Don't Know You Lately\"\ntitle [5baaf9] = “我最近不认识你了”\n# en: {friend} finds you in the cloister and sits without saying hello. \"People talk,\" {they} say{s} at last. \"I keep telling them they're wrong about you. I'd like to keep being right.\"\ntext [8427e0] = {friend}在回廊里找到你，连招呼都没打就坐下了。“大家在议论，”{they}终于开口，“我一直告诉他们，他们看错你了。我希望我能一直是对的。”\n# en: Two friends on a cold bench in the cloister, one looking straight ahead, the other looking at the first.\nscene [1d2371] = 回廊里一张冰冷的长凳上坐着两个朋友，一个直视前方，另一个看着前者。\n# en: \"You are. I'll do better.\"\nopt.left.label [c698dd] = “你是对的。我会改。”\n# en: {friend} nods, slowly, and bumps {their} shoulder against yours. It is an apology and a warning at once.\nopt.left.say.1 [0320a5] = {friend}慢慢点了点头，用肩膀撞了撞你的肩。这既是和好，也是警告。\n# en: \"Mind your own business.\"\nopt.right.label [53fb82] = “少管闲事。”\n# en: {friend} gets up and goes. {They} don't look back. That's what stays with you: {they} always used to look back.\nopt.right.say.1 [d0c677] = {friend}起身走了，没有回头。让你一直记着的正是这一点：{they}以前总会回头的。\n\n@event align_ruthless_nod\n# en: Finally, Someone Who Gets It\ntitle [b51f5a] = 总算有人懂了\n# en: {rel(other) < 10: {other} has always looked straight through you. Today {they} nod{s} as you pass|{other} catches your eye in the corridor and nod{s} again}, slow and approving, the way people nod at someone who has finally learned the rules of a game.\ntext [6a6df3] = {rel(other) < 10: {other}向来对你视而不见。今天你经过时，{they}却朝你点了点头|{other}在走廊里和你对上目光，又一次朝你点了点头}——缓慢而赞许，就像人们朝一个终于学会游戏规则的人点头那样。\n# en: A hard-faced student leaning on a pillar, giving another a slow, approving nod across a busy corridor.\nscene [8d42a3] = 一个神情冷硬的学生倚着柱子，隔着熙攘的走廊朝另一人缓缓点头示意。\n# en: Nod back\nopt.left.label [ae3c6d] = 点头回应\n# en: The nod comes back to you. It feels like being let into a room. You can't tell whether the room is warm.\nopt.left.say.1 [375363] = 点头被回敬了过来。那感觉像是被请进了一间屋子。你说不清那屋子是否温暖。\n# en: Keep walking\nopt.right.label [6ca967] = 继续往前走\n# en: You keep walking. {other} snorts, as if you'd failed a test you didn't know you were sitting.\nopt.right.say.1 [1c408e] = 你继续往前走。{other}嗤了一声，好像你没通过一场你都不知道自己在考的考试。\n\n@event align_northern_parcel\n# en: A Parcel for the North\ntitle [917cb6] = 寄往北境的包裹\n# en: {A hooded courier stops you behind the bakery.|The hooded courier is waiting again, same alley, same black oilcloth.} \"For the boathouse, after dark,\" {they say, and hold out the parcel and a purse.|they say. \"The families remember who is reliable.\"} The oilcloth is cold enough to ache.\ntext [e57cd7] = {一个戴兜帽的信使在面包房后面拦住了你。|那个戴兜帽的信使又在等你了，同一条小巷，同一块黑油布。}“天黑后送到船屋，”{对方说着，递来包裹和一只钱袋。|对方说，“世家记得谁靠得住。”}那块油布冷得发疼。\n# en: A hooded courier in a rain-dark alley behind the village, holding out a small parcel wrapped in black oilcloth and a purse.\nscene [ab51fb] = 村子后面一条被雨淋暗的小巷里，一个戴兜帽的信使递出一个裹着黑油布的小包裹和一只钱袋。\n# en: Carry it\nopt.left.label [f4d75d] = 替他们送去\n# en: You leave it on the boathouse step at midnight and don't look back. In the morning there's frost on the step in the shape of a hand, and the purse is heavier than it looked.\nopt.left.say.1 [f86ac1] = 你在午夜把它放在船屋的台阶上，没有回头。早上，台阶上结着一只手形的霜，而那只钱袋比看上去要沉。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.right.label [96da1a] = 转身离开\n# en: You leave the courier holding it. \"Pity,\" they say, without any pity at all, and the alley is empty when you look back.\nopt.right.say.1 [371c1f] = 你让信使自己拿着它。“可惜，”对方说，语气里没有半点可惜。你回头时，小巷已经空了。\n\n@event align_cruel_advantage\n# en: Something You Could Use\ntitle [e223de] = 可以利用的东西\n# en: {mark} has dropped a letter in the library: something private, something {they} would hate the corridor to hear. You could hand it back. You could also keep it, and {they} would owe you, quietly, for a long time.\ntext [306670] = {mark}在图书馆掉了一封信：私密的事，{they}最不愿意让整条走廊听到的事。你可以把它还回去。你也可以留着它——那样{they}就会悄悄地欠你很久。\n# en: A crumpled letter on a library floor, a student's name on the envelope, another student bending to pick it up.\nscene [8a21c3] = 图书馆地板上一封揉皱的信，信封上写着一个学生的名字，另一个学生正弯腰去捡。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.left.label [87c2f5] = 留着它\n# en: You fold it into your book. When you mention it, very lightly, {mark} goes pale and agrees to everything. It works. It keeps working.\nopt.left.say.1 [18005f] = 你把它夹进书里。当你轻描淡写地提起它时，{mark}脸色发白，什么都答应了。这招管用，而且一直管用。\n# en: Hand it back\nopt.right.label [cb62a0] = 还给对方\n# en: You hand it back unread. {mark} stares at you, then at the letter, then says \"thank you\" as if it costs something. It does, for both of you.\nopt.right.say.1 [414642] = 你没拆开就把信还了回去。{mark}盯着你，又盯着那封信，然后说了声“谢谢”，好像这要付出什么代价。确实要——对你们俩都是。\n\n@event align_frost_in_the_spell\n# en: The Cold That Helps\ntitle [7dac5f] = 有用的寒意\n# en: Practising alone, you notice that your spells come easier when you let the cold in, the cold that has been following you lately. It climbs your sleeve like ivy. The candle beside you leans away.\ntext [a3ee9f] = 独自练习时，你发现只要放那股寒意进来——最近一直跟着你的那股寒意——咒语就来得更容易。它像常春藤一样爬上你的袖子。身旁的蜡烛避开了你。\n# en: A student in an empty practice room, a spell flaring unnaturally bright in one hand, frost creeping up the sleeve to the elbow.\nscene [2b39b1] = 空荡荡的练习室里，一名学生手中的咒语亮得反常，冰霜正沿着袖子爬到手肘。\n# en: Let the cold in\nopt.left.label [13fba8] = 放寒意进来\n# en: The spell flares bright and clean, brighter than anything you've managed in daylight. It takes an hour for the feeling to come back into your fingers. You don't mind as much as you should.\nopt.left.say.1 [ddd5a7] = 咒语亮得干净利落，比你在白天施展过的任何一次都亮。手指过了一个小时才恢复知觉。你没有本该有的那么在意。\n# en: Practise the slow way\nopt.right.label [210853] = 用笨办法练\n# en: You shake the cold out of your hands and do it the slow way, badly, again and again. It is your own bad spell, at least.\nopt.right.say.1 [21c347] = 你甩掉手上的寒意，用笨办法练，练得很差，一遍又一遍。至少那是你自己的蹩脚咒语。\n\n@event align_asked_for_help\n# en: \"You're the One People Ask\"\ntitle [8a706f] = “大家都来找你”\n# en: {~A first-year hovers by your table with a torn essay and a face like a wet weekend.|A first-year you've never met asks if you're the one who helps people. Apparently you are.|Someone has written your name on the common-room board under \"ask them\".} \"Somebody said you'd know what to do,\" {~they say, as if that settles it.|they say, and then wait.|they say, pointing back at the board.}\ntext [775c60] = {~一个一年级生拿着撕破的论文在你桌边徘徊，脸色像个阴雨的周末。|一个你从没见过的一年级生问你是不是那个会帮人的人。看来你是。|有人在公共休息室的告示板上“找他们问”一栏下写了你的名字。}“有人说你会知道该怎么办，”{~对方说，好像这就说明了一切。|对方说完，便等着。|对方说着，回头指了指那块板。}\n# en: A nervous first-year with an armful of torn notes hovering at the door of a common room, looking at one particular older student.\nscene [dd1c6d] = 一个紧张的一年级生抱着一摞撕破的笔记，在公共休息室门口徘徊，眼睛望着某一位高年级学生。\n# en: Sit them down and help\nopt.left.label [d2700f] = 让对方坐下，帮忙\n# en: It takes the whole evening and half your sleep. They leave lighter than they came. So, oddly, do you.\nopt.left.say.1 [c8dac7] = 这花了整整一个晚上，还搭上了你一半的睡眠。对方走的时候比来时轻松了。说来奇怪，你也是。\n# en: Point them to a tutor\nopt.right.label [0b7f21] = 介绍给辅导员\n# en: You give them a name and a smile and get back to your own work. They look disappointed for a moment, then grateful anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [9dd1bd] = 你给了对方一个名字和一个微笑，然后回去做自己的功课。对方失望了片刻，但还是很感激。\n\n@event align_warm_regard\n# en: A Good Word\ntitle [352a57] = 一句好话\n# en: {friend} pushes the last biscuit across to you. \"People talk, you know. About the kind things you do when you think nobody's looking,\" {they} say{s}. \"Don't make a face. It's good. Someone should say so.\"\ntext [6b8c11] = {friend}把最后一块饼干推到你面前。“你知道吗，大家都在说。说你以为没人看见时做的那些好事，”{they}说，“别摆那副表情。那是好事。总得有人说出来。”\n# en: Two students sharing a pot of tea in a sunny window seat, one pushing the last biscuit towards the other.\nscene [7e8fe3] = 两个学生坐在阳光照进来的窗边座位上分一壶茶，其中一人把最后一块饼干推给另一人。\n# en: Accept the biscuit, and the praise\nopt.left.label [80690f] = 收下饼干和夸奖\n# en: You eat the biscuit. {friend} looks very pleased. You buy the next pot.\nopt.left.say.1 [909198] = 你吃掉了饼干。{friend}看起来非常得意。下一壶茶你请。\n# en: Say anyone would have done the same\nopt.right.label [c1a72c] = 说换了谁都会这么做\n# en: \"Anyone would,\" you say. \"Nobody did,\" says {friend}, and lets you change the subject, but the look {they} give{s} you says it has only been postponed.\nopt.right.say.1 [af9984] = “换了谁都会的，”你说。“可没人做，”{friend}说，然后由着你换了话题，但{they}看你的眼神说明，这个话题只是暂时搁置。\n\n@event align_saint_jibe\n# en: \"Saint\"\ntitle [136318] = “圣人”\n# en: {other} blocks the doorway with one arm. \"Here comes the saint,\" {they} say{s}, loud enough for the corridor. \"Tell me, does it get heavy, being better than everyone?\"\ntext [58f004] = {other}用一只胳膊挡住门口。“圣人来了，”{they}说，声音大得整条走廊都能听见，“告诉我，比所有人都好，累不累啊？”\n# en: A smirking student blocking a doorway with one arm, another student standing in front of them with an armful of books.\nscene [1e2bb0] = 一个冷笑着的学生用一只胳膊挡住门口，另一个学生抱着一摞书站在面前。\n# en: Let it go\nopt.left.label [7a5d2f] = 算了\n# en: You wait. Eventually {other} gets bored and drops the arm. Two first-years behind you look at you like you've done something brave. You're not sure you have.\nopt.left.say.1 [657e06] = 你等着。{other}终于觉得没意思，放下了胳膊。你身后两个一年级生看着你，好像你做了什么勇敢的事。你自己可不确定。\n# en: Give as good as you get\nopt.right.label [6cf248] = 以牙还牙\n# en: Your answer is quick and much funnier than theirs. The corridor laughs. {other} goes red. It feels wonderful for about an hour.\nopt.right.say.1 [28c5ab] = 你的回嘴又快又比对方的好笑得多。走廊里一片哄笑，{other}涨红了脸。这感觉美妙了大约一个小时。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/between_hearts.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/between_hearts.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event between_circling\n# en: Circling\ntitle [bfe19a] = 兜圈子\n# en: {~{a} and {b} have sat next to each other at three lectures running, by accident, all three times on purpose.|{a} asks you, very casually, whether {b} is seeing anyone. Ten minutes later {b} asks you the same about {a}.|{a} and {b} keep arriving at the same doorway at the same moment, and then insisting the other goes first, for some time.} Everyone can see it except, apparently, the two of them.\ntext [dc179e] = {~{a}和{b}已经连着三节课坐在一起了，每次都是“碰巧”，每次都是故意的。|{a}很随意地问你，{b}有没有在跟谁交往。十分钟后，{b}也来问你{a}的事。|{a}和{b}总是同时走到同一扇门口，然后互相谦让，让对方先走，让了好半天。}所有人都看得出来，除了他们俩自己。\n# en: Two students reaching for the same door handle at the same moment, both pulling back, a third watching from the stairs with a hand over their eyes.\nscene [438ec9] = 两个学生同时伸手去拉同一个门把手，又同时缩回手；第三个人在楼梯上看着，一只手捂住了眼睛。\n# en: Give them a push\nopt.left.label [0b2807] = 推他们一把\n# en: {~You make sure the only free seat at the next lecture is the one beside {b}, and that {a} knows it. They walk out together.|You tell {a} that {b} asked. You tell {b} that {a} asked. Both of these things are true. That evening they're on the same bench.|You hold the door yourself and wave them both through at once. They collide, apologise, and laugh, and keep laughing all the way down the corridor.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b90cbf] = {~你确保下节课唯一的空位就在{b}旁边，还让{a}知道了这一点。下课时，他们是一起走出去的。|你对{a}说，{b}问起过。你对{b}说，{a}也问起过。这两件事都是真的。当天晚上，他们坐在了同一条长凳上。|你亲自扶着门，挥手让他们俩同时进去。两人撞了个满怀，连声道歉，然后笑了起来，一路笑到走廊尽头。}\n# en: {~Your hint lands like a brick. {a} goes scarlet and sits at the far end of the hall for a week.|{b} asks you, coolly, what exactly you meant by that. You don't have a good answer.|You arrange it all perfectly and both of them see straight through it. They're mortified, and a little annoyed with you, separately.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f3a1b5] = {~你的暗示像块砖头一样砸了下去。{a}的脸涨得通红，接下来一个星期都坐在大厅的另一头。|{b}冷冷地问你，那话到底是什么意思。你答不上来。|你安排得天衣无缝，两人却都一眼看穿了。他们羞得无地自容，还各自对你有点恼火。}\n# en: Leave them to it\nopt.right.label [4dcfa4] = 随他们去\n# en: {~You keep your nose in your book. A month on they're still sitting together by accident.|You say nothing. {a} looks at you as if hoping you would.|You leave them to it. They hold the door for each other for the rest of the term.}\nopt.right.say.1 [26f5e2] = {~你埋头看书。一个月后，他们还在“碰巧”坐在一起。|你什么也没说。{a}看着你，好像盼着你说点什么。|你随他们去了。整个学期，他们都在互相给对方扶门。}\n\n@event between_love_note\n# en: Sealed With Wax\ntitle [6e60f3] = 蜡封的纸条\n# en: {a} presses a folded note into your hand, sealed with candle wax and, by the look of it, a great deal of worry. \"For {b}. Don't read it. Don't let {b.them} open it in front of anyone. Don't say it's from me.\" You point out that {b} will know who it's from. {a} looks stricken.\ntext [6ba3a2] = {a}把一张折好的纸条塞进你手里，用烛蜡封着，看样子还封进了满满的忐忑。“给{b}的。别看。别让{b.them}当着别人的面拆。别说是我写的。”你指出，{b}会知道是谁写的。{a}一脸惊恐。\n# en: A student pressing a folded note sealed with a blob of candle wax into a friend's hand, looking over their shoulder.\nscene [e51b6c] = 一个学生把一张用烛蜡封好的折叠纸条塞进朋友手里，一边回头张望。\n# en: Deliver it as asked\nopt.left.label [710169] = 照吩咐送去\n# en: You give up the evening you'd meant for your essay, find {b} alone in the library, and hand it over. {b} reads it twice, goes pink to the ears, and asks you, with great dignity, to tell {a} \"yes. Probably. Thursday.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [d65912] = 你推掉了原本留给论文的那个晚上，在图书馆找到独自一人的{b}，把纸条递了过去。{b}读了两遍，红到了耳根，然后非常庄重地请你转告{a}：“好。大概吧。星期四。”\n# en: {b} reads it in the library and goes quiet. \"Tell {a} it's very kind,\" {b.they} say{b.s}, which is the worst thing anyone can say. You carry it back as gently as you can.\nopt.left.say.2 [470daa] = {b}在图书馆里读完纸条，沉默了。“告诉{a}，这真的很贴心，”{b.they}说——这是世上最糟的一句话。你尽可能温柔地把它带了回去。\n# en: Help {a} write it better\nopt.right.label [4738eb] = 帮{a}把它写得更好\n# en: You sit up with {a} over four drafts, crossing out the poetry. What's left is three plain lines, the best thing {a} has ever written, and {b} answers them the same evening. You find out how it went at breakfast, from the way they arrive together.\nopt.right.say.1 [07d52e] = 你陪{a}熬夜改了四稿，把诗句统统划掉。剩下的是三行朴素的话，是{a}写过的最好的东西，{b}当天傍晚就回了信。第二天早饭时，看他们俩一起走进来的样子，你就知道结果了。\n# en: By the fourth draft it's a sonnet, and a bad one, and {a} has gone off the whole idea. \"I'll just tell {b.them}. One day.\" The note stays in {their} pocket for the rest of the term. You can see the corner of it.\nopt.right.say.2 [0ddcd4] = 改到第四稿，它成了一首十四行诗，还是首蹩脚的，{a}也彻底没了兴致。“我还是直接告诉{b.them}吧。总有一天。”整个学期，那张纸条都待在{their}口袋里。你看得见露出来的那个角。\n\n@event between_find_out\n# en: Asking for a Friend\ntitle [7efb36] = 替朋友问问\n# en: \"You know {b},\" says {a}, in a library whisper that carries for three shelves. \"Could you find out if {b.they} ever, you know. Thinks about me. At all. Without saying I asked.\" {a} adds, after a moment, \"Especially without saying I asked.\"\ntext [77faab] = “你认识{b}，”{a}用图书馆式的耳语说，声音却传出了三排书架。“你能不能打听一下，{b.they}有没有，你懂的，想过我。哪怕一点点。别说是我问的。”过了一会儿，{a}又补充道：“尤其别说是我问的。”\n# en: A student whispering urgently to a friend in a library alcove, both of them looking across the room at a third student who hasn't noticed.\nscene [e805d5] = 图书馆的壁龛里，一个学生急切地对朋友耳语，两人都望着房间另一头一个毫无察觉的学生。\n# en: Ask {b} straight out\nopt.left.label [b91ef8] = 直接去问{b}\n# en: {b} looks at you for a long moment, then laughs. \"Did {a} put you up to this? Tell {a} the answer's yes, and that {a} can ask me {a.them}self next time.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [a5db32] = {b}看了你好一会儿，然后笑了。“是{a}让你来的吧？告诉{a}，答案是‘是’，下次让{a}自己来问。”\n# en: {b} is kind about it, which makes it harder. \"I like {a}. Just not like that.\" You carry it back, and {a} says \"right, of course\", and talks about the weather for some time.\nopt.left.say.2 [985aee] = {b}回答得很体贴，这反而更难受。“我喜欢{a}。只是不是那种喜欢。”你把话带了回去，{a}说“好吧，当然”，然后聊了很久的天气。\n# en: Find out sideways\nopt.right.label [80b8e5] = 旁敲侧击\n# en: Three casual questions, one about the Midwinter Ball, and you have your answer: {b} goes slightly pink at {a}'s name. You report back. {a} has to sit down.\nopt.right.say.1 [d536ed] = 三个随口的问题，其中一个关于仲冬舞会，你就有了答案：一提到{a}的名字，{b}的脸就微微泛红。你回去汇报。{a}不得不坐下来。\n# en: You are not as subtle as you think. \"Is this about {a}?\" says {b}, and the whole table turns round to look.\nopt.right.say.2 [37ccc7] = 你没有自己以为的那么不动声色。“这是为了{a}吧？”{b}说，整张桌子的人都转过头来看。\n\n@event between_setup_supper\n# en: The Long Supper\ntitle [9550a9] = 漫长的晚饭\n# en: It was your idea that {a} and {b} share the little table by the window at supper. Twenty minutes in they have covered the weather, the soup, and the weather again. {a} keeps looking at you across the hall like someone going under for the third time.\ntext [5dbf84] = 让{a}和{b}在晚饭时共坐窗边那张小桌，是你的主意。二十分钟过去，他们聊了天气、汤，又聊了一遍天气。{a}隔着大厅不停地看你，像个第三次沉下水的人。\n# en: Two students at a small refectory table being very polite over their soup, a third watching anxiously from across the hall.\nscene [580e8e] = 膳堂里一张小桌旁，两个学生对着汤客客气气；第三个人在大厅另一头焦急地望着。\n# en: Go over and rescue it\nopt.left.label [969cf4] = 过去救场\n# en: You sit down for five minutes and tell the story about the goat and the Lumen Ledger. Somewhere in the middle they start finishing it for you. You leave, and neither of them notices.\nopt.left.say.1 [b88dbf] = 你坐过去五分钟，讲了那个关于山羊和《流明纪事报》的故事。讲到一半，他们开始替你往下讲。你起身离开，两人谁都没注意到。\n# en: You sit down and the silence grows to fit three people. {b} remembers an essay. {a} thanks you for trying, in the voice of someone who wishes you hadn't.\nopt.left.say.2 [0bd5e2] = 你一坐下，沉默就扩大到能装下三个人。{b}想起了一篇论文。{a}谢谢你的好意，语气却像是希望你没来过。\n# en: Let it run its course\nopt.right.label [0407c9] = 顺其自然\n# en: You eat your own supper in peace. By pudding they've found something, possibly cheese, to disagree about cheerfully. It goes on past the candles.\nopt.right.say.1 [f68627] = 你安安静静吃完自己的晚饭。到上布丁时，他们已经找到了某样可以开心争论的东西——大概是奶酪。这一聊，聊到了蜡烛燃尽之后。\n# en: You eat your own supper in peace. They part at the door with a handshake, which is the saddest thing you have seen all term.\nopt.right.say.2 [0d65ef] = 你安安静静吃完自己的晚饭。他们在门口握手道别，这是你整个学期见过的最让人心酸的一幕。\n\n@event between_match_made\n# en: Going Well\ntitle [56dfbf] = 进展顺利\n# en: {npc} finds you {~after class|on the stairs|at breakfast} with the look of someone who has news and has decided you're owed it first. It's going well, with the one you nudged {them} towards. Better than well. \"And it was you, so. Thank you. Don't tell anyone it was you.\"\ntext [a4e063] = {npc}{~下课后|在楼梯上|早饭时}找到你，那神情像是有了消息，而且觉得该第一个告诉你。和你撮合的那个人，进展得很顺利。比顺利还要好。“这都是因为你，所以……谢谢你。别告诉别人是你撮合的。”\n# en: A student grinning at a friend on a staircase, trying and failing to look casual about it.\nscene [c51b95] = 楼梯上，一个学生冲朋友咧嘴笑着，想装作若无其事，却没装成。\n# en: Tell just a few people\nopt.left.label [be1878] = 只告诉几个人\n# en: You tell exactly two people. By the end of the week you are the castle's matchmaker, whether you like it or not, and {npc} has forgiven you, mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [67a3f7] = 你只告诉了两个人。到周末时，不管你愿不愿意，你都成了全城堡的红娘；而{npc}也原谅了你，基本上。\n# en: \"It was all you.\"\nopt.right.label [447635] = “全是你自己的功劳。”\n# en: \"It really wasn't,\" says {npc}, but {they} look{s} pleased anyway, and stand{s} a little taller for the rest of the month.\nopt.right.say.1 [e5b035] = “真不是，”{npc}说，可看上去还是很高兴，接下来一个月都挺直了腰板。\n\n@event between_match_soured\n# en: Not Going Well\ntitle [25cbcc] = 进展不顺\n# en: It's over, the thing you nudged {npc} into, and it ended badly at a dance nobody enjoyed. {npc} has decided, on reflection, that some of this is your fault.\ntext [63bff2] = 你撮合{npc}的那段关系结束了，结束在一场谁都不开心的舞会上，结束得很难看。{npc}想了又想，认定这里头有一部分是你的错。\n# en: A student with folded arms on a cold window seat, refusing to look at the friend who has come to sit beside them.\nscene [af7a25] = 一个学生抱着胳膊坐在冰冷的窗台座上，不肯看过来坐在身边的朋友。\n# en: Apologise\nopt.left.label [22e908] = 道歉\n# en: You say sorry for the push, and mean it. {npc} sighs. \"It wasn't the push. It was the part after.\" By the end of the evening {npc} is telling you about it, all of it, twice.\nopt.left.say.1 [2e4653] = 你为那一推道歉，是真心的。{npc}叹了口气。“不怪那一推。怪的是后来那些。”到晚上结束时，{npc}已经把来龙去脉全讲给你听了，讲了两遍。\n# en: \"You were keen too.\"\nopt.right.label [264e0e] = “你当时也很上心。”\n# en: It's true, and {npc} knows it, and that doesn't help at all. {They} leave{s} you with the window seat. It comes back round in a week or two; it isn't quick.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f4b89] = 这是实话，{npc}也知道，可这一点用都没有。{they}把窗台座留给了你。过一两个星期就会缓过来；不会太快。\n\n@event between_matchmaker\n# en: Ask the Matchmaker\ntitle [74e8e8] = 找红娘\n# en: {~{a} corners you after class. There's someone in the year below, and {a} has heard you're the person to ask.|There's a note under your door, from {a}: \"I hear you do introductions.\"|{a} sits down next to you at lunch with a list. It is a list of names. {a} would like your opinion on all of them.}\ntext [4b8ae8] = {~{a}下课后堵住了你。低年级有个人，{a}听说找你准没错。|你门缝底下有张{a}写的纸条：“听说你负责牵线。”|{a}午饭时在你身边坐下，手里拿着一张单子。是一张名单。{a}想听听你对每个人的看法。}\n# en: A student sitting down opposite a friend at a library table with a folded list of names, looking hopeful.\nscene [42a9f4] = 图书馆的桌边，一个学生拿着一张折好的名单在朋友对面坐下，满怀期待。\n# en: Take the case\nopt.left.label [e5e36f] = 接下这桩活\n# en: {~You spend an evening on it, and arrange a chance meeting by the owlery that goes off almost by chance. {a} owes you a cake.|You write back: Thursday, the library, the second window. You'll never know if it worked, but {a} smiles at you in corridors now.|You go through the list, crossing out, with commentary. {a} laughs so much that the list ends up being beside the point.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f7298f] = {~你花了一个晚上安排了一场在猫头鹰棚旁的“偶遇”，结果几乎真成了偶遇。{a}欠你一块蛋糕。|你回信写道：星期四，图书馆，第二扇窗。你永远不会知道成没成，但{a}现在在走廊里会冲你笑。|你逐个过名单，边划边点评。{a}笑得前仰后合，名单本身倒成了次要的。}\n# en: \"I'm retired.\"\nopt.right.label [8247c9] = “我已经退休了。”\n# en: {~\"Retired,\" says {a}, unconvinced. \"From what, one success?\" You keep your evening.|You write \"retired\" under the note and slip it back. You have your evening to yourself, and you spend it, contentedly, on nobody's love life.|{a} folds the list away with great dignity. You eat your lunch in peace, and you are, you notice, a little sorry.}\nopt.right.say.1 [30bd70] = {~“退休？”{a}不信。“就凭一次成功？”你保住了自己的晚上。|你在纸条下面写上“已退休”，塞了回去。你独享了一个晚上，心满意足，没为任何人的感情操心。|{a}庄重地把名单收了起来。你安安静静吃完午饭，却发现自己有点遗憾。}\n\n@event between_likes_your_suitor\n# en: Of All People\ntitle [b05a9a] = 偏偏是他们\n# en: {a} has something to confess, and takes a long time getting there. \"It's {b},\" {a} says at last. \"I think I like {b.them}. Is that stupid?\" It isn't stupid. It is complicated, because {b} has spent the last month looking for you in every room.\ntext [b05882] = {a}有话要坦白，绕了好久才说到正题。“是{b}，”{a}终于说，“我觉得我喜欢{b.them}。这是不是很傻？”这不傻。只是很复杂，因为过去一个月，{b}走进每个房间都在找你。\n# en: Two friends on a window seat, one confiding with a hand over their face, the other going very still.\nscene [508f62] = 窗台座上，一个朋友捂着脸倾诉，另一个人一下子僵住了。\n# en: Tell {a} the truth\nopt.left.label [98ccef] = 告诉{a}实情\n# en: {a} laughs, not very happily. \"Of course. Of course it's you.\" You stay on the window seat until {a} can say \"thank you for telling me\" and mean it, which takes most of the evening.\nopt.left.say.1 [5160e5] = {a}笑了，笑得不怎么开心。“当然。当然是你。”你在窗台座上一直陪着，直到{a}能真心说出“谢谢你告诉我”，这几乎花了一整晚。\n# en: Step back for {a}\nopt.right.label [a73b0e] = 为{a}退一步\n# en: You're a little cooler with {b} after that, and {b} notices, and drifts. {a} starts turning up wherever {b} is. You find you don't mind as much as you thought you would.\nopt.right.say.1 [2893f5] = 那之后你对{b}冷淡了一些，{b}察觉到了，渐渐走远。{a}开始出现在{b}出现的每个地方。你发现自己并没有想象中那么在意。\n\n@event between_partner_meets_ex\n# en: The Same Fire\ntitle [68103a] = 同一炉火\n# en: The common room has a fire, a kettle and, tonight, both {a} and {b}. Your partner and your ex. They have never properly met. By the look on {b}'s face, that is about to change.\ntext [f8877b] = 公共休息室里有炉火，有水壶，今晚还有{a}和{b}。你的恋人，和你的前任。他们从没正式见过面。看{b}的表情，这马上就要改变了。\n# en: A common room in the evening, two people on opposite sides of the fire turning at the same moment to look at a third coming through the door.\nscene [15e272] = 傍晚的公共休息室，炉火两侧的两个人同时转头，看向刚进门的第三个人。\n# en: Introduce them properly\nopt.left.label [bcff05] = 正式介绍他们认识\n# en: You make the introductions as if they were the most ordinary thing in the world. By the second cup they've found common ground: you, and a shared low opinion of your singing.\nopt.left.say.1 [53f2e1] = 你介绍得仿佛这是世上最平常的事。到第二杯茶时，他们找到了共同话题：你，以及对你唱歌水平同样不高的评价。\n# en: It is very polite and extremely short. Later, walking back, {a} asks one careful question, and then another, and you answer them both.\nopt.left.say.2 [3c080a] = 场面非常客气，也极其短暂。后来走回去的路上，{a}问了一个小心翼翼的问题，接着又问了一个，你都答了。\n# en: Steer {a} away first\nopt.right.label [dbbf5b] = 先把{a}引开\n# en: You invent an urgent errand and {a} lets you, which is generous. {b} watches you both go. You feel it on the back of your neck all the way up the stairs.\nopt.right.say.1 [ffa76e] = 你编了个急事，{a}也由着你，这很大度。{b}看着你们俩离开。一路上楼，你都觉得后颈发烫。\n\n@event between_admirer_gift\n# en: In Front of Everyone\ntitle [fa9f47] = 众目睽睽\n# en: {~{b} turns up with a little bunch of something from the greenhouses, for you, in front of {a}.|{b} has made you a bookmark, painstakingly, and gives it to you at lunch, while {a} is sitting right there.|{b} has brought you a pastry back from the village. One pastry. {a}'s eyebrows say a great deal.}\ntext [923ede] = {~{b}拿着一小束温室里摘的什么花来送你，当着{a}的面。|{b}费了好大功夫给你做了个书签，午饭时送给你，而{a}就坐在旁边。|{b}从山下小镇给你带回一个酥饼。就一个。{a}的眉毛说明了一切。}\n# en: A student holding out a small wrapped gift across a refectory table while the person beside its recipient raises an eyebrow.\nscene [76cd8d] = 膳堂的桌边，一个学生隔着桌子递出一件包好的小礼物，收礼人身旁的人挑起了眉毛。\n# en: Thank {b} warmly\nopt.left.label [7f524b] = 热情地谢谢{b}\n# en: {~You thank {b} and put the flowers in water. {a} is very interested in the window for the rest of the meal.|You use the bookmark that afternoon. {a} doesn't say anything about it, twice.|You eat the pastry. It is excellent. {a} watches you eat it, and the silence afterwards is long.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9e5822] = {~你谢过{b}，把花插进了水里。这顿饭剩下的时间，{a}对窗外格外感兴趣。|你当天下午就用上了那个书签。{a}什么都没说，说了两次。|你把酥饼吃了。非常好吃。{a}看着你吃完，之后的沉默很长。}\n# en: Hand it back, kindly\nopt.right.label [9d258d] = 委婉地还回去\n# en: {~\"They're lovely, but I can't.\" {b} takes them back, pink, and leaves quickly. {a} squeezes your hand under the table.|You give the bookmark back. {b} nods, too fast. {a} doesn't gloat, which you love {them} a little more for.|You push the pastry gently back across the table. {b} eats it {b.them}self, defiantly. {a} hides a smile.}\nopt.right.say.1 [514795] = {~“很漂亮，可我不能收。”{b}红着脸收回花，匆匆走了。{a}在桌下捏了捏你的手。|你把书签还了回去。{b}点头点得太快。{a}没有得意，你因此更爱{them}一点。|你把酥饼轻轻推回桌子那头。{b}赌气似的自己吃了。{a}忍住了笑。}\n\n@event between_suitors_compare\n# en: Comparing Notes\ntitle [96ff6a] = 交流心得\n# en: You come round the corner and find {a} and {b}, your two most persistent admirers, sitting together on the stairs with their heads together. Comparing notes. About you. \"Oh,\" says {b}. \"We were just...\" \"Comparing,\" says {a}.\ntext [db068b] = 你一转过拐角，就看见{a}和{b}——你最执着的两位追求者——并排坐在楼梯上，头凑在一起。在交流心得。关于你的。“哦，”{b}说，“我们只是……”“在交流，”{a}说。\n# en: Two students sitting side by side on a staircase, heads together over a list, both looking up guiltily at a third coming round the corner.\nscene [01760d] = 两个学生并排坐在楼梯上，头凑在一张单子上，一起心虚地抬头看向从拐角走来的第三个人。\n# en: Sit down and join in\nopt.left.label [6f9b9f] = 坐下一起聊\n# en: You sit on the step below and dispute every point. By the end all three of you are laughing, they've agreed you're impossible, and nobody has to choose anything today.\nopt.left.say.1 [702999] = 你在下一级台阶坐下，对每一条都提出异议。到最后，三个人都笑了，他们一致认定你不可理喻，今天谁也不用做选择。\n# en: You try to be funny about it and it comes out defensive. They exchange a look that you are not part of. That's new.\nopt.left.say.2 [5383ec] = 你想说得幽默些，出口却像在辩解。他们交换了一个你不在其中的眼神。这倒是头一回。\n# en: Walk on with dignity\nopt.right.label [a570cd] = 体面地走开\n# en: You step over them and carry on down the stairs. Behind you, the comparing resumes, louder, and someone says \"the hair, though\", and they both laugh.\nopt.right.say.1 [4636ed] = 你从他们身边跨过去，继续下楼。身后的交流又开始了，声音更大，有人说“不过那头发啊”，两人都笑了。\n\n@event between_secret_about\n# en: Not Mine to Tell\ntitle [8af199] = 轮不到我说\n# en: \"You can't tell anyone,\" says {a}, and then tells you anyway: {b} failed last term's practical, and has been resitting it in secret every week, and is terrified of anyone finding out. {a} looks relieved to have put it down. Now you're holding it.\ntext [2bbb16] = “你不能告诉任何人，”{a}说，然后还是告诉了你：{b}上学期的实践课没过，每个星期都在偷偷补考，生怕被人知道。{a}看上去像是终于卸下了担子。现在担子在你手里了。\n# en: A student leaning close to whisper to a friend in a corridor, a third student visible at the far end, unaware.\nscene [103bbe] = 走廊里，一个学生凑近朋友耳语，走廊尽头隐约可见第三个毫不知情的学生。\n# en: Tell {a} it wasn't {a.theirs} to tell\nopt.left.label [c25eb2] = 告诉{a}这轮不到{a.them}说\n# en: {a} flushes. \"I only told you.\" You point out that this is what everyone says. {a} doesn't speak to you until supper, and is quieter about other people afterwards.\nopt.left.say.1 [375a5d] = {a}脸红了。“我只告诉了你。”你指出，大家都是这么说的。直到晚饭前{a}都没跟你说话，之后谈论别人时也安静了许多。\n# en: Keep it, and help {b} quietly\nopt.right.label [d0b5f2] = 守住秘密，悄悄帮{b}\n# en: You start turning up where {b} revises, with your notes, as if by accident. {b} doesn't ask why. The practical gets easier, for both of you.\nopt.right.say.1 [9402d8] = 你开始“碰巧”带着笔记出现在{b}复习的地方。{b}没问为什么。那门实践课变得容易了些，对你们俩都是。\n\n@event between_did_they_tell\n# en: Somebody Told You\ntitle [da8bcd] = 有人告诉你了\n# en: {npc} sits down across from you with the face of someone who has rehearsed this. \"Somebody told you. About the practical. Didn't they?\"\ntext [5831f9] = {npc}在你对面坐下，一副排练过的样子。“有人告诉你了。实践课的事。对不对？”\n# en: A student sitting down across a library table from a friend, hands flat on the wood, clearly having rehearsed something.\nscene [56a191] = 图书馆的桌边，一个学生在朋友对面坐下，双手平放在桌上，显然事先想好了要说什么。\n# en: Tell the truth\nopt.left.label [d8bf00] = 说实话\n# en: {npc} nods slowly. \"I thought so. Thank you for not pretending.\" {npc} doesn't ask who. You suspect {they} know{s}.\nopt.left.say.1 [3c9d0b] = {npc}慢慢点了点头。“我就知道。谢谢你没有装傻。”{npc}没问是谁。你猜{they}心里有数。\n# en: \"Nobody told me anything.\"\nopt.right.label [9880ab] = “没人跟我说过什么。”\n# en: {npc} lets out a breath held for a month. \"Good. Good. Sorry. I've been seeing it in everyone's face.\" You change the subject, and {they} let{s} you.\nopt.right.say.1 [b8cf0b] = {npc}长出了一口憋了一个月的气。“那就好。好。抱歉。我总觉得在每个人脸上都看到了。”你转开了话题，{they}也由着你。\n# en: {npc} doesn't believe a word, and now there are two people {they} can't quite trust. {They} pick{s} up {their} books and leave{s}.\nopt.right.say.2 [f0dcd0] = {npc}一个字都不信，现在{they}又多了一个不太能信任的人。{they}收起书走了。\n\n@event between_rumour_from_you\n# en: How It Grew\ntitle [5ca387] = 越传越大\n# en: {~You mentioned, once, in passing, that {a} had lent {b} a scarf. By supper they're engaged.|You said {a} and {b} were revising together. By morning it's a secret romance, with details.|You told one person that {a} walked {b} back from the library. The story has since acquired a moonlit lake.} Both of them have heard it. Both of them know where it started.\ntext [371e56] = {~你曾随口提过一句，{a}借了条围巾给{b}。到晚饭时，他们已经订婚了。|你说过{a}和{b}在一起复习。到第二天早上，就成了一段秘密恋情，还有细节。|你只跟一个人说过，{a}从图书馆送{b}回来。这个故事后来添上了一片月光下的湖。}两人都听说了。两人都知道是从哪儿传出来的。\n# en: A cluster of students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, two others at opposite ends of it pointedly not looking at each other.\nscene [f3bbe6] = 走廊里一群学生捂着嘴窃窃私语，走廊两头的两个人刻意不看对方。\n# en: Set the record straight\nopt.left.label [07105d] = 澄清事实\n# en: {~You spend a day explaining that a scarf is only a scarf. Half the castle is disappointed in you. {a} and {b} are not.|You correct it at every table, which makes it sound truer. {b} thanks you anyway.|You say it was a library, and a corridor, and no lake. People believe you, sadly. {a} buys you a cider.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0cac56] = {~你花了一整天解释，一条围巾只是一条围巾。半个城堡都对你很失望。{a}和{b}没有。|你在每张桌边纠正，结果越纠正越像真的。{b}还是谢了你。|你说只有图书馆和走廊，没有湖。大家信了，有点遗憾。{a}请你喝了杯苹果酒。}\n# en: Let it run\nopt.right.label [cf7c46] = 由它去传\n# en: {~It's quite funny, the first week. {a} doesn't find it funny at all. {b} starts wearing the scarf.|The details get better. {a} stops sitting with you. {b} seems, oddly, not to mind.|By the end of the month nobody can remember whether it was true, including, it seems, {a} and {b}.}\nopt.right.say.1 [856604] = {~头一个星期还挺好笑。{a}一点都不觉得好笑。{b}开始围那条围巾了。|细节越编越精彩。{a}不再跟你坐一起了。{b}倒是出奇地不在意。|到月底，谁也记不清那是不是真的了，似乎连{a}和{b}自己也记不清了。}\n\n@event between_overheard\n# en: Behind Their Back\ntitle [b15768] = 背后议论\n# en: {~Behind the library shelves, {a} is telling a little group exactly what {a.they} think{a.s} of {b}. It isn't kind, and it isn't true.|On the stairs, {a} is doing an impression of {b}. It's a good impression. People are laughing.|{a} is holding court at the refectory table: {b}'s spells, {b}'s family, {b}'s hair.} {b} is a friend of yours, and isn't here.\ntext [5a28a8] = {~图书馆书架后面，{a}正对着一小群人细数{a.they}对{b}的看法。既不厚道，也不属实。|楼梯上，{a}在模仿{b}。模仿得很像。大家都在笑。|{a}在膳堂的桌边高谈阔论：{b}的咒语，{b}的家人，{b}的头发。}{b}是你的朋友，而{b.they}不在场。\n# en: A student holding court at a refectory table, mid-impression, the others laughing, a fourth student behind them stopping still with a tray.\nscene [b14eb0] = 膳堂的桌边，一个学生正模仿别人模仿到一半，旁人哄笑，身后第四个端着餐盘的学生停住了脚步。\n# en: Speak up\nopt.left.label [a3103e] = 站出来说话\n# en: {~\"That isn't true, and you know it.\" The group goes quiet. {a} looks at you as if you've changed sides in a war you didn't know about. Word gets back to {b}, eventually.|\"Do me next,\" you say. Nobody laughs. {a} stops. It gets back to {b} by the end of the week.|You put your tray down at the table and say {b}'s name kindly, once, and leave it there. {a} finds someone else to talk about.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f623ea] = {~“这不是真的，你自己也知道。”那群人安静了下来。{a}看着你，仿佛你在一场你都不知道的战争里倒戈了。这话最终还是传到了{b}耳朵里。|“下一个学我吧，”你说。没人笑。{a}停了下来。不到周末，这事就传到了{b}那里。|你把餐盘放到那张桌上，和气地提了一次{b}的名字，就此打住。{a}换了个人议论。}\n# en: Say nothing\nopt.right.label [d1151d] = 一言不发\n# en: {~You keep walking. The laughing follows you down the aisle. You sit with {b} at supper and don't mention it, and it sits between you.|You laugh, a little, because everyone is. You think about it for the rest of the day.|You eat your lunch somewhere else. It doesn't help as much as you'd like.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bb50d6] = {~你继续往前走。笑声一路跟着你。晚饭时你和{b}坐在一起，没提这事，它就横在你们之间。|你也笑了一点，因为大家都在笑。这件事你想了一整天。|你去别处吃了午饭。这并没有你希望的那么管用。}\n\n@event between_betting_book\n# en: The Book on Them\ntitle [27e044] = 押在他们身上\n# en: There's a book running in the common room, and the odds are on {a} and {b}: together by the end of term, three to one. Somebody you don't much like is taking the money. Everyone is very interested in what you know.\ntext [d6b253] = 公共休息室里有人开了赌盘，押的是{a}和{b}：学期结束前在一起，三赔一。收钱的是个你不太喜欢的人。大家都很想知道你知道些什么。\n# en: A grubby ledger open on a common-room table, students crowding round to write names and coins in its columns.\nscene [d9c63d] = 公共休息室的桌上摊着一本脏兮兮的账簿，学生们围过去，在栏目里写下名字和钱数。\n# en: Put a crown on them\nopt.left.label [d0c53f] = 押一克朗\n# en: You put your crown on \"together\". Three weeks later they are, and you collect. You never tell either of them, and you feel slightly odd every time they hold hands.\nopt.left.say.1 [7b903f] = 你把一克朗押在“在一起”上。三个星期后，他们真的在一起了，你收了钱。你从没告诉过他们俩，每次看见他们牵手，你都觉得有点怪。\n# en: You put your crown on \"together\", and they aren't, quite, and the bookmaker smiles at you every morning for a month.\nopt.left.say.2 [5c41c9] = 你把一克朗押在“在一起”上，可他们终究没能走到那一步，接下来一个月，那个庄家每天早上都冲你笑。\n# en: Shut the book down\nopt.right.label [447c4f] = 把赌盘掀了\n# en: You take the ledger and drop it in the fire, which is not a popular move. The bookmaker sulks. {a} and {b} never find out about it, or if they do they never say.\nopt.right.say.1 [8bba8e] = 你拿起账簿扔进了炉火，这一举动很不受欢迎。庄家生起了闷气。{a}和{b}始终不知道这件事，就算知道，也从没提过。\n\n@event between_couple_bubble\n# en: Party of Two\ntitle [f6b4e2] = 两人世界\n# en: {~{a} and {b} have become inseparable, and now they finish each other's sentences, and you finish your supper.|You've been the third chair at {a} and {b}'s table for a month now. Tonight they forgot to pull it out.|{a} and {b} save you a place on the window seat. When you get there, it's a seat for two and a cushion for you.}\ntext [e219dd] = {~{a}和{b}变得形影不离，如今他们能接上彼此的话，而你只能接着吃完自己的晚饭。|你在{a}和{b}的桌边当了一个月的第三把椅子。今晚，他们忘了给你拉开它。|{a}和{b}在窗边座位上给你留了个位置。你到了才发现，是两人份的座位，外加给你的一个坐垫。}\n# en: Two students sharing one cushion on a window seat, a third sitting slightly apart with a sandwich.\nscene [20d15f] = 窗边座位上，两个学生共用一个坐垫，第三个人拿着三明治坐得稍远。\n# en: Say something\nopt.left.label [fac1f4] = 说出来\n# en: {~\"I'm still here, you know.\" They look at you, horrified, and then they laugh, and the next evening there are three cups.|It comes out more plaintive than you meant. {b} hugs you. {a} saves your chair for a week, pointedly.|You mention the cushion. {a} apologises for some time. It's awkward, and then it's better.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1259a2] = {~“我还在这儿呢。”他们惊恐地看着你，然后笑了，第二天晚上桌上就有了三个杯子。|说出口比你想的要可怜巴巴。{b}抱了抱你。{a}特意给你留了一个星期的椅子。|你提起了那个坐垫。{a}道歉道了好半天。场面先是尴尬，然后就好了。}\n# en: Give them room\nopt.right.label [645df9] = 给他们留点空间\n# en: {~You eat early and read late. You've never got so much done. You miss them.|You leave them to it. Your essays improve. Your evenings don't.|You find other company for a while. They come back round, eventually, the way people do.}\nopt.right.say.1 [843292] = {~你早早吃饭，晚晚看书。你从没这么高效过。你想念他们。|你随他们去了。你的论文进步了。你的晚上没有。|你找了别的伙伴待了一阵子。他们最终会回来的，人总是这样。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/between_known_2.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/between_known_2.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event seraphine_between_warning\n# en: Not the North Stair\ntitle [ee2ddf] = 别走北楼梯\n# en: Seraphine stops {o} on the landing, puts a hand on {o.their} sleeve, and says, quite exactly, \"Not the north stair tomorrow. The south one. Before the bell.\" Then she drifts off. {o} laughs, and then stops laughing, and asks you, \"She's joking. Isn't she?\"\ntext [505ee1] = 瑟拉芬在楼梯平台上拦住{o}，一只手搭在{o.their}袖子上，一字一句地说：“明天别走北楼梯。走南边那条。打铃之前。”说完就飘走了。{o}笑了，然后笑不出来了，转头问你：“她在开玩笑。对吧？”\n# en: A dreamy student touching another's sleeve on a staircase landing, saying something quiet, while the other laughs.\nscene [471cc2] = 楼梯平台上，一个神情恍惚的学生轻碰另一人的袖子，低声说了句什么，对方却笑了起来。\n# en: \"Take the south stair.\"\nopt.left.label [ed1019] = “走南楼梯吧。”\n# en: {o} takes the south stair, feeling silly. Nothing happens on the north stair, as far as anyone can tell. But that evening {o} sits beside Seraphine at supper, and she finishes {o.their} sentence for {o.them}, and {o} doesn't mind.\nopt.left.say.1 [f20281] = {o}走了南楼梯，觉得自己有点傻。北楼梯上什么事也没发生，至少没人知道发生了什么。可那天晚饭时，{o}坐到了瑟拉芬旁边，她替{o.them}说完了一句话，而{o}并不介意。\n# en: \"Nobody knows what she means.\"\nopt.right.label [10537c] = “谁也不知道她什么意思。”\n# en: {o} takes the north stair out of principle, and meets a prefect with a notebook coming the other way, and a demerit for running. Seraphine looks faintly apologetic, a day early.\nopt.right.say.1 [c2e81f] = {o}出于原则走了北楼梯，迎面撞上一个拿着小本子的级长，因为在楼梯上奔跑被记了一笔。瑟拉芬看上去有点抱歉——提前了一天。\n\n@event seraphine_between_sentences\n# en: The Other Half\ntitle [11388f] = 另一半\n# en: {~{o} has started a sentence three times today, and Seraphine has finished it three times, correctly, half a second early.|Seraphine answered a question {o} hadn't asked yet. {o} asked it anyway, to be sure. It was the right answer.|{o} told a joke at supper, and Seraphine laughed at the punchline before {o} got to it.} \"It's not that I mind,\" {o} tells you. \"It's that I can't tell if I'm still talking to her, or to next week.\"\ntext [5c551a] = {~{o}今天三次开口说话，瑟拉芬三次替{o.them}说完了，都提前半秒，而且都对。|瑟拉芬回答了一个{o}还没问的问题。{o}为了确认，还是问了一遍。答案是对的。|{o}晚饭时讲了个笑话，瑟拉芬在{o}讲到笑点之前就笑了。}“我不是介意，”{o}对你说，“我只是分不清自己是在跟她说话，还是在跟下个星期说话。”\n# en: Two students side by side on a window seat, one talking, the other gazing out of the window and quietly saying the end of the sentence a moment before it comes.\nscene [b2089e] = 窗台座上并排坐着两个学生，一个在说话，另一个望着窗外，在话说完之前就轻轻说出了结尾。\n# en: Tell {o} it's how she listens\nopt.left.label [defcd6] = 告诉{o}这是她倾听的方式\n# en: {~\"She only does it to people she likes,\" you say, which you aren't sure is true, and then find is. {o} starts leaving gaps in sentences, on purpose. Seraphine fills them, delighted.|\"It means she's already enjoying the conversation,\" you say. {o} thinks about that for a whole afternoon and decides it's a compliment.|\"Let her,\" you say. \"She's usually right about the ending.\" {o} lets her. They get on, after that, in a way that skips the middle.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4f7d51] = {~“她只对喜欢的人这样，”你说，其实你也不确定这是真的，后来发现确实如此。{o}开始故意在句子里留空。瑟拉芬高高兴兴地把空填上。|“这说明她已经在享受这场对话了，”你说。{o}想了整整一个下午，认定这是在夸人。|“随她去吧，”你说，“结尾她通常都说得对。”{o}随她去了。从那以后，她们相处得很好，好得直接跳过了中间。}\n# en: Ask Seraphine to stay in today\nopt.right.label [d12ef9] = 请瑟拉芬今天留在“今天”\n# en: {~You tell Seraphine, gently, that {o} likes to say {o.their} own endings. \"Oh,\" she says, \"{o} will. On Thursday.\" She says nothing else all evening, and looks pleased about it.|Seraphine listens to you, nods, and then, very carefully, lets {o} finish every sentence for a week. It's somehow more unnerving.|\"Today,\" Seraphine repeats, as if it were a foreign word. \"I'll try.\" She manages most of a lunch.}\nopt.right.say.1 [087f5d] = {~你温和地告诉瑟拉芬，{o}喜欢自己把话说完。“哦，”她说，“{o}会的。星期四。”那天晚上她什么都没再说，看上去还挺高兴。|瑟拉芬听你说完，点点头，然后小心翼翼地让{o}把每句话都说完，坚持了一个星期。不知怎么，这反而更让人发毛。|“今天，”瑟拉芬重复了一遍，像在念一个外语词。“我试试。”她坚持了大半顿午饭。}\n\n@event tobias_between_sayings\n# en: Where I'm From\ntitle [42fa31] = 我老家那边\n# en: Tobias said, at supper, that the soup \"wanted more rain on it\", which is what his mother says. {o} has been saying it all week, in a farmer's voice, with a pretend pitchfork. Tobias laughs from the belly every time. He's the only one who doesn't look like he's laughing.\ntext [2582de] = 托比亚斯晚饭时说，这汤“还欠一场雨”，这是他母亲的口头禅。{o}这一个星期都在学这句话，用农夫的腔调，还假装扛着一把干草叉。托比亚斯每次都笑得前仰后合。只有他看上去不像是在笑。\n# en: A refectory table, a big broad-shouldered student laughing along a little too hard while a classmate across from him does an impression with a pretend pitchfork.\nscene [79cf53] = 膳堂的桌边，一个宽肩膀的大个子学生笑得有点过头，对面的同学正假装拿着干草叉学人说话。\n# en: Tell {o} to drop it\nopt.left.label [5e932e] = 让{o}别再学了\n# en: \"That's his mum you're doing,\" you say. {o} goes red and stops. Tobias tells you, later, that he didn't mind, really, and apologises for you having to say anything, and then thanks you three times.\nopt.left.say.1 [05bd1f] = “你学的是他妈妈，”你说。{o}脸一红，不学了。后来托比亚斯告诉你，他其实不介意，还为你不得不开口道了歉，然后谢了你三遍。\n# en: Ask Tobias for the rest of the sayings\nopt.right.label [47937c] = 请托比亚斯多讲几句家乡话\n# en: You ask Tobias what else his mother says. He tells you, for an hour, with goats. By the end, {o} is laughing with him instead of at him, and asking what \"a frost in the cream\" means.\nopt.right.say.1 [561e49] = 你问托比亚斯，他母亲还爱说些什么。他讲了一个小时，里面有好多山羊。到最后，{o}是在跟他一起笑，而不是笑他了，还问“奶油里结了霜”是什么意思。\n# en: Tobias tries, and {o} does the voice again, and the table laughs again. Tobias laughs too. He eats the rest of his supper very fast and goes to the greenhouses.\nopt.right.say.2 [9ffe56] = 托比亚斯刚开口，{o}又学起了那个腔调，满桌人又笑了。托比亚斯也笑了。他飞快地吃完剩下的晚饭，去了温室。\n\n@event tobias_between_supper\n# en: Proper Food\ntitle [a04021] = 像样的饭菜\n# en: {~{o} has been homesick all week and not saying so. Tobias noticed. He has borrowed a stove and is cooking, in the corner of the greenhouses, \"proper food\".|Tobias heard {o} say the refectory's bread tastes of nothing. There is now a loaf proving under a cloche in the greenhouse, and an invitation.|{o} missed a letter from home this month. Tobias is making a stew \"big enough for a barn\", and wants you to come and help eat it.} He asks if you'll come.\ntext [f99f28] = {~{o}想家想了一个星期，却一直没说。托比亚斯注意到了。他借了个炉子，正在温室的角落里做“像样的饭菜”。|托比亚斯听{o}说膳堂的面包吃起来什么味道都没有。现在温室的玻璃罩下正发着一团面，还附带一份邀请。|{o}这个月没收到家里的信。托比亚斯在炖一锅“够一个谷仓吃的”炖菜，想请你去帮忙吃。}他问你来不来。\n# en: A greenhouse corner at night with a little stove, a big student stirring a pot, a homesick classmate wrapped in a blanket holding a bowl.\nscene [4c2fbe] = 夜里温室的一角，一个小炉子，大个子学生在搅锅，一个想家的同学裹着毯子捧着碗。\n# en: Bring something to the table\nopt.left.label [ea2f5f] = 带点东西过去\n# en: {~You bring cheese from the village. Tobias treats it like a holy relic. {o} eats three bowls and tells you about home, all of it, and it's a good evening.|You bring a jar of honey. Tobias's bread, it turns out, was waiting for exactly that. {o} laughs for the first time all week.|You bring the cider. Tobias tells the story of the goat that ate a hat. {o} tells one back. It's past midnight when you leave the greenhouse.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fe199e] = {~你从山下小镇带了块奶酪。托比亚斯把它当圣物一样供着。{o}吃了三碗，把家里的事全讲给你听，那是个美好的夜晚。|你带了一罐蜂蜜。原来托比亚斯的面包等的就是它。{o}一个星期来第一次笑了。|你带了苹果酒。托比亚斯讲了那只吃掉帽子的山羊的故事。{o}也讲了一个。你离开温室时已经过了午夜。}\n# en: Leave them to it\nopt.right.label [4dcfa4] = 让他们俩自己吃\n# en: {~You plead an essay. In the morning {o} looks better than you've seen {o.them} in weeks, and there's a heel of bread wrapped in a cloth on your desk.|You stay in. You can smell the stew from the dormitory. Tobias saves you a bowl, and apologises that it's cold.|You leave them to it. Somebody plays a fiddle badly in the greenhouses until late, and somebody else sings along.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8950c1] = {~你推说要写论文。第二天早上，{o}的气色是你几个星期来见过最好的，你桌上还多了一块用布包着的面包头。|你待在宿舍。炖菜的香味一路飘到宿舍。托比亚斯给你留了一碗，还为它凉了道歉。|你让他们自己吃。温室里有人把小提琴拉得很糟，一直拉到很晚，还有人跟着唱。}\n\n@event tobias_between_sulking\n# en: The Ferns Are Sulking\ntitle [703d3f] = 蕨类在生闷气\n# en: {o} knocked over a tray of seedlings in the greenhouse last week, and swore at them. Now every plant on {o}'s bench leans away from {o.them}, and the ferns curl up when {o} comes in. Tobias, very gravely, says they're sulking. \"They'll come round. But somebody's got to say sorry, and it'd better be {o}.\"\ntext [2b202b] = {o}上个星期在温室里打翻了一盘幼苗，还冲它们骂了句粗话。现在{o}工作台上的每一株植物都背着{o.them}长，{o}一进门，蕨类就卷起叶子。托比亚斯一本正经地说，它们在生闷气。“它们会消气的。可总得有人道个歉，最好是{o}。”\n# en: A greenhouse bench of ferns all leaning pointedly away from one embarrassed student, a big student crouching to talk to them.\nscene [b0271f] = 温室的工作台上，一排蕨类全都刻意背着一个尴尬的学生倾斜，一个大个子学生蹲下来跟它们说话。\n# en: Help {o} say sorry to the ferns\nopt.left.label [beda0b] = 帮{o}向蕨类道歉\n# en: {o} kneels by the bench, mortified, while you hold the watering can and Tobias coaches in a whisper: \"Lower. They like it lower.\" By the end of the lesson one fern has uncurled. Tobias claps {o} on the back hard enough to rattle the glass.\nopt.left.say.1 [afbd9d] = {o}跪在工作台边，羞得无地自容；你拿着水壶，托比亚斯在一旁低声指导：“再低一点。它们喜欢低一点。”到下课时，有一株蕨舒展开了。托比亚斯拍了拍{o}的背，力气大得震响了玻璃。\n# en: Tell {o} it's only plants\nopt.right.label [6f8ffa] = 告诉{o}那只是植物\n# en: {o} laughs, relieved, and goes back to the bench. The ferns go on leaning away from {o} for the rest of term, and {o} quietly moves benches in the spring. Tobias doesn't say anything, which, for Tobias, is saying a great deal.\nopt.right.say.1 [d4a8f0] = {o}松了口气，笑着回到工作台。整个学期，那些蕨类都背着{o}长，到了春天，{o}悄悄换了张工作台。托比亚斯什么都没说——对托比亚斯来说，这已经说明了很多。\n\n@event maud_between_soup\n# en: Carry This, Dearie\ntitle [1569ee] = 把这个端去，孩子\n# en: Old Maud catches you on the path with a covered pot. \"{o}'s abed with a chill, dearie, and too proud to say so. Take this up. Hot, mind. And sit with {o.them} till it's eaten. That's the important bit, the sitting. Soup's just the excuse.\"\ntext [1e24f2] = 老莫德端着一口盖着的锅在小路上拦住你。“{o}着了凉躺下了，孩子，又好面子不肯说。把这个端上去。趁热，当心。再坐在{o.them}旁边看着吃完。要紧的是坐着陪的那一会儿。汤只是个由头。”\n# en: An old woman in a garden apron handing a covered pot to a student at her cottage door, the lamp lit behind her.\nscene [b0cf6e] = 小屋门口，一个系着园艺围裙的老妇人把一口盖着的锅递给一个学生，身后亮着灯。\n# en: Carry it, and sit with {o}\nopt.left.label [0ac78f] = 端上去，陪{o}坐坐\n# en: You carry the pot up three flights without spilling, and sit on the end of {o}'s bed while {o} eats it. {o} talks, eventually, about being ill and far from home. The soup does what Maud's soup does. So, you think, does the sitting.\nopt.left.say.1 [9226b4] = 你端着锅爬了三层楼，一滴都没洒，然后坐在{o}床尾看{o}吃。{o}终于开口了，说起生病、说起离家很远。汤起了莫德的汤该起的作用。你想，陪着坐的那一会儿也是。\n# en: Leave it at {o}'s door\nopt.right.label [122a93] = 放在{o}门口\n# en: You knock, leave the pot on the step, and hurry off to your essay. The pot comes back to Maud empty. Maud looks at you over her spectacles. \"Soup's only half of it, dearie,\" she says, and you understand what she meant a fortnight later.\nopt.right.say.1 [9b2d39] = 你敲了门，把锅放在台阶上，就匆匆赶回去写论文。锅空着回到了莫德那儿。莫德从眼镜上方看着你。“汤只是一半，孩子，”她说。两个星期后你才明白她的意思。\n\n@event maud_between_beans\n# en: The Bean Rows\ntitle [d28c57] = 豆架\n# en: Someone cut through Maud's garden in the dark and took out a whole row of runner beans. You know it was {o}, chasing a runaway hat. {o} knows you know. Maud is standing at the end of the row with her hands on her hips, saying, to nobody in particular, \"Only, I'd like to know, that's all.\"\ntext [9ea25d] = 有人摸黑抄近路穿过莫德的菜园，踩倒了一整排红花菜豆。你知道是{o}，为了追一顶被风吹跑的帽子。{o}也知道你知道。莫德双手叉腰站在那排豆架尽头，不冲着谁地说：“我就是想知道，没别的。”\n# en: A trampled row of runner beans beside a cottage garden, a goat looking innocent, an old woman with her hands on her hips.\nscene [39f42c] = 小屋菜园边，一排红花菜豆被踩得东倒西歪，一只山羊一脸无辜，一个老妇人双手叉腰。\n# en: Get {o} to own up\nopt.left.label [07b848] = 劝{o}去认错\n# en: {o} goes and owns up, scarlet. Maud listens, nods, and hands {o} a trowel. By evening the beans are re-staked and {o} has been given soup, a lecture on beans, and a standing invitation to Sunday weeding.\nopt.left.say.1 [0eef9b] = {o}满脸通红地去认了错。莫德听完，点点头，递给{o}一把小铲子。到傍晚，豆架重新立好了，{o}得到了一碗汤、一堂关于豆子的课，还有一份星期天来除草的长期邀请。\n# en: {o} won't. \"She'll tell the headmistress.\" She wouldn't, you know she wouldn't, but {o} won't hear it. Maud re-stakes the beans herself, slowly, and you feel every pole.\nopt.left.say.2 [2ea4ce] = {o}不肯去。“她会告诉院长的。”她不会的，你知道她不会，可{o}听不进去。莫德自己慢慢重新立好了豆架，每一根杆子你都看在眼里。\n# en: Re-stake them yourself\nopt.right.label [b23090] = 自己去把豆架立好\n# en: You come back at dawn and re-stake the row before Maud's up. She finds you at it. \"Funny,\" she says, \"how a person's bean rows mend themselves.\" She doesn't ask. She gives you a jar of honey, and another one \"for your friend\".\nopt.right.say.1 [e3fafb] = 你天一亮就回去，赶在莫德起床前把那排豆架立好了。她撞见你正在干活。“真怪，”她说，“这豆架怎么自己就好了。”她没有问。她给了你一罐蜂蜜，又给了一罐，“给你朋友的”。\n\n@event maud_between_advice\n# en: Two Weeks Later\ntitle [df9b72] = 两个星期后\n# en: {o} went to Old Maud about a friend who'd stopped speaking to {o.them}, and came back with this: \"Water the roots, dearie, not the leaves.\" {o} has been turning it over for days. \"Is it about plants? It's not about plants. Is it?\"\ntext [5f295d] = 有个朋友不跟{o.them}说话了，{o}去找老莫德，带回来这么一句：“浇根，孩子，别浇叶子。”{o}琢磨了好几天。“是说植物吗？不是说植物吧。是吗？”\n# en: Two students on a garden bench puzzling over something, an old woman with a watering can walking away from them, smiling to herself.\nscene [e68e99] = 花园长凳上两个学生对着什么苦思冥想，一个拎着水壶的老妇人从他们身边走开，自顾自地笑着。\n# en: Puzzle it out together\nopt.left.label [3d38bc] = 一起琢磨\n# en: You sit up half the night on it. Somewhere around midnight you both get it at once: stop arguing about the thing, and go and see the person. {o} does, the next day. It works. Maud, told, says \"did I say that, dearie?\", which you choose to take as modesty.\nopt.left.say.1 [17977e] = 你们熬了大半夜。快到午夜时，你们俩同时想通了：别再为那件事争了，去见那个人。第二天{o}去了。成了。告诉莫德时，她说：“我说过这话吗，孩子？”你们决定把这当作谦虚。\n# en: \"Give it two weeks.\"\nopt.right.label [6c4246] = “过两个星期就懂了。”\n# en: \"Maud's advice takes two weeks,\" you say. {o} rolls {o.their} eyes. A fortnight later {o} stops you in a corridor, shining: \"The roots! It was the roots!\" You still don't know what {o} worked out. You decide not to ask.\nopt.right.say.1 [3faa77] = “莫德的建议要过两个星期才见效，”你说。{o}翻了个白眼。两个星期后，{o}在走廊里拦住你，两眼放光：“根！说的是根！”你至今不知道{o}想明白了什么。你决定不问了。\n\n@event thorne_between_hard\n# en: Hm.\ntitle [52fec0] = 嗯。\n# en: Thorne has handed {o}'s essay back for the third week running, more red ink than black. \"Hm,\" he said, which is all he said. {o} is close to tears in the corridor. \"He hates me. He doesn't do this to anyone else. Will you talk to him? He listens to you.\"\ntext [027122] = 索恩教授已经连续三个星期把{o}的论文退回来，红墨水比黑墨水还多。“嗯，”他说，就只说了这一个字。{o}在走廊里快哭了。“他讨厌我。他从不这样对别人。你能去跟他说说吗？他听你的。”\n# en: A severe old professor handing back an essay covered in red ink, a student staring at it, a third student at the next desk.\nscene [3101c1] = 一位严厉的老教授递回一篇布满红墨水的论文，学生盯着它，邻桌坐着第三个学生。\n# en: Talk to Thorne\nopt.left.label [a7a48c] = 去找索恩教授谈\n# en: Thorne hears you out, unmoved, and then says, \"The student in question is the only one in that class worth correcting.\" He doesn't change a word of the red ink. But the next essay comes back with one line at the bottom, in black: \"Better.\" {o} frames it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f64255] = 索恩教授面无表情地听你说完，然后说：“你说的那位学生，是那个班上唯一值得纠正的人。”他一个红字都没改。可下一篇论文发回来时，末尾用黑墨水写了一行：“有进步。”{o}把它裱了起来。\n# en: \"Flattery on another's behalf is still flattery,\" says Thorne, and turns back to his marking. You leave, having made it, possibly, slightly worse.\nopt.left.say.2 [c798e1] = “替别人说好话，也还是说好话，”索恩教授说着，转回去批改作业。你离开了，大概把事情弄得更糟了一点。\n# en: Read the red ink with {o}\nopt.right.label [854dee] = 和{o}一起读那些红字\n# en: You go through it line by line with {o}. Half of it, you point out, is Thorne arguing with {o}'s ideas, which he only does with ideas he thinks are worth it. {o} looks at the essay differently after that. So, for a moment, do you.\nopt.right.say.1 [d4be4d] = 你和{o}逐行看下去。你指出，其中一半是索恩教授在跟{o}的观点争论，而他只跟他认为值得的观点争论。从那以后，{o}看那篇论文的眼光不一样了。有那么一会儿，你也是。\n\n@event thorne_between_flattery\n# en: Allergic\ntitle [0c41c4] = 过敏\n# en: {o} has a plan for the next practical mark: to be nice to Professor Thorne. Very nice. There's a speech about his \"legendary career\", and a rose. {o} is heading for his office now. You know, as everyone who has met Thorne knows, that he is allergic to flattery, possibly literally.\ntext [5fc9e0] = {o}为下一次实践课的分数想了个办法：对索恩教授好一点。非常好。准备了一段关于他“传奇生涯”的发言，还有一朵玫瑰。{o}正往他的办公室走去。你知道，所有见过索恩教授的人都知道，他对奉承过敏，也许字面意义上也是。\n# en: A student bowing slightly too low to a severe old professor in a corridor, holding out a single rose, while a friend behind them winces.\nscene [c3525d] = 走廊里，一个学生向一位严厉的老教授鞠躬鞠得太低，手里举着一朵玫瑰，身后的朋友不忍直视。\n# en: Head {o} off\nopt.left.label [dec8c3] = 把{o}拦下来\n# en: You catch {o} on the stair and explain, quickly, about Thorne. {o} goes pale and gives you the rose. You keep it on your desk. It's a perfectly nice rose; it's just in no danger of reaching Thorne.\nopt.left.say.1 [32f468] = 你在楼梯上截住{o}，飞快地讲了讲索恩教授的事。{o}脸色发白，把玫瑰给了你。你把它放在书桌上。那是朵很好的玫瑰；只是再也没有落到索恩教授手里的危险了。\n# en: Let {o} find out\nopt.right.label [548892] = 让{o}自己去发现\n# en: You hear about it at supper, from four people. Thorne listened to the speech in silence, took the rose, and set {o} an additional essay on \"the uses of sincerity\". {o} doesn't speak to you for a day, and then laughs about it for a month.\nopt.right.say.1 [7b7cdd] = 晚饭时，你从四个人那里听说了这件事。索恩教授一言不发地听完发言，收下玫瑰，然后给{o}加布置了一篇论文，题目是《论真诚的用处》。{o}一天没理你，然后笑了一个月。\n\n@event isolde_between_remembers\n# en: The Second Week\ntitle [658fcc] = 第二个星期\n# en: The Headmistress stops beside {o} in the Great Hall, pleasant and unhurried. \"{o}. How is the potion that turned the stairs blue? The second week, I believe. Nobody's ever managed that shade since.\" She moves on. {o} wants the floor to swallow {o.them}. \"She remembers. She remembers everything.\"\ntext [f61cc1] = 院长在大礼堂里停在{o}身边，和气而从容。“{o}。那瓶把楼梯染成蓝色的药剂怎么样了？我记得是第二个星期。之后再没人调出过那种蓝。”她走开了。{o}恨不得地板把{o.them}吞下去。“她记得。她什么都记得。”\n# en: A tall pale headmistress pausing in the Great Hall beside a student, smiling pleasantly, while the student turns slowly crimson.\nscene [416f54] = 大礼堂里，一位高挑苍白的院长在一个学生身边停下，和气地微笑，那学生的脸慢慢涨得通红。\n# en: \"She remembered your name.\"\nopt.left.label [d7696b] = “她记得你的名字。”\n# en: \"She remembered your name, and the shade of blue,\" you say. \"That's a compliment, from her.\" {o} doesn't believe you. But {o} walks a little taller past the Headmistress's tower for the rest of the term.\nopt.left.say.1 [d2e943] = “她记得你的名字，还记得那种蓝，”你说，“从她嘴里说出来，这是夸奖。”{o}不信。可这个学期剩下的日子里，{o}经过院长塔时腰板都挺得直了些。\n# en: Confess your own first week\nopt.right.label [94693b] = 坦白你自己的第一周\n# en: You confess your own first-week disaster, and that you live in fear of the day she mentions it. {o} laughs until {o} has to sit down. You feel it was worth it, nearly.\nopt.right.say.1 [1771ce] = 你坦白了自己第一周闯的祸，还有你一直害怕她哪天提起这件事。{o}笑个不停，笑到{o}只好坐下来。你觉得，这差不多值了。\n\n@event isolde_between_complaint\n# en: An Appointment\ntitle [783267] = 一次约见\n# en: {o} has written a complaint about a lecturer who marks unfairly, and has an appointment with the Headmistress to deliver it in person. {o} has rewritten it eleven times. \"Come with me,\" {o} says. \"Just stand there. I can't do it alone.\"\ntext [a00cd5] = {o}写了一封投诉信，投诉一位打分不公的讲师，还约好了要亲手交给院长。{o}已经改写了十一遍。“陪我去吧，”{o}说，“就站在那儿。我一个人做不到。”\n# en: The door of a tall tower office ajar, a student hesitating on the threshold with a folded letter, a friend at their shoulder.\nscene [13454e] = 高塔办公室的门半开着，一个学生攥着一封折好的信在门口犹豫，朋友站在身旁。\n# en: Go in with {o}\nopt.left.label [a2fd4f] = 陪{o}进去\n# en: The Headmistress reads the letter slowly, asks {o} two questions, each placed like a chess piece, and then says, \"Thank you. That was not easy.\" The marks are reviewed by the end of the month. {o} doesn't stop shaking until supper.\nopt.left.say.1 [275264] = 院长慢慢读完信，问了{o}两个问题，每个都像落下的一枚棋子，然后说：“谢谢你。这并不容易。”到月底，那些分数被重新审核了。{o}一直抖到晚饭时才停下。\n# en: The Headmistress listens pleasantly and says she will \"look into it\". {o} can't tell, afterwards, whether that means yes or no. Neither can you. Nothing seems to change for a month, and then, quietly, something does.\nopt.left.say.2 [0399ee] = 院长和气地听着，说她会“调查一下”。事后，{o}分不清那是答应还是拒绝。你也分不清。一个月里似乎什么都没变，然后，悄无声息地，有些事变了。\n# en: Help {o} rewrite it once more\nopt.right.label [1cfb8e] = 帮{o}再改一遍\n# en: You sit with {o} and cut the letter down to six lines, all of them true. {o} goes in alone, in the end, and comes out ten minutes later, astonished. \"She read every line. Then she asked about the third one.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [b418fa] = 你和{o}坐下来，把信删到六行，每一行都是实话。最后{o}是一个人进去的，十分钟后出来，一脸惊讶。“她每一行都读了。然后问起了第三行。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/between_known.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/between_known.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lyra_between_drills\n# en: Again\ntitle [fb56ef] = 再来\n# en: {~{o} asked Lyra to help {o.them} with {o.their} duelling. Lyra said yes. That was a week ago. {o} now flinches at the word \"again\".|{o} limps into supper with singed cuffs and the look of someone who has been told \"again\" two hundred times.|You find {o} asleep on the library steps, wand still in hand. \"Lyra,\" {o} says, without opening {o.their} eyes, as if that explains it.} \"She won't stop,\" {o} tells you. \"And I can't ask her to. You know what she's like.\"\ntext [87210a] = {~{o}请莱拉帮{o.them}练决斗。莱拉答应了。那是一个星期前的事。如今{o}一听到“再来”两个字就发抖。|{o}一瘸一拐地来吃晚饭，袖口烧焦了，一副被说了两百遍“再来”的样子。|你发现{o}在图书馆台阶上睡着了，手里还握着魔杖。“莱拉，”{o}眼睛都没睁就说，好像这就解释了一切。}“她不肯停，”{o}对你说，“我又没法开口求她。你知道她是什么样的人。”\n# en: An empty duelling floor at dusk, one student lying flat on their back, another standing over them with a wand, saying something short.\nscene [505963] = 黄昏时空荡的决斗场上，一个学生仰面躺倒，另一个拿着魔杖站在旁边，简短地说了句什么。\n# en: Talk to Lyra\nopt.left.label [1dfb56] = 去跟莱拉谈谈\n# en: {~\"Too hard?\" Lyra looks genuinely baffled. \"I'm not being hard. I'm being thorough.\" The next morning there's a flask of tea by the duelling ring, and nobody says who brought it.|Lyra listens with her arms folded. \"Fine,\" she says. That afternoon she lets {o} rest for a full minute, and then, without being asked, shows {o.them} how to fall.|\"{o} said that?\" Lyra is silent for a while. \"{o} should have said so.\" She doesn't apologise. She does turn up with new cuffs.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1d36a3] = {~“太狠了？”莱拉一脸真心的困惑。“我不是狠。我是严谨。”第二天早上，决斗场边多了一壶茶，没人说是谁带来的。|莱拉抱着胳膊听你说完。“行，”她说。那天下午，她让{o}整整休息了一分钟，然后没人要求，就教了{o.them}怎么摔倒。|“{o}这么说的？”莱拉沉默了一会儿。“{o}应该直说。”她没有道歉。但她带来了新袖口。}\n# en: {~\"If {o} wanted it gentle, {o} should have asked someone gentle.\" Lyra turns back to the ring. \"Again.\"|Lyra hears you out and says, \"No.\" Then, after a moment: \"Tell {o} the footwork is improving.\" You're not sure that helps.|\"Do I tell you how to study?\" says Lyra, who tells you how to study weekly. The drills continue.}\nopt.left.say.2 [915b14] = {~“{o}要是想要温柔的，{o}就该去找温柔的人。”莱拉转回场上。“再来。”|莱拉听完说：“不。”过了一会儿又说：“告诉{o}，步法有进步。”你不确定这算不算帮上忙。|“我教过你怎么学习吗？”每周都在教你怎么学习的莱拉说。训练照旧。}\n# en: Tell {o} to stick with it\nopt.right.label [153231] = 劝{o}坚持下去\n# en: {~{o} sticks with it. A month later {o} disarms a third-year in practice and looks so astonished that Lyra almost smiles.|{o} groans, and goes back. Lyra doesn't say \"well done\". She says \"acceptable\", which, you explain to {o}, is the same thing.|{o} goes back to the ring with the face of someone going to the gallows. It does get easier. It never gets easy.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e1b469] = {~{o}坚持了下来。一个月后，{o}在练习中缴了一个三年级生的械，惊讶得连莱拉都差点笑了。|{o}哀叹一声，回去了。莱拉没说“干得好”。她说“还行”——你向{o}解释，这是同一个意思。|{o}一脸上刑场的表情回到场上。确实变得容易了些。但从来没变得容易。}\n\n@event lyra_between_furniture\n# en: You Sit Next to Her\ntitle [ea12e1] = 你就坐她旁边\n# en: {o} has sat next to Lyra in lectures since the first week. Today Lyra turns to you, across {o}, and asks, \"Who is this?\" {o} is right there. {o} has lent her ink. Twice.\ntext [d109e2] = 从第一周起，{o}上课就坐在莱拉旁边。今天，莱拉隔着{o}转过头问你：“这是谁？”{o}就在那儿。{o}借过她墨水。两次。\n# en: A lecture bench, a proud student looking straight ahead, the classmate beside her waving a hand slowly in front of her face.\nscene [1488dc] = 讲堂的长凳上，一个骄傲的学生目视前方，旁边的同学正慢慢在她眼前挥手。\n# en: Introduce them properly\nopt.left.label [bcff05] = 好好介绍他们认识\n# en: \"This is {o}, who sits next to you, and lends you ink.\" Lyra looks at {o} as if {o} had just appeared out of the air, then nods, very formally. The next morning, there's a new bottle of ink on {o}'s desk. Expensive ink.\nopt.left.say.1 [7d5caa] = “这是{o}，坐你旁边，借你墨水的人。”莱拉看着{o}，像是{o}刚从空气里冒出来，然后郑重地点了点头。第二天早上，{o}桌上多了一瓶新墨水。很贵的墨水。\n# en: Laugh it off\nopt.right.label [29c02a] = 笑着带过\n# en: You laugh, and Lyra doesn't see the joke, and {o} laughs too, a beat late. {o} moves two seats along the next week. Lyra doesn't notice that either.\nopt.right.say.1 [9ab81e] = 你笑了，莱拉没明白笑点，{o}也跟着笑了，慢了半拍。下个星期，{o}挪远了两个座位。莱拉同样没注意到。\n\n@event lyra_between_beaten\n# en: How Did They Do It\ntitle [5af8bc] = 怎么做到的\n# en: {o} beat Lyra in practice today. Only a practice bout, only a point, and the whole hall saw. Lyra finds you afterwards. She doesn't say please; she has never said please first. \"You were watching. How did {o} do it?\"\ntext [57a714] = {o}今天在练习中赢了莱拉。只是一场练习赛，只差一分，可全场都看见了。莱拉事后找到你。她没说“请”；她从来不先说“请”。“你看着呢。{o}是怎么做到的？”\n# en: A practice ring after a bout, a winner being clapped on the back, a proud student standing alone at the edge, very still.\nscene [c11f7d] = 练习场上一局刚结束，赢家正被人拍着后背，一个骄傲的学生独自站在场边，一动不动。\n# en: Tell her what you saw\nopt.left.label [68bb2b] = 告诉她你看到了什么\n# en: You walk her through it: {o}'s feint, the half-step back, the moment she overreached. Lyra listens without blinking. \"Again,\" she says to herself, and goes to the ring, and doesn't come to supper.\nopt.left.say.1 [649c24] = 你给她一步步讲：{o}的佯攻，半步后撤，她冒进的那一刻。莱拉听着，眼都不眨。“再来，”她对自己说，转身去了练习场，晚饭也没来吃。\n# en: \"Ask {o}.\"\nopt.right.label [15b1e3] = “去问{o}。”\n# en: Lyra looks at you as if you've suggested she eat soup with a fork. Then she goes and asks. You watch {o}, startled, show her the feint, and Lyra, stiffly, shows {o} something back.\nopt.right.say.1 [b7874f] = 莱拉看着你，像你建议她用叉子喝汤。然后她真的去问了。你看着吃了一惊的{o}给她演示那个佯攻，而莱拉僵硬地也教了{o}一招作为回礼。\n# en: \"I don't ask,\" Lyra says, and leaves. She doesn't come to supper, and the next morning the practice dummies have scorch marks that weren't there before.\nopt.right.say.2 [f6cb57] = “我不问人，”莱拉说着走了。她没来吃晚饭，第二天早上，练习用的假人身上多了几道之前没有的焦痕。\n\n@event kael_between_kindness\n# en: He Keeps Leaving\ntitle [765f0c] = 他总是走开\n# en: {~{o} has been trying to make friends with Kael for weeks. Every time {o} sits down, Kael finds somewhere else to be.|{o} saved Kael a seat at supper. Kael looked at it as if it might be a trap, and ate standing up.|{o} gave Kael a scarf. Kael said \"why?\" and meant it, and {o} didn't know what to say.} \"What am I doing wrong?\" {o} asks you.\ntext [14dbc5] = {~{o}想跟凯尔交朋友已经好几个星期了。每次{o}一坐下，凯尔就找到别的地方要去。|{o}晚饭时给凯尔留了个位子。凯尔看着那个位子，像在看一个陷阱，然后站着吃完了饭。|{o}送了凯尔一条围巾。凯尔问“为什么？”，是真心在问，{o}不知道该怎么回答。}“我哪里做错了？”{o}问你。\n# en: A student holding out a mug of something hot towards a boy with frost on his sleeves, who is already half turned away.\nscene [a72fb8] = 一个学生捧着一杯热饮递向袖子上结着霜的男孩，男孩已经转过去一半了。\n# en: Tell {o} to give him time\nopt.left.label [c0c058] = 告诉{o}给他点时间\n# en: {~\"He isn't used to it,\" you say. \"Keep sitting down.\" {o} does. In the third week, Kael stays.|\"Kindness makes him nervous,\" you say. {o} looks at you oddly, and then keeps being kind, quietly, until it doesn't.|\"Don't ask why,\" you say. \"Just do it again.\" The scarf turns up round Kael's neck a month later. Nobody mentions it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1b9435] = {~“他不习惯这个，”你说，“继续坐下就好。”{o}照做了。第三个星期，凯尔留了下来。|“善意会让他紧张，”你说。{o}奇怪地看了你一眼，然后继续默默地对他好，直到他不再紧张。|“别问为什么，”你说，“再来一次就好。”一个月后，那条围巾出现在凯尔的脖子上。没人提起。}\n# en: Bring them both to the boathouse\nopt.right.label [38e6fe] = 带他们俩去船屋\n# en: {~You bring {o} down to the boathouse, where Kael goes in the evenings. Nobody talks much. {o} learns that this is how Kael says yes.|You take the three of you down to the water. Kael shows {o} how to skip a stone, without a word. That is apparently a friendship now.|At the boathouse, Kael is all elbows and silence for an hour. Then he tells {o} the name for snow that falls upward. It is the most you've ever heard him say.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a91e71] = {~你带{o}去了船屋，凯尔傍晚常在那儿。没人说多少话。{o}明白了，这就是凯尔说“好”的方式。|你们三个去了水边。凯尔一言不发地教{o}打水漂。这显然就算是交上朋友了。|在船屋里，凯尔手足无措、沉默了一个小时。然后他告诉{o}，那种往上飘的雪叫什么名字。这是你听他说过最多的话。}\n\n@event kael_between_steps_in\n# en: Frost on the Floor\ntitle [cd99fb] = 地上的霜\n# en: Some older students have {o} pinned against the corridor wall over something about a borrowed book, and it is turning ugly. Before you can move, the air goes cold enough to sting. Kael is standing at the end of the corridor, very still, and frost is feathering up the walls around him.\ntext [1207bf] = 几个高年级生为了一本借来的书，把{o}堵在走廊墙边，事情越闹越难看。你还没来得及动，空气就冷得刺人。凯尔站在走廊尽头，一动不动，霜花正沿着他四周的墙壁向上蔓延。\n# en: A corridor with frost feathering up the walls around one still student, a group of others backing away from a cornered classmate.\nscene [3bc59d] = 走廊里，霜花正沿着墙壁围着一个一动不动的学生向上蔓延，一群人从被逼到墙角的同学身边往后退。\n# en: Back him up\nopt.left.label [e6a7f9] = 站到他身边\n# en: You step up beside him. The older students look at the walls, at Kael's face, at you, and decide the book wasn't worth it. Kael walks {o} to the infirmary without a word. Later, he asks you, very seriously, whether he did it right.\nopt.left.say.1 [8b0fd0] = 你走到他身旁。那几个高年级生看看墙壁，看看凯尔的脸，又看看你，决定那本书不值得。凯尔一言不发地陪{o}去了医务室。后来，他非常认真地问你，他这么做对不对。\n# en: Fetch a prefect instead\nopt.right.label [cd8621] = 去找级长\n# en: You run for a prefect. By the time you're back the corridor is empty and the walls are dripping, and Kael is gone. {o} tells you he didn't do anything; he just stood there until they left. He doesn't speak of it. He avoids you, a little, for a few days.\nopt.right.say.1 [d82535] = 你跑去找级长。等你回来，走廊里空无一人，墙上在滴水，凯尔也走了。{o}告诉你，他什么都没做，只是站在那儿，直到那些人离开。他绝口不提这件事。之后几天，他有点躲着你。\n\n@event bram_between_writes_up\n# en: Name in the Ledger\ntitle [a261df] = 名字上了记录簿\n# en: {~Bram has {o} by the sleeve for \"loitering with intent\", which turns out to mean waiting for you.|Bram is writing {o} up for \"an insolent hat\". {o}'s hat is a perfectly ordinary hat.|Bram has caught {o} running on the stairs, and is reading out the rule number with relish.} {o} looks at you over Bram's shoulder: help.\ntext [460a2d] = {~布拉姆揪着{o}的袖子，罪名是“意图不轨地逗留”，其实就是在等你。|布拉姆正在给{o}记一笔，罪名是“无礼的帽子”。{o}的帽子是顶再普通不过的帽子。|布拉姆抓到{o}在楼梯上跑，正津津有味地念着校规编号。}{o}越过布拉姆的肩膀看着你：救命。\n# en: A prefect with a red sash writing in a ledger while a student argues, a third student hurrying over.\nscene [802cba] = 一个披着红色绶带的级长正在记录簿上写字，一个学生在争辩，第三个学生匆匆赶来。\n# en: Plead {o}'s case\nopt.left.label [f1b202] = 替{o}求情\n# en: {~\"Rule fourteen is about loitering with intent,\" you say. \"{o} had no intent. Look at {o.them}.\" Bram looks. He crosses it out, with ill grace.|You argue that a hat cannot be insolent. Bram argues that it can. You win on a technicality he invented himself last term. He hates that.|You remind Bram that he ran on the same stairs on Tuesday. He goes red, says \"prefect's business\", and lets {o} go.}\nopt.left.say.1 [80779e] = {~“第十四条说的是意图不轨地逗留，”你说，“{o}没有意图。你看看{o.them}。”布拉姆看了看。他不情不愿地划掉了。|你论证帽子不可能无礼。布拉姆论证可以。你凭着一条他自己上学期发明的细则赢了。他恨死了。|你提醒布拉姆，他星期二也在这段楼梯上跑过。他脸一红，说了句“级长公务”，放{o}走了。}\n# en: {~\"And a demerit for you, for obstruction.\" Bram writes it with a flourish. {o} mouths \"sorry\" at you.|Bram writes up {o}, then you, then {o} again for laughing. It's a productive afternoon for the ledger.|\"Noted,\" says Bram, and writes up {o} anyway, and adds a little note in the margin about you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [30190c] = {~“你也记一笔，妨碍公务。”布拉姆大笔一挥。{o}冲你做口型：“对不起。”|布拉姆给{o}记了一笔，又给你记了一笔，然后因为{o}笑了又记了一笔。记录簿度过了一个充实的下午。|“已记录，”布拉姆说，照样给{o}记了一笔，还在页边给你添了条小注。}\n# en: Take the demerit for {o}\nopt.right.label [1920b3] = 替{o}领下这次处分\n# en: {~\"Write me up instead. It was my fault.\" Bram looks at you for a long moment and writes your name. Afterwards, gruffly, he says, \"That was decent.\" Then he walks off before you can answer.|You say {o} was waiting for you, so it's your demerit. Bram, baffled, agrees. {o} buys you a cake.|You take it. Bram writes it slowly. You catch him, much later, crossing it out when he thinks nobody's looking.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b48d5d] = {~“记我吧。是我的错。”布拉姆看了你好一会儿，写下了你的名字。事后他粗声粗气地说：“这事做得体面。”没等你回答就走了。|你说{o}是在等你，所以处分该是你的。布拉姆一头雾水地同意了。{o}请你吃了块蛋糕。|你领了。布拉姆写得很慢。很久以后，你撞见他趁没人注意时把它划掉了。}\n\n@event bram_between_prank\n# en: The Sash\ntitle [83b6ff] = 绶带\n# en: {o} has a pot of dye, a plan and a grudge. Bram's prefect sash hangs on its peg outside the bathhouse every evening at eight. \"Pink,\" says {o}, glowing. \"Just for one day. Are you in?\"\ntext [a4d36d] = {o}有一罐染料、一个计划和一肚子怨气。每晚八点，布拉姆的级长绶带都挂在浴室外的挂钩上。“粉色，”{o}容光焕发，“就一天。你加入吗？”\n# en: Two students crouched behind a pillar with a pot of dye, a prefect's red sash hanging on a peg nearby.\nscene [b18be1] = 两个学生拿着一罐染料蹲在柱子后面，附近的挂钩上挂着一条级长的红色绶带。\n# en: Be the lookout\nopt.left.label [d31cde] = 负责望风\n# en: Bram wears a pink sash to breakfast before he notices. The refectory goes very quiet, and then very loud. He knows it was {o}. He suspects you. He never proves it.\nopt.left.say.1 [36277f] = 布拉姆戴着粉色绶带去吃早饭，走进去才发现。膳堂先是一片寂静，然后炸开了锅。他知道是{o}干的。他怀疑你。他从来没能证明。\n# en: Bram comes out of the bathhouse early, and finds you standing very innocently next to his sash and a pot of pink dye. The demerits take him most of a page.\nopt.left.say.2 [fbf4eb] = 布拉姆提早从浴室出来，发现你正一脸无辜地站在他的绶带和一罐粉色染料旁边。他写处分写了大半页。\n# en: Talk {o} out of it\nopt.right.label [4aa154] = 劝{o}打消念头\n# en: \"It's his whole world, that sash,\" you say. \"It wouldn't be funny to him. It would be the end of the world.\" {o} looks at you oddly, and puts the dye away. Bram never finds out how close it was.\nopt.right.say.1 [f2c778] = “那条绶带就是他的一切，”你说，“对他来说那不好笑。那是天塌了。”{o}奇怪地看了你一眼，把染料收了起来。布拉姆始终不知道那次有多险。\n\n@event bram_between_coaching\n# en: Prefect's Business\ntitle [869dc4] = 级长公务\n# en: You go into the duelling hall late and find Bram there, without his sash, patiently correcting {o}'s footwork. {o} is terrible. Bram is being kind about it. They both freeze when they see you. \"Prefect's business,\" says Bram, too quickly.\ntext [98f565] = 你深夜走进决斗厅，发现布拉姆在那儿，没戴绶带，正耐心地纠正{o}的步法。{o}练得糟透了。布拉姆却很和气。看见你，两人都僵住了。“级长公务，”布拉姆说得太快了。\n# en: A duelling floor late at night, a prefect patiently correcting a younger student's stance, both freezing as a third student opens the door.\nscene [e221a8] = 深夜的决斗场上，一个级长正耐心地纠正低年级学生的站姿，第三个学生推开门时两人都愣住了。\n# en: Tease him about it\nopt.left.label [8849ef] = 取笑他\n# en: \"Prefect's business,\" you repeat, delighted. Bram goes the colour of his sash. He's short with you for a week, and keeps coaching {o} anyway, which you suspect was never in question.\nopt.left.say.1 [950837] = “级长公务，”你乐不可支地重复了一遍。布拉姆的脸红得跟他的绶带一个颜色。接下来一个星期他对你都没好气，可照样继续教{o}——你怀疑这从来就不是问题。\n# en: Say you never saw a thing\nopt.right.label [173993] = 说你什么也没看见\n# en: You back out of the door. The next morning Bram passes you in the corridor and says, without looking at you, \"Don't make it weird.\" You don't. {o} wins a practice bout the following week, and Bram claps once, loudly, then stops.\nopt.right.say.1 [468532] = 你退出了门外。第二天早上，布拉姆在走廊里与你擦肩而过，眼睛都没看你，说了句：“别搞得怪怪的。”你没有。下个星期{o}赢了一场练习赛，布拉姆响亮地拍了一下手，然后停了。\n\n@event corvin_between_odds\n# en: Corvin Makes a Book\ntitle [dbd12d] = 科尔文开了盘\n# en: {o} and {p} are going to settle it with wands in the courtyard at sunset, and Corvin is making a book. \"Seven to four on {o}, saint,\" he says, chalking it on the back of a door. \"Want in? It's practically charity. I'm charity.\"\ntext [11f52f] = {o}和{p}打算日落时在庭院里用魔杖了结恩怨，科尔文正在开盘。“{o}，七赔四，圣人，”他边说边用粉笔写在门背后，“要不要来一注？这简直是做慈善。我就是慈善。”\n# en: A student with a deck of cards tucked behind one ear writing odds in chalk on the back of a door, a small crowd pressing coins into his hand.\nscene [b0e215] = 一个耳后夹着一副牌的学生正用粉笔在门背后写赔率，一小群人往他手里塞硬币。\n# en: Put a crown on {o}\nopt.left.label [9bc252] = 押一克朗在{o}身上\n# en: {o} wins in a flash of green sparks, and Corvin pays you out with a flourish and a wince. \"Beginner's luck. I'll get it back. I always get it back.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [270fe5] = {o}在一阵绿色火花中赢了，科尔文潇洒又肉疼地付了钱。“新手运气。我会赢回来的。我总能赢回来。”\n# en: {p} wins, with a hex nobody saw coming. Corvin pockets your crown with real sympathy. \"Should've asked me,\" he says. \"I'd have told you. For a fee.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [f0d948] = {p}赢了，用了一道谁都没料到的咒语。科尔文真诚同情地收起了你那枚克朗。“早该问我的，”他说，“我会告诉你的。收费的。”\n# en: Tip off a prefect\nopt.right.label [4c63af] = 向级长告密\n# en: The prefects break it up before the first spell, and confiscate the chalk, and Corvin's name goes in the book.{knows(corvin.last_warning):  He is one detention from being sent home.|} He knows exactly who told. He doesn't say anything. That's worse than if he had.\nopt.right.say.1 [8add0e] = 级长们在第一道咒语之前就把事情平息了，没收了粉笔，还把科尔文的名字记进了本子。{knows(corvin.last_warning): 他再挨一次禁闭就要被送回家。|}他很清楚是谁告的密。他什么都没说。这比他说了更糟。\n\n@event corvin_between_debt\n# en: Corvin Owes {o}\ntitle [ac4c6b] = 科尔文欠{o}的钱\n# en: Corvin owes {o} eight crowns, and has owed it since the leaves turned. {o} has an IOU in Corvin's handwriting, with a small drawing of a goat on it. {o} would like you, as Corvin's friend, to collect.\ntext [e795b7] = 科尔文欠{o}八克朗，从叶子变黄时欠到现在。{o}手里有一张科尔文亲笔写的欠条，上面还画了只小山羊。{o}希望你，作为科尔文的朋友，去把钱要回来。\n# en: A student waving a scrap of paper at another, who is shrugging with both hands empty and grinning.\nscene [697341] = 一个学生朝另一个挥着一张纸条，对方咧嘴笑着，摊开两只空手耸了耸肩。\n# en: Pay it yourself\nopt.left.label [3d512f] = 自己替他还\n# en: You pay {o} and tell Corvin he owes you now. \"Saint,\" he says, appalled and touched in equal measure. \"Who does that?\" He pays you back three weeks later, in coins and a very good story about where he got them.\nopt.left.say.1 [62eb17] = 你付了钱给{o}，然后告诉科尔文，他现在欠你了。“圣人，”他又惊恐又感动，“谁会这么干？”三个星期后他还了你，用的是硬币，外加一个关于钱从哪儿来的好故事。\n# en: Get Corvin to pay\nopt.right.label [1ae443] = 让科尔文自己还\n# en: You corner Corvin and refuse to laugh at any of his jokes until he pays. It takes four jokes. He pays {o}, all eight, and draws {o} a better goat as interest.\nopt.right.say.1 [fc652e] = 你把科尔文堵住，在他还钱之前对他的任何笑话都不笑。这花了四个笑话。他把八克朗全还给了{o}，还给{o}画了只更好的山羊当利息。\n# en: Corvin turns it into a joke, then a card trick, then a story about trolls, and by the end you've somehow agreed it's {o}'s fault for lending. {o} doesn't find that funny.\nopt.right.say.2 [84af7c] = 科尔文把事情变成一个笑话，又变成一个牌技，又变成一个关于巨魔的故事，到最后你不知怎么竟同意这是{o}自己借钱的错。{o}一点都不觉得好笑。\n\n@event corvin_between_witness\n# en: Where Corvin Was\ntitle [5d18c8] = 科尔文当时在哪儿\n# en: {o} is up before the headmistress: a silver inkstand gone from the library, and {o} was seen near it. Corvin knows {o} didn't take it; he saw who did, at two in the morning. To say so, he'd have to say where he was at two in the morning. \"One more detention, saint,\" he says quietly, \"and they send me home.\"\ntext [ffb2f4] = {o}被叫到了院长面前：图书馆丢了一个银墨水台，有人看见{o}在附近。科尔文知道不是{o}拿的；凌晨两点，他看见了是谁拿的。可要说出来，他就得说出自己凌晨两点在哪儿。“再挨一次禁闭，圣人，”他低声说，“他们就会把我送回家。”\n# en: A student in a dark stairwell holding up an unlocked padlock, another student beside him shaking their head urgently.\nscene [cd4a0d] = 昏暗的楼梯间里，一个学生举着一把被打开的挂锁，旁边另一个学生拼命摇头。\n# en: Tell him to speak up\nopt.left.label [87da40] = 劝他说出来\n# en: Corvin goes in, and tells it straight, without a single joke. The headmistress gives him a long look and a warning, not a detention, \"in view of the circumstances\". {o} doesn't know what to say. Corvin says it for both of them: \"Don't make a habit of being innocent near me.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [489e7a] = 科尔文进去了，一个笑话都没讲，把事情原原本本说了。院长看了他很久，“鉴于情况特殊”，只给了警告，没给禁闭。{o}不知道该说什么。科尔文替两人都说了：“别养成在我附近当无辜者的习惯。”\n# en: Corvin shakes his head. \"I can't, saint. I can't go home.\" You don't push. {o} gets the detention, and the real thief is never named, and Corvin can't look at {o} for weeks.\nopt.left.say.2 [d1e1f0] = 科尔文摇摇头。“我做不到，圣人。我不能回家。”你没有再逼他。{o}挨了禁闭，真正的小偷始终没被点名，好几个星期科尔文都不敢看{o}。\n# en: Find another way to clear {o}\nopt.right.label [de66a3] = 另想办法替{o}洗清\n# en: You go through the library ledger until midnight and find that the inkstand was booked out for cleaning to someone else entirely. {o} is cleared. Corvin buys you a drink and doesn't say why.\nopt.right.say.1 [e05e7c] = 你把图书馆的登记簿翻到半夜，发现那个墨水台被另一个人借去清洗了。{o}洗清了嫌疑。科尔文请你喝了一杯，没说为什么。\n# en: You turn the library inside out and find nothing anyone will believe. {o} serves the detention. Corvin sits with {o} at supper every night of it.\nopt.right.say.2 [6fa756] = 你把图书馆翻了个底朝天，却找不到任何能让人信服的东西。{o}挨了禁闭。禁闭的每个晚上，科尔文都陪{o}吃晚饭。\n\n@event nessa_between_story\n# en: The Human Angle\ntitle [9164ce] = 人情味\n# en: {o} failed a practical exam badly enough that the lecturer wrote home, and somehow Nessa has heard. \"It's a story about pressure,\" she says, in headline voice. \"The human angle. I'd change the name. Mostly.\" {o} has found out, and caught you in the corridor first. \"Please. She'll listen to you.\"\ntext [b3e8b9] = {o}的一场实践考试考砸了，砸到讲师写信寄回了家，而内莎不知怎么听说了。“这是一个关于压力的故事，”她用标题腔说，“人情味。我会改掉名字。大部分吧。”{o}知道了，先在走廊里拦住了你。“求你了。她会听你的。”\n# en: A newspaper office cluttered with proofs, an editor holding up a draft headline while a student in the doorway begs her not to run it.\nscene [86f2ec] = 堆满校样的报社办公室里，一位编辑举着一张标题草稿，门口的学生正求她别登。\n# en: Talk Nessa out of it\nopt.left.label [f2d423] = 劝内莎打消念头\n# en: You put it to her plainly: it's not a story about pressure, it's a story about {o}. Nessa goes very quiet, the way she does off the record. Then she spikes it. \"You're right. I hate that you're right.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [d71347] = 你直截了当地告诉她：这不是一个关于压力的故事，是一个关于{o}的故事。内莎安静了下来，就像她不公开记录时那样。然后她撤了稿。“你说得对。我讨厌你说得对。”\n# en: \"Everyone's a story,\" Nessa says. \"Some just aren't finished.\" She runs it, with the name changed. Everyone knows who it is. {o} doesn't speak to either of you for a fortnight.\nopt.left.say.2 [bf9eb0] = “人人都是一个故事，”内莎说，“只是有些还没写完。”她登了，改了名字。可人人都知道是谁。{o}两个星期没跟你们俩说话。\n# en: Offer her a better story\nopt.right.label [f7aa4c] = 给她一个更好的故事\n# en: You give Nessa something of your own instead: a long, unflattering account of your worst week this term. She takes it, delighted. {o}'s story never runs. Yours does, on page two, and people are very kind about it for a week.\nopt.right.say.1 [c23d11] = 你转而给了内莎一个你自己的故事：一篇冗长又不太体面的叙述，讲你本学期最糟糕的一个星期。她如获至宝。{o}的故事始终没登。你的登在了第二版，接下来一个星期，大家都对你特别和气。\n\n@event nessa_between_match\n# en: Match of the Month\ntitle [d38ac6] = 本月佳偶\n# en: The Lumen Ledger is launching \"Match of the Month\", anonymous and entirely scientific, and Nessa needs a first nomination by the printers' deadline. She has heard you know {o}. \"Who's {o} sweet on? You don't have to say. Just write it down. That's not saying.\"\ntext [01170c] = 《流明纪事报》要推出“本月佳偶”栏目，匿名，绝对科学，内莎需要在付印截止前拿到第一个提名。她听说你认识{o}。“{o}暗恋谁？你不用说出来。写下来就行。写下来不算说。”\n# en: An editor at a cluttered desk holding out a quill and a blank form titled \"Match of the Month\", grinning.\nscene [04dbc8] = 一位编辑坐在乱糟糟的书桌后，笑着递出一支羽毛笔和一张标题为“本月佳偶”的空白表格。\n# en: Nominate {o}\nopt.left.label [216a97] = 提名{o}\n# en: You write a name down. On Friday the whole castle reads that \"a certain student in the year\" is pining for \"a certain other student\", with details. {o} recognises every detail. So does the other student, it turns out, and the next week they're seen at the Gilded Newt together. {o} is still furious with you.\nopt.left.say.1 [c0e567] = 你写下了一个名字。星期五，全城堡都读到“本年级的某位同学”正苦苦思慕“另一位同学”，还附有细节。{o}认出了每一个细节。结果另一位同学也认出来了，下个星期，有人看见他们一起去了镀金蝾螈酒馆。{o}至今还在生你的气。\n# en: Tell Nessa it's not yours to print\nopt.right.label [c9fb2c] = 告诉内莎这轮不到你来登\n# en: \"Off the record?\" says Nessa. \"It's off the record that I'm not telling you,\" you say. She laughs, and nominates herself instead, which sells out the issue.\nopt.right.say.1 [9c750a] = “不公开记录？”内莎说。“不公开记录的是，我不告诉你，”你说。她笑了，转而提名了她自己，那一期卖了个精光。\n\n@event mir_between_owl\n# en: The Owl Has Opinions\ntitle [282081] = 猫头鹰有意见\n# en: Mir's clockwork owl, which remembers deadlines, birthdays and, slightly, grudges, has taken against {o}. It hoots when {o} comes in. It drops screws on {o} from the rafters. \"It's not personal!\" says Mir. \"Probably. It might be personal. I didn't build it to be personal.\"\ntext [9b2d8f] = 米尔的发条猫头鹰会记住截止日期、生日，还有，稍微记一点仇——它看{o}不顺眼。{o}一进门它就叫。它从房梁上往{o}身上扔螺丝。“这不是针对谁！”米尔说，“大概吧。也可能是针对的。我造它的时候没打算让它针对谁。”\n# en: A clockwork owl perched on a workshop lamp, glaring with brass eyes at a student who is ducking, while its maker apologises with both hands.\nscene [5180da] = 一只发条猫头鹰蹲在工坊的灯上，用黄铜眼睛瞪着一个正在躲闪的学生，它的制造者举着双手道歉。\n# en: Help Mir find out why\nopt.left.label [bfbdef] = 帮米尔找出原因\n# en: You go through the owl's memory spool with Mir, half the night, and find it: {o} once moved a teacup off the owl's favourite shelf, in the first week. Mir has a long, gentle talk with the owl. The screws stop. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [ebbbf3] = 你和米尔翻查了猫头鹰的记忆发条，翻了半个晚上，终于找到了：第一周时，{o}把一只茶杯从猫头鹰最喜欢的架子上挪开过。米尔和猫头鹰进行了一次漫长而温和的谈话。螺丝不再掉了。基本上。\n# en: You and Mir take the owl apart and put it back together and it is still furious with {o}. \"It's a feature now,\" Mir decides. {o} starts wearing a hat.\nopt.left.say.2 [b29ea3] = 你和米尔把猫头鹰拆开又装好，它还是对{o}怒气冲冲。“现在这是个功能了，”米尔宣布。{o}开始戴帽子了。\n# en: Tell {o} to apologise to it\nopt.right.label [6bc3a5] = 让{o}去给它道歉\n# en: You suggest {o} apologise. {o} stands under the rafters and apologises to the owl, formally, for whatever it was. The owl considers this for a whole minute, then hoots once, and from then on drops screws on {o} only on Tuesdays. Mir is moved almost to tears.\nopt.right.say.1 [d5a6bb] = 你建议{o}去道个歉。{o}站在房梁下，郑重其事地向猫头鹰道歉，不管当初做错了什么。猫头鹰考虑了整整一分钟，叫了一声，从此只在星期二往{o}身上扔螺丝。米尔感动得差点哭了。\n\n@event mir_between_blunt\n# en: Probably Fine\ntitle [47432a] = 大概没问题\n# en: {~{o} asked to help Mir in the workshop. Mir said, cheerfully, \"No, you'll touch things,\" and then talked to a gearbox for ten minutes.|{o} showed Mir a design. Mir said, \"That'll explode,\" with total delight, and went back to the lathe.|{o} has been holding the same lamp for Mir for an hour. Mir has thanked the lamp twice and {o} not once.} {o} finds you afterwards. \"Do they even like me?\"\ntext [cd845b] = {~{o}想在工坊里给米尔帮忙。米尔开开心心地说：“不行，你会乱碰东西的。”然后对着一个齿轮箱说了十分钟话。|{o}给米尔看了一张设计图。米尔兴高采烈地说：“这会爆炸的，”然后回到了车床边。|{o}给米尔举着同一盏灯举了一个小时。米尔谢了那盏灯两次，一次都没谢{o}。}事后{o}来找你。“TA到底喜不喜欢我？”\n# en: A workshop bench strewn with gears, an artificer talking excitedly to a half-built machine while a classmate stands to one side, looking hurt.\nscene [11288c] = 摆满齿轮的工坊长凳边，一位工匠兴奋地对着半成品机器说话，一个同学站在一旁，神情受伤。\n# en: Translate for Mir\nopt.left.label [c5f106] = 替米尔翻译\n# en: {~You explain that \"you'll touch things\" is high praise from Mir; it means you'd be interested. {o} goes back. Mir gives {o} a screwdriver of {o.their} very own, which is practically a ring.|\"It'll explode\" means \"I'd want to watch,\" you tell {o}. {o} brings the design back, and Mir spends three hours making it explode properly.|You tell {o} that Mir thanks lamps because lamps can't be hurt. {o} laughs. The next time, Mir, prompted by you, thanks {o}, and then the lamp, in that order.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c134ab] = {~你解释说，“你会乱碰东西”在米尔那儿是极高的赞誉，意思是你会感兴趣。{o}回去了。米尔送了{o}一把专属的螺丝刀，这几乎等于一枚戒指。|你告诉{o}，“这会爆炸的”意思是“我想看看”。{o}把设计图拿了回去，米尔花了三个小时让它好好地炸了一回。|你告诉{o}，米尔谢灯是因为灯不会受伤。{o}笑了。下一次，在你的提醒下，米尔先谢了{o}，再谢了灯，按这个顺序。}\n# en: Tell Mir directly\nopt.right.label [c596c4] = 直接告诉米尔\n# en: {~\"Oh!\" says Mir, stricken. \"Oh no. Probably I should say things to people.\" They go and say several things to {o}, all at once, most of them about gears. It works.|Mir listens, goggles pushed up, and then goes and apologises to {o} with a small clockwork frog. The frog says sorry. Mir says it too.|Mir takes it very seriously and writes \"SAY THANK YOU TO {o}\" on the workshop wall in chalk, where it stays for a year.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6dfedf] = {~“哦！”米尔大惊失色，“哦不。我大概应该对人说点什么。”TA跑去一口气对{o}说了好几件事，大部分是关于齿轮的。管用了。|米尔把护目镜推到额头上听完，然后带着一只小小的发条青蛙去向{o}道歉。青蛙说了对不起。米尔也说了。|米尔非常认真，用粉笔在工坊墙上写下“要对{o}说谢谢”，那行字在墙上留了一年。}\n# en: {~Mir doesn't really hear it; they're halfway into a gearbox. \"Hm? Yes. Probably,\" they say. {o} gives up on the workshop.|\"But I do like {o},\" says Mir, baffled, \"I let {o} hold the lamp.\" You don't get further than that.|Mir nods along and then forgets, which is very Mir. {o} decides the workshop isn't for {o.them}.}\nopt.right.say.2 [b7bcc3] = {~米尔其实没听进去；TA的半个身子在齿轮箱里。“嗯？是。大概吧，”TA说。{o}放弃了工坊。|“可我是喜欢{o}的啊，”米尔困惑地说，“我都让{o}举灯了。”谈话到此为止。|米尔连连点头，然后就忘了，很米尔。{o}认定工坊不适合{o.them}。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/between_stories.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/between_stories.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@thread bram_corvin_feud\n# en: The Ledger War\nname [940dc3] = 记录簿之战\n# en: Bram had written Corvin up eleven times in one term. Corvin had started writing back.\npremise [2abeb9] = 布拉姆一个学期给科尔文记了十一次处分。科尔文开始还击了。\n# en: Bram and Corvin went to war over the demerit ledger, and you stepped into the middle of it.\njournal.1 [999971] = 布拉姆和科尔文为了处分记录簿开战，而你踏进了战场正中。\n# en: Bram's ledger vanished. You found it in Corvin's coat and put it back before anyone knew.\njournal.2 [1dd23d] = 布拉姆的记录簿不见了。你在科尔文的外套里找到了它，赶在任何人发现之前放了回去。\n# en: Bram's ledger vanished, and you let it stay vanished. The castle held its breath for a week.\njournal.3 [b99d15] = 布拉姆的记录簿不见了，你让它继续不见。整个城堡屏息了一个星期。\n# en: The headmistress set them to clear the confiscation vault together. You locked the door on them for an hour.\njournal.4 [88ade8] = 院长让他们俩一起清理没收库。你把门锁上了一个小时。\n# en: The headmistress set them to clear the confiscation vault together, and you watched them by the rules.\njournal.5 [fa19ec] = 院长让他们俩一起清理没收库，你照规矩看着他们。\n# en: Bram and Corvin shook hands in the vault doorway. Neither will say who offered first.\njournal.6 [06cabb] = 布拉姆和科尔文在库房门口握了手。谁先伸的手，两人都不肯说。\n# en: Bram and Corvin came out of the vault still enemies, if quieter ones.\njournal.7 [45d414] = 布拉姆和科尔文走出库房时还是对头，只是安静了些。\n\n@event feud_ledger_war\n# en: Say Please\ntitle [4ee6be] = 说“请”\n# en: Bram has written Corvin up for the eleventh time this term (\"existing in a corridor\"), and Corvin has answered by charming the lock on Bram's demerit ledger so that it opens only for the word \"please\". Bram has been saying \"OPEN\" at it for ten minutes. Half the courtyard is watching. Both of them see you at the same moment.\ntext [e36d96] = 布拉姆这学期已经第十一次给科尔文记处分了（罪名是“在走廊里存在”），而科尔文的回应是给布拉姆的处分记录簿的锁施了个咒，只有听到“请”字才肯打开。布拉姆已经冲着它喊了十分钟“开！”。半个庭院的人都在看。两人同时看见了你。\n# en: A courtyard fountain, a prefect shouting at a locked ledger in his hands, a grinning student leaning on the fountain rim with a deck of cards.\nscene [44a364] = 庭院的喷泉边，一个级长冲着手里上了锁的记录簿大喊，一个咧嘴笑的学生拿着一副牌靠在喷泉边上。\n# en: Step in between them\nopt.left.label [d21ee8] = 站到他们中间\n# en: \"Just say it, Bram.\" Bram says it, through his teeth. The ledger opens. Corvin laughs so hard he has to hold on to the fountain, and Bram writes your name on a fresh page, very neatly, and underlines it.\nopt.left.say.1 [51bfaa] = “说一声就行了，布拉姆。”布拉姆咬着牙说了。记录簿开了。科尔文笑得只好扶着喷泉，而布拉姆在新的一页上工工整整地写下你的名字，还划了条线。\n# en: Keep walking\nopt.right.label [6ca967] = 继续走\n# en: You keep walking. Behind you, the courtyard counts along with Bram: \"OPEN. OPEN.\" It's still going when you reach the library.\nopt.right.say.1 [9adcc7] = 你继续往前走。身后，整个庭院跟着布拉姆一起数：“开！开！”你走到图书馆时，那边还没停。\n\n@event feud_ledger_gone\n# en: The Missing Ledger\ntitle [42ed15] = 失踪的记录簿\n# en: Bram's ledger has gone. Not locked: gone. Bram is white with fury and quoting rules at the walls. Corvin is very helpful about looking for it. Then you borrow Corvin's coat on a cold evening, and find the ledger in its inside pocket. {knows(corvin.last_warning): Corvin is one detention from being sent home. If Bram finds it there, that's the end of him.|If Bram finds it there, Corvin is finished.}\ntext [1631b8] = 布拉姆的记录簿不见了。不是锁上了：是不见了。布拉姆气得脸色发白，对着墙壁背校规。科尔文帮着找，热心极了。后来一个冷天的晚上，你借了科尔文的外套，在内袋里摸到了那本记录簿。{knows(corvin.last_warning): 科尔文再挨一次禁闭就要被送回家。要是布拉姆在那儿找到它，科尔文就完了。|要是布拉姆在那儿找到它，科尔文就完了。}\n# en: A coat hanging on a dormitory peg with the corner of a red leather ledger sticking out of its pocket.\nscene [771a96] = 宿舍挂钩上挂着一件外套，口袋里露出一本红色皮面记录簿的一角。\n# en: Put it back quietly\nopt.left.label [eb4271] = 悄悄放回去\n# en: You leave it on Bram's desk in the prefects' room, open at today's page. Bram never knows how. Corvin knows exactly how. \"Saint,\" he says, and for once it isn't quite a joke.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a5865] = 你把它放在级长室布拉姆的桌上，翻到今天那一页。布拉姆始终不知道是怎么回事。科尔文完全知道是怎么回事。“圣人，”他说，这一次不全是玩笑。\n# en: Leave it where it is\nopt.right.label [e26ef5] = 就让它留在那儿\n# en: You hang the coat back up. For a week nobody in the castle gets a demerit, and Bram stalks the corridors like a man who has lost a hand. Corvin buys everyone cider.\nopt.right.say.1 [f35048] = 你把外套挂了回去。整整一个星期，城堡里没有一个人挨处分，布拉姆在走廊里游荡，像丢了一只手。科尔文请大家喝苹果酒。\n\n@event feud_vault\n# en: A Week in the Vault\ntitle [52f8f0] = 没收库里的一周\n# en: The headmistress has had enough of both of them. Bram and Corvin will clear out the confiscation vault together, every evening for a week, and you, having been seen in the middle of all of it, are to watch them. Twenty minutes in, Bram is labelling things. Corvin is unlabelling them.\ntext [b8ac7c] = 院长受够了他们俩。布拉姆和科尔文要一起清理没收库，连续一个星期，每个晚上；而你，因为被人看见一直夹在中间，负责看着他们。二十分钟后，布拉姆在给东西贴标签。科尔文在把标签撕掉。\n# en: A cluttered vault of confiscated oddities, two students glaring at each other over a crate, a third leaning in the doorway with a lantern.\nscene [c93751] = 堆满没收来的古怪物件的库房里，两个学生隔着一只板条箱怒目相对，第三个人提着灯靠在门口。\n# en: Lock them in for an hour\nopt.left.label [4e8357] = 把他们锁在里面一小时\n# en: You step out \"for a lantern\" and turn the key. When you open the door an hour later they're sitting on the same crate, arguing about a confiscated board game, and Bram is winning, and Corvin is letting him.\nopt.left.say.1 [89db9a] = 你说出去“拿盏灯”，转动了钥匙。一小时后你开门时，他们坐在同一只板条箱上，为一盘被没收的棋争论，布拉姆在赢，而科尔文在让他赢。\n# en: Corvin hears the key turn and has the lock open before you've let go of it. \"Nice try, saint.\" Bram writes you up for \"obstruction of a punishment\", which isn't a rule, until he invents it.\nopt.left.say.2 [3dc95b] = 钥匙一转，科尔文就听见了，你还没松手他就把锁打开了。“不错的尝试，圣人。”布拉姆给你记了一笔“妨碍惩罚”，校规里本没有这一条，直到他发明了它。\n# en: Watch them by the rules\nopt.right.label [5ed0fb] = 照规矩看着他们\n# en: You sit in the doorway and revise while they snipe. By the end of the week the vault is spotless and they've stopped speaking altogether, which is, the headmistress says, an improvement.\nopt.right.say.1 [7c7c1f] = 你坐在门口复习，他们互相冷嘲热讽。到周末，库房一尘不染，两人干脆一句话都不说了——院长说，这是一种进步。\n\n@event feud_end\n# en: The Vault Door\ntitle [b709f0] = 库房门口\n# en: The last evening in the vault. {npc_rel(bram, corvin) > -20: Something has changed this week. Bram has stopped quoting rules at Corvin. Corvin has stopped quoting Bram back.|Nothing much has changed. They've cleared the vault by working at opposite ends of it.} At the door, Bram stops, and looks at Corvin, and then at you.\ntext [1a511e] = 在没收库的最后一个晚上。{npc_rel(bram, corvin) > -20: 这个星期有些东西变了。布拉姆不再对科尔文背校规。科尔文也不再学着背回去。|没什么变化。他们一人一头，把库房清完了。}到门口时，布拉姆停下来，看看科尔文，又看看你。\n# en: Two students in a vault doorway at the end of a long week, one in a prefect's sash, one with cards behind his ear, a third watching.\nscene [9eb3e9] = 漫长的一周结束时，两个学生站在库房门口，一个披着级长绶带，一个耳后夹着牌，第三个人在旁边看着。\n# en: Say something to both of them\nopt.left.label [4e97fd] = 对他们俩说点什么\n# en: You say they make a better team than either would admit. Bram goes red. Corvin grins. Then Bram, stiffly, holds out a hand, and Corvin shakes it, and immediately palms the prefect's pencil. Bram lets him keep it.\nopt.left.say.1 [39df83] = 你说，他们俩搭档起来比任何一个人愿意承认的都要好。布拉姆脸红了。科尔文咧嘴一笑。然后布拉姆僵硬地伸出手，科尔文握了握，顺手摸走了级长的铅笔。布拉姆让他留着了。\n# en: You try. Bram says \"noted\". Corvin says \"saint\". They leave by different stairs. But the ledger, after that, has Corvin's name in it a little less often.\nopt.left.say.2 [51d0c0] = 你试了。布拉姆说“已记录”。科尔文说“圣人”。两人从不同的楼梯离开。不过从那以后，科尔文的名字在记录簿上出现得少了一点。\n# en: Leave them to it\nopt.right.label [4dcfa4] = 让他们自己待着\n# en: You go ahead up the stairs. Behind you, a long silence, and then Corvin's laugh, and then, astonishingly, Bram's. At breakfast they nod to each other. Nobody knows what to make of it.\nopt.right.say.1 [71f0b3] = 你先上了楼。身后是长长的沉默，接着是科尔文的笑声，然后，令人惊讶地，是布拉姆的。早饭时，两人互相点了点头。谁都不知道该怎么看这件事。\n# en: You leave them to it. Neither of them says goodbye to the other. The war is over, more or less, because both of them are tired of it.\nopt.right.say.2 [0bb882] = 你让他们自己待着。两人谁也没跟对方道别。战争结束了，差不多吧，因为两人都打累了。\n\n@thread tobias_mir\n# en: The Watering Engine\nname [da92b1] = 浇水机\n# en: Mir built Tobias a machine to water the greenhouse. Then neither of them could stop talking about it.\npremise [687e0b] = 米尔给托比亚斯造了一台给温室浇水的机器。然后两人都停不下来地聊它。\n# en: Mir built Tobias a watering engine, and you noticed how long they spent explaining it to each other.\njournal.1 [fa1e2f] = 米尔给托比亚斯造了一台浇水机，你注意到他们互相讲解它讲了多久。\n# en: Tobias asked how to invite Mir to the orchard \"without it being a thing\". You helped him plan the thing.\njournal.2 [ac6d7c] = 托比亚斯问你怎么请米尔去果园，“又不算那回事”。你帮他把“那回事”计划好了。\n# en: Tobias asked how to invite Mir somewhere. You told him to just ask.\njournal.3 [c23ec0] = 托比亚斯问你怎么请米尔出去。你让他直接去问。\n# en: Mir wondered if Tobias only liked the engine. You told Mir otherwise.\njournal.4 [75e416] = 米尔怀疑托比亚斯只是喜欢那台机器。你告诉米尔不是这样。\n# en: Mir wondered if Tobias only liked the engine. You told Mir to ask him.\njournal.5 [d413ba] = 米尔怀疑托比亚斯只是喜欢那台机器。你让米尔自己去问他。\n# en: Tobias and Mir turned up at the orchard together. Whatever it is, it's their business, and it's going well.\njournal.6 [57fed4] = 托比亚斯和米尔一起来到了果园。不管那是什么，那是他们的事，而且进展得很好。\n# en: Tobias and Mir are the best of friends, and the greenhouse is the best-watered in the country.\njournal.7 [d0f658] = 托比亚斯和米尔成了最好的朋友，那座温室是全国浇水最勤的温室。\n\n@event tm_watering_engine\n# en: The Watering Engine\ntitle [da92b1] = 浇水机\n# en: There's a brass contraption trundling along the greenhouse benches, watering seedlings with a little copper spout. Mir built it. Tobias is explaining to it, gently, which plants like it lower. Mir is listening to Tobias the way Mir usually listens only to machines. Neither of them has noticed you.\ntext [6c60c0] = 温室的工作台上，一台黄铜机器正慢吞吞地走着，用一个小铜嘴给幼苗浇水。是米尔造的。托比亚斯正温和地向它解释，哪些植物喜欢浇得低一点。米尔听托比亚斯说话的样子，平时只有听机器时才会那样。两人都没注意到你。\n# en: A greenhouse with a brass contraption trundling along the benches watering seedlings, a big student and a goggled artificer crouched beside it, deep in talk.\nscene [cf2ce3] = 温室里，一台黄铜机器沿着工作台给幼苗浇水，一个大个子学生和一个戴护目镜的工匠蹲在旁边聊得正起劲。\n# en: Ask them how it works\nopt.left.label [443201] = 问问它是怎么工作的\n# en: They tell you at the same time, in different directions: Mir about gears, Tobias about ferns. They keep interrupting each other to agree. You leave an hour later. They don't notice that either.\nopt.left.say.1 [f5e35d] = 他们同时开口，各讲各的：米尔讲齿轮，托比亚斯讲蕨类。两人不停地打断对方，为的是表示同意。一小时后你离开了。他们也没注意到。\n# en: Leave them to it\nopt.right.label [4dcfa4] = 让他们自己忙\n# en: You take your notes to the far end of the greenhouse. The engine squeaks past you twice, followed by two people arguing happily about tomatoes.\nopt.right.say.1 [288ace] = 你把笔记拿到温室另一头。那台机器吱吱呀呀地从你身边经过了两次，后面跟着两个为西红柿开心争论的人。\n\n@event tm_invitation\n# en: Not a Thing\ntitle [007dd5] = 不算那回事\n# en: Tobias corners you by the potting benches, turning his cap round and round in his big hands. \"I'd like to ask Mir to the orchard on Sunday,\" he says. \"To see the bees. Only, without it being a thing. Unless it's a thing. How do you do that?\"\ntext [0e41c2] = 托比亚斯在育苗台边堵住你，一双大手把帽子转了又转。“我想请米尔星期天去果园，”他说，“去看蜜蜂。只是，又不算那回事。除非它就是那回事。这该怎么弄？”\n# en: A big student sitting on an upturned flowerpot, turning his cap round and round in his hands, asking a friend for advice.\nscene [b06e94] = 一个大个子学生坐在倒扣的花盆上，手里不停地转着帽子，向朋友讨主意。\n# en: Help him plan it\nopt.left.label [e52788] = 帮他计划\n# en: You spend an evening on it: a picnic, a jar of honey, a spare pair of goggles in case of bees. Tobias writes it all down on a seed packet. He apologises for the trouble four times and thanks you seven.\nopt.left.say.1 [e5be9f] = 你花了一个晚上：野餐，一罐蜂蜜，再备一副护目镜以防蜜蜂。托比亚斯把这些全写在一个种子袋上。他为给你添麻烦道歉了四次，道谢了七次。\n# en: Tell him to just ask\nopt.right.label [e662fc] = 让他直接去问\n# en: \"Just ask,\" you say. Tobias takes a breath that lifts his whole frame, and goes and asks. You hear Mir say \"probably!\" from across the greenhouse, which from Mir is a yes.\nopt.right.say.1 [28f38c] = “直接问，”你说。托比亚斯深吸一口气，整个身板都挺了起来，然后去问了。你听见米尔在温室另一头说“大概吧！”——从米尔嘴里说出来，这就是答应。\n# en: Tobias goes to ask, and comes back having talked about the engine for twenty minutes instead. \"I'll ask tomorrow,\" he says. He does, in the end. It takes a week.\nopt.right.say.2 [328841] = 托比亚斯去问了，回来时却是聊了二十分钟机器。“我明天再问，”他说。他最后还是问了。花了一个星期。\n\n@event tm_misread\n# en: Only the Engine?\ntitle [d43b83] = 只是因为机器？\n# en: Mir finds you, goggles pushed up, unusually quiet. \"Tobias wants to go to the orchard. To see the bees.\" A long pause. \"Is that about the engine? Probably it's about the engine. It's a very good engine. It's fine if it's about the engine.\"\ntext [345a4a] = 米尔来找你，护目镜推到额头上，难得这么安静。“托比亚斯想去果园。看蜜蜂。”长长的停顿。“这是因为那台机器吗？大概是因为机器吧。那是台很好的机器。要是因为机器，也没关系。”\n# en: A workshop at night, an artificer sitting on a bench with goggles pushed up, staring at a half-built engine, a friend in the doorway.\nscene [a19d46] = 夜里的工坊，一个工匠把护目镜推到额头上坐在长凳上，盯着一台半成品机器，朋友站在门口。\n# en: Tell Mir it isn't\nopt.left.label [2ae7a2] = 告诉米尔不是这样\n# en: \"It isn't about the engine,\" you say. Mir looks at you for a long moment, and then goes slowly pink from the collar up. \"Oh,\" says Mir. \"Oh. Probably I should bring something. Not the engine.\" You suggest honey.\nopt.left.say.1 [3352e1] = “不是因为机器，”你说。米尔看了你很久，然后从领口开始慢慢红了上来。“哦，”米尔说，“哦。我大概该带点什么去。别带机器。”你建议带蜂蜜。\n# en: \"Ask him yourself.\"\nopt.right.label [b2317d] = “你自己去问他。”\n# en: Mir marches into the greenhouse and asks him, straight out, in front of a bench of seedlings. You don't hear the answer. You see Mir's face, after. That's enough.\nopt.right.say.1 [9f828e] = 米尔大步走进温室，当着一工作台幼苗的面，直截了当地问了他。你没听见回答。你看见了米尔之后的表情。这就够了。\n# en: Mir doesn't ask. Mir builds Tobias a second engine instead, a smaller one, for the windowsill. It's a kind of asking. You're not sure Tobias reads it.\nopt.right.say.2 [284ce2] = 米尔没去问。米尔转而又给托比亚斯造了第二台机器，小一些的，放在窗台上用。这也算一种问法。你不确定托比亚斯看懂了没有。\n\n@event tm_end\n# en: The Orchard\ntitle [f7870f] = 果园\n# en: Sunday, the orchard, the bees. {npc_rel(tobias, mir) >= 30: Tobias and Mir arrive together, carrying a picnic between them and arguing happily about bees.|Tobias and Mir arrive separately, and spend the afternoon talking about the engine.} They've saved you a corner of the blanket, though you weren't sure you were invited.\ntext [4cf02b] = 星期天，果园，蜜蜂。{npc_rel(tobias, mir) >= 30: 托比亚斯和米尔一起来了，两人抬着一篮野餐，开开心心地争论着蜜蜂。|托比亚斯和米尔分头来了，整个下午都在聊那台机器。}他们在毯子上给你留了个角，虽然你也不确定自己是不是被邀请了。\n# en: An orchard on a sunny afternoon, two friends on a blanket among beehives, one in goggles, one with a jar of honey, laughing.\nscene [28f7e1] = 阳光明媚的午后果园，蜂箱之间铺着一条毯子，两个朋友坐在上面，一个戴着护目镜，一个拿着一罐蜂蜜，笑着。\n# en: Stay for the picnic\nopt.left.label [07dc2a] = 留下来野餐\n# en: You stay an hour, and then slip away, and neither of them notices you go. Whatever it is, it's their business. It looks like it's going well.\nopt.left.say.1 [99fc2e] = 你待了一个小时，然后悄悄溜走了，两人谁都没注意到你走了。不管那是什么，那是他们的事。看样子进展得很好。\n# en: It's a lovely afternoon. Whatever it might have been, it's a friendship, and a good one: the greenhouse gets watered on time for the rest of the year.\nopt.left.say.2 [e818a7] = 那是个美好的下午。不管它本可能是什么，它是一段友谊，而且是很好的一段：这一年剩下的日子里，温室都准时浇了水。\n# en: Leave them the blanket\nopt.right.label [cad02a] = 把毯子留给他们\n# en: You say you've an essay, which is true. From the orchard gate you look back once. They're sharing the honey. You don't look back again.\nopt.right.say.1 [2035e1] = 你说你有篇论文要写，这是真的。走到果园门口时，你回头看了一眼。他们在分着吃蜂蜜。你没有再回头。\n# en: You leave them to the bees. At supper, they're still talking about the engine. Best friends, then. It's not nothing.\nopt.right.say.2 [3aebeb] = 你把他们留给了蜜蜂。晚饭时，他们还在聊那台机器。那就是最好的朋友了。这也不算什么都没有。\n\n@thread study_circle\n# en: The Thursday Circle\nname [6d1812] = 星期四学习会\n# en: A study circle that started with two chairs and a borrowed lamp.\npremise [8ca799] = 一个以两把椅子和一盏借来的灯开始的学习会。\n# en: You and a friend started meeting in the library on Thursdays, with one lamp between you.\njournal.1 [4aac88] = 你和一个朋友开始每个星期四在图书馆碰头，两人共用一盏灯。\n# en: Someone nobody much liked asked to join. You said yes, and the circle got bigger.\njournal.2 [44b2fa] = 一个大家都不太喜欢的人想加入。你说好，学习会变大了。\n# en: Someone nobody much liked asked to join. The circle said no, and stayed cosy.\njournal.3 [976ff1] = 一个大家都不太喜欢的人想加入。学习会拒绝了，依旧温馨。\n# en: The night before exams, the circle nearly came apart. You held it together.\njournal.4 [6c7f40] = 考试前一晚，学习会差点散了。你把它撑了下来。\n# en: The night before exams, the circle came apart, and put itself back together after.\njournal.5 [425704] = 考试前一晚，学习会散了，考完后又自己凑了回来。\n# en: The Thursday Circle has its own table now, and a name carved under it.\njournal.6 [7580b9] = 星期四学习会如今有了自己的桌子，桌子底下还刻着一个名字。\n\n@event circle_founded\n# en: Thursdays\ntitle [6e5f2c] = 星期四\n# en: {o} drops into the chair across from you in the library with a stack of books and a borrowed lamp. \"I can't revise alone. I just read the same page. What if we did this every week? Thursdays. You and me, and whoever else.\"\ntext [ac19dc] = {o}抱着一摞书和一盏借来的灯，在图书馆里你对面的椅子上坐下。“我一个人复习不进去。同一页看了又看。要不我们每个星期都这样？星期四。你和我，再加上谁都行。”\n# en: A library alcove in the evening, two students sharing a single lamp over open books, an empty third chair pulled up.\nscene [d679a7] = 傍晚图书馆的壁龛里，两个学生在一盏灯下翻着书，旁边拉过来一把空着的第三把椅子。\n# en: \"Thursdays, then.\"\nopt.left.label [dd5103] = “那就星期四。”\n# en: You shake on it over the lamp. The first Thursday you get through a chapter each and an argument about it. The second Thursday someone else asks if the third chair is taken.\nopt.left.say.1 [19a427] = 你们隔着灯握手为定。第一个星期四，你们各读完一章，还为此吵了一架。第二个星期四，有人来问第三把椅子有没有人。\n# en: \"I work better alone.\"\nopt.right.label [b2a822] = “我一个人效率更高。”\n# en: {o} nods, and takes the lamp to another alcove. You get a great deal done. Every Thursday after that you see the lamp lit in the far alcove, and sometimes two heads under it.\nopt.right.say.1 [234137] = {o}点点头，把灯拿到另一个壁龛去了。你完成了很多。之后的每个星期四，你都能看见远处那个壁龛亮着灯，有时灯下是两个脑袋。\n\n@event circle_newcomer\n# en: The Fourth Chair\ntitle [bd4b61] = 第四把椅子\n# en: The circle is four now, sometimes five. Tonight {n} is hovering at the end of the table with an armful of books: {n}, whom nobody in the circle much likes, and who has, it turns out, failed the last two practicals. \"Could I sit in?\" {n} asks. The others look at you.\ntext [cb7a3d] = 学习会现在有四个人了，有时五个。今晚，{n}抱着一堆书在桌子尽头徘徊：学习会里没人太喜欢这个人，而且原来{n}最近两次实践课都没过。“我能坐下来一起吗？”{n}问。其他人都看着你。\n# en: A library table with three students around a lamp, a fourth standing a little way off with books clutched to their chest.\nscene [e380e9] = 图书馆的桌边，三个学生围着一盏灯，第四个人站在不远处，把书紧紧抱在胸前。\n# en: \"Pull up a chair.\"\nopt.left.label [368005] = “拉把椅子过来。”\n# en: {n} sits down. It's stiff for a week. Then {n} explains, better than any of you, the one thing all of you had wrong, and the circle shifts round to make room.\nopt.left.say.1 [5147b2] = {n}坐了下来。头一个星期很僵。后来{n}把一件你们大家都弄错了的事讲得比谁都明白，于是学习会挪了挪，腾出了位置。\n# en: \"We're full, sorry.\"\nopt.right.label [c199d1] = “抱歉，满了。”\n# en: {n} says \"of course\" and goes. The circle stays as it was, cosy and quick. You see {n} revising alone the next Thursday, at the far end of the room.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bfaa8] = {n}说了句“当然”，走了。学习会还是老样子，温馨又高效。下个星期四，你看见{n}一个人在房间另一头复习。\n\n@event circle_exam_night\n# en: The Night Before\ntitle [6e1f49] = 前一晚\n# en: The night before exams the circle sits till the lamps are trimmed, and it goes wrong. {w} hasn't done the notes {w.they} promised, and somebody says so, loudly, and {w} says some things back. Half the table is on its feet.\ntext [bf85b2] = 考试前一晚，学习会一直坐到灯芯都剪短了，然后出了问题。{w}没做完{w.they}答应要做的笔记，有人大声说了出来，{w}也回了几句难听的。半张桌子的人都站了起来。\n# en: A library table late at night strewn with notes and cold tea, students glaring at each other across it, one of them on their feet.\nscene [f23605] = 深夜的图书馆桌上散落着笔记和凉掉的茶，学生们隔着桌子怒目相视，其中一人站了起来。\n# en: Hold it together\nopt.left.label [6430cd] = 把大家拢住\n# en: You split {w}'s chapter four ways and start reading it aloud before anyone can leave. By midnight it's done, and {w} brings everyone breakfast the next morning without being asked.\nopt.left.say.1 [7982f1] = 你把{w}那一章分成四份，在任何人离开之前就开始大声朗读。到午夜就完成了，第二天早上{w}没人要求就给大家带来了早饭。\n# en: You try, but it's too late and too tired. The circle breaks up at the door. You revise alone till two. It comes back together after the exams, a little shy of itself.\nopt.left.say.2 [c2aca6] = 你试了，可太晚了，大家也太累了。学习会在门口散了。你一个人复习到两点。考完后它又凑了回来，彼此都有点不好意思。\n# en: Take {w}'s chapter yourself\nopt.right.label [2a8c0d] = 把{w}那一章揽下来\n# en: \"I'll do it,\" you say, and the table goes quiet. You're up till three. {w} sits up with you, not saying much, and makes the tea.\nopt.right.say.1 [4c7ba6] = “我来做，”你说，桌边安静了下来。你熬到了三点。{w}陪你坐着，没说什么，一直在泡茶。\n\n@event circle_last\n# en: The Carved Table\ntitle [870a51] = 刻字的桌子\n# en: The Thursday Circle has outlasted the term. It has its own table now, and its own lamp, which the librarian pretends not to know about. Someone has brought a penknife and suggests, in a whisper, that the table should have a name on it. Underneath, where nobody will see.\ntext [06ff03] = 星期四学习会撑过了这个学期。它现在有了自己的桌子，还有自己的灯，图书管理员假装不知道。有人带了把小刀，压低声音提议，这张桌子该刻上个名字。刻在底下，没人看得见的地方。\n# en: A library table in the evening, a group of students round a lamp, one of them carving something small into the underside of the table with a penknife.\nscene [d47f52] = 傍晚图书馆的桌边，一群学生围着一盏灯，其中一人正用小刀在桌子底面刻着什么小字。\n# en: Carve it\nopt.left.label [9be41d] = 刻上去\n# en: You carve \"THURSDAYS\" on the underside of the table, very small, while the others keep lookout. Years from now somebody will find it and wonder. You hope they start a circle.\nopt.left.say.1 [5fd719] = 你在桌子底面刻下小小的“星期四”，其他人帮你望风。多年以后，会有人发现它，好奇它的来历。你希望那人也办一个学习会。\n# en: Leave the table be\nopt.right.label [230a14] = 别动这张桌子\n# en: You talk them out of the penknife. Somebody writes \"Thursdays\" on the inside cover of a library book instead, in pencil, and puts it back on the shelf where it'll be found by the right person.\nopt.right.say.1 [fb555a] = 你劝他们收起了小刀。有人改用铅笔在一本图书馆藏书的封面内页写下“星期四”，再把书放回架上，等着对的人去发现。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/between.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/between.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event between_two_seats\n# en: Two Saved Seats\ntitle [a606ac] = 两个留好的座位\n# en: {~{a} has put a bag on the bench beside {them}, and at the far end {b} has put a book on the bench too. Both are for you.|{a} waves you over to a saved place. So, from the other end of the table, does {b}. They have not looked at each other once.|There are two places kept for you in the refectory, one at each end of the table, and the people keeping them would rather eat soup through a straw than sit together.}\ntext [ef3dfc] = {~{a}在{their}身边的长凳上放了个书包，桌子另一头，{b}也在长凳上放了本书。两个位子都是给你留的。|{a}朝你招手，示意给你留了位子。桌子另一头的{b}也在朝你招手。他们俩一眼都没看过对方。|膳堂里有两个位子给你留着，桌子两头各一个；留位子的两个人宁可用吸管喝汤，也不肯坐到一块儿。}\n# en: A long refectory table at mealtime, two students at opposite ends, each with a hand on an empty stretch of bench.\nscene [6ecc4a] = 饭点时膳堂里一张长桌，两个学生各坐一头，各自用手按着身边一段空长凳。\n# en: Sit with {a}\nopt.left.label [1864e2] = 坐到{a}那边\n# en: {~{a} moves the bag. Down the table, {b} moves the book back onto the bench, slowly, so you see.|{a} has saved you the crust. {b} eats facing the window.|You sit with {a}. {b} leaves before pudding, loudly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [35ac8c] = {~{a}挪开了书包。桌子那头，{b}慢慢地把书放回长凳上，故意让你看见。|{a}把面包皮给你留着。{b}一直对着窗户吃饭。|你坐到了{a}身边。{b}没等到布丁就走了，动静还不小。}\n# en: Pull them to one table\nopt.right.label [6fedd1] = 把他们拉到一张桌上\n# en: {~You carry your tray to the middle and wave them both in. They come, sulking, and argue about the gravy, which is at least talking.|You sit dead centre and start a story that needs both of them to finish it. By the end they're correcting each other instead of glaring.|You make them pass you things. Salt, bread, the jug. By the end they're passing each other things, which is how it starts.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4593c4] = {~你端着餐盘坐到中间，招手让两人都过来。他们来了，板着脸，为肉汁吵了一架——好歹算是在说话。|你坐在正中间，讲了个非得两个人一起才讲得完的故事。讲到最后，他们已经从瞪眼变成互相纠正了。|你让他们给你递东西。盐、面包、水壶。到最后，他们开始互相递东西了——事情往往就是这么开始的。}\n# en: {~You sit in the middle and they both stay where they are. You eat alone between two empty places, like a referee nobody asked for.|You try. {a} takes {their} tray to the window, {b} takes {b.theirs} to the door, and you're left with three puddings.|Neither of them moves. The whole table watches you eat. It's the longest meal of the term.}\nopt.right.say.2 [d64c84] = {~你坐到中间，可他们俩谁也没动。你一个人夹在两个空位之间吃饭，像个没人请来的裁判。|你试了。{a}把{their}餐盘端去了窗边，{b}把{b.theirs}端去了门口，留下你和三份布丁。|谁都没动。整张桌子都看着你吃饭。这是本学期最漫长的一顿饭。}\n\n@event between_messenger\n# en: Pass It On\ntitle [2bd77a] = 帮忙传个话\n# en: {~{a} and {b} aren't speaking, and you've become the post. \"Tell {b} the common-room lamp is NOT {b.theirs},\" says {a}.|{a} hands you a folded note for {b}. It isn't sealed. The first word is underlined three times.|\"Would you let {b} know,\" says {a}, very politely, \"that some of us have to sleep.\"}\ntext [79e5de] = {~{a}和{b}互不说话，你成了他们的邮差。“告诉{b}，公共休息室那盏灯可不是{b.theirs}，”{a}说。|{a}递给你一张折好的纸条，让你交给{b}。没封口。第一个词下面划了三道线。|“能不能请你转告{b}，”{a}非常客气地说，“有些人是要睡觉的。”}\n# en: A student in a corridor holding a folded note out at arm's length, another student turned pointedly away at the far end.\nscene [ae1c70] = 走廊里，一个学生伸长胳膊递出一张折好的纸条，走廊另一头，另一个学生刻意背过身去。\n# en: Pass it on word for word\nopt.left.label [9c53c3] = 一字不差地转达\n# en: {~You deliver it exactly. {b} sends one back, worse. By supper you've carried six, and you're tired in a way that has nothing to do with stairs.|You hand it over unread. {b} reads it, writes \"NO\" under it, and hands it back to you to carry.|You say it word for word. {b} says something word for word back. You're not paid enough for this, and you're not paid.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8b0885] = {~你一字不差地带了过去。{b}回了一张，措辞更难听。到晚饭时你已经跑了六趟，累得和爬楼梯毫无关系。|你没看就交了过去。{b}读完，在下面写了个“不”，又交给你带回去。|你一字一句地转达了。{b}也一字一句地回了话。这份差事给的钱不够——何况根本没钱。}\n# en: Soften it on the way\nopt.right.label [90adec] = 路上替他们改软一点\n# en: {~By the time it reaches {b} it's a polite question about the lamp. {b} answers politely. {a} is baffled, and a little disarmed.|You lose the underlining somewhere on the stairs. {b} reads a reasonable note and writes a reasonable answer. Nobody knows why it worked.|\"Would you mind\" becomes \"would you mind, please\". It's enough. They nod at each other at breakfast.}\nopt.right.say.1 [122ed5] = {~等传到{b}手里，已经变成一句关于那盏灯的客气询问。{b}也客气地答了。{a}一头雾水，气也消了一点。|那几道下划线不知在哪段楼梯上弄丢了。{b}读到一张讲理的纸条，回了一张讲理的答复。谁也不知道怎么就成了。|“你介意吗”变成了“请问你介意吗”。这就够了。第二天早饭时，两人互相点了点头。}\n# en: {~They compare notes, literally. Neither recognises what you delivered. For once they agree on something: you.|{b} asks {a} where the underlining went. Both of them turn to look at you.|It comes out at supper. They're united, briefly, in being annoyed with the post.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1283cb] = {~他们真的拿纸条对了一遍。谁都认不出你送去的是什么。难得一回，两人意见一致：都冲着你来。|{b}问{a}那几道下划线哪儿去了。两人一起转过头来看你。|晚饭时事情败露了。他们短暂地团结了起来——一起生邮差的气。}\n\n@event between_whose_idea\n# en: Whose Idea Was It\ntitle [43cb9c] = 到底是谁的主意\n# en: The lecturer praises the trick that made last week's practical work, and asks whose it was. {a} says \"mine\", and so does {b}, at the same moment. You were there when {a} thought of it, on the back of an envelope. Both of them look at you.\ntext [6fc982] = 讲师表扬了让上周实践课成功的那个窍门，问是谁想出来的。{a}说“我”，{b}也在同一刻说“我”。{a}在信封背面想出这个主意时，你就在旁边。两人都看向了你。\n# en: Two students in front of a lecturer's desk, both pointing at the same page of notes, a third student standing just behind them.\nscene [957c85] = 讲台前站着两个学生，都指着同一页笔记，第三个学生就站在他们身后。\n# en: \"It was {a}'s.\"\nopt.left.label [d6a4d8] = “是{a}想的。”\n# en: {b} goes red to the ears. {a} gets the praise and, later, buys you a cake. {b} doesn't speak to either of you until the end of the week, and is extremely polite when {b.their} silence ends.\nopt.left.say.1 [722392] = {b}红到了耳根。{a}得了表扬，后来还请你吃了块蛋糕。{b}直到周末都不跟你们俩说话，沉默结束后又客气得出奇。\n# en: \"We worked it out together.\"\nopt.right.label [c06886] = “是我们一起想出来的。”\n# en: The lecturer gives the three of you the credit and moves on. {b} looks relieved. {a} looks at you for a long moment, and doesn't mention the envelope again.\nopt.right.say.1 [c549cc] = 讲师把功劳记在你们三个人头上，接着讲课。{b}松了口气。{a}看了你很久，后来再没提过那个信封。\n\n@event between_boot_hex\n# en: Dancing Boots\ntitle [425b3a] = 跳舞的靴子\n# en: {~{b}'s boots have started to dance, on their own, in the middle of the corridor.|{b}'s boots squelch at every step, though it hasn't rained for a week.|{b}'s laces have tied themselves together, in a bow, very neatly.} You saw {a} flick a wand at them. So did nobody else. \"Who did this?\" {b} demands, of the corridor, and then of you.\ntext [c2d479] = {~{b}的靴子在走廊正中间自己跳起舞来。|{b}的靴子每走一步都咕叽作响，可已经一个星期没下雨了。|{b}的鞋带自己打成了一个蝴蝶结，打得还很整齐。}你看见{a}朝那双靴子弹了一下魔杖。别人都没看见。“谁干的？”{b}先冲着整条走廊质问，然后冲着你。\n# en: A corridor where one student's boots are jigging along the flagstones on their own, the student in them furious, another student by a pillar looking innocent.\nscene [28aacc] = 走廊里，一个学生的靴子自顾自地在石板上跳着吉格舞，穿靴子的人气急败坏，柱子旁另一个学生一脸无辜。\n# en: Tell {b} the truth\nopt.left.label [a0b54c] = 告诉{b}实话\n# en: {~{b} stalks off to find {a}, still jigging. You hear about the rematch at supper.|{b} squelches off towards {a}. You don't stay to watch.|{b} hops away in search of {a}, still tied. There's a prefect involved by lunchtime.}\nopt.left.say.1 [33d368] = {~{b}一边跳着一边气冲冲地去找{a}了。晚饭时你听说了这场报复战。|{b}咕叽咕叽地朝{a}走去。你没留下来看。|{b}单脚蹦着去找{a}，鞋带还绑着。到午饭时，已经惊动了级长。}\n# en: \"I didn't see a thing.\"\nopt.right.label [52d462] = “我什么都没看见。”\n# en: {~{b} dances off to the infirmary. {a} winks at you across the corridor, and you find you don't like being winked at.|{b} squelches away, sure it was someone else. {a} grins. It sits badly at lunch.|{b} sits on the floor and unpicks the bow with dignity. {a} owes you, and you'd rather {they} didn't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d6deb4] = {~{b}一路跳着去了医务室。{a}隔着走廊朝你眨了眨眼，你发现自己并不喜欢被人这样眨眼。|{b}咕叽咕叽地走了，认定是别人干的。{a}咧嘴一笑。这事让你午饭都吃得不是滋味。|{b}坐在地上，体面地解开了那个蝴蝶结。{a}欠了你一个人情，而你宁愿{they}没欠。}\n\n@event between_not_them\n# en: Not Them Again\ntitle [4f6da4] = 又是他们\n# en: {a} catches you at the common-room door. \"Every time you sit with me, {b} comes too,\" {they} say{s}. \"I'm not asking you to choose. I'm just asking you to stop bringing {b}.\" Over by the fire, {b} looks up, and smiles at you.\ntext [961c40] = {a}在公共休息室门口拦住了你。“每次你跟我坐，{b}都跟着来，”{they}说，“我不是要你选。我只是想请你别再带上{b}。”壁炉边，{b}抬起头，朝你笑了笑。\n# en: Two students talking low in a common-room doorway, one glancing over the other's shoulder at a third by the fire.\nscene [4edcd4] = 公共休息室门口，两个学生低声交谈，其中一人越过对方的肩膀，看向壁炉边的第三个人。\n# en: Keep bringing {b}\nopt.left.label [c143c8] = 照样带上{b}\n# en: \"Then I'll sit somewhere else,\" says {a}, and does, for a week. {b} never knows it was about {b.them}. You do.\nopt.left.say.1 [32e55e] = “那我就坐别处去，”{a}说，接下来一个星期果真如此。{b}始终不知道这事跟{b.them}有关。你知道。\n# en: Quietly leave {b} out\nopt.right.label [c60d87] = 悄悄不带{b}\n# en: You start meeting {a} in the library, where {b} never goes. It works. {b} asks once, cheerfully, where you've been. You say \"around\", which is true and isn't.\nopt.right.say.1 [b8b569] = 你开始和{a}约在图书馆见面，{b}从来不去那儿。这招管用。{b}只问过一次，语气轻快，问你最近去哪儿了。你说“到处转转”——这话不假，却也不真。\n\n@event between_peace_offering\n# en: A Peace Offering\ntitle [3e57a4] = 求和的礼物\n# en: {a} has a parcel in one hand and a letter in the other, and the look of someone who's been carrying both for days. \"It's for {b}. I was out of order. I said something I can't take back.\" {They} hold{s} them out. \"Would you give {b.them} these? I can't face it.\"\ntext [d7d93a] = {a}一手拿着个包裹，一手拿着封信，看样子已经把这两样东西揣了好几天。“是给{b}的。我那次太过分了。我说了收不回来的话。”{they}把东西递过来。“你能替我交给{b.them}吗？我没脸去。”\n# en: A student holding out a small wrapped parcel and a folded letter, looking anywhere but at the person they're handing it to.\nscene [3d3a03] = 一个学生递出一个包好的小包裹和一封折好的信，眼睛看着哪儿都行，就是不看接东西的人。\n# en: Make {a} do it in person\nopt.left.label [fc12ae] = 让{a}亲自去\n# en: You walk {a} there yourself, all the way to the door. It takes {them} two goes to say it. {b} takes the parcel, and then, after a long moment, takes {a}'s hand as well. You leave them to it.\nopt.left.say.1 [768295] = 你亲自把{a}送了过去，一直送到门口。{they}试了两次才把话说出口。{b}接过包裹，过了好一会儿，又握住了{a}的手。你让他们俩自己说去了。\n# en: {a} gets as far as the door, then turns round and walks straight back past you. \"I said I couldn't,\" {they} say{s}. The parcel goes back in a drawer.\nopt.left.say.2 [6857ca] = {a}走到门口，转身又从你身边径直走了回来。“我说了我做不到，”{they}说。包裹被放回了抽屉里。\n# en: Carry it for {a}\nopt.right.label [247acd] = 替{a}送去\n# en: You spend the evening you meant for revising finding {b}, who reads the letter twice and says \"Tell {a} I'll think about it.\" That's not nothing. You say so.\nopt.right.say.1 [7b813c] = 你把原本用来复习的晚上花在了找{b}上。对方把信读了两遍，说：“告诉{a}，我会考虑的。”这不算什么都没有。你也这么转告了。\n\n@event between_seconds\n# en: Seconds\ntitle [12f48a] = 决斗助手\n# en: What started between {a} and {b} over a library book has become a matter of honour: a formal challenge, dawn, the far lawn. Neither will back down in front of the other, and {a} has asked you, of all people, to stand at {their} shoulder. \"You only have to be there. And if I lose, don't let me hear about it.\"\ntext [6dfecd] = {a}和{b}之间起于一本图书馆借书的争执，如今成了荣誉之争：正式挑战，黎明，远处的草坪。两人谁也不肯在对方面前退让，而{a}偏偏请你站在{their}身边。“你只要在场就行。要是我输了，别让我听见有人提。”\n# en: Dawn on a dew-wet lawn, two students with wands squared off, a third standing at one of their shoulders in the mist.\nscene [168981] = 黎明时沾满露水的草坪上，两个学生举着魔杖对峙，第三个人站在其中一人肩旁，没在雾里。\n# en: Stand as {a}'s second\nopt.left.label [eaafe8] = 做{a}的决斗助手\n# en: You stand at {a}'s shoulder {season == \"winter\": with your teeth chattering|in the wet grass}. The duel is short, loud and inconclusive; a stray hex singes your sleeve. {a} shakes your hand afterwards like a general. Half the dormitory heard about it by breakfast.\nopt.left.say.1 [828aae] = 你站在{a}肩旁，{season == \"winter\": 冻得牙齿打战|脚下是湿漉漉的草}。决斗短促、吵闹、不分胜负；一道流弹咒烧焦了你的袖子。事后{a}像将军一样跟你握手。到早饭时，半个宿舍都听说了。\n# en: Talk them out of it at dawn\nopt.right.label [ce517c] = 黎明时劝他们罢手\n# en: You stand between them on the {season == \"winter\": frosted|wet} grass and talk until the sun is up and both of them are too {season == \"winter\": cold|hungry} to want anything but toast. Nobody wins. Nobody has to.\nopt.right.say.1 [4765b3] = 你站在{season == \"winter\": 结霜|湿漉漉}的草地上，挡在两人中间，一直说到太阳升起，说到他们{season == \"winter\": 冻|饿}得只想吃吐司。没人赢。也没人非得赢。\n# en: They duel anyway, around you. You're not a very good wall. {a} says you embarrassed {them}, and {b} doesn't say anything, which is worse.\nopt.right.say.2 [c68c0c] = 他们还是绕过你打了起来。你这堵墙不太称职。{a}说你让{them}丢了脸；{b}什么都没说，这更糟。\n\n@event between_dorm_vote\n# en: The Common-Room Vote\ntitle [a858e1] = 公共休息室的投票\n# en: {~The common room is voting on whether the window stays open at night. {a} is leading the cold side, {b} the warm one.|Today's vote: who gets the good armchair by the fire on exam weeks. {a} has a rota. {b} has a counter-rota.|The common room votes on the portrait over the fire, which snores: turned to the wall at night, or not. {a} says turn it. {b} would sooner die than agree with {a}.} It's tied. Your hand will decide it.\ntext [a956e2] = {~公共休息室正在投票表决夜里窗户开不开。{a}领着怕热的一方，{b}领着怕冷的一方。|今天的议题：考试周里壁炉边那把好扶手椅归谁。{a}排了张轮值表。{b}排了张反轮值表。|公共休息室要表决壁炉上方那幅会打呼噜的画像夜里要不要转过去面朝墙。{a}说转。{b}宁死也不肯同意{a}。}票数持平。你这一票说了算。\n# en: A crowded common room, two students standing on chairs at opposite ends making speeches, everyone else holding up hands.\nscene [ce48e2] = 挤满人的公共休息室，两个学生分别站在两头的椅子上发表演说，其他人都举着手。\n# en: Vote with {a}\nopt.left.label [5da9a4] = 投{a}一票\n# en: {~The window stays open. {b} sleeps in two jumpers and a grievance.|The rota stands. {b} reads in the cold chair for a month and makes a point of it.|The portrait goes to the wall. {b} turns it back every night, on principle, and apologises to it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2ab723] = {~窗户开着。{b}穿着两件毛衣、揣着一肚子怨气睡觉。|轮值表照旧。{b}整整一个月都坐在冷椅子上看书，还特意让人看见。|画像转向了墙。{b}每晚都把它转回来，出于原则，还向它道歉。}\n# en: Propose a middle way\nopt.right.label [220580] = 提个折中的办法\n# en: {~You say the window should be open on even nights, and suddenly that's what everyone wants. Nobody's happy. Everyone's slightly warmer.|You say the chair should go to whoever's actually revising. They both look at you as if you've cheated.|You say the portrait can face the wall on exam weeks only. It's a coward's answer. It passes unanimously, and the portrait sulks.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b8fa0a] = {~你说窗户单数夜开、双数夜关——忽然大家都想要这个了。没人满意。每个人都暖和了一点。|你说椅子应该归真正在复习的人。两人看你的眼神，像你作了弊。|你说画像只在考试周面朝墙。这是个胆小鬼的答案。全票通过，画像生起了闷气。}\n\n@event between_lab_partners\n# en: A Bench for Three\ntitle [5c410d] = 三人一张台\n# en: {~The alchemy practical comes in threes, and the lecturer has put you at a bench with {a} and {b}.|A bench for three, a cauldron for one, and {a} and {b} have taken the two stools furthest from each other.|You're paired again: you, {a}, {b}, and one set of scales.} By the second step they're working two separate halves of the recipe and arguing through you.\ntext [576d6c] = {~炼金实践课三人一组，讲师把你和{a}、{b}分到了一张工作台。|三个人一张台，一口坩埚，{a}和{b}占了离彼此最远的两张凳子。|你又被分到一组：你、{a}、{b}，外加一杆秤。}到第二步时，他们已经各做各的半份配方，还隔着你吵架。\n# en: A practical-class workbench, three students around one bubbling cauldron, two of them very carefully not passing each other anything.\nscene [72c4ac] = 实践课的工作台，三个学生围着一口冒泡的坩埚，其中两个小心翼翼地不给对方递任何东西。\n# en: Do the work yourself\nopt.left.label [c9d72d] = 自己全包了\n# en: {~You do the whole thing while they sulk at the ends of the bench. It comes out right, and you're the only one who knows how.|You make the brew. They each claim the other ruined it. It's perfectly fine.|You weigh, stir and bottle it alone. The mark is good. The afternoon was not.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e8d77f] = {~他们在台子两头生闷气，你一个人把活全干了。药剂做对了，而只有你知道是怎么做成的。|你熬好了药。他们都说是对方搞砸的。其实完全没问题。|你一个人称量、搅拌、装瓶。分数不错。这个下午不怎么样。}\n# en: Make them work it together\nopt.right.label [aa3269] = 让他们一起干\n# en: {~You give {a} the stirring and {b} the timing, and make them call it out to each other. It works. The cauldron doesn't explode, and neither do they.|You hand each of them one half of the recipe. They have to read it out to each other. By the end {b} is laughing at {a}'s pronunciation, and {a} lets {b.them}.|You stand back with your arms folded until they talk. Eventually, they do. The brew is late, and passable.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7425fc] = {~你让{a}负责搅拌，{b}负责计时，逼他们互相报数。成了。坩埚没炸，他们俩也没炸。|你把配方一人一半分给他们。他们只能互相念给对方听。到最后，{b}笑话起{a}的发音，{a}也由着{b.them}笑。|你抱着胳膊站在一旁，直到他们开口说话。最终，他们开口了。药剂交晚了，勉强及格。}\n# en: {~They won't. The brew curdles while they argue about whose fault the curdling is.|The cauldron boils over while {a} and {b} each wait for the other to move it.|You try to referee and the brew goes grey. All three of you get the same bad mark, which is, at least, fair.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1c5f37] = {~他们就是不肯。药剂结了块，他们还在争是谁害它结了块。|{a}和{b}都等着对方去挪坩埚，结果它沸溢了。|你想当裁判，药剂却变成了灰色。三个人拿了同样的低分，至少还算公平。}\n\n@event between_cipher\n# en: The Cipher\ntitle [a7fefb] = 密码\n# en: {a} and {b} have been passing notes all term in a cipher of their own: little drawings that stand for words, a fish for \"boring\", a tower for \"Thorne\". You've watched them laugh at blank-looking scraps for weeks. Today {b} slides one to you. It's the key, and it's a test.\ntext [4f45be] = {a}和{b}整个学期都在用自创的密码传纸条：用小图画代表词语，一条鱼代表“无聊”，一座塔代表“索恩”。你已经看着他们对着看似空白的纸片笑了好几个星期。今天，{b}把一张纸条推给了你。是密码表，也是一场考验。\n# en: Two students passing a scrap of paper covered in little drawn symbols under a lecture-hall desk, a third leaning in to see.\nscene [d9463c] = 讲堂的课桌底下，两个学生传递着一张画满小符号的纸片，第三个人凑过去看。\n# en: Learn the cipher\nopt.left.label [1d4945] = 学会这套密码\n# en: You spend a lecture you should have spent listening learning forty little drawings instead. By the end of it you can read \"this lecture is a fish\" from three rows away, and the three of you have a language nobody else in the castle speaks.\nopt.left.say.1 [80da79] = 本该听课的一节课，你用来记了四十个小图画。下课时，你已经能隔着三排读出“这节课是条鱼”，而你们三个有了一门全城堡没人会说的语言。\n# en: Hand the key back\nopt.right.label [939e43] = 把密码表还回去\n# en: \"I'd only get you both caught,\" you whisper, and slide it back. {b} shrugs and tucks it away. The notes go on passing in front of you, and you find you'd quite like to know what the fish means.\nopt.right.say.1 [fc8a85] = “我只会害你们俩被抓，”你小声说，把纸条推了回去。{b}耸耸肩，把它收好了。纸条照样在你面前传来传去，你发现自己挺想知道那条鱼是什么意思的。\n\n@event between_inside_joke\n# en: The Joke You Missed\ntitle [c90ac9] = 你没听懂的笑话\n# en: {~{b} says \"the goose\" and {a} has to sit down on the stairs.|{a} hums four notes and {b} snorts tea out of {b.their} nose.|{b} only has to raise one eyebrow and {a} is gone, helpless, crying with laughter.} You have no idea why. They look at you, still laughing, and wait to see what you'll do.\ntext [ad340c] = {~{b}说了句“那只鹅”，{a}笑得只好在楼梯上坐下。|{a}哼了四个音，{b}一口茶从鼻子里喷了出来。|{b}只消挑一下眉毛，{a}就彻底不行了，笑得眼泪直流。}你完全不知道为什么。他们看着你，还在笑，等着看你怎么办。\n# en: Two students doubled over with laughter on a staircase, a third standing a step below them, smiling politely.\nscene [ebf5ae] = 两个学生在楼梯上笑弯了腰，第三个人站在下面一级台阶上，礼貌地微笑着。\n# en: Laugh along anyway\nopt.left.label [b615b4] = 跟着笑就是了\n# en: {~You laugh. {a} can tell you've no idea, and it's somehow worse than not laughing.|You laugh along, too late and a bit too loud. {b} pats your shoulder, kindly.|You laugh. They stop. \"You don't know, do you,\" says {a}, delighted.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7fac4c] = {~你笑了。{a}看得出你根本不懂，这不知怎么比不笑还糟。|你跟着笑，笑晚了，还笑得太大声。{b}好心地拍了拍你的肩。|你笑了。他们停了下来。“你不知道，对吧，”{a}说，乐坏了。}\n# en: \"All right. What's the joke?\"\nopt.right.label [3bcf3b] = “好吧。到底什么这么好笑？”\n# en: {~It takes ten minutes to explain, involves a professor and a pond, and is about half as funny told. But now it's yours too, and the next time someone says \"the goose\" you're the one on the stairs.|They explain. It's about you, a little, from your first week. You laugh anyway. It is quite funny.|\"You had to be there,\" says {b}. Then they make you be there, re-enacting the whole thing on the landing. You're in it now.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e161a3] = {~解释花了十分钟，牵扯到一位教授和一个池塘，讲出来只有一半好笑。可现在它也是你的了，下次有人说“那只鹅”，坐在楼梯上的就是你。|他们解释了。这事有一点是关于你的，出自你入学第一周。你还是笑了。确实挺好笑。|“你得在场才懂，”{b}说。然后他们硬是让你“在场”了一回，在楼梯平台上把整件事重演了一遍。现在你也是其中一员了。}\n\n@event between_cover_roll\n# en: Answering for Two\ntitle [f14798] = 替人答到\n# en: Roll call. {b}'s seat is empty, and {a} leans over. \"{b}'s not ill, {b}'s just not here. One more absence and it's a letter home. Answer for {b.them}? I can't, the lecturer knows my voice.\" The lecturer is three names away.\ntext [779176] = 点名了。{b}的座位空着，{a}凑过来。“{b}没病，{b}只是不在。再缺一次，信就要寄回家了。你替{b.them}答一声？我不行，讲师认得我的声音。”讲师离这儿还有三个名字。\n# en: A lecture hall at the start of class, a lecturer reading names from a register, two students whispering in the third row beside an empty seat.\nscene [51fc7b] = 上课伊始的讲堂，讲师照着名册点名，第三排一张空座位旁，两个学生在窃窃私语。\n# en: \"Present.\"\nopt.left.label [98e6cc] = “到。”\n# en: You say it in a voice you hope is {b}'s. The lecturer ticks the register without looking up. {b} buys you both breakfast for a week and never tells you where {b.they} went.\nopt.left.say.1 [fff9d7] = 你用一种希望像{b}的声音答了。讲师头也没抬就在名册上打了勾。{b}请你们俩吃了一个星期的早饭，却始终没告诉你{b.they}去了哪儿。\n# en: The lecturer looks up. \"Remarkable, how {b} has your face.\" It's a detention for you and the letter home for {b} anyway. {b} is grateful, which almost makes it worse.\nopt.left.say.2 [240daf] = 讲师抬起头。“真神奇，{b}居然长着你的脸。”你被罚了禁闭，{b}的信照样寄回了家。{b}很感激你，这几乎让事情更糟。\n# en: Stay quiet\nopt.right.label [9adbd8] = 不出声\n# en: The name hangs in the air and goes down as absent. {a} doesn't look at you for the rest of the lecture. {b} gets the letter home, and turns up the next day, and never asks who didn't answer.\nopt.right.say.1 [c12f86] = 那个名字悬在半空，被记作缺席。整节课{a}都没再看你一眼。家信寄了出去；第二天{b}照常来上课，从没问过是谁没替{b.them}答到。\n\n@event between_closed_table\n# en: Saved, Sorry\ntitle [f46bb5] = 有人了，抱歉\n# en: You, {a} and {b} have had the corner table for a term now. A first-year with a full tray and nowhere to sit asks if the fourth place is free. {a} and {b} exchange a look you know very well. It means \"no\".\ntext [851766] = 你、{a}和{b}占着角落那张桌子已经一个学期了。一个端着满满一盘饭、无处可坐的一年级生问第四个位子有没有人。{a}和{b}交换了一个你再熟悉不过的眼神。意思是“没空”。\n# en: A corner table in a busy refectory, three friends crowded round it, a first-year with a full tray hovering at the end.\nscene [5a8f9f] = 热闹膳堂里的一张角落餐桌，三个朋友挤在桌边，一个端着满盘饭菜的一年级生在桌尾踌躇。\n# en: Make room\nopt.left.label [d02342] = 腾个位子\n# en: You shove your bag along and the first-year sits down, pink with relief. {a} is cool for the length of the meal. By the end of the week the first-year is the one who saves the table, and {b} has adopted them.\nopt.left.say.1 [d2c528] = 你把书包往边上一推，那个一年级生坐了下来，如释重负，脸都红了。整顿饭{a}都有点冷淡。到周末时，已经是那个一年级生在给大家占桌子了，而{b}也把这孩子收作了小跟班。\n# en: \"Sorry, it's saved.\"\nopt.right.label [c762ea] = “抱歉，有人了。”\n# en: The first-year says \"oh, of course,\" and walks away very straight. The corner stays yours. {a} relaxes. You find you can't remember what the fourth place was ever saved for.\nopt.right.say.1 [d25e43] = 一年级生说了句“哦，当然”，挺直了背走开了。角落还是你们的。{a}放松下来。你发现自己想不起来，第四个位子当初到底是给谁留的。\n\n@event between_night_roof\n# en: The Roof at Night\ntitle [b7c65d] = 夜里的屋顶\n# en: {~After lights out, {a} and {b} are halfway out of the window with a blanket and a stolen jar of honey.|{a} taps on your door after lights out. {b} is behind {them}, holding the roof-ladder.|It's a clear night, and {a} and {b} are already on the roof. A hand appears at your window.} \"Come on,\" whispers {a}. \"The stars are out and the prefects aren't.\"\ntext [61cb97] = {~熄灯之后，{a}和{b}抱着一条毯子和一罐偷来的蜂蜜，已经翻出了半个窗户。|熄灯后，{a}敲响你的门。{b}跟在{them}身后，扛着上屋顶的梯子。|今夜晴朗，{a}和{b}早已上了屋顶。一只手出现在你的窗前。}“快来，”{a}低声说，“星星出来了，级长没出来。”\n# en: Two students halfway out of a dormitory window onto a moonlit roof, one of them holding a hand back in for a third.\nscene [55e92a] = 月光下，两个学生已经半个身子探出宿舍窗外、爬上屋顶，其中一人把手伸回窗内，去拉第三个人。\n# en: Climb out with them\nopt.left.label [185840] = 跟他们爬出去\n# en: {~You sit on the slates until the honey's gone and you've named three constellations wrongly. Tomorrow will be long. You don't mind yet.|Up on the roof the three of you say the kind of things you only say at that height. You're tired all the next day, and it was worth it.|The slates are freezing and the view is everything. You get in at three, and nobody catches you, this time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6e3b65] = {~你们坐在石板瓦上，直到蜂蜜吃光，认错了三个星座。明天会很漫长。眼下你还不在乎。|在屋顶上，你们三个说了些只有在那个高度才说得出口的话。第二天你困了一整天，值了。|石板瓦冰凉，景色却是一切。你凌晨三点才溜回来，这一回没人抓到你。}\n# en: Stay in bed\nopt.right.label [50ea64] = 待在床上\n# en: {~You hear them laughing above your ceiling until late. You sleep well. You're not sure that was the point.|\"Your loss,\" whispers {b}, and the window clicks shut. In the morning they share a look you aren't in.|You stay warm. At breakfast they're yawning and pleased with themselves, and talk about something called \"the honey incident\".}\nopt.right.say.1 [f6da30] = {~你听见他们在你天花板上面笑到很晚。你睡得很好。你不确定这是不是重点。|“你的损失，”{b}小声说，窗户咔哒一声关上了。早上，他们交换了一个你不在其中的眼神。|你暖暖和和的。早饭时他们哈欠连天，又得意洋洋，聊着一件叫“蜂蜜事件”的事。}\n\n@event between_falling_out\n# en: Both Sides\ntitle [4e9822] = 各执一词\n# en: {a} and {b} have fallen out, properly, over something neither will name. Each has told you their side by lunchtime, and the sides don't match. \"You'll tell {b} I'm right,\" says {a}. An hour later, {b} says exactly the same about {a}.\ntext [0cf4c6] = {a}和{b}闹翻了，闹得很僵，为的是一件谁都不肯说的事。到午饭时，两人都跟你讲了各自的说法，而说法对不上。“你会告诉{b}我是对的，”{a}说。一小时后，{b}说起{a}，一字不差。\n# en: Two students on opposite sides of a cloister garden, arms folded, each with their back to the other, a third student on the path between them.\nscene [728282] = 回廊花园的两侧站着两个学生，都抱着胳膊，背对着彼此，第三个学生站在他们之间的小径上。\n# en: Get them in one room\nopt.left.label [0e8eb1] = 把他们弄到一个屋里\n# en: You book the window seat in the library and send each of them a note that says the other one is sorry. Neither was. By the time they've worked that out, they're arguing about you instead, and it's over.\nopt.left.say.1 [47759e] = 你订下了图书馆的窗边座，给两人各送了张纸条，说对方很抱歉。其实谁都没有。等他们弄明白这一点，已经转而一起数落你了——事情也就过去了。\n# en: They come, see each other, and leave by different doors. Each of them thinks you took the other's side. For a while you have two friends who don't talk to each other, and both talk to you about it.\nopt.left.say.2 [405dee] = 他们来了，看见彼此，从两扇门各自走了。两人都以为你站在了对方那边。有一阵子，你有了两个彼此不说话、却都跟你说这件事的朋友。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.right.label [03d24a] = 置身事外\n# en: You listen to both, agree with neither, and keep your head down. It takes them a month to start speaking again, and they never quite sit together the way they did. You wonder sometimes if a word from you would have done it.\nopt.right.say.1 [7dce05] = 你两边都听，谁也不附和，低着头过日子。他们花了一个月才重新说上话，再也没像从前那样坐在一起。有时你会想，要是你说一句话，会不会就好了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/bonds/friends.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/bonds/friends.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lyra_who_beat_her\n# en: Who Beat Her\ntitle [dd3b8e] = 谁赢了她\n# en: Lyra is on a window seat with an old duelling programme, soft from being folded and unfolded. The Winter Trials, the year before she came here. Second place: L. Vaneshade. \"My mother wrote the week after,\" she says, without looking up. \"Not to ask how I was. To ask who beat me.\"\ntext [22c475] = 莱拉坐在窗台上，手里一份旧决斗秩序册，折了又展、展了又折，已经软了。冬季考核，她来这里的前一年。第二名：L.凡妮希德。“一周后我母亲写信来，”她头也不抬地说，“不是问我怎么样。是问谁赢了我。”\n# en: A red-haired student on a window seat holding an old folded duelling programme, soft from handling, her thumb over one line.\nscene [b1650e] = 一个红发学生坐在窗台上，手里一份翻得发软的旧决斗秩序册，拇指按着其中一行。\n# en: Ask who did\nopt.left.label [2247f3] = 问是谁赢了她\n# en: \"A boy from a fishing town who never duelled again,\" she says. \"He was better that day. I've thought about him every day since.\" She folds the programme along its old creases. \"I hope he's happy. I hope he never thinks about it at all.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [f2c291] = “一个渔村来的男孩，后来再没决斗过，”她说，“那天他比我强。从那以后我每天都会想起他。”她把秩序册沿着旧折痕折好。“我希望他过得开心。我希望他压根不会想起这件事。”\n# en: Ask what she wrote back\nopt.right.label [c58ac6] = 问她回信写了什么\n# en: \"His name,\" Lyra says. \"What else?\" She walks away. Later you see her at the practice ring, alone, long after the torches are out.\nopt.right.say.1 [43dfee] = “他的名字，”莱拉说，“不然还能写什么？”她走开了。后来你看见她独自在练习场，火把灭了很久，她还在。\n\n@event lyra_well_done\n# en: Well Done\ntitle [842677] = 做得好\n# en: Lyra comes second in an exhibition bout, to a fourth-year twice her size, and comes off the ring with her face set like stone. Nobody says anything to her. They know better. You know her mother has never once said the two words.\ntext [607a59] = 莱拉在一场表演赛里输给一个比她高大一倍的四年级生，拿了第二，下场时脸绷得像石头。没人跟她说话。大家都知道最好别说。你知道，她母亲从没对她说过那三个字。\n# en: A practice ring at dusk, a red-haired duellist sitting on the edge with her wand across her knees, another student holding out a cup of water.\nscene [c024a3] = 黄昏的练习场，一个红发决斗者坐在场边，魔杖横在膝上，另一个学生递过去一杯水。\n# en: Say them\nopt.left.label [779a02] = 说出那三个字\n# en: \"Well done,\" you say. She looks at you as if you've spoken another language. Then she sits down very suddenly on the edge of the ring, and laughs, and doesn't stop for a while, and when she does her eyes are wet. \"Don't,\" she says. \"Don't you dare tell anyone.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [e1bf8d] = “做得好。”你说。她看着你，好像你说的是外语。然后她一下子坐在场边，笑了起来，笑了好一阵，停下时眼睛湿了。“别，”她说，“你敢告诉任何人试试。”\n# en: Spar with her until she's won something\nopt.right.label [8b09bc] = 陪她对练到她赢为止\n# en: You don't say anything. You pick up a practice wand. She beats you four times running, harder each time, and after the fourth she's breathing easier. \"Thank you,\" she says, which is as close as she gets.\nopt.right.say.1 [463665] = 你什么都没说，拿起一根练习杖。她连赢你四局，一局比一局狠，到第四局她的呼吸平顺了。“谢谢。”她说——这已经是她能说到的极限。\n\n@event lyra_study_hall_truce\n# en: The Study Hall Truce\ntitle [4a0fc5] = 自习室停战协定\n# en: Lyra has built a wall of books down the middle of your library table. \"My side,\" she says. \"Your side. No talking.\" {~Twenty minutes later she slides a note over the wall: \"What did Thorne mean by the second law?\"|An hour in, a biscuit appears on your side of the wall, unexplained.}\ntext [63f9f1] = 莱拉在你们的图书馆长桌中间用书垒了一道墙。“我这边，”她说，“你那边。不许说话。”{~二十分钟后，她从墙上递过来一张纸条：“索恩说的第二定律是什么意思？”|一个钟头后，你这边莫名其妙地多了一块饼干。}\n# en: Two students at opposite ends of a long library table, a line of books stacked down the middle like a wall, both pretending not to look over.\nscene [fb20e7] = 两个学生坐在一张图书馆长桌的两端，桌子中间一摞书垒成一道墙，两人都假装不往对面看。\n# en: Answer over the wall\nopt.left.label [fcc080] = 隔着墙回答她\n# en: {~You write the answer and slide it back. She writes a better one and slides that back. By closing time the wall is gone and you've both written an essay neither of you could have alone.|You pass notes over the wall all evening, half about Thorne and half not. She keeps the notes. You see them later, pinned in her book.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2db8ed] = {~你写好答案递回去。她写了个更好的递回来。到闭馆时墙已经没了，你们合写出一篇谁也写不出来的论文。|你们隔着墙传了一晚上纸条，一半关于索恩，一半不是。她把纸条都留着。后来你看见它们夹在她的书里。}\n# en: Near midnight you slide one more note over the wall: \"Bed.\" She reads it twice, glares at you, and, for once, goes.\nopt.left.say.2 [a3c67d] = 快到午夜时，你又从书墙那边递过去一张字条：“睡觉。”她读了两遍，瞪了你一眼，然后破天荒地去睡了。\n# en: Keep to your side\nopt.right.label [419f2b] = 守住你那边\n# en: {~You keep to your side and get more done than in a week. So does she. At closing she looks almost disappointed that you didn't break the truce.|Silence, all evening. Your notes have never been better. Lyra packs up first and doesn't say goodnight, then comes back and says it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c36156] = {~你守着你那边，一晚上干的活比一周还多。她也是。闭馆时她看上去几乎有点失望你没打破协定。|一整晚都很安静。你的笔记从没这么好过。莱拉先收拾好，没说晚安，又折回来说了。}\n\n@event lyra_become_close\n# en: Let It Run\ntitle [a021c6] = 由它燃吧\n# en: It's 3am. You find Lyra on the battlements, making a small flame walk across her knuckles. \"I can't sleep,\" she says. \"I've never told anyone that. Vaneshades sleep exactly eight hours.\" The flame wavers. \"My grandmother had a spell she only taught to people she trusted. I've never had anyone to teach it to.\"\ntext [858d17] = 凌晨三点。你在城垛上找到莱拉，她让一小簇火在指节上走来走去。“我睡不着，”她说，“我从没告诉过任何人。凡妮希德家的人每天恰好睡八个钟头。”火苗晃了晃。“我祖母有个咒语，只教给她信任的人。我从来没有人可以教。”\n# en: Two friends on a castle battlement at 3am, one of them making a small flame dance between her fingers, lighting both their faces.\nscene [5c03fc] = 凌晨三点的城堡城垛上，两个朋友并肩坐着，其中一个让一小簇火在指间舞动，照亮两人的脸。\n# en: Tell her she doesn't owe you anything\nopt.left.label [12406b] = 告诉她她不欠你什么\n# en: \"I know,\" she says. \"That's why I'd want to.\" But she lets the flame go out, and you sit there until the sky greys, and she doesn't mention the spell again, not yet.\nopt.left.say.1 [42b1d3] = “我知道，”她说，“所以我才会想。”可她让火灭了，你们一直坐到天色发灰，她没再提那个咒语，暂时没有。\n# en: Stay until sunrise\nopt.right.label [b5c233] = 陪她到日出\n# en: You talk until the sun comes up over the lake. About her grandmother, about fire that isn't for showing off, about being tired. When the light comes, she says, \"Wildfire. That's the spell. When you're ready.\" She means it as the biggest thing she has.\nopt.right.say.1 [4a34b7] = 你们一直聊到太阳从湖面升起。聊她的祖母，聊不是为了炫耀的火，聊累。天亮时她说：“野火。就是那个咒语。等你准备好。”她是把她最大的东西交给了你。\n\n@event tobias_moonflower_vigil\n# en: Every Eleven Years\ntitle [a3be6f] = 每十一年一次\n# en: Tobias is sitting in the greenhouse at night, beside a bed of tight pale buds, with a flask and a blanket. \"Moonflowers,\" he says. \"Once every eleven years. Could be tonight. Could be next spring.\" He's been coming every night for a month.\ntext [e1fcc8] = 夜里，托比亚斯坐在温室里一片紧闭的苍白花苞旁，带着一个水壶和一条毯子。“月光花，”他说，“十一年才开一次。可能是今晚。也可能是明年春天。”他已经每晚都来，来了一个月了。\n# en: A greenhouse at night lit by one lantern, a broad-shouldered student sitting on an upturned pot beside a bed of tightly closed pale buds.\nscene [68009c] = 夜里的温室只点着一盏灯，一个宽肩膀的学生坐在倒扣的花盆上，身旁是一片紧闭的苍白花苞。\n# en: Keep watch with him\nopt.left.label [7a29b3] = 陪他守着\n# en: You keep watch until the lantern runs out. They don't bloom. Tobias isn't disappointed. \"That's the point,\" he says. \"You wait for them. They don't wait for you.\" He tucks the blanket round you both.\nopt.left.say.1 [ce9d45] = 你陪他守到灯油燃尽。花没开。托比亚斯并不失望。“意义就在这儿，”他说，“是你等它们。它们不等你。”他把毯子裹在你们俩身上。\n# en: Bring him a proper dinner\nopt.right.label [c45fb4] = 给他带顿像样的晚饭\n# en: You bring a pie from the kitchens. He eats it with real gratitude, and tells you about his mother's moonflowers at home, which never bloomed either, and how she kept the bed weeded for twenty years anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [1fcdfa] = 你从厨房带来一个馅饼。他吃得满怀感激，跟你讲起他母亲在老家种的月光花，也从没开过，可她照样除了二十年的草。\n\n@event tobias_moonflower_bloom\n# en: Tonight\ntitle [56e80a] = 就是今晚\n# en: Tobias bangs on your door after dark. \"Tonight,\" he says. \"They're opening. Come on. Come ON.\" He's already running. In the greenhouse the moonflowers are unfolding, one by one, silver at the edges, and the whole place smells like rain.\ntext [9f39f0] = 天黑后托比亚斯砰砰敲你的门。“今晚，”他说，“它们要开了。快来。快来啊。”他已经跑出去了。温室里，月光花一朵接一朵地舒展开来，边缘泛着银光，整个屋子闻起来像雨。\n# en: A greenhouse at midnight full of pale open blossoms glowing faintly silver, a big student standing among them with his mouth open.\nscene [53d064] = 午夜的温室里开满了苍白的花，微微泛着银光，一个高大的学生站在花丛中张着嘴。\n# en: Run with him\nopt.left.label [8039e4] = 跟他一起跑\n# en: You run. You're in time. Every bud opens while you watch, and the greenhouse glows, and Tobias doesn't say a word for an hour. At the end he says, \"Eleven years,\" and then, \"Thank you for waiting with me,\" as if you'd done the waiting. You'll remember it longer than anything you were meant to study tonight.\nopt.left.say.1 [0c8ab7] = 你跑了起来。赶上了。每一个花苞都在你们眼前打开，温室亮了，托比亚斯一个钟头没说一句话。最后他说：“十一年。”然后：“谢谢你陪我等。”好像等的是你。你会记得这一晚，比今晚本该温习的任何东西都久。\n# en: Tell him to go; you'll catch up\nopt.right.label [370f56] = 叫他先去，你随后到\n# en: You finish your essay first. By the time you get there, half are open and Tobias is crying, quietly, the happy way. He saves you the best one to watch. It opens just for you, he insists. You don't argue.\nopt.right.say.1 [fe8f50] = 你先写完了论文。等你赶到时，花已经开了一半，托比亚斯在哭，静静地，是高兴的那种。他给你留了开得最好的那一朵。他坚持说，那一朵是只为你开的。你没争。\n\n@event tobias_plants_vote\n# en: The Greenhouse Votes\ntitle [3db2c0] = 温室投票\n# en: You've got a decision to make, and Tobias offers to ask the greenhouse. \"They're good judges,\" he says. \"Plants don't lie. They just turn towards what's good for them.\" He holds a seedling up to your face. {~It leans toward you, slowly.|It leans away, very slightly, and Tobias frowns.}\ntext [7b9ae8] = 你有个决定要做，托比亚斯提议去问问温室。“它们判断得很准，”他说，“植物不撒谎。它们只会转向对自己好的东西。”他把一株幼苗举到你面前。{~它慢慢地朝你倾过来。|它非常轻微地往后偏了偏，托比亚斯皱起眉头。}\n# en: A big student in a greenhouse holding his ear close to a row of seedlings, another student watching with folded arms.\nscene [44c46a] = 温室里，一个高大的学生把耳朵凑近一排幼苗，另一个学生抱着胳膊看着。\n# en: Do what the seedling says\nopt.left.label [bfc5ce] = 照幼苗说的做\n# en: {~\"Yes, then,\" Tobias says, satisfied. It turns out to be the right call, though you'll never be sure it was the seedling's.|\"Not that, then,\" he says, and you don't, and you never find out what would have happened, which is how good advice usually works.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ba6b5e] = {~“那就是好。”托比亚斯满意地说。结果证明这个决定是对的，虽然你永远不确定该不该归功于幼苗。|“那就不要。”他说。你没做，也永远不会知道做了会怎样——好建议通常就是这样。}\n# en: Ask him what he thinks\nopt.right.label [8b1202] = 问他怎么看\n# en: {~\"Me?\" He's surprised. He thinks it through, slowly, the way he does everything. His answer is better than the seedling's. He doesn't tell the seedling.|He gives you an answer so sensible you're embarrassed you asked a plant. \"Don't tell them I said that,\" he whispers.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2e1bd9] = {~“我？”他很意外。他慢慢地想，像他做所有事一样。他的答案比幼苗的好。他没告诉幼苗。|他给出的答案合理得让你不好意思去问一株植物。“别告诉它们是我说的。”他小声说。}\n\n@event tobias_scholarship_review\n# en: The Turnip Board\ntitle [9ff69e] = 萝卜奖学金委员会\n# en: Tobias has a letter from the agricultural scholarship board: a review of his marks, next week, in person. \"If they take it away I go home,\" he says. \"Not that home's bad. But I'm not done here.\" He's opened four textbooks and read none of them.\ntext [aa3f2f] = 托比亚斯收到农业奖学金委员会的信：下周当面复审他的成绩。“要是他们收回奖学金，我就得回家，”他说，“倒不是家里不好。可我在这儿还没完。”他翻开了四本课本，一本都没读进去。\n# en: A big student at a library table with a letter on top of a pile of open textbooks, head in his hands.\nscene [33a7be] = 一个高大的学生坐在图书馆桌前，一封信压在一堆摊开的课本上，双手抱着头。\n# en: Drill him all week\nopt.left.label [6b5389] = 陪他操练一整周\n# en: You drill him every night. At the review he answers everything, slowly and right, and one of the board members, a farmer herself, shakes his hand. He brings you a turnip. It's the best turnip you've ever had.\nopt.left.say.1 [2e1e75] = 你每晚陪他操练。复审时他每个问题都答上了，慢，但是对，一位委员——她自己也是农夫——和他握了手。他送你一个萝卜。是你吃过最好的萝卜。\n# en: You drill him, but the board is hard. He keeps the scholarship on probation. \"Probation's fine,\" he says. \"Probation means I'm still here.\" He hugs you anyway, which lifts you off the floor.\nopt.left.say.2 [5b8a79] = 你陪他操练了，可委员会很严。他的奖学金保住了，但处于察看期。“察看挺好，”他说，“察看就是说我还在这儿。”他照样给了你一个拥抱，把你抱离了地面。\n# en: Write to the board for him\nopt.right.label [2e91a7] = 替他给委员会写信\n# en: You write a letter about the boy who keeps the greenhouse alive and talks the mandrakes through exams. He never knows you sent it. The board renews him with a note about \"character\", and he can't think why.\nopt.right.say.1 [130617] = 你写了封信，讲那个让温室活下来、在考试时给曼德拉草打气的男孩。他永远不知道是你寄的。委员会续了他的奖学金，附注里提到“品格”，他想不通为什么。\n# en: You put in the moonflowers, eleven years of waiting for one night, and how he would stand in a storm for them. The board, it turns out, has farmers on it.\nopt.right.say.2 [fba1af] = 你在信里写了月光花，写了为了一个夜晚等上的十一年，写了他会怎样为它们站在风暴里。原来委员会里也有农民。\n\n@event seraphine_oracle_letter\n# en: A Letter From Vell\ntitle [2ba9f9] = 维尔来信\n# en: Seraphine has a letter with a silver seal. She hasn't opened it. \"My mother,\" she says. \"She's the Oracle of Vell. She tells kings what they want to hear and they pay her in castles. She wants me home, to learn it.\" She turns it over. \"I already know what it says. I see it, a little, through the paper.\"\ntext [348e68] = 瑟拉芬有一封盖着银色火漆的信。她还没拆。“我母亲，”她说，“她是维尔神谕。她告诉国王们他们想听的话，他们用城堡付钱。她想让我回家学这个。”她把信翻过来。“我已经知道里面写了什么。我能隔着信纸看见一点。”\n# en: A girl in a paint-stained smock holding a letter sealed in silver wax at arm's length, as if it were warm.\nscene [d4d6ed] = 一个穿着沾满颜料罩衫的女孩伸直胳膊拿着一封盖银色火漆的信，好像它是热的。\n# en: Ask what she wants\nopt.left.label [8b24a1] = 问她想要什么\n# en: Nobody has asked her that. She thinks for a long time. \"To be wrong sometimes,\" she says. \"Here, I'm wrong about small things. It's lovely.\" She puts the letter in a drawer, unopened. \"She'll write again. She always knows when I've stopped reading.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [472801] = 从没人问过她这个。她想了很久。“偶尔犯错，”她说，“在这里，我会在小事上出错。很美妙。”她把信放进抽屉，没拆。“她还会写的。我一不读，她总是知道。”\n# en: Offer to read it to her\nopt.right.label [fd46ab] = 提出替她念\n# en: You read it out. It's exactly what she said it would be, word for word. \"See,\" she says, quietly. \"Nothing in it I didn't know. That's the worst part. Thank you for saying it in someone else's voice.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [5fa884] = 你念了出来。和她说的一模一样，一字不差。“你看，”她轻声说，“没有一句是我不知道的。这才是最糟的。谢谢你用别人的声音把它说出来。”\n\n@event seraphine_what_you_want_to_hear\n# en: What You Want to Hear\ntitle [6ffa11] = 你想听的话\n# en: \"My mother's trick,\" Seraphine says, taking your hands, eyes half-closed. \"Watch. I'll tell you what you want to hear.\" {~\"You will pass every exam, be loved by everyone and find a small dog.\"|\"Your enemies will grow warts and your friends will grow taller.\"} She opens one eye. \"Now. Do you want the true one?\"\ntext [04e76a] = “我母亲的把戏，”瑟拉芬握住你的双手，半闭着眼说，“看好了。我会告诉你你想听的话。”{~“你会通过每一场考试，被所有人爱戴，还会捡到一只小狗。”|“你的敌人会长疣，你的朋友会长高。”}她睁开一只眼。“好了。你想听真的那个吗？”\n# en: A seer holding both of another student's hands across a table, eyes half-closed, a theatrical candle burning between them.\nscene [9bee52] = 一个预言者隔着桌子握住另一个学生的双手，半闭着眼，两人之间点着一支装模作样的蜡烛。\n# en: Keep the pretty one\nopt.left.label [1a0239] = 留着好听的那个\n# en: {~\"Wise,\" she says. \"Kings always pick that one too.\" She blows out the candle with a flourish. You do, briefly, believe in the small dog.|\"Good choice.\" She looks relieved. \"The true one has a lot of rain in it.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [7c9cfc] = {~“明智，”她说，“国王们也总是选这个。”她潇洒地吹灭蜡烛。有那么一会儿，你真的相信会有一只小狗。|“好选择。”她看上去松了口气。“真的那个里有好多雨。”}\n# en: Ask for the true one\nopt.right.label [072bb6] = 要听真的那个\n# en: {~\"You'll be tired a lot, and it will be worth it.\" She lets go of your hands. \"That's the only true one I'll give anyone. Nobody else asks for it.\"|\"Something will be hard, and you'll do it anyway, and someone will be proud of you.\" She shrugs. \"It's not much. It's true.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [be278d] = {~“你会经常很累，而这是值得的。”她松开你的手。“这是我唯一会给人的真话。别人都不要。”|“有件事会很难，你还是会去做，而有人会为你骄傲。”她耸耸肩。“不多。但是真的。”}\n\n@event seraphine_too_loud\n# en: Too Loud\ntitle [a08867] = 太吵了\n# en: In the middle of a full refectory Seraphine puts both hands over her ears and goes white. Nobody notices but you. \"Everyone's futures,\" she whispers, when you get to her. \"All at once. It's like a hundred people shouting what they'll be.\"\ntext [6c8b7b] = 在坐满人的膳堂中间，瑟拉芬双手捂住耳朵，脸色发白。除了你没人注意到。“大家的未来，”你走到她身边时，她低声说，“一下子全来了。像一百个人同时喊着自己将来会成为什么。”\n# en: A crowded refectory at lunchtime, a girl with her hands pressed over her ears in the middle of it, everyone else oblivious.\nscene [2d6ce2] = 午饭时坐满人的膳堂，一个女孩在人群中用双手捂着耳朵，其他人浑然不觉。\n# en: Get her out of there\nopt.left.label [729ad3] = 带她出去\n# en: You walk her out into the cloister, where it's quiet. She breathes. \"Yours is quiet,\" she says, after a while. \"I've always meant to say. It's like a room with the door shut.\" You miss your lecture. You don't mind.\nopt.left.say.1 [02599d] = 你扶着她走到回廊里，那里很安静。她喘过气来。“你的很安静，”过了一会儿她说，“我一直想告诉你。像一间关着门的屋子。”你错过了那节课。你不介意。\n# en: Talk to her, loudly, about nothing\nopt.right.label [63e5f8] = 大声跟她聊些闲话\n# en: You talk about the soup, the weather, a dog you once knew, loudly, right next to her. It works: she can hear the now over the noise. \"Keep going,\" she says, eyes shut. You talk about soup for twenty minutes.\nopt.right.say.1 [79a07c] = 你就在她旁边，大声聊汤、聊天气、聊你以前认识的一只狗。管用：她能在噪声之上听见此刻了。“继续说。”她闭着眼说。你聊了二十分钟的汤。\n\n@event corvin_lock_lesson\n# en: Every Door\ntitle [5fee74] = 每一扇门\n# en: Corvin is crouched at the Alchemy store's door after dark, two picks in the lock. \"Hold that lantern up,\" he says, as if you'd agreed. \"There isn't a locked door in this castle I can't open. I'm not taking anything. I'm keeping in practice.\"\ntext [9cac30] = 天黑后，科尔文蹲在炼金储藏室门前，两根细撬针插在锁里。“把灯举高点。”他说，好像你已经答应了。“这城堡里没有我开不了的锁。我不拿东西。我在保持手感。”\n# en: A lanky student crouched at a classroom door after dark, two thin picks in the lock, another student holding the lantern.\nscene [4b366c] = 天黑后，一个瘦高的学生蹲在教室门前，锁里插着两根细撬针，另一个学生举着灯。\n# en: Ask him to teach you\nopt.left.label [69348f] = 请他教你\n# en: He shows you how to feel the pins, one at a time. On your eleventh try the lock clicks. Corvin applauds silently. Then he locks it again, carefully, and leaves it exactly as it was. \"Rule one,\" he says. \"Always leave it how you found it.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [eb891c] = 他教你怎么一根一根地感觉锁芯里的弹子。第十一次，锁咔嗒一声开了。科尔文无声地鼓掌。然后他又把锁仔细锁上，恢复原样。“第一条规矩，”他说，“永远让它保持你来时的样子。”\n# en: Keep watch instead\nopt.right.label [ad394d] = 替他望风\n# en: You keep watch. He's in and out in a minute, with nothing in his hands but a single sugared almond from the jar on the master's desk. \"Tradition,\" he says. He splits it with you.\nopt.right.say.1 [e1435d] = 你替他望风。他一分钟就进出了，手里什么都没拿，只有讲师桌上罐子里的一颗糖杏仁。“传统。”他说。他分了你一半。\n\n@event corvin_takes_the_blame\n# en: One More Detention\ntitle [267a14] = 再关一次禁闭\n# en: Professor Vey holds up a set of lock picks found outside the Restricted Section. \"Whose?\" Corvin opens his mouth to make a joke, and you remember: one more detention and he's sent home, and home is not a good place.\ntext [71350e] = 维伊教授举起一套在禁书区外捡到的撬锁工具。“谁的？”科尔文张嘴要开玩笑，而你想起来了：再关一次禁闭，他就会被送回家，而家不是个好地方。\n# en: A stern professor in a corridor holding up a confiscated set of lock picks, two students in front of her, one of them grinning too widely.\nscene [033f46] = 走廊里，一位严厉的教授举着一套没收的撬锁工具，面前站着两个学生，其中一个笑得太过灿烂。\n# en: \"They're mine.\"\nopt.left.label [6d9cc8] = “是我的。”\n# en: Vey looks at you for a long moment, and at Corvin, and writes your name down. Corvin doesn't speak to you all day. That night there's a note under your door. It says \"I owe you\" and, underneath, smaller, \"I'm not joking.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [ae3edd] = 维伊看了你很久，又看了看科尔文，写下了你的名字。科尔文一整天没跟你说话。那晚你门下塞进一张纸条，写着“我欠你的”，下面一行小字：“我没开玩笑。”\n# en: Let him talk his way out\nopt.right.label [0c2a2d] = 让他自己圆过去\n# en: He talks. It's a masterpiece: picks, a jackdaw, a misunderstanding about a cupboard. Vey, who is in a good mood today, doesn't believe a word and lets him go anyway, for the performance.\nopt.right.say.1 [3fbfb9] = 他开口了。堪称杰作：撬针、一只寒鸦、一场关于橱柜的误会。维伊今天心情不错，一个字都不信，但为了这场表演还是放他走了。\n# en: He talks, and Vey, who is not in a good mood today, isn't having it. She can't prove the picks are his, so there's no detention; she keeps them, and writes his name in a little book. He laughs it off. His hands shake for the rest of the day.\nopt.right.say.2 [ccb621] = 他开口了，可维伊今天心情不好，不吃这一套。她证明不了撬针是他的，所以没有禁闭；她把撬针收了，在一个小本子上记下他的名字。他一笑置之。那一整天他的手都在抖。\n\n@event corvin_cartographer_hint\n# en: The Club That Doesn't Exist\ntitle [475340] = 不存在的社团\n# en: There's a map in your library book that wasn't there before. Corridors you've never seen, inked in a careful hand, and in the corner, very small: \"M.C. — we don't exist. Bring a lantern.\" Corvin, across the table, is reading upside down.\ntext [e45157] = 你的图书馆借书里多了一张原本没有的地图。一些你从没见过的走廊，用工整的笔迹画出，角落里很小的一行字：“午夜制图社——我们不存在。带盏灯。”桌对面的科尔文正倒着看书。\n# en: A folded map slipped into a book in a library, the margins full of tiny inked doors, a lanky student pretending not to have left it.\nscene [4027d7] = 图书馆的一本书里夹着一张折好的地图，页边画满小小的门，一个瘦高的学生假装不是他放的。\n# en: Ask him outright\nopt.left.label [fa721a] = 直接问他\n# en: \"Never heard of them,\" he says. \"Terrible people. Midnight, the third gargoyle on the left. Don't bring anyone.\" He goes back to his upside-down book. He's grinning at it.\nopt.left.say.1 [3bacd8] = “从没听说过，”他说，“一群可怕的人。午夜，左边第三座石像鬼。别带别人。”他继续看他倒着的书。他在对着书咧嘴笑。\n# en: Follow the map alone\nopt.right.label [4cacb2] = 独自照着地图走\n# en: You follow it into a passage that smells of old candle wax, and bark your shin on a step that isn't on the map. At the end, someone has chalked \"TOLD YOU\" on the wall. It's in Corvin's handwriting.\nopt.right.say.1 [fd3c10] = 你照着它走进一条散发着旧蜡烛味的通道，在一级地图上没画的台阶上磕了小腿。尽头的墙上有人用粉笔写着“早告诉你了”。是科尔文的笔迹。\n\n@event mir_small_fire\n# en: A Small Fire\ntitle [0853d5] = 一场小火\n# en: There's a small fire on Mir's workbench. Mir is looking at it with interest. \"It's fine,\" they say. \"It's a controlled fire. I'm controlling it by watching it.\" The fire grows. \"I'm watching it harder.\"\ntext [5eb966] = 米尔的工作台上着了一小团火。米尔饶有兴致地看着它。“没事，”TA说，“这是可控的火。我在通过看着它来控制它。”火变大了。“我看得更用力了。”\n# en: A workbench with a tiny cheerful fire burning in the middle of it, an artificer in goggles looking at it with professional interest rather than alarm.\nscene [808187] = 工作台中央欢快地燃着一小团火，戴护目镜的奇械师用专业的兴趣而非惊慌看着它。\n# en: Put it out\nopt.left.label [baa0ef] = 把火扑灭\n# en: You smother it with a lab coat. Mir sighs, and writes \"fire: yes\" in a notebook, under a long column of \"fire: no\". \"That's useful data,\" they say, sincerely. \"Thank you for ruining it.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [f50ed3] = 你用实验服把火捂灭了。米尔叹了口气，在笔记本上长长一列“着火：否”的下面写下“着火：是”。“这是有用的数据，”TA真诚地说，“谢谢你毁了它。”\n# en: Ask what it was meant to be\nopt.right.label [360171] = 问它本来是要做什么\n# en: \"A kettle,\" they say, as the fire eats the kettle. \"A self-heating kettle.\" The bucket under the bench, it turns out, is always full for exactly this reason.\nopt.right.say.1 [644191] = “一把水壶，”TA说，火正在吞掉那把水壶，“一把自加热水壶。”原来，工作台底下那桶水永远是满的，就是为了这个。\n\n@event mir_padlock\n# en: The Padlock\ntitle [175fe6] = 挂锁\n# en: Mir's workshop is still padlocked, and whatever they've been making do with isn't working: they've been soldering in the corridor outside the old door, on the floor, for a week. \"It's fine,\" they say, soldering on their knee. \"It's character-building. My character is very built.\"\ntext [50e6a3] = 米尔的工坊还挂着锁，而TA凑合用的地方也不管用：TA已经在旧门外的走廊地上焊了一周的东西。“没事，”TA一边在膝盖上焊接一边说，“磨炼品格。我的品格已经磨炼得很好了。”\n# en: An artificer sitting on the floor outside a padlocked door, back against it, notebooks stacked beside them, a student coming down the corridor with a box.\nscene [6e94fe] = 一个奇械师背靠着一扇挂了锁的门坐在地上，身边摞着笔记本，另一个学生抱着一个箱子从走廊那头走来。\n# en: Give them your desk\nopt.left.label [3517b2] = 把你的书桌让给TA\n# en: You clear the desk in your room and carry their boxes up four flights. Mir moves in within the hour. The first thing they build there is a sign: \"Workshop. Fires: probably fine.\" You do your reading on the stairs for a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a1e87] = 你清空了房间里的书桌，把TA的箱子搬上四层楼。一个钟头内米尔就搬了进来。TA在那里造的第一件东西是一块牌子：“工坊。着火：大概没事。”接下来一个月你都在楼梯上看书。\n# en: Ward the workshop and prove it\nopt.right.label [964c83] = 给工坊加护咒并证明给他看\n# en: Make the old room safe, and show Thorne.\nopt.right.hint [794539] = 让旧房间变安全，再给索恩看。\n# en: You spend a week warding every bench. Thorne lights a match on the worst one, watches it go out on its own, sighs like a dying bellows and hands Mir the key. \"Bucket within reach,\" he says. They keep three.\nopt.right.say.1 [c95f0b] = 你花了一周给每张工作台加上护咒。索恩在最糟的那张上划了根火柴，看着它自己熄灭，叹了口气，像一只快没气的风箱，把钥匙交给了米尔。“手边放一桶水，”他说。TA放了三桶。\n# en: The ward holds until it doesn't. Thorne puts out the bench with one hand and files the request with the other. But you sit in the corridor with Mir that evening and hand them screws, and it's less fine with company.\nopt.right.say.2 [88acb8] = 护咒撑住了，直到撑不住。索恩一只手扑灭了工作台，另一只手把申请归了档。可那晚你在走廊里陪米尔坐着，给TA递螺丝，有人陪着就没那么有事了。\n\n@event mir_family_clocks\n# en: Not a Hobby\ntitle [249789] = 不是爱好\n# en: {flag(mir_family_wrote): Mir's mother has named her prize: the guild medal for young artificers, entries closing in a fortnight. Mir has three inventions half-built and no time. \"Win a prize,\" they mutter, to a clock. \"Win a prize.\"|A letter from Mir's father, the first he's ever written them himself: could they send something they've made, to show the men at the clockworks? Mir has started the reply four times.}\ntext [855db5] = {flag(mir_family_wrote): 米尔的母亲说出了她要的奖：给年轻奇械师的行会奖章，两周后截止报名。米尔有三件做了一半的发明，却没有时间。“拿个奖，”TA对着一只钟嘟囔，“拿个奖。”|米尔的父亲来了封信，这是他第一次亲手给TA写信：能不能寄一件TA做的东西来，好给作坊里的人看看？米尔的回信已经开了四次头。}\n# en: A letter on a workbench, held down by a small brass clock, the artificer reading it with their goggles pushed up and their jaw tight.\nscene [c3aa64] = 工作台上一封信被一只小黄铜钟压着，奇械师把护目镜推到额头上读着信，下巴绷得紧紧的。\n# en: Help them write it up\nopt.left.label [9c261d] = 帮TA写出来\n# en: {flag(mir_family_wrote): You write up the entry together, all evening: what it does, why it matters, who'll use it. It's three pages and not one apology. \"Whatever the judges say,\" Mir says, \"it's finished.\"|You write it together, all evening: what Mir built this year, what it does, who uses it. Three pages of inventions and not one apology. \"He'll read every word,\" Mir says. \"Twice. He reads instructions twice.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [37f998] = {flag(mir_family_wrote): 你们一起写参赛说明，写了一整晚：它能做什么，为什么重要，谁会用它。三页纸，没有一句道歉。“不管评委怎么说，”米尔说，“它完成了。”|你们一起写了一整晚：米尔今年造了什么，能做什么，谁在用。三页纸全是发明，没有一句道歉。“他会一字一字地读，”米尔说，“读两遍。说明书他都读两遍。”}\n# en: Tell them to send the owl\nopt.right.label [573578] = 叫TA把猫头鹰寄去\n# en: {flag(mir_family_wrote): They send in the brass owl. It comes second. Mir's mother writes back one line: \"Second is a prize.\" Mir frames it.|Mir looks at the brass owl on the shelf for a long time. Then they wrap it and post it. The letter back is very short: \"Dad cried. He says it's not a hobby. I said I know.\"} You miss the owl on the shelf.\nopt.right.say.1 [1cfc54] = {flag(mir_family_wrote): TA把黄铜猫头鹰送去参赛。得了第二名。米尔的母亲回信只有一行：“第二名也是奖。”米尔把它裱了起来。|米尔盯着架子上的黄铜猫头鹰看了很久。然后TA把它包好寄了出去。回信很短：“爸爸哭了。他说这不是爱好。我说我知道。”}架子上少了那只猫头鹰，你有点想它。\n\n@event kael_northern_supper\n# en: A Northern Supper\ntitle [606d45] = 北境晚餐\n# en: You find Kael in the castle kitchens after hours, cooking something dark and sour-smelling over the smallest fire. \"Northern,\" he says. \"Fish, berries, and something you don't want to know. Nobody here makes it.\" There's already a second bowl out.\ntext [ca1037] = 熄灯后你在城堡厨房里找到凯尔，他在最小的一簇火上煮着什么，颜色很深，闻着发酸。“北境的，”他说，“鱼、浆果，还有一样你不会想知道的东西。这里没人做。”第二只碗已经摆好了。\n# en: A pale boy in a fur coat at the kitchen hearth late at night, carefully stirring a pot of dark broth, a second bowl set out without being asked for.\nscene [56c5f1] = 深夜里，一个穿毛皮大衣的苍白男孩在厨房炉边小心地搅着一锅深色的汤，旁边没等人要就多摆了一只碗。\n# en: Eat it all\nopt.left.label [187f65] = 全都吃光\n# en: It's the strangest thing you've ever eaten. You finish the bowl. Kael watches you with an expression you've never seen on him: hope. \"Next time,\" he says, \"I'll tell you what was in it.\" You hope he doesn't.\nopt.left.say.1 [cbe032] = 这是你吃过最古怪的东西。你吃完了一整碗。凯尔看着你，脸上是你从没见过的神情：希望。“下次，”他说，“我告诉你里面有什么。”你希望他别说。\n# en: Ask him to tell you about home\nopt.right.label [237455] = 请他讲讲家乡\n# en: He tells you about winter that lasts eight months and summer that lasts eight days, and singing on the ice to keep the dark back. He doesn't mention his family once. You notice. You don't ask.\nopt.right.say.1 [2201dd] = 他跟你讲八个月的冬天和八天的夏天，讲人们在冰上唱歌把黑暗挡在外面。他一次都没提到他的家人。你注意到了。你没问。\n\n@event kael_gloves\n# en: Gloves\ntitle [0edcb7] = 手套\n# en: Kael's hands are so cold the ink in his pen keeps freezing in lectures. You've noticed. He's noticed you noticing. Winter's coming, and he still doesn't own a pair of gloves.\ntext [ed9a39] = 凯尔的手冷得让他上课时笔里的墨水一直结冰。你注意到了。他也注意到你注意到了。冬天要来了，他还是没有一副手套。\n# en: A pair of thick knitted gloves, slightly lopsided, on a pale boy's hands as he turns them over and over, frost beginning to form on the wool.\nscene [8905a8] = 一副厚厚的手织手套，有点歪，戴在一个苍白男孩的手上，他翻来覆去地看，毛线上开始结霜。\n# en: Knit him some\nopt.left.label [795b79] = 给他织一副\n# en: They take a month and are lopsided. Frost forms on the wool within the hour. He wears them anyway, every day, until the thumbs wear through, and then he darns the thumbs himself, badly, rather than give them up.\nopt.left.say.1 [2e5d91] = 织了一个月，还织歪了。一个钟头内毛线上就结了霜。他还是每天戴着，直到拇指磨破，然后他自己笨手笨脚地补好拇指，也不肯换。\n# en: Buy him some in the village\nopt.right.label [57d15d] = 去镇上给他买一副\n# en: You buy a good warm pair. He holds them as if no one had ever given him a thing just for being cold. \"I don't feel it, mostly,\" he says. He wears them anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [d42760] = 你买了一副暖和的好手套。他捧着它们，好像从没有人只因为他冷就送他东西。“我大多时候感觉不到。”他说。他还是戴上了。\n\n@event kael_frost_fern\n# en: The Frost-Fern Trick\ntitle [aeec3f] = 霜蕨把戏\n# en: Kael shows you a children's trick from the North: you draw on a frosted window and the frost finishes the drawing for you. {~He draws a line; the frost turns it into a fern.|You draw a circle; the frost turns it into a snowflake the size of your hand.}\ntext [843f79] = 凯尔教你一个北境的儿童把戏：在结霜的窗上画画，霜会替你画完。{~他画一道线；霜把它变成一片蕨叶。|你画一个圈；霜把它变成一片巴掌大的雪花。}\n# en: A frosted window in a common room, a pale boy and another student drawing on it with their fingertips, ferns of ice spreading from each line.\nscene [56790a] = 公共休息室一扇结霜的窗前，一个苍白的男孩和另一个学生用指尖在上面画画，每一笔都长出冰蕨。\n# en: Draw something silly\nopt.left.label [0600e8] = 画个傻东西\n# en: {~You draw a goat. The frost, after a moment's thought, gives it a magnificent beard. Kael makes a sound you've never heard from him. It's a laugh.|You draw Professor Thorne. The frost gives him icicles for eyebrows. Kael has to sit down.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8a855a] = {~你画了只山羊。霜想了想，给它添了一把威风的胡子。凯尔发出一个你从没听过的声音。是笑声。|你画了索恩教授。霜给他添了冰柱眉毛。凯尔不得不坐下。}\n# en: Ask him how it works\nopt.right.label [acef1d] = 问他原理\n# en: {~\"You don't make it,\" he says. \"You let it. Frost wants to grow. You show it where.\" He's a better teacher than he thinks.|He explains, slowly, and then guides your hand. The frost follows your finger like a cat. \"There,\" he says. \"You're Northern now. A bit.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [b68818] = {~“不是你造出来的，”他说，“是你让它长。霜想长。你指给它方向。”他比他自己以为的会教。|他慢慢讲解，然后握着你的手引导。霜像猫一样跟着你的手指走。“好了，”他说，“你现在是北境人了。一点点。”}\n\n@event nessa_interview_prep\n# en: Twenty Years\ntitle [5fb846] = 二十年\n# en: Nessa has two hundred questions for the headmistress, pinned to her wall. \"Nobody's interviewed her in twenty years,\" she says. \"I'll get one chance. I have to pick the question that matters.\" She's crossed out a hundred and ninety.\ntext [0e5447] = 内莎有两百个问题要问院长，全钉在她墙上。“二十年没人采访过她，”她说，“我只有一次机会。我得挑出真正要紧的那个问题。”她已经划掉了一百九十个。\n# en: A girl in an untidy office with a long list of questions pinned to a wall, crossing out most of them, another student reading over her shoulder.\nscene [4317ff] = 一间凌乱的办公室里，一个女孩把一长串问题钉在墙上，把大部分划掉，另一个学生从她肩后看着。\n# en: Help her pick\nopt.left.label [fd3e52] = 帮她挑\n# en: You go through the last ten together until midnight. The one you pick is very simple: \"What do you wish students asked you?\" Nessa stares at it. \"That's either the best question I've ever asked or the worst,\" she says. \"Good.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [15360d] = 你们一起把最后十个过了一遍，一直到午夜。你们挑中的那个非常简单：“您希望学生问您什么？”内莎盯着它。“这要么是我问过最好的问题，要么是最糟的，”她说，“很好。”\n# en: Play the headmistress for practice\nopt.right.label [beaff8] = 扮演院长陪她练\n# en: You play the headmistress, badly, with a pencil for a wand. Nessa grills you for an hour and makes you cry with laughter twice and nearly cry the other way once. \"She'll be harder,\" she says. \"She'll be you, but calmer.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [963911] = 你扮演院长，演得很糟，拿一支铅笔当魔杖。内莎盘问了你一个钟头，有两次让你笑出了眼泪，有一次差点让你哭出来。“她会更难对付，”她说，“她会是你，只是更冷静。”\n\n@event nessa_fact_check\n# en: Fact-Check\ntitle [06d856] = 事实核查\n# en: Nessa slaps a proof down in front of you. \"Fact-check it. I don't trust my quill and I don't trust me.\" {~It's an article about the history of the castle's founding.|It's a profile of the Duelling Circle's captain.} There are, she warns you, at least three errors, and one of them is planted, to see if you're paying attention.\ntext [8f25b1] = 内莎把一份校样拍在你面前。“核查一下。我不信我的羽毛笔，也不信我自己。”{~是一篇关于城堡建校史的文章。|是一篇决斗社社长的人物特写。}她警告你，里面至少有三处错误，其中一处是故意埋的，看你有没有用心。\n# en: A newspaper proof covered in red-ink circles, a girl handing it across a desk to another student with a pencil behind her ear.\nscene [130535] = 一份画满红墨圈的报纸校样，一个耳后夹着铅笔的女孩隔着书桌把它递给另一个学生。\n# en: Go through it properly\nopt.left.label [f2d0e0] = 好好核一遍\n# en: {~You find all three, and a fourth she didn't plant. Nessa looks at you with something close to love, the editorial kind. \"Staff,\" she says. \"You're staff now.\"|You find the planted one first, then the others. Nessa writes \"GOOD\" on your hand in red ink. It takes three days to wash off.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ad7f38] = {~三处你全找到了，还找出一处她没埋的。内莎看着你，眼神近乎爱慕，编辑式的那种。“编辑部的人，”她说，“你现在是编辑部的人了。”|你先找到了埋的那处，然后是另外两处。内莎用红墨水在你手背上写了个“好”。洗了三天才洗掉。}\n# en: {~You find two. The planted one gets past you. Nessa circles it, gleefully, and tells you why it was obvious. You learn more from that than from the article.|You find one, and mark a true fact as wrong. Nessa is delighted: now she has to check it again, and it's still true, and she likes being right twice.}\nopt.left.say.2 [895615] = {~你找到两处。埋的那处从你眼皮底下溜了过去。内莎兴高采烈地把它圈出来，告诉你它为什么一目了然。你从中学到的比从文章里学到的还多。|你找到一处，还把一个正确的事实标成了错的。内莎高兴极了：现在她得再核查一遍，结果它还是对的，而她喜欢对两次。}\n# en: Hand it back: \"It's fine.\"\nopt.right.label [ab97b9] = 还给她：“没问题。”\n# en: {~\"Nothing's fine,\" Nessa says. She takes it back and finds all three in five minutes. \"Lazy,\" she says, but she's smiling.|She looks at you over the proof. \"That's what my quill says,\" she says. \"I don't trust it either.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [6e3028] = {~“什么都有问题。”内莎说。她拿回去，五分钟就找出了三处。“偷懒。”她说，但她在笑。|她从校样上方看着你。“我的羽毛笔也这么说，”她说，“我也不信它。”}\n\n@event nessa_quill_loose\n# en: The Quill Gets Loose\ntitle [52889c] = 羽毛笔跑了\n# en: Nessa's quill has escaped. It's loose in the castle, writing news. You've seen three of its headlines on the walls already: \"PROFESSOR'S CAT IS SECRETLY A PROFESSOR\", \"LAKE: TOO WET?\", and one about you that isn't true but is, you have to admit, flattering.\ntext [0367af] = 内莎的羽毛笔逃跑了。它在城堡里乱窜，到处写新闻。你已经在墙上看到它的三条标题：“教授的猫其实是一位教授”“湖：是否太湿？”，还有一条写你的，不是真的，但你得承认，挺讨人喜欢。\n# en: An enchanted quill flying down a castle corridor trailing a long scroll, a girl chasing it with an inkpot, students ducking.\nscene [af4c21] = 一支施了魔法的羽毛笔拖着一长卷纸飞过城堡走廊，一个女孩拿着墨水瓶在后面追，学生们纷纷低头躲避。\n# en: Help her catch it\nopt.left.label [8fa173] = 帮她抓住它\n# en: You corner it in the owlery. It writes \"CAUGHT\" on your sleeve as you grab it. Nessa stuffs it in her inkpot and screws the lid on. \"Thank you,\" she says, breathless. \"It was going to do a horoscope page. Again.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [c14881] = 你在猫头鹰棚把它逼进了角落。你抓住它时，它在你袖子上写了“已捕获”。内莎把它塞进墨水瓶，拧紧了盖子。“谢谢，”她气喘吁吁地说，“它又要搞星座专版了。”\n# en: Leave the headline about you up\nopt.right.label [ded518] = 留着写你的那条\n# en: You leave it up. People read it for a week. Nessa takes it down eventually, with a look. \"It made it up,\" she says. \"You know that.\" You do. It's still a lovely headline.\nopt.right.say.1 [6a8da3] = 你没撕。人们读了一周。最后内莎把它取了下来，看了你一眼。“它编的，”她说，“你知道的。”你知道。那还是一条很美的标题。\n\n@event bram_portrait\n# en: Head Boy, Forty Years Ago\ntitle [5e66a0] = 四十年前的男生代表\n# en: You find Bram in the corridor of head boys' portraits, in front of one in particular: a big man with Bram's jaw and none of his doubt. \"He walks past this every visit,\" Bram says. \"Stops. Looks at it. Then looks at me.\" He doesn't need to say the rest.\ntext [76dd48] = 你在历届男生代表的肖像走廊里找到布拉姆，他站在其中一幅前：一个高大的男人，有布拉姆的下颌，却没有他的半分犹疑。“他每次来都从这儿走过，”布拉姆说，“停下来。看着它。然后看着我。”剩下的话他不必说。\n# en: A row of old portraits of head boys in a dim corridor, a big student in a prefect's sash standing in front of one, not moving.\nscene [495874] = 昏暗的走廊里挂着一排历届男生代表的旧肖像，一个佩着级长绶带的高大学生站在其中一幅前，一动不动。\n# en: Say the portrait's a bad likeness\nopt.left.label [3844f0] = 说这画画得不像\n# en: \"It is,\" Bram says, surprised into it. \"Painter made him look kind.\" He laughs, short and rough, and it changes his face. \"Don't tell anyone I laughed at a portrait.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [8e58e5] = “是不像，”布拉姆被逗得脱口而出，“画家把他画得太和善了。”他短促、粗哑地笑了一声，整张脸都变了。“别告诉别人我对着一幅肖像笑了。”\n# en: Ask what he wants to be\nopt.right.label [3ac212] = 问他想成为什么样的人\n# en: He's never been asked. \"Not that,\" he says, nodding at the portrait. Then, after a long time: \"A teacher. Small school. Somewhere nobody's ever heard of my father.\" He walks off before you can answer. He looks lighter.\nopt.right.say.1 [cdc4ed] = 从没人问过他。“不是那样。”他朝肖像点点头。过了很久：“老师。小学校。一个没人听说过我父亲的地方。”你还没来得及回答他就走开了。他看上去轻松了些。\n\n@event bram_owns_up\n# en: Bram Owns Up\ntitle [84cd2d] = 布拉姆认了\n# en: Bram finds you. \"That exam,\" he says. \"You saw. You never said.\" He's holding his sash. \"I'm going to tell Thorne. I wanted you to know first. In case you wanted to stop me, I suppose.\" He doesn't look like he wants to be stopped.\ntext [262f3a] = 布拉姆找到你。“那场考试，”他说，“你看见了。你从没说出去。”他手里攥着他的绶带。“我要去告诉索恩。我想先让你知道。万一你想拦着我呢，我猜。”他看上去并不想被拦。\n# en: A big student in a prefect's sash standing in front of a professor's desk, cap in hand, another student waiting outside the door.\nscene [da84e6] = 一个佩着级长绶带的高大学生站在教授的书桌前，手里捏着帽子，另一个学生在门外等着。\n# en: Go in with him\nopt.left.label [1e8eec] = 陪他进去\n# en: You stand by the door while he tells Thorne everything. Thorne listens, takes the sash, looks at it for a while, and hands it back. \"Resit in a month,\" he says. \"Wear it like you mean it.\" Bram walks out shaking. \"Don't,\" he says. \"Don't make it weird.\" You don't.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a1d6e] = 你站在门边，他把一切告诉了索恩。索恩听完，拿过绶带看了一会儿，又递了回去。“一个月后补考，”他说，“戴着它，要名副其实。”布拉姆出来时在发抖。“别，”他说，“别弄得怪怪的。”你没有。\n# en: Tell him it's past\nopt.right.label [46f041] = 告诉他都过去了\n# en: \"It's done,\" you say. \"Leave it.\" He hesitates, then puts the sash back on. You can see it sits differently. He doesn't cheat again; you'd bet on it. He doesn't forget, either.\nopt.right.say.1 [0054bd] = “都过去了，”你说，“算了吧。”他犹豫了一下，把绶带重新戴上。你看得出它戴起来不一样了。他不会再作弊了，你敢打赌。他也不会忘。\n\n@event bram_become_close\n# en: Not Weird\ntitle [e43d2f] = 不怪\n# en: It's 3am and you can't sleep. Bram is on the stable fence, sash in his lap. \"Night rounds,\" he says, which is a lie; rounds ended at midnight. \"Sit if you want. Don't talk if you don't want.\" You sit. After a while he says, \"You're the only one who didn't believe I was him.\"\ntext [2bab87] = 凌晨三点你睡不着。布拉姆坐在马厩的栅栏上，绶带搁在膝头。“夜间巡查。”他说——这是谎话，巡查午夜就结束了。“想坐就坐。不想说话就别说。”你坐下了。过了一会儿他说：“只有你不信我就是他。”\n# en: A big student sitting on the stable fence at 3am beside another, a red sash folded in his lap, the hippogriffs asleep behind them.\nscene [46193b] = 凌晨三点，一个高大的学生和另一个人并肩坐在马厩栅栏上，膝上叠着一条红色绶带，身后的鹰头马身有翼兽都睡着了。\n# en: Leave him be\nopt.left.label [ce4663] = 让他一个人待着\n# en: You sit a while longer and then go. He says goodnight like it's an order. In the morning your demerits for the week have been crossed out, with no explanation.\nopt.left.say.1 [ac99ab] = 你又坐了一会儿就走了。他道晚安道得像下命令。第二天早上，你这一周的记过全被划掉了，没有任何解释。\n# en: Stay until morning\nopt.right.label [d46a70] = 陪到天亮\n# en: You stay. He talks about his mother, who's kind and doesn't say much, and the dog he had as a boy, and what he's afraid of, which is everything. At sunrise he says, \"This is weird.\" Then: \"It's fine. It's fine if it's weird.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [ec831b] = 你留下了。他讲他母亲，和善、话不多；讲他小时候养的狗；讲他害怕什么——什么都怕。日出时他说：“这很怪。”然后：“没事。怪就怪吧。”\n\n@event ilse_frozen_coins\n# en: Coins That Freeze\ntitle [28bf39] = 会结冰的硬币\n# en: Ilse is at Mabel's counter again, glaring at a coin that has frozen to the wood. Mabel is pouring hot water on it. It isn't working. \"It does this,\" Ilse says, through her teeth. \"When I'm cross. I'm cross because it does this.\"\ntext [ca3584] = 伊尔泽又站在梅布尔的柜台前，瞪着一枚冻在木头上的硬币。梅布尔在往上浇热水。不管用。“它就是会这样，”伊尔泽咬着牙说，“我一生气就这样。而我生气就是因为它这样。”\n# en: A teashop counter with a coin frozen to the wood, a girl in white furs glaring at it, a teashop owner holding a kettle over it hopefully.\nscene [32ad34] = 茶馆柜台上冻着一枚硬币，一个穿白裘的女孩瞪着它，茶馆老板娘满怀希望地提着水壶往上浇。\n# en: Pay, and warm the coin\nopt.left.label [79b381] = 替她付，把硬币焐热\n# en: You pay. Then you take the frozen coin and carry it in your pocket all afternoon, and give it back to her warm. She looks at it for a long time. \"That's stupid,\" she says. She keeps it, separately from the others.\nopt.left.say.1 [dc9f20] = 你付了钱。然后你拿走那枚冻住的硬币，揣在口袋里一整个下午，焐热了还给她。她盯着它看了很久。“真傻。”她说。她把它和别的硬币分开收着。\n# en: Make her laugh until it thaws\nopt.right.label [8dcdc7] = 逗她笑到它化开\n# en: You do Thorne's voice. Then Bram's. On Bram, the coin cracks free of the counter with a pop, and Ilse makes a noise she clearly hadn't planned to make. \"Again,\" she says. \"No. Don't. Again.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [a97a64] = 你学索恩说话。又学布拉姆。学到布拉姆时，硬币啪的一声从柜台上脱开了，伊尔泽发出一个显然不在她计划中的声音。“再来，”她说，“不。别。再来。”\n\n@event ilse_private_storm\n# en: Her Weather\ntitle [ff0fd8] = 她的天气\n# en: Ilse's private snowstorm follows her about the village. You've learned to read it. {~Today it's thick and sideways, which means stay away.|Today it's three flakes, slow, which means something's wrong and she won't say what.}\ntext [834dc1] = 伊尔泽的私人暴风雪跟着她在镇上走来走去。你已经学会读它了。{~今天它又密又斜，意思是离远点。|今天只有三片雪，慢慢地落，意思是出了什么事，她不肯说。}\n# en: A girl in white furs walking through the village under a small private snowstorm that follows her, a second student stepping under it with her.\nscene [8bc63e] = 一个穿白裘的女孩在镇上走着，头顶跟着一场小小的私人暴风雪，另一个学生走进雪里和她并肩。\n# en: Step under it with her\nopt.left.label [c53c7d] = 走进雪里陪她\n# en: {~You walk beside her, inside the storm, getting snowed on. She doesn't say anything. After a street, the snow slows. After two, it stops. \"Don't read it,\" she says. \"It's rude.\" You read it anyway. It says: better.|You walk with her, getting snow down your collar. At the bridge she says, \"My brother used to do that,\" and the three flakes become one, and then none.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bb577e] = {~你走在她身边，在暴风雪里，被雪落了一身。她什么都没说。走过一条街，雪慢了。走过两条，雪停了。“别读它，”她说，“没礼貌。”你还是读了。它说的是：好些了。|你陪着她走，雪钻进你的领口。走到桥边她说：“我哥哥以前也这样。”三片雪变成一片，然后一片也没有了。}\n# en: Wait it out at a distance\nopt.right.label [31a6ec] = 远远地等它过去\n# en: {~You give her room. The storm passes in an hour. She nods to you later across the square, which, for Ilse, is an apology for the weather.|You let her be. Later, a single snowflake lands on your sleeve, perfectly formed, and doesn't melt until you're indoors. You think it's a thank you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [98b638] = {~你给她留出空间。一个钟头后暴风雪过去了。后来她隔着广场冲你点了点头——对伊尔泽来说，这是为天气道歉。|你由她去。后来一片雪花落在你袖子上，形状完美，直到你进了屋才化。你觉得这是一声谢谢。}\n\n@event ilse_frost_craft_trade\n# en: A Fair Trade\ntitle [e62624] = 公平交换\n# en: \"Teach me something,\" Ilse says, at the pond. \"Something of yours. I taught you frost. It's your turn. Something that isn't useful.\" She seems to think that's the hardest kind.\ntext [87b9f2] = “教我点东西，”伊尔泽在池塘边说，“你的东西。我教了你霜术。该你了。教点没用的。”她似乎觉得这是最难的一种。\n# en: A frozen village pond, a girl in white furs teaching another student to throw a ring of frost across the ice, both breathing clouds.\nscene [b40f88] = 结冰的镇上池塘边，一个穿白裘的女孩教另一个学生在冰面上甩出一圈霜，两人都呵着白气。\n# en: Teach her a card game\nopt.left.label [25b4a6] = 教她一种纸牌游戏\n# en: You teach her a card game from home. She's terrible, and then, suddenly, ruthless. She beats you six times running on the ice and laughs out loud, once, before she catches herself. \"That's not useful,\" she says. \"Good.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [d65397] = 你教她一种家乡的纸牌游戏。她先是糟透了，然后突然变得冷酷无情。她在冰上连赢你六局，还笑出了声，就一次，随即收住了。“这没用，”她说，“很好。”\n# en: Teach her to skip stones\nopt.right.label [ff3bdd] = 教她打水漂\n# en: There's no open water, so you skip stones across the ice. Hers go further. Of course they do. She counts every bounce out loud, in Northern, and makes you count yours too.\nopt.right.say.1 [b8980f] = 没有开冻的水面，你们就在冰上打水漂。她的漂得更远。当然了。她用北境话大声数着每一跳，还让你也数你的。\n\n@event ilse_become_close\n# en: Not a Trick\ntitle [81c3ce] = 不是把戏\n# en: It's 3am. You walk down to the shore, and Ilse is already there, on the ice, under her own small snowfall. \"You keep being kind,\" she says, not looking at you. \"I keep waiting to find out what it's for.\"\ntext [d867ed] = 凌晨三点。你走到湖岸边，伊尔泽已经在那里了，站在冰上，头顶下着她自己的小雪。“你一直对我好，”她没看你，“我一直在等着看这是为了什么。”\n# en: A girl in white furs and a student sitting on the frozen lakeshore at 3am under a sky full of stars, snow falling very gently around only the two of them.\nscene [d6d0be] = 凌晨三点结冰的湖岸，一个穿白裘的女孩和一个学生坐在满天星斗下，雪只在两人周围非常轻柔地飘落。\n# en: Tell her it isn't for anything, and go\nopt.left.label [f82cc3] = 告诉她不为什么，然后离开\n# en: \"Everything's for something,\" she says. But she lets you go without an argument, which is new. In the morning there's a small perfect snowflake on your windowsill that doesn't melt.\nopt.left.say.1 [3dc34c] = “什么都是为了什么。”她说。可她没争辩就让你走了，这是头一回。第二天早上，你的窗台上有一片完美的小雪花，没有融化。\n# en: Stay, and let her wait\nopt.right.label [4eec40] = 留下来，让她等\n# en: You stay. Neither of you says much. Around dawn the snow around her changes: it starts falling on you too, gently, the same as on her. She notices. \"Oh,\" she says, very quietly, as if she's just learned a word. \"Oh. It isn't a trick.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [a766c9] = 你留下了。你们都没说什么。快天亮时，她周围的雪变了：雪也开始轻轻落在你身上，和落在她身上一样。她注意到了。“哦，”她非常轻地说，像刚学会一个词，“哦。不是把戏。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/bonds/rivals.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/bonds/rivals.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lyra_rankings_war\n# en: The Rankings War\ntitle [0d42bd] = 排名之战\n# en: The rankings are up outside the Great Hall. {~You're one place above Lyra.|Lyra is one place above you.} She's standing in front of the list when you get there, and she doesn't move aside. \"Enjoy it,\" she says. \"It's the last time.\"\ntext [fd298e] = 大礼堂外贴出了排名。{~你比莱拉高一名。|莱拉比你高一名。}你到的时候她正站在榜前，没有让开。“好好享受吧，”她说，“这是最后一次了。”\n# en: A noticeboard outside the Great Hall with the term's rankings pinned up, two names side by side at the top, a red-haired student tapping one with her wand.\nscene [641a6e] = 大礼堂外的告示板上贴着本学期排名，榜首两个名字并排，一个红发学生用魔杖点着其中一个。\n# en: Out-study her this term\nopt.left.label [0141b6] = 这学期在功课上压过她\n# en: {~You take the library table opposite hers and don't leave it for a month. She doesn't either. Thorne remarks that he's never seen the library so tense. The next list has you in the same order. Neither of you is satisfied.|You study every night. So does she; you can see her lamp from your window. It's the best work either of you has done, and you'd both rather die than say so.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d543d2] = {~你在图书馆坐到她对面那张桌子，一个月没挪窝。她也没有。索恩说他从没见过图书馆这么紧张。下一张榜上你们的名次没变。谁都不满意。|你每晚都在用功。她也是，从你的窗户看得见她的灯。这是你们俩做过最好的功课，而你们宁死也不会承认。}\n# en: Leave a note on her desk\nopt.right.label [57d546] = 在她桌上留张字条\n# en: {~\"Second suits you.\" She finds it in front of the whole class, and goes the colour of her hair. The next morning there's a note on yours: \"Says the person who'll be third.\"|You leave a single word: \"Close.\" It drives her mad for a week. It was meant to.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bfeed5] = {~“第二名挺适合你。”她当着全班的面发现了它，脸红得和她的头发一个颜色。第二天早上你桌上也有一张：“说这话的人会是第三。”|你只留了两个字：“很近。”它让她抓狂了一整周。本来就是这个目的。}\n\n@event lyra_sabotage_returned\n# en: Pink Bubbles, Returned\ntitle [527a71] = 粉色泡泡的回礼\n# en: In the middle of Thorne's lecture your wand produces a cloud of blue smoke shaped like a donkey. Three rows away, Lyra is taking very careful notes. It's your move. It's always your move.\ntext [f09ef1] = 索恩讲课讲到一半，你的魔杖喷出一团驴子形状的蓝烟。隔着三排，莱拉正非常认真地记笔记。该你出招了。永远该你出招。\n# en: A student's wand producing a cloud of blue smoke shaped like a donkey in the middle of a lecture, a red-haired student three rows away not looking.\nscene [e6829e] = 课堂中途，一个学生的魔杖喷出一团驴子形状的蓝烟，三排之外一个红发学生目不斜视。\n# en: Call a ceasefire\nopt.left.label [664f1d] = 提议停火\n# en: You slip her a note: \"Truce? I'm running out of eyebrows.\" She reads it twice, and writes underneath: \"Truce. For now. The donkey was very good though.\" It was.\nopt.left.say.1 [55e576] = 你塞给她一张纸条：“停火？我的眉毛快不够用了。”她读了两遍，在下面写：“停火。暂时。不过那头驴真的很好。”确实很好。\n# en: Escalate\nopt.right.label [21c8b9] = 升级\n# en: Her next spell comes out as a choir of tiny frogs singing her family motto. The whole class applauds. Lyra stands there with her wand out, absolutely white, and then, unbelievably, starts to laugh.\nopt.right.say.1 [27e9db] = 她的下一个咒语变成了一群小青蛙合唱她的家族格言。全班鼓掌。莱拉举着魔杖站在那里，脸色煞白，然后，难以置信地，笑了起来。\n# en: She sees it coming a mile off and turns it round. You spend detention scrubbing cauldrons. She spends it in the next room, also scrubbing cauldrons, for the donkey.\nopt.right.say.2 [f93474] = 她老远就看穿了，还把它原样奉还。你在禁闭里刷坩埚。她在隔壁房间，也在刷坩埚，为了那头驴。\n\n@event lyra_glove_thrown\n# en: The Glove Thrown\ntitle [91e54d] = 掷下手套\n# en: Lyra drops a glove at your feet in the courtyard, in front of everyone. \"The ring. Tonight. No referee but the crowd.\" She's been waiting for this all term. So, if you're honest, have you.\ntext [10948b] = 莱拉在庭院里当着所有人的面，把一只手套丢在你脚边。“决斗场。今晚。除了观众，没有裁判。”她等这一刻等了一整个学期。说实话，你也是。\n# en: A white duelling glove on the flagstones of a crowded courtyard, a red-haired student standing over it with her wand drawn, a ring of watching students.\nscene [bb50e5] = 拥挤的庭院石板上躺着一只白色决斗手套，一个红发学生拔出魔杖站在它跟前，周围一圈学生在看。\n# en: Leave the glove where it lies\nopt.left.label [b434b9] = 由手套躺在那儿\n# en: You step over it. The crowd mutters. Lyra picks it up herself, very slowly. \"Next time,\" she says. \"There's always a next time.\" There is.\nopt.left.say.1 [6a6d8c] = 你从它上面跨了过去。人群窃窃私语。莱拉自己把它捡了起来，非常慢。“下次，”她说，“总有下次。”确实有。\n# en: Pick it up\nopt.right.label [b90f6a] = 捡起来\n# en: You win, barely, in front of half the school. Lyra gets up off the flagstones and, after a long moment, holds out the glove. \"Keep it,\" she says. \"Until I win it back.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [9a0717] = 你赢了，险胜，当着半个学校的面。莱拉从石板上站起来，过了好一会儿，把手套递给你。“留着，”她说，“等我把它赢回来。”\n# en: She wins, and it costs her: she's limping, and she knows you made her work for every point. \"Good,\" she says, breathing hard. \"Good. Again, soon.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [2c079c] = 她赢了，赢得很辛苦：她一瘸一拐，也知道你让她每一分都拿得不容易。“很好，”她喘着气说，“很好。再来，快点。”\n\n@event lyra_allies_thornwick\n# en: Allies, for an Afternoon\ntitle [3df90d] = 一个下午的盟友\n# en: Three Thornwick students in green are laughing at the Lumenhold crest in the entrance hall. Lyra hears it at the same moment you do. She looks at you. You look at her. For the first time all year, you're on the same side.\ntext [0c0842] = 三个穿绿衣的荆木学院学生在门厅里嘲笑流明堡的校徽。莱拉和你同时听见了。她看着你。你看着她。这一年里第一次，你们站在了同一边。\n# en: Two rival students standing shoulder to shoulder in a corridor facing a group of visitors in green Thornwick colours, both wands half-drawn.\nscene [3f27b0] = 走廊里，两个对手肩并肩面对一群穿荆木学院绿色校服的访客，魔杖都拔出了一半。\n# en: Stand with her\nopt.left.label [3ba99e] = 站在她身边\n# en: You stand shoulder to shoulder. The Thornwick students take one look at the two of you and remember an appointment elsewhere, though the last one flicks a jinx at your ankles on the way out, and it stings for an hour. Lyra doesn't thank you. She does walk with you as far as the stairs, which is unheard of.\nopt.left.say.1 [ccd253] = 你们肩并着肩。荆木学院的学生看了你们俩一眼，突然想起别处还有约，不过最后一个走的时候朝你脚踝甩了个恶咒，疼了一个钟头。莱拉没谢你。可她陪你一直走到楼梯口，这可是闻所未闻。\n# en: Let her handle it\nopt.right.label [d5ddde] = 交给她处理\n# en: You leave her to it and get an hour's reading done in the library. She handles it alone, magnificently, and you hear about it at dinner from four different people. \"You missed it,\" she says, passing. She sounds as if she minds.\nopt.right.say.1 [37cc3f] = 你把这事留给她，自己去图书馆读了一个钟头的书。她一个人处理了，处理得漂亮极了，晚饭时你从四个人嘴里听说了这件事。“你错过了。”她经过时说。听上去她很在意。\n\n@event lyra_mother_watches\n# en: Her Mother Watches You Win\ntitle [0c2219] = 她母亲看着你赢\n# en: An exhibition bout, and you're paired with Lyra. Up in the gallery, in black, is Lady Vaneshade. Halfway through, you're winning, clearly, and Lyra knows it, and you see her glance up once at the gallery and then not again.\ntext [64ce6b] = 一场表演赛，你的对手是莱拉。看台上，一身黑衣的，是凡妮希德夫人。打到一半，你明显占了上风，莱拉知道，你看见她朝看台瞥了一眼，然后再也没有看。\n# en: An exhibition duelling ring with a gallery above, an elegant woman in black watching a red-haired student who is losing.\nscene [e20024] = 一个表演决斗场，上方是看台，一位穿黑衣的优雅女士看着一个正在落败的红发学生。\n# en: Win it\nopt.left.label [1d93a6] = 赢下来\n# en: You win cleanly. The gallery applauds. Lady Vaneshade doesn't. She leaves before the bows. Lyra watches her go, and then turns and shakes your hand properly. \"Don't you dare go easy on me,\" she says. \"Ever.\" You won't.\nopt.left.say.1 [b070c8] = 你干净利落地赢了。看台鼓掌。凡妮希德夫人没有。她没等行礼就走了。莱拉看着她离开，然后转过身来，郑重地和你握手。“你敢让着我试试，”她说，“永远别。”你不会。\n# en: Let her come back\nopt.right.label [92da46] = 让她扳回来\n# en: You slow down, just enough. Lyra wins. In the gallery, her mother nods once. Afterwards Lyra corners you, furious. \"You let me,\" she says. Then, so quietly you nearly miss it: \"Thank you. Never again.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [b9979e] = 你放慢了一点，刚好够。莱拉赢了。看台上，她母亲点了一下头。事后莱拉把你堵在角落里，气坏了。“你让我，”她说。然后，轻得你差点没听见：“谢谢。下不为例。”\n\n@event lyra_rival_letter\n# en: A Rival's Letter\ntitle [c65d7a] = 对手的来信\n# en: A letter with the Vaneshade seal. Inside: a ruled table of her term marks, beside yours, with every subject she beat you in underlined. {~At the bottom: \"Enjoy your holiday. I'm not taking one.\"|At the bottom: \"You're welcome to check my sums. They're right.\"}\ntext [d150df] = 一封盖着凡妮希德家火漆的信。里面是一张画好格子的表，列着她这学期的成绩和你的并排，凡是她胜过你的科目都画了线。{~底下写着：“祝你假期愉快。我不放假。”|底下写着：“欢迎核对我的计算。都是对的。”}\n# en: A letter with a black Vaneshade seal on a windowsill, inside it a neatly ruled table of marks with one line underlined twice.\nscene [a6f1ba] = 窗台上一封盖着黑色凡妮希德火漆的信，里面一张画得整整齐齐的成绩表，有一行下面画了两道线。\n# en: Write back with corrections\nopt.left.label [11b838] = 回信附上更正\n# en: {~You find one mistake in her sums, in your favour, and write back about it at length. Her reply is two words: \"Noted. Grrr.\"|You send back her table with your own column added: hours slept. She doesn't answer for a week. Then: \"Fine. I'll sleep. Some.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [db1772] = {~你在她的计算里找到一处错误，对你有利，于是写了长长的回信。她的回复只有两个字：“已阅。哼。”|你把她的表寄回去，加了一栏你自己的：睡了几个钟头。她一周没回。然后：“行。我会睡。一点。”}\n# en: Pin it above your desk\nopt.right.label [94c02c] = 钉在书桌上方\n# en: {~You pin it up where you'll see it every morning. It works better than any timetable.|Up it goes, above your desk. Every time you want to stop, there it is, underlined twice.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e590d0] = {~你把它钉在每天早上都看得见的地方。比任何课表都管用。|它被钉在了你书桌上方。每当你想停下，它就在那儿，下面画着两道线。}\n\n@event lyra_last_match\n# en: The Last Rankings\ntitle [d8ac87] = 最后的排名\n# en: The last rankings you'll ever see at Lumenhold. You and Lyra are tied. Exactly. She stands in front of the list for a long time. \"Four years,\" she says. \"And it comes to that.\" She doesn't sound angry. She sounds almost happy.\ntext [a4f700] = 你在流明堡会看到的最后一张排名。你和莱拉并列第一。分毫不差。她在榜前站了很久。“四年，”她说，“到头来就是这样。”她听上去不生气。她听上去几乎是高兴的。\n# en: A final rankings list on a noticeboard, two names tied at the top, two students standing in front of it in silence.\nscene [4248b8] = 告示板上贴着最终排名，榜首两个名字并列，两个学生默默站在榜前。\n# en: Share it\nopt.left.label [cce3a5] = 一起分享\n# en: \"Joint,\" you say. She looks at you, and then holds out her hand. You shake. \"It was never going to be anyone else,\" she says. \"I'm glad it was you I didn't beat.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [928556] = “并列。”你说。她看着你，然后伸出手。你们握了手。“从来就不会是别人，”她说，“我很高兴没打败的那个人是你。”\n# en: Then you say \"well done\", because somebody should, once. She doesn't answer. She holds on to your hand a moment longer.\nopt.left.say.2 [e3aa24] = 然后你说了声“干得好”，因为总该有人说一次。她没有回答。她握着你的手，又多握了一会儿。\n# en: Ask Thorne for a decider\nopt.right.label [98e038] = 请索恩出一道决胜题\n# en: Thorne sets you both one question, the hardest he has. You get it by a hair. Lyra reads your answer, and nods, and says, \"That's right. That's annoyingly right.\" She's proud of you. She'll never say it.\nopt.right.say.1 [253d9e] = 索恩给你们俩出了一道题，他手头最难的一道。你以毫厘之差答对了。莱拉读了你的答案，点点头说：“对的。对得让人生气。”她为你骄傲。她永远不会说出口。\n# en: Thorne sets you both one question. She gets it. You don't. She doesn't gloat, which is how you know she means it when she says, \"Thank you. That's the only one I wanted.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [21e833] = 索恩给你们俩出了一道题。她答对了。你没有。她没有得意，所以你知道她是真心的，当她说：“谢谢。我想要的就只有这一个。”\n\n@event bram_marked\n# en: Top of the Ledger\ntitle [74cfb7] = 记过簿第一页\n# en: Bram opens his demerit ledger in the middle of the corridor and writes your name at the top of a fresh page, slowly, so you can watch him do it. \"A page of your own,\" he says. \"You've earned it.\"\ntext [698e35] = 布拉姆在走廊正中翻开他的记过簿，在新的一页最上方写下你的名字，写得很慢，好让你看着他写。“专属于你的一页，”他说，“你应得的。”\n# en: A big student in a red prefect's sash writing a name at the top of a demerit ledger with great ceremony, another student watching from a doorway.\nscene [ac25c0] = 一个佩着红色级长绶带的高大学生郑重其事地在记过簿顶端写下一个名字，另一个学生在门口看着。\n# en: Keep your head down\nopt.left.label [b59315] = 低头走过\n# en: You walk past without a word. He looks almost disappointed. The page stays empty for a while. It doesn't stay empty for long.\nopt.left.say.1 [e26ec7] = 你一言不发地走了过去。他看上去几乎有点失望。那一页空了一阵子。没空太久。\n# en: Meet his eye\nopt.right.label [47716f] = 迎上他的目光\n# en: You stop, and look at him, and don't look away. \"Good,\" says Bram, and snaps the ledger shut. \"I was hoping you would.\" It's war, and you're both, somehow, pleased about it.\nopt.right.say.1 [d5df67] = 你停下，看着他，不移开视线。“很好，”布拉姆说着啪地合上记过簿，“我就盼着你这样。”宣战了，而你们俩不知怎么都挺满意。\n\n@event bram_demerit_ledger\n# en: The Demerit Ledger\ntitle [d68875] = 记过簿\n# en: Bram has given you a demerit for {~\"walking in a smug manner\".|\"breathing in a provocative direction\".} It's the fourth this month. The ledger is starting to look like a novel.\ntext [2cb93a] = 布拉姆给你记了一过，理由是{~“走路姿态自鸣得意”。|“朝挑衅的方向呼吸”。}这已经是这个月的第四次了。记过簿开始像一本小说了。\n# en: A prefect's ledger open on a windowsill, a column of demerits next to one name, each with a reason in neat block capitals.\nscene [02d917] = 窗台上摊开一本级长的记过簿，一个名字旁边列着一长串记过，每条理由都用工整的大写字母写着。\n# en: Accept it with dignity\nopt.left.label [eca928] = 体面地接受\n# en: {~You thank him for his diligence. He has no idea what to do with that. He writes \"THANKED ME\" in the margin, puzzled.|You nod gravely and walk on. He follows you for a corridor, waiting for you to crack. You don't.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5b126b] = {~你感谢他的尽职。他完全不知道该怎么接。他在页边写下“谢了我”，一脸困惑。|你庄重地点点头，继续走。他跟了你一条走廊，等着你绷不住。你没有。}\n# en: As you go, you add that at least it isn't a hall tax. He goes red to the ears.\nopt.left.say.2 [6c22fd] = 走的时候你补了一句，好歹这不是走廊税。他的脸一直红到了耳根。\n# en: Appeal it to his face\nopt.right.label [0ead85] = 当面申诉\n# en: {~You point out, with diagrams, that it's impossible to walk smugly. A crowd gathers. Bram crosses it out to make you stop.|You argue it so well that two first-years applaud. Bram crosses it out, then gives you a fresh one for \"public speaking\".}\nopt.right.say.1 [8a64a0] = {~你画着示意图论证：走路不可能自鸣得意。围了一圈人。布拉姆为了让你闭嘴把它划掉了。|你申诉得太好，两个一年级生鼓起了掌。布拉姆把它划掉，又因为“公开演讲”给你记了一过。}\n# en: {~\"Arguing with a prefect,\" Bram says happily, writing. \"That's a detention.\" It is.|You argue, and he writes, and the demerit becomes a detention. \"Thank you,\" he says. \"That's my quota.\"}\nopt.right.say.2 [17aa39] = {~“顶撞级长，”布拉姆一边写一边高兴地说，“关禁闭。”确实。|你争，他写，记过变成了禁闭。“谢谢，”他说，“这下我完成指标了。”}\n\n@event bram_inspection\n# en: Inspection\ntitle [f74fb3] = 检查\n# en: \"Room inspection,\" Bram announces, at your door, with a clipboard. There is no such thing as room inspection. He's already in. He's already opening your trunk.\ntext [74dcba] = “查寝。”布拉姆拿着记录板站在你门口宣布。根本没有查寝这回事。他已经进来了。他已经在开你的箱子了。\n# en: A big prefect going through a student's dormitory trunk with exaggerated care, holding up a sock with two fingers, the owner standing in the doorway.\nscene [43343f] = 一个高大的级长夸张地翻着一个学生的宿舍箱子，用两根手指拎起一只袜子，箱子的主人站在门口。\n# en: Let him search\nopt.left.label [b89090] = 让他搜\n# en: He finds the kitchen's missing jar of honey under your bed. You have no idea how it got there. He does, you suspect. Detention.\nopt.left.say.1 [27c00c] = 他在你床底下找到了厨房丢的那罐蜂蜜。你完全不知道它怎么会在那儿。你怀疑他知道。禁闭。\n# en: He finds nothing worse than socks. He's almost disappointed. On his way out he straightens your books, without thinking, and then glares at you as if you made him do it.\nopt.left.say.2 [7318b7] = 他除了袜子什么也没找到。他几乎有点失望。出门时他下意识地帮你把书摆整齐，然后瞪着你，好像是你逼他这么做的。\n# en: Inspect him back\nopt.right.label [492398] = 反过来检查他\n# en: While he's in your trunk, you inspect his clipboard. It's mostly a drawing of a horse, quite good. You hold it up for the corridor to see. He leaves. Quickly.\nopt.right.say.1 [1345c9] = 他在翻你箱子的时候，你检查了他的记录板。上面主要是一匹马的素描，画得挺好。你把它举起来给走廊里的人看。他走了。很快。\n# en: He catches you reading his clipboard. \"Interfering with an inspection,\" he says. \"Detention.\" He covers the clipboard with his hand as he goes.\nopt.right.say.2 [6083e9] = 他抓到你在看他的记录板。“妨碍检查，”他说，“禁闭。”他出门时用手捂着记录板。\n\n@event bram_rumour\n# en: The Rumour He Started\ntitle [e30018] = 他起的谣言\n# en: There's a rumour going round that you cried at the Sorting Mirror. There's a rumour that you're afraid of owls. People are being very nice to you about the owls. You know exactly where it started.\ntext [99239f] = 有谣言说你在分院镜前哭了。有谣言说你怕猫头鹰。大家因为猫头鹰的事对你格外和气。你很清楚谣言是从哪儿起的。\n# en: Students whispering behind their hands in a corridor as one student passes, a big prefect at the far end leaning on a wall, looking innocent.\nscene [4e16f6] = 一个学生走过时，走廊里的学生们捂着嘴窃窃私语，一个高大的级长靠在远处的墙上，一脸无辜。\n# en: Rise above it\nopt.left.label [fe2376] = 不予理会\n# en: You let it run. It dies in a fortnight, as rumours do. Bram looks faintly cheated. You catch him later telling a first-year that the owl one wasn't true, quite firmly.\nopt.left.say.1 [2874b4] = 你由它传。两周后它自己就消了，谣言都这样。布拉姆看上去有点被骗了的样子。后来你撞见他非常坚决地告诉一个一年级生，猫头鹰那个不是真的。\n# en: Start one back\nopt.right.label [b11062] = 也起一个\n# en: By Friday the whole school believes Bram sleeps with a night-light shaped like a duck. He doesn't deny it fast enough. It is, it turns out, true.\nopt.right.say.1 [4f67c6] = 到周五，全校都相信布拉姆睡觉要点一盏鸭子形状的夜灯。他否认得不够快。后来发现，这是真的。\n\n@event bram_hall_tax_escalated\n# en: The Hall Tax, Escalated\ntitle [d9d876] = 楼梯税升级\n# en: The hall tax has spread. There's a tin at the foot of the west stairs now, too, and a hand-lettered sign, and Bram behind it looking enormously pleased with himself. A queue of first-years are emptying their pockets.\ntext [706168] = 楼梯税扩张了。西楼梯脚下现在也放了个罐子，立着一块手写的牌子，布拉姆站在后面，得意得不得了。一队一年级生正在掏空口袋。\n# en: A big prefect standing at the foot of a second staircase with a collecting tin, a hand-lettered sign beside him reading WEST STAIRS, a queue of first-years.\nscene [3d6de2] = 第二道楼梯脚下，一个高大的级长守着一个募捐罐，旁边一块手写牌子写着“西楼梯”，排着一队一年级生。\n# en: Pay, and the first-years' too\nopt.left.label [9e3697] = 付钱，连一年级生的也付\n# en: You drop in enough for the whole queue. The first-years look at you like you've slain a dragon. Bram looks at the tin, and at you, and for some reason shuts it. The sign is gone the next day.\nopt.left.say.1 [7b41c7] = 你投进去的钱够整队人用。一年级生们看着你，像你刚屠了一条龙。布拉姆看看罐子，又看看你，不知为什么把它盖上了。第二天牌子就没了。\n# en: Organise a boycott\nopt.right.label [6b196e] = 组织抵制\n# en: By lunch, nobody in the castle is using the west stairs. They're using the windows, a laundry chute and, in one case, a broom. Bram stands by his empty tin until dinner. Then he quietly takes the sign down.\nopt.right.say.1 [80db6b] = 到午饭时，城堡里没有一个人走西楼梯。大家走窗户、走洗衣滑道，还有一个人骑了扫帚。布拉姆守着他的空罐子一直站到晚饭。然后他悄悄把牌子摘了。\n# en: Nobody joins your boycott. The stairs are the shortest way to lunch. Bram waves at you, cheerfully, from behind his tin.\nopt.right.say.2 [4078f4] = 没人加入你的抵制。那道楼梯是去吃午饭最近的路。布拉姆在罐子后面愉快地冲你挥手。\n\n@event bram_after_curfew\n# en: Caught After Curfew\ntitle [b4b659] = 宵禁后被抓\n# en: Bram's lantern catches you halfway up the north stairs, an hour after curfew. He smiles, slowly. He's been waiting all year for this. \"Well,\" he says. \"Well, well, well.\"\ntext [3e3aef] = 宵禁后一个钟头，布拉姆的灯光照住了半截北楼梯上的你。他慢慢地笑了。这一刻他等了一整年。“哎呀，”他说，“哎呀呀呀。”\n# en: A dark castle corridor at night, a lantern held high by a big prefect, a student caught halfway up a staircase.\nscene [1f37bb] = 夜里漆黑的城堡走廊，一个高大的级长高举着灯，一个学生被堵在楼梯半中间。\n# en: Take the detention\nopt.left.label [ca5031] = 认了禁闭\n# en: \"Fair,\" you say. It throws him; he'd prepared a speech. He writes you up. Then, as you go, he says, \"Use the east stairs next time. They don't creak.\" You decide not to wonder why he told you.\nopt.left.say.1 [351188] = “公平。”你说。这让他措手不及；他准备了一篇讲话。他给你记了一笔。然后你走的时候他说：“下次走东楼梯。那儿不响。”你决定不去想他为什么告诉你。\n# en: Talk your way past\nopt.right.label [f5bc3b] = 糊弄过去\n# en: You tell him Professor Vey sent you for a book. He doesn't believe you. But he can't prove it, and he's not brave enough to ask Vey. He lets you go, fuming.\nopt.right.say.1 [0d6c63] = 你告诉他是维伊教授叫你来拿一本书。他不信。可他证明不了，又没胆子去问维伊。他放你走了，气得冒烟。\n# en: You try a story about sleepwalking. He lets you finish. Then he writes you up for curfew and for \"insulting my intelligence\", which he underlines.\nopt.right.say.2 [0c20bb] = 你编了个梦游的故事。他让你讲完。然后以违反宵禁和“侮辱我的智商”给你记了一笔，后半句还画了线。\n\n@event bram_rematch\n# en: The Rematch\ntitle [c607c6] = 再战\n# en: Bram has been practising. Everyone knows; you've heard him on the duelling green every evening for a month. Tonight he's waiting for you there. \"Last time doesn't count,\" he says. \"Again. Properly.\"\ntext [fd5be7] = 布拉姆一直在练。人人都知道；一个月来你每晚都能在决斗草坪上听见他。今晚他在那儿等你。“上次不算，”他说，“再来。认真的。”\n# en: The duelling green at dusk, a big student in a prefect's sash pulling on his gloves, a crowd forming a ring around him.\nscene [1f1797] = 黄昏的决斗草坪，一个佩着级长绶带的高大学生在戴手套，周围的人群围成一圈。\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.left.label [4bf72b] = 今晚不行\n# en: \"Scared,\" says Bram, but he doesn't sound sure. He practises alone until dark. You can hear him from your window.\nopt.left.say.1 [a8e7d7] = “怕了。”布拉姆说，可他听上去并不确定。他一个人练到天黑。你在窗前都听得见。\n# en: Accept\nopt.right.label [b26149] = 应战\n# en: He's better. Much better. You win anyway, by one exchange. He lies on the grass looking at the sky for a while. \"A month,\" he says. \"A whole month.\" Then he laughs, and you help him up, and he doesn't let go of your hand at once.\nopt.right.say.1 [661cf2] = 他进步了。进步很大。你还是赢了，只多一个回合。他躺在草地上看了一会儿天。“一个月，”他说，“整整一个月。”然后他笑了，你把他拉起来，他没有马上松开你的手。\n# en: He wins. He stands over you, breathing hard, and doesn't gloat, and holds out a hand. \"Now we're even,\" he says. \"Now it's interesting.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [b79cdc] = 他赢了。他站在你面前喘着粗气，没有得意，伸出一只手。“现在我们扯平了，”他说，“现在才有意思。”\n\n@event bram_steps_in\n# en: He Steps In\ntitle [9d5d38] = 他挺身而出\n# en: Two fourth-years have a first-year backed against the boathouse wall. Before you can move, Bram is there, between them, sash and all. \"Problem?\" he says. There are two of them, and they're bigger. He doesn't step aside.\ntext [64e58b] = 两个四年级生把一个一年级生堵在船屋墙边。你还没来得及动，布拉姆已经到了，挡在他们中间，绶带一样不少。“有问题？”他说。他们有两个人，而且比他壮。他没让开。\n# en: A big prefect standing between two older students and a frightened first-year at the boathouse, another student arriving at a run.\nscene [4eae20] = 船屋旁，一个高大的级长挡在两个高年级生和一个吓坏了的一年级生之间，另一个学生正跑过来。\n# en: Stand beside him\nopt.left.label [7359d5] = 站到他身边\n# en: You step up next to him. One of them shoves you into the wall for form's sake, and then they decide it isn't worth it. Bram watches them go. Then he looks at you as if you've done something he can't file. \"Don't make it weird,\" he says. He walks the first-year back to the castle himself.\nopt.left.say.1 [c1e5cb] = 你走上前，站到他身边。他们中有一个为了面子把你推到墙上，然后觉得不值得。布拉姆看着他们走远。然后他看着你，好像你做了件他没法归档的事。“别弄得怪怪的。”他说。他亲自把那个一年级生送回了城堡。\n# en: Run for a professor\nopt.right.label [0fdbff] = 跑去找教授\n# en: You bring Brask at a run. By the time you get there it's over: Bram has a split lip and the first-year is fine. He doesn't say thank you. He doesn't give you a demerit that week, either.\nopt.right.say.1 [9cd2de] = 你飞奔着把布拉斯克找来了。等你们赶到，事情已经结束了：布拉姆嘴唇裂了，一年级生没事。他没说谢谢。那一周他也没给你记过。\n\n@event bram_fathers_shadow\n# en: His Father's Shadow\ntitle [3d707b] = 他父亲的影子\n# en: There's a big man in the entrance hall with Bram's jaw, talking to Bram in a low steady voice. Bram is standing very straight and getting smaller. Then the man sees you on the stairs. \"Is that the one?\" he says. \"The one beating you?\"\ntext [de1169] = 门厅里有个高大的男人，有布拉姆的下颌，正用低沉平稳的声音跟布拉姆说话。布拉姆站得笔直，却越来越小。然后那男人看见了楼梯上的你。“就是这个？”他说，“打败你的那个？”\n# en: A big man in an old-fashioned prefect's pin standing in the entrance hall with a student who looks smaller than usual, another student watching from the stairs.\nscene [e2f9bd] = 门厅里，一个别着老式级长徽章的高大男人和一个看上去比平时小了一圈的学生站在一起，另一个学生在楼梯上看着。\n# en: Walk on as if you didn't hear\nopt.left.label [b28ef3] = 装作没听见，继续走\n# en: You keep walking. Behind you, Bram says nothing at all. That evening he finds you, and stands there, and says, \"You didn't hear that.\" \"Hear what?\" you say. He nods once and goes.\nopt.left.say.1 [bb83fc] = 你继续往前走。你身后，布拉姆什么都没说。那天晚上他找到你，站在那儿，说：“你什么都没听见。”“听见什么？”你说。他点了一下头，走了。\n# en: Tell his father he's the best prefect here\nopt.right.label [604413] = 告诉他父亲他是这里最好的级长\n# en: The man looks at you as if a chair had spoken. Bram looks at you as if you've hit him. Later, he finds you. \"Why,\" he says. Not a question. You shrug. He gives you no demerits for a month, and never says why either.\nopt.right.say.1 [623d58] = 那男人看着你，像看见一把椅子开口说话。布拉姆看着你，像挨了你一拳。后来他找到你。“为什么。”他说。不是在问。你耸耸肩。他一个月没给你记过，也从没说为什么。\n\n@event corvin_locked_out\n# en: Locked Out\ntitle [ce7443] = 被锁在门外\n# en: There's a new padlock on your door. Corvin is leaning beside it, spinning a key on one finger. \"Funny thing,\" he says. \"Locks. They don't care whose side you're on. Make it worth my while.\"\ntext [4507a9] = 你的门上多了一把新挂锁。科尔文靠在旁边，一根手指转着一把钥匙。“说来好笑，”他说，“锁。它们可不在乎你站哪边。让我觉得值。”\n# en: A dormitory door with a new padlock on it, a lanky student leaning beside it and spinning a key on one finger.\nscene [305985] = 宿舍门上挂着一把新挂锁，一个瘦高的学生靠在门边，一根手指上转着一把钥匙。\n# en: Pay him off\nopt.left.label [e88f0a] = 给钱打发他\n# en: He takes the coins, unlocks the door and bows you in. \"Pleasure doing business,\" he says. He sounds tired, not pleased. The padlock is gone in the morning.\nopt.left.say.1 [9ca632] = 他收下钱，开了锁，躬身请你进去。“合作愉快。”他说。他听上去很累，并不高兴。第二天早上挂锁不见了。\n# en: Pick it yourself\nopt.right.label [13d28e] = 自己撬开\n# en: You pick it in four minutes with two hairpins. Corvin watches, and stops spinning the key. \"Huh,\" he says. \"Huh.\" It's the first thing he's said to you in a month that isn't a joke.\nopt.right.say.1 [2a5991] = 你用两根发夹四分钟就撬开了。科尔文看着，钥匙不转了。“嗯，”他说，“嗯。”这是一个月来他对你说的第一句不是玩笑的话。\n# en: You can't. He lets you struggle for twenty minutes, then unlocks it without a word and walks off. It's worse than if he'd laughed.\nopt.right.say.2 [43ed52] = 你撬不开。他看着你折腾了二十分钟，然后一言不发地打开锁，走了。这比他笑出来还难受。\n\n@event corvin_bad_odds\n# en: Bad Odds\ntitle [284112] = 不看好你\n# en: Corvin is running a book on you: whether you'll fail Friday's mock paper. The odds aren't flattering. Half the common room has a coin on it. He catches your eye across the room and raises the chalk, as if to say: well?\ntext [81882e] = 科尔文在拿你开盘：赌你周五的模拟考会不会挂。赔率可不好看。半个公共休息室的人都押了钱。他隔着屋子和你对上视线，举起粉笔，像在说：怎么样？\n# en: A chalked odds board in a dim corner of the common room, a student's name on it with long odds against them, a lanky bookmaker taking coins.\nscene [8a04aa] = 公共休息室昏暗的角落里有一块粉笔写的赔率板，上面一个学生的名字赔率极低，一个瘦高的庄家在收钱。\n# en: Bet on yourself\nopt.left.label [42fb46] = 押自己\n# en: A wager on the mock: study for it.\nopt.left.hint [efcb7b] = 押模拟考：为它用功。\n# en: You put your coin on yourself, at his long odds. Then you study like you've never studied. You pass the mock. Corvin pays out, every coin, and says, \"Fine. I'll stop.\" He doesn't quite, but he shortens the odds.\nopt.left.say.1 [712863] = 你按他的高赔率押了自己一枚硬币。然后你拼了命地用功。你考过了模拟考。科尔文一枚不少地赔了钱，说：“行。我不开了。”他没完全停，不过把赔率调低了。\n# en: You put your coin on yourself and study all week. You pass, by one mark under his line. Corvin keeps your coin and chalks the result up with a flourish. \"Close,\" he says. \"Closer than the odds.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [037a1d] = 你押了自己一枚硬币，用功了一整周。你考过了，却比他画的线低了一分。科尔文收下你的硬币，花哨地把结果记在板上。“差一点，”他说，“比赔率近多了。”\n# en: Shrug it off\nopt.right.label [9485f8] = 一笑置之\n# en: You let it go. The book runs for a week and closes. Corvin wipes the board clean himself and doesn't start another. He nods to you after, which is something.\nopt.right.say.1 [480113] = 你没理会。那个盘开了一周就收了。科尔文亲手把赔率板擦干净，再没开新的。后来他冲你点了点头，这也算点什么。\n\n@event ilse_frost_door\n# en: Frost on the Door\ntitle [b53dff] = 门上的霜\n# en: Your door is sealed in ice, a hand thick, clear as glass. Someone has scratched a word into the frost: a Northern word you don't know. It doesn't sound kind. In the village, they say, a girl in white furs bought nothing today, and smiled.\ntext [6050ce] = 你的门被冰封住了，一掌厚，像玻璃一样透明。有人在霜上刻了一个词：一个你不认识的北境词。听起来不怎么友善。镇上的人说，今天有个穿白裘的女孩什么也没买，还笑了。\n# en: A dormitory door completely sealed in clear ice, a student with a kettle staring at it, a single word scratched into the frost.\nscene [bc78e9] = 一扇宿舍门完全封在透明的冰里，一个学生提着水壶盯着它，霜上刻着一个词。\n# en: Thaw it the slow way\nopt.left.label [eabfa1] = 慢慢化开它\n# en: Kettle after kettle. It takes all evening. When the last of it goes, the Northern word goes with it. You never find out what it meant, which might be the point.\nopt.left.say.1 [bdf414] = 一壶接一壶。花了一整晚。最后一块冰化掉时，那个北境词也跟着没了。你永远不会知道它是什么意思，也许这正是用意。\n# en: Go and find her\nopt.right.label [061e72] = 去找她\n# en: You find her by the frozen pond. \"Your door,\" you say. She looks at you without blinking. \"Your fault,\" she says. Then, after a long time, \"It was a stupid word. I take it back.\" The ice is gone when you get home.\nopt.right.say.1 [8a69ff] = 你在结冰的池塘边找到她。“你干的门。”你说。她一眨不眨地看着你。“你的错。”她说。过了很久：“那个词很蠢。我收回。”你回到家时冰已经没了。\n\n@event ilse_contempt\n# en: Beneath Her\ntitle [5bea57] = 不屑一顾\n# en: Ilse walks past you in the village square as if you were weather. Her private storm, by accident or not, empties itself into your shoes. Two villagers laugh. She doesn't look back.\ntext [83bc0b] = 伊尔泽在镇上广场与你擦肩而过，好像你只是天气。她的私人暴风雪，不知是不是故意，全倒进了你的鞋里。两个镇民笑了。她没回头。\n# en: A girl in white furs walking past another student in the village square without looking, a gust of snow following her that soaks the other student's shoes.\nscene [e122d7] = 镇上广场，一个穿白裘的女孩目不斜视地走过另一个学生身边，一阵风雪跟着她，浇湿了对方的鞋。\n# en: Say her name\nopt.left.label [7e575e] = 叫她的名字\n# en: She stops. She doesn't turn round. \"What,\" she says. You don't have an answer. Neither does she. But the storm over her head thins, just a little, before she goes.\nopt.left.say.1 [75989f] = 她停下了，没有转身。“什么事。”她说。你答不上来。她也是。可她走之前，头顶的风雪稀薄了一点点。\n# en: Let it go\nopt.right.label [7a5d2f] = 算了\n# en: You squelch home. It's the kind of small cold that lasts all day.\nopt.right.say.1 [7ac91d] = 你一路吧唧着鞋走回去。这种小小的寒意，会跟你一整天。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/breaks.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/breaks.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event brk_one_long_table\n# en: One Long Table\ntitle [ca265b] = 一张长桌\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The Great Hall has given up on house tables for the break. Everyone left eats at one long table by the fire: a handful of students, two professors and a cook who sits down with you, which nobody has ever seen.|Half the candles are out for the summer. The few of you left eat at one long table under the one lit chandelier, and the cook brings out whatever she likes.} {~Someone has started a game of passing the salt without asking.|A professor tells a story about the founders that is almost certainly libel.|The cook asks everyone what they'd change about her soup. Only one answer is the right one.}\ntext [8bb643] = {season == \"winter\": 假期里，大礼堂放弃了按学院分桌。留下的人都在炉火边的一张长桌上吃饭：几个学生，两位教授，还有一位跟你们坐在一起吃饭的厨娘，这事以前从没人见过。|夏天，一半的蜡烛都熄了。留下的寥寥几人在唯一亮着的吊灯下围着一张长桌吃饭，厨娘想端什么就端什么。} {~有人开始了一个不打招呼就传盐的游戏。|一位教授讲了个关于创始人的故事，几乎肯定是诽谤。|厨娘问每个人会怎么改她的汤。只有一个答案是对的。}\n# en: A vast empty dining hall with a single long table at one end, a handful of students and staff eating together under one chandelier.\nscene [0a13a6] = 一间空旷的巨大餐厅，一端只摆着一张长桌，几个学生和教职员在同一盏吊灯下一起吃饭。\n# en: Stay for the whole evening\nopt.left.label [af8c47] = 待一整晚\n# en: {~You stay until the fire is embers, and learn more about the staff in one evening than in a term. Your reading waits on the bench, unread.|You stay and talk until the cook throws you all out. It's the best supper of the year, and your essay notes never leave your bag.|You stay, and the table gets louder and warmer, and you forget the work you came down meaning to do.}\nopt.left.say.1 [be2a51] = {~你一直待到炉火只剩余烬，一个晚上了解的教职员比一整个学期还多。你的书搁在长凳上，一页没读。|你一直聊到厨娘把你们全轰出去。这是一年里最好的一顿晚饭，你的论文笔记一次也没从包里拿出来。|你待着，长桌越来越热闹、越来越暖和，你忘了下楼时本打算做的功课。}\n# en: Eat, then go and work\nopt.right.label [46534a] = 吃完就去用功\n# en: {~You eat fast and go back to your books. Through the floor, later, you can hear them laughing.|You take your bowl up to the library. The laughter follows you up the stairs, then thins.|You thank the cook, slip out, and get a good evening's work done in the quiet. It is very quiet.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5c3615] = {~你吃得飞快，回去看书了。后来，隔着地板，你能听见他们在笑。|你端着碗上了图书馆。笑声跟着你上了楼梯，然后渐渐稀了。|你谢过厨娘，溜了出来，在安静里做了一晚上好功课。非常安静。}\n\n@event brk_kitchen_pie\n# en: The Cook's Holiday Pie\ntitle [680b1c] = 厨娘的节日馅饼\n# en: The kitchens are nearly empty for the break, and the cook has decided to make the pie she never has time for in term: a pie the size of a cartwheel, with {season == \"winter\": a frost-apple filling and pastry stars on top.|a summer-berry filling and a pastry lake on top, with a pastry boat.} She looks at you. \"Hands,\" she says. \"Washed.\"\ntext [fe7f29] = 假期里厨房几乎空了，厨娘决定做那个她在学期里从来没空做的馅饼：一个车轮那么大的馅饼，{season == \"winter\": 霜苹果馅，上面盖着酥皮星星。|夏日莓果馅，上面盖着一片酥皮湖，湖上还有一条酥皮小船。} 她看着你。“手，”她说。“洗干净。”\n# en: A steamy castle kitchen, a red-faced cook rolling out pastry the size of a tabletop, a student up to the elbows in flour.\nscene [1e70a4] = 热气腾腾的城堡厨房里，一个红脸厨娘在擀一张桌面大的酥皮，一个学生两条胳膊沾满了面粉。\n# en: Help with the pastry\nopt.left.label [e46b4e] = 帮忙做酥皮\n# en: {~You roll and crimp and get flour in your eyebrows. The pie is enormous and lopsided, and the cook says it's the best she's made. You get the first slice.|Making pastry is just alchemy with butter, you realise. The cook agrees, and gives you a slice to prove it.|You stamp out pastry stars until your arms ache. The pie is glorious. You eat too much of it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9d54ad] = {~你擀、捏边，眉毛上都沾了面粉。馅饼巨大又歪斜，厨娘说这是她做过最好的。你分到了第一块。|你意识到，做酥皮就是加了黄油的炼金术。厨娘同意，还给了你一块来证明。|你压酥皮星星压到胳膊发酸。馅饼美极了。你吃得太多了。}\n# en: Lick the bowl and run\nopt.right.label [4308ed] = 舔完碗就跑\n# en: {~You take the bowl, a spoon and your leave. The cook shouts after you, without much heat.|The bowl is the best part of any pie, and you're not ashamed. The cook pretends to be furious.|You steal the bowl and eat it on the stairs. It's worth every word she says.}\nopt.right.say.1 [74d46e] = {~你拿了碗、一把勺子，然后告辞。厨娘在后面喊你，没什么火气。|碗才是任何馅饼最好的部分，你一点也不害臊。厨娘假装气坏了。|你偷了碗，坐在楼梯上吃。她说的每一句都值得。}\n\n@event brk_courtyard_quiet\n# en: The Empty Courtyard\ntitle [0a74ee] = 空荡荡的庭院\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Snow has filled the courtyard knee-deep and nobody has dug a path. The fountain is a cake of ice with a frozen fish in it, looking surprised.|The courtyard bakes in the sun with nobody to cross it. The fountain is low and warm, and a cat has claimed the rim.} You can hear the castle breathe.\ntext [c63e1e] = {season == \"winter\": 雪在庭院里积到膝盖深，没人铲出一条路。喷泉成了一块冰糕，里面冻着一条鱼，一脸惊讶。|庭院在太阳下烤着，没人穿过。喷泉的水很低，温温的，一只猫占了它的边沿。} 你能听见城堡在呼吸。\n# en: A castle courtyard with no one in it, the fountain still, a single student sitting on its rim.\nscene [039ad2] = 一座空无一人的城堡庭院，喷泉静止着，只有一个学生坐在它的边沿上。\n# en: Make the first tracks\nopt.left.label [8ee2c2] = 踩出第一行脚印\n# en: {season == \"winter\": You wade across it on purpose, then back, then in a large figure of eight. Tomorrow someone will wonder who did it, and you'll know.|You walk every flagstone you've hurried across all term, slowly, barefoot. The stones are hot, and so are your soles.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9a1478] = {season == \"winter\": 你故意蹚过去，又蹚回来，然后踩出一个大大的八字。明天会有人好奇是谁干的，而你会知道。|你光着脚，慢慢走过整个学期里匆匆穿过的每一块石板。石头很烫，你的脚底也很烫。}\n# en: Sit and do nothing\nopt.right.label [4e16b2] = 坐着什么都不做\n# en: {season == \"winter\": You sit on the fountain's icy rim and watch the snow land on nothing. Your feet go numb. You don't mind.|You sit by the cat and do nothing until the shadows move. The cat permits it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0ffe63] = {season == \"winter\": 你坐在喷泉结冰的边沿上，看雪落在空处。你的脚冻麻了。你不在乎。|你在猫旁边坐着，什么都不做，直到影子挪了位置。猫准许了。}\n\n@event brk_snowball_bram\n# en: Bram in the Snow\ntitle [fee04c] = 雪中的布拉姆\n# en: Bram has stayed for the break, and he is patrolling an empty courtyard because somebody must. He is writing in his ledger. There is nobody to write down. You have a snowball in your hand, and you don't remember making it.\ntext [8f2ddc] = 布拉姆假期留了下来，他在一个空荡荡的庭院里巡逻，因为总得有人巡。他在账本上写东西。没有人可以记。你手里有一个雪球，你不记得什么时候捏的。\n# en: A stern prefect with a red sash brushing snow off his ledger in a snowy courtyard, a snowball clearly having just hit him.\nscene [64e10d] = 雪中的庭院里，一个系红肩带的严肃级长正拍掉账本上的雪，显然刚被一个雪球砸中。\n# en: Throw it\nopt.left.label [542258] = 扔出去\n# en: It takes his sash clean off. For a moment his face is outraged. Then he puts down the ledger, very deliberately, and makes a snowball of his own. Twenty minutes later you're both soaked, and he's laughing, and he'll deny it by Monday.\nopt.left.say.1 [814d30] = 它把他的肩带直接打飞了。有那么一刻他一脸愤怒。然后他非常刻意地放下账本，自己捏了一个雪球。二十分钟后，你们俩都湿透了，他在笑，而到星期一他会否认这件事。\n# en: Drop it and say hello\nopt.right.label [02d583] = 扔掉它，打个招呼\n# en: \"Prefect.\" \"Student.\" He looks at the snowball melting at your feet, and at you. \"Very wise,\" he says, and walks on, and you think, just possibly, he was disappointed.\nopt.right.say.1 [235f5b] = “级长。”“同学。”他看看你脚边正在化的雪球，又看看你。“非常明智，”他说，然后走开了，你觉得，说不定，他有点失望。\n\n@event brk_library_alone\n# en: The Library to Yourself\ntitle [67cd41] = 独享图书馆\n# en: The library is yours. No queue for the good desk, no whispering, no one shelving loudly in the next row. Even the librarian's cat has gone off duty, and is asleep on the returns cart.\ntext [dd19fc] = 图书馆是你的了。好座位不用排队，没有窃窃私语，隔壁一排也没人哐哐地上架。连图书管理员的猫都下班了，在还书车上睡着。\n# en: A huge empty library with long shafts of light, one student lying on the floor between the stacks with a book held up overhead.\nscene [735b83] = 一座空旷的巨大图书馆，长长的光柱，一个学生躺在书架之间的地板上，把一本书举在头顶。\n# en: Read for the exams\nopt.left.label [6d9dfd] = 为考试读书\n# en: {~You work through the term's reading list in a week. It's dull, and your next exam will thank you.|You take the good desk and keep it all break, and the reading gets done.|You read until the light goes, and get further than you have all term.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ed946b] = {~你一个星期就读完了这学期的书单。很枯燥，你的下一场考试会感谢你。|你占了好座位，整个假期都没让出去，书也读完了。|你一直读到天黑，比这一整个学期读得都远。}\n# en: Read whatever you like\nopt.right.label [419fa9] = 想读什么读什么\n# en: {~You read a history of the castle's drains, a book of Northern poems and half a novel. None of it is on any syllabus. All of it is wonderful.|You find a shelf of old letters bound into books, and read strangers' lives all afternoon.|You read lying on the floor between the stacks, which you've wanted to do since your first week.}\nopt.right.say.1 [53dec4] = {~你读了一本城堡排水系统史、一本北方诗集，还有半本小说。没有一本在任何课程大纲上。每一本都很棒。|你找到一架装订成书的旧信件，一下午都在读陌生人的人生。|你躺在书架之间的地板上读书，这事你从入学第一周就想干了。}\n\n@event brk_kael_shore\n# en: Kael at the Water\ntitle [348fe9] = 水边的凯尔\n# en: Kael hasn't gone home for the break. He doesn't say why, and you don't ask. He's on a rock by the lake, skimming stones that leave a trail of frost across the water, and he moves up to make room without looking round.\ntext [d97fe6] = 凯尔假期没有回家。他没说为什么，你也没问。他坐在湖边的一块石头上打水漂，石子在水面上留下一道霜痕，他没回头，往旁边挪了挪给你腾地方。\n# en: A pale Northern student sitting alone on a rock at a lake's edge, skimming stones, frost on the stones around him.\nscene [cc4fa1] = 一个苍白的北方学生独自坐在湖边的一块石头上打水漂，周围的石头上结着霜。\n# en: Sit with him\nopt.left.label [6fadbf] = 陪他坐着\n# en: {~You sit. The rock is cold. Neither of you says much, and after a while he hands you a stone, and you skim it, badly. He almost smiles.|You sit with him until the light goes. Somewhere in the second hour, he starts talking about snow, and doesn't stop for a long time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1c2969] = {~你坐下了。石头很冷。你们俩都没怎么说话，过了一会儿他递给你一颗石子，你打了个水漂，打得很糟。他几乎笑了。|你陪他坐到天黑。第二个小时里的某个时候，他开始讲雪，讲了很久很久。}\n# en: Leave him his quiet\nopt.right.label [c00de6] = 让他一个人静静\n# en: {~You leave him to the stones. From the path you look back: his trail of frost is still going out across the lake, further than seems possible.|He's earned his quiet. You let him have it, and feel the lack of the company all evening.}\nopt.right.say.1 [26e26b] = {~你让他继续打水漂。你从小路上回头看：他那道霜痕还在往湖心延伸，远得不像是可能的。|这份安静是他应得的。你让给了他，整个晚上都觉得少了个伴。}\n\n@event brk_starfall_observatory\n# en: Starfall, Almost Alone\ntitle [837b59] = 星落，几乎独自一人\n# en: Starfall, and almost nobody left to see it. Seraphine is lying on the observatory floor with the dome open, watching the stars fall. \"I painted this,\" she says, without looking at you. \"Last spring. I didn't know you'd be in it.\"\ntext [7ef3b7] = 星落时节，却几乎没人留下来看。瑟拉芬躺在观星台的地板上，穹顶打开着，看星星落下。“我画过这个，”她没看你就说。“去年春天。我不知道你会在画里。”\n# en: An observatory dome open to a sky full of falling stars, a seer in a paint-stained smock lying on the floor looking up.\nscene [bc5085] = 观星台的穹顶向满是流星的天空敞开，一个穿着沾满颜料的罩衫的预言者躺在地板上仰望。\n# en: Lie down beside her\nopt.left.label [7eadc8] = 在她身边躺下\n# en: {~You lie on the cold floor beside her and count stars until you lose count, and then keep going. Somewhere around midnight she says, \"That's the one,\" and doesn't say which.|You lie and watch. She tells you which ones she's already seen fall, last spring, in paint.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8d13eb] = {~你在她身边冰冷的地板上躺下，数星星数到数乱了，然后接着数。快到午夜时，她说：“就是那颗，”却没说是哪颗。|你躺着看。她告诉你哪些她已经见过落下了，去年春天，在颜料里。}\n# en: Ask what else she painted\nopt.right.label [1a2506] = 问她还画了什么\n# en: {~\"Nothing you'd want to know,\" she says, too quickly. She goes back to the stars. So do you, but it isn't quite the same.|She shakes her head and points at the sky. \"Watch. Don't ask. That's the whole trick.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [3c0e43] = {~“你不会想知道的，”她说得太快了。她又看向星星。你也是，可感觉不太一样了。|她摇摇头，指着天空。“看着。别问。诀窍就这些。”}\n\n@event brk_mir_project\n# en: The Break Project\ntitle [dab2a2] = 假期作品\n# en: Mir has had the workshop to themself for a week and it shows. In the middle of the floor is something the size of a wardrobe with too many gears. \"It's a machine,\" Mir says, \"for turning pages. Any book. Hands free.\" It's very loud. \"It's not finished.\"\ntext [b0c454] = 米尔独占工坊一个星期了，看得出来。地板中央有个衣柜那么大的东西，齿轮多得过分。“这是一台机器，”米尔说，“用来翻书。任何书。不用手。”它非常吵。“还没做完。”\n# en: A cluttered workshop with a half-built clockwork machine the size of a wardrobe, an artificer in goggles waving a spanner.\nscene [8dadc7] = 杂乱的工坊里有一台衣柜大小、做了一半的发条机器，一个戴护目镜的工匠挥着一把扳手。\n# en: Hold the spanner\nopt.left.label [53ea92] = 帮忙拿扳手\n# en: {~You hold the spanner, then the lamp, then a gear that's hotter than it looks. By midnight the machine turns one page, beautifully, and then eats the book. Mir is ecstatic.|You spend the evening as Mir's assistant, which mostly means holding things and being told about them. It's the best kind of evening.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a97b4e] = {~你拿着扳手，然后是灯，然后是一个比看起来烫的齿轮。到午夜，机器漂亮地翻了一页，然后把书吃了。米尔欣喜若狂。|你当了一晚上米尔的助手，主要就是拿东西、听他们讲这些东西。这是最好的那种夜晚。}\n# en: Suggest a smaller machine\nopt.right.label [7d7fb3] = 建议做台小一点的\n# en: {~\"Smaller,\" Mir repeats, as if you'd said \"duller\". But they write it down. \"Next break,\" they say, \"something the size of a teacup that turns pages very politely.\" You'll believe it when you see it.|Mir sulks for an hour, and then admits you're right, and then sulks about that.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a0e477] = {~“小一点，”米尔重复道，好像你说的是“无聊一点”。可TA还是记了下来。“下个假期，”TA说，“做一个茶杯大小、翻书翻得非常有礼貌的东西。”等亲眼见到再信吧。|米尔赌气赌了一个小时，然后承认你是对的，然后又为这个赌气。}\n\n@event brk_holiday_edition\n# en: The Holiday Edition\ntitle [f456fa] = 假日特刊\n# en: Nessa is putting out a holiday edition of the Ledger for the eleven people still in the castle. There is no news. Her quill keeps trying to invent some. \"Give me a story,\" Nessa says. \"Anything. Please. Before it writes that the lake has resigned.\"\ntext [b68e45] = 内莎在给城堡里剩下的十一个人出一期《流明纪事报》假日特刊。没有新闻。她的羽毛笔一直想编点出来。“给我一个故事，”内莎说。“什么都行。求你了。趁它还没写出湖辞职了。”\n# en: A cluttered newspaper office in an empty castle, an editor with ink on her nose surrounded by blank pages, a quill hovering.\nscene [e55499] = 空荡荡的城堡里一间杂乱的报社办公室，一个鼻尖沾着墨水的编辑被空白的稿纸围着，一支羽毛笔悬在空中。\n# en: Give her a real one\nopt.left.label [9dd190] = 给她一个真故事\n# en: {~You tell her the cook has been baking for a full school out of habit, and feeding the surplus to the gargoyles. She makes it the front page. It sells out, all eleven copies.|You tell her a portrait on the east stair has moved two frames along while nobody was looking. She runs it with a drawing. Bram buys three copies and pretends not to.}\nopt.left.say.1 [eadc69] = {~你告诉她，厨娘出于习惯还在按全校的人数烤东西，多出来的都喂了石像鬼。她把它做成了头版。全部卖光，十一份。|你告诉她，东楼梯上有幅肖像趁没人看的时候往旁边挪了两个画框。她配了张插图登了出来。布拉姆买了三份，还假装没买。}\n# en: Let the quill have its way\nopt.right.label [239ac7] = 让羽毛笔随便写\n# en: {~The quill writes that the lake has resigned and been replaced by a smaller, more manageable pond. Nessa prints it under protest. It's the most-read edition of the year.|The quill invents a romance between two gargoyles. Nessa refuses to print it, and then prints it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0e69c3] = {~羽毛笔写道，湖辞职了，由一个更小、更好管的池塘接任。内莎抗议着把它印了出来。这是一年里读的人最多的一期。|羽毛笔编了一段两座石像鬼之间的恋情。内莎拒绝印，然后还是印了。}\n\n@event brk_duel_trophies\n# en: Polishing the Trophies\ntitle [2b8187] = 擦奖杯\n# en: The duelling hall is empty except for the trophy cabinets, which are open, and a bucket of polish with a note on it: \"Volunteers. —Thorne\". Two hundred years of cups and shields stare at you, tarnished.\ntext [cb6253] = 决斗厅里空空荡荡，只有敞开着的奖杯柜，和一桶擦亮剂，上面贴着一张纸条：“志愿者。——索恩”。两百年的奖杯和盾牌暗淡无光地盯着你。\n# en: An empty duelling hall, trophy cabinets standing open, a student polishing a silver cup with a cloth.\nscene [15b247] = 空荡荡的决斗厅里，奖杯柜敞开着，一个学生正用布擦一只银杯。\n# en: Polish them all\nopt.left.label [a53131] = 全部擦一遍\n# en: {~It takes all afternoon. Halfway through you find a cup won by a student from your own corner of the country, a hundred years ago. You polish that one twice.|By the end your arms ache and the cabinets shine. Thorne, passing, nods once at the work. It feels like an honour.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ffd443] = {~擦了一整个下午。擦到一半，你发现一只奖杯是一百年前一个和你同乡的学生赢的。那一只你擦了两遍。|到最后你胳膊酸了，柜子亮了。索恩路过，冲这活儿点了一下头。感觉像一份荣誉。}\n# en: Read the names instead\nopt.right.label [f8d714] = 改读名字\n# en: {~You read every name on every cup instead. Some of them are on portraits in the halls. One of them is carved on the bench you sit on every morning.|You read the names and the years, and the losers' names scratched underneath by someone with a grudge. It's a whole history, written in spite.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5f3655] = {~你转而读了每只奖杯上的每个名字。有些人的肖像挂在走廊里。其中一个名字刻在你每天早上坐的那张长凳上。|你读名字和年份，还有被某个记仇的人刻在下面的输家的名字。这是一整部历史，用怨气写成。}\n\n@event brk_amaranth_restock\n# en: Restocking the Infirmary\ntitle [47f8a6] = 补充医务室\n# en: With the beds empty for the break, Sister Amaranth is restocking the infirmary from the bottom shelf to the top. She hands you a list and a ladder. \"Count everything,\" she says. \"Then count it again. The second count is the true one.\"\ntext [0b7ad8] = 假期里病床都空了，阿玛兰斯修女正在从最底层到最顶层补充医务室的存货。她递给你一张清单和一架梯子。“全部数一遍，”她说。“然后再数一遍。第二遍才是真的。”\n# en: A healer in a white apron counting jars on high shelves in an empty infirmary, a student up a ladder handing them down.\nscene [ceb987] = 空荡荡的医务室里，一个系白围裙的医者在清点高处架子上的罐子，一个学生站在梯子上往下递。\n# en: Count everything twice\nopt.left.label [61f8f1] = 全部数两遍\n# en: {~You count salves and tinctures until you dream in labels. The second count is different from the first in three places. Amaranth is not surprised.|By the end you know where every remedy in the castle lives. That will be useful, one day, at the worst possible time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2e18a1] = {~你数药膏和酊剂，数到做梦都是标签。第二遍和第一遍有三处不一样。阿玛兰斯并不意外。|到最后你知道了城堡里每一种药放在哪儿。总有一天这会派上用场，在最糟糕的时候。}\n# en: Ask her about the strange jars\nopt.right.label [76a103] = 问她那些奇怪的罐子\n# en: {~She tells you what's in the black jar on the top shelf, and then why it's on the top shelf. You come down the ladder very carefully.|\"That one,\" she says, \"is for a curse we haven't had since my first year. I keep it in case.\" She doesn't say which curse.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a178dc] = {~她告诉你顶层那只黑罐子里装的是什么，然后告诉你它为什么放在顶层。你下梯子的时候非常小心。|“那一罐，”她说，“是治一种诅咒的，从我入职第一年以后就没再见过。我留着以防万一。”她没说是哪种诅咒。}\n\n@event brk_stables_round\n# en: The Feeding Round\ntitle [8c5ca0] = 喂食巡视\n# en: Most of the stable hands have gone home, so Professor Brask does the feeding round herself, at dawn, every day. She doesn't ask for help. She hands you a bucket anyway.\ntext [8daf28] = 大部分马夫都回家了，所以布拉斯克教授每天黎明亲自巡视喂食。她不叫人帮忙。她还是递给了你一只桶。\n# en: A stable in winter or summer with every stall occupied, a professor and a student carrying buckets down the long aisle at dawn.\nscene [a98b67] = 冬天或夏天的马厩里每个隔栏都住着，黎明时分，一位教授和一个学生拎着桶走在长长的过道上。\n# en: Do the whole round\nopt.left.label [e3ddd4] = 走完整个巡视\n# en: {~You carry buckets until your shoulders burn. The hippogriffs eat out of your hand by the end. Brask says nothing, which from her is a speech.|You do the round, then the next day's round. On the third day a griffon lets you scratch its neck. Brask raises an eyebrow, impressed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3e4679] = {~你拎桶拎到肩膀火辣辣的。到最后，鹰头马身有翼兽们都从你手里吃东西了。布拉斯克什么都没说，从她嘴里出来，这就是一篇演讲。|你走完一轮，又走了第二天的一轮。第三天，一只狮鹫让你挠了它的脖子。布拉斯克挑了挑眉，很是佩服。}\n# en: Feed the old ones slowly\nopt.right.label [915d3e] = 慢慢喂那些老家伙\n# en: {~You spend the whole round with the oldest horse, who takes a full hour to eat and is worth every minute.|You feed the elderly creatures at the end of the row by hand. Brask lets you. \"They don't get visitors,\" she says.}\nopt.right.say.1 [630f1b] = {~你整个巡视都陪着最老的那匹马，它吃一顿要整整一个小时，每一分钟都值得。|你亲手喂这一排尽头那些上了年纪的动物。布拉斯克由着你。“它们没人来看，”她说。}\n\n@event brk_corridors_echo\n# en: The Echo\ntitle [ae6b95] = 回声\n# en: With nobody on the stairs, the lecture tower echoes like a well. You say \"hello\" on the first landing and it comes back, a moment later, from somewhere near the top, in a voice that isn't quite yours.\ntext [b26436] = 楼梯上没人，讲堂塔里的回声像井一样。你在第一个平台上说“你好”，过了一会儿，它从靠近塔顶的某个地方传回来，用的声音不太像你的。\n# en: An empty spiral staircase in a castle tower, a student halfway up shouting a word and listening to it come back.\nscene [9bd24e] = 城堡塔楼里空荡荡的旋转楼梯，一个学生站在半中间喊出一个词，听它传回来。\n# en: Run all the stairs\nopt.left.label [c80c48] = 把楼梯全跑一遍\n# en: {~You race your own echo to the top and lose. You lie on the top landing, wheezing, while it laughs at you on the way down.|You run up and down until you can't. The echo keeps going a while without you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [922018] = {~你跟自己的回声比赛跑到塔顶，输了。你躺在顶层平台上喘气，它一路笑着下去了。|你上上下下跑到跑不动。回声没有你也接着跑了一会儿。}\n# en: Talk to the echo\nopt.right.label [d7b254] = 跟回声说话\n# en: {~You ask the echo a question. It answers a different one. You spend an hour trying to work out which.|You tell the echo your name. It tells you someone else's, very faintly. You look it up later. A student from a hundred years ago.}\nopt.right.say.1 [144ba8] = {~你问了回声一个问题。它回答了另一个问题。你花了一个小时琢磨是哪一个。|你把你的名字告诉回声。它很轻很轻地告诉你另一个人的名字。后来你去查了。一个一百年前的学生。}\n\n@event brk_corvin_route\n# en: The Holiday Route\ntitle [c677a5] = 假日路线\n# en: Corvin is in the passage behind the third gargoyle with a lantern and a grin. \"Holidays,\" he says, \"are when the castle forgets to lock things. I've found three doors this week. One of them goes to a room full of hats.\" He offers you the lantern.\ntext [666369] = 科尔文在第三座石像鬼后面的通道里，提着灯笼，咧着嘴笑。“假期，”他说，“就是城堡忘了锁门的时候。我这个星期找到了三扇门。其中一扇通到一间全是帽子的屋子。”他把灯笼递给你。\n# en: A lanky student with a lock-pick behind one ear grinning beside a narrow secret passage behind a gargoyle, holding a lantern.\nscene [e60ce7] = 一个耳后夹着撬锁针的瘦高学生提着灯笼，在石像鬼后面一条狭窄的密道旁咧嘴笑着。\n# en: Take the lantern\nopt.left.label [3ae185] = 接过灯笼\n# en: {~The room full of hats is real. So is a second room, full of umbrellas, and a third you don't talk about. You put everything back. Mostly.|You explore until dawn. Corvin draws a map on the back of his hand and loses half of it washing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [50900a] = {~全是帽子的屋子是真的。还有第二间，全是雨伞，以及第三间，你们闭口不提。你把东西都放回去了。大部分吧。|你探索到天亮。科尔文在手背上画了张地图，洗手时洗掉了一半。}\n# en: Keep him company, not the route\nopt.right.label [1f06d8] = 陪他聊天，不走路线\n# en: {~You sit in the passage and talk instead. He tells you about the doors he hasn't tried, and why, and it's more interesting than the doors.|You stay with the lantern while he goes. He comes back with a hat. It suits you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cffcc7] = {~你坐在通道里跟他聊天。他跟你讲那些他还没试过的门，以及为什么没试，这比门本身还有意思。|他去探路时你守着灯笼。他回来时带了一顶帽子。挺适合你的。}\n\n@event home_cottage_rain\n# en: Rain on the Roof\ntitle [7c62e7] = 屋顶上的雨\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Sleet on the roof all day, and nobody going anywhere.|A summer storm, sudden and loud, and the whole house shut in together.} {~Someone gets the old cards out.|Someone starts telling a story you've heard a hundred times.}\ntext [1183cd] = {season == \"winter\": 雨夹雪打了一整天屋顶，谁也出不去。|一场夏日暴雨，来得又急又响，全家人都被关在了一起。} {~有人拿出了那副旧扑克牌。|有人开始讲一个你听过一百遍的故事。}\n# en: A cottage kitchen in heavy rain, a family playing cards around the table, a kettle steaming.\nscene [5899bb] = 大雨中的小屋厨房，一家人围着桌子打牌，水壶冒着热气。\n# en: Play every hand\nopt.left.label [4c6199] = 每一局都打\n# en: {~You lose three hands, win one, and lose the winnings in the next. Your family cheats outrageously. You remember why you love them.|You play until the rain stops, and then a few more, and pay your debts in coppers.}\nopt.left.say.1 [be2e97] = {~你输了三局，赢了一局，又在下一局把赢的输了回去。你的家人明目张胆地作弊。你想起了你为什么爱他们。|你一直打到雨停，又多打了几局，用铜板还清了债。}\n# en: Read by the window\nopt.right.label [9776cf] = 靠窗读书\n# en: {~You read by the rain-streaked window while the cards go on behind you. Every so often someone asks you to settle an argument, and you lose your page.|You get through a whole book while the storm goes over. Somebody saves you the last biscuit, and somebody else eats it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8ba445] = {~你在被雨打湿的窗边读书，身后的牌局还在继续。时不时有人让你评理，你就找不到读到哪儿了。|暴雨过去的时候，你读完了一整本书。有人给你留了最后一块饼干，又有人把它吃了。}\n\n@event home_cottage_photograph\n# en: The Picture on the Mantel\ntitle [4f9289] = 壁炉架上的画像\n# en: There's a new picture on the mantel: you, on the day you left for the academy, in robes two sizes too big. {final_year: It looks like someone else's child now.|You look terrified. You were.} Someone has polished the frame.\ntext [6b5ed3] = 壁炉架上多了一张新画像：你，出发去学院那天，穿着大了两号的长袍。{final_year: 现在看起来像别人家的孩子了。|你看上去吓坏了。你当时确实吓坏了。} 有人把画框擦得锃亮。\n# en: A small framed picture on a cottage mantelpiece of a child in oversized robes, a student holding it and smiling ruefully.\nscene [f8921f] = 小屋壁炉架上一幅小小的画像，画里的孩子穿着过大的长袍，一个学生拿着它，苦笑着。\n# en: Tell them what it's really like\nopt.left.label [b5aa1f] = 告诉他们那里真实的样子\n# en: {~You tell them about the stairs that move, the professor who throws chalk, the friend who can't make toast. They laugh in the right places, and look worried in the others.|You tell them everything you can, and not the rest. They know there's a rest. They let you keep it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [94a95d] = {~你跟他们讲会动的楼梯，扔粉笔的教授，烤不好面包的朋友。他们在该笑的地方笑，在别的地方一脸担心。|你把能说的都告诉了他们，剩下的没说。他们知道还有剩下的。他们让你留着。}\n# en: Put it back and say nothing\nopt.right.label [b2ea95] = 放回去，什么都不说\n# en: {~You put it back, straighter. Later, you see someone straighten it again.|You put it back. Some things are better on a mantel than said out loud.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b9954e] = {~你把它放回去，摆得更正了些。后来，你看见有人又把它摆正了一次。|你把它放了回去。有些事放在壁炉架上，比说出口要好。}\n\n@event home_fields_harvest_help\n# en: All Hands\ntitle [b6ce9a] = 全员上阵\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The neighbours are clearing a fallen tree from the far field before the thaw, and every pair of hands is wanted.|The neighbours are getting in a late crop before the weather turns, and every pair of hands is wanted.} You still remember how. Your back remembers more.\ntext [d30748] = {season == \"winter\": 邻居们要在解冻前把一棵倒在远处田里的树清走，每一双手都用得上。|邻居们要赶在天气变坏前收完一茬晚熟的庄稼，每一双手都用得上。} 你还记得怎么干。你的腰记得更清楚。\n# en: A field at harvest or a frozen field being cleared, neighbours working in a line, a student in academy boots among them.\nscene [ffad2e] = 一片收获中或正在清理的冰冻田地，邻居们排成一行干活，一个穿学院靴子的学生夹在中间。\n# en: Work the whole day\nopt.left.label [2345e2] = 干上一整天\n# en: {~By sunset you're filthy and aching, the job is done, and a neighbour presses a coin on you you can't refuse.|You work till dark. Nobody mentions the academy once, and you like that more than you expected.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b939a0] = {~到日落时你又脏又疼，活儿干完了，一个邻居硬塞给你一枚硬币，你推都推不掉。|你一直干到天黑。没人提过一次学院，你喜欢这样，比你预想的还喜欢。}\n# en: Do a bit of quiet magic\nopt.right.label [c1bd86] = 用一点悄悄的魔法\n# en: {~A little lift here, a little warmth there. The work goes twice as fast. Nobody says anything, but an old neighbour watches your hands the whole time, and doesn't smile.|You make it easier than it should be. The neighbours thank you warily, as if you'd brought a stranger.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7cf34a] = {~这里抬一下，那里暖一下。活儿快了一倍。没人说什么，可一位老邻居一直盯着你的手，没有笑。|你让活儿变得比它该有的轻松。邻居们小心翼翼地道谢，好像你带来了一个陌生人。}\n\n@event home_fields_old_friend\n# en: Someone You Grew Up With\ntitle [ad478a] = 一起长大的人\n# en: {~Someone you grew up with is leaning on the gate at the end of the lane, the way they always did. They're married now.|At the gate, the friend from before. You've both changed; the gate hasn't. They've taken over the mill, and never mean to leave.}\ntext [faf8cf] = {~一个和你一起长大的人倚在小巷尽头的门边，就像他们以前一直那样。他们结婚了。|门边站着从前的那个朋友。你们都变了；那扇门没变。他们接手了磨坊，从没打算离开。}\n# en: Two young people leaning on a field gate at sunset, one in work clothes, one in academy robes, talking easily.\nscene [d8bc0d] = 日落时，两个年轻人倚在田间的大门上轻松地聊天，一个穿着干活的衣服，一个穿着学院长袍。\n# en: Talk until it's dark\nopt.left.label [3e41aa] = 一直聊到天黑\n# en: {~You talk until you can't see each other's faces. It's easy, the way it is with people who knew you before you were anyone.|You talk about nothing for two hours. It's the best conversation you've had in months.}\nopt.left.say.1 [37edc4] = {~你们聊到看不清彼此的脸。很轻松，就像和那些在你还谁都不是时就认识你的人在一起一样。|你们闲聊了两个小时。这是你几个月来最好的一次谈话。}\n# en: Tell them about the academy\nopt.right.label [e245d0] = 跟他们讲学院的事\n# en: {~They listen politely. Halfway through you notice they've stopped asking questions, and you stop.|You tell them everything. They say \"huh\" a lot. You realise you're talking about a place they'll never see.}\nopt.right.say.1 [72b9ea] = {~他们礼貌地听着。讲到一半你发现他们不再提问了，你就停了。|你把一切都告诉了他们。他们一直说“嗯”。你意识到，你在讲一个他们永远也不会见到的地方。}\n\n@event home_fields_night_sky\n# en: The Sky Without Towers\ntitle [bd172d] = 没有塔楼的天空\n# en: You stay out in the field until the light goes. At home the sky comes right down to the ground on every side. No towers, no observatory dome, just stars from one edge to the other. {month == 11 or month == 12: And Starfall, which looks different out here: closer, somehow, and less like a show.|You'd forgotten how many there are.}\ntext [1fc3e2] = 你在田里一直待到天光散尽。在家里，天空四面八方一直垂到地面。没有塔楼，没有观星台的穹顶，只有从这头到那头的星星。{month == 11 or month == 12: 还有星落，在这里看起来不一样：不知怎么的更近了，也不那么像一场表演了。|你都忘了有这么多。}\n# en: A student lying on their back in a dark field at night, the sky enormous and full of stars, no buildings anywhere.\nscene [7af3d4] = 夜里，一个学生仰面躺在漆黑的田野上，天空巨大而布满星星，四周没有一座建筑。\n# en: Name every one you know\nopt.left.label [8ebd51] = 叫出每一颗你认识的\n# en: {~You name them until the dew soaks through. You know more than you did last year. You know which ones are lying.|You name them aloud, softly. A fox somewhere across the field barks, as if correcting you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9bd12a] = {~你一颗颗叫着它们的名字，直到露水渗透了衣服。你比去年认识得多了。你知道哪些在撒谎。|你轻声念出它们的名字。田野那头某处一只狐狸叫了一声，像是在纠正你。}\n# en: Just lie there\nopt.right.label [de0f5b] = 就那样躺着\n# en: {~You don't name anything. You just lie there until you're cold, and walk home feeling enormous and very small.|You lie in the grass and let the sky be a sky. It's the first time in months nothing has been a lesson.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cfae81] = {~你什么都没叫。你就那样躺着直到冷了，走回家时觉得自己既巨大又渺小。|你躺在草里，让天空只是天空。这是几个月来第一次，什么都不是一堂课。}\n\n@event home_market_stallholder\n# en: The Stallholder Remembers\ntitle [c58e20] = 摊主还记得\n# en: The cheese-seller remembers you. \"The one who went to the magic school!\" she tells everyone in earshot. \"Do us a trick, then.\" The whole queue turns round to look.\ntext [1e19f8] = 卖奶酪的记得你。“去魔法学校的那个！”她冲着所有听得见的人说。“那给我们变个戏法吧。”整个队伍都转过来看。\n# en: A country market stall piled with cheeses, a cheerful stallholder pointing at a student and laughing.\nscene [2e31f6] = 乡村集市上堆满奶酪的摊位，一个快活的摊主指着一个学生大笑。\n# en: Do a small trick\nopt.left.label [62f443] = 变个小戏法\n# en: {~You make a cheese float, very slightly. The queue applauds. The cheese-seller gives you the cheese. Your family is mortified and proud in equal parts.|You light a candle with a look. A child bursts into tears of joy. You get a discount for life.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9563fa] = {~你让一块奶酪浮了起来，就一点点。队伍里响起掌声。卖奶酪的把奶酪送给了你。你的家人又难为情又骄傲，两者不相上下。|你看了一眼就点燃了一根蜡烛。一个孩子喜极而泣。你得到了终身折扣。}\n# en: Laugh and buy some cheese\nopt.right.label [bd5f9e] = 笑一笑，买点奶酪\n# en: {~\"Next time,\" you say, and buy the cheese. She's disappointed and gives you a bigger piece anyway.|You buy the cheese and refuse the trick. The queue loses interest. You're oddly relieved.}\nopt.right.say.1 [432ef5] = {~“下次吧，”你说，买了奶酪。她很失望，还是给了你更大的一块。|你买了奶酪，没变戏法。队伍失去了兴趣。你莫名松了口气。}\n\n@event home_market_letter_home\n# en: A Letter for the Castle\ntitle [7a2624] = 寄往城堡的信\n# en: The market has a post counter, and you find yourself writing a letter to the castle: to someone you miss already. It's strange, missing the castle from home. You didn't expect to.\ntext [93f070] = 集市上有个邮政柜台，你发现自己在写一封寄往城堡的信：写给一个你已经开始想念的人。在家里想念城堡，这感觉很奇怪。你没料到会这样。\n# en: A village post counter at a country market, a student writing a letter on the counter with a borrowed pen.\nscene [f0ae59] = 乡村集市上的一个邮政柜台，一个学生用借来的笔在柜台上写信。\n# en: Write a long one\nopt.left.label [9fc2eb] = 写一封长的\n# en: {~You write four pages and pay for the postage twice. It'll arrive before you do, and be read aloud, probably, to people you'd rather it wasn't.|You write until the postmaster coughs. It's a good letter. You'll find out how good when you get back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d69c0b] = {~你写了四页，付了两份邮费。它会比你先到，而且多半会被大声念给一些你宁愿他们没听到的人听。|你一直写到邮政局长咳嗽。是封好信。你回去就知道有多好了。}\n# en: Write two lines and go\nopt.right.label [0b833a] = 写两行就走\n# en: {~\"Home is fine. Miss you. See you soon.\" It's enough. It's more than enough.|You write two lines, then a third, then stop before it turns into a confession.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7154c5] = {~“家里挺好。想你。很快见。”够了。足够了。|你写了两行，然后第三行，然后在它变成一封告白之前停了笔。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/calendar_2b.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/calendar_2b.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event enrolment\n# en: Enrolment\ntitle [cbadef] = 选课\n# en: {term_index == 1: Your first term. The registrar slides a list across the desk: four courses open to first-years.|A new term, and a new list. Courses you've passed are gone from it; new levels have appeared.} {member(thesis): Choose one; the thesis takes your other slot.|Choose two.} Course fees are eight crowns. A notice by the door says the library sets out revision tables before exam month: an afternoon there counts toward every exam.\ntext [dbd945] = {term_index == 1: 你的第一个学期。教务员把一张清单推过桌面：四门对一年级开放的课。|新学期，新清单。你已通过的课不见了，新的级别冒了出来。}{member(thesis): 选一门；毕业论文占了你另一个名额。|选两门。}课程费八克朗。门边的告示写着：考试月之前，图书馆会摆出复习桌；在那里待一个下午，对每门考试都有帮助。\n# en: The registrar's hall at the start of term, students queueing at long tables with course lists that rewrite themselves.\nscene [6d30e9] = 开学时的教务大厅，学生们在长桌前排队，手里的课程表会自己改写。\n# en: There is nothing left for you to take\nopt.none.label [b606eb] = 没有你能选的课了\n# en: The registrar checks the list twice. Nothing on it is open to you this term. \"Enjoy the quiet,\" she says, without much conviction.\nopt.none.say.1 [3ba6f1] = 教务员把名单核对了两遍。这学期上面没有一门课对你开放。“享受清静吧。”她说，语气不太确定。\n\n@event club_fair_v3\n# en: The Club Fair\ntitle [ce4fee] = 社团招新会\n# en: The Club Fair fills the courtyard. Every table wants you; every captain swears theirs is the one that matters. {rel(lyra) >= 6: Lyra is at the Duelling Circle's table, pretending not to watch which way you walk.|}\ntext [fa6309] = 社团招新会挤满了庭院。每张桌子都想要你，每位社长都发誓自家才是最要紧的。{rel(lyra) >= 6: 莱拉站在决斗社的桌边，假装没在看你往哪儿走。|}\n# en: The courtyard full of recruiting tables, banners snapping, a duelling demonstration drawing a crowd.\nscene [72616b] = 庭院里摆满招新桌，旗帜猎猎作响，一场决斗示范引来一圈围观者。\n# en: {member(club): Stay with your club|Not this year}\nopt.none.label [9e77c4] = {member(club): 留在你的社团|今年算了}\n# en: {member(club): Another year with the same table.|You can join at next year's fair.}\nopt.none.hint [5c873f] = {member(club): 和同一张桌子再过一年。|明年的招新会还能加入。}\n# en: {member(club): {~You stop by your own club's table to help hand out leaflets. Someone asks you if it's worth joining. You say yes and mean it.|You walk past every other table with the ease of someone already spoken for.}|You collect three leaflets and a free biscuit, and sign nothing.}\nopt.none.say.1 [b61f4a] = {member(club): {~你在自己社团的桌边帮忙发传单。有人问你值不值得加入。你说值得，而且是真心的。|你从别的桌子前走过，带着一种名花有主的从容。}|你领了三张传单和一块免费饼干，什么也没签。}\n# en: Resign from your club\nopt.quit.label [990542] = 退出你的社团\n# en: You can join again at next year's fair, as a new member.\nopt.quit.hint [5bb00e] = 明年的招新会还能以新成员身份重新加入。\n# en: {~You hand back your club pin at the table where you got it. It feels smaller than it did.|You resign. Your evenings are yours again, and nobody will expect you anywhere.}\nopt.quit.say.1 [0a0a06] = {~你在当初领徽章的那张桌子前把社团徽章交了回去。它看起来比当时小了。|你退出了。晚上又归你自己了，也没人会在哪儿等你。}\n\n@event job_board_visit\n# en: The Job Board\ntitle [377c1b] = 招工告示板\n# en: Notices for work, three layers deep: the library, the Gilded Newt, Pell & Daughters, the courier loft. Each wants someone reliable. Each says so twice.{term and (month == 4 or month == 10):  This week every notice ends the same way: new staff start next term.|}\ntext [b2a7f4] = 招工启事叠了三层：图书馆、镀金蝾螈酒馆、佩尔父女药铺、信使阁楼。每家都要个靠得住的人，每家都说了两遍。{term and (month == 4 or month == 10):这周每张启事的结尾都一样：新人下学期开始上工。|}\n# en: A cork board crowded with notices, a student running a finger down a column of shift times.\nscene [62cc7b] = 一块挤满告示的软木板，一名学生用手指顺着一列排班时间往下划。\n# en: Just looking\nopt.look.label [4af612] = 只是看看\n# en: You read every notice twice and take none of them down.\nopt.look.say.1 [5234e4] = 你把每张告示都读了两遍，一张也没揭下来。\n# en: Hand in your notice\nopt.quit.label [e9edc6] = 递交辞呈\n# en: You can find work again later.\nopt.quit.hint [974ede] = 以后还能再找工作。\n# en: You hand in your notice. Your evenings are yours again; so is your empty purse.\nopt.quit.say.1 [3dd0ab] = 你递交了辞呈。晚上又归你自己了，空荡荡的钱袋也是。\n\n@event job_board_odd_job\n# en: One Evening's Work\ntitle [edcb06] = 一晚上的活\n# en: Every notice says \"from next term\", except one, pinned low and crooked: {~the refectory wants hands for the end-of-term clear-out|the porters want someone to carry exam desks back to the store rooms|a professor wants her rooms boxed up before the break}. One evening, paid on the night.\ntext [347c76] = 每张启事都写着“下学期起”，只有一张例外，钉得又低又歪：{~食堂要人手做学期末的大扫除|门房要人把考试用的课桌搬回储藏室|一位教授想在放假前把房间打包好}。就一晚上，当晚结钱。\n# en: A cork board of notices that all say \"from next term\", and one small card pinned low and crooked beneath them.\nscene [4dee36] = 一块软木板上的启事全写着“下学期起”，下面钉着一张又低又歪的小卡片。\n# en: Take the evening's work\nopt.left.label [f36d9c] = 接下这晚的活\n# en: {~You scrub, carry and stack until the lamps go out. The pay is counted into your hand, coin by coin, by someone too tired to talk.|It is heavier work than the card let on. Your arms ache all the next day, and your purse is heavier for it.|You finish late, sore and paid. Nobody remembers your name, which is the restful part.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2de5ef] = {~你擦洗、搬运、码放，一直干到熄灯。工钱由一个累得不想说话的人一枚一枚数到你手里。|活比卡片上写的重。第二天你胳膊酸了一整天，钱袋也沉了些。|你干到很晚，浑身酸痛，钱也拿到了。没人记得你的名字，这倒挺省心。}\n# en: Leave it for someone else\nopt.right.label [43df3f] = 留给别人吧\n# en: {~You leave the card where it is. Somebody poorer will want it more, and you want your evening.|You read it twice and walk on. The term is nearly over; so is your patience for work.|You let the card be. The evening goes on a long walk instead, which pays nothing and feels like a lot.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5f075c] = {~你让卡片留在原处。总有更缺钱的人更需要它，而你想要你的晚上。|你读了两遍，走开了。学期快结束了，你干活的耐心也是。|你没动那张卡片。这个晚上换成一次长长的散步，一分钱不挣，却觉得值。}\n\n@event break_plan\n# en: The Break\ntitle [e3e693] = 假期\n# en: {month == 5: The winter break.|The summer break.} Two months without lectures. The castle empties, mostly.\ntext [78e780] = {month == 5: 冬假。|暑假。}两个月没有课。城堡几乎空了。\n# en: The academy gates thrown open, barges loading trunks, a few students lingering on the steps with nowhere they'd rather be.\nscene [003006] = 学院大门敞开，驳船装着行李箱，几个学生在台阶上逗留，哪儿也不想去。\n# en: Stay at the castle\nopt.castle.label [04365a] = 留在城堡\n# en: Quiet corridors and secrets. Board 8 crowns a month.\nopt.castle.hint [6c24c3] = 安静的走廊和秘密。每月食宿 8 克朗。\n# en: The castle in the holidays is a different building: louder echoes, kinder staff, doors you've never seen open.\nopt.castle.say.1 [f9e1bd] = 假期里的城堡是另一座建筑：回声更响，职员更和气，还有你从没见开过的门。\n# en: Go home\nopt.home.label [c93952] = 回家\n# en: Family, old friends, free board.\nopt.home.hint [1bbb35] = 家人、老朋友、免费食宿。\n# en: The barge, the coach, the last mile on foot. Home is smaller than you remember, and warmer.\nopt.home.say.1 [0b9342] = 驳船、马车，最后一里路靠双脚。家比记忆中小，也比记忆中暖。\n# en: Work on campus\nopt.work.label [2ebfbe] = 留校打工\n# en: A holiday gig that pays well. Board 8 crowns a month.\nopt.work.hint [6b010a] = 一份工钱不错的假期零工。食宿每月 8 克朗。\n# en: You trade the holidays for wages. Your feet will remember it.\nopt.work.say.1 [e054a1] = 你用假期换了工钱。你的脚会记住这一切。\n# en: Start your internship\nopt.intern.label [744abc] = 开始实习\n# en: A stipend, a supervisor and a project.\nopt.intern.hint [abd74b] = 一份津贴、一位导师和一个课题。\n# en: You pack a trunk of notebooks and set off {member(intern_expedition): up the long road to the high plateau|{member(intern_field_station): across the moors to the field station|{member(intern_thornwick): on the coach to Thornwick, of all places|on the long coach to the capital}}}.\nopt.intern.say.1 [db63d2] = 你装了一箱笔记本，{member(intern_expedition): 踏上去高原的漫长山路|{member(intern_field_station): 穿过荒原前往野外站|{member(intern_thornwick): 坐上去荆木学院的马车，偏偏是那儿|坐上去王都的长途马车}}}。\n\n@event course_exam\n# en: Exam Day\ntitle [482384] = 考试日\n# en: Exam day for {track}. The hall is silent except for quills and somebody's knee bouncing under a desk. {~The paper is long.|The first question is one you revised.|The first question is one you didn't.|The invigilator turns the hourglass with a click you feel in your teeth.}\ntext [6e3a75] = {track}考试日。考场里鸦雀无声，只有羽毛笔的沙沙声，还有谁的膝盖在桌下抖个不停。{~试卷很长。|第一道题正好是你复习过的。|第一道题恰好是你没复习的。|监考老师把沙漏一翻，那一声咔哒你连牙齿都感觉得到。}\n# en: A silent exam hall in late light, rows of bent heads, an hourglass on the invigilator's desk.\nscene [43a564] = 傍晚光线里寂静的考场，一排排低着的头，监考桌上放着一只沙漏。\n# en: Answer what you know, carefully\nopt.left.label [7dfdbe] = 稳扎稳打，写你会的\n# en: {~You write slowly: everything you're sure of, and nothing you aren't.|You check every answer twice and hand in at the bell.|The first question is one you didn't revise, so you leave it and bank the rest.|You pace yourself by the hourglass and finish with sand to spare.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0fc20f] = {~你写得很慢：有把握的都写上，没把握的一个字也不写。|你每道题都检查两遍，铃响才交卷。|第一题你没复习，你就跳过它，把剩下的稳稳拿下。|你照着沙漏控制节奏，答完时沙子还剩不少。}\n# en: Go for top marks\nopt.right.label [4d6661] = 冲击高分\n# en: {~You take the hardest question first and gamble the essay on an idea you had at breakfast.|You write the answer you'd want to read, not the safe one.|The first question is one you didn't revise. You answer it anyway, boldly, from first principles.|You race the hourglass and write until the last grain, and past it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ab3ae7] = {~你先做最难的题，把整篇论述押在早饭时冒出的一个想法上。|你写下自己想读的答案，而不是稳妥的那个。|第一题你没复习。你还是答了，大胆地从基本原理推起。|你和沙漏赛跑，一直写到最后一粒沙落下，还多写了几笔。}\n\n@event track_warning\n# en: A Word About Your Attendance\ntitle [6bd5a3] = 关于你的出勤\n# en: {kind_is(track, club): A note about {track}: you haven't shown up once all term, and it has been noticed. Members who never come stop being members.|A note about {track}: your attendance and your efforts have slipped, and it has been noticed. One more term like this and there will be consequences.}\ntext [86e8d5] = {kind_is(track, club): 一张关于{track}的便条：你整个学期一次都没来，有人注意到了。从不露面的成员，迟早不再是成员。|一张关于{track}的便条：你的出勤和努力都在下滑，有人注意到了。再这样一个学期，就要有后果了。}\n# en: A folded note on official paper, the words \"performance\" and \"concern\" underlined twice.\nscene [7cac89] = 一张折起来的公文纸，“表现”和“关切”两个词下面各划了两道线。\n# en: Promise to do better\nopt.left.label [cba502] = 保证会改进\n# en: You write back a promise in your neatest hand. Now you'll have to keep it.\nopt.left.say.1 [5983e6] = 你用最工整的字回了一份保证。现在你得兑现它了。\n# en: Let it slide\nopt.right.label [344baa] = 随它去\n# en: You put the note in a drawer and skip the next session to prove a point. The note doesn't stop existing in the drawer.\nopt.right.say.1 [4ea3c0] = 你把便条塞进抽屉，还故意翘掉了下一次，以示态度。便条在抽屉里也照样存在。\n\n@event job_term_review\n# en: Another Term?\ntitle [9e843b] = 再干一学期？\n# en: The term is over, and so is the rota for {track}. A fresh one goes up for next term, and there's a gap where your name usually goes. {~Nobody would blame you either way.|Your feet have an opinion. So does your purse.|Someone has already pencilled a question mark next to it.}\ntext [7bad94] = 学期结束了，{track}的排班表也到头了。下学期的新表会贴出来，你平常写名字的地方空着一格。{~怎么选都没人会怪你。|你的脚有意见。你的钱袋也有。|已经有人在旁边用铅笔画了个问号。}\n# en: A staff rota pinned to a door, last term's names crossed off, a blank column waiting for next term's.\nscene [2d74da] = 门上钉着一张员工排班表，上学期的名字都划掉了，空出一栏等着下学期。\n# en: Stay on next term\nopt.left.label [7e4424] = 下学期继续干\n# en: {~You write your name in the gap. Another term of shifts, another term of wages.|Your name goes back on the rota, in ink this time.|You rub out the question mark and write your name over it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [007bb9] = {~你在空格里写上名字。又一学期的班，又一学期的工钱。|你的名字回到了排班表上，这次用的是墨水。|你擦掉问号，在上面写上了自己的名字。}\n# en: Resign\nopt.right.label [b07941] = 辞职\n# en: {~You hand in your notice, politely. The evenings are yours again; the purse is lighter for it.|You leave the gap empty. You can always find work again, if you need to.|You leave the question mark where it is. Someone else can answer it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [41ee3c] = {~你客客气气地递了辞呈。晚上又归你了；钱袋也因此轻了。|你让那一格空着。真需要的话，总还能再找活干。|你让那个问号留在原处。让别人去回答吧。}\n\n@event track_promotion\n# en: A Step Up\ntitle [39f03f] = 更上一层\n# en: {track} wants to promote you. More responsibility, more respect, and in the right places, more pay.\ntext [a932cb] = {track}想提拔你。更多责任，更多尊重，在某些地方，还有更多薪水。\n# en: A small ceremony in a cramped room, someone pinning a badge to a student's collar while the others applaud.\nscene [c03b55] = 狭小房间里的一场小仪式，有人把徽章别在一名学生的领口上，其他人鼓掌。\n# en: Accept\nopt.left.label [b26149] = 接受\n# en: The badge is a little crooked. You don't straighten it.\nopt.left.say.1 [7c4086] = 徽章有点歪。你没去扶正它。\n# en: Not yet\nopt.right.label [8dd27c] = 还不是时候\n# en: \"When you're ready, then.\" They mean it. The offer will come again.\nopt.right.say.1 [ffa0ad] = “那就等你准备好。”他们是认真的。这个机会还会再来。\n\n@event intern_application\n# en: Applications Open\ntitle [e7df06] = 开始申请\n# en: Summer research posts are open. Six notices, six seals, six lists of requirements. Apply to as many as you like: the first letter is an evening's work, and every one after it costs another evening and the postage.\ntext [c4837d] = 夏季研究岗位开放申请了。六张告示，六枚火漆，六份要求清单。想申请几个都行：第一封信花你一个晚上，之后每多一封，就再搭上一个晚上和邮费。\n# en: A cluster of notices with wax seals pinned above all the others: summer research posts, each with its own demands.\nscene [a45b3c] = 一簇盖着火漆的告示钉在所有告示上方：夏季研究岗位，各有各的要求。\n# en: The Royal Library\nopt.royal.label [64f580] = 皇家图书馆\n# en: GPA 2.5, an interview on lore, a love of indexes.\nopt.royal.hint [fbd651] = GPA 2.5，学识面试，以及对索引的热爱。\n# en: {member(intern_royal_library): They've already asked you back.|Needs Astromancy II.}\nopt.royal.locked [c8e0b8] = {member(intern_royal_library): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要星象与占卜 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.royal.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.royal.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\nopt.royal.say.3 [ccb58d] = 你写好了信，却发现付不起邮费。它只能留在你桌上。\n# en: The Conclave Laboratory\nopt.conclave.label [858e84] = 七塔议会实验室\n# en: GPA 3.0, an interview on arcana, and a word from Thorne helps.\nopt.conclave.hint [fd28c9] = GPA 3.0，奥术面试，有索恩的一句推荐会更好。\n# en: {member(intern_conclave): They've already asked you back.|Needs Evocation II.}\nopt.conclave.locked [fb2c57] = {member(intern_conclave): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要塑能术 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.conclave.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.conclave.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\nopt.conclave.say.3 [ccb58d] = 你写好了信，却发现付不起邮费。它只能留在你桌上。\n# en: The Guild of Alchemists' Works\nopt.alchemists.label [80cc20] = 炼金师公会工坊\n# en: GPA 2.0, an interview on alchemy, and a strong stomach.\nopt.alchemists.hint [2d4395] = GPA 2.0，炼金面试，还得有个好胃口。\n# en: {member(intern_alchemists): They've already asked you back.|Needs Alchemy II.}\nopt.alchemists.locked [e156fa] = {member(intern_alchemists): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要炼金与治愈 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.alchemists.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.alchemists.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\nopt.alchemists.say.3 [ccb58d] = 你写好了信，却发现付不起邮费。它只能留在你桌上。\n# en: The Observatory Expedition\nopt.expedition.label [a8e59b] = 天文台远征队\n# en: An interview on vigor, and the legs for a plateau.\nopt.expedition.hint [6f139e] = 体魄面试，外加能爬上高原的双腿。\n# en: {member(intern_expedition): They've already asked you back.|Needs Astromancy II.}\nopt.expedition.locked [f35bc6] = {member(intern_expedition): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要星象与占卜 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.expedition.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.expedition.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\nopt.expedition.say.3 [ccb58d] = 你写好了信，却发现付不起邮费。它只能留在你桌上。\n# en: The Beastcraft Field Station\nopt.field.label [93e08b] = 驭兽野外站\n# en: An interview on vigor. Boots provided, mostly.\nopt.field.hint [c9d453] = 体魄面试。靴子基本上会发。\n# en: {member(intern_field_station): They've already asked you back.|Needs Beastcraft II.}\nopt.field.locked [5a1cba] = {member(intern_field_station): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要驭兽与飞行 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.field.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.field.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\nopt.field.say.3 [ccb58d] = 你写好了信，却发现付不起邮费。它只能留在你桌上。\n# en: The Thornwick Exchange\nopt.thornwick.label [eb19f0] = 荆木学院交换项目\n# en: GPA 2.5, an interview on charm. Bring a thick skin.\nopt.thornwick.hint [5c642d] = GPA 2.5，魅力面试。脸皮得厚一点。\n# en: {member(intern_thornwick): They've already asked you back.|Needs Illusion II.}\nopt.thornwick.locked [9eb17a] = {member(intern_thornwick): 他们已经请你回去了。|需要幻术与魅影 II。}\n# en: {~Another letter, another evening, another stamp. Your hand aches in a very particular place.|You write it once, cross out half, write it again. The postage comes out of your own purse.}\nopt.thornwick.say.1 [69905a] = {~又一封信，又一个晚上，又一张邮票。你的手在一个非常特定的地方发酸。|你写了一遍，划掉一半，又写一遍。邮费从你自己的钱袋里出。}\n# en: You write the letter three times and send the best one. Probably the best one.\nopt.thornwick.say.2 [b67a70] = 信你写了三遍，寄出了最好的一封。大概是最好的一封。\n# en: You write the letter, and then find you can't pay the postage. It stays on your desk.\nopt.thornwick.say.3 [ccb58d] = 你写好了信，却发现付不起邮费。它只能留在你桌上。\n# en: Not this year\nopt.none.label [594ecf] = 今年算了\n# en: You let the notices flap. There's always next year, until there isn't.\nopt.none.say.1 [cd2444] = 你任由告示在风里拍打。总还有明年——直到没有为止。\n\n@event intern_offer\n# en: Letters With Seals\ntitle [51ad68] = 盖着火漆的信\n# en: The spring post brings answers. {applied(internship): There are sealed letters on your desk. Which do you open first?|There are no more sealed letters.} {guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office): There is also a plain grey envelope you never asked for.|}\ntext [7b4927] = 春天的邮件带来了回音。{applied(internship): 你的书桌上放着几封封了火漆的信。先拆哪一封？|再没有封着火漆的信了。}{guile >= 35 and rel(isolde) >= 6 and not member(intern_unseen_office): 还有一只你从没申请过的素灰色信封。|}\n# en: Heavy envelopes with wax seals on a dormitory desk, one of them plain grey with no seal at all.\nscene [28d6f5] = 宿舍书桌上放着几只封着火漆的厚信封，其中一只是素灰色的，根本没有封漆。\n# en: Letter from the Royal Library\nopt.royal.label [04415c] = 皇家图书馆的来信\n# en: They asked for a GPA of 2.5. The interview tests lore.\nopt.royal.hint [693412] = 他们要求 GPA 2.5。面试考学识。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the oldest archives in the realm.\nopt.royal.say.1 [d43827] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，待在王国最古老的档案馆里。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.royal.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.5, and they say so twice.\nopt.royal.say.3 [ee2e12] = “我们很遗憾。”他们要的是 GPA 2.5，而且说了两遍。\n# en: Letter from the Conclave\nopt.conclave.label [187867] = 七塔议会的来信\n# en: They asked for a GPA of 3.0. The interview tests arcana. A word from Thorne would help.\nopt.conclave.hint [e87947] = 他们要求 GPA 3.0。面试考奥术。有索恩的一句推荐会更好。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in spell research under the Seven Towers.\nopt.conclave.say.1 [b0dd08] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，在七塔之下研究法术。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.conclave.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in spell research under the Seven Towers.\nopt.conclave.say.3 [b0dd08] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，在七塔之下研究法术。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.conclave.say.4 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 3.0, and they say so twice.\nopt.conclave.say.5 [fa762a] = “我们很遗憾。”他们要的是 GPA 3.0，而且说了两遍。\n# en: Letter from the Alchemists' Works\nopt.alchemists.label [3ad0f0] = 炼金师工坊的来信\n# en: They asked for a GPA of 2.0. The interview tests alchemy.\nopt.alchemists.hint [0b2080] = 他们要求 GPA 2.0。面试考炼金。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the great vats of the Guild.\nopt.alchemists.say.1 [d15c64] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，守着公会的大缸。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.alchemists.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.0, and they say so twice.\nopt.alchemists.say.3 [1f5965] = “我们很遗憾。”他们要的是 GPA 2.0，而且说了两遍。\n# en: Letter from the Expedition\nopt.expedition.label [ca58f7] = 远征队的来信\n# en: The interview tests vigor.\nopt.expedition.hint [0bd21d] = 面试考体魄。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer of sky charts on the high plateau.\nopt.expedition.say.1 [9d271b] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，在高原上绘制星图。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.expedition.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: Letter from the Field Station\nopt.field.label [b25fe8] = 野外站的来信\n# en: The interview tests vigor.\nopt.field.hint [0bd21d] = 面试考体魄。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the moors, the heather and the creatures in them.\nopt.field.say.1 [27ceb5] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，在荒原和石楠丛里，与那里的生灵为伴。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.field.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: Letter from Thornwick\nopt.thornwick.label [c344dc] = 荆木学院的来信\n# en: They asked for a GPA of 2.5. The interview tests charm.\nopt.thornwick.hint [5c32c3] = 他们要求 GPA 2.5。面试考魅力。\n# en: \"We are pleased.\" You read the letter until the seal cracks. A summer in the rival academy's illusion laboratory.\nopt.thornwick.say.1 [64ec58] = “我们很高兴。”你把信读了一遍又一遍，直到火漆裂开。一整个夏天，待在对手学院的幻术实验室里。\n# en: \"We regret.\" The interview went well, they write, and not quite well enough.\nopt.thornwick.say.2 [4db135] = “我们很遗憾。”信上说，你的面试表现不错，只是还差一点。\n# en: \"We regret.\" They wanted a GPA of 2.5, and they say so twice.\nopt.thornwick.say.3 [ee2e12] = “我们很遗憾。”他们要的是 GPA 2.5，而且说了两遍。\n# en: Open the plain envelope\nopt.unseen.label [ca484a] = 拆开那只素信封\n# en: Inside: a time, an address and no signature. The paper is blank again by morning. You go anyway.\nopt.unseen.say.1 [702952] = 里面写着一个时间、一个地址，没有署名。到第二天早上，纸又变成了白纸。你还是去了。\n# en: Fold the letters away\nopt.decline.label [15c522] = 把信都收起来\n# en: You fold them carefully. Next year, perhaps.\nopt.decline.say.1 [b0ac8d] = 你把信仔细折好。也许明年吧。\n\n@event intern_assessment\n# en: The Supervisor's Report\ntitle [d9d7b2] = 导师的评语\n# en: Your last day at {member(intern_royal_library): the Royal Library Archives|{member(intern_conclave): the Conclave Laboratory|{member(intern_alchemists): the Guild of Alchemists' Works|{member(intern_expedition): the Observatory Expedition|{member(intern_field_station): the Beastcraft Field Station|{member(intern_thornwick): the Thornwick Exchange|the office with no name}}}}}}. Your supervisor turns the report over. {performance(internship) >= 80: \"Exceptional,\" it says at the top, underlined, and the rest is better.|{performance(internship) >= 60: \"Reliable and capable.\" A few notes in the margin, all of them fair.|{performance(internship) >= 40: \"Adequate.\" The word sits on the page by itself.|\"Did not meet expectations.\" Your supervisor doesn't quite look at you.}}}\ntext [b597e5] = 这是你在{member(intern_royal_library): 皇家图书馆档案馆|{member(intern_conclave): 七塔议会实验室|{member(intern_alchemists): 炼金师公会工坊|{member(intern_expedition): 观星远征队|{member(intern_field_station): 驭兽术野外站|{member(intern_thornwick): 荆木学院交换项目|那间没有名字的办公室}}}}}}的最后一天。导师把评语翻了过来。{performance(internship) >= 80: 最上面写着“出类拔萃”，还画了一道线，下面写得更好。|{performance(internship) >= 60: “可靠，能干。”页边有几条批注，条条中肯。|{performance(internship) >= 40: “尚可。”纸上孤零零就这两个字。|“未达预期。”导师的目光有点躲着你。}}}\n# en: A supervisor's desk on the last day of a placement, a written report face down under a pen.\nscene [8b3d61] = 实习最后一天，导师的书桌上，一份手写评语扣在桌面，上面压着一支笔。\n# en: Ask for a letter of reference\nopt.left.label [333892] = 请导师写一封推荐信\n# en: The letter comes a week later, sealed, and the registrar files a copy with a look you haven't had from her before.\nopt.left.say.1 [dc6c0f] = 一周后信到了，封得严严实实。教务员把副本归档时，看你的眼神是你从没见过的。\n# en: The letter comes a month later: two lines, your name spelled wrong in one of them. You put it at the bottom of a drawer.\nopt.left.say.2 [cc9272] = 一个月后信才到：短短两行，其中一行还把你的名字拼错了。你把它塞进了抽屉最底下。\n# en: Ask what you could have done better\nopt.right.label [af80e6] = 问问自己哪里还能做得更好\n# en: Your supervisor talks for twenty minutes without notes. Some of it stings. You write all of it down on the coach home, and read it twice.\nopt.right.say.1 [20ff3d] = 导师不看笔记，一口气讲了二十分钟。有些话很刺耳。回程的马车上你把它们全都记了下来，又读了两遍。\n\n@event intern_asked_back\n# en: Asked Back\ntitle [8aa9d6] = 再次相邀\n# en: A letter from {member(intern_royal_library): the Royal Library Archives|{member(intern_conclave): the Conclave Laboratory|{member(intern_alchemists): the Guild of Alchemists' Works|{member(intern_expedition): the Observatory Expedition|{member(intern_field_station): the Beastcraft Field Station|{member(intern_thornwick): the Thornwick Exchange|the office with no name}}}}}}. They were pleased with your summer, and the post is yours again next summer if you want it.\ntext [92bde8] = 一封来自{member(intern_royal_library): 皇家图书馆档案馆|{member(intern_conclave): 七塔议会实验室|{member(intern_alchemists): 炼金师公会工坊|{member(intern_expedition): 观星远征队|{member(intern_field_station): 驭兽术野外站|{member(intern_thornwick): 荆木学院交换项目|那间没有名字的办公室}}}}}}的信。他们对你这个夏天很满意，只要你愿意，明年夏天这个岗位还是你的。\n# en: A letter on good paper with a familiar seal, the words \"next summer\" underlined once, neatly.\nscene [522edb] = 一封用上好纸张写的信，盖着熟悉的火漆，“明年夏天”几个字下面工整地划了一道线。\n# en: Write back yes\nopt.left.label [622f58] = 回信答应\n# en: {~You write back the same evening. Next summer is spoken for, and you find you're glad.|\"Yes,\" you write, and then three more lines to make it sound less eager.}\nopt.left.say.1 [71669f] = {~你当晚就回了信。明年夏天有着落了，你发现自己挺高兴。|你写下“好的”，又添了三行，好让自己显得没那么迫不及待。}\n# en: Thank them, and decline\nopt.right.label [1b8626] = 致谢，婉拒\n# en: {~You thank them warmly and say no. Next summer is yours to spend, or to apply for something new.|A polite no. You keep the letter anyway; it's good to be asked.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a75a4d] = {~你热情地道谢，然后说不。明年夏天由你自己安排，或者去申请些新的。|一句礼貌的拒绝。信你还是留着了；被人邀请总是好事。}\n\n@event thesis_setup\n# en: Choosing a Thesis\ntitle [ed9c10] = 选定论文\n# en: The final year opens with a thesis. Before you choose this term's courses, Professor Thorne lays out three topics, at three levels of ambition.\ntext [c76290] = 最后一年以毕业论文开场。在你选定本学期的课程之前，索恩教授摆出了三个题目，野心各不相同。\n# en: A professor's study, three topic proposals laid out on the desk like cards.\nscene [82a455] = 教授的书房，三份选题提案像纸牌一样摊在桌上。\n# en: A careful survey\nopt.safe.label [d4d296] = 一篇稳妥的综述\n# en: Easier, and it will read as easier.\nopt.safe.hint [8d81c3] = 更容易，读起来也会显得更容易。\n# en: A sensible topic. Thorne says \"Sensible,\" the way other people say \"Disappointing.\"\nopt.safe.say.1 [5a3672] = 一个明智的题目。索恩说“明智”的语气，就像别人说“失望”。\n# en: An original study\nopt.solid.label [0d9f34] = 一项原创研究\n# en: The usual path.\nopt.solid.hint [8adec9] = 寻常的路。\n# en: Thorne nods. \"Good. Bring me something I don't already know.\"\nopt.solid.say.1 [9feb76] = 索恩点点头。“好。给我带来点我还不知道的东西。”\n# en: Something no one has done\nopt.ambitious.label [326da2] = 前无古人的课题\n# en: Harder, and worth more.\nopt.ambitious.hint [83c12d] = 更难，也更有分量。\n# en: Thorne is quiet for a long time. \"Hm,\" he says, and for once it sounds like hope.\nopt.ambitious.say.1 [18b887] = 索恩沉默了很久。“嗯，”他说，这一次听起来竟像是希望。\n\n@event thesis_crunch\n# en: The Crunch\ntitle [5ad669] = 最后冲刺\n# en: The defence is next month and your thesis is not ready. Your advisor's note is three words long: \"Well? Final draft.\"\ntext [db90b3] = 答辩就在下个月，你的论文还没写完。导师的便条只有短短一句：\"如何？交终稿。\"\n# en: A thesis carrel at dawn, a candle burned to a stub, drafts on the floor and a student asleep on the keyboard of a typing-quill.\nscene [d2cdf9] = 黎明时分的论文隔间，蜡烛燃成了残根，草稿散落一地，一名学生趴在打字羽毛笔的键盘上睡着了。\n# en: Work through the nights\nopt.left.label [ec1e9d] = 通宵赶工\n# en: You stop seeing daylight. The draft gets better and you get worse, which seems to be how this works.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f566c] = 你再也见不到日光。稿子越改越好，你却越来越糟，事情大概就是这样。\n# en: Hand in what you have\nopt.right.label [648c9a] = 有多少交多少\n# en: You tidy what's there and accept the gaps. Your advisor sighs, which is not the same as no.\nopt.right.say.1 [5cc846] = 你把现有的整理好，接受那些空缺。导师叹了口气——叹气不等于拒绝。\n\n@event thesis_defence\n# en: The Defence\ntitle [3a2f48] = 答辩\n# en: Three examiners, one lectern, and a year of work. {progress(thesis) >= 60: You know this material better than anyone in the room.|There are gaps, and you know where they are.} First, you present. Then they ask.\ntext [9ab052] = 三位考官，一座讲台，一年的心血。{progress(thesis) >= 60: 这些内容你比屋里任何人都熟。|有漏洞，而且你知道它们在哪儿。} 先是你陈述。然后他们提问。\n# en: A semicircular table with three examiners, a student standing at a lectern with a stack of notes.\nscene [f8606f] = 半圆形的长桌后坐着三位考官，一名学生站在讲台前，手里一摞笔记。\n# en: Defend it\nopt.left.label [0249ce] = 为它辩护\n# en: A safe topic is easier to defend and earns less; an ambitious one is harder and earns more. A year of thesis work counts.\nopt.left.hint [fa3c91] = 稳妥的题目更好答辩，回报也更少；大胆的题目更难，回报也更多。一年的论文功夫也会算数。\n# en: Your presentation runs clean from first slide to last. One examiner has stopped taking notes and is just listening.\nopt.left.say.1 [f074e0] = 你的陈述从头到尾干净利落。一位考官已经不再记笔记，只是在听。\n# en: A solid presentation with one wobble in the middle. You recover. They noticed the wobble.\nopt.left.say.2 [c4010c] = 一次扎实的陈述，中间晃了一下。你稳住了。他们注意到了那一下。\n# en: You lose your place twice and your nerve once. The examiners exchange a look you'll remember for years.\nopt.left.say.3 [6f66d2] = 你两次讲丢了位置，一次丢了胆子。考官们交换了一个你会记很多年的眼神。\n# en: A careful survey, carefully presented. Nobody could fault a word of it. Nobody looks thrilled, either.\nopt.left.say.4 [867002] = 一份严谨的综述，严谨地陈述。一个字都挑不出错。也没人显得激动。\n# en: Careful, competent and a little dull, and you can see one examiner fighting a yawn.\nopt.left.say.5 [02b389] = 严谨、称职，还有点乏味，你能看见一位考官在忍住哈欠。\n# en: Even a safe topic has its gaps, and your presentation walks straight into one of them.\nopt.left.say.6 [b10848] = 稳妥的题目也有漏洞，而你的陈述径直走进了其中一个。\n# en: Something no one has done, laid out so clearly they can't look away. One examiner has put down their pen.\nopt.left.say.7 [33edc7] = 前人从未做过的事，讲得清清楚楚，让他们移不开眼睛。一位考官放下了笔。\n# en: They follow you most of the way, arguing among themselves about whether it's brilliant or mad. Possibly both.\nopt.left.say.8 [c3424d] = 他们大半程都跟上了你，彼此争论这究竟是天才还是疯狂。也许两者都是。\n# en: You reach too far in the first ten minutes, and spend the rest of the hour trying to get back. They let you.\nopt.left.say.9 [8a9009] = 你在头十分钟就走得太远，剩下的一个小时都在设法往回走。他们由着你。\n# en: And under the presentation, a year of work they can feel. Every page they turn has been turned before.\nopt.left.say.10 [90a675] = 陈述之下，是他们能感觉到的一整年功夫。他们翻过的每一页，你都早已翻过。\n# en: Under the presentation, the thin chapters show. One examiner starts counting pages.\nopt.left.say.11 [cbd512] = 陈述之下，单薄的章节露了馅。一位考官开始数页数。\n# en: Open with your weakest chapter\nopt.right.label [dd91ef] = 从最薄弱的一章讲起\n# en: Name the gaps before they find them. Safe, and modest.\nopt.right.hint [047c2c] = 在他们发现之前先说出漏洞。稳妥，也平淡。\n# en: You start with what you couldn't prove, and why, and what you'd do next. It takes the wind out of their sails, and a little out of yours.\nopt.right.say.1 [e57cc7] = 你从无法证明的部分讲起，讲为什么，讲下一步会怎么做。这让他们泄了气，也让你泄了一点气。\n# en: The rest of it stands on a year of real work, and they can tell.\nopt.right.say.2 [f4fa42] = 其余部分立在一整年扎实的功夫上，他们看得出来。\n# en: The trouble is how many chapters are weak. They notice that too.\nopt.right.say.3 [3088c9] = 麻烦在于薄弱的章节太多了。这一点他们也注意到了。\n\n@event thesis_defence_question\n# en: The Question\ntitle [b2a4e8] = 那个问题\n# en: The examiner who hasn't spoken yet opens your thesis to a page near the end, puts a finger on one line and asks the question you hoped no one would ask.\ntext [f3f712] = 那位一直没开口的考官把你的论文翻到靠后的一页，手指按在一行字上，问出了你最希望没人问的那个问题。\n# en: An examiner leaning over a semicircular table, pointing at one line in a thick bound thesis, the student at the lectern very still.\nscene [24c9e8] = 一位考官俯身在半圆形的桌子上，指着一本厚厚的装订论文中的一行，讲台旁的学生一动不动。\n# en: Answer it head-on\nopt.left.label [f11e30] = 正面回答\n# en: You answer it, and then the question behind it, and then the one behind that. The examiner closes the thesis, slowly.\nopt.left.say.1 [298ca3] = 你回答了它，然后回答了它背后的问题，然后是再背后的那个。考官慢慢合上了论文。\n# en: Your answer starts well and ends somewhere you didn't mean to go. The examiner lets the silence do the rest.\nopt.left.say.2 [90a716] = 你的回答开头很好，结尾却去了一个你没打算去的地方。考官让沉默替自己说完了剩下的话。\n# en: Admit what you don't know\nopt.right.label [cb596c] = 承认你不知道的\n# en: \"I don't know,\" you say, \"and here is how I'd find out.\" From someone who has just presented so well, it sounds like mastery. Two examiners nod.\nopt.right.say.1 [ce3f4f] = “我不知道，”你说，“但我会这样去弄清楚。”出自一个刚刚陈述得如此出色的人之口，这听起来像是驾轻就熟。两位考官点了点头。\n# en: \"I don't know,\" you say, \"and here is how I'd find out.\" It stings a little, and earns you nothing. The examiner moves on.\nopt.right.say.2 [875e12] = “我不知道，”你说，“但我会这样去弄清楚。”这让你有点难受，也没给你加分。考官继续往下问了。\n\n@event thesis_defence_verdict\n# en: The Verdict\ntitle [5a8455] = 结果\n# en: They send you out. Twenty minutes on a bench in the corridor, with the clock and your heartbeat. Then the door opens, and they call you back in.\ntext [1f091a] = 他们请你出去。在走廊的长椅上等了二十分钟，只有钟声和你的心跳。然后门开了，他们叫你回去。\n# en: A student waiting alone on a bench in a long stone corridor outside a closed oak door, hands clasped, a clock on the wall.\nscene [bef3d0] = 一个学生独自坐在长长的石头走廊里的长椅上，紧闭的橡木门外，双手紧握，墙上挂着一只钟。\n# en: Go back in\nopt.ok.label [b89225] = 回去\n# en: They stop asking questions and start asking for copies. Distinction. One of them shakes your hand twice, as if the first didn't count.\nopt.ok.say.1 [9fca70] = 他们不再提问，开始讨要副本。优等。其中一位和你握了两次手，好像第一次不算数。\n# en: A pass, with one examiner's handwriting all over your conclusion. \"Good work,\" says the chair, and means it, mostly.\nopt.ok.say.2 [efa14a] = 通过了，只是结论部分写满了某位考官的批注。“干得好，”主席说，大体上是真心的。\n# en: They send you away to resubmit. It isn't the end. It feels like it. The idea was good, says the chair. The proof wasn't there yet.\nopt.ok.say.3 [c92bcd] = 他们让你回去重交。这不是终点。只是感觉像。主席说，想法是好的。只是证明还没到位。\n\n@event lantern_making\n# en: A Lantern to Launch\ntitle [38e8b8] = 一盏要放的灯\n# en: {year == 1: Tonight is Lantern Night, and everyone else seems to have known for weeks. You need a lantern.|{final_year: Your last Lantern Night. The lantern should be a good one.|Lantern Night again, and last year's lantern did not survive the trunk.}} {~The village stationer has fine rice paper, at a price.|Your roommate is folding theirs with their tongue between their teeth.|The common room is ankle-deep in paper offcuts.}\ntext [741bc8] = {year == 1: 今晚是灯笼夜，好像别人几个星期前就都知道了。你需要一盏灯笼。|{final_year: 你的最后一个灯笼夜。这盏灯得做得好一点。|又是灯笼夜，去年的灯笼没能在箱子里活下来。}} {~山下小镇的文具店有上好的宣纸，价钱也上好。|你的室友正咬着舌尖叠灯笼。|公共休息室里的碎纸片没过了脚踝。}\n# en: A dormitory table covered in paper, wire and candle stubs, a student folding a lopsided paper lantern.\nscene [db9f44] = 宿舍桌上铺满纸、铁丝和蜡烛头，一个学生正在叠一盏歪歪扭扭的纸灯笼。\n# en: Buy fine paper in the village\nopt.left.label [7b1bfd] = 去镇上买好纸\n# en: {~Rice paper so thin you can read through it, and a proper beeswax candle. It folds like a dream.|The stationer sells you paper the colour of the dawn sky. Your lantern will be the finest on the shore.|Gold-flecked paper, too dear and entirely worth it. You fold it slowly, as if it might mind.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7c81ac] = {~纸薄得能透过它看字，还配了一支真正的蜂蜡蜡烛。叠起来顺得像做梦。|文具店卖给你一张黎明天色的纸。你的灯笼会是湖岸上最漂亮的。|洒金纸，贵得离谱，也完全值得。你叠得很慢，好像它会介意似的。}\n# en: Make your own from scraps\nopt.middle.label [dc142c] = 用碎料自己做\n# en: {~Old exam paper, a bent wire and a candle stub. Your fingers smell of wax for a day, and one of them is burnt.|You glue it with porridge and patience. It's lopsided, and it's yours.|You fold it from a letter from home. It feels right to send that out over the water.}\nopt.middle.say.1 [cf0c1a] = {~旧考卷、一根弯铁丝、一截蜡烛头。你的手指一整天都是蜡味，还烫伤了一根。|你用麦片粥和耐心把它粘好。它歪歪扭扭，但它是你的。|你用一封家信叠成了它。把它放到水面上，感觉刚刚好。}\n# en: Glamour a plain one\nopt.right.label [0b61bd] = 给普通灯笼施幻术\n# en: Needs Glamour.\nopt.right.hint [e70ce7] = 需要魅影。\n# en: {~A plain paper bag becomes a lantern of stained glass. Nobody guesses.|You glamour your lantern into a little silver moon. Three people ask where you bought it.|Your lantern shimmers like mother-of-pearl. You'll have to keep it lit all evening, or it'll show.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6cf5ed] = {~一只普通纸袋变成了一盏彩色玻璃灯笼。没人看得出来。|你把灯笼幻化成一轮小小的银月亮。三个人问你在哪儿买的。|你的灯笼泛着珍珠母的光泽。你得让它整晚亮着，否则就要露馅。}\n# en: {~The glamour slips at dusk, and your stained-glass lantern turns back into a paper bag in front of everyone.|Your silver moon fades to brown paper halfway to the shore. A first-year points.|The pearl sheen peels off like old paint. You carry it lit-side-in, and fool nobody.}\nopt.right.say.2 [552a81] = {~黄昏时幻术滑落，你的彩色玻璃灯笼当着所有人的面变回了纸袋。|去湖岸的半路上，你的银月亮褪成了牛皮纸。一个一年级生指着它看。|珍珠光泽像旧油漆一样剥落。你把亮的那面朝里拿着，谁也没骗过。}\n\n@event lantern_night_v3\n# en: Lantern Night\ntitle [0b1f19] = 灯笼夜\n# en: Lantern Night. The whole academy gathers on the lakeshore with paper lanterns, each carrying a wish out over the water. {month == 2 and year == 1: Your first one.|{final_year: Your last one, and you know it.|Another year, another wish.}}{flag(lantern_fine):  Yours is one of the finest on the shore.|} Lanterns that wash back up are read by whoever finds them, they say.\ntext [91047a] = 灯笼夜。全院的人带着纸灯笼聚在湖岸，每盏灯都载着一个愿望飘向水面。{month == 2 and year == 1: 这是你的第一次。|{final_year: 这是你的最后一次，你心里清楚。|又一年，又一个愿望。}}{flag(lantern_fine): 你的灯笼是湖岸上最漂亮的之一。|}据说，漂回岸边的灯笼，谁捡到谁就可以读。\n# en: The Mirror Lake at night, hundreds of paper lanterns drifting over the water, students crowding the shore.\nscene [cd9b4c] = 夜晚的镜湖，数百盏纸灯笼在水面上漂，学生们挤满湖岸。\n# en: Wish for something selfish\nopt.left.label [7e0393] = 许一个自私的愿望\n# en: {year == 1: Your lantern goes out over the water and keeps its secret. You feel lighter for having asked.|{final_year: You wish for what comes after. The lantern climbs high and goes out of sight before anyone else's.|You wish for the same thing as last year, more firmly. The lantern seems to take note.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [ce2302] = {year == 1: 你的灯笼飘向湖面，守着它的秘密。问出口之后，你觉得轻松了些。|{final_year: 你许愿给毕业之后。灯笼升得很高，比谁的都先消失在视线里。|你许下和去年一样的愿望，这次更坚定。灯笼似乎记下了。}}\n# en: Wish for someone else\nopt.right.label [bc6686] = 为别人许愿\n# en: Someone close may ask you about it.\nopt.right.hint [611278] = 亲近的人也许会问起。\n# en: {year == 1: You don't tell anyone who. The lantern seems to know; it flies straight.|{final_year: You wish for everyone you'll leave behind here. It's a heavy lantern, and it flies anyway.|You wish for someone you love, and keep their name behind your teeth. The lantern flies straight.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [66afea] = {year == 1: 你没告诉任何人是谁。灯笼似乎知道，它飞得笔直。|{final_year: 你为所有要留在这里的人许愿。这是一盏沉甸甸的灯笼，它照样飞了起来。|你为一个你爱的人许愿，把名字咽在齿间。灯笼飞得笔直。}}\n# en: Read other people's wishes\nopt.read.label [58ac33] = 偷看别人的愿望\n# en: Tempting. Not kind.\nopt.read.hint [f010be] = 很诱人。不太厚道。\n# en: {year == 1: You fish a lantern out of the shallows and read it by its own candle. Someone wants to pass Alchemy. Someone wants a kiss. You feel like a thief, and read another.|{final_year: In your last year you finally do what everyone dares: you read the wishes caught on the jetty. Half are about leaving. You put them back very carefully.|You read three wishes caught in the reeds. One of them has your name on it. You put it back, and don't sleep.}}\nopt.read.say.1 [614390] = {year == 1: 你从浅水里捞起一盏灯笼，借着它自己的烛光读。有人想通过炼金课。有人想要一个吻。你觉得自己像个小偷，又读了一盏。|{final_year: 最后一年，你终于做了大家都敢做的事：读了卡在栈桥边的愿望。一半都是关于离开的。你小心翼翼地把它们放了回去。|你读了三个卡在芦苇里的愿望。其中一个写着你的名字。你把它放了回去，一夜没睡。}}\n\n@event lantern_reeds\n# en: Caught in the Reeds\ntitle [4c3cbe] = 困在芦苇里\n# en: Further along the shore a first-year is crying quietly. Their lantern caught in the reeds a few yards out, and it is guttering, wish and all.\ntext [a9e7ef] = 湖岸更远处，一个一年级生在悄悄地哭。他的灯笼卡在离岸几码的芦苇丛里，烛火摇摇欲灭，愿望也跟着要灭了。\n# en: A small paper lantern tangled in lake reeds near the shore, its candle guttering, a tearful first-year on the bank.\nscene [be3133] = 一盏小纸灯笼缠在岸边的湖芦苇里，烛火摇曳，岸上站着一个泪汪汪的一年级生。\n# en: Wade in and free it\nopt.left.label [d9da23] = 蹚水去解救它\n# en: The water is shockingly cold. You lift the lantern clear and push it out, and it catches the air and goes. The first-year looks at you as if you'd done magic, which, in a way, you have.\nopt.left.say.1 [7e27c9] = 湖水冷得吓人。你把灯笼托出来往外一推，它乘着风飞走了。那个一年级生看着你，好像你施了魔法——某种意义上，你确实施了。\n# en: Tell them wishes don't need lanterns\nopt.right.label [33fdb0] = 告诉他愿望不需要灯笼\n# en: You sit with them on the bank and say the true thing: the wish counts when you make it. They stop crying. The lantern goes out in the reeds, and neither of you watches.\nopt.right.say.1 [ea7e74] = 你陪他坐在岸边，说了句实话：许下愿望的那一刻，它就算数了。他不哭了。灯笼在芦苇里熄灭，你们谁也没去看。\n\n@event lantern_washed_up\n# en: A Lantern Washes Up\ntitle [acaa04] = 漂回来的灯笼\n# en: Weeks after Lantern Night a lantern washes up at your feet on the shore, burnt out, the wish still folded inside it. The handwriting is neat and nervous.\ntext [3a0729] = 灯笼夜几周后，一盏烧尽的灯笼漂到你脚边的岸上，里面还折着那张愿望。字迹工整，带着几分紧张。\n# en: A burnt-out paper lantern lying on the wet stones of a lakeshore at dawn, a folded wish still tucked inside it.\nscene [4fb291] = 黎明时分，一盏烧尽的纸灯笼躺在湖岸湿漉漉的石头上，里面还夹着一张折好的愿望。\n# en: Read it\nopt.left.label [fb8482] = 读一读\n# en: It's a wish about someone, a student who \"never notices anything\". You recognise the handwriting from the library sign-out book, and say nothing, ever.\nopt.left.say.1 [22c2f2] = 那是一个关于某人的愿望，关于一个“什么都注意不到”的学生。你从图书馆借书登记簿上认出了这笔迹，从此一个字也没说。\n# en: Send it back out unread\nopt.right.label [d12536] = 原封不动送回水里\n# en: You relight the stub with a snap of your fingers and push it out again. It wobbles off over the water, its secret intact, and you never find out.\nopt.right.say.1 [08d45b] = 你打个响指重新点燃蜡烛头，把它推回水里。它摇摇晃晃地漂远了，秘密完好无损，你永远也不会知道。\n\n@event lantern_asked\n# en: What Did You Wish For?\ntitle [4bee90] = 你许了什么愿？\n# en: {dear} drops onto the window seat beside you with two mugs of cocoa. \"So,\" {they} say{s}. \"Lantern Night. What did you wish for? You can tell me. I won't tell the lake.\"\ntext [a5829e] = {dear}端着两杯可可，一屁股坐到你身边的窗台上。“说吧，”{they}说，“灯笼夜。你许了什么愿？可以告诉我。我不会告诉湖的。”\n# en: Two students on a window seat with mugs of cocoa, one teasing the other, a paper lantern on the sill between them.\nscene [1e266f] = 两个学生端着可可坐在窗台上，一个在逗另一个，两人之间的窗台上放着一盏纸灯笼。\n# en: Tell {them} the truth\nopt.left.label [9b4c6a] = 跟{them}说实话\n# en: You tell {them}. {dear} goes quiet for a moment, and then bumps {their} shoulder against yours and doesn't move it away. Saying it out loud was harder than writing it.\nopt.left.say.1 [50b05d] = 你告诉了{them}。{dear}沉默了一会儿，然后用肩膀轻轻撞了你一下，就那么靠着，没有挪开。说出口比写下来难多了。\n# en: \"If I tell you, it won't come true.\"\nopt.right.label [b8838d] = “说出来就不灵了。”\n# en: {dear} groans, and spends the rest of the cocoa guessing, each guess more ridiculous than the last. You laugh so hard you spill yours.\nopt.right.say.1 [c4c44a] = {dear}哀嚎一声，接下来一整杯可可的工夫都在猜，一个比一个离谱。你笑得把自己的可可都洒了。\n\n@event year_four\n# en: The Final Year\ntitle [a30775] = 最后一年\n# en: The barge again, the towers again, and a thesis waiting. The first-years on the deck look at you the way you once looked at the fourth-years. At the opening feast the headmistress says, \"Fourth years, this is your last year in these halls. What you become here, you will be for a long time.\" She looks, for a moment, directly at you.\ntext [cb5447] = 又是驳船，又是高塔，还有一篇论文在等着。甲板上的一年级生看你的眼神，就像你当年看四年级生那样。开学宴上，院长说：“四年级的同学们，这是你们在这些厅堂里的最后一年。你们在这里成为什么样的人，就会长久地是那样的人。”有那么一刻，她直直地看着你。\n# en: The seven towers at dawn from the barge, the same view as the first day, but the student watching it has changed.\nscene [542188] = 从驳船上望见的黎明七塔，和第一天的景色一样，但看风景的学生已经变了。\n# en: Here we go\nopt.ok.label [e1aa95] = 走吧\n# en: You know which steps creak and which doors stick. The thesis will want evenings of its own each term; something else may have to give, a club or a job.\nopt.ok.say.1 [e66c41] = 你知道哪级台阶会响，哪扇门会卡。毕业论文每学期都要占去好些晚上；总得放下点什么，一个社团，或者一份工作。\n\n@event grade_appeal\n# en: The Appeal\ntitle [83680b] = 申诉\n# en: The examiners' list is pinned up, and beside {track} there is an F. The clerk mentions, without looking up, that a student may appeal a grade in person, once, before the board. Most don't. Some of those who do come out with a C.\ntext [dc09dc] = 考官名单贴出来了，{track}旁边是一个 F。办事员头也不抬地提了一句：学生可以亲自向委员会申诉一次成绩。大多数人不去。去了的人里，有些拿回了一个 C。\n# en: A long table of grey-robed examiners in a panelled room, a single student standing before them holding an exam paper.\nscene [ba2029] = 镶着护墙板的房间里，一长排身穿灰袍的考官坐在桌后，一个学生拿着考卷独自站在他们面前。\n# en: Appeal before the board\nopt.left.label [fecb7e] = 向委员会申诉\n# en: You talk for ten minutes about the paper, the question and the answer they marked wrong. The board confers in whispers. The F becomes a C, in red ink, with a sigh.\nopt.left.say.1 [20d3bd] = 你就那张考卷、那道题和那个被判错的答案讲了十分钟。委员会低声商议。F 变成了 C，红墨水写的，附带一声叹气。\n# en: The board hears you out, then reads your paper aloud. It's worse aloud. The F stands, and the queue outside the door heard every word.\nopt.left.say.2 [63e52e] = 委员会听完你的话，然后把你的考卷念了一遍。念出来更糟。F 维持原判，门外排队的人一字不落全听见了。\n# en: Accept it, and read the comments\nopt.right.label [b758f4] = 接受成绩，读读评语\n# en: The examiner's comments are fair, which is the worst thing about them. You copy out every one. Next time you'll know exactly where it went wrong.\nopt.right.say.1 [1fbf52] = 考官的评语很公道，这正是最糟的地方。你一条一条抄下来。下次你会清楚地知道自己错在哪儿。\n\n@event registrar_list\n# en: The Registrar's List\ntitle [0ca2e7] = 教务处的课程表\n# en: Before enrolment, the registrar's list goes up in the entrance hall: each course, what it tests, which spells it teaches, and which open only next term. A second-year taps Evocation I. \"If you came here to fight, that one: Flame inside a month.\" She taps Illusion I. \"If you'd rather talk your way out of things, that one. Glamour wins more arguments than Flame ever did.\"\ntext [586cd1] = 选课前，教务处的课程表贴在了门厅：每门课考什么，教哪些咒语，哪些下学期才开。一个二年级生点了点塑能术 I。“要是你来这儿是为了打架，就选这门：一个月之内就能放火焰。”她又点了点幻术与魅影 I。“要是你宁愿靠嘴脱身，就选这门。魅影赢下的争论，比火焰多得多。”\n# en: A crowd of new students around a long parchment list pinned in an entrance hall, one of them tracing a line with a finger.\nscene [ddd05b] = 门厅里一群新生围着一张长长的羊皮纸名单，其中一个用手指顺着一行往下划。\n# en: Read it twice\nopt.ok.label [7de276] = 看两遍\n# en: Two courses a term, a fee each time, and an exam at the end of each. Pass a level and you learn its spells.\nopt.ok.say.1 [fb0418] = 每学期两门课，每次都要交课程费，每学期末各有一场考试。通过一个级别，就能学会它的咒语。\n\n@event club_fair_flyer\n# en: Flyers Under the Door\ntitle [fb904d] = 门缝下的传单\n# en: One flyer slides under your door, then another, then nine. The Club Fair is next month in the courtyard: the Duelling Circle, the Lumen Ledger, the Verdant Circle. One club at a time, says the small print, and each wants a couple of evenings a term.\ntext [f8867e] = 一张传单从门缝下塞进来，又一张，然后是九张。社团招新会下个月在庭院举行：决斗社、《流明纪事报》社、青翠社。小字写着：一次只能加入一个社团，每个社团每学期要占你几个晚上。\n# en: A dormitory floor scattered with colourful club flyers, more sliding under the door as a student watches.\nscene [f482c7] = 宿舍地板上散落着五颜六色的社团传单，还有更多正从门缝下塞进来，一个学生在一旁看着。\n# en: Keep the flyers\nopt.ok.label [e3ac1e] = 把传单留着\n# en: You pin them over your bed and argue with them until you fall asleep.\nopt.ok.say.1 [ce52b4] = 你把它们钉在床头，和它们争论到睡着。\n\n@event job_board_notice\n# en: Hands Wanted\ntitle [82086c] = 招人\n# en: Pinned to the refectory door: \"Hands wanted. See the Job Board, entrance hall.\" Under it, in pencil: \"The Newt pays best. The library is warmest.\" Under that, in a third hand: \"Pell counts.\"\ntext [eb646b] = 膳堂门上钉着：“招人。详见门厅招工告示板。”下面有人用铅笔写：“酒馆给钱最多。图书馆最暖和。”再下面是第三种笔迹：“佩尔要数数。”\n# en: A refectory door with a notice pinned to it, three different handwritings added beneath the printed part.\nscene [ee5aec] = 膳堂门上钉着一张告示，印刷字下面添了三种不同的笔迹。\n# en: Make a note of it\nopt.ok.label [70f916] = 记下来\n# en: One job at a time, a few shifts a term, paid by the shift. The board is up from morning until evening.\nopt.ok.say.1 [611dbe] = 一次只能做一份工，每学期几个班次，按班次付钱。告示板从早上一直开到傍晚。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/calendar.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/calendar.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event intro_arrival\n# en: The Sky-Barge\ntitle [59d00d] = 天空驳船\n# en: The barge drops through the clouds and there it is: Lumenhold, seven towers stitched together with bridges of light. A first-year beside you is quietly being sick over the side. A prefect with a ledger counts heads and frowns at yours.\ntext [402ca7] = 驳船穿过云层下降，它就在那里：流明堡，七座高塔由光之桥缝合在一起。你身旁一个一年级新生正悄悄趴在船舷边呕吐。一位拿着名册的级长在清点人头，数到你时皱了皱眉。\n# en: A wooden barge with patched sails drifting between clouds toward seven impossibly tall towers above a mirror-still lake.\nscene [45ee12] = 一艘打着补丁的木帆驳船在云间漂浮，驶向矗立在镜面般湖泊之上、高得不可思议的七座塔楼。\n# en: Step onto the landing\nopt.ok.label [2c37cd] = 踏上码头\n# en: Your boots touch stone that hums faintly underfoot. Four years. Forty-eight moons.\nopt.ok.say.1 [c9a4d2] = 你的靴子踩上了石板，脚下微微嗡鸣。四年。四十八轮月。\n\n@event choose_origin\n# en: Who Were You Before?\ntitle [3aa3fb] = 来这里之前，你是谁？\n# en: The Sorting Mirror in the entrance hall does not sort you into anything. It simply asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came here. It waits.\ntext [408653] = 门厅里的“分院镜”并不会把你分到哪里去。它只是用冷水般的声音问你：来这里之前，你是谁？然后，它静静等待。\n# en: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall, showing a different version of the viewer in each pane.\nscene [c49f90] = 门厅里一面高大的魔法镜，每一格镜面都映出观者不同的模样。\n# en: A younger child of minor nobility\nopt.noble.label [354ff7] = 小贵族家的小儿女\n# en: Charm and a known name. 60 crowns from home now, and an allowance each later term, with strings attached.\nopt.noble.hint [cbb0d2] = 魅力和响亮的姓氏。现在家里给 60 克朗，此后每学期还有零花钱，附带条件。\n# en: The mirror shows you a crest you have tried to outgrow. It approves, faintly.\nopt.noble.say.1 [5615e9] = 镜中出现一枚你一直想摆脱的家徽。它微微表示认可。\n# en: A provincial scholarship student\nopt.scholar.label [096b86] = 外省的奖学金学生\n# en: Lore above all. 30 crowns now, and a stipend each later term while your grades hold.\nopt.scholar.hint [285185] = 学识为先。现在 30 克朗，此后每学期只要成绩保得住就有津贴。\n# en: The mirror shows you a candle burning very late in a very small room.\nopt.scholar.say.1 [e182c1] = 镜中出现一间很小的屋子，一支蜡烛燃到很晚很晚。\n# en: Raised by a marsh hedge-witch\nopt.hedge.label [700715] = 由沼泽乡野女巫养大\n# en: Alchemy, some Arcana, good spirits. 15 crowns, and no money from home: you'll need a job.\nopt.hedge.hint [3634f4] = 炼金、一点奥术、好心情。15 克朗，家里不寄钱：你得找份工作。\n# en: The mirror smells, briefly, of wet moss and woodsmoke. You feel at home.\nopt.hedge.say.1 [734ca9] = 镜子里一瞬间飘出湿苔藓和柴火烟的气味。你觉得像回到了家。\n# en: Raised on the streets of Port Calder\nopt.street.label [5f489c] = 在考尔德港的街头长大\n# en: Guile and grit. 8 crowns, and no one to send more: you'll need a job.\nopt.street.hint [8778d2] = 诡计与韧劲。8 克朗，也没人会再寄：你得找份工作。\n# en: The mirror shows a pickpocket's quick hands. You check your pockets out of habit.\nopt.street.say.1 [dd71c2] = 镜中出现一双扒手的快手。你习惯性地摸了摸口袋。\n# en: The heir to a trading company\nopt.merchant.label [2fc5e4] = 一家商行的继承人\n# en: The biggest purse and a nose for bargains: 120 crowns now, and more from the company when trade is good.\nopt.merchant.hint [a2889b] = 最鼓的钱袋，对买卖嗅觉灵敏：现在 120 克朗，生意好时商行还会寄钱。\n# en: The mirror shows ledgers, ships and your mother's disappointed face. Still: gold.\nopt.merchant.say.1 [604ea4] = 镜中出现账簿、货船，还有你母亲失望的脸。不过：钱是真的多。\n\n@event sorting_mirror\n# en: The Sorting Mirror\ntitle [6d3a7b] = 分院镜\n# en: The Sorting Mirror in the entrance hall does not sort you into anything. It simply asks, in a voice like cold water, who you were before you came here. You tell it.\ntext [43f613] = 门厅里的“分院镜”并不会把你分到哪里去。它只是用冷水般的声音问你：来这里之前，你是谁？你告诉了它。\n# en: A tall enchanted mirror in the entrance hall, showing a different version of the viewer in each pane.\nscene [c49f90] = 门厅里一面高大的魔法镜，每一格镜面都映出观者不同的模样。\n# en: Look into the mirror\nopt.ok.label [bb5682] = 望向镜中\n# en: {has(origin_noble): The mirror shows you a crest you have tried to outgrow. It approves, faintly.|{has(origin_scholar): The mirror shows you a candle burning very late in a very small room.|{has(origin_hedge): The mirror smells, briefly, of wet moss and woodsmoke. You feel at home.|{has(origin_street): The mirror shows a pickpocket's quick hands. You check your pockets out of habit.|The mirror shows ledgers, ships and your mother's disappointed face. Still: gold.}}}}\nopt.ok.say.1 [8c73e5] = {has(origin_noble): 镜中出现一枚你一直想摆脱的家徽。它微微表示认可。|{has(origin_scholar): 镜中出现一间很小的屋子，一支蜡烛燃到很晚很晚。|{has(origin_hedge): 镜子里一瞬间飘出湿苔藓和柴火烟的气味。你觉得像回到了家。|{has(origin_street): 镜中出现一双扒手的快手。你习惯性地摸了摸口袋。|镜中出现账簿、货船，还有你母亲失望的脸。不过：钱是真的多。}}}}\n\n@event wrapup_begins\n# en: The Last Weeks\ntitle [0438f9] = 最后的几周\n# en: A notice on the great hall door: classes are over, duties are done, and final-years may stay on until graduation, board on the house. The castle is yours for one last summer.\ntext [172a68] = 大礼堂门上贴着一张告示：课程结束，职责已了，毕业生可以留到毕业典礼，食宿全免。最后一个夏天，城堡属于你。\n# en: An emptying castle in late summer light, trunks stacked in a corridor, a notice pinned to the great hall door.\nscene [1cbaa4] = 夏末的阳光里，城堡渐渐空了，走廊里堆着行李箱，大礼堂门上钉着一张告示。\n# en: Make the most of it\nopt.ok.label [793c29] = 好好享受\n# en: You walk the long way to breakfast, past every door you ever got lost behind.\nopt.ok.say.1 [725c27] = 你绕远路去吃早饭，经过每一扇你曾在后面迷过路的门。\n\n@event choose_elective\n# en: Choosing an Elective\ntitle [03e0dd] = 选择选修课\n# en: The registrar's noticeboard flaps with sign-up sheets for this term's electives. Core lectures are compulsory; your elective is yours to choose. The sheets jostle for your attention.\ntext [6ed4b3] = 教务处的告示板上，本学期选修课的报名表哗哗作响。核心课程是必修的；选修课由你自己挑。报名表们推推搡搡，争着吸引你的注意。\n# en: A long noticeboard covered in fluttering enchanted parchment, each sheet trying to shoulder the others aside.\nscene [803e43] = 一块长长的告示板，贴满了飘动的魔法羊皮纸，每一张都想用肩膀把别的挤开。\n# en: Pyromancy\nopt.pyro.label [22dcef] = 火焰术\n# en: +Arcana each month, small burns. Taught by Professor Thorne.\nopt.pyro.hint [cea401] = 每月 +奥术，偶有小烧伤。由索恩教授执教。\n# en: Tidecalling\nopt.tide.label [43908b] = 唤潮术\n# en: +Arcana, +Vigor, lakeside calm.\nopt.tide.hint [a0ea17] = +奥术，+体魄，湖畔的宁静。\n# en: Illusion & Glamour\nopt.illusion.label [5cdf61] = 幻术与魅影\n# en: +Guile, +Charm.\nopt.illusion.hint [21f570] = +诡计，+魅力。\n# en: Practical Alchemy\nopt.alchemy.label [990d4f] = 实用炼金\n# en: +Alchemy.\nopt.alchemy.hint [f7922d] = +炼金。\n# en: Divination\nopt.divination.label [61fc6c] = 占卜学\n# en: +Lore, occasional gloomy omens.\nopt.divination.hint [a946bb] = +学识，偶尔会有阴郁的预兆。\n# en: Artifice\nopt.artifice.label [de3328] = 奇械术\n# en: +Alchemy, +Lore.\nopt.artifice.hint [5dba10] = +炼金，+学识。\n# en: Beastcraft\nopt.beast.label [573a56] = 驭兽学\n# en: +Vigor, +Mood. Smells of hay.\nopt.beast.hint [2aa6d4] = +体魄，+心情。一股干草味。\n# en: Astromancy\nopt.astro.label [65a198] = 星象学\n# en: +Lore, late nights.\nopt.astro.hint [dc29a9] = +学识，经常熬夜。\n# en: Healing Arts\nopt.healing.label [e8c0bd] = 治愈术\n# en: +Alchemy, +Lore, a little health.\nopt.healing.hint [eab2fb] = +炼金，+学识，少量健康。\n# en: Umbral Theory (restricted)\nopt.umbral.label [430c05] = 暗影理论（受限）\n# en: Requires Lore 20 or second year. +Arcana, dark moods.\nopt.umbral.hint [6e81e4] = 需要学识 20 或二年级。+奥术，心情阴沉。\n# en: Needs Lore 20, or your second year.\nopt.umbral.locked [cd9acd] = 需要学识 20，或进入第二年。\n\n@event club_fair\n# en: The Club Fair\ntitle [ce4fee] = 社团招新会\n# en: The courtyard is a riot of banners. Every club wants you, or at least your membership fee. A broom whizzes overhead trailing a SKYRACE TRYOUTS banner; somewhere a choir is harmonising with a cat.\ntext [2193b8] = 庭院里旗帜招展，热闹非凡。每个社团都想要你——至少想要你的会费。一把飞帚从头顶呼啸而过，拖着“天空竞速队招新”的横幅；不知哪里，一支合唱团正在和一只猫合唱。\n# en: The great courtyard filled with banners and stalls: a duelist juggling sparks, a choir floating a foot off the ground, a girl handing out newspapers that shout headlines.\nscene [60c086] = 宏伟的庭院里满是旗帜和摊位：一名决斗者在抛接火花，一支合唱团悬浮在离地一尺的空中，一个女孩在分发会喊出头条的报纸。\n# en: Dueling Circle\nopt.duel.label [db424f] = 决斗社\n# en: +Vigor +Arcana, bruises. Home of the Spring Tournament.\nopt.duel.hint [72c44c] = +体魄 +奥术，满身淤青。春季锦标赛的大本营。\n# en: Choir of the Spheres\nopt.choir.label [41a5f0] = 天球合唱团\n# en: +Charm, +Mood.\nopt.choir.hint [ffc919] = +魅力，+心情。\n# en: Skyrace Squad\nopt.sky.label [b4b285] = 天空竞速队\n# en: +Vigor, crowd glory, crashes.\nopt.sky.hint [c4c7f0] = +体魄，观众的欢呼，以及坠机。\n# en: The Lumen Ledger\nopt.ledger.label [2606ae] = 《流明纪事报》社\n# en: +Guile +Charm. Nessa Quill's student paper.\nopt.ledger.hint [d5542a] = +诡计 +魅力。内莎·奎尔的学生报。\n# en: Verdant Circle\nopt.garden.label [7ac3fd] = 青翠社\n# en: +Alchemy +Mood. Greenhouses and gossip.\nopt.garden.hint [8c86f5] = +炼金 +心情。温室与八卦。\n# en: Stargazers\nopt.stars.label [44dc13] = 观星社\n# en: +Lore +Mood.\nopt.stars.hint [60f956] = +学识 +心情。\n# en: Keep your evenings free\nopt.none.label [418ad6] = 晚上留给自己\n# en: No club (leaves your current club, if any).\nopt.none.hint [6ad50e] = 不参加社团（若已有社团则退出）。\n\n@event job_board\n# en: The Job Board\ntitle [377c1b] = 招工告示板\n# en: Rent is ten crowns a month and your purse is a fragile thing. The board outside the bursar's office offers honest work, and some that is merely work.\ntext [185fc4] = 房租每月十克朗，而你的钱袋是个脆弱的东西。财务处门外的告示板上，有正经的工作，也有仅仅算是工作的工作。\n# en: A cork board outside the bursar's office, papered with notices; one is on fire, one is wriggling.\nscene [bbe568] = 财务处门外的一块软木告示板，贴满了启事；其中一张着了火，另一张在扭动。\n# en: Library Assistant — 10 gold/month\nopt.library.label [dece88] = 图书馆助理——每月 10 金币\n# en: +Lore, a little dull.\nopt.library.hint [1434fc] = +学识，有点无聊。\n# en: The Gilded Newt — 16 gold/month\nopt.tavern.label [13979f] = 镀金蝾螈酒馆——每月 16 金币\n# en: +Charm, tiring evenings in the village.\nopt.tavern.hint [8090e3] = +魅力，镇上累人的夜班。\n# en: Apothecary Clerk — 12 gold/month\nopt.apothecary.label [f748e6] = 药铺店员——每月 12 金币\n# en: Requires Alchemy 12. +Alchemy.\nopt.apothecary.hint [0e1e4f] = 需要炼金 12。+炼金。\n# en: Needs Alchemy 12.\nopt.apothecary.locked [784af2] = 需要炼金 12。\n# en: Familiar Stables — 11 gold/month\nopt.stables.label [8ccd8f] = 魔宠马厩——每月 11 金币\n# en: +Vigor. Things bite.\nopt.stables.hint [cf864a] = +体魄。会被咬。\n# en: First-Year Tutor — 12 gold/month\nopt.tutor.label [7403eb] = 一年级辅导员——每月 12 金币\n# en: Requires Lore 18. +Standing.\nopt.tutor.hint [9594b7] = 需要学识 18。+学业。\n# en: Needs Lore 18.\nopt.tutor.locked [ce2e73] = 需要学识 18。\n# en: Broom Courier — 18 gold/month\nopt.courier.label [142261] = 飞帚信使——每月 18 金币\n# en: Requires Vigor 14. Dangerous.\nopt.courier.hint [981b47] = 需要体魄 14。很危险。\n# en: Needs Vigor 14.\nopt.courier.locked [1f61e8] = 需要体魄 14。\n# en: No job (quit any current one)\nopt.none.label [7a08f4] = 不打工（辞去现有工作）\n\n@event lantern_night\n# en: Lantern Night\ntitle [0b1f19] = 灯笼夜\n# en: On Lantern Night every student writes a wish on a paper lantern and lets it go over the lake. Rumour says the lanterns that sink were wishes the lake wanted for itself.\ntext [608e29] = 灯笼夜里，每个学生都会在纸灯笼上写下一个愿望，让它飘过湖面。传说那些沉下去的灯笼，是湖想要据为己有的愿望。\n# en: Hundreds of paper lanterns rising off the lake at dusk, each with a wish written on it in glowing ink.\nscene [3a7795] = 黄昏时分，数百只纸灯笼从湖面升起，每一只上都用发光的墨水写着一个愿望。\n# en: Wish for something selfish\nopt.left.label [7e0393] = 许一个自私的愿望\n# en: Your lantern rises high. The next morning you find coins in your shoe. The lake is generous, and a little creepy.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f05e5] = 你的灯笼升得很高。第二天早上，你在鞋子里发现了硬币。湖很大方，也有点吓人。\n# en: Your lantern sinks with a hiss. Something under the water now knows what you want.\nopt.left.say.2 [f9d757] = 你的灯笼嘶的一声沉了下去。水下有什么东西，现在知道你想要什么了。\n# en: Wish for someone else\nopt.right.label [bc6686] = 为别人许愿\n# en: Your lantern rises and catches the wind. Across the shore, someone you have never met laughs out loud for no reason.\nopt.right.say.1 [002a2f] = 你的灯笼升起来，乘上了风。湖岸对面，一个你从未见过的人无缘无故地放声大笑。\n\n@event kael_arrives\n# en: The Boy From the North\ntitle [c3bb2e] = 来自北方的少年\n# en: Mid-term, the dining hall doors open and frost crawls across the floor. A boy in a fur coat walks in, takes the empty seat beside you, and says nothing at all. The headmistress introduces him as Kael Dumont, a transfer. She does not say from where.\ntext [fde128] = 学期过半，餐厅的大门打开，寒霜爬过地板。一个穿毛皮大衣的少年走进来，坐在你身旁的空位上，一言不发。院长介绍说他叫凯尔·杜蒙，是转学生。她没说他从哪里来。\n# en: A pale young man in a fur-lined coat standing at the door of the dining hall, frost spreading from his boots across the floor.\nscene [f60e23] = 一个面色苍白的年轻人身穿毛皮衬里的大衣站在餐厅门口，霜花从他的靴底向地板蔓延。\n# en: Leave him be\nopt.left.label [ce4663] = 别打扰他\n# en: You eat in silence. When you leave, your cup of tea has frozen solid.\nopt.left.say.1 [930b83] = 你们沉默地吃完了饭。你离开时，你那杯茶已经冻成了冰坨。\n# en: Offer him the bread\nopt.right.label [494eb6] = 把面包递给他\n# en: He looks at the bread as if no one has offered him anything in a long time. \"...Thank you.\" His voice sounds like wind over snow.\nopt.right.say.1 [6aa70f] = 他看着面包，仿佛很久没人给过他任何东西。“……谢谢。”他的声音像掠过雪原的风。\n\n@event winter_trials\n# en: The Winter Trials\ntitle [01bc52] = 冬季考核\n# en: The Winter Trials. A hundred desks, a hundred floating quills, one very old proctor who may be asleep. The first question asks you to explain the Third Law of Correspondence. The second asks you to demonstrate it.\ntext [f596dd] = 冬季考核。一百张课桌，一百支悬浮的羽毛笔，一位很老很老、可能已经睡着的监考官。第一题要你解释“第三对应定律”。第二题要你当场演示。\n# en: A vast examination hall where floating quills hover over a hundred desks and the windows are thick with frost.\nscene [f46856] = 宽阔的考场里，悬浮的羽毛笔停在一百张课桌上方，窗户上结着厚厚的霜。\n# en: Lean on theory\nopt.left.label [f71caf] = 靠理论取胜\n# en: Your answer is so thorough the quill gets a cramp. Top marks.\nopt.left.say.1 [eb0c7e] = 你的答案详尽到羽毛笔都抽筋了。满分。\n# en: You write three pages and realise halfway that you have been answering a different question.\nopt.left.say.2 [6df893] = 你写了三页，写到一半才发现自己答的是另一道题。\n# en: Lean on practice\nopt.right.label [e24f03] = 靠实践取胜\n# en: Your demonstration is clean and bright. The proctor wakes up and applauds.\nopt.right.say.1 [5963aa] = 你的演示干净利落、光彩夺目。监考官醒过来，鼓起了掌。\n# en: The demonstration backfires. Politely, but with smoke.\nopt.right.say.2 [5c503b] = 演示失败了。很有礼貌地失败了，但冒了烟。\n\n@event midwinter_ball\n# en: The Midwinter Ball\ntitle [81d1d0] = 仲冬舞会\n# en: The great hall has been turned to ice and starlight for the Midwinter Ball. Couples float above the floor. Who will you go with? You can only ask someone you know well.\ntext [e4d78e] = 为了仲冬舞会，大礼堂化作了冰雪与星光的世界。一对对舞伴悬浮在地板上方起舞。你要和谁一起去？只能邀请你熟悉的人。\n# en: The great hall transformed: an ice-crystal chandelier, couples waltzing a foot above the floor, snow falling indoors without ever landing.\nscene [84cb48] = 焕然一新的大礼堂：冰晶吊灯，离地一尺旋转华尔兹的舞伴，室内飘着永不落地的雪。\n# en: Ask Lyra\nopt.lyra.label [b5812c] = 邀请莱拉\n# en: {&She has had six invitations. She hasn't said yes to any.|She has had invitations again. She is pretending not to count them.}\nopt.lyra.hint [31cae8] = {&她收到了六份邀请，一份也没答应。|她又收到了一堆邀请，正假装没在数。}\n# en: {bond(lyra) == \"partner\": \"Obviously,\" she says, before you finish asking. She leads, of course.|{flag(ball_lyra): \"Again? Fine. You've improved.\" She dances like a duel, and this year you nearly keep up.|\"Finally, someone who doesn't stammer.\" She dances like a duel — and wins.}}\nopt.lyra.say.1 [053333] = {bond(lyra) == \"partner\": “那还用说，”你还没问完，她就答应了。当然是她领舞。|{flag(ball_lyra): “又来？行吧。你有进步。”她跳舞还是像决斗，今年你差点就跟上了。|“总算来了个不结巴的。”她跳舞像决斗一样——而且赢了。}}\n# en: \"I'm going alone. It's more efficient.\" You watch her dance alone all night, perfectly.\nopt.lyra.say.2 [ba4ab4] = “我一个人去。效率更高。”你看着她独自跳了一整晚，完美无缺。\n# en: Ask Tobias\nopt.tobias.label [81d042] = 邀请托比亚斯\n# en: {flag(ball_tobias): He has been practising with a broom again. The broom has improved.|He has been practising with a broom.}\nopt.tobias.hint [7aa1e2] = {flag(ball_tobias): 他又拿扫帚练了一年。扫帚的舞技进步了。|他一直在拿扫帚当舞伴练习。}\n# en: {flag(ball_tobias): He only steps on your feet twice this year, and is so proud of it that he tells everyone. It's the best night of the term.|He steps on your feet nine times and apologises ten. It's the best night of the term.}\nopt.tobias.say.1 [dd7f73] = {flag(ball_tobias): 今年他只踩了你两次，得意得逢人就说。这是本学期最美好的一晚。|他踩了你的脚九次，道歉了十次。这是本学期最美好的一晚。}\n# en: Ask Seraphine\nopt.seraphine.label [4c88b3] = 邀请瑟拉芬\n# en: She may already know you'll ask.\nopt.seraphine.hint [da4e86] = 她可能早就知道你会邀请她。\n# en: {flag(ball_seraphine): \"Same as last time,\" Seraphine says, \"but you'll wear blue.\" You hadn't planned to. You do.|\"I said yes three weeks ago,\" she says, \"you just hadn't asked yet.\" She dances a beat ahead of the music.}\nopt.seraphine.say.1 [8d2828] = {flag(ball_seraphine): “和上回一样，”瑟拉芬说，“不过你会穿蓝色。”你本来没打算穿。结果你穿了。|“我三周前就答应了，”她说，“只是你那时还没开口。”她的舞步总比音乐快半拍。}\n# en: Ask Corvin\nopt.corvin.label [cbffdf] = 邀请科尔文\n# en: Odds he shows up: unclear.\nopt.corvin.hint [bd223d] = 他会不会出现：不好说。\n# en: {flag(ball_corvin): He arrives on time, which alarms everyone, in a jacket he swears is borrowed. He is still the best dancer in the room.|He arrives late, in a stolen jacket, and is the best dancer in the room.}\nopt.corvin.say.1 [48f270] = {flag(ball_corvin): 他准时到了，把所有人都吓了一跳，身上那件外套他发誓是借的。他依然是全场跳得最好的人。|他迟到了，穿着一件偷来的外套，却是全场跳得最好的人。}\n# en: {flag(ball_corvin_arrested): He never shows. A note arrives at midnight: \"Not arrested this time. Worse. Explain later.\" He never does.|He never shows. A note arrives at midnight: \"Got arrested. Long story. Save me a dance next year.\"}\nopt.corvin.say.2 [0ac25d] = {flag(ball_corvin_arrested): 他始终没来。午夜时分送来一张字条：“这次没被捕。更糟。回头解释。”他始终没解释。|他始终没来。午夜时分送来一张字条：“被捕了。说来话长。明年给我留支舞。”}\n# en: Ask Mir\nopt.mir.label [c272fe] = 邀请米尔\n# en: {flag(ball_mir): They promise the violin has been fixed.|They're bringing a self-playing violin.}\nopt.mir.hint [ff16f8] = {flag(ball_mir): TA 保证小提琴已经修好了。|TA 要带一把会自己演奏的小提琴。}\n# en: {flag(ball_mir): The violin behaves all night. Mir is almost disappointed, until it plays the last waltz entirely on its own and bows.|Mir's violin plays itself, then plays the chandelier, then plays the windows. You are asked to leave early. Worth it.}\nopt.mir.say.1 [dddb91] = {flag(ball_mir): 小提琴整晚都很规矩。米尔几乎有点失望，直到最后一支华尔兹它完全自己拉完，还鞠了一躬。|米尔的小提琴自己拉了起来，然后拉起了吊灯，然后拉起了窗户。你们被请提前离场。值了。}\n# en: Ask Kael\nopt.kael.label [bf9bdb] = 邀请凯尔\n# en: He doesn't seem to feel the cold.\nopt.kael.hint [3c4856] = 他似乎感觉不到寒冷。\n# en: {flag(ball_kael): He takes your hand without asking this time, as if it were already decided. The frost-ferns on the glass grow into a whole winter garden.|He holds your hand like it is something fragile. Where your fingers touch, frost-ferns bloom on the glass.}\nopt.kael.say.1 [a16e50] = {flag(ball_kael): 这一次他没问就牵起了你的手，仿佛早已说定。玻璃上的霜蕨长成了一整座冬日花园。|他握着你的手，像握着什么易碎的东西。你们指尖相触之处，玻璃上绽开了霜蕨。}\n# en: Ask Nessa\nopt.nessa.label [6cfd84] = 邀请内莎\n# en: She'll be working. She's always working.\nopt.nessa.hint [698b3a] = 她会边跳边工作。她永远在工作。\n# en: {flag(ball_nessa): She leaves her notebook in her bag all night. \"The column can make something up,\" she says. It does.|She takes notes between dances. \"For the society column.\" Then she stops taking notes.}\nopt.nessa.say.1 [56ee97] = {flag(ball_nessa): 她整晚都把笔记本留在包里。“专栏可以随便编点什么，”她说。专栏真的编了。|她在舞曲间隙做笔记。“写社交专栏用的。”后来，她不再做笔记了。}\n# en: Go with friends\nopt.friends.label [596790] = 和朋友们一起去\n# en: {flag(ball_friends): You go as a pack again, and this year someone has taught everyone the same dance, badly. It is still perfect.|You go as a pack and dance badly together and it is perfect.}\nopt.friends.say.1 [7848f2] = {flag(ball_friends): 你们又成群结队地去了，今年有人教了大家同一支舞，大家都跳得很糟。依然完美。|你们成群结队地去，一起跳得乱七八糟，完美极了。}\n# en: Stay in and study\nopt.skip.label [6d8b57] = 留在宿舍学习\n# en: The music drifts up through the floor while you read. Somewhere, people are happy. You are productive.\nopt.skip.say.1 [c55696] = 你看书时，音乐透过地板飘上来。某个地方，人们很快乐。而你，很高效。\n\n@thread tournament\n# en: The Spring Tournament\nname [e40010] = 春季锦标赛\n# en: Duelling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching.\npremise [2fa46f] = 在大草坪上决斗，全学院都在看。\n# en: You fought your way to the semifinal of the Spring Tournament.\njournal.1 [a3081f] = 你一路打进了春季锦标赛的半决赛。\n# en: In the final, Lyra was waiting. \"I was hoping it would be you.\"\njournal.2 [2269d6] = 决赛场上，莱拉在等你。“我就盼着是你。”\n# en: You won the Spring Tournament, and the lawn erupted.\njournal.3 [cc0536] = 你赢下了春季锦标赛，整片草坪沸腾了。\n\n@event spring_tournament\n# en: The Spring Tournament\ntitle [e40010] = 春季锦标赛\n# en: The Spring Tournament: dueling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching. Entry is open to anyone foolish enough.\ntext [75ecd9] = 春季锦标赛：在大草坪上决斗，全院围观。任何够傻的人都可以报名。\n# en: A ring of white stone on the lawn, crowds in the stands, two duelists bowing as their wands spark.\nscene [903c03] = 草坪上一圈白石决斗场，看台上挤满人群，两名决斗者行礼致意，魔杖迸出火花。\n# en: Watch from the stands\nopt.left.label [8d4dec] = 在看台上观战\n# en: You eat candied chestnuts and scream for strangers.\nopt.left.say.1 [8ad597] = 你吃着糖炒栗子，为素不相识的人尖叫助威。\n# en: Enter the tournament\nopt.right.label [dd70a1] = 报名参赛\n# en: First round: you win, barely, and {flag(tournament_champion): the crowd remembers your name from last year and chants it.|the crowd learns your name.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a286bf] = 第一轮：你赢了，险胜，{flag(tournament_champion): 观众还记得你去年的名字，齐声喊了起来。|观众记住了你的名字。}\n# en: First round: you are knocked out of the ring before you've found your footing. The crowd has already turned to the next bout.\nopt.right.say.2 [7f5e0d] = 第一轮：你还没站稳脚跟就被打出了场外。观众已经转去看下一场了。\n\n@event spring_tournament_circle\n# en: The Spring Tournament\ntitle [e40010] = 春季锦标赛\n# en: The Spring Tournament: duelling on the great lawn, the whole academy watching. The Duelling Circle's names went up on the board a week ago, yours among them. {rank(duelling) >= 3: You are the captain, and the Circle expects its captain to win something.|The captain wants results, and says so.}\ntext [baac89] = 春季锦标赛：在大草坪上决斗，全院围观。一周前，决斗社的名单就贴上了公告板，你的名字也在其中。{rank(duelling) >= 3: 你就是社长，决斗社指望社长拿点什么回来。|社长要的是成绩，而且直说了。}\n# en: The great lawn ringed with banners, a chalk duelling circle at its centre and the Duelling Circle's names on a board.\nscene [0ee357] = 挂满旗帜的大草坪，中央用粉笔画着决斗圈，一块板上写着决斗社成员的名字。\n# en: Fight to make a good showing\nopt.left.label [ad260b] = 打出一场像样的比赛\n# en: You win your first bout cleanly and lose the second honourably. {rank(duelling) >= 3: Your second-years cheer their captain louder than anyone in the stands.|The captain ticks your name, which from her is a medal.}\nopt.left.say.1 [84d823] = 你干净利落地赢下第一场，又体面地输掉第二场。{rank(duelling) >= 3: 你的二年级社员为社长欢呼，比看台上任何人都响。|社长在你的名字旁打了个勾——从她那儿来说，这就是一枚奖章。}\n# en: You go out in the first round, on your feet at least. {rank(duelling) >= 3: Your second-years are kind about it, which is worse.|The captain says you fought like someone who expected to lose.}\nopt.left.say.2 [9fdebe] = 你在第一轮就出局了，好歹是站着出去的。{rank(duelling) >= 3: 你的二年级社员对此很体贴，这更糟。|社长说你打得像个早就料到自己会输的人。}\n# en: Go for the title\nopt.right.label [a6e251] = 冲击冠军\n# en: First round: you win, barely, and the crowd learns your name.\nopt.right.say.1 [5dba97] = 第一轮：你赢了，赢得很险，全场都记住了你的名字。\n# en: First round: you are knocked out of the ring before you've found your footing, and the Circle's bench goes very quiet.\nopt.right.say.2 [fdc482] = 第一轮：你还没站稳脚跟就被打出了场外，决斗社的长凳一下子安静得出奇。\n\n@event tournament_semifinal\n# en: Semifinal\ntitle [4ddbc9] = 半决赛\n# en: The semifinal. Your opponent is a fourth-year who has never lost. The crowd is louder now; you can hear your own name in it.\ntext [69650a] = 半决赛。你的对手是一位从未输过的四年级生。人群更吵了；你能在喧闹中听见自己的名字。\n# en: The same ring at sunset, scorch marks on the stone. Your opponent is grinning.\nscene [54c532] = 夕阳下的同一座决斗场，石面上满是焦痕。你的对手咧嘴笑着。\n# en: Fight clean\nopt.left.label [e5ec00] = 堂堂正正地战斗\n# en: You trade spells like questions and answers — and get the last word.\nopt.left.say.1 [a411dd] = 你们你来我往地交换咒语，像一问一答——最后一句是你说的。\n# en: A good fight. Not good enough. The crowd applauds you off the lawn.\nopt.left.say.2 [2ec6f6] = 打得很好。但还不够好。人群鼓掌送你离开草坪。\n# en: Fight dirty\nopt.right.label [2323b9] = 耍点小手段\n# en: A feint, a trip, a jinx to the shoelaces. Legal. Mostly.{met(bram):  On the prefects' bench Bram watches the whole thing with his jaw set, and doesn't clap.|}\nopt.right.say.1 [e37fae] = 一个虚招，一个绊子，一个冲着鞋带的小咒。合规。大体上。{met(bram): 级长席上，布拉姆紧绷着下巴看完了全程，没有鼓掌。|}\n# en: The referee sees the trick. Disqualified, and booed.\nopt.right.say.2 [06fbf6] = 裁判看穿了把戏。你被取消资格，还挨了嘘声。\n\n@event tournament_final\n# en: The Final\ntitle [5a826c] = 决赛\n# en: The final. Across the ring stands {lyra}, wand raised, eyes blazing. \"I was hoping it would be you,\" she says, and she is not being friendly.\ntext [9091dd] = 决赛。决斗场对面站着{lyra}，魔杖高举，双眼燃着火光。“我就希望是你，”她说，语气可一点也不友好。\n# en: The dueling ring under floating lanterns, a silver cup on a plinth, and across from you, a familiar face.\nscene [2b21ea] = 悬浮灯笼下的决斗场，底座上放着一只银杯，对面是一张熟悉的面孔。\n# en: Give everything you have\nopt.left.label [eef34c] = 拼尽全力\n# en: Your final spell lands like a bell. Silence — then the lawn erupts. Lyra stares at you as if seeing you for the first time, then walks off the lawn without shaking your hand.\nopt.left.say.1 [e71e19] = 你最后一道咒语像钟声一样落下。一片寂静——随后草坪沸腾了。莱拉看着你，好像第一次真正看见你，然后没和你握手就离开了草坪。\n# en: She wins, but only just. Afterwards, she offers her hand. \"Next year,\" she says. It is a promise and a threat.\nopt.left.say.2 [6d4f46] = 她赢了，但只是险胜。赛后，她向你伸出手。“明年。”她说。这既是承诺，也是威胁。\n# en: Yield with a bow\nopt.right.label [7ad851] = 鞠躬认输\n# en: The crowd boos. Lyra does not cheer her victory. Later she finds you: \"Why?\" You don't have a good answer, and she seems to like that.\nopt.right.say.1 [957fd4] = 人群发出嘘声。莱拉没有为自己的胜利欢呼。后来她找到你：“为什么？”你答不上来，而她似乎正喜欢这一点。\n\n@event grand_exams\n# en: Grand Examinations\ntitle [9cf559] = 年度大考\n# en: Year-end examinations. Three days, five examiners and a practical in front of a panel that includes the headmistress. Your standing for the year rides on this.\ntext [148501] = 学年末考试。三天，五位考官，还要在包括院长在内的评审团面前完成一项实践考核。你这一年的学业，全押在这上面了。\n# en: A circular examination chamber with a domed ceiling of moving constellations; examiners in grey robes sit behind a long table.\nscene [a8e8ed] = 一间圆形考场，穹顶上的星座缓缓移动；身着灰袍的考官们坐在长桌后。\n# en: Prepare a dazzling practical\nopt.left.label [6b5ebd] = 准备一场惊艳的实践考核\n# en: You finish your practical and the examiners actually lean forward. Distinction.\nopt.left.say.1 [1ca3b3] = 你完成实践考核时，考官们居然真的向前倾了倾身子。优秀。\n# en: The practical sputters. An examiner writes something very short.\nopt.left.say.2 [1a07cb] = 实践考核噼啪作响地熄火了。一位考官写下了很短的一句话。\n# en: Write a meticulous thesis\nopt.right.label [018303] = 写一篇严谨的论文\n# en: Your thesis is cited by one examiner in an argument with another. Distinction.\nopt.right.say.1 [dbc14d] = 一位考官在和另一位争论时引用了你的论文。优秀。\n# en: Your thesis is described, aloud, as \"ambitious\". It is not a compliment.\nopt.right.say.2 [067468] = 你的论文被当众评价为“很有野心”。这可不是夸奖。\n\n@event summer_plans\n# en: Summer Recess\ntitle [84178b] = 暑假\n# en: The towers empty for the summer. Two months of freedom. How will you spend them?\ntext [28317e] = 塔楼在夏天空了下来。两个月的自由。你打算怎么过？\n# en: The academy gates thrown open to green hills; students with trunks waving from departing sky-barges.\nscene [35fcaa] = 学院大门向青翠的山丘敞开；拖着行李箱的学生们在离港的天空驳船上挥手。\n# en: Go home\nopt.home.label [c93952] = 回家\n# en: Rest and recover. No rent while away.\nopt.home.hint [735a7b] = 休息与恢复。离校期间不交房租。\n# en: Your family hosts three dinners in your honour and asks about your marriage prospects at all of them.\nopt.home.say.1 [47c092] = 家里为你办了三场晚宴，每一场都有人问起你的婚事。\n# en: The old crowd in Port Calder treats you like a returning hero, and steals your trunk only once.\nopt.home.say.2 [c552ac] = 考尔德港的老伙计们把你当成荣归故里的英雄，只偷了你的行李箱一次。\n# en: Home is smaller than you remember, and warmer. Someone made your favourite pie.\nopt.home.say.3 [c3fe6f] = 家比你记忆中小，也更温暖。有人做了你最爱吃的馅饼。\n# en: Take summer work in the village\nopt.work.label [3f8e3c] = 在镇上打暑期工\n# en: About 50 gold over the summer. Tiring.\nopt.work.hint [6173e0] = 整个夏天约 50 金币。很累。\n# en: You haul crates, serve tables and polish brass until your hands know the shape of every coin.\nopt.work.say.1 [55acbe] = 你搬箱子、端盘子、擦铜器，直到双手熟悉每一枚硬币的形状。\n# en: Stay and study in the empty library\nopt.study.label [968f6e] = 留在空荡的图书馆学习\n# en: +Lore, +Arcana, +Standing. Lonely.\nopt.study.hint [8c75a3] = +学识，+奥术，+学业。很孤单。\n# en: Just you, the books and the dust motes. You learn things the term never had time for.\nopt.study.say.1 [fe7d5b] = 只有你、书本和浮尘。你学到了学期里永远没时间学的东西。\n# en: Travel to the coast\nopt.travel.label [78a0b3] = 去海边旅行\n# en: Costs 40 gold. Great for mood.\nopt.travel.hint [b46376] = 花费 40 金币。心情大好。\n# en: Needs 40 crowns.\nopt.travel.locked [4deed8] = 需要 40 克朗。\n# en: Salt air, shell markets, a sea serpent at sunset. You come back tanned and ten years younger.\nopt.travel.say.1 [1db6b9] = 咸咸的海风，贝壳集市，夕阳下的海蛇。你回来时晒黑了，年轻了十岁。\n# en: Join Old Maud's herb expedition\nopt.expedition.label [49633f] = 参加老莫德的采药远征\n# en: Requires knowing Maud. Adventure.\nopt.expedition.hint [362c6b] = 需要认识老莫德。冒险。\n# en: Two months in the Whisperwood with Maud and her goat. You learn which mushrooms sing and which ones lie.\nopt.expedition.say.1 [929c02] = 和莫德还有她的山羊在低语森林里待了两个月。你学会了分辨哪些蘑菇会唱歌，哪些会撒谎。\n\n@event summer_letter\n# en: A Letter From Home\ntitle [418673] = 一封家书\n# en: A letter arrives in the last month of summer, the handwriting so familiar it hurts. They want to know how you are doing. Really doing.\ntext [67658a] = 夏天的最后一个月，一封信寄到了，字迹熟悉得让人心疼。他们想知道你过得怎么样。真正过得怎么样。\n# en: A letter sealed with candle wax lying on a sunlit windowsill.\nscene [ac2d7b] = 一封用烛蜡封口的信，躺在洒满阳光的窗台上。\n# en: Write back honestly\nopt.left.label [24b451] = 如实回信\n# en: You tell them about your grades. Their reply is kind, which is worse.\nopt.left.say.1 [5e0fac] = 你告诉了他们你的成绩。他们的回信很温柔，这反而更难受。\n# en: You write six pages. It feels like setting down a heavy bag.\nopt.left.say.2 [f6372c] = 你写了六页。感觉像放下了一个沉重的包袱。\n# en: Write back that everything is perfect\nopt.right.label [979dc2] = 回信说一切都很完美\n# en: Proud of you, they write, and enclose a little money{has(origin_noble) or has(origin_merchant): , as if a purse were the right answer to a letter.|  you suspect they couldn't spare.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b6e0ca] = 他们回信说为你骄傲，还附上了一点钱{has(origin_noble) or has(origin_merchant): ，好像一袋钱就是回信的正确答案。|——你猜他们其实拿不出来。}\n# en: They reply with exclamation marks. The lie sits in your stomach all week.\nopt.right.say.2 [75bc50] = 他们的回信满是感叹号。这个谎言在你胃里堵了一整个星期。\n\n@event starfall\n# en: Starfall\ntitle [ab87b4] = 星坠节\n# en: {year == 1: The last night of the academic year. Everyone climbs onto the observatory roof, and the rest spill out onto the lawns with blankets, to watch the stars fall. They say if you catch one in a jar, you get a year of luck.|Starfall again. The observatory roof, the lawns full of blankets, the whole academy lying on its back.} {starfall_jar >= 1: Your jar is ready, cork in hand.|} {~The first star falls before anyone is ready.|Somebody has brought a lute, unwisely.|The lake is full of reflected stars.}\ntext [56614b] = {year == 1: 学年的最后一夜。大家都爬上观星台屋顶，其余的人带着毯子涌到草坪上，看星星坠落。据说，如果你能用罐子接住一颗，就能交一整年的好运。|又是星坠节。观星台的屋顶，铺满毯子的草坪，整个学院都仰面躺着。} {starfall_jar >= 1: 你的罐子准备好了，软木塞就在手里。|} {~第一颗星星在大家还没准备好时就落下了。|有人带了一把鲁特琴，不太明智。|湖里满是倒映的星星。}\n# en: Students lying on the observatory roof under a sky streaked with falling stars.\nscene [7b9deb] = 学生们躺在天文台屋顶上，头顶的夜空划满了流星。\n# en: Lie back and just watch\nopt.left.label [df7032] = 躺下来，只是看着\n# en: You count forty falling stars and then stop counting. It's enough.\nopt.left.say.1 [d2a569] = 你数了四十颗流星，然后不再数了。这就够了。\n# en: Try to catch a star\nopt.right.label [734781] = 试着接住一颗星\n# en: Nobody has caught one in years. A good jar from the observatory improves the odds.\nopt.right.hint [26a72f] = 好些年没人接住过一颗了。观星台做的好罐子能提高几分胜算。\n# en: It lands in your jar with a hiss and glows for weeks. Students come to see it.\nopt.right.say.1 [f06369] = 它嘶的一声落进你的罐子，发光发了好几个星期。同学们都跑来看。\n# en: You lunge, miss, and nearly roll off the roof. A prefect catches you by the collar.\nopt.right.say.2 [dd228d] = 你扑了过去，没接到，差点滚下屋顶。一位级长揪住了你的衣领。\n# en: It lands in your jar with a hiss and glows for weeks. Students come to see it.\nopt.right.say.3 [f06369] = 它嘶的一声落进你的罐子，发了好几个星期的光。同学们都来看它。\n# en: You lunge, miss, and nearly roll off the roof. A prefect catches you by the collar.\nopt.right.say.4 [dd228d] = 你扑过去，没接住，差点从屋顶滚下去。一位级长揪住了你的领子。\n# en: Find your sweetheart on the lawn\nopt.together.label [6cf90b] = 去草坪上找你的心上人\n# en: You leave the roof and go looking. You know exactly where to look.\nopt.together.say.1 [8e8e09] = 你离开屋顶去找。你很清楚该去哪儿找。\n# en: Make the Starfall wish\nopt.wish.label [a6bf48] = 许下星坠节的愿望\n# en: You climb to the very top of the roof, where the old custom says the wishes go.\nopt.wish.say.1 [c0d3b2] = 你爬到屋顶的最高处，老习俗说愿望都从那里升上去。\n\n@event year_two\n# en: Second Year\ntitle [c51ea2] = 第二学年\n# en: The barge brings new first-years, and you watch them gawp at the towers. Was that really you a year ago? You know which staircases move now, and which professors you can charm. Second-years, a prefect announces, may apply for summer research posts in Stormbloom.{chosen(break_plan, home):  Your trunk still smells of home, and there's a cake in it you were told to share.|}\ntext [b83f07] = 驳船载来了新的一年级生，你看着他们仰望七塔，目瞪口呆。一年前那真的是你吗？如今你知道哪些楼梯会动，哪些教授能哄得住。一位级长宣布：二年级生可以在风暴月申请暑期研究职位。{chosen(break_plan, home): 你的箱子里还带着家的味道，里面还有一块蛋糕，家里人叮嘱你一定要分给大家。|}\n# en: The seven towers in autumn colours, the first-year barge arriving below, tiny and nervous.\nscene [aa7c3d] = 秋色中的七座高塔，下方新生的驳船正在靠岸，渺小而紧张。\n# en: Begin the second year\nopt.ok.label [338fd8] = 开始第二学年\n# en: You unpack faster than last year, and in the right order. Someone in your year already has a list of the research posts, and the castle's midnight doors, side by side.\nopt.ok.say.1 [08f4a0] = 你收拾行李比去年快，顺序也对了。你们年级已经有人列好了研究职位的清单，旁边并排列着城堡午夜开放的门。\n\n@event term_begins\n# en: The Spring Term\ntitle [c548b1] = 春季学期\n# en: {chosen(break_plan, home): You come back from home with a tin of something your family insists you'll need, and the castle feels enormous after a winter of small rooms.|{chosen(break_plan, work): Your holiday wages are in your purse and your feet still ache from earning them. The castle fills up around you, trunk by trunk.|After a winter of empty corridors, the castle fills up around you again, and the noise of it is almost too much.}} The spring term begins, and the registrar's hall is already queueing.\ntext [f78fe0] = {chosen(break_plan, home): 你从家里回来，带着一罐家人坚持说你用得上的东西。过了一个冬天的小屋子，城堡显得大得出奇。|{chosen(break_plan, work): 假期挣的工钱揣在钱袋里，挣钱时磨疼的脚还没缓过来。城堡里一只只箱子搬进来，又热闹起来了。|空荡荡的走廊过了一个冬天，城堡又热闹起来，吵得你几乎有点受不了。}}春季学期开始了，教务大厅已经排起了长队。\n# en: The castle steps after the winter break, trunks stacked by the doors and the first lamps lit in the towers.\nscene [6e70cd] = 寒假过后的城堡台阶，门边堆着箱子，塔楼里亮起了第一批灯。\n# en: Get a head start on the reading\nopt.left.label [62ecd0] = 先把书读起来\n# en: You find a window seat in the library before the term has properly started. By the time the others arrive, you have a list of questions and a crick in your neck.\nopt.left.say.1 [1998c7] = 学期还没正式开始，你就在图书馆占了个靠窗的位子。等别人到了，你已经攒了一串问题，脖子也落了枕。\n# en: Catch up with everyone first\nopt.right.label [55ea0a] = 先和大家叙叙旧\n# en: You spend the first evening in the common room, then at the Newt, hearing everyone's winter. The reading can wait a day. It waits three.\nopt.right.say.1 [ceec42] = 第一个晚上你先泡在公共休息室，后来又去了镀金蝾螈酒馆，听每个人讲自己的冬天。书可以晚一天再读。结果晚了三天。\n\n@event year_three\n# en: Third Year\ntitle [c19627] = 第三年\n# en: \"Third years,\" the headmistress says, \"you are past the middle of your time here. Next year will ask a great deal of you; spend this one finding out what you want from it.\" She looks, for a moment, directly at you.{chosen(break_plan, home):  You came straight from home, and the summer's quiet is still on you.|}\ntext [320bae] = “三年级的同学们，”院长说，“你们在这里的时光已经过半。明年会对你们提出很多要求；今年就用来弄明白，你们想从中得到什么。”有那么一刻，她直直地看着你。{chosen(break_plan, home): 你是从家里直接回来的，夏天的那份安静还留在身上。|}\n# en: The headmistress addressing a hall of third-years, every candle in the room leaning toward her.\nscene [3c61bc] = 院长向满堂的三年级生讲话，屋里的每一支蜡烛都向她倾斜。\n# en: Begin the third year\nopt.ok.label [85c8b5] = 开始第三年\n# en: You know the castle well enough now to get lost in it on purpose. This is the last year for a summer research post, and the holiday notices by the gates pay better every year.\nopt.ok.say.1 [9f3f57] = 你对城堡已经熟到可以故意在里面迷路了。今年是申请暑期研究职位的最后一年，而校门边的假期招工告示，一年比一年给得多。\n\n@event isolde_asks_again\n# en: Ask Me Again\ntitle [27b3f8] = 再问我一次\n# en: Headmistress Marrow finds you in the cloister, the same small notebook under her arm, four years more worn. \"I asked you what was just,\" she says, and reads it back: {flag(just_mercy): \"Mercy.\"|{flag(just_fair): \"Fairness.\"|\"Keeping your own safe.\"}} She looks at you over the page. {(flag(just_mercy) and alignment >= 20) or (flag(just_fair) and alignment > -20 and alignment < 20) or (flag(just_strong) and alignment <= -20): \"You kept to it. I watched.\"|\"You changed your answer without telling me. Most people do.\"} \"Well? I'm asking again.\"\ntext [032164] = 马罗院长在回廊里找到你，胳膊底下夹着同一本小笔记本，比四年前又旧了些。“我问过你什么是公正，”她说，把它念了出来：{flag(just_mercy): “仁慈。”|{flag(just_fair): “公平。”|“护住自己人。”}}她从书页上方看着你。{(flag(just_mercy) and alignment >= 20) or (flag(just_fair) and alignment > -20 and alignment < 20) or (flag(just_strong) and alignment <= -20): “你守住了。我一直看着。”|“你改了答案，却没告诉我。大多数人都这样。”}“怎么样？我再问一次。”\n# en: A grey-haired headmistress in an evening cloister holding a small worn notebook open, a final-year in graduation robes beside her.\nscene [2eb142] = 傍晚的回廊里，一位头发花白的院长摊开一本旧了的小笔记本，身旁是一个穿着毕业袍的毕业生。\n# en: Give the same answer\nopt.left.label [416493] = 给出同样的答案\n# en: {(flag(just_mercy) and alignment >= 20) or (flag(just_fair) and alignment > -20 and alignment < 20) or (flag(just_strong) and alignment <= -20): You say it again, and this time you know what it cost. She writes it under the first, in the same hand. \"Good,\" she says. \"Some answers are worth giving twice.\"|You say it again, though you both know the four years said something else. She writes it down anyway. \"Then go and live up to it,\" she says. \"You've time. Not much, but some.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [6928e3] = {(flag(just_mercy) and alignment >= 20) or (flag(just_fair) and alignment > -20 and alignment < 20) or (flag(just_strong) and alignment <= -20): 你又说了一遍，这回你知道它的代价了。她把它写在第一个下面，同样的笔迹。“好，”她说，“有些答案值得说两遍。”|你又说了一遍，尽管你们俩都知道这四年说的是另一回事。她还是写了下来。“那就去做到它，”她说，“你还有时间。不多，但有一些。”}\n# en: Give a new answer\nopt.right.label [36f9bd] = 给出新的答案\n# en: You tell her what four years taught you instead. She listens to all of it, writes it under the first, and closes the notebook on both. \"That,\" she says, \"is what I hoped the castle would do to you.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [642efd] = 你告诉她这四年教会你的东西。她全都听完，写在第一个下面，把两个一起合进笔记本。“这，”她说，“正是我希望城堡对你做的事。”\n\n@event graduation_eve\n# en: The Eve of Graduation\ntitle [16a29d] = 毕业前夜\n# en: Tomorrow you graduate. Your robes are laid out on the bed, slightly too long. Outside, the towers are lit gold. Four years have gone like a single held breath.\ntext [feebe6] = 明天你就要毕业了。学士袍摊在床上，稍微长了一点。窗外，高塔被点亮成金色。四年时光，像屏住的一口气，倏忽而过。\n# en: Graduation robes laid out on a dormitory bed, a single candle burning, the towers lit gold outside the window.\nscene [47f9c3] = 宿舍床上摊着毕业袍，一支蜡烛独自燃烧，窗外的塔楼灯火金黄。\n# en: Spend the night with friends\nopt.left.label [476937] = 和朋友们共度今夜\n# en: {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0: Laughter until dawn. Promises to write. Some of them, you'll keep.|You knock on a few doors. Nobody who answers is a close friend, not yet, but they pour you a cup and keep you up laughing till dawn. You trade addresses anyway.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1265bf] = {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) > 0: 笑到天亮。约好要写信。其中一些约定，你会遵守。|你敲了几扇门。来开门的人都还算不上挚友，但他们给你倒了一杯，陪你笑到天亮。你们还是交换了地址。}\n# en: Walk the halls alone one last time\nopt.right.label [9cc549] = 最后一次独自走遍厅廊\n# en: Every corridor holds a memory. At the end of the last one, the castle sighs, as if saying goodbye.\nopt.right.say.1 [fce683] = 每一条走廊都藏着回忆。走到最后一条的尽头时，城堡轻轻叹了口气，像是在道别。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/careers.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/careers.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event career_letter_conclave\n# en: A Letter From the Seven Towers\ntitle [843914] = 七塔来信\n# en: Silver wax, seven little towers in it. The Conclave writes that Professor Thorne has mentioned your name, \"more than once and without being asked\", and offers you an apprenticeship at the Seven Towers after graduation. Your answer is due on the last day.\ntext [9fc6fa] = 银色封蜡，里面压着七座小塔。七塔议会来信说，索恩教授提过你的名字，“不止一次，而且没人问他”，并邀请你毕业后去七塔当学徒。你需在最后一天答复。\n# en: A letter sealed in silver wax on a dormitory desk, seven small towers pressed into the seal.\nscene [1cb250] = 宿舍书桌上一封用银蜡封口的信，封蜡上压着七座小塔。\n# en: Write back that you're interested\nopt.left.label [ac32a4] = 回信说你有意\n# en: You write three drafts and send the shortest. Thorne passes you in the corridor the next day and says \"Hm,\" which from him is a parade.\nopt.left.say.1 [008c22] = 你写了三稿，寄出最短的那份。第二天索恩在走廊里经过你身边，说了声“嗯”，从他嘴里出来，这简直是一场游行。\n# en: Ask what an apprentice actually does\nopt.right.label [e09784] = 问学徒到底做些什么\n# en: The answer comes by return: long hours, a grey robe, and seven years before anyone lets you near a real ward. It is less glamorous on paper. The offer stands.\nopt.right.say.1 [e28410] = 回信很快：工时很长，一件灰袍，七年后才会有人让你碰真正的护界。纸面上没那么光鲜。聘约依然有效。\n\n@event career_letter_fellowship\n# en: A Fellowship at the Spire\ntitle [c606b6] = 尖塔的研究员职位\n# en: The note is inside a book you returned last week, which is how the Spire Library's fellows do things. They have a junior fellowship free, a carrel with your name already chalked on it, and a question: would you like to spend your life among books that bite? Answer by the last day.\ntext [f66f0c] = 字条夹在你上周还回去的一本书里，尖塔图书馆的研究员们就是这么办事的。他们空出一个初级研究员职位，一个已用粉笔写上你名字的隔间，还有一个问题：你愿意在会咬人的书中间度过一生吗？最后一天前答复。\n# en: A folded note tucked inside a returned library book, written in tiny, careful faculty handwriting.\nscene [bd38bb] = 一张折起的字条夹在还回去的图书馆藏书里，字迹细小而工整，是教职员的笔迹。\n# en: Chalk \"yes\" under your name\nopt.left.label [7f720d] = 在名字下写“愿意”\n# en: You go down to the carrel that evening and chalk a small \"yes\" under your name. In the morning someone has underlined it.\nopt.left.say.1 [dec700] = 那天傍晚你下到隔间，在自己名字下用粉笔写了个小小的“愿意”。第二天早上，有人在它下面画了一道线。\n# en: Ask how many fellows the books have eaten\nopt.right.label [d2af51] = 问书吃掉过几个研究员\n# en: \"Three,\" the reply says, \"in two hundred years, and all of them were careless.\" You are not entirely reassured. The carrel waits.\nopt.right.say.1 [92f0dd] = “三个，”回信说，“两百年里，而且都是不小心的。”你并没有完全放心。隔间在等你。\n\n@event career_letter_shop\n# en: A Lease by the Harbour\ntitle [f6fdaf] = 港口边的租约\n# en: The lease comes with a brass key and a letter from a harbour landlord whose wife swears by your hair tonic. A crooked little shop, two rooms and a still, yours from the autumn if you sign on the last day. Nobody has ever offered you a door of your own before.\ntext [22f9e8] = 租约附着一把铜钥匙，和一位港口房东的信，他妻子对你的生发水赞不绝口。一间歪歪扭扭的小铺子，两个房间和一口蒸馏器，只要你在最后一天签字，秋天起就归你。从来没有人给过你一扇属于自己的门。\n# en: A rolled lease tied with string on a dormitory desk, a small brass key on top of it.\nscene [ec5323] = 宿舍书桌上一卷用细绳扎起的租约，上面搁着一把小铜钥匙。\n# en: Keep the key on a string round your neck\nopt.left.label [ea40fd] = 把钥匙挂在脖子上\n# en: You wear the key under your robes for the rest of the year. It's warm when you touch it, which is probably the hair tonic's doing.\nopt.left.say.1 [d0ddde] = 这一年剩下的日子，你把钥匙藏在袍子底下戴着。一摸它就是温的，多半是生发水的功劳。\n# en: Write back about the drains\nopt.right.label [818ea0] = 回信问问下水道\n# en: The landlord's reply is two pages about the drains, which you read twice. An alchemist's shop lives or dies by its drains. This one, apparently, will live.\nopt.right.say.1 [61dea7] = 房东回了两页纸讲下水道，你读了两遍。一家炼金铺子的死活就看下水道。这一家，看来能活。\n\n@event career_letter_unseen\n# en: A Letter That Wasn't Posted\ntitle [bfd784] = 一封没寄出的信\n# en: The envelope is on your pillow, though the door was locked and the window shut. Inside, a single line: the Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs has a desk for you, if you want it, from the autumn. There is no address to reply to. They'll know your answer on the last day.\ntext [2910b8] = 信封就躺在你的枕头上，可门锁着，窗也关着。里面只有一行字：王室隐秘事务署为你留了一张书桌，如果你想要，秋天起生效。没有回信地址。最后一天，他们自会知道你的答复。\n# en: A plain envelope with no stamp and no seal lying on a pillow that was empty a moment ago.\nscene [8c52f6] = 一个没有邮票也没有封蜡的素信封，躺在刚刚还空着的枕头上。\n# en: Leave a \"yes\" on the pillow\nopt.left.label [bc7c45] = 在枕头上留个“好”\n# en: You leave one word on the pillow and go to supper. When you come back, the paper is gone and the bed has been made. You did not make it.\nopt.left.say.1 [4e7f3f] = 你在枕头上留下一个字，就去吃晚饭了。回来时，纸不见了，床也铺好了。不是你铺的。\n# en: Find out how they got in\nopt.right.label [6530a3] = 查出他们怎么进来的\n# en: You spend a week on it and find the scratch on the window catch, the loose tile, the borrowed key. It's very good work. That's the trouble.\nopt.right.say.1 [d31946] = 你花了一个星期，找到了窗闩上的划痕、松动的瓦片、借来的钥匙。手法非常漂亮。麻烦就在这儿。\n\n@event career_letter_trading\n# en: A Partnership Offered\ntitle [aa46bb] = 一份合伙邀约\n# en: A shipping house in the harbour has heard about your purse, or rather about how you filled it. Its old partner wants to retire, and offers you his desk and his share of the ledger from the autumn. \"Bring your crowns,\" he writes. \"Bring your nerve. Answer on the last day.\"\ntext [5da9ff] = 港口的一家航运商行听说了你的钱袋，准确说，是听说了你怎么把它填满的。老合伙人想退休，邀请你秋天起接下他的书桌和他在账簿里的份额。“带上你的克朗，”他写道，“带上你的胆量。最后一天答复。”\n# en: A thick letter on cream paper, stamped with a shipping line's anchor, a tally of figures down its margin.\nscene [ec81db] = 一封写在奶白色纸上的厚信，盖着一家航运公司的船锚印，页边写着一列数字。\n# en: Write back that you're interested\nopt.left.label [ac32a4] = 回信说你有意\n# en: You reply on the cream paper's own back, which seems businesslike. By return comes a ledger page with a column already headed with your name.\nopt.left.say.1 [9ed1bd] = 你就在那张奶白色信纸的背面回信，显得很像做生意的样子。回信寄来一页账簿，有一栏已经写上了你的名字。\n# en: Ask to see the books first\nopt.right.label [e8145f] = 先要看账本\n# en: He sends them, all of them, and you find the hole in the third year that he hoped you'd find. \"Good,\" says his next letter. \"The last partner didn't.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [f6ceef] = 他寄来了，全部，你在第三年那里找到了他希望你找到的窟窿。“很好，”他的下一封信说，“上一个合伙人就没找到。”\n\n@event career_return_royal_library\n# en: The Archives Write Again\ntitle [712ad4] = 档案馆又来信了\n# en: The Royal Library Archives remember your summer. The keeper writes that a cataloguer's post is free from the autumn, \"for someone who reads the footnotes\", and that your name was the only one proposed. Answer by the last day.\ntext [da3b67] = 皇家图书馆档案馆记得你的那个夏天。馆长来信说，秋天起空出一个编目员职位，“给读脚注的人”，而被提名的只有你一个。最后一天前答复。\n# en: A letter in archivist's copperplate, a pressed slip of catalogue card tucked into the fold.\nscene [b79102] = 一封档案员铜版体写的信，折缝里夹着一小片压平的目录卡。\n# en: Write back in your best copperplate\nopt.left.label [46d416] = 用最好的铜版体回信\n# en: It takes an evening and four nibs. The keeper replies with a catalogue card made out in your name, under \"Staff\".\nopt.left.say.1 [e33d71] = 你花了一个晚上和四个笔尖。馆长回了一张目录卡，上面写着你的名字，归在“职员”一栏。\n# en: Ask which room you'd be given\nopt.right.label [75825e] = 问会分到哪个房间\n# en: \"The cold one,\" the keeper writes, \"with the folios that object to being opened.\" You will need gloves. You will also, you suspect, love it.\nopt.right.say.1 [6d8a5f] = “冷的那间，”馆长写道，“里面的对开本都不愿被翻开。”你会需要手套。你猜，你也会爱上那里。\n\n@event career_return_conclave\n# en: The Laboratory Writes Again\ntitle [f4b323] = 实验室又来信了\n# en: The Conclave Laboratory remembers your summer, and the report your supervisor wrote at the end of it. She writes that a place as a junior researcher is yours from the autumn if you'll take it. \"Don't make me ask twice,\" she adds. Answer by the last day.\ntext [ebdcbd] = 七塔议会实验室记得你的那个夏天，也记得夏末你的导师写的那份报告。她来信说，秋天起有一个初级研究员的位置，只要你肯来。“别让我问第二遍，”她补了一句。最后一天前答复。\n# en: A letter on laboratory paper, slightly singed at one corner, sealed with seven small towers.\nscene [22e3be] = 一封写在实验室用纸上的信，一角有点烧焦，封口压着七座小塔。\n# en: Write back yes, in principle\nopt.left.label [1a40b3] = 回信原则上答应\n# en: You write back the same night. Her reply is one line: \"Good. Bring your own gloves.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [2093d0] = 你当晚就回了信。她的回复只有一行：“好。自带手套。”\n# en: Ask what you'd be testing\nopt.right.label [b27f7d] = 问要测试些什么\n# en: Wards that are meant to fail, she writes, so that the ones on the city gates don't. You'd be standing very close to a great many explosions.\nopt.right.say.1 [4b68d8] = 她写道，测试那些本该失败的护界，好让城门上的那些不失败。你会站得离一大堆爆炸非常近。\n\n@event career_return_guild\n# en: The Works Write Again\ntitle [d13685] = 工坊又来信了\n# en: The Guild of Alchemists' works remember your summer, and how you kept your head at the vats. The foreman writes, in a hand that has clearly been burned more than once, that a journeyman's bench is yours from the autumn. Answer by the last day.\ntext [7267a7] = 炼金师公会的工坊记得你的那个夏天，记得你在大缸边沉得住气。工头的字迹显然被烧过不止一回，他写道，秋天起有一张熟练工的工作台归你。最后一天前答复。\n# en: A grubby letter with a chemical stain on the envelope, stamped with the Guild of Alchemists' retort.\nscene [02c284] = 一封脏兮兮的信，信封上有化学药渍，盖着炼金师公会的曲颈瓶印。\n# en: Write back yes, in principle\nopt.left.label [1a40b3] = 回信原则上答应\n# en: You write back yes. The foreman's reply is a list of protective clothing you will need to buy yourself, which you take as a welcome.\nopt.left.say.1 [955eae] = 你回信说愿意。工头回了一张你得自掏腰包买的防护服清单，你把它当作欢迎词。\n# en: Ask about the explosions\nopt.right.label [d22aa0] = 问问爆炸的事\n# en: \"Two a month,\" he writes, \"and none fatal since my father's day.\" You read it three times. The bench is still yours.\nopt.right.say.1 [d970ed] = “一个月两回，”他写道，“从我父亲那辈起就没死过人。”你读了三遍。工作台还是你的。\n\n@event career_return_observatory\n# en: The Expedition Writes Again\ntitle [331fc9] = 远征队又来信了\n# en: The Observatory Expedition remembers your summer on the plateau. Its leader writes that next year's survey needs a chartmaker who can stay awake till dawn, and a salaried place is yours from the autumn. Answer by the last day.\ntext [0170e1] = 观星远征记得你在高原上的那个夏天。领队来信说，明年的测绘需要一个能熬到天亮的绘图员，秋天起有一个领薪的位置归你。最后一天前答复。\n# en: A letter with a star chart sketched on the back, the paper creased from a long journey off a plateau.\nscene [c98210] = 一封信，背面画着星图草稿，纸张因从高原长途寄来而起了褶。\n# en: Write back yes, in principle\nopt.left.label [1a40b3] = 回信原则上答应\n# en: You write back under the star chart, adding one they'd missed. The leader's reply circles it twice.\nopt.left.say.1 [efcb66] = 你在星图下面回信，还补上了一颗他们漏掉的星。领队的回信把它圈了两圈。\n# en: Ask how cold the plateau gets\nopt.right.label [558c84] = 问高原有多冷\n# en: Very, is the answer, with a list of the frostbite remedies they carry. The stars, the letter adds, are worth it. You remember that they are.\nopt.right.say.1 [066f1b] = 回答是：非常冷，附一张他们随身带的冻疮药清单。信上又补了一句，星星值得。你记得，确实值得。\n\n@event career_return_field_station\n# en: The Field Station Writes Again\ntitle [0bb624] = 野外站又来信了\n# en: The Beastcraft Field Station remembers your summer on the moors. The warden writes that a keeper's post is yours from the autumn, with a cottage, a lamp and more creatures than any sane person would count. Answer by the last day.\ntext [60182c] = 驭兽术野外站记得你在荒原上的那个夏天。站长来信说，秋天起有一个看守的职位归你，配一间小屋、一盏灯，还有正常人根本数不过来的生灵。最后一天前答复。\n# en: A muddy letter with a feather pressed in it and a paw print smudged across the address.\nscene [1167f9] = 一封沾着泥的信，里面夹着一根羽毛，地址上抹着一个爪印。\n# en: Write back yes, in principle\nopt.left.label [1a40b3] = 回信原则上答应\n# en: You write back yes. The next letter has a second paw print on it, which the warden says belongs to whoever was sitting on the desk, and counts as a vote.\nopt.left.say.1 [aec20d] = 你回信说愿意。下一封信上多了一个爪印，站长说那是当时坐在桌上的那位留下的，算一票。\n# en: Ask about the winters\nopt.right.label [e04222] = 问问冬天的情形\n# en: Long, wet and lonely, the warden writes, honestly, and the best work there is. You believe the second half.\nopt.right.say.1 [91d4f3] = 站长老实写道：又长又湿又孤单，也是世上最好的活儿。后半句你信。\n\n@event career_return_thornwick\n# en: Thornwick Writes Again\ntitle [7df9d8] = 荆木又来信了\n# en: Thornwick's illusion laboratory remembers your summer{flag(thornwick_offer): and the offer you kept sealed in your trunk|}. The dean writes that a researcher's bench is yours from the autumn, across the hills, among people who think glamour is a science. Answer by the last day.\ntext [8c4535] = 荆木学院的幻术实验室记得你的那个夏天{flag(thornwick_offer): ，还有你封在箱底的那份聘约|}。院长来信说，秋天起有一张研究员工作台归你，就在山那边，和一群认为魅影是门科学的人一起。最后一天前答复。\n# en: A green-sealed letter on lacquered paper, from the rival academy over the hills.\nscene [0f2d6d] = 一封写在漆光纸上的绿封蜡信，来自山那边的对手学院。\n# en: Write back yes, in principle\nopt.left.label [1a40b3] = 回信原则上答应\n# en: You write back yes, in green ink, to annoy someone. The dean's reply says the ink was noticed and admired.\nopt.left.say.1 [68c007] = 你用绿墨水回信说愿意，就为了气气某个人。院长回信说，墨水颜色有人注意到了，还很欣赏。\n# en: Ask what Lumenhold would say\nopt.right.label [ecf2f6] = 问流明堡会怎么说\n# en: \"Something cutting,\" the dean writes, \"and then they'd hire you back at twice the salary.\" You suspect she's right about both.\nopt.right.say.1 [68d657] = “说些尖刻话，”院长写道，“然后开双倍薪水把你请回去。”你怀疑她两样都说对了。\n\n@event career_return_unseen\n# en: The Office Writes Again\ntitle [415c04] = 事务署又来信了\n# en: A blank page, until you hold it to the candle. The Office of Unseen Affairs remembers your summer, and how few questions you asked. A desk is yours from the autumn. No reply is needed; they will know your answer on the last day.\ntext [11bfc4] = 一张白纸，直到你把它凑近烛火。隐秘事务署记得你的那个夏天，记得你几乎什么都没问。秋天起有一张书桌归你。无需回信；最后一天，他们自会知道你的答复。\n# en: A blank sheet of paper on a desk that, held to a candle, shows writing in brown ink.\nscene [88d581] = 书桌上一张白纸，凑近烛火时显出棕色墨水写的字。\n# en: Burn it, as they'd want\nopt.left.label [177438] = 照他们的意思烧掉\n# en: You burn it over the candle and grind the ash into the grate. It feels like the first day of something.\nopt.left.say.1 [f0e511] = 你在烛火上把它烧了，把灰碾进炉栅里。感觉像是某件事的第一天。\n# en: Keep it, and read it again\nopt.right.label [312b07] = 留着它，再读一遍\n# en: You keep it, which they'll know, and read it by candle every few nights. There's a second line you missed the first time: \"We'd have kept it too.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [6e6f6e] = 你留下了它，他们会知道的，隔几晚就凑着烛光读一遍。上面还有一行你第一次漏看了：“换作我们，也会留着。”\n\n@event career_job_spire\n# en: The Stacks Want You Back\ntitle [91692d] = 书库想留你\n# en: A note under the bell on the reference desk, after your shift. The head librarian wants to keep you on after graduation, full time, as a keeper of the stacks. \"Footnote has stopped biting you,\" it says. \"That is a qualification.\" Answer by the last day.\ntext [f09636] = 下班后，咨询台的铃铛底下压着一张字条。总馆员想在你毕业后留你全职，做书库看守人。“脚注已经不咬你了，”上面写着，“这就是资格。”最后一天前答复。\n# en: The library's reference desk after hours, a note left under the bell in the head librarian's careful hand.\nscene [9b6321] = 闭馆后的图书馆咨询台，铃铛底下压着一张总馆员工整笔迹写的字条。\n# en: Leave a note under the bell: yes\nopt.left.label [47998a] = 在铃下留张字条：好\n# en: You leave your answer under the bell. In the morning there's a second note: \"Good. Bring a longer ladder.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [49bb26] = 你把答复压在铃铛底下。第二天早上多了一张字条：“好。带一架更长的梯子来。”\n# en: Ask about the restricted shelves\nopt.right.label [740975] = 问问禁书架的事\n# en: \"In time,\" says the next note, \"and only with gloves.\" It is not a no, and you find you're counting the years.\nopt.right.say.1 [0d0b4c] = “到时候，”下一张字条说，“而且得戴手套。”这不是拒绝，你发现自己已经在数年头了。\n\n@event career_job_newt\n# en: The Landlady's Offer\ntitle [1ffb69] = 老板娘的提议\n# en: After closing, the landlady counts the till, then counts you in. She wants to keep you on after graduation, full time, behind the bar and, in a year or two, in charge of it. \"You remember who ordered the spiced cider,\" she says. \"That's rarer than magic.\" Answer by the last day.\ntext [cc0b84] = 打烊后，老板娘数完钱柜，又把你也算了进去。她想在你毕业后留你全职，站吧台，过一两年就由你来管。“你记得谁点了香料苹果酒，”她说，“这比魔法还难得。”最后一天前答复。\n# en: The Gilded Newt after closing, chairs up on the tables, the landlady counting the till with a cloth over her shoulder.\nscene [8da86b] = 打烊后的镀金蝾螈酒馆，椅子倒扣在桌上，老板娘肩上搭着抹布在数钱柜。\n# en: Shake on it, in principle\nopt.left.label [20edc8] = 原则上握手成交\n# en: You shake on it over the bar. She pours you a cider on the house, which has never happened to anyone before.\nopt.left.say.1 [9dd798] = 你们隔着吧台握了手。她请你喝了一杯苹果酒，这种事以前从没发生在任何人身上。\n# en: Ask about the card games upstairs\nopt.right.label [63786c] = 问问楼上的牌局\n# en: \"You'd look the other way,\" she says, \"and so would I.\" The offer stands. So does the question of what you'd be looking away from.\nopt.right.say.1 [6a1006] = “你会装作没看见，”她说，“我也会。”提议依然有效。你得装作没看见什么，这个问题也依然在。\n\n@event career_job_pell\n# en: The Counter at Pell's\ntitle [53c9ea] = 佩尔家的柜台\n# en: Pell's daughters ask you into the back, where Old Pell is counting drops. They want to keep you on after graduation, full time, at the counter and, one day, at the drawers that nobody else may open. \"Father says you count well,\" says the eldest. Answer by the last day.\ntext [2769de] = 佩尔的女儿们把你叫到后间，老佩尔正在那里数药滴。她们想在你毕业后留你全职，守柜台，将来某一天，还要管那些别人不许打开的抽屉。“父亲说你数得好，”大女儿说。最后一天前答复。\n# en: A narrow apothecary counter, a thousand labelled drawers behind it, an old man counting drops aloud at the back.\nscene [ed663d] = 一条窄窄的药铺柜台，后面是上千个贴着标签的抽屉，一位老人在后头大声数着药滴。\n# en: Say yes, in principle\nopt.left.label [2f7cb0] = 原则上答应\n# en: Old Pell stops counting long enough to nod at you, then starts again from one. His daughters say that's the highest praise there is.\nopt.left.say.1 [2a018b] = 老佩尔停下来朝你点了点头，又从一开始数。他的女儿们说，这是最高的夸奖。\n# en: Ask about the drawers that never open\nopt.right.label [fe8db3] = 问那些从不打开的抽屉\n# en: \"In ten years,\" says the eldest, \"if you're still careful.\" You realise you'd like to still be careful in ten years.\nopt.right.say.1 [03f294] = “十年后，”大女儿说，“如果你还够仔细。”你发现，你希望十年后自己还够仔细。\n\n@event career_job_courier\n# en: A Satchel of Your Own\ntitle [6c1996] = 你自己的挎包\n# en: There's a new satchel on your peg at the loft, with your name stitched on the flap. The chief wants to keep you on after graduation, full time, flying the lake in all weathers. \"The broom still pulls left,\" she says. \"You don't.\" Answer by the last day.\ntext [a0bf81] = 阁楼里你的挂钩上挂着一个新挎包，翻盖上绣着你的名字。信使长想在你毕业后留你全职，风雨无阻地飞越湖面。“扫帚还是往左偏，”她说，“你不会。”最后一天前答复。\n# en: A courier loft at dusk, brooms racked along the wall, a satchel with a newly stitched name hanging on a peg.\nscene [fa81b4] = 黄昏时的信使阁楼，扫帚沿墙排开，一个新绣了名字的挎包挂在挂钩上。\n# en: Sling the satchel over your shoulder\nopt.left.label [41bb92] = 把挎包甩上肩\n# en: It fits. You fly a lap of the lake that evening for no reason at all, and the loft cheers you down.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a0e3c] = 正合适。那天傍晚你毫无理由地绕湖飞了一圈，阁楼里的人把你欢呼着迎了下来。\n# en: Ask about the winter routes\nopt.right.label [805575] = 问问冬天的航线\n# en: The chief shows you the map of the winter routes, with the crosses where couriers came down. There are not many. There are some.\nopt.right.say.1 [5e01e4] = 信使长给你看冬季航线图，上面标着信使坠落过的地方。不多。但有。\n\n@event career_last_call\n# en: The Last Call\ntitle [58f03c] = 最后的机会\n# en: Everyone else seems to have a letter. The registrar, who has seen this a hundred times, pins a short list beside her window: posts still open for this year's graduates. \"Some people find their work late,\" she says. \"Some people find it here. Some people find it in a fortnight's time, in their mother's kitchen. Choose.\"\ntext [30ff39] = 别人似乎都有了信。教务员这种事见过一百回了，她在窗边钉了一张短短的清单：今年毕业生还能去的职位。“有些人找到活儿找得晚，”她说，“有些人在这里找到。有些人两个星期后在自家妈妈的厨房里找到。选吧。”\n# en: The registrar's window in the entrance hall at closing time, a short typed list of posts pinned beside it, one student left in the queue.\nscene [806258] = 闭门时分入口大厅的教务窗口，旁边钉着一张打字的短清单，队伍里只剩一个学生。\n# en: Go back to where you worked\nopt.back.label [f226fd] = 回你干过的地方\n# en: The people who know your work. A friendly interview.\nopt.back.hint [1783d9] = 了解你本事的人。一场友好的面试。\n# en: You knock on a door you know. The person behind it remembers you, and remembers your work better than you do. \"We'd been wondering when you'd ask,\" they say, and write your name on a list that matters.\nopt.back.say.1 [d6f6eb] = 你敲响一扇熟悉的门。门后的人记得你，记得你的活儿比你自己还清楚。“我们还在想你什么时候会来问，”对方说，然后把你的名字写在一张要紧的名单上。\n# en: You knock on a door you know. They remember you warmly, and they've filled the post, last week, with someone who asked first. They wish you luck and mean it.\nopt.back.say.2 [8956b8] = 你敲响一扇熟悉的门。他们热情地记得你，可职位上周已经给了一个先开口的人。他们祝你好运，是真心的。\n# en: Take the registrar's list\nopt.registrar.label [dd94dc] = 接下教务员的清单\n# en: A clerk's post, here or in the village. Yours if you want it.\nopt.registrar.hint [f820c7] = 一个书记员职位，在这里或村里。你想要就是你的。\n# en: You put your name to the first post on the list: a junior clerk's desk, stamps and ledgers, a window that looks at a wall. It isn't a dream. It is a start date, and today that feels like a great deal.\nopt.registrar.say.1 [35af95] = 你在清单第一项上写下名字：一张初级书记员的桌子，印章和账簿，一扇对着墙的窗。这不是梦想。但它是一个入职日期，而今天，这就很多了。\n# en: Write to the post you really want\nopt.reach.label [803dcc] = 写信给你真正想去的地方\n# en: A long shot, in your best subject.\nopt.reach.hint [fb8b24] = 孤注一掷，凭你最好的那门本事。\n# en: You write the letter you'd been too proud to write at the fair, and send it before you can tear it up. A reply comes by the next owl: come and see us. You go. They say yes.\nopt.reach.say.1 [4d68da] = 你写下那封在招聘会上太骄傲而没写的信，趁自己还没撕掉就寄了出去。下一只猫头鹰带来回信：来见见我们吧。你去了。他们说好。\n# en: You write the letter you'd been too proud to write at the fair. The reply is kind and short, and the post went in the autumn to someone who wrote then.\nopt.reach.say.2 [d70d5f] = 你写下那封在招聘会上太骄傲而没写的信。回信客气而简短，那个职位秋天就给了当时写信的人。\n# en: Leave it until after graduation\nopt.wait.label [d45cad] = 等毕业后再说\n# en: You thank the registrar and walk away from the window. The world will still be there in a fortnight. So, she says to your back, will her list.\nopt.wait.say.1 [88f56c] = 你谢过教务员，离开了窗口。两个星期后世界还在那里。她对着你的背影说，她的清单也会在。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/cartographers.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/cartographers.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@thread carto_master_map\n# en: The Master Map\nname [d14ae0] = 总图\n# en: The Cartographers' Master Map has two great blanks nobody has filled in thirty years. Draw two lines there, and Corvin will ask you to keep the map with him.\npremise [b3906a] = 制图社的总图上有两大块空白，三十年没人填过。在那儿画下两条线，科尔文就会请你和他一起保管总图。\n# en: Corvin unrolled the Master Map and showed you its two great blanks. \"Two lines on it,\" he said, \"and you keep it with me.\"\njournal.1 [c9c73f] = 科尔文展开总图，给你看上面的两大块空白。“在上面画两条线，”他说，“你就和我一起保管它。”\n# en: You charted the stair under the last chained shelf, down to the deep stacks and the confiscation vault.\njournal.2 [e3474f] = 你在最后一排锁链书架下面画出了那段楼梯，一直通到深层书库和没收库。\n# en: You found the seventh tower's second stair, inside the wall, from the cellars to the landing of candles.\njournal.3 [2905be] = 你找到了第七塔的第二道楼梯，藏在墙里，从地窖一直通到蜡烛平台。\n# en: You rode the old book-lift down its shaft, from the bindery deep into the stacks, to where the shelves start breathing.\njournal.4 [54c278] = 你坐着旧书梯顺着竖井下去，从装订室一直深入书库，到书架开始呼吸的地方。\n# en: You climbed the seventh tower's old chimney from the cellars and came out in a classroom a floor above the landing of candles.\njournal.5 [6a0ea1] = 你从地窖爬上第七塔的旧烟囱，从蜡烛平台上面一层的一间教室里钻了出来。\n# en: Corvin put the Master Map in your hands. You keep it together now.\njournal.6 [56b6e5] = 科尔文把总图放到你手里。现在由你们俩一起保管它。\n# en: You told Corvin to keep the map himself. He looked relieved.\njournal.7 [7dc114] = 你让科尔文自己保管总图。他看起来松了一口气。\n\n@event carto_map_blanks\n# en: The Blanks on the Map\ntitle [bc61e9] = 总图上的空白\n# en: {seen(carto_map_blanks) > 1: Corvin has the Master Map out again, and the two blanks are still white as snow. \"Still thirty years,\" he says. \"Still yours if you want them.\"|Corvin has the Master Map out on the passage floor, weighted with candle stubs: silver lines that move a little, like breathing, and two great blanks. One is under the library, below the last chained shelf; one is inside the seventh tower's wall. \"The last Keeper left it to me this summer,\" he says. \"Two lines on those, and you keep it with me. Fancy it?\"}\ntext [f36fa0] = {seen(carto_map_blanks) > 1: 科尔文又把总图拿了出来，那两块空白还是白得像雪。“还是三十年，”他说。“你要的话，还是你的。”|科尔文把总图摊在通道的地上，四角压着蜡烛头：会微微移动的银线，像在呼吸，还有两大块空白。一块在图书馆底下，最后一排锁链书架的下方；一块在第七塔的墙里面。“上一任保管人今年夏天把它留给了我，”他说。“在那两块上画两条线，你就和我一起保管它。有兴趣吗？”}\n# en: A huge map in shifting silver ink spread on the stone floor of a secret passage, weighted with candle stubs, two regions of it completely blank.\nscene [6c3a33] = 秘密通道的石地上摊着一张巨大的地图，银色墨线不停变动，四角压着蜡烛头，其中两块区域完全空白。\n# en: \"Show me where to start.\"\nopt.left.label [372df6] = “告诉我从哪儿开始。”\n# en: Corvin grins and rolls the map to the library end. \"The chained shelf, then,\" he says. \"Bring chalk. Don't touch the chains.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [e13ce7] = 科尔文咧嘴一笑，把地图卷到图书馆那一端。“那就从锁链书架开始，”他说。“带上粉笔。别碰那些锁链。”\n# en: \"Not this year.\"\nopt.right.label [7485e3] = “今年不行。”\n# en: {seen(carto_map_blanks) > 1: \"One day,\" says Corvin, and rolls it up without a joke this time.|\"Thirty-one years, then,\" says Corvin, and rolls the map up a little too neatly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [13bda9] = {seen(carto_map_blanks) > 1: “总有一天，”科尔文说，这次卷起地图时没开玩笑。|“那就三十一年了，”科尔文说着把地图卷起来，卷得有点太整齐了。}\n\n@event carto_deep_stacks\n# en: The Stair Under the Chained Shelf\ntitle [3e1ddc] = 锁链书架下的楼梯\n# en: Below the east stacks' last chained shelf in the Restricted Section, by the book-return hatch, the Master Map simply stops. The chains on that shelf run down through the floor, and at night something below pulls them taut. Corvin's way in is a book-return hatch nobody has locked since it was built. The shelf is yours to chart.\ntext [1dc658] = 到了禁书区东区书库最后一排锁链书架的下方，还书口旁边，总图就断了。那排书架的锁链穿过地板往下延伸，到了夜里，下面有什么东西把它们拉得紧紧的。科尔文的进门办法就是那个还书口，自打造好就没人锁过。这排书架归你来画。\n# en: The bottom shelf of a dark library aisle, iron chains running from the books down through holes in the floorboards, a student kneeling with a stub of chalk.\nscene [3ae0b9] = 黑暗的图书馆过道里最底层的书架，铁链从书上垂下，穿过地板上的孔洞，一个学生拿着一截粉笔跪在那里。\n# en: Find the stair and pace it down\nopt.left.label [80382b] = 找到楼梯，一步步量下去\n# en: Behind the shelf's kickboard, a stair goes down forty-four steps, between shelves that breathe in their sleep, to stacks no lamp reaches. At the bottom is a door with a prefects' padlock: the confiscation vault. You chart it all, and come up very quietly.\nopt.left.say.1 [fa08d7] = 书架踢脚板后面有一道楼梯往下走了四十四级，两边的书架在睡梦中呼吸，一直通到灯光照不到的书库。最底下是一扇挂着级长挂锁的门：没收库。你把这一切都画了下来，然后非常安静地上来了。\n# en: You find the stair, and it turns more times than it has steps. You come up in the same aisle you went down from, with your chalk worn to nothing and the chains above you swinging.\nopt.left.say.2 [54fabc] = 你找到了楼梯，可它转的弯比台阶还多。你从下去的那条过道里又冒了出来，粉笔用光了，头顶的锁链还在晃。\n# en: Copy what the old margins say\nopt.right.label [8fade4] = 抄下旧图页边写的东西\n# en: The oldest map in the club has a note in the margin by the chained shelf, in brown ink: \"Stair: yes. Down: no. Ask the chains.\" You copy it out neatly. The blank stays blank.\nopt.right.say.1 [666def] = 社里最老的那张地图上，锁链书架旁的页边有一行褐色墨水写的批注：“楼梯：有。往下：别。去问锁链。”你工工整整地抄了下来。空白还是空白。\n\n@event carto_tower_stair\n# en: Two Strides Fatter\ntitle [79c577] = 胖了两步\n# en: The seventh tower's blank on the Master Map isn't its own stair, locked as it is; that's on every old plan. It's the wall. Corvin has measured the tower from outside and from in, and the outside is two strides fatter. \"There's a second stair in there,\" he says. \"A servants' stair, from the cellars up. Find its door.\"\ntext [b4ebe4] = 总图上第七塔的空白不在它自己的楼梯上，那段楼梯虽然锁着，每张旧图上都有。空白在墙里。科尔文从外面和里面各量过一次塔，外面要胖两步。“墙里还有一道楼梯，”他说。“仆人走的楼梯，从地窖往上。找到它的门。”\n# en: A student in a dark vaulted cellar pressing an ear to a curved stone wall, a candle and a coil of measuring string at their feet.\nscene [89996c] = 昏暗的拱顶地窖里，一个学生把耳朵贴在一面弧形石墙上，脚边放着一支蜡烛和一卷量绳。\n# en: Sound the cellar walls\nopt.left.label [7b7edf] = 敲遍地窖的墙\n# en: Behind a rack of empty bottles, one stone rings hollow. The door behind it opens on a stair one shoulder wide, curling up inside the wall. You follow it to a loose panel that comes out on the landing of candles, halfway up, and nobody on the main stair any the wiser.\nopt.left.say.1 [b1653b] = 在一排空酒瓶架后面，有一块石头敲起来是空的。后面的门通向一道只容一肩的楼梯，在墙里盘旋而上。你顺着它走到一块松动的嵌板前，出来就是半腰的蜡烛平台，主楼梯上的人谁也没察觉。\n# en: You knock on stone until your knuckles are raw and every wall in the cellar sounds the same. The tower keeps its two strides to itself, for now.\nopt.left.say.2 [329b96] = 你敲石头敲到指节都破了，地窖里每面墙听起来都一样。塔把它那两步暂时留给了自己。\n# en: Measure it again from outside\nopt.right.label [f9127b] = 再从外面量一次\n# en: You walk the tower's foot in the cold with Corvin's string, and get the same answer he did: two strides. Your fingers are numb and the blank is still blank, but now you believe in it.\nopt.right.say.1 [7c1a96] = 你拿着科尔文的绳子在寒风里绕塔脚走了一圈，得到和他一样的答案：两步。你的手指冻麻了，空白还是空白，但现在你相信它了。\n\n@event carto_book_lift\n# en: The Book-Lift\ntitle [a84949] = 书梯\n# en: Under the library, the bindery has an old book-lift: a wooden box on a rope in a shaft, from the days when the stacks went deeper than anyone now admits. The rope is new. Somebody still uses it.\ntext [013cec] = 图书馆底下的装订室里有一部旧书梯：竖井里一根绳子吊着一只木箱，是书库比如今人们承认的还要深的那个年代留下的。绳子是新的。还有人在用它。\n# en: A dusty wooden box on a rope inside a narrow shaft, an old library bindery around it, a student climbing into the box with a candle.\nscene [74721f] = 一间旧的图书馆装订室，狭窄的竖井里一根绳子吊着一只落满灰的木箱，一个学生举着蜡烛往箱里爬。\n# en: Ride it down\nopt.left.label [db9ed4] = 坐它下去\n# en: The box lowers you in the dark past floor after floor of shelves, and stops where the shelves start breathing, so gently it might know you. A quicker way down than any stair. You ink the shaft onto the map before you've stopped shaking.\nopt.left.say.1 [0eef63] = 木箱载着你在黑暗中经过一层又一层书架，在书架开始呼吸的地方停下，轻得好像认识你。比哪段楼梯都快的一条下去的路。你还没停止发抖，就把竖井画上了总图。\n# en: The rope slips a yard, then another, then catches. You climb out at the first floor down with skinned palms and a new respect for whoever oils it.\nopt.left.say.2 [6de146] = 绳子滑了一码，又一码，然后卡住了。你在往下第一层就爬了出来，掌心擦破了皮，对那个给绳子上油的人多了一分敬意。\n# en: Find out who oils the rope\nopt.right.label [199e38] = 打听是谁在给绳子上油\n# en: Nobody in the bindery will say. Footnote, the library cat, sits by the shaft all afternoon, as if waiting for a lift that never comes.\nopt.right.say.1 [660f61] = 装订室里没人肯说。图书馆的猫脚注在竖井边坐了一下午，好像在等一趟永远不来的书梯。\n\n@event carto_tower_chimney\n# en: Wide as a Coffin\ntitle [3715f2] = 宽得像口棺材\n# en: The old classrooms in the seventh tower each had a fireplace, and the fireplaces share one chimney, which starts in the cellar beside your wall stair. A Cartographer fifty years gone wrote in the margin beside it: \"Chimney: wide as a coffin. Climbable. Don't.\"\ntext [6a4f79] = 第七塔的旧教室各有一个壁炉，这些壁炉共用一根烟囱，烟囱就从你那道墙中楼梯旁边的地窖里起头。一个五十年前的制图社成员在旁边的页边写道：“烟囱：宽如棺材。能爬。别爬。”\n# en: A student wedged inside a narrow soot-black chimney, climbing with back and feet, a lantern hung from their belt lighting the brick above.\nscene [bd004b] = 一个学生卡在狭窄乌黑的烟囱里，背抵一边、脚撑一边往上爬，腰间挂着的提灯照亮上方的砖壁。\n# en: Climb it\nopt.left.label [494793] = 爬上去\n# en: Back against one side, feet against the other, soot to the elbows. You come out of a classroom grate a floor above the landing of candles, past the wall stair's slow part, and sit in a century of dust laughing quietly.\nopt.left.say.1 [af05ef] = 背抵一边，脚撑另一边，煤灰一直沾到手肘。你从蜡烛平台上面一层的一间教室的炉栅里钻出来，绕过了墙中楼梯最慢的那一段，坐在一个世纪的灰尘里悄悄笑了起来。\n# en: Halfway up the chimney narrows, and so do you, and for a long minute neither of you gives. You come down the way you went up, black from head to foot.\nopt.left.say.2 [122bc2] = 爬到一半烟囱变窄了，你也只好缩起来，有好一会儿谁都不肯让步。你原路下来，从头到脚一身黑。\n# en: Send a lantern up on a cord\nopt.right.label [60c97b] = 用绳子把提灯吊上去\n# en: The lantern goes up and up and comes back sooty, swinging. The margin was right about the coffin.\nopt.right.say.1 [f81041] = 提灯一直往上升，回来时晃晃悠悠，满是煤灰。页边那句关于棺材的话说得没错。\n\n@event carto_map_keeper\n# en: The Keeping of the Map\ntitle [c5cf29] = 总图的保管\n# en: The Cartographers gather round the Master Map with candles. {(flag(heist_stacks_known) and (flag(heist_tower_known) or flag(carto_book_lift))) or (flag(heist_tower_known) and flag(carto_tower_chimney)): Where the blanks were, your lines are, faintly silver and not quite settled.|The blanks are still mostly blank, but the club has watched you try. \"Close enough is a Cartographer's whole trade,\" says Corvin.} \"The club says you keep it with me. There's a test. There's always a test. Walk from here to the Thursday stair with the map in your head and no candle.\"\ntext [074531] = 制图社的人举着蜡烛围在总图旁。{(flag(heist_stacks_known) and (flag(heist_tower_known) or flag(carto_book_lift))) or (flag(heist_tower_known) and flag(carto_tower_chimney)): 原来空白的地方，现在是你画的线，微微泛着银光，还没完全定下来。|空白大多还是空白，但社里的人看着你努力过。“差不多就是制图社的全部手艺，”科尔文说。}“社里说了，由你和我一起保管它。有个考验。总有个考验。不带蜡烛，凭脑子里的地图，从这儿走到周四楼梯。”\n# en: A circle of students with candles in a secret passage, one of them holding out a rolled map in silver ink to another.\nscene [98f310] = 秘密通道里，一圈学生举着蜡烛，其中一个把一卷银墨地图递给另一个。\n# en: Walk it in the dark\nopt.left.label [e58a75] = 摸黑走过去\n# en: You walk it blind, every turning where the map says it is, and at the foot of the Thursday stair the club is waiting with candles. Corvin puts the rolled map in your hands. \"Keepers,\" he says, and for once he doesn't laugh after.\nopt.left.say.1 [9b06b1] = 你闭着眼走完了全程，每个拐角都在地图说的地方，在周四楼梯脚下，社里的人举着蜡烛等着你。科尔文把卷好的地图放到你手里。“两位保管人，”他说，这一次他说完没有笑。\n# en: Three turnings in, the castle moves one. You walk into a wall, very gently, in the dark, and stand there until someone comes with a candle. \"It wants more of you,\" says Corvin kindly. \"Come back.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [4960f9] = 走到第三个拐角，城堡挪动了一个。你在黑暗里非常轻地撞上了一堵墙，站在那儿直到有人拿着蜡烛过来。“它还想要你多付出一点，”科尔文温和地说。“再来。”\n# en: \"It's yours, Corvin. Keep it.\"\nopt.right.label [a70c61] = “它是你的，科尔文。你来保管。”\n# en: Corvin looks at you for a long moment, then rolls the map back up and tucks it into his coat, where it has lived since the summer. He looks relieved, and then embarrassed about looking relieved.\nopt.right.say.1 [7bc74f] = 科尔文看了你很久，然后把地图卷回去，塞进外套里，它从夏天起就一直待在那儿。他看起来松了一口气，接着又为自己松了一口气而难为情。\n\n@event carto_forged_map\n# en: A Map for Sale\ntitle [5ea074] = 出售地图\n# en: A first-year is selling \"the secret map of the castle\" in the corridor for a crown a copy. It is a forgery, and a good one, and every passage on it leads, by a lovely roundabout way, straight to the prefects' office. Corvin swears it wasn't him.\ntext [c5235f] = 一个一年级生在走廊里卖“城堡秘密地图”，一个克朗一份。那是伪造的，还造得很好，上面每一条通道都绕着一条可爱的弯路，直通级长办公室。科尔文发誓不是他干的。\n# en: A first-year in a corridor selling folded paper maps from a satchel to a small crowd of younger students.\nscene [835972] = 走廊里，一个一年级生从书包里拿出折好的纸地图，卖给一小群更小的学生。\n# en: Let it sell\nopt.left.label [bc5a41] = 让它卖下去\n# en: You get a copy for the club's wall. All week first-years turn up, breathless, at the prefects' door. The prefects are delighted. Nobody looks behind the third gargoyle at all.\nopt.left.say.1 [eca176] = 你弄到一份挂在社里的墙上。整整一个星期，一年级生们气喘吁吁地出现在级长办公室门口。级长们高兴极了。根本没人去看第三座石像鬼后面。\n# en: Buy up the stock and burn it\nopt.right.label [6df54a] = 把货全买下来烧掉\n# en: You buy the whole satchel and burn it in the passage grate. The first-year is thrilled with the money. Somewhere, the forger is very annoyed.\nopt.right.say.1 [bb2fcb] = 你买下了整书包，在通道的炉栅里烧了。那个一年级生拿到钱高兴得不得了。在某个地方，那个伪造者非常恼火。\n\n@event carto_quiet_prefect\n# en: The Prefect Who Doesn't Look\ntitle [114054] = 不看的级长\n# en: A senior prefect walks the north parapet on her rounds and never once looks at the third gargoyle. This time she stops beside it, still not looking. \"The draught behind this one's got worse,\" she says to the dark. \"Tell whoever needs telling. And somebody's been eating the grapes in the dish. Those were mine.\"\ntext [d57789] = 有位高年级级长巡逻时总要走北边的女儿墙，却从来不看第三座石像鬼一眼。这一次她在它旁边停下，还是不看它。“这一座后面的穿堂风更大了，”她对着黑暗说。“告诉该知道的人。还有，有人在吃盘子里的葡萄。那是我的。”\n# en: A senior prefect with a lantern on a windy parapet at night, standing beside a gargoyle and not looking at it, talking to a student in the shadows.\nscene [f2cf13] = 夜里刮风的女儿墙上，一位提着灯的高年级级长站在一座石像鬼旁却不看它，对暗处的一个学生说着话。\n# en: Trade a shortcut for her rota\nopt.left.label [28d384] = 用一条近路换她的值班表\n# en: You give her the back way from the parapet to the prefects' common room, four minutes quicker. She gives you, without a word, the times of every round on her rota. Corvin, when he hears, says giving routes away is how clubs end.\nopt.left.say.1 [3e5707] = 你把从女儿墙到级长休息室的后路告诉了她，能快四分钟。她一言不发，把她值班表上每一轮巡逻的时间都给了你。科尔文听说后说，把路线送人，社团就是这么完蛋的。\n# en: Play dumb\nopt.right.label [98ad99] = 装傻\n# en: \"Draught?\" you say. \"What gargoyle?\" She almost smiles, and walks on, swinging the lantern. You keep the club's secrets, and she keeps hers.\nopt.right.say.1 [d78563] = “穿堂风？”你说。“什么石像鬼？”她差点笑了，晃着灯走开了。你守着社里的秘密，她守着她的。\n\n@event carto_member_out\n# en: One Map Fewer\ntitle [91f250] = 少一张地图\n# en: One of the club's seniors has been caught twice this term, and wants out before a third. The club's rule is that leavers burn their maps. She has a year of them in a bundle, and she asks you to sit with her while she does it. \"Or hide them,\" she says. \"I can't watch them go.\"\ntext [a954d3] = 社里一位高年级生这学期已经被抓了两次，想在第三次之前退出。社里的规矩是，退出的人要烧掉自己的地图。她攒了一年的地图捆成一捆，请你陪她一起烧。“或者藏起来，”她说。“我看不得它们烧掉。”\n# en: Two students kneeling by a small grate in a stone passage, a stack of hand-drawn maps between them, one holding a lit match.\nscene [97e332] = 石头通道里，两个学生跪在一个小炉栅旁，中间放着一叠手绘地图，其中一个举着一根点着的火柴。\n# en: Burn them with her\nopt.left.label [a80d45] = 陪她一起烧\n# en: You feed the grate one map at a time and she reads each one aloud before it goes. At the end she thanks you, and goes up to bed a member of nothing, and lighter.\nopt.left.say.1 [f56653] = 你一张一张地把地图喂进炉栅，每一张烧掉之前她都大声念一遍。最后她向你道谢，上楼睡觉去了，不再属于任何社团，也轻松了。\n# en: Keep them for the club\nopt.right.label [1c2cba] = 替社里留着\n# en: You hide the bundle in the passage where only the club will find it, which means carrying a caught-twice member's maps through the castle after curfew. She doesn't ask where. She'll know, every time she passes the gargoyle, that she didn't really leave.\nopt.right.say.1 [850c61] = 你把那捆地图藏在通道里只有社里的人才找得到的地方，这意味着要在宵禁后带着一个被抓过两次的社员的地图穿过城堡。她没问在哪儿。每次经过那座石像鬼，她都会知道，自己并没有真正离开。\n\n@event carto_recruit\n# en: Your Turn to Invite\ntitle [69d65f] = 轮到你邀请\n# en: Corvin hands you a black card, blank but for a silver line that wanders. \"Members bring members,\" he says. \"Your turn. Who?\" You think of {pick} before he's finished asking.\ntext [43c69a] = 科尔文递给你一张黑卡片，上面空空的，只有一道蜿蜒的银线。“社员带社员，”他说。“轮到你了。请谁？”他还没问完，你就想到了{pick}。\n# en: A black card with a silver map drawn on it being slid under a dormitory door in a dark corridor.\nscene [ae6848] = 黑暗的走廊里，一张画着银色地图的黑卡片正被塞进宿舍的门缝。\n# en: Slip it under {pick}'s door\nopt.left.label [083279] = 把它塞进{pick}的门缝\n# en: You slide the card under {pick}'s door at midnight and run. Three nights later {pick} comes in under the gargoyle with a candle and a look you'll remember: someone finding out the castle is bigger than they thought.\nopt.left.say.1 [5dc605] = 午夜你把卡片塞进{pick}的门缝就跑了。三个晚上之后，{pick}举着蜡烛钻到石像鬼底下，脸上的神情你会一直记得：一个人发现城堡比自己以为的要大。\n# en: \"Nobody I'd trust with it.\"\nopt.right.label [3e88a5] = “没有我信得过的人。”\n# en: Corvin takes the card back. \"Careful's a good thing in a Cartographer,\" he says, and doesn't sound as if he means it.\nopt.right.say.1 [fb1948] = 科尔文把卡片收了回去。“小心对制图社的人来说是好事，”他说，听上去并不是真心的。\n\n@event gargoyle_midnight_one_short\n# en: One Short\ntitle [86f07d] = 少了一个\n# en: Midnight under the gargoyle, and the headcount comes up one short. {~Someone went into the passages alone an hour ago, to settle a bet about a stair that only goes down on wet nights.|Someone took a dare about the kitchens' dumbwaiter, and the dare was \"alone\".|A new member, it turns out, sleepwalks, and nodded off on the bench an hour ago, and the bench is empty.} Nobody says anything for a moment.\ntext [15b2bf] = 午夜，石像鬼底下点人数，少了一个。{~有人一个钟头前独自进了通道，为了一个赌：有段楼梯只在下雨的夜里往下通。|有人接了个关于厨房送菜升降机的赌，赌注是“一个人去”。|原来有个新社员会梦游，一个钟头前在长凳上打起了盹，现在长凳空了。}一时间谁都没说话。\n# en: A huddle of students with candles in a dark stone passage, one of them counting heads, an empty gap in the circle.\nscene [86d231] = 黑暗的石头通道里，一群学生举着蜡烛挤在一起，其中一个在点人数，圈子里空着一个位置。\n# en: Go after them\nopt.left.label [8fffa5] = 去找他们\n# en: {~You find them two floors down, on a step that wasn't there yesterday and can't now be got off. You talk them down one stride at a time.|You find them stuck in the dumbwaiter between two floors, very glad of company. You wind them down by the rope, slowly, and walk them back.|You find them on the parapet, asleep on their feet and walking the edge. You turn them round by the shoulders, gently, and walk them home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1f0f54] = {~你在往下两层的地方找到了那个人，坐在一级昨天还不存在、现在又下不来的台阶上。你一步一步把那人劝了下来。|你在两层楼之间的送菜升降机里找到了那个人，卡在那儿，有人作伴高兴得不得了。你拉着绳子慢慢把那人放下来，送了回去。|你在女儿墙上找到了那个人，站着睡着了，沿着墙边走。你轻轻扶着那人的肩膀让人转过身来，送回了家。}\n# en: {~The passages turn you round twice, and a prefect's lantern turns you round a third time. The missing member gets back on their own at dawn, grinning.|You never find the dumbwaiter's shaft, and a prefect nearly finds you. They come back at dawn, floury and triumphant.|You search every passage but the right one. At dawn they wake in their own bed, with no idea anyone looked.}\nopt.left.say.2 [00da66] = {~通道把你转了两圈，一位级长的提灯又把你转了第三圈。失踪的那位天亮时自己回来了，笑嘻嘻的。|你始终没找到升降机的竖井，倒差点被一位级长找到。那人天亮时回来了，满身面粉，得意洋洋。|你把每条通道都找遍了，就是没找对那一条。天亮时那人在自己床上醒来，完全不知道有人找过自己。}\n# en: Wait with the others\nopt.right.label [8454ee] = 和其他人一起等\n# en: {~You sit in the circle with the gap in it, and at dawn they climb back out of the wall, filthy and triumphant. The waiting was worse than going.|At dawn they come back through the wall, floury to the elbows, with a story nobody believes. The waiting was worse than going.|At dawn somebody finds them asleep in the library. The waiting was worse than going.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1e4631] = {~你坐在那个缺了一个人的圈子里，天亮时那人从墙里爬出来，满身脏污，得意洋洋。等待比自己去找还难熬。|天亮时那人从墙里回来，面粉一直沾到手肘，讲了一个没人相信的故事。等待比自己去找还难熬。|天亮时有人发现那人睡在图书馆里。等待比自己去找还难熬。}\n\n@event gargoyle_midnight_lost_map\n# en: A Map on the Wrong Table\ntitle [48c545] = 放错桌子的地图\n# en: {~A second-year Cartographer dropped their copy of the kitchens' passages, and it's turned up on a table in the prefects' common room.|One of the club's old route maps, the one to the boathouse, is lying on the prefects' study desk, where a cleaner left it.|A first-year member lent their map to a friend, and the friend lost it to a prefect's coat pocket, and the coat is hanging in the prefects' common room.} Nobody has opened it yet. It's still there at midnight.\ntext [10dcc4] = {~一个二年级的制图社成员弄丢了自己那份厨房通道图，它出现在级长休息室的一张桌子上。|社里一张通往船屋的旧路线图躺在级长自习室的书桌上，是清洁工留在那儿的。|一个一年级社员把地图借给了朋友，朋友又把它弄丢在一位级长的外套口袋里，而那件外套就挂在级长休息室里。}还没人打开过它。到了午夜它还在那儿。\n# en: A dark common room with red sashes hung over chairs, a folded hand-drawn map lying on a table in a pool of moonlight.\nscene [0feb4c] = 昏暗的休息室里，椅背上搭着红肩带，一张折好的手绘地图躺在桌上的一片月光里。\n# en: Steal it back\nopt.left.label [bf3560] = 把它偷回来\n# en: {~In through the door, across the moonlit rug, map into your shirt, out. A prefect turns over in an armchair by the fire and doesn't wake.|You lift it off the desk between two snores. The club has it back by one o'clock.|You go through the coat's pockets in the dark, find the map and a toffee, and take only the map.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c30bcc] = {~进门，穿过月光下的地毯，地图塞进衬衫，出来。炉火边扶手椅里的一位级长翻了个身，没醒。|你在两声鼾声之间把它从桌上拿走了。一点钟时社里已经把它拿回来了。|你在黑暗中摸遍外套的口袋，找到了地图和一块太妃糖，只拿了地图。}\n# en: {~You get the map, and a prefect gets a very good look at you holding it. You run. The map is safe; you aren't, quite.|A floorboard by the desk squeaks. You get out with the map and a prefect's voice behind you, asking your name.|The coat's owner comes back for it while your hand's in the pocket. You run, map and all, and they know your face.}\nopt.left.say.2 [6bfe8b] = {~你拿到了地图，一位级长也把你拿着地图的样子看得清清楚楚。你跑了。地图安全了；你不太安全。|书桌旁的一块地板吱呀一响。你带着地图逃出来，身后一位级长在问你的名字。|你的手还在口袋里，外套的主人回来取它了。你连地图带人跑了，他们认得你的脸。}\n# en: Redraw the routes instead\nopt.right.label [2ded4f] = 改掉那些路线\n# en: {~The club spends the night changing every route to the kitchens: new chalk, new turnings, a passage given up for good.|The club gives up the old boathouse route for good, and spends the night finding a worse one.|The club spends the night redrawing everything the lost map showed. By dawn it's worth nothing to anyone.}\nopt.right.say.1 [33e4a6] = {~社里花了一整夜，把通往厨房的每一条路线都改了：新的粉笔记号，新的拐角，一条通道就此永远放弃。|社里永远放弃了那条去船屋的老路线，花了一整夜找到一条更差的。|社里花了一整夜，把丢失的地图上画的一切都重画了。到天亮，它对谁都一文不值。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_hollowstar_acts.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_hollowstar_acts.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event hs_lake_breathes\n# en: The Lake Breathes\ntitle [6ab0a7] = 湖在呼吸\n# en: At supper the lake outside the tall windows goes white in one breath, and every candle in the Great Hall leans toward it. Far under the water a bell tolls. Professor Thorne's spoon freezes into his soup. The headmistress rises. \"A cold current,\" she says pleasantly. \"Eat.\" Then the lake breathes out, and is water again. Nobody eats.\ntext [631628] = 晚餐时，高窗外的湖在一口气间变白，大礼堂里的每一支蜡烛都朝它倾斜。湖水深处，一口钟响了。索恩教授的勺子冻在了汤里。院长站了起来。“一股冷流而已，”她和颜悦色地说，“吃饭。”然后湖呼出那口气，又变回了水。没有人吃得下。\n# en: The Great Hall at supper, every head turned to the tall windows, the lake beyond them gone white with sudden frost.\nscene [96af25] = 晚餐时的大礼堂，所有人都转头望向高窗，窗外的湖面骤然覆上一层白霜。\n# en: Run down to the shore\nopt.left.label [3bc011] = 跑到湖边去\n# en: The shore is soaked and freezing, and the reeds are white to the root. Old Maud is already there with a lantern, staring at the water. \"Last time it did that, I lost a friend,\" she says, not quite to you. Then she sends you to bed, sharply, and you go.\nopt.left.say.1 [88d600] = 湖岸又湿又冷，芦苇从根到梢都白了。老莫德已经提着灯在那里了，盯着湖水。“上一次它这样，我失去了一个朋友。”她说，不太像是在对你说。然后她厉声叫你回去睡觉，你就回去了。\n# en: Follow Thorne out of the hall\nopt.right.label [a49e19] = 跟着索恩离开大礼堂\n# en: Thorne leaves his frozen soup and walks straight to the library archive, fast for a man of his age. You hear the vault door slam. You lie awake wondering what he went down there to read.\nopt.right.say.1 [5176f6] = 索恩撇下冻住的汤，径直走向图书馆档案室，以他的年纪走得飞快。你听见金库的门砰地关上。你躺着睡不着，琢磨他到底下去读什么。\n\n@event hs_silver_rings\n# en: Silver at Supper\ntitle [65d7d8] = 晚餐桌上的银戒\n# en: At supper you notice three identical silver rings at the high table: on the headmistress, on Professor Thorne and on Old Maud's earthy hand. After curfew, from your window, you see all three walking the lakeshore with lanterns, stopping at the foot of each tower in turn.\ntext [0e82e1] = 晚餐时，你注意到主宾席上有三枚一模一样的银戒指：一枚戴在院长手上，一枚在索恩教授手上，还有一枚在老莫德那双沾着泥土的手上。宵禁之后，你从窗口望见他们三人提着灯笼沿湖岸走，在每座塔脚下依次停步。\n# en: The Great Hall at supper, candlelight catching three identical silver rings on three very different hands at the high table.\nscene [7eebf1] = 晚餐时的大礼堂，烛光照亮主宾席上三只截然不同的手上三枚一模一样的银戒指。\n# en: Follow them down\nopt.left.label [088fbe] = 跟下去看看\n# en: You shadow them from tower to tower. At each one they lay a palm on the stone and wait. At the last, Maud takes her hand away and blows on her fingers as if the wall were ice.\nopt.left.say.1 [ec6d76] = 你从一座塔跟到另一座塔。每到一处，他们都把手掌贴在石墙上，静静等着。到了最后一座，莫德把手收回来，对着指尖呵气，仿佛那面墙是冰做的。\n# en: A lantern swings toward you. \"Hm,\" says Professor Thorne. \"Bed.\" He doesn't report you, which somehow feels worse.\nopt.left.say.2 [c0b820] = 一盏灯笼朝你转过来。“嗯。”索恩教授说，“睡觉去。”他没有上报你，不知为何这反而更难受。\n# en: Draw the curtains\nopt.right.label [899463] = 拉上窗帘\n# en: You sleep well, mostly. In the morning the rings are still there at breakfast, and nobody at the high table looks as if they slept at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [b6139e] = 你睡得还算不错。第二天早餐时，那几枚戒指还在，而主宾席上没有一个人看起来睡过觉。\n\n@event hs_seraphine_towers\n# en: Seven, Then Six\ntitle [257255] = 七座，然后六座\n# en: Seraphine is on the observatory balcony, counting towers on her fingers. \"Seven,\" she says. \"Seven. Six, and then… no. Seven, for now.\" She looks at you as if you had arrived a little early. \"What month is it? I lose track around the… you know.\"\ntext [a96e33] = 瑟拉芬站在观星台的阳台上，掰着手指数塔。“七座，”她说，“七座。六座，然后……不。七座，暂时。”她看着你，仿佛你来得稍微早了一点。“现在是几月？我总是在……你知道的那个地方，弄丢日子。”\n# en: A seer on the observatory balcony at night, counting the academy's towers on her fingers, one finger held back.\nscene [34ef65] = 夜里观星台的阳台上，一位预言者掰着手指数学院的塔，有一根手指迟迟没有伸出来。\n# en: \"Which one goes out?\"\nopt.left.label [a243d5] = “哪一座会熄灭？”\n# en: She points, then frowns at her own hand. \"That one. Or the one next to it. Next year, I think. Not the seventh; nobody's lit the seventh for a hundred years.\" She doesn't remember saying it the next morning, but you do.\nopt.left.say.1 [f52252] = 她指了一下，又对着自己的手皱起眉。“那一座。或者旁边那座。明年吧，我想。不是第七座；第七座已经一百年没人点灯了。”第二天早上她已经不记得说过这话，但你记得。\n# en: \"It's {month}. Evening.\"\nopt.right.label [1c7b30] = “现在是{month}，傍晚。”\n# en: She lets out a long breath and sits down on the cold stone. \"Thank you. I was halfway into a winter.\" You stay until she is sure which one she's in.\nopt.right.say.1 [1f863d] = 她长长地呼出一口气，在冰凉的石头上坐了下来。“谢谢。我已经半只脚踏进冬天了。”你一直陪着她，直到她确定自己身在哪个季节。\n\n@event hs_ilse_arrives\n# en: A Storm at the Gate\ntitle [d881d0] = 门前的风暴\n# en: At noon a snowstorm walks in through the academy gate, with a girl in white furs at the middle of it. Kael drops his books. \"Go home, Ilse.\" \"Make me.\" They shout at each other across the courtyard in Northern, and the fountain between them cracks in half. Half the school is watching. Then she turns on her heel and goes, down the road to the village.\ntext [eec352] = 正午，一场暴风雪从学院大门走了进来，正中间是一个穿白裘的女孩。凯尔的书掉在了地上。“回家去，伊尔泽。”“有本事你让我回。”他们隔着庭院用北境话对吼，两人之间的喷泉裂成了两半。半个学校都在看。然后她一转身走了，沿着大路往村子去。\n# en: A snowstorm falling on one girl in white furs in the middle of a sunny courtyard, a pale young man facing her, students scattering, a cracked fountain between them.\nscene [c05a43] = 阳光明媚的庭院正中，一场暴风雪只落在一个穿白裘的女孩身上，一个苍白的年轻人面对着她，学生们四散奔逃，两人之间是一座裂开的喷泉。\n# en: Go after her\nopt.left.label [7ddfb6] = 追上她\n# en: Kael catches your sleeve. \"Don't. She's here for the door, not for me.\" He lets go when you don't stop. By the time you reach the road the snow has stopped too, and there's only a line of frozen footprints toward the bakery.\nopt.left.say.1 [6976ac] = 凯尔拽住你的袖子。“别去。她是为那扇门来的，不是为我。”你没停下，他就松了手。等你走到大路上，雪也停了，只剩一行冻住的脚印通向面包房。\n# en: Stay with Kael\nopt.right.label [2f5601] = 留在凯尔身边\n# en: You pick up his books. He stands looking at the broken fountain until the bell goes. \"My sister,\" he says at last. \"She's taken a room above the bakery. She isn't here to visit.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [059ef4] = 你帮他捡起书。他一直站着看那座裂开的喷泉，直到上课铃响。“我妹妹，”他终于开口，“她在面包房楼上租了间屋子。她不是来探亲的。”\n\n@event hs_frost_ring\n# en: Things Come Ashore\ntitle [1186a4] = 它们上岸了\n# en: {season == \"winter\": A still, starless night|A warm night}, and a ring of frost as wide as the Great Hall has formed in the middle of the lake. Far below, the bell tolls, and tolls. Then things climb out of the ring onto the shore: shapes of frost and whispers, walking like people who have forgotten how. The headmistress stands alone at the waterline with a lantern, talking to the lake.\ntext [bc66df] = {season == \"winter\": 一个寂静无星的夜晚|一个温暖的夜晚}，湖中央出现了一个和大礼堂一样宽的霜环。深处的钟响了，一下又一下。接着有东西从霜环里爬上了岸：由霜与低语构成的形体，走路的样子像是忘了该怎么走路的人。院长独自提着灯站在水边，对着湖说话。\n# en: The Mirror Lake on a warm night, a perfect white ring of frost on the water, figures of frost and whispers climbing onto the shore, a tall woman with a lantern facing them.\nscene [e742bc] = 温暖夜晚的镜湖，水面上一个完美的白色霜环，由霜与低语构成的身影爬上岸，一个提灯的高个子女人面对着它们。\n# en: Meet them on the shore\nopt.fight.label [67179b] = 在岸边迎击它们\n# en: Combat. Kael fights beside you, if he's your ally.\nopt.fight.hint [5999d9] = 战斗。如果凯尔是你的盟友，他会和你并肩作战。\n# en: Kael's frost and your spells meet the first of them on the shingle, and it comes apart into snow and a sound like someone saying your name. The rest turn back to the water. Under the ice, as it closes, you hear the bell's note clearly for the first time.\nopt.fight.say.1 [bd5e29] = 凯尔的霜和你的咒语在卵石滩上迎上了第一个，它散成了雪，还有一个像是有人在念你名字的声音。其余的转身回到水里。冰面合拢时，你第一次清楚地听见了那口钟的音。\n# en: It walks through your spell as if it were weather and puts a cold hand on your chest, and you can't breathe for a long moment. Kael drags you back up the shingle. When you can see again, the ring has closed and the shapes are gone.\nopt.fight.say.2 [2a6599] = 它像穿过天气一样穿过你的咒语，把一只冰冷的手按在你胸口，你好一阵喘不过气。凯尔把你拖回卵石滩上。等你能看清时，霜环已经合拢，那些形体也不见了。\n# en: Your spell meets the first of them on the shingle, and it comes apart into snow and a sound like someone saying your name. The rest turn back to the water. Under the ice, as it closes, you hear the bell's note clearly for the first time.\nopt.fight.say.3 [7285db] = 你的咒语在卵石滩上迎上了第一个，它散成了雪，还有一个像是有人在念你名字的声音。其余的转身回到水里。冰面合拢时，你第一次清楚地听见了那口钟的音。\n# en: It walks through your spell as if it were weather and puts a cold hand on your chest, and you can't breathe for a long moment. When you can see again, the ring has closed, the shapes are gone, and the headmistress is helping you up.\nopt.fight.say.4 [5386b7] = 它像穿过天气一样穿过你的咒语，把一只冰冷的手按在你胸口，你好一阵喘不过气。等你能看清时，霜环已经合拢，那些形体也不见了，院长正扶你起来。\n# en: Watch what she does\nopt.watch.label [29f82d] = 看她要做什么\n# en: Guile. She doesn't like an audience.\nopt.watch.hint [592c3d] = 诡计。她不喜欢有人围观。\n# en: She kneels and speaks to the water, slow and patient, the way you'd speak to a sleeping animal. The shapes stop on the shingle and listen. The ring shrinks, cracks, and is gone, and the bell under it rings one long note you'll know again anywhere.\nopt.watch.say.1 [57fb5e] = 她跪下来对着湖水说话，缓慢而耐心，就像对一头熟睡的野兽说话。那些形体在卵石滩上停下来倾听。霜环缩小、碎裂、消失，下面的钟敲出一个长音，此后你在哪里都认得出它。\n# en: \"Good evening,\" she says, without turning round, as a shape of frost walks past her into the lake. \"You are the third student this week. The other two asked questions.\" She tells you nothing, and remembers your name.\nopt.watch.say.2 [92f8ce] = “晚上好。”她头也不回地说，一个霜之形体从她身边走进湖里。“这周你是第三个来的学生。另外两个问了问题。”她什么也没告诉你，但记住了你的名字。\n# en: Get the first-years inside\nopt.help.label [b5bef7] = 把一年级生带进屋\n# en: Nobody will see what happens at the water.\nopt.help.hint [c86325] = 没人会看见水边发生了什么。\n# en: You run the length of the first-year tower banging on doors, and get forty children into the common room and the shutters closed. Something taps on the glass all night, very politely. In the morning there are wet footprints on the stairs, going up.\nopt.help.say.1 [e756c0] = 你沿着一年级塔一路砸门，把四十个孩子赶进公共休息室，关上了百叶窗。整夜都有什么东西非常有礼貌地敲着玻璃。早上，楼梯上有湿脚印，一路往上。\n\n@event hs_dark_tower\n# en: The Tower That Went Dark\ntitle [eb9204] = 熄灭的塔\n# en: Mid-lecture, every lamp in the Lecture Tower goes out at once, and none of them will light again. In the morning there's a black star burned into the door of the Great Hall, still smoking, and half the lake has frozen {season == \"winter\": black and glassy, harder than any winter ice|weeks before any frost}, in a sheet that ends in a clean edge, as if cut. Out on it, at dawn, someone in black furs is walking away.\ntext [4af36c] = 课上到一半，讲堂塔里的每一盏灯同时熄灭，再也点不亮了。第二天早上，大礼堂的门上烙着一颗黑星，还在冒烟；湖已冻住了一半，{season == \"winter\": 冻得乌黑如玻璃，比任何冬天的冰都硬|比任何霜冻都早了好几周}，冰面边缘齐整，像是被切出来的。黎明时，冰上有个穿黑裘的人正在走远。\n# en: An academy tower standing black against the night sky among its lit neighbours, a black star burned into a great wooden door, and below them a lake half-covered in ice.\nscene [d4a9f6] = 一座学院塔楼漆黑地矗立在夜空下，周围的塔都亮着灯，一扇巨大的木门上烙着一颗黑星，下方的湖一半盖着冰。\n# en: Go after the figure on the ice\nopt.left.label [3ab56b] = 追冰上那个人影\n# en: Nerve. The ice is thinner than it looks.\nopt.left.hint [25bd25] = 胆量。冰比看上去薄。\n# en: You follow the footprints to the middle of the ice, where they simply stop. Under your boots the ice is clear as glass. Far down, something round and dark sits on the lakebed, and a glow runs along its rim. Then the glow turns, slowly, as if to look up.\nopt.left.say.1 [01453c] = 你跟着脚印走到冰面中央，脚印就在那里断了。你脚下的冰像玻璃一样清澈。深处，湖底卧着一个又圆又黑的东西，边缘流动着一圈光。然后那圈光慢慢转动，像是要抬头看。\n# en: The ice groans and a crack races past your feet. You scramble back to the shore soaked to the knee, and a prefect writes your name down very slowly. The figure in black furs never once looks back.\nopt.left.say.2 [a926ea] = 冰面呻吟着，一道裂缝从你脚边飞窜而过。你连滚带爬回到岸上，膝盖以下都湿透了，一个级长慢吞吞地记下了你的名字。那个穿黑裘的人影一次也没有回头。\n# en: Carry lamps for the prefects\nopt.right.label [6083a9] = 帮级长们搬灯\n# en: You spend the night carrying lamps up the Lecture Tower stairs. Every one goes out at the doorway. The first-years have started sleeping in their clothes. From her window across the courtyard, the headmistress watches you try.\nopt.right.say.1 [66a8f5] = 你整夜提着灯爬讲堂塔的楼梯。每一盏灯一到门口就灭。一年级生已经开始和衣而睡了。院长在庭院对面的窗口看着你一次次尝试。\n\n@event hs_maud_locks\n# en: Maud Has Seen Ice Before\ntitle [b78fd5] = 莫德见过这种冰\n# en: At Maud's gate the headmistress is saying, quite pleasantly, \"Keep the young ones out of it, Maud.\" \"No,\" says Maud, \"and you know why.\" The headmistress walks away very straight. Maud pushes a mug of tea at you. \"{season == \"winter\": It doesn't freeze like that, dearie, not even in winter|The lake doesn't freeze this time of year, dearie}. Sixty years ago it did, and three of us went down. Sit. Or ask.\"\ntext [84a878] = 莫德的院门口，院长正和气地说：“别让年轻人掺和进来，莫德。”“不，”莫德说，“你知道为什么。”院长走开了，背挺得笔直。莫德把一杯茶推给你。“{season == \"winter\": 它不会那样结冰的，亲爱的，冬天也不会|一年里的这个时候，湖是不会结冰的，亲爱的}。六十年前它结过一次，我们三个下去了。坐下吧。要么就问。”\n# en: An old woman wrapped in a shawl on her cottage step, two mugs steaming beside her, a tall woman in dark robes walking stiffly away down the path, a half-frozen lake beyond.\nscene [d4760c] = 一个裹着披肩的老妇人坐在小屋门阶上，身边两杯热气腾腾的茶，一个穿深色长袍的高个子女人僵硬地沿着小路走远，远处是半冻的湖。\n# en: Sit with her\nopt.left.label [654e0a] = 陪她坐着\n# en: You drink your tea and watch the ice. She hums something old. When the mugs are empty she squeezes your hand. \"That was the right one,\" she says, and you'll only understand why much later.\nopt.left.say.1 [7b5291] = 你喝着茶，看着冰面。她哼着一支老调子。杯子空了，她捏了捏你的手。“选对了。”她说。你要过很久才明白为什么。\n# en: Ask what's holding it shut\nopt.right.label [8d8004] = 问是什么把门锁住\n# en: \"Seven towers, seven locks, one in each,\" she says. \"When a tower goes dark, so does its lock.\" She looks at the Lecture Tower. \"Locks can be mended. They need hands, and they need a key, and she knows it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [bcb6f3] = “七座塔，七把锁，每座一把，”她说，“一座塔黑了，它的锁也就哑了。”她看着讲堂塔。“锁是能修的。要有手，还要有钥匙，这她清楚。”\n\n@event hs_two_shadows\n# en: Two Wands on the Ice\ntitle [412c93] = 冰上的两根魔杖\n# en: At dusk the headmistress and Kael walk out onto the frozen half of the lake from opposite shores and meet in the middle. Kael's voice carries all the way to the towers: \"You'll seal it with students standing in the door!\" Hers never rises, and carries anyway: \"Better than your mother's way.\" Both wands come up. For a long moment nobody moves. Then she lowers hers first, and they part in silence.\ntext [dae66a] = 黄昏时，院长和凯尔从两岸走上湖面结冰的那一半，在中央相遇。凯尔的声音一直传到塔楼：“你要把门封上，连站在门口的学生一起！”她的声音从不提高，却也传了过来：“总比你母亲的办法好。”两根魔杖都举了起来。很长一段时间谁也没动。然后她先放下了魔杖，两人沉默着分开了。\n# en: Dusk on a half-frozen lake, two figures facing each other in the middle of the ice with wands raised, one holding a lantern, the dark tower behind them.\nscene [08771e] = 黄昏时半冻的湖，两个身影在冰面中央举着魔杖对峙，其中一人提着灯，身后是那座黑塔。\n# en: Ask Kael what they said\nopt.left.label [91a8f0] = 问凯尔他们说了什么\n# en: \"She wants the door shut her way. I want it shut mine.\" He stirs his tea until it freezes. \"She asked about you. So did I. Don't make that face. You'll be asked.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [3a12d8] = “她想按她的办法关上那扇门。我想按我的。”他搅着茶，直到茶冻住。“她问起了你。我也问了。别做那种表情。会有人来问你的。”\n# en: Ask the headmistress\nopt.right.label [733de3] = 去问校长\n# en: \"We disagree about method,\" she says, \"and agree about the door.\" She studies you as if you were a chess problem. \"Expect an invitation. Possibly two. Choose with your eyes open.\" Kael, across the hall, sees you leave her tower.\nopt.right.say.1 [7d491d] = “我们在方法上有分歧，”她说，“在门的问题上意见一致。”她打量着你，像在研究一道棋局。“等着收邀请吧。可能是两份。睁大眼睛再选。”大厅另一头，凯尔看见你从她的塔里出来。\n\n@event hs_choosing_sides\n# en: Two Invitations\ntitle [b97cfd] = 两份邀请\n# en: Two invitations, both in front of the whole refectory. The headmistress, passing your table: \"My study, after supper. Come alone. There will be chess.\" And Kael, not ten seconds later, loud enough for her to hear: \"She wants you in her circle. I know the door from the other side. Come with us.\" Every head in the hall turns to you.\ntext [10ee8c] = 两份邀请，都当着整个膳堂的面。院长经过你的桌边：“晚饭后来我书房。一个人来。会有棋下。”不到十秒，凯尔就开口了，声音大得足够让她听见：“她想让你进她的圈子。我从门的另一边认识那扇门。跟我们走吧。”整个大厅的人都转过头来看你。\n# en: A crowded refectory gone quiet, a student at a table between a tall woman in dark robes and a pale young man, both waiting for an answer.\nscene [d5678c] = 拥挤的膳堂安静下来，一个学生坐在桌边，一边是穿深色长袍的高个子女人，一边是苍白的年轻人，两人都在等一个答复。\n# en: Join the headmistress's circle\nopt.wardens.label [d0d300] = 加入院长的圈子\n# en: The Wardens: old rules, old wards.\nopt.wardens.hint [8082aa] = 守望者：古老的规矩，古老的护界。\n# en: She beats you at chess in eleven moves, then tells you about the seal while she resets the board. \"Rings are for people who have stood on the ice,\" she says. \"Until then, you have my door.\"\nopt.wardens.say.1 [1f494e] = 她用十一步棋赢了你，一边重新摆棋盘，一边给你讲封印的事。“戒指是给站上过冰面的人的。”她说，“在那之前，我的门为你开着。”\n# en: Stand with the Dumonts\nopt.dumonts.label [1cf610] = 站在杜蒙兄妹一边\n# en: Kael's way, against his own family.\nopt.dumonts.hint [3d8ab6] = 凯尔的路，与他自己的家族为敌。\n# en: \"Not my family,\" Kael says, to the whole hall. \"Us.\" He teaches you to hold a ward in a wind that wants your fingers. His hands are cold. They are steadier than yours.\nopt.dumonts.say.1 [42717e] = “不是我的家族，”凯尔对着整个大厅说，“是我们。”他教你在一阵想把你手指吹掉的风里稳住护界。他的手很冷。比你的稳。\n# en: Go my own way\nopt.self.label [90c2bc] = 走我自己的路\n# en: Nobody's circle. Nobody's help.\nopt.self.hint [06bcd0] = 不入任何圈子，也没人帮忙。\n# en: You thank them both, out loud, and keep your own notes in your own cipher. It's lonelier than you expected. Both of them, you notice, keep an eye on you anyway.\nopt.self.say.1 [725a6a] = 你大声谢过他们两人，然后用自己的密码记自己的笔记。这比你想象的孤独。你注意到，他们俩还是都在留意着你。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.none.label [03d24a] = 置身事外\n# en: It isn't your door.\nopt.none.hint [4c3c5a] = 那不是你的门。\n# en: You turn both invitations down. Kael nods as if he expected it. The headmistress's note simply says, the next morning, \"Understood. Mind the ice.\"\nopt.none.say.1 [80761e] = 你婉拒了两份邀请。凯尔点点头，像是早就料到。第二天早上，院长的字条上只写着：“明白了。当心冰面。”\n\n@event hs_last_winter\n# en: The Last Winter Before\ntitle [77b708] = 最后一个冬天之前\n# en: The Lecture Tower has been dark for a year, and the lake freezes overnight in circles now. In spring the seal will be at its weakest. In the same week, one after another, they come and say it plainly: they'll stand with you beneath the lake, if you'll have them. You have one winter, and evenings enough for one of them.\ntext [d16c82] = 讲堂塔已经黑了一年，如今湖面一夜之间就会冻出一圈圈的冰。到了春天，封印会是最弱的时候。同一周里，他们一个接一个来找你，把话说得明明白白：如果你愿意，他们会在湖底与你并肩。你只有一个冬天，晚上的时间只够陪其中一个人。\n# en: Lantern light in a frost-rimed window, the frozen edge of the lake beyond it, a student's desk covered in maps of seven towers.\nscene [e5b843] = 结霜的窗户里透出灯笼的光，窗外是湖面冰冻的边缘，学生的书桌上铺满了七座塔的地图。\n# en: Train with Kael on the ice\nopt.kael.label [f6da05] = 和凯尔在冰上训练\n# en: He'll come down with you, and fight beside you.\nopt.kael.hint [23f740] = 他会和你一起下去，并肩作战。\n# en: He drills you on the ice until you can hold a ward with numb hands. \"Again,\" he says, and then, quietly, \"Good.\" You will remember that he said it.\nopt.kael.say.1 [e69c38] = 他在冰上一遍遍操练你，直到你能用冻僵的手撑住护界。“再来。”他说，然后轻声补了一句：“很好。”你会记得他说过这句话。\n# en: Mend wards with Maud\nopt.maud.label [7044a1] = 和莫德修补护界\n# en: She'll come down with you, and knows the founders' wall.\nopt.maud.hint [5b909a] = 她会和你一起下去，她认得创始者之墙。\n# en: You walk the towers with her, tracing old wards back into the stone. \"Your hands are steadier than mine were,\" she says, and doesn't say whose hands she means.\nopt.maud.say.1 [9b7f55] = 你陪她走遍七塔，把古老的护界一笔一笔描回石头里。“你的手比我当年的稳。”她说，却没说她指的是谁的手。\n# en: Study with the headmistress\nopt.isolde.label [d9f337] = 跟院长学习\n# en: She'll come down with you; you'll know the founders' words.\nopt.isolde.hint [9053dc] = 她会和你一起下去；你会熟记创始者的话。\n# en: You lose at chess every evening and learn the founders' words between moves. Once you nearly win. Her short, delighted laugh is the warmest sound you hear all winter.\nopt.isolde.say.1 [627308] = 每个傍晚你都输棋，在落子之间学会了创始者的咒文。有一次你差点赢了。她那短促而欣喜的笑声，是你整个冬天听过最温暖的声音。\n# en: Spar with Ilse at the shore\nopt.ilse.label [b13627] = 和伊尔泽在湖边对练\n# en: She'll come down with you, and fight beside you.\nopt.ilse.hint [355c01] = 她会和你一起下去，并肩作战。\n# en: She knocks you into the snow eleven times. The twelfth time you don't fall, and she looks at you as if you had done a trick she has never learned the counter to.\nopt.ilse.say.1 [86c9c1] = 她把你打进雪里十一次。第十二次你没有倒下，她看着你，仿佛你使出了一招她从没学过怎么破解的戏法。\n# en: Rest while you still can\nopt.alone.label [639301] = 趁还能休息就休息\n# en: A quiet winter. No new training, but rested.\nopt.alone.hint [ead4d4] = 一个安静的冬天。没有新的训练，但精力充沛。\n# en: You sleep, you eat, you read novels with no doors in them. It's the kindest winter you've had in years, and you can feel the lake getting colder anyway.\nopt.alone.say.1 [7e93f5] = 你睡觉，吃饭，读那些里面没有门的小说。这是你多年来最温柔的一个冬天，可你还是感觉得到湖水在一天天变冷。\n\n@event hs_ilse_door\n# en: What Ilse Was Taught\ntitle [8a9ef8] = 伊尔泽学过的东西\n# en: Ilse draws a circle in the snow with her wand, then a smaller one inside it. \"This is the door,\" she says. \"I have drawn it every day since I was six. I have never seen it.\" She doesn't look up. \"Ask me what they taught me to do. Or don't. I don't care which.\"\ntext [f6aa80] = 伊尔泽用魔杖在雪地上画了一个圆，又在里面画了一个小圆。“这就是那扇门。”她说，“我从六岁起每天都画它。我从没见过它。”她没有抬头。“问我他们教了我做什么。或者别问。我无所谓。”\n# en: A young woman in white furs drawing a round door in the snow with the tip of a wand, a frozen lake behind her.\nscene [1adcfd] = 一个身披白裘的年轻女子用魔杖尖在雪地上画一扇圆门，身后是冰封的湖。\n# en: \"What did they teach you?\"\nopt.left.label [ee8d4b] = “他们教了你什么？”\n# en: She tells you, flatly, like a lesson recited: how to stand, what to say, where to put her hands. \"They raised me to open it,\" she says. \"Nobody asked me if I wanted to.\" Then, sharply: \"Now you know. Don't look at me like that.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [e300d4] = 她平板地讲给你听，像在背诵一堂课：怎么站，说什么，手放在哪里。“他们把我养大，就是为了让我打开它。”她说，“从没人问过我想不想。”然后她语气一冷：“现在你知道了。别用那种眼神看我。”\n# en: \"You don't have to tell me.\"\nopt.right.label [194cf6] = “你不必告诉我。”\n# en: She rubs the circle out with her boot. \"Kindness,\" she says, as if naming a trick. \"I haven't learned the counter to that one yet.\" She doesn't leave, though.\nopt.right.say.1 [8dd039] = 她用靴子把那个圆抹掉。“善意。”她说，像在叫出一个戏法的名字，“这一招，我还没学会怎么破解。”不过，她没有走。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_hollowstar.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_hollowstar.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event hs_dreams_begin\n# en: The Black Star Dream\ntitle [c0e94a] = 黑星之梦\n# en: You dream of the lake from underneath. There is a round door at the bottom, and above the water a star that is not a light but an absence. A voice says your name like it is tasting it. You wake with frost on your pillow.\ntext [aadef3] = 你梦见从水下仰望湖面。湖底有一扇圆门，水面之上有一颗星——它不是光，而是一种缺失。一个声音念出你的名字，像在品尝它。你醒来时，枕头上结着霜。\n# en: A dreamscape: a frozen lake seen from beneath, a round door glowing at the bottom, and above it a star that is a hole in the sky.\nscene [ba146d] = 梦境：从水下仰望结冰的湖面，湖底一扇发光的圆门，上方一颗如同天空破洞的星。\n# en: Write the dream down\nopt.left.label [f34e00] = 把梦记下来\n# en: Your notes fill four pages. In the morning you don't remember writing the last line: \"It is not asleep. It is waiting.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [5620a5] = 你的笔记写满了四页。早上，你不记得自己写过最后一行：“它没有沉睡。它在等待。”\n# en: Ask Seraphine what it means\nopt.right.label [37112a] = 问瑟拉芬这是什么意思\n# en: Seraphine listens, then hands you a sprig of lavender. \"It won't stop the dreams,\" she says. \"But it'll make them smell nicer.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2b221e] = 瑟拉芬听完，递给你一枝薰衣草。“它止不住那些梦，”她说，“但能让梦闻起来香一点。”\n\n@event hs_grey_stranger\n# en: A Man in Grey\ntitle [52d66e] = 灰衣人\n# en: The courtyard fountain freezes mid-splash with a crack like a whip. Kael, who never raises his voice, is shouting at a tall man in a grey coat: \"Tell her no. I did.\" The man smiles. \"The door under the lake will be opened, by you or by your sister. Your mother doesn't mind which.\" Then he turns his head and says your name, which nobody told him.\ntext [4b0834] = 庭院的喷泉在溅起的一瞬间冻住了，发出鞭子抽响般的一声脆响。从不提高嗓门的凯尔，正冲着一个穿灰外套的高个子男人大吼：“告诉她不。我已经说过了。”那男人笑了。“湖底的门会被打开，由你，或者由你妹妹。你母亲并不在乎是谁。”然后他转过头，叫出了你的名字，而从来没有人告诉过他。\n# en: A frozen fountain in a moonlit courtyard; a tall man in a grey coat facing a pale young man who is shouting, frost spreading from both.\nscene [57e116] = 月光下庭院里一座冻住的喷泉；一个穿灰外套的高个子男人面对一个正在大吼的苍白年轻人，霜从两人身上蔓延开来。\n# en: Back into the shadows\nopt.left.label [c90bea] = 退回阴影里\n# en: You back away. He looks at the exact spot where you're hiding, smiles, and walks out through the gate with frost blooming in his footprints. You don't sleep. Neither, you suspect, does Kael.\nopt.left.say.1 [1003f6] = 你往后退。他看着你藏身的那个位置，分毫不差，笑了笑，走出大门，霜在他的脚印里开花。你一夜没睡。你猜凯尔也没有。\n# en: Step out beside Kael\nopt.right.label [23ee9c] = 站到凯尔身边\n# en: He'll remember your face.\nopt.right.hint [3fb718] = 他会记住你的脸。\n# en: \"He said no.\" It comes out louder than you meant. The man in grey studies you, inclines his head and leaves; the cold he leaves behind bites through your sleeves. Kael's hands are shaking. \"You shouldn't have done that,\" he says. \"...Thank you.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [0f8ac0] = “他说了不。”你的声音比你想的大。灰衣人打量着你，点了点头，走了；他留下的寒气咬透了你的袖子。凯尔的手在发抖。“你不该那么做的，”他说，“……谢谢。”\n\n@event hs_founders_records\n# en: The Founders' Ledger\ntitle [92688a] = 创始者账簿\n# en: In the archive vault you find the Founders' Ledger, three hundred years old. The entry for the academy's founding year has been cut out with a razor. But the page beneath still holds the impression of the pen.\ntext [8885d9] = 你在档案库里找到了三百年前的《创始者账簿》。记载学院创立那一年的条目被人用刀片割掉了。但下面那一页上，仍留着笔尖的压痕。\n# en: A dusty archive vault, a giant ledger open on a lectern, one page carefully razored out.\nscene [f6e330] = 积满灰尘的档案库，讲台上摊开一本巨大的账簿，其中一页被小心地割掉了。\n# en: Leave it — this feels dangerous\nopt.left.label [7ed54a] = 算了——这感觉很危险\n# en: You close the ledger. The dust settles. Some doors are better left alone. Probably.\nopt.left.say.1 [cce53b] = 你合上账簿。灰尘落定。有些门最好别去碰。大概吧。\n# en: Rub charcoal over the page\nopt.right.label [3eeead] = 用炭笔拓印那一页\n# en: Ghostly letters emerge: \"Seven towers, seven locks. The Star sleeps beneath the water. The key is a star that holds no light.\" The founders built the academy as a seal.\nopt.right.say.1 [8ceb50] = 幽灵般的字迹浮现出来：“七塔七锁。星眠于水下。钥匙是一颗不盛光的星。”创始者建起这座学院，正是为了作一道封印。\n# en: The impression is too faint. You make out only three words: \"...beneath the water...\"\nopt.right.say.2 [bee5f6] = 压痕太浅了。你只辨认出四个字：“……于水下……”\n\n@event hs_restricted_founders\n# en: The Chained Drafts\ntitle [872dc4] = 锁链里的草稿\n# en: The archive's ledger has its founding page cut out. The Restricted Section keeps the founders' own drafts, chained to a shelf at the very back, and the chain is warm to the touch. Somewhere behind you, a book turns its own page.\ntext [c28920] = 档案库那本账簿里建校的那一页被割掉了。禁书区里藏着创始者们亲笔的草稿，用锁链锁在最里面的书架上，那锁链摸上去是温的。你身后某处，一本书自己翻了一页。\n# en: A dark aisle of the Restricted Section at midnight, a bundle of yellowed drafts chained to a shelf, the chain faintly glowing.\nscene [1a2ec0] = 午夜禁书区一条昏暗的过道，一捆发黄的草稿被锁链锁在书架上，锁链隐隐发光。\n# en: Work the chain loose\nopt.left.label [aa3f60] = 把锁链弄松\n# en: The links give one by one, and burn your fingers doing it. The drafts say it plainly, in three hands: \"Seven towers, seven locks. The Star sleeps beneath the water. The key is a star that holds no light.\" Lumenhold is a seal.\nopt.left.say.1 [9baf12] = 链环一节一节松开，烫伤了你的手指。草稿上用三种笔迹写得明明白白：“七座塔，七把锁。星沉睡于水下。钥匙是一颗不含光的星。”流明堡就是一道封印。\n# en: The chain shrieks like a ward. You're out of the window before the prefect arrives, but not before he sees your face. Detention, and a long look from the librarian.\nopt.left.say.2 [c540d6] = 锁链像护界一样尖叫起来。你在级长赶到前翻出了窗，但他还是看见了你的脸。禁闭，外加图书管理员长长的一眼。\n# en: Copy the shelf-list instead\nopt.right.label [e5a618] = 改抄书目清单\n# en: The drafts' shelf-list is less guarded and nearly as strange. One entry reads only \"Key: the seventh tower, top room. Do not lend.\" You copy it out by the light of a reading charm you have to keep alive for an hour, and it leaves you drained.\nopt.right.say.1 [ba1ef3] = 草稿的书目清单看守得没那么严，却几乎一样古怪。其中一条只写着：“钥匙：第七塔，顶层。不外借。”你借着一道得撑上一个钟头的阅读咒的光把它抄下来，抄完人都虚脱了。\n\n@event hs_boathouse_bell\n# en: The Hatch in the Boathouse\ntitle [333304] = 船屋里的活板门\n# en: Under a coil of rotten rope in the boathouse there's a hatch, and under the hatch, stone steps going down into the lake. The air coming up smells of cold bronze. From somewhere below, very faint, a bell is tolling under the water.\ntext [d8dbb2] = 船屋里一卷烂绳子底下有一扇活板门，门下是通往湖里的石阶。往上涌的空气带着冰冷的青铜味。下面某处，非常微弱，一口钟正在水底鸣响。\n# en: The inside of an old wooden boathouse at night, a square hatch open in the floorboards, stone steps leading down into dark water.\nscene [947bde] = 夜里一座旧木船屋的内部，地板上一扇方形活板门敞开着，石阶通向漆黑的水里。\n# en: Go down the steps\nopt.left.label [26f409] = 走下台阶\n# en: The steps become a tunnel under the lakebed, dry and ice-cold, running toward the sound. You follow it until you can see the bell's green rim in the dark ahead. Then you turn back, frozen to the bone, and remember every turning.\nopt.left.say.1 [1ef535] = 台阶变成了湖床下的一条隧道，干燥而冰冷，朝着钟声延伸。你一直走到能看见前方黑暗中那口钟青绿的边沿。然后你转身往回走，冻得透骨，每一个拐弯都记在心里。\n# en: The third step is ice. You go into the water to the chest and come up gasping, but you've seen it: a tunnel under the lake, running toward the bell. You walk home soaked.\nopt.left.say.2 [c6b05c] = 第三级台阶结了冰。你一下子掉进齐胸深的水里，喘着气爬出来，但你看见了：湖底有条隧道，通向那口钟。你湿淋淋地走回了家。\n# en: Listen at the waterline\nopt.right.label [8f90c9] = 在水边倾听\n# en: You lie on the boards with your ear to the gap. The bell tolls seven times, slow, and between the strokes you hear a draught moving under the lake, the way air moves in a corridor. Something down there is a door. You don't sleep well.\nopt.right.say.1 [0754e3] = 你趴在木板上，耳朵贴着缝隙。钟慢慢地敲了七下，在两响之间，你听见湖底有气流在移动，就像空气在走廊里流动那样。下面有一扇门。你一夜没睡好。\n\n@event hs_seraphine_shadow\n# en: One Star Too Many\ntitle [bd51fe] = 多出来的一颗星\n# en: Seraphine has turned the great telescope down, not at the sky but at the lake, where the stars are reflected. \"Look,\" she says. \"In the water. There's one star too many, and it's dark.\" She counts under her breath. \"It wasn't there in my dream. It was in yours.\"\ntext [f656d5] = 瑟拉芬把那架大望远镜往下转，没对着天空，而是对着倒映着星星的湖面。“看，”她说，“水里。多了一颗星，而且是暗的。”她低声数着。“我的梦里没有它。是你的梦里有。”\n# en: An observatory at night, the great telescope tilted down toward the window and the lake below, a seer pointing at the reflection of the stars in the water.\nscene [93fed0] = 夜里的观星台，大望远镜朝下对着窗外的湖，一位预言者指着水中星星的倒影。\n# en: Chart it with her\nopt.left.label [c4492b] = 和她一起绘出它的位置\n# en: You chart it till dawn on the cold floor: a small dark shape in the reflection that has no star above it, sitting exactly over the middle of the lake. Your knees never quite forgive you.\nopt.left.say.1 [ff1233] = 你们在冰冷的地板上一直画到天亮：倒影里有一个小小的暗影，上方的天空里却没有对应的星，它正好停在湖的正中央。你的膝盖再也没完全原谅你。\n# en: Tell her about your dream\nopt.right.label [cc1d2c] = 告诉她你的梦\n# en: She finishes your sentences, three of them, before you've started them. Then she shows you the dark star in the water, right over the middle of the lake. You both stop talking for a long time.\nopt.right.say.1 [167dc3] = 你还没开口，她就替你说完了三句话。然后她指给你看水中那颗暗星，正在湖心上方。你们俩很久都没再说话。\n\n@event hs_kael_truth\n# en: What Kael Is Running From\ntitle [5fe24d] = 凯尔在逃避什么\n# en: It comes to a head on the observatory roof. You've had a year of his silences and you tell him so, loudly. He shouts back, for the first time ever: \"My family serve the thing under this lake! They sent me here to open its door!\" Frost races out from his boots across the roof. Then, quieter: \"I came to stop them. Some nights I'm not sure which I'll do.\"\ntext [ec30b4] = 事情在观星台屋顶上爆发了。你受够了他一整年的沉默，大声告诉了他。他第一次吼了回来：“我的家族侍奉这湖底下的东西！他们派我来这里打开它的门！”霜从他的靴子底下飞窜过整个屋顶。然后，声音低了下去：“我来是为了阻止他们。有些夜里，我不确定自己会做哪一件。”\n# en: Two students on an observatory roof at night, one shouting with frost racing out from his boots across the leads, the other standing their ground.\nscene [ec8aff] = 夜里观星台屋顶上的两个学生，一个在大吼，霜从他靴底飞窜过铅皮屋顶，另一个寸步不让。\n# en: \"Then we stop them together.\"\nopt.left.label [72f262] = “那我们一起阻止他们。”\n# en: He looks at you for a long moment, and the frost on the roof melts a little. \"Together,\" he repeats, as if trying out a word from another language.\nopt.left.say.1 [80bf53] = 他看了你很久，屋顶上的霜化了一点。“一起。”他重复道，像在试一个外语单词。\n# en: Tell the headmistress\nopt.right.label [bcf093] = 告诉院长\n# en: The headmistress thanks you gravely. The next day Kael is moved to a room in the headmistress's tower. He doesn't look at you anymore.\nopt.right.say.1 [07e3f1] = 院长郑重地感谢了你。第二天，凯尔被搬进了院长塔楼里的一个房间。他再也不看你了。\n\n@event hs_star_mark\n# en: The Cold Coin Burns\ntitle [2ace55] = 冰冷的硬币在灼烧\n# en: The black-starred relic you've been carrying burns suddenly, so cold it feels hot. When you drop it, there's a pale star-shaped mark on your palm. It hums when you're near the lake.\ntext [32d3ff] = 你一直带在身上的那件黑星遗物突然灼烧起来，冷到像是滚烫。你把它扔下时，掌心多了一枚苍白的星形印记。靠近湖边时，它会嗡嗡作响。\n# en: A student's open palm, a pale star-shaped mark glowing on it, a blackened coin smoking on the floor beside them.\nscene [be9e03] = 一个学生摊开的掌心，上面闪着苍白的星形印记，一枚发黑的硬币在旁边的地上冒着烟。\n# en: Throw the relic into the lake\nopt.left.label [9e07e3] = 把遗物扔进湖里\n# en: It sinks without a ripple. The mark on your hand fades — almost entirely. Something under the water sighs.\nopt.left.say.1 [f57ba3] = 它沉了下去，没起一丝涟漪。你手上的印记褪去了——几乎完全褪去。水下有什么东西叹了口气。\n# en: Keep it. Feel the power.\nopt.right.label [9c18f9] = 留着它。感受这份力量。\n# en: The mark pulses with every heartbeat. Spells come easier now. Much easier. That should probably worry you more than it does.\nopt.right.say.1 [49d379] = 印记随着每一次心跳搏动。现在施法容易多了。容易太多了。这本该比现在更让你担心才对。\n\n@event hs_grey_offer\n# en: An Offer in Grey\ntitle [72fb9a] = 灰色的提议\n# en: Every candle in the room goes blue at once. The man in grey is sitting at your table, uninvited, a black velvet box open in front of him. \"You've been asking about the Star,\" he says. \"It has started asking about you. It could make you extraordinary. All it wants is a door opened.\" He turns the box toward you. Inside it you see yourself, burning.\ntext [92c6d3] = 屋里的每一支蜡烛同时变成了蓝色。灰衣人不请自来，坐在你的桌边，面前打开着一个黑丝绒盒子。“你一直在打听那颗星，”他说，“它也开始打听你了。它能让你非凡。它要的只是一扇门被打开。”他把盒子转向你。盒子里，你看见了自己，在燃烧。\n# en: A grey-coated man sitting uninvited at a library table, a black velvet box open in front of him, every candle around him burning blue.\nscene [ed2d17] = 一个穿灰外套的男人不请自来地坐在图书馆桌边，面前打开着一个黑丝绒盒子，他周围的每一支蜡烛都燃着蓝火。\n# en: \"Get out.\"\nopt.left.label [f967c3] = “滚出去。”\n# en: He leaves. He remembers.\nopt.left.hint [7103e7] = 他会走。他会记得。\n# en: He inclines his head and leaves. The candles go back to yellow. You realise your hands are trembling, and that he looked at you on the way out the way a man looks at a lock.\nopt.left.say.1 [462c88] = 他点了点头，走了。蜡烛变回了黄色。你这才发觉自己的手在发抖，而他离开时看你的眼神，就像一个人在看一把锁。\n# en: \"...What would it give me?\"\nopt.right.label [d466af] = “……它能给我什么？”\n# en: Everything, a little. The nights turn cold.\nopt.right.hint [40ed04] = 一切都会好一点。夜会变冷。\n# en: He shows you, just for a moment. Power, endless and cold, in everything you can do at once. When he's gone you can still taste it, and that night you dream of water closing over your head.\nopt.right.say.1 [f32dee] = 他给你看了，只有一瞬间。力量，无穷无尽，冰冷彻骨，同时流进你会做的每一件事。他走后，你仍能尝到那股滋味；那天夜里，你梦见水在头顶合拢。\n\n@event hs_grey_return\n# en: A Small Service\ntitle [225de0] = 小小的差事\n# en: {flag(hollow_tempted): The man in grey is on a bench in the winter garden, and the fountain beside him has frozen mid-splash. \"You liked what you tasted,\" he says. \"Here is how it continues.\" He holds out a sealed letter. \"Leave this under the third plank of the boathouse jetty. Nothing more.\"|The man in grey is on a bench in the winter garden, and the fountain beside him has frozen mid-splash. \"You told me to get out,\" he says, pleasantly. \"I did. Now a small thing, and I'll stay out.\" He holds out a sealed letter. \"The third plank of the boathouse jetty. Nothing more.\"}\ntext [d954a1] = {flag(hollow_tempted): 灰衣人坐在冬日花园的长椅上，他身旁的喷泉在半空中冻住了。“你喜欢那滋味，”他说，“接下来是这样。”他递出一封封好的信。“把它放在船屋栈桥第三块木板底下。仅此而已。”|灰衣人坐在冬日花园的长椅上，他身旁的喷泉在半空中冻住了。“你叫我滚出去，”他和颜悦色地说，“我滚了。现在只是件小事，办完我就不再来。”他递出一封封好的信。“船屋栈桥，第三块木板。仅此而已。”}\n# en: A grey-coated man on a frozen garden bench, a sealed letter held out between two gloved fingers, the fountain beside him iced over.\nscene [ecc098] = 一个穿灰外套的男人坐在结冰的花园长椅上，两根戴手套的手指夹着一封封好的信，身旁的喷泉结满了冰。\n# en: Take the letter to the jetty\nopt.left.label [935473] = 把信送到栈桥\n# en: Hollow. He pays in what he promised.\nopt.left.hint [89d3bb] = 空洞。他会兑现承诺。\n# en: You do it at midnight, with the lake breathing under the planks. In the morning there's a purse on your pillow and the cold dreams are back, sweeter than before.\nopt.left.say.1 [cdaade] = 你在午夜去办了，湖水在木板下呼吸。第二天早上，枕头上放着一只钱袋，冰冷的梦又回来了，比以前更甜美。\n# en: In the purse, under the coins, a slip of black paper with a few lines on it. You read them once, and a bruise on your wrist stops hurting and starts to hum. Pain taken, power held: Eclipse.\nopt.left.say.2 [c309f8] = 钱袋里，硬币底下，有一张写着几行字的黑纸条。你读了一遍，手腕上的一块淤青就不疼了，开始嗡嗡作响。承受的痛，化为握住的力：蚀。\n# en: Drop it in the fountain unread\nopt.right.label [c6d2c0] = 把信丢进喷泉\n# en: Lumen. He won't thank you.\nopt.right.hint [032137] = 流明。他不会感谢你。\n# en: You break the ice and push the letter under. The ink runs blue. He watches it sink, then you, and then he isn't there, and the fountain starts to splash again as if nothing had happened.\nopt.right.say.1 [573f87] = 你敲开冰面，把信按了下去。墨水洇成了蓝色。他看着信沉下去，又看着你，然后他就不在了，喷泉重新溅起水花，好像什么都没发生过。\n\n@event hs_grey_invitation\n# en: A Seat at the Northern Table\ntitle [c52641] = 北境之席\n# en: There is a letter on your pillow, sealed with a black star. The handwriting is beautiful. \"The families have watched you with great interest,\" it says. \"There is a seat for you at our table, when the door is opened. Come to the boathouse at midnight, or burn this. Either is an answer.\"\ntext [13f2ac] = 你的枕头上放着一封信，用一枚黑色的星封着蜡。字迹很漂亮。“世家怀着极大的兴趣关注着你，”信上写道，“门打开之时，我们的席上会有你一个座位。午夜到船屋来，或者烧了这封信。两者都是答复。”\n# en: A black-edged letter on a dormitory pillow, its wax seal pressed with a star that has no light in it.\nscene [f56301] = 宿舍枕头上一封镶黑边的信，封蜡上压着一颗不发光的星。\n# en: Go to the boathouse\nopt.left.label [526aa1] = 去船屋\n# en: The man in grey is waiting with two cups of something hot. He talks to you as an equal for an hour. You come back with frost in your hair and the feeling of having been measured, and found to fit.\nopt.left.say.1 [c84e12] = 灰衣人端着两杯热饮在等你。他像对待平辈一样和你谈了一个小时。你回来时头发上沾着霜，觉得自己被丈量过，而且尺寸正合适。\n# en: Before you go he takes your cold hand in his and shows you how to keep what hurts: pain taken, power held. Eclipse. Your fingers ache all the way back, and hum.\nopt.left.say.2 [f8337c] = 临走前，他把你冰冷的手握在他手里，教你怎样留住伤痛：承受的痛，化为握住的力。蚀。回去的一路上你的手指都在疼，也在嗡鸣。\n# en: Burn it\nopt.right.label [162285] = 烧掉它\n# en: The paper burns blue and slow, and smells of the lake. It takes a long time to go out. Climbing back from where you've been is slower still, but this is a step.\nopt.right.say.1 [dfcc08] = 信纸燃起蓝色的火，烧得很慢，散发着湖水的气味。它很久才烧尽。从你走到的地方爬回来还要更慢，但这是一步。\n\n@event hs_grey_tests\n# en: The Good Argument\ntitle [e22d78] = 好听的道理\n# en: The man in grey falls into step beside you outside the infirmary. \"You help people,\" he says pleasantly. \"I admire it. But think how many more you could help with real power. The Star doesn't care what it's used for. Only that someone uses it.\"\ntext [9adfef] = 灰衣人在医务室外与你并肩而行。“你帮助别人，”他和气地说，“我很欣赏。可想想看，若有真正的力量，你能多帮多少人。星不在乎它被用来做什么，只在乎有人用它。”\n# en: A grey-coated man standing beside a student at the infirmary window, both looking down at a first-year with a bandaged arm.\nscene [86ebbc] = 一个穿灰外套的男人和一名学生并肩站在医务室窗前，一起低头看着一个手臂缠着绷带的一年级生。\n# en: \"No.\"\nopt.left.label [371a7a] = “不。”\n# en: \"No,\" you say. He smiles as if you'd said something charming, and leaves. The first-year in the window waves at you. You wave back, and your hand is shaking.\nopt.left.say.1 [ce8c7e] = “不，”你说。他笑了笑，仿佛你说了句讨人喜欢的话，然后离开了。窗边的一年级生朝你挥手。你也挥手，手在发抖。\n# en: \"...How many more?\"\nopt.right.label [faa864] = “……能多帮多少？”\n# en: He tells you. The numbers are very large and very cold. That night, you dream of the lake from underneath, and you are not frightened in the dream.\nopt.right.say.1 [3239c8] = 他告诉了你。那些数字非常大，也非常冷。那天夜里，你梦见从湖底往上看，而梦里的你并不害怕。\n\n@event hs_wardens_notice\n# en: The Headmistress Takes Note\ntitle [f7baa8] = 院长记下了你\n# en: On the grand stair the headmistress stops beside you. \"Several people have told me about you,\" she says, \"and not one of them was complaining. That is rare.\" Her silver ring catches the light. \"I keep a list of students I would trust with a door. Don't let it go to your head.\"\ntext [aef554] = 在大楼梯上，院长在你身旁停下。“好几个人跟我提起过你，”她说，“没有一个是来告状的。这很少见。”她的银戒指映着光。“我有一份名单，上面是我愿意托付一扇门的学生。别因此得意忘形。”\n# en: A tall woman in dark robes pausing on a stair to look down at one student among many, a silver ring glinting on her hand.\nscene [25a5bf] = 一位身着深色长袍的高个女人在楼梯上停步，低头看着众多学生中的一个，手上的银戒指闪着光。\n# en: \"I'd be honoured.\"\nopt.left.label [bec864] = “我深感荣幸。”\n# en: \"Good,\" she says. \"Honour is a heavy thing to carry. Practise.\" She walks on. You stand on the stair for some time.\nopt.left.say.1 [e74b53] = “很好，”她说，“荣誉是件沉重的东西。多练练。”她继续往前走。你在楼梯上站了好一会儿。\n# en: \"What kind of door?\"\nopt.right.label [ee5679] = “什么样的门？”\n# en: Her short, delighted laugh. \"The kind that ought to stay shut,\" she says. \"Keep asking questions like that. Just not on the stairs.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [c568f6] = 她发出那声短促而愉快的笑。“应该一直关着的那种，”她说，“继续问这样的问题吧。只是别在楼梯上问。”\n\n@event hs_seventh_tower\n# en: The Seventh Tower\ntitle [44b8dc] = 第七塔\n# en: The seventh tower has been locked for a century, and tonight you mean to see what it keeps. You go up two steps at a time to the very top: a single room of dust and moonlight, and a star-shaped hollow in the wall, holding something that does not reflect any light. The wards are woven round the hollow itself. They hum as you come near, and they will shriek the moment it's touched.\ntext [5c4a01] = 第七塔已经锁了一百年，而今晚你打算去看看它守着什么。你一步跨两级，一直上到塔顶：只有一个房间，灰尘，月光，墙上一个星形的凹槽，里面放着一样不反射任何光的东西。结界就织在凹槽四周。你一走近，它们就嗡嗡作响，只要一碰到它，它们就会尖叫起来。\n# en: The top of a narrow, abandoned tower, cobwebbed and moonlit, with a wall bearing a star-shaped recess.\nscene [c775ba] = 一座狭窄废塔的顶层，蛛网密布，月光洒入，墙上有一个星形凹槽。\n# en: Take it quietly\nopt.left.label [2a0b10] = 悄悄拿走\n# en: A key shaped like a star, black as a hole in the world. As you lift it, every candle in the academy goes out for one breath. You walk back in the dark with it cold against your ribs, and you don't sleep.\nopt.left.say.1 [f9b295] = 一把星形的钥匙，黑得像世界上的一个洞。你拿起它的一刻，学院里每一支蜡烛都熄灭了一口气的工夫。你揣着它在黑暗中走回去，它冰冷地贴着你的肋骨，你一夜没睡。\n# en: A ward shrieks. A prefect finds you on the stairs. You get detention and a very long look from the headmistress.\nopt.left.say.2 [ca0973] = 防护咒尖叫起来。一位级长在楼梯上逮住了你。你被关了禁闭，还挨了院长很长很长的一眼。\n# en: Take it boldly\nopt.right.label [f86c9d] = 强行拿走\n# en: You tear through the ward with brute force, and it drains you to the bone. The key comes free. Every candle in the academy goes out for one breath, and somewhere below, a door hears it.\nopt.right.say.1 [777369] = 你凭蛮力撕开了护界，这把你耗得精疲力尽。钥匙脱落下来。学院里每一支蜡烛都熄灭了一口气的工夫，而在下面某处，一扇门听见了。\n# en: The ward throws you down two flights of stairs.\nopt.right.say.2 [1014e6] = 防护咒把你甩下了两层楼梯。\n\n@event hs_key_breakfast\n# en: Something of Mine\ntitle [f9fe6f] = 我的东西\n# en: At breakfast the headmistress sets down her cup and looks straight at you along the whole length of the hall. Afterwards she is waiting at the door. \"Every candle in my academy went out,\" she says pleasantly, \"and you have something of mine.\"\ntext [e0137d] = 早餐时，校长放下杯子，隔着整个大厅直直地看着你。之后她在门口等你。“我学院里的每一支蜡烛都灭了，”她愉快地说，“而你拿着我的一样东西。”\n# en: The high table at breakfast, the headmistress setting down her cup and looking along the hall at one student, the candles behind her newly lit.\nscene [fdadd9] = 早餐时的高桌，校长放下杯子，沿着大厅望向一名学生，她身后的蜡烛刚刚重新点亮。\n# en: Give her the key\nopt.left.label [28b877] = 把钥匙交给她\n# en: She takes it in a gloved hand without looking at it. \"Thank you. It will be where it is needed, on the night. So, I hope, will you.\" You feel lighter, and a little robbed.\nopt.left.say.1 [86a9c7] = 她用戴手套的手接过去，看都没看一眼。“谢谢。那一夜，它会在需要它的地方。我希望你也会。”你觉得轻松了些，又有点像被抢了。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.right.label [87c2f5] = 留着它\n# en: A long pause. \"Then keep it close,\" she says, \"and never let it near the water before its time. People will come asking for it. Not all of them will ask.\" From then on, the man in grey always seems to know where you are.\nopt.right.say.1 [d35dc1] = 长长的停顿。“那就贴身收好，”她说，“时候未到，绝不要让它靠近湖水。会有人来讨要它。不是每个人都会开口讨。”从那以后，灰衣人似乎总知道你在哪儿。\n\n@event hs_isolde_summons\n# en: The Headmistress Knows\ntitle [214bdc] = 院长知道了\n# en: The headmistress summons you to her tower. Thorne is there, and Maud, and on the desk between them is a letter with a black-star seal, opened. \"The Dumonts' mother is coming south for the Blossom moon,\" Isolde says. \"She means to open the door herself. I need people I trust beneath the lake. Or you may walk away, and no one in this room will blame you.\"\ntext [840fcc] = 院长召你去她的塔。索恩在，莫德也在，他们之间的桌上放着一封拆开的黑星封蜡信。“杜蒙夫人要在花潮之月南下，”伊索尔德说，“她打算亲手打开那扇门。我需要我信得过的人到湖底去。你也可以离开，这个房间里没有人会怪你。”\n# en: A circular study at the top of a tower, walls lined with sleeping portraits, the headmistress standing by a window over the lake, Thorne and Maud beside her, a black-sealed letter on the desk.\nscene [9c23a6] = 一座塔顶的圆形书房，墙上挂满沉睡的肖像，院长站在俯瞰湖面的窗前，索恩和莫德在她身旁，桌上放着一封黑色封蜡的信。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.left.label [96da1a] = 离开\n# en: \"Wise,\" she says, and you can't tell if she means it. Maud looks at her tea. \"Then stay above,\" says the headmistress, \"and keep the first-years indoors.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [617954] = “明智。”她说，你分辨不出她是不是真心的。莫德看着自己的茶。“那就留在上面，”院长说，“让一年级生待在屋里。”\n# en: \"I'll be there.\"\nopt.right.label [d06338] = “我会去的。”\n# en: She presses a silver ward-ring into your hand. \"{flag(star_key): The founders' key is with you. Keep it close.|{flag(gave_star_key) or flag(key_taken): The founders' key is in my desk; the Wardens saw to that.|The founders' key is kept at the top of the seventh tower.}} On the night, it comes down with us. Learn quickly. You have until Blossomtide.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [4f7604] = 她把一枚银色的护界戒指按进你手里。“{flag(star_key): 创始者的钥匙在你身上。带好它。|{flag(gave_star_key) or flag(key_taken): 创始者的钥匙在我桌子里；守望者们办妥了。|创始者的钥匙存放在第七塔的顶上。}}那一夜，它要随我们一起下去。学快点。你只有到花潮月的时间。”\n\n@event hs_the_night\n# en: The Night the Lake Froze\ntitle [6f7fcc] = 湖面冰封之夜\n# en: In the middle of spring, the lake freezes in a single breath, from shore to shore. Cracks glow beneath the ice. Every candle in the academy leans toward the water. Out on the ice, lanterns are moving in a ring: someone is already there. Under it all the bell is tolling, {clue(lake_bell): the note you followed once|a note you've never heard and somehow know}.\ntext [1c4991] = 春天正盛，湖却在一口气间冻住了，从这岸到那岸。冰下的裂缝发着光。学院里的每一支蜡烛都朝水面倾斜。冰上有一圈提灯在移动：已经有人在那里了。在这一切之下，钟在鸣响，{clue(lake_bell): 那是你曾追寻过的音|那是你从没听过、却莫名认得的音}。\n# en: The whole lake frozen white in the middle of spring, cracks glowing from beneath, a ring of lanterns out on the ice, students crowding the towers' windows.\nscene [8ddb0d] = 整片湖在春天里冻得雪白，冰下的裂缝发着光，冰面上一圈提灯，学生们挤在塔楼窗前。\n# en: Go down beneath the lake\nopt.left.label [983634] = 潜入湖底\n# en: You run for the boathouse. Behind you, someone is shouting your name. You don't stop.\nopt.left.say.1 [28f348] = 你朝船屋奔去。身后有人在喊你的名字。你没有停下。\n# en: Stay above and protect the students\nopt.right.label [3d4500] = 留在上面保护学生\n# en: You hold the doors of the first-year tower all night against things made of frost and whispers. By dawn the lake thaws. Something below is still asleep, for now, and forty children are alive who might not have been.\nopt.right.say.1 [477de2] = 你守着一年级塔的门，整夜抵挡那些由霜与低语构成的东西。到黎明，湖解冻了。下面的东西还在沉睡，暂时如此，而四十个本可能活不下来的孩子都活着。\n\n@event hs_tunnel\n# en: The Man in the Tunnel\ntitle [809d66] = 隧道里的人\n# en: The tunnel runs down under the ice, slick and glowing blue. Halfway down, the man in grey is waiting, alone, as if he has all night. {flag(night_kael): \"Kael. Your mother is disappointed.\" \"She always is,\" says Kael, and the walls frost over.|{flag(night_ilse): \"Ilse. You were meant to open it.\" \"I know what I was meant for,\" she says.|{flag(night_isolde): \"Headmistress. You're late by sixty years.\" \"Then I'll be thorough,\" she says.|\"Go back up,\" he says pleasantly. \"This isn't your door.\"}}}\ntext [61b72b] = 隧道在冰下一路向下，湿滑，泛着蓝光。走到一半，灰衣人在那里等着，独自一人，好像他有整夜的时间。{flag(night_kael): “凯尔。你母亲很失望。”“她一向如此。”凯尔说，墙壁结满了霜。|{flag(night_ilse): “伊尔泽。你本该打开它的。”“我知道我本该做什么。”她说。|{flag(night_isolde): “院长。你晚了六十年。”“那我就做得彻底些。”她说。|“回上面去吧，”他和颜悦色地说，“这不是你的门。”}}}\n# en: A slick stone tunnel under the ice, lit blue from above, a tall man in a grey coat standing in the middle of it with his hands folded.\nscene [07762c] = 冰层下一条湿滑的石隧道，上方透下蓝光，一个穿灰大衣的高个男人双手交握，站在隧道中间。\n# en: Break through him\nopt.fight.label [07b59f] = 从他身上闯过去\n# en: Combat. Whoever came down with you fights beside you.\nopt.fight.hint [af8914] = 战斗。和你一起下来的人会并肩作战。\n# en: You hit him {flag(night_kael): with Kael's frost at the same moment|square}, and he slides back along the ice, {flag(grey_beaten): and this time he isn't surprised; he's angry|surprised for the first time in four years}. He bows, and is simply not there any more. The road down is clear.\nopt.fight.say.1 [f664d8] = 你{flag(night_kael): 和凯尔的霜同时|正中}击中了他，他在冰上向后滑去，{flag(grey_beaten): 这一次他没有惊讶，而是动了怒|四年来第一次露出惊讶}。他鞠了一躬，便不在那里了。下去的路畅通了。\n# en: He takes your last spell on his own gloved hand and closes his fist on it, then steps back to let you pass, smiling. \"Go on, then. She's waiting.\" You go down bruised, and late.\nopt.fight.say.2 [41a7f7] = 他用戴着手套的手接住你最后一道咒语，握拳把它捏碎，然后退后一步让你过去，微笑着。“去吧。她在等你。”你带着一身淤伤下去了，也迟了。\n# en: Talk your way past\nopt.talk.label [f5bc3b] = 说服他让路\n# en: Charm. What the Dumonts were raised for is your best argument.\nopt.talk.hint [5818f7] = 魅力。杜蒙家的孩子是为什么被养大的，就是你最好的理由。\n# en: You tell him {flag(ilse_taken): that Kael has chosen, and Ilse will|that the Dumont children have chosen, both of them}, and that you know exactly what they were raised for and what it cost them. For a moment he looks old. Then he steps aside. It costs you something, seeing that look.\nopt.talk.say.1 [3983ce] = 你告诉他，{flag(ilse_taken): 凯尔已经做了选择，伊尔泽也会|杜蒙家的两个孩子都已经做了选择}，你清清楚楚地知道他们是为什么被养大的，又为此付出了什么。有那么一瞬间，他显得很老。然后他让开了。看见那种神情，你也付出了些什么。\n# en: He listens courteously, then tells you, kindly, everything the Dumonts did to their children and why. It takes a long time. He lets you pass only once you've heard every word. You arrive shaken, and late.\nopt.talk.say.2 [3f6ed7] = 他礼貌地听完，然后和善地告诉你杜蒙家对他们的孩子做过的一切，以及为什么。说了很久。直到你听完每一个字，他才让你过去。你到达时浑身发抖，也迟了。\n# en: Find another way down\nopt.slip.label [1e4f9e] = 另找一条路下去\n# en: Guile. Easier if you know the old tunnels, or the bell's note.\nopt.slip.hint [ab0e75] = 诡计。如果你知道那些旧隧道，或者那口钟的音，会更容易。\n# en: You follow the bell's note into a side passage he doesn't know, the one its rope once ran through. You come out below him, breathless, and he never sees you go.\nopt.slip.say.1 [7f673b] = 你循着钟声走进一条他不知道的岔道，就是当年钟绳穿过的那条。你从他下方钻出来，气喘吁吁，他始终没看见你走过。\n# en: The side passage is half flooded. You come out soaked and freezing below him, and late, and he calls a polite good evening after you down the tunnel.\nopt.slip.say.2 [4a074a] = 岔道里一半是水。你从他下方钻出来，浑身湿透冻僵，也迟了，他沿着隧道在你身后礼貌地道了声晚安。\n\n@event hs_threshold\n# en: The Founders' Words\ntitle [5a0a8c] = 创始者的咒文\n# en: The tunnel ends at a wall of black stone, carved with the founders' words in a script that drifts when you look away, and set with seven round locks, one for each tower. {chosen(hs_maud_locks, right): You know them. Maud told you.|{clue(seven_locks): You know them.|You count them twice.}} Behind it the bell is tolling. {flag(night_maud): Maud lays a hand on the stone and says, \"Hello again.\"|}\ntext [80135d] = 隧道尽头是一面黑石墙，刻着创始者的话，字迹一移开视线就会漂移，墙上嵌着七把圆锁，每座塔一把。{chosen(hs_maud_locks, right): 你认得它们。莫德告诉过你。|{clue(seven_locks): 你认得它们。|你数了两遍。}}墙后，钟在鸣响。{flag(night_maud): 莫德把一只手按在石头上，说：“又见面了。”|}\n# en: A wall of black stone at the end of a tunnel, carved with lines of script that drift when not looked at, seven round locks set in it, a faint bell-tone hanging in the air.\nscene [17845a] = 隧道尽头一面黑石墙，刻着不被注视时便会漂移的文字，墙上嵌着七把圆锁，空气里悬着一丝隐约的钟声。\n# en: Read the founders' words\nopt.read.label [78313a] = 念出创始者的咒文\n# en: Lore. Everything you learned about the seal makes it easier.\nopt.read.hint [19bc2e] = 学识。你对封印了解的一切都会让它更容易。\n# en: {clue(knows_founders_seal): You've read these words before, in pieces.|You've never read them, but they read themselves to you, line by line.} Said whole, they turn the seven locks one after another, and the wall opens like a door. \"Seven towers, seven locks,\" you hear yourself say. \"The key is a star that holds no light.\"\nopt.read.say.1 [7b08f2] = {clue(knows_founders_seal): 这些话你以前零零碎碎读过。|你从没读过它们，它们却一行一行自己念给了你。}完整地念出来，它们让七把锁一把接一把转开，墙像门一样打开了。“七座塔，七把锁，”你听见自己说，“钥匙是一颗不发光的星。”\n# en: You stumble on the last line, and only six of the locks turn. The wall opens a crack. You squeeze through it, and it takes skin off your shoulder on the way, and the words follow you down the dark like a rhyme.\nopt.read.say.2 [fc390d] = 你在最后一行卡住了，只有六把锁转开。墙开了一道缝。你从缝里挤过去，肩膀蹭掉一层皮，那些话像童谣一样在黑暗中一路追着你。\n# en: Force the locks\nopt.force.label [9bdd4b] = 强行打开锁\n# en: Arcana, and it hurts. Maud's wards and Edmund's wand help.\nopt.force.hint [1af26a] = 奥术，而且会疼。莫德的护界和埃德蒙的魔杖会有帮助。\n# en: {has_item(edmunds_silver_wand): Edmund's wand hums the bell's note against the stone, and the locks turn to it one by one.|You trace the old wards backwards into the stone, {flag(winter_maud): the way Maud showed you|the way the stone seems to want}, and the locks turn one by one.} The wall cracks down the middle and opens. Your hands ache to the elbow.\nopt.force.say.1 [479b78] = {has_item(edmunds_silver_wand): 埃德蒙的魔杖贴着石头嗡鸣出那口钟的音，锁一把接一把应声转开。|你{flag(winter_maud): 按莫德教你的那样|顺着石头似乎想要的方向}，把古老的护界反向描进石头里，锁一把接一把转开。}墙从中间裂开，打开了。你的手一直疼到手肘。\n# en: The stone fights back. Six locks give; the seventh you break, and it breaks something in your hands too. You get through in the end, raw and shaking.\nopt.force.say.2 [4b85cc] = 石头在反抗。六把锁打开了；第七把是你砸开的，它也砸坏了你手里的什么东西。你最后还是过去了，双手血肉模糊，抖个不停。\n\n@event hs_beneath\n# en: The Door Beneath the Lake\ntitle [76c2d3] = 湖底之门\n# en: Beyond the wall, the round door, open a crack, and through the crack the Hollow Star looks at you. It is beautiful, and it is hungry, and it speaks in your own voice: \"You came all this way. Open me. Or take what I'm offering. Or run.\" {flag(night_kael): Kael is beside you, frost spreading from his feet.|{flag(night_ilse): Ilse is beside you, breathing steady for once.|{flag(night_maud): Maud is beside you{has_item(edmunds_silver_wand): , and Edmund's wand in your hand is humming the bell's note|, humming the bell's note under her breath}.|{flag(night_isolde): The headmistress stands beside you, her ring lit like a lamp.|You are alone.}}}}\ntext [2a13a3] = 墙后是那扇圆门，开着一道缝，空洞之星透过那道缝看着你。它美丽，它饥饿，它用你自己的声音说话：“你走了这么远。打开我。或者接受我给的东西。或者逃跑。”{flag(night_kael): 凯尔在你身边，霜从他脚下蔓延开来。|{flag(night_ilse): 伊尔泽在你身边，难得地呼吸平稳。|{flag(night_maud): 莫德在你身边{has_item(edmunds_silver_wand): ，你手里埃德蒙的魔杖正嗡鸣着那口钟的音|，低声哼着那口钟的音}。|{flag(night_isolde): 院长站在你身边，她的戒指亮得像一盏灯。|你是一个人。}}}}\n# en: A vast domed cavern under the lake, a round door of black stone standing open a crack, and through it a star that is pure absence.\nscene [d3244b] = 湖底一座巨大的穹形洞窟，一扇黑石圆门开着一道缝，门后是一颗纯粹由“缺失”构成的星。\n# en: Seal the door\nopt.seal.label [b5b84d] = 封印这扇门\n# en: Needs the star key, the founders' secret or a ward-ring. Arcana; everything you did tonight counts. It can fail.\nopt.seal.hint [c5722d] = 需要星钥、创始者的秘密或一枚护界戒指。奥术；你今夜做的一切都算数。可能失败。\n# en: {flag(star_key): The star key turns in the round door, and you|{flag(gave_star_key) and flag(night_isolde): The headmistress turns the star key in the round door, and you|You}} speak the founders' words with everything you have, and the door grinds shut. The Star screams without sound. Above you, the ice cracks from shore to shore, and the lake breathes again.\nopt.seal.say.1 [bc56ab] = {flag(star_key): 星钥在圆门里转动，你|{flag(gave_star_key) and flag(night_isolde): 院长把星钥插进圆门转动，你|你}}用尽全力念出创始者的话，门轧轧地关上了。星无声地尖叫。在你头顶，冰从这岸裂到那岸，湖又开始呼吸。\n# en: Your seal falters. Kael throws every ounce of frost in him into it, and it still isn't enough: the door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\nopt.seal.say.2 [69a006] = 你的封印动摇了。凯尔把体内每一分寒冰都押了上去，仍然不够：门依旧半开着。你看见的最后一样东西，是校长的提灯沿着隧道下来。\n# en: Your seal falters and breaks, and takes most of you with it. The door stays ajar. The last thing you see is the headmistress's lantern coming down the tunnel.\nopt.seal.say.3 [c2675b] = 你的封印动摇、碎裂，几乎带走了你的全部。门依旧半开着。你看见的最后一样东西，是校长的提灯沿着隧道下来。\n# en: Accept the Star's bargain\nopt.bargain.label [3a1372] = 接受星的交易\n# en: Its power, now, and a voice in your head forever. Only for those it has already tempted.\nopt.bargain.hint [cbcdc9] = 它的力量，就在此刻，还有一个永远在你脑中的声音。只给那些已经被它诱惑过的人。\n# en: You take what it offers, and it feels like coming home. The door closes on its own, for now. The Star settles into the back of your mind like a cat finding a warm lap, and pours itself into your hands.\nopt.bargain.say.1 [1871c8] = 你接受了它给的东西，感觉像回到了家。门自己关上了，暂时。星在你脑海深处安顿下来，像一只猫找到了温暖的膝头，然后把自己倾注进你的双手。\n# en: You take what it offers. The door closes on its own, for now. The Star settles into the back of your mind like a cat finding a warm lap, and pours itself into your hands, and you can't stop shaking.\nopt.bargain.say.2 [0af862] = 你接受了它给的东西。门自己关上了，暂时。星在你脑海深处安顿下来，像一只猫找到了温暖的膝头，然后把自己倾注进你的双手，你止不住地发抖。\n# en: Open the door\nopt.open.label [6f1706] = 打开这扇门\n# en: It wants out. Only one already deep in the Hollow could walk through beside it.\nopt.open.hint [55783a] = 它想出来。只有早已深陷空洞的人，才能与它并肩走过这扇门。\n# en: You set your palm on the black stone and push. The door swings wide. The Star rises past you like a breath held for three hundred years, and on its way out it touches your shoulder, the way a lord touches a sword. Above, every candle in Lumenhold goes out at once.\nopt.open.say.1 [c042d8] = 你把手掌按在黑石上，推开。门大大敞开。星从你身边升起，像一口憋了三百年的气，经过时它碰了碰你的肩，就像领主用剑轻点骑士。在上面，流明堡的每一支蜡烛同时熄灭。\n# en: Run\nopt.flee.label [57e66a] = 逃跑\n# en: You run. You run until the tunnel spits you out onto the shore, and you don't look back once. Behind you, under the ice, the door is still ajar.\nopt.flee.say.1 [14a017] = 你跑了。你一直跑到隧道把你吐到岸上，一次也没回头。在你身后，冰下，那扇门还虚掩着。\n\n@event hs_aftermath\n# en: After the Thaw\ntitle [a5fec6] = 解冻之后\n# en: The lake glitters as if nothing happened. Most of the academy believes there was a strange spring frost. The headmistress knows better. So does Kael, who finds you on the jetty with two cups of tea. {flag(ilse_taken): He doesn't say his sister's name. She went north before dawn, with the man in grey.|{flag(dumont_reunited) and not flag(ilse_went_north): Ilse is further down the jetty, pretending not to watch.|}}\ntext [092bd4] = 湖面波光粼粼，仿佛什么都没发生过。学院里大多数人以为那只是一场奇怪的春霜。院长知道真相。凯尔也知道——他端着两杯茶，在栈桥上找到了你。{flag(ilse_taken): 他不提妹妹的名字。黎明前，她跟着灰衣人去了北方。|{flag(dumont_reunited) and not flag(ilse_went_north): 伊尔泽在栈桥更远的那头，假装没在看。|}}\n# en: The lake glittering in spring sunshine, students skimming stones, a bandaged figure sitting on the jetty.\nscene [c9174b] = 春日阳光下波光粼粼的湖面，学生们在打水漂，一个缠着绷带的身影坐在栈桥上。\n# en: Sit with him in silence\nopt.left.label [ed0b65] = 和他静静坐着\n# en: You watch the water. It's just water, now. Kael's tea, for once, doesn't freeze.\nopt.left.say.1 [c6b7cf] = 你们看着湖水。现在，它只是水而已。凯尔的茶，难得地没有结冰。\n# en: Tell him how you feel\nopt.right.label [cb477f] = 告诉他你的心意\n# en: He kisses you, and for the first time since you've known him, his hands are warm.\nopt.right.say.1 [b9bd38] = 他吻了你。认识他以来第一次，他的手是温暖的。\n# en: \"I owe you everything,\" he says. \"I'm not good at owing people. I'll learn.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [f06ead] = “我欠你一切，”他说，“我不擅长欠人情。我会学的。”\n\n@event hs_aftermath_lost\n# en: The Headmistress Closed It\ntitle [1bb5f9] = 校长关上了它\n# en: You wake in the infirmary. The lake has thawed, and the door is shut: the headmistress came down after you and closed it with her own hands. She is sitting by your bed. Her hair has gone white overnight, and her silver ring is gone. {flag(ilse_taken): Ilse went north before dawn.|}\ntext [29d9e8] = 你在医务室里醒来。湖水已经解冻，门也关上了：院长跟在你后面下去，亲手把它关上了。她就坐在你床边。她的头发一夜之间全白了，那枚银戒指也不见了。{flag(ilse_taken): 黎明前，伊尔泽去了北方。|}\n# en: An infirmary bed by a window, the lake thawed outside, a tall woman with newly white hair sitting beside it without her silver ring.\nscene [e20e7d] = 窗边的一张医务室病床，窗外湖水已解冻，一个刚刚白了头的高个女人坐在床边，手上没有了银戒指。\n# en: \"I'm sorry.\"\nopt.left.label [c7fcad] = “对不起。”\n# en: \"You went down,\" she says. \"Most don't. The door is shut, and I am old, and that was always the price for someone.\" She pats your hand once, like a chess move. You can't stop thinking about it for weeks.\nopt.left.say.1 [a621a0] = “你下去了，”她说，“大多数人不会。门关上了，我老了，这本来就总得有人付出代价。”她拍了一下你的手，像下了一步棋。好几个星期，你都没法不去想这件事。\n# en: \"What did it cost you?\"\nopt.right.label [05910a] = “这让你付出了什么？”\n# en: She tells you, plainly, and you wish she hadn't. The seal will hold for another lifetime. She won't. She asks you not to tell the others, and you don't, and it weighs on you.\nopt.right.say.1 [edb9ba] = 她平静地告诉了你，你却希望她没说。封印能再撑一辈子。她撑不了。她请你别告诉别人，你没说，这件事一直压在你心上。\n\n@event hs_kael_sister\n# en: The Sister\ntitle [e9e230] = 妹妹\n# en: A girl in white furs is waiting at the gates in a private snowstorm. Kael's younger sister, Ilse. \"If my brother won't open it,\" she says, \"I will. Unless you can give me a reason not to.\"\ntext [f83d6e] = 一个身穿白色毛皮的女孩等在校门口，一场只属于她的暴风雪围着她下。凯尔的妹妹，伊尔泽。“如果我哥哥不肯打开它，”她说，“我来。除非你能给我一个不这么做的理由。”\n# en: A girl in white furs standing at the academy gates in a snowstorm that is falling only on her.\nscene [695730] = 一个身穿白色毛皮的女孩站在学院大门前，一场只落在她身上的暴风雪。\n# en: Convince her\nopt.left.label [089b27] = 说服她\n# en: You talk for an hour. Then she's crying, and Kael is holding her, and the snow stops. She stays in the village to be near him.\nopt.left.say.1 [43c937] = 你们谈了一个小时。然后她哭了，凯尔抱住了她，雪停了。她留在镇上，好离哥哥近一些。\n# en: She listens politely and leaves. \"We'll see each other again,\" she says. \"Beneath the water.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [905bac] = 她礼貌地听完，走了。“我们还会再见的，”她说，“在水下。”\n# en: Warn the headmistress\nopt.right.label [084aca] = 警告院长\n# en: Ilse is escorted from the grounds. Kael understands. He says he understands. He's quiet for a long time.\nopt.right.say.1 [1fbc19] = 伊尔泽被护送出了校园。凯尔理解。他说他理解。他沉默了很久。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_ilse.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_ilse.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@thread ilse\n# en: Ilse's winter\nname [45fc77] = 伊尔泽的冬天\n# en: Kael's sister, in white furs, in a village that has never seen anyone like her.\npremise [b06810] = 凯尔的妹妹，一身白裘，出现在一个从没见过她这种人的村子里。\n# en: In the village bakery a girl in white furs froze her coin to the counter. She has Kael's eyes. Her name is Ilse.\njournal.1 [b77a50] = 村里的面包房里，一个穿白裘的女孩把硬币冻在了柜台上。她有一双凯尔那样的眼睛。她叫伊尔泽。\n# en: Ilse traded you Northern frost-craft for news of her brother.\njournal.2 [78fdb8] = 伊尔泽用北境的霜术，换你讲她哥哥的近况。\n# en: When the man in grey came for her in the lane, Ilse went with him.\njournal.3 [ce040d] = 灰衣人在小巷里来找她，伊尔泽跟他走了。\n# en: When the man in grey came for her, she stepped out from under his hand. You were there.\njournal.4 [41f082] = 灰衣人来找她时，她从他手底下走了出来。你就在那里。\n# en: Kael and Ilse talked at last: a careful arm's length apart, then less.\njournal.5 [bbcced] = 凯尔和伊尔泽终于说上了话：先是小心地隔着一臂远，后来近了些。\n# en: Ilse took the dawn barge north, to tell her family it's over.\njournal.6 [ba8cd8] = 伊尔泽搭黎明的驳船北上，去告诉她的家族：一切都结束了。\n# en: Ilse stayed, and sewed a sliver of winter into your collar in case they come.\njournal.7 [5b28ca] = 伊尔泽留了下来，在你衣领里缝进了一缕冬天，以防他们找来。\n# en: Under one snowing tree in blossom, Ilse said goodbye.\njournal.8 [7efd4b] = 在一棵开着花、却独自下着雪的树下，伊尔泽向你道别。\n\n@event ilse_village\n# en: The Girl at the Bakery\ntitle [a7ff00] = 面包店里的女孩\n# en: In the village bakery a girl in white furs is trying to pay for a bun with a coin so cold it has frozen to the counter. The baker is losing patience. You recognise those eyes: they're Kael's. \"Ilse,\" she says, before you ask. \"Don't.\"\ntext [7c5df9] = 村里的面包店里，一个穿白色毛皮的女孩正想用一枚硬币买个小面包——那硬币冷得已经冻在了柜台上。面包师快没耐心了。你认得那双眼睛：是凯尔的。“伊尔泽，”不等你开口她就说，“别问。”\n# en: A village bakery counter, a pale girl in white furs pushing a coin that is visibly frosted across the wood, a baffled baker.\nscene [1cd5c0] = 村里面包店的柜台前，一个穿白色毛皮的苍白女孩把一枚明显结了霜的硬币推过木台，面包师一脸困惑。\n# en: Pay for her bun\nopt.left.label [31d334] = 替她付面包钱\n# en: She stares at the bun, then at you, as if kindness is a trick she hasn't learned the counter to yet. \"Fine,\" she says. \"Thank you. Fine.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [7b1308] = 她盯着面包，又盯着你，好像善意是一种她还没学会破解的把戏。“好吧，”她说，“谢谢。好吧。”\n# en: Leave her to it\nopt.right.label [d94459] = 随她去\n# en: You leave. Through the window you see the coin crack, and the baker's breath freeze in the air. Ilse walks out with the bun. Nobody stops her.\nopt.right.say.1 [1e970f] = 你走了。透过窗户，你看见那枚硬币裂开，面包师呼出的气在空中结成了冰。伊尔泽拿着面包走了出来。没人拦她。\n\n@event ilse_frost_lessons\n# en: A Trade in Winter\ntitle [9ac35e] = 冬日的交易\n# en: Ilse finds you by the frozen pond. \"My brother won't talk to me. You will. Tell me how he really is, and I'll teach you Northern frost-craft. It's better than anything your professors know.\"\ntext [5847bc] = 伊尔泽在结冰的池塘边找到你。“我哥哥不跟我说话。你会。告诉我他现在到底过得怎么样，我就教你北境霜术。比你那些教授懂的都强。”\n# en: A frozen pond at the edge of the woods, a girl in white furs drawing a perfect hexagon of frost in the air, a student copying her clumsily.\nscene [4361bc] = 树林边结冰的池塘，一个穿白色毛皮的女孩在空中画出一个完美的六角霜花，一名学生笨拙地跟着模仿。\n# en: Tell her the truth about Kael\nopt.left.label [63ce17] = 把凯尔的实情告诉她\n# en: You tell her about the cold hands, {seen(astro_kael_roof): the roof at three in the morning|the nights he doesn't sleep}, the bread. She listens without moving. Then she teaches you to freeze a falling leaf mid-air.\nopt.left.say.1 [9069e5] = 你告诉她那双冰冷的手、{seen(astro_kael_roof): 凌晨三点的屋顶|他睡不着的那些夜晚}、那些面包。她一动不动地听着。然后她教你让一片飘落的叶子在半空中冻住。\n# en: Protect Kael's privacy\nopt.right.label [4b5f67] = 守住凯尔的隐私\n# en: \"He can tell you himself,\" you say. Ilse's eyes go flat. But the next day, she's at the pond again. \"Fine. I'll teach you anyway. You'll need it.\" She teaches you badly on purpose for an hour, and then, despite herself, well.\nopt.right.say.1 [976a4c] = “他可以自己告诉你，”你说。伊尔泽的眼神冷了下来。但第二天，她又出现在池塘边。“行吧。我还是教你。你会用得上的。”她故意教得很糟，教了一个钟头，然后不由自主地教得很好。\n\n@event ilse_grey_returns\n# en: The Man in Grey Comes for Ilse\ntitle [4eacc9] = 灰衣人来带走伊尔泽\n# en: A lane behind the bakery, after dark, every window on it frosted over. The man in grey has a hand on Ilse's shoulder and is talking to her softly: \"Home. Your mother is waiting. The door is waiting.\" Ilse's jaw is set and her eyes are wet. She sees you over his shoulder and doesn't call out. She just looks.\ntext [711c1b] = 面包房后面的一条小巷，天已经黑了，巷子里每扇窗都结满了霜。灰衣人一只手搭在伊尔泽肩上，低声对她说：“回家吧。你母亲在等你。那扇门也在等你。”伊尔泽咬紧了牙，眼里含着泪。她越过他的肩膀看见了你，没有喊出声。她只是看着你。\n# en: A moonlit village lane with every window frosted over, a man in a grey coat with one hand on a girl's shoulder, frost and shadow coiling around them both.\nscene [242a3a] = 月光下的村中小巷，每扇窗都结了霜，一个穿灰外套的男人一只手搭在一个女孩肩上，霜与影子在他们周身盘绕。\n# en: Step between them\nopt.left.label [f242e3] = 挡在他们之间\n# en: Combat. He fights like winter. Lose, and he takes her.\nopt.left.hint [a2ab77] = 战斗。他打起来像冬天。输了，他就会带走她。\n# en: Your spell breaks his grip. He looks at his frosted glove with mild surprise, bows, and walks into the dark. Ilse is shaking. \"Nobody's ever...\" she says, and stops, and shouts after him in Northern instead, every word of it a door slammed.\nopt.left.say.1 [dafd30] = 你的咒语打断了他的手。他略带惊讶地看了看自己结霜的手套，鞠了一躬，走进了黑暗。伊尔泽在发抖。“从来没有人……”她说，然后停住了，转而用北境话冲着他的背影大喊，每个字都像摔上一扇门。\n# en: He's breathing hard when it ends, which is somehow worse. When you get up, the lane is empty except for a single white glove in the snow.\nopt.left.say.2 [8dbabf] = 结束时他也在喘粗气，这反倒更糟。等你爬起来，巷子里已空无一人，只有雪地上的一只白手套。\n# en: \"She's not yours to take.\"\nopt.talk.label [fd9e52] = “她不是你能带走的。”\n# en: Charm. Better if you know what she was raised for.\nopt.talk.hint [0bfc43] = 魅力。如果你知道她是为什么被养大的，会更容易。\n# en: You tell him, out loud, in the street, what they raised her for and what she's chosen instead. Ilse says \"Yes,\" very clearly, and steps out from under his hand. He looks at the two of you for a long moment, then bows and walks into the dark.\nopt.talk.say.1 [0a2903] = 你当街大声告诉他，他们把她养大是为了什么，而她如今选择了什么。伊尔泽清清楚楚地说了一声“是”，从他手底下走了出来。他看着你们俩看了很久，然后鞠了一躬，走进了黑暗。\n# en: He agrees with every word, gently, and Ilse's shoulders drop as he talks. When he turns her toward the road, she goes. At the corner she looks back once. In the snow there's a single white glove.\nopt.talk.say.2 [b2069c] = 他温和地赞同你说的每一个字，而他说着说着，伊尔泽的肩膀垂了下去。当他把她转向大路时，她跟着走了。在拐角处她回头看了一眼。雪地上留着一只白手套。\n# en: Run for Kael\nopt.right.label [839eee] = 跑去找凯尔\n# en: Sure, if Kael is here to fetch. She'll know who you went for.\nopt.right.hint [3aba17] = 一定成功，只要凯尔在这里能被叫来。她会知道你是为谁去的。\n# en: Kael arrives like a blizzard, shouting her name. The man in grey takes one look at him and leaves without a word. Brother and sister stand in the snow, not touching, not speaking, until Ilse punches him in the arm and the snow stops. \"You went for him,\" she tells you later. \"Not for me.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [bee3a9] = 凯尔像一场暴风雪般赶到，喊着她的名字。灰衣人只看了他一眼，一言不发地走了。兄妹俩站在雪里，不碰对方，也不说话，直到伊尔泽一拳打在他胳膊上，雪停了。“你是去找他的，”她后来对你说，“不是为了我。”\n# en: By the time you're back with help, the lane is empty. There's frost on the cobbles in the shape of a closed door.\nopt.right.say.2 [8c5d56] = 等你带着帮手回来，巷子已经空了。鹅卵石上的霜结成了一扇紧闭的门的形状。\n\n@event ilse_kael_reunion\n# en: Brother and Sister\ntitle [2c92a4] = 兄与妹\n# en: Kael and Ilse have been in the same village for weeks and have spoken eleven words to each other. You have three cups of tea, a frozen jetty and a very bad idea.\ntext [210c93] = 凯尔和伊尔泽在同一个村子里待了好几个星期，一共只对彼此说了十一个字。你有三杯茶、一座结冰的栈桥，和一个非常糟糕的主意。\n# en: Two pale siblings on opposite ends of a frozen jetty, a third person between them holding three cups of tea.\nscene [d059c4] = 结冰栈桥的两端各坐着一个苍白的兄妹，中间一个人端着三杯茶。\n# en: Sit them both down\nopt.left.label [05e582] = 让他俩坐下来谈\n# en: It takes an hour and two cups of tea are frozen solid. Then Ilse says \"I was scared,\" and Kael says \"So was I,\" and you quietly leave them to it.\nopt.left.say.1 [75d491] = 花了一个钟头，有两杯茶冻成了冰坨。然后伊尔泽说：“我很害怕。”凯尔说：“我也是。”你悄悄离开，把时间留给他们。\n# en: They argue in a language you don't speak, at a temperature you can't endure. But they argue — which, you suspect, is a start.\nopt.left.say.2 [299f28] = 他们用一种你听不懂的语言、在一种你受不了的温度里吵架。但他们在吵——你猜，这算是个开始。\n# en: Let them find their own way\nopt.right.label [a54eb8] = 让他们自己找到路\n# en: You leave the tea on the jetty. A week later you see them walking by the lake together, a careful arm's length apart. Then less.\nopt.right.say.1 [79a710] = 你把茶留在栈桥上。一星期后，你看见他们一起沿着湖边散步，小心地隔着一臂远。后来就没那么远了。\n\n@event ilse_northern_letter\n# en: A Summons From the North\ntitle [2572c4] = 来自北方的召唤\n# en: A letter with a black-star seal. The Dumont family summons Ilse home. \"If I don't go, they'll send someone worse than the man in grey,\" she says. \"If I do go, I could tell them to their faces that it's over. Or they could keep me.\"\ntext [97b031] = 一封盖着黑星封印的信。杜蒙家族召伊尔泽回家。“如果我不去，他们会派一个比灰衣人更糟的人来，”她说，“如果我去了，我可以当面告诉他们一切都结束了。或者，他们会把我留下。”\n# en: A girl holding a letter sealed with a black star, standing at a window that looks north toward far white mountains.\nscene [4e2efd] = 一个女孩手握盖着黑星封印的信，站在一扇朝北的窗前，远处是白茫茫的群山。\n# en: \"Go. Tell them it's over.\"\nopt.left.label [6759bb] = “去吧，告诉他们结束了。”\n# en: She leaves on the dawn barge, chin up. Before she goes, she presses her forehead to yours, a Northern farewell. \"If I'm not back by graduation,\" she says, \"come and get me.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [fae690] = 她坐黎明的驳船走了，下巴抬得高高的。走之前，她把额头贴在你额头上——北境的告别礼。“如果毕业前我没回来，”她说，“就来接我。”\n# en: \"Stay. Let them come.\"\nopt.right.label [ef10a1] = “留下，让他们来。”\n# en: She stays. She sews a sliver of winter into your collar \"in case they do come\". Nobody does. Not yet.\nopt.right.say.1 [3114b6] = 她留了下来。她把一缕冬天缝进你的衣领，“万一他们真的来了”。没有人来。至少现在还没有。\n\n@event ilse_farewell\n# en: Snow in Blossomtide\ntitle [224b90] = 花潮月的雪\n# en: It's spring, the orchard is in full blossom, and it is snowing — gently, over one tree. Ilse is underneath it. \"Graduation's soon,\" she says. \"Everyone's leaving. I wanted to give you something before you forgot me.\"\ntext [ccd97c] = 春天，果园里繁花盛开，却下起了雪——轻轻地，只落在一棵树上。伊尔泽就站在树下。“快毕业了，”她说，“大家都要走了。我想在你忘记我之前送你点东西。”\n# en: A single snowflake falling in a spring orchard full of blossom, a girl in white furs catching it on her glove and holding it out.\nscene [63dad1] = 繁花盛开的春日果园里飘落一片雪花，一个穿白色毛皮的女孩用手套接住它，递了过来。\n# en: \"I won't forget you.\"\nopt.left.label [6d1b23] = “我不会忘记你的。”\n# en: She doesn't believe you, but she smiles anyway — the first real smile you've seen on her. She gives you a snowflake that never melts.\nopt.left.say.1 [48bf7c] = 她不相信你，但还是笑了——你第一次在她脸上看到真正的笑容。她送给你一片永不融化的雪花。\n# en: Invite her to the graduation\nopt.right.label [d72a91] = 邀请她来参加毕业典礼\n# en: \"Me? At a graduation?\" She looks horrified, and then, slowly, delighted. On the day, she sits next to Kael in the second row and cries more than anyone.\nopt.right.say.1 [55ef3a] = “我？去毕业典礼？”她一脸惊恐，然后，慢慢地，变成了欣喜。那天，她坐在第二排凯尔身边，哭得比谁都厉害。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_thornwick.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_thornwick.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event tamsin_second_letter\n# en: Insufferable AND Rude\ntitle [1faa8e] = 让人受不了，而且没礼貌\n# en: A second letter from Thornwick, in the same quick hand. \"Insufferable AND rude. Noted. I'm giving you one more chance, because I'm a generous person and because my roommate bet me you'd never answer. Prove her wrong. T. Reed.\"\ntext [77fe54] = 荆木学院的第二封信，同样潦草的笔迹。“让人受不了，而且没礼貌。记下了。我再给你一次机会，因为我是个大方的人，也因为我室友跟我打赌说你永远不会回信。证明她错了。T. 里德。”\n# en: A second letter with a green Thornwick seal on a dormitory pillow, the word \"NOTED\" underlined three times.\nscene [85442f] = 宿舍枕头上放着第二封信，盖着荆木学院的绿色封蜡，“记下了”三个字下面划了三道线。\n# en: Write back, finally\nopt.left.label [ebbeda] = 终于回信\n# en: You write back, grudgingly at first and then not at all grudgingly. Four pages. The answer comes within a week, with a postscript: \"My roommate owes me a pie. Thank you.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [b76498] = 你回了信，一开始很不情愿，后来一点也不。四页。不到一星期回信就到了，附言写着：“我室友欠我一个派。谢谢你。”\n# en: Let it lie\nopt.right.label [9e1bb5] = 随它去\n# en: You leave it on the pillow. Somewhere across the hills, a roommate wins a pie. You'll never know what you missed, which is how it should be, probably.\nopt.right.say.1 [2d2a7b] = 你把信留在枕头上。山那边的某处，一个室友赢了一个派。你永远不会知道自己错过了什么，大概本该如此。\n\n@event tamsin_letter_gossip\n# en: News From Thornwick\ntitle [ab4189] = 荆木来信\n# en: {Tamsin has written again, six pages of Thornwick gossip with sketches of the professors in the margins.|Another letter from Tamsin, signed \"your rival, nemesis and only friend west of the hills\".|Tamsin's letter is mostly a drawing of their illusion professor as a goat.} {Their head of house has banned whistling.|Someone at Thornwick set the library on fire, a little.|They want to know if Lumenhold's food is really as bad as everyone says.} Answering properly would take an evening.\ntext [b4f018] = {塔姆辛又来信了，六页荆木学院的八卦，页边画满了教授们的速写。|塔姆辛又来信了，署名“你的对手、宿敌，以及山这边唯一的朋友”。|塔姆辛的信大半是一幅画，画的是 TA 的幻术教授变成了一只山羊。} {TA 的学院院长禁止了吹口哨。|荆木学院有人把图书馆点着了，一点点。|TA 想知道流明堡的伙食是不是真像大家说的那么糟。} 认真回信得花一个晚上。\n# en: A letter with a green seal open on a desk, its margins full of tiny unflattering sketches of Thornwick professors.\nscene [23ad0a] = 桌上摊开一封盖着绿色封蜡的信，页边画满了荆木学院教授们小小的、毫不留情的速写。\n# en: Write back at length\nopt.left.label [7bb28c] = 写一封长信回去\n# en: {You write till the candle gutters, with sketches of your own professors. Tamsin's reply calls your drawing of Thorne \"libellous and correct\".|Five pages back, with the castle's gossip and your worst week. They answer the worst week first.|You answer the goat with your own worst professor as a hen, and four pages on the refectory food. Tamsin replies that you've undersold the food.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1db4f8] = {你写到蜡烛燃尽，附上你自己画的教授速写。塔姆辛的回信说你画的索恩教授“诽谤，但准确”。|回了五页，有城堡的八卦，也有你最糟的一周。TA 先回了那最糟的一周。|你回了一幅把你最头疼的教授画成母鸡的画，外加四页膳堂伙食。塔姆辛回信说你把伙食说得太好了。}\n# en: A few lines and a sketch\nopt.right.label [79d945] = 几行字和一幅速写\n# en: {A short note and a drawing of a gargoyle. Tamsin writes back that it's \"the most Lumenhold thing ever sent through the post\".|Three lines. Tamsin replies with three pages, offended and delighted.|A few lines about the food and a goat of your own in the margin. Tamsin replies with a whole menagerie.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7e1d88] = {一张短笺和一幅石像鬼的画。塔姆辛回信说，这是“邮路上寄过的最流明堡的东西”。|三行字。塔姆辛回了三页，又气又乐。|几行关于伙食的话，页边再画一只你自己的山羊。塔姆辛回了一整个动物园。}\n\n@event tamsin_letter_questions\n# en: Twenty Questions\ntitle [87b695] = 二十个问题\n# en: {Tamsin's letter is a numbered list of twenty questions about life at Lumenhold, from \"Is the lake haunted?\" to \"What do you eat for breakfast?\"|Another list of questions. Number seven is \"Who is the most annoying person at your school?\"|Questions again. \"Be honest,\" says number one. \"Number twenty can be a lie.\"} {Number twelve asks about your exams.|Number fifteen asks about the lake.|Number three asks about the food.} Number nineteen just says \"Are you happy?\"\ntext [e5eb74] = {塔姆辛的信是一份编了号的清单，二十个关于流明堡生活的问题，从“湖里闹鬼吗？”到“你早餐吃什么？”。|又是一张问题清单。第七个是“你们学校最烦人的是谁？”|又是问题。“说实话，”第一个写道。“第二十个可以撒谎。”} {第十二个问你的考试。|第十五个问那座湖。|第三个问伙食。}第十九个只写着“你快乐吗？”\n# en: A letter numbered down the margin from one to twenty, each line a question, a student chewing a quill over the answers.\nscene [06c1b8] = 一封信的页边从一编号到二十，每一行都是一个问题，一个学生咬着羽毛笔思考答案。\n# en: Answer every one honestly\nopt.left.label [68babc] = 每一个都老实回答\n# en: {Twenty honest answers, even number nineteen. It's harder than it looks. Their reply begins, \"Thank you for nineteen.\"|You answer the lot. Writing it down, you learn a few things about yourself.|You answer all twenty honestly, even number three, which is about the food and unkind. Number twenty you leave as a lie, as permitted.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5040a5] = {二十个老实的回答，连第十九个也是。这比看起来难。TA 的回信开头是：“谢谢你回答了第十九个。”|你全答了。写下来的时候，你对自己多了几分了解。|二十个你都老实回答了，连第三个也是，那个关于伙食，答案不太客气。第二十个你按规定撒了个谎。}\n# en: Make the answers outrageous\nopt.right.label [2a7503] = 把答案编得离谱些\n# en: {The lake is haunted by a choir of geese. Breakfast is boiled bricks. Tamsin's reply is two pages of laughter and a request for \"the true answers, eventually\".|You invent a Lumenhold of dragons and duels before dawn. Tamsin believes none of it and asks for more.|Every answer is outrageous except number twenty, which is the true one. Tamsin works out which, and writes back only \"I knew it.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [ef7243] = {湖里闹的是一支鹅组成的合唱团。早餐是水煮砖头。塔姆辛回了两页笑声，并要求“以后告诉我真正的答案”。|你编出了一个有龙、黎明前决斗的流明堡。塔姆辛一个字也不信，还要你再多写点。|每个回答都荒唐透顶，只有第二十个是真的。塔姆辛看出来了，回信只写了一句：“我就知道。”}\n\n@event tamsin_letter_trouble\n# en: A Short Letter\ntitle [fc5c66] = 一封短信\n# en: Tamsin's letter is one page, which has never happened. They failed their illusion examination, the one thing they were good at, and their grandmother's theatre has been sold. There's a sentence crossed out so hard the nib went through the paper.\ntext [b7b79a] = 塔姆辛的信只有一页，这从来没有过。TA 的幻术考试挂了，那是 TA 唯一擅长的东西，祖母的剧团也卖掉了。有一句话被划得太狠，笔尖戳穿了纸。\n# en: A single short page with a green seal, the handwriting cramped and unsteady, a blot where a sentence was crossed out hard.\nscene [c5d9d6] = 一页短短的信盖着绿色封蜡，字迹局促而颤抖，一处墨渍是被狠狠划掉的一句话。\n# en: Write back that night\nopt.left.label [bb6a52] = 当晚就回信\n# en: You write until two in the morning: the time you failed, what helped, a list of everything they're good at that isn't illusion. Their next letter is six pages again, and the first line is \"You idiot, you made me cry in the refectory.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [8541b5] = 你一直写到凌晨两点：你挂科的那次，什么帮了你，还有一张清单，列出 TA 除了幻术之外擅长的所有事。TA 的下一封信又是六页，第一行是：“你这个笨蛋，害我在膳堂里哭了。”\n# en: Send them something instead\nopt.right.label [75de79] = 改寄点东西过去\n# en: You send a parcel: Mabel's best biscuits, a book of plays, a drawing of a gargoyle holding a sign that says \"RETAKE\". Tamsin writes back that the gargoyle is on their wall now, and that they passed.\nopt.right.say.1 [866768] = 你寄了个包裹：梅布尔茶馆最好的饼干、一本剧本集、一幅石像鬼举着“补考”牌子的画。塔姆辛回信说那只石像鬼现在挂在 TA 墙上，而且 TA 补考过了。\n\n@event tamsin_letter_asks_secret\n# en: A Small Favour\ntitle [24d010] = 一个小忙\n# en: Tamsin's letter is cheerful until the last paragraph. \"Small favour. Thornwick's duelling captain wants to know Lumenhold's tournament order. Just the order. Everyone does it, honestly. I'd owe you.\" The underline is very neat, as if they did it twice.\ntext [fdb7c9] = 塔姆辛的信一直很开心，直到最后一段。“帮个小忙。荆木的决斗队长想知道流明堡的比赛出场顺序。只要顺序。大家都这么干，真的。我会欠你一个人情。”那道下划线非常整齐，像是划了两遍。\n# en: A letter half-read on a desk, a single paragraph underlined twice, a student staring out of a window at the hills.\nscene [114522] = 一封读了一半的信放在桌上，有一段话下面划了两道线，一个学生望着窗外的群山发呆。\n# en: Send the real order\nopt.left.label [21dd30] = 寄出真实的顺序\n# en: You write it out: who fights first, who last. It feels like nothing at all until you post it, and then it feels like a stone. Tamsin's reply thanks you, too warmly.\nopt.left.say.1 [817ae0] = 你写了出来：谁先上，谁最后。在寄出之前，这感觉什么也不是；寄出之后，它像一块石头。塔姆辛回信道谢，热情得过了头。\n# en: Send a false one\nopt.right.label [bdb397] = 寄一份假的\n# en: You invent an order, plausible and wrong. At the tournament, Thornwick's captain looks baffled for the whole first round. Tamsin's next letter is short and doesn't mention it, which is how you know they know.\nopt.right.say.1 [9998b8] = 你编了一个顺序，合理，但全错。比赛上，荆木的队长整个第一轮都一脸茫然。塔姆辛的下一封信很短，只字未提，你就是这样知道 TA 知道了。\n# en: Say no, and say why\nopt.refuse.label [f281a1] = 拒绝，并说明理由\n# en: \"Ask me anything else,\" you write. Their reply is a week late and very polite. Then, a fortnight after: \"You were right. Sorry. Still friends?\" You write back yes before you've finished reading.\nopt.refuse.say.1 [15d176] = “问我别的什么都行，”你写道。TA 的回信晚了一个星期，非常客气。又过了两个星期：“你是对的。对不起。还是朋友吗？”你还没读完就回了“是”。\n\n@event tamsin_letter_offers_secret\n# en: Their Turn\ntitle [9873fa] = 轮到 TA 了\n# en: Folded inside Tamsin's letter is a second paper: Thornwick's whole plan for this year's contest, every pairing, every trick. \"Fair's fair,\" they've written. \"Burn it or use it. I won't ask which.\"\ntext [570c1c] = 塔姆辛的信里夹着另一张纸：荆木学院今年比赛的全盘计划，每一组对阵，每一个花招。“公平起见，”TA 写道，“烧了它，或者用它。我不会问你选了哪个。”\n# en: A folded paper inside a letter, covered in diagrams of duelling circles and arrows, a student holding it at arm's length as if it were hot.\nscene [cbfb50] = 一封信里夹着一张折起的纸，画满了决斗圈和箭头的图示，一个学生伸直胳膊拿着它，仿佛它烫手。\n# en: Use it\nopt.left.label [77da81] = 用它\n# en: You keep the plan and study it until you know it better than Thornwick does. It will help. You can't quite look at your own reflection while you read it.\nopt.left.say.1 [300161] = 你留下那份计划，研究到比荆木的人还熟。它会有用的。读它的时候，你有点不敢看镜子里的自己。\n# en: Burn it unread\nopt.right.label [f39f04] = 看都不看就烧掉\n# en: You burn it in the candle without unfolding it, and write back that you did. Their reply has one line: \"I knew you would. That's why I sent it.\" You're still not sure if it was a test.\nopt.right.say.1 [672e02] = 你没展开就在烛火上烧了它，并回信告诉 TA。TA 的回信只有一行：“我就知道你会。所以我才寄给你。”你到现在也不确定那是不是一场考验。\n\n@event tamsin_confession\n# en: What I Was Sent to Do\ntitle [f7c453] = 我被派来做的事\n# en: Tamsin's letter is long and the hand is unsteady. \"I have to tell you. The first letter wasn't random. Thornwick set some of us to write to Lumenhold students and report back. I reported for two letters. Then I liked you, and I stopped, and I've been ashamed ever since. You can stop writing. I'd understand.\"\ntext [d82e6f] = 塔姆辛的信很长，字迹颤抖。“我必须告诉你。第一封信不是随机的。荆木让我们中的一些人写信给流明堡的学生，然后回去汇报。我汇报了两封信的内容。后来我喜欢上了你，就停了，从那以后一直觉得羞愧。你可以不再写信了。我能理解。”\n# en: A long letter in a shaky hand spread across a desk, a green seal broken beside it, a student reading with one hand over their mouth.\nscene [71b1c0] = 一封长信摊在桌上，字迹颤抖，旁边是一枚碎开的绿色封蜡，一个学生一手捂着嘴读着。\n# en: Forgive them\nopt.left.label [6c4629] = 原谅 TA\n# en: You write back one line: \"Third letter onwards, then. That's when I started liking you too.\" Their reply is the longest yet, and the first not signed with an insult.\nopt.left.say.1 [b80866] = 你只回了一行：“那就从第三封信算起吧。我也是从那时开始喜欢你的。”TA 的回信是有史以来最长的一封，也是第一封署名里没有一句损人话的。\n# en: Ask what they reported\nopt.right.label [0e7b6d] = 问 TA 汇报了什么\n# en: They tell you, exactly: the food, the weather, your opinion of Professor Thorne. Nothing that mattered. \"I made it boring on purpose,\" they write. You laugh, and then you forgive them anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [232dfa] = TA 一五一十地告诉了你：伙食、天气、你对索恩教授的看法。没有一件要紧的事。“我故意写得很无聊，”TA 写道。你笑了，然后还是原谅了 TA。\n\n@event tamsin_letter_after\n# en: After Graduation\ntitle [f78127] = 毕业之后\n# en: Tamsin's letter has a map on the back with two towns circled: Lumenhold and Thornwick, and a line between them. \"After graduation, there'll be no school to be rivals for. What are we, then?\" Under it, smaller: \"Don't answer that. Answer it.\"\ntext [275f1a] = 塔姆辛的信背面画着一张地图，圈出了两座城：流明堡和荆木，中间连着一条线。“毕业以后，就没有学校让我们较劲了。那我们算什么？”下面一行小字：“别回答。回答我。”\n# en: A letter with a green seal and a pressed flower, a map of the realm sketched on the back with two towns circled.\nscene [4c98cd] = 一封盖着绿色封蜡、夹着压花的信，背面草草画着王国的地图，圈出了两座城。\n# en: \"Friends. Obviously.\"\nopt.left.label [28c81e] = “朋友。显然。”\n# en: You write it in letters an inch high. Their reply has the map again, with a third town circled halfway between: \"Meeting point. Every spring. Bring biscuits.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [2b1665] = 你用一寸高的字写了下来。TA 的回信又附上了那张地图，在两城正中间圈了第三座小镇：“碰头地点。每年春天。带饼干。”\n# en: \"Still rivals. Obviously.\"\nopt.right.label [292e36] = “还是对手。显然。”\n# en: \"Excellent,\" they write back. \"I was worried you'd go soft.\" Under it, very small: \"Friends too, though. Obviously.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [8691d3] = “太好了，”TA 回信说，“我还担心你会变软呢。”下面是很小的字：“不过也是朋友。显然。”\n\n@event rival_return_visit\n# en: To Thornwick\ntitle [7f07af] = 前往荆木\n# en: This year it's Lumenhold's turn to visit. A barge leaves for Thornwick at dawn with a delegation of students who have been told to behave. {met(tamsin): Tamsin has written three letters about it. The last one just says \"COME\".|Thornwick has, by all accounts, better plumbing.}\ntext [88f77d] = 今年轮到流明堡回访了。一艘驳船将在黎明时分载着一支被叮嘱要守规矩的学生代表团驶向荆木学院。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛为这事写了三封信。最后一封只写着“来”。|据大家说，荆木学院的水管比这儿好。}\n# en: A barge full of Lumenhold students arriving at a rival academy of green-roofed towers, a crowd of green-robed students waiting on the jetty.\nscene [88b117] = 一艘满载流明堡学生的驳船抵达一座绿顶塔楼林立的对手学院，码头上等着一群穿绿袍的学生。\n# en: Join the delegation\nopt.go.label [81e864] = 加入代表团\n# en: Two days across the hills. The barge fare is yours.\nopt.go.hint [6f7ab8] = 翻山越岭两天。船费你自己出。\n# en: Tamsin is on the jetty, smaller than their letters and louder, holding a sign with your name spelled wrong on purpose. They show you every corner of Thornwick and argue that each one beats Lumenhold. They're mostly wrong.\nopt.go.say.1 [e034ec] = 塔姆辛就在码头上，比信里矮，也比信里吵，举着一块故意把你名字拼错的牌子。TA 带你看遍了荆木的每个角落，坚称每一处都比流明堡强。大多数时候 TA 是错的。\n# en: Thornwick's towers are green-roofed and smug, and their plumbing really is better. A student in green follows you around the library, asking about the lake.\nopt.go.say.2 [d86a94] = 荆木学院的塔楼顶着绿瓦，一副得意洋洋的样子，他们的水管确实更好。一个穿绿袍的学生在图书馆里一直跟着你，打听那座湖的事。\n# en: Stay at the castle\nopt.stay.label [04365a] = 留在城堡\n# en: A quiet weekend with half the school gone.\nopt.stay.hint [2234e2] = 半个学校都走了，一个安静的周末。\n# en: You stay. The castle is wonderfully quiet. Tamsin's next letter is very short, and sulks, and then forgives you on the last line.\nopt.stay.say.1 [4ce199] = 你留下了。城堡安静得美妙。塔姆辛的下一封信非常短，在赌气，然后在最后一行原谅了你。\n# en: The castle is half empty and wholly peaceful. You read in the window seat all weekend and don't miss Thornwick at all.\nopt.stay.say.2 [638ff5] = 城堡空了一半，也彻底安宁了。你整个周末都窝在窗边座位上看书，一点也不想念荆木。\n\n@event rival_contest\n# en: The Inter-School Contest\ntitle [451f5d] = 校际比赛\n# en: The inter-school contest, at Lumenhold this year, before both schools and half the village. You've made the team. {met(tamsin): In the final, the name opposite yours is Tamsin Reed. They wave from across the ring, apologetically, and then stop smiling.|In the final, a Thornwick illusionist faces you, smiling far too much.}{flag(thornwick_secrets): You know every trick they've planned.|}\ntext [01c439] = 校际比赛今年在流明堡举行，两校师生和半个村子都来了。你入选了队伍。{met(tamsin): 决赛里，你对面的名字是塔姆辛·里德。TA 隔着赛场冲你挥手，带着歉意，然后收起了笑容。|决赛里，一个荆木的幻术师站在你对面，笑得过分灿烂。}{flag(thornwick_secrets): 他们计划的每一个花招你都知道。|}\n# en: A packed duelling arena under banners of blue and green, two duellists facing each other in the chalk ring, a crowd on its feet.\nscene [1dd393] = 座无虚席的决斗场，蓝绿两色旗帜高悬，两名决斗者在粉笔圈里对峙，观众全都站了起来。\n# en: Fight to win\nopt.left.label [0f68e4] = 全力求胜\n# en: You counter every planned trick before it's cast. Lumenhold wins the contest, and the castle roars. {met(tamsin): Tamsin shakes your hand afterwards and holds on too long. \"You read the plan,\" they say quietly. \"Good.\" You can't tell if they mean it.|}\nopt.left.say.1 [053f35] = 每一个预先计划的花招，你都在对方出手前化解了。流明堡赢下了比赛，整座城堡都在欢呼。{met(tamsin): 赛后塔姆辛和你握手，握得太久了。“你看了那份计划，”TA 轻声说。“很好。”你分辨不出 TA 是不是真心的。|}\n# en: Knowing the plan isn't enough: they change it in the ring. Thornwick takes the cup. The plan, in your pocket, feels heavier than ever.\nopt.left.say.2 [7a8a18] = 光知道计划是不够的：他们在场上改了。荆木捧走了奖杯。口袋里的那份计划，从没这么沉过。\n# en: An honest win, hard and close. Lumenhold takes the cup and carries you round the arena. {met(tamsin): Tamsin is on the ground laughing. \"Next year,\" they gasp. \"Next year I'll flatten you.\"|}\nopt.left.say.3 [d0c2e2] = 一场光明正大的胜利，艰难而胶着。流明堡捧起奖杯，抬着你绕场一周。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛躺在地上笑。“明年，”TA 喘着气说，“明年我要把你打趴下。”|}\n# en: You lose by a breath. Thornwick sings all the way back to their barge. {met(tamsin): Tamsin helps you up and doesn't gloat, not once, which you'll never forget.|}\nopt.left.say.4 [668fb9] = 你以毫厘之差输了。荆木的人一路唱着歌回到驳船上。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛把你拉起来，一次也没有炫耀，这一点你永远不会忘记。|}\n# en: Fight clean and generous\nopt.right.label [928af5] = 打得干净又大度\n# en: You fight well and fairly, and when you have them down you let them stand before the next exchange. You lose on points. The crowd stands for both of you anyway, and Lyra, in the stands, actually claps.\nopt.right.say.1 [fbea80] = 你打得漂亮而公平，把对手击倒后，还让 TA 站起来再进行下一回合。你按积分输了。观众依然为你们两个起立鼓掌，看台上的莱拉居然也拍了手。\n\n@event rival_last_match\n# en: The Last Match\ntitle [bf66e5] = 最后一场比赛\n# en: The last match of your year against Thornwick's: an exhibition on the lawn, lanterns in the trees, nothing at stake but four years of grudges. {met(tamsin): Tamsin has asked to be your opponent. \"For the record,\" they say. \"Ours.\"|Thornwick's final-years have brought cake, which feels like a trick.}\ntext [8a2019] = 你们这一届和荆木同届的最后一场比赛：草坪上的表演赛，树上挂着灯笼，赌注只有四年的积怨。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛申请当你的对手。“为了记录，”TA 说。“我们俩的。”|荆木的毕业生带来了蛋糕，感觉像个圈套。}\n# en: An evening exhibition match on the lawn between two academies, lanterns strung between the trees, final-year students in blue and green laughing in the ring.\nscene [d7aa4a] = 两所学院在草坪上的傍晚表演赛，灯笼挂在树间，穿蓝袍和绿袍的毕业生在场中大笑。\n# en: Give it everything\nopt.left.label [607b0e] = 全力以赴\n# en: You win the last exchange of your schooling against Thornwick, and the lawn erupts. {met(tamsin): Tamsin, flat on the grass, laughs up at the lanterns. \"Fine,\" they say. \"Fine. Record's yours.\"|}\nopt.left.say.1 [163d65] = 你赢下了学生时代对荆木的最后一次交锋，草坪上一片沸腾。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛平躺在草地上，冲着灯笼大笑。“好吧，”TA 说。“好吧。纪录归你了。”|}\n# en: You lose, gloriously, and lie on the grass looking at the lanterns. {met(tamsin): Tamsin lies down next to you. \"Record's mine,\" they say, and then, quieter, \"I'll miss this.\"|}\nopt.left.say.2 [9110fe] = 你输了，输得很漂亮，躺在草地上看着灯笼。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛在你旁边躺下。“纪录归我了，”TA 说，然后更轻地说：“我会想念这个的。”|}\n# en: Turn it into a show\nopt.right.label [f31225] = 把它变成一场表演\n# en: You turn the match into a performance, illusions and flourishes and a duel that never lands a blow. {met(tamsin): Tamsin catches on at once and plays along. The two schools applaud the same thing for the first time in a century.|Both schools applaud the same thing for the first time in a century.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cf1fab] = 你把比赛变成了一场演出，幻术、花哨的动作，一场从没真正命中过的决斗。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛立刻会意，配合着演了下去。一百年来，两所学校第一次为同一件事鼓掌。|一百年来，两所学校第一次为同一件事鼓掌。}\n\n@event tamsin_farewell\n# en: Across the Hills\ntitle [d009b3] = 山那边\n# en: At the barge landing, before Thornwick's delegation leaves for the last time, Tamsin gives you a small parcel. Inside: every letter you ever sent them, tied with string. \"So you can read how insufferable you were,\" they say. \"I kept copies. Obviously.\"\ntext [bedb5b] = 在驳船码头，荆木的代表团最后一次离开之前，塔姆辛递给你一个小包裹。里面是你寄给 TA 的每一封信，用绳子捆着。“好让你读读自己当年有多让人受不了，”TA 说。“我留了副本。显然。”\n# en: Two students at a barge landing at dusk, one in Lumenhold blue and one in Thornwick green, exchanging small wrapped parcels.\nscene [ab4aec] = 黄昏时的驳船码头，两个学生，一个穿流明堡蓝，一个穿荆木绿，互相交换着小小的包裹。\n# en: Give them yours\nopt.left.label [e830e2] = 把你的那份给 TA\n# en: You hand over the box you've kept under your bed for four years: every letter they sent, every insult, every postscript. They read the first one on the barge steps and laugh, and the barge leaves with them still laughing.\nopt.left.say.1 [9b6365] = 你递过去那个在床底下放了四年的盒子：TA 寄来的每一封信，每一句损人话，每一则附言。TA 在驳船台阶上读了第一封就笑了起来，驳船载着还在笑的 TA 离开了。\n# en: \"Write to me. Same as ever.\"\nopt.right.label [4bc311] = “给我写信。和以前一样。”\n# en: \"Every week,\" they promise. \"Insufferably.\" The barge pulls away. They're still waving when it rounds the bend, and so are you.\nopt.right.say.1 [056d39] = “每周一封，”TA 保证，“保证让人受不了。”驳船驶离。直到它转过河湾，TA 还在挥手，你也是。\n\n@event intern_thornwick_tamsin\n# en: A Familiar Hand\ntitle [3d7a98] = 熟悉的笔迹\n# en: At the Thornwick lab, the student at the next bench, back from a week away, looks up from a glowing illusion and grins. It's Tamsin. \"They said a Lumenhold intern had come,\" they say. \"I told them it had better be you, or I'd sabotage everything.\"\ntext [60fe67] = 在荆木实验室，隔壁工作台那个出门一周刚回来的学生从一个发光的幻象上抬起头，咧嘴一笑。是塔姆辛。“他们说来了个流明堡的实习生，”TA 说。“我告诉他们最好是你，不然我就把一切都搞砸。”\n# en: A laboratory at a rival academy, a student in Thornwick green looking up from a glowing illusion on a bench, mid-grin, at a visitor in the doorway.\nscene [1fa6b7] = 对手学院的一间实验室里，一个穿荆木绿的学生从工作台上发光的幻象抬起头，正咧着嘴笑向门口的访客。\n# en: Work beside them from now on\nopt.left.label [66b3d1] = 从此在 TA 旁边干活\n# en: The rest of the summer at the next bench. They show you every trick in Thornwick's book, and you show them three Lumenhold ones they swear aren't legal. Your supervisor calls you \"the inseparables\", not kindly.\nopt.left.say.1 [e74fe4] = 夏天剩下的日子都在隔壁工作台。TA 把荆木的每个诀窍都教给了你，你教了 TA 三个流明堡的，TA 发誓那些不合法。你的导师管你们叫“连体婴”，并不是夸奖。\n# en: Keep it professional\nopt.right.label [a540e8] = 保持公事公办\n# en: You work at your own bench and keep your mind on the work. The lab notices; Tamsin notices more. \"Fine,\" they say at the end. \"You were right. But I missed you, at the next bench.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [134fe0] = 你在自己的工作台干活，心思全放在工作上。实验室注意到了；塔姆辛注意到的更多。“好吧，”最后 TA 说。“你是对的。但我想念隔壁工作台上的你。”\n\n@event lyra_on_thornwick\n# en: Lyra on Thornwick\ntitle [06748b] = 莱拉谈荆木\n# en: Lyra has the Ledger folded to the contest results. \"Thornwick,\" she says, as if it were a mild disease. {met(tamsin): \"And you write letters to one of them. Every week. I've seen the green seals.\"|\"I hope you enjoyed humiliating them. Or being humiliated. I was too angry to watch closely.\"}\ntext [4b0bc5] = 莱拉把《流明纪事报》折到比赛结果那一页。“荆木，”她说，像在说一种轻微的疾病。{met(tamsin): “而你每周都给他们中的一个写信。我看见那些绿色封蜡了。”|“希望你羞辱他们羞辱得愉快。或者被羞辱得愉快。我气得没法仔细看。”}\n# en: A proud red-haired student sitting on a bench by the duelling hall, a newspaper folded to the contest results across her knee.\nscene [e50666] = 一个骄傲的红发学生坐在决斗厅旁的长椅上，膝上横着一份折到比赛结果的报纸。\n# en: Defend Thornwick\nopt.left.label [3f76fa] = 为荆木说话\n# en: You tell her Thornwick's people are people. She listens with enormous scepticism. A month later she asks, very casually, whether {met(tamsin): your Thornwick friend|any of Thornwick's duellists} would ever want a practice duel. \"For research,\" she says.\nopt.left.say.1 [c188c8] = 你告诉她，荆木的人也是人。她带着极大的怀疑听着。一个月后，她非常随意地问你，{met(tamsin): 你那位荆木的朋友|荆木的哪位决斗手}愿不愿意来一场练习决斗。“为了研究，”她说。\n# en: Agree they're insufferable\nopt.right.label [8f826f] = 同意他们让人受不了\n# en: \"Insufferable,\" you agree. She looks almost happy. \"Exactly,\" she says. \"Thank you.\" {met(tamsin): You don't tell Tamsin, who would agree with her.|You keep to yourself that the Thornwick illusionist was rather good.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b1271a] = “让人受不了，”你附和道。她看起来几乎是高兴的。“没错，”她说。“谢谢你。”{met(tamsin): 你没告诉塔姆辛，TA 会同意她的。|你没说出口的是，那位荆木幻术师其实相当厉害。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chain_timeloop.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chain_timeloop.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@thread timeloop\n# en: Tuesday\nname [18fbb0] = 星期二\n# en: It's Tuesday. It was Tuesday yesterday, too.\npremise [9c2f10] = 今天是星期二。昨天也是星期二。\n# en: You woke to eggs, and Thorne tripping on the third step, and it was Tuesday again.\njournal.1 [eef44a] = 你醒来，又是鸡蛋，又是索恩教授在第三级台阶上绊了一下，又是星期二。\n# en: Seraphine saw the creases in the day. Tomorrow she won't remember saying so.\njournal.2 [d10032] = 瑟拉芬看出了这一天的褶皱。明天她就不会记得自己说过。\n# en: Every bell in the castle strikes on time except the astronomy tower's.\njournal.3 [f3db1b] = 城堡里的每一口钟都准时敲响，只有天文塔的不是。\n# en: One lamp burns in the astronomy tower before dawn, and goes out the moment the morning begins.\njournal.4 [81d0b4] = 黎明前，天文塔里亮着一盏灯，早晨开始的那一刻就熄了。\n# en: A first-year who dreads Wednesday has written \"one more day\" in his margin.\njournal.5 [1f019e] = 一个害怕星期三的一年级生，在页边写着“再多一天”。\n# en: You gave a lonely stranger one perfect Tuesday. Only you remember it.\njournal.6 [4158fa] = 你给了一个孤单的陌生人一个完美的星期二。只有你记得。\n# en: The great clock in the astronomy tower burst its springs, and it was Wednesday.\njournal.7 [ff07f7] = 天文塔里的大钟崩断了发条，然后，到了星期三。\n# en: Weeks later, the Tuesdays still visit you in dreams.\njournal.8 [4875f4] = 几个星期后，那些星期二还会来你梦里。\n\n@event timeloop_exploit\n# en: Every Bet, Every Answer\ntitle [082f70] = 每一场赌，每一道题\n# en: {loop_exploit == 0: You know which way every coin will fall, and what's on this afternoon's quiz.|{loop_exploit == 1: The bets again. Your classmates are starting to look at you oddly when you win.|The bets, the quiz, the same faces losing. Their groans have stopped being funny.}} {~The quiz is on the founders.|A fourth-year bets you can't name every tower.|Someone bets on which way the prefect will fall.}\ntext [0a1a07] = {loop_exploit == 0: 每一枚硬币会落向哪面，今天下午的小测考什么，你都知道。|{loop_exploit == 1: 又是那些赌局。你赢的时候，同学们开始用奇怪的眼神看你了。|赌局、小测、同样输掉的面孔。他们的哀叹已经不好笑了。}} {~小测考的是创始者。|一个四年级生跟你打赌，说你叫不全每一座塔的名字。|有人赌级长会往哪边摔。}\n# en: A student at the centre of a crowd in the courtyard collecting coins from groaning classmates, a quiz paper marked full marks in their other hand.\nscene [a57222] = 庭院里，一个学生被人群围在中间，从哀叹的同学手里收着硬币，另一只手拿着一张满分的小测卷。\n# en: Take everything you can\nopt.left.label [0c2b42] = 能拿的全拿\n# en: You win it all again, and the winning has a taste like tin. The loop tightens: the morning is shorter now, the edges closer. And someone is watching you across the courtyard as if they know.\nopt.left.say.1 [4bf76c] = 你又赢了个精光，胜利的滋味像锡。循环收紧了：早晨更短了，边缘更近了。而庭院对面有人正看着你，好像知道些什么。\n# en: {~The coins are real when you wake, and the marks stay in the ledger. So does the hollow feeling, a little deeper each time.|You win again. The crowns carry over into the next Tuesday. So does the weight of them.|You win the bet on the prefect, who falls exactly where you knew. The coins carry over; so does the look on the prefect's face.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7f5156] = {~醒来时硬币是真的，分数也留在成绩册上。那种空落落的感觉也留着，每次都更深一点。|你又赢了。金币带进了下一个星期二。它们的分量也是。|你赌赢了级长那一局，级长正好摔在你料到的地方。硬币带进了下一天；级长脸上的表情也是。}\n# en: Just the quiz, then stop\nopt.right.label [938eb6] = 只做小测，然后收手\n# en: {~Full marks, and nothing else. It's a small cheat, and it still sits badly at supper.|You ace the quiz and leave the bets alone. A small mercy, to yourself mostly.|You take the quiz and leave the bet on the prefect alone. You don't even watch the fall.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3f4c96] = {~满分，别的什么都不要。这只是个小小的作弊，晚饭时还是让你不舒服。|你考了满分，没碰那些赌局。一点小小的仁慈，主要是对你自己。|你参加了小测，没碰级长那一局。你连级长摔倒都没看。}\n\n@event timeloop_noticed\n# en: \"You've Done This Before\"\ntitle [54cfff] = “你以前做过这个”\n# en: Seraphine catches your sleeve in the corridor. Her eyes are very clear. \"You've done this before,\" she says. \"Today. Many times. I can see the creases in it.\" She doesn't let go. \"Whatever you're taking from it, it's taking from you too.\"\ntext [1b521a] = 瑟拉芬在走廊里拉住你的袖子。她的眼睛非常清澈。“你以前做过这个，”她说。“今天。很多次。我能看见它上面的折痕。”她没有松手。“不管你从里面拿走了什么，它也在从你身上拿走东西。”\n# en: A seer catching a student's sleeve in a crowded corridor, her eyes very clear, the other students blurred around them as if moving too fast.\nscene [9ea92b] = 一位预言者在拥挤的走廊里拉住一个学生的袖子，她的眼睛非常清澈，周围的学生模糊成一片，仿佛走得太快。\n# en: Tell her everything\nopt.left.label [92d56a] = 全都告诉她\n# en: You tell her, all of it, in a window seat. She listens and then says, \"Ask the clocks in the astronomy tower.\" Tomorrow she won't remember. Today, she holds your hand like she will.\nopt.left.say.1 [a61455] = 你在一个窗边座位上把一切都告诉了她。她听完说：“去问天文塔里的钟。”明天她就不会记得了。今天，她握着你的手，就像她会记得一样。\n# en: Pull away\nopt.right.label [1414c0] = 挣开\n# en: You pull free and walk fast. She doesn't follow. \"I'll forget this,\" she calls after you. \"You won't.\" She's right, and that's the worst of it.\nopt.right.say.1 [8fd623] = 你挣脱开，快步走开。她没有追上来。“我会忘了这件事，”她在你身后喊。“你不会。”她说得对，这才是最糟的。\n\n@event timeloop_dare_confess\n# en: Things You'd Never Say\ntitle [06d735] = 永远说不出口的话\n# en: {Nothing today will last. So you find the person you've never told, and you tell them.|Another Tuesday, another confession nobody will remember.|You've confessed to them three times now, three different ways.} {They look at you as if you've handed them something breakable.|They laugh, kindly, then don't.|They take a long time to answer.}\ntext [b12772] = {今天的一切都不会留下。于是你找到那个你从没告诉过的人，告诉了 TA。|又一个星期二，又一次没人会记得的告白。|你已经向 TA 告白了三次，三种不同的说法。} {TA 看着你，仿佛你递给了 TA 一件易碎的东西。|TA 笑了，很温柔，然后不笑了。|TA 过了很久才回答。}\n# en: Two students in an empty corridor at dusk, one mid-sentence with their heart on their sleeve, the other's face caught between surprise and joy.\nscene [76e15b] = 黄昏空荡的走廊里，两个学生，一个正把心里话说到一半，另一个的脸定格在惊讶与喜悦之间。\n# en: Say it all, properly\nopt.left.label [7d3c43] = 好好地，全说出来\n# en: {You say all of it. They say something back that you'll keep for the rest of your life, and they'll have forgotten by breakfast. It's the most bittersweet thing you've ever done.|They kiss you, or hug you, or cry; it's different each time. By morning it never happened. Only you know.|You say it a fourth way. They take a long time to answer, and when they do it's the best answer yet. Gone by morning.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e98d7f] = {你全说了。TA 回了你一句你会记一辈子的话，而到早餐时 TA 就会忘掉。这是你做过的最苦乐参半的事。|TA 吻了你，或者抱了你，或者哭了；每次都不一样。到了早上，什么都没发生过。只有你知道。|你换了第四种说法。TA 过了很久才回答，回答的是迄今为止最好的话。到早上就没了。}\n# en: Say it badly, on purpose\nopt.right.label [a92326] = 故意说得很糟\n# en: {You make a terrible speech, as a rehearsal. They're baffled. You learn exactly which words you'd use if this were real, and file them away.|You stumble through it, and practise, and stumble less. One Tuesday you'll say it on a Wednesday.|You say it badly on purpose, and they take a long time to answer anyway, as if they heard what you meant.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0d926e] = {你发表了一通糟糕透顶的演讲，当作彩排。TA 一头雾水。你弄清了如果这是真的，你会用哪些词，把它们收好。|你磕磕绊绊地说完，练习，再说得少磕绊一点。总有一个星期二，你会在星期三把它说出来。|你故意说得很糟，TA 还是过了很久才回答，好像听懂了你真正的意思。}\n\n@event timeloop_dare_fall\n# en: Off the Observatory Roof\ntitle [44cb45] = 从观星台屋顶跳下\n# en: {If nothing lasts, then nothing can hurt for long. You climb the observatory parapet with the whole lake below you.|The parapet again. You know exactly how the wind feels at the top.|You've done this before. You still can't quite believe you do it.} {The lake is very blue.|Gulls wheel below you.|The bell in the tower is about to ring.}\ntext [e67a8a] = {如果什么都留不下，那就没有什么能疼很久。你爬上观星台的护墙，整座湖就在你脚下。|又是护墙。你清楚地知道顶上的风是什么感觉。|你以前做过这个。你仍然不太相信自己会做。} {湖水很蓝。|海鸥在你脚下盘旋。|塔里的钟就要敲响了。}\n# en: A student standing on the observatory parapet with arms wide, the lake far below, the morning sky enormous.\nscene [6e1128] = 一个学生张开双臂站在观星台的护墙上，湖在很远的下方，清晨的天空无比辽阔。\n# en: Jump\nopt.left.label [1ef32d] = 跳\n# en: {You fall for a long, bright moment, and the lake rushes up, and you wake in your bed to the smell of eggs. Your heart is still pounding. It was glorious, and you'll never do it again. You'll do it again.|You hit the water like a bell. Then: eggs, sunlight, your own bed. You laugh out loud at breakfast and the prefect stares.|You jump as the bell rings, and fall in time with it. Eggs, sunlight, bed, and the bell still in your ears.}\nopt.left.say.1 [eeebdb] = {你下坠了一个漫长而明亮的瞬间，湖面扑面而来，然后你在床上醒来，闻到鸡蛋的味道。心还在狂跳。这太壮丽了，你再也不会这么做了。你还会再做的。|你像一口钟一样撞进水里。然后：鸡蛋、阳光、你自己的床。你在早餐时放声大笑，级长瞪着你。|钟响的那一刻你跳了下去，随着钟声下坠。鸡蛋、阳光、床，耳朵里还回荡着钟声。}\n# en: Just look\nopt.right.label [eee4be] = 只是看看\n# en: {You sit on the parapet and watch the whole valley wake, the same as yesterday, the same as tomorrow. From up here the loop looks almost peaceful.|You don't jump. You count every roof in the village instead, and find one more than there should be.|The bell rings while you're sitting on the edge. You count the strokes, the same as yesterday, and climb down.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d0fe61] = {你坐在护墙上，看着整个山谷醒来，和昨天一样，和明天一样。从这上面看，循环几乎是安宁的。|你没跳。你数起了村子里的每一个屋顶，发现比应有的多了一个。|你坐在边上时钟响了。你数着钟声，和昨天一样，然后爬了下来。}\n\n@event timeloop_dare_kindness\n# en: One Perfect Day for a Stranger\ntitle [7ec626] = 给陌生人的完美一天\n# en: {There's a shy first-year who eats alone every Tuesday. Today you know everything that will go wrong for them, and you can stop all of it.|The same first-year, the same table. You know their day by heart now.|{flag(loop_perfect_day): You've made this stranger's Tuesday perfect before. You do it again anyway.|You've sat with this stranger before. Today you could do more.}} {They get the juice in the face at ten.|They fail the quiz by one mark.|Someone laughs at their hat.}\ntext [89afd9] = {有个害羞的一年级生每个星期二都独自吃饭。今天你知道 TA 会遇到的每一件倒霉事，你可以全都阻止。|同一个一年级生，同一张桌子。你现在能背出 TA 的一天了。|{flag(loop_perfect_day): 你已经给这个陌生人过过完美的星期二了。你还是又做了一遍。|你以前陪这个陌生人坐过。今天你可以做得更多。}} {十点钟 TA 会被泼一脸果汁。|TA 的小测差一分不及格。|有人会嘲笑 TA 的帽子。}\n# en: A shy first-year in the refectory looking astonished at a slice of cake, a note and a flower appearing one after another in front of them.\nscene [62e470] = 膳堂里，一个害羞的一年级生惊讶地看着面前的一块蛋糕，一张字条和一朵花接连出现在 TA 面前。\n# en: Make it perfect\nopt.left.label [26d75d] = 让它完美\n# en: {You move the juice, whisper the quiz answer, compliment the hat and buy them a slice of cake at tea. By evening they're laughing with three new friends. Tomorrow none of it will have happened, except in you.|A perfect day, built carefully, down to the cake you paid for, gone by morning. You don't mind as much as you thought you would.|You get to the hat before the laughter does, and by lunch it's the fashion. By evening three people are wearing one. None of them will remember.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b68e8f] = {你挪开果汁，悄悄告诉 TA 小测答案，夸了那顶帽子，下午茶时还给 TA 买了块蛋糕。到傍晚，TA 和三个新朋友笑成一团。明天这一切都不会发生过，除了在你心里。|精心搭建的完美一天，连你掏钱买的蛋糕都算在内，到早上就没了。你没有想象中那么在意。|你赶在笑声之前替那顶帽子说了话，到午饭时它成了时髦。到傍晚，有三个人戴上了同样的帽子。他们谁都不会记得。}\n# en: Just sit with them\nopt.right.label [0e1419] = 只是陪 TA 坐坐\n# en: {You sit with them at lunch and talk about nothing. They smile once. That one, somehow, you're sure they keep.|An hour of company for someone lonely. It doesn't last. It isn't nothing.|Someone laughs at their hat, and you laugh too, kindly, with them. They decide to keep it on.}\nopt.right.say.1 [673eaa] = {午饭时你和 TA 坐在一起，聊些无关紧要的事。TA 笑了一次。不知怎么，你确信 TA 留住了那一次。|陪一个孤单的人待了一个小时。它不会持久。但它不是什么都没有。|有人笑那顶帽子，你也笑了，善意地，和 TA 一起笑。TA 决定继续戴着。}\n\n@event timeloop_clue_bells\n# en: The Bells Are Wrong\ntitle [9032a5] = 钟声不对\n# en: You spend this Tuesday listening. Every bell in the castle strikes on time, except the astronomy tower's, which strikes a beat late, and then a beat early, as if it's arguing with itself.\ntext [e09433] = 这个星期二你一直在听。城堡里的每一口钟都准时敲响，只有天文塔的那口，晚了一拍，然后又早了一拍，像在跟自己吵架。\n# en: A student counting chimes in a castle stairwell, one hand raised, the great clock face above them stopped between two hours.\nscene [bab28b] = 一个学生在城堡楼梯间里数着钟声，一只手举起，头顶巨大的钟面停在两个钟点之间。\n# en: Follow the sound up the tower\nopt.left.label [d80a5b] = 循着声音爬上塔\n# en: Four hundred steps. At the top the door is locked and humming. Through the keyhole, gears turning the wrong way. The morning folds before you can open it.\nopt.left.say.1 [350d2d] = 四百级台阶。顶上的门锁着，嗡嗡作响。透过钥匙孔，齿轮正反着转。你还没打开门，早晨就折了起来。\n# en: Ask the clockmaster\nopt.right.label [2333c1] = 去问钟表师\n# en: The clockmaster swears the tower clock is perfect. \"The big chronometer up there? Not wound for fifty years.\" He looks at you oddly. \"Unless someone's been at it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [e028ed] = 钟表师发誓塔钟分毫不差。“上面那台大计时仪？五十年没上过发条了。”他古怪地看了你一眼。“除非有人动过它。”\n\n@event timeloop_clue_light\n# en: A Light in the Astronomy Tower\ntitle [762a1b] = 天文塔里的一盏灯\n# en: You wake before the eggs this Tuesday and look out of the window. One lamp is burning in the astronomy tower, the only light in the castle. It goes out at the exact moment the morning begins.\ntext [a928c7] = 这个星期二，你在鸡蛋之前醒来，往窗外看。天文塔里亮着一盏灯，是整座城堡唯一的光。在早晨开始的那一刻，它熄灭了。\n# en: A dark astronomy tower at dawn, a single lamp burning in its highest window, a student watching from a courtyard far below.\nscene [dcbcd8] = 黎明时分黑暗的天文塔，最高的窗户里亮着一盏孤灯，一个学生在远远的下方庭院里望着。\n# en: Be there when it goes out\nopt.left.label [84a88b] = 在它熄灭时赶到\n# en: You run the stairs in the dark and reach the top as the lamp dies. Someone small is curled by a great brass clock, asleep. Then the morning folds, and you're in bed again, breathless.\nopt.left.say.1 [344f2b] = 你在黑暗中冲上楼梯，赶到顶层时灯正好熄灭。一个小小的人蜷在一台巨大的黄铜钟旁边，睡着了。然后早晨折了起来，你又回到了床上，上气不接下气。\n# en: Watch the window all night\nopt.right.label [b3086c] = 整夜盯着那扇窗\n# en: You don't sleep. At three a shadow passes the window: someone small, with a wand. At four the lamp goes out, and so do you.\nopt.right.say.1 [e222fc] = 你没睡。三点，一个影子经过窗前：一个小个子，拿着魔杖。四点，灯灭了，你也睡着了。\n\n@event timeloop_clue_firstyear\n# en: The Boy Who Dreads Wednesday\ntitle [8239a7] = 害怕星期三的男孩\n# en: {There's a first-year who doesn't eat breakfast on Tuesdays. He reads the same page of a textbook over and over. In the margin he's written \"one more day\".|The boy again, the page again. He's in your dreams now.|You sit down opposite the boy before he can leave.} Tomorrow is his first exam.\ntext [8e8bda] = {有个一年级生星期二从不吃早饭。他一遍又一遍地读课本的同一页。页边写着“再多一天”。|又是那个男孩，又是那一页。他现在都出现在你梦里了。|你抢在男孩离开之前，坐到了他对面。} 明天是他的第一场考试。\n# en: A small first-year hunched over a textbook at breakfast, not eating, a note in the margin reading \"one more day\".\nscene [8ec428] = 早餐桌旁一个瘦小的一年级生弓着背对着课本，一口没吃，页边的字写着“再多一天”。\n# en: Ask him about the exam\nopt.left.label [68b305] = 问他考试的事\n# en: He's terrified of it: if he fails, he goes home. \"I wished for one more day,\" he says, and then looks frightened. \"I mean, I just wished.\" He glances at the astronomy tower without meaning to.\nopt.left.say.1 [290d4d] = 他怕极了：如果挂科，他就得回家。“我许愿再多要一天，”他说，然后显得很害怕。“我是说，我只是许了个愿。”他不由自主地瞟了一眼天文塔。\n# en: Help him study instead\nopt.right.label [99b23b] = 改为帮他复习\n# en: You teach him the chapter he's been failing to read. He nearly gets it. He mentions, offhand, the big clock in the astronomy tower, \"the one that grants wishes, people say\".\nopt.right.say.1 [5721a5] = 你给他讲了那一章，他一直读不进去的那一章。他差不多懂了。他随口提到天文塔里的那座大钟，“人们说，能实现愿望的那座”。\n\n@event timeloop_cap\n# en: Wednesday, Whether You Like It or Not\ntitle [a038bd] = 不管你喜不喜欢，星期三来了\n# en: The loop pulls tight one last time and snaps. Somewhere above, a great clock bursts its springs, and the morning comes in like a wave. It's Wednesday. You feel as if you've run every Tuesday at once.\ntext [0ed344] = 循环最后一次收紧，然后崩断了。头顶某处，一座大钟的发条迸裂，早晨像潮水一样涌了进来。是星期三了。你觉得自己像是一口气跑完了所有的星期二。\n# en: A shattered brass chronometer in a tower room at dawn, springs and gears scattered across the floor, a first-year and a student staring at the wreck.\nscene [9c34ea] = 黎明时塔楼房间里一台摔碎的黄铜计时仪，发条和齿轮散落一地，一个一年级生和一个学生呆呆地看着残骸。\n# en: Climb the tower to see\nopt.left.label [834daa] = 爬上塔去看看\n# en: The astronomy tower's chronometer lies in pieces, and a first-year sits among them, white-faced. \"I just wanted one more day,\" he whispers. You sit with him until the clockmaster comes. Your bones ache for a week.\nopt.left.say.1 [f85055] = 天文塔的计时仪碎成了一地，一个一年级生坐在碎片中间，脸色煞白。“我只是想再多要一天，”他低声说。你陪他坐着，直到钟表师赶来。你的骨头疼了一个星期。\n# en: Go back to bed\nopt.right.label [ef6924] = 回去睡觉\n# en: You sleep through Wednesday's breakfast, and it is the best sleep of your life. When you wake, nobody remembers anything, and you remember all of it.\nopt.right.say.1 [684fad] = 你睡过了星期三的早餐，这是你这辈子睡得最好的一觉。醒来时，谁都不记得任何事，而你全都记得。\n\n@event timeloop_after\n# en: Only You Remember\ntitle [bfd163] = 只有你记得\n# en: Weeks later, the Tuesdays still visit you in dreams. {loop_exploit >= 2: People remember your winning streak, though not why, and some of them have stopped betting with you.|Nobody else remembers anything.} You remember the eggs, the bells, the same morning over and over. You could tell someone, if you wanted.\ntext [623b3d] = 几个星期后，那些星期二还会在梦里来找你。{loop_exploit >= 2: 人们记得你的连胜，虽然不记得原因，有些人已经不跟你打赌了。|别人什么都不记得。} 你记得那些鸡蛋、那些钟声，同一个早晨一遍又一遍。如果你愿意，可以告诉某个人。\n# en: A student alone at a window seat on an ordinary morning, a cup of tea going cold, a clock on the wall ticking forward at last.\nscene [236d1b] = 一个寻常的早晨，一个学生独自坐在窗边座位上，一杯茶渐渐凉了，墙上的钟终于向前走着。\n# en: Tell one person the truth\nopt.left.label [f50d5d] = 把真相告诉一个人\n# en: You tell a friend everything over tea. They don't believe a word and believe all of it. At the end they ask, gently, whether you gave back the winnings. You didn't. It weighs, a little.\nopt.left.say.1 [476b55] = 你在喝茶时把一切都告诉了一个朋友。对方一个字都不信，又全都信了。最后，对方温和地问你有没有把赢来的钱还回去。你没有。这让你心里有点沉。\n# en: You tell a friend everything over tea. They don't believe a word and believe all of it. {seen(timeloop_dare_kindness) > 0: At the end they say, \"So you were kind to a stranger, again and again, and nobody knew.\" You nod. \"Sounds like you,\" they say.|At the end they say, \"So you lived one day over and over, and nobody knew.\" You nod. \"Sounds exhausting,\" they say.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a330bb] = 你在喝茶时把一切都告诉了一个朋友。对方一个字都不信，又全都信了。{seen(timeloop_dare_kindness) > 0: 最后对方说：“所以你一次又一次地对一个陌生人好，而没人知道。”你点点头。“听起来像你，”对方说。|最后对方说：“所以你把同一天过了一遍又一遍，而没人知道。”你点点头。“听起来就累，”对方说。}\n# en: Keep it to yourself\nopt.right.label [09de43] = 埋在心里\n# en: You keep it. Some mornings you catch a phrase you once said in a loop, and the person you say it to looks at you as if they almost remember. They never quite do.\nopt.right.say.1 [0e7b0c] = 你留着它。有些早上，你会脱口说出某句曾在循环里说过的话，听的人看着你，像是差一点就想起来了。但他们从来没有真正想起。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/chains_minor.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/chains_minor.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@thread forbidden\n# en: The silver key\nname [d5ce1e] = 银钥匙\n# en: A graduating fourth-year's notebook, in three colours of ink, and a key to the Restricted Section.\npremise [6e6f1b] = 一个即将毕业的四年级生的笔记本，三种颜色的墨水，还有一把禁书区的钥匙。\n# en: Odile Varn left you her notebook, and a silver key to the Restricted Section.\njournal.1 [87f129] = 奥迪尔·瓦恩把她的笔记本留给了你，还有一把禁书区的银钥匙。\n# en: You learned the prefects' rounds from Odile's green ink.\njournal.2 [ad19ec] = 你从奥迪尔的绿墨水里记下了级长的巡逻路线。\n# en: Odile's red ink, underlined twice: never with bare hands.\njournal.3 [f0bb93] = 奥迪尔的红墨水，下面画了两道线：千万别空手碰。\n# en: You locked the door again and hung the key in your wardrobe. Some nights it rattles.\njournal.4 [8fede1] = 你重新锁上了门，把钥匙挂进衣柜。有些夜里它会叮当作响。\n# en: You slipped into the Restricted Section after hours and read by candle-stub.\njournal.5 [7d220b] = 你在闭馆后溜进禁书区，就着蜡烛头读书。\n# en: Odile's last advice: if a book falls open at your feet, don't let it choose you.\njournal.6 [07ea09] = 奥迪尔最后的忠告：如果有本书在你脚边自己翻开，别让它选中你。\n# en: A book fell open at your feet, and you read it until dawn.\njournal.7 [dd7399] = 一本书在你脚边自己翻开了，你一直读到天亮。\n# en: The headmistress herself caught you with that book in your hands.\njournal.8 [6c08d0] = 院长亲自撞见那本书在你手里。\n\n@event forbidden_annotations\n# en: Three Colours of Ink\ntitle [287aa3] = 三色墨水\n# en: You read Odile's notebook properly at last, at a desk within sight of the locked door. Black ink for the shelves, green for the prefects' rounds, red for warnings. Half the red is underlined twice.\ntext [b5e905] = 你终于认真读起了奥迪尔的笔记本，就坐在能望见那扇锁着的门的书桌前。黑墨水记书架，绿墨水记级长巡逻，红墨水记警告。一半的红字下面画了两道线。\n# en: A battered notebook open on a library desk, its margins crowded with notes in red, green and black ink, a silver key lying across the page.\nscene [904394] = 图书馆书桌上摊开一本破旧的笔记本，页边挤满红、绿、黑三色批注，一把银钥匙横放在书页上。\n# en: Learn the prefects' rounds\nopt.left.label [be7ef0] = 记下级长的巡逻\n# en: Easier to slip in unseen.\nopt.left.hint [fd6b1e] = 更容易悄悄溜进去。\n# en: Green ink: the prefect on library duty walks the east stacks at nine and the west at ten, and stops for tea in between. You learn it like a timetable.\nopt.left.say.1 [e877ee] = 绿墨水写着：图书馆值班的级长九点巡东边书架，十点巡西边，中间要喝一杯茶。你像背课表一样把它背了下来。\n# en: Learn the shelf marks\nopt.right.label [9e4e50] = 记下书架标记\n# en: Know what not to touch.\nopt.right.hint [d93e85] = 知道什么不能碰。\n# en: Black ink and red: which shelves are safe, which books are bound in something that isn't leather, and, underlined twice, \"never with bare hands\".\nopt.right.say.1 [06490c] = 黑墨水和红墨水写着：哪些书架是安全的，哪些书的封皮不是皮革，还有一句画了两道线的——“千万别用手直接碰”。\n\n@event forbidden_section_1\n# en: The Silver Key\ntitle [f6e05e] = 银钥匙\n# en: After hours. The silver key fits the Restricted Section door perfectly. Beyond it, shelves of black-bound books breathe softly in the dark. Somewhere, a prefect's footsteps.\ntext [190679] = 过了闭馆时间。银钥匙严丝合缝地插进了禁书区的门锁。门后，一排排黑皮书在黑暗中轻轻呼吸。不知哪里，传来级长的脚步声。\n# en: A heavy iron door at the end of a library aisle, a silver key turning in its lock, a candle held low.\nscene [f9b84e] = 图书馆过道尽头一扇沉重的铁门，一把银钥匙在锁孔里转动，一支蜡烛压得很低。\n# en: Turn back\nopt.left.label [2f9f2d] = 回头\n# en: You lock the door again and hang the key on a nail in your wardrobe. Some nights you hear it rattling.\nopt.left.say.1 [9742c9] = 你把门重新锁好，把钥匙挂在衣柜里的钉子上。有些夜里，你能听见它在哗啦作响。\n# en: Slip inside\nopt.right.label [316541] = 溜进去\n# en: You spend two hours reading by candle-stub. You leave with a head full of things no student should know, and the certainty that you'll come back.\nopt.right.say.1 [ed65bb] = 你借着蜡烛头读了两个小时。离开时，脑子里装满了任何学生都不该知道的东西，也确信自己还会再来。\n# en: Bram Hollis is waiting by the door when you come out. He is smiling. It's the worst smile you've ever seen.\nopt.right.say.2 [84c17d] = 你出来时，布拉姆·霍利斯正等在门边。他在笑。那是你见过最糟糕的笑容。\n\n@event forbidden_bram_watch\n# en: The Prefect at the Door\ntitle [056c09] = 门口的级长\n# en: {Bram Hollis is leaning on the Restricted Section door, lantern at his feet, as if he has all night. Somebody has told him about the key, or he has guessed.|Bram is at the door again. He has brought a sandwich this time.|Bram's watch on the door has become a habit. He hums while he waits, badly.}\ntext [c78094] = {布拉姆·霍利斯靠在禁书区的门上，提灯放在脚边，好像有一整夜的时间。有人跟他说了钥匙的事，要不就是他猜到了。|布拉姆又守在门口。这回他还带了个三明治。|布拉姆守门已经成了习惯。他一边等一边哼歌，哼得很难听。}\n# en: A broad prefect leaning against an iron library door with folded arms, a lantern at his feet, a student watching from behind a bookcase.\nscene [6c3388] = 一个身材魁梧的级长抱着胳膊靠在图书馆的铁门上，提灯放在脚边，一个学生躲在书架后面偷看。\n# en: Wait him out in the stacks\nopt.left.label [33dbcb] = 躲在书架间等他走\n# en: Cold and cramped, but unseen.\nopt.left.hint [b9f103] = 又冷又挤，但不会被发现。\n# en: {You crouch behind the history shelves for an hour, reading by the light of his lantern, until he gives up and goes to bed.|You wait him out, and he eats the whole sandwich first. Your knees will never forgive you.|You know his humming now. When it stops, he's asleep on his feet, and you slip past.}\nopt.left.say.1 [176864] = {你蹲在历史书架后面一个小时，借着他的提灯光看书，直到他放弃回去睡觉。|你等他走，可他先把整个三明治吃完了。你的膝盖永远不会原谅你。|你现在听得出他的哼歌了。歌声一停，他就站着睡着了，你从他身边溜了过去。}\n# en: Bluff your way past\nopt.right.label [24be4a] = 骗过他\n# en: Guile against a prefect who wants to catch you.\nopt.right.hint [8e22c2] = 用诡术对付一个一心想抓你的级长。\n# en: {You tell him a professor is looking for him in the east tower. He goes. You are inside before his lantern reaches the stair.|\"Thorne wants you,\" you say, and Bram's face goes pale. Nobody checks whether Thorne wants them.|You tell him there's a fight in the courtyard. There isn't. He's gone before you finish the sentence.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bd8b7f] = {你告诉他有位教授在东塔找他。他走了。他的提灯还没到楼梯口，你已经进去了。|“索恩教授找你。”你说，布拉姆的脸一下子白了。没人会去核实索恩教授是不是真在找自己。|你告诉他院子里有人打架。根本没有。你话还没说完他就跑了。}\n# en: {\"Nice try,\" says Bram, and writes your name in his little book with great pleasure.|He laughs, actually laughs, and walks you back to your dormitory by the ear.|Bram has heard that one before, from you. He takes your name, and his time over it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [779b9f] = {“想得美。”布拉姆说，兴高采烈地把你的名字记进他的小本子。|他笑了，真的笑出了声，然后揪着你的耳朵把你送回了宿舍。|这套说辞布拉姆已经从你嘴里听过一次了。他记下你的名字，慢条斯理，不慌不忙。}\n\n@event forbidden_section_2\n# en: The Codex of Hollow Names\ntitle [b71797] = 《空洞名录》\n# en: You keep going back. One night, a book finds you. It falls from a high shelf and lands open at your feet: the Codex of Hollow Names. The first page is a list of names. The last one is still being written. It's yours.{flag(forbidden_warned):  Odile's warning comes back to you: don't let a book choose you.|}\ntext [64e882] = 你一次又一次地回去。某天夜里，一本书找上了你。它从高高的书架上掉下来，摊开落在你脚边：《空洞名录》。第一页是一串名字。最后一个名字还在书写中。是你的名字。{flag(forbidden_warned): 奥迪尔的警告浮上心头：别让一本书选中你。|}\n# en: A book bound in something that is not leather, open on a reading stand, its letters slowly rearranging themselves.\nscene [a07d02] = 一本用某种不是皮革的东西装订的书摊在阅读架上，字母正缓缓重新排列。\n# en: Put it back\nopt.left.label [862ed2] = 把它放回去\n# en: You shelve it. When you look back, it has fallen again, open at the same page.\nopt.left.say.1 [5dc723] = 你把它放回书架。回头一看，它又掉了下来，摊开在同一页。\n# en: Read it\nopt.right.label [fb8482] = 读它\n# en: You read until dawn. You learn about the thing under the lake. When you finally close the book, your fingertips are grey.\nopt.right.say.1 [f6835c] = 你一直读到天亮。你了解了湖底的那个东西。终于合上书时，你的指尖变成了灰色。\n# en: You read until dawn and close the book with shaking hands. Some things you can't unread.\nopt.right.say.2 [c47a6e] = 你一直读到天亮，用颤抖的手合上了书。有些东西，读过就再也忘不掉。\n\n@event forbidden_fourth_year\n# en: Odile's Last Week\ntitle [e07520] = 奥迪尔的最后一周\n# en: Odile Varn finds you between the stacks with her satchel already packed. She leaves at the end of term. \"You used the key,\" she says. It isn't a question. \"I won't ask what you found. Unless you want to tell me.\"\ntext [67d7ff] = 奥迪尔·瓦恩在书架间找到你，书包已经收拾好了。她这学期结束就走。“你用过钥匙了。”她说。这不是问句。“我不问你发现了什么。除非你想告诉我。”\n# en: A fourth-year with a packed satchel leaning on a library shelf, talking quietly to a younger student between the stacks.\nscene [91edf1] = 一个背着收拾好的书包的四年级生靠在图书馆书架上，在书架间低声和一个年轻学生说话。\n# en: Tell her what you've seen\nopt.left.label [d36021] = 告诉她你看到的\n# en: She has a warning for you.\nopt.left.hint [a01664] = 她有一句警告给你。\n# en: She listens to all of it, then puts a hand on your notebook. \"Some books want readers,\" she says. \"If one falls open at your feet, don't let it choose you.\" Then she hugs you, briefly, and is gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [05ed28] = 她听完了一切，然后把手按在你的笔记本上。“有些书想要读者，”她说，“要是有一本摊开落在你脚边，别让它选中你。”然后她匆匆抱了你一下，走了。\n# en: Keep it to yourself\nopt.right.label [09de43] = 守口如瓶\n# en: Your secret stays yours.\nopt.right.hint [9d2616] = 你的秘密只属于你。\n# en: She smiles, as if that were the right answer. \"Good,\" she says. \"That's how I kept it too.\" She leaves you her spare candle-stubs and a list of the quietest desks.\nopt.right.say.1 [2312ee] = 她笑了，好像这才是正确答案。“很好，”她说，“我当年也是这么守着的。”她把剩下的蜡烛头和一张最安静书桌的清单留给了你。\n\n@event forbidden_section_3\n# en: Caught\ntitle [63a445] = 被抓了\n# en: A lantern blazes in the doorway. Not a prefect this time: the headmistress herself. She looks at the book in your hands, then at you, for a very long time. \"Most students who find that book,\" she says, \"don't put it down.\"\ntext [cff27d] = 门口亮起一盏灯笼。这次不是级长：是院长本人。她看了看你手里的书，又看了看你，看了很久很久。“大多数找到那本书的学生，”她说，“都放不下它。”\n# en: A tall silhouette in the doorway of the Restricted Section, holding a lantern that casts long shadows over a startled student.\nscene [21af3f] = 禁书区门口一个高大的剪影，手中提灯在受惊的学生身上投下长长的影子。\n# en: Apologise and accept punishment\nopt.left.label [17d414] = 道歉，接受惩罚\n# en: She takes the key. \"Detention, every evening this month; your coursework can wait. And come to my tower on Thursdays. If you're going to learn dangerous things, you'll learn them properly.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [19d763] = 她收走了钥匙。“禁闭，这个月每天晚上；你的功课只能先放一放。还有，每周四到我的塔楼来。既然你要学危险的东西，就该学得像样。”\n# en: Ask her what it means\nopt.right.label [a565a0] = 问她这意味着什么\n# en: A long pause. \"It means something is waking, and it is looking for help. Make sure it doesn't find yours.\" She takes the key, leaves you the candle, and forgets, or chooses to forget, the detention.\nopt.right.say.1 [c0f71e] = 长久的沉默。“意味着有东西在苏醒，而且它在寻找帮手。别让它找到你的帮助。”她拿走了钥匙，把蜡烛留给了你，也忘了——或者说故意忘了——禁闭的事。\n\n@thread curse\n# en: The cold that won't leave\nname [fcb8af] = 挥之不去的寒意\n# en: A cold sits under your skin, and Sister Amaranth's tonics do nothing for it.\npremise [39f3ee] = 一股寒意盘踞在你皮肤底下，阿玛兰斯修女的补药对它毫无用处。\n# en: The cold under your skin isn't an illness, Sister Amaranth said. It's a hunger.\njournal.1 [a1dba2] = 阿玛兰斯修女说，你皮肤底下的寒意不是病，而是一种饥饿。\n# en: Old Maud told you the cure: moonpetal from the Whisperwood, picked with your own hand, after dark.\njournal.2 [440318] = 老莫德告诉了你解法：低语森林里的月瓣花，天黑以后，亲手去摘。\n# en: In the Whisperwood after dark, a stag made of shadow stood between you and the flower.\njournal.3 [f09b85] = 天黑后的低语森林里，一头由影子构成的鹿挡在你和那朵花之间。\n# en: Maud's brew drove the cold out. It tasted of moonlight and burnt sugar.\njournal.4 [4bc96f] = 莫德熬的药把寒意赶了出去。它尝起来像月光和焦糖。\n\n@event curse_cure_1\n# en: A Cold That Won't Leave\ntitle [a74f1f] = 驱不散的寒意\n# en: {&The cold under your skin is getting worse.|The cold is back, worse than before.} Sister Amaranth's tonics do nothing. She looks troubled. \"This isn't an illness. It's a hunger.\" She lowers her voice. \"Old Maud might know something. Don't tell anyone I said so.\"\ntext [fd21ec] = {&皮肤下的寒意越来越重了。|寒意又回来了，比上次更重。}阿玛兰斯修女的药剂毫无作用。她面露忧色。“这不是病。是一种饥饿。”她压低声音，“老莫德也许知道些什么。别说是我说的。”\n# en: A student wrapped in three blankets beside a roaring fire, breath still fogging the air, fingertips grey.\nscene [ecf450] = 一个学生裹着三层毯子坐在熊熊炉火旁，呼出的气仍凝成白雾，指尖发灰。\n# en: Endure it\nopt.left.label [9e8205] = 忍着\n# en: You pile on blankets and keep going. The cold whispers sometimes. You pretend not to hear. Maud, you suppose, will still be there if it gets worse.\nopt.left.say.1 [10575f] = 你堆上毯子，继续过日子。寒意有时会低语。你假装没听见。你想，要是更糟了，莫德总还在那儿。\n# en: Go to Old Maud\nopt.right.label [fb88bc] = 去找老莫德\n# en: Maud takes one look at your fingertips and puts the kettle on. \"Moonpetal,\" she says. \"Grows in the Whisperwood. Only blooms after dark. You'll fetch it yourself — the cure has to come from the cursed.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [aa1b58] = 莫德看了一眼你的指尖，就把水壶放上了炉子。“月瓣花，”她说，“长在低语森林里。只在天黑后开。你得自己去采——解药必须来自被诅咒的人。”\n\n@event curse_cure_2\n# en: Moonpetal\ntitle [676f04] = 月瓣花\n# en: The Whisperwood after dark. Things move between the trees. In a clearing, a single silver flower is opening, and between you and it stands a stag made of shadow.\ntext [b47cd6] = 天黑后的低语森林。树林间有东西在移动。一片空地上，一朵银色的花正在绽放，而你和它之间，站着一头由阴影构成的雄鹿。\n# en: A dark forest at nightfall, a single silver flower glowing in a clearing, eyes watching from between the trees.\nscene [4d6ecf] = 暮色中漆黑的森林，空地上一朵银花发着光，树影间有眼睛在窥视。\n# en: Sneak around it\nopt.left.label [efbf19] = 绕过它\n# en: You creep through the bracken and pluck the moonpetal. Maud's brew tastes like moonlight and burnt sugar. The cold is gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [1fb52a] = 你悄悄穿过蕨丛，摘下了月瓣花。莫德熬的药尝起来像月光和焦糖。寒意消失了。\n# en: The stag sees you. You run. No moonpetal tonight; you'll have to come back.\nopt.left.say.2 [f03c88] = 雄鹿看见了你。你拔腿就跑。今晚没有月瓣花了；你得再来一趟。\n# en: Face the stag\nopt.right.label [c629ba] = 直面雄鹿\n# en: You stand your ground. The stag lowers its head — and steps aside. Maud's brew drives the cold out of your bones.\nopt.right.say.1 [eecd0a] = 你站定不动。雄鹿低下头——然后让开了路。莫德的药把寒意从你骨子里驱了出去。\n# en: The stag charges. You wake up in the bracken at dawn. Maud bandages you. \"Another night,\" she says.\nopt.right.say.2 [056977] = 雄鹿冲了过来。黎明时你在蕨丛里醒来。莫德给你包扎。“改天晚上再来。”她说。\n\n@thread debt\n# en: Mister Grisk's ledger\nname [6a9bf0] = 格里斯克先生的账簿\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat: friend to the temporarily embarrassed.\npremise [c298af] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精：一时手头拮据者的朋友。\n# en: Grisk wrote your name in his ledger, with a flourish that had teeth in it.\njournal.1 [025c8b] = 格里斯克把你的名字写进了他的账簿，那一笔花体带着牙。\n# en: You begged the bursar for time instead, and got a month's grace and a long look over spectacles.\njournal.2 [f34bb4] = 你转而去求财务主管宽限，换来一个月的缓期，和镜片上方久久的一瞥。\n# en: A note on your door: Mister Grisk charges interest on his patience.\njournal.3 [c655e3] = 你门上贴了张字条：格里斯克先生的耐心也是要收利息的。\n# en: Two trolls in waistcoats came to say that Mister Grisk was disappointed.\njournal.4 [82c6ea] = 两个穿马甲的巨魔找上门来，说格里斯克先生很失望。\n# en: You cut a page from the Founders' Ledger for Grisk's client. It had a black star on it.\njournal.5 [86ec37] = 你为格里斯克的客户从《创始者账簿》上割下了一页。那一页上有一颗黑星。\n# en: Grisk told you how his parcels travel: the jetty at midnight, oilcloth, a cobbler's back door.\njournal.6 [bc9dce] = 格里斯克告诉你他的包裹怎么走：午夜的码头、油布、一家鞋匠铺的后门。\n# en: You told Grisk you were done with him. You never saw him again.\njournal.7 [ddeeb1] = 你告诉格里斯克你跟他两清了。从此再没见过他。\n\n@event grisk_offer\n# en: A Helpful Goblin\ntitle [6c54bd] = 乐于助人的地精\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat falls into step beside you. \"Grisk,\" he says, \"friend to the temporarily embarrassed. Sixty crowns, today. Twelve a month for six months. Very reasonable.\"\ntext [266d6d] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精走到你身边，和你并肩同行。“格里斯克，”他说，“暂时手头紧的人的朋友。今天给你六十克朗。每月还十二，还六个月。非常合理。”\n# en: A small goblin in a velvet waistcoat and pince-nez, sitting on a crate in an alley, a ledger in one hand and a quill behind his ear.\nscene [a36b2a] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲、戴夹鼻眼镜的小地精坐在小巷的木箱上，一手拿着账簿，耳后夹着羽毛笔。\n# en: No thank you\nopt.left.label [6c52d1] = 不用了，谢谢\n# en: \"My door is always open,\" Grisk says. You suspect it is actually always locked.\nopt.left.say.1 [1a3e29] = “我的门永远为你敞开。”格里斯克说。你怀疑它其实永远锁着。\n# en: Take the loan\nopt.right.label [e30491] = 接受贷款\n# en: He counts out sixty crowns, writes your name in the ledger and smiles with a great many teeth.\nopt.right.say.1 [35d10d] = 他数出六十克朗，把你的名字写进账簿，笑着露出了非常多的牙齿。\n\n@event grisk_board_debt\n# en: Settled, With Interest\ntitle [3033c4] = 已结清，外加利息\n# en: The bursar's office has stopped sending reminders. Instead, Grisk is waiting outside it with a receipt. \"Two months' board, paid in full,\" he beams. \"By me. A loan, naturally, with a little extra for your pocket. Twelve a month for six months. You can sign here.\"\ntext [72d609] = 财务处已经不再寄催缴单了。取而代之的是，格里斯克拿着一张收据等在门外。“两个月的伙食费，全额付清，”他笑容满面，“我付的。当然是借款，外加一点零花钱。每月十二，还六个月。在这儿签字就行。”\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat waiting outside the bursar's office, waving a receipt like a small flag.\nscene [c90a78] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精等在财务处门外，像挥小旗一样挥着一张收据。\n# en: Sign his ledger\nopt.left.label [d542c5] = 在他的账本上签字\n# en: You sign. He has already paid the board out of it; he counts the rest into your palm and writes your name in the ledger with a flourish that has teeth in it.\nopt.left.say.1 [28c617] = 你签了字。伙食费他已经从里头付掉了；剩下的他数进你的掌心，在账本上龙飞凤舞地写下你的名字——那一笔带着獠牙。\n# en: Beg the bursar for time\nopt.right.label [ba6715] = 去求财务处宽限\n# en: You spend an afternoon on a hard bench outside the bursar's office and leave with a month's grace and the feeling of having been looked at over spectacles for a very long time.\nopt.right.say.1 [1267b3] = 你在财务处门外的硬板凳上坐了一下午，最后换来一个月的宽限，以及被人从眼镜上方久久打量的感觉。\n\n@event grisk_favour\n# en: Grisk's Proposition\ntitle [5cdd5b] = 格里斯克的提议\n# en: Grisk finds you in the library. \"I could cancel your debt,\" he murmurs. \"A small favour. There's a book in the archive — the Founders' Ledger. A client wants a page. Just one page.\"\ntext [816cbf] = 格里斯克在图书馆找到了你。“我可以免了你的债，”他低声说，“帮个小忙就行。档案库里有本书——《创始者账簿》。有位客户想要其中一页。就一页。”\n# en: The goblin moneylender leaning close in a library alcove, pushing a slip of paper across a table.\nscene [64ffac] = 地精放贷人在图书馆的壁龛里凑近，把一张纸条推过桌面。\n# en: Refuse\nopt.left.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Pity.\" He makes a note in his ledger. It looks expensive.\nopt.left.say.1 [84fdf0] = “可惜。”他在账簿上记了一笔。看起来代价不小。\n# en: Do the favour\nopt.right.label [c95c1c] = 帮这个忙\n# en: You razor a page from the oldest ledger in the archive. Grisk tears up your debt. You notice the page had a black star on it.\nopt.right.say.1 [3798eb] = 你从档案库最古老的账簿上割下了一页。格里斯克撕掉了你的欠条。你注意到那一页上有一颗黑星。\n# en: The archivist catches you with a razor in your hand. Detention. Grisk is nowhere to be found, and the debt stands.\nopt.right.say.2 [17b625] = 档案管理员撞见你手里拿着刀片。禁闭。格里斯克不见踪影，债务照旧。\n\n@event grisk_smuggling\n# en: One More Job\ntitle [5823eb] = 再干一票\n# en: Grisk again. \"You were so good last time. I have parcels that need carrying: collected at the boathouse at midnight, delivered to the village by day. Thirty crowns a parcel. Steady work, for steady hands.\"\ntext [e0fb8b] = 又是格里斯克。“上次你干得真漂亮。我有些包裹需要人送：午夜在船屋取货，白天送到村里。一个包裹三十克朗。稳定的活儿，给手稳的人。”\n# en: A crate of clinking bottles hidden under a tarp on a dark boathouse dock, a small goblin silhouetted against the lake.\nscene [539e69] = 漆黑的船屋码头上，一只装满叮当作响的瓶子的箱子藏在油布下，湖面映出一个小地精的剪影。\n# en: You're done with him\nopt.left.label [71da7e] = 你和他到此为止了\n# en: \"Everyone's done with me eventually,\" Grisk says, without rancour. You never see him again.\nopt.left.say.1 [288fa5] = “大家最后都会跟我到此为止的。”格里斯克毫无怨气地说。你再也没见过他。\n# en: Hear him out\nopt.right.label [b1225b] = 听他说下去\n# en: He explains the arrangement: the jetty, midnight, oilcloth, a cobbler's back door. \"Any month you like,\" he says. \"I'm very flexible.\" You walk away with the arrangement in your head, and it won't leave.\nopt.right.say.1 [69c60d] = 他把安排讲给你听：码头，午夜，油布包，鞋匠铺的后门。“哪个月都行，”他说，“我很好商量。”你走开时，这套安排一直在你脑子里转，赶也赶不走。\n\n@thread familiar\n# en: Ember\nname [c95de4] = 余烬\n# en: A fox with a smouldering tail, caught in a snare at the forest's edge.\npremise [1d33aa] = 一只尾巴冒着烟的狐狸，被套索困在林子边上。\n# en: You freed a fox from a snare at the forest's edge. She bit you, and let you bandage her paw.\njournal.1 [85f544] = 你在林子边上把一只狐狸从套索里放了出来。它咬了你一口，然后让你给它包扎了爪子。\n# en: Two months later she was on your windowsill, with a dusty ruby at her feet.\njournal.2 [d42747] = 两个月后，它蹲在你的窗台上，脚边放着一颗沾满灰的红宝石。\n# en: She has decided she's yours. Her name is Ember.\njournal.3 [4f6c9a] = 它认定了自己是你的。它的名字叫余烬。\n\n@event fox_injured\n# en: Smoke in the Woods\ntitle [bf8cb4] = 林间青烟\n# en: At the edge of the forest, a thin plume of smoke. A small fox is caught in a rusted snare, its tail smouldering like an ember. It snarls at you, then whimpers.\ntext [b3132b] = 森林边缘，升起一缕细细的烟。一只小狐狸被生锈的捕兽夹夹住了，尾巴像余烬一样冒着火星。它冲你龇牙，然后呜咽起来。\n# en: A small red fox lying in the snow at the forest edge, its tail smouldering like a dying campfire, one paw caught in a rusted snare.\nscene [b579d6] = 森林边缘的雪地里躺着一只小红狐，尾巴像将熄的篝火般闷燃，一只爪子卡在生锈的捕兽夹里。\n# en: Leave it — it's wild\nopt.left.label [d9cfe4] = 别管——它是野的\n# en: You walk away. That night you dream of smoke.\nopt.left.say.1 [6bc432] = 你走开了。那天夜里，你梦见了烟。\n# en: Free it and bandage the paw\nopt.right.label [8a0394] = 放了它，包扎爪子\n# en: It bites you, of course. But it lets you wrap the paw. Then it limps into the trees, glancing back once with eyes like embers.\nopt.right.say.1 [113ea7] = 它当然咬了你一口。但它让你把爪子包扎好了。然后它一瘸一拐地走进树林，回头看了你一眼，眼睛像余烬。\n\n@event fox_returns\n# en: A Visitor at the Window\ntitle [8da400] = 窗边的访客\n# en: Two months later, a fox with a glowing tail is sitting on your windowsill. Its paw has healed. At its feet: a small, dusty ruby.\ntext [635017] = 两个月后，一只尾巴发光的狐狸坐在你的窗台上。它的爪子已经好了。它脚边放着一颗小小的、蒙尘的红宝石。\n# en: A fox with a softly glowing tail sitting on a stone windowsill at dawn, a ruby glinting at its feet.\nscene [41ab76] = 黎明时分，一只尾巴柔柔发光的狐狸坐在石砌窗台上，脚边闪着一颗红宝石。\n# en: Shoo it away\nopt.left.label [01018d] = 把它赶走\n# en: It leaves the ruby and vanishes. You sell the ruby. Somehow you feel you've failed a test.\nopt.left.say.1 [4959d7] = 它留下红宝石就消失了。你卖掉了红宝石。不知为何，你觉得自己没通过某种考验。\n# en: Open the window\nopt.right.label [15dfe6] = 打开窗户\n# en: It hops in, inspects your room thoroughly and falls asleep on your pillow. Your pillow is now slightly scorched. You don't mind.\nopt.right.say.1 [d3c253] = 它跳进来，把你的房间仔仔细细检查了一遍，然后在你的枕头上睡着了。你的枕头现在有点焦了。你不介意。\n\n@event fox_bond\n# en: Ember\ntitle [c95de4] = 余烬\n# en: The fox has decided. {term: It follows you to class|It follows you through the half-empty corridors}, sleeps on your feet and growls at Bram. Students aren't allowed unregistered familiars. Registering one costs fifteen crowns.\ntext [ccfb40] = 狐狸已经做了决定。{term: 它跟着你去上课|它跟着你穿过半空的走廊}，睡在你脚上，还冲布拉姆低吼。学生不允许拥有未登记的魔宠。登记一只要十五克朗。\n# en: A student and a small fire-tailed fox nose to nose, a soft golden light connecting them.\nscene [f6fbd2] = 一个学生和一只火尾小狐狸鼻尖相对，一道柔和的金光把他们连在一起。\n# en: Hide her\nopt.left.label [ce99de] = 把她藏起来\n# en: You name her Ember. Nobody notices a fox that doesn't want to be noticed.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f2aee] = 你给她取名“余烬”。一只不想被注意的狐狸，没人会注意到。\n# en: A professor spots her at once. You pay what you can of the fee — and Ember is yours, officially.\nopt.left.say.2 [8afb09] = 一位教授一眼就发现了她。你尽力交了能交的费用——余烬正式成了你的魔宠。\n# en: Register her properly\nopt.right.label [9506c8] = 正式登记\n# en: The registrar stamps a form. \"Name?\" \"Ember,\" you say. The fox looks smug.\nopt.right.say.1 [c579b1] = 登记员在表格上盖了章。“名字？”“余烬。”你说。狐狸一脸得意。\n\n@event fox_helps\n# en: Ember Finds Something\ntitle [020478] = 余烬找到了东西\n# en: {~Ember drags you, by the sleeve, through three corridors and a hedge. She's found something. She's very proud of it.|Ember sits on your book until you look at her. She has found something, and she has buried it, and now she wants you to dig.|Ember comes back from the lake with her tail steaming and something in her jaws. She drops it at your feet like a gift.}\ntext [1b03f8] = {~余烬叼着你的袖子，拖你穿过三条走廊和一道树篱。她找到了东西。她非常骄傲。|余烬坐在你的书上，直到你看向她。她找到了东西，还把它埋了起来，现在要你去挖。|余烬从湖边回来，尾巴冒着热气，嘴里叼着什么。她把它放在你脚边，像献上一份礼物。}\n# en: A fox nosing through dry leaves under a hedge and emerging with a purse in its jaws.\nscene [fa286c] = 一只狐狸在树篱下的枯叶里拱来拱去，叼出一只钱袋。\n# en: Let her keep it\nopt.left.label [dacce7] = 让她留着吧\n# en: {~It's a sock. Ember is thrilled. You are thrilled for her.|It's a glove that smells of the stables. Ember sleeps on it for a week.|It's a pine cone, slightly charred. She guards it from Bram personally.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0fa900] = {~是一只袜子。余烬高兴坏了。你也替她高兴。|是一只带着马厩味的手套。余烬在上面睡了一个星期。|是一颗烧焦了一点的松果。她亲自看着它，不让布拉姆靠近。}\n# en: See what it is\nopt.right.label [c3d611] = 看看是什么\n# en: A lost purse. You return it to its owner, who insists you keep the reward.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c1c68] = 一只丢失的钱袋。你把它还给了失主，失主坚持要你收下酬谢。\n# en: A lost page from a professor's lecture notes. Enlightening.\nopt.right.say.2 [3f4db6] = 一页遗失的教授讲义。很有启发。\n\n@thread ghost\n# en: Prudence\nname [a6d175] = 普鲁登丝\n# en: Somebody has been rearranging your socks.\npremise [ea471d] = 有人一直在重新摆放你的袜子。\n# en: Your socks spelled out HELP. You stayed up, and at three in the morning the drawer opened by itself.\njournal.1 [30509e] = 你的袜子拼出了“救命”。你熬夜守着，凌晨三点，抽屉自己滑开了。\n# en: The ghost is Prudence Ashworth: very polite, and two hundred years late with a letter.\njournal.2 [e06024] = 那个幽灵是普鲁登丝·阿什沃思：很有礼貌，一封信迟了两百年。\n# en: You spoke the banishing rite. She faded with a small, hurt look.\njournal.3 [dd2620] = 你念了驱逐的咒文。她带着一点受伤的神情淡去了。\n# en: You promised to deliver her letter to the bakery. She clapped her translucent hands.\njournal.4 [a66229] = 你答应把她的信送到面包房。她拍起了半透明的手。\n\n@event ghost_socks\n# en: Rearranged Socks\ntitle [c1194d] = 被整理的袜子\n# en: Every morning, your socks have been rearranged. By colour. Then alphabetically by colour. Last night they spelled out \"HELP\".\ntext [79fec3] = 每天早上，你的袜子都被重新整理过。按颜色。然后按颜色的首字母排序。昨晚，它们拼出了“救命”。\n# en: A dormitory drawer open in candlelight, socks arranged in a careful spiral, a faint shimmer in the air above them.\nscene [c7a0d1] = 烛光中一只打开的宿舍抽屉，袜子被整齐地排成螺旋，上方的空气里有一丝微光。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.left.label [e2ae0c] = 不去管它\n# en: You buy new socks. The next morning they're arranged by mood.\nopt.left.say.1 [57e0f1] = 你买了新袜子。第二天早上，它们按心情排好了。\n# en: Stay up and watch\nopt.right.label [03406b] = 熬夜守着\n# en: At three in the morning, the drawer slides open by itself.\nopt.right.say.1 [69a0e4] = 凌晨三点，抽屉自己滑开了。\n\n@event ghost_reveal\n# en: Prudence\ntitle [a6d175] = 普鲁登丝\n# en: A girl in old-fashioned robes, translucent and very polite. \"Prudence Ashworth, class of 1793. Terribly sorry about the socks. I never finished my letter. Could you deliver it? The village. The bakery. Please.\"\ntext [fd675e] = 一个穿着老式长袍的女孩，半透明，非常有礼貌。“普鲁登丝·阿什沃斯，1793 届。袜子的事实在抱歉。我的信一直没写完。你能帮我送去吗？镇上。面包店。拜托了。”\n# en: A translucent girl in old-fashioned school robes, hovering by a window, holding a faded letter.\nscene [2de1ba] = 一个身穿老式校袍的半透明女孩悬浮在窗边，手里拿着一封褪色的信。\n# en: Exorcise her\nopt.left.label [eb2bc2] = 驱散她\n# en: You speak the banishing rite. She fades with a small, hurt look. Your socks stay put after that. You feel a bit rotten.\nopt.left.say.1 [940567] = 你念出驱逐仪式的咒文。她带着一点受伤的神情消散了。从那以后你的袜子再没动过。你觉得自己有点混蛋。\n# en: Promise to deliver it\nopt.right.label [32f8b3] = 答应帮她送信\n# en: She claps her translucent hands. \"Oh, thank you! It's only taken two hundred years.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [e468ed] = 她拍着半透明的双手。“噢，谢谢你！才等了两百年而已。”\n\n@event ghost_letter\n# en: A Letter Two Hundred Years Late\ntitle [4de394] = 迟到两百年的信\n# en: The bakery has been in the same family for two hundred years. The old baker reads Prudence's letter at the counter. \"My great-great-great-grandmother waited for this,\" she says. \"She never stopped.\"\ntext [07ef08] = 这家面包店两百年来一直由同一个家族经营。老面包师在柜台前读着普鲁登丝的信。“我的高高高祖母一直在等这封信，”她说，“她从没停止过等待。”\n# en: A cosy village bakery, an old baker reading a yellowed letter with tears on her cheeks, a ghostly figure smiling in the doorway.\nscene [481d5e] = 温馨的小镇面包店，一位老面包师读着一封泛黄的信，泪流满面，门口一个幽灵般的身影在微笑。\n# en: Stay and listen to the story\nopt.left.label [7f246b] = 留下来听这个故事\n# en: Prudence was in love with the baker's daughter. When you return, the ghost is waiting, glowing like a sunrise. Then she's gone, at peace.\nopt.left.say.1 [623354] = 原来普鲁登丝爱着面包师的女儿。你回去时，幽灵正等着你，像日出一样发着光。然后她走了，安详地。\n# en: Leave quietly\nopt.right.label [69ae5f] = 悄悄离开\n# en: The baker presses a free loaf into your hands. That night, your socks are arranged into a heart. Then never again.\nopt.right.say.1 [104d89] = 面包师把一条面包塞进你手里，不收钱。那天夜里，你的袜子被摆成了一颗心。之后再也没有过。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/classes.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/classes.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event pyro_candle\n# en: Light the Candle\ntitle [b66d59] = 点燃蜡烛\n# en: Professor Thorne places a candle at the far end of the hall. \"Light it. From there.\" The class holds its breath. Last week a boy lit his own sleeve.\ntext [249ae3] = 索恩教授把一支蜡烛放在大厅的尽头。“点燃它。就站在那儿。”全班屏住了呼吸。上周有个男生点着了自己的袖子。\n# en: A dark classroom, a single candle on a stool at the far end, Professor Thorne's silhouette with folded arms.\nscene [b8447a] = 昏暗的教室，远处凳子上立着一支蜡烛，索恩教授抱着双臂的剪影。\n# en: A careful spark\nopt.left.label [1455cd] = 一点小心的火花\n# en: The wick catches with a polite little flame. Thorne grunts. From him, that is applause.\nopt.left.say.1 [e34a27] = 烛芯燃起一朵彬彬有礼的小火苗。索恩哼了一声。从他嘴里出来，这就算掌声了。\n# en: Nothing. Then a wisp of smoke from your fingertips. A few people giggle.\nopt.left.say.2 [266366] = 什么也没有。然后你的指尖冒出一缕青烟。几个人咯咯笑了起来。\n# en: Show them real fire\nopt.right.label [c2dc32] = 让他们见识真正的火\n# en: The candle erupts in a pillar of white flame and then settles, obedient. The room goes quiet.\nopt.right.say.1 [d02a9d] = 蜡烛喷出一道白色火柱，随即乖乖地平息下来。教室里鸦雀无声。\n# en: The candle lights. So do the curtains. And, briefly, your eyebrows.\nopt.right.say.2 [ed4c08] = 蜡烛点着了。窗帘也点着了。还有——短暂地——你的眉毛。\n\n@event pyro_ember_heart\n# en: The Ember Within\ntitle [819842] = 心中余烬\n# en: Today's exercise: hold a spark inside your chest and let it grow. \"Fire is fed by will,\" says Thorne. \"And by you, if you are careless.\" It burns. It is supposed to burn.\ntext [9171f7] = 今天的练习：把一点火星含在胸中，让它慢慢长大。“火以意志为食，”索恩说，“如果你不小心，也以你为食。”很烫。本来就该烫。\n# en: Students sitting cross-legged in a stone circle, each with a faint glow behind their ribs.\nscene [c54174] = 学生们盘腿坐在石圈里，每个人的肋骨后都透出微弱的光。\n# en: Stop when it hurts\nopt.left.label [d5afc2] = 痛了就停\n# en: You let the spark gutter out. Thorne says nothing. The silence is a grade.\nopt.left.say.1 [189b77] = 你让火星熄灭了。索恩一言不发。这份沉默就是分数。\n# en: Hold it longer than anyone\nopt.right.label [a7c8d5] = 坚持得比谁都久\n# en: Your vision goes white at the edges. When you open your eyes, your breath is steaming and the stones around you are warm.\nopt.right.say.1 [4859e1] = 你的视野边缘泛起一片白。睁开眼时，你呼出的气在冒白烟，周围的石头都是温热的。\n\n@event pyro_lyra_spar\n# en: A Challenge in Class\ntitle [68aa64] = 课堂上的挑战\n# en: \"Professor, I'd like a demonstration partner.\" Lyra Vaneshade points her wand at you without looking. \"That one.\"\ntext [c4c320] = “教授，我想要一个演示搭档。”莱拉·凡妮希德看都没看，就把魔杖指向了你。“就那个。”\n# en: Two students facing each other across a scorched practice ring; one is a tall girl with crimson hair, flames curling around her wrists.\nscene [377b0a] = 两名学生隔着烧焦的练习圈对峙；其中一个是高挑的红发女孩，火焰缠绕在她的手腕上。\n# en: Politely decline\nopt.left.label [8a601d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: \"As expected,\" Lyra says, and turns away. It stings worse than a burn would.\nopt.left.say.1 [6071b1] = “果然。”莱拉说完便转过身去。这比被火烫还疼。\n# en: Step into the ring\nopt.right.label [bf566a] = 走进圈内\n# en: Your fire meets hers and holds. When Thorne calls time, Lyra is breathing hard. \"Again. Next week,\" she says.\nopt.right.say.1 [4d681f] = 你的火焰迎上她的，稳稳顶住。索恩喊停时，莱拉气喘吁吁。“再来。下周。”她说。\n# en: She wins in under a minute — but she looks at you afterwards like she's filing you away for later.\nopt.right.say.2 [ab3e00] = 她不到一分钟就赢了——但她事后看你的眼神，像是把你归档留待日后处理。\n\n@event tide_lake_lesson\n# en: The Lake Answers\ntitle [146cec] = 湖水的回应\n# en: Waist-deep in the freezing lake at dawn, Professor Marisol tells you to ask the water to rise. \"Ask,\" she says. \"Never demand. The lake remembers rudeness.\"\ntext [781568] = 黎明时分，你站在齐腰深的冰冷湖水里，玛丽索尔教授让你请求湖水升起。“是请求，”她说，“永远别命令。湖会记仇。”\n# en: Students standing waist-deep in a silver lake at dawn, ripples spiralling out from their outstretched hands.\nscene [398563] = 黎明时分，学生们站在齐腰深的银色湖水中，涟漪从他们伸出的手掌间旋转着荡开。\n# en: Command it anyway\nopt.left.label [400388] = 偏要命令它\n# en: The water rises in a shivering column. Marisol raises an eyebrow. \"Effective. Rude.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [4cfa1b] = 湖水升起一道颤抖的水柱。玛丽索尔挑了挑眉。“有效。无礼。”\n# en: The lake declines, and expresses its displeasure by knocking you flat.\nopt.left.say.2 [412991] = 湖拒绝了，并用一个浪头把你拍倒来表达不满。\n# en: Listen first\nopt.right.label [c35e18] = 先倾听\n# en: You stand still until the cold stops hurting. The lake murmurs — and for a moment you could swear it said your name.\nopt.right.say.1 [17e74d] = 你一动不动，直到寒冷不再刺痛。湖水低声絮语——有一瞬间，你敢发誓它叫了你的名字。\n\n@event tide_storm_drill\n# en: Storm Drill\ntitle [cb32cb] = 风暴演练\n# en: A storm rolls over the lake and Marisol is delighted. \"Perfect conditions! Hold back the rain for as long as you can.\" Most of the class has already fled.\ntext [1b19ce] = 一场风暴卷过湖面，玛丽索尔高兴坏了。“完美的条件！尽可能久地挡住雨！”班上大部分人早已逃之夭夭。\n# en: A rocky shore under black clouds, a few figures in soaked robes holding back a curtain of rain with raised hands.\nscene [04bfa1] = 乌云下的岩石湖岸，几个浑身湿透的身影举起双手，挡住一道雨幕。\n# en: Shelter with the sensible ones\nopt.left.label [897439] = 和明智的人一起躲雨\n# en: You watch from the boathouse, dry and slightly ashamed.\nopt.left.say.1 [061ff3] = 你在船屋里观看，身上干爽，心里略感惭愧。\n# en: Stay out in it\nopt.right.label [f1db6c] = 留在雨里\n# en: You hold the rain back for almost a minute. You also catch Lake Fever.\nopt.right.say.1 [28d2b9] = 你挡住雨将近一分钟。你也染上了湖热病。\n# en: For a full minute, the rain parts around you. Marisol whoops like a child.\nopt.right.say.2 [814a26] = 整整一分钟，雨水在你周围分开。玛丽索尔像个孩子一样欢呼。\n\n@event tide_drowned_bell\n# en: The Drowned Bell\ntitle [5e6210] = 沉没的钟\n# en: Ever since the lake held its breath in Mistveil, you listen for it. During a breathing-charm lesson you hear it: a bell, deep under the lake, tolling slow and soft. Nobody else seems to notice.\ntext [c76f61] = 自从迷雾月湖水屏住呼吸之后，你就一直在留意。在一堂水下呼吸咒的课上，你听见了：湖底深处有一口钟，缓慢而轻柔地敲响。似乎没有别人注意到。\n# en: Murky green water, a shaft of light catching the bronze curve of an enormous bell half-buried in silt.\nscene [7fdf05] = 浑浊的绿色湖水中，一束光照亮了一口半埋在淤泥里的巨钟的青铜弧面。\n# en: Mention it to Professor Marisol\nopt.left.label [dfb6fd] = 告诉玛丽索尔教授\n# en: Her face goes very still. \"There is no bell,\" she says. \"There hasn't been a bell for three hundred years.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [084e01] = 她的表情一下子僵住了。“没有什么钟，”她说，“三百年来，那里都没有钟。”\n# en: Dive down to find it\nopt.right.label [86c496] = 潜下去找它\n# en: Deep in the silt you find a broken piece of bronze, warm to the touch, engraved with a black star.\nopt.right.say.1 [6a378c] = 在深深的淤泥里，你找到一块破碎的青铜，摸上去是温的，上面刻着一颗黑色的星。\n# en: The water closes over you, dark and cold. You surface gasping, empty-handed, with the bell still ringing in your teeth.\nopt.right.say.2 [d6f863] = 湖水在你头顶合拢，黑暗而冰冷。你两手空空地浮上水面，大口喘气，那钟声还在你牙齿间回荡。\n\n@event illusion_mirror_self\n# en: A Second You\ntitle [77533b] = 另一个你\n# en: Assignment: conjure a convincing illusion of yourself. Professor Vey circles the room. \"Most students make themselves taller. Interesting, what people choose.\"\ntext [a41437] = 作业：变出一个以假乱真的自己。维伊教授在教室里踱步。“大多数学生会把自己变高一点。人们的选择，真有意思。”\n# en: A student facing a shimmering duplicate of themselves; the duplicate's smile is a little too wide.\nscene [a36cd8] = 一个学生面对着自己闪闪发光的分身；分身的笑容咧得有点太开了。\n# en: Make it flattering\nopt.left.label [90461e] = 美化一下\n# en: Your double is radiant, confident, slightly better-looking. Vey writes something down and smiles thinly.\nopt.left.say.1 [07e402] = 你的分身容光焕发、自信满满，长得还比你好看一点。维伊记下了什么，淡淡一笑。\n# en: Make it accurate\nopt.right.label [a663ce] = 如实呈现\n# en: Your double slouches the way you slouch and chews its lip the way you do. It is deeply unsettling. Vey awards top marks.\nopt.right.say.1 [6dfcc6] = 你的分身和你一样驼着背，和你一样咬着嘴唇。这让人非常不安。维伊给了满分。\n\n@event illusion_professor_prank\n# en: The Professor's Face\ntitle [795234] = 教授的脸\n# en: Your classmates dare you: wear a glamour of Professor Thorne and cancel tomorrow's Arcana lecture. \"Nobody will know,\" says someone who will definitely tell.\ntext [f7081e] = 同学们怂恿你：用幻术变成索恩教授的模样，取消明天的奥术课。“没人会发现的。”说这话的人一定会去告密。\n# en: A student wearing a shimmering glamour of a stern old professor, classmates stifling laughter behind a door.\nscene [6c7f98] = 一个学生顶着严厉老教授的闪烁幻象，同学们躲在门后憋着笑。\n# en: Absolutely not\nopt.left.label [c1af76] = 绝对不行\n# en: Wise. Someone else tries it the next day and spends a week scrubbing gargoyles.\nopt.left.say.1 [2654ad] = 明智。第二天另一个人试了，结果刷了一个星期的石像鬼。\n# en: Do it\nopt.right.label [f29ea1] = 干了\n# en: \"Class is cancelled,\" you announce in Thorne's gravel voice. The cheering is audible from three towers away.\nopt.right.say.1 [52cb62] = “今天停课。”你用索恩那砂砾般的嗓音宣布。欢呼声在三座塔外都听得见。\n# en: Halfway through, the glamour flickers. The real Thorne is standing in the doorway. He is not laughing.\nopt.right.say.2 [c3574a] = 演到一半，幻术闪了一下。真正的索恩就站在门口。他没有笑。\n\n@event illusion_dragon\n# en: The Courtyard Dragon\ntitle [e608ce] = 庭院里的龙\n# en: For your term project you must create an illusion that fools a crowd. You could make something small and flawless. Or you could put a dragon in the courtyard at lunchtime.\ntext [47084a] = 你的学期作业是制造一个能骗过一群人的幻象。你可以做个小而完美的东西。或者，你可以在午饭时间往庭院里放一条龙。\n# en: A vast translucent dragon coiled around the academy's central fountain, students frozen mid-step beneath it.\nscene [56332c] = 一条巨大的半透明的龙盘绕在学院中央喷泉上，下方的学生们僵在原地。\n# en: Small and flawless\nopt.left.label [1a8234] = 小而完美\n# en: A single butterfly that nobody can tell is fake. Vey taps it with her wand and it bursts into light. \"Perfect.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [b8332b] = 一只谁也看不出是假的蝴蝶。维伊用魔杖轻轻一点，它化作一团光。“完美。”\n# en: Dragon. Obviously.\nopt.right.label [602404] = 龙。当然是龙。\n# en: The dragon uncurls around the fountain and yawns. Forty students scream. Vey laughs until she cries.\nopt.right.say.1 [617c57] = 龙绕着喷泉舒展身体，打了个哈欠。四十个学生尖叫起来。维伊笑得眼泪都出来了。\n# en: The dragon is magnificent for four seconds and then sneezes into a cloud of glitter. The headmistress was eating lunch below it.\nopt.right.say.2 [f1e819] = 那条龙壮观了四秒钟，然后打了个喷嚏，化作一团亮片。院长当时正在下面吃午饭。\n\n@event alch_first_brew\n# en: Draught of Mild Courage\ntitle [da711f] = 微勇药剂\n# en: The recipe is on the board: Draught of Mild Courage. Seven ingredients, three stirs clockwise, one counter-clockwise, and never — never — add the newt eye early.\ntext [74f236] = 配方写在黑板上：微勇药剂。七种原料，顺时针搅三下，逆时针一下，而且绝不——绝不——能提前放蝾螈眼。\n# en: Rows of bubbling cauldrons in a low stone room, each giving off a different coloured steam.\nscene [88ce92] = 低矮的石室里一排排冒泡的坩埚，每一口都升起不同颜色的蒸汽。\n# en: Follow the recipe exactly\nopt.left.label [ae651f] = 严格按配方来\n# en: A clear, honey-coloured brew. The professor sniffs it and nods. Safe, solid, unremarkable.\nopt.left.say.1 [6cc5ac] = 一锅清澈的蜂蜜色药剂。教授闻了闻，点点头。安全、扎实、平平无奇。\n# en: Improvise a little\nopt.right.label [99aea4] = 稍微即兴发挥\n# en: Your variant tastes like cinnamon and makes people brave enough to ask for things. A fourth-year buys the whole flask.\nopt.right.say.1 [228a65] = 你的改良版尝起来有肉桂味，能让人鼓起勇气开口要东西。一个四年级生把整瓶都买走了。\n# en: You taste-test. Mistake. For the next month, everything you say comes out as a deep, wet croak.\nopt.right.say.2 [875ffe] = 你亲口尝了一下。失误。接下来一个月，你说的每句话都变成了低沉潮湿的呱呱声。\n\n@event alch_unstable_cauldron\n# en: Unstable Cauldron\ntitle [69d2ed] = 不稳定的坩埚\n# en: The cauldron next to yours starts to shudder. Its owner, a terrified first-year, has added the newt eye early. It is going to blow.\ntext [e609c0] = 你旁边的坩埚开始颤抖。它的主人，一个吓坏了的一年级生，提前放了蝾螈眼。它要炸了。\n# en: A cauldron shuddering on its iron legs, violet bubbles swelling over the rim, a student frozen beside it.\nscene [444a20] = 一口坩埚在铁脚上颤动，紫色气泡从锅沿鼓出，旁边的学生僵住了。\n# en: Dive for cover\nopt.left.label [5f718b] = 扑倒找掩护\n# en: The explosion paints the ceiling violet. Nobody is hurt. Everybody is purple.\nopt.left.say.1 [c25dd0] = 爆炸把天花板染成了紫色。没人受伤。所有人都变紫了。\n# en: Try to neutralise it\nopt.right.label [cd26e0] = 试着中和它\n# en: A pinch of salt, a drop of moonwater, a quick counter-stir. The bubbling dies. The first-year bursts into grateful tears.\nopt.right.say.1 [21799b] = 一撮盐，一滴月光水，飞快地反向搅动。冒泡停了。那个一年级生感激得哭了出来。\n# en: The cauldron goes off in your face. You spend a week with violet eyebrows and a ringing in your ears.\nopt.right.say.2 [2f0478] = 坩埚在你脸上炸开了。你顶着紫色眉毛、耳朵嗡嗡响地过了一个星期。\n\n@event alch_corvin_market\n# en: A Side Business\ntitle [5b5ae3] = 副业\n# en: Corvin turns your leftover potions over in his hands. \"These would sell for real crowns in the village. I know a buyer. I take a third. Nobody needs to know they came from a classroom.\"\ntext [179554] = 科尔文把你剩下的药剂在手里翻来覆去地看。“这些在镇上能卖真金白银。我认识个买家。我抽三成。没人需要知道它们是从教室里出来的。”\n# en: A young man leaning on a cauldron table, holding up a vial against the light with a merchant's grin.\nscene [95a8de] = 一个年轻人倚着坩埚桌，把一只小瓶举到光下，脸上挂着商人的笑。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Your loss, saint.\" He tosses the vial back. You fumble it.\nopt.left.say.1 [160723] = “你的损失，圣人。”他把小瓶扔回来。你手忙脚乱地接住。\n# en: Shake on it\nopt.right.label [982713] = 一言为定\n# en: It goes well until a vial turns up in a professor's tea. You and Corvin share a detention and an alibi.\nopt.right.say.1 [f97a4d] = 一切顺利，直到一只小瓶出现在某位教授的茶里。你和科尔文共享了一次禁闭和一份不在场证明。\n# en: Thirty crowns, no questions asked. Corvin winks. \"Pleasure doing crime with you.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [d740ff] = 三十克朗，不问来路。科尔文眨眨眼。“和你一起犯罪真愉快。”\n\n@event div_tea_leaves\n# en: Reading the Leaves\ntitle [0b5484] = 解读茶叶\n# en: You read your partner's tea leaves. The shape at the bottom of the cup is unmistakably a skull. Your partner is looking at you hopefully.\ntext [724f94] = 你为搭档解读茶叶。杯底的形状明明白白是一个骷髅。你的搭档满怀希望地看着你。\n# en: A delicate teacup tipped on its saucer, the dregs forming a shape like a skull — or possibly a mushroom.\nscene [006d77] = 一只精致的茶杯倾倒在茶碟上，杯底的残渣形如骷髅——也可能是蘑菇。\n# en: Tell the truth\nopt.left.label [d8bf00] = 说实话\n# en: \"A skull?\" They laugh. \"Professor says a skull means change.\" They're relieved. You aren't sure you should be.\nopt.left.say.1 [fd9f44] = “骷髅？”对方笑了，“教授说骷髅代表改变。”对方松了口气。你却不确定该不该松这口气。\n# en: They go pale and leave early. The next day they trip down the stairs. Nothing serious. Still.\nopt.left.say.2 [e9a649] = 对方脸色煞白，提前离开了。第二天对方从楼梯上摔了一跤。不严重。但还是。\n# en: Lie kindly\nopt.right.label [542b7a] = 善意地撒谎\n# en: \"A mushroom,\" you say. \"Means good fortune.\" They beam. The professor, passing behind you, says nothing at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [739452] = “是蘑菇，”你说，“代表好运。”对方笑开了花。从你身后走过的教授什么也没说。\n# en: Foretell what it really means\nopt.up.label [ce137c] = 预言它真正的含义\n# en: You say, out loud and quite certain, that the skull means your partner will lose a bet before Friday. They lose a bet on Thursday, to you, and are thrilled the skull wasn't worse.\nopt.up.say.1 [c2eccc] = 你笃定地大声说，那个骷髅的意思是你的搭档周五前会输掉一个赌。周四他就输了一个赌，输给了你，而且庆幸骷髅没意味着更糟的事。\n\n@event div_next_tuesday\n# en: The Mirror of Next Tuesday\ntitle [765386] = 下周二之镜\n# en: The Mirror of Next Tuesday shows exactly what it promises. Students take turns looking. Some come away smiling. One comes away and goes straight to the infirmary.\ntext [3591e5] = “下周二之镜”展示的正如其名。学生们轮流去看。有人笑着离开。有一个人看完直接去了医务室。\n# en: An ornate standing mirror whose reflection shows the room on a different day — the light at a different angle, a different person sitting in your chair.\nscene [abfe7b] = 一面华丽的落地镜，镜中映出的是另一天的这间屋子——光线角度不同，你的椅子上坐着别人。\n# en: Take a look\nopt.left.label [a3cf08] = 看一眼\n# en: Next Tuesday you are sitting an Astromancy quiz. You memorise the questions. Is that cheating? It's at least ambiguous.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a0d2f] = 下周二你在参加星象学小测。你记下了题目。这算作弊吗？至少说不清。\n# en: Next Tuesday you are crying in the rain outside the observatory. You don't know why. You spend the week dreading it.\nopt.left.say.2 [004b37] = 下周二你在天文台外的雨里哭泣。你不知道为什么。接下来一整周你都提心吊胆。\n# en: Decline\nopt.right.label [eaef39] = 不看了\n# en: \"I'd rather be surprised.\" The professor smiles as if you have passed a different test entirely.\nopt.right.say.1 [18dfd0] = “我宁愿到时候再惊喜。”教授笑了，好像你通过了另一场完全不同的考试。\n\n@event div_seraphine_vision\n# en: She Grabs Your Hand\ntitle [a002e6] = 她抓住了你的手\n# en: In the middle of crystal-gazing, Seraphine Hale seizes your wrist. Her eyes have gone silver. \"The door under the lake,\" she whispers. \"Don't let him open it. Or do. I can't see which.\"\ntext [52e443] = 正凝视水晶球时，瑟拉芬·海尔一把抓住你的手腕。她的眼睛变成了银色。“湖下的门，”她低语，“别让他打开它。或者让他打开。我看不清是哪个。”\n# en: A dreamy-eyed girl gripping a classmate's wrist across a table of crystal balls, her pupils wide and silver.\nscene [bda6a7] = 一个眼神迷离的女孩隔着摆满水晶球的桌子抓住同学的手腕，瞳孔放大，泛着银光。\n# en: Pull away\nopt.left.label [1414c0] = 抽回手\n# en: She blinks and lets go. \"Sorry. That happens.\" She goes back to her crystal as if nothing occurred.\nopt.left.say.1 [d082da] = 她眨了眨眼，松开了手。“抱歉。偶尔会这样。”她若无其事地继续看她的水晶球。\n# en: Ask who she means\nopt.right.label [301f4e] = 问她说的是谁\n# en: \"Someone cold,\" she says. \"Someone kind.\" Then she shakes her head, smiles vaguely, and asks if you'd like a biscuit.\nopt.right.say.1 [c004e1] = “一个冰冷的人，”她说，“一个温柔的人。”然后她摇摇头，迷迷糊糊地笑了笑，问你要不要来块饼干。\n\n@event art_clockwork_beetle\n# en: The Clockwork Beetle\ntitle [8b7d56] = 发条甲虫\n# en: Your project: a brass beetle that walks. The gears are in. The spring is wound. You could make it efficient, or you could give it a little personality.\ntext [982bd2] = 你的作业：一只会走路的黄铜甲虫。齿轮装好了。发条上紧了。你可以让它高效，也可以给它一点个性。\n# en: A brass beetle the size of a fist on a workbench, tiny gears visible through its translucent wing-cases.\nscene [3cbbcc] = 工作台上一只拳头大小的黄铜甲虫，透过半透明的鞘翅能看见细小的齿轮。\n# en: Make it efficient\nopt.left.label [b934bb] = 让它高效\n# en: It walks in a perfect straight line until it hits a wall. Full marks.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f4098] = 它沿着一条完美的直线前进，直到撞上墙。满分。\n# en: Give it personality\nopt.right.label [40ed9d] = 给它点个性\n# en: It escapes during the presentation and lives in the walls for a month, occasionally stealing biscuits. It becomes a minor legend.\nopt.right.say.1 [a6886f] = 它在展示时逃跑了，在墙里住了一个月，时不时偷块饼干。它成了一个小小的传说。\n# en: It refuses to walk unless you compliment it. The professor is torn between marking you down and asking how you did it.\nopt.right.say.2 [550353] = 除非你夸它，不然它就不肯走。教授在扣你分和问你怎么做到的之间左右为难。\n# en: Animate it instead of winding it\nopt.up.label [f9c01b] = 不上发条，直接活化\n# en: You skip the spring and give the beetle a want instead: to find the warmest spot on the bench. It does, and sits there, content, and nothing in the class is wound tighter or happier.\nopt.up.say.1 [cf2421] = 你跳过发条，给了甲虫一个念想：去找桌上最暖和的地方。它找到了，心满意足地待在那儿。全班没有哪只甲虫比它上得更紧、过得更开心。\n\n@event art_mir_collab\n# en: A Joint Project\ntitle [7a3675] = 合作项目\n# en: Mir Ten drops onto the bench beside you with a sketch of something that is either a self-stirring cauldron or a small war machine. \"Partners? Your hands, my brain. Or the other way round. Both, actually.\"\ntext [687dbc] = 米尔·滕一屁股坐到你旁边的长凳上，拿着一张草图，上面画的东西要么是自动搅拌坩埚，要么是一台小型战争机器。“搭档吗？你的手，我的脑子。或者反过来。其实两样都要。”\n# en: A cluttered workbench covered in tools, sketches and one smoking teapot; a grinning student with goggles pushed up into wild hair.\nscene [455820] = 堆满工具和草图的工作台上还有一只冒烟的茶壶；一个咧嘴笑的学生把护目镜推到了蓬乱的头发上。\n# en: Work alone\nopt.left.label [e54a87] = 自己干\n# en: \"Fair,\" says Mir, with no hard feelings, and is immediately on fire. Not badly.\nopt.left.say.1 [461fb1] = “公平。”米尔毫无芥蒂地说，随即就着了火。不算严重。\n# en: Partner with Mir\nopt.right.label [89e907] = 和米尔搭档\n# en: The cauldron stirs itself, hums a tune and only threatens the professor once. Mir hugs you with oily hands.\nopt.right.say.1 [51d7a9] = 坩埚自己搅拌，还哼着小曲，只威胁了教授一次。米尔用满是油污的手抱住了你。\n# en: The prototype explodes. Mir laughs so hard they cry. \"Again! We were so close!\" You find you're laughing too.\nopt.right.say.2 [48c576] = 原型机爆炸了。米尔笑得眼泪直流。“再来！我们差一点就成了！”你发现自己也在笑。\n# en: Build it on the spot\nopt.up.label [7c2686] = 当场拼出来\n# en: You don't sketch it. You ask the bits what they want to be, and they become a cauldron that stirs and occasionally salutes. Mir makes a noise you've never heard a person make. \"Partners,\" Mir says. \"Forever. Probably.\"\nopt.up.say.1 [2c7e08] = 你不画图。你问那些零件想变成什么，它们就变成了一口会自己搅拌、偶尔还会敬个礼的坩埚。米尔发出一种你从没听人发出过的声音。“搭档，”米尔说，“永远的。大概吧。”\n\n@event art_enchanted_quill\n# en: The Self-Writing Quill\ntitle [0d291a] = 自动羽毛笔\n# en: You've enchanted a quill to take dictation. It works beautifully. A thought occurs: what if it wrote your essays, too?\ntext [8d3e40] = 你给一支羽毛笔施了魔法，让它听写。效果好极了。你冒出一个念头：要是让它连论文也写了呢？\n# en: A quill writing busily on its own over a sheaf of paper while its owner sleeps on folded arms.\nscene [789717] = 一支羽毛笔在一沓纸上自己奋笔疾书，它的主人趴在胳膊上睡着了。\n# en: Just use it for notes\nopt.left.label [1cd0cf] = 只用它记笔记\n# en: Your notes are flawless. Even the professor's jokes are transcribed, with a small marginal comment: \"(not funny).\"\nopt.left.say.1 [04dda1] = 你的笔记无懈可击。连教授的笑话都记下来了，旁边还有一行小小的批注：“（不好笑）”。\n# en: Let it write your essays\nopt.right.label [904901] = 让它替你写论文\n# en: Your essays arrive on time, in impeccable penmanship. A little too impeccable. Nobody notices. Yet.\nopt.right.say.1 [fd9908] = 你的论文按时交上，字迹工整无比。工整得有点过分了。没人发现。暂时。\n# en: The quill develops opinions and writes a scathing review of the professor. It signs your name.\nopt.right.say.2 [7b012f] = 羽毛笔有了自己的主见，写了一篇痛批教授的檄文。署的是你的名字。\n\n@event beast_griffon_chick\n# en: Hatching Day\ntitle [e94ec5] = 破壳日\n# en: The griffon egg is cracking. Professor Brask whispers that the first face a chick sees, it trusts forever — or attacks forever. Hard to predict.\ntext [713b1c] = 狮鹫蛋正在裂开。布拉斯克教授低声说，雏鸟看见的第一张脸，它会永远信任——或者永远攻击。很难预料。\n# en: A straw nest under a heat lamp, a cracked golden egg, and a damp fluffy griffon chick blinking at the world.\nscene [561fcb] = 保温灯下的草窝里，一枚裂开的金蛋，一只湿漉漉、毛茸茸的狮鹫雏鸟正眨着眼打量世界。\n# en: Hang back\nopt.left.label [7bd914] = 往后站\n# en: The chick sees Brask first and bites her. She seems delighted.\nopt.left.say.1 [72d465] = 雏鸟第一眼看见了布拉斯克，咬了她一口。她看起来很开心。\n# en: Offer your hand\nopt.right.label [1f34dd] = 伸出手\n# en: The chick peeps, nuzzles your palm and falls asleep. You are, apparently, its mother now.\nopt.right.say.1 [dd4810] = 雏鸟啾啾叫着，蹭了蹭你的掌心，睡着了。看来，你现在是它妈妈了。\n# en: It bites. Hard. Griffon beaks are sharper than they look, and they look extremely sharp.\nopt.right.say.2 [96c90d] = 它咬了你。狠狠地。狮鹫的喙比看上去锋利，而它看上去就极其锋利。\n\n@event beast_manticore\n# en: The Manticore Is Loose\ntitle [c92a9e] = 蝎尾狮跑出来了\n# en: Someone left the manticore pen open. It's in the greenhouse, tail twitching, between you and the only exit.\ntext [1d8358] = 有人没关蝎尾狮的围栏。它在温室里，尾巴抽动着，正挡在你和唯一的出口之间。\n# en: A shadowy lion-shape with a scorpion's tail prowling between the greenhouse benches, students pressed against the glass.\nscene [5076e9] = 一个带着蝎尾的狮形黑影在温室长凳间徘徊，学生们紧贴着玻璃墙。\n# en: Back away slowly\nopt.left.label [b7e862] = 慢慢后退\n# en: You edge out. Professor Brask arrives with a bucket of meat and a lullaby and the crisis is over in minutes.\nopt.left.say.1 [92deb4] = 你一点点挪了出去。布拉斯克教授带着一桶肉和一首摇篮曲赶来，几分钟就平息了危机。\n# en: Try to calm it\nopt.right.label [469057] = 试着安抚它\n# en: You hum the lullaby Brask taught you. The manticore yawns, sits, and lets you scratch behind its ear. The whole class saw.\nopt.right.say.1 [3bf240] = 你哼起布拉斯克教的摇篮曲。蝎尾狮打了个哈欠，坐下来，让你挠它的耳后。全班都看见了。\n# en: The manticore does not like your humming. The tail is quicker than you are.\nopt.right.say.2 [422c18] = 蝎尾狮不喜欢你的哼唱。它的尾巴比你快。\n\n@event beast_familiar_call\n# en: Calling a Familiar\ntitle [b91c9f] = 召唤魔宠\n# en: Brask teaches the summoning circle. \"It doesn't summon,\" she says. \"It invites. Most of the time, nothing comes. That's fine. Familiars choose you.\"\ntext [3a2457] = 布拉斯克教授教你们召唤法阵。“它并不召唤，”她说，“它只是邀请。大多数时候什么也不会来。没关系。魔宠会自己选择你。”\n# en: A circle of salt and candles in a forest clearing at dusk, shapes of small animals watching from the edge of the trees.\nscene [e641a7] = 黄昏时林间空地上一圈盐和蜡烛，林边有小动物的身影在注视。\n# en: Just watch the others\nopt.left.label [4c0d66] = 只看别人做\n# en: A first-year is chosen by a very large toad. The toad looks smug about it.\nopt.left.say.1 [4d8722] = 一个一年级生被一只非常大的蟾蜍选中了。蟾蜍看起来很得意。\n# en: Sit in the circle\nopt.right.label [69d42b] = 坐进法阵\n# en: Nothing comes. But for a long moment, something at the edge of the trees watches you with eyes like embers. Then it is gone.\nopt.right.say.1 [9c9f2a] = 什么也没来。但有很长一段时间，林边有什么东西用余烬般的眼睛注视着你。然后它消失了。\n\n@event astro_comet\n# en: A New Comet\ntitle [6445f6] = 新彗星\n# en: A comet appears that isn't in any of the charts. The observatory is in uproar. Everyone wants to be the one to name it.\ntext [b8052f] = 一颗不在任何星图上的彗星出现了。天文台炸开了锅。人人都想当给它命名的那个人。\n# en: An observatory dome open to the night, a long-tailed comet smeared across the sky, students crowded at the telescopes.\nscene [9a815d] = 向夜空敞开的天文台穹顶，一颗长尾彗星划过天际，学生们挤在望远镜前。\n# en: Sketch it carefully\nopt.left.label [5702fd] = 仔细画下它\n# en: Your drawing is lovely. Someone else's name goes on the comet. You keep the drawing.\nopt.left.say.1 [605f74] = 你画得很美。彗星用了别人的名字。你留下了那幅画。\n# en: Calculate its path\nopt.right.label [e158e4] = 计算它的轨道\n# en: Your math is right. The comet is named after you, or at least after a slight misspelling of you.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b11c9] = 你算对了。彗星以你的名字命名——至少是你名字的一个小小的错误拼写。\n# en: Your calculation suggests the comet will hit the academy on Thursday. It does not. You are teased about it for weeks.\nopt.right.say.2 [0e494e] = 你的计算显示彗星将在周四撞上学院。它没有。你被取笑了好几个星期。\n\n@event astro_kael_roof\n# en: Up All Night\ntitle [f8c2d5] = 彻夜未眠\n# en: On your way to the morning lecture you find Kael alone on the observatory roof. He has been there all night. Frost has spread across the tiles around him. He is staring at a patch of sky where there are no stars at all.\ntext [d9607d] = 去上早课的路上，你发现凯尔独自坐在天文台屋顶上。他在那儿待了一整夜。他周围的瓦片上结满了霜。他盯着天上一块一颗星星也没有的地方。\n# en: A young man in a fur coat sitting alone on the observatory roof, frost on the tiles around him, staring at one spot in the stars.\nscene [4d08d9] = 一个穿毛皮大衣的年轻人独自坐在天文台屋顶，身旁瓦片结霜，凝视着星空中的某一点。\n# en: Leave him be\nopt.left.label [ce4663] = 别打扰他\n# en: You go down to class. By evening there's a perfect circle of frost on the roof where he sat, and no Kael.\nopt.left.say.1 [8f95a8] = 你下楼去上课了。到了傍晚，屋顶上他坐过的地方留下了一个完美的霜圈，凯尔却不见了。\n# en: Sit beside him\nopt.right.label [746969] = 坐到他身边\n# en: \"There,\" he says, pointing at the dark. \"Can you see it? A star that eats light.\" You look for a long time. You think you can.\nopt.right.say.1 [28b9f2] = “那里，”他指着黑暗说，“你看见了吗？一颗吞噬光的星。”你看了很久。你觉得你看见了。\n\n@event astro_sleepless\n# en: Sleepless Weeks\ntitle [848700] = 无眠的几周\n# en: Midnight lectures, dawn homework, morning classes. You have started seeing constellations in your porridge.\ntext [5695ef] = 午夜讲座，黎明作业，上午上课。你已经开始在粥里看见星座了。\n# en: A student asleep on an open star atlas at a library desk, a guttering candle beside them, dawn light in the window.\nscene [e3c887] = 一个学生趴在图书馆书桌上摊开的星图集上睡着了，蜡烛将熄，窗外透进晨光。\n# en: Skip a few lectures\nopt.left.label [659dd6] = 翘几节讲座\n# en: You sleep for fourteen hours and wake up feeling like a person.\nopt.left.say.1 [c5fbc8] = 你睡了十四个小时，醒来时终于觉得自己像个人了。\n# en: Power through\nopt.right.label [8bebcb] = 硬撑过去\n# en: You learn the names of forty stars and forget the names of two friends. Temporarily.\nopt.right.say.1 [a83f16] = 你记住了四十颗星星的名字，忘了两个朋友的名字。暂时的。\n\n@event heal_first_patient\n# en: Your First Patient\ntitle [aad265] = 你的第一位病人\n# en: A first-year stumbles into the practice ward with a wrist that bends the wrong way. Sister Amaranth is busy. The first-year looks at you with enormous, trusting eyes.\ntext [2614f1] = 一个一年级生跌跌撞撞地走进实习病房，手腕弯向了错误的方向。阿玛兰斯修女正忙着。那个一年级生用一双大大的、充满信任的眼睛看着你。\n# en: A pale first-year sitting on an infirmary cot holding a wrist at a strange angle, a nervous student healer kneeling beside them.\nscene [0b1ccb] = 一个脸色苍白的一年级生坐在医务室病床上，托着一只角度怪异的手腕，一位紧张的学生治疗师跪在旁边。\n# en: Fetch Sister Amaranth\nopt.left.label [0ebdc9] = 去叫阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth fixes it in seconds and nods at you. \"Knowing your limits is the first lesson.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [386b03] = 阿玛兰斯几秒钟就治好了，对你点点头。“知道自己的极限，是第一课。”\n# en: Heal it yourself\nopt.right.label [6b0949] = 自己来治\n# en: The bone slides back with a soft click and a glow of warm light. The first-year tells everyone you're a genius.\nopt.right.say.1 [b682dc] = 骨头轻轻咔哒一声归位，泛起一阵温暖的光。那个一年级生逢人便说你是天才。\n# en: The wrist is now straight. The fingers, however, are pointing backwards. Amaranth sighs and fixes both.\nopt.right.say.2 [2d4421] = 手腕是直了。可手指却朝后指着。阿玛兰斯叹了口气，两样都修好了。\n\n@event heal_fever_ward\n# en: Fever in the Village\ntitle [3e73df] = 小镇里的热病\n# en: Lake Fever is spreading through the village below the academy. Sister Amaranth asks for student volunteers. It's hard, unpaid, and you might catch it.\ntext [ea106a] = 湖热病正在学院山下的小镇蔓延。阿玛兰斯修女在征集学生志愿者。辛苦、没报酬，你还可能被传染。\n# en: A crowded village hall lined with cots, lanterns hanging from the rafters, student healers moving between patients.\nscene [945032] = 一间摆满病床的拥挤镇公所，椽子上挂着灯笼，学生治疗师们在病人之间穿梭。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: You watch the volunteers board the barge in the morning. You do your homework. It feels like the wrong homework.\nopt.left.say.1 [70c215] = 早上，你看着志愿者们登上驳船。你做着你的作业。感觉像是在做错的作业。\n# en: Volunteer\nopt.right.label [39a371] = 报名志愿\n# en: You save lives, and catch the fever yourself. The village sends a basket of honey cakes to your sickbed.\nopt.right.say.1 [d1e957] = 你救了人，自己也染上了热病。镇上送了一篮蜂蜜蛋糕到你的病床边。\n# en: A week of long nights and cool cloths. When you leave, the baker's daughter gives you her best loaf.\nopt.right.say.2 [61b0a3] = 一个星期的漫漫长夜和凉毛巾。你离开时，面包师的女儿送了你她烤得最好的一条面包。\n\n@event heal_tobias_sting\n# en: Stung\ntitle [1eecd2] = 被蛰了\n# en: Tobias wanders into the practice ward holding up a hand swollen like a pumpkin. \"Mandrake,\" he says. \"She didn't like my singing.\" He looks hopefully at you.\ntext [91e6f0] = 托比亚斯晃进实习病房，举着一只肿得像南瓜的手。“曼德拉草，”他说，“她不喜欢我唱歌。”他满怀希望地看着你。\n# en: A broad-shouldered boy sitting on a greenhouse bench, grimacing, holding up a hand swollen to twice its size.\nscene [37ac94] = 一个宽肩膀的男生坐在温室长凳上，龇牙咧嘴，举着一只肿成两倍大的手。\n# en: Send him to the infirmary\nopt.left.label [d6e106] = 让他去医务室\n# en: \"Right. Proper healer. Good idea.\" He goes, a little disappointed.\nopt.left.say.1 [d77544] = “对。找正经治疗师。好主意。”他走了，有点失望。\n# en: Treat him yourself\nopt.right.label [2aa3de] = 亲自为他治疗\n# en: Your hands are steady. The swelling goes down. Tobias stares at his hand, then at you. \"You're really good at this,\" he says softly.\nopt.right.say.1 [eb2831] = 你的手很稳。肿消了下去。托比亚斯盯着自己的手，又盯着你。“你真的很擅长这个。”他轻声说。\n\n@event umbral_whisper\n# en: The Shadow Speaks\ntitle [041067] = 影子开口了\n# en: In the middle of a lecture on the Nature of Absence, your shadow leans away from the lamp, toward your ear, and says your name.\ntext [a8a6b1] = 在一堂关于“缺席之本质”的讲座中途，你的影子背离了灯光，凑到你耳边，说出了你的名字。\n# en: A student at a desk in a dim lecture hall, their shadow on the wall behind them turned the wrong way, leaning close to whisper.\nscene [51c4bd] = 昏暗讲堂里的一个学生，身后墙上的影子朝向不对，正凑近低语。\n# en: Banish it\nopt.left.label [78332f] = 驱散它\n# en: You speak the warding phrase. Your shadow snaps back into place, sulking. It does not speak again. You think.\nopt.left.say.1 [9451ed] = 你念出防护咒语。影子啪地弹回原位，生着闷气。它没有再说话。你是这么想的。\n# en: Answer it\nopt.right.label [b623c8] = 回应它\n# en: It tells you something about death you can't unlearn. The cold starts the next morning.\nopt.right.say.1 [f3763b] = 它告诉了你一件关于死亡的事，你再也无法忘记。第二天早上，寒意开始了。\n# en: It tells you where a lost book is shelved in the library. It is right. You decide not to wonder how it knew.\nopt.right.say.2 [55a529] = 它告诉你图书馆里一本遗失的书放在哪里。它说对了。你决定不去想它是怎么知道的。\n\n@event umbral_bone_mouse\n# en: The Bone Mouse\ntitle [ae9175] = 骨鼠\n# en: Practical exercise: animate the skeleton of a field mouse. \"Purely educational,\" the professor says, with a smile nobody trusts.\ntext [c20b3b] = 实践练习：让一具田鼠骨架活动起来。“纯属教学目的。”教授说，露出一个没人信得过的微笑。\n# en: A tiny mouse skeleton on a silver tray, one bony paw twitching, students leaning away from it.\nscene [e2a825] = 银托盘上一具小小的老鼠骨架，一只骨爪在抽动，学生们纷纷向后躲。\n# en: Refuse on principle\nopt.left.label [d08672] = 出于原则拒绝\n# en: You write a short essay on why instead. The professor reads it with surprising attention.\nopt.left.say.1 [ceeff8] = 你改写了一篇短文说明理由。教授读得出奇地认真。\n# en: Do the exercise\nopt.right.label [eb5633] = 完成练习\n# en: The little skeleton stands up, washes its non-existent whiskers, and lies down again. Perfect control.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bb00e] = 小骨架站起来，洗了洗并不存在的胡须，又躺了回去。控制得完美无缺。\n# en: It gets up. It does not lie down again. It follows you back to your dormitory and sits on your pillow every night.\nopt.right.say.2 [6046b9] = 它站了起来。然后再也没躺回去。它跟着你回了宿舍，每晚都坐在你的枕头上。\n\n@event umbral_forbidden_notes\n# en: Forbidden Notes\ntitle [b392f3] = 禁忌笔记\n# en: A fourth-year, Odile Varn, slides a battered notebook across the table. \"My notes on the Restricted Section. I'm graduating. You look like you'd use them.\" A silver key is tucked in the spine.\ntext [bc2745] = 一个叫奥迪尔·瓦恩的四年级生把一本破旧的笔记本推过桌面。“我关于禁书区的笔记。我要毕业了。你看起来用得上。”书脊里夹着一把银钥匙。\n# en: A battered notebook with a cracked leather cover being slid across a library table, a silver key tucked into its spine.\nscene [9bca00] = 一本皮面开裂的破旧笔记本被推过图书馆的桌面，书脊里夹着一把银钥匙。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: The fourth-year shrugs and slides the notebook to the next student. You watch it go.\nopt.left.say.1 [d26ee6] = 四年级生耸耸肩，把笔记本推给了下一个学生。你看着它被推走。\n# en: Take the notebook\nopt.right.label [beda2e] = 收下笔记本\n# en: The notes are brilliant, half-mad and annotated in three colours of ink. The key is cold in your pocket: it opens the Restricted Section, the locked door at the back of the library, after hours.\nopt.right.say.1 [402121] = 笔记精彩绝伦、半疯半癫，用三种颜色的墨水做了批注。那把钥匙在你口袋里冰凉：它能打开禁书区——图书馆后面那扇锁着的门，闭馆之后就能用。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/club_spell_chains.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/club_spell_chains.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@thread brace_quest\n# en: The captain's charm\nname [7a7935] = 队长的护符\n# en: An old captain's charm in the duelling dummy empties only if someone holds firm while it fires.\npremise [ac57f9] = 决斗木人里有一枚老队长的护符，只有在它开火时有人站稳不退，才能把它耗空。\n# en: You said you'd try to empty the old captain's charm. Someone drew a stretcher under your name.\njournal.1 [1f5a84] = 你说你要试着耗空那枚老队长的护符。有人在你名字下面画了副担架。\n# en: The captain's notes: don't stop the first blow, let it land on something that isn't you.\njournal.2 [4d5179] = 队长的笔记：别去挡第一击，让它落在不是你的东西上。\n# en: Bram threw hexes at you in the chalk circle until he ran out.\njournal.3 [e55913] = 布拉姆在粉笔圈里朝你扔咒，一直扔到他没了力气。\n# en: Lyra drilled your stance until dark. \"Better,\" she said.\njournal.4 [60ee21] = 莱拉逼你练站姿直到天黑。“好些了，”她说。\n# en: The first time, the charm lifted you clean out of the circle.\njournal.5 [c76d4f] = 第一次，那枚护符把你整个掀出了圈子。\n# en: You held the circle until the charm sputtered out. Lyra nodded, once.\njournal.6 [a44c67] = 你守住了圈子，直到护符噗地熄灭。莱拉点了一下头。\n\n@event duel_dummy_hexes\n# en: The Dummy Hits Back\ntitle [73ba1d] = 假人反击了\n# en: The eyepatched dummy has started throwing hexes back. Lyra splits its seam and pulls out an old captain's charm, sewn in decades ago and still humming. \"It empties if someone holds firm in the circle while it fires,\" she says. \"Nobody's managed it. Nobody's tried properly.\"\ntext [81980f] = 那个戴眼罩的假人开始把恶咒扔回来了。莱拉拆开它的接缝，拽出一枚老队长的护符，是几十年前缝进去的，至今还在嗡嗡作响。“只要有人在圈里站稳、等它放完，它就空了，”她说，“没人做到过。没人好好试过。”\n# en: A duel hall's practice corner, an eyepatched straw dummy flinging a crackling hex back at a startled student, stuffing spilling from a split seam.\nscene [23661b] = 决斗厅的练习角落，一个戴眼罩的草人朝一个吓了一跳的学生甩回一道噼啪作响的恶咒，裂开的接缝里漏出稻草。\n# en: \"I'll try properly.\"\nopt.left.label [91bd61] = “我来好好试试。”\n# en: Lyra sews the charm back in, looks at you, and writes your name on the board under the dummy. Underneath, someone adds a small drawing of a stretcher.\nopt.left.say.1 [61b029] = 莱拉把护符缝了回去，看了看你，把你的名字写在假人下方的板子上。下面有人添了一个小小的担架图。\n# en: Leave it to the seniors\nopt.right.label [08b604] = 留给高年级生\n# en: You go back to your bout, and the dummy goes back to hexing whoever passes. A senior limps past at the end of practice, still smoking slightly.\nopt.right.say.1 [3e1dce] = 你回去打你的那一局，假人回去对每个路过的人放恶咒。练习结束时，一个高年级生一瘸一拐地走过，身上还冒着点烟。\n\n@event duel_captains_notes\n# en: The Captain's Notes\ntitle [ca8e13] = 队长的笔记\n# en: The captain who made the charm left notes, in a cupboard under the trophy shelf, in a hand like barbed wire. Half of it is about footwork and the other half is insults addressed to people long graduated.\ntext [718aeb] = 造护符的那位队长留下了笔记，就在奖杯架下的柜子里，字迹像带刺的铁丝。一半讲步法，另一半是写给早已毕业之人的骂话。\n# en: A dusty cupboard in the duel hall, a student reading a cracked leather notebook by the light of a single lamp.\nscene [a225a8] = 决斗厅里一个落满灰的柜子，一个学生借着一盏孤灯读一本开裂的皮面笔记本。\n# en: Read them properly\nopt.left.label [58ece6] = 好好读一遍\n# en: Between the insults is the trick: you don't stop the first blow, you let it land on something that isn't you. You copy the page out and put the notebook back where the captain would have wanted it.\nopt.left.say.1 [a2ca36] = 在骂话之间藏着诀窍：你不去挡第一击，而是让它落在不是你的东西上。你把那一页抄下来，把笔记本放回队长会希望的位置。\n# en: You read until the lamp gutters and learn a great deal about a student called Pim, who could not parry. The trick is in there somewhere. You'll have to come back for it.\nopt.left.say.2 [c40f86] = 你读到灯芯快灭，对一个叫皮姆、怎么也学不会格挡的学生了解了很多。诀窍就在里面某处。你只能改天再来。\n# en: Spar instead tonight\nopt.right.label [ced9ea] = 今晚改去对练\n# en: You leave the notes for another evening and take a bout instead. You win it, which is the other kind of useful.\nopt.right.say.1 [cbb7a5] = 你把笔记留到另一个晚上，去打了一局。你赢了，这是另一种有用。\n\n@event duel_bram_barrage\n# en: Inside the Chalk Circle\ntitle [6ebe8b] = 粉笔圈里\n# en: Word reaches Bram that you mean to empty the captain's charm. He turns up with his sleeves rolled. \"Regulation hazing,\" he announces, \"section four. You stand in the circle. I throw. You don't step out. Ready?\"\ntext [ebedae] = 布拉姆听说你打算放空队长的护符。他挽着袖子来了。“标准欺新，”他宣布，“第四条。你站在圈里。我扔。你不许出圈。准备好了吗？”\n# en: A student standing in a chalk circle on a scorched floor while a broad prefect hurls a stream of stinging hexes from the edge.\nscene [0fd424] = 一个学生站在烧焦地板上的粉笔圈里，一个魁梧的级长从圈边连珠炮似的扔出蛰人的恶咒。\n# en: Hold the circle against him\nopt.left.label [d2886c] = 在圈里顶住他\n# en: You don't step out. By the twentieth hex Bram is red in the face and you are still in the circle, scorched and planted. \"Adequate,\" he says, gruffly, and doesn't put you on report.\nopt.left.say.1 [2ebcf5] = 你没出圈。到第二十道恶咒时，布拉姆满脸通红，你还站在圈里，焦了，却扎了根。“尚可，”他粗声说，没有记你的过。\n# en: The ninth hex knocks you clean out of the circle. Bram notes it in his book. \"Section four, failed,\" he says. \"Again next week.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [dc3867] = 第九道恶咒把你掀出了圈。布拉姆在本子上记下。“第四条，未通过，”他说，“下周再来。”\n# en: Take it all on your shield arm\nopt.right.label [e34207] = 全用盾牌手臂扛\n# en: You don't try to be clever. You just stand there and take it, every hex, on your arm, until Bram runs out. Your arm is purple for a week. Bram looks almost impressed.\nopt.right.say.1 [33cb6d] = 你没耍聪明。你就站在那儿扛，每一道恶咒都落在胳膊上，直到布拉姆扔完。你的胳膊紫了一周。布拉姆看上去几乎有点佩服。\n# en: Shrug it off in the coat\nopt.up.label [46b38b] = 穿着外套硬扛\n# en: You button the quilted coat to the chin and plant your feet. The hexes thud into the padding like snowballs into a wall. Bram runs out first. \"That's cheating,\" he says. \"Section four doesn't mention coats,\" you say.\nopt.up.say.1 [eeb3c5] = 你把绗缝外套扣到下巴，站稳脚跟。恶咒砸进衬垫，像雪球砸在墙上。布拉姆先扔完了。“这算作弊，”他说。“第四条可没提外套，”你说。\n\n@event duel_footwork_lyra\n# en: Footwork Until Dark\ntitle [b94ac8] = 练步法到天黑\n# en: Lyra finds you after practice. \"The captain's notes say the stance is everything,\" she says. \"Your stance is a disgrace. Three hours. Now.\" She has already chalked the marks on the floor.\ntext [2d594e] = 练习后莱拉找到你。“队长笔记说站姿就是一切，”她说，“你的站姿是个耻辱。三个钟头。现在。”她已经在地上画好了粉笔记号。\n# en: An empty duel hall at dusk, two students drilling footwork across chalk marks, one of them pointing sharply at the other's feet.\nscene [95915c] = 黄昏空荡荡的决斗厅，两个学生沿着粉笔记号练步法，其中一人严厉地指着另一人的脚。\n# en: Drill until dark\nopt.left.label [811d2a] = 练到天黑\n# en: She is merciless and exact. By dark you can set your feet without thinking, and you never want to see a chalk mark again. \"Better,\" she says, which from Lyra is a sonnet.\nopt.left.say.1 [237639] = 她毫不留情，分毫不差。到天黑，你不假思索就能站稳脚步，而且这辈子都不想再看见粉笔记号。“好些了，”她说，从莱拉嘴里出来这就是一首十四行诗。\n# en: \"Tomorrow. I have an essay.\"\nopt.right.label [3c02a9] = “明天吧。我有篇论文。”\n# en: She looks at you as if you'd chosen to lose. You write the essay. It is a fine essay, and Lyra mentions your stance at breakfast.\nopt.right.say.1 [fd14f0] = 她看着你，好像你是自己选择了认输。你写了论文。那是篇不错的论文，早饭时莱拉提起了你的站姿。\n\n@event duel_brace_final\n# en: Hold Firm\ntitle [7cdab4] = 站稳\n# en: The club gathers on the benches. You step into the chalk circle, and the eyepatched dummy turns its head toward you with an audible creak. The captain's charm begins to fire. {flag(brace_notes) and flag(brace_circle) and flag(brace_footwork): You know how to stand, and why.|You have done some of the work. Not all of it.}\ntext [251e18] = 社员们聚到长凳上。你走进粉笔圈，那个戴眼罩的假人嘎吱一声朝你转过头来。队长的护符开始发射。{flag(brace_notes) and flag(brace_circle) and flag(brace_footwork): 你知道该怎么站，也知道为什么。|你做了一部分功课。不是全部。}\n# en: A student standing firm in a chalk circle in a crowded duel hall as an eyepatched dummy fires hex after hex, the club watching from the benches.\nscene [d18fe2] = 一个学生在挤满人的决斗厅里稳稳站在粉笔圈中，一个戴眼罩的假人一道接一道地放恶咒，社员们在长凳上看着。\n# en: Hold firm\nopt.left.label [5c71d4] = 站稳\n# en: The hexes break on you one after another, and each lands softer than the last. When the charm finally sputters out, the club is on its feet. Lyra doesn't clap. She nods, once, which is louder.\nopt.left.say.1 [2151b8] = 恶咒一道接一道在你身上碎开，每一道都比上一道轻。护符终于噼啪一声熄了，全社都站了起来。莱拉没有鼓掌。她点了一下头，这比掌声更响。\n# en: The fourteenth hex lifts you out of the circle and into the benches. The dummy turns its head back with a creak. It will be there at practice, waiting.\nopt.left.say.2 [98d202] = 第十四道恶咒把你从圈里掀到了长凳上。假人嘎吱一声把头转了回去。它会在练习时等着你。\n# en: Borrow the captain's old gauntlets\nopt.right.label [eaf6bb] = 借老队长的护手\n# en: The club quartermaster rents you the captain's own gauntlets for the night. They take the first blow for you, and the rest you take yourself. When the charm sputters out, the benches roar.\nopt.right.say.1 [0c809b] = 社里的管理员把老队长自己的护手借给你一晚上。它们替你挡下了第一击，剩下的你自己扛。护符噼啪熄灭时，长凳上一片欢呼。\n# en: Even in the gauntlets, the fourteenth hex lifts you out of the circle. The rental is not refunded. The dummy creaks its head back and waits.\nopt.right.say.2 [586ef6] = 就算戴着护手，第十四道恶咒还是把你掀出了圈。租金不退。假人嘎吱一声转回头，等着。\n\n@thread quickquill_quest\n# en: The quill that talks back\nname [0707e8] = 会顶嘴的羽毛笔\n# en: A spare quill at the Ledger has started inventing your sentences.\npremise [9309d1] = 《纪事报》的一支备用羽毛笔，开始替你编句子了。\n# en: You took the Ledger's runaway quill home in a handkerchief. It wrote \"kidnapped\" on it.\njournal.1 [7175c8] = 你用手帕把《纪事报》那支不听话的羽毛笔裹回了家。它在手帕上写了“绑架”。\n# en: You kept the quill to the facts on forty letters about the soup.\njournal.2 [eac39c] = 四十封关于汤的投诉信，你都按住了羽毛笔，只写事实。\n# en: You bought it proper ink, black as a well. It underlines the bits you'll need.\njournal.3 [8cdeed] = 你给它买了上好的墨水，黑得像口井。它会把你用得着的地方划出来。\n# en: You and the quill wrote until four on deadline night. Nessa put your name on the page.\njournal.4 [0291f7] = 截稿那夜，你和羽毛笔一直写到四点。内莎把你的名字印上了版面。\n# en: The first interview got away from you, and from the quill.\njournal.5 [8c283b] = 第一次采访，你和羽毛笔都没能把住。\n# en: The chair denied everything, word for word. \"Front page,\" said Nessa.\njournal.6 [b747a9] = 委员会主席什么都否认，一字不差地被记了下来。“头版，”内莎说。\n\n@event ledger_spare_quill\n# en: The Spare Quill\ntitle [29f326] = 备用羽毛笔\n# en: A spare quill on the drying line has been finishing your sentences. Today it started inventing them: your proof now says the refectory serves dragon. \"Oh, it's woken up,\" says Nessa, delighted. \"Tame it and it's yours. Nobody's tamed one since I've been editor.\"\ntext [b0d093] = 晾稿绳上的一支备用羽毛笔一直在替你把话写完。今天它开始自己编了：你的校样上现在写着膳堂供应龙肉。“哦，它醒了，”内莎高兴地说，“驯服它，它就归你。我当编辑以来还没人驯服过一支。”\n# en: A newspaper office with proofs pegged on a drying line, a lone quill hanging among them scribbling on a proof by itself.\nscene [a7cf40] = 一间报社，校样夹在晾稿绳上，一支孤零零的羽毛笔挂在其中，自己在一张校样上涂写。\n# en: Take the quill home\nopt.left.label [53212f] = 把羽毛笔带回家\n# en: You wrap it in a handkerchief. It writes \"kidnapped\" on the handkerchief. Nessa writes your name on the office board under QUILL, with a question mark.\nopt.left.say.1 [c856e1] = 你用手帕把它包起来。它在手帕上写了“绑架”。内莎在办公室的板子上“羽毛笔”下面写了你的名字，后面打了个问号。\n# en: Peg it back on the line\nopt.right.label [db33eb] = 把它夹回绳上\n# en: You peg it back. It writes a small, hurt editorial about you on the next proof, which Nessa cuts, laughing.\nopt.right.say.1 [c59c09] = 你把它夹了回去。它在下一张校样上写了一篇受伤的小社论讲你，内莎笑着把它删了。\n\n@event ledger_soup_complaint\n# en: The Soup Complaint\ntitle [d5021e] = 汤的投诉\n# en: Forty furious letters about the refectory soup have arrived. Nessa wants one calm reply, printed. You decide to dictate it to the quill, which wants very badly to make it more exciting.\ntext [7caf2e] = 关于膳堂的汤，来了四十封怒气冲冲的信。内莎要一篇冷静的回复，要登出来。你决定口述给那支羽毛笔，而它非常想把回复写得更刺激些。\n# en: A desk buried in angry letters about soup, a student dictating firmly to a quill that keeps trying to add flourishes.\nscene [9b8d46] = 一张被关于汤的愤怒来信淹没的书桌，一个学生坚定地对一支总想加点花样的羽毛笔口述。\n# en: Keep the quill to the facts\nopt.left.label [38c15f] = 让羽毛笔只写事实\n# en: Every time it reaches for a flourish, you catch it. The reply comes out calm, true and very dull. Nessa prints it. The quill sulks, and then, grudgingly, writes the next one straight.\nopt.left.say.1 [1545a6] = 它每次伸手去加花样，你都把它截住。回复写得冷静、真实，而且非常无聊。内莎登了。羽毛笔闹了脾气，然后不情不愿地把下一封写得规规矩矩。\n# en: You blink and the quill has written that the soup contains a mild curse. It prints. You spend Thursday apologising to the cooks.\nopt.left.say.2 [f1776c] = 一眨眼，羽毛笔写了汤里有轻微诅咒。登出来了。你花了整个周四向厨子们道歉。\n# en: Write the reply by hand\nopt.right.label [54b39d] = 亲手写回复\n# en: You write it yourself, slowly. It's a good reply. The quill watches from the inkwell, offended at being left out.\nopt.right.say.1 [4ad58a] = 你自己写，写得很慢。回复不错。羽毛笔从墨水瓶里看着，因为被晾在一边而生气。\n\n@event ledger_proper_ink\n# en: Proper Ink\ntitle [c79087] = 像样的墨水\n# en: The quill refuses the school ink. It blots, it sulks, it writes \"no\" in large letters. The stationer in the village has proper ink, black as a well and priced like it.\ntext [7d5688] = 羽毛笔拒绝学校的墨水。它洇墨、闹脾气、用大字写“不”。村里的文具店有像样的墨水，黑得像井，价钱也一样深。\n# en: A stationer's counter in the village, a student holding a small dark bottle of ink up to the light while a quill pokes out of their pocket.\nscene [fd2560] = 村里文具店的柜台，一个学生举起一小瓶深色墨水对着光看，口袋里探出一支羽毛笔。\n# en: Buy the proper ink\nopt.left.label [98fa9e] = 买像样的墨水\n# en: The quill drinks the new ink and writes a whole page of your notes, neatly, in a better hand than yours. It underlines the bits you'll need. You feel slightly managed.\nopt.left.say.1 [b2fa8a] = 羽毛笔喝下新墨水，替你整整齐齐写了一整页笔记，字比你的还好看。它还把你要用的地方划了线。你觉得自己有点被安排了。\n# en: Make it use the school ink\nopt.right.label [0f3f50] = 让它用学校的墨水\n# en: It uses the school ink under protest and blots every third word, including, pointedly, your name.\nopt.right.say.1 [c3e17e] = 它不情不愿地用着学校的墨水，每三个字洇一个，包括——显然是故意的——你的名字。\n\n@event ledger_deadline_night\n# en: Deadline Night\ntitle [d06cb7] = 截稿夜\n# en: Deadline night. The front page fell through at nine and Nessa is pacing. \"We need eight hundred words by four,\" she says. \"Anybody with a quill that writes fast, now is the time.\"\ntext [a82a66] = 截稿夜。九点头版泡了汤，内莎来回踱步。“四点前我们需要八百字，”她说，“谁有写得快的羽毛笔，现在就是时候。”\n# en: A newspaper office after midnight, cold tea and candle stubs everywhere, a student and a quill working side by side while an editor paces.\nscene [3fdd7a] = 午夜后的报社，到处是冷茶和蜡烛头，一个学生和一支羽毛笔并肩工作，一位编辑来回踱步。\n# en: Sit it out with the quill\nopt.left.label [921566] = 陪羽毛笔熬过去\n# en: You and the quill write until four. By the end it finishes your sentences only when you want it to. You are both too tired to argue. Nessa puts your name on the page, small.\nopt.left.say.1 [dd5438] = 你和羽毛笔一直写到四点。到最后，只有你想让它接话时它才接。你们俩都累得吵不动了。内莎把你的名字印在版面上，字很小。\n# en: Go to bed\nopt.right.label [2fa817] = 去睡觉\n# en: You sleep. In the morning the front page is late and Nessa is hoarse, and the quill has written a bitter little poem about you on the pillowcase.\nopt.right.say.1 [c3ec9a] = 你睡了。早上头版晚了，内莎嗓子哑了，羽毛笔在你的枕套上写了一首关于你的刻薄小诗。\n\n@event ledger_quill_interview\n# en: Deny Everything\ntitle [745920] = 一概否认\n# en: Nessa sends you to interview the chair of the refectory committee, who will deny everything, including the soup. \"Get it word for word,\" she says. \"Every word. They'll deny the words too.\" {flag(quickquill_soup) and flag(quickquill_ink) and flag(quickquill_deadline): Your quill sits up in your pocket, ready.|Your quill is half-tamed at best.}\ntext [df81a3] = 内莎派你去采访膳堂委员会主席，那人会一概否认，包括那锅汤。“一字不落地记下来，”她说，“每一个字。他们连说过的字都会否认。”{flag(quickquill_soup) and flag(quickquill_ink) and flag(quickquill_deadline): 你的羽毛笔在口袋里坐直了，随时待命。|你的羽毛笔充其量驯服了一半。}\n# en: A stuffy committee room, a smooth official smiling across a table at a student whose quill writes furiously on its own.\nscene [a90711] = 一间闷热的委员会会议室，一位圆滑的官员隔着桌子朝一个学生微笑，学生的羽毛笔正自己飞快地写着。\n# en: Interview them with the quill\nopt.left.label [b85ec0] = 带着羽毛笔去采访\n# en: The chair denies everything, and the quill takes every denial down word for word, including the one that contradicts the other three. Nessa reads it and goes very quiet. \"Front page,\" she says.\nopt.left.say.1 [432baf] = 主席一概否认，羽毛笔把每一句否认都逐字记下，包括与另外三句自相矛盾的那一句。内莎读完，安静了下来。“头版，”她说。\n# en: The quill gets bored halfway and starts improving the chair's answers. Nothing is printable. The quill sulks in the WILL NOT PRINT basket until your next front page.\nopt.left.say.2 [46c476] = 羽毛笔到一半就腻了，开始替主席润色回答。一个字都不能登。羽毛笔在“不予刊登”的篮子里赌气，直到你下一次上头版。\n# en: Prepare all night first\nopt.right.label [fa7c4f] = 先通宵准备\n# en: You go in tired and over-prepared, with every question written out. The quill follows your lead all the way through. Nessa reads the transcript twice. \"Front page.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [6bc6d3] = 你带着倦意、准备过头地去了，每个问题都写好了。羽毛笔从头到尾都跟着你的节奏。内莎把记录读了两遍。“头版。”\n# en: All night's preparation, and the chair simply doesn't turn up. The quill sulks in the WILL NOT PRINT basket until your next front page.\nopt.right.say.2 [d13a9a] = 准备了一整夜，主席干脆没来。羽毛笔在“不予刊登”的篮子里赌气，直到你下一次上头版。\n\n@thread bramble_quest\n# en: The seed in your pocket\nname [e1a78b] = 口袋里的种子\n# en: A hedge-seed that chose you, and has already put out a shoot.\npremise [53a28f] = 一颗挑中了你的篱笆种，已经冒出了芽。\n# en: \"That's a hedge-seed,\" Tobias said. \"They choose people.\" This one chose you.\njournal.1 [aed022] = “这是篱笆种，”托比亚斯说，“它们会自己挑人。”这一颗挑中了你。\n# en: You found your seedling a bed of its own.\njournal.2 [5b1e6f] = 你给你的幼苗找了一块属于它的苗床。\n# en: You sat up with it through the first frost, feeding it warmth. It grew a thorn.\njournal.3 [f21750] = 第一场霜降那夜，你守着它，喂它温暖。它长出了一根刺。\n# en: You quieted the mandrake choir, and your seedling put out a vine.\njournal.4 [449e35] = 你让曼德拉草合唱团安静了下来，你的幼苗抽出了一根藤。\n# en: The night it bolted, the hedge didn't know you yet.\njournal.5 [5d180b] = 它疯长的那一夜，那片荆篱还不认得你。\n# en: The night it bolted across the greenhouse, it stopped for you.\njournal.6 [1108fa] = 它在温室里疯长的那一夜，为你停了下来。\n\n@event verdant_far_bed\n# en: The Far Bed Answers\ntitle [d21f95] = 最里头的苗床回话了\n# en: The far bed answers back today: every vine in it turns to follow you. On the way out you find a seed in your coat pocket that wasn't there this morning, and it has already put out a shoot.\ntext [f601cf] = 今天最里头那块苗床回话了：苗床里每一根藤都转过来跟着你。出门时，你在外套口袋里发现一颗早上还没有的种子，它已经冒出了一根芽。\n# en: The far end of a greenhouse, a tangle of vines reaching toward a student who has just found a green shoot curling out of their coat pocket.\nscene [00541a] = 温室的尽头，一团藤蔓朝一个学生伸过来，学生刚发现外套口袋里卷出一根绿芽。\n# en: Keep it and grow it\nopt.left.label [49383c] = 留着它，养大它\n# en: You cup it in your palm. The shoot curls round your finger, firmly. Tobias, when you show him, goes pale and then delighted. \"That's a hedge-seed,\" he says. \"They choose people.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [0bab8b] = 你把它捧在掌心。那根芽稳稳地缠住了你的手指。你拿给托比亚斯看时，他先是脸色发白，然后高兴起来。“这是篱笆种，”他说，“它们会自己挑人。”\n# en: Give it to Tobias\nopt.right.label [e597fe] = 交给托比亚斯\n# en: Tobias takes it gently and puts it in a pot by the door. \"It'll want you back,\" he says. \"They usually do.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [ce4a73] = 托比亚斯轻轻接过，把它种进门边的花盆。“它会想你的，”他说，“它们通常都会。”\n\n@event verdant_plant_seed\n# en: Somewhere to Grow\ntitle [ca8d3c] = 找个地方生长\n# en: The seed has outgrown your pocket. It needs a bed. Tobias offers the corner of his own in the greenhouse. Old Maud, passing, says she still keeps a bed at the back of the same greenhouse, by the old pipes, that nobody else is allowed to touch, \"if you can keep a secret, dearie.\"\ntext [0bfe48] = 那颗种子已经装不下你的口袋了。它需要一块苗床。托比亚斯把自己那块的一角让给你。老莫德路过时说，她在同一座温室的最里头、挨着旧管道的地方，还留着一块谁都不许碰的苗床，“要是你守得住秘密的话，亲爱的。”\n# en: Two students kneeling in a greenhouse bed planting a seedling, a lantern between them and earth up to their elbows.\nscene [d4bbf7] = 两个学生跪在温室苗床里种一株幼苗，中间放着一盏灯，泥土一直沾到手肘。\n# en: Plant it with Tobias\nopt.left.label [57094f] = 和托比亚斯一起种\n# en: You plant it together by lantern light, and Tobias tells it a long story about a goat to help it settle. It settles. So does Tobias, eventually.\nopt.left.say.1 [2baafd] = 你们在灯下一起把它种下，托比亚斯给它讲了一个关于山羊的长故事，帮它安下心来。它安下心来了。托比亚斯过了一会儿也是。\n# en: Plant it in Maud's corner\nopt.right.label [7a656c] = 种在莫德的角落里\n# en: Maud leads you to the back of the greenhouse, behind a stack of broken frames you'd never have looked past, to a bed she has kept for forty years. You plant it there. \"It'll be happy,\" she says, \"for a while.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [3070a4] = 莫德领你走到温室最里头，绕过一摞你从没想过要往后看的破苗框，来到一块她守了四十年的苗床前。你把它种在那儿。“它会开心的，”她说，“开心一阵子。”\n\n@event verdant_cold_snap\n# en: The Cold Snap\ntitle [deb4a6] = 寒潮\n# en: A hard frost is coming tonight, the first of the season. Your seedling is too young for it. Somebody would have to sit with it till dawn, feeding it warmth.\ntext [b3caad] = 今晚要下一场重霜，是这一季的头一场。你的幼苗还太小，扛不住。得有人陪它坐到天亮，把暖意喂给它。\n# en: Frost creeping over a greenhouse bed at night, a student wrapped in a blanket sitting beside a shivering seedling with a lantern.\nscene [888a42] = 夜里白霜爬上温室苗床，一个裹着毯子的学生提着灯坐在一株瑟瑟发抖的幼苗旁。\n# en: Sit up with it all night\nopt.left.label [18ab48] = 陪它坐一整夜\n# en: You sit wrapped round the lantern till dawn, feeding it what warmth you have. By morning you're stiff and coughing, and the seedling has grown a thorn, which it seems proud of.\nopt.left.say.1 [912299] = 你裹着灯一直坐到天亮，把自己能给的暖意都喂给它。到早上你浑身僵硬、咳个不停，幼苗长出了一根刺，看上去很是得意。\n# en: Cover it and hope\nopt.right.label [36916e] = 盖上，听天由命\n# en: You cover it with sacking and go to bed. It survives, sulking, and loses a leaf. It is noticeably cooler towards you.\nopt.right.say.1 [0ad654] = 你用麻袋把它盖上就去睡了。它活下来了，赌着气，掉了一片叶子。它对你明显冷淡了。\n\n@event verdant_mandrake_choir\n# en: The Mandrake Choir\ntitle [a16982] = 曼德拉草合唱团\n# en: The mandrakes in the next bed have taken against your seedling and scream at it in chorus every evening. It's wilting from the noise. Gerald is conducting.\ntext [db08b7] = 隔壁苗床的曼德拉草看你的幼苗不顺眼，每天傍晚齐声冲它尖叫。它被吵得蔫了。杰拉尔德在指挥。\n# en: A row of potted mandrakes in knitted hats screaming in chorus at a nervous seedling, a student with earmuffs approaching with a watering can.\nscene [500265] = 一排戴着针织帽的盆栽曼德拉草齐声冲一株紧张的幼苗尖叫，一个戴着耳罩的学生提着水壶走近。\n# en: Quiet the choir\nopt.left.label [0b5253] = 让合唱团安静\n# en: A drop of valerian in each pot and a firm word with Gerald, and the choir settles into a low hum. Your seedling straightens up and puts out a vine.\nopt.left.say.1 [163e3b] = 每盆滴一滴缬草，再跟杰拉尔德严肃地谈一谈，合唱团就降成了低低的哼唱。你的幼苗直起腰，伸出了一根藤。\n# en: The choir screams at you instead. Your ears ring till supper. Gerald looks unrepentant in his hat.\nopt.left.say.2 [4a8e2e] = 合唱团转而冲你尖叫。你的耳朵一直嗡嗡响到晚饭。杰拉尔德戴着帽子，毫无悔意。\n# en: Move the seedling's pot\nopt.right.label [619d89] = 把幼苗的盆挪开\n# en: You move it to the quiet end. It perks up, but it's away from the warm pipes now, and it isn't growing.\nopt.right.say.1 [f49f23] = 你把它挪到安静的那头。它精神了，但现在离暖管远了，长不动了。\n\n@event verdant_bramble_bolts\n# en: The Night It Bolts\ntitle [256113] = 疯长之夜\n# en: Tobias wakes you at midnight. Your plant has bolted: a hedge of thorns is surging across the greenhouse toward the moonflower beds, and it is not stopping for anyone but you. {flag(bramble_fed) and flag(bramble_choir): It knows your voice.|You're not sure it knows you at all.}\ntext [0b7d5b] = 午夜托比亚斯把你叫醒。你的植物疯长了：一道荆棘树篱正横扫温室，冲向月光花的苗床，除了你谁也拦不住。{flag(bramble_fed) and flag(bramble_choir): 它认得你的声音。|你不确定它到底认不认得你。}\n# en: A greenhouse at night, a thorny hedge surging across the beds toward a row of pale flowers, a student with arms outstretched trying to hold it back.\nscene [26a79e] = 夜里的温室，一道带刺的树篱横扫苗床，冲向一排苍白的花，一个学生张开双臂想拦住它。\n# en: Call it to heel\nopt.left.label [ea558f] = 唤它回来\n# en: You put out your hand and the hedge stops a finger's width from the moonflowers, then folds itself back into your palm like a sleeping cat. Tobias sits down on a bucket and laughs from the belly.\nopt.left.say.1 [ee8753] = 你伸出手，树篱在离月光花一指宽的地方停下，然后像一只睡着的猫一样卷回你的掌心。托比亚斯一屁股坐在水桶上，从肚子里笑了出来。\n# en: The thorns take your sleeve and some of your arm before Tobias shears it back. Maud keeps a cutting, and says she'll offer it to you again in Stormbloom.\nopt.left.say.2 [d878fc] = 荆棘扯走了你的袖子和你胳膊上的一些皮，托比亚斯才把它剪了回去。莫德留了一截插枝，说风暴月时会再给你一次机会。\n# en: Wade in and grab the root\nopt.right.label [ac8d4f] = 蹚进去抓住根\n# en: You wade through thorns to the root and hold it. The hedge goes still, then curls round you instead, gently. You're scratched to ribbons and it's yours.\nopt.right.say.1 [f23f29] = 你蹚过荆棘找到根，把它握住。树篱静了下来，然后转而轻轻地缠住你。你被划得遍体鳞伤，但它是你的了。\n# en: You get the root and it gets you. Tobias shears the hedge back by dawn. Maud keeps a cutting for Stormbloom.\nopt.right.say.2 [01dde4] = 你抓住了根，它也抓住了你。天亮前托比亚斯把树篱剪了回去。莫德为风暴月留了一截插枝。\n\n@thread index_quest\n# en: Footnote's drawer\nname [f8bf3c] = 脚注的抽屉\n# en: The library cat guards a card for a book eleven years overdue.\npremise [7b0eb8] = 图书馆的猫守着一张卡片，卡上那本书已经逾期十一年。\n# en: Footnote was guarding the card of a book on wards, borrowed by Professor Thorne eleven years ago.\njournal.1 [58af1d] = 脚注守着的是一本结界书的借书卡，十一年前由索恩教授借走。\n# en: A proper herring bought you the card, and apparently the cat.\njournal.2 [1f0570] = 一条像样的鲱鱼换来了那张卡片，看样子还换来了那只猫。\n# en: You walked the night hush with the old lamp and marked which shelf went quiet last.\njournal.3 [16e205] = 你提着旧灯走了一趟夜间静巡，记下了哪个书架最后安静下来。\n# en: You followed the wandering section on a string for six hours.\njournal.4 [c09fce] = 你牵着一根线，跟着游走的书区走了六个小时。\n# en: The first night the shelves moved, the book stayed lost.\njournal.5 [200a60] = 书架移动的第一个夜里，那本书还是没找到。\n# en: You found the book, eleven years overdue. Every book falls open at the right page for you now.\njournal.6 [ecf968] = 你找到了那本逾期十一年的书。如今每本书都会为你翻到正确的那一页。\n\n@event library_footnote_drawer\n# en: Footnote's Drawer\ntitle [a6341c] = 脚注的抽屉\n# en: Footnote has sat on one catalogue drawer for a week and hisses at anyone who comes near it. The label underneath him says OVERDUE, ELEVEN YEARS. The head librarian says whoever finds that book is welcome to the trick of finding books.\ntext [426aad] = 脚注已经在一个目录抽屉上蹲了一周，谁靠近就冲谁哈气。它身下的标签写着：逾期，十一年。总馆员说，谁找到那本书，找书的诀窍就归谁。\n# en: A long library catalogue cabinet, a large cat sitting immovably on one drawer, a student crouching to read its label.\nscene [13482f] = 一排长长的图书馆目录柜，一只大猫纹丝不动地蹲在其中一个抽屉上，一个学生蹲下来读抽屉的标签。\n# en: Find out what he's guarding\nopt.left.label [f87004] = 看看它守着什么\n# en: You read the card around his tail. A book on wards, borrowed by a Professor Thorne, eleven years ago. Footnote looks at you as if you've finally asked the right question.\nopt.left.say.1 [0745bd] = 你读了它尾巴周围的卡片。一本关于护界的书，十一年前被一位索恩教授借走。脚注看着你，好像你终于问对了问题。\n# en: Shelve around him\nopt.right.label [cb3631] = 绕开它上架\n# en: You shelve around Footnote for the rest of the shift. He watches every book you put away, as if marking them.\nopt.right.say.1 [706e72] = 这一整班你都绕着脚注上架。它盯着你放回的每一本书，好像在打分。\n\n@event library_fish_footnote\n# en: Bribing the Cat\ntitle [e6afcd] = 贿赂猫\n# en: Footnote won't move from the drawer for less than fish. Good fish, from the village, not the refectory's. The head librarian says this is \"between you and the cat\".\ntext [0b74f5] = 不给鱼，脚注就不挪窝。要好鱼，村里的，不是膳堂的。总馆员说这是“你和猫之间的事”。\n# en: A student offering a small paper parcel of fish to a large, unimpressed cat on a library cabinet.\nscene [f6e2c8] = 一个学生向一只大猫递上一小包纸裹的鱼，猫蹲在图书馆的柜子上，毫不动容。\n# en: Buy him a proper herring\nopt.left.label [3982c9] = 给它买条好鲱鱼\n# en: Footnote eats the herring slowly, looking at you the whole time, then steps off the drawer and onto your lap. The card is yours. So, apparently, is the cat.\nopt.left.say.1 [22fcf2] = 脚注慢慢吃着鲱鱼，全程盯着你，然后走下抽屉，走到你腿上。卡片是你的了。看样子，猫也是。\n# en: Wait him out\nopt.right.label [d67522] = 跟它耗\n# en: You wait. Footnote waits better. At closing he yawns, stretches, and lies back down on the drawer.\nopt.right.say.1 [e00cf3] = 你等。脚注更会等。闭馆时它打了个哈欠，伸了个懒腰，又在抽屉上躺下了。\n\n@event library_hush_lamp\n# en: The Night Hush\ntitle [d45a3e] = 夜间静巡\n# en: The overdue book will only be on the shelves the night hush sends it to. The head librarian lends you the old lamp. \"Walk the hush,\" she says, \"and listen to which shelf goes quiet last.\"\ntext [5a1796] = 那本逾期的书，只会出现在夜间静巡把它送去的书架上。总馆员把那盏旧灯借给你。“去巡一趟静，”她说，“听听哪个书架最后安静下来。”\n# en: A dark library reading room at night, a student walking the shelves with a lamp held high while the books seem to lean toward the light.\nscene [5702f8] = 夜里黑暗的图书馆阅览室，一个学生高举着灯走过书架，书似乎都朝光倾过来。\n# en: Walk the hush and listen\nopt.left.label [963e2f] = 巡一趟静，仔细听\n# en: Three laps, and on the third, the shelves on the north side go quiet a heartbeat after the rest. You mark it on your map. The lamp seems pleased with you.\nopt.left.say.1 [0b4ada] = 走了三圈，第三圈时，北边的书架比其他的晚一拍才安静下来。你在地图上做了标记。那盏灯似乎对你很满意。\n# en: Every shelf goes quiet at once, which is either very helpful or a joke at your expense. You walk until midnight and mark nothing.\nopt.left.say.2 [8540fc] = 所有书架同时安静下来，这要么非常有用，要么是在拿你开玩笑。你一直巡到午夜，什么也没标出来。\n# en: Ask the head librarian outright\nopt.right.label [dab481] = 直接问总馆员\n# en: \"If I told you,\" she says, \"you wouldn't have found it.\" She gives you extra shelving for asking.\nopt.right.say.1 [47780a] = “要是我告诉你，”她说，“你就不会找到了。”她因为你问了这句，给你多派了些上架的活。\n\n@event library_wandering_section\n# en: The Wandering Section\ntitle [ed4770] = 游走的书区\n# en: The section the book belongs to wanders. It was by the stairs on Monday and in the map room on Wednesday. The only way to follow it is to tie a string to the end shelf and walk where it goes, however long that takes.\ntext [7df408] = 那本书所在的书区会游走。周一它在楼梯边，周三在地图室。想跟上它，只能把一根线拴在末端书架上，它去哪儿你跟到哪儿，不管要多久。\n# en: A student trailing a long ball of string through a maze of library shelves that have visibly rearranged themselves behind them.\nscene [4023b6] = 一个学生拖着一长团线穿行在图书馆的书架迷宫里，身后的书架明显已经重新排列过了。\n# en: Follow it with string\nopt.left.label [66d2f0] = 拴根线跟着它\n# en: You follow it for six hours, through rooms you didn't know the library had. At the end you know its route by heart and never want to see a ball of string again.\nopt.left.say.1 [fd7610] = 你跟了它六个小时，穿过一些你不知道图书馆有的房间。最后你把它的路线背得滚瓜烂熟，再也不想看见线团了。\n# en: Wait for it to come back\nopt.right.label [4c2d5a] = 等它自己回来\n# en: You read at the desk until the section wanders past again, a day later. You learn nothing about its route, and a little about medieval ward-lattices.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bbd90] = 你在桌边看书，等那个书区一天后又游过来。你没弄清它的路线，倒是对中世纪的护界晶格懂了一点。\n\n@event library_overdue_book\n# en: Eleven Years Overdue\ntitle [8b1cf2] = 逾期十一年\n# en: Tonight the shelves are moving. Somewhere in the wandering section is the book, eleven years overdue, and every book you touch tries to fall open at the wrong page. {flag(index_fish): Footnote is on your shoulder.|You're on your own.}\ntext [b8e5d2] = 今晚书架在移动。那本逾期十一年的书就在游走的书区某处，你碰到的每一本书都想翻到错的那一页。{flag(index_fish): 脚注蹲在你肩上。|你只能靠自己。}\n# en: A student high on a rolling ladder in a library where the shelves are visibly moving, a cat on their shoulder, one dusty book glowing faintly.\nscene [1570eb] = 一个学生站在移动梯的高处，图书馆的书架明显在移动，一只猫蹲在学生肩上，一本落满灰的书发着微光。\n# en: Find the page\nopt.left.label [672d94] = 找到那一页\n# en: Your hand finds the spine before your eyes do, and the book falls open at exactly the page it was borrowed for. Every book you touch afterwards does the same. The head librarian stamps the card, eleven years late, without a word.\nopt.left.say.1 [0c241a] = 你的手比眼睛先摸到书脊，书正好翻开在当年被借走的那一页。此后你碰到的每本书都这样。总馆员一言不发地在卡片上盖了章，晚了十一年。\n# en: You get lost on the seventh floor among shelves that don't exist by day. Footnote leads you out at dawn, looking smug. The card is still on the drawer.\nopt.left.say.2 [e48270] = 你在一些白天并不存在的书架之间迷失在七楼。天亮时脚注一脸得意地把你领了出来。卡片还在抽屉上。\n# en: Pay a senior clerk for their map\nopt.right.label [cbd312] = 花钱买资深职员的地图\n# en: The senior clerk's map is ten years out of date and exactly right where it matters. You find the book, and it falls open at the page it was borrowed for. Every book does, after that.\nopt.right.say.1 [c585bc] = 资深职员的地图过时了十年，但在要紧的地方分毫不差。你找到了那本书，它翻开在被借走的那一页。从那以后，每本书都这样。\n# en: The map is ten years out of date and mostly wrong. Footnote leads you out at dawn. The book stays lost, for now.\nopt.right.say.2 [d1b216] = 地图过时了十年，大部分是错的。天亮时脚注把你领了出来。那本书暂时还丢着。\n\n@thread rapport_quest\n# en: Two taps on the bar\nname [12c408] = 吧台上敲两下\n# en: The Newt's landlady stops fights with two taps and one sentence.\npremise [9210b7] = 蝾螈酒馆的老板娘敲两下吧台、说一句话，就能把一场架拦下来。\n# en: \"Can't teach it,\" the landlady said. \"You earn it. Learn the room, buy the room, work the room.\"\njournal.1 [1909d4] = “这教不了，”老板娘说，“得自己挣来。摸清这屋子，请这屋子喝一轮，镇住这屋子。”\n# en: You learned the old barge-man's eel story and told it back.\njournal.2 [7b5c40] = 你学会了老驳船工的鳗鱼故事，又讲给大家听。\n# en: You stood the regulars' corner a round. They know your name now.\njournal.3 [840029] = 你请熟客角喝了一轮。现在他们都知道你的名字了。\n# en: Fourteen hours on your feet: now you can read the room from across it.\njournal.4 [f0d193] = 站了十四个小时：现在你隔着整间屋子就能看懂里面的人。\n# en: The first night the room was yours, it got away from you.\njournal.5 [8b2bf1] = 第一次让你独自看场的那夜，场子没镇住。\n# en: You held the Newt on a packed night. The landlady knew at once, and nodded.\njournal.6 [9a40b0] = 一个满座的夜里，你镇住了蝾螈酒馆。老板娘一回来就知道了，点了点头。\n\n@event tavern_two_taps\n# en: Two Taps on the Bar\ntitle [6ad648] = 吧台上敲两下\n# en: Two barge-men are halfway out of their chairs when the landlady taps the bar twice, says one sentence you don't catch, and the whole room laughs instead. The fight is over before it began. She catches you staring. \"Want to know how?\"\ntext [5be858] = 两个驳船工半个身子已经离了椅子，老板娘在吧台上敲了两下，说了一句你没听清的话，整个酒馆就笑了起来。架还没打就散了。她发现你在盯着看。“想知道怎么做到的吗？”\n# en: A crowded tavern on the edge of a brawl, a landlady tapping the bar twice and the whole room suddenly laughing instead.\nscene [0feadf] = 一家拥挤的酒馆眼看要打起来，老板娘在吧台上敲了两下，满屋子的人忽然都笑了。\n# en: \"Yes. Teach me.\"\nopt.left.label [8d2667] = “想。教我。”\n# en: \"Can't teach it,\" she says. \"You earn it. Learn the room, buy the room, work the room.\" She taps the bar twice at you, and you laugh, and you don't know why.\nopt.left.say.1 [f853d9] = “教不了，”她说，“得自己挣。了解这间屋子，请这间屋子喝一轮，在这间屋子里干活。”她冲你在吧台上敲了两下，你笑了，却不知道为什么。\n# en: Keep pouring\nopt.right.label [d1291a] = 接着倒酒\n# en: You keep pouring. The barge-men buy each other a drink and sing. The landlady watches you, thoughtfully, for the rest of the shift.\nopt.right.say.1 [39eec5] = 你接着倒酒。驳船工们互相请了一杯，唱起歌来。这一班剩下的时间，老板娘若有所思地看着你。\n\n@event tavern_eel_story\n# en: The Eel Story\ntitle [77827b] = 鳗鱼的故事\n# en: The old barge-man's eel story is the most famous story at the Newt. Regulars mouth along. The landlady says if you can tell it as well as he does, the room will be half yours. He's willing to teach it, slowly, for an evening.\ntext [891f3d] = 老驳船工的鳗鱼故事是蝾螈酒馆最有名的故事。熟客们会跟着念。老板娘说，你要是能讲得和他一样好，这屋子就有一半归你。他愿意花一个晚上，慢慢教你。\n# en: A barge-man at a tavern table with his arms spread wide, telling a story to a student taking notes on a bar napkin.\nscene [0b5a36] = 酒馆桌边一位老驳船工张开双臂讲故事，一个学生在吧台的餐巾上记笔记。\n# en: Learn it and tell it back\nopt.left.label [25ce86] = 学会它，讲回去\n# en: You tell it back that same night, to the same corner, and get the laugh at the eel's second escape. The barge-man wipes his eyes and buys you a cider.\nopt.left.say.1 [692985] = 当晚你就对着同一个角落讲了回去，在鳗鱼第二次逃跑时赢得了笑声。老驳船工抹着眼泪，请你喝了一杯苹果酒。\n# en: You get the eel's name wrong and the ending backwards. The regulars are very kind about it, which is worse.\nopt.left.say.2 [f8158d] = 你把鳗鱼的名字讲错了，结尾也讲反了。熟客们对此非常体贴，这反而更糟。\n# en: Just enjoy the story\nopt.right.label [0efa4a] = 就好好听故事\n# en: You sit and listen to the eel story twice through, and laugh both times. You couldn't tell it, but you've rarely had a better evening.\nopt.right.say.1 [03bb5d] = 你坐下来把鳗鱼的故事从头听了两遍，两遍都笑了。你讲不出来，但你很少有过比这更好的晚上。\n\n@event tavern_regulars_round\n# en: The Regulars' Corner\ntitle [98ab7f] = 熟客角\n# en: The regulars' corner has never had a round bought by staff. The landlady says that's exactly why you should. \"Nobody listens to a story from someone who's never stood them a drink.\"\ntext [cecaa4] = 熟客角从没被店员请过一轮。老板娘说这正是你该请的理由。“没请人喝过一杯的人讲的故事，没人会听。”\n# en: A student setting down a tray of mugs at a crowded corner table in a tavern, the regulars raising them in a toast.\nscene [c4c30c] = 一个学生把一托盘杯子放在酒馆拥挤的角落桌上，熟客们举杯敬酒。\n# en: Stand the corner a round\nopt.left.label [e38d06] = 请熟客角喝一轮\n# en: The corner raises the mugs to you, by name, which you didn't know they knew. For the rest of the night they call you over to settle every argument.\nopt.left.say.1 [96836a] = 熟客角举杯敬你，叫出了你的名字，你都不知道他们知道你叫什么。那一晚剩下的时间，每有争执他们都叫你过去评理。\n# en: Keep your tips\nopt.right.label [224ca7] = 留着你的小费\n# en: You pocket your tips. The corner doesn't notice either way, which is rather the point.\nopt.right.say.1 [2ed62f] = 你把小费揣进兜里。熟客角对此毫无察觉，这恰恰就是重点。\n\n@event tavern_double_shift\n# en: The Double Shift\ntitle [6df679] = 双班\n# en: Two servers are down with Lake Fever, and the landlady needs a double shift: lunch through closing. \"You'll learn more about a room in fourteen hours,\" she says, \"than in a term of evenings.\"\ntext [d66301] = 两个侍应得了湖热病，老板娘需要一个人上双班：从午饭一直到打烊。“十四个钟头，”她说，“你对一间屋子的了解，会比一学期的晚班还多。”\n# en: A tired student wiping down a bar at closing time after a very long day, the landlady counting the till beside them.\nscene [6435ce] = 打烊时分，一个疲惫的学生在擦吧台，干了很长的一天，老板娘在旁边数钱柜。\n# en: Work the double\nopt.left.label [63ca5c] = 上这个双班\n# en: Fourteen hours on your feet. By closing you can tell who wants to talk and who wants to be left alone from across the room. Your feet are a separate, angrier person.\nopt.left.say.1 [334ef3] = 站了十四个钟头。打烊时，你隔着整间屋子就能看出谁想聊天、谁想清静。你的脚已经成了另一个更暴躁的人。\n# en: Turn it down\nopt.right.label [99bc49] = 推掉\n# en: You say you can't. The landlady works it herself, and is short with you for a week.\nopt.right.say.1 [e083d3] = 你说你干不了。老板娘自己顶上了，一周都对你没好脸色。\n\n@event tavern_festival_room\n# en: The Room Is Yours\ntitle [05ee85] = 这间屋子归你了\n# en: The landlady is away for the night and the Newt is packed to the rafters, two barges in and a fight brewing by the fire. It's yours to hold. {flag(rapport_eel) and flag(rapport_round) and flag(rapport_shift): You know this room now.|You know some of this room.}\ntext [2f03ff] = 老板娘今晚不在，蝾螈酒馆挤得满满当当，来了两条驳船的人，壁炉边还有一场架在酝酿。这间屋子归你镇住。{flag(rapport_eel) and flag(rapport_round) and flag(rapport_shift): 你现在了解这间屋子。|这间屋子你只了解一部分。}\n# en: A packed tavern on a festival night, a student standing on a chair telling a story while the crowd hangs on every word.\nscene [da8c84] = 节日夜挤得满满的酒馆，一个学生站在椅子上讲故事，人群屏息听着每一个字。\n# en: Tap the bar twice\nopt.left.label [ef6f14] = 在吧台上敲两下\n# en: You tap the bar twice, say one sentence about an eel, and the room laughs. The fight by the fire turns into a song. When the landlady comes back, she knows at once. She just nods.\nopt.left.say.1 [a874d5] = 你在吧台上敲了两下，说了一句关于鳗鱼的话，满屋子都笑了。壁炉边的那场架变成了一首歌。老板娘回来时一眼就明白了。她只是点点头。\n# en: The tap comes out as a knock and the laugh never comes. A chair breaks, and so do some glasses, and the fine comes out of your wages. Next festival night, she says, you can try again.\nopt.left.say.2 [b92402] = 敲出来成了叩门声，笑声始终没来。一把椅子断了，几只杯子碎了，罚款从你的工钱里扣。下一个节日夜，她说，你可以再试。\n# en: Buy the first round, then talk\nopt.right.label [8de009] = 先请一轮，再开口\n# en: A round on the house first, then a story while they drink it. By the time the mugs are empty, nobody remembers wanting to fight. The landlady hears about it before she's through the door.\nopt.right.say.1 [1ac8d6] = 先请全场喝一轮，趁他们喝的时候讲个故事。等杯子见了底，谁也不记得自己想打架了。老板娘还没进门就听说了。\n# en: They drink the round and fight anyway. You mop up with the rest of the staff. Next festival night, the landlady says.\nopt.right.say.2 [ca000a] = 他们喝了那一轮，照样打了起来。你和其他店员一起收拾残局。下一个节日夜，老板娘说。\n\n@thread distil_quest\n# en: Pell's oldest drawer\nname [16c158] = 佩尔最老的抽屉\n# en: A yellowed card in Pell's oldest drawer: a draught \"of your own strength\".\npremise [c0d0e8] = 佩尔最老的抽屉里有一张发黄的卡片：一剂“取自己之力”的药。\n# en: You copied out the old draught's recipe. Pell corrected one of your measures, which is permission.\njournal.1 [849870] = 你抄下了那剂老药的方子。佩尔替你改了一个剂量，这就算是准许了。\n# en: You counted four hundred and twelve drops with Pell and didn't lose count. \"Fair,\" he said.\njournal.2 [0f0ad2] = 你陪佩尔数了四百一十二滴，一次也没数错。“还行，”他说。\n# en: You bought the good moonwort and carried it home without humming.\njournal.3 [aca8b4] = 你买下了上好的月蕨，一路没哼歌地把它带回了家。\n# en: You tasted the draught first, the daughters' way, and spent a day in bed learning where it pulls from.\njournal.4 [970669] = 你照女儿们的法子先尝了一口，在床上躺了一天，弄清了它从哪里抽走力气。\n# en: The first brew boiled over and took more out of you than it should.\njournal.5 [38da21] = 第一次熬药时锅沸溢了，从你身上抽走的比该抽的还多。\n# en: The draught came down to one shining drop. Pell stopped counting. \"Fair.\"\njournal.6 [dfc6ac] = 那剂药凝成了闪亮的一滴。佩尔停下了数数。“还行。”\n\n@event pell_oldest_drawer\n# en: The Oldest Drawer\ntitle [6f192c] = 最老的抽屉\n# en: Restocking, you open the oldest drawer at the bottom of Pell's wall. Inside is one yellowed card: a draught \"of your own strength\", in a hand older than Pell's. Pell sees it and counts to three under his breath. \"That one costs the brewer,\" he says. \"Measure twice.\"\ntext [24f57e] = 补货时，你打开了佩尔那面墙最底下最老的抽屉。里面只有一张泛黄的卡片：一剂“取自本身之力”的药，笔迹比佩尔的还老。佩尔看见了，低声数到三。“那剂药要熬药的人付出代价，”他说，“量两遍。”\n# en: An old apothecary's wall of tiny drawers, one at the very bottom pulled open to reveal a yellowed recipe card, a gruff old man watching from the counter.\nscene [186551] = 一面老药铺的小抽屉墙，最底下的一个被拉开，露出一张泛黄的配方卡，一个粗声粗气的老人在柜台边看着。\n# en: Copy the recipe\nopt.left.label [077927] = 抄下配方\n# en: You copy it out carefully. Pell watches you do it and says nothing, then corrects one of your measures in your copy. That is the closest he comes to permission.\nopt.left.say.1 [3d8ed7] = 你仔仔细细地抄了一份。佩尔看着你抄，什么也没说，然后改正了你抄本里的一个剂量。这已经是他最接近“准许”的表示了。\n# en: Close the drawer\nopt.right.label [f59ddd] = 关上抽屉\n# en: You close it. Pell nods once and goes back to his scales. Later you notice he has relabelled the drawer, clearly, \"Not yet.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [6b9f22] = 你把它关上了。佩尔点了一下头，回去看他的秤。后来你发现他把抽屉的标签换了，写得清清楚楚：“还没到时候。”\n\n@event pell_counting_drops\n# en: Counting Drops\ntitle [77984e] = 数药滴\n# en: Pell distills by the drop, all night, counting aloud. \"If you mean to try the old draught,\" he says, \"you'll count with me first. Lose count once and we start again.\"\ntext [be6174] = 佩尔蒸馏是一滴一滴来的，整夜大声数着。“你要是想试那剂老药，”他说，“就先陪我数一夜。数错一次就从头来。”\n# en: A dim apothecary at night, an old man and a student bent over a slowly dripping still, both counting under their breath.\nscene [762f29] = 夜里昏暗的药铺，一个老人和一个学生俯身在一台缓缓滴落的蒸馏器前，两人都在低声数数。\n# en: Count all night with him\nopt.left.label [5b4fd1] = 陪他数一整夜\n# en: Four hundred and twelve drops, and you don't lose count once. At dawn Pell says \"Fair,\" which is once a decade, and lets you stopper the bottle.\nopt.left.say.1 [f234d7] = 四百一十二滴，你一次都没数错。天亮时佩尔说了声“公道”，十年才说一次，并让你给瓶子塞上了塞。\n# en: You lose count at three hundred and something. Pell starts again without comment. So do you. You lose it again.\nopt.left.say.2 [504e92] = 数到三百多时你数乱了。佩尔一言不发地从头开始。你也是。你又数乱了。\n# en: Go home and sleep\nopt.right.label [9aa5d0] = 回家睡觉\n# en: You go home. Pell counts on alone, steadily, through the night. In the morning the bottle is full and labelled, and he says nothing about it.\nopt.right.say.1 [45817c] = 你回家了。佩尔一个人稳稳地数了一整夜。早上瓶子满了，贴好了标签，他对此只字未提。\n\n@event pell_buy_moonwort\n# en: Moonwort\ntitle [66d660] = 月蕨\n# en: The old draught needs moonwort, and the only good moonwort in the village is the jar on Pell's top shelf, labelled \"Do not hum near.\" Pell sells it to staff at cost. The cost is not small.\ntext [3ca560] = 那剂老药需要月蕨，而村里唯一好的月蕨就在佩尔最高那层架子上的罐子里，标签写着“附近勿哼歌”。佩尔按成本价卖给店员。成本可不低。\n# en: An apothecary counter with a small jar labelled \"Moonwort. Do not hum near.\", a student counting out coins beside it.\nscene [18cf79] = 药铺柜台上一个标着“月蕨，附近勿哼歌”的小罐子，一个学生在旁边数着硬币。\n# en: Buy the moonwort\nopt.left.label [d9f851] = 买下月蕨\n# en: Pell weighs it twice and wraps it in waxed paper. You carry it home without humming, which is harder than it sounds.\nopt.left.say.1 [3e1ea5] = 佩尔称了两遍，用蜡纸包好。你一路没哼歌把它带回了家，这比听起来难。\n# en: Try the cheap substitute\nopt.right.label [162b22] = 试试便宜的替代品\n# en: The substitute smells wrong the moment you open it. Pell takes it from you without a word and puts it in the bin.\nopt.right.say.1 [15c4d9] = 替代品一打开就不对味。佩尔一言不发地从你手里拿走，扔进了垃圾桶。\n\n@event pell_daughters_shortcut\n# en: The Daughters' Shortcut\ntitle [ac5208] = 女儿们的捷径\n# en: Pell's daughters say there's a shortcut: learn what the draught takes out of a brewer by tasting a little of it first. \"Father hates it,\" says one. \"It works,\" says the other. \"You'll be ill for a day.\"\ntext [4ba2b2] = 佩尔的女儿们说有条捷径：先尝一点，弄清这剂药会从熬药人身上拿走什么。“父亲讨厌这个，”一个说。“但管用，”另一个说，“你会病一天。”\n# en: The back room of an apothecary, a student drinking a small cup of something dark while two women watch with professional interest.\nscene [33c062] = 药铺的里屋，一个学生喝下一小杯深色的东西，两个女人带着职业性的兴趣看着。\n# en: Try it on yourself\nopt.left.label [164155] = 在自己身上试\n# en: It takes the strength out of your knees and then, slowly, gives it back. You spend a day in bed. When you get up, you know exactly where the draught will pull from.\nopt.left.say.1 [39fb0c] = 它先把力气从你膝盖里抽走，再慢慢还回来。你在床上躺了一天。起来时，你清楚地知道这剂药会从哪里抽力。\n# en: Do it Pell's way\nopt.right.label [7b2559] = 按佩尔的规矩来\n# en: You decline. The daughters shrug. Pell, who was listening, corrects a label more firmly than it needed.\nopt.right.say.1 [fb421f] = 你谢绝了。女儿们耸耸肩。一直在听的佩尔，把一张标签改得比需要的更用力。\n\n@event pell_draught\n# en: Of Your Own Strength\ntitle [96c35c] = 取自本身之力\n# en: Pell closes the shop early, sets the old still on the bench and holds the lamp himself. \"Brew it,\" he says. \"I'll count.\" The recipe card lies beside you. {flag(distil_drops) and flag(distil_moonwort) and flag(distil_shortcut): You know every step of it.|There are steps you haven't practised.}\ntext [1dc449] = 佩尔提早关了店，把那台老蒸馏器放在工作台上，亲自掌灯。“熬吧，”他说，“我来数。”配方卡就放在你手边。{flag(distil_drops) and flag(distil_moonwort) and flag(distil_shortcut): 每一步你都了然于心。|有几步你还没练过。}\n# en: An apothecary's still at night, a student drawing a single shining drop out of a dark mixture while an old man holds the lamp.\nscene [ebd457] = 夜里药铺的蒸馏器旁，一个学生从深色混合物中萃出一滴闪亮的液体，一个老人举着灯。\n# en: Brew the draught\nopt.left.label [b27087] = 熬这剂药\n# en: The draught comes down to one shining drop, and as it falls you feel exactly what it took from you, and how to take it again. Pell stops counting. \"Fair,\" he says, twice in one year.\nopt.left.say.1 [50ca10] = 那剂药浓缩成闪亮的一滴，落下时你清楚地感觉到它从你身上拿走了什么，以及怎样再拿一次。佩尔停止了数数。“公道，”他说，一年里说了两次。\n# en: The mixture boils over and takes more out of you than it should. Pell puts the card back in the drawer and writes \"Don't.\" on the front. Next term, perhaps.\nopt.left.say.2 [fbced8] = 混合物沸溢了，从你身上拿走的比该拿的多。佩尔把卡片放回抽屉，在正面写上“别”。也许下学期吧。\n# en: Use Pell's own moonwort stock\nopt.right.label [f7c024] = 用佩尔自己的月蕨存货\n# en: With the good stock, it comes down clean to one drop. You feel it pull, and you feel where it pulls from. Pell writes the cost in his ledger, and your name beside the recipe.\nopt.right.say.1 [43bab8] = 用了好存货，它干干净净地浓缩成一滴。你感觉到它在拉扯，也感觉到它从哪儿拉扯。佩尔把成本记进账本，在配方旁写上了你的名字。\n# en: Even with his best stock it boils over. Pell writes \"Don't.\" on the drawer and the cost in the ledger. Next term, perhaps.\nopt.right.say.2 [2fc843] = 就算用了他最好的存货，它还是沸溢了。佩尔在抽屉上写了“别”，把成本记进了账本。也许下学期吧。\n\n@thread tailwind_quest\n# en: The jar of wind\nname [4a78a8] = 罐中之风\n# en: A stoppered jar on the dead-letter shelf: FOR WHOEVER IS FAST ENOUGH.\npremise [5304e7] = 死信架上一只塞着瓶塞的罐子：给够快的人。\n# en: You took the wind jar down. The loft master wrote your name under one too faded to read.\njournal.1 [6f23b3] = 你把那罐风取了下来。阁楼主管在一个褪色到看不清的名字下面写上了你的名字。\n# en: The old fisherman flew the jar once. Never stop, he said. Stopping's what kills it.\njournal.2 [67182d] = 老渔夫曾带着这只罐子飞过。别停下，他说。一停，风就死了。\n# en: You beat the gulls from the jetty to the far bank, and the wind in the jar turned over once.\njournal.3 [bfb8be] = 你从码头到对岸赢过了海鸥，罐子里的风翻了个身。\n# en: You had your broom trued. It flies straight for the first time in living memory.\njournal.4 [b033c5] = 你把扫帚找人校直了。有人记得以来，它头一回飞得笔直。\n# en: The first storm run, a gust took you into the shallows.\njournal.5 [878b0c] = 第一次冒着风暴送件时，一阵狂风把你吹进了浅滩。\n# en: You crossed the lake in a storm with the wind behind you. It stays when you call it.\njournal.6 [83e9e9] = 你在风暴中带着身后的风飞过了湖。如今你一唤，它就留下。\n\n@event courier_wind_jar\n# en: Wind in a Jar\ntitle [396974] = 罐中之风\n# en: On the dead-letter shelf, behind a parcel addressed to nobody, is a stoppered jar with a wind in it, going round and round. The label says FOR WHOEVER IS FAST ENOUGH. The loft master says someone flew with it once, before her time.\ntext [ccddb6] = 在死信架上，一个寄给“无人”的包裹后面，有一只塞着瓶塞的罐子，里面有一阵风在打转。标签上写着：给够快的人。阁楼主管说，在她之前有人带着它飞过。\n# en: A courier loft with a dusty shelf of unclaimed letters, a student holding up a stoppered jar in which a small wind is visibly whirling.\nscene [973bd6] = 一间信使阁楼，落满灰的架子上摆着无人认领的信，一个学生举起一只塞着瓶塞的罐子，里面一小阵风明显在打转。\n# en: Take the jar down\nopt.left.label [b3ece8] = 把罐子取下来\n# en: The jar hums in your hands, and every broom in the loft leans toward you. You strap it on for one loop of the yard, just to see, and come back windblown and breathless. The loft master writes your name on the jar's label, under a name too faded to read.\nopt.left.say.1 [734087] = 罐子在你手里嗡嗡作响，阁楼里每一把扫帚都朝你倾过来。你把它绑上，绕着院子飞了一圈，只为试试，回来时头发被吹得乱七八糟，气都喘不匀。阁楼主管把你的名字写在罐子的标签上，就在一个褪色得认不出的名字下面。\n# en: Leave it on the shelf\nopt.right.label [52ecc9] = 留在架子上\n# en: You leave it for whoever is fast enough. You fly your round. The jar's wind stirs the dead letters as you go.\nopt.right.say.1 [967b48] = 你把它留给够快的人。你飞你的线路。你走时，罐中之风吹动了那些死信。\n\n@event courier_fisherman_waves\n# en: The Fisherman Waves\ntitle [c74709] = 渔夫挥手了\n# en: The old fisherman in the reed beds has never waved at a courier. Today, seeing the jar strapped to your broom, he waves, and beckons you down. \"That jar,\" he says. \"I flew it once. Fastest year of my life.\"\ntext [9be0ca] = 芦苇丛里那个老渔夫从来不向信使挥手。今天他看见绑在你扫帚上的罐子，挥了挥手，招呼你下来。“那个罐子，”他说，“我带它飞过。那是我这辈子最快的一年。”\n# en: An old fisherman in a small boat on a grey lake waving up at a student on a broom flying low overhead.\nscene [8d25dd] = 灰色湖面上一条小船里，一个老渔夫朝低空飞过的骑扫帚学生挥手。\n# en: Land and ask him about it\nopt.left.label [6354d0] = 落下来问他\n# en: He tells you, slowly, between casts: you don't fly the wind, you let it get behind you and keep going. Never stop. \"Stopping's what kills it,\" he says, and goes back to his line.\nopt.left.say.1 [cedb6e] = 他在两次甩竿之间慢慢告诉你：风不是你驾驭的，你让它绕到身后，然后一直往前。永不停下。“停下才会害死它，”他说完，又回去守他的鱼线。\n# en: He talks about fish for an hour, then says the light's going, and rows off. You'll have to come back.\nopt.left.say.2 [6f6d6d] = 他讲了一个钟头的鱼，然后说天色晚了，划走了。你只能改天再来。\n# en: Wave back and fly on\nopt.right.label [0c1d4c] = 挥手回应，继续飞\n# en: You wave and fly on with your round, on time. He watches you go, and doesn't wave again.\nopt.right.say.1 [fcc4c8] = 你挥了挥手，继续飞你的线路，准时送达。他看着你离开，没再挥手。\n\n@event courier_race_gulls\n# en: Racing the Gulls\ntitle [9ce849] = 和海鸥比赛\n# en: The gulls over the lake race couriers for fun. {flag(tailwind_fisherman): The fisherman says the jar's wind won't follow you until you've beaten them once, flat out, from the jetty to the far bank.|The jar's label has grown a second line, in the same faded hand: BEAT THE GULLS FIRST. Jetty to far bank, flat out.}\ntext [b822a4] = 湖上的海鸥喜欢和信使比赛取乐。{flag(tailwind_fisherman): 渔夫说，除非你全速从码头飞到对岸、赢它们一次，否则罐中之风不会跟随你。|罐子的标签上多了一行字，还是那褪色的笔迹：先赢海鸥。从码头到对岸，全速。}\n# en: A student on a broom flying flat out over a lake in a cloud of wheeling gulls, letters fluttering in a satchel.\nscene [ea7b1f] = 一个学生骑着扫帚在一群盘旋的海鸥中全速飞过湖面，挎包里的信在扑扑作响。\n# en: Race them flat out\nopt.left.label [2cf84d] = 全速和它们比\n# en: You hit the far bank a beak ahead of the lead gull. The wind in the jar turns over once, like a cat settling. The gulls follow you home, offended.\nopt.left.say.1 [137ff7] = 你比领头的海鸥早一个鸟嘴到达对岸。罐子里的风翻了个身，像一只猫安顿下来。海鸥们跟着你回家，很是不服。\n# en: You clip a wave at full speed and skip across the lake like a stone. The gulls are delighted. You are wet to the bone.\nopt.left.say.2 [c26046] = 你全速擦到一个浪头，像打水漂的石子一样在湖面上蹦了出去。海鸥们乐坏了。你湿透到了骨头。\n# en: Fly your round steady\nopt.right.label [e3e15d] = 稳稳飞你的线路\n# en: You fly the round steady and deliver everything dry. The gulls lose interest. So does the jar.\nopt.right.say.1 [efd0ae] = 你稳稳飞完线路，所有东西都送得干干的。海鸥们没了兴趣。罐子也是。\n\n@event courier_fix_broom\n# en: The Broom That Pulls Left\ntitle [146da3] = 总往左偏的扫帚\n# en: Your loft broom pulls left, always has. With the jar's wind behind it, it would pull you into the lake. The broom-maker in the village can true it, for a price.\ntext [c55b82] = 你那把阁楼扫帚总往左偏，一直如此。要是罐中之风在后面推，它会把你直接拽进湖里。村里的扫帚匠能把它调直，要价不菲。\n# en: A broom-maker's bench in the village, a craftsman sighting along a broom handle while a student waits with their satchel.\nscene [e4d60b] = 村里扫帚匠的工作台，一位工匠顺着扫帚柄瞄看，一个学生背着挎包在旁边等。\n# en: Pay to have it trued\nopt.left.label [060449] = 付钱把它调直\n# en: The broom-maker sights along it, tuts, and spends an afternoon on it. It flies straight for the first time in living memory. You miss the pull, a little.\nopt.left.say.1 [d2dfad] = 扫帚匠顺着柄瞄了瞄，啧了一声，花了一下午调它。它飞得笔直，有史以来头一回。你倒有点怀念那股偏劲了。\n# en: Learn to fly it crooked\nopt.right.label [dc7c45] = 学会歪着飞\n# en: You learn to lean right all the time. Your back complains, and the broom keeps pulling left out of habit.\nopt.right.say.1 [a75c5e] = 你学会了一直往右倾。你的背在抗议，扫帚出于习惯还是往左偏。\n\n@event courier_storm_delivery\n# en: The Storm Run\ntitle [a96ce0] = 风暴快递\n# en: A storm is down on the lake, and a parcel must cross it tonight. The loft master looks at the jar at your belt, then at you. \"Nobody else will go,\" she says. {flag(tailwind_fisherman) and flag(tailwind_gulls) and flag(tailwind_broom): You know how to let the wind get behind you.|You've never flown it in weather.}\ntext [272501] = 湖上压着一场风暴，一个包裹今晚必须送过去。阁楼主管看看你腰间的罐子，又看看你。“没别人肯去，”她说。{flag(tailwind_fisherman) and flag(tailwind_gulls) and flag(tailwind_broom): 你知道怎么让风绕到身后。|你从没在恶劣天气里飞过它。}\n# en: A student on a broom flying through a storm over a dark lake, a jar of wind unstoppered at their belt, lightning behind them.\nscene [5e21d4] = 一个学生骑着扫帚在黑暗的湖上穿过风暴，腰间一只拔开塞子的罐子里装着风，身后电光闪闪。\n# en: Open the jar and go\nopt.left.label [f5ca84] = 打开罐子，出发\n# en: You pull the stopper and the wind gets behind you and doesn't stop. You cross the lake faster than the lightning can find you. The parcel arrives dry. The wind stays with you after, when you call it.\nopt.left.say.1 [04a55b] = 你拔开瓶塞，风绕到你身后，再也没停。你穿过湖面，快得闪电都找不到你。包裹送到时还是干的。从那以后，你一唤，风就跟着你。\n# en: Halfway across, a gust takes you and you ditch in the shallows. The parcel survives. You barely do. The next storm season, the loft master says, you can try again.\nopt.left.say.2 [17bc2c] = 飞到一半，一阵狂风把你掀了下去，你栽进了浅滩。包裹保住了。你差点没保住。下一个风暴季，阁楼主管说，你可以再试。\n# en: Wait for the lull, then go\nopt.right.label [6f2d3e] = 等风势稍缓再出发\n# en: You wait, nerves screaming, for the gap in the storm, then open the jar and go. The wind gets behind you and stays. The parcel arrives an hour late and dry, and the wind is yours.\nopt.right.say.1 [267a25] = 你等着，神经绷得快要尖叫，等风暴露出那道缝，然后打开罐子出发。风绕到你身后，一直没走。包裹晚到了一个钟头，干干的，那阵风归你了。\n# en: The lull never comes, and you go anyway and ditch in the shallows. The next storm season, the loft master says.\nopt.right.say.2 [1cbca9] = 风势始终没缓，你还是出发了，栽进了浅滩。下一个风暴季，阁楼主管说。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/clubs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/clubs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event duel_practice_bout\n# en: Practice Bout\ntitle [39d05a] = 练习对决\n# en: Wednesday practice. The captain pairs you with a fourth-year who has a reputation for ending bouts quickly. \"Defence or attack,\" the captain says. \"Pick one and commit.\"\ntext [4d124e] = 周三的练习。社长把你和一个以速战速决出名的四年级生分在一组。“防守还是进攻，”社长说，“选一个，然后坚持到底。”\n# en: A chalk circle on a stone floor under high windows, two students mid-duel, a ring of members cheering.\nscene [d4da5e] = 高窗下石地板上的粉笔圈，两名学生正在对决，一圈社员在欢呼。\n# en: Hold your ground\nopt.left.label [52ce0a] = 坚守阵地\n# en: You weather everything she throws until she runs out of breath. The captain nods slowly.\nopt.left.say.1 [209cbc] = 你扛住了她的每一招，直到她气喘吁吁。社长缓缓点头。\n# en: You hold your ground very bravely, right up until you're on the floor.\nopt.left.say.2 [774fd6] = 你非常勇敢地坚守着阵地，一直坚守到躺在地上为止。\n# en: Go on the attack\nopt.right.label [0fc83d] = 主动进攻\n# en: Your opening hex catches her off guard. Point to you. The circle whoops.\nopt.right.say.1 [25eae8] = 你的开局恶咒打了她个措手不及。你得一分。社员们一片欢呼。\n# en: She sidesteps and you hex the wall. The wall wins.\nopt.right.say.2 [1d6bcd] = 她一个侧身，你的恶咒打在了墙上。墙赢了。\n# en: Brace and let them come\nopt.up.label [83a55a] = 坚守，让他们来\n# en: You plant your feet and the fourth-year's whole attack breaks on you, each blow softer than the last, until they run out of ideas. The captain marks the bout a draw and looks at you for a long moment.\nopt.up.say.1 [d280f1] = 你站稳脚跟，那个四年级生的整套攻势都在你身上碎开，一击比一击轻，直到对方没了招数。队长把这局记成平局，盯着你看了很久。\n\n@event duel_bram_hazing\n# en: Initiation, Prefect-Style\ntitle [d6f56a] = 级长式入社仪式\n# en: Bram Hollis, prefect and the Circle's loudest member, has decided new members need \"toughening up\". He's made you hold a shield-charm against him for an hour. The hour is not up.\ntext [1ad653] = 布拉姆·霍利斯——级长，也是决斗社里嗓门最大的一个——认定新人需要“磨练磨练”。他让你顶着他的攻击维持护盾咒一小时。一小时还没到。\n# en: A burly student in a prefect's sash blocking a corridor, wand twirling, a crowd of members watching nervously.\nscene [c5bf3b] = 一个身披级长绶带的魁梧学生堵在走廊里转着魔杖，一群社员紧张地围观。\n# en: Endure it\nopt.left.label [9e8205] = 忍下去\n# en: Your arms shake. You don't drop the charm. Bram grunts, almost approvingly, and finds someone else to torment.\nopt.left.say.1 [cda0fd] = 你的手臂在发抖。你没有放下护盾。布拉姆哼了一声，几乎带着赞许，转身去折磨别人了。\n# en: Drop the shield and duel him\nopt.right.label [df3e2f] = 撤掉护盾，和他决斗\n# en: You disarm him in front of everyone. His wand clatters across the floor. The silence is delicious. He will remember this.\nopt.right.say.1 [df1203] = 你当众缴了他的械。他的魔杖哐啷滚过地板。那份寂静美妙极了。他会记住这一刻的。\n# en: Bram flattens you with a single bored flick. \"Toughening up,\" he reminds the room.\nopt.right.say.2 [675677] = 布拉姆百无聊赖地一挥杖就把你拍扁了。“磨练磨练。”他提醒全场。\n# en: Brace and outlast him\nopt.up.label [8f1af7] = 坚守，熬过他\n# en: You brace, and the hour stops hurting. At the end Bram is out of breath and you are still standing. \"Section nine,\" he mutters. \"Unusual fortitude.\" He writes it down, which from Bram is a medal.\nopt.up.say.1 [6dce0f] = 你一坚守，这一小时就不疼了。到最后布拉姆气喘吁吁，你还站着。“第九条，”他嘟囔着，“耐力异常。”他把它记了下来，这对布拉姆来说就是一枚勋章。\n\n@event duel_lyra_training\n# en: Training Partners\ntitle [6e568e] = 训练搭档\n# en: Lyra finds you after practice. \"The tournament is in two months. You're the only person in this club who doesn't bore me. Train with me. Dawn. Every day. Don't be late.\"\ntext [7cf72d] = 练习结束后莱拉找到你。“锦标赛还有两个月。你是这个社里唯一不让我觉得无聊的人。跟我一起练。黎明。每天。别迟到。”\n# en: A misty lawn at dawn, two figures practising wand forms side by side, frost on the grass.\nscene [bd3dcc] = 黎明时雾气笼罩的草坪，两个身影并肩练习杖法，草上结着霜。\n# en: Decline — you need sleep\nopt.left.label [e4f9c1] = 拒绝——你需要睡觉\n# en: \"Sleep,\" she repeats, as if it's a foreign word. \"Fine.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [ceaa69] = “睡觉。”她重复了一遍，好像那是个外语词。“随你。”\n# en: Agree\nopt.right.label [362be3] = 答应\n# en: She's a merciless coach. Somewhere around the third week, you notice she's started bringing two cups of tea.\nopt.right.say.1 [28ecad] = 她是个毫不留情的教练。大约第三周，你发现她开始带两杯茶来了。\n# en: Since she can't make toast, you bring it. She eats it at the edge of the training ground without a word, and the next morning there are two slices waiting for you.\nopt.right.say.2 [2ee4e1] = 既然她不会烤吐司，你就带了吐司去。她在训练场边上一言不发地吃完，第二天早上，那儿已经有两片在等你了。\n# en: Train at her fire, not her side\nopt.up.label [12aa02] = 陪她烧火，不是陪她练\n# en: You tell her you'll come at dawn, but to spar, not to drill. Lyra's eyes narrow, then light up. Every morning for a week you both let the fire run and come in scorched to breakfast.\nopt.up.say.1 [bcee00] = 你告诉她你天亮会来，但是来对练，不是来操练。莱拉眯起眼睛，然后眼睛亮了。整整一周，每天早上你们俩都放开野火，带着一身焦痕去吃早饭。\n\n@event choir_solo\n# en: Solo Auditions\ntitle [c9c57e] = 独唱选拔\n# en: Auditions for the Midwinter solo. The choirmaster's eyebrows are famously hard to impress. Your throat is dry. Your hands are wet.\ntext [d9b167] = 仲冬独唱的选拔。合唱指挥的眉毛出了名的难以打动。你的喉咙发干。你的手心冒汗。\n# en: A circular choir loft under a painted dome, a single student standing in a pool of light, the rest of the choir floating gently above their seats.\nscene [8e9373] = 彩绘穹顶下的圆形唱诗台，一名学生独自站在一束光里，其余团员轻轻悬浮在座位上方。\n# en: Stay in the back row\nopt.left.label [1db21b] = 待在后排\n# en: You harmonise from safety, and it's lovely. Somebody else gets the solo and cries with relief.\nopt.left.say.1 [7b529b] = 你在安全的位置和声，很动听。别人拿到了独唱，如释重负地哭了。\n# en: Audition\nopt.right.label [41aa8e] = 参加选拔\n# en: You hit the high note and the candles in the loft all flare at once. The choirmaster's eyebrows rise one millimetre. You got it.\nopt.right.say.1 [93eca5] = 你唱上了高音，唱诗台上所有的蜡烛同时一亮。指挥的眉毛抬高了一毫米。你选上了。\n# en: Your voice cracks on the high note and a candle goes out in protest.\nopt.right.say.2 [dae132] = 你的高音破了，一支蜡烛抗议般地熄灭了。\n\n@event choir_levitation\n# en: Off-Key Altitude\ntitle [8a0afa] = 跑调的海拔\n# en: The Choir of the Spheres literally floats when it harmonises. You keep falling out of the air on the third verse. People are starting to notice the thumps.\ntext [8ceebd] = 天球合唱团唱和声时真的会飘起来。可你每唱到第三段就会从空中掉下来。大家开始注意到那一声声“咚”了。\n# en: A choir hovering a few feet off the floor, all in harmony — except one singer, who has dropped back to the ground with a thump.\nscene [282038] = 一支悬浮在离地几尺高处齐声合唱的合唱团——除了一名团员，他“咚”地掉回了地上。\n# en: Mime the hard bits\nopt.left.label [583519] = 难的地方假唱\n# en: Nobody notices. You float serenely, mouthing words. It's a little bit like fraud.\nopt.left.say.1 [018b6b] = 没人发现。你安详地飘着，只动嘴不出声。有点像诈骗。\n# en: Practise every night\nopt.right.label [c7a2c5] = 每晚加练\n# en: A week of late nights and you float through the third verse like a feather. The choirmaster gives you a tiny nod.\nopt.right.say.1 [56e1a4] = 熬了一周夜，你像羽毛一样飘过了第三段。指挥朝你极轻地点了点头。\n\n@event choir_concert\n# en: The Winter Concert\ntitle [428813] = 冬季音乐会\n# en: The winter concert. The great hall is packed. Somewhere in the audience, the Ledger's critic is sharpening her quill.\ntext [ba472a] = 冬季音乐会。大礼堂座无虚席。观众席某处，《纪事报》的乐评人正在削她的羽毛笔。\n# en: The great hall at night, a floating choir lit by candles, the audience below with faces tilted up.\nscene [229006] = 夜晚的大礼堂，一支烛光映照的悬浮合唱团，下方的观众仰着脸。\n# en: Sing for the audience\nopt.left.label [44bf70] = 为观众而唱\n# en: You sing and the hall sways. The review calls the choir \"unearthly\". You cut it out and keep it.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a05a2] = 你一开口，整个礼堂都随之摇曳。乐评称合唱团“不似人间”。你把它剪下来收好了。\n# en: Sing for yourself\nopt.right.label [bcd2d2] = 为自己而唱\n# en: You close your eyes and forget anyone is listening. When you open them, you are higher than everyone else.\nopt.right.say.1 [e5902a] = 你闭上眼，忘了有人在听。再睁开时，你飘得比所有人都高。\n\n@event sky_tryouts\n# en: Tryouts\ntitle [066773] = 选拔赛\n# en: Skyrace tryouts: three laps around the towers, through the hoops, without dying. The captain has a stopwatch and no visible sense of humour.\ntext [ad510d] = 天空竞速队选拔：绕塔三圈，穿过所有光环，别摔死。队长手里拿着秒表，脸上看不出任何幽默感。\n# en: Brooms streaking around the towers, hoops of light hanging in the sky, a captain with a whistle on the battlements.\nscene [465a53] = 飞帚绕着高塔疾驰，光环悬在空中，城垛上站着一个吹哨子的队长。\n# en: Fly clean and steady\nopt.left.label [596f30] = 飞得干净稳当\n# en: You're not the fastest, but you hit every hoop. \"Reserve,\" says the captain. Then: \"...no. Team.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [a95632] = 你不是最快的，但每个光环都穿过了。“替补。”队长说。随即又改口：“……不。正式队员。”\n# en: You miss two hoops and nearly a gargoyle. \"Reserves,\" says the captain. \"Keep practising.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [35b3d5] = 你漏了两个光环，还差点撞上一个石像鬼。“替补。”队长说，“继续练。”\n# en: Go full speed\nopt.right.label [58ac43] = 全速冲刺\n# en: You scream through the hoops so fast the stopwatch has to be checked. You're on the team. Obviously.\nopt.right.say.1 [c5ef95] = 你尖叫着冲过光环，快到队长得核对一下秒表。你入选了。毫无悬念。\n# en: You go full speed into the north tower. The tower is fine.\nopt.right.say.2 [c3625d] = 你全速冲进了北塔。塔没事。\n# en: Surge round the towers\nopt.up.label [6da9bc] = 顺风绕塔疾飞\n# en: The wind gets behind you on the first lap and you never let it go. Three laps, every hoop, faster each time, and your hands are raw on the handle. The captain looks at the stopwatch, then at you, and writes your name down.\nopt.up.say.1 [c50191] = 第一圈风就绕到了你身后，你再也没让它走。三圈，每个圈环，一圈比一圈快，你的手在扫帚柄上磨破了。队长看看秒表，又看看你，写下了你的名字。\n\n@event sky_crash\n# en: A Bad Landing\ntitle [cc3b0c] = 糟糕的着陆\n# en: Practice. A gust catches you mid-turn, and the ground arrives rather sooner than expected. Your ankle is making an interesting shape.\ntext [ff2751] = 训练中。一阵风在你转弯时把你掀翻，地面来得比预想的要早一些。你的脚踝正呈现出一种有趣的形状。\n# en: A broom snapped in two on the grass, a student sitting up dazed, teammates landing around them.\nscene [6a9e37] = 草地上一把折成两截的飞帚，一个学生晕乎乎地坐起来，队友们在周围降落。\n# en: Walk it off\nopt.left.label [0e45b4] = 走两步就好了\n# en: You do not walk it off. You limp it off, for a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [4fdb76] = 并没有好。你一瘸一拐了一个月。\n# en: Tough as old boots. The team cheers when you get back on your broom.\nopt.left.say.2 [f9f523] = 皮实得很。你重新骑上飞帚时，全队都在欢呼。\n# en: Straight to the infirmary\nopt.right.label [510ab8] = 直奔医务室\n# en: Sister Amaranth sets it in a minute and charges you three crowns for \"carelessness\". Fair.\nopt.right.say.1 [7345b6] = 阿玛兰斯修女一分钟就把它接好了，还收了你三克朗的“粗心费”。公道。\n\n@event sky_championship\n# en: Lumenhold vs. Thornwick\ntitle [da4be7] = 流明堡对荆木学院\n# en: The championship against Thornwick Academy. Final lap. You're neck and neck with their captain, and the golden hoop is at the top of the tallest tower.\ntext [bd74b2] = 与荆木学院的冠军赛。最后一圈。你和对方队长并驾齐驱，金色光环就在最高塔的塔顶。\n# en: Two teams of broom-riders in blue and green above a packed stadium, the golden finishing hoop glowing at the top of the highest tower.\nscene [d82217] = 座无虚席的赛场上空，蓝绿两队飞帚手交错，金色终点光环在最高塔顶闪耀。\n# en: Play it safe\nopt.left.label [882711] = 稳妥起见\n# en: Thornwick takes the hoop by a whisker. Still: second place in the region. The team carries you off the field anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [68b920] = 荆木以毫厘之差夺得光环。但不管怎样：地区亚军。全队还是把你扛出了场。\n# en: The suicide dive\nopt.right.label [494b9a] = 玩命俯冲\n# en: You dive straight down the tower face and pull up at the last second, through the hoop. The stadium explodes.\nopt.right.say.1 [742576] = 你沿着塔壁垂直俯冲，在最后一秒拉起，穿过光环。全场沸腾了。\n# en: You dive. You miss. The crowd gasps, then cheers you anyway when you stagger up waving.\nopt.right.say.2 [c58127] = 你俯冲了。你没穿中。观众倒吸一口凉气，等你踉跄着爬起来挥手时，又为你欢呼起来。\n\n@event ledger_pie_scoop\n# en: The Case of the Missing Pies\ntitle [269d69] = 馅饼失踪案\n# en: Nessa slams a file on your desk. \"Pies. Forty-two of them, gone from the kitchens in a single night. I want the culprit, I want a photo and I want it by Friday.\"\ntext [bcd7f3] = 内莎把一份卷宗拍在你桌上。“馅饼。四十二个，一夜之间从厨房消失。我要找出犯人，要照片，周五之前要。”\n# en: A cramped newspaper office in a tower attic, a printing press clanking, a girl with an ink-stained grin pointing at a map pinned with string.\nscene [6fc23f] = 塔楼阁楼里拥挤的报社，印刷机哐当作响，一个满脸墨迹、咧嘴笑的女孩指着一张钉满细绳的地图。\n# en: Write a light-hearted piece\nopt.left.label [ffe459] = 写篇轻松的小文\n# en: \"Pie-rates Strike Again.\" Nessa groans at the headline and prints it anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a7002] = 《馅饼大盗再度出手》。内莎对着标题哀叹了一声，还是印了。\n# en: Actually investigate\nopt.right.label [42eb33] = 认真调查\n# en: The culprit: a family of brownies living in the chimney. Your story runs on the front page. Nessa frames it.\nopt.right.say.1 [5dd8ed] = 犯人：住在烟囱里的一窝棕仙。你的报道登上了头版。内莎把它裱了起来。\n# en: You stake out the kitchens for three nights and catch nothing but a cold. Nessa pats your head.\nopt.right.say.2 [6e3684] = 你在厨房蹲守了三晚，除了感冒什么也没逮到。内莎拍了拍你的头。\n# en: Scribble every alibi down\nopt.up.label [9c4bc5] = 把每份不在场证明记下\n# en: Your quill takes down every alibi in the kitchens word for word. Laid side by side, two of them describe the same pie differently. Nessa reads your notes and goes very quiet, which means it's good.\nopt.up.say.1 [cb08c8] = 你的羽毛笔把厨房里每个人的不在场证明一字不落地记下。并排一放，有两份对同一个馅饼的描述不一样。内莎读完你的笔记，安静了好一会儿——这说明东西不错。\n\n@event ledger_bram_story\n# en: The Prefect's Bribes\ntitle [609034] = 级长的贿赂\n# en: An anonymous tip: Bram Hollis has been taking bribes from first-years to skip their demerits. You have a witness. Nessa wants your byline on it. \"This is real journalism,\" she says, eyes shining.\ntext [78f450] = 一条匿名线报：布拉姆·霍利斯一直在收一年级生的贿赂，帮他们免掉记过。你有目击证人。内莎想让这篇报道署你的名字。“这才是真正的新闻，”她说，眼睛闪闪发亮。\n# en: An anonymous note spread on a desk under a lamp, next to a sketch of a prefect taking a coin purse from a first-year.\nscene [b9aaf0] = 台灯下摊开的一张匿名字条，旁边是一幅速写：一名级长正从一年级生手里接过钱袋。\n# en: Kill the story\nopt.left.label [50cd1f] = 压下这篇报道\n# en: Nessa is disappointed in you. Somehow, Bram finds out you buried it. He nods at you in the corridor. You feel slightly dirty.\nopt.left.say.1 [307410] = 内莎对你很失望。不知怎么的，布拉姆知道是你压下了它。他在走廊里朝你点点头。你觉得自己有点脏。\n# en: Publish it\nopt.right.label [d859b5] = 发表它\n# en: The story runs. Bram is stripped of his sash for a month. He gets it back. And now he knows exactly who you are.\nopt.right.say.1 [017011] = 报道刊出了。布拉姆被摘掉绶带一个月。后来他又拿回去了。而现在，他非常清楚你是谁。\n# en: Take the witness down word for word\nopt.up.label [c2ae93] = 把证人的话逐字记下\n# en: Your quill takes the witness down word for word, every hesitation included, and the story runs on what was said and nothing more. Bram can't deny a word of it. He tries, at length, in the corridor.\nopt.up.say.1 [de7e2f] = 你的羽毛笔把证人的话逐字记下，连每次迟疑都记了，报道只登说过的话，一句不多。布拉姆一个字也赖不掉。他在走廊里试着赖了很久。\n\n@event ledger_deadline\n# en: Deadline Night\ntitle [d06cb7] = 截稿之夜\n# en: The press runs at dawn and half the paper is blank. Nessa is chewing her quill. \"I need you tonight. All night. I'll owe you.\"\ntext [75d368] = 印刷机天亮开印，报纸还空着一半。内莎咬着羽毛笔。“今晚我需要你。整晚。我欠你一个人情。”\n# en: The newspaper office at 3am, crumpled drafts on the floor, two students slumped over typesetting trays.\nscene [4bbe74] = 凌晨三点的报社，地上满是揉皱的草稿，两个学生趴在排字盘上。\n# en: Say you have revision to do\nopt.left.label [12b3be] = 说你还要复习\n# en: You sleep. The paper comes out with a large drawing of a duck on page four. Nessa doesn't speak to you for a day.\nopt.left.say.1 [f578ae] = 你去睡了。报纸第四版上印了一幅巨大的鸭子画。内莎一整天没理你。\n# en: Pull an all-nighter\nopt.right.label [d42cd2] = 熬个通宵\n# en: You write three articles and a horoscope. At dawn Nessa falls asleep on your shoulder, ink on her nose.\nopt.right.say.1 [99e236] = 你写了三篇文章和一份星座运势。天亮时，内莎靠在你肩上睡着了，鼻尖上沾着墨水。\n# en: You keep an eye on her quill all night, too, and twice catch it inventing a fire in the east wing. The paper is, for once, entirely true.\nopt.right.say.2 [f03a3c] = 你整晚还盯着她的羽毛笔，两次逮到它在编造东翼起火的新闻。这一期报纸破天荒地句句属实。\n\n@event garden_mandrakes\n# en: The Mandrake Choir\ntitle [a16982] = 曼德拉草合唱团\n# en: The greenhouse mandrakes have started singing again. Off-key and enthusiastic. The earmuffs are on the hook by the door. Tobias isn't wearing his; he's harmonising.\ntext [df724d] = 温室里的曼德拉草又开始唱歌了。跑调，但热情洋溢。耳罩挂在门边的钩子上。托比亚斯没戴他的；他在跟着和声。\n# en: Rows of potted mandrakes in a steamy greenhouse, their little leafy heads opened in song.\nscene [65b35a] = 雾气蒸腾的温室里一排排盆栽曼德拉草，它们长着叶子的小脑袋张嘴高歌。\n# en: Earmuffs on\nopt.left.label [1586df] = 戴上耳罩\n# en: You repot six mandrakes in blissful silence. Good honest work.\nopt.left.say.1 [652505] = 你在幸福的寂静中给六株曼德拉草换了盆。踏踏实实的好活儿。\n# en: Sing along\nopt.right.label [91a84f] = 跟着唱\n# en: You sing along and promptly faint. Mandrake song is strong stuff. You wake up very relaxed.\nopt.right.say.1 [5d48fe] = 你跟着唱，然后立刻晕了过去。曼德拉草的歌声劲儿很大。你醒来时非常放松。\n# en: The mandrakes adore you. The greenhouse sounds like a pub at closing time.\nopt.right.say.2 [acd59a] = 曼德拉草们爱死你了。温室里听起来像打烊时的酒馆。\n\n@event garden_moonflower\n# en: The Moonflower\ntitle [924066] = 月光花\n# en: \"The moonflower's going to bloom tonight,\" Tobias says, a little breathless. \"Once every eleven years. I'm staying up to see it. You... could stay too. If you want.\"\ntext [1d0b11] = “今晚月光花要开了，”托比亚斯有点喘不过气地说，“十一年才开一次。我要熬夜看。你……也可以留下。如果你想的话。”\n# en: A single silver flower opening in the dark of a glass greenhouse, two faces lit by its glow.\nscene [ada882] = 漆黑的玻璃温室里一朵银色的花正在绽放，两张脸被它的光芒照亮。\n# en: You need sleep\nopt.left.label [145c0b] = 你得睡觉了\n# en: \"Course. Yeah.\" He tells you about it at breakfast, in detail, with gestures.\nopt.left.say.1 [a1e34d] = “当然。嗯。”早饭时他给你讲了一遍，事无巨细，手舞足蹈。\n# en: Stay up with him\nopt.right.label [4a8ffd] = 陪他熬夜\n# en: At 2am the flower opens and fills the greenhouse with silver light. Tobias doesn't look at the flower. He looks at you.\nopt.right.say.1 [7c8d48] = 凌晨两点，花开了，银光洒满温室。托比亚斯没有看花。他在看你。\n# en: Grow a hedge against the wind\nopt.up.label [c446da] = 种道树篱挡风\n# en: You stay, and when the wind gets up you grow a thorny screen round the bed. The moonflower opens at midnight in perfect stillness. Tobias doesn't say anything for a long time. Then he says, \"Thank you,\" and means all of it.\nopt.up.say.1 [0e023f] = 你留了下来，风起时你在花床周围长出一道带刺的屏障。午夜，月光花在一片静止中开了。托比亚斯很久什么也没说。然后他说：“谢谢你。”每个字都是真心的。\n\n@event garden_maud_secret\n# en: The Secret Garden\ntitle [2193f1] = 秘密花园\n# en: The old groundskeeper beckons you behind the potting shed, to a door in the wall you've walked past a hundred times without seeing. \"You've got the hands for it,\" Old Maud says. \"Want to see what grows where nobody's looking?\"\ntext [e9e7ee] = 老园丁招手让你到盆栽棚后面，那里墙上有一扇门，你路过了上百次都没看见。“你有这双手，”老莫德说，“想看看在没人注意的地方长着什么吗？”\n# en: An old woman in a battered straw hat holding open a hidden door in an ivy-covered wall; beyond, impossible flowers glow.\nscene [044583] = 一位戴破草帽的老妇人推开爬满常春藤的墙上一扇隐秘的门；门后，不可思议的花朵闪着光。\n# en: Thank her and decline\nopt.left.label [4d277b] = 谢过她，婉拒了\n# en: \"Suit yourself.\" The door is gone when you look back. You make your class with a minute to spare, oddly pleased with yourself.\nopt.left.say.1 [68610a] = “随你。”你回头时，那扇门已经不见了。你赶在上课前一分钟进了教室，莫名地有点得意。\n# en: Follow her in\nopt.right.label [499306] = 跟她进去\n# en: Inside: glowing ferns, silver bees, a pear tree that hums. Maud tucks a sprig of rosemary into your pocket. \"For luck. Don't lose it.\" You lose the whole afternoon in there, and a class with it.\nopt.right.say.1 [9b2aee] = 里面有发光的蕨类、银色的蜜蜂，还有一棵会哼歌的梨树。莫德把一枝迷迭香塞进你口袋。“保佑你的。别弄丢了。”你在里面待掉了整个下午，还错过了一节课。\n\n@event stars_cocoa_night\n# en: Cocoa on the Roof\ntitle [a1a0e0] = 屋顶上的热可可\n# en: Stargazers meet on the roof with blankets, telescopes and an illegal quantity of cocoa. Tonight is cloudy. Nobody cares.\ntext [9a36b4] = 观星社在屋顶聚会，带着毯子、望远镜和数量不太合法的热可可。今晚多云。没人在乎。\n# en: Students wrapped in blankets on a flat roof, steaming mugs in hand, telescopes pointed at a velvet sky.\nscene [c74695] = 学生们裹着毯子坐在平坦的屋顶上，手捧冒热气的杯子，望远镜对着天鹅绒般的夜空。\n# en: Talk until dawn\nopt.left.label [2f64c7] = 聊到天亮\n# en: You talk about home, about the future, about whether stars are lonely. Someone falls asleep on your shoulder.\nopt.left.say.1 [6965e5] = 你们聊家乡，聊未来，聊星星会不会孤单。有人靠在你肩上睡着了。\n# en: Do your homework by starlight\nopt.right.label [5cf04c] = 借着星光写作业\n# en: You finish your essay by the light of the cocoa-warmer charm. Productive. A little lonely.\nopt.right.say.1 [6dcd21] = 你借着暖可可咒的光写完了论文。很高效。有点孤单。\n\n@event stars_meteor_bet\n# en: The Meteor Wager\ntitle [262c9e] = 流星赌局\n# en: The club is running a betting pool on tonight's meteor count. The current favourite is 40. The charts suggest something else. Or do they?\ntext [25d6ed] = 社团在为今晚的流星数量开赌盘。目前热门是 40 颗。星图暗示的数字却不一样。真的吗？\n# en: A chalkboard on an observatory wall covered in tally marks and names, a pile of coins on a stool beneath it.\nscene [e0c74a] = 天文台墙上的黑板写满计数和名字，下面凳子上堆着一堆硬币。\n# en: Bet on the charts\nopt.left.label [b8316b] = 押星图的数字\n# en: Fifty-three meteors. You called fifty-two. Close enough to win the pot.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a5d53] = 五十三颗流星。你押的是五十二。足够接近，赢下了奖池。\n# en: The charts were wrong, or you read them wrong. Eight crowns gone.\nopt.left.say.2 [bd7c8a] = 星图错了，或者是你看错了。八克朗没了。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.right.label [03d24a] = 不参与\n# en: You watch the meteors for their own sake. Turns out that's the best seat in the house.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c78f1] = 你只是单纯地看流星。原来这才是最好的位置。\n# en: Recall the old meteor charts\nopt.up.label [1fe4c7] = 调出旧流星图\n# en: The observatory's oldest chart book falls open at this exact night a century ago: sixty-one meteors. You bet on sixty. You win, and the club demands to know what you know. You don't tell them.\nopt.up.say.1 [ff8674] = 观星台最老的星图册翻开在一百年前的今夜：六十一颗流星。你押了六十。你赢了，社员们追问你知道些什么。你没告诉他们。\n\n@event stars_constellation\n# en: An Unnamed Shape\ntitle [42283b] = 无名之形\n# en: Plotting the autumn sky, you notice seven faint stars that form a ring around nothing. The charts call that patch of sky \"empty\". It doesn't look empty.\ntext [541d68] = 绘制秋季星空时，你注意到七颗暗淡的星星围成一个环，环中空无一物。星图把那片天空标为“空白”。可它看起来并不空。\n# en: A star chart spread on a table, a student's finger tracing a new pattern between unmarked stars.\nscene [cb6cc5] = 桌上摊开的星图，一名学生的手指在未标注的星星间描出新的图案。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.left.label [e2ae0c] = 不去管它\n# en: You plot the usual constellations. Your chart gets top marks.\nopt.left.say.1 [9e48fd] = 你画了常规的星座。你的星图得了最高分。\n# en: Investigate\nopt.right.label [c3e054] = 调查一下\n# en: You spend a week in the archives. Three hundred years ago, the patch was called the Hollow. Then someone scraped the name off every chart.\nopt.right.say.1 [81c647] = 你在档案馆泡了一周。三百年前，那片天空叫作“空洞”。后来有人把这个名字从每一张星图上刮掉了。\n\n@event carto_invite\n# en: An Invitation at Midnight\ntitle [324f7e] = 午夜邀请\n# en: A black card under your door. On it, a map in silver ink that redraws itself while you watch. Corvin's handwriting: \"Midnight. Under the third gargoyle. Bring a candle. Tell no one.\"\ntext [afdfc2] = 门缝下塞进一张黑色卡片。上面是一张银墨地图，在你眼前不断重绘。科尔文的笔迹：“午夜。第三尊石像鬼下面。带上蜡烛。别告诉任何人。”\n# en: A slip of black paper pushed under a dormitory door, a silver ink map on it that rearranges itself as you watch.\nscene [f40da5] = 一张从宿舍门缝塞进来的黑纸条，上面的银墨地图在你注视下自行重组。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.left.label [e2ae0c] = 不理它\n# en: The card blanks itself the next morning. Corvin doesn't mention it, but he's a little cooler for a week.\nopt.left.say.1 [e32163] = 第二天早上卡片变成了空白。科尔文没提这事，但接下来一周对你冷淡了些。\n# en: Go\nopt.right.label [d0c56b] = 去\n# en: Under the gargoyle: six students, a stack of maps and a passage you'd never seen. \"Welcome,\" says Corvin, \"to the club that doesn't exist.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [de031b] = 石像鬼下面：六个学生、一摞地图，还有一条你从未见过的通道。“欢迎，”科尔文说，“来到这个不存在的社团。”\n\n@event carto_thursday_stairs\n# en: The Thursday Staircase\ntitle [571623] = 周四楼梯\n# en: The Cartographers have heard of a staircase that only exists on Thursdays between midnight and one. Tonight is Thursday. It is 11:58.\ntext [95afb9] = 制图社听说有一道楼梯，只在周四午夜到一点之间存在。今晚是周四。现在是 11:58。\n# en: A spiral staircase emerging from a blank wall in moonlight, its steps unfolding one by one.\nscene [54fe61] = 月光下一道螺旋楼梯从空白的墙面里冒出来，台阶一级一级地展开。\n# en: Map it carefully\nopt.left.label [150195] = 仔细绘制它\n# en: You chart forty-one steps and a landing with a window onto a sky that isn't this one. The map is the club's pride.\nopt.left.say.1 [0210dc] = 你画下了四十一级台阶，和一个平台——平台的窗外是另一片天空。这张地图成了社团的骄傲。\n# en: Climb it to the top\nopt.right.label [fb1da8] = 一直爬到顶\n# en: At the top, a forgotten storeroom. Inside: a chest of old academy coins. The club splits it. Corvin cries a little.\nopt.right.say.1 [13aaa2] = 顶上是一间被遗忘的储藏室。里面有一箱古老的学院硬币。社团把它分了。科尔文哭了一小会儿。\n# en: At one o'clock the staircase stops existing. You were on it. You land in a laundry chute.\nopt.right.say.2 [714828] = 一点整，楼梯不再存在。而你正站在上面。你掉进了洗衣房的滑道。\n\n@event carto_lake_tunnel\n# en: The Tunnel Under the Lake\ntitle [7b054e] = 湖底隧道\n# en: A loose stone in the boathouse cellar opens on a tunnel running out under the lake. The bricks drip. At the far end, a round door carved with a black star. It is warm to the touch.\ntext [ea3eb1] = 船屋地窖里一块松动的石头后面，是一条延伸到湖底的隧道。砖墙滴着水。尽头是一扇刻着黑色星星的圆门。摸上去是温的。\n# en: A narrow brick tunnel dripping with water, lit by a single candle, ending at a round door carved with a black star.\nscene [1d60a2] = 一条滴水的狭窄砖砌隧道，只有一支蜡烛照明，尽头是一扇刻着黑星的圆门。\n# en: Mark it and leave\nopt.left.label [98295f] = 做个标记就离开\n# en: You add the tunnel to the club map with a skull beside it. Corvin adds a second skull, to be safe.\nopt.left.say.1 [837f6b] = 你把隧道添到社团地图上，旁边画了个骷髅。科尔文又加了一个骷髅，以防万一。\n# en: Press your palm to the star\nopt.right.label [f4d16b] = 把手掌按在星上\n# en: The star drinks the warmth out of your hand. Behind the door, something very large shifts in its sleep.\nopt.right.say.1 [d08b1b] = 星星吸走了你掌心的温度。门后，有什么非常庞大的东西在睡梦中翻了个身。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/contraband.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/contraband.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event elixir_trunk\n# en: The Bottle in Your Trunk\ntitle [03c728] = 箱子里的瓶子\n# en: {has(brightwater_bottle) and has(owls_ink_bottle): Under your spare robes, wrapped in two socks, the bottles wait, one gold and one black.|{Under your spare robes, wrapped in a sock, the bottle waits.|The bottle is still in your trunk. You have checked on it more often than on your homework.|You know exactly where the bottle is without looking.}} {has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want): Since the last dose wore off, it has been very easy to think about.|{before_finals(): Finals are close.|Nobody knows it's there, you're nearly sure.}}\ntext [20a0b4] = {has(brightwater_bottle) and has(owls_ink_bottle): 在你备用的长袍底下，裹在两只袜子里，两只瓶子在等着，一金一黑。|{在你备用的长袍底下，裹在一只袜子里，那只瓶子在等着。|瓶子还在你的箱子里。你检查它的次数，比检查作业还多。|你不用看也知道瓶子在哪儿。}} {has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want): 上一剂的劲儿过去以后，想起它变得太容易了。|{before_finals(): 期末快到了。|没人知道它在那儿，你几乎可以肯定。}}\n# en: A student kneeling at an open school trunk at night, a small stoppered bottle in a sock in one hand, a candle on the floor beside them.\nscene [4f544f] = 夜里，一个学生跪在打开的学生箱前，一手拿着裹在袜子里的小塞瓶，地上放着一支蜡烛。\n# en: Drink the Brightwater\nopt.bright.label [26149d] = 喝下亮泉水\n# en: Two good months. After that, maybe not.\nopt.bright.hint [2a9571] = 两个好月份。之后，就不一定了。\n# en: {It tastes of honey and pennies. Within the hour you're warm to your fingertips, and the day ahead looks easy.|You drink it at the window. The tiredness lifts like a lid. You know it's borrowed, and you don't mind yet.|One swallow, and everything that ached stops. You hide the empty bottle at the bottom of the trunk.}\nopt.bright.say.1 [e405ab] = {它尝起来像蜂蜜和铜板。不到一个钟头，你从头暖到指尖，接下来的日子看起来很轻松。|你站在窗边喝下它。疲惫像盖子一样被揭开了。你知道这是借来的，但暂时还不在乎。|一口下去，所有酸痛都停了。你把空瓶藏到箱子最底下。}\n# en: Drink the Owl's Ink\nopt.ink.label [e516da] = 喝下枭墨\n# en: Two sharp months, exams included. After that, maybe not.\nopt.ink.hint [b0c0ec] = 两个清醒的月份，考试也算在内。之后，就不一定了。\n# en: {It's bitter as a burnt quill. By morning your head is a lamp-lit room, every shelf in order, and magic hums in your fingers.|You drink it standing up. The words on your desk sharpen, line by line, like a page coming into focus.|Black, bitter, and then clear. You read three chapters before breakfast and remember every word of them.}\nopt.ink.say.1 [306ce7] = {苦得像烧焦的羽毛笔。到早上，你的脑子成了一间点着灯的屋子，每一层书架都井井有条，魔力在指尖嗡嗡作响。|你站着把它喝了。桌上的字一行一行变得清晰，像一页纸慢慢对上了焦。|黑的，苦的，然后一切清楚了。早饭前你读完了三章，每个字都记得。}\n# en: Pour the Brightwater away\nopt.pour_bright.label [7f06a3] = 把亮泉水倒掉\n# en: {You tip the gold out of the window into the ivy. The ivy, next morning, is a remarkable colour.|You pour the gold down the washbasin and run the tap for a long time afterwards. You feel lighter, and worse.|Out of the window, every gold drop. You watch it go and want it back the whole time.}\nopt.pour_bright.say.1 [19c6d7] = {你把那金色的东西从窗口倒进常春藤里。第二天早上，那片常春藤的颜色非同寻常。|你把那金色的东西倒进洗脸盆，然后开着水龙头冲了很久。你觉得轻松了，也觉得更糟了。|每一滴金色都倒出了窗外。你看着它流走，从头到尾都想把它要回来。}\n# en: Pour the Owl's Ink away\nopt.pour_ink.label [77383a] = 把枭墨倒掉\n# en: {You tip the black out of the window into the ivy. By morning the leaves it touched have curled up tight.|You pour the black down the washbasin, and it stains the porcelain grey. You scrub until it's gone.|Out of the window, every black drop. Finals will be exactly as hard as they are.}\nopt.pour_ink.say.1 [4cb8f6] = {你把那黑色的东西从窗口倒进常春藤里。到早上，沾到它的叶子都紧紧卷了起来。|你把那黑色的东西倒进洗脸盆，瓷面被染成了灰色。你一直擦到看不出来为止。|每一滴黑色都倒出了窗外。期末会和它本来一样难。}\n# en: Not yet\nopt.wait.label [8dd27c] = 还不到时候\n# en: {You wrap it up again and close the lid.|Not tonight. You put the trunk back under the bed and lie awake knowing where it is.|You leave it where it is, for now. For a better reason, later.}\nopt.wait.say.1 [7a1dfc] = {你把它重新裹好，合上箱盖。|今晚不喝。你把箱子推回床底，躺着睡不着，心里清楚它在哪儿。|你暂时不动它。以后，会有更好的理由。}\n\n@event elixir_offered_bright\n# en: Something for the Grey Days\ntitle [6c63d1] = 留给阴天的东西\n# en: {other} has been glowing for a week: sleeping well, laughing loudly, top of every drill. In the corridor {they} put{s} a small cloudy-gold bottle in your hand. \"Brightwater. From the village. It's only banned because it works.\"\ntext [7d2d71] = {other}已经容光焕发一个星期了：睡得好，笑得响，每次操练都拿第一。在走廊里，{they}把一只浑浊金色的小瓶塞进你手里。“亮泉水。村里来的。它被禁，只是因为太管用了。”\n# en: A school corridor, one student pressing a small cloudy-gold bottle into another student's hand, glancing down the corridor for prefects.\nscene [288b21] = 学校走廊里，一个学生把一只浑浊金色的小瓶塞进另一个学生手里，一边往走廊那头张望有没有级长。\n# en: Keep it, for a grey day\nopt.left.label [3db690] = 收下，留给阴天\n# en: You wrap it in a sock and bury it in your trunk. It feels heavier there than it did in your hand.\nopt.left.say.1 [8bfbc7] = 你把它裹进袜子，埋在箱子里。它在那儿比在你手里时更沉。\n# en: Give it back\nopt.right.label [b4bc84] = 还回去\n# en: \"Suit yourself,\" says {other}, and pockets it, a little stung. For a day or two {they} look{s} at you as if you'd said something unkind.\nopt.right.say.1 [86a32e] = “随你，”{other}说着把它揣回兜里，有点受伤。之后一两天，{they}看你的眼神，就像你说了什么刻薄话。\n\n@event elixir_borrowed_bag\n# en: Not Your Bottle\ntitle [f50c67] = 不是你的瓶子\n# en: You borrow {other}'s satchel to carry your books to a lecture, and at the bottom of it, wrapped in a sock, is a small black bottle with a village label. Owl's Ink. Everyone knows what it is, and that it's banned.\ntext [ecf0bc] = 你借了{other}的书包去上课，在包底，裹在一只袜子里，有一只贴着村里标签的小黑瓶。枭墨。人人都知道那是什么，也知道它是违禁的。\n# en: A student kneeling over a borrowed satchel, holding up a small black bottle wrapped in a sock, with an uneasy face.\nscene [c18af8] = 一个学生跪在借来的书包旁，举起一只裹在袜子里的小黑瓶，脸色不安。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.left.label [87c2f5] = 留下它\n# en: You slip it into your own pocket and give the satchel back with a smile. {other} doesn't say a word. Neither, it turns out, do you, for a week.\nopt.left.say.1 [307035] = 你把它塞进自己的口袋，笑着把书包还了回去。{other}一个字也没说。结果，你也一个星期没说。\n# en: Give it back, and say why\nopt.right.label [4c869c] = 还回去，并说明原因\n# en: You hand it back in private. {other} goes red, then quiet, then thanks you, which is the hardest part to listen to.\nopt.right.say.1 [47baa7] = 你私下把它还了回去。{other}先是脸红，然后沉默，然后向你道谢，那句谢谢最难听进去。\n\n@event elixir_first_year\n# en: A Tonic from Home\ntitle [27c4ee] = 家里带来的补药\n# en: A first-year on your corridor has been dancing on the stairs at midnight and sleeping through breakfast. On their windowsill is their \"tonic from home\": cloudy gold, a village label. You know exactly what it is, and so, by the look of the stairs, does everyone else.\ntext [feb3c1] = 你那条走廊上的一个一年级生，半夜在楼梯上跳舞，早饭总是睡过头。TA的窗台上放着“家里带来的补药”：浑浊的金色，村里的标签。你很清楚那是什么，看楼梯的样子，别人也都清楚。\n# en: A first-year's dormitory windowsill with a cloudy-gold bottle on it, the first-year asleep on top of the covers at midday.\nscene [7b6fdf] = 一年级生宿舍的窗台上放着一只浑浊金色的瓶子，那个一年级生正午还和衣睡在被子上。\n# en: Buy it off them, quietly\nopt.left.label [954bd8] = 悄悄从TA手里买下\n# en: You pay a fair price and tell them where it really comes from. They look relieved to be rid of it. Now it's in your trunk instead of theirs.\nopt.left.say.1 [393fb3] = 你付了公道的价钱，告诉TA这东西真正是从哪儿来的。TA看起来如释重负。现在它在你的箱子里，而不是TA的箱子里。\n# en: Walk them to the infirmary\nopt.right.label [6b11df] = 陪TA去医务室\n# en: Sister Amaranth takes the bottle in one hand and the first-year in the other. \"Good. Next time, come sooner,\" she tells you. You spend the evening on a hard bench, worrying, until you hear they're all right.\nopt.right.say.1 [89c8c0] = 阿玛兰斯修女一手接过瓶子，一手接过那个一年级生。“很好。下次早点来，”她对你说。你在硬板凳上坐了一晚上，提心吊胆，直到听说那孩子没事。\n\n@event elixir_finals_spare\n# en: Two Bottles Too Many\ntitle [f5eb25] = 多出来的一瓶\n# en: {~{other} catches you on the dormitory stairs, pale and sheepish. In a panic about finals {they} bought two bottles of Owl's Ink from someone behind the refectory, and now want{s} rid of the second. \"At cost,\" {they} say{s}. \"Please. I can't have both in my trunk.\"|{other} sits down next to you in the library and slides a black bottle across the desk behind a book. A cousin in the village sent two. \"I only need one to pass,\" {they} whisper{s}. \"At cost. Please.\"|{other} finds you after supper with a black bottle in each coat pocket, bought off a fifth-year at the Newt at a terrible price. \"I panicked,\" {they} say{s}. \"Take one off me at cost? Please?\"}\ntext [9391a1] = {~{other}在宿舍楼梯上拦住你，脸色苍白，一脸窘迫。因为担心期末，{they}慌慌张张地从食堂后面的人手里买了两瓶枭墨，现在想把第二瓶出手。“按原价，”{they}说。“求你了。我的箱子里不能放两瓶。”|{other}在图书馆里坐到你旁边，借一本书挡着，把一只黑瓶推过桌面。村里的一个表亲寄来了两瓶。“我只要一瓶就能及格，”{they}小声说。“按原价。求你了。”|晚饭后{other}找到你，外套两边口袋里各装着一只黑瓶，是在镀金蝾螈酒馆从一个五年级生手里高价买的。“我慌了，”{they}说。“按原价匀一瓶给你？求你了？”}\n# en: A student in a dormitory doorway holding out a small black bottle in an open palm, a second identical bottle in the other hand, looking embarrassed.\nscene [408c48] = 宿舍门口，一个学生摊开手掌递出一只小黑瓶，另一只手里拿着一模一样的第二瓶，神情尴尬。\n# en: Take it off {their} hands\nopt.left.label [d81a12] = 替{them}接手\n# en: {~The coins go one way and the bottle the other, on the stairs, both of you looking at your shoes. {other} looks lighter for it. You don't.|You slip the bottle inside your book and the coins under {other}'s. Neither of you reads another word that evening.|You take the bottle from {other}'s pocket yourself, so nobody sees it change hands. The price was terrible; you pay it anyway.}\nopt.left.say.1 [11eea2] = {~在楼梯上，钱币往一边去，瓶子往另一边去，你们俩都低头看着鞋。{other}看起来轻松了。你没有。|你把瓶子夹进自己的书里，把钱塞到{other}的书底下。那天晚上你们俩谁都没再读进一个字。|你亲手从{other}的口袋里取出瓶子，这样就没人看见它换了手。价钱很离谱；你还是付了。}\n# en: Tell {them} to pour them both away\nopt.right.label [6c69d6] = 叫{them}把两瓶都倒掉\n# en: {~\"Both?\" says {other}, as if you'd asked {them} to pour away a term's work. {other} goes off to think about it, and won't quite meet your eye for a week.|{other} puts the bottle back behind the book without a word, and spends the rest of the evening reading the same page.|\"At that price?\" says {other}, appalled, and goes off with a bottle in each pocket, and looks at you sideways at breakfast.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0e1358] = {~“两瓶都倒？”{other}说，好像你叫{them}倒掉的是一整个学期的功课。{other}走开去想这件事，之后一个星期都不太敢看你的眼睛。|{other}一言不发地把瓶子放回书后面，整个晚上都在读同一页。|“花了那么多钱？”{other}吓了一跳，口袋里揣着两瓶走了，第二天早饭时斜眼看你。}\n\n@event elixir_finals_buyers\n# en: Customers Before Finals\ntitle [84c962] = 期末前的买家\n# en: {Word has got round that you have a bottle of Owl's Ink. {buyer} corners you on the stairs, purse already open. \"Name your price. I'm going to fail otherwise.\"|{buyer} has heard about your bottle too, and doesn't haggle.|Finals, and {buyer} on the stairs, purse in hand. Somehow they always know.}\ntext [98dfdd] = {你有一瓶枭墨的消息传开了。{buyer}在楼梯上堵住你，钱袋已经打开。“你开个价。不然我就要挂科了。”|{buyer}也听说了你的瓶子，而且不还价。|期末，{buyer}拿着钱袋在楼梯上。不知怎么的，他们总会知道。}\n# en: A student cornered on a stair landing by a desperate classmate holding out a purse.\nscene [437756] = 楼梯平台上，一个学生被一个拿着钱袋、走投无路的同学堵住。\n# en: Sell it\nopt.left.label [4ac442] = 卖掉\n# en: {You sell it. {buyer} holds the bottle like a holy relic and runs. You count the coins twice and don't feel richer.|The bottle changes hands on the landing. Your purse is heavy, and so, for a day or two, is something else.|You sell, and {buyer} thanks you as if you'd done something kind. You haven't.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d73a1f] = {你卖了。{buyer}像捧圣物一样捧着瓶子跑了。你把钱数了两遍，并没觉得更富。|瓶子在平台上换了手。你的钱袋沉了，有一两天，别的什么也沉甸甸的。|你卖了，{buyer}向你道谢，好像你做了件好事。你没有。}\n# en: Keep it for your own exams\nopt.right.label [5dc4d5] = 留着自己考试用\n# en: {\"It's spoken for,\" you say. {buyer}'s face falls, and stays fallen for a week.|You keep it. {buyer} calls you something on the way down the stairs, not quite quietly enough.|No sale. {buyer} doesn't believe you need it more, and says so.}\nopt.right.say.1 [91f8eb] = {“已经有主了，”你说。{buyer}的脸沉了下去，沉了一个星期。|你留下了它。{buyer}下楼时骂了你一句，声音并不算小。|不卖。{buyer}不相信你比自己更需要它，还说了出来。}\n\n@event grisk_elixir_offer\n# en: Grisk's Better Bottles\ntitle [190c46] = 格里斯克的好瓶子\n# en: {Grisk beckons you to his corner table, where a gold bottle and a black one stand beside his ledger. \"I hear you've a taste for the village's better remedies,\" he purrs. \"I can keep you in them. Not only for money. Between friends, favours keep better. The boathouse jetty, any midnight.\"|Grisk's corner table, and the two bottles again. \"The offer keeps,\" he says, \"like the bottles.\"|The corner table, the bottles, the little bow from his chair. \"Still a friend in need of friends?\" he asks.}\ntext [46cf46] = {格里斯克招手让你到他的角落桌边，账本旁立着一只金瓶和一只黑瓶。“听说你对村里的上等药方有点兴趣，”他柔声说。“我能让你一直不缺。不只收钱。朋友之间，人情比钱更经得住放。船屋码头，哪个午夜都行。”|格里斯克的角落桌，那两只瓶子又在。“这提议一直有效，”他说，“跟这些瓶子一样。”|角落桌，瓶子，他坐在椅子上微微一躬。“还是那个需要朋友的朋友吗？”他问。}\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat at a corner table of a candlelit tavern, a gold bottle and a black one standing on the table beside his ledger.\nscene [c6d256] = 烛光昏暗的酒馆角落桌边，一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精坐着，账本旁立着一只金瓶和一只黑瓶。\n# en: Shake his hand\nopt.left.label [e76d26] = 和他握手\n# en: {His hand is dry and very warm. He slides the gold bottle across the table. \"A sample,\" he says. \"Between friends, the first is a gift. The rest, at the jetty.\"|You shake. Grisk writes something in his ledger with a flourish, and hands you the gold bottle \"for goodwill\". \"Midnight, the jetty,\" he says. \"I keep.\"|You shake on it. He tucks the black bottle away and gives you the gold one. \"The black costs a favour,\" he says. \"Come down to the jetty when you want one.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [8b1b37] = {他的手干燥而温热。他把金瓶推过桌面。“样品，”他说。“朋友之间，第一瓶是送的。往后的，在码头。”|你们握了手。格里斯克花哨地在账本上写了点什么，把金瓶递给你，“聊表善意”。“午夜，码头，”他说。“我一直都在。”|你们握手成交。他把黑瓶收起来，把金瓶给了你。“黑的要一个人情，”他说。“想要的时候就到码头来。”}\n# en: Not interested\nopt.right.label [ccbe6d] = 不感兴趣\n# en: {\"The offer keeps,\" says Grisk, unoffended, and turns back to his ledger.|He bows you away from the table without getting up. \"Another night, then. I'm a patient friend.\"|\"Of course,\" says Grisk. He doesn't believe you, and he lets you see it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a4e85f] = {“提议一直有效，”格里斯克毫不介意，又低头看他的账本。|他坐着没起身，鞠躬送你离开桌边。“那就改天。我是个有耐心的朋友。”|“当然，”格里斯克说。他不信你，也故意让你看出来。}\n\n@event grisk_elixir_favour\n# en: A Bottle for a Favour\ntitle [ab3a91] = 一瓶换一个人情\n# en: Grisk has a bottle and a favour, and they come as a pair. {&\"Watch the jetty for an hour, and see nothing.\"|\"Keep a crate under your bed till Thursday.\"|\"A kind word to a prefect, about a friend of mine.\"} {before_finals() or has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want): \"And a few crowns on delivery,\" he adds. \"{before_finals(): Ink is dear this close to finals.|You look like someone who needs a friend, and friends are dear.}\"|He smiles. \"Small things.\"}\ntext [ff7ee2] = 格里斯克手里有一只瓶子和一个人情，两样是配套的。{&“替我看一个钟头码头，什么也别看见。”|“帮我把一只木箱在你床底下放到星期四。”|“替我的一个朋友，在某位级长面前美言一句。”} {before_finals() or has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want): “交货时再加几个克朗，”他补了一句。“{before_finals(): 离期末这么近，墨可贵了。|你看起来像个需要朋友的人，而朋友是很贵的。}”|他微笑着。“小事而已。”}\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat on a midnight jetty holding up a small bottle between two fingers, a ledger open on a crate beside him.\nscene [cf12b3] = 午夜的码头上，一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精用两根手指夹着一只小瓶举起来，旁边一只木箱上摊着账本。\n# en: Owl's Ink, for the favour\nopt.ink.label [40d55a] = 用这个人情换枭墨\n# en: A few crowns more before finals, and when he knows you need it.\nopt.ink.hint [ada41d] = 期末前要多付几个克朗，他知道你需要它时也是。\n# en: {&You stand on the jetty for an hour and see nothing, very carefully. At dawn there's a black bottle on your windowsill, with a note that says \"Friends.\"|The crate sits under your bed till Thursday, humming faintly. Then it's gone, and a black bottle is where it stood.|You put in Grisk's word with a prefect, and it lands. The black bottle arrives in your shoe, which you don't like thinking about.}\nopt.ink.say.1 [762d35] = {&你在码头站了一个钟头，非常小心地什么也没看见。天亮时窗台上有一只黑瓶，还有一张纸条，上面写着“朋友”。|木箱在你床底下放到星期四，一直在轻轻嗡鸣。然后它不见了，原地放着一只黑瓶。|你替格里斯克在一位级长面前说了话，说成了。黑瓶出现在你的鞋里，你不愿多想这件事。}\n# en: {&A prefect's lantern comes down the lawn while you're watching the jetty. You're in their notebook before you've thought of a reason. No bottle; Grisk doesn't pay for failures.|A prefect doing rounds finds the crate under your bed and asks what's in it. You don't know, which is the worst answer. The crate goes, and no bottle comes.|The prefect hears you out, then asks who put you up to it. You don't say, and your name goes in the notebook anyway. Grisk sends only his regrets.}\nopt.ink.say.2 [7c238c] = {&你正看着码头，一位级长的提灯从草坪那头过来了。你还没想好理由，名字已经进了小本子。没有瓶子；格里斯克不为失败付账。|一位巡夜的级长发现了你床底下的木箱，问里面是什么。你不知道，这是最糟的回答。木箱被拿走了，瓶子也没来。|级长听完你的话，问是谁让你来的。你没说，名字照样进了小本子。格里斯克只送来他的遗憾。}\n# en: Brightwater, for the favour\nopt.bright.label [b7f3e9] = 用这个人情换亮泉水\n# en: A few crowns more before finals, and when he knows you need it.\nopt.bright.hint [ada41d] = 期末前要多付几个克朗，他知道你需要它时也是。\n# en: {&You stand on the jetty for an hour and see nothing, very carefully. At dawn there's a gold bottle on your windowsill, with a note that says \"Friends.\"|The crate sits under your bed till Thursday, humming faintly. Then it's gone, and a gold bottle is where it stood.|You put in Grisk's word with a prefect, and it lands. The gold bottle arrives in your shoe, which you don't like thinking about.}\nopt.bright.say.1 [8fa2fe] = {&你在码头站了一个钟头，非常小心地什么也没看见。天亮时窗台上有一只金瓶，还有一张纸条，上面写着“朋友”。|木箱在你床底下放到星期四，一直在轻轻嗡鸣。然后它不见了，原地放着一只金瓶。|你替格里斯克在一位级长面前说了话，说成了。金瓶出现在你的鞋里，你不愿多想这件事。}\n# en: {&A prefect's lantern comes down the lawn while you're watching the jetty. You're in their notebook before you've thought of a reason. No bottle; Grisk doesn't pay for failures.|A prefect doing rounds finds the crate under your bed and asks what's in it. You don't know, which is the worst answer. The crate goes, and no bottle comes.|The prefect hears you out, then asks who put you up to it. You don't say, and your name goes in the notebook anyway. Grisk sends only his regrets.}\nopt.bright.say.2 [7c238c] = {&你正看着码头，一位级长的提灯从草坪那头过来了。你还没想好理由，名字已经进了小本子。没有瓶子；格里斯克不为失败付账。|一位巡夜的级长发现了你床底下的木箱，问里面是什么。你不知道，这是最糟的回答。木箱被拿走了，瓶子也没来。|级长听完你的话，问是谁让你来的。你没说，名字照样进了小本子。格里斯克只送来他的遗憾。}\n# en: Tell him you're done\nopt.done.label [1b5f57] = 告诉他你不干了\n# en: {&\"Everyone is, eventually,\" says Grisk pleasantly, and closes his ledger. \"Until they aren't.\" You walk back up the lawn without a bottle, and it's the longest walk you've had.|Grisk shrugs. \"My door doesn't lock,\" he says. You go back to bed shaking, and proud of it.|\"Done,\" you say. Grisk bows, as if you'd paid him a compliment, and turns the lamp down.}\nopt.done.say.1 [27b9d1] = {&“人人最后都会这么说，”格里斯克和气地说，合上账本。“直到他们不再这么说。”你没带瓶子走回草坪，那是你走过最长的一段路。|格里斯克耸耸肩。“我的门不上锁，”他说。你回去睡觉时浑身发抖，也为此骄傲。|“不干了，”你说。格里斯克鞠了一躬，好像你恭维了他，然后把灯拧暗。}\n# en: Not this month\nopt.none.label [9e6244] = 这个月不要\n# en: {&Grisk tucks the bottle away. \"Next month, then.\"|You shake your head. Grisk makes a small note in his ledger, as if you'd ordered something.|\"Of course,\" says Grisk. \"I keep.\"}\nopt.none.say.1 [082407] = {&格里斯克把瓶子收起来。“那就下个月。”|你摇摇头。格里斯克在账本上记了一笔，好像你订了什么货。|“当然，”格里斯克说。“我一直都在。”}\n\n@event contraband_caught\n# en: A Search at Dawn\ntitle [0602c9] = 黎明搜查\n# en: The prefects are going down your corridor at dawn, trunk by trunk, sniffing bottles. {has(brightwater_bottle) or has(owls_ink_bottle): There is a bottle in yours.|Your trunk is clean, nearly, but you know what has been through your hands lately, and the prefect sniffing at the lid looks as if they know it too.}\ntext [61f1b4] = 天刚亮，级长们正沿着你的走廊一只箱子一只箱子地搜，挨个闻瓶子。{has(brightwater_bottle) or has(owls_ink_bottle): 你的箱子里有一只瓶子。|你的箱子差不多是干净的，可你知道最近有什么经过了你的手，而在箱盖边嗅来嗅去的那位级长，看起来也知道。}\n# en: A dormitory corridor at dawn, two prefects in red sashes going through an open school trunk, students in nightclothes watching from their doors.\nscene [838f02] = 黎明的宿舍走廊，两个系红肩带的级长在翻一只打开的学生箱，穿睡衣的学生们从门口看着。\n# en: Bluff it out\nopt.left.label [9cc9e7] = 硬撑过去\n# en: You're half asleep and mildly offended, and they believe both. The prefects move on to the next trunk. You sit on the end of your bed until your heart slows down.\nopt.left.say.1 [ba9b16] = 你半睡半醒，还有点被冒犯，他们两样都信了。级长们去搜下一只箱子。你坐在床尾，等心跳慢下来。\n# en: They find it in under a minute. The bottle goes into a bag labelled for the confiscation vault, your name goes into a notebook, and the whole corridor watches you go red.\nopt.left.say.2 [a25840] = 他们不到一分钟就找到了。瓶子进了一只标着“没收库”的袋子，你的名字进了一个小本子，整条走廊都看着你脸红。\n# en: There's nothing in the trunk to find, but your face gives you away. You get a warning, a line in the prefect's notebook, and a long look from the whole corridor.\nopt.left.say.3 [c5ded6] = 箱子里没什么可找的，可你的脸出卖了你。你得到一次警告，级长的小本子上多了一行，还有整条走廊投来的长长的目光。\n# en: Own up before they look\nopt.right.label [673e92] = 在他们搜之前主动交出\n# en: You hand it over before they reach your trunk. The prefect bags it for the confiscation vault, looks at you for a long moment, and writes only a warning. It still stings.\nopt.right.say.1 [e2da48] = 他们还没搜到你的箱子，你就交了出来。级长把它装袋送往没收库，久久地看了你一眼，只记了一次警告。还是很刺痛。\n# en: You tell them before they ask. With nothing to confiscate, the prefect writes only a warning, and gives you a long look. It still stings.\nopt.right.say.2 [0f4b94] = 他们还没开口，你就说了。没什么可没收的，级长只记了一次警告，久久地看了你一眼。还是很刺痛。\n\n@event contraband_friend_notices\n# en: You Don't Look Right\ntitle [664bfb] = 你看起来不对劲\n# en: {has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want): {~{friend} catches your wrist at supper and turns your hand over. \"You're shaking,\" {they} say{s}. \"What is it?\"|{friend} sits down beside you without a word and looks at your hands until you put them in your lap. \"What is it?\"}|{~\"You've been too bright for weeks,\" {friend} says at supper. \"Nobody's that well. What is it?\"|{friend} watches you finish a week's reading in an evening and says, quietly, \"That isn't you. What is it?\"}}\ntext [fc80a4] = {has(brightwater_want) or has(owls_ink_want): {~晚饭时{friend}抓住你的手腕，把你的手翻过来。“你在发抖，”{they}说。“怎么回事？”|{friend}一言不发地在你身边坐下，一直看着你的手，直到你把手放到膝上。“怎么回事？”}|{~“你已经好几个星期精神好得过头了，”{friend}在晚饭时说。“没人能好成这样。怎么回事？”|{friend}看着你一个晚上读完一个星期的书，轻声说：“这不像你。怎么回事？”}}\n# en: Two students on a stone bench in an empty corridor, one holding the other's hand and looking hard at their face.\nscene [76be65] = 空荡荡的走廊里，两个学生坐在石凳上，一个握着另一个的手，仔细端详着对方的脸。\n# en: Tell {them} the truth\nopt.left.label [9b4c6a] = 告诉{them}实话\n# en: {~You tell {friend} about the bottle. {they} listen{s} without interrupting, and at the end only say{s}, \"All right. Now I know. Sister Amaranth sees this every year, you know.\"|You tell {them} all of it. {friend} doesn't lecture, and doesn't leave, and mentions the infirmary only once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e77de1] = {~你把瓶子的事告诉了{friend}。{they}听着，没有打断，最后只说：“好。现在我知道了。阿玛兰斯修女每年都见这种事，你知道的。”|你把一切都告诉了{them}。{friend}没有说教，也没有离开，只提了一次医务室。}\n# en: Laugh it off\nopt.right.label [29c02a] = 一笑带过\n# en: {~\"Exams,\" you say, and laugh. {friend} lets the subject go, slowly, and doesn't believe you.|\"I'm fine.\" {friend} looks at you for a long moment. \"All right,\" {they} say{s}. It isn't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2abe7e] = {~“考试嘛，”你说着笑了。{friend}慢慢放下了这个话题，并不相信。|“我没事。”{friend}看了你很久。“好吧，”{they}说。并不好。}\n\n@event wd_bright_grey\n# en: The First Thought\ntitle [6e97af] = 醒来的第一个念头\n# en: Your first thought on waking is the gold bottle, and so is your second. The Brightwater wore off weeks ago. Everything since has needed a little more pushing than it should.\ntext [822d6f] = 你醒来的第一个念头是那只金瓶，第二个也是。亮泉水的劲儿几个星期前就过去了。从那以后，每件事都要多推一把才动得了。\n# en: A student lying awake in bed at dawn, eyes on the school trunk at the foot of the bed, one hand already reaching for the lid.\nscene [b94190] = 黎明时分，一个学生醒着躺在床上，眼睛盯着床尾的学生箱，一只手已经伸向箱盖。\n# en: Get up anyway\nopt.rise.label [318971] = 照样起床\n# en: You get up, dress, and go to every lecture, thinking about the trunk the whole way. It counts. It doesn't feel like it counts.\nopt.rise.say.1 [8c89c2] = 你起床，穿衣，去上每一节课，一路上都想着那只箱子。这算数。只是感觉不像算数。\n# en: Stay in bed\nopt.bed.label [50ea64] = 赖在床上\n# en: You sleep through the morning and most of the afternoon. You miss two lectures and feel no better for it, only rested.\nopt.bed.say.1 [b4ff70] = 你睡过了整个上午和大半个下午。你错过了两节课，并没觉得好些，只是休息够了。\n# en: Drink the bottle in your trunk\nopt.dose.label [fd39ce] = 喝下箱子里那瓶\n# en: One swallow, and the pushing stops. Everything is easy again at once. You hate how easy that was.\nopt.dose.say.1 [8d638b] = 一口下去，那股要推的劲儿没了。一切一下子又变得容易。你恨这有多容易。\n\n@event wd_bright_shakes\n# en: Hands That Won't Hold Still\ntitle [90661c] = 停不下来的手\n# en: In the practical your hands won't hold still. The vial shivers; the measure goes over the line, twice. The student at the next bench pretends not to see.\ntext [ce51f6] = 实验课上，你的手停不下来。小瓶在抖；量了两次都超了刻度。隔壁桌的同学假装没看见。\n# en: A student at a laboratory bench, their hand trembling so that the liquid in a glass vial shivers.\nscene [e1a1ab] = 实验台前，一个学生的手抖得厉害，玻璃小瓶里的液体跟着颤动。\n# en: Push through it\nopt.push.label [be8889] = 硬撑下去\n# en: You grip your wrist with your other hand and finish the measure, badly. Afterwards you sit in the corridor until the shaking stops.\nopt.push.say.1 [050528] = 你用另一只手攥住手腕，把这次量完了，量得很糟。课后你坐在走廊里，直到不再发抖。\n# en: Ask to sit it out\nopt.sit.label [14ec20] = 请求先不做\n# en: You ask to sit out. The lecturer looks at your hands, then at your face, and says yes too quickly.\nopt.sit.say.1 [9d00c7] = 你请求先不做。讲师看看你的手，又看看你的脸，答应得太快了。\n# en: Drink the bottle in your trunk\nopt.dose.label [fd39ce] = 喝下箱子里那瓶\n# en: You leave the practical \"for some air\" and drink it in the washroom. Your hands go still. Your face in the mirror doesn't look like a winner's.\nopt.dose.say.1 [523922] = 你借口“出去透透气”离开实验课，在盥洗室里喝了它。你的手稳了。镜子里那张脸不像赢家。\n\n@event wd_bright_grisk\n# en: Grisk Knows\ntitle [68002d] = 格里斯克知道\n# en: Grisk is sitting on your bed. You didn't leave the window open. He holds a gold bottle up to the candle and turns it. \"You look peaky,\" he says kindly. \"I have just the thing.\" {flag(grisk_supply): \"The price has gone up, of course. Everything has.\"|\"Not a friend's price, I'm afraid. You never did come to see me.\"}\ntext [18470c] = 格里斯克坐在你的床上。你没有开窗。他把一只金瓶举到烛光下转着看。“你脸色不太好，”他和蔼地说。“我正好有对症的东西。”{flag(grisk_supply): “当然，价钱涨了。什么都涨了。”|“恐怕不是朋友价了。你一直没来找过我。”}\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting uninvited on a student's bed at night, a gold bottle held up to the candle.\nscene [5b9e67] = 夜里，一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精不请自来地坐在学生床上，把一只金瓶举到烛光前。\n# en: Pay what he asks\nopt.buy.label [047cea] = 付他要的价\n# en: You pay. He counts it twice, sets the bottle on your pillow like a mint, and leaves by the window.\nopt.buy.say.1 [f5c19e] = 你付了钱。他数了两遍，把瓶子像薄荷糖一样放在你的枕头上，从窗口走了。\n# en: Show him the window\nopt.no.label [d76863] = 请他从窗口出去\n# en: \"Another night,\" says Grisk, quite unbothered, and goes out the way he came. You don't sleep, but you didn't buy it.\nopt.no.say.1 [05ad93] = “那就改天，”格里斯克毫不在意，从原路出去了。你没睡着，但你没买。\n\n@event wd_ink_blank\n# en: A Blank Page\ntitle [161c6d] = 一张白纸\n# en: In the lecture, the words go past you like carriages. Two months ago you could have recited them back. Now your page is blank, and the lecturer is already on the next board.\ntext [70b45c] = 课上，那些话像马车一样从你身边驶过。两个月前你还能一字不差地复述。现在你的纸上一片空白，讲师已经写到下一块黑板了。\n# en: A student in a lecture hall staring at a completely blank page, quill hovering, the lecturer's words filling the board.\nscene [40709c] = 阶梯教室里，一个学生盯着一张完全空白的纸，羽毛笔悬着，讲师的字写满了黑板。\n# en: Sit it out, blank\nopt.stare.label [c92946] = 空着坐完\n# en: You sit through the hour writing nothing. At the end you have the date at the top of the page, and that's all.\nopt.stare.say.1 [609ccf] = 你坐了一个钟头，什么也没写。下课时，你的纸上只有顶上的日期。\n# en: Admit you can't follow\nopt.ask.label [a6fe19] = 承认自己跟不上\n# en: You put your hand up and say you've lost the thread. The lecturer is kind about it, which is worse, and goes back over it slowly, for you.\nopt.ask.say.1 [da5265] = 你举手说自己跟丢了。讲师很和善，这更糟，还专门为你慢慢讲了一遍。\n# en: Drink the bottle in your trunk\nopt.dose.label [fd39ce] = 喝下箱子里那瓶\n# en: You drink it behind the lecture hall and go back in. The board snaps into focus mid-sentence. You copy it all, and your hand doesn't feel like yours.\nopt.dose.say.1 [a594ea] = 你在讲堂后面喝了它，然后回去。黑板在半句话中间猛地对上了焦。你全抄了下来，那只手不像是你的。\n\n@event wd_ink_empty_well\n# en: The Well Runs Dry\ntitle [ace09b] = 井干了\n# en: You reach for your magic in the practice room, and there's nothing there: not empty, exactly, but dry, like a well at the end of a long summer. It used to be so easy, on the ink.\ntext [ed1cb0] = 你在练习室里伸手去够你的魔力，那里什么也没有：不完全是空的，而是干的，像漫长夏天尽头的一口井。用着墨的时候，这曾经那么容易。\n# en: A student in a practice room, hand outstretched, a spell fizzling out into a few weak sparks.\nscene [91bbcf] = 练习室里，一个学生伸出手，一个法术熄成几点微弱的火星。\n# en: Force it\nopt.force.label [9d7811] = 硬逼出来\n# en: You wring the spell out of yourself. It comes, thin and late. You sit down on the floor afterwards without meaning to.\nopt.force.say.1 [2c5654] = 你把法术从自己身上拧了出来。它来了，又细又迟。之后你不由自主地坐到了地上。\n# en: Leave it for today\nopt.wait.label [1afaa7] = 今天先算了\n# en: You put your hands in your pockets and leave the practice for another day. The day after, it's still dry.\nopt.wait.say.1 [c8c1d4] = 你把手插进口袋，把练习留到改天。第二天，井还是干的。\n# en: Drink the bottle in your trunk\nopt.dose.label [fd39ce] = 喝下箱子里那瓶\n# en: One dose and the well is full again, brimming, easy. You stand there with sparks on your fingers, ashamed of how good it feels.\nopt.dose.say.1 [667d2e] = 一剂下去，井又满了，满得溢出来，轻而易举。你站在那儿，指尖冒着火星，为这感觉有多好而羞愧。\n\n@event wd_ink_dreams\n# en: Ink in Your Sleep\ntitle [ba807a] = 梦里的墨\n# en: You dream of black ink rising in a well, over your feet, your knees, your mouth, and you wake with the taste of a burnt quill. The candle has burned down, and the castle is very quiet.\ntext [9a4fd9] = 你梦见黑墨在一口井里上涨，漫过你的脚，你的膝盖，你的嘴，醒来时嘴里有烧焦羽毛笔的味道。蜡烛已经燃尽，城堡里非常安静。\n# en: A student sitting up in bed in the dark, sheets tangled, staring at their hands as if expecting ink on them.\nscene [738e0f] = 黑暗中，一个学生在床上坐起，床单缠成一团，盯着自己的双手，好像以为上面有墨。\n# en: Lie awake till dawn\nopt.lie.label [e73fd5] = 醒着躺到天亮\n# en: You lie and watch the window go from black to grey. By breakfast you feel scraped thin.\nopt.lie.say.1 [5c54bc] = 你躺着看窗户从黑变灰。到早饭时，你觉得自己被刮薄了一层。\n# en: Walk the corridors till it passes\nopt.walk.label [e3a765] = 在走廊里走到它过去\n# en: You walk the dark corridors in your socks until your head clears. A lantern turns a far corner; you don't wait to see whose.\nopt.walk.say.1 [747508] = 你穿着袜子在黑暗的走廊里走，直到脑子清醒。远处拐角有一盏提灯转了过来；你没等着看是谁的。\n# en: Drink the bottle in your trunk\nopt.dose.label [fd39ce] = 喝下箱子里那瓶\n# en: You drink it in the dark. The dream doesn't come back. In the morning you can't decide whether that was rescue or surrender.\nopt.dose.say.1 [b5b241] = 你在黑暗中喝了它。那个梦没再回来。早上，你分不清那是得救还是投降。\n\n@event amaranth_quit\n# en: Sister Amaranth's Remedy\ntitle [85b5c5] = 阿玛兰斯修女的药方\n# en: Sister Amaranth looks at you once, the way she looks at a fever chart. \"Sit,\" she says, still rolling a bandage. \"I've seen that face before. Brightwater, or the ink? Don't answer. Drink this every morning for a week. It's foul. It works.\"\ntext [f1e33e] = 阿玛兰斯修女看了你一眼，就像看一张体温表。“坐，”她说，手里还在卷绷带。“这张脸我见过。亮泉水，还是墨？别回答。这个每天早上喝，喝一个星期。难喝。管用。”\n# en: A stern healer in an infirmary looking at a student over a rolled bandage, a bitter-looking cup of medicine on the table between them.\nscene [18653b] = 医务室里，一位严厉的医护修女越过一卷绷带看着一个学生，两人之间的桌上放着一杯看起来很苦的药。\n# en: Take the remedy\nopt.left.label [8d5c87] = 接受这药方\n# en: A week of bitter mornings, and her hand held out each time for any bottle you still have. On the eighth day the world comes back in colour. \"Drink it all,\" she says, \"and don't come back for the same thing.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [ee8ddd] = 一个星期苦涩的早晨，每次她都伸出手，要走你手上还剩的瓶子。第八天，世界的颜色回来了。“全喝完，”她说，“别再为同一件事回来。”\n# en: \"It's just a cold, Sister.\"\nopt.right.label [a2ea9f] = “只是感冒，修女。”\n# en: \"Hm,\" says Sister Amaranth, and goes back to her bandage. \"The door's open. It always is.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [895eb3] = “嗯，”阿玛兰斯修女说，又回去卷她的绷带。“门开着。一直开着。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/crisis.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/crisis.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event crisis_collapse\n# en: Collapse\ntitle [59ccfe] = 倒下\n# en: {collapse_cures == 0: The world tilts. When it rights itself you are in the infirmary, and Sister Amaranth is looking at you over her spectacles. \"You have been running your body like a borrowed broom,\" she says.|{collapse_cures == 1: The world tilts again, harder. You wake in the infirmary bed you know too well, and Sister Amaranth isn't looking over her spectacles this time; she's taken them off. \"Twice,\" she says. \"The body keeps count, even when you don't. The cure's dearer now, because you are worse.\"|You come round in the infirmary with no memory of falling. Sister Amaranth is already mixing the black tonic, the one she keeps on the top shelf. \"Again,\" she says, flatly. \"Each time it takes more to bring you back, and costs more. One of these times it won't.\"}}\ntext [73070a] = {collapse_cures == 0: 世界倾斜了。等它恢复正常时，你已经躺在医务室里，阿玛兰斯修女正从眼镜上方看着你。“你对待自己的身体，就像对待一把借来的飞帚。”她说。|{collapse_cures == 1: 世界又一次倾斜，这回更猛。你在那张再熟悉不过的病床上醒来，阿玛兰斯修女这次没有从眼镜上方看你——她把眼镜摘了。“第二次了，”她说，“你不记账，身体会记。药更贵了，因为你更糟了。”|你在医务室里醒来，完全不记得自己是怎么倒下的。阿玛兰斯修女已经在调那瓶黑色的药剂，就是她放在最高一层架子上的那种。“又来了，”她平平地说，“每一次，要把你拉回来都得花更多力气，也更花钱。总有一次会拉不回来。”}}\n# en: A narrow infirmary bed under a high arched window, a healer's lamp floating beside it.\nscene [27d146] = 高高的拱窗下一张窄窄的病床，旁边悬浮着一盏治疗师的灯。\n# en: {collapse_cures == 0: Pay for the full cure (30 crowns)|{collapse_cures == 1: Pay for the dearer cure (60 crowns)|Pay for the black tonic (120 crowns)}}\nopt.left.label [8b06af] = {collapse_cures == 0: 付钱彻底治好（30 金币）|{collapse_cures == 1: 付钱买更贵的药（60 金币）|付钱买黑色药剂（120 金币）}}\n# en: Three foul tonics and two days' sleep later, you feel almost human.\nopt.left.say.1 [ecd52f] = 三剂难喝的药和两天的睡眠之后，你感觉差不多像个人了。\n# en: Six foul tonics and four days' sleep later, you feel almost human, and you can still taste the third one.\nopt.left.say.2 [f2524e] = 六剂难喝的药和四天的睡眠之后，你感觉差不多像个人了，第三剂的味道到现在还留在嘴里。\n# en: The black tonic tastes of tar and lightning. It takes a week to work and you remember none of it, but your body comes back to you, sullenly, like a dog that's been kicked.\nopt.left.say.3 [043ef6] = 黑色药剂尝起来像柏油和闪电。它花了一周才起效，你什么也不记得，但你的身体回来了，不情不愿的，像一条挨过踢的狗。\n# en: Ask to rest instead\nopt.right.label [8eabcd] = 请求静养\n# en: A long, dull fortnight in bed. Sister Amaranth warns you: \"Next time, I write to your family.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [b20134] = 在床上躺了漫长而无聊的两周。阿玛兰斯修女警告你：“下次，我就写信给你家里。”\n\n@event crisis_despair\n# en: The Grey\ntitle [9c424f] = 灰色\n# en: Your spirits have hit bottom. Everything has gone grey: food tastes of nothing, lectures are noise, and you have packed your trunk twice this week and unpacked it once. It would be so easy to take the next barge and simply stop.\ntext [2115ba] = 你的心情跌到了谷底。一切都变成了灰色：吃什么都没味道，课堂只剩噪音，这周你收拾了两次行李，又拆开了一次。坐上下一班驳船，就此停下，是多么容易。\n# en: A student sitting on the edge of a dormitory bed, rain on the window, a packed trunk by the door.\nscene [dc74cf] = 一个学生坐在宿舍床沿，窗上有雨，门边放着收拾好的行李箱。\n# en: Reach out to someone\nopt.left.label [e2c5e9] = 向别人伸出手\n# en: Spirits come back, after a night that breaks something else.\nopt.left.hint [b0fa2b] = 心情会回来，但要先熬过一个毁掉别的东西的夜晚。\n# en: First you cry, properly, the way you haven't since you were small, until the window goes pale. Then you sleep straight through the morning's lectures, and wake ashamed of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [818977] = 你先是哭，痛痛快快地哭，像小时候那样，一直哭到窗外发白。然后你一觉睡过了上午所有的课，醒来时满心羞愧。\n# en: You walk out to the empty practice field and cast until there is nothing left to cast: fire, wind, light, anything. You come back hollow and shaking, and lighter.\nopt.left.say.2 [0e034a] = 你走到空荡荡的练习场，一直施法，直到再也施不出任何东西：火、风、光，什么都行。回来时你空空荡荡、浑身发抖，却轻松了。\n# en: You walk the grounds in the rain all night without a cloak, round and round the lawns. A prefect finds you at dawn, soaked to the skin, and the story is round the dormitory by breakfast.\nopt.left.say.3 [ddf53e] = 你没披斗篷，在雨里绕着草坪走了一整夜。黎明时一个级长发现了你，浑身湿透，早饭前这事就传遍了宿舍。\n# en: You throw your inkwell at the wall, then the chair, then the little mirror. Afterwards you sit among the pieces, oddly calm. Replacing it all costs a small fortune.\nopt.left.say.4 [6262b2] = 你把墨水瓶砸向墙，然后是椅子，然后是那面小镜子。之后你坐在一地碎片中间，出奇地平静。把这些东西全部换新，花了一小笔钱。\n# en: In the morning you knock on a friend's door. They don't ask questions. They just make tea, and stay.\nopt.left.say.5 [ddca3a] = 早上你敲响了朋友的门。对方什么也没问。只是泡了茶，陪着你。\n# en: Old Maud finds you in the gardens and puts a trowel in your hand. \"Dig,\" she says. You dig. It helps more than it should.\nopt.left.say.6 [a27142] = 老莫德在花园里找到你，把一把小铲子塞进你手里。“挖。”她说。你挖了。这比想象中更有用。\n# en: Afterwards you talk to the night porter, of all people. He tells you about his own first year. It's enough, for now.\nopt.left.say.7 [ae8ca6] = 事后你竟然和守夜门房聊了起来。他给你讲了他自己的一年级。暂时，这就够了。\n# en: Take the next barge\nopt.right.label [c52bdf] = 坐下一班驳船\n# en: Spirits back for now; a second time, you go.\nopt.right.hint [e4dad5] = 心情暂时回来；第二次，你就会离开。\n# en: You get as far as the landing. The barge is late. By the time it arrives, you have decided to stay one more month.\nopt.right.say.1 [d9e779] = 你走到了码头。驳船晚点了。等它到的时候，你决定再留一个月。\n\n@event crisis_probation\n# en: The Disciplinary Board\ntitle [f764c3] = 纪律委员会\n# en: Two terms of failing marks. You are summoned before the Disciplinary Board: three professors and the headmistress, who looks at you with an expression of deep, patient disappointment. {flag(board_reprieve): They have seen you here before.|}\ntext [db6aa2] = 连续两个学期挂科。你被传唤到纪律委员会面前：三位教授和院长，院长看着你的表情里满是深沉而耐心的失望。{flag(board_reprieve): 他们以前在这里见过你。|}\n# en: Three professors behind a high bench in a dim chamber, the headmistress in the centre, hands folded.\nscene [e88ce4] = 昏暗的厅堂里，三位教授坐在高高的长桌后，院长居中，双手交握。\n# en: Plead your case\nopt.left.label [bb8fcc] = 为自己辩护\n# en: You speak honestly and well. They grant you one reprieve, and a study plan that fills every evening. \"There will not be a second,\" says the headmistress, pleasantly.\nopt.left.say.1 [03505f] = 你说得诚恳而得体。他们给了你一次宽限，还有一份把每个晚上都排满的学习计划。“不会有第二次了，”院长和气地说。\n# en: Promise to work harder\nopt.right.label [a78172] = 保证会更努力\n# en: \"Promises are cheap,\" says Professor Thorne. \"We'll take this one. Only this one.\" A reprieve, a study plan in his own hand, and a very long term ahead.\nopt.right.say.1 [8df15e] = “承诺不值钱，”索恩教授说，“这一次我们收下。只此一次。”一次宽限，一份他亲笔写的学习计划，以及一个漫长的学期。\n\n@event grisk_reminder\n# en: A Friendly Reminder\ntitle [6aaed3] = 友情提醒\n# en: A note on your door, in a tidy goblin hand: \"A payment was missed. Mister Grisk is a patient businessman. Mister Grisk charges interest on his patience.\" Underneath, smaller: \"Pay something, or another month will be added.\"\ntext [67613b] = 你门上钉着一张字条，是工整的地精笔迹：“有一笔还款逾期了。格里斯克先生是位有耐心的生意人。格里斯克先生的耐心是要收利息的。”下面一行小字：“多少还一点，否则再加一个月。”\n# en: A small, very neat note pinned to a dormitory door with a brass tack, the handwriting tiny and precise.\nscene [309f11] = 一张小小的、非常整洁的字条用黄铜图钉钉在宿舍门上，字迹细小而一丝不苟。\n# en: Scrape together what you can\nopt.left.label [d226e0] = 能凑多少凑多少\n# en: You empty your purse into an envelope and slide it under his door. It isn't twelve crowns. It is, says the note in reply, \"a gesture. Noted.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [acdacd] = 你把钱袋倒进一个信封，塞到他门下。不到十二克朗。回信的字条上写着：“一份心意。已记录。”\n# en: There is nothing to put in the envelope. You write \"next month, I promise\" on a scrap and slide that under his door instead. The reply is a single line, \"Promises are added to the ledger,\" and another month is.\nopt.left.say.2 [b418a0] = 信封里没什么可放的。你在一张纸片上写下“下个月，我保证”，从他门缝底下塞了进去。回信只有一行：“承诺记入账簿。”账簿上又多了一个月。\n# en: Put it out of your mind\nopt.right.label [68419d] = 别去想它\n# en: You put the note in a drawer. Another month goes on the ledger, and next month the knock on your door will be louder.\nopt.right.say.1 [2bdb82] = 你把字条塞进抽屉。账本上又多了一个月，下个月的敲门声会更响。\n\n@event grisk_collectors\n# en: Grisk's Collectors\ntitle [eb1401] = 格里斯克的讨债人\n# en: Two trolls in waistcoats are waiting outside your dormitory. \"Mister Grisk,\" says the larger one, \"is disappointed.\" The smaller one cracks his knuckles, which sounds like a rockslide.\ntext [6dfc11] = 两个穿马甲的巨魔等在你的宿舍外。“格里斯克先生，”大个子说，“很失望。”小个子把指关节捏得咔咔响，听起来像山崩。\n# en: Two large trolls in ill-fitting waistcoats looming in a dormitory doorway, one holding a ledger.\nscene [8592b2] = 两个穿着不合身马甲的高大巨魔堵在宿舍门口，其中一个拿着账簿。\n# en: Hand over everything you have\nopt.left.label [eec2ea] = 交出所有的钱\n# en: They count your purse twice and declare the debt settled. \"Pleasure,\" says the large one, meaning it.\nopt.left.say.1 [377b97] = 他们把你的钱袋数了两遍，宣布债务两清。“荣幸。”大个子说，是真心的。\n# en: They take every coin and your good boots, in front of half the corridor. \"Partial,\" they say, and leave.\nopt.left.say.2 [8315e9] = 他们当着半条走廊的人，拿走了每一枚硬币和你最好的靴子。“部分还款。”他们说完就走了。\n# en: Run\nopt.right.label [57e66a] = 逃跑\n# en: You escape across the rooftops. They'll be back, but not this month.\nopt.right.say.1 [24257a] = 你从屋顶上逃走了。他们还会回来，但不是这个月。\n# en: You do not escape. The trolls are surprisingly gentle about breaking only one rib.\nopt.right.say.2 [0374eb] = 你没逃掉。巨魔们出人意料地温柔，只断了你一根肋骨。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/detention.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/detention.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event det_vault_catalogue\n# en: Cataloguing the Vault\ntitle [f48c6e] = 清点没收品库\n# en: {Detention in the confiscation vault, among three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student, and a ledger nobody has kept properly in decades.|Today's shelf is the cursed dice. The goblin alarm over the door watches you take down the first box.|The goblin alarm over the door remembers you, and says so, loudly, until the prefect hushes it.} {A fanged flying disc snaps at your sleeve.|A goblet of endless soup is still, after all these years, gently simmering.|A box marked DO NOT OPEN is rattling politely.}\ntext [ca1cab] = {禁闭地点是没收品库，三百年来教职员从学生手里没收的所有东西都堆在这里，还有一本几十年没人好好记过的登记簿。|今天要清的是放诅咒骰子的那一层。门上那只地精警报器看着你取下第一只盒子。|门上那只地精警报器认出了你，还大声嚷嚷了出来，直到级长让它闭嘴。} {一只长着獠牙的飞盘咬住了你的袖子。|一只永远舀不空的汤杯，过了这么多年，还在轻轻冒着热气。|一只写着“请勿打开”的盒子正彬彬有礼地咔哒作响。}\n# en: A cramped stone vault crammed with confiscated student contraband, a student with a ledger and a candle, a goblin-shaped alarm glaring from above the door.\nscene [dc3972] = 一间狭小的石头库房，架子上塞满了从学生那里没收来的违禁品，一个学生拿着登记簿和一支蜡烛，门上方一只地精模样的警报器怒目而视。\n# en: Catalogue it shelf by shelf\nopt.left.label [0789e4] = 一层一层地登记\n# en: {You write up forty things by candlelight, and by the end you know where the vault keeps what. Your eyes feel sandpapered.|Shelf by shelf. You find where this year's confiscations go, by the door, and where nothing has been touched since before Maud was a student. Your wrist aches.|Today it's the love potions, shelf after shelf of them. It's dull work, and you now know the vault better than the prefects do.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ad8a09] = {你借着烛光登记了四十样东西，到最后，库里什么放在哪儿你都清清楚楚。眼睛像被砂纸磨过。|一层接一层。你找到了今年没收品放的地方，就在门边；也找到了从莫德还是学生之前就没人动过的地方。手腕酸得厉害。|今天是迷情药水，一层又一层。活儿很枯燥，而你现在比级长们更熟悉这座库房了。}\n# en: Read what students left behind\nopt.right.label [abf214] = 读学生们留下的东西\n# en: {Some of the confiscated things are diaries. You read one instead of cataloguing, and laugh until the goblin coughs, and feel bad about it after.|A whole folder of notes passed in class, taken and never given back. \"Meet me after Astromancy.\" You read them all. The ledger doesn't move an inch.|A love letter, taken in a lecture and never given back. You read it twice, feel like a thief, and put it back exactly where it was.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b46246] = {有些没收品是日记。你没去登记，而是读了一本，笑到地精警报器咳嗽起来，事后又觉得过意不去。|一整夹课堂上传的纸条，被没收后再没还回去。“天文课后见。”你一张张全读了。登记簿上一个字也没多。|一封情书，在课堂上被没收，再也没还回去。你读了两遍，觉得自己像个贼，又把它原样放了回去。}\n\n@event det_prefects_room\n# en: Clerk to the Prefects\ntitle [3c7fe4] = 级长室的抄写员\n# en: {Your detention is clerking. The prefects' demerit ledger has to be copied out fair, every name and every crime, in a hand they can read.|The rota on the wall has a new prefect's initials on it, and the week's demerits are waiting to be copied.|The ledger, the clean copy, and a prefect at the window who hums while you work.} {{other} has three demerits this week, one for \"insolent humming\".|{other} is in this week, for \"a tone of voice\".|{other}'s name comes up twice, both times in the same prefect's hand.} The rota above the desk says who walks which corridor tonight.\ntext [31ba50] = {你的禁闭是当抄写员。级长们的记过簿要誊写一份干净的，每个名字、每条罪名，都得写得让他们看得懂。|墙上的值勤表多了一个新级长的姓名缩写，这一周的记过正等着誊写。|记过簿、誊清本，还有一个站在窗边、边看你干活边哼歌的级长。} {这周{other}被记了三次过，其中一次是因为“傲慢地哼歌”。|{other}这周也上榜了，罪名是“说话的语气”。|{other}的名字出现了两次，都是同一个级长的笔迹。}桌子上方的值勤表写着今晚谁巡哪条走廊。\n# en: A cramped prefects' room with red sashes on pegs, a student copying names from a battered ledger into a clean one, a duty rota pinned to the wall above the desk.\nscene [4ebe78] = 一间狭小的级长室，钉子上挂着红色绶带，一个学生正把一本破旧记过簿上的名字誊到一本新的上，桌子上方的墙上钉着值勤表。\n# en: Copy it, and the rota too\nopt.left.label [f218c5] = 照抄，顺便记下值勤表\n# en: {You copy the ledger faithfully, and the rota into your head while you're at it. Now you know who walks the east stair, and when the library goes unwatched. Your hand cramps by the end.|Every name, every crime, and in the back of your mind the rota, quarter-hour by quarter-hour. It's the dullest useful {slot == \"evening\": evening|afternoon} you've had.|You copy the week out neatly. A fresh rota is pinned up; you learn it without moving your lips.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8465da] = {你一字不差地誊完记过簿，顺手把值勤表也记进了脑子里。现在你知道谁巡东楼梯，图书馆什么时候没人看着了。抄到最后手都抽筋了。|每个名字，每条罪名，脑子里还有那张值勤表，一刻钟一刻钟地排着。这是你过过的最无聊也最有用的一个{slot == \"evening\": 晚上|下午}。|你把这一周的记录工工整整地誊好。墙上钉了一张新的值勤表；你嘴唇都没动，就把它记住了。}\n# en: Leave {other}'s name out\nopt.right.label [d29e95] = 漏掉{other}的名字\n# en: {You copy everything but one line. Nobody checks. {other} never knows why their week was so quiet, and you never say.|One name fewer in the clean copy. The prefect signs it without reading. {other} buys you a cup of tea a week later, for no reason they can name.|You leave out {other}'s line. The ledger is lighter by one insolence, and you feel lighter by none.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8150bc] = {你誊了所有内容，只漏了一行。没人核对。{other}永远不会知道自己这周为什么这么清静，你也永远不会说。|誊清本上少了一个名字。级长看都没看就签了字。一周后，{other}请你喝了杯茶，说不上为什么。|你漏掉了{other}那一行。记过簿上少了一桩傲慢，你心里却没轻松半分。}\n# en: {The prefect checks your copy against the old one, finds the gap, and writes {other}'s name back in, and yours underneath.|\"You missed a line,\" says the prefect, and reads it aloud. You copy it in, in your best hand, while they watch.|A prefect reads over your shoulder. \"Friend of yours?\" They put the name back in red, and stay to watch you finish.}\nopt.right.say.2 [230cc1] = {级长把你的誊本和旧本对了一遍，发现了空缺，把{other}的名字补了回去，你的名字就写在下面。|“你漏了一行，”级长说，还大声念了出来。你用最好的字迹把它补上，他就在旁边看着。|一个级长从你肩后看过来。“你朋友？”他用红笔把名字补回去，还留下来看着你抄完。}\n\n@event det_apology\n# en: Four Hundred Words of Sorry\ntitle [34c0a8] = 四百字的道歉\n# en: {Detention, and a note pinned to the desk. A letter of apology, four hundred words, sincere, to be read by the professor concerned.|The professor's note adds, this time, that spelling counts.|This time the professor has left two good apologies as examples, both by the same student, ten years apart.} {The note underlines \"sincere\" twice.|Someone has carved SORRY into the desk, very deep.|The quill provided is the scratchiest in the castle.}\ntext [905499] = {禁闭，桌上钉着一张字条。一封道歉信，四百字，要真诚，由当事的教授亲自过目。|这回教授的字条上还加了一句：拼写也算分。|这回教授留了两封写得好的道歉信作范例，出自同一个学生之手，前后相隔十年。} {字条上的“真诚”被划了两道线。|有人在桌面上刻了“对不起”，刻得很深。|配给你的羽毛笔是全城堡最刮纸的一支。}\n# en: An empty classroom in fading light, a student frowning at a nearly blank sheet of parchment, a professor's note pinned to the desk.\nscene [d0f816] = 暮色中一间空教室，一个学生对着几乎空白的羊皮纸皱眉，桌上钉着一位教授留的字条。\n# en: Mean every word\nopt.left.label [6447e5] = 每个字都出自真心\n# en: {You write it plainly, and it's harder than lying would have been. The professor reads it, folds it, and says \"thank you\", and means it.|You find you are sorry, about two hundred words in. The rest come slowly. The professor keeps the letter, which feels worse than if they'd burned it.|It doesn't get easier to mean it; you only get better at it. The professor nods at the last line and doesn't make you read it aloud.}\nopt.left.say.1 [de7f4b] = {你写得很朴实，这比撒谎难多了。教授读完，把信折好，说了声“谢谢”，是真心的。|写到两百字左右，你发现自己确实感到抱歉。剩下的字写得很慢。教授把信留下了，这比烧掉还让人难受。|真心并没有变得更容易，你只是更擅长了。教授对着最后一行点点头，没让你念出来。}\n# en: Write a magnificent one\nopt.right.label [dc2cba] = 写一封华丽的\n# en: {Four hundred words of polished regret, with a quotation from the founders. The professor reads it twice and looks almost moved.|You write the apology of the century. The professor calls it \"very thorough\" and files it with the others, which are apparently a collection.|A masterpiece of remorse, and you enjoy writing it more than you should.}\nopt.right.say.1 [461108] = {四百字打磨得光亮的悔意，还引了一句创院者的话。教授读了两遍，看上去几乎被感动了。|你写出了本世纪最好的道歉信。教授称之为“非常周全”，把它和别的道歉信归在一起，那些显然是一套收藏。|一篇悔过的杰作，而你写得比应该的更开心。}\n# en: {\"This is a performance,\" says the professor, and hands it back. \"Again. Sincerely, this time.\" You're there until the lamps go out.|The professor reads one line aloud, flatly, and the whole thing sounds as hollow as it is. You start again on a fresh sheet, until your hand cramps and the room goes cold.|\"Very pretty,\" says the professor. \"Now the real one.\" You write the real one, late, with cold feet.}\nopt.right.say.2 [dd203d] = {“这是表演，”教授说着把信递还给你，“重写。这回要真诚。”你一直写到熄灯。|教授平板地念出其中一行，整封信听上去和它本身一样空洞。你换了一张新纸重新开始，写到手抽筋、屋里发冷。|“很漂亮，”教授说，“现在写真的。”你写了真的那封，写得很晚，脚都冻僵了。}\n\n@event det_candle_wax\n# en: Wax on the Long Tables\ntitle [4e06cc] = 长桌上的蜡\n# en: {The floating candles over the Great Hall drip all through every meal, and between meals someone must scrape the long tables clean. Today it's you.|The long tables, the scraper, and a meal's worth of wax, with an extra puddle under the high table.|The kitchens have left you a better scraper, which is either a kindness or a hint.} {One candle hangs lower than the others, dripping with enthusiasm.|The house banners rustle above you.|The ceiling shows tomorrow's weather: {season == \"winter\": snow|rain}.}\ntext [42c0aa] = {大礼堂的浮空蜡烛每顿饭都滴个不停，两顿饭之间得有人把长桌刮干净。今天轮到你。|长桌、刮刀，还有一顿饭积下的蜡，高桌底下还多了一摊。|厨房给你留了一把更好的刮刀，不知是好意还是暗示。} {有一支蜡烛飘得比别的低，滴得格外起劲。|学院旗在你头顶沙沙作响。|天花板显示着明天的天气：{season == \"winter\": 下雪|下雨}。}\n# en: The great hall emptied after a meal, floating candles drifting overhead, a student scraping hardened wax off a long wooden table.\nscene [3b750d] = 饭后空荡荡的大礼堂，浮空蜡烛在头顶飘荡，一个学生正从一张长木桌上刮下凝固的蜡。\n# en: Scrape every table clean\nopt.left.label [37d01b] = 把每张桌子都刮干净\n# en: {Five long tables, one scraper, and a sore back. By the end the oak shines and you can see your tired face in it.|You scrape until your shoulders burn, and the tables come up like new. Somewhere above you a candle drips, in the middle of the first one.|You know which tables drip worst now, and start there. It still takes until your arms give out.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d7dbc8] = {五张长桌，一把刮刀，一副酸痛的腰背。最后橡木桌面锃亮，你能从里面看见自己疲惫的脸。|你刮到肩膀发烫，桌子焕然一新。头顶某处，一支蜡烛正好滴在第一张桌子正中间。|你现在知道哪几张桌子滴得最凶，就从那儿开始。还是一直刮到胳膊使不上劲为止。}\n# en: Coax the candles down to trim them\nopt.right.label [355a14] = 把蜡烛哄下来剪烛芯\n# en: {You call the candles down one by one and trim their wicks. The dripping stops. Tomorrow's breakfast will be the cleanest in a year.|The candles come down to your hand like tame birds. You trim them, and they bob back up looking smart.|Down they come, all forty. You trim them neatly, and one of them, you'd swear, bows.}\nopt.right.say.1 [847eee] = {你把蜡烛一支支唤下来，剪好烛芯。滴蜡停了。明天的早饭会是一年来最干净的一顿。|蜡烛像驯服的鸟一样落到你手上。你把它们修剪好，它们又精神抖擞地飘了回去。|四十支全下来了。你把它们剪得整整齐齐，有一支——你敢发誓——还鞠了个躬。}\n# en: {A candle comes down too fast and drips a perfect circle of hot wax onto your head. The prefect laughs for a full minute.|The candles scatter like startled pigeons and drip on everything, you included. You scrape for twice as long.|Three candles come down; one comes down on your hand. You finish with a scraper and a blister.}\nopt.right.say.2 [b73e83] = {一支蜡烛下来得太快，在你头顶滴了一个完美的热蜡圈。级长足足笑了一分钟。|蜡烛像受惊的鸽子一样四散，滴得到处都是，你也没能幸免。你多刮了一倍的时间。|下来了三支蜡烛，其中一支落在了你手上。你拿着刮刀、顶着一个水泡把活儿干完了。}\n\n@event det_great_lens\n# en: The Great Lens\ntitle [70cfab] = 大透镜\n# en: {Detention in the observatory. The great telescope's brass and its lens are to be polished by hand, with a chamois and a bucket of warm water that goes cold at once.|Someone has written a rude word in the dust on the great lens, backwards, so it reads right from the eyepiece.|The telescope has turned itself to face the door, as if it were expecting you.} {{slot == \"evening\": The dome is open, and the stars are coming out over your shoulder.|The dome is half open on a pale sky.}|The brass is green at the joints.|A moth is asleep on the eyepiece.}\ntext [3f2566] = {禁闭地点在观星台。那台大望远镜的黄铜和镜片要用麂皮手工擦亮，配一桶刚提来就凉透的温水。|有人在大透镜的灰上写了个脏字，还是反着写的，从目镜里看正好是正的。|望远镜自己转过来对着门，好像在等你。} {{slot == \"evening\": 穹顶开着，星星正从你肩后冒出来。|穹顶半开，外面是一片苍白的天。}|黄铜接口处长了绿锈。|一只飞蛾睡在目镜上。}\n# en: The inside of a brass observatory dome, a huge telescope, a student on a ladder rubbing its lens with a chamois cloth.\nscene [d86a94] = 黄铜观星穹顶内部，一台巨大的望远镜，一个学生站在梯子上用麂皮擦拭镜片。\n# en: Polish every inch of it\nopt.left.label [2820f3] = 每一寸都擦亮\n# en: {You polish the brass until it gleams and the lens until it disappears. Your fingers are numb. The keeper of the dome checks it and says nothing, which here is praise.|You learn the telescope's whole body by polishing it, every screw and gear. You also learn how cold a brass dome can get.|An hour of small circles. When you climb down, the lens is so clean the sky looks closer.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fcca94] = {你把黄铜擦得锃亮，把镜片擦得仿佛消失了。手指都冻麻了。穹顶的管理员检查了一遍，什么也没说，在这儿这就是夸奖。|你靠擦拭摸清了望远镜的整个身子，每颗螺丝、每个齿轮。你也领教了黄铜穹顶能冷到什么地步。|一个钟头的小圈圈。你爬下梯子时，镜片干净得让天空都近了些。}\n# en: Look through it instead\nopt.right.label [12e5ae] = 改为看一看\n# en: {{slot == \"evening\": You put your eye to the lens and the first stars come up out of the dark. The keeper finds you an hour later, the brass still dull.|It swings, as it likes, to the far shore and the mountains past it. The keeper finds you an hour later, the brass still dull.} You owe your revision an evening.|You look instead of polishing. The sky, close up, is worth an essay you won't now write.|You look, and the lens is so smeared it's like looking through honey, and still it's beautiful. The reading you'd planned waits for another night.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c09806] = {{slot == \"evening\": 你把眼睛凑到目镜上，第一批星星从黑暗中升起。一个钟头后管理员找到你，黄铜还是灰扑扑的。|它随心所欲地转向湖对岸和更远处的群山。一个钟头后管理员找到你，黄铜还是灰扑扑的。}你欠了复习一个晚上。|你没擦，光顾着看。凑近了看，天空值得一篇你如今写不成的论文。|你看了，镜片脏得像隔着蜂蜜，却依然很美。原本计划的阅读只好等下一个晚上了。}\n\n@event det_flight_divots\n# en: Filling the Divots\ntitle [32cb9c] = 填坑\n# en: {Detention on the Flight Field. Every bad landing of the week has left a divot, and every divot needs filling before the next practice.|The weekend's race has left a crater the exact shape of a broom-handle, and plenty of its friends.|Somebody from the race team has left a bag of turf by the fence, with a note: SORRY.} {One crater is large enough to sit in.|The brooms in the rack by the fence are unwatched.|{season == \"winter\": The turf is frozen into bricks.|The grass smells of rain.}}\ntext [826fa2] = {禁闭地点在飞行场。这一周每次糟糕的落地都留下了一个坑，每个坑都得在下次训练前填平。|周末的比赛留下了一个扫帚柄形状的坑，还有一大堆它的同伴。|竞速队有人在围栏边留了一袋草皮，附一张字条：对不起。} {有一个坑大得能坐进去。|围栏边架子上的扫帚没人看管。|{season == \"winter\": 草皮冻成了砖头。|草地上有雨水的气味。}}\n# en: A grassy flight field pocked with crater-like divots, a student with a spade and a barrow of turf, brooms racked by the fence.\nscene [e96fd1] = 一片满是坑洼的草地飞行场，一个学生拿着铁锹推着一车草皮，扫帚靠在围栏边的架子上。\n# en: Fill every last one\nopt.left.label [b9a9fd] = 一个不落地填平\n# en: {You dig and stamp and dig until the field is level and your blisters have blisters.|By the last divot you can lift a full barrow one-handed. You mean to be proud of that once your arms stop shaking.|You fill the field flat. The next practice lands on it and makes new divots before you've left the fence.}\nopt.left.say.1 [133be3] = {你挖了填，填了踩，一直干到场地平整，水泡上又起了水泡。|填到最后一个坑时，你能单手推起一满车。等胳膊不抖了，你打算为此骄傲一下。|你把整片场地填平了。下一场训练落地时又砸出了新坑，你还没走出围栏。}\n# en: Borrow a broom while nobody's looking\nopt.right.label [219125] = 趁没人看着借把扫帚\n# en: {One lap of the field, low and fast, and back on the ground before the prefect looks up. The divots can wait for the wind.|You take a broom from the rack and fly the length of the field and back. Nobody saw. It was the best part of the week.|A lap, then another. You land beside your spade and pick it up, breathless, as the prefect turns round.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f232b3] = {绕场一圈，又低又快，在级长抬头前落回了地面。坑可以等风来填。|你从架子上抽了把扫帚，沿着场地飞了个来回。没人看见。这是这周最棒的一刻。|一圈，又一圈。你降落在铁锹旁，喘着气把它捡起来，级长正好转过身来。}\n# en: {The prefect turns round just as you take off. The whole race team, arriving for practice, sees you marched back to your barrow.|You're spotted halfway down the field. The prefect makes you fill the divot you made landing.|You get as far as the fence. The prefect is waiting there with your spade, and the team cheers, sarcastically.}\nopt.right.say.2 [0ca64e] = {你刚起飞，级长就转过了身。前来训练的整个竞速队都看着你被押回手推车边。|你飞到半场就被发现了。级长让你把自己落地砸出的坑也填上。|你只飞到了围栏。级长拿着你的铁锹在那儿等着，竞速队阴阳怪气地欢呼起来。}\n\n@event det_inkwells\n# en: Three Hundred Inkwells\ntitle [a512dc] = 三百个墨水瓶\n# en: {Detention in the Lecture Tower. Every inkwell in every hall, three hundred of them, to be filled before morning from one heavy jug.|Somebody has been drinking from one of the inkwells, and carved a confession into the desk beside it.|Three hundred inkwells, and a new jug, heavier than the last one.} {The lecturer's own well at the front is empty.|Someone has left their spectacles on a desk.|A paper bird has been left on a desk.}\ntext [374f34] = {禁闭地点在讲堂塔。每间讲堂的每个墨水瓶，一共三百个，都得在早上之前用一只沉甸甸的大壶灌满。|有人从某个墨水瓶里喝过东西，还在旁边的课桌上刻了一段忏悔。|三百个墨水瓶，外加一只新壶，比上一只还沉。} {讲台上讲师自己的墨水瓶是空的。|有人把眼镜落在了课桌上。|课桌上留着一只纸鸟。}\n# en: A tiered lecture hall emptied for the night, rows of desks with brass inkwells, a student walking the rows with a big jug of ink.\nscene [1975db] = 夜里清空的阶梯讲堂，一排排课桌上摆着黄铜墨水瓶，一个学生提着一大壶墨水沿着过道走。\n# en: Fill them all, neatly\nopt.left.label [381266] = 全部仔细灌满\n# en: {Row by row, hall by hall, with the jug getting heavier. In the quiet you plan your next essay from first line to last.|Three hundred wells, not one spilled. Your arms ache, and somewhere around the hundredth well the problem you were stuck on came loose.|You fill the wells in the order you'd answer an exam, and mutter the answers as you go. The halls don't mind.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6b4f71] = {一排接一排，一间接一间，壶越来越沉。安静中你把下一篇论文从第一句到最后一句都想好了。|三百个墨水瓶，一滴没洒。你胳膊酸了，而在第一百个瓶子前后，你卡了很久的那道题忽然想通了。|你按答考卷的顺序灌墨水，边灌边念叨答案。讲堂们并不介意。}\n# en: Doctor the lecturer's well\nopt.right.label [84cceb] = 在讲师的墨水瓶里做手脚\n# en: {A drop of something that turns blue in the light, in the lecturer's well only. By noon tomorrow the whole year knows why the lecturer's hands are blue.|You add a pinch of sneezing powder to the front well. The next morning's lecture is punctuated, and nobody can prove a thing.|A drop of colour-change ink in the lecturer's well. The notes come out a shy pink. The class will talk of nothing else for a week.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7696d5] = {只在讲师的墨水瓶里滴了一滴遇光变蓝的东西。到明天中午，全年级都知道讲师的手为什么是蓝的了。|你往讲台的墨水瓶里撒了一撮喷嚏粉。第二天早上的课被喷嚏打断了好几回，谁也证明不了什么。|讲师的墨水瓶里多了一滴变色墨水。笔记写出来是羞答答的粉红色。全班会议论一个星期。}\n# en: {The prefect sees the little bottle in your hand. You refill all three hundred wells again, from fresh ink, until midnight.|The lecturer finds the blue ink before the lecture, and you in front of it. You stay until every well is clean and refilled.|Caught with the bottle. You empty and refill every well in the hall while the prefect reads aloud from the rules.}\nopt.right.say.2 [738ad9] = {级长看见了你手里的小瓶子。你用新墨水把三百个墨水瓶全部重新灌了一遍，一直干到午夜。|讲师在上课前发现了蓝墨水，也发现了站在它跟前的你。你留到每个墨水瓶都洗净重灌为止。|你被抓了个正着。级长大声念着校规，你把整间讲堂的墨水瓶倒空又灌满。}\n\n@event det_gargoyle_scrub\n# en: The Gargoyles' Bath\ntitle [4d9e77] = 石像鬼洗澡\n# en: {Detention on the north parapet. The gargoyles, a bucket and a brush, and a year of pigeons to scrub off them. The third one along has a draught behind it.|A pigeon has moved into the mouth of the cleanest gargoyle on the parapet, the third one along.|The wind is up, and the third gargoyle along is humming in it, very faintly.} {The wind tries to take the bucket.|The lake below is very still.|{season == \"winter\": The water in the bucket keeps freezing.|A pigeon watches you clean its work, offended.}}\ntext [7120ed] = {禁闭地点在北面的城垛上。一排石像鬼，一只桶，一把刷子，还有积了一年的鸽子粪要刷掉。第三座后面有一股穿堂风。|一只鸽子住进了城垛上最干净的那座石像鬼嘴里，就是第三座。|起风了，第三座石像鬼在风里发出极轻的嗡鸣。} {风想把桶刮走。|下面的湖面一动不动。|{season == \"winter\": 桶里的水一直在结冰。|一只鸽子看着你清理它的杰作，很是不满。}}\n# en: A windy castle parapet lined with stone gargoyles, a student with a bucket and a stiff brush scrubbing pigeon mess off a grinning stone face, the lake far below.\nscene [697e46] = 刮着风的城堡城垛上排着石像鬼，一个学生提着桶、拿着硬毛刷，正刷掉一张咧嘴笑的石脸上的鸽子粪，远处下方是湖。\n# en: Scrub them till they grin\nopt.left.label [360135] = 刷到它们咧嘴笑\n# en: {You scrub until every face along the parapet is clean and grinning. Your knuckles are raw and you smell of pigeon.|A whole row of clean gargoyles. The pigeons are already lining up to undo it.|You scrub hard and fast in the wind. By the end you're freezing and the gargoyles look delighted.}\nopt.left.say.1 [47f0c4] = {你一直刷到城垛上每张脸都干干净净、咧着嘴笑。指节磨破了，身上一股鸽子味。|一整排干干净净的石像鬼。鸽子们已经排好队准备重新弄脏它们了。|你在风里又快又狠地刷。刷完时你冻僵了，石像鬼们看上去乐坏了。}\n# en: Linger at the third gargoyle\nopt.right.label [e30b83] = 在第三座石像鬼前逗留\n# en: {member(cartographers): {You check the chalk mark behind the third gargoyle is still fresh while you scrub, and smudge it a little, so it looks old.|You give the third gargoyle a very thorough wash, and the passage behind it a very thorough look. All clear.|You scrub the third gargoyle last and slowest, and leave it shining as if it had nothing to hide.}|{You scrub around the draught slowly, and it comes from no wall. You feel for the edge of something.|The stone behind the third one is warmer than it should be. You don't find a door. You're nearly sure there is one.|You spend so long on the third gargoyle that the prefect asks if you're in love with it. You learn nothing, and suspect a great deal.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [55d2ae] = {member(cartographers): {你一边刷，一边检查第三座石像鬼后面的粉笔记号是否还新鲜，又把它抹淡了些，好让它看上去像旧的。|你把第三座石像鬼洗得非常彻底，也把它后面的通道看得非常彻底。一切正常。|你最后才刷第三座，刷得最慢，把它擦得锃亮，仿佛它什么也没藏。}|{你慢慢绕着那股穿堂风刷，风却不是从哪面墙来的。你摸索着某样东西的边缘。|它后面的石头比该有的温度暖。你没找到门。你几乎可以肯定那儿有一扇。|你在第三座石像鬼上花了太久，级长问你是不是爱上它了。你什么也没查到，却怀疑了很多。}}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_classes.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_classes.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event core_flying_lesson\n# en: Compulsory Broom Flight\ntitle [014809] = 必修飞帚课\n# en: Compulsory first-year broom flight. The instructor blows her whistle. Your broom rises, hesitates, and then — clearly having taken a dislike to you — begins drifting toward the lake.\ntext [d69dcf] = 一年级必修的飞帚课。教练吹响了哨子。你的扫帚升起来，犹豫了一下，然后——显然是不太喜欢你——开始往湖那边飘。\n# en: A row of nervous first-years standing beside brooms on the lawn, one broom already rising on its own, an instructor with a whistle.\nscene [40aef9] = 草坪上一排紧张的一年级生站在扫帚旁，其中一把已经自己飞了起来，教练手里拿着哨子。\n# en: Hold on and steer it back\nopt.left.label [231b0d] = 抓紧，把它拽回来\n# en: You wrestle it round and land, wobbling, exactly on the mark. The instructor nods. The broom grudgingly accepts you.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f0eb8] = 你硬是把它扳了回来，摇摇晃晃地正好落在标记上。教练点了点头。扫帚勉强认了你。\n# en: The broom wins. You land in the shallows. Everyone sees. Everyone remembers.\nopt.left.say.2 [ccfc57] = 扫帚赢了。你落进了浅水里。所有人都看见了。所有人都记住了。\n# en: Jump off while it's still low\nopt.right.label [c7b2ec] = 趁它还飞得低跳下来\n# en: You drop four feet onto the grass. Undignified, but dry. The broom carries on to the lake alone and sinks with a sulky gurgle.\nopt.right.say.1 [ce5e6e] = 你从四英尺高的地方跳到草地上。不太体面，但没湿。扫帚独自飞到湖上，咕嘟一声闷闷不乐地沉了下去。\n\n@event pyro_dragon_breath\n# en: A Sample of Dragonfire\ntitle [8d15de] = 一缕龙焰样本\n# en: Thorne holds up a sealed jar containing one flame of genuine dragonfire, blue-white and furious. \"Who would like to try holding it?\" he asks. Nobody moves. He looks at you.\ntext [d3969c] = 索恩举起一个密封的罐子，里面是一团货真价实的龙焰，蓝白色，怒气冲冲。“谁想试着捧一下？”他问。没人动。他看向了你。\n# en: A glass jar containing a single writhing flame of deep blue, held in tongs by a stern professor, students leaning back from it.\nscene [5714c4] = 一个玻璃罐里扭动着一团深蓝色的火焰，被一位严厉的教授用钳子夹着，学生们纷纷往后仰。\n# en: Hold it\nopt.left.label [bf9ba5] = 捧住它\n# en: It sears and struggles and then, astonishingly, settles in your palm like a small warm animal. The class stares. Thorne almost smiles.\nopt.left.say.1 [1f13d1] = 它灼烧着、挣扎着，然后——令人震惊地——像一只温暖的小动物一样在你掌心安顿下来。全班都看呆了。索恩差点笑了。\n# en: The dragonfire disagrees. You spend a week with bandaged hands and a new respect for dragons.\nopt.left.say.2 [77f691] = 龙焰不同意。你缠着绷带过了一个星期，对龙有了全新的敬意。\n# en: Watch someone else\nopt.right.label [1e1e35] = 看别人来\n# en: A fourth-year volunteers and loses a sleeve. You take careful notes on what not to do.\nopt.right.say.1 [4fe5a6] = 一个四年级生自告奋勇，结果烧掉了一只袖子。你仔细记下了哪些事不该做。\n\n@event tide_water_spirit_race\n# en: The Water Spirit Race\ntitle [da1049] = 水灵竞速\n# en: Professor Marisol's final challenge: swim the lake while the water spirits race you. \"They cheat,\" she says cheerfully. \"Ask them nicely not to.\"\ntext [2365f2] = 玛丽索尔教授的期末挑战：游过湖去，同时有水灵和你比赛。“它们会作弊，”她兴高采烈地说，“好好请它们别作弊。”\n# en: Students swimming across a lake at dawn, translucent water-spirits racing alongside them like silver dolphins.\nscene [561442] = 黎明时分，学生们游过湖面，半透明的水灵像银色海豚一样在他们身旁竞速。\n# en: Race them flat out\nopt.left.label [2cf84d] = 全力和它们比\n# en: You touch the far shore a hand's length ahead of the fastest spirit. It sulks, then laughs like a fountain.\nopt.left.say.1 [5f4a18] = 你比最快的那个水灵早一掌的距离摸到对岸。它生了会儿闷气，然后像喷泉一样笑了起来。\n# en: The spirits tow you in circles. You arrive last, but very clean.\nopt.left.say.2 [320621] = 水灵拖着你转圈圈。你最后一个到，不过洗得非常干净。\n# en: Ask them nicely\nopt.right.label [a2f34a] = 好好请求它们\n# en: You ask. They carry you across on their backs like a raft. Marisol says that counts. Marisol says it's the whole point.\nopt.right.say.1 [c7d0ee] = 你开口请求。它们像木筏一样把你驮了过去。玛丽索尔说这算数。玛丽索尔说这才是重点。\n# en: They pretend not to hear. Water spirits are terrible at manners.\nopt.right.say.2 [eac302] = 它们假装没听见。水灵最不讲礼貌了。\n\n@event illusion_mirror_maze\n# en: The Mirror Maze Exam\ntitle [55c841] = 镜之迷宫考试\n# en: Professor Vey's exam: a maze of enchanted mirrors. Find the exit. Some reflections lie. One of them is — she promises — the real you.\ntext [0aec9f] = 维伊教授的考试：一座魔法镜子组成的迷宫。找到出口。有些倒影会说谎。其中一个——她保证——是真正的你。\n# en: A maze of tall mirrors in a dark hall, each reflecting a different version of the same student, one reflection grinning.\nscene [b9068c] = 黑暗大厅里的高镜迷宫，每面镜子都映出同一个学生的不同模样，其中一个倒影在咧嘴笑。\n# en: Trust your own eyes\nopt.left.label [ccacee] = 相信自己的眼睛\n# en: You spot the reflection that blinks late, and follow it out. Vey awards top marks and says, \"Most people trust the one that looks happiest.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [f97c79] = 你发现了那个眨眼慢半拍的倒影，跟着它走了出去。维伊给了你最高分，说：“大多数人都相信看起来最开心的那个。”\n# en: You follow the happiest reflection for an hour. It leads you in a circle and waves goodbye.\nopt.left.say.2 [21a1ac] = 你跟着最开心的那个倒影走了一个钟头。它领你绕了一圈，然后挥手道别。\n# en: Shatter the illusion\nopt.right.label [aa2560] = 击碎幻象\n# en: Every mirror goes dark at once. Exit found. Vey writes, \"Effective. Inelegant. Pass.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [137f22] = 所有镜子同时暗了下去。出口找到了。维伊写道：“有效。不优雅。及格。”\n# en: You shatter one mirror. It was real. Seven years of bad luck, the caretaker says, or three crowns for the glass.\nopt.right.say.2 [a46c96] = 你击碎了一面镜子。它是真的。管理员说，要么倒霉七年，要么赔三克朗玻璃钱。\n\n@event alch_love_potion\n# en: The Love Potion Assignment\ntitle [d13e1c] = 爱情药水作业\n# en: This week's assignment: brew a love potion — \"for analytical purposes only\". Your flask glows a suspicious pink. Someone at the next bench asks if they can buy it. Several people ask.\ntext [3c39e1] = 本周作业：调制一剂爱情药水——“仅供分析之用”。你的烧瓶泛着可疑的粉光。隔壁桌有人问能不能买下来。好几个人都问了。\n# en: A pink, glittering potion in a heart-shaped flask on a workbench, a student holding it up to the light with a doubtful expression.\nscene [1a4005] = 工作台上一个心形烧瓶里装着闪亮的粉色药水，一名学生把它举到光下，满脸怀疑。\n# en: Brew it perfectly, then pour it away\nopt.left.label [8fa0a2] = 完美调好，然后倒掉\n# en: Perfect colour, perfect scent. You pour it down the sink. The pipes are affectionate for a week.\nopt.left.say.1 [d421ab] = 完美的颜色，完美的香气。你把它倒进了水槽。水管一整个星期都含情脉脉的。\n# en: Sell it\nopt.right.label [4ac442] = 卖掉它\n# en: A prefect falls in love with the headmistress's portrait. It's traced back to you. Worth it, almost.\nopt.right.say.1 [1f1132] = 一位级长爱上了院长的画像。追查下来找到了你。差不多值了。\n# en: Fifteen crowns. You never ask what it was used for. You see a lot of unusual couples at dinner.\nopt.right.say.2 [9fdb68] = 十五克朗。你从没问过它被用来干什么。你在晚餐时看到了很多不寻常的情侣。\n\n@event div_prophecy_exam\n# en: The Prophecy Exam\ntitle [fa5039] = 预言考试\n# en: The mock Divination exam: gaze into the crystal and make one prophecy about the coming month. The examiner will check. \"Vague prophecies,\" she warns, \"fail.\"\ntext [a8f59f] = 占卜学模拟考：凝视水晶球，对下个月做出一条预言。考官会核实。“含糊的预言，”她警告道，“不及格。”\n# en: A dim divination classroom, a single student staring into a crystal ball, the examiner's quill hovering expectantly over a blank page.\nscene [91fdd6] = 昏暗的占卜教室，一名学生独自凝视着水晶球，考官的羽毛笔悬在空白的纸上，满怀期待。\n# en: Make a bold, specific prophecy\nopt.left.label [d1a2b7] = 做一个大胆而具体的预言\n# en: \"On the eighth, the kitchen cat will have kittens in the headmistress's hat.\" On the eighth, she does. Top marks in the mock.\nopt.left.say.1 [8b2bab] = “八号那天，厨房的猫会在院长的帽子里生小猫。”八号那天，它真的生了。模拟考满分。\n# en: \"On the eighth, it will snow indoors.\" It does not snow indoors. The examiner writes \"ambitious\".\nopt.left.say.2 [fc7167] = “八号那天，室内会下雪。”室内并没有下雪。考官写下“有野心”。\n# en: Make a careful, safe prophecy\nopt.right.label [e30df1] = 做一个谨慎稳妥的预言\n# en: \"Someone will be late to breakfast.\" Someone is. The examiner sighs and gives you a bare pass: it was vague, but it was checkable.\nopt.right.say.1 [ab1063] = “有人早饭会迟到。”确实有人迟到了。考官叹了口气，给你打了个勉强及格：说得含糊，但好歹能核实。\n# en: Foretell it, out loud, so it lands\nopt.up.label [1036de] = 大声预言，让它应验\n# en: You say it plainly, in that flat certain voice: the examiner's kettle will boil dry on Friday. It does. She gives you full marks and looks at you as if you'd done it on purpose. You aren't sure you didn't.\nopt.up.say.1 [6e7716] = 你用那种平板而笃定的声音直说：考官的水壶周五会烧干。它烧干了。她给了你满分，看你的眼神好像你是故意的。你自己也说不准是不是。\n\n@event art_golem_helper\n# en: The Little Golem\ntitle [6f3f24] = 小魔像\n# en: Your term project: a knee-high clay golem that carries books. It works! It works so well that it has started carrying everyone's books, whether they want it to or not.\ntext [677080] = 你的学期作业：一个及膝高的黏土魔像，专门搬书。它成功了！成功得过了头，开始搬所有人的书，不管人家愿不愿意。\n# en: A knee-high clay golem carrying a teetering stack of books across a classroom, a student watching anxiously as it nears a staircase.\nscene [4ac493] = 一个及膝高的黏土魔像抱着一摞摇摇欲坠的书穿过教室，一名学生紧张地看着它走近楼梯。\n# en: Add a \"stop\" command\nopt.left.label [3c6ffc] = 加一个“停下”指令\n# en: The golem stops. It also waits politely. The professor asks if she can borrow it for her office.\nopt.left.say.1 [6ad2e4] = 魔像停下了，还会礼貌地等待。教授问能不能借去她的办公室用。\n# en: Now it stops randomly, mid-staircase, holding forty books. Nobody can get past it. It looks very pleased.\nopt.left.say.2 [d28e81] = 现在它会随机停下——在楼梯中间，抱着四十本书。谁也过不去。它看起来非常得意。\n# en: Let it roam free\nopt.right.label [d4c542] = 让它自由游荡\n# en: The golem becomes a beloved castle fixture, delivering books to whoever looks sad. It signs every delivery with your initials, in clay.\nopt.right.say.1 [4659c3] = 魔像成了城堡里人见人爱的一景，专给看起来难过的人送书。每次送书，它都会用黏土签上你名字的缩写。\n# en: It also delivers a Restricted Section book to a first-year. The librarian follows the clay smudges straight to your door.\nopt.right.say.2 [94ea9a] = 它还把一本禁书区的书送到了一个一年级生手上。图书管理员顺着泥印一路找到了你的门口。\n\n@event beast_hippocampus\n# en: The Hippocampus Foal\ntitle [21cc8d] = 小海马驹\n# en: A hippocampus foal — half pony, half fish — has been stranded in a shrinking rock pool on the lakeshore. Professor Brask is on the other side of the grounds. The foal is panicking.\ntext [4890ea] = 一只海马驹——半是小马，半是鱼——被困在湖边一个不断缩小的石潭里。布拉斯克教授在校园另一头。小马驹惊慌失措。\n# en: A tiny sea-horse foal with a fish's tail struggling in a rock pool on the lake shore, a student kneeling beside the water.\nscene [205918] = 湖边石潭里，一只长着鱼尾的小海马驹在挣扎，一名学生跪在水边。\n# en: Carry it to the lake\nopt.left.label [d9060c] = 把它抱回湖里\n# en: It thrashes, then trusts you. You wade out and let it go. It circles you three times before diving deep.\nopt.left.say.1 [4d71d5] = 它扑腾了一阵，然后信任了你。你蹚进水里放开它。它绕着你转了三圈，然后潜入深处。\n# en: It kicks you in the stomach with surprising force — but you get it to the water. Brask says that's how they say thank you.\nopt.left.say.2 [7f1482] = 它力气大得惊人地踢中了你的肚子——但你还是把它送回了水里。布拉斯克说，它们就是这样道谢的。\n# en: Run for Professor Brask\nopt.right.label [cb87c9] = 跑去找布拉斯克教授\n# en: Brask arrives with a bucket and a lullaby. She makes you hold the bucket. You learn more in ten minutes than in a whole term.\nopt.right.say.1 [07b643] = 布拉斯克带着一只水桶和一首摇篮曲赶来了。她让你拎着水桶。你在十分钟里学到的比一整个学期还多。\n\n@event astro_eclipse\n# en: The Eclipse\ntitle [deb525] = 日食\n# en: An eclipse, the first in forty years. The astromancy class is on the roof with smoked-glass lenses. At the moment of totality, you notice something the charts don't show: a second shadow, smaller, moving the wrong way.\ntext [1ca60e] = 一场日食，四十年来的第一次。星象学课的学生们拿着烟熏玻璃镜片站在屋顶上。全食的那一刻，你注意到了星图上没有的东西：第二道阴影，更小，朝着相反的方向移动。\n# en: A black disc sliding across the sun above the observatory dome, students on the roof with smoked-glass lenses, a halo of light around the darkness.\nscene [5b724a] = 天文台穹顶上方，一个黑色圆盘滑过太阳，屋顶上的学生们举着烟熏玻璃镜片，黑暗周围一圈光晕。\n# en: Record it precisely\nopt.left.label [ee9627] = 精确记录下来\n# en: You chart the second shadow's path exactly. It crosses the sky and stops — directly above the lake.\nopt.left.say.1 [07b204] = 你精确地画出了第二道阴影的轨迹。它划过天空，停了下来——正好停在湖的上方。\n# en: Just watch the eclipse\nopt.right.label [b5b48a] = 只是看日食\n# en: For four minutes the world holds its breath. You'll remember the cold, and the silence, and the strange birdsong, for the rest of your life.\nopt.right.say.1 [3ab28b] = 有四分钟，整个世界屏住了呼吸。你这辈子都会记得那份寒冷、那份寂静，还有那奇怪的鸟鸣。\n\n@event heal_triage_drill\n# en: Triage\ntitle [090621] = 伤员分诊\n# en: Sister Amaranth's triage drill: twelve \"injured\" students, one healer — you — and ten minutes. Some are faking worse than others. One, you realise, is genuinely hurt.\ntext [c92712] = 阿玛兰斯修女的分诊演练：十二个“受伤”的学生，一个治疗师——就是你——还有十分钟。有些人装得比别人更夸张。而有一个，你意识到，是真的受伤了。\n# en: A practice ward full of students acting injured, groaning theatrically on cots, a single student healer in the middle with a clipboard.\nscene [5bb9f0] = 一间练习病房里满是假装受伤的学生，在病床上夸张地呻吟，一名学生治疗师拿着记录板站在中间。\n# en: Treat the genuine injury first\nopt.left.label [ab25ba] = 先处理真正的伤\n# en: You spot the real injury in the first minute. Amaranth nods, once, which is the highest honour in the infirmary.\nopt.left.say.1 [ac5815] = 你在第一分钟就发现了真正的伤员。阿玛兰斯点了一下头——这是医务室里的最高荣誉。\n# en: You find the real injury in minute nine. Late, but not too late. Amaranth's lips thin. \"Again tomorrow.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [da92d2] = 你在第九分钟才找到真正的伤员。晚了，但还不算太晚。阿玛兰斯抿紧了嘴唇。“明天再来。”\n# en: Work systematically down the line\nopt.right.label [7c7311] = 按顺序一个个处理\n# en: Methodical, thorough and slow. The real patient waits seven minutes. Amaranth makes you write an essay on why.\nopt.right.say.1 [503f17] = 有条不紊、细致而缓慢。真正的病人等了七分钟。阿玛兰斯让你写一篇文章解释为什么。\n\n@event umbral_last_lecture\n# en: The Last Lecture on Absence\ntitle [9b0f64] = 关于虚无的最后一课\n# en: The Umbral professor draws a star with a hollow centre on the board. \"This is not on the syllabus,\" she says. \"Three hundred years ago this academy imprisoned an absence that thinks. I believe you should know that before you graduate.\"\ntext [3f5b86] = 暗影理论教授在黑板上画了一颗中心空洞的星星。“这不在教学大纲里，”她说，“三百年前，这所学院囚禁了一个会思考的虚无。我认为你们在毕业前应该知道这件事。”\n# en: An Umbral Theory lecture hall lit only by a black candle, the professor drawing a star with a hollow centre on the board.\nscene [59edcc] = 暗影理论的讲堂只点着一支黑色蜡烛，教授在黑板上画着一颗中心空洞的星星。\n# en: Take careful notes\nopt.left.label [4d95af] = 认真记笔记\n# en: She speaks for an hour about seals and locks and the founders' fear. At the end she wipes the board clean and says, \"This lecture never happened.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [4517c0] = 她讲了一个钟头，关于封印、锁和创始者的恐惧。最后她把黑板擦干净，说：“这堂课从没发生过。”\n# en: Ask why she's telling you\nopt.right.label [16a97a] = 问她为什么告诉你们\n# en: \"Because something is stirring,\" she says, \"and the headmistress asked me to find out which of you would ask that question.\" She writes your name down.\nopt.right.say.1 [9e5f65] = “因为有什么东西正在苏醒，”她说，“院长让我看看你们当中谁会问这个问题。”她写下了你的名字。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_faculty.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_faculty.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event thorne_old_rival\n# en: An Old Rival\ntitle [357013] = 宿敌老友\n# en: A flamboyant old wizard in purple has arrived to settle \"a forty-year-old matter\" with Professor Thorne. A formal duel needs a second. Thorne looks around the courtyard and, to everyone's astonishment, points at you.\ntext [067b32] = 一位身着紫袍、浮夸的老巫师来了，要和索恩教授了结“一桩四十年前的旧事”。正式决斗需要一名副手。索恩环顾庭院，然后——令所有人震惊地——指向了你。\n# en: Two elderly mages facing each other across a dueling ring at dusk, one stern and grey, one flamboyant in purple, a nervous student holding a towel at the side.\nscene [40cb18] = 黄昏，两位年迈的法师在决斗场两端对峙，一位严肃灰白，一位紫袍浮夸，一名紧张的学生拿着毛巾站在一旁。\n# en: Stand as his second\nopt.left.label [e6354b] = 当他的副手\n# en: The duel lasts four seconds, the longest of your life. Thorne wins. Then both old men sit on the grass and laugh until they wheeze. You've never seen him laugh. Neither has anyone else.\nopt.left.say.1 [8b9a7a] = 决斗只持续了四秒，却是你一生中最漫长的四秒。索恩赢了。然后两个老头坐在草地上，一直笑到喘不过气。你从没见过他笑。别人也没见过。\n# en: Politely decline\nopt.right.label [8a601d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: \"Hm,\" says Thorne, and asks the caretaker instead. He wins anyway. He does not mention it again, which is somehow worse.\nopt.right.say.1 [ddf880] = “嗯，”索恩说，转而请了管理员。他照样赢了。他再也没提起这件事，这不知怎么反而更糟。\n\n@event thorne_roses\n# en: The Professor's Roses\ntitle [ce1627] = 教授的玫瑰\n# en: At dawn, behind the observatory, you find Professor Thorne on his knees in a tiny walled rose garden, murmuring encouragement to a sickly bush. He sees you. Neither of you breathes.\ntext [163ce7] = 黎明时分，在天文台后面，你发现索恩教授跪在一个围墙环绕的小玫瑰园里，低声鼓励着一株病恹恹的玫瑰。他看见了你。你们俩都屏住了呼吸。\n# en: A severe old professor kneeling in a hidden rose garden at dawn, sleeves rolled up, murmuring encouragement to a single struggling rosebush.\nscene [0c6758] = 黎明，一位严厉的老教授跪在隐秘的玫瑰园里，袖子卷起，低声鼓励着一株挣扎求生的玫瑰。\n# en: Back away and never speak of it\nopt.left.label [225e23] = 悄悄退开，永不提起\n# en: You leave. The next day in class he calls on you for the hardest question — and then, very quietly, gives you full marks for a so-so answer.\nopt.left.say.1 [f662e1] = 你离开了。第二天上课时，他点你回答最难的问题——然后，非常小声地，给你那个马马虎虎的答案打了满分。\n# en: Offer to help with the sick bush\nopt.right.label [aa5c3c] = 主动帮忙救那株玫瑰\n# en: You know the blight; Maud taught you. Within a month the bush is covered in red roses. Thorne leaves one, without comment, on your desk.\nopt.right.say.1 [4837e1] = 你认得这种枯萎病，是莫德教你的。一个月内，那株玫瑰开满了红花。索恩在你桌上留下一朵，什么也没说。\n# en: Your remedy does nothing. But Thorne lets you kneel beside him for an hour, saying nothing, which you suspect is a great honour.\nopt.right.say.2 [629b27] = 你的药方毫无效果。但索恩让你在他身边跪了一个钟头，一言不发——你猜这是莫大的荣幸。\n\n@event thorne_shaking_hands\n# en: Thorne's Hands\ntitle [aad4f1] = 索恩的手\n# en: After class you see Thorne's wand slip from fingers that won't stop trembling. He picks it up, slowly. \"Age,\" he says, not looking at you. \"It comes for every storm eventually. You'll say nothing.\"\ntext [b146e2] = 下课后，你看见索恩的魔杖从一双抖个不停的手指间滑落。他慢慢地把它捡起来。“年纪，”他说，没有看你，“每一场风暴终有平息之时。你什么也不会说。”\n# en: An old professor gripping the edge of a lectern with both hands, a dropped wand on the floor, a single student in the doorway.\nscene [d913fc] = 一位老教授双手紧抓讲台边缘，一根魔杖掉在地上，门口站着一名学生。\n# en: Say nothing, as he asks\nopt.left.label [08876a] = 照他说的，什么也不说\n# en: You never mention it. But you start arriving early to set up his demonstrations. He notices. He lets you.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d89d2] = 你从没提起这件事。但你开始提早到教室帮他布置演示。他注意到了。他默许了。\n# en: Tell Sister Amaranth\nopt.right.label [3f4d80] = 告诉阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth's tonic steadies his hands. Thorne is furious with you for a week. Then he stops being furious, and starts, grudgingly, teaching you his hardest spells — \"before I can't.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [f813ac] = 阿玛兰斯的药剂让他的手稳了下来。索恩对你大发雷霆了一个星期。然后他不再生气，开始勉为其难地教你他最难的咒语——“趁我还能教。”\n\n@event maud_goat\n# en: Bartholomew in the Library\ntitle [07ab64] = 图书馆里的巴塞洛缪\n# en: Maud's goat, Bartholomew, has got into the library and is eating the Restricted Section. The librarian is apoplectic. Maud, hurrying in, grabs your arm. \"He likes you. Get him out before he eats anything with teeth.\"\ntext [ff1025] = 莫德的山羊巴塞洛缪闯进了图书馆，正在吃禁书区。图书管理员气得快中风了。莫德匆匆赶来，一把抓住你的胳膊。“它喜欢你。在它吃下什么长牙的书之前，把它弄出去。”\n# en: A shaggy goat standing on a library table chewing a book, an apoplectic librarian below, an old woman in a straw hat hurrying through the stacks.\nscene [579e73] = 一只毛蓬蓬的山羊站在图书馆的桌子上嚼书，下面是气急败坏的图书管理员，一位戴草帽的老妇人匆匆穿过书架。\n# en: Lure him out with an apple\nopt.left.label [89957a] = 用苹果把它引出来\n# en: Bartholomew follows the apple, and you, out into the sunlight. He has eaten one-third of \"Curses of the Northern Wastes\". He seems fine. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [2ec444] = 巴塞洛缪跟着苹果——也跟着你——走进了阳光里。它吃掉了三分之一本《北方荒原诅咒大全》。它看起来没事。大体上没事。\n# en: Bartholomew eats the apple, then your sleeve, then headbutts you into a shelf. Maud gets him out in the end, laughing.\nopt.left.say.2 [cef45a] = 巴塞洛缪吃了苹果，然后吃了你的袖子，然后一头把你顶进了书架。最后是莫德笑着把它弄出去的。\n# en: Pick him up and carry him out\nopt.right.label [5273c3] = 把它抱起来扛出去\n# en: He is heavier than he looks and struggles like a sack of angry cats. You carry him all the way to the paddock. The librarian applauds.\nopt.right.say.1 [d608cc] = 它比看上去重，挣扎起来像一麻袋愤怒的猫。你一路把它扛到了围场。图书管理员鼓起了掌。\n\n@event maud_recipe\n# en: Maud's Soup\ntitle [913763] = 莫德的汤\n# en: \"I'm going to teach you my soup,\" says Maud, rolling up her sleeves. \"Cures colds, broken hearts and most curses. Don't write it down. You'll remember it if you need it.\"\ntext [dcac02] = “我要教你我的汤，”莫德卷起袖子说，“治感冒、治心碎、治大多数诅咒。别写下来。需要的时候你自然会记得。”\n# en: A small cottage kitchen, a black iron pot over the fire, an old woman guiding a student's hand as they stir.\nscene [a5474e] = 一间小小的农舍厨房，炉火上架着一口黑铁锅，一位老妇人握着学生的手教他搅拌。\n# en: Learn it properly\nopt.left.label [9c42bb] = 认真学会它\n# en: It takes all afternoon. Some of the ingredients are herbs; some, as far as you can tell, are just stories. You remember every step.\nopt.left.say.1 [d6eaaf] = 花了一整个下午。有些材料是草药；有些，在你看来，只是故事。你记住了每一步。\n# en: Mostly eat it\nopt.right.label [390278] = 主要负责吃\n# en: You learn about half of it and eat three bowls. Maud declares that the most important part of the recipe anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [51d561] = 你学会了大约一半，喝了三碗。莫德宣布，这反正是这道菜谱里最重要的部分。\n\n@event maud_old_wand\n# en: A Wand From Sixty Years Ago\ntitle [017dae] = 六十年前的魔杖\n# en: Maud unwraps something from a faded scarf: a slender silver wand. \"Edmund's,\" she says. \"He was the one who didn't come back. He'd have wanted it to go beneath the lake again, with someone who meant to close the door.\"\ntext [8bc08b] = 莫德从一条褪色的围巾里解开一样东西：一根纤细的银魔杖。“埃德蒙的，”她说，“他就是那个没回来的人。他会希望它再到湖底去一次，跟着一个真心想关上那扇门的人。”\n# en: An old woman unwrapping a slender silver wand from a faded silk scarf, a photograph of three young students on the table beside it.\nscene [b81801] = 一位老妇人从一条褪色的丝巾里解出一根纤细的银魔杖，旁边桌上放着一张三个年轻学生的照片。\n# en: Accept it\nopt.left.label [5003f6] = 接受它\n# en: It fits your hand like it was waiting. When you hold it near the lake, it hums the same note as the drowned bell.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe42ab] = 它在你手里服帖得好像一直在等你。你把它举到湖边时，它和那口沉没的钟发出同一个音。\n# en: Tell her to keep it\nopt.right.label [680674] = 请她留着\n# en: \"Keep it for him,\" you say. She holds it for a long time. \"Sixty years,\" she says, \"and nobody ever said that.\" She makes tea, and you sit with her until dark.\nopt.right.say.1 [da80fd] = “替他留着吧，”你说。她握着它很久很久。“六十年了，”她说，“从来没有人这么说过。”她泡了茶，你陪她坐到天黑。\n\n@event isolde_chess\n# en: A Game of Tower Chess\ntitle [b9eeea] = 一局高塔棋\n# en: The headmistress keeps a board of crystal towers by her window. \"Do you play?\" she asks. It is clearly not a question. The pieces are already moving into position by themselves.\ntext [c9c818] = 院长在窗边放着一副水晶塔棋盘。“你下棋吗？”她问。这显然不是一个问题。棋子已经自己走到了位置上。\n# en: A chessboard of carved crystal towers on a small table by a tower window, two players, one candle, the lake silver below.\nscene [8c661f] = 塔楼窗边的小桌上放着一副雕刻水晶塔的棋盘，两位棋手，一支蜡烛，窗下的湖泛着银光。\n# en: Play to win\nopt.left.label [6c2256] = 为赢而下\n# en: You win. She looks at the board for a long moment, then laughs — a short, delighted sound. \"Again,\" she says. \"Thursday.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [ad5261] = 你赢了。她盯着棋盘看了很久，然后笑了——短促而开心的一声。“再来，”她说，“星期四。”\n# en: She wins in nine moves and spends the next hour showing you exactly why. It is the most useful lesson you've had all year.\nopt.left.say.2 [a26db6] = 她九步就赢了，然后花了一个钟头告诉你究竟为什么。这是你一整年里最有用的一课。\n# en: Between games you mention, very innocently, the observatory. She looks at you over the towers. \"Twice,\" she says. \"And I would do it again.\"\nopt.left.say.3 [093dc7] = 两局之间，你装作若无其事地提起观星台。她隔着那些塔看着你。“两次，”她说，“而且我还会再来一次。”\n# en: Play to learn how she thinks\nopt.right.label [51a7ef] = 为了解她的想法而下\n# en: You lose, slowly, watching. She sacrifices pieces she seems to love. By the end, you understand a great deal about how the academy is run.\nopt.right.say.1 [21753e] = 你输了，输得很慢，一边输一边看。她会牺牲那些她似乎很珍爱的棋子。到最后，你对这所学院是怎么运转的明白了许多。\n\n@event isolde_portrait\n# en: The Young Headmistress\ntitle [9f225c] = 年轻的院长\n# en: In a forgotten corridor you find a portrait of a laughing girl with a wand stuck behind her ear and ink on her nose. The plaque has been scratched off. The eyes are unmistakably the headmistress's. The portrait winks.\ntext [2b1741] = 在一条被遗忘的走廊里，你发现了一幅画像：一个笑着的女孩，耳后别着魔杖，鼻尖沾着墨水。铭牌被刮掉了。那双眼睛无疑是院长的。画像眨了眨眼。\n# en: A portrait in a forgotten corridor of a laughing young woman in student robes with a wand behind her ear, frame dusty, eyes unmistakably the headmistress's.\nscene [b07fba] = 被遗忘的走廊里一幅画像：一位身穿学生长袍、耳后别着魔杖、笑着的年轻女子，画框积满灰尘，眼睛无疑是院长的。\n# en: Ask the portrait about her\nopt.left.label [38d936] = 向画像打听她\n# en: \"Detention record of the decade,\" whispers the portrait. \"Set the observatory on fire twice. Best student this castle ever had.\" It winks again.\nopt.left.say.1 [a6720c] = “十年来禁闭记录最多的人，”画像低声说，“把天文台烧了两次。这座城堡有过的最好的学生。”它又眨了眨眼。\n# en: The portrait pretends to be asleep, loudly. By the time you give up, the corridor has rearranged itself, and it takes you half the evening to find your way out.\nopt.left.say.2 [b05711] = 肖像大声地装睡。等你放弃时，走廊已经自己换了样子，你花了半个晚上才找到出去的路。\n# en: Tell the headmistress you found it\nopt.right.label [966699] = 告诉院长你发现了它\n# en: She is quiet for a moment. \"I thought they'd burned it.\" She almost smiles. \"Leave it where it is. Someone should remember her.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [1bb647] = 她沉默了片刻。“我还以为他们把它烧了。”她几乎笑了。“就让它留在那儿吧。总该有人记得她。”\n\n@event isolde_judgement\n# en: A Question of Mercy\ntitle [a10d01] = 关于仁慈的问题\n# en: The headmistress slides a file across her desk. A second-year stole from the apothecary to pay for a sick mother's medicine. The rules say expulsion. \"You're a student,\" she says. \"Tell me what a student thinks is just.\"\ntext [050856] = 院长把一份卷宗推过桌面。一个二年级生为了给生病的母亲买药，从药铺偷了东西。按校规应当开除。“你是学生，”她说，“告诉我，学生认为什么才算公正。”\n# en: The headmistress at her desk with a single file open, a student seated opposite, rain on the window behind them.\nscene [d36bff] = 院长坐在书桌后，面前摊开一份卷宗，一名学生坐在对面，身后的窗上落着雨。\n# en: Argue for mercy\nopt.left.label [a3a062] = 为仁慈辩护\n# en: You argue for an hour. She listens to all of it. The second-year is given a job in the infirmary and a stern lecture. The mother recovers.\nopt.left.say.1 [eb948e] = 你辩了一个钟头。她全都听完了。那个二年级生被安排到医务室打工，外加一顿严厉的训诫。那位母亲康复了。\n# en: The rules exist for a reason\nopt.right.label [194310] = 校规自有其道理\n# en: \"Yes,\" she says, \"they do.\" She closes the file. You never learn what she decided, but the second-year is still in class the next week, looking grateful at nobody in particular.\nopt.right.say.1 [2c1752] = “是的，”她说，“它有。”她合上卷宗。你始终不知道她最后怎么决定的，但下个星期那个二年级生还在上课，对着不特定的某个人一脸感激。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_life.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_life.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event life_timeloop_1\n# en: Haven't We Done This?\ntitle [f7db95] = 这事是不是发生过？\n# en: Breakfast. Eggs. Thorne trips on the third step. A first-year spills juice on a prefect. You know all of this is about to happen, because it already happened. Yesterday. Which was also Tuesday.\ntext [8f145a] = 早餐。鸡蛋。索恩在第三级台阶上绊了一下。一个一年级生把果汁洒在了级长身上。你知道这一切马上就要发生，因为它已经发生过了。昨天。昨天也是星期二。\n# en: A student at breakfast staring at a plate of eggs with intense déjà vu, a clock on the wall with its hands spinning backward.\nscene [f64ed4] = 一名学生在早餐桌前盯着一盘鸡蛋，强烈的既视感涌上心头，墙上的钟指针在倒转。\n# en: Ignore it, you're probably just tired\nopt.left.label [f68ce0] = 别管它，大概只是累了\n# en: You eat the eggs. They taste exactly as they did yesterday. Thorne trips on the third step. You go to bed early, uneasy, and wake to eggs.\nopt.left.say.1 [1ccd6d] = 你吃了鸡蛋。味道和昨天一模一样。索恩教授在第三级台阶上绊了一下。你早早上床，心神不宁，醒来又是鸡蛋。\n# en: Warn Thorne about the step\nopt.right.label [eb6211] = 提醒索恩小心台阶\n# en: \"The third step, Professor.\" He looks at you strangely and steps over it. Somewhere, a clock skips. You go to bed, and wake, and it's still Tuesday.\nopt.right.say.1 [fc4336] = “第三级台阶，教授。”他奇怪地看了你一眼，跨了过去。某处，一座钟跳了一格。你上床，醒来，还是星期二。\n\n@event life_timeloop_2\n# en: Tuesday, Again\ntitle [d04f00] = 又是星期二\n# en: {loop_n <= 1: It's Tuesday again. Eggs, the third step, the juice, the prefect. Nobody else notices anything.|{loop_n <= 3: Tuesday. You could recite breakfast. You could recite the prefect's yelp.|{loop_n <= 5: Tuesday, again. The days are starting to feel thin, like a page read too often.|{loop_n <= 7: Tuesday. The eggs taste of nothing now. The clock in the hall ticks a beat late.|Tuesday. The edges of the morning are fraying, like a loop pulled too tight. It can't hold much longer.}}}} {loop_clues >= 3: You know where it comes from now.|{loop_clues >= 1: You have a thread to pull.|You have no idea why.}}\ntext [c737ca] = {loop_n <= 1: 又是星期二。鸡蛋、第三级台阶、果汁、级长。没有别人注意到任何事。|{loop_n <= 3: 星期二。你能背出早餐，能背出级长的那声惨叫。|{loop_n <= 5: 又是星期二。日子开始变薄了，像一页被翻了太多遍的纸。|{loop_n <= 7: 星期二。鸡蛋已经没有味道了。大厅里的钟晚了一拍。|星期二。早晨的边缘正在磨损，像一个被拉得太紧的圈。它撑不了多久了。}}}} {loop_clues >= 3: 现在你知道它从哪儿来了。|{loop_clues >= 1: 你手里有一根可以拉的线头。|你完全不知道为什么。}}\n# en: The same breakfast scene repeated, but the student is now standing on the table, holding up a sign that reads \"IT'S STILL TUESDAY\".\nscene [443eec] = 同样的早餐场景再次上演，但这一次学生站在了桌子上，举着一块牌子：“现在还是星期二”。\n# en: Use it: win every bet, ace the quiz\nopt.exploit.label [42a58e] = 利用它：每赌必赢，小测满分\n# en: Crowns and marks carry over. It wears on you, and someone may notice.\nopt.exploit.hint [522272] = 金币和分数都会留下。它会消磨你，也可能有人察觉。\n# en: You know every answer and every coin-toss. Why not?\nopt.exploit.say.1 [ceb5c5] = 每一道答案、每一次抛硬币你都知道。为什么不呢？\n# en: Do something you'd never dare\nopt.experiment.label [d06730] = 做一件你永远不敢做的事\n# en: Nothing you do today will last. Almost nothing.\nopt.experiment.hint [36f434] = 今天你做的一切都不会留下。几乎一切。\n# en: Nothing today can stick. You might as well find out who you are when nothing sticks.\nopt.experiment.say.1 [3cf8fb] = 今天什么都留不住。那不如看看，什么都留不住的时候，你是个怎样的人。\n# en: Look for the cause\nopt.investigate.label [c6ff51] = 寻找原因\n# en: Each loop, a clue. Clues make the fix possible.\nopt.investigate.hint [c9830b] = 每一轮，一条线索。线索让解法成为可能。\n# en: Something is doing this. Clocks don't stop on their own.\nopt.investigate.say.1 [21f23e] = 有什么东西在这么做。钟不会自己停下。\n# en: Try to break the loop\nopt.fix.label [e608ff] = 试着打破循环\n# en: A spell against the clock. Hard at first; easier every loop, and with what you've found.\nopt.fix.hint [7437c3] = 一道对抗时间的咒语。一开始很难；每一轮都会更容易，你找到的线索也有帮助。\n# en: You go up the astronomy tower before breakfast, while the morning is still new.\nopt.fix.say.1 [455845] = 趁早晨还新鲜，你在早餐前爬上了天文塔。\n\n@event life_timeloop_3\n# en: The Broken Chronometer\ntitle [d82d49] = 坏掉的计时仪\n# en: In the astronomy tower, a first-year is asleep beside an enormous brass chronometer, wand still stuck in its winding key. \"I just wanted one more day before my exam,\" says the note on his chest. {loop_clues >= 3: You know exactly which gear, and why.|You know roughly where to start.}\ntext [a07ba5] = 天文塔里，一个一年级生睡在一台巨大的黄铜计时仪旁边，魔杖还卡在发条钥匙里。他胸前的字条上写着：“我只是想在考试前多要一天。”{loop_clues >= 3: 你清楚地知道是哪个齿轮，也知道为什么。|你大概知道从哪儿下手。}\n# en: An astronomy tower at night, a giant brass chronometer with its gears spinning in reverse, a sleeping first-year slumped beside it with a wand still in hand.\nscene [4ad62e] = 夜晚的天文塔，一台巨型黄铜计时仪的齿轮在倒转，一个睡着的一年级生瘫在旁边，手里还握着魔杖。\n# en: Wrench the clock forward\nopt.left.label [86cabc] = 把钟硬拧向前\n# en: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\nopt.left.say.1 [aa5594] = 齿轮呻吟着，卡住，然后向前转动。某处，一座钟正常地敲响了，这是{loop_n >= 6: 仿佛一个月|好几天}以来的第一次。是星期三了。角落里的一年级生醒过来，如释重负地哭了。\n# en: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\nopt.left.say.2 [e63c45] = 计时仪咬了你一口，早晨像一封信一样折了起来。你醒来，鸡蛋、第三级台阶、果汁。是星期二。你对那座钟又多了解了一点。\n# en: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\nopt.left.say.3 [aa5594] = 齿轮呻吟着，卡住，然后向前转动。某处，一座钟正常地敲响了，这是{loop_n >= 6: 仿佛一个月|好几天}以来的第一次。是星期三了。角落里的一年级生醒过来，如释重负地哭了。\n# en: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\nopt.left.say.4 [e63c45] = 计时仪咬了你一口，早晨像一封信一样折了起来。你醒来，鸡蛋、第三级台阶、果汁。是星期二。你对那座钟又多了解了一点。\n# en: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\nopt.left.say.5 [aa5594] = 齿轮呻吟着，卡住，然后向前转动。某处，一座钟正常地敲响了，这是{loop_n >= 6: 仿佛一个月|好几天}以来的第一次。是星期三了。角落里的一年级生醒过来，如释重负地哭了。\n# en: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\nopt.left.say.6 [e63c45] = 计时仪咬了你一口，早晨像一封信一样折了起来。你醒来，鸡蛋、第三级台阶、果汁。是星期二。你对那座钟又多了解了一点。\n# en: The gears groan, catch and turn forward. Somewhere a clock strikes, properly, for the first time in {loop_n >= 6: what feels like a month|days}. It's Wednesday. The first-year in the corner wakes up and starts to cry with relief.\nopt.left.say.7 [aa5594] = 齿轮呻吟着，卡住，然后向前转动。某处，一座钟正常地敲响了，这是{loop_n >= 6: 仿佛一个月|好几天}以来的第一次。是星期三了。角落里的一年级生醒过来，如释重负地哭了。\n# en: The chronometer bites, and the morning folds up like a letter. You wake to eggs, and the third step, and the juice. It's Tuesday. You know a little more about the clock.\nopt.left.say.8 [e63c45] = 计时仪咬了你一口，早晨像一封信一样折了起来。你醒来，鸡蛋、第三级台阶、果汁。是星期二。你对那座钟又多了解了一点。\n# en: Wake the first-year and fix it together\nopt.right.label [64c5c1] = 叫醒一年级生，一起修好它\n# en: You shake him awake. He's horrified, then helpful. Between your clues and his guilt you find the gear together, and the clock strikes Wednesday. He passes his exam. You never tell him how many Tuesdays it was.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f81c1] = 你把他摇醒。他先是吓坏了，然后帮了大忙。靠你的线索和他的愧疚，你们一起找到了那个齿轮，钟敲响了星期三。他考试及格了。你从没告诉他到底过了多少个星期二。\n# en: You shake him awake, and he panics, and the wand snaps in the key. The morning folds up. You wake to eggs. It's Tuesday, and you know a little more about the boy.\nopt.right.say.2 [a2a494] = 你把他摇醒，他慌了，魔杖断在了钥匙里。早晨折了起来。你醒来，又是鸡蛋。是星期二，你对那个男孩又多了解了一点。\n\n@event life_portrait_escape\n# en: The Escaped Portrait\ntitle [2a676c] = 出逃的画像\n# en: An empty frame in the long gallery. The Duchess of Ambry has escaped her portrait and is hiding in a landscape three corridors away, refusing to come back. \"That frame,\" she declares, \"is hideous.\"\ntext [a18812] = 长廊里一个空画框。安布里公爵夫人逃出了她的画像，躲进了三条走廊外的一幅风景画里，拒绝回来。“那个画框，”她宣布，“丑死了。”\n# en: An empty gilded picture frame on a gallery wall, and further down the corridor a painted duchess hiding inside a landscape of a picnic.\nscene [805579] = 长廊墙上一个空空的镀金画框，走廊尽头，一位画中的公爵夫人正躲在一幅野餐风景画里。\n# en: Persuade her back\nopt.left.label [fd187a] = 劝她回来\n# en: You promise to have her frame regilded. She returns, mollified. The caretaker regilds it. You've acquired a duchess who now considers you a personal friend.\nopt.left.say.1 [af9e67] = 你答应给她的画框重新镀金。她回来了，气消了。管理员重新镀了金。你收获了一位公爵夫人，她现在把你当作私人朋友。\n# en: She calls you a peasant and moves to a painting of a shipwreck. She's still there. She says she likes the sea air.\nopt.left.say.2 [7c07eb] = 她骂你是个乡巴佬，搬进了一幅沉船图里。她至今还在那儿。她说她喜欢海风。\n# en: Leave her in the picnic\nopt.right.label [f13545] = 让她留在野餐里\n# en: She seems happier among the sandwiches. So does the picnic. Some arrangements simply work.\nopt.right.say.1 [09beab] = 她在三明治之间似乎更快乐。野餐也是。有些安排就是刚刚好。\n\n@event life_library_cat\n# en: The Library Cat\ntitle [d3cfff] = 图书馆的猫\n# en: The library cat — a vast, ancient beast called Footnote — is asleep on the only copy of the book you need. You have an essay due tomorrow. Footnote has no deadlines.\ntext [968aad] = 图书馆的猫——一只名叫“脚注”的巨大古老生物——正睡在你需要的那本书上，而那是唯一的一本。你的论文明天就要交。脚注没有截止日期。\n# en: An enormous fluffy cat asleep on the exact book a student needs, on a high library shelf, one eye half-open.\nscene [8ac48f] = 一只巨大的蓬松猫咪睡在图书馆高处书架上，身下正是学生需要的那本书，一只眼睛半睁着。\n# en: Move the cat\nopt.left.label [967e4f] = 把猫挪开\n# en: You lift Footnote like a sack of warm flour. He allows it, with contempt. You get the book. He gets your chair.\nopt.left.say.1 [52e940] = 你像抱一袋温热的面粉一样抱起脚注。它允许了，带着鄙夷。你拿到了书。它拿到了你的椅子。\n# en: Footnote does not consent to being moved. You now have three long scratches and still no book.\nopt.left.say.2 [6230a8] = 脚注不同意被挪动。你现在多了三道长长的抓痕，书还是没拿到。\n# en: Bribe the cat\nopt.right.label [011f22] = 贿赂这只猫\n# en: One sardine. Footnote rises like a sultan, stretches, and moves exactly one book to the left. It's enough.\nopt.right.say.1 [b290a1] = 一条沙丁鱼。脚注像苏丹一样起身，伸了个懒腰，正好往左挪了一本书的距离。够了。\n\n@event life_invent_spell\n# en: A Spell of Your Own\ntitle [9d35aa] = 属于你的咒语\n# en: Late at night you realise there's a gap — a spell nobody has written yet, just a small one, to make ink dry instantly. You could try to invent it. Inventing spells, Thorne says, is \"how most of my former students lost their eyebrows\".\ntext [40816d] = 深夜，你意识到有个空白——一个还没人写过的咒语，只是个小咒语，能让墨水瞬间变干。你可以试着发明它。索恩说，发明咒语是“我大多数前学生失去眉毛的方式”。\n# en: A student at a desk covered in crossed-out incantations, a tiny spiral of light hovering over the page, an expression of dawning triumph.\nscene [c075ca] = 一名学生坐在写满划掉的咒文的书桌前，一小团螺旋状的光悬浮在纸上，脸上渐渐露出胜利的神情。\n# en: Invent it carefully\nopt.left.label [6adb19] = 小心地发明它\n# en: Three weeks of theory and one perfect evening. The ink dries, and so does a paper cut on your thumb: what you've written is a cousin of Mend. Thorne grunts and puts your name on next year's lecture slide.\nopt.left.say.1 [b2a1fc] = 三周的理论，加一个完美的夜晚。墨水干了，你拇指上被纸划的口子也愈合了：你写出来的是愈合咒的一个远亲。索恩教授哼了一声，把你的名字写进了明年的讲义。\n# en: Your spell dries ink instantly. It also dries the paper, the desk and your eyes. You shelve it for now.\nopt.left.say.2 [20cce9] = 你的咒语能瞬间弄干墨水。也能弄干纸、书桌和你的眼睛。你暂时把它搁置了。\n# en: Just try things until something works\nopt.right.label [847e81] = 乱试一通，直到有效\n# en: Somehow — through chaos and luck — it works. You couldn't explain why under oath. It still counts.\nopt.right.say.1 [c2baba] = 不知怎么的——靠着混乱和运气——它成功了。就算发誓你也解释不了为什么。但它照样算数。\n# en: Your eyebrows survive. Your desk does not.\nopt.right.say.2 [182ef8] = 你的眉毛幸存了。你的书桌没有。\n\n@event life_cursed_umbrella\n# en: The Cursed Umbrella\ntitle [8c3b3e] = 被诅咒的伞\n# en: You borrowed an umbrella from lost property. It's black, elegant and cursed: underneath it, it is always raining. The rest of the world is sunny. You are very wet.\ntext [b42225] = 你从失物招领处借了一把伞。它是黑色的，很优雅，还被诅咒了：伞底下永远在下雨。外面整个世界阳光明媚。你浑身湿透。\n# en: A student holding a black umbrella under which a tiny private rainstorm is pouring, while the sky outside is perfectly clear.\nscene [3ed21b] = 一名学生撑着一把黑伞，伞下正下着一场私人小暴雨，而外面的天空晴朗无云。\n# en: Break the curse\nopt.left.label [bbf151] = 解除诅咒\n# en: The curse lifts with a soft hiss. The umbrella is now just a very nice umbrella. It isn't yours, but you sell it to Corvin anyway; he wants it \"for drama\".\nopt.left.say.1 [5d8f53] = 诅咒轻轻嘶的一声解除了。现在它只是一把非常好看的伞。它不是你的，但你还是把它卖给了科尔文，他说要拿来“营造戏剧效果”。\n# en: Now it rains sideways.\nopt.left.say.2 [1a72a4] = 现在雨是横着下的。\n# en: Return it to lost property\nopt.right.label [5ea06a] = 还给失物招领处\n# en: The caretaker takes it back, sighs, and hangs it on a hook labelled \"DO NOT\". There are six other things on that hook.\nopt.right.say.1 [c90b55] = 管理员收了回去，叹了口气，把它挂在一个标着“别碰”的钩子上。那个钩子上已经挂了六样别的东西。\n\n@event life_exam_dream\n# en: The Exam Dream\ntitle [e7738c] = 考试噩梦\n# en: You dream you're in the exam hall in your pyjamas. The paper is in a language that keeps changing. The proctor is Bartholomew the goat. You wake at 4am, heart pounding.\ntext [4ad83e] = 你梦见自己穿着睡衣坐在考场里。试卷上的语言不停地变来变去。监考官是山羊巴塞洛缪。凌晨四点你惊醒了，心怦怦直跳。\n# en: A dream-image: a student in an exam hall in their pyjamas, the exam paper written in a language that keeps changing, a clock with no hands.\nscene [e45f4e] = 梦境画面：一名学生穿着睡衣坐在考场里，试卷上的文字不停变换，一座没有指针的钟。\n# en: Get up and revise\nopt.left.label [02956b] = 起床复习\n# en: You revise until breakfast. The real exam paper is written in a normal language. You're absurdly relieved.\nopt.left.say.1 [ba0aba] = 你一直复习到早餐。真正的试卷是用正常语言写的。你如释重负得有点荒唐。\n# en: Go back to sleep\nopt.right.label [097b30] = 接着睡\n# en: You dream again. This time the goat gives you full marks. You choose to take this as an omen.\nopt.right.say.1 [c9ff1e] = 你又做梦了。这次山羊给了你满分。你决定把这当作一个好兆头。\n\n@event life_garden_plot\n# en: A Patch of Earth\ntitle [675687] = 一方泥土\n# en: Old Maud is giving out the abandoned plots behind the greenhouses. There's one left: small, weedy and full of rocks. \"It's yours if you want it,\" she says. \"Gardens are a lot of work. Most of the work is noticing.\"\ntext [492379] = 老莫德正在分发温室后面那些废弃的小块地。还剩一块：小小的，杂草丛生，满是石头。“想要的话就归你，”她说，“种园子很费功夫。大部分功夫是用心去看。”\n# en: A small square of freshly turned earth behind the greenhouses, a wooden stake with a student's name painted on it, a watering can beside it.\nscene [ae502d] = 温室后面一小块刚翻过的泥土，插着一根写有学生名字的木桩，旁边放着一只水壶。\n# en: Take the plot\nopt.left.label [a249fc] = 接下这块地\n# en: You spend a whole weekend clearing rocks and earn a back that complains for a week. By spring, you have lettuces, three violent mandrakes and a strange purple flower nobody can name.\nopt.left.say.1 [dcd8a6] = 你花了整整一个周末清理石头，换来一副抱怨了一星期的腰背。到了春天，你有了生菜、三棵暴躁的曼德拉草，和一朵谁也叫不出名字的奇怪紫花。\n# en: Leave it for someone else\nopt.right.label [43df3f] = 留给别人\n# en: A shy first-year takes it and grows giant pumpkins. You feel oddly proud of them.\nopt.right.say.1 [a9e348] = 一个害羞的一年级生接手了它，种出了巨大的南瓜。你莫名为它们感到骄傲。\n\n@event life_pen_pal\n# en: A Letter From Thornwick\ntitle [64e8f7] = 荆木来信\n# en: A letter arrives from a stranger at Thornwick Academy, the rival school. \"Dear Lumenhold student chosen at random,\" it begins. \"I'm told you're all insufferable. Prove me wrong? T. Reed (illusion, first year, not insufferable).\"\ntext [36cb26] = 一封信来自对手学校荆木学院的一个陌生人。“亲爱的、随机抽中的流明堡学生，”信的开头写道，“听说你们全都让人受不了。能证明我错了吗？T. 里德（幻术，一年级，并非让人受不了）。”\n# en: A letter with a green Thornwick seal open on a desk, beside a reply half-written in Lumenhold blue ink.\nscene [72beee] = 书桌上摊开一封盖着荆木绿色封印的信，旁边是一封用流明堡蓝墨水写了一半的回信。\n# en: Write back\nopt.left.label [ae4ebf] = 回信\n# en: Your reply is four pages long and only slightly insufferable, and it takes the whole evening. The answer arrives within a week, signed \"Tamsin\". You've made a friend in enemy territory.\nopt.left.say.1 [b1f05a] = 你的回信写了四页，只是稍微有点让人受不了，花了一整个晚上。不到一星期回信就到了，署名“塔姆辛”。你在敌方领土交到了一个朋友。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.right.label [e2ae0c] = 不理它\n# en: You ignore it, and enjoy the feeling of being unbothered for about a day. Some letters, you suspect, don't take no for an answer.\nopt.right.say.1 [a29868] = 你没理它，享受了大约一天的无所谓。你怀疑，有些信是不接受拒绝的。\n\n@event life_three_headed_puppy\n# en: A Very Lost Puppy\ntitle [b1e6d0] = 一只迷路的小狗\n# en: There is a puppy in the dormitory corridor. It has three heads. One is eating a shoe, one is licking the wall, one is howling. It is very lost and, all three heads agree, very hungry.\ntext [01f554] = 宿舍走廊里有一只小狗。它有三个头。一个在啃鞋，一个在舔墙，一个在嚎叫。它彻底迷路了，而且——三个头一致认为——非常饿。\n# en: A puppy with three heads sitting in a corridor, one head chewing a shoe, one licking a wall, one howling mournfully.\nscene [9614f5] = 一只三头小狗坐在走廊里，一个头在嚼鞋子，一个在舔墙，一个在悲伤地嚎叫。\n# en: Feed it and find its owner\nopt.left.label [f395df] = 喂它，然后找它的主人\n# en: You buy it a string of sausages from the kitchens, then follow its owner's frantic shouting to Professor Brask's office. She weeps with relief. The puppy's three heads each lick you once, in strict order.\nopt.left.say.1 [658f91] = 你从厨房给它买了一串香肠，然后循着主人焦急的呼喊找到了布拉斯克教授的办公室。她松了一口气，哭了出来。小狗的三个头严格按顺序各舔了你一下。\n# en: Call Professor Brask\nopt.right.label [a54d9d] = 叫布拉斯克教授来\n# en: Brask arrives at a run with a lead and three biscuits, and explains to you, at length, how to tell which head is in charge. \"Bad boys,\" she says fondly. All three heads look deeply ashamed.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c0fca] = 布拉斯克拿着牵绳和三块饼干一路跑来，还拉着你讲了好半天怎么分辨哪个头说了算。“坏孩子们，”她宠溺地说。三个头看起来都羞愧极了。\n\n@event life_bell_tower\n# en: The Midnight Bell\ntitle [2cdb21] = 午夜钟声\n# en: Tradition: once in your time at Lumenhold, you must ring the old bell at midnight without getting caught. Your dorm has decided tonight is your night.\ntext [97f4cd] = 传统：在流明堡的日子里，你必须有一次在午夜敲响古钟而不被抓到。你的宿舍认定今晚就是你的夜晚。\n# en: A dark bell tower interior, ropes hanging down, a group of students at the bottom daring one of their number to climb.\nscene [33ec20] = 漆黑的钟楼内部，钟绳垂落，一群学生站在底下怂恿其中一人爬上去。\n# en: Climb and ring it\nopt.left.label [5bb0b4] = 爬上去敲钟\n# en: BONG. The whole castle wakes. By the time the prefects reach the tower you're back in bed, innocent and smug.\nopt.left.say.1 [37e43a] = 当——整座城堡都醒了。等级长们赶到钟楼时，你已经回到床上，一脸无辜又得意。\n# en: BONG — and Bram is at the bottom of the stairs when you come down. Detention. But you rang it, and everybody heard.\nopt.left.say.2 [0df3f9] = 当——而你下来时，布拉姆就站在楼梯底下。禁闭。但你敲响了它，所有人都听见了。\n# en: Decline the dare\nopt.right.label [78dc24] = 拒绝这个挑战\n# en: Someone else rings it instead and becomes a legend. You'll always wonder.\nopt.right.say.1 [a936f5] = 别人替你敲响了钟，成了传奇。你会一直好奇的。\n\n@event life_fog_maze\n# en: The Fog Maze\ntitle [a0d483] = 雾中迷宫\n# en: Fog has come up off the lake so thick you can't see your own feet. The path to the greenhouses has become a maze. Somewhere ahead, someone is calling for help, faintly.\ntext [ee15c0] = 湖上升起的雾浓得你连自己的脚都看不见。通往温室的小路变成了一座迷宫。前方某处，有人在微弱地呼救。\n# en: The academy grounds swallowed by thick white fog, hedges looming like walls, a lantern glowing faintly ahead.\nscene [666c24] = 学院的庭院被浓密的白雾吞没，树篱像墙一样隐约耸立，前方一盏灯笼发着微光。\n# en: Follow the voice\nopt.left.label [f27ba5] = 循着声音走\n# en: You find a first-year sitting on a bench, completely lost, sixty feet from the door. You walk them in. They tell everyone you saved their life.\nopt.left.say.1 [2c5755] = 你找到一个坐在长椅上、彻底迷路的一年级生，离门只有六十英尺。你领着对方进去。对方逢人便说你救了自己一命。\n# en: You walk in circles for an hour and end up exactly where you started. The voice was a very clever crow.\nopt.left.say.2 [fc0168] = 你绕了一个钟头的圈子，最后回到了起点。那声音是一只非常聪明的乌鸦。\n# en: Cast a light and wait\nopt.right.label [988921] = 点亮光芒，原地等待\n# en: You raise a floating light and wait. One by one, three lost students find their way to it. You feel like a lighthouse.\nopt.right.say.1 [380b7f] = 你升起一团漂浮的光，静静等待。三个迷路的学生一个接一个地找到了它。你觉得自己像一座灯塔。\n\n@event life_wishing_well\n# en: The Wishing Well\ntitle [8cab57] = 许愿井\n# en: In the old cloister there's a wishing well. The inscription says: \"ONE COIN, ONE WISH, ONE PRICE.\" The bottom glitters with three hundred years of coins.\ntext [0ae5ea] = 古老的回廊里有一口许愿井。铭文写着：“一枚硬币，一个愿望，一个代价。”井底闪烁着三百年来的硬币。\n# en: An old stone well in the corner of a cloister, coins glinting at the bottom, a carved inscription half-worn away.\nscene [9ea5b9] = 回廊角落里一口古老的石井，井底硬币闪闪发光，一段刻字已经磨损了一半。\n# en: Throw in a coin and wish\nopt.left.label [ce57aa] = 投一枚硬币许愿\n# en: You wish for a good month. It is, mostly. Coincidence, probably.\nopt.left.say.1 [4c845e] = 你许愿这个月过得顺利。大体上确实如此。大概是巧合。\n# en: You wish. The well hiccups and spits your coin back out. Apparently, you can't afford that wish.\nopt.left.say.2 [3fc324] = 你许了愿。井打了个嗝，把你的硬币吐了出来。看来这个愿望你付不起。\n# en: Fish out a few old coins\nopt.right.label [dbef4d] = 捞几枚古币出来\n# en: Fifteen crowns' worth of antique coins. The well is silent. Disapprovingly silent.\nopt.right.say.1 [f67aeb] = 价值十五克朗的古董硬币。井沉默着。不以为然地沉默着。\n# en: The well bites. You lose your own purse in it. ONE PRICE indeed.\nopt.right.say.2 [708beb] = 井咬人。你把自己的钱袋掉了进去。果然是“一个代价”。\n\n@event life_borrowed_broom\n# en: The Borrowed Broom\ntitle [c73e99] = 借来的扫帚\n# en: A second-year lends you their broom for an errand. Midway across the lake, you discover why they were so keen to lend it: it is extremely ticklish, and it hates you.\ntext [ca31e2] = 一个二年级生把扫帚借给你去跑腿。飞到湖中央时，你才发现对方为什么那么乐意借给你：它极其怕痒，而且讨厌你。\n# en: A student clinging to a bucking broom high above the castle, its bristles on fire, a smaller student on the ground waving frantically.\nscene [c7a1ed] = 一名学生紧紧抓着一把在城堡上空乱蹦的扫帚，帚尾着了火，地上一个小个子学生在拼命挥手。\n# en: Wrestle it home\nopt.left.label [745cb7] = 硬把它拽回去\n# en: You land in a heap on the lawn, triumphant and windswept. The broom sulks. The second-year owes you a favour.\nopt.left.say.1 [90315b] = 你一头栽在草坪上，得意洋洋、头发凌乱。扫帚生着闷气。那个二年级生欠你一个人情。\n# en: You land in the lake. The broom flies home without you, very pleased with itself.\nopt.left.say.2 [a5f5be] = 你掉进了湖里。扫帚自己飞回了家，非常得意。\n# en: Soothe it\nopt.right.label [52ec4c] = 安抚它\n# en: You talk to it gently all the way across. It purrs. Brooms shouldn't purr. Now it follows you around.\nopt.right.say.1 [542704] = 你一路温柔地跟它说话。它发出了呼噜声。扫帚不该会呼噜的。现在它到哪儿都跟着你。\n# en: It is not soothed. It is, if anything, more ticklish, and it tips you into the shallows on the way home.\nopt.right.say.2 [69c8e2] = 它没有被安抚。要说有什么变化，那就是更怕痒了，回去的路上还把你甩进了浅水里。\n\n@event life_double_trouble\n# en: Your Double Is Loose\ntitle [0b749f] = 你的分身跑了\n# en: An illusion of you — one you thought you'd dismissed — is still wandering the castle. It has been to three of your classes, flirted with a prefect, and volunteered you for the choir.\ntext [e2deca] = 一个你以为早就解除了的分身幻象，还在城堡里到处游荡。它去上了你的三节课，和一位级长调情，还替你报名了合唱团。\n# en: A student staring in horror across a crowded corridor at a perfect copy of themselves, cheerfully chatting up a professor.\nscene [f7b09f] = 一名学生惊恐地隔着拥挤的走廊望着一个和自己一模一样的复制品，那复制品正兴高采烈地和一位教授攀谈。\n# en: Catch it\nopt.left.label [6ffc79] = 抓住它\n# en: You corner it in a broom cupboard. It looks at you, shrugs and pops like a bubble. The prefect, however, still expects a date.\nopt.left.say.1 [75bb7a] = 你把它堵在扫帚间里。它看着你，耸了耸肩，像泡泡一样“啪”地消失了。不过，那位级长仍然在等着约会。\n# en: It escapes, and spends a week as the most charming version of you. People are disappointed when you come back.\nopt.left.say.2 [4bfacb] = 它溜走了，当了一个星期最有魅力的你。你回来的时候，大家都有点失望。\n# en: Let it run its course\nopt.right.label [0407c9] = 随它去吧\n# en: It attends your lectures and takes better notes than you do. When it fades after a week, you're almost sorry.\nopt.right.say.1 [c344b5] = 它替你上课，笔记记得比你还好。一星期后它消散时，你几乎有点舍不得。\n\n@event life_auction\n# en: The Lost-Property Auction\ntitle [a95e5d] = 失物拍卖会\n# en: Once a year the caretaker auctions off unclaimed lost property. There's a box nobody wants that hums faintly. The bidding starts at eight crowns. Nobody's bidding.\ntext [8cfccd] = 每年一次，管理员会拍卖无人认领的失物。有一个没人要的盒子在微微嗡鸣。起拍价八克朗。没人出价。\n# en: A crowded hall, a caretaker with a gavel at a lectern, tables piled with lost scarves, wands, boots and one suspiciously humming box.\nscene [c3957c] = 拥挤的大厅，管理员手持木槌站在讲台后，桌上堆满了丢失的围巾、魔杖、靴子，还有一个可疑地嗡嗡作响的盒子。\n# en: Bid on the humming box\nopt.left.label [fb0360] = 竞拍那个嗡鸣的盒子\n# en: Inside: a music box that plays a different song every time you open it. Each one makes you think of something new.\nopt.left.say.1 [2acaf5] = 里面是一个音乐盒，每次打开都会放一首不同的曲子。每一首都让你想到新的东西。\n# en: Inside: a very old, very annoyed hiccup curse, which immediately transfers itself to you.\nopt.left.say.2 [04b453] = 里面是一个非常古老、非常恼火的打嗝诅咒，它立刻转移到了你身上。\n# en: Just watch the bidding\nopt.right.label [dc6130] = 只看看拍卖\n# en: Nobody bids on the box. A first-year buys a single left boot for a crown and looks thrilled. You leave with nothing and a good story.\nopt.right.say.1 [f4e0bc] = 没人给那个盒子出价。一个一年级生花一克朗买了一只左脚靴子，看上去高兴极了。你两手空空地离开，带走了一个好故事。\n\n@event life_village_busking\n# en: Busking in the Village\ntitle [2797e6] = 小镇卖艺\n# en: You're short on crowns, and the village square has a fountain, an empty hat and a crowd of shoppers. You do know a few songs.\ntext [f6178e] = 你手头缺克朗，而小镇广场上有一座喷泉、一顶空帽子和一群购物的人。你确实会几首歌。\n# en: A cobbled village square, a student singing beside a fountain with an upturned hat at their feet, villagers pausing to listen.\nscene [96e354] = 铺着鹅卵石的小镇广场，一名学生在喷泉边唱歌，脚边放着一顶翻过来的帽子，镇民们驻足聆听。\n# en: Sing your heart out\nopt.left.label [ebf8e1] = 尽情放声歌唱\n# en: A small crowd gathers. Someone requests an encore. The hat fills with copper and one silver coin from a weeping old man.\nopt.left.say.1 [f60a68] = 一小群人围了过来。有人要求返场。帽子里装满了铜币，还有一位泪流满面的老人投下的一枚银币。\n# en: Three crowns and a sympathetic look from a dog. Busking is harder than it looks.\nopt.left.say.2 [06183f] = 三克朗，外加一条狗同情的眼神。卖艺比看起来难多了。\n# en: Play the street-magic act instead\nopt.right.label [1f6f47] = 改演街头魔术\n# en: You make doves out of the fountain spray. Children scream with joy. The hat overflows.\nopt.right.say.1 [747727] = 你用喷泉的水雾变出鸽子。孩子们开心得尖叫。帽子都装不下了。\n# en: Your doves come out as damp sparrows. People are kind about it.\nopt.right.say.2 [def817] = 你变出来的鸽子成了湿漉漉的麻雀。大家都很包容。\n\n@event life_moths\n# en: A Storm of Moths\ntitle [34c527] = 飞蛾风暴\n# en: You open your door to find your room full of moths — thousands of them, pale as moonlight, circling your candle in silent spirals. It's eerie. It's also, you can't help noticing, beautiful.\ntext [b3079c] = 你推开门，发现房间里满是飞蛾——成千上万只，白得像月光，绕着你的蜡烛无声地盘旋。很诡异。你也不得不承认，很美。\n# en: A dormitory room filled with thousands of pale moths circling a single candle, a student standing in the doorway, astonished.\nscene [ba25b5] = 宿舍房间里成千上万只苍白的飞蛾绕着一支蜡烛盘旋，一名学生站在门口，惊呆了。\n# en: Open the window and let them go\nopt.left.label [09f522] = 打开窗放它们走\n# en: They pour out into the night in a silver river. One stays behind, on your pillow, for a week. You name it Gregory.\nopt.left.say.1 [582ae8] = 它们像一条银色的河流涌入夜色。有一只留了下来，在你的枕头上待了一个星期。你给它起名叫格雷戈里。\n# en: Study them\nopt.right.label [3a81ec] = 研究它们\n# en: They're luna moths, migrating — and drawn to something in your room. You find it: a crumb of moonpetal on your sleeve. Mystery solved. Mostly.\nopt.right.say.1 [eb5366] = 它们是迁徙中的月形天蚕蛾——被你房间里的什么东西吸引了过来。你找到了：袖子上一点月瓣花的碎屑。谜团解开了。大体上。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_lightrag_2026_10.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_lightrag_2026_10.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event stables_hippogriff_grooming\n# en: Feathers the Wrong Way\ntitle [08770c] = 逆着长的羽毛\n# en: {A hippogriff has rolled in the mud and lies in her stall with every feather pointing the wrong way, waiting to be groomed.|The muddy hippogriff again. She lies down the moment she sees you with a brush.|The hippogriff has kept her muddiest wing for you, and holds it out like a gift.} {season == \"autumn\": Leaves are stuck in her feathers.|{The stable cat supervises from a beam.|The owls are watching to see if you get it wrong.|Brask is somewhere nearby, humming to a feed sack.}}\ntext [cd2c78] = {一头鹰头马身有翼兽在泥里打了个滚，躺在厩栏里，每根羽毛都朝着反方向，等人来梳理。|又是那头满身泥的鹰头马身有翼兽。她一看见你拿着刷子就躺下了。|鹰头马身有翼兽把最脏的那只翅膀留给了你，像献礼一样伸过来。} {season == \"autumn\": 她的羽毛里卡着落叶。|{马厩的猫在横梁上监工。|猫头鹰们等着看你出错。|布拉斯克就在附近，对着一袋饲料哼歌。}}\n# en: A student brushing the wing feathers of a lying-down hippogriff in a straw-filled stall, loose feathers drifting in a shaft of light.\nscene [302c3b] = 铺满稻草的厩栏里，一名学生在给一头卧着的鹰头马身有翼兽梳理翅羽，散落的羽毛在一束光里飘浮。\n# en: Groom her properly, feather by feather\nopt.left.label [eded20] = 一根一根，好好梳\n# en: {It takes the whole afternoon and both your arms. She falls asleep with her head on your boot, which is the highest mark a hippogriff gives.|You do it the slow way, with the grain. She clicks her beak at you every time you're right, and once, gently, when you're wrong.|You know her feathers now. She lets you do the hard bit under the wing, and Brask, passing, says nothing at all.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9888b4] = {这花了整整一个下午，外加你的两条胳膊。她把头搁在你的靴子上睡着了，这是鹰头马身有翼兽能给的最高评价。|你慢慢来，顺着羽毛的走向梳。每次梳对，她就冲你咔哒一下喙；梳错时也咔哒一下，很轻。|你现在熟悉她的羽毛了。她让你梳翅膀底下最难的那块。布拉斯克路过，一个字也没说。}\n# en: Read Brask's grooming notes on the wall\nopt.right.label [2c0b1f] = 读墙上布拉斯克的梳理笔记\n# en: {The notes are long, opinionated and illustrated. You learn a great deal about feathers. The hippogriff stays muddy and sighs at you.|You read the notes. Brask catches you at it and says, \"She doesn't need a scholar, she needs a brush.\"|You finish the notes, which you could recite by now, and leave the brush for someone else. The hippogriff looks away.}\nopt.right.say.1 [49fcee] = {笔记又长又固执，还配了插图。你学到了很多关于羽毛的知识。鹰头马身有翼兽依旧满身泥，冲你叹了口气。|你读着笔记。布拉斯克撞见了，说：“她不需要学者，她需要一把刷子。”|你把笔记读完了，现在都能背下来了，然后把刷子留给了别人。鹰头马身有翼兽扭开了头。}\n\n@event stables_tack_room\n# en: The Tack Room\ntitle [a30d64] = 马具间\n# en: {The tack room is a mess of harnesses, and the oil pot is out. Someone has to do the straps before Beastcraft tomorrow.|The tack room again. The griffon chick has been at the buckles.|The tack room has your bucket in it now, the one you sit on.} {season == \"winter\": It's the warmest room in the stables, and it smells of leather and oil.|{One strap has teeth marks all along it.|A saddle is missing; nobody wants to say whose.|There is a nest in the old harness, and it is occupied.}}\ntext [036030] = {马具间里的挽具乱成一团，油罐已经摆出来了。明天驭兽课之前，总得有人把皮带收拾好。|又是马具间。狮鹫幼崽把扣环啃过了。|马具间里现在有你的桶了，就是你坐的那只。} {season == \"winter\": 这是马厩里最暖和的屋子，满是皮革和油的味道。|{有一根皮带上从头到尾都是牙印。|少了一副马鞍，谁也不肯说是谁的。|旧挽具里有个窝，而且有主了。}}\n# en: A cramped tack room hung with harnesses and saddles, a student sitting on an upturned bucket oiling a strap, a griffon chick peering in at the door.\nscene [fded30] = 挂满挽具和马鞍的狭小马具间，一名学生坐在倒扣的桶上给皮带上油，一只狮鹫幼崽从门口探进头来。\n# en: Oil and mend the straps\nopt.left.label [692558] = 给皮带上油、修补\n# en: {You oil straps until your hands are black and the room smells like a cobbler's. Every buckle works. Nobody thanks you, which is how you know it's done.|You mend the bitten strap and replace the buckle the chick swallowed. It will reappear, Brask says darkly.|You do the straps by touch now, talking to the nest in the old harness. Its owner answers.}\nopt.left.say.1 [970da8] = {你给皮带上油，直到双手乌黑，屋里闻起来像鞋匠铺。每个扣环都好使了。没人道谢，你就知道活干完了。|你补好了被咬的皮带，换掉了幼崽吞下去的扣环。布拉斯克阴沉地说，它会再冒出来的。|你现在闭着眼都能收拾皮带，一边干一边跟旧挽具里的窝说话。窝的主人回了话。}\n# en: Slip out before anyone hands you the pot\nopt.right.label [120ded] = 趁没人递油罐，溜走\n# en: {You leave the straps for the morning. In the morning Brask hands you the pot, and a look.|You're out of the door before the oil pot is. Behind you the griffon chick starts on another buckle.|You slip away. You hear Brask say your name to the hippogriffs, in a tone.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9ce37f] = {你把皮带留到早上。到了早上，布拉斯克把油罐递给你，外加一个眼神。|油罐还没递过来，你已经出门了。身后，狮鹫幼崽开始啃另一个扣环。|你溜走了。你听见布拉斯克对着鹰头马身有翼兽们念你的名字，语气不太妙。}\n\n@event courtyard_fountain_wishes\n# en: Coins in the Fountain\ntitle [fa2295] = 喷泉里的硬币\n# en: {Students throw coins in the courtyard fountain for luck before exams. The coins are still there, glinting.|The fountain is full of exam-luck again.|You know exactly how many coins are in the fountain this week, and that is a bad sign.} {season == \"winter\": The water is cold enough to make your teeth ache by looking at it.|{A sign says the coins go to the infirmary fund.|A first-year is throwing in a coin with a long, whispered wish.|The fountain is freezing at one edge, as it does when it shouldn't.}}\ntext [30a6f7] = {学生们考试前会往庭院喷泉里扔硬币求好运。硬币还在水底闪着光。|喷泉里又满是考试的好运了。|你清楚地知道这周喷泉里有多少枚硬币，这可不是好兆头。} {season == \"winter\": 那水冷得光是看着都牙疼。|{一块牌子写着硬币归医务室基金。|一个一年级生扔进一枚硬币，许了个又长又小声的愿。|喷泉的一边结冰了，就像它不该结冰时那样。}}\n# en: A courtyard fountain with coins glinting on the bottom, a student crouching at the rim with a sleeve rolled up, a groundskeeper's rake leaning nearby.\nscene [281e8f] = 庭院喷泉的水底闪着硬币，一名学生卷起袖子蹲在池边，旁边靠着一把园丁的耙子。\n# en: Fish out a few coins for yourself\nopt.left.label [dbe634] = 给自己捞几枚\n# en: {Nobody's watching. Your sleeve is soaked to the shoulder and your pocket is heavier. The sign about the infirmary fund keeps looking at you.|You fish quickly and walk away fast. A first-year's wish is in your pocket now, and the coin is warm.|You've become good at this, which is not a compliment.}\nopt.left.say.1 [618a0c] = {没人看着。你的袖子湿到了肩膀，口袋沉了一些。那块医务室基金的牌子一直盯着你。|你捞得飞快，走得更快。一个一年级生的愿望现在在你口袋里，那枚硬币还是温的。|你已经很擅长这个了，这可不是夸奖。}\n# en: Throw one in yourself\nopt.right.label [b7ed27] = 自己也扔一枚\n# en: {You throw a coin and wish for something you won't admit to. It's the best you've felt all week.|You throw your coin in after the first-year's. They grin at you, as if you're in on something together.|You throw a coin in. It lands on top of an old one of yours, which you take to mean something.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5b0af7] = {你扔了一枚硬币，许了个你不会承认的愿。这是你这一周最好的时刻。|你跟着那个一年级生扔了一枚。{they}冲你咧嘴一笑，好像你们一起知道了什么秘密。|你扔了一枚硬币。它落在你以前扔的一枚上面，你觉得这总有点意思。}\n\n@event courtyard_lost_cat\n# en: Footnote on the Wall\ntitle [bc5a1f] = 墙头上的脚注\n# en: {Footnote, the library cat, is sitting on top of the courtyard wall and refusing to come down. A small crowd has gathered.|Footnote is on the courtyard wall again, being worshipped.|Footnote is on the wall. A few faces in the crowd know you from last time, and move up to make room.} {season == \"spring\": Blossom is blowing over the wall onto his head.|{The library will not reopen until he is found, apparently.|Someone has a sardine. Someone else has a ladder.|A first-year is trying to bribe him with a sock.}}\ntext [564c01] = {图书馆的猫脚注坐在庭院墙头，死活不肯下来。底下围了一小群人。|脚注又在庭院墙头上接受朝拜了。|脚注在墙上。人群里有几张脸记得你上回的事，挪了挪给你让出位置。} {season == \"spring\": 花瓣越过墙头落在他头上。|{据说找不到他，图书馆就不开门。|有人拿着沙丁鱼，有人拿着梯子。|一个一年级生想用一只袜子贿赂他。}}\n# en: A grey library cat sitting on top of a high courtyard wall, a small crowd of students below calling up to it, one holding out a sardine.\nscene [8dbc15] = 一只灰色的图书馆猫坐在高高的庭院墙头，底下一小群学生仰头喊它，其中一人举着一条沙丁鱼。\n# en: Climb up after him\nopt.left.label [7a4523] = 爬上去抓他\n# en: {You climb the ivy, take the cat and come down to applause. Footnote looks as if he arranged the whole thing.|You get him on the second try. He purrs, for the crowd only.|You're up the wall before the crowd can offer the ladder. Footnote walks into your arms as if he's been waiting all afternoon.}\nopt.left.say.1 [37d75a] = {你顺着常春藤爬上去，抱下猫，赢得一片掌声。脚注一副这一切都是他安排的样子。|你第二次才抓住他。他呼噜呼噜的，只是演给观众看。|人群还没递来梯子，你已经上了墙。脚注走进你怀里，好像等了一下午。}\n# en: {The ivy gives. You land in the flowerbed. Footnote walks down the ladder someone else brought.|You get halfway, and Footnote jumps down past you and walks off. The crowd's sympathy is mostly laughter.|You slip at the top. He watches you fall with professional interest, then comes down by himself.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b40ff8] = {常春藤断了。你掉进了花坛。脚注顺着别人搬来的梯子走了下来。|你爬到一半，脚注从你身边跳下去走掉了。人群的同情大多是笑声。|你在墙顶滑了一下。他以专业的兴趣看着你掉下去，然后自己下来了。}\n# en: Wait him out with the crowd\nopt.right.label [6505d4] = 和人群一起等他下来\n# en: {You stand with the crowd and trade news while Footnote ignores you all. He comes down when he's bored, which takes an hour.|You chat with the others until the cat gives up. Someone shares a pastry. Footnote takes half.|You spend the hour in good company under the wall. Footnote comes down and sits on your foot, to make a point.}\nopt.right.say.1 [56d83c] = {你和人群站在一起交换消息，脚注对你们一概不理。他无聊了才下来，花了一个小时。|你和其他人聊天，直到猫放弃。有人分了块点心。脚注拿走了一半。|你在墙下和大家度过了愉快的一小时。脚注下来后坐在你脚上，以示态度。}\n\n@event company_card_widow\n# en: The Widow's Game\ntitle [14d27e] = 寡妇的牌局\n# en: {Tonight's guest is a widow in black lace who has come up from the river towns to play cards with somebody young. She deals before you've sat down.|The widow is back, and asked for you by description: \"the one who loses nicely.\"|The widow again. She has brought her own deck this time, and a flask she says is cough syrup.} The landlady murmurs: she tips the ones who make her laugh, and she always knows who's letting her win.\ntext [39db69] = {今晚的客人是一位穿黑色蕾丝的寡妇，她从河边的镇子上来，想找个年轻人打牌。你还没坐下，她就发好了牌。|那位寡妇又来了，点名要你，原话是：“输得很体面的那个。”|又是那位寡妇。这次她带了自己的牌，还有一个小酒壶，她说里面是止咳糖浆。} 老板娘低声说：能逗她笑的人她就给小费，而且谁在让她赢，她总是一清二楚。\n# en: A tavern's upstairs room after midnight, an elegant old woman in black dealing cards at a small table, a student in a borrowed jacket sitting opposite her.\nscene [ce383a] = 午夜后酒馆楼上的房间，一位优雅的黑衣老妇人在小桌边发牌，一名穿着借来外套的学生坐在她对面。\n# en: Play her honestly, and well\nopt.left.label [82766b] = 老老实实，好好打\n# en: She tips well for a good game; a dull one gets a small tip and an early night.\nopt.left.hint [9b00f0] = 打得好她会多给小费；打得沉闷，小费就少，她也早早散场。\n# en: {You play your best and lose by a hair, and she laughs until she has to take her spectacles off. She tips you twice what you'd have won.|You beat her, once, and she's so pleased she tells you about her late husband's three duels. You go home at four, paid and full of stories.|You keep it close all night. When she leaves she pinches your cheek, presses a fold of notes on you, and says, \"Next time, cheat a little. It's more fun.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [104b7c] = {你使出全力，以毫厘之差输了，她笑得不得不摘下眼镜。给你的小费是你本该赢的两倍。|你赢了她一次，她高兴得给你讲了她亡夫的三场决斗。你凌晨四点才回去，钱包满了，故事也满了。|你整晚都咬得很紧。她走时捏了捏你的脸，塞给你一卷钞票，说：“下次稍微作点弊，更好玩。”}\n# en: {She sees through you in three hands, and is bored by the fourth. A small tip and an early night.|She plays you off the table, kindly, and tips like a woman who has won. It is not much.|You try too hard to amuse her. She pats your hand. \"Never mind, dear,\" she says, and leaves early.}\nopt.left.say.2 [4a9f2c] = {三手牌她就看穿了你，第四手就没了兴致。一点小费，早早收工。|她和蔼地把你打下了牌桌，小费给得像个赢家。没多少。|你太想逗她开心了。她拍拍你的手。“没关系，亲爱的。”她说完就早早走了。}\n# en: Let her win, gracefully\nopt.right.label [4b092b] = 体面地让她赢\n# en: Steady pay, and you know what she thinks of it.\nopt.right.hint [3ce8f3] = 报酬稳定，而你知道她怎么看。\n# en: {You lose with care all night. She knows, and tips you anyway, the way you'd tip a waiter for a bad soup.|You let her win. She thanks you very politely, which stings more than you expected.|You lose gracefully, as the landlady asked. The widow gives you exactly the going rate, and not a coin more.}\nopt.right.say.1 [68e88b] = {你整晚小心翼翼地输。她心知肚明，还是给了小费，就像给端来一碗难喝汤的侍者小费。|你让她赢了。她非常客气地向你道谢，这比你想的更刺人。|你按老板娘的吩咐体面地输了。寡妇给了你不多不少的行价。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_people.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_people.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lyra_toast\n# en: Lyra Cannot Cook\ntitle [96d00a] = 莱拉不会做饭\n# en: Smoke pours from the kitchenette. Lyra Vaneshade, pyromancy prodigy, is holding a piece of bread that is now mostly charcoal. \"I can incinerate a target at forty paces,\" she says. \"I cannot make toast. Don't. Say. Anything.\"\ntext [0b5742] = 浓烟从小厨房里涌出来。莱拉·凡妮希德，火焰术天才，手里拿着一片如今基本是木炭的面包。“我能在四十步外把目标烧成灰，”她说，“我不会烤面包。别。说。话。”\n# en: A dormitory kitchenette full of smoke, a crimson-haired girl holding a blackened slice of toast at arm's length, a fire-charm still sparking in her other hand.\nscene [60f547] = 宿舍小厨房里满是浓烟，一个红发女孩把一片烤焦的面包举得远远的，另一只手上的火焰咒还在噼啪作响。\n# en: Say absolutely nothing\nopt.left.label [274efb] = 一个字也不说\n# en: You open a window, take the bread, and make two perfect slices with a toasting fork. She eats hers in dignified silence. Then she asks for another.\nopt.left.say.1 [daffed] = 你打开窗，接过面包，用烤叉烤出两片完美的吐司。她庄严地默默吃完了自己那片。然后又要了一片。\n# en: Teach her — gently\nopt.right.label [13a212] = 教她——温柔地\n# en: \"Less fire,\" you say. \"No, less. Less than that.\" It takes eleven slices. On the twelfth, she makes perfect golden toast and looks prouder than after any duel.\nopt.right.say.1 [84dd3f] = “火小一点，”你说，“不，再小。比那还小。”烤了十一片。到第十二片，她烤出了完美的金黄吐司，看起来比赢下任何一场决斗都骄傲。\n\n@event lyra_mother_visit\n# en: Lady Vaneshade\ntitle [6393ec] = 凡妮希德夫人\n# en: Lady Vaneshade has come to inspect the academy, and her daughter. She stops in front of you. \"You must be the one my daughter mentions,\" she says. \"Constantly. I'd like to know why.\"\ntext [b4cf90] = 凡妮希德夫人来视察学院，也来视察她的女儿。她在你面前停下。“你一定就是我女儿提起的那个人，”她说，“一直提。我想知道为什么。”\n# en: A tall woman in crimson and black walking through the courtyard like a queen inspecting troops, Lyra two steps behind her, very straight.\nscene [cce72a] = 一位身着红黑长裙的高个女人像女王检阅军队一样走过庭院，莱拉跟在她身后两步远，站得笔直。\n# en: Answer with perfect manners\nopt.left.label [d8a5a2] = 礼数周全地回答\n# en: You are polite, precise and unimpressed. Lady Vaneshade studies you, then says, \"Hm. Adequate.\" Lyra looks at you like you've slain a dragon.\nopt.left.say.1 [304a6e] = 你礼貌、精准，而且不卑不亢。凡妮希德夫人打量了你一番，说：“嗯。还行。”莱拉看你的眼神就像你屠了一条龙。\n# en: You stumble over a title. Lady Vaneshade's eyebrow says everything. Later, Lyra finds you. \"She does that to everyone,\" she says. \"You lasted longer than most.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [04ac64] = 你把一个头衔说结巴了。凡妮希德夫人的眉毛说明了一切。后来莱拉找到你。“她对谁都这样，”她说，“你撑得比大多数人久。”\n# en: Answer honestly\nopt.right.label [d41011] = 老实回答\n# en: \"Because she's brilliant,\" you say, \"and nobody tells her.\" Lady Vaneshade is silent. Lyra is redder than her hair. The visit ends early.\nopt.right.say.1 [756ede] = “因为她很出色，”你说，“而从没有人告诉她。”凡妮希德夫人沉默了。莱拉的脸比她的头发还红。视察提前结束了。\n\n@event lyra_rival_respect\n# en: Respect, Grudgingly\ntitle [7c61e5] = 不情愿的敬意\n# en: After another draw, Lyra drops onto the edge of the ring beside you. \"You're the only person here who makes me better,\" she says, as if confessing a crime. \"I hate it. I don't want it to stop.\"\ntext [f4816d] = 又一次平局之后，莱拉在你身边的场边坐下。“你是这里唯一能让我变得更强的人，”她说，像在坦白罪行，“我讨厌这样。我又不想它停下。”\n# en: Two rivals sitting on the edge of a scorched practice ring, wands laid down between them, sharing a flask of water.\nscene [da0f66] = 两个对手坐在一片焦黑的练习场边上，魔杖放在两人之间，分享着一壶水。\n# en: \"Then let's keep it a rivalry.\"\nopt.left.label [d4b161] = “那就继续当对手。”\n# en: \"Good,\" she says, relieved. \"Rivalry I understand.\" She shoves you, lightly. You shove her back. The rivalry continues, warmer.\nopt.left.say.1 [ceccda] = “好，”她松了一口气，“对手我懂。”她轻轻推了你一把。你推了回去。竞争还在继续，只是更暖了。\n# en: \"Or we could just be friends.\"\nopt.right.label [f2a2ab] = “或者我们可以只是朋友。”\n# en: She considers the word like an unfamiliar spell. \"Friends,\" she says. \"Fine. But I'm still going to beat you.\" You'd be disappointed if she didn't.\nopt.right.say.1 [988725] = 她像琢磨一个陌生咒语一样琢磨着这个词。“朋友，”她说，“行。但我还是要打败你。”她要是不这么说，你反而会失望。\n\n@event tobias_scarecrow\n# en: The Scarecrow Project\ntitle [2e1da2] = 稻草人计划\n# en: Tobias has built a scarecrow for the greenhouse plots and wants to enchant it to scare crows. \"Just a little life,\" he says. \"Just enough to wave. Nothing creepy.\"\ntext [3fc6d7] = 托比亚斯给温室的菜地做了一个稻草人，想给它施魔法来吓乌鸦。“就一点点生命，”他说，“刚好够它挥手。一点都不吓人。”\n# en: A scarecrow in a straw hat waving its arms at crows in a vegetable patch, a big farm boy and a student watching it proudly.\nscene [9ba2d5] = 菜地里一个戴草帽的稻草人朝乌鸦挥舞手臂，一个高大的农家男孩和一名学生自豪地看着它。\n# en: Help enchant it\nopt.left.label [d67ea8] = 帮忙施法\n# en: The scarecrow waves. It also learns to wave hello, and to tip its hat to the headmistress. The crows are terrified. The first-years adore it.\nopt.left.say.1 [0e53a1] = 稻草人挥手了。它还学会了挥手打招呼，以及向院长脱帽致意。乌鸦们吓坏了。一年级生们爱死它了。\n# en: The scarecrow comes to life, looks at you both, and walks off into the forest. You see it sometimes, at dusk, waving.\nopt.left.say.2 [96ecab] = 稻草人活了过来，看了看你们俩，然后走进了森林。你有时会在黄昏看见它，在挥手。\n# en: Suggest a cat instead\nopt.right.label [0efd5c] = 建议改用一只猫\n# en: You borrow the greenhouse cat. The crows leave immediately. Tobias is a little sad about the scarecrow, so you help him make it a hat.\nopt.right.say.1 [481aac] = 你借来了温室的猫。乌鸦立刻飞走了。托比亚斯为稻草人有点难过，于是你帮他给它做了一顶帽子。\n\n@event tobias_dance_lessons\n# en: Two Left Feet\ntitle [ed37de] = 两只左脚\n# en: Tobias has pushed the desks against the walls of an empty classroom. \"The Ball's {month == 4: tonight|next month},\" he says, mortified. \"I've been practising with a broom. The broom has complained.\"\ntext [71b9e3] = 托比亚斯把一间空教室的桌子都推到了墙边。“舞会{month == 4: 就在今晚|就在下个月}，”他窘得要命，“我一直在和一把扫帚练习。扫帚投诉了。”\n# en: An empty classroom with desks pushed to the walls, a big boy counting steps aloud, a broom propped against the wall as his former partner.\nscene [592891] = 一间空教室，桌子推到墙边，一个高大的男孩大声数着舞步，一把扫帚靠在墙边——他的前舞伴。\n# en: Teach him to waltz\nopt.left.label [97e7a3] = 教他跳华尔兹\n# en: One-two-three, one-two-three. He steps on you exactly fifty times. On the fifty-first bar, something clicks, and for a moment he's graceful. He looks astonished.\nopt.left.say.1 [4f58a0] = 一二三，一二三。他不多不少踩了你五十脚。到第五十一个小节，突然开了窍，有那么一会儿，他跳得很优雅。他自己都惊呆了。\n# en: Tell him nobody cares about footwork\nopt.right.label [22afe9] = 告诉他没人在乎舞步\n# en: \"Just enjoy it,\" you say. He thinks about this, nods, and dances like a delighted bear. It's terrible. It's wonderful.\nopt.right.say.1 [4eca0a] = “开心就好，”你说。他想了想，点点头，然后像一头快乐的熊一样跳了起来。跳得糟透了。也美妙极了。\n\n@event tobias_storm_greenhouse\n# en: Storm Over the Greenhouses\ntitle [3fabc5] = 温室上空的风暴\n# en: A storm hits at midnight. Glass is cracking in the greenhouses. Tobias is out there in his nightshirt, trying to hold a door shut against the wind with his whole body. \"The moonflowers!\" he shouts. \"Eleven years!\"\ntext [fb15f6] = 午夜，暴风雨来袭。温室的玻璃在开裂。托比亚斯穿着睡衣站在外面，用整个身体顶着门不让风吹开。“月光花！”他大喊，“十一年了！”\n# en: Rain lashing a glass greenhouse at night, panes cracking, a big boy holding a door shut against the wind while plants rattle inside.\nscene [f9b9b9] = 夜里，暴雨抽打着玻璃温室，窗格开裂，一个高大的男孩顶着狂风抵住门，里面的植物哗哗作响。\n# en: Run out and help\nopt.left.label [007212] = 冲出去帮忙\n# en: You hold the panes together with every shielding charm you know until dawn. Every moonflower survives. Tobias hugs you, soaked, laughing, half crying.\nopt.left.say.1 [d599ab] = 你用你会的每一个护盾咒把玻璃撑住，一直撑到黎明。每一朵月光花都活了下来。托比亚斯浑身湿透地抱住你，又笑又像要哭。\n# en: Fetch Old Maud\nopt.right.label [263929] = 去叫老莫德\n# en: Maud arrives in her dressing gown, mutters three words at the sky, and the wind politely goes elsewhere. Tobias stares at her. \"Teach me that,\" he says.\nopt.right.say.1 [efe395] = 莫德穿着睡袍赶来，对着天空嘟囔了三个词，风就礼貌地去了别处。托比亚斯盯着她。“教我这个，”他说。\n# en: Grow a tree to shelter the glass\nopt.up.label [e62232] = 种棵树挡住玻璃\n# en: You kneel in the mud and a tree comes up between the wind and the greenhouse, and spreads its branches over the glass. The storm breaks on it all night. In the morning Tobias sits under it with you and doesn't say anything at all.\nopt.up.say.1 [21ffb9] = 你跪进泥里，一棵树在风和温室之间长了起来，把枝条撑在玻璃上方。风暴在它身上撞了一整夜。早上托比亚斯和你一起坐在树下，一句话也没说。\n\n@event seraphine_lost_day\n# en: Seraphine Is Tomorrow\ntitle [d55aad] = 瑟拉芬活在明天\n# en: Seraphine is sitting in an empty classroom, waiting for a lesson that's happening tomorrow. \"I've slipped,\" she says calmly. \"I'm a day ahead. It happens. I need someone to remind me what today is.\"\ntext [2839c8] = 瑟拉芬坐在一间空教室里，等一堂明天才上的课。“我滑过头了，”她平静地说，“我提前了一天。这种事会发生。我需要有人提醒我今天是哪天。”\n# en: A girl sitting at the wrong desk in an empty classroom, a teacup in her hands, the calendar on the wall showing the next day's date.\nscene [5de877] = 一个女孩坐在空教室里不该坐的座位上，手捧茶杯，墙上的日历显示着第二天的日期。\n# en: Talk her back into today\nopt.left.label [cd1172] = 把她拉回今天\n# en: You tell her what you had for breakfast, what the weather's like, what Thorne said this morning. Slowly her eyes stop looking through you. \"Oh,\" she says. \"Hello. Today.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [de7259] = 你告诉她你早饭吃了什么、天气怎么样、索恩今早说了什么。她的目光慢慢不再穿过你。“哦，”她说，“你好。今天。”\n# en: Ask her what tomorrow holds\nopt.right.label [b4a8a2] = 问她明天会发生什么\n# en: \"There's a surprise quiz in every class,\" she says. \"They planned it together. Question three is always a trick.\" It is, every time. You get them all right.\nopt.right.say.1 [117549] = “每门课都会有突击小测，”她说，“他们串通好的。第三题永远是陷阱。”每次都是。你全答对了。\n# en: \"It rains,\" she says. \"You get wet. You'll be fine.\" You are not fine. You get very wet.\nopt.right.say.2 [111bc0] = “会下雨，”她说，“你会淋湿。你会没事的。”你并没事。你淋得透湿。\n\n@event seraphine_painting\n# en: A Painting of Somewhere Else\ntitle [e371b7] = 别处的风景\n# en: Seraphine has painted a small cottage by the sea, with a crooked chimney and a blue door. \"It's yours,\" she says. \"In twelve years. I thought you should see it now. In case you want a different door.\"\ntext [63e289] = 瑟拉芬画了一座海边的小屋，烟囱歪歪的，门是蓝色的。“这是你的，”她说，“十二年后。我觉得你应该现在就看看。万一你想要一扇不一样的门。”\n# en: A small canvas on an easel showing a stranger's cottage by the sea, a girl with paint on her fingers watching someone look at it.\nscene [88e8e6] = 画架上一幅小画布，画着一座陌生的海边小屋，一个手指沾着颜料的女孩看着别人端详它。\n# en: Keep the painting\nopt.left.label [93b1f9] = 收下这幅画\n# en: You hang it over your bed. Some nights you swear the smoke from the chimney moves. You decide, privately, that you like the blue door.\nopt.left.say.1 [c384bc] = 你把它挂在床头。有些夜晚，你发誓烟囱里的烟在动。你暗自决定，你喜欢那扇蓝色的门。\n# en: You hang it over your bed. Some mornings the door is a different colour, and you lie there wondering which one you chose.\nopt.left.say.2 [7a0c78] = 你把它挂在床头。有些早晨，那扇门换了颜色，你躺在床上琢磨自己选的是哪一扇。\n# en: Ask her to paint her own future\nopt.right.label [ab2144] = 请她画画自己的未来\n# en: She looks at the blank canvas for a long time. \"I never can,\" she says. \"Seers can't see themselves.\" Then, quietly: \"Unless someone's standing next to us.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [685acd] = 她对着空白的画布看了很久。“我画不出来，”她说，“先知看不见自己。”然后，轻声地：“除非有人站在我们身边。”\n\n@event seraphine_mother\n# en: The Oracle's Summons\ntitle [ef26c8] = 神谕的召唤\n# en: Seraphine's mother, the Oracle of Vell, wants her home to take up the family seat. \"I'd spend my life telling kings what they want to hear,\" Seraphine says. \"But she's already seen me say yes. She always sees me say yes.\"\ntext [bdd88c] = 瑟拉芬的母亲——维尔神谕——要她回家继承家族的席位。“我这辈子都得对国王们说他们想听的话，”瑟拉芬说，“可她已经看见我答应了。她总是看见我答应。”\n# en: A girl holding a letter that glows faintly silver, standing at the top of a tower stair, looking down as if about to fall or fly.\nscene [02abdd] = 一个女孩站在塔楼楼梯顶端，手握一封泛着微弱银光的信，俯视下方，仿佛就要坠落或飞起。\n# en: \"Then surprise her.\"\nopt.left.label [7766b6] = “那就给她个惊喜。”\n# en: Seraphine writes back: no. For a whole day she's terrified, and so are you. Then she's giddy. \"I didn't see that coming,\" she keeps saying. \"I didn't see it coming!\"\nopt.left.say.1 [913a47] = 瑟拉芬回信：不。整整一天她都吓坏了，你也一样。然后她兴奋得晕乎乎的。“我没预见到，”她一遍遍地说，“我没预见到！”\n# en: \"Maybe she's right.\"\nopt.right.label [0cb3e9] = “也许她是对的。”\n# en: She nods slowly. She doesn't go — not yet. But for a long while she stops telling you her visions, and you miss them more than you expected.\nopt.right.say.1 [7f4282] = 她慢慢点了点头。她没有回去——暂时还没有。但有很长一段时间，她不再告诉你她的预见了，而你比自己想象的更想念那些预见。\n\n@event corvin_confiscation_vault\n# en: The Confiscation Vault\ntitle [404008] = 没收品库\n# en: \"The confiscation vault,\" Corvin breathes. \"Three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student. My grandfather's lucky deck is in there. I just want it back. Lookout?\"\ntext [724ca5] = “没收品库，”科尔文屏息道，“三百年来教职员从学生手里没收的所有东西。我祖父的幸运纸牌就在里面。我只想把它拿回来。帮我望风？”\n# en: A dark vault crammed with confiscated student contraband — fanged frisbees, cursed dice, a goblet of endless soup — two figures with a lantern at the door.\nscene [efcf9c] = 一间塞满学生违禁品的黑暗库房——长着獠牙的飞盘、被诅咒的骰子、一只永远盛满汤的高脚杯——门口两个提着灯笼的身影。\n# en: Keep lookout\nopt.left.label [89becd] = 帮他望风\n# en: He comes out with the deck and a handful of confiscated coins \"for your trouble\". The deck is, it turns out, genuinely lucky. He wins at cards for a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [2b5759] = 他拿着纸牌出来了，还有一把没收来的硬币，“算你的辛苦费”。结果那副牌真的很幸运。他打牌赢了一个月。\n# en: The vault alarm is a very loud goblin. You both spend a week cataloguing contraband — which Corvin considers a victory.\nopt.left.say.2 [4c5953] = 库房的警报是一只嗓门非常大的地精。你们俩花了一星期给违禁品编目——科尔文认为这算胜利。\n# en: Talk him out of it\nopt.right.label [3af407] = 劝他打消念头\n# en: You convince him to just ask Thorne. Thorne, astonishingly, gives it back. \"Your grandfather cheated me with it in '41,\" he says. \"Tell him I said hello.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [df43d8] = 你说服他直接去问索恩。令人震惊的是，索恩把牌还给了他。“你祖父四一年用它出老千坑过我，”他说，“替我向他问好。”\n# en: He goes anyway, alone. He comes back with the deck and a black eye from the goblin. Worth it, he says.\nopt.right.say.2 [8846c7] = 他还是去了，一个人。他带着纸牌和被地精打出的一只黑眼圈回来了。他说值了。\n\n@event corvin_brother\n# en: Corvin's Brother\ntitle [b626d0] = 科尔文的哥哥\n# en: There's a rough-looking man at the gates, and Corvin has gone very still. \"My brother,\" he says. \"He wants money. He always wants money. If I don't pay, he tells the headmistress where I learned to pick locks.\"\ntext [45c115] = 校门口站着一个看起来很粗野的男人，科尔文整个人僵住了。“我哥哥，”他说，“他要钱。他永远都要钱。我要是不给，他就去告诉院长我是在哪儿学会撬锁的。”\n# en: A rough-looking young man leaning on the academy gate, cap pulled low, Corvin facing him with his hands in his pockets.\nscene [a1863b] = 一个看起来粗野的年轻人倚在学院大门上，帽子压得很低，科尔文双手插兜面对着他。\n# en: Help Corvin face him down\nopt.left.label [cb6fb2] = 帮科尔文硬顶回去\n# en: You stand beside Corvin while he says no — the first time, he tells you later, that he's ever said it. His brother leaves. Corvin sits down on the steps and laughs and shakes.\nopt.left.say.1 [8f14e6] = 你站在科尔文身边，听他说“不”——他后来告诉你，这是他第一次说出这个字。他哥哥走了。科尔文在台阶上坐下，笑着，浑身发抖。\n# en: It turns ugly. In the end you empty your purse into his hand, just to make him leave. Corvin presses every coin back into yours that evening, with a strange new seriousness.\nopt.left.say.2 [27a527] = 场面变得很难看。最后你把钱袋倒空塞给他，只为让他走人。当天晚上，科尔文把每一枚硬币都塞回了你手里，带着一种陌生的郑重。\n# en: Stay out of family business\nopt.right.label [fef823] = 不掺和别人的家事\n# en: Corvin pays. You see him counting out every coin he has. He's quiet for a long while after.\nopt.right.say.1 [9f72cb] = 科尔文付了钱。你看见他把身上每一枚硬币都数了出来。之后他沉默了很久。\n\n@event corvin_cards\n# en: How to Cheat at Cards\ntitle [1de29d] = 怎样打牌出老千\n# en: \"Rule one: never cheat,\" says Corvin, shuffling so fast the cards blur. \"Rule two: if you're going to cheat, never get caught. Rule three: I'm about to teach you how they cheat, so you never lose to them.\"\ntext [e191e5] = “规则一：永远不要出千，”科尔文说着洗牌，快得纸牌都成了虚影，“规则二：如果要出千，永远别被抓到。规则三：我现在要教你他们是怎么出千的，这样你就永远不会输给他们。”\n# en: A candlelit table in a corner of the common room, a boy fanning a deck of cards with a flourish, a student watching his hands very closely.\nscene [373538] = 公共休息室角落一张烛光摇曳的桌子，一个男孩花哨地把一副牌摊成扇形，一名学生紧盯着他的手。\n# en: Learn the tricks\nopt.left.label [f47312] = 学会这些把戏\n# en: By midnight you can spot a marked card, a bottom deal and a mirror charm. You will never be fleeced at Wizard's Hand again.\nopt.left.say.1 [4eb4c5] = 到午夜，你已经能识破记号牌、底牌发牌和镜像咒。你再也不会在“巫师之手”牌局上被人宰了。\n# en: Learn to play honestly — and well\nopt.right.label [e8fdeb] = 学会诚实地打——还要打得好\n# en: He teaches you odds and nerve instead. That weekend you win eight crowns at the common-room game without cheating. Corvin is weirdly proud.\nopt.right.say.1 [ff72c2] = 他转而教你算概率和稳住心态。那个周末，你在休息室牌局上一把千都没出就赢了八克朗。科尔文骄傲得有点莫名其妙。\n# en: He teaches you odds and nerve instead. That weekend the odds take three crowns off you. \"Also a lesson,\" says Corvin.\nopt.right.say.2 [27b9a8] = 他改教你概率和胆量。那个周末，概率从你身上拿走了三克朗。“这也是一课，”科尔文说。\n# en: Near the end he shuffles the slow way, the rainy-Sunday way, and you say you know whose shuffle it is. He doesn't answer. He deals you in anyway, for good.\nopt.right.say.3 [8a25a0] = 快结束时，他用那种慢慢的、下雨的星期天的方式洗牌，你说你知道这是谁的洗法。他没回答。但他还是给你发了牌，从此一直算你一份。\n\n@event mir_ornithopter\n# en: The Test Flight\ntitle [5ac4c6] = 试飞\n# en: Mir has built a flying machine with canvas wings and seven propellers. It is balanced on the edge of the north tower. \"Brooms are so last century,\" they say, strapping in. \"I need someone on the rope. Probably. Possibly.\"\ntext [800a69] = 米尔造了一台飞行器，有帆布翅膀和七个螺旋桨，正架在北塔的边缘。“扫帚太上个世纪了，”TA一边系安全带一边说，“我需要有人拉绳子。大概吧。也许。”\n# en: A rickety winged contraption balanced on the edge of a tower roof, a goggled artificer strapping into it, a friend holding a rope.\nscene [846151] = 一台摇摇晃晃的带翼装置架在塔顶边缘，一个戴护目镜的奇械师正把自己绑进去，一个朋友抓着一根绳子。\n# en: Hold the rope\nopt.left.label [710f01] = 拉住绳子\n# en: The machine flies — wobbly, loud, glorious — twice around the tower before landing in the lake. Mir surfaces whooping. You're both legends by dinner.\nopt.left.say.1 [15a88f] = 机器飞起来了——摇摇晃晃、吵吵闹闹、辉煌灿烂——绕塔飞了两圈，然后落进了湖里。米尔欢呼着冒出水面。到晚饭时，你们俩都成了传奇。\n# en: The rope wins. You are dragged eight feet along the roof before letting go. The machine lands in a tree. Mir waves from it, delighted.\nopt.left.say.2 [a4ec4a] = 绳子赢了。你被拖着在屋顶上滑了八英尺才松手。机器落在了一棵树上。米尔在树上开心地挥手。\n# en: Insist on a safety net\nopt.right.label [31e7de] = 坚持要装安全网\n# en: You spend a day building a net. The machine flies four feet and drops into it. \"Data!\" says Mir happily. Nobody dies. It's a good day.\nopt.right.say.1 [8acffd] = 你花了一天搭网。机器飞了四英尺，掉进了网里。“数据！”米尔开心地说。没人丧命。是美好的一天。\n\n@event mir_lab_fire\n# en: Fire in the Workshop\ntitle [d8f498] = 工坊失火\n# en: The workshop is on fire. Properly this time. Mir is in the doorway, coughing — and trying to go back in. \"My notebooks! Three years of notes!\"\ntext [b4fc7c] = 工坊着火了。这次是真的着火了。米尔站在门口咳嗽着——还想往里冲。“我的笔记本！三年的笔记！”\n# en: A workshop full of smoke and orange light, notebooks scattered across a burning bench, a figure in goggles coughing in the doorway.\nscene [1abf1a] = 满是浓烟和橙色火光的工坊，笔记本散落在燃烧的工作台上，一个戴护目镜的身影在门口咳嗽。\n# en: Grab the notebooks\nopt.left.label [6162da] = 去抢笔记本\n# en: You come out singed, smoking, with an armful of notebooks. Mir stares at you, then at the notebooks, then bursts into tears and hugs you, both of you coughing.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d9fd7] = 你出来时衣服烧焦、浑身冒烟，怀里抱着一摞笔记本。米尔盯着你，又盯着笔记本，然后哭着抱住了你，你们俩都在咳嗽。\n# en: You get halfway before the smoke drives you back. You save one notebook. Mir says it's the most important one. You both know it isn't. They mean it anyway.\nopt.left.say.2 [338a3e] = 你冲到一半就被浓烟逼了回来。你救出了一本。米尔说那是最重要的一本。你们都知道不是。但TA是真心的。\n# en: Drag Mir away\nopt.right.label [9bf39b] = 把米尔拖走\n# en: You haul Mir back from the door. The notebooks burn. Mir is silent for a week. Then they start a new notebook. The first page just says: \"Thank you.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [1a7747] = 你把米尔从门口拽了回来。笔记本烧光了。米尔沉默了一个星期。然后TA开始了一本新笔记。第一页只写着：“谢谢你。”\n# en: The next morning you find them soldering on the corridor floor outside the burnt door, and bring them a cushion, and tea.\nopt.right.say.2 [5b33fb] = 第二天早上，你发现 TA 坐在烧焦的门外的走廊地板上焊东西，于是给 TA 带去了一个垫子，还有茶。\n# en: Freeze the fire out\nopt.up.label [ff2903] = 把火冻熄\n# en: You throw frost at the doorway until the flames grey over and give up with a hiss. The notebooks are wet, cold and whole. Mir hugs them, then you, then the notebooks again, shivering.\nopt.up.say.1 [442a03] = 你朝门口一阵阵甩出白霜，直到火焰蒙上一层灰，嘶的一声认了输。笔记本又湿又冷，但完好无损。米尔抱住笔记本，又抱住你，再抱住笔记本，浑身发抖。\n\n@event mir_name_day\n# en: Mir's Name Day\ntitle [fc2a16] = 米尔的命名日\n# en: It's Mir's name day. They mention it casually, as if it doesn't matter. It clearly matters. Nobody else seems to know.\ntext [e4d87b] = 今天是米尔的命名日。TA随口提了一句，好像无所谓似的。显然很有所谓。其他人似乎都不知道。\n# en: A cluttered workshop decorated with paper chains and one small cake with a single gear-shaped candle, an artificer looking embarrassed and pleased.\nscene [7e06f4] = 一间杂乱的工坊挂着纸链装饰，一个小蛋糕上插着一根齿轮形状的蜡烛，奇械师看起来又难为情又开心。\n# en: Throw a surprise party\nopt.left.label [7bc69f] = 办一场惊喜派对\n# en: Six friends, one cake and a banner you painted badly. Mir walks in, stops, and has to sit down. \"Nobody's ever...\" they start, and can't finish.\nopt.left.say.1 [a115e8] = 六个朋友、一个蛋糕，还有一条你画得很烂的横幅。米尔走进来，愣住了，不得不坐下。“从来没有人……”TA开口，却说不下去。\n# en: Give them a handmade gift\nopt.right.label [36112f] = 送TA一件手工礼物\n# en: A little brass key that fits nothing. \"For whatever you build next,\" you say. Mir wears it on a chain, every day, for years.\nopt.right.say.1 [400f81] = 一把什么锁也开不了的小铜钥匙。“送给你接下来要造的东西，”你说。米尔把它挂在链子上，天天戴着，戴了好多年。\n\n@event nessa_headmistress_interview\n# en: The Impossible Interview\ntitle [4748ca] = 不可能的采访\n# en: \"Nobody has interviewed the headmistress in twenty years,\" Nessa says. \"But she likes you. Probably. Get me in the door and I'll name my firstborn after you.\"\ntext [f4a9d2] = “二十年来没人采访过院长，”内莎说，“但她喜欢你。大概吧。帮我进那扇门，我就用你的名字给我的头一个孩子取名。”\n# en: A newspaper editor and a student waiting outside the headmistress's tower door, the editor clutching a list of questions three pages long.\nscene [af2317] = 一位报社编辑和一名学生等在院长塔楼门外，编辑手里攥着一张三页长的问题清单。\n# en: Ask the headmistress\nopt.left.label [733de3] = 去问院长\n# en: The headmistress agrees to ten minutes. She gives Nessa forty, and gives you a look that says you have spent a favour. The interview runs on the front page and sells out three printings.\nopt.left.say.1 [825648] = 院长答应给十分钟。结果给了内莎四十分钟，还给了你一个眼神，意思是你已经用掉了一次人情。采访登上头版，加印了三次都卖光了。\n# en: \"No,\" says the headmistress, pleasantly. \"But tell Miss Quill her piece on the kitchens was excellent.\" Nessa frames the rejection. The headmistress is a shade cooler with you in the corridors after that.\nopt.left.say.2 [0b6722] = “不行，”院长和颜悦色地说，“不过请转告奎尔小姐，她写厨房的那篇非常出色。”内莎把这次拒绝裱了起来。从那以后，院长在走廊里对你冷淡了几分。\n# en: Suggest someone overlooked instead\nopt.right.label [d4c0f5] = 建议改采访一位被忽视的人\n# en: Nessa interviews the night porter instead. It's the most-read piece of the year. Half the academy cries over breakfast.\nopt.right.say.1 [c59390] = 内莎改为采访了守夜的门房。那成了全年阅读量最高的文章。半个学院的人在早餐时哭了。\n\n@event nessa_typo\n# en: A Catastrophic Typo\ntitle [f9820b] = 灾难性的错字\n# en: The Ledger has gone to print. In the profile of Professor Thorne, a single wrong letter has turned \"Thorne: Still Lively\" into \"Thorne: Still Lovely\". By breakfast the whole academy will be calling the most frightening man in the castle lovely. Five hundred copies.\ntext [8e82c5] = 《流明纪事报》已经付印。在索恩教授的人物专访里，一个错字把“索恩：依然矍铄”变成了“索恩：依然可爱”。到早饭时，全学院都会管城堡里最吓人的那个人叫“可爱”了。五百份。\n# en: A newspaper with a single word circled in red ink, an editor with her head in her hands, a stack of papers still warm from the press.\nscene [224cf5] = 一份报纸上有一个词被红墨水圈了出来，编辑双手抱头，一摞刚印好的报纸还带着余温。\n# en: Help her fix every copy overnight\nopt.left.label [5d1986] = 帮她连夜改好每一份\n# en: You correct five hundred copies with a scraping charm and a lot of tea. At dawn, Nessa falls asleep on the press. Thorne never knows.\nopt.left.say.1 [63580e] = 你用刮除咒和大量的茶改好了五百份。黎明时，内莎趴在印刷机上睡着了。索恩永远不会知道。\n# en: At dawn you ask whether this was one of her planted errors. \"No,\" she says, horrified, and then laughs until she cries.\nopt.left.say.2 [295140] = 天亮时你问她，这是不是她故意埋的错误之一。“不是！”她惊恐地说，然后笑到流泪。\n# en: Tell her to run a correction and own it\nopt.right.label [f52406] = 让她登更正启事，坦然认错\n# en: The correction is gracious. Thorne, astonishingly, cuts it out and pins it to his door. \"Precision,\" he says in class, \"is a virtue. So is apology.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [02e473] = 更正启事写得很得体。令人震惊的是，索恩把它剪下来钉在了自己门上。“精确，”他在课上说，“是一种美德。道歉也是。”\n\n@event nessa_burnout\n# en: The Editor Sleeps\ntitle [7126ab] = 编辑睡着了\n# en: Nessa hasn't slept properly in a week. She's asleep at her desk now, and her quill — unsupervised — is still writing next week's paper. It has started inventing news.\ntext [b075ee] = 内莎已经一个星期没好好睡觉了。此刻她趴在桌上睡着了，而她的羽毛笔——无人看管——还在写下周的报纸。它已经开始编造新闻了。\n# en: A girl asleep at a desk in a newspaper office, her self-writing quill still scratching on its own, the candle burned down to a stub.\nscene [6ef002] = 报社办公室里一个女孩趴在桌上睡着了，她的自动羽毛笔还在自己沙沙书写，蜡烛已经烧成了一小截。\n# en: Put her to bed and run the paper yourself\nopt.left.label [9b9579] = 送她上床，你来办报\n# en: You edit, typeset and print one issue, and miss a day of classes doing it. It's adequate. When Nessa wakes she reads it, winces at your headlines, and hugs you for a long time.\nopt.left.say.1 [784bde] = 你编辑、排版、印出了一期，为此缺了一天的课。还过得去。内莎醒来读了，对你起的标题皱了皱眉，然后抱了你很久。\n# en: Wake her and make her rest\nopt.right.label [c44266] = 叫醒她，逼她休息\n# en: She argues for ten minutes, then sleeps for fourteen hours. The quill's invented news about a dragon in the kitchens turns out to be true.\nopt.right.say.1 [46fb0e] = 她争了十分钟，然后睡了十四个小时。羽毛笔编造的“厨房里有条龙”的新闻，结果是真的。\n\n@event kael_northern_lights\n# en: The Lights of the North\ntitle [8eda52] = 北方之光\n# en: Kael wakes you at midnight. \"Come,\" he says. On the hill above the lake, the sky is moving — green and violet ribbons, the lights of his home, here, for him. \"I called them,\" he says. \"I wanted you to see.\"\ntext [874ab7] = 凯尔在午夜叫醒了你。“来，”他说。湖上方的山坡上，天空在流动——绿色与紫色的光带，那是他家乡的极光，在这里，为他而来。“是我召来的，”他说，“我想让你看看。”\n# en: Two figures on a frozen hillside under ribbons of green and violet light, one pointing upward, frost forming a circle around them.\nscene [e2a410] = 两个身影站在结冰的山坡上，头顶是绿紫色的光带，其中一人指向天空，霜在他们周围结成一个圆。\n# en: Watch in silence\nopt.left.label [3bfadc] = 静静地看\n# en: You watch until the lights fade. Neither of you speaks. On the walk down, he takes your hand without seeming to notice he's done it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f93df4] = 你们一直看到极光消散。谁也没说话。下山时，他牵起了你的手，似乎自己都没察觉。\n# en: Ask him about home\nopt.right.label [946458] = 问他关于家乡的事\n# en: He tells you about ice palaces and singing glaciers and a mother who never once said she was proud. Then he says: \"This place is more home now.\" He means the hill. He means you.\nopt.right.say.1 [d63dfe] = 他给你讲冰之宫殿、会唱歌的冰川，还有一位从没说过一次为他骄傲的母亲。然后他说：“现在这里更像家了。”他说的是这座山坡。他说的是你。\n\n@event kael_fever\n# en: Kael Burns\ntitle [19ecba] = 凯尔在发烧\n# en: Kael has a fever — and for someone made of frost, a fever is dangerous. The infirmary's warming charms are making it worse. Sister Amaranth looks frightened. \"He needs cold,\" she says. \"Real cold. I don't know where to get it.\"\ntext [0e9394] = 凯尔发烧了——对一个由霜构成的人来说，发烧很危险。医务室的温暖咒只会让情况更糟。阿玛兰斯修女看起来很害怕。“他需要寒冷，”她说，“真正的寒冷。我不知道去哪儿弄。”\n# en: A pale boy in a narrow bed, sweating, frost forming and melting on the pillow around his head in a constant cycle.\nscene [44bb31] = 一张窄床上躺着一个苍白的男孩，满身是汗，他头边枕头上的霜不断凝结又融化。\n# en: Bring him snow from the mountain\nopt.left.label [395e96] = 去山上给他取雪\n# en: You fly to the peaks on a borrowed broom and come back with a sack of ancient snow. By morning his fever breaks. He doesn't let go of your sleeve all day.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f1fbf] = 你骑着借来的扫帚飞上山巅，带回一麻袋远古积雪。到早上，他的烧退了。他一整天都没松开你的袖子。\n# en: The mountain wins. You come back half-frozen and empty-handed. Kael recovers anyway, slowly — and visits you in the next bed.\nopt.left.say.2 [c8178a] = 大山赢了。你冻得半死、两手空空地回来。凯尔还是慢慢好了起来——还去隔壁病床探望了你。\n# en: Later you remember that his hands were warm when you left, for the first time since you've known him, and how much that frightened you.\nopt.left.say.3 [4730df] = 后来你还记得，你离开时他的手是热的，自你认识他以来第一次，那让你多么害怕。\n# en: Send word to Ilse\nopt.right.label [18fc37] = 给伊尔泽捎信\n# en: Ilse arrives in a whirl of snow, puts her hand on his forehead and says something sharp in Northern. The fever breaks. Kael stares at his sister for a long time.\nopt.right.say.1 [8cf203] = 伊尔泽裹着一阵飞雪赶到，把手放在他额头上，用北境语厉声说了句什么。烧退了。凯尔盯着他的妹妹看了很久。\n# en: You can't find her. Kael recovers slowly, on his own, the way he's always done everything.\nopt.right.say.2 [efbddf] = 你找不到她。凯尔靠自己慢慢好了起来，就像他一向独自扛下所有事那样。\n\n@event kael_letter_home\n# en: A Letter He Won't Send\ntitle [ff94b6] = 一封他不会寄出的信\n# en: Kael is writing a letter. He's rewritten the first line eleven times. \"To my mother,\" he says. \"I'm telling her I won't come home. I won't open the door. I'm telling her I've found...\" He stops.\ntext [71254e] = 凯尔在写信。第一行他已经重写了十一遍。“写给我母亲，”他说，“告诉她我不会回家了。我不会打开那扇门。告诉她我找到了……”他停住了。\n# en: A boy at a library desk, a letter half-written in sharp Northern script, a candle beside it, ink frozen in the pen.\nscene [fc0e7c] = 一个男孩坐在图书馆的书桌前，一封用凌厉的北境字体写了一半的信，旁边一支蜡烛，笔里的墨水冻住了。\n# en: \"Found what?\"\nopt.left.label [10295e] = “找到了什么？”\n# en: He looks at you for a long time. Then he writes the last word of the sentence without showing you, seals it, and sends it. He's lighter for days.\nopt.left.say.1 [cdad8b] = 他看了你很久。然后他没让你看，写下了那句话的最后一个词，封好信，寄了出去。之后好几天他都轻松了许多。\n# en: Help him find the words\nopt.right.label [a0a115] = 帮他找到合适的话\n# en: You sit together until the candle's gone. The letter, when it's finished, is short. It doesn't apologise once. He sends it at dawn.\nopt.right.say.1 [c25f80] = 你们一起坐到蜡烛燃尽。写完的信很短，一次也没道歉。他在黎明时寄了出去。\n\n@event bram_father_visit\n# en: Bram's Father\ntitle [26834d] = 布拉姆的父亲\n# en: A tall man in an old head-boy's sash is speaking to Bram in the entrance hall, quietly and terribly. Bram has shrunk two sizes. \"...never amount to anything,\" the man is saying.\ntext [1a56a1] = 一个披着旧式男生代表绶带的高个男人在门厅里和布拉姆说话，声音很轻，却很可怕。布拉姆缩小了两圈。“……永远不会有出息，”那人说。\n# en: A towering grey-haired man in an old head-boy's sash speaking down to a red-faced prefect in the entrance hall, students hurrying past pretending not to look.\nscene [d8766d] = 门厅里，一个身材高大、头发灰白、披着旧式男生代表绶带的男人居高临下地对一个满脸通红的级长说话，学生们匆匆走过，假装没在看。\n# en: Interrupt — say something good\nopt.left.label [720c7c] = 插话——说些好话\n# en: You tell his father {seen(bram_turns): about the night at the boathouse|what Bram is like when nobody's keeping score}. The man falls silent. Bram looks at you like you've handed him a sword.\nopt.left.say.1 [f142e1] = 你跟他父亲讲了{seen(bram_turns): 船屋那晚的事|没人记分的时候布拉姆是什么样的人}。那人沉默了。布拉姆看你的眼神，就像你递给了他一把剑。\n# en: His father looks through you as if you're furniture. But afterwards, Bram finds you and says, roughly, \"Nobody's ever stood there before. Thanks.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [bb9dcb] = 他父亲看你就像看一件家具。但事后布拉姆找到你，粗声粗气地说：“以前从没有人站在那儿过。谢了。”\n# en: Find Bram afterwards\nopt.right.label [46a529] = 事后去找布拉姆\n# en: He's behind the stables, again. You sit on the bucket next to his. You don't talk. After a while he says, \"Same time next year, probably,\" and almost laughs.\nopt.right.say.1 [7e1535] = 他又在马厩后面。你在他旁边的水桶上坐下。你们不说话。过了一会儿他说：“明年大概还是这个时候，”然后差点笑了出来。\n# en: On the way back you take him the long way round, so you don't pass the corridor of head boys. He notices, and says nothing, which from Bram is thanks.\nopt.right.say.2 [ed9492] = 回去的路上你带他绕了远路，避开那条挂着历届男生学生会主席画像的走廊。他注意到了，什么也没说，这在布拉姆那里就是谢谢。\n\n@event bram_cheating\n# en: Bram Cheats\ntitle [cf8d51] = 布拉姆作弊\n# en: In the exam hall, two desks away, you see Bram Hollis — prefect, enforcer of rules — slip a tiny scroll out of his sleeve. He sees you seeing him. His face goes white.\ntext [5bf337] = 考场上，隔着两张桌子，你看见布拉姆·霍利斯——级长、校规的执行者——从袖子里抽出一卷小纸条。他看见你看见了他。他的脸一下子白了。\n# en: A prefect in an exam hall slipping a tiny scroll out of his sleeve, a student two desks away watching.\nscene [6230cf] = 考场里一位级长正从袖子里抽出一卷小纸条，隔两张桌子的一名学生看在眼里。\n# en: Report him\nopt.left.label [794001] = 举报他\n# en: Bram loses his sash for the rest of the year. Some students cheer. You don't feel like cheering. Neither, later, does he.\nopt.left.say.1 [bf4a08] = 布拉姆被摘掉了绶带，直到学年结束。有些学生欢呼。你并不想欢呼。后来，他也一样。\n# en: Say nothing — and let him know you saw\nopt.right.label [5c554c] = 什么也不说——但让他知道你看见了\n# en: After the exam you just look at him. He nods, once. You never mention it. But his demerits stop, for you and for everyone you know.\nopt.right.say.1 [88ddd1] = 考完后你只是看着他。他点了一下头。你从没提起这件事。但他的记过停了——对你，也对你认识的每一个人。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_social.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_social.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event soc_lost_notes\n# en: The Borrowed Notes\ntitle [f46501] = 借走的笔记\n# en: {npc} borrowed all your lecture notes last week. {They} {are} now standing in front of you holding a single soggy page. \"There was a stream,\" {they} say{s}. \"And a duck. Mostly a duck.\"\ntext [093f86] = {npc}上星期借走了你所有的课堂笔记。现在{they}站在你面前，手里只剩一页湿透的纸。“有条小溪，”{they}说，“还有一只鸭子。主要是鸭子。”\n# en: A student holding a single soggy page of notes, the rest of the stack floating away down a stream behind them.\nscene [daf951] = 一名学生手里攥着一页湿透的笔记，身后剩下的那叠正顺着小溪漂走。\n# en: Laugh it off\nopt.left.label [29c02a] = 一笑了之\n# en: You rewrite your notes from memory. {npc} brings you a cake every day for a week, by way of apology. The cakes are better than the notes were.\nopt.left.say.1 [37846c] = 你凭记忆重写了笔记。{npc}一连一星期每天给你带一块蛋糕赔罪。蛋糕比笔记还好。\n# en: Make {them} rewrite them\nopt.right.label [53cd0f] = 让{them}重抄一份\n# en: {npc} copies out forty pages from a friend's notes, grumbling. The new notes are neater than yours ever were.\nopt.right.say.1 [a8c805] = {npc}一边抱怨一边从朋友那儿抄了四十页。新笔记比你自己写的还工整。\n\n@event soc_birthday\n# en: A Birthday\ntitle [492d55] = 生日\n# en: {&You find out — by accident — that it's {npc}'s birthday. {They} haven't told anyone. {They} {are} spending it in the library, alone, on purpose.|It's {npc}'s birthday again, and again {they} haven't told anyone. {They} {are} in the library, alone, as if you might have forgotten.}\ntext [8423a8] = {&你偶然发现今天是{npc}的生日。{they}谁也没告诉，正一个人待在图书馆里——故意的。|又到了{npc}的生日，{they}又是谁也没告诉。{they}一个人待在图书馆里，好像你会忘记似的。}\n# en: A small cake with a single candle in a dark common room, a group of students crouched behind a sofa, a surprised face in the doorway.\nscene [85796d] = 漆黑的公共休息室里一个插着一根蜡烛的小蛋糕，一群学生蹲在沙发后面，门口一张惊讶的脸。\n# en: Organise a surprise\nopt.left.label [c1b395] = 策划一场惊喜\n# en: {&Seven people jump out from behind a sofa. {npc} screams, then laughs, then has to sit down. \"How did you know?\" You'll never tell.|This year it's a cake shaped, badly, like the library. {npc} pretends to be annoyed for nearly a minute.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8021f6] = {&七个人从沙发后面跳出来。{npc}尖叫，然后大笑，然后不得不坐下。“你们怎么知道的？”你永远不会说。|今年是一个做成图书馆形状的蛋糕，做得很糟。{npc}假装生气了将近一分钟。}\n# en: Join {them} in the library\nopt.right.label [d7c810] = 去图书馆陪{them}\n# en: {&You sit down opposite {them} with your own book.|You take the same seat as last year, with your own book.} Neither of you says happy birthday. At midnight {they} slide{s} a biscuit across the table, and you both grin.\nopt.right.say.1 [fa64ac] = {&你带着自己的书在{them}对面坐下。|你带着自己的书，坐在和去年一样的位置。}谁也没说生日快乐。午夜时，{they}把一块饼干推过桌面，你们相视一笑。\n\n@event soc_bad_news\n# en: Bad News From Home\ntitle [9ab55c] = 家里来的坏消息\n# en: {npc} is sitting on the stairs with a letter in {their} lap, not moving. {Their} grandmother is ill. The barge home takes two days. \"I don't know whether to go,\" {they} say{s}.\ntext [c5229c] = {npc}坐在楼梯上，膝上放着一封信，一动不动。{their}祖母病了。坐驳船回家要两天。“我不知道该不该回去，”{they}说。\n# en: A student sitting on a stone staircase with a crumpled letter, head bowed, a friend sitting down beside them.\nscene [e888fc] = 一名学生坐在石阶上，手里攥着一封揉皱的信，低着头，一个朋友在旁边坐下。\n# en: \"Go. We'll cover your classes.\"\nopt.left.label [6868e0] = “去吧，课我们帮你顶着。”\n# en: {npc} catches the next barge. Every evening you copy out the day's notes for {them} from {their} classmates. When {npc} comes back, {their} grandmother is better, and {they} hug{s} you on the landing.\nopt.left.say.1 [564714] = {npc}赶上了下一班驳船。每天晚上，你都从{their}同学那里替{them}抄好当天的笔记。{npc}回来时，{their}祖母好些了，{they}在楼梯平台上抱住了你。\n# en: Just sit with {them}\nopt.right.label [75d0fd] = 只是陪{them}坐着\n# en: You sit there until the stairs move under you. You don't fix anything. That's not what {they} needed.\nopt.right.say.1 [439080] = 你们一直坐到楼梯在身下挪动。你什么也没解决。那本来就不是{they}需要的。\n\n@event soc_stargazing\n# en: Counting Stars\ntitle [559b85] = 数星星\n# en: {&{npc} has dragged a blanket up to the roof.|{npc} is on the roof again with the blanket, and a flask this time.|{npc} has found a better spot on the roof, out of the wind, and a better blanket.} \"There's a meteor shower,\" {they} say{s}. \"Supposedly.\" There's room on the blanket for two, just about.\ntext [e2e98e] = {&{npc}把一条毯子拖上了屋顶。|{npc}又带着毯子上了屋顶，这回还带了一只水壶。|{npc}在屋顶上找到了一个更避风的地方，还换了一条更好的毯子。}“有流星雨，”{they}说，“据说。”毯子上刚好够两个人躺。\n# en: Two students lying on their backs on a flat roof, close but not quite touching, a sky full of stars above.\nscene [6f5d93] = 两名学生仰面躺在平屋顶上，挨得很近却又没碰到，头顶是满天繁星。\n# en: Count meteors\nopt.left.label [b22e25] = 数流星\n# en: You count thirty-one. {npc} counts thirty-four and accuses you of not looking properly. You weren't, entirely. You go in when the shower's over, warm under the blanket all the way down the stairs.\nopt.left.say.1 [bc27d2] = 你数到三十一颗。{npc}数到三十四颗，指责你没认真看。你确实没完全在看。流星雨一停你们就回屋了，一路下楼都裹在毯子里，暖暖的。\n# en: Talk about everything else, till dawn\nopt.right.label [8e7b47] = 聊些别的一切，直到天亮\n# en: You don't see a single meteor. You talk about home and fear and what you'd do if you could do anything. When you look up, the sky's gone grey with dawn.\nopt.right.say.1 [88b087] = 你一颗流星也没看到。你们聊家乡、聊恐惧、聊如果什么都能做会去做什么。等你抬起头，天已经泛起了黎明的灰白。\n# en: The roof's cold has got into your bones. You sneeze for a week, and {npc} sends you a scarf with no note.\nopt.right.say.2 [06c336] = 屋顶的寒气钻进了你的骨头。你打了一周的喷嚏，{npc}送来一条围巾，没附字条。\n\n@event soc_jealous_friend\n# en: Jealousy\ntitle [bace8d] = 嫉妒\n# en: {~{npc} has been odd all week. Finally it comes out.|{npc} has been short with you at every meal. Finally, on the stairs, it comes out.|{npc} cancels on you twice and then turns up at your door. It comes out in a rush.} \"You've got all these new friends now. I just — I don't know where I fit anymore.\"\ntext [56bc6b] = {~{npc}一整个星期都怪怪的。终于说出来了。|{npc}每顿饭都对你爱答不理。终于，在楼梯上，说出来了。|{npc}放了你两次鸽子，然后又出现在你门口。话一股脑儿地涌了出来。}“你现在有那么多新朋友了。我就是——不知道自己还算什么了。”\n# en: A student standing alone at the edge of a laughing group, arms folded, watching someone else take what used to be their seat.\nscene [560bd3] = 一名学生抱着胳膊独自站在一群欢笑的人边上，看着别人坐上了曾经属于自己的位置。\n# en: Reassure {them}\nopt.left.label [c0cad1] = 让{them}安心\n# en: You tell {them} exactly where {they} fit: right here, first. {npc} rolls {their} eyes, but {they}'re smiling. The oddness is gone by dinner.\nopt.left.say.1 [944299] = 你明明白白地告诉{them}：{they}的位置就在这里，排第一。{npc}翻了个白眼，但在笑。晚饭前那股别扭劲就没了。\n# en: Tell {them} friendship isn't a contest\nopt.right.label [147a3c] = 告诉{them}友情不是比赛\n# en: You're right, technically. {npc} knows it. It still lands badly. It takes a few weeks for things to feel easy again. You spend the evenings you'd have spent soothing {them} in the library instead.\nopt.right.say.1 [80b7ce] = 严格来说你是对的。{npc}也知道。但这话还是说得不太好听。过了好几个星期，你们之间才重新自在起来。那些本该用来哄{them}的晚上，你都待在了图书馆。\n\n@event soc_teach_spell\n# en: A Trick Worth Learning\ntitle [8a5e67] = 值得一学的小把戏\n# en: {npc} has a spell {they} invented — a little thing, half-useful, half-ridiculous — and wants to teach it to someone. {&\"It makes your tea exactly the right temperature,\" {they} say{s}. \"Forever.\"|\"It finds the page you were reading,\" {they} say{s}. \"Any book, anywhere.\"|\"It keeps your socks dry,\" {they} say{s}. \"Lake, rain, puddles. Dry.\"}\ntext [be669b] = {npc}有一个自己发明的咒语——一个小东西，一半有用，一半荒唐——想教给别人。{&“它能让你的茶永远保持刚刚好的温度，”{they}说，“永远。”|“它能找到你读到的那一页，”{they}说，“任何书，任何地方。”|“它能让你的袜子保持干燥，”{they}说，“湖水、雨水、水洼，都不怕。”}\n# en: Two students in an empty classroom, one demonstrating a wand movement, the other copying it with a tiny spark of light.\nscene [bfffc4] = 空教室里两名学生，一个在示范魔杖的动作，另一个跟着模仿，杖尖冒出一点小火花。\n# en: Learn it properly\nopt.left.label [9c42bb] = 认真学会\n# en: It takes an hour. {&Your tea is now perfect, always.|You will never lose your page again.|Your socks are dry. Your socks will always be dry.} You'll use this spell more than anything you learn in class.\nopt.left.say.1 [75f1a5] = 花了一个钟头。{&你的茶现在永远是完美的。|你再也不会找不到读到的那一页了。|你的袜子是干的。你的袜子永远都会是干的。}这个咒语你用得会比课上学的任何东西都多。\n# en: {&The first attempt freezes your tea solid, and your eyebrows with it. The second works. Your eyebrows take a day to thaw.|The first attempt finds every page in the library at once. The second works. The librarian is not amused.|The first attempt dries your socks, your shoes and most of your feet. The second works. Your skin flakes for a day.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b46561] = {&第一次尝试把你的茶冻成了冰坨，连眉毛也一起冻上了。第二次成功了。你的眉毛花了一天才解冻。|第一次尝试同时找出了图书馆里的每一页。第二次成功了。图书管理员一点也不觉得好笑。|第一次尝试把你的袜子、鞋子和大半双脚都烘干了。第二次成功了。你的皮肤脱了一天的皮。}\n# en: Teach {them} one of yours in return\nopt.right.label [a24082] = 也教{them}一个你的\n# en: You trade spells like secrets. By midnight you've both got a new trick and an inside joke that will last three years.\nopt.right.say.1 [42f01a] = 你们像交换秘密一样交换咒语。到午夜时，你们都多了一个新把戏，还有一个能笑上三年的内部笑话。\n\n@event soc_secret_admirer\n# en: A Secret Admirer\ntitle [6041b8] = 神秘的仰慕者\n# en: Folded notes keep appearing in your library books. Unsigned. They're funny, and a little nervous, and they know things about you that only someone paying attention would know. The handwriting looks familiar.\ntext [a4cea9] = 你的图书馆借书里不断出现折好的字条。没有署名。它们很有趣，有点紧张，而且知道一些只有一直留意你的人才会知道的事。那笔迹看着有点眼熟。\n# en: A folded note tucked into a library book with a pressed flower, unsigned, the handwriting careful and slightly shaky.\nscene [469c2b] = 一张夹在图书馆书里的折叠字条，附着一朵压花，没有署名，字迹小心又微微发抖。\n# en: Leave a note back\nopt.left.label [ad88dc] = 回一张字条\n# en: You write back. The notes get longer. One day the last line just says: \"It's {npc}. You knew, didn't you?\" You did.\nopt.left.say.1 [5ee7cc] = 你回了信。字条越写越长。有一天，最后一行只写着：“是{npc}。你早就知道了，对吧？”你确实知道。\n# en: Stake out the library\nopt.right.label [010099] = 在图书馆蹲守\n# en: You catch {npc} red-handed, mid-tuck. {They} turn{s} the colour of a pyromancy exam. You both laugh until the librarian throws you out.\nopt.right.say.1 [a59ad0] = 你当场抓住了正往书里塞字条的{npc}。{they}的脸红得像火焰术考试。你们俩笑到被图书管理员赶了出去。\n# en: You wait three nights and catch nobody. On the fourth morning the note says: \"Nice try.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [ec1e48] = 你守了三个晚上，谁也没抓到。第四天早上，字条上写着：“想得美。”\n\n@event soc_injured_friend\n# en: Carried\ntitle [c0fda1] = 背回来\n# en: {npc} has twisted an ankle on the far side of the grounds, a long way from the infirmary, and it's getting dark. {They} {are} trying very hard to pretend it doesn't hurt.\ntext [f03529] = {npc}在校园的另一头扭伤了脚踝，离医务室很远，天也快黑了。{they}正非常努力地假装一点也不疼。\n# en: A student carrying another piggyback across a muddy lawn, the injured one's ankle wrapped in a scarf.\nscene [042d6a] = 一名学生背着另一名学生穿过泥泞的草坪，受伤那位的脚踝用围巾裹着。\n# en: Carry {them} on your back\nopt.left.label [374549] = 把{them}背回去\n# en: It's a long way. {npc} narrates the whole journey like a horse race. By the time you reach the infirmary you're both laughing too hard to breathe.\nopt.left.say.1 [6e8e08] = 路很长。{npc}一路像解说赛马一样解说着。等你们到医务室时，两个人都笑得喘不上气。\n# en: Run for help\nopt.right.label [689bdc] = 跑去找人帮忙\n# en: You run for Sister Amaranth, who arrives with a floating stretcher and a lot of opinions. {npc} is fine. {They} send{s} you a thank-you sweet.\nopt.right.say.1 [b2b6c2] = 你跑去找阿玛兰斯修女，她带着一副漂浮担架和一肚子意见赶来了。{npc}没事。{they}送了你一块谢礼糖。\n\n@event soc_rival_decency\n# en: An Unexpected Kindness\ntitle [10ec52] = 意外的善意\n# en: {&You drop your books in the rain, and the first person to stoop and help pick them up is {npc}.|You slip on the wet stairs, and the hand that catches your elbow is {npc}'s.|The wind off the lake takes your scarf, and {npc} is the one who runs after it.} {They} hand{s} it back without a word, looking as surprised as you are.\ntext [157405] = {&你的书在雨里掉了一地，第一个弯腰帮你捡的人竟是{npc}。|你在湿滑的楼梯上一滑，扶住你胳膊肘的那只手是{npc}的。|湖上吹来的风卷走了你的围巾，追上去的人是{npc}。}{they}一言不发地把东西递还给你，看上去和你一样惊讶。\n# en: A student picking up a rival's scattered books from a wet floor, the rival frozen mid-step, not sure what to say.\nscene [b9f110] = 一名学生从湿漉漉的地上捡起对手散落的书，对手僵在半步之中，不知该说什么。\n# en: Thank {them} properly\nopt.left.label [023f60] = 好好谢谢{them}\n# en: \"Don't make it a thing,\" {npc} mutters. But the next day {they} nod{s} to you in the corridor. {&Something has thawed.|It's getting harder to stay enemies.|You're not sure what you are to each other anymore.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6849d6] = “别弄得像回事似的，”{npc}嘟囔道。但第二天，{they}在走廊里冲你点了点头。{&有什么东西解冻了。|要继续当敌人越来越难了。|你也说不清你们现在算什么了。}\n# en: Suspect a trick\nopt.right.label [5792ea] = 怀疑这是个圈套\n# en: You check for hexes. There aren't any. {npc} saw you checking. You'll both remember that.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b0327] = 你检查有没有恶咒。没有。{npc}看见你在检查。你们俩都会记住这件事。\n\n@event soc_partner_anniversary\n# en: An Anniversary\ntitle [a63670] = 纪念日\n# en: {bond_months(npc) >= 36: Three years|{bond_months(npc) >= 24: Two years|A year}} to the month since you and {npc} stopped pretending — long enough that {npc} calls things \"ours\" now. Today {they} hand{s} you a small wrapped box and won't meet your eyes.\ntext [193ccd] = {bond_months(npc) >= 36: 三年|{bond_months(npc) >= 24: 两年|一年}}了，从你和{npc}不再假装的那个月算起——久到{npc}如今把东西都叫作“我们的”。今天{they}递给你一个包好的小盒子，不敢看你的眼睛。\n# en: Two people on a bench by the lake at sunset, one holding out a small wrapped box, the other's hands over their mouth.\nscene [3c4e6d] = 日落时分，两个人坐在湖边的长椅上，一个递出一个包好的小盒子，另一个双手捂着嘴。\n# en: Open it now\nopt.left.label [a90872] = 现在就打开\n# en: Inside: a pebble from the lakeshore, polished to a shine, from the day you first really talked. \"It's stupid,\" {npc} says. It isn't. You carry it everywhere, and spend the next day hunting for something half as good to give back.\nopt.left.say.1 [dc0d28] = 里面是一颗湖边的鹅卵石，被打磨得闪闪发亮，是你们第一次真正谈心那天捡的。“这很傻，”{npc}说。一点也不傻。你走到哪儿都带着它，第二天又跑遍各处，想找一件哪怕有它一半好的回礼。\n# en: Give {them} yours first\nopt.right.label [64f5f8] = 先把你的送给{them}\n# en: You'd had the same idea, and spent most of your purse on it. You both open your boxes at once. They're nearly the same gift. You laugh until the sun goes down.\nopt.right.say.1 [f20b70] = 你们想到一块儿去了，你为此花掉了大半个钱袋。你们同时打开盒子。两份礼物几乎一模一样。你们一直笑到太阳落山。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_summer.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_summer.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event sum_empty_castle\n# en: The Empty Castle\ntitle [38b303] = 空荡荡的城堡\n# en: In summer the castle breathes differently. Corridors you've walked a thousand times are silent; doors that are always locked stand slightly open. A portrait yawns. Nobody is watching.\ntext [b8e5ca] = 夏天，城堡的呼吸都不一样了。你走过上千次的走廊寂静无声；那些永远上锁的门微微敞开着。一幅画像打了个哈欠。没有人在看。\n# en: A vast empty dining hall in summer, chairs upturned on the tables, a single student eating alone in a shaft of dusty sunlight.\nscene [06e080] = 夏日里一间空旷的大餐厅，椅子倒扣在桌上，一名学生独自坐在满是浮尘的阳光里吃饭。\n# en: Explore the open doors\nopt.left.label [476a88] = 探索那些敞开的门\n# en: Behind one door: a forgotten trophy room. Behind another: a professor's stash of very old toffee and a purse of lost-property coins nobody will miss. You pocket it anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [08ba74] = 一扇门后：一间被遗忘的奖杯室。另一扇门后：某位教授私藏的陈年太妃糖，还有一袋没人会惦记的失物招领硬币。你还是把它揣进了兜里。\n# en: Behind the first door: the caretaker, in a dressing gown, very surprised. You both agree this never happened.\nopt.left.say.2 [eeeeef] = 第一扇门后：穿着睡袍、大吃一惊的管理员。你们俩一致同意，这事从没发生过。\n# en: Enjoy the quiet\nopt.right.label [293287] = 享受这份安静\n# en: You read in window seats, nap on the great lawn and eat breakfast at noon. For two weeks the most famous academy in the kingdom is just a big, peaceful house.\nopt.right.say.1 [08d11a] = 你在窗台上看书，在大草坪上打盹，中午才吃早饭。整整两个星期，王国最有名的学院只是一座又大又安宁的房子。\n\n@event sum_home_chores\n# en: Help Around the House\ntitle [da7efc] = 帮家里干活\n# en: Home is lovely for about three days. Then the list appears on the kitchen door: the roof, the goats, the fence, the cousin's wedding. Your summer reading list is hidden under a tea towel.\ntext [e1f4e8] = 家的美好大概能维持三天。然后清单就贴上了厨房门：屋顶、山羊、篱笆、表亲的婚礼。你的暑期书单被藏在一块茶巾底下。\n# en: A farmhouse kitchen in high summer, a basket of laundry, a list of chores pinned to the door, a textbook hidden under a tea towel.\nscene [c81350] = 盛夏的农舍厨房，一篮待洗的衣物，门上钉着家务清单，一本课本藏在茶巾底下。\n# en: Do the chores — with magic\nopt.left.label [2b5166] = 干家务——用魔法\n# en: The fence mends itself, the goats are briefly levitated, the roof is better than new. The whole street comes to watch. Your mother pretends not to be proud.\nopt.left.say.1 [6cae25] = 篱笆自己补好了，山羊短暂地浮了起来，屋顶比新的还好。整条街的人都跑来看。你妈妈假装一点也不骄傲。\n# en: The goats are briefly levitated. Unfortunately so is the fence. Your family is very patient about it.\nopt.left.say.2 [4bdcaf] = 山羊短暂地浮了起来。不幸的是，篱笆也浮起来了。你的家人对此非常有耐心。\n# en: Do them the slow way, and talk\nopt.right.label [b2c300] = 慢慢干，顺便聊聊\n# en: You fix the fence by hand with your father, saying very little and then, suddenly, a lot. It's the best conversation you've had all year.\nopt.right.say.1 [775d96] = 你和爸爸一起亲手修篱笆，一开始几乎不说话，后来突然说了很多。这是你一整年里最好的一次谈话。\n\n@event sum_old_friend\n# en: An Old Friend\ntitle [8809c5] = 老朋友\n# en: Your oldest friend from home is waiting at the crossroads, like always. But something's different. \"So,\" they say, not quite looking at you, \"I suppose you're too grand for us now.\"\ntext [66198b] = 你在家乡最老的朋友照例等在十字路口。但有些东西不一样了。“所以，”对方说，眼睛没怎么看你，“我猜你现在看不上我们了吧。”\n# en: Two young people sitting on a stone wall above a village, one in academy robes, one in work clothes, a little space between them.\nscene [b48b9c] = 两个年轻人坐在村子上方的石墙上，一个穿着学院长袍，一个穿着干活的衣服，中间隔着一点距离。\n# en: Prove you're not\nopt.left.label [4cb06b] = 证明你没有\n# en: You spend the summer exactly as you used to — orchard raids, river swims, terrible jokes. By Starfall, the space between you on the wall is gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [8019a4] = 你像从前一样度过这个夏天——偷果园、下河游泳、讲烂笑话。到了星坠月，石墙上你们之间的那点距离不见了。\n# en: Admit you've changed\nopt.right.label [11cd37] = 承认你变了\n# en: \"I have,\" you say. \"So have you.\" They laugh, surprised. You talk all night about who you're both becoming. It's different from before. It might be better.\nopt.right.say.1 [84ee4c] = “我是变了，”你说，“你也变了。”对方惊讶地笑了。你们聊了一整夜，聊你们各自正在变成什么样的人。和从前不一样了。也许会更好。\n\n@event sum_midsummer_fair\n# en: The Midsummer Fair\ntitle [fa85d7] = 仲夏集市\n# en: The village Midsummer Fair: enchanted carousel, ring-toss, a strongman, and a booth promising \"GUESS YOUR WEIGHT IN CHEESE\". You have a few crowns in your pocket and a whole afternoon.\ntext [d583e2] = 村里的仲夏集市：魔法旋转木马、套圈、大力士，还有一个摊位承诺“猜猜你的体重等于多少奶酪”。你兜里有几个克朗，还有一整个下午。\n# en: A village green full of striped tents, a carousel of enchanted wooden animals, lanterns strung between trees, a strongman lifting a cow.\nscene [b2cfbf] = 村子草地上满是条纹帐篷，一座由施了魔法的木头动物组成的旋转木马，树间挂着灯笼，一个大力士正举起一头牛。\n# en: Win something at the ring-toss\nopt.left.label [1e7b9b] = 套圈赢个奖\n# en: Three rings, three hits. You win a stuffed griffon the size of a sheep and sell it to a crying child's father for fifteen crowns. Everyone wins.\nopt.left.say.1 [b0aa3b] = 三个圈，三个全中。你赢了一只绵羊那么大的狮鹫玩偶，又以十五克朗卖给了一个哭闹孩子的爸爸。皆大欢喜。\n# en: Every ring bounces off. The ring-toss is definitely enchanted. You don't care; you're eating candied apples.\nopt.left.say.2 [09da43] = 每个圈都弹开了。这套圈摊绝对施了魔法。你不在乎，你正在吃糖苹果。\n# en: Ride the carousel\nopt.right.label [a83496] = 坐旋转木马\n# en: Your wooden hippogriff takes off, very slightly, at the top of every turn. You ride it nine times.\nopt.right.say.1 [8ddedf] = 你的木头鹰马每转到最高处都会轻轻地飞起来一点点。你坐了九次。\n\n@event sum_letter_from_friend\n# en: A Summer Letter\ntitle [947c1f] = 夏日来信\n# en: An owl arrives with a thick, slightly crumpled letter from {npc}. Six pages of summer news, two doodles, one pressed flower, and at the end, in smaller writing: \"I miss the castle. I miss you, actually.\"\ntext [915df1] = 一只猫头鹰送来了{npc}一封厚厚的、有点皱的信。六页夏日见闻，两幅涂鸦，一朵压花，最后用小一号的字写着：“我想念城堡。其实，我想你。”\n# en: A thick letter with a doodle on the envelope arriving by owl at a summer window.\nscene [c7230f] = 一封信封上画着涂鸦的厚信，由猫头鹰送到夏日的窗前。\n# en: Write a short reply\nopt.left.label [2e26d1] = 写一封简短的回信\n# en: You write a page. It's warm, if brief. {npc} keeps it anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [ae145d] = 你写了一页。温暖，虽然简短。{npc}还是把它收了起来。\n# en: Write back for hours\nopt.right.label [c44657] = 回信写上几个钟头\n# en: You write until the candle gutters. Twelve pages. You can't remember the last time you wrote that much that wasn't an essay.\nopt.right.say.1 [d776a8] = 你一直写到蜡烛快灭。十二页。你都不记得上次写这么多不是论文的东西是什么时候了。\n\n@event sum_rain_week\n# en: A Week of Rain\ntitle [fa6851] = 一星期的雨\n# en: It rains for a solid week. Not dramatic rain: just steady, grey, endless rain. Every plan you made for the summer is quietly drowning.\ntext [8b734b] = 雨整整下了一个星期。不是戏剧性的雨，只是稳定、灰暗、没完没了的雨。你为这个夏天做的每一个计划都在悄悄被淹没。\n# en: Rain streaming down a tall window, a student curled in an armchair with a fat novel, a teapot steaming on the sill.\nscene [ea0fc1] = 雨水顺着高窗流下，一名学生蜷在扶手椅里捧着一本厚小说，窗台上的茶壶冒着热气。\n# en: Read a very long novel\nopt.left.label [fc9cb2] = 读一本很长的小说\n# en: You buy an eight-hundred-page novel about a family of sea-witches, read every word and cry at the end. It's the best week of the summer.\nopt.left.say.1 [f8199f] = 你买了一本八百页的小说，讲一个海巫家族的故事，一字不落地读完，读到结尾哭了。这是整个夏天最好的一周。\n# en: Practise spells indoors\nopt.right.label [8f2ff7] = 在室内练咒语\n# en: You practise until you can light every candle in the room with a flick. You also set fire to one curtain. It's raining; it goes out quickly.\nopt.right.say.1 [f19727] = 你一直练到手指一弹就能点亮房间里每一支蜡烛。你还烧着了一块窗帘。外面在下雨，火很快就灭了。\n\n@event sum_sea_serpent\n# en: Fishing With an Uncle\ntitle [340021] = 和叔叔去钓鱼\n# en: Your uncle takes you fishing. Something takes the bait. Something very large. The line sings. The boat tilts. A long scaled neck rises out of the water and looks at you with polite interest.\ntext [fbf017] = 叔叔带你去钓鱼。有东西咬钩了。非常大的东西。鱼线嗡嗡作响，船身倾斜。一条长满鳞片的长脖子从水里升起，带着礼貌的好奇看着你。\n# en: A small fishing boat on a grey-green sea, a fishing line drawn taut, something very long and scaled rising beside the hull.\nscene [035dc5] = 灰绿色海面上的一条小渔船，鱼线绷得紧紧的，一个很长、长满鳞片的东西正从船边升起。\n# en: Cut the line\nopt.left.label [c4df32] = 剪断鱼线\n# en: The serpent sinks back with a sigh like a disappointed whale. Your uncle tells the story for the rest of his life, and it gets bigger every time.\nopt.left.say.1 [f3a3c3] = 海蛇叹了口气沉了回去，像一头失望的鲸鱼。你叔叔后半辈子都在讲这个故事，而且每讲一次就更大一点。\n# en: Try to talk to it\nopt.right.label [2c2211] = 试着和它说话\n# en: You greet it in the old courtesies {met(brask): Professor Brask taught you|from a book you once read and never expected to need}. It bows its great head, leaves a pearl on the deck and swims away. Your uncle faints.\nopt.right.say.1 [299c43] = 你用{met(brask): 布拉斯克教授教过的|从一本书上读来、从没想过会用上的}古老礼节向它问好。它低下巨大的头，在甲板上留下一颗珍珠，然后游走了。你叔叔晕了过去。\n# en: The serpent is not interested in conversation. It is interested in the boat. You swim home.\nopt.right.say.2 [c01ca7] = 海蛇对谈话没兴趣。它对船有兴趣。你是游回家的。\n\n@event sum_reading_list\n# en: The Summer Reading List\ntitle [2a9b43] = 暑期书单\n# en: The summer reading list arrives by owl. It is a scroll. It unrolls across the floor, out of the door and partway down the stairs. Thorne has added a note at the bottom: \"Optional. (It is not optional.)\"\ntext [56544a] = 暑期书单由猫头鹰送达。是一卷羊皮纸。它在地板上展开，滚出门外，一直滚到楼梯中间。索恩在最下面加了一句：“选读。（并不是选读。）”\n# en: A long scroll unrolling off a table and across the floor, a list of titles in tiny handwriting, a student staring at it in despair.\nscene [593aae] = 一卷长长的羊皮纸从桌上滚落、铺满地板，上面用极小的字写满书名，一名学生绝望地盯着它。\n# en: Read all of it\nopt.left.label [281ec8] = 全部读完\n# en: You read everything. When term starts, Thorne asks a question about the last book and you're the only one who can answer. His eyebrow rises half a millimetre.\nopt.left.say.1 [0c1e02] = 你全读完了。开学时，索恩问了一个关于最后一本书的问题，只有你答得上来。他的眉毛抬高了半毫米。\n# en: Read the first and last chapters of each\nopt.right.label [2423ad] = 每本只读第一章和最后一章\n# en: A time-honoured strategy. You can talk confidently about the beginning and end of forty books. The middle remains a mystery, like most of life.\nopt.right.say.1 [bca605] = 一个历史悠久的策略。你能自信地谈论四十本书的开头和结尾。中间部分仍然是个谜，就像人生的大部分一样。\n\n@event sum_night_market\n# en: The Night Market\ntitle [8fbd8e] = 夜市\n# en: A night market appears in a field outside the village — it wasn't there yesterday and won't be tomorrow. A hooded stall-keeper offers you a stoppered bottle with a tiny thundercloud inside. \"Fifteen crowns. What it does is a surprise.\"\ntext [8af6b0] = 村外的田野里出现了一个夜市——昨天还没有，明天也不会在。一个戴兜帽的摊主递给你一个塞着瓶塞的瓶子，里面有一小朵雷雨云。“十五克朗。它有什么用？那是个惊喜。”\n# en: A market that only appears after midnight, stalls lit with coloured flames, hooded sellers, a bottle that seems to contain a small thundercloud.\nscene [ad8492] = 一个只在午夜后出现的集市，摊位亮着彩色火焰，戴兜帽的卖家，一个瓶子里似乎装着一小朵雷雨云。\n# en: Buy the bottle\nopt.left.label [19c560] = 买下瓶子\n# en: You uncork it in your room. The little storm rains ideas on you for a fortnight. Your notebooks overflow.\nopt.left.say.1 [3066ff] = 你在房间里拔开瓶塞。那场小风暴往你身上下了两星期的灵感雨。你的笔记本都写满了。\n# en: You uncork it in your room. The little storm rains on you. Just rain. For a fortnight. You catch a cold.\nopt.left.say.2 [07314d] = 你在房间里拔开瓶塞。那场小风暴往你身上下雨。就是雨。下了两星期。你感冒了。\n# en: Just look around\nopt.right.label [1c076f] = 只是逛逛\n# en: You wander until dawn, buying nothing, watching everything. When the sun rises the field is empty except for a single glowing feather. You keep it.\nopt.right.say.1 [a046d1] = 你一直逛到黎明，什么也没买，什么都看了。太阳升起时，田野里空空荡荡，只剩一根发光的羽毛。你把它留了下来。\n\n@event sum_end_of_summer\n# en: The Last Week of Summer\ntitle [64767a] = 夏天的最后一周\n# en: The last week of summer has that particular light — golden, heavy, already nostalgic. Your trunk is half-packed. Part of you is desperate to get back. Part of you wants this week to last forever.\ntext [fa47ef] = 夏天的最后一周有一种特别的光——金黄、沉甸甸的，已经带着怀念。你的箱子收拾了一半。一部分的你迫不及待想回去，一部分的你希望这一周永远不要结束。\n# en: A student packing a trunk in golden late-summer light, a half-finished jar of jam, a pressed flower and a new textbook on the bed.\nscene [79a65d] = 一名学生在夏末的金色光线里收拾箱子，床上放着半罐果酱、一朵压花和一本新课本。\n# en: Squeeze every drop out of it\nopt.left.label [660c83] = 榨干它的每一滴\n# en: Late swims, long dinners, one final sunset from the hill. You arrive at the barge sunburnt and happy and only slightly late.\nopt.left.say.1 [3d2155] = 很晚的游泳、很长的晚餐、山顶上最后一次日落。你到达驳船时晒伤了、很开心，只迟到了一点点。\n# en: Get ready for the new year\nopt.right.label [50fe4f] = 为新学年做准备\n# en: You re-read your notes, sharpen your quills and polish your wand. When term starts you are, for once, ahead. It's an unfamiliar feeling.\nopt.right.say.1 [fbc642] = 你重读笔记，削好羽毛笔，擦亮魔杖。开学时你难得地领先了一步。这是一种陌生的感觉。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/expansion_y3.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/expansion_y3.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@thread careers\n# en: What comes next\nname [6ed4f7] = 往后的路\n# en: The final year, and the question everyone asks at supper.\npremise [11fc94] = 最后一年，还有晚饭时每个人都会问的那个问题。\n# en: You walked the career fair and left your name at the stalls that mattered.\njournal.1 [59bae7] = 你逛了职业招聘会，在要紧的摊位前留下了名字。\n# en: You sat an interview, and waited for the letter.\njournal.2 [5d85f9] = 你参加了面试，然后等着来信。\n# en: They wrote back: they'd have you.\njournal.3 [6dbb3b] = 他们回信了：愿意要你。\n# en: A polite letter said no.\njournal.4 [236713] = 一封客气的信说了不。\n# en: An offer waits on your desk with your name on it. The answer is due on the last day.\njournal.5 [59ffc8] = 一份写着你名字的聘约正躺在你桌上。最后一天要给答复。\n# en: On the last day, you said yes to what comes next.\njournal.6 [8eaa85] = 最后一天，你对往后的路说了“好”。\n# en: On the last day you said yes to nothing yet, and that was an answer too.\njournal.7 [2cd634] = 最后一天，你还没对任何事说“好”，这也是一种答复。\n\n@event y3_career_fair\n# en: The Career Fair\ntitle [a6ba30] = 职业招聘会\n# en: {month == 3: The fair's second week, and the great hall is half packed: the booths that are left have stopped being polite and started being keen. This is the last chance to leave your name.|Every guild, order and institution in the kingdom has sent someone to the great hall. They are all very polite. They are all, very clearly, shopping. Where will you leave your name?}\ntext [60d297] = {month == 3: 招聘会的第二周，大礼堂已收拾了一半：还留着的摊位不再客气，变得急切。这是留下名字的最后机会。|王国里每一个公会、修会和机构都派了人到大礼堂来。他们都非常客气。他们也都非常明显地在挑人。你要把名字留在哪里？}\n# en: The great hall lined with recruiters' booths — a Conclave mage in silver, a librarian behind a wall of books, a circus ringmaster juggling fire.\nscene [7e3280] = 大礼堂里摆满了招聘摊位——一位身着银袍的议会法师、一位坐在书墙后的图书管理员、一位抛着火把的马戏团团长。\n# en: The Conclave of the Seven Towers\nopt.conclave.label [c832a4] = 七塔议会\n# en: Apprentice archmages. The interview tests your Arcana.\nopt.conclave.hint [04186b] = 大法师学徒。面试考你的奥术。\n# en: You leave your name with the mage in silver, who writes it down without looking up. The interview is set.\nopt.conclave.say.1 [fcffbb] = 你把名字留给了那位银袍法师，他头也没抬就记下了。面试定下来了。\n# en: The Royal Library\nopt.library.label [64f580] = 皇家图书馆\n# en: Keepers of every book ever written. The interview tests your Lore.\nopt.library.hint [e1ce52] = 世上每一本书的守护者。面试考你的学识。\n# en: The librarian takes your name and asks what you're reading at the moment. You answer. The interview is set.\nopt.library.say.1 [84b5cd] = 图书馆员记下你的名字，问你最近在读什么。你回答了。面试定下来了。\n# en: The Guild of Alchemists\nopt.guild.label [8a8f99] = 炼金师公会\n# en: Potions, profit, explosions. The interview tests your Alchemy.\nopt.guild.hint [acaaa6] = 药水、利润、爆炸。面试考你的炼金。\n# en: The alchemist at the guild's booth shakes your hand and checks it for burns. The interview is set.\nopt.guild.say.1 [342128] = 炼金师公会摊位上的人和你握了握手，顺便检查了一下有没有烫伤。面试定下来了。\n# en: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs\nopt.unseen.label [a4101a] = 王室隐秘事务署\n# en: They found you, actually. The interview tests your Guile.\nopt.unseen.hint [606647] = 其实是他们先找到你的。面试考你的诡计。\n# en: A woman nobody else seems to notice takes your name and tells you where to be. The interview is set.\nopt.unseen.say.1 [7f6715] = 一个似乎谁都没注意到的女人记下你的名字，告诉你该去哪儿。面试定下来了。\n# en: The Travelling Circus of Wonders\nopt.circus.label [f3ec0c] = 奇迹巡回马戏团\n# en: Wagons, crowds, illusions. The interview tests your Charm.\nopt.circus.hint [6df8a7] = 马车、人群、幻术。面试考你的魅力。\n# en: The ringmaster juggles your name into the air, catches it and writes it down. The interview is set.\nopt.circus.say.1 [fca380] = 马戏团团长把你的名字抛上空中，接住，然后记了下来。面试定下来了。\n# en: Stay at Lumenhold as a junior teacher\nopt.teaching.label [27030b] = 留在流明堡当见习教师\n# en: Needs a faculty member who trusts you: Thorne, Maud or the Headmistress.\nopt.teaching.hint [e8bcda] = 需要一位信任你的教职员：索恩教授、老莫德或院长。\n# en: Nobody on the faculty knows you well enough to vouch for you.\nopt.teaching.locked [bbf1f9] = 教职员里没有谁足够了解你，能为你担保。\n# en: Your old professor puts your name forward to the faculty, with a few words you don't hear. The interview is set.\nopt.teaching.say.1 [4a845e] = 你从前的教授把你的名字推荐给了教职员会，还附了几句你没听见的话。面试定下来了。\n# en: Not yet — you'd rather not decide\nopt.none.label [0a96fd] = 还不急——你暂时不想决定\n# en: You collect a tote bag, three pamphlets and a free quill. {month == 2: The future can wait one more month; the fair's second week is next month.|The fair packs up for good. The future will have to come and find you, by letter or at the registrar's window.}\nopt.none.say.1 [c72450] = 你领了一个布袋、三本小册子和一支免费羽毛笔。{month == 2: 未来可以再等一个月；招聘会的第二周就在下个月。|招聘会彻底收摊了。未来只好自己来找你，靠来信，或者在教务员的窗口。}\n\n@event y3_interview\n# en: The Interview\ntitle [7d775f] = 面试\n# en: Three people behind a long table. One takes notes; one smiles too much; one says nothing at all, which is clearly the one who decides. \"Tell us,\" says the smiling one, \"why you.\"\ntext [06648e] = 长桌后坐着三个人。一个做笔记；一个笑得太多；一个一言不发——显然，做决定的就是这一位。“说说看，”爱笑的那位开口，“为什么是你？”\n# en: A small panelled room, a long table, three interviewers and one empty chair facing them.\nscene [05e47c] = 一间镶着护墙板的小房间，一张长桌，三位面试官，对面一把空椅子。\n# en: Let your record speak\nopt.left.label [1ea228] = 让履历说话\n# en: You talk about what you've learned and, more importantly, what you got wrong and fixed. The silent one writes a single word. You suspect it's \"yes\".\nopt.left.say.1 [c8179c] = 你讲你学到了什么，更重要的是，你搞错过什么、又是怎么改正的。沉默的那位只写下一个词。你猜那是“行”。\n# en: You recite your achievements like a shopping list. The silent one looks at the clock. \"Now,\" says the smiling one, \"a small practical.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [2da86c] = 你像念购物清单一样背诵自己的成就。沉默的那位看了一眼钟。“现在，”爱笑的那位说，“来个小小的实操。”\n# en: Tell them a story\nopt.right.label [a082be] = 给他们讲个故事\n# en: You tell them about the night the lake froze, or the night you caught a star, or the night you were wrong. The silent one laughs. Out loud.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b6366] = 你讲起湖面结冰的那一夜，或者你接住一颗星星的那一夜，或者你犯错的那一夜。沉默的那位笑了。出声地笑了。\n# en: The story goes on too long and ends in the wrong place. Somewhere in the middle you lost them, and yourself. \"Now,\" says the smiling one, \"a small practical.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [4096b3] = 故事讲得太长，结尾也落错了地方。讲到一半，你就把他们弄丢了，也把自己弄丢了。“现在，”爱笑的那位说，“来个小小的实操。”\n\n@event y3_interview_practical\n# en: The Practical\ntitle [eaf727] = 实操\n# en: The note-taker sets a task on the table. {flag(career_conclave): A ward with a flaw in it, somewhere.|{flag(career_library): A crumbling manuscript in three hands and no catalogue.|{flag(career_guild): A cauldron, a recipe with one ingredient missing, and a clock.|{flag(career_unseen): A sealed letter, and the request to read it without breaking the seal.|{flag(career_circus): An empty stage, a sceptical crowd of three, and five minutes.|A class of imaginary first-years, and a lesson to give them.}}}}} \"In your own time,\" says the smiling one, and turns over an hourglass.\ntext [2862ee] = 记录的那位在桌上摆出一项任务。{flag(career_conclave): 一道结界，某处有个破绽。|{flag(career_library): 一份出自三人之手、没有目录、正在碎裂的手稿。|{flag(career_guild): 一口坩埚，一张缺了一味材料的配方，还有一只钟。|{flag(career_unseen): 一封封好的信，要求你不拆封蜡读出内容。|{flag(career_circus): 一个空舞台，三个将信将疑的观众，还有五分钟。|一群想象中的一年级生，和一堂要给他们上的课。}}}}} “不急，”爱笑的那位说着，把一只沙漏翻了过来。\n# en: A plain table in a panelled room set with a single task, three interviewers watching a student roll up their sleeves.\nscene [9735fb] = 镶板房间里一张朴素的桌子上摆着一项任务，三位面试官看着一个学生卷起袖子。\n# en: Do it properly\nopt.left.label [859632] = 认真完成\n# en: You find the flaw, name it and close it, and the ward rings like a struck glass. The silent one leans forward for the first time.\nopt.left.say.1 [19a282] = 你找到了破绽，说出它，然后补上，结界像被敲响的玻璃一样鸣响。沉默的那位第一次身体前倾。\n# en: The flaw finds you first. The ward fizzes out in your hands, and the note-taker writes for a long time.\nopt.left.say.2 [4a3fa4] = 破绽先找上了你。结界在你手里嘶嘶地熄灭了，记录的那位写了很久。\n# en: Three hands, three centuries, one lost chapter. You date them all before the sand runs out. The silent one nods, once.\nopt.left.say.3 [7bd96d] = 三种笔迹，三个世纪，一个遗失的章节。沙子漏完之前，你把它们的年代全断了出来。沉默的那位点了一下头。\n# en: You date the second hand a century wrong, and know it the moment you say it. The note-taker writes for a long time.\nopt.left.say.4 [c75925] = 你把第二种笔迹的年代断错了一个世纪，话一出口就知道了。记录的那位写了很久。\n# en: You find the missing ingredient by its absence and the cauldron settles to a perfect simmer. Someone behind the table sniffs, approvingly.\nopt.left.say.5 [220baa] = 你凭着缺失察觉出那味少了的材料，坩埚安定下来，完美地文火慢炖。桌子后面有人嗅了嗅，表示赞许。\n# en: The cauldron spits and turns a colour no recipe asks for. The note-taker writes for a long time.\nopt.left.say.6 [796d95] = 坩埚噼啪作响，变成了一种没有配方会要求的颜色。记录的那位写了很久。\n# en: Steam, a thin blade, a little patience. You read it, reseal it and hand it back. The silent one smiles, which is somehow worse.\nopt.left.say.7 [a79e9e] = 蒸汽、一片薄刃、一点耐心。你读完，重新封好，递了回去。沉默的那位笑了，不知怎么这更吓人。\n# en: The seal cracks with a sound like a shot. The silent one does not look surprised. The note-taker writes for a long time.\nopt.left.say.8 [e395b2] = 封蜡裂开，声音像一声枪响。沉默的那位毫不意外。记录的那位写了很久。\n# en: Five minutes of nonsense and wonder, and the crowd of three are on their feet. One of them is the silent one.\nopt.left.say.9 [c96f52] = 五分钟的胡闹与奇迹，三个观众站了起来。其中一个就是沉默的那位。\n# en: The crowd of three stays seated, arms folded, for all five minutes. The note-taker writes for a long time.\nopt.left.say.10 [e66680] = 整整五分钟，三个观众抱着胳膊坐着没动。记录的那位写了很久。\n# en: You teach the empty chairs the thing you most wish someone had taught you, and forget for a while that they're empty. The silent one nods.\nopt.left.say.11 [efb9c1] = 你把自己最希望当年有人教给你的东西教给了那些空椅子，有一阵子忘了它们是空的。沉默的那位点了点头。\n# en: You lose the imaginary class in the first minute, which you hadn't known was possible. The note-taker writes for a long time.\nopt.left.say.12 [a516ac] = 你在第一分钟就镇不住那群想象中的学生了，你都不知道这还有可能。记录的那位写了很久。\n# en: Ask questions before you start\nopt.right.label [00676d] = 开始前先提问\n# en: You ask three good questions, then do the task carefully and well. It's slower than they'd like. The silent one doesn't seem to mind.\nopt.right.say.1 [97b7a6] = 你提了三个好问题，然后仔细、出色地完成了任务。比他们希望的慢一些。沉默的那位似乎并不介意。\n# en: You ask three good questions and run out of sand before you've finished. \"Careful,\" says the smiling one, in a tone that isn't quite praise.\nopt.right.say.2 [50d652] = 你提了三个好问题，结果沙子漏完了还没做完。“仔细点，”爱笑的那位说，那语气算不上夸奖。\n\n@event y3_interview_question\n# en: The Silent One's Question\ntitle [740fdd] = 沉默者的问题\n# en: At last the silent one speaks. \"Tell me about a time you did something you weren't proud of,\" they say, \"and whether you'd do it again.\"\ntext [d81e5e] = 沉默的那位终于开口了。“说说你做过的一件不光彩的事，”对方说，“以及你会不会再做一次。”\n# en: A small panelled room, an interviewer who hasn't spoken leaning forward over folded hands, a student on the other side of the table very still.\nscene [5eab0f] = 一间小小的镶板房间，一位始终没开口的面试官双手交握、身体前倾，桌子另一边的学生一动不动。\n# en: Tell the truth\nopt.left.label [d8bf00] = 说实话\n# en: You tell them the truth, all of it, and what you learned, and that you wouldn't. The silent one is quiet for a long moment. \"Thank you,\" they say, and it sounds like the end of a search.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a3687] = 你说了实话，全部，还有你学到了什么，以及你不会再做。沉默的那位安静了很久。“谢谢你，”对方说，听起来像一场寻找终于结束了。\n# en: You tell them the truth, and halfway through you realise you might do it again. So does the silent one. \"Honest,\" they say. It isn't clear whether that's good.\nopt.left.say.2 [694938] = 你说了实话，说到一半你意识到自己可能还会再做。沉默的那位也意识到了。“很诚实，”对方说。不清楚这是好是坏。\n# en: Tell them what they want to hear\nopt.right.label [2318d9] = 说他们想听的\n# en: A small mistake, gracefully regretted, neatly learned from. It isn't quite true. The silent one writes something down and underlines it.\nopt.right.say.1 [450ed2] = 一个小小的过失，体面地后悔，干净地吸取了教训。这不完全是真的。沉默的那位写下了什么，还划了线。\n# en: The story is too neat, and the silent one has heard it before, from better liars. \"I see,\" they say, and you hear the door closing.\nopt.right.say.2 [ae64c0] = 这个故事太完美了，沉默的那位从更会撒谎的人那里听过。“我明白了，”对方说，你听见了门关上的声音。\n\n@event y3_interview_verdict\n# en: We'll Be in Touch\ntitle [8502a1] = 我们会再联系你\n# en: \"We'll be in touch,\" says the smiling one, and stands, and so you stand too. In the corridor your legs feel like someone else's. {iv_score >= 3: You've never been so sure of anything.|{iv_score >= 2: You think it went well. You think.|{iv_score >= 1: It could have gone either way.|You know exactly how it went.}}}\ntext [0b6c26] = “我们会再联系你的，”爱笑的那位说着站了起来，于是你也站了起来。到了走廊里，你的腿像是别人的。{iv_score >= 3: 你从没对一件事这么有把握过。|{iv_score >= 2: 你觉得还不错。你觉得。|{iv_score >= 1: 结果可能是好是坏。|你清楚地知道结果如何。}}}\n# en: A student walking out of a panelled room into a bright corridor, loosening their collar, a closed door behind them.\nscene [6f60cb] = 一个学生走出镶板房间，来到明亮的走廊里，松了松领口，身后的门关着。\n# en: Wait for the letter\nopt.ok.label [b2e924] = 等那封信\n# en: You go back to your room and lie on the bed and don't move for an hour. Somewhere, three people are deciding.\nopt.ok.say.1 [bddf2b] = 你回到房间，躺在床上，一个小时一动不动。某个地方，三个人正在做决定。\n# en: You go back to your room and replay every answer. The better ones get better each time; the bad ones get worse.\nopt.ok.say.2 [7f2983] = 你回到房间，把每个回答都回放了一遍。好的那些每回放一次都更好，糟的那些更糟。\n# en: You go back to your room and don't replay anything at all. Some things you just know.\nopt.ok.say.3 [964087] = 你回到房间，什么都没有回放。有些事你就是知道。\n\n@event y3_waitlist\n# en: The Waiting List\ntitle [b22c0d] = 候补名单\n# en: A thin letter. You are \"a strong candidate\" and on \"a short list\", and they \"hope to write again\". It's not a no. It's not anything.\ntext [c3aabb] = 一封薄薄的信。你是“一位很有实力的候选人”，在“一份短名单”上，他们“希望能再次来信”。这不是拒绝。这什么也不是。\n# en: A thin envelope on a dormitory desk, a single page beside it, a student reading it a second time with their chin on their fist.\nscene [1828d6] = 薄薄的信封放在宿舍书桌上，旁边一页信纸，一个学生用拳头托着下巴，正读第二遍。\n# en: Write back and make your case\nopt.left.label [625a25] = 回信为自己争取\n# en: Two pages, plain and sure: why you, why them, why now. A fortnight later, a heavier envelope.\nopt.left.say.1 [7ac0fb] = 两页，朴实而笃定：为什么是你，为什么是他们，为什么是现在。两个星期后，来了一个更厚的信封。\n# en: You write two pages, then four, then a fifth you shouldn't send, and do. No heavier envelope comes.\nopt.left.say.2 [987fe8] = 你写了两页，然后四页，然后写了不该寄的第五页，还是寄了。更厚的信封一直没来。\n# en: Let it lie and see\nopt.right.label [73a78b] = 先放着，看看再说\n# en: You let it lie. Two months later, when you'd almost stopped looking, the heavier envelope comes after all.\nopt.right.say.1 [b8c566] = 你把它放着。两个月后，就在你快要不再留意的时候，那个更厚的信封终究还是来了。\n# en: You let it lie. It stays lying. Other paths open; you try not to look back at this one.\nopt.right.say.2 [080470] = 你把它放着。它就一直放着。别的路打开了；你尽量不回头看这一条。\n\n@event y3_rejection\n# en: A Polite Letter\ntitle [d7fd3b] = 一封客气的信\n# en: A short letter, very politely written. They were \"impressed by your enthusiasm\". They regret. They wish you every success.\ntext [a0b886] = 一封短信，写得非常客气。他们“对你的热情印象深刻”。他们很遗憾。他们祝你一切顺利。\n# en: A crumpled letter on a windowsill, a student looking out at the lake with their arms folded.\nscene [c16037] = 一封揉皱的信放在窗台上，一个学生抱着胳膊望向湖面。\n# en: Ask them what went wrong\nopt.left.label [7fa9d5] = 问他们哪里出了问题\n# en: You write and ask. To your surprise they answer, frankly, two pages of it. It stings, and it's the most useful thing anyone's told you all year.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a0ada] = 你写信去问。出乎意料，他们坦率地回答了，足足两页。这很刺痛，也是今年别人告诉你的最有用的东西。\n# en: Throw it in the lake\nopt.right.label [0e9dff] = 扔进湖里\n# en: You fold it into a boat and launch it from the jetty. It sinks with great dignity. Some doors close; there are a great many doors.\nopt.right.say.1 [dda54f] = 你把它折成一只小船，从码头放下水。它极有尊严地沉了下去。有些门关上了；门多的是。\n\n@event y3_offer_letter\n# en: The Offer\ntitle [d66d52] = 录用通知\n# en: A heavy envelope with a seal you recognise from the fair. Inside, a single page: an offer. A place. A future with a start date. {iv_score >= 3: There is a signing purse, too, which they call \"a token\".|} The contract itself is signed on the last day, with any other you've had.\ntext [e81c08] = 一个沉甸甸的信封，封蜡你在招聘会上见过。里面只有一页纸：一份聘书。一个位置。一个有入职日期的未来。{iv_score >= 3: 还附了一笔签约金，他们称之为“一点心意”。|}合同本身要在最后一天签，和你收到的其他聘书一起。\n# en: An envelope with a heavy wax seal on a dormitory desk, unopened, a hand hovering over it.\nscene [fc8326] = 宿舍书桌上一个盖着厚重火漆的信封，还没拆开，一只手悬在上面。\n# en: Say yes today\nopt.left.label [f4801a] = 今天就答应\n# en: You write \"yes\" so hard the nib goes through. The contract will wait for the last day, they write back, but the place is held for you. You pin the letter above your bed and look at it every night.\nopt.left.say.1 [eafaa5] = 你写下“愿意”，笔尖都戳破了纸。他们回信说，合同等最后一天再签，但位置给你留着。你把信钉在床头，每晚都看一眼。\n# en: Ask them to wait\nopt.right.label [3eda22] = 请他们再等等\n# en: You write back asking for time. They grant it, coolly: the offer stands until the last day, and not an hour after.\nopt.right.say.1 [c15b0e] = 你回信请他们宽限些时间。他们冷淡地答应了：聘书在最后一天之前有效，晚一个钟头都不行。\n\n@event y3_first_year_mentee\n# en: Your First-Year\ntitle [891c46] = 你的一年级生\n# en: Fourth-years are assigned a first-year to mentor. Yours is called Pim, is about the size of a large cat, and has already asked forty-one questions, including \"are the stairs alive\" and \"are YOU alive\".\ntext [0665b1] = 四年级生都会分配到一个一年级生来带。你的那个叫皮姆，个头和一只大猫差不多，已经问了四十一个问题，包括“楼梯是活的吗”和“你是活的吗”。\n# en: A tiny first-year trotting beside a fourth-year across the courtyard, firing questions, clutching an enormous timetable.\nscene [7ece82] = 一个小小的一年级生小跑着跟在四年级生身边穿过庭院，连珠炮似的发问，怀里紧抱着一张巨大的课表。\n# en: Answer every question honestly\nopt.left.label [54cf3c] = 老老实实回答每个问题\n# en: Yes, the stairs are alive. Yes, you are alive. No, the lake is not \"friendly\". Pim takes notes. By spring, Pim is explaining things to other first-years, wrongly but with total confidence.\nopt.left.say.1 [e5db2e] = 是的，楼梯是活的。是的，你是活的。不，湖并不“友好”。皮姆一一记下。到了春天，皮姆已经在给别的一年级生讲解了——讲错了，但无比自信。\n# en: Teach Pim to find answers alone\nopt.right.label [9c42d4] = 教皮姆自己找答案\n# en: You hand Pim a library card and a map. Pim is furious for a week, then unstoppable. The librarian now knows Pim by name. And fears Pim, slightly.\nopt.right.say.1 [515043] = 你递给皮姆一张借书证和一张地图。皮姆气了一个星期，然后就势不可挡了。图书管理员现在叫得出皮姆的名字。而且有一点怕皮姆。\n\n@event y3_last_lanterns\n# en: The Last Lantern Night\ntitle [781522] = 最后一个灯笼夜\n# en: Your last Lantern Night. You stand on the shore with an unlit lantern and a pen and realise you have no idea what to wish for. Three years ago it was obvious. Now it isn't.\ntext [a78390] = 你的最后一个灯笼夜。你站在湖岸上，手里是一盏没点的灯笼和一支笔，忽然发现自己完全不知道该许什么愿。三年前答案再明显不过。现在不是了。\n# en: A lone student on the shore at night holding an unlit lantern, hundreds of others already drifting over the dark lake.\nscene [2d5995] = 夜里，一名学生独自站在岸边，手捧一盏未点亮的灯笼，数百盏灯笼已经飘向漆黑的湖面。\n# en: Wish for the people you'll leave behind\nopt.left.label [af62f4] = 为要留下的人们许愿\n# en: You write all their names. It takes the whole lantern. It rises slowly, heavily, and you watch it until it is just a star among stars.\nopt.left.say.1 [a49d3f] = 你写下了他们所有人的名字，写满了整盏灯笼。它缓缓地、沉甸甸地升起，你一直望着它，直到它变成群星中的一颗。\n# en: Leave it blank\nopt.right.label [54fbdb] = 留白\n# en: You let it go empty. Strangely, it rises higher than all the others. Maybe the lake likes not knowing either.\nopt.right.say.1 [154e37] = 你放走了一盏空灯笼。奇怪的是，它比其他所有灯笼都升得更高。也许湖也喜欢不知道答案。\n\n@event y3_thesis\n# en: The Last Chapter\ntitle [87e9c9] = 最后一章\n# en: Your thesis needs one more chapter. You could write something safe and solid, or chase the question your sources keep circling and nobody has answered in three hundred years: why the academy was built above a lake.\ntext [5724af] = 你的论文还差最后一章。你可以写点稳妥扎实的东西，也可以去追那个你的资料总在打转、三百年来无人解答的问题：学院为什么要建在湖上？\n# en: A desk buried in drafts, a wall of pinned notes connected by string, a single candle and a single exhausted student.\nscene [df58af] = 一张被草稿淹没的书桌，一面钉满笔记、用细绳相连的墙，一支蜡烛，一个筋疲力尽的学生。\n# en: Safe and solid\nopt.left.label [f81e8e] = 稳妥扎实\n# en: Forty pages on the thermodynamics of hovering charms. Impeccable. Unremarkable. Your examiners will sleep well.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe97ef] = 四十页论悬浮咒的热力学。无可挑剔，平平无奇。你的考官们会睡得很香。\n# en: The question nobody answered\nopt.right.label [e94f06] = 那个无人解答的问题\n# en: The chapter is so good that your supervisor shows it round the staff room, and the headmistress asks for the only copy — and then, very politely, does not give it back.\nopt.right.say.1 [e0a83d] = 这一章好到导师拿着它在教员休息室里到处给人看，院长还亲自索要了唯一的一份——然后，非常客气地，没有还回来。\n# en: You chase the question until it runs out of archive. The chapter ends mid-sentence, like the founders' ledger. The examiners are not amused.\nopt.right.say.2 [044bc6] = 你追着这个问题一直追到档案的尽头。这一章在一句话中间戛然而止，就像创始者的账簿。考官们一点也不觉得有趣。\n\n@event y3_portrait\n# en: Sitting for the Portrait\ntitle [a99086] = 坐着画像\n# en: Every graduate's portrait hangs in the long gallery — and every portrait, eventually, starts talking. You have one sitting to decide who will be gossiping on that wall for the next three hundred years.\ntext [d9377f] = 每位毕业生的画像都会挂在长廊里——而每幅画像迟早都会开口说话。你只有一次坐下来的机会，来决定接下来三百年在那面墙上说闲话的会是什么样的人。\n# en: An old painter at an easel in a sunlit studio, a student posing stiffly in robes, half-finished portraits of past graduates watching from the walls.\nscene [2a1426] = 阳光充足的画室里，一位老画家站在画架前，一名身着长袍的学生僵硬地摆着姿势，往届毕业生未完成的画像从墙上注视着他们。\n# en: Pose solemn and dignified\nopt.left.label [f39072] = 庄重得体地摆姿势\n# en: The painter captures you looking wise and distant. Your portrait will be dignified. It will also be dreadfully bored.\nopt.left.say.1 [af471d] = 画家画出了你睿智而疏远的神情。你的画像会很体面。它也会无聊得要命。\n# en: Pose as yourself, mid-laugh\nopt.right.label [e9bd51] = 做你自己，笑到一半\n# en: Your portrait is laughing. Three days after it's hung, it starts telling the other portraits jokes. The gallery has never been louder.\nopt.right.say.1 [c325e4] = 你的画像在笑。挂上去三天后，它开始给其他画像讲笑话。长廊从来没有这么吵过。\n\n@event y3_letter_to_self\n# en: A Letter to Your First-Year Self\ntitle [64655e] = 写给一年级的自己\n# en: The Divination department runs a tradition: write a letter to your first-year self. They swear it will be delivered. Nobody knows how. Some fourth-years swear they received theirs.\ntext [eb06b6] = 占卜系有个传统：给一年级的自己写一封信。他们发誓信一定会送到，没人知道是怎么送的。有些四年级生发誓自己收到过。\n# en: A fourth-year writing by candlelight, a box of keepsakes open beside them: a pressed flower, a ticket stub, a borrowed wand returned.\nscene [61521a] = 一个四年级生在烛光下写信，身旁打开着一个纪念品盒子：一朵压花、一张票根、一根借来又还掉的魔杖。\n# en: Tell them it'll be all right\nopt.left.label [c88d16] = 告诉自己一切都会好的\n# en: You write: \"It's going to be all right. Not easy. All right.\" The next morning the letter is gone, and you remember, very faintly, reading it once on a sky-barge.\nopt.left.say.1 [1f6e42] = 你写道：“会好起来的。不会容易，但会好起来。”第二天早上信不见了，而你隐约记起，自己曾在一艘天空驳船上读过它。\n# en: You try to write something comforting, and it keeps coming out as a list of warnings. You seal it anyway. The next morning it is gone.\nopt.left.say.2 [8efc83] = 你想写些安慰的话，可写出来总像一张警告清单。你还是把信封好了。第二天早上，它不见了。\n# en: Warn them about everything\nopt.right.label [065681] = 把所有事都警告一遍\n# en: You write eleven pages of warnings. The letter is returned unopened with a note in Divination ink: \"Some things must be learned the hard way.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [4ed2e4] = 你写了十一页的警告。信原封未动地退了回来，附着一张用占卜墨水写的字条：“有些事必须吃过苦头才学得会。”\n\n@event y3_maud_seeds\n# en: Seeds for Later\ntitle [6b0198] = 留给以后的种子\n# en: Maud presses a paper packet into your hands. \"Seeds,\" she says. \"From the secret garden. Plant them wherever you end up. They'll grow anywhere someone is a bit kind to them.\" She sniffs. \"Like you did.\"\ntext [59075b] = 莫德把一个纸包塞进你手里。“种子，”她说，“秘密花园里的。不管你最后去了哪儿，都种下去。只要有人对它们好一点，它们在哪儿都能长。”她吸了吸鼻子。“就像你一样。”\n# en: An old woman pressing a small paper packet of seeds into a student's hands at the garden gate, bees drifting in the evening light.\nscene [f66df9] = 花园门口，一位老妇人把一小包种子按进学生手里，暮色中蜜蜂缓缓飞舞。\n# en: Hug her\nopt.left.label [042130] = 拥抱她\n# en: She protests for exactly one second and then hugs back, fierce and small and smelling of rosemary. \"Go on,\" she says. \"Before I get soppy.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [51b153] = 她抗议了整整一秒钟，然后用力回抱你，又小又结实，带着迷迭香的味道。“去吧，”她说，“趁我还没变得肉麻。”\n# en: Promise to come back and visit\nopt.right.label [17c08e] = 答应回来看她\n# en: \"Every summer,\" you promise. \"We'll see,\" she says, but she writes it on her calendar, in ink, which for Maud is a blood oath.\nopt.right.say.1 [96e47e] = “每年夏天都来，”你保证。“走着瞧，”她说，却把这件事写在了日历上——用墨水写的，对莫德来说这等于血誓。\n\n@event y3_last_duel\n# en: One Last Duel\ntitle [15df69] = 最后一场决斗\n# en: Dawn, the empty dueling ring, no crowd. Lyra is waiting. \"No referee,\" she says. \"No points. Just one last duel, for us. So we know.\"\ntext [5b0db9] = 黎明，空荡荡的决斗场，没有观众。莱拉在等你。“没有裁判，”她说，“不计分。就最后一场决斗，为了我们自己。这样我们就知道了。”\n# en: Two duelists bowing in an empty dueling ring at dawn, no crowd, no referee, just the mist and the lake beyond.\nscene [b6c6d5] = 黎明时分，两名决斗者在空无一人的决斗场里互相鞠躬，没有观众，没有裁判，只有薄雾和远处的湖。\n# en: Duel her with everything\nopt.left.label [9ed8a1] = 全力以赴和她决斗\n# en: It lasts eleven minutes. Nobody wins. You both end up flat on your backs, laughing at the sky. \"Now we know,\" she says.\nopt.left.say.1 [e4826f] = 打了十一分钟。没有人赢。最后你们俩都仰面躺在地上，对着天空大笑。“现在我们知道了，”她说。\n# en: She wins, cleanly. Then she helps you up and doesn't let go of your hand for a moment longer than necessary. \"Now we know.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [cc0906] = 她干净利落地赢了。然后她把你拉起来，握着你的手比必要的多停了一会儿。“现在我们知道了。”\n# en: Lower your wand and just talk\nopt.right.label [075cd4] = 放下魔杖，只是聊聊\n# en: You talk until the mist burns off. About fire, and mothers, and what comes after. You both miss the morning lecture. It turns out you didn't need to duel to know.\nopt.right.say.1 [e438e7] = 你们一直聊到薄雾散尽。聊火焰，聊母亲，聊以后的事。你们俩都错过了早课。原来不需要决斗也能知道。\n\n@event y3_time_capsule\n# en: The Time Capsule\ntitle [f63962] = 时间胶囊\n# en: Your friends have a battered tin box, a shovel and a plan: bury a time capsule under the old oak, to be opened in twenty years. Everyone puts something in. What will you give?\ntext [950958] = 你的朋友们带来了一个破旧的铁皮盒、一把铲子和一个计划：在老橡树下埋一个时间胶囊，二十年后再打开。每个人都要放点东西进去。你放什么？\n# en: A group of students kneeling around a hole under an old oak, lowering in a battered tin box sealed with wax.\nscene [cd24b8] = 一群学生跪在老橡树下的坑边，把一个用蜡封好的破铁皮盒放进去。\n# en: Something precious\nopt.left.label [3a8593] = 放一件珍贵的东西\n# en: You put in the first thing you ever enchanted. It's ugly and crooked and it still hums. Someone cries, and blames the dirt.\nopt.left.say.1 [1fe988] = 你放进了你施过魔法的第一件东西。它又丑又歪，至今还在嗡嗡作响。有人哭了，然后怪是土进了眼睛。\n# en: Something ridiculous\nopt.right.label [957c3e] = 放一件荒唐的东西\n# en: You put in a single sock, labelled \"EVIDENCE\". In twenty years, nobody will remember why. That's the point.\nopt.right.say.1 [fa92c3] = 你放进了一只袜子，上面贴着标签：“证物”。二十年后，没人会记得为什么。这正是意义所在。\n\n@event y3_speech\n# en: The Graduation Speech\ntitle [ffc856] = 毕业致辞\n# en: The headmistress catches you in the great hall, where chairs are already being set out. \"Each year one graduate speaks for their class,\" she says. \"This year, the faculty would like it to be you.\"\ntext [eebc6f] = 院长在大礼堂里叫住了你，那里已经开始摆椅子了。“每年都由一位毕业生代表全班致辞，”她说，“今年，教职员们希望是你。”\n# en: A headmistress handing a scroll to a student in an empty great hall, rows of chairs set out for a ceremony that hasn't happened yet.\nscene [9ef184] = 空荡荡的大礼堂里，一位院长把一卷纸递给一名学生，一排排椅子已为尚未举行的典礼摆好。\n# en: Accept and write something honest\nopt.left.label [d1eed2] = 接受，写些真心话\n# en: You write about fear, and friends, and the lake, and the things you didn't know you'd miss. When you practise it alone, your voice cracks in the same place every time. You leave it in.\nopt.left.say.1 [35d90b] = 你写恐惧，写朋友，写湖，写那些你从不知道自己会怀念的东西。独自练习时，你的声音每次都在同一个地方哽住。你把它留在了稿子里。\n# en: Decline — someone else deserves it\nopt.right.label [121bee] = 婉拒——值得的另有其人\n# en: \"Interesting,\" says the headmistress, which from her is a medal. The speech goes to a nervous first-generation student who absolutely brings the house down.\nopt.right.say.1 [9ddc3d] = “有意思，”院长说——从她嘴里说出来，这就是一枚勋章。致辞的机会给了一位紧张的、家里第一代上学的学生，那位学生把全场都点燃了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/festivals.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/festivals.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event starfall_jars\n# en: Star Jars\ntitle [b671d5] = 星罐\n# en: {The Stargazers have set up tables under the dome, and everyone is making a jar to catch a falling star in tonight. Nobody has caught one in years. Everyone tries.|The jar tables are out under the dome again, and the first-years are making theirs all wrong.|Star jars again. You know which glass holds and which cracks in the cold.} {The Stargazers are arguing about the forecast.|Somebody has painted a jar entirely gold.|The telescope keeps swinging toward the lake.}\ntext [495f07] = {观星社在穹顶下摆好了桌子，每个人都在做一只罐子，好在今晚接住一颗坠落的星星。好些年没人接住过了。人人都还是要试。|罐子桌又摆到了穹顶下，一年级生们的罐子全做错了。|又是星罐。哪种玻璃经得住寒气、哪种会裂，你已经心里有数了。} {观星社的人在争论天气预报。|有人把一只罐子整个涂成了金色。|望远镜总是往湖的方向转。}\n# en: Long trestle tables under the observatory dome covered in glass jars, students painting stars on them, one of the Stargazers handing out corks.\nscene [04d125] = 观星台穹顶下的长条桌上摆满了玻璃罐，学生们在上面画星星，一位观星社的社员在分发软木塞。\n# en: Make a proper jar\nopt.left.label [2c03ab] = 做一只像样的罐子\n# en: {Thick glass, a cork sealed with wax, a rune for luck scratched into the lid. \"Better odds tonight,\" says one of the Stargazers. \"Theoretically.\"|You buy the good glass and take your time over it. It's the best jar on the table, and you know it.|You pick the glass that holds in the cold without thinking, and seal it twice. It's the jar the first-years copy.}\nopt.left.say.1 [752062] = {厚玻璃，用蜡封的软木塞，盖子上刻一道祈求好运的符文。“今晚的胜算更大了，”一位观星社的社员说。“理论上。”|你买了好玻璃，慢慢地做。这是桌上最好的一只罐子，你自己也知道。|你想都没想就挑了耐冻的玻璃，封了两道。一年级生们照着做的就是你这只。}\n# en: Help the Stargazers set up the roof\nopt.right.label [7691a1] = 帮观星社布置屋顶\n# en: {You haul blankets and telescopes up the observatory stairs all afternoon. Your arms ache. The Stargazers save you the best spot on the roof.|Blankets, cushions, a kettle that has to be carried up four hundred steps. The Stargazers know your name by the end of it.|You haul the blankets up and turn the telescope back from the lake three times. It keeps swinging back. The Stargazers give up and save you a spot.}\nopt.right.say.1 [73b06c] = {你一下午都在把毯子和望远镜往观星台的楼梯上搬。胳膊酸疼。观星社给你留了屋顶上最好的位置。|毯子、坐垫，还有一只得扛着爬四百级台阶的水壶。到最后，观星社的人都记住了你的名字。|你把毯子搬上去，三次把望远镜从湖那边转回来。它总是又转回去。观星社的人放弃了，给你留了个位置。}\n\n@event starfall_together\n# en: A Blanket for Two\ntitle [7474ae] = 两人一条毯子\n# en: You find {partner} on the lawn below the observatory with a blanket already spread for two and a flask of something hot. \"I saved you the sky,\" {they} say{s}. The first star falls before you've sat down.\ntext [894c38] = 你在观星台下的草坪上找到了 {partner}，毯子已经为两个人铺好了，还带着一壶热乎乎的东西。“我给你留了一片天空，”{they} 说。你还没坐下，第一颗星星就落了下来。\n# en: Two students lying on a blanket on the lawn at night, wrapped in one shawl, a falling star streaking over the castle towers.\nscene [cd2222] = 夜晚的草坪上，两个学生裹着同一条披肩躺在毯子上，一颗流星划过城堡的塔楼。\n# en: Stay out until dawn\nopt.left.label [b0f836] = 在外面待到天亮\n# en: You stay out until the sky turns grey, counting stars and then not counting anything. You're both frozen and hoarse and late for breakfast, and it was worth all of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [ce070c] = 你们一直待到天色发灰，先是数星星，后来什么也不数了。你们俩都冻僵了，嗓子也哑了，早饭也迟到了，而这一切都值得。\n# en: Tell {them} what you wished for\nopt.right.label [28c1f2] = 告诉 {them} 你许了什么愿\n# en: You say it out loud, which you're told ruins a wish. {partner} goes very quiet and then tells you {their} own. Neither of you minds if it's ruined.\nopt.right.say.1 [069e3e] = 你把它说出了口，据说这样愿望就不灵了。{partner} 安静了很久，然后也告诉了你 {their}愿望。你们俩都不在乎它灵不灵了。\n\n@event starfall_wish\n# en: A Wish on a Falling Star\ntitle [a426d8] = 向流星许愿\n# en: The oldest Starfall custom: pick one star, the brightest you'll see all night, and give it one wish. The Stargazers say a wish you spend on someone else falls slower. Nobody knows what that means. A very bright one is coming down over the lake.\ntext [c2e706] = 星坠节最古老的习俗：挑一颗星，这一夜你能看到的最亮的那颗，把一个愿望交给它。观星社的人说，为别人许的愿，星星会落得慢一些。没人知道这是什么意思。一颗非常亮的星正朝湖面落下。\n# en: A student kneeling on the observatory roof with eyes closed, hands clasped, a bright star falling over the dark lake below.\nscene [ba4b65] = 一个学生跪在观星台屋顶上，闭着眼睛，双手合十，一颗明亮的星星正落向下方漆黑的湖面。\n# en: Wish for your own year\nopt.left.label [e770b9] = 为自己的这一年许愿\n# en: You wish hard for your own year, and the star burns out over the water. It feels like a promise you've made to yourself, which is heavier than you expected.\nopt.left.say.1 [3035ed] = 你用力地为自己的这一年许了愿，那颗星在水面上燃尽了。这感觉像是对自己许下的承诺，比你想象的要沉。\n# en: Wish for someone else\nopt.right.label [bc6686] = 为别人许愿\n# en: You don't say who. The star falls slowly, or you imagine it does, all the way down to the lake.\nopt.right.say.1 [7dd3ec] = 你没说是为谁。那颗星落得很慢，或者是你以为它很慢，一直落进了湖里。\n\n@event starfall_caught\n# en: The Star in the Jar\ntitle [3b66bd] = 罐中之星\n# en: The star you caught at Starfall still glows in its jar on your windowsill, a little dimmer each night. A collector in the village has offered good money for it. One of the Stargazers says it would like to go back up, if stars like anything.\ntext [f775be] = 你在星坠节接住的那颗星，还在窗台上的罐子里发着光，一夜比一夜暗一点。村里有位收藏家出了个好价钱。一位观星社的社员说，如果星星也会想要什么的话，它想回到天上去。\n# en: A glass jar on a dormitory windowsill holding a tiny glowing star, a pale light on the curtains, frost on the outside of the glass.\nscene [c5924e] = 宿舍窗台上的一只玻璃罐里装着一颗发光的小星星，窗帘上映着淡淡的光，玻璃外结了霜。\n# en: Keep it by your bed\nopt.keep.label [b48f7a] = 把它放在床边\n# en: You keep it, and buy oil to keep the window frost off it. It fades by spring, slowly, like a coal. The last night it glows, you sit up with it.\nopt.keep.say.1 [fb1cf4] = 你留下了它，还买了灯油来防止窗户结霜。它到春天才慢慢熄灭，像一块炭。它发光的最后一夜，你一直陪着它。\n# en: Sell it to the collector\nopt.sell.label [90e67f] = 卖给收藏家\n# en: The collector holds it up to the light and pays without haggling. The windowsill looks very dark that night.\nopt.sell.say.1 [30c842] = 收藏家把它举到灯下，一分没还价就付了钱。那天晚上，窗台显得格外暗。\n# en: Let it go from the roof\nopt.release.label [6abecc] = 从屋顶放它走\n# en: You climb the observatory at midnight and open the jar. It hangs there a moment, then rises, slow and sure, until you can't tell it from the others. A few people saw. The story goes round by breakfast.\nopt.release.say.1 [233362] = 你半夜爬上观星台，打开了罐子。它在那儿悬停了片刻，然后缓缓地、坚定地升起，直到你再也分不清它和其他星星。有几个人看见了。到早饭时，这个故事已经传开了。\n\n@event starfall_at_home\n# en: Starfall at Home\ntitle [e21d08] = 家中的星坠节\n# en: {Starfall at home. There's no observatory here, only the cottage roof and the whole family on it in quilts, as when you were small.|Starfall at home again. Someone has saved you the warm patch of roof by the chimney.|The roof, the quilts, the chimney. Someone asks what the academy does for Starfall, and you find you'd rather be here.} {The village dogs howl at every star.|Somebody's brought cocoa.|A neighbour is telling the one about the star that fell in the duck pond.}\ntext [0fc251] = {家里的星坠节。这里没有观星台，只有小屋的屋顶，全家人裹着被子坐在上面，就像你小时候那样。|又是在家过星坠节。有人给你留了烟囱旁那块暖和的屋顶。|屋顶、被子、烟囱。有人问学院里怎么过星坠节，你发现自己更愿意待在这儿。} {村里的狗冲着每一颗星星嚎叫。|有人带了热可可。|一位邻居在讲那颗星星掉进鸭塘的老故事。}\n# en: A family on the roof of a cottage at night, wrapped in quilts, a grandmother pointing at a streak of light across the sky.\nscene [bb653e] = 夜里，一家人裹着被子坐在小屋的屋顶上，一位老奶奶指着划过天空的一道光。\n# en: Tell them about the academy\nopt.left.label [e245d0] = 给他们讲学院的事\n# en: {You tell them about the towers and the lake and the staircase that moves, and they ask questions until the stars run out. It's past three when you climb down, cold to the bone and glad.|Everyone wants a story. You give them the best ones, slightly improved, until your feet have gone numb on the slates. Nobody minds.|You tell them about the academy, and then the neighbour tells the one about the duck pond, which is better. You laugh until the slates are cold.}\nopt.left.say.1 [70d425] = {你给他们讲塔楼、湖，还有会移动的楼梯，他们一直问到星星落完。你爬下屋顶时已经过了三点，冻透了，心里却很高兴。|每个人都想听故事。你把最好的那些讲给他们听，稍微润色了一下，一直讲到脚在瓦片上冻麻了。没人介意。|你给他们讲学院的事，然后邻居讲了鸭塘那个故事，讲得比你好。你们笑到瓦片都凉了。}\n# en: Slip off to the hill alone\nopt.right.label [1f9874] = 独自溜到山坡上\n# en: {From the hill behind the cottage you name every star the way they taught you, and a few the way you learned as a child. Both names are right.|Alone on the hill, you watch the sky the way an academy student does now, and miss, a little, the way you used to.|From the hill you can still hear the neighbour telling the one about the duck pond. You find you'd rather be on the roof, and walk back before the end.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d6c0a0] = {在小屋后面的山坡上，你按学院教的方法给每颗星星命名，又按小时候学的叫法叫了几颗。两个名字都对。|你独自在山坡上，用学院学生的眼光看着天空，有一点怀念从前看天的样子。|在山坡上，你还能听见邻居在讲鸭塘那个故事。你发现自己更想待在屋顶上，没等故事讲完就走了回去。}\n\n@event starfall_on_internship\n# en: Starfall Far From Home\ntitle [c56ee2] = 远离家乡的星坠节\n# en: Starfall, and you're far from the observatory roof. {member(intern_expedition): Up at the ridge camp the sky is enormous, and the expedition's astronomers are working through it, counting every streak.|{member(intern_thornwick): Thornwick marks Starfall with illusions, and the lawn is full of false stars falling among the real ones. {met(tamsin): Tamsin finds you and points out which ones are theirs.|}|{member(intern_field_station): At the field station the creatures are restless under the falling stars, and someone has to sit up with them.|{member(intern_royal_library) or member(intern_unseen_office): The city has no lawns, only rooftops, and every one of them has people on it tonight.|The other interns have found a flat roof and a crate of something fizzy, and your supervisor is pretending not to know.}}}}\ntext [daf3a0] = 星坠节，而你离观星台的屋顶很远。{member(intern_expedition): 山脊营地上的天空无比辽阔，考察队的天文学家们正在一颗颗地记录，每一道流光都不放过。|{member(intern_thornwick): 荆木学院用幻术庆祝星坠节，草坪上假的星星和真的星星一起坠落。{met(tamsin): 塔姆辛找到了你，指给你看哪些是 TA 做的。|}|{member(intern_field_station): 在野外观测站，动物们在坠落的星光下躁动不安，总得有人守着它们。|{member(intern_royal_library) or member(intern_unseen_office): 城里没有草坪，只有屋顶，而今晚每一片屋顶上都有人。|其他实习生找到了一片平屋顶和一箱冒泡的饮料，你的导师假装不知道。}}}}\n# en: A group of interns on a flat roof far from the academy, wrapped in coats, watching falling stars over unfamiliar rooftops.\nscene [2a4dd5] = 远离学院的一片平屋顶上，一群实习生裹着外套，看着陌生屋顶上空坠落的星星。\n# en: Join the others on the roof\nopt.left.label [a3d8dd] = 和其他人一起上屋顶\n# en: {~You lie on a strange roof among people you met two months ago and count stars until they feel like old friends. Tomorrow's work goes slowly.|Coats, a borrowed blanket, someone's terrible song. It's not the observatory roof, and it's good anyway, though the morning's work comes out half asleep.}\nopt.left.say.1 [985ac4] = {~你躺在一片陌生的屋顶上，身边是两个月前才认识的人，数星星数到他们仿佛成了老朋友。第二天的工作进展很慢。|外套、借来的毯子、某人糟糕透顶的歌。这不是观星台的屋顶，但也很好，只是第二天早上的活儿干得半梦半醒。}\n# en: Keep working through the night\nopt.right.label [8ae9d3] = 通宵工作\n# en: {~You work through it with the window open, glancing up now and then. Your supervisor finds the finished work on the desk at dawn and raises an eyebrow, which is a great deal from them.|The stars fall outside while you finish the job. You see a few. Your supervisor sees all of it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [88a708] = {~你开着窗工作了一整夜，时不时抬头看一眼。导师在黎明时发现桌上完成的工作，扬了扬眉毛，对他们来说这已经是很高的评价了。|星星在窗外坠落，你在屋里把活干完。你看到了几颗。你的导师看到了全部。}\n\n@event ball_robes\n# en: Something to Wear\ntitle [d096f3] = 穿什么好\n# en: {year == 1: The Midwinter Ball is tonight, and you have nothing to wear that isn't a uniform or a nightshirt.|{final_year: The last ball of your life as a student. The old robes look very tired.|{~Ball night again, and last year's robes have shrunk, somehow, in the trunk.|Ball night again. Your robes have a stain on the cuff you'd forgotten about.|Ball night again. The robes fit, but you're tired of the sight of them.}}} {~The village tailor is open till three.|Your roommate is already dressed and gloating.|The mirror has opinions.}\ntext [f420a9] = {year == 1: 仲冬舞会就在今晚，而你除了校服和睡衣，什么能穿的都没有。|{final_year: 你学生时代的最后一场舞会。旧礼袍看起来疲惫极了。|{~又到舞会之夜，去年的礼袍不知怎么在箱子里缩水了。|又到舞会之夜。你的礼袍袖口上有块你早忘了的污渍。|又到舞会之夜。礼袍倒是合身，可你已经看腻了。}}} {~村里的裁缝铺开到三点。|你的室友已经穿戴整齐，正在得意。|镜子有自己的看法。}\n# en: A dormitory room strewn with robes, a student holding up a moth-eaten formal robe against themselves before a cracked mirror.\nscene [e36b69] = 宿舍里散落着各式礼袍，一个学生站在裂了缝的镜子前，把一件被虫蛀过的礼袍比在身上。\n# en: Buy new robes in the village\nopt.left.label [b6ca12] = 去村里买新礼袍\n# en: {~Deep blue, with a lining like the night sky. In the tailor's mirror you look, for once, like someone in a portrait.|New robes, a sensible colour and an insensible price. They fit as if they were waiting for you.|Something bold, chosen in a hurry. The mirror's opinions, for once, are all good ones.}\nopt.left.say.1 [97f189] = {~深蓝色，衬里像夜空。在裁缝铺的镜子里，你难得一次看起来像画像里的人。|新礼袍，颜色稳重，价格不稳重。合身得像是一直在等你。|匆匆挑了一件大胆的。镜子这回的意见，难得全是好话。}\n# en: Glamour your old ones\nopt.right.label [f20065] = 给旧礼袍施个幻术\n# en: The old robes shimmer into silk. Nobody guesses, and three people ask where you had them made.\nopt.right.say.1 [3d5149] = 旧礼袍闪烁着变成了丝绸。没人看出来，还有三个人问你是在哪儿做的。\n# en: At the stroke of nine your glamour slips, and the mended elbow shows through the silk like a confession. You keep your arm by your side all night.\nopt.right.say.2 [76df3a] = 九点钟一到，你的幻术失效了，补过的手肘透过丝绸露了出来，像一份供词。你整晚都把胳膊贴在身侧。\n\n@event ball_ice_garden\n# en: The Ice Garden\ntitle [ed8110] = 冰之花园\n# en: {The frost-mages have turned the courtyard into an ice garden for the ball. Frozen roses line the paths, and the fountain has stopped mid-splash.|The ice garden again, bigger this year, with a hedge maze of frost.|The ice garden, and you know its paths by heart now.} {Couples are walking it before the music has started.|A first-year has got their tongue stuck to a rose.|The lanterns make the ice glow blue.}\ntext [398f71] = {霜法师们把庭院变成了一座迎接舞会的冰之花园。冰冻的玫瑰排列在小径两旁，喷泉停在了水花溅起的那一刻。|又是冰之花园，今年更大了，还有一座霜做的树篱迷宫。|冰之花园，你现在已经熟悉它的每一条小径了。} {音乐还没响起，情侣们已经在里面漫步了。|一个一年级生的舌头粘在了一朵玫瑰上。|灯笼让冰透出蓝光。}\n# en: A castle courtyard turned into a garden of ice, frozen roses along glittering paths, a fountain stopped mid-splash, students walking under lanterns.\nscene [47b745] = 城堡庭院变成了一座冰的花园，闪光的小径旁是冰冻的玫瑰，喷泉停在水花溅起的一刻，学生们在灯笼下漫步。\n# en: Walk it slowly\nopt.left.label [3e196d] = 慢慢地逛\n# en: {You walk every path twice, until your toes are numb and your head is clear. The fountain's frozen spray catches the light like a held breath.|You find the centre of the maze, where someone has left a frozen bench and a frozen apple. You sit a while, until the cold comes up through your shoes.|You know the paths by heart, so you take the ones nobody else finds. Under the blue lanterns you have the garden to yourself for a while.}\nopt.left.say.1 [db9c17] = {每条小径你都走了两遍，直到脚趾冻麻、脑袋清醒。喷泉冻住的水花映着光，像一口屏住的呼吸。|你找到了迷宫的中心，那里有人留下了一张冰冻的长椅和一个冰冻的苹果。你坐了一会儿，直到寒气从鞋底往上钻。|小径你都烂熟于心，于是专挑别人找不到的走。在蓝色的灯笼下，花园有一阵子只属于你。}\n# en: Help the frost-mages finish it\nopt.right.label [946705] = 帮霜法师们收尾\n# en: {They show you how to hold the cold in a petal until it sets. Your hands ache for an hour. One of the roses on the path is yours.|You shape a whole archway of ice under instruction, fingers stiff with cold, and it holds. The frost-mages nod, which is how they applaud.|This year they let you shape a lantern of ice. It glows blue on the path, and nobody knows it's yours but you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f206d8] = {他们教你怎么把寒气留在花瓣里直到它定形。你的手疼了一个小时。小径上有一朵玫瑰是你做的。|你在指导下塑出了一整座冰拱门，手指冻得发僵，而它立住了。霜法师们点了点头，他们就是这样鼓掌的。|今年他们让你塑了一盏冰灯笼。它在小径上发着蓝光，除了你，谁也不知道是你做的。}\n# en: Wear the midnight-blue\nopt.up.label [c3e196] = 穿上午夜蓝\n# en: {In the midnight-blue among the frozen roses, people turn to look, and keep looking. You walk the paths slowly on purpose.|Someone asks where you had your robes made. Someone else asks who you are. Your toes are frozen and you don't care.|The ice catches the silver at your hem. A first-year whispers that you look like the portrait in the east stair.}\nopt.up.say.1 [075cfa] = {你穿着午夜蓝走在冰冻的玫瑰间，人们转头看你，一直看。你故意走得很慢。|有人问你的袍子在哪儿做的。另一个人问你是谁。你的脚趾冻僵了，你不在乎。|冰映着你下摆的银边。一个一年级生小声说，你像东楼梯上那幅肖像。}\n\n@event ball_last_dance\n# en: The Last Waltz\ntitle [686d0e] = 最后一支华尔兹\n# en: Near midnight the musicians play the last waltz, slow and a little sad. {npc} holds out a hand without saying anything. Half the hall has gone to bed. The chandelier is dripping.\ntext [ddb155] = 快到午夜时，乐师们奏起了最后一支华尔兹，缓慢，带着一点忧伤。{npc} 一言不发地伸出手。大厅里一半的人已经去睡了。枝形吊灯在滴水。\n# en: The great hall near midnight, candles burning low, a single couple still turning slowly beneath a dripping ice-crystal chandelier.\nscene [cf17c9] = 将近午夜的大礼堂，蜡烛快要燃尽，一盏滴着水的冰晶吊灯下，只剩一对舞伴还在缓缓旋转。\n# en: Dance until the music stops\nopt.left.label [bf83ad] = 一直跳到音乐停止\n# en: You dance it slowly, and then once more after the musicians have packed up, to no music at all. Nobody else is left to see, which is the point.\nopt.left.say.1 [7c3db6] = 你们慢慢地跳完这一支，然后在乐师们收拾好乐器之后，在没有音乐的寂静里又跳了一遍。没有别人留下来看，这正是重点。\n# en: Slip out onto the frozen terrace\nopt.right.label [50e9ea] = 溜到结冰的露台上\n# en: Outside it's bitter, and the stars are out over the lake. You talk until you can't feel your fingers, about nothing and then about everything, and go back in blue-lipped and grinning.\nopt.right.say.1 [dfa865] = 外面冷得刺骨，湖面上空满是星星。你们一直聊到手指失去知觉，先聊无关紧要的事，后来聊了一切，回去时嘴唇发紫，笑得合不拢嘴。\n\n@event ball_snowball_war\n# en: The Snowball War\ntitle [da2e94] = 雪仗\n# en: {After the ball, someone throws the first snowball in the courtyard, and within a minute every house is at war, in ball robes.|The snowball war, as much a tradition as the ball. The forts went up during the last waltz.|The snowball war again. The first-years have built a fort that is frankly impressive.} {A prefect is trying to restore order and failing.|Someone has enchanted their snowballs, which is cheating.|The robes are already ruined.}\ntext [6ef7aa] = {舞会结束后，有人在庭院里扔出了第一个雪球，不到一分钟，各学院就穿着舞会礼服打成了一片。|雪仗，和舞会一样是传统。雪堡是在最后一支华尔兹时堆起来的。|又是雪仗。一年级生们堆的雪堡坦白说相当壮观。} {一位级长想恢复秩序，没成功。|有人给自己的雪球施了魔法，这是作弊。|礼服早就毁了。}\n# en: A snowy courtyard at night full of students in ruined ball robes hurling snowballs from behind snow forts, a prefect ducking.\nscene [733bb8] = 夜晚积雪的庭院里，一群穿着破烂舞会礼服的学生躲在雪堡后面互扔雪球，一位级长正在闪躲。\n# en: Charge the enemy fort\nopt.left.label [f5a047] = 冲向敌方雪堡\n# en: You take the fort with a war cry and a single perfect throw. Your house carries you round the fountain on its shoulders. Your robes will never recover.\nopt.left.say.1 [0b4ca7] = 你一声战吼加一记完美的投掷，攻下了雪堡。你的学院把你扛在肩上绕着喷泉转圈。你的礼服再也恢复不了了。\n# en: A snowball, then fourteen. You go down laughing under a drift, and someone has to dig you out. Worth it.\nopt.left.say.2 [ff0315] = 一个雪球，然后是十四个。你笑着被埋进了雪堆，还得有人把你挖出来。值了。\n# en: Watch from the cloister with cocoa\nopt.right.label [6ac2a1] = 端着可可在回廊里看\n# en: {You watch from the cloister, warm and dry, and cheer for whoever's losing. Nobody throws at you. Mostly.|You hold the cocoa and the coats, and you're the only one who goes to bed warm.|You watch the first-years' fort hold against all comers. Yours are the only robes in the courtyard that survive the night.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bcd394] = {你在回廊里看着，温暖又干燥，为输的那一方加油。没人朝你扔雪球。大多数时候。|你替大家拿着可可和外套，是唯一一个暖暖和和上床睡觉的人。|你看着一年级生的堡垒挡住了所有来犯者。整个庭院里，只有你的礼袍熬过了这一夜。}\n# en: Pull the ward-stitched hood up\nopt.up.label [ad0df3] = 拉起护界绣兜帽\n# en: {Snowballs slide off the ward thread like rain off a window. You walk through the whole war untouched, and both sides declare you a neutral country.|You stroll into the enemy fort with your hood up. Twelve snowballs later they surrender out of sheer confusion.|The cloak turns every snowball aside. By the end the first-years are following you around for cover.}\nopt.up.say.1 [3c3668] = {雪球从护界线上滑开，像雨水滑过窗户。你毫发无伤地穿过整场雪仗，双方都宣布你是中立国。|你拉着兜帽走进敌方堡垒。挨了十二个雪球后，他们纯粹因为困惑投降了。|斗篷挡开了每一个雪球。到最后，一年级生们都跟在你身后找掩护。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/fixtures.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/fixtures.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event amaranth_intro\n# en: No Excuses\ntitle [8daa28] = 不许找借口\n# en: You came in for a plaster. Sister Amaranth takes your hand, turns it to the light and asks how it happened. You tell her. She waits. You tell her the true version. \"Better,\" she says, and reaches for a bottle that is not a plaster.\ntext [2645db] = 你本来只是来要块膏药。阿玛兰斯修女拉过你的手，对着光转了转，问你怎么弄的。你告诉了她。她等着。你又把真实的经过讲了一遍。“这还差不多，”她说着，伸手去拿一个瓶子——不是膏药。\n# en: A stern healer in a white apron holding a student's wrist up to the window light, a row of tonic bottles behind her.\nscene [fc4802] = 一位系着白围裙的严厉治疗师，把一名学生的手腕举到窗前的光里，身后摆着一排补剂瓶。\n# en: Drink whatever it is\nopt.left.label [6cbe5e] = 不管是什么，喝了\n# en: It tastes of iron and regret. The hand stops throbbing before you reach the door. \"Come back if it swells,\" she calls. \"Don't come back if it doesn't.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [9fbd34] = 一股铁锈和后悔的味道。还没走到门口，手就不疼了。“肿了再来，”她在身后喊，“不肿就别来。”\n# en: Just the plaster, thanks\nopt.right.label [1353de] = 给块膏药就行，谢谢\n# en: She gives you the plaster, and a look you will remember the next time you lie to a healer. \"Three crowns,\" she says. \"The look is free.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [606886] = 她给了你膏药，外加一个眼神——下次你再想对治疗师撒谎时，一定会想起它。“三克朗，”她说，“眼神免费。”\n\n@event amaranth_rounds\n# en: Evening Rounds\ntitle [ee9b30] = 晚间查房\n# en: {Sister Amaranth catches you in the corridor and hands you a tray of tonics without a word of explanation.|Amaranth holds out the tonic tray as you pass. It seems you are on the rota now.|The tray is waiting for you on the ward table, labelled YOURS in Amaranth's square hand.} {Six beds on the ward today.|A first-year with hiccups that light up.|Someone has charmed their own eyebrows off.}\ntext [985a3b] = {阿玛兰斯修女在走廊里拦住你，一言不发地把一盘补剂塞到你手里。|你路过时，阿玛兰斯把补剂托盘递了过来。看来你已经上了值班表。|托盘在病房桌上等着你，上面贴着阿玛兰斯方方正正的字：“你的”。} {今天病房里六张床。|一个一年级生，打嗝会发光。|有人把自己的眉毛给咒没了。}\n# en: A healer and a student walking a long infirmary ward at dusk, the student carrying a tray of small glass bottles, lamps being lit along the beds.\nscene [9f326b] = 黄昏时分，一位治疗师和一名学生走在长长的病房里，学生端着一盘小玻璃瓶，病床边的灯一盏盏点亮。\n# en: Do the rounds with her\nopt.left.label [640527] = 跟她一起查房\n# en: {She tells you what each tonic does and why it tastes like that. You remember all of it, to your surprise.|You learn which patients are brave and which are loud, and that they are rarely the same ones.|At the last bed she lets you measure the dose. \"Again,\" she says. You do it again. \"Fine.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [24b737] = {她告诉你每种补剂的用处，以及为什么是这个味道。出乎你自己意料，你全记住了。|你弄明白了哪些病人勇敢、哪些病人吵闹，而这两种人很少是同一批。|到最后一张床，她让你量剂量。“再来，”她说。你又量了一遍。“行。”}\n# en: You have an essay due\nopt.right.label [427f10] = 你还有篇论文要交\n# en: {\"So does everyone,\" she says, and takes the tray back. \"Off you go.\"|She nods, which is somehow worse than a lecture.|\"Next time,\" she says, as a fact, not a question.}\nopt.right.say.1 [afe44c] = {“谁不是呢，”她说着把托盘拿了回去，“走吧。”|她点点头，这比训你一顿还难受。|“下次，”她说——是陈述，不是询问。}\n# en: Mend the worst one yourself\nopt.up.label [0b5686] = 亲手治好最重的那个\n# en: {You close a split knuckle with a whisper of light. Amaranth inspects it for a long time. \"Neat,\" she says, the highest word she owns.|The sprain knits under your hands. The patient thanks Amaranth. Amaranth points at you.|The student with no eyebrows is the worst case. You coax them halfway back. Amaranth finishes the job, and writes your name on tomorrow's tray as well.}\nopt.up.say.1 [21db53] = {你轻声一念，一道微光合上了裂开的指节。阿玛兰斯端详了很久。“利落，”她说——这是她嘴里最高的评价。|扭伤在你手下愈合。病人向阿玛兰斯道谢，阿玛兰斯指了指你。|那个没了眉毛的学生最棘手。你哄回来了一半。阿玛兰斯收了尾，把你的名字也写上了明天的托盘。}\n\n@event amaranth_orders\n# en: Doctor's Orders\ntitle [abe905] = 医嘱\n# en: {Sister Amaranth has come to find you, which has never happened to anyone. \"You look like a draught of bad decisions,\" she says. \"Bed.\"|Amaranth again, at your door, arms folded. \"I can hear you coughing from the infirmary.\"|\"Bed,\" says Amaranth, before you've opened the door all the way.}\ntext [321559] = {阿玛兰斯修女竟然主动来找你了，这种事从没发生在任何人身上。“你看起来像一剂馊主意，”她说，“上床。”|又是阿玛兰斯，抱着胳膊站在你门口。“我在医务室都听得见你咳嗽。”|门还没完全打开，阿玛兰斯就说：“上床。”}\n# en: A healer standing in a dormitory doorway with her arms folded, a pale student halfway out of bed with a stack of books.\nscene [a0e5b7] = 一位治疗师抱着胳膊站在宿舍门口，一名脸色苍白的学生正抱着一摞书，半个身子下了床。\n# en: Go to bed as ordered\nopt.left.label [c01d90] = 遵医嘱，上床\n# en: {You sleep fourteen hours. She checks on you twice and leaves a tonic on the sill both times.|Two days of broth and boredom. You come out of it a person again.|You argue for one more chapter. You lose, and sleep.}\nopt.left.say.1 [42f270] = {你睡了十四个小时。她来看了你两次，两次都在窗台上留了一瓶补剂。|两天的肉汤和无聊。熬过去，你又像个人了。|你争取再读一章。你输了，睡觉。}\n# en: \"I'm fine, honestly.\"\nopt.right.label [9142ac] = “我没事，真的。”\n# en: {\"Nobody who says 'honestly' is,\" she says, and leaves. You are not fine by Thursday.|She writes something on a card and walks off. It turns out to be your name, on her list.|\"Bed,\" she says again, as if you hadn't spoken. You don't go. By the weekend you wish you had.}\nopt.right.say.1 [091428] = {“说‘真的’的人都有事，”她说完就走了。到了周四，你果然有事。|她在一张卡片上写了点什么就走了。后来发现那是你的名字，写在她的名单上。|“床，”她又说了一遍，好像你什么也没说。你没去。到了周末，你后悔了。}\n\n@event amaranth_trust\n# en: The Cupboard Key\ntitle [11b16f] = 柜子钥匙\n# en: Sister Amaranth closes the storeroom door behind you both. \"I'm away for the solstice and the junior healer faints at blood,\" she says. \"You don't.\" She holds out the key to the tonic cupboard, and with it a page of her own notes on the mending charm.\ntext [8d3c0e] = 阿玛兰斯修女随手关上储藏室的门。“冬至那几天我不在，见习治疗师一见血就晕，”她说，“你不会。”她递来补剂柜的钥匙，还有一页她自己写的愈合咒笔记。\n# en: An infirmary storeroom at night lit by a single lamp, a healer pressing a small brass key into a student's palm, shelves of labelled bottles all around.\nscene [f064f3] = 夜里的医务室储藏室，只点着一盏灯，一位治疗师把一把小铜钥匙按进学生掌心，四周的架子上全是贴好标签的瓶子。\n# en: Take the key and the notes\nopt.left.label [bdfda9] = 收下钥匙和笔记\n# en: \"Mend what you can, send the rest to me, and lie to nobody,\" she says. The notes are written for someone she expects to be good at this. You read them twice and try very hard to be.\nopt.left.say.1 [f5ce15] = “能治的治，治不了的送来给我，对谁都别撒谎，”她说。那页笔记是写给一个她认定会擅长此道的人的。你读了两遍，拼命想成为那样的人。\n# en: \"I'm not ready for that.\"\nopt.right.label [5088da] = “我还没准备好。”\n# en: She looks at you for a long moment. \"Good answer. Wrong, but good.\" She keeps the key. She gives you the notes anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [a29e05] = 她看了你好一会儿。“好回答。错了，但是好回答。”钥匙她收回去了，笔记还是给了你。\n\n@event brask_shovel\n# en: Hands Wanted\ntitle [82086c] = 缺人手\n# en: {Professor Brask holds out a shovel as you pass the stables. She doesn't say anything. The hippogriff behind her does, loudly.|Brask hands you the shovel before you're through the door.|\"Ah,\" says Brask, and the shovel is already in your hands.} {The griffon chick is in a mood.|The owls have opinions about the new straw.|A hippogriff has eaten a glove. Possibly a professor's.}\ntext [25a91e] = {你路过马厩时，布拉斯克教授递过来一把铲子。她一句话没说。她身后的鹰头马身有翼兽倒是说了，而且很大声。|你还没进门，布拉斯克就把铲子递了过来。|“啊，”布拉斯克说，铲子已经在你手里了。} {狮鹫幼崽在闹脾气。|猫头鹰们对新铺的稻草颇有意见。|一只鹰头马身有翼兽吃了一只手套，可能是某位教授的。}\n# en: A weathered professor in a leather apron holding out a shovel to a student at a stable door, a hippogriff watching suspiciously from its stall.\nscene [b7d66c] = 一位饱经风霜、系着皮围裙的教授在马厩门口把铲子递给一名学生，一只鹰头马身有翼兽在隔栏里狐疑地看着。\n# en: Muck out the stalls\nopt.left.label [6ef185] = 清理马厩\n# en: {An hour of straw and silence. At the end Brask says, \"The grey one likes you,\" which is more than she has said to anyone this term.|She tells you a story about a manticore, a bucket and a bishop. It ends with a bite and a moral. You miss the moral.|The hippogriff bows to you on your way out. Brask pretends not to see.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e1fc8d] = {一个小时的稻草和沉默。最后布拉斯克说：“那只灰的喜欢你。”这比她这学期对任何人说的都多。|她给你讲了一个关于蝎尾狮、一只水桶和一位主教的故事。结尾是被咬了一口和一个道理。道理你没听清。|你走时，那只鹰头马身有翼兽向你鞠了一躬。布拉斯克假装没看见。}\n# en: Decline, politely\nopt.right.label [96c3eb] = 客气地推辞\n# en: {\"Suit yourself,\" she says, and takes the shovel back. \"The straw won't mind.\"|\"The hippogriff doesn't want to either,\" says Brask. \"It helps anyway.\"|You hand the shovel back. Brask takes it, and the hippogriff looks at your gloves with interest. You leave quickly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [022ce5] = {“随你，”她说，把铲子拿了回去，“稻草不会介意的。”|“那只鹰头马身有翼兽也不想干，”布拉斯克说，“可它照样帮忙。”|你把铲子递了回去。布拉斯克接过来，那只鹰头马身有翼兽饶有兴趣地盯着你的手套。你赶紧走了。}\n# en: Hasten through the stalls\nopt.up.label [196a17] = 疾行着打扫马厩\n# en: {You clear six stalls before the hippogriff has finished glaring at you. Brask watches with her arms folded and something almost like a smile.|You muck out, refill and re-straw in a blur. The griffon chick follows your hands, dizzy. \"Show-off,\" says Brask, approving.|You're done before Brask has found her own shovel. The hippogriff, outpaced, gives back the glove. Brask takes it, damp, and says nothing, which from her is thanks.}\nopt.up.say.1 [ec9879] = {鹰头马身有翼兽还没瞪完你，你已经清完了六个隔栏。布拉斯克抱着胳膊看着，脸上几乎有点笑意。|铲粪、添料、换草，一气呵成，快得只剩残影。狮鹫幼崽盯着你的手，看得头晕。“爱显摆，”布拉斯克说，语气是赞许的。|布拉斯克还没找到自己的铲子，你已经干完了。被比下去的鹰头马身有翼兽把手套还了回来。布拉斯克接过湿漉漉的手套，什么也没说，对她来说这就是道谢。}\n\n@event brask_night_owl\n# en: The Sick Owl\ntitle [a56a50] = 生病的猫头鹰\n# en: Brask stops you in the corridor and falls into step without asking, which is a first. \"The old barn owl won't eat,\" she says. \"Somebody should sit with her tonight. Somebody quiet.\" She is looking at you.\ntext [56bef6] = 布拉斯克在走廊里拦住你，招呼也不打就和你并肩走，这还是头一回。“那只老仓鸮不肯吃东西，”她说，“今晚得有人陪着她。得是个安静的人。”她看着你。\n# en: A dim stable loft at night, a student sitting cross-legged beside a straw nest where a sick barn owl sleeps under a blanket, a lantern turned low.\nscene [939266] = 夜里昏暗的马厩阁楼，一名学生盘腿坐在草窝旁，一只生病的仓鸮盖着毯子睡着，提灯拧得很暗。\n# en: Sit up with the owl\nopt.left.label [2fd221] = 守着猫头鹰熬夜\n# en: You sit in the loft till dawn, dripping water onto the owl's beak. At four she takes a mouse from your fingers. In the morning Brask looks at the empty dish for a long time, then claps you once on the shoulder, hard.\nopt.left.say.1 [bae058] = 你在阁楼里坐到天亮，一滴一滴往猫头鹰喙里喂水。凌晨四点，她从你指间叼走了一只老鼠。早上，布拉斯克盯着空碗看了很久，然后在你肩上狠狠拍了一下。\n# en: Send her to Sister Amaranth\nopt.right.label [53c847] = 让她去找阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth looks at the owl, looks at you, and says she doesn't do owls. Then she sits with it all night. Brask is not sure how to feel about this, and neither are you.\nopt.right.say.1 [4f82b4] = 阿玛兰斯看看猫头鹰，又看看你，说她不治猫头鹰。然后她陪了它一整夜。布拉斯克不知该作何感想，你也一样。\n\n@event brask_trust\n# en: Old Courtesies\ntitle [39af84] = 古老的礼节\n# en: Brask takes you to the edge of the Whisperwood one afternoon. \"Most people talk at animals,\" she says. \"A few talk to them. Fewer listen.\" She bows, very low, to something in the trees. \"Do as I do. Keep still, and keep your eyes down.\"\ntext [c09f63] = 一天下午，布拉斯克带你到低语森林边。“大多数人对动物说话，”她说，“少数人和它们说话。更少的人肯听。”她朝树林里的什么东西深深鞠了一躬。“照我做。别动，眼睛朝下。”\n# en: A professor and a student at the edge of a forest at dusk, both bowing low to a great antlered stag half-hidden in the trees.\nscene [5eaec1] = 黄昏的森林边缘，一位教授和一名学生向半隐在树间的一头巨大雄鹿深深鞠躬。\n# en: Bow, and learn\nopt.left.label [80ee0e] = 鞠躬，然后学\n# en: It takes until full dark, bent double until your back aches, and something huge breathes on your neck for most of it. Then the stag bows back, very slightly. Brask laughs once, loudly, the way she does once a term.\nopt.left.say.1 [0335fb] = 一直学到天全黑，你弯着腰弯到背都酸了，大部分时间还有个庞然大物在你脖子后面喘气。然后，那头雄鹿也微微回了一礼。布拉斯克大笑了一声——就是她一学期一次的那种。\n# en: Keep your distance\nopt.right.label [0141a6] = 保持距离\n# en: You stay by the path and watch. She doesn't press you. On the walk back she talks about hoof care for an hour, which you suspect is a kindness.\nopt.right.say.1 [2e5c6a] = 你站在小路边看着。她没有勉强你。回去的路上她讲了一个小时的蹄子护理，你怀疑这是一种体贴。\n\n@event vey_pop_quiz\n# en: Is It There?\ntitle [5bb9f5] = 它在那儿吗？\n# en: {Professor Vey is in the corridor with two cups of tea. She offers you one. \"Only one of them is real,\" she says pleasantly.|Vey stops you on the stairs. \"There's a coin behind your ear. Or there isn't.\"|\"Good morning,\" says Vey, and the door you were about to open is, possibly, a wall.} {She looks delighted already.|A student behind you walks into something.|She is humming.}\ntext [b25450] = {维伊教授端着两杯茶站在走廊里，递给你一杯。“只有一杯是真的，”她和颜悦色地说。|维伊在楼梯上拦住你。“你耳朵后面有枚硬币。也可能没有。”|“早上好，”维伊说，而你正要推开的那扇门，可能是一堵墙。} {她看上去已经很高兴了。|你身后有个学生撞上了什么东西。|她在哼歌。}\n# en: A smiling professor in a violet scarf leaning against a corridor wall, a cup of tea in her hand, a second, slightly too perfect cup on the windowsill beside her.\nscene [ad6db0] = 一位系着紫罗兰色围巾的教授笑着倚在走廊墙边，手里端着一杯茶，旁边窗台上还有一杯，完美得有点过分。\n# en: Look closely\nopt.left.label [66d2dd] = 仔细看\n# en: {You pick the real one. Vey raises an eyebrow. \"Insufferable,\" she says, beaming.|You catch the shimmer at the edge. \"Ah,\" says Vey. \"You've been paying attention. Dreadful habit. Keep it.\"|You lay your hand flat on it first. It's a door. Vey stops humming. \"Pity,\" she says, delighted.}\nopt.left.say.1 [145cd8] = {你挑中了真的那个。维伊挑了挑眉。“讨厌鬼，”她笑逐颜开地说。|你捕捉到了边缘那一丝微光。“啊，”维伊说，“你一直在留心。坏习惯。保持住。”|你先把手掌贴了上去。是门。维伊不哼了。“可惜，”她说，高兴得很。}\n# en: {You reach for the real one and close your fingers on air. Vey enjoys this very much.|Wrong. \"It's usually the other one,\" she says, kindly, which is worse.|You walk confidently into what turns out to be a wall. Vey hums on, very pleased, while you hold your nose.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a8dd9c] = {你伸手去拿真的那个，手指却合在了空气上。维伊对此相当享受。|错了。“通常都是另一个，”她和气地说，这更糟。|你信心十足地走了过去，结果那是一堵墙。你捂着鼻子，维伊继续哼着歌，非常满意。}\n# en: Play along and get it wrong\nopt.right.label [e10069] = 配合她，故意猜错\n# en: {You choose the false one on purpose, with feeling. Vey narrows her eyes. \"Either you're bad at this, or very good,\" she says. \"I'll find out.\"|You let her win. She knows. She lets you know she knows.|You walk into it on purpose, braced for a wall. It's a door, and you fall through it. Vey hums all the way down the corridor.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f007d2] = {你故意、而且声情并茂地选了假的那个。维伊眯起眼睛。“要么你很不擅长这个，要么你非常擅长，”她说，“我会弄清楚的。”|你让她赢了。她知道。她也让你知道她知道。|你故意撞上去，准备好了撞墙。结果是门，你一头栽了进去。维伊哼着歌走完了整条走廊。}\n\n@event vey_revue\n# en: The Faculty Revue\ntitle [22db23] = 教职员汇演\n# en: Professor Vey is producing the faculty revue, in which professors make fools of themselves for charity. \"I need someone to play a king who isn't there,\" she says. \"You'd be wonderful. You'll be terrible. Either is fine.\"\ntext [551685] = 维伊教授在排演教职员汇演——教授们为慈善出丑的那种。“我需要一个人演一位并不在场的国王，”她说，“你会很出色。你会演砸。哪样都行。”\n# en: A small stage in the Great Hall hung with violet curtains, a professor directing a nervous student holding a paper crown, chairs being set out below.\nscene [44137b] = 大礼堂里一座挂着紫罗兰色幕布的小舞台，一位教授在指导一名拿着纸王冠的紧张学生，台下正在摆椅子。\n# en: Take the part\nopt.left.label [e6818e] = 接下这个角色\n# en: You are a king who isn't there, magnificently. Thorne, in the front row, says \"Hm\" twice, which the faculty agree is a standing ovation.\nopt.left.say.1 [d507d2] = 你把一位不在场的国王演得气势恢宏。前排的索恩说了两声“嗯”，教职员们一致认为这相当于起立鼓掌。\n# en: You forget your lines, invent new ones and are, by general agreement, the best thing in it for the wrong reasons. Vey keeps the paper crown on her desk.\nopt.left.say.2 [cd7f8a] = 你忘了台词，现编了新的，结果大家一致认为你是整场最精彩的部分——只是原因不太对。维伊把那顶纸王冠留在了她的书桌上。\n# en: At the curtain call you hand her a cup of tea that isn't there. She drinks it without blinking, and gives you full marks.\nopt.left.say.3 [56ed53] = 谢幕时你递给她一杯并不存在的茶。她眼都不眨地喝了下去，给了你满分。\n# en: Hide in the wings\nopt.right.label [f0270b] = 躲到侧幕\n# en: You run the curtain. The revue goes ahead without a king, which Vey says was the point all along.\nopt.right.say.1 [d2705a] = 你负责拉幕。汇演在没有国王的情况下照常进行，维伊说这正是重点所在。\n\n@event vey_trust\n# en: The Mirror Trick\ntitle [2b6124] = 镜像戏法\n# en: Vey keeps you after class and tilts a tall mirror towards you. Your reflection steps out of it and sits on a desk. \"The trick isn't making a copy,\" she says. \"It's knowing which of you to send. Want to see how it's done?\"\ntext [da4b4b] = 维伊课后留下你，把一面高高的镜子朝你倾过来。你的倒影从镜子里走出来，坐到了一张桌子上。“诀窍不在于造一个复制品，”她说，“而在于知道该派哪个你出去。想看看怎么做的吗？”\n# en: An empty classroom at twilight, two identical students standing either side of a tall mirror, a professor seated on a desk between them, clapping softly.\nscene [30e560] = 黄昏时空荡荡的教室，一面高大的镜子两侧站着两个一模一样的学生，一位教授坐在中间的桌子上轻轻鼓掌。\n# en: Study the trick with her\nopt.left.label [ad081d] = 跟她琢磨这个把戏\n# en: It takes a week of evenings, and you never manage the copy. On the last night your reflection refuses to go back for her. Vey talks it round over tea, then says, \"Now you know why I don't do this in public.\" You know a great deal more about faces.\nopt.left.say.1 [be691c] = 你花了一周的晚上，始终没能变出那个复制品。最后一晚，你的倒影不肯为她回去。维伊边喝茶边把它劝了回去，然后说：“现在你知道我为什么不在人前用这个了。”你对“脸”这回事懂得多了很多。\n# en: Keep your own face, thanks\nopt.right.label [78e875] = 谢了，我还是用自己的脸\n# en: \"Sensible,\" she says, disappointed. She teaches you instead how to tell when someone else is using it. It turns out a lot of people are.\nopt.right.say.1 [28577d] = “明智，”她说，有点失望。她转而教你怎么识破别人在用这招。结果发现，用的人还真不少。\n\n@event marisol_intro\n# en: A Dry Patch of Air\ntitle [7b3a88] = 一小片干燥的空气\n# en: It is raining on the whole lake except for one circle on the boathouse steps, where a woman in a soaked hat is reading poetry, perfectly dry. She looks up. \"It's not hard,\" says Professor Marisol. \"You just ask the water to go round. Sit, if you like.\"\ntext [86e87f] = 整片湖都在下雨，只有船屋台阶上的一个圆圈例外：一个戴着湿透帽子的女人在那里读诗，浑身干爽。她抬起头。“不难，”玛丽索尔教授说，“只要请水绕开就行。想坐的话就坐吧。”\n# en: Heavy rain on a lake shore, a woman reading on the boathouse steps beneath a perfectly dry circle of air, raindrops sliding around it as if around glass.\nscene [765fe2] = 湖岸大雨，一个女人坐在船屋台阶上读书，头顶是一圈完全干燥的空气，雨滴像绕过玻璃一样从四周滑落。\n# en: Sit and ask how\nopt.left.label [9059dd] = 坐下，问她怎么做到的\n# en: She explains it as a conversation. The water, apparently, is willing. By the end of the poem the rain is going round you too, for almost a minute, and then it remembers you. \"There,\" she says, and goes back to the poem.\nopt.left.say.1 [806739] = 她把这解释成一场对话。水显然是愿意的。一首诗读完时，雨也绕着你走了，差不多一分钟，然后它又想起了你。“好了，”她说，接着读她的诗。\n# en: Leave her to her book\nopt.right.label [1f57ac] = 别打扰她读书\n# en: You nod and run for cover. Behind you she reads aloud, to nobody, a poem about a storm that comes back every year to see if you've changed.\nopt.right.say.1 [b3911a] = 你点点头，跑去躲雨。身后，她对着空无一人的湖岸朗读一首诗，写的是一场每年都回来看看你变了没有的暴风雨。\n\n@event marisol_cold_water\n# en: Cold Water Is Good for You\ntitle [c84271] = 冷水对你有好处\n# en: {Professor Marisol is in the lake, waist-deep, and waves at you as though this were normal.|Marisol again, in the lake. \"It's lovely once you're in,\" she calls. It is not lovely.|\"You came!\" calls Marisol from the water. You had been walking past.} {There is ice at the edges.|A heron is watching her with professional respect.|She has a book in one hand, held perfectly dry.}\ntext [c110b4] = {玛丽索尔教授站在齐腰深的湖里，朝你挥手，仿佛这再正常不过。|又是玛丽索尔，又在湖里。“下来就舒服了！”她喊。一点也不舒服。|“你来啦！”玛丽索尔在水里喊。你本来只是路过。} {湖边结了冰。|一只苍鹭正以同行的敬意看着她。|她一只手举着书，书一点没湿。}\n# en: Dawn mist on a still lake, a professor standing waist-deep in the water waving cheerfully to a shivering student on the jetty.\nscene [2bea30] = 黎明的薄雾笼罩着平静的湖面，一位教授站在齐腰深的水里，朝码头上冻得发抖的学生快活地挥手。\n# en: Wade in\nopt.left.label [f7f4b9] = 蹚下水\n# en: {She teaches you to feel the lake breathing. You cannot feel your feet. Both are, she says, normal.|You ask the water to rise and it rises, a little, around your knees. Marisol whoops like a child.|By the time you climb out you've stopped shivering, which she says is a good sign and Amaranth will say is a bad one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5536c9] = {她教你感受湖的呼吸。你感受不到自己的脚。她说两者都很正常。|你请水升起来，它真的在你膝边升起了一点。玛丽索尔像个孩子一样欢呼。|等你爬上岸，你已经不发抖了。她说这是好兆头，阿玛兰斯会说这是坏兆头。}\n# en: Wave back from the jetty\nopt.right.label [c55e26] = 在码头上朝她挥手\n# en: {She waves again, unoffended, and goes under.|\"Coward,\" she calls cheerfully. You are fine with that.|\"Next time, then,\" she calls, and goes back to her book, held perfectly dry above the water.}\nopt.right.say.1 [154360] = {她毫不介意地又挥了挥手，沉了下去。|“胆小鬼，”她快活地喊。你觉得这样挺好。|“那就下次，”她喊道，又看起了书，书在水面上举得干干的。}\n# en: Burn the chill off the shallows\nopt.up.label [65c5ec] = 烧掉浅水的寒气\n# en: {You hold a flame under the surface until the shallows steam. Marisol swims over to sit in your warm patch, delighted.|Your flame warms the water around your ankles and, briefly, a very surprised heron. Marisol laughs so hard she sinks.|The shallows steam around you like a bath. \"Cheating,\" calls Marisol, and paddles over to share it.}\nopt.up.say.1 [526dec] = {你在水面下托着一团火，直到浅水冒起热气。玛丽索尔游过来，坐进你这片暖水里，高兴极了。|你的火把你脚踝周围的水烘暖了，顺带短暂地烘到了一只非常吃惊的苍鹭。玛丽索尔笑得沉了下去。|浅水在你身边冒着热气，像泡澡。“作弊，”玛丽索尔喊道，划着水过来一起泡。}\n\n@event marisol_storm\n# en: Weather\ntitle [eab633] = 天气\n# en: There's a knock at your window during the worst storm of the year. Professor Marisol is outside, hair everywhere, grinning. \"Nobody learns tides on a calm day,\" she shouts over the wind. \"Coming?\"\ntext [837719] = 一年里最猛的暴风雨中，有人敲你的窗。玛丽索尔教授站在外面，头发乱飞，满脸笑容。“风平浪静的日子里谁也学不会潮汐，”她在风里大喊，“来不来？”\n# en: A wild storm over a dark lake, a professor and a student standing on a rocky point with their arms raised, lightning splitting the sky behind them.\nscene [69a9a9] = 漆黑湖面上的狂暴风雨，一位教授和一名学生站在岩石岬角上高举双臂，身后闪电劈开天空。\n# en: Go out into it\nopt.left.label [d9d690] = 冲进风雨里\n# en: On the point, in the roar, you hold back one wave for three whole seconds. Marisol screams with joy. You go home soaked to the bone and lit up like a lamp.\nopt.left.say.1 [b3da39] = 在岬角上、在轰鸣声中，你把一道浪整整挡住了三秒。玛丽索尔开心得尖叫。你浑身湿透地回家，却像一盏点亮的灯。\n# en: The lake throws you on your back. Marisol hauls you out by the collar, laughing and apologising in equal parts, and walks you all the way to the infirmary.\nopt.left.say.2 [b1eb0b] = 湖水把你掀翻在地。玛丽索尔揪着你的领子把你拖出来，一半在笑一半在道歉，一路把你送进了医务室。\n# en: Close the window\nopt.right.label [486e14] = 关上窗\n# en: She shrugs, cheerful, and runs off into the rain. You lie awake listening to the lake and wondering what you missed.\nopt.right.say.1 [457fde] = 她耸耸肩，毫不在意地跑进雨里。你躺着听了一夜湖水，想知道自己错过了什么。\n# en: In the morning there's a poem about rain under your door, in ink that's still wet. It has no last line.\nopt.right.say.2 [d34f65] = 第二天早上，你门缝下塞着一首关于雨的诗，墨迹未干。它没有最后一行。\n# en: Root yourselves a shelter\nopt.up.label [98acac] = 为你们扎根造个避处\n# en: On the point you grow a tree out of bare rock, roots and all, and the two of you watch the storm from under it. Marisol is delighted and soaked. \"That's not tidecalling,\" she shouts, \"but I'll allow it!\"\nopt.up.say.1 [3bac7e] = 在岬角上，你从光秃秃的岩石里长出一棵树，连根带枝，你们俩躲在树下看风暴。玛丽索尔又高兴又湿透。“这可不是唤潮术，”她喊道，“不过我认了！”\n\n@event marisol_trust\n# en: The Last Line\ntitle [66ff3e] = 最后一行\n# en: Marisol presses her old book of water poems into your hands. \"I never remember the last lines,\" she says. \"You will. My notes are in the margins; I wrote them there when I was your age. Read it on the shore. It works better on the shore.\"\ntext [a0bb7f] = 玛丽索尔把她那本旧的咏水诗集塞到你手里。“我总记不住最后一行，”她说，“你会记住的。我的笔记写在页边，是我在你这个年纪时写的。去湖边读。在湖边读效果更好。”\n# en: A quiet lakeside at sunset, a professor handing a student a worn book of poems with a ribbon in it, the water perfectly still before them.\nscene [41e145] = 日落时宁静的湖边，一位教授把一本夹着丝带的旧诗集递给学生，眼前的湖水平静如镜。\n# en: Read it on the shore\nopt.left.label [0950d9] = 去湖边读\n# en: You read until dark, ankle-deep. On the last page the lake answers you, softly, all the way out to the far bank. You remember the last line. You write it in, for her.\nopt.left.say.1 [2dd6f7] = 你站在没过脚踝的水里一直读到天黑。翻到最后一页时，湖水轻轻地回应了你，一直传到对岸。你记住了最后一行。你替她把它写了上去。\n# en: Give it back; it's hers\nopt.right.label [404c00] = 还给她，这是她的\n# en: She looks at the book and then at you. \"Nobody's ever given it back,\" she says, and reads you the poem herself. She forgets the last line. You tell her.\nopt.right.say.1 [fcc45a] = 她看看书，又看看你。“从来没人还回来过，”她说，然后亲自给你读了那首诗。她忘了最后一行。你告诉了她。\n\n@event mabel_intro\n# en: The Way You Open a Door\ntitle [3e82a5] = 你推门的样子\n# en: The bell over the door rings. The woman behind the counter looks up, considers how you came in, and reaches for a tin before you've said a word. \"Homesick,\" says Mabel, \"with a side of exam. Sit by the window, petal.\"\ntext [0c0a87] = 门上的铃铛响了。柜台后的女人抬起头，打量了一下你进门的样子，你还没开口，她已经伸手去拿一罐茶了。“想家，”梅布尔说，“外加一份考试。坐窗边吧，小宝贝。”\n# en: A cosy village tea shop with a bell over the door, a woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin of tea as a student steps in from the cold.\nscene [436b7b] = 温馨的村庄茶馆，门上挂着铃铛，一名学生从寒风中走进来，柜台后的女人已经伸手去拿一罐茶。\n# en: Sit by the window\nopt.left.label [8f510d] = 坐到窗边\n# en: The tea is exactly right and you can't say why. She brings a slice of cake you didn't order and doesn't charge for it. \"First visit,\" she says. \"After that I charge double.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [e7981f] = 茶恰到好处，你说不出为什么。她端来一块你没点的蛋糕，没收钱。“第一次来，”她说，“以后收双倍。”\n# en: Say you'll come back another time\nopt.right.label [64684d] = 说你改天再来\n# en: She looks at you over the counter. \"Come back when you've got time to sit,\" she says. \"You'll know when.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [088ded] = 她隔着柜台看着你。“等你有空坐下了再来，”她说，“到时候你自己会知道。”\n\n@event mabel_parcel\n# en: A Tin From Mabel\ntitle [89fe3c] = 梅布尔寄来的茶罐\n# en: {A small tin arrives on the barge with your name on it, in Mabel's round hand. The label says \"FOR THE COUGH I HEARD THROUGH THE DOOR.\"|Another tin from Mabel. The label says \"FOR WHATEVER THIS WEEK IS.\"|Mabel's tins have a shelf of their own now. Today's label says only \"YOU KNOW.\"} {There's a pressed violet under the string.|It smells of honey and woodsmoke.|There's a biscuit taped to the lid.}\ntext [6c5e1e] = {驳船送来一个小罐子，上面是梅布尔圆圆的字迹写着你的名字。标签上写着“治那声隔着门我都听见了的咳嗽”。|梅布尔又寄来一罐。标签上写着“治这一周不管是什么的那些事”。|梅布尔的茶罐已经占了你一整层架子。今天的标签只写着“你懂的”。} {绳子下面压着一朵干紫罗兰。|闻起来有蜂蜜和柴烟的味道。|盖子上用胶带粘着一块饼干。}\n# en: A small tin of tea wrapped in brown paper and string on a dormitory pillow, a handwritten label on top and a pressed flower tucked under the string.\nscene [7e98e5] = 宿舍枕头上放着一小罐用牛皮纸和细绳包好的茶，上面贴着手写标签，绳子下压着一朵干花。\n# en: Bring her something back\nopt.left.label [1f40bf] = 给她捎点东西回去\n# en: {You bring her a jar of honey from the market. She calls it bribery and puts it on the shelf behind the counter.|You bring her flowers from the village stall. \"Don't,\" she says. \"Drink it.\" You do. It works.|You bring her a tin of your own, labelled \"YOU KNOW\". She laughs until she has to sit down, and keeps it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5f75b3] = {你从集市给她带了一罐蜂蜜。她说这是贿赂，把它摆上了柜台后面的架子。|你从镇上的花摊给她带了束花。“别谢，”她说，“喝。”你喝了。管用。|你给她带去一罐自己的，标签上写着“你知道”。她笑得不得不坐下来，然后收下了。}\n# en: Send it back with a note\nopt.right.label [7dbd32] = 附张字条寄回去\n# en: {She sends it back again with a longer note. You give up and drink it.|The tin comes back on the next barge. The label now says \"STUBBORN.\"|You send it back with a note that says \"I KNOW\". It comes back with two biscuits taped to the lid.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9369ce] = {她又寄了回来，附上一张更长的字条。你放弃抵抗，喝了。|茶罐搭下一班驳船回来了。标签现在写着“犟”。|你寄了回去，附一张字条写着“我知道”。它又回来了，盖子上粘了两块饼干。}\n\n@event mabel_cup\n# en: Your Cup on the Hook\ntitle [8777bb] = 挂钩上你的杯子\n# en: Behind Mabel's counter is a row of hooks with cups on them, each painted with a letter. You've never asked whose they are. Today, while you watch, Mabel hangs a new one with your initial on it. \"Regulars,\" she says, as if that settled it.\ntext [2202c5] = 梅布尔的柜台后面有一排挂钩，挂着杯子，每只都画着一个字母。你从没问过它们是谁的。今天，你看着梅布尔挂上一只新杯子，上面画着你名字的首字母。“常客，”她说，好像这就说明了一切。\n# en: A row of mismatched cups hanging on hooks behind a tea-shop counter, a woman hanging a new one with a student's initial painted on it.\nscene [ea24f5] = 茶馆柜台后挂钩上一排不成套的杯子，一个女人正挂上一只新杯子，上面画着一名学生名字的首字母。\n# en: Ask whose the others are\nopt.left.label [858fea] = 问问其他杯子是谁的\n# en: She tells you, cup by cup: a fisherman, a dead headmaster, a girl who became a duchess and still writes. \"And you,\" she says. Something in your chest unknots.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a1266] = 她一只一只告诉你：一个渔夫，一位已故的校长，一个后来成了公爵夫人、至今仍写信来的姑娘。“还有你，”她说。你胸口有什么东西松开了。\n# en: Say thank you and sit down\nopt.right.label [d9af96] = 道声谢，坐下\n# en: You sit. She brings your tea in your cup. You don't need to say anything else, and she doesn't either.\nopt.right.say.1 [77da72] = 你坐下。她用你的杯子给你端来茶。你不需要再说什么，她也一样。\n\n@event grimsby_intro\n# en: Not That One\ntitle [40767f] = 不是那本\n# en: You ask for a textbook. An elderly man on a ladder says \"Ah!\" with enormous enthusiasm and hands you a history of lighthouses. \"Not what you asked for,\" says Mr Grimsby. \"What you'll need. You'll see.\"\ntext [cb47fb] = 你要一本教科书。梯子上的老人热情洋溢地“啊！”了一声，递给你一本灯塔史。“不是你要的，”格林斯比先生说，“是你需要的。你会明白的。”\n# en: A cramped bookshop with leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller on a ladder holding out a book to a student below, the student holding a list.\nscene [ee1fec] = 拥挤的书店里书架东倒西歪，一位年迈的书商站在梯子上把一本书递给下面的学生，学生手里捏着一张书单。\n# en: Buy the lighthouse book\nopt.left.label [aed25d] = 买下那本灯塔书\n# en: You read it on the barge home. Chapter four has the exact proof your essay was missing. When you go back to say so, he only nods. \"They always come back,\" he says. \"Lighthouses.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [ae1dbc] = 你在回程的驳船上读了它。第四章恰好有你论文缺的那个证明。你回去告诉他，他只是点点头。“它们总会回来的，”他说，“灯塔。”\n# en: Say you'll think about it\nopt.right.label [b4769e] = 说你考虑一下\n# en: He puts the lighthouses back on the shelf, sadly, like a doctor whose prescription has been refused. \"It'll keep,\" he says. \"So will you.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [9586aa] = 他把那本讲灯塔的书放回书架，满脸遗憾，就像一个医生的处方被拒绝了。“它跑不了，”他说，“你也跑不了。”\n\n@event grimsby_parcel\n# en: The Wrong Book, Posted\ntitle [da6a85] = 寄来的错书\n# en: {A parcel arrives from Grimsby's. Inside is a book you didn't order and a note that says \"Thought of you. Pay when convenient. A. G.\"|Another parcel from Mr Grimsby. This one is about eels.|Grimsby's parcels come monthly now, like weather.} {It smells of rain and pipe smoke.|There's a bus ticket from another century inside the cover.|The note is written on the back of a recipe.}\ntext [79ef26] = {格林斯比书店寄来一个包裹。里面是一本你没订的书，附着一张字条，写着“想到了你。方便时再付。A.G.”|格林斯比先生又寄来一个包裹。这本讲的是鳗鱼。|格林斯比的包裹现在每月都来，像天气一样。} {闻起来有雨水和烟斗的味道。|封面里夹着一张上个世纪的车票。|字条写在一张菜谱的背面。}\n# en: A brown-paper parcel tied with string on a library desk, a student unwrapping an odd old book while a cat watches from the next chair.\nscene [443ef4] = 图书馆书桌上放着一个用牛皮纸和细绳捆着的包裹，一名学生正拆开一本古怪的旧书，邻座的猫在一旁看着。\n# en: Keep it and pay him\nopt.left.label [37a868] = 留下，付钱给他\n# en: {It's the wrong book, exactly as promised, and somewhere in it is the right page. It always is.|You read it out of politeness and finish it out of something else.|You pay him, and try the recipe on the back of the note. The book and the cake are both strange, and both good.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b466d3] = {果然是本错书，正如承诺；而书里某处，正是你要的那一页。向来如此。|你出于礼貌开始读，读完却是出于别的原因。|你付了钱，还试了字条背面的菜谱。书和蛋糕都很怪，也都很好。}\n# en: Send it back with the carrier\nopt.right.label [46b3c6] = 交给邮差退回去\n# en: {The carrier takes it back for nothing, grumbling. Grimsby sends a cheerful note that says \"Next time, then.\"|He sends it straight back with a second, wronger book. You keep both.|You send it back. Next month's parcel arrives anyway, as if nothing had happened, with a new recipe on the back of the note.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4792f3] = {邮差骂骂咧咧地把它免费带了回去。格林斯比寄来一张欢快的字条，写着“那就下次吧”。|他立刻把它寄了回来，外加一本更错的书。两本你都留下了。|你寄了回去。下个月的包裹照样寄到，好像什么都没发生过，字条背面换了一份新菜谱。}\n\n@event grimsby_mind_the_shop\n# en: Mind the Shop\ntitle [2eaf46] = 照看书店\n# en: Mr Grimsby has a ladder accident (a mild one; he lands on the poetry) and needs someone to mind the shop for a week. \"You know where everything is,\" he says, which is untrue. \"Better than me,\" he adds, which is.\ntext [355c82] = 格林斯比先生从梯子上摔了下来（不严重，落在了诗歌区），需要有人替他照看书店一周。“你知道东西都在哪儿，”他说，这不是真的。“比我清楚，”他补了一句，这倒是真的。\n# en: A student alone behind the counter of a crowded old bookshop at dusk, a ring of keys on the counter and a customer peering in through the dusty window.\nscene [5a432f] = 黄昏时一名学生独自站在拥挤的旧书店柜台后，柜台上放着一串钥匙，一位顾客透过满是灰尘的橱窗往里张望。\n# en: Mind the shop\nopt.left.label [d31c26] = 照看书店\n# en: You sell eleven books, all of them the wrong ones, and every customer comes back to thank you. Grimsby, on crutches, says you have the gift. He means it as a warning.\nopt.left.say.1 [60e74c] = 你卖出了十一本书，全是错的，而每位顾客都回来道谢。拄着拐杖的格林斯比说你有天分。他是当作警告说的。\n# en: \"I can't, I'm sorry.\"\nopt.right.label [c71ad9] = “我做不到，抱歉。”\n# en: He closes for the week instead, with a sign that says GONE TO LIGHTHOUSE. Nobody knows which one.\nopt.right.say.1 [c09b7c] = 他干脆歇业一周，门上挂了块牌子：“看灯塔去了”。没人知道是哪一座。\n\n@event pell_intro\n# en: A Thousand Drawers\ntitle [e5d6dc] = 一千个抽屉\n# en: You ask for something for a headache. An old man on a ladder stops, counts the drawers under his breath and comes down with exactly one twist of paper. \"Willow and feverfew,\" says Old Pell. \"Three pinches. Not four. Four is for people who don't listen.\"\ntext [b95382] = 你想买点治头痛的药。梯子上的老人停下来，低声数了数抽屉，下来时手里恰好拿着一小包纸。“柳树皮加小白菊，”老佩尔说，“三撮。不是四撮。四撮是给不听话的人的。”\n# en: An old apothecary on a library ladder in front of a wall of tiny labelled drawers, peering down over his spectacles at a student at the counter.\nscene [7dc69c] = 一位老药剂师站在一面贴满标签的小抽屉墙前的梯子上，从眼镜上方俯视着柜台前的学生。\n# en: Ask why not four\nopt.left.label [3eee3f] = 问他为什么不是四撮\n# en: He tells you, at length, with diagrams drawn in the counter dust. He's so pleased someone asked that he forgets to charge you. You leave with the cure, a headache from the lecture and the dosage for nine other things.\nopt.left.say.1 [38fb49] = 他讲了很久，还在柜台的灰上画了图。有人肯问，他高兴得忘了收钱。你带着药、一场讲座带来的新头痛，以及另外九种东西的剂量离开了。\n# en: Pay and go\nopt.right.label [9ac792] = 付钱走人\n# en: He nods at the door as you leave, as if adding you to a ledger of people who will be back. The headache is gone before the quay, and you are back within a month.\nopt.right.say.1 [b2eaf4] = 你走时他朝门口点点头，仿佛把你记进了一本“会回来的人”的账。还没到码头，头就不疼了；不到一个月，你果然回来了。\n\n@event pell_count_drops\n# en: Count Them Aloud\ntitle [6a0261] = 大声数出来\n# en: {Old Pell sets a dropper in your hand. \"Forty. Aloud.\" He watches your lips, not the vial.|\"Forty,\" says Pell, and stands behind you. You can hear him counting along under his breath.|Pell has started leaving you the hard tinctures. That is how he says he trusts you.} {A daughter rolls her eyes at the counter.|The drawers rattle when a cart goes by.|Something in drawer three hundred is humming.}\ntext [2f8c1a] = {老佩尔把一支滴管放进你手里。“四十滴。数出声。”他盯着的是你的嘴唇，不是药瓶。|“四十，”佩尔说，然后站到你身后。你能听见他在低声跟着数。|佩尔开始把难配的酊剂留给你了。这就是他表达信任的方式。} {一个女儿在柜台那边翻了个白眼。|有马车经过时，抽屉们咔嗒作响。|三百号抽屉里有什么东西在嗡嗡响。}\n# en: An old apothecary at a wooden counter holding a glass dropper over a vial, a student beside him counting on their fingers, a wall of tiny labelled drawers behind them.\nscene [6dff31] = 一位老药剂师站在木柜台前，把一支玻璃滴管悬在药瓶上方，身旁的学生掰着手指数数，身后是一整面贴着标签的小抽屉。\n# en: Count, aloud, every drop\nopt.left.label [f6db0f] = 一滴一滴大声数\n# en: {Forty, exactly. Pell says nothing for a full minute, then \"Again.\" You are fairly sure that was praise.|You lose count at thirty-one and start over without being told. Pell grunts approvingly.|Forty, of a tincture that fights back. Pell holds the vial to the light and puts it in drawer three hundred. The humming stops.}\nopt.left.say.1 [426397] = {正好四十滴。佩尔整整一分钟没说话，然后说“再来”。你相当确定那是在夸你。|数到三十一你乱了，没等人说就从头再来。佩尔赞许地哼了一声。|四十滴，还是一种会反抗的酊剂。佩尔把药瓶举到光下看了看，放进了第三百号抽屉。嗡嗡声停了。}\n# en: Eyeball it; it's close enough\nopt.right.label [e3a574] = 目测一下，差不多就行\n# en: {It's right. Pell checks it three times and is visibly annoyed that it's right.|Close enough is, today, exactly right. \"Luck,\" says Pell. \"Don't.\"|The hard tincture forgives you, today. Pell sniffs it and puts it away without comment, which is worse than comment.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5f639d] = {对了。佩尔查了三遍，对它居然是对的这件事明显很恼火。|差不多，今天恰好就是刚好。“运气，”佩尔说，“别指望。”|今天，这难缠的酊剂放了你一马。佩尔闻了闻，一句评价也没有就收了起来，这比评价更糟。}\n# en: {Forty-three. Pell pours it out without a word and hands you the dropper again. The silence lasts all shift.|Too many. A daughter fixes it before he sees. He sees.|Too many, of the hard one. Drawer three hundred hums louder, and Pell keeps the dropper for the rest of the week.}\nopt.right.say.2 [9adae6] = {四十三滴。佩尔一言不发地倒掉，把滴管又递给你。这份沉默持续了整个班。|多了。一个女儿赶在他看见前改好了。他还是看见了。|多了，偏偏是难缠的那种。第三百号抽屉嗡得更响了，佩尔这一星期剩下的日子都没再把滴管给你。}\n\n@event pell_daughters\n# en: Pell & Daughters\ntitle [e047f1] = 佩尔父女\n# en: Pell's daughters want a new sign, brighter labels and a counter that faces the door. Pell wants it to be fifty years ago. All three of them turn to you, the only person in the shop who isn't family. \"Well?\" says Pell.\ntext [5b6af3] = 佩尔的女儿们想要一块新招牌、更醒目的标签，还想把柜台转过来朝着门。佩尔想要五十年前的样子。三个人一齐转向你——铺子里唯一不是家里人的那个。“怎么说？”佩尔问。\n# en: An old apothecary and his two grown daughters arguing across a counter over a new painted shop sign, a student standing awkwardly between them.\nscene [d34c2c] = 一位老药剂师和两个成年的女儿隔着柜台为一块新漆的招牌争吵，一名学生尴尬地站在中间。\n# en: Side with the daughters\nopt.left.label [3b974c] = 站在女儿们一边\n# en: Pell stamps into the back room. A week later the new sign goes up. Underneath it, in small old letters, it still says PELL. He shows you, gruffly, as though you'd asked.\nopt.left.say.1 [fb675a] = 佩尔跺着脚进了里屋。一周后，新招牌挂了上去。招牌下面，用小小的旧字，依然写着“佩尔”。他板着脸指给你看，好像是你问起似的。\n# en: \"The drawers have never been wrong.\"\nopt.right.label [d09cc9] = “抽屉从来没错过。”\n# en: Pell glows. The daughters glare. Pell gives you a bonus and the daughters give you all the worst stocktakes. Everyone feels this is fair.\nopt.right.say.1 [dc138f] = 佩尔容光焕发。女儿们怒目而视。佩尔给了你一笔奖金，女儿们把最糟的盘点全派给了你。大家都觉得这很公平。\n# en: Pell glows. The daughters glare. Pell presses a bottle of his best tonic on you, and the daughters give you a look that lasts a month.\nopt.right.say.2 [3fae36] = 佩尔容光焕发。女儿们怒目而视。佩尔硬塞给你一瓶他最好的补剂，女儿们给了你一个持续一个月的眼神。\n\n@event grisk_pleasantries\n# en: Pleasantries\ntitle [3f3d10] = 寒暄\n# en: {Grisk raises a small glass from the corner table of the Gilded Newt. \"My friend,\" he says. \"Do sit. I'm not working.\"|Grisk, at the corner table again. \"How is the purse?\" he asks, with what sounds like real concern.|{has(in_debt) or flag(grisk_done): \"Ah, my favourite client,\" purrs Grisk.|\"Ah, my favourite non-client,\" purrs Grisk.}} {His ledger stays closed, which feels deliberate.|He is eating a single olive very slowly.|The landlady gives you a warning look.}\ntext [30a799] = {格里斯克在镀金蝾螈酒馆的角落桌边举起一只小酒杯。“我的朋友，”他说，“请坐。我今天不营业。”|格里斯克又坐在角落那张桌子。“钱袋还好吗？”他问，听起来像是真心关切。|{has(in_debt) or flag(grisk_done): “啊，我最喜欢的客户，”格里斯克轻声说。|“啊，我最喜欢的非客户，”格里斯克轻声说。}} {他的账簿一直合着，显然是故意的。|他正非常缓慢地吃着一颗橄榄。|老板娘朝你投来警告的一瞥。}\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting at a corner table in a low-beamed tavern, raising a small glass to a student, a fat ledger at his elbow.\nscene [83bdbd] = 低矮横梁的酒馆角落，一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精向一名学生举起一只小酒杯，手肘边放着一本厚厚的账簿。\n# en: Sit for one drink\nopt.left.label [b539a6] = 坐下喝一杯\n# en: {He talks about money the way other people talk about weather, and you learn more in an hour than in a term of Umbral Theory.|He tells you which of the village shops are in trouble and why. It feels like gossip. It is a lesson.|He pays for the drinks and writes nothing down, which is the most unsettling thing he has ever done.}\nopt.left.say.1 [aecf5e] = {他谈起钱来就像别人谈天气，你在一小时里学到的比一学期暗影理论还多。|他告诉你村里哪些铺子遇到了麻烦，以及原因。听着像闲话，其实是一堂课。|他付了酒钱，什么也没记——这是他做过最让人不安的事。}\n# en: Keep walking\nopt.right.label [6ca967] = 继续走\n# en: {\"Another time,\" he calls after you. \"I'm very patient.\"|He raises the glass anyway. You feel it between your shoulder blades all the way out.|You keep walking. The landlady nods to you on your way out, very slightly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8ecc7b] = {“改天，”他在你身后喊，“我很有耐心。”|他照样举了杯。直到走出门，你都觉得那目光落在你两肩之间。|你继续往外走。经过时，老板娘冲你微微点了下头。}\n\n@event grisk_tip\n# en: A Tip, Between Friends\ntitle [576ce2] = 朋友之间的消息\n# en: Grisk slides a coin across the table. \"Between friends,\" he murmurs. \"The barge company is about to buy a second barge. The ferryman's cousin will sell his share this week, cheap, because he doesn't know. You could know.\"\ntext [127226] = 格里斯克把一枚硬币推过桌面。“朋友之间，”他低声说，“驳船公司要买第二条驳船了。船夫的表亲这周会贱卖他的股份，因为他不知道。你可以知道。”\n# en: A goblin leaning across a tavern table to murmur into a student's ear, sliding a single gold coin across the wood with one finger.\nscene [95b950] = 一个地精隔着酒馆桌子凑到学生耳边低语，用一根手指把一枚金币推过木桌。\n# en: Buy the share\nopt.left.label [cddc5b] = 买下那份股份\n# en: The share costs a fair few crowns.\nopt.left.hint [af6e64] = 这份股份要花上不少克朗。\n# en: The second barge arrives. The share triples. Grisk lifts his glass across the Newt. You feel rich and slightly unwell.\nopt.left.say.1 [d08cfb] = 第二条驳船到了。股份翻了三倍。格里斯克隔着蝾螈酒馆朝你举杯。你觉得自己很有钱，又有点不舒服。\n# en: The second barge sinks on its first crossing. Grisk is sympathetic. \"Risk,\" he says. \"Delicious, and sometimes poison.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [086af1] = 第二条驳船第一次渡湖就沉了。格里斯克很同情。“风险，”他说，“美味，有时也是毒药。”\n# en: Leave the cousin his share\nopt.right.label [f20d63] = 把股份留给那位表亲\n# en: Grisk pockets the coin. \"Honest,\" he says, as if diagnosing something. \"It'll pass. Or it won't, and you'll be poor and admired.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [89bb79] = 格里斯克收起硬币。“老实，”他说，像在诊断什么病，“会好的。要么好不了，那你就会又穷又受人敬重。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/full_picture.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/full_picture.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event picture_lyra\n# en: Unbearable\ntitle [447d58] = 受不了\n# en: You bring Lyra toast after practice, because she can't make it, and set it down without a word, because she won't ask. She looks at it, and at you. \"You know about the toast,\" she says. \"And my mother. And the Trials, and the letter, and the flourish.\" A pause. \"You know everything. It's unbearable.\" She takes a slice anyway.\ntext [b249fa] = 练习结束后，你给莱拉送去吐司，因为她不会做；你一言不发地放下，因为她不会开口要。她看看吐司，又看看你。“你知道吐司的事，”她说，“还有我母亲。还有冬季考核，那封信，那个起手式。”停顿了一下。“你什么都知道。真让人受不了。”她还是拿起了一片。\n# en: A duelling hall after dark, one student sitting on the floor against the wall, another handing her a plate of slightly burnt toast.\nscene [d6684b] = 入夜后的决斗厅，一个学生靠墙坐在地上，另一个递给她一盘有点烤焦的吐司。\n# en: \"I'd like to know more.\"\nopt.left.label [0aefe3] = “我还想知道更多。”\n# en: \"There isn't any more,\" she says, and then, after a long time, tells you one more thing: the name of her grandmother's horse. It doesn't matter at all. It's the first thing she has ever told you that she didn't have to.\nopt.left.say.1 [7fe72b] = “没有更多了，”她说，然后过了很久，又告诉了你一件事：她祖母那匹马的名字。这根本无关紧要。这是她第一次告诉你一件她没必要说的事。\n# en: \"I'll forget half of it, if you like.\"\nopt.right.label [c5e014] = “你愿意的话，我忘掉一半。”\n# en: \"Don't you dare,\" she says, too quickly, and then goes red. She eats the toast. It's burnt. She says it's adequate, which from Lyra is a bouquet, and doesn't let you leave until it's finished.\nopt.right.say.1 [6755df] = “你敢，”她说得太快，随即红了脸。她吃了吐司。烤焦了。她说还过得去，这话从莱拉嘴里说出来就是一束花，而且在你吃完之前不许你走。\n\n@event picture_corvin\n# en: No Jokes\ntitle [08a66e] = 不开玩笑\n# en: Up on the parapet, Corvin shuffles his grandfather's deck and doesn't deal. \"You know about the trolls,\" he says. \"And my brother. And the warning. And the Sundays.\" He laughs, but only a little. \"Nobody knows all of it, saint. I don't usually let anyone have more than two.\" The jokes have stopped. So has he.\ntext [1e8301] = 在矮墙上，科尔文洗着他祖父的那副牌，却不发。“你知道巨魔的事，”他说，“还有我哥哥。还有最后警告。还有星期天。”他笑了，但只笑了一点点。“没人知道全部，圣人。我通常只让人知道两件。”玩笑停了。他也停了。\n# en: A gargoyle passage at night, two students sitting on the parapet, one shuffling a worn old deck of cards very slowly.\nscene [94ea19] = 夜里的石像鬼通道，两个学生坐在矮墙上，其中一个非常缓慢地洗着一副旧牌。\n# en: Tell him something of yours\nopt.left.label [cd3f79] = 告诉他一件你自己的事\n# en: You tell him the thing you don't tell anyone. He listens, the way his grandfather probably did, and then deals you a hand. \"Now we're even,\" he says. \"Don't you dare tell anyone I said something nice.\" You don't.\nopt.left.say.1 [713d18] = 你告诉了他那件你从不告诉任何人的事。他听着，大概就像他祖父当年那样听着，然后给你发了一手牌。“现在扯平了，”他说，“敢告诉别人我说了好话，你就完了。”你没说。\n# en: Let him deal, and say nothing\nopt.right.label [838085] = 让他发牌，什么也不说\n# en: You don't say anything, which turns out to be right. He deals. You play badly, and he lets you win once, which you both pretend not to notice. Somewhere around midnight he starts joking again, and it sounds different.\nopt.right.say.1 [e129f7] = 你什么也没说，结果这样才对。他发了牌。你打得很差，他让你赢了一把，你们俩都假装没注意到。快到午夜时，他又开始开玩笑了，听起来却不一样了。\n\n@event picture_tobias\n# en: A Saying for It\ntitle [b205e7] = 有句老话\n# en: Repotting seedlings, Tobias goes quiet for so long that Gerald, on his shelf, stops screaming to look. \"You know about my ma, and the blight, and the broom,\" he says. \"And about after. I haven't told anyone about after.\" He wipes his hands on his apron. \"Back home there's a saying for someone who knows you like that. I'll get it wrong.\"\ntext [f88488] = 给秧苗换盆的时候，托拜厄斯沉默了很久，久到架子上的杰拉德都停下尖叫来看他。“你知道我娘的事，还有枯萎病，还有那把扫帚，”他说，“还有以后的事。以后的事我谁都没告诉过。”他在围裙上擦了擦手。“老家有句话，说的就是像你这样了解一个人的人。我会说错的。”\n# en: A greenhouse in the evening, two students repotting seedlings side by side, a small mandrake in a knitted hat watching from a shelf.\nscene [c48a01] = 傍晚的温室里，两个学生并肩给秧苗换盆，一株戴着针织帽的小曼德拉草在架子上看着。\n# en: Ask him to say it anyway\nopt.left.label [4d16c0] = 请他照样说出来\n# en: He says it, and gets it wrong, and then tells you the long version, which is about a goat. By the end you're both laughing so hard you knock over a tray of seedlings. They don't seem to mind. Gerald does.\nopt.left.say.1 [220ca0] = 他说了，说错了，然后又讲了个长版本，是关于一头山羊的。讲到最后，你们俩笑得打翻了一盘秧苗。秧苗似乎不介意。杰拉德介意。\n# en: Promise to help, whatever he decides\nopt.right.label [f0e79e] = 无论他怎么决定，都答应帮他\n# en: He looks at you for a long moment, and then laughs from the belly, the way he does, and apologises for laughing, the way he does. \"That's the saying,\" he says. \"Near enough.\" He gives you the best seedling to keep.\nopt.right.say.1 [0de78a] = 他看了你好一会儿，然后像他平常那样从肚子里笑出来，又像他平常那样为笑而道歉。“就是这句话，”他说，“差不多。”他把最好的一株秧苗送给了你。\n\n@event picture_seraphine\n# en: Forty-One Todays\ntitle [09d448] = 四十一个今天\n# en: Seraphine gives you a little calendar she has stitched herself. Forty-one of its days have a tiny star on them. \"The days you told me what today was,\" she says. \"I slip, and you always know. And you know the rest: my mother, the lake, what gets too loud.\" She frowns, the way she does at a future. \"Nobody's ever kept me in today before. I wanted you to have the count.\"\ntext [3943ec] = 塞拉芬送给你一本她亲手缝的小日历。上面有四十一天画着一颗小小的星。“这些是你告诉我今天是哪天的日子，”她说，“我会滑走，而你总是知道。其余的你也知道：我母亲，湖，什么会变得太吵。”她皱起眉，就像对着一个未来。“以前从没人把我留在今天。我想让你拿着这个数。”\n# en: A seer at the lakeshore in the afternoon holding out a small hand-stitched calendar, dozens of its days marked with the same tiny drawn star.\nscene [6f7650] = 午后的湖岸，一位先知递出一本手缝的小日历，几十个日子上都画着同样一颗小星星。\n# en: Promise to keep counting\nopt.left.label [9bc895] = 答应继续数下去\n# en: \"Forty-two,\" you say, because it's today and she's here. She laughs, a real one, finished. For the rest of the afternoon she doesn't slip once, and when you part she says \"see you tomorrow\", and means tomorrow.\nopt.left.say.1 [45fb07] = “四十二，”你说，因为今天就是今天，而她就在这里。她笑了，一个真正的、完整的笑。那个下午她一次也没有滑走，分别时她说“明天见”，指的就是明天。\n# en: Teach her a trick to find today herself\nopt.right.label [ab0ccd] = 教她自己找到今天的诀窍\n# en: You teach her to count the bells: the castle rings the day in a pattern, if you listen. She practises all afternoon, half a sentence at a time. The next week she slips, and finds her own way back, and comes to tell you, exact and pleased.\nopt.right.say.1 [dca9ea] = 你教她数钟声：城堡每天敲钟的方式不一样，只要你听。她练了一下午，每次半句话。下个星期她又滑走了，却自己找回了路，跑来告诉你，精确而得意。\n\n@event picture_mir\n# en: Documented\ntitle [6cb365] = 已记录\n# en: Mir holds out a notebook, the newest one, open at a page with your name at the top. \"I've been documenting you,\" they say. \"Fair's fair. You know about the owl and the family and the name day and the fires. Probably all the fires. So I've been writing down what I know about you, to see if it's even.\" They look worried. \"It isn't even. You're winning.\"\ntext [5e582b] = 米尔递过来一本笔记本，最新的那本，翻开的那页最上面写着你的名字。“我一直在记录你，”TA 说，“这才公平。你知道猫头鹰、家里、命名日和那些火灾的事。大概所有的火灾。所以我把我知道的关于你的事都写下来，看看是不是扯平了。”TA 显得很担心。“没扯平。你领先。”\n# en: A cluttered workshop at night, an artificer holding out a battered notebook open at a page with someone's name at the top.\nscene [2e264b] = 入夜后杂乱的工坊里，一位奇械师递出一本破旧的笔记本，翻开的那页最上面写着某人的名字。\n# en: Help them fill in the page\nopt.left.label [9c56ba] = 帮 TA 把这页填满\n# en: You sit on the bench and answer every question, fast, the way they ask them, with exclamation marks. It takes until midnight. When it's done they read it through twice and say \"huh\", which from Mir means something has been mended. They file it with the precious ones.\nopt.left.say.1 [8d2752] = 你坐在工作台上回答每一个问题，又快又急，就像 TA 提问那样，带着感叹号。一直弄到午夜。写完后 TA 从头读了两遍，说了声“嗯”，这在米尔那里意味着有什么东西修好了。TA 把它和那些最珍贵的放在了一起。\n# en: Tell them it doesn't have to be even\nopt.right.label [d37847] = 告诉 TA 不必扯平\n# en: \"It doesn't have to be even,\" you say. Mir thinks about it, visibly, like a machine running a sum. \"That's very inefficient,\" they say. \"I like it.\" They close the notebook. The owl on the shelf blinks twice, which is how it says \"noted\".\nopt.right.say.1 [5ee632] = “不必扯平的，”你说。米尔想了想，看得出来，就像一台机器在算一道题。“这效率很低，”TA 说，“我喜欢。”TA 合上了笔记本。架子上的猫头鹰眨了两下眼睛，那是它说“已记下”的方式。\n\n@event picture_bram\n# en: Don't Make It Weird\ntitle [4ae597] = 别弄得那么奇怪\n# en: You find Bram in the corridor of head boys, in front of his father's portrait. He doesn't move when you come up. \"You know all of it,\" he says, to the portrait. \"The exam. The hall tax. Him. Every rematch.\" He clears his throat. \"Most people know the sash. You know the rest, and you never once used it.\" He's very red. \"Don't make it weird.\"\ntext [5f3f3a] = 你在历届男生学生会主席的画像走廊里找到了布拉姆，他站在他父亲的画像前。你走过来时他没动。“你全都知道，”他对着画像说，“那场考试。走廊税。他。每一次重赛。”他清了清嗓子。“大多数人只知道这条绶带。你知道其余的，却从没拿来用过一次。”他脸涨得通红。“别弄得那么奇怪。”\n# en: A corridor of old portraits of head boys, a prefect in a red sash standing in front of one of them with a student beside him.\nscene [ddb9c2] = 一条挂满历届男生学生会主席旧画像的走廊，一位系着红绶带的级长站在其中一幅前，身旁站着一个学生。\n# en: Make it a little weird\nopt.left.label [07cc14] = 弄得稍微奇怪一点\n# en: You put your arm round his shoulders and tell the portrait, firmly, that its son is going to be a better prefect than it ever was. Bram makes a noise like a kettle. Then he laughs, really laughs, and gives you a demerit for \"insolence to a painting\".\nopt.left.say.1 [ab34c4] = 你搂住他的肩膀，郑重地告诉那幅画像，它的儿子会成为一个比它当年好得多的级长。布拉姆发出一声像水壶一样的声音。然后他笑了，真正地笑了，给你记了一笔“对画作无礼”。\n# en: Stand there with him, and don't\nopt.right.label [12cc60] = 陪他站着，什么也不做\n# en: You stand there with him and say nothing at all, which is the kindest thing you've ever done for him. After a while he says \"right\" and walks off, briskly. The next day there's a note in your bag in block capitals: THANK YOU. DON'T.\nopt.right.say.1 [56b995] = 你陪他站在那儿，一句话也没说，这是你为他做过的最温柔的事。过了一会儿，他说了声“好吧”，快步走开了。第二天你的书包里有一张用大写字母写的字条：谢谢。别。\n\n@event picture_maud\n# en: Over Tea, Dearie\ntitle [8347d7] = 喝杯茶吧，亲爱的\n# en: Maud pours two cups and doesn't sit. \"You know about Edmund,\" she says. \"And the garden, and Tuppence, and my knees, and the soup. You've heard all my sad stories, dearie, and you've never once looked sorry for me.\" She sits, finally. \"Sixty years since anyone knew me all the way through. I'd forgotten what it was like.\"\ntext [ea92d6] = 莫德倒了两杯茶，却没坐下。“你知道埃德蒙的事，”她说，“还有那座花园，还有塔彭丝，还有我的膝盖，还有汤。我那些伤心事你全听过了，亲爱的，可你从没露出过一次可怜我的样子。”她终于坐下了。“六十年来，再没人这样从里到外地了解我。我都忘了那是什么滋味。”\n# en: A cottage kitchen, a pot of soup on the stove, an old woman pouring two cups of tea at a scrubbed table, a goat looking in at the window.\nscene [f0df80] = 小屋的厨房里，炉子上炖着一锅汤，一位老妇人在擦洗干净的桌边倒两杯茶，一只山羊从窗外往里看。\n# en: Ask her to teach you the soup\nopt.left.label [250e0e] = 请她教你做那锅汤\n# en: \"You're not allowed to write it down,\" she says, and teaches you anyway, slowly, with her hands over yours on the spoon. You understand, two weeks later, that she wasn't teaching you the soup at all.\nopt.left.say.1 [41c58f] = “不许写下来，”她说，还是教了你，慢慢地，她的手覆在你握勺的手上。两个星期后你才明白，她教你的根本不是汤。\n# en: Tell her a sad story of yours\nopt.right.label [f5bc7c] = 给她讲一件你的伤心事\n# en: You tell her one, as if it were a recipe. She listens, and nods, and at the end says, \"Needs salt.\" It's exactly right. Bartholomew bleats at the window. She gives him a biscuit, and you another.\nopt.right.say.1 [5fef2b] = 你讲了一件，像讲一道菜谱。她听着，点着头，最后说：“得加点盐。”说得一点没错。巴塞洛缪在窗外咩咩叫。她给了它一块饼干，又给了你一块。\n\n@event picture_thorne\n# en: Hm\ntitle [eb3e6e] = 嗯\n# en: You hold the basket while Thorne cuts roses at dawn, and his hands shake, and he lets you see them shake. \"You know about the storm,\" he says. \"The real version. And the roses. And these.\" He holds up his hands. \"And the old fool in purple.\" A long pause. \"Hm.\" From Thorne, it's a speech.\ntext [8a75c7] = 黎明时，索恩剪玫瑰，你替他提着篮子，他的手在抖，而他让你看见了。“你知道那场风暴的事，”他说，“真正的版本。还有玫瑰。还有这个。”他举起双手。“还有那个穿紫衣服的老傻瓜。”长长的停顿。“嗯。”从索恩嘴里说出来，这就是一篇演讲。\n# en: A tiny walled rose garden behind an observatory at dawn, an old professor with secateurs, his hands not quite steady, a student holding the basket.\nscene [26481e] = 观星台后面一座小小的围墙玫瑰园，黎明时分，一位老教授拿着修枝剪，手不太稳，一个学生提着篮子。\n# en: Ask him to tell you about the storm again\nopt.left.label [1a6fd8] = 请他再讲讲那场风暴\n# en: He tells you, the whole of it, the way he never tells it in lectures: how badly he lost, and how. At the end he hands you the secateurs. \"Your turn,\" he says, meaning the roses, meaning more than the roses. \"Badly is fine. Badly is the point.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [af6a4a] = 他讲了，从头到尾，用一种他在课上从不讲的方式：他输得有多惨，又是怎么输的。最后他把修枝剪递给你。“轮到你了，”他说，指的是玫瑰，又不只是玫瑰。“输得惨没关系。输得惨才是重点。”\n# en: Say nothing, and cut the next rose for him\nopt.right.label [384a48] = 什么也不说，替他剪下一朵\n# en: You take the secateurs and cut the next rose, carefully, and the one after that. He watches, and doesn't correct you, which has never happened. At the end he says nothing at all, and doesn't take the secateurs back. You'll be proud of that for years.\nopt.right.say.1 [618145] = 你接过修枝剪，小心地剪下一朵，又剪下一朵。他看着，没有纠正你，这是从来没有过的事。最后他什么也没说，也没把修枝剪要回去。这会让你骄傲好多年。\n\n@event picture_brask\n# en: Half a Sentence More\ntitle [1ef755] = 多说半句\n# en: In the loft, Brask feeds the old owl and, for once, talks to you in a whole sentence. \"You know about the owl, and the feather, and the woman with the blackthorn, and that I bow to the wood.\" She feeds the owl another mouse. \"Students don't usually stay long enough to learn that much. The owl's noticed. So have I.\"\ntext [6b848b] = 在阁楼上，布拉斯克一边喂那只老猫头鹰，一边破天荒地对你说了一句完整的话。“你知道猫头鹰的事，还有那根羽毛，还有那个砍黑刺李的女人，还有我向林子鞠躬。”她又喂了猫头鹰一只老鼠。“学生通常待不了那么久，学不到那么多。猫头鹰注意到了。我也注意到了。”\n# en: A stable loft at dusk, an old barn owl on a beam, a professor in a leather apron sitting on a hay bale beside a student.\nscene [cc1991] = 黄昏时的马厩阁楼，一只老仓鸮蹲在横梁上，一位系着皮围裙的教授和一个学生并排坐在干草捆上。\n# en: Ask her which loss hurt most\nopt.left.label [0b9c27] = 问她哪一次失去最痛\n# en: She's quiet for a long time. Then she tells you, in a whole sentence, then another, and you understand why she never would before. At the end the owl hoots once, softly, as if it had been waiting. \"There,\" she says. \"That's the moral.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [a95e84] = 她沉默了很久。然后她告诉了你，用一句完整的话，又一句，你明白了她以前为什么从不肯说。最后，猫头鹰轻轻叫了一声，仿佛一直在等。“好了，”她说，“这就是寓意。”\n# en: Ask the owl instead\nopt.right.label [c6ca6d] = 改问猫头鹰\n# en: You turn to the owl and ask it, very politely, what it thinks of you. The owl regards you with contempt. Brask laughs, loudly, the once-a-term laugh, a month early. \"It likes you,\" she says. \"That's its liking face.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [5bfd52] = 你转向猫头鹰，非常礼貌地问它对你有什么看法。猫头鹰轻蔑地打量着你。布拉斯克大笑起来，那一学期一次的大笑，提前了一个月。“它喜欢你，”她说，“这就是它喜欢人的表情。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/heists/shared.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/heists/shared.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event heist_way_out\n# en: Out Before Dawn\ntitle [8d8cfc] = 天亮前出来\n# en: {heist_loot == 0: {heist_deck_tonight == 1: You come out with nothing to sell but a tin of cards that was never yours to sell, and a grin.|You come out with nothing but dust on your knees and your heart going like a drum.}|{heist_loot <= 2: You come out with a few small, old things under your shirt, each worth more than it looks.|{heist_loot <= 4: You come out with a real bundle: {heist_where == 1: pages, and something heavier that sighs when you move|papers, and something heavier that clinks when you move}.|You come out with more than your shirt will hold, and walk very carefully.}}} {heist_where == 1: The library is grey with dawn behind you.|The tower's slits are going grey above you.}\ntext [2cc658] = {heist_loot == 0: {heist_deck_tonight == 1: 你出来时没有什么可卖的，只有一铁盒本来就不归你卖的牌，还有一脸笑。|你出来时除了膝盖上的灰和咚咚直跳的心，什么也没带。}|{heist_loot <= 2: 你衬衫底下揣着几样又小又旧的东西出来了，每一样都比看上去值钱。|{heist_loot <= 4: 你带出来真正的一包：{heist_where == 1: 几页纸，还有一样更沉的东西，你一动它就叹气|几张纸，还有一样更沉的东西，你一动它就叮当响}。|你带出来的东西多得衬衫装不下，走得非常小心。}}} {heist_where == 1: 身后的图书馆被晨光染灰了。|头顶塔上的箭孔渐渐泛灰。}\n# en: A student slipping out of a dark doorway at the grey end of night, a bundle held under one arm, looking both ways down a castle corridor.\nscene [42dbc8] = 夜色将尽时，一个学生从黑暗的门口溜出来，胳膊底下夹着一个包，在城堡走廊里左右张望。\n# en: Sell it to the collector\nopt.left.label [90e67f] = 卖给收藏家\n# en: Crowns, by what you carry. A little Hollow.\nopt.left.hint [28243a] = 按你带的东西换克朗。稍偏空洞。\n# en: {heist_loot == 0: {heist_deck_tonight == 1: The tin goes to Corvin, not the collector. You go to bed, and lie awake grinning.|There's nothing to sell. You go to bed, and lie awake anyway.}|{~The collector in the village back room weighs each thing in a gloved hand and pays in old crowns, without a question.|The collector's man meets you by the village pump at noon. He counts your things, and then his coins, and both counts are fair.|The collector holds your best piece up to the window and is quiet for a long time. Then he pays more than you'd have asked.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [7c9cf0] = {heist_loot == 0: {heist_deck_tonight == 1: 铁盒归科尔文，不归收藏家。你上床睡觉，睁着眼躺着，一直在笑。|没什么可卖的。你上床睡觉，却照样睁着眼躺着。}|{~村里那间后屋的收藏家戴着手套把每件东西掂了掂，付了旧克朗，一个问题也没问。|收藏家的人中午在村里的水泵边等你。他先数你的东西，再数他的钱，两样都数得公道。|收藏家把你最好的那件举到窗前，沉默了很久。然后他付的比你开的价还多。}}\n# en: Keep it, and read it\nopt.right.label [c7a87f] = 留着，读一读\n# en: Lore, by what you carry. A little Hollow.\nopt.right.hint [c67ffe] = 按你带的东西得学识。稍偏空洞。\n# en: {heist_loot == 0: There's nothing to keep but the night, and you keep that.|{~You spread it all on your bed behind drawn curtains and read until breakfast. Half of it you shouldn't know. You know it now.|You hide it under a loose floorboard and take it out a piece at a time, over weeks, like a box of sweets.|You read it all twice and burn the worst of it. The rest you keep, and it keeps you up.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [43e1e2] = {heist_loot == 0: 除了这一夜，没什么可留的，那你就留着这一夜。|{~你拉上床帘，把东西全摊在床上，一直读到早饭。有一半你本不该知道。现在你知道了。|你把它们藏在一块松动的地板底下，几个星期里一件一件拿出来，像一盒糖。|你全都读了两遍，把最糟的那些烧了。其余的你留着，它们让你睡不着。}}\n\n@event heist_slip\n# en: A Near Thing\ntitle [920312] = 险些\n# en: {heist_dropped == 1: {~You get away, just, and something you carried doesn't: it's lying out there in the dark where you dropped it.|You end up flat in an alcove with your heart in your mouth and one thing fewer in your shirt.|You're clear, scraped and shaking, and you've left something behind for somebody to find.}|{~You get away, just, with nothing to lose but your nerve, and some of that.|You end up flat in an alcove with your heart in your mouth, glad your shirt was empty.|You're clear, scraped and shaking, and you've left nothing behind but a scuff.}} {heist_cover > 0: You've one more near thing in you, maybe.|That was your last near thing.}\ntext [0cf283] = {heist_dropped == 1: {~你逃开了，勉强，可你带的一样东西没逃开：它躺在后面的黑暗里，就在你掉下它的地方。|你最后贴在一个壁龛里，心提到了嗓子眼，衬衫里少了一样东西。|你脱身了，擦破了皮，浑身发抖，还给别人留下了点东西去发现。}|{~你逃开了，勉强，除了胆子没什么可丢的，胆子也丢了一点。|你最后贴在一个壁龛里，心提到了嗓子眼，庆幸衬衫里是空的。|你脱身了，擦破了皮，浑身发抖，除了一道擦痕什么也没留下。}} {heist_cover > 0: 也许你还能再险一次。|那是你最后一次险些。}\n# en: A student flattened into a dark alcove, holding their breath, as the glow of a lantern slides past along the floor.\nscene [bf7e6a] = 一个学生贴在黑暗的壁龛里屏住呼吸，提灯的光晕沿着地面从旁边滑过。\n# en: That's enough for tonight\nopt.left.label [e41fc4] = 今晚到此为止\n# en: {~You take what's left and go, by the long way.|You decide the night has made its point.|You go while you can.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9b9c3e] = {~你带着剩下的东西，绕远路走了。|你认定今晚已经把话说清楚了。|趁还走得了，你走了。}\n# en: Wait, and go on\nopt.right.label [5458f2] = 等一等，接着走\n# en: On: the next slip may not be a near thing.\nopt.right.hint [1c2ad6] = 继续：下一次失手可能就不只是险些了。\n# en: {~You wait till the dark is quiet again, and go on.|You count to five hundred, and go on.|You go on. Somewhere behind you, someone is looking at the place where you were.}\nopt.right.say.1 [726ea1] = {~你等到黑暗重新安静下来，接着往前走。|你数到五百，接着往前走。|你接着往前走。身后某处，有人正看着你刚才待过的地方。}\n\n@event heist_caught\n# en: Caught\ntitle [63a445] = 被抓\n# en: A lantern comes up full in your face. {heist_where == 1: Down in the stacks, at night,|Halfway up the seventh tower, at night,} {heist_loot == 0: and empty-handed, which convinces nobody.|with your arms full of things that aren't yours. The prefect takes them all, into a sack for the confiscation vault.} The prefect behind the lantern looks less angry than tired.\ntext [a37b5d] = 一盏提灯正对着你的脸照过来。{heist_where == 1: 深夜，在书库底下，|深夜，在第七塔半腰，}{heist_loot == 0: 两手空空，这谁也说服不了。|怀里抱满了不属于你的东西。级长把它们全收进一只送往没收库的袋子。}提灯后面的级长看起来与其说生气，不如说疲惫。\n# en: A prefect's lantern held high in a dark doorway, lighting up a student caught with a bundle of stolen things in their arms.\nscene [75041a] = 黑暗的门口，一位级长高举提灯，照出一个怀里抱满偷来的东西、被当场抓住的学生。\n# en: Go quietly\nopt.left.label [250d1c] = 乖乖跟他走\n# en: {heist_warned == 1: You go quietly. You'd been warned: a month of detention, and three of a door that is checked at midnight.|You go quietly. It doesn't help much: a month of detention, starting with the first free afternoon, and your name on the prefects' list.}\nopt.left.say.1 [793562] = {heist_warned == 1: 你乖乖跟着走。你被警告过的：一个月禁闭，外加三个月午夜有人查的门。|你乖乖跟着走。没多大用：一个月禁闭，从第一个空闲的下午开始，你的名字也上了级长的名单。}\n# en: Run for it\nopt.right.label [38d292] = 拔腿就跑\n# en: {heist_loot == 0: You run, and know every turn better than they do. You get away. You also know they saw your face.|You leave the sack to the prefect and run, and know every turn better than they do. You get away. You also know they saw your face.}\nopt.right.say.1 [015d77] = {heist_loot == 0: 你拔腿就跑，每个拐角你都比他们熟。你逃掉了。你也知道他们看见了你的脸。|你把袋子留给级长，拔腿就跑，每个拐角你都比他们熟。你逃掉了。你也知道他们看见了你的脸。}\n# en: {heist_warned == 1: You run two corridors and into a second prefect. You'd been warned: a month of detention, three of a locked door at midnight, and a bruised shin.|You run two corridors and into a second prefect. A month of detention, a bruised shin, and your name on the prefects' list.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ed51ce] = {heist_warned == 1: 你跑过两条走廊，撞上了第二位级长。你被警告过的：一个月禁闭，三个月午夜锁上的门，还有一块青肿的小腿。|你跑过两条走廊，撞上了第二位级长。一个月禁闭，一块青肿的小腿，你的名字也上了级长的名单。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/heists/stacks.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/heists/stacks.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event heist_stacks_enter\n# en: The Stair Under the Chains\ntitle [06a836] = 锁链下的楼梯\n# en: {heist_stacks_deepest == 0: Midnight in the Restricted Section. By the book-return hatch, the kickboard under the east stacks' last chained shelf swings open where your map says it will, on the stair going down. Below, the deep stacks: books nobody has signed out in a century, and the confiscation vault at the bottom.|{~The kickboard, the stair, the chains above you pulled taut by something below.|The stair under the chained shelf, and the cold breath coming up it.|Midnight, the chained shelf, and the dark going down.}} {flag(carto_book_lift): Or there's the bindery's book-lift, a floor below, which goes down as far as the breathing shelves.|}\ntext [9cc5e6] = {heist_stacks_deepest == 0: 午夜的禁书区。还书口旁边，东区书库最后一排锁链书架下的踢脚板，正如你的地图所说，一推就开，露出往下的楼梯。下面是深层书库：一百年没人借过的书，最底下是没收库。|{~踢脚板，楼梯，头顶那些被下面的东西拉紧的锁链。|锁链书架下的楼梯，还有顺着它往上冒的冷气。|午夜，锁链书架，往下延伸的黑暗。}} {flag(carto_book_lift): 或者还有装订室的书梯，在下面一层，能一直下到会呼吸的书架那里。|}\n# en: A dark library aisle at midnight, the last shelf's kickboard swung open on a narrow stair going down, a student with a shaded candle at the top.\nscene [13823b] = 午夜昏暗的图书馆过道，最后一排书架的踢脚板敞开着，露出一道往下的窄楼梯，一个学生举着遮了光的蜡烛站在楼梯顶上。\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.left.label [4bf72b] = 今晚不去\n# en: {~You put the kickboard back and go to bed with the stair in your head.|Not tonight. The chains settle as you leave, as if disappointed.|You listen at the top of the stair for a while, and go no further.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d22360] = {~你把踢脚板放回去，带着脑子里的楼梯去睡觉。|今晚不去。你离开时锁链松了下来，像是有点失望。|你在楼梯顶上听了一会儿，没再往下走。}\n# en: Go down\nopt.right.label [2ff2a1] = 下去\n# en: A heist: press on card by card, slip away with what you carry any time.\nopt.right.hint [f5be0b] = 一次偷盗：一张牌一张牌地往前，随时可以带着手里的东西溜走。\n# en: {flag(carto_book_lift): You go down to the bindery, climb into the book-lift's box and let the rope down hand over hand, until its knots stop you where the shelves start breathing.|{~You pull the kickboard shut behind you and go down by your map.|You shade your candle and go down, counting steps.|You go down quietly, one hand on the cold wall, the chains creaking overhead.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [084368] = {flag(carto_book_lift): 你下到装订室，爬进书梯的木箱，一把一把地放下绳子，直到绳上的结让你停在书架开始呼吸的地方。|{~你在身后关上踢脚板，照着地图往下走。|你遮住烛光往下走，一级一级地数。|你一只手扶着冰冷的墙，悄悄往下走，锁链在头顶吱吱作响。}}\n\n@event heist_stacks_unmapped\n# en: Chains Below\ntitle [c9588a] = 下面的锁链\n# en: {Midnight in the Restricted Section. The chains on the east stacks' last shelf run down through the floor, and something below pulls them taut, then lets them go, like breathing.|The chains on the last shelf again, taut, then slack. There's a stair down there somewhere. You've no map for it.} {seen(carto_map_blanks) > 0: The Cartographers have a blank on their map just here.|Someone in the castle must have a map of what's below.}\ntext [2b1809] = {午夜的禁书区。东区书库最后一排书架上的锁链穿过地板往下延伸，下面有什么东西把它们拉紧，又放松，像在呼吸。|又是最后一排书架上的锁链，拉紧，又放松。下面某处有一道楼梯。你没有它的地图。} {seen(carto_map_blanks) > 0: 制图社的地图上，正好这儿有一块空白。|城堡里一定有人有下面的地图。}\n# en: A student in a dark library aisle at midnight with an ear to the bottom shelf, iron chains running down through holes in the floor and pulled taut.\nscene [9a4e65] = 午夜昏暗的图书馆过道，一个学生把耳朵贴在最底层的书架上，铁链穿过地板上的孔洞，被拉得紧紧的。\n# en: Listen at the shelf\nopt.left.label [228e78] = 贴着书架听\n# en: {You lie on the cold floor with an ear to the boards. Far down, pages turn, a great many of them, all at once.|You listen until your ear is numb. Something down there is very large and very quiet, and it's a library.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dfdcec] = {你趴在冰冷的地板上，耳朵贴着木板。在很深的下面，有书页在翻，很多很多，同时在翻。|你一直听到耳朵发麻。下面有个东西，非常大，非常安静，而且是一座图书馆。}\n# en: Go back to bed\nopt.right.label [ef6924] = 回去睡觉\n# en: {You leave the chains to whatever is pulling them.|Not without a map. You go back up to bed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [172c47] = {你把锁链留给拉着它们的那个东西。|没有地图不行。你回楼上睡觉去了。}\n\n@thread heist_stacks\n# en: The Restricted Stacks\nname [700d36] = 禁书区书库\n# en: Below the last chained shelf, a stair goes down to the deep stacks and the confiscation vault. The Cartographers go at midnight.\npremise [ada2ab] = 最后一排锁链书架的下方，一道楼梯通往深层书库和没收库。制图社的人午夜去。\n# en: You went down the stair under the chained shelf at midnight.\njournal.1 [f35138] = 你在午夜走下了锁链书架下的楼梯。\n# en: Down in the stacks you read a book that charged you a drop for every page, and learned Torment from it.\njournal.2 [8d8b30] = 在书库深处，你读了一本每翻一页就要你一滴血的书，从中学会了折磨。\n# en: At the mirror desk your reflection read the book back to you, and you learned Reflect.\njournal.3 [c08f96] = 在镜面书桌前，你的倒影把书反读给你听，你学会了反照。\n# en: You picked the prefects' padlock on the confiscation vault.\njournal.4 [2173c4] = 你撬开了没收库门上级长的挂锁。\n\n@event st_dead_end\n# en: The Way Shut\ntitle [0989f3] = 路断了\n# en: {heist_depth >= 7: The goblin over the vault door is still muttering, and the padlock won't take another pick tonight. The only way left is up.|{~The shelves ahead have breathed in and not breathed out. The aisle is shut, and the only way that's still a way is back.|Ahead, the stacks have closed ranks, spine to spine, like a door. Whatever's further down is not for tonight.|A shelf has come down across the way, gently, as if it meant to. Behind it, the dark goes on without you.}}\ntext [920303] = {heist_depth >= 7: 没收库门上的地精还在嘟囔，今晚这把挂锁再也受不了一根撬锁针了。唯一剩下的路是往上。|{~前面的书架吸了一口气，就没再呼出来。过道合上了，唯一还算路的路是回头。|前面的书库肩并肩排成一列，书脊贴着书脊，像一扇门。再往下的东西，今晚不属于你。|一排书架轻轻倒下，横在路上，像是有意的。它后面，黑暗没有你也照样延续。}}\n# en: A narrow library aisle in near darkness, two tall bookcases leaned together overhead to close it like a door, a student's candle at the bottom.\nscene [b2dc72] = 近乎全黑的狭窄图书馆过道，两排高书架在头顶倾斜相抵，像一扇门一样把路封住，底下是一个学生的蜡烛。\n# en: Go back up with what you carry\nopt.left.label [b0f3b5] = 带着手里的东西回上去\n# en: {heist_depth >= 7: You leave the goblin to its muttering and climb, all forty-four steps, with what you have.|{~You turn round and go up by the way you know.|You take the hint, and the stair.|You go, and the shelves let out their breath behind you.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [90b637] = {heist_depth >= 7: 你让地精自己嘟囔去，带着手里的东西往上爬，四十四级全爬完。|{~你转身，沿着认得的路上去。|你领会了这个暗示，也领会了楼梯。|你走了，书架在你身后呼出了那口气。}}\n# en: Search where you stand\nopt.right.label [f7f345] = 就地搜一搜\n# en: {heist_depth >= 7: Among the crates stacked outside the vault door, one lid is loose. Something small and old comes with you.|{~The shelf that closed the aisle has a gap in it, and in the gap a small book nobody's missed. You take it up with you.|You run your hand along the closed shelf and find one spine that's loose. It comes with you.|Behind the fallen shelf, a sheaf of loose pages, very old. You roll them into your sleeve.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [2854f4] = {heist_depth >= 7: 没收库门外堆着的木箱里，有一个箱盖松了。一样又小又旧的东西跟你走了。|{~挡住过道的那排书架上有个缺口，缺口里有一本没人惦记的小书。你把它带了上去。|你的手沿着合上的书架摸过去，摸到一根松动的书脊。它跟你走了。|倒下的书架后面有一叠散页，非常旧。你把它们卷进了袖子。}}\n# en: {heist_depth >= 7: The goblin catches you at the crates and says so. You leave them be.|{~The shelf leans on you, very gently, until you stop.|You find nothing but dust, and a splinter.|The shelf breathes in, and you jump back, and leave it be.}}\nopt.right.say.2 [96fb4c] = {heist_depth >= 7: 你在木箱边被地精逮了个正着，它还大声说了出来。你不再碰它们。|{~书架非常轻地压在你身上，直到你停手。|你什么也没找到，只有灰尘和一根木刺。|书架吸了一口气，你往后一跳，不再碰它。}}\n\n@event st1_taut_chains\n# en: The Chains Pull Taut\ntitle [20c429] = 锁链拉紧了\n# en: {seen(st1_taut_chains) > 1: The chains know you now. They draw taut across the aisle at knee and throat as you come, slower than they used to, as if to see whether you've got careless.|{heist_depth == 1: At the foot of the stair the chains from the shelf above come down through the ceiling and run off between the shelves, and as you step off the last stair they draw taut across the aisle, one at the knee, one at the throat.|Round the next bookcase the chains from the shelf above run between the shelves, and as you come they draw taut across the aisle, one at the knee, one at the throat.} Something is pulling them, slowly, to see what you'll do.}\ntext [82226d] = {seen(st1_taut_chains) > 1: 锁链认得你了。你一来，它们就在膝盖和喉咙的高度横着过道绷紧，比以前慢一些，像是要看看你有没有变得大意。|{heist_depth == 1: 楼梯底下，上面书架的锁链穿过天花板垂下来，在书架之间延伸开去。你刚踏下最后一级台阶，它们就横着过道绷紧了，一根在膝盖高，一根在喉咙高。|绕过下一个书架，上面书架的锁链在书架之间延伸，你一走近，它们就横着过道绷紧了，一根在膝盖高，一根在喉咙高。}有什么东西在拉着它们，慢慢地，要看你怎么办。}\n# en: A narrow dark aisle between library shelves strung with iron chains, the chains drawn tight across the aisle at different heights, a student ducking between them.\nscene [49eadb] = 狭窄黑暗的过道里，书架之间拉着铁链，高低不一地横在过道上，一个学生正从链子之间钻过去。\n# en: Slip back the way you came\nopt.left.label [ebfef5] = 原路溜回去\n# en: {~You go back the way you came, and the chains go slack behind you, satisfied.|You decide the chains have made their point.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6e3dea] = {~你原路退回去，锁链在身后松了下来，心满意足。|你认定锁链已经把意思表达清楚了。}\n# en: Thread between them\nopt.right.label [fe387d] = 从链子之间钻过去\n# en: {~Under, over, sideways, holding your breath. On the far side a page has fallen from a chained book, gold-leafed. You take it.|You go through the chains like a cat through railings. One of them twangs, very softly, as you pass, and a loose illuminated page drifts down. You catch it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6dfc60] = {~从下面，从上面，侧着身，屏住呼吸。到了另一边，有一张从锁链书上掉下来的泥金书页。你拿走了。|你像猫钻栏杆一样穿过锁链。你经过时，其中一根非常轻地铮了一声，一张散落的泥金插图书页飘了下来。你接住了它。}\n# en: {~A chain catches your ankle and rings like a bell all the way up to the shelf.|You misjudge one and it takes you across the shoulder, hard, and every chain in the aisle shivers.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a8e0bf] = {~一根链子钩住了你的脚踝，像钟一样一路响到上面的书架。|你判断错了一根，它重重地抽在你肩上，过道里每一根链子都抖了起来。}\n\n@event st1_library_rounds\n# en: The Night Lamp\ntitle [3b4238] = 夜灯\n# en: {seen(st1_library_rounds) > 1: The night lamp again, along the gallery overhead, on the same slow round, looking everywhere.|A lamp comes along the gallery overhead: a prefect walking the library's night round, who has no reason to look down the stair and every reason to look everywhere.} {flag(forbidden_rounds): By Odile's green ink, this is the late round, and it turns back at the reading room.|The glow moves slowly. You have no idea where it goes next.}\ntext [932ad5] = {seen(st1_library_rounds) > 1: 夜灯又来了，沿着头顶的回廊，走着同样缓慢的巡逻路线，四处都看。|一盏灯沿着头顶的回廊过来了：一位级长在走图书馆的夜间巡逻，他没有理由往楼梯下看，却有一切理由四处看。}{flag(forbidden_rounds): 照奥迪尔的绿墨水笔记，这是晚班巡逻，到阅览室就折回。|光晕移动得很慢。你完全不知道它下一步去哪儿。}\n# en: A lamp's glow moving along the top of a library bookcase, seen from below in a dark aisle, a student pressed flat against the books.\nscene [e00291] = 从黑暗的过道往上看，一盏灯的光晕沿着书架顶部移动，一个学生紧紧贴在书上。\n# en: Back up the stair while you can\nopt.left.label [c71785] = 趁还来得及，退回楼梯上\n# en: {~You go back up behind the lamp, step for step, and out.|You let the round have the library tonight.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1ea635] = {~你跟在灯后面一步一步回到上面，出去了。|今晚就把图书馆让给巡逻吧。}\n# en: Stand still in the shelves\nopt.right.label [11c3eb] = 在书架间一动不动\n# en: {~The lamp passes over you, over the stair, over the dark, and goes on. You breathe out for what feels like a minute.|You stand so still among the books you could be one. The lamp moves on to the reading room.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e1bbff] = {~灯光从你身上掠过，从楼梯上掠过，从黑暗上掠过，继续往前去了。你长长地呼出一口气，好像呼了一分钟。|你在书中间站得那么静，简直成了其中一本。灯往阅览室去了。}\n# en: {~The lamp stops. \"Hello?\" says the prefect, to the dark. You run.|You shift your weight, and a board answers. The lamp swings down the stair.}\nopt.right.say.2 [53cc35] = {~灯停住了。“有人吗？”级长对着黑暗说。你跑了。|你挪了一下重心，一块地板回应了。灯转向了楼梯下面。}\n\n@event st1_catalogue\n# en: The Catalogue Below\ntitle [4bef84] = 下面的目录\n# en: {seen(st1_catalogue) > 1: The catalogue at the bend again. WHAT IS WHERE has a new card in it since last time, in fresher ink.|An old card catalogue stands at the bend of the aisle, for the books down here that the library upstairs pretends not to own. One drawer is labelled in a dead librarian's hand: WHAT IS WHERE, AND WHAT MUST NOT BE MOVED.}\ntext [c6b6f5] = {seen(st1_catalogue) > 1: 拐弯处的目录柜又在那儿。“什么在哪里”里比上次多了一张卡，墨迹更新。|过道拐弯处立着一个旧卡片目录柜，收录的是楼上图书馆假装没有的下面这些书。其中一个抽屉上，一位已故图书管理员的笔迹写着：什么在哪里，以及什么绝不能挪动。}\n# en: An old wooden card catalogue in a dark library basement, one long drawer pulled out, cards written in faded brown ink, a candle on top.\nscene [26e778] = 黑暗的图书馆地下室里，一个旧木卡片目录柜，一个长抽屉被拉开，卡片上是褪色的褐色墨水字，柜顶放着一支蜡烛。\n# en: Leave it, and slip away\nopt.left.label [98273a] = 别管它，溜走\n# en: {~You leave the drawer shut and the dead librarian's secrets with it.|You go, and take nothing, not even a look.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8f7f86] = {~你没打开抽屉，把那位已故图书管理员的秘密留在了里面。|你走了，什么也没拿，连看都没看一眼。}\n# en: Read the drawer\nopt.right.label [716e6f] = 读那个抽屉\n# en: {~WHAT IS WHERE turns out to be a map in cards. You pocket the three that matter and go on knowing where you're going.|You read fast by candle and pocket the card for the deep stacks. Half of it is a warning. You take the other half.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2e9932] = {~“什么在哪里”原来是一张用卡片拼成的地图。你把要紧的三张揣进口袋，知道自己要去哪儿了。|你借着烛光飞快地读，把通往深层书库的那张卡揣进口袋。一半是警告。你拿了另一半。}\n# en: {~The drawer jams, then comes out all at once, and four hundred cards go across the floor.|You read too long. The candle gutters, and in the dark you knock the whole catalogue a foot sideways, loudly.}\nopt.right.say.2 [aeca9c] = {~抽屉卡住了，然后一下子全滑了出来，四百张卡片撒了一地。|你读得太久了。蜡烛摇曳将熄，黑暗中你把整个目录柜撞得往旁边挪了一尺，动静很大。}\n\n@event st2_breathing\n# en: Between Breaths\ntitle [d5c4f4] = 两次呼吸之间\n# en: {seen(st2_breathing) > 1: The breathing shelves again. You know their rhythm now, mostly, and they seem to know yours: tonight they breathe a little faster.|Down here the shelves breathe, and they're close enough together that when they breathe in, the aisle closes. You'd have to go through on the out-breath, all the way, before the next in.}\ntext [ab2480] = {seen(st2_breathing) > 1: 又是会呼吸的书架。你如今大致摸清了它们的节奏，它们似乎也摸清了你的：今晚它们呼吸得快了一点。|到了这下面，书架在呼吸，而且挨得很近，它们一吸气，过道就合上了。你得趁它们呼气时一口气穿过去，赶在下一次吸气之前。}\n# en: Tall black bookcases in deep darkness, leaning very slightly inward and outward as if breathing, a narrow gap closing between them, a student waiting to slip through.\nscene [05b3f2] = 深沉的黑暗中，高大的黑色书架微微向里向外倾斜，像在呼吸，它们之间的一道窄缝正在合拢，一个学生等着钻过去。\n# en: Leave the breathing to them\nopt.left.label [a5dde8] = 让它们自己呼吸去\n# en: {~You go back the way you came, and the shelves breathe easier behind you.|You decide you don't want to be between them on an in-breath.}\nopt.left.say.1 [769695] = {~你原路返回，书架在你身后呼吸得轻松了些。|你认定自己不想在它们吸气时夹在中间。}\n# en: Go on the out-breath\nopt.right.label [e5f858] = 趁呼气时过去\n# en: {~You go on the out-breath, fast and flat, and the shelves sigh shut behind your heels. A book has fallen in the gap. It's yours now.|You time it, and go, and come out the far side with a black-bound book that fell into your arms on the way.}\nopt.right.say.1 [98feb8] = {~你趁着呼气，又快又平地冲过去，书架在你脚跟后叹息着合上了。缝里掉了一本书。现在它是你的了。|你算准时机，冲过去，到了另一边，怀里多了一本途中掉进你怀里的黑皮书。}\n# en: {~You're late by a breath. The shelves hold you, gently, all over, for a long minute, and let go.|The aisle closes on your satchel and keeps it a while. Its idea of a while.}\nopt.right.say.2 [8f8ded] = {~你晚了一口气。书架轻轻地、全方位地夹住了你，夹了长长的一分钟，才放开。|过道合上时夹住了你的书包，夹了好一阵。按它的标准，好一阵。}\n\n@event st2_charging_book\n# en: The Book That Charges\ntitle [de4687] = 收费的书\n# en: {seen(st2_charging_book) > 1: The book that charges is open on its lectern again, at the page where you stopped, and it hasn't forgotten what you owe.|On a lectern in the dark is a book with a duellist's hand on the cover, a forbidden manual from some war nobody teaches. When you turn its page, the corner pricks your finger and takes a drop. The next page will cost another. It charges its reader for everything it teaches.}\ntext [11c30c] = {seen(st2_charging_book) > 1: 那本收费的书又摊在读经台上，停在你上次停下的那一页，它没忘记你还欠它什么。|黑暗中的一个读经台上放着一本书，封面上是一只决斗者的手，是某场没人讲授的战争留下的禁用手册。你翻页时，页角刺破你的手指，取走一滴血。下一页还要再收一滴。它教你的一切，都要读者付钱。}\n# en: An open black book on a lectern in a dark library stack, a single drop of blood on the corner of its page, a student's pricked finger held above it.\nscene [71442a] = 黑暗书库里的读经台上摊着一本黑书，书页一角有一滴血，一个学生被刺破的手指悬在书上方。\n# en: Close it, and slip away\nopt.left.label [170c49] = 合上它，溜走\n# en: {~You close it. It lets you, this time. You suck your finger all the way back up the stair.|You leave the book on its lectern, owed nothing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9df04a] = {~你合上了它。这次它让你合上了。回楼梯的一路上你都在吮手指。|你把书留在读经台上，谁也不欠谁。}\n# en: Read it to the end, and pay\nopt.right.label [0e1528] = 读到最后，付钱\n# en: Torment, at a price.\nopt.right.hint [8355e9] = 折磨，要付出代价。\n# en: {~Page by page, drop by drop. By the end your fingertips are sore and you understand the trick of it: make every spell the other side casts cost them, and hurt. Torment. You feel the shape of it in your hands, and wish you didn't.|You pay to the last page. The book closes itself, satisfied. You know Torment now, and how it feels to be charged for every step.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8283ee] = {~一页一滴，一页一滴。读到最后你的指尖都疼了，你也明白了其中的诀窍：让对方施的每一个法术都要付出代价，而且痛。折磨。你在手里感觉到了它的形状，却希望自己没有。|你一直付到最后一页。书自己合上了，心满意足。现在你会折磨了，也知道了每走一步都被收费是什么滋味。}\n# en: {~You read too fast, and it charges too much. You come to on the floor with the book shut and the room very loud.|It takes more than a drop. You stumble back from the lectern into a shelf, which tells everyone.}\nopt.right.say.2 [184115] = {~你读得太快，它收得太多。你醒来时躺在地上，书合着，屋里非常吵。|它取走的不止一滴。你踉跄着从读经台退开，撞上了一排书架，这下谁都知道了。}\n\n@event st2_other_lamp\n# en: Someone Else's Lamp\ntitle [d48244] = 别人的灯\n# en: {seen(st2_other_lamp) > 1: Another lamp ahead, shaded low. Someone else is copying down here again, a different hood, the same hurry. They haven't seen you.|There's a lamp ahead, shaded low. Someone else is down here: a hooded student copying out of a black book, very fast, with a satchel of copied pages at their feet. They haven't seen you. They will in a minute.}\ntext [c35c7c] = {seen(st2_other_lamp) > 1: 前面又有一盏灯，光压得很低。又有人在下面抄东西，换了一顶兜帽，还是一样匆忙。他们还没看见你。|前面有一盏灯，光压得很低。还有人在下面：一个戴兜帽的学生正飞快地从一本黑书里抄东西，脚边放着一书包抄好的书页。他们还没看见你。再过一分钟就会看见了。}\n# en: A hooded student at a desk deep in dark library stacks, copying from a black book by a small shaded lamp, not yet aware of a second student watching from the aisle.\nscene [e4db58] = 深处黑暗的书库里，一个戴兜帽的学生在桌前借着一盏遮了光的小灯抄一本黑书，还没察觉过道里有另一个学生在看着。\n# en: Back away before they look up\nopt.left.label [eb7267] = 趁他们抬头前退开\n# en: {~You back away into the dark, and leave them to their copying and their luck.|You go. One thief in the stacks is plenty.}\nopt.left.say.1 [937834] = {~你退回黑暗里，把那人留给抄写和运气。|你走了。书库里有一个贼就够了。}\n# en: Slip past them\nopt.right.label [ff0971] = 从他们身边溜过去\n# en: {~You go past behind their back, close enough to read their page. They never look up. You never ask who.|You wait till they bend to the book, and go by like a draught.}\nopt.right.say.1 [68a159] = {~你从那人背后溜过去，近得能看清那一页。那人始终没抬头。你也始终没问是谁。|你等那人俯身看书时，像一阵穿堂风一样过去了。}\n# en: {~They look up, and you both freeze, and then you both run, in opposite directions, loudly.|They see you and blow out their lamp, and in the dark you go straight into a shelf.}\nopt.right.say.2 [c6df31] = {~那人抬起头，你们俩都僵住了，然后都跑了，方向相反，动静很大。|那人看见了你，吹灭了灯，黑暗中你一头撞上了书架。}\n# en: Take a sheaf of their pages\nopt.take.label [f424a9] = 拿走一叠他们的抄页\n# en: {~You lift a sheaf from the top of their satchel while they turn a page, and are three aisles away before they reach for it.|You take their copied pages, which is stealing from a thief, which still counts.}\nopt.take.say.1 [2530d6] = {~趁那人翻页，你从书包顶上抽走一叠，等那人伸手去拿时，你已经在三排书架开外了。|你拿走了那人抄好的书页，这是偷贼的东西，但照样算偷。}\n# en: {~They catch your wrist, and shout. Somewhere above, a lamp comes on.|The satchel's buckle rings on the floor, and they're up and shouting before you've got a page.}\nopt.take.say.2 [be2dcb] = {~那人抓住你的手腕，喊了起来。上面某处，一盏灯亮了。|书包的搭扣当啷一声掉在地上，你一页还没拿到，那人就已经站起来大喊了。}\n\n@event st3_mirror_desk\n# en: The Mirror Desk\ntitle [236cd8] = 镜面书桌\n# en: {seen(st3_mirror_desk) > 1: The mirror desk again. Your reflection is already there, a line further on than where you stopped.|Deep in the stacks there's a reading desk of black mirror-glass with a book open on it. When you lean over, your reflection is already reading, a line ahead of you, and its lips are moving. Whatever you read, it reads back.}\ntext [a06a00] = {seen(st3_mirror_desk) > 1: 又是那张镜面书桌。你的倒影已经在那儿了，比你上次停下的地方多读了一行。|书库深处有一张黑色镜面玻璃做的读书桌，上面摊着一本书。你俯身过去，你的倒影已经在读了，比你快一行，嘴唇还在动。不管你读什么，它都反读回来。}\n# en: A reading desk of black mirrored glass deep in a dark library, a book open on it, the student's reflection in the glass reading the book with its lips moving.\nscene [42b487] = 黑暗图书馆深处一张黑色镜面玻璃读书桌，上面摊着一本书，玻璃里学生的倒影正在读那本书，嘴唇在动。\n# en: Look away, and slip off\nopt.left.label [db4338] = 移开目光，溜走\n# en: {~You look away first. For a week your reflection in windows is a line ahead.|You leave the desk to its reader.}\nopt.left.say.1 [acf1c9] = {~你先移开了目光。之后一个星期，你在窗玻璃里的倒影都比你快一行。|你把书桌留给它的读者。}\n# en: Read along with it\nopt.right.label [de1d41] = 跟着它一起读\n# en: Reflect, if you can keep up.\nopt.right.hint [e5db6c] = 反照，如果你跟得上。\n# en: {~You read with your reflection until you're reading together, and then it reads you, and gives it all back. That's the trick: give it all back. Reflect.|By the last line you understand the desk. Everything put to it comes back, every word. You take the lesson up the stairs with you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6523a2] = {~你跟着倒影一起读，直到你们读在一处，然后它读起了你，又把一切都还给你。诀窍就在这儿：全部还回去。反照。|读到最后一行，你明白了这张书桌。递给它的一切都会回来，一字不差。你带着这一课走上了楼梯。}\n# en: {~Your reflection gets ahead, then a page ahead, then shuts the book on you. The glass rings like a struck glass.|You read too slowly, and the desk reads your face back at you, all of it, very loud.}\nopt.right.say.2 [16c61c] = {~你的倒影抢先了，然后抢先了一页，然后当着你的面把书合上了。玻璃像被敲响的玻璃杯一样嗡鸣。|你读得太慢，书桌把你的脸反读回给你，全部，非常响。}\n\n@event st3_no_lamp\n# en: Where No Lamp Reaches\ntitle [53bcd0] = 灯照不到的地方\n# en: {seen(st3_no_lamp) > 1: The dark at the bottom again, where your candle gives up. You know the first twenty spines by touch now; it's the rest that worry you.|Down here no lamp reaches, and your candle won't stay lit; it leans over and gives up. The last stretch to the vault is by touch: spines under your fingers, and the floor going down by inches.} ⚠ Lose your way here, and there's no near thing.\ntext [eb6e9b] = {seen(st3_no_lamp) > 1: 又到了最底下的黑暗，你的蜡烛在这里放弃。前二十根书脊你如今凭手感都认得了；让你担心的是剩下的。|到了这下面，灯照不到，你的蜡烛也点不住；它歪过去，放弃了。通往没收库的最后一段只能靠摸：手指下的书脊，一寸一寸往下的地面。}⚠ 在这里迷了路，就没有险些这回事了。\n# en: Pitch darkness in a vast library, a single hand on the spine of a book, the faintest outline of endless shelves.\nscene [cc7e51] = 巨大图书馆里一片漆黑，只有一只手搭在一本书的书脊上，无尽书架的轮廓若隐若现。\n# en: Turn back while you know the way\nopt.left.label [34f4ba] = 趁还认得路，回头\n# en: {~You turn round in the dark and walk back by the spines you know.|You decide that the dark here is someone's, and not yours.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ab4e71] = {~你在黑暗中转过身，顺着认得的书脊走回去。|你认定这里的黑暗属于别人，不属于你。}\n# en: Go on by touch\nopt.right.label [d375da] = 摸着往前走\n# en: {~Spine by spine, in a dark like deep water. Your hand closes on a small book lying loose on a shelf, and you take it, and keep going.|You count the shelves by touch, and at the end of the count, a door.}\nopt.right.say.1 [19fd2f] = {~在深水般的黑暗里一根书脊一根书脊地摸过去。你的手碰到书架上一本松放着的小书，你拿上它，继续走。|你凭手感数着书架，数到头，是一扇门。}\n# en: {~You lose the spines, then the floor, then the way. When the lantern finds you, you're sitting in the dark holding a book you can't see, nowhere near anywhere.|Something in the dark takes your hand and leads you, kindly, all the way to the prefects' door.}\nopt.right.say.2 [85499b] = {~你摸丢了书脊，接着是地面，接着是路。提灯找到你时，你正坐在黑暗里，抱着一本看不见的书，离哪儿都很远。|黑暗里有个东西握住你的手，好心地领着你，一路领到了级长的门口。}\n\n@event st3_glass_case\n# en: The Glass Case\ntitle [5a3f24] = 玻璃柜\n# en: {seen(st3_glass_case) > 1: {flag(heist_glass_robbed): The glass case again, a few books fewer, and the charm on its lock rewoven tighter than before. Someone has noticed.|The glass case again, every little book where it was, the charm humming as before.}|In an alcove stands a glass case of grimoires no bigger than your thumb, each with a clasp of gold, a hundred spells to a book. The lock has no keyhole, only a charm laid over it that hums when your breath fogs the glass. Any collector would sell their mother for one of them.}\ntext [3f9d80] = {seen(st3_glass_case) > 1: {flag(heist_glass_robbed): 又是那个玻璃柜，小书少了几本，锁上的咒语比以前织得更紧了。有人注意到了。|又是那个玻璃柜，每本小书都还在原处，咒语照旧嗡嗡作响。}|一个壁龛里立着一个玻璃柜，里面的魔法书都不比你的拇指大，每本都有金扣，一本里有一百个法术。锁没有钥匙孔，只覆着一道咒语，你的呼吸在玻璃上起雾时，它就嗡嗡作响。随便哪个收藏家都愿意为其中一本卖掉亲娘。}\n# en: A small glass display case in a dark library, full of tiny leather-bound books no bigger than a thumb, a faint shimmer of a charm over its lock.\nscene [516541] = 黑暗图书馆里一个小小的玻璃陈列柜，里面满是不比拇指大的皮面小书，锁上微微闪着一道咒语的光。\n# en: Leave them under glass\nopt.left.label [e1a19c] = 让它们待在玻璃里\n# en: {~You leave the little books to their glass and go back up the way you came.|You fog the glass once more, for the pleasure of it, and go.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1c17bd] = {~你把小书留在玻璃里，原路回上去。|你又在玻璃上呵了一次雾，图个乐子，然后走了。}\n# en: Unpick the charm\nopt.right.label [723673] = 拆开那道咒语\n# en: {~The charm comes apart under your fingers like wet paper. You take two of the little books, and the case closes itself behind you as if offended.|You unpick the charm thread by thread and lift out the smallest book. Then, because you're here, a second.}\nopt.right.say.1 [18f1b7] = {~咒语在你指下像湿纸一样散开。你拿了两本小书，柜子在你身后自己合上了，像是被冒犯了。|你一根丝一根丝地拆开咒语，取出最小的那本书。然后，既然来都来了，又拿了一本。}\n# en: {~The charm bites back, a white flash, and the glass rings like a struck bell all through the stacks.|You pull the wrong thread, and the whole charm goes off in your face with a noise like a slammed book.}\nopt.right.say.2 [62016a] = {~咒语反咬了一口，一道白光，玻璃像被敲响的钟一样响彻整个书库。|你拉错了一根丝，整道咒语在你脸前炸开，声音像合上一本书。}\n\n@event st_vault\n# en: The Confiscation Vault\ntitle [404008] = 没收库\n# en: At the bottom of the deep stacks, a door with a prefects' padlock and crates outside labelled for the confiscation vault. Behind it, three hundred years of everything the faculty ever took off a student. {flag(vault_catalogued): You catalogued half of it yourself, in detention. You know which shelf is which.|You've no idea what's where.} {flag(elixir_confiscated): Somewhere in there is the bottle the prefects took from your trunk.|}\ntext [bd1ad3] = 深层书库的最底下，一扇挂着级长挂锁的门，门外堆着标明送往没收库的木箱。门后是三百年来教职员从学生身上收走的一切。{flag(vault_catalogued): 其中一半是你关禁闭时亲手登记的。你知道哪个架子放着什么。|你完全不知道什么在哪儿。} {flag(elixir_confiscated): 级长从你箱子里拿走的那只瓶子，就在里面某处。|}\n# en: An iron-banded oak door in a dark library basement, a heavy prefects' padlock on it, crates labelled for confiscation stacked outside, a student kneeling at the lock with picks.\nscene [3bf709] = 黑暗图书馆地下室里一扇包铁的橡木门，门上挂着沉重的级长挂锁，门外堆着标明没收的木箱，一个学生拿着撬锁针跪在锁前。\n# en: Leave it locked, and go\nopt.leave.label [676cf0] = 让它锁着，走吧\n# en: {~You leave the vault to its padlock. Getting here was the heist.|You touch the padlock once, for luck, and go back up.}\nopt.leave.say.1 [36e663] = {~你把没收库留给它的挂锁。能走到这儿，就算偷成了。|你为了运气摸了一下挂锁，然后回上去了。}\n# en: Pick the lock for the crates\nopt.crates.label [0733ae] = 撬锁去拿箱子里的东西\n# en: {~The padlock gives with a click like a knuckle. Inside, a century of contraband on shelves: charmed dice, love letters, a stuffed owl that blinks. You fill your shirt with the small valuable things and lock it again behind you.|You go straight to the shelf you catalogued in detention and take the three best things on it. The padlock clicks shut after you as if nothing happened.}\nopt.crates.say.1 [e8e5d3] = {~挂锁咔哒一声开了，像掰响指节。里面是一个世纪又一个世纪的违禁品摆在架子上：施了咒的骰子，情书，一只会眨眼的猫头鹰标本。你把值钱的小东西塞满衬衫，出来时又把门锁上了。|你直奔关禁闭时登记过的那个架子，拿走上面最好的三样东西。挂锁在你身后咔哒锁上，像什么也没发生过。}\n# en: {~The pick snaps in the lock, with a sound like a shot down here.|The vault's alarm, a very loud goblin, wakes up and says so.}\nopt.crates.say.2 [864544] = {~撬锁针断在锁里，在这下面响得像一声枪响。|没收库的警报器，一只嗓门非常大的地精，醒过来了，还大声说了出来。}\n# en: Pick the lock for your own bottle back\nopt.bottle.label [89f81b] = 撬锁拿回你自己的瓶子\n# en: {~The padlock gives. Your things are on a shelf with a label in a prefect's neat hand, your name spelled right. You take them back, and one small thing more for the trouble.|Inside, a shelf of confiscated elixirs, gold and black, each labelled with a name. You find what's yours, and leave the rest to their owners' consciences.}\nopt.bottle.say.1 [46f990] = {~挂锁开了。你的东西在一个架子上，标签是某位级长工整的笔迹，你的名字拼得一字不差。你把它们拿了回来，又顺手拿了一件小东西，算是辛苦费。|里面有一架子没收来的药水，金的黑的，每瓶都标着名字。你找到了属于你的，其余的留给它们主人的良心。}\n# en: {~The pick snaps in the lock, with a sound like a shot down here.|The vault's alarm, a very loud goblin, wakes up and says so.}\nopt.bottle.say.2 [864544] = {~撬锁针断在锁里，在这下面响得像一声枪响。|没收库的警报器，一只嗓门非常大的地精，醒过来了，还大声说了出来。}\n# en: Fetch Corvin's grandfather's deck\nopt.deck.label [9c8ad9] = 拿回科尔文祖父的那副牌\n# en: {~The deck is in a tin on the third shelf, labelled in faded ink with a date and a very rude word. You give it to Corvin at breakfast. He doesn't say anything for a whole minute, which is a record.|You find the deck, and the faculty's note on it: \"Marked. Obviously.\" Corvin, when you give it to him, laughs until he has to sit down, and then holds it very carefully.}\nopt.deck.say.1 [0e4568] = {~那副牌在第三层架子上的一个铁盒里，褪色的墨水标签上写着日期和一个非常粗鲁的词。早饭时你把它交给科尔文。他整整一分钟什么也没说，这是破纪录的。|你找到了那副牌，还有教职员附在上面的字条：“做过记号。显然。”你把它交给科尔文时，他笑得不得不坐下，然后小心翼翼地捧着它。}\n# en: {~The pick snaps in the lock, with a sound like a shot down here.|The vault's alarm, a very loud goblin, wakes up and says so.}\nopt.deck.say.2 [864544] = {~撬锁针断在锁里，在这下面响得像一声枪响。|没收库的警报器，一只嗓门非常大的地精，醒过来了，还大声说了出来。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/heists/tower.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/heists/tower.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event heist_tower_enter\n# en: The Stair in the Wall\ntitle [c613e5] = 墙里的楼梯\n# en: {heist_tower_highest == 0: Midnight in the seventh tower's cellar. Behind the rack of empty bottles, your stair curls up inside the wall, one shoulder wide. Above the landing of candles are the old classrooms, shut for a century, with everything their teachers left behind.|{~The bottle rack, the hollow stone, the stair in the wall.|The cellar at midnight, and the cold coming down the wall stair.|Your stair, curling up into the dark inside the stone.}} {flag(carto_tower_chimney): The chimney's soot-black mouth gapes beside it.|}\ntext [c4535a] = {heist_tower_highest == 0: 午夜，第七塔的地窖。空酒瓶架后面，你的楼梯在墙里盘旋而上，只容一肩。蜡烛平台上面是旧教室，关了一百年，里面是老师们留下的一切。|{~酒瓶架，空心的石头，墙里的楼梯。|午夜的地窖，冷气顺着墙中楼梯往下流。|你的楼梯，盘旋着钻进石头里的黑暗。}} {flag(carto_tower_chimney): 旁边，烟囱乌黑的口子张着。|}\n# en: A dark vaulted cellar at midnight, a rack of empty bottles swung aside from a curved stone wall, a narrow stair curling up inside it, a student with a shaded candle.\nscene [5885b1] = 午夜昏暗的拱顶地窖里，一个空酒瓶架从弧形石墙边挪开，一道窄楼梯在墙里盘旋而上，一个学生举着遮了光的蜡烛。\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.left.label [4bf72b] = 今晚不去\n# en: {~You put the rack back and go to bed with the stair in your head.|Not tonight. The tower can keep its classrooms a while longer.|You stand at the foot of the stair and listen to the tower settle, and go no further.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ad84df] = {~你把酒瓶架挪回去，带着脑子里的楼梯去睡觉。|今晚不去。塔可以把它的教室再留一阵子。|你站在楼梯脚下，听着塔慢慢安静下来，没再往上走。}\n# en: Go up\nopt.right.label [4c7014] = 上去\n# en: A heist: press on card by card, slip away with what you carry any time.\nopt.right.hint [f5be0b] = 一次偷盗：一张牌一张牌地往前，随时可以带着手里的东西溜走。\n# en: {flag(carto_tower_chimney): You go up the chimney, back to one side and feet to the other, and come out in a classroom grate, sooty and grinning.|{~You turn sideways and go up into the wall.|You shade your candle and climb, counting the turns.|You go up quietly, one shoulder to the stone, the tower breathing round you.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [14772e] = {flag(carto_tower_chimney): 你背抵一边、脚撑一边爬上烟囱，从一间教室的炉栅里钻出来，满身煤灰，咧着嘴笑。|{~你侧过身，钻进墙里往上走。|你遮住烛光往上爬，数着拐了几个弯。|你悄悄往上走，一侧肩膀贴着石头，塔在你四周呼吸。}}\n\n@event heist_tower_unmapped\n# en: The Tower at Midnight\ntitle [421c96] = 午夜的塔\n# en: {~Midnight at the foot of the seventh tower. The stair is locked, as it has been for a century, and the arrow-slits above are full of moon.|The seventh tower at midnight, locked, and very quiet. Somewhere up in it, the wind finds a slit and plays one note.} {member(cartographers): The Cartographers swear the tower is fatter outside than in.|Somebody in the castle must know another way up.}\ntext [f47c20] = {~午夜，第七塔脚下。楼梯锁着，一百年来一直锁着，上面的箭孔里满是月光。|午夜的第七塔，锁着，非常安静。塔上某处，风找到一个箭孔，吹出一个音。} {member(cartographers): 制图社的人发誓，这座塔外面比里面胖。|城堡里一定有人知道另一条上去的路。}\n# en: The foot of a dark tower stair at midnight, the door locked, a student with a candle looking up at arrow-slits full of moonlight.\nscene [1aa783] = 午夜，黑暗的塔楼楼梯脚下，门锁着，一个学生举着蜡烛，仰望着洒满月光的箭孔。\n# en: Listen to the tower\nopt.left.label [c23269] = 听这座塔\n# en: {~You sit on the cold bottom step and listen. The tower creaks and settles, floor by floor, and then on one floor it stops, as if something up there is listening back.|You listen until the candle gutters. High up, a door that nobody opens bangs once in the wind.}\nopt.left.say.1 [06fcc0] = {~你坐在冰冷的最底一级台阶上听着。塔一层一层地吱嘎作响、慢慢安顿，然后在某一层停住了，好像上面有什么东西也在听你。|你一直听到蜡烛快灭了。高处，一扇从没人开过的门被风吹得砰地响了一下。}\n# en: Go back to bed\nopt.right.label [ef6924] = 回去睡觉\n# en: {~You leave the tower to its century.|Not without a way up. You go back to bed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2a15b2] = {~你把这座塔留给它的一百年。|没有上去的路不行。你回去睡觉了。}\n\n@thread heist_tower\n# en: The Seventh Tower by Night\nname [4bf97b] = 夜里的第七塔\n# en: A servants' stair climbs inside the seventh tower's wall to the old classrooms, shut for a century. The Cartographers go at midnight.\npremise [79a26f] = 一道仆人楼梯在第七塔的墙里往上，通到关了一百年的旧教室。制图社的人午夜去。\n# en: You climbed the stair inside the seventh tower's wall at midnight.\njournal.1 [3c1340] = 你在午夜爬上了第七塔墙里的楼梯。\n# en: In the old duelling master's study you read how to take another's magic, and learned Extraction.\njournal.2 [9ffc0f] = 在老决斗师的书房里，你读到了怎样夺取别人的魔力，学会了汲取。\n# en: You reached the old duelling master's study, at the top of the classrooms.\njournal.3 [179c86] = 你到了老决斗师的书房，就在那些教室的最上面。\n\n@event tw_dead_end\n# en: A Door That Won't\ntitle [28bdda] = 打不开的门\n# en: {heist_depth >= 7: The study's lock won't take another try tonight, and every board on the landing has heard you. The only way left is down.|{~The door to the next floor has swollen shut in a century of damp, and it isn't going to open quietly tonight.|The stair turns, and turns, and comes back to the same landing. The tower has decided you've climbed enough.|A whole flight of the old stair has rotted away above you. The rest of the tower is out of reach tonight.}}\ntext [d3637b] = {heist_depth >= 7: 书房的锁今晚受不了再试一次了，平台上的每一块木板都听见了你。唯一剩下的路是往下。|{~通往上一层的门在一百年的潮气里胀死在门框里，今晚它不会安安静静地开。|楼梯转啊转，又回到同一个平台。塔已经认定你爬得够高了。|你头顶一整段旧楼梯都烂掉了。今晚塔的其余部分够不着了。}}\n# en: A narrow tower landing in darkness, an old classroom door swollen shut in its frame, a student's candle at its foot.\nscene [4048a0] = 黑暗中狭窄的塔楼平台，一扇旧教室的门胀死在门框里，门脚下是一个学生的蜡烛。\n# en: Go back down with what you carry\nopt.left.label [324650] = 带着手里的东西下去\n# en: {heist_depth >= 7: You leave the master's door to its century and go down, all the way, with what you have.|{~You go back down by the way you know.|You take the hint, and the stair.|You go down, and the tower settles behind you.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [1f2d1e] = {heist_depth >= 7: 你把决斗师的门留给它的一百年，带着手里的东西一路下去了。|{~你沿着认得的路下去。|你领会了这个暗示，也领会了楼梯。|你下去了，塔在你身后安顿下来。}}\n# en: Search where you stand\nopt.right.label [f7f345] = 就地搜一搜\n# en: {heist_depth >= 7: Behind the stacked desks by the study door, a drawer has come loose. Something small and old comes with you.|{~Under a loose board on the landing, a tin of old marbles and a note that just says \"Sorry about the frog\". You take the tin.|In a niche in the wall, a brass bell-pull handle, very fine. It comes down with you.|On the window ledge, a pair of spectacles with gold rims, left a century ago. You take them.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [a3d5ab] = {heist_depth >= 7: 书房门边摞着的课桌里，有一个抽屉松了。一样又小又旧的东西跟你走了。|{~平台上一块松动的木板底下，有一铁盒旧弹珠和一张纸条，上面只写着“青蛙的事，对不起”。你拿了那个铁盒。|墙上一个壁龛里，有一个黄铜拉铃把手，非常精致。它跟你下来了。|窗台上，一副金边眼镜，一百年前落下的。你拿走了。}}\n# en: {heist_depth >= 7: The desks shift as you reach in, and the whole heap groans. You leave them be.|{~You find a splinter, and dust, and nothing.|A board gives under your knee, and you leave the landing alone.|The ledge is crumbling, and so, briefly, are you.}}\nopt.right.say.2 [0dbb42] = {heist_depth >= 7: 你一伸手，课桌就挪了，整堆都在呻吟。你不再碰它们。|{~你找到一根木刺，一些灰尘，别的什么也没有。|一块木板在你膝盖下塌了，你不再碰这个平台。|窗台在碎，有那么一会儿，你也是。}}\n\n@event tw1_one_shoulder\n# en: One Shoulder Wide\ntitle [99f680] = 只容一肩\n# en: {seen(tw1_one_shoulder) > 1: The pinch in the wall stair again. You know exactly how far to breathe out now, and the stone still takes a button for its trouble.|Halfway up the wall stair narrows from one shoulder to less. The servants who built it must have been very thin, or very determined. Somewhere above the pinch, a draught; somewhere below it, everything you've climbed.}\ntext [28eaf3] = {seen(tw1_one_shoulder) > 1: 又是墙中楼梯的那道窄处。你现在清楚该呼出多少气了，石头照样要收走一颗纽扣当过路费。|爬到一半，墙里的楼梯从只容一肩变得更窄。造它的仆人们一定很瘦，或者很执着。窄处上方有一阵穿堂风；下方是你爬过的一切。}\n# en: A student squeezing sideways up an impossibly narrow spiral stair inside a stone wall, candle held up, cobwebs in their hair.\nscene [92509a] = 一个学生侧着身子挤上石墙里一道窄得不可思议的螺旋楼梯，举着蜡烛，头发上沾着蛛网。\n# en: Back down while you can turn round\nopt.left.label [bd5f61] = 趁还转得过身，退下去\n# en: {~You back down into the cellar and the stair lets you go.|You decide the pinch is the tower's way of saying no.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9fa44d] = {~你退回地窖，楼梯放你走了。|你认定这道窄处是塔在说不。}\n# en: Breathe out and squeeze\nopt.right.label [c65b53] = 呼出一口气，挤过去\n# en: {~You breathe out, turn sideways and go up the pinch an inch at a time. On the far side, the stair widens like a sigh.|You squeeze through with your candle held over your head and a button left behind on the stone.}\nopt.right.say.1 [46aca9] = {~你呼出一口气，侧过身，一寸一寸地挤过窄处。到了另一边，楼梯像松了一口气似的宽了。|你把蜡烛举过头顶挤了过去，在石头上留下了一颗纽扣。}\n# en: {~You stick, halfway, for a very long minute, and come loose with a scrape that the whole tower hears.|Your candle catches the cobwebs, and for a moment the stair is full of light and smoke and you.}\nopt.right.say.2 [2960c1] = {~你卡在了半中间，卡了长长的一分钟，脱身时刮出的声音整座塔都听见了。|你的蜡烛点着了蛛网，有那么一瞬间，楼梯里满是光、烟，还有你。}\n\n@event tw1_someone_else\n# en: Someone Else's Climb\ntitle [26225b] = 别人也在爬\n# en: {seen(tw1_someone_else) > 1: A light coming down the wall stair again, and footsteps. You're not the only one who uses it, and never were.|A light is coming down the wall stair towards you, and footsteps. Someone else knows this stair.} The only place to go is a niche in the stone, barely deep enough for a candle, let alone you.\ntext [7c1539] = {seen(tw1_someone_else) > 1: 又有一盏灯顺着墙里的楼梯下来，还有脚步声。用这道楼梯的不止你一个，从来都不止。|一盏灯顺着墙里的楼梯朝你下来，还有脚步声。还有别人知道这道楼梯。}唯一能躲的地方是石头上的一个壁龛，放一支蜡烛都勉强，更别说你了。\n# en: A narrow stone stair in darkness, a lantern's glow coming down it from above, a student pressed into a tiny niche in the wall.\nscene [e4f0e0] = 黑暗中狭窄的石楼梯，一盏提灯的光从上面照下来，一个学生紧紧缩在墙上一个小壁龛里。\n# en: Go back down before they reach you\nopt.left.label [c6843d] = 趁他们还没下来，退回去\n# en: {~You go back down faster than you came up, and out through the cellar, and never see who it was.|You retreat. The light stops at the top of the pinch, as if listening, and goes back up.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b81c77] = {~你下得比上来时还快，从地窖出去了，始终没看见是谁。|你退了下去。那盏灯在窄处上方停住，像在听，然后又回上去了。}\n# en: Press into the niche\nopt.right.label [21605c] = 缩进壁龛\n# en: {~They pass so close their sleeve brushes yours: a student in a house scarf, out of bounds with a lantern and a sack, who never looks sideways. On the step where they paused, a dropped coin. You take it and climb on.|A student with Cartographer chalk on their sleeve goes down past you, humming. On the step above, they've dropped a silver thimble. It's yours now.}\nopt.right.say.1 [86ffc5] = {~那人经过时袖子擦到了你的袖子：一个围着学院围巾的学生，越界出来，提着灯和一只麻袋，始终没往旁边看。在那人停过的台阶上，有一枚掉落的硬币。你拿上它，接着往上爬。|一个袖子上沾着制图社粉笔灰的学生哼着歌从你身边下去了。上一级台阶上，那人掉了一枚银顶针。现在它是你的了。}\n# en: {~The lantern swings round into your face, and you bolt before the prefect behind it can find your name.|You sneeze. It's the dust. The lantern stops.}\nopt.right.say.2 [31fa92] = {~提灯一转，照在你脸上，你趁提灯后面的级长还没想起你的名字，拔腿就跑。|你打了个喷嚏。是灰。提灯停住了。}\n\n@event tw1_spyhole\n# en: The Spyhole\ntitle [26c948] = 窥孔\n# en: {seen(tw1_spyhole) > 1: The spyhole again, and through it the round's lantern on the main stair, on time to the minute.|The wall stair has a spyhole onto the tower's main stair, so the servants could see who was coming. Through it, a lantern: a prefect on the tower's round, slow, thorough, and heading up the way you're going.}\ntext [8f5fe2] = {seen(tw1_spyhole) > 1: 又是那个窥孔，透过它，巡逻的提灯在主楼梯上，分秒不差。|墙里的楼梯上有个窥孔，正对着塔的主楼梯，好让仆人们看见谁来了。透过它，一盏提灯：一位级长在巡塔，慢，仔细，正朝你要去的方向往上走。}\n# en: A tiny spyhole in a stone wall, a student's eye at it, beyond it a broad tower staircase with a prefect's lantern moving down it.\nscene [51bbb2] = 石墙上一个小小的窥孔，一个学生把眼睛贴在上面，外面是宽阔的塔楼主楼梯，一位级长的提灯正往下移动。\n# en: Wait for the round to pass, then go down\nopt.left.label [0999df] = 等巡逻过去，然后下去\n# en: {~You watch the prefect all the way up and all the way down, and then you go back to bed.|You take the round as the tower's answer, and go.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c8ac03] = {~你看着级长一路上去、一路下来，然后回去睡觉。|你把这次巡逻当作塔的回答，走了。}\n# en: Time your climb to the round\nopt.right.label [767d30] = 跟着巡逻的节奏往上爬\n# en: {~You count the prefect's steps through the spyhole, and climb when they climb, so their footsteps cover yours.|You learn the round in one pass, and go up behind it, a floor behind, like a shadow it hasn't noticed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c7fe5b] = {~你透过窥孔数着级长的步子，他迈步时你也迈步，让他的脚步声盖住你的。|你看一遍就摸清了巡逻的路线，跟在后面往上走，隔着一层楼，像一个它没察觉的影子。}\n# en: {~You climb a beat too late. Your step lands in a silence, and the prefect's lantern stops.|You lose count, and come out on the landing face to face with the round.}\nopt.right.say.2 [2a565f] = {~你晚了一拍。你的脚步落在一片寂静里，级长的提灯停住了。|你数乱了，一出平台就和巡逻撞了个正着。}\n\n@event tw2_exam_cupboard\n# en: The Examiner's Cupboard\ntitle [c8fdd7] = 考官的柜子\n# en: {seen(tw2_exam_cupboard) > 1: {flag(heist_papers_taken): The examiner's cupboard again, its lock scratched now, a bundle or two lighter than it was.|The examiner's cupboard again, locked as ever, the ribbons still tied.}|The first classroom off the landing still has its examiner's cupboard, locked, and through the crack in the doors, bundles of exam papers tied with faded ribbon, a century of them, answers and all. Collectors pay for old papers. Historians pay more.}\ntext [07642f] = {seen(tw2_exam_cupboard) > 1: {flag(heist_papers_taken): 又是考官的柜子，锁上如今有了划痕，考卷少了一两捆。|又是考官的柜子，照旧锁着，缎带照旧系着。}|平台边第一间教室里，考官的柜子还在，锁着，从柜门缝里能看到一捆捆用褪色缎带扎着的考卷，一百年的量，连答案都在。收藏家会为旧考卷付钱。历史学家付得更多。}\n# en: A dusty old classroom at night, a tall locked cupboard with its doors open a crack, bundles of yellowed exam papers tied with ribbon inside.\nscene [8baa9f] = 夜里一间落满灰的旧教室，一个高高的上锁柜子开着一条缝，里面是用缎带扎着的发黄考卷。\n# en: Leave the papers, and go\nopt.left.label [22a7fb] = 别管考卷，走吧\n# en: {~You leave the past's exams to the past.|You go back down the wall stair with clean hands and nothing in them.}\nopt.left.say.1 [828ad0] = {~你把过去的考试留给过去。|你空着干净的手，顺着墙里的楼梯下去了。}\n# en: Pick the cupboard's lock\nopt.right.label [546d5e] = 撬开柜子的锁\n# en: {~The old lock gives in a puff of dust. You take the two bundles with the best seals, a century of sweat in faded ink.|Inside, the papers, and on top, a marked script with a teacher's note: \"See me.\" You take that one, and two bundles more.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2411b3] = {~老锁在一团灰尘里开了。你拿走封印最好的两捆，一百年的汗水，墨迹都褪了。|里面是考卷，最上面是一份批改过的答卷，老师写着：“来见我。”你拿了那一份，又拿了两捆。}\n# en: {~The lock gives, and so does the cupboard, slowly, forward, onto the floor.|The pick snaps, and the cupboard door swings out and bangs like a shot.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1e834a] = {~锁开了，柜子也跟着慢慢往前倒，倒在了地上。|撬锁针断了，柜门甩出来，砰的一声像枪响。}\n\n@event tw2_telling_floor\n# en: The Telling Floor\ntitle [714647] = 会告密的地板\n# en: {seen(tw2_telling_floor) > 1: The telling floor again. You know half the chalked crosses by heart now, and the other half by bruises.|The old classrooms are a run of rooms, one into the next, and their floorboards have had a century to learn to talk. Every one creaks a different note. Someone, a hundred years ago, chalked a few of them with a cross.}\ntext [107f18] = {seen(tw2_telling_floor) > 1: 又是那块会告密的地板。画叉的木板你如今记住了一半，另一半靠的是淤青。|旧教室是一间连着一间的，地板有一百年的时间学说话。每一块吱嘎的音都不一样。一百年前，有人用粉笔在其中几块上画了叉。}\n# en: A long dark classroom with old desks in rows, moonlight in stripes across worn floorboards, a student stepping carefully from board to board.\nscene [86b688] = 一间长长的昏暗教室，旧课桌一排排摆着，月光一道道落在磨旧的地板上，一个学生小心地一块一块踩过去。\n# en: Go back the way you know\nopt.left.label [091e28] = 沿认得的路回去\n# en: {~You go back over the boards you've already crossed, and down.|You leave the floor to its talking.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cf232e] = {~你踩着已经走过的木板回去，下楼了。|你让地板自己说话去。}\n# en: Cross on the chalked boards\nopt.right.label [768b56] = 踩着画叉的木板过去\n# en: {~You go from cross to cross like stepping stones, and the floor keeps its mouth shut all the way across.|You cross in a silence so careful the floor seems to hold its breath with you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8f6a86] = {~你一个叉一个叉地踩过去，像踩着垫脚石，地板一路都闭着嘴。|你走得那么小心，那么安静，地板好像也跟你一起屏住了呼吸。}\n# en: {~One cross was a joke. The board under it shrieks like a gull.|You miss a chalk mark by an inch, and the floor tells the whole tower.}\nopt.right.say.2 [5b122e] = {~有一个叉是开玩笑的。它底下的木板像海鸥一样尖叫。|你差了一寸没踩上粉笔记号，地板告诉了整座塔。}\n\n@event tw2_class_register\n# en: The Last Register\ntitle [75a4f5] = 最后的点名册\n# en: {seen(tw2_class_register) > 1: The register still lies open at its last day, everyone present, its silver clasp catching the moon.|On the teacher's desk, a class register lies open at its last day, a century ago. Every name is ticked present. After the last name, in the same hand: \"Tower closed. Lessons moved.\" Nothing about why. The register is bound in good leather, with a silver clasp.}\ntext [973cb1] = {seen(tw2_class_register) > 1: 点名册还摊在它的最后一天，人人到勤，银扣映着月光。|讲台上，一本点名册摊在它的最后一天，一百年前。每个名字都打了到勤的勾。最后一个名字后面，同一个笔迹写着：“塔楼关闭。课程迁走。”没说为什么。点名册是上好的皮面，带一个银扣。}\n# en: A teacher's desk in an abandoned classroom, an old leather register open on it, the last page of names with a date a century old, moonlight on it.\nscene [66e119] = 废弃教室里的讲台，上面摊着一本旧皮面点名册，最后一页的名字上写着一百年前的日期，月光照在上面。\n# en: Leave it open at its last day\nopt.left.label [6e9e58] = 让它摊在最后一天\n# en: {~You leave the register as it was, everyone present.|You don't take it. It seems rude to the class.}\nopt.left.say.1 [143b88] = {~你让点名册保持原样，人人到勤。|你没拿它。对这个班来说，拿走好像很失礼。}\n# en: Take the register\nopt.right.label [030806] = 拿走点名册\n# en: {~You slip it into your shirt, silver clasp cold against your ribs, and go on as quietly as a class that has been told to.|You take it. Somewhere a door bangs in the wind, and nobody comes.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6c3c26] = {~你把它塞进衬衫，银扣凉凉地贴着肋骨，然后像一个被叮嘱过要安静的班级那样安静地往前走。|你拿走了它。某处一扇门被风吹得砰地一响，没有人来。}\n# en: {~The silver clasp snaps shut on your finger with a click that carries.|As you lift it, the whole desk shifts on the boards, and the tower answers.}\nopt.right.say.2 [3104c5] = {~银扣咔哒一声夹住了你的手指，声音传得很远。|你一拿起它，整张讲台在地板上挪了一下，塔回应了。}\n\n@event tw3_no_wind\n# en: When the Wind Stops\ntitle [ec09e9] = 风停的时候\n# en: {seen(tw3_no_wind) > 1: The corridor of slits again, and again the wind drops as you reach it, as if it knows your step.|Up here the arrow-slits have been playing the wind all night, covering your steps. Now, between one note and the next, the wind stops, and the tower is so quiet you can hear your candle burn.} ⚠ One wrong sound here, and there's no near thing.\ntext [5567f4] = {seen(tw3_no_wind) > 1: 又是那条满是箭孔的走廊，你一走到，风又停了，好像它认得你的脚步。|到了上面，箭孔整夜都在吹奏风声，盖住你的脚步。此刻，在两个音之间，风停了，塔里静得你能听见蜡烛在燃烧。}⚠ 在这里出一点错的声音，就没有险些这回事了。\n# en: A dark tower corridor lined with arrow-slits, moonlight falling through them in bars, utterly still air, a student frozen mid-step.\nscene [5ce270] = 黑暗的塔楼走廊两侧排着箭孔，月光一道道落进来，空气完全静止，一个学生停在半步之中。\n# en: Wait for the wind, then go back down\nopt.left.label [931e08] = 等风回来，然后下去\n# en: {~You wait, not breathing, until the wind comes back, and go down under its cover.|You stand like a statue until the slits start singing again, and take the hint.}\nopt.left.say.1 [710dc9] = {~你一动不动地等着，屏住呼吸，直到风回来，借着它的掩护下去了。|你像雕像一样站着，直到箭孔重新唱起来，然后领会了暗示。}\n# en: Cross in the silence\nopt.right.label [4f78a6] = 在寂静中穿过去\n# en: {~You cross the whole corridor in the silence, step by soundless step. At the end, on a sill, a silver pencil someone left in a hurry. You take it.|You go through the quiet like a cat through a sleeping house, and find on the far side a dropped purse, very old.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cf2639] = {~你在寂静中一步一步无声地走完了整条走廊。尽头的窗台上，有一支别人匆忙间落下的银铅笔。你拿走了。|你像猫穿过一座熟睡的房子一样穿过寂静，在另一头捡到一个掉落的钱包，非常旧。}\n# en: {~Your shoe squeaks. In that silence, it's a shout.|You drop the candle. It rolls, very loudly, all the way down the corridor and the stair, to a prefect's feet.}\nopt.right.say.2 [03922a] = {~你的鞋吱了一声。在那样的寂静里，那就是一声大喊。|你的蜡烛掉了。它滚着，非常响，一路滚下走廊，滚下楼梯，滚到一位级长脚边。}\n\n@event tw3_orrery\n# en: The Orrery\ntitle [e04159] = 太阳系仪\n# en: {seen(tw3_orrery) > 1: {flag(heist_pearl_taken): The orrery is still turning, one planet short where you took the pearl.|The orrery is still turning, the pearl still riding its little arm.}|The astronomy classroom's brass orrery is still turning, a century after anyone wound it, its planets on long arms going round and round in the dark. The smallest planet is a pearl. It would come off its arm, if you were quick, and the orrery wouldn't stop.}\ntext [7d7740] = {seen(tw3_orrery) > 1: {flag(heist_pearl_taken): 太阳系仪还在转，在你取走珍珠的地方少了一颗行星。|太阳系仪还在转，珍珠还骑在它的小臂上。}|天文教室里的黄铜太阳系仪还在转，一百年没人上过发条了，行星在长臂上一圈又一圈地在黑暗中转着。最小的那颗行星是一颗珍珠。手快的话，它能从臂上取下来，而仪器不会停。}\n# en: An abandoned astronomy classroom at night, a large brass orrery of planets on arms still slowly turning in the dark, a student reaching carefully toward one small planet.\nscene [cf692b] = 夜里一间废弃的天文教室，一台大型黄铜太阳系仪的行星在臂上于黑暗中缓缓转动，一个学生小心地伸手去够一颗小行星。\n# en: Leave it turning\nopt.left.label [661034] = 让它继续转\n# en: {~You watch it go round once more and leave it to its century.|You leave the planets where they are. Somebody should.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ca9692] = {~你看着它又转了一圈，把它留给它的一百年。|你让行星待在原地。总得有人这么做。}\n# en: Take the pearl as it passes\nopt.right.label [08d00d] = 趁它经过取下珍珠\n# en: {~You time the arm, and the pearl is in your palm before it's gone round. The orrery doesn't notice. It goes on turning, one planet short.|Quick as a pickpocket, you lift the pearl as it passes. The little arm swings on, empty, keeping time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ff75fa] = {~你算准长臂的时机，珍珠在它转走之前就到了你掌心。仪器毫无察觉，少了一颗行星，照样转着。|你像扒手一样快，在珍珠经过时取了下来。那根小臂空着荡过去，照样计时。}\n# en: {~You catch the arm instead of the pearl, and the whole orrery stops with a groan of brass that wakes the tower.|You're too slow, and the arm takes your knuckles going round, and you yelp.}\nopt.right.say.2 [4af1e6] = {~你抓住的是长臂而不是珍珠，整台仪器嘎吱一声停了，把塔吵醒了。|你太慢了，长臂转过来打在你指节上，你叫出了声。}\n\n@event tw3_portrait_eyes\n# en: The Portrait on the Landing\ntitle [03be50] = 平台上的画像\n# en: {seen(tw3_portrait_eyes) > 1: The portrait at the top of the classrooms opens its painted eyes before you've reached it. \"You again,\" it says.|At the top of the classrooms hangs a portrait of a professor a century dead, in a frame worth more than your trunk. The painted eyes follow you, and the painted mouth is opening, as portraits here do, to ask what you think you're doing.}\ntext [126207] = {seen(tw3_portrait_eyes) > 1: 你还没走到，教室最上头的那幅画像就睁开了画中的眼睛。“又是你，”它说。|教室的最上头挂着一幅一百年前去世的教授的画像，画框比你的箱子还值钱。画中的眼睛跟着你，画中的嘴正张开，像这里的画像都会做的那样，要问你以为自己在干什么。}\n# en: A dim tower corridor with a large stern painted portrait of a robed professor, a student creeping past beneath it, the painted eyes turned toward them.\nscene [e5336c] = 昏暗的塔楼走廊里挂着一幅严厉的穿长袍教授的大画像，一个学生从下面悄悄走过，画中的眼睛转向了他们。\n# en: Apologise to him, and go\nopt.left.label [f38032] = 向他道歉，然后走\n# en: {~You bow, and say you were just leaving, and he says \"Quite,\" and you were.|You tell the portrait you're lost. \"Down,\" he says, and you go down.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fd6f21] = {~你鞠了一躬，说你正要走，他说“正是”，于是你走了。|你告诉画像你迷路了。“往下，”他说，你就往下走了。}\n# en: Talk your way past him\nopt.right.label [93fd71] = 说服他放你过去\n# en: {~You tell him you're the new caretaker, sent to check the damp. He's been bored for a century, and believes you because he wants to.|You tell him the lessons are moving back up. He's so pleased he forgets to ask your name.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d480f2] = {~你告诉他你是新来的看门人，来检查潮气。他无聊了一百年，因为想信，所以信了你。|你告诉他课程要搬回楼上了。他高兴得忘了问你的名字。}\n# en: {~\"Intruder,\" says the portrait, in a voice built for a full hall, and doesn't stop saying it.|He doesn't believe a word, and he has a century of saved-up shouting.}\nopt.right.say.2 [500d47] = {~“有人闯入，”画像用一副为满堂听众准备的嗓子说，而且不停地说。|他一个字都不信，而且攒了一百年的吼叫。}\n\n@event tw_masters_study\n# en: The Duelling Master's Study\ntitle [78a98f] = 决斗师的书房\n# en: At the top of the old classrooms, the stair is choked with stacked desks, and beside them is a locked study with a brass plate: MASTER OF DUELLING. It was locked with the rest of the tower, and nobody has had its key since. {flag(star_key) or flag(gave_star_key) or flag(key_taken): Up past the desks, the tower is quiet.|Up past the desks, something in the tower hums at midnight, and every hair on your arms stands up. You won't be going higher.}\ntext [ba76c4] = 旧教室的最上头，楼梯被摞起来的课桌堵住了，旁边是一间锁着的书房，铜牌上写着：决斗师。它和塔的其余部分一起锁上了，从那以后谁也没有过它的钥匙。{flag(star_key) or flag(gave_star_key) or flag(key_taken): 课桌再往上，塔里很安静。|课桌再往上，塔里有什么东西在午夜嗡嗡作响，让你胳膊上的每根汗毛都竖了起来。你不会再往上了。}\n# en: A small locked study at the top of an old tower's classrooms, a desk under a dust sheet, a leather notebook in a glass case, a student kneeling at the lock with picks.\nscene [7bbcc0] = 一座旧塔楼教室最上头一间锁着的小书房，书桌上蒙着防尘布，玻璃盒里放着一本皮面笔记本，一个学生拿着撬锁针跪在锁前。\n# en: Leave the master to his study\nopt.left.label [3030c9] = 把书房留给决斗师\n# en: {~You touch the brass plate once and go back down. You got this far.|You don't try the door. Some rooms are closed for a reason.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5ea37b] = {~你摸了一下铜牌就下去了。能走到这儿就够了。|你没碰那扇门。有些房间是有理由关着的。}\n# en: Pick the lock and search the study\nopt.right.label [d89aeb] = 撬开锁，搜一搜书房\n# en: In a glass case lies a notebook the master confiscated from a student duellist long ago, its ink faded brown. In a neat and terrible hand it tells how to reach into someone and take their magic for yours. You read it through, and close it with a strength in your hands that isn't yours. Extraction.\nopt.right.say.1 [d58513] = 玻璃盒里放着一本笔记本，是决斗师很久以前从一个学生决斗者那里没收来的，墨迹已经褪成褐色。它用工整而可怕的笔迹写着，怎样伸进别人体内，把他们的魔力据为己有。你把它读完，合上时，手里有了一股不属于你的力量。汲取。\n# en: The confiscated notebook is in its glass case, and you know already what it says. You leave it, and take the master's silver inkstand and a box of old duelling medals instead.\nopt.right.say.2 [3999db] = 那本没收来的笔记本在玻璃盒里，你已经知道里面写的是什么。你没碰它，拿走了决斗师的银墨水台和一盒旧决斗奖章。\n# en: {~The old lock is warded, and it rings like a struck sword.|The door gives, and so does its top hinge, and it comes down on the desks with a crash that wakes everything.}\nopt.right.say.3 [bbf712] = {~老锁上有结界，像被敲响的剑一样鸣响。|门开了，上面的合页也开了，门砸在课桌上，轰隆一声把一切都吵醒了。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/hollowstar/convergence.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/hollowstar/convergence.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event hs_dumont_letter\n# en: A Letter From the North\ntitle [586de4] = 来自北境的信\n# en: Kael finds you and puts a letter on the table between you without a word. Black-star seal, broken. One line, in a beautiful hand: \"I will be at the lake for the Blossom moon. Be there, my son, and bring your friend.\" It's signed \"Mother\". {seen(kael_letter_home) > 0: It's her answer to the one he rewrote eleven times. |}His hands are shaking. \"She has never come south,\" he says. \"She has never once called me that.\"\ntext [9dc1d3] = 凯尔找到你，一言不发地把一封信放在你们之间的桌上。黑星封蜡，已经拆开。只有一行字，笔迹很美：“花潮之月，我会在湖边。到场吧，我的儿子，带上你的朋友。”署名是“母亲”。{seen(kael_letter_home) > 0: 这是她对那封他改写了十一遍的信的回复。|}他的手在发抖。“她从来没到过南方，”他说，“她从来没这样叫过我。”\n# en: A pale young man holding a letter sealed with a black star at arm's length, as if it might bite, frost creeping up the paper from his fingers.\nscene [8ef001] = 一个苍白的年轻人把一封黑星封蜡的信远远拿开，好像它会咬人，霜从他的指尖沿着信纸往上爬。\n# en: Burn it together\nopt.left.label [be972d] = 一起烧掉它\n# en: He'll remember who held the match.\nopt.left.hint [c0e4af] = 他会记得是谁划的火柴。\n# en: You hold it over a candle between you until the black star curls. The flame burns blue, then white, then out, and leaves frost on the candlestick. \"She'll know,\" Kael says. \"Good.\" It's the most like his sister you've ever heard him sound.\nopt.left.say.1 [df36cd] = 你们俩把它举在蜡烛上，直到那颗黑星卷曲起来。火焰先是蓝色，然后变白，然后熄灭，在烛台上留下一层霜。“她会知道的，”凯尔说，“很好。”这是你听过的他最像他妹妹的一次。\n# en: Keep it. Know your enemy.\nopt.right.label [f75413] = 留着它。知己知彼。\n# en: Useful. He won't like it.\nopt.right.hint [7aa19d] = 有用。他不会喜欢。\n# en: You fold it into your notes. Kael watches you do it and says nothing, which is worse than if he had. That night you read the line again by lamplight. The handwriting is beautiful, and patient, and very sure of itself.\nopt.right.say.1 [f292cb] = 你把它折好夹进笔记里。凯尔看着你这么做，什么也没说，这比说了还糟。那天夜里你借着灯光又读了一遍那行字。笔迹很美，很耐心，也非常笃定。\n\n@event hs_go_down_early\n# en: Before They're Ready\ntitle [6f3947] = 趁他们还没准备好\n# en: The lake is freezing in circles tonight, and the key in your pocket is so cold it burns. {seen(hs_dumont_letter) > 0 or seen(hs_isolde_summons) > 0: The Dumonts' mother is coming for the Blossom moon.|Someone is coming for the Blossom moon; the lake knows it.} Her circle isn't ready yet, and neither is the headmistress's. If you went down tonight you'd catch them all unready, and you'd have only the ones who promised you beside you.\ntext [cb40e2] = 今晚湖面一圈一圈地冻结，你口袋里的钥匙冷得发烫。{seen(hs_dumont_letter) > 0 or seen(hs_isolde_summons) > 0: 杜蒙夫人会在花潮之月到来。|有人会在花潮之月到来；湖知道。}她的圈子还没准备好，院长的也还没有。如果你今晚下去，就能趁所有人都没准备好时抓住他们，而你身边只会有那些向你许过诺的人。\n# en: A frozen lakeshore at night, a student holding a black star-shaped key up against the moon, a boathouse door ajar behind them.\nscene [a4e8ee] = 夜晚结冰的湖岸，一个学生把一把星形的黑钥匙举向月亮，身后船屋的门半开着。\n# en: Go down tonight\nopt.left.label [7e7ce0] = 今晚就下去\n# en: The Night, now. Only the allies who promised come.\nopt.left.hint [8490b2] = 那一夜，就是现在。只有许过诺的同伴会来。\n# en: You walk down to the boathouse without telling anyone. As you lift the latch, the whole lake behind you goes white in one breath, from shore to shore, and somewhere under it the bell begins to toll.\nopt.left.say.1 [709f1f] = 你没告诉任何人，独自走向船屋。你抬起门闩时，身后的整片湖在一口气间变白，从这岸到那岸，冰下某处，那口钟开始鸣响。\n# en: Wait for the Blossom moon\nopt.right.label [4f1efa] = 等到花潮之月\n# en: Everyone who'd stand with you will be there. So will everyone against you.\nopt.right.hint [dec936] = 所有愿意与你并肩的人都会在。所有与你为敌的人也是。\n# en: You stand on the shore until your feet are numb, then go back up to bed. The ice creaks after you all the way. Not yet.\nopt.right.say.1 [fc9e35] = 你在岸边站到脚都冻麻了，然后回去睡觉。冰一路在你身后咯吱作响。还不是时候。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/hollowstar/escalation.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/hollowstar/escalation.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event hs_shadow_late\n# en: A Moment Late\ntitle [2e700c] = 慢了半拍\n# en: On the stairs by lamplight you wave to a friend and drop your hand, and your shadow on the wall goes on waving a moment longer. Then it lowers its hand, slowly, as if it had only just noticed you were watching. You stand on the stair for a long time. It doesn't move again. Neither do you.\ntext [e248d2] = 灯光下的楼梯上，你朝一个朋友挥手，然后放下手，可墙上你的影子又多挥了一会儿。接着它慢慢放下手，好像才刚发现你在看着它。你在楼梯上站了很久。它没有再动。你也没有。\n# en: A student on a lamplit staircase looking at their own shadow on the wall, which is still raising its hand after theirs has dropped.\nscene [6adf27] = 灯光照亮的楼梯上，一个学生看着墙上自己的影子，自己的手已经放下了，影子的手还举着。\n# en: Find Seraphine, now\nopt.left.label [0da869] = 现在就去找瑟拉芬\n# en: She opens her door before you knock, already in her coat. \"Yours too,\" she says. \"I painted it last winter. I didn't know whose it was.\" She won't show you the painting. She does walk you back to your room and makes you leave the lamp lit.\nopt.left.say.1 [a97a5d] = 你还没敲门，她就开了门，已经穿好了外套。“你的也是，”她说，“去年冬天我画过它。我不知道是谁的。”她不肯给你看那幅画。不过她一路把你送回房间，让你别熄灯。\n# en: Blow the lamp out\nopt.right.label [956508] = 吹灭灯\n# en: In the dark there are no shadows, and you go up to bed without looking at the walls. You sleep badly. In the morning the frost on your window has melted in the shape of a hand, on the inside of the glass.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c6d70] = 黑暗里没有影子，你上楼睡觉，不去看墙。你睡得很糟。早上，你窗上的霜融出了一只手的形状，在玻璃里面那一侧。\n\n@event hs_grey_daylight\n# en: In Broad Daylight\ntitle [150ac4] = 光天化日\n# en: Noon, the courtyard full, and the man in grey walks in through the gate as if he had an appointment. The fountain freezes, then the flagstones, then the air. Students scramble back against the walls. He stops in front of you. {flag(star_key): \"The key,\" he says pleasantly, holding out a gloved hand. \"It was never yours.\"|\"Where is Kael Dumont?\" he says pleasantly. \"His mother would like a word.\"}\ntext [67cf7e] = 正午，庭院里满是人，灰衣人从大门走进来，像是赴一个约。喷泉冻住了，接着是石板，接着是空气。学生们慌忙退到墙边。他在你面前停下。{flag(star_key): “钥匙，”他和颜悦色地说，伸出一只戴手套的手，“它从来就不是你的。”|“凯尔·杜蒙在哪儿？”他和颜悦色地说，“他母亲想和他谈谈。”}\n# en: A sunlit courtyard at noon gone silent and frozen, students pressed against the walls, a tall man in grey walking straight across it toward one student.\nscene [80df20] = 正午阳光下的庭院一片寂静、冻结，学生们贴着墙，一个穿灰衣的高个子男人径直穿过庭院走向一个学生。\n# en: Draw on him, here\nopt.fight.label [43758c] = 就在这里对他出杖\n# en: Combat, in front of everyone. Lose, and he takes what he came for.\nopt.fight.hint [35fb0c] = 战斗，当着所有人的面。输了，他就会拿走他要的东西。\n# en: Kael's frost and your spell hit him together, and the man in grey goes skidding back across the ice he made. For the first time he looks surprised. He straightens his coat, bows to the whole courtyard, and walks out of the gate. Then the cheering starts.\nopt.fight.say.1 [9bcfde] = 凯尔的霜和你的咒语同时击中了他，灰衣人在他自己造的冰上向后滑了出去。他第一次露出惊讶的神色。他整了整外套，向整个庭院鞠了一躬，走出了大门。然后欢呼声响了起来。\n# en: It goes on for a long minute, in front of everyone, and then your last spell is still in the air when the flagstones come up to meet you both. {flag(key_taken): When you get up, the key is gone from your pocket, and so is he.|When you get up he is gone, and Kael is bleeding from a cut over his eye that frosts over as you watch.}\nopt.fight.say.2 [e68208] = 当着所有人的面，你们斗了整整一分钟，然后你最后一道咒语还悬在空中，石板就迎面撞了上来，你们俩都倒了。{flag(key_taken): 等你爬起来，钥匙已经不在你口袋里了，他也不在了。|等你爬起来，他已经走了，凯尔眼睛上方的伤口在流血，你眼看着它结上了霜。}\n# en: Your spell catches him square and he goes skidding back across the ice he made. For the first time he looks surprised. He straightens his coat, bows to the whole courtyard, and walks out of the gate. Then the cheering starts.\nopt.fight.say.3 [c04347] = 你的咒语正中他，他在他自己造的冰上向后滑了出去。他第一次露出惊讶的神色。他整了整外套，向整个庭院鞠了一躬，走出了大门。然后欢呼声响了起来。\n# en: You trade spells with him across the frozen fountain for longer than anyone thought you could, and then the flagstones come up to meet you. {flag(key_taken): When you get up, the key is gone from your pocket, and so is he.|When you get up he is gone, and the frost on the flagstones spells out a name: Kael's.}\nopt.fight.say.4 [1eed80] = 你隔着冻住的喷泉和他对攻，撑得比任何人想的都久，然后石板迎面撞了上来。{flag(key_taken): 等你爬起来，钥匙已经不在你口袋里了，他也不在了。|等你爬起来，他已经走了，石板上的霜拼出了一个名字：凯尔。}\n# en: Run, and take what he wants with you\nopt.hide.label [90131f] = 带上他要的东西，跑\n# en: Guile. Half the school is between you and the gate{flag(confronted_grey): , and he knows your face|}.\nopt.hide.hint [2fedb0] = 诡计。你和大门之间隔着半个学校的人{flag(confronted_grey): ，而他认得你的脸|}。\n# en: You duck into the crowd and the crowd closes behind you; a dozen first-years suddenly need to cross the courtyard all at once. By the time he has walked through them, you're three corridors away {flag(star_key): with the key|with Kael}, and the bell for lessons is ringing.\nopt.hide.say.1 [01312d] = 你钻进人群，人群在你身后合拢；一打一年级生突然同时需要穿过庭院。等他走过他们，你已经{flag(star_key): 带着钥匙|带着凯尔}跑出了三条走廊，上课铃正在响。\n# en: He doesn't chase you. He simply arrives, at the end of the corridor, ahead of you. {flag(key_taken): He holds out his hand, and the key leaves your pocket on its own and goes to him.|\"Tell him she's coming,\" he says, and leaves you shaking.}\nopt.hide.say.2 [8f5ca1] = 他没有追你。他只是先到了，在走廊尽头，在你前面。{flag(key_taken): 他伸出手，钥匙自己离开你的口袋，到了他那里。|“告诉他，她要来了。”他说完就走了，留下你发抖。}\n# en: Shout for the headmistress\nopt.call.label [8e6b96] = 大喊院长\n# en: Sure. She'll want to know why he came for you.\nopt.call.hint [dc25e6] = 一定成功。她会想知道他为什么来找你。\n# en: She is there before you finish her name, and the courtyard thaws around her feet. He bows to her and goes. Then she holds out her own hand. \"The key,\" she says, exactly as he did. \"For safekeeping.\" You give it to her.\nopt.call.say.1 [401296] = 你还没喊完她的名字，她就到了，庭院在她脚边解冻。他向她鞠了一躬，走了。然后她伸出了自己的手。“钥匙，”她说，和他一模一样，“替你保管。”你把它交给了她。\n# en: She is there before you finish her name, and the courtyard thaws around her feet. He bows to her and goes. Kael, who heard the whole thing from a window, doesn't speak to you for a week: \"You told her he was looking for me.\"\nopt.call.say.2 [67e092] = 你还没喊完她的名字，她就到了，庭院在她脚边解冻。他向她鞠了一躬，走了。凯尔在窗边听见了这一切，一整周没和你说话：“你告诉她他在找我。”\n\n@event hs_key_recover\n# en: Getting It Back\ntitle [1e5dab] = 把它拿回来\n# en: The man in grey has the star key, and by now the whole academy knows it. He has taken the top room at the Gilded Newt, and the landlord says he never seems to sleep. The headmistress has said nothing to you. Kael hasn't stopped saying things. There are ways to get it back, and none of them involve asking him nicely.\ntext [fbf9ca] = 灰衣人拿着星钥，现在全学院都知道了。他住进了镀金蝾螈酒馆的顶楼房间，老板说他好像从来不睡觉。院长对你什么也没说。凯尔则说个不停。有办法把它拿回来，只是没有一种是好好地开口请他还。\n# en: A narrow upstairs corridor of a village inn at night, a door with frost on its handle, a student with a candle hesitating outside it.\nscene [943194] = 夜里村中旅店楼上的一条窄走廊，一扇门把手上结着霜，一个学生举着蜡烛在门外犹豫。\n# en: Go to the headmistress\nopt.isolde.label [c956ad] = 去找院长\n# en: She'll get it back, and keep it until the night.\nopt.isolde.hint [12a812] = 她会拿回来，并保管到那一夜。\n# en: She listens without a word, then puts on her coat. In the morning the key is on her desk, and there is frost on her sleeve up to the elbow. \"He was polite about it,\" she says. \"They always are.\" She doesn't give it back to you.\nopt.isolde.say.1 [18009b] = 她一言不发地听完，然后穿上了外套。第二天早上，钥匙就在她桌上，她的袖子上一直到手肘都是霜。“他倒是很有礼貌，”她说，“他们向来如此。”她没有把钥匙还给你。\n# en: Steal it back from the Newt\nopt.steal.label [44f3ab] = 从酒馆把它偷回来\n# en: Guile. He never seems to sleep.\nopt.steal.hint [56e6b3] = 诡计。他好像从来不睡。\n# en: He is sitting up in the dark with his eyes open, and he doesn't see you. The key is on the windowsill, in a dish of snow. You take it, and back out, and don't breathe until you're on the road.\nopt.steal.say.1 [c75c6b] = 他睁着眼坐在黑暗里，却没看见你。钥匙在窗台上，放在一碟雪里。你拿起它，退出来，直到走上大路才敢喘气。\n# en: The landing creaks. He says \"Good evening\" through the closed door, and the cold comes under it like water. You run, and slip on the stairs, and get back to the castle with a sprained wrist and nothing else.\nopt.steal.say.2 [98ff28] = 楼梯平台嘎吱一响。他隔着关着的门说了声“晚上好”，寒气像水一样从门底下漫过来。你跑了，在楼梯上滑了一跤，回到城堡时只多了一只扭伤的手腕。\n# en: Ask a Dumont where he'd keep it\nopt.family.label [b1704d] = 问杜蒙家的人他会藏在哪儿\n# en: Charm. Kael or Ilse know how the families hide things.\nopt.family.hint [ca6a0c] = 魅力。凯尔或伊尔泽知道那些家族怎么藏东西。\n# en: \"Snow,\" says {flag(ilse_saved): Ilse|Kael}, at once. \"He'll keep it in snow, so it can't call to anyone.\" It's on his windowsill, in a dish of it. The Dumont who told you stands lookout in the lane while you climb, and doesn't say a word on the way home.\nopt.family.say.1 [d0043f] = “雪里，”{flag(ilse_saved): 伊尔泽|凯尔}立刻说，“他会把它放在雪里，这样它就没法呼唤任何人。”它就在他的窗台上，放在一碟雪里。告诉你的那个杜蒙在巷子里替你望风，回去的路上一句话也没说。\n# en: \"I don't know,\" {flag(ilse_saved): Ilse|Kael} says, too quickly, and won't look at you. Whatever the families do with what they take, it isn't something they want to tell you. Not yet.\nopt.family.say.2 [5629f1] = “我不知道。”{flag(ilse_saved): 伊尔泽|凯尔}说得太快了，也不看你。不管那些家族怎么处置他们拿走的东西，那都不是他们想告诉你的事。还不是时候。\n# en: Let Edmund's wand find it\nopt.wand.label [724195] = 让埃德蒙的魔杖去找\n# en: Arcana. The wand hums near anything the founders made.\nopt.wand.hint [4b2c87] = 奥术。这根魔杖靠近创始者造的任何东西都会嗡鸣。\n# en: The silver wand drags at your hand like a dog on a lead, all the way to the Newt and up the back stairs, and goes quiet beside a dish of snow on a windowsill. The man in grey is out. The key is not.\nopt.wand.say.1 [c757e8] = 银魔杖像拴着绳的狗一样拽着你的手，一路拽到酒馆，拽上后楼梯，在窗台上的一碟雪旁安静下来。灰衣人出去了。钥匙没有。\n# en: The wand hums, and wavers, and points at the lake every time. Wherever he's keeping it, it's near the water, and you can't follow it there. Not tonight.\nopt.wand.say.2 [1de714] = 魔杖嗡鸣着，摇摆着，每次都指向湖。不管他把它藏在哪里，都在水边，而你没法跟到那里去。今晚不行。\n# en: Not yet\nopt.wait.label [8dd27c] = 还不是时候\n# en: It'll keep. He will too.\nopt.wait.hint [0673fb] = 它跑不了。他也跑不了。\n# en: You leave it for now. Every night you can feel it from across the village, cold, the way you feel a draught from a door left open.\nopt.wait.say.1 [a760bf] = 你暂时放下了。每天夜里你都能隔着整个村子感觉到它，冷冷的，就像感觉到一扇没关的门透进来的风。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/hollowstar/night.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/hollowstar/night.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event hs_the_night_above\n# en: Someone Else's Night\ntitle [8f89ac] = 别人的那一夜\n# en: You wake in the night to the bell under the lake, tolling and tolling. From your window the whole lake is white, and out on the ice there are lanterns moving in a ring, and figures you can't make out. Somebody runs past your door. Nobody knocks on it.\ntext [5c6011] = 夜里你被湖底的钟声惊醒，钟声一下接一下。从窗口望去，整片湖都白了，冰面上有一圈提灯在移动，还有你看不清的人影。有人从你门前跑过。没有人敲你的门。\n# en: A dormitory window at night, a lake frozen white below, lanterns moving out on the ice, a student watching from behind the glass.\nscene [80eae2] = 夜晚的宿舍窗户，窗下的湖冻得雪白，冰面上提灯在移动，一个学生在玻璃后面看着。\n# en: Keep the first-years in their beds\nopt.left.label [fdfae3] = 让一年级生们待在床上\n# en: You sit on the stairs of the first-year tower until dawn with a lamp, saying \"go back to bed\" forty times. By morning the lake has thawed. Someone else went down with a lantern. You never learn the whole of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [a93ea5] = 你提着灯在一年级塔的楼梯上坐到天亮，说了四十遍“回去睡觉”。到早上，湖解冻了。有别人提着灯下去了。你始终没能知道事情的全貌。\n# en: Watch from the window\nopt.right.label [f8add9] = 在窗边看着\n# en: You watch until the lanterns go out, one by one, and then something under the ice glows and goes dark. By morning the lake is blue. At breakfast the headmistress's hair is a shade whiter, and she smiles at nobody.\nopt.right.say.1 [7c3b54] = 你一直看到提灯一盏接一盏熄灭，然后冰下有什么东西亮了一下，又暗了。到早上湖是蓝的。早餐时，院长的头发白了一点，她对着空无一人的地方微笑。\n\n@event hs_night_company\n# en: Who Comes Down With You\ntitle [be20d9] = 谁陪你下去\n# en: The boathouse hatch is open, and the steps go down into blue light. {night_kael() or night_ilse() or night_maud() or night_isolde(): {flag(hs_early): You came without telling anyone, and only the ones who promised have followed you down the shore.|Behind you, people are running across the frozen lawn: the ones who said they'd stand with you, and meant it.} The tunnel down is narrow. There's room for two of you, and no more. Who comes?|Nobody has followed you down the shore. The tunnel is narrow, and the dark at the bottom is very quiet.}\ntext [274147] = 船屋的活板门开着，台阶一路向下，没入蓝光之中。{night_kael() or night_ilse() or night_maud() or night_isolde(): {flag(hs_early): 你没告诉任何人就来了，只有那些许过诺的人沿着湖岸跟了过来。|你身后，有人正跑过结冰的草坪：那些说过会与你并肩、并且是认真的人。}往下的隧道很窄。只容得下你们两个人，不能再多了。谁来？|没有人沿着湖岸跟来。隧道很窄，底下的黑暗非常安静。}\n# en: The boathouse at night, its hatch open on stone steps going down into blue light, a student at the top with a lantern and figures hurrying in behind.\nscene [c81373] = 夜里的船屋，活板门开着，石阶通向下方的蓝光，一个学生提着灯站在顶上，身后有人匆匆赶进来。\n# en: Kael\nopt.kael.label [9735b0] = 凯尔\n# en: Hoarfrost beside you in every fight. His mother is on the ice.\nopt.kael.hint [b07fec] = 每场战斗他的白霜都在你身边。他母亲就在冰上。\n# en: Kael takes the lantern out of your hand without asking. \"My family,\" he says. \"My door.\" Frost runs down the steps ahead of you, so you don't slip.\nopt.kael.say.1 [78c9be] = 凯尔没问就从你手里接过了提灯。“我的家族，”他说，“我的门。”霜在你前面沿着台阶铺下去，免得你滑倒。\n# en: Ilse\nopt.ilse.label [a098fc] = 伊尔泽\n# en: Her frost beside you in every fight. Her mother is on the ice.\nopt.ilse.hint [984c7c] = 每场战斗她的霜都在你身边。她母亲就在冰上。\n# en: Ilse is already on the top step, white furs and a wand like an icicle. \"I was raised for this door,\" she says. \"I'd like to see their faces when I close it.\"\nopt.ilse.say.1 [07512f] = 伊尔泽已经站在最上面一级台阶上，一身白裘，魔杖像一根冰柱。“我就是为这扇门被养大的，”她说，“我想看看我关上它的时候，他们是什么表情。”\n# en: Old Maud\nopt.maud.label [9c77f5] = 老莫德\n# en: No help in a fight. The founders' wall knows her.\nopt.maud.hint [9ebd04] = 战斗中帮不上忙。创始者之墙认得她。\n# en: Maud comes down the lawn at a pace that should be impossible at her age. \"I lost him to that door,\" she says. \"I'm not losing you, dearie.\" {has_item(edmunds_silver_wand): She taps the wand in your hand. \"Keep his wand up. It knows the way.\"|}\nopt.maud.say.1 [12b3d5] = 莫德沿着草坪走下来，速度快得不像她这个年纪的人。“我把他输给了那扇门，”她说，“我可不会再把你输掉，亲爱的。”{has_item(edmunds_silver_wand): 她敲了敲你手里的魔杖。“把他的魔杖举好。它认得路。”|}\n# en: The headmistress\nopt.isolde.label [fc1c54] = 院长\n# en: No help in a fight. Her seal beside yours at the door.\nopt.isolde.hint [54b7a5] = 战斗中帮不上忙。在门前，她的封缄与你的并肩。\n# en: The headmistress walks into the boathouse as if into her own study, her ring lit like a lamp. \"After you,\" she says. \"I've read about this door all my life.\"\nopt.isolde.say.1 [f7868c] = 院长走进船屋，像走进自己的书房，她的戒指亮得像一盏灯。“你先请，”她说，“这扇门我读了一辈子。”\n# en: No one. Keep them safe above.\nopt.alone.label [6c5427] = 谁也不带。让他们在上面平安。\n# en: Every fight is yours alone. Nobody you love goes under the lake.\nopt.alone.hint [4ded4f] = 每场战斗都只有你自己。你爱的人没有一个会下到湖底。\n# en: You tell them to guard the towers, and close the hatch on their protests. The steps are slick and the dark is very quiet, and it's the bravest and loneliest thing you've ever done.\nopt.alone.say.1 [895e06] = 你让他们去守塔，不顾他们的抗议关上了活板门。台阶很滑，黑暗非常安静，这是你做过的最勇敢、也最孤独的事。\n\n@event hs_night_cult\n# en: The Circle on the Ice\ntitle [e57069] = 冰上的圆圈\n# en: The tunnel comes up through the ice inside a ring of lanterns: twelve figures in black furs, chanting{flag(hs_early): , half the lanterns not yet lit|}. At the centre stands a tall woman in white with Kael's face, thirty years on{flag(hs_early): , snow from the North still on her shoulders|}. Madame Dumont. {flag(night_kael): \"My son. You came after all.\" \"You never came for me,\" Kael shouts. \"You came for the door!\"|{flag(night_ilse): \"Ilse,\" she says, the way you'd call a dog. \"I'm not yours to call,\" says Ilse, white to the lips.|{flag(night_isolde): \"Headmistress,\" she says. \"Still guarding a hole in the ground.\" \"Still trying to open one, Madame,\" says the headmistress.|\"Kael's little friend,\" she says. \"Step aside.\"}}}\ntext [e63c30] = 隧道从一圈提灯中间穿出冰面：十二个穿黑裘的人影在吟诵{flag(hs_early): ，一半的提灯还没点亮|}。正中站着一个穿白衣的高个子女人，长着凯尔的脸，只是老了三十岁{flag(hs_early): ，肩上还落着北方的雪|}。杜蒙夫人。{flag(night_kael): “我的儿子。你到底还是来了。”“你从来不是为我而来，”凯尔吼道，“你是为那扇门来的！”|{flag(night_ilse): “伊尔泽。”她说，像在唤一条狗。“你没资格这样叫我。”伊尔泽说，嘴唇都白了。|{flag(night_isolde): “院长，”她说，“还在守着地上的一个洞。”“夫人还在想着打开它。”院长说。|“凯尔的小朋友，”她说，“让开。”}}}\n# en: A ring of figures in black furs with lanterns on a frozen lake at night, and at its centre a tall woman in white furs with a pale, proud face, her wand raised.\nscene [3ccb55] = 夜里结冰的湖面上，一圈穿黑裘、提着灯的人影，正中一个穿白裘的高个子女人，面容苍白而骄傲，举着魔杖。\n# en: Break the circle by force\nopt.fight.label [2d477d] = 用武力打破圆圈\n# en: Combat against her{flag(hs_early): . Half her circle isn't here.|.}\nopt.fight.hint [3b6088] = 与她战斗{flag(hs_early): 。她的圆圈有一半人还没来。|。}\n# en: Her ward breaks and she goes down on one knee on the ice, and the chant stops dead. She looks up at you with Kael's eyes. \"So this is what he chose,\" she says. The circle scatters into the dark, carrying her.\nopt.fight.say.1 [6398c9] = 她的护界碎了，她单膝跪在冰上，吟诵戛然而止。她用凯尔的眼睛抬头看着你。“原来他选的是这个。”她说。圆圈四散进黑暗，把她也带走了。\n# en: Her last word lands before your last spell does, and the ice throws you down. \"Go down, then,\" she says. \"The door will have you either way.\" The chant goes on, louder, and the glow under the ring grows.\nopt.fight.say.2 [fa5cb6] = 她最后一个字抢在你最后一道咒语之前落下，冰面把你掀翻在地。“那就下去吧，”她说，“不管怎样，那扇门都会要你。”吟诵继续，越来越响，圆圈下的光越来越亮。\n# en: Speak the names from the Codex\nopt.names.label [997c36] = 念出《空洞名录》上的名字\n# en: Lore. {clue(codex_names): You've read them.|You'll have to read them off their furs.}\nopt.names.hint [4df2c2] = 学识。{clue(codex_names): 你读过它们。|你得从他们的毛皮上读出来。}\n# en: You say their names aloud, the ones from the Codex's first page, one for each of them. Each name you say stops a mouth. When you reach \"Dumont\", the woman in white stops chanting, and the ring of lanterns goes out.\nopt.names.say.1 [ef6ec0] = 你大声念出他们的名字，《空洞名录》第一页上的那些，一人一个。每念出一个名字，就有一张嘴停下来。念到“杜蒙”时，白衣女人停止了吟诵，那一圈提灯熄灭了。\n# en: You lose your place halfway down the list, and the ones you haven't named say yours back. Your fingertips go grey with cold. The circle closes again behind you as you run for the tunnel.\nopt.names.say.2 [66a5a4] = 念到一半你找不到念到哪儿了，那些你还没念到的人，反过来念出了你的名字。你的指尖冻得发灰。你朝隧道跑去时，圆圈在你身后重新合拢。\n# en: Their names are stitched into the hems of their furs, the way the Codex lists them. You read them off one by one as they turn, and each name you say stops a mouth. At \"Dumont\", the woman in white falls silent.\nopt.names.say.3 [30ec45] = 他们的名字绣在毛皮的下摆上，排列方式和《空洞名录》一样。他们转身时你一个一个读出来，每念出一个名字，就有一张嘴停下来。念到“杜蒙”时，白衣女人沉默了。\n# en: You can read the names stitched in their furs, but not fast enough. Half of them say yours back, and your fingertips go grey with cold. You run for the tunnel with the chant at your back.\nopt.names.say.4 [b5d586] = 你读得出他们毛皮上绣的名字，可是不够快。有一半人反过来念出了你的名字，你的指尖冻得发灰。你在背后的吟诵声中跑向隧道。\n# en: Let them answer their mother\nopt.family.label [43e939] = 让他们回答自己的母亲\n# en: Charm. Say what she sent them for, and what they chose.\nopt.family.hint [b47c8d] = 魅力。说出她送他们来是为了什么，以及他们选择了什么。\n# en: You start it, and then you stop, because {flag(night_kael): Kael|Ilse} is shouting, at last, everything a whole life kept in: \"You never once said well done. Not once!\" The circle wavers. The chant breaks. Madame Dumont lowers her wand and says nothing at all.\nopt.family.say.1 [6cedc6] = 你开了个头，然后停下了，因为{flag(night_kael): 凯尔|伊尔泽}终于喊了出来，一辈子憋在心里的一切：“你从来没说过一句做得好。一次都没有！”圆圈动摇了。吟诵断了。杜蒙夫人放下魔杖，一句话也没说。\n# en: She lets them shout, and then says, quite gently, \"Are you finished?\" {flag(night_kael): Kael|Ilse} is shaking. The circle goes on chanting as if nobody had spoken, and you drag your friend down the tunnel before the ice opens.\nopt.family.say.2 [2d20a0] = 她任由他们喊，然后非常温和地说：“说完了吗？”{flag(night_kael): 凯尔|伊尔泽}在发抖。圆圈继续吟诵，好像谁也没说过话，你在冰面裂开之前把你的朋友拖进了隧道。\n# en: Go round the circle\nopt.round.label [a3b7a0] = 绕过圆圈\n# en: Guile. You'll get past. They'll still be chanting.\nopt.round.hint [179df9] = 诡计。你过得去。他们会继续吟诵。\n# en: You go round the outside of the ring, low, in the dark between the lanterns, and nobody turns. At the tunnel mouth you look back. She is looking straight at you, and she smiles, and doesn't stop chanting.\nopt.round.say.1 [8ff38a] = 你压低身子，在提灯之间的黑暗里绕着圆圈外沿走，没有人回头。到了隧道口你回头看。她正直直地看着你，笑了，没有停止吟诵。\n# en: A lantern swings your way, and the woman in white says a word, and the ice bucks under your feet. You reach the tunnel on your hands and knees, with the cold all the way into your chest.\nopt.round.say.2 [b159d4] = 一盏提灯朝你晃过来，白衣女人说了一个字，冰面在你脚下猛地一拱。你手脚并用地爬到隧道，寒气一直灌进胸口。\n\n@event hs_night_ilse\n# en: Ilse in the Dark\ntitle [9ab9ac] = 黑暗中的伊尔泽\n# en: Where the tunnel opens into the dark, Ilse is standing in the way. Her eyes are on nothing, and frost is falling from her hands like flour. \"Go back,\" she says, in the man in grey's pleasant voice, not her own. \"This is my door. I was raised for it.\" {flag(night_kael): Kael says her name. Then he shouts it, and it echoes all the way down.|}\ntext [7d1eba] = 在隧道通向黑暗的地方，伊尔泽挡在路上。她的眼睛望着虚空，霜像面粉一样从她手里落下。“回去，”她说，用的是灰衣人和气的声音，不是她自己的，“这是我的门。我就是为它被养大的。”{flag(night_kael): 凯尔叫了她的名字。然后他喊了出来，一路回荡到底。|}\n# en: A young woman in white furs standing in a dark tunnel under ice, eyes empty, frost falling from her hands like flour.\nscene [872d27] = 冰下黑暗的隧道里站着一个穿白裘的年轻女人，眼神空洞，霜像面粉一样从她手中落下。\n# en: Reach her\nopt.reach.label [0ea9b7] = 唤回她\n# en: Charm. What she was raised for, and who she is, are your words.\nopt.reach.hint [b945ff] = 魅力。她是为什么被养大的，她是谁，就是你要说的话。\n# en: You tell her {flag(ilse_confided): what she told you once in the snow: that nobody asked her|that nobody ever asked her what she wanted}. Her eyes find yours. Then she's crying, furiously, and the frost stops falling. \"I can hear him,\" she says. \"I can't hear him any more.\" {flag(night_kael): Kael gets to her first.|}\nopt.reach.say.1 [822194] = 你告诉她{flag(ilse_confided): 她曾在雪地里对你说的话：从来没人问过她|从来没人问过她想要什么}。她的目光找到了你。然后她哭了，哭得很凶，霜不再落下。“我能听见他，”她说，“我听不见他了。”{flag(night_kael): 凯尔先冲到了她身边。|}\n# en: She listens to every word with the man in grey's polite face, and steps aside to let you pass. When you look back, she is walking up the tunnel toward {flag(cult_broken): the empty ice where her mother's circle was|her mother's circle}, and doesn't turn round.\nopt.reach.say.2 [147e31] = 她顶着灰衣人那张客气的脸听完了每一个字，然后让到一边让你过去。你回头看时，她正沿着隧道往上走，走向{flag(cult_broken): 她母亲的圆圈曾经所在的那片空冰|她母亲的圆圈}，没有回头。\n# en: Break his hold on her\nopt.fight.label [704415] = 打破他对她的控制\n# en: Combat, against her. Win, and the compulsion breaks.\nopt.fight.hint [d2b22a] = 战斗，对手是她。赢了，强令就会破除。\n# en: Your last spell hits the frost in her hands, not her, and it shatters, and so does whatever was holding her. She sits down hard on the tunnel floor. \"That hurt,\" she says, in her own voice, and then, \"Thank you,\" and then she gets up.\nopt.fight.say.1 [2db22b] = 你最后一道咒打中的是她手里的霜，不是她，霜碎了，控制着她的东西也碎了。她重重地坐在隧道地上。“好疼。”她用自己的声音说，然后说“谢谢”，然后站了起来。\n# en: She fights like what she was raised to be. When you get up, she's gone, up the tunnel toward the ice, and there's a single white glove on the stone.\nopt.fight.say.2 [2a6909] = 她打起来就是他们把她养成的样子。等你爬起来，她已经走了，沿着隧道朝冰面去了，石头上留着一只白手套。\n# en: Go past her\nopt.past.label [ac9e6a] = 从她身边走过\n# en: She won't stop you. She won't come with you.\nopt.past.hint [51b93f] = 她不会拦你。她也不会跟你走。\n# en: You step round her. She doesn't stop you; she doesn't see you. {flag(night_kael): Kael stands with her a moment longer, then comes after you with his jaw set and doesn't speak again until the wall.|You don't look back. You'll think about not looking back for a long time.}\nopt.past.say.1 [d9f80e] = 你从她身边绕过去。她没有拦你；她看不见你。{flag(night_kael): 凯尔在她身边多站了一会儿，然后咬着牙跟上你，直到那面墙前都没再说话。|你没有回头。你会在很长时间里想着自己没有回头这件事。}\n\n@event hs_night_shadow\n# en: Your Own Shadow\ntitle [39a1da] = 你自己的影子\n# en: Where the tunnel narrows, your lantern throws your shadow onto the wet stone, and it stays there when you stop. Then it turns round. It has your face, and the Star's eyes. {clue(second_shadow): Seraphine painted this. You knew it was coming.|Nobody warned you. Nobody could have.} \"Let me in,\" it says, in your voice. \"We'd be so much stronger.\"\ntext [ca0245] = 隧道变窄的地方，你的提灯把你的影子投在湿漉漉的石壁上，你停下时它没有跟着停下。然后它转过身来。它长着你的脸，和星的眼睛。{clue(second_shadow): 瑟拉芬画过这一幕。你知道它会来。|没有人警告过你。也没有人能警告你。}“让我进去，”它用你的声音说，“我们会强大得多。”\n# en: A narrow stone tunnel under the ice, a student's shadow standing out from the wet wall on its own, with the student's face and eyes like holes.\nscene [ede0f2] = 冰下一条狭窄的石头隧道，一个学生的影子独自从湿墙上站出来，长着那个学生的脸，眼睛像两个洞。\n# en: Fight it\nopt.fight.label [533b96] = 和它战斗\n# en: Combat, against everything you know.\nopt.fight.hint [54444d] = 战斗，对手是你会的一切。\n# en: It fights with your spells, in your order, and it is a beat late, every time, the way it was on the stairs. You use the beat. It breaks against the wall like water, and your own shadow lies down on the floor where it belongs.\nopt.fight.say.1 [c202ed] = 它用你的咒语、按你的顺序战斗，每一次都慢了半拍，就像在楼梯上那样。你利用了这半拍。它像水一样在墙上撞碎，你自己的影子躺回了它该在的地上。\n# en: It knows every spell you're going to cast, because you do. It puts you on the floor, then sinks back into the wall and waits there, smiling with your mouth.\nopt.fight.say.2 [7695cf] = 你要施的每一道咒它都知道，因为你知道。它把你放倒在地，然后沉回墙里，在那里等着，用你的嘴微笑。\n# en: Hold up a star's light\nopt.light.label [3f402c] = 举起一颗星的光\n# en: Arcana. Light the Star can't swallow; Starlight helps.\nopt.light.hint [eef3d3] = 奥术。星吞不下的光；星光会有帮助。\n# en: You call down the light of a named star into your hand, and the shadow has nowhere to stand. It shrinks back into your outline on the floor with a sound like a sigh, and stays there.\nopt.light.say.1 [5b564a] = 你把一颗有名字的星的光唤到手中，影子无处立足。它叹息般地缩回你在地上的轮廓里，再也没出来。\n# en: The light comes, and wavers, and the shadow simply steps into the dark behind it. It is still there on the wall when you go on, a moment late behind you, all the way to the door.\nopt.light.say.2 [f2e003] = 光来了，摇曳着，影子只是退进光背后的黑暗里。你继续前行时它还在墙上，比你慢半拍地跟着，一直跟到门前。\n# en: Let it back in\nopt.let.label [16d56d] = 让它回到你身体里\n# en: It fits. That's the trouble.\nopt.let.hint [80ee89] = 它很合适。麻烦就在这里。\n# en: You open your arms and it steps into you, and it fits, it fits so well. You've never felt so strong, or so certain, or so cold. {flag(night_kael): Kael takes a step back from you, and doesn't know he's done it.|}\nopt.let.say.1 [d83953] = 你张开双臂，它走进了你，那么合适，合适极了。你从没觉得这么强大，这么笃定，这么冰冷。{flag(night_kael): 凯尔从你身边后退了一步，自己都没察觉。|}\n\n@event hs_aftermath_bargain\n# en: The Star's Gift\ntitle [75e568] = 星的馈赠\n# en: You wake with the Star's gift already in you. Eclipse, a spell you never studied, sits in your hands like an old habit. There's a purse on your pillow heavy with coins nobody gave you. The mark on your palm has grown a crown of points, and a voice at the back of your mind says, pleasantly, \"Good morning.\" {flag(ilse_taken): Ilse went north before dawn, with the man in grey.|}\ntext [0f6da3] = 你醒来时，星的馈赠已经在你身上了。蚀，一道你从没学过的咒语，像老习惯一样待在你手里。你枕头上放着一个钱袋，沉甸甸的，装着没人给过你的硬币。你掌心的印记长出了一圈冠冕般的尖角，脑海深处有个声音和气地说：“早上好。”{flag(ilse_taken): 黎明前，伊尔泽跟着灰衣人去了北方。|}\n# en: A student at a dormitory mirror at dawn, a crowned black star glowing on their palm, a purse spilling coins on the bed behind them.\nscene [c5bba3] = 黎明时分，一个学生站在宿舍镜前，掌心一颗戴冠的黑星在发光，身后床上的钱袋里撒出了硬币。\n# en: Use it\nopt.left.label [77da81] = 用它\n# en: The Star likes to be used.\nopt.left.hint [d870af] = 星喜欢被使用。\n# en: You light every candle in your room with a look, and then put them all out with another. It's easy. It's so easy. Somewhere under the lake, something settles in to wait for what it's owed.\nopt.left.say.1 [29757d] = 你看一眼就点亮了房间里所有的蜡烛，再看一眼又把它们全熄灭了。很容易。太容易了。在湖底某处，有什么东西安顿下来，等着收它应得的那份。\n# en: Hide the mark\nopt.right.label [e267a7] = 藏起印记\n# en: Gloves, in spring. People will ask.\nopt.right.hint [b0fe59] = 春天戴手套。会有人问的。\n# en: You wear gloves from that day on. People ask, and you say it's a burn, and it is, in a way. At night the crown on your palm glows through the leather, and you lie awake watching it.\nopt.right.say.1 [f929a7] = 从那天起你一直戴着手套。有人问，你就说是烫伤，某种意义上也确实是。夜里你掌心的冠冕透过皮革发着光，你躺着睁眼看它。\n\n@event hs_aftermath_quiet\n# en: Only Water Now\ntitle [ec28f8] = 如今只是湖水\n# en: The lake glitters as if nothing happened. Most of the academy believes there was a strange spring frost. {flag(night_ilse): Ilse is at the end of the jetty, boots off, feet in water that is only water now.|{flag(night_maud): Maud waves from her step, two mugs of tea beside her.|{flag(night_isolde): Across the courtyard, the headmistress inclines her head to you, once.|Nobody knows where you were last night. Nobody thanks you.}}} {flag(ilse_taken): Ilse went north before dawn, with the man in grey.|}\ntext [af55e9] = 湖面波光粼粼，仿佛什么都没发生过。学院里大多数人以为那只是一场奇怪的春霜。{flag(night_ilse): 伊尔泽坐在栈桥尽头，脱了靴子，把脚伸进如今只是湖水的水里。|{flag(night_maud): 莫德在门阶上朝你招手，身边放着两杯茶。|{flag(night_isolde): 隔着庭院，院长朝你微微点了一下头。|没人知道你昨晚在哪儿。也没人谢你。}}}{flag(ilse_taken): 黎明前，伊尔泽跟着灰衣人去了北方。|}\n# en: The lake glittering in spring sunshine, students skimming stones off the jetty, one student sitting a little apart on the shore.\nscene [3fe5a5] = 春日阳光下波光粼粼的湖，学生们在栈桥上打水漂，一个学生独自坐在稍远的岸边。\n# en: Go and sit with someone\nopt.left.label [5ba2e6] = 去陪陪谁\n# en: {flag(night_ilse): You put your feet in beside hers. It's freezing. She laughs, properly, for the first time you've ever heard.|{flag(night_maud): She pours you a cup without asking. \"Edmund would have liked you,\" she says, and that's all she says about it.|{flag(night_isolde): She beats you at chess that evening in nine moves, and lets you see her smile.|You find friends who never knew, and let them talk about nothing all afternoon. It's the best afternoon of the year.}}}\nopt.left.say.1 [7db3b7] = {flag(night_ilse): 你把脚伸进她旁边的水里。冷得要命。她笑了，真正地笑了，你第一次听见她这样笑。|{flag(night_maud): 她不问就给你倒了一杯。“埃德蒙会喜欢你的。”她说，关于这件事她就只说了这一句。|{flag(night_isolde): 那天晚上她九步就在棋盘上赢了你，还让你看见了她的笑。|你去找那些什么都不知道的朋友，听他们聊了一下午无关紧要的事。这是一年里最好的一个下午。}}}\n# en: Write it all down\nopt.right.label [c13862] = 全都写下来\n# en: You walk the shore alone and write down every word of the founders' wall before it fades. Nobody will ever read it. You need it to have happened somewhere.\nopt.right.say.1 [443a8a] = 你独自沿着湖岸走，趁创始者之墙上的每一个字还没褪去，把它们全写了下来。永远不会有人读它。你只是需要它在某处发生过。\n\n@event hs_aftermath_freed\n# en: Every Candle Out\ntitle [e4b25d] = 烛火尽灭\n# en: Every candle in Lumenhold went out at midnight and didn't come back until dawn. The lake is blue and empty and very deep. On your pillow there's a letter with a black-star seal: \"The families thank you. Your seat at our table is waiting.\" At breakfast the headmistress looks at you once, and then never again. {flag(ilse_taken): The room above the bakery is empty; Ilse went north before dawn.|}\ntext [f213f3] = 午夜时分，流明堡的每一支蜡烛都熄灭了，直到黎明才重新亮起。湖是蓝的，空的，深不见底。你枕头上有一封黑星封蜡的信：“诸家族感谢你。我们席上为你留着座位。”早餐时，院长看了你一眼，此后再也没看过你。{flag(ilse_taken): 面包房楼上的房间空了；黎明前伊尔泽去了北方。|}\n# en: An academy at dawn with every window dark, students on the lawns staring at an empty sky, a black-sealed letter in one student's hand.\nscene [4fbffd] = 黎明时分的学院，每扇窗都是黑的，学生们站在草坪上望着空荡荡的天空，一个学生手里拿着一封黑色封蜡的信。\n# en: Answer the letter\nopt.left.label [c83974] = 回信\n# en: They'd be glad of you.\nopt.left.hint [9280b9] = 他们会很高兴有你。\n# en: You write back, and a purse comes by the next barge, and after that a letter every month in that beautiful hand. They call you \"herald\". You find, a little more each time, that you answer to it.\nopt.left.say.1 [daba25] = 你回了信，下一班驳船就送来一个钱袋，此后每个月都有一封那样漂亮笔迹的信。他们称你为“使者”。你发现，每多一次，你就更习惯应这个称呼。\n# en: Burn it\nopt.right.label [162285] = 烧掉它\n# en: What's done stays done.\nopt.right.hint [ebca83] = 做过的事，就是做过了。\n# en: The letter burns blue. It doesn't change anything, and you knew it wouldn't, and you burn the next one too, and the one after that. They keep coming.\nopt.right.say.1 [86a907] = 信烧成了蓝色。这改变不了什么，你也知道改变不了，下一封你也烧了，再下一封也是。它们还是不断地来。\n\n@event hs_aftermath_fled\n# en: The Door Left Ajar\ntitle [8b2193] = 虚掩的门\n# en: The headmistress {flag(night_isolde): stayed at the door when you ran|went down after you ran}, and came back up at dawn with her hair gone white. The door is still ajar, she says, but it will hold, for now, with the Wardens sitting up beside it in shifts. She doesn't ask why you ran. That's worse. {flag(ilse_taken): Ilse went north before dawn.|}\ntext [45975e] = 院长{flag(night_isolde): 在你逃走时留在了门边|在你逃走后下去了}，黎明时上来，头发全白了。她说那扇门还虚掩着，但暂时撑得住，守望者们轮班坐在它旁边守着。她没有问你为什么跑。这更糟。{flag(ilse_taken): 黎明前，伊尔泽去了北方。|}\n# en: A tall woman with newly white hair standing at a window above a lake, a student in the doorway behind her, neither speaking.\nscene [cd8f2f] = 一个头发刚刚变白的高个子女人站在湖上方的窗前，一个学生站在她身后的门口，两人都不说话。\n# en: \"I'm sorry.\"\nopt.left.label [c7fcad] = “对不起。”\n# en: \"Everyone runs, the first time,\" she says. \"Most don't come back down. You may yet.\" She gives you a silver ring without a word, and a rota with your name on it, and you take both.\nopt.left.say.1 [4c29ee] = “第一次，谁都会跑，”她说，“大多数人不会再下去。你也许会。”她一言不发地给了你一枚银戒指，还有一张写着你名字的值班表，你两样都接下了。\n# en: Say nothing\nopt.right.label [d1151d] = 什么也不说\n# en: You stand in her doorway until she turns back to the window. Every night after that, you dream of the gap between the door and the frame, and the dark looking out of it, and what you'd say to it if you went back.\nopt.right.say.1 [71038c] = 你在她门口站着，直到她转回窗前。此后的每一夜，你都梦见门与门框之间的那道缝，梦见从缝里向外看的黑暗，梦见如果你回去会对它说什么。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/incidents.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/incidents.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event inc_fever_bill\n# en: A Fever and a Bill\ntitle [a86a8d] = 一场高烧，一张账单\n# en: You wake in the infirmary with a fever that makes the ceiling breathe. Sister Amaranth holds up a small blue bottle. \"This clears it by morning. It is also not free,\" she says. \"Or you can sweat it out, like your ancestors.\"\ntext [9f097a] = 你在医务室醒来，烧得连天花板都像在呼吸。阿玛兰斯修女举起一只蓝色小瓶。“这个能让你明早退烧。它也不是免费的，”她说，“要不然你就像你的祖先那样，硬扛过去。”\n# en: A pale student in an infirmary bed, a stern nurse holding out a small blue bottle and a folded bill.\nscene [b357c5] = 医务室病床上一个脸色苍白的学生，一位严厉的修女递来一只蓝色小瓶和一张折好的账单。\n# en: Buy the tonic\nopt.left.label [424c55] = 买下药剂\n# en: {~It tastes of tin and regret.|It tastes like a pond that has given up.} By morning the ceiling is a ceiling again, and your purse is noticeably lighter.\nopt.left.say.1 [b9ca12] = {~它尝起来像锡和悔恨。|它尝起来像一潭放弃了的池水。}到了早上，天花板又只是天花板了，你的钱袋却明显轻了一截。\n# en: Sweat it out\nopt.right.label [cf0e70] = 硬扛过去\n# en: Three days of damp sheets and strange dreams about stairs. You miss a week of notes, and come back feeling like a wrung-out dishcloth.\nopt.right.say.1 [fe076d] = 三天的湿床单，还有一堆关于楼梯的怪梦。你漏了一整周的笔记，回来时整个人像一块拧干的抹布。\n\n@event inc_broken_wand\n# en: Snapped on the Stairs\ntitle [fbb3ae] = 断在楼梯上\n# en: The moving stairs shift without warning. You catch yourself; your wand doesn't. It lies in two pieces on the landing, still fizzing at the break like a sulking firework.\ntext [e32b26] = 移动楼梯毫无预兆地挪了位置。你稳住了自己，你的魔杖却没有。它断成两截躺在平台上，断口还在嘶嘶冒火花，像一支赌气的烟花。\n# en: A student kneeling on a stone staircase, holding the two halves of a wand that still fizzes faintly at the break.\nscene [8c70b7] = 一个学生跪在石阶上，手里捧着断成两截的魔杖，断口仍微微冒着火花。\n# en: Have it properly rebound\nopt.left.label [97789e] = 找人好好修\n# en: The repairer in the village tuts, splints it with silver wire and charges you for every tut. It works better than before, which is somehow worse.\nopt.left.say.1 [d64a94] = 山下小镇的修理匠啧啧连声，用银丝把它绑好，每啧一声都算了钱。它比以前更好用了，这反倒更让人憋屈。\n# en: Bind it with string and hope\nopt.right.label [1dbf63] = 拿绳子绑上，听天由命\n# en: It holds. It spits a little when you cast, like a cat that forgives you but wants it noted.\nopt.right.say.1 [75ab3f] = 它撑住了。施法时会稍微啐一下，像一只原谅了你、但要你记住这笔账的猫。\n# en: It holds for three days, then sneezes a spell backwards. Your eyebrows grow back within the week; your marks take longer.\nopt.right.say.2 [76926c] = 它撑了三天，然后打了个喷嚏，把一道咒语倒着放了出去。你的眉毛一周内就长回来了，你的成绩可没那么快。\n\n@event inc_library_fine\n# en: The Overdue Notice\ntitle [716b08] = 逾期通知\n# en: A prefect in a red sash slides a notice across your desk. The library book you borrowed has come back late, damp and, somehow, with teeth marks. \"Damages,\" she says. \"Or you can argue. People do. It never goes well.\"\ntext [14a576] = 一位系着红绶带的级长把一张通知推到你桌上。你借的那本图书馆的书逾期归还，湿漉漉的，还不知怎么多了一圈牙印。“赔偿，”她说，“或者你也可以争辩。有人这么干过。从来没好结果。”\n# en: A prefect in a red sash holding out a long paper notice, a book with teeth marks on the desk between them.\nscene [618e44] = 一位系红绶带的级长举着一张长长的纸质通知，两人之间的桌上放着一本带牙印的书。\n# en: Pay the fine\nopt.left.label [4e47fa] = 交罚款\n# en: She stamps your receipt with great satisfaction. The book is reshelved in a cage, which feels like the right outcome for everyone.\nopt.left.say.1 [197c1b] = 她心满意足地在你的收据上盖了章。那本书被重新上架——关进了笼子里，这对大家来说似乎都是最好的结局。\n# en: Argue that the book bit first\nopt.right.label [ab548f] = 坚称是书先咬的\n# en: You produce the bite on your thumb as evidence. The prefect inspects it, then the book, and waives the fine. \"Fair,\" she says. \"It has a history.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [efd7a4] = 你亮出拇指上的咬痕作为证据。级长检查了它，又检查了那本书，免了罚款。“说得通，”她说，“它有前科。”\n# en: The prefect writes down every word you say, then doubles the fine for \"contempt of librarian\". You pay it, and lose an evening's revision to the demerit hearing besides.\nopt.right.say.2 [3fbf09] = 级长把你说的每个字都记了下来，然后以“藐视图书管理员”为由把罚款翻倍。你交了钱，还为记过听证搭进去一晚上的复习时间。\n\n@event inc_letter_home\n# en: Bad Weather at Home\ntitle [ae05d8] = 家里遭了风雨\n# en: A letter from home, written in a hurry. A storm took half the roof, and the repairs will cost more than anyone has. Nobody asks you for anything. That's how you know it's bad.\ntext [0fccb4] = 一封家信，字迹潦草。一场暴风雨掀掉了半边屋顶，修缮的花费比谁手头的钱都多。没有人向你开口要任何东西。正因如此，你知道情况很糟。\n# en: A student at a window reading a rain-spotted letter, a small pile of coins on the sill beside them.\nscene [e9924d] = 一个学生站在窗边读一封落了雨点的信，窗台上放着一小堆硬币。\n# en: Send crowns home\nopt.left.label [ca82d0] = 往家里寄克朗\n# en: You send everything you can spare and a little you can't. The reply comes a month later: a drawing of a roof, finished, and a pressed flower.\nopt.left.say.1 [bf78ca] = 你寄出了所有能省下的钱，还多寄了一点省不下的。一个月后回信来了：一张画着修好的屋顶的画，还有一朵压干的花。\n# en: Write back with love, not money\nopt.right.label [f26600] = 回信寄去爱，不寄钱\n# en: You write four pages and keep your purse. The reply is kind. It doesn't mention the roof. That night you lie awake and listen to the rain.\nopt.right.say.1 [75b549] = 你写了四页信，钱袋没动。回信很温柔，只字未提屋顶。那晚你躺着睡不着，听了一夜的雨。\n\n@event inc_stolen_purse\n# en: Market Day Fingers\ntitle [597745] = 集市日的快手\n# en: Somewhere between the cheese stall and the man selling enchanted socks, your purse stops being yours. You catch a grey hood slipping between the stalls, already three stalls ahead.\ntext [2d2565] = 在奶酪摊和那个卖魔法袜子的男人之间的某处，你的钱袋不再属于你了。你瞥见一顶灰色兜帽在摊位间溜走，已经领先你三个摊位。\n# en: A crowded village square on market day, a figure in a grey hood slipping away between the stalls with a small purse.\nscene [50e0d7] = 集市日拥挤的小镇广场上，一个戴灰兜帽的身影拿着一只小钱袋从摊位间溜走。\n# en: Run after the hood\nopt.left.label [3e6b9b] = 追那顶兜帽\n# en: You corner the thief behind the bakery. The purse comes back a little lighter; they'd already spent some on a bun. You let them keep the bun.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a1779] = 你在面包房后面堵住了小偷。钱袋还回来时轻了一点——他们已经拿去买了个面包。你让他们留着那个面包。\n# en: You trip over a goose, then a second goose. When you get up, the hood is gone, and so are your crowns.\nopt.left.say.2 [52dac0] = 你被一只鹅绊倒，接着又是第二只鹅。等你爬起来，兜帽不见了，你的克朗也不见了。\n# en: Let it go\nopt.right.label [7a5d2f] = 算了吧\n# en: You tell yourself it's only money. You tell yourself that all the way back up the hill, and it never quite takes.\nopt.right.say.1 [78bd08] = 你告诉自己，不过是钱而已。你一路走回山上都这么告诉自己，可始终没能真的说服自己。\n\n@event inc_hiccuping_robe\n# en: The Robe That Hiccups\ntitle [2955d6] = 会打嗝的长袍\n# en: A cauldron two benches down boils over, and a whole batch of Draught of Unending Hiccups soaks your best robe. The stain fades by supper. The hiccups don't. Every few minutes, the robe jumps.\ntext [291843] = 隔着两张实验台的一口坩埚沸溢了，一整锅“无尽打嗝药剂”泼透了你最好的长袍。污渍到晚饭时就褪了，打嗝却没停。每隔几分钟，长袍就要跳一下。\n# en: A student in potion-stained robes jolting mid-step as their robe gives a visible hiccup, classmates hiding smiles.\nscene [26b228] = 一个穿着沾满药渍长袍的学生走到一半猛地一颤，长袍明显打了个嗝，同学们憋着笑。\n# en: Buy a new robe\nopt.left.label [ce85a0] = 买件新长袍\n# en: The tailor in the village fits you in an afternoon and charges you for a week. The old robe now hiccups quietly in the bottom of your trunk.\nopt.left.say.1 [0564c8] = 山下小镇的裁缝一个下午就给你量好做好，却收了一周的工钱。旧长袍如今在你箱底悄悄地打着嗝。\n# en: Wear it with dignity\nopt.right.label [1a8893] = 体面地穿着它\n# en: {~It hiccups through a lecture on silence.|It hiccups through the headmistress's announcements.} Someone starts a tally on the common-room wall. You are briefly famous for the wrong reasons.\nopt.right.say.1 [20fdad] = {~它在一堂讲沉默的课上一路打嗝。|它在院长讲话时一路打嗝。}有人在公共休息室的墙上开始记次数。你短暂地出了名，原因却不太对。\n\n@event patron_tournament_purse\n# en: A Patron's Purse\ntitle [21cf49] = 赞助人的奖金袋\n# en: The Spring Tournament committee has a velvet cushion, a very thin prize purse and a list of patrons with a lot of room left on it. Patrons are read out before the first bout, in a loud voice, to the whole academy.\ntext [eebe25] = 春季锦标赛筹委会有一块天鹅绒垫子、一只瘪瘪的奖金袋，还有一份空着大片位置的赞助人名单。第一场比赛前，赞助人的名字会被高声念给全学院听。\n# en: A festival committee table on a lawn, a thin purse on a velvet cushion and a list of patrons with plenty of space left.\nscene [614979] = 草坪上一张节庆筹委会的桌子，天鹅绒垫子上放着一只瘪瘪的钱袋，旁边一份还空着很多位置的赞助人名单。\n# en: Fund the champion's purse\nopt.left.label [151eb5] = 包下冠军奖金\n# en: {~Your name is read out first, and mispronounced only slightly.|Your name rings across the lawn before the first bout. Two first-years bow to you, unsure why.} The champion thanks you from the podium.\nopt.left.say.1 [ce25e1] = {~你的名字第一个被念出来，只念错了一点点。|第一场比赛前，你的名字响彻草坪。两个一年级生朝你鞠躬，也不知为什么。}冠军在领奖台上向你致谢。\n# en: Cheer from the stands instead\nopt.right.label [cf44f8] = 改在看台上加油\n# en: Other names are read out, among them a professor's cat and \"a well-wisher\". You cheer for all of them, and loudest for the champion.\nopt.right.say.1 [083b9f] = 念到了别人的名字，其中有一位教授的猫和“一位好心人”。你为他们每一个欢呼，为冠军喊得最响。\n\n@event patron_ledger_print\n# en: Paper for the Press\ntitle [f32e02] = 印刷机要纸\n# en: Nessa Quill corners you by the library stairs, holding an empty paper tray like evidence. \"Special edition. Biggest story of the term. No paper. You,\" she says, pointing, \"look solvent. Patrons get their name on the masthead.\"\ntext [2bc86d] = 内莎·奎尔在图书馆楼梯边把你堵住，举着一只空纸盘，像举着证物。“特刊。本学期最大新闻。没纸了。你，”她指着你说，“看起来有钱。赞助人能上报头。”\n# en: A cramped office under a staircase, a groaning printing press, and a young editor holding out an empty paper tray.\nscene [a206ca] = 楼梯下一间狭小的办公室，一台呻吟着的印刷机，一位年轻的编辑举着一只空纸盘。\n# en: Pay for the whole print run\nopt.left.label [451de8] = 包下整期印刷\n# en: \"Headline: generous student saves free press,\" Nessa says, already writing it. The masthead lists you in tiny capitals, which she says is the most honour anyone gets.\nopt.left.say.1 [079497] = “头条：慷慨学生拯救自由报业，”内莎说着，已经开始写了。报头上用极小的字印着你的名字，她说这已是任何人能得到的最高荣誉。\n# en: Help her fold the edition instead\nopt.right.label [5a2823] = 改为帮她折报纸\n# en: You'd rather give her your time than your crowns. Nessa scrounges paper from the Ledger's scrap bin, and you fold until midnight, fingers grey with ink. Nessa credits you on the back page as \"the folding department\".\nopt.right.say.1 [177253] = 你宁愿给她时间，也不想给她克朗。内莎从《流明纪事报》的废纸箱里搜罗出纸来，你一直折到午夜，手指被油墨染得灰扑扑的。内莎在报纸背面给你署了名：“折页部”。\n\n@event donate_greenhouse_glass\n# en: The Broken Panes\ntitle [cd7452] = 碎掉的玻璃\n# en: Last night's hail went through the greenhouse glass like a hammer through toffee. A collection tin hangs on the gate with a hand-lettered sign: every pane paid for keeps the mandrakes from catching cold.\ntext [fa105f] = 昨夜的冰雹砸穿温室的玻璃，就像锤子砸进太妃糖。大门上挂着一只募捐罐和一块手写的牌子：每捐一块玻璃，曼德拉草就少一分着凉的危险。\n# en: A collection tin on a garden gate, broken glass glittering in the grass behind it and steam escaping from a damaged greenhouse.\nscene [45c440] = 花园大门上挂着一只募捐罐，后面草地上碎玻璃闪闪发光，一座破损的温室正往外冒着热气。\n# en: Pay for a whole pane\nopt.left.label [14b4c4] = 捐一整块玻璃\n# en: A gardener with mud to the elbows insists on showing you your pane. Inside it is warm, green and loud with opinions. You are welcome, she says, any morning.\nopt.left.say.1 [96b7a3] = 一位泥巴糊到手肘的园丁非要带你去看“你的”那块玻璃。里面温暖、葱绿，满是各种意见。她说，你哪天早上来都欢迎。\n# en: Walk on\nopt.right.label [383259] = 径直走过\n# en: You walk past the tin. Behind the gate, something small and leafy sneezes. You feel, obscurely, that you've been judged.\nopt.right.say.1 [8ff62f] = 你从募捐罐旁走过。大门后，有个小小的、长着叶子的东西打了个喷嚏。你隐约觉得自己被评判了。\n\n@event donate_duel_floor\n# en: The Scorched Floor\ntitle [47b123] = 烧焦的地板\n# en: A duellist with a bandaged hand rattles a box at the foot of the east stair. \"The hall floor,\" he says. \"Someone set it on fire again. Every board you buy, we carve your initials on the underside. Honour, and splinters.\"\ntext [d8070e] = 一个手上缠着绷带的决斗者在东楼梯脚下摇着一只箱子。“大厅的地板，”他说，“又有人把它点着了。你买一块板，我们就把你的姓名缩写刻在背面。荣誉，外加木刺。”\n# en: A sweaty duellist with a collection box at the foot of a stair, scorched floorboards stacked behind them.\nscene [ea8375] = 一个满头大汗的决斗者在楼梯脚下捧着募捐箱，身后堆着烧焦的地板。\n# en: Buy a row of floorboards\nopt.left.label [a3aa26] = 买下一整排地板\n# en: He drags you upstairs to see. The hall smells of smoke and wax, and the practice dummies glare at you. Your initials go on the underside of the board by the door.\nopt.left.say.1 [58bb9f] = 他拽着你上楼去看。大厅里一股烟和蜡的味道，练习假人们瞪着你。你的姓名缩写刻在了门边那块板的背面。\n# en: Wish him luck with the bandage\nopt.right.label [8e4b8e] = 祝他绷带早日拆掉\n# en: He thanks you with exaggerated courtesy and goes back to rattling. Somewhere above, steel rings on steel, and nobody invites you up.\nopt.right.say.1 [c52343] = 他夸张地向你行礼致谢，又回去接着摇箱子。楼上某处传来钢铁相击的声音，没有人邀请你上去。\n\n@event xchg_arrival\n# en: Guests in Green\ntitle [d9ca04] = 身着绿袍的客人\n# en: Four students in Thornwick green arrive for a term, with trunks and very polite smiles. Within an hour every portrait in the entrance hall is winking at the wrong people. A prefect looks around for someone, anyone, to show them the castle.\ntext [5855c8] = 四个穿着荆木绿袍的学生来这里待一个学期，带着箱子和非常客气的笑容。不到一个小时，门厅里每一幅肖像都在冲错的人眨眼。一位级长四处张望，想找个人——随便谁——带他们参观城堡。\n# en: Four students in green Thornwick robes standing in a grand entrance hall, the portraits on the walls all turned to stare at them.\nscene [4447a8] = 四个穿绿色荆木长袍的学生站在宏伟的门厅里，墙上的肖像全都转过头盯着他们。\n# en: Offer to show them round\nopt.left.label [52352b] = 主动带他们参观\n# en: You give the full tour, moving stairs included. By the end the boy with the sketchbook admits Lumenhold's towers are \"not as crooked as reported\".\nopt.left.say.1 [110d38] = 你带他们走完了全程，移动楼梯也算在内。最后那个拿速写本的男生承认，流明堡的塔“没有传闻中那么歪”。\n# en: Halfway round, they convince you a wall is a door. It isn't. They are very sorry, and laughing too hard to say so.\nopt.left.say.2 [5cd71f] = 走到一半，他们让你相信一面墙是一扇门。它不是。他们非常抱歉，只是笑得太厉害，说不出口。\n# en: Watch them from a distance\nopt.right.label [e05dd0] = 远远地观察他们\n# en: You watch them all week. They watch back. You learn that the quiet one counts doorways, and that they know you're watching.\nopt.right.say.1 [175ece] = 你观察了他们整整一周。他们也观察你。你发现那个安静的人会数门洞，也发现他们知道你在看。\n\n@event xchg_ink_cuffs\n# en: The Ink-Stained Cuffs\ntitle [a6af14] = 沾墨的袖口\n# en: Late in the library, you find the Thornwick girl with the ink-stained cuffs copying from a chained book she shouldn't have reached. She sees you see her. \"It's only history,\" she says. \"Unless you want it to be a scandal.\"\ntext [2833d3] = 深夜的图书馆里，你撞见那个袖口沾着墨迹的荆木女生正在抄一本她本不该够得着的锁链书。她看见你看见了她。“只是历史而已，”她说，“除非你想让它变成丑闻。”\n# en: A library at dusk, a student in green robes with ink-stained cuffs copying from a book chained to a desk.\nscene [00e6b0] = 黄昏的图书馆，一个袖口沾墨的绿袍学生正从一本锁在桌上的书里抄写。\n# en: Report her to the librarian\nopt.left.label [c73db6] = 向管理员举报她\n# en: The librarian thanks you, coldly. The Thornwick girl is confined to the guest wing for a week. She nods to you at dinner afterwards, as if you had both played well.\nopt.left.say.1 [66098b] = 图书管理员冷冷地向你道了谢。那个荆木女生被禁足在客房区一周。之后的晚饭上她朝你点点头，好像你们俩都下了一手好棋。\n# en: Keep quiet, for a favour\nopt.right.label [cca079] = 保持沉默，换个人情\n# en: She tears out the page she copied and hands you the other half of her notes instead. \"We're even,\" she says. You're fairly sure you aren't.\nopt.right.say.1 [ee5c42] = 她撕下抄好的那页，转而把自己笔记的另一半递给你。“我们扯平了，”她说。你很确定并没有。\n\n@event xchg_farewell\n# en: A Thornwick Farewell\ntitle [7e24b5] = 荆木的告别\n# en: The Thornwick students leave on today's barge. The boy with the sketchbook finds you on the jetty and holds out his scarf. \"It's a custom,\" he says. \"Probably. I've just decided.\" Behind him his friends pretend not to listen.\ntext [c9e163] = 荆木的学生们今天乘驳船离开。拿速写本的男生在栈桥上找到你，递出他的围巾。“这是个习俗，”他说，“大概吧。我刚决定的。”他身后的朋友们假装没在听。\n# en: Students in green and blue robes on a jetty at dawn, a barge waiting, one of them holding out a striped scarf.\nscene [75f77e] = 黎明时分的栈桥上，穿绿袍和蓝袍的学生们站着，一艘驳船在等，其中一人递出一条条纹围巾。\n# en: Swap scarves\nopt.left.label [1fdbff] = 交换围巾\n# en: You trade scarves on the jetty. Someone from your own house mutters \"traitor\" at breakfast. You wear it anyway, and get a letter from across the hills by the end of the month.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d5b9e] = 你们在栈桥上交换了围巾。早饭时，你自己学院的某人嘀咕了一句“叛徒”。你照样围着它，月底前还收到了一封翻山而来的信。\n# en: Challenge him to one last duel\nopt.right.label [07d107] = 邀他最后决斗一场\n# en: You win, narrowly, on the wet boards. He laughs, bows, and sketches you mid-spell on the barge as it pulls away. The sketch arrives by post, flattering.\nopt.right.say.1 [debf60] = 你在湿漉漉的木板上险胜。他大笑着鞠了一躬，驳船离岸时还在给施法中的你画速写。那张速写后来寄到了，画得相当美化。\n# en: He wins with an illusion of a second you. You both end up in the lake. It is, somehow, the best goodbye you've had.\nopt.right.say.2 [cbf2ca] = 他用一个“第二个你”的幻象赢了。你们俩最后都掉进了湖里。不知怎么，这竟是你经历过最好的一次告别。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/intro.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/intro.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event intro_welcome\n# en: The Welcome\ntitle [ac6c10] = 欢迎仪式\n# en: Floating candles, two hundred first-years trying not to look up. Headmistress Marrow speaks for four minutes: four years of twelve moons, a name that travels, and a castle that remembers who you were in it. \"Before you choose your courses,\" she ends, \"the prefects will show you how things work here. Those who think they know already may go straight to the registrar.\"\ntext [e809d0] = 漂浮的蜡烛，两百个一年级新生努力不抬头看。马罗院长讲了四分钟：四年，每年十二个月，一个会传开的名字，还有一座会记住你在这里是什么样的人的城堡。“在你们选课之前，”她最后说，“级长们会带你们看看这里的规矩。自认为已经知道的人，可以直接去找教务。”\n# en: The Great Hall full of new first-years under floating candles, a tall headmistress at the lectern.\nscene [a752a8] = 大礼堂里挤满了新来的一年级生，头顶飘着蜡烛，高个子的院长站在讲台前。\n# en: Skip the orientation\nopt.left.label [7bb86b] = 跳过入学引导\n# en: You slip out ahead of the crowd to find the registrar's list. Whatever the castle has to teach you, you'll learn it the hard way. (The safety talk is compulsory: you'll still have that.)\nopt.left.say.1 [c27814] = 你赶在人群前头溜出去找教务的名单。城堡要教你的东西，你打算用吃苦头的方式学。（安全课是必修的：那个你还是得上。）\n# en: Stay for the orientation\nopt.right.label [b7a0f6] = 留下参加引导\n# en: You stay. The hall empties toward the prefects, and the headmistress comes down from the lectern, straight toward you.\nopt.right.say.1 [88c52b] = 你留了下来。大厅里的人朝级长们散去，院长从讲台上走下来，径直朝你走来。\n\n@event intro_what_is_just\n# en: What Is Just?\ntitle [ebf19a] = 何为公正？\n# en: Headmistress Marrow stops in front of you, notebook open, as if she'd been looking for you in particular. \"One question before the prefects have you,\" she says. \"I ask it of everyone. Very few remember. What do you think is just?\"\ntext [f37f9c] = 马罗院长在你面前停下，笔记本摊开着，好像特意在找你。“在级长们把你领走之前，问一个问题，”她说，“我问每一个人。记得的人很少。你觉得什么是公正？”\n# en: A tall headmistress with a small notebook stopping in front of one first-year in an emptying hall, candles guttering overhead.\nscene [bd9664] = 渐空的大厅里，一位高个子的院长拿着小笔记本，停在一个一年级生面前，头顶的蜡烛摇曳将熄。\n# en: \"Mercy.\"\nopt.mercy.label [715ccb] = “仁慈。”\n# en: Giving people more than they've earned.\nopt.mercy.hint [18f8eb] = 给人超过他们应得的。\n# en: \"Mercy,\" she repeats, and writes it down. \"Expensive. Most people who say it haven't been asked to pay for it yet.\" She closes the notebook on her finger. \"Ask me again in four years.\"\nopt.mercy.say.1 [87a6e2] = “仁慈，”她重复一遍，写了下来。“代价高昂。说这话的人大多还没被要求付账。”她用手指夹住笔记本合上。“四年后再问我一次。”\n# en: \"Fairness.\"\nopt.fair.label [a659a4] = “公平。”\n# en: One rule, the same for everyone.\nopt.fair.hint [0a96a2] = 一条规矩，对谁都一样。\n# en: \"Fairness,\" she repeats, and writes it down. \"The same rule for the duke and the pickpocket. Harder than it sounds, when you know one of them.\" She closes the notebook on her finger. \"Ask me again in four years.\"\nopt.fair.say.1 [73d120] = “公平，”她重复一遍，写了下来。“公爵和扒手守同一条规矩。等你认识其中一个，就知道这比听上去难。”她用手指夹住笔记本合上。“四年后再问我一次。”\n# en: \"Keeping your own safe.\"\nopt.strong.label [ae02c4] = “护住自己人。”\n# en: Whatever it takes, for the people who are yours.\nopt.strong.hint [02410d] = 为了属于你的人，不惜一切。\n# en: \"Keeping your own safe,\" she repeats, and writes it down. \"An honest answer. Most people dress it up.\" She closes the notebook on her finger. \"Ask me again in four years. I'd like to know who you decided was yours.\"\nopt.strong.say.1 [4caa6c] = “护住自己人，”她重复一遍，写了下来。“很诚实的回答。大多数人会粉饰一番。”她用手指夹住笔记本合上。“四年后再问我一次。我想知道你最后认定谁是你的人。”\n\n@event intro_tour\n# en: The Prefect's Tour\ntitle [5bfdbd] = 级长带你参观\n# en: A burly prefect in a red sash gathers the first-years with a ledger. \"Bram Hollis. Prefect. Tour. Each day you get a few free hours, and you spend them on the map: one place an hour. Some doors open only once you've earned them. Some never open for you. Keep up.\"\ntext [d68b57] = 一个魁梧的、系红肩带的级长拿着账本召集一年级生。“布拉姆·霍利斯。级长。参观。每天你有几个空闲时段，在地图上花掉它们：一个时段去一个地方。有些门要等你配得上才会开。有些门永远不会为你开。跟上。”\n# en: A prefect in a red sash leading a straggle of first-years through a castle corridor, holding up a ledger like a flag.\nscene [99ab49] = 一个系红肩带的级长领着一串一年级生穿过城堡走廊，像举旗一样举着账本。\n# en: Skip the tour\nopt.left.label [e05505] = 不参观了\n# en: You slip away and find your own way round. You get lost twice and find the kitchens once, which is a fair trade.\nopt.left.say.1 [d82ebc] = 你溜走了，自己摸路。迷路两次，找到厨房一次，挺划算。\n# en: Follow him round\nopt.right.label [3e4916] = 跟着他转\n# en: Bram walks you past every door and names its rules. At a blank stretch of wall, a boy with a lockpick behind his ear winks at you, as if the wall were a joke you'd both get later.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f75b9] = 布拉姆领你走过每一扇门，说出每一扇门的规矩。在一段光秃秃的墙前，一个耳后夹着撬锁针的男孩冲你眨了眨眼，好像这堵墙是个你们俩以后都会懂的笑话。\n\n@event intro_places\n# en: Where Things Are\ntitle [168cb7] = 东西都在哪儿\n# en: Bram reads the castle to you off his ledger. \"Job Board, by the stairs: work and wages. Library: revision tables before every exam. Infirmary, on the Alchemy floor: Sister Amaranth, for when you break something, yourself included. The village, down the hill: robes and wands on market day, tea at Mabel's, tonics at Pell's.\" He snaps the ledger shut. \"Questions? No. Good.\"\ntext [dceb2a] = 布拉姆照着账本把城堡念给你听。“招工告示板，在楼梯边：活儿和工钱。图书馆：每次考试前都摆好复习桌。医务室，在炼金那层：阿玛兰斯修女，摔坏了什么都找她，包括你自己。村子，在山下：集市日买长袍和魔杖，梅布尔茶馆喝茶，佩尔父女药铺买补剂。”他啪地合上账本。“有问题吗？没有。很好。”\n# en: A prefect reading from a ledger at the top of a castle stair, pointing down corridors with his quill, first-years craning to follow.\nscene [b6f58c] = 城堡楼梯顶上，一个级长照着账本念，用羽毛笔朝各条走廊指点，一年级生伸长脖子跟着看。\n# en: \"No questions.\"\nopt.left.label [e3ca6b] = “没有问题。”\n# en: \"Excellent,\" says Bram, and means it. He leaves you at the foot of the stairs with a map in your head and a strong sense of having been processed.\nopt.left.say.1 [90461f] = “好极了，”布拉姆说，是真心的。他把你丢在楼梯底下，脑子里多了张地图，还有一种被办完了手续的强烈感觉。\n# en: Ask what he left out\nopt.right.label [23dbe0] = 问他漏了什么\n# en: He looks pained. \"The Gilded Newt, in the village, after dark: supper, cider and people you shouldn't be seen with. The Lakeshore, for thinking. That's all.\" He writes something in the ledger. It might be your name.\nopt.right.say.1 [bbe3ed] = 他一脸痛苦。“镀金蝾螈酒馆，在村里，天黑以后：晚饭、苹果酒，还有你不该被人看见跟他们在一起的人。湖岸，想事情用的。就这些。”他在账本上写了点什么。可能是你的名字。\n\n@event intro_wrong_tower\n# en: The Wrong Tower\ntitle [658cc8] = 走错的塔楼\n# en: Your trunk has gone to the wrong tower. The house steward has a list, and the list says it hasn't. \"If it's on the list,\" she says, \"it's where it should be.\" Behind her, through a door, you can see your trunk with your name on it.\ntext [2251e2] = 你的箱子被送到了错的塔楼。舍监手里有一张名单，名单上说没送错。“名单上怎么写，东西就在哪儿，”她说。在她身后，透过一扇门，你能看见写着你名字的箱子。\n# en: A harassed house steward behind a desk heaped with lists, a student's trunk visible through a doorway in the wrong tower.\nscene [91c6b6] = 一位手忙脚乱的舍监坐在堆满名单的桌后，透过门口能看见错的塔楼里有一个学生的箱子。\n# en: Carry it over yourself\nopt.left.label [a8f8c6] = 自己搬过去\n# en: You haul it across two bridges and up ninety stairs. The steward ticks something on the list as you pass, satisfied that it was always right.\nopt.left.say.1 [246fa2] = 你拖着它过了两座桥，爬了九十级台阶。你经过时，舍监在名单上打了个勾，满意地认为它一直都是对的。\n# en: Talk her round\nopt.right.label [53212c] = 说服她\n# en: You ask to see the list. Then the line. Then the tower on the line. The steward goes pink, corrects it in red ink and sends two porters. \"Clerical,\" she says.\nopt.right.say.1 [f54cdc] = 你要求看名单。然后看那一行。然后看那一行写的是哪座塔。舍监脸红了，用红墨水改过来，派了两个搬运工。“笔误，”她说。\n# en: She won't change the list, but she lends you a porter \"as a courtesy\". The porter and the list never speak again.\nopt.right.say.2 [2de114] = 她不肯改名单，但“出于礼貌”借给你一个搬运工。搬运工和名单从此再没说过话。\n# en: She has an answer for everything, most of it the list. You carry the trunk yourself in the end, but you've learned how she argues, which is worth a tower.\nopt.right.say.3 [d9aebe] = 她什么都有理由，大部分理由就是那张名单。最后你还是自己搬了箱子，但你学会了她怎么争论，这值一座塔。\n\n@event intro_lunch\n# en: The Lunch Table\ntitle [a7afb4] = 午餐桌\n# en: Lunch in the Great Hall, under the gallery where the refectory tables run. You can sit anywhere. At noon people find you, or you find them; either way, who you sit with tends to become who you know.\ntext [b43e18] = 大礼堂里的午餐，膳堂的长桌摆在楼廊底下。你可以随便坐。中午，别人会找到你，或者你找到别人；不管怎样，你跟谁坐在一起，往往就会认识谁。\n# en: Long refectory tables at noon, first-years spread along the benches, one empty place beside a familiar face.\nscene [319811] = 中午的膳堂长桌，一年级生沿着长凳散坐，一张熟悉的脸旁边空着一个位子。\n# en: Eat with whoever's there\nopt.left.label [99acdb] = 随便跟旁边的人吃\n# en: You squeeze in between two strangers who are arguing about soup. By pudding they're not strangers, quite.\nopt.left.say.1 [5284d5] = 你挤进两个正为汤争论的陌生人中间。吃到布丁时，他们已经不太算陌生人了。\n# en: Sit with {friend}\nopt.right.label [c9b8a7] = 和{friend}坐在一起\n# en: {friend} shifts along the bench to make room. You talk about nothing much, which is how most friendships start.\nopt.right.say.1 [2dae0b] = {friend}在长凳上挪了挪给你让位子。你们聊了些没什么要紧的事，大多数友谊就是这么开始的。\n\n@event intro_lunch_alone\n# en: The Lunch Table\ntitle [a7afb4] = 午餐桌\n# en: Lunch in the Great Hall, under the gallery where the refectory tables run. You can sit anywhere, and you don't know anyone yet. At noon people find you, or you find them; either way, who you sit with tends to become who you know.\ntext [0a4985] = 大礼堂里的午餐，膳堂的长桌摆在楼廊底下。你可以随便坐，而你还谁都不认识。中午，别人会找到你，或者你找到别人；不管怎样，你跟谁坐在一起，往往就会认识谁。\n# en: Long refectory tables at noon, first-years spread along the benches, a student with a tray looking for a place.\nscene [93647d] = 正午膳堂的长桌，一年级生沿长凳散坐，一个端着餐盘的学生在找位子。\n# en: Eat with whoever's there\nopt.left.label [99acdb] = 随便跟旁边的人吃\n# en: You squeeze in between two strangers who are arguing about soup. By pudding they're not strangers, quite.\nopt.left.say.1 [5284d5] = 你挤进两个正为汤争论的陌生人中间。吃到布丁时，他们已经不太算陌生人了。\n# en: Sit at the end and listen\nopt.right.label [981f2a] = 坐到桌尾听着\n# en: You take the end of a bench and listen. By the end of the meal you know who runs the Ledger, who duels, who talks to plants, and that the girl with the floating quill will find you whether you like it or not.\nopt.right.say.1 [27bcdf] = 你在长凳尽头坐下，听着。一顿饭吃完，你已经知道谁在办《纪事报》、谁决斗、谁跟植物说话，还知道那个羽毛笔浮在肩头的女孩迟早会找上你，不管你愿不愿意。\n\n@event intro_vey_tea\n# en: Tea That Isn't There\ntitle [52dadd] = 不存在的茶\n# en: Professor Vey stops you in a corridor and offers you a cup of tea. There is no cup. There is no tea. She waits to see what you'll do. \"Every attempt has its odds,\" she says pleasantly. \"Skill moves them. Luck moves them less. Drink up.\"\ntext [747e3f] = 维伊教授在走廊里拦住你，请你喝一杯茶。没有杯子。没有茶。她等着看你怎么做。“每次尝试都有它的胜算，”她和气地说，“本事能改变胜算。运气改变得少一点。喝吧。”\n# en: A corridor alcove, a sharp-eyed professor holding out an empty hand as if it held a teacup, a student hesitating.\nscene [8c672c] = 走廊的壁龛里，一位目光锐利的教授伸着一只空手，好像端着茶杯，一个学生犹豫着。\n# en: Decline the tea\nopt.left.label [5a06b4] = 谢绝这杯茶\n# en: \"No, thank you,\" you say. Vey sighs, drinks the tea that isn't there, and walks on. You feel as though you've failed something small.\nopt.left.say.1 [c1f092] = “不了，谢谢，”你说。维伊叹了口气，喝掉了那杯不存在的茶，走开了。你觉得自己好像没通过一件小事。\n# en: Pretend to drink it\nopt.right.label [603f5b] = 假装喝下去\n# en: You sip, wince at the heat and compliment the blend. Vey laughs, delighted, and writes something down. \"Points,\" she says. \"Nobody compliments the blend.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [bb798d] = 你抿了一口，被烫得皱眉，夸了夸茶的配方。维伊高兴地笑了，写下了什么。“得分，”她说，“从没人夸过配方。”\n# en: You drink it convincingly and then try to hand back the cup. There is no cup. Vey smiles. \"Half points,\" she says.\nopt.right.say.2 [f5309d] = 你喝得很像样，然后想把杯子递回去。没有杯子。维伊笑了。“半分，”她说。\n# en: You laugh halfway through the sip. Vey sighs, theatrically. \"Most do,\" she says. \"I keep score, you know. Everyone starts at nought.\"\nopt.right.say.3 [40b8ab] = 你喝到一半笑了出来。维伊夸张地叹了口气。“大多数人都这样，”她说，“我会记分的，你知道吧。每个人都从零开始。”\n\n@event intro_room\n# en: Your Room\ntitle [d2c355] = 你的房间\n# en: Your room in the tower: a bed, a trunk, a window with half the lake in it. On the pillow, a note from the bursar, folded twice.\ntext [be66cd] = 你在塔楼里的房间：一张床，一只箱子，一扇装着半个湖的窗。枕头上有一张总务写的字条，折了两折。\n# en: A narrow tower room with a bed, a trunk at its foot and a window full of lake, a folded note on the pillow.\nscene [efd3ce] = 一间窄窄的塔楼房间，一张床，床脚一只箱子，窗外满是湖水，枕头上一张折好的字条。\n# en: Unpack later\nopt.left.label [5dce1d] = 晚点再收拾\n# en: You fall onto the bed in your robes and sleep like a dropped stone. The note is still under your cheek in the morning.\nopt.left.say.1 [053903] = 你穿着袍子倒在床上，睡得像块落地的石头。第二天早上，字条还压在你脸颊下。\n# en: Read the bursar's note\nopt.right.label [aec687] = 读总务的字条\n# en: Board is paid each month, it says, or you go hungry. Sleep mends the body and the mood; a bad night shows. Mana comes back on its own. And please do not feed the gargoyles.\nopt.right.say.1 [fc0300] = 上面说，伙食费每月交，不交就挨饿。睡眠能修复身体和心情；没睡好会看出来。法力会自己回来。另外请不要喂石像鬼。\n\n@event intro_practice_circle\n# en: The Practice Circle\ntitle [635caf] = 练习圈\n# en: Professor Thorne has chalked a circle and stood a straw mannequin in it. \"Every first-year fights it once,\" he says. \"It moves first, and it shows you what it means to do. Watch it. Lose to it if you must. Losing well is the only thing worth teaching.\"\ntext [dd8433] = 索恩教授用粉笔画了一个圈，在里面立了一个稻草假人。“每个一年级生都要跟它打一次，”他说，“它先出招，而且会让你看到它想干什么。看着它。必要的话就输给它。输得漂亮，是唯一值得教的东西。”\n# en: A chalk circle on a scorched duelling-hall floor, a straw mannequin standing in it, a grizzled professor watching from the edge.\nscene [f32b3e] = 焦黑的决斗厅地板上画着粉笔圈，圈里立着一个稻草假人，一位头发花白的教授在圈边看着。\n# en: Watch from the bench\nopt.left.label [ff2b65] = 在长椅上看\n# en: You watch three first-years go down to the mannequin, then one beat it. Thorne says the same thing to all four: \"Good. Again.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [5db4f2] = 你看着三个一年级生倒在假人面前，然后一个打赢了。索恩对四个人说的都是同一句话：“好。再来。”\n# en: Step into the circle\nopt.right.label [7ce474] = 走进圈里\n# en: The mannequin topples in a puff of straw. Thorne nods. \"Now lose one sometime,\" he says. \"Then you'll know what you're doing.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [9491b1] = 假人扑的一声倒下，扬起一阵稻草。索恩点点头。“找时间输一场，”他说，“那你才知道自己在干什么。”\n# en: You trade blows until the bell. Thorne looks pleased. \"A draw is a fight you both learned from,\" he says.\nopt.right.say.2 [e70a65] = 你们来回交手直到下课铃响。索恩看起来很满意。“平局就是两边都学到东西的一场打斗，”他说。\n# en: The mannequin wins, which is humiliating and educational. Thorne helps you up. \"I lost to a storm once,\" he says. \"Badly. Losing well is the whole art.\"\nopt.right.say.3 [522c58] = 假人赢了，这既丢脸又有教育意义。索恩把你扶起来。“我输给过一场风暴，”他说，“输得很惨。输得漂亮，就是全部的本事。”\n\n@event intro_kit\n# en: What You Carry\ntitle [14863b] = 随身之物\n# en: Afterwards Thorne taps your borrowed wand with one finger. \"Held together with hope,\" he says. \"What you carry matters in a fight, and only there: a wand, a robe, one thing in your pocket. What you own opens other doors. Keep your kit in order.\"\ntext [56c4a3] = 之后，索恩用一根手指敲了敲你借来的魔杖。“全靠希望撑着，”他说，“你带的东西在打斗里才有用，也只在那里有用：一根魔杖，一件长袍，口袋里的一样东西。你拥有的东西会打开别的门。把你的行装打理好。”\n# en: A grizzled professor tapping a student's battered borrowed wand, a robe and a small pouch laid out on a bench.\nscene [e32613] = 一位头发花白的教授敲着学生那根破旧的借用魔杖，长凳上摆着一件长袍和一个小袋子。\n# en: Later\nopt.left.label [f62b49] = 以后再说\n# en: You nod and put the wand away. It sparks once, sideways, as if it had heard.\nopt.left.say.1 [a6841a] = 你点点头，把魔杖收起来。它往旁边冒了一下火花，好像听见了。\n# en: Show me\nopt.right.label [4e9f5a] = 给我看看\n# en: He shows you: what you wear only counts when you fight; what you own lets you do things on the right day, like a spell does. Your kit is on its own tab, beside the others. \"And trade that wand in,\" he says, \"when you can.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [008a04] = 他给你看：穿戴的东西只在打斗时算数；拥有的东西能让你在合适的日子做些事，就像咒语一样。你的行装在单独的标签页里，就在其他标签旁边。“还有，那根魔杖，”他说，“有机会就换掉。”\n\n@event jit_after_curfew\n# en: After Curfew\ntitle [0fdaf7] = 宵禁之后\n# en: After curfew the castle changes hands. The corridors belong to prefects and to whatever else walks them. You can stay in and sleep, or slip out at midnight to wherever is open then. Get caught often enough and people start to notice.\ntext [dd6308] = 宵禁之后，城堡换了主人。走廊属于级长，也属于其他在走廊里游荡的东西。你可以待在房里睡觉，也可以在午夜溜出去，去那时还开着的地方。被抓得多了，人们就会开始注意你。\n# en: A dark castle corridor after curfew, a prefect's lantern far off at one end, a student pressed into a doorway.\nscene [fae1e0] = 宵禁后黑暗的城堡走廊，远处一头有一盏级长的提灯，一个学生贴在门洞里。\n# en: Stay in tonight\nopt.left.label [1ab842] = 今晚待在房里\n# en: You listen to the castle settle and decide midnight can wait.\nopt.left.say.1 [e24a2a] = 你听着城堡慢慢安静下来，决定午夜可以等等。\n# en: Watch the prefect's lantern go by\nopt.right.label [984529] = 看着级长的提灯经过\n# en: The lantern passes your door, pauses and moves on. You learn the round by heart. It feels like knowing a secret.\nopt.right.say.1 [c313f1] = 提灯经过你的门口，停了一下，又走了。你把巡夜路线记得一清二楚。感觉像是知道了一个秘密。\n\n@event jit_red_string\n# en: Red String\ntitle [b7fb15] = 红线\n# en: You've started keeping what you notice: a scrap of paper here, a remembered word there. Tonight you pin them to the wall above your desk and join them with red string. It doesn't make sense yet. It isn't supposed to.\ntext [a4cca7] = 你开始记下自己注意到的东西：这里一张纸条，那里一句记住的话。今晚你把它们钉在书桌上方的墙上，用红线连起来。现在还说不通。本来也不该说得通。\n# en: A student's desk by candlelight, scraps of paper pinned to the wall above it, joined by lengths of red string.\nscene [ee5fd5] = 烛光下学生的书桌，上方墙上钉着纸片，用一段段红线连在一起。\n# en: Go to bed\nopt.left.label [2fa817] = 去睡觉\n# en: You leave the string where it is. In the morning it looks like a constellation you don't know the name of.\nopt.left.say.1 [be60a4] = 你让红线留在那儿。早上看，它像一个你叫不出名字的星座。\n# en: Stare at it a while\nopt.right.label [3f9d74] = 盯着看一会儿\n# en: The things you've found are all there in your notes now, and the leads you haven't followed. Nobody will make you follow them. That's the frightening part.\nopt.right.say.1 [39ac5e] = 你发现的东西现在都记在笔记里了，还有你没追下去的线索。没人会逼你去追。这才是可怕的地方。\n\n@event jit_exam_nerves\n# en: Exam Nerves\ntitle [a019fb] = 考前紧张\n# en: Exams. Outside the hall someone is reciting the stirring count under their breath. Every class you went to and every night you revised is in your head somewhere, and how much of it is there is most of what decides the paper.\ntext [a5b31c] = 考试。考场外有人在低声背搅拌的次数。你上过的每一节课、复习过的每一个晚上都在你脑子里某处，而还剩下多少，基本就决定了这张卷子。\n# en: A queue of pale first-years outside a closed examination hall door, one of them reciting under their breath.\nscene [ee9c1f] = 一群脸色发白的一年级生在紧闭的考场门外排队，其中一个在低声背诵。\n# en: Walk straight in\nopt.left.label [566c0d] = 直接走进去\n# en: You walk in before the nerves can catch you. The door shuts like a held breath.\nopt.left.say.1 [52c3cc] = 你趁紧张还没追上就走了进去。门关上了，像屏住的一口气。\n# en: Go over your notes once more\nopt.right.label [a8cb0d] = 再看一遍笔记\n# en: Preparation counts more than talent here, a fourth-year once told you. You hope she was right. She usually is.\nopt.right.say.1 [2d8c31] = 准备比天赋更重要，一个四年级生曾这么告诉你。你希望她说得对。她通常是对的。\n\n@event jit_long_break\n# en: The Long Break\ntitle [66ec9d] = 长假\n# en: The term is over and the castle empties. {off_campus: You watch it go from the barge rail, your trunk at your feet.|} A break is your own: no lectures, and four free visits a day if you stay at the castle or go home, three if you take on paid work. Some friends leave; some stay. Letters come instead.\ntext [75d834] = 学期结束了，城堡空了下来。{off_campus: 你站在驳船栏杆边看着它远去，箱子就在脚边。|}假期是你自己的：没有课，如果留在城堡或者回家，每天有四个空闲时段；如果去打工，就是三个。有些朋友走了，有些留下。信件取而代之。\n# en: An emptying castle courtyard, trunks being loaded onto a barge, a few students waving and a few staying behind.\nscene [a85881] = 渐渐空下来的城堡庭院，箱子被搬上驳船，几个学生在挥手，几个留了下来。\n# en: Make plans later\nopt.left.label [ce1835] = 以后再计划\n# en: {off_campus: You put your feet up for an afternoon first. Home is very quiet after the castle, and nobody rings a bell at you.|You put your feet up for an afternoon first. The castle is very quiet, and very much yours.}\nopt.left.say.1 [90e8ec] = {off_campus: 你先把脚翘起来歇了一个下午。离开城堡，家里安静极了，也没有钟声冲你响。|你先把脚翘起来歇了一个下午。城堡非常安静，完完全全是你的。}\n# en: {off_campus: Walk round home|Walk the empty halls}\nopt.right.label [2cb97b] = {off_campus: 在家里转转|在空荡荡的大厅里走走}\n# en: {off_campus: Everything is smaller than you remember. Your old room is full of apples now, and someone has kept your bed made anyway.|Your footsteps echo. The kitchens are open all day on a break, the cook says, and the cook is lonely.}\nopt.right.say.1 [21cdea] = {off_campus: 一切都比你记得的要小。你原来的房间现在堆满了苹果，可还是有人把你的床铺得整整齐齐。|你的脚步声回荡着。厨师说，假期里厨房整天开着，而厨师很寂寞。}\n\n@event jit_counter\n# en: Across the Counter\ntitle [abe25b] = 柜台那边\n# en: The village sells what the castle doesn't. Every shop has its counter, with its prices on the slate; some things are only offered once you've proved something, or know someone, or can pay a great deal. And the pawnbroker buys back what you're tired of.\ntext [d74b4b] = 村子卖城堡里没有的东西。每家店都有柜台，价钱写在石板上；有些东西只有在你证明了什么、认识了谁，或者付得起一大笔钱时才会拿出来。当铺还会收你厌倦了的东西。\n# en: A village shop counter seen from a student's side, a price slate, a few goods and a shopkeeper waiting patiently.\nscene [d6bed1] = 从学生这边看过去的村店柜台，一块价目石板，几样商品，店主在耐心等着。\n# en: Browse another day\nopt.left.label [a6f176] = 改天再逛\n# en: You walk the square with your hands in your pockets. Everything in the windows looks expensive and possible.\nopt.left.say.1 [723a4c] = 你双手插兜在广场上转了一圈。橱窗里的一切看起来都很贵，也都很可能。\n# en: Read every slate\nopt.right.label [4f5893] = 把每块石板都读一遍\n# en: Buns, robes, wands, tea, tonics, one at a time. You make a list of what you'd buy if you had money. It's a long list.\nopt.right.say.1 [2428e0] = 面包、长袍、魔杖、茶、补剂，一次一种。你列了一张有钱就买的清单。清单很长。\n\n@event jit_real_fight\n# en: A Real Fight\ntitle [725eee] = 真正的打斗\n# en: The mannequin was a lesson. Real fights aren't. Whoever you face fights to win, the way you would; Health lost below the line stays lost, and anything you'd rather not be seen casting, someone may see. You can always refuse a fight, at a price of its own.\ntext [87f511] = 假人是一堂课。真正的打斗不是。你的对手会像你一样拼命想赢；生命掉到那条线以下就真的没了；你不想被人看见施放的东西，可能会被人看见。你总可以拒绝一场打斗，代价另算。\n# en: A duelling hall with a real duel under way, two students circling, a third nursing a split lip on the bench.\nscene [253250] = 决斗厅里正在进行一场真正的决斗，两个学生绕着圈，第三个在长椅上捂着破了的嘴唇。\n# en: Nod and move on\nopt.left.label [fd56d5] = 点点头走开\n# en: You file it away. Somewhere in the hall, someone is losing well. Thorne would be pleased.\nopt.left.say.1 [a76909] = 你记下了。厅里某处，有人正输得漂亮。索恩会高兴的。\n# en: Watch a bout to the end\nopt.right.label [a77d1e] = 把一局看完\n# en: You watch two third-years fight to a standstill, bow and go to supper together. It looked like the opposite of a lesson. It was the same thing.\nopt.right.say.1 [da1e9f] = 你看着两个三年级生打到僵持不下，鞠了一躬，一起去吃晚饭。看起来跟上课正相反。其实是同一回事。\n\n@event jit_real_negotiation\n# en: A Real Negotiation\ntitle [a656f1] = 真正的交涉\n# en: Not every talk is a quick word. Some are negotiations: someone across the table with reasons of their own, and something at stake. Charm, lore and guile all count, and so does how you come across today. The steward and her list were practice.\ntext [b69d87] = 不是每次谈话都只是一句话的事。有些是交涉：桌子对面的人有自己的理由，而且事关输赢。魅力、学识和诡计都有用，你今天给人的印象也有用。舍监和她的名单只是练习。\n# en: A student and an official facing each other across a desk heaped with papers, both leaning forward.\nscene [8e5ce0] = 一个学生和一位官员隔着堆满文件的桌子对坐，都向前倾着身子。\n# en: Fair enough\nopt.left.label [5ceb95] = 有道理\n# en: You resolve to argue only with people who aren't holding a ledger.\nopt.left.say.1 [5a9666] = 你决定以后只跟手里没拿账本的人争论。\n# en: Remember the steward and her list\nopt.right.label [38d6d1] = 记住舍监和她的名单\n# en: However it went with the steward, the lesson was the same: the list was her whole argument. You make a note: always ask to see the line.\nopt.right.say.1 [9228ea] = 不管和舍监那次结果如何，道理都一样：那份名单就是她全部的理由。你记下一条：永远要求看那一行。\n\n@event jit_infirmary\n# en: A Word From the Infirmary\ntitle [7e8c40] = 医务室的叮嘱\n# en: Sister Amaranth catches you on the stairs, looks at you once and steers you to a bed. \"Low Health,\" she says. \"Under thirty, the body starts to lose things it doesn't get back easily. Sleep. Eat. Stop fighting things.\" She doesn't believe you'll listen.\ntext [1c4072] = 阿玛兰斯修女在楼梯上拦住你，看了你一眼，就把你领到一张病床前。“健康太低了，”她说，“低于三十，身体就会开始失去一些不容易找回来的东西。睡觉。吃饭。别再跟东西打了。”她不相信你会听。\n# en: A stern healer in a white apron writing on a chart at the foot of a student's bed.\nscene [103642] = 一位严厉的治疗师系着白围裙，在学生床尾的病历上写着什么。\n# en: Promise to rest\nopt.left.label [cd2cf3] = 保证好好休息\n# en: You promise. She writes it on your chart, which feels binding in a way promises usually don't.\nopt.left.say.1 [190987] = 你保证了。她把它写进你的病历，这比一般的保证更有约束力。\n# en: Ask how low is too low\nopt.right.label [5ecf56] = 问多低才算太低\n# en: \"Lower than you are,\" she says, \"is too low.\" She gives you a tonic that tastes of iron and disapproval.\nopt.right.say.1 [f79c03] = “比你现在还低，”她说，“就太低了。”她给了你一瓶尝起来像铁和不赞成的补剂。\n\n@event jit_something_more\n# en: Something More\ntitle [5acec4] = 不止如此\n# en: You catch yourself looking for {x} in every room. That's new. Friendship grows on its own; this is something else, and it grows only when you let it. It can also go cold if you leave it too long.\ntext [6f22e2] = 你发现自己在每个房间里找{x}。这是新鲜事。友谊会自己生长；这是另一回事，只有你允许，它才会长。你放着不管太久，它也会冷下来。\n# en: Two students in a crowded corridor, one of them looking back over their shoulder a moment longer than they meant to.\nscene [1a1a5b] = 拥挤的走廊里两个学生擦肩而过，其中一个回头看得比本想的久了一点。\n# en: Think about something else\nopt.left.label [a1058f] = 想点别的\n# en: You think about something else, with great determination, for almost an hour.\nopt.left.say.1 [99e64e] = 你下定决心想别的事，坚持了快一个小时。\n# en: Admit it, to yourself at least\nopt.right.label [7b1c1d] = 承认吧，至少对自己\n# en: You admit it to your pillow, which keeps it. Tomorrow you'll be braver. Possibly.\nopt.right.say.1 [1d0022] = 你对枕头承认了，枕头替你保密。明天你会勇敢些。大概吧。\n\n@event jit_rivals\n# en: Rivals\ntitle [9f39c8] = 对手\n# en: Somebody here has decided you're the one to beat. A rival watches what you do and does it better, or tries to, and you'll find yourself doing the same. Rivals can soften into something else. They can also sharpen.\ntext [c8aff0] = 这里有人认定你就是要打败的那个人。对手盯着你做的事，然后做得更好，或者想做得更好，而你会发现自己也在这么做。对手可以软化成别的关系。也可以变得更尖锐。\n# en: Two students passing on a staircase, neither giving way, both pretending not to notice the other.\nscene [bba80b] = 两个学生在楼梯上相遇，谁都不让路，都假装没注意到对方。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.left.label [e2ae0c] = 别理它\n# en: You resolve to ignore it. You fail almost at once, which is how rivalry works.\nopt.left.say.1 [596911] = 你决定不理它。几乎立刻就失败了，对手就是这么回事。\n# en: Get better\nopt.right.label [018f1f] = 变得更好\n# en: You stay late in the library out of pure spite. It is extraordinarily motivating.\nopt.right.say.1 [7df6b8] = 你纯粹出于赌气在图书馆待到很晚。这动力大得惊人。\n\n@event jit_mirror\n# en: The Mirror Remembers\ntitle [a9c2e3] = 镜子记得\n# en: You pass the Sorting Mirror on the way to breakfast and your reflection is a half-step slow. {alignment >= 15: It looks brighter than you feel.|It looks colder than you feel.} What you do when it costs you something is being remembered, somewhere, and it leans you one way or the other.\ntext [a31764] = 你去吃早饭时经过分院镜，镜中的倒影慢了半步。{alignment >= 15: 它看起来比你感觉的更明亮。|它看起来比你感觉的更冰冷。}你在付出代价时所做的事，正被某处记着，它会让你向这边或那边倾斜。\n# en: A student passing the tall sorting mirror in the entrance hall, their reflection a half-step behind them.\nscene [29c9fa] = 一个学生经过入口大厅那面高大的分院镜，倒影落后了他们半步。\n# en: Walk faster\nopt.left.label [ce8449] = 走快点\n# en: Your reflection catches up by the end of the hall. You decide not to wonder how.\nopt.left.say.1 [246381] = 到大厅尽头，倒影赶上了你。你决定不去想它是怎么做到的。\n# en: Look back at it\nopt.right.label [08174f] = 回头看它\n# en: It looks back. Then it straightens its robes, which you hadn't, and you straighten yours.\nopt.right.say.1 [3603d3] = 它也回看你。然后它整了整长袍——你还没整——于是你也整了整你的。\n\n@event jit_noticed\n# en: Noticed\ntitle [15acef] = 引人注目\n# en: Heads turn when you come into the common room. A first-year whispers your name to another. Being known opens doors and invitations; it also fades if you stop giving people something to talk about.\ntext [3d44cb] = 你一走进公共休息室，就有人转过头来。一个一年级生小声把你的名字告诉另一个。出名能带来机会和邀请；如果你不再给人们谈资，名声也会慢慢消退。\n# en: A student walking into a crowded common room as a few heads turn, someone whispering behind a hand.\nscene [1ff97c] = 一个学生走进拥挤的公共休息室，几个人转过头，有人用手挡着嘴说悄悄话。\n# en: Pretend not to notice\nopt.left.label [57b529] = 假装没注意\n# en: You pretend not to notice. Everyone notices you pretending.\nopt.left.say.1 [790a19] = 你假装没注意。所有人都注意到你在假装。\n# en: Wave\nopt.right.label [96ccf4] = 挥挥手\n# en: You wave. Three people wave back, and one of them has no idea who you are.\nopt.right.say.1 [d17e6b] = 你挥了挥手。三个人挥手回应，其中一个根本不知道你是谁。\n\n@event jit_first_shift\n# en: First Shift\ntitle [11f651] = 第一次上班\n# en: A job is a few shifts a term, shown on the map when they're due; each one pays, and a missed one costs the boss's good opinion. Work well and you're promoted. Turn up on a break and it's just a visit.\ntext [f01a2a] = 一份工作每学期要上几次班，该上的时候会显示在地图上；每次都有工钱，缺勤会让老板对你印象变差。干得好就能升职。假期去了就只算一次拜访。\n# en: A student tying on an apron in a back room, a list of duties pinned on the wall beside them.\nscene [84af70] = 一个学生在后屋系围裙，旁边墙上钉着一张职责单。\n# en: Tie the apron and go\nopt.left.label [22208f] = 系上围裙就去\n# en: You tie the apron backwards. Nobody tells you for an hour.\nopt.left.say.1 [a3b87d] = 你把围裙系反了。一个小时都没人告诉你。\n# en: Read the duty list twice\nopt.right.label [626776] = 把职责单读两遍\n# en: You read it twice. The second time, you notice the line at the bottom: \"Ask if unsure.\" You decide to be sure instead.\nopt.right.say.1 [4ab7ca] = 你读了两遍。第二遍时，你注意到最下面一行：“不确定就问。”你决定干脆确定一点。\n\n@event jit_applications\n# en: Applications\ntitle [7f7f22] = 申请\n# en: The internship notices are up. A summer's placement means a summer away doing the work, and a letter from a supervisor that follows you after. Each wants a course passed, or more, and asks once a year.\ntext [1b9c79] = 实习的通知贴出来了。一个暑期实习意味着一个夏天在外面干活，还有一封之后会一直跟着你的导师推荐信。每个都要求通过某门课或更多，而且一年只招一次。\n# en: A noticeboard plastered with internship notices, a student copying deadlines into a notebook.\nscene [259d8b] = 贴满实习通知的布告栏，一个学生把截止日期抄进笔记本。\n# en: Look later\nopt.left.label [39e3b4] = 以后再看\n# en: You walk past the board. The notices will still be there tomorrow, mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [c82002] = 你从布告栏前走过。那些通知明天大部分还会在那儿。\n# en: Copy the deadlines down\nopt.right.label [424d96] = 把截止日期抄下来\n# en: You copy every deadline into your notebook in your best hand, and underline the ones you could actually meet.\nopt.right.say.1 [2074de] = 你用最好的字迹把每个截止日期抄进笔记本，在你真能赶上的那几个下面划了线。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/jobs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/jobs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lib_biting_book\n# en: The Book That Bites\ntitle [e0be45] = 会咬人的书\n# en: Reshelving in the zoology section, a fat leather book clamps onto your finger and refuses to let go. The head librarian, without looking up, says: \"Stroke the spine.\"\ntext [68fee7] = 你在动物学区上架时，一本厚厚的皮面书咬住了你的手指，死活不松口。图书馆馆长头也不抬地说：“摸摸它的书脊。”\n# en: A thick leather book with tiny teeth along its pages snapping at a hand reaching for it on a high shelf.\nscene [b648b2] = 一本书页边缘长着细牙的厚皮书，正冲着伸向高处书架的手咬去。\n# en: Muzzle it with a belt\nopt.left.label [c9d229] = 用皮带把它的嘴捆上\n# en: You wrestle it into submission and shelve it. It growls at you every time you pass for the rest of the term.\nopt.left.say.1 [91c33d] = 你把它摁服了，放回书架。接下来整个学期，你每次经过它都会冲你低吼。\n# en: Stroke the spine and read it\nopt.right.label [b279ad] = 摸摸书脊，读一读\n# en: It purrs, opens and turns out to be a fascinating treatise on carnivorous plants. It only bites twice more. The reshelving waits until tomorrow.\nopt.right.say.1 [ad9f03] = 它发出呼噜声，自己翻开了——原来是一部关于食肉植物的精彩论著。它只又咬了你两次。上架的活只好等到明天。\n\n@event lib_overdue_professor\n# en: Overdue\ntitle [40f62b] = 逾期未还\n# en: Professor Thorne returns a book that is eleven years overdue. The fine, by library rules, is forty crowns. He looks at you. You look at the ledger.\ntext [f16fb3] = 索恩教授还了一本逾期十一年的书。按照图书馆规定，罚款是四十克朗。他看着你。你看着罚款簿。\n# en: A severe professor towering over a library desk, a stack of books under one arm, a very small student assistant on the other side holding a fines ledger.\nscene [9af617] = 一位严厉的教授高高站在图书馆柜台前，胳膊下夹着一摞书，柜台另一边一个小小的学生助理捧着罚款簿。\n# en: Waive the fine\nopt.left.label [20e10a] = 免了罚款\n# en: \"Hm,\" says Thorne. You can't tell if you've earned his respect or lost it.\nopt.left.say.1 [30eaf0] = “嗯。”索恩说。你说不清自己是赢得了他的尊重，还是失去了它。\n# en: Enforce the rules\nopt.right.label [32a605] = 照章办事\n# en: A long silence. Then he pays, in exact change. \"Good,\" he says, and you could swear he almost smiles.\nopt.right.say.1 [a7c7e3] = 漫长的沉默。然后他付了钱，分文不差。“很好。”他说，你敢发誓他差点笑了。\n# en: Recall the amnesty clause\nopt.up.label [9dc279] = 调出赦免条款\n# en: The ledger falls open at a rule from three hundred years ago: books returned by a professor on a wet Tuesday are forgiven. It is a wet Tuesday. Thorne reads the page twice and says, \"Hm,\" which in this case means you'll go far.\nopt.up.say.1 [7a8e54] = 账簿翻开在三百年前的一条规定：雨天的星期二由教授归还的书一律免罚。今天正是下雨的星期二。索恩把那一页读了两遍，说了声“嗯”，此时的意思是你前途无量。\n\n@event lib_restricted_cart\n# en: The Restricted Cart\ntitle [1e31b9] = 禁书推车\n# en: You are asked to wheel the Restricted Section returns to the vault. The cart's chain has come loose. One of the books has fallen open on a page that is writing itself.\ntext [e83ef7] = 你被派去把禁书区归还的书推回书库。推车的锁链松了。其中一本书摊开在一页正在自己书写的纸上。\n# en: A wheeled library cart chained shut, piled with black-bound books, parked alone in a candlelit aisle.\nscene [e3b4b3] = 一辆用锁链锁着的图书推车，堆满黑皮书，孤零零地停在烛光摇曳的过道里。\n# en: Close it without looking\nopt.left.label [69f4b5] = 不看，直接合上\n# en: The book snaps shut with a sound like a disappointed sigh. The vault door clangs. The head librarian notes the time, approvingly.\nopt.left.say.1 [2fb0b1] = 书啪地合上，发出一声像是失望的叹息。书库的门哐当关上了。馆长记下了时间，满意地点点头。\n# en: Read a few lines\nopt.right.label [40815b] = 读上几行\n# en: The words crawl off the page and up your sleeve. That night, you can't get warm.\nopt.right.say.1 [166cd2] = 文字从书页上爬出来，钻进了你的袖子。那天夜里，你怎么也暖和不起来。\n# en: A spell for walking through mirrors. You will never try it. Probably.\nopt.right.say.2 [ee6c9d] = 一个穿过镜子的咒语。你永远不会去试的。大概吧。\n# en: Seal the book shut\nopt.up.label [d4918e] = 把书封死\n# en: You seal the book shut before you've read a line. It stops writing. The vault keeper takes it from you, tries the clasp, and gives you a look you can't read. \"Good,\" he says, eventually.\nopt.up.say.1 [81c9b1] = 你一行都没读，就把书封死了。它停止了书写。库管从你手里接过书，试了试书扣，给了你一个看不透的眼神。“好，”他终于说。\n\n@event tavern_brawl\n# en: Brawl at the Newt\ntitle [6ed513] = 蝾螈酒馆斗殴\n# en: A dwarf and a troll disagree about the price of turnips, and the Gilded Newt erupts. A chair flies past your head. The owner is hiding behind the bar, gesturing at you to do something.\ntext [f434ad] = 一个矮人和一个巨魔为芜菁的价格吵了起来，镀金蝾螈酒馆瞬间炸了锅。一把椅子从你头顶飞过。老板躲在吧台后面，比划着让你想想办法。\n# en: A low-beamed tavern mid-brawl: a chair in mid-air, a dwarf on a table, cider sloshing everywhere.\nscene [d9c407] = 一间低梁小酒馆正在混战：一把椅子悬在半空，一个矮人站在桌上，苹果酒洒得到处都是。\n# en: Duck behind the bar too\nopt.left.label [56a05c] = 也躲到吧台后面\n# en: You crouch beside the owner and share a bag of crisps while the room destroys itself.\nopt.left.say.1 [87132f] = 你蹲在老板旁边，和他分吃一袋薯片，看着整个屋子自我毁灭。\n# en: Break it up\nopt.right.label [12966a] = 上去劝架\n# en: You slam a tray on the bar and bellow. Silence. The troll apologises. The dwarf tips you fifteen crowns.\nopt.right.say.1 [b417fb] = 你把托盘往吧台上一拍，大吼一声。全场安静。巨魔道了歉。矮人给了你十五克朗小费。\n# en: You step in. Someone's elbow steps into your face.\nopt.right.say.2 [763c5b] = 你上前一步。不知谁的胳膊肘也上前一步，到了你脸上。\n# en: Tap the bar twice\nopt.up.label [ef6f14] = 在吧台上敲两下\n# en: You tap the bar twice and say the one thing about turnips that makes a dwarf and a troll both laugh. The chair comes down. The owner comes out from behind the bar and pours you a cider, staring.\nopt.up.say.1 [c70db9] = 你在吧台上敲了两下，说了一句关于芜菁的话，矮人和巨魔都笑了。椅子落回了地上。老板从吧台后面出来，给你倒了杯苹果酒，眼睛直直地看着你。\n\n@event tavern_star_coin\n# en: A Generous Stranger\ntitle [678324] = 慷慨的陌生人\n# en: A hooded stranger nurses a cider all evening and leaves a tip: a black coin stamped with a hollow star. \"For the student,\" they say. \"Tell Kael Dumont his family sends regards.\"\ntext [b2c4b8] = 一个戴兜帽的陌生人一整晚只喝了一杯苹果酒，留下的小费是一枚印着空心星的黑色硬币。“给这个学生的，”对方说，“转告凯尔·杜蒙，他的家人向他问好。”\n# en: A hooded figure at a corner table sliding a black coin across the wood, its surface stamped with a dark star.\nscene [fe800a] = 角落桌旁一个戴兜帽的身影把一枚黑色硬币推过木桌，币面上印着一颗暗星。\n# en: Refuse the coin\nopt.left.label [999c57] = 拒绝这枚硬币\n# en: The stranger shrugs and pockets it. When you look again, the corner table is empty and the cider is frozen solid.\nopt.left.say.1 [53629a] = 陌生人耸耸肩，把它收回口袋。你再看时，角落的桌子空了，那杯苹果酒冻成了冰。\n# en: Take it\nopt.right.label [18a43f] = 收下它\n# en: The coin is cold as ice and heavier than it should be. You find yourself turning it over in your pocket for days.\nopt.right.say.1 [2b7ed8] = 硬币冷得像冰，比看上去要重。接下来好几天，你发现自己总在口袋里摩挲它。\n\n@event tavern_extra_shifts\n# en: More Shifts\ntitle [bc17d5] = 加班\n# en: The owner is short-handed. \"Double shifts for a month,\" he says. \"Double pay. I know you've got studies, but you've also got rent.\"\ntext [daff5a] = 老板人手不够。“一个月双班，”他说，“双倍工钱。我知道你还要上学，但你也还要交房租。”\n# en: A ruddy tavern owner leaning on the bar, wiping a mug, a rota pinned behind him covered in crossed-out names.\nscene [205014] = 一个红光满面的酒馆老板靠在吧台上擦杯子，身后钉着的排班表上划掉了一个又一个名字。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Fair enough,\" he says, and asks again next week.\nopt.left.say.1 [2ad5ce] = “行吧。”他说，然后下周又来问。\n# en: Take the extra shifts\nopt.right.label [124a34] = 接下加班\n# en: A month of late nights and early lectures. Your purse is heavy. So are your eyelids.\nopt.right.say.1 [bef75a] = 一个月的深夜打工和清早上课。你的钱袋沉甸甸的。眼皮也是。\n\n@event tavern_broken_glass\n# en: Broken Glass\ntitle [6a7d8c] = 打碎的酒杯\n# en: {A tray of tankards meets a regular's elbow, and the floor is suddenly all glass.|Another tray, another floor of glass. The Newt goes through tankards the way other places go through napkins.|Glass on the floor again, and the same regular's elbow to blame.} {The regular is very sorry, loudly.|Everyone lifts their feet.|The dog is already heading for it.}\ntext [f64d54] = {一托盘酒杯撞上了一位常客的胳膊肘，地上顿时满是碎玻璃。|又一托盘，又是一地玻璃。蝾螈酒馆消耗酒杯的速度，就像别处消耗餐巾。|地上又是玻璃，又是那位常客的胳膊肘惹的祸。} {那位常客大声地连连道歉。|所有人都把脚抬了起来。|那条狗已经朝碎片走过去了。}\n# en: A tavern floor glittering with broken tankards, a server crouching with a cloth, a dog padding closer.\nscene [6d91e1] = 酒馆地板上闪着碎酒杯的光，一名侍应蹲着拿抹布，一条狗正慢慢凑近。\n# en: Clear it by hand, fast\nopt.left.label [b1cbdf] = 徒手快快收拾\n# en: {You have it cleared before the dog gets there, with a cut across your palm to show for it. The landlady binds it with a bar towel.|Fast and thorough, apart from the sliver you find in your thumb at midnight.|You beat the dog to it by a whisker and a nicked finger. The landlady gives the dog a sausage for its trouble.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7d2b36] = {你赶在狗之前收拾干净了，手掌上多了一道口子作证。老板娘用一条吧台毛巾给你包上。|又快又干净，只是半夜你才在拇指里发现一根玻璃碴。|你以一根胡须的差距和一根划破的手指抢在了狗前面。老板娘给了狗一根香肠作为补偿。}\n# en: Fetch the broom and do it properly\nopt.right.label [32e157] = 拿扫帚好好扫\n# en: {By the time you're back with the broom, three customers have left with their cider unfinished. The landlady counts them.|You sweep slowly and safely while the bar goes dry. The landlady taps the clock.|You go for the broom, and the landlady has to hold the dog back by the collar while the customers' cider goes warm.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e43aa3] = {等你拿着扫帚回来，已经有三个客人苹果酒没喝完就走了。老板娘一个个数着。|你慢慢地、稳稳地扫，吧台那边却断了酒。老板娘敲了敲钟。|你去拿扫帚，老板娘只好揪着狗的项圈拦住它，客人们的苹果酒都放温了。}\n\n@event tavern_after_closing\n# en: After Closing\ntitle [c895a2] = 打烊之后\n# en: {Your shift tonight runs until the Newt closes, long after the last barge.|Another late shift. By closing time the lake will be dark.|Late shift again. You've stopped expecting to see the last barge.} {season == \"winter\": These nights the shore path is ice under snow.|{Fog comes off the water most nights now.|The shore path has no lamps.|Somebody always sings, badly, on the way home.}} It's a long walk round the lake to the castle.\ntext [920705] = {你今晚的班要上到蝾螈酒馆打烊，那时末班驳船早就开走了。|又是晚班。到打烊时，湖面会一片漆黑。|又是晚班。你已经不指望赶上末班驳船了。} {season == \"winter\": 这些夜里，湖岸小路上是雪下的冰。|{这阵子湖上夜夜起雾。|湖岸小路上没有灯。|回家路上总有人在唱歌，唱得很难听。}} 绕湖走回城堡，路还很长。\n# en: A lantern-lit shore path at night, a tired server walking alone beside black water, the castle's towers far off.\nscene [f876cc] = 夜里一条提灯照亮的湖岸小路，一名疲惫的侍应独自走在黑水边，城堡的塔楼远在天边。\n# en: Walk the shore path\nopt.left.label [8e64c1] = 走湖岸小路\n# en: {Two hours round the lake, and every noise in the reeds is something. You reach the gates with wet boots and a turned ankle.|You walk home in the dark and slip twice. You fall into bed as the first bell rings.|You walk home with the other late workers, singing badly to keep the dark off. You're hoarse by the gates, and late to bed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [aebb96] = {绕湖走了两个钟头，芦苇里每一声响都像有什么东西。你走到城门时靴子湿透，脚踝也扭了。|你摸黑走回去，滑倒了两次。第一遍钟响时你才倒在床上。|你和其他上晚班的人一起走回去，唱着难听的歌驱散黑暗。到城门时嗓子哑了，睡得也晚了。}\n# en: Sleep by the Newt's fire\nopt.right.label [66046e] = 睡在酒馆炉边\n# en: {The landlady lends you a blanket and the settle by the fire. You wake at dawn smelling of woodsmoke and miss the first lecture.|You sleep on the settle under somebody's coat and catch the morning barge, too late for your first class.|You hear the others singing their way home along the shore and choose the fire. The settle is lumpy, and you miss the first lecture anyway.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cf7c9a] = {老板娘借你一条毯子，让你睡炉边的长椅。你在黎明醒来，一身木烟味，错过了第一节课。|你盖着不知谁的外套睡在长椅上，搭上了早班驳船，却赶不上第一节课了。|你听见别人沿着湖岸唱着歌回去，自己选了炉火。长椅硌得慌，第一节课你照样错过了。}\n\n@event apoth_wrong_label\n# en: A Mislabelled Jar\ntitle [2e5055] = 贴错的标签\n# en: The jar labelled \"Sleeping Draught\" is green. Sleeping Draught is blue. You're fairly sure the green stuff is Draught of Unending Hiccups. Old Pell is very proud of his labelling.\ntext [90816e] = 标着“安眠药剂”的罐子是绿色的。安眠药剂是蓝色的。你很确定这绿色的东西是“无尽打嗝药剂”。老佩尔对自己的标签工作非常自豪。\n# en: Shelves of glass jars in a narrow shop, one jar labelled SLEEPING DRAUGHT glowing an ominous green.\nscene [da4c60] = 狭窄店铺里一排排玻璃罐，其中一只标着“安眠药剂”的罐子发出不祥的绿光。\n# en: Quietly fix the label\nopt.left.label [d3ca76] = 悄悄改掉标签\n# en: Nobody ever knows. Somewhere, a customer sleeps soundly instead of hiccupping for a week.\nopt.left.say.1 [3b7685] = 永远没人知道。某个地方，一位顾客安然入睡，而不是打一星期的嗝。\n# en: Point it out to Pell\nopt.right.label [4225c6] = 告诉佩尔\n# en: Pell goes red, then thanks you gruffly and slips you three crowns \"for your eyes\".\nopt.right.say.1 [cdda86] = 佩尔脸一红，粗声道了谢，塞给你三克朗，“算你眼尖”。\n\n@event apoth_poor_mother\n# en: Can't Afford It\ntitle [f4bd61] = 买不起\n# en: A woman counts out coppers for fever tonic. She's fifteen crowns short. Her son coughs into her coat. Pell is in the back room.\ntext [1d93f2] = 一位妇人一枚枚数着铜板买退烧药。还差十五克朗。她的儿子靠在她外套上咳嗽。佩尔在后屋。\n# en: A tired woman at a shop counter counting copper coins, a small child coughing beside her.\nscene [6ddd5b] = 一位疲惫的妇人在柜台前数着铜币，身边一个小孩在咳嗽。\n# en: Charge the full price\nopt.left.label [9ea316] = 照价收钱\n# en: She leaves without it. The bell on the door rings for a very long time.\nopt.left.say.1 [b36f54] = 她没买成就走了。门上的铃铛响了很久很久。\n# en: Pay the difference yourself\nopt.right.label [28226a] = 自己垫上差价\n# en: You slip the tonic into her basket. Next week a pie appears on the counter with your name on it.\nopt.right.say.1 [8762e7] = 你把药悄悄放进她的篮子。下周，柜台上出现了一个写着你名字的馅饼。\n\n@event apoth_nightshade_order\n# en: The Nightshade Order\ntitle [d8aa3b] = 颠茄订单\n# en: A note arrives with a sealed purse: a large order of nightshade, grave-moss and powdered moonstone. Legal, technically. Ingredients for something very old and very forbidden, also technically.\ntext [7f5f4d] = 一张便条随一只封好的钱袋送来：大量的颠茄、墓苔和月长石粉。严格来说，合法。严格来说，也是某种非常古老、非常禁忌之物的原料。\n# en: An order slip on a counter listing a dozen deadly reagents, signed with an elegant, illegible flourish.\nscene [fe014b] = 柜台上一张订货单，列着十几种致命试剂，签名是一个优雅却无法辨认的花体字。\n# en: Fill the order\nopt.left.label [c3e9db] = 照单配货\n# en: The courier who collects it never shows his face. The purse is very generous.\nopt.left.say.1 [c33bee] = 来取货的信使始终没有露脸。钱袋非常丰厚。\n# en: Refuse and report it\nopt.right.label [ce8f5b] = 拒绝并上报\n# en: The headmistress listens to your report in silence. \"Thank you,\" she says. \"Tell no one else.\" She looks worried.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c685a] = 院长沉默地听完你的报告。“谢谢你，”她说，“别再告诉任何人。”她看起来很担忧。\n# en: Seal the order for the wardens\nopt.up.label [ad0565] = 封好订单交给守望者\n# en: You seal the note and the purse together, so nobody can change a word, and send them up to the castle. The headmistress writes back the same day: one line, thanking you for the seal, which is how she knows it's true.\nopt.up.say.1 [99e5e4] = 你把便条和钱袋一起封缄，谁也改不了一个字，然后送上了城堡。院长当天就回了信：只有一行，感谢你的封缄——她正是凭这个知道内容属实。\n\n@event stables_hippogriff\n# en: Bow to the Hippogriff\ntitle [0d01e9] = 向鹰头马身有翼兽鞠躬\n# en: The new hippogriff won't let anyone near the feed trough. The stablemaster says to bow, keep eye contact and not blink. \"Whatever you do, don't blink.\"\ntext [e114f7] = 新来的鹰头马身有翼兽不让任何人靠近食槽。马厩总管说要鞠躬、保持对视、不许眨眼。“无论如何，别眨眼。”\n# en: A proud hippogriff in a straw-strewn stall, head lowered, regarding a stable hand who is bowing very deeply.\nscene [3828d0] = 铺满稻草的马厩里，一头高傲的鹰头马身有翼兽低着头，打量着一个深深鞠躬的马厩工。\n# en: Leave the feed and run\nopt.left.label [4fb2e9] = 扔下饲料就跑\n# en: You toss the feed bucket and flee. The hippogriff eats it, and the bucket.\nopt.left.say.1 [e88a40] = 你扔下饲料桶就逃。它把饲料吃了，连桶一起。\n# en: Bow and hold its gaze\nopt.right.label [d95cb6] = 鞠躬，与它对视\n# en: It bows back. After that it follows you around the stables like a large, feathered dog.\nopt.right.say.1 [9dbab4] = 它回了一个礼。从那以后，它在马厩里跟着你转，像一只长了羽毛的大狗。\n# en: You blinked. It knocks you flat against the trough, and you spend the evening in the infirmary with your arm in a sling.\nopt.right.say.2 [a82f7a] = 你眨眼了。它一下把你撞倒在食槽边，你吊着胳膊在医务室里度过了整个晚上。\n\n@event stables_dragon_egg\n# en: An Egg in the Hay\ntitle [b1a78f] = 草堆里的蛋\n# en: Mucking out the far stall, you find an egg the size of a melon, nestled in the hay and steaming gently. It's very warm. It's very definitely not a chicken's.\ntext [067bf4] = 清扫最里面的隔间时，你在草堆里发现一枚西瓜大的蛋，正轻轻冒着热气。它很烫。它绝对不是鸡蛋。\n# en: A speckled egg the size of a melon nestled in hay, steam rising from it, a stable hand's lantern held over it.\nscene [bb4b8b] = 一枚西瓜大小、带斑点的蛋窝在干草里冒着热气，马厩工的提灯举在它上方。\n# en: Report it to Professor Brask\nopt.left.label [983969] = 报告布拉斯克教授\n# en: Brask nearly faints. \"Smugglers! A firedrake egg!\" It's taken to the menagerie. You get a commendation.\nopt.left.say.1 [95ded5] = 布拉斯克差点晕过去。“走私犯！火龙蛋！”蛋被送进了动物园。你得到了一份嘉奖。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.right.label [87c2f5] = 留下它\n# en: You smuggle it back to your dormitory wrapped in a scarf. It purrs. Eggs shouldn't purr.\nopt.right.say.1 [8852b1] = 你用围巾把它裹好，偷偷带回了宿舍。它在打呼噜。蛋不该会打呼噜的。\n\n@event egg_hatches\n# en: It Hatches\ntitle [8c317e] = 破壳了\n# en: At 3am your pillow catches fire. The egg has hatched: a firedrake the size of a kitten, delighted with itself and setting fire to everything it loves. It loves you very much.\ntext [c42d90] = 凌晨三点，你的枕头着火了。蛋孵出来了：一条小猫大的火龙，对自己满意极了，见到喜欢的东西就点着。它非常非常喜欢你。\n# en: A tiny red dragon perched on a scorched dormitory pillow, a puff of smoke rising from its nostrils, a horrified roommate in the doorway.\nscene [41e73c] = 一条小红龙蹲在烧焦的宿舍枕头上，鼻孔冒出一缕烟，门口的室友吓呆了。\n# en: Hand it over to the menagerie\nopt.left.label [141f8c] = 交给动物园\n# en: Brask takes it gently. \"You'll be able to visit.\" You do, every week. It always tries to set you on fire, affectionately.\nopt.left.say.1 [695354] = 布拉斯克轻轻接过它。“你可以来探望。”你每周都去。它总是试图把你点着，满怀爱意地。\n# en: Hide it as long as you can\nopt.right.label [7752e9] = 能藏多久藏多久\n# en: You keep it secret for a month — buying fireproof sheets, raw meat, an asbestos sock — before releasing it in the mountains. It circles once, roaring.\nopt.right.say.1 [40bff7] = 你把它藏了一个月——买防火床单、生肉、一只石棉袜子——然后在山里放了它。它盘旋一圈，长啸一声。\n# en: The smoke alarm charm goes off in three towers at once. Discovered, singed, and in very deep trouble.\nopt.right.say.2 [25dba6] = 三座塔的烟雾警报咒同时响起。被发现了，被烧焦了，而且麻烦大了。\n\n@event stables_night_shift\n# en: The Sick Pegasus\ntitle [651cda] = 生病的天马\n# en: The oldest pegasus in the stables is sick. Someone has to sit with her overnight, keep her warm, and make sure she drinks. It's unpaid. Old Maud says she'll do it if no one else will. Maud is eighty.\ntext [e3c4b6] = 马厩里最老的天马病了。得有人整夜守着她、给她保暖、确保她喝水。没有报酬。老莫德说没人去的话她就去。莫德八十岁了。\n# en: A white pegasus lying in a moonlit stall, wings folded, a student sitting beside it with a lantern and a blanket.\nscene [e7765d] = 一匹白色天马收着翅膀卧在月光照亮的马厩里，一个学生提着灯、抱着毯子坐在它身旁。\n# en: Let Maud do it\nopt.left.label [e408cf] = 让莫德去\n# en: You sleep well. Maud is a little slower the next day.\nopt.left.say.1 [d77695] = 你睡得很好。第二天，莫德的动作慢了一些。\n# en: Take the night shift\nopt.right.label [ead26b] = 你来值夜班\n# en: She drinks. You talk to her all night about home. At dawn she stands, shakes her wings, and nuzzles your hair.\nopt.right.say.1 [e093f9] = 她喝了水。你整夜跟她聊家乡。黎明时，她站起来抖了抖翅膀，用鼻子蹭了蹭你的头发。\n\n@event tutor_hopeless\n# en: The Hopeless Case\ntitle [323c15] = 没救的学生\n# en: Your tutee has been trying to levitate a feather for three weeks. The feather has not moved. The tutee is starting to cry. You have one more session before their exam.\ntext [04a494] = 你辅导的学生已经尝试让一根羽毛悬浮三个星期了。羽毛一动没动。学生快哭了。考试前你们只剩一次课。\n# en: A tiny first-year in oversized robes pointing a wand at a feather, which is stubbornly not moving, while a tutor watches with infinite patience.\nscene [29897d] = 一个穿着过大长袍的小个子一年级生用魔杖指着一根纹丝不动的羽毛，辅导员以无限的耐心在旁边看着。\n# en: Drill the basics, again\nopt.left.label [652518] = 再练一遍基础\n# en: Wrist, word, will. Wrist, word, will. On the exam, the feather twitches. It's enough to pass. Your own levitation has never been cleaner.\nopt.left.say.1 [1d2c6c] = 手腕、咒语、意志。手腕、咒语、意志。考试时，羽毛抽动了一下。够及格了。你自己的悬浮咒从没这么干净过。\n# en: Try something unorthodox\nopt.right.label [b16c64] = 试点不寻常的办法\n# en: You tell them to imagine the feather is in love with the ceiling. It shoots upward and sticks there. They pass with distinction.\nopt.right.say.1 [e57332] = 你让对方想象羽毛爱上了天花板。羽毛嗖地升上去，粘在了那里。对方以优异成绩通过。\n# en: You tell them to imagine the feather is in love with the ceiling. They are confused. So is the feather.\nopt.right.say.2 [79cec2] = 你让对方想象羽毛爱上了天花板。对方很困惑。羽毛也很困惑。\n# en: Build them a feather-lifter\nopt.up.label [8e6338] = 给他造台举羽机\n# en: You build a little machine of springs and a fan that lifts the feather on a puff. Your tutee laughs so hard they lift the next one themselves, by accident, out of sheer surprise. Neither of you is sure what the lesson was.\nopt.up.say.1 [cb54e5] = 你用弹簧和小风扇造了一台机器，呼一下就把羽毛举起来。你的学生笑得太厉害，下一根羽毛竟被他自己意外举了起来，纯属吓了一跳。你们俩都说不清这一课学的是什么。\n\n@event tutor_bribe\n# en: An Indecent Proposal\ntitle [e847fe] = 不体面的提议\n# en: A rich second-year doesn't want tutoring. He wants an essay. \"Thirty crowns,\" he says, dropping a purse on the table. \"Nobody needs to know.\"\ntext [5e864c] = 一个富家二年级生不想要辅导。他想要一篇论文。“三十克朗，”他说着把钱袋扔在桌上，“没人需要知道。”\n# en: A rich student in tailored robes sliding a heavy coin purse across a study table, smiling smugly.\nscene [188c2e] = 一个穿着定制长袍的富家学生把一只沉甸甸的钱袋推过书桌，得意地笑着。\n# en: Refuse\nopt.left.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: He sneers and goes elsewhere. You spend the evening on your own essays. Later you see him in detention, scrubbing a cauldron.\nopt.left.say.1 [1faaba] = 他冷笑一声，另寻他人去了。你把晚上花在了自己的论文上。后来你看见他在禁闭室里刷坩埚。\n# en: Write the essay\nopt.right.label [b2934c] = 替他写\n# en: His professor recognises your style. You both end up in detention. He doesn't speak to you. Fine.\nopt.right.say.1 [955908] = 他的教授认出了你的文风。你们俩都被关了禁闭。他不再理你。正好。\n# en: He gets a decent mark. You get thirty crowns and a slightly sour feeling.\nopt.right.say.2 [b854de] = 他拿到了不错的分数。你拿到了三十克朗，和一丝酸溜溜的感觉。\n\n@event tutor_prodigy\n# en: Surpassed\ntitle [679359] = 青出于蓝\n# en: Your newest tutee, a first-year barely a month in, casts in a week what took you a year. Then she asks you a question you can't answer.\ntext [04f3f6] = 你新带的学生，一个入学才一个月的一年级生，一周就学会了你花一年才学会的咒语。然后她问了你一个你答不上来的问题。\n# en: A small first-year casting a dazzling spell that fills a classroom with light, their tutor watching with complicated feelings.\nscene [9e6498] = 一个小个子一年级生施展出一道耀眼的咒语，满屋光芒，辅导员在一旁神情复杂。\n# en: Bluff an answer\nopt.left.label [86ba79] = 硬编一个答案\n# en: She nods politely. You both know.\nopt.left.say.1 [7e3422] = 她礼貌地点点头。你们俩心里都明白。\n# en: Admit you don't know and find out together\nopt.right.label [b4d010] = 承认不知道，一起去查\n# en: You spend an evening in the library together. She thanks you as if you've taught her something. Maybe you have.\nopt.right.say.1 [92c9bb] = 你们在图书馆待了一晚上。她向你道谢，好像你教了她什么。也许你真的教了。\n\n@event courier_storm\n# en: Delivery in a Storm\ntitle [d6be36] = 风暴中送件\n# en: An urgent parcel needs to cross the lake tonight. There's a storm. The dispatcher offers double pay to whoever's fool enough. Everyone looks at you.\ntext [7b10ad] = 一件急件今晚必须送过湖。外面有风暴。调度员给愿意去的傻瓜开双倍工钱。所有人都看着你。\n# en: A courier on a broom bent low against a lightning-lit sky, a parcel strapped to their back, rain sheeting sideways.\nscene [eccc6d] = 一名信使伏低身子骑着飞帚冲进闪电照亮的夜空，背上绑着包裹，雨水横扫。\n# en: Wait for the storm to pass\nopt.left.label [fd0d0f] = 等风暴过去\n# en: The parcel arrives late. The client deducts it from your wages. Rude, but survivable.\nopt.left.say.1 [c849f6] = 包裹迟到了。客户从你的工钱里扣了钱。无礼，但还活得下去。\n# en: Fly through it\nopt.right.label [d49b80] = 穿过风暴\n# en: You thread the lightning like a needle and land soaked, laughing, with the parcel dry.\nopt.right.say.1 [419505] = 你像穿针一样穿过闪电，浑身湿透地落地，大笑着，包裹却是干的。\n# en: Lightning strikes close enough to stand your hair on end. You lose the broom, the parcel and the pay, and gain a limp.\nopt.right.say.2 [71c971] = 一道闪电劈得太近，你的头发都竖了起来。你丢了飞帚、包裹和报酬，得了一条瘸腿。\n# en: Surge across before the worst\nopt.up.label [eabf5d] = 趁最糟之前顺风冲过去\n# en: You call the wind behind you and go flat out, under the worst of the storm, and never stop. You land on the far bank soaked and shaking, with the parcel dry. The dispatcher pays double without a word.\nopt.up.say.1 [c94334] = 你唤风绕到身后，全速冲出去，从风暴最猛的地方下面穿过，一刻不停。你落在对岸，浑身湿透、直打哆嗦，包裹却是干的。调度员一言不发地付了双倍。\n\n@event courier_whispering_parcel\n# en: The Whispering Parcel\ntitle [00dbb7] = 会低语的包裹\n# en: Your parcel is addressed to \"K. Dumont, Stillwater House\". It is ice cold and it whispers when you aren't looking at it.\ntext [a7f392] = 你的包裹寄给“静水院，K. 杜蒙”。它冷得像冰，你不看它的时候，它会低语。\n# en: A brown paper parcel tied with black string on a courier's satchel, frost forming on its corners, faint whispers rising from it.\nscene [cef7c7] = 信使挎包上一个用黑绳捆着的牛皮纸包裹，边角结着霜，隐隐传出低语声。\n# en: Deliver it unopened\nopt.left.label [edbe83] = 原封不动地送达\n# en: Kael takes it without a word. His hands shake. The next day, he looks like he hasn't slept.\nopt.left.say.1 [6ad92e] = 凯尔一言不发地接过包裹。他的手在抖。第二天，他看起来像一夜没睡。\n# en: Peek inside\nopt.right.label [95f6de] = 偷看一眼\n# en: Inside: a letter in a cold, beautiful hand. \"The Star wakes. Come home, or open the door. Your family.\" You reseal it perfectly.\nopt.right.say.1 [49350a] = 里面是一封信，字迹冰冷而优美：“星辰苏醒了。回家，或者打开那扇门。——你的家人。”你把它完美地重新封好。\n# en: The seal hisses as it breaks. Kael is waiting at the door when you arrive. He saw. He says nothing, which is worse.\nopt.right.say.2 [1d94cc] = 封印裂开时发出嘶嘶声。你到的时候，凯尔已经等在门口了。他看见了。他什么也没说，这更糟。\n\n@event courier_sky_pirates\n# en: Sky Pirates\ntitle [933c94] = 空中海盗\n# en: Three figures on patched brooms drop out of a cloud and box you in. \"Toll,\" says their leader, a woman with a parrot that is also on a tiny broom. \"Eight crowns or the parcel.\"\ntext [0892d1] = 三个骑着补丁飞帚的身影从云里冲出来围住了你。“过路费，”领头的女人说，她的鹦鹉也骑着一把小飞帚，“八克朗，或者包裹。”\n# en: Three ragged figures on patched brooms surrounding a courier above the clouds, cutlasses glinting.\nscene [5f6143] = 云层之上，三个衣衫褴褛、骑着补丁飞帚的身影围住一名信使，弯刀闪闪发亮。\n# en: Pay the toll\nopt.left.label [a1a28f] = 交过路费\n# en: They let you pass with a salute. The parrot salutes too.\nopt.left.say.1 [de94eb] = 他们敬了个礼放你过去。鹦鹉也敬了个礼。\n# en: Outfly them\nopt.right.label [a811f6] = 甩掉他们\n# en: You dive through a cloudbank and lose them over the lake. The client tips extra for the excitement.\nopt.right.say.1 [b17031] = 你俯冲穿过云堤，在湖面上空甩掉了他们。客户因为这份刺激多给了小费。\n# en: They catch you, take the toll, the parcel and your hat. The parrot wears it.\nopt.right.say.2 [8905f7] = 他们追上了你，拿走了过路费、包裹，还有你的帽子。鹦鹉戴上了它。\n\n@event broom_courier_bad_landing\n# en: A Bad Landing\ntitle [cc3b0c] = 摔了一跤\n# en: {The courtyard flagstones are wet, and your broom has opinions about wet flagstones.|Another wet landing. Your knees remember the last one.|Wet flagstones again. You've learned to dread this courtyard.} {You come in too fast.|A gust takes the tail as you drop.|A pigeon chooses this moment to exist.} You hit the ground rolling, and your ankle says no.\ntext [afa176] = {庭院的石板是湿的，而你的扫帚对湿石板很有意见。|又是一次湿漉漉的降落。你的膝盖还记得上一次。|又是湿石板。你已经学会害怕这个庭院了。} {你降得太快了。|你往下落时一阵风掀起了扫帚尾。|一只鸽子偏偏挑这个时候出现。} 你滚着落了地，脚踝说：不行。\n# en: A courier sprawled on wet flagstones beside a broom, parcels scattered across a rain-slick courtyard.\nscene [20fcd9] = 一名信使摔倒在湿漉漉的石板上，扫帚在旁，包裹散落在雨水打湿的庭院里。\n# en: Finish the round anyway\nopt.left.label [bde896] = 坚持送完这一趟\n# en: {You limp the last three parcels to their doors. The dispatcher notes your time and, later, your ankle.|You finish the round on one good leg and a great deal of stubbornness. The bruise is spectacular by evening.|You finish the round with a limp and a grudge against pigeons. The dispatcher writes down both.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7abcb4] = {你一瘸一拐地把最后三个包裹送到门口。派件员记下了你的用时，后来又记下了你的脚踝。|你靠一条好腿和满满的倔劲送完了这一趟。到晚上，那块淤青壮观极了。|你一瘸一拐地送完了这一趟，还记恨上了鸽子。派件员把两样都记下了。}\n# en: Hand your sack to another courier\nopt.right.label [54b1a8] = 把邮袋交给别人\n# en: {Another courier takes your sack with a sympathetic wince. Half a shift's pay, and an evening with your foot up.|You sit on the loft steps with a cold cloth on your ankle while someone else flies your round. They keep half the pay.|You hand over your sack and spend the evening on the loft steps, foot up, glaring at pigeons.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0a316e] = {另一位信使同情地皱了皱眉，接过你的邮袋。半个班次的工钱，外加一晚上把脚架高。|你坐在阁楼台阶上，用凉布敷着脚踝，别人替你飞完了这一趟。工钱他们分走一半。|你交出邮袋，一晚上坐在阁楼台阶上，脚架得高高的，狠狠瞪着鸽子。}\n\n@event broom_courier_lost_parcel\n# en: One Parcel Short\ntitle [bffcca] = 少了一个包裹\n# en: {Somewhere between the loft and the lecture tower, a parcel has left your sack.|One parcel short, again. You've started counting at every landing.|Another parcel gone. You count at every landing, and still you lose one.} {It was marked \"Fragile\".|It was marked \"Urgent\", in red, twice.|It was addressed to a professor.}\ntext [4d5e5e] = {从阁楼到讲堂塔之间的某处，一个包裹离开了你的邮袋。|又少了一个包裹。你已经开始每次落地都数一遍了。|又丢了一个包裹。你每次落地都数，可还是丢了一个。} {上面写着“易碎”。|上面用红笔写着“加急”，写了两遍。|收件人是一位教授。}\n# en: A courier on a windy tower top turning an empty mail sack inside out, the lake far below.\nscene [6920d2] = 一名信使站在大风的塔顶上，把空邮袋翻了个底朝天，湖面远在下方。\n# en: Retrace your route\nopt.left.label [0a2eb5] = 沿原路找回去\n# en: {You find it in a hedge behind the greenhouses, whole and slightly damp. You deliver it late, with a story nobody believes.|It's on the boathouse roof. You climb up for it and deliver it with moss on your knees.|You find it in a gutter of the lecture tower and deliver it to the professor, who signs for it without looking up.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e91e55] = {你在温室后面的树篱里找到了它，完好无损，只是有点潮。你迟到着送达，还附上一个没人相信的故事。|它在船屋屋顶上。你爬上去拿，送到时膝盖上沾满了青苔。|你在讲堂塔的排水沟里找到了它，送到那位教授手里，教授头也没抬就签收了。}\n# en: {You search until dark. The loft takes the parcel's worth from your pay, and the dispatcher stops calling you reliable.|Nothing. The client complains twice, in writing, and the loss comes out of your wages.|The professor's parcel never turns up. The professor does, at the loft, and the loss comes out of your wages.}\nopt.left.say.2 [770b4a] = {你一直找到天黑。阁楼从你的工钱里扣了包裹的钱，派件员也不再说你靠得住了。|什么也没找到。客户写信投诉了两次，损失从你的工钱里扣。|教授的包裹始终没找到。教授本人倒是找到阁楼来了，损失从你的工钱里扣。}\n# en: Report it lost at once\nopt.right.label [5e3d08] = 立刻报告丢失\n# en: {The dispatcher writes it in the ledger and the loss in your pay. \"Happens,\" they say, in a voice that means it shouldn't.|You own up straight away. The loft takes it out of your wages, but the dispatcher seems to think better of you for it.|You report it at once. The dispatcher winces at the address, a professor's. It comes out of your pay, with an apology to write.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ead196] = {派件员把它记进账本，把损失记进你的工钱。“常有的事，”派件员说，语气却分明是说不该有。|你马上就承认了。阁楼从你的工钱里扣了钱，但派件员似乎对你更看得起了。|你立刻报了丢失。派件员看到地址是位教授，皱了皱眉。钱从你的工钱里扣，你还得写一封道歉信。}\n\n@event job_off_shift\n# en: Not Your Shift\ntitle [4d3d09] = 不是你的班\n# en: You turn up for work out of habit. The roster by the door says your shifts this term are done, and whoever runs the place says so too. \"Nobody pays you to be here today. Go and have a life.\"\ntext [d6e252] = 你出于习惯来上工。门边的值班表写着你这学期的班都上完了，管事的人也这么说：“今天没人付钱让你待在这儿。去过你自己的日子吧。”\n# en: A duty roster pinned by a staff door, every one of a student's shifts for the term already ticked off.\nscene [dbf031] = 员工门边钉着一张值班表，一个学生这学期的每一班都已打了勾。\n# en: Take the time for yourself\nopt.left.label [52cbbe] = 把时间留给自己\n# en: {~You leave before anyone can change their mind, and spend the hours on nothing in particular. It's wonderful.|You take the advice. The place gets on without you, and so, it turns out, do you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [78a31c] = {~你趁别人改主意之前就走了，把这几个钟头花在无所事事上。美极了。|你听了劝。这地方没有你照样转，而你，原来没有它也照样过得好。}\n# en: Stay and help anyway\nopt.right.label [0f8ed9] = 还是留下帮忙\n# en: {~You stay, unpaid. Someone notices. Nobody thanks you, and you're tired the next morning.|You lend a hand for an hour. It's not a shift and it doesn't pay, but people remember who stayed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [990370] = {~你留了下来，没有工钱。有人注意到了。没人道谢，第二天早上你很累。|你搭了一个钟头的手。这不算一班，也没有钱，但大家记得谁留下了。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/journal_cards.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/journal_cards.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event recap_year_one\n# en: The First Year, Read Back\ntitle [cefe2e] = 重读第一年\n# en: Before the summer swallows it, you read your first year back in your journal. You can't believe how lost you were in the autumn. {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3: By spring there are more friends in it than fit at one table.|{bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 1: By spring there's a friend in it, or two, who would notice if you went missing.|By spring there are still more meals alone in it than you'd like.}} {gpa >= 3: Your marks were better than you feared.|Your marks were what they were.} {clues(hollowstar) >= 1: And on the last page there's a question you can't put down.|}\ntext [d21606] = 趁夏天还没把它吞掉，你在日记里把第一年重读了一遍。你简直不敢相信，秋天时自己有多迷茫。{bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3: 到了春天，日记里的朋友已经多得一张桌子坐不下了。|{bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 1: 到了春天，日记里有了一两个朋友，你要是不见了，他们会发现的。|到了春天，日记里一个人吃饭的日子还是比你希望的多。}} {gpa >= 3: 你的成绩比你担心的要好。|你的成绩就是那样了。} {clues(hollowstar) >= 1: 而在最后一页，有一个你放不下的问题。|}\n# en: A journal open on a windowsill in summer light, its pages crowded with a year of small handwriting, a pressed leaf between two of them.\nscene [f5200a] = 夏日阳光下窗台上摊开的一本日记，书页上挤满了一整年的小字，两页之间夹着一片压平的叶子。\n# en: Read it all again, slowly\nopt.left.label [65ee53] = 慢慢地再读一遍\n# en: You read it twice, the second time for the parts you skimmed: the wrong tower, the first spell, the first person who saved you a seat. Then you buy a second journal, because this one is full.\nopt.left.say.1 [6c65a1] = 你读了两遍，第二遍专读那些跳过的地方：走错的塔，第一道咒语，第一个给你留座位的人。然后你买了第二本日记，因为这本已经写满了。\n# en: Write down what you want next year\nopt.right.label [223177] = 写下明年想要什么\n# en: You turn to a clean page and write a list for next year, honest and slightly embarrassing. You read it again before term, and cross one thing off already.\nopt.right.say.1 [6483c4] = 你翻到空白的一页，给明年列了一张清单，坦白得有点难为情。开学前你又读了一遍，已经划掉了一项。\n\n@event recap_year_two\n# en: The Second Year, Read Back\ntitle [8f7460] = 重读第二年\n# en: The second journal is fuller than the first. You read the year back over a long summer evening. {bonds(partner) > 0: There's a name in it that wasn't there last summer, written more often than any other.|{bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3: The same names come up on nearly every page now, which is what having friends looks like, written down.|There are new names in it, and some old ones have gone quiet.}} {renown_known(): Somewhere in the spring, people you've never met started to know your face.|Hardly anyone outside your year knows your name yet, which has its uses.} {clues(hollowstar) >= 2: The question at the back has grown pages of its own.|}\ntext [05583d] = 第二本日记比第一本写得更满。你花了一个漫长的夏夜把这一年重读了一遍。{bonds(partner) > 0: 里面有一个去年夏天还没有的名字，出现得比哪个都多。|{bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3: 如今几乎每一页都有同样几个名字，有了朋友，写下来就是这个样子。|里面多了些新名字，也有些老名字沉寂了。}} {renown_known(): 春天的某个时候，素不相识的人开始认得你的脸了。|你们年级以外几乎还没人知道你的名字，这自有它的好处。} {clues(hollowstar) >= 2: 日记最后那个问题，已经自己长出了好几页。|}\n# en: Two worn journals side by side on a summer desk, the newer one thicker, a quill resting across them.\nscene [8632c7] = 夏日书桌上并排放着两本磨旧的日记，新的那本更厚，一支羽毛笔横搁在上面。\n# en: Mark the pages worth keeping\nopt.left.label [f6819c] = 标出值得留下的几页\n# en: You go through with a ribbon and mark the pages you'd save from a fire. There are more of them than you expected, and fewer of them are about exams than you'd have guessed a year ago.\nopt.left.say.1 [e5a301] = 你拿一根丝带翻了一遍，标出那些着火时你会抢出来的页。比你预想的多，而且关于考试的，比一年前的你猜的要少。\n# en: Count what's left to do\nopt.right.label [039797] = 数数还剩什么要做\n# en: Two years left. You count the courses, the doors you haven't opened and the people you haven't thanked, and make a list for each. The third list is the longest.\nopt.right.say.1 [27c7d7] = 还剩两年。你数了数要上的课、还没打开的门、还没道谢的人，各列了一张清单。第三张最长。\n\n@event recap_year_three\n# en: The Third Year, Read Back\ntitle [c6ea79] = 重读第三年\n# en: Three journals now. Next year is the last, and the castle will want everything. Before it does, you read the third year back. {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) >= 3: The people in it would walk into the lake for you, and you're fairly sure you'd do the same.|{bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) >= 1: There are one or two people in it you'll write to for the rest of your life.|It's a quieter book than you'd once hoped, and some of the quiet you chose.}} {renown_famous(): Half the castle knows your name now, and some of what they say is even true.|{renown_known(): People know your face in the corridors, and nod.|You've stayed mostly unnoticed, and done a great deal there.}}\ntext [104a26] = 现在已经有三本日记了。明年是最后一年，城堡会要走你的一切。趁它还没开口，你把第三年重读了一遍。{bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) >= 3: 日记里的那些人会为你走进湖里，而你几乎可以肯定，你也会为他们这么做。|{bonds(friend) + bonds(close) + bonds(partner) >= 1: 里面有一两个人，你这辈子都会给他们写信。|这是一本比你曾经希望的更安静的书，而其中有些安静是你自己选的。}} {renown_famous(): 如今半个城堡都知道你的名字，他们说的有些甚至是真的。|{renown_known(): 走廊里的人认得你的脸，会冲你点头。|你大体上没被人注意，却在那里做了很多事。}}\n# en: A stack of three journals on a summer windowsill, the top one open, a student sitting with their knees up reading it, the towers far off through the glass.\nscene [0b7c7f] = 夏日窗台上叠着三本日记，最上面那本摊开着，一个学生抱膝坐着读它，玻璃外远处是那几座塔。\n# en: Read the first one again\nopt.left.label [fd8564] = 重读第一本\n# en: You go back to the first journal, the one from the autumn you were lost in. You laugh at most of it. One page, about the first person who saved you a seat, you read three times.\nopt.left.say.1 [2224ce] = 你翻回第一本日记，就是你迷路的那个秋天写的。大部分内容让你发笑。有一页写的是第一个给你留座位的人，你读了三遍。\n# en: Start the fourth one today\nopt.right.label [55f459] = 今天就开始第四本\n# en: You open a fresh journal and write the date, and under it the one thing you most want from the last year. You don't show it to anyone. You'll know in a year whether you kept it.\nopt.right.say.1 [109405] = 你翻开一本新日记，写下日期，在下面写下你最想从最后一年得到的那一件事。你没给任何人看。一年后你就会知道，自己有没有做到。\n\n@event nudge_quickquill\n# en: How's My Quill?\ntitle [fb930f] = 我的羽毛笔怎么样了？\n# en: \"How's my quill?\" says Nessa, reading over your shoulder. On Tuesday the spare quill announced the engagement of two of the gargoyles and slipped it into a proof. \"You took it home half a year ago. Have you tamed it yet, or has it tamed you?\"\ntext [14d1a4] = “我的羽毛笔怎么样了？”内莎越过你的肩膀看着说。星期二，那支备用羽毛笔宣布了两座石像鬼订婚的消息，还偷偷塞进了校样。“你半年前把它带回家的。是你驯服了它，还是它驯服了你？”\n# en: A newspaper office, an editor leaning over a student's shoulder, a quill on the desk writing something rude in the margin all by itself.\nscene [a9911b] = 报社编辑部里，一位编辑俯身越过一个学生的肩膀，桌上一支羽毛笔正自己在页边写着什么不礼貌的话。\n# en: \"I'm working on it.\"\nopt.left.label [c61049] = “我正在努力。”\n# en: She gives you the good ink from her own drawer. \"Proper ink first,\" she says, \"then sit a deadline night with it. That's where quills learn manners. Mine did.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [69db53] = 她从自己的抽屉里拿出好墨水给你。“先换上像样的墨水，”她说，“然后陪它熬一个截稿夜。羽毛笔就是在那儿学规矩的。我的就是。”\n# en: \"It's a lost cause.\"\nopt.right.label [cdd6be] = “它没救了。”\n# en: \"Nothing's a lost cause,\" says Nessa, \"except the gargoyles.\" She doesn't take the quill back. She tells you to give it until the end of term, and goes off to cut the engagement.\nopt.right.say.1 [0030f1] = “没有什么是没救的，”内莎说，“除了那两座石像鬼。”她没有把羽毛笔收回去，叫你再给它一个学期，然后走去删掉那条订婚消息。\n\n@event nudge_footnote\n# en: Still Guarding It\ntitle [6c8c65] = 还守着它\n# en: Footnote is still on the overdue drawer, eleven years and half a year now, and the head librarian has started leaving his saucer there. When you pass, he stops washing and stares at you, as if you'd promised him something and he has been very patient about it.\ntext [3277a6] = 脚注还坐在那个逾期的抽屉上，如今已经十一年零半年了，馆长开始把它的小碟子直接放在那儿。你经过时，它停下舔毛，盯着你，好像你答应过它什么，而它一直很有耐心。\n# en: A library catalogue cabinet, a large grey cat sitting on one drawer, glaring at a student with an armful of books.\nscene [14ebc5] = 图书馆的目录柜，一只大灰猫坐在其中一个抽屉上，瞪着一个抱着一摞书的学生。\n# en: Go looking for the book again\nopt.left.label [bb05cf] = 再去找那本书\n# en: You spend the evening on the shelves that wander, with the card in your hand. They move twice while you're in them. You don't find the book, but you find where it was last week, which Footnote seems to accept as a start.\nopt.left.say.1 [7aef27] = 你拿着那张卡片，在那些会游走的书架间待了一晚上。你在里面时，它们挪了两次。你没找到那本书，但找到了它上星期待过的地方，脚注似乎认为这算是个开始。\n# en: Tell him you're still looking\nopt.right.label [c6e55a] = 告诉它你还在找\n# en: You tell Footnote, solemnly, that you're still looking. He considers this for a long time, then goes back to washing, which seems to mean he'll allow it for now.\nopt.right.say.1 [64ba8c] = 你郑重地告诉脚注，你还在找。它考虑了很久，然后继续舔毛，这似乎是说它暂且允许了。\n\n@event nudge_two_taps\n# en: How Far Have You Got?\ntitle [929fa8] = 你进展到哪儿了？\n# en: The landlady catches your eye across the Newt and taps the bar twice, gently, like a question. It's half a year since she said you could earn it. \"Learn the room, buy the room, work the room,\" she says, polishing a glass. \"How far have you got?\"\ntext [48e316] = 老板娘隔着蝾螈酒馆对上你的目光，轻轻敲了两下吧台，像在提问。她说你可以自己挣来这一手，已经是半年前的事了。“摸清这屋子，请这屋子喝一轮，再在这屋子里干活，”她擦着杯子说，“你进展到哪儿了？”\n# en: A crowded tavern at night, a landlady behind the bar polishing a glass, tapping the counter twice with one finger, looking across at a student.\nscene [cf2e67] = 夜里拥挤的酒馆，老板娘在吧台后擦着杯子，用一根手指在台面上敲了两下，看向一个学生。\n# en: Ask where to start\nopt.left.label [c651c6] = 问该从哪儿开始\n# en: \"The corner table,\" she says. \"He's told everyone his story but you. Then the round, then a double shift. In that order, or the room knows.\" She taps the bar twice at you, and you catch half the sentence.\nopt.left.say.1 [37dd21] = “角落那张桌子，”她说，“他的故事讲给了所有人，就差你。然后请一轮，再上一个双班。按这个顺序来，不然这屋子会知道。”她冲你敲了两下吧台，这一次你听清了半句。\n# en: Say you'd rather just pour\nopt.right.label [cd7c6a] = 说你宁愿只管倒酒\n# en: \"Pouring's honest,\" she says, and leaves it there. The tips are good that night. She doesn't tap the bar at you again that term.\nopt.right.say.1 [692fc7] = “倒酒是老实活儿，”她说，就此作罢。那晚的小费不错。那个学期她再没冲你敲过吧台。\n\n@event nudge_brace\n# en: Your Name on the Board\ntitle [984715] = 板上你的名字\n# en: Lyra stops you outside the duelling hall. \"Your name's been under that dummy for half a year,\" she says. \"Someone's drawn a second stretcher.\" She folds her arms. \"Either try properly, or let me rub it out. I'm not having it look like you're scared.\"\ntext [f16dc8] = 莱拉在决斗厅外拦住你。“你的名字在那个假人底下挂了半年了，”她说，“有人又画了第二副担架。”她抱起胳膊。“要么认真试一次，要么让我擦掉。我可不想让人觉得你怕了。”\n# en: A duelling hall doorway, a chalk board on the wall with a name under a drawing of a practice dummy and two drawings of stretchers, a student with folded arms beside it.\nscene [a86d20] = 决斗厅门口，墙上一块黑板，一个名字写在练习假人的画下面，旁边画着两副担架，一个学生抱着胳膊站在一旁。\n# en: \"Leave it. I'll try.\"\nopt.left.label [459542] = “别擦。我会试的。”\n# en: \"Good,\" says Lyra, and rubs out the second stretcher herself. \"Read the old captain's notes first. Nobody does. Then come and stand my barrage, and Bram's, and stop flinching.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [54f66c] = “很好，”莱拉说，亲手擦掉了第二副担架。“先读那位老队长的笔记。没人读。然后来扛我的连击，还有布拉姆的，别再缩了。”\n# en: \"Rub it out.\"\nopt.right.label [743a7b] = “擦掉吧。”\n# en: She rubs it out, slowly, so that you have to watch. The dummy goes on hexing whoever passes. Lyra says nothing more about it, which is worse.\nopt.right.say.1 [39b95d] = 她慢慢地擦，让你不得不看着。那个假人继续向每个路过的人甩恶咒。莱拉再没提过这件事，这更糟。\n\n@event nudge_red_ink\n# en: Still Waiting\ntitle [d19fa6] = 还在等\n# en: There's a note in red ink tucked into the crack in the lecture tower stair, in the small round hand you know. It says only: \"Still waiting for the next draft. Paragraph four is still the best thing you've written. Don't make it the last.\"\ntext [e43562] = 讲堂塔楼梯的墙缝里塞着一张红墨水字条，是你认得的那手小而圆的字。上面只写着：“还在等你的下一稿。第四段仍然是你写过最好的东西。别让它成为最后一段。”\n# en: A narrow stone stair in a lecture tower, a folded note in red ink tucked into a crack in the wall at eye level.\nscene [e72c72] = 讲堂塔里一段狭窄的石阶，一张红墨水写的折好的字条塞在与视线齐平的墙缝里。\n# en: Write the next draft tonight\nopt.left.label [a94276] = 今晚就写下一稿\n# en: You write it that night and leave it on the stair in the morning. By evening it's gone, and someone has drawn a single small red tick on the step where it lay.\nopt.left.say.1 [71b8a1] = 你当晚就写好了，第二天早上放在楼梯上。到傍晚它不见了，放过它的那级台阶上，有人画了一个小小的红勾。\n# en: Put the note in a drawer\nopt.right.label [d9085e] = 把字条放进抽屉\n# en: You put the note in a drawer, under the other pages. It doesn't get any easier to forget, and the stair is on your way to everything.\nopt.right.say.1 [2a2ace] = 你把字条放进抽屉，压在其他几页下面。它并没有因此更容易忘掉，何况那段楼梯是你去哪儿都要经过的。\n\n@event nudge_wind_jar\n# en: The Jar Is Bored\ntitle [ae51ad] = 罐子等烦了\n# en: The jar of wind has moved. It was on the dead-letter shelf; this morning it's on your peg, going round and round, and every broom in the loft is leaning toward it. The loft master doesn't look up from her ledger. \"It does that,\" she says, \"when it's bored of waiting.\"\ntext [72ae19] = 那罐风挪了地方。它原本在死信架上；今天早上却到了你的挂钩上，转啊转的，阁楼里每一把扫帚都朝它倾斜。阁楼主管头也不抬地看着账本。“它会这样的，”她说，“等烦了的时候。”\n# en: A courier loft full of brooms on pegs, all leaning the same way, toward a stoppered glass jar with a small wind going round inside it.\nscene [97ccef] = 信使阁楼里挂满了扫帚，全都朝同一个方向倾斜，朝着一只塞着塞子的玻璃罐，罐里有一小股风在打转。\n# en: Ask who flew with it\nopt.left.label [9c8a01] = 问是谁带着它飞过\n# en: \"Before my time,\" she says, \"but the old logbooks are in the trunk.\" You read them until dark. The faded name on the jar's label turns up three times, always on the worst weather days.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f31e4] = “在我之前的事了，”她说，“不过旧航行日志在箱子里。”你一直读到天黑。罐子标签上那个褪色的名字出现了三次，每次都是天气最坏的日子。\n# en: Put it back on the shelf\nopt.right.label [bf3750] = 把它放回架子上\n# en: You put it back on the dead-letter shelf. In the morning it's on your peg again, and the brooms look at you reproachfully.\nopt.right.say.1 [0f3628] = 你把它放回死信架。第二天早上，它又出现在你的挂钩上，扫帚们责备地看着你。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/kit/item_stories.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/kit/item_stories.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event item_borrowed_initials\n# en: Initials on the Handle\ntitle [656344] = 杖柄上的缩写\n# en: Grimsby sees your borrowed wand and asks to hold it, very carefully. Someone scratched initials into the handle years ago, and he turns it to the light. \"Oh,\" he says. \"Oh, I know this hand. She used to buy maps from me. She went off to keep a lighthouse.\"\ntext [6ca4d8] = 格林斯比看到你借来的魔杖，请求拿一下，非常小心。多年前有人在杖柄上刻了姓名缩写，他把它转到亮处。“哦，”他说，“哦，我认得这笔迹。她以前常来我这儿买地图。后来她去守灯塔了。”\n# en: An old bookseller peering through half-moon glasses at the scratched initials on a battered student wand.\nscene [b15f73] = 一位老书商透过半月形眼镜，端详一根破旧学生魔杖上刻着的缩写。\n# en: Ask him about her\nopt.left.label [2f0782] = 问他关于她的事\n# en: He talks about her for an hour, and then about lighthouses for another. You leave knowing a great deal about lamps, and that the wand once belonged to someone who wanted to see a long way.\nopt.left.say.1 [62cc74] = 他讲了她一个小时，又讲了一个小时灯塔。你离开时对灯懂了很多，也知道了这根魔杖曾属于一个想看得很远的人。\n# en: Ask if it's worth anything\nopt.right.label [95481a] = 问它值不值钱\n# en: \"Worth?\" He hands it back as if you'd spoken ill of the dead. \"It's worth four years of somebody. Mind it.\" Then, because he can't help himself, he buys the old maps you've been using as bookmarks, at a fair price, and doesn't quite forgive you.\nopt.right.say.1 [cb58a4] = “值钱？”他把魔杖还给你，好像你说了死者的坏话。“它值某个人的四年。爱惜它。”然后，因为忍不住，他按公道价买下了你一直拿来当书签的旧地图，却还是没完全原谅你。\n\n@event item_borrowed_sideways\n# en: It Sparks Sideways\ntitle [93456d] = 火花跑偏了\n# en: Your borrowed wand chooses today, of all days, to spark sideways. The flame you aimed at the target lands in the hat stand, which takes it personally. The class is delighted. The professor less so.\ntext [e7ae55] = 你借来的魔杖偏偏挑今天把火花打偏。你瞄准靶子的火焰落到了衣帽架上，衣帽架很当回事。全班都乐了。教授没那么乐。\n# en: A classroom full of students casting at targets, one student's spark flying off at a right angle into a hat stand.\nscene [b95ab8] = 满是对着靶子施法的学生的教室，一个学生的火花拐了个直角飞进衣帽架。\n# en: Laugh it off and try again\nopt.left.label [c9b1fa] = 一笑了之，再试一次\n# en: You laugh, the class laughs, and on the second try the spark goes where you send it. The hat stand is put outside to recover.\nopt.left.say.1 [7980e0] = 你笑了，全班也笑了，第二次火花飞到了你要它去的地方。衣帽架被搬到外面去休养。\n# en: Blame the wand, loudly\nopt.right.label [448428] = 大声怪魔杖\n# en: \"It's borrowed!\" you protest. Someone at the back says \"Mir trades them in at the market, you know.\" You file that away and the hat stand forgives no one.\nopt.right.say.1 [a7db57] = “它是借来的！”你抗议。后排有人说：“米尔在集市上收旧杖抵价，你知道吧。”你记下了这件事，衣帽架谁也不原谅。\n\n@event item_hazel_tuned\n# en: Mir Tunes It\ntitle [e70409] = 米尔调校它\n# en: Mir spots your hazel wand and makes a noise like a kettle. \"Can I? I can. Two minutes.\" They clamp it in a vice, screw a loupe into one eye and start talking to the wand much more kindly than they talk to people.\ntext [2358ec] = 米尔看到你的榛木魔杖，发出了水壶烧开似的声音。“我能看看吗？我能。两分钟。”TA把它夹进台钳，把放大镜拧进一只眼睛，开始对魔杖说话，比对人说话温柔多了。\n# en: A cluttered workshop bench, an artificer with a jeweller's loupe bent over a hazel wand clamped in a tiny vice.\nscene [e831e4] = 杂乱的工作台，一个做奇械的学生戴着珠宝放大镜，俯身看着夹在小台钳里的榛木魔杖。\n# en: Let them tinker\nopt.left.label [31c247] = 让TA捣鼓\n# en: Two minutes is two hours, and Mir has you hold the wand the whole time while it drinks from your hand. Mir hands it back humming, and you swear it's quicker. \"Tuned,\" they say. \"Probably. Don't cast anything near a cabbage for a day.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [fffd3a] = 两分钟变成了两小时，米尔让你一直握着魔杖，任它从你手上汲取魔力。米尔哼着歌把它还给你，你发誓它快了。“调好了，”TA说，“大概吧。一天之内别在卷心菜附近施法。”\n# en: Ask about the notebooks on the shelf\nopt.right.label [973108] = 问起架子上的笔记本\n# en: Mir goes pink and pulls one down: three years of sketches, wands and owls and things with wings. \"Don't spill on them,\" they say, and talk you through every page.\nopt.right.say.1 [5654cf] = 米尔脸红了，抽出一本：三年的草图，魔杖、猫头鹰和长翅膀的东西。“别洒东西上去，”TA说，然后一页一页给你讲。\n\n@event item_hazel_water\n# en: Finding Water\ntitle [32f0bd] = 找水\n# en: Maud sees your hazel wand and chuckles. \"A hazel one, is it? My gran found three wells with a hazel twig and a bad temper. Hold it loose, dearie. Let it pull.\"\ntext [7762b7] = 莫德看到你的榛木魔杖，咯咯笑了。“榛木的？我奶奶靠一根榛木枝和一副坏脾气找到过三口井。松松地拿着，亲爱的。让它拉你。”\n# en: An old woman in a garden holding a forked twig of hazel loosely in two hands, a student copying her with a wand.\nscene [a52e30] = 花园里，一个老妇人两手松松地握着一根分叉的榛木枝，一个学生拿着魔杖学她。\n# en: Learn the trick\nopt.left.label [8a7395] = 学这一招\n# en: You walk her garden in circles for an hour with the wand held loose. Then it dips, hard, over the old pump. Maud cackles. \"There. You'll never be thirsty.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [acb9af] = 你松松地拿着魔杖，在她的花园里绕了一个小时圈。然后它在旧水泵上方猛地一沉。莫德咯咯大笑。“看。你这辈子都不会渴着了。”\n# en: Thank her and leave the trick\nopt.right.label [626b52] = 谢过她，不学了\n# en: \"Suit yourself,\" says Maud, and gives you a jar of something pickled for the road. It is very good, and you never find out what it was.\nopt.right.say.1 [d823f8] = “随你，”莫德说，给你一罐腌菜带着路上吃。非常好吃，你始终不知道那是什么。\n\n@event item_robes_singed\n# en: Singed\ntitle [8cf43f] = 烧焦了\n# en: A stray hex from the next circle catches your sleeve. There's a smell of hot wool and a small, sad hole. Somewhere in the village, a tailor feels a disturbance.\ntext [79e02f] = 隔壁圈子飞来一道流弹般的恶咒，燎到了你的袖子。一股羊毛烧焦的味道，一个难过的小洞。村子里某处，一位裁缝感到了不安。\n# en: A duelling hall with a scorch mark on the floor and a student patting out a smouldering sleeve.\nscene [c9a707] = 决斗厅的地板上有一块焦痕，一个学生正拍灭冒烟的袖子。\n# en: Take it down to the tailor\nopt.left.label [993c2c] = 拿去给裁缝\n# en: The tailor tuts, darns it in five minutes, and charges you for ten. \"Duelling hall,\" she says. \"It's always the duelling hall.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [965c42] = 裁缝啧啧两声，五分钟就补好了，收了你十分钟的钱。“决斗厅，”她说，“总是决斗厅。”\n# en: Wear the hole with pride\nopt.right.label [f179c5] = 骄傲地穿着破洞\n# en: You wear it singed. By the end of the week two first-years have singed their own sleeves on purpose. You feel responsible, and slightly famous.\nopt.right.say.1 [fab02b] = 你就穿着烧焦的袍子。不到一周，两个一年级生故意把自己的袖子也烧了。你觉得有点责任，也有点出名。\n\n@event item_robes_copied\n# en: A First-Year Copies Your Robes\ntitle [8e6a77] = 一年级生模仿你的长袍\n# en: A first-year has turned up in midnight-blue robes exactly like yours, down to the silver at the hem. They have also, you notice, started walking the way you do.\ntext [5c75c6] = 一个一年级生穿着和你一模一样的午夜蓝长袍出现了，连下摆的银边都一样。你还注意到，TA开始学你走路的样子。\n# en: A courtyard where a first-year in brand-new midnight-blue robes is trying very hard to walk exactly like an older student.\nscene [bdf049] = 庭院里，一个穿着崭新午夜蓝长袍的一年级生，正努力学着一位高年级生走路。\n# en: Tell them they look good\nopt.left.label [082912] = 告诉TA很好看\n# en: They go scarlet with joy. By supper the whole first-year corridor is wearing blue. The tailor sends you a thank-you note and no discount.\nopt.left.say.1 [dd9966] = TA高兴得满脸通红。到晚饭时，整条一年级走廊的人都穿上了蓝色。裁缝寄来一封感谢信，没有折扣。\n# en: Walk faster\nopt.right.label [ce8449] = 走快点\n# en: You lose them on the east stair. They find you again at dinner, still walking like you, slightly out of breath.\nopt.right.say.1 [57bf8a] = 你在东楼梯甩掉了TA。晚饭时TA又找到了你，还在学你走路，有点喘。\n\n@event item_ashwood_lyra\n# en: Lyra Notices\ntitle [46f916] = 莱拉注意到了\n# en: Lyra's eyes go straight to the ashwood wand in your hand. \"Ashwood,\" she says. \"Duellist's wood. Mother says it's for people who can't win on talent.\" She draws her family's pale wand. \"One bout. Ashwood against Vaneshade. Unless you're shy.\"\ntext [9d68ed] = 莱拉的目光直接落在你手里的白蜡木魔杖上。“白蜡木，”她说，“决斗者的木头。母亲说那是给靠天赋赢不了的人用的。”她抽出家传的浅色魔杖。“一局。白蜡木对凡妮希德。除非你害羞。”\n# en: A duelling hall, a proud student with a pale family wand looking narrowly at the dark ashwood wand in another student's hand.\nscene [b09be0] = 决斗厅里，一个骄傲的学生拿着浅色的家传魔杖，眯眼看着另一个学生手里深色的白蜡木魔杖。\n# en: Decline, with a bow\nopt.left.label [6958fe] = 鞠躬婉拒\n# en: \"Another time,\" you say. Lyra looks almost disappointed, then annoyed at herself for looking it. She practises alone, very loudly, for an hour.\nopt.left.say.1 [9585cd] = “改天吧，”你说。莱拉看起来几乎有点失望，随即又为自己露出失望而恼火。她一个人练了一个小时，动静很大。\n# en: Accept the bout\nopt.right.label [fa2b7c] = 接受比试\n# en: Her ward goes up and the ashwood bites straight through it. Lyra stares at the scorch on her sleeve. \"Again,\" she says. It's the most respectful thing she's ever said to you.\nopt.right.say.1 [dc7efe] = 她的护界升起，白蜡木一口咬穿了它。莱拉盯着自己袖子上的焦痕。“再来，”她说。这是她对你说过最尊重的一句话。\n# en: You trade hex for hex until Thorne wanders in and sends you both to supper. Lyra says \"Adequate\" to your wand, not to you.\nopt.right.say.2 [5030e1] = 你们一咒换一咒，直到索恩教授晃进来，把你们俩赶去吃晚饭。莱拉对你的魔杖说了句“还行”，不是对你。\n# en: She's faster than the ashwood, and she knows it. \"Talent,\" she says, and walks off. The wand hums in your hand, sulking.\nopt.right.say.3 [5d8d47] = 她比白蜡木快，她自己也知道。“天赋，”她说完就走了。魔杖在你手里嗡嗡作响，闹着脾气。\n\n@event item_frost_ward_cold\n# en: Cold Things Think Twice\ntitle [480898] = 冷东西也会三思\n# en: The cold comes into the castle tonight like something looking for someone. Frost runs along the corridor windows ahead of you, then stops at your feet and goes round, as if it had remembered its manners. Your collar, where Ilse's ward is sewn, is warm.\ntext [e853f4] = 今晚寒气进了城堡，像在找什么人。霜沿着走廊的窗户跑在你前面，到你脚边却停住，绕了过去，好像想起了礼貌。你的衣领，缝着伊尔泽护符的地方，是暖的。\n# en: A dark castle corridor at night, frost spreading across the windows, a student walking through it untouched.\nscene [9a59df] = 夜里漆黑的城堡走廊，霜爬满窗户，一个学生毫发无伤地穿过。\n# en: Walk the long corridor anyway\nopt.left.label [1ccbb4] = 照样走完长廊\n# en: You walk the whole east wing in the frost and it parts for you every time. You get back to bed cold-footed and oddly proud.\nopt.left.say.1 [74aa76] = 你在霜里走完了整个东翼，霜每次都为你让开。你回到床上，脚冰凉，却莫名地骄傲。\n# en: Go straight to bed\nopt.right.label [da9f17] = 直接上床\n# en: You don't push your luck. In the morning the frost on your window has drawn a careful circle round your handprint.\nopt.right.say.1 [c5beb7] = 你没去碰运气。早上，你窗上的霜在你的手印周围画了一个仔细的圆。\n\n@event item_frost_ward_thread\n# en: A Northern Thread\ntitle [23c4d6] = 北境的针脚\n# en: Kael stops beside you in the corridor and, without asking, touches your collar where Ilse's ward is sewn. \"That's my mother's stitch,\" he says. \"Ilse learned it from her.\" He takes his hand back as if the thread were hot.\ntext [1e5175] = 凯尔在走廊里停在你身边，没问一声就碰了碰你衣领上缝着伊尔泽护符的地方。“这是我母亲的针法，”他说，“伊尔泽跟她学的。”他把手收回去，好像那线是烫的。\n# en: A pale northern student touching the stitched collar of another student's robe, his face unreadable.\nscene [228b49] = 一个苍白的北境学生摸着另一个学生长袍上缝过的衣领，脸上看不出表情。\n# en: Ask him about his mother\nopt.left.label [6cccd0] = 问他关于母亲的事\n# en: He says three sentences, which for Kael is a speech. She stitched wards into everything, even the dogs' collars. Then he goes, but he walks slower than usual.\nopt.left.say.1 [61d1b6] = 他说了三句话，对凯尔来说这算演讲了。她把护界缝进所有东西里，连狗项圈都缝。然后他走了，但走得比平时慢。\n# en: Let it be\nopt.right.label [71cc4c] = 随它去\n# en: You say nothing, and he seems grateful. At supper he passes you the bread without being asked.\nopt.right.say.1 [67b172] = 你什么也没说，他似乎很感激。晚饭时，他没等你开口就把面包递了过来。\n\n@event item_star_coin_turning\n# en: Turning It Over\ntitle [795c77] = 翻来覆去\n# en: You've been turning the black coin over in your fingers again without noticing. It's cold enough to ache. You dreamed last night of cold, dark water, and woke with it in your fist.\ntext [5ae26d] = 你又不知不觉地把那枚黑硬币在手指间翻来覆去。它冷得发疼。昨晚你梦见冰冷漆黑的水，醒来时它攥在你拳头里。\n# en: A student awake in a dark dormitory, turning a black coin over and over in their fingers by the window.\nscene [7d7141] = 漆黑的宿舍里，一个学生醒着，在窗边把一枚黑硬币翻来覆去。\n# en: Put it at the bottom of your trunk\nopt.left.label [e09b00] = 把它压在箱底\n# en: You bury it under your winter socks and sleep well. In the morning it's in your pocket. You don't remember putting it there.\nopt.left.say.1 [02d187] = 你把它埋在冬袜下面，睡得很好。早上它在你口袋里。你不记得放过它。\n# en: Keep turning it\nopt.right.label [aae84e] = 接着翻\n# en: You turn it until dawn. It never warms. By morning you know every scratch on it, and you couldn't say why that seemed important.\nopt.right.say.1 [a8da6e] = 你翻到天亮。它始终没有暖起来。到早上你认得它身上每一道划痕，却说不出这为什么重要。\n\n@event item_star_coin_kael\n# en: Kael Sees It\ntitle [c52d3d] = 凯尔看到了\n# en: The black coin slides out of your pocket onto the library table. Kael, across from you, goes completely still. He doesn't touch it. He doesn't look at you. \"Put that away,\" he says quietly.\ntext [d10682] = 黑硬币从你口袋里滑出来，落在图书馆的桌子上。对面的凯尔一动不动。他没碰它，也没看你。“收起来，”他轻声说。\n# en: A northern student going very still at the sight of a black coin on a library table.\nscene [fefe49] = 一个北境学生看到图书馆桌上的黑硬币，僵住了。\n# en: Put it away\nopt.left.label [c1c78b] = 收起来\n# en: You put it away. After a long while he goes back to his book, but he doesn't turn a page for the rest of the hour.\nopt.left.say.1 [d29b3d] = 你收了起来。过了很久，他才回到书上，可那一个小时他一页都没翻。\n# en: Ask him what it is\nopt.right.label [44657d] = 问他那是什么\n# en: \"Nothing you want,\" he says, and closes his book, and leaves. You notice his hands were shaking. The coin is very cold in your pocket on the way back.\nopt.right.say.1 [a2255d] = “你不会想要的东西，”他说完合上书走了。你注意到他的手在抖。回去的路上，口袋里的硬币冰凉。\n\n@event item_almanac_lighthouse\n# en: The Lighthouse Page\ntitle [a33e7f] = 灯塔那一页\n# en: Page forty of the almanac is all Grimsby: the lighthouse at the point, its lamp times for every season, and in the margin, very small, \"best on a clear night in Frostcall, if one goes alone\".\ntext [7efbbf] = 年历的第四十页全是格林斯比的字：海角的灯塔，每个季节的亮灯时间，页边还有一行很小的字：“霜唤月晴夜最好，独自去。”\n# en: A student reading a tiny thick almanac by candlelight, a page of pencil notes about a lighthouse open under their thumb.\nscene [69eb2f] = 一个学生在烛光下读一本又小又厚的年历，拇指下翻开的一页写满了关于灯塔的铅笔批注。\n# en: Go down to the point one clear night\nopt.left.label [9fcdcc] = 找个晴夜去海角\n# en: You find the lighthouse where the page said, turning its long arm over the water. You stand there until your ears freeze. On the way back you understand Grimsby much better.\nopt.left.say.1 [fa37bc] = 你在那一页说的地方找到了灯塔，它长长的光臂在水面上转动。你站到耳朵冻僵。回去的路上，你更懂格林斯比了。\n# en: Copy his notes into your own book\nopt.right.label [2fd80d] = 把他的批注抄进自己的本子\n# en: You copy all of it, the lamp times and the tides and the margin note. Some of it turns up, oddly, on your next astronomy paper.\nopt.right.say.1 [531c64] = 你全抄了下来，亮灯时间、潮汐和页边的那行字。其中一些，奇怪地出现在你下一份占星作业里。\n\n@event item_spoon_daughters\n# en: The Daughters Want It Back\ntitle [599785] = 女儿们想要回来\n# en: Pell's daughters are waiting at the counter with their arms folded. \"That's our spoon,\" says the elder. \"We learned to measure on it. Dad had no right.\" Behind them, Old Pell counts drops loudly and pretends not to hear.\ntext [d02e60] = 佩尔的女儿们抱着胳膊在柜台后等你。“那是我们的勺子，”大女儿说，“我们是用它学的量药。爸爸没权利给人。”她们身后，老佩尔大声数着药滴，假装没听见。\n# en: Two women behind an apothecary counter, arms folded, looking pointedly at a brass spoon in a student's hand.\nscene [e34bf6] = 药铺柜台后两个女人抱着胳膊，意味深长地看着一个学生手里的黄铜勺。\n# en: Give the spoon back\nopt.left.label [2c0161] = 把勺子还给她们\n# en: They take it, and the younger one cries a little, and Pell stops counting for a moment. On your way out he catches your sleeve. \"Measure by eye, then,\" he says. \"You've the eye for it.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [6a41de] = 她们接过勺子，小女儿哭了一下，佩尔停了一会儿没数。你出门时他拉住你的袖子。“那就凭眼睛量吧，”他说，“你有这个眼力。”\n# en: Keep it; he gave it to you\nopt.right.label [6ff897] = 留着，是他给你的\n# en: \"He gave it to me,\" you say. The daughters look at their father. Pell keeps counting. Later the younger one says, not unkindly, \"Then measure well with it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [01a578] = “是他给我的，”你说。女儿们看向父亲。佩尔继续数。后来小女儿不太凶地说：“那就好好用它量。”\n\n@event item_honey_bees\n# en: Silver Bees Follow You\ntitle [760669] = 银蜂跟着你\n# en: A small cloud of silver bees has followed you up from Maud's cottage and through the castle gates. They hum round your bag, where the honey tin is. A prefect looks at them, then at you, and visibly decides it isn't his problem.\ntext [b5ee84] = 一小群银蜂从莫德的小屋一路跟你进了城堡大门。它们绕着你装蜂蜜罐的包嗡嗡转。一个级长看看它们，又看看你，明显决定这不关他的事。\n# en: A student walking across a castle courtyard trailed by a small glittering cloud of silver bees.\nscene [0c114a] = 一个学生穿过城堡庭院，身后跟着一小团闪闪发光的银蜂。\n# en: Walk them back to Maud\nopt.left.label [f7a0d9] = 把它们送回莫德那儿\n# en: You walk the long way down with the bees in tow. Maud laughs until she has to sit down. \"They're checking on their honey, dearie. Nosy things.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [938fe6] = 你绕远路下山，蜜蜂跟在后面。莫德笑得不得不坐下。“它们是来查看自己的蜂蜜的，亲爱的。爱管闲事的小东西。”\n# en: Let them come along\nopt.right.label [8e39ed] = 让它们跟着\n# en: {term: The bees sit in a row on the windowsill through all of Astromancy. The lecturer pauses, nods to them, and carries on.|The bees follow you round the half-empty castle and sit in a row on the library windowsill while you read. The librarian pauses, nods to them, and carries on.} Everyone talks about it for a week.\nopt.right.say.1 [e8de3b] = {term: 蜜蜂在窗台上排成一排，听完了整节占星课。讲师停下来冲它们点点头，接着讲。|蜜蜂跟着你在半空的城堡里转，你读书时它们就在图书馆的窗台上排成一排。图书管理员停下来冲它们点点头，接着忙。}大家议论了一个星期。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/kit/shops.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/kit/shops.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event shop_square\n# en: Market Day\ntitle [0bcdaf] = 集市日\n# en: {The square is all stalls today, with the bakery's trestle, the tailor's rail of robes, the pawnbroker's sign creaking over his door.|Market day again. The baker's girl is shouting the price of buns as if it were news.|The tailor has put a new robe in the window and is standing behind it, watching people's faces.} {met(mir): {Mir has a stall by the well, under a sign that says WANDS, IMPROVED (PROBABLY).|Mir's stall has grown a canopy and a small, worried sign: PLEASE DO NOT TEST ON THE BAKERY.|Mir is at the stall, rewinding something that should not need rewinding.}|{A pigeon is eating the display.|Somebody is haggling over a cabbage with real feeling.|A barge horn sounds from the jetty.}}\ntext [190a3d] = {今天广场上全是摊子：烘焙坊的支架桌，裁缝的长袍衣架，当铺门上吱呀作响的招牌。|又是集市日。烘焙坊的姑娘大声报着面包的价钱，好像那是什么新闻。|裁缝在橱窗里挂了一件新袍子，自己站在后面，看路人的表情。} {met(mir): {米尔在井边摆了个摊，招牌上写着：魔杖，改良版（大概）。|米尔的摊子多了个顶棚，还有一块忧心忡忡的小牌子：请勿对着烘焙坊试杖。|米尔在摊子上，正给一样本不该需要上发条的东西上发条。}|{一只鸽子在啄摊上的货。|有人正满怀激情地为一棵卷心菜讨价还价。|码头传来驳船的汽笛声。}}\n# en: A cobbled market square, a bakery stall steaming, a tailor's window full of robes, a pawnbroker's sign and a student's stall of odd wands.\nscene [6a4545] = 铺着鹅卵石的集市广场，烘焙坊的摊子冒着热气，裁缝的橱窗挂满长袍，当铺挂着招牌，还有一个学生摆的古怪魔杖摊。\n# en: A bag of warm buns\nopt.buns.label [5281f2] = 一袋热面包\n# en: A little Health.\nopt.buns.hint [f30bb0] = 少许健康。\n# en: {The buns are still hot enough to hurt. You eat two on the walk back and save one, nearly.|The baker's girl throws in a crooked one for free. It's the best of the lot.|Sugar on your fingers, sugar on your robes. Worth it.}\nopt.buns.say.1 [fa67ed] = {面包还烫得能伤人。回去路上你吃了两个，差点留下了一个。|烘焙坊的姑娘白送你一个歪的。那是最好吃的一个。|手指上是糖，袍子上也是糖。值了。}\n# en: The quilted duelling coat\nopt.coat.label [a636dd] = 绗缝决斗外套\n# en: A robe for fights: more Health, and Brace costs less.\nopt.coat.hint [82d4d4] = 战斗用长袍：更多生命，坚守更省。\n# en: {The tailor buttons you into it and thumps you on the chest. \"There. Now you're a wall.\"|It's heavier than it looks and smells faintly of the duelling hall. You feel braver at once, which may be the padding.|\"Singed in the duelling hall? Bring it back and I'll patch it,\" says the tailor, which is not reassuring.}\nopt.coat.say.1 [da3dcb] = {裁缝替你扣好扣子，在你胸口拍了一下。“好了。现在你是一堵墙。”|它比看上去重，隐约有决斗厅的味道。你立刻觉得勇敢了些，也许只是因为垫得厚。|“在决斗厅烧坏了？拿回来我给你补，”裁缝说。这话并不让人安心。}\n# en: The duelling coat, at the Circle's price\nopt.coat_circle.label [0134d6] = 决斗外套，决斗社价\n# en: A robe for fights: more Health, and Brace costs less.\nopt.coat_circle.hint [82d4d4] = 战斗用长袍：更多生命，坚守更省。\n# en: {\"Circle members pay what the Circle pays,\" says the tailor, and charges you half. \"Don't tell the others.\"|The tailor knows the Circle's crest and knocks the price down without being asked.|\"Half price for the Circle. Your captain sent three of you last week. Two came back singed.\"}\nopt.coat_circle.say.1 [30228c] = {“决斗社的人，照决斗社的价，”裁缝说，只收了你一半。“别告诉别人。”|裁缝认得决斗社的徽章，没等你开口就把价钱降了下来。|“决斗社半价。你们社长上周送来三个，回去两个被烧焦了。”}\n# en: Midnight-blue robes\nopt.robes.label [528263] = 午夜蓝长袍\n# en: A good robe: a little more Health in a fight, and people stand up straighter.\nopt.robes.hint [e2baf5] = 好长袍：战斗中多一点生命，别人见了也会挺直腰板。\n# en: {Deep blue wool, silver at the hem, a lining like the night sky. In the tailor's mirror you look like someone in a portrait.|The tailor pins the hem with her mouth full of pins and still manages to say \"Lovely\".|It costs more than you meant to spend. It also fits as if it had been waiting for you.}\nopt.robes.say.1 [02b645] = {深蓝羊毛，镶着银边，里衬像夜空。在裁缝的镜子里，你像肖像画里的人。|裁缝嘴里含满大头针给你别下摆，居然还能说出一句“真好看”。|它比你打算花的钱多。可它合身得好像一直在等你。}\n# en: Midnight-blue robes, for your old ones\nopt.robes_swap.label [e96b29] = 午夜蓝长袍，以旧换新\n# en: Bring the robes you bought before; the tailor takes them off your hands at half price.\nopt.robes_swap.hint [d691f6] = 带上你以前买的长袍，裁缝半价收走。\n# en: {\"Stained at the Ball? Bring them here.\" The tailor takes your old robes, sniffs them, and fits you properly at half price.|She holds your old robes up to the light, tuts at a stain, and takes them off your hands. The new ones are the same blue, properly cut.|\"Half price for the old ones back. I'll turn them into cushions.\" You don't ask whose cushions.}\nopt.robes_swap.say.1 [9c9403] = {“在舞会上弄脏了？拿到这儿来。”裁缝收下你的旧袍子，闻了闻，半价给你重新量体裁衣。|她把旧袍子举到亮处，对着一块污渍啧啧两声，收下了。新的同样是深蓝，剪裁得体。|“旧的交回来，半价。我拿它们做靠垫。”你没问是谁的靠垫。}\n# en: A hazelwood wand from Mir's stall\nopt.hazel.label [19f1f9] = 米尔摊上的榛木魔杖\n# en: A light, quick wand: a little more Mana in a fight.\nopt.hazel.hint [4bbe94] = 轻巧灵敏的魔杖：战斗中多一点法力。\n# en: {Mir wraps it in a page of their own notes. \"This one likes to be used. Probably.\"|\"I re-cored it twice,\" Mir says. \"The second time on purpose.\"|Mir waves it once over the stall and a cabbage rolls politely out of the way. \"See? Improved.\"}\nopt.hazel.say.1 [fc6781] = {米尔用一页自己的笔记把它包好。“这根喜欢被用。大概吧。”|“我给它换了两次杖芯，”米尔说，“第二次是故意的。”|米尔在摊子上方挥了一下，一棵卷心菜很有礼貌地滚开了。“看到没？改良版。”}\n# en: Trade the borrowed wand to Mir for hazel\nopt.hazel_swap.label [8c9fa8] = 把借来的魔杖换成榛木的\n# en: Mir takes the academy's loaner in part-exchange: a little more Mana in a fight.\nopt.hazel_swap.hint [64cd5e] = 米尔收下学院的借用杖抵一部分钱：战斗中多一点法力。\n# en: {\"That borrowed wand's held together with hope,\" says Mir, taking it gently. \"I'll give it a better one. Hope, I mean.\"|Mir weighs the old wand, winces at the chipped tip and hands you a hazel one, warm from the sun.|\"I'll fix this one up for next year's first-years,\" Mir says. \"Someone will need it. Probably you, in spirit.\"}\nopt.hazel_swap.say.1 [1540fa] = {“这根借来的魔杖全靠希望撑着，”米尔小心地接过去，“我给它换个更好的。我是说，换个更好的希望。”|米尔掂了掂旧魔杖，看到崩掉的杖尖皱了皱眉，递给你一根晒得暖烘烘的榛木杖。|“我把这根修好，留给明年的一年级生，”米尔说，“总会有人需要它的。精神上大概就是你。”}\n# en: Sell something at the pawnbroker's\nopt.pawn.label [8103da] = 去当铺卖点东西\n# en: He buys what you no longer want, at less than you paid.\nopt.pawn.hint [f6ae90] = 他收你不再想要的东西，价钱比你买时低。\n# en: {The pawnbroker's bell rings like a sigh.|The stuffed owl in his window watches you go in.|\"Ah,\" says the pawnbroker, who says \"ah\" to everyone.}\nopt.pawn.say.1 [882f26] = {当铺的门铃响得像一声叹息。|他橱窗里那只猫头鹰标本目送你进门。|“啊，”当铺老板说。他对谁都说“啊”。}\n# en: Just looking\nopt.look.label [4af612] = 随便看看\n# en: {You walk the stalls with your hands in your pockets. The smell of the bakery is free.|You admire the tailor's window from a safe, inexpensive distance.|You buy nothing and enjoy the noise. Market days are good for that.}\nopt.look.say.1 [55200c] = {你双手插兜在摊子间闲逛。烘焙坊的香味不要钱。|你在安全、不花钱的距离外欣赏裁缝的橱窗。|你什么也没买，享受着热闹。集市日就适合这样。}\n\n@event shop_pawn\n# en: The Pawnbroker's Counter\ntitle [d08e15] = 当铺柜台\n# en: {The pawnbroker turns each thing over twice, as if it might be hiding a second, cheaper thing underneath.|\"Ah,\" says the pawnbroker, screwing in his loupe. \"Let's see what you're tired of.\"|Behind him, the stuffed owl. In front of him, a ledger. Neither looks sentimental.} He pays fair, he says, for what is no longer loved.\ntext [2de4e7] = {当铺老板把每样东西都翻过来看两遍，好像底下还藏着一样更便宜的东西。|“啊，”当铺老板说着，把放大镜拧进眼眶，“看看你厌倦了什么。”|他身后是猫头鹰标本，身前是账本。两样看起来都不念旧。}他说，不再被珍爱的东西，他都给公道价。\n# en: A narrow shop crammed with other people's treasures, a stuffed owl on a shelf and a pawnbroker with a loupe in one eye.\nscene [7a09a8] = 一间塞满别人宝贝的窄小铺子，架子上一只猫头鹰标本，老板一只眼睛夹着放大镜。\n# en: The hazelwood wand\nopt.hazel.label [2c3297] = 榛木魔杖\n# en: He tests it on a candle and nods. \"Light. Some student will like it.\"\nopt.hazel.say.1 [781493] = 他拿它在蜡烛上试了试，点点头。“轻。会有学生喜欢的。”\n# en: The ashwood duelling wand\nopt.ashwood.label [eb833d] = 白蜡木决斗魔杖\n# en: He holds it at arm's length, as if it might bite. \"Duellists come in here sometimes. Usually limping.\"\nopt.ashwood.say.1 [766c04] = 他把它拿得远远的，好像它会咬人。“决斗的人有时会来这儿。通常是瘸着来的。”\n# en: The heartwood wand\nopt.heartwood.label [5ee7b9] = 心材魔杖\n# en: He goes quiet over the grain. \"That's not from any wood I sell.\" He pays well. You feel it go, the whole way home.\nopt.heartwood.say.1 [6b250c] = 他对着木纹沉默了。“这不是我卖过的任何一种木头。”他出价很高。回去的一路上，你都能感觉到它不在了。\n# en: The quilted duelling coat\nopt.coat.label [a636dd] = 绗缝决斗外套\n# en: He pokes a finger through a singed patch and docks the price for it.\nopt.coat.say.1 [f311c3] = 他用手指戳了戳一块烧焦的地方，为此压了价。\n# en: The ward-stitched cloak\nopt.cloak.label [5947b9] = 护界绣斗篷\n# en: He holds the hem to the lamp and the ward thread flickers. \"Sister Amaranth's work. I'll keep it out of the window.\"\nopt.cloak.say.1 [10b531] = 他把下摆凑到灯下，护界线闪了一下。“阿玛兰斯修女的手艺。这件我不放橱窗。”\n# en: The midnight-blue robes\nopt.robes.label [7b2814] = 午夜蓝长袍\n# en: \"Lovely cut,\" he says, and pays you a third of it.\nopt.robes.say.1 [8867b8] = “剪裁真好，”他说，然后只付了三分之一的价。\n# en: The Spinner-silk robe\nopt.silk.label [b79365] = 织网者丝袍\n# en: He touches the silk and takes his hand back very quickly. Then he pays, a great deal, without haggling.\nopt.silk.say.1 [ec28ba] = 他碰了碰丝绸，飞快地把手缩了回去。然后一分没还价，付了一大笔钱。\n# en: The lodestone pendant\nopt.lodestone.label [c54545] = 磁石吊坠\n# en: He sets it on the counter and it turns, slowly, to the north wall. \"Huh,\" he says, and pays.\nopt.lodestone.say.1 [83070b] = 他把它放在柜台上，它慢慢转向了北面的墙。“嗯，”他说，付了钱。\n# en: The stag's tine\nopt.tine.label [3d2dbd] = 鹿角尖\n# en: He doesn't ask where you got it. The stuffed owl seems to.\nopt.tine.say.1 [e479c6] = 他没问你从哪儿弄来的。猫头鹰标本好像想问。\n# en: The owl-feather quill\nopt.quill.label [2cd309] = 猫头鹰羽毛笔\n# en: \"A quill's a quill,\" he says. It writes \"a quill's a quill\" on his ledger by itself, and he pays a little more.\nopt.quill.say.1 [e7172a] = “笔就是笔，”他说。笔自己在他账本上写下“笔就是笔”，他多付了一点。\n# en: The moonpetal locket\nopt.locket.label [4a8f97] = 月瓣吊盒\n# en: He opens it, sees the petal, closes it again. \"Pretty,\" he says. It's the kindest word he's used all day.\nopt.locket.say.1 [ec6625] = 他打开，看见那片花瓣，又合上。“好看，”他说。这是他今天说过最温柔的一个词。\n# en: The pocket almanac\nopt.almanac.label [3082b1] = 袖珍年历\n# en: He flicks through Grimsby's pencil notes and snorts. \"He'll buy it back off me by Tuesday.\"\nopt.almanac.say.1 [307c53] = 他翻了翻格林斯比的铅笔批注，哼了一声。“不出星期二，他就会从我这儿买回去。”\n# en: Pell's measuring spoon\nopt.spoon.label [ad193a] = 佩尔的量勺\n# en: \"Brass,\" he says, \"worn thin.\" He pays for the brass.\nopt.spoon.say.1 [68a601] = “黄铜，”他说，“磨薄了。”他按黄铜的价付钱。\n# en: The honey tin\nopt.honey.label [3d60fc] = 蜂蜜罐\n# en: He shakes it, sniffs it and pays a few coins. A silver bee follows you all the way to the jetty, looking disappointed.\nopt.honey.say.1 [cb7a66] = 他摇了摇，闻了闻，给了几个硬币。一只银蜂一路跟你到码头，看上去很失望。\n# en: Keep it all\nopt.keep.label [dce93a] = 都留着吧\n# en: {You put everything back in your bag. The pawnbroker shrugs. \"Ah.\"|Not today. The owl watches you go.|You find you're fond of it all after all.}\nopt.keep.say.1 [d1a8fd] = {你把东西全放回包里。当铺老板耸耸肩。“啊。”|今天不卖了。猫头鹰目送你离开。|你发现自己还是舍不得这些东西。}\n\n@event shop_mabels\n# en: Mabel's Counter\ntitle [160cd0] = 梅布尔的柜台\n# en: {Mabel looks at you once as you come in and is already reaching for a tin. \"Sit, petal. I know what you want; let's see if you do.\"|\"There's my petal,\" says Mabel. \"You've the look of someone who needs something steeped.\"|The bell over the door. Mabel doesn't turn round. \"Ginger, by the sound of that door,\" she says.} {The courage blend is in the green tin; the calm blend in the blue.|A sign on the counter reads: TONICS — ONE AT A TIME. MABEL.|The cups on the hooks behind her catch the light.}\ntext [3e1a8b] = {你一进门，梅布尔看了你一眼，已经伸手去拿茶罐了。“坐吧，小花瓣。我知道你要什么，看看你自己知不知道。”|“我的小花瓣来了，”梅布尔说，“你一脸需要泡点什么的样子。”|门铃响了。梅布尔没回头。“听这开门声，是要姜茶，”她说。} {勇气茶在绿罐子里，安神茶在蓝罐子里。|柜台上的牌子写着：补剂——一次一种。梅布尔。|她身后挂钩上的茶杯闪着光。}\n# en: A small warm tea shop with forty tins on the shelves, three cakes under glass and a woman behind the counter already reaching for a pot.\nscene [a2659a] = 一间温暖的小茶馆，架上四十个茶罐，玻璃罩下三种蛋糕，柜台后的女人已经伸手去拿茶壶了。\n# en: A pot of tea\nopt.tea.label [3591b6] = 一壶茶\n# en: A little Mood.\nopt.tea.hint [26b71f] = 少许心情。\n# en: {She brings it before you've sat down. It's exactly right, which is worrying.|A pot, a cup and a slice of cake you didn't order and don't pay for. \"Crumbs,\" she says. \"Day-old.\"|You drink it by the window and watch the barge come in. The day gets a little easier.}\nopt.tea.say.1 [4ee98a] = {你还没坐下她就端来了。正合你意，这让人有点担心。|一壶茶，一个杯子，还有一块你没点、也不用付钱的蛋糕。“边角料，”她说，“隔夜的。”|你坐在窗边喝茶，看驳船靠岸。这一天好过了一点。}\n# en: The courage blend\nopt.courage.label [196e93] = 勇气茶\n# en: A tonic for a month: asking for things and performing come easier.\nopt.courage.hint [92b427] = 一个月的补剂：开口求人和登台表演更容易。\n# en: {\"Ginger, honey and something I don't tell,\" says Mabel, screwing the lid on. \"One cup a morning. Speak up, petal.\"|She weighs it out on her little scales. \"For the nerve,\" she says. \"Not for being rude. Different tin.\"|You drink a cup there and then. Your voice comes out a size bigger when you thank her.}\nopt.courage.say.1 [af18e1] = {“姜、蜂蜜，还有一样我不说的，”梅布尔拧上盖子，“每天早上一杯。大声说话，小花瓣。”|她在小秤上称好。“是给胆子用的，”她说，“不是给你没礼貌用的。那是另一罐。”|你当场喝了一杯。道谢时，你的声音大了一号。}\n# en: The calm blend\nopt.calm.label [420ab8] = 安神茶\n# en: A tonic for a month: exams and frightened animals come easier.\nopt.calm.hint [b400aa] = 一个月的补剂：考试和安抚受惊的动物更容易。\n# en: {\"Chamomile, lavender, a pinch of moonwort,\" says Mabel. \"For exams. And for your hands, petal. They're shaking.\"|She hands you the blue tin. \"Breathe before you open the paper. The tea only does half.\"|\"Good for nerves and nervous beasts,\" says Mabel. \"Don't ask me why. It just is.\"}\nopt.calm.say.1 [7990cf] = {“洋甘菊、薰衣草，一小撮月草，”梅布尔说，“考试用的。还有你的手，小花瓣。它们在抖。”|她递给你蓝罐子。“翻开试卷前先深呼吸。茶只管一半。”|“对紧张的人和紧张的牲口都管用，”梅布尔说，“别问我为什么。就是管用。”}\n# en: Just say hello\nopt.chat.label [781d00] = 只是打个招呼\n# en: {Mabel tells you three things about the village and one about yourself. The last one is right.|You lean on the counter and let her talk. It costs nothing and helps more than tea.|\"Off you go, then,\" she says, \"and come back with better news.\"}\nopt.chat.say.1 [c092cb] = {梅布尔告诉你三件村里的事，一件关于你的事。最后一件说对了。|你靠在柜台上听她说话。不花钱，比茶还管用。|“去吧，”她说，“下次带好消息来。”}\n\n@event shop_grimsbys\n# en: Grimsby's Counter\ntitle [f5d1f8] = 格林斯比的柜台\n# en: {Mr Grimsby is up a ladder, sniffing a spine. \"Ah! Not that one,\" he says, and hands you a different one.|Grimsby greets you like an old customer, by the title of a book you've never read. He has no idea what you came in for today.|The shop smells of dust and pipe smoke and one shelf, somewhere, of the sea.} {A stack of exam primers leans on the counter like a tired student.|A box by the door says BOOKS BOUGHT BACK, FAIR PRICES, NO QUESTIONS (SOME QUESTIONS).|A lighthouse print hangs crooked behind the till.}\ntext [149f00] = {格林斯比先生爬在梯子上，闻着一本书的书脊。“啊！不是这本，”他说，递给你另一本。|格林斯比像招呼老主顾一样跟你打招呼，报的却是一本你从没读过的书名。他完全不知道你今天来买什么。|店里有灰尘和烟斗的味道，某个书架上，还有大海的味道。} {一摞备考手册靠在柜台上，像个累坏了的学生。|门边的箱子上写着：旧书回收，价钱公道，不问问题（问几个）。|收银台后面歪挂着一幅灯塔版画。}\n# en: A bookshop crowded to the rafters, shelves leaning on each other, an old man in half-moon glasses sniffing the spine of a book.\nscene [ebe92b] = 一家书塞到房梁的书店，书架互相倚靠，戴半月形眼镜的老人正闻着一本书的书脊。\n# en: Let him choose you a book\nopt.pick.label [99cfbe] = 让他替你挑本书\n# en: Skill XP, in whatever he thinks you need.\nopt.pick.hint [fd7e6c] = 技能经验，他觉得你缺什么就给什么。\n# en: He sniffs three books and hands you the fourth. It's on a subject you'd never have picked, and you can't put it down.\nopt.pick.say.1 [3f2faa] = 他闻了三本书，递给你第四本。那个题目你从来不会选，可你放不下了。\n# en: \"This one,\" he says, holding out a battered spellbook. \"It smells of thunder.\" It does, and it's very good.\nopt.pick.say.2 [1e30df] = “这本，”他说着递来一本破旧的咒语书，“它有雷雨的味道。”确实有，而且写得很好。\n# en: He gives you a herbal with someone's pressed flowers still inside. \"The flowers were extra. I mean free. I mean extra.\"\nopt.pick.say.3 [c9b692] = 他给你一本草药志，里面还夹着别人压的花。“花是额外的。我是说免费的。我是说额外的。”\n# en: An exam primer\nopt.primer.label [682108] = 一本备考手册\n# en: A little exam preparation for every course you're taking.\nopt.primer.hint [981bc2] = 你修的每门课都多一点考试准备。\n# en: {The primer is dog-eared in all the right places. Someone passed with this book before you.|Grimsby hands it over reluctantly. \"Exams,\" he says, as if naming a sad illness.|You read the first chapter on the barge home and, for once, the questions look like ones you could answer.}\nopt.primer.say.1 [cd438e] = {手册在恰当的地方都折了角。在你之前，有人靠这本书考过了。|格林斯比不太情愿地递过来。“考试，”他说，好像在说一种令人难过的病。|你在回去的驳船上读了第一章，难得地，那些题目看起来你能答。}\n# en: Sell a book back\nopt.sell.label [8e49b6] = 卖回一本书\n# en: A few crowns, and a book fewer.\nopt.sell.hint [629956] = 几个克朗，少一本书。\n# en: {He pays for it and puts it on a shelf you'll never find again. You miss it at once.|\"Fair price,\" he says, and it is. You miss the book anyway.|He sniffs it, approves, and pays. Your shelf at the castle looks a little emptier tonight.}\nopt.sell.say.1 [8a09ca] = {他付了钱，把书放到一个你再也找不到的架子上。你立刻就想它了。|“公道价，”他说，确实公道。你还是会想那本书。|他闻了闻，满意地付了钱。今晚城堡里你的书架看起来空了一点。}\n# en: Browse the shelves\nopt.browse.label [9177cc] = 逛逛书架\n# en: {You spend an hour reading the first pages of twenty books. It's the best hour of the week.|You find a book you want and can't afford, and read a chapter of it standing up.|Grimsby recommends a book by its smell. You leave with nothing and an appetite.}\nopt.browse.say.1 [2168b2] = {你花一个小时读了二十本书的开头几页。这是一周里最好的一个小时。|你找到一本想要却买不起的书，站着读完了一章。|格林斯比凭气味给你推荐书。你空手而归，胃口大开。}\n\n@event shop_pell\n# en: Pell's Counter\ntitle [3574ec] = 佩尔的柜台\n# en: {One of Pell's daughters is at the counter. Behind her, Old Pell counts drops aloud and doesn't look up.|\"Tonic or draught?\" asks the daughter on the counter. \"Dad will say draught. Dad always says draught.\"|The drawers rattle as you come in, as if they were sorting themselves.} {A card on the till reads MEASURED TWICE, SOLD ONCE.|Old Pell is arguing with a label.|The smell of mint and something sterner.}\ntext [8e1a84] = {佩尔的一个女儿在柜台。她身后，老佩尔大声数着药滴，头也不抬。|“补剂还是药剂？”柜台后的女儿问，“爸爸会说药剂。爸爸总说药剂。”|你一进门，抽屉们就咔嗒作响，好像在自己整理。} {收银台上的卡片写着：量两遍，卖一次。|老佩尔在跟一张标签争论。|薄荷味，还有一种更严肃的味道。}\n# en: An apothecary of a thousand labelled drawers, a daughter behind the counter, an old man counting drops at the back.\nscene [22b99b] = 一家有上千个贴着标签的抽屉的药铺，柜台后站着一个女儿，后面一个老人在数药滴。\n# en: Pell's tonic\nopt.tonic.label [6e083d] = 佩尔的补剂\n# en: A little Health.\nopt.tonic.hint [f30bb0] = 少许健康。\n# en: {It tastes of mint and iron. Halfway back to the castle you realise you've stopped yawning.|The daughter pours it; Pell checks the measure from across the room. \"Right,\" he says. It is.|\"Two spoons at bedtime,\" says the daughter. \"Not three. Dad will know.\"}\nopt.tonic.say.1 [8918b7] = {有薄荷和铁的味道。回城堡走到一半，你发现自己不打哈欠了。|女儿倒药，佩尔隔着屋子检查分量。“对了，”他说。确实对了。|“睡前两勺，”女儿说，“别三勺。爸爸会知道的。”}\n# en: The steadying draught\nopt.draught.label [a644e3] = 定神药剂\n# en: A tonic for a month: research, brewing and enchanting come easier.\nopt.draught.hint [266879] = 一个月的补剂：研究、调制和附魔更容易。\n# en: {Old Pell measures it himself, counting aloud. \"Steady hands,\" he says. \"That's all it does. That's all anyone needs.\"|The draught tastes of nothing at all, which the daughter says is the expensive part.|\"For the fine work,\" says Pell, corking it. \"Don't waste it on the coarse work.\"}\nopt.draught.say.1 [e6d447] = {老佩尔亲自量，一边大声数着。“手要稳，”他说，“它就管这个。人人需要的也就这个。”|这药剂什么味道都没有，女儿说这就是贵的地方。|“做精细活用的，”佩尔塞上瓶塞，“别浪费在粗活上。”}\n# en: Just looking\nopt.look.label [4af612] = 随便看看\n# en: {You read labels until the daughter asks if you're lost. You are, a little.|You leave with nothing but the smell of mint in your robes.|Pell glances up once, decides you're not buying, and goes back to his drops.}\nopt.look.say.1 [dac02a] = {你读标签读到女儿问你是不是迷路了。你确实有点迷路。|你什么也没买，只带走了袍子上的薄荷味。|佩尔抬头看了一眼，断定你不买，又低头去数他的药滴。}\n\n@event shop_newt\n# en: The Newt's Counter\ntitle [aa46a0] = 蝾螈酒馆的吧台\n# en: {{slot == \"afternoon\": By day the Gilded Newt is quiet, just a fire, a few regulars and the landlady with a cloth over her shoulder.|At this hour the Gilded Newt is all elbows and cider, and the landlady works the bar with a cloth over her shoulder.}|{slot == \"afternoon\": The Newt in the afternoon smells of bread and last night's cider. Someone is asleep by the fire.|The Newt smells of cider and wet coats tonight. Someone is singing by the fire, badly.}|The landlady nods at you over the bar. \"Supper's on. Cider's cold. Table's free.\"} {The slate says HOT SUPPER, A ROUND, CIDER.|A cat is asleep in the bread basket.|A barge crew is arguing about weather.}\ntext [438b30] = {{slot == \"afternoon\": 白天的镀金蝾螈很安静，只有炉火、几个常客，和肩上搭着抹布的老板娘。|这个钟点的镀金蝾螈挤满了胳膊肘和苹果酒，老板娘肩上搭着抹布在吧台后忙活。}|{slot == \"afternoon\": 午后的蝾螈有面包和昨晚苹果酒的味道。有人在炉火边睡着了。|今晚的蝾螈有苹果酒和湿外套的味道。有人在炉火边唱歌，唱得很糟。}|老板娘隔着吧台冲你点点头。“晚饭好了。苹果酒是凉的。桌子空着。”} {石板上写着：热晚饭、请一轮、苹果酒。|一只猫睡在面包篮里。|一群驳船船员在争论天气。}\n# en: A low-beamed tavern with small windows, a fire that never goes out and a landlady polishing glasses behind the bar.\nscene [062fe7] = 低矮梁柱的酒馆，小小的窗户，炉火永不熄灭，老板娘在吧台后擦杯子。\n# en: A hot supper\nopt.supper.label [5ef676] = 一顿热晚饭\n# en: Health and Mood; it ends a hungry month.\nopt.supper.hint [dd3967] = 健康和心情；能结束挨饿的一个月。\n# en: {Stew, bread, an apple you didn't ask for. You leave warm all the way through.|The landlady watches you eat as if she'd been worried about you. Perhaps she had.|You finish the bowl and she fills it again without asking. \"Growing,\" she says, though you're not.}\nopt.supper.say.1 [fe473d] = {炖菜、面包，还有一个你没要的苹果。你从里暖到外地离开。|老板娘看着你吃，好像一直在担心你。也许真是。|你吃完一碗，她没问就又盛满了。“长身体呢，”她说，虽然你早就不长了。}\n# en: Buy a round for the table\nopt.round.label [d861a8] = 请全桌喝一轮\n# en: Renown; the regulars will remember.\nopt.round.hint [bcc9a8] = 声望；常客们会记得。\n# en: {The regulars lift their cups to you. One of them, very drunk, makes a speech about students.|\"To the castle!\" someone shouts, and the whole room drinks to it, and to you.|The barge crew drink your health and then argue about whether you're a good sort. They decide you are.}\nopt.round.say.1 [9eeb04] = {常客们向你举杯。其中一个醉得厉害，发表了一通关于学生的演讲。|“为城堡干杯！”有人喊道，满屋子人都为城堡、也为你喝了一杯。|驳船船员们祝你健康，然后争论你算不算个好人。结论是算。}\n# en: A cup of cider\nopt.cider.label [26847e] = 一杯苹果酒\n# en: A little Mood.\nopt.cider.hint [26b71f] = 少许心情。\n# en: {Cold, sharp and tasting of last autumn's apples.|You drink it by the window and watch the lake turn gold.|The cat in the bread basket opens one eye at you and closes it again.}\nopt.cider.say.1 [fc5417] = {冰凉、清冽，有去年秋天苹果的味道。|你在窗边喝着，看湖面变成金色。|面包篮里的猫睁开一只眼睛看看你，又闭上了。}\n# en: Just warm your hands at the fire\nopt.look.label [65a973] = 只在炉火边暖暖手\n# en: {Nobody minds. The fire never goes out; that's the point of it.|You sit by the fire until your hands remember they're hands.|The landlady leaves you be. Sometimes that's the service.}\nopt.look.say.1 [d0f115] = {没人介意。炉火永不熄灭，就是为了这个。|你在炉火边坐到双手想起自己是手。|老板娘没打扰你。有时候这就是服务。}\n\n@event offer_ashwood_wand\n# en: Prove It\ntitle [ef8e7a] = 证明给我看\n# en: Under a cloth at the back of Mir's stall is an ashwood duelling wand, dense and dark. \"It bites anything that blocks it,\" says Mir. \"I'm not selling it to just anyone. Show me you can hold it, and it's yours, for what the ash cost me.\"\ntext [f6f4c9] = 米尔摊子后面的布底下，有一根白蜡木决斗魔杖，沉实而黝黑。“谁挡它，它就咬谁，”米尔说，“我不随便卖。证明你拿得住它，它就归你，价钱就是白蜡木的成本。”\n# en: A market stall with a single dark wand on a velvet cloth, an artificer student holding a hand over it protectively.\nscene [147e14] = 集市摊位上，天鹅绒布上只放着一根深色魔杖，一个做奇械的学生护着它。\n# en: Leave it under its cloth\nopt.left.label [ef3629] = 让它留在布底下\n# en: Mir covers it again, relieved. \"It'd probably bite me too.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [d7954d] = 米尔又把它盖好，松了口气。“它大概也会咬我。”\n# en: Pay up, and prove it\nopt.right.label [8f8d0e] = 付钱，证明给他看\n# en: A dear price, handed back if it won't answer you. A wand for fights: anything that blocks it takes a jolt.\nopt.right.hint [017731] = 价钱不菲，它不认你就退钱。战斗用魔杖：谁挡它，谁就挨一下。\n# en: The wand jumps in your hand like a live thing and a spark cracks off the well. Mir whoops. \"It likes you! Probably.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [05d962] = 魔杖在你手里像活物一样一跳，一道火花从井沿上炸开。米尔欢呼起来。“它喜欢你！大概吧。”\n# en: The wand sulks in your hand and won't so much as spark. Mir gives you your money back, kindly, which is worse.\nopt.right.say.2 [62df5c] = 魔杖在你手里闹脾气，连个火星都不冒。米尔客气地把钱退给你，这更难受。\n\n@event offer_ward_cloak\n# en: Ward Thread\ntitle [29797d] = 护界线\n# en: The tailor beckons you into her back room. On the table is a spool of thread that shines faintly when you aren't looking at it. \"Sister Amaranth sent it down. Ward thread. She says you're to have a cloak with it, if you can pay for the hemming. Fire and frost slide off it like rain.\"\ntext [b3e0cd] = 裁缝招手让你进里屋。桌上有一轴线，你不看它的时候，它会微微发光。“阿玛兰斯修女送下来的。护界线。她说要给你做一件斗篷，只要你付得起缝边的钱。火和霜会像雨水一样从上面滑走。”\n# en: A tailor's back room, a spool of faintly shining thread on the table, a grey cloak half-hemmed beside it.\nscene [9b8cd6] = 裁缝的里屋，桌上一轴微微发光的线，旁边一件缝了一半边的灰斗篷。\n# en: You can't afford a cloak\nopt.left.label [3833e6] = 你买不起斗篷\n# en: The tailor puts the spool away in a drawer marked YOURS. \"It'll keep,\" she says.\nopt.left.say.1 [4c4177] = 裁缝把那轴线收进一个写着“你的”的抽屉。“放得住，”她说。\n# en: Have the cloak made\nopt.right.label [fb8f9d] = 请她做斗篷\n# en: A robe for fights: more Health, and spells of fire, frost and starlight hit softer.\nopt.right.hint [491fd9] = 战斗用长袍：更多生命，火焰、冰霜和星光的法术打得更轻。\n# en: She hems it in an afternoon, muttering. When you put it on, the thread warms, and the draught from the door stops at your collar.\nopt.right.say.1 [a90063] = 她一个下午就缝好了，嘴里嘟嘟囔囔。你穿上时，线温热起来，门口的冷风停在了你的衣领处。\n\n@event offer_pocket_almanac\n# en: The Lighthouse Page\ntitle [a33e7f] = 灯塔那一页\n# en: On Grimsby's counter is a pocket almanac: fifty years of tides and moons, every margin full of his own pencil notes. You reach for it and he moves it, gently, out of reach. \"Not for sale,\" he says. \"Well. Not for sale to someone who doesn't know what it's for.\"\ntext [0a69e2] = 格林斯比的柜台上有一本袖珍年历：五十年的潮汐和月相，每一页的页边都写满了他的铅笔批注。你伸手去拿，他轻轻地把它挪开了。“非卖品，”他说，“嗯。不卖给不知道它有什么用的人。”\n# en: An old bookseller holding a tiny thick book out of reach, its margins crowded with pencil notes.\nscene [e6b8ee] = 一位老书商把一本又小又厚的书举在够不着的地方，页边挤满了铅笔批注。\n# en: Leave him his almanac\nopt.left.label [c7df99] = 把年历留给他\n# en: He tucks it back in his waistcoat pocket and pats it. \"Thank you,\" he says, oddly formal.\nopt.left.say.1 [e70ebd] = 他把它塞回背心口袋，拍了拍。“谢谢你，”他说，客气得有点奇怪。\n# en: {knows(grimsby.lighthouses): Talk to him about lighthouses|Argue that you'd use it}\nopt.right.label [8f7876] = {knows(grimsby.lighthouses): 和他聊灯塔|说服他你会用它}\n# en: {knows(grimsby.lighthouses): You know what he loves.|A negotiation.} A pocket almanac: tides, moons and weather on the right cards.\nopt.right.hint [995f75] = {knows(grimsby.lighthouses): 你知道他爱什么。|一场交涉。}袖珍年历：在合适的卡牌上查潮汐、月相和天气。\n# en: You ask him about the lighthouse at the point and he talks for an hour. At the end he presses the almanac into your hands for a few coins. \"Page forty,\" he says. \"That's the good one.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [43013d] = 你问起海角上的灯塔，他一说就是一个小时。最后他只收了几个硬币，把年历塞到你手里。“第四十页，”他说，“那页最好。”\n# en: You tell him what you'd look up first: the next spring tide, the next new moon. He considers this for a long time. \"Yes,\" he says at last, and sells it to you.\nopt.right.say.2 [2ba27d] = 你告诉他你会先查什么：下一次大潮，下一次新月。他想了很久。“好吧，”他终于说，把它卖给了你。\n# en: \"No, no,\" he says kindly, \"you'd look up the weather and nothing else.\" He's right, and that's the worst of it.\nopt.right.say.3 [bbdf4e] = “不，不，”他和气地说，“你只会查天气。”他说得对，这才最糟。\n\n@event offer_measuring_spoon\n# en: Measure Twice\ntitle [8b97fb] = 量两遍\n# en: Old Pell is holding up a brass spoon worn thin on one side. \"Forty years,\" he says. \"It's never been wrong. I'm getting a new one.\" He sets six vials in front of you. \"Measure these. If you count right, it's yours, for what the brass is worth.\"\ntext [6c08a1] = 老佩尔举着一把一侧磨薄了的黄铜勺。“四十年，”他说，“从没错过。我要换把新的了。”他在你面前摆了六个小瓶。“量这些。数对了，它就归你，按黄铜的价钱。”\n# en: An old apothecary holding a thin brass spoon up to the light, a row of empty vials in front of a student.\nscene [f9f936] = 一位老药剂师把一把细细的黄铜勺举到亮处，学生面前摆着一排空瓶。\n# en: Not today\nopt.left.label [479f6f] = 今天不了\n# en: He shrugs and puts the spoon in his waistcoat pocket. \"It'll wait. Spoons are patient.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [d9900d] = 他耸耸肩，把勺子放进背心口袋。“它等得起。勺子有耐心。”\n# en: Count the drops aloud\nopt.right.label [80f706] = 大声数药滴\n# en: Money down, handed back if you miscount. A spoon for brewing cards.\nopt.right.hint [3cc148] = 先付钱，数错了就退。一把用于调制卡牌的勺子。\n# en: You count aloud, every drop, and every vial is right. Pell says nothing for a long moment. Then: \"Measure twice,\" and hands you the spoon.\nopt.right.say.1 [05ae35] = 你大声数着，每一滴都数，每一瓶都对。佩尔好半天没说话。然后：“量两遍。”他把勺子递给了你。\n# en: You lose count in the fifth vial. Pell hands your money back. \"Count aloud,\" he says. \"Every time. Then come back.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [b23077] = 你在第五瓶数乱了。佩尔把钱退给你。“大声数，”他说，“每一次都要。然后再来。”\n\n@event offer_honey_tin\n# en: A Tin of Silver\ntitle [1674d1] = 一罐银色\n# en: Maud is at the hives, veil down, lifting out a frame of comb that shines like poured silver. \"Silver bees,\" she says. \"They don't take to everyone. They've taken to you, dearie. Help me with the frames and there's a tin in it.\"\ntext [8b654f] = 莫德在蜂箱边，放下面纱，提起一框亮得像倒出来的银子一样的蜂巢。“银蜂，”她说，“它们不是谁都亲近。它们亲近你，亲爱的。帮我搬巢框，就送你一罐。”\n# en: A thatched cottage's garden full of beehives, an old woman in a veil lifting a frame of silver comb.\nscene [67ecd4] = 茅草小屋的花园里摆满蜂箱，一个戴面纱的老妇人正提起一框银色的蜂巢。\n# en: Keep well back from the bees\nopt.left.label [0b8fbf] = 离蜜蜂远远的\n# en: Maud laughs and does it herself. A silver bee follows you to the gate anyway, curious.\nopt.left.say.1 [f9e219] = 莫德笑着自己干了。一只银蜂还是好奇地跟你到了门口。\n# en: Help with the frames\nopt.right.label [f22555] = 帮忙搬巢框\n# en: A tin of honey: a way to sweeten someone on the right cards.\nopt.right.hint [1431a0] = 一罐蜂蜜：在合适的卡牌上给人甜头。\n# en: You get stung once, politely, on the thumb. Maud fills a dented tin with silver honey and presses it into your hands. \"Share it,\" she says. \"That's what it's for.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [978366] = 你被礼貌地蛰了一下拇指。莫德往一只磕瘪的罐子里装满银色的蜂蜜，塞到你手里。“分给别人，”她说，“它就是干这个用的。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/knowing_2.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/knowing_2.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event know_grisk_collectors\n# en: Running Late\ntitle [359088] = 逾期了\n# en: {x} is counting coins on the common-room table, and coming up short. {They} borrowed from Grisk in the village to get through the month, and the payment was due on Friday. \"He was so nice about it,\" {x} says. \"He called me his friend. He'll give me another week, surely.\"\ntext [f2adcf] = {x}在公共休息室的桌上数硬币，怎么数都不够。{they}为了熬过这个月，向村里的格里斯克借了钱，星期五就该还了。“他人可好了，”{x}说，“还叫我朋友。他肯定会再宽限我一个星期的。”\n# en: A common room at night, a student counting a few coins on a table and coming up short, a friend watching from the doorway.\nscene [f4eb0e] = 夜里的公共休息室，一个学生在桌上数着几枚硬币，怎么数都不够，一个朋友在门口看着。\n# en: \"Ask him for more time, then.\"\nopt.left.label [8449b2] = “那就去求他宽限几天吧。”\n# en: {x} goes down to the village to ask. The next evening there are two large, polite visitors at the castle gate asking for {x} by name, and {x} spends a fortnight's supper money making them go away. Nobody hears {x} call Grisk a friend after that.\nopt.left.say.1 [be9e81] = {x}下山去村里求情。第二天傍晚，城堡大门口来了两位块头很大、彬彬有礼的访客，指名要找{x}；{x}花掉了两个星期的晚饭钱才把他们打发走。从那以后，再没人听见{x}管格里斯克叫朋友。\n# en: \"Pay him tonight. Trust me.\"\nopt.right.label [bb34b8] = “今晚就还他。相信我。”\n# en: You tell {x} about the waistcoats, and the trolls inside them, and how polite and thorough they are. {x} goes pale, and you lend {x.them} the rest. Grisk takes the coins at the Newt that night with real regret. \"Such a pity,\" he says. \"They were looking forward to it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [363e6b] = 你给{x}讲了那两件马甲，马甲里的巨魔，还有他们有多客气、多彻底。{x}脸都白了，你把差的钱借给了{x.them}。当晚格里斯克在蝾螈酒馆收下硬币，看上去真心遗憾。“太可惜了，”他说，“他们可期待了。”\n\n@event know_grisk_newt\n# en: Paid in Advance\ntitle [e9d7a4] = 预付\n# en: A stationer's in the village is selling a whole term of ink, paper and candles at half price, paid in advance. Half the castle is queuing down the lane. It's a real bargain, as long as the shop is still there at the end of term.\ntext [94e8ad] = 村里一家文具店在半价预售一整个学期的墨水、纸和蜡烛。半个城堡的人都在巷子里排队。这确实划算，只要学期末这家店还在。\n# en: A village lane, a long queue of students outside a small shop with a hand-painted sign in the window offering half price.\nscene [fb2af1] = 村里的小巷，一家小店门外排着长长的学生队伍，橱窗里挂着手绘的半价招牌。\n# en: Buy a term's worth\nopt.buy.label [4c28d4] = 买一学期的量\n# en: Three weeks later the shutters are down and there's a notice on the door about creditors. Half the castle stands in the lane reading it. So do you.\nopt.buy.say.1 [b7be27] = 三个星期后，店门的卷帘拉下了，门上贴着一张关于债主的告示。半个城堡的人站在巷子里读它。你也在其中。\n# en: The shop lasts the term, just. You don't buy ink again until spring, and feel very clever every time you don't.\nopt.buy.say.2 [53c8b7] = 那家店勉强撑过了这个学期。直到开春你都没再买过墨水，每次不用买，你都觉得自己特别聪明。\n# en: Walk past the queue\nopt.pass.label [1cdab8] = 从队伍旁走过\n# en: You walk past. You'll never know whether it was a bargain. That bothers you more than it should.\nopt.pass.say.1 [8668e9] = 你走了过去。你永远不会知道那到底划不划算。这件事比它应该的更让你耿耿于怀。\n# en: Ask Grisk at the Newt first\nopt.ask.label [e915ee] = 先去蝾螈酒馆问问格里斯克\n# en: You buy Grisk a drink at his corner table and mention the shop. He sighs at the ceiling like a man discussing rain. \"Three weeks,\" he says. \"Four, if the landlord is sentimental.\" It's gone in three, and you were not in the queue.\nopt.ask.say.1 [d3fabd] = 你在格里斯克的角落桌请他喝了一杯，提起那家店。他对着天花板叹了口气，像在谈论下雨。“三个星期，”他说，“房东心软的话，四个。”三个星期后店就没了，而你没有排那个队。\n\n@event know_grisk_favours\n# en: Just a Small Favour\ntitle [e6ac78] = 只是帮个小忙\n# en: {x} looks relieved, which worries you. Grisk has offered to forget {x.their} whole debt for one small favour: carrying a parcel across the village after dark, for a client he didn't name. \"It's nothing,\" {x} says. \"It's a parcel. What's in a parcel?\"\ntext [4650db] = {x}看上去松了一口气，这让你担心。格里斯克提出，只要帮一个小忙，就把{x.their}债一笔勾销：天黑后替一位他没说名字的客人把一个包裹送过村子。“没什么的，”{x}说，“就是个包裹。包裹里能有什么？”\n# en: A castle stairwell, a relieved-looking student holding a small parcel wrapped in oilcloth, a friend frowning at it.\nscene [aec74c] = 城堡的楼梯间，一个如释重负的学生抱着一个油布包着的小包裹，一个朋友皱眉看着它。\n# en: \"A parcel's a parcel.\"\nopt.left.label [351231] = “包裹就是包裹。”\n# en: {x} carries the parcel, and the debt is gone, just like that. {They} won't tell you where {they} took it, and for the rest of term {they} won't go down to the village alone.\nopt.left.say.1 [33d6fa] = {x}送了包裹，债就这么没了。{they}不肯告诉你送去了哪儿，而且这学期剩下的日子里，{they}再也不肯一个人下山去村里。\n# en: \"Pay in coin. I'll help.\"\nopt.right.label [11950e] = “用钱还。我帮你。”\n# en: You know about Grisk's favours, and the clients he never names. You and {x} scrape the coins together between you instead. Grisk takes them with a little bow. \"Prudent,\" he says, and sounds almost disappointed.\nopt.right.say.1 [9a76a4] = 你知道格里斯克的那些“小忙”，还有他从不说名字的客人。于是你和{x}一起凑齐了钱。格里斯克微微鞠了一躬收下。“明智，”他说，听上去几乎有点失望。\n\n@event know_amaranth_book\n# en: Peppermint and a Charm\ntitle [29b8fc] = 薄荷和一道咒语\n# en: {x} has had a cold for a week and has cured it, {they} say{s}, with a remedy of {x.their} own: peppermint, honey, a pinch of something from the alchemy lab and a charm {they} won't explain. It works. {They} {are} too embarrassed to show anyone. \"Amaranth would laugh,\" {they} say{s}.\ntext [1864dd] = {x}感冒了一个星期，据{they}说，是用{x.their}自己的方子治好的：薄荷、蜂蜜、从炼金实验室弄来的一小撮什么东西，再加一道{they}不肯解释的咒语。真的管用。{they}不好意思给任何人看。“阿玛兰斯会笑话我的，”{they}说。\n# en: A dormitory window seat in winter, a student holding a steaming mug of something green, a friend sniffing it doubtfully.\nscene [1ad9c5] = 冬天宿舍的窗边座位，一个学生捧着一杯冒着热气的绿色东西，一个朋友满腹狐疑地闻了闻。\n# en: Ask for a cup yourself\nopt.left.label [8eeac3] = 自己也讨一杯\n# en: {x} makes you a cup. It's dreadful, and it works. {They} make{s} you promise to tell no one, and you never do, which seems a pity.\nopt.left.say.1 [82a25a] = {x}给你冲了一杯。难喝极了，但管用。{they}让你保证谁也不告诉，你也真的从没说过，想想挺可惜的。\n# en: \"She keeps a book of these.\"\nopt.right.label [9ff1b9] = “她有一本专门记这个的簿子。”\n# en: You tell {x} about Sister Amaranth's book of her students' remedies. It takes a week of persuading. When {x} finally shows her, Amaranth tries it, says nothing at all, and copies it out on a fresh page in her square hand. {x} doesn't stop smiling for a day.\nopt.right.say.1 [effaf3] = 你告诉{x}，阿玛兰斯修女有一本簿子，专门抄录学生们的方子。你劝了一个星期。{x}终于拿给她看时，阿玛兰斯尝了尝，一句话也没说，用她方正的字迹把它抄在了新的一页上。{x}笑了一整天。\n\n@event know_amaranth_rounds\n# en: Who Comes at Night\ntitle [4db2d9] = 夜里谁会来\n# en: {x} is in the infirmary overnight with a fever, and frightened, the way people are when they're ill and it's getting dark. \"What if something happens,\" {they} say{s}, \"and nobody comes?\" Visiting hours are nearly over.\ntext [5a794f] = {x}发着烧，要在医务室过夜，而且很害怕，人生病、天又黑下来的时候就会这样。“万一出了什么事，”{they}说，“却没人来怎么办？”探视时间快结束了。\n# en: An infirmary ward at dusk, a feverish student in a bed by the window, a friend on the chair beside it, the lamps being lit.\nscene [9da953] = 黄昏时的医务室病房，一个发烧的学生躺在窗边的床上，一个朋友坐在床边的椅子上，灯正一盏盏点亮。\n# en: Stay until they send you out\nopt.left.label [526b43] = 一直待到被赶出去\n# en: You stay, talking about nothing, until you're sent out with a scolding. {x} is asleep by then. You aren't, for hours.\nopt.left.say.1 [db2576] = 你留下来，东拉西扯，直到挨了一顿训被赶出去。那时{x}已经睡着了。而你几个小时都没睡着。\n# en: \"She does every round herself.\"\nopt.right.label [1acad4] = “每一轮查房都是她亲自来。”\n# en: You tell {x} about the rounds: every bed, every night, by Amaranth herself, and never once missed. {x} doesn't quite believe you. In the morning {they} tell{s} you {they} woke at two, and there she was with her lamp. \"She nodded at me,\" {they} say{s}, amazed.\nopt.right.say.1 [4d273b] = 你给{x}讲了查房的事：每一张床，每一个晚上，都是阿玛兰斯亲自来，从没漏过一次。{x}不太相信。第二天早上{they}告诉你，{they}两点醒来，她果然提着灯在那儿。“她还冲我点了点头，”{they}惊奇地说。\n\n@event know_pell_not_yet\n# en: Not Yet\ntitle [033adc] = 还不行\n# en: Pell is restocking the bottom of the wall when you come in, and his oldest drawer is open beside him, the one with the yellowed card in it. He looks at you over the drawer for longer than usual, as if measuring something twice.\ntext [e88357] = 你进门时，佩尔正在给墙最底下补货，他最老的那个抽屉在他身边开着，就是放着那张发黄卡片的那个。他隔着抽屉看了你比平时更久，像是在把什么东西量第二遍。\n# en: An apothecary's wall of a thousand small drawers, one very old drawer open near the floor, an old man kneeling beside it with a yellowed card.\nscene [bd211c] = 药铺里一整面墙的上千个小抽屉，靠近地面的一个很老的抽屉开着，一位老人跪在旁边，拿着一张发黄的卡片。\n# en: Wait for him to finish\nopt.left.label [2fc371] = 等他忙完\n# en: He finishes, shuts the drawer and serves you as if nothing happened, counting aloud, as always. On your way out you notice he's watching you go.\nopt.left.say.1 [0c7669] = 他忙完了，关上抽屉，像什么都没发生过一样招呼你，照旧大声数着。出门时你发现他一直看着你走。\n# en: Ask about the card\nopt.right.label [ec7472] = 问问那张卡片\n# en: You ask about the card, the draught \"of your own strength\". Pell takes it out, reads it, and looks at you again. Then he puts it back, and under \"Not yet.\" he adds one word in his precise hand: \"Nearly.\" He doesn't explain. He doesn't need to.\nopt.right.say.1 [84ca5a] = 你问起那张卡片，那剂“以己之力”的药。佩尔把它拿出来读了一遍，又看了看你。然后他把它放回去，在“还不行。”下面用他工整的字迹添了一个词：“快了。”他没有解释。他也不需要解释。\n\n@event know_vey_own_face\n# en: It Slides Off\ntitle [16a343] = 它滑掉了\n# en: {x} has been trying all week to glamour {x.their} roommate's hair green, for a dare, and it won't hold: it slides off within the hour, every time. {They} {are} about ready to give up on illusion altogether.\ntext [730c92] = 为了一个赌，{x}一整个星期都在想用幻术把{x.their}室友的头发变绿，可就是撑不住：每次不到一个小时就滑掉了。{they}差不多准备彻底放弃幻术了。\n# en: A dormitory mirror, a frustrated student pointing a wand at a roommate's hair, which is flickering between two colours.\nscene [fac88b] = 宿舍的镜子前，一个懊恼的学生拿魔杖指着室友的头发，那头发在两种颜色之间闪个不停。\n# en: Practise with {x.them} all evening\nopt.left.label [5784eb] = 陪{x.them}练一整晚\n# en: You practise all evening. By midnight the green holds for two whole hours. {x} says that's progress, and goes to bed looking like someone who doesn't believe it.\nopt.left.say.1 [dbe15a] = 你们练了一整晚。到午夜，那抹绿色撑了整整两个小时。{x}说这算进步，上床时却是一副不信的样子。\n# en: \"Try it on your own face first.\"\nopt.right.label [f6c5fe] = “先在你自己脸上试试。”\n# en: You tell {x} what Vey says: a glamour you can't hold on yourself won't hold on anyone. {x} tries it on {x.their} own nose, swears at the mirror for an hour, and then it holds. The roommate is green for a week. Vey hears of it, awards nobody any points, and smiles.\nopt.right.say.1 [14ccff] = 你把维伊的话告诉{x}：自己身上都撑不住的幻术，放到谁身上也撑不住。{x}在{x.their}自己的鼻子上试，对着镜子骂了一个小时，然后就撑住了。室友绿了一个星期。维伊听说后，谁也没加分，只是笑了。\n\n@event know_marisol_margins\n# en: The Book in the Margins\ntitle [542b1c] = 页边里的书\n# en: {x} has the tidecalling textbook open at the same page {they} had last week, and the week before. \"It makes sense when I read it,\" {they} say{s}, \"and no sense at all in the lake.\" The exam is close. {They} {are} starting to talk about dropping the course.\ntext [dc7b05] = {x}的潮汐术课本翻开在上周、上上周的同一页。“读的时候明明都懂，”{they}说，“一下湖就全不懂了。”考试快到了。{they}开始说要退掉这门课。\n# en: A library table, a student staring at the same open page of a tidecalling textbook, a friend sitting down across from them.\nscene [1b837e] = 图书馆的桌边，一个学生盯着潮汐术课本的同一页，一个朋友在对面坐下。\n# en: Drill {x.them} at dawn\nopt.left.label [5b3c23] = 黎明时陪{x.them}练\n# en: You spend three cold dawns at the lake with {x}, going through it line by line. It helps, a little. {x} passes narrowly, and is grateful, and still hates the book.\nopt.left.say.1 [03c8cf] = 你陪{x}在湖边过了三个寒冷的黎明，一行一行地过。有点帮助。{x}勉强及格了，心怀感激，但还是讨厌那本书。\n# en: \"Ask Marisol about her poems.\"\nopt.right.label [54da73] = “去问问玛丽索尔她的诗集。”\n# en: You tell {x} about the book of poems Marisol wrote her own tidecalling into as a student. {x} asks her, mortified. Marisol lends it on the spot, dripping. The margins make sense of everything. {x} passes well, and says the poems were awful but the margins were wonderful.\nopt.right.say.1 [0d6706] = 你告诉{x}，玛丽索尔当学生时把自己的潮汐术写在了一本诗集的页边。{x}窘得要命地去问她。玛丽索尔浑身滴着水，当场就把书借了出来。页边的笔记让一切都说得通了。{x}考得很好，说那些诗糟透了，但页边棒极了。\n\n@event know_mabel_tins\n# en: A Tin on the Barge\ntitle [3181a2] = 驳船上的一只铁罐\n# en: {x} has gone quiet this month. {They} {are} homesick, though {they} would never say so. A letter from home came on Tuesday, and it's still unopened beside {x.their} plate.\ntext [c7d504] = {x}这个月变得很安静。{they}想家了，虽然{they}绝不会说出口。星期二家里来了一封信，现在还没拆，就放在{x.their}盘子旁边。\n# en: A refectory table at supper, a quiet student with an unopened letter beside the plate, a friend sliding onto the bench next to them.\nscene [24ad8b] = 晚饭时膳堂的桌边，一个沉默的学生盘子旁放着一封没拆的信，一个朋友挪到旁边的长凳上坐下。\n# en: Sit with {x.them} at supper\nopt.left.label [0ec4df] = 晚饭时陪{x.them}坐着\n# en: You sit with {x} at supper every night that week and talk about nothing much. It doesn't fix anything. On Sunday {they} open{s} the letter, with you there.\nopt.left.say.1 [b7ca94] = 那个星期你每天晚饭都陪{x}坐着，随便聊些什么。这什么也解决不了。星期天，{they}当着你的面拆开了那封信。\n# en: Write to Mabel\nopt.right.label [5ac6ac] = 给梅布尔写信\n# en: You send a note down to Mabel in the village. Two days later a tin comes up on the barge, addressed to {x} in round handwriting: tea, a slab of cake, and no note. {x} never finds out who sent it, and keeps the tin on {x.their} windowsill all year.\nopt.right.say.1 [1f2a64] = 你给村里的梅布尔捎了张字条。两天后，驳船送来一只铁罐，圆圆的字迹写着{x}的名字：里面是茶、一大块蛋糕，没有字条。{x}始终不知道是谁寄的，那只铁罐在{x.their}窗台上放了一整年。\n\n@event know_mabel_fourth_cake\n# en: A Cake With a Name\ntitle [b102ea] = 有名字的蛋糕\n# en: It's {x}'s birthday, and you've brought {x.them} to Mabel's, because there's nowhere better. {x} looks at the three cakes on the counter as if choosing between them were the hardest decision of the year.\ntext [5d819e] = 今天是{x}的生日，你带{x.them}来了梅布尔茶馆，因为没有更好的地方了。{x}看着柜台上的三块蛋糕，仿佛在它们之间挑一块是今年最难的决定。\n# en: A village tea shop, three cakes under glass domes on the counter, two students leaning over them, a woman in an apron watching.\nscene [ea669f] = 村里的茶馆，柜台上三块蛋糕罩在玻璃罩里，两个学生俯身看着，一个系围裙的女人在一旁看着他们。\n# en: Let {x.them} choose\nopt.left.label [93f23b] = 让{x.them}自己挑\n# en: {x} chooses the lemon after ten minutes, and says it was the right choice, and then eats half of yours.\nopt.left.say.1 [df1461] = 十分钟后{x}选了柠檬的，说这是正确的选择，然后吃掉了你的一半。\n# en: Ask Mabel for a fourth cake\nopt.right.label [a78873] = 问梅布尔要第四种蛋糕\n# en: You ask, quietly, whether there's a fourth. Mabel looks at {x}, vanishes into the back and comes out with something pink and alarming. She writes its card while you watch: {x}'s name, and the date. {x} eats it, survives, and is very proud to be on the card.\nopt.right.say.1 [a6722d] = 你悄悄问有没有第四种。梅布尔看了看{x}，消失在后厨，端出来一样粉红色、看着叫人心惊的东西。她当着你的面写好卡片：{x}的名字，还有日期。{x}吃了，活了下来，并且为上了那张卡片而非常骄傲。\n\n@event know_isolde_mercy\n# en: A Witness to Character\ntitle [0a6753] = 品格证人\n# en: {x} has been sent to the headmistress over a prank that went further than {they} meant, and has asked you along as a witness to {x.their} good character. Headmistress Marrow hears all of it without a word. Then she turns to you.\ntext [6416c5] = {x}因为一个闹过了头的恶作剧被送到院长那里，请你一起去，为{x.their}品格作证。马罗院长一言不发地听完了全部经过。然后她转向你。\n# en: A tall tower study, a headmistress behind her desk with her hands folded, two students standing before it, one of them very pale.\nscene [56a706] = 高塔里的书房，院长双手交叠坐在桌后，两个学生站在桌前，其中一个脸色煞白。\n# en: Vouch for {x.them}, warmly\nopt.left.label [d7e1f9] = 热情地为{x.them}担保\n# en: You vouch for {x}, warmly and at length. The headmistress thanks you, and gives {x} a month of detentions anyway. \"Character,\" she says, \"is what one has left after the detention.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [e8ec1b] = 你热情洋溢地为{x}担保了好半天。院长谢过你，照样罚了{x}一个月的禁闭。“品格，”她说，“是禁闭之后剩下的东西。”\n# en: Wait for her question\nopt.right.label [02c130] = 等她发问\n# en: You say nothing, and wait. Sure enough, she asks what you think would be just. You tell her honestly: not nothing, but less than a month, and the damage put right by {x}'s own hands. She considers it, and, to {x}'s astonishment, she listens.\nopt.right.say.1 [1c27d0] = 你什么也没说，只是等着。果然，她问你觉得怎样才算公正。你老实告诉她：不是不罚，但不到一个月，而且损坏的东西要{x}亲手修好。她想了想，而让{x}大吃一惊的是，她听进去了。\n\n@event know_grimsby_upper_floor\n# en: Out of Print\ntitle [64df47] = 绝版\n# en: You need a book for an essay: the one every reading list cites and nobody has seen in thirty years. The library's copy went missing before you arrived. Grimsby, asked, gives you a book about lighthouses instead and says it's the next best thing. He may even be right.\ntext [47ab6c] = 你写论文需要一本书：每份书单都引用、却三十年没人见过的那本。图书馆那本在你入学前就丢了。你去问格林斯比，他给了你一本关于灯塔的书，说这是退而求其次的最佳选择。他说不定还真说对了。\n# en: A crowded bookshop, an old bookseller holding out a book about lighthouses, a student behind the counter's ladder looking upward.\nscene [d80181] = 拥挤的书店里，一位老书商递出一本关于灯塔的书，一个学生站在柜台后的梯子旁抬头往上看。\n# en: Take the lighthouse book\nopt.left.label [f88d9c] = 拿那本灯塔书\n# en: You read the lighthouse book. There's a chapter on lenses that turns out, absurdly, to help with your essay. It's still not the book, and Grimsby is unbearable about it.\nopt.left.say.1 [d44ccb] = 你读了那本灯塔书。里面有一章讲透镜，荒唐的是，它居然对你的论文有帮助。但它还是不是那本书，而格林斯比为此得意得叫人受不了。\n# en: Climb to the upper floor\nopt.right.label [e65a5a] = 爬上楼上那层\n# en: You climb the ladder behind the shop, skipping the third and fourth rungs, and spend an hour in the dust among the remainders. The book is there, under a stack of almanacs. Grimsby, below, says he had no idea, and charges you half price for knowing about the ladder.\nopt.right.say.1 [685272] = 你爬上店后面的梯子，跳过第三和第四级，在滞销书的灰尘里待了一个小时。那本书就在那儿，压在一摞年鉴下面。格林斯比在底下说他完全不知道，然后因为你知道那架梯子，只收了你半价。\n\n@event know_grimsby_parcels\n# en: On Credit\ntitle [28a515] = 赊账\n# en: {x} has been avoiding the village for a month. It comes out eventually: Grimsby posted {x.them} three books on credit, exactly the right ones, and {they} can't pay, and {they} {are} too ashamed to walk past the shop. \"He'll have my name on a list,\" {they} say{s}.\ntext [427dec] = {x}一个月都在躲着村子。最后还是说出来了：格林斯比赊账给{x.them}寄了三本书，恰好都是{they}需要的，可{they}付不起，羞得连那家店门口都不敢走。“他肯定把我的名字记在单子上了，”{they}说。\n# en: A castle corridor window looking down toward the village, a student turning away from it, a friend catching their sleeve.\nscene [45b2c9] = 城堡走廊的窗户俯瞰着村子，一个学生转身背对窗户，一个朋友拉住了对方的袖子。\n# en: Help {x.them} save up\nopt.left.label [0e5740] = 帮{x.them}攒钱\n# en: You help {x} put coins aside, week by week. It takes the rest of term. When {x} finally goes in to pay, Grimsby looks puzzled: he'd forgotten. {x} hadn't, for a single day.\nopt.left.say.1 [97e1a8] = 你帮{x}一周一周地攒钱。这花了整个学期剩下的时间。{x}终于进店去还钱时，格林斯比一脸困惑：他早忘了。而{x}一天也没忘过。\n# en: \"He's never chased a debt.\"\nopt.right.label [7f98c4] = “他从来不催债。”\n# en: You tell {x} that Grimsby posts books on credit and has never chased a debt in his life. {x} goes down that afternoon to say sorry, and comes back with a fourth book and a cup of tea inside {x.them}. \"He said to pay when I can,\" {they} say{s}, \"and to stop being silly.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [b86d30] = 你告诉{x}，格林斯比常赊账寄书，这辈子从没催过一笔债。{x}当天下午就下山去道歉，回来时多了第四本书，肚子里还多了一杯茶。“他说等我有钱再付，”{they}说，“还叫我别犯傻。”\n\n@event know_ilse_frost\n# en: Frost on the Inside\ntitle [8bbca7] = 结在里面的霜\n# en: Winter has got into the dormitories. The windows frost over on the inside every night, and the warming ward the professors put up keeps cracking. Everyone is sleeping in their coats, and the porters say it will be mended next week, as they said last week.\ntext [0e26f5] = 冬天钻进了宿舍。每天夜里窗户都从里面结霜，教授们布下的取暖结界一再开裂。大家都穿着大衣睡觉，门房说下星期就修好，上星期他们也是这么说的。\n# en: A dormitory corridor in deep winter, windows frosted over on the inside, students in coats and hats, a girl in white furs looking unimpressed at a cracked ward.\nscene [f3ea25] = 深冬的宿舍走廊，窗户从里面结满了霜，学生们穿着大衣戴着帽子，一个裹着白色毛皮的女孩不以为然地看着一道裂开的结界。\n# en: Patch the ward with the others\nopt.left.label [c87c72] = 和大家一起修补结界\n# en: You and half the corridor patch it, badly, out of a textbook. It holds most nights. You get very good at sleeping in a hat.\nopt.left.say.1 [071e26] = 你和半条走廊的人照着课本把它补上了，补得很糟。大多数夜里它还撑得住。你练就了戴着帽子睡觉的好本事。\n# en: Ask Ilse\nopt.right.label [0a147f] = 去找伊尔泽\n# en: You go to Ilse, who knows Northern frost-craft better than anything the professors teach. She looks at the ward for less than a minute, says something rude about it in Northern, and reworks it with two fingers. The windows are clear by morning. She refuses all thanks, and accepts a scarf.\nopt.right.say.1 [4d41df] = 你去找伊尔泽，她对北境霜术的了解胜过教授们教的任何东西。她看了那道结界不到一分钟，用北境话说了句难听的，用两根手指把它重新编了一遍。到早上窗户就干净了。她拒绝了所有道谢，却收下了一条围巾。\n\n@event know_tamsin_backdrop\n# en: A Forest by Post\ntitle [604d28] = 寄来的森林\n# en: The first-years' end-of-term play needs a backdrop, a forest at night, and you, somehow, have been volunteered to paint it. You have a week, a bucket of grey and no idea at all.\ntext [888980] = 一年级生的期末戏需要一块背景幕，画夜晚的森林，而不知怎么的，你被“自愿”去画它了。你有一个星期、一桶灰颜料，以及毫无头绪。\n# en: A hall stage after supper, a huge blank canvas propped against the wall, a student with a bucket of grey paint and a letter full of sketches.\nscene [14314f] = 晚饭后礼堂的舞台上，一大块空白画布靠在墙边，一个学生提着一桶灰颜料，手里拿着一封画满速写的信。\n# en: Paint the best forest you can\nopt.left.label [7f3232] = 尽力画一片森林\n# en: You paint a forest. It looks like a forest from the back of the hall, if the lamps are low. The first-years are very kind about it.\nopt.left.say.1 [06721a] = 你画了一片森林。从礼堂最后排看，灯光暗一点的话，它像一片森林。一年级生们对此非常宽容。\n# en: Write to Tamsin for help\nopt.right.label [741f5f] = 写信向塔姆辛求助\n# en: You write to Tamsin, who learned illusion painting their grandmother's backdrops. The reply is six pages of sketches and insults about your brushwork. You follow it to the letter. On the night, the forest stirs in a wind that isn't there, and the hall gasps. You send Tamsin the programme.\nopt.right.say.1 [bb7db7] = 你写信给塔姆辛，TA是给祖母画背景幕学会幻术的。回信是六页速写，外加对你笔法的各种挖苦。你一字不差地照做。演出那晚，森林在一阵并不存在的风里摇曳，整个礼堂都倒吸了一口气。你把节目单寄给了塔姆辛。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/knowing.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/knowing.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event know_kael_thaw\n# en: Before the Break\ntitle [60004e] = 放假之前\n# en: The last barge before the winter break is loading, and Kael is at the landing with his trunk, not quite looking at you. He's been strange all week. Back home, he told you once, saying goodbye is bad luck. The barge horn sounds. He's waiting, as if there were something you might say.\ntext [e113f2] = 寒假前的最后一班驳船正在装货，凯尔带着行李箱站在码头上，不太看你。他这一星期都怪怪的。他跟你说过，在他老家，说再见是不吉利的。驳船的汽笛响了。他在等，好像你也许会说点什么。\n# en: A barge landing in the snow at the end of term, students with trunks waiting to leave, two of them standing face to face at the edge of the jetty.\nscene [923204] = 学期末雪中的驳船码头，学生们带着行李等着离开，其中两人在栈桥边上面对面站着。\n# en: Shake his hand\nopt.left.label [e76d26] = 和他握手\n# en: You shake his hand, and wish him a good break. He says \"and you\", and climbs aboard, and doesn't look back. You realise, as the barge rounds the bend, that he was waiting for something else.\nopt.left.say.1 [6f20ed] = 你和他握了握手，祝他假期愉快。他说“你也是”，爬上了船，没有回头。驳船绕过河弯时你才意识到，他等的是别的东西。\n# en: \"Until the thaw.\"\nopt.right.label [23517c] = “冰融时见。”\n# en: \"Until the thaw,\" you say, and touch your forehead to his. He goes very still. Then he laughs, a short surprised breath, and says it back, so the cold knows you're coming back. He waves from the barge all the way round the bend.\nopt.right.say.1 [af7bd8] = “冰融时见，”你说，把额头抵在他的额头上。他一下子僵住了。然后他笑了，短短地、惊讶地吐了口气，也对你说了这句话，好让寒冷知道你们还会回来。驳船绕过河弯之前，他一直在挥手。\n\n@event know_ilse_useless\n# en: Ilse, Losing\ntitle [a863da] = 伊尔泽输了\n# en: Ilse's frost-work has gone wrong for the first time anyone has seen. She's sitting on the frozen pond beside a sculpture that has cracked down the middle, and the private snowstorm over her head has turned to sleet. \"Go away,\" she says. \"Or teach me something. I don't care which.\"\ntext [d92fac] = 伊尔泽的冰霜作品头一回在众人眼前出了岔子。她独自坐在结冰的池塘上，身旁一座冰雕从中间裂开了，头顶那场私人暴风雪变成了冻雨。“走开，”她说，“要么教我点什么。哪样都行。”\n# en: A frozen pond at dusk, a girl in white furs sitting alone on the ice with a ruined frost-sculpture beside her, snow falling only on her.\nscene [7c8d9f] = 黄昏结冰的池塘上，一个裹着白色毛皮的女孩独自坐着，身旁是一座毁掉的冰雕，雪只落在她一个人身上。\n# en: Help her mend the sculpture\nopt.left.label [f184b7] = 帮她修补冰雕\n# en: You hold the crack while she seals it, and it takes, and the sculpture stands. She looks at it critically. \"Useful,\" she says, as if that were an insult. The sleet eases, a little.\nopt.left.say.1 [7c3675] = 你扶住裂缝，她把它封上，封住了，冰雕立了起来。她挑剔地看着它。“有用，”她说，像是在骂人。冻雨稍微小了一点。\n# en: You make it worse. The sculpture falls in two. Ilse looks at the pieces, then at you. \"Well,\" she says. \"Now we're both bad at it.\" It isn't quite a joke.\nopt.left.say.2 [f2415f] = 你越弄越糟。冰雕裂成了两半。伊尔泽看看碎块，又看看你。“好吧，”她说，“现在我们俩都不行了。”这不太算是个玩笑。\n# en: Teach her to skim stones across the ice\nopt.right.label [f27895] = 教她在冰上打水漂\n# en: You teach her something completely useless: skimming stones across the ice, as far as they'll go. She counts every bounce out loud, in Northern. The sleet turns back to snow. When you leave, she's still at it, alone, counting, and very nearly smiling.\nopt.right.say.1 [04ff9a] = 你教她一件毫无用处的事：让石子在冰面上跳着滑出去，越远越好。每跳一下她都用北地话大声数出来。冻雨又变回了雪。你离开时她还在那儿，一个人，数着，几乎在笑。\n\n@event know_ilse_stitch\n# en: A Torn Ward\ntitle [aec809] = 撕破的护符\n# en: You find a frost-ward charm in the snow by the courtyard fountain, torn half through, the kind Ilse makes. Without it, whoever lost it will feel the cold all winter. The stitching is unusual, a looping Northern pattern. You have a needle and some silver thread.\ntext [4f5032] = 你在庭院喷泉边的雪里捡到一个冰霜护符，撕破了一半，是伊尔泽做的那种。没了它，丢东西的人一整个冬天都会觉得冷。针脚很特别，是一种绕圈的北地针法。你身上正好有一根针和一些银线。\n# en: A courtyard bench in winter, a torn white frost-ward charm lying in a student's hands, a needle and silver thread beside it.\nscene [b895e3] = 冬天庭院的长凳上，一个撕破的白色冰霜护符躺在一个学生手里，旁边放着针和银线。\n# en: Take it to the lost-property box\nopt.left.label [1b78a2] = 交到失物招领箱\n# en: You hand it in. Somebody claims it the next day: a second-year who has been shivering for a week. Ilse, you hear later, made her a new one, without being asked, and without saying anything.\nopt.left.say.1 [5bd2cf] = 你把它交了上去。第二天就有人认领了：一个已经冻得发抖一个星期的二年级生。你后来听说，伊尔泽给她做了个新的，没人请她，她也什么都没说。\n# en: Mend it with her mother's stitch\nopt.right.label [b4f66b] = 用她母亲的针法补好\n# en: You mend it slowly, in the looping stitch, the one that was her mother's. When you hand it to Ilse, she turns it over, finds the mend, and goes very quiet. Kael, beside her, touches it once. \"Mother's,\" he says. Neither of them says anything else, for a long time.\nopt.right.say.1 [887280] = 你用那种绕圈的针法慢慢地补，那是她母亲的针法。你把它交给伊尔泽时，她翻过来，找到了补过的地方，一下子安静了。她身边的凯尔摸了一下。“是母亲的，”他说。很长一段时间，他们俩谁都没再说话。\n\n@event know_isolde_chess\n# en: Called to the Tower\ntitle [f0d27f] = 被叫到塔上\n# en: You've been sent to the headmistress over a complaint from a lecturer: a broken window, a misunderstanding, a lot of shouting. Headmistress Marrow reads the letter, then looks at you over it. Behind her, the tower chess game by the window is half finished, and one of her towers is in trouble.\ntext [34c356] = 一位讲师投诉了你，你被送到了院长那里：一扇打碎的窗，一场误会，一通大喊大叫。马罗院长读完信，从信纸上方看着你。她身后，窗边的塔棋下到一半，她的一座塔有麻烦了。\n# en: A tall tower study, a chessboard of crystal towers by the window mid-game, a headmistress behind a desk with a letter of complaint open in front of her.\nscene [981aaa] = 高高的塔楼书房，窗边一盘下到一半的水晶塔棋，院长坐在书桌后，面前摊着一封投诉信。\n# en: Explain, very carefully\nopt.left.label [da77bf] = 非常小心地解释\n# en: You explain, carefully and truthfully, and she listens without interrupting. At the end she folds the letter. \"Pay for the glass,\" she says. \"And apologise properly.\" It could have gone a great deal worse.\nopt.left.say.1 [5a065a] = 你小心而如实地解释了，她听着，没有打断。最后她把信折了起来。“赔玻璃，”她说，“好好道个歉。”本来可能糟糕得多。\n# en: You explain, and it comes out sounding like an excuse even to you. She hears you out, and fines you for the window, and makes you write the apology twice.\nopt.left.say.2 [6bff15] = 你解释了，可连你自己听着都像借口。她听你说完，罚你赔窗户，还让你把道歉信写了两遍。\n# en: Offer to finish her game\nopt.right.label [8d2ae4] = 提出替她下完这盘棋\n# en: \"Your tower's in trouble,\" you say. She looks at you, and then at the board, and says \"Is it?\" You play it out together. She loses, and laughs, short and delighted. Then she folds the letter. \"Pay for the glass,\" she says. \"Come back and lose to me properly, sometime.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [81aaaa] = “您的塔有麻烦了，”你说。她看看你，又看看棋盘，说：“是吗？”你们一起下完了。她输了，短促而高兴地笑了一声。然后她把信折了起来。“赔玻璃，”她说，“改天再来，好好输给我一回。”\n\n@event know_nessa_off_record\n# en: Asking About a Friend\ntitle [69bed0] = 打听一位朋友\n# en: Nessa wants to ask you about a friend of yours, who's in trouble with the prefects and who you'd rather nobody wrote about. \"I'll print it either way,\" she says. \"I'd rather print it right.\" Her quill is already moving, taking down everything you say, including your sighs.\ntext [03b52e] = 内莎想问你一位朋友的事，对方和级长们闹了麻烦，你宁愿谁也别写这件事。“不管怎样我都会登，”她说，“我宁可登得准确。”她的羽毛笔已经在动了，把你说的一切都记了下来，包括你的叹气。\n# en: A newspaper office, an editor at her desk, a self-writing quill poised over a notebook, a student in the visitor's chair with arms folded.\nscene [5a27a1] = 报社办公室里，一位编辑坐在桌边，一支自动羽毛笔悬在笔记本上方，来访者椅子上的学生抱着胳膊。\n# en: Give her the friendly version\nopt.left.label [de53f7] = 给她一个友善的版本\n# en: You give her the kindest true version you can, and the quill takes it down. The piece runs, fair and short. Your friend is grateful, mostly. The quill added an adjective you didn't say.\nopt.left.say.1 [b304fb] = 你给了她你能给的最友善的真实版本，羽毛笔记了下来。报道登了，公道而简短。你的朋友大体上很感激。羽毛笔多加了一个你没说过的形容词。\n# en: \"This is off the record.\"\nopt.right.label [045e5e] = “这是不对外的。”\n# en: \"Off the record,\" you say, and the quill stops mid-word and lies down on the desk. Nessa looks at it, and at you, and something in her face changes. You tell her the whole of it. She never prints a line, and the next week she helps your friend with the prefects instead.\nopt.right.say.1 [d50fc7] = “不对外，”你说，羽毛笔在一个字写到一半时停了下来，躺倒在桌上。内莎看看它，又看看你，脸上有什么变了。你把整件事都告诉了她。她一行字都没登，下个星期还去帮你的朋友应付了级长。\n\n@event know_nessa_planted\n# en: The Proofs\ntitle [b816b3] = 校样\n# en: Nessa drops a stack of proofs on the desk in front of you and goes to make tea, very casually. \"Errors,\" she says from the doorway. \"Find them before the printers come. There might be one.\" There are, you can see already, at least four.\ntext [1ee516] = 内莎把一摞校样扔在你面前的桌上，若无其事地去泡茶了。“错误，”她在门口说，“印刷工来之前找出来。也许有一个。”你一眼就看出来，至少有四个。\n# en: A cluttered newspaper office at night, galley proofs spread over a desk under a lamp, an editor watching a student from across the room.\nscene [50060f] = 入夜后杂乱的报社办公室，灯下的桌上摊着长条校样，一位编辑从房间那头看着一个学生。\n# en: Fix everything you can find\nopt.left.label [26b85f] = 能找到的都改掉\n# en: You find four errors and fix them, and a fifth that turns out to be the editor's style. Nessa reads your corrections, nods, and points to a sixth, in the headline, that you missed. \"Close,\" she says.\nopt.left.say.1 [99755d] = 你找到了四个错误改掉了，还有第五个，结果是编辑的文风。内莎读了你的修改，点点头，指出了第六个，在标题里，你漏了。“差一点，”她说。\n# en: Find hers first\nopt.right.label [5e8da4] = 先找她埋的那个\n# en: You find the planted one first: a date that couldn't be true, set in the middle of the page like a trap. You circle it, and only then fix the others. Nessa comes back with the tea, sees the circle, and laughs out loud. \"Nobody's ever gone for that one first,\" she says. \"You're hired. Unpaid, obviously.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [db5c10] = 你先找到了她埋的那个：一个不可能对的日期，像陷阱一样摆在版面中间。你把它圈了出来，然后才去改别的。内莎端着茶回来，看见那个圈，大笑出声。“从没人先去找那个，”她说，“你被录用了。没有工钱，当然。”\n# en: You hunt for whichever error she meant, and can't tell which it is, and run out of time before the printers come. Nessa takes the proofs back with a small, satisfied smile. \"There's always one,\" she says, and doesn't say which. The hunt, you have to admit, was fun.\nopt.right.say.2 [3a3090] = 你到处找她说的是哪个错误，分辨不出来，印刷工来之前就没时间了。内莎带着一丝得意的微笑把校样收了回去。“总会有一个的，”她说，却没说是哪个。你得承认，找的过程挺有意思。\n\n@event know_seraphine_too_loud\n# en: Lost at Lantern Night\ntitle [65fdca] = 灯笼夜走失\n# en: Lantern Night, and the whole academy and half the village are on the lakeshore, laughing, singing, all their futures at once. Somebody says Seraphine was here a minute ago and now she isn't. Her friends are searching the crowd, calling her name over the music.\ntext [25f04e] = 灯笼夜，整个学院和半个村子都在湖岸上，笑着，唱着，所有人的未来一齐涌来。有人说塞拉芬刚才还在这儿，现在不见了。她的朋友们在人群里找，压过音乐喊着她的名字。\n# en: A crowded lakeshore at night strung with floating lanterns, a press of students and villagers, one dark boathouse at the edge with its door ajar.\nscene [c92fb1] = 夜里挂满浮灯的湖岸挤满了学生和村民，岸边尽头一间黑黢黢的船屋，门半开着。\n# en: Search the crowd with them\nopt.left.label [48ac21] = 和他们一起在人群里找\n# en: You push through the crowd for an hour, calling her name. Someone finds her in the end, sitting very still in the dark of the boathouse, and she says she's fine, and isn't, and goes back to bed early.\nopt.left.say.1 [2a40c7] = 你在人群里挤了一个小时，喊着她的名字。最后有人在船屋的黑暗里找到了她，她一动不动地坐着，说她没事，其实有事，早早回去睡了。\n# en: Look for her somewhere dark\nopt.right.label [d6e0c7] = 去黑暗的地方找她\n# en: You don't look in the crowd. You walk away from it, to the dark boathouse at the end, and she's there on an upturned boat with her eyes shut. You sit down beside her without a word. After a while she says, \"Quiet.\" Then: \"Yours.\" You stay till the lanterns are all out.\nopt.right.say.1 [eeac21] = 你没在人群里找。你离开人群，走向尽头黑黢黢的船屋，她就在那儿，坐在一只翻扣的船上，闭着眼睛。你一言不发地在她身边坐下。过了一会儿她说：“安静。”然后：“你的。”你一直待到灯笼全都熄灭。\n\n@event know_thorne_hands\n# en: The Demonstration\ntitle [6b9817] = 示范\n# en: Thorne is demonstrating a duelling stance to the whole year, wand raised, and halfway through the demonstration his wand hand begins to shake. It's small. Most of the hall hasn't noticed yet. He has. You can see him deciding whether to stop.\ntext [ea2ef6] = 索恩正在给整个年级示范一个决斗架势，魔杖举着，示范到一半，他握杖的手开始发抖。很轻微。大厅里大多数人还没注意到。他注意到了。你看得出他在决定要不要停下来。\n# en: A lecture hall, an old professor at the front with his wand raised for a demonstration, its tip trembling very slightly, a student in the front row rising from their seat.\nscene [2c6a85] = 阶梯教室里，一位老教授站在前面举着魔杖示范，杖尖微微颤抖，第一排一个学生正从座位上站起来。\n# en: Look away, and let him finish\nopt.left.label [9f4249] = 移开目光，让他示范完\n# en: You look down at your notes, and so, after a moment, does the rest of the front row. Thorne finishes the demonstration with his other hand, briskly, and dismisses the class early. You're fairly sure that was the right thing.\nopt.left.say.1 [ef5468] = 你低头看笔记，过了一会儿，第一排其他人也跟着低下了头。索恩换了一只手，利落地示范完，提前下了课。你很确定这样做是对的。\n# en: Ask to be his demonstration partner\nopt.right.label [3bc361] = 请求当他的示范搭档\n# en: \"Could you show it on someone, sir?\" you ask, and step up before he can answer. He takes your wrist instead of his wand, and shows the stance with your arm, steady, the whole year watching. Afterwards he says, \"Hm.\" It's the warmest thing he's ever said to you.\nopt.right.say.1 [f22b54] = “先生，能在人身上示范一下吗？”你问，不等他回答就走了上去。他没拿魔杖，而是握住你的手腕，用你的胳膊示范那个架势，稳稳的，全年级都看着。之后他说：“嗯。”这是他对你说过的最温暖的话。\n\n@event know_tamsin_failed\n# en: Exam Nerves, by Post\ntitle [4ae610] = 邮寄来的考前紧张\n# en: Tamsin's letter is short again. They're sitting their advanced illusion examination next week, and they can't sleep, and every insult in the letter is half-hearted, which is how you know. \"Say something,\" the postscript says. \"Anything. I'm not asking. Obviously.\"\ntext [5a73ae] = 塔姆辛的信又很短。TA下周要考高级幻术，睡不着觉，信里每一句挖苦都有气无力，你就是凭这个看出来的。“说点什么，”附言写道，“什么都行。我可没在求你。显然。”\n# en: A letter on a dormitory desk, its handwriting cramped and blotted, a student holding a quill over a blank reply.\nscene [e5cdca] = 宿舍书桌上放着一封信，字迹挤成一团，还有墨渍，一个学生拿着羽毛笔，对着一张空白的回信。\n# en: Send them luck and a sketch\nopt.left.label [727f5e] = 寄去好运和一幅速写\n# en: You send a sketch of a goat in a mortarboard, labelled \"your examiner\", and a great deal of luck. Their reply comes a fortnight later: \"Passed. The goat helped. Insufferable of you.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [151e76] = 你寄去了一幅戴学位帽的山羊速写，标注“你的考官”，还有满满的好运。两个星期后TA回了信：“过了。山羊帮了忙。你真讨厌。”\n# en: Remind them of the last time\nopt.right.label [7967f5] = 提醒TA上一次的事\n# en: You write about the last time: the failed exam, the sold theatre, the retake, and how they passed it anyway with everything gone. \"You've done harder,\" you write. Their reply comes the day after the exam, one line: \"You remembered. Top marks. I cried in the refectory again.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [f3d4ef] = 你写了上一次的事：那场失利的考试，卖掉的剧团，补考，以及TA在一无所有的时候照样考过了。“你经历过更难的，”你写道。TA的回信在考试第二天到了，只有一行：“你记得。最高分。我又在膳堂里哭了。”\n# en: You write that they'll be fine, and that you're sure they've done harder things. Their reply is cheerful and vague. They passed, but they don't say much else, and you have the feeling you missed something.\nopt.right.say.2 [51ac9d] = 你写道TA会没事的，你相信TA经历过更难的事。TA的回信轻松而含糊。TA考过了，却没多说什么，你隐约觉得自己错过了什么。\n\n@event know_grimsby_nose\n# en: The Night Before the Essay\ntitle [c699b5] = 交论文的前一晚\n# en: Your essay is due tomorrow, and the one book you need isn't in the library. You reach Grimsby's as he's turning the sign to \"closed\". He lets you in, holds up the lamp to a wall of a thousand spines, and says, \"Well? What does it look like?\"\ntext [2502ec] = 你的论文明天就要交，而你需要的那本书图书馆里没有。你赶到格林斯比书店时，他正把牌子翻到“打烊”。他让你进来，举着灯照向一整面墙上千本书脊，说：“说吧？它长什么样？”\n# en: A village bookshop at closing time, a student at the counter out of breath, the bookseller holding a lamp up to a wall of shelves.\nscene [b7c026] = 打烊时分的村里书店，一个学生气喘吁吁地站在柜台前，书商把一盏灯举向一整面书架。\n# en: Describe the title and the author\nopt.left.label [b313c2] = 说出书名和作者\n# en: You tell him the title and the author. He brightens and goes off into the shelves, and comes back an hour later with a book about lighthouses, and then, at last, the right one, as the clock in the square strikes ten.\nopt.left.say.1 [051b19] = 你告诉他书名和作者。他眼睛一亮，钻进书架里，一个小时后先拿回来一本关于灯塔的书，然后，终于，拿来了对的那本，这时广场上的钟敲了十下。\n# en: Describe how it smells\nopt.right.label [6981b9] = 描述它的气味\n# en: \"Old paper and rain,\" you say, \"and something like cloves.\" Grimsby's face lights up. He closes his eyes, takes three steps, and pulls it off the shelf without looking. \"Nobody ever asks properly,\" he says, and won't take your money.\nopt.right.say.1 [747160] = “旧纸和雨水，”你说，“还有点像丁香。”格林斯比的脸一下子亮了。他闭上眼睛，走了三步，看都不看就从书架上抽出了那本书。“从没人好好地问过，”他说，不肯收你的钱。\n\n@event know_pell_counting\n# en: The Good Stock\ntitle [086c07] = 上等的存货\n# en: You need a strong remedy for a friend with a fever, and Pell has the good stock, in a dark bottle on the top shelf he sells only to people he trusts. Everyone else gets the shop's ordinary tincture. Pell looks at you over his spectacles and starts to measure out the ordinary one.\ntext [777216] = 你需要一剂强效药给一个发烧的朋友，佩尔有上等的存货，装在最高一层架子上的一只深色瓶子里，只卖给他信得过的人。其他人拿到的是店里普通的酊剂。佩尔从眼镜上方看了看你，开始量那普通的。\n# en: An apothecary's counter lined with brass scales and a wall of tiny drawers, an old man in spectacles measuring drops into a vial while a student watches.\nscene [65ae1f] = 药铺柜台上摆着一排黄铜秤，背后是一面墙的小抽屉，一位戴眼镜的老人正往小瓶里滴药，一个学生在旁边看着。\n# en: Go to the infirmary instead\nopt.left.label [9b6746] = 改去医务室\n# en: You thank him and take your friend to the infirmary instead, where Sister Amaranth's ordinary tonic works, slowly. Your friend is up in a week, and grateful, and you wonder whether the dark bottle would have done it in a day.\nopt.left.say.1 [aa2748] = 你谢过他，带朋友去了医务室，阿玛兰斯修女普通的补药慢慢起了作用。你的朋友一个星期后就起来了，很感激你，而你在想，那只深色瓶子会不会一天就能治好。\n# en: Count the drops aloud with him\nopt.right.label [28b940] = 和他一起大声数滴数\n# en: You count the drops aloud as he measures, quietly, one to nine. Pell stops at four and looks at you. Then he puts the ordinary bottle away and reaches for the top shelf. \"Measure twice,\" he says, counting with you. Your friend is up the next morning.\nopt.right.say.1 [29b854] = 他量的时候你小声数着，从一数到九。数到四时佩尔停了下来，看着你。然后他把普通的瓶子放回去，伸手去够最高一层。“量两遍，”他说，和你一起数着。第二天早上你的朋友就起来了。\n\n@event know_amaranth_truth\n# en: Patched Up\ntitle [6b956d] = 包扎\n# en: You come into the infirmary with an arm that's swelling fast, from something you'd rather not explain. Sister Amaranth turns it to the light and asks, as she always does, how it happened. Behind you there's a queue. Your story is ready: a fall on the stairs.\ntext [b31677] = 你走进医务室，一只胳膊肿得飞快，原因你不太想解释。阿玛兰斯修女把它转向光，像往常一样问你是怎么弄的。你身后排着队。你的说辞已经准备好了：在楼梯上摔了一跤。\n# en: An infirmary bench, a student holding a bruised arm, a healer in a grey apron turning it to the light from the window.\nscene [7f18a3] = 医务室的长凳上，一个学生捧着一只淤青的胳膊，一位系灰围裙的医师把它转向窗口的光。\n# en: Tell her about the stairs\nopt.left.label [507bd7] = 跟她说楼梯的事\n# en: You tell her about the stairs. She listens, and waits, and treats it, slowly, with a salve that stings. \"Come back in two days,\" she says. \"Bring the truth with you next time. It heals faster.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [77e374] = 你跟她说了楼梯的事。她听着，等着，然后慢慢地给你涂了一种刺痛的药膏。“两天后再来，”她说，“下次把实话带来。实话好得快。”\n# en: Tell her the true version, first\nopt.right.label [ddb70c] = 先告诉她真实的版本\n# en: You skip the stairs and tell her exactly what happened, embarrassing as it is. She nods once. \"Better,\" she says, and reaches for a bottle that isn't a salve. The swelling's gone before you reach the door.\nopt.right.say.1 [724bdb] = 你跳过楼梯，原原本本地告诉她发生了什么，尽管很丢脸。她点了一下头。“这才对，”她说，伸手拿了一瓶不是药膏的东西。你还没走到门口，肿就消了。\n\n@event know_marisol_spirits\n# en: The Storm Crossing\ntitle [9188db] = 风暴中渡湖\n# en: You're halfway across the lake in a borrowed boat when the storm comes in, fast and black. The waves are full of shapes like galloping horses: the water spirits, racing the boat and, by the look of it, enjoying themselves. The shore is a long way off.\ntext [46bb4f] = 你划着一条借来的船到了湖中央，风暴来了，又快又黑。浪里满是像奔马一样的影子：是水灵，在和小船赛跑，看样子玩得很开心。岸还远着呢。\n# en: A small boat on a choppy grey lake under a storm sky, a student at the oars, shapes like galloping horses moving in the waves around the boat.\nscene [83893a] = 暴风雨的天空下，一条小船在灰色起伏的湖面上，一个学生握着桨，船周围的浪里有奔马一样的影子在跑。\n# en: Row for it\nopt.left.label [6a8d31] = 拼命划\n# en: You row until your hands are raw, and the spirits race you all the way, and lose, just. You land on the shingle soaked to the skin, and lie there laughing at the sky.\nopt.left.say.1 [a658f9] = 你划到手都磨破了，水灵一路和你赛跑，最后以毫厘之差输了。你浑身湿透地爬上卵石滩，躺在那里对着天空大笑。\n# en: The spirits win. They tip the boat over, cheerfully, ten yards from shore, and you swim the rest in the cold. You arrive half drowned, and they gallop off, delighted with themselves.\nopt.left.say.2 [641100] = 水灵赢了。它们兴高采烈地在离岸十码的地方把船掀翻了，你在冷水里游完了剩下的路。你上岸时半死不活，它们得意洋洋地跑开了。\n# en: Ask them nicely not to cheat\nopt.right.label [c54aa7] = 客客气气地请它们别作弊\n# en: You ship the oars, lean over the side and ask the spirits, very politely, not to cheat. There's a pause. Then they gather under the boat like a raft and carry you to shore at a gallop. Marisol, on the steps with her book, laughs so hard she drops it.\nopt.right.say.1 [46a9c5] = 你收起桨，探出船舷，非常礼貌地请水灵们不要作弊。安静了一下。然后它们聚在船底，像一只木筏一样驮着你一路奔向岸边。拿着书坐在台阶上的玛丽索尔笑得书都掉了。\n\n@event know_vey_smell\n# en: The Feast That Wasn't\ntitle [cd1736] = 不存在的盛宴\n# en: Some third-years have laid on a feast at the end of the long table: roast fowl, pies, a cake with candles. The whole table is reaching for it. It's beautiful. It's a little too beautiful. Somewhere at the far end, the third-years are trying very hard not to laugh.\ntext [5026c1] = 几个三年级生在长桌尽头摆了一桌盛宴：烤禽、馅饼、插着蜡烛的蛋糕。整张桌子的人都在伸手。它很美。美得有点过头了。远处那几个三年级生正拼命忍着不笑。\n# en: A refectory table laden with a glorious feast, students reaching hungrily for it, one student pausing with a fork in mid-air and sniffing.\nscene [e999fc] = 膳堂的长桌上摆满了丰盛的宴席，学生们饥肠辘辘地伸手去拿，一个学生举着叉子停在半空，嗅了嗅。\n# en: Dig in with everyone\nopt.left.label [625d2f] = 和大家一起开吃\n# en: You reach for the pie with everyone else, and your fork goes through it and into the table, and the whole feast dissolves into air and a few crumbs of real bread. The third-years cheer. Everyone laughs, you included, eventually.\nopt.left.say.1 [71e4c1] = 你和大家一起伸手去拿馅饼，叉子穿过馅饼戳到了桌子上，整桌宴席化作空气和几粒真面包屑。三年级生们欢呼起来。大家都笑了，你最后也笑了。\n# en: Smell it first\nopt.right.label [c9ea73] = 先闻一闻\n# en: You lean in and sniff. Nothing: not roast, not pastry, not a crumb. \"It doesn't smell of anything,\" you say, loudly. The table stops. The third-years groan. Somewhere, you feel, Professor Vey would be proud, and would never say so.\nopt.right.say.1 [00f92e] = 你凑过去闻了闻。什么味道也没有：没有烤肉味，没有面点味，一点碎屑味都没有。“它什么味道都没有，”你大声说。整张桌子停了下来。三年级生们哀叹一声。你觉得，维伊教授要是在场会很骄傲，却绝不会说出口。\n\n@event know_brask_asking\n# en: Out of Your Depth\ntitle [706966] = 力不从心\n# en: Brask has handed you a lead rope and a griffin with opinions, and walked away to the fence. The griffin has decided it would like to be somewhere else, and it's bigger than you. Everyone else in the class seems to be managing. Brask is watching, arms folded, saying nothing.\ntext [c13d5d] = 布拉斯克把一根缰绳和一头很有主见的狮鹫交给你，然后走到了栅栏边。狮鹫打定主意想去别处，而且它比你大。班上其他人似乎都应付得来。布拉斯克抱着胳膊看着，一言不发。\n# en: A stable yard, a large and irritable griffin pulling against a rope held by a nervous student, a professor in a leather apron watching from the fence.\nscene [98467c] = 马厩的院子里，一头暴躁的大狮鹫拽着一个紧张的学生手里的缰绳，一位系皮围裙的教授在栅栏边看着。\n# en: Manage it yourself\nopt.left.label [624184] = 自己搞定\n# en: You dig your heels in and talk to it in a low voice, the way Brask talks to everything but students, and slowly the griffin comes round. Brask nods, once. From her that's a medal.\nopt.left.say.1 [b40eb7] = 你蹬住脚跟，用低沉的声音跟它说话，就像布拉斯克跟除了学生以外的一切说话那样，狮鹫慢慢平静了下来。布拉斯克点了一下头。从她那里得到这个，就是一枚勋章。\n# en: The griffin wins. It drags you halfway across the yard and into a water trough before Brask takes the rope from your hand with two fingers. \"Hm,\" she says. \"Again tomorrow.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [9d390a] = 狮鹫赢了。它把你拖过半个院子，拖进了水槽，布拉斯克才用两根手指从你手里接过缰绳。“嗯，”她说，“明天再来。”\n# en: Ask Brask for help\nopt.right.label [e6a413] = 请布拉斯克帮忙\n# en: \"I can't,\" you say. \"Show me.\" Brask comes over from the fence, takes your hands and puts them on the rope the right way, and the griffin goes quiet as a cat. \"Good,\" she says. \"Most never ask.\" You realise she's remembering it.\nopt.right.say.1 [b3a8df] = “我不行，”你说，“教教我。”布拉斯克从栅栏边走过来，把你的手按到缰绳上正确的位置，狮鹫一下子安静得像只猫。“很好，”她说，“大多数人从不开口。”你意识到，她会记住这一次。\n\n@event heard_bram_corvin\n# en: Bram on Corvin\ntitle [577591] = 布拉姆谈科尔文\n# en: Bram takes you aside, which he never does. \"You're friendly with Corvin,\" he says, not quite a question. \"Then you should know. He's on his last warning. One more detention and they send him home.\" He looks at the fountain, where Corvin is laughing at something. \"I shouldn't have told you. I'm telling you anyway.\"\ntext [a93314] = 布拉姆把你拉到一边，他从不这么做。“你和科尔文走得近，”他说，不太像问句，“那你该知道。他在最后警告期。再挨一次禁闭，他们就送他回家。”他看着喷泉边正为什么事笑着的科尔文。“我不该告诉你的。可我还是告诉你了。”\n# en: A courtyard at noon, a prefect in a red sash speaking low to a student, glancing across at another student lounging by the fountain.\nscene [8e80b7] = 正午的庭院，一位系红绶带的级长压低声音对一个学生说话，瞥向喷泉边懒洋洋靠着的另一个学生。\n# en: Thank Bram, and keep it quiet\nopt.left.label [8ef4ba] = 谢谢布拉姆，保守秘密\n# en: You thank him, and don't tell anyone. Over the next month you notice yourself steering Corvin away from trouble, without saying why. Bram notices too, and pretends not to.\nopt.left.say.1 [15981d] = 你谢过他，没告诉任何人。接下来的一个月，你发现自己总在不动声色地把科尔文从麻烦边上引开。布拉姆也注意到了，假装没看见。\n# en: Ask Bram why he cares\nopt.right.label [662703] = 问布拉姆为什么在乎\n# en: \"Why do you care?\" you ask. Bram goes red. \"Somebody has to,\" he says, and walks off. You don't tell Corvin what Bram said. You do sit with him at lunch more, and he notices, and makes a joke of it.\nopt.right.say.1 [429bbf] = “你为什么在乎？”你问。布拉姆脸红了。“总得有人在乎，”他说完就走了。你没告诉科尔文布拉姆说了什么。你倒是午饭时更常和他坐在一起了，他注意到了，还拿这事开玩笑。\n\n@event heard_corvin_bram\n# en: Corvin on Bram\ntitle [22e611] = 科尔文谈布拉姆\n# en: Corvin is shuffling his deck on the parapet. \"You know why Bram's like he is?\" he says. \"His father was head boy. Tells him every holiday he'll never amount to anything. I heard it myself, on the stairs, one parents' day.\" He deals a card and looks at it. \"Don't tell him I told you. He'd hate that more than anything I've ever done to him.\"\ntext [489be4] = 科尔文在矮墙上洗着牌。“你知道布拉姆为什么是那个样子吗？”他说，“他父亲当年是男生学生会主席。每个假期都告诉他，他不会有出息。我亲耳听到的，在楼梯上，有一年家长日。”他发了一张牌，看了看。“别告诉他是我说的。比起我对他做过的任何事，他会更恨这个。”\n# en: A gargoyle passage at night, a student shuffling cards on a parapet, telling a story to another in a low voice.\nscene [e88648] = 夜里的石像鬼通道，一个学生在矮墙上洗着牌，低声给另一个人讲一个故事。\n# en: Keep it to yourself\nopt.left.label [09de43] = 自己知道就好\n# en: You keep it to yourself. But the next time Bram gives you a demerit, you hear his father's voice under it, and you take it a little more gently than before. Bram notices, and doesn't know why.\nopt.left.say.1 [0d2cf8] = 你没告诉任何人。可下一次布拉姆给你记过时，你听见了藏在底下的他父亲的声音，你接受得比以前温和了一点。布拉姆注意到了，却不知道为什么。\n# en: Ask Corvin why he told you\nopt.right.label [996936] = 问科尔文为什么告诉你\n# en: \"Why tell me?\" you ask. Corvin shuffles. \"So someone's kind to him,\" he says, \"and it isn't me.\" Then he deals you a hand, and talks about something else very fast.\nopt.right.say.1 [9e0101] = “为什么告诉我？”你问。科尔文洗着牌。“好让有人对他好一点，”他说，“而那个人不是我。”然后他给你发了一手牌，飞快地聊起了别的事。\n\n@event heard_maud_thorne\n# en: Maud on Thorne\ntitle [361335] = 莫德谈索恩\n# en: Maud is cutting back the hedge. \"You know old Thorne?\" she says. \"Every dawn, dearie, he's up behind the observatory in that little walled garden of his, talking to his roses. Thinks nobody knows. I've known for thirty years.\" She snips. \"He's kinder to them than to any of you. Don't tell him I said.\"\ntext [f5c2b7] = 莫德在修剪树篱。“你认识老索恩吧？”她说，“每天黎明，亲爱的，他都在观星台后面那个围墙小花园里，跟他的玫瑰说话。以为没人知道。我知道三十年了。”她剪了一刀。“他对玫瑰比对你们谁都好。别告诉他是我说的。”\n# en: A cottage garden at dusk, an old woman pruning a hedge with shears, nodding toward the dark shape of an observatory on the hill.\nscene [7bbafe] = 黄昏时的小屋花园，一位老妇人拿着剪子修剪树篱，朝山上观星台黑黢黢的轮廓点了点头。\n# en: Promise not to tell\nopt.left.label [a46fa3] = 答应不说出去\n# en: You promise. You don't tell. But the next time Thorne is terrible to you in a lecture, you imagine him at dawn, talking to a rose, and it helps more than you'd have thought.\nopt.left.say.1 [eecd1a] = 你答应了。你没说出去。可下一次索恩在课上对你很凶时，你想象他在黎明跟一朵玫瑰说话，这比你想象的还管用。\n# en: Ask whether she'd show you\nopt.right.label [776870] = 问她能不能带你去看看\n# en: \"Show you? Certainly not,\" says Maud, delighted. She does anyway, from behind the hedge one dawn, both of you crouching. Thorne is pruning and murmuring. He looks up, just once, at the hedge. You both hold your breath.\nopt.right.say.1 [05220e] = “带你去看？当然不行，”莫德说，乐坏了。可她还是带你去了，某个黎明，你们俩一起蹲在树篱后面。索恩一边修剪一边低语。他抬起头，只有一次，看向那道树篱。你们俩都屏住了呼吸。\n\n@event heard_mabel_grimsby\n# en: Mabel on Grimsby\ntitle [b13f12] = 梅布尔谈格林斯比\n# en: \"Shut again,\" says Mabel, nodding at Grimsby's across the lane. \"You know where he goes, petal? Lighthouses. Walks out to look at one, any one, whenever the shop gets too much. Forty years I've watched him go.\" She pours your tea. \"He thinks it's a secret. Everyone in the village knows.\"\ntext [0bfe0d] = “又关门了，”梅布尔朝巷子对面的格林斯比书店点点头，“你知道他去哪儿吗，小可爱？灯塔。只要书店让他受不了，他就走出去看一座灯塔，随便哪一座。我看着他这么走了四十年。”她给你倒了茶。“他以为这是秘密。全村人都知道。”\n# en: A village tea shop, a woman behind the counter wiping cups, nodding through the window at a bookshop across the lane with its sign turned to closed.\nscene [f6be2b] = 村里的茶馆，一个女人在柜台后擦着杯子，隔着窗户朝巷子对面一家挂着打烊牌子的书店点了点头。\n# en: Keep his secret, such as it is\nopt.left.label [88b9ea] = 替他保守这个秘密\n# en: You keep it, such as it is. Next time you're in the shop, you notice the little brass lighthouse on his counter for the first time, and the picture of one above the till, and you don't say anything.\nopt.left.say.1 [6dda65] = 你替他守着这个秘密，如果它还算秘密的话。下次去书店时，你第一次注意到他柜台上那座小小的黄铜灯塔，还有收银台上方的一幅灯塔画，你什么也没说。\n# en: Ask which lighthouse he likes best\nopt.right.label [7a98fe] = 问他最喜欢哪座灯塔\n# en: \"The one at the point,\" says Mabel at once. \"It's the ugliest. He says it tries the hardest.\" She thinks about it. \"That's the nicest thing anyone's ever said about anything round here, and he said it about a lighthouse.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [230c8e] = “岬角上那座，”梅布尔立刻说，“最丑的那座。他说它最努力。”她想了想。“这是这一带有人对任何东西说过的最好听的话，而他是对一座灯塔说的。”\n\n@event heard_marisol_vey\n# en: Marisol on Vey\ntitle [020979] = 玛丽索尔谈维伊\n# en: Marisol closes her book of poems. \"Has Vey shown you the mirror trick yet?\" she asks. \"She won't, in public. Once, years ago, her reflection didn't come back for a week. Walked around the castle as her. Taught two of her classes.\" She looks at the rain. \"Vey still won't talk about it. I think she was frightened. I think she still is.\"\ntext [8403ea] = 玛丽索尔合上了她的诗集。“维伊给你看过镜子把戏了吗？”她问，“她不会当众做的。很多年前有一次，她的倒影一个星期都没回来。顶着她的样子在城堡里走来走去。替她上了两节课。”她望着雨。“维伊到现在都不肯谈这件事。我想她是被吓到了。我想她现在还怕。”\n# en: A rainy lakeshore, a professor reading under a dry patch of air on the steps, talking quietly to a student beside her.\nscene [658626] = 下雨的湖岸，一位教授坐在台阶上一块干燥的空气下读书，低声对身边的学生说话。\n# en: Promise never to mention it to Vey\nopt.left.label [0901d9] = 答应永远不对维伊提起\n# en: You promise. You never mention it. But the next time Vey passes the long mirror on the stairs, you see her glance at it, just once, and look away quickly, and you understand.\nopt.left.say.1 [8b182a] = 你答应了。你从没提起过。可下一次维伊经过楼梯上那面长镜时，你看见她朝镜子瞥了一眼，就一眼，又飞快地移开了目光，你明白了。\n# en: Ask who noticed it wasn't her\nopt.right.label [2a9b3c] = 问是谁发现那不是她\n# en: \"Nobody,\" says Marisol. \"That's what frightened her. Nobody noticed for a week.\" She opens her book again. \"The poem I can't remember the end of is about that. I've just realised.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [c13193] = “没人发现，”玛丽索尔说，“这才是吓到她的地方。一个星期都没人发现。”她又翻开了书。“我总记不起结尾的那首诗，写的就是这件事。我刚刚才意识到。”\n\n@event heard_nessa_isolde\n# en: Nessa on the Headmistress\ntitle [7697af] = 内莎谈院长\n# en: Nessa taps a sketch on her wall: the headmistress's tower, one window circled. \"Tower chess,\" she says. \"Every evening. She plays both sides, and when she loses to herself she laughs out loud. I've heard it from the courtyard.\" She lowers her voice. \"That's my way in, one day. Nobody interviews a woman who's winning.\"\ntext [b177f5] = 内莎敲了敲墙上的一张速写：院长塔，一扇窗户被圈了出来。“塔棋，”她说，“每天晚上。她自己跟自己下，输给自己时会大笑出声。我在庭院里都听见过。”她压低声音。“那就是我的突破口，总有一天。没人能采访一个正在赢棋的女人。”\n# en: A newspaper office, an editor pointing at a sketch of a tower window pinned to a cluttered wall, a student leaning in to look.\nscene [6f4e93] = 报社办公室里，一位编辑指着钉在杂乱墙上的一张塔窗速写，一个学生凑过去看。\n# en: Wish her luck with the interview\nopt.left.label [3ad1a6] = 祝她采访顺利\n# en: You wish her luck. She says she doesn't need luck, she needs a chessboard, and spends the evening learning tower chess from a library book with a great deal of muttering. She's terrible at it.\nopt.left.say.1 [857f68] = 你祝她好运。她说她不需要运气，她需要一副棋盘，然后花了一个晚上，嘟嘟囔囔地对着图书馆的一本书学塔棋。她下得糟透了。\n# en: Ask her not to use it as a trick\nopt.right.label [87a971] = 请她别拿这个当把戏\n# en: \"Don't make it a trick,\" you say. Nessa looks at you for a long moment. \"It isn't a trick,\" she says. \"It's a door.\" But she takes the sketch down off the wall, and puts it in a drawer.\nopt.right.say.1 [737eab] = “别把它当成把戏，”你说。内莎看了你好一会儿。“这不是把戏，”她说，“这是一扇门。”可她还是把那张速写从墙上取下来，放进了抽屉。\n\n@event heard_tobias_mir\n# en: Tobias on Mir\ntitle [86c9da] = 托拜厄斯谈米尔\n# en: \"Mir's family,\" Tobias says, repotting, \"run a clockworks. Big one. And they think all of it, the inventing, is a hobby.\" He presses the soil down very gently. \"Mir told me that like it was nothing. It wasn't nothing. On the farm we'd say a calf that isn't let run never learns its legs.\"\ntext [859f7b] = “米尔家，”托拜厄斯一边换盆一边说，“开着一间钟表工坊。很大的一间。他们觉得那些东西，那些发明，全都只是爱好。”他非常轻地压实泥土。“米尔跟我说的时候，就像那不算什么。那可不是不算什么。在农场上我们说，不让跑的小牛犊永远学不会用腿。”\n# en: A greenhouse in the afternoon, a broad farm boy repotting seedlings and talking over his shoulder to a student holding a watering can.\nscene [24d787] = 午后的温室里，一个壮实的农家男孩一边给秧苗换盆，一边回头对一个拿着水壶的学生说话。\n# en: Say you'll keep it between you\nopt.left.label [a94b5d] = 说这话就你们俩知道\n# en: \"Between us,\" you say. Tobias nods, relieved. Later you notice that every time someone calls one of Mir's machines \"a nice little toy\", Tobias, very politely, changes the subject.\nopt.left.say.1 [59db45] = “就我们俩知道，”你说。托拜厄斯松了口气，点点头。后来你注意到，每当有人管米尔的机器叫“不错的小玩具”，托拜厄斯都会非常客气地岔开话题。\n# en: Say Mir should hear that saying\nopt.right.label [e5e097] = 说米尔该听听这句老话\n# en: \"Tell Mir that,\" you say. Tobias goes pink. He does, a week later, at supper, and gets it slightly wrong, and Mir laughs, and then goes quiet, and then says it back to him, correctly, as if writing it down.\nopt.right.say.1 [89d853] = “把这句话告诉米尔，”你说。托拜厄斯脸红了。一个星期后，他在晚饭时说了，说得有点不对，米尔笑了，然后安静下来，然后把这句话一字不差地说给他听，就像在把它记下来。\n\n@event heard_grimsby_mabel\n# en: Grimsby on Mabel\ntitle [e5cb12] = 格林斯比谈梅布尔\n# en: Grimsby nods across the lane at Mabel's. \"Those cups behind her counter,\" he says. \"Every one belongs to a regular. Living or dead. She never takes one down.\" He turns a page without reading it. \"Mine's the blue one, third from the left. She hung it my first winter in the village, when I knew nobody. I've never told her I know why.\"\ntext [f20660] = 格林斯比朝巷子对面的梅布尔茶馆点点头。“她柜台后面那些杯子，”他说，“每一只都属于一位常客。活着的，还是去世的。她从不取下任何一只。”他翻过一页，并没在读。“我的是蓝色那只，左数第三个。她是在我刚来村里的第一个冬天挂上的，那时我谁也不认识。我从没告诉过她，我知道为什么。”\n# en: A bookshop window, an old bookseller pointing across the lane at a tea shop with cups hanging on hooks behind its counter.\nscene [db4641] = 书店的橱窗前，一位老书商指着巷子对面一家茶馆，茶馆柜台后面的挂钩上挂着一排杯子。\n# en: Keep it between you and him\nopt.left.label [6096ed] = 这事就你们俩知道\n# en: You keep it. The next time you're at Mabel's, you look for the blue cup, third from the left, and find it, and say nothing. Mabel sees you looking, and smiles, and pours your tea.\nopt.left.say.1 [674eb1] = 你守住了这件事。下次去梅布尔那里，你寻找那只蓝杯子，左数第三个，找到了，什么也没说。梅布尔看见你在看，笑了笑，给你倒了茶。\n# en: Ask whether he'll tell her\nopt.right.label [884e69] = 问他会不会告诉她\n# en: \"Will you tell her?\" you ask. Grimsby considers it, then shakes his head. \"She knows I know,\" he says. \"That's the whole arrangement.\" He sells you a book about lighthouses you didn't ask for. You buy it.\nopt.right.say.1 [69fdcb] = “你会告诉她吗？”你问。格林斯比想了想，摇了摇头。“她知道我知道，”他说，“这就是全部的默契。”他卖给你一本你没要的关于灯塔的书。你买了。\n\n@event sell_a_secret\n# en: What's It Worth?\ntitle [cebc57] = 值多少钱？\n# en: The man who prints the gossip sheets in the village stops you in the lane. He's heard you're close to {x}, he says, and that you know something about {x.them} nobody else does. \"Whatever it is,\" he says, weighing a purse, \"from someone like you, it would sell out by noon. Your name on it, if you like. Or not.\"\ntext [78d642] = 村里印八卦小报的那个人在巷子里拦住你。他说他听说你和{x}很要好，还知道一件关于{x.them}的、别人都不知道的事。“不管是什么，”他掂着一只钱袋说，“从你这样的人嘴里说出来，不到中午就能卖光。愿意的话，署你的名。不愿意也行。”\n# en: A village lane, a sly man in a printer's apron leaning in a doorway, holding up a purse, a student stopped in front of him.\nscene [ffc4ee] = 村里的一条小巷，一个系着印刷工围裙、神情狡黠的男人倚在门口，举着一只钱袋，一个学生在他面前停下脚步。\n# en: Sell it\nopt.left.label [4ac442] = 卖了\n# en: You tell him. The sheet sells out by noon. {x} comes to breakfast, reads it, puts down {x.their} fork, and looks across the hall at you, and doesn't say anything at all. That's the worst part.\nopt.left.say.1 [75c754] = 你告诉了他。小报不到中午就卖光了。{x}来吃早饭，读了它，放下{x.their}叉子，隔着大厅看着你，一句话也没说。这才是最难受的。\n# en: Say you know nothing\nopt.right.label [b227c3] = 说你什么都不知道\n# en: \"Never heard of it,\" you say, and walk on. The man shrugs and pockets his purse. That evening {x} sits down beside you at supper for no reason {x.they} could name, and stays longer than usual.\nopt.right.say.1 [89630a] = “没听说过，”你说完就走了。那人耸耸肩，收起了钱袋。那天晚上，{x}在晚饭时坐到你身边，{x.they}自己也说不出为什么，而且比平时待得久。\n\n@event scandal_secret_out\n# en: Who Sold It\ntitle [d89340] = 是谁卖的\n# en: It's out: everyone knows who sold the gossip sheet the story about {npc}. The printer told the Newt, and the Newt told the castle. People who never cared for {npc} are disgusted with you anyway: nobody likes someone who sells a friend. {npc} hasn't said a word. Everyone is waiting to see what you'll do.\ntext [17c57f] = 事情败露了：所有人都知道是谁把{npc}的事卖给了八卦小报。印刷工告诉了蝾螈酒馆，蝾螈酒馆告诉了城堡。那些从不在乎{npc}的人照样厌恶你：没人喜欢出卖朋友的人。{npc}一个字都没说。大家都在等你怎么做。\n# en: A refectory at breakfast, a gossip sheet passed hand to hand, a whole table turning to look at one student, another student sitting very straight at the next table.\nscene [26df37] = 早餐时的膳堂，一份八卦小报在人们手里传着，一整张桌子的人转头看向一个学生，另一张桌子边一个学生坐得笔直。\n# en: Apologise to {npc}, in front of everyone\nopt.own.label [da654c] = 当着所有人的面向{npc}道歉\n# en: You stand up at breakfast, cross the hall and apologise to {npc}, out loud, with everyone watching. {They} look{s} at you for a long time, and then nod{s}, once. It doesn't mend it. It's a start.\nopt.own.say.1 [f17247] = 早饭时你站起来，穿过大厅，当着所有人的面大声向{npc}道歉。{they}看了你很久，然后点了一下头。这并没有弥补什么。但这是个开始。\n# en: Say the printer is lying\nopt.deny.label [e40b0c] = 说印刷工在撒谎\n# en: You say the printer is a liar who'd sell his own mother, which is true. People decide to believe you, more or less. {npc} doesn't, and says nothing, and doesn't need to.\nopt.deny.say.1 [6d356b] = 你说那个印刷工是个连亲娘都肯卖的骗子，这倒是真的。人们大体上决定相信你。{npc}不信，却什么也没说，也用不着说。\n# en: You say the printer is lying, and the printer produces the receipt with your initial on it. The castle has its verdict. {npc} walks past you in the corridor as if you were a draught.\nopt.deny.say.2 [3f2a1f] = 你说印刷工在撒谎，印刷工拿出了收据，上面有你名字的首字母。城堡有了定论。{npc}在走廊里从你身边走过，就像你是一阵穿堂风。\n# en: Say it was true, and someone had to\nopt.spin.label [8e107d] = 说那是真的，总得有人说\n# en: You say, coolly, that it was true, and someone had to say it. Some people agree with you. {npc} hears it. {They} stop{s} coming to meals for a week, and the castle forgets who started it, and you never will.\nopt.spin.say.1 [e1bce9] = 你冷冷地说，那是真的，总得有人说出来。有些人同意你。{npc}听到了。{they}一个星期没来吃饭，城堡忘了是谁起的头，而你永远不会忘。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/last_day.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/last_day.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event last_morning\n# en: The Last Morning\ntitle [1e4ad4] = 最后的早晨\n# en: You wake before the bell, out of habit, and there's no bell. Your trunk is packed and strapped. The robes hang on the wardrobe door. It's Starfall, the last day of the year, and tomorrow you graduate. The castle is very quiet, as if it's listening.\ntext [c12f0c] = 你习惯性地在钟声前醒来，可已经没有钟声了。你的箱子收拾好了，捆紧了。礼袍挂在衣柜门上。今天是星坠节，一年的最后一天，明天你就毕业了。城堡非常安静，像是在倾听。\n# en: A dormitory room in early light, trunks packed and strapped, graduation robes hanging on the wardrobe door, the towers gold through the window.\nscene [b7f47f] = 清晨微光中的宿舍，箱子已经收拾好捆紧，毕业礼袍挂在衣柜门上，窗外的塔楼一片金黄。\n# en: Get up and begin the last day\nopt.ok.label [72f307] = 起床，开始最后一天\n# en: {has(origin_noble): You dress with care, the way you were raised to.|{has(origin_street): You check your pockets out of habit, then laugh at yourself.|You dress slowly, wanting the morning to last.}} The staircase that moves waits for you at the top, for once, as if it knows.\nopt.ok.say.1 [c634ce] = {has(origin_noble): 你按从小被教导的那样，仔细地穿戴整齐。|{has(origin_street): 你习惯性地摸了摸口袋，然后笑自己。|你慢慢地穿衣服，想让这个早晨长一点。}} 那道会移动的楼梯难得一次在顶上等着你，好像它知道。\n\n@event last_results\n# en: The Final Lists\ntitle [8ae58d] = 最终名单\n# en: The final lists are pinned to the great hall door, and the whole year is crowded in front of them. {gpa >= 3.5: Your name is near the top, and someone has drawn a small star beside it.|{gpa >= 2.5: Your name sits in the middle of the list, solid and unremarkable, exactly where you hoped.|{gpa >= 2.0: Your name is near the bottom, but it's there.|Your name is on the list, just. Someone has pencilled \"phew\" next to it, possibly you.}}} {flag(thesis_distinction): In the thesis column, one word in gold ink, Distinction.|{flag(thesis_passed): In the thesis column, a plain pass.|{flag(thesis_resubmit): In the thesis column, in red, To be resubmitted.|}}}\ntext [fc0c76] = 最终名单钉在大礼堂的门上，全年级的人都挤在前面。{gpa >= 3.5: 你的名字靠近最上面，有人在旁边画了一颗小星星。|{gpa >= 2.5: 你的名字在名单中间，稳稳当当，毫不起眼，正是你希望的位置。|{gpa >= 2.0: 你的名字靠近末尾，但它在上面。|你的名字在名单上，勉强。有人在旁边用铅笔写了“呼”，可能是你自己。}}} {flag(thesis_distinction): 论文一栏里，一个金色墨水写的词，优等。|{flag(thesis_passed): 论文一栏里，平平常常的通过。|{flag(thesis_resubmit): 论文一栏里，红字写着，需重交。|}}}\n# en: A crowd of final-year students around a great oak door in the entrance hall, long lists of names pinned to it, one student reaching up to touch a line.\nscene [888bd9] = 门厅里一群毕业生围着一扇巨大的橡木门，门上钉着长长的名单，一个学生伸手去摸其中一行。\n# en: Touch your name, for luck\nopt.ok.label [85aada] = 摸一摸你的名字，讨个好运\n# en: {gpa >= 3.0: People you barely know shake your hand on the way out. You let them.|{gpa >= 2.0: You run a finger along the line. Four years, one line. It's enough.|You stand there a while after the crowd has gone. It's on the list. It counts.}}\nopt.ok.say.1 [a8c0d4] = {gpa >= 3.0: 出去的路上，几乎不认识的人都来和你握手。你由着他们。|{gpa >= 2.0: 你用手指划过那一行。四年，一行字。够了。|人群散去后，你在那儿站了一会儿。名字在名单上。这就算数。}}\n\n@event last_career\n# en: What Comes Next\ntitle [4fbe37] = 接下来\n# en: {has_offer(): Every letter you've had this year is spread across the lid of your trunk. Tomorrow you graduate, and today each of them wants its answer. You can sign one. You can, if you're brave or foolish, sign none.|No letter, no start date. Everyone else seems to know where they're going, or says so. The registrar's list is still pinned by her window, but you didn't put your name to it.}\ntext [5c5668] = {has_offer(): 你这一年收到的每一封信都摊在箱盖上。明天你就毕业了，今天它们每一封都要你答复。你可以签一份。如果你够勇敢或者够傻，也可以一份都不签。|没有信，没有入职日期。别人似乎都知道自己要去哪儿，或者嘴上这么说。教务员的清单还钉在她的窗边，但你没在上面写下名字。}\n# en: Letters with heavy seals spread across the lid of a packed trunk, a student sitting cross-legged among them with a pen.\nscene [873f9e] = 几封封蜡厚重的信摊在打包好的箱盖上，一个学生盘腿坐在信中间，手里拿着笔。\n# en: Sign with the Conclave\nopt.conclave.label [6e5f2a] = 与七塔议会签约\n# en: An apprentice at the Seven Towers.\nopt.conclave.hint [87cf0c] = 七塔的一名学徒。\n# en: You sign the Conclave's contract with the silver-sealed pen they sent. In the autumn you climb the Seven Towers in grey, an apprentice with seven years of wards ahead of you.\nopt.conclave.say.1 [087327] = 你用他们寄来的银封笔签下七塔议会的合同。秋天，你会穿着灰袍登上七塔，前面是七年的护界功课。\n# en: Sign with the Royal Library\nopt.royal_library.label [479883] = 与皇家图书馆签约\n# en: A cataloguer in the old archives.\nopt.royal_library.hint [8c80bd] = 老档案馆里的编目员。\n# en: You sign for the cold room at the Royal Library Archives and the folios that object to being opened. You'll need gloves. You've bought two pairs already.\nopt.royal_library.say.1 [33a055] = 你签下了皇家图书馆档案馆的那间冷房间，和那些不愿被翻开的对开本。你会需要手套。你已经买了两副。\n# en: Take the Spire fellowship\nopt.fellowship.label [acdb67] = 接下尖塔的研究员职位\n# en: A junior fellow among books that bite.\nopt.fellowship.hint [e854c7] = 会咬人的书中间的初级研究员。\n# en: You chalk your name properly over the carrel at the Spire Library. A junior fellow, from the autumn: a life among books that bite, and secrets that wait.\nopt.fellowship.say.1 [ddad84] = 你在尖塔图书馆的隔间上方，正式用粉笔写下自己的名字。从秋天起你就是初级研究员：一生与会咬人的书和静静等待的秘密为伴。\n# en: Sign with the Guild's works\nopt.guild.label [ca66c0] = 与公会工坊签约\n# en: A journeyman's bench, two explosions a month.\nopt.guild.hint [08cd47] = 熟练工的工作台，每月炸两回。\n# en: You sign for the journeyman's bench at the Guild of Alchemists' works, and order the protective clothing on the same day.\nopt.guild.say.1 [222898] = 你签下了炼金师公会工坊的熟练工工作台，当天就订好了防护服。\n# en: Sign the harbour lease\nopt.shop.label [2fd82e] = 签下港口的租约\n# en: A crooked shop and a still of your own.\nopt.shop.hint [277347] = 一间歪铺子，一口自己的蒸馏器。\n# en: You sign the lease on the crooked little shop by the harbour and hang the brass key back round your neck. In the autumn, the sign will read your name.\nopt.shop.say.1 [fd1d2b] = 你签下港口边那间歪歪扭扭的小铺子的租约，把那把铜钥匙重新挂回脖子上。秋天，招牌上会写着你的名字。\n# en: Answer the Office of Unseen Affairs\nopt.unseen.label [2d4e0d] = 答复隐秘事务署\n# en: A desk at an address that doesn't exist.\nopt.unseen.hint [366d0b] = 一张设在不存在地址的书桌。\n# en: You write one word on a slip of paper and leave it under your pillow. In the morning it's gone. In the autumn, a desk at the Office of Unseen Affairs will be waiting.\nopt.unseen.say.1 [7751c4] = 你在一张纸条上写下一个词，压在枕头底下。早上它不见了。秋天，隐秘事务署会有一张书桌等着你。\n# en: Take the shipping house's desk\nopt.trading.label [44c85c] = 接下航运商行的书桌\n# en: A partner's share of a harbour ledger.\nopt.trading.hint [8cc31d] = 港口一本账簿里合伙人的份额。\n# en: You sign for the old partner's desk and his share of the ledger. The shipping house's anchor is pressed into the wax beside your name.\nopt.trading.say.1 [b305ed] = 你签下了老合伙人的书桌和他在账簿里的份额。航运商行的船锚印在你名字旁边的封蜡里。\n# en: Stay at Lumenhold to teach\nopt.teaching.label [0f1b87] = 留在流明堡教书\n# en: The faculty barge, on the wrong side of the lectern.\nopt.teaching.hint [9dbc90] = 教职员的渡船，讲台的另一边。\n# en: You sign the faculty's contract in the staff room, at a desk with somebody else's initials carved in it. In the autumn you'll be on the faculty barge.\nopt.teaching.say.1 [ef32c7] = 你在教员休息室签下教职合同，桌上刻着别人名字的缩写。秋天，你会坐上教职员的渡船。\n# en: Join the Circus of Wonders\nopt.circus.label [9011fa] = 加入奇迹马戏团\n# en: Wagons, crowds and illusions.\nopt.circus.hint [04818f] = 篷车、人群和幻术。\n# en: You sign the ringmaster's contract. In a week the circus wagons pass the village, and they'll slow down for you, and this time they'll stop.\nopt.circus.say.1 [84f0e1] = 你签下马戏团团长的合同。一周后，马戏团的篷车会经过村子，它们会为你放慢，而这一次，会停下。\n# en: Go back to the plateau\nopt.observatory.label [8dfc6a] = 回到高原去\n# en: Salaried chartmaker for the Observatory Expedition.\nopt.observatory.hint [e6ff79] = 观星远征的受薪绘图员。\n# en: You sign for a salaried place with the Observatory Expedition and pack the frostbite remedies first. The plateau, and its nights, are yours from the autumn.\nopt.observatory.say.1 [7fb370] = 你签下观星远征的受薪职位，第一件打包的就是冻疮药。从秋天起，高原和它的夜晚都是你的。\n# en: Take the keeper's cottage\nopt.field_station.label [a8d7b6] = 接下看守小屋\n# en: The Beastcraft Field Station, on the moors.\nopt.field_station.hint [54337f] = 荒原上的驭兽术野外站。\n# en: You sign for the keeper's post at the Beastcraft Field Station: a cottage, a lamp and more creatures than anyone could count.\nopt.field_station.say.1 [ae945c] = 你签下驭兽术野外站的看守职位：一间小屋、一盏灯，还有谁也数不清的生灵。\n# en: Cross the hills to Thornwick\nopt.thornwick.label [5bb0a2] = 翻山去荆木学院\n# en: A researcher's bench at the rival academy.\nopt.thornwick.hint [539ab0] = 对手学院里一张研究员工作台。\n# en: You sign Thornwick's contract in green ink. In the autumn you'll cross the hills, and Lumenhold will say something cutting, and you'll go anyway.\nopt.thornwick.say.1 [f9ecd9] = 你用绿墨水签下荆木学院的合同。秋天你会翻过山去，流明堡会说些尖刻话，而你还是会去。\n# en: Stay on in the Spire's stacks\nopt.spire.label [f58a33] = 留在尖塔的书库\n# en: A keeper of the stacks, full time.\nopt.spire.hint [065fd9] = 全职的书库看守人。\n# en: You leave your signature under the bell on the reference desk. In the autumn you'll be back behind it, with a longer ladder.\nopt.spire.say.1 [d56103] = 你把签名压在咨询台的铃铛底下。秋天，你会回到那张台子后面，带着一架更长的梯子。\n# en: Stay on at the Gilded Newt\nopt.newt.label [e8b560] = 留在镀金蝾螈酒馆\n# en: Behind the bar, then in charge of it.\nopt.newt.hint [270c77] = 先站吧台，再管整个店。\n# en: You shake on it over the bar with the landlady, properly this time. Full time from the autumn, and in charge in a year or two.\nopt.newt.say.1 [c656ef] = 你隔着吧台和老板娘握了手，这次是正式的。秋天起全职，过一两年就由你管。\n# en: Stay on at Pell & Daughters\nopt.pell.label [818d9e] = 留在佩尔父女药铺\n# en: The counter, and one day the drawers.\nopt.pell.hint [9e0e1c] = 柜台，以及将来的那些抽屉。\n# en: You sign Pell's ledger under his daughters' names. Old Pell stops counting long enough to watch you do it, then starts again from one.\nopt.pell.say.1 [1773aa] = 你在佩尔的账簿上签名，签在他女儿们的名字下面。老佩尔停下数数，看你签完，又从一开始数。\n# en: Keep your courier's satchel\nopt.courier.label [1311d0] = 留着你的信使挎包\n# en: Flying the lake in all weathers.\nopt.courier.hint [b8a70d] = 风雨无阻地飞越湖面。\n# en: You hang the satchel with your name on it back on its peg, where it will be in the autumn, and the autumn after that.\nopt.courier.say.1 [9ab9b1] = 你把绣着自己名字的挎包挂回挂钩上。秋天它会在那里，再下一个秋天也是。\n# en: Take the registrar's clerk's desk\nopt.clerk.label [64cce4] = 接下教务处的书记员桌\n# en: Stamps, ledgers and a window on a wall.\nopt.clerk.hint [467957] = 印章、账簿，和一扇对墙的窗。\n# en: You sign for the clerk's desk at the registrar's window. It pays the rent. It is a start date. Today that is enough.\nopt.clerk.say.1 [19fc4e] = 你在教务员的窗口签下书记员的桌子。它付得起房租。它是一个入职日期。今天，这就够了。\n# en: {has_offer(): Sign none of them, and keep looking|Keep looking}\nopt.look.label [50ef1e] = {has_offer(): 一份都不签，继续找|继续找}\n# en: {gpa >= 2.5 or renown >= 30: Your grades and your name will open doors, in time.|Without a post, it's home for a while.}\nopt.look.hint [96ac02] = {gpa >= 2.5 or renown >= 30: 你的成绩和名声，迟早会打开门。|没有职位，先回家待一阵。}\n# en: {gpa >= 2.5 or renown >= 30: You put the pen down. Your grades are good and people know your name; the world can make you a better offer in its own time. You'll rent a room and write letters.|You put the pen down, and the letter you'll write home is the hardest you've ever had to write. There's a room there, and a bed that's too short, and time.}\nopt.look.say.1 [8d6180] = {gpa >= 2.5 or renown >= 30: 你放下笔。你成绩不错，也有人知道你的名字；这个世界可以在它自己的时候给你开个更好的价。你会租个房间，写信。|你放下笔，接下来要写回家的那封信，是你写过最难的一封。那里有一个房间，一张太短的床，还有时间。}\n\n@event last_lake\n# en: The Lake, One Last Time\ntitle [3fc408] = 最后一次看湖\n# en: You walk down to the lake before breakfast, the way you did your first morning. {flag(hollow_sealed): It's clearer than you've ever seen it, clear all the way down, and nothing looks back. You did that. Almost nobody knows.|{flag(hollow_freed): It has been different since the spring it froze, warmer somehow, and the candles along the shore lean toward you as you pass. You did that too.|{flag(hollow_bargain): The water is still. The mark on your palm is not. It hums as you come near, like something greeting something.|{flag(hollow_fled): The water looks ordinary. It isn't. Somewhere under it a door stands ajar, because you ran, and the headmistress's hair has been white since that night.|{flag(hollow_held_line): The first-years you held the tower for are skimming stones off the jetty. One of them waves at you. Whatever happened under the ice that night, it happened without you, and they are alive.|{flag(hollow_failed): It's calm this morning. You know what lies beneath it, and who paid to keep it closed. You stand on the jetty a long time.|{clues(hollowstar) >= 1: There were stories about this lake, and you followed some of them a little way. You never learned what they meant, or whether they meant anything. The lake isn't telling.|It's flat and blue and ordinary, and the towers stand upside down in it. Four years, and it never once did anything strange. That you saw.}}}}}}}\ntext [3ea458] = 你像第一个早晨那样，早饭前走到湖边。{flag(hollow_sealed): 湖水清得前所未见，一直清到底，什么也没有回望你。这是你做到的。几乎没人知道。|{flag(hollow_freed): 自从它结冰的那个春天，湖就不一样了，像是暖了些；你走过时，岸边的蜡烛都朝你倾斜。这也是你做到的。|{flag(hollow_bargain): 湖水静止。你掌心的印记却没有。你一走近它就嗡嗡作响，像是某种东西在问候另一种东西。|{flag(hollow_fled): 湖水看起来很平常。它不是。在水下某处，一扇门虚掩着，因为你逃了；院长的头发从那一夜起就白了。|{flag(hollow_held_line): 你那一夜守护过的一年级生正在栈桥上打水漂。有一个朝你挥手。那夜冰下发生了什么，都与你无关地发生了，而他们都活着。|{flag(hollow_failed): 今早湖面很平静。你知道它底下是什么，也知道是谁付了代价让它关着。你在栈桥上站了很久。|{clues(hollowstar) >= 1: 关于这片湖有些传说，你追着其中几个走了一小段。你从没弄明白它们的意思，或者它们到底有没有意思。湖什么也不说。|湖面平坦湛蓝，平平常常，塔楼倒映在里面。四年了，它从没做过一件怪事。至少你没看见。}}}}}}}\n# en: A student standing alone at the end of a wooden jetty at dawn, the Mirror Lake perfectly still, the towers reflected upside down in it.\nscene [0a0e22] = 黎明时分，一个学生独自站在木码头的尽头，镜湖一片静止，塔楼倒映在水中。\n# en: Say goodbye to the lake\nopt.ok.label [822a12] = 向湖道别\n# en: {~You skim one stone. It skips seven times, which is your record, and nobody saw.|You crouch and put your hand in the water. It's cold, the way it always was.} Then you walk back up to the castle without looking round.\nopt.ok.say.1 [3cf7b9] = {~你打了一个水漂。跳了七下，是你的纪录，没人看见。|你蹲下来把手伸进水里。很凉，和以前一样。} 然后你走回城堡，没有回头。\n\n@event last_friends\n# en: The Last Ordinary Morning\ntitle [090df6] = 最后一个寻常的早晨\n# en: {friend} finds you in the courtyard with two mugs of tea and no particular reason. \"Last ordinary morning,\" {they} say{s}. \"I thought it should be spent properly.\" {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3: The others drift over one by one, the friends who stayed, until the fountain's rim is full.|You sit on the rim of the fountain and don't talk about tomorrow.}\ntext [e8e8ac] = {friend}端着两杯茶在庭院里找到你，没什么特别的理由。“最后一个寻常的早晨，”{they}说，“我觉得应该好好过。”{bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3: 其他人一个接一个地晃过来，那些一直留在你身边的朋友，直到喷泉边坐满了人。|你们坐在喷泉边上，不谈明天。}\n# en: Two students sitting on the rim of a courtyard fountain with mugs of tea, packed trunks stacked by the doors behind them.\nscene [d79b2e] = 两个学生端着茶杯坐在庭院的喷泉边上，身后的门边堆着收拾好的箱子。\n# en: Promise to visit, and mean it\nopt.left.label [c31f13] = 约好去看对方，说到做到\n# en: {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3: The yearbook will remember the ones who stayed.|A date, a place and a time.}\nopt.left.hint [46ded0] = {bonds(friend) + bonds(close) >= 3: 毕业纪念册会记住一直留下的人。|一个日子、一个地点、一个时间。}\n# en: You set a date, a real one, with a place and a time, and write it in every notebook round the fountain. {friend} underlines it twice. You all know which of you will be late.\nopt.left.say.1 [29eb37] = 你们定了一个日子，真的日子，有地点有时间，写进喷泉边每个人的笔记本里。{friend}在下面画了两道线。你们都知道谁会迟到。\n# en: Say goodbye properly, here\nopt.right.label [d5f9ee] = 就在这里好好道别\n# en: A clean farewell, and no promises to break.\nopt.right.hint [8c6a83] = 干脆的告别，没有要违背的承诺。\n# en: You give {friend} the thing on your desk you'd most miss: a cracked mug, a dog-eared book, the stone from the lake. No promises; you've both seen what happens to promises. {They} turn{s} it over and over and say{s} nothing, which says enough.\nopt.right.say.1 [7ea96d] = 你把桌上最舍不得的东西送给{friend}：一只裂了的杯子、一本卷了角的书、湖边捡的那块石头。不许诺；你们都见过承诺的下场。{they}把它翻来覆去地看，一句话也不说，这就够了。\n\n@event last_partner\n# en: After Tomorrow\ntitle [2d7d5c] = 明天之后\n# en: {partner} has a map, an apple and the whole afternoon. \"Tomorrow we're not students,\" {they} say{s}. {flag(after_together): \"We said we'd go together. I still mean it. Do you?\"|\"So I thought we'd better have a plan. Or decide that we don't have one.\"} The map is spread on the battlement between you, weighted at one corner with the apple.\ntext [9126e2] = {partner}带着一张地图、一个苹果和一整个下午。“明天我们就不是学生了，”{they}说。{flag(after_together): “我们说过要一起走。我现在还是这么想。你呢？”|“所以我觉得我们最好有个计划。或者干脆决定不要。”}地图摊在你们之间的城垛上，一角压着那个苹果。\n# en: Two students sitting on a castle battlement at noon, legs dangling over the edge, a map of the realm spread between them, weighted with an apple.\nscene [c72168] = 正午时分，两个学生坐在城堡的城垛上，双腿悬在外面，王国的地图摊在两人之间，用一个苹果压着。\n# en: Plan the first year together\nopt.left.label [ed42fa] = 一起规划第一年\n# en: Whatever comes, together.\nopt.left.hint [143f72] = 不管来的是什么，一起。\n# en: You plan it badly and in detail: a town on the map, a room above a shop, whose turn it is to cook. {partner} draws a circle round the town in ink, so it can't be rubbed out.\nopt.left.say.1 [345a32] = 你们规划得很糟又很细：地图上的一座小镇、一间店铺楼上的屋子、轮到谁做饭。{partner} 用墨水把那座小镇圈了起来，这样就擦不掉了。\n# en: Part at the gate tomorrow\nopt.right.label [17f610] = 明天在大门口分开\n# en: Kindly, and for good.\nopt.right.hint [3593d7] = 温柔地，永远地。\n# en: You tell {partner} the truth: that tomorrow you'll walk out of the gates in different directions, and that it's right. {They} fold{s} the map very carefully and give{s} you the apple. Neither of you eats it.\nopt.right.say.1 [eb154d] = 你对{partner}说了实话：明天你们会走出大门，走向不同的方向，而这是对的。{they}非常小心地把地图叠好，把苹果递给你。你们谁也没吃。\n\n@event last_rival\n# en: One Last Word\ntitle [38a94f] = 最后一句话\n# en: {rival} is waiting in the empty duelling hall, as if by appointment. Four years of grudges, and tomorrow there won't be a school to have them in. \"Well,\" {they} say{s}. \"That's that, then.\"\ntext [aa9863] = {rival} 在空荡荡的决斗厅里等着，像是约好了似的。四年的积怨，而明天就没有学校可以让你们较劲了。“好吧，”{they} 说。“就这样了。”\n# en: Two students facing each other in an empty duelling hall, wands holstered, packed trunks visible through the open door behind them.\nscene [fe4904] = 空荡荡的决斗厅里，两个学生面对面站着，魔杖都收在套里，身后敞开的门外能看见收拾好的箱子。\n# en: Offer your hand\nopt.left.label [1f34dd] = 伸出你的手\n# en: {rival} looks at your hand for a long moment, then takes it, hard. \"You were worth beating,\" {they} say{s}, and then, almost too quietly to hear, \"and worth losing to.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [598903] = {rival} 盯着你的手看了很久，然后握住了，握得很用力。“你值得被打败，”{they} 说，然后，轻得几乎听不见，“也值得输给你。”\n# en: \"Rematch, in ten years.\"\nopt.right.label [3736fb] = “十年后再战。”\n# en: \"Ten years,\" says {rival}, \"and I'll still win.\" You shake on it in front of the last of the Circle, who spread it round the year by supper. It's the closest either of you will come to saying anything kind.\nopt.right.say.1 [ae5a6c] = “十年，”{rival} 说，“我还是会赢。”你们当着决斗社最后几个人的面握手为定，到晚饭时全年级都知道了。这是你们俩最接近说句好话的时刻了。\n\n@event last_starfall\n# en: The Last Starfall\ntitle [78d505] = 最后的星坠节\n# en: Tonight is Starfall, and by tradition the final-years have the observatory roof to themselves. The first-years are down on the lawns with their jars. Up here, everyone is pretending not to be sad.\ntext [b059cd] = 今晚是星坠节，按照传统，观星台的屋顶归毕业生们独享。一年级生们拿着罐子在下面的草坪上。在这上面，每个人都在假装不难过。\n# en: Final-year students in a row on the observatory roof under a sky full of falling stars, graduation robes thrown over their shoulders like blankets.\nscene [f698e6] = 毕业生们在观星台屋顶上排成一排，头顶的天空满是坠落的星星，毕业礼袍像毯子一样披在肩上。\n# en: Catch one for the road\nopt.left.label [cc3892] = 接一颗带上路\n# en: It drops into your hands with a hiss, the last thing Lumenhold gives you. The whole roof cheers. You'll keep it in its jar on a windowsill somewhere you haven't seen yet.\nopt.left.say.1 [317c17] = 它嘶的一声落进你手里，这是流明堡给你的最后一样东西。整个屋顶都在欢呼。你会把它装在罐子里，放在某个你还没见过的地方的窗台上。\n# en: You lunge, miss, and sit down hard, and the whole roof laughs, and you laugh too. Some things you're supposed to leave behind.\nopt.left.say.2 [83ff36] = 你扑过去，没接住，一屁股坐下，整个屋顶都笑了，你也笑了。有些东西本来就该留下。\n# en: Lie back with everyone\nopt.right.label [41957d] = 和大家一起躺下\n# en: You lie in a row with your year, shoulder to shoulder, and count the stars aloud until the counting falls apart. Somebody starts to cry, and then everybody is laughing.\nopt.right.say.1 [d8e7fe] = 你和同年级的人肩并肩躺成一排，大声数着星星，直到数乱了。有人开始哭，然后所有人都笑了起来。\n\n@event last_dumonts\n# en: Two Pale Heads\ntitle [a1309c] = 两个淡色的脑袋\n# en: {flag(dumont_reunited): The photographer is herding the year onto the great hall steps. At the end of a row, two pale heads lean together, Kael and Ilse, who was raised to open the door he refused. Kael sees you and moves up, which leaves a space between them and the edge.|The photographer is herding the year onto the great hall steps. At the end of a row Kael stands beside a space nobody is allowed to fill. Ilse should be in it. The man in grey took her north, and no letter has come.}\ntext [d5ee08] = {flag(dumont_reunited): 摄影师正把这一届赶上大礼堂的台阶。一排的尽头，两个淡色头发的脑袋靠在一起：凯尔，和被养大来打开他拒绝打开那扇门的伊尔泽。凯尔看见你，往里挪了挪，在他们和边上之间留出一个空位。|摄影师正把这一届赶上大礼堂的台阶。一排的尽头，凯尔站在一个谁都不许填的空位旁边。伊尔泽本该站在那里。灰衣人把她带去了北方，至今没有来信。}\n# en: A photographer's tripod on the great hall steps, graduates jostling into rows, a pale young man at the end of a row beside an empty space.\nscene [8f4e4f] = 大礼堂台阶上立着摄影师的三脚架，毕业生们挤着排成一排排，一排尽头有个脸色苍白的年轻人站在空位旁。\n# en: {flag(dumont_reunited): Squeeze into the photograph with them|Leave the space empty with him}\nopt.left.label [a8ad08] = {flag(dumont_reunited): 挤进他们的合影里|和他一起留着空位}\n# en: The yearbook will keep this.\nopt.left.hint [90f8eb] = 毕业纪念册会留下这一幕。\n# en: You squeeze in beside them, and Ilse elbows you, and Kael, for once, laughs out loud just as the shutter goes. It's the only photograph of the three of you, and it hangs in two houses for the rest of your lives.\nopt.left.say.1 [6b2556] = 你挤到他们旁边，伊尔泽用胳膊肘捅你，而快门按下的那一刻，凯尔难得地大声笑了出来。这是你们三人唯一的合影，此后一生都挂在两户人家里。\n# en: You stand at the edge of the space, so it stays a space, and Kael's hand finds your shoulder for a moment. In the photograph there's a gap in the row the size of a girl in white furs.\nopt.left.say.2 [a0722a] = 你站在空位边上，让它一直空着，凯尔的手在你肩上停了一会儿。照片里，那一排有一个空缺，正好是一个穿白裘的女孩那么大。\n# en: {flag(dumont_reunited): Take the photograph yourself|Talk Kael into closing the gap}\nopt.right.label [1f7e9a] = {flag(dumont_reunited): 你来给他们拍|劝凯尔把空位补上}\n# en: Today, and not the yearbook.\nopt.right.hint [5717c7] = 留给今天，不留给纪念册。\n# en: You take the camera from the photographer and make them stand still for once. Two Dumonts, squinting at the sun, in nobody's photograph but yours.\nopt.right.say.1 [6c112b] = 你从摄影师手里接过相机，难得让他们站着别动。两个杜蒙，眯着眼睛看太阳，只在你的照片里。\n# en: \"She'd hate being a gap,\" you tell him, and he almost smiles, and moves up. In the photograph the row closes. Neither of you forgets why it was open.\nopt.right.say.2 [3ac096] = “她会讨厌当个空缺的，”你对他说，他差点笑了，往里挪了挪。照片里，那一排合上了。你们谁都没忘记它为什么曾经空着。\n\n@event last_tamsin\n# en: A Page for Thornwick\ntitle [2862d3] = 给荆木的一页\n# en: There's a space in the corner of your yearbook page where a friend's message should go, and a green-sealed letter on your desk that came this morning: \"Four years of rivalry, four hundred letters. Leave me the corner. Insufferable to the end, T.\"\ntext [a9cac3] = 你的毕业纪念册那一页角落里有块空白，本该写朋友的留言；桌上还有一封今早寄到的绿封蜡信：“四年的较劲，四百封信。把那个角留给我。讨人嫌到底的，T。”\n# en: A yearbook page and a sheet of green-sealed paper side by side on a desk, a student holding a pen over both.\nscene [6b262f] = 一页毕业纪念册和一张绿封蜡的信纸并排放在桌上，一个学生握笔悬在两者之上。\n# en: Leave Tamsin the corner\nopt.left.label [d02ef0] = 把角落留给塔姆辛\n# en: The yearbook will keep this.\nopt.left.hint [90f8eb] = 毕业纪念册会留下这一笔。\n# en: You send the page over the hills by the evening owl. It comes back by morning, a green seal pressed into the corner, which is not allowed, and two lines you'll know by heart for the rest of your life.\nopt.left.say.1 [06778c] = 你让傍晚的猫头鹰把那一页送过山去。第二天早上它回来了，角落里压着一枚绿色封蜡，这是不允许的，还有两行字，你此后一生都会背得出来。\n# en: Save it for the spring meeting\nopt.right.label [ec0477] = 留到春天见面时再写\n# en: Biscuits, halfway between, every spring.\nopt.right.hint [20589f] = 每年春天，在中间那座镇上，带上饼干。\n# en: You write back that the corner can wait: you'll fill it in person, at the town halfway between, in the spring, with biscuits. Tamsin's reply says \"Coward\" and \"Yes\" in the same line.\nopt.right.say.1 [29c7ef] = 你回信说那个角可以等：你会在春天、在两地中间的小镇上、带着饼干，当面把它填上。塔姆辛的回信在同一行里写着“胆小鬼”和“好”。\n\n@event last_remembered\n# en: What the Yearbook Keeps\ntitle [da80e6] = 纪念册留下什么\n# en: The yearbook committee has your page in proof, with the lines they mean to print under your portrait. You may let them stand or strike them out. \"Two at most,\" says the editor, a second-year with ink on her nose. \"We're short of room.\"\ntext [c1a7fb] = 毕业纪念册编委会把你那一页的校样拿来了，你的肖像下面印着他们打算登的几行字。你可以让它们留着，也可以划掉。“最多两行，”编辑说，她是个二年级生，鼻子上沾着墨，“版面不够了。”\n# en: A yearbook committee's table in the library, proofs of portrait pages spread out, a student reading their own page upside down.\nscene [06c77d] = 图书馆里纪念册编委会的桌子，肖像页的校样摊开着，一个学生倒着读自己的那一页。\n# en: Let them print your fame\nopt.renown.label [37aacd] = 让他们登你的名气\n# en: The most talked-about student in a generation.\nopt.renown.hint [42c06f] = 一代人里最受议论的学生。\n# en: You leave the line about the Ledger's special edition. The editor looks relieved; it was her favourite.\nopt.renown.say.1 [655a07] = 你留下了那行关于《流明纪事报》特刊的字。编辑松了口气；那是她最喜欢的一行。\n# en: Let them print the line about you\nopt.lumen.label [670a28] = 让他们登那句写你的话\n# en: \"The one you went to when it mattered.\"\nopt.lumen.hint [7eb53f] = “要紧的时候，大家会去找的那个人。”\n# en: Someone has written \"The one you went to when it mattered.\" You don't know who. You leave it.\nopt.lumen.say.1 [66f3e3] = 有人写了一句：“要紧的时候，大家会去找的那个人。”你不知道是谁写的。你把它留下了。\n# en: Let the shadow stand\nopt.hollow.label [b25ab3] = 让那片阴影留着\n# en: The candles behind your portrait went out.\nopt.hollow.hint [3a627c] = 你肖像后面的蜡烛熄了。\n# en: In the proof, the candles behind your portrait have gone out. The editor offers to retouch it. You tell her to leave it as it is.\nopt.hollow.say.1 [2a31c9] = 校样上，你肖像后面的蜡烛都熄了。编辑提出要修一修。你告诉她就这样留着。\n# en: Keep the line about your thesis\nopt.thesis.label [acdc53] = 留下关于论文的那行\n# en: The thesis they locked away.\nopt.thesis.hint [0b0954] = 被锁起来的那篇论文。\n# en: The line about your thesis on the founders' seal has been half struck through already, in the headmistress's hand. You un-strike it.\nopt.thesis.say.1 [083da3] = 关于你那篇创始者封印的论文，那行字已经被划掉一半，是院长的笔迹。你把它恢复了。\n# en: Strike the rest\nopt.plain.label [c3de5a] = 划掉其余的\n# en: Only the line you keep; on its own, just your name and the year.\nopt.plain.hint [498c61] = 只留你保留的那一行；若一行都不留，就只有你的名字和年份。\n# en: You strike out everything else on the page. The editor sighs and reaches for the red pen. Less, it turns out, suits you.\nopt.plain.say.1 [e1fc7a] = 你把页面上其余的一切都划掉了。编辑叹了口气，伸手去拿红笔。原来，少一点更适合你。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/life.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/life.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event life_staircase\n# en: The Staircase Moves\ntitle [1e1113] = 楼梯动了\n# en: Halfway up the west staircase, it decides to be somewhere else. You're now heading toward a floor you've never seen, at speed, and you're late for class.\ntext [7bd8a9] = 西楼梯爬到一半，它决定去别的地方。你正被高速送往一层你从没见过的楼，而你上课要迟到了。\n# en: A grand spiral staircase in mid-rotation, a student clinging to the banister as the steps swing toward a different floor.\nscene [684196] = 一道宏伟的螺旋楼梯正在旋转，一个学生紧抓扶手，台阶正转向另一层楼。\n# en: Hold on and see where it goes\nopt.left.label [441219] = 抓紧，看它去哪儿\n# en: It deposits you in a dusty forgotten classroom. In a desk: eight old crowns and a love letter from 1802.\nopt.left.say.1 [0aecae] = 它把你放在一间落满灰尘、被遗忘的教室里。课桌里有八枚旧克朗和一封 1802 年的情书。\n# en: It takes you to the roof. Then the cellar. Then the roof again. You miss your morning lectures entirely.\nopt.left.say.2 [cf6831] = 它把你送上屋顶。然后是地窖。然后又是屋顶。你完美错过了整个上午的课。\n# en: Jump for the landing\nopt.right.label [fc3ab6] = 跳向平台\n# en: You leap and land neatly. A passing portrait applauds.\nopt.right.say.1 [e697a6] = 你一跃而起，稳稳落地。路过的一幅肖像画鼓起了掌。\n# en: You leap and land, but not neatly. A passing portrait winces.\nopt.right.say.2 [ff31a3] = 你一跃而起，落地了，但不太稳。路过的一幅肖像画皱了皱眉。\n\n@event life_mystery_stew\n# en: Mystery Stew\ntitle [afd265] = 神秘炖菜\n# en: Tonight's dinner is described on the board only as \"Stew\". It is grey-green. The ladle is standing up by itself. The cook is watching you with hope.\ntext [70292b] = 今晚的晚餐在菜单板上只写着“炖菜”。它是灰绿色的。汤勺自己立在里面。厨师满怀希望地看着你。\n# en: A huge cauldron of grey-green stew in the dining hall, a ladle standing upright in it unaided.\nscene [1a7e62] = 餐厅里一大锅灰绿色的炖菜，一把汤勺自己笔直地立在锅中。\n# en: Eat it bravely\nopt.left.label [b372d7] = 勇敢地吃下去\n# en: It's... delicious? Rich, peppery, faintly glowing. You go back for seconds. The cook beams.\nopt.left.say.1 [768d2a] = 它……很好吃？浓郁、带着胡椒味，还微微发光。你又去盛了一碗。厨师笑开了花。\n# en: It's not delicious. It's not anything a stomach should know about. You spend the night regretting it.\nopt.left.say.2 [995531] = 不好吃。它不是任何胃应该认识的东西。你后悔了一整晚。\n# en: Toast and jam again\nopt.right.label [31a8e9] = 还是吐司配果酱\n# en: You eat toast. The cook looks hurt. You feel guilty all evening.\nopt.right.say.1 [e74e29] = 你吃了吐司。厨师一脸受伤。你内疚了一整晚。\n\n@event life_lost_wand\n# en: Where Is Your Wand?\ntitle [e9e5f4] = 你的魔杖呢？\n# en: Your wand is gone. It was on your desk. Now it isn't. Classes start in twenty minutes.\ntext [006487] = 你的魔杖不见了。它刚才还在桌上。现在没了。二十分钟后就要上课了。\n# en: A dormitory turned upside down, clothes everywhere, a student on hands and knees peering under a bed.\nscene [9d31f7] = 一间被翻得底朝天的宿舍，衣服扔得到处都是，一个学生趴在地上往床底下看。\n# en: Tear the room apart\nopt.left.label [a2a2bf] = 把房间翻个底朝天\n# en: It's in your boot. Why is it in your boot? You make it to class with seconds to spare.\nopt.left.say.1 [f6b100] = 它在你的靴子里。它为什么会在你靴子里？你在最后几秒赶到了教室。\n# en: You find it at lunch, inside the pillowcase. You've missed a whole morning.\nopt.left.say.2 [2b5185] = 午饭时你在枕套里找到了它。你错过了整整一上午。\n# en: Try a summoning charm\nopt.right.label [18e9e7] = 试试召唤咒\n# en: It flies into your hand from inside the wardrobe, offended at having been lost.\nopt.right.say.1 [d8a6af] = 它从衣柜里飞进你手里，对自己被弄丢一事颇为不满。\n# en: Every pencil in the dormitory flies at your face. The wand stays hidden out of spite.\nopt.right.say.2 [1fe091] = 宿舍里每一支铅笔都朝你脸上飞来。魔杖出于怨气，继续藏着。\n\n@event life_snoring_roommate\n# en: The Snorer\ntitle [b3c66f] = 打呼噜的人\n# en: Someone in your dormitory snores like a dragon with a head cold, and nobody will admit to it. You haven't slept properly in a week.\ntext [ab60f0] = 宿舍里有人打起呼噜来像一条感冒的龙，而且没人承认。你已经一个星期没睡好了。\n# en: A dark dormitory, one bed shaking with snores so loud the curtains flutter, another occupant glaring with a pillow over their head.\nscene [a2130d] = 漆黑的宿舍里，一张床随着震天的呼噜声摇晃，窗帘都在飘，另一个人用枕头捂着头怒目而视。\n# en: Brew a silencing draught\nopt.left.label [716d57] = 熬一剂静音药剂\n# en: Perfect silence. Too perfect. The culprit, the quiet one by the window, is now silent even when awake. It wears off by Tuesday. You never quite ask their permission.\nopt.left.say.1 [f6a256] = 完美的寂静。太完美了。罪魁祸首——靠窗那个安静的家伙——现在醒着也发不出声音。到周二才失效。你始终没问过人家同不同意。\n# en: The draught finds the wrong bed. Now two people snore, in harmony.\nopt.left.say.2 [80815d] = 药剂找错了床。现在有两个人在打呼噜，还是和声。\n# en: Move to the common room sofa\nopt.right.label [65d659] = 搬到公共休息室的沙发上\n# en: You sleep on the sofa and meet everyone who comes down at 3am. Some of them are very interesting.\nopt.right.say.1 [168a41] = 你睡在沙发上，结识了每一个凌晨三点下楼的人。其中有些人非常有意思。\n\n@event life_portrait_gossip\n# en: The Portraits Talk\ntitle [b767ea] = 肖像画在说话\n# en: Two portraits in the east gallery are gossiping about a student. Then one of them says your name and they both go suspiciously quiet.\ntext [feda64] = 东画廊里两幅肖像画正在八卦某个学生。然后其中一幅说出了你的名字，两幅画都可疑地安静了下来。\n# en: A long gallery of gilded portraits, painted figures leaning out of their frames toward each other, whispering behind painted hands.\nscene [58f7f0] = 长长的画廊里挂满镀金肖像，画中人探出画框凑在一起，用画出来的手捂着嘴窃窃私语。\n# en: Bribe them with flattery\nopt.left.label [729d7c] = 用奉承贿赂它们\n# en: Ten minutes of compliments on their brushwork, and they tell you everything. Gossip about half the faculty, too.\nopt.left.say.1 [b45427] = 夸了十分钟它们的笔触，它们就什么都告诉你了。还附送了半个教职员团队的八卦。\n# en: \"Flattery,\" sniffs the duchess, \"is so common.\" They turn their backs. Literally.\nopt.left.say.2 [20a283] = “奉承，”公爵夫人嗤之以鼻，“太俗气了。”它们背过身去。字面意义上的。\n# en: Pretend you didn't hear\nopt.right.label [e071a6] = 假装没听见\n# en: You walk on. Behind you, the whispering resumes, louder.\nopt.right.say.1 [546c2f] = 你继续往前走。身后，窃窃私语又响了起来，更大声了。\n\n@event life_pixies\n# en: Pixie Infestation\ntitle [12ac66] = 小精灵泛滥\n# en: Someone left a window open and the dormitory is now full of pixies. They're stealing socks, unscrewing bedposts and laughing in very high voices.\ntext [53332f] = 有人没关窗户，宿舍里现在满是小精灵。它们在偷袜子、拧床柱，还用尖细的嗓音大笑。\n# en: A dormitory full of tiny blue winged creatures swinging from curtains, stealing socks and pulling hair.\nscene [9a096c] = 宿舍里到处是蓝色带翅膀的小生物，荡在窗帘上，偷袜子，扯头发。\n# en: Fight them off\nopt.left.label [a5f0b8] = 把它们打跑\n# en: You catch nine with a laundry bag. The dorm cheers. The pixies swear at you, colourfully.\nopt.left.say.1 [db8849] = 你用洗衣袋抓住了九只。宿舍一片欢呼。小精灵们花样百出地骂你。\n# en: You lose. The pixies hang you from the chandelier by your robe. It takes an hour to get down.\nopt.left.say.2 [87d5ba] = 你输了。小精灵把你的长袍挂在吊灯上，连人一起。你花了一个小时才下来。\n# en: Bribe them with sugar\nopt.right.label [8661f5] = 用糖贿赂它们\n# en: You lay a trail of sugar cubes out of the window. The pixies follow it, munching, and leave one sock as a tip.\nopt.right.say.1 [ace496] = 你在窗外铺了一路方糖。小精灵们边吃边跟着走了，还留下一只袜子当小费。\n\n@event life_gargoyle\n# en: A Talkative Gargoyle\ntitle [7093dd] = 话痨石像鬼\n# en: The gargoyle above the east door clears its throat as you pass. \"You,\" it rumbles. \"You look like someone who'd listen. Nobody listens to gargoyles.\"\ntext [4162d1] = 你经过时，东门上方的石像鬼清了清嗓子。“你，”它低沉地说，“你看起来像会听人说话的人。没人愿意听石像鬼说话。”\n# en: A weathered stone gargoyle on a parapet, one eye open, mouth moving, a student sitting beside it with a sandwich.\nscene [2eb36a] = 护墙上一尊饱经风霜的石像鬼睁着一只眼，嘴在动，一个学生拿着三明治坐在它旁边。\n# en: Hurry along\nopt.left.label [1a6798] = 赶紧走开\n# en: \"Typical,\" says the gargoyle. It sulks until spring.\nopt.left.say.1 [852a4c] = “典型。”石像鬼说。它一直闷闷不乐到了春天。\n# en: Sit and listen\nopt.right.label [1a3cf5] = 坐下来听\n# en: It tells you three hundred years of academy gossip, including where the founders hid the good biscuits. You find the biscuits.\nopt.right.say.1 [3415ec] = 它给你讲了三百年的学院八卦，包括创始者把好饼干藏在了哪里。你找到了那些饼干。\n\n@event life_brownies\n# en: Help in the Night\ntitle [fae0e4] = 夜里的帮手\n# en: You wake to find your desk tidied, your socks darned and your essay proofread. Brownies. They expect something in return. Traditionally: cream.\ntext [bfda31] = 你醒来发现书桌被收拾整齐、袜子被补好、论文被校对过了。是棕仙。它们期待回报。按照传统：奶油。\n# en: Tiny bearded figures in aprons tidying a cluttered student desk by moonlight, one darning a sock.\nscene [bb94a0] = 月光下，系着围裙的长胡子小人在整理一张凌乱的书桌，其中一个在补袜子。\n# en: Leave out a bowl of cream\nopt.left.label [a73198] = 放一碗奶油\n# en: Your room stays spotless all term. Your essays have suspiciously good spelling.\nopt.left.say.1 [f0b341] = 整个学期你的房间都一尘不染。你的论文拼写好得可疑。\n# en: Leave them nothing\nopt.right.label [760b5f] = 什么都不留\n# en: The next morning, every one of your socks is tied in knots. Brownies keep score.\nopt.right.say.1 [ac042b] = 第二天早上，你的每一只袜子都被打了结。棕仙是记账的。\n\n@event life_midnight_snack\n# en: Midnight Hunger\ntitle [5673a0] = 午夜饥饿\n# en: It's midnight and you're starving. The kitchens are three floors down, past a prefect's post.\ntext [d97eb5] = 午夜了，你饿得要命。厨房在三层楼下，要经过一个级长的岗哨。\n# en: A student in pyjamas tiptoeing past a sleeping prefect toward a door marked KITCHENS, a candle cupped in their hand.\nscene [6a7e01] = 一个穿睡衣的学生踮着脚经过打盹的级长，走向一扇写着“厨房”的门，双手护着一支蜡烛。\n# en: Go back to bed hungry\nopt.left.label [6a67a5] = 饿着回去睡\n# en: You dream of sandwiches. Long, detailed, beautiful sandwiches.\nopt.left.say.1 [d77265] = 你梦见了三明治。长长的、细节丰富的、美丽的三明治。\n# en: Sneak down\nopt.right.label [a9ae15] = 偷偷溜下去\n# en: Cold pie, a pot of custard, a heel of bread. Feast of kings.\nopt.right.say.1 [2a2136] = 冷馅饼、一罐蛋奶冻、一块面包头。帝王盛宴。\n# en: The prefect is awake. You spend a week washing the pots you were trying to raid.\nopt.right.say.2 [151ad8] = 级长醒着。你洗了一星期你本想去偷吃的那些锅。\n\n@event life_insomnia\n# en: The Ceiling at 3am\ntitle [bbad03] = 凌晨三点的天花板\n# en: You can't sleep. Your brain has decided to replay every embarrassing thing you've said since you were six.\ntext [0cce8a] = 你睡不着。你的大脑决定把你六岁以来说过的每一句丢人的话都重播一遍。\n# en: A student lying awake staring at a ceiling where faint painted stars have started slowly moving.\nscene [eda828] = 一个学生睁眼躺着，盯着天花板，上面画着的淡淡星星开始缓缓移动。\n# en: Get up and study\nopt.left.label [dd9384] = 起床学习\n# en: By dawn you've read three chapters and remembered one of them.\nopt.left.say.1 [d35323] = 到天亮时你读了三章，记住了其中一章。\n# en: Chamomile, and count the stars\nopt.right.label [b39bbc] = 泡杯洋甘菊，数星星\n# en: Halfway through counting, the stars start counting back. Then you're asleep.\nopt.right.say.1 [297bdf] = 数到一半，星星开始反过来数你。然后你就睡着了。\n\n@event life_morning_run\n# en: The Dawn Run\ntitle [97a783] = 黎明晨跑\n# en: A group of students runs the lakeshore every dawn. They've invited you. It's very early. It's very cold.\ntext [b909a7] = 有一群学生每天黎明沿着湖岸跑步。他们邀请了你。很早。很冷。\n# en: The lakeshore path at dawn, mist on the water, a lone runner's breath steaming, the towers catching the first gold light.\nscene [0d10ee] = 黎明的湖岸小路，水面上浮着薄雾，一个孤独的跑者呼着白气，塔楼映着第一缕金光。\n# en: Sleep in\nopt.left.label [de2af7] = 睡懒觉\n# en: You hear them come back, glowing and smug. You are warm and unrepentant.\nopt.left.say.1 [58ff7c] = 你听见他们回来了，容光焕发，一脸得意。你暖暖和和，毫无悔意。\n# en: Run with them\nopt.right.label [8e7ee8] = 和他们一起跑\n# en: You nearly die at the first mile and feel immortal by the third. The lake shines like a coin. You feel every step of it all through your lectures.\nopt.right.say.1 [2e95a0] = 第一英里你差点死掉，到第三英里你觉得自己长生不老。湖面亮得像一枚硬币。整堂课上，你都能感觉到每一步的后劲。\n\n@event life_homework_mountain\n# en: The Pile\ntitle [654764] = 作业山\n# en: Three essays, a potion log, a star chart and a reading list the length of your arm. All due this week. The pile has achieved its own weather.\ntext [68b440] = 三篇论文、一份药剂日志、一张星图，还有一份和你胳膊一样长的阅读书单。全部本周截止。这座作业山已经形成了自己的小气候。\n# en: A desk buried under a precarious tower of scrolls and books, a student peeking hopelessly over the top.\nscene [7c65a4] = 书桌被一座摇摇欲坠的卷轴和书本之塔淹没，一个学生绝望地从塔顶探出头来。\n# en: Grind through it\nopt.left.label [6c90f1] = 埋头苦干\n# en: You finish at 4am on the last day. Every piece is handed in. You sleep through breakfast.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f2b04] = 最后一天凌晨四点你全部完成。每一份都交上去了。你睡过了早饭。\n# en: Pick what matters, skip the rest\nopt.right.label [321412] = 挑重要的做，其余的跳过\n# en: You hand in two of five and go for a walk. The professors are not pleased. You feel great, temporarily.\nopt.right.say.1 [c142df] = 你交了五份中的两份，然后出去散步了。教授们不太高兴。你感觉好极了，暂时。\n\n@event life_stolen_answers\n# en: The Answers\ntitle [48b36f] = 答案\n# en: A third-year is selling copies of next week's exam papers. Eight crowns for the set. \"Guaranteed real,\" he whispers. \"Straight from the examiner's desk.\"\ntext [47adc0] = 一个三年级生在卖下周考试的整套试卷。八克朗一套。“保真，”他低声说，“直接从考官桌上拿的。”\n# en: A folded paper being passed under a library table, the faint glow of an examiner's seal on it.\nscene [fab1a5] = 一张折好的纸从图书馆桌下传过，上面隐隐闪着考官的印章。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.left.label [96da1a] = 走开\n# en: Two weeks later, five students who bought copies are suspended. The answers were fake.\nopt.left.say.1 [882a6f] = 两周后，买了复印件的五个学生被停学了。那些答案是假的。\n# en: Buy a copy\nopt.right.label [cae18d] = 买一份\n# en: They're real. You know every question before you sit down. You also can't quite look the examiner in the eye.\nopt.right.say.1 [775981] = 是真的。你还没坐下就知道了每一道题。你也没法直视考官的眼睛了。\n# en: They're fake, and planted. You're caught with a copy. Detention, and a very cold letter home.\nopt.right.say.2 [aaad15] = 是假的，而且是个圈套。你被抓到持有复印件。禁闭，外加一封非常冰冷的家书。\n\n@event life_backfire\n# en: Spell Backfire\ntitle [195f68] = 咒语反噬\n# en: You mispronounce a minor cheering charm in the corridor. Something goes wrong. Every time you hiccup, a tiny firework comes out.\ntext [4df613] = 你在走廊里把一个小小的振奋咒念错了。出了点问题。现在你每打一个嗝，就会冒出一朵小烟花。\n# en: A student mid-hiccup, a small firework bursting from their mouth, classmates ducking.\nscene [89a5a5] = 一个学生正在打嗝，一朵小烟花从嘴里炸开，同学们纷纷闪躲。\n# en: Go to the infirmary\nopt.left.label [f09b9c] = 去医务室\n# en: Sister Amaranth cures it in a minute and charges you three crowns. \"For the ceiling,\" she says.\nopt.left.say.1 [4e5350] = 阿玛兰斯修女一分钟就治好了，收了你三克朗。“赔天花板的。”她说。\n# en: Live with it\nopt.right.label [a97cce] = 就这么过吧\n# en: You become briefly famous as \"the sparkler\". It's not the fame you wanted.\nopt.right.say.1 [01b18e] = 你作为“烟花人”短暂地出了名。这不是你想要的那种出名。\n\n@event life_lake_swim\n# en: A Swim in the Lake\ntitle [939267] = 湖中游泳\n# en: It's hot and the whole year has gone down to the jetty. Something large is rumoured to live in the deep part. People are swimming anyway.\ntext [9f8141] = 天很热，整个年级都跑到栈桥边去了。传说湖的深处住着什么大东西。大家还是照游不误。\n# en: Students leaping off a wooden jetty into a sparkling lake on a hot afternoon, something large and dark moving far beneath the surface.\nscene [36073b] = 炎热的午后，学生们从木栈桥跳进波光粼粼的湖里，水面下极深处有个巨大的黑影在移动。\n# en: Stay on the jetty\nopt.left.label [a30932] = 待在栈桥上\n# en: You dangle your feet and eat cherries. Something brushes your toe. You keep your feet up after that.\nopt.left.say.1 [5776ea] = 你晃着脚丫吃樱桃。有什么东西擦过你的脚趾。从那以后你一直把脚抬着。\n# en: Dive in\nopt.right.label [eb830e] = 跳下去\n# en: In the deep part, you open your eyes underwater and see a round door, far below, glowing. Then you need air.\nopt.right.say.1 [0ca583] = 在深水区，你在水下睁开眼，看见很深很深的地方有一扇圆门在发光。然后你需要换气了。\n# en: The water's perfect. Something large and friendly swims under you, and you decide not to panic.\nopt.right.say.2 [0e8868] = 水温完美。有个友善的大家伙从你身下游过，你决定不慌。\n\n@event life_stray_cat\n# en: A Cat Moves In\ntitle [9441a9] = 猫搬进来了\n# en: A black cat with one torn ear is sitting on your pillow. It looks at you as though you are the one trespassing.\ntext [f24184] = 一只耳朵缺了一角的黑猫正坐在你的枕头上。它看着你，好像闯进来的人是你。\n# en: A scruffy black cat sitting on a dormitory pillow as if it has always lived there, one torn ear, very green eyes.\nscene [2adf64] = 一只邋遢的黑猫坐在宿舍枕头上，仿佛一直住在这里，一只耳朵破了，眼睛绿得发亮。\n# en: Shoo it out\nopt.left.label [bd0ff5] = 把它赶出去\n# en: It leaves with enormous dignity and moves in with your neighbour, who spoils it rotten.\nopt.left.say.1 [73a8df] = 它极其庄重地离开了，搬去和你的邻居住，邻居把它宠上了天。\n# en: Let it stay\nopt.right.label [b09a3c] = 让它留下\n# en: It's called Professor now. It sleeps on your homework. You've never been happier.\nopt.right.say.1 [7e205d] = 它现在叫“教授”。它睡在你的作业上。你从没这么幸福过。\n\n@event life_storm_night\n# en: The Lights Go Out\ntitle [3af5ad] = 灯灭了\n# en: A storm knocks out every light-crystal in the dormitory. The common room fills with students, blankets and somebody's terrible ghost story.\ntext [cb21f0] = 一场风暴让宿舍里所有的光晶石都熄灭了。公共休息室里挤满了学生、毯子，和某人讲的烂透了的鬼故事。\n# en: A dark common room lit only by lightning, students huddled under blankets, one telling a ghost story.\nscene [09a304] = 漆黑的公共休息室只被闪电照亮，学生们裹着毯子挤在一起，有人在讲鬼故事。\n# en: Tell an even scarier story\nopt.left.label [36f701] = 讲个更吓人的故事\n# en: Your story is so good that three people sleep with the lights on for a week. You're a legend.\nopt.left.say.1 [d941b3] = 你的故事太好了，有三个人开着灯睡了一个星期。你成了传奇。\n# en: Your story is not scary. It is, however, accidentally hilarious.\nopt.left.say.2 [226bee] = 你的故事不吓人。不过，意外地非常好笑。\n# en: Light the room with a spell\nopt.right.label [928a24] = 用咒语照亮房间\n# en: You fill the room with soft floating lights. Someone starts singing. It turns into the best night of term.\nopt.right.say.1 [a9ec71] = 你让屋里飘满柔和的光点。有人唱起了歌。那成了本学期最美好的一夜。\n# en: You light the sofa. Briefly. The storm seems less frightening after that.\nopt.right.say.2 [725129] = 你点着了沙发。短暂地。那之后，风暴似乎也没那么可怕了。\n\n@event life_mirror_talks\n# en: The Honest Mirror\ntitle [11488a] = 诚实的镜子\n# en: The bathroom mirror has started commenting on your appearance. \"Hair,\" it says this morning. Just: \"Hair.\"\ntext [197720] = 浴室的镜子开始点评你的外表了。今天早上它说：“头发。”就两个字：“头发。”\n# en: A bathroom mirror with a stern painted face in its silvering, frowning at a student mid-toothbrush.\nscene [bed5d4] = 一面浴室镜子的镀银里嵌着一张严肃的画中脸，正对着刷牙刷到一半的学生皱眉。\n# en: Ask for its advice\nopt.left.label [97bf15] = 向它请教\n# en: It turns out to be an excellent stylist. You look great. You hate that it's right.\nopt.left.say.1 [88d699] = 原来它是个出色的造型师。你看起来棒极了。你讨厌它说得对。\n# en: Cover it with a towel\nopt.right.label [719e1f] = 用毛巾盖住它\n# en: It sulks under the towel. Muffled: \"Hair.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2b3d43] = 它在毛巾下面生闷气。闷声闷气地：“头发。”\n\n@event life_prank_war\n# en: Prank War\ntitle [562813] = 恶作剧大战\n# en: The dormitory across the corridor turned your door into a goat. The goat is fine. Honour, however, demands retaliation.\ntext [a19079] = 走廊对面的宿舍把你的门变成了一只山羊。山羊没事。然而荣誉要求你报复。\n# en: A corridor ankle-deep in enchanted soap bubbles, two dormitories' worth of students hurling them at each other.\nscene [9791ed] = 走廊里没过脚踝的魔法肥皂泡，两个宿舍的学生互相扔着泡泡。\n# en: Rise above it\nopt.left.label [fe2376] = 不跟他们计较\n# en: You take the high road. The goat eats your homework. You let it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f7115b] = 你选择了高尚。山羊吃了你的作业。你随它去了。\n# en: Retaliate\nopt.right.label [5cb1e8] = 报复回去\n# en: You fill their dorm with a thousand singing bubbles. They concede by dawn.\nopt.right.say.1 [59c97e] = 你让他们宿舍里塞满了一千个会唱歌的泡泡。天亮前他们就认输了。\n# en: You're caught carrying a crate of bubbles. Detention, but the goat seems proud of you.\nopt.right.say.2 [aee56b] = 你抱着一箱泡泡时被逮住了。禁闭，但那只山羊似乎为你骄傲。\n# en: Glamour their door into a goat\nopt.up.label [9c44fe] = 用魅影把他们的门变成山羊\n# en: You return the compliment with a glamour: their door becomes a goat, an extremely convincing one, which bleats when knocked. It lasts three days. Nobody can prove a thing.\nopt.up.say.1 [d28289] = 你用一道魅影回敬：他们的门变成了一只山羊，逼真极了，一敲门就咩咩叫。这持续了三天。谁也拿不出证据。\n\n@event life_mail_owl\n# en: The Wrong Letter\ntitle [38e547] = 送错的信\n# en: A delivery owl drops a scented pink letter in your porridge. It's addressed to someone else. It's a love letter. It's very, very passionate.\ntext [6296cf] = 一只送信猫头鹰把一封带香味的粉色信扔进了你的粥里。收件人不是你。是封情书。非常、非常热烈的情书。\n# en: A ruffled delivery owl dropping a scented pink envelope onto a breakfast plate, a student looking up in confusion.\nscene [2c78c3] = 一只羽毛凌乱的送信猫头鹰把一只带香味的粉色信封扔到早餐盘上，一个学生困惑地抬起头。\n# en: Deliver it to its owner\nopt.left.label [6dedfd] = 把它送给收信人\n# en: The recipient goes crimson and thanks you. Two weeks later, they're holding hands with the sender in the dining hall.\nopt.left.say.1 [364f07] = 收信人满脸通红地向你道谢。两周后，他们和写信人在餐厅里手牵着手。\n# en: Read it first\nopt.right.label [e728e4] = 先读一读\n# en: You read it. You will never be able to look the potions professor in the eye again.\nopt.right.say.1 [038e70] = 你读了。你再也无法直视魔药教授的眼睛了。\n\n@event life_money_from_home\n# en: A Parcel From Home\ntitle [0fa1f2] = 家里寄来的包裹\n# en: A parcel from home: a knitted scarf, a jar of jam, a note telling you to eat vegetables, and a small purse of coins.\ntext [e2ed27] = 一个家里寄来的包裹：一条织好的围巾、一罐果酱、一张叮嘱你多吃蔬菜的字条，还有一小袋硬币。\n# en: A brown paper parcel tied with string on a dormitory bed, a knitted scarf, a jar of jam and a small coin purse spilling out.\nscene [632059] = 宿舍床上一个用绳子捆着的牛皮纸包裹，露出一条针织围巾、一罐果酱和一只小钱袋。\n# en: Save the money\nopt.left.label [59078b] = 把钱存起来\n# en: You tuck it away. The jam lasts six days.\nopt.left.say.1 [c9f65f] = 你把钱收好。果酱吃了六天。\n# en: Spend it on a treat for your friends\nopt.right.label [c9ff29] = 请朋友们吃点好的\n# en: Cake from the village for everyone. You're popular for an entire afternoon.\nopt.right.say.1 [182e55] = 从镇上买了蛋糕分给大家。你受欢迎了整整一个下午。\n\n@event life_found_purse\n# en: A Purse in the Grass\ntitle [558b87] = 草丛里的钱袋\n# en: A fat velvet purse lies in the grass by the path. Thirty crowns at least. Embroidered initials: A.T.\ntext [bc1f08] = 小路边的草丛里躺着一只鼓鼓的天鹅绒钱袋。至少三十克朗。上面绣着缩写：A.T.\n# en: A velvet purse lying in the long grass beside a path, coins glinting at its open mouth, initials embroidered on it.\nscene [b64283] = 小路旁长草间躺着一只天鹅绒钱袋，袋口露出闪亮的硬币，上面绣着姓名缩写。\n# en: Return it to its owner\nopt.left.label [c3ae44] = 物归原主\n# en: A.T. turns out to be Aldric Thorne. He takes the purse and stares at you a long moment. \"Honest,\" he says. It sounds like a diagnosis.\nopt.left.say.1 [ccc630] = A.T. 原来是奥尔德里克·索恩。他接过钱袋，盯着你看了很久。“诚实。”他说。听起来像是诊断结果。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.right.label [87c2f5] = 据为己有\n# en: Thirty crowns richer. A few days later, Thorne mentions in class that he lost something. He looks at no one in particular.\nopt.right.say.1 [3a88fb] = 你多了三十克朗。几天后，索恩在课上提到他丢了东西。他没有特意看向任何人。\n\n@event life_peddler\n# en: The Travelling Peddler\ntitle [c295f6] = 行脚商人\n# en: A peddler's wagon has parked by the gates. \"Lucky charms! Genuine article! Thirty crowns, and fortune follows you for a season!\"\ntext [985dd5] = 一辆货郎车停在了校门边。“幸运护身符！货真价实！三十克朗，好运伴你一整季！”\n# en: A gaudy painted wagon at the academy gates, hung with lanterns and trinkets, a peddler with a gold tooth grinning.\nscene [aca905] = 学院门口一辆花哨的彩绘货车，挂满灯笼和小玩意儿，一个镶金牙的货郎咧嘴笑着。\n# en: Buy a lucky charm\nopt.left.label [00deab] = 买个幸运护身符\n# en: 30 gold\nopt.left.hint [54caf0] = 30 金币\n# en: It's a real one. Things go right all month. You start talking to it.\nopt.left.say.1 [eb2634] = 是真货。整个月诸事顺遂。你开始跟它说话了。\n# en: It turns your neck green. Fortune does not follow you. The peddler is long gone.\nopt.left.say.2 [a11a74] = 它把你的脖子染绿了。好运并没有跟着你。货郎早就跑没影了。\n# en: Just browse\nopt.right.label [dbbc5f] = 随便看看\n# en: You buy nothing and enjoy a sugared apple and the peddler's outrageous stories.\nopt.right.say.1 [017402] = 你什么都没买，享受了一只糖苹果和货郎天花乱坠的故事。\n\n@event life_bookshop\n# en: Sale at Grimsby's\ntitle [89ece4] = 格林斯比书店大促\n# en: Grimsby's Books is having a sale. An old copy of \"Advanced Arcane Theory\" for fifteen crowns. Normally forty. It's annotated, apparently, by a famous wizard.\ntext [e4e3d1] = 格林斯比书店在打折。一本旧版《高等奥术理论》只要十五克朗。平时要四十。据说上面有一位著名巫师的批注。\n# en: A crooked bookshop with books stacked to the ceiling, a hand-painted sign in the window reading HALF OFF, MOSTLY.\nscene [3daef9] = 一家歪歪扭扭的书店，书一直堆到天花板，橱窗上手绘的招牌写着：半价，大部分。\n# en: Buy it\nopt.left.label [0aa234] = 买下\n# en: 15 gold\nopt.left.hint [05537f] = 15 金币\n# en: The annotations are brilliant, rude and occasionally about the author's divorce. You learn a great deal.\nopt.left.say.1 [0f1da3] = 批注精彩、刻薄，偶尔还聊到作者的离婚。你学到了很多。\n# en: Leave it\nopt.right.label [3e2fed] = 算了\n# en: You walk away. You think about it for weeks.\nopt.right.say.1 [08ccd0] = 你走开了。接下来好几周你都在惦记它。\n\n@event life_new_robes\n# en: Shabby Robes\ntitle [a4fae7] = 破旧的长袍\n# en: Your robes are fraying at every hem. Someone at dinner called them \"vintage\". It was not a compliment.\ntext [afc5fd] = 你的长袍每一处下摆都磨毛了。晚饭时有人说它们很“复古”。那可不是夸奖。\n# en: A student in a hall mirror holding up a fraying sleeve, a tailor's flyer in their other hand.\nscene [c82b72] = 一个学生站在大厅的镜子前，举起磨毛的袖子，另一只手里拿着裁缝铺的传单。\n# en: Patch them yourself\nopt.left.label [475422] = 自己缝补\n# en: Your stitching is crooked but cheerful. Some people start copying it.\nopt.left.say.1 [019c7e] = 你的针脚歪歪扭扭，却很欢快。有些人开始模仿了。\n# en: Buy new robes (30 gold)\nopt.right.label [ed7264] = 买新长袍（30 金币）\n# en: 30 gold\nopt.right.hint [54caf0] = 30 金币\n# en: Midnight-blue wool, silver trim. People actually turn to look. You walk differently all week.\nopt.right.say.1 [2c96cc] = 午夜蓝的羊毛，银色镶边。人们真的会回头看。你一整周走路的姿势都不一样了。\n\n@event life_card_game\n# en: A Game of Wizard's Hand\ntitle [7c9c91] = 一局巫师牌\n# en: A late-night game of Wizard's Hand. The cards change faces when no one's looking. The pot is thirty crowns. The upper-years look hungry.\ntext [0d1104] = 深夜的一局巫师牌。没人看时，牌面会自己变。奖池三十克朗。高年级生们看起来很饿。\n# en: A smoky corner of the common room, students around a low table with cards and coins, one card floating and changing face.\nscene [404f4d] = 公共休息室烟雾缭绕的角落，学生们围着矮桌打牌，桌上堆着硬币，一张牌浮在空中变换牌面。\n# en: Play\nopt.left.label [ab6f83] = 下场\n# en: You read every bluff and keep a straight face. Thirty crowns and a reputation.\nopt.left.say.1 [36d8bf] = 你识破了每一次虚张声势，始终面不改色。三十克朗，外加名声。\n# en: You lose fifteen crowns and the upper-years' respect, in that order.\nopt.left.say.2 [47f34e] = 你输掉了十五克朗和高年级生的尊重，按这个顺序。\n# en: Watch and learn\nopt.right.label [37e8ef] = 看着学\n# en: You learn more watching than you would have playing. Mostly about who cheats.\nopt.right.say.1 [11df36] = 你看到的比下场玩学到的还多。主要是谁在出老千。\n\n@event life_essay_prize\n# en: The Essay Prize\ntitle [f26093] = 论文奖\n# en: The Lumen Essay Prize: thirty crowns for the best essay on \"Magic and Duty\". The deadline is Friday. You have an idea already.\ntext [04145b] = 流明论文奖：五十克朗，奖给以“魔法与责任”为题的最佳论文。周五截止。你已经有了一个想法。\n# en: A brass plaque on a noticeboard announcing THE LUMEN ESSAY PRIZE — 30 CROWNS, a quill pinned beneath it.\nscene [cb3beb] = 布告栏上一块黄铜牌宣布“流明论文奖——30 克朗”，下面钉着一支羽毛笔。\n# en: Enter\nopt.left.label [f863ad] = 参赛\n# en: The essay eats your week, and wins. Your essay is read aloud at dinner. You don't know where to look.\nopt.left.say.1 [3f2c62] = 这篇论文吃掉了你一整个星期，然后赢了。晚饭时你的论文被当众朗读。你不知道眼睛该往哪儿看。\n# en: The essay eats your week, and doesn't win, but the judges' notes are glowing. Next year, maybe.\nopt.left.say.2 [0043f5] = 这篇论文吃掉了你一整个星期，没能获奖，但评委的点评赞不绝口。也许明年吧。\n# en: Skip it\nopt.right.label [9909cb] = 不参加\n# en: The winner reads their essay aloud at dinner. Yours would have been better. Probably. You had a very pleasant week instead.\nopt.right.say.1 [071ce6] = 晚饭时获奖者朗读了自己的论文。你的本来会更好。大概吧。而你度过了非常惬意的一个星期。\n\n@event life_charity\n# en: The Winter Fund\ntitle [04edf0] = 冬季基金\n# en: The village winter fund is collecting for families who can't afford fuel this winter. The jar is half full.\ntext [cf1ae3] = 小镇冬季基金正在为今冬买不起柴火的家庭募捐。募捐罐已经满了一半。\n# en: A collection jar shaped like a lantern on the dining hall table, half full of coins, a hand-lettered sign: FOR THE VILLAGE WINTER FUND.\nscene [cb02b0] = 餐厅桌上一只灯笼形状的募捐罐，半满的硬币，手写的牌子：小镇冬季基金。\n# en: Donate fifteen crowns\nopt.left.label [645481] = 捐十五克朗\n# en: 15 gold\nopt.left.hint [05537f] = 15 金币\n# en: Your name goes on a little list by the jar. You feel warm, which is the point.\nopt.left.say.1 [e202cc] = 你的名字被写进了罐子旁边的小名单。你觉得心里暖暖的，这正是意义所在。\n# en: Walk past\nopt.right.label [d5359c] = 走过去\n# en: {gold >= 15: You walk past. The jar fills anyway. You think about it on cold nights.|You have nothing to give this year. The jar fills anyway. You think about it on cold nights.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9ee81d] = {gold >= 15: 你走了过去。罐子还是被装满了。寒冷的夜里，你会想起这件事。|今年你什么也拿不出来。罐子还是被装满了。寒冷的夜里，你会想起这件事。}\n\n@event life_shady_offer\n# en: A Quiet Job\ntitle [c6d337] = 一份安静的差事\n# en: A hooded figure behind the tavern: \"Deliver this envelope to the boathouse tonight. Don't open it. Thirty crowns.\" The envelope is warm. Under the hood, a glint of pince-nez, and teeth: this is one of Grisk's errands.\ntext [8351fe] = 酒馆后巷里一个戴兜帽的人：“今晚把这封信送到船屋。别拆开。三十克朗。”信封是温的。兜帽底下闪过一副夹鼻眼镜，还有牙齿：这是格里斯克的差事。\n# en: A hooded figure in an alley behind the village tavern holding out a sealed envelope, a purse visible in their other hand.\nscene [7d377f] = 镇上酒馆后的小巷里，一个戴兜帽的身影递出一封封好的信，另一只手里露出钱袋。\n# en: Refuse\nopt.left.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: The figure melts into the dark. The next morning, someone's boat is missing, and you wonder what was in the envelope, and whether you could have stopped it.\nopt.left.say.1 [b7d42e] = 那人融进了黑暗。第二天早上，有人的船不见了，你忍不住想那信封里装的是什么，你本来能不能拦住这件事。\n# en: Do the job\nopt.right.label [573204] = 接下这份差事\n# en: You make the drop. Bram is watching from the boathouse window and writes something down. On the jetty, a note in a neat hand: \"Good work. Midnight, any month. G.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [5ad49e] = 你送到了。布拉姆正从船屋的窗口看着，记下了什么。码头上有一张字迹工整的字条：“干得好。午夜，哪个月都行。G。”\n# en: You make the drop. Nobody sees. Thirty crowns, a cold feeling in your stomach, and a note on the jetty: \"Good work. Midnight, any month. G.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [9f54a8] = 你送到了。没人看见。三十克朗，胃里一点凉飕飕的感觉，还有码头上的一张字条：“干得好。午夜，哪个月都行。G。”\n\n@event life_food_poisoning\n# en: The Fish Was Off\ntitle [e4f066] = 鱼坏了\n# en: The fish pie at dinner was a mistake. Your stomach is staging a revolution.\ntext [cd4a4a] = 晚饭的鱼肉馅饼是个错误。你的胃正在闹革命。\n# en: A student curled on a dormitory bed clutching their stomach, a half-eaten fish pie on the bedside table.\nscene [4844ba] = 一个学生蜷缩在宿舍床上捂着肚子，床头柜上放着吃了一半的鱼肉馅饼。\n# en: Ride it out\nopt.left.label [43478c] = 硬扛过去\n# en: A miserable night. You never look at fish pie again.\nopt.left.say.1 [6ff02f] = 悲惨的一夜。你再也不看鱼肉馅饼一眼了。\n# en: Get a tonic from the infirmary\nopt.right.label [16a4f3] = 去医务室拿药\n# en: The tonic tastes like old pennies, but it works.\nopt.right.say.1 [f9e7fb] = 药尝起来像旧铜板，但是管用。\n\n@event life_self_doubt\n# en: Imposter\ntitle [ee3a4e] = 冒牌货\n# en: Everyone around you seems to belong here more than you do. They cast faster, learn quicker, laugh easier. Maybe the letter was a mistake.\ntext [5f044e] = 你身边的每个人似乎都比你更属于这里。他们施法更快、学得更快、笑得更自在。也许那封录取信是个错误。\n# en: A student sitting alone on the edge of a fountain at night, staring at their reflection broken up by ripples.\nscene [1842d0] = 夜里一个学生独自坐在喷泉边沿，盯着被涟漪打碎的倒影。\n# en: Write down everything you've learned\nopt.left.label [19e441] = 写下你学到的一切\n# en: The list is longer than you expected. Much longer. You pin it above your bed.\nopt.left.say.1 [c065df] = 清单比你想象的长。长得多。你把它钉在了床头。\n# en: Talk to someone about it\nopt.right.label [9fbd4a] = 找人聊聊\n# en: Your friend laughs — not at you. \"You? Everyone thinks you're the one who belongs.\" It helps more than you can say.\nopt.right.say.1 [eac20a] = 你的朋友笑了——不是在笑你。“你？大家都觉得你才是那个真正属于这里的人。”这比你能说出口的还要管用。\n# en: You talk to the night porter. He tells you everyone feels this way. You decide to believe him.\nopt.right.say.2 [bebf73] = 你和守夜门房聊了聊。他告诉你每个人都有这种感觉。你决定相信他。\n\n@event life_sleepwalking\n# en: Sleepwalking\ntitle [8fc4f7] = 梦游\n# en: You wake up on the battlements in your pyjamas, at the very edge, with no memory of how you got there. Your feet are wet with lake water.\ntext [8d1ce1] = 你醒来时穿着睡衣站在城垛上，就在最边缘，完全不记得自己是怎么上来的。你的脚被湖水打湿了。\n# en: A student in pyjamas standing on a moonlit battlement, eyes closed, one foot raised over the edge, an owl watching.\nscene [ee10e3] = 月光下一个穿睡衣的学生站在城垛上，双眼紧闭，一只脚悬在边缘外，一只猫头鹰在旁观。\n# en: Tell Sister Amaranth\nopt.left.label [3f4d80] = 告诉阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: A sleeping draught and a stern warning. It doesn't happen again.\nopt.left.say.1 [c0917f] = 一剂安眠药和一番严厉的警告。再也没发生过。\n# en: Keep it to yourself\nopt.right.label [09de43] = 自己藏着\n# en: It happens twice more. Each time you're facing the lake. Each time, you're a little closer to the water.\nopt.right.say.1 [bd1bd8] = 又发生了两次。每一次你都面朝湖水。每一次，你都离水更近一点。\n# en: It doesn't happen again. You start locking your door from the inside, just in case.\nopt.right.say.2 [3c9aa9] = 再也没发生过。你开始从里面反锁房门，以防万一。\n\n@event life_quiet_afternoon\n# en: A Quiet Afternoon\ntitle [4b76f8] = 安静的午后\n# en: For once, nothing is due, nobody needs you, and the sun is out. A whole afternoon, unclaimed.\ntext [135c6b] = 难得一次，没有作业要交，没有人需要你，太阳也出来了。一整个午后，无人认领。\n# en: A student lying in the long grass under an oak tree, a book open on their chest, clouds drifting above the towers.\nscene [9574c3] = 一个学生躺在橡树下的长草里，一本书摊在胸口，云朵飘过塔楼上空。\n# en: Nap under the oak tree\nopt.left.label [320d04] = 在橡树下打个盹\n# en: You wake to find a bird sitting on your book. It seems content. So are you.\nopt.left.say.1 [6fb673] = 你醒来时发现一只鸟落在你的书上。它看起来很满足。你也是。\n# en: Practise spells in the meadow\nopt.right.label [2c28fa] = 在草地上练习咒语\n# en: You make dandelion seeds dance in spirals for an hour. Nobody sees. It's perfect.\nopt.right.say.1 [2cbd71] = 你让蒲公英的种子跳了一个小时的螺旋舞。没人看见。完美。\n\n@event life_injury_stairs\n# en: The Trick Step\ntitle [cc11b3] = 陷阱台阶\n# en: The ninth step on the north stair is a trick step. Everyone knows. You forgot. Your leg sinks to the knee and the stair holds on.\ntext [9cc7ba] = 北楼梯的第九级是陷阱台阶。人人都知道。你忘了。你的腿陷到了膝盖，台阶紧紧咬住不放。\n# en: A stone staircase with one step glowing faintly, a student mid-fall, books flying everywhere.\nscene [02874d] = 一道石阶上有一级微微发光，一个学生正往下摔，书本飞得到处都是。\n# en: Wait for someone to help\nopt.left.label [8e8e6b] = 等人来帮忙\n# en: Forty minutes later, a first-year pulls you out. You're late for everything.\nopt.left.say.1 [be4901] = 四十分钟后，一个一年级生把你拔了出来。你什么都迟到了。\n# en: Wrench yourself free\nopt.right.label [ec2221] = 硬把腿拔出来\n# en: You pull free with a pop. Your shoe stays in the stair forever.\nopt.right.say.1 [652719] = 你“啵”的一声挣脱了。你的鞋永远留在了台阶里。\n# en: Something in your knee goes click. Not a good click.\nopt.right.say.2 [17703b] = 你膝盖里有什么东西咔哒响了一声。不是好的那种咔哒。\n\n@event life_lake_fever\n# en: Lake Fever Season\ntitle [5e2ca1] = 湖热病季节\n# en: Half your dormitory has Lake Fever. Noses are glowing blue. You feel a tickle in your throat.\ntext [77a4bc] = 你宿舍里一半的人得了湖热病。鼻子都泛着蓝光。你觉得喉咙有点痒。\n# en: A dormitory where half the beds hold sniffling students with faintly blue-glowing noses, tissues everywhere.\nscene [87b40c] = 宿舍里半数床位躺着吸鼻子的学生，鼻头泛着淡淡的蓝光，纸巾扔得到处都是。\n# en: Dose up on Maud's tea\nopt.left.label [f3b875] = 猛灌莫德的茶\n# en: Maud's lemon-and-thunder tea keeps you upright through the whole outbreak.\nopt.left.say.1 [b6b4d0] = 莫德那杯柠檬加雷声的茶让你挺过了整场流行病。\n# en: The tea from the kitchens isn't the same. Your nose starts glowing on Thursday.\nopt.left.say.2 [9b0859] = 厨房的茶不是一回事。周四你的鼻子开始发光了。\n# en: You dodge it. Somehow.\nopt.left.say.3 [61efce] = 你躲过去了。不知怎么做到的。\n# en: Keep going as normal\nopt.right.label [5303bd] = 照常生活\n# en: You keep going as normal for exactly two days.\nopt.right.say.1 [68c5c6] = 你照常生活了整整两天。\n# en: Iron constitution. You sail through. Everyone hates you a little.\nopt.right.say.2 [7e0fc1] = 铁打的体质。你安然度过。大家都有点恨你。\n\n@event life_first_snow\n# en: First Snow\ntitle [16a8b8] = 初雪\n# en: The first snow of winter falls overnight and the whole academy wakes up giddy. Classes are optional, officially. Unofficially, they're cancelled by snowball.\ntext [59861c] = 冬天的第一场雪在夜里落下，整个学院醒来时都兴奋不已。官方说法是课程自愿参加。非官方说法是课程被雪球取消了。\n# en: The academy courtyard blanketed in fresh snow, students mid-snowball fight, a snowman wearing a professor's hat.\nscene [c59afe] = 学院庭院铺满新雪，学生们正在打雪仗，一个雪人戴着教授的帽子。\n# en: Join the snowball war\nopt.left.label [dd3116] = 加入雪仗\n# en: You take a snowball to the face from the headmistress herself. Her aim is terrifying.\nopt.left.say.1 [286cf4] = 你脸上挨了一个雪球——是院长本人扔的。她的准头吓死人。\n# en: Your side wins. You build a snow-fort that lasts until Thawmoon.\nopt.left.say.2 [d4a0aa] = 你们这边赢了。你堆的雪堡一直立到了融雪月。\n# en: Hot chocolate by the fire\nopt.right.label [cba09c] = 在炉边喝热可可\n# en: You watch from the window, warm and smug, while the snowballs fly.\nopt.right.say.1 [da3e9c] = 你在窗边看着雪球横飞，暖和又得意。\n\n@event life_spring_cleaning\n# en: Spring Cleaning\ntitle [757d73] = 春季大扫除\n# en: Spring cleaning. The brooms are enchanted, but the enchantment needs supervising. Your dorm's brooms have formed a union.\ntext [cd3829] = 春季大扫除。扫帚都施了魔法，但魔法需要监督。你们宿舍的扫帚成立了工会。\n# en: A dormitory with every window thrown open, brooms sweeping by themselves, dust swirling in sunbeams.\nscene [d4aefd] = 宿舍里所有窗户都敞开着，扫帚自己扫着地，灰尘在阳光里打转。\n# en: Negotiate with the brooms\nopt.left.label [086b0c] = 和扫帚谈判\n# en: You agree to one day off a week and a polish. The dorm sparkles.\nopt.left.say.1 [627f3a] = 你同意每周给它们放一天假，外加一次抛光。宿舍焕然一新。\n# en: The brooms do not accept your terms. They sweep you into a cupboard.\nopt.left.say.2 [56e3e0] = 扫帚们不接受你的条件。它们把你扫进了柜子里。\n# en: Clean by hand\nopt.right.label [7a78f4] = 亲手打扫\n# en: An afternoon of scrubbing. It's oddly satisfying. You find three lost quills and a sandwich from Thawmoon.\nopt.right.say.1 [ffa004] = 擦洗了一个下午。出奇地让人满足。你找到了三支丢失的羽毛笔和一个融雪月的三明治。\n\n@event life_summer_heat\n# en: The Heat\ntitle [24ddd6] = 酷暑\n# en: The heat is unbearable. Even the gargoyles are sweating. The lake is crowded, the library stifling, the dormitories ovens.\ntext [b86aba] = 热得让人受不了。连石像鬼都在冒汗。湖边挤满了人，图书馆闷得透不过气，宿舍就是烤炉。\n# en: Students lying flat on the cool stone floor of a shaded colloquy, fanning themselves with exam papers.\nscene [ecf9e7] = 学生们平躺在阴凉讲堂冰凉的石地板上，用考卷扇着风。\n# en: Cast a cooling charm on your room\nopt.left.label [71928c] = 给房间施个降温咒\n# en: Your room becomes an icebox. People pay to visit. You let them for free. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [4b7a2e] = 你的房间变成了冰窖。有人付钱来参观。你让他们免费进来。大部分人。\n# en: You make it colder. Briefly. Then hotter. Then it rains inside.\nopt.left.say.2 [fdb685] = 你让它变冷了。短暂地。然后变得更热了。然后屋里下起了雨。\n# en: Sleep on the roof\nopt.right.label [3e5cc0] = 睡在屋顶上\n# en: You sleep under the stars with half the dormitory. It's the best idea anyone's had all year.\nopt.right.say.1 [d71668] = 你和半个宿舍的人一起睡在星空下。这是今年所有人想出来的最好的主意。\n\n@event life_harvest_festival\n# en: The Harvest Feast\ntitle [d8f2a6] = 丰收宴\n# en: The Harvest Feast: pies the size of cartwheels, cider, and {&a pumpkin carved to look like the headmistress, which she pretends not to notice.|a pumpkin carved to look like Professor Thorne this year, which he pretends not to notice, badly.|a turnip carved to look like the headmistress, by popular demand. She is seen smiling at it.}\ntext [e6b42d] = 丰收宴：车轮那么大的馅饼，苹果酒，还有{&一个刻成院长模样的南瓜——她假装没注意到。|今年刻成索恩教授模样的南瓜——他假装没注意到，装得很差。|应大家要求，一个刻成院长模样的芜菁。有人看见她对着它微笑。}\n# en: Long tables groaning under pies and roast vegetables, candles floating above, a giant pumpkin carved like the headmistress.\nscene [749624] = 长桌上堆满了馅饼和烤蔬菜，蜡烛浮在上方，一个巨大的南瓜被刻成了院长的模样。\n# en: Eat until you can't move\nopt.left.label [36bf14] = 吃到动不了为止\n# en: {&You eat three helpings of everything. You're carried back to the dorm by friends. Worth it.|You pace yourself this year, and still lose to the pies. Worth it.|You win the cider-tasting by not falling over. Worth it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [186b8b] = {&每样东西你都吃了三份。朋友们把你抬回了宿舍。值了。|今年你控制了节奏，结果还是败给了馅饼。值了。|你靠着没摔倒赢了苹果酒品鉴。值了。}\n# en: Help in the kitchens for extra pay\nopt.right.label [9e76c8] = 去厨房帮忙赚外快\n# en: {&You serve pie to the entire academy. The cooks slip you coins and a whole pie of your own.|The cooks remember you and put you on gravy, a promotion. Coins, and a pie of your own.|You carve the pumpkin yourself this year. The cooks pay you double in pie and the usual in coins.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b6d05c] = {&你给全院的人端馅饼。厨师们悄悄塞给你一些硬币，还有一整个属于你的馅饼。|厨师们还记得你，让你负责浇肉汁——算是升职了。硬币，外加一个属于你的馅饼。|今年南瓜是你亲手刻的。厨师们付了你双份的馅饼和照常的硬币。}\n\n@event life_rival_academy\n# en: Visitors From Thornwick\ntitle [ac57b9] = 荆木来客\n# en: A delegation from Thornwick Academy is visiting. Their students are smug, polished and very loudly unimpressed with Lumenhold's plumbing. {met(tamsin): One of them is scanning the crowd with a letter in their hand, and you know the handwriting.|}\ntext [061f04] = 荆木学院的代表团来访。他们的学生傲慢、体面，而且非常大声地对流明堡的水管表示不屑。{met(tamsin): 其中一人手里捏着一封信，在人群里张望，你认得那笔迹。|}\n# en: A delegation of students in green robes marching through the gates, noses raised, a Lumenhold crowd watching suspiciously.\nscene [78c6a2] = 一队身穿绿袍的学生昂首阔步地走进校门，流明堡的人群怀疑地注视着他们。\n# en: Be a gracious host\nopt.left.label [071642] = 做个大方的东道主\n# en: Tamsin finds you before you find them, and is exactly like their letters, only faster. You show them the observatory. They admit, grudgingly, that it's better than theirs.\nopt.left.say.1 [d7a689] = 塔姆辛先找到了你，和信里一模一样，只是语速更快。你带 TA 参观了观星台。TA 不情不愿地承认，这比他们的好。\n# en: You show them the observatory. One of them admits, grudgingly, that it's better than theirs.\nopt.left.say.2 [a32053] = 你带他们参观了观星台。其中一人不情不愿地承认，这比他们的好。\n# en: Their illusion professor has come with them, and you recognise him at once, from a hundred goats in a hundred margins, and have to look at the floor. Tamsin is no help at all.\nopt.left.say.3 [d639e5] = 他们的幻术教授也跟着来了，你一眼就认出了他，在一百页页边上见过一百只山羊，只好低头盯着地板。塔姆辛一点忙也帮不上。\n# en: Defend Lumenhold's honour\nopt.right.label [cfa83c] = 捍卫流明堡的荣誉\n# en: You challenge their best to a friendly duel and win. The Thornwick students are quieter at dinner.\nopt.right.say.1 [291905] = 你向他们最强的人发起友谊决斗，并且赢了。晚饭时荆木的学生安静多了。\n# en: You challenge their best to a friendly duel and lose, quite unfriendly. Thornwick will be insufferable.\nopt.right.say.2 [ad3104] = 你向他们最强的人发起友谊决斗，然后输得一点也不友谊。荆木的人会嚣张得让人受不了了。\n\n@event life_lost_firstyear\n# en: A Lost First-Year\ntitle [ed808e] = 迷路的一年级生\n# en: A tiny first-year is standing in the middle of the corridor, clutching an upside-down timetable and trying very hard not to cry.\ntext [a1cbbc] = 一个小个子一年级生站在走廊中间，攥着一张拿倒了的课表，拼命忍着不哭。\n# en: A tiny first-year in enormous robes standing in the middle of a junction of five identical corridors, clutching a timetable upside down.\nscene [6a5ef7] = 一个穿着宽大长袍的小个子一年级生站在五条一模一样的走廊交汇处，倒拿着课表。\n# en: Point the way and hurry on\nopt.left.label [ee0d43] = 指个路就赶紧走\n# en: You point. They go the wrong way anyway. You hope they're all right.\nopt.left.say.1 [f7d5b2] = 你指了路。对方还是走错了。你希望对方没事。\n# en: Walk them to class\nopt.right.label [e53c7b] = 送对方去教室\n# en: You walk them all the way and they talk the entire time. You're late, but they've decided you're their hero.\nopt.right.say.1 [344963] = 你一路把对方送到，对方一路说个不停。你迟到了，但对方已经认定你是自己的英雄。\n\n@event life_frog_escape\n# en: The Frogs Are Out\ntitle [abdd0d] = 青蛙跑出来了\n# en: Someone knocked over the frog crate in Potions. Thirty frogs are loose. One has climbed into the professor's hat. He has not noticed yet.\ntext [0c896d] = 有人在魔药课上打翻了青蛙箱。三十只青蛙跑了出来。一只爬进了教授的帽子里。他还没发现。\n# en: A potions classroom overrun by dozens of hopping green frogs, one sitting smugly on the professor's desk.\nscene [8a2633] = 魔药教室里到处是蹦跳的绿青蛙，一只得意地蹲在教授的讲台上。\n# en: Help round them up\nopt.left.label [d47151] = 帮忙把它们抓回来\n# en: You catch eleven, a personal best. The professor finds the hat frog at dinner. You were not involved.\nopt.left.say.1 [87225e] = 你抓了十一只，个人最佳纪录。教授在晚饭时发现了帽子里的那只。与你无关。\n# en: Quietly let a few escape out the window\nopt.right.label [73e9bc] = 悄悄放几只从窗户逃走\n# en: Five frogs hop to freedom in the lake. You feel like a liberator.\nopt.right.say.1 [076f02] = 五只青蛙跳进湖里重获自由。你觉得自己像个解放者。\n\n@event life_fortune_teller\n# en: The Village Fortune-Teller\ntitle [203728] = 镇上的算命婆\n# en: In the village, an old fortune-teller beckons you into her tent. \"Eight crowns for a good future, dearie. The bad ones I tell for free.\"\ntext [a3e3a4] = 在镇上，一个算命的老婆婆招手让你进她的帐篷。“八克朗算个好未来，孩子。坏的我免费说。”\n# en: A beaded doorway into a tiny dim tent, an old woman with a crystal ball and too many rings beckoning.\nscene [0e06ff] = 一顶昏暗小帐篷的珠帘门口，一个戴着太多戒指、捧着水晶球的老妇人在招手。\n# en: Decline politely\nopt.left.label [c5820d] = 礼貌地谢绝\n# en: \"Suit yourself,\" she calls after you. \"You will meet a tall stranger.\" You already have. Several. It's an academy.\nopt.left.say.1 [97b3f6] = “随你，”她在你身后喊，“你会遇到一个高个子陌生人。”你已经遇到了。好几个。这是所学院。\n# en: \"Beware of water,\" she calls after you, free of charge. She won't say more. You think about the lake a lot.\nopt.left.say.2 [96d8c7] = “小心水，”她在你身后喊，免费的。她不肯再多说。你常常想起那片湖。\n# en: Pay eight crowns\nopt.right.label [196642] = 付八克朗\n# en: Your future is wonderful. She's very specific. You are fairly sure she's making it up, and you don't care.\nopt.right.say.1 [7d6032] = 你的未来美好极了。她说得非常具体。你很确定她是在瞎编，而你不在乎。\n\n@event life_volunteer\n# en: Clean-Up Duty\ntitle [2e9a08] = 清理值日\n# en: After the storm, the grounds are a wreck. Volunteers needed to help clear branches and rebuild the hen-house. No pay. Lots of mud.\ntext [540f81] = 风暴过后，校园一片狼藉。需要志愿者帮忙清理断枝、重建鸡舍。没有报酬。满地泥巴。\n# en: Students in rolled-up sleeves raking leaves on the great lawn, a groundskeeper directing them with a pitchfork.\nscene [5de831] = 卷起袖子的学生们在大草坪上耙落叶，一位园丁拿着草叉指挥。\n# en: Volunteer\nopt.left.label [39a371] = 报名志愿\n# en: A long day of mud and laughter. An old woman in a straw hat brings everyone cocoa and remembers your name.\nopt.left.say.1 [e39352] = 漫长的一天，满身泥巴和笑声。一位戴草帽的老妇人给每个人送来了热可可，还记住了你的名字。\n# en: Stay in and study\nopt.right.label [6d8b57] = 留在屋里学习\n# en: You study through the shouting and hammering outside. The hen-house is very nice when it's done.\nopt.right.say.1 [1e9737] = 你在外面的喧闹和敲打声中学习。鸡舍建好后很漂亮。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/miniplots_2026_10.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/miniplots_2026_10.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@thread missed_round\n# en: The missed round\nname [5ab68d] = 漏掉的巡房\n# en: Sister Amaranth never misses a round. One night her lamp stayed dark.\npremise [ce64fd] = 阿玛兰斯修女从不漏掉一次巡房。可有一夜，她的灯一直没亮。\n# en: You went looking for Sister Amaranth when her lamp stayed dark, and found her grey with Lake Fever.\njournal.1 [1c10bd] = 她的灯一直没亮，你去找阿玛兰斯修女，发现她染了湖热病，脸色发灰。\n# en: You fetched the headmistress. Sister Amaranth didn't speak to you for a week, then told you to run faster next time.\njournal.2 [b89e22] = 你去叫了院长。阿玛兰斯修女一个星期没跟你说话，然后吩咐你下次跑快点。\n# en: You kept her secret and did the rounds she called out from her chair.\njournal.3 [7ec1be] = 你替她保守秘密，照着她坐在椅子上报出的吩咐去巡房。\n# en: You covered eight beds on your own, one remedy at a time.\njournal.4 [a807a6] = 你一个人照看了八张病床，一剂药一剂药地来。\n# en: Bed six owned up: a flat rock, a dare, and three friends coughing in secret. You brought them all down.\njournal.5 [edd4b8] = 六号床说了实话：一块平石头、一个赌约，还有三个偷偷咳嗽的朋友。你把她们都领下了楼。\n# en: You treated bed six for the chill she claimed, and her fever took its time.\njournal.6 [eb8937] = 你照她说的着凉给六号床开了药，她的烧拖了好些天才退。\n# en: Sister Amaranth came back a week too soon. You did the round with her, and she let you.\njournal.7 [f01ee9] = 阿玛兰斯修女早回来了一个星期。你陪她巡了房，她也由着你。\n\n@event infirmary_missed_round\n# en: The Lamp That Went Out\ntitle [6678b7] = 熄灭的灯\n# en: The fever charm over the far bed has burned down to a red glow. It should have been turned an hour ago. Sister Amaranth does every round herself, and has never once missed one. Her lamp is dark.\ntext [980ef1] = 最里面那张床上方的退热符已经烧成一点暗红。一个钟头前就该转了。阿玛兰斯修女每一轮巡房都亲自去，从没漏过一次。她的灯是暗的。\n# en: A dim infirmary ward at evening, a fever charm over the far bed glowing a dull red, a healer's desk lamp unlit.\nscene [6a0e30] = 黄昏时昏暗的医务室病房，最里面那张床上方的退热符泛着暗红的光，医师桌上的灯没有点。\n# en: Go and find her\nopt.left.label [061e72] = 去找她\n# en: You go looking through the cold back corridor in your shirtsleeves. The evening tray is still on the ward table, every bottle full. Her office door, at the back, is open a crack, and dark.\nopt.left.say.1 [b39fdc] = 你只穿着衬衫，穿过冰冷的后走廊去找。晚间的药盘还搁在病房桌上，每个瓶子都是满的。后头她办公室的门开着一条缝，里面黑着。\n# en: It isn't your ward\nopt.right.label [c6562a] = 这不是你的病房\n# en: You tell yourself she's only late. On your way out, the patient in the far bed is still awake, watching the dark.\nopt.right.say.1 [a9f3b8] = 你告诉自己她只是来晚了。出门时，最里面那张床的病人还醒着，盯着黑暗。\n\n@event infirmary_sister_fevered\n# en: The Healer's Own Fever\ntitle [3f2185] = 医师自己的高烧\n# en: You find her in the little office behind the ward, upright in her chair, a cold compress on her own neck and the patient list in her lap. Her face is grey. \"Lake Fever,\" she says. \"From bed six. It's nothing. Go to bed.\"\ntext [14ad94] = 你在病房后面那间小办公室里找到她。她在椅子上坐得笔直，自己脖子上敷着冷毛巾，腿上摊着病人名单。她脸色发灰。“湖热病，”她说，“六号床传的。没什么。去睡觉。”\n# en: A small office behind a hospital ward, a grey-faced healer upright in her chair with a compress on her neck and a list of patients in her lap.\nscene [3c4215] = 病房后面的一间小办公室，面色发灰的医师在椅子上坐得笔直，脖子上敷着毛巾，腿上放着病人名单。\n# en: Fetch the Headmistress\nopt.left.label [f62300] = 去叫院长\n# en: The headmistress comes in her dressing gown, and two healers from the village by morning. Sister Amaranth doesn't speak to you for a week. Then she thanks you, as an instruction: \"Next time, run faster.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [fc5764] = 院长穿着睡袍赶来，天亮前村里又来了两位医师。阿玛兰斯修女整整一周不跟你说话。然后她谢了你，用的是吩咐的口气：“下次跑快点。”\n# en: Do the rounds she dictates\nopt.right.label [069257] = 照她口述去巡房\n# en: \"Bed two, the blue bottle, half a spoon. Bed four, turn the charm. Bed six…\" She stops. \"Bed six will lie to you. Don't believe her.\" You do the rounds until midnight.\nopt.right.say.1 [080508] = “二号床，蓝瓶子，半勺。四号床，转符。六号床……”她顿了一下，“六号床会骗你。别信她。”你一直巡到半夜。\n\n@event infirmary_covering_rounds\n# en: Eight Beds, One of You\ntitle [49dfde] = 八张床，一个你\n# en: {flag(rounds_secret): Sister Amaranth still can't stand, and is still giving orders from her chair.|The village healers are run off their feet, and Sister Amaranth, propped up in her chair, is giving them orders too.} Eight beds, eight remedies, one of you. A second-year with a burned hand wants to know if it will scar.\ntext [a5133b] = {flag(rounds_secret): 阿玛兰斯修女还是站不起来，还是坐在椅子上发号施令。|村里来的医师忙得脚不沾地，阿玛兰斯修女靠在椅子上，连他们也一起指挥。} 八张床，八种药，只有你一个人。一个手被烫伤的二年级生想知道会不会留疤。\n# en: A row of infirmary beds at evening, a student in rolled-up sleeves measuring a remedy while a healer gives orders from a chair.\nscene [856bc9] = 黄昏的一排病床，一个挽着袖子的学生正在量药，一位医师坐在椅子上指挥。\n# en: Do every bed properly\nopt.left.label [b70095] = 每张床都照顾周全\n# en: Every bed, every spoon, every charm on the hour. The burned hand won't scar. At midnight the chair says, \"Bed three. Well done,\" and then you hear her fall asleep sitting up.\nopt.left.say.1 [4e9ba5] = 每张床、每一勺、每道符都准点照料到。烫伤不会留疤。半夜，椅子上传来一句：“三号床，做得好。”接着你就听见她坐着睡着了。\n# en: You mix up beds three and five. Nobody notices but her. She makes you recite every dose on the ward back to her, with your eyes shut, before she lets you sleep.\nopt.left.say.2 [0a4e69] = 你把三号床和五号床弄混了。除了她谁都没发现。她让你闭着眼，把病房里每一剂药量都背给她听，背完才放你去睡。\n# en: Do the urgent ones, then revise\nopt.right.label [7b2a0a] = 先顾急的，再去复习\n# en: You see to the burns and the fevers and leave the rest for the morning. From the chair comes a small, disappointed silence.\nopt.right.say.1 [469acd] = 你处理好烫伤和高烧，其余的留到早上。椅子那边传来一阵小小的、失望的沉默。\n\n@event infirmary_bed_six\n# en: Bed Six's Story\ntitle [b331ff] = 六号床的说法\n# en: Bed six is a second-year with Lake Fever, blue to the ears, who swears she never went near the lake. \"I fell asleep in the bath,\" she says. From her chair, Sister Amaranth raises one eyebrow and keeps it there. The fever isn't breaking. A cure for a bath is not a cure for the lake.\ntext [0fe3df] = 六号床是个得了湖热病的二年级生，冻得耳朵发蓝，却发誓自己从没靠近过湖。“我是在浴缸里睡着了，”她说。阿玛兰斯修女在椅子上挑起一边眉毛，一直没放下来。烧退不下去。治浴缸的药，治不了湖。\n# en: A shivering second-year in an infirmary bed with blue-tinged ears, insisting on something, while a healer watches sceptically from a chair across the ward.\nscene [8f759c] = 医务室病床上一个瑟瑟发抖、耳朵泛蓝的二年级生正在坚持什么，病房另一头椅子上的医师满脸怀疑地看着。\n# en: Get the true story out of her\nopt.left.label [cf4525] = 套出她的实话\n# en: It comes out in a rush: the flat rock, a dare, four of them. The other three are in her dormitory, coughing into their scarves so nobody will know. You fetch them down one by one. By midnight the ward is full, and all four fevers are breaking.\nopt.left.say.1 [230782] = 实话一股脑儿涌了出来：那块平顶石，一个赌约，一共四个人。另外三个还躲在她们宿舍里，捂着围巾咳嗽，免得被人发现。你一个一个把她们领下来。到半夜病房住满了，四个人的烧都在退。\n# en: She sticks to the bath. You treat her for the lake anyway, on Sister Amaranth's say-so, and on Thursday three more second-years turn up blue to the ears.\nopt.left.say.2 [32d29b] = 她咬死了浴缸的说法。你还是照阿玛兰斯修女的吩咐按湖热病治她。到了星期四，又有三个二年级生冻得耳朵发蓝地找上门来。\n# en: Treat what she tells you\nopt.right.label [67acaa] = 照她说的治\n# en: You dose her for a chill and get an early night. The fever takes three days longer than it should, and the chair across the ward watches every one of them.\nopt.right.say.1 [e3829f] = 你按着凉给她配了药，自己早早睡下。烧比该退的晚了三天，病房那头的椅子一天不落地全看在眼里。\n\n@event infirmary_own_prescription\n# en: Her Own Prescription\ntitle [b2ec64] = 她自己的药方\n# en: Sister Amaranth is up a week too soon, grey at the edges, taking the tray out for the evening round as if nothing had happened. At bed two she sways and puts a hand on the rail. {flag(bed_six_truth): Four second-years sit up in their beds to watch.|The whole ward goes quiet.} Everyone looks at you.\ntext [7ded39] = 阿玛兰斯修女早了一个星期就下了床，脸色还泛着灰，端起药盘出去做晚间巡房，好像什么都没发生过。走到二号床时她晃了一下，伸手扶住床栏。{flag(bed_six_truth): 四个二年级生在床上坐起来看着。|整个病房一下子安静了。}所有人都看向你。\n# en: A grey-faced healer in her apron holding a tray of tonics at an infirmary bedside, swaying slightly, while a student reaches to take the tray.\nscene [e6bf63] = 一位脸色发灰、系着围裙的医师端着一盘药剂站在医务室床边，身子微微摇晃，一个学生伸手去接药盘。\n# en: \"Bed.\"\nopt.left.label [09b6cc] = “上床。”\n# en: You take the tray out of her hands and say it the way she says it to everyone else. She looks at you for a long moment. Then she goes to bed. You do the round. In the morning there's a card on the tray, in her square hand: \"Bed four needs less.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [2d0661] = 你从她手里接过药盘，用她对别人说这话的那种口气说了出来。她盯着你看了很久，然后回去躺下了。巡房你来做。第二天早上，药盘上多了一张卡片，是她方方正正的字：“四号床减量。”\n# en: Walk the round beside her\nopt.right.label [2bda60] = 陪她一起巡房\n# en: You don't argue. You carry the tray and have each bottle ready before she reaches for it. At the last bed she sits down on the end of it, and lets you measure out her own dose. \"Half a spoon,\" she says. \"I'm not a horse.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [447498] = 你没有争。你端着药盘，在她伸手之前就把每个瓶子递到她手边。到最后一张床，她在床尾坐下，让你给她自己量了一剂药。“半勺，”她说，“我又不是马。”\n\n@thread red_ink\n# en: Red ink\nname [77bee0] = 红墨水\n# en: Someone unsigned keeps marking your essays in red, and they're right.\npremise [27df6f] = 有人不署名，总在你的论文上用红墨水批改，而且批得都对。\n# en: Someone marked your essay in red ink, unsigned, and was right. You rewrote it and left it on the stair.\njournal.1 [d71b70] = 有人用红墨水批改了你的论文，没有署名，而且批得对。你重写了一遍，把它留在楼梯上。\n# en: The ink asked why you want to be good at this. You answered in the margin.\njournal.2 [658ef1] = 红墨水问你为什么想把这件事做好。你在页边写下了回答。\n# en: You hid on the landing and saw who took the essay: a first-year in robes two sizes too big.\njournal.3 [7f3754] = 你躲在楼梯平台上，看见了来取论文的人：一个长袍大了两号的一年级生。\n# en: The red ink is hers: a first-year who can't say a word in front of a room. She has a seminar on Thursday.\njournal.4 [2f09dd] = 红墨水是她的：一个在一屋子人面前一个字也说不出的一年级生。她星期四有一场研讨课。\n# en: On Thursday she spoke, all the way to the end.\njournal.5 [c4d0f0] = 星期四，她开口了，一直讲到最后。\n# en: On Thursday she sat down without reading. Her red ink is still the best marking you've ever had.\njournal.6 [408a1c] = 星期四，她没有念就坐下了。她的红墨水批注，至今仍是你得到过的最好的批改。\n\n@event lecture_tower_red_ink\n# en: Two Sets of Marks\ntitle [8b53b5] = 两种批改\n# en: Your returned essay has two sets of marks. The marker's, in polite pencil: \"Good.\" And another, in red ink and unsigned, in a small round hand, savaging every paragraph and fixing three of them. The last line reads: \"You can do better than good. Paragraph four proves it.\"\ntext [90c611] = 你拿回来的论文上有两种批改。批改老师用的是客气的铅笔：“好。”另一种是红墨水，没有署名，字小而圆，把每一段都批得体无完肤，还顺手改好了三段。最后一行写着：“你能写得比‘好’更好。第四段就是证明。”\n# en: A student on a spiral stair reading an essay covered in neat pencil and, beneath it, furious corrections in red ink.\nscene [580e72] = 一个学生坐在螺旋楼梯上，读着一篇论文，上面是工整的铅笔批注，底下是怒气冲冲的红墨水修改。\n# en: Rewrite it properly\nopt.left.label [ddefc9] = 认真重写\n# en: You rewrite it twice. On the third morning you leave it on the second-floor stair, where you found the first. By evening it's gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [874517] = 你重写了两遍。第三天早上，你把它放在二楼楼梯上，就是你捡到第一份的地方。到傍晚，它不见了。\n# en: Bin it\nopt.right.label [0a2a07] = 扔掉\n# en: Good is good. You drop the red pages in the scorched-room bin, and feel a little foolish about how much you want to fish them out.\nopt.right.say.1 [20fc67] = “好”就是好。你把那几页红字丢进烧焦教室的废纸篓，又有点难为情地发现，自己多想把它们捡回来。\n\n@event lecture_tower_red_ink_reply\n# en: A Question in the Margin\ntitle [a0e86a] = 页边的问题\n# en: The essay is back on the stair, bleeding red. Most of it is about your argument. At the bottom, smaller: \"Why do you want to be good at this?\" It's the first question the ink has asked.\ntext [630977] = 论文又回到了楼梯上，满纸红字。大部分是在说你的论证。最底下，字小了些：“你为什么想把这件事做好？”这是红墨水第一次提问。\n# en: An essay left on a stone stair, its pages bleeding red ink, a single question written small at the bottom of the last page.\nscene [bdf66c] = 一篇论文放在石阶上，纸页上满是红墨水，最后一页底部用小字写着一个问题。\n# en: Answer it honestly in the margin\nopt.left.label [e7dc4e] = 在页边老实作答\n# en: You write three lines you'd never say aloud, and leave the essay on the stair. It's gone by noon. You spend the afternoon wishing you'd written two.\nopt.left.say.1 [9ace38] = 你写下三行永远不会说出口的话，把论文留在楼梯上。中午前它就不见了。你一下午都在后悔，应该只写两行。\n# en: Wait on the stair to see who takes it\nopt.right.label [0b86d8] = 守在楼梯看谁来取\n# en: You hide on the landing above. A first-year in robes two sizes too big gathers up the pages, reads the first line right there on the step, and makes a small, furious noise.\nopt.right.say.1 [1ab338] = 你躲在上一层的平台上。一个穿着大两号袍子的一年级生把纸页收起来，就站在台阶上读了第一行，发出一声又小又气的哼声。\n# en: You fall asleep on the stair. When you wake the essay is gone, and there's a note on your chest in red: \"Paragraph six. Also, you snore.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [5b6506] = 你在楼梯上睡着了。醒来时论文不见了，胸口上放着一张红字纸条：“第六段。还有，你打呼噜。”\n\n@event lecture_tower_red_ink_front_row\n# en: The Front Row\ntitle [9d64e7] = 第一排\n# en: You cut through the first-years' lecture on your way somewhere else. In the front row, alone, sits a first-year with red ink on her fingers. The lecturer asks her a question. She opens her mouth, and nothing comes out, and the hall waits until she looks at the floor. {flag(red_ink_glimpse): The robes are the ones from the stair, two sizes too big.|Her notes, you can see from the aisle, are perfect.}\ntext [04a185] = 你抄近路穿过一年级生的讲堂。第一排独自坐着一个一年级生，手指上沾着红墨水。讲师向她提问。她张开嘴，却一个字也说不出来，整个讲堂就那么等着，直到她低头看着地板。{flag(red_ink_glimpse): 那件袍子就是楼梯上那件，大了两号。|从过道上你就能看见，她的笔记完美无缺。}\n# en: A lecture hall of first-years, one small student alone in the front row with red ink on her fingers, looking down at a page of perfect notes while the lecturer waits.\nscene [8bd5f5] = 一间坐满一年级生的讲堂，一个瘦小的学生独自坐在第一排，手指沾着红墨水，低头看着一页完美的笔记，讲师在等她回答。\n# en: Sit by her afterwards, and write\nopt.left.label [3d6382] = 散场后坐到她旁边，用笔说话\n# en: You sit down beside her after the hall empties and slide your last essay across, without a word. She writes on it for twenty minutes without looking up. At the bottom: \"Seminar on Thursday. I have to speak. I can't.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [8c43b8] = 讲堂散空后，你在她旁边坐下，一言不发地把你最近的论文推过去。她低着头在上面写了二十分钟。最底下写着：“星期四有研讨课。我得发言。我做不到。”\n# en: Leave her the secret\nopt.right.label [56b4e0] = 替她守着这个秘密\n# en: You walk on, and leave your next essay on the stair as usual. It comes back with the usual red, and one line more at the bottom: \"Seminar on Thursday. I have to speak. Please don't come.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [531188] = 你走开了，照旧把下一篇论文放在楼梯上。它回来时还是满纸红字，最底下多了一行：“星期四有研讨课。我得发言。请别来。”\n\n@event lecture_tower_red_ink_seminar\n# en: Thursday's Seminar\ntitle [ffda16] = 星期四的研讨课\n# en: Thursday. The first-year stands at the seminar lectern with her paper in both hands and no voice at all. A dozen students wait. The lecturer looks at the clock. Her eyes find you at the back of the room.\ntext [111352] = 星期四。那个一年级生站在研讨课的讲台前，双手攥着讲稿，一点声音也发不出来。十几个学生在等。讲师看了看钟。她的目光在教室后排找到了你。\n# en: A small seminar room, a first-year frozen at a lectern holding her paper in both hands, a dozen students waiting, one older student at the back half out of their seat.\nscene [c9437a] = 一间小研讨室，一个一年级生僵在讲台前，双手攥着讲稿，十几个学生在等，后排一个高年级生半站起身。\n# en: Stand up and argue with her paper\nopt.left.label [785e58] = 站起来和她的论文辩论\n# en: You stand and say her fourth paragraph is wrong. It isn't, and she knows it, and she's so annoyed she forgets to be frightened. She takes your objection apart for ten minutes. The room applauds, mostly for her.\nopt.left.say.1 [593b7e] = 你站起来说她的第四段是错的。其实没错，她也知道，结果她气得忘了害怕，花了十分钟把你的反驳拆得七零八落。全场鼓掌，大半是为她。\n# en: You stand and object, and your objection is bad, and she's too polite to say so out loud. She sits down without reading. Your essay comes back that week with one line of red: \"Good try. Bad argument.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [7d93e6] = 你站起来提出异议，可你的异议很烂，她又太客气，没当面说破。她没读就坐下了。那个星期你的论文发回来时，上面只有一行红字：“试得好。论证烂。”\n# en: Offer to read it for her\nopt.right.label [dde38f] = 提出替她念\n# en: You walk to the front and hold out your hand. She shakes her head, then gives you the first page. You read the first paragraph aloud. She takes the second page back, and reads the rest herself, quietly, all the way to the end.\nopt.right.say.1 [dbadbf] = 你走到前面，伸出手。她摇摇头，然后把第一页递给了你。你大声念完第一段。她把第二页拿了回去，自己轻声念完了剩下的，一直念到最后。\n\n@thread late_bloomer\n# en: The sweet shop's daughter\nname [056316] = 糖果铺的女儿\n# en: At home, the sweet-shop daughter has fire in her hands and nobody to teach her.\npremise [4170c4] = 家乡糖果铺的女儿手里有火，却没有人教她。\n# en: The sweet-shop daughter's sugar pan boiled on a cold stove. You taught her to breathe the heat down.\njournal.1 [99ec4e] = 糖果铺女儿的糖锅在冷灶上沸了起来。你教她用呼吸把热压下去。\n# en: Out past the fields, she learned to hold a flame the size of a fingernail.\njournal.2 [01aa4d] = 在田地尽头，她学会了托住一朵指甲盖大的火苗。\n# en: You promised her father you'd ask at the castle.\njournal.3 [6addf1] = 你答应她父亲，会去城堡里问问。\n# en: Her father asked what all that fire was for. You told him you didn't know.\njournal.4 [cf91b6] = 她父亲问，那么多火是做什么用的。你告诉他，你也不知道。\n# en: She bought the charm seller's grey glass bead, and the pans stayed cold.\njournal.5 [71ea36] = 她买下了护符贩子那颗灰玻璃珠，糖锅从此一直是凉的。\n# en: You told her the charm was only glass. She kept her coins.\njournal.6 [39a79c] = 你告诉她那护符只是玻璃。她把钱收了回去。\n# en: Frost ran from her feet across the square and caught a carter's arm in the trough. You got him out.\njournal.7 [9b7881] = 寒霜从她脚下蔓过广场，把一个车夫的胳膊冻在了水槽里。你把他救了出来。\n# en: You wrote to the castle about her.\njournal.8 [2a2851] = 你给城堡写了信，讲了她的事。\n# en: You left her your first practice candle, burned down to a stub.\njournal.9 [54dca9] = 你把自己第一根练习用的蜡烛留给了她，已经烧得只剩一截。\n\n@event home_market_late_bloomer\n# en: The Cold Stove\ntitle [ab26e7] = 冷灶\n# en: The sweet shop is the one thing in town that hasn't changed. Then the owner's daughter, a year or two older than you, bangs down a ladle and swears, and the sugar pan on the cold stove boils over into caramel. There is no fire under it. Her father looks at you, then at your castle robes. \"You're the one who'd know,\" he says quietly.\ntext [80d1ef] = 镇上只有这家糖果铺一点没变。接着，店主的女儿——比你大一两岁——把勺子往台上一摔，骂了一句，冷灶上的糖锅就溢成了焦糖。锅底下根本没有火。她父亲看看你，又看看你身上的城堡学院袍。“你是懂这个的人，”他低声说。\n# en: The back of a small sweet shop, a copper sugar pan boiling over into dark caramel on a stove with no fire in it, a young woman staring at her own hands.\nscene [ecd843] = 一间小糖果铺的后屋，一口铜糖锅在没有生火的灶上溢出深色的焦糖，一个年轻女人盯着自己的双手。\n# en: Show her how to breathe it down\nopt.left.label [1a87b0] = 教她把它压下去\n# en: You show her the first thing anyone at the castle learns: breathe in for four, hold the heat, let it go for four. The pan stops seething. She looks at her hands as if they belonged to someone else.\nopt.left.say.1 [673b25] = 你教她城堡里每个人学的第一件事：吸气数四下，把热憋住，再数四下放出去。糖锅不再翻腾了。她看着自己的手，好像那是别人的手。\n# en: \"It happens. It'll settle.\"\nopt.right.label [6cc646] = “常有的事。会过去的。”\n# en: Her father nods, glad to be told so. Behind him she scrapes the burned sugar out of the pan, very carefully, and doesn't go near the stove again.\nopt.right.say.1 [d7c158] = 她父亲点点头，很高兴听到这句话。在他身后，她小心翼翼地把烧焦的糖刮出锅，再也没靠近灶台。\n\n@event home_fields_bloomer_lessons\n# en: Where Nothing Can Catch\ntitle [aa3840] = 什么都烧不着的地方\n# en: She's waiting past the last of the long rows, where the ground is all stones and nothing can catch, with a candle stub in each pocket. \"Teach me something,\" she says. \"Properly. Before you go back.\"\ntext [e00f96] = 她等在最后一垄地的外头，那里满地石头，什么都烧不着，她两个口袋里各揣着一截蜡烛头。“教我点什么，”她说，“正经教。趁你回去之前。”\n# en: A young woman and a student in castle robes on a bare, stony patch past the last field row at sunset, the young woman holding a candle stub at arm's length, its flame far too tall.\nscene [dde514] = 日落时分，最后一垄田外一块光秃秃的石头地上，一个年轻女人和一个穿城堡学院袍的学生，年轻女人伸直手臂举着一截蜡烛头，火苗蹿得太高。\n# en: Teach her to keep a flame small\nopt.left.label [04fe90] = 教她把火苗压小\n# en: By sunset she can hold a flame the size of a fingernail for ten breaths. She counts every one of them out loud.\nopt.left.say.1 [19772d] = 到日落时，她已经能让指甲盖大小的火苗稳住十次呼吸。她每一下都大声数出来。\n# en: The candle goes off like a firework. You both lie in the grass laughing until the farmer shouts. \"Next time,\" she says.\nopt.left.say.2 [de9e7a] = 蜡烛像烟花一样炸开了。你们俩躺在草地上笑，直到农夫开骂。“下次吧。”她说。\n# en: Spend the afternoon at home\nopt.right.label [177620] = 下午留在家里\n# en: You promise her next time. She shrugs, the way people do when they've stopped expecting things.\nopt.right.say.1 [f41615] = 你答应她下次。她耸耸肩，就像人们已经不再指望什么时那样。\n\n@event home_cottage_bloomer_father\n# en: Sweets on the Step\ntitle [a4badc] = 台阶上的糖\n# en: Her father is at the door with a paper bag of the sweets you liked when you were small. He doesn't come in. \"They take them young, at the castle, don't they,\" he says. \"She's past it. So what is it for, all that fire, in a sweet shop?\"\ntext [1d4d9f] = 她父亲站在门口，拿着一小纸袋你小时候爱吃的糖。他没有进来。“城堡收的都是年纪小的，是吧，”他说，“她已经过了那个年纪。那一身的火，在糖果铺里有什么用？”\n# en: A worried shopkeeper at a cottage door holding a small paper bag of sweets, not stepping inside.\nscene [9f38ed] = 一个忧心忡忡的店主站在小屋门口，拿着一小纸袋糖，没有进门。\n# en: Tell him the truth\nopt.left.label [48bf7e] = 对他说实话\n# en: \"I don't know what it's for,\" you tell him. \"I don't think anyone does, at her age.\" He thanks you for not lying, and leaves the sweets on the step.\nopt.left.say.1 [98beb1] = “我不知道有什么用，”你告诉他，“到了她这个年纪，我想谁也不知道。”他谢谢你没有骗他，把糖留在了台阶上。\n# en: Promise to ask at the castle\nopt.right.label [aafec0] = 答应去城堡问问\n# en: His face does something complicated. \"Would you,\" he says, not quite a question. You hear yourself promise. The sweets taste exactly as they used to.\nopt.right.say.1 [e74743] = 他的脸上掠过很复杂的神情。“你会吗。”他说，不太像是在问。你听见自己答应了。那些糖的味道和从前一模一样。\n\n@event home_market_bloomer_charm\n# en: A Lid on the Pot\ntitle [f09a76] = 锅上的盖子\n# en: In the market you find her at the charm seller's stall. He's holding up a grey glass bead on a cord. \"Lumenhold-blessed,\" he's saying. \"Smothers it, like a lid on a pot. No more flares, no more fuss.\" It's plain glass; you can see that from here. She's counting out her savings.\ntext [7577dc] = 你在集市上卖护身符的人的摊子前找到她。他正举着一颗系着绳子的灰玻璃珠。“流明堡加持过的，”他说，“能把它闷住，就像给锅盖上盖子。再不会冒火，再不会出乱子。”那就是一颗普通玻璃珠，你站在这儿都看得出来。她正在数自己的积蓄。\n# en: A market charm seller holding up a grey glass bead on a cord to a young woman counting coins from her apron.\nscene [97e8d2] = 集市上卖护身符的人举着一颗系着绳的灰色玻璃珠，一个年轻女人正从围裙里数出硬币。\n# en: Let her believe in it\nopt.left.label [f3c641] = 让她信下去\n# en: You say nothing. She buys it and puts it on. It's only glass, but she believes in it, and the sugar pans stay cold all week. She looks lighter, and smaller.\nopt.left.say.1 [b3f436] = 你什么都没说。她买下它戴上了。那不过是玻璃，可她信它，糖锅一整个星期都是冷的。她看上去轻松了些，也小了些。\n# en: Tell her it's only glass\nopt.right.label [2d22ae] = 告诉她那只是玻璃\n# en: \"It's glass,\" you tell her. \"You did the hard part yourself, out past the fields.\" The charm seller gives you the look of a man revising his prices. She puts her coins back in her apron, slowly.\nopt.right.say.1 [45bba8] = “那是玻璃，”你告诉她，“最难的那部分，是你自己在田那头做到的。”卖护身符的人看你的眼神，像是在重新盘算价钱。她慢慢把硬币放回了围裙口袋。\n# en: She buys it anyway. \"Easy for you,\" she says, not unkindly. \"You get to go back.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [d6500c] = 她还是买了。“你说得轻巧，”她说，并没有恶意，“你还能回去。”\n\n@event home_market_bloomer_ice\n# en: Ice in the Square\ntitle [44b78b] = 广场上的冰\n# en: Not ten minutes later, a shout from the square. The horse trough has frozen solid, with a carter's arm in it to the elbow, and she's standing beside it white with panic. Frost is creeping across the cobbles from her feet.\ntext [6aaab7] = 不到十分钟，广场上传来一声大喊。饮马槽冻得结结实实，一个车夫的胳膊冻在里面，一直冻到手肘。她站在旁边，吓得脸色发白。霜正从她脚下爬过石板路。\n# en: A market-square horse trough frozen solid around a carter's arm, a panicking young woman beside it and frost spreading across the cobbles.\nscene [4e5996] = 集市广场上的饮马槽冻得结结实实，把一个车夫的胳膊封在里面，一个惊慌的年轻女人站在一旁，霜在石板路上蔓延。\n# en: Break the ice by hand\nopt.left.label [fe2922] = 用手砸开冰\n# en: You hammer at the ice with a cobble until it gives with a crack like a door. The carter pulls his arm free, swearing, mostly at the trough.\nopt.left.say.1 [280b1c] = 你抄起一块铺路石砸冰，直到它像一扇门一样咔嚓一声裂开。车夫抽出胳膊，骂骂咧咧，主要是在骂那个饮马槽。\n# en: You get him free in the end, but the ice takes the skin off your knuckles doing it. You'll feel that trough for days.\nopt.left.say.2 [f780c2] = 你最后还是把他弄了出来，可冰把你的指节磨掉了一层皮。那个饮马槽你得疼上好几天。\n# en: Talk her down so it melts\nopt.right.label [2bd1f0] = 安抚她让冰化开\n# en: \"Four in. Hold. Four out.\" Her breath slows, and the ice goes grey, then soft, then water. The carter stares at his arm, and then at her.\nopt.right.say.1 [62e526] = “吸气四下。憋住。呼气四下。”她的呼吸慢下来，冰变灰，变软，化成了水。车夫盯着自己的胳膊，又盯着她。\n# en: It takes an hour, three kettles from the inn carried at a run, and your own hands scalded red holding the carter's arm in the steam. He will tell this story for years.\nopt.right.say.2 [1ef41e] = 花了一个钟头，从旅店一路小跑提来三壶热水，你自己的手在蒸汽里托着车夫的胳膊，被烫得通红。这事他会讲上好多年。\n\n@event home_cottage_bloomer_letter\n# en: A Flame That Stays\ntitle [62c792] = 不灭的火苗\n# en: {flag(bloomer_damped): She comes by one evening, the grey bead on its cord. \"I know it's only glass,\" she says. \"I keep it in my pocket anyway.\" She lights a candle with her finger, small and steady, to show you.|She comes by one evening with a candle she lit at noon and has kept burning, small and steady, all day. \"Look,\" she says. \"It stays.\"}\ntext [de4168] = {flag(bloomer_damped): 一天傍晚她来了，那颗灰珠子系在绳上。“我知道那只是玻璃，”她说，“可我还是揣在口袋里。”她用手指点燃一支蜡烛给你看，火苗小而稳。|一天傍晚她来了，手里拿着一支中午点着、一直烧到现在的蜡烛，火苗小而稳。“你看，”她说，“它不灭。”}\n# en: A young woman at a cottage table at evening, a small steady candle flame burning on the tip of her finger.\nscene [a44cc7] = 傍晚，一个年轻女人坐在小屋的桌旁，指尖上燃着一朵小小的、稳定的烛火。\n# en: Write to the castle for her\nopt.left.label [3d556e] = 替她给城堡写信\n# en: {flag(bloomer_promise): You keep your promise to her father and write to the castle that night|You write to the castle that night}: what she can do, what she learned with no teacher but you, and that some people come late to things. You pay the postage; she walks it to the post office herself.\nopt.left.say.1 [e70f80] = {flag(bloomer_promise): 你遵守了对她父亲的承诺，当晚就给城堡写了信|当晚你给城堡写了信}：她能做什么，她在除了你之外没有老师的情况下学会了什么，还有，有些人就是来得晚一些。邮费你付了；信是她自己走去邮局寄的。\n# en: Leave her your practice candle\nopt.right.label [e7919c] = 把你的练习蜡烛留给她\n# en: {flag(bloomer_promise): You never did ask at the castle. Instead you leave her|You leave her} the practice candle you've had since your first week, the one you learned on and still revise by, burned down to a stub. You'll be reading by the window for a while. A month later a letter comes with a drawing of the stub, still lit.\nopt.right.say.1 [9ca8a2] = {flag(bloomer_promise): 你到底没去城堡问。你把|你把}入学第一个星期就开始用的那支练习蜡烛留给了她，你就是用它学会的，至今复习还靠它，如今只剩一截蜡烛头。接下来一阵子，你得靠着窗户读书了。一个月后来了一封信，里面画着那截蜡烛头，还亮着。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/night.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/night.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event night_lead_in\n# en: An Unsigned Note\ntitle [f1e5f3] = 一张没有署名的字条\n# en: A note under your door, unsigned: \"The castle has a second timetable. It starts at midnight. Curfew is a suggestion made by people who go to bed early.\" In the corner, a map no bigger than a thumbnail, showing six doors that are open when they shouldn't be.\ntext [e2406e] = 门下塞着一张没有署名的字条：“城堡有第二张时间表，从午夜开始。宵禁只是早睡的人提的建议。”角落里画着一张不比拇指大的地图，标出六扇本不该开着的门。\n# en: A folded note slipped under a dormitory door at night, unsigned, a tiny silver map drawn in one corner.\nscene [c4d793] = 夜里塞进宿舍门缝的一张折好的字条，没有署名，角落里画着一张小小的银色地图。\n# en: Burn it\nopt.left.label [162285] = 烧掉它\n# en: You hold it to the candle. The map in the corner is the last thing to burn, and you could swear it rearranged itself first.\nopt.left.say.1 [23cef0] = 你把它凑到烛火上。角落的地图最后才烧着，你敢发誓它在那之前先自己重新排列了一番。\n# en: Keep it\nopt.right.label [87c2f5] = 留着它\n# en: You fold it into your book. Some nights you take it out and look at the six doors.\nopt.right.say.1 [6468db] = 你把它折起来夹进书里。有些夜晚，你会拿出来看看那六扇门。\n\n@event night_out\n# en: After Curfew\ntitle [0fdaf7] = 宵禁之后\n# en: {month == 11: Starfall is close, and the sky over the towers is already restless.|{month == 12: The last of Starfall is still falling over the towers, slow and silver.|{month == 4 or month == 10: Exams soon. Half the windows on your corridor are still lit.|{season == \"winter\": {~Frost on the window, and the corridors are colder than the snow.|Snow on the sills; the tower creaks like a ship.|Ice in the water jug, and the tower silent with snow.}|{season == \"summer\": {~The night is warm and the castle's windows are all open.|Moths at the window, and somebody laughing on the lawn.|The night is short and never quite dark.}|{~The castle settles for the night, beam by beam.|Rain on the leads, and a gargoyle gargling it.|The wind finds every keyhole in the tower.}}}}}} {has(watched): Bram does his rounds earlier these nights. You've learned the sound of his boots.|{health < 30: Every bone you own would like to stay in bed.|{mood < 25: You can't sleep anyway.|{gold < 10: Your purse is light, and some doors after midnight pay.|{bonds(partner) > 0: {~A note under your door, in a hand you know: \"Roof?\"|You could be on the observatory roof with someone right now.|Somewhere across the castle, someone you love is also awake.}|{final_year: {~Your last year. The castle at night won't be yours much longer.|The doors you know by heart will open for someone else next year.|Next year someone else will lie awake in this bed.}|{~Somewhere a door that should be locked isn't.|Down in the courtyard, a lantern goes out on purpose.|Someone has left the servants' stair unbolted again.}}}}}}}\ntext [254c91] = {month == 11: 星坠节快到了，塔楼上方的天空已经躁动起来。|{month == 12: 星坠节的余光仍缓缓落在塔楼上方，银白而缓慢。|{month == 4 or month == 10: 快考试了。你们走廊上一半的窗户还亮着。|{season == \"winter\": {~窗上结了霜，走廊比雪还冷。|窗台上积着雪；塔楼像一艘船一样嘎吱作响。|水罐里结了冰，塔楼被雪捂得悄无声息。}|{season == \"summer\": {~夜很暖，城堡的窗户全开着。|飞蛾扑着窗户，有人在草坪上笑。|夜很短，始终不曾全黑。}|{~城堡一根梁接一根梁地沉入夜色。|雨打在屋顶的铅皮上，一只石像鬼在咕噜咕噜地漱口。|风钻进了塔楼里的每一个锁眼。}}}}}}{has(watched): 这些夜里布拉姆巡夜更早了。你已经听熟了他的靴子声。|{health < 30: 你身上的每一根骨头都想待在床上。|{mood < 25: 反正你也睡不着。|{gold < 10: 你的钱袋很轻，而午夜之后，有些门是给钱的。|{bonds(partner) > 0: {~门缝底下塞进一张字条，字迹你认得：“屋顶？”|此刻你本可以和某个人待在观星台的屋顶上。|城堡另一头，你爱的人也还醒着。}|{final_year: {~这是你的最后一年。夜里的城堡很快就不再属于你了。|你烂熟于心的那些门，明年会为别人打开。|明年，会有别人在这张床上睁着眼睡不着。}|{~某处一扇本该锁着的门没有锁。|庭院里，一盏灯笼被人故意熄灭了。|又有人没闩上仆役楼梯的门。}}}}}}}\n# en: A dormitory window at midnight, the castle dark beyond it, one lantern moving along a far battlement.\nscene [0df004] = 午夜的宿舍窗户，窗外城堡一片漆黑，远处城垛上有一盏提灯在移动。\n# en: Sleep\nopt.left.label [a62ca8] = 睡觉\n# en: A night's rest.\nopt.left.hint [dfe4c4] = 好好睡一晚。\n# en: {~You blow out the candle and let the castle keep its secrets for one more night.|Sleep. The night can wait. It always does.|You pull the blanket up and listen to the castle until you're asleep.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5e979b] = {~你吹灭蜡烛，让城堡再守住一晚它的秘密。|睡吧。夜晚可以等。它向来会等。|你把毯子拉上来，听着城堡的声音，直到睡着。}\n# en: Go out\nopt.right.label [1d9fab] = 出去\n# en: No rest tonight.\nopt.right.hint [abd6a5] = 今晚不休息。\n# en: {~You wait for the last footsteps to fade, then ease the door open.|Boots in hand, you take the servants' stair.|You slip out while the castle is still settling, and nobody hears.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c72d45] = {~你等最后的脚步声消失，然后轻轻推开门。|你拎着靴子，走仆人用的楼梯。|你趁城堡还在安顿下来时溜了出去，谁也没听见。}\n\n@event night_sleep\n# en: Lights Out\ntitle [794e40] = 熄灯\n# en: {has(locked_out): {~The lock on your door turns at curfew now, from the outside. The castle has decided where you sleep.|Bram's boots go past your door twice before midnight. There's nowhere to go, so you go to bed.|Locked in for the night. You can hear the castle's second timetable starting without you.}|{break and has(plan_home): {~Your old bed at home, too short now, and the smell of the kitchen still in the walls.|At home the night is so quiet you can hear the kettle cooling downstairs.|Your own pillow, your own ceiling, and nobody's footsteps but family's.}|{has(plan_internship): {~Your lodgings are small, the bed is narrow, and you're too tired to mind.|The city doesn't stop at night, but you do.|Tomorrow's work is already laid out on the chair. The bed is the only other furniture.}|{off_campus: {~A borrowed bed, a strange ceiling, and the day finally over.|The night is quiet here, far from the towers.|You fall asleep before you've finished deciding to.}|{season == \"winter\": {~Frost on the window, and the blankets pulled up to your ears.|Snow on the sills; the tower creaks like a ship.|Ice in the water jug, and the warmest place in the castle is your bed.}|{~The castle settles for the night, beam by beam.|Rain on the leads, and a gargoyle gargling it.|The day is done, and the candle is low.}}}}}}\ntext [cfe782] = {has(locked_out): {~现在每到宵禁，你的房门就会从外面锁上。城堡替你决定了在哪儿睡。|午夜前，布拉姆的靴子声两次经过你的门口。无处可去，你只好上床。|今晚被锁在屋里。你能听见城堡的第二张时间表在没有你的情况下开始了。}|{break and has(plan_home): {~家里你那张旧床，如今已经嫌短了，墙里还留着厨房的味道。|家里的夜那么安静，你能听见楼下的水壶慢慢变凉。|自己的枕头，自己的天花板，除了家人，没有别人的脚步声。}|{has(plan_internship): {~你的住处很小，床很窄，可你累得顾不上计较。|城市夜里不会停下，但你会。|明天的活儿已经摆在椅子上了。除了床，那是屋里唯一的家具。}|{off_campus: {~借来的床，陌生的天花板，这一天总算结束了。|远离七塔的地方，夜很安静。|你还没想好要不要睡，就已经睡着了。}|{season == \"winter\": {~窗上结了霜，被子一直拉到耳朵。|窗台上积着雪，塔楼像船一样吱呀作响。|水罐里结了冰，城堡里最暖和的地方就是你的床。}|{~城堡一根梁一根梁地安顿下来。|雨落在铅皮屋顶上，一只滴水兽咕噜咕噜地漱着口。|一天过完了，蜡烛也快燃尽了。}}}}}}\n# en: A narrow bed under a slanting ceiling, a candle just blown out, its thread of smoke curling up in the moonlight.\nscene [a78cfa] = 斜顶下的一张窄床，刚吹灭的蜡烛冒出一缕细烟，在月光里袅袅升起。\n# en: Sleep\nopt.ok.label [a62ca8] = 睡觉\n# en: A night's rest.\nopt.ok.hint [dfe4c4] = 好好睡一晚。\n# en: {break and has(plan_home): {~You sleep the way you only sleep at home: all night, without a single dream you remember.|Somebody looks in on you before they go to bed, as if you were still small. You pretend to be asleep, and then you are.|You wake late, to bread and the smell of the garden. Home mends things.}|{~You blow out the candle, and the day goes with it.|You're asleep before the candle has stopped smoking.|You lie awake a little, listening, and then you don't.}}\nopt.ok.say.1 [84548d] = {break and has(plan_home): {~你睡得只有在家才睡得那样沉：一整夜，一个梦也不记得。|睡前有人进来看了你一眼，好像你还是个孩子。你假装睡着了，然后就真的睡着了。|你很晚才醒，闻到面包和花园的味道。家能治好很多事。}|{~你吹灭蜡烛，这一天也跟着熄了。|蜡烛还没停止冒烟，你就已经睡着了。|你醒着躺了一会儿，听着，然后就不再听了。}}\n\n@event night_suspicion\n# en: A Word in the Morning\ntitle [42e6c8] = 早上的一句话\n# en: {has(watched): Bram is waiting at the foot of the stair, and this time he has a notebook open. \"Twice,\" he says. \"I did warn you.\"|Bram is waiting at the foot of the stair. \"Someone was out last night,\" he says, looking at nobody in particular, which is to say at you.}\ntext [04ce49] = {has(watched): 布拉姆等在楼梯脚下，这次手里摊着一本笔记本。“两次了，”他说，“我警告过你。”|布拉姆等在楼梯脚下。“昨晚有人出去了。”他说着，没看任何人——也就是说，看着你。}\n# en: A prefect waiting at the foot of a dormitory stair at breakfast time, arms folded, a small notebook open.\nscene [e8d3a9] = 早饭时分，一名级长抱着胳膊等在宿舍楼梯脚下，摊开着一本小笔记本。\n# en: Deny everything\nopt.left.label [b2b240] = 一口否认\n# en: A month of detention, and three of a door that is checked at midnight. Bram writes it all down, neatly.\nopt.left.say.1 [297ea2] = 一个月的禁闭，外加三个月午夜有人查的门。布拉姆把这一切工工整整地记了下来。\n# en: \"I'll be keeping an eye on you.\" He means it literally.\nopt.left.say.2 [772653] = “我会盯着你的。”他是字面意思。\n# en: Own up\nopt.right.label [3a0128] = 承认\n# en: A month of detention, and three of a door that is checked at midnight. Bram writes it all down, neatly.\nopt.right.say.1 [297ea2] = 一个月的禁闭，外加三个月午夜有人查的门。布拉姆把这一切工工整整地记了下来。\n# en: \"I'll be keeping an eye on you.\" He means it literally.\nopt.right.say.2 [772653] = “我会盯着你的。”他是字面意思。\n\n@event midnight_newt_cards\n# en: Upstairs at the Newt\ntitle [fe653a] = 镀金蝾螈的楼上\n# en: Upstairs at the Gilded Newt, after hours, the real game starts. The stakes are higher than anything downstairs, and nobody asks your name.\ntext [ef03fa] = 打烊之后，镀金蝾螈酒馆楼上，真正的牌局才开始。赌注比楼下任何一桌都大，也没人问你的名字。\n# en: A smoky upstairs room at a tavern after hours, a card table under one lamp, coins stacked high.\nscene [e9f7a8] = 打烊后酒馆楼上烟雾缭绕的房间，一盏灯下一张牌桌，硬币堆得老高。\n# en: Play a few hands\nopt.left.label [efba54] = 玩几把\n# en: {~You leave with more than you came with and a few new enemies who smile too much.|Luck, nerve and one well-timed sigh. The pot is yours.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6262fc] = {~你走的时候比来的时候多了钱，也多了几个笑得太多的新仇人。|运气、胆量，加上一声恰到好处的叹气。彩池归你了。}\n# en: {~The cards go cold, and so does your purse. On the stair down, a face from the castle turns away a little too fast.|You lose your stake and walk back in the dark, poorer, wiser and not sure you went unseen.}\nopt.left.say.2 [10d4e5] = {~牌运转冷，你的钱袋也跟着凉了。下楼时，一张城堡里的面孔转开得有点太快。|你输掉了赌本，摸黑走回去，钱少了，人精了，也说不准有没有被人看见。}\n# en: Watch, then leave\nopt.right.label [908b14] = 看一会儿就走\n# en: {~You watch one hand, see someone lose a familiar on a bluff, and leave before you're tempted.|You stay long enough to learn the rules, and leave before they learn you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b1d011] = {~你看了一把，看见有人虚张声势输掉了自己的使魔，趁自己还没心动就走了。|你待到学会了规矩，又在他们认清你之前离开了。}\n\n@event midnight_boathouse_ring\n# en: The Ring Under the Boathouse\ntitle [31b605] = 船屋下的擂台\n# en: {Under the boathouse someone has chalked a ring on the flagstones. Inside it, wands out, anything goes, and nobody here reports a forbidden spell. What it does to you is your own affair.|The chalk ring under the boathouse again, scorched from last time. The student holding the money knows your face.|The ring is busier than ever, the air sharp with spent spells, and the money-holder nods to you like a regular.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Tonight a slate by the steps reads LAST STANDING: whoever steps in fights until they drop or have beaten four.|\"Next,\" says the student holding the money, looking at you. Win, and you can fight on for a bigger purse.}\ntext [e1eae4] = {船屋底下，有人在石板上用粉笔画了个圈。圈里魔杖出鞘，怎么打都行，这里也没人会举报禁咒。它对你做了什么，是你自己的事。|船屋底下的粉笔圈又画好了，上回的焦痕还在。管钱的学生认得你的脸。|擂台比以往都热闹，空气里满是施过的咒语的焦味，管钱的学生朝你点点头，像对熟客一样。} {flag(ring_last_standing): 今晚台阶旁的石板上写着“站到最后”：谁进场，就要一直打到倒下，或者连赢四场。|“下一个。”管钱的学生看着你说。赢了，你就能接着打，钱袋更大。}\n# en: A cellar under a boathouse, lanterns hung from the beams, a crowd around two wizards in a chalk ring, sparks in the air, coins changing hands.\nscene [ebc7a5] = 船屋下的地窖，梁上挂着提灯，人群围着粉笔圈里的两名巫师，火花四溅，硬币在人手间传递。\n# en: Step into the ring\nopt.left.label [bf566a] = 走进圈里\n# en: Four bouts at most, each harder and richer. Nothing cast here is reported.\nopt.left.hint [bb2a10] = 最多四场，一场比一场难，一场比一场肥。在这里施的咒没人举报。\n# en: {Your opponent's wand skitters out of the chalk, and the ring's respect comes your way, which is worth less and lasts longer.|Your opponent yields, smoking at the cuffs. Somebody whistles, and the money-holder looks at you: another?|You put your opponent down in the first exchange, and the crowd starts calling your name.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e0a35f] = {对手的魔杖滑出了粉笔圈，擂台的尊重归了你，后者不值钱，却更持久。|对手认输了，袖口还冒着烟。有人吹了声口哨，管钱的学生看着你：再来一场？|第一回合你就把对手撂倒了，人群开始喊你的名字。}\n# en: {You lose, and limp home singed past a window where a light is still burning.|A bruising loss, and a lantern somewhere on the path home. Maybe it saw you; maybe not.|You go down to a first-year with a very fast wand. The crowd enjoys it more than you do.}\nopt.left.say.2 [dbee47] = {你输了，带着一身焦痕一瘸一拐地回去，路过一扇还亮着灯的窗。|输得鼻青脸肿，回去的路上远处有一盏提灯。也许它看见了你，也许没有。|你栽在一个出杖极快的一年级生手里。人群比你开心多了。}\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.right.label [4bf72b] = 今晚算了\n# en: Watch, and bet if you like.\nopt.right.hint [6e4615] = 在旁边看，想押就押。\n# en: {You stay at the edge of the crowd and watch someone else's turn.|You shake your head. The money-holder shrugs and looks for somebody braver.|\"Not tonight,\" you say, and find a place on the cellar steps to watch.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6e2d17] = {你待在人群边上，看别人上场。|你摇摇头。管钱的学生耸耸肩，去找更勇敢的人。|“今晚算了。”你说，然后在地窖台阶上找了个位置看。}\n\n@event midnight_kitchen_raid\n# en: The Kitchens at Midnight\ntitle [174e5a] = 午夜的厨房\n# en: The refectory kitchens at midnight: banked fires, a larder that isn't locked, and the smell of tomorrow's bread already rising.\ntext [14230d] = 午夜的膳堂后厨：封好的炉火，一间没上锁的储藏室，明天的面包已经在发酵，香气飘了出来。\n# en: A dark castle kitchen at midnight, a student with a candle reaching into a larder, a cat watching from the hearth.\nscene [22b604] = 午夜昏暗的城堡厨房，一个学生举着蜡烛把手伸进储藏室，一只猫在炉边看着。\n# en: Fill your pockets\nopt.left.label [4d57c0] = 装满口袋\n# en: {~Bread, cheese and half a pie. Nobody counts the pies, you hope, and the scullery door creaks as you leave.|You eat by the banked fire with the kitchen cat. On your way out, a kitchen boy is up early. Did he see you?}\nopt.left.say.1 [e01feb] = {~面包、奶酪和半个馅饼。没人会数馅饼——但愿如此。你离开时，洗碗间的门吱呀响了一声。|你和厨房的猫一起在封好的炉火边吃东西。出门时，一个厨房小工起得很早。他看见你了吗？}\n# en: Just warm yourself by the fire\nopt.right.label [9fd3db] = 只在火边暖暖身子\n# en: {~You sit by the embers until your hands stop hurting, and take nothing. The walk back is cold.|The kitchen cat sits with you. You leave the larder alone, feel strangely proud of it, and shiver all the way back to bed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ff8310] = {~你坐在余烬旁直到手不再疼，什么也没拿。回去的路很冷。|厨房的猫陪你坐着。你没碰储藏室，心里有种奇怪的自豪，一路哆嗦着回到床上。}\n\n@event midnight_restricted_stacks\n# en: The Restricted Stacks\ntitle [700d36] = 禁书区的书架\n# en: The Restricted Section at midnight. The chains on the shelves clink when nobody touches them. One book has been left open, as if for you.\ntext [a86ad6] = 午夜的禁书区。书架上的锁链在没人碰的时候叮当作响。有一本书摊开着，仿佛是为你留的。\n# en: A chained library section at midnight, a student reading by a stolen candle, shelves leaning in like listeners.\nscene [9d85ce] = 午夜被锁链锁住的藏书区，一个学生借着偷来的蜡烛读书，书架像听众一样倾身靠近。\n# en: Read the open book\nopt.left.label [5b587f] = 读那本摊开的书\n# en: {~Three pages before the book closes itself. You remember every word, which is worrying.|The book shows you a diagram you're not supposed to understand. You understand it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [133dfa] = {~读了三页，书就自己合上了。你记住了每一个字，这让人不安。|书给你看了一张你本不该看懂的图。你看懂了。}\n# en: {~The book slams shut and a lamp comes on two floors down. You're gone before it reaches the stair.|The pages scream, briefly. You leave quickly, and hope nobody heard.}\nopt.left.say.2 [4c4306] = {~书啪地合上，楼下两层亮起一盏灯。等它照到楼梯，你已经走了。|书页短促地尖叫了一声。你赶紧离开，但愿没人听见。}\n# en: Leave it open and go\nopt.right.label [a40ffc] = 让它开着，离开\n# en: {~You back away from the book. It seems disappointed.|You leave it open for whoever it was waiting for. It wasn't you, you decide.}\nopt.right.say.1 [43824e] = {~你从书前退开。它似乎很失望。|你让它摊着，留给它在等的人。你认定那不是你。}\n\n@event midnight_observatory_stars\n# en: Stars at Midnight\ntitle [aa78c1] = 午夜的星星\n# en: The observatory roof at midnight is the quietest place in the castle. {bonds(partner) > 0: You've brought someone.|The stars are very close tonight.} Nobody comes up here after curfew, mostly.\ntext [ef69f2] = 午夜的观星台屋顶是城堡里最安静的地方。{bonds(partner) > 0: 你带了个人来。|今晚星星离得很近。}宵禁之后，基本上没人上来。\n# en: The observatory roof at midnight, a student lying on the leads, the sky thick with stars.\nscene [1bfcc3] = 午夜的观星台屋顶，一个学生躺在铅皮屋面上，满天繁星。\n# en: Stay until the sky turns\nopt.left.label [6b603a] = 待到天色转亮\n# en: {~You count stars until you lose count, and then until the sky goes grey. You'll be tired. It was worth it.|A meteor, then another. You stay until the first bell.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8ea599] = {~你数星星数到数乱了，又一直数到天色发灰。你会很累。值得。|一颗流星，又一颗。你一直待到第一声钟响。}\n# en: An hour, then bed\nopt.right.label [d38b58] = 待一个钟头就睡\n# en: {~An hour of stars, and you're back in bed before anyone stirs, you think.|You slip back down the stair, the sky still in your eyes. A lantern turns a far corner, and you don't wait to see whose.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a31603] = {~看了一个钟头星星，你回到床上时还没人醒——你是这么想的。|你溜下楼梯，眼里还满是星空。远处拐角转过一盏提灯，你没等着看是谁的。}\n\n@event midnight_passage_mapping\n# en: The Passage After Dark\ntitle [8077de] = 入夜后的密道\n# en: Behind the third gargoyle the passage is busier at midnight than at noon. {met(corvin): Corvin's chalk marks are fresh.|Someone's chalk marks are fresh.} A draught comes from a direction that has no wall.\ntext [77c2e1] = 第三座石像鬼后面的密道，午夜比正午还热闹。{met(corvin): 科尔文的粉笔记号还是新的。|有人的粉笔记号还是新的。}一阵风从一个没有墙的方向吹来。\n# en: A secret passage behind a gargoyle at midnight, chalk marks on the walls, a candle guttering in a draught from nowhere.\nscene [e3c614] = 午夜石像鬼后的密道，墙上有粉笔记号，一支蜡烛在不知从哪来的风里摇曳。\n# en: Follow the draught\nopt.left.label [989dda] = 顺着风走\n# en: {~The draught leads you to a door you've never seen, and then, politely, closes it.|A new turning, a new stair. You chalk it for the map.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8bc3e0] = {~风把你领到一扇你从没见过的门前，然后礼貌地把它关上了。|一个新的拐角，一段新的楼梯。你用粉笔记下来，画进地图。}\n# en: {~After an hour of cold stone, the passage lets you out somewhere unexpected: the prefects' corridor, empty, you hope.|You get lost for an hour and come out in the wrong tower, cold and dusty, and not alone on the stair.}\nopt.left.say.2 [84c0e8] = {~在冰冷的石头间走了一个钟头后，密道把你放到了一个意想不到的地方：级长的走廊，但愿是空的。|你迷路了一个钟头，从错误的塔里钻出来，又冷又满身灰，楼梯上还不止你一个人。}\n# en: Mark it and go back\nopt.right.label [776869] = 做个记号就回去\n# en: {~You chalk an arrow at the turning and leave the rest for another night.|You mark it on your copy of the map and go back to bed, a little sorry.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c06aa7] = {~你在拐角处用粉笔画了个箭头，剩下的留给下一个夜晚。|你在自己那份地图上做了记号，回去睡觉，有点遗憾。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/origin_funding.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/origin_funding.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event noble_letter\n# en: A Letter from Home\ntitle [56bb53] = 家书\n# en: {month == 6: Before the spring term|Before the new year} a letter comes with your family's seal and a draft on the family bank. {flag(family_gift): {&Folded inside is a smaller note from your grandmother, and a little more money \"for something nice\".|There's an extra sum this time, marked \"a gift\", which in your family means it will be mentioned at dinner for a year.|Your father has added something on top, with a line about how proud he would be of better marks.}|{&The allowance, to the crown, and three pages about cousins who are doing very well.|The usual sum, and a reminder that the family name goes wherever you go.|The allowance, and a postscript asking whether you have met anyone suitable.}}\ntext [f5f6d8] = {month == 6: 春季学期开学前|新学年开始前}，一封盖着家族印章的信寄到了，里面夹着一张家族银行的汇票。{flag(family_gift): {&信里还折着一张祖母写的小纸条，外加一点钱，“买点好东西”。|这次多了一笔，标着“礼物”——在你们家，这意味着接下来一年的晚饭桌上都会被提起。|父亲在上面又添了一笔，附了一句：要是成绩再好些，他会多么骄傲。}|{&零花钱一个克朗不差，外加三页纸讲表亲们过得多么出色。|还是那个数目，外加一句提醒：家族的姓氏走到哪儿都跟着你。|零花钱，外加一句附言，问你有没有遇到合适的人。}}\n# en: A heavy envelope with a wax family seal on a writing desk, a bank draft half out of it.\nscene [72b255] = 书桌上一个厚重的信封，封着家族的蜡印，一张银行汇票露出一半。\n# en: Fold the letter away\nopt.ok.label [81ff5f] = 把信折好收起\n# en: {&You read it twice: once for the money, once for what it wants from you.|You put the draft in your purse and the letter in a drawer, and the letter is heavier.|You write back the same night: thank you, yes, of course, you'll try.}\nopt.ok.say.1 [704304] = {&你读了两遍：一遍为了钱，一遍为了信里对你的期望。|你把汇票放进钱袋，把信放进抽屉，而信要沉得多。|你当晚就回了信：谢谢，好的，当然，我会努力。}\n\n@event scholarship_award\n# en: The Bursary List\ntitle [a6c6d0] = 助学金名单\n# en: The bursary list {month == 6: for the spring term|for the new year} goes up outside the registrar's office. {term_gpa >= 3.5: {&Your name is near the top with a small star beside it: the full stipend, and a merit bonus.|The clerk hands you the envelope herself. The full stipend, and a little extra \"for distinction\".|Your name, the full sum, and the word \"Merit\" in red ink, which the clerk says she rarely gets to use.}|{term_gpa >= 3.0: {&Your name is there: the stipend is renewed for another term.|\"Renewed,\" says the list, in the clerk's tidy hand. You breathe out.|Your stipend stands for another term. The marks held, if only just.}|{term_gpa > 0: {&Your name is there with a line through the amount. Last term's marks fell below the 3.0 the Crown pays for, and the stipend waits until they come back up.|The clerk is apologetic: the Crown pays for marks of 3.0 and above, and last term yours were not. Next term, perhaps.|No stipend this term. Your marks dipped below what the Crown pays for, and the list says so in plain ink.}|{&Your name isn't on the list. No marks were recorded for you last term, and the Crown pays for marks.|The clerk checks twice. No grades last term means no stipend this one.|An empty line where your amount should be: the Crown pays for marks, and last term you had none.}}}}\ntext [2fe469] = {month == 6: 春季学期|新学年}的助学金名单贴在了教务处门外。{term_gpa >= 3.5: {&你的名字排在前头，旁边画着一颗小星：全额津贴，外加一笔奖学金。|教务员亲手把信封递给你。全额津贴，外加一点“以资嘉奖”。|你的名字、全额数目，还有用红墨水写的“优秀”二字——教务员说她难得用上这个词。}|{term_gpa >= 3.0: {&你的名字在上面：津贴续发一个学期。|名单上，教务员工整地写着“续发”。你松了口气。|你的津贴又保住了一个学期。成绩撑住了，虽然只是勉强。}|{term_gpa > 0: {&你的名字在上面，金额却被划掉了。上学期的成绩没达到王室资助所要求的 3.0，津贴要等成绩回来才会恢复。|教务员一脸歉意：王室只资助 3.0 及以上的成绩，而你上学期没有达到。下学期吧，也许。|这学期没有津贴。你的成绩掉到了王室资助的线下，名单上白纸黑字写着。}|{&名单上没有你的名字。上学期你没有任何成绩记录，而王室资助的是成绩。|教务员查了两遍。上学期没有成绩，这学期就没有津贴。|你的金额那一栏空着：王室资助的是成绩，而上学期你一门也没有。}}}}\n# en: A list of names pinned outside the registrar's office, a clerk's red ink beside some of them.\nscene [fa74fa] = 教务处门外钉着的一张名单，有些名字旁边写着教务员的红墨水字。\n# en: Read your line again\nopt.ok.label [7b4222] = 再读一遍你那一行\n# en: {term_gpa >= 3.0: {&You fold the envelope into your book and walk a little taller to breakfast.|Tuition, board and something left over. You allow yourself one good dinner.|You write home that the stipend held. Your family will read the letter aloud to the neighbours.}|{&You do the sums on the back of a lecture note, and they come out short. Work, then, or thinner meals.|You walk away from the list without looking back at it. Next term the marks will be better; they have to be.|You go straight to the library, which is free, and stay until it closes.}}\nopt.ok.say.1 [075262] = {term_gpa >= 3.0: {&你把信封夹进书里，走去吃早饭时腰板都挺直了些。|学费、膳宿，还能剩一点。你允许自己吃一顿好的。|你写信回家说津贴保住了。家里人会把信念给邻居们听。}|{&你在一张讲义背面算了算，钱不够。那就打工，或者吃得更省些。|你离开名单，没有回头看。下学期成绩会更好的；必须更好。|你径直去了图书馆——那里不要钱——一直待到闭馆。}}\n\n@event trading_report\n# en: The Company's Report\ntitle [79d431] = 商行的报告\n# en: {month == 6: The company's half-year report|The company's year-end report} arrives with a clerk's note on top. {flag(trade_good): {&The spice fleet came in early and whole. Your share is paid in full.|A good season: the northern warehouses sold out by midsummer. Your share comes with your mother's compliments.|Profit on every route but one. Your share is enclosed, and so is a list of what it cost.}|{&A ship lost off the southern coast, and a cargo of silk ruined by rain. There is no share this term.|The markets turned. The report is long and the envelope is empty: no share until trade recovers.|Pirates, tariffs and a warehouse fire, in that order. Your mother writes that the company cannot spare anything this term.}}\ntext [0265ca] = {month == 6: 商行的半年报告|商行的年终报告}寄到了，最上面是一位书记员的便条。{flag(trade_good): {&香料船队提早到港，完好无损。你的分红全额付清。|好年景：北方的货栈仲夏就卖空了。你的分红随信附上，还有母亲的问候。|除了一条航线，条条都赚了钱。你的分红附在里面，还有一张清单，写着这一切的代价。}|{&一艘船在南方海岸外沉没，一批丝绸被雨水泡坏。这学期没有分红。|行情变了。报告写得很长，信封却是空的：要等生意好转才有分红。|海盗、关税、一场货栈大火，依次而来。母亲写信说，商行这学期一分钱也挪不出来。}}\n# en: A merchant company's ledger report on thick paper, columns of figures, a bank draft clipped to the first page or not.\nscene [3a2c29] = 厚纸上的商行账目报告，一栏栏数字，第一页上别着一张银行汇票——或者没有。\n# en: Fold the report away\nopt.ok.label [452c3d] = 把报告折好收起\n# en: {flag(trade_good): {&You count it twice and lock it away. Next term's tuition is safe.|You read the figures like a story with a happy ending, and put the draft somewhere safe.|You think about what the good season cost someone else, and then about tuition, and put the draft away.}|{&You count what's in your purse instead. It will have to do.|You read the figures twice, as if they might change. They don't.|You write back that you understand, and then go and look at the job board.}}\nopt.ok.say.1 [8a9933] = {flag(trade_good): {&你数了两遍，锁了起来。下学期的学费有着落了。|你像读一个圆满结局的故事那样读那些数字，然后把汇票收好。|你想了想这个好年景让别人付出了什么，又想了想学费，然后把汇票收了起来。}|{&你转而数了数钱袋里的钱。只能将就了。|你把数字读了两遍，仿佛它们会变。它们没有。|你回信说你理解，然后去看了看招工告示板。}}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/people_others.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/people_others.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event kael_snowball\n# en: Snow in Thawmoon\ntitle [1b8dde] = 融雪月里的雪\n# en: It's snowing in the courtyard. Only in a small circle, around Kael, who is holding a snowball and looking at it as though he's never been allowed to throw one.\ntext [540758] = 庭院里在下雪。只在一小圈范围里，围着凯尔下。他手里捧着一个雪球，盯着它看，好像从来没被允许扔过雪球。\n# en: A courtyard where it is snowing in one small circle only, a boy in a fur coat standing at its centre holding a perfect snowball.\nscene [8ebdb4] = 庭院里只有一小圈地方在下雪，一个穿毛皮大衣的少年站在中央，捧着一个完美的雪球。\n# en: Leave him to it\nopt.left.label [b84f2c] = 让他自己待着\n# en: When you pass again, the circle of snow is gone, and there's a small, lopsided snowman in its place.\nopt.left.say.1 [631d77] = 你再经过时，那圈雪已经不见了，原地立着一个歪歪扭扭的小雪人。\n# en: Throw the first one\nopt.right.label [c2945e] = 先扔出第一个\n# en: Your snowball hits him in the ear. He stares. Then he laughs — a real laugh, startled out of him — and the snow triples.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c45fb] = 你的雪球砸中了他的耳朵。他愣愣地看着你。然后他笑了——一声真正的、被吓出来的笑——雪下得大了三倍。\n# en: Afterwards you take his hands to warm them, out of habit, and for once he doesn't tell you they're always cold. He just lets you.\nopt.right.say.2 [a3afa4] = 之后你习惯性地握住他的手给他暖着，这一次他没说它们总是冷的。他就那样让你握着。\n\n@event kael_cold_hands\n# en: Cold Hands\ntitle [a4a2d1] = 冰冷的手\n# en: Kael's hands are always cold. In the library tonight they're so cold the ink in his pen has frozen. He's trying to thaw it with his breath, embarrassed.\ntext [2a76e5] = 凯尔的手总是冰的。今晚在图书馆里，冷到他笔里的墨水都冻住了。他正难为情地往笔上哈气，想把它化开。\n# en: Two students sitting close in a library alcove, one holding the other's frost-white hand between both of theirs.\nscene [d7ab82] = 图书馆的壁龛里，两个学生挨坐在一起，一人用双手捧着另一人霜白的手。\n# en: Lend him your gloves\nopt.left.label [7bd15d] = 把手套借给他\n# en: He wears them for the rest of the term. They're far too small. He doesn't care. Your own fingers go numb on the walk back.\nopt.left.say.1 [6436b9] = 他戴了一整个学期。手套小得离谱。他不在乎。回去的路上，你自己的手指冻得发麻。\n# en: Warm his hands in yours\nopt.right.label [e4839d] = 用你的手暖他的手\n# en: He goes very still. Then, slowly, the frost on his fingertips melts. \"Nobody's ever been warm enough,\" he says quietly. \"Before.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [79ec72] = 他一动不动。然后，慢慢地，他指尖的霜融化了。“从来没有人足够温暖，”他轻声说，“在你之前。”\n\n@event kael_confession\n# en: Thaw\ntitle [448405] = 解冻\n# en: \"I was taught not to want things,\" Kael says. \"Wanting things is how they control you.\" Frost blooms across the window behind him. \"I want you. I'm sorry. I don't know what to do with it.\"\ntext [6c0866] = “我被教导不要想要任何东西，”凯尔说，“想要东西，他们就能控制你。”他身后的窗户上绽开了霜花。“我想要你。对不起。我不知道该拿它怎么办。”\n# en: Frost-ferns spreading across a dark window behind a pale boy who is, for once, looking directly at someone.\nscene [992624] = 一个苍白的少年身后，黑暗的窗上蔓延着霜蕨；这一次，他直视着某个人。\n# en: Gently say no\nopt.left.label [f46b92] = 温柔地拒绝\n# en: He nods, as if he expected it. \"Thank you for being honest. Nobody is.\" The frost on the window fades slowly.\nopt.left.say.1 [b81eae] = 他点点头，像是早有预料。“谢谢你的诚实。没有人诚实过。”窗上的霜慢慢褪去了。\n# en: Show him what to do with it\nopt.right.label [6ebbe7] = 告诉他该怎么办\n# en: You kiss him. Every window in the corridor frosts over at once, and then, very slowly, melts.\nopt.right.say.1 [775fed] = 你吻了他。走廊里所有的窗户一下子都结满了霜，然后，非常非常缓慢地，融化了。\n\n@event bram_intro\n# en: Running in the Halls\ntitle [b6d158] = 走廊奔跑\n# en: \"Running in the halls.\" {met(bram): Bram Hollis, the prefect from the tour,|A burly prefect with a red sash} steps into your path, ledger already open. \"Name?\" {met(bram): He knows it. |}You weren't running. You were walking briskly.\ntext [15d614] = “在走廊里奔跑。”{met(bram): 布拉姆·霍利斯，参观时那个级长，|一个系着红绶带的魁梧级长}挡住你的去路，记过簿已经翻开了。“名字？”{met(bram): 他明明知道。|}你没在跑。你只是走得比较快。\n# en: A burly prefect with a red sash blocking a corridor, a demerit ledger open in one hand and a quill poised.\nscene [de700d] = 一个系红绶带的魁梧级长堵在走廊里，一手翻开记过簿，羽毛笔蓄势待发。\n# en: Accept the demerit\nopt.left.label [758a3a] = 接受记过\n# en: He writes your name very slowly, enjoying it. Bram Hollis. You will learn to recognise his footsteps.\nopt.left.say.1 [67c2a7] = 他非常缓慢地写下你的名字，乐在其中。布拉姆·霍利斯。你会学会辨认他的脚步声的。\n# en: Argue\nopt.right.label [2f9da9] = 争辩\n# en: \"Walking briskly,\" you insist. Two demerits: one for running, one for \"lip\". Some students nearby clap quietly.\nopt.right.say.1 [4c2d1e] = “是快走。”你坚持道。记两次过：一次因为奔跑，一次因为“顶嘴”。附近几个学生悄悄鼓起了掌。\n\n@event bram_hall_tax\n# en: The Hall Tax\ntitle [86f712] = 走廊税\n# en: Bram has invented a \"hall tax\" for using the east staircase. Eight crowns. \"Maintenance,\" he says. The first-years are paying. He looks at you expectantly.\ntext [7f337c] = 布拉姆发明了一种使用东楼梯的“走廊税”。八克朗。“维护费。”他说。一年级生们都在交。他满怀期待地看着你。\n# en: A prefect leaning against a stone archway, palm out, a line of nervous first-years shuffling past him.\nscene [77c192] = 一名级长靠在石拱门上，摊着手掌，一排紧张的一年级生从他身边挪过。\n# en: Pay it\nopt.left.label [e4d5d6] = 交钱\n# en: He pockets the coins. \"Pleasure.\" You take the west staircase forever after.\nopt.left.say.1 [ed6293] = 他把硬币揣进兜里。“荣幸。”从此以后你永远走西楼梯。\n# en: Refuse loudly\nopt.right.label [c7392c] = 大声拒绝\n# en: The first-years stop paying too. Bram is furious. He'll find a way to make you pay later. He always does.\nopt.right.say.1 [9b89a4] = 一年级生们也不交了。布拉姆气坏了。他迟早会想办法让你付出代价。他总会的。\n# en: Your refusal spreads. Within a week, nobody pays the hall tax. Bram drops it, scowling.\nopt.right.say.2 [7da052] = 你的拒绝传开了。不出一周，再也没人交走廊税。布拉姆黑着脸取消了它。\n\n@event bram_sabotage\n# en: Missing Notes\ntitle [a08f2a] = 消失的笔记\n# en: Two days before exams, your revision notes vanish from your desk. All of them. You saw a red sash in the corridor.\ntext [bb41e4] = 考试前两天，你的复习笔记从桌上消失了。全部。你在走廊里看见了一抹红绶带。\n# en: An empty desk drawer, a few scattered pages on the floor, a red prefect's sash glimpsed disappearing around a corner.\nscene [a3c002] = 一只空荡荡的抽屉，地上散落着几页纸，一条红色的级长绶带消失在拐角。\n# en: Rewrite them from memory\nopt.left.label [429e64] = 凭记忆重写\n# en: You rewrite everything in one furious night. Your new notes are better than the old ones. Take that, Bram.\nopt.left.say.1 [2171a3] = 你愤怒地熬了一夜，把一切重写了一遍。新笔记比旧的还好。吃瘪吧，布拉姆。\n# en: You rewrite what you can. It isn't enough. Exams go worse than they should.\nopt.left.say.2 [0f1b73] = 你重写了能记住的部分。不够。考试考得比该有的差。\n# en: Search his room\nopt.right.label [8fc51c] = 搜他的房间\n# en: You find your notes under his mattress, and also a teddy bear. You take your notes and leave the bear exactly where it was, slightly straightened. He will know.\nopt.right.say.1 [0edcbd] = 你在他床垫下找到了你的笔记，还有一只泰迪熊。你拿回笔记，把熊原样放好，还替它摆正了一点。他会明白的。\n# en: He catches you in his room. That's two weeks of detention, and he gets to supervise.\nopt.right.say.2 [297e67] = 他在自己房间里逮住了你。两周禁闭，由他亲自监督。\n\n@event bram_crying\n# en: Behind the Stables\ntitle [321daa] = 马厩后面\n# en: Behind the stables, you find Bram Hollis sitting on a bucket, crying into a crumpled letter. He sees you and freezes. \"If you tell anyone,\" he begins, but his voice cracks.\ntext [b7fc13] = 在马厩后面，你发现布拉姆·霍利斯坐在一只水桶上，对着一封揉皱的信哭。他看见你，僵住了。“你要是敢告诉别人——”他开口，声音却哑了。\n# en: A big prefect sitting on an upturned bucket behind the stables, a crumpled letter in his fist, shoulders shaking.\nscene [a696df] = 一个高大的级长坐在马厩后倒扣的水桶上，拳头里攥着揉皱的信，肩膀在发抖。\n# en: Walk away and never mention it\nopt.left.label [6b7a64] = 走开，永远不提\n# en: You leave. You never tell anyone. Somehow, he knows you didn't. His demerits become fewer.\nopt.left.say.1 [27fbd6] = 你走开了。你从没告诉任何人。不知怎么的，他知道你没说。他给你记的过变少了。\n# en: Sit down and ask what happened\nopt.right.label [bf652a] = 坐下来，问他出了什么事\n# en: His father. A letter saying he's a disappointment, again. \"He was a prefect too,\" Bram says. \"Head boy. I'm just... loud.\" He wipes his face. \"Don't be nice to me. I don't know what to do with it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [0fc8cf] = 是他父亲。又一封说他令人失望的信。“他以前也是级长，”布拉姆说，“学生会主席。我只是……嗓门大。”他擦了擦脸。“别对我好。我不知道该拿它怎么办。”\n\n@event bram_turns\n# en: An Unexpected Ally\ntitle [fb9811] = 意外的盟友\n# en: Two fourth-years corner you by the boathouse, looking for trouble. Then a red sash steps between you. \"Problem?\" says Bram, cracking his knuckles. They leave fast.\ntext [8efcfc] = 两个四年级生在船屋边把你堵住，想找茬。这时一条红绶带插到了你们中间。“有问题吗？”布拉姆捏着指关节说。那两人很快就走了。\n# en: A prefect stepping between a cornered student and a pair of older bullies, arms crossed.\nscene [b5e93a] = 一名级长抱着双臂挡在被逼到角落的学生和两个高年级恶霸之间。\n# en: Thank him awkwardly\nopt.left.label [a37131] = 别扭地道谢\n# en: \"Don't make it weird,\" he says, and walks off. He's smiling, slightly.\nopt.left.say.1 [bd632c] = “别搞得这么肉麻。”他说完就走了。他在笑，有一点。\n# en: Offer your hand\nopt.right.label [1f34dd] = 伸出手\n# en: He stares at your hand. Then he shakes it, hard. \"Fine. Friends. But I'm still giving you demerits.\" He does. Fewer.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c04b4] = 他盯着你的手。然后用力握住。“行吧。朋友。但我照样给你记过。”他照样记。少了些。\n\n@event bram_duel\n# en: A Formal Challenge\ntitle [0903a6] = 正式挑战\n# en: Bram throws a glove at your feet in front of half the dormitory. \"Formal challenge. The dueling green, right now. Unless you're a coward.\" Everyone is watching.\ntext [28f81c] = 布拉姆当着半个宿舍的人把一只手套扔在你脚下。“正式挑战。决斗草坪，现在就去。除非你是个懦夫。”所有人都在看。\n# en: A white glove lying on a dormitory floor, a red sash folded beside it, a crowd peering through the doorway.\nscene [c0de53] = 宿舍地板上一只白手套，旁边叠着一条红绶带，门口挤满了张望的人。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Coward,\" he says, delighted. It sticks for a while. Then people forget. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [821d12] = “懦夫。”他兴高采烈地说。这个外号跟了你一阵子。后来大家就忘了。大体上。\n# en: Accept\nopt.right.label [b26149] = 接受\n# en: You disarm him in three moves. The crowd goes wild. Bram, on the grass, starts laughing. \"Fine,\" he says. \"Fine.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [5700d2] = 你三招就缴了他的械。人群沸腾了。躺在草地上的布拉姆笑了起来。“行，”他说，“行。”\n# en: He wins. But it takes him a full ten minutes, and he's limping too. He nods to you after. Grudging respect.\nopt.right.say.2 [a5c4a7] = 他赢了。但足足花了十分钟，而且他也一瘸一拐的。事后他朝你点了点头。不情不愿的尊重。\n\n@event thorne_intro\n# en: The First Question\ntitle [7b3e5e] = 第一个问题\n# en: First Evocation lecture. Professor Thorne surveys the room like a hawk over a field of mice. His finger lands on you. \"You. What is the first law of the Arcane?\"\ntext [94bd6c] = 第一堂塑能术课。索恩教授扫视教室，像一只盯着田鼠的鹰。他的手指落在你身上。“你。奥术第一定律是什么？”\n# en: A steep lecture theatre, a stern grey-bearded professor pointing a long finger at one student in the middle row.\nscene [662f2a] = 陡峭的阶梯讲堂，一位严厉的灰胡子教授用修长的手指指着中间一排的一个学生。\n# en: Admit you don't know\nopt.left.label [a10b0e] = 承认你不知道\n# en: \"Correct answer,\" he says. \"The first law is: know what you don't know. The rest of you, write that down.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [7cfbd1] = “正确答案，”他说，“第一定律是：知道自己不知道什么。其余的人，记下来。”\n# en: Take a guess\nopt.right.label [b3c370] = 猜一个\n# en: \"Energy follows intention.\" His eyebrows twitch. \"Acceptable.\" The room exhales.\nopt.right.say.1 [a4daea] = “能量追随意念。”他的眉毛抽动了一下。“可以接受。”全班松了口气。\n# en: Your guess is so wrong the student next to you gasps. Thorne simply says, \"No,\" and moves on.\nopt.right.say.2 [0a5012] = 你猜得错得离谱，旁边的同学倒吸一口冷气。索恩只说了一句“不对”，就继续讲下去了。\n\n@event thorne_office_hours\n# en: Office Hours\ntitle [2f554a] = 答疑时间\n# en: Thorne stops you after class. \"Your casting is sloppy and your instincts are good. That's an annoying combination. Thursdays. Seven o'clock. My office. Don't be late.\"\ntext [e4245b] = 下课后索恩叫住了你。“你的施法很马虎，直觉却很好。这是个恼人的组合。周四。七点。我的办公室。别迟到。”\n# en: A cramped office full of scorched books and a single lit candle, a stern professor leaning back in his chair, fingers steepled.\nscene [78fbf0] = 一间堆满烧焦书本、只点着一支蜡烛的狭小办公室，严厉的教授靠在椅背上，十指相抵。\n# en: Politely decline — you're stretched thin\nopt.left.label [d714b1] = 礼貌地拒绝——你已经分身乏术\n# en: \"Hm.\" He seems neither surprised nor pleased. Thorne is rarely either.\nopt.left.say.1 [c8eaad] = “嗯。”他看起来既不意外，也不高兴。索恩很少有这两种情绪。\n# en: Be there at seven\nopt.right.label [5fe202] = 七点准时到\n# en: Two hours of brutal drills. At the end, he hands you a book from his own shelf. \"Read it. Return it unburnt.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [80a8e2] = 两个小时残酷的操练。结束时，他从自己的书架上抽出一本书递给你。“读完。还的时候别烧着。”\n\n@event thorne_storm_story\n# en: The Storm Duel\ntitle [c9a5ed] = 风暴决斗\n# en: During a thunderstorm, Thorne stops mid-lecture and watches the lightning. \"They say I out-dueled a storm once,\" he says, to nobody in particular. \"Would you like to know what actually happened?\"\ntext [1d58d7] = 雷雨中，索恩讲到一半停了下来，望着窗外的闪电。“他们说我曾经在决斗中赢过一场风暴，”他说，不知是对谁说的，“想知道真正发生了什么吗？”\n# en: An old professor standing at a rain-lashed window, lightning illuminating a long scar along his jaw.\nscene [3aa02a] = 一位年迈的教授站在被雨水抽打的窗前，闪电照亮了他下颌上一道长长的伤疤。\n# en: \"Yes, sir.\"\nopt.left.label [ad622a] = “想，教授。”\n# en: He lost, he says. Badly. He spent a year learning why. \"Losing well,\" he says, \"is the only thing worth teaching.\" The class is silent. The lecture never resumes.\nopt.left.say.1 [d85954] = 他说，他输了。输得很惨。他花了一年才弄明白为什么。“输得漂亮，”他说，“是唯一值得教的东西。”全班鸦雀无声。那堂课再也没讲下去。\n# en: Stay quiet\nopt.right.label [9adbd8] = 保持沉默\n# en: He shakes his head and returns to the lecture, which is a good one. You'll always wonder.\nopt.right.say.1 [01c1de] = 他摇摇头，继续讲课，讲得很好。你会一直好奇下去。\n\n@event thorne_recommendation\n# en: A Letter of Recommendation\ntitle [c8fd60] = 推荐信\n# en: Thorne calls you to his office and slides an envelope across the desk. \"The Conclave asked for my recommendations. I wrote one. I don't write many.\" He pauses. \"Don't make me regret it.\"\ntext [126063] = 索恩把你叫到办公室，把一个信封推过桌面。“议会请我写推荐信。我写了一封。我很少写。”他顿了顿，“别让我后悔。”\n# en: A wax-sealed envelope on a professor's desk with a student's name written on it in sharp, angular handwriting.\nscene [96717b] = 教授桌上一个封着火漆的信封，上面用锋利棱角的字迹写着一个学生的名字。\n# en: Thank him sincerely\nopt.left.label [028201] = 真诚地道谢\n# en: \"Go away,\" he says gruffly. \"I have marking.\" You see him smile at the window as you leave.\nopt.left.say.1 [91553f] = “走吧，”他粗声说，“我还要批作业。”你离开时，看见他对着窗户笑了。\n# en: On your way out you leave a single rose on the corner of his desk, from the village, not from his garden. He pretends not to see it. The next day it's in a glass of water.\nopt.left.say.2 [a88357] = 出门时你在他桌角放了一朵玫瑰，是从村里买的，不是他园子里的。他假装没看见。第二天，它被插在了一杯水里。\n# en: Ask why you\nopt.right.label [ca1833] = 问为什么是你\n# en: \"Because you ask questions like that,\" he says. \"Most students don't. Go.\" You leave unsure whether you were praised or dismissed.\nopt.right.say.1 [52bd7f] = “因为你会问这种问题，”他说，“大多数学生不会。走吧。”你离开时也说不清自己是被夸了还是被打发了。\n\n@event thorne_disappointed\n# en: A Word in Private\ntitle [ff3088] = 私下谈谈\n# en: Thorne holds up your last three Evocation essays. All red. \"You're better than this,\" he says. \"That's not a compliment. It's an accusation.\"\ntext [c9cdc7] = 索恩举起你最近的三篇塑能术论文。全是红的。“你不止这个水平，”他说，“这不是夸奖。是指控。”\n# en: A professor standing by his desk with a sheaf of red-marked papers, a student standing awkwardly before him.\nscene [9d745e] = 一位教授站在桌旁，手持一沓满是红批的论文，一个学生尴尬地站在他面前。\n# en: Make excuses\nopt.left.label [271029] = 找借口\n# en: He listens to them all and says nothing. The silence is the worst grade yet.\nopt.left.say.1 [f08449] = 他听完了你所有的借口，一言不发。这份沉默是你得过最差的分数。\n# en: Ask for help\nopt.right.label [e11804] = 请求帮助\n# en: He sighs and pulls out a chair. \"Sit. We'll start with the first essay, and we'll stay until it's right.\" You stay until midnight.\nopt.right.say.1 [0a15d2] = 他叹了口气，拉出一把椅子。“坐。我们从第一篇开始，改到对为止。”你们一直改到半夜。\n\n@event maud_chickens\n# en: The Great Chicken Escape\ntitle [c0698d] = 鸡群大逃亡\n# en: Forty chickens are loose on the main lawn, pursued by an old woman in a straw hat. \"Don't just stand there, dearie!\" she shouts at you. \"Grab the speckled one, she's the ringleader!\"\ntext [c7632d] = 四十只鸡在大草坪上四处逃窜，一位戴草帽的老妇人在后面追。“别光站着呀，孩子！”她冲你喊，“抓那只带斑点的，她是头儿！”\n# en: Chickens fleeing across a lawn in every direction, an old woman in a straw hat waving a broom, students diving after birds.\nscene [f345a3] = 鸡群在草坪上四散奔逃，戴草帽的老妇人挥着扫帚，学生们扑向鸡群。\n# en: Keep walking\nopt.left.label [6ca967] = 继续走你的路\n# en: You hear a crash, a squawk and a triumphant cackle behind you. The chickens, it seems, are caught.\nopt.left.say.1 [62bfac] = 你听见身后一声巨响、一声鸡叫和一阵得意的笑。看来鸡都被抓住了。\n# en: Grab the speckled one\nopt.right.label [5c1b61] = 抓住那只带斑点的\n# en: You tackle the ringleader. The rest give up. \"Good hands,\" says Old Maud, pressing a warm egg into your palm. \"Come for tea.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [a339b6] = 你扑倒了头儿。其余的都放弃了。“好身手。”老莫德说着把一枚温热的鸡蛋塞进你手心，“来喝茶吧。”\n# en: The ringleader escapes and pecks you, but Maud is impressed by your enthusiasm. \"Come for tea. Bring plasters.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [bfdfbf] = 头儿逃走了还啄了你一口，但莫德很欣赏你的热情。“来喝茶吧。带上创可贴。”\n\n@event maud_honey\n# en: Honey for a Cold\ntitle [b7cd42] = 治感冒的蜂蜜\n# en: Maud spots you sniffling across the lawn and drags you into her cottage. \"Sit,\" she orders, pouring honey into something steaming that smells of lemon and thunder.\ntext [7490d5] = 莫德隔着草坪看见你在吸鼻子，把你拽进了她的小屋。“坐下。”她命令道，把蜂蜜倒进一杯冒着热气、闻起来像柠檬和雷声的东西里。\n# en: A small cottage kitchen with bundles of herbs hanging from the beams, an old woman pouring golden honey into a steaming mug.\nscene [eeaeed] = 小屋厨房的房梁上挂着一捆捆草药，老妇人正把金色的蜂蜜倒进一只冒热气的杯子。\n# en: Drink it all\nopt.left.label [06ea01] = 一饮而尽\n# en: It tastes like summer and lightning. You sneeze sparks for the rest of the day. By morning you're cured and slightly glowing.\nopt.left.say.1 [2d602b] = 它尝起来像夏天和闪电。你一整天都在打喷嚏，喷出小火星。第二天早上你就好了，身上还微微发光。\n# en: Drink it and help with chores\nopt.right.label [cbf3a4] = 边喝边帮她干活\n# en: You sip and sort herbs while she tells you the names of every plant in the room. You remember most of them.\nopt.right.say.1 [d8a82c] = 你一边小口喝着，一边分拣草药，她把屋里每一株植物的名字都告诉了你。你记住了大半。\n\n@event maud_old_student\n# en: Maud Was a Student Once\ntitle [bad37c] = 莫德曾经也是学生\n# en: \"I was a student here, you know,\" Maud says, watching the lake. \"Sixty years ago. The lake froze one spring. Three of my friends went down to the boathouse that night. Only two came back.\" She doesn't look at you. \"Ask me what I know.\"\ntext [eef642] = “你知道吗，我以前也是这里的学生，”莫德望着湖面说，“六十年前。有一年春天，湖结冰了。那天夜里，我的三个朋友下到了船屋。只回来了两个。”她没有看你。“问我知道些什么吧。”\n# en: An old woman sitting on a bench by the lake at dusk, looking at the water as if it might look back.\nscene [89e70b] = 黄昏时分，一位老妇人坐在湖边长椅上，望着湖水，仿佛湖水也会回望她。\n# en: \"You don't have to tell me.\"\nopt.left.label [194cf6] = “您不必告诉我的。”\n# en: She pats your hand. \"Kind. But time's short, dearie.\" She tells you anyway, a little at a time, over many cups of tea.\nopt.left.say.1 [9b1d22] = 她拍了拍你的手。“好孩子。可时间不多了。”她还是告诉了你，一点一点地，就着许多杯茶。\n# en: \"What do you know?\"\nopt.right.label [099f17] = “您知道些什么？”\n# en: \"The founders built this place as a lid. Seven towers, and the key kept in the one nobody climbs.\" She finally looks at you. \"The thing below loves a lonely heart. Don't be lonely.\" It's a long walk back in the dark.\nopt.right.say.1 [bcd702] = “创始者把这地方建成了一个盖子。七座塔，钥匙藏在那座没人爬的塔里。”她终于看向你。“下面那个东西，最爱孤独的心。别孤单。”回去的路在黑暗里显得很长。\n\n@event maud_bees\n# en: The Silver Bees\ntitle [1eab66] = 银蜂\n# en: \"The silver bees need moving to the summer meadow,\" Maud says, handing you a veiled hat. \"They're friendly. Mostly. Don't make any sudden thoughts.\"\ntext [30a86a] = “银蜂要搬去夏季草场了，”莫德说着递给你一顶带面纱的帽子，“它们很友好。大体上。别突然冒出什么念头就行。”\n# en: Wooden hives in a wildflower meadow, an old woman in a veiled hat lifting a frame dripping with silver honey, bees glinting in the sun.\nscene [53eac5] = 野花草场上的木蜂箱，一位戴面纱帽的老妇人举起一框滴着银色蜂蜜的巢脾，蜜蜂在阳光下闪闪发亮。\n# en: Help with the hives\nopt.left.label [05ba5e] = 帮忙搬蜂箱\n# en: The bees hum around you like a blessing. Maud gives you a jar of silver honey that sells for eight crowns. You eat half first.\nopt.left.say.1 [6e9b07] = 蜜蜂围着你嗡嗡作响，像一种祝福。莫德给了你一罐能卖八克朗的银蜜。你先吃了一半。\n# en: You have a sudden thought. The bees know. You spend the evening with vinegar on your stings.\nopt.left.say.2 [8b74be] = 你突然冒出了一个念头。蜜蜂知道了。你整晚都在往蜇伤上抹醋。\n# en: Watch from a safe distance\nopt.right.label [5c0ef7] = 在安全距离外观看\n# en: You watch Maud move hives barehanded, singing to them. It's the calmest you've felt all month.\nopt.right.say.1 [fca1ee] = 你看着莫德赤手搬动蜂箱，一边对它们唱歌。这是你这个月最平静的时刻。\n\n@event maud_tea_tells\n# en: Over Many Cups of Tea\ntitle [dc0f5a] = 一杯又一杯茶\n# en: Maud keeps her word, a little at a time. Over a month of afternoons and more tea than is good for anyone, the story of that spring comes out in pieces: the boathouse, the cold, the friend who didn't come back.\ntext [062301] = 莫德说话算话，一点一点地讲。一个月的下午，加上多得对谁都没好处的茶，那年春天的故事一片片拼了出来：船屋、寒冷，还有那个没能回来的朋友。\n# en: A cluttered cottage kitchen at dusk, two teacups and a pot between an old woman and a student, the lake grey in the window.\nscene [906453] = 黄昏时杂乱的农舍厨房，老妇人和学生之间摆着两只茶杯和一把茶壶，窗外的湖一片灰色。\n# en: Listen to the end\nopt.left.label [ba4aa9] = 听她讲完\n# en: \"The founders built this place as a lid,\" she says on the last afternoon. \"Seven towers, and the key kept in the one nobody climbs. The thing below loves a lonely heart. Don't be lonely.\" She pours another cup.\nopt.left.say.1 [16e283] = “创始者把这地方建成了一个盖子，”最后一个下午她说，“七座塔，钥匙藏在没人爬的那一座里。下面那东西喜欢孤独的心。别孤独。”她又倒了一杯。\n# en: Tell her it can wait\nopt.right.label [ada58d] = 告诉她不急\n# en: \"It can't, dearie,\" she says, \"but I like you for saying so.\" She tells you about her chickens instead. You'll come back for the rest.\nopt.right.say.1 [f1347f] = “急的，亲爱的，”她说，“不过你这么说，我很喜欢。”她改讲起了她的鸡。剩下的，你还会回来听。\n\n@event maud_ill\n# en: Maud Is Unwell\ntitle [1570b9] = 莫德病了\n# en: Maud's chickens are running wild, unfed. You find her in bed, grey-faced and coughing. \"Just a chill,\" she wheezes. \"I'm eighty-one. I'm allowed.\"\ntext [d16f4b] = 莫德的鸡没人喂，满地乱跑。你发现她躺在床上，脸色灰白，咳个不停。“只是着了点凉，”她喘着气说，“我八十一了。有这个资格。”\n# en: A small cottage bedroom with a patchwork quilt, an old woman propped up on pillows, a student reading aloud by candlelight.\nscene [a2af84] = 小屋卧室里铺着拼布被，一位老妇人靠在枕头上，一个学生在烛光下为她读报。\n# en: Fetch Sister Amaranth\nopt.left.label [0ebdc9] = 去叫阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth brings tonics and a stern lecture. Maud is back on her feet in a week, grumbling about fuss.\nopt.left.say.1 [79a7ad] = 阿玛兰斯带来了药剂和一通严厉的训话。一周后莫德就下了床，嘟囔着大家小题大做。\n# en: Look after her yourself for a week\nopt.right.label [c0447c] = 亲自照顾她一周\n# en: You feed chickens, brew teas, read her the newspaper. When she's well, she gives you a sprig of rosemary. \"You'll do,\" she says. \"You'll do.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [4fecac] = 你喂鸡、煮茶、给她读报纸。她康复后，给了你一枝迷迭香。“你行，”她说，“你行的。”\n# en: On the third day you make her own soup, from memory, since you're not allowed to write it down. She tastes it, narrows her eyes and says it wants more pepper. It doesn't.\nopt.right.say.2 [0c2aa2] = 第三天，你凭记忆做了她的那锅汤，反正她不许你写下来。她尝了尝，眯起眼睛，说还得多放点胡椒。其实并不用。\n\n@event isolde_intro\n# en: She Knows Your Name\ntitle [9a7a2b] = 她知道你的名字\n# en: At dinner, a cool hand rests on the back of your chair. The headmistress. \"{player},\" she says, though you've never met. \"Your marks in Arcana are interesting. Your marks in punctuality, less so.\" She moves on.\ntext [0b5a32] = 晚餐时，一只微凉的手搭在你的椅背上。是院长。“{player}，”她说，尽管你们从未见过，“你的奥术成绩很有意思。你的守时成绩，就没那么有意思了。”她继续往前走了。\n# en: A tall pale woman in dark robes pausing behind a student's chair in a candlelit dining hall, a hand resting lightly on the chair back.\nscene [b2fa1a] = 烛光摇曳的餐厅里，一位身着深色长袍的高挑苍白女子在一名学生的椅子后驻足，一只手轻轻搭在椅背上。\n# en: Stammer an apology\nopt.left.label [d97a4c] = 结结巴巴地道歉\n# en: She's already three tables away. Every student between you sits a little straighter.\nopt.left.say.1 [e1b364] = 她已经走出三张桌子远了。你们之间的每个学生都坐直了一点。\n# en: \"Interesting how, Headmistress?\"\nopt.right.label [836bca] = “怎么个有意思法，院长？”\n# en: She pauses. Turns. Almost smiles. \"Ask me again at the end of your first year,\" she says. The table stares at you.\nopt.right.say.1 [912689] = 她停下脚步。转身。几乎笑了。“等你一年级结束时再问我一次。”她说。整桌人都盯着你看。\n\n@event isolde_tea\n# en: Tea in the Tower\ntitle [b7137e] = 塔楼下午茶\n# en: An invitation to tea with the headmistress. Students talk about these the way soldiers talk about battles. She pours. \"Tell me, {player}: what do you want to become?\"\ntext [9726d0] = 一份与院长共进下午茶的邀请。学生们谈论这种事，就像士兵谈论战役。她倒着茶。“告诉我，{player}：你想成为什么样的人？”\n# en: A round tower study at twilight, two delicate teacups on a small table, a headmistress pouring tea with a perfectly steady hand.\nscene [71ceab] = 暮色中的圆形塔楼书房，小桌上两只精致的茶杯，院长倒茶的手稳得纹丝不动。\n# en: Give an ambitious answer\nopt.left.label [aa3b70] = 给一个雄心勃勃的回答\n# en: She listens without interrupting, then says, \"Good. Ambition without arrogance. Rarer than you'd think.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [7c5144] = 她一句也没打断地听完，然后说：“很好。有雄心而不傲慢。比你想象的要少见。”\n# en: She nods politely. \"Ambition is a fine servant,\" she says, \"and a poor master.\" You're dismissed shortly after.\nopt.left.say.2 [7dba0b] = 她礼貌地点点头。“雄心是个好仆人，”她说，“却是个坏主人。”没过多久你就被请走了。\n# en: Admit you don't know yet\nopt.right.label [0c2530] = 承认你还不知道\n# en: \"Neither did I, at your age,\" she says. \"I still don't, some days. That's the point of an academy.\" She refills your cup.\nopt.right.say.1 [80f029] = “我在你这个年纪时也不知道，”她说，“有些日子我现在也不知道。这正是学院存在的意义。”她为你续上了茶。\n\n@event isolde_warning\n# en: A Quiet Warning\ntitle [a5c708] = 一句轻声的警告\n# en: The headmistress falls into step beside you in the colonnade. \"I hear your name more often than I'd like,\" she says pleasantly. \"In the wrong rooms. I'd hate to hear it in mine.\"\ntext [01291a] = 院长在柱廊里走到你身边，与你并肩而行。“我听到你名字的次数比我希望的要多，”她和气地说，“在不该听到的房间里。我可不想在我的房间里听到它。”\n# en: A headmistress walking alongside a student in a long colonnade, hands clasped behind her back, speaking without looking at them.\nscene [052fbe] = 长长的柱廊里，院长背着手与一名学生并肩而行，说话时并不看对方。\n# en: Promise to do better\nopt.left.label [cba502] = 保证会改\n# en: \"Good.\" She stops walking. You keep going. When you look back, she's gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [b48ece] = “很好。”她停下脚步。你继续往前走。回头时，她已经不见了。\n# en: Defend yourself\nopt.right.label [b9d2ca] = 为自己辩护\n# en: You lay it out plainly. She listens, then nods once. \"Fair. I'll have a word with certain prefects.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [72ff59] = 你把事情原原本本讲清楚。她听完，点了一下头。“公道。我会找某些级长谈谈。”\n# en: \"Hm,\" she says. \"Let's hope you're right.\" It doesn't feel like hope.\nopt.right.say.2 [d3a208] = “嗯，”她说，“但愿你是对的。”这听起来可不像但愿。\n\n@event vey_intro\n# en: Tea That Isn't There\ntitle [52dadd] = 不存在的茶\n# en: First Illusion lecture. Professor Vey pours you a cup of tea from a pot you would swear is empty, and waits. \"Milk?\" she asks. The whole row turns to see what you'll do with a cup of nothing.\ntext [e330e1] = 第一节幻术课。维伊教授从一只你敢发誓是空的茶壶里给你倒了一杯茶，然后等着。“加奶吗？”她问。整排人都转过头来，看你怎么对付一杯什么都没有的茶。\n# en: A lecture room hung with velvet curtains, a woman in a violet coat pouring from an empty teapot into a student's empty cup.\nscene [7245af] = 挂着天鹅绒帘子的教室里，一个穿紫罗兰色外套的女人从空茶壶往学生的空杯子里倒茶。\n# en: Drink it, and thank her\nopt.left.label [374044] = 喝下去，再道谢\n# en: You sip, sigh and ask where she buys it. Vey's mouth twitches. \"Lapsang. There was never any tea, of course. Sit down; you'll do.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [5466bf] = 你抿一口，叹口气，问她在哪儿买的。维伊的嘴角抽动了一下。“正山小种。当然，从来就没有茶。坐吧，你行。”\n# en: You raise the cup with a flourish and tip it a little too far. Nothing pours out, and the whole row sees nothing pour out. Vey writes something down, and smiles at it.\nopt.left.say.2 [2dbeaf] = 你潇洒地举起杯子，却倾得太过了。什么也没倒出来，而整排人都看见什么也没倒出来。维伊记了点什么，对着它笑了。\n# en: Say the cup is empty\nopt.right.label [9ea330] = 说杯子是空的\n# en: \"It's empty,\" you say. \"So it is,\" says Vey, pleased and disappointed at once. \"Honest eyes. We shall have to do something about those.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [73d780] = “是空的，”你说。“确实，”维伊说，既满意又失望。“诚实的眼睛。我们得想办法治一治。”\n\n@event brask_intro\n# en: Shovel. There. Go.\ntitle [ee049c] = 铲子。那儿。去。\n# en: First Beastcraft lesson, at the stables. Professor Brask is explaining to a hippogriff, in full and courteous sentences, that the new students will be clumsy and she is to be patient with them. Then she turns to you. \"Shovel. There. Go.\"\ntext [dc2351] = 第一节驭兽课，在马厩。布拉斯克教授正用完整而客气的句子向一头鹰头马身有翼兽解释：新生会笨手笨脚，请它多担待。然后她转向你。“铲子。那儿。去。”\n# en: A long stable in morning light, a grey-haired professor talking earnestly to a hippogriff while a student stands holding a shovel.\nscene [98b211] = 晨光中长长的马厩，一位灰发教授正认真地对一头鹰头马身有翼兽说话，一个学生拿着铲子站在一旁。\n# en: Muck out without a word\nopt.left.label [ef11b2] = 一声不吭地铲\n# en: You shovel until your arms shake. Brask watches, then tells the hippogriff, loud enough for you to hear, \"This one might do.\" To you she says nothing, which you later learn is praise.\nopt.left.say.1 [2284b5] = 你铲到胳膊发抖。布拉斯克看着，然后对鹰头马身有翼兽说，声音大得刚好让你听见：“这个兴许行。”她对你什么也没说——后来你才知道，那就是夸奖。\n# en: Ask what today's lesson is\nopt.right.label [068c9f] = 问今天学什么\n# en: Brask tells the hippogriff a story about a student who asked questions before shovelling. It ends with a bite. \"Moral: shovel.\" You shovel, a little behind the others.\nopt.right.say.1 [af14ce] = 布拉斯克给鹰头马身有翼兽讲了个故事：有个学生总在铲粪之前提问。结尾是被咬了一口。“寓意：铲。”你开始铲，比别人慢了一步。\n\n@event pell_first_shift\n# en: Count Aloud\ntitle [39b25b] = 数出声来\n# en: Your first shift at Pell & Daughters. Old Pell climbs down from his ladder, hands you a dropper and a bottle of tincture, and points at a waiting customer. \"Twelve drops,\" he says. \"Aloud. Everyone counts aloud in this shop. The drawers like it.\"\ntext [e01d70] = 你在佩尔父女药铺的第一个班。老佩尔从梯子上爬下来，递给你一支滴管和一瓶酊剂，指了指一位等着的顾客。“十二滴，”他说，“数出声。这店里人人都数出声。抽屉们喜欢听。”\n# en: A narrow apothecary lined with tiny labelled drawers, an old man on a ladder holding out a glass dropper to a nervous new clerk.\nscene [9281b1] = 一间狭长的药铺，墙上满是贴着标签的小抽屉，梯子上的老人向一个紧张的新店员递过一支玻璃滴管。\n# en: Count every drop aloud\nopt.left.label [2046bb] = 每一滴都数出声\n# en: \"One. Two. Three.\" Your voice fills the shop; the customer joins in at nine. Pell nods once. \"Twelve. Not eleven. Not thirteen. You'll count like that until they bury you.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [5a7443] = “一。二。三。”你的声音充满了整间铺子；数到九时顾客也跟着数。佩尔点了一下头。“十二。不是十一，不是十三。你就这么数，一直数到入土。”\n# en: Count in your head, quickly\nopt.right.label [585870] = 在心里快快地数\n# en: Twelve drops, done in a breath; the customer tells the whole queue how quick the new clerk is. Pell counts the level against the light. \"Correct. Next time, aloud. I like to hear a thing being right.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [95b946] = 十二滴，一口气滴完；顾客跟整条队的人夸新店员手快。佩尔把瓶子举到光下看刻度。“对。下次数出声。我喜欢听见事情做对的声音。”\n# en: You lose count at eight and guess. Pell tips the bottle out and starts it again, aloud, without a word to you. The customer leaves with the right dose and a look at you.\nopt.right.say.2 [2e54e1] = 数到八你就数乱了，只好估。佩尔把瓶子倒空，重新数了一遍，出声地数，一句话也没对你说。顾客拿着正确的剂量走了，临走看了你一眼。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/people_students.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/people_students.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lyra_intro\n# en: The Last Copy\ntitle [370a21] = 最后一本\n# en: You reach for the last copy of \"Foundations of Flame\" and a ringed hand gets there first. \"I need it more,\" says a tall girl with crimson hair, not looking at you. \"You'll only underline the wrong bits.\"\ntext [81419e] = 你伸手去拿最后一本《火焰基础》，一只戴戒指的手抢先了一步。“我更需要它，”一个高挑的红发女孩说，看都没看你，“你只会划错重点。”\n# en: Two hands reaching for the same red-bound book on a library shelf; one hand wears a signet ring with a flame crest.\nscene [396ae6] = 两只手伸向图书馆书架上同一本红皮书；其中一只手上戴着刻有火焰家徽的印章戒指。\n# en: Let her have it\nopt.left.label [6e7c4e] = 让给她\n# en: \"At least you know your place,\" she says, which is somehow worse than rudeness. Lyra Vaneshade. Everyone knows the name.\nopt.left.say.1 [69ebd2] = “至少你有自知之明。”她说，这不知怎么比无礼更伤人。莱拉·凡妮希德。人人都知道这个名字。\n# en: Argue for it\nopt.right.label [503eb7] = 据理力争\n# en: You quote chapter three at her. She raises an eyebrow, then hands you the book. \"Fine. Return it underlined correctly.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [bbdc0c] = 你给她背了一段第三章。她挑起眉毛，然后把书递给了你。“行。还的时候把重点划对。”\n# en: She wins the argument in four sentences and leaves with the book. You think of the perfect comeback that night.\nopt.right.say.2 [b0bbf8] = 她四句话就赢了这场争论，拿着书走了。那天晚上你才想到完美的回嘴。\n\n@event lyra_letter\n# en: A Letter With a Black Seal\ntitle [47420d] = 黑色封印的信\n# en: You find Lyra on a window seat with a crumpled letter. Black seal. Her family's. She's not crying; Vaneshades don't. But she's not quite not crying either.\ntext [4caeea] = 你发现莱拉坐在窗台上，手里攥着一封揉皱的信。黑色封印。她家族的。她没有哭；凡妮希德家的人不哭。但她也不完全是没在哭。\n# en: A girl sitting on a window seat in an empty corridor, crushing a letter with a black wax seal in her fist, face turned away.\nscene [ed4b56] = 空荡荡的走廊里，一个女孩坐在窗台上，把一封黑蜡封口的信攥在拳中，脸转向一边。\n# en: Pretend you didn't see\nopt.left.label [25b345] = 假装没看见\n# en: You walk past. Later, she's flawless again, as if nothing happened. Maybe nothing did.\nopt.left.say.1 [8cb435] = 你走了过去。后来，她又是那个无懈可击的她了，仿佛什么都没发生。也许真的什么都没发生。\n# en: Sit down beside her\nopt.right.label [e6df6e] = 在她身边坐下\n# en: \"Second place,\" she says eventually. \"I came second in the Winter Trials. My mother wants to know who beat me.\" She almost laughs. \"It's always someone.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2ea3fc] = “第二名，”她终于开口，“我在冬季考核里拿了第二。我母亲想知道是谁赢了我。”她几乎笑了出来。“总会有那么一个人。”\n\n@event lyra_study_rivalry\n# en: Top of the Class\ntitle [76842e] = 全班第一\n# en: The professors post this week's rankings, with an open paper this afternoon for anyone who wants to improve their place. Lyra is first{&. You're close enough to see the back of her head.|, again. You're close enough to read her handwriting.|. Of course she is. You're one name below her, which is somehow worse than three.} She notices you noticing.\ntext [f904e6] = 教授们公布了本周排名，还说今天下午有一场公开考试，想提高名次的人都可以参加。莱拉第一{&。你离她近到能看见她的后脑勺。|，又是第一。你离她近到能看清她的笔迹。|。当然是她。你就排在她下面一名，这不知怎么比差三名还难受。}她注意到了你在注意她。\n# en: Two students at opposite ends of a long library table, surrounded by towers of books, glaring over the tops of them.\nscene [e05ebb] = 长长的图书馆桌两端各坐一个学生，被一摞摞书包围着，隔着书堆互相瞪视。\n# en: Suggest studying together\nopt.left.label [d07df2] = 提议一起学习\n# en: {&\"Together?\" She considers this like a foreign custom. Then she pulls out a chair. She's an excellent, merciless study partner.|She already has two cups of tea and your chair pulled out. \"You're late,\" she says. You aren't.|\"Fine. But I'm quizzing you first.\" She is merciless again, and you're better for it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a3ad5c] = {&“一起？”她像在考虑一种外国习俗。然后她拉出了一把椅子。她是个出色而无情的学习搭档。|她已经备好了两杯茶，把你的椅子也拉了出来。“你迟到了，”她说。你并没有。|“行。但我先考你。”她又一次毫不留情，而你因此进步了。}\n# en: Beat her outright\nopt.right.label [7983cc] = 正面击败她\n# en: The afternoon paper puts you first. Lyra stares at the list for a long time. Then she finds you and says, \"Good.\" She means it.\nopt.right.say.1 [8e7fb5] = 下午的考试让你排到了第一。莱拉盯着名单看了很久。然后她找到你，说：“很好。”她是认真的。\n# en: {&You cram through lunch and come third. Lyra is still first. She doesn't gloat. That's worse.|Second, by one mark. Lyra leaves a note on your desk: \"Close.\" You keep it.|You come fourth. Lyra looks almost disappointed on your behalf, which is unbearable.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1024cc] = {&你午饭时间都在临阵磨枪，结果是第三。莱拉还是第一。她没有得意。这更糟。|第二，差一分。莱拉在你桌上留了张字条：“很接近。”你把它收了起来。|你排第四。莱拉看上去几乎在替你失望，这简直让人受不了。}\n\n@event lyra_family_gala\n# en: The Vaneshade Winter Gala\ntitle [cc6875] = 凡妮希德冬季晚宴\n# en: \"My family's winter gala,\" Lyra says, too casually. \"I need a guest who won't embarrass me. You'll do. Probably. You'll need proper clothes.\"\ntext [f5575c] = “我家的冬季晚宴，”莱拉说，语气太过随意，“我需要一个不会让我丢脸的客人。你还行。大概。你得穿得像样点。”\n# en: A glittering ballroom in a noble house, candles floating in mid-air, a tall woman in crimson watching the crowd like a hawk.\nscene [bf8dd3] = 贵族宅邸里金碧辉煌的舞厅，蜡烛悬浮在空中，一位身着深红衣裙的高挑女人像鹰一样注视着人群。\n# en: Decline\nopt.left.label [eaef39] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Of course.\" A pause. \"It doesn't matter.\" It clearly does.\nopt.left.say.1 [1fe8ea] = “当然。”停顿。“无所谓。”显然有所谓。\n# en: Go with her (15 gold for clothes)\nopt.right.label [e8a9e8] = 陪她去（置装花费 15 金币）\n# en: Lyra's mother asks who your parents are, and then asks no further questions at all. Lyra squeezes your hand under the table, hard.\nopt.right.say.1 [3ae20f] = 莱拉的母亲问起你父母是谁，然后就再也没有问别的了。莱拉在桌下用力握住了你的手。\n# en: You dance, you charm a duke, you don't spill anything. In the carriage home, Lyra falls asleep on your shoulder.\nopt.right.say.2 [db666b] = 你跳舞，你迷倒了一位公爵，你什么都没洒。回家的马车上，莱拉靠在你肩上睡着了。\n\n@event lyra_confession\n# en: Lyra Doesn't Lose\ntitle [4e22c6] = 莱拉从不认输\n# en: After practice, Lyra doesn't leave. \"I don't lose,\" she says, staring at the scorch marks. \"I don't. But I think — \" She stops. Starts again. \"I think I've lost to you. Say something before I set something on fire.\"\ntext [0b2d38] = 练习结束后，莱拉没有走。“我不会输，”她盯着地上的焦痕说，“我不会。但我觉得——”她停住了。又重新开口。“我觉得我输给你了。快说点什么，不然我要烧东西了。”\n# en: A girl with crimson hair standing very close in a practice ring at dusk, sparks still drifting from her wand, cheeks flushed.\nscene [fa1888] = 黄昏的练习圈里，一个红发女孩站得很近，魔杖上还飘着火星，脸颊泛红。\n# en: Let her down gently\nopt.left.label [c62f10] = 温柔地拒绝她\n# en: She nods, sharp and quick. \"Understood.\" She practises alone for a month. Then one day she nods to you in the corridor, and it's almost all right.\nopt.left.say.1 [d294fb] = 她干脆利落地点了点头。“明白了。”她独自练了一个月。后来有一天她在走廊里朝你点点头，一切差不多都好了。\n# en: Kiss her\nopt.right.label [5fa79c] = 吻她\n# en: Nothing catches fire. Well. Something does, but it's only the practice dummy, and neither of you notices for a while.\nopt.right.say.1 [90be4c] = 什么都没着火。好吧。有东西着了，但只是练习用的假人，而且你们好一阵子都没注意到。\n\n@event lyra_family_ultimatum\n# en: The Ultimatum\ntitle [f0d1f2] = 最后通牒\n# en: Another black-sealed letter. Lyra reads it twice and goes white. \"My mother has found out about us. End it, she says, or she ends my inheritance.\" She looks at you. \"Tell me what to do.\"\ntext [fa5d3c] = 又一封黑色封印的信。莱拉读了两遍，脸色发白。“我母亲知道我们的事了。她说，要么断了，要么她断了我的继承权。”她看着你。“告诉我该怎么办。”\n# en: Lyra holding a letter with a black seal, her other hand clenched around a ring she has just pulled off.\nscene [8c7e83] = 莱拉手持一封黑色封印的信，另一只手紧紧攥着一枚刚摘下的戒指。\n# en: \"Choose yourself, not them.\"\nopt.left.label [1f5be4] = “选择你自己，而不是他们。”\n# en: She tears the letter in half. Then in quarters. Then she sets the quarters on fire and laughs like she hasn't in years.\nopt.left.say.1 [3f7f4b] = 她把信撕成两半。再撕成四片。然后把碎片点着，笑得像好多年没这样笑过。\n# en: \"Easy for you to say,\" she snaps. She doesn't mean it. She also doesn't take it back.\nopt.left.say.2 [66097c] = “说得轻巧。”她冲你吼道。她不是真心的。但她也没有收回这句话。\n# en: She tears the letter in half. Then in quarters. Then she sets the quarters on fire and laughs like she hasn't in years.\nopt.left.say.3 [3f7f4b] = 她把信撕成两半。再撕成四片。然后把碎片点着，笑得像好多年没这样笑过。\n# en: \"Easy for you to say,\" she snaps. She doesn't mean it. She also doesn't take it back.\nopt.left.say.4 [66097c] = “说得轻巧。”她冲你吼道。她不是真心的。但她也没有收回这句话。\n# en: Step back so she doesn't lose everything\nopt.right.label [9c8862] = 退一步，让她不至于失去一切\n# en: You end it before she has to. She doesn't argue. That's how you know how much it costs her.\nopt.right.say.1 [27b1b0] = 在她不得不开口之前，你先结束了这段感情。她没有争辩。正因如此，你才知道这对她代价有多大。\n\n@event lyra_rival_sabotage\n# en: Sabotage in Class\ntitle [1db464] = 课堂上的暗算\n# en: Your perfect potion turns black and starts screaming. Across the room, Lyra is very carefully not looking at you.\ntext [8b133c] = 你那锅完美的药剂突然变黑，开始尖叫。教室另一头，莱拉非常小心地没往你这边看。\n# en: A cauldron spitting black smoke, a crimson-haired student walking away with a tiny vial hidden in her sleeve.\nscene [f64dbb] = 一口坩埚喷出黑烟，一个红发学生走开，袖子里藏着一只小瓶。\n# en: Retaliate\nopt.left.label [5cb1e8] = 报复回去\n# en: Next lesson, her wand produces only bubbles. Pink ones. She knows. You both know. It's war. It's sort of fun.\nopt.left.say.1 [6d8609] = 下节课，她的魔杖只能冒出泡泡。粉红色的。她知道。你们俩都知道。开战了。还挺好玩的。\n# en: You're caught slipping something into her bag. She looks genuinely delighted.\nopt.left.say.2 [7dfe71] = 你往她包里塞东西时被逮了个正着。她看起来真心高兴。\n# en: Confront her directly\nopt.right.label [eed285] = 当面质问她\n# en: \"That was beneath you.\" She flushes. \"...Yes. It was.\" The next day there's a perfect replacement potion on your desk.\nopt.right.say.1 [3c2fd2] = “这有失你的身份。”她脸红了。“……是。是有失身份。”第二天，你桌上放着一份完美的替代药剂。\n\n@event lyra_burnout\n# en: Lyra Falls\ntitle [d0abf9] = 莱拉倒下了\n# en: You find Lyra face-down on a library desk at dawn, not asleep — out cold. She's been studying for three days straight. \"Don't tell anyone,\" she mumbles when she comes round. \"Mother can't know.\"\ntext [794170] = 黎明时分你发现莱拉趴在图书馆的桌上，不是睡着了——是昏过去了。她已经连续学了三天。“别告诉任何人，”她醒过来时含糊地说，“不能让母亲知道。”\n# en: A girl collapsed across a library desk among scattered notes, quill still in hand, candle burned down to nothing.\nscene [91fdd5] = 一个女孩瘫倒在图书馆书桌上，周围散落着笔记，手里还握着羽毛笔，蜡烛已经燃尽。\n# en: Take her to the infirmary anyway\nopt.left.label [26e217] = 还是把她送去医务室\n# en: She's furious. Then she sleeps for nineteen hours. When she wakes up, she says, quietly, \"Thank you.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [0d4dbd] = 她气坏了。然后她睡了十九个小时。醒来时，她轻声说：“谢谢你。”\n# en: Cover for her and make her rest\nopt.right.label [bfbbb0] = 替她打掩护，逼她休息\n# en: You tell her professors she has a cold and sit by her door with your homework. She sleeps. You keep watch.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b1d64] = 你告诉她的教授们她感冒了，然后带着作业守在她门口。她睡了。你守着。\n\n@event tobias_intro\n# en: The Lost Boy\ntitle [3f2dc0] = 迷路的男孩\n# en: A big, broad-shouldered boy is leading a crying first-year through the corridors. \"Don't worry, I know exactly where the infirmary is,\" he says, and walks confidently into a broom cupboard.\ntext [03f851] = 一个高大宽肩的男生正领着一个哭鼻子的一年级生穿过走廊。“别担心，我很清楚医务室在哪儿。”他说着，满怀信心地走进了扫帚间。\n# en: A large, gentle-faced boy leading a crying first-year by the hand down the wrong corridor, both very lost.\nscene [0ae971] = 一个面容温和的大个子男生牵着一个哭泣的一年级生走在错误的走廊里，两人都迷路了。\n# en: Point them the right way\nopt.left.label [f53c4f] = 给他们指路\n# en: \"Ah. Left, you say. Right. I mean, correct.\" He grins, enormous and sheepish. \"Tobias. Turnip scholarship. Thanks.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [74c134] = “啊。你说左边。对。我是说，没错。”他咧嘴一笑，笑容大大的，有点不好意思。“托比亚斯。芜菁奖学金。谢啦。”\n# en: Walk with them\nopt.right.label [799c3a] = 陪他们一起走\n# en: You end up taking the scenic route, and the first-year stops crying because Tobias is telling a very long story about a goat.\nopt.right.say.1 [c5721c] = 你们最后绕了一条风景路线，一年级生不哭了，因为托比亚斯在讲一个关于山羊的超长故事。\n\n@event tobias_homesick\n# en: Homesick\ntitle [a133e0] = 想家\n# en: Tobias has gone quiet. He's planted seedlings in every cup in the common room. \"Harvest at home,\" he says. \"First one I've missed. Ma's doing it alone.\"\ntext [fd42a8] = 托比亚斯变得沉默了。他在公共休息室的每个杯子里都种了幼苗。“家里在收庄稼，”他说，“这是我第一次错过。我妈一个人在干。”\n# en: A dormitory windowsill crowded with clay pots of seedlings, a big boy watering them with a tiny can.\nscene [433f64] = 宿舍窗台上挤满了种着幼苗的陶盆，一个大个子男生正用一只小水壶浇水。\n# en: Cheer him up with a joke\nopt.left.label [c33487] = 讲个笑话逗他开心\n# en: Your joke is terrible. He laughs anyway, the big rumbling laugh, and that seems to help.\nopt.left.say.1 [e60401] = 你的笑话烂透了。他还是笑了，那种轰隆隆的大笑，好像真的管用。\n# en: Help him build a window garden\nopt.right.label [8aab52] = 帮他建一个窗台菜园\n# en: You buy seeds and a stack of pots. By the end of the week the common room smells of tomato leaves. Tobias whistles while he waters them.\nopt.right.say.1 [4f8881] = 你买来种子和一摞花盆。到周末，公共休息室里满是番茄叶的味道。托比亚斯浇水时吹着口哨。\n\n@event tobias_bad_harvest\n# en: The Harvest Failed\ntitle [aab2ca] = 歉收\n# en: Tobias is packing. Blight took the harvest. His family needs money, and hands. \"Thirty crowns would get them through winter,\" he says, not asking. \"But I haven't got thirty crowns. I've got two arms.\"\ntext [b3ac08] = 托比亚斯在收拾行李。枯萎病毁了收成。他家需要钱，也需要人手。“三十克朗就能让他们熬过冬天，”他说，并不是在开口要，“可我没有三十克朗。我只有两条胳膊。”\n# en: A boy packing a battered trunk in an empty dormitory, a letter with smudged handwriting open on the bed.\nscene [7cc0f7] = 空荡荡的宿舍里，一个男生在收拾一只破旧的行李箱，床上摊着一封字迹模糊的信。\n# en: Persuade him to stay and find work here\nopt.left.label [4e1985] = 劝他留下，在这里找工作\n# en: You walk him to the job board. He signs up for double shifts at the stables and sends every crown home. He stays.\nopt.left.say.1 [3cac05] = 你陪他去了招工告示板。他报了马厩的双班，把每一枚克朗都寄回了家。他留下了。\n# en: He listens, nods, and takes the barge in the morning. He's back in two months, thinner and quieter.\nopt.left.say.2 [373ae1] = 他听着，点点头，第二天早上坐上了驳船。两个月后他回来了，瘦了，也更沉默了。\n# en: Give him thirty crowns\nopt.right.label [902ad2] = 给他三十克朗\n# en: He tries to refuse three times. Then he hugs you so hard your spine cracks. \"I'll pay you back. Every crown. Every one.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2214f7] = 他推辞了三次。然后抱住你，抱得你脊椎咔咔响。“我会还你的。每一枚克朗。每一枚。”\n\n@event tobias_repays\n# en: Every Crown\ntitle [807b67] = 每一枚克朗\n# en: A lumpy, hand-stitched pouch on your pillow: thirty crowns exactly, in small coins. And a jar of honey from home. A note in big, careful letters: \"TOLD YOU. — T.\"\ntext [150790] = 你枕头上放着一只鼓鼓囊囊、手工缝制的小袋子：正好三十克朗，全是零钱。还有一罐家里的蜂蜜。一张字条，字写得又大又认真：“说到做到。——托”\n# en: A clumsy hand-stitched pouch of coins and a jar of honey left on a dormitory pillow.\nscene [1e69a7] = 宿舍枕头上一只笨拙的手缝钱袋和一罐蜂蜜。\n# en: Smile\nopt.ok.label [c2972f] = 笑一笑\n# en: The honey tastes of a farm you have never seen and somehow already miss.\nopt.ok.say.1 [8639ca] = 那罐蜂蜜尝起来像一座你从没见过、却已经开始想念的农场。\n\n@event tobias_godparent\n# en: The Mandrake Baby\ntitle [5adaaf] = 曼德拉草宝宝\n# en: Tobias presents a flowerpot containing a very small mandrake in a knitted hat. \"This is Gerald,\" he says. \"I'd like you to be his godparent.\" Gerald screams softly.\ntext [287127] = 托比亚斯捧出一个花盆，里面是一株戴着针织帽的小小曼德拉草。“这是杰拉尔德，”他说，“我想请你当他的教父母。”杰拉尔德轻声尖叫。\n# en: A tiny mandrake in a pot wearing a knitted hat, a beaming boy presenting it like a newborn.\nscene [39f8e3] = 一株戴着针织帽的小曼德拉草种在盆里，一个笑容灿烂的男生像捧着新生儿一样把它捧出来。\n# en: Politely decline\nopt.left.label [8a601d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: \"No pressure,\" says Tobias, visibly pressured. Gerald glares at you from under his hat.\nopt.left.say.1 [00f5a4] = “没压力的。”托比亚斯说，明显压力很大。杰拉尔德从帽子底下瞪着你。\n# en: Accept with honour\nopt.right.label [395d80] = 荣幸地接受\n# en: You are now Gerald's godparent. Gerald bites you. Tobias says this means Gerald loves you.\nopt.right.say.1 [95091f] = 你现在是杰拉尔德的教父母了。杰拉尔德咬了你一口。托比亚斯说这代表杰拉尔德爱你。\n\n@event tobias_bullied\n# en: Tobias and the Prefect\ntitle [0d0b40] = 托比亚斯与级长\n# en: Bram is doing an impression of Tobias's accent in the corridor. People are laughing. Tobias is pretending not to hear, ears burning red.\ntext [cf84da] = 布拉姆在走廊里模仿托比亚斯的口音。大家都在笑。托比亚斯假装没听见，耳朵红得发烫。\n# en: A burly prefect mocking a big farm boy in a crowded corridor, students sniggering, the boy's ears bright red.\nscene [d6b2fe] = 拥挤的走廊里，一个魁梧的级长在嘲笑一个高大的农家男孩，学生们在窃笑，男孩的耳朵通红。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.left.label [03d24a] = 不掺和\n# en: Tobias doesn't mention it. He doesn't sit with you at dinner, either.\nopt.left.say.1 [be0b18] = 托比亚斯没提这事。晚饭时他也没和你坐一起。\n# en: Step in\nopt.right.label [db9576] = 站出来\n# en: \"Funny,\" you say. \"Do your father next.\" The laughter turns on Bram. He stalks off. Tobias stares at you in awe.\nopt.right.say.1 [26c55a] = “真好笑，”你说，“下一个学你爸吧。”笑声转向了布拉姆。他气冲冲地走了。托比亚斯敬畏地看着你。\n# en: Bram turns his attention to you instead, with interest. Tobias brings you a pie later. A whole pie.\nopt.right.say.2 [a956b8] = 布拉姆转而饶有兴趣地盯上了你。后来托比亚斯给你送来一个馅饼。一整个。\n\n@event tobias_confession\n# en: A Confession, With Vegetables\ntitle [a19fc5] = 一场带着蔬菜的告白\n# en: Tobias corners you in the greenhouse holding a very large pumpkin. \"I grew this,\" he says. \"For you. It's the best one. I didn't know how else to — I like you. Like that. I've practised this on the pumpkin. Sorry.\"\ntext [157367] = 托比亚斯抱着一个很大的南瓜在温室里拦住了你。“这是我种的，”他说，“送你的。是最好的那个。我不知道还能怎么——我喜欢你。是那种喜欢。我对着南瓜练习过了。对不起。”\n# en: A big boy holding out a perfect pumpkin like an offering in a greenhouse, cheeks scarlet.\nscene [563b87] = 温室里一个大个子男生像献礼一样捧出一个完美的南瓜，脸颊通红。\n# en: Let him down gently\nopt.left.label [b009f9] = 温柔地拒绝他\n# en: He nods a lot. \"That's all right. That's fine. You can still have the pumpkin.\" You keep it until it rots, out of loyalty.\nopt.left.say.1 [f837ce] = 他拼命点头。“没关系。没事的。南瓜你还是可以留着。”出于情义，你一直留到它烂掉。\n# en: Say yes\nopt.right.label [c73ccb] = 答应他\n# en: He drops the pumpkin. It's fine; pumpkins are sturdy. So, it turns out, are his hugs.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f29ab] = 他手里的南瓜掉了。没事；南瓜很结实。事实证明，他的拥抱也是。\n\n@event tobias_picnic\n# en: A Picnic in the Orchard\ntitle [2dff91] = 果园野餐\n# en: Tobias has packed a picnic: {~bread he baked, cheese he begged from the kitchens, apples from the orchard he has befriended|a pie with your initials in the crust, slightly burnt, and a jar of honey from someone he helped|plums, hard cheese and something he calls a salad, which is mostly flowers}. \"No {~exams|essays|professors} for six hours,\" he announces. \"I checked.\"\ntext [70bc74] = 托比亚斯准备了野餐：{~他自己烤的面包，从厨房软磨硬泡来的奶酪，还有他“交了朋友”的果园里的苹果|一个酥皮上刻着你名字缩写的馅饼，有点烤焦了，还有一罐他帮过的人送的蜂蜜|李子、硬奶酪，还有一样他称为沙拉的东西，基本上全是花}。“六个小时内没有{~考试|论文|教授}，”他宣布，“我查过了。”\n# en: A checked blanket under blossoming apple trees, a basket of bread and cheese, two students lying in the dappled light.\nscene [66c097] = 开花的苹果树下铺着格子毯，一篮面包和奶酪，两个学生躺在斑驳的光影里。\n# en: Bring your books anyway\nopt.left.label [b8ab05] = 还是带上书\n# en: You study on the blanket. He eyes the books, sighs a very small sigh, and falls asleep with his head in your lap. It's still lovely.\nopt.left.say.1 [fcc2e1] = 你在毯子上学习。他瞄了一眼那些书，很轻很轻地叹了口气，然后枕着你的腿睡着了。依然很美好。\n# en: Leave everything behind\nopt.right.label [1ca23f] = 把一切都抛在脑后\n# en: The afternoon goes golden and slow. You can't remember the last time you didn't think about grades.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c67d2] = 午后变得金黄而缓慢。你已经记不起上一次不去想成绩是什么时候了。\n\n@event seraphine_intro\n# en: Your Left Shoe\ntitle [0404ae] = 你的左脚鞋\n# en: A girl drifts past you in the corridor and says, without stopping, \"You're going to lose your left shoe today. Don't worry, it's under the stairs in the east wing.\"\ntext [01fffe] = 一个女孩从你身边飘过，头也不回地说：“你今天会丢一只左脚的鞋。别担心，它在东翼的楼梯底下。”\n# en: A slight girl with silver-grey eyes and a scarf of stars passing in a corridor, pointing absently at someone's feet.\nscene [c9cd41] = 一个纤瘦的女孩围着星星图案的围巾，长着银灰色的眼睛，在走廊里经过时漫不经心地指着某人的脚。\n# en: Ignore her\nopt.left.label [44f360] = 不理她\n# en: That afternoon a staircase moves and eats your left shoe. It is, eventually, under the stairs in the east wing.\nopt.left.say.1 [b13d26] = 那天下午，一道楼梯挪了位置，吃掉了你的左脚鞋。最终，它确实在东翼的楼梯底下。\n# en: Ask how she knows\nopt.right.label [48f36e] = 问她怎么知道的\n# en: \"I don't know things,\" she says. \"Things know me.\" Then: \"Seraphine. You'll want the shoe back by four.\" You get it back at 3:58.\nopt.right.say.1 [ef5d82] = “我不知道事情，”她说，“是事情知道我。”然后又说：“瑟拉芬。你会想在四点前把鞋拿回来的。”你在三点五十八分拿了回来。\n\n@event seraphine_tarot\n# en: The Cards\ntitle [c5101b] = 牌\n# en: Seraphine shuffles a battered tarot deck. \"One card,\" she says. \"For you. Only if you want it. They're quite rude sometimes.\"\ntext [95625a] = 瑟拉芬洗着一副破旧的塔罗牌。“抽一张，”她说，“给你的。只要你想。它们有时候挺没礼貌的。”\n# en: A tarot spread on a velvet cloth in candlelight, a girl's pale fingers turning over a card showing a lightning-struck tower.\nscene [8a2f94] = 烛光下天鹅绒布上摊开的塔罗牌阵，一个女孩苍白的手指翻开一张画着雷击高塔的牌。\n# en: Draw a card\nopt.left.label [a80dc6] = 抽一张\n# en: The Star. \"Hope,\" she says, delighted. \"And someone's going to tell you a secret. Oh — it's me. I like you.\" She blushes.\nopt.left.say.1 [bfb1c6] = 星星。“希望，”她高兴地说，“还有人要告诉你一个秘密。噢——是我。我喜欢你。”她脸红了。\n# en: The Tower. She looks at it a long time. \"It's fine,\" she says. \"Towers fall. That's what they're for.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [ecbdc5] = 高塔。她看了很久。“没关系，”她说，“塔会倒。塔就是用来倒的。”\n# en: Decline\nopt.right.label [eaef39] = 不抽\n# en: \"Good,\" she says, putting the deck away. \"Knowing is overrated.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [1ff9e2] = “很好。”她把牌收起来，“知道太多被高估了。”\n\n@event seraphine_lake_vision\n# en: She Walks Into the Lake\ntitle [7cd453] = 她走进了湖里\n# en: From your window you see Seraphine walking into the lake in her nightgown, eyes shining silver, already up to her waist.\ntext [89cff3] = 你从窗口看见瑟拉芬穿着睡袍走进湖里，眼睛泛着银光，水已经没到了腰。\n# en: A girl in a nightgown wading into a misty lake at night, eyes glowing silver, arms outstretched toward something under the water.\nscene [21af5a] = 夜里一个穿睡袍的女孩涉入雾气笼罩的湖中，双眼泛着银光，双臂伸向水下的某样东西。\n# en: Wake a prefect\nopt.left.label [9162cd] = 叫醒一位级长\n# en: The prefect drags her out, coughing. She doesn't remember anything. She looks at you strangely for days.\nopt.left.say.1 [f25863] = 级长把她拖了出来，她咳个不停。她什么都不记得了。接下来几天，她看你的眼神都怪怪的。\n# en: Run down and pull her out\nopt.right.label [4787f1] = 冲下去把她拉出来\n# en: You reach her just as she goes under. On the shore she wakes and whispers, \"It was calling me. It called you too.\" Then she holds on very tight.\nopt.right.say.1 [d29a81] = 她沉下去的那一刻你抓住了她。在岸上，她醒过来低声说：“它在呼唤我。它也呼唤了你。”然后她紧紧抱住了你。\n# en: You both nearly drown. You both come down with Lake Fever. She brings you soup that tastes faintly of the future.\nopt.right.say.2 [571f07] = 你们俩都差点淹死。你们俩都得了湖热病。她给你送来的汤，隐约有未来的味道。\n\n@event seraphine_future\n# en: I Saw You Old\ntitle [9b881b] = 我看见你老了\n# en: \"I saw you old,\" Seraphine says suddenly. \"Last night. You were happy. Really happy. There was someone with you.\" She pauses. \"Do you want to know who?\"\ntext [c5d67f] = “我看见你老了，”瑟拉芬突然说，“昨晚。你很幸福。真的很幸福。有人陪着你。”她顿了顿，“你想知道是谁吗？”\n# en: Two students sitting on the rim of a dry fountain, one looking up at the stars, the other looking at the first with a soft smile.\nscene [027150] = 两个学生坐在干涸的喷泉边沿，一个仰望星空，另一个温柔地笑着看向前者。\n# en: Yes, tell me\nopt.left.label [f7e607] = 想，告诉我\n# en: She looks at you for a long time. \"I'll tell you when you're old,\" she says, and goes pink.\nopt.left.say.1 [4bb771] = 她看了你很久。“等你老了我再告诉你。”她说，脸红了。\n# en: \"Someone kind,\" she says. \"That's all I could see. Their hands were kind.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [19f673] = “一个温柔的人，”她说，“我只看得到这么多。那双手很温柔。”\n# en: No, let it be a surprise\nopt.right.label [97a4ad] = 不了，留个惊喜吧\n# en: She smiles like you've given her a present. \"That's the right answer. Nobody ever gives the right answer.\" Not knowing nags at you for days.\nopt.right.say.1 [ce9281] = 她笑了，像你送了她一份礼物。“这是正确答案。从来没人给出过正确答案。”不知道答案这件事，让你心痒了好几天。\n# en: Later you wonder whether she could see the someone only because you were standing next to her when she looked. You don't ask. She goes pink anyway, as if you had.\nopt.right.say.2 [e8c8b2] = 后来你想，她之所以能看见那个人，会不会只是因为她看的时候你正站在她身边。你没有问。她却还是红了脸，好像你问了一样。\n\n@event seraphine_overwhelmed\n# en: Too Many Tomorrows\ntitle [409210] = 太多个明天\n# en: Seraphine hasn't come to class in a week. You find her in a dark storeroom with her hands over her eyes. \"It's too loud,\" she whispers. \"Everyone's futures. All at once.\"\ntext [92ab7f] = 瑟拉芬已经一周没来上课了。你在一间黑漆漆的储藏室里找到了她，她用双手捂着眼睛。“太吵了，”她低声说，“每个人的未来。一下子全涌过来。”\n# en: A girl curled in a dark corner of an observatory with her hands over her eyes, a teacup gone cold beside her.\nscene [7f0e93] = 天文台黑暗的角落里蜷缩着一个女孩，双手捂着眼睛，身旁的茶早已凉透。\n# en: Fetch Sister Amaranth\nopt.left.label [0ebdc9] = 去叫阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Amaranth gives her a sleeping draught and a dark room. She recovers. She doesn't say much about it.\nopt.left.say.1 [42cef1] = 阿玛兰斯给了她一剂安眠药和一间暗室。她恢复了。她对此不怎么提起。\n# en: Sit with her in the dark\nopt.right.label [30debe] = 在黑暗中陪着她\n# en: You don't speak. After an hour she takes her hands from her eyes. \"Yours is quiet,\" she says. \"Your future. It's very quiet. I like it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [44b951] = 你没有说话。一个小时后，她把手从眼睛上拿开。“你的很安静，”她说，“你的未来。非常安静。我喜欢。”\n\n@event seraphine_confession\n# en: She Already Knew\ntitle [e91972] = 她早就知道\n# en: \"I've seen this conversation forty-one times,\" Seraphine says. \"In twenty-nine of them, you say yes. I've been very nervous about the other twelve.\" She holds out her hand. \"I like you. That part was always the same.\"\ntext [94995b] = “这段对话我已经看过四十一次了，”瑟拉芬说，“其中二十九次你答应了。另外十二次让我很紧张。”她伸出手。“我喜欢你。这一部分每次都一样。”\n# en: A girl with silver eyes holding out a single falling-star seed in her palm on a dark observatory balcony.\nscene [966292] = 漆黑的天文台阳台上，一个银色眼睛的女孩掌心托着一粒流星的种子递过来。\n# en: Gently say no\nopt.left.label [f46b92] = 温柔地拒绝\n# en: She nods. \"Twelve,\" she says softly. \"I knew it might be twelve.\" She's kind to you afterwards. Somehow that hurts more.\nopt.left.say.1 [4e2d00] = 她点点头。“十二，”她轻声说，“我就知道可能是十二。”之后她对你依然很温柔。不知为何，这更让人难过。\n# en: Take her hand\nopt.right.label [4658a2] = 握住她的手\n# en: \"Twenty-nine,\" she whispers, and laughs, and for once she's entirely in the present.\nopt.right.say.1 [cd7951] = “二十九。”她低声说，笑了起来，难得地，她完完全全活在当下。\n\n@event corvin_intro\n# en: Light Fingers\ntitle [fc7fa5] = 手快\n# en: Someone bumps you in the crowd. A moment later a dark-haired boy is bowing and holding up your purse. \"You dropped this,\" he says. \"Into my pocket. Terrible habit of yours. Corvin Ashby.\"\ntext [88b693] = 人群里有人撞了你一下。片刻之后，一个黑发男生朝你鞠躬，手里举着你的钱袋。“你掉了这个，”他说，“掉进了我的口袋。你这习惯可真糟。科尔文·阿什比。”\n# en: A dark-haired student with a crooked grin holding up a purse between two fingers, mid-bow.\nscene [77be60] = 一个嘴角歪歪笑着的黑发学生，两指夹着一只钱袋，正弯腰行礼。\n# en: Snatch it back and report him\nopt.left.label [69c705] = 一把夺回来，举报他\n# en: He's gone before the prefect arrives. Your purse is all there, plus an IOU you don't remember receiving.\nopt.left.say.1 [2b1b37] = 级长来之前他就不见了。你的钱袋一分不少，只是多了一张你不记得收过的欠条。\n# en: Laugh\nopt.right.label [52d15e] = 笑出声\n# en: \"Oh, I like you,\" he says, and teaches you the trick on the spot. You practise it on him. He lets you win. Probably. Later, you are three crowns short. Probably a coincidence.\nopt.right.say.1 [0c1a5b] = “噢，我喜欢你。”他说，当场就把这招教给了你。你拿他练手。他让你赢了。大概吧。后来你发现少了三克朗。大概是巧合。\n\n@event corvin_kitchen_raid\n# en: The Kitchen Raid\ntitle [0e6a0c] = 突袭厨房\n# en: \"Midnight,\" Corvin whispers. \"The cooks have made forty treacle tarts for the faculty dinner. Forty. Nobody needs forty.\"\ntext [ccccc4] = “午夜，”科尔文低声说，“厨师们给教职员晚宴做了四十个糖浆馅饼。四十个。没人需要四十个。”\n# en: Two students creeping through a cavernous castle kitchen at night, a mountain of tarts on a table, a sleeping cook snoring by the hearth.\nscene [c71678] = 夜里两个学生蹑手蹑脚地穿过城堡里洞穴般的大厨房，桌上堆着小山一样的馅饼，炉边的厨师打着呼噜。\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.left.label [4bf72b] = 今晚不行\n# en: He goes alone. The next morning there's a treacle tart on your pillow. You decide not to ask.\nopt.left.say.1 [4aa606] = 他自己去了。第二天早上你的枕头上放着一个糖浆馅饼。你决定不问。\n# en: He only took the one, he tells you later, because you'd reminded him about his last warning. It's the closest he comes to saying thank you.\nopt.left.say.2 [adbc71] = 他后来告诉你，他只拿了那一个，因为你提醒过他那是最后警告。这是他最接近说谢谢的一次。\n# en: Lead the raid\nopt.right.label [a419e4] = 带头突袭\n# en: You get out with nine tarts and a bottle of elderflower cordial. You eat them on the roof until sunrise.\nopt.right.say.1 [f86666] = 你们带着九个馅饼和一瓶接骨木花露溜了出来。在屋顶上一直吃到日出。\n# en: The cook wakes up. You both spend a week scrubbing pans, laughing too hard to work.\nopt.right.say.2 [eb0bd4] = 厨师醒了。你们俩刷了一星期的锅，笑得根本干不动活。\n# en: Step past the cook\nopt.up.label [53ad3a] = 从厨子身边一步闪过\n# en: You step from shadow to shadow across the kitchen, past the sleeping cook, and back with an armful of tarts before Corvin has finished picking the lock. He stares at you. \"Saint,\" he says, \"where did you learn that?\"\nopt.up.say.1 [e8e205] = 你从一片阴影跨到另一片阴影，穿过厨房，经过睡着的厨子，抱着一怀馅饼回来时，科尔文的锁还没撬完。他瞪着你。“圣人，”他说，“你在哪儿学的这个？”\n\n@event corvin_debt\n# en: Corvin's Little Problem\ntitle [7d9f5d] = 科尔文的小麻烦\n# en: Corvin has a black eye and a very casual expression. \"Small misunderstanding with some trolls,\" he says. \"Thirty crowns would clear it right up. I'll pay you back. Probably with interest. Possibly with a goat.\"\ntext [6c578d] = 科尔文顶着一只黑眼圈，表情非常随意。“和几个巨魔有点小误会，”他说，“三十克朗就能摆平。我会还你的。大概还带利息。也可能带一只山羊。”\n# en: A boy with a black eye sitting on the dormitory stairs, trying very hard to look casual.\nscene [720349] = 一个顶着黑眼圈的男生坐在宿舍楼梯上，非常努力地装作若无其事。\n# en: Refuse\nopt.left.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: \"Course. No problem.\" He has another black eye the next week. He's still friendly. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [71b32c] = “当然。没问题。”下周他又多了一只黑眼圈。他对你依然友好。大体上。\n# en: Give him thirty crowns\nopt.right.label [902ad2] = 给他三十克朗\n# en: He stares at the coins, then at you. \"No one's ever just... done that.\" He's quiet for a whole minute. A record.\nopt.right.say.1 [9d7807] = 他盯着硬币，又盯着你。“从来没有人就这么……给过。”他安静了整整一分钟。破纪录了。\n\n@event corvin_repays\n# en: Interest\ntitle [8f88f3] = 利息\n# en: There's a fat purse on your desk and a jack of spades tucked under it. It's heavy, and it clinks.\ntext [5a0eec] = 你桌上放着一只鼓鼓的钱袋，下面压着一张黑桃 J。沉甸甸的，叮当作响。\n# en: A small brass key and a fat purse left on a desk with a playing card, the jack of spades, tucked under them.\nscene [476a3b] = 桌上放着一把黄铜小钥匙和一只鼓鼓的钱袋，下面压着一张扑克牌——黑桃 J。\n# en: Count it\nopt.left.label [5fce81] = 数一数\n# en: Sixty crowns. A note: \"Interest. Don't ask. — C.\" You don't ask.\nopt.left.say.1 [f9e2eb] = 六十克朗。一张字条：“利息。别问。——科”你没问。\n# en: Three crowns and a very convincing IOU for the other twenty-seven, with a doodle of a goat.\nopt.left.say.2 [8ec290] = 三克朗，外加一张写着剩下二十七克朗、非常有说服力的欠条，上面还画了只山羊。\n# en: Find him and ask where it came from\nopt.right.label [397662] = 找到他，问钱哪儿来的\n# en: \"I won it,\" he says. \"Fairly. Mostly.\" He pushes the exact thirty into your hand. \"The rest's mine. I've got another debt to pay.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [efb600] = “赢来的，”他说，“公平地。大体上。”他把正好三十克朗塞进你手里。“剩下的是我的。我还有另一笔债要还。”\n\n@event corvin_rooftop\n# en: Up on the Roof\ntitle [6ebee8] = 屋顶上\n# en: On the roof at 2am, Corvin goes quiet. \"I'm on my last warning,\" he says. \"One more detention and they send me home. And home is... not a good place.\" He laughs, badly.\ntext [dc6f28] = 凌晨两点的屋顶上，科尔文沉默了下来。“我已经是最后一次警告了，”他说，“再关一次禁闭，他们就送我回家。而家……不是个好地方。”他笑了，笑得很难看。\n# en: Two students sitting on a steep slate roof under a full moon, legs dangling over a dizzying drop, sharing a bottle of something fizzy.\nscene [802031] = 满月下两个学生坐在陡峭的石板屋顶上，双腿悬在令人眩晕的高处，分享着一瓶冒泡的饮料。\n# en: Tell him to keep his head down\nopt.left.label [b7afec] = 叫他低调点\n# en: \"Head down. Right. I'll try anything once.\" He keeps his head down for almost a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [199c3a] = “低调。好。什么我都愿意试一次。”他低调了将近一个月。\n# en: Promise you'll cover for him\nopt.right.label [f74b9c] = 保证会帮他打掩护\n# en: He looks at you like nobody has ever promised him anything. \"You're an idiot,\" he says. \"Thank you.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [34b1d4] = 他看着你，好像从没有人给过他任何承诺。“你是个傻瓜，”他说，“谢谢你。”\n\n@event corvin_framed\n# en: Corvin Is Framed\ntitle [bd8e3b] = 科尔文被陷害了\n# en: The headmistress's silver goblet has been found in Corvin's trunk. He swears he didn't take it. Bram, who found it, looks very pleased. Corvin is one detention from expulsion.\ntext [7254e4] = 院长的银高脚杯在科尔文的行李箱里被找到了。他发誓不是他拿的。发现它的布拉姆看起来非常得意。科尔文离被开除只差一次禁闭。\n# en: A boy standing before a disciplinary desk, a stolen silver goblet on it, a smug prefect at his shoulder.\nscene [c0253d] = 一个男生站在纪律桌前，桌上放着一只失窃的银高脚杯，一个得意的级长站在他肩旁。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.left.label [03d24a] = 不掺和\n# en: Corvin talks his way out, barely. He doesn't look at you for a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [90a1f1] = 科尔文勉强靠嘴皮子脱了身。他一个月没正眼看你。\n# en: Corvin is suspended for a month. When he comes back, he's different. Quieter.\nopt.left.say.2 [506649] = 科尔文被停学一个月。回来时，他变了。更沉默了。\n# en: Find proof he was framed\nopt.right.label [0665b1] = 找出他被陷害的证据\n# en: You find the bill of sale: Bram bought a fake. Bram is stripped of his sash for the rest of the year. Corvin buys you a goat. An actual goat.\nopt.right.say.1 [e722b3] = 你找到了那张收据：是布拉姆买了个赝品。布拉姆被摘掉了绶带，这一年都别想再戴。科尔文送了你一只山羊。真的山羊。\n# en: You can't prove it. But you stood up for him in front of everyone, and he'll never forget it. Neither will Bram.\nopt.right.say.2 [1b083f] = 你证明不了。但你当着所有人的面为他站了出来，他永远不会忘记。布拉姆也不会。\n\n@event corvin_confession\n# en: Corvin, Serious for Once\ntitle [4a31ce] = 难得认真的科尔文\n# en: Corvin holds out a playing card. The queen of hearts. His hand isn't quite steady. \"No trick,\" he says. \"No angle. I just — I like you more than I like anything, and I'm terrified, and I wanted you to have this.\"\ntext [87fdba] = 科尔文递过来一张扑克牌。红桃 Q。他的手有点不稳。“没有把戏，”他说，“没有算计。我只是——我喜欢你，胜过喜欢任何东西，我怕得要命，而且我想把这个给你。”\n# en: A boy holding out a playing card — the queen of hearts — with an unsteady hand, no grin for once.\nscene [e450dd] = 一个男生用不太稳的手递出一张扑克牌——红桃 Q——这一次他没有笑。\n# en: Gently decline\nopt.left.label [975956] = 温柔地拒绝\n# en: He laughs, too quickly. \"Worth a shot.\" He keeps the card. You see it sometimes, tucked in his sleeve.\nopt.left.say.1 [cd0293] = 他笑了，笑得太快。“值得一试。”他留着那张牌。你有时会看见它藏在他的袖子里。\n# en: Take the card\nopt.right.label [e7f785] = 接过那张牌\n# en: For once, Corvin Ashby has nothing to say. He just kisses you, and then grins, and then can't stop grinning.\nopt.right.say.1 [108f24] = 科尔文·阿什比难得地无话可说。他只是吻了你，然后咧嘴笑，然后再也停不下来。\n\n@event corvin_runaway\n# en: Let's Leave Tonight\ntitle [03c686] = 今晚就走吧\n# en: Corvin's at your door at midnight, bag over his shoulder. \"There's a sky-barge at the dock and nobody guarding it. Let's go. Tonight. The world's huge and we're wasting it in classrooms.\"\ntext [c8d466] = 午夜，科尔文背着包站在你门口。“码头上停着一艘天空驳船，没人看守。走吧。今晚。世界那么大，我们却在教室里浪费它。”\n# en: A small sky-barge tied to the academy dock at midnight, lanterns lit, a boy at the tiller holding out his hand.\nscene [6e8955] = 午夜时分，一艘小天空驳船系在学院码头上，灯笼亮着，一个男生站在舵边向你伸出手。\n# en: Stay and finish what you started\nopt.left.label [6b504c] = 留下来，把开始的事做完\n# en: He looks at you for a long time. Then he drops the bag. \"Fine. But after graduation, we steal a boat. A big one.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [d46286] = 他看了你很久。然后放下了包。“行。但毕业以后，我们去偷条船。一条大的。”\n# en: Take his hand\nopt.right.label [0c017b] = 握住他的手\n\n@event mir_intro\n# en: An Explosion in the West Wing\ntitle [dbebf9] = 西翼爆炸\n# en: The west wing explodes. Not badly. A figure in goggles stumbles out of the purple smoke, coughing, and gives you a thumbs-up. \"Don't worry! That was intentional! Mostly! Can you hold this?\" They hand you a ticking teapot.\ntext [61d334] = 西翼炸了。不严重。一个戴护目镜的身影咳嗽着从紫烟里踉跄出来，冲你竖起大拇指。“别担心！是故意的！大体上！能帮我拿一下这个吗？”TA递给你一只滴答作响的茶壶。\n# en: A cloud of purple smoke billowing from a workshop door, a soot-covered student emerging with goggles and a thumbs-up.\nscene [6592e9] = 一团紫烟从工坊门口滚滚涌出，一个满身煤灰、戴着护目镜的学生走出来竖起大拇指。\n# en: Put the teapot down and back away\nopt.left.label [0e8c08] = 放下茶壶，往后退\n# en: The teapot stops ticking and starts humming the academy anthem. \"Mir,\" they say. \"They/them. Thanks for not throwing it.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [e3a008] = 茶壶停止了滴答，开始哼起院歌。“米尔，”TA说，“代词用 TA。谢谢你没把它扔出去。”\n# en: Hold the teapot steady\nopt.right.label [74455a] = 稳稳端住茶壶\n# en: You keep it level while they twist a valve. It stops ticking. \"You've got good hands,\" Mir says. \"I'm going to need those.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [ce47f9] = 你端平茶壶，TA拧了拧一个阀门。滴答声停了。“你的手很稳，”米尔说，“我会需要这双手的。”\n# en: The teapot goes off. Your eyebrows survive, mostly. Mir is apologising and laughing at the same time.\nopt.right.say.2 [3dba78] = 茶壶炸了。你的眉毛基本保住了。米尔一边道歉一边笑。\n\n@event mir_owl_gift\n# en: A Present With Gears\ntitle [940c6d] = 带齿轮的礼物\n# en: Mir presents a brass owl with amber glass eyes. It blinks, hoots the hour and says \"HOMEWORK\" in a tinny voice. \"Made it for you. It remembers deadlines. And birthdays. And grudges, slightly.\"\ntext [1b3dc2] = 米尔拿出一只长着琥珀玻璃眼睛的黄铜猫头鹰。它眨眨眼，报了时，然后用细细的声音说：“作业。”“给你做的。它会记住截止日期。还有生日。还有一点点仇。”\n# en: A small brass owl with glowing amber eyes perched on a student's wrist, its clockwork feathers fluffing.\nscene [0ec99d] = 一只长着发光琥珀眼睛的小黄铜猫头鹰栖在学生的手腕上，发条羽毛蓬松起来。\n# en: It's too much\nopt.left.label [8c1449] = 这太贵重了\n# en: Mir looks crushed, then rallies. \"I'll give it to Thorne. He needs reminding to smile.\" The owl, you hear, refuses to work for him.\nopt.left.say.1 [b9afa1] = 米尔一脸受伤，随即振作起来。“那我送给索恩。他需要有人提醒他笑一笑。”你听说，那只猫头鹰拒绝为他工作。\n# en: Accept it gratefully\nopt.right.label [0fb4bf] = 感激地收下\n# en: The owl settles on your shoulder and says, \"WELCOME.\" Mir pretends to have something in their eye. At six the next morning, the owl says \"HOMEWORK.\" It says it every morning for the rest of the year.\nopt.right.say.1 [4cd7a1] = 猫头鹰落在你肩上，说：“欢迎。”米尔假装眼睛里进了东西。第二天早上六点，猫头鹰说：“作业。”接下来的一整年，它每天早上都这么说。\n\n@event mir_fair\n# en: The Artificers' Fair\ntitle [e5694f] = 奇械博览会\n# en: The Artificers' Fair. Mir's entry is under a sheet and occasionally growls. \"I need an assistant to stand next to it and look confident,\" they say. \"And possibly run.\"\ntext [f589cf] = 奇械博览会。米尔的参赛作品盖在一块布下，时不时发出低吼。“我需要一个助手站在它旁边，看起来很自信，”TA说，“可能还需要跑。”\n# en: A hall crammed with bizarre inventions — walking teapots, singing clocks — and one enormous brass machine draped in a sheet.\nscene [63de4d] = 大厅里挤满了千奇百怪的发明——会走路的茶壶、会唱歌的钟——还有一台盖着布的巨大黄铜机器。\n# en: Watch from a safe distance\nopt.left.label [5c0ef7] = 在安全距离外观看\n# en: The machine turns out to be a mechanical bear that bakes bread. It wins second place. Mir is ecstatic and flour-covered.\nopt.left.say.1 [f14a15] = 那台机器原来是一只会烤面包的机械熊。它拿了二等奖。米尔欣喜若狂，满身面粉。\n# en: You tell Mir, while they're still covered in flour, that nobody wins second prize at a hobby. They stop in the middle of a sentence.\nopt.left.say.2 [a030c8] = 趁米尔还满身面粉，你告诉 TA：没有人会在业余爱好上拿二等奖。TA 话说到一半停住了。\n# en: Be the confident assistant\nopt.right.label [bfb41b] = 当那个自信的助手\n# en: You present it like a showman. The bear bakes a perfect loaf and bows. First prize. Mir kisses the trophy, then, in a fluster, your cheek.\nopt.right.say.1 [cb3cfc] = 你像个表演家一样介绍它。熊烤出一条完美的面包，还鞠了一躬。一等奖。米尔亲了一下奖杯，然后慌慌张张地，亲了一下你的脸颊。\n# en: The bear eats your sleeve and swats you into the flour bins on its way to the judges. The crowd loves it. Third prize and a very good story.\nopt.right.say.2 [6f8c14] = 熊吃了你的袖子，在走向评委的路上还把你一巴掌拍进了面粉桶。观众爱死了。三等奖，外加一个特别好的故事。\n\n@event mir_workshop_closed\n# en: The Workshop Is Shut\ntitle [61b17b] = 工坊被封了\n# en: After the third fire this term, the faculty has padlocked Mir's workshop. Mir is sitting on a toolbox outside it, looking like someone took their heart and filed it under \"hazards\".\ntext [f5f0a5] = 这学期第三场火灾之后，学院给米尔的工坊上了锁。米尔坐在门外的工具箱上，看起来像有人把TA的心拿走，归档到了“危险品”一栏。\n# en: A workshop door chained shut with a faculty notice nailed to it, a forlorn student sitting on a toolbox outside.\nscene [d57061] = 一扇被铁链锁住的工坊门，上面钉着学院告示，一个失魂落魄的学生坐在门外的工具箱上。\n# en: Plead their case to Professor Thorne\nopt.left.label [fa15a7] = 向索恩教授求情\n# en: Thorne agrees to reopen it — with fire buckets and a weekly inspection. Mir cries on you. Oil and all.\nopt.left.say.1 [f9ab11] = 索恩同意重新开放——条件是配备消防桶并每周检查。米尔抱着你哭了。连油带泪。\n# en: Thorne refuses. But Mir heard you try. \"Nobody's ever argued for me before,\" they say.\nopt.left.say.2 [d56047] = 索恩拒绝了。但米尔听到了你的争取。“从来没有人替我争过。”TA说。\n# en: Help them build a secret workshop\nopt.right.label [4db846] = 帮TA建一个秘密工坊\n# en: An abandoned classroom behind a tapestry. You bring candles; Mir brings everything else. It's perfect. It's almost entirely fireproof.\nopt.right.say.1 [090930] = 挂毯后面一间废弃的教室。你带来了蜡烛；米尔带来了其他所有东西。完美。而且几乎完全防火。\n# en: Bram finds the secret workshop on day two. Worth it, says Mir, scrubbing cauldrons beside you.\nopt.right.say.2 [f9bf9c] = 第二天布拉姆就发现了秘密工坊。值了，米尔一边在你旁边刷坩埚一边说。\n\n@event mir_dance_machine\n# en: The Dancing Machine\ntitle [88d879] = 跳舞机\n# en: Mir has built a machine that teaches dancing. It has six legs and opinions. \"It needs a test subject,\" they say. \"It's mostly safe. It only kicked me twice.\"\ntext [afec3b] = 米尔造了一台教人跳舞的机器。它有六条腿，还有自己的主见。“它需要一个测试对象，”TA说，“基本上是安全的。它只踢了我两次。”\n# en: A spindly brass contraption with too many legs attempting a waltz in an empty classroom, a student dancing with it.\nscene [76ab76] = 空荡荡的教室里，一台腿太多的细长黄铜装置正试图跳华尔兹，一个学生在和它共舞。\n# en: Make Mir test it\nopt.left.label [78abd6] = 让米尔自己测\n# en: You watch Mir and the machine waltz, badly, across the room. It kicks them twice; Mir gives you a look. It's the funniest thing you've ever seen.\nopt.left.say.1 [e8b145] = 你看着米尔和机器笨拙地在教室里跳华尔兹。它踢了米尔两脚；米尔瞪了你一眼。这是你见过最好笑的事。\n# en: Dance with the machine\nopt.right.label [39f27e] = 和机器跳舞\n# en: It kicks you twice too. But by the end you can waltz, and Mir is clapping with oily hands.\nopt.right.say.1 [4fe4d1] = 它也踢了你两次。但最后你学会了华尔兹，米尔用满是油污的手鼓着掌。\n\n@event mir_confession\n# en: Blueprint\ntitle [680200] = 蓝图\n# en: Mir unrolls a blueprint. It's a mechanical heart, labelled in tiny careful handwriting: \"Does not work without the other part.\" They clear their throat. \"I'm bad at saying things. So I drew it. You're the other part. If you want.\"\ntext [f59e74] = 米尔展开一张蓝图。上面是一颗机械心脏，用细小工整的字迹标注着：“缺少另一个部件时无法运转。”TA清了清嗓子。“我不擅长说话。所以我画了出来。你就是那个部件。如果你愿意的话。”\n# en: A rolled-out blueprint on a workbench showing a heart-shaped mechanism labelled with tiny careful notes, a nervous artificer beside it.\nscene [4f0a67] = 工作台上展开一张蓝图，画着心形机械装置并标满细小工整的注释，旁边站着一个紧张的奇械师。\n# en: Kindly say no\nopt.left.label [304162] = 委婉地拒绝\n# en: Mir rolls up the blueprint carefully. \"Good to know. Data is data.\" They're fine. They're mostly fine.\nopt.left.say.1 [457a7a] = 米尔小心地卷起蓝图。“知道了。数据就是数据。”TA没事。TA基本上没事。\n# en: \"I want.\"\nopt.right.label [12191a] = “我愿意。”\n# en: The heart doesn't need to work. But Mir builds it anyway, and it ticks on your windowsill, in time with your own.\nopt.right.say.1 [7bae7b] = 那颗心不需要真的运转。但米尔还是把它造了出来，它在你的窗台上滴答作响，与你的心跳同拍。\n\n@event mir_family_business\n# en: The Family Clockworks\ntitle [a29c6c] = 家族钟表行\n# en: A letter from Mir's parents: the family clockworks needs them home. A ticket is enclosed. \"They think this is a hobby,\" Mir says. \"This. Is. Not. A. Hobby.\"\ntext [51cd75] = 米尔父母来信：家族钟表行需要TA回家。信里附了一张车票。“他们以为这是兴趣爱好，”米尔说，“这。不是。兴趣。爱好。”\n# en: An artificer holding a stiff letter embossed with a gear crest, a train ticket tucked inside, staring at it blankly.\nscene [2991ee] = 一个奇械师拿着一封印有齿轮家徽的硬挺信件，里面夹着一张车票，茫然地盯着它。\n# en: Tell them family comes first\nopt.left.label [170ef7] = 告诉TA家人最重要\n# en: Mir goes home for a month and comes back with a new scar and a clearer jaw. \"They know now,\" they say. \"It's not a hobby. You were right to send me.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [68c5b3] = 米尔回家待了一个月，回来时多了一道新疤，下颌线也更坚定了。“他们现在知道了，”TA说，“这不是兴趣爱好。你让我回去是对的。”\n# en: Help them write back\nopt.right.label [7d4c22] = 帮TA写回信\n# en: Together you spend three evenings, and most of your coursework, on a letter so persuasive it should be framed. Mir's mother replies: \"Fine. Win a prize.\" Mir intends to win all of them.\nopt.right.say.1 [e7acb4] = 你们一起花了三个晚上，搭进去大半功课，写了一封有说服力到该裱起来的信。米尔的母亲回信：“好吧。拿个奖回来。”米尔打算把所有奖都拿回来。\n\n@event nessa_intro\n# en: First Impressions\ntitle [eee0a7] = 第一印象\n# en: A girl with a quill floating at her shoulder plants herself in front of you. \"Nessa Quill, Lumen Ledger. First impressions of Lumenhold, go. Be interesting, please. The last six were boring.\"\ntext [569607] = 一个肩头飘着羽毛笔的女孩往你面前一站。“内莎·奎尔，《流明纪事报》。对流明堡的第一印象，说吧。拜托说点有意思的。前六个都很无聊。”\n# en: A girl with ink-stained fingers and a self-writing quill floating at her shoulder, holding a notebook out expectantly.\nscene [e133f9] = 一个手指沾满墨迹的女孩，肩头飘着一支自动羽毛笔，满怀期待地递出笔记本。\n# en: Give a safe answer\nopt.left.label [6b761a] = 给个稳妥的回答\n# en: The quill writes \"SAFE\" in the margin and underlines it twice. Nessa sighs and moves on.\nopt.left.say.1 [9dd6db] = 羽毛笔在页边写下“稳妥”，还划了两道线。内莎叹了口气，走向下一个人。\n# en: Say something outrageous\nopt.right.label [9ecd42] = 说点惊人之语\n# en: You're quoted on the front page. Half the academy now thinks you're hilarious. The other half thinks you're dangerous. Nessa thinks both.\nopt.right.say.1 [83d022] = 你上了头版。现在半个学院觉得你很搞笑。另一半觉得你很危险。内莎觉得两者都是。\n# en: You are quoted. Out of context. Extremely out of context.\nopt.right.say.2 [8af99f] = 你被引用了。断章取义了。极其断章取义。\n\n@event nessa_gossip_column\n# en: You're in the Gossip Column\ntitle [22e6ad] = 你上了八卦专栏\n# en: This week's Ledger gossip column features you, prominently, under the headline \"WHO IS THE MYSTERY STUDENT ALWAYS IN THE LIBRARY AT MIDNIGHT?\" Nessa swears she didn't write it.\ntext [6890e5] = 本周《纪事报》的八卦专栏以醒目的标题刊登了你：“总在午夜出现在图书馆的神秘学生是谁？”内莎发誓不是她写的。\n# en: A copy of the Lumen Ledger pinned to a noticeboard, a gossip column circled in red ink, students gathered round giggling.\nscene [ab1073] = 布告栏上钉着一份《流明纪事报》，八卦专栏被红墨水圈了出来，学生们围在一起咯咯笑。\n# en: Confront Nessa\nopt.left.label [e29ecc] = 找内莎对质\n# en: She prints a correction. Sort of. It's a much more flattering paragraph about you.\nopt.left.say.1 [4756b3] = 她登了一则更正。算是吧。是一段把你夸得天花乱坠的文字。\n# en: \"Freedom of the press,\" says Nessa, sweetly. You both know she wrote it.\nopt.left.say.2 [8b1a52] = “新闻自由。”内莎甜甜地说。你们都知道是她写的。\n# en: Lean into the mystery\nopt.right.label [0d58af] = 顺水推舟，故弄玄虚\n# en: You start leaving cryptic notes in the library. The column runs for six more weeks. Nessa is delighted.\nopt.right.say.1 [ddffb0] = 你开始在图书馆里留下神秘的字条。这个专栏又连载了六周。内莎乐坏了。\n# en: The column runs for six more weeks. By the fourth it has decided you are haunting the library, and people move tables when you sit down.\nopt.right.say.2 [524d7e] = 专栏又连载了六周。到第四周，它认定你在图书馆闹鬼，你一坐下，别人就换桌子。\n\n@event nessa_source\n# en: The Source\ntitle [86f411] = 线人\n# en: Nessa leans in. \"You've got faculty access. The professors are planning to change the exam format and they're hiding it. I just need a quote. Anonymous. Please?\"\ntext [9e9d3b] = 内莎凑过来。“你能接触到教职员。教授们打算改考试形式，还瞒着大家。我只需要一句引述。匿名的。拜托？”\n# en: Two students in a dark corner of the library, one whispering, the other writing furiously in a notebook.\nscene [7686b3] = 图书馆昏暗的角落里两个学生，一个在低语，另一个在笔记本上奋笔疾书。\n# en: Refuse — that's a breach of trust\nopt.left.label [a7260f] = 拒绝——这是背信\n# en: Nessa sulks for a day. Then she respects you for it, which she hates. You sleep well.\nopt.left.say.1 [06a158] = 内莎生了一天闷气。然后她因此尊重你，这让她很恼火。你睡得很安稳。\n# en: Give her the scoop\nopt.right.label [3204b4] = 把独家消息给她\n# en: The story runs. Thorne knows exactly who talked. His glare could curdle milk.\nopt.right.say.1 [019448] = 报道刊出了。索恩完全清楚是谁说出去的。他的瞪视能让牛奶结块。\n# en: The story runs. Students are grateful. Nobody knows it was you. Nessa buys you cake.\nopt.right.say.2 [baa867] = 报道刊出了。学生们心怀感激。没人知道是你。内莎请你吃了蛋糕。\n\n@event nessa_hollow_story\n# en: Nessa Smells a Story\ntitle [9fa969] = 内莎嗅到了新闻\n# en: Nessa's office wall is covered in red string. In the middle: a sketch of a black star. \"Frozen fountains. Missing pages. A transfer from the North. Something's going on and you know about it. Spill.\"\ntext [f90ecc] = 内莎办公室的墙上缠满了红线。正中间：一幅黑星的速写。“结冰的喷泉。失踪的书页。一个从北方来的转学生。有什么事正在发生，而你知道内情。说吧。”\n# en: A wall in the newspaper office covered in pinned clippings and red string connecting a frozen lake, a black star and a fur-coated boy.\nscene [320fce] = 报社墙上钉满剪报，红线连接着冰封的湖、一颗黑星和一个穿毛皮大衣的少年。\n# en: Warn her off — it's dangerous\nopt.left.label [deabae] = 劝她收手——太危险了\n# en: \"That's what people say right before the best stories.\" But she takes the string down. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [448d70] = “大家都是在最好的新闻出现之前这么说的。”但她还是把红线拆了。大部分。\n# en: Share what you know\nopt.right.label [6ba460] = 告诉她你知道的\n# en: Nessa's article runs with half the facts. The man in grey reads it. So does Bram, who starts following you around.\nopt.right.say.1 [84698a] = 内莎的文章只登出了一半事实。灰衣人读到了。布拉姆也读到了，他开始到处跟着你。\n# en: Nessa's notes are scarily thorough. She's found a record of three students who vanished in 1723. All on Blossom moons.\nopt.right.say.2 [ab1477] = 内莎的笔记详尽得吓人。她找到了一份记录：1723 年有三个学生失踪。全都在花潮之月。\n\n@event nessa_paper_threatened\n# en: Stop the Presses\ntitle [447c01] = 停印\n# en: The headmistress has threatened to shut down the Ledger after an article about faculty salaries. Nessa is organising a protest. \"I need people on the steps. Visible people. You.\"\ntext [2ab439] = 一篇关于教职员薪水的文章刊出后，院长威胁要关停《纪事报》。内莎正在组织抗议。“我需要有人站在台阶上。显眼的人。你。”\n# en: A printing press with a faculty seal stamped across it, a defiant student standing in front of it with arms crossed.\nscene [94267d] = 一台被盖上学院封印的印刷机，一个倔强的学生抱着双臂站在它前面。\n# en: Stay out of it\nopt.left.label [03d24a] = 不掺和\n# en: The protest is small. The Ledger survives, barely. Nessa notices who wasn't there.\nopt.left.say.1 [98928f] = 抗议规模很小。《纪事报》勉强保住了。内莎记住了谁没来。\n# en: Stand on the steps\nopt.right.label [1c328c] = 站上台阶\n# en: Forty students on the steps, chanting. The headmistress comes out and, astonishingly, listens, looking straight at you the whole time, and not warmly. The Ledger stays.\nopt.right.say.1 [adae4f] = 四十个学生站在台阶上高呼口号。院长走了出来，出人意料地，她听了，而且自始至终直直地看着你，目光并不温和。《纪事报》保住了。\n\n@event nessa_confession\n# en: Off the Record\ntitle [a5d3b6] = 不予记录\n# en: Nessa puts down her quill. Then she tells it to stop writing. It's the first time you've seen it still. \"This is off the record,\" she says. \"Completely. I like you. I've drafted this eleven times. None of the drafts were good enough.\"\ntext [b22faa] = 内莎放下了羽毛笔。然后命令它停止书写。这是你第一次看见它静止不动。“这段不予记录，”她说，“完全不记。我喜欢你。这段话我打了十一遍草稿。没有一稿够好。”\n# en: A girl with an inkpot in one hand, her self-writing quill hovering above a blank page that she has commanded to stay blank.\nscene [b44415] = 一个女孩一手拿着墨水瓶，她的自动羽毛笔悬在一张空白的纸上方——她命令那张纸保持空白。\n# en: Gently say no\nopt.left.label [f46b92] = 温柔地拒绝\n# en: \"Noted,\" she says crisply. She keeps her quill still a moment longer. Then it starts writing again, and so does she.\nopt.left.say.1 [3ad266] = “记下了。”她干脆地说。她让羽毛笔多静止了一会儿。然后它重新开始书写，她也是。\n# en: Kiss her\nopt.right.label [5fa79c] = 吻她\n# en: Her quill, unattended, writes a single word in the margin: \"FINALLY.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [995169] = 无人看管的羽毛笔在页边写下了两个字：“终于。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/castle_halls.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/castle_halls.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event courtyard_sundial_bench\n# en: The Slow Sundial\ntitle [eef7f9] = 慢吞吞的日晷\n# en: {There's a bench by the old sundial, which runs eleven minutes slow.|Someone has scratched a tiny \"+11\" by the dial's noon. A fellow sufferer.|The sundial bench is yours by custom. A first-year asked if you came with it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost fills the hour lines, and the shadow skates across them.|{Pigeons are holding a meeting on the gnomon.|A first-year checks a watch against it, horrified.|Someone has chalked a face where noon should be.}}\ntext [211578] = {老日晷旁有一张长椅。这日晷慢十一分钟。|有人在晷面正午旁刻了个小小的“+11”。同病相怜的人。|日晷长椅按惯例归你了。有个一年级生问过，你是不是和它配套的。} {season == \"winter\": 霜填满了时刻刻线，影子在上面滑冰。|{一群鸽子正在晷针上开会。|一个一年级生拿怀表对它，吓坏了。|有人在正午的位置用粉笔画了张脸。}}\n# en: A stone bench beside a weathered sundial in a flagstone courtyard, a student reading with their back to the dial.\nscene [549ef0] = 石板庭院里，一张石凳挨着一座饱经风霜的日晷，一个学生背对晷面读书。\n# en: Read until the shadow moves\nopt.left.label [7b2740] = 读到影子挪动为止\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {Your fingers go numb by page two. The chapter is worth it.|You read in gloves, turning pages with your teeth.|You read in your own bench's frost-free corner. A first-year brings you a hot chestnut, as a tribute to the bench.}|{You finish a chapter in the minutes the sundial owes you, squinting into the glare.|A pigeon reads over your shoulder, unimpressed, and your neck has a crick in it by the end.|The shadow crawls; you outpace it by three pages and a stiff back.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [47ae9e] = {season == \"winter\": {读到第二页手指就冻麻了。这一章值得。|你戴着手套读，用牙齿翻页。|你在自己那条长椅上不结霜的角落里读书。一个一年级生给你送来一颗热栗子，算是向长椅致敬。}|{日晷欠你的那几分钟，够你眯着眼迎着日光读完一章。|一只鸽子凑在你肩头一起读，不以为然；读完时你的脖子也僵了。|影子慢慢爬，你领先它三页，背也坐僵了。}}\n# en: Close your eyes a while\nopt.right.label [64f7ca] = 闭眼歇一会儿\n# en: {You wake with a chalk moustache and no suspects.|You wake to a prefect clearing his throat an inch from your ear.|You wake rested and late, eleven minutes late by the dial.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1d321d] = {你醒来时脸上多了粉笔胡子，却找不到嫌疑人。|你醒来时，一位级长正贴着你耳边清嗓子。|你醒来时神清气爽，也迟到了——按日晷算，正好迟到十一分钟。}\n\n@event courtyard_saved_seat\n# en: A Seat on the Rim\ntitle [c87795] = 喷泉边的座位\n# en: {{here} has claimed the dry stretch of the fountain rim and waves you over.|{here} is on your favourite stretch of the fountain rim, the one the sun reaches first, and waves you over.|Your stretch of the fountain rim is known now. {here} is sitting on it and shifts over without being asked.} {Two towers' bells are arguing about the hour.|Pigeons circle like creditors.|{season == \"winter\": Your breath hangs between you.|Someone's lunch smells better than yours.}}\ntext [a68fe1] = {{here}占了喷泉边沿上干燥的一段，朝你招手。|{here}坐在你最喜欢的那段喷泉边沿上——太阳最先照到的那段——朝你招手。|喷泉边沿上你的那一段如今人尽皆知。{here}正坐在上面，不等你开口就挪了挪。} {两座塔的钟正为几点钟争执不休。|鸽子像债主一样盘旋。|{season == \"winter\": 你们之间呵出的白气悬在半空。|别人的午饭闻起来比你的香。}}\n# en: Two students sitting on the dry edge of a courtyard fountain, bags between them, pigeons circling overhead.\nscene [9547e3] = 两个学生坐在庭院喷泉干燥的边沿上，书包放在中间，鸽子在头顶盘旋。\n# en: Sit down and catch up\nopt.left.label [ebe7f0] = 坐下聊聊\n# en: {Corvin tells you three stories, one of them true, and calls you \"saint\" when you don't ask which.|Corvin spots a prefect, palms something off the rim, and goes on talking as if nothing happened.|Corvin shifts over and hands you half of something he shouldn't have. \"Your seat, saint. I kept it warm. Illegally.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [96bb2a] = {科尔文给你讲了三个故事，只有一个是真的；你没问是哪个，他便叫你“圣人”。|科尔文瞥见一位级长，从边沿上顺走了什么，接着若无其事地往下说。|科尔文挪了挪，递给你半块他本不该有的东西。“你的座，圣人。我替你捂热了。非法地。”}\n# en: {Nessa interviews you about the weather, the soup and your love life, in that order, and writes down all three.|Nessa reads you tomorrow's headline and asks if it's too cruel. It is. She keeps it.|Nessa shifts over without looking up from her notes. \"You're in tomorrow's column,\" she says. \"Nicely, for once.\"}\nopt.left.say.2 [3929a9] = {内莎就天气、汤和你的感情生活依次采访了你，三样都记了下来。|内莎给你念明天的头条，问是不是太刻薄了。是的。她决定照登。|内莎头也不抬地挪了挪位置。“你上了明天的专栏，”她说，“难得是好话。”}\n# en: {Bram lists every rule broken in the courtyard while you sit. Then, gruffly, he asks how your exams went.|Bram writes a demerit for a passing first-year, then tears it up when he thinks you aren't looking.|Bram shifts over with great dignity and informs you the seat is technically unassigned. Then he guards it while you fetch a cider.}\nopt.left.say.3 [f1eb70] = {你坐着的工夫，布拉姆把庭院里违反的校规一条条数了一遍。然后，他粗声粗气地问你考得怎么样。|布拉姆给路过的一年级生记了一次过，以为你没看见时又把单子撕了。|布拉姆郑重其事地挪了挪，通知你这个座位严格来说不属于任何人。然后在你去买苹果酒时替你守着它。}\n# en: {You trade the castle's news for an hour. {here} does most of the trading.|You talk until the shadows cross the fountain. Neither of you can remember about what.|You sit on your stretch of the rim and catch up. {here} already knows half your news and makes you tell it anyway.}\nopt.left.say.4 [eb16ba] = {你们交换了一小时城堡新闻，大多是{here}在说。|你们一直聊到影子爬过喷泉，谁也想不起聊了些什么。|你坐在你那段池沿上叙旧。你的消息{here}已经知道一半了，可还是让你从头讲。}\n# en: The cider from the cart by the gate is on you.\nopt.left.say.5 [e9717b] = 城门边小推车上的苹果酒，算你请的。\n# en: Wave, and keep walking\nopt.right.label [ff1be9] = 挥挥手，继续走\n# en: {You promise next time and take your book to the library instead. {here} looks as if {they} {are} counting.|You spend the hour with a book instead. It is a good book; it is not much company.|You wave and keep walking. {here} stays on your stretch of the rim, which feels like a small theft.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5791d7] = {你说下次一定，拿着书去了图书馆。{here}看上去像是在记账。|你改和一本书待了一小时。书很好，只是不太能陪人。|你挥挥手，继续走。{here}留在了你那段池沿上，感觉像被偷了一小样东西。}\n\n@event courtyard_seasonal_game\n# en: The Courtyard Game\ntitle [bfcc17] = 庭院游戏\n# en: {season == \"winter\": A snowball war between Emberhall and Stillwater has reached the treaty stage.|{season == \"spring\": A charmed ball is loose, dodging everyone who kicks at it.|{season == \"summer\": It's too hot for sport, so it's catch with a charmed apple that bites.|It's conker season. The champion conker is said to be older than the fountain.}}} {Someone shouts for one more player.|They shout for you by name.|Both sides shout for you, and start bidding.}\ntext [d0e0af] = {season == \"winter\": 烬厅和静水之间的雪仗已经打到了签订和约的阶段。|{season == \"spring\": 一只施了魔法的球在庭院里乱窜，谁去踢就躲谁。|{season == \"summer\": 天太热，没法运动，于是改玩抛接一只会咬人的魔法苹果。|七叶树果大战的季节到了。据说那颗冠军果比喷泉还老。}}} {有人喊着还差一个人。|这回他们直接喊你的名字。|两边都在喊你，还开始竞价。}\n# en: Students playing a rowdy game across a flagstone courtyard while others crowd the edges shouting bets.\nscene [9846ca] = 学生们在石板庭院里玩得热火朝天，旁边围着的人大声下注。\n# en: Join in\nopt.left.label [a87be8] = 加入\n# en: {Your side wins, and the result is disputed until supper.|You score the deciding point and are carried three whole steps before they drop you.|You join the side that bid more. They win, and the other side says you were bought, which you were.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d99628] = {你们这边赢了，比分一直争到晚饭。|你拿下了制胜一分，被大家抬着走了整整三步，然后摔了下来。|你加入了出价更高的那边。你们赢了，另一边说你是被收买的，确实如此。}\n# en: {You are flattened, politely, by both sides at once.|You spend the rest of the game as the thing the other side aims at.|Both sides think you're on theirs. Both sides find out, at once, in the middle of the courtyard.}\nopt.left.say.2 [5f9187] = {你被两队人马同时、礼貌地压扁了。|余下的比赛里，你成了对方瞄准的那个东西。|两边都以为你是自己人。两边同时发现了真相，就在庭院正中央。}\n# en: Hold the coats and the bets\nopt.right.label [175f1b] = 帮忙看外套和赌注\n# en: {The favourite loses. You are the only person in the courtyard who is pleased about it.|The underdog wins by a whisker, and the coats you are holding are suddenly lighter on your conscience.|You hold the coats and both sides' bids. The underdog wins. You are, briefly, the richest person in the courtyard.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f80232] = {大热门输了。整个庭院里只有你一个人高兴。|冷门险胜，你手里抱着的外套忽然让你良心轻了不少。|你抱着外套，也替两边保管着赌注。冷门赢了。有那么一会儿，你是庭院里最有钱的人。}\n# en: {The favourite wins, and so does everyone who backed them, out of your pocket.|Everyone bet the same way, and everyone was right. You pay out in a hail of small coins.|The favourite wins, and both sides had bet on it through you. You pay out twice, which shouldn't be possible.}\nopt.right.say.2 [42e100] = {大热门赢了，押它的人也都赢了——钱从你兜里出。|大家都押了同一边，也都押对了。你在一阵小硬币的冰雹里赔了钱。|大热门赢了，而两边都是通过你押的它。你赔了两次，这本不该发生。}\n# en: The favourite wins. With nothing in your pocket to pay out, you're chased twice round the fountain and lose a coat button to the crowd.\nopt.right.say.3 [69835b] = 热门赢了。你口袋里一个子儿也没有，赔不出钱，被人追着绕喷泉跑了两圈，还在人堆里丢了一颗外套扣子。\n\n@event library_wandering_book\n# en: The Book That Moved\ntitle [fe710d] = 会挪窝的书\n# en: {Yesterday your book was on the third floor. Today the third floor has never heard of it.|Your book has moved again, leaving a bookmark in the gap, like a note.|You keep a map of where the shelves hide your books. They've started hiding the map.} {In its place is a shelf of cookery spells.|In its place are bound weather reports.|In its place is one slim volume, face out, waiting.} {final_year: Soon they'll play these games with someone else.|}\ntext [cfc482] = {昨天你要的书还在三楼。今天三楼说从没听说过它。|你的书又挪走了，空位上留了一张书签，像一张字条。|你画了一张书架藏书的地图。书架开始藏那张地图了。} {原位上是一架烹饪咒语。|原位上是一排装订好的天气报告。|原位上只有一本薄薄的书，封面朝外，像在等你。} {final_year: 很快，它们就要和别人玩这种游戏了。|}\n# en: Tall library shelves leaning very slightly, a gap where one book should be, a student on a ladder peering into it.\nscene [d131b1] = 高高的书架微微倾斜，本该放一本书的地方空着，一个学生站在梯子上往缝里瞧。\n# en: Hunt it down\nopt.left.label [e1addf] = 把它找出来\n# en: {You find it on the sixth floor, filed under \"regret\".|You find it in the stairwell, halfway between floors, as if it had been trying to leave.|You find it at once. Either you are learning the shelves, or they are getting lazy.}\nopt.left.say.1 [05a6d5] = {你在六楼找到了它，归在“悔恨”类下。|你在楼梯间找到了它，卡在两层之间，好像它正打算出走。|你一下子就找到了。要么是你摸透了书架，要么是书架变懒了。}\n# en: Read what's in front of you\nopt.right.label [5bdb99] = 读手边这本\n# en: {You read the wrong book with great conviction, and quote it at supper.|You learn a great deal about something nobody will ever ask you about.|The wrong book turns out to be a very good novel. You read all of it, and none of it will be on any exam.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bb68b5] = {你满怀信心地读了一本错的书，晚饭时还引用了它。|你学到了大量永远不会有人问起的知识。|读错的书原来是一本很好的小说。你把它读完了，而里面没有一个字会出现在考试里。}\n\n@event library_shared_table\n# en: The Last Free Chair\ntitle [694ef7] = 最后一把空椅子\n# en: {The library is full. The only free chair is opposite {here}.|The only free chair is opposite {here}. A shelf has leaned over it, as if keeping it for you.|The library knows your habits now. The free chair, opposite {here}, has a book you wanted waiting on it.|The chair opposite {here} is so reliably free that you suspect the library of arranging it.} {Rain ticks on the tall windows.|A shelf nearby is quietly rearranging itself.|Footnote is asleep in the returns basket.|{season == \"winter\": The radiators clank like a ghost with a grudge.|Dust turns gold in the light.}}\ntext [ce55ed] = {图书馆满了。唯一的空椅子在{here}对面。|唯一的空椅子在{here}对面。一个书架斜靠在椅子上方，像在替你占座。|图书馆已经摸清了你的习惯。{here}对面那把空椅子上，放着一本你想看的书在等你。|{here}对面的椅子总是空着，空得太可靠，你怀疑是图书馆安排的。} {雨点敲着高窗。|附近的书架正悄悄自己重新排列。|“脚注”睡在还书筐里。|{season == \"winter\": 暖气片哐哐作响，像个怀恨在心的幽灵。|灰尘在光里变成金色。}}\n# en: A crowded library reading room, every table full but one, a single empty chair facing a student absorbed in a book.\nscene [b58e03] = 拥挤的图书馆阅览室，所有桌子都坐满了，只有一把空椅子，对面是个埋头读书的学生。\n# en: Take the chair and work\nopt.left.label [4d48da] = 坐下来用功\n# en: {Kael doesn't look up. By the end of the hour there is frost on your inkwell and a tiny, careful correction in your margin.|Kael pushes a page of his notes across to you, then looks away as if it had moved on its own.|Kael moves his inkwell to make room for yours. By the end, there's frost on both, and a note in his hand: \"Your third line.\"|Kael says one sentence in the whole hour. It's the answer to the question you were stuck on. He goes back to reading.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fbd64b] = {凯尔没有抬头。一小时后，你的墨水瓶上结了霜，页边多了一处细小工整的订正。|凯尔把一页笔记推到你面前，然后移开视线，仿佛那页纸是自己挪过去的。|凯尔挪开墨水瓶，给你的腾出地方。到最后，两只墨水瓶上都结了霜，还有一张他写的字条：“你的第三行。”|凯尔一个钟头里只说了一句话。那正是你卡住的那道题的答案。他接着看书。}\n# en: {Lyra slides the candle an inch toward your side without looking up. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra finishes before you, and waits, and says she wasn't waiting.|Lyra corrects your notation without asking and is right. You correct hers and are also right. Neither of you mentions it.|Lyra works at your pace for once, and you finish together. She says it was coincidence, twice.}\nopt.left.say.2 [6bcf1c] = {莱拉头也不抬，把蜡烛往你这边推了一寸。你们谁也没提。|莱拉比你先写完，却等着你，还说她没在等。|莱拉没问就改了你的记法，改得对。你改了她的，也改得对。你们谁都没提。|莱拉难得和你同步，你们一起写完了。她说那是巧合，说了两遍。}\n# en: {Seraphine writes a word in your notes that you won't understand until next week.|Seraphine reads the same page for an hour, smiling at something that isn't on it.|Seraphine slides you a book you hadn't asked for. You'll need it on Thursday, it turns out.|Seraphine works in silence, then says your name once, softly, as if checking it was still there.}\nopt.left.say.3 [06a254] = {瑟拉芬在你的笔记上写了一个词，你要到下周才会明白。|瑟拉芬对着同一页读了一小时，冲着一件不在书上的事微笑。|瑟拉芬把一本你没要过的书推给你。结果你星期四正好用得上。|瑟拉芬默默用功，然后轻轻念了一声你的名字，好像在确认它还在。}\n# en: {Bram informs you that you breathe too loudly. Then he lends you his ruler.|Bram underlines every rule in his book twice. Once, he asks your opinion of one.|Bram shares his lamp and lectures you on its proper use. The lecture is long. The lamp is very good.|Bram reads your essay upside down from across the table and finds the mistake. He's insufferable about it, and right.}\nopt.left.say.4 [3cbff8] = {布拉姆通知你：你呼吸太大声了。然后他把尺子借给了你。|布拉姆把书里每一条规则都划了两道线。有一次，他问你对其中一条怎么看。|布拉姆和你共用他的灯，还就它的正确用法给你上了一课。课很长。灯很好。|布拉姆隔着桌子倒着读你的论文，找出了错处。他为此得意得让人受不了，可他是对的。}\n# en: {You work in silence for an hour, which turns out to be a kind of company.|Neither of you speaks. Both of you get further than you would have alone.|You work opposite {here} as if you'd planned to. By the end you've both got further, and nodded once.|You share the table for the hour and the cat for the last ten minutes. It's a good arrangement.}\nopt.left.say.5 [1c2a28] = {你们默默用功了一小时，原来这也算一种陪伴。|你们谁也没说话，却都比一个人时走得更远。|你在{here}对面用功，就像早就约好了。到最后你们都比原来走得更远，彼此点了一下头。|一个钟头里你们共用一张桌子，最后十分钟还共用了一只猫。这安排不错。}\n# en: Whisper a question\nopt.right.label [1952d6] = 小声问个问题\n# en: {The conversation is worth it. The look from the front desk has a long memory.|You get three sentences in before a shush arrives from two floors up.|You whisper your question to {here} and get a whispered answer. The front desk lets it pass, this once.|Your whisper wakes the cat in the returns basket, who complains loudly. You are shushed for the cat's noise.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ea8730] = {这番交谈很值。只是前台投来的那一眼，记性很长。|你刚说了三句，两层楼上就传来一声“嘘”。|你小声向{here}提了个问题，得到了一个小声的回答。前台这回放过了你们。|你的低语吵醒了还书筐里的猫，它大声抗议。你却因为猫的动静挨了一声“嘘”。}\n\n@event library_overdue\n# en: Overdue\ntitle [40f62b] = 逾期\n# en: {The book you borrowed in a hurry is overdue, and the library knows it. The spine has started to sigh.|Another overdue book. The front desk has begun keeping a little card with your name on it.|Your overdue books sigh in chorus now, and the card at the front desk has a second side.} {Footnote watches you approach the returns basket with professional interest.|The front desk is, for once, unattended.|A queue of first-years is paying fines in small, sad coins.}\ntext [3a56cd] = {你匆忙借的那本书逾期了，图书馆心知肚明。书脊开始叹气。|又一本逾期的书。前台开始为你单独留一张写着你名字的小卡片。|你的逾期书现在齐声叹气，前台那张卡片已经写到了背面。} {“脚注”以专业的兴趣看着你走近还书筐。|前台难得没人。|一队一年级生正用小小的、可怜的硬币交罚款。}\n# en: A student holding a book behind their back near a wicker returns basket, a cat on the front desk watching them.\nscene [9136c2] = 一个学生把书藏在身后，靠近一只柳条还书筐，前台上的猫盯着他们。\n# en: Own up and pay the fine\nopt.left.label [1ffaa0] = 认错交罚款\n# en: {The fine is paid; the desk, mollified, lends you the sequel.|You pay, and are lent another book, which you suspect is a trap.|The desk sighs, takes your coins and hands you a book it says you'll like. It is always right, which is the annoying part.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e1d0b0] = {罚款交了，前台消了气，把续集借给了你。|你交了钱，又被借了一本书，你怀疑这是个圈套。|前台叹口气，收下硬币，递给你一本说你会喜欢的书。它总是对的，这才让人恼火。}\n# en: You own up with empty pockets. The desk lets you work the fine off reshelving, which takes all evening, and lends you the sequel anyway.\nopt.left.say.2 [6ba749] = 你两手空空地去认错。前台让你靠整理书架抵罚款，整整花了一个晚上，还照样把续集借给了你。\n# en: Slip it into the basket\nopt.right.label [aa0b65] = 偷偷塞进还书筐\n# en: {The book goes in without a sound. It stops sighing at once, which feels like forgiveness.|You drop it in under cover of a sneeze. Footnote saw. Footnote will keep it to himself, for a price.|You slip it in while the first-years pay their fines. Nobody notices one more sad sound.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ca2e56] = {书悄无声息地滑了进去，立刻不再叹气，像是原谅了你。|你借着一个喷嚏把书丢了进去。“脚注”看见了。“脚注”会替你保密——有代价的。|你趁一年级生们交罚款时把书塞了进去。多一声难过的声音，没人注意。}\n# en: {The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. You pay double.|It lands with a groan. The front desk adds a mark to your card, and the fine, doubled.|The book sighs in harmony with your other overdue books. The desk adds a third side to your card.}\nopt.right.say.2 [fe215d] = {那本书发出一声垂死管风琴般的叹息，宣告了自己的到来。所有人都回过头。你交了双倍罚款。|书落进筐里，呻吟了一声。前台在你的卡片上又记了一笔，罚款翻倍。|那本书和你的其他逾期书一起和声叹气。前台给你的卡片添了第三面。}\n# en: The book announces itself with a sigh like a dying organ. Everyone turns. With nothing to pay, you're put on reshelving until the fine is worked off twice over.\nopt.right.say.3 [cb93a1] = 那本书发出一声垂死管风琴般的叹息，宣告了自己的到来。所有人都回过头。你没钱可付，只好去整理书架，直到把双倍罚款抵完。\n\n@event refectory_late_supper\n# en: Late Supper\ntitle [095a3c] = 晚来的晚饭\n# en: {You come in late. The benches are empty and the cooks are wiping the far tables.|The cook leaves a lamp lit at the end of the bench now, where the draught can't find you.|Your late bowl is waiting under a plate, with a spoon laid beside it. Nobody admits to it.} {year == 1: Home feels very far away tonight.|{final_year: You count the suppers you have left, and stop.|{The candles are down to stubs.|Someone has left a half-done puzzle on the next table.|The cook is humming in the kitchen behind the hatch.}}}\ntext [aeeedf] = {你来晚了。长凳都空着，厨工们在擦远处的桌子。|厨娘现在会在长凳尽头、穿堂风找不到的地方给你留一盏灯。|你那碗晚饭扣在盘子下等着你，旁边摆好了勺子。没人承认是谁放的。} {year == 1: 今晚，家显得格外遥远。|{final_year: 你数了数还剩几顿这样的晚饭，然后不数了。|{蜡烛快烧到底了。|有人在隔壁桌上留了一幅拼了一半的拼图。|厨娘在窗口后的厨房里哼着歌。}}}\n# en: The far end of a long refectory table at night, most benches empty, a single student with a bowl and a half-written letter.\nscene [0600ae] = 深夜膳堂长桌的尽头，大部分长凳空着，一个学生面前放着一只碗和一封写了一半的信。\n# en: Write a letter home\nopt.left.label [67bcd4] = 给家里写信\n# en: {You write two pages and post them with the last of your coppers.|You write about the food. You do not write about anything else, but they'll know.|You write a letter that is mostly questions. You post it before you can make it braver.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bbe078] = {你写了两页，用最后几枚铜板寄了出去。|你写了饭菜，别的什么都没写，但他们会懂的。|你写了一封几乎全是问题的信，趁自己还没想把它写得更勇敢之前寄了出去。}\n# en: Revise over the bowl\nopt.right.label [5fbeee] = 边吃边复习\n# en: {You learn three dates and a spill of gravy across all of them.|The cook reads over your shoulder and corrects one of your answers. She is right.|You revise over your bowl. The cook takes the plate away when you've finished and leaves a second helping.}\nopt.right.say.1 [99e02c] = {你记住了三个年份，还有一摊洒在上面的肉汁。|厨娘从你肩后看了一眼，改正了你的一个答案。她是对的。|你对着碗复习。你吃完后，厨娘把盘子收走，又留下了第二份。}\n\n@event refectory_house_tables\n# en: Where to Sit\ntitle [3b38cd] = 坐哪儿\n# en: {Supper, and the question of where to sit.|Your house table has started saving a gap on the bench, roughly your width.|The gap is a named seat now, and the quiet table nods at you too.} Your house table is {loud with a story about a goat|in the middle of a pudding treaty|singing, badly}. At the far end, {season == \"winter\": some second-years from Rootward huddle round a warming charm.|some second-years from Duskgate are arguing quietly about a map.}\ntext [f8a7d4] = {晚饭时间，又是那个问题：坐哪儿。|你们学院那桌开始在长凳上留一个空位，差不多你那么宽。|那个空位现在有了名字，安静的那桌也会朝你点头了。} 你们学院那桌{正在大声讲一个关于山羊的故事|正在谈判一份布丁和约|正在唱歌，唱得很难听}。远处那头，{season == \"winter\": 几个根庐的二年级生围着一个取暖咒挤成一团。|几个暮门的二年级生正低声争论一张地图。}\n# en: A crowded refectory at suppertime, a student standing with a tray between a loud house table and a quieter one of strangers.\nscene [6196b3] = 晚饭时拥挤的膳堂，一个学生端着托盘站在一张喧闹的学院长桌和一张陌生人的安静桌子之间。\n# en: Squeeze in with your house\nopt.left.label [956401] = 挤进自己学院那桌\n# en: {You eat with an elbow in your ribs and laugh the whole way through.|Somebody's story gets better every time you hear it. So does the pudding.|You squeeze into your gap and join the singing, badly, to fit in. It works.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7c3c22] = {你肋下顶着别人的胳膊肘吃完了饭，从头笑到尾。|有人的故事每听一遍就更精彩一点，布丁也是。|你挤进你的空位，跟着唱，唱得很差，以示合群。效果很好。}\n# en: Sit with the strangers\nopt.right.label [88cc69] = 和陌生人坐一起\n# en: {They are polite, then curious, then actually rather good company.|It is awkward for exactly one course. By pudding, they have told you their secret map route.|The quiet table makes room as if you'd always sat there. They ask about your house's singing, with sympathy.}\nopt.right.say.1 [542ca6] = {他们先是客气，然后好奇，然后竟成了相当不错的伙伴。|尴尬只持续了一道菜。到布丁时，他们已经把秘密地图路线告诉了你。|那桌安静的人给你让出位置，像你一直就坐在那儿。他们同情地问起你们学院的歌声。}\n\n@event refectory_pudding_tax\n# en: The Pudding Tax\ntitle [03d210] = 布丁税\n# en: {{friend} sits down opposite you and looks at your pudding the way a cat looks at a fish.|Your pudding has a reputation now. {friend} sits down opposite you with a spoon already in hand.|Word of your pudding tax has got round the refectory. Tonight {friend} comes to collect, with a little receipt.} {season == \"winter\": Tonight it's treacle sponge, steaming.|{Tonight it's apple crumble.|Tonight it's a custard that wobbles with intent.|Tonight it's jam roly-poly, much defended.}}\ntext [277329] = {{friend}在你对面坐下，看你的布丁就像猫看鱼。|你的布丁如今声名在外。{friend}在你对面坐下，勺子已经握在手里。|你的布丁税已经在膳堂传开了。今晚{friend}来收税，还带着一张小收据。} {season == \"winter\": 今晚是热气腾腾的糖浆海绵布丁。|{今晚是苹果酥。|今晚是一份意图不明地颤动着的蛋奶冻。|今晚是果酱卷布丁，戒备森严。}}\n# en: Two friends at a refectory table, one guarding a bowl of treacle pudding, the other holding a spoon at the ready.\nscene [2f960a] = 膳堂桌边的两个朋友，一个护着一碗糖浆布丁，另一个举着勺子随时准备出手。\n# en: Pay the pudding tax\nopt.left.label [7c4562] = 交布丁税\n# en: {Tobias apologises four times, eats it all, then tells you a long story about a goat who did the same. It helps, somehow.|Tobias eats half, then pushes the bowl back with a sprig of mint from his pocket on top, as an apology.|Tobias pays you back with a receipt of his own: half a jar of honey from home. You call it even.}\nopt.left.say.1 [47bf0f] = {托比亚斯道了四次歉，把它吃得一干二净，然后给你讲了个长长的故事，说一只山羊也干过这种事。不知怎么，这让你好受了些。|托比亚斯吃了一半，把碗推回来，上面放着一枝从口袋里掏出的薄荷，算是赔礼。|托比亚斯用他自己的“收据”还了你：半罐家里带来的蜂蜜。你们算两清了。}\n# en: {\"Efficient,\" Lyra says, finishing it. The next day a whole pudding appears on your tray. She doesn't mention it.|Lyra eats it in four exact bites and critiques the custard. She does not give it back.|Lyra presents the receipt, takes exactly the tax and not a crumb more. She is, you have to admit, scrupulous.}\nopt.left.say.2 [71588b] = {“高效。”莱拉吃完说道。第二天，你的托盘上出现了一整份布丁。她只字未提。|莱拉精确地四口吃完，还点评了蛋奶酱。她没还给你。|莱拉出示收据，不多不少正好拿走税额，一点渣也不多拿。你不得不承认，她一丝不苟。}\n# en: {Mir eats it while explaining a pudding-defence device that would probably work. Probably.|Mir eats it with a spoon they built themselves, which hums. They say the humming is probably normal.|Mir hands you a receipt they've engraved on brass, collects, and promises a refund mechanism by spring.}\nopt.left.say.3 [694d5c] = {米尔边吃边讲解一种防布丁被偷的装置，说大概能用。大概。|米尔用自己做的勺子吃，那勺子嗡嗡作响。TA说嗡嗡响大概是正常的。|米尔递给你一张刻在黄铜上的收据，收了税，承诺春天前做出退款装置。}\n# en: {{friend} eats exactly half and leaves you the burnt corner.|{friend} eats it slowly, savouring your defeat.|{friend} collects the tax and writes \"paid\" on the receipt. You're starting to suspect the receipts are the point.}\nopt.left.say.4 [10b26e] = {{friend}正好吃了一半，把烤焦的那角留给了你。|{friend}慢慢地吃，细细品味你的失败。|{friend}收了税，在收据上写上“已付”。你开始怀疑收据才是重点。}\n# en: Defend your pudding\nopt.right.label [c73449] = 捍卫你的布丁\n# en: {You hide it under a napkin and point at the window. It works. {friend} will remember this.|You claim it's been sneezed on. {friend} retreats in disgust, and you eat in peace.|You refuse to recognise the receipt. {friend} appeals to the table, which rules in your favour, narrowly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b3831f] = {你把它藏在餐巾下，指着窗外。成功了。{friend}会记住这一笔的。|你声称有人对着它打过喷嚏。{friend}嫌恶地退却，你安心吃完。|你拒不承认那张收据。{friend}向全桌申诉，全桌以微弱多数判你胜诉。}\n# en: {{friend} sees straight through you and takes the whole thing as a penalty. It is hard to stay cross.|{friend} distracts you with a story and the bowl is empty before it ends.|You dispute the receipt. {friend} produces a second receipt for the dispute, and takes the pudding as costs.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a94a04] = {{friend}一眼看穿了你，把整份没收作为罚款。很难一直生气。|{friend}用一个故事分散你的注意力，故事还没讲完碗就空了。|你对收据提出异议。{friend}又拿出一张收据，专门针对这次异议，并把布丁收作诉讼费。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_old_board\n# en: Someone Else's Lecture\ntitle [108850] = 别人的课\n# en: {term: {The fourth-floor hall is empty, but someone else's lecture is still on the board.|In the empty hall, a stub of chalk waits on the ledge, as if for you.|The board has a corner fenced off in chalk, labelled \"answers here\".}|In the break the boards are wiped, except one nobody dares touch.} {Today it's a ward diagram, labelled \"wrong, but how?\"|Today it's half a proof, ending in a thumbprint.|Today it's a list headed \"on the exam\", crossed out.}\ntext [91923d] = {term: {四楼的讲堂空着，黑板上却还留着别人的课。|空讲堂里，一截粉笔头在槽里等着，像是留给你的。|黑板角上用粉笔圈出一块，标着“答案写这里”。}|假期里黑板都擦干净了，只有一块没人敢碰。} {今天是一幅护界图，标注着“错了，但错在哪？”|今天是半个证明，结尾是一枚指纹。|今天是一张题为“会考的内容”的清单，被划掉了。}\n# en: An empty spiral lecture hall, a blackboard still dense with chalk diagrams from a class that has long gone.\nscene [b21ec8] = 空荡荡的螺旋讲堂，黑板上还密密麻麻留着一堂早已散场的课的粉笔图解。\n# en: Copy it all down\nopt.left.label [71d09a] = 全部抄下来\n# en: {Your hand cramps. Your head, for once, does not.|You copy it all and understand most of it. The rest you'll grow into.|You copy the board, fenced corner and all. Your answer from last time is still there, with a tick beside it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [337380] = {你的手抽筋了。你的脑子却难得没有。|你全抄了下来，大部分都懂。剩下的，你会慢慢长进去。|你把黑板连同那个围起来的角落一起抄下。你上次写的答案还在，旁边多了一个勾。}\n# en: Add your own answer\nopt.right.label [b05ba7] = 写上你的答案\n# en: {Next time, someone has added \"yes!\" under your line.|Someone has answered your answer. The board has become a correspondence.|By the afternoon someone has drawn a box around your line, to keep it safe.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9a51e5] = {下次再来，有人在你那行下面加了个“对！”|有人回答了你的回答。黑板成了一场书信往来。|到了下午，有人在你那行字周围画了个框，好好护着。}\n# en: {The professor whose board it was finds you holding the chalk. \"Hm,\" says the look.|Your answer is wiped by the time you reach the door, by someone who knew better.|You write in the \"answers here\" corner. The next morning there's a line through it, and \"nearly\", underlined.}\nopt.right.say.2 [92a4ef] = {那块黑板的主人——一位教授——撞见你手里拿着粉笔。那一眼在说：“嗯。”|你还没走到门口，你的答案就被一个更懂行的人擦掉了。|你在“答案写这里”的角落写了。第二天早上，上面被划了一道，旁边写着“差一点”，还加了下划线。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_stair_question\n# en: One Question a Flight\ntitle [d64af4] = 一层楼一个问题\n# en: {You meet Professor Thorne on the spiral stair. He allows one question per flight, and climbs fast.|Professor Thorne on the stair. He has slowed down by about one step in seven, which is how you know he expects you.|Thorne hands you half his stack of papers at the foot of the stair, without a word, and starts climbing.} {term: |The tower is half empty for the break; your footsteps echo twice.}{final_year: There are not many of these climbs left.|}\ntext [fa2b42] = {你在螺旋楼梯上遇见索恩教授。他允许每层楼问一个问题，而且爬得很快。|楼梯上的索恩教授。他每七级台阶慢下一级，你由此知道他在等你。|索恩在楼梯口一言不发地把一半文件塞给你，然后开始往上爬。} {term: |假期里塔楼空了一半，你的脚步声有两重回音。}{final_year: 这样的攀爬已经不剩几次了。|}\n# en: A narrow spiral stair inside a stone tower, a severe professor climbing ahead with a stack of papers, a student a step behind.\nscene [0a9608] = 石塔里狭窄的螺旋楼梯，一位严厉的教授抱着一摞文件走在前面，一个学生落后一级台阶。\n# en: Ask a hard question\nopt.left.label [7531ee] = 问个难题\n# en: {He answers in four words, and the four words are enough.|\"Hm,\" he says, then answers it properly, all the way to the seventh floor.|You carry half his papers and ask your question on the fourth flight. He answers on the seventh, and it was worth the wait.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2020c3] = {他只用四个字回答，而这四个字就够了。|“嗯。”他说，然后认真地回答了，一直讲到七楼。|你抱着他的一半文件，在第四层提了问。他在第七层才回答，值得等。}\n# en: {He answers with a harder question and leaves you on the landing.|\"Wrong question,\" he says. \"Find the right one by Thursday.\"|He takes his papers back on the landing. \"Better question next time,\" he says, which means he'll be expecting one.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8660d6] = {他用一个更难的问题作答，把你留在了楼梯平台上。|“问错了。”他说，“星期四之前找到对的问题。”|他在平台上拿回了文件。“下次问个更好的，”他说，意思是他会等着。}\n# en: Climb in silence beside him\nopt.right.label [f05732] = 默默陪他爬楼\n# en: {He climbs faster than you. At the top he says, \"Adequate,\" and you are fairly sure he means the climbing.|Neither of you says a word for seven flights. It is oddly restful, apart from your legs.|You carry his papers in silence. At the top he takes them back and says, \"Thursday,\" which is an invitation.}\nopt.right.say.1 [03a653] = {他爬得比你快。到了顶上，他说：“尚可。”你相当确定他指的是爬楼。|七层楼里你们一句话也没说。除了腿，这出奇地让人放松。|你默默地替他抱着文件。到顶后他接回去，说了句“星期四”，那是邀请。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_scorched_room\n# en: The Scorched Room\ntitle [b134c8] = 烧焦的练习室\n# en: {The practice room on the Evocation floor is empty. A sign forbids unsupervised casting.|The scorched room is empty. Since your last visit, someone has underlined the sign.|The scorched room. The sign is underlined three times now, and signed by the headmistress.} {A fresh scorch mark is shaped like a surprised face.|Someone has left a bucket of sand by the door.|{season == \"winter\": The walls are the only warm thing here.|The air smells faintly of singed eyebrow.}}\ntext [6ca0fe] = {塑能术那层的练习室空着。告示禁止无人监督施法。|烧焦的练习室空着。自你上次来过，有人在告示下划了线。|烧焦的练习室。告示上现在划了三道线，还有院长的签名。} {一道新焦痕的形状像一张吃惊的脸。|有人在门边放了一桶沙子。|{season == \"winter\": 这里只有墙是暖的。|空气里隐约有烧焦眉毛的味道。}}\n# en: A practice room on a lower floor of a stone tower, walls streaked with scorch marks, a sign by the door forbidding unsupervised casting.\nscene [5b6deb] = 石塔低层的一间练习室，墙上满是焦痕，门边的告示禁止无人监督施法。\n# en: Practise anyway\nopt.left.label [5b8fa1] = 照练不误\n# en: {Your spell lands clean in the centre of the oldest scorch mark. You leave no evidence.|You practise until the flame does what you mean, not what you say.|You practise under the headmistress's signature. Your spell is very good. You leave before it's tempted to be better.}\nopt.left.say.1 [681bca] = {你的咒语正中最古老那道焦痕的中心，没留下任何证据。|你一直练到火焰照你的意思行事，而不是照你的话。|你在院长的签名底下练习。你的咒语很好。在它想变得更好之前，你走了。}\n# en: {A new scorch mark joins the wall, shaped very like you.|The spell comes back at you. You leave with your eyebrows, mostly.|Your scorch mark lands right under the headmistress's signature. You'd swear the signature looks disappointed.}\nopt.left.say.2 [22262c] = {墙上添了一道新焦痕，形状很像你。|咒语反弹到你身上。你离开时眉毛还在——大部分。|你的焦痕正好落在院长的签名下面。你敢发誓那签名看起来很失望。}\n# en: Work through the theory\nopt.right.label [0d1f5b] = 钻研理论\n# en: {You draw the spell on paper fifty times. Paper is very forgiving.|You learn exactly why the walls look like that, and resolve not to add to them.|You work the theory under the thrice-underlined sign. You'll cast it properly, supervised, and it will be perfect.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bc9305] = {你在纸上把咒语画了五十遍。纸很宽容。|你彻底弄懂了墙为什么是这副模样，并决心不再给它添彩。|你在那块划了三道线的告示下面钻研理论。等到有人监督时你会好好施放，那时它会完美无缺。}\n\n@event great_hall_tomorrow_sky\n# en: Tomorrow's Weather\ntitle [fdb53c] = 明天的天气\n# en: {The great hall is empty but for the candles. The ceiling shows tomorrow.|One candle drifts over to you and hovers at reading height.|Your candle meets you at the door now and leads you to your bench.} {season == \"winter\": Tomorrow, it says, it will snow.|{season == \"spring\": Tomorrow, a storm with bad manners.|{season == \"summer\": Tomorrow is cloudless, stars and all.|Tomorrow is fog, rolling across the vaults.}}} {final_year: There are fewer tomorrows here now.|}\ntext [e5b36c] = {大礼堂里空无一人，只有蜡烛。天花板在放映明天。|一支蜡烛飘到你身边，停在适合读书的高度。|你的蜡烛现在会在门口迎你，把你领到你的长凳。} {season == \"winter\": 天花板说，明天要下雪。|{season == \"spring\": 明天，一场不讲礼貌的暴风雨。|{season == \"summer\": 明天万里无云，满天星斗。|明天是雾，在拱顶间缓缓滚过。}}} {final_year: 这里剩下的明天不多了。|}\n# en: An empty great hall in the evening, floating candles drifting under a vaulted ceiling that shows a different sky from the windows.\nscene [85f031] = 傍晚空荡的大礼堂，漂浮的蜡烛在拱顶下飘移，天花板上的天空与窗外不同。\n# en: Lie on a bench and watch\nopt.left.label [e7d231] = 躺在长凳上看\n# en: {You watch tomorrow until your neck stiffens. It is worth it.|A candle drifts down to see if you are all right, and stays.|You fall half asleep and dream tomorrow a little early.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a177d2] = {你看着明天，直到脖子发僵。值得。|一支蜡烛飘下来看你是否安好，然后就留下了。|你半睡半醒，提前梦见了一点明天。}\n# en: Read by the floating candles\nopt.right.label [e66c83] = 借烛光读书\n# en: {A candle drips wax onto the best paragraph. You read it anyway, through the wax.|The candles follow your page like curious moths. It is the best light in the castle.|Your candle sits on the bench beside you like a friend. The light is steady, and you read further than you meant to.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ec7422] = {一支蜡烛把蜡滴在了最好的那段上。你隔着蜡照读不误。|蜡烛像好奇的飞蛾一样跟着你的书页。这是城堡里最好的光。|你的蜡烛像朋友一样待在你身边的长凳上。光很稳，你读得比打算的更远。}\n\n@event great_hall_choir_rehearsal\n# en: The Choir Rehearses\ntitle [2fc949] = 合唱团排练\n# en: {The choir is rehearsing on the stage, a foot off the floor, and one voice short.|Still a voice short. The conductor has left a spare songbook on your bench.|The spare songbook has your name pencilled in it, and the alto line marked in red.} {season == \"winter\": They are learning the Midwinter music.|{Tonight it's a round that nobody can find the end of.|Tonight it's a lament, sung far too cheerfully.|Tonight it's a song for the lake, sung very softly.}}\ntext [9d02cd] = {合唱团在舞台上排练，离地一尺，还差一个声部。|还是差一个声部。指挥在你的长凳上放了一本备用歌本。|那本备用歌本上用铅笔写了你的名字，女低音声部用红笔标了出来。} {season == \"winter\": 他们在学仲冬的曲子。|{今晚是一首没人找得到结尾的轮唱。|今晚是一首挽歌，唱得未免太欢快了。|今晚是一首唱给湖的歌，唱得很轻很轻。}}\n# en: A choir rehearsing on the stage of a candlelit great hall, a few singers floating a foot off the boards, the benches mostly empty.\nscene [f55935] = 烛光摇曳的大礼堂舞台上，合唱团正在排练，几位歌手离地漂浮一尺，长凳上几乎空着。\n# en: Listen from the back\nopt.left.label [73c47c] = 在后排听\n# en: {The collection hat comes round. It is worth every coin.|You listen until the candles gutter. The hat comes round twice.|You listen from the back with your name pencilled into a book you're not singing from. It's the best of both.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8391f5] = {募捐的帽子传了过来。每一枚硬币都值。|你一直听到蜡烛摇摇欲灭。帽子传了两回。|你在后排听着，手里那本没用来唱的歌本里用铅笔写着你的名字。两全其美。}\n# en: Fill the missing voice\nopt.right.label [2ec625] = 补上那个声部\n# en: {You hit every note but one. The conductor says it was the best one.|You float a little off the floor on the last chord. Nobody mentions it, which is how you know.|You sing your marked alto line and the choir is whole. The conductor doesn't say anything, and doesn't need to.}\nopt.right.say.1 [af0466] = {你只错了一个音。指挥说那是最好的一个。|最后一个和弦时你微微离了地。没人提起，你就知道了。|你唱起标好的女低音声部，合唱团完整了。指挥什么也没说，也不需要说。}\n# en: {You come in confidently, in the wrong key, in the wrong song. The choir is very kind about it, which is worse.|You sing the round in a straight line and finish alone. The conductor thanks you for your enthusiasm.|You sing the red line, which is the soprano line, it turns out. The choir carries you, out of kindness.}\nopt.right.say.2 [5f53e1] = {你信心满满地进了拍——调不对，歌也不对。合唱团对此非常宽容，这更糟。|你把轮唱唱成了一条直线，最后一个人唱完。指挥感谢你的热情。|你唱了那条红线，结果那是女高音声部。合唱团出于好心带着你唱完。}\n\n@event great_hall_high_table_dare\n# en: The High Table Dare\ntitle [09ad02] = 高桌之赌\n# en: {Someone dares you to sit in the headmistress's chair at the empty high table.|The dare comes round again, and someone has started keeping a tally in chalk on the doorframe.|The tally on the doorframe has your column and everyone else's. First-years dare each other to dare you.} {The chair is still warm.|A cushion embroidered with an owl sits on it.|Her teacup has been left behind, still steaming, which is worse.}\ntext [3bd135] = {有人赌你不敢坐到空着的高桌上，坐院长的椅子。|这个赌又来了，有人开始在门框上用粉笔记分。|门框上的记分表里有你的一栏和其他所有人的。一年级生们互相怂恿着来赌你。} {椅子还是温的。|椅子上放着一个绣着猫头鹰的坐垫。|她的茶杯落在那儿，还冒着热气，这更糟。}\n# en: An empty high table on a dais at the end of a dark great hall, a carved chair at its centre, students daring each other from the doorway.\nscene [788e3e] = 昏暗大礼堂尽头的高台上，一张空着的高桌，中央是一把雕花椅子，学生们在门口互相起哄。\n# en: Take the dare\nopt.left.label [6cb2fd] = 接下赌约\n# en: {You sit, cross your legs, and sip the air. The doorway erupts.|You sit for a full minute. The chair is surprisingly comfortable, and very slightly warm.|You take the dare, and lift the steaming teacup, and drink. Nobody in the doorway breathes. It's very good tea.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8a9adf] = {你坐下，跷起腿，对着空气啜了一口。门口炸开了锅。|你坐了整整一分钟。椅子出奇地舒服，还微微有点暖。|你接下赌约，端起那杯冒着热气的茶，喝了一口。门口没人敢出气。茶非常好。}\n# en: {As you sit, a quiet, delighted laugh comes from the dark beyond the dais. You leave very fast.|A portrait above the table clears its throat. You have never moved so fast in your life.|You reach for the steaming teacup and a voice behind you says, \"That's mine.\" You don't look round. You simply leave.}\nopt.left.say.2 [ffe96a] = {你刚坐下，高台后的黑暗里传来一声轻轻的、愉快的笑。你飞快地离开了。|高桌上方的一幅肖像清了清嗓子。你这辈子从没跑得这么快过。|你伸手去拿那杯冒着热气的茶，身后一个声音说：“那是我的。”你没回头。你直接走了。}\n# en: Tell them you value your life\nopt.right.label [bbde75] = 说你还想活命\n# en: {They call you a coward, fondly. You keep your dignity, and your place at the academy.|\"Coward,\" they chant. You bow. It seems the safest thing to do in that hall.|You decline, and point out the steaming cup. The first-years go very quiet, and so does the dare.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cd4ef0] = {他们亲切地骂你胆小鬼。你保住了尊严，也保住了学籍。|“胆小鬼！”他们齐声喊。你鞠了一躬。在那间礼堂里，这似乎是最安全的做法。|你拒绝了，还指了指那杯冒着热气的茶。一年级生们一下子安静了，赌约也跟着安静了。}\n\n@event infirmary_spare_bed\n# en: The Spare Bed\ntitle [f2835c] = 空床位\n# en: {You only came in for a plaster.|Your name is on the slate by the end bed, in pencil.|Your name on the slate is in ink now.} {health < 40: Sister Amaranth takes one look at you and points at the spare bed.|Sister Amaranth decides, over her spectacles, that you'll live.} {It smells of lavender and singed wool.|Two beds down, someone hiccups in a minor key.|{season == \"winter\": A warming charm hums under every blanket.|The curtains breathe.}}\ntext [babfb3] = {你只是来要块膏药的。|尽头那张床边的石板上，用铅笔写着你的名字。|石板上你的名字现在是用墨水写的了。} {health < 40: 阿玛兰斯修女看了你一眼，指了指那张空床。|阿玛兰斯修女从眼镜上方打量你，判定你死不了。} {屋里有薰衣草和烧焦羊毛的味道。|隔两张床，有人在用小调打嗝。|{season == \"winter\": 每条毯子底下都嗡嗡响着取暖咒。|窗帘在呼吸。}}\n# en: A row of white infirmary beds under a high arched window, one turned down, a stern healer pointing at it.\nscene [6baaff] = 高拱窗下一排白色病床，其中一张已铺开，一位严厉的医护指着它。\n# en: Lie down for an hour\nopt.left.label [a6694f] = 躺一个钟头\n# en: {You sleep the deep, dreamless sleep of the infirmary. The tonic afterwards costs a few crowns and tastes of regret.|You wake under a blanket you didn't pull up. There is a tonic on the side, and a bill under it.|Your name is on the slate in ink, so you take the end bed without asking. The tonic is waiting, and so is the bill.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e69f26] = {你睡了一场医务室式的、无梦的深眠。醒来后的补药要几个克朗，味道像悔恨。|你醒来时身上盖着一条不是你拉的毯子。床头有一瓶补药，下面压着账单。|石板上你的名字是用墨水写的，所以你没问就躺到了最边上那张床。补药已经备好，账单也是。}\n# en: Help roll bandages\nopt.right.label [a9dc7c] = 帮忙卷绷带\n# en: {Sister Amaranth teaches you the knot she uses. You will never tie your boots any other way.|You roll bandages and listen to her opinion of duelling, which is long and specific.|You roll bandages as if you work here. Sister Amaranth says that if you're going to be here this often you may as well.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b86073] = {阿玛兰斯修女教你她打的那种结。从此你系鞋带再也不用别的结。|你一边卷绷带，一边听她对决斗的看法——又长又具体。|你卷绷带的样子像在这儿上班。阿玛兰斯修女说，既然你来得这么勤，不如干脆在这儿上班。}\n\n@event infirmary_visiting_hour\n# en: Visiting Hour\ntitle [d29dca] = 探视时间\n# en: {Visiting hour. {friend} is in the bed by the window,|Visiting hour. Sister Amaranth waves you to the stool you used last time. Today it's {friend},|Visiting hour. The stool by the window has worn to your shape. Today it's {friend},} {nursing a duelling bruise the colour of plums.|recovering from a draught of hiccups that hiccups back.|wrapped in three blankets against a lake chill.} {They} brighten{s} when you come in.\ntext [f59c0b] = {探视时间。{friend}躺在靠窗的床上，|探视时间。阿玛兰斯修女朝你上回坐的凳子招招手。今天是{friend}，|探视时间。窗边那只凳子已经磨成了你的形状。今天是{friend}，} {正护着一块李子色的决斗淤青。|正从一剂会反过来打嗝的打嗝药里恢复。|正裹着三条毯子抵御湖上的寒气。} 看见你进来，{they}眼睛一亮。\n# en: A friend propped up in an infirmary bed by a window, a visitor on a stool beside them with a paper bag of grapes.\nscene [3e7207] = 一位朋友靠坐在窗边的病床上，探视者坐在旁边的凳子上，手里拿着一纸袋葡萄。\n# en: Sit and keep {them} company\nopt.left.label [036ca8] = 坐下陪陪{them}\n# en: {You bring grapes and eat most of them yourself. {friend} doesn't mind.|You stay until Sister Amaranth sweeps you out. {friend} waves from the pillows.|You sit on your worn stool and tell {friend} everything they've missed. {They} fall{s} asleep halfway, smiling.}\nopt.left.say.1 [37af3a] = {你带了葡萄，自己吃了大半。{friend}并不介意。|你一直待到阿玛兰斯修女把你扫地出门。{friend}在枕头间朝你挥手。|你坐在你那张磨旧的凳子上，把{friend}错过的事都讲给{them}听。{they}听到一半就睡着了，还带着笑。}\n# en: Smuggle in a pastry\nopt.right.label [1dcbe0] = 偷带一块点心\n# en: {The pastry makes it past the desk. {friend} eats it under the blanket like a crime.|You hide the pastry in a hollowed-out book. {friend} is delighted by both.|You smuggle in a hot pastry under your coat. {friend} eats it wrapped in all three blankets and declares you a hero.}\nopt.right.say.1 [93dc84] = {点心顺利混过了前台。{friend}躲在毯子底下吃，像在作案。|你把点心藏在一本挖空的书里。{friend}对两样都很开心。|你把一个热点心藏在外套底下带了进来。{friend}裹着三条毯子吃完，宣布你是英雄。}\n# en: {Sister Amaranth confiscates the pastry and eats it herself, slowly, while looking at you.|Sister Amaranth smells the pastry from across the ward and holds out her hand without a word.|Sister Amaranth takes the pastry, cuts it in three, and gives you, {friend} and herself a piece each. Then she lectures you both.}\nopt.right.say.2 [032f46] = {阿玛兰斯修女没收了点心，然后看着你，慢慢地自己吃掉了。|阿玛兰斯修女隔着整个病房就闻到了点心味，一言不发地伸出手。|阿玛兰斯修女拿走点心，切成三块，你、{friend}和她自己各一块。然后把你们俩都训了一顿。}\n\n@event infirmary_tonic_cupboard\n# en: The Tonic Cupboard\ntitle [f80f9b] = 补药柜\n# en: {The tonic cupboard is open, and Sister Amaranth is in the back having her tea.|The tonic cupboard is open at teatime. A new label on the door reads \"counted daily\".|At teatime the cupboard is open, and the label now reads \"counted daily, by name\". The bright blue bottles have moved to the front.} {mana < 20: Your head aches with the dry ache of spent magic.|{The bottles clink in the draught.|A label reads, in her handwriting, \"absolutely not\".|The bright blue bottles have a new lock.}}\ntext [8d3dac] = {补药柜开着，阿玛兰斯修女在里屋喝茶。|下午茶时间，补药柜开着。门上新贴了一张标签：“每日清点”。|下午茶时间，柜门开着，标签现在写着“每日清点，按人登记”。亮蓝色的瓶子都挪到了前排。} {mana < 20: 你的头隐隐作痛，是法力耗尽后那种干涩的疼。|{瓶子在穿堂风里叮当作响。|一张标签上是她的字迹：“绝对不行”。|那些亮蓝色的瓶子上了一把新锁。}}\n# en: A tall glass-fronted cupboard of coloured tonics in an empty infirmary, its door ajar, a teacup steaming on the healer's desk.\nscene [e6e707] = 空荡荡的医务室里，一个装满彩色补药的高玻璃柜，柜门半开，医护的桌上一杯茶正冒着热气。\n# en: Help yourself to a tonic\nopt.left.label [3899be] = 自己拿一瓶\n# en: {You drink it in the corridor. It tastes of peppermint and getting away with things.|You pocket the bottle, rinse it later, and put it back full of water. You feel slightly worse about that.|You take a blue one from the front, since that's where they've been put. You suspect that was the point.}\nopt.left.say.1 [121e38] = {你在走廊里喝了它。薄荷味，外加侥幸得手的味道。|你把瓶子揣进兜里，后来洗净，灌满清水放了回去。这让你心里更过意不去了一点。|你从最前排拿了一瓶蓝的，既然它们被摆在那儿。你怀疑这正是用意。}\n# en: {\"That one is for goats,\" says Sister Amaranth from the doorway. She makes you drink the right one, which is worse.|Sister Amaranth's teacup appears at your elbow. \"Put it back,\" she says, \"and sit down.\"|The blue bottles were put at the front to see who'd take one. Sister Amaranth writes your name in the \"counted daily\" book.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c812eb] = {“那瓶是给山羊的。”阿玛兰斯修女在门口说。她让你喝下对的那瓶，这更糟。|阿玛兰斯修女的茶杯出现在你手肘边。“放回去，”她说，“然后坐下。”|那些蓝瓶子被摆到前排，就是为了看谁会拿。阿玛兰斯修女把你的名字写进了“每日清点”的本子。}\n# en: Ask for one properly\nopt.right.label [8a2146] = 好好开口要\n# en: {She charges you, lectures you, and gives you the good one.|She charges you, lectures you a little less, and gives you the good one.|\"You again,\" she says, and hands it over before you ask.}\nopt.right.say.1 [381f3d] = {她收了钱，训了你一顿，给了你好的那瓶。|她收了钱，训得少了些，给了你好的那瓶。|“又是你。”她说，没等你开口就递了过来。}\n# en: She looks at your empty purse, gives you the good one anyway, and lectures you twice as long.\nopt.right.say.2 [58433e] = 她看了看你空空的钱袋，照样给了你好的那瓶，训话却长了一倍。\n\n@event alchemy_lab_spare_cauldron\n# en: A Spare Cauldron\ntitle [9b8e1b] = 一口空坩埚\n# en: {There is a spare cauldron on the second bench, unclaimed.|The spare cauldron has a chalk mark on its rim like your initial.|Your cauldron, everyone calls it. Someone has scratched your initial into the handle.} {Someone has left a ladle standing upright in something.|The bench smells of mint and burnt sugar.|{season == \"summer\": The heat makes every brew twice as eager.|The windows have steamed over with other people's experiments.}}\ntext [ae405d] = {第二张工作台上有一口空坩埚，没人认领。|那口空坩埚的边沿上有个粉笔记号，像你名字的首字母。|大家都管它叫“你的坩埚”。有人把你的首字母正经刻在了把手上。} {有人把一把长勺竖着插在某种东西里。|工作台上有薄荷和焦糖的味道。|{season == \"summer\": 炎热让每锅药剂都格外急躁。|窗户被别人的实验蒸得一片模糊。}}\n# en: A long laboratory bench in the afternoon light, a small cauldron free among larger ones, jars of ingredients lined up beside it.\nscene [eac0fa] = 午后阳光里一张长长的实验台，大坩埚之间空着一口小坩埚，旁边排着一列药材罐。\n# en: Brew a practice batch\nopt.left.label [7eec57] = 熬一锅练手\n# en: {The brew turns the right colour at the right moment. It feels like being trusted.|It comes out clear and smells of nothing, which is exactly right.|You brew in your cauldron, by your initial. It knows your hand by now and doesn't argue.}\nopt.left.say.1 [89c735] = {药剂在恰好的时刻变成了恰好的颜色。那感觉像是被信任。|成品清澈、无味，正是该有的样子。|你在刻着你缩写的坩埚里熬药。它已经熟悉你的手法，不再跟你较劲。}\n# en: {The brew turns a colour you have never seen. So do your eyebrows.|It foams over and sets, firmly, around your sleeve.|Even your own cauldron turns on you today. The brew sets, and your initial is the only part of the handle you can see.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7e93ad] = {药剂变成了一种你从没见过的颜色，你的眉毛也是。|它沸溢出来，牢牢地凝固在你的袖子上。|今天连你自己的坩埚都和你作对。药剂凝固了，手柄上只露出你刻的缩写。}\n# en: Scrub the cauldrons for crowns\nopt.right.label [cb89c5] = 刷坩埚挣几个钱\n# en: {You learn more about failed potions from their residue than from any lecture.|You scrub until the bench is clean. Then you scratch your own initials into it, small, under the rim.|You scrub every cauldron and yours last, with care. It shines. Someone else uses it the next morning, and you mind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [874eee] = {从失败药剂的残渣里，你学到的比任何一堂课都多。|你把工作台刷得干干净净，然后在边沿下面小小地刻上了自己的名字缩写。|你把每口坩埚都刷了，最后才仔细刷你自己的。它亮闪闪的。第二天早上别人用了它，你很介意。}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_third_floor\n# en: The Third-Floor Fumes\ntitle [fa28aa] = 三楼的烟雾\n# en: {On the third floor the ventilation charm has given up, and the fumes have taken charge.|Someone has hung a sign by the stairs giving the fumes' mood of the day.|The fumes' sign has a column for forecasts now, in several hands.} {Everyone is speaking in rhyme and trying to hide it.|Everyone is laughing at nothing and cannot stop.|Everyone is being unusually honest about everyone else's hair.} {season == \"winter\": The windows are frozen shut.|}\ntext [aa2f98] = {三楼的通风咒已经放弃了，烟雾接管了这里。|有人在楼梯口挂了块牌子，写着烟雾今天的心情。|烟雾的牌子现在多了一栏预报，好几种笔迹。} {所有人都在用押韵说话，还想掩饰。|所有人都在莫名其妙地笑，停不下来。|所有人都对别人的发型异常坦诚。} {season == \"winter\": 窗户冻死了。|}\n# en: A crowded upper floor of an alchemy laboratory, coloured vapour hanging under the ceiling, students working with handkerchiefs tied over their faces.\nscene [51e9da] = 炼金实验室拥挤的楼上，彩色的蒸气悬在天花板下，学生们脸上蒙着手帕干活。\n# en: Stay and work in the fug\nopt.left.label [d14428] = 留在烟里干活\n# en: {You get a great deal done and agree with everyone about everything.|You leave with good notes and a headache that hums.|You work through the honesty fumes and hear what everyone thinks of your hair. Your notes are excellent. Your hair is fine.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6775d1] = {你干了一大堆活，而且对所有人的所有话都表示赞同。|你带着一份好笔记和一阵嗡嗡响的头疼离开。|你在“说实话”的烟雾里干活，听到了每个人对你发型的看法。你的笔记很出色。你的头发没问题。}\n# en: Force the windows open\nopt.right.label [0d2916] = 把窗户撬开\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {The ice cracks. Half the floor cheers; half complains of the cold.|The frozen latch gives, and so does your thumbnail.|The window cracks open and the cold rushes in. Everyone stops being honest at once, with relief.}|{The windows give. The fumes leave, sulking, and the floor applauds.|You wedge the window with a textbook. The fumes drift out, and nearly the textbook too.|You open the window and the honest fumes leave. The silence that follows is extremely polite.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [015c79] = {season == \"winter\": {冰裂开了。半层楼欢呼，另一半抱怨冷。|冻住的插销开了，你的指甲也裂了。|窗户裂开一道缝，冷风灌了进来。大家立刻都不再说实话了，松了口气。}|{窗户开了。烟雾悻悻离去，整层楼鼓掌。|你用一本课本卡住窗户。烟雾飘了出去，课本也差点跟着出去。|你打开窗，那股让人说实话的烟雾散了。随后的沉默极其礼貌。}}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_unlabelled_jar\n# en: The Unlabelled Jar\ntitle [a3c74d] = 没贴标签的罐子\n# en: {Someone has left an unlabelled jar on the shared shelf. It glows, faintly.|Another unlabelled jar on the shared shelf, beside the cautious label someone wrote for the last one.|The shared shelf now has a row of careful labels, and one new jar at the end of it, waiting its turn.} {It smells of thunder.|Something inside it turns over when you look.|It is warm to the touch.} {gpa >= 3: You could probably identify it, given time.|You have no idea what it is.}\ntext [23e27f] = {有人在公用架上落下一只没贴标签的罐子。它微微发光。|公用架上又一只没贴标签的罐子，旁边是有人给上一只写的谨慎标签。|公用架上现在排着一列仔细写好的标签，末尾一只新罐子在排队等着。} {它闻起来像雷声。|你一看它，里面就有东西翻了个身。|摸上去是温的。} {gpa >= 3: 给你点时间，你大概能认出它。|你完全不知道它是什么。}\n# en: A dusty shelf of ingredient jars in an alchemy laboratory, one jar with no label glowing faintly, a student reaching for it.\nscene [328708] = 炼金实验室里一排积灰的药材罐，其中一只没有标签、微微发光，一个学生正伸手去拿。\n# en: Add a pinch to your brew\nopt.left.label [163e45] = 往药里加一撮\n# en: {The brew turns gold and so do you, briefly. You feel enormous.|The brew sings a single note and leaves you humming with power.|A pinch of the warm stuff and your brew purrs like a cat. You don't know what it did, but it's better.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0900d9] = {药剂变成金色，你也短暂地变成了金色。你觉得自己无比庞大。|药剂唱出一个音，让你浑身嗡嗡作响，充满力量。|加了一撮那温热的东西，你的药剂像猫一样咕噜起来。你不知道它起了什么作用，但确实更好了。}\n# en: {The brew ignites, politely, and then impolitely.|Your cauldron sneezes. So, for the rest of the afternoon, do you.|The warm stuff makes your brew too warm, and then your bench, and then your sleeve.}\nopt.left.say.2 [cd1004] = {药剂先是礼貌地、随后很不礼貌地烧了起来。|你的坩埚打了个喷嚏。整个下午，你也跟着打喷嚏。|那温热的东西让你的药剂太热了，然后是你的工作台，然后是你的袖子。}\n# en: Label it, carefully\nopt.right.label [0222a1] = 小心地贴上标签\n# en: {An hour of tests later, you write a cautious label. Someone adds a skull to it.|You work out what it is. Then you wash your hands three times.|You label it and add it to the row. Yours is the most careful label on the shelf, and the most honest: \"Warm. Unsure.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [7f65dd] = {一小时的测试之后，你写下一张谨慎的标签。有人在上面加了个骷髅。|你弄清了它是什么。然后把手洗了三遍。|你给它贴上标签，排进那一行。你的标签是架子上最谨慎、也最诚实的一张：“温热。不确定。”}\n\n@event job_board_odd_jobs\n# en: Odd Jobs\ntitle [99e6aa] = 零活\n# en: {Under the proper postings, someone has pinned an odd job.|Under the proper postings is an odd job, and a second pin someone has left beside it, for answers.|The odd-jobs corner has a card addressed to you by name, in a hand you're starting to recognise.} {Someone wants a homesick portrait read to.|Someone wants a ladder held, no questions asked.|Someone wants a reluctant gargoyle walked.} {term: |In the break the board is half bare, and the odd jobs pay a little better.}\ntext [11f8f5] = {正式招工启事底下，有人钉了一张零活。|正式启事底下有一张零活，旁边还多钉了一枚图钉，留着给人回复。|零活角有一张写着你名字的卡片，笔迹你开始认得了。} {有人想找人给一幅想家的肖像画念书。|有人想找人扶梯子，不许问问题。|有人想找人遛一只不情愿的石像鬼。} {term: |假期里告示板空了一半，零活的工钱也高了一点。}\n# en: A cork board in an entrance hall, a small notice pinned at an angle under the official job postings, a student reading it with a frown.\nscene [b55309] = 门厅里的软木告示板，一张小启事歪钉在正式招工启事下面，一个学生皱着眉读它。\n# en: Take the job\nopt.left.label [a7359a] = 接下这活\n# en: {It takes all afternoon and pays exactly what it said, which is rarer than it should be.|It is stranger than the notice suggested, and pays slightly less.|You take the gargoyle for its walk. It doesn't want to go, and then it doesn't want to come back. The pay is fair.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e17840] = {花了一整个下午，报酬和说好的分毫不差——这比应有的要少见。|比启事上写的更古怪，工钱也少了一点。|你带石像鬼去散步。它不想去，后来又不想回来。工钱公道。}\n# en: Haggle first\nopt.right.label [789545] = 先讲讲价\n# en: {You haggle the fee up by a third. Your employer seems to respect you more for it.|You agree a price and a biscuit. The biscuit turns out to be the better half of the deal.|You haggle with the hand you recognise. They pay what you ask, and leave a note: \"Worth it.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [c1ddf4] = {你把价钱抬高了三分之一。雇主似乎因此更尊重你了。|你们谈定了价钱外加一块饼干。结果饼干才是这笔买卖里更划算的那一半。|你和那个你认得笔迹的人讨价还价。对方照你说的价付了，还留了张字条：“值。”}\n# en: {Your employer takes the notice down and pins up a new one, asking for \"someone less difficult\".|The job goes to a first-year who asked for nothing. They seem very happy with it.|You haggle, and the next card in that hand is addressed to someone else. You've been dropped, politely.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ee0c42] = {雇主把启事撕下来，换上一张新的，要找个“好说话一点的人”。|活儿给了一个什么都没要求的一年级生。那孩子看起来非常高兴。|你讨价还价，结果那个笔迹的下一张卡片写给了别人。你被礼貌地换掉了。}\n\n@event job_board_lost_familiars\n# en: Lost and Found\ntitle [7ffffa] = 失物招领\n# en: {The corner of the board for lost things is busier than the jobs.|An old poster in the lost-things corner now reads \"found, thanks to a student\".|The lost-things corner has thank-you notes now. One draws the rescuer with a halo.} {A toad called \"Sir\" is missing, last seen being brave.|A ransom note demands three biscuits for one sock.|A small owl is lost, answers to nothing, and bites.} {season == \"winter\": Half the familiars have simply gone somewhere warm.|}\ntext [d3e159] = {告示板上的失物角比招工那边还热闹。|失物角里一张旧启事上现在写着“已找到，多亏一位学生”。|失物角如今贴着感谢信。有一张把救星画上了光环。} {一只叫“爵士”的蟾蜍走失了，最后一次被人看见时正在逞英雄。|一封勒索信要求三块饼干，换回一只袜子。|一只小猫头鹰走丢了，叫什么都不应，还咬人。} {season == \"winter\": 一半的魔宠只是自己跑去暖和地方了。|}\n# en: The corner of a cork board crowded with hand-drawn posters of lost familiars and a ransom note for a sock.\nscene [8f714b] = 软木告示板的一角，贴满了手绘的走失魔宠启事，还有一封索要一只袜子赎金的勒索信。\n# en: Go looking for it\nopt.left.label [24390e] = 去找找看\n# en: {You find it behind a radiator in the east tower, deeply unrepentant. The reward is small and grateful.|You find it, and the owner cries, and the reward is mostly biscuits.|You find the owl in the rafters of the owlery, pretending to belong. It bites you. The owner pays double, for the bite.}\nopt.left.say.1 [995f44] = {你在东塔的暖气片后面找到了它，毫无悔意。酬金不多，谢意满满。|你找到了它，失主哭了，酬金大部分是饼干。|你在猫头鹰舍的椽子上找到了那只猫头鹰，它正装作是这儿的。它咬了你。失主付了双倍，因为那一口。}\n# en: {You search for an hour and find only a different, much angrier creature.|You don't find it. The next day the poster says \"Found! Thanks anyway.\"|You find a small owl. It is not the small owl. It also bites.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f9f327] = {你找了一小时，只找到另一只脾气暴躁得多的生物。|你没找到。第二天，启事上写着：“找到了！还是谢谢。”|你找到了一只小猫头鹰。不是那一只。它也咬人。}\n# en: Read every notice twice\nopt.right.label [778bc2] = 每张启事读两遍\n# en: {The board is the castle's diary. You learn who lost what, and who owes whom.|You learn three secrets from the small print, and who has terrible handwriting.|You read every notice, including the thank-you notes. One of them is about you. You read that one three times.}\nopt.right.say.1 [de4eb9] = {告示板是城堡的日记。你知道了谁丢了什么，谁欠谁的。|你从小字里读出了三个秘密，还知道了谁的字写得最难看。|你读了每一张启事，包括那些感谢信。其中一封写的是你。那封你读了三遍。}\n\n@event job_board_yellow_notice\n# en: The Oldest Notice\ntitle [0c2305] = 最老的告示\n# en: In the bottom corner of the board, under years of newer notices, is one gone yellow and soft: \"Wanted, Thursdays: someone to read the newssheet aloud to the portrait on the west stair. He likes the sport and the obituaries. Small pay.\" Someone has renewed the pin.\ntext [b5755a] = 告示板底角，压在多年来的新告示下面，有一张已经发黄变软：“招人，每周四：为西侧楼梯上的肖像朗读新闻报。他爱看体育版和讣告。酬劳微薄。”有人换过了图钉。\n# en: A corner of a cork board where one notice, yellowed and curling, is pinned beneath years of newer ones.\nscene [f55d35] = 软木告示板的一角，一张发黄卷边的告示压在多年来的新告示底下。\n# en: Answer it, this Thursday\nopt.left.label [716d02] = 这周四就去应聘\n# en: The portrait is a bald man in an old-fashioned collar who hasn't had the news read to him in years. He cheers the duelling results and sighs at the obituaries. You lose your Thursday evening, and he asks you to come back.\nopt.left.say.1 [129d7b] = 肖像是个戴老式领子的秃顶男人，好多年没人给他读报了。他为决斗赛果欢呼，对着讣告叹气。你搭进了周四晚上，他请你再来。\n# en: Ask the porter who pins it\nopt.right.label [f1824a] = 问门房是谁钉的\n# en: The porter says the notice was there when he started, and that a few crowns appear on the stair every Thursday whether anyone reads or not. He has never asked who leaves it. He suggests you don't either.\nopt.right.say.1 [506c26] = 门房说，他来的时候这张告示就在了，而且每周四楼梯上都会出现几枚克朗，不管有没有人去读。他从没问过是谁留的。他建议你也别问。\n\n@event job_board_torn_notice\n# en: Half a Notice\ntitle [067821] = 半张告示\n# en: A well-paid notice (\"one evening's portering, generous rates\") has drawn two students, who have both got hold of it. It tears down the middle as you arrive. One holds the half with the pay; the other holds the half with the address. Both look at you.\ntext [cdc4cd] = 一张报酬丰厚的告示（“一晚上的搬运活，酬劳优厚”）引来了两个学生，两人都抓住了它。你走过来时它从中间撕开了。一个拿着写报酬的那半，另一个拿着写地址的那半。两人都看着你。\n# en: Two students tugging at either end of a single job notice in front of a crowded cork board, the paper starting to tear.\nscene [2ca221] = 拥挤的软木告示板前，两个学生各拽着一张招工告示的一头，纸开始撕裂。\n# en: Settle it between them\nopt.left.label [c453e7] = 帮他们调解\n# en: You suggest they share the evening and split the fee. They both hate the idea, then both agree to it, then both thank you. You get nothing but a reputation for being reasonable.\nopt.left.say.1 [9bba47] = 你建议他们一起干那一晚，平分报酬。两人都讨厌这个主意，然后都同意了，然后都谢了你。你什么也没得到，除了一个通情达理的名声。\n# en: Read the address and go yourself\nopt.right.label [ff6244] = 看清地址自己去\n# en: You read the address over a shoulder and get there first. The job pays well. By supper both of them have told everyone what you did.\nopt.right.say.1 [9c63ca] = 你从别人肩后瞥见了地址，抢先赶到。这活儿给钱很多。到晚饭时，他俩已经跟所有人说了你干的事。\n\n@event job_board_monthly_letter\n# en: A Letter a Month\ntitle [22898d] = 每月一封信\n# en: A small notice in a careful, shaky hand: \"Wanted: a student to post a letter for me in the village, first of each month. A few crowns. I can't manage the hill any more.\" The letter is pinned beside it, sealed and addressed to someone in the village.\ntext [184718] = 一张小告示，字迹工整却发抖：“招一名学生，每月初帮我到山下小镇寄一封信。几枚克朗。我已经爬不动那座山了。”信就钉在旁边，封好了，寄给小镇上的某个人。\n# en: A small handwritten notice pinned beside a sealed letter on a cork board, a student reaching to unpin both.\nscene [144090] = 软木告示板上，一张手写的小告示旁钉着一封封好的信，一个学生伸手要把两样都取下来。\n# en: Take the letter down the hill\nopt.left.label [6cf051] = 把信带下山\n# en: You post it, then the next, then the next. In the third month the letter-writer leaves a note: the replies have started coming. You never learn who either of them is. You keep the crowns in a jar.\nopt.left.say.1 [9043ac] = 你寄了这封，又寄下一封，再下一封。第三个月，写信人留了张字条：回信开始来了。你始终不知道他们两个是谁。你把那些克朗存在一个罐子里。\n# en: Leave it for someone else\nopt.right.label [43df3f] = 留给别人\n# en: You leave it on the board. The next morning it's gone, and someone has drawn a small heart beside the notice in pencil.\nopt.right.say.1 [5241d2] = 你把它留在告示板上。第二天早上它不见了，有人用铅笔在告示旁画了一颗小小的心。\n\n@event job_board_copying\n# en: Fair Copies Wanted\ntitle [38430b] = 誊抄启事\n# en: {A notice in a shaky hand. A student down with fever needs a fortnight of lecture notes copied out fair, and will pay for neatness.|Another fever, another fortnight of notes to copy fair. The notice says \"neat hand preferred\", and someone has written your name beside it.|The copying notice again. By now you can copy a page without reading a word of it, if you want to.}\ntext [789ff1] = {一张字迹发抖的告示。一个发烧病倒的学生需要人把两周的课堂笔记誊抄干净，字写得工整就给钱。|又一个发烧的，又是两周的笔记要誊清。告示上写着“字迹工整者优先”，旁边有人写上了你的名字。|又是誊抄的告示。到了现在，只要你愿意，你可以抄完一页而一个字都不读进去。}\n# en: A cork board with a notice in a sick student's shaky hand, a stack of borrowed lecture notes tied with string beneath it.\nscene [ce95da] = 一块软木板上钉着一张病中学生手抖写下的告示，下面是一摞用绳子捆着的借来的课堂笔记。\n# en: Copy them for the pay\nopt.left.label [146f74] = 为了报酬抄完\n# en: {Two evenings of careful letters. The pay arrives with a note that says your sevens are beautiful.|You copy every page, blot nothing and are paid exactly. Your wrist aches until the weekend.|You copy without reading, as fast as the ink allows. The pay is fair and the evening is gone.}\nopt.left.say.1 [652de9] = {两个晚上小心翼翼地写字。报酬送来时附了一张字条，说你的“7”写得真漂亮。|你抄完每一页，一个墨点都没有，报酬分毫不差。你的手腕一直酸到周末。|你一个字不读，墨水干得多快你就抄得多快。报酬公道，晚上也没了。}\n# en: Copy them slowly, and learn them too\nopt.right.label [11f745] = 慢慢抄，顺便学会\n# en: {You read as you copy. Half the pay goes on candles, and you learn a course you aren't even taking.|You copy slowly and read every line. The notes are better than your own, and you keep a few of the ideas.|You read it all, slowly. The student pays a little less for the delay, and you know a little more for it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [efde5d] = {你边抄边读。一半报酬花在了蜡烛上，你却学会了一门你根本没选的课。|你抄得很慢，每一行都读了。这些笔记比你自己的还好，你留下了其中几个想法。|你慢慢地全读了一遍。那个学生因为拖延少付了一点，你也因此多懂了一点。}\n\n@event job_board_message_run\n# en: Messages Across the Castle\ntitle [7ff393] = 穿越城堡送信\n# en: {A professor wants a runner for the afternoon, with notes for every tower before the bell. A small fee, and no reading them.|The runner's notice is back, with a longer list of towers.|The runner's notice, and a list you could walk in your sleep. The staircases have learned your face.}\ntext [4f0fd0] = {一位教授想找个人跑一下午腿，在钟响前把字条送到每座塔。报酬不多，不许偷看。|跑腿的告示又来了，塔楼的名单更长了。|跑腿的告示，和一张你闭着眼都能走完的名单。楼梯们已经认得你的脸了。}\n# en: A student with a satchel of folded notes running across a castle bridge between towers, a staircase swinging away behind them.\nscene [772972] = 一个背着装满折好字条的挎包的学生在塔楼之间的廊桥上奔跑，身后一段楼梯正荡开。\n# en: Run the round yourself\nopt.left.label [8d64a6] = 亲自跑完这一趟\n# en: {You reach the last tower as the bell goes. Your legs are jelly and the fee is honest.|Seven towers, two moving staircases and one argument with a portrait. Paid in full.|You take the shortcuts the staircases allow you now. Paid, and back in time for supper.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2686fa] = {钟响时你正好到了最后一座塔。腿软得像果冻，报酬实打实。|七座塔、两段会动的楼梯，外加和一幅肖像吵了一架。报酬全额付清。|你走那些楼梯现在肯让你走的近路。拿了钱，还赶上了晚饭。}\n# en: Send the owls instead\nopt.right.label [b749ed] = 改派猫头鹰去送\n# en: {You pay the owlery out of the fee and keep the rest, and the afternoon. The professor never knows.|The owls do it faster than you would have. You pocket what's left and feel only slightly guilty.|The owls know the round now as well as you. You keep a little of the fee and all of your afternoon.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a4dbd9] = {你从报酬里付了猫头鹰棚的钱，剩下的归你，下午也归你。教授永远不会知道。|猫头鹰送得比你快。你把剩下的钱揣进兜里，只有一点点内疚。|猫头鹰现在和你一样熟悉这条路线了。你留下一点报酬，还有整个下午。}\n\n@event job_board_market_stall\n# en: Mind the Stall\ntitle [e20a8c] = 帮忙看摊\n# en: {A villager with a bad knee wants someone to mind her market stall on Saturday morning, for a share of the takings.|The stall again: jars, woollens, the bad knee and the share of the takings.|The stall owner pins the notice up with your name already on it, and a question mark, to be polite.}\ntext [31b0c3] = {一个膝盖不好的村民想找人周六上午替她看集市摊位，按营业额分成。|又是那个摊位：罐子、毛织品、那个坏膝盖，还有那份分成。|摊主钉告示时已经写上了你的名字，后面出于礼貌加了个问号。}\n# en: A village market stall heaped with jars and woollens, a student behind it with a cash tin, the owner hobbling away towards a chair in the sun.\nscene [1561ec] = 村里集市上一个堆满罐子和毛织品的摊位，一个学生守在钱箱后面，摊主一瘸一拐地走向阳光下的一把椅子。\n# en: Mind it as she asked\nopt.left.label [2530bc] = 照她说的看好摊\n# en: {You sell three jars and a scarf and count the tin twice. She pays you a fair share and a jar.|A slow morning, honestly counted. She is pleased with the tin and gives you your share.|You know the prices by heart now. She hands you your share without counting it, which is a kind of compliment.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0d8fb2] = {你卖出三个罐子和一条围巾，把钱箱数了两遍。她分给你公道的一份，外加一个罐子。|一个冷清的上午，账目清清楚楚。她对钱箱很满意，把你那份给了你。|价钱你早已烂熟于心。她没数就把你那份递给你，这也算是一种夸奖。}\n# en: Talk up the wares\nopt.right.label [f95d45] = 卖力吆喝\n# en: {You sell like a pedlar and the takings are up. Most of the extra goes back to her, as it should, and you've learned a patter.|You talk a farmer into two scarves he didn't know he needed. Your share is small and your voice is gone.|You could sell her jars to anyone now. She jokes about leaving you the stall.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5f455f] = {你像个货郎一样吆喝，营业额涨了。多出来的大部分理所当然地归了她，你学会了一套说辞。|你说动一个农夫买了两条他原本不知道自己需要的围巾。你的分成不多，嗓子却哑了。|现在你能把她的罐子卖给任何人。她开玩笑说要把摊位留给你。}\n\n@event job_board_lost_property\n# en: The Lost-Property Box\ntitle [312ea9] = 失物箱\n# en: {The porter wants the lost-property box sorted before it starts to smell, and pays by the hour.|The lost-property box is full again, the porter says, mostly of the same gloves.|The porter slides the box across without a word. You know where the odd boot goes now.}\ntext [a8b4a9] = {门房想在失物箱发臭之前找人整理一下，按钟点付钱。|门房说失物箱又满了，大多还是那几只手套。|门房一言不发地把箱子推过来。那只落单的靴子该放哪儿，你现在已经知道了。}\n# en: The porter's lodge, a vast wooden box of odd gloves, scarves, books and one boot, a student sorting them into piles.\nscene [2e859c] = 门房小屋里，一个巨大的木箱装满了不成对的手套、围巾、书和一只靴子，一个学生正把它们分成几堆。\n# en: Sort it quickly for the pay\nopt.left.label [cf84ec] = 为了报酬快快理完\n# en: {Gloves, scarves, books and one boot, in piles by dusk. The porter pays and keeps the boot for himself.|You sort it in an hour. The porter pays for two, out of habit.|You sort it fast. The porter pays, and then tries the odd boot on again, hopefully.}\nopt.left.say.1 [37988e] = {手套、围巾、书和一只靴子，天黑前分成了几堆。门房付了钱，把那只靴子留给了自己。|你一个钟头就理完了。门房出于习惯，按两个钟头付了钱。|你理得飞快。门房付了钱，然后满怀希望地又试穿了一次那只落单的靴子。}\n# en: Read what people lose\nopt.right.label [51b2bd] = 看看大家都丢了什么\n# en: {You learn who carries love letters and who never returns library books. The porter pays you less for taking so long.|You read the names in the books and the notes in the pockets, and put everything back exactly. A slow hour, and a useful one.|You know the castle by what it drops. The porter pays a little less, and you put the gossip away for later.}\nopt.right.say.1 [144709] = {你知道了谁随身带着情书，谁从来不还图书馆的书。门房嫌你磨蹭，少付了一点。|你读了书里的名字和口袋里的字条，又把一切原样放回。一个慢吞吞却很有用的钟头。|你从城堡掉落的东西里认识了这座城堡。门房少付了一点，你把这些八卦收好留着以后用。}\n\n@event job_board_sick_familiar\n# en: A Sick Familiar\ntitle [d87019] = 生病的魔宠\n# en: {A student going home for a funeral needs someone to sit up with their sick familiar for two nights. They will pay.|Another notice for a night sitter, for a sick toad this time, and the pay is better.|The familiars' night-sitter: that's you, apparently. A cat this time, and a worried owner.}\ntext [966bf7] = {一个要回家奔丧的学生需要人帮忙陪护生病的魔宠两个晚上。会付钱。|又一张找夜间看护的告示，这回是一只生病的蟾蜍，报酬更高。|魔宠的夜间看护：看来就是你了。这回是一只猫，和一个忧心忡忡的主人。}\n# en: A student sitting up by lamplight beside a basket where a small sick familiar is curled in a blanket, a bottle of tonic on the table.\nscene [b6ed91] = 灯下，一个学生坐在篮子旁守夜，篮子里一只生病的小魔宠裹在毯子里蜷着，桌上放着一瓶补药。\n# en: Sit up with it, both nights\nopt.left.label [1a5705] = 两个晚上都守着\n# en: {Two nights of tonic every hour and very little sleep. It recovers. So, eventually, do you.|The toad recovers and you nearly don't. The owner pays double and calls you a saint.|The cat is ungrateful and lives. You're paid, and spend the next day asleep in lectures.}\nopt.left.say.1 [57e360] = {两个晚上每小时喂一次补药，几乎没睡。它好了。你最后也缓了过来。|蟾蜍好了，你却差点没撑住。主人付了双倍，说你是圣人。|那只猫毫不领情，但活了下来。你拿到了钱，第二天在课上睡了一整天。}\n# en: Sit up one night, and find a second sitter\nopt.right.label [ca3c8a] = 守一晚，再找个人守第二晚\n# en: {You take the first night and talk a friend into the second. The pay splits; the sleep doesn't.|One night each, you and a sleepy first-year. The familiar is fine. Half the pay is still pay.|You know who will sit up for a little money now. You split the nights and the pay, and sleep.}\nopt.right.say.1 [80e56e] = {你守第一晚，说动一个朋友守第二晚。报酬分了，觉却没少睡。|你和一个睡眼惺忪的一年级生一人守一晚。魔宠没事。一半报酬也是报酬。|你现在知道谁愿意为一点小钱熬夜了。你们分了夜班和报酬，你睡了个好觉。}\n\n@event job_board_porters_crates\n# en: Crates for the Porter\ntitle [63c7e3] = 帮门房搬箱子\n# en: {The porter needs crates carried up from the barge, a great many of them, up a great many stairs.|Crates again, the porter says, pointing at the barge. More of them than last time.|You hear the barge bell and the porter waves before you're even down the stairs. Crates.}\ntext [fd3c49] = {门房需要人把箱子从驳船上搬上来，箱子很多，楼梯也很多。|门房指着驳船说，又是箱子，比上回还多。|你一听见驳船的铃声，还没下楼门房就冲你挥手了。箱子。}\n# en: A cart of wooden crates at the foot of a long castle stair, a student lifting one while the porter points upward.\nscene [f08061] = 一段长长的城堡楼梯脚下停着一车木箱，一个学生正抬起一只，门房指着楼上。\n# en: Carry them all\nopt.left.label [7cb1d5] = 全部搬完\n# en: {Forty crates up seventy stairs. The porter pays you and a little extra for not dropping the one that clinked.|Your back will remember every crate. The pay is good.|You carry the lot and don't count them any more. Paid, and sore.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c46a9e] = {四十只箱子，七十级台阶。门房付了钱，还因为你没摔那只叮当响的箱子多给了一点。|你的背会记住每一只箱子。报酬不错。|你搬完了所有箱子，已经懒得去数了。拿了钱，浑身酸痛。}\n# en: Carry what you can, and train with it\nopt.right.label [ca388f] = 能搬多少搬多少，当作锻炼\n# en: {You take them slowly, two steps at a time, and count it as exercise. The porter pays for the crates you managed.|Half the crates, carried properly. Your legs are stronger and your purse a little lighter than it could be.|You carry what you can and stop before your back does. Paid for half, and stronger for it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4873fd] = {你慢慢地搬，一次两级台阶，权当锻炼。门房按你搬上去的箱子付钱。|搬了一半，每一只都搬得稳稳当当。你的腿更有力了，钱袋比本可以的轻了一点。|你能搬多少搬多少，在背扛不住之前停了下来。拿到一半的钱，人也更结实了。}\n\n@event job_board_tutor_hour\n# en: An Hour of Tutoring\ntitle [e711ef] = 一小时辅导\n# en: {A first-year's notice, written with great care, asks for an hour's help with an essay. They can pay a little.|The same first-year's careful handwriting: another essay, another hour.|The first-year has stopped writing notices. They just pin up the essay, with a coin taped to the corner.}\ntext [7ff910] = {一个一年级生写得一丝不苟的告示，想请人帮忙改一小时论文。对方能付一点钱。|还是那个一年级生工工整整的字迹：又一篇论文，又一个钟头。|那个一年级生已经不写告示了。对方直接把论文钉上去，角上用胶带粘着一枚硬币。}\n# en: An empty classroom, a nervous first-year with an essay full of crossings-out and an older student pointing at a line.\nscene [31065a] = 一间空教室里，一个紧张的一年级生拿着一篇涂改满篇的论文，一个高年级学生正指着其中一行。\n# en: Take the hour, and the coin\nopt.left.label [6ef63a] = 收下这一小时和那枚硬币\n# en: {An hour of commas and confusion. The essay improves; so does your purse, slightly.|You take them through it line by line. They pay, and thank you twice.|You know their mistakes before they make them now. The hour goes quickly, and they pay.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b044e6] = {一个钟头的逗号和困惑。论文改好了，你的钱袋也稍微鼓了一点。|你带着对方一行一行地过。对方付了钱，道了两次谢。|对方还没犯错你就知道会错在哪儿了。一个钟头过得很快，对方付了钱。}\n# en: Help, and leave the coin pinned\nopt.right.label [2ddff2] = 帮忙，把硬币留在板上\n# en: {You leave the coin taped to the board. The essay comes back with a good mark, and the first-year tells everyone.|You help for nothing. They try to pay you anyway, and you won't let them.|You leave the coin where it is, as always. Somebody has drawn a small sun beside the notice.}\nopt.right.say.1 [53ee57] = {你让那枚硬币继续粘在告示板上。论文拿回了不错的分数，那个一年级生逢人就说。|你分文不取地帮了忙。对方还是想付钱，你没让。|你照旧把硬币留在原处。有人在告示旁边画了一个小小的太阳。}\n\n@event job_board_greenhouse_pots\n# en: Pots for Old Maud\ntitle [fb81b6] = 帮老莫德洗花盆\n# en: {Old Maud's notice asks for clay pots scrubbed before planting, for a small wage and a cup of tea.|Maud's notice again. More pots, the same tea.|Maud has given up pinning notices. The pots are just waiting at the greenhouse door, with your brush on top.}\ntext [60254d] = {老莫德的告示：种植前要把陶盆刷干净，给一点工钱，外加一杯茶。|又是莫德的告示。更多的花盆，同样的茶。|莫德已经懒得钉告示了。花盆就堆在温室门口等着，你的刷子放在最上面。}\n# en: A greenhouse doorway stacked with muddy clay pots, an old gardener on a stool directing a student with a scrubbing brush.\nscene [289e8d] = 温室门口堆着满是泥巴的陶盆，一位老园丁坐在凳子上，指挥着一个拿刷子的学生。\n# en: Scrub them for the wage\nopt.left.label [e405b2] = 为了工钱刷完\n# en: {A hundred pots, scrubbed and stacked. Maud pays you and says you're slower than her and twice as thorough.|You scrub them all and drink the tea. The wage is small and honestly earned.|You scrub in silence, and so does she. She pays you and lets you take a cutting home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f75392] = {一百只花盆，刷净摞好。莫德付了钱，说你比她慢，却比她仔细一倍。|你刷完了所有花盆，喝了那杯茶。工钱不多，挣得踏实。|你默默地刷，她也默默地看。她付了钱，还让你带一根插条回家。}\n# en: Scrub them and ask about the plants\nopt.right.label [efad4e] = 边刷边问她植物的事\n# en: {Maud talks while you scrub, about which leaf cures what and which leaf kills. She pays a little less, because of the talking.|You learn three remedies and a poison you should never brew. The wage is smaller; the lesson isn't.|Maud tests you on last month's lesson while you scrub. You pass, mostly. The wage is small and the tea is good.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7f0d49] = {你刷的时候莫德一直在讲：哪片叶子治什么，哪片叶子会要命。因为说了话，她少付了一点。|你学会了三种药方，还有一种永远别熬的毒药。工钱少了，学到的东西可不少。|你刷盆的时候，莫德考你上个月学的东西。你大致都答上了。工钱不多，茶很好喝。}\n\n@event library_revision\n# en: The Revision Tables\ntitle [4e066a] = 复习桌\n# en: The librarians have set out the revision tables early this term: past papers, model answers, and a sign promising the lamps stay lit until the bell. {~Footnote is asleep on last year's Evocation paper.|A shelf has rearranged itself into exam order, which it has never done for anyone before.|Someone has left a full set of notes behind, with a note of their own: \"Take them. I passed.\"}\ntext [18698e] = 这学期图书馆员早早摆出了复习桌：历年试卷、范文答案，还有一块牌子，保证灯一直亮到打铃。{~脚注正睡在去年的塑能术试卷上。|一排书架自己按考试顺序重新排好了，它从来没为谁这么做过。|有人留下了一整套笔记，还附了张字条：“拿去吧。我过了。”}\n# en: A long library table under green-shaded lamps, stacked with past exam papers, students bent over them and a cat asleep at the far end.\nscene [b57a6b] = 一张长长的图书馆桌子，绿罩台灯下堆满历年试卷，学生们埋头苦读，桌子另一头睡着一只猫。\n# en: Sit down and revise\nopt.left.label [2fa5e8] = 坐下来复习\n# en: {~You work through two past papers and most of a third. The questions start to look like ones you have seen before, which is the point.|You copy out the model answers until your hand aches, then close the book and write them again from memory. Mostly right.|By the bell you have a page of everything you didn't know this morning. It is a shorter page than you feared.}\nopt.left.say.1 [134bfd] = {~你做完了两套历年试卷，第三套也做了大半。题目开始看着眼熟，这正是目的所在。|你抄范文答案抄到手酸，然后合上书凭记忆再写一遍。大体都对。|到打铃时，你写满了一页今早还不知道的东西。比你担心的要短。}\n# en: Not today\nopt.right.label [479f6f] = 今天算了\n# en: {~You leave the tables to the people who look more worried than you. The afternoon is yours.|You borrow a novel instead and read it in the window seat. The exams can wait for another day, you hope.|You nod at the revision tables on your way past, like an acquaintance you mean to call on.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ee8bce] = {~你把复习桌让给看起来比你更焦虑的人。这个下午归你了。|你转而借了本小说，坐在窗边座位上读。考试可以改天再说，你希望如此。|你路过时朝复习桌点点头，像对一位打算改天拜访的熟人。}\n# en: Take notes with the owl quill\nopt.up.label [81b0f5] = 用猫头鹰羽毛笔记笔记\n# en: {~The owl quill takes your notes tidier than your hand could, and then underlines the bits you'll be asked. You argue with it for an hour.|The quill writes faster than you think. By the bell you have a page of everything, in a hand you could almost be proud of.|You let the quill copy out the model answers. It corrects your spelling, then your reasoning, then your posture.}\nopt.up.say.1 [80cdb8] = {~猫头鹰羽毛笔记的笔记比你的手写得整齐，然后还把会考的地方划了出来。你跟它争了一个小时。|这支笔写得比你想得还快。到下课铃响，你有了一页什么都有的笔记，字迹你几乎可以引以为傲。|你让笔抄写范文。它纠正了你的拼写，然后是你的推理，然后是你的坐姿。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/castle_more.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/castle_more.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event library_whispering_dome\n# en: The Whispering Dome\ntitle [36615d] = 低语穹顶\n# en: {Under the dome on the sixth floor the echoes are wrong in a useful way. A whisper anywhere in the library arrives here, clear as a bell.|The dome is carrying voices again. Someone has chalked a small ear on the railing.|You know the dome's geography now: by the pillar you hear the front desk; by the window, the stacks.} {Two voices below are discussing a test that hasn't been set yet.|Someone below is rehearsing a proposal of marriage, badly.|{season == \"winter\": The radiators hiss over half of it.|A prefect is reading out demerits to himself, with relish.}}\ntext [408c73] = {六楼穹顶下的回声乱得恰到好处：图书馆里任何角落的一句耳语，都会清清楚楚地传到这里，像钟声一样。|穹顶又在传声了。有人在栏杆上用粉笔画了只小耳朵。|穹顶的“地形”你已了如指掌：靠柱子能听见前台，靠窗户能听见书库。} {楼下有两个声音在讨论一场还没出题的测验。|楼下有人在排练求婚，排得很糟。|{season == \"winter\": 暖气片的嘶嘶声盖住了一半。|一位级长正对着自己念记过名单，念得津津有味。}}\n# en: A gallery railing under a painted dome at the top of the library, a student leaning over it with one ear cocked, seven floors of shelves below.\nscene [dc6a4d] = 图书馆顶层彩绘穹顶下的回廊栏杆，一个学生侧着耳朵趴在上面，脚下是七层书架。\n# en: Lean on the rail and listen\nopt.left.label [7f4f05] = 趴在栏杆上偷听\n# en: {You learn the third question of next week's test, probably. Eavesdroppers, it turns out, don't feel as clever as they'd hoped.|The rehearsal ends with a yes, said in the same voice as the question. You feel like a thief who has stolen a wedding that hasn't happened yet.|You learn exactly what the front desk thinks of you. It's fair, which is worse.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b0b140] = {你听到了下周测验的第三题——大概吧。原来偷听的人并不像自己想的那么得意。|排练以一声“我愿意”收场，用的和提问的是同一个声音。你觉得自己像个贼，偷走了一场还没发生的婚礼。|你听到了前台对你的确切评价。评价很公道，这就更糟了。}\n# en: Cough loudly, and read\nopt.right.label [23bfb3] = 大声咳嗽，然后读书\n# en: {Your cough rolls down the dome like thunder. Seven floors stop whispering at once, and you read in a silence you made.|You cough. Somewhere below, the proposal stops mid-sentence. You read a whole chapter before you stop wondering how it ended.|You cough on purpose now. Below, every whisper stops, and the dome is yours, and so is the chapter.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1ec9bc] = {你的咳嗽声像雷一样滚下穹顶。七层楼的耳语戛然而止，你在自己造出来的寂静里读书。|你咳了一声。楼下的求婚断在半句。你读完一整章，才不再琢磨那句话的下文。|如今你是故意咳的。楼下的耳语全都停了，穹顶归你，这一章也归你。}\n\n@event library_after_closing\n# en: After the Closing Bell\ntitle [dda602] = 闭馆钟响之后\n# en: {The bell rings to close the upper galleries, and their lamps begin to go out from the top floor down, one floor a minute. Students drift down to the reading room, which keeps its lamps for the night.|The galleries' closing bell. You've noticed the shelves up here lean together a little after hours, as if to talk.|The bell, the lamps, the drift down the stairs. You know the alcove on the fourth floor where the last lamp dies.} {Footnote is doing his rounds, tail up.|Rain is loud on the high windows.|{final_year: You wonder who will hide in your alcove next year.|Somewhere a book closes itself with a snap.}}\ntext [672efb] = {闭馆钟响了，楼上各层书廊开始熄灯，从顶楼往下，一分钟熄一层。学生们慢慢下楼去阅览室，那里的灯整夜都亮着。|书廊的闭馆钟。你发现闭馆后这上面的书架会微微靠拢，像要说悄悄话。|钟声、熄灯、下楼的人流。你知道四楼那个最后一盏灯熄灭的壁龛。} {脚注竖着尾巴在巡夜。|雨点重重敲着高窗。|{final_year: 你不禁想，明年会是谁躲进你的壁龛。|某处一本书啪地自己合上了。}}\n# en: A library at closing time, lamps going out floor by floor from the top down, a student half-hidden between two shelves with a book held to their chest.\nscene [ec5e6d] = 闭馆时分的图书馆，灯光从顶楼一层层往下熄灭，一个学生半藏在两排书架之间，把一本书抱在胸前。\n# en: Stay hidden past closing\nopt.left.label [b8c8f7] = 闭馆后躲着不走\n# en: {You read by a stolen candle until the small hours. The library is a different animal at night, and it lets you be.|The shelves lean together and whisper while you read. You catch one word in three, and none of them are kind about the history section.|You read in your alcove till the candle drowns. Walking back across the bridges at two, you feel like a burglar who stole only sentences.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e3f1c9] = {你借着偷来的蜡烛一直读到深夜。夜里的图书馆是另一种动物，而它容你留下。|书架靠拢过来，在你读书时窃窃私语。你三个词里听懂一个，没有一个是在夸历史区。|你在壁龛里读到蜡烛淹没在蜡油里。凌晨两点走过光之桥回去，你觉得自己像个只偷句子的贼。}\n# en: {A shelf shuffles sideways and nudges you, firmly, toward the stairs. A prefect on the stair watches you evicted by furniture.|The shelves close the aisle behind you like a mouth and walk you down to the reading room. The story is round the house by breakfast.|Footnote finds you, sits on your book and yowls until the porter comes. The porter tells everyone. Footnote is unrepentant.}\nopt.left.say.2 [214899] = {一排书架侧身挪过来，坚定地把你往楼梯推。楼梯上的一位级长目睹你被家具赶了出去。|书架在你身后像一张嘴一样合拢过道，把你押送到楼下的阅览室。早饭前这事就传遍了学院。|脚注找到了你，一屁股坐在你的书上，叫个不停，直到门房赶来。门房告诉了所有人。脚注毫无悔意。}\n# en: Leave with the crowd\nopt.right.label [cad4b2] = 随人群离开\n# en: {You go to bed at a decent hour, which feels almost illicit.|You leave with everyone else and sleep like a shelved book.|You walk out under the dying lamps and sleep well. The alcove can wait for you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d94182] = {你在体面的时间上床睡觉，这感觉几乎像在犯规。|你和大家一起离开，睡得像一本上了架的书。|你在渐暗的灯下走出去，睡得很好。壁龛可以等你。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_faculty_basket\n# en: The Faculty Basket\ntitle [64ecae] = 教职员吊篮\n# en: {Up the hollow middle of the Lecture Tower runs a wicker basket on a rope, for faculty knees. A sign says FACULTY ONLY. It's two hundred and twelve steps to the top.|The basket is waiting at the bottom again. Someone has added \"AND THEIR LUGGAGE\" to the sign.|The basket. You know its creak, its sway, and the exact landing where it likes to stop.} {Professor Thorne's satchel is riding up in it, alone.|It still smells faintly of Professor Vey's perfume.|{season == \"summer\": The stairwell is an oven by the fifth landing.|A draught howls up the stairwell like a chimney.}}\ntext [bcb047] = {讲堂塔中空的塔心里吊着一只柳条篮，专为教职员的膝盖准备。牌子上写着“仅限教职员”。走到塔顶要两百一十二级台阶。|吊篮又停在底下。有人在牌子上添了一句“及其行李”。|吊篮。你熟悉它的吱呀声、它的摇晃，还有它最爱停住的那个楼梯平台。} {索恩教授的书包正独自坐着它往上升。|篮子里还残留着维伊教授的香水味。|{season == \"summer\": 到第五个平台，楼梯井就成了烤炉。|一股穿堂风像烟囱一样在楼梯井里呼啸。}}\n# en: A wicker basket on a rope rising up the hollow core of a spiral tower, a sign on it reading FACULTY ONLY, a student eyeing it from the foot of the stairs.\nscene [6bee7c] = 一只柳条篮沿着绳索在螺旋塔的中空塔心里上升，篮上挂着“仅限教职员”的牌子，一个学生在楼梯脚下打量着它。\n# en: Ride it up, quietly\nopt.left.label [5f422c] = 悄悄坐上去\n# en: {You ride up beside the satchel, which seems to disapprove, and step off at the top fresh as morning.|You glide past six landings and step out at the top as if you'd walked. Your knees send their thanks.|The basket stops where it likes to stop; you know the trick of the rope now, and arrive at the top unwinded and unseen.}\nopt.left.say.1 [41a80a] = {你坐在那只书包旁边升上去，书包似乎很不以为然。你到顶时神清气爽，像清晨一样。|你飘过六个平台，在塔顶迈出篮子，仿佛是走上来的。你的膝盖向你道谢。|篮子停在它想停的地方；你如今懂得拽绳的窍门，到顶时气不喘、人不见。}\n# en: {The satchel's owner is waiting at the top. Thorne looks at you, then at the sign. \"Hm.\" You walk back down, then up.|The basket stops between floors. Professor Vey leans over the rail above. \"Faculty,\" she says, \"are generally taller.\" It's ten minutes before anyone winds you down.|It sticks at its favourite landing, and a whole lecture hall files past, one by one, to look at you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8d1514] = {书包的主人在塔顶等着。索恩看看你，又看看牌子。“嗯。”你走下去，再走上来。|篮子卡在两层之间。维伊教授从上面的栏杆探出头：“教职员，”她说，“一般都要高一些。”十分钟后才有人把你摇下去。|它卡在最爱停的平台上，整整一个讲堂的学生排着队经过，一个接一个地看你。}\n# en: Climb all two hundred steps\nopt.right.label [12a788] = 爬完两百级台阶\n# en: {You arrive at the top red-faced and righteous, legs like wet bread.|You count the steps aloud. Somewhere beside you the basket creaks past, empty, smug.|You take the steps two at a time now, and only stop to breathe at the landing where the basket stops.}\nopt.right.say.1 [774da2] = {你到顶时满脸通红、理直气壮，两腿软得像泡湿的面包。|你大声数着台阶。吊篮在你身边吱吱呀呀地升过去，空着，得意洋洋。|如今你一步跨两级，只在吊篮爱停的那个平台歇口气。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_office_hours\n# en: The Office-Hours Queue\ntitle [7191e1] = 答疑的队伍\n# en: {{prof} keeps office hours on the landing, and the queue already reaches the stairs.|Office hours. {prof}'s queue has its etiquette now: no pushing, no crying, no essays read aloud.|The office-hours queue. You know the landing's cold step and the warm one.} At the very back a first-year is holding an essay like a shield{season == \"winter\": , breath fogging|}.\ntext [bfa1be] = {{prof}在楼梯平台上答疑，队伍已经排到了楼梯口。|答疑时间。{prof}的队伍如今有了规矩：不许插队，不许哭，不许大声朗读论文。|答疑的队伍。你知道平台上哪级台阶冷、哪级暖。} 队尾有个一年级生把一篇论文举在身前，像举着盾牌{season == \"winter\": ，呵着白气|}。\n# en: A queue of students on a stone landing outside a half-open office door, one nervous first-year clutching an essay at the very back.\nscene [700a7a] = 石砌平台上，学生们在半掩的办公室门外排队，队尾一个紧张的一年级生攥着一篇论文。\n# en: Wait your turn and ask\nopt.left.label [eb3536] = 排队等着提问\n# en: {Forty minutes on the stair for four minutes of Thorne. \"Hm. Wrong, but interestingly.\" You'll take it.|Thorne reads your question, crosses out half of it, and answers the half that's left. It was the half that mattered.|Thorne lets you in, answers you in three sentences and says \"Next.\" You carry the three sentences around for a week.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dd6d4f] = {在楼梯上站四十分钟，换来索恩四分钟。“嗯。错了，但错得有意思。”你认了。|索恩读了你的问题，划掉一半，回答了剩下的一半。恰恰是要紧的那一半。|索恩让你进去，用三句话答完，说“下一个”。那三句话你揣了一个星期。}\n# en: {Vey answers your question with a question, and then another, until you answer it yourself. It takes an hour you didn't have.|Vey asks how you think you did. You lie. She smiles and tells you exactly how you did.|Vey listens, then asks what you actually came to ask. You hadn't known, until then.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7fb858] = {维伊用一个问题回答你的问题，接着又一个，直到你自己答了出来。这花掉了你本来没有的一个小时。|维伊问你觉得自己考得怎样。你撒了谎。她笑了笑，把你到底考得怎样一五一十告诉了你。|维伊听完，问你其实是来问什么的。在那之前，你自己都不知道。}\n# en: {Marisol answers with a poem about rivers, forgets the last line, and somehow the forgetting answers your question.|Marisol talks you through it while watering a fern on the sill. It takes an hour. It's a very good hour.|Marisol hears you out, then opens the window to let the weather in. \"There. Now ask me again.\"}\nopt.left.say.3 [003260] = {玛丽索尔用一首关于河流的诗作答，忘了最后一句，可那遗忘本身竟回答了你的问题。|玛丽索尔一边给窗台上的蕨类浇水，一边给你讲。讲了一个小时。非常好的一个小时。|玛丽索尔听你说完，推开窗让天气进来。“好了。现在再问我一遍。”}\n# en: {The wait is long, the answer short, and worth it.|You reach the door just before it shuts and get your answer standing on the threshold.|You get your answer, and a reading list longer than the queue.}\nopt.left.say.4 [ec0a1f] = {等得很久，答得很短，值得。|你赶在门关上前到了，站在门槛上得到了答案。|你得到了答案，外加一份比队伍还长的书单。}\n# en: Give the first-year your place\nopt.right.label [b89c4f] = 把位置让给一年级生\n# en: {The first-year goes in white and comes out pink and grateful. You never do get to ask your question.|You swap places. The first-year comes out an hour later having been told their essay \"has a pulse\". They take it as a triumph. So do you.|You give up your place again. The first-year waves the essay at you on the way out, like a flag.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8a9503] = {一年级生进去时脸色煞白，出来时红扑扑的，满怀感激。你始终没能问出你的问题。|你们换了位置。一小时后一年级生出来，说导师评价那篇论文“还有脉搏”。TA 当成了凯旋。你也是。|你又让出了位置。一年级生出来时冲你挥着论文，像挥一面旗。}\n\n@event great_hall_house_banners\n# en: The House Banners\ntitle [e49f1f] = 学院旗帜\n# en: {The five house banners over the long tables move with their houses' moods. Today Duskgate's hangs upside down, and is furious about it.|Someone has been at the banners again: Brasswick's has been embroidered with a small, elegant moustache.|Another banner prank: Rootward's has been swapped for a tablecloth with a turnip painted on it. The real one is sulking in a corner.} {The tables are empty; supper is an hour off.|A ladder leans against the wall, still warm from the culprit.|{season == \"winter\": The banners shiver in the draught from the high windows.|Emberhall's banner is smouldering slightly, in sympathy or envy.}}\ntext [82b62b] = {长桌上方的五面学院旗帜随各自学院的心情飘动。今天暮门的旗被倒挂了，气得要命。|又有人动了旗帜：铜芯的旗上被绣了一撇优雅的小胡子。|又一桩旗帜恶作剧：根庐的旗被换成了一块画着萝卜的桌布。真旗正在角落里生闷气。} {长桌空着；离晚饭还有一个钟头。|墙边靠着一架梯子，还带着肇事者的余温。|{season == \"winter\": 高窗漏进来的风吹得旗帜瑟瑟发抖。|烬厅的旗微微冒烟，不知是同情还是嫉妒。}}\n# en: Five long house banners hanging over the tables of an empty great hall, one of them conspicuously wrong.\nscene [9fb8b1] = 空荡荡的大礼堂里，五面长长的学院旗帜悬在长桌上方，其中一面明显不对劲。\n# en: Put it right before supper\nopt.left.label [04096b] = 赶在晚饭前摆正\n# en: {Twenty minutes up a wobbling ladder. Duskgate's banner, righted, gives a grudging flap. Your palms are rope-burned.|You unpick the moustache stitch by stitch from the top of the ladder. Brasswick's banner shudders with relief; your neck doesn't.|You haul the real banner up and the tablecloth down. Rootward's banner settles over its table like a hen on eggs. Your arms ache till morning.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cf7cc2] = {在晃晃悠悠的梯子上爬了二十分钟。被摆正的暮门旗不情不愿地扑扇了一下。你的手掌被绳子磨破了。|你站在梯子顶上把小胡子一针一针拆掉。铜芯的旗如释重负地抖了抖；你的脖子可没这么轻松。|你把真旗拉上去，把桌布取下来。根庐的旗像母鸡孵蛋一样稳稳罩住自家长桌。你的胳膊酸到第二天早上。}\n# en: Improve the prank\nopt.right.label [d0061e] = 把恶作剧升级\n# en: {You add a matching upside-down candle to the table below. At supper Duskgate laughs hardest, which is how you know it was them.|You give the moustache a monocle. Brasswick declares it an improvement and votes to keep it.|You paint a crown on the turnip. Rootward is outraged, then proud, then outraged again.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c954fc] = {你在下面的桌上配了一支倒插的蜡烛。晚饭时暮门笑得最响，于是你知道就是他们自己干的。|你给小胡子添了一副单片眼镜。铜芯宣布这是一项改进，投票决定保留。|你在萝卜上画了一顶王冠。根庐先是暴怒，然后自豪，然后又暴怒。}\n# en: {A prefect finds you on the ladder with an upside-down candle, and the whole prank is pinned on you by supper.|You're halfway through a monocle when Brasswick's entire table walks in. You are now the moustache person.|You're caught with paint on your hands and a crown half done. Rootward sends a strongly worded turnip to your table.}\nopt.right.say.2 [55af89] = {一位级长发现你拿着倒插的蜡烛站在梯子上，到晚饭时整桩恶作剧都算到了你头上。|单片眼镜画到一半，铜芯一整桌人走了进来。从此你就是“小胡子那位”。|你被当场抓住，手上沾着颜料，王冠才画了一半。根庐往你桌上送来一只措辞严厉的萝卜。}\n\n@event great_hall_stagehands\n# en: Stagehands Wanted\ntitle [8cdfa4] = 招募布景工\n# en: {season == \"autumn\": Scenery for the Harvest Feast is going up on the stage: a painted moon the size of a barn.|{season == \"winter\": They're building the Midwinter Ball's ice arch out of painted canvas and optimism.|{season == \"spring\": The Spring Tournament's banners are being hemmed on the stage, in five house colours.|They're painting the Starfall backdrop, a whole sky, star by star.}}} {A sign by the stage says STAGEHANDS WANTED, NO EXPERIENCE NECESSARY.|The sign now says SOME EXPERIENCE PREFERRED. Nobody is checking.|Someone hands you a brush as you come in, just in case.} Meanwhile the kitchens are testing the feast menu on anyone who sits still.\ntext [110a75] = {season == \"autumn\": 丰收宴的布景正在舞台上搭起来：一轮谷仓那么大的彩绘月亮。|{season == \"winter\": 他们正用彩绘帆布和乐观精神搭仲冬舞会的冰拱门。|{season == \"spring\": 春季锦标赛的五色学院旗正在舞台上锁边。|他们在画星坠节的背景幕，一整片天空，一颗星一颗星地画。}}} {舞台边的牌子写着“招募布景工，无需经验”。|牌子如今写着“有经验者优先”。没人查。|你一进门就有人塞给你一把刷子，以防万一。} 与此同时，厨房正拿节庆菜单给所有坐得住的人试吃。\n# en: Students on ladders painting a huge backdrop on the great hall's stage while a cook carries in a tray of tasting plates.\nscene [a412c0] = 学生们站在梯子上给大礼堂舞台上的巨幅背景幕上色，一位厨子端着一盘试吃小碟走进来。\n# en: Paint, hammer and hold ladders\nopt.left.label [7b1f2c] = 刷漆、敲钉、扶梯子\n# en: {You paint for three hours and charm the paint to shimmer at the edges. It's the best thing on the stage. Your shoulders disagree.|You hold ladders, hammer, paint, hit your thumb twice and get paint in your hair that won't come out till the festival. Someone stands back and says \"oh\", the good kind.|They give you the tricky bit, the part that has to move when the candles do, because nobody else will. It does. You get splinters, and applause.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fb7d1d] = {你画了三个小时，还给颜料施了咒，让边缘闪闪发亮。这是舞台上最好的一块。你的肩膀不同意。|你扶梯子、敲钉子、刷漆，还砸了两回拇指，头发里沾上的颜料要到节庆才洗得掉。有人退后一步，“哦”了一声，是好的那种。|他们把最难的那块交给你：烛光一动它就得跟着动的那部分，因为别人都不肯接。它动了。你扎了满手木刺，也赢得了掌声。}\n# en: Taste-test for the kitchens\nopt.right.label [0e1f68] = 替厨房试吃\n# en: {You eat seven tasting plates and give honest marks. The pear tart wins. Your revision notes stay shut.|You taste and judge and taste again. The cook writes down everything you say, and your afternoon of reading is gone.|The cook saves you the corner seat and the first spoonful. The menu is better for you; your reading pile is taller.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e16841] = {你吃了七碟试吃，诚实打分。梨挞胜出。你的复习笔记一页没翻。|你尝了评、评了尝。厨子把你说的每个字都记下来，你本打算读书的下午就这么没了。|厨子给你留了角落的座位和第一勺。菜单因你变好了，你的待读书堆却更高了。}\n\n@event infirmary_night_watch\n# en: Turning the Fever Charm\ntitle [093688] = 转动退烧咒\n# en: {A first-year in the end bed has a fever, and the cooling charm over the bed has to be turned by hand every hour until dawn. Sister Amaranth looks at you, then at the chair.|Another fever, another chair. The cooling charm's brass ring is worn smooth where hands turn it.|The chair by the end bed. Sister Amaranth has left a cushion on it, which is as close as she comes to asking.} {The ward is quiet except for the clock.|Rain is drumming on the infirmary roof.|{season == \"winter\": Frost feathers the window behind the bed.|Moths keep the lamp company.}}\ntext [d708b0] = {最里面那张床上的一年级生发着烧，床上方的降温咒环得每小时用手转一次，一直转到天亮。阿玛兰斯修女看看你，又看看那把椅子。|又一场烧，又一把椅子。降温咒的黄铜环被一双双手转得溜光。|最里面那张床边的椅子。阿玛兰斯修女在上面放了个坐垫，这已是她最接近“请求”的表示。} {病房里静得只剩钟声。|雨点密密地打在医务室屋顶上。|{season == \"winter\": 床后的窗上结满了羽毛似的霜。|几只飞蛾陪着那盏灯。}}\n# en: A dim infirmary ward at night, one lamp lit by a bed where a sleeping student lies under a slowly turning ring of pale light.\nscene [d38816] = 夜里昏暗的医务室病房，一张床边点着一盏灯，床上熟睡的学生头顶有一圈缓缓转动的淡光。\n# en: Take the night watch\nopt.left.label [e7307c] = 守这一夜\n# en: {You turn the ring every hour and watch the fever ebb by inches. At dawn Sister Amaranth says \"Keep doing that,\" and takes the chair.|You doze between turns and wake on the hour, every hour, as if the ring calls you. The patient wakes clear-eyed. You don't.|You know the charm's weight now and turn it half asleep. At dawn the patient asks your name. You're too tired to remember it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ed5f04] = {你每小时转一次咒环，看着高烧一点一点退去。天亮时阿玛兰斯修女说：“保持下去。”然后接过了椅子。|你在两次转动之间打盹，每到整点就醒，仿佛咒环在叫你。病人醒来时神志清明。你可没有。|你如今熟悉咒环的分量，半睡半醒也能转。天亮时病人问你叫什么。你累得想不起来。}\n# en: Promise next time, and sleep\nopt.right.label [4e91f2] = 答应下次，去睡觉\n# en: {Sister Amaranth turns the charm herself, without comment, and that's worse than a comment.|You go to bed and lie awake a while, counting hours you're not turning.|You leave the cushion on the chair for someone else. You sleep, mostly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [138f2b] = {阿玛兰斯修女一言不发地自己转起了咒环，这比说什么都糟。|你上床躺了好一会儿，数着那些不是你在转的钟点。|你把坐垫留在椅子上给别人。你睡着了，大致上。}\n\n@event infirmary_curious_case\n# en: A Curious Case\ntitle [f62dc6] = 奇特的病例\n# en: {A second-year sits in bed four hiccuping soap bubbles.|In bed four today: a student whose hair has turned to feathers, and keeps trying to migrate.|Bed four has a student who can only speak in rhyme, and is running out of rhymes.} {Sister Amaranth has three books open and no patience left.|Sister Amaranth glances at you. \"You. Useful or scarce. Pick.\"|{season == \"winter\": Half the ward has the sniffles, and the hiccups have gone round.|Sister Amaranth is writing a stern note to whoever did this.}}\ntext [f641a6] = {四号床上有个二年级生，一打嗝就冒肥皂泡。|今天四号床：一个学生的头发变成了羽毛，还老想往南迁徙。|四号床的学生只会用押韵的话说话，而且韵脚快用完了。} {阿玛兰斯修女翻开了三本书，耐心已经用光。|阿玛兰斯修女瞥了你一眼。“你。要么帮忙，要么走人。选。”|{season == \"winter\": 病房里一半人在流鼻涕，打嗝也传染开了。|阿玛兰斯修女正给始作俑者写一张措辞严厉的字条。}}\n# en: A student sitting up in an infirmary bed with a strange magical symptom while a stern healer frowns at a thick reference book.\nscene [7b657d] = 一个学生坐在医务室病床上，带着奇怪的魔法症状，一位严厉的治疗师对着一本厚厚的参考书皱眉。\n# en: Help look it up\nopt.left.label [fa07db] = 帮忙查资料\n# en: {You find it on page four hundred. A soap-tongue jinx, cured by mint and silence. Sister Amaranth says \"Good,\" without looking up.|You trace the feathers to a half-learned transformation and the cure to a bowl of warm salt water. The student moults gratefully.|You find the rhyming hex and the counter-word. The student's first plain sentence is \"thank you\", which is a relief to everyone.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e6faf1] = {你在第四百页找到了：皂舌咒，用薄荷和沉默治。阿玛兰斯修女头也不抬地说：“好。”|你查出羽毛来自一个没学全的变形术，解法是一碗温盐水。那学生感激地掉起了毛。|你找到了押韵咒和破解词。那学生说出的第一句不押韵的话是“谢谢”，所有人都松了口气。}\n# en: {You read for an hour and find three cures for things nobody has. Sister Amaranth solves it with a cup of tea and a look.|You recommend plucking. Everyone stares at you. Sister Amaranth sends you to fetch towels instead.|You suggest a cure that makes the rhymes worse and slightly operatic. Sister Amaranth fixes it, and you, with a glance.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8a1760] = {你读了一小时，找到三种治没人得的病的方子。阿玛兰斯修女用一杯茶和一个眼神就解决了。|你建议拔毛。所有人都盯着你。阿玛兰斯修女打发你去拿毛巾。|你提出的疗法让韵押得更狠，还带上了几分歌剧腔。阿玛兰斯修女一个眼神就把它——连同你——一起纠正了。}\n# en: Keep the patient company\nopt.right.label [39c935] = 陪病人解闷\n# en: {You see who can blow the biggest bubble. You lose, but you laugh so hard your ribs ache.|You help the student hold on to the bedframe whenever a flock goes over. Feathers get everywhere, including your lungs.|You rhyme back. You run out of rhymes together, and by then you're both crying with laughter and very sore.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ea670d] = {你们比谁吹的泡泡大。你输了，可笑得肋骨生疼。|每当一群羽毛飞过，你就帮那学生抓住床架。羽毛钻得到处都是，包括你的肺。|你也用韵脚回话。你们一起把韵用光，那时两人都笑出了眼泪，浑身酸痛。}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_crystal_sill\n# en: The Crystal on the Sill\ntitle [fc5a20] = 窗台上的晶簇\n# en: {On the windowsill sits somebody's abandoned crystal experiment, a jar of blue spikes labelled DO NOT FEED.|The crystal has grown out of its jar and halfway down the wall. The label now reads DO NOT FEED (WE MEAN IT).|The crystal reaches the floor now and has started on the next window. Someone has given it a name and a little hat.} {It chimes faintly when the door slams.|It has grown a new spike toward the warmest cauldron.|{season == \"winter\": Frost and crystal are racing each other up the glass.|In the afternoon light it throws blue all over the benches.}}\ntext [602fb0] = {窗台上摆着不知谁丢下的晶体实验：一罐蓝色尖晶，标签写着“请勿喂食”。|晶体已经长出罐子，爬到半面墙了。标签改成了“请勿喂食（说真的）”。|晶体如今已经长到地板，开始往下一扇窗进军。有人给它起了名字，还戴了顶小帽子。} {门一摔，它就轻轻作响。|它朝最暖的那口坩埚长出了一根新尖。|{season == \"winter\": 霜和晶体在玻璃上比赛往上爬。|午后的阳光透过它，把蓝光洒满了工作台。}}\n# en: A blue crystal growth spilling out of a jar on a laboratory windowsill and creeping slowly down the wall.\nscene [a619bc] = 实验室窗台上，一簇蓝色晶体从罐子里溢出来，正慢慢爬下墙面。\n# en: Feed it a pinch of salt\nopt.left.label [67b9d2] = 喂它一撮盐\n# en: {It drinks the salt with a sound like a sigh and grows one perfect new spike. You understand, suddenly, how it's built.|A new spike unfolds slowly, like a fern. You sketch the growth, and it makes sense of a whole chapter.|It grows a spike in your exact direction, which is either affection or aim. You take notes either way.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5df698] = {它叹息般地吸进那撮盐，长出一根完美的新尖。你忽然明白了它是怎么长成的。|一根新尖像蕨叶一样慢慢舒展。你把生长过程画下来，一整章内容豁然开朗。|它朝你的方向长出一根尖，要么是喜欢你，要么是瞄准你。不管哪种，你都记了笔记。}\n# en: {It shoots out a spike the size of a finger and catches your sleeve and your wrist. The label, you concede, was right.|It grows a spike straight through your notes and into your thumb. You are not the first; there's a list.|It sneezes crystal dust all over you. You sparkle blue until bedtime and itch till morning.}\nopt.left.say.2 [04c0e9] = {它猛地刺出一根手指长的尖，扎住了你的袖子和手腕。你承认，标签说得没错。|它长出一根尖，穿透你的笔记扎进了你的拇指。你不是第一个；有张名单。|它朝你打了个喷嚏，喷了你一身晶粉。你一直闪着蓝光到睡觉，痒到第二天早上。}\n# en: Snap off a spike for Pell's\nopt.right.label [094017] = 掰一根卖给佩尔\n# en: {Pell & Daughters pays well for clean crystal, no questions asked. The jar seems to notice the gap.|You snap off a spike while it's busy growing toward the cauldron. It chimes once, sharply, as if taking your name. Pell's pays anyway.|You break off a piece from the bottom where nobody will notice and sell it in the village. The little hat tilts at you when you come back.}\nopt.right.say.1 [da7bee] = {佩尔父女药铺收干净的晶体出价不错，不问来路。罐子似乎注意到了那个缺口。|你趁它朝坩埚长的时候掰下一根尖。它尖锐地响了一声，像是记下了你的名字。佩尔家照样付了钱。|你从底部没人注意的地方掰下一块，拿到镇上卖了。你回来时，那顶小帽子朝你歪了歪。}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_good_scales\n# en: The Good Scales\ntitle [1a9080] = 那台好秤\n# en: {The laboratory has twelve sets of scales and one that's accurate. The queue for it reaches the door.|The queue for the good scales has rules now, chalked on the bench: one weighing each, no hovering, no charms.|The good scales. You can tell from the door how long the wait is by how many people are sighing.} {A third-year at the front is weighing powdered moonstone grain by grain.|The other scales are off by a hair, a whisker or a small mouse.|{season == \"summer\": It's hot, and every powder in the room is clumping.|The windows have steamed over and nobody can read their numbers.}}\ntext [9f28b0] = {实验室有十二台秤，只有一台是准的。等它的队伍排到了门口。|那台好秤的队伍如今有了规矩，用粉笔写在工作台上：每人称一次，不许围观，不许施咒。|那台好秤。你在门口听叹气声有多少，就知道要等多久。} {队首一个三年级生正一粒一粒地称月长石粉。|其他秤的误差有一根头发、一根胡须，或一只小老鼠那么大。|{season == \"summer\": 天热，屋里每种粉末都结了块。|窗户全起了雾，谁也看不清读数。}}\n# en: A queue of students with jars and papers waiting at a single fine brass balance in a cluttered laboratory, while battered scales sit unused along the bench.\nscene [594ce4] = 杂乱的实验室里，学生们拿着罐子和纸排队等一台精致的黄铜天平，工作台上其他破旧的秤无人问津。\n# en: Charm your way to the front\nopt.left.label [1a2ccd] = 靠嘴皮子插到前面\n# en: {You admire the third-year's technique until they wave you ahead out of sheer flattered confusion.|You quote the chalked rules back at the queue in such a reasonable voice that they let you through to weigh \"just one thing\".|You've learned the queue's weak points. A compliment, a promise and a borrowed spatula, and you're at the scales in a minute.}\nopt.left.say.1 [760c9c] = {你夸那个三年级生手法好，夸到对方又受用又糊涂，挥手让你先称。|你用极其通情达理的语气把粉笔写的规矩念给队伍听，他们竟放你过去称“就一样东西”。|你摸清了这支队伍的软肋。一句恭维、一个承诺、一把借来的药勺，一分钟你就站到了秤前。}\n# en: {The queue turns as one and points at the back. You go to the back.|Someone reads out rule two, NO HOVERING, and looks at you while they do it. The whole queue laughs.|The queue closes ranks like a wall of elbows. You spend the wait at the back, flushed.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3d72d7] = {整支队伍齐刷刷转过身，指向队尾。你去了队尾。|有人念出第二条“不许围观”，念的时候看着你。全队都笑了。|队伍像一堵胳膊肘砌成的墙一样合拢。你在队尾等完了全程，满脸通红。}\n# en: Use the bad scales and correct\nopt.right.label [940954] = 用烂秤再校正\n# en: {You weigh everything twice on two bad scales and average the errors. Your measurements are right; your head hurts.|You work out exactly how wrong the mouse-scale is and correct for it. It's tedious, and the best arithmetic you've done all week.|You've mapped all eleven bad scales' errors on a card in your pocket. It doesn't make the work less dull.}\nopt.right.say.1 [05103c] = {你在两台烂秤上各称两遍，把误差取平均。数据是对的；你的头疼了。|你算出那台“老鼠秤”到底偏了多少，再做修正。很枯燥，却是你这周做过最好的算术。|你把十一台烂秤的误差都记在口袋里的一张卡片上了。这并没让活儿变得不无聊。}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_mind_my_cauldron\n# en: Mind My Cauldron?\ntitle [39a03a] = 帮我看着坩埚？\n# en: {{mate} has a six-hour brew going and a supper {they} can't miss. \"Stir it widdershins when the sand runs out. Twice. I owe you.\"|{mate} spots you across the lab and is already holding out the spoon. \"Hourglass. Twice. Widdershins. You're a lifesaver.\"|{mate} hands you the spoon with the air of someone who knows you'll say yes. The hourglass is half run.} {The brew smells of oranges and thunder.|The brew is the colour of a bruise, and humming.|{season == \"winter\": The lab windows are black and the cauldrons the only warmth.|Somebody's cauldron at the far end goes off like a cork.}}\ntext [54ad4a] = {{mate}有一锅要熬六小时的药，还有一顿非去不可的晚饭。“沙漏流完就逆时针搅两下。两下。我欠你的。”|{mate}隔着实验室看见你，已经把勺子递过来了。“沙漏。两下。逆时针。你是我的救星。”|{mate}把勺子递给你，一副知道你会答应的样子。沙漏已经流了一半。} {药闻起来像橘子和雷雨。|药是瘀青的颜色，还在嗡嗡响。|{season == \"winter\": 实验室的窗外一片漆黑，只有坩埚是暖的。|远处有人的坩埚像拔塞子一样砰地炸了。}}\n# en: A student hurrying out of an evening laboratory, pointing back at a bubbling cauldron and an hourglass, while another student is left holding the spoon.\nscene [6fc9df] = 一个学生匆匆走出傍晚的实验室，回头指着一口冒泡的坩埚和一个沙漏，另一个学生被留下来握着勺子。\n# en: Stay and mind it\nopt.left.label [e68e54] = 留下来看着\n# en: {You miss supper and stir twice, exactly. {mate} comes back to a perfect brew, and you to a rumbling stomach.|You mind it, and pick up three of {mate}'s tricks just by watching the sand. Your own supper is cold bread.|The brew behaves for you. {mate} comes back, sniffs, and says your wrist is better than {theirs}. Your stomach disagrees with the praise.}\nopt.left.say.1 [af872b] = {你错过了晚饭，不多不少搅了两下。{mate}回来时药熬得完美，你的肚子咕咕直叫。|你看着它，光是盯着沙漏就学会了{mate}的三个窍门。你自己的晚饭是冷面包。|药在你手里很听话。{mate}回来闻了闻，说你的手腕比TA的还稳。你的肚子对这番夸奖不以为然。}\n# en: Sorry, I've plans\nopt.right.label [8918b3] = 抱歉，我有安排\n# en: {You go to supper. {mate} goes to supper late, and the brew turns slightly orange in revenge.|You make your excuses. {mate} finds someone else, and mentions it, twice.|You say no this time. {mate} looks at the spoon, then at you, and sighs the sigh of the betrayed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fe3b89] = {你去吃晚饭了。{mate}晚饭去晚了，药为了报复微微泛了橙。|你找了借口。{mate}另找了人，还提了两次。|这次你拒绝了。{mate}看看勺子，又看看你，叹出一口被辜负者的长气。}\n\n@event workshop_after_the_bang\n# en: After the Bang\ntitle [2e3e78] = 爆炸之后\n# en: {You hear the bang from the corridor. Inside, the smoke is thinning, and Mir waves through it, eyebrows mostly intact. \"That was the good kind of bang! Probably!\"|Another bang. Mir has chalked a tally by the door: GOOD BANGS and BAD BANGS. Today's goes in a third column, MUSICAL.|You don't even flinch at the bang now. Mir hands you a broom before the smoke clears.} {Gears are still rolling across the floor.|A small cog is spinning on its edge, refusing to fall.|{season == \"winter\": The blast has blown the window open and snow is coming in.|The fire bucket by the door is already empty.}}\ntext [e5761c] = {你在走廊上就听见了那声巨响。屋里烟雾渐散，米尔隔着烟挥手，眉毛大体还在。“那是好的那种爆炸！大概！”|又一声巨响。米尔在门边画了个计分表：“好爆炸”和“坏爆炸”。今天这声记在第三栏：“有旋律的”。|如今你听见巨响连眼都不眨。烟还没散，米尔就递给你一把扫帚。} {齿轮还在满地乱滚。|一个小齿轮立着边打转，就是不倒。|{season == \"winter\": 爆炸把窗户震开了，雪正往里飘。|门边的消防水桶已经空了。}}\n# en: A workshop wreathed in drifting smoke, a soot-blackened inventor waving cheerfully through it, springs and gears scattered across the floor.\nscene [2bae78] = 烟雾缭绕的工坊里，一个被熏黑的发明家隔着烟雾愉快地挥手，弹簧和齿轮散落一地。\n# en: Help sweep up and salvage\nopt.left.label [091e3f] = 帮忙清扫回收\n# en: {You sort springs from gears from bits of the ceiling. A spring bites your finger; Mir says that one's always been like that.|You salvage a box of parts and a burnt sleeve. Mir promises to name the next bang after you.|You sweep like a professional now, and pocket a cog that wouldn't fall. Mir pretends not to see. Your fingers are cut in two places.}\nopt.left.say.1 [997f7f] = {你把弹簧、齿轮和天花板碎块分门别类。一根弹簧咬了你的手指；米尔说那根一直这样。|你回收了一箱零件，烧焦了一只袖子。米尔保证下一次爆炸用你的名字命名。|你扫得像个行家，还顺手揣走了那个不肯倒的齿轮。米尔装作没看见。你的手指划破了两处。}\n# en: Read the singed blueprint\nopt.right.label [63e633] = 研究烧焦的图纸\n# en: {You find the mistake, a rune drawn backwards, and tell Mir, who is delighted, then mortified, then delighted. \"Probably that's what it was!\"|You study the scorched blueprint while Mir sweeps around you, and learn more from the burn marks than from the lines.|You can read Mir's blueprints now, even singed. You show them where today's bang came from. They want to do it again, on purpose.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8564ce] = {你找到了错误：一个符文画反了。你告诉米尔，TA先是大喜，然后羞愧，然后又大喜。“大概就是它！”|米尔在你身边扫地，你研究烧焦的图纸，从焦痕里学到的比从线条里还多。|如今就算烧焦了，你也看得懂米尔的图纸。你指给 TA 看今天那声巨响是从哪儿来的。TA 想故意再来一次。}\n\n@event ledger_office_crossword\n# en: The Crossword Is Late\ntitle [fda6e0] = 填字游戏迟了\n# en: {The Ledger's crossword setter has vanished before the deadline, leaving half a grid pinned over the desk and a pencil on a string.|The crossword is late again. The setter's note says \"back soon\", and has said it since Tuesday.|The crossword grid again, half done. The pencil on its string swings toward you as if it knows.} {Seven across is blank. Nine down is a rude word, crossed out.|A furious letter about last week's puzzle is pinned beside it, in a professor's hand.|{season == \"winter\": The ink in the pot has a skin of ice.|The press is warming up, and so is everyone's temper.}}\ntext [376585] = {《流明纪事报》的填字出题人在截稿前人间蒸发，桌上方钉着半张格子，旁边一支用绳子拴着的铅笔。|填字游戏又迟了。出题人的字条写着“马上回来”，从星期二写到现在。|又是那半张填字格。绳子上的铅笔朝你荡过来，仿佛心里有数。} {横七空着。竖九是个粗话，被划掉了。|旁边钉着一封对上周谜题大发雷霆的信，是某位教授的笔迹。|{season == \"winter\": 墨水瓶里的墨结了一层冰皮。|印刷机正在预热，所有人的脾气也是。}}\n# en: A half-finished crossword grid pinned above a cluttered desk in a newspaper office, a pencil dangling from a string beside it.\nscene [b9e191] = 报社编辑部杂乱的桌子上方钉着一张填了一半的填字格，旁边一支铅笔用绳子吊着。\n# en: Finish setting it, properly\nopt.left.label [6d3af6] = 规规矩矩出完题\n# en: {You fill the grid with honest clues and one fiendish one. Your eyes ache; it goes to print with your pencil marks still on it.|You set it so carefully that the professor who complained will have nothing to complain about. It takes all evening.|You set the whole grid by lamplight. Nine down is no longer rude. You're a little sorry.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dfca65] = {你用老实的线索填满格子，外加一道刁钻的。你眼睛发酸；它付印时还留着你的铅笔印。|你出得如此仔细，那位抱怨的教授这回将无话可说。这花掉了一整晚。|你在灯下出完了整张格子。竖九不再是粗话了。你有点遗憾。}\n# en: Hide a message in the clues\nopt.right.label [c88709] = 在线索里藏句话\n# en: {The first letters of the across clues spell SOUP IS A CRIME. By lunch the whole refectory has solved it, and your name is going round with it.|You hide a riddle in the down clues. Half the castle spends a week on it, the professor writes in again, delighted, and somebody tells him who set it.|Your hidden message is the talk of breakfast, and the Ledger lets slip who set it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f9c114] = {横向线索的首字连起来是“汤是一种罪”。到午饭时整个膳堂都解出来了，你的名字也跟着传开。|你在竖向线索里藏了个谜语。半个城堡花了一周去猜，那位教授又来信了，这回很高兴，还有人告诉了他出题的是谁。|你藏的话成了早餐桌上的话题，纪事报也漏出了出题人是谁。}\n# en: {Nessa reads the proof, finds SOUP IS A CRIME in the first letters and circles it in red, then circles you.|The message comes out garbled: the clues spell SOUP IS A CRIMP. Nobody understands it. Somehow that's worse.|Nessa spots it at the drying line and pulls the proof. \"Clever,\" she says. \"Not printed. Next.\"}\nopt.right.say.2 [d4a5ca] = {内莎读校样时发现了首字里的“汤是一种罪”，用红笔圈了出来，然后把你也圈了进去。|藏的话印出来是乱的：线索拼成了“汤是一种醉”。没人看得懂。不知怎的，这更糟。|内莎在晾稿绳边发现了它，把校样撤了下来。“聪明，”她说，“不登。下一个。”}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_window_boxes\n# en: The Window Boxes\ntitle [4ccf5d] = 窗台花箱\n# en: {\"Would you see to the window boxes while I finish this?\" {host} says, without looking up. The boxes run along the outside of the tower window, high over the lake.|The window boxes again. {host} has left the watering can on the sill, which counts as an invitation.|You reach for the watering can before {host} asks. She smiles at her letter.} {The far box is just out of reach without leaning.|One plant in the far box has opened a single silver flower.|{season == \"winter\": Frost has nipped the herbs; they need covering.|Bees from somewhere have found the boxes, this high up.}}\ntext [99eb51] = {“我写完这封信之前，你能照看一下窗台花箱吗？”{host}头也不抬地说。花箱沿着塔窗外侧排开，高悬在湖面之上。|又是窗台花箱。{host}把洒水壶留在了窗台上，这就算邀请了。|不等{host}开口，你就伸手去拿洒水壶。她对着信纸微微一笑。} {最远的那个花箱不探身就够不着。|远处花箱里有一株开出了一朵银色的花。|{season == \"winter\": 霜冻伤了香草；得给它们盖上。|不知从哪来的蜜蜂，竟找到了这么高的花箱。}}\n# en: A high tower window open over a dizzying drop to the lake, a row of window boxes of night-flowering herbs along the sill, a calm woman writing at a desk behind.\nscene [7f1ed7] = 高塔的窗户敞开在通往湖面的令人目眩的高处，窗台外一排种着夜间开花香草的花箱，一位沉静的女士在身后的书桌前写信。\n# en: Lean out to the far box\nopt.left.label [3fffe1] = 探身照看最远的\n# en: {You lean out over the drop with one hand on the frame and tend the far box. When you pull back in, {host} is watching. \"Steady,\" she says. It's praise.|You reach the far box and the silver flower opens a second bloom as you water it. You stay leaning out a moment longer than you need to, for the view.|You know the reach now: knee on the sill, hand on the frame. The far box is tended in a minute, and the lake glitters far below.}\nopt.left.say.1 [53422d] = {你一手扶着窗框，探身到深渊上方照看最远的花箱。缩回来时，{host}正看着你。“稳。”她说。这是夸奖。|你够到了最远的花箱，你浇水时那朵银花又开了一朵。你多探了一会儿，为了看风景。|你如今熟悉怎么够了：膝盖抵着窗台，手扶窗框。一分钟就照看完最远的花箱，湖面在远远的下方闪闪发光。}\n# en: {Your foot slips on the sill and the watering can drops a very long way. {host} doesn't look up. \"There's another in the cupboard,\" she says. Your shin is skinned.|You bang your head on the frame coming back in. {host}'s quill pauses, then goes on. The silver flower doesn't get its water.|You reach, wobble, and decide against it with your heart pounding and an elbow scraped. {host} tends the far box herself afterwards, without comment.}\nopt.left.say.2 [1c2d38] = {你的脚在窗台上一滑，洒水壶掉了很长很长一段距离。{host}没抬头。“柜子里还有一个。”她说。你的小腿擦破了。|你缩回来时头撞上了窗框。{host}的羽毛笔顿了一下，又继续写。那朵银花没浇上水。|你伸手、晃了一下，心怦怦直跳，手肘也擦破了，决定还是算了。事后{host}自己照看了最远的花箱，什么也没说。}\n# en: Tend the near boxes and talk\nopt.right.label [4087b3] = 照看近处，聊聊天\n# en: {You water the near herbs while {host} tells you their names, moonmint, star-sorrel, a thyme that only opens after dark. She knows each one's story.|{host} asks what you're reading this term and listens to the answer as she writes. You leave having said more than you meant to, and learned the names of six plants.|You tend the near boxes while {host} reads you a line from her letter and asks what you'd reply. You think about it on the stairs all the way down.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c364f0] = {你给近处的香草浇水，{host}告诉你它们的名字：月薄荷、星酢浆草、一种只在天黑后开放的百里香。每一株的来历她都知道。|{host}一边写一边问你这学期在读什么，还认真听你回答。你离开时说的比你打算的多，还记住了六种植物的名字。|你照看近处的花箱，{host}给你念了信里的一句话，问你会怎么回。你在下楼的一路上都在想这件事。}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_waiting_bench\n# en: The Waiting Bench\ntitle [4dcd6d] = 候见长凳\n# en: {On the bench at the foot of the stair sits a first-year with a summons in one hand and doom on their face. \"What's she like?\" they whisper.|Another summoned first-year on the bench, another whisper: \"Is it true she can read minds?\"|The waiting bench has a first-year on it again; you suspect it always will. \"Have you been in? What happens?\"} {The stone owl watches you both.|Somewhere above, a door opens and closes.|{final_year: By next year you'll be gone, and the bench will still have someone on it.|The summons is smudged from being held so hard.}}\ntext [986040] = {楼梯脚下的长凳上坐着一个一年级生，一手捏着传唤单，脸上写满了末日。“她是什么样的人？”TA 小声问。|长凳上又一个被传唤的一年级生，又一句耳语：“听说她能读心，是真的吗？”|候见长凳上又坐着一个一年级生；你怀疑永远都会有。“你进去过吗？会发生什么？”} {石猫头鹰注视着你们俩。|楼上某处，一扇门开了又关。|{final_year: 到明年你就走了，这张长凳上还会坐着人。|传唤单被攥得字迹都糊了。}}\n# en: A narrow stone bench at the foot of a spiral stair, a pale first-year sitting on it clutching a summons, a stone owl watching from above.\nscene [68e729] = 螺旋楼梯脚下一条窄窄的石凳，一个脸色苍白的一年级生坐在上面攥着传唤单，一只石猫头鹰在上方看着。\n# en: Tell them the kind truth\nopt.left.label [4c08e6] = 好心地说实话\n# en: {\"She'll listen,\" you say, \"and it will be worse than shouting, and then it will be better.\" The first-year breathes for the first time in an hour.|\"She can't read minds,\" you say. \"She just doesn't need to.\" The first-year laughs, shakily, and goes up a little taller.|\"She'll pour you tea,\" you say. \"Drink it. Tell her the truth.\" The first-year nods and goes up the stair like someone visiting a friend.}\nopt.left.say.1 [eeb0bd] = {“她会听你说，”你说，“那比骂你还难受，然后就会好起来。”那个一年级生一小时来第一次喘了口气。|“她不会读心，”你说，“她只是用不着。”一年级生颤巍巍地笑了，挺直了一点走上楼去。|“她会给你倒茶，”你说，“喝了它。跟她说实话。”一年级生点点头，走上楼梯，像去拜访朋友。}\n# en: Tell them a horror story\nopt.right.label [184b74] = 讲个恐怖故事\n# en: {You describe the trapdoor, the pit and the crocodiles in loving detail. The first-year goes up grey. You feel wonderful, then less wonderful.|\"Only on Thursdays,\" you say gravely. It's Thursday. The first-year's face is a work of art.|You tell them about the owl that eats liars. The owl, above you, opens one eye and looks at you for a long time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cdebc2] = {你绘声绘色地描述了活板门、深坑和鳄鱼。一年级生面如死灰地上了楼。你感觉好极了，然后没那么好了。|“只在星期四。”你严肃地说。今天是星期四。一年级生的表情堪称艺术品。|你给 TA 讲那只专吃说谎者的猫头鹰。你头顶的猫头鹰睁开一只眼，盯着你看了很久。}\n\n@event seventh_tower_candle_landing\n# en: Other People's Candles\ntitle [17e4e4] = 别人的蜡烛\n# en: {Halfway up the seventh tower is a landing crowded with candle stubs, a century of them, with initials scratched in the wall above. Whoever climbed here, climbed with a light.|The candle landing. You recognise some of the initials now, the way you recognise faces in a corridor.|Back at the landing of candles. You've found your own favourite set of initials, deep and careful, and wonder who cut them.} {One stub is still faintly warm.|{season == \"winter\": The wax is cold as stone.|A draught from nowhere leans every wick the same way.}|One stub has been lit and blown out since your last climb.}\ntext [720f78] = {第七塔半腰有个平台，挤满了蜡烛头，一个世纪攒下来的，上方墙上刻着名字的缩写。来过这里的人，都是举着灯上来的。|蜡烛平台。如今有些缩写你认得了，就像认得走廊里的面孔。|又回到蜡烛平台。你找到了自己最喜欢的一组缩写，刻得又深又仔细，不禁好奇是谁刻的。} {有一截蜡烛头还微微温热。|{season == \"winter\": 蜡冷得像石头。|一股不知从哪来的风把每根烛芯都吹向同一边。}|自你上次来过，有一截蜡烛头被点燃又吹灭了。}\n# en: A narrow stone landing crowded with old candle stubs of every colour, initials scratched into the wall above them, one stub still faintly warm.\nscene [f39440] = 狭窄的石砌平台上挤满了各色旧蜡烛头，上方墙上刻着名字缩写，其中一截还微微温热。\n# en: Leave a candle of your own\nopt.left.label [193834] = 留下一支自己的蜡烛\n# en: {You light the candle you bought in the village and set it among the others. For a moment the landing is bright, and the initials look almost cheerful.|You light a new candle and sit with it while it burns down. It feels like adding your name to a very long, very quiet register.|You leave a fresh candle beside the careful initials. When you look back from the stair, its flame is leaning toward them.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cfae91] = {你点燃在镇上买的蜡烛，放在其他蜡烛中间。有那么一会儿，平台亮堂起来，那些缩写看上去几乎是欢快的。|你点一支新蜡烛，陪它烧完。就像在一本很长、很安静的名册上添了你的名字。|你在那组仔细刻下的缩写旁留下一支新蜡烛。你在楼梯上回头看时，火苗正朝它们倾斜。}\n# en: Read the initials by lamplight\nopt.right.label [2a3be6] = 借灯读那些缩写\n# en: {You copy down the initials and the dates. Most of them are a century old or more; one is from last year. Nobody you ask admits to it.|You find two sets of initials cut in the same hand, forty years apart. You leave without deciding what that means.|You read the wall until your lamp gutters. All those people, climbing up here with a light, alone. It sits with you all evening.}\nopt.right.say.1 [313a4f] = {你抄下缩写和日期。大多有一个世纪甚至更久了；有一组是去年的。你问过的人都不承认。|你找到两组同一笔迹刻的缩写，相隔四十年。你没想清楚那意味着什么就离开了。|你读着墙，直到灯火摇曳将熄。那么多人，独自举着一盏灯爬上来。这念头陪了你一整晚。}\n\n@event seventh_tower_wind_song\n# en: The Wind in the Slits\ntitle [6b8a81] = 箭孔里的风\n# en: {The wind plays the seventh tower's arrow-slits like a flute, a different note at every window.|The tower is playing again. You'd swear the tune has more notes than there are windows.|The wind's song. You can hum the first part now; nobody else in the castle knows it.} {Today it sounds almost like a lullaby.|Today it is quick and fretful, as if late for something.|{season == \"winter\": It plays low and slow, like something under snow.|It plays in fits, between gusts.}}\ntext [2ef009] = {风把第七塔的箭孔当长笛吹，每扇窗一个音。|塔又在吹奏了。你敢发誓，这曲子的音比窗户还多。|风之歌。如今你能哼出前半段了；城堡里没别人会。} {今天它听起来几乎像一首摇篮曲。|今天它急促不安，像是要迟到了。|{season == \"winter\": 它吹得又低又慢，像雪下的什么东西。|它在阵风之间断断续续地吹。}}\n# en: Thin arrow-slit windows in the curved wall of a dusty tower stair, wind whistling through them, a student with one hand pressed to the cold stone.\nscene [aca41f] = 尘封的塔楼楼梯弧墙上开着细长的箭孔，风从中呼啸而过，一个学生用一只手按着冰冷的石头。\n# en: Hum along and listen\nopt.left.label [9b8c64] = 跟着哼，静静听\n# en: {You hum back. The tower answers in harmony, and you feel it drawing your magic out through the tune, thin and bright. You come away drained and happy, as after a long song.|You hum, and one window falls silent as if to listen. The tune pulls at your magic like a tide; it costs you something to keep going. You keep going.|You hum the part you know and the tower plays the rest, on your magic as much as the wind. When it's done your fingertips are cold and empty, and you're smiling.}\nopt.left.say.1 [90d609] = {你哼了回去。塔以和声作答，你感到它顺着曲调把你的魔力抽了出去，细而明亮。你离开时疲惫又快乐，像唱完一首长歌。|你一哼，有一扇窗静了下来，像在倾听。曲调像潮水一样拉扯你的魔力；继续哼要花你些什么。你继续哼。|你哼你会的那段，塔吹完剩下的，用的既是风，也是你的魔力。结束时你的指尖又冷又空，脸上却在笑。}\n# en: Write the tune down\nopt.right.label [469fb9] = 把曲子记下来\n# en: {You mark each window's note in your notebook. It's a scale nobody uses any more; you find it later in a very old songbook.|You write the notes down and get a different tune each time you read them back. That seems important, and you can't think why.|Your page of notes is filling up. The tune has the same shape as the stair, you realise; you climb it, more or less.}\nopt.right.say.1 [73a6c8] = {你把每扇窗的音都记进笔记本。这是一种如今没人用的音阶；后来你在一本很老的歌集里找到了它。|你把音符记下来，每次读回去都是不同的曲子。这似乎很重要，你却想不出为什么。|你那页音符快写满了。你发现这曲子和楼梯一个形状；你大致是在爬它。}\n\n@event seventh_tower_outside_ledge\n# en: The Desks on the Stair\ntitle [6605dd] = 楼梯上的课桌\n# en: {Two turns below the top, the stair is choked with old classroom desks, stacked legs-up to the ceiling, the way somebody left them a century ago. There's a gap, just.|The desks on the stair have shifted again; the gap is somewhere else this time.|You know the heap now: the desk with the cracked lid, the one that wobbles, the one you never lean on.} {A draught whistles through the legs.|{season == \"winter\": Frost has furred the old varnish.|A mouse watches you from an inkwell.}|Your candle finds a carved date, and loses it.}\ntext [9cd22e] = {离塔顶还有两个拐角，楼梯被一堆旧课桌堵住了，桌腿朝上一直摞到天花板，就像一百年前有人随手留下的样子。中间有道缝，勉强够过。|楼梯上的课桌又挪过了；这回缝隙换了地方。|如今你熟悉这堆课桌了：盖子裂了的那张，晃悠的那张，你从来不靠的那张。} {一股穿堂风从桌腿间呼啸而过。|{season == \"winter\": 霜给旧清漆蒙上了一层白毛。|一只老鼠从墨水池里看着你。}|你的蜡烛照见一个刻着的日期，又照丢了。}\n# en: A narrow tower stair choked with a heap of old wooden classroom desks stacked legs-up, a student with a candle looking for a way through.\nscene [dd21da] = 一道狭窄的塔楼楼梯，被一堆桌腿朝上摞着的旧木课桌堵住，一个学生举着蜡烛找路过去。\n# en: Shift the desks and clear the way\nopt.left.label [8ac9ce] = 搬开课桌，清出路来\n# en: {You haul the desks off the heap one by one and stand them against the wall, legs down, properly. When you're done the stair is a stair again, and your arms are lead.|You lift, slide and wedge until the gap is wide enough to walk through upright. Someone has been at the heap since; you put it right again.|Cracked lid, wobbler, the one you never lean on: in that order. You're through in ten minutes and up at the top with the dust and the moon.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4b3d21] = {你把课桌一张张从堆上搬下来，靠墙放好，桌腿朝下，规规矩矩。等你弄完，楼梯又是楼梯了，你的胳膊却沉得像铅。|你抬、推、塞，直到缝隙宽得能直着身子走过去。之后又有人动过这堆桌子；你又把它摆正了。|裂盖的、晃悠的、你从来不靠的：按这个顺序。十分钟你就过去了，和灰尘与月亮一起到了塔顶。}\n# en: {The heap shifts the wrong way and a desk comes down the stair after you. You get out of its way, mostly. Your shin does not.|You pull the wrong desk and the whole heap groans. You back down the stair with a splinter the size of a pen in your palm.|The wobbler wobbles. You catch it with your shoulder, hard, and decide the top of the tower can wait.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7fc85e] = {那堆桌子往错的方向一歪，一张课桌顺着楼梯追着你滑下来。你大致躲开了。你的小腿没躲开。|你抽错了一张，整堆桌子呻吟起来。你退下楼梯，掌心扎了一根钢笔那么粗的木刺。|晃悠的那张晃了。你用肩膀狠狠接住它，决定塔顶改天再去。}\n# en: Look inside the desks\nopt.right.label [d4279f] = 看看课桌里面\n# en: {You lift one cracked lid. Inside are a quill, a ruler and a page of conjugations copied out a hundred years ago, stopping halfway down. You finish it, for them, and feel oddly sad.|Another lid, another desk: a note passed in class, \"meet me by the lake\", never answered. Underneath it is the lesson, and you read that instead.|You've been through half the desks now. Every one stops in the middle of something, as if the bell rang and nobody came back.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8b9cb9] = {你掀开一张裂了的桌盖。里面有一支羽毛笔、一把尺子，还有一页一百年前抄的动词变位，抄到一半就停了。你替那人把它抄完，心里莫名地难过。|又一张桌盖，又一张课桌：一张课上传的纸条，“湖边见”，从没有回音。纸条底下是那堂课的内容，你读了那个。|你已经翻过一半的课桌了。每一张都停在某件事的中间，好像下课钟响了，就再没人回来。}\n\n@event thesis_lab_tea_urn\n# en: The Tea Urn\ntitle [6408c5] = 茶炉\n# en: {The thesis lab runs on the tea urn, a dented copper monster that has outlived forty years of final-years. Round it, people confess things.|At the urn today someone is crying quietly into their mug about chapter three.|The urn. Someone has taped a list to it: STAGES OF THESIS GRIEF. Everyone has initialled the stage they're at.} {The urn is making the noise that means it's about to boil over.|Someone has hidden a tin of biscuits behind it, labelled NOT BISCUITS.|{season == \"spring\": The windows are open, and blossom keeps landing in the mugs.|The lamps are lit already, though it's barely evening.}}\ntext [7c0eae] = {论文实验室靠茶炉运转：一只坑坑洼洼的铜怪物，熬走了四十届毕业生。人们围着它坦白心事。|今天茶炉边有人对着杯子小声哭第三章。|茶炉。有人在上面贴了张单子：“论文悲伤五阶段”。每个人都在自己所处的阶段签了缩写。} {茶炉正发出那种快要沸出来的声音。|有人在它后面藏了一罐饼干，标签写着“不是饼干”。|{season == \"spring\": 窗开着，花瓣不停落进杯子里。|天才刚傍晚，灯就已经点上了。}}\n# en: A great dented copper tea urn on a trolley at the end of a row of study carrels, final-year students clustered round it holding mugs and complaints.\nscene [50c5e3] = 一排自习隔间尽头的推车上放着一只坑坑洼洼的大铜茶炉，毕业班学生端着杯子、带着牢骚围在旁边。\n# en: Stay and listen\nopt.left.label [465e74] = 留下来听听\n# en: {You hear about an advisor who writes only in question marks, a source that turned out to be a forgery and a cat that ate a bibliography. You say almost nothing, and everyone thanks you.|You sit with the one crying about chapter three until they're laughing about chapter three.|You find your stage on the list and initial it. Then you listen to everyone else's, which is better than any advice.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cf25d2] = {你听说了一位只用问号写批注的导师、一份原来是伪造的史料，和一只吃掉参考书目的猫。你几乎没说话，大家却都谢谢你。|你陪那个为第三章哭的人坐着，直到 TA 为第三章笑起来。|你在单子上找到自己的阶段，签了名。然后听别人讲他们的，这比任何建议都管用。}\n# en: Fill your mug and get back to work\nopt.right.label [15de02] = 倒满杯子回去干活\n# en: {You fill your mug and walk away from the confessions. Your carrel is quiet and your notes go well, and you feel slightly like a deserter.|You pat the crying student's shoulder on your way past and go back to work. It's the best you can do today.|You take your tea back to your carrel. Behind you, the list of stages grows another set of initials.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ee6663] = {你倒满杯子，离开了那些坦白。你的隔间很安静，笔记写得很顺，你却有点像个逃兵。|你经过时拍拍那个哭泣的学生的肩，回去干活。今天你只能做到这样。|你端着茶回到隔间。在你身后，那张阶段单上又多了一组缩写。}\n\n@event thesis_lab_old_theses\n# en: The Shelf of Old Theses\ntitle [818258] = 旧论文书架\n# en: {Along the back wall run three centuries of finished theses, bound in cloth that has faded to the same brown.|The thesis shelf. You've started a list of the best titles. Today's find: a study of the emotional lives of doorknobs.|The old theses. You can find the good ones by feel now: the spines are worn where people keep taking them down.} {One near the end has a pressed flower for a bookmark.|One has a note on the flyleaf: \"Passed. Barely. Worth it.\"|{season == \"spring\": Blossom drifts in through the open window and settles on the spines.|A spider has made its home between two famous ones.}}\ntext [b228d3] = {后墙上排着三个世纪的论文，布面封皮都褪成了一样的棕色。|论文书架。你开始收集最好的标题了。今天的发现：一篇关于门把手情感生活的研究。|旧论文。如今你凭手感就能找到好的：书脊上被人反复取下的地方都磨旧了。} {靠近末尾的一本夹着一朵压花当书签。|有一本的扉页上写着：“过了。勉强。值得。”|{season == \"spring\": 花瓣从开着的窗户飘进来，落在书脊上。|一只蜘蛛在两本名作之间安了家。}}\n# en: A long shelf of bound theses in faded cloth, a student pulling one out and blowing dust off a spine with a ridiculous title.\nscene [cd4c63] = 一长排褪色布面的装订论文，一个学生抽出一本，吹掉书脊上的灰，书名荒唐可笑。\n# en: Read a famous one\nopt.left.label [e7cbd7] = 读一篇名作\n# en: {It's brilliant, and it was written by someone younger than you. You put it back very carefully and go and sit somewhere quiet.|It's the one everybody cites. Reading it in full is like seeing a mountain from the bottom.|You read the most-worn one cover to cover. It's so good it hurts, which you suppose is the point.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fae5c1] = {它才华横溢，而作者比你还年轻。你小心翼翼地把它放回去，找个安静的地方坐了一会儿。|这是人人都引用的那篇。完整读一遍，就像站在山脚下仰望一座山。|你把磨得最旧的那本从头读到尾。好到让人心疼，你猜这正是重点。}\n# en: Read a gloriously bad one\nopt.right.label [a7a3a0] = 读一篇烂得辉煌的\n# en: {It argues for forty pages that rain is a kind of apology. It passed. You feel enormously better about everything.|You read the doorknob one. It is three hundred pages long and completely serious, and you laugh until the carrels shush you.|You find one with footnotes longer than its chapters, and footnotes to the footnotes. Your own thesis suddenly seems sane.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6885f4] = {它用四十页论证雨是一种道歉。它通过了。你对一切都好受多了。|你读了门把手那篇。三百页，一本正经，你笑到隔间里的人都来嘘你。|你找到一篇脚注比正文还长、脚注还有脚注的。你自己的论文顿时显得很正常。}\n\n@event thesis_lab_mock_defence\n# en: The Mock Defence\ntitle [e3f5f2] = 模拟答辩\n# en: {Final-years run mock defences at the end of the lab. Three in borrowed gowns play the committee, and they play it cruel.|Mock defence day. The pretend committee has added a fourth chair and a tiny gavel.|The mock committee nods at you as you come in. They've started keeping score on the board.} {Tonight's chief examiner is doing an impression of Thorne that is unkindly accurate.|The chair has a bell, which they ring for every \"um\".|{season == \"winter\": Everyone defends in scarves; the lab's fire is out.|Someone has borrowed a skeleton from the infirmary to sit in the audience.}}\ntext [635064] = {毕业班在实验室尽头举办模拟答辩。三个人披着借来的长袍扮委员会，扮得很凶。|模拟答辩日。假委员会添了第四把椅子和一把小法槌。|模拟委员会在你进门时朝你点头。他们开始在黑板上记分了。} {今晚的主考在模仿索恩，模仿得刻薄而精准。|主席有个铃铛，你每说一个“呃”就摇一下。|{season == \"winter\": 实验室的炉火灭了，大家裹着围巾答辩。|有人从医务室借来一副骨架，坐在观众席上。}}\n# en: A chalkboard at the end of the thesis lab, three final-year students sitting as stern pretend examiners in borrowed gowns while a fourth stands to defend.\nscene [4910d2] = 论文实验室尽头的黑板前，三个毕业生披着借来的长袍扮作严厉的考官，第四个站着答辩。\n# en: Take the hot seat\nopt.left.label [dafea3] = 坐上被告席\n# en: {You field every question, even the cruel one about your sources. The chief examiner says \"Hm,\" in Thorne's voice, and means it.|They ring the bell for \"um\" only twice. That's a record, and the committee applauds with the tiny gavel.|You hold your ground for an hour. Your score on the board is the highest of the term, and people come to ask how.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4afa89] = {你接住了每个问题，连那个关于史料来源的刁钻问题也不例外。主考用索恩的腔调说了声“嗯”，而且是真心的。|他们只为“呃”摇了两次铃。这是纪录，委员会用小法槌为你鼓掌。|你顶住了一个小时。黑板上你的分数是本学期最高，有人跑来问你怎么做到的。}\n# en: {The third question undoes you. You stand at the board saying \"well\" while the chief examiner does Thorne's eyebrow at you.|They find the hole in your argument in four minutes, and the bell rings for every \"um\" after that. Better here than in front of the real thing.|You lose your thread under the gavel. The committee is merciful afterwards, which is worse.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c48a11] = {第三个问题就把你击溃了。你站在黑板前一个劲地说“那个”，主考冲你挑起索恩式的眉毛。|他们四分钟就找到了你论证里的漏洞，此后你每个“呃”都换来一声铃。在这儿露馅总比在真答辩上强。|你在法槌下丢了思路。事后委员会很宽容，这反而更糟。}\n# en: Sit on the committee\nopt.right.label [d112ba] = 坐在委员会席上\n# en: {You ask one question, quietly, and watch the defender's face as they see the hole. You feel like a cat. A kind cat.|You learn to find the weak joint in an argument by watching three of them crack. It's educational and a little shameful.|You're the committee's favourite examiner now: gentle voice, terrible questions.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3075b2] = {你只问了一个问题，轻声细语，看着答辩者发现漏洞时的脸色。你觉得自己像只猫。一只善良的猫。|看着三个论证一一崩塌，你学会了寻找论证的薄弱关节。很有教育意义，也有点可耻。|你如今是委员会最受欢迎的考官：声音温柔，问题可怕。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/castle_towers.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/castle_towers.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event observatory_night_log\n# en: The Night Log\ntitle [9e1784] = 观星日志\n# en: {The observatory keeps a night log, and anyone may write in it.|{flag(wrote_night_log): Since your last entry|Since you were last here}, someone has drawn a moustache on the moon.|You know the log's hands by now: the careful one, the frantic one, the one who only ever writes \"cloudy\".} {season == \"winter\": Frost feathers the inside of the dome.|{The telescope turns, unasked, towards the north.|A draught turns the pages for you.|The lamp is low on oil, as always.}}\ntext [02074c] = {观星台有一本观星日志，谁都可以往里写。|{flag(wrote_night_log): 自你上次写过之后|自你上次来过之后}，有人给月亮画了两撇胡子。|日志里的笔迹你都认得了：工整的那位，潦草的那位，还有永远只写“多云”的那位。} {season == \"winter\": 霜花在穹顶内侧结成羽毛。|{望远镜没人碰，自己转向了北方。|一阵穿堂风替你翻开了书页。|灯油照例快烧干了。}}\n# en: A brass observatory dome at night, one student writing in a huge logbook by lamplight while the great telescope swings round on its own.\nscene [59d5ae] = 夜晚的黄铜穹顶下，一名学生借灯光在一本巨大的日志上书写，大望远镜自顾自地转动。\n# en: Log what the telescope finds\nopt.left.label [8d5562] = 记下望远镜所见\n# en: {You chart a star that wasn't there last month. The log already has it, in a hand forty years older.|Three hours, two pages and one sneeze that smudges a constellation.|The telescope settles on a quiet blue star and waits until you've written it down properly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b0df4e] = {你标出一颗上个月还不存在的星。日志里早有记载，笔迹比你老四十年。|三个小时，两页纸，外加一个喷嚏糊掉了一整个星座。|望远镜停在一颗安静的蓝星上，一直等到你把它好好写下来。}\n# en: Read the old entries instead\nopt.right.label [c05d01] = 改读旧日的记录\n# en: {A winter entry from long ago says \"Saw nothing. Stayed anyway. Worth it.\"|A love letter written across a page of moon sightings. Very bad. Very sweet.|{final_year and flag(wrote_night_log): You find your own first entry and barely know the hand.|A student from your year logged your star a week before you did, with a small drawn smile.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [6075ac] = {很久以前的一个冬夜，有人写道：“什么也没看到。还是留下了。值得。”|一封情书，横跨一整页的月相记录。写得很烂。也很甜。|{final_year and flag(wrote_night_log): 你找到了自己的第一条记录，几乎认不出那笔迹。|你同届的一个学生比你早一周记下了你那颗星，旁边还画了个小笑脸。}}\n# en: Check the almanac first\nopt.up.label [de9e21] = 先查年历\n# en: {The almanac says the moon sets at eleven, so you log the faint stars after it, and get three the log has never had.|Grimsby's pencil in the margin says \"watch the east at two\". You do. Something small and bright crosses it, and you write it down.|You check the tides and the moon before you start, and for once the telescope and you agree on where to look.}\nopt.up.say.1 [192dc4] = {年历说月亮十一点落下，于是你在那之后记录暗星，记下了三颗日志里从没有过的。|格林斯比在页边用铅笔写着“两点看东边”。你照做了。有个小小的亮东西划过，你把它记了下来。|开始前你查好了潮汐和月相，难得望远镜和你对该看哪儿意见一致。}\n\n@event observatory_one_star_each\n# en: One Star Each\ntitle [0eea9a] = 每人一颗星\n# en: {The rule chalked on the observatory door says one star each, then pass it on. {here} has been at the eyepiece for some time.|Someone has added \"two if they're small\" under the chalk rule. {here} is at the eyepiece.|Someone is always at the telescope when you arrive; tonight it's {here}.} {season == \"summer\": The nights are short and the queue is impatient.|{The dome is cold enough to see your breath.|The telescope creaks round to follow something.|Below, the bridges of light are going out one by one.}}\ntext [4ceaec] = {观星台门上用粉笔写着规矩：每人一颗星，看完就让。{here}已经在目镜前待了好一阵。|有人在粉笔规矩下面添了一句“小的可以看两颗”。{here}正守着目镜。|你每次来，望远镜前总有人；今晚是{here}。} {season == \"summer\": 夏夜短，排队的人都很急。|{穹顶里冷得能看见呼出的白气。|望远镜吱呀一声，转去追什么东西。|下方，光之桥一座接一座熄灭。}}\n# en: Two students at the eyepiece of a great brass telescope, one stepping aside to let the other look.\nscene [7fe37e] = 两名学生在一架巨大的黄铜望远镜目镜旁，一人侧身让另一人观看。\n# en: Share the eyepiece\nopt.left.label [b40888] = 一起用目镜\n# en: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {\"That one,\" Seraphine says, \"is going to matter in, oh.\" She doesn't finish.|\"Left of it,\" Seraphine says. \"Next spring's left.\" You look anyway.|\"There,\" Seraphine says, and moves your hand an inch. \"That's the one you'll want. Don't ask me when.\"}|{cast_as(here, kael): {Kael steps aside and points. When your hands get cold, he stands closer.|Kael says the star's name in a Northern word, once, and doesn't explain.|Kael shares the eyepiece without being asked. Below, the bridges go out one by one, and neither of you counts them.}|You take turns until neither of you remembers whose star it was.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [f1688b] = {cast_as(here, seraphine): {“那一颗，”瑟拉芬说，“将来会很要紧，大概在——噢。”她没说完。|“它左边，”瑟拉芬说，“明年春天的左边。”你还是看了。|“那儿，”瑟拉芬说着把你的手挪了一寸，“那一颗是你会想要的。别问我什么时候。”}|{cast_as(here, kael): {凯尔让开，伸手指给你看。你的手冻僵时，他站得近了些。|凯尔用一个北境的词念出那颗星的名字，只念一次，不作解释。|凯尔没等你开口就让出了目镜。下面，光之桥一座接一座地熄灭，你们谁也没去数。}|你们轮流看，看到谁也记不清那颗星是谁先找到的。}}\n# en: Wait your turn properly\nopt.right.label [da2ba7] = 规规矩矩排队\n# en: {cast_as(here, seraphine): {Seraphine leaves mid-sentence. \"You'll want it,\" she says. \"Later. Now, I mean.\"|\"Yours is the next one,\" Seraphine says, and it is.|Seraphine steps back as you arrive, as if she'd been holding your place. \"Right on time,\" she says, puzzling you.}|{cast_as(here, kael): {Kael yields with a formal nod and goes out onto the roof. Frost follows.|Kael waits behind you. When you turn round, he's gone.|Kael gives up the eyepiece the moment you arrive and stays to watch the bridges go out with you.}|By your turn the star has set. The next one is nearly as good.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [eae430] = {cast_as(here, seraphine): {瑟拉芬话说到一半就走了。“你会用得上的，”她说，“以后。我是说现在。”|“下一颗是你的。”瑟拉芬说。果然如此。|你一到，瑟拉芬就退开了，好像一直在替你占位。“正好，”她说，让你摸不着头脑。}|{cast_as(here, kael): {凯尔郑重地点头让开，走到屋顶上去。霜跟着他。|凯尔在你身后等着。你回头时，他已经不在了。|你一到，凯尔就让出了目镜，留下来陪你看光之桥一座座熄灭。}|轮到你时，那颗星已经落下了。下一颗也差不多好。}}\n\n@event observatory_roof_ledge\n# en: The Roof Ledge\ntitle [9c29ff] = 屋顶的窄檐\n# en: {A window in the dome opens onto the roof, and onto a ledge that everyone swears they have never sat on.|Someone else who never sits there has oiled the latch.|The ledge. You know where the loose tile is now.} {season == \"winter\": The tiles are white with frost.|{The lake below is a second sky.|A warm wind comes up from the village.|Someone's scarf is snagged on the gutter.}}\ntext [95a3fa] = {穹顶上有扇窗通向屋顶，窗外有一道窄檐——人人都发誓自己从没坐过。|另一位“从没坐过”的人给窗闩上了油。|那道窄檐。你已经知道哪块瓦是松的了。} {season == \"winter\": 瓦片上白霜一片。|{下方的湖是第二片天空。|一阵暖风从山下小镇吹上来。|不知谁的围巾挂在了檐沟上。}}\n# en: A student sitting on a narrow ledge outside a brass dome, legs dangling over the castle roofs, the lake far below.\nscene [00d358] = 一名学生坐在黄铜穹顶外的窄檐上，双腿悬在城堡屋顶之上，湖在远远的下方。\n# en: Climb out onto the ledge\nopt.left.label [3e2750] = 爬到窄檐上去\n# en: {You sit with the whole sky to yourself until the stars begin to wheel.|The castle looks small from here, and kind, and very old.|You step over the loose tile without looking. Someone's scarf is still on the gutter; you leave it, for whoever comes next.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c6653f] = {整片天空归你一个人，你一直坐到群星开始旋转。|从这里看，城堡显得很小，很温柔，也很老。|你看都不看就跨过了那块松瓦。不知谁的围巾还挂在檐沟上；你没动它，留给下一个人。}\n# en: {The loose tile is loose in a new place. You slide, grab, and hang on, and climb back in with skinned palms.|A gust takes your balance and most of your dignity. The gutter keeps the rest.|You reach for the snagged scarf and the gutter reaches for you. You get back in with the scarf and a bruise.}\nopt.left.say.2 [416f31] = {那块松瓦这回在别处松了。你一滑，一抓，挂住了，爬回来时手掌都磨破了皮。|一阵风夺走了你的平衡和大半体面。剩下的归檐沟。|你伸手去够挂住的围巾，檐沟也伸手够你。你带着围巾和一块淤青爬了回来。}\n# en: Watch through the glass\nopt.right.label [8e53e6] = 隔着玻璃看\n# en: {You see half the sky and all of the smudges.|Through the glass the stars look like they're behind a shop window. You can look; you can't have.|You watch through the glass. On the ledge outside, someone else is sitting where you'd have sat, and they wave.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bb55c6] = {你看到了半边天空，和玻璃上所有的污渍。|隔着玻璃，星星像摆在橱窗里。能看，不能拿。|你隔着玻璃看。外面的窗台上，有人坐在你本会坐的地方，朝你挥了挥手。}\n\n@event observatory_scraped_word\n# en: The Scraped-Out Word\ntitle [5f3087] = 被刮掉的词\n# en: {In the night log's oldest volume, every entry by a student who had finished Advanced Astromancy uses the same word, and every time it has been scraped out.|You've worked out that the missing word was something you did to a star, not something you saw.|You know these pages now. The word is gone; the shape of the work around it is not.} {In one margin, \"Ask. Be told.\"|A pressed flower falls out.|The lamp gutters as you turn the page.}\ntext [622aaf] = {日志最老的那一卷里，凡是修完高等星象学的学生写下的记录，都用到同一个词，而每一处都被刮掉了。|你已经弄明白，那个缺失的词是你对星星做的事，而不是你看见的东西。|这些书页你已经很熟了。那个词不在了，可它周围那份功课的轮廓还在。} {页边写着：“去问。会有人告诉你。”|一朵压干的花掉了出来。|你翻页时，灯焰摇曳了一下。}\n# en: An old observatory logbook open by lamplight, the same word scraped out of line after line.\nscene [cccc67] = 灯下摊开一本古老的观星日志，同一个词被一行又一行地刮掉。\n# en: Trace the gaps by lamplight\nopt.left.label [5f1275] = 借灯光描摹空缺\n# en: You feed a thread of witchlight into the gaps, and it drinks more than you meant to give. {The words around the gaps say \"called\", \"named\", \"down\". Whoever taught it taught it aloud, to people who had finished Advanced Astromancy.|\"...and the light came down when I said its name...\" Then the gap. You will need someone to tell you the word.|The shape around the gap is a verb. Whatever it was, it was done to a star, and it was taught, not written.}\nopt.left.say.1 [960edb] = 你把一缕巫光送进空缺里，它吸走的比你想给的多。{空缺周围的词是“唤”“名”“降下”。不管是谁教的，都是口头教的，教给已经修完高等星象学的人。|“……当我念出它的名字，光便降了下来……”然后是空缺。你得有人把这个词告诉你。|空缺周围的形状说明那是个动词。不管是什么，都是对一颗星做的，而且是口授的，不是写下来的。}\n# en: Ask the telescope, half joking\nopt.right.label [de5250] = 半开玩笑地问望远镜\n# en: {The telescope swings to one bright star and stays there, patient as a teacher who knows you aren't ready.|It turns to a named star and holds it. You get the feeling you have to finish Advanced Astromancy before it explains.|The telescope turns, very slowly, to the same star as always. You're beginning to think it's a reminder, not an answer.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c7ee59] = {望远镜转向一颗明亮的星，停住不动，耐心得像个知道你还没准备好的老师。|它转向一颗有名字的星，稳稳对准。你觉得，得先修完高等星象学，它才肯解释。|望远镜极慢地转向了那颗老地方的星。你开始觉得这是提醒，不是答案。}\n\n@event workshop_spare_bench\n# en: The Spare Bench\ntitle [b782e9] = 备用工作台\n# en: {Mir keeps a spare bench for visitors, under a sign that says TOUCH THINGS (CAREFULLY).|The sign over the spare bench has grown a second line, (MORE CAREFULLY).|Your bench, as Mir has started calling it, has a jar of springs waiting.} {Mir waves a spanner at you without looking up.|Something on a high shelf is ticking.|{season == \"winter\": The forge-lamp makes it the warmest room in the west wing.|The window is open to let the smoke out.}}\ntext [52eaf0] = {米尔给访客留了一张备用工作台，上方挂着牌子：随便摸（小心点）。|备用工作台上方的牌子多了一行：（再小心点）。|“你的工作台”——米尔开始这么叫它了——上面放着一罐弹簧等你。} {米尔头也不抬，冲你挥了挥扳手。|高处的架子上有什么在滴答作响。|{season == \"winter\": 锻炉灯让这里成了西翼最暖和的房间。|窗开着，好放烟出去。}}\n# en: A cluttered workshop bench under a hand-painted sign, a music box in pieces beside a jar of springs, fire buckets by the door.\nscene [d9570d] = 杂乱的工坊工作台上方挂着手绘的牌子，一只拆散的音乐盒旁放着一罐弹簧，门边摆着灭火水桶。\n# en: Mend a jammed music box\nopt.left.label [84f39e] = 修一只卡住的音乐盒\n# en: A loose spring bites your thumb on the way in. {It plays again, a little too fast, like it's late for something.|You fix the tune and break the lid. Mir says that's probably progress.|It plays a waltz nobody has heard in years. You leave it on the sill to finish.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c84c0f] = 进门时一根松掉的弹簧咬了你的拇指一下。{它又响了，稍微快了点，像是赶着去哪儿。|曲子修好了，盒盖弄坏了。米尔说这大概也算进步。|它奏起一支多年没人听过的华尔兹。你把它放在窗台上，让它自己奏完。}\n# en: Just watch Mir work\nopt.right.label [b61e05] = 就看米尔干活\n# en: {Mir explains a gear train for an hour. You follow about a third, which they say is probably a record.|You sit by the fire buckets and watch the sparks. Your eyes sting. It's a good afternoon.|Mir hands you things to hold and forgets you're holding them. It's oddly restful.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fc41a6] = {米尔讲了一个钟头的齿轮传动。你听懂了三分之一，TA说这大概是纪录。|你坐在灭火水桶旁看火花。眼睛被熏得发酸。是个好下午。|米尔把东西递给你拿着，然后忘了你还拿着。出奇地让人放松。}\n\n@event workshop_volunteer\n# en: A Volunteer With Hands\ntitle [1b4b0c] = 有手的志愿者\n# en: {\"Perfect timing! I need a volunteer and you have hands!\" {maker} holds up {a pair of boots that walk on their own|a hat that remembers where you left things|goggles that see round corners}.|{maker} has a new invention and a familiar look. Today: {boots that walk on their own|a hat with a memory|goggles for corners}.|You don't ask anymore. You just look at what {maker} is holding, then at the fire bucket.}\ntext [28d24b] = {“来得正好！我需要一个志愿者，而你有手！”{maker}举起{一双会自己走路的靴子|一顶记得你把东西放在哪儿的帽子|一副能看见拐角后面的护目镜}。|{maker}有了新发明，还有那熟悉的眼神。今天是：{自己走路的靴子|有记性的帽子|看拐角的护目镜}。|你已经不问了。你只是看看{maker}手里的东西，再看看灭火水桶。}\n# en: An artificer in soot-streaked goggles holding out a strange brass contraption, a fire bucket waiting hopefully on the floor.\nscene [204031] = 一位戴着沾满煤灰护目镜的奇械师举着一件古怪的黄铜装置，地上一只灭火水桶满怀期待地候着。\n# en: Try it on\nopt.left.label [fdeee0] = 试戴一下\n# en: {It works! For eleven glorious minutes, it works, and {maker} writes down every one of them.|It does exactly what it should. {maker} looks almost suspicious and walks round it three times.|It works. You still don't know what it was for, and {maker} is too delighted to tell you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1f3f1b] = {成功了！整整十一分钟的辉煌，它一直在运转，{maker}把每一分钟都记了下来。|它完全按设计运转。{maker}几乎起了疑心，围着它转了三圈。|成功了。你还是不知道它是干什么用的，{maker}高兴得顾不上告诉你。}\n# en: {It works in the wrong direction. You spend a while upside down. {maker} apologises at length, with diagrams.|There's a bang, a smell of singed eyebrow, and {maker} saying \"Interesting!\" in a voice that means sorry.|It goes off with a pop and a smell of hot brass. {maker} writes \"promising\" and hands you the bucket.}\nopt.left.say.2 [deb94d] = {它朝反方向运转了。你倒立了好一阵。{maker}长篇大论地道歉，还附了图解。|砰的一声，一股烧焦眉毛的味道，{maker}说着“有意思！”——那语气其实是“对不起”。|它“砰”的一声炸了，一股热黄铜的味道。{maker}写下“有前途”，把水桶递给了你。}\n# en: Hold the fire bucket\nopt.right.label [f76215] = 帮忙拎水桶\n# en: {You hold the bucket. You don't need the bucket. {maker} looks faintly disappointed.|You need the bucket. Briefly, splendidly, you need the bucket.|You hold the bucket. You need it twice, which {maker} calls an improvement.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fdbcfe] = {你拎着水桶。水桶没派上用场。{maker}看起来有点失望。|水桶派上用场了。短暂而壮丽地派上了用场。|你拎着水桶。用上了两次，{maker}说这算是进步。}\n\n@event workshop_stirring_cauldron\n# en: The Self-Stirring Cauldron\ntitle [0d87b7] = 自动搅拌坩埚\n# en: {The self-stirring cauldron in the corner wears a note. DO NOT ADJUST. IT KNOWS WHAT IT DID.|Someone has underlined the cauldron's note twice and added the word STILL.|The cauldron's spoon pauses when you come in, as if it remembers you.} {It's going anticlockwise, which it isn't supposed to.|It smells of toffee and lightning.|{season == \"spring\": Someone has put flowers in it. It is stirring them.|It's stirring soup for nobody.}}\ntext [28bf0c] = {角落里那口自动搅拌的坩埚上贴着纸条。禁止调整。它知道自己干了什么。|有人在坩埚的纸条下划了两道线，又添了两个字：仍然。|你一进门，坩埚的勺子就停了一下，好像认得你。} {它在逆时针搅，按理不该这样。|它闻起来像太妃糖和闪电。|{season == \"spring\": 有人往里面放了花。它正在搅花。|它在给谁也不是的人搅汤。}}\n# en: A small cauldron in a workshop corner stirring itself with a wooden spoon, a stern paper note pinned to its rim.\nscene [2055ca] = 工坊角落里一口小坩埚正用木勺自己搅拌，锅沿上别着一张严厉的纸条。\n# en: Adjust the enchantment\nopt.left.label [826eac] = 调整附魔\n# en: {It settles into a slow, contented clockwise. Mir, passing, says \"Oh!\" and then \"Huh,\" and then \"Teach me.\"|The spoon taps the rim twice, like a thank-you, and gets on with it.|You adjust it, gently, the way it seems to want. It stirs the soup for nobody a little more cheerfully.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7a273c] = {它慢下来，心满意足地顺时针转。米尔路过，先说“噢！”，再说“嗯？”，然后说“教我。”|勺子在锅沿上敲了两下，像在道谢，然后继续干活。|你照它似乎想要的样子，轻轻调了一下。它给没人喝的汤搅得更欢快了些。}\n# en: {It stirs your sleeve, then you, then your sleeve again.|It speeds up instead. The ceiling now has toffee on it, and so do you.|It recognises you, and holds a grudge. The spoon flicks soup at you until you back away.}\nopt.left.say.2 [afcde7] = {它搅了你的袖子，然后搅你，然后又搅袖子。|它反而转得更快了。天花板上现在有太妃糖，你身上也有。|它认出了你，而且记仇。勺子一个劲儿朝你弹汤，直到你退开。}\n# en: Take notes from a safe distance\nopt.right.label [cca44e] = 远远地做笔记\n# en: {You write down everything it's doing wrong. The list is long and slightly envious.|You sketch the rune on its base. The cauldron splashes your page, on purpose you think.|You take notes from the doorway. The spoon pauses, as if waiting for you to finish the sentence.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1dc55a] = {你把它做错的地方全记了下来。清单很长，还带着点羡慕。|你描下它底座上的符文。坩埚溅湿了你的纸——你觉得是故意的。|你在门口记笔记。勺子停了停，好像在等你把那句话写完。}\n\n@event workshop_glass_cabinet\n# en: The Glass Cabinet\ntitle [331550] = 玻璃柜\n# en: {At the back of the workshop is a glass cabinet that isn't Mir's, full of faculty machines, very still.|A clockwork mouse in the glass cabinet has moved since your last visit, and now faces the door.|The cabinet. Mir says the machines in it think, probably, and are waiting for the right student.} A card in old ink: \"Artifice. By invitation, to those who have passed Alchemy II and Evocation II.\"\ntext [b26316] = {工坊最里面有一只不属于米尔的玻璃柜，装满了教职员做的机器，一动不动。|玻璃柜里那只发条老鼠比你上次来时挪了位置，现在正对着门。|那只柜子。米尔说里面的机器会思考，大概吧，它们在等合适的学生。} 一张旧墨水写的卡片：“奇械术。仅限受邀，须已通过炼金学 II 与塑能术 II。”\n# en: A tall glass cabinet at the back of a workshop, clockwork animals inside it, a faded card propped against the glass.\nscene [beb857] = 工坊后方一只高高的玻璃柜，里面摆着发条动物，一张褪色的卡片斜靠在玻璃上。\n# en: Study the machines through the glass\nopt.left.label [4744ef] = 隔着玻璃研究机器\n# en: {Nothing moves while you watch. The moment you look away, something ticks.|You count the gears in a brass beetle and lose count. Somebody made this who could make things and make them burn.|The machines don't move, but you're sure they're thinking. One of them, very slightly, nods.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4109c3] = {你盯着看时，什么都不动。你一移开目光，就有东西滴答一声。|你数一只黄铜甲虫身上的齿轮，数乱了。造它的人，既会造东西，也会让东西烧起来。|机器们一动不动，可你确信它们在思考。其中一台极轻微地点了点头。}\n# en: Try the cabinet lock\nopt.right.label [c81d94] = 试试柜子的锁\n# en: {The lock gives. The mouse winds itself up, bows, and goes back to sleep. You close the door very quietly.|The door swings open an inch, and every machine inside turns its head to look at you. You close it again, politely.|The lock opens at a touch, as if it had been waiting. The machines look at you, and then, satisfied, at each other.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b3b98e] = {锁开了。老鼠给自己上紧发条，鞠了一躬，又睡了。你轻轻地关上柜门。|柜门开了一寸，里面每一台机器都转过头来看你。你又礼貌地把门关上了。|锁一碰就开了，好像一直在等着。机器们看看你，然后满意地看看彼此。}\n# en: {The cabinet shrieks like a kettle. Mir is delighted. The corridor is not.|The lock bites your pick and keeps it. Mir says it does that, probably, to people it likes.|The lock doesn't bite this time. It simply refuses, the way a cat refuses, and Mir says that's progress.}\nopt.right.say.2 [5ff644] = {柜子像水壶一样尖叫起来。米尔乐坏了。走廊里的人可没有。|锁咬住了你的撬片，不还了。米尔说，它大概对喜欢的人都这样。|这回锁没咬你。它只是拒绝，像猫那样拒绝，米尔说这就是进步。}\n\n@event ledger_office_unprintable\n# en: Letters We Will Not Print\ntitle [ae0100] = 不予刊登的来信\n# en: {By the Ledger's door sits a basket marked LETTERS WE WILL NOT PRINT. Visitors may read them.|The unprintable basket has overflowed into a second basket, which is also full.|You are a connoisseur of the unprintable basket now.} {On top is a complaint about the soup, in verse.|One is just a drawing of a prefect, unflattering and accurate.|{season == \"winter\": Someone demands that snow be cancelled.|Someone asks who keeps moving the stairs.}}\ntext [9924ff] = {《流明纪事报》编辑部门口放着一只篮子，标着“不予刊登的来信”。访客可以随便读。|不予刊登的来信溢到了第二只篮子里，第二只也满了。|你现在是这只篮子的资深鉴赏家了。} {最上面是一封用韵文写的、投诉膳堂汤的信。|有一封只画了一位级长，不好看，但很像。|{season == \"winter\": 有人要求取消下雪。|有人问到底是谁老在挪楼梯。}}\n# en: A wicker basket overflowing with letters by the door of a cramped newspaper office, front pages papering the walls.\nscene [276fb2] = 狭小的报社编辑部门口，一只柳条篮里塞满了信，墙上贴满了旧头版。\n# en: Read the whole basket\nopt.left.label [7b9421] = 把整篮都读完\n# en: {The best one is an apology to a goat.|One is from a first-year who misses home. You put it back gently.|{final_year and flag(ledger_letter_sent): A letter in your own first-year hand. You don't remember sending it. You were right.|Three letters about the same broken window, all blaming different people.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [0cb17b] = {最好的一封，是写给一只山羊的道歉信。|有一封来自一个想家的一年级生。你轻轻把它放了回去。|{final_year and flag(ledger_letter_sent): 一封你自己一年级时的笔迹。你不记得寄过它。你当时说得没错。|三封信写的是同一扇碎窗户，各怪各的人。}}\n# en: Write one of your own\nopt.right.label [fec258] = 自己也写一封\n# en: {They print it. Two people quote it at you at breakfast; one of them disagrees with it loudly.|It runs on the back page, under your initials. You feel oddly famous for a day.|They print it, with a footnote from the editor disagreeing. It's the most-read thing in the issue.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ed4137] = {他们登了。早饭时有两个人向你引用了它，其中一个大声表示反对。|它登在了末版，署着你的缩写。你莫名其妙地出了一天名。|他们登了，还附了一条编辑表示反对的脚注。那是这一期读的人最多的东西。}\n# en: {It goes in the basket. By next week, someone is reading it aloud.|It's rejected with a note: \"Too sensible.\" You aren't sure that's a criticism.|It goes in the basket. You find it there next week, with someone else's reply pinned to it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a4de71] = {它进了篮子。下个星期，就有人在大声朗读它。|退稿附了一张字条：“太有道理了。”你不确定这算不算批评。|它进了篮子。下周你在那儿找到它时，上面别着一封别人的回信。}\n\n@event ledger_office_deadline_night\n# en: Deadline Night\ntitle [d06cb7] = 截稿之夜\n# en: {The press is groaning like a dragon on deadline, and {here} eyes you like a captain eyes a spare oar.|{here} hands you a stack before you've said hello, and a rag for your fingers.|You take a stack and start folding. {here} doesn't look up, which is a compliment.} {The ink on the front page is still wet.|A headline has a spelling mistake nobody will admit to.|{final_year and flag(ledger_folded): You've folded this paper for years now.|The press shudders as if it's thinking of stopping.}}\ntext [b3ba57] = {印刷机像截稿夜的龙一样呻吟，{here}打量你的眼神，就像船长打量一支备用的桨。|你还没打招呼，{here}就递来一摞报纸，外加一块擦手的布。|你拿起一摞就开始折。{here}头都不抬——这已经是夸奖了。} {头版的墨迹还没干。|有个标题拼错了，谁也不肯认。|{final_year and flag(ledger_folded): 你已经折了好几年这份报纸。|印刷机一阵哆嗦，像在考虑罢工。}}\n# en: A cramped office lit by one lamp, a groaning printing press, two students folding newspapers into tall stacks.\nscene [241781] = 一盏灯照亮狭小的编辑部，印刷机轰鸣呻吟，两名学生把报纸折成高高的几摞。\n# en: Fold until the press stops\nopt.left.label [b5b377] = 折到印刷机停下\n# en: {cast_as(here, nessa): {\"Good. Fast. Again,\" says Nessa. From her, that's a sonnet.|Nessa folds twice as fast as you and reads every page as she goes.|Nessa doesn't say anything. She just slides the next stack to you, which is the highest honour on deadline night.}|{By dawn your fingers are grey with ink, and you know tomorrow's news before anyone.|You fold until the stacks are taller than you. Then you fold the ones that fell over.|You fold in rhythm with the press. When it stops, you keep going for a moment, out of habit.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [618b63] = {cast_as(here, nessa): {“好。快。再来。”内莎说。从她嘴里出来，这就是一首十四行诗。|内莎折得比你快一倍，还边折边把每一页读完。|内莎什么也没说。她只是把下一摞推给你，这是截稿夜的最高礼遇。}|{天亮时你的手指被墨染成灰色，明天的新闻你比谁都先知道。|你折到报纸堆得比你还高。然后再把倒下来的那些重新折好。|你跟着印刷机的节奏折。它停了，你还习惯性地多折了一会儿。}}\n# en: Make your excuses\nopt.right.label [1694c0] = 找个借口溜走\n# en: {cast_as(here, nessa): {\"Deserter,\" Nessa calls. \"Noted. Quoted. Forgiven by Thursday.\"|Nessa doesn't look up. \"Coward,\" she says, almost fondly, and writes something down.|Nessa looks up for once. \"Fine. Go. You owe me a Thursday.\" You do, and she collects.}|{You slip away before the second stack. The press groans after you, reproachfully.|You plead an essay. Nobody believes you. Tomorrow's paper mentions a certain absent volunteer.|You leave, and the press shudders to a stop as you go. Somebody blames you, and it goes in the paper.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [959246] = {cast_as(here, nessa): {“逃兵，”内莎喊，“已记录。已引用。周四前原谅你。”|内莎头也不抬。“胆小鬼。”她说，语气近乎亲昵，然后记下了点什么。|内莎难得抬了头。“行。走吧。你欠我一个周四。”你确实欠，她也确实来讨了。}|{第二摞还没折，你就溜了。印刷机在你身后埋怨地呻吟。|你说要赶论文。没人信。第二天的报上提到了某位缺席的志愿者。|你走了，印刷机随着你离开哆嗦着停了下来。有人把账算到你头上，还上了报。}}\n\n@event ledger_office_drying_line\n# en: The Drying Line\ntitle [7b0073] = 晾报绳\n# en: {Tomorrow's gossip column is pegged on a line to dry, and nobody is watching it.|The proofs hang on the line, and the peg on the gossip column is a new red one.|The drying line. You know which peg holds the gossip column.} {One item names your house.|One item begins \"Which second-year was seen...\"|{final_year: One item is about the graduating year. Possibly you.|A whole paragraph is about a broken window.}}\ntext [f77302] = {明天的八卦专栏夹在绳子上晾着，没人看着。|校样挂在绳上，夹八卦专栏的换成了一个新的红夹子。|那根晾报绳。你知道哪个夹子夹着八卦专栏。} {有一条提到了你的学院。|有一条开头是“哪位二年级生被人看见……”|{final_year: 有一条写的是毕业班。也许就是你。|整整一段都在说一扇碎窗户。}}\n# en: Freshly printed pages pegged on a line across a cramped office to dry, one page hanging slightly crooked.\nscene [125d66] = 刚印好的报纸夹在横贯狭小编辑部的绳子上晾干，其中一页挂得有点歪。\n# en: Read ahead\nopt.left.label [f50674] = 先睹为快\n# en: {You read it all and hang it back exactly as crooked as it was.|You know tomorrow's scandal tonight. It is much smaller than it will look in print.|You read the item under the red peg. It's about you, and it's true, and it's very short. You leave it exactly as it was.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f01188] = {你全读完了，又把它原样挂回去，歪得一模一样。|今晚你就知道了明天的丑闻。它远没有印出来时看着那么大。|你读了红夹子下面那条。写的是你，是真的，而且很短。你原样挂了回去。}\n# en: {Caught. The next column has an item beginning \"Which nosy visitor...\"|A peg snaps. The page lands face-down in the ink tray, and everyone turns round.|You lift the red peg and the whole line comes down. Tomorrow's column has a new item, and it's about you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [cc8425] = {被抓了。下一期专栏里有一条，开头是“哪位好奇的访客……”|一个夹子崩断了。那页纸正面朝下掉进了墨盘，所有人都转过头来。|你一提那只红夹子，整条绳子都塌了。明天的专栏多了一条，写的是你。}\n# en: Leave the line alone\nopt.right.label [1d8c18] = 别碰那根绳子\n# en: {You spend the night wondering. That's the price of manners.|You find out with everyone else, at breakfast. It was about you anyway.|You leave it. You know which peg it is, and that's almost the same as knowing.}\nopt.right.say.1 [dbd5de] = {你琢磨了一整晚。这就是讲礼貌的代价。|第二天早饭时，你和大家一起知道了。反正写的就是你。|你没碰。你知道是哪一个夹子，这几乎等于知道了。}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_stone_owl\n# en: The Stone Owl\ntitle [6ea1cf] = 石雕猫头鹰\n# en: {The stone owl on the stair opens one eye. \"A question first,\" it says, in a voice like a door closing.|The owl's other eye is half open tonight. It has been waiting all day for someone to ask.|The owl opens both eyes for you now, which may or may not be an honour.} {Today's question is about the founders.|Today's question is a riddle with no right answer.|{season == \"winter\": There is snow on its head, and it is not amused.|A moth has landed on its beak. It is pretending not to notice.}}\ntext [ae3fd2] = {楼梯上的石雕猫头鹰睁开一只眼。“先回答一个问题。”它说，声音像一扇正在合上的门。|今晚猫头鹰的另一只眼也半睁着。它等了一整天，就盼着有人来问。|猫头鹰现在会为你睁开两只眼了，这也许是种荣幸，也许不是。} {今天的问题关于创始者。|今天的问题是个没有正确答案的谜语。|{season == \"winter\": 它头上积了雪，很不高兴。|一只飞蛾停在它的喙上。它假装没注意到。}}\n# en: A stone owl on the newel post of a spiral stair, one eye open, a student standing very still before it.\nscene [770d5b] = 螺旋楼梯的立柱上蹲着一只石雕猫头鹰，睁着一只眼，一名学生在它面前一动不敢动。\n# en: Answer the question\nopt.left.label [65b450] = 回答问题\n# en: {The owl closes its eyes and the stair is yours. Upstairs, someone laughs, short and pleased.|\"Adequate,\" says the owl, which from a stone owl is a standing ovation.|You answer before it finishes asking. The owl looks at you with both eyes for a long time, then closes them.}\nopt.left.say.1 [75b8c5] = {猫头鹰闭上眼，楼梯归你了。楼上有人笑了一声，短促而愉快。|“尚可。”猫头鹰说。从一只石头猫头鹰嘴里说出来，这等于全场起立鼓掌。|它还没问完你就答了。猫头鹰睁着两只眼睛看了你很久，然后闭上了。}\n# en: {\"No,\" says the owl, and closes both eyes. You climb anyway, feeling marked down.|The owl repeats your answer back to you, slowly. It sounds worse.|Your answer is right and the owl says \"No\" anyway, to see what you'll do. You climb on. It seems satisfied.}\nopt.left.say.2 [566636] = {“不对。”猫头鹰说着，闭上了两只眼。你还是爬了上去，感觉被扣了分。|猫头鹰把你的答案慢慢复述了一遍。听起来更糟了。|你答对了，猫头鹰却照样说“不对”，想看看你会怎么做。你继续往上爬。它似乎很满意。}\n# en: Climb past without a word\nopt.right.label [ef7de3] = 一言不发地走过去\n# en: {The owl says nothing. At the top, the door is shut. You have the strong feeling you've been noted.|You get past. The owl's eye follows you all the way up, and all the way down again.|You climb past and nod to it, as you'd nod to a porter. It nods back, very slightly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a5a9f6] = {猫头鹰什么也没说。到了顶上，门关着。你强烈地感觉自己被记了一笔。|你过去了。猫头鹰的眼睛一路跟着你上楼，又一路跟着你下楼。|你经过时朝它点点头，就像朝门房点头一样。它极轻微地点头回礼。}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_tea\n# en: Two Cups Poured\ntitle [91cc0d] = 两杯已斟好\n# en: {The headmistress pours two cups unasked. \"Sit. Tell me how your term is going, and then how it is really going.\"|Two cups, already poured. \"Well,\" says {host}, \"how is it really going?\"|You sit before she asks. She looks pleased, and doesn't say so.} {The crystal towers on her board have moved.|Rain ticks on the high windows.|{final_year: \"Your last year,\" she says. \"I remember your first.\"|Footnote, the library cat, sleeps on her papers.}}\ntext [0b49e7] = {院长没问你要不要，就斟了两杯茶。“坐。说说你这学期过得怎么样，再说说真正过得怎么样。”|两杯茶，已经斟好。“那么，”{host}说，“真正过得怎么样？”|她还没开口，你就坐下了。她看上去很满意，但没说出来。} {她棋盘上的水晶塔挪了位置。|雨点轻敲着高窗。|{final_year: “你的最后一年了，”她说，“我还记得你的第一年。”|图书馆的猫脚注睡在她的文件上。}}\n# en: A tower study full of books, a tall pale woman pouring two cups of tea beside a board of crystal towers by the window.\nscene [56714a] = 堆满书的塔楼书房里，一位高挑苍白的女士在窗边的水晶塔棋盘旁斟着两杯茶。\n# en: Tell her the truth\nopt.left.label [bfb1ef] = 跟她说实话\n# en: {She listens without interrupting, which is worse than any lecture and better than most comfort.|She hears all of it and says only, \"Yes. I thought so.\" Somehow that helps, later.|You tell her before she asks. She hears it all, then tells you something true about her own first years. It doesn't help, and then it does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [133c55] = {她从头听到尾，一句也不打断。这比任何训话都难熬，又比大多数安慰都管用。|她全听完了，只说：“是的。我也这么想。”不知怎么，过后这句话很有用。|她还没问你就说了。她全听完，然后讲了一件她自己刚入学那几年的真事。起初没用，后来有用了。}\n# en: Give her the polished version\nopt.right.label [02edb8] = 给她一个漂亮的版本\n# en: {She laughs, short and delighted, and lets you get away with it. You aren't sure that's a good sign.|\"Very good,\" she says. \"Next time, the true one.\" She means both halves.|She lets the polished version stand and pours more tea. \"You've got better at it,\" she says. You can't tell if that's praise.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5cf7c6] = {她笑了一声，短促而愉快，放你过了关。你拿不准这是不是好兆头。|“很好，”她说，“下次讲真的那个。”两句她都是认真的。|她任由那个体面的版本过关，又添了茶。“你说得越来越好了，”她说。你听不出这是不是夸奖。}\n# en: {\"Mm,\" she says, and pours you more tea, and waits. You end up telling her anyway.|She lets the silence go on until it says everything you didn't.|She hears the polished version, then repeats the one sentence of it that was true. You talk for an hour after that.}\nopt.right.say.2 [92b23b] = {“嗯。”她说着，又给你添了茶，然后等着。最后你还是全说了。|她任由沉默延续，直到沉默把你没说的都说了。|她听完那个体面的版本，然后把其中唯一一句真话复述了一遍。之后你说了一个钟头。}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_window_seat\n# en: The Window Seat\ntitle [15c249] = 窗边座位\n# en: {The headmistress waves you to the window seat without looking up from her letters. \"Read anything. Break nothing.\"|A book has been left on the window seat, open, as if for you.|Your window seat, the headmistress calls it now, as though it always was.} {Below, the lake is perfectly still.|Her quill scratches; the clock does not tick.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves blow past at the height of the clouds.|The bridges of light come on one by one below.}}\ntext [e9f89d] = {院长头也不抬地朝窗边座位挥了挥手，继续写信。“随便读。别弄坏东西。”|窗边座位上摊着一本书，像是给你留的。|院长现在管它叫“你的窗边座位”，好像一直如此。} {下方的湖面纹丝不动。|她的笔沙沙作响；钟却不走。|{season == \"autumn\": 落叶在云的高度飘过。|下方的光之桥一座座亮起来。}}\n# en: A deep window seat high in a tower, a student reading with the lake far below, a headmistress writing letters at a desk behind.\nscene [830655] = 高塔上一个深深的窗边座位，一名学生在读书，湖在远远的下方，院长在身后的书桌前写信。\n# en: Read what she left out\nopt.left.label [95fb60] = 读她摊开的书\n# en: {A history of the seven towers, annotated in her hand. One note just says \"No.\"|A book of old law, with a dried rose marking a page about second chances.|A treatise on wards so dull it must be important. You get through it. She notices.}\nopt.left.say.1 [43c36e] = {一本七塔史，页边有她的批注。有一条只写着“不”。|一本古老的律法书，一朵干玫瑰夹在讲第二次机会的那一页。|一本讲护界的论著，枯燥得一定很重要。你读完了。她注意到了。}\n# en: Doze on the window seat\nopt.right.label [c962c8] = 在窗边座位上打个盹\n# en: {You wake to find a blanket over you and no comment at all, which is its own kind of comment.|You sleep through most of the visit. When you wake, she is still writing, and gives you a look over her spectacles that says she had hoped you would read.|You doze in your window seat. You wake to find she's moved her desk lamp so it doesn't shine in your eyes.}\nopt.right.say.1 [74052f] = {你醒来时身上多了条毯子，她一句评论也没有——这本身就是一种评论。|你睡掉了大半次拜访。醒来时她还在写，从眼镜上方看了你一眼，那眼神是说她本来希望你会读点书。|你在你的窗边座位上打盹。醒来时发现她把台灯挪了个位置，不让光照到你眼睛。}\n\n@event restricted_section_breathing_shelves\n# en: The Breathing Shelves\ntitle [e6d5c6] = 会呼吸的书架\n# en: {The black-bound books breathe in the dark, slow as sleepers. You find a chair and light a candle.|You've learned to breathe with the shelves; it helps.|Your chair is where you left it. The books shift, as if making room.} {One spine is warm to the touch.|Somewhere a page turns by itself.|{season == \"winter\": Your breath fogs. Theirs doesn't.|The candle burns low and blue.}}\ntext [50fdb9] = {黑色封皮的书在黑暗里呼吸，缓慢得像熟睡的人。你找了把椅子，点上蜡烛。|你学会了跟着书架一起呼吸，这有帮助。|你的椅子还在原处。书挪了挪，像在给你腾地方。} {有一本书脊摸上去是温的。|某处有一页书自己翻了过去。|{season == \"winter\": 你呼出白气。它们没有。|烛火烧得又低又蓝。}}\n# en: Black-bound books on tall shelves in the dark, a single candle on a reading table, the spines faintly rising and falling.\nscene [4628e1] = 黑暗中高高的书架上摆满黑色封皮的书，阅读桌上只点着一支蜡烛，书脊微微起伏。\n# en: Read until the candle gutters\nopt.left.label [15382b] = 读到蜡烛燃尽\n# en: {You read about wards that were old when the towers were new. Afterwards you can't sleep, and don't quite want to.|You read a whole chapter before you notice the book has been reading along, a line ahead.|You read in your chair by a low blue candle. The shelves breathe with you, and the chapter goes in like a held breath.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1ebbef] = {你读到了一些护界，它们在七塔落成时就已经古老。之后你睡不着，也不太想睡。|读完一整章，你才发现那本书一直在跟着读，比你快一行。|你在你的椅子上，就着低低的蓝色烛光读书。书架跟着你呼吸，那一章像一口屏住的气一样被你吸了进去。}\n# en: Read only the spines\nopt.right.label [5119aa] = 只读书脊\n# en: {Even the titles take something out of you. One of them is just your house's name.|You walk the shelves by candlelight. The spines lean towards you, and then politely away.|You walk the spines, and they make room for you as you pass. One spine you haven't seen before has no title at all.}\nopt.right.say.1 [542757] = {光是书名就让你耗神。其中一本只写着你学院的名字。|你借着烛光走过一排排书架。书脊朝你倾过来，又礼貌地退了回去。|你走过一排排书脊，它们在你经过时给你让路。有一本你从没见过的，书脊上什么字也没有。}\n\n@event restricted_section_lantern\n# en: Holding the Lantern\ntitle [7c19fb] = 提灯的人\n# en: {{friend} came with you, for courage, and is holding the lantern in both hands.|You bring a second candle this time; {friend} takes the lantern without being asked.|You know which aisles creak now. {friend} holds the lantern where you point.} {\"Did that book just sigh?\" {they} whisper{s}.|A shelf creaks behind you both.|{final_year: \"I'll miss this,\" {they} say{s}, not sounding sure.|The lantern makes the shadows walk.}}\ntext [61f06e] = {{friend}陪你来壮胆，双手提着灯。|你这回多带了一支蜡烛；{friend}不等你开口就接过了灯。|哪条过道会吱呀响，你现在都知道了。{friend}把灯举到你指的地方。} {“那本书是不是叹了口气？”{they}小声说。|你们身后有个书架吱呀了一声。|{final_year: “我会想念这个的。”{they}说，听起来没什么把握。|灯光让影子走动起来。}}\n# en: Two students in a dark library aisle, one holding a lantern high, the other reading a black book, shadows stretching behind them.\nscene [403cba] = 黑暗的图书馆过道里，一名学生高举提灯，另一名在读一本黑色的书，影子在两人身后拉长。\n# en: Read while they keep watch\nopt.left.label [9567b8] = 你读书，TA放哨\n# en: {You read; {friend} keeps watch and keeps count of every noise, out loud.|You lose an hour in a book. {friend} loses an hour staring at a shelf that keeps staring back.|You read; {friend} holds the light where you point, and the shadows walk where {they} hold{s} it. Neither of you says much on the way back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2727b8] = {你读书，{friend}放哨，并且把每一声响动都大声数出来。|你在一本书里丢了一个钟头。{friend}丢了一个钟头，跟一个不停回瞪的书架对视。|你读书；{friend}照你指的地方举灯，影子就随着{them}举的地方走。回去的路上你们都没怎么说话。}\n# en: Take the lantern for a while\nopt.right.label [83afe8] = 换你来提一会儿灯\n# en: {You hold the light so {friend} can read. Something in the dark rustles the whole time, and you don't let it show.|{friend} reads aloud in a whisper. The books seem to lean in to listen.|You take the lantern and {friend} reads to you, a page you'll both remember. The shadows stop walking to listen.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9b56bf] = {你提着灯让{friend}读书。黑暗里一直有什么在窸窣作响，你没露出来。|{friend}小声地朗读。书好像都凑过来听。|你接过灯笼，{friend}给你念了一页，你们俩都会记住的那一页。影子都停下来听。}\n\n@event restricted_section_chained_book\n# en: The Chained Book\ntitle [1aa621] = 锁链之书\n# en: {One book here is chained to its shelf, and tonight it has opened itself to a page.|The chained book lies open at a different page, and the chain is a link longer.|The chained book has been expecting you. That's the worst part.} {The page is in a script you almost know.|The page shows a lock with no keyhole.|The page is blank until you look away.}\ntext [84ae3a] = {这里有一本书被锁链拴在书架上，今晚它自己翻开到了某一页。|锁链之书摊开在另一页，锁链长了一节。|锁链之书一直在等你。这才是最糟的。} {那一页的文字你几乎认得。|那一页画着一把没有锁孔的锁。|那一页是空白的，直到你移开目光。}\n# en: A heavy book chained to a library shelf, lying open on its own under a dim candle, the chain hanging slack.\nscene [04b543] = 一本沉重的书被锁链拴在图书馆书架上，在昏暗的烛光下自己摊开，锁链松垂。\n# en: Read the open page\nopt.left.label [a03931] = 读那翻开的一页\n# en: A thread of stored power runs up your arm from the page. {The words make sense for exactly as long as you're reading them. Afterwards you only remember that they did.|You read it, and the book turns its own page, satisfied, as if you'd passed something.|The page stays blank while you look and fills when you blink. You read it in blinks. It takes all night.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3233d6] = 一缕储存的力量从书页顺着你的手臂流上来。{那些字只在你读着的时候才有意义。读完后，你只记得它们曾经有意义。|你读完了，书满意地自己翻了一页，好像你通过了什么。|你盯着看时那一页是空白的，一眨眼就写满了。你一眨一眨地读。读了一整夜。}\n# en: {The words read you back. For a day afterwards you keep feeling underlined.|The candle goes out mid-line. In the dark, the page keeps going.|You look away and the page fills; you look back and it's blank, and smug. Your eyes ache for a day.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3ce6bf] = {那些字反过来读了你。之后一整天，你总觉得自己被划了线。|蜡烛在读到一半时灭了。黑暗中，那一页还在继续。|你一移开目光那页就写满了；一回头又是空白，还带着得意。你的眼睛疼了一天。}\n# en: Close it and step back\nopt.right.label [fad0c0] = 合上它，退后\n# en: As the cover shuts, something that had been quietly drawing on your magic lets go. {The chain rattles as the cover shuts, like a sigh. You stand with your back to it until your hands stop shaking.|You close it gently, the way you'd close a door on someone sleeping.|You close it before it can finish expecting you. The chain lies still, a link shorter.}\nopt.right.say.1 [eb31e6] = 封面合上时，那个一直悄悄汲取你魔力的东西松开了。{锁链哗啦一响，像一声叹息。你背对着它站着，直到手不再发抖。|你轻轻地合上它，就像给熟睡的人关门。|你在它把期待说完之前合上了它。锁链静静躺着，短了一节。}\n\n@event restricted_section_handleless_door\n# en: The Door Without a Handle\ntitle [8f3217] = 没有把手的门\n# en: {At the very back is a door with no handle and no window. On it, in chalk that never smudges, HOLLOW STUDIES, BY LEAVE OF THE HEADMISTRESS.|Tonight the door without a handle is warm, like the outer door.|You've read its chalk so often you could recite it.} Underneath, smaller: \"Umbral Theory II. Astromancy II. And know what the founders made, and why.\"\ntext [9b1c7d] = {最深处有一扇没有把手也没有窗的门。门上用永不褪色的粉笔写着：空洞研究，须经院长准许。|今晚，那扇没有把手的门是温的，和外面那扇门一样。|门上的粉笔字你读得太多，都能背下来了。} 下面一行小字：“暗影理论 II。星象学 II。并且知道创始者造了什么，又为何而造。”\n# en: A plain door with no handle at the back of a dark library, chalk writing on it that has not smudged in years.\nscene [2de116] = 漆黑图书馆的最深处，一扇没有把手的朴素木门，门上的粉笔字多年未曾模糊。\n# en: Knock\nopt.left.label [60f31f] = 敲门\n# en: {Nobody answers. Then, very far away, a pleasant voice says, \"Not yet.\"|Your knock doesn't echo. It just stops, as if something on the other side caught it. Next day the headmistress nods to you in the corridor, as if she heard.|You knock, as you always do. This time, from far away, someone knocks back, once, as if to say they've noted it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [db6448] = {没人应。然后，在很远很远的地方，一个温和的声音说：“还不是时候。”|你的敲门声没有回音。它就那么停了，像门另一边有什么接住了它。第二天，院长在走廊里朝你点了点头，好像她听见了。|你像往常一样敲了门。这一回，在很远的地方，有人回敲了一下，好像在说已经记下了。}\n# en: Copy the chalk into your notes\nopt.right.label [d25ac8] = 把粉笔字抄进笔记\n# en: Copying the chalk draws on your magic like a cold draught. {The chalk makes your pencil cold. Two courses and a question, and the question is the hard part.|You copy it word for word. Back in your room, your notes have the same words, and one more underline than you made.|You copy it from memory and check it against the door. The chalk agrees with you, which feels like being agreed with.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b95b40] = 抄写粉笔字像一阵冷风似的抽走你的魔力。{粉笔字让你的铅笔变得冰凉。两门课和一个问题，难的是那个问题。|你一字不差地抄了下来。回到房间，笔记上还是那些字，却比你画的多了一道下划线。|你凭记忆抄下来，再对着门核对。粉笔字和你一致，感觉像被认可了。}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_old_marks\n# en: Marks on the Wall\ntitle [1ff1e7] = 墙上的刻痕\n# en: {The passage walls are covered in initials, dates and tiny arrows, scratched by generations who were never here.|Among the marks on the wall is a new one, still sharp.|You know these walls now: the arrows that lie, the dates that can't be right, the initials that keep coming back.} {The wind sings through the gargoyle's teeth.|Below, a door closes that shouldn't be there.|{season == \"winter\": Frost picks out every letter.|Moths sleep in the deeper scratches.}}\ntext [b1f5d2] = {通道的墙上刻满了缩写、日期和小箭头，出自一代又一代“从没来过这里”的人。|墙上的刻痕里多了一道新的，还很锋利。|这些墙你已经熟了：说谎的箭头，不可能对的日期，反复出现的缩写。} {风穿过石像鬼的牙缝唱着歌。|下方，一扇不该存在的门关上了。|{season == \"winter\": 霜把每个字母都描了出来。|飞蛾睡在较深的刻痕里。}}\n# en: A narrow stone passage behind a gargoyle, its walls covered in scratched initials, dates and tiny arrows, lit by a stub of candle.\nscene [5e6982] = 石像鬼身后一条狭窄的石头通道，墙上刻满缩写、日期和小箭头，一截蜡烛头照着它们。\n# en: Add your own mark\nopt.left.label [5a8fd8] = 刻下你自己的记号\n# en: {You scratch your initials small and low, with a tiny arrow pointing nowhere. It feels like signing something.|{final_year and flag(left_wall_mark): You add the year beside your old mark. It looks so much younger than you.|A knuckle pays for it. The mark is crooked and entirely yours.}|You add your mark beside the initials that keep coming back. Next time, there's a tiny arrow pointing to yours.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c9b8ca] = {你把缩写刻得又小又低，再加一个指向虚空的小箭头。感觉像在签什么契约。|{final_year and flag(left_wall_mark): 你在旧记号旁添上了年份。它看起来比你年轻太多。|赔进去一个指关节。记号歪歪扭扭，完全属于你。}|你在那组反复出现的缩写旁刻下自己的记号。下次来时，有个小箭头指向了你的。}\n# en: Follow an old arrow\nopt.right.label [40d53f] = 跟着旧箭头走\n# en: {The arrow leads to another arrow, which leads to a wall, which has a joke scratched on it.|The arrow leads to a slit window with a view of the lake no plan admits to. Your knees disagree with the route.|You follow the arrow you know lies, to see how it lies. It lies beautifully, all the way to a view of the stars.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d4dff3] = {箭头指向另一个箭头，又指向一堵墙，墙上刻着一个笑话。|箭头通向一道窄窗，窗外的湖景没有哪张地图承认。你的膝盖不同意这条路线。|你顺着那个你知道会撒谎的箭头走，想看看它怎么撒谎。它撒得很漂亮，一路把你带到了能看星星的地方。}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_offering_dish\n# en: The Gargoyle's Dish\ntitle [695172] = 石像鬼的碟子\n# en: {The third gargoyle has a dish worn into its paw, and in the dish are offerings, a button, a biscuit, a bent key.|Someone has left the gargoyle a single grape, peeled.|The gargoyle looks at you the way a landlord looks at the first of the month.} {Tonight it looks hungry.|Tonight it looks smug.|{season == \"summer\": The stone is still warm from the day.|Rain runs out of its mouth in a steady complaint.}}\ntext [0e642d] = {第三座石像鬼的爪子上磨出了一只碟子，碟里放着供品——一颗纽扣，一块饼干，一把弯钥匙。|有人给石像鬼留了一颗葡萄，还剥了皮。|石像鬼看你的眼神，就像房东在月初看房客。} {今晚它看起来很饿。|今晚它看起来很得意。|{season == \"summer\": 石头还留着白天的余温。|雨水从它嘴里流出来，像一串不停的抱怨。}}\n# en: A weathered gargoyle on a moonlit parapet with a dish worn into its stone paw, holding a button, a biscuit and a bent key.\nscene [1fa1f4] = 月光下的护墙上，一座饱经风霜的石像鬼，石爪上磨出一只碟子，盛着一颗纽扣、一块饼干和一把弯钥匙。\n# en: Leave a few crowns in the dish\nopt.left.label [adb70b] = 往碟里放几枚克朗\n# en: {By morning the crowns are gone, and so is the biscuit. Nobody admits to either.|The crowns clink in. The gargoyle's expression doesn't change, but the wind drops.|You pay the gargoyle its crowns, like rent. The night is calm, and the stair doesn't move once on your way back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a9c805] = {第二天早上，克朗不见了，饼干也不见了。谁都不承认。|几枚克朗叮当落进碟里。石像鬼的表情没变，但风停了。|你像交房租一样把克朗付给石像鬼。夜很平静，回去的路上楼梯一次也没挪。}\n# en: Borrow the biscuit\nopt.right.label [d81344] = 借走那块饼干\n# en: {It's a very good biscuit. Somebody's mother made it. You feel a little bad, and then less.|Shortbread, still crisp. The gargoyle allows it, this once.|You borrow the peeled grape. The gargoyle's look follows you down the stair, but the grape is excellent.}\nopt.right.say.1 [67d63f] = {是块很好的饼干。准是谁的母亲做的。你有点愧疚，然后愧疚少了些。|黄油酥饼，还是脆的。石像鬼这回准了。|你借走了那颗剥好的葡萄。石像鬼的目光一路跟着你下楼，可葡萄好吃极了。}\n# en: {It's made of stone. The gargoyle's joke, and it's been waiting a long time to tell it.|The wind gets up the moment you take it, and stays up all night, rattling your window.|You take the grape, and the gargoyle collects. Your boots are full of rainwater by the bottom of the stair.}\nopt.right.say.2 [d84807] = {是石头做的。石像鬼的玩笑，它等了很久才等到有人上当。|你一拿起它，风就刮了起来，刮了一整夜，把你的窗户摇得哐哐响。|你拿了葡萄，石像鬼也来收账了。走到楼梯底时，你的靴子里灌满了雨水。}\n\n@event gargoyle_passage_parapet_cards\n# en: Cards on the Parapet\ntitle [d8d199] = 护墙上的牌局\n# en: {On the parapet, by lantern-light, {here} is dealing cards for buttons, and crowns for anyone who dares.|{here} has a new deck, and the pot of buttons has grown.|{here} deals you in before you've sat down.} {The wind keeps trying to take the jack.|A pigeon watches the game with too much interest.|{final_year: \"One more for the road,\" {they} say{s}.|The lake below holds the lantern perfectly still.}}\ntext [e4e500] = {护墙上，借着灯光，{here}正以纽扣为注发牌，谁有胆量也可以押克朗。|{here}换了一副新牌，纽扣堆也变大了。|你还没坐下，{here}就给你发了牌。} {风一直想把那张杰克卷走。|一只鸽子对牌局关心得过了头。|{final_year: “最后再来一局。”{they}说。|下方的湖把灯影托得纹丝不动。}}\n# en: Two students playing cards by lantern-light on a high castle parapet, a pot of buttons between them and the lake far below.\nscene [68ef4e] = 两名学生借着灯光在城堡高处的护墙上打牌，中间一堆纽扣，湖在远远的下方。\n# en: Play a hand\nopt.left.label [fc6de9] = 来一手\n# en: {cast_as(here, corvin): {\"Well, look at you,\" Corvin says, paying out. \"Who taught you that? Don't say me.\"|\"Remind me never to owe you money,\" Corvin tells the pigeon, and pays you in buttons and, grudgingly, crowns.|Corvin deals you in, loses, and laughs. \"You're getting good. I'll have to start cheating properly.\"}|{You win the pot, buttons and a few actual crowns.|You bluff with nothing and the wind turns your card over at the right moment. You take the buttons, and the crowns under them, with dignity.|You're dealt in before you sit and win before you've warmed up. The pot is mostly buttons, and some of it is crowns.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [dcb430] = {cast_as(here, corvin): {“哟，瞧瞧你，”科尔文边付账边说，“谁教你的？别说是我。”|“提醒我永远别欠你钱。”科尔文对鸽子说，然后用纽扣付了账，又不情不愿地添了几枚克朗。|科尔文给你发牌，输了，笑起来。“你越来越厉害了。我得开始认真作弊了。”}|{你赢下了那堆纽扣，外加几枚真克朗。|你手里什么都没有却虚张声势，风恰好在对的时候掀开了你的牌。你体面地收下了纽扣，还有压在下面的克朗。|你还没坐下就被发了牌，还没暖手就赢了。赌注大多是纽扣，也有些克朗。}}\n# en: {cast_as(here, corvin): {Corvin scoops the pot and winces. \"Saint, you're the worst liar I love playing.\" He gives you your buttons back, at least.|\"Never bluff a man who owes three people money,\" Corvin says, kindly.|Corvin deals from the bottom and you know it and can't prove it. You lose with a smile, which annoys him.}|{You lose your buttons, then some crowns, then the will to bluff.|The wind takes your best card over the parapet. It was the only one you had.|You lose the hand to a card the pigeon was standing on. The pigeon will not be questioned.}}\nopt.left.say.2 [dfaef2] = {cast_as(here, corvin): {科尔文把赌注一扫而空，龇了龇牙。“圣人，你是我最爱的最烂骗子。”至少他把你的纽扣还给了你。|“别跟一个欠了三个人钱的人诈牌。”科尔文和气地说。|科尔文从牌底发牌，你知道却证明不了。你笑着输了，这让他很恼火。}|{你输光了纽扣，又输了几枚克朗，最后连虚张声势的心气也输了。|风把你最好的那张牌卷过了护墙。那也是你唯一一张好牌。|你输给了一张鸽子一直踩着的牌。鸽子拒绝接受质询。}}\n# en: Watch the lake instead\nopt.right.label [1e5c6c] = 还是看湖吧\n# en: {cast_as(here, corvin): {\"Saint,\" Corvin says, not looking up, \"you'll never be rich.\" He passes you his scarf.|Corvin deals you out without a word and tosses you his scarf when the wind picks up.|Corvin deals you out without asking and hands you his scarf before the wind does. \"Lake's nice tonight,\" he says. It is.}|{You sit with your back to the stone and watch the dark water while the cards slap behind you. It's cold, and peaceful.|You count the lights in the village until the game's over.|You watch the lake hold the lantern still. Behind you, someone wins, and someone else blames the pigeon.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [5bd2f1] = {cast_as(here, corvin): {“圣人，”科尔文头也不抬地说，“你这辈子发不了财。”他把围巾递给了你。|科尔文一言不发地不再给你发牌，风大起来时，把围巾扔给了你。|科尔文没问就不给你发牌了，风还没起就把围巾递给了你。“今晚湖不错，”他说。确实不错。}|{你背靠石墙，看着漆黑的湖水，身后纸牌啪啪作响。很冷，也很安宁。|你数着山下小镇的灯火，一直数到牌局结束。|你看着湖面把灯光托得稳稳的。身后有人赢了，另一个人怪到鸽子头上。}}\n\n@event seventh_tower_stair_count\n# en: The Stair That Changes\ntitle [cf510f] = 会变的楼梯\n# en: {The seventh tower's stair never has the same number of steps twice. Scholars count it to learn how the castle bends; everyone else says it lets you up faster if you don't look.|Back on the stair that won't keep its count. You've started chalking each total on the wall; no two agree.|You've stopped expecting the stair to agree with you. You count anyway; it's a kind of manners.} {Dust lies thick on every step but yours.|The moon follows you from window to window.|{season == \"winter\": The cold comes up through your shoes.|Your candle leans towards the top, like a dog on a lead.}}\ntext [04d107] = {第七塔的楼梯从来不会两次有同样多的台阶。学者们数它，是为了弄懂城堡是怎么弯折的；其他人则说，你不看，它就让你上得更快。|又来到这道数不准的楼梯。你开始用粉笔把每次的总数写在墙上；没有两个是一样的。|你已经不指望楼梯跟你意见一致了。你照数不误，这算一种礼貌。} {每级台阶都积着厚灰，只有你踩过的除外。|月亮从一扇窗跟到另一扇窗。|{season == \"winter\": 寒气从鞋底往上钻。|你的烛火朝塔顶倾斜，像一条被牵着的狗。}}\n# en: A narrow spiral stair inside a dusty stone tower, a single candle climbing it, moonlight falling through slit windows.\nscene [e0f863] = 落满灰尘的石塔里一道狭窄的螺旋楼梯，一支蜡烛向上移动，月光从细窄的窗缝洒进来。\n# en: Count every step\nopt.left.label [08b067] = 一级一级地数\n# en: {You lose count twice and get two answers, both certain, both wrong.|Up, one number; down, one more. You don't stay to find the extra step.|{final_year and flag(counted_seventh_stair): The count matches your very first climb. You can't say why that frightens you.|You count a step that isn't there, and your foot believes you.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [a74ac1] = {你数乱了两次，得出两个答案，都很笃定，都不对。|上楼一个数，下楼多一级。你没留下来找那多出来的一级。|{final_year and flag(counted_seventh_stair): 这次的数字和你第一次爬时一模一样。你说不出为什么这让你害怕。|你数到了一级并不存在的台阶，而你的脚信了。}}\n# en: Climb without counting\nopt.right.label [c06bdf] = 不数，直接爬\n# en: {You climb by feel, in the dark between windows. The stair seems to like that better.|You stop counting and the climb is suddenly short, as if the tower was in a hurry too.|You climb without counting, candle leaning ahead. You're at the top before you've thought about it, and it feels allowed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f4397d] = {你在窗与窗之间的黑暗里摸着爬。楼梯似乎更喜欢这样。|你一不数，路就忽然变短了，好像塔也急着赶路。|你不数台阶，烛火在前面倾着。还没来得及想，你已经到了顶上，感觉像是被允许的。}\n\n@event seventh_tower_dust_room\n# en: Dust and Moonlight\ntitle [17a713] = 尘埃与月光\n# en: {At the top is one room of dust and moonlight, with a star-shaped hollow in the wall. The footprints in the dust aren't yours.|More footprints in the dust; some are yours now.|The room keeps your footprints like a guest book.} {The moon sits in the window as if it lives here.|The hollow in the wall is darker than the night.|A moth beats at the glass from outside, trying to get in.}\ntext [4d4f05] = {塔顶只有一间满是尘埃与月光的房间，墙上有一个星形的凹槽。灰尘里的脚印不是你的。|灰尘里的脚印更多了；有些是你的了。|这间房像本访客簿一样收着你的脚印。} {月亮坐在窗里，好像住在这儿。|墙上的凹槽比夜色还黑。|一只飞蛾在窗外扑打玻璃，想要进来。}\n# en: A bare round room at the top of a tower, thick dust on the floor marked by footprints, moonlight slanting through one window onto a star-shaped hollow in the wall.\nscene [e73e97] = 塔顶一间空荡的圆形房间，地上厚厚的灰尘留着脚印，月光从唯一的窗户斜照在墙上一个星形凹槽上。\n# en: Sit in the moonlight\nopt.left.label [dda8de] = 坐在月光里\n# en: {The light is cold and kind. You sit until your shadow forgets which way to fall.|You sit where the moon is brightest. It feels like being looked at by someone very old and very tired.|You sit with the moth tapping at the glass and the moon in the window. Nothing happens, very deeply, for an hour.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4c9aa0] = {月光冷，却温柔。你一直坐到影子忘了该往哪边倒。|你坐在月光最亮的地方。感觉像被一个非常古老、非常疲倦的人注视着。|你坐着，飞蛾在玻璃上轻叩，月亮在窗里。一个钟头里，什么也没发生，深深地什么也没发生。}\n# en: Sweep away your footprints\nopt.right.label [6070ae] = 抹掉你的脚印\n# en: {You sweep your way out backwards with your sleeve. The dust settles behind you, and then, you'd swear, rearranges itself.|You cough your way to the door. The other footprints you leave alone. They seem to want to stay.|You sweep your prints away and leave the others. By the next visit, yours are back, in the same places.}\nopt.right.say.1 [69ac17] = {你用袖子边退边扫出了门。灰尘在你身后落定，然后——你敢发誓——自己重新排列了一遍。|你一路咳着走到门口。别人的脚印你没动。它们似乎想留下。|你扫掉了自己的脚印，留下了别人的。下次来时，你的又回来了，在原来的位置。}\n\n@event seventh_tower_lake_window\n# en: The Window Over the Lake\ntitle [749eec] = 临湖的窗\n# en: {The tower's one window looks down on the Mirror Lake, and the latch isn't locked. They say if you open it, you can hear the lake.|The latch moves more easily than last time, as if someone else has been practising.|The window. You've stood here often enough that your hand finds the latch before you decide anything.} {The lake is so still it looks painted.|There's a light on the water that isn't the moon's.|{season == \"winter\": The lake is grey and hard.|Mist lies on the water.}}\ntext [a83495] = {塔里唯一的窗俯瞰着镜湖，窗闩没有锁。据说打开它，就能听见湖的声音。|窗闩比上次更好拨了，好像另有人在练习。|这扇窗。你在这里站过太多次，还没拿定主意，手就已经找到了窗闩。} {湖面静得像画上去的。|水面上有一点光，不是月亮的。|{season == \"winter\": 湖面灰而坚硬。|雾躺在水面上。}}\n# en: A small arched window high in a tower, its latch half open, the Mirror Lake lying still and silver far below.\nscene [4ee686] = 高塔上一扇小小的拱窗，窗闩半开，镜湖静静地泛着银光，躺在远远的下方。\n# en: Open it and listen\nopt.left.label [846a0a] = 打开窗听一听\n# en: {You hear water, and under it a sound too low to be wind. You decide it was the pipes, and don't quite believe yourself.|Nothing but wind and water. You stay until you're sure, and you're never quite sure.|You open it without deciding to. The lake is quiet, and then not, and then quiet again, as if it changed its mind about you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5c06d2] = {你听见水声，水声底下还有一种低得不像风的声音。你认定那是水管，自己却不太信。|只有风声和水声。你一直待到确定为止，可你从没真正确定过。|你还没决定，手就把窗打开了。湖很安静，然后不安静，然后又安静了，像是对你改了主意。}\n# en: {The cold comes in and won't leave. For days you hear dripping in dry rooms.|The lake is silent, entirely, the way a room goes silent when you walk in on a conversation.|The window opens on its own before your hand gets there. You shut it fast, and the latch is cold for a week.}\nopt.left.say.2 [aaa95c] = {寒气钻进来就不走了。之后好几天，你在干燥的房间里也听见滴水声。|湖彻底安静了，就像你走进一间屋子时，屋里的谈话戛然而止。|你的手还没碰到，窗就自己开了。你赶紧关上，窗闩冷了一个星期。}\n# en: Leave it latched\nopt.right.label [d532fe] = 让它闩着\n# en: {You leave the latch alone and go down to bed, and lie awake wondering what you didn't hear.|You put your palm flat on the cold glass instead. The lake does nothing. It's very good at that.|You leave it latched and rest your hand on the latch a while. It feels like keeping a promise to someone.}\nopt.right.say.1 [64571c] = {你没碰窗闩，下楼睡觉，然后醒着躺了半夜，想着你没听见的是什么。|你把手掌平贴在冰冷的玻璃上。湖什么也没做。它很擅长这个。|你没打开，只是把手在窗闩上搁了一会儿。感觉像是在对谁守一个诺言。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/clubs_jobs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/clubs_jobs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event duelling_ward_line\n# en: The Ward Line\ntitle [56f9d8] = 护盾阵线\n# en: {Ward drill. Members hold shields in a line while the seniors lob stinging hexes down the hall.|You know by now which senior aims low, and which one apologises.|You could hold a shield in your sleep, and one night you did.} {A dummy at the end of the line has a shield of its own, out of pity.|Someone has chalked a tally of scorched floorboards. It is long.|A first-year holds the shield upside down and does surprisingly well.}\ntext [86fffa] = {护盾演练。社员排成一列举着护盾，高年级生从大厅那头往这边扔刺痛咒。|你早就知道哪位学长专往低处打，哪位打完还会道歉。|你闭着眼都能举盾，有一晚还真在梦里举了一整夜。} {队尾的假人也被分到一面盾，纯属同情。|有人用粉笔记下了烧焦的地板数，那一串很长。|一个一年级生把盾举反了，居然撑得出奇地好。}\n# en: A row of students holding shimmering shields across a scorched hall while seniors lob sparks at them.\nscene [271b31] = 一排学生在焦黑的大厅里举着微光闪烁的护盾，高年级生朝他们抛掷火花。\n# en: Take the front of the line\nopt.left.label [d28df6] = 站到队伍最前面\n# en: {Your shield takes everything and hums about it afterwards.|Hexes splash off your ward like rain off a good hat.|You hold until the bell. {around(lyra): Lyra|The senior on the whistle} writes something down, which is either praise or a list of your faults.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c1da68] = {你的护盾照单全收，事后还嗡嗡响了好一阵。|咒语从你的护界上溅开，像雨水打在一顶好帽子上。|你一直撑到铃响。{around(lyra): 莱拉|吹哨的高年级生}记了点什么，要么是夸奖，要么是你的毛病清单。}\n# en: {A hex finds the gap by your elbow. Your arm fizzes until supper.|Your ward folds like wet paper. The senior apologises, mostly to the floor.|You hold the front half asleep, as usual, and a hex wakes you. Your arm fizzes until supper, and the first-year beside you holds.}\nopt.left.say.2 [4d8fe9] = {一道咒语钻进你手肘边的空隙，整条胳膊麻到晚饭。|你的护盾像湿纸一样折了。那位学长道了歉，主要是对着地板。|你照常半睡半醒地守在前排，一道恶咒把你惊醒了。你的胳膊一直麻到晚饭，旁边那个一年级生却守住了。}\n# en: Hold the back row tonight\nopt.right.label [a28ef7] = 今晚待在后排\n# en: {From the back you watch how the good shields bend instead of break. Nobody watches you.|You spend the drill studying the front row's mistakes, which feels useful and looks like hiding.|Safe at the back, you learn a great deal about the ceiling.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b86875] = {在后排，你看清了好护盾是弯而不折的。没人看你。|你整场都在研究前排的失误，感觉很有用，看起来很像在躲。|在安全的后排，你对天花板有了深入了解。}\n# en: Hold the line with a real ward\nopt.up.label [c704b4] = 用真正的护界守住阵线\n# en: {You raise a proper ward, the one from your first week, and the seniors' hexes slide off it like rain off glass. {around(lyra): Lyra stops the drill to look.|The seniors stop the drill to look.}|Your ward holds the whole line's flank. Your arm aches from the mana, not the hexes.|You raise the real ward without thinking, the way you hold a shield in your sleep. The seniors give up hexing your end of the line.}\nopt.up.say.1 [b916f3] = {你立起一道真正的护界，就是入学第一周学的那种，高年级生的恶咒从上面滑开，像雨水滑过玻璃。{around(lyra): 莱拉停下训练来看。|高年级生们停下训练来看。}|你的护界守住了整条阵线的侧翼。你的胳膊酸痛，是因为法力，不是因为恶咒。|你不假思索就立起了真正的护界，就像你睡着都能举盾一样。高年级生们放弃了朝你这头放恶咒。}\n\n@event duelling_dummy_mending\n# en: Mending the Dummies\ntitle [d3be80] = 缝补假人\n# en: {After practice someone has to mend the dummies, and tonight the rota says it's you.|Mending night again, and the rota has your name on it.|You know every dummy here by its scars. They look at you with something like trust.} {The tall dummy is missing an arm. Someone always takes the arm.|One dummy is now mostly scorch mark.|Someone has painted a new name on a dummy's chest, and a grudge beneath it.}\ntext [556795] = {练习结束后总得有人缝补假人，值日表上今晚写的是你。|又是缝补之夜，值日表上又有你的名字。|这里每个假人身上的伤疤你都认得。它们看你的眼神有点像信任了。} {那个高个子假人少了一条胳膊。总有人把胳膊拿走。|有个假人现在基本只剩焦痕。|有人在一个假人胸口画了新名字，下面还写着一笔旧怨。}\n# en: A student alone in a duelling hall at night, stitching straw back into a battered practice dummy.\nscene [29efd2] = 夜里，一个学生独自坐在决斗厅里，把稻草塞回一个破旧的练习假人。\n# en: Stay late and stitch them up\nopt.left.label [84da41] = 留下来把它们缝好\n# en: {By midnight every dummy is whole and you have straw in places straw should not be.|You sew one dummy's arm on slightly backwards. It looks more dangerous for it.|The hall is quiet, the needle is steady, and for an hour nobody is shouting about footwork.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2293d0] = {到午夜，所有假人都完好如初，你身上也多了几处不该有稻草的地方。|你把一个假人的胳膊缝反了。它看起来反倒更危险了。|大厅很安静，针脚很稳，整整一个钟头没人嚷嚷步法。}\n# en: Leave them for the morning\nopt.right.label [3c93be] = 留到明早再说\n# en: {In the morning the dummies are still in pieces, and so is the seniors' patience.|You slip out early. Someone finds the dummies and puts your name on next week's rota, underlined twice.|You leave them. In the morning the dummy with the new name has been mended by someone else, with your name painted under the grudge.}\nopt.right.say.1 [552491] = {第二天早上，假人们还是一地零件，高年级生们的耐心也是。|你提前溜了。有人发现了这些假人，把你的名字写进下周值日表，还画了两道线。|你没管它们。第二天早上，那个写着新名字的假人被别人补好了，怨气下面还被画上了你的名字。}\n\n@event duelling_refereeing_lyra\n# en: Refereeing Lyra\ntitle [9a9369] = 给莱拉当裁判\n# en: {Officers referee, so tonight you hold the whistle while Lyra duels a second-year.|You know the circle's chalk by heart now, and Lyra's footwork better.|{rel(lyra) >= 12: Lyra no longer asks who is refereeing. She looks for you.|The whistle has a dent in it from some earlier, unrecorded argument.}} {Her last spell landed a breath after the bell.|She stepped out of the circle by a boot's width, and knows it.|Her last hex went in after her opponent had called the yield.} {final_year: One of the last bouts you will call for her.|}\ntext [e5a2df] = {干部要当裁判，于是今晚你拿着哨子，看莱拉和一个二年级生对决。|决斗圈的粉笔线你已烂熟于心，莱拉的步法你更熟。|{rel(lyra) >= 12: 莱拉已经不问谁当裁判了。她直接找你。|哨子上有个凹痕，来自某次没记录在案的争执。}} {她最后一道咒语比铃声晚了一口气。|她踏出圈外一只靴子宽，她自己也知道。|她最后一道恶咒是在对手喊了认输之后才打出去的。} {final_year: 这是你最后几次为她判比赛了。|}\n# en: A student with a whistle watching a noble duellist in a chalk circle, her opponent's shield just failing.\nscene [a5fea0] = 一个拿着哨子的学生注视着圈中的贵族决斗者，她对手的护盾正要崩溃。\n# en: Call it against her\nopt.left.label [dff616] = 判她犯规\n# en: {\"Noted,\" Lyra says, in a voice that could chill wine. Next day her feet are perfect.|Lyra stares at you, then shakes her opponent's hand. Later a cup of tea appears on your bench.|The hall goes silent. Lyra bows to the second-year, very correctly, and to you, slightly less.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f1b129] = {“记下了。”莱拉说，那声音能把酒冰镇。第二天，她的步法无懈可击。|莱拉盯着你看了好一会儿，然后和对手握了手。后来你的长凳上出现了一杯茶。|大厅一片寂静。莱拉向那个二年级生行礼，极其标准；向你行礼，稍逊一些。}\n# en: She would never have forgiven you for letting it stand, and you both know why: you're the one person here who makes her better.\nopt.left.say.2 [940131] = 你要是让它算数，她永远不会原谅你，你们俩都知道为什么：你是这里唯一能让她变得更好的人。\n# en: Let it stand\nopt.right.label [d81aa7] = 维持原判\n# en: {Lyra wins. The second-year looks at you, and you find something interesting about your whistle.|Nobody objects. Lyra does not thank you; if anything, she looks a little disappointed in you.|The bout goes in the book as hers. It sits there all week like a stone in your shoe.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f9ce12] = {莱拉赢了。那个二年级生看向你，你忽然觉得自己的哨子很有意思。|没人反对。莱拉没有谢你，甚至显得对你有点失望。|这场比赛作为她的胜场记进了册子，整整一周都像鞋里的一颗石子。}\n\n@event duelling_running_practice\n# en: Running Practice\ntitle [9e7aed] = 主持练习\n# en: A note is pinned to the hall door in Lyra's sharp hand: \"Called home. You are running practice. Footwork first. Do not let anyone set the ceiling on fire again.\" The whole Circle turns to see what you will say.\ntext [189cd9] = 决斗厅门上钉着一张字条，是莱拉凌厉的笔迹：“家里有事。你来主持练习。先练步法。别再让任何人把天花板点着。”整个决斗社都转过头来，看你要说什么。\n# en: A note pinned to a duelling hall door, a crowd of students waiting inside in chalk circles.\nscene [6d7864] = 决斗厅门上钉着一张字条，门内一群学生站在粉笔圈里等着。\n# en: Run it exactly her way\nopt.left.label [b06e03] = 完全照她的方式来\n# en: Footwork, then shields, then footwork again. Nobody sets the ceiling on fire. Lyra reads your practice log on her return and says \"adequate\", then reads it again when she thinks you've gone.\nopt.left.say.1 [a1b74e] = 步法，护盾，再练步法。没人把天花板点着。莱拉回来读了你的练习记录，说了句“还行”，又趁她以为你走了的时候读了一遍。\n# en: Run it your own way\nopt.right.label [68c6ad] = 按你自己的方式来\n# en: You turn the footwork drill into a game of tag with stinging hexes. The ceiling only smoulders. A week later Lyra runs the same game and calls it her idea, which is the highest praise she has.\nopt.right.say.1 [b32c5f] = 你把步法练习改成了带刺痛咒的捉人游戏。天花板只是冒了点烟。一周后莱拉照搬这个游戏，说是她自己的主意——这是她能给出的最高赞美。\n\n@event lumen_ledger_letters_box\n# en: The Letters Box\ntitle [4a9d28] = 读者来信箱\n# en: {The letters box is full. Stringers sort it, Nessa says, having no dignity yet to lose.|Sorting day. A regular correspondent has started numbering their letters.|Under the stairs the box has burst its lid. You know the regulars by their handwriting now.} {Porridge complaints, a poem about the lake and a sock.|A furious letter about the crossword, in a professor's hand.|An anonymous tip in lemon juice, noticed only because it smells nice.}\ntext [547c71] = {读者来信箱满了。内莎说，来信由见习记者来分，因为他们还没有尊严可丢。|分信日。有位常客开始给自己的信编号了。|楼梯下，信箱被撑开了盖。如今你光看笔迹就认得出那些常客。} {抱怨粥的信、一首写湖的诗，还有一只袜子。|一封痛骂填字游戏的信，显然是某位教授的笔迹。|一条用柠檬汁写的匿名线索，你注意到它只因为它闻起来很香。}\n# en: A battered wooden box under a library staircase, overflowing with folded letters and one sock.\nscene [83935d] = 图书馆楼梯下一只破旧的木箱，塞满了折好的信，还有一只袜子。\n# en: Print the angriest letter\nopt.left.label [321002] = 刊登最愤怒的那封\n# en: {It runs on page four. By breakfast several people are angry about the angry letter, which Nessa calls \"engagement\".|The letter runs. Its author glares at you over the porridge, as if you had written it.|You print the lemon-juice tip, warmed over a candle. Half the castle holds the page to the fire. Nessa calls it the best-selling issue of term.}\nopt.left.say.1 [60d770] = {它登在第四版。到早饭时，已经有好几个人对这封愤怒的信感到愤怒，内莎管这叫“读者互动”。|信登出去了。写信人在早餐桌上瞪着你，好像信是你写的。|你把那条柠檬汁写的线报放在蜡烛上烘出字来，登了出去。半个城堡的人都把那一页凑到火边烤。内莎说这是本学期卖得最好的一期。}\n# en: Answer them all by hand\nopt.right.label [931b86] = 逐一亲笔回信\n# en: {You write back to every one of them, even the ones that weren't really letters. Your hand cramps; somewhere in the castle a lonely first-year gets a reply.|A pile of replies, all polite. Nessa calls it \"a waste of a perfectly good grudge\", then borrows your phrasing.|You write back to every regular, by number. The numbering correspondent replies with a number of their own, and a thank-you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c7409a] = {你给每一封都回了信，连那些算不上信的也回了。手抽筋了，但城堡某处，一个孤单的一年级生收到了回信。|一摞回信，封封客气。内莎说这是“浪费了一份上好的怨气”，然后借用了你的措辞。|你按编号给每位常客回了信。那位编号的通信者回了一封，也编了号，还附上一句谢谢。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_courtyard_quotes\n# en: A Word from the Courtyard\ntitle [2bef78] = 庭院之声\n# en: {Every issue needs a word from the courtyard. One question, many opinions.|Word has got round the courtyard that the Ledger is asking questions.|Half the courtyard now takes the long way round your notebook.} This week: {is the soup getting thinner?|who will win the Spring Tournament?|is the lake louder lately?} {season == \"winter\": Everyone is hurrying indoors.|{Some second-years pretend not to see you.|A passing professor looks very quotable.|A prefect makes a detour to avoid you, which is a quote of a sort.}}\ntext [8f39dd] = {每期报纸都要一段“庭院之声”。一个问题，众说纷纭。|庭院里已经传开了，说《流明纪事报》在到处提问。|如今半个庭院见了你的本子都绕道走。} 本周的问题是{汤是不是越来越稀了？|谁会赢得春季锦标赛？|湖最近是不是更吵了？} {season == \"winter\": 人人都急着往屋里走。|{几个二年级生假装没看见你。|一位路过的教授看上去很值得引用。|一位级长为了躲你特意绕了路，这也算是一句引语。}}\n# en: A student with a notebook stopping passers-by in a busy courtyard.\nscene [3bd888] = 一个拿着笔记本的学生在热闹的庭院里拦住路人。\n# en: Stop strangers and ask\nopt.left.label [91c2da] = 拦住陌生人提问\n# en: {A gardener, a prefect and a very small first-year give you the best quotes of the term. The first-year's is unprintable, and perfect.|You get a quote so good Nessa moves it to the front page and pretends it was her idea to ask.|You ask the question to the people who take the long way round. They answer, to get rid of you, and the answers are the best yet.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b843d5] = {一位园丁、一位级长和一个特别小的一年级生给了你本学期最好的引语。那个一年级生的没法登，但完美。|你拿到一句绝妙的引语，内莎把它挪上头版，还假装是她想到要去问的。|你专问那些绕远路躲你的人。他们为了打发你才回答，答案却是到目前为止最好的。}\n# en: {Everyone is busy, cold or suddenly deaf. You come back with \"no comment\" in five different tones of voice.|A fourth-year answers at length, entirely about himself.|Everyone takes the long way round you. You get one quote, from the prefect, and it is \"No.\"}\nopt.left.say.2 [9bebc2] = {人人都忙、都冷、或者突然聋了。你带回了五种语气的“无可奉告”。|一个四年级生滔滔不绝地回答，内容全是他自己。|人人都绕开你走。你只拿到一句引语，来自那位级长，内容是“不”。}\n# en: Just quote your friends\nopt.right.label [1f9ee3] = 直接引用朋友的话\n# en: {Every opinion comes from someone you had lunch with. Nessa reads them and says, \"Interesting. Your friends are very consistent.\"|It's quick. It's also the courtyard as represented by one person in several scarves.|You quote your friends again. Nessa runs it under a headline about the same four people, and it's somehow popular.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a242a3] = {每条意见都来自和你一起吃午饭的人。内莎读完说：“有意思。你的朋友们观点很统一。”|又快又省事。只是整个庭院的意见，都由同一个人围着几条不同的围巾代表了。|你又引用了你的朋友。内莎给它配了个关于“还是那四个人”的标题，不知怎么还挺受欢迎。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_red_ink\n# en: Red Ink\ntitle [025680] = 红墨水\n# en: {Nessa edits your story with a red quill and no mercy.|Nessa has your draft, and this time the red quill hesitates once before it moves.|Edit night. You have learned to bring two copies, one for Nessa to kill.} \"{Kill your darlings. Bury them. Page six.|Lead's buried. Dig it up. Headline it.|Good. Too long. Shorter. Shorter than that.}\" She has cut your favourite paragraph.\ntext [4f9392] = {内莎用一支红羽毛笔毫不留情地改你的稿子。|内莎拿到了你的草稿，这一次，红笔落下前迟疑了一下。|改稿之夜。你学会了带两份稿子，一份留给内莎去杀。} “{杀掉你的心头好。埋了。第六版。|导语埋太深了。挖出来。做标题。|好。太长。再短。比那还短。}”她删掉了你最喜欢的那一段。\n# en: A cramped office papered with old front pages, an editor slashing a manuscript with a red quill.\nscene [f3d694] = 贴满旧头版的狭小办公室里，一位编辑正用红羽毛笔大刀阔斧地删改稿件。\n# en: Fight for the paragraph\nopt.left.label [528d97] = 为那一段据理力争\n# en: {Nessa reads it again, out loud, and puts it back. \"Fine. Stays. Don't get used to it.\"|You win the paragraph. Nessa makes you cut a different one, as a tax.|You fight for it, and it gets shorter, and shorter than that, and it stays. Nessa calls it \"a paragraph now\".}\nopt.left.say.1 [6f10cf] = {内莎把那段大声念了一遍，又放了回去。“行。留着。别习惯。”|你保住了那一段。内莎让你删掉另一段，当作税。|你为它争，它越来越短，短了又短，但留下了。内莎说“这才算一段”。}\n# en: {\"No,\" says Nessa, and that is the whole argument.|She listens to your case with great interest, then cuts the paragraph anyway, plus the one you defended it with.|You fight with your second copy ready. Nessa cuts the paragraph from both.}\nopt.left.say.2 [76aa1d] = {“不行。”内莎说，这就是全部论证。|她兴致勃勃地听完你的辩护，然后照删不误，连你用来辩护的那段也删了。|你备着第二份稿子来争。内莎把两份里的那段都删了。}\n# en: Let her cut it\nopt.right.label [8f19c2] = 让她删\n# en: {It reads better without it. You will never forgive her.|The story runs, tighter and less yours. Nessa scribbles \"good\" in the margin, a word Nessa hands out like gold.|You let her cut it, and slip it into next week's story instead. She notices, and lets it stand.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ede85e] = {删了确实更好。你永远不会原谅她。|稿子登了，更紧凑，也更不像你的。内莎在页边写了个“好”，这个字她发得像金子一样省。|你让她删了，转手塞进了下周的稿子。她发现了，也就让它留着了。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_front_page\n# en: Front Page\ntitle [c50df3] = 头版\n# en: The press groans like a dragon and spits out the dawn edition. Your story is on the front page, under your own name. Nessa hands you a tack without looking up. \"Wall,\" she says. \"That's where front pages go.\"\ntext [911ced] = 印刷机像龙一样呻吟着，吐出了黎明版。你的报道登上头版，署着你自己的名字。内莎头也不抬地递给你一枚图钉。“墙上，”她说，“头版都钉那儿。”\n# en: A creaking printing press at dawn spitting out newspapers, a student holding the first copy up to the light.\nscene [632373] = 黎明时分，吱嘎作响的印刷机吐出报纸，一个学生把第一份举向光亮处。\n# en: Pin it to the wall yourself\nopt.left.label [ea7224] = 亲手把它钉上墙\n# en: You find a gap between a scandal from before you were born and a recipe someone printed by mistake. It looks small up there, and permanent. Nessa pretends not to watch you step back and admire it.\nopt.left.say.1 [1fa814] = 你在一桩你出生前的丑闻和一份误印的菜谱之间找到一处空隙。它在墙上显得很小，也很长久。内莎假装没看见你退后一步欣赏它。\n# en: Send the first copy home\nopt.right.label [a16d32] = 把第一份寄回家\n# en: You post it home with a note that says nothing about how long it took. The reply comes within the week: they have framed it, slightly crooked, over the kitchen fire.\nopt.right.say.1 [0f1990] = 你把它寄回家，附的字条里只字未提这花了多久。一周之内回信就到了：家里把它装了框，挂在厨房炉火上方，有点歪。\n\n@event verdant_watering_rota\n# en: The Watering Rota\ntitle [c1243f] = 浇水值日表\n# en: {You're on the watering rota, a chart the size of a door.|The ferns have learned your footsteps and lean in.|You no longer need the chart, luckily, since a fern ate it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost outside the glass, drips down your collar inside.|{season == \"summer\": It's so hot the cacti look smug.|{The biting ferns are in a mood.|Something in the far bed grew overnight.|The moonflowers do nothing, beautifully, as they will for years.}}}\ntext [3987d3] = {你上了浇水值日表，那是一张门板大的图表。|蕨类已经认得你的脚步，纷纷凑过来。|你已经不需要那张表了，幸好，因为它被一株蕨吃了。} {season == \"winter\": 玻璃外结着霜，玻璃内的水滴顺着你的领口往下流。|{season == \"summer\": 热得连仙人掌都一脸得意。|{咬人蕨今天心情不好。|远处苗床里有东西一夜之间长高了。|月光花什么也不做，美美地，接下来许多年都会这样。}}}\n# en: A student with a watering can and a huge chart in a steaming greenhouse, ferns leaning in hungrily.\nscene [535c02] = 热气蒸腾的温室里，一个学生拿着水壶和一张巨大的图表，几株蕨饥渴地凑过来。\n# en: Follow the chart exactly\nopt.left.label [577433] = 严格按表来\n# en: {Warm rainwater for the ferns, cold tea for the moss, nothing at all for the cactus that bites. It takes all afternoon.|By the chart, to the drop. {around(tobias): Tobias says the plants look \"right pleased\", and they do, in a leafy way.|The plants look pleased, in a leafy way.}|You follow the chart from memory, since the fern ate it. You're right to the drop, and the fern looks unrepentant.}\nopt.left.say.1 [02d5da] = {给蕨浇温雨水，给苔藓浇冷茶，那株咬人的仙人掌一滴也不给。整整花了一下午。|按表来，一滴不差。{around(tobias): 托比亚斯说植物们看着“可高兴了”，确实，以叶子的方式。|植物们看着很高兴，以叶子的方式。}|你凭记忆照着那张表浇，因为表被蕨吃了。一滴不差，那株蕨毫无悔意。}\n# en: Water by feel\nopt.right.label [36cb6b] = 凭感觉浇\n# en: {You read the leaves instead of the chart. Everything perks up, and one fern makes a sound suspiciously like purring.|You guess right on every bed. {around(tobias): Tobias asks how you knew. You honestly don't.|You couldn't say how you knew.}|You water by feel and talk to the moonflowers. They still do nothing, beautifully, and the rest of the house thrives.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5cac1b] = {你看叶子而不看表。一切都精神起来了，有一株蕨发出的声音很像呼噜。|每一床你都猜对了。{around(tobias): 托比亚斯问你怎么知道的。你真的不知道。|你自己也说不清是怎么知道的。}|你凭感觉浇水，还跟月光花说话。它们依然美丽地什么都不做，温室其余的植物倒是长得很旺。}\n# en: {You drown a pot of sundew, and a biting fern takes a polite nip of your thumb.|The moss wanted tea, not water. It sulks visibly, and the cactus you reach past bites.|You water the moonflowers by feel and drown them by feel. They do nothing, as usual, but wetly.}\nopt.right.say.2 [4dd1ee] = {你淹了一盆茅膏菜，一株咬人蕨还客客气气地啃了你的拇指一口。|苔藓要的是茶，不是水。它明显在生闷气，你伸手越过的那株仙人掌还咬了你一口。|你凭感觉给月光花浇水，也凭感觉把它们淹了。它们照旧什么都不做，只是湿漉漉的。}\n\n@event verdant_repotting_day\n# en: Repotting Day\ntitle [5b9bc1] = 换盆日\n# en: {Repotting day. The mandrakes have outgrown their pots and have opinions about it.|Someone always takes the good earmuffs, so you bring your own now.|You know which mandrakes kick, and which only pretend to.} {Tobias's own mandrake watches from a shelf in a knitted hat, superior.|A seed tray of something blue has started to hum.|{season == \"spring\": Everything is trying to grow at once.|The glass is fogged and the benches crowded.}}\ntext [56f63b] = {换盆日。曼德拉草长得比盆大了，而且对此很有意见。|好耳罩总被人抢走，所以你现在自己带一副。|你知道哪些曼德拉草真会踢人，哪些只是装装样子。} {托比亚斯自己的那株曼德拉草戴着针织帽在架子上旁观，一脸高傲。|一盘蓝色的幼苗开始嗡嗡作响。|{season == \"spring\": 一切都在同时拼命生长。|玻璃上起了雾，苗床挤得满满当当。}}\n# en: Students in earmuffs wrestling squalling mandrakes into bigger pots on a crowded greenhouse bench.\nscene [24e9d7] = 戴耳罩的学生们在拥挤的温室长桌上，把尖叫的曼德拉草塞进更大的花盆。\n# en: Take on the mandrakes\nopt.left.label [4dbb5a] = 去对付曼德拉草\n# en: {One mandrake has rooted into the bench itself. It takes a trowel, a lullaby and both hands to talk it out.|Mandrake after mandrake, each into a bigger pot. Your ears ring all the way to supper.|The biggest one goes in headfirst and comes out, somehow, the right way up. You are not sure who won.}\nopt.left.say.1 [20e693] = {有一株曼德拉草把根扎进了长桌里。你用了一把小铲、一首摇篮曲和两只手，才把它劝出来。|一株接一株，全换进了大盆。你的耳朵一直响到晚饭。|最大的那株头朝下进了盆，不知怎么又正着出来了。你说不清是谁赢了。}\n# en: Take the quiet seed trays\nopt.right.label [62e44f] = 去弄安静的育苗盘\n# en: {Hundreds of seedlings, pricked out one by one. Your back will remember this tomorrow.|The seed trays are peaceful and endless. By dusk you have names for most of them.|You do the seed trays with your own earmuffs on, for the peace. One tray hums, and you hum back.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5af201] = {几百株幼苗，一株一株地分栽。你的背明天会记住这件事。|育苗盘安静又没有尽头。到黄昏，你给大部分幼苗都起了名字。|你戴着自己的耳罩做育苗盘，图个清静。有一盘在哼歌，你也哼回去。}\n\n@event verdant_tobias_cuttings\n# en: The Cuttings Question\ntitle [95ea7f] = 插条之难\n# en: {The benches are full and someone must choose which cuttings stay. Tobias can't. \"It's like choosing between goats,\" he says.|Bench day. Tobias hovers over a tray of cuttings, apologising to them in advance and glancing at you.|Tobias hands you the tray before he can change his mind. He is grateful in a way that looks like grief.} {season == \"autumn\": The frost will take the weakest soon anyway.|}\ntext [964812] = {苗床满了，得有人决定留下哪些插条。托比亚斯做不到。“这就像在山羊里挑一只。”他说。|整理苗床的日子。托比亚斯守着一盘插条，提前向它们道歉，一边偷偷看你。|托比亚斯赶在自己反悔之前把盘子递给你。他的感激看起来很像悲伤。} {season == \"autumn\": 反正霜冻很快就会带走最弱的那些。|}\n# en: A big gentle student cradling a tray of leggy cuttings, eyeing a full compost heap with dread.\nscene [9813c9] = 一个高大温和的学生抱着一盘细长的插条，忧心忡忡地看着堆满的堆肥堆。\n# en: Choose, and compost the rest\nopt.left.label [0d0865] = 挑好，其余堆肥\n# en: {You keep the strongest and compost the rest. Tobias says \"fair's fair\", then goes very quiet over the heap.|The benches breathe again. Tobias thanks you, then tells you a long story about a goat he couldn't sell. You understand it is about the cuttings.|You choose quickly, so Tobias doesn't have to watch. He takes the compost bucket himself, and you let him.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bd94fd] = {你留下最壮的，其余的堆了肥。托比亚斯说“公平就是公平”，然后对着肥堆沉默了很久。|苗床终于松了口气。托比亚斯谢了你，又讲了个关于一只卖不掉的山羊的长故事。你明白，他说的是插条。|你选得很快，好让托比亚斯不用在旁边看。他自己提起了堆肥桶，你由他去。}\n# en: Find room for every one\nopt.right.label [31bf75] = 给每一株都找地方\n# en: {You find room on windowsills, under benches and in a teacup. Nothing grows well, but everything grows. Tobias laughs from the belly.|\"Ma always said a crowded field still feeds you,\" Tobias says, beaming. The benches groan. So does Old Maud, when she sees.|You find room again, on the last windowsill nobody had thought of. Tobias names that one after you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [885501] = {窗台上、长桌下、一只茶杯里，你都找到了地方。哪株都长得不太好，但哪株都在长。托比亚斯笑得肚子直颤。|“俺娘说，挤一挤的地也照样养人。”托比亚斯笑得合不拢嘴。苗床在呻吟。老莫德看见了，也在呻吟。|你又找到了地方，在一个谁都没想到的窗台上。托比亚斯用你的名字给那一株起了名。}\n\n@event verdant_greenhouse_keys\n# en: The Greenhouse Keys\ntitle [5b68d3] = 温室钥匙\n# en: At dusk Tobias holds out the iron key ring, warm from his pocket. \"Ma says you give the keys to whoever shuts the gate without being asked,\" he says. \"You always shut the gate.\" From now on, opening up and locking up are yours.\ntext [f2975e] = 黄昏时，托比亚斯递过来那串铁钥匙，还带着他口袋里的温度。“俺娘说，钥匙要交给不用人提醒就会关门的人，”他说，“你每次都关门。”从今往后，开门和锁门都归你了。\n# en: A heavy ring of old iron keys handed over in a steaming glasshouse at dusk.\nscene [0698f9] = 黄昏时热气蒸腾的玻璃温室里，一串沉重的旧铁钥匙被交到另一个人手中。\n# en: Open up at dawn\nopt.left.label [75086f] = 黎明时开门\n# en: Dawn, the glass silver with dew, every plant turning to the light you let in. You had never noticed they all turn at once. For a moment you are the only person in the world.\nopt.left.say.1 [186b94] = 黎明，玻璃上挂着银色的露水，每株植物都转向你放进来的光。你从没注意过它们是同时转过去的。那一刻，世上仿佛只有你一个人。\n# en: Walk the night round with a lamp\nopt.right.label [4c41e6] = 提灯巡夜\n# en: You walk the benches with a lamp, checking latches and saying goodnight, because Tobias does and it feels rude not to. Something in the far bed says it back. You decide not to mention it.\nopt.right.say.1 [03e898] = 你提着灯沿苗床走一圈，检查门闩，道声晚安，因为托比亚斯就这么做，不说好像不太礼貌。远处苗床里有什么东西也回了一句。你决定不提这事。\n\n@event cartographers_survey_run\n# en: A Survey Run\ntitle [4bf18f] = 测绘之行\n# en: {Initiates do the pacing. Corvin hands you chalk and a passage to measure before the castle changes its mind.|Your chalk is a stub now, so you mark the turns with notches.|Your stride is a unit of measurement on the master map now, and the castle seems to know it.} {The passage smells of oranges, which nobody can explain.|Behind the wall, a clock ticks that no one has ever found.|Your candle leans, very slightly, toward the left-hand turning.}\ntext [85a385] = {步测是新人的活。科尔文递给你粉笔和一条要量的通道，得赶在城堡改主意之前量完。|你的粉笔只剩一小截了，于是你改用刻痕标记拐角。|你的步幅如今已是总图上的计量单位，城堡似乎也知道。} {通道里有股橘子味，谁也解释不了。|墙后有一座钟在走，从来没人找到过它。|你的蜡烛火苗微微偏向左边的岔口。}\n# en: A student pacing a dark stone passage by candlelight, chalking marks on the wall.\nscene [04b918] = 一个学生借着烛光在黑暗的石砌通道里步测，在墙上做粉笔记号。\n# en: Pace it properly\nopt.left.label [6eb50c] = 老老实实步测\n# en: {Every turning chalked and counted. The passage is exactly where it was last month, which on the master map counts as a scandal.|The passage is two strides longer from this end than from the other. Corvin is delighted.|You pace it with the stride the map uses. The left turning, where your candle leans, wasn't there last week. Corvin inks it in.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3a9fc5] = {每个拐角都标好数清。通道和上个月一模一样，这在总图上算得上丑闻。|这条通道从这头量比从那头量长两步。科尔文乐坏了。|你用总图采用的那种步幅去量。你的蜡烛偏向的那个左拐口，上周还不存在。科尔文把它画了上去。}\n# en: {Your chalk marks lead neatly into a wall that wasn't there when you started.|You lose count somewhere in the dark and have to admit it, which in this club is rarer than a map.|You follow the leaning candle into the left-hand turning and come out a floor below, with no stairs in between.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0f3a54] = {你的粉笔记号整整齐齐地通进一堵你开始时还不存在的墙。|你在黑暗里数乱了，只好承认，这在本社比地图还稀罕。|你跟着倾斜的烛火走进左边的岔道，出来时到了下面一层，中间没有楼梯。}\n# en: Copy last month's figures\nopt.right.label [47f515] = 抄上个月的数\n# en: {You copy the old figures and sit in an alcove eating toffee. The castle, as if offended, moves the passage a foot.|The figures match last month's exactly. Corvin looks at them, then at you. \"Forger,\" he says, approvingly.|You copy the figures with your own stride as the unit. The castle, offended, changes your stride.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1ae24e] = {你抄了旧数据，坐在壁龛里吃太妃糖。城堡像是被冒犯了，把通道挪了一尺。|数据和上个月分毫不差。科尔文看看数据，又看看你。“伪造犯。”他说，语气里带着赞许。|你用自己的步幅作单位抄了数据。城堡被冒犯了，改了你的步幅。}\n\n@event cartographers_lantern_patrol\n# en: The Prefect's Lantern\ntitle [709761] = 级长的提灯\n# en: {Every expedition has the moment a lantern comes round the corner. Tonight's has a red sash behind it.|A lantern, a red sash, footsteps. You know this part.|You can tell the prefects on patrol apart by their footsteps now.} {The alcove beside you is just big enough for one.|A loose flagstone ahead clanks if you step on it.|The tapestry here already has feet showing under it, and they aren't yours.}\ntext [b69280] = {每次探险都有那么一刻，一盏提灯从拐角绕过来。今晚的提灯后面是一条红色绶带。|提灯、红绶带、脚步声。这一段你熟。|你现在光听脚步就分得出是哪位级长在巡夜。} {你身边的壁龛刚好只够藏一个人。|前面有块松动的石板，一踩就响。|这里的挂毯底下已经露出了一双脚，不是你的。}\n# en: Students pressed into a stone alcove as a lantern's glow sweeps along a dark corridor.\nscene [0eba4e] = 几个学生紧贴在石砌壁龛里，提灯的光扫过黑暗的走廊。\n# en: Freeze in the shadows\nopt.left.label [df6ae3] = 在阴影里一动不动\n# en: {The light slides over you like water off a duck. The footsteps go on.|You don't breathe for a very long time. The prefect sneezes, apologises to nobody and walks on.|You freeze beside the tapestry with the feet. The prefect sees the feet, not yours, and someone else spends Saturday scrubbing pans.}\nopt.left.say.1 [47e514] = {灯光从你身上滑过，像水从鸭子背上流走。脚步声继续往前。|你很久很久没敢呼吸。那位级长打了个喷嚏，不知向谁道了歉，走了。|你在那块露着脚的挂毯边僵住了。级长看见的是那双脚，不是你的，于是刷锅的星期六归了别人。}\n# en: {The lantern stops on your face. You spend Saturday scrubbing pans and inventing a reason you were inside a wall.|You talk your way out with a story about a lost cat. It costs you the night's mapping and most of your dignity.|The feet under the tapestry turn out to be the prefect's own. You both have some explaining to do.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3c0370] = {灯光停在你脸上。你的星期六用来刷锅，顺便编一个你为什么在墙里的理由。|你编了个找丢失的猫的故事脱了身。代价是一晚的测绘和大半的尊严。|挂毯下那双脚原来是级长自己的。你们俩都有得解释了。}\n# en: Go back the long way\nopt.right.label [457126] = 绕远路回去\n# en: {The long way is four flights of freezing stairs. You learn every one of them by heart.|You double back through the kitchens and arrive late, cold and unseen. Corvin raises a mug of milk he certainly didn't pay for.|You know these footsteps. You take the long way at exactly the prefect's pace, one corridor behind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [299511] = {远路是四段冰冷的楼梯。你把每一级都记在了心里。|你从厨房绕回去，到得又晚又冷，但没人看见。科尔文举起一杯他肯定没付钱的牛奶。|你认得这脚步声。你走远路，步子和那位级长一模一样，始终落后一条走廊。}\n\n@event cartographers_inking_night\n# en: Inking Night\ntitle [e28233] = 上墨之夜\n# en: {Mappers ink the master map. Corvin unrolls it, silver lines shifting like breath, and passes you the pen.|Inking night. The map has redrawn a stair since your last visit, out of spite.|The map has started leaving a blank wherever you hesitate.} {\"Leave the one behind the kitchens off,\" he says. \"Not for long. For me.\"|\"The stair under the clock. Don't draw it. Don't ask why.\"|He taps a corridor. \"That one stays off the map. Humour me.\"}\ntext [7d989b] = {绘图员负责给总图上墨。科尔文展开地图，银色线条如呼吸般起伏，把笔递给你。|上墨之夜。自你上次来过，地图重画了一段楼梯，纯属赌气。|地图开始在你每一处犹豫的地方留下空白。} {“厨房后面那条别画上去，”他说，“不会太久。算帮我。”|“钟底下那段楼梯。别画。别问为什么。”|他点了点一条走廊。“这条不上图。就当迁就我。”}\n# en: Two students bent over a huge map drawn in silver ink that shifts as they watch, in a hidden candlelit room.\nscene [172414] = 隐秘的烛光小屋里，两个学生俯身在一张用银墨绘成、边看边变的巨大地图上。\n# en: Ink it anyway\nopt.left.label [f376f9] = 照样画上去\n# en: {You draw it, line by honest line. Corvin watches, then laughs, a little too late. \"Saint,\" he says, without the usual smile.|It goes on the map. Corvin is quiet for the rest of the night, which on him is very loud.|You ink it, and the map leaves a blank there anyway, as if it agreed with Corvin. He looks at you and says nothing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b28752] = {你一笔一画老老实实地画了。科尔文看着，然后笑了，笑得晚了一点。“圣人。”他说，没有平常那种笑意。|它上了图。科尔文一整晚都没说话，这在他身上响亮得很。|你画上了，可地图还是在那里留出一块空白，好像它站在科尔文那边。他看着你，什么也没说。}\n# en: Leave it off for him\nopt.right.label [1f8fe9] = 为他留白\n# en: {You leave a careful blank. Corvin squeezes your shoulder and doesn't make a joke, which is how you know it matters.|The map gets a gap and Corvin keeps his secret. You wonder, later, what it's for.|You leave the corridor off. The map closes over the gap so smoothly you'd never know. Neither would anyone else.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9ac197] = {你小心地留出一块空白。科尔文捏了捏你的肩膀，没开玩笑，你就知道这事要紧。|地图多了块空白，科尔文守住了他的秘密。后来你想，那到底是为了什么。|你没画那条走廊。地图把空隙合得严丝合缝，谁也看不出来，别人也不会。}\n\n@event cartographers_silver_pen\n# en: A Pen of Your Own\ntitle [e2f8fb] = 你自己的笔\n# en: Corvin presses a pen into your hand: silver nib, black barrel, warm from his pocket. \"Mappers carry their own,\" he says. \"And the first passage you find, you name. Club rule. I made it up, but it's a rule.\" Tonight you found one.\ntext [4a3c7e] = 科尔文把一支笔按进你手里：银笔尖，黑笔杆，还带着他口袋里的温度。“绘图员都带自己的笔，”他说，“而且你找到的第一条通道，由你命名。社规。我编的，但它是社规。”今晚你找到了一条。\n# en: A silver-nibbed pen laid on a folded map in a candlelit stone alcove.\nscene [7722b8] = 烛光摇曳的石砌壁龛里，一支银尖钢笔放在一张折好的地图上。\n# en: Name it after yourself\nopt.left.label [080f1a] = 以自己的名字命名\n# en: Your name goes on the master map in your own silver hand, at the mouth of a passage that smells faintly of rain. The ink shivers, then settles, as if the castle had agreed.\nopt.left.say.1 [6ddc6c] = 你用自己的银墨把名字写在总图上，就在一条隐隐有雨味的通道口。墨迹颤了颤，又安定下来，好像城堡同意了。\n# en: Name it for a club joke\nopt.right.label [cf8658] = 用社里的笑话命名\n# en: You name it after the night's worst joke. Corvin laughs so hard a prefect two floors down stops to listen. \"Keeper material,\" he whispers, and for once he doesn't laugh after.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bb20d] = 你用当晚最烂的笑话给它命名。科尔文笑得太厉害，两层楼下的级长都停下来听。“守图人的料。”他低声说，这一次他说完没有笑。\n\n@event library_assistant_wandering_shelves\n# en: The Wandering Section\ntitle [ed4770] = 出走的书区\n# en: {The shelves rearranged themselves overnight. History is missing, and a queue is forming for it.|The shelves have wandered, this time the {poetry|herbals|atlases}. You have brought string to mark the way.|The head librarian simply hands you the catalogue now and points upward.} {Footnote sits where the section used to be.|A first-year swears she saw it go upstairs.|There is a faint trail in the dust, as if something heavy had tiptoed.}\ntext [c7c525] = {书架一夜之间自己挪了位置。历史区不见了，找它的人排起了队。|书架又出走了，这回是{诗歌区|草药区|地图集区}。你带了根绳子来标路。|图书馆馆长现在直接把目录递给你，再往上一指。} {脚注坐在那个书区原来的位置上。|一个一年级生发誓说她看见它上楼了。|灰尘里有一道淡淡的痕迹，好像有什么重东西踮着脚走过。}\n# en: A library aisle with an empty gap where a whole bookcase should be, a student holding a catalogue.\nscene [e42286] = 图书馆过道里本该立着一整排书架的地方空空如也，一个学生拿着目录。\n# en: Hunt it down by catalogue\nopt.left.label [8c8f5d] = 按目录把它找回来\n# en: {You find it on the fifth floor, wedged between the cookery books, looking innocent.|You track it by its shelfmarks to a landing that wasn't there yesterday. The queue cheers, quietly, because it's a library.|You follow the tiptoe trail to the atlases, three floors up. They are pretending to be geography.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bc8194] = {你在五楼找到它，夹在烹饪书中间，一脸无辜。|你按索书号追到一个昨天还不存在的楼梯平台。排队的人欢呼了，小声地，毕竟这是图书馆。|你顺着那串踮脚的痕迹找到了地图集，在三层楼上。它们正假装自己是地理书。}\n# en: {You search until closing. In the morning the section is back where it started, faintly smug.|You find the wrong section, twice. The queue disperses with sighs you will hear in your sleep.|You follow the trail in the dust to a blank wall. The trail continues on the other side, presumably.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3c2d26] = {你一直找到闭馆。第二天早上，那个书区回到了原处，隐隐有点得意。|你两次找错了书区。排队的人叹着气散了，那叹气声你在梦里都听得见。|你顺着灰尘里的痕迹走到一堵空墙前。痕迹大概在墙的另一边继续。}\n# en: Put up a sign and wait\nopt.right.label [f19929] = 贴个告示等它回来\n# en: {\"Back shortly,\" says your sign. The section returns in the afternoon. The queue does not.|You sit at the desk and wait. A student asks whether the sign means you or the books.|You put up the sign and the catalogue, open at the right page. The section comes back to see what you wrote about it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d713c9] = {你的告示写着“马上回来”。书区下午回来了，排队的人没有。|你坐在柜台后等。一个学生问，告示说的是你还是那些书。|你挂上告示，再把目录翻到对的那一页摆着。那个书区回来，想看看你写了它什么。}\n\n@event library_assistant_hush_round\n# en: The Hush Round\ntitle [bd75f0] = 肃静巡视\n# en: {Hush round. One lap of the reading room, a lamp, a finger to your lips.|The reading room has learned the sound of your lamp. Whispers stop a table ahead of you.|The regulars hear your step and straighten up before you reach them.} {A table of second-years is giggling.|An old scholar snores on his notes, in a scholarly key.|Somebody's quill is scratching so loudly it echoes.} {final_year: Soon this will be someone else's lap.|}\ntext [88e9ee] = {肃静巡视。在阅览室走一圈，一盏灯，一根竖在唇边的手指。|阅览室已经认得你提灯的声音，你还隔着一张桌子，低语就停了。|常客们听到你的脚步，不等你走近就坐直了。} {一桌二年级生笑个不停。|一位老学者趴在笔记上打鼾，鼾声都带着学术腔调。|不知谁的羽毛笔沙沙响得都有回声了。} {final_year: 很快这一圈就是别人的了。|}\n# en: A student assistant with a lamp walking between study tables in a dim library, one finger to her lips.\nscene [207931] = 昏暗的图书馆里，一个提灯的学生助理在自习桌之间走过，一根手指竖在唇边。\n# en: Hush them firmly\nopt.left.label [b21b8c] = 严厉地让他们安静\n# en: {One look and the room falls silent. Somewhere a bookcase seems to approve.|You hush them. They glare. In the morning one of them leaves a note on the desk: \"sorry\", in very small writing.|You hush the scratching quill. Its owner hears your step before you arrive and is already writing quietly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [77efeb] = {你一个眼神，整间屋子就安静了。某个书架似乎表示赞许。|你让他们安静。他们瞪你。第二天早上，柜台上有张字条写着“对不起”，字很小很小。|你让那支沙沙作响的羽毛笔安静下来。笔的主人在你走到之前就听见了你的脚步，已经轻轻地写了起来。}\n# en: Let them be\nopt.right.label [29efb1] = 由他们去\n# en: {You let it go. The noise rises, the head librarian appears from nowhere, and looks at you, not at them.|You leave them to it. For a while the murmur is the nicest sound in the building.|You let them be. The regulars straighten up anyway when you pass, out of habit, and the noise sorts itself out.}\nopt.right.say.1 [42969a] = {你没管。声音越来越大，图书馆馆长不知从哪儿冒了出来，看的是你，不是他们。|你随他们去。有那么一会儿，那阵低语是整栋楼里最好听的声音。|你随他们去。你经过时常客们还是习惯性地坐直了，声音自己就小了下去。}\n\n@event library_assistant_reference_desk\n# en: The Reference Desk\ntitle [6eeb1e] = 咨询台\n# en: {Clerks take the reference desk alone. The first question arrives before you sit down.|The desk has a worn patch now, exactly where your elbows go.|You keep a list of the strangest questions now, and it is long.} {A professor wants \"the green book, about this thick, I read it in my youth\".|A first-year wants the book their professor \"definitely mentioned\".|A visiting scholar wants the one book that explains why bees hum in thirds, by tonight.}\ntext [5dc304] = {文员要独自值咨询台。你还没坐下，第一个问题就来了。|柜台上如今磨出了一块亮斑，正好是你搁手肘的地方。|你现在专门记录最古怪的问题，单子已经很长了。} {一位教授要找“那本绿皮书，大概这么厚，我年轻时读过”。|一个一年级生要找他们教授“肯定提过”的那本书。|一位来访学者要在今晚之前找到那本解释蜜蜂为什么用三度音嗡嗡叫的书。}\n# en: A student behind a reference desk facing a line of readers, one of them holding up a green ribbon.\nscene [d1f7a3] = 一个学生坐在咨询台后，面对一队读者，其中一人举着一根绿丝带。\n# en: Dig until you find it\nopt.left.label [f45a7c] = 一直找到为止\n# en: {You find it in the end, in the wrong section, under a cat. The reader looks at you as if you had worked a miracle, and you have.|Three ladders, two catalogues and an educated guess. It is the right book.|There's no such book. You find three that explain it between them, by tonight. The scholar adds your name to the acknowledgements.}\nopt.left.say.1 [31df1b] = {你终于找到了，在错误的书区，压在一只猫底下。读者看你的眼神像你创造了奇迹，你确实创造了。|三架梯子、两本目录，外加一个有根据的猜测。就是这本。|根本没有那样一本书。你找来三本，合起来把它讲清楚了，赶在当晚。那位学者把你的名字加进了致谢。}\n# en: {Hours later you admit defeat. The book, it turns out, was on the desk all along, propping up the inkpot.|You never find it. You dream of card catalogues all night.|The bees remain unexplained. You add the question to your list, at the top.}\nopt.left.say.2 [fcd48c] = {几个小时后你认输了。结果那本书一直在柜台上，垫着墨水瓶。|你始终没找到。你梦了一整夜的卡片目录。|蜜蜂的问题依旧没有答案。你把这个问题加进了清单，放在最上面。}\n# en: Send them off with a good guess\nopt.right.label [1e3693] = 随口指个方向\n# en: {You point confidently at a staircase. They come back an hour later with a different book, which they say they liked better.|\"Try the fourth floor.\" They do. You hope the fourth floor helps.|You send them to the apiary. They come back with a bee and no book, delighted.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a45acf] = {你自信地指向一道楼梯。一小时后他们带着另一本书回来，说更喜欢这本。|“去四楼找找。”他们去了。你希望四楼帮得上忙。|你让他们去养蜂场。他们带着一只蜜蜂回来了，没带书，开心得很。}\n\n@event library_assistant_stack_keys\n# en: The Stack Keys\ntitle [dd3ec0] = 书库钥匙\n# en: At closing the head librarian presses a brass ring of keys into your hand: the upper stacks, the map drawers, the ladder chains. Not the Restricted Section. \"Lock up. The shelves settle after dark. Let them.\"\ntext [3fbf9b] = 闭馆时，图书馆馆长把一串黄铜钥匙按进你手里：上层书库、地图抽屉、梯子的锁链。不包括禁书区。“锁门。天黑后书架会安顿下来。随它们去。”\n# en: An old librarian handing a brass ring of keys to a student among towering shelves at closing time.\nscene [5cab1e] = 闭馆时分，高耸的书架间，一位老馆员把一串黄铜钥匙交给一个学生。\n# en: Do the closing round alone\nopt.left.label [4df85b] = 独自巡视闭馆\n# en: You walk every floor with the keys chiming. The shelves shift and creak and settle, like an old house going to sleep, and this time you are the one who gets to hear it.\nopt.left.say.1 [185d52] = 你带着叮当作响的钥匙走遍每一层。书架挪动、吱呀、安定下来，像一座老房子入睡，而这一次，听见这一切的是你。\n# en: Let Footnote come along\nopt.right.label [7a3ccf] = 让脚注一起来\n# en: Footnote pads ahead of you up every stair, checking each lock before you do. At the top he sits in the window and lets you scratch his ears, once. You suspect that was the real promotion.\nopt.right.say.1 [dd5a65] = 脚注在你前面轻步走上每一级台阶，抢在你之前检查每一把锁。到了顶层，它坐在窗台上，让你挠了一下耳朵，就一下。你怀疑那才是真正的晋升。\n\n@event tavern_cider_rush\n# en: The Cider Rush\ntitle [9095b3] = 苹果酒高峰\n# en: {The rush at the Gilded Newt. A dozen orders shouted at once, and a tray that holds fewer.|The door won't stay shut. You have started remembering faces by their drink, which works until two people want spiced cider.|The regulars just point now, and you know what the points mean.} {A barge crew has come in soaked and thirsty.|The card table is arguing about the rules again.|{season == \"winter\": Snow is melting off every coat in the room.|Half the village is on the step.}}\ntext [926886] = {镀金蝾螈酒馆的高峰。十几份点单同时喊过来，托盘却放不下那么多。|门怎么也关不上。你开始凭各人点的酒记住他们的脸，直到有两个人都要香料苹果酒。|常客们现在只用手指，你也都看得懂。} {一队驳船船员浑身湿透地进来，口渴得很。|牌桌那边又在为规则吵架了。|{season == \"winter\": 满屋大衣上的雪都在化。|半个小镇的人都站在门口台阶上。}}\n# en: A crowded low-beamed tavern, a server balancing a tray of steaming mugs through the press of bodies.\nscene [3aaff4] = 低梁的酒馆里人头攒动，一个侍应托着一盘冒热气的杯子穿过人群。\n# en: Keep every order in your head\nopt.left.label [588aa1] = 全凭脑子记单\n# en: {Every mug to the right hand. The whole room tips like it's payday.|You don't miss once. The landlady, who remembers every tab to the penny, gives you a look of professional respect.|The regulars point, and you deliver. The door won't stay shut, and nobody waits more than a minute.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1f2c5d] = {每一杯都送到了对的人手里。整屋人给小费都像发了工钱。|你一次也没错。那位把每笔账记到分毫的老板娘，向你投来同行间的敬意。|常客们一指，你就送到。门关不上，可谁都没等过一分钟。}\n# en: {The spiced cider goes to the wrong table. The right table has opinions. The wrong table drinks it anyway.|You mix up two orders and three tempers.|Half the village points at once. You misread two points and a wave.}\nopt.left.say.2 [246428] = {香料苹果酒送错了桌。对的那桌很有意见。错的那桌照喝不误。|你弄混了两份点单和三个人的脾气。|半个村子的人同时在指。你看错了两个指向和一个挥手。}\n# en: Write every order down\nopt.right.label [80b465] = 每单都写下来\n# en: {Slow but sure. Nobody gets the wrong drink; nobody tips the slow server either.|Your order pad fills with a shorthand only you can read. By closing, you can't either.|You write every order, even the points. By closing you have a full record of the village's habits.}\nopt.right.say.1 [100d2c] = {慢，但稳。没人拿错酒，也没人给慢吞吞的侍应小费。|你的点单本上写满了只有你看得懂的速记。到打烊时，你也看不懂了。|你把每一份点单都记了下来，连手指的也记了。到打烊时，你有了一份全村习惯的完整记录。}\n\n@event tavern_regulars_corner\n# en: The Regulars' Corner\ntitle [98ab7f] = 常客角落\n# en: {The regulars' corner by the fire wants you to sit for a minute. The fire is warm, and the minute is tempting.|The regulars' corner has saved you a stool now, with a cushion on it.|The regulars wave you over by name. They want to know how your exams went, all of them, in order.} {The old barge-man is telling the eel story again.|The herb-wife from the lane wants her pie warmed \"exactly as before\".|A quiet traveller is listening to everything.}\ntext [d6ae20] = {炉火边的常客角落想让你坐一会儿。炉火很暖，这一会儿很诱人。|常客角落如今给你留了张凳子，上面还垫了个软垫。|常客们喊着你的名字招手。他们想知道你的考试考得怎样，每一门，按顺序。} {老驳船工又在讲那个鳗鱼的故事。|巷子里那位卖草药的大婶要她的馅饼“和上次一样”热一热。|一个安静的旅人什么都在听。}\n# en: An old barge-man, a herb-wife from the lane and a quiet traveller at a fireside table, beckoning a server over.\nscene [017fca] = 炉火边的桌旁，一位老驳船工、一位巷子里卖草药的大婶和一个安静的旅人正招呼侍应过去。\n# en: Sit with them a while\nopt.left.label [c29077] = 陪他们坐一会儿\n# en: {You hear the latest instalment of everyone's story. They tip you for listening, which is a better rate than serving.|You sit for one minute and stay for ten. The landlady clears her throat across the room like a falling tree.|You tell them about your exams, all of them, in order. The quiet traveller listens hardest and tips best.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9dc187] = {你听完了每个人故事的最新一集。他们为你肯听付了小费，比端盘子还划算。|你说坐一会儿，结果坐了好一阵。老板娘在屋子那头清了清嗓子，像一棵树倒下。|你把你的考试一门一门按顺序讲给他们听。那位安静的旅人听得最认真，小费给得也最多。}\n# en: Keep the trays moving\nopt.right.label [579530] = 托盘不停手\n# en: {You wave and keep going. The corner toasts your back. The eel story will keep.|You work the room like weather. The corner calls you \"the brisk one\" now, which is not a compliment.|You keep moving. The quiet traveller watches you all evening, and leaves without a word, and a large tip.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9813b7] = {你挥挥手继续忙。角落里的人朝你背影举杯。鳗鱼的故事跑不了。|你像一阵风一样忙遍全场。角落里的人现在叫你“利索的那个”，这可不是夸奖。|你一直在忙。那位安静的旅人看了你一整晚，一言不发地走了，留下一大笔小费。}\n\n@event tavern_tab_book\n# en: The Tab Book\ntitle [c2811c] = 赊账簿\n# en: {Barkeeps keep the tab book, and the tab book says {regular} owes the Newt rather a lot.|The tab book has grown a second volume, and {regular} has a page in it.|{regular} is at the end of the bar, and so, in the book, is a long column of figures.} {{They} smile{s} at you hopefully.|{They} {are} pretending to be fascinated by the fire.|{They} {are} counting coins that clearly won't be enough.}\ntext [cf577d] = {吧台侍应管着赊账簿，账簿上说{regular}欠了酒馆不少钱。|赊账簿已经续到第二册了，{regular}在里面也有一页。|{regular}坐在吧台尽头，账簿里也排着长长一列数字。} {{They}满怀希望地冲你笑。|{They}假装对炉火很着迷。|{They}在数一把显然不够的硬币。}\n# en: A ledger open on a tavern bar, a long column of figures, a hopeful customer at the far end.\nscene [1a746d] = 酒馆吧台上摊开一本账簿，一长列数字，吧台尽头坐着一位满怀希望的客人。\n# en: Call the tab in\nopt.left.label [6ebe68] = 把账收回来\n# en: {{regular} pays, slowly, coin by coin, as if each one were a friend leaving. The landlady slides you a few crowns for the collecting.|You call it in. {regular} pays half and an apology. The landlady counts both, and gives you a few crowns of it for your trouble.|You call it in. {regular} pays in coins and a promise, and the landlady accepts the coins. You get a share of those.}\nopt.left.say.1 [05b268] = {{regular}一枚一枚慢慢付钱，好像每枚硬币都是一位离去的朋友。老板娘塞给你几枚克朗，算收账的辛苦费。|你开口要账。{regular}付了一半，外加一句道歉。老板娘把两样都点了数，从里头分了你几枚克朗作辛苦费。|你开口要账。{regular}付了些硬币，外加一个承诺，老板娘收了硬币。你分到了一点。}\n# en: Let it ride another week\nopt.right.label [2f306c] = 再宽限一周\n# en: {{regular} looks at you as if you had hung the moon. The landlady looks at you as if you had lost it.|Another week. The column grows, and so, oddly, does {regular}'s loyalty.|You let it ride and add a line to the long column. {regular} buys you a drink with money {they} clearly owe the Newt.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f41919] = {{regular}看你的眼神像是你把月亮挂上了天。老板娘看你的眼神像是你把脑子弄丢了。|又一周。那列数字更长了，{regular}的忠诚也奇怪地跟着长了。|你又宽限了一周，在那长长的一列下又添了一行。{regular}用显然欠着酒馆的钱请你喝了一杯。}\n\n@event tavern_behind_the_bar\n# en: Behind the Bar\ntitle [7962ab] = 吧台之后\n# en: The landlady sets the cellar key on the bar between you. \"You pour from tonight,\" she says. \"Barkeeps feed the fire, too. It hasn't gone out in my lifetime, nor my mother's. Don't be the one.\" The hearth crackles, as if listening.\ntext [8a0483] = 老板娘把地窖钥匙放在你们之间的吧台上。“从今晚起你来倒酒，”她说，“吧台的人也要添火。这火在我这辈子、我娘那辈子都没灭过。别让它灭在你手里。”炉火噼啪作响，像在听着。\n# en: A landlady setting a heavy cellar key on a polished tavern bar, the hearth fire glowing behind her.\nscene [818643] = 炉火在后方闪着光，老板娘把一把沉重的地窖钥匙放在擦得锃亮的吧台上。\n# en: Bank the fire at closing\nopt.left.label [065f76] = 打烊时封好炉火\n# en: You stay after the last song, banking the coals the way she showed you, slow and careful as tucking in a child. In the morning it is still burning. You check twice anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [b13718] = 最后一支歌唱完，你留下来，照她教的封住炭火，又慢又小心，像给孩子掖被子。第二天早上火还燃着。你还是检查了两遍。\n# en: Pour the staff a round\nopt.right.label [82794d] = 给伙计们倒一轮\n# en: You pour your first round for the cooks and the pot-boy. The landlady takes hers without a word, then taps the bar twice, which apparently means \"well poured\" in a language older than the Newt.\nopt.right.say.1 [64fdee] = 你给厨子和洗杯小工倒了你的第一轮酒。老板娘一言不发地接过她那杯，在吧台上敲了两下——据说在一门比镀金蝾螈酒馆还老的语言里，这意思是“倒得好”。\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_counting_drops\n# en: Counting Drops\ntitle [77984e] = 数滴数\n# en: {Old Pell's first rule is to count every drop aloud. Today the counter is lined with tinctures waiting to be measured.|Drop-counting. Your tongue knows the numbers before your eyes do.|Counting drops. Old Pell says you count like a metronome now, which from him is a sonnet.} {A customer is waiting, tapping a coin on the glass.|One of the daughters is humming, which throws off your count.|Today's tincture is the colour of a bruise and smells of violets.}\ntext [bf51c4] = {老佩尔的第一条规矩是每一滴都要大声数出来。今天柜台上排满了等着量的酊剂。|数滴数。你的舌头比眼睛先知道数字。|数滴数。老佩尔说你现在数得像节拍器，这话从他嘴里说出来就是一首十四行诗。} {一位客人在等，用硬币轻敲玻璃柜台。|老佩尔的一个女儿在哼歌，打乱了你的节奏。|今天的酊剂是淤青的颜色，闻起来却是紫罗兰味。}\n# en: A student squinting at a glass dropper over a tiny vial at an apothecary counter, a wall of labelled drawers behind.\nscene [44e4e3] = 药铺柜台前，一个学生眯眼盯着小药瓶上的玻璃滴管，身后是一整墙贴着标签的抽屉。\n# en: Count aloud, every drop\nopt.left.label [2ee43b] = 一滴一滴大声数\n# en: {Every vial exact. Anyone in the shop has now heard you count higher than anyone should.|Aloud, drop by drop. Old Pell, passing, nods once without breaking stride.|You count the violet one aloud in perfect time. Pell, passing, nods in time too.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9792a6] = {每一瓶都分毫不差。铺子里谁都已经听你数到了一个谁都不该数到的数。|大声地，一滴一滴。老佩尔路过，头也不停地点了一下。|你把那瓶紫罗兰色的大声数完，节拍分毫不差。佩尔路过，点头也合着拍子。}\n# en: Count by eye, faster\nopt.right.label [f2a5b1] = 目测，快一些\n# en: {Quick and true. You clear the counter before the tea goes cold, and a satisfied customer leaves a coin.|By eye, and spot on. One of the daughters raises an eyebrow and says nothing to her father.|You count by eye in your metronome rhythm. Pell checks one vial, finds it perfect, and stops checking.}\nopt.right.say.1 [55f6e2] = {又快又准。茶还没凉，你就清空了柜台，一位满意的客人留下一枚硬币。|目测，正中。佩尔的一个女儿挑了挑眉，什么也没跟她父亲说。|你按自己节拍器般的节奏目测。佩尔查了一瓶，分毫不差，就不再查了。}\n# en: {One drop over. Old Pell can tell from across the shop. He tips the vial out and makes you count the next one aloud, twice.|You overshoot. The tincture turns from violet to a colour with no name, and Pell sighs like a closing door.|You go by eye and the violet one goes the colour of a worse bruise. Pell makes you count the rest aloud, as punishment and as lesson.}\nopt.right.say.2 [975042] = {多了一滴。老佩尔隔着整间铺子都看得出来。他把药瓶倒空，让你把下一瓶大声数两遍。|你倒多了。酊剂从紫罗兰色变成一种没有名字的颜色，佩尔叹了口气，像一扇门关上。|你凭目测，那瓶紫罗兰色的变成了更难看的淤青色。佩尔罚你把剩下的都大声数完，既是惩罚，也是一课。}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_drawer_check\n# en: The Thousand Drawers\ntitle [76310c] = 千屉之墙\n# en: {Each shift a clerk checks a row of drawers against the stock book. Today's row starts with one that is stuck.|A different row today, and the same feeling that something in it is awake.|Your row for the week. You have learned that the wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.} {One is labelled in a hand that isn't Pell's.|One is rattling, very gently, as if politely.|One smells of snow, in {season == \"winter\": a shop this warm|the wrong season entirely}.}\ntext [c43280] = {每个班次，店员要对着库存簿核对一排抽屉。今天这一排的第一个就卡住了。|今天换了一排，还是那种里面有什么醒着的感觉。|这周轮到你这一排。你已经明白，拉错的抽屉从来不只是拉错了抽屉。} {有一个抽屉的标签不是佩尔的笔迹。|有一个抽屉在轻轻地响，几乎是礼貌地。|有一个抽屉闻起来像雪，{season == \"winter\": 在这么暖和的铺子里|完全不合时节}。}\n# en: A wall of tiny labelled wooden drawers, one of them rattling on its own.\nscene [c05965] = 一整墙贴着标签的小木抽屉，其中一个自己在咔哒作响。\n# en: Open the odd drawer\nopt.left.label [7cc2f8] = 打开那个古怪的抽屉\n# en: {Inside, a nest of dried moonpetals that belong on the top row. You move them, and the drawer stops sulking.|You coax it open and find the missing powdered newt. Pell crosses something off a list he has kept for a year.|Inside is a little snow that hasn't melted, and a note in Pell's hand: \"Don't.\" You close it. Pell thanks you, oddly sincerely.}\nopt.left.say.1 [599d4d] = {里面是一窝干月光花瓣，本该放在最上排。你挪好了，抽屉不再赌气。|你哄开抽屉，找到了失踪的蝾螈粉。佩尔在一张他留了一年的清单上划掉了一项。|里面是一点没化的雪，还有一张佩尔笔迹的字条：“别。”你关上了。佩尔向你道谢，出奇地真诚。}\n# en: {The drawer opens with a sneeze of pepperwort. You sneeze until closing.|Something small and grey bolts out of the drawer and into another, and you never find out which.|The snow-smelling drawer is full of cold. It gets out, and the shop is frosted until noon.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f0921c] = {抽屉一开，喷出一股胡椒草粉。你一直打喷嚏到打烊。|一个灰色的小东西从抽屉里窜出来，钻进了另一个抽屉，你始终不知道是哪个。|那个有雪味的抽屉里装满了寒气。寒气跑了出来，铺子到中午都结着霜。}\n# en: Sign it off unchecked\nopt.right.label [49847c] = 没核对就签字\n# en: {You tick the row and move on. Behind you a drawer rattles, as if to say it noticed.|\"Checked,\" you write. Pell reads it, opens one drawer at random, and closes it again with a look.|You sign off the row, cold drawer and all. Pell checks that one himself, as if he'd expected you to.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1abc55] = {你打了个勾，继续往下。身后一个抽屉咔哒一响，像是在说它看见了。|你写下“已核对”。佩尔看了看，随手拉开一个抽屉，又关上，看了你一眼。|你把这一排签了，连那个冷冰冰的抽屉也签了。佩尔亲自查了那一个，好像早料到你会这样。}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_counter_advice\n# en: Asking the Dispenser\ntitle [23739a] = 请教配药师\n# en: {Dispensers advise, and today a customer asks you instead of Pell.|Pell has moved your stool to the end of the counter, where the customers come.|You know the regular coughs by sound now, before anyone opens the door.} {A farmer has a cough that sounds like a gate.|A nervous student wants something for courage before exams.|A barge-hand has a back that hasn't straightened since autumn.}\ntext [13f53a] = {配药师要给人出主意，今天有位客人没找佩尔，而是来问你。|佩尔把你的凳子挪到了柜台尽头，客人们来的那一头。|如今常客的咳嗽声你一听就认得，门还没开就认得。} {一个农夫咳得像一扇破门。|一个紧张的学生想要点考前壮胆的东西。|一个驳船工的腰从秋天起就没直过。}\n# en: A worried customer leaning over an apothecary counter, a student behind it consulting a heavy recipe book.\nscene [7ad077] = 一位忧心的客人倚在药铺柜台上，柜台后的学生正翻阅一本厚重的配方书。\n# en: Prescribe it yourself\nopt.left.label [34d97a] = 自己开方\n# en: {You mix it, label it and explain the dose twice. A week later they come back just to say it worked.|Your remedy, your writing on the bottle. Pell reads the label, reaches for his pen, and leaves it as it is.|You know the barge-hand's back from the sound of the door. The liniment is ready before they reach the counter.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2154a0] = {你配好、贴上标签，把用量讲了两遍。一周后他们专程回来，只为说一句管用。|你的方子，瓶上是你的字。佩尔读了标签，伸手去拿笔，又原样留下了。|你听开门声就知道是那个船工的腰又来了。人还没走到柜台，药膏已经备好了。}\n# en: {It helps a little, then not at all. They come back, and this time they ask for Pell.|Pell takes the bottle from your hand, adds one thing and gives it back. He doesn't say a word, which is worse.|You recommend the liniment for the barge-hand's back. It's the wrong one, and the barge-hand smells of it for a week.}\nopt.left.say.2 [73fce8] = {起初有点用，后来就没用了。他们又来了，这回点名要佩尔。|佩尔从你手里接过瓶子，加了一样东西，又递还给你。他一句话也没说，这更糟。|你给船工的腰推荐了药膏。推荐错了，船工身上那股味儿留了一个星期。}\n# en: Fetch one of the daughters\nopt.right.label [f428e6] = 去叫佩尔家的女儿\n# en: {The elder daughter takes over and talks the customer down like a nervous horse. You watch and write it all down.|The younger daughter mixes it in half the time you would have. She lets you hold the funnel.|Both daughters come, and argue the case across you. You learn twice as much and get the cure from neither.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fd0320] = {大女儿接过手，像安抚一匹受惊的马一样把客人安抚下来。你在一旁看，全记了下来。|小女儿配药只用了你一半的时间。她让你帮忙扶漏斗。|两个女儿都来了，隔着你争论起来。你学到了双倍的东西，可方子哪个也没给你。}\n\n@event apothecary_clerk_labelling_pen\n# en: The Labelling Pen\ntitle [cbd6f5] = 标签笔\n# en: Old Pell is very proud of his labelling. So it means something when he puts his own pen in your hand, its nib worn to his slant, and slides a stack of blank labels across the counter. \"New stock,\" he says. \"Your hand from now on. Neat, mind.\"\ntext [ea44d4] = 老佩尔对自己写的标签极为自豪。所以当他把自己的笔放进你手里——笔尖已经磨成他的斜度——又把一摞空白标签推过柜台时，这就意味着什么。“新货，”他说，“往后用你的字。写工整点。”\n# en: An old apothecary handing over a well-worn pen beside a stack of fresh blank labels.\nscene [5f9c82] = 一位老药剂师递出一支用旧了的笔，旁边是一摞崭新的空白标签。\n# en: Label the new drawers yourself\nopt.left.label [15781b] = 亲手给新抽屉贴标签\n# en: You write every label slowly, in your best hand. Pell inspects them through his spectacles one by one and changes not a single letter. His daughters notice before you do.\nopt.left.say.1 [a6af78] = 你用最好的字迹，一张一张慢慢写。佩尔隔着眼镜逐张检查，一个字母也没改。他的女儿们比你先察觉到这意味着什么。\n# en: Ask him to watch the first one\nopt.right.label [bd2847] = 请他看你写第一张\n# en: He watches. Your first label wobbles at the end. Pell takes the pen, adds a tiny flourish to your wobble so that it looks deliberate, and returns the pen for good.\nopt.right.say.1 [f27abb] = 他看着。你的第一张标签末尾抖了一下。佩尔拿过笔，在那一抖上添了个小小的花饰，让它看起来像是故意的，然后把笔还给你，从此归你。\n\n@event broom_courier_morning_crossing\n# en: The Lake Crossing\ntitle [6090d5] = 过湖\n# en: {The dispatch bell rings. The village sack is ready, and the quickest way is over the lake.|You have started timing yourself against the gulls.|The lake knows your shadow by now.} {season == \"winter\": Ice fog sits on the water like a lid.|{season == \"summer\": The water is so still you fly over your own reflection.|{A headwind is coming off the hills.|Gulls are holding a meeting on your route.|The far shore is lost in mist.}}}\ntext [03ae44] = {派件铃响了。送往山下小镇的邮袋已经备好，最快的路是飞过湖面。|你开始和海鸥比速度了。|湖早就认得你的影子了。} {season == \"winter\": 冰雾像锅盖一样压在水面上。|{season == \"summer\": 湖水静得你像在自己的倒影上飞。|{山那边吹来一阵逆风。|一群海鸥在你的航线上开会。|对岸隐没在雾里。}}}\n# en: A courier on a broom skimming low over a mirror-still lake at dawn, a mail sack over one shoulder.\nscene [021120] = 黎明时分，一个肩挎邮袋的信使骑着飞帚，贴着镜面般平静的湖水低飞。\n# en: Fly straight across\nopt.left.label [d53a49] = 直线飞过去\n# en: {Straight as a thrown stone. You're early, and the village postmistress tips early couriers.|You skim the water so low your boots leave a wake. Nobody sees, which is a shame.|You fly straight into the mist, by memory. The far shore appears exactly where you expected it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a90b36] = {直得像扔出去的石子。你到早了，小镇的女邮政局长会给早到的信使小费。|你贴着水面飞得那么低，靴子都划出了水痕。没人看见，真可惜。|你凭记忆一头扎进雾里。对岸恰好出现在你预料的地方。}\n# en: {A gust drops you into the shallows. The sack stays dry; you do not.|The far shore vanishes and you fly a slow circle round a buoy. You arrive late and very damp.|The mist takes the far shore and your sense of direction with it. You arrive at the right village from the wrong side.}\nopt.left.say.2 [e92243] = {一阵风把你吹进了浅水。邮袋没湿，你湿透了。|对岸消失了，你绕着一个浮标慢慢兜圈子。到的时候又晚又湿。|雾把对岸连同你的方向感一起吞了。你从错的一边到了对的村子。}\n# en: Take the long way round\nopt.right.label [d7b80a] = 沿湖岸绕远路\n# en: {Round the shore, safe and slow. The dispatcher checks the clock and sighs.|The long way. You wave to a fisherman, who does not wave back.|The long way, round the mist. The fisherman waves, for the first time. You're so surprised you nearly fall off.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e98d91] = {沿着湖岸，稳稳当当，慢慢悠悠。派件员看了看钟，叹了口气。|绕远路。你朝一个渔夫挥手，他没挥回来。|绕远路，避开了雾。那个渔夫第一次朝你挥了手。你吃惊得差点从扫帚上掉下来。}\n\n@event broom_courier_broom_scramble\n# en: The Broom Scramble\ntitle [90ac74] = 抢飞帚\n# en: {Only one loft broom is in a good state, and every courier on shift wants it.|You have started arriving early for the brooms, and so has everyone else.|You could pick the good broom blindfold, by the smell of its varnish.} {What's left is one that pulls left.|What's left sheds twigs when nervous.|What's left only flies if you're polite to it.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has got into all their bristles.|}\ntext [78fa0f] = {阁楼里只有一把飞帚是好的，当班的信使人人都想要它。|你开始提早到场抢飞帚，别人也一样。|你闭着眼凭清漆味都能挑出那把好飞帚。} {剩下的那把总往左偏。|剩下的那把一紧张就掉枝条。|剩下的那把只有你对它客气才肯飞。} {season == \"winter\": 霜已经钻进了所有飞帚的帚毛里。|}\n# en: A draughty loft rack of battered brooms, couriers jostling to grab the least battered one.\nscene [bf0220] = 透风的阁楼里一排破旧的飞帚，信使们推搡着去抢最不破的那一把。\n# en: Shoulder in for the good broom\nopt.left.label [fe1030] = 挤上去抢好飞帚\n# en: {You get there first, by a whisker and an elbow. The second-year you elbowed will remember.|You shoulder a second-year aside and the good broom is yours. It flies like a dream; theirs flies like a complaint.|You pick out the good broom by its varnish while the others are still arguing. You're over the lake before they notice.}\nopt.left.say.1 [30a9d7] = {你抢先一步，赢在毫厘和一记手肘。被你挤开的那个二年级生会记住的。|你用肩膀把一个二年级生挤到一边，好飞帚归你了。它飞起来像一场梦；他们那把飞起来像一串抱怨。|别人还在争，你已经凭清漆的气味挑出了好飞帚。等他们发现时，你已经飞过了湖。}\n# en: Take the difficult one\nopt.right.label [c8d2e7] = 骑那把难缠的\n# en: {It fights you the whole way across the lake. Your arms will remember this, and so will you.|It takes the whole route to come to an understanding with it. You win, narrowly.|You're polite to the difficult one, very polite. It flies well, and everyone else is too embarrassed to try it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7f0918] = {它一路和你较劲，直到过了湖。你的胳膊会记住，你也会。|整条路线下来，你们才算达成了谅解。你赢了，险胜。|你对那把难伺候的很客气，非常客气。它飞得不错，别人都不好意思去试。}\n\n@event broom_courier_dead_letters\n# en: The Dead-Letter Shelf\ntitle [c14849] = 死信架\n# en: {Couriers sort the dead letters, post with no proper address, waiting on a dusty shelf.|The dead-letter shelf has grown a second row.|You know which parcels have waited longest now, and which have stopped expecting anyone.} Today, a parcel addressed only to {\"the tall one in the red scarf, third tower\"|\"whoever plays the flute at dawn\"|\"my brother, the one who never writes\"}.\ntext [56186c] = {信使要整理死信，就是那些地址不全、躺在落灰架子上的邮件。|死信架又多了一层。|你现在知道哪些包裹等得最久，哪些已经不再指望有人来取。} 今天有个包裹，收件人只写着{“围红围巾的高个子，第三塔”|“黎明时吹长笛的人”|“我哥哥，从来不写信的那个”}。\n# en: A dusty shelf of undeliverable parcels in a courier loft, one tied with a faded ribbon.\nscene [dcc319] = 信使阁楼里一架落满灰尘的无法投递的包裹，其中一个系着褪色的丝带。\n# en: Track down the recipient\nopt.left.label [6456ac] = 找到收件人\n# en: {It takes three towers and a dozen questions, but the parcel reaches the right hands. They open it at once and cry a little, in a good way.|Found them. The look on their face is worth every stair.|You find the brother. He's a porter in the next village and he does write; the letters just never arrive. Now one has.}\nopt.left.say.1 [040dc3] = {跑了三座塔、问了十几个问题，包裹终于送到了对的人手里。他们当场拆开，哭了一小会儿，是好的那种。|找到了。他们脸上的表情值得爬每一级楼梯。|你找到了那个哥哥。他是邻村的门房，其实他写信的，只是信总寄不到。现在有一封到了。}\n# en: {You knock on every door you can think of. The parcel goes back on the shelf, a little more crumpled, a little more sad.|Wrong door. Wrong tower. Wrong week.|There are a great many brothers who never write. None of them is this one.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7d911b] = {你敲遍了能想到的每一扇门。包裹回到了架子上，更皱了一点，也更难过了一点。|敲错门。找错塔。赶错周。|从不写信的哥哥多得是。没有一个是这一个。}\n# en: Leave it on the shelf\nopt.right.label [52ecc9] = 放回架子上\n# en: {It goes back with the others. Some of them have been waiting longer than you have been alive.|You leave it. That night you dream of a parcel on a high tower, waiting.|You leave it, but you dust the shelf. The parcels that have stopped expecting anyone look a little less forgotten.}\nopt.right.say.1 [39d2ed] = {它回到了其他包裹中间。有些在那儿等的时间比你活的还久。|你没管它。那晚你梦见一个包裹在高塔上，等着。|你没管它，但掸了掸架子。那些已经不再等人的包裹，看上去不那么被遗忘了。}\n\n@event broom_courier_own_pigeonhole\n# en: Your Own Pigeonhole\ntitle [a87c3e] = 你自己的信格\n# en: The dispatcher has painted your name over a pigeonhole, in letters that will outlast the loft's roof. \"Your route from now on,\" the dispatcher says. \"Village, barge landing, the high towers. When the bell rings for you, you answer.\" The dispatch bell rings. It's for you.\ntext [f9338e] = 派件员在一个信格上方漆上了你的名字，那字迹会比阁楼的屋顶活得还久。“从今往后这是你的路线，”派件员说，“山下小镇、驳船码头、高塔区。铃为你响，你就去。”派件铃响了。是找你的。\n# en: A courier loft wall of pigeonholes, one freshly painted with a student's name, a brass bell above.\nscene [517d9b] = 信使阁楼里一整面墙的信格，其中一格刚漆上一个学生的名字，上方挂着一只黄铜铃。\n# en: Fly the route at once\nopt.left.label [9240b6] = 立刻飞一趟\n# en: You fly your own route for the first time, every turning yours. At the barge landing someone calls you by name to hand over a letter. The wind is cold, and you don't mind at all.\nopt.left.say.1 [b37714] = 你第一次飞自己的路线，每个拐弯都属于你。在驳船码头，有人喊着你的名字递来一封信。风很冷，你一点也不在意。\n# en: Stand the runners a round\nopt.right.label [631a00] = 请跑腿的伙计们喝一轮\n# en: The runners toast your pigeonhole with mugs of loft tea. Somebody chalks a little crown above your name. It is still there at the end of the year, smudged, the most permanent thing in the loft.\nopt.right.say.1 [0c186d] = 跑腿的伙计们端着阁楼茶，为你的信格干杯。有人用粉笔在你名字上方画了顶小王冠。到年底它还在，糊了，却是阁楼里最长久的东西。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_a.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_a.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event evo_snuffing_drill\n# en: The Snuffing Drill\ntitle [1894a9] = 灭火操练\n# en: {Evocation I opens with a lesson nobody expected. \"Anyone can start a fire,\" says the lecturer. \"Stopping one is the course.\"|The braziers are lit before you arrive. You know which one smokes.|Your brazier goes out when you walk in now, out of habit. The lecturer relights it, unamused.} {The front row smells of singed eyebrow.|Someone's brazier has gone out from fright.|The lecturer is timing you with an egg-timer.}\ntext [cc5e7b] = {塑能术 I 的第一课出乎所有人意料。“谁都会点火，”讲师说，“学会灭火，才是这门课。”|你到之前火盆就已经点上了。哪一只爱冒烟，你心里有数。|如今你一走进来，你的火盆就习惯性地熄了。讲师把它重新点上，脸色不太好看。}{前排飘着一股烧焦眉毛的味道。|有人的火盆被吓灭了。|讲师正拿着煮蛋计时器给你们计时。}\n# en: A row of iron braziers burning in a scorched lecture hall, students standing before them with their hands raised as if to calm a horse.\nscene [9cf592] = 焦痕斑斑的讲堂里，一排铁火盆燃着火，学生们站在盆前举起双手，像在安抚一匹马。\n# en: Call it back slowly\nopt.left.label [e24679] = 慢慢收回来\n# en: {The flames shrink into embers, then into a warm ache in your palm.|It takes the whole hour. Your brazier goes out like a sigh.|You call it back while the egg-timer runs. It goes out with a second to spare, and the lecturer turns the timer over again.} {season == \"winter\": The hall is colder afterwards, and it's your fault.|The lecturer nods once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [33d908] = {火焰缩成余烬，又缩成你掌心里一阵温热的酸胀。|整整用了一节课。你的火盆像叹了口气似的熄了。|你在沙漏计时下把火召回。它熄灭时还剩一秒，讲师又把计时器翻了过来。}{season == \"winter\": 之后讲堂冷了一截，这得怪你。|讲师点了一下头。}\n# en: Snuff it in one breath\nopt.right.label [f77f0b] = 一口气掐灭\n# en: {The fire folds itself into your hand all at once.|One breath, and the brazier is dark.|You snuff it before the egg-timer has turned.} The lecturer writes something down without looking surprised, which is the compliment.\nopt.right.say.1 [607399] = {火一下子把自己折进了你的手心。|一口气，火盆就暗了。|计时器还没翻过来，你已经把它掐灭了。}讲师头也不抬地记了一笔，一点也不显得惊讶——这就是夸奖了。\n# en: {The fire goes out. It goes out up your sleeve first.|The brazier flares, then sulks, then goes out on its own terms.|You rush it for the timer, and the fire rushes back.} You spend the rest of the hour holding your wrist in a bucket.\nopt.right.say.2 [a8748b] = {火是灭了。不过它先顺着你的袖子灭了一遍。|火盆猛地一窜，接着闹起脾气，最后按它自己的意思熄了。|你为了赶计时匆匆下手，火也匆匆反扑。}这节课剩下的时间，你都把手腕泡在水桶里。\n\n@event evo_bucket_duty\n# en: Bucket Duty\ntitle [af4be1] = 水桶值日\n# en: Every Evocation I lesson needs someone on the buckets: sand for the pyromancers, water for everyone else. {&Today it's your name on the board.|It's your turn again. The rota has a sense of humour.|Your name is on the board. You suspect the rota is personal.} {&The water bucket has a frog in it.|Someone has labelled the sand bucket \"Hope\".|The water bucket is half empty, because the tidecallers borrowed it.}\ntext [ad6c20] = 每节塑能术 I 都得有人守着水桶：沙桶留给火焰术的人，水桶留给其他所有人。{&今天黑板上写的是你的名字。|又轮到你了。这张值日表挺有幽默感。|黑板上又是你的名字。你怀疑这张值日表针对你。}{&水桶里有只青蛙。|有人在沙桶上贴了张标签：“希望”。|水桶只剩半桶，因为唤潮术的人借走了。}\n# en: A student standing at the edge of a busy lecture hall beside a bucket of sand and a bucket of water, watching sparks fly.\nscene [b53c8a] = 一个学生站在热闹讲堂的边上，身旁一桶沙、一桶水，看着火星四溅。\n# en: Mind the buckets, watch closely\nopt.left.label [6fe757] = 守好水桶，看仔细\n# en: {&You put out four sleeves and a textbook, and learn more from their mistakes than from your own.|Watching from the side, you finally see what the lecturer means by \"breathe first\".|You keep the buckets full and borrowed back from the tidecallers, and watch the whole lesson from the best seat in the hall.} {month == 4 or month == 10: It's the best revision you've done all term.|Nobody thanks the bucket monitor.}\nopt.left.say.1 [eb2b4f] = {&你扑灭了四只袖子和一本课本，从别人的失误里学到的比从自己的还多。|站在一旁看着，你终于明白了讲师说的“先呼吸”是什么意思。|你把水桶装满，又从唤潮术的人那儿要了回来，然后坐在大厅里最好的位置看完整节课。}{month == 4 or month == 10: 这是你整个学期做过的最好的复习。|没人会感谢守桶的人。}\n# en: Swap for a turn up front\nopt.right.label [38056d] = 换个机会上前练\n# en: {&You trade your duty for a turn at the practice line. Nobody is minding the buckets when your spark jumps the desk.|A first-year takes your buckets for a promise of help with their essay. Your practice goes well; your thumb goes pink.|You swap, and the tidecallers have the water bucket. Your spark finds the one dry curtain in the hall.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c6bfcf] = {&你用值日换了一次上前练习的机会。你的火星跳过课桌时，没人在守水桶。|一个一年级生答应替你守桶，条件是你帮他们改论文。你练得不错，拇指却红了一块。|你换了班，可水桶在唤潮术的人那儿。你的火星偏偏找到了大厅里唯一一块干窗帘。}\n\n@event evo_steady_cup\n# en: The Steady Cup\ntitle [1aa292] = 稳住杯子\n# en: {Professor Marisol's Tidecalling mornings begin with a full cup held at arm's length. Keep the water level by will alone while she reads poetry aloud.|Marisol has brought a new book of poems, thicker than the last.|You can hold the cup half asleep now, and often are.} {season == \"winter\": The water has a skin of ice, which she says is cheating.|{The poem is very long.|The poem is about the sea, and so are your sleeves.|A gull is watching your cup with intent.}}\ntext [31f8b5] = {玛丽索尔教授的唤潮术早课，从伸直手臂端一满杯水开始。她朗读诗歌时，你得单凭意志让水面保持平稳。|玛丽索尔带了本新诗集，比上一本还厚。|你现在半睡半醒也端得稳杯子，也确实常常半睡半醒。}{season == \"winter\": 水面结了一层薄冰，她说这算作弊。|{这首诗很长。|这首诗写的是大海，你的袖子也成了大海。|一只海鸥正意味深长地盯着你的杯子。}}\n# en: A row of students on a stone terrace holding brimming cups at arm's length while a professor reads aloud from a slim book of poems.\nscene [a1e523] = 石砌露台上站着一排学生，手臂伸直端着满溢的杯子，一位教授在一旁捧着薄薄的诗集朗读。\n# en: Hold it to the last line\nopt.left.label [18e90f] = 撑到最后一行\n# en: {Your arm burns by the third stanza. The water doesn't move.|By the end your arm is shaking and the water isn't.|The gull lands on your wrist in the last verse. The water doesn't notice, and neither, quite, do you.} The poem goes on for ever, and so, it feels, do you. Marisol closes the book at last. \"There. The lake would approve.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [a0a25d] = {读到第三节，你的手臂火辣辣地疼。水纹丝不动。|到最后，你的手臂在抖，水却没有。|最后一节时海鸥落在了你的手腕上。水毫无察觉，你也差不多。}那首诗像是永远念不完，你也觉得自己永远熬不完。玛丽索尔终于合上书：“好了。湖会满意的。”\n# en: Spill, and study her hands\nopt.right.label [9eabd6] = 洒了，转而看她的手\n# en: {Your cup tips over in the second verse. You spend the rest watching how Marisol turns her wrist, and write it down.|You give up on the cup and watch Marisol instead. She holds a whole pond steady while turning pages.|The gull takes your cup the moment you spill it. You watch Marisol hold hers steady through the gull's attempt on hers too.} {final_year: You'll miss her terrible poems.|Your shoes are wet.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4c881d] = {第二段时你的杯子就翻了。剩下的时间你都在看玛丽索尔怎么转手腕，并把它记了下来。|你放弃了杯子，改看玛丽索尔。她一边翻书，一边让整整一池水纹丝不动。|你的杯子一洒，海鸥就把它叼走了。你看着玛丽索尔在海鸥对她下手时，照样稳稳托住了自己那杯。}{final_year: 你会想念她那些糟糕的诗的。|你的鞋湿了。}\n\n@event evo_shaping_hour\n# en: Shaping Hour\ntitle [45641d] = 塑形课\n# en: Evocation II is shaping: flame made into a sphere, a key, a bird. The lecturer keeps a ledger of every scorch mark in the hall and makes you sign for yours. {flag(scorched_ledger): Your page in the ledger has a few signatures on it already.|Your page in the ledger is still blank.} {&Today's shape is a bird.|Today's shape is a key that turns.|Today's shape is your own initial, which is harder than it sounds.}\ntext [909b1c] = 塑能术 II 学的是塑形：把火捏成球、钥匙、鸟。讲师有一本账簿，记着讲堂里每一处焦痕，谁烧的谁签字。{flag(scorched_ledger): 你那一页上已经有几个签名了。|你在账簿上那一页还是空白。}{&今天要捏的是一只鸟。|今天要捏的是一把能转动的钥匙。|今天要捏的是你自己名字的首字母，比听上去难。}\n# en: A lecture hall full of small fires shaped like birds, keys and letters, one of them visibly trying to escape through a window.\nscene [876135] = 讲堂里满是捏成鸟、钥匙和字母形状的小火团，其中一团明显正想从窗户溜走。\n# en: A hundred plain spheres\nopt.left.label [d097ee] = 捏一百个普通火球\n# en: {&Sphere after sphere, until your hands do it without asking you.|It's dull, honest work. Your spheres come out round, every one.|Spheres until your fingers know the curve. Your initial would come easier after this, you suspect.} {season == \"summer\": The hall is an oven by noon.|The lecturer says nothing, which is approval.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c0d2bb] = {&一个接一个，直到你的手不用问你就会做。|枯燥而老实的活儿。你捏出的每个火球都是圆的。|一直捏火球，直到手指记住了那道弧线。你猜，这之后捏你的缩写会容易些。}{season == \"summer\": 不到中午，讲堂就成了烤炉。|讲师什么也没说，这就是认可。}\n# en: Try the hard shape\nopt.right.label [183457] = 挑战难的形状\n# en: {&Your bird flaps twice, circles the lectern and goes out on a perch.|Your key turns in the air, with a click you feel more than hear.|Your initial hangs in the air, crisp at every edge.} The lecturer turns the ledger round to show it hasn't moved.\nopt.right.say.1 [03bae9] = {&你的鸟扑了两下翅膀，绕讲台飞了一圈，落在栖木上熄灭了。|你的钥匙在半空中转动，那一声咔哒与其说听见，不如说感觉到。|你的首字母悬在空中，每一道边都清清楚楚。}讲师把账簿转过来给你看：一笔也没添。\n# en: {&Your bird has opinions, and leaves through the window.|Your key comes out as a fork, and a hot one.|Your initial comes out as somebody else's, and singes your cuff.} You sign the ledger. The lecturer blots it for you, almost kindly.\nopt.right.say.2 [a197f3] = {&你的鸟很有主见，从窗户飞走了。|你的钥匙捏成了一把叉子，还是烫的。|你的首字母捏成了别人的，还燎到了你的袖口。}你在账簿上签了字。讲师替你吸干了墨迹，几乎称得上温柔。\n# en: Shape it at a whisper\nopt.up.label [457949] = 轻声唤火塑形\n# en: {&You call the flame softly and it comes, small and obedient as a candle. The lecturer leans in to watch.|The shape comes out right first time, at a whisper. Your page in the ledger stays clean.|The flame stays the size you asked for, which is the whole lesson.}\nopt.up.say.1 [044f8c] = {&你轻轻唤火，它便来了，小小的，乖得像一支蜡烛。讲师凑近来看。|形状一次就成了，轻声细语之间。你在账簿上那一页依旧干干净净。|火焰保持着你要的大小——这门课教的就是这个。}\n\n@event evo_kettle_pairs\n# en: Fire Meets Water\ntitle [566646] = 水火相逢\n# en: {This is why Pyromancy and Tidecalling are taught together. One of you heats the kettle, one cools it, and it must simmer and never boil.|The kettles come out of the cupboard, every dent a story.|You know every kettle by its dents now. Two dents are yours.} {Your partner is a nervous second-year from Stillwater.|Your partner is very confident, which is worrying.|Your partner yawns hugely.}\ntext [6afbdf] = {火焰术和唤潮术之所以一起教，就是为了这个。你们一人加热水壶，一人给它降温，要它一直微沸，绝不能滚开。|水壶从柜子里搬了出来，每一处凹痕都有故事。|如今每把水壶你都能凭凹痕认出来。其中两处是你的杰作。}{你的搭档是个紧张的静水院二年级生。|你的搭档信心十足，这让人担心。|你的搭档打了个大大的哈欠。}\n# en: Pairs of students on either side of copper kettles, one with a palm of flame and one with a palm of water, steam curling between them.\nscene [a6ec61] = 学生两两站在铜水壶两侧，一人掌心托火，一人掌心托水，蒸汽在两人之间袅袅升起。\n# en: Take the fire side\nopt.left.label [60e2d6] = 负责加热\n# en: {You and your partner find the rhythm together, and the kettle hums all hour.|Push, give, push. The kettle simmers like a contented cat.|Your partner yawns through the whole hour, and the kettle simmers anyway. You've been doing both halves.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3fbb32] = {你和搭档一起找到了节奏，水壶整节课都在轻轻哼唱。|推，让，再推。水壶微微沸着，像只心满意足的猫。|你的搭档打了一节课的哈欠，水壶照样微沸。原来你一个人干了两份。}\n# en: {The kettle boils, then whistles, then lifts its lid at you. You both get a faceful of steam.|You push too hard. Your partner panics and freezes the spout, and the trapped steam bursts out of the lid at you.|Your partner falls asleep mid-lesson and the kettle boils. You both wake up very suddenly.}\nopt.left.say.2 [baf5b3] = {水壶滚了，接着鸣笛，然后冲你掀起了盖子。你们俩都被蒸汽扑了一脸。|你推得太猛。搭档一慌，把壶嘴冻住了，憋住的蒸汽从壶盖冲出来扑了你一脸。|搭档上课上到一半睡着了，水壶滚开了。你们俩都一下子醒了。}\n# en: Take the water side\nopt.right.label [9f0835] = 负责降温\n# en: {Cooling is the patient half. You hold the kettle back all hour and your partner gets the credit.|You lend the kettle your cold for an hour and feel drained by noon.|You hold the water side alone while your partner yawns. The lecturer notices who did the work.} {final_year: The kettle has been here longer than you, and will stay after you've gone.|Your fingertips stay damp until lunch.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b0936b] = {降温是需要耐心的那一半。你整节课都在压着水壶，功劳却归了搭档。|你把自己的凉意借给水壶一整节课，还没到中午就觉得被掏空了。|搭档打着哈欠，你独自撑着水的那一边。讲师注意到了是谁在干活。}{final_year: 这把水壶在这儿的年头比你长，你走后它也还会在。|你的指尖一直湿到午饭时分。}\n\n@event evo_deep_call\n# en: Calling From the Deep\ntitle [758834] = 唤自深处\n# en: {Advanced Evocation calls water from the lake bed, where it is cold and old and in no hurry.|The jetty is slick at this hour.|The jetty boards know your boots by now.} The lecturer paces behind the line. The water comes up carrying things. {Today, a bottle with no message in it.|Today, a single boot, laced.|Today, nothing at all, which is somehow worse.} {season == \"winter\": Ice grinds at the edges of the column.|Mist sits on the water.}\ntext [c7918a] = {高等塑能术要从湖底唤水，那里的水又冷又老，一点也不着急。|这个时辰栈桥很滑。|栈桥的木板已经认得你的靴子了。}讲师在队伍后面来回踱步。水上来时总带着东西。{今天是一只瓶子，里面没有信。|今天是一只靴子，鞋带还系着。|今天什么也没有，不知怎的反而更糟。}{season == \"winter\": 冰在水柱边缘咯吱作响。|水面上浮着一层雾。}\n# en: Advanced students on a jetty with their arms raised, a column of dark, old lake water rising toward them out of the depths.\nscene [285a58] = 高年级学生站在栈桥上高举双臂，一柱幽暗古老的湖水正从深处朝他们升起。\n# en: Reach deeper than the rest\nopt.left.label [3c22bf] = 探得比别人更深\n# en: {You call water up from somewhere it has been still for a century. It rises like a held breath and comes down gently.|The water obeys, then lingers, as if it wanted to talk.|You call from deeper than anyone, and the water brings nothing up at all. It feels like being refused.} The lecturer stops pacing.\nopt.left.say.1 [40261d] = {你从一个静止了百年的地方唤起水来。它像一口屏住的气那样升起，又轻轻落下。|水服从了，随后又流连不去，像是想说点什么。|你从比谁都深的地方唤水，水却什么也没带上来。感觉像是被拒绝了。}讲师停下了脚步。\n# en: {The lake pulls back. You're on your knees on the jetty, cold to the bone.|The water rises, considers you, and drops onto your head.|You reach for the deep water and something reaches back, gently, and lets go. You sit down on the jetty for a while.} The lecturer pulls you up with no comment, which is a comment.\nopt.left.say.2 [96ea88] = {湖往回一拽。你跪倒在栈桥上，冷到骨头里。|水升起来，打量了你一番，然后浇在你头上。|你朝深水伸手，有什么也轻轻伸了回来，然后松开了。你在栈桥上坐了好一会儿。}讲师一言不发地把你拉起来——不说话本身就是评语。\n# en: Keep to the shallows\nopt.right.label [98776f] = 守在浅水\n# en: {You keep to safer water and log what the others bring up. It's useful and it's dull.|You hold your column at a sensible depth and watch someone else go white with the cold.|You keep to the shallows. The others bring up nothing today, and look at the water a long time.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says you have nothing to prove. You'd like to.|You'll go deeper next time, perhaps.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1b3c19] = {你守着更安全的水域，记录别人捞上来的东西。有用，也无聊。|你把水柱维持在稳妥的深度，看着别人冻得脸色发白。|你守在浅水处。今天别人什么也没捞上来，都盯着水面看了很久。}{gpa >= 3: 讲师说你没什么需要证明的。你倒想证明一下。|下次也许再探深一点。}\n\n@event evo_signature_flame\n# en: A Fire of Your Own\ntitle [aff5b8] = 属于你的火\n# en: Advanced Evocation asks every student for a signature flame: its own colour, its own heat, its own temper. {Yours is a sulky orange.|Yours has turned a gold that you're quietly proud of.|Yours knows you now. It leans toward you when you come in.} {Somebody's flame across the hall is green and smells of pine.|Somebody's flame is silver and hums.|The lecturer's own flame is lit on the lectern, blue and tiny, never discussed.}\ntext [55236d] = 高等塑能术要求每个学生有一簇自己的标志之火：自己的颜色、自己的温度、自己的脾气。{你的是一团闹别扭的橙色。|你的已经变成一种金色，你暗暗为它骄傲。|你的火认得你了。你一进门，它就朝你倾过来。}{讲堂那头有人的火是绿的，闻起来像松林。|有人的火是银色的，还会嗡嗡响。|讲师自己的火点在讲台上，小小的，蓝色的，从不提起。}\n# en: A student alone at a stone bench in a dim hall, coaxing a flame of an unusual colour above their palm.\nscene [e0b3dd] = 昏暗的讲堂里，一个学生独自坐在石凳旁，掌心上方哄着一簇颜色不寻常的火。\n# en: Push it somewhere new\nopt.left.label [2cb28f] = 推它去新的地方\n# en: {Your flame slides into a colour nobody has a name for. The lecturer asks you to do it again, slowly.|For a moment your flame burns without smoke and without heat, just light.|Your flame leans toward the lecturer's blue one and takes a little of its colour. The lecturer covers hers.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2a3c0a] = {你的火滑进了一种没人叫得出名字的颜色。讲师请你再做一遍，慢一点。|有那么一刻，你的火燃得既无烟也无热，只有光。|你的火朝讲师那簇蓝火倾过去，借走了它一点颜色。讲师把自己的那簇罩住了。}\n# en: {Your flame flares white, takes a bite of your hand and goes back to orange as if nothing happened.|The new colour lasts a heartbeat. The blister lasts a week.|Your flame reaches for something new and finds your sleeve. It knows you, apparently, and still bites.}\nopt.left.say.2 [bd6a2a] = {你的火猛地变白，咬了你的手一口，又若无其事地变回橙色。|新颜色只维持了一次心跳。水泡却维持了一个星期。|你的火想去够点新东西，结果够到了你的袖子。它显然认得你，可照样咬。}\n# en: Refine what it already is\nopt.right.label [83d981] = 打磨它本来的样子\n# en: {You spend the morning making it steadier, not stranger. It's a better flame by noon, and a less exciting one.|You tune the heat by a hair, then another hair.|You refine it until it leans toward you on its own. That's all it does, and it's enough.} {final_year: You'll carry this fire out of Lumenhold with you.|It's yours, at least.}\nopt.right.say.1 [67bbc3] = {你花了一上午让它更稳，而不是更奇。到中午它是一簇更好的火，也是一簇更无趣的火。|你把温度调了一丝，又调了一丝。|你把它调到会自己朝你倾过来。它也就做到这一步，但够了。}{final_year: 你会带着这簇火离开流明堡。|至少，它是你的。}\n\n@event astro_tea_rota\n# en: Another Pot of Tea\ntitle [62fbae] = 又一壶茶\n# en: Astromancy I has a tea-leaf morning every week, and tea leaves require tea. {The class gets through three pots before ten.|The tea rota has your name on it again.|The teapot greets you like an old colleague.} {Your cup shows something that is either a ship or a boot.|Your cup shows a shape the textbook calls the ladder. It looks like a spoon.|Your cup shows rain. Your cup always shows rain.}\ntext [caf8a2] = 星象与占卜 I 每周都有一个读茶叶的早上，而读茶叶得先喝茶。{全班十点前就喝光了三壶。|泡茶值日表上又是你的名字。|茶壶像老同事一样跟你打招呼。}{你的杯底要么是一艘船，要么是一只靴子。|你的杯底有个形状，课本管它叫梯子。看着像把勺子。|你的杯底显示有雨。你的杯底永远显示有雨。}\n# en: A round classroom under a brass dome, every desk crowded with teacups, a huge brown teapot steaming on the lectern.\nscene [990f2d] = 黄铜穹顶下的圆形教室，每张桌上都挤满茶杯，讲台上一把棕色大茶壶冒着热气。\n# en: Drink the pot and read\nopt.left.label [d0f9d4] = 喝完一壶再读\n# en: {By the fourth cup your hands are shaking and the leaves are making more sense.|You read six cups in a row and see the pattern underneath the shapes.|Your cup shows rain again. It rains. You're starting to think that's the reading.} {season == \"winter\": At least the tea is warm.|You will not sleep tonight.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5069bc] = {到第四杯，你的手开始抖，茶叶却越来越说得通了。|你一连读了六杯，看出了形状底下的规律。|你的杯子又显出了雨。下雨了。你开始觉得那就是你的解读。}{season == \"winter\": 好在茶是热的。|你今晚别想睡了。}\n# en: Brew the next pot\nopt.right.label [213708] = 去泡下一壶\n# en: {You take the rota and brew for the room. Watching leaves settle in a whole pot teaches you more than any one cup.|You brew it strong, the way the lecturer likes it, and learn the shapes as they sink.|You brew the pot as the teapot likes it, which is the lecturer's way plus a pinch. The room reads better for it.} {season == \"summer\": The classroom is a steam bath by ten.|Nobody thanks the brewer, but everyone notices weak tea.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6aff56] = {你接下值日，给全屋泡茶。看一整壶茶叶慢慢沉淀，比读哪一只杯子学到的都多。|你按讲师喜欢的那样泡得浓浓的，看着茶叶下沉时记住了它们的形状。|你按茶壶喜欢的方式泡茶，也就是讲师的方法再多加一撮。全屋的人都读得更好了。}{season == \"summer\": 不到十点，教室就成了蒸笼。|没人感谢泡茶的人，可茶一淡，人人都会发现。}\n\n@event astro_star_names\n# en: Naming the Stars\ntitle [fb678b] = 群星之名\n# en: {Astromancy I begins with names. Three hundred stars painted on the dome, to be recited on request.|The long pole comes out. The lecturer points; you name.|You dream the star names some nights now, and wake reciting.} {Today it's the winter constellations.|Today it's the faint stars nobody can see without squinting.|Today the lecturer points at a patch of bare plaster and waits to see who invents a name.}\ntext [c2d99a] = {星象与占卜 I 从名字学起。穹顶上画着三百颗星，点到哪颗就得背出哪颗。|长杆又拿出来了。讲师指，你报名字。|如今有些夜里你会梦见星名，醒来时还在背。}{今天背冬季星座。|今天背那些不眯眼就看不见的暗星。|今天讲师指着一块光秃秃的灰泥，等着看谁会编出个名字。}\n# en: Students craning their necks under a painted dome of constellations while a lecturer points upward with a very long pole.\nscene [2a227c] = 学生们仰着脖子，头顶是画满星座的穹顶，讲师拿一根很长的杆子往上指。\n# en: Recite them until perfect\nopt.left.label [d30e26] = 背到一字不差\n# en: {You chant star names under your breath all morning, like a prayer you don't quite believe in.|By noon you can name the whole southern sky backwards.|You recite them in your sleep, apparently, and again at breakfast. Your roommate can name the southern sky now too.} {month == 4 or month == 10: The exam is close, and the names are finally sticking.|Your neck aches from looking up.}\nopt.left.say.1 [510c73] = {你一上午都在低声念星名，像在念一段自己不太信的祷词。|到中午，你已经能把整片南天倒着背出来。|显然你在梦里背，早饭时又背。你的室友现在也能报出整片南天了。}{month == 4 or month == 10: 考试近了，这些名字总算记牢了。|你的脖子仰得发酸。}\n# en: Volunteer to go first\nopt.right.label [391066] = 自告奋勇先背\n# en: {You name every star the pole touches, including the bare plaster, which you correctly call \"nothing\".|The lecturer runs out of stars before you run out of names.|The lecturer points at bare plaster. You invent a name so plausible that two people write it down.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1dc2c2] = {长杆点到哪颗你就报出哪颗，连那块灰泥也没难倒你——你准确地称它为“什么也没有”。|讲师的星星都点完了，你的名字还没报完。|讲师指向一块光秃秃的灰泥。你编了个名字，像模像样到有两个人记了下来。}\n# en: {You call a bright star by the name of a lesser one. The lesser one's champion in the back row is offended.|You blank on the fourth star, and a whole constellation falls out of your head.|The lecturer points at bare plaster and you name it confidently, with a star that doesn't exist. It's in three people's notes by lunch.}\nopt.right.say.2 [6cd980] = {你把一颗亮星叫成了一颗暗星的名字。后排那颗暗星的拥护者很不高兴。|背到第四颗你脑子一空，一整个星座从你脑袋里掉了出去。|讲师指着一块灰泥，你信心满满地叫出了一颗不存在的星。到午饭时，它已经出现在三个人的笔记里了。}\n\n@event astro_cold_dome\n# en: The Open Dome\ntitle [06fe07] = 敞开的穹顶\n# en: {Astromancy mornings start before the sun, under a dome cranked open to the sky.|The dome creaks open. You've learned which side of the stove is warmest.|The telescope's eyepiece has the shape of your eye by now.} {season == \"winter\": It is bitterly cold, and the stove is the most popular student in the class.|{season == \"summer\": The air is soft and the sky is already going pale.|There's dew on the telescope and on you.}} Somebody has to take the telescope.\ntext [7c7b4e] = {星象学的早课在日出前开始，穹顶摇开，直对天空。|穹顶吱呀呀地打开。你已经摸清了火炉哪一边最暖。|望远镜的目镜如今已经是你眼睛的形状了。}{season == \"winter\": 冷得刺骨，火炉成了班上最受欢迎的同学。|{season == \"summer\": 空气柔和，天色已经开始泛白。|望远镜上有露水，你身上也有。}}总得有人去守望远镜。\n# en: The observatory's brass dome cranked open to a pale dawn sky, one student at the great telescope and the rest huddled round an iron stove.\nscene [66aa5f] = 观星台的黄铜穹顶摇开，露出淡淡的晨空，一个学生守在巨大的望远镜前，其余人围着一只铁炉子取暖。\n# en: Take the telescope turn\nopt.left.label [d7ad20] = 轮班守望远镜\n# en: {You track a planet across the eyepiece until {season == \"winter\": your eyelashes stick to it|your eyes water and your neck gives up}.|The great telescope wanders, as it does. You coax it back to the sky you were assigned.|The eyepiece fits your eye. You find the planet before the lecturer has finished saying where it is.} {final_year: You've looked through it more times than you can count, and you'll still miss it.|{season == \"winter\": Your fingers take an hour to thaw.|You yawn through the whole of breakfast.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [3d1054] = {你在目镜里追着一颗行星，直到{season == \"winter\": 睫毛粘在了上面|眼睛流泪、脖子也撑不住了}。|巨大的望远镜照例四处乱转。你把它哄回分给你的那片天。|目镜正合你的眼。讲师还没说完它在哪儿，你已经找到了那颗行星。}{final_year: 你已经数不清从它里面看过多少次了，可你还是会想念它。|{season == \"winter\": 你的手指过了一个钟头才暖过来。|整顿早饭你都在打哈欠。}}\n# en: Stay by the stove with charts\nopt.right.label [d88b06] = 守着炉子看星图\n# en: {The stove students form a little society of the warm and the guilty. You copy charts and hear every complaint.|You annotate the charts while the others shiver. The lecturer notices who is warm.|You keep the stove and the charts. Someone else takes the telescope and complains it's shaped wrong.}\nopt.right.say.1 [166768] = {守炉子的学生自成一个小团体，又暖和又心虚。你抄着星图，听遍了每一句抱怨。|别人冻得发抖，你在给星图做批注。讲师注意到了谁是暖和的。|你守着炉子和星图。别人接了望远镜，抱怨说它的形状不对。}\n\n@event astro_foresight_drill\n# en: Catch the Chalk\ntitle [f960c8] = 接住粉笔\n# en: {Foresight at Astromancy II is practised on chalk. The lecturer drops a stick without warning; you catch it before it falls, by seeing it a heartbeat early.|The lecturer has opened a new box of chalk, which is a threat.|The lecturer's drops have got sneakier, and so have your hands.} {The floor is white with failures.|A student from Stillwater has caught four in a row and is unbearable about it.|The lecturer is dropping two at once today.}\ntext [c74eb0] = {星象与占卜 II 用粉笔练预见。讲师毫无预兆地松手，你得在粉笔落地前接住它——靠的是提前一个心跳看见它。|讲师拆开了一盒新粉笔，这是一种威胁。|讲师的出手越来越刁钻，你的手也是。}{地上一片白，全是失手的痕迹。|一个静水院的学生连接了四根，得意得让人受不了。|讲师今天一次扔两根。}\n# en: A lecturer letting a stick of chalk fall from an outstretched hand while a student lunges to catch it a moment before it drops.\nscene [9286f6] = 讲师伸手松开一根粉笔，一个学生扑上前，在它落下前的一瞬接住了它。\n# en: Catch it before it falls\nopt.left.label [351adb] = 落地前接住它\n# en: {Your hand is there before the chalk is. The lecturer looks at it, then at you, then drops another.|You catch it a breath before the drop.|Two sticks at once. You catch one in each hand, and the lecturer drops a third out of spite.}\nopt.left.say.1 [69469f] = {粉笔还没到，你的手已经在那儿了。讲师看看粉笔，看看你，又扔了一根。|你在它落下前一口气的工夫接住了。|一次两根。你一手接一根，讲师赌气又扔了第三根。}\n# en: {You see it falling, clearly, half a second after it lands.|You catch a stick of chalk that wasn't dropped. The real one hits your shoe.|Two sticks at once, and you catch the one in the past.}\nopt.left.say.2 [6af02e] = {你清清楚楚地看见它在下落——在它落地半秒之后。|你接住了一根根本没扔的粉笔。真的那根砸在了你鞋上。|一次两根，你接住的那根是过去的。}\n# en: Time the drops instead\nopt.right.label [873a97] = 改为计时观察\n# en: {You keep a tally of every drop and every catch and start to see the lecturer's rhythm.|You work out that the lecturer always blinks first. It's not foresight, but it helps.|Your tally shows the lecturer drops the second stick a beat after the first, always. It's not foresight. It works.} Holding a little seeing-charm open the whole hour to check your tally leaves your head aching. {gpa >= 3: The lecturer asks to see your tally.|You'd rather have caught one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8b3cef] = {你记下了每一次扔和每一次接，开始摸到讲师的节奏。|你发现讲师每次都先眨眼。这不是预见，但有用。|你的记录显示，讲师总是在第一根之后隔一拍扔第二根。这不是预见。但管用。}为了核对记录，你整整一小时都开着一个小小的视咒，头都疼了。{gpa >= 3: 讲师要看你的记录。|你宁愿接住一根。}\n# en: Let foresight do it\nopt.up.label [b09d2f] = 交给预见术\n# en: {You stop trying and let the spell look ahead for you. Your hand opens, and the chalk drops into it like a tame bird.|It's a two-chalk day. The spell shows you both, and you catch both.|The lecturer stops mid-drop and just looks at you. You catch it anyway.}\nopt.up.say.1 [6f00f3] = {你不再使劲，让咒语替你往前看。你的手一张开，粉笔就像一只驯服的鸟落了进来。|今天是两根粉笔的日子。咒语让你两根都看见了，你两根都接住了。|讲师扔到一半停了手，只是看着你。你照样接住了。}\n\n@event astro_starlight_jars\n# en: Bottling Starlight\ntitle [5ed382] = 装瓶星光\n# en: {Starlight is gathered before dawn, one star at a time, in glass jars on the observatory roof.|The roof is slippery with frost or dew, depending.|The starlight jars. Yours has a chip in the rim you've grown fond of.} {Most of the class is aiming for the brightest stars.|The lecturer says faint light keeps longer.|There's a slip of cloud coming in from the lake.}\ntext [ff47ba] = {星光要在黎明前采集，在观星台屋顶上一颗一颗地装进玻璃罐。|屋顶上不是霜就是露，滑得很。|星光罐。你那只罐口有个缺口，你已经对它有了感情。}{班上大多数人都瞄准最亮的星。|讲师说，暗淡的光存得更久。|一缕云正从湖上飘过来。}\n# en: Students on the observatory roof before dawn, holding glass jars up to the sky, each jar glowing faintly with captured light.\nscene [066f15] = 黎明前的观星台屋顶上，学生们把玻璃罐举向天空，每只罐里都微微亮着捕获的光。\n# en: Wait for the faint stars\nopt.left.label [ccc16b] = 等那些暗淡的星\n# en: {Your jar fills slowly with a cool, pale light that lasts all day in your pocket.|The faint stars come one at a time.|You wait out the cloud with your chipped jar. The faint stars come back for you, one by one.} The lecturer holds your jar up and says, \"That will keep.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [87f22b] = {你的罐子慢慢盛满了一种清凉苍白的光，在口袋里亮了一整天。|暗星一颗一颗地来了。|你抱着那只有缺口的罐子等云散。暗星一颗一颗地为你回来了。}讲师举起你的罐子：“这个存得住。”\n# en: {The cloud comes in before your jar is half full, and you're cold for nothing.|You wait so long the sun rises on you and burns the jar to plain glass.|The cloud from the lake settles in for the morning. Your chipped jar stays empty, and you stay cold.}\nopt.left.say.2 [abb18b] = {罐子还没装到一半，云就来了，你白挨了一场冻。|你等得太久，太阳升起来，把罐子晒回了一只普通的玻璃瓶。|湖上来的云整个早上都赖着不走。你那只有缺口的罐子一直空着，你也一直冷着。}\n# en: Take the bright, easy ones\nopt.right.label [a8446a] = 收亮的、容易的\n# en: {Your jar is full in ten minutes and glows like a lamp. The lecturer calls it a lamp.|Easy light, quickly gathered and gone by noon.|You fill your chipped jar with the bright ones before the cloud arrives. It's gone by lunch, as usual.} {season == \"summer\": The short nights are no help.|You're back in bed before the bell.}\nopt.right.say.1 [435903] = {十分钟你的罐子就满了，亮得像盏灯。讲师管它叫灯。|来得容易的光，收得快，不到中午就散了。|云来之前，你用亮星把那只有缺口的罐子装满了。照例，不到午饭就散了。}{season == \"summer\": 夏夜太短，帮不上忙。|铃响之前你就回到了床上。}\n\n@event astro_moving_charts\n# en: The Charts Move\ntitle [1e1b5e] = 会动的星图\n# en: {Advanced Astromancy uses charts that move. The inked stars drift to where they will be, not where they are.|The charts are livelier this morning, and one has crept off the table.|You've stopped flinching when the stars move. You still check twice.} {Your chart keeps rearranging itself into a shape like a doorway.|Your chart is sulking in one corner.|Your chart is going faster than the others.} Pin it and read it, or read it as it goes.\ntext [ea583c] = {高等星象学用的星图会动。墨画的星星会漂向它们将要在的位置，而不是现在的位置。|今早的星图格外活泼，有一张已经爬下了桌子。|星星移动时你已经不再吓一跳了。不过你还是会核对两遍。}{你的星图总是自己排成一个门洞似的形状。|你的星图缩在一角闹脾气。|你的星图跑得比别人的都快。}是把它钉住再读，还是任它走着读？\n# en: A long table covered in star charts whose inked constellations are visibly sliding across the parchment, students pinning corners down.\nscene [042f72] = 长桌上铺满星图，墨画的星座在羊皮纸上清晰可见地滑动，学生们正按住纸角。\n# en: Pin it and trace the drift\nopt.left.label [89dcbd] = 钉住它，描出轨迹\n# en: {You pin each star and trace its path, and for a moment you can see the whole season ahead laid out in ink.|The drift makes sense all at once.|You pin the fast chart and trace a season so quickly it makes you dizzy. The lecturer asks to keep the tracing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1a33d5] = {你把每颗星钉住，描出它的轨迹，有那么一刻，整个下一季在墨迹里铺展在你眼前。|漂移的规律一下子全通了。|你钉住那张跑得快的星图，描出一整季的轨迹，快得你头晕。讲师要了你的描图留着。}\n# en: {You pour magic into the pins to hold it, and the chart slides out from under them anyway, leaving ink on your sleeves.|You hold the chart down with a binding until your magic runs thin, then trace the drift for an hour. It goes back to where it started, laughing at you in its own way.|The fast chart drags the pins with it across the table and onto the floor. You chase it on your knees.}\nopt.left.say.2 [42508a] = {你往图钉里灌注魔力想把它按住，星图还是从底下溜走了，在你袖子上留了一片墨。|你用一道束缚把星图按住，直到魔力快要耗尽，然后描了一个钟头的轨迹。它又回到了原处，用它自己的方式嘲笑你。|那张跑得快的星图拖着图钉，一路跑过桌面掉到地上。你跪着追它。}\n# en: Sketch every shape it makes\nopt.right.label [25762b] = 画下它的每个形状\n# en: {You fill four pages with the shapes the chart passes through. Most are nothing. One is almost something.|You draw it as it moves, like a portrait of someone who won't sit still.|You sketch the fast chart at its own pace. Your drawing hand is exhausted. The shapes are beautiful.} {final_year: Soon the stars will move without you watching.|The lecturer collects your pages without a word.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6cf0ae] = {你画满了四页星图经过的形状。大多什么也不是。有一个差点是点什么。|你边看边画，像给一个坐不住的人画肖像。|你按它自己的速度画那张跑得快的星图。你画画的手累坏了。那些形状很美。}{final_year: 很快，星星就会在没有你注视的地方移动了。|讲师一言不发地收走了你的画稿。}\n\n@event astro_far_glimpse\n# en: Looking Too Far\ntitle [2bc51d] = 看得太远\n# en: {At this level Foresight reaches a season ahead. The lecturer allows one glimpse each, timed by an hourglass. People who look longer come back strange.|The dark mirror waits behind its curtain.|The hourglass is turned before you sit down. The lecturer knows your pace.} {final_year: This is your last year of looking ahead from this room, which makes the mirror harder to look at.|{The curtains are drawn against the morning.|Someone before you came out very quiet.|The mirror is very clean today, which is somehow worse.}}\ntext [8e31d1] = {到了这一级，预见术能看到一季之后。讲师只许每人看一眼，用沙漏计时。看得更久的人，回来时都有些不对劲。|暗镜在帘子后面等着。|你还没坐下，沙漏就翻过来了。讲师知道你的节奏。}{final_year: 这是你在这间屋里向前看的最后一年，这让那面镜子更难看下去。|{窗帘拉着，挡住了早晨。|你前面那个人出来时一声不吭。|镜子今天格外干净，不知怎么反而更糟。}}\n# en: A student staring into a dark mirror at the centre of a curtained room, a lecturer standing close with an hourglass.\nscene [3ee78e] = 拉着帘子的房间中央，一个学生凝视着一面暗色的镜子，讲师拿着沙漏站在近旁。\n# en: Look a season ahead\nopt.left.label [39e11f] = 看一季之后\n# en: {You see a lit window and a cup going cold, and you know exactly where and when it will be. You keep it to yourself.|You see something small and true. You'll remember it when it happens.|You see a season ahead before the hourglass is half gone. You stop yourself, which the lecturer notes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [671289] = {你看见一扇亮着的窗和一杯渐凉的茶，清楚地知道那会在何时何地。你没告诉任何人。|你看见一件小而真实的事。等它发生时你会想起来。|沙漏还没漏完一半，你就看见了一季之后。你自己停了下来，讲师记下了这一点。}\n# en: {The mirror shows you too much at once. You come out with a nosebleed and a headache that lasts till dinner.|You see nothing, and then you see everything.|You look until the sand runs out, and a little after. The lecturer pulls you back by the shoulder.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0784b6] = {镜子一下子给你看了太多。你出来时流着鼻血，头疼到晚饭。|你什么也没看见，然后看见了一切。|你一直看到沙子漏完，又多看了一点。讲师抓着你的肩膀把你拉了回来。}\n# en: Look only as far as tomorrow\nopt.right.label [9c5502] = 只看到明天\n# en: {Tomorrow, says the mirror, it will rain at noon. It does. It's not much, but it's right.|You look only one day ahead. Lunch will be soup.|You look only to tomorrow, in your timed turn. It will be cold. It is.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says caution is a talent too.|It's safe, and it feels small.}\nopt.right.say.1 [da131a] = {镜子说，明天正午会下雨。真下了。不算什么，但它是对的。|你只往前看了一天。午饭是汤。|在计时的轮次里，你只看到明天。会很冷。果然很冷。}{gpa >= 3: 讲师说谨慎也是一种天赋。|稳妥，却显得渺小。}\n\n@event alch_stir_count\n# en: Clockwise\ntitle [255eb5] = 顺时针\n# en: {Alchemy I is mostly stirring, and the stirring is counted aloud by the whole class, in unison, like a choir with spoons.|The chorus is getting good. Someone has started harmonising.|You count stirs in your sleep now, and sometimes on the stairs.} {Today's draught is a simple tonic.|Today's draught is meant to turn blue at forty.|Today the lecturer keeps changing the count to see who's listening.}\ntext [e3e914] = {炼金与治愈 I 大半是搅拌，全班齐声数着搅拌的次数，像一支拿勺子的合唱团。|合唱越来越齐了，已经有人开始唱和声。|如今你睡着了都在数搅拌，有时走楼梯也在数。}{今天的药剂是一种简单的补剂。|今天的药剂应该在第四十下时变蓝。|今天讲师不停地改拍子，看谁在认真听。}\n# en: A laboratory of identical cauldrons, a whole class stirring in unison while the lecturer counts aloud from the front.\nscene [ddbff8] = 一间摆满同样坩埚的实验室，全班整齐划一地搅拌，讲师在前面大声数数。\n# en: Count every stir aloud\nopt.left.label [363802] = 每一下都数出声\n# en: {By the hundredth stir your voice is gone and your tonic is perfect.|Your count is right and your draught is dull. Both are what was asked.|The lecturer changes the count; you change with it, aloud. Your tonic is the only one that's right.} {month == 4 or month == 10: The exam recipes come back to you in rhythm.|Your wrist aches.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b51928] = {搅到第一百下，你的嗓子哑了，补剂完美无缺。|你数得一下不差，药剂平平无奇。这两样都正是要求的。|讲师改了数，你也跟着改，大声地。全班只有你的补剂是对的。}{month == 4 or month == 10: 考试要背的配方随着节拍回到了你脑子里。|你的手腕酸了。}\n# en: Stir by the colour instead\nopt.right.label [b579ed] = 改看颜色来搅\n# en: {You stop counting and watch the colour. It turns blue exactly when you expect it to.|You lose the count and keep the draught. The lecturer can tell, and allows it.|The count changes and you don't care, because you're watching the colour. The lecturer asks how you knew.}\nopt.right.say.1 [16cf77] = {你不再数数，只盯着颜色。它正好在你预料的那一刻变蓝。|你数乱了，药剂却保住了。讲师看得出来，也由着你。|数改了，你不在乎，因为你在看颜色。讲师问你是怎么知道的。}\n# en: {You watch the colour too closely and miss it changing. The fumes make your eyes water.|Your draught turns a colour the lecturer has never seen. It's not a good sign.|You watch the colour and miss the count changing. Your draught is right for a different recipe.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ad5f71] = {你盯颜色盯得太紧，反倒错过了它变色的那一刻。烟熏得你眼泪直流。|你的药剂变成了讲师从没见过的颜色。这可不是好兆头。|你盯着颜色，错过了改数。你的药剂对的是另一个方子。}\n# en: Measure twice with Pell's spoon\nopt.up.label [f0862f] = 用佩尔的量勺量两遍\n# en: {You measure every reagent twice with the worn brass spoon. The tonic turns blue on the fortieth stir, exactly.|The spoon makes you slow, and slow makes you right. The lecturer holds your vial up to the window.|Pell's spoon has never been wrong, and today, neither are you.}\nopt.up.say.1 [e039ea] = {你用那把磨旧的黄铜勺把每种试剂都量两遍。补剂在第四十下搅拌时准时变蓝。|勺子让你慢下来，慢让你做对。讲师把你的小瓶举到窗前。|佩尔的勺子从没错过，今天你也没错。}\n# en: {You measure twice and stir once too many. The spoon was right; you weren't.|The measures are perfect. Your stirring arm gives out at sixty.|You get every measure right and the fumes wrong, and your eyes water all afternoon.}\nopt.up.say.2 [7ba041] = {你量了两遍，却多搅了一下。勺子是对的，你不是。|分量完美。你的搅拌手在第六十下没劲了。|你每样都量对了，烟雾却没躲开，整个下午眼泪直流。}\n\n@event alch_chopping_board\n# en: Prep Work\ntitle [41ffcb] = 备料\n# en: {&Half of alchemy, says the lecturer, is cutting things up correctly. The ingredients come from Pell & Daughters in the village, packed in straw, every one labelled in a careful hand.|The crate from the village is heavier today.|Your knife knows the way, and your thumb knows where not to be.} {&Today it's roots, to be sliced paper-thin.|Today it's beetles, to be crushed and not thought about.|Today it's berries, which stain everything.}\ntext [d8fc32] = {&讲师说，炼金有一半功夫在于把东西切对。材料来自村里的佩尔父女药铺，用稻草包着，每一样都贴着字迹工整的标签。|今天从村里运来的箱子更沉。|你的刀认得路，你的拇指也知道该躲在哪儿。}{&今天是根茎，要切得薄如纸。|今天是甲虫，要碾碎，而且别多想。|今天是浆果，什么都会被染色。}\n# en: A long scrubbed bench heaped with roots, berries and dried beetles, students bent over chopping boards with small sharp knives.\nscene [89c924] = 一条擦洗干净的长桌上堆满根茎、浆果和干甲虫，学生们俯身在砧板前，握着锋利的小刀。\n# en: Prep for the whole bench\nopt.left.label [65b98b] = 替整张桌备料\n# en: {&You cut enough roots for six. Your bench-mates bring you tea, which is fair.|You crush beetles for an hour and think firmly about other things.|You prepare enough berries for six, and your hands stay purple till dinner.} The crate is empty when you're done.\nopt.left.say.1 [caae87] = {&你切了六个人份的根茎。同桌给你端来了茶，这很公平。|你碾了一个钟头的甲虫，坚决地想着别的事。|你备好了六个人份的浆果，手一直紫到晚饭。}你忙完时，箱子空了。\n# en: Prep yours, read ahead\nopt.right.label [da51d3] = 切完自己的就预习\n# en: You rush your share and get to the reading, plus a small cut on your thumb you'll be explaining all day.\nopt.right.say.1 [31b264] = 你匆匆备完自己那份赶去预习，外加拇指上一道小伤口，得解释一整天。\n\n@event alch_bandage_round\n# en: Bandage Round\ntitle [f4fd11] = 绷带值班\n# en: {&The healing half of Alchemy I is taught in the infirmary, where Sister Amaranth has the class roll bandages and watch her work.|Sister Amaranth inspects every roll, and unrolls one of yours.|Sister Amaranth hands you a roll without looking up.} {&A third-year is in with a burnt hand from Evocation.|A first-year is in with a scraped knee and a great deal to say about it.|A second-year is in with a sprained wrist from the flight field.}\ntext [f052ed] = {&炼金与治愈 I 的治愈部分在医务室上，阿玛兰斯修女让全班卷绷带，看她做事。|阿玛兰斯修女每一卷都要检查，还拆开了你的一卷。|阿玛兰斯修女头也不抬地递给你一卷绷带。}{&一个三年级生上塑能术烧伤了手，正在这儿。|一个一年级生擦伤了膝盖，而且对此有很多话要说。|一个二年级生在飞行场扭伤了手腕。}\n# en: A sunny infirmary ward where students roll bandages at a long table while a nun in a starched apron checks their work.\nscene [fb0ff4] = 阳光明亮的医务室病房里，学生们在长桌边卷绷带，一位系着浆硬围裙的修女检查他们的活儿。\n# en: Roll bandages and watch\nopt.left.label [216947] = 卷绷带，在旁观察\n# en: {&You roll, and watch Sister Amaranth clean a cut in about three movements.|Your bandages pass inspection.|You roll, and watch Sister Amaranth strap a wrist so it can't move and doesn't hurt. You try it on your own wrist later.} {season == \"winter\": The ward fills with sniffling students all morning.|She says nothing, which is high praise.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ef727f] = {&你一边卷，一边看阿玛兰斯修女三两下就清理好一道伤口。|你卷的绷带通过了检查。|你一边卷，一边看阿玛兰斯修女把一只手腕绑得既动不了又不疼。后来你在自己手腕上试了试。}{season == \"winter\": 一上午，病房里挤满了吸鼻子的学生。|她什么也没说，这已是很高的评价。}\n# en: Offer to treat a patient\nopt.right.label [e0bf94] = 主动接诊一位病人\n# en: {&You treat the burn properly. Sister Amaranth says, \"Hm.\" From her, that is a speech.|You clean and bind the scraped knee neatly. The patient doesn't even wince. Sister Amaranth adjusts one knot.|You strap the wrist firmly and neatly. The patient flexes it and looks impressed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [90c6f1] = {&你把烧伤处理得很妥当。阿玛兰斯修女说了声“嗯”。从她嘴里出来，这就算一篇演讲了。|你把擦伤的膝盖清理包扎得干净利落。病人连眉头都没皱。阿玛兰斯修女只调了一个结。|你把手腕绑得又紧又整齐。病人活动了一下，一脸佩服。}\n# en: {&You bandage the wrong place entirely. The patient is too polite to say so; Sister Amaranth is not.|Your bandage unrolls itself and the patient has to wait while you start again.|You strap the wrist so tightly the patient's fingers go white. Sister Amaranth loosens it, and looks at you.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ffb39c] = {&你完全包错了地方。病人太客气没说，阿玛兰斯修女可不客气。|你的绷带自己散开了，病人只好等你从头再来。|你把手腕绑得太紧，病人的手指都发白了。阿玛兰斯修女把它松开，看了你一眼。}\n\n@event alch_purify_samples\n# en: Lake Water Samples\ntitle [468eaa] = 湖水样本\n# en: {Alchemy II hands out jars of murky lake water, to be made fit to drink. The lecturer has a habit of drinking the results.|The lecturer looks thirsty, which is never a good sign.|The lecturer has brought a favourite cup for this, chipped at the rim.} {Your jar has a small weed in it.|Your jar is suspiciously green.|Your jar has a tiny snail in it, which you set free.}\ntext [8b5060] = {炼金与治愈 II 发下一罐罐浑浊的湖水，要处理到能喝。讲师有个习惯，总要亲口喝下成果。|讲师看上去渴了，这从来不是好兆头。|讲师为此带来了一只心爱的杯子，杯沿有个缺口。}{你的罐子里有一根小水草。|你的罐子绿得可疑。|你的罐子里有只小蜗牛，你把它放生了。}\n# en: A bench lined with jars of murky lake water, a lecturer at the end raising one now-clear jar to drink it.\nscene [3c5465] = 实验桌上摆着一排浑浊湖水的罐子，桌尾的讲师正举起一罐已变清澈的水要喝。\n# en: Purify it thoroughly\nopt.left.label [be5a08] = 彻底净化\n# en: {Your water comes out clear and sweet. The lecturer drinks it, pauses and nods.|Clean as rainwater.|Your water goes into the lecturer's chipped cup, and the lecturer drinks it to the bottom.} {flag(purified_well): The lecturer drinks a second glass, and pretends not to.|The lecturer drinks a second glass, which nobody has seen before.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f9cb5c] = {你的水出来清澈甘甜。讲师喝了，顿了一下，点点头。|干净得像雨水。|你的水倒进了讲师那只有缺口的杯子，讲师一饮而尽。}{flag(purified_well): 讲师喝了第二杯，还假装没有。|讲师又喝了第二杯，这可是谁都没见过的。}\n# en: {You test it first, just a sip, as the lecturer always does. You spend the afternoon close to the washroom.|Your water clears, then clouds again. You taste it anyway, to be sure, and regret it all afternoon.|You purify it so hard the snail you set free comes back to complain. The water tastes of snail anyway.}\nopt.left.say.2 [02a39b] = {你学讲师的样子先抿了一口试试。整个下午你都待在离盥洗室不远的地方。|你的水清了，又浑了。为了确认，你还是尝了一口，后悔了一下午。|你净化得太狠，连你放走的那只小蜗牛都回来抗议了。水还是有股蜗牛味。}\n# en: Filter it the slow way\nopt.right.label [425b53] = 用老办法慢慢滤\n# en: {Cloth, sand and charcoal, the old way. It takes the whole lesson.|You filter it by hand and write every step down. The lecturer drinks it and says, \"Patient.\"|You filter it slowly and the snail goes back to the lake in a cup of its own. The lecturer approves of both.} {final_year: You'll be filtering water like this for the rest of your life, probably.|Your hands are grey with charcoal.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e4ea79] = {布、沙、木炭，老办法。花了整整一节课。|你亲手过滤，把每一步都记了下来。讲师喝了，说：“有耐心。”|你慢慢地过滤，那只蜗牛被装在专属的杯子里送回了湖里。讲师对两件事都表示赞许。}{final_year: 这辈子你大概都会这样滤水了。|你的手被木炭染得灰扑扑的。}\n\n@event alch_long_simmer\n# en: The Long Simmer\ntitle [191077] = 漫长的文火\n# en: {Some potions simmer for hours and must be watched. Whoever is on watch when the colour turns gets the credit, or the blame.|The class takes turns at the cauldron, and the rota has come round.|Your turn at the simmer. You've brought a cushion this time.} {The rest of the class has gone to lunch.|The potion is making a noise like a contented cat.|The lecturer has left a note that says \"Do not leave it.\"}\ntext [c30c9e] = {有些药剂要文火熬上好几个钟头，得有人看着。颜色变的那一刻谁在值守，功劳或黑锅就归谁。|全班轮流守坩埚，值日表又轮回来了。|轮到你守文火了。这回你带了个坐垫。}{其他人都去吃午饭了。|药剂发出的声音像只心满意足的猫。|讲师留了张字条，上面写着“别走开”。}\n# en: A bubbling cauldron in an empty laboratory, a single student slumped on a stool beside it with an hourglass and a book.\nscene [d188f1] = 空荡荡的实验室里，一口坩埚咕嘟冒泡，旁边一个学生瘫坐在凳子上，手边一只沙漏、一本书。\n# en: Sit the whole watch\nopt.left.label [85d1b0] = 守满整班\n# en: {Three hours, one cauldron, one hourglass. You see it turn and catch it at the right moment.|You read the same paragraph forty times and stir at every bubble.|You sit the watch on your cushion, as the note says. The potion turns, and you're there.} {season == \"summer\": The laboratory is stifling.|By the end, you can tell its mood from the bubbles.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fde0ad] = {三个钟头，一口坩埚，一只沙漏。你看着它变色，在恰好的时刻接住了。|同一段话你读了四十遍，每冒一个泡就搅一下。|你照字条说的，坐在你的垫子上守着。药剂变色了，你就在那儿。}{season == \"summer\": 实验室闷得透不过气。|到最后，你能从气泡听出它的心情。}\n# en: Set a charm to watch it\nopt.right.label [5a82a4] = 设个咒语替你看\n# en: {Your charm rings like a bell when the colour turns. You come back from lunch in time to take it off the fire.|The charm works and you get to eat.|You set the charm and leave a note of your own under the lecturer's: \"I didn't, technically.\" It works.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4479a5] = {颜色一变，你的咒语就像铃铛一样响了。你吃完午饭回来，正好把它端下火。|咒语管用，你也吃上了饭。|你设好咒语，在讲师的字条下面留了一张自己的：“严格来说，我没走。”成功了。}\n# en: {Your charm falls asleep. The potion boils over and you breathe in a lot of steam getting it off the fire.|The charm rings for no reason, three times, and the fourth time you ignore it. That was the real one: you get a faceful of steam pulling the cauldron off the fire.|The charm watches it perfectly until the lecturer's note blows onto it. Then it watches the note.}\nopt.right.say.2 [17c1a9] = {你的咒语睡着了。药剂溢了出来，你把它端下火时吸了一大口蒸汽。|咒语无缘无故响了三次，第四次你没理它。偏偏那次是真的：你把坩埚端下火时被蒸汽扑了一脸。|咒语看得好好的，直到讲师的字条被风吹到它上面。然后它就盯着字条看了。}\n\n@event alch_volatile_reagents\n# en: Volatile Reagents\ntitle [2e9938] = 不稳定的试剂\n# en: {Advanced Alchemy handles reagents that react to being looked at the wrong way. The lecturer has one rule. One person speaks at a time, and that person is the one holding the vial.|Nobody is speaking. Somebody is breathing too loudly.|You've stopped holding your breath, mostly.} {Today's vial is pale yellow and fizzing.|Today's vial is dark red and perfectly still, which is worse.|Today's vial is labelled in the lecturer's hand: \"Gently.\"}\ntext [1918d3] = {高等炼金学处理的试剂，被人看错一眼都会起反应。讲师只有一条规矩：一次只许一个人说话，那个人就是拿着药瓶的人。|没人说话。有人喘气声太大了。|你已经不再屏住呼吸了，大体上。}{今天的药瓶是淡黄色的，滋滋冒泡。|今天的药瓶是暗红色的，一动不动，这更糟。|今天的药瓶上是讲师的笔迹：“轻拿。”}\n# en: A hushed laboratory, a student holding a glass vial of glowing liquid at arm's length, everyone else very still behind a screen.\nscene [448114] = 鸦雀无声的实验室里，一个学生伸直手臂捏着一只发光的玻璃药瓶，其余人都一动不动地躲在屏风后。\n# en: Handle it yourself\nopt.left.label [733ca1] = 亲手处理\n# en: {You measure it drop by drop, speaking only the numbers. It settles exactly as it should.|Your hands don't shake.|You handle it gently, as labelled, and it behaves for you. The lecturer takes the label off and keeps it.} The lecturer lets out a breath.\nopt.left.say.1 [38830a] = {你一滴一滴地量，只开口念数字。它不偏不倚地沉静下来。|你的手一点没抖。|你照标签说的轻手轻脚，它也乖乖听话。讲师揭下标签收了起来。}讲师长舒了一口气。\n# en: {The vial pops like a cork. Your eyebrows survive; your gloves do not.|A drop too many. The flask hisses and coughs out blue smoke, and you spend the hour in the infirmary.|You handle it gently, which turns out not to be gently enough. The label lands in your hair.}\nopt.left.say.2 [d5e284] = {药瓶像软木塞一样砰地一响。你的眉毛保住了，手套没保住。|多了一滴。烧瓶嘶嘶作响，咳出一股蓝烟，你在医务室待了一个钟头。|你轻手轻脚了，结果还不够轻。标签落在了你头发上。}\n# en: Pass it to the steadiest hand\nopt.right.label [699cc4] = 交给手最稳的人\n# en: {You hand the vial to someone with steadier hands and take careful notes. It stings a little.|You watch someone else do it right. You'll know how next time.|You pass the \"Gently\" vial on. The steady hand drops it, very gently, into the sink. Everyone breathes again.} {gpa >= 3: The lecturer says you're allowed to be cautious.|You note every step.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7c59ec] = {你把药瓶递给手更稳的人，自己仔细记笔记。心里有点刺痛。|你看着别人做对了。下次你就知道怎么做了。|你把那瓶“轻拿”递了出去。那只稳手非常轻地把它丢进了水槽。大家又能喘气了。}{gpa >= 3: 讲师说你可以谨慎。|你记下了每一步。}\n\n@event alch_original_remedy\n# en: A Remedy of Your Own\ntitle [8095a0] = 你自己的药方\n# en: {Advanced Alchemy asks for an original remedy, invented, written up, and tested under Sister Amaranth's eye.|Another revision. The notebook has more crossings-out than words.|Your remedy is nearly finished. It has been nearly finished for weeks.} {This version is for headaches.|This version is for the common cold, which has resisted remedies for centuries.|This version is for exam nerves, which you need.}\ntext [554fde] = {高等炼金学要求一份原创药方：自己发明、自己写成，在阿玛兰斯修女的注视下试验。|又一次修改。笔记本上划掉的比留下的还多。|你的药方快完成了。它已经“快完成了”好几个星期。}{这一版治头疼。|这一版治普通感冒——几百年来它一直抵抗着各种药方。|这一版治考前紧张，你正需要。}\n# en: A student at a cluttered bench writing a recipe in a notebook, a small flask of their own remedy steaming beside it.\nscene [811658] = 一个学生在杂乱的实验桌前往笔记本上写配方，旁边一小瓶自制药剂冒着热气。\n# en: Test it on yourself\nopt.left.label [2dfc84] = 在自己身上试\n# en: {It works. Your headache lifts and your mood with it.|It works better than you expected.|It works. Your hands stop shaking, and for an afternoon so does your dread of exams.} {flag(remedy_in_book): Sister Amaranth adds a note to your page in her book.|Sister Amaranth writes your recipe in her own book.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4e43d6] = {成了。头疼消了，心情也跟着好了。|效果比你预想的还好。|成了。你的手不抖了，一个下午里，你对考试的恐惧也不抖了。}{flag(remedy_in_book): 阿玛兰斯修女在本子里你那一页添了一条批注。|阿玛兰斯修女把你的配方抄进了她自己的本子。}\n# en: {It does something, but not what you wanted. Your ears are warm for the rest of the day.|It cures the sniffles and replaces them with hiccups.|It calms your nerves so thoroughly that you fall asleep at the bench.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f580ae] = {它确实起了作用，只是不是你想要的。你的耳朵热了一整天。|鼻塞治好了，换来了打嗝。|它把你的神经安抚得太彻底，你在工作台前睡着了。}\n# en: Ask Sister Amaranth to review it\nopt.right.label [1828ec] = 请阿玛兰斯修女审阅\n# en: {She crosses out three ingredients and one adjective.|She reads it twice, then hands it back. \"Sound,\" she says. \"Dull.\"|She reads it and takes one dose herself, before the exam she's invigilating. \"Adequate,\" she says, calmly.} {final_year: You'll keep this recipe long after you leave Lumenhold.|It's better already, and you hate it a little.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4f9f35] = {她划掉了三种材料和一个形容词。|她读了两遍，递还给你。“可靠，”她说，“乏味。”|她读完，在她要监考的那场考试前自己服了一剂。“尚可，”她说，很平静。}{final_year: 离开流明堡很久以后，你还会留着这张药方。|已经好多了，你却有点恨它。}\n# en: Distill the base first\nopt.up.label [99a042] = 先把基底萃取\n# en: {You distill the base to its strength before you start, and pay for it with a yawn. The remedy comes out clear and gentle.|The base comes down to one clean drop, and you feel the drop in your knees. Sister Amaranth smells it and says nothing, which is approval.|With the base distilled, the fault in your recipe finally shows. You fix it, a little light-headed.}\nopt.up.say.1 [029546] = {动手之前，你把基底萃取到只剩精华，代价是一个哈欠。药剂清澈温和。|基底浓缩成干净的一滴，你在膝盖里感觉到了那一滴。阿玛兰斯修女闻了闻，什么也没说——这就是认可。|基底一萃，配方里的毛病终于显了出来。你把它改好了，有点头晕。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_b.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_b.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event illu_candle_drill\n# en: The Candle Drill\ntitle [81a84c] = 蜡烛操练\n# en: {Illusion I opens with a row of cold candles. Make them burn, Professor Vey says, without burning.|Vey's drawer of candles is open. Every one is older than you.|The candle drill. Some mornings you could do it asleep.} {Vey passes her hand through each flame and says \"cold\" in the same flat voice.|Someone's flame leans in a draught only it can feel.|The student beside you has made a lovely candle and forgotten the flame.}\ntext [a2d324] = {幻术 I 以一排冷蜡烛开场。让它们燃烧，维伊教授说，但不许真的烧起来。|维伊那一抽屉蜡烛开着，每一根都比你年纪大。|蜡烛操练。有些早晨你闭着眼都能做。} {维伊把手掌从每一朵火焰中穿过，用同样平板的声音说：“冷的。”|有人的火焰朝一侧倾斜，像在躲一阵只有它感觉得到的穿堂风。|你旁边的同学造出了一根漂亮的蜡烛，却忘了火焰。}\n# en: A row of unlit candles on a lecture bench, each crowned with a flame that casts no shadow, a professor holding her palm over one.\nscene [f627aa] = 讲台长桌上一排未点燃的蜡烛，每根都顶着一朵不投影子的火焰，一位教授把手掌悬在其中一朵上方。\n# en: Hold the flame until it's right\nopt.left.label [6ee431] = 守着火焰直到对为止\n# en: {Your flame steadies, then flickers in time with your breathing, which Vey says is the point.|Vey's palm hovers over your flame a moment longer than the others. \"Almost warm,\" she says. From her, that's a sonnet.|By the bell your flame has a proper blue heart. You feel oddly proud of something that isn't there.}\nopt.left.say.1 [11c5cf] = {你的火焰稳住了，随后跟着你的呼吸一起闪烁，维伊说这正是要点。|维伊的手掌在你的火焰上方比别人多停了一会儿。“几乎是暖的。”她说。出自她口，这简直是一首十四行诗。|下课铃响时，你的火焰有了一颗蓝色的芯。你为一样根本不存在的东西感到莫名骄傲。}\n# en: Make it gutter and smoke\nopt.right.label [5f6b6b] = 让它摇曳冒烟\n# en: {You add a thread of smoke and a little gutter. The student behind you reaches to pinch it out.|Your candle drips wax that isn't there onto a desk that is. Vey watches it fall and says nothing, which means yes.|Your candle gutters and smokes so well that the student beside you lends it their flame. Vey writes \"two for one\".}\nopt.right.say.1 [e4aead] = {你添上一缕烟和一点摇曳。后排的同学伸手想把它掐灭。|你的蜡烛把不存在的蜡滴在真实的桌面上。维伊看着它落下，一言不发，意思是行。|你的蜡烛摇曳冒烟得太逼真，旁边的同学把自己的火苗借给了它。维伊写下“一举两得”。}\n# en: {The smoke goes up and the flame goes out, as though embarrassed by its own ambition.|Vey waves a hand through the whole performance. \"A candle doesn't try this hard,\" she says.|You add so much smoke the drill becomes a fog. Vey opens a window that isn't there, and the fog obligingly leaves.}\nopt.right.say.2 [b6c491] = {烟升了起来，火却灭了，像是为自己的野心难为情。|维伊一挥手穿过你的整场表演。“蜡烛不会这么卖力。”她说。|你添的烟太多，练习成了一场雾。维伊打开一扇并不存在的窗，雾也很配合地出去了。}\n\n@event illu_one_small_change\n# en: One Small Change\ntitle [d7183c] = 一处小改动\n# en: {Hand mirrors all round. Glamour your own face, Vey says; change one small thing and hold it.|Out come the mirrors. A glamour you can't hold on yourself, Vey says, won't hold on anyone.|Mirror practice. You know your own face too well by now.} {Vey says people always change their eyes first.|A second-year from Rootward is giving himself a better chin, slowly.|The mirrors fog, as they do {season == \"winter\": in the cold|when the room warms}.}\ntext [2e07ab] = {人手一面小镜子。给自己的脸施魅影，维伊说；改一处小地方，并且维持住。|镜子拿出来了。维伊说，连自己身上都撑不住的魅影，在谁身上也撑不住。|镜子练习。到如今你对自己的脸太熟悉了。} {维伊说，人们总是先改眼睛。|一个根庐的二年级生正在慢慢给自己换个更好看的下巴。|镜子起了雾，{season == \"winter\": 天冷时总是这样。|屋里一暖就会这样。}}\n# en: Students bent over little hand mirrors, one changing the colour of her own eyes, another squinting at a nose that isn't quite his.\nscene [274c55] = 学生们俯身对着小手镜，一个正在改变自己眼睛的颜色，另一个眯眼看着一只不太像自己的鼻子。\n# en: Change something trivial\nopt.left.label [1bc550] = 改点无关紧要的\n# en: {You move a freckle half an inch. It takes the whole hour. Vey initials your page without looking up.|You darken one eyebrow and keep it dark. Dull, careful work, and the glamour holds.|A small scar vanishes and comes back, vanishes and comes back. By the end it stays gone until you let it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a7b2a4] = {你把一颗雀斑挪了半寸，花了整整一节课。维伊头也不抬地在你的纸上签了字。|你把一边眉毛描深，一直保持着。枯燥又细致的活儿，魅影稳住了。|一道小疤消失又出现，消失又出现。到最后，除非你放手，它一直不见。}\n# en: Change what you'd really change\nopt.right.label [e542b4] = 改你真正想改的\n# en: {It holds beautifully. You look at it longer than the exercise requires, and letting it go is the hard part. The mirror hands back the face you came in with.|Vey glances over your shoulder. \"Ah,\" she says, kindly enough. \"That one. Everybody has one.\" When the hour ends you give it back, and the old face comes back with the old feeling.|You hold it all hour. Afterwards your real face seems slightly unfamiliar, like a coat you've lent out, and you don't like it any better for the rest.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3a9132] = {它维持得很美。你看它的时间比练习要求的长，而放手才是最难的。镜子把你进门时那张脸还给了你。|维伊从你肩后瞥了一眼。“啊，”她说，语气还算温和，“那一处。每个人都有一处。”下课时你把它还了回去，旧的脸回来了，旧的心事也跟着回来了。|你维持了整节课。之后你真正的脸显得有点陌生，像一件借出去又还回来的外套，而你并没有因此更喜欢它。}\n\n@event illu_spot_the_coin\n# en: Spot the Coin\ntitle [5eda6f] = 找出那枚硬币\n# en: {Partner work. {partner} lays out three crowns, and one of them is an illusion. Say which.|Three crowns from {partner}. One isn't there, and {they} look{s} pleased about it.|You and {partner} have started keeping score.} {Vey walks the room flicking real coins, to confuse everyone.|The windows are bright and the illusions show it.|The next bench is arguing whether a coin can be \"partly fake\".}\ntext [6cbe59] = {搭档练习。{partner}摆出三枚克朗，其中一枚是幻象。说出是哪一枚。|{partner}摆出三枚克朗。有一枚并不存在，{they}看起来对此很得意。|你和{partner}已经开始记分了。} {维伊在教室里走动，往空中弹真硬币，存心把大家搅糊涂。|窗外很亮，幻象在光里露了馅。|隔壁桌在争论一枚硬币能不能“部分是假的”。}\n# en: Two students at a bench with three coins between them, one coin shimmering very slightly at the edge.\nscene [056f6c] = 两个学生对坐在长桌旁，中间放着三枚硬币，其中一枚边缘微微闪烁。\n# en: Study the coins honestly\nopt.left.label [1297d9] = 老老实实看硬币\n# en: {It's the one with no tarnish. Illusions always come out cleaner than money does.|You stare until your eyes water and pick the middle one. Wrong. {partner} pockets the real two, laughing.|You get it on the third try, by the shine that doesn't move when the light does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [69ff81] = {是没有锈迹的那枚。幻象总比真钱干净。|你盯到眼睛发酸，选了中间那枚。错了。{partner}笑着把两枚真的收进口袋。|第三次你猜对了，凭的是那道不随光线移动的反光。}\n# en: Watch their face, not the coins\nopt.right.label [8b4837] = 看对方的脸，不看硬币\n# en: {{partner} glances at the left coin a fraction too fondly. You point. {They} groan{s}.|You keep smiling until {partner} gives it away by smiling back. Vey notes that this isn't the exercise, and that it worked.|You know {partner}'s face by now. {They} know{s} you know, and pick{s} a coin with a straight face to beat you. It doesn't work.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fda8ff] = {{partner}瞄向左边那枚的眼神稍稍太温柔了。你一指。{they}哀叹一声。|你一直笑，直到{partner}回了你一个笑，露了底。维伊指出这不是练习的内容，也指出它奏效了。|你现在已经熟悉{partner}的表情了。{they}知道你熟悉，于是板着脸挑了一枚来对付你。没用。}\n# en: {{partner} has a better face than you'd thought. Your wrong guess buys {them} a cup of tea.|You watch {partner} so hard you miss the coin blinking out of existence entirely.|{partner} has been practising a blank face in the mirror. It shows. You lose a point and buy the tea.}\nopt.right.say.2 [86633f] = {{partner}的表情管理比你想的好。你猜错的代价是请{them}喝一杯茶。|你盯{partner}盯得太用力，完全没看见那枚硬币凭空消失。|{partner}一直在对着镜子练面无表情。看得出来。你丢了一分，还得请喝茶。}\n\n@event illu_bread_smell\n# en: The Smell of Bread\ntitle [9ea386] = 面包的香味\n# en: {Illusion II adds the other senses. Today, a loaf that looks, smells and sounds fresh when broken.|More bread. Sight is easy, Vey says; smell is where liars are caught.|The bread lesson. The floor smells of loaves that never existed.} {Somebody's loaf smells strongly of wet dog.|One loaf crackles beautifully and smells of nothing.|{gpa >= 3: Vey asks you to go first.|Vey asks you to go last, which feels pointed.}}\ntext [0bd632] = {幻术 II 加入了其他感官。今天的题目是一条看起来、闻起来、掰开时听起来都新鲜的面包。|面包课还没完。视觉容易，维伊说；骗子都栽在气味上。|面包课。整层楼都弥漫着从未存在过的面包的味道。} {有人的面包浓浓一股湿狗味。|有一条面包咔嚓作响，却什么味道也没有。|{gpa >= 3: 维伊让你第一个来。|维伊让你最后一个来，像是意有所指。}}\n# en: A lecture bench with an illusory loaf of bread on a board, steam rising from it, and several students sniffing it doubtfully.\nscene [92b758] = 讲台长桌上，木板上放着一条幻象面包，正冒着热气，几个学生半信半疑地凑上去闻。\n# en: Get the smell right\nopt.left.label [721e15] = 把香味做对\n# en: {You spend an hour on the smell of a crust. By the end it's nearly convincing and you're desperately hungry.|You think of a bakery on a cold street and the smell arrives with it. Vey sniffs, and nods once.|The smell is right. The sound of breaking is wrong. Vey says that's the usual order.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5c9a39] = {你花了一小时琢磨面包皮的味道。到最后几乎以假乱真，而你饿得发慌。|你想起寒冷街角的一家面包房，香味便随之而来。维伊嗅了嗅，点了一下头。|香味对了，掰开的声音不对。维伊说这是常见的顺序。}\n# en: Add the warmth of the oven\nopt.right.label [111260] = 加上烤炉的温度\n# en: {Vey picks your loaf up and puts it down hastily, as if it had burnt her. The class actually applauds.|The bench beside you swears the room got warmer. It didn't. That's the point.|You add the warmth of the oven and the smell of the morning after. Vey takes your loaf home, she claims, by mistake.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7b1411] = {维伊拿起你的面包，又匆忙放下，像被烫着了。全班真的鼓起了掌。|旁边那桌发誓屋里变暖了。并没有。这正是重点。|你添上了烤炉的温度和第二天清晨的气味。维伊把你的面包带回了家，她说是拿错了。}\n# en: {The warmth arrives and the loaf leaves. You're left holding a hot, empty board.|Your bread steams, then smokes, then smells of a cupboard. Vey writes something short.|You add warmth, and the smell goes with it. The loaf is perfect and hot and smells of absolutely nothing.}\nopt.right.say.2 [9771e5] = {温度来了，面包走了。你手里只剩一块发烫的空木板。|你的面包冒气，然后冒烟，然后有股橱柜味。维伊写下了很短的一句评语。|你添了温度，气味却跟着没了。面包完美、滚烫，而且一点味道都没有。}\n\n@event illu_find_vey\n# en: Find the Professor\ntitle [4d9a54] = 找出教授\n# en: {Professor Vey is rarely at the lectern in Illusion II. She is somewhere in the room, wearing someone else, and the first to name her wins.|Vey's chair is empty. Everyone eyes the porter with the mop.|The lectern is empty. You trust nothing in this room.} {There's a pigeon on the sill.|A student nobody remembers has taken the back row.|One of the portraits seems unusually attentive.}\ntext [93c884] = {幻术 II 的课上，维伊教授很少站在讲台后。她就在教室某处，扮成别人，第一个叫出她的人获胜。|维伊的椅子空着。大家都盯着那个拿拖把的杂役。|讲台空着。这间屋里你什么都不信了。} {窗台上有只鸽子。|后排坐着一个谁也不记得的学生。|有幅肖像画看起来格外专注。}\n# en: A lecture hall where the professor's chair is empty and everyone is looking suspiciously at everyone else, including a pigeon on the sill.\nscene [f6ad02] = 一间讲堂，教授的椅子空着，每个人都狐疑地打量着别人，包括窗台上的一只鸽子。\n# en: Watch everyone, all lesson\nopt.left.label [b92af8] = 整节课盯着每个人\n# en: {You don't find her, but you notice who blinks late. Vey turns out to have been the pigeon.|At the end she steps out of the porter's coat. You'd suspected the mop. The mop was real.|You learn three classmates' nervous habits and nothing about Vey, who was the attentive portrait all along.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e45672] = {你没找到她，但留意到了谁眨眼慢半拍。原来维伊是那只鸽子。|最后她从门房的外套里走了出来。你本来怀疑的是拖把。拖把是真的。|你摸清了三个同学的紧张小动作，却对维伊一无所知——她从头到尾都是那幅专注的肖像。}\n# en: Name her early\nopt.right.label [cf9752] = 抢先叫出她\n# en: {You name her before the bell has finished ringing. She says, \"Hm,\" which from Vey means a great deal.|\"Professor,\" you say to the new student in the back row. The glamour falls away like a dropped shawl. Vey looks almost pleased.|You name the attentive portrait. The paint slides off Vey's face like a curtain. \"Hm,\" she says.}\nopt.right.say.1 [80bf8b] = {铃声还没响完，你就叫出了她。她说了声“嗯”，从维伊嘴里说出来，分量很重。|“教授。”你对后排那个新面孔说。魅影像一条滑落的披肩般褪去。维伊看上去几乎是满意的。|你叫出了那幅专注的肖像。颜料像帘子一样从维伊脸上滑落。“嗯，”她说。}\n# en: {You accuse a perfectly ordinary classmate. He will remember it. So will Vey, who was standing behind you.|You accuse the quiet student in the back row, who turns out to be a quiet student in the back row. Vey was the mop.|You point at the portrait. The portrait points back. Vey, at the lectern after all, makes a note.}\nopt.right.say.2 [4d12ea] = {你指认了一个再普通不过的同学。他会记住这件事。维伊也会——她就站在你身后。|你指认了后排那个安静的学生，结果那真的只是个后排的安静学生。维伊是那把拖把。|你指向那幅肖像。肖像也指向你。维伊原来就在讲台后，记了一笔。}\n\n@event illu_courtyard_hour\n# en: Holding the Courtyard\ntitle [9a561c] = 撑住庭院\n# en: {Advanced Illusion leaves the classroom. Hold an illusion over the busy courtyard for an hour, and don't let it fray.|Another courtyard hour. The porters step round you now.|The courtyard. {final_year: One of the last times you'll hold this sky.|Your hands know the weight of it.}} {A flock of swallows today.|Rain that doesn't wet anyone.|{season == \"winter\": Snow that never lands.|{season == \"spring\": Blossom that never lands.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves that never land.|Thistledown that never lands.}}}}\ntext [3cbd95] = {高等幻术走出了教室。在热闹的庭院上空维持一个幻象一小时，别让它散开。|庭院一小时，照旧。杂役们现在会绕着你走。|庭院。{final_year: 这是你最后几次撑起这片天空了。|你的双手已熟悉它的分量。}} {今天是一群燕子。|淋不湿任何人的雨。|{season == \"winter\": 永远落不了地的雪。|{season == \"spring\": 永远落不了地的花瓣。|{season == \"autumn\": 永远落不了地的落叶。|永远落不了地的蓟花绒。}}}}\n# en: A student standing very still in a busy courtyard, sweat on their brow, while a flock of illusory swallows circles above the crowd.\nscene [84d039] = 一个学生一动不动地站在热闹的庭院里，额上冒汗，一群幻象燕子在人群上方盘旋。\n# en: Hold it the full hour\nopt.left.label [f25e9f] = 撑满一整个小时\n# en: {By the end your head rings like a struck bell. Nobody looked up once, which is how you know it worked.|A first-year holds out a hand to catch something you made. You keep it falling for her until the bell.|You hold it, then sit on the fountain's edge for longer than you'd like anyone to see.}\nopt.left.say.1 [40c796] = {结束时你的脑袋嗡嗡作响，像被敲过的钟。没有一个人抬头看过，这说明你成功了。|一个一年级生伸手去接你造出来的东西。你一直让它为她飘落，直到下课铃响。|你撑住了，然后在喷泉边坐了好一会儿，比你愿意让人看见的更久。}\n# en: Keep it small and steady\nopt.right.label [e4edc7] = 做小一点，求稳\n# en: {You keep only a corner of it, perfect, for an hour. Vey says ambition is cheap and an hour is not.|You shrink it to a patch of weather over one bench. It never frays, and nobody notices, which is the point and the pity.|You keep it small: a few flakes over one bench, falling for the hour. A first-year sits under it and doesn't know why she's happy.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2f1933] = {你只留住它的一角，完美无瑕，整整一小时。维伊说野心廉价，一小时可不廉价。|你把它缩成一张长椅上空的一小片天气。它始终没散，也没人注意到——这既是重点，也是遗憾。|你把它缩得很小：一张长椅上空飘着几片，落了整整一个钟头。一个一年级生坐在下面，不知道自己为什么高兴。}\n\n@event illu_original_work\n# en: An Illusion of Your Own\ntitle [61c895] = 你自己的幻象\n# en: {Original work, an illusion nobody taught you. Vey asks one question of each piece. \"What does it make people feel?\"|More original work. Vey's question hasn't changed; your answers get longer.|Studio morning. {final_year: Next year, Vey says, she'll ask someone else.|Vey goes round the room asking her question.}} {A classmate has made a door that sighs.|Someone has made the smell of rain on hot stone.|Someone's illusion is humming.}\ntext [2e4955] = {原创作业，一个没人教过你的幻象。维伊对每件作品只问一个问题。“它让人感受到什么？”|原创作业继续。维伊的问题没变，你的回答越来越长。|工作室的早晨。{final_year: 明年，维伊说，她就要问别人了。|维伊在屋里挨个问她的问题。}} {有个同学做了一扇会叹气的门。|有人做出了雨落在热石头上的气味。|有人的幻象在哼歌。}\n# en: A darkened studio room, a student alone at the centre, and around them the half-formed image of a lit window at dusk.\nscene [62326f] = 一间昏暗的工作室，一个学生独自站在中央，四周是一扇黄昏亮灯的窗户的半成形影像。\n# en: Make something true\nopt.left.label [dd57e1] = 做一件真实的东西\n# en: {You make the window of a house you grew up near, at dusk. Vey stands in front of it a long time and writes nothing down.|You make a corridor that remembers who walked down it. It's sadder than you meant. Vey says that's usually the good sign.|You make the hum of a kitchen at night, kettle cooling. Vey sits down in front of it, which she never does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4d3103] = {你做出了童年附近一栋房子黄昏时的窗。维伊在它前面站了很久，什么也没写。|你做了一条记得谁走过的走廊。它比你预想的更悲伤。维伊说，这通常是好兆头。|你做出了夜里厨房的嗡嗡声，水壶慢慢凉下去。维伊在它前面坐了下来，她从不这样。}\n# en: Make something dazzling\nopt.right.label [9f8272] = 做一件耀眼的东西\n# en: {A staircase of light climbs into the ceiling and keeps going. The class goes quiet. \"Yes,\" says Vey. \"But what does it feel like?\"|Your piece fills the room with lanterns, one for each person watching. Vey counts them, and finds one for herself.|Your piece hums and blazes and then, at the end, goes very quiet and small. Vey writes \"that last part\" in her notes.}\nopt.right.say.1 [664883] = {一道光之阶梯升进天花板，还在继续往上。全班安静下来。“是的，”维伊说，“可它感觉起来像什么？”|你的作品让满屋亮起灯笼，每个观看的人一盏。维伊数了数，发现有一盏是给她的。|你的作品嗡鸣、绽放，最后变得非常安静、非常小。维伊在笔记里写下“最后那一段”。}\n# en: {It's dazzling for a breath and then empty, like a fair after the fair has gone.|Vey watches it all, politely. \"Very bright,\" she says. You'd have preferred anything else.|It hums, and blazes, and Vey waits politely for it to stop. \"And when it's over?\" she asks. You don't have an answer.}\nopt.right.say.2 [5c7316] = {它耀眼了一瞬，然后空了，像集市散场后的集市。|维伊礼貌地看完了全程。“很亮。”她说。你宁愿听到任何别的评语。|它嗡鸣、绽放，维伊礼貌地等它结束。“结束以后呢？”她问。你答不上来。}\n\n@event beast_mucking_out\n# en: Mucking Out\ntitle [2efb0b] = 清理马厩\n# en: {Beastcraft I begins in the stables, with a pitchfork. Professor Brask says you don't touch a creature until you've cleaned up after it.|Pitchforks. Brask still hasn't said when that rule ends.|The stables, early. You know which stall to start with.} {The hippogriff in the end stall is watching you.|Owls shift in the rafters, unimpressed.|{season == \"summer\": The heat doubles the smell.|A cold wind finds every gap.}}\ntext [99dfd0] = {驭兽学 I 从马厩和一把干草叉开始。布拉斯克教授说，没替一只生灵收拾过，就别碰它。|干草叉。布拉斯克仍然没说这条规矩什么时候结束。|清早的马厩。你知道该从哪个隔栏开始了。} {尽头隔栏里的鹰头马正盯着你。|猫头鹰在椽子上挪动，一脸不以为然。|{season == \"summer\": 热天让气味翻了一倍。|冷风钻进每一道缝。}}\n# en: A student with a pitchfork in a straw-strewn stable, a hippogriff watching from its stall with a look of deep judgement.\nscene [8f7a38] = 一个学生拿着干草叉站在铺满稻草的马厩里，一头鹰头马从隔栏里投来深深审视的目光。\n# en: Muck out properly\nopt.left.label [795f12] = 认真清理干净\n# en: {Two stalls, a barrow and a blister. Brask inspects your work and calls the straw \"nearly decent\".|You finish before anyone else. The hippogriff treads straight through your clean straw, deliberately.|Brask says nothing, which you've learned is the best review she gives humans.}\nopt.left.say.1 [390e61] = {两个隔栏、一辆推车、一个水泡。布拉斯克检查后说这稻草“勉强像样”。|你第一个干完。鹰头马故意从你刚铺好的干净稻草上踩了过去。|布拉斯克一言不发，你已经明白这是她给人类的最高评价。}\n# en: Bow to the hippogriff first\nopt.right.label [d249bf] = 先向鹰头马鞠躬\n# en: {You bow low and hold it. The hippogriff considers, then bows back. Brask writes your name on the board, without comment.|The hippogriff lets you near enough to see where its feathers turn to fur. You'll remember the exact line.|You bow to the hippogriff in the end stall, as you always do now. It bows back before you've finished.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d4557e] = {你深深鞠躬并保持住。鹰头马考虑了一下，回了一礼。布拉斯克在黑板上写下你的名字，不加评语。|鹰头马让你走近到能看清它的羽毛在哪儿变成皮毛。你会记住那条分界线。|你像现在每次那样，朝最里面隔间的鹰头马鞠躬。你还没鞠完，它就回礼了。}\n# en: {You bow too fast. The hippogriff takes it as a challenge and accepts.|The hippogriff looks at your bow, then at your hat, and shoves you into the barrow to get at it. It eats the hat.|You bow and the hippogriff ignores you entirely, which is worse than the hat. You muck out under its scorn.}\nopt.right.say.2 [03c9ed] = {你鞠躬太快了。鹰头马把它当成挑战，并且接受了。|鹰头马看了看你的鞠躬，又看了看你的帽子，一把把你拱进推车，够着了帽子。它吃掉了帽子。|你鞠了躬，鹰头马完全不理你，这比吃帽子还糟。你在它的鄙夷下清完了马厩。}\n\n@event beast_hover_line\n# en: The Hover Line\ntitle [253240] = 悬停队列\n# en: {Flight is half the course. Brask lines you up on the lawn to hover a foot up and hold still while she counts.|The hover line. Brask counts slowly on purpose, you're sure.|Hover line. You and your broom have an understanding, of sorts.} {Somebody's broom keeps edging toward the lake.|An owl has landed on the end of a broom, and its rider is pretending not to notice.|Brask says, as always, that brooms are harder to reason with than griffons.}\ntext [2eec93] = {飞行占这门课的一半。布拉斯克让你们在草坪上排成一行，离地一尺悬停，她数数时不许动。|悬停队列。你确信布拉斯克是故意数得这么慢。|悬停队列。你和你的扫帚已经算是达成了某种共识。} {有人的扫帚一直往湖那边挪。|一只猫头鹰落在一把扫帚的尾端，骑手假装没注意到。|布拉斯克照例说，扫帚比狮鹫难讲道理多了。}\n# en: A line of students hovering a foot above a lawn on brooms, one broom drifting out of line, a stern professor counting slowly.\nscene [10dd11] = 一排学生骑着扫帚悬停在离草坪一尺高的地方，一把扫帚偏离了队列，一位严厉的教授正慢慢数数。\n# en: Hold still until the count\nopt.left.label [37c0d2] = 一动不动直到数完\n# en: {You hold it until forty. Your legs remember it for a day.|The broom bucks once at thirty-one. You hold on. Brask gets to fifty and nods.|Steady all the way. Brask says it's nice to see a broom that knows who's in charge, and she means the broom.}\nopt.left.say.1 [749e71] = {你撑到了四十。你的腿记了一整天。|数到三十一时扫帚猛地一颠。你抓住了。布拉斯克数到五十，点了点头。|全程稳稳当当。布拉斯克说很高兴看见一把知道谁说了算的扫帚——她指的是扫帚。}\n# en: Study how the others fall off\nopt.right.label [4c91b6] = 研究别人怎么摔下来\n# en: {You stand at the end and watch. You learn a great deal about balance, and nobody lets you forget you didn't fly.|You take notes on every wobble. Brask reads them and says you should consider a career in inquests.|You watch the owl on the broom-end win the hover line. Brask writes down the owl's name.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ae91bb] = {你站在队尾观察。你学到了很多关于平衡的东西，也没人让你忘记你没飞。|你把每一次摇晃都记了下来。布拉斯克读完说你该考虑去当验尸官。|你看着那只落在扫帚尾上的猫头鹰赢下了悬停。布拉斯克记下了猫头鹰的名字。}\n\n@event beast_feeding_round\n# en: Feeding Round\ntitle [d6808f] = 喂食巡回\n# en: {Feeding round, in pairs. You and {partner} have two buckets and a list, and the griffon chick is first on it.|Feeding round with {partner}. The griffon chick has heard the buckets.|You and {partner} could do the round blindfolded now. The chick would still sulk.} {Brask has written \"DO NOT HURRY\" at the top of the list.|The owls lean down from the rafters to watch.|{partner} is humming to the buckets.}\ntext [34e935] = {两人一组喂食。你和{partner}有两只桶和一张清单，狮鹫雏鸟排在第一个。|和{partner}一起喂食。狮鹫雏鸟已经听见了桶的声音。|你和{partner}现在蒙着眼都能走完一圈。雏鸟照样会闹脾气。} {布拉斯克在清单顶上写着“不许着急”。|猫头鹰从椽子上探下身来看。|{partner}在对着桶哼歌。}\n# en: Two students carrying buckets between stalls, a sulky griffon chick in a nest of straw, owls leaning down from the rafters.\nscene [28f709] = 两个学生提着桶在隔栏间穿行，一只闹脾气的狮鹫雏鸟窝在草堆里，猫头鹰从椽子上探下身来。\n# en: Take the griffon chick\nopt.left.label [2348a0] = 负责狮鹫雏鸟\n# en: {The chick eats from the bucket, then from your hand, then tries your sleeve. {partner} applauds quietly.|It sulks, then eats, then sulks about having eaten. Brask says that's progress for a griffon.|You and {partner} feed the chick in turns, humming. It sulks, but eats in rhythm.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1639b7] = {雏鸟先从桶里吃，再从你手里吃，然后尝了尝你的袖子。{partner}轻轻鼓掌。|它闹脾气，然后吃，然后为自己吃了而闹脾气。布拉斯克说对狮鹫来说这就是进步。|你和{partner}轮流喂雏鸟，一边哼歌。它还在闹脾气，却跟着节拍吃完了。}\n# en: {The chick bites the bucket, then the hand holding it. {partner} finishes the round alone, kindly.|It knocks the bucket over, and you with it, and sits in it, glaring. You spend the hour, bruised and soaked, fishing out its breakfast.|{partner}'s humming puts the chick in a mood. It takes the mood out on your hand.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7f9047] = {雏鸟咬了桶，接着咬了提桶的手。{partner}好心地独自喂完了一圈。|它把桶打翻，连你也一起撞倒，然后坐在桶里怒目而视。你浑身淤青、湿透，花了一小时把它的早饭捞出来。|{partner}的哼歌惹得雏鸟心情不好。它把气全撒在了你的手上。}\n# en: Take the owls instead\nopt.right.label [40efde] = 改去喂猫头鹰\n# en: {You learn the owls' names from the tags on their perches, and what a pellet is, in detail.|Up and down the ladder for an hour. {partner} survives the chick and is grateful in a quiet way.|The owls take their mice with great ceremony. One of them seems to be counting yours.}\nopt.right.say.1 [436aaa] = {你从栖木上的名牌认全了猫头鹰的名字，还详细了解了什么是食丸。|在梯子上爬上爬下一小时。{partner}熬过了雏鸟那一关，默默地心怀感激。|猫头鹰们隆重地接过各自的老鼠。其中一只似乎在数你手里的。}\n\n@event beast_hare_paddock\n# en: Running with Hares\ntitle [07bbef] = 与野兔同跑\n# en: {Beastcraft II teaches haste the old way. Run with the hares until their speed rubs off. \"Borrow it,\" Brask says. \"Don't steal it.\"|The hare paddock. The hares remember you.|Hares. You dream in sudden right angles now.} {The grass is wet and the hares don't care.|Brask times you with a crooked hourglass.|{season == \"summer\": It's hot and the hares are lazy.|The paddock smells of rain.}}\ntext [54e3b7] = {驭兽学 II 用老办法教疾速：跟着野兔跑，直到它们的速度沾到你身上。“借，”布拉斯克说，“别偷。”|野兔围场。野兔们记得你。|野兔。你现在连做梦都是突然的直角转弯。} {草是湿的，野兔不在乎。|布拉斯克用一只歪斜的沙漏给你计时。|{season == \"summer\": 天很热，野兔很懒。|围场里有雨的气味。}}\n# en: Students sprinting across a paddock alongside a scattering of brown hares, one student's feet suddenly much faster than the rest of them.\nscene [853b14] = 学生们和一群棕色野兔一起冲过围场，其中一个学生的脚突然比身体其他部分快了许多。\n# en: Run until it catches\nopt.left.label [e48d98] = 跑到速度沾上身为止\n# en: {For three strides you're faster than you've any right to be. Then you're in the hedge.|You catch it on the fourth lap, a lightness behind the knees. You pay for it with a nap you can't refuse.|The hares let you keep up for a moment, out of curiosity. It's the best moment of your week.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5f6f03] = {有三步你快得毫无道理。然后你一头扎进了树篱。|第四圈时你抓住了它，膝盖后面一阵轻盈。代价是一场你拒绝不了的午睡。|野兔们出于好奇，让你跟上了一会儿。这是你这周最好的时刻。}\n# en: Watch the hares and take notes\nopt.right.label [5a946d] = 观察野兔，记笔记\n# en: {You sketch the way a hare turns, everything at once, no warning. Brask says your sketch is better than your running.|You note how the hares rest between bursts. Brask reads it and says, \"Yes. That's the half everyone forgets.\"|You sketch the hares in sudden right angles. Brask pins your page up by the paddock gate.}\nopt.right.say.1 [42dee1] = {你画下野兔转身的样子，一切同时发生，毫无预兆。布拉斯克说你画得比跑得好。|你记下野兔在两次冲刺之间如何休息。布拉斯克读完说：“对。这是大家都忘掉的那一半。”|你画下野兔突然的直角转弯。布拉斯克把你那一页钉在了围场门边。}\n\n@event beast_complaints\n# en: The Complaints Hour\ntitle [225734] = 投诉时间\n# en: {Beast-speech practice. You sit on a bucket in the stables and listen. Every creature has been waiting to complain, and the owls start with the draught.|Another hour on the bucket. The owls open, as always, with the draught.|The bucket, the owls, the draught. Brask calls it fieldwork.} {The hippogriff wants different hay. It is very specific.|The griffon chick has a list.|{gpa >= 3: Brask leaves you alone with them, which feels like trust.|Brask stays near the door, which feels like supervision.}}\ntext [f1a417] = {兽语练习。你坐在马厩里一只倒扣的桶上倾听。每只生灵都早就等着要投诉，猫头鹰先说穿堂风。|还是那只桶，再坐一小时。猫头鹰照例先说穿堂风。|桶，猫头鹰，穿堂风。布拉斯克管这叫田野调查。} {鹰头马想换一种干草，要求非常具体。|狮鹫雏鸟列了一张清单。|{gpa >= 3: 布拉斯克让你单独和它们待着，像是一种信任。|布拉斯克一直守在门边，像是一种监督。}}\n# en: A student sitting on an upturned bucket in a stable, surrounded by animals who all seem to be talking at once.\nscene [1a419d] = 一个学生坐在马厩里一只倒扣的桶上，周围的动物似乎都在同时说话。\n# en: Hear every complaint out\nopt.left.label [770d17] = 把每条投诉听完\n# en: {The owls talk for forty minutes about the draught. You understand every word now, which is the trouble.|The hippogriff explains its views on hay at length. You come away fluent and exhausted.|A mouse you didn't know was there wants to be on the record. You put it on the record.}\nopt.left.say.1 [27f588] = {猫头鹰就穿堂风讲了四十分钟。你现在每个字都听得懂，麻烦就在这儿。|鹰头马详尽阐述了它对干草的看法。你出来时既流利又筋疲力尽。|一只你都不知道在场的老鼠要求记录在案。你把它记录在案了。}\n# en: Fix the draught yourself\nopt.right.label [79b85b] = 亲手把漏风堵上\n# en: {You climb into the rafters with a hammer and a plank. The owls supervise. The draught stops, and so do the complaints, briefly.|You patch the gap with straw and a board. The owls say thank you, then mention the window.|You fix the draught. The owls thank you, then move on to the window, the hay and the mouse, in order.}\nopt.right.say.1 [912b4b] = {你带着锤子和木板爬上椽子。猫头鹰在一旁监工。风停了，投诉也短暂地停了。|你用稻草和一块木板堵上了缝。猫头鹰道了谢，然后提起了窗户。|你修好了漏风处。猫头鹰道了谢，然后依次说起窗户、干草和那只老鼠。}\n\n@event beast_lake_flight\n# en: Over the Lake\ntitle [abc6c7] = 掠过湖面\n# en: {Advanced Beastcraft. A hippogriff each, over the lake, no saddle unless you ask. Brask won't think less of you for asking. She'll just remember.|The lake flight. Your hippogriff knows the way.|Over the lake. {final_year: You catch yourself memorising the view.|Your reflection keeps pace.}} {The water is perfectly still.|A barge below, its passengers waving.|{season == \"summer\": The air is full of midges.|The wind off the water stings.}}\ntext [2da61e] = {高等驭兽学。每人一头鹰头马，飞越湖面，除非你开口要，否则没有鞍。布拉斯克不会因为你要鞍而看轻你。她只会记住。|湖上飞行。你的鹰头马比你认路。|湖面上空。{final_year: 你发现自己在默默记住这片风景。|你的倒影一路跟着你。}} {湖水纹丝不动。|下方有一艘驳船，乘客们在挥手。|{season == \"summer\": 空中满是蠓虫。|湖面吹来的风刺痛脸颊。}}\n# en: A student on a hippogriff's back skimming low over a mirror-still lake, their reflection racing along beneath them.\nscene [20d2f8] = 一个学生骑在鹰头马背上低低掠过镜面般平静的湖，倒影在下方飞驰。\n# en: Ride bareback, low over the water\nopt.left.label [e0de3a] = 无鞍贴着水面飞\n# en: {You skim so low a wingtip draws a line across the lake. For one breath you and your reflection touch hands.|The hippogriff banks and you go with it, as if you'd always known how. Brask says nothing at all, then grins.|You fly low enough for your reflection to keep pace, wingtip to wingtip. You don't want to land.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5a8524] = {你飞得那么低，一侧翼尖在湖面划出一道线。有那么一口气的工夫，你和你的倒影碰了碰手。|鹰头马侧身转弯，你顺势而动，仿佛生来就会。布拉斯克一言不发，然后咧嘴笑了。|你飞得低到倒影能和你齐头并进，翼尖对着翼尖。你不想降落。}\n# en: {The hippogriff dips and you don't. The lake is colder than it looks. It always is.|You slide off on the turn and hang on by a fistful of feathers, all the way back to shore.|You race your reflection and your reflection wins. You surface laughing, and very cold.}\nopt.left.say.2 [d60787] = {鹰头马往下一沉，你没有跟上。湖水比看上去冷。向来如此。|你在转弯时滑了下去，攥着一把羽毛吊了一路，直到岸边。|你和自己的倒影赛跑，倒影赢了。你从水里冒出来，笑着，冷得要命。}\n# en: Take the saddle and the high circuit\nopt.right.label [6b0bf1] = 上鞍，走高处的航线\n# en: {Saddled, high and steady. You see all seven towers at once, and nobody sees you.|The safe circuit, twice. Brask remembers, as promised, and nods at you all the same.|You take the saddle and the high circuit, and look down at the lake as if memorising it. Brask lets you take a second lap.}\nopt.right.say.1 [92de41] = {上鞍，高飞，平稳。你一眼看尽七塔，却没人看见你。|安全航线，飞了两圈。布拉斯克果然记住了，但还是朝你点了点头。|你上了鞍，走高线，低头看着湖，像要把它背下来。布拉斯克让你再飞一圈。}\n\n@event beast_manticore_rounds\n# en: Manticore Rounds\ntitle [f18c88] = 蝎尾狮巡检\n# en: {Advanced students check the manticore once a month. Someone holds the tail; someone hums the lullaby. Brask lets you choose.|Manticore rounds. It knows the lullaby better than you do.|The manticore yawns at you, which is either a welcome or a threat.} {Its tail twitches in its sleep.|It has been off its food, Brask says, and is in a mood.|{final_year: You wonder who'll hold the tail after you.|Brask has brought a bucket of meat and a smaller one of patience.}}\ntext [c5506e] = {高年级学生每月检查一次蝎尾狮。一人握住尾巴，一人哼摇篮曲。布拉斯克让你自己选。|蝎尾狮巡检。那首摇篮曲它比你还熟。|蝎尾狮冲你打了个哈欠，不知是欢迎还是威胁。} {它的尾巴在睡梦中抽动。|布拉斯克说它最近不肯好好吃东西，正闹情绪。|{final_year: 你想知道你走后谁来握这条尾巴。|布拉斯克带来了一桶肉，和一小桶耐心。}}\n# en: A manticore lying in straw with its eyes half-closed, one student gently holding its scorpion tail while another hums nearby.\nscene [a03d57] = 一头蝎尾狮半眯着眼躺在稻草里，一个学生轻轻握着它的蝎尾，另一个在旁边哼歌。\n# en: Hold the tail\nopt.left.label [d27267] = 握住尾巴\n# en: {You hold the tail still while Brask checks the sting. It's warm and heavy and does not, at any point, stab you.|The manticore lets you hold its tail the way a cat lets you hold a paw. Under protest, briefly, as a favour.|You hold the tail while Brask feeds it from the bucket. It eats from her hand and watches you the whole time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [edb591] = {布拉斯克检查毒刺时，你稳稳握着尾巴。它温热、沉重，而且自始至终没有蜇你。|蝎尾狮让你握着它的尾巴，就像猫让你握它的爪子——不情不愿，片刻而已，算是赏脸。|布拉斯克从桶里喂它时，你握着尾巴。它从她手里吃，眼睛却一直盯着你。}\n# en: {The tail flicks. You let go just in time, mostly. Sister Amaranth asks no questions; she's seen this course before.|It rolls over, tail and all, onto you. Nothing breaks, but you're bruised in the exact shape of a manticore.|It's in a mood, and the tail knows. You let go a moment too late and spend an hour with Sister Amaranth.}\nopt.left.say.2 [d4a6ce] = {尾巴一甩。你及时松了手，大体上及时。阿玛兰斯修女什么也没问；这门课她见多了。|它连尾巴带身子翻了过来，压在你身上。没断什么，但你身上的淤青恰好是一头蝎尾狮的形状。|它心情不好，尾巴也知道。你松手晚了一刻，在阿玛兰斯修女那儿待了一个钟头。}\n# en: Hum the lullaby\nopt.right.label [978181] = 哼摇篮曲\n# en: {You hum the whole lullaby twice, off-key on the high bit. The manticore sleeps through its own check-up.|You hum. A classmate holds the tail. Brask says the humming is the harder job, and nobody believes her.|You hum while Brask brings the meat. The manticore eats, slowly, and for once the mood lifts.}\nopt.right.say.1 [58461b] = {你把整首摇篮曲哼了两遍，高音那段跑了调。蝎尾狮在自己的体检中一觉到底。|你哼歌，一个同学握尾巴。布拉斯克说哼歌才是更难的活儿，没人信她。|布拉斯克拿来肉时你在哼歌。蝎尾狮慢慢吃着，难得心情好了起来。}\n\n@event umbral_dim_lamps\n# en: Reading by Shadow\ntitle [1839c4] = 借影识物\n# en: {Umbral Theory I, top of the tower, lamps low on purpose. Name what's under each cloth by its shadow alone.|The cloths are back. The lamps are as low as ever.|Shadow reading. The dark no longer seems strange, which the professor says is the first lesson.} {One shadow is slightly larger than its object.|The professor moves the lamp an inch, and every answer changes.|{season == \"summer\": It's warm outside, never up here.|Rain on the windows makes the shadows shiver.}}\ntext [bfc068] = {暗影理论 I，塔顶，灯故意调得很暗。只凭影子说出每块布下面是什么。|布又盖回桌上了。灯和往常一样暗。|读影。黑暗不再显得陌生——教授说，这就是第一课。} {有一道影子比它的物体略大一点。|教授把灯挪了一寸，每个答案都变了。|{season == \"summer\": 外面很暖，这上面从来不暖。|窗上的雨让影子瑟瑟发抖。}}\n# en: A dim lecture room at the top of a tower, objects hidden under cloths on a table, their shadows thrown long on the wall behind.\nscene [3e8e65] = 塔顶一间昏暗的教室，桌上的物件盖着布，它们的影子长长地投在身后的墙上。\n# en: Read them patiently\nopt.left.label [21c0ff] = 耐心地读影子\n# en: {A teapot, a skull, a folded letter addressed to you. You open it later. It's blank.|You name every shadow but one. Under that cloth there is nothing at all. The professor doesn't explain.|Slowly, you get them all. The shadows told you, the professor says; you only had to stop interrupting.}\nopt.left.say.1 [962f6c] = {一把茶壶，一个头骨，一封写给你的折起来的信。你后来拆开，是空白的。|你认出了所有影子，只有一个例外。那块布下面什么也没有。教授没有解释。|慢慢地，你全认出来了。是影子告诉你的，教授说；你只是不再打断它们。}\n# en: Slip in a false shadow\nopt.right.label [131d22] = 混进一道假影子\n# en: {You add the shadow of a cat to the wall. Three classmates write \"cat\". The professor writes your name, and underlines it.|Your false shadow passes all morning. The professor only catches it when it doesn't move with the lamp.|Your false shadow shivers with the rain like the real ones. Nobody finds it. The professor shakes your hand, which is cold.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8fc52d] = {你在墙上添了一道猫的影子。三个同学写下“猫”。教授写下你的名字，还划了线。|你的假影子撑过了整个上午。直到它没跟着灯动，教授才发现。|你的假影子像真影子一样随雨发抖。没人发现它。教授和你握了手，那只手是冷的。}\n# en: {Your false shadow points the wrong way. The professor turns it round gently, like straightening a picture.|Your shadow-cat sits up and leaves. It wasn't meant to do that. The professor doesn't seem surprised.|The rain makes every shadow shiver except yours. The professor notices the one that keeps still.}\nopt.right.say.2 [b417db] = {你的假影子朝向反了。教授轻轻把它转了回来，像扶正一幅画。|你的影子猫坐起来，走了。它本不该这样。教授似乎并不意外。|雨让每道影子都在发抖，唯独你的没有。教授注意到了那道不动的影子。}\n\n@event umbral_chalk_ward\n# en: Pale Shells\ntitle [003c50] = 苍白的壳\n# en: {Ward drill, in pairs. One of you raises a pale shell over the other, and the professor lets the lamp-shadows loose along the floor to find the gap.|Pairs again. The professor's shadows have learned new tricks since last time.|The paired drill. You know how the shadows like to come: low, and from behind.} {Your partner's shell keeps flickering.|Your partner is a first-year who has never held a ward for more than a minute.|{gpa >= 3: The professor pairs you with the weakest student, on purpose.|The professor pairs you with someone as nervous as you are.}}\ntext [36de71] = {护界操练，两人一组。一人在另一人头上撑起一层苍白的壳，教授放出灯影沿着地面去找缝隙。|又是两人一组。教授的影子比上次多学了几招。|结对操练。你知道影子喜欢怎么来：贴着地，从背后。} {你搭档的壳一直在闪。|你的搭档是个一年级生，从没撑过一分钟以上的护界。|{gpa >= 3: 教授故意把你和最弱的学生分在一组。|教授把你和一个跟你一样紧张的人分在一组。}}\n# en: Students behind faint, pale bubbles of light as a professor flicks pieces of chalk at them from the front of a dim room.\nscene [710e30] = 学生们躲在一层层淡淡的苍白光罩后面，一位教授在昏暗的教室前方朝他们弹粉笔。\n# en: Hold the shell over both of you\nopt.left.label [bd8e9e] = 把护界撑在你们俩头上\n# en: {You stretch your shell over your partner as well. The shadows nose along its edge for an hour and find nothing. You feel every touch in your teeth.|Two people under one ward is twice the weight. Yours holds until the bell, just.|The shadows give up on your shell and go after the next pair. The professor nods at you, once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6dfac8] = {你把壳也撑到搭档头上。影子沿着边缘嗅探了一整个钟头，什么也没找到。每一下触碰你都震在牙根里。|一个护界罩两个人，就是双倍的分量。你的撑到了下课，勉强。|影子放弃了你的壳，转去找下一组。教授朝你点了一下头。}\n# en: Coach your partner through theirs\nopt.right.label [a6ede4] = 指导搭档撑起自己的\n# en: {You talk your partner through it, breath by breath. Their shell holds for nine minutes, then a shadow slips under it and makes you both jump. \"Teaching,\" says the professor, \"is also a ward.\"|You keep your own shell down and watch for the gaps in theirs. By the end, they've stopped flickering. You've learned where shadows like to get in.|You coach your nervous partner, and you're nervous too. The shadows come low and from behind; you both turn in time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d45323] = {你一口气一口气地带着搭档撑。他们的壳撑了九分钟，然后一道影子从底下钻了进来，把你们俩都吓了一跳。“教人，”教授说，“也是一种护界。”|你撤下自己的壳，盯着对方壳上的缝隙。到最后，他们的壳不再闪了。你也弄明白了影子喜欢从哪儿钻进来。|你带着紧张的搭档，自己也紧张。影子从低处、从背后来；你们俩都及时转过了身。}\n\n@event umbral_quiet_minutes\n# en: The Quiet Minutes\ntitle [089065] = 静默的十分钟\n# en: {Each lesson opens with ten minutes of silence. \"Listen to what's hidden,\" the professor says, and puts out every lamp but one.|The quiet minutes. You can hear the tower settle, stone by stone.|Ten minutes of silence. You look forward to it, and also not.} {Someone's stomach rumbles at the worst moment.|The candle leans toward the door, though nothing comes in.|{season == \"summer\": A bee is lost in the room somewhere.|The wind hums in the stairwell.}}\ntext [540b0c] = {每节课都以十分钟的静默开始。“去听隐藏之物。”教授说，然后熄灭了所有灯，只留一盏。|静默的十分钟。你能听见塔一块石头一块石头地沉降。|十分钟的静默。你有点期待它，又有点不。} {有人的肚子在最不合适的时候叫了。|烛火朝门口倾斜，尽管什么也没进来。|{season == \"summer\": 屋里不知哪儿有只迷路的蜜蜂。|风在楼梯井里低鸣。}}\n# en: A circle of students sitting perfectly still in near darkness, a single candle in the middle, everyone listening very hard.\nscene [5f3b47] = 一圈学生在近乎全黑中一动不动地坐着，中间只有一支蜡烛，每个人都在用力倾听。\n# en: Listen properly\nopt.left.label [e31a7f] = 认真倾听\n# en: {You hear the tower breathing, which it shouldn't, and a sigh on the stair that nobody makes.|You hear the lake, which is impossible from up here. The professor says that's normal for this room. It is not reassuring.|In the silence you hear someone you miss, very faintly. When the lamps come up, you don't mention it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0ee9e7] = {你听见塔在呼吸——它不该呼吸——还有楼梯上一声没人发出的叹息。|你听见了湖，从这里本不可能听见。教授说这在这间屋里很正常。这话并不让人安心。|静默里，你隐约听见一个你思念的人。灯亮起来时，你没提这件事。}\n# en: Quietly finish other homework\nopt.right.label [29deb1] = 悄悄写别的作业\n# en: {You finish a whole essay by feel in the dark. The professor never notices, or never says.|Not a scratch of the quill. When the lamps come up, your essay is done and your face is innocent.|You write in the dark with the wind humming the rhythm. The essay is done when the lamps come up.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c2c5c4] = {你在黑暗里摸索着写完了一整篇论文。教授始终没发现，或者始终没说。|羽毛笔一点声音都没出。灯亮时，你的论文写完了，脸上一派无辜。|你在黑暗里写着，风在楼梯间哼着节拍。灯亮时，论文写完了。}\n# en: {When the lamps come up the professor is behind you, reading your essay. \"Your third paragraph is weak,\" she says.|Your quill squeaks. In that silence it sounds like a scream. Everyone turns.|A gust slams the stairwell door and you drop your quill. It rolls, loudly, to the professor's feet.}\nopt.right.say.2 [f43498] = {灯亮时，教授就站在你身后读你的论文。“你的第三段很弱。”她说。|你的羽毛笔吱了一声。在那片寂静里，听起来像一声尖叫。所有人都转过头来。|一阵风把楼梯间的门撞上了，你的羽毛笔掉了。它骨碌碌地、响亮地滚到了教授脚边。}\n\n@event umbral_short_distances\n# en: Short Distances\ntitle [35c701] = 短距离\n# en: {Umbral Theory II brings the shadowstep, desk to desk. \"The shadows in between are not friendly,\" the professor says, \"so don't linger.\"|Shadowstep drill. Three desks this time, then four.|The desks. You know the way between them, and the cold that lives there.} {Someone comes out under the wrong desk.|The lamps are lower than usual.|{gpa >= 3: The professor sets you the long way round.|The professor sets you the short way, and watches.}}\ntext [142afe] = {暗影理论 II 教影步，从一张桌子到另一张。“中间那些影子可不友善，”教授说，“所以别逗留。”|影步操练。这次三张桌子，然后四张。|这些桌子。你认得它们之间的路，也认得住在那里的寒意。} {有人从错误的桌子底下钻了出来。|灯比平时更暗。|{gpa >= 3: 教授让你绕远路。|教授让你走近路，并且盯着。}}\n# en: A student stepping into the shadow under one desk and halfway out of the shadow under another, the room's lamps burning low.\nscene [18d81b] = 一个学生踏进一张桌子下的影子，半个身子从另一张桌子下的影子里出来，屋里的灯低低燃着。\n# en: Step across\nopt.left.label [894fac] = 一步跨过去\n# en: {In, through, out. It takes no time and lasts forever. You come out under the far desk with frost on your sleeves.|Four desks, clean. Something in between brushes your ankle, curious, and lets you pass.|You take your set route and come out exactly where the professor pointed. The cold in between nods at you, almost.}\nopt.left.say.1 [83def2] = {进去，穿过，出来。不花一点时间，又像过了一辈子。你从最远那张桌子下出来，袖子上结了霜。|四张桌子，干净利落。中间有什么东西好奇地擦过你的脚踝，然后放你过去了。|你走指定的路线，正好从教授指的地方出来。中间的寒冷几乎朝你点了点头。}\n# en: {You come out one desk short and somewhat upside down. The cold stays in your fingers until lunch.|You linger, just for a moment. It's enough. You come back pale, and the professor sits you by the one warm lamp.|The professor's route is longer than you'd like. You come out at the end, but so does the cold, with you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a8397a] = {你差一张桌子就出来了，而且有点头朝下。寒意在你指尖一直留到午饭。|你逗留了，只有一瞬。这就够了。你脸色苍白地回来，教授让你坐到唯一一盏暖灯旁。|教授给的路线比你想的长。你出来了，寒冷也跟着你一起出来了。}\n# en: Map the route on paper first\nopt.right.label [f66070] = 先在纸上画好路线\n# en: {You draw every shadow in the room, and where it goes at each hour. The professor keeps your map.|You work out the route in ink and walk it once, slowly, in the light. It's dull, and correct.|You map the set route desk by desk. The professor checks your map and adds one shadow you'd missed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [95dc62] = {你画下屋里每一道影子，以及它每个钟点会移到哪里。教授留下了你的图。|你用墨水推好路线，然后在光亮里慢慢走了一遍。枯燥，但正确。|你把指定路线一张桌子一张桌子地画了出来。教授检查后添上了一道你漏掉的影子。}\n\n@event umbral_hidden_object\n# en: Present but Unseen\ntitle [a63725] = 在场而不可见\n# en: {Hide a small key in this room, present but unseen. \"Hiding is not removal,\" the professor says. \"Removal is for amateurs.\"|The key, and somewhere to hide it. Your classmates are better at finding things.|The key exercise. Half the class pats their pockets out of habit.} {The key is brass today.|The key is silver and very cold.|Last time a key hid in a shadow for two days.}\ntext [c74599] = {把一把小钥匙藏在这间屋里，让它在场却不可见。“藏不是拿走，”教授说，“拿走是外行干的。”|钥匙，和一个藏它的地方。同学们找东西的本事见长。|钥匙练习。半个班都养成了拍口袋的习惯。} {今天的钥匙是黄铜的。|钥匙是银的，冰凉刺骨。|上次有把钥匙在影子里躲了两天。}\n# en: A dim classroom being searched by several students at once while one of them sits calmly at a desk with a small brass key in plain sight.\nscene [e384e8] = 一间昏暗的教室里，好几个学生同时在翻找，其中一个却平静地坐在桌旁，一把小铜钥匙就摆在眼前。\n# en: Hide it, and stay calm\nopt.left.label [7d28db] = 藏好它，保持镇定\n# en: {You leave the key on the desk in plain sight, in your own shadow. Nobody sees it. The professor smiles, which is rarer than a key.|You hide it in the dark behind your own ear. They search the room twice and never once look at you.|You hide the key in a shadow and leave it there for two days, like the last one. Nobody finds it. The professor does, and approves.}\nopt.left.say.1 [50e23d] = {你把钥匙明晃晃地放在桌上，就在你自己的影子里。没人看见。教授笑了，这比钥匙还难找。|你把它藏在自己耳后的暗处。他们把屋子搜了两遍，一次也没看你。|你像上一把那样，把钥匙藏进一道影子，放了两天。没人找到。教授找到了，表示赞许。}\n# en: {A first-rate hiding place, found in under a minute by someone who simply asked the shadow.|You hide it so well you can't find it yourself. The professor retrieves it from the wall with a sigh.|You hide it in a shadow, and the shadow moves with the afternoon. By the bell the key is in the corridor.}\nopt.left.say.2 [178ee3] = {一个一流的藏处，被人不到一分钟就找到了——对方只是问了问影子。|你藏得太好，连自己都找不到了。教授叹了口气，从墙里把它取了出来。|你把它藏进一道影子，可影子随着午后的光移动了。下课铃响时，钥匙已经到了走廊上。}\n# en: Search for everyone else's\nopt.right.label [69b186] = 去找别人藏的\n# en: {You find four keys, one in a hat, one in a hinge, one in a shadow that didn't want to let go.|You find one key and a pencil nobody has missed for years. The professor counts both.|You find the key that hid for two days. It's still in its shadow, and you have to ask it nicely to come out.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6d225e] = {你找到了四把钥匙：一把在帽子里，一把在合页里，一把在一道不肯松手的影子里。|你找到了一把钥匙，和一支多年没人想起的铅笔。教授两样都算数。|你找到了那把藏了两天的钥匙。它还在那道影子里，你得好言相劝它才肯出来。}\n\n@event umbral_stair_wards\n# en: The Old Stair Wards\ntitle [a4469b] = 古老的阶梯护界\n# en: {Advanced students renew the small wards on the tower's old stair. Some are newer than the stone. Some, the professor says, are much older.|The stair wards. You know which step hums.|The stair. {final_year: Someone else will kneel here next year.|Your knees know every step.}} {One ward is warm, as if someone's just left.|Something beyond a ward leans back when you touch it.|The stair lamps are mostly a suggestion.}\ntext [594d21] = {高年级学生负责加固塔里古老阶梯上的小护界。有些比石头新。有些，教授说，要古老得多。|阶梯护界。你知道哪一级会嗡鸣。|阶梯。{final_year: 明年会是别人跪在这里。|你的膝盖认得每一级台阶。}} {有一道护界是温的，像刚有人离开。|你一碰，护界另一侧有什么东西往后靠了靠。|阶梯上的灯基本只是个意思。}\n# en: A student kneeling on an ancient spiral stair, hands pressed to a faint ring of light set into the stone.\nscene [c60e93] = 一个学生跪在古老的螺旋阶梯上，双手按着嵌进石头里的一圈微光。\n# en: Renew them carefully\nopt.left.label [405067] = 仔细地加固\n# en: {Step by step, ring by ring. Each ward takes a little of you and hums.|You renew eleven and pause at the twelfth. Leave that one, the professor says, and doesn't say whose it is.|At the top you're light-headed and the wards are bright. Something on the other side has gone quiet.}\nopt.left.say.1 [84a4db] = {一级一级，一圈一圈。每道护界都取走你一点东西，然后嗡嗡作响。|你加固了十一道，在第十二道前停了下来。教授说那道别碰，却没说是谁的。|到了顶上你头晕目眩，护界都亮了。另一侧的什么已经安静下来。}\n# en: Study the oldest ward instead\nopt.right.label [6da660] = 转而研究最老的那道\n# en: {You trace the oldest ring with one finger. The pattern folds in on itself like a knot nobody was meant to untie, and you feel watched for noticing even that.|The old ward is cold, careful work, made by someone who was afraid. You can feel the fear still in the stone.|You study the oldest ward by your hands alone, the lamps being no help. It's older than the stair. You're sure of that much.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d84215] = {你用一根手指描着最古老的那一圈。那图案向内层层折叠，像一个本就不该被人解开的结；仅仅察觉到这一点，你就觉得被什么盯上了。|那道古老的护界是冰冷而小心的手艺，出自一个害怕的人。那份恐惧至今还留在石头里。|灯帮不上忙，你只凭双手研究那道最古老的护界。它比楼梯还老。这一点你很确定。}\n\n@event umbral_errand\n# en: The Shadow's Errand\ntitle [febd12] = 影子的差事\n# en: {Original work. Send your own shadow on an errand across the room, the professor says, and have it come back. \"It will want to wander. They all do.\"|Shadow errands. Yours picks its own route now.|Errand day. {final_year: Does your shadow know you're leaving soon?|Your shadow seems eager, which worries you.}} {Someone's shadow is still under a desk from last week.|The professor's own shadow is missing today.|The lamps are low and the shadows long.}\ntext [e2911a] = {原创作业。派你自己的影子到屋子另一头办件差事，教授说，然后让它回来。“它会想乱跑。它们都这样。”|影子差事。你的影子现在会自己挑路线了。|差事日。{final_year: 你的影子知道你快要离开了吗？|你的影子显得跃跃欲试，这让你担心。}} {有人的影子从上周起还待在一张桌子底下。|教授自己的影子今天不见了。|灯很暗，影子很长。}\n# en: A student standing in lamplight with no shadow at all, while across the room their shadow lifts a book from a shelf.\nscene [fe154c] = 一个学生站在灯光下，身上没有一丝影子，而屋子另一头，他的影子正从书架上取下一本书。\n# en: Send it, and trust it\nopt.left.label [80242c] = 派它去，信任它\n# en: {Your shadow crosses the room, lifts a book, and brings it back open at a page you needed. You didn't ask it to.|It comes back with a feather from the rafters, which wasn't the errand. The professor counts it as initiative.|Your shadow goes out long and comes back short, carrying a stub of candle. You don't know where it got it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f2385c] = {你的影子穿过屋子，取下一本书，带回来时正翻在你需要的那一页。你并没有叫它这么做。|它带回一根椽子上的羽毛，这不是差事内容。教授把它算作主动性。|你的影子拖得长长地出去，短短地回来，还带着一截蜡烛头。你不知道它是从哪儿弄来的。}\n# en: {It comes back an hour late and a little the wrong shape. For a day it lags behind you on stairs, sulking, and you can't get warm.|It doesn't come back until dusk. When it does, it smells of the lake. The professor makes you sit with the lamp until your hands stop shaking with cold.|It stretches out under the low lamps and doesn't want to come back. When it does, it's longer than you for a day.}\nopt.left.say.2 [9d55ca] = {它晚了一小时才回来，形状还有点不对。接下来一整天，它在楼梯上总落在你后面，闹着别扭，而你怎么也暖和不起来。|它直到黄昏才回来。回来时带着一股湖水的气味。教授让你在灯旁坐着，直到你的手不再冷得发抖。|在低矮的灯光下它伸得长长的，不想回来。回来后，一整天它都比你还长。}\n# en: Keep it on a short leash\nopt.right.label [bfc9a2] = 把它拴短一点\n# en: {Your shadow goes as far as the window and no further. It looks out a long time before it comes back.|Two yards and home. Dull, safe and precise. The professor says precision is how shadow-workers get old.|You keep it close under the low lamps. It strains toward the long shadows and comes back to you, reluctantly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [306102] = {你的影子走到窗边就停下了。它朝外望了很久，才回来。|两码远就回家。枯燥、安全、精确。教授说，精确就是影术师能活到老的原因。|在低矮的灯光下，你把它拴得很近。它朝那些长影子使劲，最后还是不情不愿地回到了你身边。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_hidden.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_hidden.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event art_escapement_drill\n# en: The Ticking Hour\ntitle [0f189f] = 滴答一课\n# en: {Artifice begins with a heart, a fist of clockwork that must tick evenly before it is allowed to think.|Your clockwork heart is waiting where you left it, sulking at the wrong speed.|You can hear your own heart on the bench from the doorway now. Its tick is almost yours.} {The bench beside you ticks like hail.|One heart near the door is running backwards, proudly.|The lecturer listens to each bench with eyes closed.} {season == \"winter\": The brass is cold enough to bite.|The workshop smells of solder.}\ntext [d91972] = {奇械术从一颗心开始：一团发条，必须先走得均匀，才被允许思考。|你的发条心还在你留下它的地方，用错误的速度生着闷气。|如今你在门口就能听见自己工作台上那颗心。它的滴答声几乎就是你的。} {隔壁工作台滴答得像下冰雹。|门边有一颗心在倒着走，还挺得意。|讲师闭着眼，挨个工作台地听。} {season == \"winter\": 黄铜冷得咬手。|工坊里一股焊锡味。}\n# en: A cluttered workshop bench covered in tiny brass gears, files and loupes, a row of half-built clockwork hearts ticking out of time with one another.\nscene [2c43ab] = 杂乱的工坊长凳上堆满细小的黄铜齿轮、锉刀和放大镜，一排半成品的发条心各走各的节拍。\n# en: File every tooth by hand\nopt.left.label [93a8dc] = 一齿一齿手工锉\n# en: {Tooth by tooth, under a loupe, until your eyes water. The tick evens out by the bell.|You file all morning and burn one thumb on the solder. The heart ticks steady and slightly smug.|It's slow, fiddly work, and your hands learn it faster than your head does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [89f0bd] = {一齿接一齿，伏在放大镜下，直到眼睛发酸。下课铃响前，滴答声匀了。|你锉了一上午，还被焊锡烫了一下拇指。那颗心走得稳稳当当，还有点得意。|又慢又磨人的活儿，你的手比脑子学得快。}\n# en: Charm the spring to keep time\nopt.right.label [93d2cf] = 给发条施咒定时\n# en: {The charm settles into the spring like a cat into a basket. Your heart ticks like a metronome.|You whisper the rhythm into the coil and it keeps it. The lecturer opens one eye, then closes it again.|The charm takes your own pulse as its pattern. The lecturer listens with eyes closed and nods: your heart ticks in time with you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1d3cfa] = {咒语钻进发条，像猫钻进篮子。你的心走得像节拍器一样准。|你把节奏低声说进发条圈里，它就守住了。讲师睁开一只眼，又闭上了。|咒语把你自己的脉搏当成了节拍。讲师闭着眼听了听，点点头：你的心和你走的是同一个拍子。}\n# en: {The spring takes the charm too literally and unwinds all at once, across the room.|Your heart ticks perfectly for a minute, then very fast, then not at all. Someone hands you a fire bucket just in case.|The charm copies your pulse a little too well, and when you get nervous, so does the heart. The lecturer listens, eyes closed, and winces.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a72bd2] = {发条把咒语理解得太字面，一口气全松开，飞过了整间屋子。|你的心完美地走了一分钟，然后飞快，然后彻底停了。有人递给你一只灭火桶，以防万一。|咒语把你的脉搏学得太像了，你一紧张，那颗心也跟着紧张。讲师闭着眼听了听，皱起了眉。}\n\n@event art_stirring_cauldron\n# en: The Cauldron That Stirs\ntitle [dc6732] = 自己搅拌的坩埚\n# en: {The class project is a cauldron that stirs itself. It does, mostly, in whatever direction it pleases.|The cauldron has been rebuilt again. It stirs with a new brass arm and the same old temper.|The cauldron recognises you, or behaves as if it does.} {Today it stirs clockwise, then argues.|Today it stirs so fast the porridge in it is climbing out.|Today the arm keeps tapping the rim like an impatient guest.} {final_year: You'll miss its moods, a little.|The fire buckets by the door are full, for once.}\ntext [9992ac] = {这门课的项目是一口会自己搅拌的坩埚。它确实会搅，大多数时候，朝它高兴的方向。|坩埚又被重装了一遍。新的黄铜搅臂，还是老脾气。|坩埚认得你，或者至少装得像认得。} {今天它先顺时针搅，然后开始闹别扭。|今天它搅得太快，锅里的粥都往外爬。|今天搅臂一个劲儿地敲锅沿，像个不耐烦的客人。} {final_year: 你会有点想念它的脾气。|门边的灭火桶难得都是满的。}\n# en: A copper cauldron on iron legs stirring itself with a long brass arm while students crowd round with notebooks and a wary distance.\nscene [9242ac] = 一口铁腿铜坩埚用一根长长的黄铜臂自己搅拌，学生们抱着笔记本围在一旁，小心地保持着距离。\n# en: Recut the runes on the arm\nopt.left.label [72bcaa] = 重刻搅臂上的符文\n# en: {You spend the morning with a graver and a magnifier, recutting the stirring runes one stroke at a time. Your fingers are nicked; the arm moves sweetly.|Fine work in brass, the whole lesson long. The arm stirs evenly, and you have solder burns in a neat little row.|The arm taps the rim the whole time you work, as if hurrying you. When the runes are recut it stops tapping and stirs, which may be thanks.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b5b22d] = {你拿着刻刀和放大镜过了一上午，一笔一画重刻搅拌符文。手指划了几道口子，搅臂动得顺滑了。|一整节课都在黄铜上做细活。搅臂搅得均匀了，你手上多了一排整整齐齐的焊锡烫痕。|你干活时，搅臂一直敲着锅沿，好像在催你。符文重刻好了，它不敲了，开始搅，这也许算是道谢。}\n# en: Test it on a live brew\nopt.right.label [4e638a] = 拿真药剂试一试\n# en: {The cauldron stirs your brew exactly as the recipe asks, and you take the credit for both.|The brew turns the right colour at the right minute. The cauldron seems to know it did well.|The arm stops tapping the moment your brew goes in, and stirs it perfectly. It seems to like you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [49a10c] = {坩埚完全按配方搅好了你的药剂，两份功劳你都领了。|药剂在对的那一分钟变成了对的颜色。坩埚似乎知道自己干得不错。|你的药剂一倒进去，搅臂就不敲了，搅得恰到好处。它好像挺喜欢你。}\n# en: {The arm speeds up, the brew climbs the walls, and the whole class steps back as one.|The cauldron stirs your brew into something with the texture of an argument. You scrub it out until the bell.|The arm taps the rim, impatient, then stirs your brew in a temper. You clean the ceiling until the bell.}\nopt.right.say.2 [8e8241] = {搅臂越转越快，药剂爬上锅壁，全班齐刷刷后退一步。|坩埚把你的药剂搅成了一种像吵架一样的质地。你一直刷锅刷到下课。|搅臂不耐烦地敲着锅沿，然后带着脾气搅起了你的药剂。你一直擦天花板擦到下课。}\n\n@event star_names_in_the_dark\n# en: Names in the Dark\ntitle [d9ffd3] = 黑暗中的名字\n# en: {Starcalling is taught by appointment, at night, and this morning's note gives you tonight's hour. It begins with names. \"You cannot call what you cannot name.\"|Another note at breakfast, another night appointment under the dome. The list of names is longer than it was.|Tonight's appointment under the dome again. You know where the draught comes in.} {The note warns that the great telescope has been swinging round on its own.|Meteors are expected tonight.|The note is written on the back of a star chart.} {season == \"winter\": Tonight your breath will freeze on the eyepiece.|Tonight the air will smell of cut grass from the lawns below.}\ntext [3b909c] = {唤星术只在夜里预约授课，今早的字条告诉了你今晚的钟点。它从名字开始。“叫不出名字的，就唤不下来。”|早饭时又来了一张字条，又是一次穹顶下的夜间预约。名单比上次更长了。|今晚又是穹顶下的预约。你知道冷风从哪儿钻进来。} {字条提醒说，大望远镜最近总是自己转方向。|今晚会有流星。|字条写在一张星图的背面。} {season == \"winter\": 今晚你的呼气会冻在目镜上。|今晚的夜风里会有下面草坪刚割过的青草味。}\n# en: A dark brass dome open to a sky full of stars, a student at the eyepiece of a great telescope while another recites from memory, breath steaming.\nscene [383740] = 黄铜穹顶在黑暗中向满天星斗敞开，一名学生凑在大望远镜的目镜前，另一名呼着白气凭记忆背诵。\n# en: Recite the names until perfect\nopt.left.label [5a0fad] = 把名字背到一字不差\n# en: {You say them over and over into the cold until they stop being words and start being places.|Name after name, in order and then backwards. By the end you could find them blindfold, and nearly have to.|You stumble on the same name four times. The fifth time it comes out right, and you feel it land somewhere.}\nopt.left.say.1 [eebfcf] = {你对着寒气一遍遍念，直到它们不再是词，而成了地方。|一个接一个，正着背，再倒着背。到最后你闭着眼也能找到它们，也差点真得闭着眼。|同一个名字你卡了四次。第五次终于念对，你感觉它落在了某个地方。}\n# en: Let the telescope choose a star\nopt.right.label [9ee4f3] = 让望远镜挑一颗星\n# en: {The telescope settles on a star you've never heard of. You find its name in the old charts before the lecturer can tell you.|Wherever it points, you follow, and the charts agree with it. That's a whole lesson learned the other way round.|You let the telescope choose and check it against the chart on the back of the note. It's right, and so are you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f57d71] = {望远镜停在一颗你从没听说过的星上。讲师还没开口，你已经在旧星图里找到了它的名字。|它指哪儿你跟哪儿，星图也都对得上。这一课是倒着学会的。|你让望远镜挑，再拿字条背面的星图核对。它对了，你也对了。}\n# en: {The telescope picks a star and then changes its mind every ten minutes. You chase it round the dome until midnight.|You spend the night at the eyepiece and come down with a stiff neck and one name you aren't sure of.|The telescope chooses a star that isn't on the chart on the back of the note. You spend the night in the draught, trying to prove one of them wrong.}\nopt.right.say.2 [c4490f] = {望远镜挑了一颗星，然后每十分钟改一次主意。你绕着穹顶追它追到午夜。|你在目镜前熬了一夜，下来时脖子僵了，还有一个拿不准的名字。|望远镜挑了一颗字条背面的星图上没有的星。你在穿堂风里待了一整夜，想证明它们俩之中有一个错了。}\n\n@event star_thread_of_light\n# en: A Thread of Light\ntitle [af6199] = 一缕星光\n# en: {Tonight's lesson, says the morning note, is to call down a single thread of a named star's light, no thicker than a hair.|Tonight's lesson is the same as ever: one thread, one star, held as long as you can bear it.|You've called down threads before. The morning note says tonight the lecturer wants it held longer.} {The morning sky is clear, and the night promises to be.|Thin cloud is forecast to cross the star you've chosen.|At breakfast the others are already practising the hand positions.}\ntext [49defa] = {早上的字条说，今晚这一课要试着唤下一缕某颗有名之星的光，细不过一根头发。|今晚这一课永远一样：一缕光，一颗星，能撑多久撑多久。|你以前唤下过光缕。早上的字条说，今晚讲师要你撑得更久。} {早晨的天空晴朗，夜里看来也会如此。|预报说薄云会掠过你选中的那颗星。|早饭时其他人已经在练手势了。}\n# en: A student in a dark observatory with hands cupped upward, a single silver thread of starlight running down through the open dome into their palms.\nscene [db7c41] = 昏暗的观星台里，一名学生双手向上合拢，一缕银色星光穿过敞开的穹顶落进掌心。\n# en: Call the thread down yourself\nopt.left.label [3c139b] = 亲手把光唤下来\n# en: {The thread comes down cold and bright and pours into your palms. You hold it until your hands shake.|It takes three tries. On the third, the light drops into your hands like water from a great height.|A hair of light, the length of the whole tower. When it fades, your fingers glow for an hour.}\nopt.left.say.1 [63b5e3] = {光缕冰冷而明亮地落下，注满你的掌心。你一直撑到双手发抖。|试了三次。第三次，光像从极高处落下的水一样掉进你手里。|一根头发粗的光，有整座塔那么长。它消散后，你的指尖还亮了一个钟头。}\n# en: Keep the night log instead\nopt.right.label [314549] = 改去记夜间日志\n# en: {You write down every thread the others call, with the star, the minute and how long it held. It's dull, cold work, and you start to see the pattern.|You hold the lamp and the log while the others call. Watching from outside, you see exactly where their hands go wrong.|Tonight the others hold their threads longer, and you note to the second when each one breaks. The pattern is in the breaking.} {season == \"winter\": Your ink keeps freezing.|The lecturer reads your log twice.}\nopt.right.say.1 [71a3b8] = {你记下别人唤下的每一缕光：哪颗星、几点几分、撑了多久。又冷又枯燥，但你开始看出规律了。|别人唤光时你举灯记录。从旁边看，你清清楚楚看到他们的手错在哪儿。|今晚别人的光丝都撑得更久，你把每一缕断掉的时刻记到秒。规律就藏在断开的地方。} {season == \"winter\": 你的墨水一直结冰。|讲师把你的日志读了两遍。}\n# en: Hold your own starlight up to it\nopt.up.label [658616] = 举起自己的星光迎它\n# en: {You light your own starlight and the thread finds it, like calling to like. It holds twice as long.|Your starlight steadies the thread, and the lecturer has you show the others how.|The thread drops straight into the light you're already holding. For a moment the dome is bright as noon.}\nopt.up.say.1 [48b9e8] = {你点亮自己的星光，那缕光循着它而来，同类相召。它撑了两倍长。|你的星光稳住了光缕，讲师让你示范给其他人看。|光缕径直落进你手中已有的光里。那一刻，穹顶亮如正午。}\n\n@event hollow_sealed_boxes\n# en: How It Was Sealed\ntitle [e1eab1] = 它是怎么封上的\n# en: {Each lesson, the headmistress sets a sealed thing in front of you. \"Tell me how it was closed,\" she says. \"Not what is inside. How.\"|Another sealed thing on the table. Today it is {a letter with a wax seal|a small lacquered box|a jar with its lid charmed shut}.|The headmistress places today's sealed thing with a little smile. She has started choosing harder ones.} {The lamp hisses.|The books on the shelves breathe slowly in the dark.|She sits with her hands folded, unhurried.}\ntext [e8bcf8] = {每一课，院长都把一样封住的东西放到你面前。“告诉我它是怎么封上的，”她说，“不是里面有什么。是怎么封的。”|桌上又是一样封住的东西。今天是{一封盖着蜡印的信|一只小漆盒|一个盖子被咒语封死的罐子}。|院长带着一丝微笑放下今天的封物。她已经开始挑更难的了。} {油灯嘶嘶作响。|书架上的书在黑暗里缓缓呼吸。|她双手交叠坐着，不慌不忙。}\n# en: A windowless room lined with black-bound books, a single lamp on a table, a tall calm woman setting a small locked box in front of a student.\nscene [8b6e1a] = 一间没有窗户的房间，四壁摆满黑色封皮的书，桌上一盏孤灯，一位高挑沉静的女士把一只上锁的小盒子放在学生面前。\n# en: Study the seal for the hour\nopt.left.label [b8b4fc] = 用整节课研究封印\n# en: {You trace every line of it without touching. At the end you can name each step of the sealing, in order. She nods once.|It takes the whole hour and a headache, but you work it out backwards, layer by layer.|You get two layers right and one wrong. She tells you which, kindly, and you'll remember.}\nopt.left.say.1 [af3511] = {你不碰它，只把每一道纹路都描了一遍。最后你能按顺序说出封印的每一步。她点了一下头。|花了整整一节课和一场头痛，你一层一层倒推了出来。|你对了两层，错了一层。她温和地告诉你是哪一层，你会记住的。}\n# en: Open it without leaving a trace\nopt.right.label [ffc168] = 打开它，不留痕迹\n# en: {You open it and close it again, and nothing shows. She looks at it, then at you, and laughs, short and delighted.|Open, closed, not a mark. \"Good,\" she says. \"Now never do that to a door.\"|It's a harder one, and it opens anyway, and closes, and shows nothing. She unfolds her hands for the first time all lesson.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d151b6] = {你打开又合上，什么也看不出来。她看看它，又看看你，短促而愉快地笑了一声。|开了，合了，一丝痕迹也没有。“很好，”她说，“以后永远别对一扇门这么做。”|这个更难，可它还是开了，又合上了，什么也看不出来。她整节课第一次松开了交叠的双手。}\n# en: {The seal cracks with a tiny, final sound. The headmistress writes something in her book. She remembers every mistake, and now there is one more.|It opens, and it shows. \"Everyone breaks one,\" she says pleasantly. \"Rarely so loudly.\"|The harder seals are harder for a reason. It opens with a mark that won't go, and she lets you sit with it, unhurried.}\nopt.right.say.2 [d1b681] = {封印发出一声细小而决绝的脆响。院长在本子上写了点什么。她记得每一个错误，现在又多了一个。|开是开了，痕迹也露了。“每个人都会弄坏一个，”她和颜悦色地说，“很少有人弄得这么响。”|更难的封印难是有原因的。它开了，留下一道去不掉的痕迹，她不慌不忙，让你对着它坐着。}\n\n@event hollow_reading_the_blank\n# en: Reading the Blank Page\ntitle [1973a9] = 读空白的一页\n# en: {\"Most things that are hidden are not gone,\" the headmistress says, and gives you a blank page. \"Find what was written. Gently.\"|A new blank page today. It feels warmer than paper should.|You know the weight of hidden writing in a page by now, like a pocket with something in it.} {The books on the nearest shelf seem to lean closer.|The lamp burns low and blue.|Somewhere in the stacks, a book turns its own page.} {final_year: She asks where you'll go after graduation, and listens.|She is reading while you work, and doesn't look up.}\ntext [747881] = {“大多数被藏起来的东西并没有消失，”院长说着，递给你一张白纸，“找出写过的东西。轻一点。”|今天又是一张新的白纸。它比纸该有的温度要暖。|如今你能掂出一页纸里藏着字的分量，就像口袋里装着东西。} {最近那排书架上的书似乎凑近了些。|油灯烧得又低又蓝。|书库深处，有本书自己翻了一页。} {final_year: 她问你毕业后要去哪里，然后认真听着。|她在你干活时看书，头也不抬。}\n# en: A student bent over an apparently blank page by lamplight, faint lines of ink surfacing under their fingertips, black-bound books leaning in from the shelves.\nscene [6dbe72] = 一名学生借着灯光俯身看一张看似空白的纸，淡淡的墨迹在指尖下浮现，黑色封皮的书从书架上倾身过来。\n# en: Coax the ink up slowly\nopt.left.label [a0bd04] = 慢慢把墨迹引出来\n# en: {You warm the page with a thread of power, a breath at a time. The writing surfaces slowly, safe and dull. It's someone's laundry list, very carefully hidden.|Line by line, the ink comes up. It's an old timetable. The lesson was in the finding.|It takes most of your strength. The page gives up a single sentence about the weather, decades ago.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ea3c8e] = {你用一缕法力一口气一口气地温着纸页。字迹慢慢浮上来，安全又无聊：某人的洗衣清单，藏得非常仔细。|墨迹一行一行浮现，是一张旧课表。这一课的意义在于找到它。|这几乎耗尽了你的力气。纸页只吐出一句话，讲的是几十年前的天气。}\n# en: Read it bare-handed\nopt.right.label [de8da5] = 徒手直接读\n# en: {The ink comes up fast under your fingers, and so does a chill that stays in your hand until morning.|You read it quickly and well. Your fingertips ache for an hour, and the headmistress hands you a cup of tea without comment.|You find the writing the way you'd find a coin in a pocket, and read it bare-handed. Somewhere in the stacks a page turns, and your fingers are cold until supper.}\nopt.right.say.1 [592cda] = {墨迹在你指下飞快浮现，一股寒意也一起涌上来，在你手里一直待到早上。|你读得又快又好。指尖疼了一个钟头，院长一言不发地递给你一杯茶。|你像在口袋里摸到一枚硬币那样找到了字迹，空手读了出来。书架深处有一页书翻了过去，你的手指一直凉到晚饭。}\n# en: Let your ward do the reading\nopt.up.label [3adba1] = 让护界替你读\n# en: {Your ward settles over the page like a held breath, and the writing rises into it. The headmistress raises an eyebrow, pleased.|You read through the ward as if through glass. It costs you, but not your hands.|The hidden lines surface inside your shell, one by one, in no hurry.}\nopt.up.say.1 [50deed] = {你的护界像一口屏住的气一样罩在纸页上，字迹浮进其中。院长扬起眉毛，颇为满意。|你隔着护界读，像隔着玻璃。它耗你的力，但不伤你的手。|藏起的字句在你的护壳里一行行浮现，不慌不忙。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_more.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_more.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event evo_fog_to_the_line\n# en: Fog to the Line\ntitle [db3387] = 雾到线为止\n# en: {Professor Thorne chalks a line on the wall at shoulder height. \"Water and fire together. Make fog. To the line, and not a hair over.\"|Fog day. Thorne's chalk line is lower than last time.|The fog line again. Thorne no longer explains; he just draws it and folds his arms.} {The windows have been shut.|Somebody's fog smells of burnt toast.|{season == \"winter\": The fog freezes on the windows in ferns.|The hall is warm, and the fog comes too easily.}}\ntext [b72f54] = {索恩教授在墙上齐肩高处画了一道粉笔线。“水与火并用。造雾。到线为止，多一根头发都不行。”|造雾日。索恩的粉笔线比上回低了。|又是雾线。索恩不再解释；他只是画好线，抱起胳膊。} {窗户都关上了。|有人的雾闻起来像烤焦的吐司。|{season == \"winter\": 雾在窗上冻成了蕨叶。|大厅里很暖，雾来得太容易。}}\n# en: A lecture hall filling slowly with white fog from students' hands, a chalk line drawn on the wall at shoulder height, a stern old professor holding a measuring stick.\nscene [9f8962] = 讲堂里学生们手中缓缓涌出白雾，墙上齐肩高处画着一道粉笔线，一位严厉的老教授握着一根量尺。\n# en: Balance fire and water exactly\nopt.left.label [7ee0f3] = 精准平衡水与火\n# en: {Your fog rises, thick and white, and stops at the line as if it can read. Thorne looks at it a long time. \"Hm.\"|Your fog fills the hall to the chalk precisely. Thorne makes a small mark in his book; you'd give a crown to know what.|You hold the balance so finely that your fog comes up the wall like a tide and stops. Thorne nods, once. From him, a standing ovation.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b6f29d] = {你的雾升起来，浓而白，到线就停，仿佛识字。索恩盯着它看了很久。“嗯。”|你的雾不多不少正好灌到粉笔线。索恩在本子上做了个小记号；你愿意出一个克朗知道写了什么。|你把平衡掌握得极精，雾像潮水一样爬上墙，然后停住。索恩点了一次头。对他来说，这就是起立鼓掌。}\n# en: {Too much fire. Your fog turns to scalding steam and the front row leaves. Thorne opens a window without comment.|Too much water. A small rain falls on your desk, and only your desk, and on your hands, which are cold for an hour. Thorne writes something down.|You overshoot the line by a head and scald your wrist doing it. Thorne measures the fog with his stick, twice, in silence.}\nopt.left.say.2 [d58808] = {火太多了。你的雾成了烫人的蒸汽，前排的人都撤了。索恩不发一言地打开了窗。|水太多了。一场小雨落在你的桌上，只落在你的桌上，还落在你手上，冷了一个小时。索恩记下了什么。|你的雾超线一个头，还烫着了手腕。索恩用尺量了两遍，一言不发。}\n# en: Hold the water; let a partner burn\nopt.right.label [aec9e6] = 你出水，搭档出火\n# en: {You call the water and your partner warms it. Half the credit, half the effort, and a good share of your mana.|You hold a ball of water steady for twenty minutes while your partner fusses with the flame. Your arms shake; your fog is fine.|You're the best water-holder in the class now, which Thorne calls \"a respectable specialism\" in a tone that isn't sure.}\nopt.right.say.1 [643a17] = {你唤水，搭档加热。一半功劳，一半力气，外加你相当一部分法力。|搭档摆弄火苗的二十分钟里，你一直稳稳托着一团水。你的胳膊在抖；你们的雾没问题。|你如今是班上托水最稳的人，索恩称之为“一门体面的专长”，语气却拿不准。}\n\n@event astro_dream_diary\n# en: The Dream Diary\ntitle [f68ac4] = 梦境日记\n# en: {Every first-year astromancer keeps a dream diary, and once a month you read an entry aloud for the class to interpret. Today it's your turn.|Dream diaries again. The lecturer reminds you that dreams are evidence; the class reminds you they're also very funny.|Your turn with the dream diary. The class leans in; your entries have a following.} {Last night you dreamed about a goat in a crown.|Last night you dreamed your teeth were made of keys.|{season == \"winter\": Last night you dreamed of snow falling upward.|Last night you dreamed of an exam in a language made of birds.}}\ntext [1e4979] = {每个一年级星象学生都要记梦境日记，每月一次当众朗读一篇，让全班解梦。今天轮到你了。|又是梦境日记。讲师提醒你们梦是证据；全班提醒你们梦也非常好笑。|又轮到你读梦境日记了。全班凑了过来；你的日记有一批忠实读者。} {昨晚你梦见一只戴王冠的山羊。|昨晚你梦见自己的牙齿是钥匙做的。|{season == \"winter\": 昨晚你梦见雪往天上落。|昨晚你梦见一场用鸟语出题的考试。}}\n# en: A circle of yawning students under an observatory dome, each holding a small notebook, one standing to read aloud while the others try not to laugh.\nscene [c6085a] = 观星台穹顶下一圈打着哈欠的学生，人人手捧小本子，一个站起来朗读，其他人憋着不笑。\n# en: Read the real one aloud\nopt.left.label [ee46cd] = 读真实的那篇\n# en: {You read about the goat. The class interprets it as ambition, appetite and possibly a farm. You go pink, and learn more than they do.|You read the dream of key-teeth. The lecturer finds three meanings in it; the class finds four, one of them rude.|You read it straight. The interpretations are thoughtful and humiliating, in equal parts.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5b7285] = {你读了山羊那篇。全班解读为野心、食欲，可能还有一座农场。你脸红了，学到的却比他们多。|你读了钥匙牙齿的梦。讲师从中解出三层意思；全班解出四层，其中一层很粗俗。|你照实读了。解读既深刻又丢人，各占一半。}\n# en: Invent something impressive\nopt.right.label [8292d6] = 编个了不起的梦\n# en: {You describe a dream of a silver hare on a staircase. The lecturer is deeply moved. You must remember to have it someday.|You invent a prophetic dream so convincing that half the class writes it down. The lecturer gives it full marks and a worried look.|Your invented dream gets a round of applause. You almost wish it were true.}\nopt.right.say.1 [600d7b] = {你描述了一个梦：一只银色野兔站在楼梯上。讲师深受触动。你得记着哪天真梦一回。|你编了一个预言梦，逼真到半个班都记了下来。讲师给了满分，外加一个担忧的眼神。|你编的梦赢得了一阵掌声。你几乎希望它是真的。}\n# en: {You get the silver hare's symbolism backwards. The lecturer, who keeps a book of real dream symbols, notices, and says so, gently, in front of everyone.|You overdo it. Nobody dreams in footnotes. The class is delighted to say so.|Halfway through, you forget what the hare was doing. The class finishes your dream for you, badly.}\nopt.right.say.2 [07e04d] = {你把银色野兔的寓意说反了。讲师有一本真正的梦境符号书，她发现了，并且当着所有人温和地指了出来。|你编过头了。没人会梦见脚注。全班很乐意指出这一点。|讲到一半，你忘了那只野兔在干什么。全班替你把梦编完了，编得很糟。}\n\n@event alch_antidote_race\n# en: The Antidote Race\ntitle [7842e3] = 解毒竞赛\n# en: {Sister Amaranth sets a beaker of clear liquid in front of each pair. \"A mild poison. Identify it. Counter it. Then drink both. Hourglass.\"|Antidote day. Sister Amaranth has made the poisons milder and the hourglass shorter.|The antidote race. You can smell three of Sister Amaranth's poisons from the door now; she has started using a fourth.} {The last pair to finish washes everyone's beakers.|One pair's tongues are already blue.|{season == \"summer\": The heat makes everything evaporate twice as fast.|Rain hammers the windows and nobody can hear the hourglass.}}\ntext [ea1dae] = {阿玛兰斯修女在每组面前放一烧杯清液。“轻微的毒。辨认它。解了它。然后两样都喝下去。沙漏。”|解毒日。阿玛兰斯修女把毒调得更轻，沙漏换得更短。|解毒竞赛。如今你在门口就能闻出阿玛兰斯修女的三种毒；她开始用第四种了。} {最后完成的那组要洗所有人的烧杯。|有一组人的舌头已经蓝了。|{season == \"summer\": 天热，什么都蒸发得快一倍。|雨点猛砸窗户，谁也听不见沙漏。}}\n# en: A laboratory bench lined with beakers of clear liquid, each pair of students racing to identify and counter a mild poison while a healer watches an hourglass.\nscene [da26fe] = 实验台上摆着一排装清液的烧杯，每组学生争分夺秒地辨认并化解一种轻微的毒，一位治疗师盯着沙漏。\n# en: Identify it by testing\nopt.left.label [b6e43b] = 靠试剂辨认\n# en: {Three reagents and one colour change say hiccup-root. You counter it with mint and pepper and drink both before the sand's half gone. \"Keep doing that,\" says Sister Amaranth.|You name it in two tests and brew the counter nearly blindfold. Sister Amaranth watches you drink without a word, which is praise.|You identify the fourth poison, the one she thought nobody would get. She looks at you over the hourglass and adds a fifth to her list.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d6a8df] = {三种试剂，一次变色，说明是打嗝根。你用薄荷和胡椒解了它，沙子还没漏到一半就把两样都喝了。“保持下去。”阿玛兰斯修女说。|你两次检测就认了出来，几乎蒙着眼就调好了解药。阿玛兰斯修女看着你喝下去，一言不发，这就是夸奖。|你认出了第四种毒，就是她以为没人认得出的那种。她隔着沙漏看了你一眼，在单子上添了第五种。}\n# en: {You guess wrong and drink a counter to the wrong thing. Your tongue is blue until supper.|You run out of sand, drink your best guess and hiccup in iambic rhythm for an hour.|Your antidote works on everything except the poison. Sister Amaranth hands you a second one, the right one, without comment.}\nopt.left.say.2 [5ddd66] = {你猜错了，喝下了解另一种毒的药。你的舌头一直蓝到晚饭。|沙子漏完了，你喝下最好的猜测，按抑扬格的节奏打了一小时嗝。|你的解药什么都能解，除了这种毒。阿玛兰斯修女一言不发地递给你第二瓶，对的那瓶。}\n# en: Brew a broad remedy instead\nopt.right.label [fa70a8] = 改调一剂广谱药\n# en: {You skip the testing and brew a remedy that counters most mild poisons, expensively. It works; it takes a lot out of you.|A broad remedy, slow and draining, and very safe. Sister Amaranth notes it as \"a cautious answer\", which is not praise.|Your broad remedy is so reliable now that the other pairs come to borrow it. Brewing it still leaves you hollow.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6d2edc] = {你跳过检测，调了一剂能解大多数轻毒的药，代价不菲。管用；也掏空了你。|一剂广谱药，慢而耗神，非常稳妥。阿玛兰斯修女记下“谨慎的答案”，这可不是夸奖。|你的广谱药如今可靠到别组都来借。调它仍然让你浑身发虚。}\n\n@event illu_turnip_market\n# en: The Turnip Market\ntitle [85c2c6] = 萝卜集市\n# en: {Professor Vey gives everyone a turnip and a stall. \"Glamour it into something I'll pay for. I have a purse of play-crowns, and no mercy.\"|Turnip market. Vey's purse of play-crowns is fatter this time, and so is her suspicion.|The turnip market again. Vey walks the stalls with her purse and the expression of a duchess at a bad auction.} {Your neighbour's turnip is already a very convincing tiara.|Somebody's ruby is dripping slightly.|{season == \"autumn\": The turnips are fresh from Old Maud's patch and smell of earth.|The hall smells overwhelmingly of turnip.}}\ntext [cb7975] = {维伊教授给每人发一个萝卜和一个摊位。“把它变成我愿意掏钱的东西。我有一袋游戏克朗，毫无怜悯之心。”|萝卜集市。维伊的游戏克朗钱袋这回更鼓了，她的疑心也是。|又是萝卜集市。维伊拎着钱袋在摊位间巡视，神情像个在糟糕拍卖会上的公爵夫人。} {你邻座的萝卜已经变成一顶以假乱真的冠冕。|有人的红宝石在微微滴水。|{season == \"autumn\": 萝卜是刚从老莫德菜地里拔的，带着泥土味。|大厅里萝卜味浓得化不开。}}\n# en: Students at makeshift market stalls in a lecture hall holding up dazzling jewels that flicker, now and then, into turnips, while a theatrical professor drifts between them with a purse.\nscene [2c666f] = 讲堂里搭起临时集市摊位，学生们举着璀璨的宝石，宝石时不时闪回萝卜原形，一位戏剧化的教授拎着钱袋在其间游走。\n# en: Make it dazzle\nopt.left.label [621304] = 让它光彩夺目\n# en: {Your turnip becomes a sapphire the size of an egg. Vey turns it over, sniffs it, and pays three play-crowns. \"It smells of turnip,\" she says. \"I'll allow it.\"|You turn it into a golden pear. Vey bites it, which is cheating, then pays anyway, laughing.|Your turnip becomes a pocket watch that ticks. Vey listens to it and pays you double. \"You made me want it,\" she says. \"That's the whole art.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [4ad15a] = {你的萝卜变成一颗鸡蛋大的蓝宝石。维伊翻来覆去看了看，闻了闻，付了三个游戏克朗。“有萝卜味，”她说，“我认了。”|你把它变成一只金梨。维伊咬了一口，这是作弊，然后照样付钱，笑出了声。|你的萝卜变成一块滴答作响的怀表。维伊听了听，付了双倍。“你让我想要它，”她说，“这就是全部的艺术。”}\n# en: {Your jewel flickers back into a turnip as Vey reaches for it. The whole hall sees. Vey pays you one play-crown, out of pity.|Your sapphire has roots. Vey points this out, delighted, to everyone.|Your watch ticks, then sprouts leaves. \"Charming,\" says Vey, and moves on.}\nopt.left.say.2 [57b3b4] = {维伊伸手时，你的宝石闪回了萝卜原形。整个大厅都看见了。维伊出于怜悯付了你一个游戏克朗。|你的蓝宝石长着根须。维伊兴高采烈地指给所有人看。|你的怀表滴答了几声，然后长出了叶子。“迷人，”维伊说着走开了。}\n# en: Buy, and catch the fakes\nopt.right.label [45b512] = 当买家，揪出假货\n# en: {You're given a few play-crowns and set loose as a customer. You catch three turnips in disguise by their smell. Vey nods. \"The other half of the art.\"|You haggle your way down the stalls and catch the dripping ruby out. Its maker sulks.|You can tell a glamoured turnip at ten paces now. Vey makes you her assistant buyer for the rest of the hour.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6e9565] = {你拿到几个游戏克朗，被放出去当顾客。你凭气味揪出了三个伪装的萝卜。维伊点点头。“艺术的另一半。”|你一路砍价逛遍摊位，揪出了那颗滴水的红宝石。它的主人生起了闷气。|如今隔十步你就认得出施了魅影的萝卜。维伊让你当她的助理买手，直到下课。}\n\n@event beast_bristle_trim\n# en: The Bristle Trim\ntitle [5b84c9] = 修剪扫帚尾\n# en: {Professor Brask has the class trimming broom tails. \"Broom's a creature. Sulky, stupid creature. Groom it, or it grooms you. Into a hedge.\"|Bristle day again. Brask inspects each broom tail like a hoof, muttering to the brooms more kindly than to you.|Broom trimming. Your school broom recognises you and shivers.} {A hippogriff watches from the paddock, judging.|One broom keeps flicking its twigs at whoever's trimming it.|{season == \"winter\": The twigs are brittle with frost and snap if you look at them.|The twigs are green and springy, and fight back.}}\ntext [ac9419] = {布拉斯克教授让全班修剪扫帚尾。“扫帚是生灵。又闷又蠢的生灵。你不打理它，它就打理你。打理进树篱里。”|又是修尾日。布拉斯克像检查马蹄一样检查每条扫帚尾，对扫帚嘟囔的话比对你们和气。|修剪扫帚。你的校用扫帚认出了你，打了个哆嗦。} {一头鹰头马身有翼兽在围场里看着，评头论足。|有把扫帚一直朝修剪它的人甩枝条。|{season == \"winter\": 枝条冻得发脆，你看它一眼就断。|枝条青绿有弹性，会还手。}}\n# en: A row of school brooms clamped to a bench outside the stables, students with shears trimming and binding the twigs while a stern professor inspects each tail.\nscene [ac23fc] = 马厩外一排校用扫帚夹在长凳上，学生们拿着剪子修剪捆扎枝条，一位严厉的教授挨个检查扫帚尾。\n# en: Trim it twig by twig\nopt.left.label [1799a9] = 一根一根地修\n# en: {You bind every twig and trim the tail to a perfect curve. Brask looks it over. \"Adequate. Broom thinks so too.\" Your fingers are numb, and the hour felt like three.|You trim for an hour, slowly, and your broom flies straighter for it. It's mindless and it isn't; your back aches.|You trim as Brask taught you, twig by twig. The broom purrs, if a broom can purr. Brask says it can. It's still the dullest hour of your week.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9ddc8e] = {你捆好每根枝条，把尾巴修成完美的弧线。布拉斯克看了看。“还行。扫帚也这么觉得。”你的手指都麻了，这一小时像过了三小时。|你慢慢修了一小时，扫帚飞得更直了。这活儿说无脑也无脑，说不无脑也不无脑；你的背疼了。|你照布拉斯克教的，一根一根地修。扫帚发出呼噜声，如果扫帚会呼噜的话。布拉斯克说会。这仍是你一周里最无聊的一小时。}\n# en: Test-fly it untrimmed\nopt.right.label [06c2f1] = 不修就试飞\n# en: {You fly it rough and it flies well anyway, the way some ugly ponies do. Brask watches, grunts, and lets you off.|The untrimmed broom bucks once and then settles under you like an old dog. Brask, drily: \"Lucky. Don't count on it.\"|You know your broom's moods now; you fly it shaggy and it goes like a hawk. Brask almost smiles.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5f36d9] = {你骑着毛糙的它飞，它照样飞得不错，就像有些丑小马一样。布拉斯克看着，哼了一声，放过了你。|没修的扫帚尥了一下蹶子，然后像条老狗一样在你身下安顿下来。布拉斯克淡淡地说：“走运。别指望。”|你如今摸透了扫帚的脾气；毛糙着飞，它也像鹰一样快。布拉斯克差点笑了。}\n# en: {The shaggy tail catches the wind and you go into the hedge, as Brask predicted. She says so, to the hippogriff.|The broom sheds twigs in mid-air and spins you into the paddock. The hippogriff is unimpressed. So is Brask.|It bucks you off at head height. Brask tells the broom it did well, and tells you to get up.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a43efa] = {毛糙的尾巴兜住了风，你一头扎进树篱，正如布拉斯克所料。她对那头鹰头马身有翼兽说了这话。|扫帚在半空掉枝条，把你转进了围场。鹰头马身有翼兽不为所动。布拉斯克也是。|它在一人高的地方把你甩了下来。布拉斯克夸扫帚干得好，叫你起来。}\n\n@event umbral_shadow_witness\n# en: The Shadow Witness\ntitle [1d802b] = 影子证人\n# en: {An inkpot has been taken from the professor's desk, on purpose, for the lesson. \"The shadows in this room saw who took it,\" the professor says. \"Ask them.\"|Another staged theft, a quill this time. The professor turns the lamps down. \"Shadows remember. They just don't like being asked.\"|The shadow witness lesson. You know which corner's shadow gossips and which one sulks.} {The lamps burn low and blue.|The shadow under the window is longer than it should be.|{season == \"winter\": It's dark by mid-afternoon, and the shadows are bold.|A moth keeps throwing huge shadows across the ceiling, confusing everyone.}}\ntext [7cc732] = {教授桌上的墨水瓶被拿走了，这是故意的，为了上课。“这间屋里的影子看见了是谁拿的，”教授说，“去问它们。”|又一场布置好的失窃，这回是一支羽毛笔。教授把灯调暗。“影子会记得。它们只是不喜欢被问。”|影子证人课。你知道哪个角落的影子爱八卦，哪个爱赌气。} {灯火低低的，泛着蓝。|窗下的影子比它该有的长。|{season == \"winter\": 下午过半天就黑了，影子胆子很大。|一只飞蛾不停地把巨大的影子投到天花板上，把大家都弄糊涂了。}}\n# en: A dim lecture room at the top of a tower, an empty desk with an inkpot-shaped gap in the dust, students crouching to question the long shadows in the corner.\nscene [247446] = 塔顶昏暗的讲堂里，一张空桌的灰尘上留着墨水瓶形状的空白，学生们蹲下身盘问角落里的长影子。\n# en: Question the long shadow\nopt.left.label [fd07dc] = 盘问那道长影子\n# en: {The long shadow leans toward the door, then toward a third-year's bag. The inkpot is in it. The professor nods. \"Good. Shadows point. People lie.\"|You coax the shadow into showing a hand, a sleeve, a stride. You name the thief; the thief applauds.|The gossiping corner tells you everything before you've finished asking. The professor marks you down for leading the witness, and up for results.}\nopt.left.say.1 [df26e7] = {长影子先朝门口倾斜，又朝一个三年级生的书包倾斜。墨水瓶就在里面。教授点点头。“很好。影子会指，人会撒谎。”|你哄影子显出一只手、一截袖子、一个步态。你指认了小偷；小偷鼓起了掌。|爱八卦的角落不等你问完就全说了。教授因你诱导证人扣分，又因你有结果加分。}\n# en: {The shadow stretches toward you instead, and the cold of it settles in your hands. You name the wrong student, confidently.|The shadow shows you a thief who looks a great deal like the professor. The professor finds this very funny; you're freezing.|The sulking corner gives you nothing but chill. You spend the hour with numb fingers and no answer.}\nopt.left.say.2 [2ae0a9] = {影子反而朝你伸过来，它的寒意钻进了你的手。你信心十足地指认错了人。|影子给你看的小偷长得非常像教授。教授觉得这很好笑；你冻僵了。|赌气的角落只给了你一身寒意。你一整节课手指冻麻，什么答案也没得到。}\n# en: Watch the doorway, not the dark\nopt.right.label [134e06] = 盯着门口，不看暗处\n# en: {You ignore the shadows and work out who left the room and when. You get the right answer by the wrong method. The professor sighs, but marks it.|You reason it out from the dust on the desk. Correct, and entirely unmagical.|You solve it from the doorway again. The professor has started calling you \"the magistrate\", and doesn't mean it as a compliment.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3274c9] = {你不理影子，推算出谁什么时候离开过房间。方法错了，答案对了。教授叹口气，还是给了分。|你从桌上的灰尘推理出来。正确，而且毫无魔法可言。|你又从门口解开了谜。教授开始叫你“地方法官”，而且不是在夸你。}\n\n@event art_fair_entry\n# en: Something for the Fair\ntitle [23317b] = 参展作品\n# en: {Every Artifice student is expected to have something for the Artificers' Fair. Yours is a clockwork wren that sings a little flat.|Fair entries again. Your wren has started to hop, all by itself, which nobody built into it.|Your fair entry has a name, a shelf and a temperament. The class has opinions about it.} {Your neighbour's entry, a self-pouring teapot, is pouring onto the floor.|Someone's entry has escaped and is hiding under the bench.|{season == \"spring\": The fair is weeks away and nobody is ready.|The fire buckets have been moved closer to the benches, on the faculty's orders.}}\ntext [3484c4] = {每个奇械术学生都得为奇械博览会准备点东西。你的是一只唱歌有点跑调的发条鹪鹩。|又在准备参展作品。你的鹪鹩开始蹦跳了，全凭它自己，谁也没给它造这个本事。|你的参展作品有了名字、一个架子和一副脾气。全班对它各有意见。} {你邻座的作品，一把会自己倒茶的茶壶，正往地上倒。|有人的作品逃跑了，躲在工作台底下。|{season == \"spring\": 离展会还有几周，谁都没准备好。|按教职员的命令，消防水桶挪得离工作台更近了。}}\n# en: A workshop bench crowded with half-built clockwork entries for the Artificers' Fair, a student holding a tiny screwdriver over a machine that twitches.\nscene [046c89] = 工坊的工作台上挤满了为奇械博览会准备的半成品发条作品，一个学生拿着一把小螺丝刀悬在一台抽搐着的机器上方。\n# en: Tinker with your own\nopt.left.label [1b58cd] = 调校自己的作品\n# en: {You adjust the wren's voice-reed by a hair and it sings a whole scale. The workshop goes quiet to listen.|The wren hops, sings and flutters, and nothing catches fire. That's the bar, and you've cleared it.|You get the wren to sing something nobody built into it. You don't know how. You write it down very fast.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b6983a] = {你把鹪鹩的发声簧片调了一丝，它唱出了完整的音阶。整个工坊安静下来聆听。|鹪鹩蹦着、唱着、扑腾着，什么也没着火。这就是及格线，你过了。|你让鹪鹩唱出了一段谁也没给它装进去的旋律。你不知道是怎么回事。你飞快地记了下来。}\n# en: {The wren sings one perfect note and then smokes. Your fingers are singed from catching it.|You fix the flat note and break the hop. The wren lurches round the bench like a drunk sparrow. You burn a thumb on the spring.|The wren sings, beautifully, and then won't stop. You have to pull a gear with smoking pliers to get some peace, and burn yourself doing it.}\nopt.left.say.2 [97cd04] = {鹪鹩唱出一个完美的音，然后冒起了烟。你接住它时烫伤了手指。|你修好了跑调，却弄坏了蹦跳。鹪鹩像只醉麻雀一样在工作台上东倒西歪。你被弹簧烫了拇指。|鹪鹩唱得很美，然后停不下来。你只好用冒烟的钳子拔掉一个齿轮才得清静，还烫着了自己。}\n# en: Rescue the neighbour's entry\nopt.right.label [201236] = 抢救邻座的作品\n# en: {You stop the teapot pouring, rebind its spout-charm and dry the floor. Your own wren waits, unfinished, all hour. Your neighbour will owe you for life.|You coax the escaped entry out from under the bench and fix its leg. Your wren sulks on its shelf, untouched.|You spend the hour on someone else's machine again. Theirs works now. Yours sings flat.}\nopt.right.say.1 [729187] = {你让茶壶停止倒茶，重新绑好壶嘴咒，擦干地板。你自己的鹪鹩没完工，等了一整节课。邻座这辈子都欠你的。|你把逃跑的作品从工作台底下哄出来，修好了它的腿。你的鹪鹩在架子上生闷气，没人碰它。|你又把一节课花在了别人的机器上。他们的能用了。你的还在跑调。}\n\n@event star_cloudy_appointment\n# en: A Cloudy Appointment\ntitle [f922c1] = 阴天的约课\n# en: {Tonight's appointment, says this morning's note, will go ahead \"whatever the sky thinks\". The sky thinks clouds, wall to wall.|Another clouded appointment tonight. The note is unbothered. \"Starlight comes through cloud. It simply has to want to.\"|Cloud again tonight. You know the dome's cold corners and which way the gaps will drift.} {Somewhere behind the murk, the star you know best will be up.|The note says a thin gap may open in the west.|{season == \"winter\": It will be snow-cloud, heavy and low; your breath will freeze on the rail.|It will be warm cloud, with rain coming.}}\ntext [fd4a83] = {今早的字条说，今晚的课“不管天空怎么想”都照常进行。天空想的是云，铺天盖地。|又一个阴天的约课。字条上的语气毫不在意。“星光能穿透云层。它只需要愿意。”|今晚又是云。你知道穹顶里哪个角落冷，云缝会往哪边飘。} {在那片阴霾后面，你最熟悉的那颗星会升起来。|字条说西边可能会开一道细缝。|{season == \"winter\": 会是低沉厚重的雪云；你的呼吸会冻在栏杆上。|会是暖云，雨正在来。}}\n# en: A brass observatory dome open to a heavy, clouded night sky, a student reaching up with both hands toward a faint gleam behind the clouds.\nscene [f5326a] = 黄铜观星穹顶朝着厚重的阴云夜空敞开，一个学生伸出双手，够向云后一抹微光。\n# en: Call through the cloud\nopt.left.label [6918ba] = 穿云唤星\n# en: {The starlight comes down through the cloud like a rope through water, slow and then all at once. Your palms glow till dawn.|You call, and the cloud thins over your hands alone, a little round window in the sky. The lecturer writes for a long time.|You call the star by name and it answers, cloud or no cloud. You walk back down the stairs with light still in your fingers.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1810fd] = {星光像绳子穿过水一样穿过云层降下来，先慢，然后一下子全到了。你的掌心一直亮到天明。|你一唤，云只在你手上方变薄，天上开了一扇小小的圆窗。讲师写了很久。|你叫出那颗星的名字，它应了，不管有没有云。你走下楼梯时，手指里还有光。}\n# en: {You call until your hands shake. The cloud doesn't care. You go to bed drained and dark.|A thread of light comes halfway and frays in the murk. It takes something out of you as it goes.|You reach up for an hour into nothing. The lecturer says even nothing is a lesson. It doesn't feel like one.}\nopt.left.say.2 [5ad4c4] = {你唤到双手发抖。云毫不在乎。你上床时又累又暗。|一缕光降到一半，在阴霾里散开了。它走时带走了你的一些东西。|你朝着虚空伸手伸了一个小时。讲师说就连虚空也是一课。感觉可不像。}\n# en: Chart the gaps in the cloud\nopt.right.label [fafc06] = 绘制云缝图\n# en: {You sit by the rail all night logging the gaps, when and where and which stars peeked through. Cold to the bone; good data.|You chart the drift of the cloud like a sailor and predict the next gap to the minute. It comes; you're too frozen to cheer.|Your cloud log is pages long now. You know the sky's habits even when you can't see it, and your chest aches with cold.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b00704] = {你在栏杆边坐了一整夜，记录云缝：何时、何处、哪些星探出了头。冷到骨头里；数据很好。|你像水手一样绘出云的漂移，把下一道缝预测到了分钟。它来了；你冻得欢呼不出来。|你的云层日志如今已有好几页。就算看不见天，你也摸清了它的习性，胸口冷得发疼。}\n\n@event hollow_jarred_silences\n# en: Silences in Jars\ntitle [12282e] = 罐中的寂静\n# en: {Today the headmistress hands you an empty jar. \"Seal a silence in it. Not quiet; silence. You will know the difference by the end.\"|Another empty jar. Behind the headmistress, a shelf of stoppered jars, all apparently empty, all labelled in faded hands.|Silence jars. You know the weight of a well-sealed one now, heavier than it looks.} {The lamp hisses.|The books on the shelves have stopped breathing, as if to help.|{final_year: She asks what silence you'd keep, if you could keep only one.|She works at her own jar, unhurried, while you work at yours.}}\ntext [b7f096] = {今天院长递给你一只空罐子。“把一段寂静封进去。不是安静；是寂静。到最后你会知道其中的区别。”|又一只空罐子。院长身后有一架塞着瓶塞的罐子，看上去全是空的，标签的字迹都已褪色。|寂静罐。如今你知道一只封好的罐子有多沉，比看上去沉。} {灯在嘶嘶作响。|架上的书停止了呼吸，像是在帮忙。|{final_year: 她问你，如果只能留一段寂静，你会留哪一段。|她不紧不慢地封着自己的罐子，你封你的。}}\n# en: A windowless room lined with black-bound books and a shelf of small stoppered jars, each holding nothing visible, a student holding one up to the lamplight.\nscene [37a5d9] = 一间没有窗户的屋子，四壁排满黑皮书，一架小小的塞着瓶塞的罐子里看不见任何东西，一个学生把其中一只举到灯下。\n# en: Seal the room's silence\nopt.left.label [62c333] = 封存这间屋的寂静\n# en: {You draw the room's silence into the jar a breath at a time and stopper it. When you shake it, nothing happens, very heavily. She nods.|The silence fights the stopper like a cat fights a bath. You win, eventually, emptied out.|You seal a silence so clean that the lamp seems louder when you're done. The headmistress takes the jar and shelves it with the old ones.}\nopt.left.say.1 [79f708] = {你一口气一口气地把屋里的寂静吸进罐子，塞上瓶塞。摇一摇，什么也没发生，沉甸甸地什么也没发生。她点点头。|寂静像猫抗拒洗澡一样抗拒瓶塞。你最终赢了，人也被掏空了。|你封了一段如此纯净的寂静，封完后灯声似乎都响了。院长接过罐子，和那些旧罐子摆在一起。}\n# en: Unseal one of the old jars\nopt.right.label [f5213c] = 打开一只旧罐子\n# en: {You unstopper a jar dated a century ago. Out comes the silence of a room just after someone laughed. It's unbearably sad, and you don't know why.|You open an old jar and the room fills with the silence of a held breath before a goodbye. You close it again quickly. She says nothing, kindly.|You open one more old jar. A silence after snow. You sit in it a long while before you can speak.}\nopt.right.say.1 [004de2] = {你拔开一只一百年前的罐子。放出来的是有人刚笑过之后的屋子里的寂静。悲伤得让人受不了，你却不知为何。|你打开一只旧罐子，屋里充满了告别前屏住呼吸的寂静。你赶紧又封上。她什么也没说，很体贴。|你又打开一只旧罐子。雪后的寂静。你在里面坐了很久，才说得出话。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/courses_upper.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/courses_upper.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event evo_rain_barrel\n# en: Rain on Demand\ntitle [b62937] = 呼雨即来\n# en: {Tidecalling at Evocation II. Pull a cloud off the lake, park it over a barrel, and fill the barrel to the chalk line. Not over, not under.|The barrels are out on the terrace again, each with a chalk line.|You know your barrel now, the one with the iron band that's slipped.} {Professor Marisol is reading on the steps, under a perfectly dry patch of air.|The wind keeps trying to borrow the clouds.|{season == \"winter\": The clouds come off the lake as sleet.|A heron watches the barrels with professional interest.}}\ntext [21af36] = {塑能术 II 的唤潮课。从湖上拽一朵云，停在木桶上方，把桶灌到粉笔线为止。不许多，也不许少。|木桶又摆上了露台，每只都画着粉笔线。|如今你认得自己的桶了，就是铁箍滑下来的那只。} {玛丽索尔教授坐在台阶上看书，头顶那一小块空气干干爽爽。|风一直想把云借走。|{season == \"winter\": 云从湖上过来，落下的是冻雨。|一只苍鹭以专业的兴趣盯着这些木桶。}}\n# en: A row of students on a lakeside terrace, each with a small grey cloud above a wooden barrel, one cloud raining sideways onto a neighbour.\nscene [c50d40] = 湖畔露台上站着一排学生，每人头顶一小朵灰云，下方是一只木桶，其中一朵云正斜着把雨下到邻座身上。\n# en: Wring it out to the line\nopt.left.label [0e4325] = 拧到粉笔线\n# en: {The last drop lands on the chalk. Marisol looks up from her book and back down, which counts.|You stop the rain a breath early and let the drip finish the job. Exactly to the line.|You know how much your barrel leaks at the slipped band, and you rain that much extra. Exactly to the line.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fe435c] = {最后一滴正好落在粉笔线上。玛丽索尔从书上抬眼看了看，又低下头去，这就算数了。|你提前一口气收住雨，让余滴把活干完。不多不少，正到线上。|你知道你那只桶在松脱的铁箍处漏多少，就多下那么多。不多不少，正到线上。}\n# en: {Your cloud overshoots, fills the barrel, and keeps going down your collar.|The cloud drifts left and rains on the next barrel, which is not yours, and on its owner, who is very not yours. The splashback finds your collar anyway.|The heron moves at the wrong moment and so does your cloud. It empties itself over the heron, who leaves offended.}\nopt.left.say.2 [e794c2] = {你的云下过了头，灌满木桶后接着往你衣领里灌。|云往左飘，下在了旁边的桶上，那不是你的桶，也下在了桶的主人身上，那更不是你的人。溅起的水还是找到了你的衣领。|苍鹭在不该动的时候动了，你的云也是。它把雨全倒在了苍鹭身上，苍鹭气呼呼地走了。}\n# en: Drizzle it in, slowly\nopt.right.label [b367cf] = 慢慢地洒进去\n# en: {It takes the whole lesson. Your cloud is the size of a pillow by the end, and very tired.|You fill it a thimble at a time. The others have gone in for lunch. The line is met.|You drizzle it in while your barrel leaks it out at the band. It's a long lesson, but you win in the end.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9eee07] = {这花了整整一节课。到最后你的云只剩枕头那么大，而且累坏了。|你一顶针一顶针地往里灌。别人都去吃午饭了。线终于到了。|你一点一点往里下，桶又从铁箍那儿一点一点往外漏。这节课很漫长，但最后你赢了。}\n\n@event evo_ember_stair\n# en: Carrying the Ember\ntitle [5d68d8] = 掌中余火\n# en: {Pyromancy at Evocation II. Carry a live ember in your bare palm from the ground floor to the top of the Lecture Tower. Keep it alight. Keep your hand.|The ember drill again. The stair hasn't got any shorter.|Ember day. You've counted the stair by now, and you know where the draught is.} {A door on the third floor opens just as you pass.|Someone ahead of you is climbing very fast and swearing quietly.|The lamps on the stair are out, and your ember is the only light.}\ntext [633df9] = {塑能术 II 的火焰术课。赤手托一块燃着的炭，从讲堂塔底层走到塔顶。别让它灭，也别丢了你的手。|又是托炭练习。楼梯并没有变短。|托炭日。你早数清了台阶，也知道穿堂风在哪儿。} {你经过时，三楼的一扇门恰好打开。|你前面有人爬得飞快，嘴里小声骂着。|楼梯上的灯全灭了，你掌心的炭是唯一的光。}\n# en: A student climbing a dim spiral stair, a single glowing ember resting in their bare open palm.\nscene [0a00df] = 一名学生在昏暗的螺旋楼梯上攀登，一块发光的余炭躺在摊开的赤裸掌心里。\n# en: Feed it, and climb fast\nopt.left.label [76bdf4] = 喂着火，快步上\n# en: {You keep the ember bright with small breaths of heat and take the stairs two at a time. It arrives fat and orange.|You reach the top with the ember glowing and your palm only warm. The lecturer tips it into a brazier without a word.|Your ember lights the dark stair all the way up. People wait on the landings to follow you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ca1e10] = {你用细细的热息让炭保持明亮，一步跨两级台阶。到顶时它又胖又红。|你到达塔顶，炭还亮着，掌心只是温热。讲师一言不发地把它倒进火盆。|你的炭一路照亮了漆黑的楼梯。人们在平台上等着跟你走。}\n# en: {The ember flares on the landing and you learn exactly how hot a palm can get. You arrive with it alight, and with a blister.|You feed it too much at the draughty window. It bites. You carry it up the last flight in your other hand.|You feed it in the dark and misjudge it. It bites, and you finish the climb by its light and your own swearing.}\nopt.left.say.2 [2166ef] = {炭在楼梯平台上猛地一窜，你终于知道掌心能有多烫。到顶时它还燃着，你也多了个水泡。|在透风的窗边你喂得太多，它咬了你一口。最后一段楼梯，你换了只手托着它。|黑暗里你喂错了量。它咬了你一口，你借着它的光，骂骂咧咧地爬完了剩下的路。}\n# en: Shield it, and climb slow\nopt.right.label [d3a230] = 护着火，慢慢上\n# en: {You climb at a crawl with the ember cupped against the draughts. It is alight at the top, just. So are your nerves.|Step by step, a held breath at each window. Last up, but the ember's still glowing.|You shield it from the draught you know is coming. It reaches the top small and steady, and so do you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [216201] = {你一步一挪地往上爬，双手拢着炭挡风。到顶时它还亮着，勉强亮着。你的神经也是。|一步一步，每过一扇窗就屏一口气。你最后一个到，但炭还亮着。|你提前挡住了你知道会来的那股风。它到顶时小小的、稳稳的，你也是。}\n\n@event evo_firebreak\n# en: The Reed Burn\ntitle [b50131] = 烧芦苇\n# en: {Advanced Evocation does real work, the controlled burn of the dead reeds along the lake shore. Pyromancers lead the fire, tidecallers hold the line behind it.|Burn day. The shore smells of smoke for a week afterwards.|The reed burn. You can tell a fire's mood from its smoke now.} {The wind is steady from the lake.|The wind can't decide.|The reeds are drier than anyone would like.} {season == \"summer\": The air is hot already.|Your breath steams.}\ntext [9e6bd2] = {高等塑能术要干真活，湖岸那片枯芦苇的控制烧荒。火焰术的人领着火走，唤潮术的人在后面守线。|烧荒日。之后整整一周，湖岸都是烟味。|烧芦苇。如今你看烟就知道火的脾气。} {风从湖上稳稳地吹来。|风拿不定主意。|芦苇干得让谁都不放心。} {season == \"summer\": 空气已经很热了。|你呼出的气都成了白雾。}\n# en: Advanced students along a lake shore in a line, half of them calling a low wall of fire through dry reeds, half of them holding back a curtain of lake water behind it.\nscene [c4e483] = 高年级学生沿湖岸站成一排，一半人引着一道低矮的火墙穿过枯芦苇，另一半人在火墙后撑起一道湖水的帘幕。\n# en: Walk at the fire's head\nopt.left.label [13e32e] = 走在火头\n# en: {The fire follows your hands down the shore like a dog on a lead. The lecturer lets you take it all the way to the boathouse.|You lead the fire low and even, and it goes out where you tell it. Behind you there's nothing but clean black ground.|You lead the fire through the dry reeds slowly and keep it on a short lead. Not a spark gets away.}\nopt.left.say.1 [807c0c] = {火像牵着绳的狗，跟着你的手沿岸走。讲师让你一路把它带到船屋。|你把火领得又低又匀，它在你说停的地方熄灭。身后只剩干干净净的黑土。|你领着火慢慢穿过干芦苇，绳子收得很短。一颗火星都没逃掉。}\n# en: {A gust lifts the fire over your head. The tidecallers drop it in a wall of lake water, and you with it.|The fire jumps the line and runs. It's caught in a minute, but your eyebrows weren't.|The dry reeds go up faster than you can lead them. The tidecallers save the boathouse; you save some of your sleeve.}\nopt.left.say.2 [1c028e] = {一阵风把火掀过你头顶。唤潮术的人一道湖水墙把它压了下去，连你一起。|火越过了线，跑了起来。一分钟就被逮住了，你的眉毛却没逃掉。|干芦苇烧得比你领得还快。唤潮术的人保住了船屋，你保住了一部分袖子。}\n# en: Hold the water line\nopt.right.label [efca5e] = 守住水线\n# en: {You stand in the shallows holding a curtain of lake water for an hour. Nothing gets past you. Nobody notices, which is the point.|Your arms ache from keeping the water up. It's the least glamorous job on the shore and the one the lecturer checks first.|You hold the water line through the worst of it. When the dry reeds flare, your curtain is the one that holds.}\nopt.right.say.1 [20cdcb] = {你站在浅水里撑着一道湖水帘幕，撑了一个钟头。什么都没漏过去。没人注意到，这正是要点。|手臂撑水撑得发酸。这是岸上最不光彩的差事，却是讲师头一个检查的。|你在最凶险的时候撑住了水线。干芦苇蹿起火来时，撑住的正是你那道水帘。}\n\n@event evo_drowned_flame\n# en: Fire Under Water\ntitle [29fc18] = 水下之火\n# en: {Original work in Advanced Evocation, a fire that burns underwater. Every student gets a tank. Most tanks get a new pane every week.|Your tank is waiting, with a fresh crack along one pane.|The tank again. You talk to the water now, and to the fire, separately.} {Someone's tank has boiled dry and they're staring at it.|A flame at the next bench burns green beneath the water, quite calm.|The lecturer is standing well back today.}\ntext [b9657b] = {高等塑能术的原创作业：一团在水下燃烧的火。每人一只水箱。大多数水箱每周都要换块新玻璃。|你的水箱在等你，一块玻璃上新添了一道裂纹。|又是水箱。如今你会分别跟水和火说话。} {有人的水箱烧干了，他们正盯着它发呆。|邻桌一团火在水底燃着绿光，十分平静。|讲师今天站得远远的。}\n# en: A glass tank of water on a scorched bench, a small blue flame burning steadily at the bottom of it.\nscene [c9026b] = 一张烧焦的长桌上放着一只玻璃水箱，一小团蓝色火焰在箱底稳稳燃烧。\n# en: Light it at the bottom\nopt.left.label [a339ad] = 在箱底点燃它\n# en: {A small blue flame takes hold under the water and stays. The bubbles go up in a thin silver line.|It catches, gutters, and settles. The water around it doesn't even warm. You don't know how you did it.|You light it while the lecturer stands well back. It burns, small and stubborn, and the lecturer comes a step closer.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1987da] = {一小团蓝火在水下点着了，并且留了下来。气泡排成一道细细的银线往上冒。|它点着了，摇晃了一下，安顿下来。周围的水甚至没变暖。你也不知道自己是怎么做到的。|你点火时讲师站得远远的。它烧着了，小小的，很倔，讲师往前走了一步。}\n# en: {The water boils in a heartbeat and the pane splits. You wear the steam on your face for the rest of the day.|The flame goes up instead of in. The tank cracks, and your sleeve is wet and scorched at once.|You learn why the lecturer is standing back. The pane goes, and so does your eyebrow.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b4a824] = {水一瞬间就沸了，玻璃裂开。一整天你脸上都带着那股蒸汽。|火没往里钻，反往上蹿。水箱裂了，你的袖子同时又湿又焦。|你明白了讲师为什么站得那么远。玻璃碎了，你的一边眉毛也没了。}\n# en: Wrap it in a bubble first\nopt.right.label [69bcca] = 先裹一个气泡\n# en: {You hold a skin of air around the flame and sink them both. It's half a cheat, and the lecturer says so. It burns all hour.|Your flame sits in a bubble at the bottom like a moth in a jar. Not the assignment, but not nothing.|You wrap it in a bubble, as you always do now. The lecturer says it's time to try without, and doesn't make you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [495623] = {你用一层空气包住火，把它们一起沉下去。这算半个作弊，讲师也这么说。但它烧了一整节课。|你的火待在箱底的气泡里，像罐子里的飞蛾。不是作业要求的，但也不是什么都没有。|你像现在每次那样，给它裹上气泡。讲师说是时候试试不用了，但没有逼你。}\n\n@event astro_sealed_forecast\n# en: Tomorrow's Weather\ntitle [fdb53c] = 明日天气\n# en: {Foresight at Astromancy II. Write down tomorrow's weather to the hour, seal it, pin it to the board. The lecturer opens the envelopes a day later, in public.|The envelopes come out again.|You've written so many forecasts you dream in drizzle.} {Outside the dome the sky is giving nothing away.|Last week's best forecast just said \"Wet\", and was right.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the dome, and snow somewhere in the future.|Clouds over the lake, moving with intent.}}\ntext [7b1548] = {星象学 II 的预见课。写下明天每个时辰的天气，封好，钉到板上。讲师第二天会当众拆信。|信封又拿出来了。|你写过太多预报，连做梦都在下毛毛雨。} {穹顶外的天空什么都不肯透露。|上周最好的预报只写了一个“湿”字，而且对了。|{season == \"winter\": 穹顶上结了霜，未来某处有雪。|湖上的云正带着目的移动。}}\n# en: A row of sealed envelopes pinned to a corkboard in the observatory, each labelled with a student's name and tomorrow's date.\nscene [63cf64] = 观星台的软木板上钉着一排封好的信封，每只都写着学生的名字和明天的日期。\n# en: Commit to the hour\nopt.left.label [1bcf44] = 精确到时辰\n# en: {\"Rain at the third bell, stopping at the fourth.\" It does. The lecturer reads it out twice.|You forecast a fog to the hour. When the envelope is opened, the fog is pressing on the dome.|You write \"Sleet at the second bell, from the lake.\" It comes off the lake at the second bell. The lecturer stops reading the others.}\nopt.left.say.1 [39d2c0] = {“三更钟时下雨，四更钟停。”果然如此。讲师念了两遍。|你预报了一场准点的雾。信封拆开时，雾正贴在穹顶上。|你写下“二更钟，湖上来雨夹雪。”二更钟时，雨夹雪果然从湖上来了。讲师不再念别人的了。}\n# en: {\"Clear by noon.\" It rains till evening. Your envelope is read out to great enjoyment.|You predicted wind. Tomorrow turns out so still that a feather on the sill doesn't move.|You forecast with great confidence. Tomorrow is the opposite of everything you wrote, in order.}\nopt.left.say.2 [fda6b5] = {“午前转晴。”雨一直下到傍晚。你的信封被念出来，大家听得很开心。|你预报有风。结果第二天静得连窗台上的羽毛都没动一下。|你信心满满地写下预报。第二天的天气和你写的每一条都相反，而且顺序一致。}\n# en: Hedge, and learn from it\nopt.right.label [53eefc] = 留有余地，边看边学\n# en: {\"Some weather, at some point.\" The lecturer reads it out flatly and moves on. But you spent the hour watching how the good forecasters work.|You write a careful forecast with three possibilities. It's right, technically. The lecturer calls it \"an umbrella of a prophecy\".|You hedge, and read the others' envelopes after. You can tell the good ones by how short they are.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5376c0] = {“某个时候会有某种天气。”讲师面无表情地念完，继续下一个。但这一节课你都在看高手们怎么做。|你写了一份周全的预报，列了三种可能。严格说它是对的。讲师称之为“一把伞似的预言”。|你写得模棱两可，之后读了别人的信封。好的预报一看就知道，因为它们都很短。}\n\n@event astro_stubborn_telescope\n# en: The Stubborn Telescope\ntitle [2959bb] = 倔强的望远镜\n# en: {Astromancy II needs exact positions, and that means a night on the great telescope. At the morning lecture your name goes up on tonight's rota. The great telescope, everyone warns you, points wherever it likes.|Your name is on tonight's telescope rota again. The lecturer warns you it is already pointing at something else.|Your night on the telescope again. The lecturer only raises an eyebrow at you as the rota goes up.} {This week it wants the moon.|This week it's fixed on the lake, which is not in the sky.|This week it keeps drifting east, slowly, like a sigh.}\ntext [278ba4] = {星象学 II 需要精确的星位，这就得在大望远镜前熬一夜。早课上，你的名字被排进了今晚的轮值表。大家都提醒你：大望远镜爱指哪儿就指哪儿。|今晚的望远镜轮值表上又有你。讲师提醒你，它已经指着别处了。|又轮到你守望远镜。轮值表贴出来时，讲师只是冲你挑了挑眉。} {这周它想看月亮。|这周它盯着湖面，可湖不在天上。|这周它一直慢慢往东偏，像一声叹息。}\n# en: A student tugging at a huge brass telescope in a dome, while it points stubbornly at a different patch of sky.\nscene [6f250f] = 穹顶下，一名学生拽着一架巨大的黄铜望远镜，它却固执地指着另一片天空。\n# en: Argue it round\nopt.left.label [dba882] = 把它劝回来\n# en: {You coax it, inch by inch, and it settles on your star with a creak like a shrug.|You find the right thing to say to an old brass instrument. It points where you ask. You write the position fast.|You coax it off its eastward drift with a gentle hand and a sharp word. It sighs back to your star.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1f875f] = {你一寸一寸地哄它，它终于停在你的星上，嘎吱一声，像是耸了耸肩。|你找到了对一件老黄铜仪器该说的话。它指向你要的地方。你飞快地记下星位。|你温柔地推、严厉地说，把它从往东漂的劲头里哄了回来。它叹着气回到了你的星上。}\n# en: {It spends the night pointing where it likes. So do you, in the end, cold and annoyed.|You lean on it. It leans back. You go to bed at the fourth bell with nothing written down.|It drifts east all night, slowly, and takes you with it. You write down a very good position for the wrong star.}\nopt.left.say.2 [47a320] = {它整晚都指着它想指的地方。最后你也只好陪着，又冷又恼。|你靠在它身上使劲，它也靠回来。四更钟你上床时，本子上一个字都没有。|它整晚都在慢慢往东漂，把你也带走了。你为一颗错的星记下了一个非常精确的位置。}\n# en: Work it out from tables\nopt.right.label [0fbd24] = 用星表推算\n# en: {You leave the telescope to its moods and work the positions out by candlelight from the old tables. It takes until dawn.|Pages of sums by candle. The telescope watches over your shoulder, pointedly.|You use the tables and let the telescope drift where it likes. Your positions are good, and you're in bed by midnight.}\nopt.right.say.1 [53ea77] = {你随望远镜闹脾气去，借着烛光用旧星表把星位算出来。一直算到天亮。|一页又一页的烛下演算。望远镜在你肩后盯着，意味深长。|你用星表，任望远镜漂去它想去的地方。星位算得不错，午夜前你就上床了。}\n\n@event astro_long_watch\n# en: Keeping the Light\ntitle [72610b] = 守住星光\n# en: {Advanced Starlight. At the morning lecture you're given tonight's task. Hold one star's light in a lantern through a whole night on the roof. It will want to go home before dawn. Your job is to keep it.|The night watch on the roof comes round again. After the morning lecture you collect a lantern and a blanket.|Your lantern, your star, your corner of the roof, booked for tonight.} {The star you've drawn is pale and blue.|The star you've drawn is small and stubborn.|The star you've drawn is very old and very faint.} {season == \"winter\": There will be frost on the slates.|The night will be warm and full of moths.}\ntext [903ab3] = {高等星光课。早课上，你领到了今晚的任务。把一颗星的光装进灯笼，在屋顶守上一整夜。天亮前它会想回家，你的任务是留住它。|又轮到屋顶守夜了。早课后，你领了一盏灯笼和一条毯子。|你的灯笼，你的星，你在屋顶的那个角落，今晚都订好了。} {你抽到的星又淡又蓝。|你抽到的星又小又倔。|你抽到的星非常古老，非常微弱。} {season == \"winter\": 石板瓦上会结霜。|今夜会很温暖，满是飞蛾。}\n# en: A student on the observatory roof before dawn, a glass lantern in their lap holding a single cold point of starlight.\nscene [efcd22] = 天亮前的观星台屋顶上，一名学生膝上放着一盏玻璃灯笼，里面装着一点冷冷的星光。\n# en: Keep it all night\nopt.left.label [828910] = 守它一整夜\n# en: {At dawn your star is still in the lantern, blinking at the sun it was never meant to meet. You are blinking too.|You keep it till the lecturer comes up at sunrise. Your eyes are full of sand and your lantern is full of light.|Your old faint star almost goes out at the fourth bell. You talk to it until dawn, and it stays.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8f3c73] = {天亮时你的星还在灯笼里，对着它本不该遇见的太阳眨眼。你也在眨眼。|你一直守到讲师日出时上来。你眼里全是沙，灯笼里全是光。|你那颗古老而微弱的星在四更钟几乎熄灭。你跟它说话说到天亮，它留下了。}\n# en: Let it go at midnight\nopt.right.label [d7678c] = 午夜放它走\n# en: {You open the lantern and the light goes up like a kite let loose. You watch it all the way home and then go to bed.|You let it go early. The lecturer's note just says \"Released at midnight.\" You suspect it's not a compliment.|You let the old star go at midnight. It goes slowly, as if it isn't sure of the way. You watch it until you can't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c4bdd3] = {你打开灯笼，光像脱了线的风筝一样升上去。你一路目送它回家，然后去睡觉。|你早早放了它。讲师的记录上只写着“午夜放归”。你怀疑这不是夸奖。|你在午夜放走了那颗老星。它走得很慢，好像不太认得路。你一直看到看不见为止。}\n# en: Feed it your own starlight\nopt.up.label [07c1bd] = 喂它你的星光\n# en: {You pour a little of your own starlight into the lantern. The star brightens as if it had been lonely.|Your light and the star's together are enough to read by. You read until dawn.|You give the old star some of your light. It burns brighter than it has in a long time, and you feel it go out of you.}\nopt.up.say.1 [89444f] = {你往灯笼里倒了一点自己的星光。那颗星亮了起来，好像它一直很寂寞。|你的光和星的光合在一起，足够看书了。你一直读到天亮。|你把自己的光分了一些给那颗老星。它烧得比很久以来都亮，你感觉到那份光从你身上流走了。}\n\n@event astro_two_futures\n# en: Both Futures\ntitle [c2e1aa] = 两种未来\n# en: {Advanced Foresight. The lecturer balances a coin on its edge. Read both futures at once, the one where it falls heads and the one where it falls tails, and hold them both.|The coin on its edge again. You know how it feels to be two places at once now.|The coin. People come out of this lesson pressing their temples.} {Someone in the front row says \"heads\" in two voices.|The lecturer is drinking tea and looking at nobody.|The candles burn straight up, very still.}\ntext [8cb25c] = {高等预见课。讲师把一枚硬币立在边缘上。同时读出两种未来，正面落地的那个和反面落地的那个，并把两者都握住。|又是立着的硬币。如今你知道同时身处两地是什么感觉了。|硬币。走出这节课的人都按着太阳穴。} {前排有人用两个声音说“正面”。|讲师在喝茶，谁也不看。|烛火笔直向上，一动不动。}\n# en: A coin balanced on edge on a dark table in the observatory, with two faint ghostly outcomes hovering above it.\nscene [137628] = 观星台一张深色桌上，一枚硬币立在边缘，上方悬浮着两个淡淡的、幽灵似的结果。\n# en: Hold both to the end\nopt.left.label [acd6d2] = 两者握到最后\n# en: {For a long moment you live both futures, side by side, and neither is frightening. Then the coin falls, and one of them goes out.|You hold both until the lecturer flicks the coin. One future folds away, neat as a letter.|You hold both in the still candlelight until the lecturer stops the coin with a finger. Both futures are still there. \"Interesting,\" the lecturer says.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7dbfc6] = {有那么长长的一刻，你并肩活在两种未来里，哪一种都不可怕。然后硬币倒下，其中一种熄灭了。|你一直握着两者，直到讲师弹倒硬币。一种未来折了起来，整齐得像一封信。|你在一动不动的烛光里握住两者，直到讲师用一根手指按住硬币。两种未来都还在。“有意思，”讲师说。}\n# en: {The futures tangle and you get a headache that lasts till supper. For an hour you keep reaching for the wrong door handle.|You lose track of which future is which, and of which way is down. The lecturer sits you by an open window.|You hold both too long. For the rest of the day you hear things a moment before they happen, and you hate it.}\nopt.left.say.2 [91f883] = {两种未来缠在一起，你头疼到晚饭。之后一个钟头你总是去拧错的门把手。|你分不清哪个未来是哪个了，也分不清哪边是下。讲师让你坐到开着的窗边。|你握得太久了。这一整天你都比别人早一刻听到事情发生，你讨厌这种感觉。}\n# en: Read one, then the other\nopt.right.label [e61653] = 先读一个，再读另一个\n# en: {You read heads, then tails, carefully, one after the other. It's not the exercise, but you get both right.|One future at a time. Slower, safer, and the lecturer says you'll get there.|One future, then the other, and you know both now before you read them. The lecturer says that's the start of holding them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [95f2f8] = {你先读正面，再读反面，一个接一个，小心翼翼。这不是练习的要求，但两个你都读对了。|一次一个未来。更慢，更稳，讲师说你会做到的。|一个未来，然后是另一个，现在你还没读就知道两个了。讲师说这是握住它们的开始。}\n\n@event alch_unlabelled_vial\n# en: The Unlabelled Vial\ntitle [ec7cd4] = 没有标签的药瓶\n# en: {Alchemy II hands out an unlabelled vial each, and an hour to say what's in it. Paper strips, a candle, a nose and a notebook are allowed.|Another unlabelled vial. The lecturer enjoys these.|The vial is waiting on your bench. You've started recognising the corks.} {Yours is perfectly clear and smells faintly of pears.|Yours is cloudy and keeps settling into layers.|Yours is warm to the touch.} {The ventilation charm is wheezing again.|The lab is quiet except for scratching quills.|The lecturer is humming, which means the vials are harder this week.}\ntext [8be139] = {炼金 II 发给每人一只没有标签的药瓶，一个钟头内说出里面是什么。试纸、蜡烛、鼻子和笔记本都可以用。|又是没标签的药瓶。讲师很享受这个。|药瓶在你桌上等着。你已经开始认得那些软木塞了。} {你的这瓶清澈透明，隐约有股梨香。|你的这瓶浑浊，总是沉淀分层。|你的这瓶摸上去是温的。} {通风咒又在喘了。|实验室里很安静，只有羽毛笔沙沙作响。|讲师在哼歌，这说明这周的瓶子更难了。}\n# en: A vial of clear liquid on a lab bench beside a strip of paper turning slowly purple, a student squinting at both.\nscene [3b7b2e] = 实验台上放着一瓶透明液体，旁边一条试纸正慢慢变紫，一名学生眯着眼看着两者。\n# en: Test it the long way\nopt.left.label [2f891a] = 老老实实逐项测\n# en: {Strip, flame, cold, filter. By the end of the hour you've a name and three reasons for it.|Every test, in order, written up neatly. Your notes are dull and your answer is right.|The warm one takes the whole hour, test by test. It's something you've never met, and you name it anyway, correctly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b95931] = {试纸、火焰、冷却、过滤。一个钟头结束时，你有了一个名字和三条理由。|每一项测试，按部就班，写得工工整整。你的笔记很无聊，答案是对的。|那瓶温热的花了整整一个钟头，一项一项地测。是你从没遇到过的东西，但你还是叫出了它的名字，而且叫对了。}\n# en: Trust your nose\nopt.right.label [c495ff] = 相信你的鼻子\n# en: {One sniff and you know it. You write the name and use the spare hour to check yourself.|You tip the vial under your nose and the answer arrives before the smell does. The lecturer raises an eyebrow and a tick.|It's warm, and that tells you most of it. You sniff to confirm. The lecturer stops humming.}\nopt.right.say.1 [73827a] = {一闻你就知道了。你写下名字，用多出来的一个钟头检查自己。|你把瓶子凑到鼻子下，答案比气味先到。讲师挑了挑眉，打了个勾。|它是温的，这就说明了大半。你闻了一下确认。讲师不哼歌了。}\n# en: {One sniff and your eyes stream for the rest of the morning. You guessed wrong, too.|It's not what you thought, and it is very much up your nose. You spend the hour at the window.|You sniff the warm one. It sniffs back, in a way, and you spend the hour with a damp cloth on your face.}\nopt.right.say.2 [82aa3d] = {一闻之下，你整个上午都在流眼泪。而且你还猜错了。|它不是你以为的东西，而且结结实实冲进了你的鼻子。这一节课你都待在窗边。|你闻了闻那瓶温热的。某种意义上它也闻了闻你，这一节课你都捂着一块湿布。}\n\n@event alch_cracked_tray\n# en: The Cracked Tray\ntitle [a94d69] = 一盘裂物\n# en: {Mend at Alchemy II. Sister Amaranth sets out a tray of cracked things, cup, mirror and bone. \"Mend those,\" she says, \"and then we'll see about people.\"|The tray again. Sister Amaranth has found new things to crack.|Sister Amaranth puts the tray down and nods at it. You know the drill.} {A second-year is waiting on a cot with a sprained wrist from broom practice.|A third-year with a scalded hand from Evocation is waiting.|A first-year is in with a split lip and a long story about a door.}\ntext [7de022] = {炼金 II 的愈合课。阿玛兰斯修女摆出一盘裂开的东西：杯子、镜子、骨头。“先把这些补好，”她说，“然后再说人。”|又是这盘东西。阿玛兰斯修女找到了新的可以弄裂的东西。|阿玛兰斯修女放下托盘，朝它点点头。你知道该怎么做。} {一个二年级生坐在病床上等着，手腕是练飞帚时扭的。|一个在塑能课上烫伤手的三年级生在等着。|一个一年级生嘴唇裂了，正讲着一个关于门的长故事。}\n# en: A tray in the infirmary laid with a cracked teacup, a cracked hand mirror and a cracked bone from a teaching skeleton, a student's hands hovering over them.\nscene [a85abf] = 医务室里一只托盘，放着一只裂开的茶杯、一面裂开的手镜和一根教学骨架上裂开的骨头，一名学生的双手悬在它们上方。\n# en: Mend the tray, perfectly\nopt.left.label [9eeb5c] = 把托盘补得完美\n# en: {The cracks close like seams being sewn shut. Sister Amaranth runs a thumbnail along the cup and finds nothing.|Cup, mirror, bone, whole. Sister Amaranth says \"Fine,\" which is her highest word.|You mend the tray in half the time and Sister Amaranth sends you to the first-year with the split lip. It mends cleanly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [846031] = {裂缝像被缝上的接缝一样合拢。阿玛兰斯修女用拇指甲沿杯沿划过去，什么也没摸到。|杯子、镜子、骨头，全都完好。阿玛兰斯修女说“还行”，这是她的最高评价。|你只用一半的时间就补好了托盘，阿玛兰斯修女派你去给那个嘴唇裂开的一年级生看。缝得很干净。}\n# en: {The cup mends with a ridge in it, the mirror with a ripple. Sister Amaranth sets them on the shelf of shame.|You mend the bone a little crooked. Sister Amaranth says that's how you learn and puts it back in the skeleton anyway.|The mirror mends with your face in it, slightly wrong. Sister Amaranth turns it to the wall.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0cb5fe] = {杯子补好了，却留了一道棱；镜子补好了，却起了一道波纹。阿玛兰斯修女把它们放上了耻辱架。|你把骨头补得有点歪。阿玛兰斯修女说这就是学习，还是把它装回了骨架。|镜子补好了，里面却留着你的脸，还有点走样。阿玛兰斯修女把它翻过去对着墙。}\n# en: Mend the patient instead\nopt.right.label [c58841] = 改去治病人\n# en: {You skip the tray and take the patient, with Sister Amaranth's hand on your shoulder. It goes slowly. It works.|Sister Amaranth lets you try on the patient while she watches. You're shaky and slow, and you'll never mend a cup the same way again.|You mend the first-year's lip and hear the whole story about the door. It wasn't a door. You keep that to yourself.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c520ad] = {你跳过托盘，接手病人，阿玛兰斯修女的手搭在你肩上。进展很慢，但成了。|阿玛兰斯修女看着你在病人身上试。你手抖，动作慢，而且从此再也不会用原来的方式补杯子了。|你给那个一年级生缝好了嘴唇，听了关于那扇门的全部故事。根本不是门。你没说出去。}\n\n@event alch_lake_fever\n# en: Lake Fever Season\ntitle [5e2ca1] = 湖热季\n# en: {Advanced Alchemy treats real patients. The infirmary has Lake Fever cases, from swimming where they shouldn't. Sister Amaranth wants the fever drawn out, not just cooled.|Lake Fever again. Students never learn, Sister Amaranth says, and neither do lakes.|Fever duty. Sister Amaranth hands you a list without looking up.} {Your patient says they only went in up to the knees.|Your patient is a prefect, very embarrassed.|Your patient insists they were pushed.}\ntext [d87cd6] = {高等炼金要治真正的病人。医务室里有湖热病例，都是在不该游泳的地方游了泳。阿玛兰斯修女要你把热病拔出来，而不只是降温。|又是湖热。修女说，学生从不长记性，湖也不长。|退热值班。阿玛兰斯修女头也不抬地递给你一张名单。} {你的病人说他们只下水到膝盖。|你的病人是一位级长，非常难为情。|你的病人坚称是被人推下去的。}\n# en: A row of infirmary cots, students under blankets looking flushed and sorry for themselves, an advanced student drawing a faint grey haze out of one patient's brow.\nscene [4db536] = 一排医务室病床，学生们裹着毯子，满脸通红，可怜兮兮；一名高年级生正从一位病人额头上抽出一缕淡淡的灰雾。\n# en: Draw the fever out by hand\nopt.left.label [cf9f68] = 亲手把热拔出来\n# en: {The fever comes out like a grey thread pulled from wool. Your patient sleeps. You'd like to.|You draw it out slowly and the flush leaves their face. Sister Amaranth checks your work and doesn't check twice.|You draw out the fever and the story with it. They were pushed. Sister Amaranth writes down the name.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5d5e1c] = {热病像从羊毛里抽出的一根灰线，被你拉了出来。病人睡着了。你也很想睡。|你慢慢把它抽出来，潮红从他们脸上退去。阿玛兰斯修女检查了你的活儿，没有检查第二遍。|你把热病连同那个故事一起拔了出来。对方确实是被推下去的。阿玛兰斯修女记下了那个名字。}\n# en: {You draw out half of it and the half you drew comes with you. You spend the evening shivering in the next cot.|The cure has to come from somewhere. You wake up two hours later on the floor, with a blanket on you.|The fever comes out angry and goes into you. You spend the night in the next cot, insisting you weren't pushed.}\nopt.left.say.2 [520af0] = {你只拔出了一半，而拔出来的那一半跟上了你。整个晚上你都在隔壁病床上发抖。|治愈总得从某处来。两个钟头后你在地板上醒来，身上盖着一条毯子。|热病带着怒气出来，钻进了你身上。你在隔壁病床上过了一夜，一个劲儿地说自己不是被推下去的。}\n# en: Brew the slow tonic\nopt.right.label [af7952] = 熬慢效补剂\n# en: {Three hours of stirring willow bark and lake moss. The tonic works by morning, which is slower and safer.|You brew the old remedy by the book. Sister Amaranth tastes it, grimaces, and approves.|You brew the tonic without the book now. Sister Amaranth tastes it and doesn't grimace, which worries you more.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ed12ab] = {三个钟头搅着柳树皮和湖苔。补剂到早上才见效，慢一些，也稳一些。|你照书熬了老方子。阿玛兰斯修女尝了一口，皱了皱脸，点头认可。|现在你不看书也能熬这补剂了。阿玛兰斯修女尝了一口，没皱脸，这反倒让你更担心了。}\n# en: Distill the fever out in one pass\nopt.up.label [cc24bd] = 一次把热病萃出来\n# en: {You distill the fever into a single grey drop and stopper it. The drop takes some of your strength with it, and leaves you yawning.|One pass of Distill and the patient sits up asking for soup. The tiredness lands on you an hour later.|You distill it out in one pass, and your patient sits up and says who pushed them. You're asleep before Sister Amaranth asks.}\nopt.up.say.1 [4ba2e5] = {你把热病萃成一滴灰色的液体，塞上了瓶塞。那一滴带走了你一些力气，你直打哈欠。|一遍萃取，病人就坐起来要汤喝。一个钟头后，疲倦落到了你身上。|你一遍就萃了出来，病人坐起来说出了是谁推的。阿玛兰斯修女还没来得及问，你已经睡着了。}\n\n@event alch_third_floor_still\n# en: The Still Cracks\ntitle [7ecae5] = 蒸馏器裂了\n# en: {Advanced Alchemy distils essences that would rather be gas. The ventilation charm gave up on the third floor years ago. Today your still cracks.|Distilling day, and a hiss from your still that you know.|Your still has cracked again. You're getting fast at noticing.} {The vapour is green and smells of mint.|The vapour is pale and smells of nothing, which is worse.|The vapour is curling toward the next bench.}\ntext [fbd2ac] = {高等炼金要蒸馏那些宁愿当气体的精华。三楼的通风咒多年前就放弃了。今天你的蒸馏器裂了。|蒸馏日，你的蒸馏器又发出那种你熟悉的嘶嘶声。|你的蒸馏器又裂了。你发现得越来越快了。} {蒸汽是绿的，有薄荷味。|蒸汽颜色很淡，什么味道都没有，这更糟。|蒸汽正卷向隔壁的实验台。}\n# en: A copper still on a long bench, hissing from a fresh crack, a thin green vapour creeping across the laboratory floor.\nscene [e5be1b] = 长实验台上一只铜蒸馏器从新裂口里嘶嘶作响，一缕淡绿色的蒸汽正爬过实验室的地板。\n# en: Mend the crack, still hot\nopt.left.label [127d5d] = 趁热把裂缝补上\n# en: {You close the copper under your palms while it's still singing. The hiss stops. The class breathes out.|The crack seals with a sound like a sigh, and the batch is saved. The lecturer opens a window, for form's sake.|You seal it before the vapour reaches the next bench. Its owner never knows how close it came.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a9fb6d] = {铜还在嗡鸣，你就用掌心把它合上了。嘶声停了，全班松了口气。|裂缝合拢，发出一声叹息般的轻响，这一批保住了。讲师还是开了扇窗，走个形式。|你在蒸汽飘到隔壁台子之前封好了它。台子的主人永远不知道刚才有多悬。}\n# en: {The crack closes, then reopens with a pop. You breathe in a mouthful and spend the afternoon seeing double.|You burn your fingertips on the copper and the vapour gets out anyway. The lab is cleared for an hour.|The vapour reaches the next bench before you do. Two of you spend the afternoon seeing double.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a9cec7] = {裂缝合上了，又“噗”地一声裂开。你吸进一大口，整个下午看东西都是重影。|你的指尖被铜烫伤，蒸汽还是跑了出来。实验室清场一个钟头。|蒸汽比你先到了隔壁的台子。你们两个整个下午看东西都是重影。}\n# en: Shout, and clear the bench\nopt.right.label [17c853] = 大喊一声，清空台面\n# en: {You call the warning and clear the bench. The batch is lost, nobody is hurt, and you start again next week.|Everyone is out in the corridor in a minute flat. The lecturer goes back in, a scarf over the mouth, and comes out carrying your ruined batch.|You clear the bench and the one next to it. The lecturer notes that you thought of the neighbours, and the batch is still lost.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ac5419] = {你喊出警告，清空了台面。这一批毁了，没人受伤，你下周重来。|一分钟内所有人都到了走廊上。讲师用围巾捂着口鼻回去，把你那批报废的东西端了出来。|你清空了自己的台子，也清空了隔壁的。讲师注意到你考虑到了邻座，可这一批还是毁了。}\n\n@event illu_walking_double\n# en: The Walking Double\ntitle [188b9c] = 会走的分身\n# en: {Mirror at Illusion II. Make a double of yourself and walk it across the room. \"Standing doubles are furniture,\" says Professor Vey. \"Make it walk.\"|Doubles day. Vey watches feet, not faces.|Your double's walk is improving. It has your slouch now.} {The floor has been dusted with chalk, to show footprints.|Someone's double is walking backwards.|Vey is sitting in the front row, which is unsettling.}\ntext [6e95b9] = {幻术 II 的镜像课。给自己造一个分身，让它走过教室。“站着的分身是家具，”维伊教授说，“让它走。”|分身日。维伊看脚，不看脸。|你的分身走得越来越好了。它现在连你的驼背都学会了。} {地上撒了粉笔灰，好显出脚印。|有人的分身在倒着走。|维伊坐在第一排，这让人心里发毛。}\n# en: Two identical students walking side by side across a lecture hall, one of them leaving no footprints in the chalk dust.\nscene [45f81a] = 两个一模一样的学生并肩走过讲堂，其中一个在粉笔灰上没留下脚印。\n# en: Walk it right across\nopt.left.label [8d74d0] = 让它走完全程\n# en: {Your double crosses the room at your pace and even scuffs the chalk. Vey watches its feet the whole way and says nothing, which is high praise.|It walks, it turns, it nearly sits down. Vey says, \"Better.\"|Your double slouches across the room, Vey in the front row watching every step. \"That's you,\" Vey says. It isn't a compliment, quite.}\nopt.left.say.1 [836f34] = {你的分身按你的步子穿过教室，甚至蹭花了粉笔灰。维伊一路盯着它的脚，什么也没说，这是很高的赞扬。|它走，它转身，它差点坐下。维伊说：“好些了。”|你的分身弓着背穿过教室，维伊坐在第一排盯着每一步。“那就是你，”维伊说。这不全是夸奖。}\n# en: {Halfway across, your double's legs forget to move and it glides. Vey says it's a ghost, not a person, and moves on.|Your double walks into the wall and keeps walking. The class watches it vanish into the plaster.|Your double sees Vey in the front row and freezes, exactly as you would. It's very lifelike, and it doesn't walk.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f9609c] = {走到一半，分身的腿忘了动，开始飘。维伊说那是鬼，不是人，然后走开了。|你的分身撞上墙，还在继续走。全班看着它消失在灰泥里。|你的分身看见维伊坐在第一排就僵住了，跟你一模一样。非常逼真，可它就是不走。}\n# en: Keep it still and perfect\nopt.right.label [7cb3fb] = 让它站着，完美无缺\n# en: {Your double stands at your elbow, perfect to the last button. It doesn't move. Vey calls it a very good wardrobe.|You make it flawless and keep it standing. Vey taps its nose, and frowns when it holds.|Your double stands perfectly still in front of Vey, slouch and all. Vey reaches out and adjusts its collar, which holds.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c74e86] = {你的分身站在你手肘边，连最后一颗纽扣都完美。它不动。维伊称它是一件很好的衣橱。|你把它做得天衣无缝，然后让它站着。维伊敲了敲它的鼻子，发现敲得到，皱起了眉。|你的分身一动不动地站在维伊面前，连驼背都一样。维伊伸手理了理它的领子，领子还真在。}\n\n@event illu_good_news\n# en: Good News, Read Aloud\ntitle [0d109c] = 当众念好消息\n# en: {Glamour at Illusion II, and today it's the voice. Vey hands you a dull notice and asks you to read it so the class hears good news.|Another notice from Vey's drawer of very dull notices.|Vey has saved the dullest notice for you.} {Today's is about the drains.|Today's is about a change to the laundry rota.|Today's is a list of overdue library books, in full.}\ntext [f297c7] = {幻术 II 的魅影课，今天练的是声音。维伊递给你一份枯燥的通知，要你念得让全班听成好消息。|又是一份来自维伊那抽屉超级枯燥通知的通知。|维伊把最枯燥的那份留给了你。} {今天这份讲的是排水沟。|今天这份讲的是洗衣轮值表的调整。|今天这份是图书馆逾期书目，全文。}\n# en: A student standing at a lectern reading from a dull notice while the listening class smiles as though hearing wonderful news.\nscene [74b6a2] = 一名学生站在讲台前念着一份枯燥的通知，台下的同学们却笑容满面，像在听天大的好消息。\n# en: Glamour every word\nopt.left.label [3eefc7] = 字字施魅影\n# en: {By the second line the room is beaming. Someone claps. Vey waits for the clapping to stop and asks what the notice said. Nobody knows.|You read it warm and low, and the class leans in as if you were reading their letters from home.|You read out the overdue books like a list of prize-winners. Three students go to the library that afternoon.}\nopt.left.say.1 [af6971] = {念到第二行，满屋子都笑开了。有人鼓掌。维伊等掌声停了，问通知说了什么。没人知道。|你念得温暖而低沉，全班都探过身来，好像你在念他们的家书。|你把逾期的书单念得像获奖名单。那天下午有三个学生去了图书馆。}\n# en: {Your glamour slips on a long word and the room hears exactly what it is, a notice. Someone yawns on purpose.|You overdo it. The class looks alarmed, as if the notice were about to propose.|The glamour holds for twenty titles and drops on the twenty-first. The class hears the rest exactly as they are.}\nopt.left.say.2 [4f5ba7] = {魅影在一个长词上滑脱，全屋听到的正是它本来的样子：一份通知。有人故意打了个哈欠。|你用力过猛。全班一脸惊恐，好像这份通知要求婚了。|魅影撑过了二十个书名，在第二十一个上掉了下来。剩下的全班听到的都是原样。}\n# en: Read it plain, watch others\nopt.right.label [c74a90] = 照念，看别人\n# en: {You read it straight and sit down. Then you watch the others, and notice the best of them barely change their voices at all.|Plain reading, polite applause. You spend the hour writing down where the good ones breathe.|You read the list plain. It takes a long time. You learn more watching Vey's face than you would have by trying.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7cc167] = {你照原样念完坐下。然后你看别人念，发现最好的那几个几乎没怎么改变嗓音。|平平地念，礼貌的掌声。这一节课你都在记高手们在哪里换气。|你平平地念完那张清单。念了很久。看维伊的表情，你学到的比自己试还多。}\n# en: Glamour the notice itself\nopt.up.label [286fbe] = 给告示本身施魅影\n# en: {You don't touch your voice; you glamour the paper. Every student who reads it over your shoulder sees good news in their own hand. Vey applauds, twice, slowly.|You glamour the ink so it shimmers like a wedding invitation. The class fights over the notice afterwards. It is still, underneath, a very dull notice.|You glamour the list into an honours roll. Half the class goes to look for their names, and finds their overdue books.}\nopt.up.say.1 [577a68] = {你不动嗓子，而是给纸施魅影。每个从你肩后看过去的学生，都用自己的笔迹读到了好消息。维伊鼓了两下掌，很慢。|你给墨水施魅影，让它像婚礼请柬一样闪闪发光。课后全班抢那张告示。说到底，它还是一份非常无聊的通知。|你给那张清单施魅影，让它变成一份荣誉榜。半个班都跑去找自己的名字，结果找到了自己的逾期书。}\n\n@event illu_false_room\n# en: The Room That Isn't\ntitle [04316d] = 不存在的房间\n# en: {Advanced Illusion asks for a double of a whole room. Hang it over the real one and walk a classmate through it. Vey's rule is simple. If anyone walks into a wall, it's your fault.|The room exercise. Somebody always walks into something.|You hang your room. You've learned where the real walls are.} {Vey's volunteer today is a porter with a tray of cups.|Vey's volunteer today is Vey.|The real room has been rearranged overnight.}\ntext [307d9f] = {高等幻术要你造出一整个房间的分身，罩在真房间上，再让一个同学从中走过。维伊的规矩很简单。谁撞了墙，都算你的错。|房间练习。总有人会撞上点什么。|你把房间挂上去。你已经学会记住真墙在哪儿。} {维伊今天的志愿者是一位端着一托盘杯子的门房。|维伊今天的志愿者是维伊。|真房间昨夜被重新布置过了。}\n# en: A student walking confidently into a room that is partly painted light, a real stone wall standing where the illusion shows an open doorway.\nscene [487838] = 一名学生自信地走进一个部分由光绘成的房间，幻象里敞开的门洞处，其实立着一堵真正的石墙。\n# en: Hang the whole room\nopt.left.label [f08ac4] = 挂上整个房间\n# en: {Your walker crosses the false room and sits down in a false chair, which you hold up for them. Vey writes for a long time.|The room holds. Nobody touches a real wall. Vey walks it twice to be sure.|You hang your room over the rearranged one and nobody notices which is which, including, briefly, Vey.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3df0e0] = {走的人穿过假房间，坐上一把假椅子，你替他们撑住了它。维伊写了很久。|房间撑住了。没人碰到一堵真墙。维伊走了两遍才确认。|你把你的房间挂在重新布置过的那间上，没人分得清哪个是哪个，有那么一会儿，连维伊也分不清。}\n# en: {Your walker steps through a door you painted onto solid stone. You catch them as they fall, take the edge of the stone on your own elbow, and walk them to Sister Amaranth with an apology.|Halfway across the room flickers, and the walker freezes. So do you, and you walk hip-first into a real chair.|You hang your room over where the walls were yesterday. The walker finds today's walls with their nose.}\nopt.left.say.2 [1e6334] = {走的人迈进了你画在实心石头上的门。你在他们倒下时接住了，自己的手肘磕上了石棱，然后带着歉意把他们送到阿玛兰斯修女那里。|走到一半房间闪了一下，走的人僵住了。你也僵住了，胯骨一头撞上了一把真椅子。|你把房间挂在了昨天墙所在的地方。走的人用鼻子找到了今天的墙。}\n# en: Chalk the real walls first\nopt.right.label [26c22a] = 先用粉笔标出真墙\n# en: {You hang half a room, with the real walls marked out in chalk so nobody could miss them. Vey calls it \"a room with a safety rope\".|Safe and visible, chalk lines everywhere. Vey sighs, and then copies down how you did the window.|You chalk the new walls first, all of them. Vey says that's the only correct answer to a rearranged room.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d4e5f8] = {你只挂了半个房间，真墙都用粉笔标了出来，谁也不会错过。维伊称之为“系了安全绳的房间”。|安全，显眼，到处是粉笔线。维伊叹了口气，然后抄下了你做窗户的方法。|你先把新墙都用粉笔标了出来，一面不落。维伊说，对一间重新布置过的房间，这是唯一正确的答案。}\n\n@event illu_peeling_layers\n# en: Seeing Through\ntitle [014ea1] = 看穿\n# en: {Advanced Illusion. To make illusions you must see through them. Vey layers the hall in false rooms, one on another, and you peel them back one at a time.|Layer day. The hall looks normal. That's never a good sign.|You count the layers when you come in. You're usually wrong.} {The top layer has velvet curtains.|The top layer is a summer garden.|The top layer is the hall itself, just slightly too tidy.}\ntext [fa4567] = {高等幻术。要造幻象，就得先学会看穿幻象。维伊把讲堂层层罩上假房间，一层叠一层，你要一层一层揭开。|分层日。讲堂看起来很正常。这从来不是好兆头。|你一进门就数层数。你通常数错。} {最上面一层挂着天鹅绒帘子。|最上面一层是夏日花园。|最上面一层就是讲堂本身，只是整洁得有点过分。}\n# en: A lecture hall in layers like peeled paper: one layer with velvet curtains, one bare stone, one darker one beneath, and a student standing at its centre.\nscene [ee496c] = 一间像剥开的纸一样分层的讲堂：一层挂着天鹅绒帘子，一层是裸露的石头，下面还有更暗的一层，一名学生站在中央。\n# en: Peel down to the stone\nopt.left.label [12cf64] = 一直揭到石头\n# en: {Seven layers, then bare stone. Vey asks how many. You say seven. She smiles and says eight, and points at herself.|You reach the stone and stay there, sure of it. Vey nods slowly. \"Hold on to that,\" she says.|The too-tidy hall comes off last, and under it is the real hall, untidy and wonderful. Vey says you're the first today.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3ff14c] = {七层，然后是裸石。维伊问几层。你说七层。她笑着说八层，指了指自己。|你揭到石头，并且停在那里，确信无疑。维伊慢慢点头。“抓住这个感觉，”她说。|太整洁的讲堂最后被揭下，底下是真正的讲堂，乱糟糟的，美极了。维伊说你是今天第一个。}\n# en: {You peel one too many and the stone underneath looks false. For the rest of the day you knock on walls, with a headache behind your eyes.|After the fourth layer you stop being sure which way is up. Vey walks you to a chair, which is real.|You peel the tidy hall and find another tidy hall. And another. Vey stops you at the sixth, gently.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a76a84] = {你多揭了一层，底下的石头看起来也像假的。这一整天你都在敲墙，眼睛后面隐隐作痛。|揭到第四层后，你再也不确定哪边是上。维伊把你领到一把椅子前，那把椅子是真的。|你揭开整洁的讲堂，下面还是一个整洁的讲堂。又一个。维伊在第六层时轻轻叫停了你。}\n# en: Stop at the third layer\nopt.right.label [b16cdd] = 揭到第三层就停\n# en: {Three layers and you stop, dizzy. Vey says knowing when to stop is the other half of the skill.|You get through three and sit down. The hall still looks too tidy, and you decide not to ask.|You stop at three, and you're right to: the third was the real one. Vey says so, with some surprise.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9d3b88] = {三层，你停下来，头晕目眩。维伊说，知道何时停下是这门技艺的另一半。|你揭了三层就坐下了。讲堂看起来还是太整洁，你决定不问。|你在第三层停下了，停得没错：第三层就是真的。维伊这么说了，有点意外。}\n\n@event beast_hedge_run\n# en: The Hedge Run\ntitle [d8e2dc] = 越篱飞行\n# en: {Beastcraft II puts haste on a broom, low over the hedges, as fast as you dare. Professor Brask says brooms are harder to reason with than hippogriffs, and harder to apologise to.|The hedge run. Your broom remembers last time.|Hedges. You've learned to read a broom's sulk through the handle.} {Brask is timing with a crooked hourglass.|The hedges have been trimmed, unevenly.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves come up behind every broom.|{season == \"spring\": The hedges are full of nesting birds who object.|The ground is hard and unforgiving.}}}\ntext [7b76b5] = {驭兽学 II 把疾速用在飞帚上，贴着树篱低飞，能多快就多快。布拉斯克教授说，飞帚比鹰头马身有翼兽更难讲道理，也更难道歉。|越篱飞行。你的飞帚记得上一次。|树篱。你已经学会从帚柄上读出飞帚的闷气。} {布拉斯克拿着一只歪沙漏计时。|树篱修剪过了，修得高低不平。|{season == \"autumn\": 每把飞帚身后都扬起落叶。|{season == \"spring\": 树篱里满是筑巢的鸟，它们很有意见。|地面又硬又不饶人。}}}\n# en: Students on brooms skimming low over a line of hedges in a field, one broom bucking as its rider leans forward.\nscene [a6f163] = 学生们骑着飞帚贴着田里的一排树篱掠过，其中一把飞帚在骑手前倾时尥起了蹶子。\n# en: Haste at the last hedge\nopt.left.label [73bbd3] = 最后一道篱前加速\n# en: {You borrow a hawk's speed for the last hedge and clear it by a hand. Brask stops the hourglass and nods.|The broom flinches, then commits. You come in fastest of the morning, with hedge in your hair.|You feel the broom's sulk through the handle and haste it anyway, kindly. It clears the last hedge and forgives you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8af82a] = {你借来鹰的速度飞越最后一道树篱，高出一掌。布拉斯克停了沙漏，点了点头。|飞帚一缩，然后豁出去了。你是这个早上最快的，头发里还挂着树篱。|你从帚柄上感觉到它在赌气，还是温和地给它加了疾速。它越过最后一道树篱，原谅了你。}\n# en: {The broom bucks at the haste and you go over the hedge without it.|You're fast. The broom is faster, briefly, and then upside down. You land in a bramble.|The broom has been sulking all morning and saves it for the last hedge. You go over; it stays.}\nopt.left.say.2 [96ca99] = {飞帚被疾速一惊，你独自越过了树篱，它没跟上。|你很快。飞帚短暂地比你更快，然后翻了个底朝天。你落进了荆棘丛。|飞帚赌了一上午的气，全留到了最后一道树篱。你过去了，它留下了。}\n# en: Fly it clean and steady\nopt.right.label [caf423] = 飞得干净平稳\n# en: {No haste, just a clean low line over every hedge. Brask says it's the best boring flying she's seen all week.|You fly it slow and tidy. The broom approves. Brask is less impressed.|You fly it steady and let it sulk. Brask says the broom will remember you were patient, and so will she.}\nopt.right.say.1 [27f320] = {不用疾速，每道树篱都是一条干净的低线。布拉斯克说这是她这周见过最好的无聊飞行。|你飞得又慢又整齐。飞帚很满意。布拉斯克没那么满意。|你飞得稳稳的，由它赌气。布拉斯克说飞帚会记得你有耐心，她也会。}\n\n@event beast_owl_post\n# en: Teaching the Owls\ntitle [ba75dc] = 教猫头鹰送信\n# en: {Beast-speech at Beastcraft II. The young owls must learn the post routes. Brask hands you one and says, \"Explain the castle to it.\"|A new young owl, and the same castle to explain.|Another owl. You've got a speech now, and a map scratched on a beam.} {Your owl wants to know why there are so many towers.|Your owl has strong opinions about the library.|Your owl keeps falling asleep, standing up.}\ntext [fabd5c] = {驭兽学 II 的兽语课。小猫头鹰们得学会送信的路线。布拉斯克递给你一只，说：“把城堡给它讲清楚。”|又一只小猫头鹰，又是同一座城堡要讲。|又一只猫头鹰。你如今有一套讲稿，还在梁上刻了张地图。} {你的猫头鹰想知道为什么塔有这么多座。|你的猫头鹰对图书馆有很强烈的看法。|你的猫头鹰总是站着就睡着了。}\n# en: A student in a stable loft with a young owl on their arm, pointing out of a high window toward a distant tower.\nscene [3d64bf] = 马厩阁楼里，一名学生手臂上停着一只小猫头鹰，正指着高窗外远处的一座塔。\n# en: Explain it in owl\nopt.left.label [27c58e] = 用猫头鹰的话讲\n# en: {You describe the castle in winds, perches and mice. The owl blinks, and goes and comes back with Brask's letter.|It takes a while to get \"left\" across to a bird. When it does, the owl does the route twice to show off.|You give your speech, map and all. The sleepy owl wakes for the important part and does the route without a mistake.}\nopt.left.say.1 [42dfb9] = {你用风、栖木和老鼠来描述城堡。猫头鹰眨眨眼，飞出去，带回了布拉斯克的信。|要让一只鸟明白“左”可得花点工夫。等它明白了，它把路线飞了两遍来炫耀。|你讲完你那套说辞，连地图也用上了。打瞌睡的猫头鹰在关键处醒了过来，一处没错地飞完了路线。}\n# en: {The owl listens closely, then flies to the kitchens. Every time.|The owl makes you explain \"tower\" seven times and then goes to sleep on your head.|Your owl sleeps through the whole speech, then wakes and flies to the kitchens. You're starting to take it personally.}\nopt.left.say.2 [2c3a4c] = {猫头鹰听得很认真，然后飞去了厨房。每一次都是。|猫头鹰让你把“塔”解释了七遍，然后在你头上睡着了。|你的猫头鹰整篇说辞都在睡觉，醒来就飞去了厨房。你开始觉得这是针对你了。}\n# en: Walk the route with it\nopt.right.label [beabeb] = 带它走一遍路线\n# en: {You walk the whole post route with the owl on your arm, up every stair. Your legs ache and the owl has learned it.|Up and down the castle with an owl on your wrist. People stare. The owl stares back.|You walk the route with the owl asleep on your arm. It learns it anyway, somehow. Brask says they do that.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c28df0] = {你让猫头鹰停在手臂上，把整条送信路线走了一遍，每段楼梯都爬了。你腿疼，它学会了。|胳膊上架着一只猫头鹰，在城堡里上上下下。人们盯着看。猫头鹰盯回去。|你走着那条路线，猫头鹰在你胳膊上睡着了。可它还是学会了。布拉斯克说它们就是这样。}\n\n@event beast_hooves\n# en: Hoof Day\ntitle [dcf707] = 修蹄日\n# en: {Advanced Beastcraft does the work nobody else wants. Today it's hooves. A hippogriff's need trimming each month, and the hippogriff disagrees.|Hoof day. The stall is the same; the hippogriff's opinion is too.|Hooves. You go in with a bucket of apples and a plan.} {Today's hippogriff is grey and patient.|Today's is young and very sure of itself.|Today's is old and wants to tell you about it.}\ntext [61631e] = {高等驭兽学干的是没人想干的活。今天是修蹄。鹰头马身有翼兽的蹄子每月要修一次，而它们不同意。|修蹄日。马棚还是那个马棚，它的意见也还是那个意见。|修蹄。你带着一桶苹果和一个计划进去。} {今天这只是灰色的，很有耐心。|今天这只很年轻，非常自以为是。|今天这只很老，想跟你聊聊。}\n# en: A student crouched beside a hippogriff in a stall, a hoof trimming knife in hand, the hippogriff looking down with one sceptical eye.\nscene [e931b9] = 一名学生蹲在马棚里一只鹰头马身有翼兽旁边，手里拿着修蹄刀，那只兽用一只怀疑的眼睛俯视着。\n# en: Talk it into lifting a foot\nopt.left.label [33c422] = 劝它抬起蹄子\n# en: {It listens to your case and lifts a hoof with enormous dignity. Brask watches from the door and says nothing.|You talk it through every hoof. It sighs at you between each one, like a patient uncle.|The old hippogriff tells you about its youth for every hoof. You listen to all of it, and it lifts each foot when the story ends.}\nopt.left.say.1 [067441] = {它听完你的理由，极有尊严地抬起一只蹄子。布拉斯克在门口看着，什么也没说。|你一只一只蹄子地跟它谈。每修完一只它就冲你叹口气，像个耐心的叔叔。|修每只蹄子时，老鹰头马身有翼兽都要讲一段它年轻时的事。你全听完了，每段故事讲完它就抬起一只脚。}\n# en: {It lets you get all the way to the last hoof before stepping on your foot.|It hears you out, then sits on you. Brask lifts it off with one hand and a word.|The old one talks and talks and doesn't lift a foot. You've been had, and Brask is laughing at the door.}\nopt.left.say.2 [54f491] = {它一直等到你修最后一只蹄子，才踩了你的脚。|它听你说完，然后坐在了你身上。布拉斯克一只手加一句话就把它挪开了。|那只老的说个不停，就是不抬脚。你被耍了，布拉斯克在门口笑。}\n# en: Rope, and Brask holding\nopt.right.label [34c6c0] = 上绳子，请布拉斯克按着\n# en: {The old way, then. A rope, a strong arm, Brask at its head. It's sweaty work and nobody enjoys it, including the hippogriff.|With Brask holding the head and you holding on, you finish every hoof. Your shoulders will remember it.|Apples first, then rope, then Brask. The plan works, and the hippogriff eats the plan.}\nopt.right.say.1 [278161] = {老办法：一根绳，一条有力的胳膊，布拉斯克按着它的头。又累又满身汗，谁都不喜欢，包括那只兽。|布拉斯克按着头，你死死抓着，每只蹄子都修完了。你的肩膀会记住这一天。|先给苹果，再上绳子，再叫布拉斯克。计划成功了，鹰头马身有翼兽把计划也吃了。}\n\n@event beast_night_stable\n# en: Sitting Up With the Sick\ntitle [789593] = 守着病兽\n# en: {Advanced students take turns sitting up in the stables when a creature is ill. At the morning lecture Brask hands you a lantern, a blanket and a list for tonight.|Your name is on Brask's night-watch list again, and the list is shorter.|Night watch again tonight. Brask hands over the lantern without a word; the owls will only mutter.} {This time it's the griffon chick, off its food.|This time it's an old owl with a cough.|This time it's a hippogriff who ate something it shouldn't.} {season == \"winter\": The stable will be cold enough to see your breath.|At least it's warm in the straw.}\ntext [bc241e] = {有生物生病时，高年级生轮流在马厩守夜。早课上，布拉斯克交给你一盏灯笼、一条毯子和今晚的清单。|布拉斯克的守夜名单上又有你，清单短了些。|今晚又守夜。布拉斯克一言不发地把灯笼递给你；猫头鹰们只会嘀咕几声。} {这次是不肯吃东西的狮鹫雏鸟。|这次是一只咳嗽的老猫头鹰。|这次是一只吃了不该吃的东西的鹰头马身有翼兽。} {season == \"winter\": 马厩会冷得能看见自己的呼气。|至少干草堆里很暖和。}\n# en: A student sitting in straw by lantern light, a sick creature curled beside them, the rest of the stables asleep.\nscene [88ac60] = 一名学生借着灯笼光坐在干草里，一只生病的生物蜷在身旁，马厩里其余的一切都睡着了。\n# en: Sit up all night\nopt.left.label [464a6d] = 守它一整夜\n# en: {You don't sleep, and by dawn it's breathing easier. Brask finds you asleep in the straw and leaves you there.|You watch it all night, changing the water and the straw. In the morning it eats, and so do you, enormously.|You sit up with the hippogriff while it groans about its mistake. By dawn it's eating again, and you're asleep against its side.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2d3b38] = {你一夜没睡，天亮时它呼吸顺畅了些。布拉斯克发现你睡在干草里，就让你接着睡。|你看了它一整夜，换水，换干草。早上它吃东西了，你也吃了，吃了很多。|你守着那只为自己吃错东西而呻吟的鹰头马身有翼兽。天亮时它又开始吃东西了，你靠着它的身子睡着了。}\n# en: Ask it where it hurts\nopt.right.label [b5e61b] = 问它哪里疼\n# en: {It tells you, in its own way, exactly where it hurts. You write it on Brask's list and both of you sleep.|It points with its whole body. You bring the right tonic and it drops off before the lantern does.|It tells you exactly what it ate, with some pride. You fetch the purge and it forgives you by morning.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0f5834] = {它用自己的方式准确地告诉了你哪里疼。你写在布拉斯克的清单上，你们俩都睡了。|它用整个身子指给你看。你拿来对的补剂，它比灯笼先睡着。|它带着几分得意，一五一十地告诉你吃了什么。你取来了催吐药，早上它就原谅了你。}\n# en: {It tells you a great deal, about its neighbours, the hay and the moon, and nothing about where it hurts.|It won't talk to you. It talks to the lantern. You stay up anyway, which is the point.|It won't say what it ate, out of embarrassment. You guess, and Brask corrects the guess in the morning.}\nopt.right.say.2 [23dbdb] = {它跟你讲了很多，关于邻居、干草和月亮，就是没讲哪里疼。|它不跟你说话。它跟灯笼说话。你还是守着，这才是要点。|出于难为情，它不肯说吃了什么。你猜了一个，第二天早上布拉斯克纠正了你的猜测。}\n# en: Mend what hurts\nopt.up.label [1ff0a7] = 治好疼的地方\n# en: {You find the sore place with your hands and mend it. By midnight it's breathing easier, and so are you.|You mend the cough out of its chest, slowly, a breath at a time. It sleeps before the lantern does, and so do you.|You mend the ache in its belly. It looks at you, ashamed and grateful, and Brask's lecture in the morning is short.}\nopt.up.say.1 [fabd14] = {你用手找到疼的地方，把它治好。到午夜，它呼吸顺畅了，你也是。|你把咳嗽从它胸口慢慢治出来，一口气一口气地。灯还没灭它就睡了，你也是。|你治好了它肚子里的疼。它看着你，又羞愧又感激，布拉斯克第二天早上的训话也短了。}\n\n@event umbral_ward_candle\n# en: Ward the Candle\ntitle [b4ebd5] = 护住蜡烛\n# en: {Ward at Umbral Theory II. Ward a candle while your partner tries to put it out. Today that's {partner}.|Candle wards again, and the lamps are lower. {partner} is across the bench.|The candle exercise. You're paired with {partner}, who rolls up {their} sleeves.} {The professor walks between the benches with a hand over the flames.|The room smells of wax and burnt thread.|Someone's ward has shrunk to the size of a thimble.}\ntext [294dde] = {暗影理论 II 的护界课。给一根蜡烛设下护界，同时你的搭档设法把它弄灭。今天的搭档是{partner}。|又是蜡烛护界，灯压得更低了。{partner}坐在桌子对面。|蜡烛练习。你和{partner}一组，{they}卷起了{their}袖子。} {教授在桌间走动，一只手从火焰上方掠过。|屋里一股蜡和烧焦线头的味道。|有人的护界缩得只有顶针大小。}\n# en: Two students at a dim bench under low lamps, a candle between them inside a faint pale shell, one student trying to blow it out.\nscene [5a828b] = 低垂的灯下，两名学生坐在昏暗的长桌前，中间一根蜡烛罩在一层淡淡的苍白外壳里，其中一人正试图把它吹灭。\n# en: A thick, honest shell\nopt.left.label [53a438] = 一层厚实老实的壳\n# en: {Your ward is plain and solid. {partner} blows and blows and the candle doesn't even lean.|Strong and dull. {partner} gives up and the two of you watch the flame burn down.|Your shell is thick and calm. {partner}'s sleeves are rolled for nothing; the flame doesn't even notice {them}.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d60e26] = {你的护界朴素而结实。{partner}吹了又吹，蜡烛连歪都没歪。|结实而乏味。{partner}放弃了，你们俩一起看着火焰燃尽。|你的护界又厚又沉稳。{partner}白卷了袖子，火苗根本没注意到{them}。}\n# en: A ward that turns them aside\nopt.right.label [6b490b] = 设个把人引开的护界\n# en: {Your ward turns {partner}'s breath round, and it blows {their} own hair into {their} eyes. The professor stops and says, \"Ah.\"|{partner} reaches for your candle and finds {their} hand on the wrong side of the bench. The professor approves.|Your ward turns {partner} aside so gently that {they} try{s} three times before working out what happened.}\nopt.right.say.1 [156f4c] = {你的护界把{partner}的气息拐了个弯，吹得{their}头发糊了一脸。教授停下脚步，说：“啊。”|{partner}伸手去够你的蜡烛，却发现自己的手到了桌子另一边。教授表示赞许。|你的护界把{partner}引开得如此轻柔，{they}试了三次才弄明白是怎么回事。}\n# en: {Your clever ward turns aside the wrong thing, and the candle goes out with a smug little puff. {partner} smirks.|The ward bends {partner}'s breath straight into your face. You sneeze and the candle goes out.|Your ward shrinks to a thimble, like your neighbour's, and {partner} pinches the flame out with two fingers.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1f1f04] = {你那个聪明的护界引开了错的东西，蜡烛得意地“噗”一声灭了。{partner}偷笑。|护界把{partner}的气息直直拐到你脸上。你打了个喷嚏，蜡烛灭了。|你的护界缩成了顶针大小，跟邻座的一样，{partner}两根手指就把火苗捏灭了。}\n# en: A true ward\nopt.up.label [720236] = 真正的护盾\n# en: {You cast the real thing, the spell itself, and the candle sits inside a pale shell that hums. {partner} blows, then laughs, then asks you to show {them} how.|You raise the true ward. The professor stops at your bench and holds a hand over it, and for once says nothing at all.|The true ward comes easily now. {partner} tries everything, a breath, a book, a wet finger, and the flame burns on, untroubled.}\nopt.up.say.1 [851c6a] = {你施出了真正的咒语，蜡烛被罩在一层嗡嗡作响的淡色护壳里。{partner}吹了又吹，然后笑了，然后请你教{them}。|你升起真正的护盾。教授在你桌前停下，把手悬在上面，难得一句话也没说。|真正的护盾现在来得毫不费力。{partner}什么都试了，吹气、书本、沾湿的手指，火苗依旧安然燃烧。}\n\n@event umbral_dark_roll\n# en: Who Moved\ntitle [133ff8] = 谁动了\n# en: {Umbral Theory II. The professor puts out every lamp, and in the dark one student moves. Say who, and where to.|Lamps out. You've learned to hear a sleeve.|The dark. You know the room's sounds, and which floorboard is a liar.} {Rain on the tower roof makes it harder.|It's so quiet you can hear the stair breathing.|Somebody's stomach rumbles, which helps no one.}\ntext [37cfa1] = {暗影理论 II。教授熄掉每一盏灯，黑暗中有一个学生会移动。说出是谁，挪到了哪里。|灯灭了。你已经学会听见一只袖子的声音。|黑暗。你熟悉这间屋子的声音，也知道哪块地板会撒谎。} {雨打在塔顶，让这更难了。|安静得能听见楼梯在呼吸。|有人肚子咕咕叫，这对谁都没帮助。}\n# en: A pitch-dark classroom at the top of a tower, a few vague shapes of students sitting perfectly still in the gloom.\nscene [d2fcaa] = 塔顶一间漆黑的教室，几个模糊的学生身影一动不动地坐在昏暗里。\n# en: Listen, and name them\nopt.left.label [80bc08] = 听，然后说出名字\n# en: {\"Third bench, to the window.\" Right. The professor relights a lamp and moves on.|You get it on the second try, by the creak of a bench and a faint smell of soap.|\"Second bench, toward the door, stepping over the liar.\" The professor laughs in the dark, once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8cf4b3] = {“第三排，往窗边去了。”对了。教授重新点亮一盏灯，继续下一轮。|第二次你猜中了，靠的是一张长凳的吱呀声和一丝淡淡的肥皂味。|“第二排，往门那边去，跨过了那块说谎的地板。”教授在黑暗中笑了一声。}\n# en: Be the one who moves\nopt.right.label [25e0c1] = 做那个移动的人\n# en: {You cross the whole room in the dark and nobody names you. When the lamps come up you're behind the professor.|Nobody hears you. The professor says, \"Someone moved,\" and seems pleased not to know who.|You step over the floorboard that lies and cross the room in silence. Even the professor guesses wrong.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9ff72e] = {你在黑暗中穿过整个房间，没人叫出你的名字。灯亮起时你站在教授身后。|没人听见你。教授说：“有人动了，”似乎很高兴自己不知道是谁。|你跨过那块会说谎的地板，悄无声息地穿过房间。连教授都猜错了。}\n# en: {You catch a desk with your shin. \"Window, limping,\" says everyone at once.|Your shoe squeaks on the second step, you turn your ankle stopping, and the whole class names you, with feeling.|You avoid the liar and find a new one. The whole room names you before you've reached the wall.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a666e1] = {你的小腿撞上了桌子。“窗边，瘸着腿，”所有人异口同声。|你的鞋在第二级台阶上吱了一声，急停时还崴了脚。全班带着感情叫出了你的名字。|你避开了那块说谎的地板，却踩上了一块新的。还没走到墙边，全屋就叫出了你的名字。}\n\n@event umbral_corridor_step\n# en: Across the Corridor\ntitle [d8d8cf] = 穿过走廊\n# en: {Advanced Umbral Theory. Shadowstep across the corridor, door to door, the farthest the professor allows. \"Short distances only,\" the professor says. \"This is as short as long gets.\"|The corridor. The cold in between is colder than last time.|You stand at the door with your heels on the line. You know the cold that's coming.} {The lamps are out on the landing.|A draught is moving along the floor.|Someone came back from it with frost on one shoulder.}\ntext [09146d] = {高等暗影理论。用影步穿过走廊，从这扇门到那扇门，这是教授允许的最远距离。“只能短距离，”教授说，“这已经是短里面最长的了。”|走廊。中间的寒冷比上次更冷。|你脚跟抵着线站在门口。你知道将要袭来的那股寒意。} {楼梯平台上的灯灭了。|一股穿堂风贴着地面流动。|有人回来时一边肩膀上结着霜。}\n# en: A dim stone corridor at the top of a tower, a student half stepping out of the shadow of one doorway while their own shadow lingers in another across the hall.\nscene [e52a16] = 塔顶一条昏暗的石砌走廊，一名学生半个身子正从一扇门的阴影里跨出，而他们自己的影子还逗留在走廊对面的另一扇门里。\n# en: Step across\nopt.left.label [894fac] = 一步跨过去\n# en: {One shadow, cold and long, and then the other. You come out on the far side shaking and grinning.|You step in by one door and out by the other, with a breath of frost on your collar. The professor writes your name, and a time.|You step into the cold you knew was coming and out the far door with nothing on your shoulder. The professor writes a shorter time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [63de51] = {一片影子，又冷又长，然后是另一片。你从对面出来，浑身发抖，咧着嘴笑。|你从一扇门进，从另一扇门出，衣领上带着一口霜气。教授写下你的名字和一个时间。|你踏进早知会来的寒冷，从远处的门出来，肩上什么也没沾。教授写下了一个更短的时间。}\n# en: {The cold in between is longer than you thought. You come out in the right doorway and can't feel your hands for an hour.|You step and come out in the same doorway you went in by, with the feeling that something looked at you on the way.|You come back with frost on one shoulder, like the others. It takes until supper to melt, and it isn't only cold.}\nopt.left.say.2 [78743d] = {中间的寒冷比你想的更长。你从对的门口出来，一个钟头手都没有知觉。|你跨了一步，却从进去的那扇门出来了，总觉得路上有什么东西看了你一眼。|你像别人一样，回来时一边肩膀上结了霜。一直到晚饭才化，而且不只是冷。}\n# en: Walk it, counting shadows\nopt.right.label [de4a9d] = 走过去，数着影子\n# en: {You walk the corridor and count every shadow and every lamp. The professor calls this \"mapping your road\".|You pace it out and write down each shadow's depth. Next time you'll step it. Probably.|You walk it and count the shadows, again. They're the same as last time, which you now realise is the lesson.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e6a1e7] = {你走过走廊，数清每一片影子和每一盏灯。教授称之为“给你的路画地图”。|你一步步量过去，记下每片影子的深浅。下次你会用影步。大概吧。|你又走了一遍，数着影子。它们和上次一样，你这才明白这正是这一课。}\n# en: Step with a held breath\nopt.up.label [1d6b25] = 屏住呼吸踏影\n# en: {You step without thinking about it, the way the spell knows. The cold barely touches you.|The spell takes you over quick and clean. Your fingers are cold for a minute.|You hold your breath and step. No frost, no cold, no time at all. The professor stops writing.}\nopt.up.say.1 [207e6c] = {你不假思索地一步跨过，就像咒语本身知道路。寒意几乎没碰到你。|咒语带着你又快又干净地过去了。你的手指冷了一分钟。|你屏住呼吸，迈步。没有霜，没有冷，几乎没用时间。教授停下了笔。}\n\n@event umbral_unpick_ward\n# en: Unpicking the Box\ntitle [be7a63] = 拆箱上的护界\n# en: {Advanced Umbral Theory. The professor wards a small box and you unpick the ward without it noticing. \"A ward that notices you,\" the professor says, \"tells someone.\"|Box day. The professor has a new box, and a new ward on it.|The box. You've got a favourite needle for this now.} {This ward is fine as a cobweb.|This ward is thick and patient.|This ward hums when you breathe on it.}\ntext [7eb3d9] = {高等暗影理论。教授给一只小箱子设下护界，你要在它察觉不到的情况下把护界拆掉。“察觉到你的护界，”教授说，“会告诉别人。”|拆箱日。教授拿来一只新箱子，上面是新的护界。|箱子。你现在有一根专门用来干这个的针了。} {这道护界细得像蛛网。|这道护界又厚又有耐心。|你对它呼气时，这道护界会嗡嗡作响。}\n# en: A small iron-bound box on a bench under a dim lamp, faint threads of a pale ward wrapped around it, a student leaning close with a needle.\nscene [b89192] = 昏暗灯下的长桌上放着一只铁箍小箱，淡淡的苍白护界丝线缠绕着它，一名学生拿着针凑近。\n# en: Thread by thread\nopt.left.label [085b9e] = 一根一根地拆\n# en: {An hour with a needle and a steady breath, and the box opens. The professor checks the ward and it has no idea.|You undo it strand by strand. It takes the whole lesson, and you don't look up once.|Your favourite needle and a slow breath. The humming ward goes quiet one strand at a time, and never notices it's gone.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0fc048] = {一根针，一口稳稳的气，一个钟头后箱子开了。教授检查护界，它浑然不觉。|你一缕一缕地解开它。这花了整节课，你一次头都没抬。|你最顺手的那根针，一口慢慢的气。嗡鸣的护界一缕一缕地安静下来，始终没发觉自己已经没了。}\n# en: Cut it in one stroke\nopt.right.label [bb2609] = 一刀切断\n# en: {You find the knot and cut it clean. The ward never knows. The professor raises a brow and opens the box.|One cut and the threads fall away. The professor spends a moment looking at the box and then at you.|You cut it between hums. The ward stops mid-note and never starts again. The professor writes something down.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bd0b78] = {你找到结，干净利落地切断。护界始终没察觉。教授挑了挑眉，打开了箱子。|一刀下去，丝线纷纷散落。教授看了箱子一会儿，又看了看你。|你在两声嗡鸣之间下刀。护界在半个音上停住，再也没响起。教授记了点什么。}\n# en: {The ward notices, snaps across your knuckles and rings like a little bell. The professor says, \"And now someone knows.\" Everyone looks at you.|The ward snaps back at your hand and stings. The box stays shut.|You cut it mid-hum. It shrieks instead, and the professor has to open the window to let the sound out.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ba15c0] = {护界察觉了，啪地抽过你的指节，像一只小铃铛一样响起来。教授说：“现在有人知道了。”所有人都看向你。|护界弹回来蜇了你的手。箱子还是关着。|你在嗡鸣中途下刀。它尖叫起来，教授只好开窗把声音放出去。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/dating.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/dating.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event date_market_day\n# en: Market Day\ntitle [0bcdaf] = 集市日\n# en: {Market day in the village, and {partner} has decided you're going together.|Market day again. {partner} knows which stall has the good pies now.|{partner} has a list for the market, and you're on it.} {A stall sells hats nobody should wear.|There's a juggler with one ball too many.|{season == \"winter\": Roasted chestnuts, paper cones, cold fingers.|Strawberries, a whole cart of them.}}\ntext [d9c093] = {村里的集市日，{partner} 已经决定你们要一起去。|又是集市日。{partner} 现在知道哪个摊子的派好吃了。|{partner} 列了一张集市清单，你也在上面。} {有个摊子卖谁都不该戴的帽子。|有个杂耍艺人多抛了一个球。|{season == \"winter\": 烤栗子、纸筒、冻僵的手指。|草莓，整整一车。}}\n# en: Two students wandering a village market arm in arm, stalls of ribbons and pies, one holding up a ridiculous hat.\nscene [08939c] = 两个学生挽着胳膊逛村里的集市，摊位上挂着丝带、摆着馅饼，其中一个举起一顶滑稽的帽子。\n# en: Buy {them} something silly\nopt.left.label [89db94] = 给 {them} 买点傻东西\n# en: {You buy {partner} the worst hat at the market. {They} wear{s} it all day, with enormous dignity.|A tin ring from a pedlar, too big for any finger. {partner} threads it on a string and doesn't take it off.|Halfway down {partner}'s list, between flour and string, you find \"something silly\" in your own handwriting. A paper crown. {They} wear{s} it home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f4dea1] = {你给 {partner} 买了集市上最丑的帽子。{They} 戴了一整天，极其庄重。|从小贩那儿买来一枚锡戒指，哪根手指戴都太大。{partner} 把它穿在绳子上，再没摘下来。|{partner} 的清单写到一半，在面粉和绳子之间，有一行是你的笔迹：“买点傻东西”。一顶纸王冠。{They} 戴着它回了家。}\n# en: Just walk and talk\nopt.right.label [a304f1] = 就走走聊聊\n# en: {You walk the whole market twice and buy nothing at all. You talk the entire time and remember none of it, only the voice.|An afternoon of nothing in particular, which is exactly what you both needed.|You ignore the list together and just walk. {partner} crosses things off anyway, one by one, as if you'd bought them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3a1d75] = {你们把整个集市逛了两遍，什么都没买。你们一直在聊，聊了什么一句也不记得，只记得那个声音。|一个无所事事的下午，正是你们俩都需要的。|你们一起把清单抛到脑后，只是走着。{partner} 还是一项一项地划掉，好像你们都买了似的。}\n\n@event date_study_together\n# en: Studying Together\ntitle [1131fc] = 一起学习\n# en: {{partner} suggests studying together. In theory, this is efficient.|Another study evening with {partner}. The theory remains a theory.|{partner} has saved you a seat in the library and stacked your books for you.} {The exam is in a fortnight.|The library is very quiet.|Your knees keep touching under the table.}\ntext [aaefba] = {{partner} 提议一起学习。理论上，这很高效。|又一个和 {partner} 一起学习的晚上。理论依然只是理论。|{partner} 在图书馆给你占了座，还把你的书摞好了。} {考试还有两个星期。|图书馆非常安静。|你们的膝盖在桌子底下一直碰到一起。}\n# en: Two students at a library table, books piled between them, one pretending to read while watching the other.\nscene [10169c] = 图书馆的桌旁，两个学生之间堆着书，一个假装在读，其实在看另一个。\n# en: Actually study\nopt.left.label [c324b5] = 认真学习\n# en: {You study, properly, in companionable silence. {partner} sighs once, theatrically. You both pass the next quiz.|Two hours of real work. {partner} quizzes you on the walk back, a little wistful that it was only studying, and you get them all right.|You keep your knees to yourself and your eyes on the page, mostly. {partner} stacked the books in the right order, and you get through all of them.}\nopt.left.say.1 [713429] = {你们在惬意的沉默中好好学习。{partner} 夸张地叹了一次气。你们俩下次小测都过了。|两个小时实打实的功课。回去的路上 {partner} 考你，有点遗憾这回真的只是学习，而你全答对了。|你管好自己的膝盖，眼睛也大多盯着书页。{partner} 把书按正确的顺序摞好了，你们全都读完了。}\n# en: Don't study at all\nopt.right.label [82fa8a] = 完全不学\n# en: {You pass notes, then drawings, then you're both thrown out of the library for laughing. Worth it.|Not a page is turned. The librarian moves you apart, which only makes it worse.|Your knees keep touching and neither of you moves. The books stay stacked exactly as {partner} left them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3126aa] = {你们传纸条，然后传画，然后因为笑得太大声被一起赶出了图书馆。值了。|一页书都没翻。图书管理员把你们分开坐，结果更糟了。|你们的膝盖一直碰着，谁也没挪开。书还原样摞着，跟 {partner} 摆好时一模一样。}\n\n@event date_first_quarrel\n# en: A Quarrel\ntitle [719a6c] = 一次争吵\n# en: Your first real quarrel with {partner}. It started about something small, a missed supper, and became about something large before either of you noticed. Now you're on opposite ends of a bench and neither of you will speak first.\ntext [14e59c] = 你和 {partner} 第一次真正的争吵。起因是件小事，一顿错过的晚饭，在两人都没察觉的时候，变成了一件大事。现在你们各坐长椅一头，谁也不肯先开口。\n# en: Two students on opposite ends of a cold courtyard bench, not looking at each other, a dropped scarf between them.\nscene [ebd0c4] = 两个学生坐在冷清庭院里一张长椅的两端，互不相看，中间掉着一条围巾。\n# en: Speak first\nopt.left.label [e3b9df] = 先开口\n# en: \"I'm sorry,\" you say, and it costs you. {partner} looks at you, then shuffles along the bench. \"Me too.\" You talk it through until the bell. It's better after, and you both know how it's done now.\nopt.left.say.1 [9514e8] = “对不起，”你说，说出这句话不容易。{partner} 看着你，然后挪了过来。“我也是。”你们一直谈到钟响。之后好多了，你们俩也都知道该怎么和好了。\n# en: Wait for {them}\nopt.right.label [bbb6ab] = 等 {them} 先开口\n# en: You wait. So does {partner}. You leave separately. The next day you both pretend it didn't happen, and it lies between you like the scarf on the bench.\nopt.right.say.1 [efe5a6] = 你等着。{partner} 也等着。你们各自离开。第二天你们都假装什么也没发生，它横在你们之间，就像长椅上那条围巾。\n\n@event date_meet_friends\n# en: Meeting the Friends\ntitle [92ece0] = 见朋友\n# en: {friend} and {partner} have heard a great deal about each other, all from you, and now they're at the same supper table. {friend} is being very polite. {partner} is being very polite back. You are dying.\ntext [91a7b1] = {friend} 和 {partner} 从你嘴里听说了对方很多事，现在他们坐在同一张晚餐桌旁。{friend} 非常有礼貌。{partner} 也非常有礼貌地回应。你快死了。\n# en: Three students around a refectory table, two of them sizing each other up over the bread basket, one in the middle smiling too hard.\nscene [1fa419] = 膳堂桌旁的三个学生，其中两个隔着面包篮互相打量，夹在中间的那个笑得太用力。\n# en: Steer the talk to something they share\nopt.left.label [6a05ef] = 把话题引向他们的共同点\n# en: You find it on the third try: a shared loathing of Thursday's soup. By pudding they're ganging up on you, which is exactly what you wanted.\nopt.left.say.1 [988245] = 试到第三次你找到了：两人都痛恨星期四的汤。到吃甜点时，他们已经联手对付你了，这正是你想要的。\n# en: Every subject dies. They're kind about it, both of them, and the silence is very long. \"Well,\" says {friend}, afterwards. \"They're nice.\" It's not quite a compliment.\nopt.left.say.2 [055ea9] = 每个话题都冷了场。他们俩对此都很客气，沉默非常漫长。“嗯，”事后 {friend} 说，“人挺好的。”这不完全是夸奖。\n# en: Let them find their own way\nopt.right.label [a54eb8] = 让他们自己找到相处之道\n# en: You eat your supper and let them circle each other. By the end they've agreed on nothing except that you talk in your sleep. You never find out how {partner} knew that and {friend} did.\nopt.right.say.1 [bde158] = 你吃着晚饭，让他们互相试探。到最后，他们除了一致认为你说梦话之外，什么都没达成共识。你始终没弄明白 {partner} 和 {friend} 是怎么知道的。\n\n@event date_rainy_afternoon\n# en: Rained In\ntitle [95b599] = 被雨困住\n# en: {Rain all afternoon, and {partner} has found the window seat in the west tower, a blanket and an old board game with half the pieces.|Rain again, and {partner} has claimed the window seat for the two of you.|Rain, a blanket, and a game whose missing pieces someone has replaced with ones carved from soap.} {The rain is loud on the leads.|The tea is too hot.|The game's rules are mostly invented.}\ntext [4bfc24] = {下了一下午的雨，{partner} 找到了西塔的窗边座位、一条毯子和一副缺了一半棋子的旧棋盘游戏。|又下雨了，{partner} 替你们俩占好了窗边座位。|雨、毯子，还有一副游戏，缺的棋子被人用肥皂刻的补上了。} {雨打在铅皮屋顶上很响。|茶太烫了。|游戏规则大多是编的。}\n# en: Rain streaming down a tall window seat, two students under one blanket with a board game between them, cups of tea steaming.\nscene [ce0ce8] = 雨水顺着高高的窗户淌下，窗边座位上两个学生裹着一条毯子，中间摆着棋盘，茶杯冒着热气。\n# en: Play to win\nopt.left.label [6c2256] = 认真求胜\n# en: {You win, ruthlessly. {partner} demands a rematch and wins that. Neither of you will ever forget the score.|You cheat; {partner} cheats better. The game ends in a pillow fight and a broken teacup.|You invent a rule; {partner} invents a better one. A soap piece wins in the end, and you both agree it earned it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [37cbd3] = {你赢了，毫不留情。{partner} 要求再来一局，然后赢了。你们俩永远都不会忘记这个比分。|你作弊；{partner} 作弊作得更好。游戏以一场枕头大战和一只摔碎的茶杯告终。|你发明了一条规则；{partner} 发明了一条更好的。最后赢的是一枚肥皂棋子，你们俩一致认为它实至名归。}\n# en: Forget the game\nopt.right.label [13db77] = 忘了游戏\n# en: {The game is abandoned by the second move. You talk about your families until the rain stops and the room is dark and you're late for supper.|You fall asleep under the blanket and wake up cold and late and very happy.|You carve another soap piece together while the rain goes on. Nobody can say what it was meant to be, and {partner} keeps it anyway.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6073d4] = {第二步游戏就被抛在了一边。你们聊着各自的家人，直到雨停，屋里黑了，晚饭也迟到了。|你们在毯子底下睡着了，醒来时又冷又晚，非常快乐。|雨一直下，你们一起又刻了一枚肥皂棋子。谁也说不清它本来该是什么，{partner} 还是留下了它。}\n\n@event date_forgotten_day\n# en: The Day You Forgot\ntitle [58f7f4] = 你忘了的日子\n# en: {partner} hands you a small parcel, wrapped carefully. \"It's our day,\" {they} say{s}. Apparently there is a day. You didn't know. You have nothing. {partner} sees you realise it.\ntext [24f954] = {partner} 递给你一个小包裹，包得很仔细。“今天是我们的日子，”{they}说。原来还有这么个日子。你不知道。你什么也没准备。{partner} 看见你反应过来了。\n# en: A student holding a small wrapped present out to another student, whose hands are empty and whose face has just fallen.\nscene [cac280] = 一个学生把一个小小的包好的礼物递向另一个学生，对方两手空空，脸刚刚垮下来。\n# en: Confess and make it up\nopt.left.label [112bdb] = 坦白，然后补救\n# en: \"I forgot,\" you say. {They} laugh{s}, a little hurt. That evening you turn up with flowers from the village and a terrible poem, and the poem does it.\nopt.left.say.1 [d71455] = “我忘了，”你说。{They} 笑了，有一点受伤。那天晚上你带着从村里买来的花和一首糟糕的诗出现，是那首诗打动了 {them}。\n# en: Pretend your gift is \"coming\"\nopt.right.label [d78c81] = 假装你的礼物“在路上”\n# en: You run to the village before the shops shut and spend more than you meant on something you hope looks planned. {partner} believes you, or kindly pretends to.\nopt.right.say.1 [f03c99] = 你赶在商店关门前跑到村里，花了比预想更多的钱，买了一件希望看起来像是早有准备的东西。{partner} 相信了你，或者好心地装作相信。\n# en: The shops are shut. Your gift \"is delayed\" for a week. {partner} doesn't ask about it again, and that is worse than asking.\nopt.right.say.2 [bd2585] = 商店都关门了。你的礼物“延误了”一个星期。{partner} 再也没问起过，这比问起更糟。\n\n@event date_dance_lesson\n# en: Learning to Dance\ntitle [2d93c7] = 学跳舞\n# en: With the {month == 4: Midwinter Ball|Starfall dance} coming, {partner} has decided you're going to learn properly, in an empty classroom, with the chairs pushed back. {~You start on the dance with the spin.|\"Again,\" says {partner}. \"From the turn.\"|Somebody is watching from the door.|Your feet have opinions.|There's no music, only counting.}\ntext [bab42b] = {month == 4: 仲冬舞会|星坠节舞会}快到了，{partner} 决定要你好好学一学，在一间空教室里，椅子都推到了墙边。{~你们从那支有转圈的舞开始。|“再来，”{partner} 说。“从转身开始。”|有人在门口偷看。|你的脚自有主张。|没有音乐，只有数拍子。}\n# en: Two students practising dance steps in an empty classroom, chairs pushed back, one counting aloud and the other looking at their feet.\nscene [ba646d] = 空教室里两个学生在练舞步，椅子推到一边，一个大声数着拍子，另一个盯着自己的脚。\n# en: Practise until it's right\nopt.left.label [efeeed] = 练到对为止\n# en: {~By the end of the hour you can do the turn without looking. {partner} says \"There,\" very softly, and doesn't let go straight away.|Your feet ache, and you can dance. Nearly.|Whoever is watching from the door claps when you finally get the turn. {partner} bows to them, and then to you.|Your feet stop arguing about halfway through the hour. By the end you can do the turn, and {partner} is insufferably pleased.|You count until the counting turns into a tune, and the turn comes out right on the last beat.}\nopt.left.say.1 [16140f] = {~一个小时结束时，你已经能不看脚就转身了。{partner} 很轻地说了声“对了”，没有马上松手。|你的脚很疼，但你会跳舞了。差不多吧。|你终于学会转身时，门口那个看热闹的人鼓起了掌。{partner} 向那人鞠了一躬，然后向你。|练到半个钟头，你的脚不再闹别扭了。结束时你已经会转身了，{partner} 得意得叫人受不了。|你数着拍子，数到拍子变成了曲调，转身正好落在最后一拍上。}\n# en: Make it a joke\nopt.right.label [9667e2] = 当成玩笑\n# en: {~You invent a new dance, mostly flailing. {partner} laughs so hard {they} ha{s} to sit down, then joins in.|You never learn the turn. You learn that {partner} snorts when {they} laugh{s}, which is better.|You play it up for whoever is watching at the door. They laugh, {partner} laughs, and nobody learns the turn.|You let your feet do what they like. {partner} copies them exactly, and it becomes a dance of its own.|You count in the wrong order on purpose. {partner} loses count laughing, and that's the end of the lesson.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8c4cea] = {~你发明了一种新舞，主要是乱扑腾。{partner} 笑得不得不坐下来，然后也加入了。|你始终没学会那个转身。你学到的是 {partner} 笑起来会打鼻响，这更好。|你冲着门口那个看热闹的人卖力表演。那人笑了，{partner} 也笑了，谁也没学会转身。|你由着自己的脚乱来。{partner} 一步不差地跟着学，结果成了一支自成一派的舞。|你故意把拍子数乱。{partner} 笑得数不下去，这堂课就此结束。}\n\n@event date_after_graduation\n# en: After\ntitle [dd3a69] = 以后\n# en: On the castle wall, with the light going long over the valley, {partner} asks the question neither of you has said out loud: \"After graduation. What happens to us?\" The valley below is gold. {They} {are} not looking at you, very deliberately.\ntext [070535] = 城墙上，光线在山谷上拉得长长的，{partner} 问出了你们俩都没说出口的那个问题：“毕业以后。我们怎么办？”下面的山谷一片金黄。{They} 很刻意地没有看你。\n# en: Two students sitting on the castle wall at sunset, legs dangling, the whole valley below them gold.\nscene [b579db] = 日落时分，两个学生坐在城墙上，双腿悬空，下方整个山谷一片金黄。\n# en: \"We go together.\"\nopt.left.label [1a21cd] = “我们一起走。”\n# en: {They} let{s} out a breath {they}'d been holding for months. You plan it there and then, badly: a city, a cottage, a list of rules for the kitchen. It doesn't matter that it's badly. It's yours.\nopt.left.say.1 [0e5dc5] = {They} 长长地呼出一口憋了好几个月的气。你们当场就规划起来，规划得很糟：一座城市，一间小屋，一张厨房规矩清单。规划得糟不要紧。那是你们的。\n# en: \"Let's not decide yet.\"\nopt.right.label [7da709] = “先别决定。”\n# en: \"Not yet,\" {they} agree{s}, too quickly, and takes your hand. You watch the gold go grey. It's honest, and it's lonelier than the other answer.\nopt.right.say.1 [726a88] = “先不急，”{they} 答应得太快，然后握住了你的手。你们看着金色褪成灰色。这很诚实，也比另一个答案更孤单。\n\n@event lyra_date_dawn_duel\n# en: A Duel for Two\ntitle [0910cb] = 两个人的决斗\n# en: {Lyra's idea of a romantic afternoon is a duel in the empty courtyard, \"to keep you sharp\".|Another courtyard duel. Lyra has started calling them \"our afternoons\".|Lyra is already in the courtyard, wand out, sleeves rolled.} {She's brought coffee for after.|She's in a terrible mood, which means she slept well.|\"No holding back,\" she says. \"I'll know.\"}\ntext [a39eb6] = {莱拉心目中浪漫的下午，是在空荡的庭院里来一场决斗，“让你保持敏锐”。|又一场庭院决斗。莱拉开始管它们叫“我们的下午”了。|莱拉已经在庭院里了，魔杖在手，袖子卷起。} {她带了咖啡，留着决斗后喝。|她心情糟透了，这说明她睡得很好。|“不许留手，”她说。“我会知道的。”}\n# en: Two duellists bowing in a misty courtyard at dawn, wands lowered, both trying not to smile.\nscene [faa89f] = 雾气弥漫的黎明庭院里，两名决斗者垂下魔杖互相鞠躬，都在努力憋着不笑。\n# en: Duel her properly\nopt.left.label [356d49] = 认真和她决斗\n# en: {You win. Lyra stares at you as if you'd proposed. \"Again,\" she says, breathless, and then, \"Tomorrow.\" It's the best thing anyone has ever said to you.|A clean win. She shakes your hand, then doesn't let go of it.|No holding back, so you don't, and you win. She grins as if she'd won herself, and demands you show her how.}\nopt.left.say.1 [de4d37] = {你赢了。莱拉盯着你，好像你刚向她求了婚。“再来，”她喘着气说，然后，“明天。”这是有人对你说过的最好的话。|一场干净利落的胜利。她和你握手，然后没有松开。|不许留手，你就没留，然后赢了。她笑得好像赢的是她自己，非要你教她是怎么做到的。}\n# en: {She wins, because she always does, and then sits beside you on the cold steps with the coffee and explains your mistake, very gently, for her.|You lose. She bandages your knuckle herself and pretends it's an inspection.|No holding back on either side. She wins, just, and admits on the steps that it was closer than she'll ever tell anyone else.}\nopt.left.say.2 [32f32b] = {她赢了，因为她总是赢，然后和你一起坐在冰冷的台阶上喝咖啡，讲解你的失误，以她的标准来说非常温柔。|你输了。她亲手给你包扎指关节，假装这是一次检查。|两边都没留手。她险胜，坐在台阶上承认，这一场比她会对任何别人承认的都要接近。}\n# en: Let her win, and tell her so\nopt.right.label [6e593e] = 让她赢，并告诉她\n# en: {She knows the moment you drop your guard. \"Don't you dare,\" she says, and then kisses you, furious, and then makes you do it again properly.|She's outraged. She's also, for the rest of the day, unusually soft.|\"I said I'd know,\" she says, the moment you drop your guard. She makes you start again, and this time you don't dare.}\nopt.right.say.1 [aaeac7] = {你一放下防守，她就知道了。“你敢，”她说，然后吻了你，气冲冲的，然后逼你好好再打一遍。|她气坏了。可她这一整天也格外温柔。|你一放下防守，她就说：“我说过我会知道的。”她让你重新开始，这一次你不敢了。}\n\n@event tobias_date_greenhouse_supper\n# en: Lunch Among the Tomatoes\ntitle [5ce58a] = 番茄丛中的午餐\n# en: {Tobias has cooked lunch in the greenhouse, at a table between the tomato rows, with everything he grew himself.|Lunch in the greenhouse again. The tomatoes have names now.|Tobias has added a second candle and a jar of wildflowers.} {The soup is his grandmother's recipe.|A tomato plant is leaning in to listen.|There is far too much bread.}\ntext [e1963e] = {托比亚斯在温室里做了午饭，桌子摆在番茄垄之间，所有东西都是他亲手种的。|又是温室里的午饭。番茄们现在都有名字了。|托比亚斯多点了一根蜡烛，还摆了一罐野花。} {汤是他祖母的配方。|一株番茄凑过来偷听。|面包多得离谱。}\n# en: A candlelit table set up between rows of tomato plants in a warm greenhouse at night, a big student serving soup from a pot.\nscene [128ebb] = 夜里温暖的温室中，番茄垄之间摆着一张烛光餐桌，一个大个子学生正从锅里盛汤。\n# en: Eat everything, and ask for seconds\nopt.left.label [0c79a4] = 全吃光，再要一份\n# en: {You eat three bowls and he beams like the sun. He tells you about the farm, the goats, the year the river froze. You could listen for ever, and nearly do.|Seconds, thirds, a slice of pie. Tobias watches you eat with an expression you want to keep in a jar.|You eat until the bread is gone, which takes some doing. Tobias lights the second candle off the first and says he'll bake less next time. He won't.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d65a7f] = {你吃了三碗，他笑得像太阳。他给你讲农场、山羊、河水结冰的那一年。你可以一直听下去，也差点就这么做了。|第二份、第三份、一块派。托比亚斯看你吃饭的表情，你想装进罐子里留着。|你一直吃到面包见底，这可不容易。托比亚斯用第一支蜡烛点亮了第二支，说下回少烤点。他不会的。}\n# en: Help him in the beds after\nopt.right.label [b0cee6] = 饭后帮他打理苗床\n# en: {You weed the beds in the afternoon sun, side by side. Your back aches; your hands smell of tomato leaves. He hums the whole time.|An hour of planting. He shows you how to tell a seedling it's doing well. You feel ridiculous, and do it anyway, until your knees ache from the kneeling.|You work off the bread in the beds after. He picks you a wildflower for the jar and pretends it was a weed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [72f734] = {你们在午后的阳光下并肩除草。你的背很酸，手上满是番茄叶的气味。他一直哼着歌。|种了一个小时的苗。他教你怎么告诉一株幼苗它长得很好。你觉得自己很可笑，但还是照做了，一直跪到膝盖发疼。|吃完你们去苗床干活，把面包消化掉。他给你摘了一朵野花插进罐子里，假装那是杂草。}\n\n@event seraphine_date_a_future\n# en: A Future She Saw\ntitle [d092d0] = 她看见的未来\n# en: {Seraphine says she's seen a future with the two of you in it, and wants to tell you about it on the observatory floor, under the shuttered dome.|Another future, another hour on the observatory floor.|\"I saw us again,\" Seraphine says, lying down beside you. \"Older.\"} {She's shuffling the cards without looking.|The dome's painted stars have faded to grey.|She is, for once, entirely in the present.}\ntext [0a231d] = {瑟拉芬说她看见了一个有你们俩的未来，想在观星台合拢的穹顶下、躺在地板上讲给你听。|又一个未来，又一个钟头躺在观星台的地板上。|“我又看见我们了，”瑟拉芬在你身边躺下说。“老了以后。”} {她看都不看地洗着牌。|穹顶上画的星星已经褪成了灰色。|难得一次，她完完全全地活在当下。}\n# en: A seer and a student lying on the observatory floor beneath the open dome, a scatter of tarot cards between them.\nscene [1af1ef] = 一位预言者和一个学生躺在观星台敞开的穹顶下的地板上，两人之间散落着塔罗牌。\n# en: Ask her to tell you\nopt.left.label [fb782a] = 请她讲给你听\n# en: {She tells you. A kitchen, a cat, a terrible argument about curtains, a garden. It's so ordinary you could cry. She says it's her favourite one.|A future with snow in it, and an old friend who comes to stay and never leaves. She stops halfway and holds your hand.|She tells you about the older two of you: slower, quieter, still arguing about curtains. She laughs at her own future and holds your hand all the way through it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b5d4e3] = {她讲了：一间厨房，一只猫，一场关于窗帘的激烈争吵，一座花园。平凡得让你想哭。她说这是她最喜欢的一个。|一个下着雪的未来，还有一位来做客、再也没走的老朋友。她讲到一半停下，握住了你的手。|她给你讲年老的你们俩：慢一些，安静一些，还在为窗帘吵架。她笑着讲自己的未来，从头到尾握着你的手。}\n# en: Ask her not to\nopt.right.label [5b1e61] = 请她别讲\n# en: {\"Let it surprise us,\" you say. She looks at you as if no one has ever asked her that. Then she puts the cards away, all of them.|She's quiet a while, then laughs. \"You're the only person I can't read,\" she says. \"It's very restful.\"|\"Then don't tell me,\" you say. She doesn't. For a whole hour she's only here, with you, and that's the best future yet.}\nopt.right.say.1 [eb3e1f] = {“让它给我们一个惊喜吧，”你说。她看着你，好像从来没有人这样要求过她。然后她把牌收了起来，全部。|她沉默了一会儿，然后笑了。“你是我唯一读不透的人，”她说。“这很让人放松。”|“那就别告诉我，”你说。她没说。整整一个小时，她只在这里，和你在一起，这是目前为止最好的未来。}\n\n@event corvin_date_rooftop_supper\n# en: Supper on the Roof\ntitle [5ad156] = 屋顶晚餐\n# en: {Corvin leads you through three passages and a window to a rooftop between chimneys, where a tablecloth and supper are waiting.|Another rooftop, another tablecloth. You don't ask where he finds them.|\"New roof,\" says Corvin. \"Better view. Mostly legal.\"} {There are pastries you don't recognise.|There's a candle in a jam jar.|He's brought cushions, which is unlike him.}\ntext [084c33] = {科尔文领你穿过三条通道和一扇窗户，来到烟囱之间的一片屋顶，那儿铺着桌布，晚餐已经备好了。|又一片屋顶，又一块桌布。你没问他从哪儿找来的。|“新屋顶，”科尔文说。“视野更好。基本合法。”} {有些酥皮点心你认不出来路。|果酱罐里点着一根蜡烛。|他带了靠垫，这可不像他。}\n# en: A tablecloth spread on a castle rooftop between chimneys, a candle in a jar, two students eating pastries above the sleeping towers.\nscene [3fb767] = 城堡屋顶的烟囱之间铺着一块桌布，罐子里点着蜡烛，两个学生在沉睡的塔楼上方吃着酥皮点心。\n# en: Ask where the supper came from\nopt.left.label [7accc3] = 问晚餐是哪儿来的\n# en: {\"Borrowed,\" he says. From the headmistress's kitchen. You eat it anyway, and it tastes of getting away with something. He grins all evening.|\"Don't ask, saint.\" You don't. The pastries are perfect.|\"Mostly legal,\" he repeats, when you ask. The supper is from the prefects' pantry. You eat it on his cushions and laugh until a window opens below.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9bb678] = {“借的，”他说。从院长的厨房借的。你还是吃了，吃起来有一种逃过一劫的味道。他咧嘴笑了一整晚。|“别问，圣人。”你没问。点心完美无缺。|你一问，他就重复：“基本合法。”晚饭是从级长们的食品柜里来的。你们坐在他的垫子上吃，笑到楼下有扇窗户打开了。}\n# en: Just enjoy it\nopt.right.label [a859b1] = 尽情享受\n# en: {You eat supper on the roof until the candle gutters and the cold comes up through the slates. He doesn't make a single joke for a whole hour, and when you notice, he kisses you to stop you saying so.|The chimneys are warm, the air is cold, and you climb down at midnight scraped and happy.|You lean back on the cushions and watch the towers go dark one by one. He says nothing clever all evening, and you like him better for it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c980c1] = {你们在屋顶上吃到蜡烛燃尽，寒气从石板瓦里冒上来。整整一个小时他一个笑话都没讲，你注意到的时候，他吻了你，不让你说出来。|烟囱是暖的，空气是冷的，你们半夜爬下来，蹭破了皮，满心欢喜。|你靠在垫子上，看着塔楼一座座暗下去。他一整晚没说一句俏皮话，你反而更喜欢他了。}\n\n@event mir_date_invention\n# en: They Built You Something\ntitle [bcced4] = TA 给你做了个东西\n# en: {Mir has built you a present, a brass bird that sings your name, mostly.|Another present from Mir's bench, ticking ominously.|Mir presents their newest invention with a flourish and a fire bucket.} {It's warm to the touch.|It has seven moving parts and one they don't explain.|\"It's probably safe,\" they say.}\ntext [4cafd6] = {米尔给你做了一件礼物，一只会唱你名字的黄铜鸟，大致会唱。|米尔工作台上的又一件礼物，嘀嗒作响，不太妙。|米尔挥手一扬，展示 TA 的最新发明，旁边备着一只消防桶。} {摸上去是温的。|它有七个活动部件，还有一个 TA 不肯解释。|“应该是安全的，”TA 说。}\n# en: A cluttered workshop bench, a small brass contraption with wings sitting on it, a grinning inventor and a nervous student leaning away.\nscene [3fca62] = 杂乱的工作台上放着一个带翅膀的小小黄铜装置，一个咧嘴笑的发明家，和一个紧张地往后仰的学生。\n# en: Wind it up at once\nopt.left.label [5a2193] = 马上上发条\n# en: {It sings your name, slightly wrong, all the way round the workshop, and lands in your hand. Mir bursts into happy tears and pretends it's soot.|It works. It works! They kiss you on the cheek and immediately start on version two.|It's probably safe, and for once it is. It hums a tune you both know and settles on your shoulder. Mir puts the fire bucket down, astonished.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9a7283] = {它绕着工坊唱着你的名字，稍微有点走调，然后落在你手里。米尔喜极而泣，假装是被煤灰熏的。|成功了。成功了！TA 亲了一下你的脸颊，立刻开始做第二版。|它大概是安全的，这一次还真是。它哼起一支你们俩都会的曲子，落在你肩上。米尔放下灭火桶，目瞪口呆。}\n# en: {It sings for three seconds and then explodes, gently, into springs. Mir is mortified. You laugh until they do too, and help them pick up every piece.|It sets your sleeve on fire. Mir has the bucket ready. \"Version two,\" they say grimly.|It's probably not safe after all. The fire bucket earns its keep, and Mir chalks \"probably\" on the bench as a warning to themself.}\nopt.left.say.2 [952817] = {它唱了三秒，然后轻轻地炸成了一堆弹簧。米尔羞愧极了。你笑到 TA 也笑了，然后帮 TA 把每一个零件捡起来。|它把你的袖子点着了。米尔的水桶早就备好了。“第二版，”TA 阴沉地说。|结果它大概并不安全。灭火桶派上了用场，米尔在工作台上用粉笔写下“大概”两个字，提醒自己。}\n# en: Ask them how it works first\nopt.right.label [0e03e7] = 先问 TA 它是怎么工作的\n# en: {They explain for an hour, with diagrams. You understand a third of it and love all of it. You wind it up together, very carefully, and it works.|Two hours of explanation and three cups of tea. The bird sings at the end. You've never been so proud of a thing you didn't make.|They explain why it's probably safe, at length, with the fire bucket close by. It is. You wind it up together, and it works.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f66e3f] = {TA 配着图解讲了一个小时。你听懂了三分之一，却爱上了全部。你们一起小心翼翼地上好发条，它成功了。|两个小时的讲解，三杯茶。最后那只鸟唱起了歌。你从没为一件不是自己做的东西这么骄傲过。|TA 把它为什么大概安全讲了很久，灭火桶就放在手边。它确实安全。你们一起上好发条，它成功了。}\n\n@event kael_date_snow\n# en: Snow, Just for You\ntitle [63bfb8] = 只为你下的雪\n# en: {Kael leads you to a corner of the lawn and makes it snow, only there, only on the two of you.|Another private snowfall. He's getting better at the shapes.|Kael has made it snow again. This time the flakes are stars.} {season == \"summer\": It's a hot day; the snow steams as it falls.|{The flakes are as big as coins.|Somewhere a bird is very confused.|It's the most he's said with his hands in weeks.}}\ntext [5b4dfd] = {凯尔领你走到草坪的一角，让雪落下来，只落在那里，只落在你们两个身上。|又一场私人的雪。他造的形状越来越好了。|凯尔又下雪了。这一次雪花是星星的形状。} {season == \"summer\": 天很热；雪一边落一边冒着热气。|{雪片大得像硬币。|某处有只鸟非常困惑。|这是他几个星期以来用双手说过最多的话。}}\n# en: Snow falling over one small patch of a spring lawn, two students standing in it, one holding out his hand to catch the flakes.\nscene [102324] = 雪落在春日草坪的一小块地方，两个学生站在其中，一个伸出手去接雪花。\n# en: Catch the flakes\nopt.left.label [0ecdf3] = 接雪花\n# en: {You catch them on your tongue until your teeth ache with cold, and laugh, and Kael, astonishingly, laughs too, out loud. The snow falls harder when he's happy, it turns out.|You're both soaked and frozen. He warms your hands in his, which have warmed, against all habit.|You catch a star-shaped flake on your glove and it doesn't melt for a whole minute. Kael watches it more than you do, and smiles when it goes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4ceb1d] = {你用舌头接雪花，接到牙齿都冻疼了，笑了起来，而凯尔，令人惊讶地，也笑出了声。原来他开心的时候，雪会下得更大。|你们俩都湿透了，冻僵了。他用双手焐着你的手，那双手一反常态，是暖的。|你用手套接住一片星形的雪花，它整整一分钟都没化。凯尔看它看得比你还认真，它化掉时他笑了。}\n# en: Ask him what it costs\nopt.right.label [649ee9] = 问他这要付出什么\n# en: {\"Nothing,\" he says. Then, honestly, \"A little cold in the chest, for a day.\" You make him stop. He looks at you as if no one had ever asked him to.|He tells you about Northern frost-craft and what it takes. Then he makes one last snowflake, small, and lets you keep it.|\"Less, now,\" he says, which isn't an answer. You make him stop anyway, and he lets the last star land in your hair.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fec7eb] = {“什么都不用，”他说。然后，老实地：“胸口会冷上一天。”你让他停下。他看着你，好像从来没人这样要求过他。|他给你讲了北方的霜术，以及它需要付出什么。然后他造了最后一片雪花，小小的，让你留着。|“现在少一些了，”他说，这不算回答。你还是让他停了，他让最后一颗星落在你的头发上。}\n\n@event nessa_date_off_record\n# en: Off the Record\ntitle [a5d3b6] = 不留记录\n# en: {Nessa locks the Ledger office, puts her quill in a drawer and says, \"Off the record. For as long as you like.\"|Another hour off the record. The quill sulks in its drawer.|Nessa has started saying \"off the record\" when she means \"I love you\". You've noticed.} {There's a pot of tea and a plate of toast.|The proofs are stacked and, for once, ignored.|It's raining on the skylight.}\ntext [84fbca] = {内莎锁上《流明纪事报》编辑部的门，把羽毛笔放进抽屉，说：“不留记录。想待多久都行。”|又一个不留记录的钟头。羽毛笔在抽屉里生闷气。|内莎开始在想说“我爱你”的时候说“不留记录”了。你注意到了。} {有一壶茶和一盘吐司。|校样摞得整整齐齐，难得一次被冷落在一边。|雨落在天窗上。}\n# en: A newspaper office late at night, an enchanted quill lying still on a desk for once, two students sharing a pot of tea among the proofs.\nscene [c29441] = 深夜的报社编辑部，一支施了魔法的羽毛笔难得安静地躺在桌上，两个学生在校样堆里分享一壶茶。\n# en: Ask her about herself\nopt.left.label [a87cd5] = 问她自己的事\n# en: {She talks about her childhood for an hour, which she never does. A printing shop, a father who read the news aloud. You don't take a single note. She notices.|Nessa, off the record, is quieter and funnier. You stay until the lamp runs out.|You ask about her, and she says \"off the record\" first, and then, quietly, a great deal. The rain on the skylight keeps it between the two of you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1eb01c] = {她讲了一个小时自己的童年，这是她从来不做的事：一家印刷铺，一个会把新闻大声读出来的父亲。你一个字的笔记都没记。她注意到了。|不留记录的内莎更安静，也更有趣。你一直待到灯油耗尽。|你问起她的事，她先说了句“不留记录”，然后轻声说了很多。天窗上的雨声把这些话留在了你们俩之间。}\n# en: Tell her a secret of your own\nopt.right.label [06d39a] = 告诉她一个你自己的秘密\n# en: {You tell her something nobody knows. She holds it like it's the most valuable story she's ever had, and never writes it down.|She listens, and then tells you one back. Off the record. It feels like a promise.|You tell her your secret. She says \"off the record\" back, and you both know what she means.}\nopt.right.say.1 [feb578] = {你告诉了她一件没人知道的事。她像捧着自己得到过的最珍贵的故事那样捧着它，从没写下来。|她听完，也告诉了你一个。不留记录。这感觉像一个承诺。|你把秘密告诉了她。她回了一句“不留记录”，你们俩都知道她是什么意思。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/grounds_more.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/grounds_more.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event duel_hall_reading_the_floor\n# en: Reading the Floor\ntitle [59a276] = 读地板\n# en: {Every famous bout in the Duelling Hall left its mark on the floor, a scorch or a frost-crack or a gouge. Old duellists say you can read a fight in the boards.|The floor again. You've learned which scorch belongs to which year's champion, from the plaques on the wall.|You can read the boards now, more or less: here someone parried late; here someone lost their nerve and their eyebrows.} {Today a fresh burn sits on top of the old ones.|The cleaners have been, and the boards shine like a map under glass.|{season == \"winter\": The hall is freezing and the boards creak under every step.|Dust hangs in the long light from the windows.}}\ntext [c6e5a9] = {决斗厅里每一场著名对决都在地板上留下了印记，一道焦痕、一道霜裂或一道刻痕。老决斗手说，从地板上就能读出一场对决。|又是这片地板。你已从墙上的铭牌认出哪道焦痕属于哪一年的冠军。|如今你大致读得懂这些木板了：这里有人招架晚了；这里有人丢了胆量，也丢了眉毛。} {今天一道新焦痕压在旧痕上面。|清洁工来过了，木板亮得像压在玻璃下的地图。|{season == \"winter\": 大厅冷得刺骨，每踩一步木板都吱嘎作响。|窗外斜照进来的长光里浮着灰尘。}}\n# en: Scorched and scarred floorboards in an empty duelling hall, a student crouched over them tracing a burn mark with one finger.\nscene [a65405] = 空荡荡的决斗厅里，地板满是焦痕与伤痕，一个学生蹲在上面，用一根手指描着一道烧痕。\n# en: Trace an old bout through\nopt.left.label [a50397] = 把一场旧对决描完\n# en: {You follow a duel from the first scorch to the final frost-crack, step by step, until you know where the loser went wrong. Your knees ache from crawling.|You walk a champion's footwork across the burns, slowly, like learning a dance from its footprints. It's tiring, careful work.|You read a whole tournament final in the floor and find the moment it turned, a single mark the size of a coin. You stand up stiff and certain.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bb7a9f] = {你从第一道焦痕一路追到最后一道霜裂，一步一步，直到看出输家错在哪里。爬得你膝盖生疼。|你踩着焦痕慢慢走一位冠军的步法，像照着脚印学一支舞。累人又费心的活儿。|你从地板上读完了一整场锦标赛决赛，找到了局势翻转的那一刻：一道硬币大小的痕迹。你站起来时浑身僵硬，心里笃定。}\n# en: Scrub the floor for the club\nopt.right.label [656163] = 替社团擦地板\n# en: {The club pays a crown or two for scrubbing. You scrub until the boards gleam and your arms burn.|You scrub, and the old marks stay, as they always do; the dirt goes. You're paid in coins and a sore back.|You know which boards to go easy on: the famous ones. The club treasurer pays you, and adds a nod.}\nopt.right.say.1 [403a2d] = {社团擦地板给一两个克朗。你擦到木板锃亮，胳膊火辣辣的。|你擦啊擦，旧痕照旧留着，一向如此；污垢却没了。你换来几枚硬币和一副酸背。|你知道哪些木板要下手轻些：那些著名的。社团司库付了钱，外加一个点头。}\n\n@event duel_hall_second_wanted\n# en: A Second Wanted\ntitle [47af57] = 招一名副手\n# en: {{here} has a formal practice bout today and no second. \"Hold my coat, count the paces, drop the handkerchief. You can count, I assume.\"|{here} is looking for a second again, and spots you at the door before you can leave.|{here} tosses you the coat as you come in, as if it's been decided.} {The opponent is an upper-year with a terrible moustache.|The opponent bows too low, which is a kind of insult.|{season == \"winter\": The hall is so cold the wands spark on their own.|The gallery has a dozen spectators and a bookmaker.}}\ntext [e5775f] = {{here}今天有一场正式的练习对决，却没有副手。“替我拿外套，数步子，丢手帕。你会数数吧，我想。”|{here}又在找副手了，你还没来得及溜，就在门口被看见了。|你一进门，{here}就把外套扔给你，好像早就说定了。} {对手是个留着可怕小胡子的高年级生。|对手鞠躬鞠得太低，这本身就是一种侮辱。|{season == \"winter\": 大厅冷得魔杖自己都在冒火星。|看台上有十几个观众和一个庄家。}}\n# en: Two students facing each other on a scorched duelling floor, wands lowered, while a third stands at the edge holding a coat and a handkerchief.\nscene [f12d32] = 两个学生在焦痕斑斑的决斗场上相对而立，魔杖垂着，第三个人站在场边，拿着一件外套和一块手帕。\n# en: Stand as the second\nopt.left.label [1211ae] = 当这个副手\n# en: {You count, she wins, she doesn't thank you. On the way out she hands you back your scarf, which you hadn't noticed she'd picked up.|She loses on a technicality and blames the handkerchief. Then she buys you a cider, without a word.|You count; she wins in nine seconds. \"Adequate,\" she says, which from Lyra is a medal.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f09634] = {你数步子，她赢了，她没道谢。出门时她把你的围巾递还给你，你都没发现她什么时候捡起来的。|她因为一个技术细节输了，怪到手帕头上。然后一言不发地请你喝了杯苹果酒。|你数步子；她九秒就赢了。“还行。”她说。从莱拉嘴里出来，这就是勋章。}\n# en: {Bram makes you recite the rules of seconding before he lets you hold his coat. He wins, and gives you a demerit for dropping the handkerchief early, then rescinds it.|Bram loses and stands very straight. Afterwards, gruffly, \"Fair count. Thanks.\"|Bram wins, and for a moment looks up at the gallery as if for someone who isn't there.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0530ab] = {布拉姆让你背完副手守则才肯让你拿外套。他赢了，因为你丢手帕早了记你一次过，随即又撤销了。|布拉姆输了，站得笔直。事后他粗声粗气地说：“数得公道。谢了。”|布拉姆赢了，有一瞬间他抬头望向看台，像在找一个不在那里的人。}\n# en: {You count the paces, drop the handkerchief and hold the coat through an hour of shouting. {here} owes you one, and knows it.|You stand on the line through a long, sweaty bout. {here} thanks you warmly afterwards.|You second with the calm of long practice. {here} wins, and claps you on the shoulder like an old comrade.}\nopt.left.say.3 [dbd567] = {你数步子、丢手帕，在一小时的吼叫声里一直拿着外套。{here}欠你一个人情，TA 自己也知道。|你在线上站完一场漫长又汗流浃背的对决。事后{here}热情地谢了你。|你以多年练就的镇定当好副手。{here}赢了，像拍老战友一样拍拍你的肩。}\n# en: Offer to referee instead\nopt.right.label [c1d9e4] = 改当裁判\n# en: {You referee straight down the middle and call a foul against {here}. The gallery approves. {here} does not.|You referee, fairly, and learn more watching both wands than you would holding a coat. {here} feels a little abandoned.|Your call decides the bout. It's the right call; {here} won't admit it till next week.}\nopt.right.say.1 [11abee] = {你不偏不倚地执裁，判了{here}一次犯规。看台赞成。{here}不赞成。|你公正地执裁，同时盯着两根魔杖，学到的比拿外套多得多。{here}觉得有点被抛弃了。|你的判决决定了胜负。判得没错；{here}要到下周才肯承认。}\n\n@event greenhouses_gerald_is_out\n# en: Gerald Is Out\ntitle [dff50c] = 杰拉尔德跑出来了\n# en: {Gerald, Tobias's mandrake, has escaped his pot again and planted himself among the seedlings. His knitted hat has come unravelled.|Gerald is out again. This time he's in a teacup, screaming softly at a watering can.|Gerald has moved into the pot of the tallest fern, with an air of permanence. His hat is half unravelled; the wool trails back to his own pot.} {Tobias is nowhere to be seen.|A note on Gerald's empty pot says \"BACK SOON, DO NOT LET HIM NEAR THE TOMATOES.\"|{season == \"winter\": He's shivering, defiantly.|The seedlings around him are wilting with alarm.}}\ntext [25fad7] = {托比亚斯的曼德拉草杰拉尔德又从盆里逃出来，把自己栽在了幼苗中间。他的针织帽脱线了。|杰拉尔德又跑出来了。这回他待在一只茶杯里，冲着洒水壶轻声尖叫。|杰拉尔德搬进了最高那株蕨的花盆，一副要长住的架势。他的帽子拆了一半，毛线一路拖回他自己的盆。} {托比亚斯不见人影。|杰拉尔德的空盆上贴着字条：“马上回来，别让他靠近番茄。”|{season == \"winter\": 他冻得发抖，却很倔强。|他周围的幼苗都吓蔫了。}}\n# en: A small mandrake in a lopsided knitted hat sitting in the wrong flowerpot among the seedlings, screaming very softly at a watering can.\nscene [0de02b] = 一株戴着歪斜针织帽的小曼德拉草坐在幼苗中间一个不属于他的花盆里，对着洒水壶极轻地尖叫。\n# en: Fix his hat and carry him home\nopt.left.label [9e26ec] = 补好帽子送他回家\n# en: {You re-knit the hat with two pencils and great patience. Gerald lets you, then bites your thumb, which Tobias says means he loves you.|You lift him gently out of the teacup. He screams softly the whole way home and bites you once at the end, fondly.|You tie off the trailing wool and carry him home in your cupped hands. The bite is affectionate. It still stings.}\nopt.left.say.1 [98879d] = {你用两支铅笔耐心地把帽子重新织好。杰拉尔德任你摆弄，然后咬了你的拇指，托比亚斯说这表示他爱你。|你轻轻把他从茶杯里捧出来。他一路轻声尖叫，到家时深情地咬了你一口。|你把拖着的毛线收好结，把他捧在手心送回家。这一口是出于喜爱。还是很疼。}\n# en: Re-pot the seedlings he trampled\nopt.right.label [977063] = 把踩坏的幼苗重新栽好\n# en: {You leave Gerald where he is and save the seedlings he kicked out. He watches you work, screaming softly with approval.|You re-pot a row of uprooted seedlings and leave the teacup alone. Mandrakes are Tobias's department.|You patch up the damage around the fern and let Gerald keep his new home. It seems kinder than arguing with a mandrake.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cc084d] = {你让杰拉尔德待在原处，去救他踢出来的幼苗。他看着你干活，赞许地轻声尖叫。|你重新栽好一排被拔起的幼苗，没去碰那只茶杯。曼德拉草归托比亚斯管。|你修补好那株蕨周围的狼藉，让杰拉尔德留在新家。这似乎比跟一株曼德拉草争论更仁慈。}\n\n@event greenhouses_grafting_bench\n# en: The Grafting Bench\ntitle [592530] = 嫁接台\n# en: {On the grafting bench is the club's oldest experiment, a potted tree that bears pears on one branch and lemons on the other. Beside it, knives, twine, wax and an open invitation.|The grafting bench. Somebody's new graft has taken: a rose that grows on a gooseberry, looking pleased with itself.|The grafting bench. The two-fruit tree has a third branch now, and nobody will admit to it.} {The wax pot is warm and smells of honey.|A row of failed grafts stands along the back, each with a small apologetic label.|{season == \"spring\": Every graft on the bench is in bud.|The saplings are bare, and the twine shows.}}\ntext [dd5537] = {嫁接台上摆着社团最老的实验：一棵盆栽树，一根枝上结梨，另一根结柠檬。旁边放着刀、麻绳、蜡，以及一份公开的邀请。|嫁接台。有人的新嫁接成活了：一朵长在醋栗上的玫瑰，看上去很得意。|嫁接台。那棵双果树长出了第三根枝，谁也不承认是自己干的。} {蜡锅温热，散发着蜂蜜味。|后排站着一溜嫁接失败的作品，每株都挂着一张抱歉的小标签。|{season == \"spring\": 台上每一处嫁接都冒了芽。|树苗光秃秃的，麻绳露在外面。}}\n# en: A potting bench crowded with small grafted saplings bound in twine and wax, one of them bearing two different kinds of fruit on two different branches.\nscene [b287e3] = 盆栽台上挤满了用麻绳和蜡绑着的小嫁接树苗，其中一株在两根不同的枝上结着两种不同的果子。\n# en: Try a graft of your own\nopt.left.label [be2ab7] = 自己试着嫁接\n# en: {You splice a cutting of mint onto a lavender root and wax it shut. A week later it's growing; it smells like a summer evening.|Your graft takes. Someone pins a label to it with your name and a small drawn star.|You graft a cutting onto the two-fruit tree itself. It takes, and the tree now has one branch that is entirely yours.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9c92ea] = {你把一根薄荷插条嫁到薰衣草根上，用蜡封好。一周后它活了，闻起来像夏日的傍晚。|你的嫁接成活了。有人给它别上一张标签，写着你的名字，还画了颗小星星。|你把一根插条直接嫁到双果树上。它成活了，如今那棵树有一根枝完全属于你。}\n# en: {The knife slips; so does the cutting. It joins the row of failures with its own small label, and you join the infirmary queue with a cut thumb.|Your graft sulks, browns and drops off within the week. Your thumb has a nick that takes longer to heal.|The twine won't hold and the wax burns your fingers. Your cutting goes to the failures' shelf.}\nopt.left.say.2 [127425] = {刀一滑，插条也滑了。它带着自己的小标签加入了失败者的行列，你则带着割伤的拇指加入了医务室的队伍。|你的嫁接闹了脾气，发褐，一周内就掉了。你拇指上的口子好得更慢。|麻绳绑不住，蜡还烫了你的手指。你的插条被送上了失败者的架子。}\n# en: Label the old grafts\nopt.right.label [b51afe] = 给旧嫁接贴标签\n# en: {You work out from the bark scars which graft went on when, and write it all down. The two-fruit tree is older than anyone thought.|You label the rose on the gooseberry, which turns out to be harder than growing it.|You map the two-fruit tree branch by branch. The third branch has no record at all. You write \"unknown, but welcome\".}\nopt.right.say.1 [5a89f5] = {你从树皮的疤痕推算出每处嫁接是何时做的，一一记下。那棵双果树比谁想的都老。|你给醋栗上的玫瑰贴好标签，这事比把它种出来还难。|你一根枝一根枝地绘出双果树的图谱。第三根枝毫无记录。你写下：“来历不明，但欢迎。”}\n\n@event greenhouses_seed_tin\n# en: The Seed Tin\ntitle [8ae9de] = 种子罐\n# en: {On the potting bench is the club's seed tin. Take a packet, leave a packet, write what it is; most people write what it is.|The seed tin is fuller than last time. Someone has left a packet labelled simply \"SORRY\".|The seed tin. You know the regulars' handwriting now, and the one who only draws pictures.} {One packet is rattling on its own.|One packet is warm to the touch.|{season == \"spring\": Half the packets have started sprouting through the paper.|One packet hums a single note when you lift it.}}\ntext [36c1b4] = {盆栽台上放着社团的种子罐。拿一包，留一包，写上是什么；大多数人会写。|种子罐比上回满了。有人留了一包，标签只写着“对不起”。|种子罐。你如今认得常客们的笔迹，还有那个只画图的人。} {有一包自己在沙沙响。|有一包摸上去是温的。|{season == \"spring\": 一半的种子包已经顶破纸发了芽。|有一包你一拿起来就哼出一个音。}}\n# en: An old biscuit tin of paper seed packets on a potting bench, every packet labelled in a different hand, one of them rattling on its own.\nscene [18baa0] = 盆栽台上一只旧饼干罐里装着纸种子包，每包标签笔迹各异，其中一包自己在沙沙作响。\n# en: Take the strangest packet\nopt.left.label [0117ca] = 拿最古怪的一包\n# en: {You plant it in a spare pot. By the end of the hour there's a tiny silver bell-flower that rings when you breathe on it.|You plant the warm one. It comes up in minutes, a fern that curls round your finger like a cat's tail.|You plant it and it grows into something nobody in the club can name. You get to name it. You choose well.}\nopt.left.say.1 [095f50] = {你把它种进一只空盆。一小时后长出一朵小小的银铃花，你朝它呵气它就响。|你种下温热的那包。几分钟就冒了出来，一株蕨像猫尾巴一样卷住你的手指。|你种下它，长出了一样社团里谁也叫不出名的东西。命名权归你。你起了个好名字。}\n# en: {It sprouts at once, straight up your nose. You sneeze pollen for the rest of the day.|It grows a single angry thorn and nothing else, and the thorn finds your thumb.|It's a nettle that stings in rhymes. You'll be hearing them in your sleep.}\nopt.left.say.2 [1d3743] = {它立刻发芽，直往你鼻子里钻。你一整天都在打喷嚏喷花粉。|它只长出一根气呼呼的刺，别的什么也没有，那根刺找上了你的拇指。|是一株会押韵的荨麻，蜇人时还押韵。你睡梦里都会听见。}\n# en: Sort the tin properly\nopt.right.label [938cae] = 好好整理罐子\n# en: {You sort the packets by family, season and handwriting. The club will never know it was you, which is the nice part.|You sort the lot and label the unlabelled ones. \"SORRY\" turns out to be turnips, which explains a great deal.|You file the picture-packets by guessing from the drawings. You're right about most of them, you think.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b18ef1] = {你按科属、季节和笔迹整理好种子包。社团永远不会知道是你，这正是好处。|你整理完，给没标签的贴上标签。“对不起”原来是萝卜，这就说得通了。|你凭画猜内容，把画图的那些也归了档。你觉得大部分都猜对了。}\n\n@event flight_field_kite_day\n# en: Kite Day\ntitle [601850] = 风筝日\n# en: {The wind is too wild for brooms, so the first-years are out with charmed kites, paper dragons and fish and one very angry swan.|Kite day again. The kites have learned to fight; a paper dragon has a fish in a headlock.|Whenever the wind is too wild for brooms, the kites come out. You know the swan by now, and the swan knows you.} One kite is stuck in the far hoop, {flapping like a trapped bird|and its first-year is close to tears|and the other kites are jeering at it}.\ntext [22a775] = {风大得骑不了扫帚，于是一年级生们放起了施了咒的风筝：纸龙、纸鱼，还有一只非常愤怒的天鹅。|又是风筝日。风筝们学会了打架；一条纸龙正用胳膊锁住一条鱼。|每逢风大得骑不了扫帚，风筝就出来了。你认得那只天鹅了，天鹅也认得你。} 有只风筝卡在了远处的圈环上，{像只困住的鸟一样扑腾|它的一年级主人快哭了|其他风筝都在嘲笑它}。\n# en: Enchanted paper kites shaped like dragons and fish wheeling and diving over a windswept green field, one tangled in a hoop at the far end.\nscene [e11b11] = 被施了咒的龙形、鱼形纸风筝在大风吹拂的绿色场地上空盘旋俯冲，其中一只缠在远端的圈环上。\n# en: Climb the hoop and free it\nopt.left.label [2bf483] = 爬上圈环救它\n# en: {You shin up the hoop pole and free the kite. The first-years cheer as if you'd won a race.|You climb, untangle, slide down. The first-year announces your name to the whole field.|You know the hoop pole's footholds by now. The kite goes back to its owner, who tells everyone, loudly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c7337b] = {你顺着圈环杆爬上去解下风筝。一年级生们欢呼得像你赢了一场比赛。|爬上、解开、滑下。那个一年级生向全场宣布了你的名字。|你如今熟悉圈环杆上的落脚点了。风筝回到主人手里，主人大声告诉了所有人。}\n# en: {You get halfway up the pole and the wind takes your hat and your grip. You land in the long grass, bruised.|The kite comes free and so do you, from rather high up.|You slip on the wet pole and slide down it, slowly and painfully, to applause.}\nopt.left.say.2 [58ee0e] = {你爬到一半，风卷走了你的帽子和你的抓握。你摔进长草里，青一块紫一块。|风筝解下来了，你也下来了，从相当高的地方。|你在湿杆上一滑，慢慢地、疼痛地一路滑到底，赢得一片掌声。}\n# en: Borrow a kite and fly it\nopt.right.label [17e46b] = 借只风筝来放\n# en: {You borrow a fish kite and steer it with a whispered charm. It bites the swan. Everyone is delighted.|You fly a paper dragon in loops until your arms ache with the pull and your face aches with grinning.|You fly your borrowed kite higher than the rest, holding the charm steady against the gusts.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b3c89a] = {你借了一只鱼风筝，低声念咒操纵它。它咬了天鹅一口。大家都乐坏了。|你让一条纸龙翻着筋斗飞，拽得胳膊酸，笑得脸也酸。|你把借来的风筝放得比谁都高，迎着阵风稳住咒语。}\n\n@event flight_field_mowing_day\n# en: Mowing Day\ntitle [75faec] = 割草日\n# en: {Today the enchanted scythes are mowing the Flight Field, swinging themselves in neat lines while the groundskeeper sleeps under a tree.|Mowing day. A game has sprung up among the scythes: run the line without breaking stride. The groundskeeper is asleep again.|The scythes are out. You know their rhythm now: swish, step, swish, a pause on every seventh.} {The smell of cut grass is everywhere.|Somebody's hat has already been mown in half.|{season == \"summer\": Heat shimmers over the stubble.|A light drizzle makes the grass shine.}}\ntext [9c9f9f] = {今天施了咒的镰刀在给飞行场割草，自己排成整齐的行列挥舞，场地管理员在树下睡大觉。|割草日。镰刀之间兴起了一个游戏：不停步地穿过那一排。管理员又睡着了。|镰刀出动了。你如今熟悉它们的节奏：唰、迈步、唰，每到第七下停一拍。} {到处都是青草刚割过的气味。|已经有人的帽子被齐齐割成了两半。|{season == \"summer\": 热浪在草茬上方晃动。|一阵细雨让草地闪闪发亮。}}\n# en: A row of enchanted scythes swinging themselves across a green field in neat lines while students dart between them and others rake the cut grass.\nscene [b91665] = 一排施了咒的镰刀自己挥舞着，在绿色场地上割出整齐的行列，学生们在镰刀间穿梭，另一些人在耙割下的草。\n# en: Run the scythe line\nopt.left.label [bd2127] = 穿过镰刀阵\n# en: {You dart through the line and out the other side without a scratch. The watching first-years make you do it again.|Swish, step, swish. You run the whole line and arrive at the end breathless and laughing.|You run the line on the seventh-beat pause and it feels like dancing. Nobody else has timed it that well.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0ea5bf] = {你从镰刀阵中一穿而过，毫发无损。围观的一年级生非要你再来一次。|唰、迈步、唰。你跑完整排，到头时上气不接下气，笑个不停。|你踩着第七拍的停顿穿过去，像在跳舞。没人把时机掐得这么准过。}\n# en: {A scythe catches your robe and neatly removes a sleeve. You trip on the stump of it. The groundskeeper wakes, looks, and goes back to sleep.|You trip on a tussock and fall flat as the scythes pass politely overhead. The first-years cheer, the wrong way.|You mistime the seventh beat and go sprawling. Your hat joins the other hat. Your pride follows.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c8c1be] = {一把镰刀勾住你的袍子，利落地削掉一只袖子。你被那截残袖绊了一跤。管理员醒来看了一眼，又睡了。|你被草墩绊倒，扑倒在地，镰刀们彬彬有礼地从你头顶掠过。一年级生们欢呼了，方向不对。|你错过了第七拍，摔了个四仰八叉。你的帽子和另一顶帽子作了伴。你的自尊也跟了过去。}\n# en: Rake hay for the stables\nopt.right.label [5c7bd2] = 替马厩耙干草\n# en: {You rake the cut grass into rows for the stables. The groundskeeper wakes long enough to give you a coin and a nod. Your palms blister.|You rake until the field is striped and your arms are lead. The stables send a crown, and a hippogriff's thanks.|You rake with the scythes' rhythm in your arms. The groundskeeper pays you without waking, which is a skill in itself.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a84100] = {你把割下的草耙成一行行，给马厩用。管理员醒来片刻，给了你一枚硬币和一个点头。你的手掌起了泡。|你耙到整片场地都成了条纹，胳膊沉得像灌了铅。马厩送来一个克朗，外加一头鹰头马身有翼兽的谢意。|你的胳膊里带着镰刀的节奏耙草。管理员没醒就把钱付了，这本身就是一门手艺。}\n\n@event secret_garden_two_cups\n# en: Two Cups on the Bench\ntitle [100ec2] = 长凳上的两只杯子\n# en: {On the bench by the humming pear there's a flask and two chipped cups, and a note in Old Maud's hand. \"One for you, dearie, one for whoever comes.\"|The flask on the bench again, still warm, still with two cups. Maud is never here when you are; the tea always is.|The flask, the cups, the note, and today a biscuit on a saucer beside them, for whoever comes.} {The silver bees are busy.|The ferns are glowing even in daylight.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the bench, steam from the flask.|Blossom has settled in one of the cups.}}\ntext [65f1d7] = {会哼歌的梨树旁的长凳上放着一只保温壶、两只缺口的杯子，还有老莫德亲笔写的字条。“一杯给你，亲爱的，一杯给后来的人。”|长凳上又是那只保温壶，还温着，还是两只杯子。你来时莫德从不在；茶却总在。|保温壶、杯子、字条，今天旁边的碟子里还多了一块饼干，留给后来的人。} {银蜜蜂忙个不停。|蕨类连白天都在发光。|{season == \"winter\": 长凳上结着霜，壶口冒着热气。|一只杯子里落了几片花瓣。}}\n# en: A mossy stone bench in a hidden garden, an old tartan flask and two chipped cups left on it, glowing ferns all around.\nscene [2e0281] = 隐秘花园里一条长满青苔的石凳，上面放着一只旧格子保温壶和两只缺口的杯子，四周是发光的蕨类。\n# en: Sit and drink the tea\nopt.left.label [043f61] = 坐下喝茶\n# en: {The tea tastes of honey and something green. You sit till the cup is cold and feel mended in some small, specific way.|You drink your cup and leave the second for whoever comes. Nobody does, but the pear hums as if someone had.|You drink, eat the biscuit, and wash both cups at the spring. The garden seems to approve of good manners.}\nopt.left.say.1 [91c359] = {茶有蜂蜜和某种青草的味道。你坐到杯子凉透，觉得自己在某个细小而具体的地方被修补好了。|你喝了自己那杯，把另一杯留给后来的人。没人来，可梨树哼得像是有人来过。|你喝茶、吃饼干，在泉边把两只杯子都洗了。花园似乎很赞赏好礼数。}\n# en: Weed the beds for her\nopt.right.label [d74fa1] = 替她除草\n# en: {You leave the tea and weed the beds by the wall until your knees are green and your back is stiff. Maud will know, somehow.|You pull weeds that pull back, and one of them draws blood. The ferns lean away from the worst of them, gratefully.|You weed the far beds until your palms blister, and find a trowel Maud must have lost years ago. You leave it on the bench, by the cups.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6b4155] = {你没喝茶，去墙边的花床除草，直到膝盖都染绿了，腰也僵了。莫德总会知道的。|你拔的草会反拽回来，有一株还扎出了血。蕨类感激地躲开最凶的那几株。|你给远处的花床除草，直到手掌起泡，找到一把莫德多年前弄丢的小铲子。你把它放在长凳上，杯子旁边。}\n\n@event secret_garden_remembering_moss\n# en: The Moss That Remembers\ntitle [d9ae53] = 记得人的苔藓\n# en: {Under the humming pear is a bed of moss so thick it keeps the shape of whoever last lay in it. Today it holds a small curled shape, like someone napping.|The moss again. Today's shape is long and straight, arms folded, as if someone lay there watching the leaves.|You know the moss's habits now. Today it holds two shapes, side by side, close.} {The shapes fade slowly, over an hour or so.|One silver bee is asleep in the hollow of an elbow.|{season == \"winter\": Frost rims the shape like a chalk outline.|The moss is warm where the shape is.}}\ntext [7cdb7b] = {会哼歌的梨树下有一片苔藓，厚得能留住最后躺在上面的人的形状。今天它留着一个小小的蜷缩的形状，像有人在这儿打过盹。|又是那片苔藓。今天的形状又长又直，双臂交叠，像有人躺在这儿看树叶。|你如今摸清了苔藓的脾性。今天它留着两个形状，并排着，挨得很近。} {这些形状会慢慢褪去，大约一个钟头。|一只银蜜蜂睡在一只手肘的凹陷里。|{season == \"winter\": 霜沿着形状结了一圈，像粉笔描的轮廓。|有形状的地方，苔藓是温的。}}\n# en: A deep green bed of moss under the humming pear tree, holding the faint pressed shape of someone who lay there and is gone.\nscene [638731] = 会哼歌的梨树下一片深绿的厚苔藓，上面压着一个淡淡的人形，躺过的人已经离开了。\n# en: Lie down in the moss yourself\nopt.left.label [0de546] = 自己躺进苔藓里\n# en: {You lie down beside the curled shape, not in it. The moss is warm and soft, and you sleep a while without dreaming.|You lie down and look up at the pear tree's leaves for an hour. When you get up, the moss keeps your shape too.|You lie down between the two shapes, careful not to spoil either. You wake rested, and a little lonely.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b1d6ed] = {你躺在那个蜷缩的形状旁边，而不是躺进去。苔藓温暖柔软，你无梦地睡了一会儿。|你躺下来望着梨树的叶子看了一个钟头。起身时，苔藓也留下了你的形状。|你躺在两个形状之间，小心不去破坏任何一个。醒来时你歇足了，也有点寂寞。}\n# en: Study the shapes, and wonder\nopt.right.label [ce8781] = 端详形状，暗自揣想\n# en: {You kneel and read the shape. Small hands, knees drawn up, a hat left behind in the dent of a head. Someone came here to feel safe. It's a strange thing to know about a stranger.|You trace the long shape's folded arms. It looks like someone thinking very hard about something they couldn't say. You leave quietly.|Two shapes, close. You don't know whose, and you decide you shouldn't. It stays with you all evening.}\nopt.right.say.1 [49088b] = {你跪下来读那个形状：小小的手，蜷起的膝盖，头部的凹陷里落下一顶帽子。有人来这里寻求安全感。知道一个陌生人的这种事，感觉很奇妙。|你描着那个长形状交叠的双臂。它像是某个人在苦苦思索一件说不出口的事。你悄悄离开了。|两个形状，挨得很近。你不知道是谁，也决定自己不该知道。这念头陪了你一整晚。}\n\n@event secret_garden_snail_post\n# en: The Snail Post\ntitle [0a39a1] = 蜗牛邮政\n# en: {A snail is crossing the garden wall with a folded scrap of paper tucked under its shell. Maud's garden, it seems, has a postal service.|The snail post again. Three snails today, each with a note, all heading in different directions.|Snail post. You know the route now, along the wall and round the pear, about a day each way.} {The note within reach is folded twice and sealed with a blob of resin.|One note has been answered in a different hand.|{season == \"winter\": The snails are sluggish with cold, the notes damp.|The snail nearest you is wearing a dewdrop like a hat.}}\ntext [988231] = {一只蜗牛正爬过花园的墙，壳底下塞着一张折好的纸片。看来莫德的花园有自己的邮政。|又是蜗牛邮政。今天有三只蜗牛，各驮一张字条，各奔东西。|蜗牛邮政。你如今熟悉路线了：沿着墙，绕过梨树，单程大约一天。} {你够得着的那张折了两折，用一滴树脂封着。|有一张字条被另一种笔迹回复了。|{season == \"winter\": 蜗牛冻得慢吞吞的，字条也潮了。|离你最近的那只蜗牛顶着一颗露珠，像戴了顶帽子。}}\n# en: A garden snail with a tiny folded paper note tucked under its shell, making its way slowly along a mossy wall.\nscene [3444e6] = 一只花园蜗牛壳下塞着一张折好的小字条，正沿着长满青苔的墙慢慢爬行。\n# en: Write a note and send it on\nopt.left.label [7db9c8] = 写张字条寄出去\n# en: {You write something kind for a stranger and tuck it under a shell. The snail sets off, at snail speed, with your secret.|You answer a note left by someone you'll never meet. It feels like talking across a very slow, very gentle river.|You write back to whoever's been writing. The snail takes the note, and an age, and you don't mind.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2fdf73] = {你给一个陌生人写了句好话，塞进壳底。蜗牛以蜗牛的速度出发，带着你的秘密。|你回复了一个你永远不会见到的人留下的字条。就像隔着一条非常缓慢、非常温柔的河说话。|你给那个一直在写信的人回了信。蜗牛接过字条，慢得要命，你也不在意。}\n# en: Read the notes on the way\nopt.right.label [76b094] = 半路偷看字条\n# en: {You ease a note out from under a shell. It's a confession about a stolen apple, decades old. You put it back, a little ashamed.|You read three notes before the snails notice. One of them is about someone exactly like you.|You read the snail post like a gossip column. The snails take it personally; one refuses to move till you leave.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7fda3d] = {你从壳底轻轻抽出一张字条。是几十年前偷了一个苹果的忏悔。你把它塞回去，有点惭愧。|你在蜗牛察觉前读了三张。其中一张写的人和你一模一样。|你把蜗牛邮政当八卦专栏读。蜗牛们很介意；有一只赌气不动，直到你离开。}\n\n@event courier_loft_record_board\n# en: The Record Board\ntitle [5cf4e9] = 纪录板\n# en: {On the loft wall is the record board, the fastest delivery ever flown to every tower and the village, in chalk. The village record is eleven minutes. It has stood for twelve years.|The record board. Someone has shaved four seconds off the observatory run and written the new time very large.|The record board. You know every time on it now, and which ones the couriers say were cheated.} {A courier is idly offering a parcel to anyone who fancies a go.|The wind outside is behind you, for once.|{season == \"winter\": There's ice on the landing platform.|The air is still and the lake is glass.}}\ntext [dbf5ff] = {阁楼墙上是纪录板，用粉笔写着飞往每座塔和镇上的最快送件时间。镇上的纪录是十一分钟，已经保持了十二年。|纪录板。有人把观星台航线的纪录缩短了四秒，新时间写得很大。|纪录板。如今上面每个时间你都熟悉，也知道信使们说哪些是作弊得来的。} {有个信使懒洋洋地拿着一个包裹，问谁想试试。|外面的风难得是顺风。|{season == \"winter\": 起降台上结了冰。|空气静止，湖面像玻璃。}}\n# en: A chalkboard in a draughty loft listing delivery records to every tower and the village, some times rubbed out and rewritten, a student squinting at the fastest one.\nscene [9b9e40] = 漏风的阁楼里一块粉笔板列着飞往每座塔和镇上的送件纪录，有些时间被擦掉重写，一个学生眯眼看着最快的那个。\n# en: Make a run at a record\nopt.left.label [ba9752] = 冲击一项纪录\n# en: {You fly the lecture-tower run flat out and land four seconds inside the record. A courier chalks your name on the board, grumbling.|You take the observatory run, cut the corner by the bell tower and beat the new time by a breath. The loft cheers; the old record holder sulks.|You know the shortcuts now. Your name goes up on the board for the library run, in very large chalk.}\nopt.left.say.1 [529c7f] = {你全速飞完讲堂塔航线，落地时比纪录快了四秒。一个信使嘟囔着把你的名字写上了板。|你飞观星台航线，在钟楼边抄了近道，以一口气的优势打破了新纪录。阁楼一片欢呼；原纪录保持者生起了闷气。|你如今熟悉所有近道。你的名字以很大的粉笔字写在了图书馆航线一栏。}\n# en: {You take the corner by the bell tower too fast and meet a gutter. The parcel arrives; you arrive limping.|You're on pace until a gust shoves you into a flagpole. Your time goes on the board anyway, under the heading SLOWEST.|You misjudge the landing platform and skid the length of it on your knees. The couriers award you a record for distance.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a7e12c] = {你在钟楼边转弯太快，撞上了一道排水槽。包裹到了；你一瘸一拐地到了。|你一直跟得上节奏，直到一阵风把你推向一根旗杆。你的时间照样上了板，列在“最慢”一栏。|你看错了起降台，跪着在上面滑了整整一个台长。信使们授予你一项距离纪录。}\n# en: Study the routes instead\nopt.right.label [acd663] = 改为研究航线\n# en: {You copy down every route on the board and the wind notes beside them. You won't break a record today, but you'll know how.|You ask the couriers how each record was flown. Three of the stories are lies; you can tell which.|You sit under the board working out a new route to the village. It might be a minute faster. Might.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c31313] = {你把板上每条航线和旁边的风向注记都抄了下来。今天你破不了纪录，但你会知道怎么破。|你问信使们每项纪录是怎么飞出来的。其中三个故事是假的；你分辨得出是哪三个。|你坐在板下琢磨一条去镇上的新航线。也许能快一分钟。也许。}\n\n@event courier_loft_pigeon_strike\n# en: The Pigeons Are on Strike\ntitle [4c9e50] = 鸽子罢工了\n# en: {The loft's message pigeons are on strike. They sit on the rafter in a row, puffed and silent, beside a list of demands scratched in claw-marks.|The pigeons have struck again. The demands have grown: better grain, a window that shuts, and the removal of a particular courier.|The pigeons are on strike. They look at you as you come in, as if you've been appointed to negotiate.} {The undelivered notes are piling up.|One pigeon is plainly the ringleader; it has a bald patch and a stare.|{season == \"winter\": The window that doesn't shut is letting the snow in, which seems fair.|Every courier in the loft is pretending not to be afraid of the ringleader.}}\ntext [a71cd2] = {阁楼的信鸽罢工了。它们在椽子上排成一排，鼓着毛一声不吭，旁边是一张用爪印刻出的诉求清单。|鸽子又罢工了。诉求变多了：更好的谷子、一扇关得上的窗，以及开除某位信使。|鸽子在罢工。你一进门它们就看着你，好像你被任命去谈判。} {没送出去的字条越堆越高。|有一只显然是头目：秃了一块，眼神很凶。|{season == \"winter\": 那扇关不上的窗正往里飘雪，这似乎很公平。|阁楼里每个信使都装作不怕那个头目。}}\n# en: A row of indignant pigeons sitting in a line on a loft rafter beside a scrap of paper listing their demands in tiny claw-marks.\nscene [55ffc3] = 一排愤愤不平的鸽子蹲在阁楼椽子上，旁边一张纸片上用细小的爪印列着它们的诉求。\n# en: Talk the ringleader round\nopt.left.label [4ac3df] = 劝服鸽子头目\n# en: {You offer seed, flattery and a promise about the window. The ringleader considers, coos once, and the strike is over.|You talk to the ringleader as you'd talk to a magistrate. It accepts your terms and a raisin.|The ringleader hears you out, eye to eye. The pigeons rise in a clatter and the backlog flies.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f74c40] = {你献上谷子、恭维和一个关于窗户的承诺。头目考虑片刻，咕了一声，罢工结束了。|你像跟一位地方法官说话一样跟头目谈。它接受了你的条件和一颗葡萄干。|头目和你四目相对，听你说完。鸽子们扑棱棱飞起，积压的信件全飞走了。}\n# en: {The ringleader hears your offer and pecks your knuckle, hard. Talks have broken down.|The whole rafter turns its back on you, in unison, which is very hard to recover from. One of them nips your ear on the way.|They accept the raisin and refuse the terms. One of them sits on your head, claws first, to make the point.}\nopt.left.say.2 [37fa4b] = {头目听完你的提议，狠狠啄了你的指关节一口。谈判破裂。|整排椽子齐刷刷背过身去，这很难挽回。其中一只顺路啄了你的耳朵。|它们收下了葡萄干，拒绝了条件。有一只爪子朝下坐到你头上，以示立场。}\n# en: Carry their notes by hand\nopt.right.label [f70187] = 亲手送它们的信\n# en: {You run the undelivered notes round the castle yourself, all seven towers, and come back with sore legs and the pigeons' quiet contempt.|You deliver the notes on foot. The pigeons watch you go, smug as landladies. Your feet ache by the last tower.|You run the round on foot again. You're faster than a pigeon on the stairs, if not in the air, and your calves know it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [eb1897] = {你亲自把没送出的字条送遍城堡七座塔，回来时腿酸了，还收获了鸽子们无声的鄙视。|你步行送信。鸽子们看着你走，得意得像女房东。到最后一座塔时你的脚生疼。|你又步行送了一圈。上楼梯你比鸽子快，在天上可不行，你的小腿肚很清楚。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/grounds.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/grounds.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lakeshore_shore_walk\n# en: The Long Way Round\ntitle [250a85] = 绕远路\n# en: {You came out to walk alone. {date} finds you on the shore path and falls into step without asking.|The shore path is where you go to think. Today {date} has the same idea.|You know every stone of the shore path by now. Today {date} walks it with you.} {season == \"winter\": The lake is frozen at the edges and your breath walks ahead of you.|{season == \"summer\": The water is warm and full of sky.|{Mist lies on the water like a sheet someone forgot to fold.|The towers stand upside down in the lake, lit window by lit window.|The water is so still that a dropped leaf makes rings for a minute.}}}\ntext [8ddf61] = {你本想独自走走。{date}在湖岸小径上遇见你，没问一声就和你并肩走了起来。|湖岸小径是你想事情的地方。今天{date}也是这么想的。|湖岸小径上的每块石头你都认得了。今天{date}陪你走。} {season == \"winter\": 湖边结了冰，你的呼吸走在你前面。|{season == \"summer\": 湖水温暖，盛满了天空。|{雾铺在水面上，像一张忘了叠的床单。|七塔倒映在湖里，一扇亮窗接着一扇亮窗。|湖面静得出奇，一片落叶荡起的涟漪要一分钟才平。}}}\n# en: Two figures walking a lakeside path at dusk, the towers reflected upside down in the still water beside them.\nscene [33361d] = 黄昏时两个人影走在湖边小径上，身旁静水里倒映着颠倒的高塔。\n# en: Walk the long way round\nopt.left.label [793774] = 绕远路走一圈\n# en: {final_year: Halfway round, {date} says \"next year\" and stops, because there won't be one here. You walk the rest closer together.|{season == \"winter\": {You walk until your toes go numb. {date} lends you {their} scarf and pretends not to be cold.|The ice sings under your boots. {date} takes your arm on the slippery stretch and forgets to let go.|You walk the frozen edge in step. {date} counts the herons out loud, and you let {them} lose count.}|{You walk until the far lamps go out. Neither of you mentions the time.|{date} points out a lit tower window and tells you who lives behind it. Half of it is invented. You don't mind which half.|You stand at the water's edge in silence, and the silence is the good kind.}}}\nopt.left.say.1 [8dd7fc] = {final_year: 走到一半，{date}说了句“明年”，又停住了，因为在这里不会再有明年。剩下的路，你们靠得更近。|{season == \"winter\": {你们一直走到你的脚趾冻僵。{date}把围巾借给你，假装自己不冷。|冰在你们靴子下歌唱。走到滑的那段，{date}挽住你的胳膊，忘了松开。|你们沿着结冰的岸边并肩走。{date}大声数着苍鹭，你由着{them}数乱。}|{你们一直走到对岸的灯火一盏盏熄灭。谁也没提时间。|{date}指着一扇亮着的塔窗，告诉你里面住的是谁。一半是编的。你不在乎是哪一半。|你们在水边默默站着，那是很好的那种沉默。}}}\n# en: Talk about anything but us\nopt.right.label [9c9439] = 聊什么都行，别聊我们\n# en: {You talk about exams, the cook and the weather. It's easy. It's also not what either of you came for.|You argue cheerfully about whether the lake has a bottom. It's a good walk. It's only a walk.|You talk about the lake, the cook and everything except the thing. It gets easier to leave it unsaid, which is not a good sign.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b9b89b] = {你们聊考试、聊厨娘、聊天气。很轻松。也不是你们俩来这里想说的。|你们兴致勃勃地争论这湖到底有没有底。散步很愉快。也只是散步。|你们聊湖、聊厨娘，什么都聊，除了那件事。不说出口越来越容易了，这可不是好兆头。}\n\n@event lakeshore_cold_swim\n# en: The Cold Swim\ntitle [242756] = 冷水游泳\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Students in towels are daring each other into the lake through a hole in the ice.|{season == \"summer\": Half the academy is in the lake; the other half pretends not to want to be.|A few hardy students are swimming out to the flat rock, shrieking.}} {The water looks inviting. It always does.|You know how cold the Mirror Lake really is. You're tempted anyway.|You and the lake have an understanding. It doesn't help.}\ntext [be4c03] = {season == \"winter\": 几个裹着毛巾的学生正互相怂恿，要从冰窟窿里跳进湖去。|{season == \"summer\": 半个学院都泡在湖里，另一半在假装自己不想下水。|几个不怕冷的学生正往平顶岩游去，一路尖叫。}} {湖水看上去很诱人。它一向如此。|你知道镜湖实际上有多冷。可你还是心动了。|你和这片湖之间有某种默契。这并没有用。}\n# en: Students in towels on a lakeshore, one wading in up to the knees and shrieking, the towers reflected behind them.\nscene [7cec80] = 湖岸上裹着毛巾的学生们，一个人蹚水到膝盖，正在尖叫，身后倒映着高塔。\n# en: Brave the water\nopt.left.label [5f5f4d] = 下水试试\n# en: {season == \"winter\": You go under, come up roaring, and are hauled out a hero.|{You slap the flat rock, whoop, and swim back to applause from people with dry hair.|You reach the rock first. The cold reaches you second, but you were already winning.|You swim out to the rock and back without thinking about it. That's the trick, it turns out.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [3a2e8d] = {season == \"winter\": 你沉下去，吼着冒出头，被当成英雄拖上了岸。|{你一掌拍上平顶岩，欢呼一声，在一群头发干爽的人的掌声中游了回来。|你第一个碰到岩石。寒冷第二个碰到你，可你已经赢了。|你想都没想就游到岩石那儿又游了回来。原来诀窍就是这个。}}\n# en: {You come back blue to the lips and sneezing. Sister Amaranth sighs before you've even sat down.|Halfway out, the lake reminds you who is in charge. You turn back with what dignity you have left.|The lake wins again. Sister Amaranth has a blanket ready before you've reached the shore.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8c811e] = {你回来时嘴唇发青、喷嚏连天。你还没坐下，阿玛兰斯修女就先叹了口气。|游到一半，湖水提醒你谁才是主人。你带着仅剩的体面掉头回来。|湖又赢了。你还没上岸，阿玛兰斯修女就已经备好了毯子。}\n# en: Hold the towels and heckle\nopt.right.label [a0e0ba] = 帮忙拿毛巾，顺便起哄\n# en: {You announce every dive like a herald. You're dry, popular and a tiny bit jealous.|You keep the towels warm and the commentary warmer. Someone throws a wet sock at you, which is fair.|You keep the towels and the score. The swimmers argue with your score, which means they trust it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [80a183] = {你像传令官一样播报每一次跳水。你很干爽、很受欢迎，也有一点点嫉妒。|你把毛巾捂得暖暖的，解说更热烈。有人朝你扔了一只湿袜子，这很公平。|你管着毛巾，也管着记分。游泳的人跟你争比分，说明他们信得过你。}\n\n@event duel_hall_dummies\n# en: The Practice Dummies\ntitle [01b66d] = 练习假人\n# en: {The near end of the hall belongs to the practice dummies, who watch you come in. The one with the painted moustache looks especially unforgiving.|The dummy with the moustache is still here, with a new scorch mark and, you'd swear, a new grudge.|The moustached dummy greets you with its usual silence. Someone has given it an eyepatch.} {The floorboards are still warm from the last session.|A singed glove lies abandoned on the rack.|Rain drums on the high windows.}\ntext [4bdf3a] = {大厅近端归练习假人所有，它们看着你进门。那个画了小胡子的看起来格外不肯原谅人。|画小胡子的假人还在，多了一道焦痕，你敢发誓，还多了一份怨气。|小胡子假人照例沉默地迎接你。有人给它戴了个眼罩。} {地板还留着上一场练习的余温。|架子上丢着一只烧焦的手套。|雨点敲打着高窗。}\n# en: An empty duelling hall with scorched floorboards and a row of battered straw practice dummies, one with a painted moustache.\nscene [ddc84f] = 空荡的决斗厅，地板满是焦痕，一排破旧的草扎练习假人，其中一个画着小胡子。\n# en: Drill your hexes on it\nopt.left.label [74919a] = 拿它练咒\n# en: {Hex after hex until your wrists ache. The dummy loses a sleeve and none of its contempt.|You land a clean one at last. A wisp of straw floats down, slowly, like applause.|You knock the eyepatch clean off. The dummy looks more menacing without it, which you didn't plan for.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ec81dd] = {一道咒语接一道咒语，直到手腕发酸。假人掉了一只袖子，轻蔑却一分没少。|你终于打出一记干净利落的。一缕稻草慢慢飘落，像是掌声。|你一咒把眼罩打飞了。没了眼罩，那假人看着更凶了，这可不在你的计划里。}\n# en: Restuff the battered ones\nopt.right.label [218ba9] = 给破假人重新填草\n# en: {You haul a bale of straw over from the stables and restuff three dummies. They look almost grateful, which is unsettling.|You restuff the moustached one with special care. It still looks like it's planning something.|You restuff the moustached one and straighten its eyepatch. It's the best-dressed thing in the hall.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a942de] = {你从马厩扛来一捆稻草，给三个假人重新填好。它们看上去几乎心怀感激，这让人不安。|你格外用心地给小胡子假人填了草。它看上去还是在盘算着什么。|你给小胡子假人重新填了草，扶正了它的眼罩。它成了整个大厅里穿得最体面的。}\n# en: Draw the ashwood\nopt.up.label [bfb025] = 拔出白蜡木\n# en: {The moustached dummy blocks, as dummies do, and the ashwood answers with a crack that singes its straw. Your wrist buzzes for an hour.|Every time a dummy's ward goes up, the ashwood bites through it with a spark. You stop when the hall smells of toast.|You duel the whole row. The ashwood jolts each block back at you, a little, and you learn to like it.}\nopt.up.say.1 [b0a6e5] = {留胡子的假人照例格挡，白蜡木回敬了一声脆响，燎焦了它的稻草。你的手腕麻了一个小时。|每当假人的护界升起，白蜡木就带着火花咬穿它。直到厅里有烤面包的味道你才停手。|你和整排假人对决。白蜡木把每一次格挡都稍稍震回你手上，你渐渐喜欢上了这种感觉。}\n\n@event duel_hall_gallery\n# en: From the Gallery\ntitle [f73290] = 看台之上\n# en: {From the gallery above the duelling floor you can watch whoever is practising. Today it's {here}.|You've learned to read duels from up here. Today {here} is drilling.|The gallery rail has a groove where you lean. Below, {here} is drilling.} {Sparks drift up to the gallery rail.|Somebody's familiar is asleep on the scoreboard.|The ceiling has acquired a new scorch mark.}\ntext [d11fa8] = {从决斗场上方的看台，你能看到谁在练习。今天是{here}。|你已经学会从这上面看懂决斗了。今天{here}在练。|看台栏杆上有一道你常倚的凹痕。下面，{here}在练。} {火星飘上看台的栏杆。|不知谁的使魔睡在记分板上。|天花板又添了一道新焦痕。}\n# en: A narrow wooden gallery above a duelling floor, one student watching another drill spells at a target below.\nscene [5292ba] = 决斗场上方狭窄的木制看台，一个学生看着下面另一个对着靶子练咒。\n# en: Offer to spar\nopt.left.label [ed03b2] = 提出陪练\n# en: {You take a round off {here}. {They} stare{s} at you for a long second, then ask{s} for another.|You win with a feint you stole from watching {here}. {They} notice{s}, and look{s} annoyed and pleased at once.|You spar with {here} as if you'd done it for years, because from the gallery, you have. {They} win{s} narrowly and look{s} at you differently.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0d7472] = {你从{here}手里拿下一局。{They}盯着你看了好一会儿，然后要求再来一局。|你用看{here}练习时偷学的虚招赢了。{They}看出来了，又恼又高兴。|你和{here}过招，就像练了好多年，在看台上你确实看了好多年。{They}险胜，看你的眼神不一样了。}\n# en: {{here} puts you on the floorboards, politely. From down there the ceiling's scorch marks make a sort of map.|{here} disarms you so neatly that the dummies seem to wince. Your wand skids under the gallery stairs.|{here} lands a spell that singes the ceiling and you with it. You add your own mark to the map.}\nopt.left.say.2 [96e65d] = {{here}很有礼貌地把你放倒在地板上。从下面看，天花板上的焦痕像是一张地图。|{here}缴械缴得干净利落，连假人都像是缩了一下。你的魔杖滑进了看台楼梯底下。|{here}一道咒语打得天花板焦了，你也一起焦了。你给那张“地图”添上了自己的一笔。}\n# en: Watch and take notes\nopt.right.label [d829ad] = 看着记笔记\n# en: {You fill a page with what {here} does with {their} feet. You'll never get to try it, and you know it.|You sketch {here}'s guard from above. On paper it has a gap. On the floor it doesn't.|You take notes from your groove at the rail. Your notes on {here} could fill a book now, and might.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0327ff] = {你把{here}的步法记了满满一页。你永远不会有机会试，你也知道。|你从上方画下{here}的防守架势。纸上有个破绽。场上没有。|你倚着栏杆上那道属于你的凹槽记笔记。你关于{here}的笔记已经能写成一本书了，说不定真会。}\n\n@event duel_hall_humming_circle\n# en: The Humming Circle\ntitle [1a4455] = 嗡鸣的法阵\n# en: {The evening bouts are over. At the near end of the hall the warding circle is still humming.|The warding circle is humming. Whoever's drilling at the far end isn't looking your way.|The circle hums. You know its tune by now.} {final_year: Soon someone else will be sneaking in here after hours.|{A practice wand lies on the rack, still warm.|The steward's broom leans by the door, watching.|Tonight the ceiling stain looks like a dragon.}}\ntext [52ec0c] = {晚上的比试结束了。大厅近端的护界法阵还在嗡嗡作响。|护界法阵在嗡鸣。远处练习的人没往你这边看。|法阵在嗡鸣。你已经熟悉它的调子了。} {final_year: 很快就会有别人在闭馆后溜进这里了。|{架子上放着一根练习魔杖，还是温的。|管理员的扫帚靠在门边，像在盯着你。|今晚天花板上的污渍看起来像一条龙。}}\n# en: A dark duelling hall lit only by a ring of faintly glowing runes on the floor.\nscene [939412] = 漆黑的决斗厅，只有地上一圈微微发光的符文照明。\n# en: Step in and try a hard one\nopt.left.label [9c5c46] = 走进去试个难的\n# en: {The spell comes out clean and the circle drinks it. Somewhere a dummy loses its head and nobody ever finds out how.|You try the thing you saw a fourth-year do. It works. You tell no one, and walk back grinning.|You cast the hard one and the circle hums the note back to you, a little higher. You think that's approval.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2c0d44] = {咒语干净利落地打出，法阵把它吞了下去。某处一个假人掉了脑袋，没人知道是怎么回事。|你试了一招见四年级生用过的。成功了。你谁也没告诉，一路笑着走回去。|你施出了那道难的，法阵把音调哼回给你，稍高了一点。你想那是赞许。}\n# en: {The circle hands your spell straight back to you. Your eyebrows will regrow.|The circle flares, the dummies rattle, and you leave very quickly before the steward arrives.|The circle swallows half your spell and spits out the other half in your direction. The steward's broom falls over, laughing.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f848f6] = {法阵把你的咒语原样还给了你。你的眉毛会长回来的。|法阵一闪，假人们哗啦作响，你赶在管理员来之前飞快地溜了。|法阵吞了你半道咒语，把另一半朝你吐了回来。管理员的扫帚倒了，像在大笑。}\n# en: Sweep up for the steward\nopt.right.label [b29618] = 替管理员扫地\n# en: {You sweep up a winter's worth of ash. The steward pays you a few crowns and a grunt that might be thanks.|You sweep. The circle hums at you the whole time, like a cat that wants to be let out.|You sweep in time with the hum. The steward says you're the only one who does it properly, and pays you for the compliment.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1224ce] = {你扫掉了一整个冬天的灰烬。管理员给了你几枚克朗，外加一声可能是道谢的哼哼。|你扫着地。法阵一直冲你嗡嗡叫，像一只想出门的猫。|你随着嗡嗡声的节拍扫地。管理员说只有你扫得像样，还为这句夸奖付了你钱。}\n\n@event greenhouses_warm_glass\n# en: The Warm Glass\ntitle [4d5441] = 温暖的玻璃\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Outside it's snowing. Inside, the glass runs with warm water and everything smells of earth.|{season == \"summer\": The greenhouses are sweltering, and everyone has retreated to the far end where the vents are.|The greenhouses steam gently. Someone is singing to the mandrakes at the far end.}} {You find a dry corner by the ferns, out of everyone's way.|Someone has left a cushion in your corner by the ferns.|The ferns lean toward you when you come in, as they do for you now.}\ntext [4ff1a0] = {season == \"winter\": 外面在下雪。里面，玻璃上淌着温热的水珠，到处是泥土的气味。|{season == \"summer\": 温室里闷热难当，大家都躲到了有通风口的那一头。|温室里轻轻蒸腾着水汽。远处有人在给曼德拉草唱歌。}} {你在蕨类旁找到一个干爽的角落，不碍任何人的事。|有人在你蕨类旁的那个角落里留了个垫子。|你一进门，蕨类就朝你探过来，如今它们一向如此。}\n# en: A student reading on an upturned pot among tall ferns inside a steamy glass greenhouse, snow on the panes outside.\nscene [9eaa5e] = 雾气蒸腾的玻璃温室里，一个学生坐在倒扣的花盆上，在高高的蕨类间读书，窗外落着雪。\n# en: Do your reading in the warm\nopt.left.label [2d902a] = 在暖和处读书\n# en: {You get through a whole chapter. The pages curl in the damp and you come out smelling of moss.|You read until the steam makes the ink run. What's left of the chapter sticks.|You read with the ferns leaning in to listen. The chapter goes in easily, and the moss smell stays a day.}\nopt.left.say.1 [45e294] = {你读完了整整一章。书页在潮气里卷了边，你出来时一身青苔味。|你一直读到水汽把墨迹洇开。剩下的那部分章节倒是记住了。|你读书时蕨类都凑过来听。这一章读得很顺，青苔味留了一整天。}\n# en: Nap on the potting bench\nopt.right.label [8ac69b] = 在盆栽台上打个盹\n# en: {You wake with a seed tray for a pillow and a first-year staring at you. By supper it's a story.|You wake to a mandrake humming a lullaby. You choose not to tell anyone. Someone tells everyone.|You nap on your cushion among the ferns. Someone has put a sign by your feet. It says \"Do not water\".}\nopt.right.say.1 [bca74a] = {你醒来时枕着育苗盘，一个一年级生正盯着你。到晚饭时，这已经成了一段故事。|你在曼德拉草哼的摇篮曲里醒来。你决定谁也不告诉。有人告诉了所有人。|你在蕨丛里枕着你的垫子打盹。有人在你脚边放了块牌子，上面写着“请勿浇水”。}\n# en: Let the hazel find water\nopt.up.label [dd46b5] = 让榛木找水\n# en: {The hazel dips over a bed of wilting seedlings. Under it you find a cracked pipe, and an hour of mud later the bed is drinking.|The wand tugs you to the far corner, where a rain barrel has silted up. You clear it with your sleeves rolled and come out filthy and pleased.|The hazel points at the mandrakes, firmly. They have been overwatered. You move them; they sulk; the wand seems satisfied.}\nopt.up.say.1 [3a71db] = {榛木在一床蔫掉的幼苗上方沉了下去。你在底下找到一根裂开的水管，一个小时满身泥之后，苗床喝上了水。|魔杖拽着你走到远处的角落，那里的雨水桶淤住了。你卷起袖子清理干净，出来时一身脏，心里高兴。|榛木坚定地指向曼德拉草。它们被浇多了水。你把它们挪开；它们闹脾气；魔杖似乎很满意。}\n\n@event greenhouses_repotting\n# en: Repotting Day\ntitle [5b9bc1] = 换盆日\n# en: {here} is up to the elbows in compost, repotting {season == \"spring\": seedlings that have outgrown everything|{season == \"autumn\": bulbs for the winter|something with more teeth than leaves}}. {\"Hands?\" {they} ask{s}.|{They} hand{s} you a trowel before you've said hello.|There's a trowel on the hook that has come to be yours. {here} nods at it.}\ntext [36e480] = {here}正把胳膊埋在堆肥里，给{season == \"spring\": 长得什么都装不下的幼苗|{season == \"autumn\": 准备过冬的球根|一株牙齿比叶子还多的东西}}换盆。{“搭把手？”{they}问。|你还没打招呼，{They}就递给你一把小铲子。|钩子上有一把小铲子，渐渐成了你的。{here}朝它点点头。}\n# en: A student elbow-deep in compost at a greenhouse bench, handing a trowel to someone arriving.\nscene [c4461e] = 温室工作台边，一个学生胳膊深埋在堆肥里，把小铲子递给刚到的人。\n# en: Get your hands dirty\nopt.left.label [f6a380] = 动手干活\n# en: {You repot a whole row. Something nips your thumb in thanks.|You're filthy, nipped and oddly happy. {here} says you have good hands for roots.|You take your trowel off its hook and repot the difficult ones. Nothing bites you, which {here} says is a first.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5df3ab] = {你给一整排换了盆。有什么东西咬了一下你的拇指，算是道谢。|你满身泥污，被咬了几口，却莫名开心。{here}说你天生一双侍弄根的手。|你从钩子上取下你的小铲子，给难伺候的那几株换了盆。没有东西咬你，{here}说这还是头一回。}\n# en: Ask questions, stay clean\nopt.right.label [d36613] = 光提问，不沾手\n# en: {You learn a great deal about soil. {here} learns that you're afraid of it.|You take notes. {here} gives you a look over the trowel that says notes are not hands.|You ask your questions with your trowel in your hand, which {here} counts as progress.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6e48c2] = {你学到了很多关于泥土的知识。{here}则学到了你怕泥土。|你记着笔记。{here}隔着小铲子看了你一眼，意思是笔记不能代替手。|你手里握着小铲子提问，{here}认为这算是进步了。}\n\n@event greenhouses_cuttings\n# en: The Cuttings Bench\ntitle [c5d8ff] = 插条台\n# en: {A row of silver-leaf cuttings is drying on the bench|A tray of night-orchid cuttings sits by the door|A bundle of moonpetal cuttings is wrapped in damp cloth}, and Pell & Daughters pays well for that sort of thing. {Nobody is watching. The snapdragons are.|The snapdragons have seen you here before. They remember.|The snapdragons snap at you on principle now.}\ntext [31a502] = {台子上晾着一排银叶插条|门边放着一盘夜兰插条|一捆月瓣花插条裹在湿布里}，佩尔父女药铺收这种东西出价不低。{没人在看。金鱼草在看。|金鱼草以前见过你。它们记得。|金鱼草现在出于原则见你就咬。}\n# en: A greenhouse bench of labelled cuttings drying in rows, a row of snapdragons with actual teeth watching from their pots.\nscene [4762ee] = 温室工作台上一排排晾着的插条，带标签，一排长着真牙齿的金鱼草在盆里盯着。\n# en: Pocket a cutting\nopt.left.label [44bf20] = 偷偷揣一根插条\n# en: {Pell's counts the leaves twice and pays without a question. You don't look at the snapdragons on your next visit.|It sells for more than you expected, which somehow makes it worse.|Moonpetal sells for twice the others. Pell's doesn't ask where it came from, which you notice.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d43598] = {佩尔家把叶子数了两遍，二话不说付了钱。下次来时你没敢看金鱼草。|它卖的价钱比你想的还高，不知怎么反而更糟了。|月瓣花的价钱是别的两倍。佩尔家没问是从哪儿来的，这一点你注意到了。}\n# en: {A snapdragon shrieks. Three Verdant students turn around at once. You put it back with a cough that convinces no one.|Your pocket rustles at the wrong moment. You spend the rest of the visit apologising to a fern.|The snapdragons have been waiting for this. They raise the alarm before your hand reaches the cloth.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b74995] = {一株金鱼草尖叫起来。三个青翠社的学生同时回头。你干咳一声把它放回去，谁也没被说服。|你的口袋在最不该的时候沙沙作响。这一趟剩下的时间，你都在向一株蕨类道歉。|金鱼草们就等着这一刻。你的手还没碰到湿布，它们就叫了起来。}\n# en: Feed the snapdragons\nopt.right.label [22dfda] = 喂金鱼草\n# en: {They take the food, then a little of your finger. You learn which end to hold the tongs by.|Fed, the snapdragons purr like kettles. One of them still bites you, out of habit.|You feed the snapdragons and they snap anyway, gently, out of principle. You've come to find it friendly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ff1769] = {它们吃了食物，又顺带吃了一点你的手指。你学会了夹子该拿哪一头。|吃饱的金鱼草像水壶一样咕噜咕噜。还是有一株咬了你，出于习惯。|你喂了金鱼草，它们还是咬了，轻轻地，出于原则。你已经觉得这挺友好的了。}\n\n@event flight_field_cheating_wind\n# en: The Cheating Wind\ntitle [c14839] = 耍赖的风\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The flight field is white, the hoops wearing caps of snow.|{season == \"spring\": The field is soft with new grass and the wind is playful.|{The field is empty but for the wind, practising on its own.|A lone first-year is flying very careful circles.|A pair of fourth-years are racing low over the grass.}}} {The school brooms in the shed are, at best, opinionated.|Your usual broom is in the shed, the one that pulls left.|The broom that pulls left now pulls right, out of spite.}\ntext [e42d53] = {season == \"winter\": 飞行场一片雪白，圈门都戴上了雪帽。|{season == \"spring\": 场地铺满柔软的新草，风很调皮。|{场上空无一人，只有风在自己练习。|一个一年级生在小心翼翼地绕圈飞。|两个四年级生贴着草皮比赛。}}} {棚里的学校扫帚，往好里说，也是很有主见。|你常用的那把扫帚在棚里，就是总往左偏的那把。|那把往左偏的扫帚现在往右偏了，纯属赌气。}\n# en: A long green field with tall hoops at either end, one student wobbling on a school broom against a gusting wind.\nscene [7ab1d7] = 两端立着高高圈门的长条绿地上，一个学生骑着学校扫帚在阵风里摇摇晃晃。\n# en: Practise your landings\nopt.left.label [516481] = 练习降落\n# en: {A dozen landings. A few of them on purpose. The grass keeps a careful record of the rest.|The wind waits until you're nearly down, then shoves. You land in a hoop instead. Technically, a goal.|Your broom pulls right; you lean left. The landings come out straight, for once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fba672] = {降落了十几次，其中几次是故意的。草地仔细记下了其余的。|风等你快落地时才推了一把。你落进了圈门里。严格来说，算进球。|你的扫帚往右拽，你就往左倾。这一回，降落都是直的。}\n# en: Hire a decent broom\nopt.right.label [cee8bc] = 租一把像样的扫帚\n# en: {The good broom goes where you look, not where it feels like. For an hour, flying is easy and the field is yours.|You fly the length of the field and back with the wind on your side for once. It costs a week's sweets.|You hire the good broom and leave the spiteful one in the shed. It will be waiting next time, and it will remember.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3e2550] = {好扫帚会去你看的地方，而不是它想去的地方。有那么一个钟头，飞行很轻松，整个场地都是你的。|你顺着风飞了个来回，这可是头一回。代价是一周的零食钱。|你租了好扫帚，把那把记仇的留在棚里。下次它还会在那儿等着，而且会记得。}\n\n@event flight_field_pickup_race\n# en: The Pick-Up Race\ntitle [22c25a] = 临时赛\n# en: {A pick-up race is forming at the far hoops. Three brooms, two rules, no referee.|The pick-up racers are back. The rules have grown to four.|The pick-up race is a fixture now. Someone has painted a finish line, crookedly.} {A second-year from Emberhall is taking bets.|A Brasswick student has bolted a lantern to their broom-tail.|A Rootward student is flying barefoot, for grip.}\ntext [9c1159] = {远处的圈门那边正在凑一场临时赛。三把扫帚，两条规则，没有裁判。|临时赛的人又来了。规则涨到了四条。|临时赛已经成了固定节目。有人歪歪扭扭地画了一条终点线。} {一个烬厅的二年级生在坐庄。|一个铜芯的学生在扫帚尾巴上拴了盏灯笼。|一个根庐的学生光着脚飞，说是为了抓得牢。}\n# en: Three students on brooms jostling at a crooked painted start line, a fourth holding up a scarf as a starting flag.\nscene [66b550] = 三个骑扫帚的学生在一条歪斜的起跑线前推搡，第四个人举着围巾当发令旗。\n# en: Borrow a broom and race\nopt.left.label [18a85c] = 借把扫帚去比赛\n# en: {You win by a broom's length and a lot of shouting. The second-year taking bets has to pay out and never forgives you.|You take the last hoop sideways and cross first. Someone adds a new rule, about you.|You cross the crooked finish line first, barefoot Rootward student close behind. Your name goes up on the post in chalk.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0cdf5d] = {你以一扫帚之差、外加一片叫喊声获胜。那个坐庄的二年级生只好赔钱，从此再没原谅你。|你侧着身子穿过最后一个圈门，第一个冲线。有人加了一条新规则，是关于你的。|你第一个冲过那条歪歪扭扭的终点线，光脚的根庐学生紧随其后。你的名字被用粉笔写上了柱子。}\n# en: {You finish last, and partly in a hedge.|The wind takes your side, then changes its mind. You walk the broom back to the start.|You lose to someone flying barefoot. You consider the lesson in this and decide against it.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3d662a] = {你最后一名到达，有一部分是在树篱里到达的。|风先站在你这边，然后又改了主意。你推着扫帚走回起点。|你输给了一个光脚飞的人。你想了想这里面的道理，决定不去想。}\n# en: Bet a few crowns on the outsider\nopt.right.label [abc686] = 押几枚克朗给冷门\n# en: {The outsider wins on a gust nobody else saw coming. You are suddenly very good at betting.|The outsider comes in first by a nose. You toast the nose.|You bet on the barefoot flyer. Grip, it turns out, is everything.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a6209c] = {冷门借着一阵谁也没料到的风赢了。你突然成了押注高手。|冷门以一个鼻尖的优势夺冠。你为那个鼻尖干杯。|你押了光脚的那位。原来抓地力才是一切。}\n# en: {The outsider stops halfway to admire the view. Your crowns go with them.|Your flyer is disqualified for a rule invented on the spot. You don't argue. It's a good rule.|Your outsider's broom throws them at the first hoop. The crowns stay where they landed, which is not with you.}\nopt.right.say.2 [97ab34] = {冷门飞到一半停下来欣赏风景。你的克朗也跟着去了。|你押的人因为一条当场发明的规则被取消资格。你没争辩。那是条好规则。|你押的冷门在第一个圈门就被扫帚甩了下来。克朗留在了它们落下的地方，反正不是你这儿。}\n\n@event flight_field_long_grass\n# en: The Long Grass\ntitle [f6b1e4] = 长草丛\n# en: {The long grass at the edge of the flight field catches everything that falls off a broom.|Someone has planted a little flag in the long grass, to mark where things land.|You've come to think of the long grass as the academy's real lost-and-found.|Somebody has started a ledger of the long grass, pinned to the fence. Today's entry is yours to write.} Today it's {a velvet glove|a letter with no envelope|a single boot, laced|someone's notes for an exam}. {season == \"autumn\": The leaves are trying to bury it.|{season == \"winter\": It's half under frost.|A grasshopper is sitting on it.}}\ntext [ea2f70] = {飞行场边的长草丛接住了所有从扫帚上掉下来的东西。|有人在长草丛里插了面小旗，标出东西落下的地方。|你已经把长草丛当成学院真正的失物招领处了。|有人在篱笆上钉了一本长草丛登记簿。今天这一条该你来写。}今天是{一只天鹅绒手套|一封没有信封的信|一只系好鞋带的靴子|某人的考试笔记}。{season == \"autumn\": 落叶正想把它埋起来。|{season == \"winter\": 它有一半埋在霜里。|一只蚱蜢正坐在上面。}}\n# en: The long grass at the edge of a flight field, scattered with things dropped from brooms, a student crouching to look.\nscene [b64b13] = 飞行场边的长草丛里散落着从扫帚上掉下的东西，一个学生蹲下来查看。\n# en: Keep what you find\nopt.left.label [86edf0] = 捡到就归我\n# en: {The owner lands right beside you while you're still holding it. There's a short, very long silence.|A second-year marches over the grass shouting \"Thief!\" at the sky in general and you in particular.|A boy hops across the field on one foot, shouting. He wants his boot back, and he wants to know why you're holding it.|The owner of the notes finds you reading them. Their exam is tomorrow. It turns out yours is too.}\nopt.left.say.1 [519834] = {你还拿在手里，失主就在你旁边落了地。接下来是一阵短暂而又漫长的沉默。|一个二年级生大步穿过草地，冲着天空泛泛地大喊“小偷！”，具体来说是冲着你。|一个男生单脚跳着穿过场地，一边喊。他要回他的靴子，还想知道你为什么拿着它。|笔记的主人发现你在读它们。对方明天考试。原来你明天也考。}\n# en: {Finders, keepers. The grass doesn't argue, and neither does your conscience, much.|Nobody comes looking. You tell yourself that settles it.|It's a good boot. You'll need another one, but it's a start.|The notes are better than yours. You tell yourself that settles it.}\nopt.left.say.2 [96b460] = {谁捡到归谁。草丛没意见，你的良心也没多大意见。|没人来找。你告诉自己这就算了结了。|是只好靴子。你还需要另一只，不过总算开了个头。|那份笔记比你的好。你告诉自己这就算了结了。}\n# en: Carry it to the porter's lodge\nopt.right.label [e8195a] = 送到门房去\n# en: {It's a long walk round the field. The porter writes it in a book and gives you a look of mild surprise.|The porter thanks you with a peppermint from a jar that has clearly been open since the founders.|The porter puts the boot on a shelf of single boots, all left feet. This one is a right. He looks almost moved.|The porter reads the first page of the notes, says \"Hm,\" and files them under urgent.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e772d8] = {绕场一圈可不近。门房把它记进本子，略带惊讶地看了你一眼。|门房从一个显然自创始者时代就开着的罐子里掏出一颗薄荷糖谢你。|门房把靴子放到一排单只的靴子旁边，那些全是左脚的。这只是右脚。他看上去几乎有点感动。|门房读了笔记的第一页，“嗯”了一声，归进了加急一栏。}\n\n@event stables_owl_loft\n# en: The Owl Loft\ntitle [2be609] = 猫头鹰阁楼\n# en: {Up the ladder, above the hippogriffs, is a hay loft full of owls and quiet.|The owls open one eye each, decide you're furniture, and go back to sleep.|The owls have stopped opening their eyes for you. You've been accepted.} {season == \"winter\": The warmth of the animals rises through the boards.|{Rain ticks on the roof.|A stray letter has fallen into the hay.|The griffon chick is complaining somewhere below.}}\ntext [449aa4] = {爬上梯子，在鹰头马身有翼兽的上方，是一间满是猫头鹰和安静的干草阁楼。|猫头鹰们各睁开一只眼，认定你是件家具，又睡了。|猫头鹰们已经懒得为你睁眼了。你被接纳了。} {season == \"winter\": 动物们的暖意透过木板升上来。|{雨点滴答敲着屋顶。|一封走失的信掉进了干草里。|楼下的狮鹫幼崽在什么地方抱怨。}}\n# en: A hay loft in old stables, rows of sleepy owls on the rafters above a student reading by a lantern.\nscene [d922b6] = 老马厩的干草阁楼里，椽子上一排排睡眼惺忪的猫头鹰，下面一个学生就着灯笼读书。\n# en: Read up among the owls\nopt.left.label [928968] = 在猫头鹰中间读书\n# en: {You read until the light goes. The hay gets in everywhere, and the chapter gets in too.|An owl reads over your shoulder for a whole page, then hoots, pointedly, at a footnote.|You read until the griffon below stops complaining. The owls don't stir. You belong up here now.}\nopt.left.say.1 [521bc5] = {你一直读到天黑。干草钻得到处都是，书里的内容也钻进了脑子。|一只猫头鹰趴在你肩上读了整整一页，然后意味深长地冲一条脚注叫了一声。|你一直读到楼下的狮鹫不再抱怨。猫头鹰们动也不动。现在你属于这里了。}\n# en: Help sort the owl post\nopt.right.label [37a567] = 帮忙分拣猫头鹰邮件\n# en: {You sort a dropped bundle of post by hand. One owl seems to know which letters you're going to misfile.|You deliver three soggy letters to their owners on the way back. One of them hugs you, which you weren't ready for.|You sort the owl post by the light of the hatch. An owl drops a letter in your lap; it's for you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [968d35] = {你亲手整理了一捆掉落的信件。有只猫头鹰似乎早就知道你会放错哪几封。|回去路上你把三封湿漉漉的信交给了收信人。其中一个人抱住了你，你完全没准备好。|你借着天窗的光整理猫头鹰邮件。一只猫头鹰往你膝上丢了一封信，是给你的。}\n\n@event stables_brask_rounds\n# en: Brask's Rounds\ntitle [11370d] = 布拉斯克巡厩\n# en: {Professor Brask is on her rounds, telling each hippogriff she's a very good girl. She tells you to mind the bucket.|Brask is on her rounds. She nods at you, which is more than most get.|Brask greets you by name now, somewhere after the owls.} {season == \"spring\": There are new hippogriff foals, all legs and beak.|{A hippogriff is sulking about its feathers.|The feed is late and everyone is cross.|The stable cat is asleep in the oats.}}\ntext [794b64] = {布拉斯克教授正在巡厩，挨个告诉每只鹰头马身有翼兽它是个好姑娘。她让你当心水桶。|布拉斯克在巡厩。她冲你点了点头，这比大多数人得到的都多。|布拉斯克现在会叫你的名字了，排在猫头鹰后面。} {season == \"spring\": 新生的小兽崽全是腿和喙。|{一只鹰头马身有翼兽在为自己的羽毛闹脾气。|饲料送晚了，大家都很暴躁。|马厩的猫睡在燕麦堆里。}}\n# en: A burly professor stroking a hippogriff's beak in a stable aisle, ignoring a student holding two feed buckets.\nscene [5fb5c2] = 魁梧的教授在马厩过道里抚摸一只鹰头马身有翼兽的喙，无视旁边拎着两桶饲料的学生。\n# en: Ask Brask a question\nopt.left.label [3880c8] = 向布拉斯克请教\n# en: {She answers it at length, to the hippogriff. You take notes anyway.|\"Good question,\" she says, to the owl. You decide that counts.|She answers you directly, which has never happened. The stable cat wakes up to see what's going on.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bc27a8] = {她详详细细地回答了，对着那只鹰头马身有翼兽。你还是记了笔记。|“好问题，”她对猫头鹰说。你决定这也算数。|她直接回答了你，这可是从没有过的事。马厩的猫醒过来，想看看出了什么事。}\n# en: Carry the feed buckets\nopt.right.label [2dfcee] = 帮忙提饲料桶\n# en: {Every stall, two buckets each. Brask says nothing, which is her way of saying thank you.|A hippogriff bows to you over its bucket. Brask looks almost jealous.|You carry the buckets and work around the cat in the oats. Brask hands you the last bucket without being asked.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d25cf5] = {每个隔间两桶。布拉斯克一言不发，这就是她道谢的方式。|一只鹰头马身有翼兽隔着饲料桶向你鞠了一躬。布拉斯克看起来几乎有点嫉妒。|你提着桶，绕开燕麦里的猫干活。布拉斯克没等你开口，就把最后一桶递给了你。}\n\n@event stables_griffon_sulk\n# en: The Sulking Griffon\ntitle [16bcc6] = 闹别扭的狮鹫\n# en: {The griffon chick is sulking in the corner stall, feathers puffed, refusing breakfast.|The griffon chick is sulking again. It's bigger this time, and so is the sulk.|The griffon is hardly a chick any more, and still sulks like one. It watches you come in.} {year == 1: The sign on the stall says not to hand-feed it, underlined twice.|{The sign on the stall now has a drawing of a bitten hand.|The sign on the stall has been underlined until the paper tore.|Someone has added \"I mean it\" to the sign, in Brask's hand.}}\ntext [05bcf4] = {狮鹫幼崽缩在角落的隔间里生闷气，羽毛炸着，不肯吃早饭。|狮鹫幼崽又在生闷气。它长大了些，闷气也大了些。|那只狮鹫已经算不上幼崽了，生起闷气来还和从前一样。它看着你进门。} {year == 1: 隔间上的牌子写着不许手喂，下面画了两道线。|{隔间的牌子上现在画了一只被咬的手。|隔间牌子上的线被画到纸都破了。|有人在牌子上添了句“我是认真的”，是布拉斯克的笔迹。}}\n# en: A griffon chick with puffed-up feathers glaring from the corner of a stall, a student holding out a morsel at arm's length.\nscene [63efe8] = 一只羽毛炸起的狮鹫幼崽在隔间角落里怒视，一个学生伸直胳膊递出一小块食物。\n# en: Offer it breakfast by hand\nopt.left.label [1ec395] = 亲手喂它早饭\n# en: {It considers you for a long time, then takes the morsel with great delicacy. You feel chosen.|It eats from your hand and then, briefly, leans on you. You don't move for ten minutes.|It takes the morsel and then nudges your hand for another. Brask, passing, crosses out \"I mean it\".}\nopt.left.say.1 [b7e2e4] = {它打量了你很久，然后极其斯文地叼走了那块食物。你觉得自己被选中了。|它从你手里吃了东西，然后短暂地靠在你身上。你十分钟没敢动。|它叼走了食物，又拱拱你的手要第二块。布拉斯克路过，划掉了“我是认真的”。}\n# en: {It takes the morsel, a corner of your sleeve and a nip of your thumb. The sign was right.|It turns its back on you so pointedly that a passing owl looks embarrassed, and its tail catches you hard across the shins on the way round.|It takes the morsel and your glove, and then the sign agrees with it. Brask adds a second underline.}\nopt.left.say.2 [aba662] = {它叼走了食物、你袖子的一角，还啄了一下你的拇指。牌子说得没错。|它转过身去背对你，刻意得连路过的猫头鹰都替你尴尬；转身时它的尾巴狠狠扫过你的小腿。|它叼走了食物和你的手套，牌子跟它站在了一边。布拉斯克又加了一道下划线。}\n# en: Leave it to its sulk\nopt.right.label [bcf991] = 随它生闷气\n# en: {You read the notice about griffon moods instead. It is mostly a list of things that will not help.|You leave it alone. It looks, as you go, a little disappointed you gave up.|You leave it to sulk. It follows you along the bars, sulking at a closer range.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a4d095] = {你转而读起了关于狮鹫脾气的告示。上面大部分是一张“这些都没用”的清单。|你没去打扰它。你走的时候，它看上去有点失望你就这么放弃了。|你由它生闷气。它沿着栏杆跟着你，近距离地继续生闷气。}\n\n@event boathouse_jetty_end\n# en: The End of the Jetty\ntitle [d7bf11] = 栈桥尽头\n# en: {{date} is at the end of the jetty, boots over the water. {They} move{s} over without a word.|The end of the jetty has become your place for saying things. Today {date} is already there.|You know which plank creaks. {date} doesn't, and finds it.} {season == \"winter\": The lake has frozen round the posts and creaks like an old house.|{The rowing boats knock gently together.|A heron fishes in the shallows, pretending not to listen.|The village lamps blink on across the water.}}\ntext [c3f932] = {{date}坐在栈桥尽头，靴子悬在水面上。{They}一句话没说，挪出了位置。|栈桥尽头成了你说心里话的地方。今天{date}已经在那儿了。|你知道哪块木板会响。{date}不知道，一脚就踩上了。} {season == \"winter\": 湖水在桩子周围结了冰，像老房子一样吱嘎作响。|{划艇轻轻地互相碰撞。|一只苍鹭在浅水里捕鱼，假装没在听。|对岸小镇的灯一盏盏亮起来。}}\n# en: Two people sitting side by side at the end of a wooden jetty, boots over dark water, a lantern between them.\nscene [738e69] = 两个人并肩坐在木栈桥尽头，靴子悬在黑色的水面上，中间放着一盏灯笼。\n# en: Hire a boat and row out\nopt.left.label [d01173] = 租条船划出去\n# en: {season == \"winter\": You can't row on ice, so you sit in a beached boat and pretend. {date} calls it a fine voyage.|{You row badly, {date} laughs, and then neither of you is rowing at all.|Out in the middle the shore goes quiet. {date} says your name like the end of a sentence.|You row out until the village lamps are behind you. {date} trails a hand in the water and doesn't say anything for a long time.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [726e2f] = {season == \"winter\": 冰上没法划船，你们就坐在搁浅的船里假装。{date}说这是一趟不错的航行。|{你划得很糟，{date}笑你，然后谁也不划了。|划到湖心，岸上的声音都静了。{date}念你的名字，像在说一句话的结尾。|你们一直划到村里的灯火落在身后。{date}把一只手垂进水里，很久都没说话。}}\n# en: Stay on the jetty and talk\nopt.right.label [dedd57] = 留在栈桥上聊天\n# en: {final_year: You talk about where you'll both be next year. Neither of you says \"together\", and both of you hear it.|{You talk until the lantern gutters. The jetty is cold and you don't notice until you stand.|You say one of the things you couldn't say indoors. {date} listens all the way to the end.|You stay on the jetty, on the plank that creaks. {date} says it sounds like you, and won't explain.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [8e2da1] = {final_year: 你们聊起明年各自会在哪里。谁也没说“一起”，可两个人都听见了。|{你们一直聊到灯笼的火苗摇摇欲灭。栈桥很冷，站起来时你才发觉。|你说出了一件在屋里说不出口的事。{date}一直听到了最后。|你们待在栈桥上，就坐在那块会吱嘎响的木板上。{date}说它听起来像你，却不肯解释。}}\n# en: Let Edmund's wand hum\nopt.up.label [a95a6b] = 让埃德蒙的魔杖鸣响\n# en: {Over the water the silver wand hums one low note. {date} goes quiet to listen, and so do you, until the lantern gutters.|The wand hums, and a ring spreads on the still water as if something answered. {date} takes your hand without asking why.|You hold the wand out over the lake and it hums its one note. {date} says it sounds like someone remembering. You think so too.}\nopt.up.say.1 [793b14] = {银魔杖在水面上哼出一个低音。{date}安静下来听，你也一样，直到灯笼的火苗暗下去。|魔杖鸣响，静水上荡开一圈涟漪，好像有什么在回应。{date}没问为什么，握住了你的手。|你把魔杖伸到湖面上，它哼出它唯一的那个音。{date}说那听起来像有人在回忆。你也这么觉得。}\n\n@event boathouse_green_boat\n# en: The Leaky Green Boat\ntitle [c4a35d] = 漏水的绿船\n# en: {The boathouse smells of tar and old rope. Its rowing boats are mostly leaks held together by paint, the green one worst of all.|Someone has patched the green boat since your last visit, badly, with what looks like a cake tin.|The boathouse knows you. The green boat still leaks.} {Initials are carved into every beam, some so old they've gone soft.|Rain patters on the roof.|A spider has strung a web across the oars, and seems proud of it.}\ntext [c1e87e] = {船屋里是焦油和旧绳子的气味。这里的划艇大多是用油漆粘在一起的漏洞，绿色那条最糟。|自你上次来过，有人给绿船打了补丁，打得很烂，用的像是个蛋糕模子。|船屋认得你。绿船还在漏水。} {每根房梁上都刻着姓名缩写，有些老得已经模糊了。|雨点轻敲着屋顶。|一只蜘蛛在船桨间结了网，看样子很得意。}\n# en: Inside an old wooden boathouse, an upturned green rowing boat on trestles, initials carved into every beam above.\nscene [4e9c10] = 老旧木船屋里，一条绿色划艇倒扣在支架上，上方每根房梁都刻满了姓名缩写。\n# en: Patch the green boat\nopt.left.label [df86c5] = 给绿船打补丁\n# en: {You spend an hour with tar and a mallet. The green boat floats, briefly, with an air of surprise.|Your patch holds for a whole circuit of the boathouse. You have tar in your eyebrows and a certain pride.|You patch the green boat again. It leaks again. You're starting to think that's what it's for.}\nopt.left.say.1 [84066a] = {你拿着焦油和木槌忙了一个钟头。绿船浮了起来，短暂地，带着一脸惊讶。|你的补丁撑过了绕船屋一整圈。你眉毛上沾着焦油，心里有几分骄傲。|你又给绿船打了补丁。它又漏了。你开始觉得这就是它存在的意义。}\n# en: Carve your initials in a beam\nopt.right.label [383067] = 在房梁上刻名字缩写\n# en: The boatman charges a few crowns a beam.\nopt.right.hint [f69342] = 船夫刻一根梁要收几枚克朗。\n# en: {final_year: You pay the boatman and carve them next to a pair from sixty years ago. Somebody, one day, will run a thumb over yours and wonder.|{You pay the boatman, and your penknife snaps on the last letter. The initials stay, slightly wrong, which feels right.|You pay the boatman, find a carving that could almost be yours from long ago, and add your own beneath it.|You pay the boatman and carve your initials under the spider's web. The spider doesn't object.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [037e6f] = {final_year: 你付了船夫钱，把它刻在一对六十年前的缩写旁边。总有一天，会有人用拇指摸过你的，心生好奇。|{你付了船夫钱，最后一个字母刻到一半，小刀断了。缩写就这样留着，有点歪，感觉却刚好。|你付了船夫钱，找到一处很久以前的刻痕，几乎像是你的，于是把自己的刻在它下面。|你付了船夫钱，在蜘蛛网下面刻下了你的缩写。蜘蛛没有异议。}}\n\n@event boathouse_overheard\n# en: Things Said Outside\ntitle [bff476] = 屋外的话\n# en: {Two voices out on the jetty, low and urgent. People come to the boathouse to say what they can't say indoors.|You've found the gap in the boathouse planks where every word on the jetty comes through.|Another quiet conversation on the jetty. You're starting to know the academy by its secrets.} {It sounds like a break-up.|It sounds like an apology that isn't going well.|It sounds like someone confessing to a pudding.}\ntext [2a1a7e] = {栈桥上有两个声音，低沉而急切。人们来船屋，是为了说屋里说不出口的话。|你发现了船屋木板上的一道缝，栈桥上的每个字都从那里漏进来。|栈桥上又一段悄悄话。你开始从秘密里认识这所学院了。} {听起来像是在分手。|听起来像是一次不太顺利的道歉。|听起来像是有人在向一块布丁告白。}\n# en: Two silhouettes talking quietly on a jetty at night, seen through the slats of a boathouse wall.\nscene [fbc1ce] = 夜里两个身影在栈桥上低声交谈，从船屋墙板的缝隙间望去。\n# en: Listen a little longer\nopt.left.label [9863e8] = 再多听一会儿\n# en: {A plank creaks under you. The voices stop. Two very red faces come round the door, and now they know your face too.|An oar slides off its peg with a clatter. Footsteps; a hiss of \"Who's there?\"; you are, unfortunately.|You lean on the plank you know creaks. The pudding confession stops mid-sentence, and two faces you know appear at the door.}\nopt.left.say.1 [827606] = {你脚下一块木板吱嘎一响。说话声停了。两张通红的脸从门边探过来，现在他们也认得你的脸了。|一支船桨哐当一声从钉子上滑落。脚步声；一声低喝“谁在那儿？”；很不幸，是你。|你靠在了那块你明知会响的木板上。对布丁的忏悔戛然而止，两张你认识的脸出现在门口。}\n# en: {You learn more than you wanted to. You'll carry it carefully, and never tell.|It ends well, as it happens. You slip away before anyone has to know you were there.|It's about a pudding, honestly. You leave knowing more than any academy should about one pudding.}\nopt.left.say.2 [88f8a7] = {你听到的比你想听的多。你会小心收好，永远不说出去。|结果是个好结局。你在有人发现之前悄悄溜走了。|真的是关于一个布丁。你离开时，对一个布丁的了解超过了任何学院应有的程度。}\n# en: Cough loudly and leave\nopt.right.label [eb5a76] = 大声咳嗽，走开\n# en: {They wait for you to pass, hushed and grateful. You'll never know how it ended, and that's the price.|You make a great show of looking for an oar. By the time you've found one, they've gone.|You cough and leave. On the path you meet the pudding's owner, and decide not to say anything.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8398fc] = {他们安静地等你走过，满怀感激。你永远不会知道后来怎样了，这就是代价。|你装模作样地找船桨。等你找到时，他们已经走了。|你咳嗽一声走了。在小路上你遇见了布丁的主人，决定什么也不说。}\n\n@event orchard_under_the_trees\n# en: Under the Old Trees\ntitle [42dae6] = 老树之下\n# en: {season == \"spring\": Blossom is coming down like snow, and {date} has some in {their} hair.|{season == \"autumn\": The trees are heavy with apples and wasps, and {date} is reaching for one.|{season == \"winter\": The orchard is bare and silver, and {date} is walking between the trees.|{date} has claimed the best of the orchard shade.}}} {{They} wave{s} you over.|{They} saw you coming before you saw {them}.|The old trees seem to expect you by now. {date} only half does.}\ntext [f17cc4] = {season == \"spring\": 花瓣像雪一样落下，{date}的头发上沾了几片。|{season == \"autumn\": 树上挂满了苹果和黄蜂，{date}正伸手去够一个。|{season == \"winter\": 果园光秃秃的，一片银白，{date}在树间散步。|{date}占了果园里最好的一片树荫。}}} {{They}招手让你过去。|你还没看见{them}，{They}就先看见了你。|老树们似乎已经习惯等你了。{date}倒没那么笃定。}\n# en: Two people beneath an ancient apple tree, blossom drifting down around them like snow.\nscene [f622bf] = 两个人坐在一棵古老的苹果树下，花瓣像雪一样飘落在他们周围。\n# en: Stay until the light goes\nopt.left.label [953fd7] = 待到天黑\n# en: {season == \"spring\": {You stay until the blossom buries your shoes. You miss supper, and you'd do it again.|{date} picks blossom out of your hair, one petal at a time, and takes far too long about it.|You lie in the blossom until you're both freckled with it. {date} says it suits you, and keeps a petal.}|{season == \"winter\": {Your fingers go white, and {date} takes your hands to warm them, and doesn't give them back.|You lean against the same cold trunk until the stars come out over the bare branches.|You walk the bare rows until the frost comes up. {date} breathes on your hands and says it's for practical reasons.}|{You stay until the shadows outgrow the trees. You miss supper. Neither of you mentions it.|The light goes gold, then blue. {date} says something quiet you'll turn over for days.|You stay until the first star. {date} names it after something you said, and won't tell you which thing.}}}\nopt.left.say.1 [53ad5d] = {season == \"spring\": {你们一直待到花瓣埋住了鞋子。你错过了晚饭，下次还会这样。|{date}把花瓣从你头发里一片片挑出来，挑得太慢了。|你们躺在落花里，直到两人身上都缀满了花瓣。{date}说这很衬你，留下了一片。}|{season == \"winter\": {你的手指冻得发白，{date}握住你的手替你暖着，再没还给你。|你们靠着同一根冰冷的树干，直到星星爬上光秃秃的枝头。|你们在光秃秃的树行间走到霜气升起。{date}往你手上呵气，说是出于实际考虑。}|{你们一直待到影子比树还长。你错过了晚饭。谁也没提。|光先变成金色，又变成蓝色。{date}轻声说了句什么，你会反复回味好几天。|你们待到第一颗星出来。{date}用你说过的某句话给它起了名字，却不告诉你是哪句。}}}\n# en: Help in the orchard together\nopt.right.label [1e4a49] = 一起在果园帮忙\n# en: {season == \"autumn\": {You fill two baskets and earn one wasp sting each. The cook gives you a pie, which you share on the steps.|{date} holds the ladder and says nothing useful, and you don't want {them} to.|You pick from the top branches while {date} sorts the basket below. Every good apple goes to you, which you notice.}|{season == \"winter\": {You prune dead wood side by side until your hands ache. {date} stacks it with great seriousness.|You wrap the young trees against the frost. {date} ties every knot twice.|You mend the orchard fence together, one post each. {date}'s post is straighter, and {they} won't stop mentioning it.}|{season == \"spring\": {You carry hives along the rows for the blossom. One bee follows {date} all afternoon.|You clear the grass under the blossom. {date} keeps stopping to look up.|You graft a branch onto an old tree. {date} says you'll both have to come back to see if it takes.}|{You water the young trees from a bucket chain of two. {date} is soaked by the end, and so are you.|You prop up a branch bowed with early apples. {date} holds it, and holds still, and you notice.|You rake the cut grass into rows. {date} lies down in the first row, and the work goes no further.}}}}\nopt.right.say.1 [062ac3] = {season == \"autumn\": {你们装满了两筐，各挨了一下黄蜂蜇。厨娘给了你们一个馅饼，你们坐在台阶上分着吃了。|{date}扶着梯子，什么有用的话也没说，你也不希望{them}说。|你在高处的枝头摘，{date}在下面理篮子。好苹果都留给了你，你注意到了。}|{season == \"winter\": {你们并肩修剪枯枝，直到手都酸了。{date}郑重其事地把枝条码好。|你们给小树裹上防霜的草帘。{date}每个结都系两遍。|你们一起修果园的篱笆，一人一根桩子。{date}那根更直，{they}一直提个不停。}|{season == \"spring\": {你们沿着树行搬蜂箱去授粉。一只蜜蜂跟了{date}一下午。|你们清理花下的杂草。{date}总是停下来抬头看。|你们往一棵老树上嫁接了一根枝条。{date}说你们俩都得回来看看它活没活。}|{你们俩排成一条水桶接力，给小树浇水。最后{date}浑身湿透，你也是。|你们撑起一根被早熟苹果压弯的枝条。{date}扶着它，一动不动，你注意到了。|你们把割下的草耙成一行行。{date}在第一行躺了下来，活就到此为止了。}}}}\n\n@event orchard_windfalls\n# en: Windfalls\ntitle [03d58f] = 落果\n# en: {The orchard trees are older than the towers, and never tidy.|The oldest tree has dropped its fruit in a ring, like a table laid for guests.|You know which tree drops what by now, and which one throws.} {season == \"autumn\": Pears and apples lie bruised in the grass, attended by wasps.|{season == \"winter\": Last year's apples lie frozen, hard as stones.|{season == \"spring\": It's mostly blossom, and the odd stubborn pear.|Early apples lie in the shade, small and sharp.}}}\ntext [7efe1c] = {果园的树比七塔还老，从来不收拾干净。|最老的那棵树把果子落成一圈，像为客人摆好的餐桌。|你现在知道哪棵树掉什么，哪棵树会砸人。} {season == \"autumn\": 梨和苹果磕伤了躺在草里，黄蜂在一旁看守。|{season == \"winter\": 去年的苹果冻在地上，硬得像石头。|{season == \"spring\": 大多是落花，还有几只倔强的梨。|早熟的苹果躺在树荫里，又小又酸。}}}\n# en: A student gathering fallen fruit into a basket under gnarled orchard trees, older than the towers behind them.\nscene [0d2ab4] = 盘曲的老果树下，一个学生把落果捡进篮子，树比身后的高塔还老。\n# en: Gather windfalls to sell\nopt.left.label [0a0959] = 捡落果去卖\n# en: {The cook buys your basket for the cider vats and finds {season == \"winter\": a frozen beetle|a wasp} in it. You both pretend that's normal.|A full basket, {season == \"winter\": numb fingers|two stings} and a crick in your back. The crowns feel earned.|You go straight to the trees that drop the good ones. The cook pays for a full basket and says you have an eye.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6d5b1b] = {厨娘买下你的篮子拿去酿苹果酒，在里面发现了{season == \"winter\": 一只冻僵的甲虫|一只黄蜂}。你们俩都假装这很正常。|满满一篮，{season == \"winter\": 冻僵的手指|两处蜇伤}，外加腰酸。这些克朗挣得踏实。|你径直走向掉好果子的那几棵树。厨娘为满满一篮付了钱，说你眼光不错。}\n# en: Eat one under the oldest tree\nopt.right.label [bfdcad] = 在最老的树下吃一个\n# en: {{season == \"winter\": It's frozen solid. Your teeth will remember this.|There was a wasp in it. There was, briefly, a wasp in you.}|It's sour enough to turn your face inside out, and your stomach complains about it until supper. A passing first-year laughs, and so, eventually, do you.|The one that throws gets you on the shoulder. You eat its apple out of spite, and it's {season == \"winter\": frozen through|full of wasp}, and so is your mood.}\nopt.right.say.1 [60f84f] = {{season == \"winter\": 冻得像石头。你的牙会记住这一口。|里面有只黄蜂。有那么一会儿，黄蜂在你嘴里。}|酸得你五官都皱到了一起，胃一直闹到晚饭。路过的一年级生笑了，最后你也笑了。|那棵会扔果子的树砸中了你的肩膀。你赌气吃了它的苹果，结果{season == \"winter\": 冻透了|满是黄蜂}，你的心情也一样。}\n# en: {It's the best thing you've eaten all month. The old tree creaks, as if pleased.|You eat it slowly. Above you, the oldest tree drops one more, gently, beside your hand.|You eat it under the oldest tree, as you always do now. It drops a leaf on your knee, and you keep it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1bec50] = {这是你这个月吃过最好吃的东西。老树吱呀一声，像是很高兴。|你慢慢地吃。头顶上，最老的那棵树又轻轻落下一个，正落在你手边。|你像现在每次那样，在最老的树下吃。它在你膝上落下一片叶子，你留着了。}\n\n@event orchard_cider_shed\n# en: The Cider Shed\ntitle [b0d03f] = 苹果酒棚\n# en: {In the orchard shed, a few students are running an unofficial cider press.|The lookout at the cider shed has started nodding you in.|The cider shed is a fixture now. Even the lookout seems bored.} {season == \"autumn\": The press groans with this year's apples.|The shed smells of last autumn and wet straw.} {A goat is watching from the hedge, very carefully saying nothing.|Someone has carved a menu into the door.|A prefect's scarf hangs on a nail, unexplained.}\ntext [8c11d7] = {果园的棚子里，几个学生在偷偷经营一台苹果酒榨机。|苹果酒棚望风的人开始点头放你进去了。|苹果酒棚已经成了固定去处。连望风的都显得无聊了。} {season == \"autumn\": 榨机被今年的苹果压得吱嘎作响。|棚里一股去年秋天和湿稻草的味道。} {一只山羊在树篱边看着，非常小心地一言不发。|有人在门上刻了一份酒单。|钉子上挂着一条级长的围巾，来历不明。}\n# en: A tumbledown orchard shed with a wooden cider press inside, three students turning the screw and a fourth keeping watch at the door.\nscene [31b8c3] = 破旧的果园棚子里有一台木制苹果酒榨机，三个学生在拧螺杆，第四个在门口望风。\n# en: Buy a cup\nopt.left.label [e3b76c] = 买一杯\n# en: {A prefect turns up as you raise the cup. Everyone is suddenly very interested in apples.|The lookout whistles too late. A prefect takes your name, then, after some thought, your cup.|The prefect whose scarf it is comes back for it. Everyone looks at the scarf, then at you, holding a cup.}\nopt.left.say.1 [261919] = {你刚举起杯子，一个级长就出现了。大家突然都对苹果产生了浓厚兴趣。|望风的口哨吹晚了。一个级长记下你的名字，想了想，又没收了你的杯子。|那条围巾的主人，一位级长，回来取它了。大家先看看围巾，再看看端着杯子的你。}\n# en: {It's sharp, cold and a little bit fizzy. The day goes golden at the edges.|You drink it on an upturned bucket while someone tells a story about a goose. Excellent cider. Better goose.|You drink it under the prefect's scarf. Nobody asks, and the cider is the best yet.}\nopt.left.say.2 [5b388c] = {酒又酸又凉，还有一点点气泡。这一天的边缘都变成了金色。|你坐在倒扣的桶上喝，有人在讲一只鹅的故事。酒很好。鹅更好。|你在级长的围巾底下喝着。没人问起，这一批酒是到目前为止最好的。}\n# en: Help turn the press, for a share\nopt.right.label [297dd0] = 帮忙榨酒，换一份\n# en: {You turn the screw until your shoulders burn. The second-year in charge teaches you why some apples are better bruised.|You're sticky to the elbows and you've learned more about fermentation than in a term of class.|You turn the press with the regulars now. The second-year gives you a jar of your own to take home.}\nopt.right.say.1 [908468] = {你拧螺杆拧到肩膀火辣辣地疼。主事的二年级生教你为什么有些苹果磕伤了反而更好。|你黏到了胳膊肘，学到的发酵知识比一学期的课还多。|现在你和常客们一起拧压榨机了。那个二年级生给了你一罐属于你自己的，让你带回去。}\n\n@event maud_cottage_kettle\n# en: Tea at Maud's\ntitle [14a007] = 莫德家的茶\n# en: {Old Maud's kettle is nearly boiling, as it always is. \"Sit down, dearie,\" she says. \"You look like a puddle with a worry.\"|The kettle is nearly boiling. Maud has already put out your cup, the chipped one.|Your chipped cup is waiting. Maud says it's been asking after you.|Maud pours before you've sat down. \"Same as ever, dearie,\" she says, and it is.} {Bartholomew is eating a tea towel.|Rain drums on the thatch.|A hen is asleep on the windowsill.|Bees drone in the eaves.}\ntext [6fa262] = {老莫德的水壶快要开了，一向如此。“坐吧，亲爱的，”她说，“你看着像个犯愁的水洼。”|水壶快要开了。莫德已经摆好了你的杯子，那只有缺口的。|你那只有缺口的杯子在等你。莫德说它一直在打听你。|你还没坐下，莫德就倒好了茶。“老样子，亲爱的，”她说，确实如此。} {巴塞洛缪在吃一条茶巾。|雨点敲着茅草屋顶。|一只母鸡睡在窗台上。|蜜蜂在屋檐下嗡嗡作响。}\n# en: A low cottage kitchen, a kettle on the hob, an old woman pouring tea into a chipped cup, a goat's head poking through the open door.\nscene [20892a] = 低矮的小屋厨房，炉上坐着水壶，一位老妇人往有缺口的杯子里倒茶，一只山羊从敞开的门外探进头来。\n# en: Stay for a second cup\nopt.left.label [0720c4] = 再喝一杯\n# en: {Maud tells you a sad story the way other people give a recipe. You walk home late and cold, thinking about it.|\"Never trust a dry spring, dearie,\" she says. You won't understand it for a fortnight.|The second cup becomes a third. It's dark when you leave, and you've only half noticed.|You stay for a second cup and the bees in the eaves put Maud to sleep mid-story. You wash the cups and let yourself out.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2fdeaa] = {莫德像别人讲菜谱那样讲了一个伤心的故事。你很晚才冒着寒气走回去，一路想着它。|“永远别信干燥的春天，亲爱的，”她说。两个星期后你才会明白。|第二杯变成了第三杯。你离开时天已经黑了，你几乎没察觉。|你留下喝第二杯，屋檐下的蜜蜂嗡嗡声让莫德讲着讲着就睡着了。你洗好杯子，自己出了门。}\n# en: Ask her for a remedy\nopt.right.label [72f493] = 向她讨个偏方\n# en: {She brews you something that tastes of ditches. \"Down in one, dearie.\" You feel better at once, and resent it.|\"For what ails you,\" she says, and doesn't say what she thinks that is. It tastes of nettles and of being right.|She hands you a remedy before you've said what's wrong. It works, which is the annoying part.|\"Honey and bee-balm, dearie, and stop worrying.\" It tastes of summer. You stop worrying, briefly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f3e623] = {她给你熬了一碗沟渠味的东西。“一口喝干，亲爱的。”你立刻好多了，并为此愤愤不平。|“治你的毛病，”她说，却没说她觉得你是什么毛病。那味道像荨麻，也像她说对了。|你还没说哪儿不舒服，她就把药递给了你。药管用，烦人的正是这一点。|“蜂蜜加蜂香草，亲爱的，别再发愁了。”尝起来像夏天。你短暂地不再发愁。}\n\n@event maud_cottage_bartholomew\n# en: Bartholomew Is Out\ntitle [e4bd17] = 巴塞洛缪跑了\n# en: {Bartholomew the goat has got out.|Bartholomew is out again, and has learned to lift the gate latch with his teeth.|Bartholomew has escaped so often that the hens no longer scatter.} {slot == \"evening\": Maud doesn't even look up from her knitting.|Maud's note on the door says she's at the greenhouses, and to mind the goat.} {He has eaten half a washing line.|He is standing on the roof of the henhouse, looking pleased.|He is in the cabbages, conducting a slow and thorough war.}\ntext [b77b4f] = {巴塞洛缪跑出来了。|巴塞洛缪又跑出来了，它学会了用牙齿抬门闩。|巴塞洛缪跑得太勤，母鸡们都懒得四散奔逃了。} {slot == \"evening\": 莫德连头都不从针线活上抬。|门上莫德留的字条说她去温室了，让人看好山羊。} {它已经吃掉了半根晾衣绳。|它站在鸡舍顶上，一脸得意。|它在卷心菜地里，打一场缓慢而彻底的战争。}\n# en: A goat standing triumphantly on the roof of a henhouse, chickens scattering, a student approaching with an apple held out.\nscene [d9f8cd] = 一只山羊得意洋洋地站在鸡舍顶上，鸡群四散，一个学生举着苹果靠近。\n# en: Chase him down\nopt.left.label [f61b49] = 把它追回来\n# en: {You corner him by the water butt. He gives up with the air of someone who allowed it. {slot == \"evening\": \"Ta, dearie,\" calls Maud.|You tie him to the gate and leave Maud a note.}|You catch him on the second lap. He chews your sleeve all the way back, as a souvenir.|You chase him out of the cabbages with the ease of long practice. The hens don't even look up.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0d1439] = {你在接雨水的桶边把它堵住了。它投降的样子像是在施舍你。{slot == \"evening\": “谢啦，亲爱的，”莫德喊道。|你把它拴在门上，给莫德留了张字条。}|你在第二圈抓住了它。它一路嚼着你的袖子回去，当作纪念品。|你熟门熟路地把它从卷心菜地里赶了出来。母鸡们连头都没抬。}\n# en: {He leads you twice round the orchard and once through the hen run, then trots home on his own.|You end up in the cabbages. Bartholomew watches you from the roof, chewing, unhurried.|He leads you through the cabbages and leaves you there. The war continues without you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a0e7cf] = {它领着你绕果园跑了两圈，又穿过鸡圈一回，然后自己溜达回家了。|你最后栽进了卷心菜地。巴塞洛缪站在屋顶上看着你，嚼着东西，不慌不忙。|它领你穿过卷心菜地，把你丢在那儿。战争在没有你的情况下继续。}\n# en: Bribe him with your lunch\nopt.right.label [eb4050] = 拿午饭贿赂它\n# en: {He takes your sandwich and follows you home like a gentleman. You go hungry until supper.|Your lunch disappears in three bites. Bartholomew looks at you as if you might be worth knowing.|He takes your lunch and walks home ahead of you. He knows the way better than you do by now.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c1181b] = {它接过你的三明治，像位绅士一样跟你回家。你一直饿到晚饭。|你的午饭三口就没了。巴塞洛缪看着你，像是觉得你或许值得结交。|它吃了你的午饭，走在你前面回家。如今它比你还熟悉这条路。}\n\n@event maud_cottage_top_shelf\n# en: The Top Shelf\ntitle [fd2f93] = 最高一层架子\n# en: {{slot == \"evening\": Maud has stepped out to the hens.|Maud is up at the greenhouses.} The top shelf of jars is right there, every label in her spidery hand.|Maud has stepped out, and the top shelf has gained a jar since you were last here.|You know the top shelf by heart, except for whichever jar Maud added last.} One label reads {\"not for you, dearie\"|\"only in emergencies\"|\"do not shake\"}, and the jar is {glowing faintly|humming|warm to the touch}.\ntext [523727] = {{slot == \"evening\": 莫德出去喂鸡了。|莫德去温室了。}最高一层的罐子就在眼前，每张标签都是她那蜘蛛似的笔迹。|莫德出去了，最高一层比你上次来时多了个罐子。|最高一层你已经烂熟于心，只有莫德最近添的那个罐子除外。}其中一张标签写着{“不是给你的，亲爱的”|“仅限紧急情况”|“切勿摇晃”}，那罐子{发着微光|在嗡嗡作响|摸上去是温的}。\n# en: A high cottage shelf crowded with stoppered jars and handwritten labels, one jar glowing faintly blue.\nscene [c62b98] = 小屋里高高的架子上挤满了塞着瓶塞的罐子和手写标签，其中一罐发着淡淡的蓝光。\n# en: Open it, just a little\nopt.left.label [73d5dd] = 打开一点点\n# en: {Something sweet and blue drifts out and settles in your chest. You feel, briefly, enormous.|A breath of summer rain escapes. You put the lid back on smiling.|It's warm, and so is the air that comes out. You stand in it like a cat in a sunbeam until you hear Maud's step.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fd566a] = {一缕甜甜的蓝色飘出来，落进你的胸口。有那么一会儿，你觉得自己无比庞大。|一口夏雨的气息逃了出来。你笑着把盖子盖了回去。|罐子是热的，飘出来的空气也是。你站在里面，像晒太阳的猫，直到听见莫德的脚步声。}\n# en: {Your eyebrows turn the colour of the label. When Maud sees them, she says nothing whatsoever.|It sneezes. Then you sneeze, for an hour. \"Told you, dearie,\" says Maud later, not unkindly.|You shook it, a little. The label was right. Your hair stands on end until supper.}\nopt.left.say.2 [298202] = {你的眉毛变成了标签的颜色。莫德看见后，一个字也没说。|它打了个喷嚏。然后你打了一个钟头的喷嚏。“早说过了，亲爱的，”莫德后来说，语气并不凶。|你稍微晃了它一下。标签说得没错。你的头发一直竖到晚饭。}\n# en: Dust the shelf instead\nopt.right.label [cb81d8] = 改为掸掸灰\n# en: {You dust every jar and read every label. Maud's handwriting is a whole education, if a strange one.|Maud finds a clean shelf. \"Somebody's been learning,\" she says, which isn't quite thanks.|You dust the new jar first and read its label twice. Maud finds you there and gives you a look that's almost proud.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a14959] = {你掸了每个罐子，读了每张标签。莫德的笔迹本身就是一门学问，虽然古怪。|莫德发现架子干干净净。“有人在长学问了，”她说，这不太算道谢。|你先掸了那只新罐子，把它的标签读了两遍。莫德发现你在那儿，看你的眼神几乎有点骄傲。}\n\n@event secret_garden_humming_pear\n# en: The Humming Pear\ntitle [041751] = 哼歌的梨树\n# en: {The pear tree in Maud's secret garden is humming to itself.|The pear tree has changed its tune since you were last here, as if it's been practising.|The pear tree hums when you come in now. You'd like to think it's for you.} {season == \"winter\": Its tune is low and slow, like something asleep.|{season == \"spring\": Its tune has gone quick and bright with blossom.|{Its tune is one you almost know.|Its tune has a sad turn in the middle.|Its tune is one you've heard Maud whistle.}}}\ntext [48719c] = {莫德秘密花园里的梨树在自顾自地哼歌。|自你上次来过，梨树换了个调子，像是一直在练习。|现在你一进来，梨树就开始哼歌。你愿意相信那是为你哼的。} {season == \"winter\": 它的调子又低又慢，像某种在沉睡的东西。|{season == \"spring\": 它的调子随着开花变得轻快明亮。|{那是一支你几乎会唱的曲子。|那支曲子中间有个忧伤的转折。|那支曲子你听莫德吹过口哨。}}}\n# en: A student lying in the grass under a pear tree in a hidden walled garden, ferns glowing softly around them.\nscene [69e58c] = 隐蔽的围墙花园里，一个学生躺在梨树下的草地上，周围的蕨类柔柔发光。\n# en: Hum along with it\nopt.left.label [d18c15] = 跟着它一起哼\n# en: {You find the harmony on the third try. The tree corrects you twice, kindly.|The two of you hum until the ferns glow brighter. Afterwards you can't get the tune out of your head for days.|You hum along without thinking now. The pear tree drops a pear in your lap at the end, which feels like applause.}\nopt.left.say.1 [785686] = {第三次你才找到和声。梨树温和地纠正了你两次。|你们一起哼，直到蕨类更亮了。之后好几天，那调子都在你脑子里挥之不去。|现在你不假思索就能跟着哼。末了，梨树往你膝上掉了一只梨，像是在鼓掌。}\n# en: Nap in its shade\nopt.right.label [981938] = 在树荫下打个盹\n# en: {You wake rested, a few crowns lighter, with a fresh root curled over the spot where they fell.|You sleep like a seed. A few crowns slip out of your pocket and the roots close over them. Rent, perhaps.|You sleep in your usual hollow between the roots. A few crowns are gone when you wake, and the tree looks very innocent.}\nopt.right.say.1 [11a9c3] = {你醒来时神清气爽，口袋里少了几枚克朗，掉落的地方盘着一条新长的根。|你睡得像一颗种子。几枚克朗从口袋滑出，树根把它们合拢了。大概是房租吧。|你在树根间那个熟悉的凹处睡着了。醒来时少了几枚克朗，梨树看上去一脸无辜。}\n# en: You sleep like a seed and wake with your empty pocket turned out, as if the roots had checked.\nopt.right.say.2 [85a98a] = 你睡得像一颗种子，醒来时空口袋被翻了出来，仿佛树根检查过了。\n\n@event secret_garden_silver_bees\n# en: The Silver Bees\ntitle [1eab66] = 银蜂\n# en: {The silver bees are working the ferns; their hive in the old stump drips honey the colour of moonlight.|The silver bees have built a second comb, lopsided, as if in a hurry.|The silver bees know you now, more or less.} {season == \"winter\": Even in winter they fly, slowly, like snow going the wrong way.|{The comb is fuller than you've ever seen it.|One of them lands on your sleeve and stays.|They're dancing a direction you can't read.}}\ntext [2aba13] = {银蜂在蕨类间忙碌；老树桩里的蜂巢淌着月光色的蜂蜜。|银蜂又筑了第二块蜂巢，歪歪扭扭，像是赶工。|银蜂现在或多或少认得你了。} {season == \"winter\": 即使在冬天它们也在飞，慢慢的，像飘错了方向的雪。|{蜂巢比你见过的任何时候都满。|一只落在你的袖子上，不走了。|它们跳着一个你读不懂的方向。}}\n# en: Bees made of silver light hovering around a hive in a hollow stump, a student reaching carefully towards a dripping comb.\nscene [db712c] = 银光构成的蜜蜂绕着空心树桩里的蜂巢盘旋，一个学生小心翼翼地伸手去取滴蜜的蜂巢。\n# en: Take a little comb\nopt.left.label [9d8f0c] = 取一小块蜂巢\n# en: {A silver sting feels like a cold spark. Your hand glows for an hour and aches for two.|The bees file a complaint, in the form of your thumb.|They know your hand by now, and sting it anyway, more out of habit than anger.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b98702] = {被银蜂蜇一下，像一颗冰冷的火星。你的手亮了一个钟头，又疼了两个钟头。|蜜蜂提出了抗议——抗议书就是你肿起来的拇指。|它们现在认得你的手了，还是蜇了它，与其说是生气，不如说是习惯。}\n# en: {You take a thumb's width of comb. It tastes like starlight and burns a little going down.|The bees let you take a little, and watch to make sure it's only a little.|They let you take a little and dance something at you afterwards. You'd give a lot to know what it meant.}\nopt.left.say.2 [b10aea] = {你取了拇指宽的一小块。尝起来像星光，咽下去有点烫。|蜜蜂让你取了一点，还盯着确保真的只有一点。|它们让你取了一点，之后朝你跳了一段舞。你很想知道那是什么意思。}\n# en: Follow a bee home\nopt.right.label [3d2092] = 跟着一只蜜蜂回家\n# en: {The bee leads you through brambles to a fern you've never seen, and then leaves you there, scratched and curious.|You follow it all the way round the garden and back to the stump. It seems to find this funny.|You follow a bee that's dancing the strange direction. It leads you to the garden wall, and over it, and you can't follow.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b7ed2a] = {蜜蜂领你穿过荆棘，来到一株你从没见过的蕨类前，然后把你丢在那儿，满身划痕，满心好奇。|你跟着它绕花园一圈，又回到树桩。它似乎觉得这很好笑。|你跟着一只跳着奇怪方向的蜜蜂。它把你领到花园墙边，然后飞了过去，你跟不过去。}\n\n@event secret_garden_glowing_ferns\n# en: The Glowing Ferns\ntitle [725061] = 发光的蕨类\n# en: {The ferns in the secret garden glow when the light goes. One frond would light your dormitory for a week. Maud said not to pick them.|Since your last visit a new fern has unfurled by the gate, already glowing.|The ferns glow. You know exactly what a frond is worth, and what Maud said.} {One has grown over the path, right at your hand.|A broken frond lies on the ground already, still glowing.|They're brighter than you've ever seen them.}\ntext [f2564e] = {天色一暗，秘密花园里的蕨类就亮了。一片叶子就能把你的宿舍照亮一个星期。莫德说过不许摘。|自你上次来过，门边又舒展开一株新蕨，已经亮了。|蕨类在发光。你清楚一片叶子值多少，也清楚莫德说过什么。} {有一株长到了小路上，正在你手边。|地上已经有一片折断的叶子，还在发光。|它们比你见过的任何时候都亮。}\n# en: A hidden garden at dusk, waist-high ferns glowing pale green, one student crouched with fingers near a frond.\nscene [cac6b0] = 黄昏时的隐秘花园，齐腰高的蕨类发着淡绿色的光，一个学生蹲着，手指靠近一片叶子。\n# en: Pick a single frond\nopt.left.label [ed6b96] = 只摘一片\n# en: {The frond goes dark before you reach the door. The garden feels a little colder to you the next time.|The frond curls up in your hand like a woodlouse and goes out. The silver bees follow you to the gate, accusingly.|You pick the brightest one. It goes out in your hand like a sigh, and the rest dim a little as you pass.}\nopt.left.say.1 [af70bb] = {还没走到门口，那片叶子就暗了。下次来时，花园对你冷淡了一些。|叶子在你手里像鼠妇一样蜷起来，熄灭了。银蜂一路跟你到门口，满是责备。|你摘了最亮的那片。它在你手里熄灭了，像一声叹息，你经过时其余的也暗了一点。}\n# en: {It lights your dormitory green for a week. You read by it, and feel very slightly watched.|You keep it in a jar by your bed. It glows softer every night, like something being polite.|It's the brightest frond you've ever seen, and it lights your room for a fortnight. You don't tell Maud.}\nopt.left.say.2 [368109] = {它把你的宿舍照成绿色，亮了一个星期。你借着它读书，总觉得有一点点被注视。|你把它放在床边的罐子里。它一夜比一夜暗，像在客气。|那是你见过最亮的一片叶子，把你的房间照亮了两个星期。你没告诉莫德。}\n# en: Leave them glowing\nopt.right.label [76d055] = 让它们继续发光\n# en: {You sit and watch the glow instead, and learn the way it moves with your breath. Maud would approve, if she knew.|You leave them be. On the way out, the ferns lean after you, like a dog that wanted to come.|You sit among them at their brightest. They glow brighter while you stay, and that's enough.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3a0884] = {你坐下来看那光，发现它随着你的呼吸起伏。莫德要是知道，会赞许的。|你没去碰它们。离开时，蕨类朝你的方向倾着，像一只想跟来的狗。|你在它们最亮的时候坐在它们中间。你在的时候它们更亮了，这就够了。}\n\n@event courier_loft_letter_home\n# en: A Letter Home\ntitle [8bce33] = 一封家书\n# en: {The courier loft will take a letter home for next to nothing. You've meant to write for weeks.|This time the letter has been half-written in your head since breakfast.|Writing home is a habit now, and the loft knows your handwriting.} {final_year: There aren't many letters left to send from here.|{season == \"winter\": Frost on the window; the pigeonholes stuffed with cards.|{The dispatch bell rings twice while you write.|A broken broom leans against your elbow.|A courier is reading over your shoulder, and apologises.}}}\ntext [53c5b7] = {信使阁楼花不了几个钱就能帮你把信送回家。你已经想写好几个星期了。|这一封从早饭起就在你脑子里写了一半。|往家里写信成了习惯，阁楼也认得你的笔迹了。} {final_year: 能从这里寄出的信不多了。|{season == \"winter\": 窗上结着霜，信格里塞满了贺卡。|{你写信时，发件铃响了两次。|一把坏扫帚靠在你胳膊肘边。|一个信使从你肩后看你写信，然后道了歉。}}}\n# en: A draughty loft full of pigeonholes, a student writing a letter on a parcel by the light of a high window.\nscene [953afc] = 满是信格的透风阁楼里，一个学生借着高窗的光，在一个包裹上写信。\n# en: Write it all down and send it\nopt.left.label [2de5e0] = 全写下来寄出去\n# en: {You write three pages and post them before you can cross anything out. You feel lighter, and a little homesick.|You tell them about your classes, and not about the hard parts. Then, in a postscript, you tell them about the hard parts.|You write the letter you've written before, with this month's news in it. The courier says your family must look forward to them.}\nopt.left.say.1 [877bbb] = {你写了三页，在来得及划掉任何一句之前就寄了出去。你觉得轻松了些，也有点想家。|你跟他们讲了你的课，没讲难处。然后，在附言里，你讲了难处。|你写了一封以前写过的那种信，填进这个月的消息。信使说你家人一定很盼着这些信。}\n# en: Write it, then don't send it\nopt.right.label [cf5f6f] = 写好了，但不寄\n# en: {You read it over and fold it into your pocket instead. Some letters are only for writing.|You keep this one. The courier asks if you're sure. You aren't.|You put this one with the others you didn't send. It's getting to be quite a stack.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d52bd1] = {你读了一遍，折好放进口袋。有些信只是用来写的。|这封你留下了。信使问你确定吗。你并不确定。|你把这封和其他没寄的放在一起。那一沓越来越厚了。}\n\n@event courier_loft_dispatch_bell\n# en: The Dispatch Bell\ntitle [e813ff] = 发件铃\n# en: {The dispatch bell clangs and the loft explodes into brooms. A courier shoves a parcel at you. \"You've got legs. Lecture tower!\"|The couriers have started eyeing the ladder whenever someone climbs it.|The bell. A parcel. A look. You know the drill.} {The parcel is ticking.|The parcel is addressed to \"Whoever Is On Duty\".|The parcel is very cold.}\ntext [fcbf9d] = {发件铃一响，阁楼里扫帚乱飞。一个信使把包裹塞给你。“你有腿。讲堂塔！”|现在每次有人爬梯子，信使们都会瞟上一眼。|铃声。包裹。一个眼神。你知道该怎么做。} {包裹在滴答作响。|包裹上写着“寄给值班的随便谁”。|包裹冰凉冰凉的。}\n# en: A chaotic loft as a dispatch bell clangs, couriers grabbing brooms and parcels, one holding a parcel out to a visitor.\nscene [b87d28] = 发件铃大作，乱成一团的阁楼里信使们抓起扫帚和包裹，其中一个把包裹递给来客。\n# en: Run the parcel across campus\nopt.left.label [71bfc4] = 跑一趟送包裹\n# en: {You get it there out of breath and in time. The tip is a few crowns and a very strange look.|You run it up four flights of stairs. The recipient signs for it without looking up, as if you were the post.|You run the cold parcel across campus with your hands going numb. The tip is generous, and given quickly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b2e672] = {你气喘吁吁但准时送到了。小费是几枚克朗外加一个非常奇怪的眼神。|你跑上四层楼。收件人头也不抬就签了字，好像你本来就是邮差。|你捧着那个冰冷的包裹横穿校园，手都冻麻了。小费给得很大方，也给得很快。}\n# en: Hand it back, politely\nopt.right.label [b4d5ae] = 客气地还回去\n# en: {\"Fine, fine,\" says the courier, already on a broom. You hear about it, loudly, from the rafters.|The courier takes it back with a sigh that follows you down the ladder.|You hand the cold parcel back. The courier holds it at arm's length and doesn't argue.}\nopt.right.say.1 [05070f] = {“行吧行吧，”信使说着已经跨上了扫帚。你从椽子上方听到了响亮的抱怨。|信使叹着气接回包裹，那声叹息一路跟着你下了梯子。|你把冰冷的包裹还了回去。信使伸直胳膊拿着它，没有争辩。}\n\n@event courier_loft_broom_rack\n# en: The Fast Broom\ntitle [0775c7] = 快扫帚\n# en: {At the end of the rack is the courier loft's fast broom, tagged in red. Couriers only. Nobody's watching.|The fast broom is back on the rack, red tag and all.|The red-tagged broom. You've thought about it every time you've come up here.} {season == \"winter\": The loft doors are open on a sky full of snow.|{The loft doors stand open on a blue sky.|Someone has polished it recently.|The red tag has a fresh scorch mark.}}\ntext [e04714] = {架子尽头是信使阁楼的快扫帚，挂着红牌。仅限信使。没人在看。|快扫帚又回到了架子上，红牌也在。|那把挂红牌的扫帚。每次上来你都会想到它。} {season == \"winter\": 阁楼的门敞着，外面是漫天飞雪。|{阁楼的门敞着，外面是蓝天。|最近有人给它上过油。|红牌上有一道新焦痕。}}\n# en: A rack of courier brooms in a loft, one sleek black broom set apart from the others with a red tag on its handle.\nscene [76169f] = 阁楼里一排信使扫帚，其中一把光滑的黑扫帚单独放着，柄上挂着红牌。\n# en: Take it for one lap\nopt.left.label [c03fc1] = 骑它飞一圈\n# en: {One lap of the towers, fast enough to make your ears sing. You put it back with the bristles barely warm.|You fly it under the bridges of light and come back laughing. Nobody saw, except, possibly, everybody.|You take the fast broom for one lap at last. It flies like it's been waiting for you. You put it back and can't stop smiling.}\nopt.left.say.1 [221919] = {绕七塔一圈，快得耳朵嗡嗡响。你放回去时，帚毛几乎没热。|你骑着它从光之桥底下穿过，笑着飞回来。没人看见，除了，也许，所有人。|你终于骑着快扫帚飞了一圈。它飞起来像是一直在等你。你把它放回去，笑得停不下来。}\n# en: {It knows you aren't a courier. It shows you the courtyard, very close up.|It bucks you off into a haystack outside the stables. The stable hand laughs about it for a week.|The broom with the scorched tag has a scorched temper. It puts you through the stable roof.}\nopt.left.say.2 [617e56] = {它知道你不是信使。它让你近距离地看了看庭院的地面。|它把你甩进了马厩外的干草堆。马夫为这事笑了一个星期。|标签烧焦的扫帚，脾气也烧焦了。它把你甩穿了马厩的屋顶。}\n# en: Tighten its bristles instead\nopt.right.label [8b8ed8] = 改为给它紧紧帚毛\n# en: {You spend an hour learning how a good broom is bound. It's more satisfying than you'd admit.|You retie the bristles properly. Some courier will fly a little faster tomorrow and never know why.|You rebind the bristles and the scorched tag. The head courier notices and says nothing, which is promising.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6edfab] = {你花了一个钟头学一把好扫帚是怎么扎成的。这比你愿意承认的更让人满足。|你把帚毛重新扎好。明天会有个信使飞得快一点，却永远不知道为什么。|你重新扎好帚毛，还换了那块烧焦的标签。首席信使注意到了，什么也没说，这是个好兆头。}\n\n@event whisperwood_sleeptalk\n# en: The Forest Talks\ntitle [380652] = 森林在说话\n# en: {The Whisperwood is talking in its sleep. You stand very still to make out the words.|You've started to catch whole words in the forest's mumbling.|You've learned to stand still and let the forest talk.} Today it's {listing trees that were felled|arguing about the rain|saying a name, over and over, that isn't yours}. {season == \"winter\": Snow muffles half of it.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves fall with every word.|Fog moves between the trunks.}}\ntext [27381d] = {低语森林在说梦话。你一动不动地站着，想听清那些词。|你开始能听清森林嘟囔里的整个词了。|你已经学会静静站着，让森林说下去。}今天它在{列举被砍倒的树|为下雨的事争吵|一遍遍念着一个不是你的名字}。{season == \"winter\": 雪捂住了一半。|{season == \"autumn\": 每说一个字就落下一片叶子。|雾在树干间游移。}}\n# en: A student standing very still in a misty old forest, head tilted, as if listening to the trees.\nscene [9d0c9e] = 雾气弥漫的古老森林里，一个学生一动不动地站着，侧着头，像是在听树说话。\n# en: Listen closely\nopt.left.label [21f0ed] = 仔细听\n# en: {You catch a whole sentence. It's about a winter long ago, and it's sad, and you walk home with it.|It notices you listening and goes quiet, the way a sleeper does. You leave feeling rude.|You listen to the name until you could say it in your sleep. You don't know whose it is. You'll find out, one day.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2b9f0d] = {你听清了完整的一句。那是关于很久以前的一个冬天，很悲伤，你带着它走回了家。|它发现你在听，便像睡着的人那样安静下来。你离开时觉得自己很失礼。|你听那个名字听到能在梦里说出来。你不知道那是谁的名字。总有一天你会知道。}\n# en: Walk on, humming\nopt.right.label [f1a5bc] = 哼着歌继续走\n# en: {You walk the long loop humming loudly. The forest grumbles and turns over. You come back with muddy boots and a clear head.|You hum all the way round. Twice, the forest hums back.|You hum over the name it keeps saying. The forest falls quiet, as if it had been waiting for someone to answer.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ee327a] = {你大声哼着歌走完长长的一圈。森林嘟囔着翻了个身。你带着一靴子泥和清醒的脑袋回来。|你一路哼着歌。有两次，森林也哼了回来。|你用哼唱盖过它反复念叨的名字。森林安静下来，像是一直在等人回答。}\n\n@event whisperwood_foragers\n# en: Foraging Season\ntitle [067bb9] = 采集季\n# en: {season == \"autumn\": The Whisperwood is full of mushrooms and students with baskets.|{season == \"winter\": A few hardy foragers are scraping lichen off the oaks.|{season == \"spring\": The clearings are thick with wild garlic and foragers.|The brambles are heavy with berries, and so are the foragers.}}} {A second-year from Rootward waves you over.|The Rootward forager is back, and saves you a spot.|The foragers treat you as one of their own now.}\ntext [62e283] = {season == \"autumn\": 低语森林里满是蘑菇和提着篮子的学生。|{season == \"winter\": 几个不怕冷的采集者在刮橡树上的地衣。|{season == \"spring\": 林间空地长满了野蒜，也挤满了采集者。|荆棘丛上挂满了浆果，采集者身上也挂满了。}}} {一个根庐的二年级生朝你招手。|那个根庐的采集者又来了，给你留了个位置。|采集者们现在把你当自己人了。}\n# en: A handful of students with baskets crouching in a forest clearing, one holding up a mushroom for the others to inspect.\nscene [6bbb73] = 几个提篮子的学生蹲在林间空地上，其中一个举起一只蘑菇给其他人看。\n# en: Forage for the apothecary\nopt.left.label [c317dd] = 为药铺采集\n# en: {Pell & Daughters buys your basket and throws out a third of it. You've still got nettle rash.|A good basket, a fair price, and brambles in places you'd rather not discuss.|The foragers show you their own patch. Pell & Daughters keeps the whole basket, which has never happened.}\nopt.left.say.1 [78e029] = {佩尔父女药铺收下你的篮子，扔掉了三分之一。你身上的荨麻疹还在。|一篮好货，一个公道价，还有一些你不愿细说的地方扎进了荆棘。|采集的人把他们自己的地盘指给了你。佩尔父女药铺把一整篮都留下了，这是从没有过的事。}\n# en: Learn the plants from the expert\nopt.right.label [5629c5] = 跟行家认植物\n# en: {The second-year names everything, twice, with its uses and its poisons. You come away full of names and short of patience.|You learn which mushroom is supper and which is a funeral. It takes until supper, and you'll need it.|You teach a first-year what the Rootward student taught you, and get two of the names wrong. They're corrected for you, kindly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4edc47] = {那个二年级生把每样东西都说了两遍，连用途带毒性。你满脑子名字，耐心所剩无几。|你学会了分辨哪种蘑菇是晚餐，哪种是葬礼。一直学到晚饭时分，你会用得上的。|你把根庐学生教你的东西教给一个一年级生，说错了两个名字。别人好心地替你纠正了。}\n\n@event whisperwood_off_path\n# en: The Path That Wasn't\ntitle [916a4a] = 本不存在的小路\n# en: {A path opens to your left that wasn't there last time.|Another path that wasn't there before. The Whisperwood does this.|The wood has offered you a new path again. You're starting to think it likes you.} {It's lined with pale stones.|It smells of woodsmoke where there's no fire.|It goes downhill, which is wrong, because you're at the bottom of the hill.}\ntext [3b11a8] = {你左手边出现了一条上次没有的小路。|又一条以前没有的小路。低语森林就是这样。|森林又给你开了一条新路。你开始觉得它喜欢你。} {路边排着苍白的石头。|没有火，却闻得到柴烟味。|它往下坡走，这不对，因为你已经在山脚了。}\n# en: A narrow path branching off into fog between old trees, a student pausing at the fork with a hand on a marked trunk.\nscene [a1f31c] = 古树间一条窄路分岔伸进雾里，一个学生在岔口停下，手扶着一棵做了记号的树干。\n# en: Follow it a little way\nopt.left.label [03da75] = 跟着走一小段\n# en: {It leads to a clearing you'll never find again, full of moonpetal buds. You come back with a pocketful of quiet.|It loops you round to an old stone with words on it. You copy them down. They mean nothing yet.|You follow it downhill, though there's no down left. It brings you out above the lake, which is impossible, and lovely.}\nopt.left.say.1 [842b36] = {它通向一片你再也找不到的空地，开满了月瓣花的花苞。你带回了满满一口袋的安静。|它绕着你来到一块刻着字的老石头前。你把字抄了下来。它们暂时还没有意义。|你顺着它往下走，虽然已经没有更低的地方了。它把你带到了湖的上方，这不可能，却很美。}\n# en: {You walk in circles until dusk and come out at the lake, with no idea how.|The path ends at a wall of fog. Turning back takes much longer than going in did.|You follow it downhill for an hour and come out exactly where you started. The wood seems pleased with itself.}\nopt.left.say.2 [fe46de] = {你转圈转到黄昏，出来时到了湖边，不知道是怎么走到的。|小路尽头是一堵雾墙。往回走比进来花的时间长得多。|你顺着它往下走了一个钟头，出来时正好在起点。树林看上去很得意。}\n# en: Mark a tree and turn back\nopt.right.label [851506] = 在树上做记号，回头\n# en: {You carve an arrow in the bark. The next time you come, the arrow points somewhere else.|You turn back. The path watches you go, and quietly closes behind you.|You mark a tree. The next morning there's a mark on the tree beside it, in the same hand.}\nopt.right.say.1 [56987c] = {你在树皮上刻了个箭头。下次来时，箭头指向了别处。|你转身回去。小路目送你离开，在你身后悄悄合上了。|你在一棵树上做了记号。第二天早上，旁边那棵树上出现了一个同样笔迹的记号。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/internships.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/internships.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event intern_midsummer_review\n# en: The Midsummer Review\ntitle [9f057d] = 仲夏评估\n# en: {Halfway through the placement, your supervisor asks to see you. There's a single sheet of notes on the desk, face down.|The midsummer review again: the desk, the sheet, the tea nobody drinks.|You know the review's shape by now. You still don't know what's on the sheet.} {performance(internship) >= 60: Your supervisor is almost smiling.|{performance(internship) < 40: Your supervisor is not smiling.|Your supervisor's face gives nothing away.}}\ntext [e7c69e] = {实习过半，主管叫你过去。桌上有一张笔记，扣着放。|又是仲夏评估：那张桌子，那张纸，那杯没人喝的茶。|你现在熟悉评估的套路了。你仍然不知道那张纸上写了什么。}{performance(internship) >= 60: 你的主管几乎在微笑。|{performance(internship) < 40: 你的主管没有笑。|你的主管面无表情。}}\n# en: A supervisor's desk with a single sheet of notes face down, a student sitting opposite with hands folded too tightly.\nscene [cda6e8] = 主管的桌上扣着一张笔记，一个学生坐在对面，双手握得太紧。\n# en: Ask for honest criticism\nopt.left.label [7e14a3] = 请对方直言批评\n# en: {You get it, three things you do badly, in order. You fix two of them by the end of the summer. Your supervisor notices both.|\"Most interns ask for praise,\" your supervisor says, and turns the sheet over. It's a long list. You're grateful for every line, eventually.|This time you ask to see the sheet itself. It's shorter than you feared, and every line is fair.}\nopt.left.say.1 [22ee92] = {你得到了：你做得不好的三件事，按顺序排列。到夏天结束时你改掉了两件。你的主管两件都注意到了。|“大多数实习生要的是表扬，”你的主管说着，把纸翻了过来。清单很长。最终你对每一行都心存感激。|这一次你要求看那张纸本身。它比你担心的短，每一行都很公道。}\n# en: Ask what you've done well\nopt.right.label [d826f5] = 问问自己哪里做得好\n# en: {Your supervisor reads the good half aloud. You leave glowing, and never hear the other half.|\"Your handwriting,\" your supervisor says, after a pause, \"is excellent.\" You decide to take it.|Your supervisor reads you the top of the sheet and folds the rest away. You'll wonder what's under the fold.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5452ea] = {你的主管把好的那一半念了出来。你满面春风地离开，再也没听到另一半。|“你的字，”你的主管顿了一下说，“写得非常好。”你决定接受这句话。|你的主管给你念了纸的上半截，把剩下的折了起来。你会一直好奇折痕底下写了什么。}\n\n@event intern_credit_taken\n# en: Someone Else's Name\ntitle [737ae1] = 别人的名字\n# en: {The report you spent a week on is pinned up in the corridor, praised by everyone, with another intern's name at the bottom.|Another piece of your work, another name on it.|It's happened again, and this time the other intern doesn't even look away.} {Your supervisor is reading it with approval.|The other intern is being congratulated.|Your notes are still on your desk, in your hand.}\ntext [eb5b31] = {你花了一个星期写的报告钉在走廊里，人人称赞，署名却是另一个实习生。|又一份你的成果，又一个别人的名字。|又发生了，这一次那个实习生连目光都不躲了。}{你的主管正赞许地读着它。|那个实习生正接受大家的祝贺。|你的笔记还在你桌上，是你的笔迹。}\n# en: A report pinned on a wall, a student staring at the name at the bottom of it, which is not their own.\nscene [27205c] = 一份钉在墙上的报告，一个学生盯着报告底部的名字，那不是他自己的。\n# en: Take it to your supervisor\nopt.left.label [13030f] = 找你的主管\n# en: {You bring your notes, your drafts, your dates. Your supervisor reads them in silence and changes the name in red ink. The corridor goes very quiet around you.|You make your case calmly. The name is changed. The other intern doesn't speak to you again, which is fine.|You put your notes on your supervisor's desk, in your hand and dated. The name is changed, and this time the other intern looks away.}\nopt.left.say.1 [399e9e] = {你带上笔记、草稿和日期。主管默默读完，用红墨水改了名字。你周围的走廊一下子安静了。|你冷静地陈述理由。名字改了。那个实习生再也没和你说过话，这样也好。|你把笔记放在主管桌上，你的笔迹，注明了日期。名字改了，这一次，那个实习生移开了目光。}\n# en: {Your supervisor hears you out and says it's \"a matter for the two of you\". It isn't. You go back to your desk with your face burning.|Without proof enough, you sound petty, and you know it. The name stays.|Your notes are in your hand, but so, it turns out, is a draft in theirs. Your supervisor can't tell which came first, and the name stays.}\nopt.left.say.2 [974ec6] = {主管听你说完，说这是“你们俩之间的事”。不是的。你回到桌前，脸上火辣辣的。|证据不够，你显得斤斤计较，你自己也知道。名字没改。|你的笔记是你的笔迹，可结果对方也有一份自己笔迹的草稿。主管分不清哪份在先，名字没改。}\n# en: Let it go and work faster\nopt.right.label [1f0a6c] = 算了，干得更快些\n# en: {You say nothing and do the next one twice as well, in ink that's hard to copy. It doesn't feel like justice. It feels like work.|You let it go. You write your name on every page from then on, small, in the corner.|You take your notes home. The next piece of work never leaves your sight until it's on the supervisor's desk.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d7ade9] = {你什么也没说，把下一份做得加倍好，用难以仿写的墨水。这感觉不像公道。这感觉像干活。|你算了。从那以后，你在每一页的角落里都写上自己的名字，小小的。|你把笔记带回了住处。下一份活儿，在放上主管的桌子之前，一刻也没离开过你的视线。}\n\n@event intern_city_evening\n# en: An Evening Off\ntitle [6fa683] = 休息的夜晚\n# en: {{member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): The other interns are going out tonight, three miles to a village dance, with a fiddler and a supper laid on at a few coins a head.|The other interns are going out tonight, to an inn with music and a stall that sells fried bread at midnight.}|Another evening off, and the others are already at the door.|\"Coming?\" says the intern with the biscuits. \"Last time you said next time.\"} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {The track is dark, but somebody has a lantern.|It's the only dance for thirty miles.|Someone has brought a fiddle of their own.}|{The streets are bright.|There's a fair on the river.|Somebody knows a band.}} Your work for tomorrow isn't done.\ntext [a6fa73] = {{member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): 其他实习生今晚要出去，走三英里去村里的舞会，有提琴手，还有几个钱一位的晚饭。|其他实习生今晚要出去，去一家有音乐的客栈，还有个半夜卖炸面包的小摊。}|又一个休息的夜晚，其他人已经站在门口了。|“去吗？”带饼干的那个实习生说，“上次你说下次。”}{member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {小路很黑，好在有人带了灯笼。|这是方圆三十英里唯一的舞会。|有人自己带了把小提琴。}|{街道灯火通明。|河上有个集市。|有人认识一支乐队。}}你明天的活儿还没干完。\n# en: A lamplit street far from the academy, interns spilling out of a busy inn, one looking back at the lit window of the workplace.\nscene [9f0bec] = 远离学院的一条灯火通明的街道，实习生们从热闹的客栈里涌出来，其中一个回头望着工作地点亮着灯的窗户。\n# en: Go out with them\nopt.left.label [e99515] = 和他们一起去\n# en: {You pay your share of the supper, dance badly, laugh well and walk home at two in the morning, arm in arm with people you met last month. Tomorrow's work is a little worse for it.|You go, and stand your round. You learn more about the place from the other interns in one evening than from a month of briefings, and remember half of it.|You keep last time's promise and go. The intern with the biscuits shares them on the walk home, and tomorrow's work is a little worse for it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [89a4dc] = {你付了自己那份晚饭钱，跳舞跳得很糟，笑得很开心，凌晨两点和上个月才认识的人挽着胳膊走回家。明天的活儿因此差了一点。|你去了，还请了一轮。一个晚上从其他实习生那里了解到的东西，比一个月的晨会还多，虽然只记住了一半。|你兑现了上次的承诺，去了。带饼干的那个实习生在回去的路上分给你吃，明天的活儿因此差了一点。}\n# en: Finish tomorrow's work\nopt.right.label [2c6920] = 干完明天的活\n# en: {You finish the work by lamplight. You hear them come home, singing. In the morning your work is the best on the table.|You stay in. The work is good. The evening is long.|\"Next time,\" you say again, and mean it less. Your work is excellent, and the biscuits are gone by morning.}\nopt.right.say.1 [07633f] = {你在灯下干完了活。你听见他们唱着歌回来。早上，你的成果是桌上最好的。|你留了下来。活儿干得很好。夜晚很长。|“下次吧，”你又说了一遍，这回没那么真心。你的活儿干得很出色，饼干到早上已经没了。}\n\n@event intern_letter_from_campus\n# en: A Letter From the Castle\ntitle [ffb8b9] = 城堡来信\n# en: {A letter from {friend}, forwarded twice, with the castle drawn in the margins.|Another letter from campus, this one from {friend}.|{friend}'s handwriting on the envelope. You open it before your coat is off.} {The letter misses you, in a roundabout way.|{They} {are} bored without you, {they} say{s}.|There's gossip, and a pressed flower.} Answering it properly would take the evening.\ntext [890ed9] = {一封{friend}的信，转寄了两次，页边画着城堡。|又一封来自学院的信，这次是{friend}写的。|信封上是{friend}的字迹。你外套还没脱就拆开了。}{这封信拐弯抹角地说想你。|{They}说没有你很无聊。|里面有八卦，还有一朵压干的花。}好好回信得花一个晚上。\n# en: A letter open on a desk far from the academy, its margins full of little drawings of the castle, a student smiling at it.\nscene [f98846] = 远离学院的一张桌上摊着一封信，页边画满了城堡的小插画，一个学生对着它微笑。\n# en: Write back at length\nopt.left.label [7bb28c] = 长长地回一封\n# en: {Six pages, with drawings of your own. You post it at dawn and are late for the briefing, and don't mind.|You write back everything: the work, the place, the interns. It's like talking to {them}.|You answer the gossip with gossip of your own, and press a flower from here to send back. It's like being in two places.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d15588] = {六页纸，还配了你自己画的插图。你天亮时寄出，晨会迟到了，也不在乎。|你把一切都写了回去：工作、这个地方、实习生们。就像在和{them}说话。|你用自己这边的八卦回应那边的八卦，还压了一朵这里的花寄回去。就像同时身在两处。}\n# en: Send a quick note\nopt.right.label [40b631] = 回一张短笺\n# en: {\"Busy. Miss you. More soon.\" It's true, and it's thin, and you know it.|Three lines and a promise. You mean to write more. The summer is very full.|A quick note, and the pressed flower kept in your notebook. You'll write properly when the summer's less full.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2d0d0f] = {“忙。想你。回头细说。”是真话，很单薄，你自己也知道。|三行字和一个承诺。你本想多写一些。这个夏天太满了。|一张短笺，那朵压花夹进了你的笔记本。等夏天没这么满了，你再好好写。}\n\n@event intern_conclave_your_ward\n# en: A Ward of Your Own\ntitle [dbf01a] = 属于你的护界\n# en: The Conclave is testing a new ward, and the researcher who designed it is ill. \"You've read every note,\" says your supervisor. \"Cast it. If it holds, it's yours to keep, and your name goes in the paper.\" Behind the glass, half the Conclave has come to watch.\ntext [7343f4] = 七塔议会在测试一种新的护界，设计它的研究员病了。“每一条笔记你都读过，”你的主管说，“施放它。如果它撑住了，就归你了，你的名字也会登上论文。”玻璃后面，半个议会的人都来看了。\n# en: A circular test chamber, a student at the centre holding up a shimmering ward against a bolt of light, researchers behind thick glass on their feet.\nscene [9c1957] = 一间圆形的测试室，一个学生站在中央，举起一道闪烁的护界挡住一束光，厚玻璃后面的研究员们都站了起来。\n# en: Cast it\nopt.left.label [e73317] = 施放它\n# en: The bolt hits the ward and stops, humming, an inch from your nose. Behind the glass, someone starts clapping. Your name goes in the paper, third of four, and the ward is yours for good.\nopt.left.say.1 [a78dfe] = 光束击中护界停了下来，嗡嗡作响，离你的鼻尖只有一寸。玻璃后面有人开始鼓掌。你的名字登上了论文，四位作者中的第三位，而这道护界从此永远属于你。\n# en: The ward holds for half a heartbeat and then doesn't. You wake on the floor with your hair standing up and a researcher fanning you with your own notes.\nopt.left.say.2 [aa069c] = 护界撑了半个心跳，然后没撑住。你在地板上醒来，头发竖着，一个研究员正拿你自己的笔记给你扇风。\n# en: Ask them to wait for the designer\nopt.right.label [03931f] = 请他们等设计者回来\n# en: \"Sensible,\" says your supervisor, in a voice that means \"disappointing\". The designer recovers, casts it, and thanks you in the paper's footnotes for the notes you kept.\nopt.right.say.1 [4dc945] = “明智，”你的主管说，语气的意思是“令人失望”。设计者康复后施放了它，并在论文的脚注里感谢你保存的笔记。\n# en: Layer your own Ward under theirs\nopt.up.label [11e904] = 在他们的结界下叠上你自己的结界\n# en: You cast the new ward, then your own beneath it, the one you've cast a hundred times. The bolt goes through the first like paper and stops dead on the second. Behind the glass there's an argument about whose ward held. Your supervisor settles it: \"Both. Write it up.\" The paper has your name second.\nopt.up.say.1 [963147] = 你施下新结界，又在它下面施了你自己的那道——你施过上百遍的那道。那道光矢像穿纸一样穿过第一层，在第二层上戛然而止。玻璃后面有人争论到底是谁的结界挡住了它。你的主管一锤定音：“两道都是。写进报告。”论文上，你的名字排第二。\n\n@event intern_alchemists_safety\n# en: The Cracked Vat\ntitle [177790] = 裂开的大桶\n# en: The seventh vat at the Guild works has a crack in it. Everyone knows. The foreman says it'll last the season, and the season's quota depends on it. Workers walk past it without looking. You've seen what the steam does to brass.\ntext [7b9994] = 公会工坊的第七口大桶裂了。人人都知道。工头说它能撑过这一季，这一季的定额全指望它。工人们从旁边走过，看都不看。你见过那蒸汽对黄铜做了什么。\n# en: A huge iron vat in an alchemical works, a hairline crack weeping green steam, workers walking past it without looking.\nscene [ddd11a] = 炼金工坊里一口巨大的铁桶，一道细缝正渗出绿色的蒸汽，工人们从旁边走过，看都不看。\n# en: Report it to the Guild masters\nopt.left.label [0fdc16] = 报告给公会长老\n# en: The masters close the seventh vat for a week and the foreman doesn't speak to you again. In the third week the replacement vat arrives, and an old worker shakes your hand without a word.\nopt.left.say.1 [cd1d6c] = 长老们把第七口桶关停了一个星期，工头从此再没和你说过话。第三个星期，替换的桶运到了，一位老工人一言不发地和你握了握手。\n# en: Mend it yourself, quietly\nopt.right.label [b83056] = 自己悄悄修好\n# en: You seal it from the inside in the night, with a sealant of your own. It holds through the season. The foreman never knows. The old worker does, somehow, and leaves an apple on your bench every morning.\nopt.right.say.1 [b14f13] = 你在夜里从内部用自己调的密封剂把它封好。它撑过了这一季。工头始终不知道。那位老工人不知怎么知道了，每天早上在你的工作台上放一个苹果。\n# en: The steam gets you as the seal gives. You wake in the works infirmary with blistered hands, and the vat is closed anyway.\nopt.right.say.2 [e799fa] = 密封失效时蒸汽扑到了你身上。你在工坊的医务室里醒来，双手起了水泡，那口桶还是被关停了。\n\n@event intern_expedition_new_star\n# en: A Star Nobody Charted\ntitle [6ddf06] = 无人记录的星\n# en: The lead gives you tonight's watch at the eyepiece, alone. At three in the morning there's a star in it that isn't on any chart: small, steady, blue. The expedition lead is asleep. By custom, whoever records a new star first may name it.\ntext [bf4e99] = 队长把今晚目镜前的值班交给了你一个人。凌晨三点，目镜里出现了一颗任何星图上都没有的星：小小的，稳定的，蓝色的。考察队长在睡觉。按照惯例，第一个记录新星的人可以为它命名。\n# en: A telescope on a high plateau at night, a student with an eye to the eyepiece and a chart on their knee, a single new star circled in red.\nscene [c44b5d] = 高原上夜色中的一架望远镜，一个学生一只眼贴着目镜，膝上放着星图，一颗新星被红笔圈了出来。\n# en: Wake the lead and share it\nopt.left.label [5148ef] = 叫醒队长一起分享\n# en: The lead comes in a nightshirt and stares until dawn. The star goes in the record under the expedition's name and yours, and the whole camp toasts it at breakfast.\nopt.left.say.1 [9a791d] = 队长穿着睡衣赶来，一直盯到天亮。这颗星以考察队和你的名义记入了记录，全营地在早饭时为它干杯。\n# en: Record it in your own name\nopt.right.label [43c352] = 以你自己的名字记录\n# en: You write it into the log: the time, the place, a name you choose. It's yours for as long as there are charts. At breakfast the lead reads the log twice, and is polite to you all week, which is worse.\nopt.right.say.1 [b9bb49] = 你把它写进日志：时间、位置，还有一个你挑的名字。只要星图还在，它就是你的。早饭时队长把日志读了两遍，接下来一个星期都对你很客气，这更糟。\n\n@event intern_field_station_foal\n# en: The Orphaned Foal\ntitle [5085db] = 失去母亲的幼驹\n# en: On the moor, a hippogriff foal, newborn and alone. The station's rule is plain: you count wild creatures, you don't raise them. The station chief says it won't last the night. It looks at you as if you were the only warm thing on the moor, which you are.\ntext [ed8dba] = 荒原上有一只鹰头马身有翼兽的幼驹，刚出生，孤零零的。站里的规矩很明确：野生动物只数不养。站长说它活不过今晚。它看着你，好像你是荒原上唯一温暖的东西，而你确实是。\n# en: A newborn hippogriff foal shivering in the heather on the moor, a student kneeling with a blanket, the field station lamps far off.\nscene [7b4dbb] = 一只刚出生的鹰头马身有翼兽幼驹在荒原的石楠丛中发抖，一个学生拿着毯子跪在一旁，观察站的灯光远在天边。\n# en: Take it back and raise it\nopt.left.label [f031dc] = 带回去养大\n# en: You feed it every two hours for a month and break the rule every one of them. By summer's end it flies. Back at Lumenhold, Professor Brask hears the story and invites you to supper. \"Rules,\" she says, \"are for people who've never held a foal.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [df3e67] = 你每两个小时喂它一次，喂了一个月，每一次都在违反规矩。到夏末，它能飞了。回到流明堡，布拉斯克教授听说了这件事，请你吃晚饭。“规矩，”她说，“是给从没抱过幼驹的人定的。”\n# en: Count it and leave it\nopt.right.label [16751c] = 记下它，然后离开\n# en: You write it down: one foal, alone, the date. You don't sleep. In the morning the chief tells you its mother came back in the night. You never find out if that's true.\nopt.right.say.1 [fc1a21] = 你把它记了下来：一只幼驹，孤零零的，日期。你没睡着。第二天早上站长告诉你，它的母亲夜里回来了。你永远不知道这是不是真的。\n\n@event intern_thornwick_offer\n# en: An Offer From Thornwick\ntitle [ca717f] = 荆木学院的邀请\n# en: Thornwick's dean asks to see you on your last week. \"Transfer to us,\" she says, sliding a sealed offer across the desk. \"Full scholarship, your own lab bench, a seat in our illusion faculty in time. Lumenhold never valued glamour. We do.\" She's sincere, and that's the danger.\ntext [545e64] = 荆木学院的院长在你最后一个星期请你去见她。“转学到我们这儿来，”她说着把一份封好的录取书推过桌面。“全额奖学金，你自己的实验台，将来幻术系的一个教席。流明堡从不看重魅影。我们看重。”她是真诚的，这正是危险所在。\n# en: A lacquered office at a rival academy, a smiling dean sliding a sealed offer across a desk, a window full of unfamiliar towers.\nscene [6db64f] = 对手学院一间上了漆的办公室，一位微笑的院长把一份封好的录取书推过桌面，窗外满是陌生的塔楼。\n# en: Decline, warmly\nopt.left.label [1a38c7] = 热情地谢绝\n# en: \"Lumenhold is home,\" you say. She smiles as if she expected it. \"The offer stands.\" Back at the castle, Professor Vey hears about it somehow and gives you an approving look that lasts all term.\nopt.left.say.1 [00f1eb] = “流明堡是我的家，”你说。她笑了，好像早料到了。“邀请一直有效。”回到城堡，维伊教授不知怎么听说了，给了你一个赞许的眼神，持续了整整一个学期。\n# en: Keep the offer, unopened\nopt.right.label [a4e05b] = 收下邀请，不拆开\n# en: You keep it, sealed, in the bottom of your trunk. Thornwick sends a stipend \"as a courtesy\". Knowing there's a door out makes some evenings easier, and some harder.\nopt.right.say.1 [25475a] = 你把它封着收在箱底。荆木学院寄来一笔津贴，“聊表心意”。知道有一扇退路的门，让有些夜晚好过一些，也让有些夜晚更难熬。\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_file\n# en: The File With Your Name\ntitle [725538] = 写着你名字的档案\n# en: Sorting files for the Office, you find one with your name on it. It's thin. The clerk has gone to lunch. The last page is about Lumenhold, and the lake, and a tower nobody has opened in a century.\ntext [273c79] = 替署里整理档案时，你发现一份写着你名字的档案。很薄。文书吃午饭去了。最后一页写的是流明堡、那座湖，还有一座一百年没人打开过的塔。\n# en: A windowless office lined with drawers, a student holding open a thin file with their own name on the label, a clerk's empty chair behind them.\nscene [210c9d] = 一间没有窗户、四壁都是抽屉的办公室，一个学生翻开一份标签上写着自己名字的薄档案，身后是文书空着的椅子。\n# en: Read it all\nopt.left.label [f23585] = 全部读完\n# en: They know where you sleep and what you eat, which is unsettling. The last page is mostly blacked out. What's left says the seventh tower \"is to remain closed\", and nothing about why. When the clerk comes back you are filing something else. The clerk looks at you a little too long.\nopt.left.say.1 [9882d7] = 他们知道你睡在哪儿、吃些什么，这让人不安。最后一页大半被涂黑了。剩下的部分只说第七塔“应保持封闭”，没说为什么。文书回来时，你正在整理别的东西。文书看你的时间稍微长了一点。\n# en: Put it back unread\nopt.right.label [154115] = 不看，放回去\n# en: You put it back. The clerk returns, checks the drawer and smiles for the first time all summer. \"The Office notices who doesn't look,\" the clerk says. You didn't look. You'll wonder for years.\nopt.right.say.1 [4506c9] = 你把它放了回去。文书回来，检查了抽屉，这个夏天第一次露出了笑容。“署里会注意到谁不看，”文书说。你没有看。你会为此纳闷好多年。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/letters.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/letters.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event letter_from_a_friend\n# en: A Letter From {friend}\ntitle [eb2348] = {friend}的来信\n# en: {friend} has written. {~Three pages, mostly about nothing, which is the best kind.|Half of it is crossed out and the other half underlined.|There's a drawing in the margin that is probably meant to be you.} {off_campus: The castle, they say, is too quiet without you.|They're away, and they say the quiet there is the wrong kind.}\ntext [28b6ff] = {friend}来信了。{~三页纸，大多在说些没什么要紧的事，这正是最好的那种。|一半划掉了，另一半画了线。|页边有一幅画，大概画的是你。}{off_campus: 信上说，没有你，城堡太安静了。|他们不在学校，信上说那边的安静不对劲。}\n# en: A folded letter on a windowsill, the handwriting crowded to the very edge of the page, a pressed leaf inside.\nscene [26e2f7] = 窗台上一封折好的信，字一直挤到纸边，里面夹着一片压干的叶子。\n# en: Write back at length\nopt.left.label [7bb28c] = 认认真真写封长信\n# en: {~You write until the candle gutters and spend the postage you meant for something else. It's worth it.|Four pages back, and a pressed flower of your own. You pay the extra stamp.|You write back pages and draw them in the margin, just as badly. The postage costs more than you meant it to.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cbff63] = {~你一直写到蜡烛快灭，把原本打算另作他用的钱花在了邮费上。值得。|回了四页，再夹上一朵你自己压的花。你多付了一张邮票。|你回了好几页，还在页边把他们画了进去，画得一样糟。邮费比你打算的贵。}\n# en: Send a short note\nopt.right.label [91c7fe] = 回一张短笺\n# en: {~A few lines, warm enough. They'll know you read it.|\"Missing you. More soon.\" It says enough.|A few lines, and a drawing of them in return. They'll laugh.}\nopt.right.say.1 [755e4a] = {~寥寥几行，温度刚好。他们会知道你读过了。|“想你。回头细说。”这就够了。|寥寥几行，再回赠一幅他们的画像。他们会笑的。}\n# en: {~Your note is so short it reads as cross. You meant it kindly.|Two lines. You can picture their face reading it, and it isn't pleased.|A short note, and no drawing. They'll notice the missing drawing more than the words.}\nopt.right.say.2 [fc7d32] = {~你的短笺短得像在生气。你本意是好的。|两行字。你能想象他们读信时的表情，不太高兴。|一张短笺，没有画。比起字，他们更会注意到少了那幅画。}\n\n@event letter_castle_news\n# en: News From the Castle\ntitle [8eca1f] = 城堡来的消息\n# en: Someone has forwarded the Lumen Ledger to you at home. {~A professor's hat has gone missing.|The lake froze, or thawed, depending on which column you believe.|There's a letter to the editor about the refectory soup, and it is furious.} It's strange to read about the castle from your old bedroom.\ntext [8f0788] = 有人把《流明纪事报》转寄到了你家。{~一位教授的帽子不见了。|湖结冰了，或者化冻了，取决于你信哪个专栏。|有一封写给编辑的读者来信，痛斥膳堂的汤，措辞激烈。}在自己从前的卧室里读城堡的新闻，感觉很奇怪。\n# en: A copy of the academy newssheet, forwarded in an envelope, its corners soft from travelling.\nscene [d5e48b] = 一份装在信封里转寄来的学院报纸，长途颠簸让纸角都软了。\n# en: Read it cover to cover\nopt.left.label [4fc56d] = 从头读到尾\n# en: {~You read every word, even the notices. By the end you're homesick for a place you're not at.|The whole paper, twice. Home feels very far from the castle tonight, and the castle very far from home.|You read every word, down to the furious letter about the soup, and find you miss even the soup.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dd08fe] = {~你读了每一个字，连启事都没放过。读完时，你想念起一个此刻不在的地方。|整份报纸读了两遍。今晚，家离城堡很远，城堡也离家很远。|你读了每一个字，一直读到那封关于汤的愤怒来信，发现自己连那汤都想念。}\n# en: Just the gossip page\nopt.right.label [381870] = 只看八卦版\n# en: {~The gossip is excellent. You laugh out loud and have to explain it to everyone at the table.|Someone you know is in it, for something ridiculous. Perfect.|The gossip is good, but the furious letter about the soup is better. You read it aloud at the table.}\nopt.right.say.1 [97c306] = {~八卦精彩极了。你笑出了声，只好给饭桌上的每个人解释一遍。|上面有个你认识的人，为了件荒唐事。完美。|八卦不错，可那封关于汤的愤怒来信更好。你在饭桌上把它念了出来。}\n# en: {~The gossip is about someone you like, and it's unkind.|There's nothing in it you didn't already know. Somehow that's worse.|The gossip is all about a quarrel you'd rather not hear of. Even the letter about the soup can't save it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [466e09] = {~八卦说的是你喜欢的人，而且不太厚道。|里面没有一件你不知道的事。不知怎么，这更糟。|八卦全是一场你宁愿没听说的争吵。连那封关于汤的来信也救不回来。}\n\n@event letter_writing_home\n# en: Writing Home\ntitle [b3ea96] = 给家里写信\n# en: The placement keeps you busy, and home feels further away than the miles. {~There's ink, paper and a quiet hour.|The lamp in your lodgings is good for writing.|You owe home a letter, and you know it.}\ntext [941b10] = 实习让你忙得团团转，家感觉比实际的路程还要远。{~有墨水，有纸，还有一个安静的钟头。|住处的灯很适合写字。|你欠家里一封信，你自己清楚。}\n# en: A borrowed desk in a strange room, a half-written letter under a lamp, a map of somewhere else pinned on the wall.\nscene [a9923b] = 陌生房间里一张借来的书桌，灯下一封写了一半的信，墙上钉着一张别处的地图。\n# en: Write a proper letter home\nopt.left.label [a92ffc] = 好好给家里写封信\n# en: {~Four pages about the work, the food and the people. The postage is dear from here. You send it anyway.|You tell them everything, and in telling it you find out how much you like it here.|You owe them a letter, so you write a long one, and the debt feels paid with interest. The postage hurts.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6ed788] = {~四页纸，写工作、吃食和这里的人。从这儿寄信邮费很贵。你还是寄了。|你把一切都告诉了他们，说着说着才发现自己有多喜欢这里。|你欠家里一封信，于是写了封长的，感觉连本带利都还上了。邮费让你肉疼。}\n# en: A few lines and back to work\nopt.right.label [b65885] = 写几行就回去干活\n# en: {~Short and cheerful. It'll do, and they'll be glad of it.|\"All well, more soon.\" True, mostly.|A few lines to settle what you owe. They'll be glad of it, and you'll owe them a longer one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [be7409] = {~简短又开心。够了，他们会高兴的。|“一切都好，回头细说。”大体属实。|寥寥几行，算是还了欠的信。他们会高兴的，而你又欠下了一封更长的。}\n# en: {~You write three lines and feel guilty for a week.|The letter is so brief it reads like a receipt.|You pay what you owe with three lines. It doesn't feel paid.}\nopt.right.say.2 [91454d] = {~你写了三行，内疚了一个星期。|信短得像一张收据。|你用三行字还了欠的信。感觉并没有还上。}\n\n@event letter_reading_list\n# en: A Reading List\ntitle [7ffe18] = 书单\n# en: A professor has written over the break, as some of them do, with a reading list for next term: eleven titles, three of them underlined, one with \"(if you dare)\" beside it. {~The handwriting slopes like a staircase.|There's a coffee ring on the corner.}\ntext [11229b] = 有位教授在假期里写信来，有些教授会这样，附上下学期的书单：十一本书，三本画了线，一本旁边写着“（如果你敢的话）”。{~字迹斜得像楼梯。|角落里有个咖啡渍。}\n# en: A long handwritten list of book titles on academy paper, a small inkblot at the bottom where the writer paused.\nscene [9412bb] = 学院信纸上一份长长的手写书单，底部有个小墨点，是写信人停笔的地方。\n# en: Start on the list now\nopt.left.label [df9c89] = 现在就开始读\n# en: {~You read the first two by candlelight while everyone else is resting. The third is the one marked \"if you dare\". You don't, yet.|You make real progress and lose two afternoons of holiday to it. Next term will be easier for it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b59de3] = {~别人都在休息时，你借着烛光读完了头两本。第三本就是标着“如果你敢的话”的那本。你还不敢。|你进展不小，为此搭进了两个假期的下午。下学期会因此轻松些。}\n# en: Put it under the bed until term\nopt.right.label [4a4931] = 塞到床底下等开学\n# en: {~The list goes under the bed with your winter boots. The holiday stays a holiday.|You don't read a word, and you don't feel a bit sorry.}\nopt.right.say.1 [90ed0a] = {~书单和你的冬靴一起进了床底。假期还是假期。|你一个字也没读，也一点都不后悔。}\n\n@event letter_parcel_from_home\n# en: A Parcel From Home\ntitle [0fa1f2] = 家里寄来的包裹\n# en: A parcel from home, squashed by the post: {~a cake that has become a different shape of cake|a tin of biscuits and a jar of jam|knitted socks and a fruit loaf}, and a letter that says, in four different hands, that everyone is well and the cat is not.\ntext [51b9c8] = 家里寄来的包裹，被邮路压扁了：{~一块已经变成另一种形状的蛋糕|一罐饼干和一罐果酱|织好的袜子和一条水果面包}，还有一封信，四种笔迹轮流写着：大家都好，猫不太好。\n# en: A brown paper parcel tied with string, opened to show a squashed cake, a pair of knitted socks and a short letter.\nscene [324a7a] = 一个用绳子捆着的牛皮纸包裹，打开后露出一块压扁的蛋糕、一双织好的袜子和一封短信。\n# en: Share it with everyone around you\nopt.left.label [c39a82] = 分给身边的每个人\n# en: {~You pass it round and it vanishes in minutes. People you barely know thank you by name the next day.|You share it out to the last crumb. Someone gives you a biscuit back, out of pity.|You cut the fruit loaf into as many slices as there are hands. The socks you keep.}\nopt.left.say.1 [33c1ad] = {~你传了一圈，几分钟就没了。第二天，几个你几乎不认识的人叫着你的名字道谢。|你分到最后一点渣。有人出于同情还了你一块饼干。|你把果干面包切成和伸过来的手一样多的片。袜子你留下了。}\n# en: Keep it for yourself\nopt.right.label [676f93] = 留给自己\n# en: {~You eat it slowly, over a week, a slice a night. It tastes like your kitchen table.|You hide it under your bed and ration it. It lasts, and so does the feeling of home.|You keep the loaf for the cold nights and wear the socks to bed. Both last till the end of the month.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d1c823] = {~你慢慢吃，吃了一周，一晚一片。吃起来像你家的餐桌。|你把它藏在床底下，省着吃。它撑了很久，家的感觉也是。|你把面包留到冷夜里吃，穿着袜子睡觉。两样都撑到了月底。}\n\n@event letter_wrong_address\n# en: Not For You\ntitle [2eac86] = 不是给你的\n# en: A letter arrives that isn't yours: the right house, the wrong name, a perfumed envelope sealed with green wax. It's addressed to someone you've never heard of. The seal is very thin.\ntext [b48d1a] = 来了一封不是你的信：门牌对，名字错，一只喷了香水的信封，用绿色火漆封着。收信人是个你从没听说过的人。封口非常薄。\n# en: A sealed envelope addressed in an unfamiliar hand to a name that isn't the reader's, lying on a windowsill beside a stamp.\nscene [2f479a] = 窗台上一个陌生笔迹的封好的信封，收信人不是读信的人，旁边放着一张邮票。\n# en: Forward it unopened\nopt.left.label [30b9df] = 原封不动转寄\n# en: You write \"not known here, please try again\" on the back, add a stamp of your own and send it on its way. You'll never know what it said, which is how it should be.\nopt.left.say.1 [9a3fdb] = 你在背面写上“查无此人，请重新投递”，贴上自己的邮票寄走了。你永远不会知道信里写了什么，这本来就该如此。\n# en: Open it, just to see\nopt.right.label [d16d56] = 拆开看看\n# en: It's a love letter, and a bad one, full of rhymes on \"moon\". You reseal it carefully and send it on, feeling worse than you expected.\nopt.right.say.1 [b2636c] = 是一封情书，还是一封很糟的情书，满篇都押着“月亮”的韵。你小心地重新封好寄走，心里比想象的更不是滋味。\n\n@event letter_bookshop_bill\n# en: A Bill You Don't Remember\ntitle [d2015b] = 一张你不记得的账单\n# en: A bill from the village bookshop for a book called \"On Small Explosions\", which you have never seen, never borrowed and never wanted. The bookseller writes that you signed for it. The signature looks a little like yours.\ntext [674d82] = 山下小镇书店寄来的账单，买的是一本叫《论小型爆炸》的书，你从没见过，从没借过，也从没想要过。书商说你签收了。那个签名看上去有点像你的。\n# en: A crisp bill from a bookshop, an itemised list with one title circled, held up beside a student's puzzled face.\nscene [ffa128] = 一张书店的账单，列着清单，其中一本书名被圈了起来，举在一个困惑的学生脸旁。\n# en: Pay it and be done\nopt.left.label [9a6124] = 付了了事\n# en: You pay it. A week later the book arrives, and it's actually quite good. You still don't know who signed for it.\nopt.left.say.1 [4adf54] = 你付了钱。一周后书到了，其实还挺好看的。你仍然不知道是谁签收的。\n# en: Write back and dispute it\nopt.right.label [00c863] = 回信提出异议\n# en: You write a polite, firm letter. The bookseller writes back: wrong student, same name, profuse apologies, and a bookmark by way of sorry.\nopt.right.say.1 [2d6831] = 你写了一封礼貌而坚决的信。书商回信说：搞错了学生，同名，连连道歉，还附上一枚书签聊表歉意。\n# en: The bookseller replies with a copy of the signature and a note that she has \"dealt with your sort before\". You pay, and add a stiff note of your own.\nopt.right.say.2 [cabe9d] = 书商回信附了一份签名副本，还说她“以前就对付过你这种人”。你付了钱，也附上一张措辞生硬的字条。\n# en: The bookseller replies with a copy of the signature and a note that she has \"dealt with your sort before\". Your purse is empty, so you work the bill off, shelving her stock for a long, dusty afternoon.\nopt.right.say.3 [1d253f] = 书商回信附上了签名的抄件，还写着她“以前就对付过你这种人”。你的钱袋空空，只好去帮她上架整整一个尘土飞扬的下午，把账抵掉。\n\n@event letter_old_friend\n# en: From Before\ntitle [5c8cd8] = 从前\n# en: A letter from someone you grew up with, from before Lumenhold. {~They're apprenticed to a cooper now.|They're getting married in the spring.|They've moved to the city and hate it.} The letter asks what magic is like. It's a good question, and you don't know how to answer it.\ntext [606db9] = 一个从小一起长大的人写来的信，那是流明堡之前的事了。{~TA 现在给一个箍桶匠当学徒。|TA 春天要结婚了。|TA 搬进城里，讨厌那儿。}信里问魔法是什么样的。这是个好问题，你不知道怎么回答。\n# en: A letter in a familiar childhood hand, with a small drawing of a village street in the margin.\nscene [050c3d] = 一封熟悉的童年笔迹写的信，页边画着一条小镇街道。\n# en: Write back pages and pages\nopt.left.label [a415ae] = 写上一页又一页\n# en: You try to explain magic, and the castle, and what it's like to fail an exam in front of a ghost. It takes four pages and an extra stamp. It's the best letter you've written.\nopt.left.say.1 [0d9849] = 你试着解释魔法，解释城堡，解释当着一个幽灵考砸是什么感觉。写了四页，多贴了一张邮票。这是你写过最好的信。\n# en: Send a postcard of the towers\nopt.right.label [5c4590] = 寄一张七塔明信片\n# en: A postcard of the seven towers, and \"It's hard to explain. Come and see?\" You both know they won't. It's a kind postcard all the same.\nopt.right.say.1 [5c3765] = 一张七塔明信片，写着：“很难解释。来看看？”你们都知道对方不会来。但这仍是一张温暖的明信片。\n\n@event letter_ledger_competition\n# en: The Holiday Essay Prize\ntitle [413b7f] = 假期征文奖\n# en: The Lumen Ledger's holiday issue has found you: an essay prize, \"What I Did Not Do This Holiday\", five hundred words, the winner printed on the front page and paid in crowns.\ntext [5510d3] = 《流明纪事报》的假期特刊找到了你：一个征文奖，题目是“我这个假期没做的事”，五百字，获奖作品登头版，奖金以克朗支付。\n# en: A folded copy of a student newspaper with a competition notice circled in red ink.\nscene [ca3ca9] = 一份折起来的学生报纸，一则征文启事被红墨水圈了出来。\n# en: Write an entry\nopt.left.label [e2f007] = 写一篇投稿\n# en: Your essay about the lake you didn't swim in wins. It's printed on the front page, a little cut, under your name spelled right for once.\nopt.left.say.1 [162172] = 你那篇关于没去游泳的那片湖的文章获奖了。它登上了头版，删了一点，署名难得拼对了。\n# en: You write a good essay that doesn't win. The winner wrote about a goat. Everyone agrees the goat was the right choice.\nopt.left.say.2 [4a0cae] = 你写了篇好文章，但没获奖。获奖的写的是一只山羊。大家一致认为选山羊是对的。\n# en: Let someone else win\nopt.right.label [23e569] = 让别人去赢\n# en: You don't write a word of it, and spend the afternoon doing one of the things you didn't do.\nopt.right.say.1 [85c954] = 你一个字也没写，把下午花在了做一件你没做过的事上。\n\n@event letter_from_a_firstyear\n# en: Advice, Please\ntitle [82c643] = 求教\n# en: A first-year you once showed the way to the library has written to you over the break. They're thinking of leaving. The letter is short, crossed out in places, and ends: \"Did you ever feel like this?\"\ntext [9ac181] = 一个你曾给指过去图书馆的路的一年级生，在假期里给你写信。TA 在考虑退学。信很短，有些地方划掉了，结尾是：“你有过这种感觉吗？”\n# en: A short anxious letter on cheap paper, several words crossed out, a small blot where the writer pressed too hard.\nscene [ed0b56] = 一封写在廉价纸上的焦虑短信，几个词被划掉，一处墨迹是写信人用力过猛留下的。\n# en: Write back honestly, at length\nopt.left.label [e52764] = 认真地写一封长信\n# en: You tell them about your own worst month, which isn't easy to write. Next term they find you at breakfast and say they stayed because of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f41b21] = 你跟 TA 讲了你自己最糟的那个月，这并不好写。下学期，TA 在早餐时找到你，说就是因为那封信才留下来的。\n# en: Send a few kind lines\nopt.right.label [095653] = 写几句暖心话\n# en: \"Yes. It passes. Come back.\" It's short, and it's true. You hope it's enough. At the start of term you see them in the queue for the barge.\nopt.right.say.1 [0f9557] = “有过。会过去的。回来吧。”很短，但是真话。你希望这就够了。开学时，你在登驳船的队伍里看见了 TA。\n\n@event letter_registrar_forms\n# en: Forms in Triplicate\ntitle [13e7ce] = 一式三份的表格\n# en: The registrar's office has sent forms, in triplicate, about next term: your courses, your board, your \"intentions regarding extracurricular activities\". There is a box marked \"Other (explain)\". It is very small.\ntext [f55f6a] = 教务处寄来了下学期的表格，一式三份：你的课程、你的食宿、你的“课外活动意向”。有一栏写着“其他（请说明）”。格子非常小。\n# en: A thick envelope of forms with the academy's seal, three identical copies of everything fanned out on a table.\nscene [808b43] = 一个盖着学院火漆的厚信封装满表格，所有东西都有三份一模一样的，在桌上摊成扇形。\n# en: Fill them in properly now\nopt.left.label [3b10e6] = 现在就认真填好\n# en: An afternoon of copying your own name three times. At the start of term, your enrolment takes four minutes while everyone else queues. Smug is a feeling too.\nopt.left.say.1 [ab1a4f] = 一个下午都在把自己的名字抄三遍。开学时，别人都在排队，你四分钟就选完了课。得意也是一种感觉。\n# en: Leave them until the barge\nopt.right.label [2dd2ac] = 留到驳船上再说\n# en: You fill them in on the barge, on your knee, in the wind. One copy goes over the side. The registrar sighs at you all the way through enrolment.\nopt.right.say.1 [8ebeff] = 你在驳船上，垫着膝盖，顶着风填表。有一份被吹进了湖里。整个选课过程中，教务员都冲着你叹气。\n\n@event letter_lyra_drills\n# en: A List of Drills\ntitle [04f721] = 训练清单\n# en: Lyra has written. It's a list of drills, numbered, with a note at the bottom: \"Do these. You're too slow. Everyone is.\" There's a postscript under that, scratched out and rewritten three times and then scratched out for good.\ntext [d1f640] = 莱拉来信了。是一份训练清单，编了号，底下一行：“照做。你太慢了。大家都太慢。”再下面有一句附言，划掉重写了三次，最后彻底划掉了。\n# en: A letter of numbered duelling drills in crisp handwriting, a scratched-out postscript at the bottom.\nscene [302edd] = 一封字迹利落、列着编号决斗训练的信，底部一句附言被划掉了。\n# en: Do every drill, and send your times\nopt.left.label [15f2f8] = 全部练完，寄去成绩\n# en: You do them in the cold every morning and post her your times. Her reply is one word: \"Adequate.\" It's underlined, which from Lyra is a bouquet.\nopt.left.say.1 [03a70a] = 你每天早上顶着寒气练完，把成绩寄给她。她的回信只有一个词：“尚可。”还画了线，这在莱拉那里等于一束花。\n# en: Ask what the postscript said\nopt.right.label [435145] = 问附言写了什么\n# en: Two weeks later, one line: \"It said: write back.\" Then, smaller: \"You did. Good.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [2bbcc6] = 两周后，一行字：“写的是：回信。”下面小一点：“你回了。很好。”\n# en: She ignores the question entirely and sends a second list, longer, with no postscript at all.\nopt.right.say.2 [cab3bf] = 她对这个问题完全不理，又寄来第二份清单，更长，一句附言也没有。\n\n@event letter_lyra_book\n# en: Foundations of Flame\ntitle [19e259] = 火焰基础\n# en: A parcel from Lyra: her own copy of \"Foundations of Flame\", every margin crowded with notes in red ink. On the flyleaf: \"You'll only underline the wrong bits. So I've done it for you.\" The handwriting goes a little uneven at the end.\ntext [5b0778] = 莱拉寄来的包裹：她自己的那本《火焰基础》，每一处页边都挤满了红墨水的批注。扉页上写着：“你只会画错重点。所以我替你画好了。”最后几个字有点歪。\n# en: A worn copy of a spellbook unwrapped from brown paper, every margin full of small, fierce annotations in red ink.\nscene [652cfa] = 一本翻旧了的咒语书从牛皮纸里拆出来，每处页边都是红墨水写的小而凶的批注。\n# en: Read every note in the margins\nopt.left.label [4a9afd] = 读完页边每一条批注\n# en: Her notes are sharp, funny and occasionally rude about the author. On page ninety there's a small flame drawn beside a paragraph, and your initials.\nopt.left.say.1 [891eea] = 她的批注犀利、好笑，偶尔还对作者出言不逊。第九十页的一段旁边画着一朵小火苗，还有你名字的缩写。\n# en: Annotate it back and return it\nopt.right.label [4d3ceb] = 批注回去再寄还\n# en: You add your own notes in green and post it back at your own expense. Her reply says you're wrong about chapter four. She keeps the book, and your notes, and doesn't cross them out.\nopt.right.say.1 [0b0409] = 你用绿墨水添上自己的批注，自掏邮费寄了回去。她回信说你对第四章的理解是错的。她留下了书，也留下了你的批注，一条都没划掉。\n\n@event letter_tobias_goats\n# en: Four Pages of Goats\ntitle [1d32a6] = 四页山羊\n# en: Tobias has written four pages, mostly about goats: one has escaped, one has eaten a hat, and one is, he writes, \"going through something\". Tucked in the fold is a recipe for his gran's barley soup, \"for when the castle food gets you down\".\ntext [38e597] = 托比亚斯写了四页，大部分是关于山羊的：一只跑了，一只吃了顶帽子，还有一只，他写道，“正在经历一些事”。信纸折缝里夹着他奶奶的大麦汤菜谱，“城堡的饭让你难受时喝”。\n# en: A long letter in a big, careful hand with a drawing of a goat in the corner and a recipe card tucked into the fold.\nscene [cdd1b2] = 一封长信，字又大又认真，角落里画着一只山羊，折缝里夹着一张菜谱卡。\n# en: Make the soup\nopt.left.label [ae568b] = 做那道汤\n# en: You buy the barley and borrow a pot. The soup is thick and plain and exactly what you needed. You write and tell him so; he writes back that his gran says you're welcome.\nopt.left.say.1 [524420] = 你买了大麦，借了口锅。汤又稠又朴素，正是你需要的。你写信告诉他；他回信说奶奶说不客气。\n# en: Write back with a story of your own\nopt.right.label [fdb4e3] = 回他一个你的故事\n# en: You send him the story of the ghost in the bathtub, at length. His reply says he read it to the goats, and that the one going through something seemed to feel better.\nopt.right.say.1 [920678] = 你把浴缸里那个幽灵的故事详详细细地寄给他。他回信说念给山羊听了，那只正在经历一些事的好像好些了。\n\n@event letter_tobias_seeds\n# en: Seeds for a Windowsill\ntitle [404277] = 窗台上的种子\n# en: A packet of seeds from Tobias, tied with garden twine. \"For your windowsill. They'll keep you company. Water them Tuesdays.\" Then, squashed in at the bottom: \"Sorry if that's soppy.\"\ntext [bee9b5] = 托比亚斯寄来一包种子，用园艺麻绳系着。“放你窗台上。它们会陪着你。周二浇水。”底下挤着一行：“要是太肉麻了，抱歉。”\n# en: A small paper packet of seeds tied with garden twine, a note in big careful handwriting beside it.\nscene [288954] = 一小包用园艺麻绳系着的纸袋种子，旁边一张字又大又认真的字条。\n# en: Plant them straight away\nopt.left.label [31f418] = 马上种下\n# en: Three shoots by the end of the month, leaning toward the light. You water them on Tuesdays and think of him on the other days too.\nopt.left.say.1 [1f93d2] = 到月底冒出了三棵芽，朝着光倾斜。你每周二浇水，其他日子也会想起他。\n# en: Write back that it isn't soppy\nopt.right.label [0e8c61] = 回信说一点也不肉麻\n# en: You write that it isn't soppy, and then a whole page about why. His reply is two lines long and you can tell he went red writing it.\nopt.right.say.1 [4e2c28] = 你写道一点也不肉麻，然后用整整一页解释为什么。他的回信只有两行，你看得出他写的时候脸红了。\n\n@event letter_seraphine_card\n# en: Half a Sentence\ntitle [604144] = 半句话\n# en: An envelope from Seraphine with a single tarot card inside, the Wheel, and half a sentence on the back in silver ink: \"Not this week but the one after, don't take the\"\ntext [e989bc] = 瑟拉芬寄来的信封里只有一张塔罗牌，命运之轮，背面用银墨水写着半句话：“不是这周而是下周，别走那条”\n# en: A single tarot card in an envelope, with half a sentence written across the back in silver ink.\nscene [ca331d] = 信封里一张塔罗牌，背面用银墨水写着半句话。\n# en: Try to work out the rest\nopt.left.label [4089a7] = 试着猜出后半句\n# en: You spend an evening with the card and every book on tarot you can find, and spend the following week avoiding stairs, carts and soup. Nothing happens. Probably.\nopt.left.say.1 [e71146] = 你对着那张牌和能找到的每本塔罗书耗了一个晚上，接下来一周躲着楼梯、马车和汤。什么也没发生。大概吧。\n# en: Write and ask her to finish it\nopt.right.label [b3478c] = 写信请她写完\n# en: Her reply comes ten days later, and finishes the sentence: \"…long way round. Or do. It's lovely either way.\" It is, when you take it.\nopt.right.say.1 [172042] = 十天后她的回信到了，补完了那句话：“……远路。或者走也行。怎样都很好。”等你真走的时候，确实很好。\n# en: You date your letter clearly at the top and underline it, the way she needs. Her reply begins: \"Thank you for the today.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [fd7de6] = 你在信的顶端清清楚楚地写上日期，还划了线，就像她需要的那样。她的回信开头写着：“谢谢你给我的今天。”\n\n@event letter_seraphine_painting\n# en: A Painting of You\ntitle [ef73c2] = 你的画像\n# en: Seraphine has sent a small watercolour: you, reading under a tree in blossom, lit from one side. It is very good. You have never sat under that tree, and it isn't spring.\ntext [e00bbd] = 瑟拉芬寄来一幅小小的水彩画：你在一棵开花的树下读书，光从一侧照来。画得非常好。你从没在那棵树下坐过，而且现在不是春天。\n# en: A small watercolour of a student reading under a tree in blossom, unwrapped from tissue paper on a desk.\nscene [62b63e] = 书桌上，从薄纸里拆出一幅小水彩，画着一个学生在开花的树下读书。\n# en: Hang it by your bed\nopt.left.label [fe8501] = 挂在床边\n# en: It's the first thing you see each morning. It makes you look kinder than you feel, and you start, a little, to feel it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f624b8] = 这是你每天早上看见的第一样东西。画里的你比你感觉中的自己更温柔，而你开始，一点点地，真的有那种感觉了。\n# en: Write and ask when she saw this\nopt.right.label [32b429] = 写信问她什么时候看到的\n# en: She writes back, \"Next spring,\" and nothing else, and then a second letter the same day: \"I hope that's all right.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [7d6c58] = 她回信写道：“明年春天。”别的什么也没有。同一天又来了第二封：“希望这样没关系。”\n\n@event letter_corvin_bakery\n# en: The Best Bakery in the Kingdom\ntitle [adfc9f] = 王国最好的面包房\n# en: A letter from Corvin, with a map of a town you've never heard of and a cross on one corner. \"Found the best bakery in the kingdom. Can't afford it. Thought you should know where it is, saint, in case you're ever richer than me. Low bar.\"\ntext [a47484] = 科尔文来信，附了一张你从没听说过的小镇的地图，一角打了个叉。“找到了全王国最好的面包房。买不起。觉得你该知道它在哪儿，圣人，万一哪天你比我有钱呢。门槛很低。”\n# en: A hand-drawn map of an unfamiliar town with a cross in ink over one corner, a postscript crammed into the margin.\nscene [b16f79] = 一张手绘的陌生小镇地图，一角用墨水打了个叉，页边挤着一句附言。\n# en: Send him a few crowns for a pastry\nopt.left.label [e868ed] = 寄几枚克朗让他买点心\n# en: You fold a few crowns into the reply. His next letter is mostly about the pastry, in loving detail, and then, at the very end and without a joke: \"Thank you.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [868bcb] = 你在回信里夹了几枚克朗。他下一封信几乎全在讲那块点心，细节满怀深情，然后在最末尾，没开玩笑：“谢谢。”\n# en: Write back a map of your own\nopt.right.label [06c636] = 回他一张你的地图\n# en: You draw him a map of every place you've found that he hasn't, with crosses and a key. He writes back that two of them are wrong and one of them is brilliant.\nopt.right.say.1 [15af53] = 你给他画了一张地图，标出你找到而他没找到的每个地方，打上叉，配了图例。他回信说其中两个是错的，有一个绝了。\n\n@event letter_corvin_no_jokes\n# en: A Letter With No Jokes\ntitle [69148b] = 一封不开玩笑的信\n# en: Corvin's letter has no jokes in it. You read it twice to be sure. It says the place is too quiet, that he keeps turning to say something to you, and that he isn't used to that. It isn't signed \"your favourite thief\". It's just signed.\ntext [04e616] = 科尔文的信里没有一句玩笑。你读了两遍确认。信上说那地方太安静了，他总忍不住转身想跟你说点什么，还不习惯这样。落款不是“你最喜欢的小偷”。只是落了款。\n# en: A single short page in quick handwriting, with no doodles and no crossings-out, folded very small.\nscene [12b61e] = 一页短短的信，字迹飞快，没有涂鸦，没有涂改，折得很小。\n# en: Write back just as plainly\nopt.left.label [82f63a] = 同样坦白地回信\n# en: You write back with no jokes either, and it's harder than you thought. His reply comes by return of post, and the first line is a joke, and the second line isn't.\nopt.left.say.1 [19f9f9] = 你回信时也没开玩笑，这比你想的难。他的回信很快就到了，第一行是个玩笑，第二行不是。\n# en: Send a joke to make it easier\nopt.right.label [e1a744] = 寄个笑话缓和一下\n# en: You send him the worst joke you know. He replies with a worse one, and you're both on safer ground. You wonder, later, if you missed something.\nopt.right.say.1 [ca8efc] = 你把你知道的最烂的笑话寄给他。他回了个更烂的，你们都回到了安全地带。后来你想，自己是不是错过了什么。\n\n@event letter_mir_blueprint\n# en: A Letter That Folds Itself\ntitle [059cc4] = 会自己折起来的信\n# en: Mir's letter starts folding itself back up while you're still reading it. Inside is a blueprint for a self-folding letter (\"PROBABLY works!!\") and a page of excited tangents about hinges.\ntext [08a336] = 你还没读完，米尔的信就开始自己折回去了。里面是一张自折信的设计图（“大概能用！！”），外加一页关于铰链的兴奋跑题。\n# en: A letter folding itself back up on a desk like a paper bird, a blueprint with arrows and exclamation marks spread out underneath.\nscene [30a2dd] = 一封信在桌上像纸鸟一样自己折起来，下面摊着一张画满箭头和感叹号的设计图。\n# en: Build one yourself\nopt.left.label [ce7e08] = 自己做一个\n# en: Wire, wax paper and one small enchantment. Your letter folds itself into a perfect swan and then, unprompted, into a slightly worse frog. Mir's reply is all exclamation marks.\nopt.left.say.1 [faa571] = 铁丝、蜡纸和一个小小的附魔。你的信把自己折成一只完美的天鹅，然后自作主张地又折成一只稍差的青蛙。米尔的回信全是感叹号。\n# en: Write back with improvements\nopt.right.label [d39ffe] = 回信提改进意见\n# en: You suggest a stiffer hinge and a slower fold. Mir writes back that you are a genius and that they've already tried it and it exploded, but in a promising way.\nopt.right.say.1 [e737ed] = 你建议铰链硬一点、折得慢一点。米尔回信说你是天才，还说 TA 已经试过了，炸了，但炸得很有前途。\n\n@event letter_mir_ticking\n# en: Something That Ticks\ntitle [d34a62] = 会滴答响的东西\n# en: A small brass thing the size of a walnut, ticking softly, with a note in Mir's hurried hand: \"It's a metronome. Probably. It's set to a resting heart rate. Mine, actually, it took ages. Anyway!!\"\ntext [d5b61e] = 一个核桃大小的黄铜小东西，轻轻滴答作响，附着米尔潦草的字条：“这是个节拍器。大概吧。调成了静息心率。其实是我的，调了好久。总之！！”\n# en: A tiny brass mechanism the size of a walnut ticking softly on a pillow, a note in hurried handwriting beside it.\nscene [94b0cf] = 枕头上一个核桃大小的黄铜小机关轻轻滴答作响，旁边一张字迹潦草的字条。\n# en: Keep it on your pillow\nopt.left.label [4d185e] = 放在枕头上\n# en: It ticks you to sleep every night, slightly faster than a resting heart rate. You don't mention that. Some nights it keeps you awake.\nopt.left.say.1 [aab89f] = 它每晚滴答着送你入睡，比静息心率稍微快一点。你没提这事。有些晚上它让你睡不着。\n# en: Write back how much you like it\nopt.right.label [e76305] = 回信说你有多喜欢\n# en: You write that you like it very much. Mir's reply is three pages about escapements, and then at the very end, very small: \"good. i'm glad. good.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [5b29e2] = 你写道你非常喜欢。米尔回了三页关于擒纵机构的话，然后在最末尾，很小的字：“好。我很高兴。好。”\n\n@event letter_kael_three_lines\n# en: Three Lines\ntitle [3b7ab0] = 三行字\n# en: A letter from Kael. Three lines, in careful handwriting, with a great deal of white paper below them: the weather, a star he saw, and \"I hope you are well. That is the whole letter.\"\ntext [e2a01b] = 凯尔的来信。三行字，字迹工整，下面是大片的空白：天气，他看见的一颗星，还有“希望你一切都好。信就这些。”\n# en: A sheet of good paper with only three short lines on it in careful handwriting, a great deal of white space below.\nscene [0c0d59] = 一张上好的信纸，只有三行工整的短句，下面大片空白。\n# en: Write back three lines\nopt.left.label [b6a6e8] = 回他三行\n# en: You write back three lines: your weather, a star of your own, and that you're well. His reply comes promptly. It has four lines. You count them twice.\nopt.left.say.1 [98ce2c] = 你回了三行：你那边的天气，你自己的一颗星，还有你很好。他的回信很快就到了。有四行。你数了两遍。\n# en: Write back three pages\nopt.right.label [74ab93] = 回他三页\n# en: You write three pages and pay the extra postage. His reply takes a long time and says only: \"Thank you. I read it four times. I do not know how to answer so much.\" He keeps it, though.\nopt.right.say.1 [a1028b] = 你写了三页，多付了邮费。他的回信隔了很久，只写着：“谢谢。我读了四遍。我不知道怎么回答这么多。”不过他把信留着了。\n\n@event letter_kael_star_chart\n# en: The Sky on Your Birthday\ntitle [480746] = 你出生那晚的星空\n# en: Kael has sent a star chart, inked by hand, with one cluster circled in pencil. The note says: \"The sky on the night you were born, from here. I checked the tables twice. It is a good sky.\"\ntext [b5c1fa] = 凯尔寄来一张手绘的星图，用铅笔圈出了一小簇星星。字条上写：“你出生那晚的天空，从这里看。我查了两遍星表。是片好天空。”\n# en: A hand-inked star chart unfolded on a bed, one small cluster of stars circled in pencil, a short note pinned to the corner.\nscene [6c2418] = 一张手绘墨线星图摊在床上，一小簇星星被铅笔圈起，角上别着一张短字条。\n# en: Find those stars yourself tonight\nopt.left.label [e14a16] = 今晚亲自去找那些星星\n# en: You stand outside in the cold until you find them, low over the roofs. Your feet go numb. It is a good sky; he was right.\nopt.left.say.1 [e70d0d] = 你在寒风里站着，直到在屋顶上方低低的地方找到了它们。你的脚冻麻了。是片好天空；他说得对。\n# en: Thank him plainly\nopt.right.label [790b3e] = 坦白地谢谢他\n# en: You write that it's the kindest thing anyone has sent you. His reply says \"You are welcome.\" Below that, smaller, as if added later: \"Very.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [3fed19] = 你写道，这是别人送过你最体贴的东西。他回信写：“不客气。”下面小一点，像是后来加上的：“非常。”\n# en: You sign it \"until the thaw\". His next letter is the first he signs the same way.\nopt.right.say.2 [f49446] = 你在信末写下“待到冰融”。他的下一封信，第一次用同样的话落款。\n\n@event letter_nessa_deadline\n# en: URGENT\ntitle [44fe30] = 急件\n# en: A letter from Nessa, URGENT underlined three times: the Ledger needs three hundred words on holidays for the first issue of term, \"interesting ones please, the last six were about weather\". Deadline circled. Twice.\ntext [1b7daf] = 内莎来信，“急件”两个字下面画了三道线：《纪事报》开学第一期需要三百字的假期见闻，“要有意思的，上六篇都在写天气”。截稿日期圈了出来。圈了两次。\n# en: A letter with URGENT written across the top in capitals and underlined three times, a deadline circled at the bottom.\nscene [cadd6e] = 一封信顶上用大写写着“急件”，画了三道线，底部的截稿日期被圈了起来。\n# en: Write the piece\nopt.left.label [01f165] = 写这篇稿子\n# en: You spend an evening on three hundred words and post them. Nessa cuts eighty, prints the rest on page four and writes back \"Good. Rewrite the ending next time.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [4915d3] = 你花一个晚上写了三百字寄去。内莎删了八十字，把剩下的登在第四版，回信说：“不错。下次把结尾重写。”\n# en: Write back off the record\nopt.right.label [6db94e] = 回一封不公开的信\n# en: You write back with no piece at all, just how she is. Her reply has no capitals in it. It's the longest thing she's ever written that she isn't going to print.\nopt.right.say.1 [9ae8c1] = 你一篇稿子也没写，只问她过得怎么样。她的回信里没有一个大写词。这是她写过的最长的一篇不打算刊登的东西。\n\n@event letter_nessa_off_record\n# en: Off the Record\ntitle [a5d3b6] = 不公开\n# en: Nessa's letter has OFF THE RECORD at the top, and then, for once, short sentences with nothing to sell. She's tired. The holidays are long. She keeps writing headlines about you in her head and crossing them out.\ntext [ec4fcf] = 内莎的信顶上写着“不公开”，下面难得是一些没什么可推销的短句。她累了。假期很长。她总在脑子里给你写标题，又一个个划掉。\n# en: A letter with OFF THE RECORD written at the top and then, for once, a page of short, unhurried sentences.\nscene [2fa8be] = 一封信顶上写着“不公开”，下面难得是一整页简短而从容的句子。\n# en: Reply off the record too\nopt.left.label [f9cfa8] = 也回一封不公开的\n# en: You write back slowly and honestly, which costs you more than you expected. Her answer is posted the day your letter must have reached her, which means she wrote it the moment she read yours.\nopt.left.say.1 [179402] = 你慢慢地、诚实地回了信，代价比你想的大。她的回信恰好在你的信寄到那天寄出，说明她一读完你的信就动笔了。\n# en: Ask to see the headlines\nopt.right.label [cb2616] = 要看看那些标题\n# en: She sends three. One is unprintable, one is very sweet and one is \"LOCAL STUDENT STILL NOT REPLYING\", which is fair. You reply at once.\nopt.right.say.1 [8d60fc] = 她寄来三个。一个没法登，一个很甜，还有一个是“本地学生仍未回信”，这倒公平。你立刻回了信。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/money.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/money.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event aid_notice\n# en: The Hardship Notice\ntitle [a50a45] = 困难补助告示\n# en: {A small notice by the bursar's door reads \"Hardship fund. Applications in writing. All circumstances considered.\"|The hardship notice again, under a new one about lost owls.|Someone has underlined \"all circumstances\" on the hardship notice.} {Three students are pretending not to queue.|The ink on the forms is still wet.|The bursar's door is ajar.}\ntext [071496] = {财务官门边贴着一张小告示：“困难基金。书面申请。各种情况均予考虑。”|又是那张困难补助告示，上面盖了一张寻找走失猫头鹰的新告示。|有人在困难补助告示的“各种情况”下面画了线。}{三个学生假装没在排队。|表格上的墨迹还没干。|财务官的门半开着。}\n# en: A notice pinned beside the bursar's office door, half covered by a newer notice about lost owls, a queue of three students pretending not to queue.\nscene [b50ea8] = 财务官办公室门边钉着一张告示，被一张关于走失猫头鹰的新告示盖住了一半，三个学生假装没在排队。\n# en: Fill in the forms\nopt.left.label [396803] = 填写表格\n# en: {Four pages. One asks you to list everything you own. It doesn't take long, and that is the hard part.|You fill in the forms at a library table with your arm around them so nobody can see.|The bursar's door is open, so you hand the forms in yourself. \"All circumstances,\" the bursar says, reading. \"Yes.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [5ae318] = {四页纸。其中一页要你列出你拥有的所有东西。没花多少时间，这才是难受的地方。|你在图书馆的桌子上填表，用胳膊挡着，不让别人看见。|财务室的门开着，你就亲手把表交了进去。“一切情况，”财务主管边看边说，“是的。”}\n# en: Not yet\nopt.right.label [8dd27c] = 先不了\n# en: {You walk past. Your pride is intact, and so is your empty purse.|Another month, you tell yourself. You have a button left.|You read \"all circumstances\" twice and walk past the open door. Not yet.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6ca79a] = {你走了过去。你的自尊完好无损，你的空钱袋也是。|再等一个月，你对自己说。你还剩一颗纽扣。|你把“一切情况”读了两遍，从敞开的门前走了过去。还不到时候。}\n\n@event aid_interview\n# en: The Bursar Will See You\ntitle [7f82b3] = 财务官要见你\n# en: The bursar has your forms open under her hand and a ledger the size of a tombstone at her elbow. \"The fund is small,\" she says, \"and the hands are many. Tell me why it should be yours. Or, if you'd rather not beg, I can lend you sixty crowns against your future, at no interest.\"\ntext [17eea6] = 财务官一只手按着你的申请表，手肘边是一本墓碑那么大的账簿。“基金很少，”她说，“伸手的人很多。告诉我为什么该给你。或者，如果你不想求人，我可以以你的前途作抵押借给你六十克朗，不收利息。”\n# en: A small cluttered office, a grey-haired bursar behind a ledger as large as a tombstone, a student sitting very upright on a hard chair.\nscene [9486c8] = 一间狭小杂乱的办公室，一位灰发的财务官坐在一本墓碑大小的账簿后面，一个学生笔直地坐在一张硬椅子上。\n# en: Make your case\nopt.left.label [d1740a] = 陈述你的理由\n# en: You tell her plainly, and she listens without pity, which helps. \"One term,\" she says, and stamps it. \"Come back if it's still hard.\" Walking out, you feel both lighter and smaller.\nopt.left.say.1 [5528de] = 你直截了当地说了，她听着，不带怜悯，这反倒有帮助。“一个学期，”她说着盖了章。“如果还是困难，再来。”走出去时，你觉得轻松了，也觉得自己变小了。\n# en: She hears you out, and then reads you the list of people ahead of you. Some of their circumstances are worse. \"Next year,\" she says, kindly. You almost wish she hadn't been kind.\nopt.left.say.2 [fc9f6d] = 她听你说完，然后把排在你前面的人的名单念给你听。有些人的情况更糟。“明年吧，”她和气地说。你几乎希望她没那么和气。\n# en: Take the loan\nopt.right.label [e30491] = 接受贷款\n# en: Sixty crowns, counted out in old coins, and your name in the tombstone ledger. \"Eight a month, when you can, and a little less at the end,\" she says. \"I'm a patient woman. The ledger is more patient still.\" It sits on your shoulder from then on.\nopt.right.say.1 [4e2ca5] = 六十克朗，用旧硬币数给你，你的名字记进了墓碑账簿。“每月八克朗，付得起的时候再付，最后一次少一点，”她说，“我是个有耐心的女人。账簿比我更有耐心。”从那以后，它一直压在你肩上。\n\n@event favour_vey_letter\n# en: A Discreet Errand\ntitle [14d2e3] = 一件隐秘的差事\n# en: Professor Vey stops you in an empty corridor with a sealed letter and a silver coin. \"A letter for the village. The house with the blue shutters. Into the hand of whoever opens the door, and not a word.\" Her smile is magnificent and gives nothing away.\ntext [adde6b] = 维伊教授在一条空走廊里拦住你，手里拿着一封封好的信和一枚银币。“一封送到村里的信。蓝色百叶窗的那户人家。交到开门的人手里，一个字也别说。”她的笑容光彩夺目，却什么也没透露。\n# en: A professor in a violet coat holding out a sealed letter and a silver coin between two gloved fingers, in an empty theatre corridor.\nscene [6d6c0c] = 一位穿紫罗兰色外套的教授在空荡荡的剧场走廊里，用戴着手套的两根手指递出一封封好的信和一枚银币。\n# en: Deliver it, unread\nopt.left.label [edeb48] = 送去，不看\n# en: The house with the blue shutters is at the far end of the village, in the rain. An old man opens the door, reads the name on the letter and starts to cry. You walk back soaked and never ask.\nopt.left.say.1 [1431e1] = 蓝色百叶窗的那户人家在村子最远的一头，下着雨。一位老人开了门，看了看信上的名字，哭了起来。你浑身湿透地走回去，从来没有问过。\n# en: Hold it up to the light first\nopt.right.label [eda50a] = 先对着光看一眼\n# en: You make out one line: \"...I have never stopped...\" Then you deliver it. The next day Vey says, without looking at you, \"The light in that corridor is very good, isn't it?\"\nopt.right.say.1 [8662e4] = 你辨认出一行字：“……我从未停止……”然后你把信送到了。第二天，维伊教授看都没看你一眼就说：“那条走廊的光线真好，不是吗？”\n\n@event favour_thorne_marking\n# en: Forty Essays\ntitle [fcfc15] = 四十篇论文\n# en: Professor Thorne puts forty first-year essays on your desk. \"Mark them. A crown for every five. Be honest. Honesty is a kindness they will not thank you for.\" He leaves. The first essay begins, \"Fire is hot.\"\ntext [0ac70d] = 索恩教授把四十篇一年级生的论文放在你桌上。“批改。每五篇一克朗。要诚实。诚实是一种他们不会感激的仁慈。”他走了。第一篇的开头是：“火是热的。”\n# en: A stack of forty first-year essays on a desk under a lamp, a red pen, a student with an expression of growing despair.\nscene [e38153] = 灯下桌上一摞四十篇一年级生的论文，一支红笔，一个越来越绝望的学生。\n# en: Mark them honestly\nopt.left.label [5bc996] = 诚实地批改\n# en: Forty essays, eleven of them good, one brilliant and the rest about how fire is hot. Thorne reads your marks and says \"Hm.\" You've learned that \"Hm\" is pay too.\nopt.left.say.1 [a64129] = 四十篇论文，十一篇不错，一篇出色，其余都在讲火是热的。索恩教授看了你的批改，说了声“嗯”。你已经明白，“嗯”也算一种报酬。\n# en: Mark them kindly\nopt.right.label [f8616f] = 宽厚地批改\n# en: You give every first-year a word of encouragement. Thorne raises your marks on three and lowers them on twenty. \"Kindness,\" he says, \"is not the same as a pass.\" He pays you anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [475d7f] = 你给每个一年级生都写了一句鼓励的话。索恩教授把其中三篇的分数提高了，二十篇压低了。“仁慈，”他说，“和及格不是一回事。”他还是付了你钱。\n\n@event favour_amaranth_shift\n# en: The Night Shift\ntitle [99806e] = 夜班\n# en: Sister Amaranth needs a pair of hands for the infirmary's night shift. \"Basins, bandages, and sitting with whoever can't sleep. Eight crowns. Don't faint. If you faint, you're a patient, and patients don't get paid.\"\ntext [bcef7e] = 阿玛兰斯修女需要一双手帮医务室值夜班。“端盆、换绷带，陪着睡不着的人。八克朗。别晕倒。晕倒了你就是病人，病人可拿不到钱。”\n# en: A dim infirmary ward at night, a student in an apron carrying a basin between rows of beds, a stern healer writing by a single lamp.\nscene [6cb985] = 夜里昏暗的医务室病房，一个系着围裙的学生端着水盆走在一排排病床之间，一位严厉的治疗师在一盏灯下写字。\n# en: Take the shift\nopt.left.label [384351] = 值这个夜班\n# en: A first-year with a fever, a prefect with a broken wrist, a boy who just wants someone awake. By dawn you're grey with tiredness. Amaranth says, \"Keep doing that,\" and pays you.\nopt.left.say.1 [f40aef] = 一个发烧的一年级生，一个手腕骨折的级长，一个只是想有人醒着陪他的男孩。到了天亮，你累得脸色发灰。阿玛兰斯修女说“继续这样做”，然后付了你钱。\n# en: Offer to roll bandages by day instead\nopt.right.label [a37891] = 提出白天来卷绷带\n# en: She looks at you over her spectacles and hands you a mountain of linen. \"Bandages it is. Less pay, all of the tedium.\" By the end you could roll them in your sleep.\nopt.right.say.1 [a6e389] = 她从眼镜上方看了你一眼，递给你一大堆亚麻布。“那就卷绷带。报酬少些，枯燥一点不少。”到最后你闭着眼都能卷。\n\n@event upper_year_errands\n# en: Errands for Upper-Years\ntitle [a1842a] = 替高年级跑腿\n# en: {Upper-years pay for errands, a book fetched or a queue stood in or a letter carried to the owlery.|An upper-year waves a list and a coin at you. Word has got round.|\"You again,\" says the upper-year, pleased. \"Good. The usual, plus my laundry.\"} {The list is long.|The coin is small.|One item on the list just says \"find my cat\".}\ntext [f10c64] = {高年级生会付钱让人跑腿，取本书，排个队，或者把信送到猫头鹰塔。|一个高年级生冲你挥着一张清单和一枚硬币。消息已经传开了。|“又是你，”高年级生高兴地说，“好。老样子，再加上我的脏衣服。”}{清单很长。|硬币很小。|清单上有一项只写着“找到我的猫”。}\n# en: A crowded common room, a lounging upper-year holding out a coin and a list, a younger student taking the list with a sigh.\nscene [6dbb95] = 一间拥挤的公共休息室，一个懒洋洋的高年级生递出一枚硬币和一张清单，一个低年级生叹着气接过清单。\n# en: Run the errands\nopt.left.label [54caa0] = 去跑腿\n# en: {You run up and down the towers all evening and are paid in coins and condescension.|You fetch, carry and queue. Your legs are tired; your purse is less light.|The usual, plus the laundry, plus the cat, which is on the owlery roof. You're paid for two of the three.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8ec74e] = {你一整晚在塔楼间上上下下，拿到的报酬是硬币加居高临下。|你取东西、搬东西、排队。腿累了，钱袋没那么轻了。|老一套，加上脏衣服，再加上那只猫，它在猫头鹰棚的屋顶上。三样里你拿到了两样的钱。}\n# en: Name your price\nopt.right.label [979a40] = 开个价\n# en: {\"More, or find your own cat.\" They pay. You find the cat anyway; it was in the laundry.|You haggle like a merchant's child. The upper-year pays with grudging respect.|\"The laundry's extra,\" you say. They pay it, and add the cat for free. You find it in the library.}\nopt.right.say.1 [289112] = {“加钱，不然自己找猫去。”他们付了。你还是把猫找到了，就在脏衣服堆里。|你像商人家的孩子一样讨价还价。高年级生不情不愿地带着几分佩服付了钱。|“洗衣服另算，”你说。对方付了，还白搭了找猫的活儿。你在图书馆里找到了它。}\n# en: {\"Never mind,\" says the upper-year, and gives the list to a first-year behind you. Word gets round of that too.|They laugh, and find someone cheaper. You keep your dignity and nothing else.|\"The laundry's extra,\" you say. The upper-year gives the list to someone who doesn't think so.}\nopt.right.say.2 [e96058] = {“算了，”高年级生说着，把清单给了你身后的一个一年级生。这件事也传开了。|他们笑了，找了个更便宜的人。你保住了尊严，别的什么也没留下。|“洗衣服另算，”你说。那个高年级生把清单给了一个不这么想的人。}\n\n@event pawnbroker_window\n# en: The Pawnbroker's Window\ntitle [8d463b] = 当铺的橱窗\n# en: {The pawnbroker's window is full of other people's treasures, wands and rings and a stuffed owl. The sign says \"Fair prices. Redemption within the year.\"|{flag(pawned_something): The pawnbroker's window again. Something of yours is in it now.|The pawnbroker's window again, the same treasures in new places.}|The pawnbroker nods at you through the glass like an acquaintance.} {A first-year's silver locket is in the front row.|There's a familiar's collar, empty.|The stuffed owl seems to judge you.}\ntext [29c61e] = {当铺的橱窗里满是别人的宝贝，魔杖、戒指，还有一只猫头鹰标本。招牌上写着“价格公道，一年内可赎回”。|{flag(pawned_something): 又是当铺的橱窗。现在里面有一件是你的东西。|又是当铺的橱窗，还是那些宝贝，换了位置。}|当铺老板隔着玻璃冲你点点头，像个熟人。}{一个一年级生的银挂坠摆在第一排。|有一个魔宠项圈，空的。|那只猫头鹰标本好像在评判你。}\n# en: A dusty village shop window crowded with pawned treasures, wands, rings and a stuffed owl, a student looking in with their hands in empty pockets.\nscene [4f25be] = 村里一扇积满灰尘的店铺橱窗，挤满了典当来的宝贝，魔杖、戒指和一只猫头鹰标本，一个学生双手插在空口袋里往里看。\n# en: Pawn something you'd miss\nopt.left.label [5697a9] = 当掉一件你会想念的东西\n# en: {Your good boots, or your grandmother's ring, or the wand-case with your name burned in it. The money is fair; the ticket says \"within the year\". You put the ticket somewhere safe and try not to look at it.|You hand over something you love, and take the money, and walk home faster than usual.|The pawnbroker greets you by name and writes the ticket without asking what it is. The owl watches the whole transaction.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6794d2] = {你的好靴子，或者祖母的戒指，或者烙着你名字的魔杖盒。钱给得公道；当票上写着“一年内”。你把当票收在稳妥的地方，尽量不去看它。|你交出一件心爱的东西，接过钱，走回家的步子比平常快。|当铺老板叫着你的名字打招呼，没问是什么就开了当票。那只猫头鹰全程看着这笔交易。}\n# en: Walk on\nopt.right.label [383259] = 走过去\n# en: {You walk on with your hands in your pockets. They're empty, but they're yours.|Not today. The owl watches you go.|The pawnbroker nods through the glass; you nod back, and walk on. The owl looks unconvinced.}\nopt.right.say.1 [40060d] = {你双手插在口袋里走了过去。口袋是空的，但它们是你的。|今天不了。那只猫头鹰目送你离开。|当铺老板隔着玻璃点头；你也点点头，走了过去。那只猫头鹰看起来并不信服。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/night.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/night.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event newt_company\n# en: Company for the Evening\ntitle [36541c] = 陪客的夜晚\n# en: The woman who runs the upstairs room looks you over. \"The guests like good company. Laugh at the jokes, keep the glasses full, lose gracefully at cards. Nothing more: guests who want more are shown the stair. Every night is a different party, and every party pays.\"\ntext [1705cf] = 经营楼上房间的女人上下打量着你。“客人们喜欢好伙伴。笑他们的笑话，把酒杯添满，打牌时输得体面些。仅此而已——想要更多的客人，会被请下楼。每晚都是不同的聚会，每场聚会都付钱。”\n# en: An upstairs room at a tavern after hours, card tables under low lamps, well-dressed guests laughing, a student in borrowed finery refilling a glass.\nscene [538ab9] = 打烊后酒馆楼上的房间，低垂的灯下摆着牌桌，衣着体面的客人在笑，一个穿着借来华服的学生在添酒。\n# en: Take a seat upstairs\nopt.left.label [389b09] = 上楼入座\n# en: Paid company, a different guest each night.\nopt.left.hint [bccbb5] = 有偿陪客，每晚客人不同。\n# en: She hands you a borrowed jacket that almost fits. \"Smile with your eyes,\" she says. \"The guests can tell.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [4b634c] = 她递给你一件借来的外套，差不多合身。“用眼睛笑，”她说，“客人们分得出来。”\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.right.label [4bf72b] = 今晚不了\n# en: \"Another night, then,\" says the woman, writing something in a small book. You wonder what.\nopt.right.say.1 [9ca2f4] = “那就改天，”那女人说着，在一个小本子上写了些什么。你很想知道她写了什么。\n\n@event newt_company_regular\n# en: The Upstairs Room\ntitle [4e044e] = 楼上的房间\n# en: {The woman who runs the upstairs room knows your face now. \"There's a party tonight,\" she says, already holding out the jacket.|The landlady has your jacket on a peg with your name chalked above it. \"Your table's waiting,\" she says.|\"Your regulars asked after you,\" says the landlady, which is either flattering or a warning. Upstairs the lamps are lit.}\ntext [0fbd63] = {经营楼上房间的女人现在认得你了。“今晚有场聚会。”她说，外套已经递了过来。|老板娘把你的外套挂在钉子上，上方用粉笔写着你的名字。“你的桌子在等你。”她说。|“你的常客问起你了。”老板娘说，这话不知是恭维还是警告。楼上的灯已经亮了。}\n# en: An upstairs room at a tavern after hours, a landlady holding out a borrowed jacket to a student at the top of the stair.\nscene [4791b6] = 酒馆打烊后的楼上房间，楼梯口的老板娘把一件借来的外套递给一个学生。\n# en: Work the room\nopt.left.label [58bf3e] = 去招呼客人\n# en: Paid company; tonight's guests decide the rest.\nopt.left.hint [377aa2] = 有偿陪客；其余看今晚的客人。\n# en: {You button the jacket and go up. It fits a little better than last time.|You take your jacket from its peg and climb the stair into the lamplight.|Your regulars wave you over before you've reached the top of the stair.}\nopt.left.say.1 [eb8cd0] = {你扣好外套上楼。比上次合身了一点。|你从钉子上取下外套，踏着楼梯走进灯光里。|你还没走到楼梯顶，常客们就朝你招手了。}\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.right.label [4bf72b] = 今晚算了\n# en: {\"Next week, then,\" she says, and the jacket goes back on its peg.|She shrugs and chalks a line through tonight beside your name.|\"They'll miss you,\" she says, and it sounds like the truth and the bill at once.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3ac771] = {“那就下周。”她说，外套又挂回了钉子上。|她耸耸肩，在你名字旁边用粉笔把今晚划掉。|“他们会想你的。”她说，听着既像真话，又像账单。}\n\n@event newt_high_table\n# en: The High Table\ntitle [06018c] = 大赌桌\n# en: {Behind the upstairs room there's a back room, and in it the high table. Grisk keeps the bank. Nobody here plays for crowns alone.|The high table again. Grisk nods to you as if you were a regular. You are becoming one.|Grisk has kept your seat warm, he says. It is not a comforting thought.} {A first-year's familiar sits in a cage beside the pot.|Someone has staked a silver wand and is sweating about it.|Somebody's signed promise is in the pot, folded small.}\ntext [4a665f] = {楼上房间后面还有一间后屋，里面就是大赌桌。格里斯克坐庄。这里没人只为克朗下注。|又是大赌桌。格里斯克冲你点点头，就像你是熟客。你正在变成熟客。|格里斯克说他一直给你留着座位。这话可一点也不让人安心。}{一个一年级生的魔宠被关在笼子里，就放在赌注旁边。|有人押上了一根银魔杖，正在冒汗。|赌注里有一张签了名的承诺书，折得小小的。}\n# en: A round table in a back room under a single green lamp, piles of coins, a goblin in a velvet waistcoat keeping the bank.\nscene [f589cb] = 后屋里一张圆桌，头顶一盏绿灯，桌上堆着硬币，一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精在坐庄。\n# en: Stake your purse\nopt.purse.label [6dad53] = 押上你的钱袋\n# en: Guile against the table.\nopt.purse.hint [8deb0b] = 诡计，对抗整张桌子。\n# en: {You read the table like a book you've already finished. The pot slides over, and Grisk applauds politely with two fingers.|Your nerve holds and theirs doesn't. You walk home heavy with silver and hollow with nerves.|Somebody's folded promise is in the pot. You win it, and give it back to the owner unread. Grisk thinks that's hilarious.}\nopt.purse.say.1 [65ecad] = {你把这张牌桌读得像一本早就读完的书。赌注推到了你面前，格里斯克用两根手指礼貌地鼓掌。|你的胆量撑住了，他们的没撑住。你揣着沉甸甸的银币走回家，心里却空落落的。|赌注里有一张折得小小的承诺书。你赢了，没打开就还给了它的主人。格里斯克觉得这太好笑了。}\n# en: {The cards turn on you, then the room does. You leave lighter, and a prefect's lamp is going round the courtyard.|You lose the pot and your temper with it. Grisk sympathises, with interest.|You lose, and Grisk offers to take a signed promise instead of the coin you're short. You pay in coin, all of it.}\nopt.purse.say.2 [6e767c] = {牌先跟你作对，接着整个房间都跟你作对。你走的时候钱袋轻了，一位级长的灯正绕着庭院转。|你输了赌注，脾气也跟着输掉了。格里斯克表示同情，连本带利。|你输了，格里斯克提出可以用一张签了名的承诺书抵你欠的钱。你付了现钱，一分不少。}\n# en: Stake a favour to Grisk\nopt.favour.label [7e2bea] = 押上欠格里斯克的一个人情\n# en: Win big, or owe him a job.\nopt.favour.hint [7556bd] = 赢一大笔，或者欠他一份差事。\n# en: You stake \"one favour, to be named\" and win. Grisk smiles, tears up the promise, and keeps the pieces. \"Next time,\" he says.\nopt.favour.say.1 [985418] = 你押上“一个人情，日后再说”，赢了。格里斯克笑着把承诺书撕了，却把碎片收了起来。“下次吧，”他说。\n# en: You lose, and Grisk folds your promise into his waistcoat pocket like a love letter. \"I'll be in touch,\" he says. \"Soon.\"\nopt.favour.say.2 [d7a232] = 你输了，格里斯克把你的承诺书折好，像收情书一样放进马甲口袋。“我会跟你联系的，”他说，“很快。”\n# en: Play the bone-dice\nopt.health.label [b0d402] = 玩骨骰\n# en: The old northern game. Win or bleed.\nopt.health.hint [2b4c16] = 古老的北方游戏。要么赢，要么流血。\n# en: The bone-dice game is played with a knife between the fingers. You keep all ten and win the pot. Grisk calls you \"dear heart\" for the rest of the night.\nopt.health.say.1 [5a985b] = 骨骰的玩法是把刀子插在指缝之间。你十根手指一根不少，还赢了赌注。整晚格里斯克都管你叫“心肝儿”。\n# en: You win the pot and lose a fair amount of skin from your left hand. Amaranth, in the morning, asks no questions, very loudly.\nopt.health.say.2 [6659bd] = 你赢了赌注，左手却掉了不少皮。第二天早上，阿玛兰斯修女什么也没问，问得非常大声。\n# en: Watch one hand, then go\nopt.leave.label [1c61ac] = 看一局就走\n# en: {You watch a student lose a familiar on a bluff, and go home and hug yours, or your pillow.|You watch, learn and leave. Grisk raises his glass to your back.|You watch a signed promise change hands across the table, and decide never to sign anything in this room.}\nopt.leave.say.1 [d0582e] = {你看着一个学生在一次诈唬里输掉了自己的魔宠，回家后抱紧了你的魔宠，或者你的枕头。|你看了，学了，走了。格里斯克冲着你的背影举了举杯。|你看着一张签了名的承诺书在桌上易手，决定在这个房间里永远什么都不签。}\n\n@event night_partner_asks\n# en: Where Were You?\ntitle [725fa7] = 你去哪儿了？\n# en: {partner} is waiting on the stair with a silver token from the Gilded Newt in {their} palm. It fell out of your coat. \"The upstairs room,\" {they} say{s}. \"People talk. I'd rather hear it from you.\"\ntext [5d738c] = {partner} 在楼梯上等你，掌心里放着一枚镀金蝾螈酒馆的银筹码。它是从你外套里掉出来的。“楼上那个房间，”{they}说，“大家都在议论。我宁愿听你自己说。”\n# en: Two students on a cold stone stair in the morning, one holding a tavern's silver token found in the other's coat.\nscene [67e6d6] = 清晨冰冷的石阶上的两个学生，一个手里拿着从另一个外套里掉出来的酒馆银筹码。\n# en: Tell {them} everything\nopt.left.label [002271] = 把一切都告诉{them}\n# en: You tell the truth: the glasses, the cards, the guests, the stair. {partner} is quiet a long time. \"Thank you for telling me,\" {they} say{s} at last, and means it, and it still costs you both.\nopt.left.say.1 [132cfe] = 你说了实话：酒杯、牌局、客人、楼梯。{partner} 沉默了很久。“谢谢你告诉我，”{they}终于说，是真心的，可你们俩还是都付出了代价。\n# en: \"It's nothing. A card game.\"\nopt.right.label [129d58] = “没什么。就是打牌。”\n# en: {partner} looks at the token, then at you, and puts it in your hand. \"All right,\" {they} say{s}. It isn't. For a week {they} sit{s} a little further away at supper.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b1621] = {partner} 看了看筹码，又看了看你，把它放回你手里。“好吧，”{they}说。并不好。接下来一个星期，晚饭时{they}都坐得离你远了一点。\n\n@event grisk_names_favour\n# en: The Favour, Named\ntitle [44924a] = 人情，到期了\n# en: Grisk is waiting in the library alcove with your promise between two fingers. \"The favour,\" he says. \"A small one. A crate goes from the boathouse to the village. Tonight. Or, if you prefer, the price of the crate.\" He smiles. \"I always offer a choice.\"\ntext [f47834] = 格里斯克在图书馆的壁龛里等你，两根手指夹着你的承诺书。“那个人情，”他说，“很小的一个。有个箱子要从船屋运到村里。今晚。或者，你也可以付清这箱货的价钱。”他笑了。“我总是给人选择的。”\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat waiting in a library alcove, a folded promise held up between two fingers.\nscene [91dfbe] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精在图书馆壁龛里等着，两根手指举着一张折好的承诺书。\n# en: Move the crate\nopt.left.label [f1cc88] = 运箱子\n# en: The crate clinks and, faintly, sings. You don't look inside. Grisk tears up your promise. \"You're good at this,\" he says, which is the worst thing he could say.\nopt.left.say.1 [eb8ddb] = 箱子叮当作响，还隐约在唱歌。你没往里看。格里斯克撕了你的承诺书。“你干这行很在行，”他说，这是他能说出的最糟的一句话。\n# en: Bram stops you on the jetty and lifts the tarp. Charm-wine. The crate is gone, the favour counts as paid, and Bram has your name.\nopt.left.say.2 [818fe5] = 布拉姆在码头上拦住了你，掀开了油布。是魔法酒。箱子没了，人情算是还了，布拉姆也记下了你的名字。\n# en: Pay him off\nopt.right.label [e88f0a] = 花钱了事\n# en: You count out the crowns and Grisk gives you back the promise with a small bow. It feels like buying your own name back. It is, more or less.\nopt.right.say.1 [2b02e3] = 你数出克朗，格里斯克微微鞠躬，把承诺书还给了你。感觉就像花钱买回了自己的名字。差不多就是这么回事。\n\n@event company_birthday\n# en: A Merchant's Birthday\ntitle [da530b] = 商人的生日\n# en: {A cloth merchant from the capital is sixty tonight and wants a room full of young people laughing at his stories.|The cloth merchant is back with another party. He says it's his birthday again, and nobody dares to count.|Another of the merchant's birthdays. He has started inviting his rivals, so that they can watch him be adored.} The cake is shaped like one of his ships.\ntext [9bcb45] = {一位从都城来的布商今晚六十岁，想要满屋子年轻人笑他的故事。|布商又来办派对了。他说今天又是他的生日，没人敢算。|又是商人的生日。他开始邀请他的对手们来，好让他们看看他有多受爱戴。}蛋糕做成了他一艘船的模样。\n# en: A loud birthday party in a tavern's upstairs room, a stout merchant in a paper crown, a cake shaped like a ship.\nscene [762c16] = 酒馆楼上热闹的生日聚会，一个戴纸王冠的胖商人，一块船形蛋糕。\n# en: Be the life of the party\nopt.left.label [365587] = 当全场的开心果\n# en: Charm the room; big tips, a long night.\nopt.left.hint [02f74e] = 讨满屋欢心；小费丰厚，长夜漫漫。\n# en: {You laugh, sing, and dance with his aunt. He tips like a man buying a ship. You get home with the dawn and a sore throat.|He tells the story about the storm off the southern coast again, and you gasp in the right place. The tip is heavy; so are your feet.|You toast him in front of his rivals and he nearly cries. The purse he presses on you is shaped, faintly, like a ship.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a808ac] = {你笑，你唱，还陪他姑妈跳了舞。他给小费的架势像在买船。你天亮才到家，嗓子也哑了。|他又讲了一遍南方海岸外那场风暴，你在该惊呼的地方惊呼。小费很沉，你的脚也很沉。|你当着他对手们的面向他敬酒，他差点哭了。他塞给你的钱袋，隐约也是船的形状。}\n# en: {You laugh a moment too late at the wrong story, and he notices. A thin tip, and a long walk home past the castle's lit windows.|You call his ship by his rival's ship's name. The room goes quiet. The landlady pays you off early.|His rivals are funnier than he is, and you show it. He doesn't forgive it, and the tip says so.}\nopt.left.say.2 [42906b] = {你在一个不对的故事上笑晚了一拍，他注意到了。小费很薄，回家的路很长，一路经过城堡亮着灯的窗。|你把他的船叫成了他对手的船名。满屋一片寂静。老板娘提前把你打发走了。|他的对手们比他有趣，你还表现出来了。他不肯原谅，小费也说明了这一点。}\n# en: Keep the glasses full\nopt.right.label [c78554] = 把酒杯添满\n# en: A quiet night's pay, on your feet.\nopt.right.hint [38af60] = 安静的一晚，站着挣钱。\n# en: {You pour, smile and stay out of the stories. The pay is fair, and the floor is very hard.|You keep the glasses full and your mouth shut. The merchant never learns your name, which suits you.|You pour for him and his rivals alike. Nobody notices you, and you go home with honest money and sore feet.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4127db] = {你倒酒，微笑，不掺和那些故事。工钱公道，地板很硬。|你把酒杯添满，嘴巴闭紧。商人始终不知道你的名字，这正合你意。|你给他和他的对手们一视同仁地倒酒。没人注意到你，你带着干净的钱和酸痛的脚回家。}\n\n@event company_magistrate\n# en: The Magistrate's Card Party\ntitle [967495] = 地方官的牌局\n# en: {A magistrate from the county court is holding a card party, and she likes to win, but not too easily.|The magistrate again. She deals you in without asking, and her eyes say she hasn't forgotten the last game.|The magistrate's party, and a new rule: whoever lets her win too obviously pays for the wine.} The landlady's look says: lose, gracefully.\ntext [9a23ec] = {一位郡法院的地方官在办牌局，她喜欢赢，但不喜欢赢得太容易。|又是那位地方官。她不问就给你发了牌，眼神说明她没忘记上一局。|地方官的牌局，还有一条新规矩：谁让她赢得太明显，谁就付酒钱。}老板娘的眼神说：输，要输得体面。\n# en: A card table under a green lamp, a stern magistrate in a fur collar studying her hand, a student opposite her looking at the ceiling.\nscene [7acc53] = 绿色灯罩下的牌桌，一位穿毛领的严肃女地方官审视着手里的牌，对面的学生望着天花板。\n# en: Lose exactly enough\nopt.left.label [1c11d8] = 输得恰到好处\n# en: Throw the game without being seen to.\nopt.left.hint [001349] = 故意输，又不被看出来。\n# en: {You lose by a hair after a brave fight. She is delighted, and tips accordingly. You feel faintly like a forger.|You let her catch your bluff in the last hand. She laughs for the first time all night, and pays you twice over.|You lose so well she thinks she beat you fairly. The money is good. Lying with cards is still lying.}\nopt.left.say.1 [13726b] = {你奋战一番后以毫厘之差落败。她很高兴，小费也相应丰厚。你隐约觉得自己像个造假的。|最后一手你故意让她识破你的虚张声势。她整晚头一回笑了，付了你双份。|你输得太好了，她以为自己赢得光明正大。钱不错。用牌撒谎，也还是撒谎。}\n# en: {She catches you discarding an ace and puts her cards down. \"Don't patronise me.\" Word gets about, as word from a magistrate does.|You lose too obviously, and pay for the wine, and the story of it is round the village by noon.|She sees through you in three hands and says so to the whole table. The landlady doesn't look at you on the stair.}\nopt.left.say.2 [37e2f6] = {她看见你扔掉一张王牌，把牌放下。“别哄我。”消息传开了，地方官说的话总是传得开。|你输得太明显，付了酒钱，中午之前这事就传遍了全村。|三手牌她就看穿了你，还当着全桌人说了出来。下楼时老板娘没看你一眼。}\n# en: Deal and keep score\nopt.right.label [190c7d] = 发牌记分\n# en: Honest work at the table, smaller pay.\nopt.right.hint [d84b23] = 牌桌上的老实活，钱少些。\n# en: {You deal, count and say nothing. It is the dullest money you've ever earned, and it's clean.|You keep the score, honestly, and she loses honestly, and doesn't tip the scorer.|You deal for four hours. The magistrate wins without help, and nods at you as if you'd had something to do with it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [49cc62] = {你发牌、计分，一言不发。这是你挣过最无聊的钱，也是最干净的。|你老老实实记分，她老老实实地输，也不给记分的小费。|你发了四个小时牌。地方官不靠帮忙就赢了，朝你点点头，好像你也出了份力。}\n\n@event company_masked\n# en: The Masked Night\ntitle [1c4157] = 假面之夜\n# en: {Tonight every guest wears a mask, and nobody says why. The landlady hands you one too, plain black, no feathers.|The masks again. One guest, in lacquered silver, asks for you by the colour of your mask.|The masked night. The silver mask is here, and has saved you the seat beside it.} Without faces the room is easier to read, and harder.\ntext [3ef2fc] = {今晚每位客人都戴着面具，没人说为什么。老板娘也递给你一个，纯黑的，没有羽毛。|又是面具。一位戴银漆面具的客人按面具颜色点名要你。|假面之夜。银面具来了，还给你留了旁边的座位。}没有面孔，这个房间更好读懂，也更难。\n# en: A candlelit upstairs room full of guests in feathered and lacquered masks, one masked figure watching a student without moving.\nscene [e99bf4] = 烛光下的楼上房间，客人们戴着羽毛和漆面具，一个面具人一动不动地看着一个学生。\n# en: Sit with the masks\nopt.left.label [09e12b] = 陪面具客人坐\n# en: The best tips, and nobody's name.\nopt.left.hint [bb5065] = 小费最多，没人留名。\n# en: {You learn to read a room by its hands. You go home with heavy pockets, no names, and a feeling you can't put down.|The silver mask asks you nothing at all, and tips like a duke. You lie awake wondering who that was.|Behind the masks the guests say what they mean. You come home rich and a little colder.}\nopt.left.say.1 [93928e] = {你学会了从手上读懂一个房间。回家时口袋沉甸甸的，没记住一个名字，心里有种放不下的感觉。|银面具什么也没问你，打赏得像个公爵。你醒着躺了半夜，琢磨那是谁。|面具后面，客人们说的都是真心话。你回家时富了，也冷了一点。}\n# en: {A masked guest won't take no for an answer, and nobody can see who it is. You leave by the back stair with a few coins and a very bad feeling.|The silver mask leans too close, and nobody else in the room seems to notice. You go home by the long way.|A guest takes your mask off, laughing, in front of everyone. Someone at the far table looks very like a prefect.}\nopt.left.say.2 [163a3e] = {一位戴面具的客人不肯接受拒绝，而谁也看不见那是谁。你从后楼梯离开，带着几枚硬币和很糟的预感。|银面具凑得太近，房间里别人好像都没注意到。你绕远路回了家。|一位客人笑着当众摘了你的面具。远处桌边有个人看着很像级长。}\n# en: Work the door\nopt.right.label [35e80d] = 守门\n# en: Fair pay in the cold stairwell.\nopt.right.hint [ce08e4] = 冷楼梯间里的公道工钱。\n# en: {You take coats at the door all night. The stairwell is freezing, and every coat smells of a different perfume.|You keep the door, and turn away a guest the landlady points out. The draught gets into your bones.|You work the door and watch the masks arrive one by one. The silver one bows to you, which is worse than being spoken to.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e9a28c] = {你在门口收了一整晚外套。楼梯间冷得要命，每件外套都是不同的香水味。|你守着门，把老板娘指给你的一位客人挡在外面。穿堂风钻进了骨头里。|你守着门，看着面具一个个到来。银面具朝你鞠了一躬，这比跟你说话还糟。}\n\n@event company_patron\n# en: A Guest Who Offers Patronage\ntitle [fadcec] = 想当资助人的客人\n# en: {An old collector of books has asked for \"the student who reads\". He wants company and argument, and he says he funds promising young minds.|The old collector is back with a new pile of books and a new argument. He has been thinking about what you said, for a month.|The collector again. He calls you his protégé to the whole room now, which is flattering and a little alarming.}\ntext [c4b444] = {一位上了年纪的藏书家点名要“那个爱读书的学生”。他想要人陪他聊天、跟他争论，还说他资助有前途的年轻人。|老藏书家又来了，带着一摞新书和一场新争论。他把你上次说的话想了一个月。|又是那位藏书家。他现在当着满屋人叫你他的门生，既受宠若惊，又有点吓人。}\n# en: An elderly scholar in a moth-eaten velvet coat at a tavern table, a pile of rare books beside a purse, talking earnestly to a student.\nscene [d20e0a] = 酒馆桌边一位穿着被虫蛀过的天鹅绒外套的老学者，身旁一摞珍本书和一只钱袋，正认真地和一个学生交谈。\n# en: Take the patron's purse\nopt.left.label [df6446] = 收下资助人的钱\n# en: Generous, and he'll want your evenings.\nopt.left.hint [65b734] = 出手大方，也要占你的晚上。\n# en: {He funds your books for the term, and wants you at his table every week. You argue till two, and sleep through a lecture.|The purse is generous and so is the argument, which runs until the candles do. Your coursework waits.|He pays you to disagree with him. You are good at it, and your exam reading is not getting done.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4a465b] = {他资助你这学期的书，要你每周都去他桌上。你们争到两点，你睡过了一节课。|钱袋很大方，争论也很大方，一直持续到蜡烛烧完。你的功课只好等着。|他付钱让你跟他唱反调。你很擅长，可你的备考书一页也没读。}\n# en: Ask to borrow his books\nopt.right.label [12a896] = 请他借书给你\n# en: Learning instead of money.\nopt.right.hint [dabc48] = 要学问，不要钱。\n# en: {He beams, and lends you three books older than the castle. You read them on the walk home by moonlight.|He lends you his annotated copy, full of furious notes in the margin. They are better than the book.|\"Finally,\" he says, \"someone who wants the books more than the money.\" He gives you a small purse anyway.}\nopt.right.say.1 [74202e] = {他喜笑颜开，借给你三本比城堡还老的书。你借着月光在回家路上读。|他把自己的批注本借给你，页边写满了气势汹汹的笔记。比书本身还好。|“终于，”他说，“有人想要书胜过想要钱。”他还是塞给你一个小钱袋。}\n\n@event company_castle\n# en: Someone from the Castle\ntitle [0bb5ee] = 城堡里来的人\n# en: {Halfway up the stair you see {guest} at the corner table, out of robes, with a glass of wine and the air of someone who would rather not be seen either.|{guest} is at the corner table tonight. You both pretend this is a coincidence.|Tonight it is {guest} in the corner. Half the faculty seem to drink here, and there is an arrangement: nobody has seen anybody.}\ntext [a68b01] = {上楼走到一半，你看见{guest}坐在角落的桌边，没穿袍子，端着一杯酒，一副也不想被人看见的样子。|今晚{guest}坐在角落的桌边。你们都假装这只是巧合。|今晚角落里坐的是{guest}。似乎一半的教职员都在这儿喝酒，而且有个默契：谁也没看见谁。}\n# en: A professor out of academic dress at a tavern card table, a glass of wine halfway to their mouth, looking up at a student holding a tray.\nscene [eaf60e] = 一位没穿学袍的教授坐在酒馆牌桌边，酒杯举到一半，抬头看向一个端着托盘的学生。\n# en: Serve {them} as if you'd never met\nopt.left.label [9487b1] = 装作素不相识\n# en: Discretion pays; a slip gets talked about.\nopt.left.hint [d6bdb3] = 守口如瓶有赏；说漏嘴会被议论。\n# en: {You serve {guest} like any guest, and {they} tip{s} like one, too generously. At breakfast {they} nod{s} to you as if nothing ever happened.|You keep a straight face all night. On the way out {guest} leaves a coin under {their} glass and, beside it, a very small nod.|Neither of you blinks. In class next week {guest} calls on you with something almost like respect.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bd9acf] = {你像招呼任何客人一样招呼{guest}，{they}也像任何客人一样给了小费，给得太多。早餐时{they}朝你点点头，好像什么也没发生过。|你一整晚都绷着脸。离开时{guest}在酒杯下留了一枚硬币，旁边还有一个极轻的点头。|你们谁都没眨眼。下周课上，{guest}点你回答问题时，带着近乎尊重的神情。}\n# en: {You drop a glass when {guest} looks up. Everyone turns. {guest} leaves early, and is cold to you all week.|You say \"Professor\" before you can stop yourself. The table laughs. {guest} does not.|{guest} catches your eye and you both flinch, in front of everyone. By morning half the village knows who was where.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f2b715] = {{guest}一抬头，你就摔了一只杯子。所有人都转过头来。{guest}提前走了，一整周都对你冷冰冰的。|你没忍住叫了一声“教授”。满桌人都笑了。{guest}没笑。|{guest}和你对上眼神，你们俩当众一缩。到早上，半个村子都知道谁在哪儿了。}\n# en: Slip back downstairs\nopt.right.label [29770c] = 溜回楼下\n# en: Give up the night's pay to stay unseen.\nopt.right.hint [23a5a8] = 放弃一晚工钱，不被看见。\n# en: {You give the landlady an excuse and go down the back stair. It's a lost night's pay, and the walk home is sulky.|You hand your tray to another student and leave. {guest} never sees you; your purse notices.|You go home early, and lie awake doing the sum of what you didn't earn.}\nopt.right.say.1 [319f84] = {你随便找了个借口跟老板娘说，从后楼梯下去。损失了一晚工钱，回家的路走得闷闷不乐。|你把托盘交给另一个学生就走了。{guest}从没看见你；你的钱袋可注意到了。|你早早回家，躺着算你没挣到的那笔钱。}\n\n@event company_wants_more\n# en: A Guest Who Wants More\ntitle [8893a4] = 想要更多的客人\n# en: {A guest with rings on every finger has been generous all night, and now pushes a heavy purse across the table. \"Come back to my inn,\" he says. \"Just to talk.\" The house rule is clear.|Another guest, another heavy purse, another invitation \"just to talk\". The landlady is busy across the room.|You know the look now: the generous hour, then the purse, then the invitation. Tonight it comes from a woman in pearls.}\ntext [93c89c] = {一位每根手指都戴着戒指的客人整晚出手大方，现在把一只沉甸甸的钱袋推过桌面。“跟我回旅店吧，”他说，“就聊聊天。”店里的规矩很清楚。|又一位客人，又一只沉甸甸的钱袋，又一句“就聊聊天”。老板娘在房间另一头忙着。|你现在认得这副神情了：先是慷慨的一小时，然后是钱袋，然后是邀请。今晚开口的是一位戴珍珠的女士。}\n# en: A well-dressed guest at a tavern table holding out a heavy purse, a student standing back from the table with arms folded, a landlady watching from the door.\nscene [ccd9ea] = 一位衣着考究的客人在酒馆桌边递出一只沉甸甸的钱袋，一个学生抱着胳膊退开一步，老板娘在门口看着。\n# en: Call the landlady\nopt.left.label [5a9d9b] = 叫老板娘\n# en: The house rule; the tip goes with the guest.\nopt.left.hint [a788f6] = 照店规办；小费跟客人一起走。\n# en: {The landlady has the guest down the stair in a minute, purse and all. You're paid for the hours, and shaky for the rest of the night.|\"The stair,\" says the landlady, and the guest takes it. You keep your wage and lose the tip, and your hands won't stop shaking.|The guest is shown out. The landlady pours you something strong and pays you for the night. It doesn't feel like enough.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f239f0] = {老板娘一分钟就把客人连人带钱袋请下了楼。你拿到了这几个钟头的工钱，剩下的整晚手都在抖。|“楼梯。”老板娘说，客人便下了楼。你保住了工钱，丢了小费，手怎么也停不下抖。|客人被请了出去。老板娘给你倒了杯烈的，付了你一整晚的钱。感觉还是不够。}\n# en: Keep the purse, promise nothing\nopt.right.label [06f60c] = 收下钱袋，什么也不答应\n# en: Talk them round and keep it; it can go wrong.\nopt.right.hint [5afeb3] = 哄住对方留下钱；可能会出岔子。\n# en: {You let him believe tomorrow, and then the day after. He leaves the purse. You feel like a pickpocket with good manners.|You laugh, and keep the purse, and see the guest into a carriage, alone. It's the most you've earned in a night. You don't sleep.|You promise nothing in a way that sounds like something. She leaves happy and you leave rich, and a little ashamed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b9f14b] = {你让他以为是明天，然后是后天。他留下了钱袋。你觉得自己像个彬彬有礼的扒手。|你笑着收下钱袋，把客人送上马车，独自一人。这是你一晚挣得最多的一次。你没睡着。|你什么也没答应，却说得像答应了什么。她高高兴兴地走了，你富富裕裕地走了，还有点羞愧。}\n# en: {He sees through you, and takes his purse back with a hand that grips too hard. The landlady throws him out, too late.|The guest makes a scene on the stair, loudly calling you a cheat. A prefect on the stair hears every word.|She wants the purse back, loudly, and the landlady gives it to her. You go home with nothing but a bruise on your wrist.}\nopt.right.say.2 [97b13d] = {他看穿了你，一把抓回钱袋，手攥得太紧。老板娘把他轰了出去，可已经晚了。|客人在楼梯上大闹，高声骂你是骗子。楼梯上的一个级长听得一字不漏。|她大声要回钱袋，老板娘还给了她。你回家时只带着手腕上的一块淤青。}\n\n@event newt_high_table_raise\n# en: Let It Ride\ntitle [3e70b3] = 让它接着滚\n# en: {Grisk sweeps your winnings into one neat stack and pushes it back into the middle of the table. \"Let it ride,\" he says. \"The second hand pays double. Most people stop here. Most people are dull.\"|Grisk is already shuffling. \"Again?\" he says, as if it were your idea. Your winnings sit in the middle of the table, waiting.|\"Second hand,\" says Grisk, and the players at the next table stop pretending not to watch.} The new dealer is a woman who hasn't smiled once tonight.\ntext [82708b] = {格里斯克把你赢的钱码成整整齐齐的一摞，又推回桌子中央。“让它接着滚，”他说，“第二手翻倍。大多数人到这儿就停了。大多数人都很无趣。”|格里斯克已经在洗牌了。“再来？”他说，好像这是你的主意。你赢的钱就摆在桌子中央，等着。|“第二手，”格里斯克说。隔壁桌的人不再假装没在看。}新来的荷官是个女人，今晚一次都没笑过。\n# en: A goblin banker in a velvet waistcoat pushing a tall stack of coins back into the middle of a green-lamped card table.\nscene [da05e6] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精庄家，把一大摞高高的硬币推回绿灯下牌桌的中央。\n# en: Let it ride\nopt.left.label [c9e833] = 让它接着滚\n# en: Your winnings on one hand; the table plays harder.\nopt.left.hint [23ca72] = 赢来的钱全押一手；这桌打得更狠。\n# en: {The unsmiling dealer turns her last card and looks at you properly for the first time. The pot is yours, and it's heavy.|You bluff on nothing and the whole table folds. Grisk laughs so hard he has to sit down.|You win the second hand by a single card. Somewhere behind you somebody says your name, which is not ideal.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e56bfa] = {不苟言笑的荷官翻开最后一张牌，第一次正眼看了你。彩池归你了，沉甸甸的。|你空手诈唬，整桌人都弃了牌。格里斯克笑得只好坐下。|你只赢了一张牌，拿下第二手。身后有人叫出了你的名字，这可不太妙。}\n# en: {The dealer takes the hand without a flicker. Your winnings go back to the bank, and the walk home is very long.|You read her wrong, and she lets you know it. Grisk pats your shoulder. \"That's the second hand,\" he says. \"It's always the second hand.\"|The pot goes the other way. On the stair down, a lamp moves in the courtyard and you have to wait in the dark for it to pass.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8c7f7e] = {荷官面不改色地吃下这一手。你赢的钱回到了庄家那里，回去的路格外漫长。|你看错了她，她也让你明白了这一点。格里斯克拍拍你的肩。“这就是第二手，”他说，“总是第二手。”|彩池去了另一边。下楼的时候，院子里有盏灯在移动，你只好在黑暗里等它过去。}\n# en: Cash out\nopt.right.label [62e5da] = 见好就收\n# en: Walk away with what you won.\nopt.right.hint [4be82b] = 带着赢来的钱走人。\n# en: {You stand, pocket your winnings and bow to the table. Grisk looks almost disappointed in you.|\"Dull,\" says Grisk fondly, as you go. Your purse doesn't think so.|You leave while the leaving is good, and whistle all the way back to the castle, very quietly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2f406e] = {你站起身，把赢的钱揣进口袋，向全桌鞠了一躬。格里斯克看上去几乎对你有点失望。|“无趣，”你离开时格里斯克亲昵地说。你的钱袋可不这么想。|你趁能走的时候走了，一路吹着口哨回城堡，吹得非常轻。}\n\n@event newt_high_table_last\n# en: Everything on One Hand\ntitle [a24d32] = 一手定输赢\n# en: {The room has gone quiet. Players from every other table have drifted over to stand around yours. Grisk takes off his rings, one by one, and sets them on the pot. \"Everything,\" he says. \"One hand. Then we see who goes home.\"|Grisk deals this one himself. Nobody has done that in years, someone whispers.|The whole back room is standing around your table now. Grisk rolls up his velvet sleeves.} Whatever is in the pot tonight is in it again, and more besides.\ntext [4c94ab] = {屋里安静了下来。别的桌的人都慢慢聚过来，围着你这桌站着。格里斯克把戒指一枚一枚摘下来，放进彩池。“全部，”他说，“一手。然后看谁回家。”|这一手由格里斯克亲自发牌。有人低声说，他已经好多年没这么做过了。|整间后屋的人都围在你这桌旁边了。格里斯克卷起了天鹅绒袖子。}今晚彩池里有的东西又都押了进来，还多了不少。\n# en: A crowd of gamblers standing around one card table in a tavern back room, a goblin banker taking off his rings one by one.\nscene [003020] = 酒馆后屋里，一群赌徒围着一张牌桌站着，一个地精庄家正把戒指一枚一枚摘下来。\n# en: Everything on one hand\nopt.left.label [6166b2] = 一手押上全部\n# en: The biggest pot in the room; Grisk deals.\nopt.left.hint [b65c6b] = 全屋最大的彩池；格里斯克亲自发牌。\n# en: {Grisk turns his last card, looks at yours, and starts to laugh. He puts his rings back on, one by one, and pushes the pot to you. \"Don't come back for a month,\" he says. \"I mean it kindly.\"|You win, and the back room applauds. By morning the village has three versions of the story, and in all of them you are taller.|The whole room breathes out at once. Grisk shakes your hand with both of his. \"A real player,\" he says, and it sounds like a warning.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b90541] = {格里斯克翻开最后一张牌，看了看你的，然后笑了起来。他把戒指一枚一枚戴回去，把彩池推给你。“一个月别来了，”他说，“我是好意。”|你赢了，整间后屋都在鼓掌。到早上，村里已经有了三个版本的故事，每个版本里你都更高大一点。|整屋人同时松了一口气。格里斯克用两只手握住你的手。“真正的玩家，”他说，听着像是一句警告。}\n# en: {Grisk turns a card you didn't think was left in the deck. Your stake goes back to him, a fat slice of the night's winnings with it. He's very kind about it, which is worse.|You lose the hand on one card. The crowd drifts back to their own tables as if nothing happened. On the stair, a prefect's lamp is waiting.|The pot goes to Grisk. He counts it slowly, in front of you, and offers to call you a carriage. There are no carriages.}\nopt.left.say.2 [208094] = {格里斯克翻出一张你以为牌堆里早就没有的牌。你的赌注回到了他手里，还搭上今晚赢来的一大块。他对此非常客气，这更糟。|你差一张牌输了这一手。人群像什么都没发生过一样散回各自的桌子。楼梯上，一盏级长的灯正等着你。|彩池归了格里斯克。他当着你的面慢慢地数，还说要替你叫辆马车。这里根本没有马车。}\n# en: Leave on a winning streak\nopt.right.label [2348d4] = 趁连胜离场\n# en: Two hands won, and a story to tell.\nopt.right.hint [d7c45c] = 赢了两手，还多了个好故事。\n# en: {You stand up in the middle of the hush. \"Another night,\" you say, and leave with two pots in your coat. Grisk applauds, alone, and then everyone does.|You refuse the last hand. The back room is torn between booing and admiration. Your purse settles it.|\"Not tonight,\" you say. Grisk grins all his teeth. \"That,\" he says, \"is how you get to play again.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [3c5c5c] = {你在一片寂静中站了起来。“改天吧，”你说，揣着两份彩池离开。格里斯克独自鼓起掌来，接着所有人都鼓起了掌。|你拒绝了最后一手。后屋的人又想喝倒彩又忍不住佩服。你的钱袋替你作了结论。|“今晚不了，”你说。格里斯克咧开满口牙笑了。“这样，”他说，“你才能下次再来玩。”}\n\n@event company_late_hour\n# en: The Second Party\ntitle [b35304] = 第二场派对\n# en: {Past two, the landlady catches your sleeve on the stair. \"Another party's just come up, and I'm a pair of hands short. Same pay again.\"|A second party is coming up the stair as yours goes down it. The landlady looks at you and raises an eyebrow.|\"One more,\" says the landlady, \"and I'll owe you.\" The castle will be waking in four hours.}\ntext [8abd33] = {过了两点，老板娘在楼梯上拉住你的袖子。“又上来一场派对，我缺一双手。工钱照付一份。”|你这场散了往下走，第二场派对正从楼梯往上来。老板娘看着你，挑了挑眉。|“再来一场，”老板娘说，“算我欠你的。”再过四个钟头，城堡就要醒了。}\n# en: A landlady catching a tired student by the sleeve on a tavern stair long after midnight, a second party arriving noisily below.\nscene [a17ae4] = 午夜过后很久，老板娘在酒馆楼梯上拉住一个疲惫学生的袖子，楼下第二场派对正吵吵嚷嚷地到来。\n# en: Stay till dawn\nopt.left.label [2eacef] = 待到天亮\n# en: Another party's pay; no sleep, and a later walk home.\nopt.left.hint [5cb79d] = 再挣一场的工钱；不睡觉，回去得更晚。\n# en: {You take your jacket back off the peg. The second party is louder than the first.|You splash cold water on your face and go back up. The lamps are still lit, and so are the guests.|You stay. It will be light when you get home, and the castle's early risers will see you come in.}\nopt.left.say.1 [41084c] = {你又从挂钩上取下外套。第二场派对比第一场还吵。|你往脸上泼了把冷水，又回到楼上。灯还亮着，客人们也还精神着。|你留了下来。回去时天已经亮了，城堡里早起的人会看见你进门。}\n# en: Go home to bed\nopt.right.label [7365a0] = 回去睡觉\n# en: One party is enough for one night.\nopt.right.hint [88cbd4] = 一晚一场就够了。\n# en: {You hand back the jacket and walk home under the last of the stars. Your bed has never been so welcome.|\"Next time,\" you tell the landlady. You're asleep before the castle clock strikes three.|You leave the second party to someone else and sleep the four hours you have left like a stone.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f92ac8] = {你交还外套，在最后几颗星星下走回去。你的床从没这么可爱过。|“下次吧，”你对老板娘说。城堡的钟还没敲三下，你就睡着了。|你把第二场派对留给别人，剩下的四个钟头睡得像块石头。}\n\n@event company_wedding\n# en: A Wedding Upstairs\ntitle [6049bb] = 楼上的婚礼\n# en: {A miller's daughter from the next valley married this afternoon, and the party has moved upstairs at the Newt to keep going.|Another wedding upstairs: a carter and a dressmaker, and both families determined to out-dance the other.|A wedding again, and the fiddler from last time, who waves at you with his bow.} The landlady wants the dancing kept going and the families kept apart.\ntext [0f8b0a] = {邻谷一个磨坊主的女儿今天下午出嫁，喜宴挪到了镀金蝾螈酒馆楼上接着办。|楼上又是一场婚礼：一个车夫和一个裁缝，两家人都铁了心要在跳舞上压过对方。|又是婚礼，还是上回那个提琴手，他用琴弓朝你挥了挥。}老板娘要的是舞别停，两家人别凑到一块儿。\n# en: A noisy wedding party in a tavern's upstairs room, a fiddler on a table, a bride in a crooked flower crown dancing with her grandmother.\nscene [995169] = 酒馆楼上一场热闹的婚宴，提琴手站在桌子上，戴着歪斜花冠的新娘正和她奶奶跳舞。\n# en: Keep everyone dancing\nopt.left.label [1006e1] = 让大家一直跳下去\n# en: Dance with every aunt; big tips if the families mix.\nopt.left.hint [61fc62] = 跟每位姑妈都跳一支；两家人要是混到一起，小费丰厚。\n# en: {You dance with every aunt, two uncles and the bride. By midnight the families are singing the same song. Both fathers tip you.|You get the two grandmothers dancing together, and after that nobody can remember the feud. The tips are enormous; your feet are not.|The fiddler and you keep it going until the candles go out. You walk home with a pocket of coins and a flower in your hair.}\nopt.left.say.1 [12e788] = {你跟每位姑妈、两位叔叔和新娘都跳了舞。到午夜，两家人唱起了同一首歌。两位父亲都给了你小费。|你让两位奶奶一起跳了舞，之后谁也想不起两家的过节了。小费多得惊人；你的脚可不怎么好受。|你和提琴手一直撑到蜡烛燃尽。你揣着一口袋硬币、头上插着一朵花走回去。}\n# en: {You ask the wrong cousin to dance, and the families end up on opposite sides of the room. A thin tip, and a long night.|The groom's brother picks a fight with the bride's, and you're the one standing between them. The landlady pays you off early.|Nobody wants to dance with the hired help tonight. You stand by the wall for four hours.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c7d730] = {你请错了表亲跳舞，两家人最后各站屋子一边。小费寥寥，长夜漫漫。|新郎的兄弟和新娘的兄弟打了起来，夹在中间的是你。老板娘提早把你打发走了。|今晚谁都不想跟雇来的帮手跳舞。你在墙边站了四个钟头。}\n# en: Mind the food and the fire\nopt.right.label [6d4288] = 照看吃的和炉火\n# en: Steady pay, no dancing.\nopt.right.hint [dfebc0] = 工钱稳当，不用跳舞。\n# en: {You keep the platters full and the fire fed. Nobody notices you, which at a wedding is a kind of success.|You carry cake until your arms ache. The bride kisses your cheek on her way out, and you go home sticky and paid.|You mind the fire and watch the dancing. It's honest money, and the music is good.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d3228e] = {你让盘子一直满着，炉火一直旺着。没人注意到你，在婚礼上这也算一种成功。|你端蛋糕端到胳膊发酸。新娘离开时亲了亲你的脸颊，你黏糊糊地拿着工钱回去了。|你照看着炉火，看大家跳舞。钱挣得清白，音乐也不错。}\n\n@event company_poets\n# en: The Poets' Supper\ntitle [9664c1] = 诗人的晚宴\n# en: {A society of village poets has taken the upstairs room for its supper, and needs someone to judge the contest.|{chosen(company_poets, left): The poets are back. Their president remembers your last verdict and has not forgiven it.|The poets are back. Their president remembers you pouring the wine, and asks whether you'd dare to judge this time.}|The poets' supper again, and tonight the contest is in rhyming couplets only, including the toasts.} The landlady says: whoever wins, the wine bill gets paid.\ntext [649eb1] = {村里的一个诗社包下了楼上的房间办晚宴，需要有人给比赛当评判。|{chosen(company_poets, left): 诗人们又来了。社长还记得你上回的裁决，至今没有原谅。|诗人们又来了。社长记得你上回给大家倒酒，问你这回敢不敢当评判。}|又是诗人的晚宴，今晚的比赛只许用押韵的对句，连祝酒词也算。}老板娘说：谁赢都行，酒钱得有人付。\n# en: A long tavern table covered in wine bottles and loose manuscript pages, ink-stained poets arguing, one standing on a chair to recite.\nscene [f42ab1] = 酒馆里一张长桌上摆满酒瓶和散落的手稿，满手墨迹的诗人们争论不休，有一个站在椅子上朗诵。\n# en: Judge the contest\nopt.left.label [478c97] = 给比赛当评判\n# en: A clever verdict pleases everyone; a bad one pleases nobody.\nopt.left.hint [334de3] = 裁决巧妙，人人满意；裁决糟糕，谁都不满意。\n# en: {You give a prize to everyone, each for something different, and each of them thinks theirs is the real one. The wine bill gets paid with a flourish.|Your verdict, in rhyming couplets, gets a standing ovation. The president pays you himself and asks for a copy.|You find something true to say about every poem, even the long one about turnips. They tip in verse and in coin.}\nopt.left.say.1 [285643] = {你给每个人都颁了奖，各有各的名目，每个人都觉得自己那个才是真的。酒钱被潇洒地付清了。|你用押韵的对句宣布裁决，全场起立鼓掌。社长亲自付了你工钱，还要了一份抄本。|你对每首诗都说出了一句真话，连那首关于萝卜的长诗也不例外。他们用诗句和硬币付小费。}\n# en: {You give the prize to the poem about the president's late wife. It was, it turns out, written by her rival. The supper ends early.|Your verdict starts an argument that becomes a fight that becomes a poem about you. It is not a kind poem.|Nobody agrees with you, loudly, in couplets.}\nopt.left.say.2 [058301] = {你把奖颁给了那首写社长亡妻的诗。结果那是她的对头写的。晚宴提早散了。|你的裁决引发了一场争论，争论变成了打架，打架又变成了一首写你的诗。那可不是一首客气的诗。|没人同意你，而且是大声地、用对句不同意你。}\n# en: Pour and listen\nopt.right.label [00550d] = 倒酒，听诗\n# en: Plain pay, and a night of poems.\nopt.right.hint [a684ba] = 普通的工钱，外加一晚上的诗。\n# en: {You pour wine and listen to forty poems. Three of them are good, and you remember one for weeks.|You keep the glasses full and the poets talking. You go home with fair pay and a head full of rhymes.|You pour and say nothing, and learn more about metre than a term of lectures taught you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [27e398] = {你一边倒酒一边听了四十首诗。有三首写得好，其中一首你记了好几个星期。|你让酒杯一直满着，让诗人们一直说着。你带着一份公道的工钱和满脑子韵脚回去了。|你只管倒酒，一言不发，学到的格律比一学期的课还多。}\n\n@event ring_on_yourself\n# en: Bet on Yourself\ntitle [c79e66] = 押自己赢\n# en: {The ring under the boathouse has a bookmaker now, with a slate. \"Fancy yourself?\" he says. \"Put your money where your wand is. Cast what you like. Nobody down here tells.\"|The bookmaker knows your name and your odds, and they've shortened.|Your name is on the slate tonight before you've said you'll fight.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Across the top of his slate he has chalked LAST STANDING: step in tonight and you fight until you drop or have beaten four, or you pay the house a forfeit to walk out.|Win, and the next bout pays more.}\ntext [e8ea0c] = {船屋底下的擂台现在有了个庄家，拿着一块石板。“对自己有信心？”他说，“那就拿钱来证明你的魔杖。想施什么就施什么。这底下没人告密。”|庄家知道你的名字，也知道你的赔率，而且赔率变低了。|你还没说要打，石板上已经写上了你的名字。} {flag(ring_last_standing): 他在石板顶上用粉笔写着“站到最后”：今晚进场，就要一直打到倒下或连赢四场，要么就付给庄家一笔罚金退场。|赢了，下一场钱更多。}\n# en: A lantern-lit cellar under a boathouse, a scorched chalk ring on wet flagstones, a bookmaker holding up a slate of odds, a crowd in scarves.\nscene [435e23] = 船屋下提灯照亮的地窖，湿石板上一个烧焦的粉笔圈，庄家举着写满赔率的石板，围着围巾的人群。\n# en: Bet your purse on yourself\nopt.left.label [5b980a] = 把钱袋押在自己身上\n# en: {You win, singed and grinning, and the bookmaker pays out through his teeth.|The crowd roars. You collect your bet and your opponent's grudging handshake.|Your name was on the slate before you agreed, and the crowd is bigger for it. You win, and the bookmaker pays you in front of all of them.}\nopt.left.say.1 [19bd7d] = {你赢了，一身焦痕却咧着嘴笑，庄家咬着牙付了钱。|人群欢呼。你领了赌金，还有对手不情不愿的握手。|你还没答应，名字就上了石板，来看的人也因此更多。你赢了，庄家当着所有人的面给你付钱。}\n# en: {You go down in the third exchange, and your purse goes with you. On the way home a light is on in the prefects' corridor.|A bad loss: the bet, your pride and both eyebrows.|The bigger crowd came to see you lose, it turns out. You oblige, and your purse goes with your pride.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8a5511] = {第三回合你就倒下了，钱袋也跟着没了。回去的路上，级长走廊里亮着灯。|输得很惨：赌注、面子，还有两道眉毛。|原来人多是来看你输的。你如他们所愿，钱袋和面子一起交了出去。}\n# en: Fight for the purse only\nopt.right.label [0acb58] = 只为奖金而战\n# en: {A clean win for a small purse. The money-holder asks if you'll go again.|You win and bow to your opponent. The bookmaker is already chalking your name for the next bout.|You win the purse in front of the biggest crowd yet. The bookmaker sighs at the money he didn't make on you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cddb71] = {干净利落地赢了一小笔奖金。管钱的人问你还打不打。|你赢了，向对手鞠了一躬。庄家已经在用粉笔写你下一场的名字了。|你在最多的人面前赢下了奖金。庄家为没能从你身上赚到的钱叹了口气。}\n# en: {You lose, bow, and spend the walk home finding new bruises.|A loss, and the loser's purse, which is nothing at all.|You lose in front of the biggest crowd yet. At least it was only the purse.}\nopt.right.say.2 [cc0e51] = {你输了，鞠了一躬，回家路上一路发现新的淤青。|输了，拿到的是输家的奖金，也就是什么都没有。|你在最多的人面前输了。至少输的只是奖金。}\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.no.label [4bf72b] = 今晚算了\n# en: Watch, and bet if you like.\nopt.no.hint [6e4615] = 在旁边看，想押就押。\n# en: {The bookmaker shrugs and rubs your name off the slate.|\"Your loss,\" says the bookmaker. \"Or someone else's gain. Care to back one?\"|You leave your name on the slate and your wand in your pocket.}\nopt.no.say.1 [659b1f] = {庄家耸耸肩，把你的名字从石板上擦掉了。|“你的损失，”庄家说，“或者别人的收获。要押一个吗？”|你把名字留在石板上，把魔杖留在口袋里。}\n\n@event ring_bout_2\n# en: The Second Bout\ntitle [1ddc2a] = 第二场\n# en: The money-holder points at the chalk again. {~A lanky girl steps in who quit the academy last spring. She kept the wand, and the duelling stance.|A fairground conjurer steps in, still in spangles, rolling a spark across his knuckles. The crowd knows him.|A hedge-hexer from past the village steps in, a cord of knotted charms at her wrist. Some of those knots aren't legal anywhere.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: stepping out now costs a forfeit.|The purse has doubled. Or you can take what you've won and go.}\ntext [ab1c3c] = 管钱的学生又指了指粉笔圈。{~一个瘦高的姑娘走了进来，她去年春天退了学。魔杖她留下了，决斗的架势也留下了。|一个集市上的变戏法的走了进来，还穿着亮片衣裳，一颗火花在指节间滚来滚去。人群认得他。|一个从村子那头来的野巫走了进来，手腕上缠着一串打了结的护符。有几个结在哪儿都不合法。} {flag(ring_last_standing): 站到最后：现在退场要付罚金。|钱袋翻了一倍。或者你可以拿着赢来的钱走人。}\n# en: A chalk ring under a boathouse, a fresh opponent twirling a wand, a crowd pressing in with coins in their fists.\nscene [71f7ab] = 船屋下的粉笔圈，一个新对手转着魔杖，人群挤上前来，手里攥着硬币。\n# en: Fight on\nopt.left.label [57a906] = 接着打\n# en: A harder bout, a bigger purse.\nopt.left.hint [03e670] = 更难的一场，更大的钱袋。\n# en: {~She duels by the book she walked out on. You've read the later chapters. Her wand ends up in the crowd, and the purse ends up heavier.|The conjurer's sparks are all show. You aim at the hand that isn't waving, and he sits down in a shower of spangles.|You cut her charm-cord with your first spell. Whatever the knots were for, it doesn't happen, and she spits and yields.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3e7189] = {~她按着她丢下的那本书来决斗。可后面几章你都读过。她的魔杖飞进了人群，钱袋则沉了几分。|变戏法的火花全是花架子。你瞄准他没在挥舞的那只手，他一屁股坐进了一阵亮片雨里。|你第一道咒就斩断了她的护符绳。不管那些结是干什么用的，都没能发生，她啐了一口，认输了。}\n# en: {~She was the best in her year before she left, and she still is. She helps you up off the flagstones, which is worse.|He palms a spark, and while you watch it, the other hand ends the bout. You limp up the steps with the night's winnings and singed sleeves.|One knot comes undone, and something cold walks through your head. You come round outside, with your purse, and a headache that lasts a day.}\nopt.left.say.2 [6ad1eb] = {~她退学之前是她那一届最好的，现在依然是。她把你从石板上扶起来，这更让人难受。|他手心藏了一颗火花，趁你盯着它，另一只手结束了这场比试。你带着当晚的赢钱和烧焦的袖子一瘸一拐地爬上台阶。|一个结松开了，有什么冰冷的东西从你脑子里走了过去。你在外面醒来，钱袋还在，头疼了一整天。}\n# en: {flag(ring_last_standing): Forfeit, and walk out|Take the purse and go}\nopt.right.label [b6a8d5] = {flag(ring_last_standing): 认输，付罚金走人|拿钱走人}\n# en: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: the house keeps a forfeit, and the crowd remembers.|Keep what you've won.}\nopt.right.hint [6b0d1c] = {flag(ring_last_standing): 站到最后：庄家扣下罚金，人群也会记住。|保住赢来的钱。}\n# en: {~You bow out with the purse in your fist. The dropout shrugs and finds someone else.|You leave the conjurer to the next fool, and count your winnings on the walk home.|The hexer laughs as you go, and ties another knot.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5484a9] = {~你攥着钱袋鞠躬退场。那个退学生耸耸肩，去找别人了。|你把变戏法的留给下一个傻瓜，在回去的路上数你的赢钱。|你走的时候，野巫笑了，又打了一个结。}\n# en: You raise your hand and step out of the chalk. The bookmaker chalks FORFEIT across your name, takes the house's share of your purse in front of everyone, and the cellar hisses you up the stairs.\nopt.right.say.2 [8cf242] = 你举起手，走出粉笔圈。庄家在你的名字上用粉笔写下“认输”，当着所有人的面从你的钱袋里拿走庄家的那份，地窖里一片嘘声把你送上楼梯。\n\n@event ring_bout_3\n# en: The Third Bout\ntitle [7a81f9] = 第三场\n# en: Two down. The cellar is packed now and the betting is loud. {~The house sends in a Conclave clerk in ink-stained cuffs, moonlighting for the purse. He reads your stance like a form.|The house sends in a prefect who lost the sash last year, and has been angry about it ever since.|The house sends in someone in a hood whose wand hand is grey to the wrist. The steps go quiet.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: the chalk, or the forfeit.|One more win and the purse is the biggest yet. Or walk away a winner.}\ntext [18e236] = 已经赢了两场。地窖里挤满了人，下注声震天。{~庄家派上来一个七塔议会的文书，袖口沾着墨水，为了钱袋来兼差。他读你的架势就像读一张表格。|庄家派上来一个去年被摘了绶带的级长，从那以后他一直憋着火。|庄家派上来一个戴兜帽的人，握杖的那只手一直灰到手腕。台阶上安静了。} {flag(ring_last_standing): 站到最后：要么粉笔圈，要么罚金。|再赢一场，钱袋就是今晚最大的。或者作为赢家离开。}\n# en: A packed boathouse cellar roaring at a chalk ring, a wizard in a long coat stepping in, lantern light swinging.\nscene [8f9042] = 挤满人的船屋地窖对着粉笔圈咆哮，一个穿长外套的巫师走进圈里，提灯的光来回摇晃。\n# en: Fight on\nopt.left.label [57a906] = 接着打\n# en: Harder still, and worth it.\nopt.left.hint [9186db] = 更难了，也更值。\n# en: {~He foresees every spell you planned. So you stop planning. The clerk goes down muttering about irregular procedure, and the cellar shakes.|The ex-prefect fights like a rulebook with a grudge. You break a rule he didn't know, and the purse is fat.|The hood falls back as they go down: nobody you know, and a face older than the hands. The crowd chants your name to drown the quiet.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6275cf] = {~你计划好的每一道咒他都预见到了。于是你不再计划。文书嘟囔着“程序不合规”倒下了，地窖为之震动。|前级长打起来像一本带着怨气的规章。你打破了一条他不知道的规矩，钱袋鼓鼓囊囊。|他倒下时兜帽滑落：不是你认识的人，那张脸比那双手更老。人群高喊你的名字，好压住那份寂静。}\n# en: {~The clerk dismantles you, politely, by the numbers. You wake on the steps with the purse still in your pocket and a ringing ear.|The ex-prefect's ward doesn't move, and neither, after a while, do you. Your ribs remember it all week.|The hooded caster drinks something out of you that isn't blood. You go down and stay down, and someone carries you to the door.}\nopt.left.say.2 [bff922] = {~文书礼貌地、按部就班地把你拆了。你在台阶上醒来，钱袋还在口袋里，耳朵嗡嗡作响。|前级长的护盾纹丝不动，过了一会儿，你也动不了了。你的肋骨记了它一整周。|兜帽施法者从你身上吸走了某种不是血的东西。你倒下就没再起来，有人把你抬到了门口。}\n# en: {flag(ring_last_standing): Forfeit, and walk out|Take the purse and go}\nopt.right.label [b6a8d5] = {flag(ring_last_standing): 认输，付罚金走人|拿钱走人}\n# en: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: the house keeps a forfeit, and the crowd remembers.|Leave a winner.}\nopt.right.hint [d6d885] = {flag(ring_last_standing): 站到最后：庄家扣下罚金，人群也会记住。|赢着离开。}\n# en: {~You leave the clerk waiting in the chalk with his forms. The crowd boos, and your purse doesn't care.|The ex-prefect calls you a coward. Nobody else does; you're holding too much money.|You bow to the hood and climb the steps. Some fights are better not known.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ed6195] = {~你把文书和他的表格留在粉笔圈里。人群嘘你，你的钱袋才不在乎。|前级长骂你是懦夫。别人都没说；你手里的钱太多了。|你朝兜帽人鞠了一躬，爬上台阶。有些对手，还是不认识为好。}\n# en: Two down, and you step out. It's a forfeit under the rules: the house takes its cut of your winnings, and the crowd, which had money on you, lets you know about it all the way to the door.\nopt.right.say.2 [c95208] = 已经赢了两场，你却退场了。按规矩这算认输：庄家从你赢的钱里抽走他的那份，而押了你的人群一路把不满送到门口。\n\n@event ring_bout_4\n# en: The Last Bout\ntitle [9d428e] = 最后一场\n# en: Three down, and the cellar has gone quiet. {~The ring's own champion steps in: a duellist the academy expelled, who hasn't lost down here in a year.|The bookmaker himself takes off his coat. His wand was in it all along, and the old hands step back from the chalk.|A girl in a borrowed cloak steps in, a runaway from Thornwick. Her shadow moves a moment after she does, and the old hands start betting against you.} {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: win this and the night is yours.|Win, and the cellar will talk about you for a term. Or go while you can still walk.}\ntext [154790] = 已经赢了三场，地窖安静下来。{~擂台自己的冠军走了进来：一个被学院开除的决斗者，在这底下一年没输过。|庄家亲自脱下了外套。他的魔杖一直就在外套里，老手们从粉笔圈边退开了。|一个披着借来斗篷的姑娘走了进来，是从荆木学院跑出来的。她的影子总比她慢半拍，老手们开始押你输。} {flag(ring_last_standing): 站到最后：赢下这一场，今晚就是你的。|赢了，地窖会谈论你一整个学期。或者趁还能走的时候离开。}\n# en: A boathouse cellar gone silent around a chalk ring, the night's champion stepping in, wand raised, every lantern turned toward the fighters.\nscene [1fe464] = 船屋地窖在粉笔圈周围一片寂静，当晚的冠军举着魔杖走进来，所有提灯都转向了两名斗士。\n# en: Fight the last bout\nopt.left.label [d243fc] = 打最后一场\n# en: Expert opposition; the night's whole purse.\nopt.left.hint [c13a7f] = 专家级对手；今晚的全部奖金。\n# en: {~The champion's ward breaks at last, and the cellar stamps until dust comes down from the boathouse floor. You go home with a purse you can barely lift.|He reads your next spell out of your head every time, so you cast the one you hadn't thought of. He goes down laughing and pays you from his own coat.|You hit the shadow, not the girl, and she yields, grinning, and buys you a drink you can't hold. You walk home sore and rich and famous in one cellar.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1f47e6] = {~冠军的护盾终于破了，地窖里跺脚声震得船屋地板上的灰都落了下来。你带着一个几乎提不动的钱袋回家。|他每次都能从你脑子里读出你的下一道咒，于是你施了那道你还没想到的。他笑着倒下，从自己外套里掏钱给你。|你打的是影子，不是姑娘。她笑着认输，请你喝了一杯你端不稳的酒。你走回去时浑身酸痛，却发了财，还在一个地窖里出了名。}\n# en: {~The champion ends it in one spell, and you understand why the academy wanted them gone. You wake on the steps with your night's winnings and a long walk.|The bookmaker casts things the library keeps chained, and wins clean. You crawl up the steps holding your purse and your ribs.|You can't tell the girl from her shadow, and the wrong one hits you. The crowd is so loud the prefects must hear it.}\nopt.left.say.2 [6ad91d] = {~冠军一道咒就结束了比赛，你明白了学院为什么要赶走他们。你在台阶上醒来，带着当晚的赢钱，还有一段很长的路。|庄家施的是图书馆锁在链子上的那些东西，赢得干干净净。你捂着钱袋和肋骨爬上台阶。|你分不清姑娘和她的影子，结果打中你的是错的那个。人群吵得级长们一定都听见了。}\n# en: {flag(ring_last_standing): Forfeit, and walk out|Take the purse and go}\nopt.right.label [b6a8d5] = {flag(ring_last_standing): 认输，付罚金走人|拿钱走人}\n# en: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: the house keeps a forfeit, and the crowd remembers.|Three wins is a fine night.}\nopt.right.hint [322c4a] = {flag(ring_last_standing): 站到最后：庄家扣下罚金，人群也会记住。|连赢三场，已经很好了。}\n# en: {~You bow out with three wins and a heavy purse. The champion nods: next time.|You don't fight the man who sets the odds, and knows your thoughts. You take your winnings before he can change his mind.|You look at the runaway's shadow and decide three is plenty.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5166dd] = {~你带着三场胜利和沉甸甸的钱袋鞠躬退场。冠军点点头：下次吧。|你不跟定赔率、还读得出你心思的人打。趁他改主意之前，你拿着赢钱走了。|你看了一眼那个逃学生的影子，觉得三场就够了。}\n# en: You step out before the last bout. The bookmaker counts out the forfeit from your purse with great ceremony, and the cellar boos you, and a few people, quietly, look relieved for you.\nopt.right.say.2 [ab9591] = 你在最后一场前退场了。庄家煞有介事地从你的钱袋里数出罚金，地窖里嘘声一片，也有几个人悄悄地替你松了口气。\n\n@event ring_side_bets\n# en: Side Bets\ntitle [af883e] = 场边下注\n# en: {You find a place on the cellar steps. The bookmaker works his way along them, slate in hand.|From the steps you can see the fighters' wand hands, which tell you more than their faces. The bookmaker is coming round.|You know the regulars on the steps now, and who bets with their heart. The bookmaker holds out his slate.}\ntext [f2453b] = {你在地窖台阶上找了个位置。庄家拿着石板沿台阶一路走过来。|从台阶上你能看见斗士们握杖的手，比他们的脸透露得更多。庄家过来了。|你现在认得台阶上的常客了，也知道谁是凭感情下注。庄家递出他的石板。}\n# en: Students on the steps of a boathouse cellar passing coins to a bookmaker while two fighters circle in a chalk ring below.\nscene [e7b110] = 船屋地窖台阶上的学生把硬币递给庄家，下面粉笔圈里两个斗士在绕圈。\n# en: Back the favourite\nopt.left.label [1cbc32] = 押热门\n# en: A small stake on the likely winner.\nopt.left.hint [8d953c] = 小注押可能的赢家。\n# en: {Your fighter wins. You collect quietly and leave before anyone remembers your face.|You read the wand hands right. The bookmaker pays you as if it hurt.|The favourite wins, as favourites do. You pocket a small, sensible profit.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6e3ffa] = {你押的人赢了。你悄悄领了钱，在别人记住你的脸之前就走了。|你看准了握杖的手。庄家付钱时像是很肉疼。|热门赢了，热门总是赢。你揣起一笔不大、稳妥的利润。}\n# en: {Your fighter goes down in the first exchange. So does your stake.|You picked the wand hand, not the wizard. The bookmaker smiles.|The favourite slips on the wet stone, and your crowns slip with them.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3062a3] = {你押的人第一回合就倒了。你的赌注也跟着倒了。|你押的是握杖的手，不是巫师。庄家笑了。|热门在湿石头上滑了一跤，你的克朗也跟着滑走了。}\n# en: Back the underdog\nopt.under.label [3c219b] = 押冷门\n# en: A bigger stake against the odds.\nopt.under.hint [e2b1d2] = 押大注，逆势而为。\n# en: {The underdog wins, and you're the only one on the steps who backed them. The bookmaker counts it out very slowly.|Your long shot lands one spell, and it's enough. The steps turn round to look at you.|You saw what nobody else did, the limp in the favourite's step. You walk home heavy with silver.}\nopt.under.say.1 [afce5c] = {冷门赢了，台阶上只有你押了他。庄家数钱数得特别慢。|你押的冷门打中了一道咒，一道就够了。整排台阶的人都转过头看你。|你看见了别人没看见的：热门脚下有点瘸。你揣着沉甸甸的银币回家。}\n# en: {The underdog is an underdog for a reason. Your stake is gone in a minute.|A brave fight, a long fall, and your crowns go with it.|You backed your heart. Your purse pays for it.}\nopt.under.say.2 [ed71ca] = {冷门之所以是冷门，是有原因的。你的赌注一分钟就没了。|一场勇敢的较量，一次重重的倒地，你的克朗也跟着去了。|你押的是你的心。你的钱袋为此买了单。}\n# en: Go home\nopt.home.label [c93952] = 回家\n# en: {You watch one bout and go home with your purse unopened.|You leave before the bookmaker reaches you.|You climb the steps into the cold and leave the ring to its noise.}\nopt.home.say.1 [37cf2b] = {你看了一场，钱袋没打开就回家了。|庄家还没走到你跟前，你就走了。|你爬上台阶走进寒夜，把擂台留给它的喧闹。}\n\n@event ring_cameo_corvin\n# en: A Familiar Grin\ntitle [963a6b] = 熟悉的笑脸\n# en: Before the next bout someone vaults the rope into the chalk: Corvin, sleeves rolled, wand spinning. He sees you and his grin slips. \"Saint. Of course it's you.\" Then, low: \"I owe three people money, and one of them has started describing what happens next. The house pays double for a grudge match. So hate me. Loudly.\"\ntext [f72f9b] = 下一场开始前，有人翻过绳子跳进了粉笔圈：科尔文，袖子卷着，魔杖在指间打转。他看见你，笑容一滑。“圣人。当然是你。”接着压低声音：“我欠三个人钱，其中一个已经开始描述接下来会发生什么了。庄家说，有私仇的比赛双倍赔。所以，恨我吧。恨得大声点。”\n# en: A grinning young man vaulting a rope into a chalk ring in a lantern-lit cellar, sleeves rolled, a wand spinning in his fingers.\nscene [a5617f] = 提灯照亮的地窖里，一个咧嘴笑的年轻人翻过绳子跳进粉笔圈，袖子卷着，魔杖在指间打转。\n# en: Fight him for real\nopt.left.label [44de8c] = 跟他真打\n# en: He fights dirty, and fast.\nopt.left.hint [683fb6] = 他打得又脏又快。\n# en: You knock his wand into the crowd on the fifth exchange. \"Rude,\" he says from the flagstones, and laughs, but his eyes go to the bookmaker's slate and stay there a moment too long.\nopt.left.say.1 [a5e643] = 第五回合你把他的魔杖打进了人群。“真没礼貌。”他躺在石板上说，笑了起来，可他的目光落在庄家的石板上，停得久了那么一会儿。\n# en: He was never where your spell landed. He hauls you up by the collar, collects his purse, and presses a few coins of it into your hand. \"Your cut, for the hating. You were very convincing.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [05451b] = 你的咒落下的地方，他从来都不在。他揪着你的领子把你拽起来，领了钱袋，往你手里塞了几枚硬币。“你的分成，算是恨我的辛苦费。你演得很像。”\n# en: Put on a show, and let him win\nopt.right.label [17dd33] = 演一场，让他赢\n# en: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: this counts as a forfeit.|Your night ends; his debt doesn't.}\nopt.right.hint [9d8579] = {flag(ring_last_standing): 站到最后：这算认输。|你的夜晚结束了；他的债可没有。}\n# en: You shout, he shouts, sparks fly everywhere but at each other, and you go down with a groan the back row will talk about all week. The bettors who backed you never know. Corvin does. \"I owe you,\" he says on the steps. \"Add it to the list.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [d4399d] = 你吼，他也吼，火花朝四面八方飞，就是不往对方身上去，你倒下时那一声呻吟够后排的人谈论一整周。押你的人永远不会知道。科尔文知道。“我欠你的，”他在台阶上说，“记在账上吧。”\n# en: On a last-standing night it counts as a forfeit: the bookmaker takes the house's share of your winnings on the way out.\nopt.right.say.2 [f47c5f] = 在“站到最后”的夜晚，这算认输：出门时庄家从你赢的钱里抽走他的那份。\n\n@event ring_cameo_lyra\n# en: Someone Worth Losing To\ntitle [2b98fc] = 值得输给的人\n# en: The crowd parts. Lyra Vaneshade is in the chalk, gloves off, hair tied back. \"So it's true.\" Her voice carries over the whole cellar. \"You're throwing your talent away down here, in front of drunks. Fine. Then you'll lose it to someone worth losing to.\" Somebody is already shouting odds on House Vaneshade.\ntext [edf595] = 人群分开了。莱拉·凡妮希德站在粉笔圈里，手套摘了，头发扎了起来。“原来是真的。”她的声音传遍整个地窖，“你在这底下、当着一群醉鬼的面糟蹋你的天赋。好。那你就把它输给一个值得输给的人。”已经有人在喊凡妮希德家的赔率了。\n# en: A proud red-haired student in a chalk ring, gloves off, hair tied back, a ribbon of flame curling from her wand, a cellar crowd roaring.\nscene [2d5658] = 一个骄傲的红发学生站在粉笔圈里，摘了手套，扎起头发，一条火焰丝带从她的魔杖上卷起，地窖里的人群在咆哮。\n# en: Fight her\nopt.left.label [d6ea52] = 跟她打\n# en: In front of everyone.\nopt.left.hint [f275cb] = 当着所有人的面。\n# en: Her flame breaks on your last spell and she goes down on one knee in the chalk, in front of all of them. She stands, white to the lips. \"Again,\" she says. \"In the hall. Where it counts.\" The crowd will be talking about it for a term.\nopt.left.say.1 [c995bc] = 她的火焰撞碎在你最后一道咒上，她当着所有人的面单膝跪在了粉笔圈里。她站起来，连嘴唇都白了。“再来，”她说，“在决斗厅。在算数的地方。”人群会谈论这件事一整个学期。\n# en: She takes you apart in four exchanges, beautifully. Then she walks you up the steps herself, holding your elbow. \"Now you know,\" she says, \"where you belong. It isn't here.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [761b5d] = 她四个回合就把你拆了，漂漂亮亮。然后她亲自扶着你的胳膊把你送上台阶。“现在你知道了，”她说，“你属于哪里。不是这里。”\n# en: Walk out with her\nopt.right.label [e0312b] = 跟她一起走出去\n# en: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: this counts as a forfeit.|The crowd won't like it.}\nopt.right.hint [969b9d] = {flag(ring_last_standing): 站到最后：这算认输。|人群不会喜欢的。}\n# en: You step out of the chalk and offer her your arm. The cellar boos you both up the steps. Outside, in the cold, Lyra lets go of your arm and doesn't say thank you. She does walk you all the way back.\nopt.right.say.1 [671a15] = 你走出粉笔圈，把胳膊递给她。整个地窖一路嘘着你们俩上了台阶。外面的寒气里，莱拉松开你的胳膊，没有说谢谢。但她一路把你送了回去。\n# en: On a last-standing night it counts as a forfeit: the bookmaker takes the house's share of your winnings on the way out.\nopt.right.say.2 [f47c5f] = 在“站到最后”的夜晚，这算认输：出门时庄家从你赢的钱里抽走他的那份。\n\n@event ring_cameo_bram\n# en: Out of Uniform\ntitle [f9d558] = 没穿制服\n# en: The next challenger steps in hooded, and drops the hood: Bram Hollis, without his red sash. He sees you and goes the colour of it. \"You didn't see me,\" he says, fast and low. \"I didn't see you. Nobody down here writes anything down. That's the point.\" Then, loud, for the crowd: \"Well? Get in the chalk!\"\ntext [efd3e1] = 下一个挑战者戴着兜帽走进来，然后摘下了兜帽：布拉姆·霍利斯，没系红绶带。他看见你，脸涨得跟绶带一个颜色。“你没看见我，”他又快又低地说，“我也没看见你。这底下没人记任何东西。这才是重点。”然后，冲着人群大声喊：“愣着干什么？进圈来！”\n# en: A broad young man pulling back a hood in a chalk ring, no prefect's sash, wand gripped tight, staring at someone he didn't expect.\nscene [fff819] = 一个宽肩的年轻人在粉笔圈里拉下兜帽，没系级长绶带，紧握魔杖，瞪着一个他没想到会在这里的人。\n# en: Fight him\nopt.left.label [b8f327] = 跟他打\n# en: He hits like a wall.\nopt.left.hint [a16ea6] = 他打起来像一堵墙。\n# en: His ward finally gives, and he sits down hard in the chalk. Then something odd happens: he laughs, a real one, out loud. \"Nobody's ever beaten me where my father couldn't hear about it,\" he says. He offers you his hand.\nopt.left.say.1 [523938] = 他的护盾终于撑不住了，他重重坐在粉笔圈里。接着发生了一件怪事：他笑了，是真笑，笑出了声。“从来没人在我爸听不到的地方赢过我。”他说着，把手伸给了你。\n# en: He walks through your spells like rain and puts you on the flagstones. On the steps he stops. \"Not a word,\" he says. \"And not a demerit either. We were never here.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [89c6f1] = 他像走过雨一样穿过你的咒，把你放倒在石板上。在台阶上他停下来。“一个字都别说，”他说，“也不会有处分。我们从来没来过这儿。”\n# en: Let him have the bout\nopt.right.label [c97201] = 把这场让给他\n# en: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: this counts as a forfeit.|Leave him his secret, and the purse.}\nopt.right.hint [ef3768] = {flag(ring_last_standing): 站到最后：这算认输。|留给他他的秘密，还有钱袋。}\n# en: You step back out of the chalk and bow to him. He stares at you as if it were a trick. On the stairs to the dormitory next morning, your name isn't in his ledger, and he looks away first.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f64b9] = 你退出粉笔圈，向他鞠了一躬。他瞪着你，好像这是个圈套。第二天早上在宿舍楼梯上，他的记录本里没有你的名字，而且是他先移开了目光。\n# en: On a last-standing night it counts as a forfeit: the bookmaker takes the house's share of your winnings on the way out.\nopt.right.say.2 [f47c5f] = 在“站到最后”的夜晚，这算认输：出门时庄家从你赢的钱里抽走他的那份。\n\n@event ring_cameo_ilse\n# en: Frost in the Chalk\ntitle [38d938] = 粉笔圈里的霜\n# en: The cellar goes cold. The lanterns shrink. A girl in white furs steps into the chalk and frost creeps out from her boots across the flagstones: Ilse Dumont. \"They said the academy's best come down here,\" she says. She looks you over. \"They lied. But you'll do.\"\ntext [9a4444] = 地窖变冷了。提灯的火苗缩了下去。一个穿白色毛皮的姑娘走进粉笔圈，霜从她的靴子边沿着石板蔓延开来：伊尔泽·杜蒙。“他们说学院最好的人会来这底下，”她说。她上下打量了你一番。“他们撒谎了。不过你也凑合。”\n# en: A girl in white furs in a chalk ring, frost creeping from her boots across the flagstones, lantern flames shrinking, a crowd stepping back.\nscene [5d8a80] = 一个穿白色毛皮的姑娘站在粉笔圈里，霜从她的靴子边沿着石板蔓延，提灯的火苗缩小，人群往后退。\n# en: Fight her\nopt.left.label [d6ea52] = 跟她打\n# en: She was raised to fight.\nopt.left.hint [72e00f] = 她就是被养大来战斗的。\n# en: She slips on her own ice and your spell catches her before she's up. She stays down a moment, then laughs, a short Northern sound. \"Again,\" she says. \"Not tonight. But again.\" She takes her purse and leaves the cellar colder.\nopt.left.say.1 [059246] = 她在自己的冰上滑了一下，没等她站起来，你的咒就追上了她。她在地上待了一会儿，然后笑了，一声短促的北境腔调。“再来，”她说，“不是今晚。但要再来。”她拿走她的钱袋，离开后地窖更冷了。\n# en: Rime closes over your wand hand and you can't feel it to cast. She stands over you, unimpressed. \"You fight like someone taught you,\" she says. \"Find someone who didn't.\" Your fingers ache till morning.\nopt.left.say.2 [e0c34c] = 霜封住了你握杖的手，你冻得没法施法。她站在你面前，一点也不佩服。“你打起来像是有人教过你，”她说，“去找个没被人教过的人学。”你的手指疼到了天亮。\n# en: Ask her why she's down here\nopt.right.label [318951] = 问她为什么来这底下\n# en: {flag(ring_last_standing): Last standing: this counts as a forfeit.|Talk, and give up the night's next bout.}\nopt.right.hint [8a95cf] = {flag(ring_last_standing): 站到最后：这算认输。|聊一聊，放弃今晚的下一场。}\n# en: She doesn't answer. Instead she tells you your stance is wrong, then fixes it, roughly, with her boot. \"Southerners,\" she says, as if that explained everything. The crowd boos you both, and she doesn't seem to hear.\nopt.right.say.1 [a7cbb0] = 她没有回答。她反而告诉你你的站姿不对，然后用靴子粗鲁地帮你纠正了过来。“南方人。”她说，好像这就解释了一切。人群嘘你们俩，她好像根本没听见。\n# en: On a last-standing night it counts as a forfeit: the bookmaker takes the house's share of your winnings on the way out.\nopt.right.say.2 [f47c5f] = 在“站到最后”的夜晚，这算认输：出门时庄家从你赢的钱里抽走他的那份。\n\n@event lyra_ring_word\n# en: Lyra Has Heard\ntitle [d959ef] = 莱拉听说了\n# en: Lyra blocks the corridor. \"The ring under the boathouse.\" She says it as if it were a smell. \"Duelling for coins in a cellar, in front of drunks, with spells nobody will report. You can actually duel. I've seen you. This is beneath you.\" A pause. \"I'd hate to lose to someone who does it.\"\ntext [3c8223] = 莱拉挡住了走廊。“船屋底下的擂台。”她说这话的样子像在说一股臭味，“在地窖里当着醉鬼的面为钱决斗，用的是没人会举报的咒语。你明明会决斗。我见过。这配不上你。”停顿了一下。“我可不想输给一个干这种事的人。”\n# en: A proud red-haired student blocking a corridor with folded arms, a duelling glove tucked in her belt.\nscene [437476] = 一个骄傲的红发学生双臂交叉堵在走廊上，腰带里别着一只决斗手套。\n# en: \"Then spar with me instead.\"\nopt.left.label [b55172] = “那就跟我对练吧。”\n# en: She stares, then almost smiles. \"Tomorrow, the Duelling Hall, six sharp.\" She beats you, and then shows you why, which from Lyra is close to an apology for the corridor.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f2ac9] = 她盯着你，然后差点笑了出来。“明天，决斗厅，六点整。”她打败了你，然后把原因指给你看，对莱拉来说，这差不多就是为走廊上的事道歉了。\n# en: \"It pays. You wouldn't know.\"\nopt.right.label [140619] = “它挣钱。你不会懂的。”\n# en: Her face shuts like a door. \"No,\" she says. \"I wouldn't.\" She walks off. Later the Newt's landlady hands back the deposit you left on your bar tab: Lyra has paid it off. That is somehow worse.\nopt.right.say.1 [71f78c] = 她的脸像一扇门一样关上了。“对，”她说，“我不会懂。”她走了。后来蝾螈酒馆的老板娘把你押在酒账上的钱退给了你：莱拉替你付清了。这不知怎么更糟。\n\n@event grisk_pickup\n# en: A Parcel at the Boathouse\ntitle [e699bb] = 船屋的包裹\n# en: {Grisk is waiting on the jetty, a parcel in oilcloth under his arm. \"Into the village by tomorrow afternoon. A cobbler's back door. Don't open it, don't drop it, don't be seen.\"|The jetty, the oilcloth, Grisk's teeth in the dark. \"Same arrangement,\" he says.|Grisk again, and a heavier parcel than last time.} {It ticks faintly.|It's cold enough to frost your gloves.|It smells of oranges and something under the oranges.}\ntext [4f4a69] = {格里斯克在码头上等着，胳膊底下夹着一个油布包。“明天下午前送到村里。鞋匠铺的后门。别打开，别掉了，别被人看见。”|码头，油布包，黑暗里格里斯克的牙齿。“老规矩，”他说。|又是格里斯克，这次的包裹比上次重。}{它微微地滴答作响。|它冷得能让你的手套结霜。|它闻起来有橙子味，橙子味底下还有别的什么。}\n# en: A dark boathouse jetty at midnight, a small goblin holding out a parcel wrapped in oilcloth, the lake black behind him.\nscene [df2f86] = 午夜一座漆黑的船屋码头，一个小个子地精递出一个油布包裹，身后是黑沉沉的湖。\n# en: Take the parcel\nopt.left.label [8ea6ee] = 接下包裹\n# en: {You take it. It is heavier than it looks, and the walk back to your room is very long.|You hide it under your bed. You don't sleep well with it there.|You take the heavier parcel. It smells of oranges, and under the oranges of something you decide not to name.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8e3d40] = {你接了。它比看上去重，回房间的路显得特别长。|你把它藏在床底下。有它在那儿，你睡不好。|你接过了那个更沉的包裹。它有橙子的气味，橙子底下还有一种你决定不去叫出名字的气味。}\n# en: Not this month\nopt.right.label [9e6244] = 这个月不了\n# en: {\"Next month, then,\" says Grisk, as if it were already arranged. He tucks the parcel away. You feel watched all the way back up the lawn.|Grisk shrugs. \"There's always someone.\" You don't like how true it sounds.|You turn down the heavier parcel. Grisk weighs it in his hand, then in his mind. \"Your loss,\" he says. \"Or not.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [73971d] = {“那就下个月，”格里斯克说，好像早就说定了似的。他把包裹收了起来。回草坪那头的一路上，你都觉得有人在看着你。|格里斯克耸耸肩。“总有别人的。”你不喜欢这话有多真。|你拒绝了那个更沉的包裹。格里斯克在手里掂了掂，又在心里掂了掂。“你的损失，”他说，“也许不是。”}\n\n@event grisk_delivery\n# en: The Cobbler's Back Door\ntitle [136b0a] = 鞋匠铺的后门\n# en: {~You carry Grisk's parcel down to the village in broad daylight.|Grisk's parcel, the village lane, and too many people about.|The parcel is in your satchel and the satchel is suddenly the heaviest thing you own.} Bram is at the apple stall. The cobbler's back door is past him.\ntext [6201ff] = {~你光天化日之下把格里斯克的包裹带进了村子。|格里斯克的包裹，村里的小巷，还有太多的人。|包裹在你的挎包里，挎包突然成了你拥有的最重的东西。}布拉姆在苹果摊前。鞋匠铺的后门就在他身后。\n# en: A narrow village lane in daylight, a student with an oilcloth parcel under one arm walking past a prefect who is buying apples.\nscene [8d39ee] = 白天一条狭窄的村巷，一个学生胳膊底下夹着油布包裹，正从一个买苹果的级长身边走过。\n# en: Walk past as if you belong\nopt.left.label [a61db7] = 若无其事地走过去\n# en: {~You nod to Bram, buy an apple, and knock twice at the cobbler's door. A hand takes the parcel; another gives you a purse. Your heart takes an hour to slow down.|The door opens before you knock. Coins, a nod, done. You eat the apple on the way home and taste none of it.|The satchel feels heavier with every step past Bram. He waves. You wave. The cobbler pays, and your hands shake until supper.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3b834c] = {~你冲布拉姆点点头，买了个苹果，在鞋匠铺的门上敲了两下。一只手接过包裹，另一只手递给你一个钱袋。你的心跳过了一个钟头才慢下来。|你还没敲门，门就开了。硬币，点头，完事。你在回家路上吃了那个苹果，一点味道也没尝出来。|从布拉姆身边走过，挎包每一步都更沉。他挥手，你也挥手。鞋匠付了钱，你的手一直抖到晚饭。}\n# en: {~Bram calls your name. You drop the parcel in a horse trough and walk on, very calmly. Bram follows you halfway home.|You panic at the corner and leave the parcel behind a water butt. When you go back, it's gone, and Bram is writing in his notebook.|Bram asks to see inside your satchel, kindly. You show him your books, and he never looks in the other pocket. You walk home sick.}\nopt.left.say.2 [82c22c] = {~布拉姆叫了你的名字。你把包裹扔进饮马槽，非常镇定地继续走。布拉姆跟了你半路。|你在拐角处慌了神，把包裹丢在了一个水桶后面。等你回去找，它已经不见了，布拉姆正在本子上写着什么。|布拉姆客气地要看看你的挎包。你给他看了书，他始终没看另一个口袋。你走回家时一阵恶心。}\n# en: Leave it at the door and run\nopt.right.label [44439b] = 丢在门口就跑\n# en: {~You go the long way round, drop it on the cobbler's step and run. The cobbler pays you, short, for the lack of manners.|You don't knock, and you don't wait. Short pay, slipped under your door that night.|You drop the satchel's weight on the cobbler's step and run. He pays you short, and you're glad to be rid of it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e7e8e0] = {~你绕远路，把它丢在鞋匠铺门口的台阶上就跑。鞋匠因为你没礼貌，少付了你一些钱。|你不敲门，也不等。那天夜里，一份打了折扣的报酬从你门缝底下塞了进来。|你把挎包里的东西丢在鞋匠的台阶上就跑。他少付了你钱，你却庆幸终于甩掉了它。}\n\n@event grisk_cross\n# en: Grisk Is Not Pleased\ntitle [2bad70] = 格里斯克不高兴了\n# en: Grisk is sitting on your bed. You didn't leave the window open. \"A parcel was lost,\" he says pleasantly, turning a page in his ledger. \"Lost things have a price. I've written it down. Would you like to see?\"\ntext [ecab8b] = 格里斯克坐在你的床上。你没有开窗。“一个包裹丢了，”他愉快地说，翻着账簿。“丢了的东西是有价钱的。我已经记下来了。要看看吗？”\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting on a student's bed uninvited, a ledger open on his knee, the dormitory window open behind him.\nscene [e93020] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精不请自来地坐在学生床上，膝上摊着账簿，身后宿舍的窗户开着。\n# en: Put it on your account\nopt.left.label [e01cc0] = 记在你的账上\n# en: \"Two more months,\" he says, and writes it neatly. \"Such a good customer.\" He leaves by the window. You don't sleep with it open again.\nopt.left.say.1 [aa63e2] = “再加两个月，”他说着，把它写得整整齐齐。“真是个好主顾。”他从窗户走了。从那以后你再也没开着窗睡过觉。\n# en: He writes your name in the ledger under a new line: one parcel, six payments. \"Twelve a month,\" he says, \"as usual.\" He leaves by the window. You don't sleep with it open again.\nopt.left.say.2 [0b39d1] = 他在账簿上你的名字下面新添了一行：一个包裹，分六期。“每月十二克朗，”他说，“老规矩。”他从窗户走了。从那以后你再也没开着窗睡过觉。\n# en: Tell him you're done\nopt.right.label [1b5f57] = 告诉他你不干了\n# en: \"Everyone is, eventually,\" Grisk says. On your way to breakfast, the stair you always take collapses three steps under you. Nobody saw. Everybody heard. You never see him again.\nopt.right.say.1 [1693f0] = “每个人最后都会这么说，”格里斯克说。去吃早饭的路上，你常走的那段楼梯在你脚下塌了三级。没人看见。所有人都听见了。你再也没见过他。\n\n@event kitchen_stores\n# en: The Stores\ntitle [de5442] = 储藏室\n# en: {Behind the kitchens is the storeroom, full of sugar, spices and oranges from the south. The village pays well for oranges in winter, and nobody counts the crates.|The storeroom again. You know which crate is at the back and never counted.|The stores have a new padlock, which is mostly decorative.} {A mouse watches you without judgement.|The kitchen cat does judge you.|Somewhere a pot is still dripping.}\ntext [855855] = {厨房后面是储藏室，堆满了糖、香料和南方运来的橙子。冬天村里橙子卖得好，而且没人清点这些箱子。|又是储藏室。你知道哪一箱在最里面，从来没人数过。|储藏室换了一把新挂锁，基本上是个摆设。}{一只老鼠看着你，毫无评判之意。|厨房的猫倒是在评判你。|某处一口锅还在滴水。}\n# en: A dark storeroom behind a castle kitchen, sacks of sugar and crates of oranges stacked high, a student with an empty satchel.\nscene [6082df] = 城堡厨房后面一间漆黑的储藏室，糖袋和橙子箱堆得老高，一个学生拿着空挎包。\n# en: Fill a satchel to sell\nopt.left.label [d756fa] = 装满一挎包去卖\n# en: {Oranges, a cone of sugar, a jar of saffron. The village grocer asks no questions and pays in coppers. Somebody in the castle will be short of saffron.|A full satchel and an empty conscience, or nearly. On the way out, the scullery door bangs behind you.|The new padlock opens with a hairpin. You fill the satchel, and the grocer pays, and you wonder who'll be blamed for the lock.}\nopt.left.say.1 [43fa37] = {橙子、一块圆锥形的糖、一罐藏红花。村里的杂货商什么也不问，付的是铜板。城堡里会有人缺藏红花用了。|挎包装满了，良心几乎清空了。出门时，洗碗间的门在你身后砰的一响。|新挂锁用一根发夹就开了。你装满了挎包，杂货商付了钱，你想知道这锁的事会怪到谁头上。}\n# en: Take one orange and go\nopt.right.label [1f5959] = 拿一个橙子就走\n# en: {You eat it in the dark by the banked fire. It's the best orange you have ever eaten, and you know exactly why.|One orange. You leave the peel in the fire so nobody finds it.|You take one orange past the new padlock and feel worse about it than about all the crates.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4f7fd6] = {你在黑暗中靠着封住的炉火把它吃了。这是你吃过最好吃的橙子，你也很清楚为什么。|一个橙子。你把皮丢进火里，这样没人会发现。|你绕过新挂锁拿了一个橙子，感觉比偷了所有箱子还糟。}\n\n@event kitchen_night_baker\n# en: The Night Baker\ntitle [97e00b] = 守夜的面包师\n# en: {The bakehouse behind the kitchens is lit. The night baker, flour to the elbows, looks up. \"Hungry or helpful?\" she says. \"Both is allowed.\"|The night baker waves you in without looking up. \"Dough's waiting.\"|The bakehouse smells of tomorrow's bread. The baker has saved you a heel of yesterday's.} {The oven sighs.|A tray of rolls is cooling by the door.|She is humming something old.}\ntext [1b7271] = {厨房后面的烤房亮着灯。守夜的面包师手臂上沾满面粉，抬起头来。“饿了还是来帮忙？”她说，“两样都行。”|守夜的面包师头也不抬地招手让你进来。“面团在等你。”|烤房里飘着明天的面包香。面包师给你留了一块昨天的面包头。}{炉子叹了口气。|一盘小面包正在门边晾着。|她在哼一支古老的曲子。}\n# en: A warm castle bakehouse at midnight, a flour-dusted old baker kneading dough by the light of an open oven, a student leaning in the doorway.\nscene [5fcd67] = 午夜里温暖的城堡烤房，一位满身面粉的老面包师借着敞开的炉火揉面，一个学生倚在门口。\n# en: Knead until the oven's hot\nopt.left.label [f28ed8] = 揉面直到炉子烧热\n# en: {You knead until your arms shake. She gives you the first roll out of the oven and no lecture at all.|An hour of flour and warmth, and a hot roll for your trouble. You go back to bed with your hands smelling of bread, and sleep like a stone.|You knead while she hums something old. When the loaves go in, she toasts yesterday's heel for you, and you eat it on the step.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1c4c8b] = {你揉面揉到胳膊发抖。她把第一个出炉的小面包给了你，一句说教也没有。|一个钟头的面粉和温暖，还有一个热乎乎的小面包作为酬劳。你回去睡觉时手上还带着面包味，睡得像块石头。|她哼着一支老歌，你揉着面。面包进炉后，她把昨天的面包头烤了给你，你坐在台阶上吃了。}\n# en: Just sit by the oven\nopt.right.label [363883] = 就坐在炉边\n# en: {You sit by the open oven with your knees pulled up. She works, talks to the dough, and lets you be.|You sit by the oven with a roll from the cooling tray while she works. She doesn't ask why you're up. You don't ask why she is.|You eat the heel of bread and warm your feet. Upstairs, the castle is cold; down here, it never is.}\nopt.right.say.1 [efc83a] = {你抱着膝盖坐在敞开的炉边。她干她的活，跟面团说话，由你待着。|她干活，你拿着晾盘上的一个小面包坐在炉边。她没问你为什么还醒着。你也没问她。|你吃了那块面包头，暖了暖脚。楼上的城堡很冷；这下面，从来不冷。}\n\n@event restricted_page_buyer\n# en: A Buyer for Old Pages\ntitle [8c6109] = 古书页的买主\n# en: {Grisk mentioned, once, that a collector pays for illuminated pages \"of a certain age\". The Restricted Section is full of them.|{flag(sold_page): There's a gap on the shelf where the last page you sold came from.|The illuminated book you read last time is back on its shelf, untouched.}|The illuminated books lean away from you as you pass.} {A gold-leaf dragon glints on one page.|One margin is full of tiny stars, all black.|A page here is worth a month's board.}\ntext [bb1bed] = {格里斯克曾提过一次，有个收藏家肯为“有些年头的”彩绘书页出钱。禁书区里满是这种书页。|{flag(sold_page): 书架上有个空缺，你上次卖的那一页就是从那儿来的。|你上次读过的那本彩绘书回到了书架上，原封未动。}|你经过时，那些彩绘书都往旁边躲。}{一页上有条金箔画的龙在闪光。|一处页边画满了小星星，全是黑的。|这里的一页抵得上一个月的食宿费。}\n# en: A dim Restricted Section aisle at midnight, a student holding a razor over an illuminated page, a black book watching from the shelf above.\nscene [84c409] = 午夜禁书区一条昏暗的过道，一个学生拿着刀片悬在一页彩绘书页上方，书架上一本黑书在看着。\n# en: Cut a page\nopt.left.label [5f6c23] = 割下一页\n# en: {One clean cut. The book doesn't cry out, which is somehow worse. The collector pays well, and never asks where.|You sell the page and keep the guilt, which no one will buy.|The books lean away, and you cut a page anyway. A month's board, in one stroke. You don't come back to this aisle for a long time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c1c51f] = {干净利落的一刀。书没有叫出声，这不知怎么更糟。收藏家出价很高，也从不问来路。|你卖掉了书页，留下了愧疚，这东西没人肯买。|书都朝远离你的方向倾，你还是割了一页。一刀下去，一个月的食宿费。很长一段时间你都没再来这排书架。}\n# en: Read it instead\nopt.right.label [19ccdc] = 改为读它\n# en: {You read the illuminated page by moonlight. It's a recipe for honey cakes, in exquisite gold leaf, and the gilding draws at your mana the whole time you read. You leave it where it is.|The page is a list of stars, by name, and each name pulls a little magic out of you as you read it. You learn three, and put the book back.|The books lean away from you, then back, as you read instead of cut. The page is a map of the castle as it was. It costs you a little magic to look at it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9d1c63] = {你借着月光读那页彩绘书页。是一份蜂蜜蛋糕的食谱，用精美的金箔写成，你读的时候那层金箔一直在吸你的魔力。你把它留在原处。|那一页是一份星星的名单，每个名字在你读出时都会从你身上抽走一点魔力。你记住了三颗，把书放了回去。|你读而不割，书先是朝外倾，后来又倾了回来。那一页是城堡从前的地图。看它要耗掉你一点魔力。}\n\n@event midnight_roof_partner\n# en: The Roof, Together\ntitle [ad8b7c] = 屋顶上的两个人\n# en: {You've brought {partner} up to the observatory roof, with a stolen blanket and two cups of cocoa going cold.|{partner} knows the way up now, and goes first.|{partner} has brought the blanket this time.} {The stars are very close.|A meteor, then another.|The castle below is all asleep.}\ntext [6b40c7] = {你把{partner}带上了观星台的屋顶，带着一条偷来的毯子和两杯渐渐变凉的可可。|{partner}现在认得上来的路了，走在前面。|这次是{partner}带了毯子。}{星星离得很近。|一颗流星，接着又是一颗。|下面的城堡全都睡着了。}\n# en: Two students lying side by side on an observatory roof at midnight under a sky full of stars, one blanket between them.\nscene [2af17c] = 午夜里两个学生并排躺在观星台屋顶上，头顶是满天星斗，两人盖着同一条毯子。\n# en: Stay until the sky turns\nopt.left.label [6b603a] = 待到天色变亮\n# en: {You name the stars between you, wrong, and argue about it, and stay until the sky goes grey. The stair down is lit by a prefect's lamp you just avoid.|You fall asleep up there and wake to the first bell, frozen and happy.|{partner} wraps you both in the blanket {they} brought and talks you to sleep. The castle wakes up around you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bd9e3a] = {你们俩一起给星星起名字，起错了，争了起来，一直待到天色发灰。下楼时一位级长的灯光照亮了楼梯，你们刚好躲开。|你们在上面睡着了，醒来时第一遍钟已经响了，冻得要命，却很快乐。|{partner}用{them}带来的毯子把你们俩裹起来，说着话把你哄睡了。城堡在你们周围醒来。}\n# en: An hour, then back\nopt.right.label [3dda16] = 待一个钟头就回去\n# en: {One hour, one blanket, back in bed before anyone stirs. You can still feel where {partner} was leaning on you.|You go down early. {partner} squeezes your hand on the stair, which is better than any star.|One hour under {partner}'s blanket. {They} fold{s} it and give{s} it to you at the bottom of the stair: \"For next time.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [57c547] = {一个钟头，一条毯子，在任何人醒来前回到床上。你还能感觉到{partner}靠在你身上的地方。|你们早早下了楼。{partner}在楼梯上捏了捏你的手，比哪颗星星都好。|在{partner}的毯子底下待了一个钟头。在楼梯底下，{they}把毯子叠好递给你：“下次用。”}\n\n@event kael_midnight_roof\n# en: Kael at Midnight\ntitle [c04a03] = 午夜的凯尔\n# en: {Kael is on the observatory parapet, which he shouldn't be, with frost spreading from his fingers into the stone. He moves over to make room.|Kael again, on the parapet, the night's cold not touching him.|Kael has left room on the parapet before you arrive.} {\"I can't sleep south of the mountains,\" he says. \"Too warm.\"|He is watching the lake, not the stars.|\"Northern stars,\" he says, pointing at nothing you can see.}\ntext [45cc53] = {凯尔坐在观星台的护墙上，这本来是不允许的，霜从他的指尖蔓延进石头里。他挪了挪，给你让出位置。|又是凯尔，坐在护墙上，夜里的寒气碰不到他。|你还没到，凯尔就已经在护墙上给你留好了位置。}{“在山脉以南我睡不着，”他说，“太暖和了。”|他在看湖，不在看星星。|“北方的星星，”他说着，指向一处你什么也看不见的地方。}\n# en: A northern student sitting on the observatory parapet at midnight with frost spreading from his hands, the lake far below, the sky thick with stars.\nscene [330d76] = 午夜里一个北方学生坐在观星台护墙上，霜从他手上蔓延开来，湖在远远的下方，天上星斗密布。\n# en: Sit with him\nopt.left.label [6fadbf] = 陪他坐一会儿\n# en: {You sit until your fingers ache from the cold. He names northern constellations in a language that sounds like snow falling. It's the most he's ever said.|He doesn't talk. Neither do you. When you go, he says \"Goodnight,\" as if it were difficult, and it was.|He's left room, so you take it. He points out a northern star you can't see, and you pretend you can. He knows, and is pleased.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c3f2b3] = {你一直坐到手指冻得发疼。他用一种听起来像落雪的语言说出北方星座的名字。这是他说话最多的一次。|他不说话。你也不说。你走的时候，他说“晚安”，好像这很难说出口，确实如此。|他留了位置，你就坐了。他指着一颗你看不见的北方的星，你假装看见了。他知道，还挺高兴。}\n# en: Ask what he's watching for\nopt.right.label [442add] = 问他在等什么\n# en: {\"For it to wake,\" he says, and then shuts up like a book. You've pressed too hard. But you know which way he faces now.|\"Nothing,\" he says, too fast. He's watching the middle of the lake. You leave him to it, colder than when you came.|\"Home,\" he says, about the northern stars, and nothing more. It's the most honest thing he's told you, and he looks away after.}\nopt.right.say.1 [085545] = {“等它醒来，”他说完，就像一本书一样合上了。你逼得太紧了。但现在你知道他面朝哪个方向了。|“没什么，”他说得太快。他在盯着湖中央。你留他一个人在那儿，走时比来时更冷。|“家，”他指着北方的星星说，再没多说。这是他对你说过最坦白的话，说完他移开了目光。}\n\n@event corvin_note_was_mine\n# en: Second Timetable\ntitle [4a5d2a] = 第二张作息表\n# en: In the gargoyle passage, Corvin holds up a map no bigger than a thumbnail. \"Recognise it? The castle has a second timetable. It starts at midnight.\" He grins. \"Who did you think writes notes like that? Prefects?\"\ntext [2e7f05] = 在石像鬼通道里，科尔文举起一张不比拇指大的地图。“认得吗？城堡有第二张作息表。从午夜开始。”他咧嘴一笑。“你以为是谁写那种字条的？级长吗？”\n# en: A secret passage behind a gargoyle at midnight, a grinning dark-haired student holding up a thumbnail-sized silver map, chalk marks on the wall behind him.\nscene [35de3d] = 午夜一条石像鬼后面的秘密通道，一个咧嘴笑着的黑发学生举着一张拇指大小的银色地图，身后墙上画着粉笔记号。\n# en: \"Why me?\"\nopt.left.label [e79264] = “为什么是我？”\n# en: \"Because you looked at that door at the back of the library the way I do.\" He shrugs, and doesn't joke. \"And because it's lonely, knowing all the doors.\" You don't sleep much, after.\nopt.left.say.1 [b6a5ed] = “因为你看图书馆后面那扇门的眼神，跟我一样。”他耸耸肩，没有开玩笑。“还因为，知道所有的门，挺孤单的。”那之后你没怎么睡着。\n# en: \"You could have signed it.\"\nopt.right.label [e5208c] = “你本可以署名的。”\n# en: \"And spoil it?\" He tucks the map into your pocket. \"Keep it. The doors move. The map knows.\" You check it in the morning, and one of the six doors has moved.\nopt.right.say.1 [0b70cd] = “那多没意思？”他把地图塞进你口袋。“留着吧。门会挪地方。地图知道。”第二天早上你一看，六扇门里有一扇挪了位置。\n\n@event corvin_midnight_watch\n# en: Corvin Keeps Watch\ntitle [aa7961] = 科尔文放风\n# en: {Corvin leans out of a niche as you pass. \"Bram's on the east stair. Take the west. You owe me nothing, I'm a saint.\"|Corvin's in his niche again with an apple and a list of where every prefect is.|\"West stair,\" says Corvin, before you ask.} {He looks tired.|He has a new scratch on his cheek.|He's whistling, which he shouldn't.}\ntext [45e683] = {你经过时，科尔文从一个壁龛里探出身来。“布拉姆在东边楼梯。走西边。你什么都不欠我，我是圣人。”|科尔文又待在他的壁龛里，拿着一个苹果和一张每个级长的位置清单。|“西边楼梯，”你还没开口，科尔文就说了。}{他看起来很累。|他脸颊上多了一道新划痕。|他在吹口哨，这可不应该。}\n# en: A dark corridor at midnight, a dark-haired student leaning out of a niche to wave someone past, a prefect's lantern turning a far corner.\nscene [151b9a] = 午夜一条漆黑的走廊，一个黑发学生从壁龛里探出身来挥手让人通过，远处拐角有一盏级长的灯笼在转。\n# en: Keep him company\nopt.left.label [dd3cdd] = 陪他待一会儿\n# en: {You share his apple and his niche. He tells you which tapestry hides a cupboard full of confiscated wands. You're up till the bells.|You keep watch with him for an hour. He's funnier at midnight, and sadder, and pretends to be neither.|He whistles until you shush him, then whistles quieter. You keep watch till the bell and learn three new routes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c9e2c9] = {你分享了他的苹果和他的壁龛。他告诉你哪块挂毯后面藏着一柜子没收来的魔杖。你一直待到钟声响起。|你陪他放了一个钟头的风。午夜的他更风趣，也更伤感，却假装两样都不是。|他一直吹口哨，你嘘了他，他就吹得小声些。你陪他守到钟响，学会了三条新路线。}\n# en: Take the west stair\nopt.right.label [19b545] = 走西边楼梯\n# en: {The west stair is dark and empty, as promised. Corvin's map is never wrong. You wonder who taught him to be so careful.|You slip past Bram's lamp with a corridor to spare.|West stair, before you've asked. Corvin's whistling follows you down it, far too loud, and nobody catches either of you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f44f09] = {西边楼梯漆黑而空荡，正如他所说。科尔文的地图从来不会错。你很好奇是谁教会他这么小心的。|你隔着一条走廊的距离从布拉姆的灯下溜了过去。|你还没问他就说了西边楼梯。科尔文的口哨声一路跟着你下去，响得过分，可谁也没抓到你们俩。}\n\n@event midnight_swim\n# en: The Lake at Midnight\ntitle [d7eddc] = 午夜的湖\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The lake at midnight has a skin of ice at the edges and stars in the middle.|{~The lake at midnight is black and silver and perfectly still.|Nobody swims after curfew, which is why you do.|The water is warm from the day and the air is not.}} {clock(hollowstar) >= 5: Far out, a ring of frost forms and fades.|{~An owl crosses the moon.|Somewhere on the far shore, a lantern.|A fish jumps, very loudly.}}\ntext [aaf6ce] = {season == \"winter\": 午夜的湖边缘结了一层冰，中央倒映着星星。|{~午夜的湖黑中带银，纹丝不动。|宵禁后没人游泳，所以你才去。|水还留着白天的温度，空气却没有。}}{clock(hollowstar) >= 5: 远处，一圈霜冻成形，又消散了。|{~一只猫头鹰飞过月亮。|对岸某处有一盏灯笼。|一条鱼跳了起来，声音很响。}}\n# en: A moonlit lakeshore at midnight, clothes folded on a rock, a swimmer's head and a ring of ripples in the silver water.\nscene [7c16dd] = 午夜月光下的湖岸，衣服叠放在一块石头上，银色的水面上露出一个游泳者的头和一圈圈涟漪。\n# en: Swim out to the middle\nopt.left.label [1bcb3e] = 游到湖中央\n# en: {~You swim until the castle is a toy and the stars are all around you. The cold gets into your bones and stays till breakfast.|You float on your back in the middle of the lake. Something far below hums, once. You swim back faster than you swam out.|You swim out and turn on your back. The stars and the water are the same colour, and you lose track of which is which. You swim back slowly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b1622d] = {~你一直游到城堡变得像个玩具，四面八方都是星星。寒气钻进骨头里，一直待到早饭时。|你仰面漂在湖中央。下面很深的地方嗡地响了一声。你游回去比游出来快多了。|你游出去，翻身仰躺。星星和湖水是同一种颜色，你分不清哪个是哪个。你慢慢地游了回去。}\n# en: Paddle, then get dry\nopt.right.label [cf2997] = 蹚蹚水就上岸擦干\n# en: {~Knee-deep, then waist-deep, then out, gasping and laughing at yourself. Your teeth chatter all the way up the lawn.|A quick, cold paddle, and back to bed glowing.|You paddle until the fish jumps right beside you. You're out of the water before you've decided to be.}\nopt.right.say.1 [04f629] = {~齐膝，齐腰，然后上岸，喘着气笑话自己。回草坪那头的一路上你的牙齿都在打颤。|又快又冷地蹚了一会儿水，回到床上浑身发热。|你蹚着水，直到那条鱼就在你身边跃起。你还没决定上岸，人已经在岸上了。}\n\n@event grey_on_the_shore\n# en: Someone Else Who Can't Sleep\ntitle [cc0062] = 另一个睡不着的人\n# en: The man in grey is sitting on an upturned boat at the water's edge, as if he'd been waiting for a ferry. \"You can't sleep either,\" he says pleasantly. \"Nobody sleeps well near this lake. Sit. I won't keep you long. I only want to tell you what the headmistress won't.\"\ntext [b8c4b5] = 灰衣人坐在水边一条倒扣的船上，像是在等渡船。“你也睡不着，”他愉快地说，“在这湖边没人睡得好。坐吧。我不会耽搁你太久。我只是想告诉你校长不肯说的事。”\n# en: A moonlit lakeshore at midnight, a tall man in a grey coat sitting on an upturned boat, frost spreading from his boots across the pebbles.\nscene [a47a73] = 午夜月光下的湖岸，一个穿灰大衣的高个男人坐在一条倒扣的船上，霜从他的靴子底下蔓延到卵石上。\n# en: Sit and listen\nopt.left.label [1a3cf5] = 坐下听他说\n# en: He tells you, kindly, that the founders were frightened people who buried what they couldn't understand, and that it has been lonely down there for three hundred years. It sounds true. You walk back with it, and can't put it down.\nopt.left.say.1 [7f0733] = 他和气地告诉你，创始者们是一群受了惊吓的人，把自己不理解的东西埋了起来，而那东西在下面已经孤单了三百年。听起来像是真的。你带着这些话走回去，怎么也放不下。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.right.label [96da1a] = 走开\n# en: You turn your back and walk, and the cold follows you all the way up the lawn like a dog. At the door you look back. The boat is empty, and frosted white.\nopt.right.say.1 [a9b029] = 你转身就走，寒意像条狗一样一路跟着你走过草坪。到了门口你回头一看，那条船空了，结满了白霜。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/placeholders.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/placeholders.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event courier_loft_quiet_hour\n# en: A Quiet Hour at the Loft\ntitle [d389f3] = 阁楼里安静的一小时\n# en: The Courier Loft is quiet today. The dispatch bell hangs still, the pigeonholes are full and nobody needs anything flown anywhere. It's a nice place when it isn't in a hurry.\ntext [9f81f9] = 今天信使阁楼很安静。派件铃一动不动地挂着，信格都塞满了，也没人需要把什么东西飞送到哪里去。不赶时间的时候，这里是个不错的地方。\n# en: An empty courier loft in slanting light, parcels stacked neatly, a single broom asleep in its rack.\nscene [3ebd34] = 斜阳照进空荡荡的信使阁楼，包裹码得整整齐齐，一把飞帚在架子上打盹。\n# en: Sit by the window a while\nopt.left.label [d7f0cb] = 在窗边坐一会儿\n# en: {~You watch the brooms doze in their rack and the dust turn in the light. Nothing happens, pleasantly.|An hour among the parcels with nobody shouting your name. You leave in a better mood.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1d96c5] = {~你看着飞帚在架子上打盹，灰尘在光里打转。什么也没发生，挺好。|在包裹堆里待了一个钟头，没人喊你的名字。你离开时心情好了些。}\n# en: Head off\nopt.right.label [3153de] = 走吧\n# en: {~You leave the loft to its quiet.|Nothing to fly today. You go and find something else to do.}\nopt.right.say.1 [89c506] = {~你把阁楼留给它的安静。|今天没什么要送的。你去找别的事做。}\n\n@event headmistress_tower_quiet_hour\n# en: An Empty Study\ntitle [46743e] = 空无一人的书房\n# en: The study at the top of the tower is empty. The headmistress is elsewhere, the tea has gone cold and the crystal board by the window throws little rainbows on the books. It's a nice room when nobody is deciding your future in it.\ntext [550ff3] = 塔顶的书房空着。院长去了别处，茶已经凉了，窗边的水晶棋盘把小小的彩虹投在书上。没人在这里决定你前途的时候，这是个不错的房间。\n# en: A tower study with nobody in it, tea going cold on a tray, a board of crystal towers catching the afternoon sun.\nscene [1d8fbb] = 空无一人的塔顶书房，托盘上的茶渐渐变凉，一盘水晶塔棋映着午后的阳光。\n# en: Enjoy the view a moment\nopt.left.label [b26df9] = 欣赏一会儿风景\n# en: {~You stand at the window and look down at the whole castle, small and busy. Then you go, before anyone asks why you're here.|The rainbows move slowly across the shelves. It's very peaceful, and a little like trespassing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b8cd9d] = {~你站在窗前俯瞰整座城堡，小小的，忙忙碌碌。然后在有人问你为什么在这儿之前离开。|彩虹慢慢爬过书架。很安宁，也有点像擅闯。}\n# en: Leave before anyone comes\nopt.right.label [8e0dfa] = 趁没人来先走\n# en: {~You close the door softly behind you.|Nobody here today. You take the long stair down.}\nopt.right.say.1 [555f7d] = {~你轻轻带上身后的门。|今天这里没人。你沿着长长的楼梯走下去。}\n\n@event ledger_office_quiet_hour\n# en: A Slow News Day\ntitle [a6734a] = 没什么新闻的一天\n# en: It's a slow news day at the Lumen Ledger. The press is cold, the editor's chair is empty and the only sound is the library above, creaking. It's a nice room when nobody is on deadline.\ntext [6b16f1] = 今天《流明纪事报》没什么新闻。印刷机冷着，主编的椅子空着，唯一的声音是楼上图书馆的吱呀声。没人赶截稿的时候，这是个不错的房间。\n# en: A cramped newspaper office under a staircase, the press silent, old front pages curling on the walls.\nscene [a80603] = 楼梯下狭小的报社编辑部，印刷机静悄悄的，墙上的旧头版卷着边。\n# en: Read the old front pages\nopt.left.label [ad86e4] = 读读旧头版\n# en: {~You read headlines from before you were born. Half of them are about the refectory soup.|An hour with the old front pages. The castle has always been like this, it turns out.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8c60cf] = {~你读着你出生前的头条。一半都在说膳堂的汤。|和旧头版待了一个钟头。原来这座城堡一直都是这样。}\n# en: Come back on deadline night\nopt.right.label [37993d] = 截稿那晚再来\n# en: {~Nothing to write today. You leave the press to its nap.|You tidy a stack of proofs and go.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b77242] = {~今天没什么可写的。你让印刷机继续打盹。|你理好一叠校样，走了。}\n\n@event flight_field_quiet_hour\n# en: An Empty Field\ntitle [93dd37] = 空荡荡的飞行场\n# en: Nobody is flying today. The Flight Field is all grass and wind, the hoops at either end swinging a little on their own. It's a nice place when nobody is crashing into it.\ntext [430c2b] = 今天没人飞。飞行场上只有草和风，两头的圈环自己轻轻晃着。没人往地上栽的时候，这里是个不错的地方。\n# en: A long green flying field with nobody on it, hoops at either end, grass flattened by the wind.\nscene [e2279e] = 长长的绿色飞行场上空无一人，两头立着圈环，草被风压平了。\n# en: Lie in the grass a while\nopt.left.label [8fb767] = 在草地上躺一会儿\n# en: {~You watch the clouds race the hoops. The clouds win.|The wind does the flying today. You just watch it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5a09ac] = {~你看着云和圈环赛跑。云赢了。|今天是风在飞。你只是看着。}\n# en: Walk on\nopt.right.label [383259] = 继续走\n# en: {~You cut across the empty field and go on your way.|No lessons, no races. You leave the field to the wind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1f07bf] = {~你穿过空荡荡的飞行场，继续赶路。|没有课，没有比赛。你把飞行场留给了风。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/plans_gigs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/plans_gigs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event holiday_gigs\n# en: Holiday Work\ntitle [ec8a5c] = 假期打工\n# en: Two notices are still pinned by the gates. {season == \"winter\": The snow has got under one of them.|The sun has curled the corners of both.} Old Maud wants hands for the grounds. The Refectory kitchens want someone who can peel fast and not complain about it. {rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: A third notice has your name on it.|}\ntext [9dfc2d] = 城门边还钉着两张告示。{season == \"winter\": 雪已经钻到其中一张底下了。|太阳把两张的边角都晒卷了。}老莫德要人手打理庭园。膳堂后厨要一个削皮快、还不抱怨的人。{rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: 第三张告示上写着你的名字。|}\n# en: A noticeboard by the castle gates in the emptying holidays, two handwritten notices pinned side by side.\nscene [e17fee] = 假期里渐渐空下来的城堡大门边，一块告示板上并排钉着两张手写告示。\n# en: Work the grounds for Old Maud\nopt.grounds.label [304060] = 跟老莫德打理庭园\n# en: Outdoor work, heavy and honest. Good pay; hard on the body.\nopt.grounds.hint [1918f6] = 户外活，累但实在。工钱不错；伤身体。\n# en: Maud looks you up and down, hands you a pair of gloves two sizes too big, and says, \"You'll grow into them.\"\nopt.grounds.say.1 [d53e24] = 莫德把你从头打量到脚，递给你一副大了两号的手套，说：“你会长大的。”\n# en: Work the Refectory kitchens\nopt.kitchens.label [fb1ad3] = 去膳堂后厨帮工\n# en: Long hours by the ovens. Good pay; hard on the temper.\nopt.kitchens.hint [305589] = 在炉灶边站一整天。工钱不错；磨脾气。\n# en: The head cook gives you an apron, a knife and a sack of onions, and doesn't ask your name until the sack is empty.\nopt.kitchens.say.1 [e58aab] = 大厨递给你一条围裙、一把刀和一麻袋洋葱，直到麻袋空了才问你叫什么。\n# en: Run the library's holiday stocktake\nopt.library.label [b69455] = 负责图书馆的假期盘点\n# en: For senior clerks. Better pay; long days in the stacks.\nopt.library.hint [8ad639] = 给资深馆员的活。报酬更高；整天泡在书库里。\n# en: The head librarian hands you the stocktake ledger, a lamp and a pair of gauntlets. \"Count everything,\" she says. \"Twice, if it moves.\"\nopt.library.say.1 [0880b9] = 总馆员把盘点账簿、一盏灯和一副护手套交给你。“什么都要数，”她说，“会动的数两遍。”\n# en: Run the Newt through the holiday rush\nopt.newt.label [3759b3] = 带蝾螈酒馆撑过节日高峰\n# en: For the Newt's managers. The best tips of the year; the longest nights.\nopt.newt.hint [b33ad6] = 给蝾螈酒馆经理的活。一年里最好的小费；最长的夜。\n# en: The landlady gives you the keys and goes to visit her sister. \"Don't let the fire go out,\" she says, \"or the village.\"\nopt.newt.say.1 [33e627] = 老板娘把钥匙交给你，去看她妹妹了。“别让炉火灭了，”她说，“也别让村子乱了。”\n# en: Mix the season's remedies at Pell's\nopt.pell.label [beefb9] = 在佩尔的铺子调配应季药剂\n# en: For assistant apothecaries. Good pay; the village depends on you.\nopt.pell.hint [48142a] = 给助理药师的活。报酬不错；全村都指望着你。\n# en: Old Pell ties an apron on you himself. \"Half the valley will be coughing by next week,\" he says, delighted. \"We'll be rich.\"\nopt.pell.say.1 [3c5cdc] = 老佩尔亲手给你系上围裙。“下个星期半个山谷都要咳嗽，”他高兴地说，“我们要发财了。”\n# en: Fly the holiday post\nopt.post.label [339ed9] = 飞送节日邮件\n# en: For chief couriers. Every parcel in the valley; the weather against you.\nopt.post.hint [0579c6] = 给首席信使的活。山谷里的每一个包裹；还有跟你作对的天气。\n# en: The loft is stacked to the rafters with parcels in brown paper. Your name is chalked on the board above the busiest route.\nopt.post.say.1 [50dcda] = 阁楼里牛皮纸包裹堆到了房梁。最繁忙的那条路线上方的板子上，用粉笔写着你的名字。\n\n@event holiday_notices\n# en: Notices by the Gate\ntitle [92fc07] = 城门边的告示\n# en: {Holiday work notices are going up by the gates, for the grounds and the kitchens, both paying better than any term job.|The holiday notices again, the same two, in fresher ink.|The notices by the gate. You know them by heart.} {rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: One is addressed to you by name, from your own job.|{Someone has written \"and board!\" under one.|A first-year is copying them out.|The wind is trying to take them.}}\ntext [466e79] = {城门边贴出了假期招工告示，庭园和后厨，报酬都比任何学期里的工作高。|又是那两张假期告示，墨迹更新了。|城门边的告示。你已经背下来了。}{rank(library_assistant) >= 3 or rank(tavern) >= 3 or rank(apothecary_clerk) >= 3 or rank(broom_courier) >= 3: 其中一张是你自己的工作写给你的，指名道姓。|{有人在一张下面写了“还包食宿！”|一个一年级生正在抄写。|风正想把它们刮走。}}\n# en: A noticeboard by the castle gates near the end of term, fresh notices for holiday work pinned over the old ones, students stopping to read.\nscene [ff22d4] = 学期快结束时城门边的告示板，新的假期招工告示钉在旧告示上面，学生们停下来看。\n# en: Pin them up for the cook\nopt.left.label [bead3d] = 替厨师把告示钉好\n# en: {The head cook pays you a coin to pin the new notices straight. Working the break would mean no lie-ins, and real money.|You pin them up, get a coin for it, and read the hours. It's tempting, and tiring just to read.|You pin them up against the wind for the head cook, who pays you a coin and asks, again, whether you'll be staying.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ac56f1] = {后厨大厨给你一枚硬币，让你把新告示钉正。假期打工意味着没有懒觉，但有真金白银。|你把告示钉好，得了一枚硬币，顺便看了看工时。很诱人，光是读一读就累了。|你顶着风替大厨把告示钉好，他给了你一枚硬币，又一次问你假期留不留下。}\n# en: Walk past\nopt.right.label [d5359c] = 走过去\n# en: {The break is for sleeping, you decide, and walk on. The notices flap behind you.|Not this time. You'll choose when the break comes.|You walk past. You know them by heart, and the break is still for sleeping.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bec8c3] = {假期是用来睡觉的，你决定，然后走开了。告示在你身后哗啦作响。|这次就算了。放假的时候再选吧。|你走了过去。告示你早就背熟了，假期还是用来睡觉的。}\n\n@event gig_grounds_hard_ground\n# en: Hard Ground\ntitle [fbbd07] = 硬土\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The ground is frozen two fingers deep and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.|The ground is baked hard and Maud wants the beds turned anyway.} \"The soil doesn't take holidays,\" she says. {~She hands you the heavier spade.|The bees watch you from their hives with professional interest.|She is already on her third bed.}\ntext [3eb87e] = {season == \"winter\": 地冻了两指深，莫德照样要你把苗床翻一遍。|地被晒得梆硬，莫德照样要你把苗床翻一遍。}“土可不放假。”她说。{~她把重的那把铁锹递给你。|蜂箱里的蜜蜂以一种专业的兴趣看着你。|她已经在翻第三块了。}\n# en: A student and an old woman working a frozen garden bed at dawn, breath steaming, a wheelbarrow between them.\nscene [2f53ea] = 黎明时分，一个学生和一位老妇人在结冻的苗床上干活，呼出白气，两人之间放着一辆手推车。\n# en: Dig until it's done\nopt.left.label [9aec2a] = 一直挖到干完\n# en: {~By noon your hands have blisters on their blisters. Maud counts your wages into them anyway.|You turn every bed. Maud inspects them, says \"Hm,\" and pays you a little over.|You dig until Maud's third bed is matched by your third. She pays you, and gives you the lighter spade next time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a1924d] = {~到中午，你手上的水泡又起了水泡。莫德照样把工钱数进你手里。|你翻完了每一块苗床。莫德检查一遍，说了声“嗯”，多给了你一点。|你一直挖到你翻完的苗床追上了莫德的第三块。她付了钱，还说下回给你轻些的铁锹。}\n# en: Work at Maud's pace\nopt.right.label [1e7055] = 跟着莫德的节奏\n# en: {~Maud works slowly and never stops, and by evening you understand why. Less done, a smaller purse, an easier back.|You keep to her pace and her stories. She pays for the beds you turned, not the ones she did.|You work at her pace, and she still finishes three beds to your one. She pays for yours, and laughs about it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [abb3c1] = {~莫德干得慢，却从不停手，到傍晚你才明白为什么。活干得少，钱袋轻些，腰也轻松些。|你跟着她的节奏，听着她的故事。她按你翻的苗床付钱，不算她翻的。|你照她的节奏干，她还是翻了三块，你才翻一块。她按你翻的付钱，笑个不停。}\n\n@event gig_grounds_storm_damage\n# en: After the Storm\ntitle [683893] = 风暴过后\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Last night's storm brought down half a pear tree.|A summer storm in the night has stripped the orchard.} Maud wants it cleared before the stay-behinds go walking. A cracked limb still hangs over the path.\ntext [506bdf] = {season == \"winter\": 昨夜的风暴吹倒了半棵梨树。|夜里一场夏日风暴把果园刮得七零八落。}莫德要在留校的人出来散步之前把路清出来。一根裂开的树枝还悬在小路上方。\n# en: A fallen branch across an orchard path after a storm, a student with a saw, an old woman pointing at something higher up.\nscene [a80029] = 风暴过后，一根断枝横在果园小路上，一个学生拿着锯子，一位老妇人指着更高处的什么东西。\n# en: Climb up and cut it down\nopt.left.label [d35b25] = 爬上去把它锯下来\n# en: {~The limb comes down where you meant it to. Maud pays you extra for not falling.|You lower it on a rope like a professional. Maud pretends not to be impressed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [378de5] = {~树枝正落在你想让它落的地方。莫德因为你没摔下来，多给了你钱。|你像个行家一样用绳子把它放了下来。莫德假装没被打动。}\n# en: {~The limb comes down where it wanted to. So do you, more or less. Maud pays you and sends you to the infirmary.|You get the limb and the limb gets you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [10307d] = {~树枝落在了它想落的地方。你也差不多。莫德付了钱，把你送去了医务室。|你拿下了树枝，树枝也拿下了你。}\n# en: Rope it off and clear the rest\nopt.right.label [27673a] = 拉绳隔开，清理其余\n# en: {~You clear the path and rope off the danger. Maud nods: she'll ask someone with a ladder. The pay is for the path.|Safe and sensible, and a smaller purse for it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8ff26f] = {~你清好了小路，把危险的地方用绳子隔开。莫德点点头：她会找个有梯子的人来。工钱只算小路的。|稳妥又明智，钱袋也因此轻一些。}\n\n@event gig_grounds_hive_check\n# en: Checking the Hives\ntitle [5a5a35] = 查看蜂箱\n# en: Maud's hives sit at the end of the orchard and she checks them {season == \"winter\": by ear in winter, knocking and listening|by hand in summer, slow and bare-armed}. Today she wants you to do it. \"Tell them who you are,\" she says. \"They like manners.\"\ntext [169e49] = 莫德的蜂箱放在果园尽头，她{season == \"winter\": 冬天靠耳朵检查，敲一敲，听一听|夏天亲手检查，慢慢来，光着胳膊}。今天她要你来做。“告诉它们你是谁，”她说，“它们讲礼貌。”\n# en: A student in a veiled hat leaning over an open beehive beside an old woman, who isn't wearing one.\nscene [d6c9fc] = 一个戴着面纱帽的学生俯身在打开的蜂箱前，旁边的老妇人什么也没戴。\n# en: Introduce yourself properly\nopt.left.label [182928] = 规规矩矩地自我介绍\n# en: {~You tell the bees your name and your house. Only one stings you, on principle. Maud pays you and calls you a natural.|You feel foolish for about a minute, and then you don't. The hive hums back, and one bee makes a point of it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5a9176] = {~你把名字和学院告诉了蜜蜂。只有一只蜇了你，出于原则。莫德付了钱，说你是天生的料。|你觉得自己傻了大约一分钟，然后就不觉得了。蜂箱嗡嗡地回应你，有一只蜜蜂特意强调了一下。}\n# en: Get it done quickly\nopt.right.label [b40e28] = 快点干完\n# en: {~Quick and quiet. The bees let it pass this once.|You're done before the bees notice. Maud notices.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0c0b37] = {~又快又安静。蜜蜂这次放过了你。|你干完了，蜜蜂还没察觉。莫德察觉了。}\n# en: {~The bees take your hurry personally. Maud pays you and pulls out the stings with no sympathy at all.|Three stings and a lesson about manners.}\nopt.right.say.2 [f4f88a] = {~蜜蜂把你的匆忙当成了冒犯。莫德付了钱，毫无同情地替你拔出蜂刺。|三根蜂刺，外加一堂礼貌课。}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_onions\n# en: The Onion Sack\ntitle [c188ed] = 洋葱麻袋\n# en: {The holiday kitchens feed fewer mouths and work no fewer hours.|Back at the long table, back to the sack.|The head cook has started saving the worst sack for you, as a compliment.} {season == \"winter\": The Midwinter stock needs a hundred onions.|The summer stew needs a hundred onions.} Nobody has ever needed fewer.\ntext [dc295a] = {假期里的后厨要喂的嘴少了，活一点没少。|回到长桌，回到麻袋。|大厨开始把最糟的那袋留给你，算是一种夸奖。}{season == \"winter\": 仲冬的高汤要一百个洋葱。|夏天的炖菜要一百个洋葱。}从来没人需要少一点。\n# en: A student at a scrubbed kitchen table behind a mountain of onions, eyes streaming, a huge cook stirring a cauldron behind them.\nscene [a0922b] = 擦洗干净的厨房长桌后，一个学生埋在洋葱堆里泪流满面，身后一个高大的厨子在搅一口大锅。\n# en: Peel the whole sack\nopt.left.label [211f4f] = 把整袋都削完\n# en: {A hundred onions and not one tear left in you. The head cook pays you and adds a heel of fresh bread.|You finish the sack. The head cook counts the onions, then your wages, both correctly.|You peel the worst sack in the kitchen and don't cry once. The head cook calls it a personal best, and pays it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e277dd] = {一百个洋葱，你一滴眼泪都不剩了。大厨付了钱，又塞给你一块新鲜面包。|你削完了整袋。大厨数了洋葱，又数了你的工钱，都没数错。|你削完了厨房里最难对付的那袋，一滴眼泪都没掉。大厨说这是你的个人最好成绩，照价付钱。}\n# en: Peel, then help at the stove\nopt.right.label [f73412] = 削一半，再去灶台帮忙\n# en: {Half a sack and an hour at the stove. Less pay, but now you know why the soup tastes like home.|The head cook lets you stir. Somehow the stew tastes of your grandmother's. You get paid for the onions.|You peel half the worst sack and stir the rest of the morning. The head cook pays for the onions and teaches you a stew.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f6b173] = {半袋洋葱，加上灶台边的一个钟头。钱少些，但现在你知道这汤为什么有家的味道了。|大厨让你搅锅。不知怎么，炖菜有了你外婆的味道。你拿到了洋葱的工钱。|你削了那袋最难对付的一半，剩下的上午都在搅锅。大厨按洋葱付了钱，还教了你一道炖菜。}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_burnt_pan\n# en: The Burnt Pan\ntitle [ce0174] = 烧焦的锅\n# en: You turn away for a moment and the porridge for the stay-behinds catches. The smell is already halfway to the dining hall. The head cook hasn't noticed. Yet.\ntext [3467e5] = 你一转身，给留校的人煮的粥就糊了。焦味已经飘到半路的饭堂。大厨还没发现。暂时。\n# en: A smoking pan on a range, a student holding it at arm's length, a cook turning round with a ladle raised.\nscene [078326] = 灶台上一口冒烟的锅，一个学生伸直胳膊举着它，一个厨子举着汤勺转过身来。\n# en: Own up and start again\nopt.left.label [df7779] = 坦白，重新煮\n# en: {~The head cook sighs the sigh of thirty years in kitchens and docks you for the oats. You make it again, properly.|\"Everyone burns one,\" says the head cook. \"Once.\" You are paid, minus the oats.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d4b5ef] = {~大厨叹了一口三十年后厨的气，扣了你的燕麦钱。你重新煮了一锅，这次好好煮。|“谁都烧糊过一锅，”大厨说，“一次。”你拿到了工钱，扣掉燕麦。}\n# en: Scrape it and serve it\nopt.right.label [1bae79] = 刮一刮端出去\n# en: {~Nobody notices. The stay-behinds eat it and ask for more. You don't feel great about it.|A little smoky. \"Rustic,\" says someone. You get the full day's pay.}\nopt.right.say.1 [15e03b] = {~没人发现。留校的人吃了还要添。你心里不太舒服。|有点烟熏味。“很有乡土风味。”有人说。你拿到了全天的工钱。}\n# en: {~A professor sends it back with a note. The head cook reads it aloud, slowly, to the whole kitchen.|The smell gives you away. You're paid in full and in disapproval.}\nopt.right.say.2 [8635a7] = {~一位教授把粥退了回来，附了张字条。大厨当着整个后厨，一字一字慢慢念了出来。|焦味出卖了你。工钱一分不少，白眼也一个不少。}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_feast_night\n# en: Feast Night\ntitle [17ed66] = 宴会之夜\n# en: {season == \"winter\": Tonight the castle keeps a small Midwinter table for those who stayed, and the kitchens will cook as if for the whole school.|Tonight the stay-behinds hold a summer supper in the courtyard, and the kitchens will cook as if for the whole school.} At breakfast the head cook chalks up the rota: somebody carries trays, somebody plates.\ntext [2e15f4] = {season == \"winter\": 今晚城堡要为留下来的人摆一小桌仲冬宴，后厨会按全校的分量来做。|今晚留校的人要在庭院里办夏日晚餐，后厨会按全校的分量来做。}早饭时大厨在黑板上排了班：有人端托盘，有人装盘。\n# en: A steaming kitchen on a feast night, trays going out in a stream, a student in an apron grinning through the chaos.\nscene [53b738] = 宴会之夜热气腾腾的后厨，托盘一个接一个端出去，一个系着围裙的学生在混乱中咧嘴笑着。\n# en: Volunteer to carry trays\nopt.left.label [cb7924] = 主动去端托盘\n# en: {~You carry until your arms forget being arms. The head cook pays you double for the night, in coins and leftovers.|Up and down the stairs until midnight. Somebody toasts the kitchens and you hear it through the door.}\nopt.left.say.1 [aecfbc] = {~你端到胳膊都忘了自己是胳膊。大厨付了你这一晚的双份工钱，一份是钱，一份是剩菜。|楼上楼下一直跑到半夜。有人为后厨举杯，你隔着门都听见了。}\n# en: Ask to plate in the back\nopt.right.label [c0f09c] = 申请在后面装盘\n# en: {~You plate by the ovens and listen to the feast through the wall. Smaller pay, a scorched wrist, and the best seat in the house.|Quiet hands in a hot room. The head cook gives you the last slice of pie, which is its own wage.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cae555] = {~你在烤炉边装盘，隔着墙听宴会的热闹。钱少些，手腕烫了一下，却是全场最好的位置。|热屋子里一双安静的手。大厨把最后一块派给了你，这本身就是一份工钱。}\n\n@event gig_grounds_goat\n# en: Bartholomew Escapes\ntitle [ecc00d] = 巴塞洛缪越狱了\n# en: {Bartholomew the goat has got out again and is standing on the shed roof, eating the thatch.|Bartholomew is on the shed roof. He has made this a habit.|The goat is on the roof. You don't even ask how any more.} Maud is laughing too hard to help. {The thatch is going fast.|He has a cabbage in his mouth.|He looks at you like a duke.}\ntext [b83cf2] = {山羊巴塞洛缪又跑出来了，站在棚屋顶上啃茅草。|巴塞洛缪在棚屋顶上。他已经养成习惯了。|山羊在屋顶上。你都懒得问它是怎么上去的了。}莫德笑得太厉害，帮不上忙。{茅草少得飞快。|他嘴里叼着一颗卷心菜。|他看你的样子像个公爵。}\n# en: A goat standing triumphantly on the roof of a garden shed, a student with a rope below, an old woman laughing behind them.\nscene [10fe10] = 一只山羊得意洋洋地站在园中棚屋的屋顶上，下面一个学生拿着绳子，后面一位老妇人在笑。\n# en: Climb up after him\nopt.left.label [7a4523] = 爬上去追他\n# en: {You get him down after an hour and a fall. Maud pays you double for the entertainment, she says, and a little for the bruises.|Up the ladder, along the ridge, one hand on his horn. He comes down when he's ready, which is later than you are.|You climb up and bow to him. He considers it, then lets himself be led down like a duke leaving a ball. Maud pays you for the bow.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7362ea] = {你摔了一跤，花了一个钟头才把他弄下来。莫德说为了这场好戏付你双倍工钱，再加一点淤青的钱。|爬上梯子，沿着屋脊，一只手抓住他的角。他想下来的时候才下来，比你想的晚。|你爬上去，向他鞠了一躬。他考虑了一下，然后像离开舞会的公爵一样让你牵了下来。莫德为那一躬付了钱。}\n# en: Bribe him down with an apple\nopt.right.label [bc5cca] = 用苹果哄他下来\n# en: {One apple, held very still. He considers it for a long time, like a merchant, and comes down. Maud pays for the hour you saved.|He takes the apple and stays where he is. Then, at his own pace, he comes down. Maud pays you for patience.|He takes the apple like tribute and descends. Maud pays you, and says he'll expect two next time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6324d9] = {一个苹果，一动不动地举着。他像个商人一样考虑了很久，然后下来了。莫德为你省下的那个钟头付了钱。|他接过苹果，站着不动。然后，按他自己的节奏，下来了。莫德为你的耐心付了钱。|他像收贡品一样接过苹果，屈尊下来了。莫德付了钱，说他下回会要两个。}\n\n@event gig_kitchens_staff_supper\n# en: The Staff Supper\ntitle [b2c3f9] = 员工晚餐\n# en: {At the morning briefing the head cook announces a staff supper tonight, after the last service. The whole kitchen eats at the scrubbed table, on leftovers, bread and the head cook's opinions.|Staff supper again tonight. They're saving you a place now.|The head cook bangs a ladle at breakfast. \"Staff supper tonight. Sit, or serve.\"} {Somebody is already promising a story about a swan.|The pot-boy is asleep on his feet.|There's a cake cooling that nobody will admit to making.}\ntext [cebb18] = {晨会上大厨宣布，今晚最后一轮供餐结束后有员工晚餐。整个后厨围着擦洗干净的桌子一起吃，吃剩菜、面包，还有大厨的高见。|今晚又是员工晚餐。他们现在给你留了位置。|大厨在早饭时敲了敲长柄勺。“今晚员工晚餐。要么坐，要么端。”}{已经有人许诺要讲一个关于天鹅的故事。|洗锅的男孩站着就睡着了。|有个蛋糕正在晾着，没人承认是自己做的。}\n# en: Cooks and kitchen hands crowded round a scrubbed table late at night, eating the day's leftovers, one student serving.\nscene [7186d1] = 深夜里厨师和帮厨们挤在一张擦洗干净的桌子旁吃当天的剩菜，一个学生在上菜。\n# en: Serve, then wash up\nopt.left.label [c4f447] = 先上菜，再洗碗\n# en: {You finish the washing-up at midnight. The head cook pays you extra and calls you \"the useful one\".|You serve, wash up, and wake the pot-boy at midnight to send him to bed. The head cook pays you for both jobs.|You serve forty bowls, wash forty bowls, and are paid for the extra hour in coins and a slice of the mystery cake.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e47162] = {你午夜才洗完碗。大厨多付了你钱，叫你“有用的那个”。|你端菜、洗碗，半夜还叫醒了洗锅的男孩让他去睡觉。大厨两份活都付了钱。|你端了四十碗，洗了四十碗，多干的那个钟头的报酬是硬币和一块神秘蛋糕。}\n# en: Sit and eat with them\nopt.right.label [22c3ea] = 坐下和他们一起吃\n# en: {You eat, and laugh at the swan story, and leave earlier with a smaller purse and a full stomach.|You sit. The cooks tell you which professors send back their soup. It's worth more than the wages.|You sit. Nobody admits to the cake, so everyone eats it. You leave full and a little poorer.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ca6576] = {你吃着饭，听天鹅的故事笑出声，早早离开，钱袋小了些，肚子饱了。|你坐了下来。厨师们告诉你哪些教授会把汤退回来。这比工钱值钱。|你坐了下来。没人承认那个蛋糕是谁做的，于是大家一起吃了。你离开时肚子饱了，钱少了一点。}\n\n@event gig_library_stocktake\n# en: The Stocktake\ntitle [987da1] = 盘点\n# en: {The library in the holidays is all yours and all to be counted.|The stocktake ledger is half full; the shelves are not half counted.|You've stopped counting books and started counting floors.} {A shelf of atlases has migrated to the poetry section.|One book is not on any list and seems pleased about it.|The lamp in the east wing keeps going out, politely.}\ntext [9fdd33] = {假期里的图书馆全归你，也全等着你数。|盘点账簿填了一半；书架连一半都还没数完。|你已经不数书了，开始数楼层。}{一架地图集迁移到了诗歌区。|有本书不在任何清单上，似乎还挺得意。|东翼的灯老是礼貌地熄灭。}\n# en: A deserted library in the holidays, a student on a tall ladder with a ledger, books stacked on every table, dust in the lamplight.\nscene [d520da] = 假期里空无一人的图书馆，一个学生拿着账簿站在高梯上，每张桌子上都堆满了书，灯光里浮着灰尘。\n# en: Count the restricted shelves too\nopt.left.label [0c6c3e] = 连禁书架也一起数\n# en: {The restricted shelves count back. You finish with the right number and a bite on the thumb. The librarian dresses the bite and writes it in the ledger, under \"damages\".|You count every chained volume. One of them counts you. The pay is better than term-time's by a good margin.|You count the restricted floor by the lamp that keeps going out. The books count you in the dark. The pay is the best of the break.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f84570] = {禁书架会回数你。你数对了数目，拇指上挨了一口。总馆员给你包扎好伤口，把它记在账本的“损耗”一栏。|你数遍了每一本锁链书。其中一本也数了你。报酬比学期里高出一大截。|你借着那盏老是熄灭的灯数禁书那层。书在黑暗里数你。报酬是整个假期里最高的。}\n# en: Keep to the open stacks\nopt.right.label [fa9957] = 只数开放书库\n# en: {Open stacks only, every book counted, the ledger perfect. The librarian pays you in full and says nothing about the shelves you left.|A long, dusty, honest day. You go to bed counting.|You count the open floors and leave the east wing to its lamp. The librarian pays in full and asks no questions.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0c8ec2] = {只数开放书库，每本书都数了，账簿完美。总馆员全额付了钱，对你没数的书架只字不提。|漫长、满是灰尘、老老实实的一天。你躺在床上还在数。|你数完开放的楼层，把东翼留给那盏灯。总馆员全额付钱，什么也没问。}\n\n@event gig_library_lost_shelf\n# en: The Shelf That Wasn't There\ntitle [b74ee7] = 不存在的书架\n# en: {The stocktake finds a shelf that isn't in any ledger.|The extra shelf again. It has grown a book since yesterday.|The shelf is longer than the room it's in, now.} {The books on it have no titles.|One is warm.|One is labelled with the date of tomorrow.}\ntext [ad6982] = {盘点时发现了一个任何账簿上都没有的书架。|又是那个多出来的书架。从昨天起它又长出了一本书。|现在这个书架比它所在的房间还长。}{上面的书都没有书名。|有一本是温的。|有一本标着明天的日期。}\n# en: A narrow library aisle that seems to go on further than the building, a student holding a lamp up to an unfamiliar shelf.\nscene [6b7883] = 一条窄窄的图书馆过道，似乎比建筑本身还长，一个学生把灯举向一个陌生的书架。\n# en: Catalogue it\nopt.left.label [b5435f] = 把它编目\n# en: {You list every book. The librarian reads your list, goes pale and pays you to forget it. You don't.|You catalogue the shelf by lamplight. In the morning, it's gone, and your list is the only proof.|You catalogue the book labelled with tomorrow's date. Tomorrow, the label has changed to the day after. The librarian pays you and takes your list.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fd0152] = {你列出了每一本书。总馆员读了你的清单，脸色发白，付钱让你忘掉它。你没忘。|你借着灯光给书架编了目。第二天早上它不见了，你的清单是唯一的证据。|你把那本标着明天日期的书编了目。到了明天，标签变成了后天。总馆员付了钱，收走了你的清单。}\n# en: Leave it off the ledger\nopt.right.label [bee004] = 不记进账簿\n# en: {You don't write it down. Some shelves don't want to be counted. The librarian, passing, nods as if you'd passed a test.|You skip it. The ledger balances. The shelf, you'd swear, looks relieved.|You don't write down the book with tomorrow's date. Tomorrow, it isn't there. The librarian thanks you, oddly sincerely.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b9760e] = {你没有把它写下来。有些书架不想被数。总馆员经过时点了点头，好像你通过了一场考验。|你跳过了它。账簿平了。那个书架，你敢发誓，看起来松了口气。|你没记那本标着明天日期的书。到了明天，它不见了。总馆员向你道谢，出奇地真诚。}\n\n@event gig_library_last_day\n# en: The Ledger Balances\ntitle [0c78c2] = 账目平了\n# en: {The last page of the stocktake. The librarian reads it slowly, all of it.|Another stocktake done, another ledger signed.|The librarian signs, and blots, and signs again for luck.} {\"Not one missing,\" she says. \"That has never happened.\"|\"Three missing, and one extra. Normal.\"|She closes the ledger with both hands.}\ntext [462f2e] = {盘点的最后一页。总馆员慢慢地读，一字不漏。|又一次盘点结束，又一本账簿签了字。|总馆员签了字，吸干墨迹，为了好运又签了一次。}{“一本没少，”她说，“从来没有过。”|“少了三本，多了一本。正常。”|她用双手合上账簿。}\n# en: A head librarian signing a thick stocktake ledger at a desk under a window, a tired student waiting with ink on every finger.\nscene [41e847] = 窗下书桌前一位总馆员在厚厚的盘点账簿上签字，一个疲惫的学生在等着，每根手指上都沾着墨水。\n# en: Ask for a reference\nopt.left.label [d577d3] = 请她写封推荐信\n# en: {She writes one on the spot, three lines, and the third makes you blush. The pay is good and the reference is better.|She writes you a reference in a hand like engraving. It's worth more than the wages, someone tells you later.|She signs the ledger and then, without being asked, a reference. \"You'll want it,\" she says. You will.}\nopt.left.say.1 [63ffa9] = {她当场写了一封：三行字，第三行让你脸红。报酬不错，推荐信更好。|她用镌刻般的字迹给你写了推荐信。后来有人告诉你，它比工钱值钱。|她签完账本，没等你开口又签了一封推荐信。“你会用得上的，”她说。你会的。}\n# en: Ask for the afternoon off\nopt.right.label [25c4ff] = 请她放你一个下午的假\n# en: {She gives you the afternoon and a little less pay. You spend it asleep in the reading room, which is the best use of it.|An afternoon off, and a library to yourself. You read for pleasure for the first time in months.|She closes the ledger and gives you the afternoon before you ask. You spend it in the window seat, doing nothing whatsoever.}\nopt.right.say.1 [04e177] = {她给了你一个下午，工钱少了一点。你在阅览室里睡了一下午，这是最好的用法。|一个下午的假，一整座图书馆归你。几个月来你第一次为了乐趣读书。|你还没开口，她就合上账本，给了你一个下午。你在窗边座位上度过了它，什么也没干。}\n\n@event gig_newt_rush\n# en: The Holiday Rush\ntitle [c1bbea] = 节日高峰\n# en: {The landlady hands you the keys over breakfast. Tonight, she warns, the Newt will be full to the rafters with farmers, barge crews and families home for the season.|Another holiday night ahead, and the Newt is already promised to a full house.|By morning, half the valley has said it's coming to the Newt tonight.} {A wedding party has sent word.|The fiddler has promised a song with forty verses.|The firewood is stacked to the ceiling.}\ntext [26936d] = {早饭时老板娘把钥匙交给你。她提醒你，今晚蝾螈酒馆会挤满农夫、驳船船员和回家过节的一家家人。|又一个节日之夜要来了，蝾螈酒馆早已订满。|天一亮，半个山谷的人都说今晚要来蝾螈酒馆。}{一支婚礼队伍已经捎了话来。|提琴手答应要拉一首四十段的歌。|木柴一直堆到了天花板。}\n# en: A packed village tavern at the holidays, garlands over the bar, every table full, a student manager behind the bar with three jugs in hand.\nscene [c18223] = 节日里挤满人的村酒馆，吧台上挂着花环，每张桌子都坐满了，一个学生经理站在吧台后，手里拿着三个酒壶。\n# en: Keep the bar open till the last song\nopt.left.label [687db7] = 营业到最后一首歌\n# en: {You pour till the song ends, then pour for the encore. The till is the heaviest you've ever counted, and your feet will never forgive you.|Closing at three, the till heavy, the floor sticky. The landlady's note says \"Well done\" in two places.|Half the valley comes, and you keep pouring until the firewood runs low. The till is heavy, and so is your sleep.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fef2c7] = {你一直倒酒倒到歌唱完，又为返场接着倒。这是你数过最沉的钱柜，你的脚永远不会原谅你。|凌晨三点打烊，钱柜沉甸甸的，地板黏糊糊的。老板娘的字条上有两处写着“干得好”。|半个山谷的人都来了，你一直倒酒倒到柴火见底。钱柜很沉，你睡得也很沉。}\n# en: Close at the proper hour\nopt.right.label [d3eedf] = 按时打烊\n# en: {You call time at midnight, firmly and politely. Some grumble. The till is lighter, and you sleep.|Last orders on the stroke. The till loses some takings; you keep your temper.|You close on the stroke, half the valley or not. They go home singing, which counts as a good night.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c910f0] = {你在午夜坚定而礼貌地宣布打烊。有人抱怨。钱柜轻了一些，你睡了个好觉。|准点最后点单。钱柜少了些进账；你保住了好脾气。|不管半个山谷的人在不在，你准点打烊。他们唱着歌回了家，这也算是个好夜晚。}\n\n@event gig_newt_brawl\n# en: A Fight Over a Song\ntitle [08605c] = 为一首歌打起来\n# en: {Two barge crews disagree about the last verse of a song, loudly.|The barge crews again, and the song again, and the same verse.|This time it's a farmer and a fisherman, and the song is a different one.} {A chair is already in the air.|Somebody has taken their coat off.|The fiddler has stopped playing.}\ntext [f3af46] = {两拨驳船船员为一首歌的最后一段争得不可开交。|又是那两拨船员，又是那首歌，又是那一段。|这次是一个农夫和一个渔夫，歌也换了一首。}{已经有一把椅子飞在空中了。|有人脱了外套。|提琴手停了下来。}\n# en: Two barge crews squaring up in the middle of a crowded tavern, a student manager stepping between them with a tray.\nscene [768c1a] = 两拨驳船船员在拥挤的酒馆中央对峙，一个学生经理端着托盘挡在他们中间。\n# en: Step between them\nopt.left.label [f242e3] = 站到他们中间\n# en: {You stop the first punch with a tray and the second with a look. Both crews buy a round to make up. You take the tips.|You walk between them and stay there. Eventually somebody laughs, and the song goes on.|The fiddler stops; you start the next verse yourself, badly. Both sides correct you, together, and forget to fight.}\nopt.left.say.1 [39c3a7] = {你用托盘挡住了第一拳，用眼神挡住了第二拳。两拨人各请了一轮酒赔罪。小费归你。|你走到他们中间，站着不动。最后有人笑了，歌又唱了下去。|提琴手停了；你自己唱起了下一段，唱得很糟。两边一起纠正你，忘了打架。}\n# en: {The tray takes one punch and your jaw takes the other. They pay for the chair, at least.|You get a black eye and a free drink, which you can't drink, being on duty.|The fiddler stops, and so does the fight, just as you step in. Everyone laughs at your timing. You take the bruise for nothing.}\nopt.left.say.2 [00905e] = {托盘挨了一拳，你的下巴挨了另一拳。至少他们赔了椅子。|你得了一只黑眼圈和一杯免费的酒，可你在当班，不能喝。|你刚走进去，提琴手停了，架也停了。大家都笑你来得真是时候。你白挨了一下。}\n# en: Bribe them with a free round\nopt.right.label [63b266] = 请他们喝一轮\n# en: {One round on the house, and they're singing together by the second verse. The house is a little poorer.|A free round fixes most things. The landlady's ledger will notice.|A free round, and the fiddler starts again. The farmer and the fisherman sing a duet, and the landlady's ledger winces.}\nopt.right.say.1 [912a4e] = {店里请了一轮，到第二段他们就一起唱起来了。店里穷了一点。|一轮免费的酒能解决大多数问题。老板娘的账簿会注意到的。|一轮免费的酒，提琴手又拉了起来。农夫和渔夫唱起了二重唱，老板娘的账簿疼得一缩。}\n\n@event gig_newt_fire\n# en: The Fire Must Not Go Out\ntitle [bda4da] = 炉火不能灭\n# en: {The landlady's fire has never gone out in her lifetime or her mother's. She's away tonight, and this morning the wind is already in the chimney.|The landlady is away again tonight, and the fire is already low. The wind is from the north.|The fire flickered last night as you locked up, as if to test you. Tonight it's yours again.} {The wood is damp.|There's frost on the inside of the windows.|The cat is sitting on the warmest stone, unhelpfully.}\ntext [d3a8ff] = {老板娘的炉火在她这辈子和她母亲那辈子都没灭过。她今晚不在，而今早风已经灌进了烟囱。|老板娘今晚又不在，火已经弱了。刮的是北风。|昨晚你锁门时火苗闪了闪，像是在考验你。今晚它又归你管了。}{木柴是潮的。|窗户里面结了霜。|猫坐在最暖的石头上，一点也不帮忙。}\n# en: The great hearth of a village tavern late at night, embers banked low, a student kneeling with tongs and a bucket of ash.\nscene [95b956] = 深夜里村酒馆的大壁炉，余烬低低地封着，一个学生拿着火钳和一桶炉灰跪在旁边。\n# en: Sit up with it all night\nopt.left.label [18ab48] = 守它一整夜\n# en: {You feed it one stick at a time until dawn. When the landlady comes back, she puts a hand on the stones and nods at you without a word.|You sleep in the chair with the tongs in your lap. The fire lives. So, nearly, do you.|You sit up with it, cat on the warmest stone. At dawn it's still burning, and the cat has not moved once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [00b64c] = {你一根一根地添柴，一直到天亮。老板娘回来后，把手放在炉石上，一言不发地朝你点了点头。|你抱着火钳在椅子上睡着了。火活了下来。你也差不多。|你守着火，猫占着最暖的那块石头。天亮时火还烧着，那只猫一次都没挪窝。}\n# en: Bank it and trust it\nopt.right.label [fc6608] = 封好火，相信它\n# en: {You bank it the way she showed you and go to bed. You dream of ash. In the morning, it's alive, and you're shaking.|You trust it. It holds. You don't sleep a wink anyway.|You bank it and move the cat. The cat moves back. In the morning the fire's alive, and the cat is very warm.}\nopt.right.say.1 [df4e6b] = {你按她教的方法封好火，上床睡觉。你梦见了灰烬。早上它还活着，你却在发抖。|你相信了它。它撑住了。你还是一眼都没合上。|你封好火，把猫挪开。猫又挪了回来。早上火还活着，猫暖和极了。}\n\n@event gig_pell_remedy_rush\n# en: Cough Season\ntitle [efc796] = 咳嗽季\n# en: {The whole valley is coughing, and Pell's queue is out of the door.|Day three of the cough. Pell is humming. You are not.|The queue at Pell's has its own gossip by now.} {A carter wants the cherry one.|A farmer wants something for his cow.|A professor in disguise wants something \"for a friend\".}\ntext [e7e4fe] = {整个山谷都在咳嗽，佩尔铺子的队伍排出了门外。|咳嗽的第三天。佩尔在哼歌。你没有。|佩尔铺子前的队伍已经有了自己的八卦。}{一个赶车人要樱桃味的。|一个农夫想给他的牛要点药。|一位乔装的教授要点药“给一个朋友”。}\n# en: A crowded village apothecary in winter, a queue of coughing villagers, a student and an old man filling bottles as fast as they can.\nscene [390033] = 冬天里拥挤的村药铺，一队咳嗽的村民，一个学生和一位老人飞快地灌着药瓶。\n# en: Mix to order, carefully\nopt.left.label [101dc9] = 按需调配，仔细些\n# en: {Every bottle mixed for the person in front of you. It's slow, and Pell pays you for every one of them.|You learn sixty coughs by ear. Pell says you're wasted on students.|You mix the disguised professor's remedy \"for a friend\" with great discretion. Pell charges double for the discretion and gives you half.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bb839b] = {每一瓶都为眼前的人单独调配。很慢，佩尔为每一瓶都付了钱。|你听声音就能分辨六十种咳嗽了。佩尔说你当学生真是可惜了。|你极其谨慎地为那位乔装的教授调配了“给朋友”的药。佩尔为这份谨慎收了双倍，分给你一半。}\n# en: Mix in big batches\nopt.right.label [14ba6e] = 大批量调配\n# en: {One cauldron, one recipe, three hundred bottles. It works for nearly everyone. Pell pays you and looks at the cauldron for a long time.|Fast and efficient. A farmer comes back the next day about the cow.|The professor in disguise takes a bottle from the batch and looks offended to be treated like everyone else.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0901fc] = {一口大锅，一个配方，三百瓶。对几乎所有人都管用。佩尔付了钱，盯着那口锅看了很久。|又快又省事。第二天，那个农夫为了牛的事又来了。|那位乔装的教授从大批货里拿了一瓶，被当作普通人对待，一脸不悦。}\n\n@event gig_pell_night_knock\n# en: A Knock After Closing\ntitle [55f4f5] = 打烊后的敲门声\n# en: {Pell asks you over breakfast to sleep above the shop tonight. There's a fever about, and the knocks come after closing.|Another night above the shop, Pell asks. The knocks keep coming after dark.|You're sleeping above the shop again tonight. You've stopped bothering to unlace your boots.} {Pell sleeps like a stone.|The fire goes out at ten.|The ledger says not to open after dark.}\ntext [40913e] = {早饭时佩尔请你今晚睡在铺子楼上。外面在闹热病，打烊后总有人来敲门。|佩尔又请你在铺子楼上过一夜。天黑后敲门声一直没断过。|今晚你又睡在铺子楼上。你已经懒得解鞋带了。}{佩尔睡得像块石头。|炉火十点就灭了。|账簿上写着天黑后不开门。}\n# en: An apothecary's door at night, a lantern held up to a frightened villager in the doorway, a student in a nightcap behind the counter.\nscene [742c58] = 夜里药铺的门口，一盏灯照着门口一个惊慌的村民，柜台后面一个戴睡帽的学生。\n# en: Mix any remedy yourself\nopt.left.label [a8590a] = 有人来就自己调药\n# en: {The knock comes at midnight. A shepherd, his wife burning with fever. You mix the remedy by candlelight from memory. She's better by morning; he pays in eggs and coins, and Pell pays you for the night.|The remedy is right. Pell, in the morning, checks your work and says nothing, which from him is a medal.|You sleep in your boots and mix the remedy before you're properly awake. It's right. Pell finds the mortar warm in the morning, and nods.}\nopt.left.say.1 [656516] = {半夜敲门声响了：一个牧羊人，他妻子烧得滚烫。你凭记忆在烛光下调好了药。到早上她就好多了；他用鸡蛋和硬币付了账，佩尔为这一夜付了你钱。|药是对的。佩尔早上检查了你的成果，什么也没说，这对他来说就是一枚奖章。|你穿着靴子睡，还没完全醒就把药调好了。是对的。佩尔早上发现研钵还是热的，点了点头。}\n# en: {The first batch is wrong. You wake Pell, who fixes it in a minute and pays you anyway, for trying.|You get it right on the third try, at dawn, and you shake all the next day.|You mix it half asleep and half right. The knock comes back an hour later, and Pell comes down.}\nopt.left.say.2 [1e47f9] = {第一批调错了。你叫醒佩尔，他一分钟就改好了，还是为你的尝试付了钱。|你试到第三次，天亮时才调对，第二天一整天都在发抖。|你半睡半醒地调了药，对了一半。一个钟头后敲门声又来了，佩尔下了楼。}\n# en: Wake Pell for anything\nopt.right.label [e9f587] = 有人来就叫醒佩尔\n# en: {When the knock comes, Pell comes down grumbling, mixes it in two minutes and goes back to bed. You learn more watching than mixing.|You wake him. He's not cross. \"That's what I'm for,\" he says.|You wake Pell, who's already pulling his boots on. He's started sleeping in them too.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3e8f55] = {敲门声一响，佩尔就嘟囔着下楼，两分钟调好药，又回去睡了。你看着学到的比自己调学到的还多。|你叫醒了他。他没生气。“这就是我该干的，”他说。|你叫醒佩尔，他已经在穿靴子了。他也开始穿着靴子睡了。}\n\n@event gig_pell_ledger\n# en: Pell's Books\ntitle [b8833a] = 佩尔的账\n# en: {Pell's account books have not been balanced since his daughters left home.|The account books again. Pell has found another drawer of receipts.|You're nearly at the bottom of the receipts. Pell is nearly at the bottom of his patience.} {Half the debts are owed to him.|Half the debts are owed by him.|Some debts are in a currency that no longer exists.}\ntext [9e9f3d] = {自从女儿们离家后，佩尔的账就再没平过。|又是那些账本。佩尔又找到了一抽屉收据。|收据快理到底了。佩尔的耐心也快到底了。}{一半的账是别人欠他的。|一半的账是他欠别人的。|有些账用的是早已不存在的货币。}\n# en: A cluttered apothecary back room, an old man and a student bent over a ledger full of crossings-out, jars all around them.\nscene [982dfe] = 杂乱的药铺后屋，一位老人和一个学生埋头看着一本涂改满页的账簿，四周都是药罐。\n# en: Balance them properly\nopt.left.label [39ae80] = 把账理清\n# en: {Three days of receipts. At the end, Pell is owed more than he thought. He pays you out of it, generously.|You balance every page. Pell signs it as if it were a treaty.|You convert the vanished currency at an old rate you find in a drawer. Pell is owed a goat, a few crowns and an apology. He pays you from the crowns.}\nopt.left.say.1 [abf9c5] = {三天的收据。理完后，别人欠佩尔的比他以为的多。他从中拿出一笔，大方地付给你。|你把每一页都理平了。佩尔签字的样子像在签条约。|你在抽屉里找到一个旧汇率，把那种早已不存在的货币换算了出来。有人欠佩尔一只山羊、几个克朗和一句道歉。他从那几个克朗里付了你。}\n# en: Forgive the small debts\nopt.right.label [48c657] = 免掉小额欠款\n# en: {You strike out every small debt owed to Pell, everything under a crown. Pell reads the list and nods. \"They'd never have paid,\" he says. \"Now they'll come back.\"|Small debts forgiven, the book is simpler and the village is kinder to Pell. The pay is a little less.|You forgive the debts in the vanished currency, since nobody could pay them anyway. Pell laughs for the first time in a week.}\nopt.right.say.1 [38451e] = {你把别人欠佩尔的小额欠款，凡是不到一克朗的，全划掉了。佩尔读了清单，点点头。“反正他们也不会还，”他说，“现在他们会回来了。”|小额欠款免了，账簿简单了，村里人对佩尔也更亲切了。报酬少了一点。|你把那些用已不存在的货币欠下的账都免了，反正也没人还得了。佩尔一星期来头一次笑了。}\n\n@event gig_post_storm_run\n# en: The Storm Run\ntitle [a96ce0] = 风暴航线\n# en: {The holiday post doesn't wait for weather. Tonight's route crosses the ridge in a storm.|The ridge again, and the wind again.|You've flown this route so often the farm dogs know your broom.} {The sack is heavier than the broom likes.|One parcel is labelled \"fragile\" in four languages.|The light at the last farm is always on for you.}\ntext [c327a9] = {节日邮件不等天气。今晚的路线要在风暴中翻过山脊。|又是山脊，又是大风。|这条路线你飞了太多遍，连农场的狗都认得你的飞帚。}{邮袋比飞帚喜欢的要重。|有个包裹用四种语言写着“易碎”。|最后一座农场的灯总是为你亮着。}\n# en: A courier on a broom flying low over snowy fields in a storm, a sack of parcels strapped behind them, a farmhouse light ahead.\nscene [6d3bf8] = 一个信使骑着飞帚在风暴中低低掠过雪地，身后绑着一袋包裹，前方是农舍的灯光。\n# en: Fly straight over the ridge\nopt.left.label [12b64e] = 直接飞越山脊\n# en: {Over the ridge and down to the farms in half the time. The bonus for early delivery is very good.|The wind does its worst and you do better. Every parcel delivered, dry.|You fly the ridge with the farm dogs barking you home. The light at the last farm is on, and the bonus is too.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1a587e] = {翻过山脊，用一半的时间飞到农场。提前送达的奖金非常丰厚。|风使出了浑身解数，你做得更好。每个包裹都送到了，干干的。|你翻过山脊，农场的狗一路叫着送你回家。最后那座农场的灯亮着，奖金也到手了。}\n# en: {The wind takes you into a hedge. The parcels survive. You are less sure about yourself.|You deliver everything, late and frozen, with a sprained wrist.|The storm hides the light at the last farm. You find it by the dogs, an hour late and very wet.}\nopt.left.say.2 [ed2797] = {风把你卷进了树篱。包裹们幸存了下来。你对自己就没那么有把握了。|你把所有东西都送到了，晚了，冻僵了，还扭伤了手腕。|暴风雨遮住了最后那座农场的灯。你循着狗叫声找到了它，晚了一个钟头，浑身湿透。}\n# en: Take the valley road\nopt.right.label [e98ab2] = 走山谷那条路\n# en: {The long way round, low and slow. Late, but every parcel's in one piece, and so are you.|The valley road adds two hours and no danger. The pay is the pay.|You take the valley road. The light at the last farm is still on when you get there, late. Somebody waited.}\nopt.right.say.1 [697a3e] = {绕远路，飞得又低又慢。晚了，但每个包裹都完好无损，你也一样。|山谷那条路多花两个钟头，没有危险。报酬就是报酬。|你走山谷那条路。你迟到时，最后那座农场的灯还亮着。有人一直在等。}\n\n@event gig_post_wrong_parcel\n# en: The Wrong Parcel\ntitle [3a22ae] = 送错的包裹\n# en: {A parcel you delivered yesterday has come back. The old woman says it isn't hers, and it's ticking.|Another wrong address. The holiday post is chaos.|The labels are getting worse. One just says \"Aunt\".} {The ticking is getting faster.|It smells of oranges.|It's addressed to someone who died years ago.}\ntext [b6a56d] = {你昨天送的一个包裹被退回来了。那位老太太说不是她的，而且它在滴答作响。|又一个错地址。节日邮件一团糟。|标签越来越离谱。有一张只写着“姨妈”。}{滴答声越来越快。|它闻起来有橙子味。|收件人好几年前就去世了。}\n# en: A farmhouse doorway in the snow, an old woman holding up a parcel that is clearly ticking, a courier on the step looking worried.\nscene [3247cb] = 雪中农舍的门口，一位老太太举着一个明显在滴答作响的包裹，台阶上的信使一脸担忧。\n# en: Find the right house tonight\nopt.left.label [f52daa] = 今晚就找到对的人家\n# en: {You fly the valley till midnight and find the right house. The ticking was a clock, a present for a grandfather. He gives you a slice of cake and a coin.|You find the owner three farms over. The bonus for \"lost and found\" is surprisingly good.|The person it's addressed to died years ago. You find their granddaughter instead, who opens it in the doorway and laughs and cries at once. The bonus is good.}\nopt.left.say.1 [64cc35] = {你在山谷里飞到半夜，找到了对的人家。滴答声原来是一只钟，送给一位祖父的礼物。他给了你一块蛋糕和一枚硬币。|你在三座农场外找到了主人。“失而复得”的奖金出奇地丰厚。|收件人好多年前就去世了。你转而找到了他们的孙女，她在门口拆开包裹，又笑又哭。奖金很丰厚。}\n# en: Return it to the loft\nopt.right.label [f4d50d] = 退回阁楼\n# en: {You take it back to the loft. Someone else will fly it tomorrow, and get the bonus.|The loft-master sighs and puts it on the shelf of the lost. It ticks there all week.|You take it back. The loft-master reads the name, goes quiet, and says he'll deliver this one himself.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6386e8] = {你把它带回阁楼。明天会有别人送，奖金也归别人。|阁楼主管叹了口气，把它放上失物架。它在那儿滴答了一整个星期。|你把它带了回去。阁楼主管读了那个名字，沉默了，说这一个他亲自去送。}\n\n@event gig_post_last_parcel\n# en: The Last Parcel\ntitle [364d35] = 最后一个包裹\n# en: {The last parcel of the day is small, and addressed in a shaky hand to a cottage on the far moor.|One parcel left again. Always one.|The last parcel, and the last daylight.} {It's light as a feather.|It's warm.|It rattles like a box of buttons.}\ntext [897117] = {今天的最后一个包裹很小，寄往远处荒原上的一座小屋，字迹颤抖。|又只剩一个包裹了。总是剩一个。|最后一个包裹，最后一缕天光。}{它轻得像一根羽毛。|它是温的。|它摇起来像一盒纽扣。}\n# en: An empty courier loft at the end of a holiday shift, one small parcel left on the shelf, addressed in shaky handwriting.\nscene [f9a7c2] = 节日班次结束时空荡荡的信使阁楼，架子上只剩一个小包裹，地址写得歪歪扭扭。\n# en: Fly it out now\nopt.left.label [9bacc3] = 现在就送出去\n# en: {The cottage is an hour into the dark. An old man opens the door and holds the parcel as if it were alive. \"From my son,\" he says. The pay feels like the least of it.|You deliver it at dusk to a woman who cries. You don't ask why. You fly home slowly.|It rattles all the way across the moor. Buttons, the woman says at the door, from her mother's coat. She gives you one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [df55a8] = {那座小屋在一个钟头的夜路之外。一位老人开了门，捧着包裹，好像它是活的。“我儿子寄的，”他说。报酬倒显得最不重要了。|黄昏时你把它交给一个女人，她哭了。你没问为什么。你慢慢地飞回家。|它一路叮当响着穿过荒原。门口的女人说那是纽扣，是她母亲大衣上的。她送了你一颗。}\n# en: Leave it for the morning\nopt.right.label [f26d71] = 留到明天早上\n# en: {It waits on the shelf all night and you think about it all night. You fly it at dawn.|In the morning you deliver it, a day late. Nobody minds but you.|You leave it till morning, last light being gone. It rattles on the shelf all night, as if impatient.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2ad8c9] = {它在架子上等了一整夜，你也想了它一整夜。天一亮你就送去了。|早上你把它送到了，晚了一天。除了你，没人在意。|最后一点天光已经没了，你把它留到了早上。它在架子上叮当响了一整夜，像是等不及了。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/plans_more.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/plans_more.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event break_castle_holiday_repairs\n# en: Holiday Repairs\ntitle [d8f001] = 假期修缮\n# en: {The caretaker has a list of jobs that can only be done when the castle is empty, and an eye for idle students.|The caretaker's list is out again. Your name is pencilled in at the bottom, provisionally.|The caretaker hands you the list and a mug of tea, in that order.} {season == \"winter\": Today it's snow on the battlements, heavy enough to worry the roof leads.|Today it's the dormitory mattresses, which must be carried out and beaten in the sun.}\ntext [74f49c] = {看门人有一张只能趁城堡空着时才能干的活儿清单，还有一双专盯闲人的眼睛。|看门人的清单又拿出来了。你的名字用铅笔写在最底下，暂定。|看门人递给你清单和一杯茶，顺序如上。} {season == \"winter\": 今天是城垛上的积雪，重得叫屋顶的铅皮都发愁。|今天是宿舍的床垫，得一张张搬出去，在太阳底下拍打。}\n# en: A caretaker with a long list on a clipboard in an empty castle corridor, a student trying to look busy nearby.\nscene [b4f7a1] = 空荡荡的城堡走廊里，看门人拿着夹板上的一长串清单，旁边一个学生努力装出很忙的样子。\n# en: Lend a hand\nopt.left.label [d5e12c] = 搭把手\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {You shovel snow off the battlements until you can't feel your ears. The view is worth one of them.|You and the caretaker clear a whole parapet in silence. At the end you're handed a biscuit, which is how the caretaker says thank you.|You drink the tea, then shovel the battlements till noon. The caretaker ticks you off the list, which feels like a medal.}|{You beat forty mattresses. Two of them contained socks, one a love letter, and one a very surprised mouse.|You carry mattresses down six flights of moving stairs. One flight moves halfway through, with you and the mattress on it.|Tea first, then mattresses until the sun goes down. The caretaker ticks you off the list with a flourish.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [b3529e] = {season == \"winter\": {你在城垛上铲雪，铲到耳朵没了知觉。这景色值其中一只耳朵。|你和看门人一声不吭地清完了一整段城墙。最后你得到一块饼干，这是看门人说谢谢的方式。|你喝完茶，在城垛上铲雪铲到中午。看门人在清单上把你勾掉了，感觉像得了一枚勋章。}|{你拍了四十张床垫。其中两张里有袜子，一张里有情书，还有一张里有一只非常吃惊的老鼠。|你扛着床垫下了六段会动的楼梯。有一段走到一半动了，连你带床垫一起。|先喝茶，然后拍床垫拍到太阳下山。看门人郑重其事地在清单上把你勾掉了。}}\n# en: Make myself scarce\nopt.right.label [8a06da] = 我先溜了\n# en: {You remember an urgent appointment with a book and vanish round a corner. The caretaker's sigh follows you a long way.|You hide in a window seat until the list has moved on to someone else.|You drink the tea, hand back the list and disappear before the mug is cold.}\nopt.right.say.1 [20a132] = {你想起一本书有急事等你，拐个弯就不见了。看门人的叹气声追了你好远。|你躲在窗台座里，直到清单换了别人。|你喝完茶，把清单还回去，杯子还没凉人就没影了。}\n# en: {The caretaker finds you behind a tapestry and hands you a shovel without comment.|You get as far as the stairs, which choose that moment to move you straight back to the caretaker.|You finish the tea and try to slip off. The caretaker taps the list, and you work down it until supper.}\nopt.right.say.2 [4a8e69] = {看门人在挂毯后面找到了你，一言不发地递来一把铲子。|你刚跑到楼梯口，楼梯就挑了这个时候，把你直接送回看门人跟前。|你喝完茶想溜。看门人敲了敲清单，你照着它一直干到晚饭。}\n\n@event break_castle_flight_field\n# en: The Field to Yourself\ntitle [4a919a] = 独享飞行场\n# en: {With the flying lessons over for the break, the flight field is empty and nobody has locked the broom shed.|The broom shed is unlocked again. You suspect it's on purpose now.|The flight field in the break. You know which broom in the shed pulls left.} {season == \"winter\": The hoops are rimed with frost and the wind has teeth.|The air shimmers with heat, and the wind is lazy for once.} {A crow is using the far hoop as a perch.|Somebody's lost scarf is snagged on the near hoop.|The grass is printed with a fox's tracks.}\ntext [f1561b] = {假期飞行课停了，飞行场空无一人，也没人锁扫帚棚。|扫帚棚又没锁。你现在怀疑是故意的。|假期的飞行场。你知道棚里哪把扫帚往左偏。} {season == \"winter\": 圆环上结着白霜，风像长了牙。|空气热得发颤，风难得懒洋洋的。} {一只乌鸦把远处的圆环当成了栖木。|近处的圆环上挂着不知谁丢的围巾。|草地上印着一串狐狸的脚印。}\n# en: An empty flight field in the holidays, hoops at either end, a single student on a broom high above the grass.\nscene [2e2a95] = 假期里空荡荡的飞行场，两端立着圆环，一个学生骑着扫帚高高掠过草地。\n# en: Fly the hoops, fast\nopt.left.label [91ec70] = 高速穿环\n# en: {You thread every hoop and pull up so late that the grass brushes your boots. Nobody saw. That's almost a shame.|You lap the field until the hoops blur. Landing, you find you're grinning like a first-year.|You take the broom that pulls left and fly the hoops leaning right. Below you, the fox's tracks blur into one long line.}\nopt.left.say.1 [27c099] = {你穿过每一个圆环，拉起得那么晚，靴子都擦到了草尖。没人看见。这几乎有点可惜。|你绕场飞到圆环都模糊了。落地时，你发现自己笑得像个一年级生。|你骑上那把往左偏的扫帚，身子往右压着穿圆环。在你身下，狐狸的脚印模糊成了一条长线。}\n# en: {The wind cheats, as it always does, and you meet the far hoop with your shoulder.|You land in the long grass, which has caught a great many students before you. It does not catch gently.|You forget which broom pulls left until the far hoop reminds you. You land in the fox's tracks, hard.}\nopt.left.say.2 [439eb0] = {风照例耍赖，你的肩膀撞上了远处的圆环。|你落进了长草丛，它接住过许多学生，但从不温柔。|你忘了哪把扫帚往左偏，直到远处的圆环提醒了你。你重重地落在狐狸的脚印上。}\n# en: Practise slow, careful turns\nopt.right.label [5ede57] = 慢慢练转弯\n# en: {Left, right, left, at walking pace, for an hour. It is deeply dull and, annoyingly, it works.|You practise the turn that always throws you until it doesn't. Your arms ache all evening.|You turn against the broom that pulls left until it gives up and flies straight. You land beside the fox's tracks, pleased with you both.}\nopt.right.say.1 [80c865] = {左、右、左，步行的速度，整整一个钟头。无聊透顶，而且，可气的是，真有用。|你一遍遍练那个总把你甩下去的转弯，直到它不再甩你。整个傍晚胳膊都在酸。|你一直跟那把往左偏的扫帚较劲，直到它认输，飞直了。你在狐狸的脚印旁落地，对你们俩都很满意。}\n\n@event break_castle_bored_stairs\n# en: The Bored Staircases\ntitle [1e7930] = 无聊的楼梯\n# en: {With nobody to carry up and down, the castle's staircases have grown restless.|The staircases are bored again, and they've noticed you.|The stairs know you're one of the few left. One swings over the moment you reach the landing.} {season == \"winter\": The stairwell is cold enough to see your breath.|Warm air drifts up the stairwell with the smell of cut grass.} {A flight is drifting past with a lost shoe on its top step.|Two staircases are sulking against the wall.|One flight keeps nudging the landing, like a dog with a stick.}\ntext [0534b0] = {没人可载上载下，城堡的楼梯开始坐不住了。|楼梯又无聊了，而且它们注意到了你。|楼梯知道你是少数留下的人之一。你一走到平台，就有一段荡了过来。} {season == \"winter\": 楼梯井冷得能看见自己的呼吸。|暖风带着刚割过的青草味飘上楼梯井。} {一段楼梯飘过，最上面一级放着一只丢了的鞋。|两段楼梯靠着墙在赌气。|有一段不停地拱着平台，像叼着木棍的狗。}\n# en: A grand castle stairwell with several staircases swinging slowly through the air, a lone student waiting on a landing.\nscene [491257] = 宏伟的城堡楼梯井里，几段楼梯在半空中缓缓摇荡，一个学生独自在平台上等着。\n# en: Ride whichever one comes\nopt.left.label [b32bfb] = 来哪段坐哪段\n# en: {It carries you to a corridor you've never seen, full of portraits who are thrilled to have a visitor.|It takes you the long way, through three towers, and sets you down exactly where you wanted to be.|The eager flight carries you off like a dog with a stick, the long way round, and brings you back very pleased with itself. It was a good ride.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fb796f] = {它把你送到一条你从没见过的走廊，满墙的肖像见有人来，高兴坏了。|它绕了远路，穿过三座塔，最后恰好把你放在你想去的地方。|那段热心的楼梯像叼着树枝的狗一样把你带走，绕了一大圈，又得意扬扬地把你送了回来。这一趟坐得不错。}\n# en: {It drops you on a landing in the east wing and refuses to come back. You walk for an hour.|It swings out over the stairwell and stops. You wait there, cramped and cold, with nothing to read, until it gets bored again.|The eager flight carries you off like a dog with a stick and drops you somewhere it thinks you'll like. You don't. It takes an hour to walk back.}\nopt.left.say.2 [757ffc] = {它把你丢在东翼的一个平台上，死活不肯回来。你走了一个钟头。|它荡到楼梯井半空就停住了。你在那儿干等，又冷又憋屈，手边什么书也没有，直到它又觉得无聊。|那段热心的楼梯像叼着树枝的狗一样把你带走，丢在一个它以为你会喜欢的地方。你并不喜欢。走回来花了一个钟头。}\n# en: Take the long way, on foot\nopt.right.label [a78eb5] = 绕远路，走过去\n# en: {You take the servants' stair, all two hundred steps of it. The staircases watch you go, disappointed.|You walk round by the bridges. It takes all morning, and your legs will remember it.|You walk past the eager flight. It follows you the length of the stairwell, nudging the banister, and you don't give in.}\nopt.right.say.1 [17addc] = {你走仆人楼梯，整整两百级。楼梯们目送你离开，一脸失望。|你从廊桥绕过去，花了一整个上午，腿会记住这一趟的。|你从那段热心的楼梯旁走过。它跟着你走完整个楼梯井，一下下碰着扶手，你没有让步。}\n\n@event break_castle_night_rounds\n# en: The Lone Prefect\ntitle [5e697b] = 孤独的级长\n# en: {Only one prefect has stayed for the break, and at breakfast they sigh that seven towers are a lot to patrol tonight.|The lone prefect is pinning tonight's rounds to the noticeboard again.|You know the lone prefect's rounds by now, to the quarter-hour, and tonight's are on the noticeboard.} {season == \"winter\": The corridors are cold enough to frost the portraits.|The nights are warm, and every window in the castle stands open after dark.} {A staircase shifts somewhere below with a long stone sigh.|An owl has got into the east wing.|Somewhere a door bangs in a draught.}\ntext [f2b041] = {假期只有一位级长留了下来，早饭时级长叹气说，今晚七座塔可不好巡。|那位级长又在布告栏上钉今晚的巡夜路线了。|你现在对那位级长的巡夜路线了如指掌，精确到一刻钟，今晚的路线就贴在布告栏上。} {season == \"winter\": 走廊冷得连肖像都结了霜。|夜里很暖，天黑后城堡的每扇窗都开着。} {楼下某处，一段楼梯长长地叹了口石头气，挪了位置。|一只猫头鹰钻进了东翼。|某处一扇门被穿堂风吹得砰砰响。}\n# en: A single lantern bobbing along a bridge between dark towers at night, a student watching it from a window.\nscene [1d3f26] = 夜里，一盏灯笼沿着两座黑塔之间的廊桥晃动，一个学生在窗边看着它。\n# en: Wander the towers tonight\nopt.left.label [bb3a36] = 今晚在塔里闲逛\n# en: {You have the castle to yourself for an hour. Moonlit bridges, sleeping portraits, the whole lake laid out below.|You slip past the lantern twice and watch the lake from a window nobody uses. It feels like owning the place.|You know the rounds to the quarter-hour, and you're never where the lantern is. The banging door covers your footsteps all the way to the roof.}\nopt.left.say.1 [168eae] = {整整一个钟头，城堡归你一个人：月光下的廊桥，熟睡的肖像，脚下铺开的整片湖。|你两次溜过灯笼，在一扇没人用的窗前看湖。感觉像这地方归你所有。|你对巡夜路线熟到一刻钟不差，灯笼在哪儿你就不在哪儿。那扇砰砰响的门一路替你盖住脚步声，直到屋顶。}\n# en: {The lantern swings round and finds you. The prefect writes your name in the demerit ledger with visible relief at having something to write.|You walk straight into the prefect on a dark landing. You both scream, and then you get a demerit.|The prefect changed the rounds after pinning them up. The lantern is exactly where you are, and a moment later so is your name, in the ledger.}\nopt.left.say.2 [bc0149] = {灯笼一转，照住了你。级长把你的名字记进记过簿，看得出总算有东西可记，松了口气。|你在漆黑的平台上一头撞上级长。你们俩都尖叫了，然后你被记了一过。|级长贴出路线后又改了。灯笼正好照在你身上，片刻之后，你的名字也进了记过簿。}\n# en: Offer to walk the rounds\nopt.right.label [37694d] = 主动陪着巡夜\n# en: {The prefect is glad of the company and tells you which portraits snore. You go to bed very late.|You carry the lantern while the prefect checks doors. Somewhere in the fourth tower you become friends, a little.|You walk the rounds you already know by heart. The prefect is impressed, then suspicious, and you shut the banging door together.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e4999f] = {级长很高兴有人作伴，告诉你哪些肖像会打呼。你很晚才睡。|级长查门，你提着灯笼。走到第四座塔的时候，你们成了朋友，一点点。|你陪着走那条早已烂熟于心的路线。级长先是佩服，然后起了疑心，你们一起关上了那扇砰砰响的门。}\n\n@event break_home_family_recipe\n# en: The Family Recipe\ntitle [3ea562] = 家传菜谱\n# en: {Someone at home has decided you're old enough for the family recipe, and ties an apron on you before breakfast.|The recipe lessons resume. You're trusted with the knife now, if not the salt.|You know the first half of the recipe by heart. The second half is still guarded like a treaty.} {season == \"winter\": It's the stew for the cold months, which takes all day.|It's the summer preserve, and every fruit in the house is involved.} {Nobody measures anything.|A pinch turns out to mean a handful.|The pot is older than you are.}\ntext [a7b002] = {家里有人认定你够大了，可以学家传菜谱了，早饭前就给你系上了围裙。|菜谱课继续。如今刀可以交给你了，盐还不行。|菜谱的前半你已经烂熟于心。后半还像条约一样严防死守。} {season == \"winter\": 是冬天的炖菜，要炖一整天。|是夏天的果酱，家里每一颗果子都得出力。} {谁也不称分量。|“一小撮”原来是一大把。|这口锅比你年纪还大。}\n# en: A warm family kitchen, an older relative guiding a student's hands over a steaming pot, jars and bowls everywhere.\nscene [7fcbc3] = 温暖的家中厨房，一位长辈握着学生的手在冒热气的锅上方指点，四处摆满罐子和碗。\n# en: Write it all down\nopt.left.label [c13862] = 全都记下来\n# en: {You take notes like it's a potions practical. You're corrected on every line, and then on the corrections.|Your notes are thorough, exact and, you're told, missing the point.|You write down the first half. When the second half begins, your notebook is gently confiscated.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9e0334] = {你像上魔药实践课一样做笔记。每一行都被纠正，然后纠正又被纠正。|你的笔记详尽、精确，而且据说完全没抓住要点。|你记下了前一半。后一半刚开始，你的本子就被客客气气地没收了。}\n# en: Learn it by hand, the old way\nopt.right.label [990459] = 用手学，老办法\n# en: {You burn a finger and get a nod. In this kitchen, that's a diploma.|You get it wrong in a way that turns out to be how your grandmother made it. Everyone goes quiet, and then they eat it all.|Your hands learn a step of the second half before anyone means to teach it. Nobody says a word, but you're the one left stirring the old pot.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c60525] = {你烫了一根手指，换来一个点头。在这间厨房里，这就是毕业证书。|你做错了，错法恰好跟你祖母当年一样。大家沉默了一会儿，然后把它吃了个精光。|还没人打算教你，你的手就学会了后一半里的一步。谁也没说什么，可搅那口老锅的人换成了你。}\n\n@event break_home_own_bed\n# en: Your Own Bed\ntitle [cf9821] = 自己的床\n# en: {Your own bed, after a term in the dormitory. You wake at the academy's hour anyway.|The house wakes at dawn. It always has; you'd forgotten.|You know which floorboard creaks on the way down, and that everyone else learned it first.} {season == \"winter\": There's frost on the window and the blankets weigh a ton.|Sunlight is coming through the curtains, and someone is already whistling in the yard.} Downstairs, {the kettle is going.|someone is calling your name.|the door bangs for the third time.}\ntext [290942] = {在宿舍住了一学期，终于睡回自己的床。你还是在学院的钟点醒了。|家里天一亮就醒。一直如此，你只是忘了。|你知道下楼时哪块地板会响，也知道全家早就比你先知道。} {season == \"winter\": 窗上结着霜，被子重得像一吨。|阳光透过窗帘，院子里已经有人在吹口哨。} 楼下，{水壶响了。|有人在喊你的名字。|门第三次砰地关上。}\n# en: A narrow bed under a cottage window in early light, a student buried in blankets while the house bustles below.\nscene [12a0dd] = 清晨微光中，小屋窗下一张窄床，一个学生埋在被子里，楼下一片忙碌。\n# en: Sleep until noon\nopt.left.label [7405ea] = 睡到中午\n# en: {You sleep like a stone. At lunch you're called \"the scholar\" with a new and pointed emphasis.|You wake at noon, rested and ashamed. The whole house has done a morning's work, and mentions it.|You sleep through the door banging a third time. When you finally come down, you miss the creaky floorboard, and nobody hears how late you are.}\nopt.left.say.1 [29956b] = {你睡得像块石头。午饭时，大家喊你“学者”，带着一种新鲜而意味深长的重音。|你中午醒来，神清气爽又满心惭愧。全家已经干完一上午的活，还特意提了提。|门第三次砰地响，你还在睡。等你终于下楼，你躲开了那块会响的地板，谁也没听出你起得有多晚。}\n# en: Get up with everyone\nopt.right.label [b1c969] = 跟大家一起起床\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {You break the ice on the water butt and carry wood in before breakfast. Your hands sting all morning.|You're out in the dark with a lantern before the cockerel. The cockerel resents the competition.|You step over the creaking floorboard and are at the woodpile before anyone knows you're up. Your fingers sting till noon.}|{You're in the yard by sunrise, hauling and fetching. Breakfast, when it comes, is the best of the year.|You work the cool hours with everyone else and see why they swear by them.|You step over the creaking floorboard and are in the yard before the door bangs again. By breakfast the work is half done.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [f756ab] = {season == \"winter\": {早饭前你敲开水桶上的冰，把柴搬进屋。整个上午手都在刺痛。|公鸡还没叫，你就提着灯笼出门了。公鸡对这个竞争者很不满。|你跨过那块会响的地板，还没人知道你起了，你已经在柴堆边了。手指一直刺痛到中午。}|{日出时你已经在院子里搬这搬那。早饭端上来时，是一年里最香的一顿。|你跟大家一起趁凉干活，总算明白他们为什么对这几个钟头赞不绝口。|你跨过那块会响的地板，门还没再响，你已经在院子里了。到早饭时，活已经干完一半。}}\n\n@event break_home_mending_chair\n# en: The Mending Chair\ntitle [56e7dd] = 待修的椅子\n# en: {Word goes round the house that the scholar can fix things.|The things that need fixing have been waiting for you on a chair by the door.|The mending chair fills up between breaks, like a well.} {season == \"winter\": Today it's a stove door that won't stay shut.|Today it's a window that sticks in the heat.} {Also a clock that strikes thirteen.|Also a doll with a missing eye.|Also a teapot nobody admits to breaking.}\ntext [1709e9] = {家里传开了：学者会修东西。|要修的东西已经在门边的椅子上等着你了。|这把待修的椅子在两次假期之间就会堆满，像一口井。} {season == \"winter\": 今天是一扇关不严的炉门。|今天是一扇热天卡住的窗户。} {还有一只会敲十三下的钟。|还有一个少了一只眼睛的布娃娃。|还有一把没人承认打碎的茶壶。}\n# en: A chair by a cottage door piled with broken household things, a student in academy robes eyeing them with a wand.\nscene [691b03] = 小屋门边一把椅子上堆满了坏掉的家什，一个穿学院长袍的学生拿着魔杖打量它们。\n# en: Mend it with a spell\nopt.left.label [16d363] = 用咒语修好\n# en: {It mends with a click and a little puff of light. The family applauds, then brings out three more things.|You fix it so well that it works better than new. Now everyone wants their things to work better than new.|The teapot mends without a crack showing. Somebody looks very relieved, and the chair fills up again by supper.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f638e0] = {咔哒一声，冒出一小团光，修好了。全家鼓掌，然后又搬出三件来。|你修得比新的还好用。现在人人都想让自己的东西比新的还好用。|茶壶修好了，一道裂纹也看不出。有人明显松了一口气，到晚饭时椅子上又堆满了。}\n# en: {It's mended, technically. It also hums now, and nobody can make it stop.|The spell fixes it and then, for good measure, everything else on the chair. Most of it did not need fixing that way.|The teapot mends, and now pours only for whoever broke it. Nobody will pick it up.}\nopt.left.say.2 [760c25] = {严格来说，修好了。只是它现在会嗡嗡响，谁也停不下来。|咒语修好了它，顺带把椅子上别的东西也一并修了。大部分并不需要那样修。|茶壶修好了，可现在它只给打碎它的人倒茶。谁也不肯去拿它。}\n# en: Mend it by hand\nopt.right.label [2c35c0] = 动手修\n# en: {Glue, string, a borrowed hammer and the whole morning. It holds. You don't trust it, but it holds.|You fix it the way your family always has: slowly, with a lot of opinions from the doorway.|You glue the teapot together crack by crack. Nobody admits to anything, but someone brings you tea in it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0c31d9] = {胶水、绳子、借来的锤子，外加一整个上午。它撑住了。你不太信它，可它撑住了。|你照家里一贯的法子修：慢慢来，门口一堆人发表意见。|你一道裂纹一道裂纹地把茶壶粘好。谁也没承认什么，可有人用它给你端来了茶。}\n\n@event break_home_the_trunk\n# en: The Trunk in the Corner\ntitle [bc6453] = 角落里的箱子\n# en: {Your trunk stands open in the corner, and the family keeps putting things in it.|The trunk is open again, and it's filling up again.|The trunk is a family project now.} {season == \"winter\": Woollen socks, a jar of something pickled, a cake wrapped in three cloths.|Dried fruit, a straw hat nobody asked for, a cake wrapped in three cloths.} {final_year: Someone says \"for next term\", then stops.|{The lid won't close.|Someone sits on the lid.|A note is tucked into a sock, not to be read till later.}}\ntext [82fec8] = {你的箱子敞在角落里，家里人不停地往里塞东西。|箱子又敞开了，又满起来了。|这只箱子如今是全家的工程。} {season == \"winter\": 羊毛袜、一罐不知腌了什么的东西、一块裹了三层布的蛋糕。|果干、一顶没人要的草帽、一块裹了三层布的蛋糕。} {final_year: 有人说“下学期带着”，然后停住了。|{盖子合不上。|有人坐到了盖子上。|一只袜子里塞着一张纸条，说好以后再看。}}\n# en: An open travelling trunk in the corner of a small bedroom, overflowing with jars, socks and parcels in cloth.\nscene [cc7e9b] = 小卧室角落里一只敞开的旅行箱，罐子、袜子和布包的东西满得溢了出来。\n# en: Take everything\nopt.left.label [054bdb] = 全都带上\n# en: {The bargeman charges you double for the weight, but you eat well for a month.|You pay the barge extra for the weight and haul it all back across the lake. Half the corridor smells of pickles until spring.|You take everything. The bargeman charges for the weight, and somewhere in the third cloth you find a note from home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fdcb36] = {箱子太沉，船夫收了你双倍船钱，可你一个月都吃得很好。|你为超重多付了船钱，把这些全拖过了湖。半条走廊一直到春天都是腌菜味。|你全都带上了。船夫按重量收了钱，在第三层布里，你找到了一张家里写的字条。}\n# en: Sneak half of it back\nopt.right.label [4040fd] = 偷偷放回一半\n# en: {You smuggle the jars back into the pantry at night. In the morning they're in the trunk again, wrapped tighter.|You leave the cake on a shelf by the door. You feel worse about it than you expected.|You manage to leave one jar behind. In its place, someone has tucked a note.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1c5798] = {你夜里把罐子偷偷送回储藏室。第二天早上，它们又回到了箱子里，裹得更紧了。|你把蛋糕留在门边的架子上。你比自己预想的更过意不去。|你总算留下了一个罐子。在它原来的位置上，有人塞了一张字条。}\n\n@event break_work_stocktake\n# en: Stocktaking\ntitle [8bb9d1] = 盘点库存\n# en: {The quiet days of the break are, says whoever is in charge, the time to count everything.|Stocktaking again. You have opinions about how to count things now.|Stocktaking. You count faster than your boss, and slower than you'd like.} {has(gig_newt): At the Gilded Newt that means every tankard, keg and spoon.|{has(gig_pell): At Pell & Daughters that means a thousand drawers.|{has(gig_post): At the loft that means every parcel, pigeonhole and broom.|{has(gig_library): In the library that means books that won't hold still to be counted.|{has(gig_kitchens): In the kitchens that means every ladle, pot and sack of flour.|{has(gig_grounds): In the sheds that means every trowel, rake and seed packet.|That means everything, twice.}}}}}} The tally comes out one short.\ntext [a3cf2a] = {管事的人说，假期的清闲日子正适合把东西全数一遍。|又盘点了。如今你对怎么数东西都有自己的见解了。|盘点。你数得比上头快，比你自己希望的慢。} {has(gig_newt): 在镀金蝾螈酒馆，这意味着每一只酒杯、每一桶酒、每一把勺子。|{has(gig_pell): 在佩尔父女药铺，这意味着一千个抽屉。|{has(gig_post): 在阁楼，这意味着每一个包裹、每一个信格、每一把扫帚。|{has(gig_library): 在图书馆，这意味着那些不肯老老实实让人数的书。|{has(gig_kitchens): 在后厨，这意味着每一把长柄勺、每一口锅、每一袋面粉。|{has(gig_grounds): 在工具棚，这意味着每一把小铲、每一把耙子、每一包种子。|这意味着所有东西，数两遍。}}}}}} 总数差了一个。\n# en: A student with a ledger and pencil counting rows of stock in a quiet workplace, the owner reading over their shoulder.\nscene [199b94] = 安静的工作场所里，一个学生拿着账本和铅笔清点一排排货物，老板从肩后看着。\n# en: Track down the missing one\nopt.left.label [855fc3] = 找出少的那一个\n# en: {You find it wedged behind the counter, where it has been since the last stocktake. You're tipped for your thoroughness.|You find the mistake in the ledger itself: someone counted a box as a crate. You're paid a little extra, and nothing else is said.|You count faster than your boss and find it first, under a sack nobody has moved in years. You're tipped, a little grudgingly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a484db] = {你在柜台后面找到了它，它从上次盘点起就卡在那儿了。你因为细心得了点赏钱。|你发现错在账本本身：有人把一箱记成了一桶。你多拿了一点工钱，别的谁也没提。|你数得比上头快，先找到了它，就在一个好几年没人挪过的麻袋底下。你得了点赏钱，给得有点不情愿。}\n# en: {You search until dusk, on your knees in the dust. It stays missing, and your boss looks at you as if you might have eaten it.|You count everything again and get a different number. Then a third.|You count faster than your boss, and wrong. The missing one stays missing, and now so does another.}\nopt.left.say.2 [876a80] = {你跪在灰里找到天黑。它还是不见踪影，上头看你的眼神，好像是你把它吃了。|你又全数了一遍，得出另一个数。然后是第三个。|你数得比上头快，也数错了。少的那件还是没找到，现在又少了一件。}\n# en: Write it off and move on\nopt.right.label [71a437] = 记作损耗了事\n# en: {\"Breakage,\" you write. A coin is docked from your pay, on principle.|You write it off, and your boss sighs and takes it from your wages.|You write it off before your boss has finished counting. It comes out of your wages all the same.}\nopt.right.say.1 [042fa5] = {你写下“损耗”。你的工钱照规矩被扣了一枚。|你把它记作损耗，上头叹口气，从你工钱里扣了。|上头还没数完，你就把它记成了损耗。照样从你工钱里扣。}\n\n@event break_work_complaint\n# en: The Complaint\ntitle [dd0cf5] = 投诉\n# en: {Someone has come back with a complaint and a voice built for it.|Another complaint, another raised voice. You've learned to breathe out first.|This one has been saving the complaint all week. You can tell.} {has(gig_newt): The cider was too warm, or too cold, or too cider.|{has(gig_pell): The tonic did nothing, which is worse, they say, than doing something.|{has(gig_post): The parcel arrived wet, late and somehow smug.|{has(gig_library): The book they want is out, and they want it in.|{has(gig_kitchens): The stew had a button in it, and they want to know whose.|{has(gig_grounds): A visitor's boots are ruined, and the mud on the path is apparently your mud.|The complaint has several parts, all of them loud.}}}}}} Whoever's in charge is out.\ntext [b04d1c] = {有人带着投诉回来了，嗓门天生就是为投诉准备的。|又一桩投诉，又一个大嗓门。你已经学会先呼一口气。|这位攒了一整个星期的投诉。你看得出来。} {has(gig_newt): 苹果酒太热，或者太冷，或者太苹果酒了。|{has(gig_pell): 补药毫无效果，他们说，这比有点效果还糟。|{has(gig_post): 包裹送到时又湿又迟，还不知怎么透着一股得意。|{has(gig_library): 他们要的书借出去了，他们要它现在就回来。|{has(gig_kitchens): 炖菜里有颗纽扣，他们想知道是谁的。|{has(gig_grounds): 一位访客的靴子毁了，小路上的泥据说是你的泥。|投诉分好几部分，每一部分都很响。}}}}}} 管事的人不在。\n# en: An irate customer leaning over a counter, finger raised, while a young worker stands very still behind it.\nscene [eec2a5] = 一位气冲冲的客人俯身在柜台上竖着手指，柜台后的年轻伙计一动不动。\n# en: Talk them down\nopt.left.label [dfea51] = 好言安抚\n# en: {You agree with everything until there's nothing left to agree with. They leave calm, and they leave a tip.|You listen, nod and offer them a chair. By the end they're telling you about their sister.|You let them say a week's worth of complaint without interrupting once. By the end they're almost apologetic, and they tip.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f6fb1a] = {你什么都附和，直到再没什么可附和。对方心平气和地走了，还留了小费。|你倾听、点头，请对方坐下。说到最后，对方在跟你讲他们的姐妹。|你让对方把攒了一星期的抱怨说完，一次都没打断。说到最后对方几乎有点过意不去，还给了小费。}\n# en: {It gets louder, and then it leaves, still loud, to tell everyone in the valley about you.|They demand your boss, then you, then your boss again, and you spend the rest of the day hearing about it.|A week's worth of complaint, and you interrupt it once. That's once too many.}\nopt.left.say.2 [59f1d5] = {声音越来越大，然后对方走了，一路还在嚷，要让整个山谷都知道你。|对方要找你上头，然后找你，然后又找你上头，你剩下的一整天都在听这件事。|攒了一星期的抱怨，你打断了一次。就这一次，也多了。}\n# en: Give them what they want\nopt.right.label [876ac8] = 给他们想要的\n# en: {You hand over a refund out of your wages. They leave. The quiet afterwards is worth a coin.|A free one, on you. They take it without thanks, and the next one is lovely.|You give in before they're halfway through a week's worth of complaint. They seem almost disappointed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a83120] = {你从工钱里退了款。对方走了。之后的清静值一枚钱。|这份算你请。对方连谢都没说就拿走了，下一位倒是可爱极了。|攒了一星期的抱怨，对方还没说到一半你就让步了。对方看上去几乎有点失望。}\n\n@event break_work_half_hour\n# en: Half an Hour Off\ntitle [647a16] = 半个钟头的休息\n# en: {Half an hour off in the middle of a long holiday shift.|Your half hour again. You know exactly how far it goes now.|Half an hour. You spend the first minute deciding.} {season == \"winter\": Outside, the snow is coming down sideways.|Outside, the sun is bright enough to hurt.} {The back room has one chair and one draught.|A colleague has left half a pie, possibly for sharing.|A clock somewhere strikes the wrong hour.}\ntext [8b3489] = {漫长的假期班次中间，有半个钟头休息。|又是你的半个钟头。如今你清楚它能走多远。|半个钟头。头一分钟用来做决定。} {season == \"winter\": 外面，雪横着飘。|外面，太阳亮得刺眼。} {后屋有一把椅子和一股穿堂风。|同事留下半个馅饼，也许是给大家分的。|某处的钟敲错了钟点。}\n# en: A tired student in a work apron standing in a back doorway, looking out at the weather with a cup in hand.\nscene [defd5a] = 一个系着围裙的疲惫学生站在后门口，手里捧着杯子，望着外面的天气。\n# en: Walk out into the weather\nopt.left.label [e0bf5d] = 出门走走\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {You buy roast chestnuts and eat them walking, snow in your collar. You come back soaked and much happier.|You buy a hot pie for the walk to the lake's edge and back. Your nose will not forgive you.|You spend a minute deciding and the rest in the snow, to the end of the lane and back. A clock strikes the wrong hour as you come in.}|{You buy a cold drink and sit in the sun until your neck burns. Worth it.|You buy a lemonade and walk to the water and stand there. Half an hour is longer outside.|You spend a minute deciding and the rest in the sun. A clock strikes the wrong hour, and you nearly believe it.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [d8c737] = {season == \"winter\": {你买了烤栗子边走边吃，雪灌进领口。回来时湿透了，心情却好多了。|你买了个热馅饼，边吃边走到湖边又走回来。你的鼻子不会原谅你。|你花一分钟拿主意，剩下的时间都在雪里，走到巷子尽头又走回来。你进门时，一座钟敲错了点。}|{你买了杯冷饮，坐在太阳底下晒到脖子发红。值。|你买了杯柠檬水，走到水边站着。半个钟头在外面显得更长。|你花一分钟拿主意，剩下的时间都在太阳底下。一座钟敲错了点，你差点就信了。}}\n# en: Nap in the back room\nopt.right.label [a2e9fa] = 在后屋打个盹\n# en: {You sleep sitting up and wake with the shape of a crate on your cheek.|You doze off and dream of work, which seems unfair. The bell wakes you mid-shift in the dream.|You spend a minute deciding, and wake to a clock striking the wrong hour. You're late, or early. Nobody can say.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3b3011] = {你坐着睡着了，醒来脸上印着木箱的形状。|你打了个盹，梦见在干活，这似乎不太公平。梦里干到一半，铃声把你叫醒了。|你花一分钟拿主意，然后被一座敲错点的钟吵醒。你迟到了，或者早到了。谁也说不准。}\n\n@event break_work_short_wages\n# en: A Coin Short\ntitle [996363] = 少了一枚\n# en: {Wages, counted out into your hand. You count them again.|Wage day. You count your coins at the counter, in front of everyone, on principle.|Wage day. Your boss counts slowly, and so do you.} {It's short by one.|It's short, and there's a button where a coin should be.|It's short by just enough to notice.} {has(gig_newt): The landlady remembers every tab to the penny, and has somehow forgotten this one.|{has(gig_pell): Old Pell counted it himself, drop by drop.|{has(gig_post): The dispatch ledger says otherwise, in smudged ink.|{has(gig_library): The pay envelope says otherwise, in very small writing.|{has(gig_kitchens): The head cook's tally is written in gravy.|{has(gig_grounds): Maud's pay book says otherwise, in pencil.|Someone's sums say otherwise.}}}}}}\ntext [755880] = {工钱数到你手里。你又数了一遍。|发工钱的日子。你现在当众在柜台上数钱，这是原则。|发工钱的日子。上头数得慢，你也数得慢。} {少了一枚。|少了，本该是钱的地方放着一颗纽扣。|少的刚好够你察觉。} {has(gig_newt): 老板娘记得每一笔赊账，分毫不差，偏偏把这一笔忘了。|{has(gig_pell): 是老佩尔亲手数的，一滴一滴地数。|{has(gig_post): 派送账簿上可不是这么写的，墨迹还糊了。|{has(gig_library): 工资袋上可不是这么写的，字小得很。|{has(gig_kitchens): 大厨的账是用肉汁写的。|{has(gig_grounds): 莫德的工钱簿上可不是这么写的，用的是铅笔。|有人的算术可不是这么算的。}}}}}}\n# en: A student counting a small pile of coins on a counter for the second time, frowning, an employer pretending not to watch.\nscene [5dacbb] = 一个学生在柜台上第二次清点一小堆硬币，皱着眉，雇主假装没在看。\n# en: Point it out\nopt.left.label [d2676e] = 指出来\n# en: {You show your sums. Your boss checks them, coughs, and pays you the difference with a little extra for the embarrassment.|You point it out politely and firmly. The missing coin appears, with an apology and a pastry.|You count as slowly as your boss did, aloud, coin by coin. The difference is paid before you reach the end.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9644e9] = {你把算式摆出来。上头核对一遍，咳了一声，补上差额，还为这份尴尬多给了一点。|你礼貌而坚定地指出来。少的那枚出现了，附带一句道歉和一块点心。|你像上头一样慢，一枚一枚大声数。还没数完，差额就补上了。}\n# en: {Your boss recounts, gets the same wrong answer, and looks hurt that you asked.|You're told it's for the breakage. You don't remember any breakage. Nobody else does either.|You count aloud, slowly. Your boss counts back, slower, and reaches a different number. You give up before supper.}\nopt.left.say.2 [49fbf8] = {上头重数一遍，得出同样的错数，还一脸受伤，怪你多问。|你被告知这是赔损耗的。你不记得有什么损耗。别人也不记得。|你慢慢地大声数。上头更慢地数回来，得出另一个数。晚饭前你放弃了。}\n# en: Let it go this once\nopt.right.label [b87d7e] = 这次就算了\n# en: {You pocket it without a word. Your boss sees you notice, and you're given the easy shift next week.|One coin. You decide you can afford to be the bigger person, just about.|Short by just enough to notice, and you notice, and say nothing. Your boss counts faster next time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e3a0a0] = {你一声不吭地收下了。上头看见你注意到了，下周给你排了轻松的班。|一枚而已。你决定自己大度得起，勉强吧。|少得刚好能察觉，你察觉了，什么也没说。下一回，上头数得快多了。}\n\n@event refectory_cooks_taster\n# en: The Cook's Taster\ntitle [64d79d] = 厨娘的试吃员\n# en: The cook stops you before you reach the bench and holds out a spoon. \"You're in here more than the cats,\" she says. \"So you can taste the new pot first. Regulars do.\" Behind her a dozen steaming pots wait their turn, and the other students in the queue look on with naked envy.\ntext [6626d7] = 你还没走到长凳，厨娘就拦住你，递过来一把勺子。“你来得比猫还勤，”她说，“所以新的一锅你先尝。常客都这样。”她身后十几口锅冒着热气等着轮到，排队的学生们毫不掩饰地眼红。\n# en: A refectory cook holding out a small spoon over the counter to a student, a new pot steaming behind her.\nscene [2e4b9e] = 膳堂厨娘隔着柜台递给学生一把小勺，身后一口新锅冒着热气。\n# en: Give an honest verdict\nopt.left.label [5b70db] = 说实话\n# en: You say it wants salt and less of the grey thing. The cook frowns, tastes, and nods. From then on your bowl comes from the bottom of the pot, where the good bits are.\nopt.left.say.1 [e40fb3] = 你说得多放盐，少放那灰乎乎的东西。厨娘皱眉尝了尝，点了点头。从此你的那碗都从锅底盛，好料都在那儿。\n# en: Praise it to the rafters\nopt.right.label [7c4191] = 夸上天去\n# en: You declare it the best soup in the kingdom. The cook doesn't believe a word of it, and gives you a second spoonful anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [c29b20] = 你宣布这是王国里最好喝的汤。厨娘一个字也不信，还是又给了你一勺。\n\n@event library_footnote_bag\n# en: Footnote's Approval\ntitle [c453a2] = 脚注的认可\n# en: You come back from the shelves to find Footnote asleep on your bag, curled tight, one paw over the strap. Footnote sleeps on the reference desk, as a rule, and almost never on anyone's belongings; everybody knows that. A passing librarian stops, looks from the cat to you, and nods, as if something official has happened.\ntext [c26442] = 你从书架间回来，发现脚注睡在你的书包上，蜷得紧紧的，一只爪子搭着背带。人人都知道，脚注照例只睡参考台，几乎从不睡在别人的东西上。一位路过的图书管理员停下来，看看猫又看看你，点了点头，仿佛刚发生了什么正式的事。\n# en: A huge old library cat curled asleep on a student's satchel under a reading desk, a student standing over it, uncertain.\nscene [515a63] = 阅览桌下，一只硕大的老图书馆猫蜷在学生的书包上睡觉，学生站在一旁不知所措。\n# en: Wait for him to wake\nopt.left.label [03f336] = 等他睡醒\n# en: You read standing up until the lamps are lit. Footnote wakes, stretches, and walks off without a glance. Your bag smells of warm cat for a week, and you find you don't mind.\nopt.left.say.1 [5ee19a] = 你站着看书，一直看到点灯。脚注醒了，伸个懒腰，看都不看你就走了。你的书包一个星期都带着暖烘烘的猫味，而你发现自己并不介意。\n# en: Ease the bag out, inch by inch\nopt.right.label [e23815] = 一寸寸抽出书包\n# en: You slide it free a fraction at a time. Footnote opens one eye, judges you, and allows it. The librarian says she's never seen him allow anything.\nopt.right.say.1 [2fedfc] = 你一点一点把书包抽出来。脚注睁开一只眼，审视了你一番，准了。图书管理员说她从没见他准过任何事。\n\n@event mabels_your_usual\n# en: Your Usual\ntitle [c3563c] = 老样子\n# en: You haven't even reached the counter. Mabel already has your cup out, the right tea steeping and the cake you always end up choosing on a plate. \"Your usual,\" she says, and it isn't a question. On the slate behind the till, among the regulars, there's a new line in chalk.\ntext [05c137] = 你还没走到柜台，梅布尔已经摆好了你的杯子，对的茶正在泡，盘子里放着你每次最后都会挑的那块蛋糕。“老样子，”她说，不是在问。收银台后的石板上，常客名单里多了一行新粉笔字。\n# en: A small tea shop counter with a cup already poured and a slice of cake on a plate, the shopkeeper smiling at a student in the doorway.\nscene [a81742] = 小茶馆的柜台上已经倒好一杯茶、放着一块蛋糕，店主朝门口的学生微笑。\n# en: Have the usual\nopt.left.label [de70ee] = 就要老样子\n# en: It's exactly right, as it always is. Mabel watches you take the first sip and turns back to the shelves, satisfied.\nopt.left.say.1 [ba0c49] = 恰到好处，一如既往。梅布尔看着你喝下第一口，心满意足地转回货架。\n# en: Ask for something new\nopt.right.label [35d8bb] = 来点新的\n# en: Mabel's eyebrows rise. Then she smiles like someone handed a puzzle and reaches for a tin from the very top shelf. It's odd and smoky, and you don't like it, and you're glad you asked.\nopt.right.say.1 [09aadb] = 梅布尔挑起眉毛。接着她笑得像拿到了一道谜题，伸手去够最顶层架子上的一个茶罐。那茶古怪又带烟味，你不喜欢，但你很高兴自己问了。\n\n@event bakery_bun_set_aside\n# en: The Bun Set Aside\ntitle [795ce5] = 留给你的面包\n# en: The baker's son reaches under the counter before you've said a word and brings out a bun wrapped in paper, with your initial on it in flour. \"Saved from the first batch,\" he says. \"Regulars get the corner ones.\" Behind him, by the ovens, the oldest baker nods as if she arranged it all herself.\ntext [09ad6a] = 你还没开口，面包师的儿子就从柜台下拿出一个纸包的面包，纸上用面粉写着你名字的首字母。“头一炉留的，”他说，“常客拿边角的。”他身后的炉边，最年长的面包师点点头，好像这一切都是她安排的。\n# en: A baker's son handing a paper-wrapped bun across a flour-dusted counter, a letter written on the paper in flour.\nscene [e72281] = 面包师的儿子隔着撒满面粉的柜台递过一个纸包面包，纸上用面粉写着一个字母。\n# en: Insist on paying for it\nopt.left.label [5b7a00] = 坚持付钱\n# en: He refuses twice and accepts the third time. The coin goes in a jar by the till marked, in the oldest baker's hand, \"for the post\".\nopt.left.say.1 [d333cd] = 他推辞了两次，第三次收下了。那枚钱进了收银台边的一个罐子，罐上是最年长的面包师的笔迹：“寄信用”。\n# en: Thank him and eat it warm\nopt.right.label [8942f0] = 道谢，趁热吃\n# en: It's still warm and far too big, and you eat it on the step in four bites and burn your mouth. You'd do it again tomorrow.\nopt.right.say.1 [aea84b] = 面包还热着，大得离谱，你坐在台阶上四口吃完，烫了嘴。明天你还会这么干。\n\n@event grimsbys_right_book\n# en: The Right Book, First Time\ntitle [38138c] = 一次就拿对的书\n# en: You step into Grimsby's and open your mouth, and Mr Grimsby is already holding out a book. It is exactly the one you came for. He looks as astonished as you are. \"Must be getting to know you,\" he says, and then, worried, \"Don't tell anyone.\"\ntext [c259e5] = 你走进格林斯比书店，刚张开嘴，格林斯比先生已经把一本书递了过来。正是你要找的那本。他和你一样惊讶。“大概是开始了解你了，”他说，又担心地补了一句，“别告诉别人。”\n# en: A cramped bookshop of leaning shelves, an elderly bookseller holding out a book to a student who has only just stepped inside.\nscene [83e433] = 书架东倒西歪的狭小书店里，一位年迈的书商把一本书递给刚进门的学生。\n# en: Buy it before it changes\nopt.left.label [8c5f21] = 趁它没变赶紧买\n# en: You pay at once, in case it turns into a book on beekeeping. It doesn't. You carry it all the way back to the castle, checking.\nopt.left.say.1 [e0afe4] = 你立刻付钱，以防它变成一本养蜂的书。它没变。你一路把它抱回城堡，边走边检查。\n# en: Ask what else he'd pick for me\nopt.right.label [4293db] = 问他还会给我挑啥\n# en: Restored to form, Mr Grimsby brings you a history of eels and a book of riddles, and lets you read the riddles in the window seat until closing. They're very good, and no help at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [89473a] = 格林斯比先生恢复了常态，给你拿来一本鳗鱼史和一本谜语书，还让你坐在窗边的座位上读谜语，一直读到打烊。谜语很好，一点用也没有。\n\n@event courtyard_in_the_know\n# en: In the Know\ntitle [143948] = 消息灵通\n# en: The knot of students by the fountain goes quiet as you pass, and then one of them waves you over. \"You're out here every day,\" says a second-year from Duskgate. \"So. What have you heard?\" It seems you've been counted among the courtyard's regulars, and regulars are expected to bring news.\ntext [f4ab24] = 你经过喷泉时，那一圈学生安静下来，接着其中一个朝你招手。“你天天在这儿，”一个暮门的二年级生说，“那么，你听到什么了？”看来你已经被算作庭院的常客了，而常客是要带消息来的。\n# en: A knot of students gathered on the rim of a courtyard fountain, leaning in, one waving a newcomer over.\nscene [ed9aa8] = 一群学生聚在庭院喷泉边沿，凑头说话，其中一人朝新来的人招手。\n# en: Share something harmless\nopt.left.label [743b67] = 说点无伤大雅的\n# en: You offer up a rumour about the refectory pudding. It's received with grave interest and is all over the castle by supper, much improved.\nopt.left.say.1 [04ca03] = 你贡献了一条关于膳堂布丁的传闻。大家郑重其事地听了，到晚饭时它已传遍城堡，还被添油加醋了不少。\n# en: Say I've heard nothing\nopt.right.label [4cfbdc] = 说我啥也没听说\n# en: They take your silence as proof that you know everything. By the end of the week, people are asking you for the news.\nopt.right.say.1 [60a72f] = 他们把你的沉默当成你什么都知道的证据。不到一个星期，就有人来找你打听消息了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/plans_thesis.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/plans_thesis.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event break_castle_one_fire\n# en: The One Lit Fire\ntitle [c9e9bf] = 唯一一炉火\n# en: {With the houses empty, the stewards light one common room and let the rest go dark.|Someone has brought a kettle, and now there's a rota for it.|The break common room. You know everyone's blanket by now.} {season == \"winter\": The best armchair is right by the fire.|The best armchair is by the only breeze.} {A first-year from Stillwater is asleep in it.|Footnote is in it. Footnote moves for no one.|The steward's dog is in it, and the steward is on the dog's side.}\ntext [a49c22] = {宿舍楼都空了，管事们只点亮一间公共休息室，其余的任它暗着。|有人带来了水壶，现在还排了轮值表。|假期的公共休息室。现在每个人的毯子你都认得了。} {season == \"winter\": 最好的扶手椅就在炉火旁。|最好的扶手椅就在唯一有风的窗边。} {一个静水院的一年级生在椅子上睡着了。|脚注占着那把椅子。脚注不给任何人让座。|管事的狗占着它，而管事站在狗那边。}\n# en: A cavernous common room in the holidays, a handful of students in blankets crowded around {season == \"winter\": one roaring hearth|one open window}.\nscene [24c42d] = 假期里一间空旷的公共休息室，几个裹着毯子的学生挤在{season == \"winter\": 一炉旺火|一扇敞开的窗}旁。\n# en: Talk my way into the armchair\nopt.left.label [483910] = 把扶手椅骗到手\n# en: {You mention that the kitchens are giving out free toffee. The chair empties. You feel only slightly bad.|You announce, with great authority, that the armchair is due to be re-stuffed. It is, now, by you.|You offer the dog your toast. It's a fair trade, and the chair is still warm.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dd333e] = {你随口提到厨房在免费发太妃糖。椅子空了。你只觉得有一点点愧疚。|你郑重宣布这把扶手椅要重新填絮了。现在它确实被填上了——用你。|你把吐司让给了那条狗。很公平的交易，椅子还是热的。}\n# en: {Nobody moves. Someone says, \"Nice try,\" without looking up from their book.|The whole room watches you fail to dislodge the armchair's occupant. It's the most entertainment anyone has had all break.|The dog growls, the steward sides with the dog, and you take the floor.}\nopt.left.say.2 [6998ca] = {没人动。有人头也不抬地说：“想得美。”|全屋的人看着你没能把扶手椅上的那位挪开。这是整个假期里大家看过最好看的节目。|狗冲你低吼，管事站在狗那边，你只好坐地板。}\n# en: Take the draughty corner\nopt.right.label [590fe4] = 去漏风的角落\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {The corner is cold enough to see your breath, but you read three chapters.|You read in gloves. Turning pages becomes a skill.|You find a blanket nobody claims and read until your nose goes numb.}|{It smells of old ink and warm dust. You read three chapters.|A wasp shares your corner. You reach an understanding.|The breeze turns your pages for you, mostly the right way. You read three chapters anyway.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [7c5c66] = {season == \"winter\": {角落冷得能看见自己的呼气，但你读完了三章。|你戴着手套读书。翻页成了一门手艺。|你找到一条没人认领的毯子，一直读到鼻子冻僵。}|{那里有旧墨水和晒暖的灰尘的气味。你读完了三章。|一只黄蜂和你共享角落。你们达成了谅解。|风替你翻页，大多翻对了方向。你还是读完了三章。}}\n\n@event break_castle_kitchen_rota\n# en: The Holiday Kitchen\ntitle [cef060] = 假期的厨房\n# en: {Half the kitchen staff have gone home for the break, and the cook has started drafting whoever wanders past.|The cook spots you in the corridor and holds up an apron.|The cook holds out an apron without a word. It has your name stitched on it now.} {season == \"winter\": There are puddings to steam for the few who stayed, and a goose that refuses to fit the oven.|The orchard has dropped everything at once, and every pan in the kitchen is full of jam.}\ntext [21605b] = {一半厨工回家过假期了，厨娘开始拉路过的人来帮忙。|厨娘在走廊里看见你，举起一条围裙。|厨娘一言不发地递过一条围裙。上面现在绣着你的名字。} {season == \"winter\": 要给留下的几个人蒸布丁，还有一只死活塞不进烤炉的鹅。|果园里的果子一下子全落了，厨房里每口锅都在熬果酱。}\n# en: A steaming castle kitchen with half its ovens cold, the cook handing aprons to two reluctant students.\nscene [d1a5e4] = 冒着热气的城堡厨房，一半烤炉是冷的，厨娘把围裙递给两个不情愿的学生。\n# en: Roll up your sleeves\nopt.left.label [52915f] = 挽起袖子\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {You stir puddings till your arm aches. \"Just like potion work,\" says the cook. It isn't.|You baste the goose. The goose wins, narrowly.|The goose fits at last. The cook says it was her idea.}|{A morning of jam teaches you more about setting points than a term of alchemy.|You label forty jars. The cook re-spells \"gooseberry\" on all of them.|You stone plums all morning. Your fingers are purple until Tuesday.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [85e3c1] = {season == \"winter\": {你搅布丁搅到胳膊发酸。“跟熬魔药一样。”厨娘说。并不一样。|你给鹅刷油。鹅以微弱优势获胜。|鹅终于塞进去了。厨娘说这是她的主意。}|{一上午的果酱让你对凝固点的了解超过一学期炼金课。|你给四十个罐子贴了标签。厨娘把每张上的“醋栗”都重新拼了一遍。|你一上午都在给李子去核。手指一直紫到星期二。}}\n# en: Discover urgent business elsewhere\nopt.right.label [14e943] = 想起别处有急事\n# en: {You escape to the village and buy a pie you could have had for free. The cook sees you come back with the crumbs.|You hide in the village bakery until lunch is over and buy what you could have eaten for free. The cook gives you a meaningful look all evening.|You claim a library emergency. The cook, who knows the library is shut, says nothing and saves you nothing.}\nopt.right.say.1 [73f1e1] = {你溜去山下小镇，买了一个本来可以白吃的馅饼。厨娘看见你带着饼渣回来。|你躲在镇上的面包房里，一直躲到午饭结束，还花钱买了本来可以白吃的东西。厨娘一整晚都意味深长地看着你。|你说图书馆有急事。厨娘知道图书馆关着门，什么也没说，也什么都没给你留。}\n\n@event break_castle_empty_library\n# en: The Library on Holiday\ntitle [4ce56c] = 放假的图书馆\n# en: {The librarians have gone home, and the library has been left to you and the cat.|Footnote has moved from the reference desk to your chair, and opens one eye.|Your break-morning library. One window seat is yours now.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has drawn ferns on the tall windows.|Dust turns slowly in the long summer light.} {Every desk is free.|Someone has left half an essay on the theory of hiccups.|The reshelving cart is full, and nobody is coming for it.}\ntext [8f56d2] = {图书管理员们都回家了，图书馆留给了你和那只猫。|脚注从咨询台挪到了你的椅子上，睁开一只眼。|你的假期早晨图书馆。有一个靠窗的座位现在归你了。} {season == \"winter\": 霜在高窗上画满了蕨叶。|灰尘在长长的夏日光线里慢慢打转。} {每张书桌都空着。|有人留下半篇论打嗝理论的论文。|还书车堆满了，没人会来推。}\n# en: A great library with no one at the desks, a cat asleep on the reference desk and a single student choosing a book.\nscene [e16f43] = 一座书桌空无一人的大图书馆，一只猫睡在咨询台上，一个学生在挑书。\n# en: Read whatever I like\nopt.left.label [0ceb4d] = 想读什么读什么\n# en: {You read a book about the history of spoons and forget to eat lunch. It was a very good book about spoons.|You read until the light goes, then a little after. Your eyes will mention it tomorrow.|You finish the book you started last break. The bookmark was still where you left it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1afd3a] = {你读了一本讲勺子历史的书，忘了吃午饭。那是一本非常好的讲勺子的书。|你一直读到天光散尽，又多读了一会儿。明天你的眼睛会提起这件事。|你读完了上个假期开始读的那本书。书签还夹在你留下的地方。}\n# en: Get ahead on next term\nopt.right.label [d47dc6] = 提前预习下学期\n# en: {final_year: You make notes for a term you'll only half get to use. It still feels like the right thing to do.|{You make a neat set of notes for next term. Future you will be grateful. Present you is bored stiff.|You read ahead three chapters and find the professor's favourite mistake waiting in the fourth.|You copy out next term's reading list and get through half of it. The other half looks smug.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [425249] = {final_year: 你为一个只能用上一半的学期做笔记。可还是觉得该这么做。|{你为下学期整理了一套整齐的笔记。将来的你会感激。现在的你闷得要命。|你往前读了三章，在第四章撞见了教授最爱犯的那个错误。|你抄下下学期的书单，读完了一半。另一半看起来很得意。}}\n\n@event break_castle_post_day\n# en: Post for the Stay-Behinds\ntitle [38476c] = 留守者的信件\n# en: {Post day. The porter lays the letters out on one table in the Great Hall.|The porter now sorts the stay-behinds' letters into a pile of their own.|The porter nods to you. He keeps your letters on top now.} {season == \"winter\": {Most of it is Midwinter cards for people who went home.|One parcel is exactly the shape of a scarf.|One envelope has your name on it, in handwriting you know.}|{Most of it is postcards from people's travels.|A postcard says \"Wish you were here\", and nobody knows whose it is.|One envelope has your name on it and smells of the sea.}}\ntext [5fca27] = {邮件日。门房把信摊在大礼堂的一张桌子上。|门房现在把留守者的信单独分成一小叠。|门房朝你点点头。他现在把你的信放在最上面。} {season == \"winter\": {大多是寄给回家的人的仲冬贺卡。|有个包裹的形状和一条围巾一模一样。|有一个信封上写着你的名字，字迹你认得。}|{大多是旅行中的人寄来的明信片。|一张明信片上只写着“真希望你也在”，没人知道是给谁的。|有一个信封写着你的名字，闻起来有海的味道。}}\n# en: A porter sorting a thin pile of letters on a long empty table, a few students waiting hopefully.\nscene [b2c195] = 门房在空荡的长桌上分拣一小叠信，几个学生满怀希望地等着。\n# en: Write a long letter home\nopt.left.label [b376a0] = 给家里写封长信\n# en: {final_year: You write that this is your last break at the castle, and then look at the sentence for a long time.|{You write four pages and pay for the heavy stamp. It's the closest thing to a conversation you've had all week.|You describe the empty castle so well that you feel lonelier writing it than living it.|You write about the porter, the biscuit and the empty hall. It reads like a good life, which it is.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [bc5632] = {final_year: 你写道这是你在城堡度过的最后一个假期，然后盯着这句话看了很久。|{你写了四页，付了厚信的邮费。这是你这一周里最像聊天的一次。|你把空荡荡的城堡写得太好，写的时候比住在里面还寂寞。|你写了门房、饼干和空荡荡的大厅。读起来像一种不错的生活，事实也是。}}\n# en: Help the porter sort it all\nopt.right.label [329389] = 帮门房分拣\n# en: {The porter tells you who writes to whom, in strict confidence. You know far too much about the second-years now.|You sort the post into houses. There's nothing for you this time. The porter gives you a biscuit, which is nearly as good.|You sort the pile in half the time it took you once. The porter calls you his deputy, and nobody argues.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fa9973] = {门房严格保密地告诉你谁给谁写信。现在你对二年级生知道得太多了。|你把信按学院分好。这次没有你的。门房给了你一块饼干，差不多一样好。|你分完这堆信，只用了当初一半的时间。门房管你叫他的副手，没人反对。}\n\n@event break_home_kitchen_table\n# en: Homework at the Kitchen Table\ntitle [081adf] = 厨房桌上的功课\n# en: {You spread your books across the kitchen table, where you did your sums as a child.|Your books have been moved for the bread, and one is being used as a trivet.|The family works around your books now. Mostly.} {season == \"winter\": The stove is the only warm place, so everyone is in the kitchen.|The door is open, and everyone passing asks what the book is about.} {A cousin has drawn a dragon in your margin.|The kettle boils every quarter-hour, on principle.|Someone has put a book of yours under a wobbly table leg, and it works.}\ntext [266433] = {你把书摊在厨房桌上，小时候你就在这儿算算术。|你的书被挪开给面包腾地方，有一本被拿去垫了锅。|家里人现在会绕着你的书干活。大多数时候。} {season == \"winter\": 炉子是屋里唯一暖和的地方，所以大家都在厨房。|门朝院子开着，每个路过的人都要问这书讲什么。} {一个表亲在你的页边画了条龙。|水壶每一刻钟烧开一次，原则问题。|有人把你的一本书垫在了一条晃动的桌腿下面，还真管用。}\n# en: A family kitchen in the morning, a student's books spread among the breakfast plates, someone reaching across them for the butter.\nscene [6a8ca6] = 早晨的家中厨房，学生的书摊在早餐盘子之间，有人越过书去够黄油。\n# en: Study through the noise\nopt.left.label [9218fa] = 顶着吵闹学习\n# en: {You read the same paragraph nine times and learn it perfectly, along with the price of eggs.|You get through a chapter. Your notes now have a jam stain, a crumb and the shopping list on them.|You learn to read with one hand and pass the salt with the other. A whole chapter goes in.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bc5a95] = {同一段你读了九遍，背得滚瓜烂熟，连带记住了鸡蛋的价钱。|你读完了一章。你的笔记上现在有一块果酱渍、一粒面包屑和一张购物清单。|你学会了一手看书一手递盐。读进去了整整一章。}\n# en: Give up and help with lunch\nopt.right.label [91e189] = 放弃，帮忙做午饭\n# en: {You're sent to the shop for the one thing everyone forgot, and you buy sweets with the change.|You peel, chop and get told about everyone in the village. Your book stays shut, and it's the best morning of the break.|You're on potatoes again. You're told you peel like a scholar, which isn't a compliment.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7b491a] = {你被派去店里买大家都忘了的那样东西，用找零买了糖。|你削皮、切菜，听了村里每个人的近况。书一直没打开，这是假期里最好的一个上午。|你又被派去削土豆。家里人说你削皮的样子像个学者，这不是夸奖。}\n\n@event break_home_the_box\n# en: The Box Under the Bed\ntitle [dfd52f] = 床底下的盒子\n# en: {Your old room is smaller than you remember. Under the bed is the box where you kept everything that mattered when you were nine.|Someone has dusted the box since last time and pretended not to look inside.|You've almost reached the bottom of the box.} {This time it's a wand you made from a stick and a lot of string.|This time it's the letter you wrote to the academy at age ten, asking to be let in early.|This time it's a drawing of seven towers, each with a little flag.}\ntext [00a18c] = {你的旧房间比记忆里小。床底下是你九岁时装一切重要东西的盒子。|上次之后有人掸过盒子上的灰，还假装没往里看。|你快翻到盒底了。} {这次是一根你用树枝和一大堆绳子做的魔杖。|这次是你十岁时写给学院的信，请求提前入学。|这次是一幅七座塔的画，每座塔上都插着小旗。}\n# en: A narrow childhood bedroom with a bed far too short for its occupant, an old box of keepsakes pulled out onto the floor.\nscene [2fff89] = 一间窄小的儿时卧室，床对现在的主人来说短得多，一盒旧纪念品被拖到地板上。\n# en: Pack it in your trunk\nopt.left.label [60f445] = 装进行李箱\n# en: {It comes back to Lumenhold with you. The trunk is heavier, and so, somehow, are you.|You pack it between your robes. Every time you open the trunk this term, it will be the first thing you see.|You pack it carefully. There's not much left in the box now, and that feels like something ending.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c4ea52] = {它跟你一起回了流明堡。箱子更沉了，不知怎么，你也是。|你把它夹在袍子中间。这学期每次打开箱子，它都会是你第一眼看到的东西。|你小心地把它收好。盒子里剩的不多了，感觉像什么东西要结束了。}\n# en: Give it to your cousin\nopt.right.label [a1cb8e] = 送给你的表亲\n# en: {Your cousin, who is applying to the academy next autumn, takes it with enormous seriousness. You feel very old.|Your cousin promises to keep it safe. By supper it has been traded to a neighbour for a jar of honey.|Your cousin says thank you and puts it in a box of their own, under their own bed. You feel very old and quite happy.}\nopt.right.say.1 [155820] = {你的表亲明年秋天要报考学院，无比郑重地接了过去。你觉得自己老了。|表亲保证会好好收着。到晚饭时，它已经被换给邻居，换来一罐蜂蜜。|表亲道了谢，把它放进了自己床底下的盒子里。你觉得自己老了，也挺高兴。}\n\n@event break_home_family_debate\n# en: The Family Debate\ntitle [673f25] = 家庭辩论\n# en: {At breakfast the family is arguing, and someone says, \"Ask the scholar.\"|The argument has started without you. Everyone turns as you come down the stairs.|\"The scholar is up,\" someone announces. They've saved today's argument for you.} {season == \"winter\": {Is it colder than the year of the big snow?|Can you freeze a pond by thinking at it?|Can a wizard make the hens lay in winter?}|{Do swallows sleep at the bottom of rivers?|Will it rain on the fair, and can you stop it?|Is the old well really haunted, and should it be?}}\ntext [86f635] = {早饭时一家人在争论，有人说：“问问读书人。”|争论没等你就开始了。你下楼时，所有人都转过头来。|“读书人起来了。”有人宣布。他们把今天的争论留给了你。} {season == \"winter\": {今年是不是比大雪那年还冷？|光靠想就能把池塘冻住吗？|巫师能让母鸡冬天下蛋吗？}|{燕子是不是睡在河底？|集市那天会下雨吗，你能不能拦住？|那口老井真的闹鬼吗？该不该闹？}}\n# en: A breakfast table mid-argument, several relatives pointing at a student as if they were the deciding vote.\nscene [0af97b] = 争论正酣的早餐桌，几个亲戚指着一个学生，好像那是决定性的一票。\n# en: Settle it with what I've learned\nopt.left.label [650629] = 用学到的东西解决\n# en: {You explain it so clearly that the argument ends. There is a short, respectful silence, and then a new argument starts.|You settle it. An uncle demands to see it in writing. You write it on a napkin, and he keeps the napkin.|You settle it with a diagram on the back of the almanac. It's still pinned by the stove the next time you're home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [98ec0a] = {你讲得清清楚楚，争论结束了。一阵短暂而尊敬的沉默，然后新的争论开始了。|你解决了。一位叔叔要你写下来。你写在餐巾上，他把餐巾收了起来。|你在历书背面画了张图解决了。下次你回家时，它还钉在炉子边上。}\n# en: {You explain it perfectly, and they all decide the academy has taught you nonsense.|Halfway through you realise you're wrong. So does everyone else.|You're right, and you prove it, and nobody believes you, because the almanac says otherwise.}\nopt.left.say.2 [364c86] = {你讲得完美无缺，结果他们一致认定学院教了你一堆胡话。|讲到一半你发现自己错了。其他人也发现了。|你是对的，也证明了，可没人信你，因为历书上写的不一样。}\n# en: Refuse to take sides\nopt.right.label [146465] = 拒绝站队\n# en: {You butter your toast with great care and say, \"Hm.\" It does not stop anyone, but it does save you.|You change the subject to the neighbour's fence. It works, but now there are two arguments.|You say both sides have a point. Both sides turn on you. It's the most united the family has been all year.}\nopt.right.say.1 [97d4a0] = {你非常仔细地给吐司抹黄油，说了声“嗯”。没能阻止任何人，但救了你自己。|你把话题转到邻居家的篱笆上。奏效了，但现在有两场争论了。|你说双方都有道理。双方一起转向了你。这是全家一年里最团结的时候。}\n\n@event break_home_village_children\n# en: The Wizard Is Home\ntitle [167820] = 巫师回来了\n# en: {Word has spread that you're home from the wizard school. Half the village has found a reason to pass the gate.|The neighbours are back at the gate, with a cousin from the next village to show you off to.|The gate committee is in session. They've drawn up a list.} {season == \"winter\": They want more snow for the sledging.|They want it to stop being so hot.} {The baker has brought a jar, in case you make something worth keeping.|The miller's wife asks if you've ever met a dragon.|The blacksmith has a stick that might be a wand.}\ntext [ccbdc0] = {消息传开了，说你从巫师学校回来了。半个村子的人都找了个理由从门口经过。|邻居们又来到门口，还带了个邻村的表亲来看你。|门口委员会又开会了。他们列了一张清单。} {season == \"winter\": 他们想要更多的雪来滑雪橇。|他们想让天别这么热。} {面包师带了个罐子，万一你变出什么值得留着的东西。|磨坊主的妻子问你见没见过龙。|铁匠拿着一根棍子，说不定真是魔杖。}\n# en: A gaggle of villagers at a cottage gate in the morning, staring hopefully at a student in academy robes.\nscene [3660a2] = 早晨一群村民聚在小屋门口，满怀期待地盯着一个穿学院长袍的学生。\n# en: Show them some sparks\nopt.left.label [b1cdc8] = 给他们放点火花\n# en: {You make a little burst of light over the gate. The neighbours cheer and demand it again, and again.|You make a spark dance across the gatepost. The baker tries to catch it in her apron, or thinks she does, which is the same thing.|You work through the committee's list of sparks, in order. They tick each one off and ask for the list again tomorrow.}\nopt.left.say.1 [44bf13] = {你在门上方变出一小团光。邻居们欢呼起来，要你再来，再来，再来。|你让一颗火花在门柱上跳舞。面包师想用围裙兜住它，或者以为兜住了，那也一样。|你照着委员会的单子，一个一个放火花。他们看完一个就打个勾，还要你明天再来一遍。}\n# en: Teach them something real\nopt.right.label [f0ec48] = 教他们点真东西\n# en: {You explain how the light gets into a spell. They listen politely and then ask for sparks.|You show them how to sit still and listen to a candle. The old miller really hears it. You can tell.|You make the blacksmith's stick glow for a minute. It isn't a wand, but he'll never believe that now.}\nopt.right.say.1 [93d4e1] = {你讲光是怎么进到咒语里的。他们礼貌地听完，然后要火花。|你教他们静静坐着听一支蜡烛。老磨坊主真的听见了。你看得出来。|你让铁匠那根棍子亮了一分钟。它不是魔杖，可他现在再也不会信了。}\n\n@event break_work_holiday_rush\n# en: The Holiday Rush\ntitle [c1bbea] = 假日高峰\n# en: {The holidays bring the crowds.|This time you've learned to eat breakfast before the rush, not during it.|You can feel the rush coming an hour before it arrives.} {has(gig_newt): The Gilded Newt is packed to the beams.|{has(gig_pell): The queue at Pell & Daughters is out of the door.|{has(gig_post): The loft's shelves are stacked to the rafters with parcels.|{has(gig_library): The library is full of last-minute readers.|{has(gig_kitchens): The Refectory is feeding everyone who stayed, and all their visiting families.|{has(gig_grounds): The grounds are full of visitors walking off their holiday dinners, and every one of them has a question.|Everyone wants something, and now.}}}}}} {season == \"winter\": {Everyone wants it before Midwinter.|Wet boots, short tempers.|Everyone is in a hurry to be somewhere warm.}|{Everyone is sunburnt.|Travellers keep asking the way.|The heat slows everyone down except the queue.}}\ntext [acfd1b] = {假期带来了人潮。|这次你学会了在高峰前吃早饭，而不是在高峰中。|高峰还有一个钟头才到，你已经感觉到了。} {has(gig_newt): 镀金蝾螈酒馆挤得水泄不通。|{has(gig_pell): 佩尔父女药铺的队排到了门外。|{has(gig_post): 阁楼的架子上包裹一直堆到房梁。|{has(gig_library): 图书馆里全是临时抱佛脚的读者。|{has(gig_kitchens): 食堂要喂饱所有留校的人，还有他们来探望的家人。|{has(gig_grounds): 场地上全是吃完节日大餐出来散步的访客，每个人都有问题要问。|人人都想要点什么，而且现在就要。}}}}}} {season == \"winter\": {人人都想在仲冬前拿到。|湿靴子，坏脾气。|人人都急着去暖和的地方。}|{人人都晒得通红。|旅人不停地问路。|炎热让每个人都慢下来，除了队伍。}}\n# en: A holiday rush at a busy workplace, a student in the thick of it wearing the fixed smile of someone on their fourth hour of the same question.\nscene [0eb180] = 假期里忙碌的工作场所，一个学生身处人潮中央，挂着第四个钟头回答同一个问题的僵硬笑容。\n# en: Smile through it\nopt.left.label [35213b] = 笑着撑过去\n# en: {You smile until your face aches. Somebody tips you a coin and a peppermint.|You smile through the fourth complaint about the weather, as if it were your fault. Somehow the tips are better than ever.|You have a smile for every complaint now, sorted by type. The tips follow.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6aa1dc] = {你笑到脸发酸。有人给了你一枚小费和一颗薄荷糖。|第四个人来抱怨天气，好像是你的错，你照样笑着。不知怎么，小费比哪次都多。|现在你每种抱怨都备好了一种笑容，分门别类。小费随之而来。}\n# en: Take the heavy jobs instead\nopt.right.label [7fdbc4] = 改干重活\n# en: {You haul the heaviest loads all morning and talk to no one. Your arms complain, and nobody else does.|You take the dull, heavy work nobody wants. It's tiring and blissfully quiet.|You know where every heavy thing goes by now. You're done by noon, and nobody has spoken to you. Perfect.}\nopt.right.say.1 [020c37] = {你搬了一上午最沉的东西，一句话没说。胳膊在抱怨，别的谁也没抱怨。|你揽下了没人想干的又闷又重的活。累，但安静得幸福。|现在你知道每样重东西该放哪儿。不到中午就干完了，没人跟你说过一句话。完美。}\n\n@event break_work_opening_up\n# en: Opening Up\ntitle [307370] = 开门\n# en: {In the holidays you're trusted with the keys. You open up at dawn, alone.|You've learned to lift the door as you turn the key.|You could open up in your sleep, and once you nearly did.} {season == \"winter\": It's still dark, and the stove has gone out.|The sun is up and the street smells of warm stone.} {There's a note under the door from someone who wanted to come in at midnight.|A cat waits on the step as if it has an appointment.|The place is yours for half an hour.}\ntext [b1d7dc] = {假期里你被托付了钥匙。天一亮你独自开门。|你学会了转钥匙时顺手把门往上一抬。|你闭着眼都能开门，有一次差点真这么干了。} {season == \"winter\": 天还黑着，炉子夜里灭了。|太阳已经升起，街上有暖石头的气味。} {门缝下塞着一张字条，来自半夜想进来的人。|一只猫在台阶上等着，好像有预约。|这地方有半个钟头归你一个人。}\n# en: A student unlocking a shop door in the half-light of early morning, breath steaming, the street empty behind them.\nscene [7b5489] = 清晨微光中一个学生在开店门，呼出白气，身后的街道空无一人。\n# en: Do it all by the book\nopt.left.label [433ff5] = 一切按规矩来\n# en: {Swept, stocked and opened to the minute. The owner notices and slips you a little extra.|Everything is ready when the first customer arrives. Nobody notices but you. Still, the coin is good.|You open up to the minute, as always. The owner has stopped checking.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4593ee] = {扫好、摆好、准点开门。老板注意到了，悄悄多塞给你一点。|第一位客人来时一切就绪。除了你没人注意。不过钱是实在的。|你一如既往地准点开门。老板已经不再来查了。}\n# en: Steal a quiet half hour\nopt.right.label [48ac16] = 偷半个钟头清闲\n# en: {You drink tea by the window and watch the street wake up. It's the best part of the job.|You read a chapter with your feet up. Nobody ever knows.|There's a chair by the window that's yours now. Half an hour of nothing, and nobody the wiser.}\nopt.right.say.1 [73930f] = {你在窗边喝茶，看街道醒来。这是这份工作最好的部分。|你把脚翘起来读了一章。没人知道。|现在窗边有一把你专属的椅子。半个钟头什么也不干，谁也不知道。}\n# en: {The owner arrives early and finds you with your feet up. The half hour comes out of your wages.|The first customer arrives early and finds the door locked. They leave a note. So does the owner.|The owner has taken to arriving early just to check, and today it pays off. The half hour comes out of your wages.}\nopt.right.say.2 [36d6dc] = {老板来早了，看见你翘着脚。那半个钟头从工钱里扣了。|第一位客人来早了，发现门锁着。他留了张字条。老板也留了一张。|老板开始故意早到来查岗，今天被逮个正着。那半个钟头从工钱里扣了。}\n\n@event break_work_regular\n# en: The Holiday Regular\ntitle [4ae6e4] = 假期常客\n# en: {Every holiday has its regular. This one comes every morning and wants to talk.|The regular is back. They wave to you as if you were old friends.|The regular has started calling you by your first name.} {Today it's about their late husband's beard.|Today it's about a dragon they swear they saw as a girl.|Today it's about the year the lake froze, which may or may not have happened.} The queue behind them is getting longer.\ntext [e2a150] = {每个假期都有个常客。这一位每天早上都来，而且想聊天。|常客又来了。冲你挥手，像是老朋友。|常客开始直接叫你的名字了。} {今天聊的是过世丈夫的胡子。|今天聊的是一条龙，她发誓小时候见过。|今天聊的是湖结冰的那一年，那年也许有，也许没有。} 后面的队越排越长。\n# en: An elderly customer settling in for a long chat at a counter, a student behind it glancing at the queue.\nscene [4825ff] = 一位年长的客人在柜台前坐定准备长谈，柜台后的学生瞥着排队的人。\n# en: Let them finish the story\nopt.left.label [884a81] = 让他们把故事讲完\n# en: {You hear the whole story, standing, while the queue thins out to somewhere else. Your feet ache by the end. The regular leaves happy, and so, oddly, are you.|The story has an ending you didn't expect, and you'll think about it all day. You stood through all of it, and your back says so.|You know how their stories end by now, and you let them tell it anyway. The queue waits. Your feet don't forgive you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [ca67b9] = {你站着听完了整个故事，排队的人散去别家了。到最后你的脚都站疼了。常客高高兴兴地走了，奇怪的是，你也是。|故事的结局出乎意料，你会想一整天。你从头站到尾，腰都在抗议。|现在你已经知道对方的故事怎么结尾了，还是让对方讲完。队伍在等。你的脚可不原谅你。}\n# en: Hurry them gently along\nopt.right.label [7b4db3] = 温和地催一催\n# en: {You serve the queue and the regular leaves, mid-sentence, with a little wave. It shouldn't bother you as much as it does.|The queue is grateful. The regular says, \"Another time, then,\" and you both know they mean it.|You keep the queue moving and promise the regular the rest tomorrow. They hold you to it, every time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [890677] = {你招呼了排队的人，常客话说到一半，挥挥手走了。这事不该让你这么在意。|排队的人很感激。常客说：“那下回吧。”你们都知道这话是真心的。|你让队伍动起来，答应常客明天听完剩下的。每一次，对方都会记着要你兑现。}\n\n@event break_work_new_hire\n# en: The Holiday Hire\ntitle [ff5b6a] = 假期新人\n# en: {The boss has taken on a holiday hire and put them next to you.|Another holiday hire, another nervous face. You're the old hand now.|This break's new hire looks at you the way you once looked at everyone.} {They've already dropped something.|They ask whether the job is always like this. You don't answer.|They're very keen and very fast, and very nearly right.}\ntext [86103e] = {老板招了个假期临时工，安排在你旁边。|又一个临时工，又一张紧张的脸。现在你是老手了。|这个假期的新人看你的眼神，就像你当初看所有人。} {他们已经摔了个东西。|他们问这活儿是不是一直这样。你没回答。|他们很热心、很快，而且差一点就对了。}\n# en: A nervous new worker holding a tray at the wrong angle, a student leaning over to correct them.\nscene [c6ec03] = 一个紧张的新人用错误的角度端着托盘，一个学生探身过去纠正。\n# en: Show them the ropes\nopt.left.label [f00110] = 带带新人\n# en: {By noon they know where everything is and that the boss is all bark. You've lost a morning and gained a friend.|You teach them the trick with the till. They teach you a song. You both get told off for the song.|You show them everything you wish someone had shown you. By the end of the day they're showing the next one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [be1b0b] = {到中午他们知道了每样东西在哪，也知道老板只是嘴凶。你搭进一个上午，多了个朋友。|你教他们收银台的窍门。他们教你一首歌。你们俩因为那首歌一起挨了骂。|你把当初希望有人教你的全教给了他们。到下班时，他们已经在教下一个了。}\n# en: Let them learn the hard way\nopt.right.label [c3bb81] = 让他们吃点苦头\n# en: {They learn. Unfortunately, they learn by breaking a jar that the boss puts on your account.|You let them flounder. It takes them all day to find the stockroom, and you end up covering their shift anyway. The boss takes the stock they spoiled out of your pay.|They learn fast, mostly from your mistakes, which they found in the ledger. The boss bills you for the latest one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bf6511] = {他们学会了。可惜是靠打碎一个罐子学会的，老板记在了你账上。|你让他们自己摸索。他们找了一整天库房，最后还是你替他们顶了班。他们糟蹋的货，老板从你工钱里扣了。|他们学得很快，主要是从账本上你犯过的错里学的。老板把最新的一笔记在了你头上。}\n\n@event break_internship_lodgings\n# en: Breakfast at the Lodgings\ntitle [ae293d] = 寄宿处的早餐\n# en: {{member(intern_expedition): Your lodging for the placement is a tent that flaps all night.|{member(intern_field_station): Your lodging is a bunk in the station cabin, under a shelf of jars.|Your lodging is one small room and a breakfast you pay for.}}|By now you can make any lodging home in an evening, a book on the sill and your boots by the door.|You've slept in enough borrowed beds to know breakfast matters more than the mattress.} {season == \"winter\": {Your breath steams.|The water jug has frozen.|Ice on the inside of the window.}|{It's warm before sunrise.|A bird is shouting outside.|The window is open, and someone's breakfast is frying.}} {Breakfast is porridge.|Breakfast is porridge with opinions.|Breakfast is unnamed.}\ntext [dd9184] = {{member(intern_expedition): 这次实习的住处是一顶整夜扑扑作响的帐篷。|{member(intern_field_station): 你的住处是野外站小木屋里的一张铺位，头顶一排罐子。|你的住处是一个小房间，外加一顿要付钱的早餐。}}|如今不管什么住处，你一个晚上就能住成家：窗台上一本书，门边一双靴子。|你睡过的借来的床够多了，知道早餐比床垫要紧。} {season == \"winter\": {呼出的气冒着白烟。|水壶冻住了。|窗户里面结了冰。}|{太阳没出来就暖了。|外面有只鸟在大喊。|窗开着，不知谁的早饭在煎。}} {早餐是粥。|早餐是有主见的粥。|早餐是一样没人叫得出名的东西。}\n# en: A borrowed bed at dawn, boots by the door, a bowl of breakfast waiting and a notebook open on the blanket.\nscene [7aadaf] = 黎明时一张借来的床，靴子放在门边，一碗早餐等着，毯子上摊着一本笔记。\n# en: Eat a proper breakfast\nopt.left.label [f0ec45] = 好好吃顿早饭\n# en: {You eat slowly and listen to everyone's gossip. You arrive late enough to be noticed.|Fed, warm and five minutes late. Your supervisor looks at the clock and says nothing at all.|You eat everything and ask for the recipe. You're late, and your supervisor has started to expect it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dfef2d] = {你慢慢吃，听着各种闲话。到得晚了，晚到会被注意。|吃饱了，暖和了，晚了五分钟。导师看了看钟，什么也没说。|你吃得一干二净，还要了做法。你迟到了，导师已经开始预料到这一点。}\n# en: Skip it and go in early\nopt.right.label [cfe210] = 不吃了，早点去\n# en: {You're the first one in. You get through a pile of work before anyone else arrives, and your stomach growls through every meeting after.|You read the day's notes on the way. By lunchtime you're faint and well informed.|You go in early with an apple in your pocket. It's gone by the first meeting, and the hunger stays until supper.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5cc63c] = {你第一个到。别人来之前你已经处理掉一堆活，之后的每场会上你的肚子都在叫。|你在路上读当天的笔记。到午饭时你头晕眼花，消息灵通。|你揣着一个苹果提早去了。第一场会还没开完它就没了，饿意一直撑到晚饭。}\n\n@event break_internship_briefing\n# en: The Morning Briefing\ntitle [7aa6d4] = 晨间布置\n# en: {Your supervisor reads the day's tasks aloud, once, very fast.|You bring two pens now, and a spare for the intern beside you.|You've learned to read the briefing from the way your supervisor holds the list.} {One task makes no sense at all.|One task seems to mean the opposite of what it says.|The last task is mumbled into a cup of tea.} {final_year: It's your last placement, and you want it to count.|The others all write as if they understood.}\ntext [672368] = {导师把当天的任务大声念一遍，只念一次，念得飞快。|你现在带两支笔，还给旁边的实习生备一支。|你已经能从导师拿清单的姿势读出任务了。} {有一项任务完全说不通。|有一项任务似乎和字面意思正相反。|最后一项任务是对着茶杯嘟囔出来的。} {final_year: 这是最后一次实习，你想让它有分量。|其他人都写得好像全听懂了。}\n# en: A supervisor reading a list of tasks aloud to a row of interns, one of whom has stopped writing.\nscene [ba7fb6] = 导师对着一排实习生大声念任务清单，其中一个停下了笔。\n# en: Ask what it means\nopt.left.label [5dfe5f] = 问清楚是什么意思\n# en: {Your supervisor explains it again, slowly, while the other interns smirk. Two of them come to you at lunch to ask what it meant.|\"Good question,\" says your supervisor, in a tone that suggests it wasn't. You get the task right, anyway.|You ask before anyone else can pretend. Your supervisor answers, and three pens around you start writing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1d092e] = {导师又慢慢解释了一遍，其他实习生偷笑。午饭时有两个人跑来问你那是什么意思。|“好问题。”导师说，语气表明并不是。不管怎样，你把任务做对了。|你抢在别人装懂之前就问了。导师作了答，你周围三支笔开始动起来。}\n# en: Guess and get on with it\nopt.right.label [f42afd] = 猜一猜，先干起来\n# en: {You guess right. Your supervisor nods as if they never doubted you.|It turns out the odd task was \"take the rest of the morning off\". Lucky. You spend it reading in the archive.|You guess, and you've guessed this one before. The task is done before anyone else has finished asking.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a8aac1] = {你猜对了。导师点点头，好像从没怀疑过你。|原来那项古怪的任务是“上午剩下的时间休息”。运气不错。你在档案室里读了一上午。|你猜了，而这种你以前就猜过。别人还没问完，你已经做完了。}\n# en: {You guess wrong and spend the morning carefully doing the opposite of what was wanted.|You did the right task to the wrong thing. The wrong thing will never be the same again.|Your guess is confident, thorough and wrong. Your supervisor reads the result twice and asks you, kindly, to ask next time.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ba5ae5] = {你猜错了，一上午都在一丝不苟地做和要求相反的事。|你对错的东西做了对的任务。那样东西再也回不去了。|你猜得自信、周全，而且错了。导师把结果读了两遍，好心地让你下次先问。}\n\n@event break_internship_other_interns\n# en: The Other Interns\ntitle [ebc4c3] = 其他实习生\n# en: {There are other interns here, and they've already formed a supper table.|You've learned to find the supper table on the first day, and which seat isn't anyone's.|By now you can tell within a morning which intern is the one to sit beside.} {One has a cousin on the Conclave and mentions it hourly.|One is better than all of you, and very nice about it.|One has been here before and knows where the biscuits are.} {season == \"winter\": Supper is eaten in coats.|Supper is eaten in the last of the sun.}\ntext [d1c192] = {这里还有别的实习生，他们已经凑成了一张晚饭桌。|你学会了第一天就找到晚饭桌，还有哪个座位不属于任何人。|如今一上午你就能看出该坐在哪个实习生旁边。} {有一个的表亲在七塔议会，每小时提一次。|有一个比你们都强，而且人很好。|有一个以前来过，知道好饼干藏在哪。} {season == \"winter\": 晚饭是穿着外套吃的。|晚饭是晒着最后一点太阳吃的。}\n# en: A small group of interns eating supper together, one sitting slightly apart with a notebook.\nscene [719a03] = 几个实习生一起吃晚饭，一个拿着笔记本的人坐得稍远。\n# en: Join them for supper\nopt.left.label [a8d96d] = 和他们一起吃晚饭\n# en: {You chip in for the shared food and find out which of the supervisors are kind. Useful, and fun.|You talk shop until your supper is cold. It turns out everyone else is as lost as you are, which helps.|You sit beside the one who knows where the biscuits are. By pudding you know where everything else is too.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b0f35d] = {你凑钱买了大家分的吃食，打听到哪些导师心肠好。有用，也开心。|你们聊工作聊到饭都凉了。原来大家和你一样摸不着头脑，这让人宽心。|你坐到了那个知道饼干放哪儿的人旁边。到吃甜点时，你连别的东西在哪儿也都知道了。}\n# en: Eat alone with your notes\nopt.right.label [5262d8] = 独自看笔记吃饭\n# en: {You finish the reading for tomorrow. You hear laughter from the table, and you don't look up.|You get ahead of everyone. Nobody saves you a biscuit.|You eat with your notes again. Someone leaves a biscuit by your elbow without a word.}\nopt.right.say.1 [966ee9] = {你读完了明天的材料。桌子那边传来笑声，你没抬头。|你领先了所有人。没人给你留饼干。|你又一边吃饭一边看笔记。有人一言不发地在你手肘边放了块饼干。}\n\n@event break_internship_stipend\n# en: Stipend Day\ntitle [75f50a] = 发津贴的日子\n# en: {Stipend day. A small envelope, your name spelled almost right.|You've learned to budget the envelope before you open it.|Stipend day. You know what it's for before it arrives, and it still feels like a gift.} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {The supply cart has come, with a pedlar riding on top.|A pedlar has walked a very long way to sell you socks.|The pedlar is back, and has heard about you.}|{season == \"winter\": {There's a warm scarf in a shop window you pass.|A bookshop nearby has a sale on almanacs.|The baker on the corner has started putting one aside on stipend day.}|{A cart is selling lemon ices on the corner.|A bookshop nearby has put its sale out in the sun.|The baker on the corner sells the last of the cherry buns at noon.}}}\ntext [b62368] = {发津贴了。一个小信封，你的名字几乎拼对了。|你学会了在拆信封之前就把钱安排好。|发津贴的日子。钱还没到你就知道它的用处，可它仍然像一份礼物。} {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {补给车来了，车顶上坐着一个货郎。|一个货郎走了很远的路来卖袜子给你。|那个货郎又来了，而且听说过你。}|{season == \"winter\": {你路过的橱窗里有一条暖和的围巾。|附近的书店在打折卖年历。|街角的面包师开始在发津贴的日子给你留一个。}|{街角有辆小车在卖柠檬冰。|附近的书店把打折书摆到了太阳底下。|街角的面包师中午卖最后一批樱桃面包。}}}\n# en: A small envelope of coins on a desk, a student looking at it with the expression of someone doing sums.\nscene [8956ab] = 书桌上一个装着硬币的小信封，一个学生看着它，一脸在算账的表情。\n# en: Treat yourself\nopt.left.label [ebab6c] = 犒劳一下自己\n# en: {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {You buy the pedlar's thickest socks. Your feet have never been so grateful.|You buy a jar of honey from the pedlar and eat it with a spoon, in triumph.|You buy the pedlar's whole stock of liquorice. He says you're his best customer, and you probably are.}|{season == \"winter\": {You buy it. It's quite unnecessary, and the warmest you've felt all week.|You spend the lot before lunch and regret none of it.|You spend it on something warm and something sweet, and keep a single coin back for luck.}|{You spend it before the corner and don't regret a crown.|You take your purchase to a bench in the sun and stay until your nose burns.|You buy the last cherry bun and a book you don't need, and eat one while reading the other.}}}\nopt.left.say.1 [655edc] = {member(intern_expedition) or member(intern_field_station): {你买了货郎最厚的袜子。你的脚从没这么感激过。|你从货郎那儿买了一罐蜂蜜，用勺子吃，吃得扬眉吐气。|你把货郎的甘草糖全买了。他说你是他最好的主顾，大概真是。}|{season == \"winter\": {你买下了它。完全没必要，却是你这一周最暖和的时刻。|午饭前你就花光了，一点也不后悔。|你买了点暖和的、点甜的，留下一枚硬币图个吉利。}|{没走到街角你就花完了，一个克朗都不后悔。|你带着买来的东西坐到阳光下的长椅上，一直坐到鼻子晒伤。|你买了最后一个樱桃面包和一本用不着的书，一边吃一边读。}}}\n# en: Save it and borrow the book\nopt.right.label [034d5f] = 省下钱，借那本书\n# en: {You put the envelope away and borrow the book instead. It's the dullest thing you've ever read, and the most useful. You copy out half of it the first night.|The envelope goes under your mattress. You read the borrowed book, and the next time your supervisor mentions it you nod in the right places.|The envelope joins the others under the mattress. The pile is getting interesting, and so is the book.}\nopt.right.say.1 [374872] = {你把信封收好，改去借了那本书。这是你读过最枯燥的书，也最有用。第一晚你就抄了一半。|信封塞到了床垫下面。你读了借来的书，下次导师提到它时，你在该点头的地方点了头。|信封和其他的一起塞到了床垫下面。那一沓越来越可观，书也越来越有意思。}\n\n@event thesis_margin_notes\n# en: Notes in the Margin\ntitle [6ecc14] = 页边批注\n# en: {Professor Thorne returns your chapter. In the margin of page one he has written \"No.\"|Another chapter back. The margins say \"Why?\", \"Source?\" and, once, \"Hm.\"|Thorne's notes are getting shorter. You can't decide whether that's good.} {Page six has a single word underlined three times.|One note just says \"See me.\"|There's a tick on page nine. You stare at it for a while.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: The argument is nearly there, and he knows it.|There's a long way to go.}\ntext [f474bd] = {索恩教授把你的章节退了回来。第一页的页边上他写着：“不。”|又一章退回来了。页边写着“为什么？”“出处？”，还有一处：“嗯。”|索恩的批注越来越短。你拿不准这是不是好事。} {第六页有一个词被画了三道线。|有一条批注只写着“来见我”。|第九页有一个勾。你盯着它看了好一会儿。} {progress(thesis) >= 60: 论证就快成了，他也知道。|还有很长的路要走。}\n# en: A thesis draft returned with a professor's spiky handwriting crawling up every margin.\nscene [981dda] = 一份退回的论文草稿，教授尖利的字迹爬满了每一处页边。\n# en: Rewrite to his notes\nopt.left.label [cbc3e9] = 照他的批注改\n# en: {You rewrite every page he marked. The chapter is shorter and better, and you hate that he was right.|You take every note. When he reads it again he crosses out only one thing, and you could cry.|You rewrite to his notes without arguing now. It saves a week, and he's usually right, which still annoys you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c5eee1] = {他标过的每一页你都重写了。这一章更短、更好，你恨他是对的。|你采纳了每一条批注。他再读时只划掉了一处，你差点哭出来。|现在你照他的批注改，不再争了。省了一个星期，而他通常是对的，这依旧让你恼火。}\n# en: Defend my version\nopt.right.label [6497f5] = 为我的版本辩护\n# en: {You make your case at his office door. He listens, says \"Hm,\" and crosses out his own note.|You argue for your paragraph and he lets you keep it. \"Then make it say so,\" he says, which is almost praise.|You defend the paragraph with a source he hasn't read. He reads it overnight and gives you the paragraph.}\nopt.right.say.1 [feebbe] = {你在他办公室门口陈述理由。他听完，说了声“嗯”，划掉了自己的批注。|你为自己那一段据理力争，他让你留下了。“那就让它把话说清楚。”他说，这几乎算是夸奖。|你拿一份他没读过的资料为那段辩护。他连夜读完，把那段留给了你。}\n# en: {You argue. Thorne waits until you've finished, then points at the sentence that proves him right.|He hears you out, then writes a new note in the margin. It says \"No.\" again, in smaller letters.|You argue. He agrees with every point and still writes \"No.\" You've begun to find this comforting.}\nopt.right.say.2 [e68279] = {你争了。索恩等你说完，然后指着证明他是对的那一句。|他听你说完，在页边写了一条新批注。还是“不。”，字小了一号。|你争了。他每一条都同意，可还是写下“不。”你开始觉得这有点让人安心。}\n\n@event thesis_carrel_neighbour\n# en: The Next Carrel\ntitle [1fe6fe] = 隔壁隔间\n# en: {Your carrel neighbour, a final-year from Rootward, reads aloud under their breath.|Your carrel neighbour now reads your chapter titles aloud too.|You and your carrel neighbour now speak a language of sighs.} {They're stuck on chapter three.|They've found a source that disagrees with you both.|They pass you half a sandwich over the partition.} {season == \"winter\": The thesis lab is cold enough for gloves.|Someone's notes are escaping through the window.}\ntext [3b1774] = {你的隔间邻居是一位根庐的毕业年级生，总在小声念书。|你的隔间邻居现在连你的章节标题也念出声来。|你和隔间邻居现在用叹气交流。} {他们卡在第三章了。|他们找到一份和你俩都唱反调的资料。|他们从隔板上方递给你半个三明治。} {season == \"winter\": 论文实验室冷得要戴手套。|有人的笔记正从窗口往外逃。}\n# en: Two thesis carrels side by side, the partition between them covered in pinned notes and a shared candle.\nscene [e1d83d] = 两个并排的论文隔间，中间的隔板上钉满了笔记，还有一支共用的蜡烛。\n# en: Swap drafts for an hour\nopt.left.label [d31846] = 交换草稿看一小时\n# en: {They find the hole in your argument in ten minutes. You find three in theirs. Everyone feels better and worse.|They read your chapter and say, \"Oh, I see.\" You're not sure you did, until now.|You swap drafts over the partition, sandwich crumbs and all. Both chapters come back better.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e140b0] = {他们十分钟就找到了你论证里的漏洞。你在他们的里面找到三个。大家都觉得好受了些，也难受了些。|他们读完你的章节，说：“哦，我明白了。”在这之前你都不确定自己明白。|你们隔着隔板交换草稿，连带着三明治屑。两章都改得更好了。}\n# en: {They love your chapter, which helps nobody. You spend the hour fixing their commas.|They ask so many questions that you start doubting your title.|Their notes on your chapter are longer than your chapter. You lose the afternoon reading them.}\nopt.left.say.2 [792da9] = {他们很喜欢你的章节，这对谁都没帮助。你花了一小时改他们的逗号。|他们问了太多问题，你开始怀疑起自己的标题。|他们给你那一章写的批注比那一章还长。你搭进一下午读这些批注。}\n# en: Hunt a source in the stacks\nopt.right.label [714cad] = 去书库找资料\n# en: {You find it on the bottom shelf, under a dictionary and a sleeping cat. It says exactly what you hoped, in a footnote.|Three hours of climbing ladders for one paragraph. It's a very good paragraph.|You find the source and a better one beside it. You bring both back and share the second.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1030e2] = {你在最底层书架上找到了它，压在一本词典和一只睡着的猫下面。它说的正是你希望的，在一条脚注里。|爬了三个钟头梯子换来一段话。是非常好的一段话。|你找到了那份资料，旁边还有一份更好的。你把两份都带了回来，把第二份分给了对方。}\n\n@event thesis_is_it_good\n# en: Is It Any Good?\ntitle [cfa126] = 写得好吗？\n# en: {Halfway through rereading your thesis, you can't tell if it's brilliant or obvious.|The doubt has learned your timetable. It comes the morning after good work.|You know this doubt now. It reaches your carrel first.} {progress(thesis) >= 60: Most of it is written, which almost makes it worse.|So much of it is still unwritten.} {final_year and season == \"spring\": After this term there are no more terms.|{First-years laugh in the courtyard below.|Last night's candle is a puddle.|Someone two carrels down sighs the same sigh.}}\ntext [c8d24e] = {重读论文到一半，你分不清它是精彩还是平庸。|怀疑摸清了你的作息。它总在干得不错的一夜之后的早晨出现。|你认得这种怀疑了。它比你先到隔间。} {progress(thesis) >= 60: 大部分已经写完，这几乎让事情更糟。|还有太多没写。} {final_year and season == \"spring\": 这学期过后，就再也没有学期了。|{楼下庭院里一年级生在笑。|昨晚的蜡烛化成了一摊。|隔两个隔间的人叹出了一模一样的气。}}\n# en: A student at a carrel in early light, holding a thick draft at arm's length as if it might bite.\nscene [43c23c] = 清晨的隔间里，一个学生把厚厚的草稿举得远远的，好像它会咬人。\n# en: Push on regardless\nopt.left.label [b83a2c] = 不管了，继续写\n# en: {You write a page you don't believe in. By lunch you believe in about half of it, which is a start.|You keep going. The doubt keeps you company, and gets bored by evening.|You write through it, as you have before. By evening the doubt has gone quiet, and the page is good.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bf5167] = {你写了一页自己都不信的东西。到午饭时你信了大约一半，算是个开始。|你接着写。怀疑一直陪着你，到傍晚它自己也腻了。|你像以前一样写过去。到傍晚怀疑安静了，那一页也写得不错。}\n# en: Walk by the lake instead\nopt.right.label [ff9e77] = 改去湖边散步\n# en: {You walk around the lake and come back with no new pages and one good idea. You'll find out tomorrow if it's good.|The lake is mirror-still and has no opinion on your thesis. It's a relief.|You walk the long way round the lake. The doubt can't keep up, and you come back with a sentence that works.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fcedbe] = {你绕湖走了一圈，回来时没有新页，只有一个好主意。明天就知道它好不好了。|湖面如镜，对你的论文毫无意见。真是解脱。|你绕湖走了远路。怀疑跟不上你，你带着一句好用的句子回来了。}\n\n@event thesis_lab_spare_carrel\n# en: The Spare Carrel\ntitle [59d7e8] = 空着的隔间\n# en: {There's an empty carrel at the end of the row, with a candle stub and nobody's name on it.|The spare carrel is still unclaimed. You've started thinking of its candle stub as yours.|You know the carrels by their scratches now. The spare one has a new scratch, and you suspect it's yours.} {season == \"winter\": Everyone writes in fingerless gloves.|The windows are open, and a footnote blows off someone's desk.} {Somewhere a quill scratches without stopping.|Someone is whispering \"however\" over and over.|A candle gutters out three carrels down, and someone groans.}\ntext [434cc4] = {这排尽头有个空隔间，只有一截蜡烛头，没写任何人的名字。|那个空隔间还是没人认领。你开始把那截蜡烛头当成自己的了。|你现在能凭划痕认出每个隔间。空着的那个多了一道新划痕，你怀疑是你划的。} {season == \"winter\": 人人都戴着露指手套写字。|窗户开着，一条脚注从别人桌上飞走了。} {某处一支羽毛笔沙沙写个不停。|有人在一遍遍低声念着“然而”。|隔三个隔间的蜡烛灭了，有人哀叹了一声。}\n# en: A long room of wooden carrels, one at the end empty but for a candle stub and a stray page of footnotes.\nscene [9557dc] = 一长排木制研读隔间，尽头那个空着，只有一截蜡烛头和一页散落的脚注。\n# en: Work until the candle dies\nopt.left.label [6a43fb] = 写到蜡烛燃尽\n# en: {You write until the stub drowns in its own wax. Your notes are good, and your neck will remember them.|You read one book and half of another. The room's silence does half the work for you.|You work until the stub gives out and finish the paragraph by feel. It's better than the ones you could see.}\nopt.left.say.1 [244afc] = {你一直写到烛头淹没在自己的蜡油里。笔记很好，脖子会记住它们。|你读完一本书，又读了半本。屋里的安静替你干了一半的活。|你一直干到烛头熄灭，摸着黑写完了那一段。比看得见时写的那些还好。}\n# en: Just sit in the quiet\nopt.right.label [fbb3b9] = 只是在安静里坐坐\n# en: {You sit with your hands in your lap and listen to everyone else think. It's oddly restful.|You doze off with your cheek on someone's old footnotes. Nobody minds.|You sit in the quiet and the argument rearranges itself while you're not looking. You write it down before it changes its mind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9e69a4] = {你双手放在膝上，听着别人思考。出奇地让人放松。|你脸贴着别人的旧脚注睡着了。没人介意。|你坐在安静里，论证趁你不注意自己理顺了。你赶在它改主意之前把它记了下来。}\n# en: {An advisor looks into the carrel and asks what you're working on. \"Thinking,\" you say. He writes that down.|A final-year asks if you'd mind the corner. Their own candle has given out.|An advisor finds you sitting still and lectures you on idleness for exactly as long as you'd have sat.}\nopt.right.say.2 [a0d748] = {一位导师探头问你在研究什么。“思考。”你说。他把这记了下来。|一位毕业年级生问你介不介意让出角落。他们自己的蜡烛燃尽了。|一位导师发现你坐着不动，就“游手好闲”给你训了一通话，时长正好等于你本打算坐的时间。}\n\n@event thesis_lab_footnote_war\n# en: The Footnote War\ntitle [e57e26] = 脚注之战\n# en: {Two advisors are arguing about a footnote across three carrels, and every final-year in the room has stopped pretending to work.|The footnote war has broken out again. Someone has drawn up sides on a scrap of paper.|By now the footnote war has rules, a referee and a running score chalked on the door.} {Today it's about whether an old founders' letter counts as a source.|Today it's about a comma.|Today it's about a footnote to a footnote.}\ntext [985478] = {两位导师隔着三个隔间为一条脚注争吵，屋里每个毕业年级生都不再假装干活了。|脚注之战又打响了。有人在纸片上分好了阵营。|如今脚注之战有了规则、裁判，门上还用粉笔记着比分。} {今天争的是创始者的一封旧信算不算出处。|今天争的是一个逗号。|今天争的是一条脚注的脚注。}\n# en: Two advisors arguing across a row of carrels, final-year students peering over the partitions to watch.\nscene [0da858] = 两位导师隔着一排隔间争吵，毕业年级生们从隔板上方探头观战。\n# en: Weigh in with a source\nopt.left.label [52a73e] = 拿出处加入战局\n# en: {You produce the source, open at the right page. Both advisors fall silent, and one of them asks for your name.|Your citation settles it. The loser shakes your hand and then rereads the page for an hour.|Your source wins the round. The referee chalks a point on the door under a new column, which has your name on it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [131400] = {你拿出那份资料，正翻在对的那一页。两位导师都安静了，其中一位问了你的名字。|你的引文终结了争论。输的一方和你握了手，然后把那一页重读了一个钟头。|你的资料赢下了这一回合。裁判在门上新开了一栏记上一分，栏头写着你的名字。}\n# en: {Your source turns out to be the one they were both arguing against. They unite, briefly, to explain why.|You cite the wrong edition. The room winces as one.|Your source is ruled out of order by the referee, on a technicality you'll be studying for weeks.}\nopt.left.say.2 [69773d] = {你的资料恰好是他们俩都在反对的那份。他们短暂地联手，向你解释为什么。|你引错了版本。全屋人齐齐一缩。|裁判以一条技术理由判你的资料无效，那条理由够你研究好几个星期。}\n# en: Keep my head down and listen\nopt.right.label [a9d2c8] = 低头听着\n# en: {You learn more about sources from ten minutes of the war than from a term of lectures, and you never have to say a word.|You take notes on both sides. Neither of them would like how fair your notes are.|You keep your head down and your notebook open. The war is the best seminar in the building, and it's free.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fa65d3] = {十分钟的战事教你的出处知识比一学期的课还多，而你一句话都不用说。|你把双方的论点都记了下来。他们谁都不会喜欢你记得这么公允。|你埋着头，开着笔记本。这场战争是整栋楼里最好的研讨课，还不收钱。}\n\n@event thesis_lab_borrowed_candle\n# en: Borrowing a Candle\ntitle [7f13b4] = 借一支蜡烛\n# en: {The candles here give out at the worst moment. Yours just has, and the carrel next door has a full box and nobody in it.|Your candle gives out again. Next door, the usual box of spares and nobody in the chair.|The lab's candles have outlasted no one's patience tonight, least of all yours. There's a box next door.} {A draft lies open beside the box, face up.|A note on the box says \"Help yourself\" in one hand and \"Don't\" in another.|Someone has drawn a small eye on the box.}\ntext [e34b72] = {这里的蜡烛总在最要命的时候燃尽。你的刚灭，隔壁隔间有一整盒，人却不在。|你的蜡烛又灭了。隔壁照例放着一盒备用的，椅子上没人。|今晚实验室的蜡烛没能比任何人的耐心撑得更久，尤其是你的。隔壁有一盒。} {盒子旁边摊着一份草稿，正面朝上。|盒子上一张字条写着“请自取”，另一种笔迹写着“别”。|有人在盒子上画了一只小眼睛。}\n# en: A dark carrel lit by one dying candle, a full box of candles on the empty desk next door.\nscene [a34fc6] = 一支将熄的蜡烛照着昏暗的隔间，隔壁空桌上放着满满一盒蜡烛。\n# en: Borrow one and keep working\nopt.left.label [178881] = 借一支接着干\n# en: {You light it and work until the bell. You leave a note that says \"IOU one candle\", unsigned.|One candle, two good hours. You put the stub back, which is either honest or worse.|You borrow one and replace it the next morning with two. The box owner has started leaving the lid open.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e699bf] = {你点上它一直干到钟响。你留了张字条：“欠蜡烛一支”，没署名。|一支蜡烛，两个好钟头。你把烛头放了回去，这要么算诚实，要么更糟。|你借了一支，第二天早上还了两支。盒子的主人开始把盖子开着了。}\n# en: {The owner comes back for a forgotten scarf and finds you lit by their candle. They say nothing, which is worse.|By morning the whole lab knows who borrowed the candle. Someone leaves a box on your desk, with a ribbon.|The box is counted now, and the count is short. A note appears asking the borrower to come forward. You don't.}\nopt.left.say.2 [fad0d1] = {主人回来取忘下的围巾，发现你正借着他们的烛光。他们什么也没说，这更糟。|到早上全实验室都知道是谁借了蜡烛。有人在你桌上放了一盒，还系了丝带。|那盒蜡烛现在有人数了，数目不对。一张字条出现了，请借蜡烛的人站出来。你没有。}\n# en: Go back in the dark\nopt.right.label [48d592] = 摸黑回去\n# en: {You walk back across the bridges in the dark, your argument going round and round. It is better by the time you reach your bed.|You stub your toe twice on the way out. The idea you had on the stairs was worth both.|You know the way back in the dark by now. The argument walks with you and arrives a step ahead.}\nopt.right.say.1 [330b06] = {你摸黑走过光之桥，论证在脑子里转个不停。走到床边时它已经好多了。|出门路上你踢到两次脚趾。楼梯上冒出的那个主意值这两下。|现在你摸黑也认得回去的路。论证陪你一路走着，比你早一步到。}\n\n@event intern_royal_library_slips\n# en: Request Slips\ntitle [75d659] = 借阅单\n# en: {Scholars upstairs send their requests down a brass chute. Your job is to fetch them from the stacks, which go down for miles.|You've worn a path in the dust of the third landing.|You can read the chute's rattle now. A long rattle means someone wants something from the very bottom.} {One slip is in a hand so old the ink has gone brown.|One slip asks for \"the blue one\".|One slip is a love letter, sent down by mistake.}\ntext [4e976f] = {楼上的学者把借阅单从黄铜滑槽送下来。你的工作是去书库里取书，而书库往下延伸好几里。|你在第三层平台的灰尘里踩出了一条路。|你现在听得懂滑槽的响声了。长长的一声，意味着有人要最底层的东西。} {有张单子的笔迹古老得墨水都变褐了。|有张单子写着“蓝色那本”。|有张单子是封情书，寄错了地方。}\n# en: A brass chute dropping paper slips into a basket at the top of a deep stairwell lined with shelves.\nscene [7b85db] = 一道黄铜滑槽把纸条投进篮子，篮子放在一口排满书架的深井楼梯顶上。\n# en: Take the deep requests\nopt.left.label [879821] = 接最深处的单子\n# en: {Four hundred steps down, the air is cold enough to keep books for ever. You come back up with the book and a lot of opinions about stairs.|You find the volume wedged behind a pipe. The scholar who asked for it nearly weeps.|You take the bottom slip without looking at it. Down there, you know the shelves better than the catalogue does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b77c1b] = {往下四百级台阶，空气冷得书能永远保存。你带着那本书和一肚子对楼梯的意见回到上面。|你在一根管子后面找到了那本书。要它的学者差点哭了。|你看都不看就接了最底层的单子。在下面，你比目录更熟悉那些书架。}\n# en: Take the near shelves\nopt.right.label [ee4e7d] = 只取近处书架的\n# en: {You fetch the easy ones and leave the deep slips for someone else. Someone else notices.|You're fast and pleasant, and today the third landing is as deep as you go.|You clear the near shelves twice over. The long rattles go to someone else, who looks at you as they pass.}\nopt.right.say.1 [74684a] = {你取了容易的，把深处的单子留给别人。别人注意到了。|你又快又和气，今天最深只下到第三层平台。|你把近处的书架清了两遍。长响的单子归了别人，对方经过时看了你一眼。}\n\n@event intern_royal_library_mending\n# en: The Mending Table\ntitle [1b5cc4] = 修补台\n# en: {Today you're at the mending table, where crumbling records are put back together one flake at a time.|The binder hands you the tweezers without a word, which counts as a greeting.|The binder hands you one of the older pages today, which are the worst.} {The page is a royal decree on the correct length of goose necks.|The page is a recipe, half eaten by something.|The page is a list of names, one crossed out very hard.}\ntext [06c539] = {今天你在修补台，把碎裂的档案一片一片拼回去。|装订师一言不发地把镊子递给你，这就算打招呼了。|装订师今天交给你一页更老的书页，那些最难。} {这页是一道王室法令，规定鹅脖子的正确长度。|这页是一份食谱，被什么东西啃掉了一半。|这页是一份名单，有一个名字被狠狠划掉了。}\n# en: A bright worktable under a lamp, a crumbling manuscript laid out in pieces and a student holding fine tweezers.\nscene [cb79c7] = 灯下一张明亮的工作台，一份碎裂的手稿摊成好几块，一个学生拿着细镊子。\n# en: Mend it myself\nopt.left.label [67b19a] = 我自己来修\n# en: {Every flake goes back where it belongs. The binder holds it up to the lamp and says, \"Acceptable,\" which here is a medal.|You save the page and read it while the glue dries. It's better than you expected.|You put the list back together, crossed-out name and all. The binder reads the name and says nothing for a while.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f4c658] = {每一片都回到了原处。装订师把它举到灯下说：“还行。”在这里，这就是勋章。|你救下了这一页，趁胶水干的时候读了它。比你想的有意思。|你把名单拼了回去，连那个被划掉的名字也拼好了。装订师读了那个名字，好一会儿没说话。}\n# en: {A sneeze. The binder looks at the scattered flakes and then at you. You both get the tweezers.|One flake ends up in the wrong century. Nobody can prove it was you, but everybody knows.|The oldest flake crumbles at your touch. The binder writes \"lost in repair\" in the ledger, in your hand.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a2111d] = {一个喷嚏。装订师看看散落的碎片，又看看你。你们俩一起拿起了镊子。|有一片被放进了错误的世纪。没人能证明是你，但人人都知道。|最老的一片一碰就碎了。装订师让你亲手在账簿上写下“修复中遗失”。}\n# en: Watch the binder work\nopt.right.label [5b7d5a] = 看装订师干活\n# en: {You watch for an hour and learn more than a book could tell you. The binder says you're slow. You were.|The binder works without speaking and you learn by watching. It's very peaceful and very little gets done.|You watch the binder mend the page you'd have ruined. Next time, you'll know how. That's the idea.}\nopt.right.say.1 [45e807] = {你看了一个钟头，学到的比书上能教的多。装订师说你慢。你确实慢。|装订师一言不发地干活，你在一旁看着学。非常宁静，也几乎没干成什么。|你看着装订师修好了那页你本会弄坏的纸。下次你就会了。这正是用意所在。}\n\n@event intern_conclave_test_chamber\n# en: The Test Chamber\ntitle [cb5a52] = 测试室\n# en: {The Conclave's researchers need someone to cast their newest spell for the first time. The walls are thick for a reason.|Someone has chalked a new circle on the floor. The old one is scorched.|You know every scorch mark in the chamber by now, and who made each one.} {Today's spell is meant to make a candle burn backwards.|Today's spell is meant to tidy a room. It has opinions about what tidy means.|Today's spell is unnamed. Nobody will say why.}\ntext [d61ad5] = {议会的研究员们需要有人第一次施放他们最新的咒语。墙那么厚是有原因的。|有人在地上用粉笔画了个新圈。旧的那个已经焦了。|如今测试室里每道焦痕你都认得，也知道是谁留下的。} {今天的咒语据说能让蜡烛倒着烧。|今天的咒语据说能整理房间。它对“整理”有自己的看法。|今天的咒语没有名字。没人肯说为什么。}\n# en: A circular chamber with scorch-marked walls, a student at the centre with a wand raised and researchers watching through thick glass.\nscene [033a72] = 一间墙上满是焦痕的圆形测试室，一个学生站在中央举着魔杖，研究员们隔着厚玻璃观看。\n# en: Step into the chamber\nopt.left.label [2de272] = 走进测试室\n# en: {The spell works on the first try. Behind the glass, someone writes your name in the margin of their notes.|It works, more or less, for one glorious minute before the spell remembers itself.|The spell works, and nobody behind the glass will say what it was for. They thank you very politely.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f386b1] = {咒语一次成功。玻璃后面有人在笔记页边写下了你的名字。|基本成功了，光辉的一分钟之后，咒语才想起自己是谁。|咒语成功了，玻璃后面没人肯说它是做什么用的。他们非常客气地向你道谢。}\n# en: {The spell goes off sideways. Your eyebrows are fine. Your fringe has views of its own now, and the backlash leaves your hands stinging for a day.|The spell goes off sideways, and the jolt of it leaves you dizzy until supper. Your robes will never fold the same way again.|The spell goes off sideways, you take the edge of the blast on your forearm, and it adds a new scorch mark to the wall. Someone labels it with your name.}\nopt.left.say.2 [49240f] = {咒语往旁边炸开了。你的眉毛没事。你的刘海从此有了自己的主见，反冲让你的手刺痛了一整天。|咒语往旁边炸开了，那一下震得你直到晚饭都头晕。你的袍子再也叠不回原来的样子。|咒语往旁边炸开了，爆炸的边缘擦过你的前臂，在墙上添了一道新焦痕。有人在旁边标上了你的名字。}\n# en: Take readings behind the glass\nopt.right.label [531746] = 在玻璃后记读数\n# en: {You write down numbers for three hours while someone else gets to be brave. They're very good numbers.|You note every flicker. A senior researcher reads your page and asks for your notes next time.|You take the readings for a spell nobody will name. The numbers are strange, and you're asked to forget them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b1070e] = {别人去当勇士，你记了三个钟头数字。都是非常好的数字。|你记下了每一次闪烁。一位资深研究员读了你的记录，要你下次把笔记给他。|你为一个没人肯说出名字的咒语记录读数。数字很奇怪，有人请你把它们忘掉。}\n\n@event intern_conclave_minutes\n# en: Taking the Minutes\ntitle [3271ff] = 做会议记录\n# en: {You've been asked to take the minutes of a Conclave research committee. There are nine members, and none of them ever agrees.|The committee is still arguing about last time's question, and now about the minutes too.|You could take these minutes in your sleep, and some of the committee do attend them that way.} {Two of them are not speaking to each other, and want it recorded.|One of them asks what you think.|One of them falls asleep, and wakes up to vote.}\ntext [eece1c] = {你被派去给议会的一个研究委员会做记录。委员有九位，从没有意见一致过。|委员会还在吵上次的问题，现在连上次的记录也一起吵。|这记录你闭着眼都能做，有几位委员也确实是闭着眼出席的。} {其中两位互不说话，并要求记录在案。|其中一位问你怎么看。|其中一位睡着了，醒来正好投票。}\n# en: A long table of elderly mages in heavy robes, all talking at once, a young intern writing furiously at the end.\nscene [998689] = 一张长桌旁坐满穿厚袍的年长法师，全都在同时说话，桌尾一个年轻实习生在拼命记录。\n# en: Offer an opinion when asked\nopt.left.label [48ef1a] = 被问到时说出看法\n# en: {The table goes silent. Then the oldest mage says, \"The intern is right,\" and the rest pretend they agreed all along.|Your opinion settles the argument. It also starts two new ones.|You give your opinion and the sleeper wakes up to agree with it. It carries, seven to two.}\nopt.left.say.1 [299c8d] = {桌边鸦雀无声。然后最年长的法师说：“实习生说得对。”其余人假装自己早就同意。|你的看法平息了争论。也引发了两场新的。|你说出了看法，睡着的那位醒来表示同意。七比二通过。}\n# en: {Nine heads turn towards you, slowly. You say something about wands. It isn't minuted.|You give your opinion and it is recorded in full, with the note \"(intern)\".|Your opinion is noted, discussed, voted down and then adopted next meeting without credit.}\nopt.left.say.2 [0d59e7] = {九颗脑袋慢慢转向你。你说了点关于魔杖的话。没被记录。|你的看法被完整记录在案，后面注着“（实习生）”。|你的看法被记录、讨论、否决，然后在下次会上被采纳，没提你的名字。}\n# en: Keep my head down and write\nopt.right.label [1678b2] = 低头只管记录\n# en: {Your minutes are perfect and so dull that nobody reads them, which in a committee is the highest praise.|You record every word of the argument. Your hand aches, and you understand the Conclave much better than you wanted to.|You write it all down, including the vote of the member who slept through it. The minutes are accurate, which nobody wanted.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bf8a20] = {你的记录完美无缺，枯燥到没人读，在委员会里这是最高的赞誉。|你记下了争论的每一个字。手腕发酸，对议会的了解远超你的本意。|你把一切都记了下来，包括那位睡了全程的委员的投票。记录很准确，可这正是谁都不想要的。}\n\n@event intern_alchemists_vat_watch\n# en: Vat Watch\ntitle [6ef11f] = 看守大缸\n# en: {Your task at the works is to watch one great vat and call the foreman if it changes colour.|You've brought your own colour chart now, with notes in the margins.|You and the vat understand each other now. It's an unsettling feeling.} {The amber is drifting, very slowly.|There's a bubble that keeps coming back, like a thought.|The vat smells faintly of cinnamon, which it never has before.} The foreman is all the way at the other end of the hall.\ntext [065445] = {你在工坊的任务是看守一口大缸，一旦变色就去叫工头。|你现在自带色卡，页边写满了笔记。|你和大缸现在心意相通。这感觉让人不安。} {琥珀色正在极慢地漂移。|有个气泡一直冒回来，像一个念头。|大缸有一丝肉桂味，以前从没有过。} 工头在大厅的另一头。\n# en: A huge copper vat in a steaming hall, a student on a gantry staring into it with a colour chart in hand.\nscene [e8908d] = 蒸汽弥漫的大厅里一口巨大的铜缸，一个学生站在吊台上拿着色卡盯着缸里。\n# en: Adjust it myself\nopt.left.label [76449d] = 我自己调\n# en: {A pinch of salt and a slow stir, and the vat settles back to its proper self. The foreman never knew, but the vat did.|You correct it before it goes wrong. When the foreman passes, he gives the vat a satisfied look as if he did it.|You trace the cinnamon to a cracked spice valve and close it. The vat settles, and the foreman finds the valve next week.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a41b7a] = {一撮盐，慢慢搅动，大缸恢复了本来的样子。工头永远不会知道，但大缸知道。|你在出事之前就纠正了。工头路过时满意地看了大缸一眼，好像是他干的。|你顺着肉桂味找到了一个裂开的香料阀，把它关上了。大缸平静下来，工头下个星期才发现那个阀门。}\n# en: {The vat turns a brilliant violet and hisses. The foreman arrives at a run, and you'll be hearing about it for weeks.|You add too much salt. The vat sulks a muddy brown until the foreman coaxes it back, at length, with you watching.|You make it worse. A lot worse. The whole hall smells of cinnamon until the end of the week.}\nopt.left.say.2 [816115] = {大缸变成鲜亮的紫罗兰色，嘶嘶作响。工头一路跑来，这事你要被念叨好几个星期。|你盐放多了。大缸赌气似的变成了泥褐色，工头哄了半天才把它哄回来，你在旁边看着。|你把它弄得更糟。糟得多。整座大厅到周末都是肉桂味。}\n# en: Run for the foreman\nopt.right.label [4b3ef4] = 跑去叫工头\n# en: {You run the length of the hall. The foreman fixes it in a second and explains how, at length, as you catch your breath.|By the time you're back with the foreman the colour has corrected itself. He stares at you, then at the vat.|You run for the foreman, who sniffs the air, says \"Cinnamon,\" and goes very pale. He fixes it, and doesn't explain.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ca9c22] = {你跑完整个大厅。工头一秒钟就修好了，趁你喘气时详详细细地讲了怎么修。|等你带着工头回来，颜色已经自己恢复了。他盯着你，又盯着大缸。|你跑去叫工头。他嗅了嗅空气，说了句“肉桂”，脸色发白。他修好了，没解释。}\n\n@event intern_alchemists_no_flame\n# en: The Rule About Flames\ntitle [a32af9] = 关于明火的规定\n# en: {There's a strict rule at the works against naked flames, and against any heating reaction the foreman hasn't approved. The break room has spent forty years looking for one he will.|Break time. The kettle sits on its warm stone, and the workers look at you hopefully.|By now the break room asks the academy intern first, whatever the problem.} {Today the warm stone has gone cold.|Today someone wants toast, which is a much bigger question.|Today a new inspector is touring the works.}\ntext [37d043] = {工坊有严格规定：不许明火，也不许用工头没批准的加热反应。休息室花了四十年，想找出一个他肯批的。|休息时间。水壶坐在温热的石头上，工人们满怀希望地看着你。|如今休息室不管遇到什么问题，都先问学院来的实习生。} {今天那块温石凉了。|今天有人想吃烤面包，这可是大得多的问题。|今天新来的巡查员在视察工坊。}\n# en: A break room in an alchemical works, workers gathered around a kettle sitting on a glowing stone that is not, technically, on fire.\nscene [03a5ac] = 炼金工坊的休息室，工人们围着一把放在发光石头上的水壶，严格说来那并不算着火。\n# en: Help brew the flameless tea\nopt.left.label [823819] = 帮忙泡无火茶\n# en: {You heat the kettle with a reaction you learned in your first year. The break room calls it \"the academy method\" and adopts it on the spot.|It works, though everything boiled on it comes out a little purple.|You brew the tea with the new inspector in the room, and explain the method so confidently that he takes notes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b5e9da] = {你用一年级学的一个反应把水壶烧热了。休息室管它叫“学院法”，当场采用。|成功了，不过用它煮出来的东西都有点发紫。|新来的督察就在屋里，你照样煮茶，而且把方法讲得头头是道，他还记了笔记。}\n# en: {Your method works so well that someone in charge notices the steam from a reaction nobody approved. There's a meeting about it, with you in it.|The kettle boils, and so, briefly, does the table.|The inspector walks in mid-reaction. There's a form for it, and you fill it in.}\nopt.left.say.2 [634dfa] = {你的方法太好用了，有位管事的注意到了一个没人批准过的反应冒出的蒸汽。为此开了个会，你也在场。|水壶开了，桌子也短暂地开了。|反应进行到一半，督察走了进来。这种事有专门的表格，你填了。}\n# en: Stick to the rules\nopt.right.label [b9e488] = 遵守规定\n# en: {You drink cold water while everyone else drinks tea brewed on an unapproved reaction. The foreman notes your conduct, and the break room notes it too.|You remind everyone of the rule. You are absolutely right and nobody offers you a biscuit for the rest of the week.|You keep to the rules with the inspector watching. He commends you by name, and the break room never forgives it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [91b8e6] = {别人喝着用未经批准的反应煮的茶，你喝凉水。工头记下了你的表现，休息室也记下了。|你提醒大家注意规定。你完全正确，这一整周没人再分你饼干。|你当着督察的面守规矩。他点名表扬了你，休息室从此再没原谅你。}\n\n@event intern_expedition_thin_air\n# en: Up the Ridge\ntitle [23ba3c] = 登上山脊\n# en: {The instruments have to go up to the ridge before dark, and the air on the plateau is very thin.|The path up the ridge again. The thin air is still negotiating with everybody's lungs.|The ridge, and the goats on it, who have long since stopped looking at anyone from the station.} {season == \"winter\": {Snow has drifted against the path.|The wind cuts through every layer you own.|Ice has glazed the steps cut into the rock.}|{The rocks are still hot from the afternoon.|The sky is clear all the way to the top of the world.|The evening wind smells of thyme and stone.}}\ntext [3fa996] = {天黑前仪器必须运上山脊，而高原上的空气非常稀薄。|又是上山脊的路。稀薄的空气还在和每个人的肺讨价还价。|山脊，还有山脊上的山羊，它们早就不再看站里的任何人了。} {season == \"winter\": {雪堆在了路边。|风穿透你身上的每一层衣服。|凿在岩石上的台阶结了一层冰。}|{石头还留着下午的热。|天空晴朗，一直晴到世界的顶端。|傍晚的风里有百里香和石头的气味。}}\n# en: A line of expedition members climbing a rocky ridge at dusk, carrying brass instruments wrapped in blankets.\nscene [00b5b6] = 黄昏时一队远征队员攀上岩石山脊，抱着裹在毯子里的黄铜仪器。\n# en: Carry the great lens\nopt.left.label [e7e4f2] = 扛那块大透镜\n# en: {You carry the great lens to the top without dropping it, then sit very still for a long time. The leader nods at you, once.|Your arms are numb by the top, and the lens is perfect. It's worth it when the first star shows in it.|You carry the lens up steadily, without fuss. Nobody watches you, which up here is the compliment.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cf4792] = {你把大透镜扛到山顶，一点没摔，然后一动不动地坐了很久。领队朝你点了一下头。|到山顶时你的胳膊已经麻了，透镜完好无损。当第一颗星出现在镜中，一切都值了。|你稳稳当当、不慌不忙地把透镜扛了上去。没人盯着你看，在这上面，这就是夸奖。}\n# en: Do the sums in the tent\nopt.right.label [d9b374] = 在帐篷里算数\n# en: {You work the night's sums by lamplight, warm and quiet. The lamp gutters twice. Your sums don't.|You have the charts ready when they come down. Nobody thanks you, because the charts are always ready.|You do the sums in the tent, as usual. The numbers are good, and so is the stove.}\nopt.right.say.1 [624053] = {你借着灯光算当晚的数，温暖又安静。灯芯跳了两下，你的算式一下也没跳。|他们下来时星图已经准备好了。没人道谢，因为星图总是准备好的。|你照常在帐篷里算数。数字不错，炉子也不错。}\n\n@event intern_expedition_chart_dispute\n# en: A Star Out of Place\ntitle [dbb002] = 错位的星\n# en: {Your chart and the senior astronomer's disagree about where one faint star is.|The senior astronomer's charts and yours have a running disagreement now, kept in red ink.|The senior astronomer now checks your charts first. A compliment, possibly.} {Probably your hand shook.|Probably their eyesight.|Probably the thin air.} {season == \"winter\": The nights are long, with time to recheck everything.|The nights are short, and every hour counts.}\ntext [2abc0a] = {你的星图和资深天文学家的星图，在一颗暗星的位置上不一致。|资深天文学家的星图和你的现在有一场持续的分歧，用红墨水记着。|资深天文学家现在先查你的星图。也许算是夸奖。} {大概是你的手抖了。|大概是他们的眼神。|大概是稀薄的空气。} {season == \"winter\": 夜很长，有时间把一切都复核一遍。|夜很短，每个钟头都要紧。}\n# en: Two expedition members bent over a star chart by lamplight, one pointing firmly at a dot.\nscene [576570] = 两名远征队员借着灯光俯身看一张星图，其中一人坚定地指着一个点。\n# en: Recheck it all night\nopt.left.label [19c9f0] = 通宵复核\n# en: {You recheck it until dawn. You were right by a hair, and nobody will ever care but you.|You were wrong by a hair. You fix it, and know the sky better than you did.|You recheck by lamplight and find the air was bending the star. You note it, and the astronomer copies your note.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f7e30b] = {你一直核对到天亮。你对了，差之毫厘，除了你没人会在乎。|你错了，差之毫厘。你改了过来，对星空的了解又多了一点。|你借着灯光复核，发现是空气让那颗星偏了位。你记了下来，天文学家抄走了你的笔记。}\n# en: Raise it with the astronomer\nopt.right.label [8f17a5] = 向天文学家提出来\n# en: {The astronomer checks, grumbles and corrects their chart. Then they buy you a cup of the camp's terrible coffee.|\"Well spotted,\" says the astronomer, through their teeth.|You raise it, and the astronomer has already checked it. \"You first, this time,\" they say.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e54689] = {天文学家查了一遍，嘟囔着改了自己的星图。然后请你喝了一杯营地里难喝的咖啡。|“眼尖。”天文学家咬着牙说。|你提了出来，天文学家早已核对过。“这次你先来。”对方说。}\n# en: {The astronomer checks and shows you, in front of everyone, exactly where you went wrong.|They don't check. They just look at you over their spectacles until you go away.|The astronomer blames the thin air and refuses to discuss it further. The star stays where they put it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1426da] = {天文学家查了，当着所有人的面给你指出哪里错了。|他们没查。只是从眼镜上方看着你，直到你走开。|天文学家把错推给稀薄的空气，拒绝再谈。那颗星就待在对方画的位置。}\n\n@event intern_field_station_count\n# en: The Dawn Count\ntitle [c272a9] = 黎明清点\n# en: {Every morning someone has to lie in the heather and count what's living on the moor. Today it's you.|You've learned which tussock is the least wet, and you guard it.|The dawn count again. The station's best counter, the warden says, is whoever minds the damp least.} {season == \"winter\": {Snow hares are out, white on white.|Frost has made the heather crackle under you.|A fox is counting the same hares.}|{The skylarks are going up one after another.|The midges have found you.|The heather is warm, and so is your tussock.}}\ntext [60241e] = {每天早上都得有人趴在石楠丛里，清点荒原上活着的东西。今天轮到你。|你摸清了哪个草丛最不湿，并且守着它。|又是黎明清点。站长说，站里最好的清点员，是最不在乎潮湿的那个。} {season == \"winter\": {雪兔出来了，白上加白。|霜让石楠在你身下咔咔作响。|一只狐狸在数同一群野兔。}|{云雀一只接一只飞上天。|蠓虫找到你了。|石楠是暖的，你那块草墩也是。}}\n# en: A student lying flat in the heather at dawn, a tally board in hand, watching nesting towers on the moor.\nscene [94a0ee] = 黎明时一个学生平趴在石楠丛中，手拿计数板，望着荒原上的筑巢塔。\n# en: Count every last one\nopt.left.label [1a8fb1] = 一只不落地数\n# en: {You count until your fingers are numb and the tally is perfect. The warden reads it twice and pins it on the wall.|You stay out until the very last nest is counted. You come back soaked and very proud of the number.|You count them all from your tussock. The warden copies your tally into the station book without checking it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6946d4] = {你数到手指发麻，记录完美无缺。站长读了两遍，钉在了墙上。|你一直待到最后一个巢数完。回来时浑身湿透，为这个数字非常骄傲。|你在你的草墩上把它们全数完了。站长没核对就把你的记录抄进了站志。}\n# en: Make a sensible estimate\nopt.right.label [db92d7] = 合理估一个数\n# en: {You mark it \"estimated\" in the margin, the way the station counts the far nests anyway. The warden accepts it, and you spend the extra hour by the stove with a cup of tea.|The warden checks your estimate against his own count and nods. He does the same on wet mornings.|Your estimate is close enough that the warden uses it for the far nests too. You get the stove first.}\nopt.right.say.1 [994847] = {你在页边注明“估算”，反正站里数远处的巢也是这么数的。站长认了，省下的那个钟头你在炉边喝了杯茶。|站长拿你的估算和他自己的数对了对，点点头。下雨的早上，他也是这么干的。|你的估算很接近，站长连远处的巢也用了它。你先占到了炉子。}\n# en: {The warden counted too. His number is not your number. He makes a note.|Your estimate includes a flock that left last week. The warden is very nice about it, which is worse.|Your estimate misses a new colony on the far slope. The warden found it yesterday and was waiting to see if you would.}\nopt.right.say.2 [60dffb] = {站长也数了。他的数不是你的数。他记了一笔。|你的估计里包括了一群上周就飞走的鸟。站长对此非常客气，这更糟。|你的估算漏掉了远坡上一个新的鸟群。站长昨天就发现了，一直在等着看你会不会发现。}\n\n@event intern_field_station_patient\n# en: The Patient in Paddock Three\ntitle [813ec2] = 三号围场的病号\n# en: {The station's latest patient is a moor-drake with a splinted wing and no gratitude at all.|Feeding time in paddock three. The patient sees you coming and lowers its head.|The drake in paddock three recognises you now. This hasn't made it any nicer.} {It has eaten a glove.|It is pretending to sleep, which it does very badly.|It has learned to open the bucket by itself.} {season == \"winter\": Its breath steams in the frosty air.|It is lying in the one patch of shade.}\ntext [a5113d] = {野外站最新的病号是一头荒原幼龙，翅膀上着夹板，毫无感恩之心。|三号围场喂食时间。病号看见你来，低下了头。|三号围场的幼龙现在认得你了。这并没让它变得友善。} {它吃了一只手套。|它在装睡，装得很差。|它学会了自己打开桶。} {season == \"winter\": 它的呼吸在霜冷的空气里冒着白气。|它躺在唯一一块阴凉里。}\n# en: A muddy paddock with a splinted creature glaring from a straw nest, a student holding out a bucket at arm's length.\nscene [ddabad] = 泥泞的围场里一头上着夹板的生物在草窝里瞪着眼，一个学生伸长胳膊递出一只桶。\n# en: Feed it by hand\nopt.left.label [cc2f18] = 亲手喂它\n# en: {It takes the food from your hand, very gently. Then it bites the bucket, to make a point.|It lets you check the splint, grumbling the whole time like an old man at the barber's.|It eats from your hand and then opens the bucket to check you haven't kept any back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c27074] = {它从你手里非常轻地叼走食物。然后咬了一口桶，以示立场。|它让你检查了夹板，全程嘟嘟囔囔，像理发店里的老头。|它从你手里吃完，又自己打开桶，确认你没私藏。}\n# en: {It takes the food, a bit of your sleeve and a bruise's worth of your arm.|It butts you into the mud and then looks pleased with itself.|It opens the bucket itself and eats the lot, then bites you for being slow.}\nopt.left.say.2 [69facb] = {它叼走了食物、一截你的袖子，还在你胳膊上留了一块淤青。|它把你拱进泥里，然后一脸得意。|它自己打开桶吃了个精光，然后因为你太慢咬了你一口。}\n# en: Use the long tongs\nopt.right.label [cd0b4c] = 用长夹子喂\n# en: {You feed it from a safe distance. It eats, and looks at you with deep contempt.|The tongs work perfectly. You feel you've let the patient down, somehow.|It takes the tongs off you and drops them in the mud. You feed it by hand after all.}\nopt.right.say.1 [06f014] = {你隔着安全距离喂它。它吃了，用深深的鄙夷看着你。|夹子好用极了。可你总觉得自己辜负了这位病号。|它把夹子从你手里夺走，丢进了泥里。你最后还是用手喂了它。}\n\n@event intern_thornwick_pastries\n# en: Thornwick Hospitality\ntitle [5e9c3e] = 荆木的待客之道\n# en: {Each morning your Thornwick hosts leave pastries on your desk, with a polite note.|The notes have grown longer and politer, which is worrying.|The pastries are a game now, and both sides know it.} {They look perfect, which in an illusion lab is a warning.|One is steaming slightly, which it shouldn't be.|Today there's one plain biscuit, which is somehow worse.} {season == \"winter\": The pastries look very warm.|The icing hasn't melted in the heat.}\ntext [5adad2] = {每天早上，荆木的东道主都在你桌上放一盘点心，附一张客气的字条。|字条越写越长、越写越客气，这让人担心。|点心现在成了一场游戏，双方都心知肚明。} {它们看起来完美无缺，在幻术实验室这就是警告。|有一块在微微冒热气，它不该冒的。|今天只有一块普通饼干，不知怎么更可疑了。} {season == \"winter\": 点心看起来很暖。|天这么热，糖霜却没化。}\n# en: A polished laboratory table at a rival academy, a plate of perfect pastries set out for a visiting student.\nscene [7a642b] = 对手学院一张光洁的实验桌上，一盘完美的点心摆在来访学生面前。\n# en: Eat one and smile\nopt.left.label [bb9a84] = 吃一块，再笑一笑\n# en: {It's real, and very good. The Thornwick interns look almost disappointed.|It's real. You eat it slowly, in front of everyone, and thank them warmly.|You eat the plain biscuit with every sign of delight. Nobody in the lab can tell if it was real, including you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [95343d] = {是真的，而且非常好吃。荆木的实习生看起来几乎有点失望。|是真的。你当着所有人的面慢慢吃完，热情地道谢。|你满脸欣喜地吃下了那块素饼干。实验室里没人知道它是不是真的，包括你。}\n# en: {It's a glamour over something dry and joyless. You eat the whole thing with a smile, and the lab respects that.|It tastes of nothing at all. You compliment the baker, and someone in the corner laughs.|The biscuit turns to air in your mouth. You keep chewing anyway, and the lab gives you a small round of applause.}\nopt.left.say.2 [452496] = {是套在某种又干又无趣的东西上的魅影。你笑着吃完了整块，实验室对此肃然起敬。|它什么味道都没有。你夸了夸烘焙师，角落里有人笑出了声。|饼干在你嘴里化成了空气。你还是照嚼不误，实验室为你鼓了一小阵掌。}\n# en: Bring my own breakfast\nopt.right.label [34615e] = 自带早饭\n# en: {You eat your own bread, very pointedly. The Thornwick students write something down.|You leave the plate untouched. Your supervisor here calls it \"a very Lumenhold choice\", and doesn't mean it nicely.|You eat your own bread and offer them some. They check it for glamours, which is the friendliest thing they've done.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d95cb8] = {你意味深长地吃着自己的面包。荆木的学生记了点什么。|你没碰那盘点心。这里的导师说这是“非常流明堡的选择”，不是好话。|你吃着自己的面包，还分给他们一些。他们查了查有没有魅影，这是他们做过最友好的事。}\n\n@event intern_thornwick_rivalry\n# en: The Old Grudge\ntitle [7f876b] = 老恩怨\n# en: {The Thornwick students have questions about Lumenhold. Most of them are about the last time the two schools met on the duelling field.|The Thornwick students have found a new angle on the old grudge.|You and the Thornwick students have a routine now. It's almost friendly.} {Today they want to know if Lumenhold's towers still lean.|Today they ask, sweetly, if Lumenhold teaches illusion yet.|Today one of them pretends to be you, very well.}\ntext [ffc4d0] = {荆木的学生对流明堡有些问题。大多是关于两校上一次在决斗场上交手的事。|荆木的学生为这桩老恩怨找到了新角度。|你和荆木的学生现在有了固定节目。几乎算友好。} {今天他们想知道流明堡的塔是不是还歪着。|今天他们甜甜地问，流明堡是不是终于开幻术课了。|今天他们中有人扮成了你，扮得非常像。}\n# en: A group of rival students around a lab bench, one of them leaning in with a pointed smile towards a visitor in different robes.\nscene [07d8c1] = 一群对手学院的学生围在实验台边，其中一个朝穿着不同长袍的访客探过身去，笑得意味深长。\n# en: Out-charm them\nopt.left.label [a1eb25] = 用魅力压过他们\n# en: {You answer every barb with a better one. By lunch they're laughing with you rather than at you.|You make the room laugh at their joke, and then at them. Their supervisor smiles behind her notes.|You do a better impression of them than they did of you. The lab agrees, and so, grudgingly, do they.}\nopt.left.say.1 [476851] = {每句挖苦你都回以更妙的一句。到午饭时，他们是在跟你一起笑，而不是笑你。|你让满屋人先笑他们的笑话，再笑他们。他们的导师在笔记后面露出微笑。|你学他们学得比他们学你还像。全实验室都同意，他们也不情不愿地同意了。}\n# en: {You try to be witty. It comes out as a speech about the founders. They let you finish, kindly.|You lose the exchange so badly that one of them takes a bow on your behalf.|Their impression of you is so good that you forget your answer. They finish it for you, in your voice.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8aa82a] = {你想说点俏皮话，结果说成了一篇关于创始者的演讲。他们好心地让你讲完了。|你输得太惨，他们中有人替你鞠了一躬。|他们学你学得太像了，你把要说的话都忘了。他们用你的声音替你说完了。}\n# en: Let it slide\nopt.right.label [344baa] = 随它去\n# en: {You smile and go back to work. Your supervisor here notices, and approves, and you feel like you lost anyway.|You say nothing. It takes them all morning to realise you've won.|You let it go, as usual. They seem almost disappointed, and one of them saves you a seat at lunch.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bf66ed] = {你笑笑，回去干活。这里的导师注意到了，也赞许，可你还是觉得自己输了。|你什么也没说。他们花了一上午才意识到你赢了。|你照常没接茬。他们看起来几乎有点失望，其中一个午饭时给你留了座。}\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_coffee\n# en: The Coffee Machine\ntitle [9f1fbd] = 咖啡机\n# en: {Nobody at the Office talks about anything, except the coffee machine. It's very good.|A queue forms around the machine each evening, though nobody seems to join it.|The coffee queue, you've learned, is where the Office's real work gets done.} {Two people beside you discuss the weather in a way that isn't about the weather.|A cup has a name on it that was there yesterday, under another name.|The machine hisses, and everyone falls silent until it stops.}\ntext [82c4dc] = {署里没人谈论任何事，除了咖啡机。它非常好用。|每晚咖啡机周围都会排起队，却没人看起来是去排队的。|你已经明白，咖啡队伍才是署里真正干活的地方。} {你旁边两个人在聊天气，聊法显然不是在聊天气。|一只杯子上写着一个名字，昨天它也在，写的是另一个名字。|机器一嘶响，所有人都停下话头，直到它响完。}\n# en: A plain room with no windows and a gleaming coffee machine, quiet people in plain coats standing near it, not quite talking.\nscene [2a796e] = 一间没有窗户的朴素房间，一台锃亮的咖啡机，几个穿着普通外套的安静的人站在旁边，不太算是在交谈。\n# en: Linger at the machine\nopt.left.label [a4aa91] = 在咖啡机边多待会儿\n# en: {You learn three things nobody told you, and one thing nobody knows you know.|You drink your coffee slowly and listen. By the second cup you understand what nobody is saying.|You stand in the queue long enough to be part of it. Someone tells you something, and you understand it was meant for you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f0d88c] = {你知道了三件没人告诉你的事，还有一件没人知道你知道的事。|你慢慢喝着咖啡听着。到第二杯，你听懂了没人说出口的东西。|你在队伍里站得够久，成了它的一部分。有人跟你说了件事，你明白那是说给你听的。}\n# en: {Someone notices you listening and asks, very kindly, how your day is going. You don't sleep that night.|You overhear something you very much wish you hadn't. It can't be unheard.|The machine hisses mid-sentence and the room goes silent. When it stops, everyone is looking at you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [006b70] = {有人发现你在听，非常和气地问你今天过得怎样。那晚你没睡着。|你无意中听到一件真希望没听到的事。听到了就忘不掉了。|机器在话说到一半时嘶嘶作响，屋里一片寂静。等它停下，所有人都在看着你。}\n# en: Take my cup back to my desk\nopt.right.label [f05b97] = 端回自己桌上\n# en: {You work through the evening with your coffee. The work is dull and your supervisor approves, which is a little chilling.|Your desk is very quiet. It's the most anyone at the Office will say about you, and here that's high praise.|You take your cup to your desk. When you come back for the next one, the queue has saved your place.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d9c64f] = {你就着咖啡工作了一晚。活很枯燥，导师很满意，这有点让人发冷。|你的桌子非常安静。这是署里任何人对你说过的最多的话，在这里这是高度赞扬。|你把杯子端回了桌上。等你回来续杯时，队伍给你留着位置。}\n\n@event intern_unseen_office_wrong_file\n# en: The Wrong File\ntitle [a461ed] = 错放的卷宗\n# en: {A file has been left on your desk. It isn't yours, and it isn't labelled.|A second unlabelled file. You're starting to think it's a test.|The unlabelled files keep arriving. You've stopped asking where they come from.} This one is tied shut with a red ribbon. {It's very thin.|It's very thick, with a coffee ring on the cover.|The knot is one you don't recognise.}\ntext [d1b2bf] = {有份卷宗被留在你桌上。不是你的，也没有标签。|第二份没有标签的卷宗。你开始觉得这是一场考验。|没有标签的卷宗不停地送来。你已经不再问它们从哪来。} 这份用红丝带系着。{它非常薄。|它非常厚，封面上有个咖啡杯印。|那个结你从没见过。}\n# en: A plain folder with no label lying on an empty desk under a single lamp.\nscene [395e41] = 一盏孤灯下的空桌上，放着一个没有标签的朴素文件夹。\n# en: Read it before returning it\nopt.left.label [6c5a88] = 先读再归还\n# en: {You read it, retie the ribbon perfectly and hand it in. Your supervisor checks the knot and smiles, very slightly.|You read every page. It's about a man who sells hats. Nothing has ever made you more nervous.|You read it, retie the unfamiliar knot from memory and hand it in. Your supervisor checks it twice and then teaches you a new one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b5d200] = {你读完了，把丝带系得一模一样，交了回去。导师检查了那个结，非常轻微地笑了笑。|你读了每一页。讲的是一个卖帽子的人。从没有什么让你这么紧张过。|你读完了，凭记忆系回那个陌生的结，交了回去。导师检查了两遍，然后教了你一个新的。}\n# en: {Your supervisor takes the file back and glances at the ribbon. \"Interesting knot,\" she says.|You put the pages back in the wrong order. Somehow, everyone knows by morning.|You can't retie the knot. You hand it in with an ordinary bow, and your supervisor looks at it for a long time.}\nopt.left.say.2 [bfe2f7] = {导师拿回卷宗，瞥了一眼丝带。“有意思的结。”她说。|你把书页放回去时顺序错了。不知怎么，第二天早上人人都知道了。|你系不回那个结。你系了个普通的蝴蝶结交上去，导师盯着它看了很久。}\n# en: Return it unopened\nopt.right.label [ab3e46] = 原封不动地归还\n# en: {You hand it back without looking. Your supervisor says nothing, which here is approval. You'll wonder about it for weeks.|You return it with the ribbon untouched. The next day there's a slightly better coffee waiting on your desk.|You return it unopened, as always. The files stop coming for a week, and then there are two.}\nopt.right.say.1 [dc565a] = {你看都没看就交了回去。导师什么也没说，在这里这就是认可。你会为此琢磨好几个星期。|你把丝带原样未动地还了回去。第二天你桌上多了一杯稍好一点的咖啡。|你照旧原封不动地还了回去。卷宗停了一个星期，然后一下来了两份。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/renown.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/renown.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event renown_chance\n# en: A Chance to Be Noticed\ntitle [41fb8e] = 被注意到的机会\n# en: {A gold-bordered notice goes up in the entrance hall. Twice a year the academy puts students on show.|The gold notice is up again, as it is twice a year, and the crowd around it is bigger.|The gold notice again. People glance at you as they read it now.} Three stages: a spell before the whole school, the great debate, or the village fair. Each is a hard test in public. {Win, and the school knows your name; lose, and it remembers that instead.|The village is invited, and the faculty sit in the front row: a win is talked about for months, and so is a loss.|There's a prize, and there's the shame of losing in public.}\ntext [af5e75] = {门厅里贴出一张镶金边的告示。学院每年两次让学生登台亮相。|金边告示又贴出来了，一年两次，这次围观的人更多。|又是金边告示。现在人们读它时会瞟你一眼。}三个舞台：在全校面前施法、大辩论，或者村里的集市。每一个都是当众的硬仗。{赢了，全校都会知道你的名字；输了，大家记住的就是那个。|村里人也受邀了，教职员坐在第一排：赢了会被谈论好几个月，输了也一样。|有奖品，也有当众失败的羞耻。}\n# en: A noticeboard in the entrance hall crowded with announcements, one gold-bordered notice pinned dead centre, students jostling to read it.\nscene [947222] = 门厅的告示板上挤满了通知，正中央钉着一张镶金边的告示，学生们挤着去看。\n# en: Cast a spell before the school\nopt.demonstrate.label [0e28c7] = 在全校面前施一道咒语\n# en: Arcana, hard. Fame or embarrassment.\nopt.demonstrate.hint [b4213a] = 奥术，困难。出名或出丑。\n# en: You write your name on the list before you can think better of it.\nopt.demonstrate.say.1 [d4ad55] = 你没来得及多想，就把名字写在了名单上。\n# en: Enter the great debate\nopt.debate.label [c11160] = 参加大辩论\n# en: Lore, hard. The faculty will be listening.\nopt.debate.hint [c454bd] = 学识，困难。教职员会在台下听着。\n# en: You sign up for the debate. Your hands are shaking already.\nopt.debate.say.1 [43d367] = 你报名参加了辩论。手已经在抖了。\n# en: Perform at the village fair\nopt.fair.label [54524e] = 在村里的集市上表演\n# en: Charm and a spell. The village pays and remembers.\nopt.fair.hint [e9a0c5] = 魅力加一道咒语。村里人会付钱，也会记住你。\n# en: You put your name down for the village fair. Somebody will have to teach you to bow.\nopt.fair.say.1 [86c4a5] = 你报名参加了村里的集市。得有人教你怎么鞠躬。\n# en: Keep your head down\nopt.pass.label [b59315] = 低调行事\n# en: {You read the notice and walk on. Fame can find someone else this term.|Not this time. You've got enough to do without an audience.|People glance at you as you walk past the notice. Let them. You'd rather not lose in public this term.}\nopt.pass.say.1 [e991f8] = {你读了告示，走开了。这学期，让名声去找别人吧。|这次算了。不需要观众，你要做的事已经够多了。|你从告示前走过时，有人朝你瞥了一眼。随他们去。这学期你可不想当众出丑。}\n\n@event renown_demonstration\n# en: Before the Whole School\ntitle [edcef6] = 在全校面前\n# en: The whole school is in the Great Hall, and so is the faculty, and so, at the end, is the headmistress. You're in the middle of the floor, and everyone will remember what happens next, good or bad. There's a long silence while they wait for you to begin.\ntext [49063e] = 全校都在大礼堂里，教职员也在，最后连院长也来了。你站在场地正中，接下来发生的一切，不管好坏，所有人都会记得。所有人都在等你开始，一片长长的寂静。\n# en: The Great Hall packed to the rafters, every house at its table, a single student standing in the middle of the floor with a wand raised.\nscene [923d71] = 大礼堂挤得水泄不通，各学院坐在各自的长桌旁，一个学生独自站在场地正中，举着魔杖。\n# en: Cast the hardest thing you know\nopt.left.label [4a80f8] = 施展你会的最难的咒语\n# en: It works, beautifully, better than ever before. The hall is silent and then very loud. For a month, first-years point at you in corridors.\nopt.left.say.1 [649754] = 它成功了，美得惊人，比以往任何一次都好。礼堂先是一片寂静，然后震耳欲聋。整整一个月，一年级生在走廊里对你指指点点。\n# en: It fizzles in front of everyone, with a noise like a sad goose. The hall is very kind about it, which is unbearable. You'll be \"the goose\" until someone else fails.\nopt.left.say.2 [1eb78c] = 它当着所有人的面熄了火，发出一声像伤心的鹅一样的声音。礼堂里的人对此非常体贴，这才叫人受不了。在别人出丑之前，你都会是“那只鹅”。\n# en: Cast something safe, perfectly\nopt.right.label [09f177] = 施一道稳妥的咒语，完美无缺\n# en: Something simple, done flawlessly. Polite applause. Nobody will remember it, and nobody will laugh at you either.\nopt.right.say.1 [492d38] = 简单的咒语，施得毫无瑕疵。礼貌的掌声。没人会记得，也没人会笑话你。\n\n@event renown_debate\n# en: The Great Debate\ntitle [b747aa] = 大辩论\n# en: The great debate: \"That magic should be taught to anyone who asks.\" You've drawn the side you disagree with, against a fourth-year who has never lost. The faculty are in the front row, and Thorne is taking notes: win and they'll remember you, lose and so will everyone.\ntext [4edf51] = 大辩论的辩题：“魔法应当传授给任何求学的人。”你抽到了自己不赞同的那一方，对手是一个从没输过的四年级生。教职员坐在第一排，索恩教授在做笔记：赢了，他们会记住你；输了，所有人都会记住。\n# en: Two lecterns on a stage under banners, a student at one pointing emphatically, an older student at the other frowning, a packed hall of faces in the dark.\nscene [deb5fb] = 旗帜下的舞台上有两个讲台，一个学生在一边激动地指点着，另一边一个年长的学生皱着眉，台下黑暗中坐满了人。\n# en: Argue your side brilliantly anyway\nopt.left.label [b2d090] = 照样把你那方辩得精彩\n# en: You argue the side you don't believe so well that you half-believe it. The fourth-year concedes. Thorne, afterwards, says \"Hm,\" twice, which has never happened.\nopt.left.say.1 [93af97] = 你把自己不信的一方辩得太好，好到自己都信了一半。四年级生认输了。事后，索恩教授说了两次“嗯”，这从来没发生过。\n# en: The fourth-year takes you apart, politely and completely. The hall applauds you for trying, which is the cruellest kind of applause.\nopt.left.say.2 [709e30] = 四年级生客客气气、彻彻底底地把你驳倒了。全场为你的努力鼓掌，这是最残忍的一种掌声。\n# en: Argue what you actually believe\nopt.right.label [fa097f] = 说出你真正相信的\n# en: You throw away the brief and argue your own side, which is against the rules. You lose on points and win the hall. Thorne writes something down, and you'll never know what.\nopt.right.say.1 [76c0d1] = 你扔掉了辩稿，为自己的立场辩护，这是违反规则的。你按积分输了，却赢得了全场。索恩教授写下了什么，你永远不会知道写的是什么。\n\n@event renown_village_fair\n# en: Magic at the Fair\ntitle [ba2f28] = 集市上的魔法\n# en: The village fair has a stage, bunting and a crowd of farmers who have seen every trick there is. The village apprentices sit at the front, cross-legged, unimpressed in advance. Win them over and the village talks of you for a year; flop, and it talks of that.\ntext [31802d] = 村里的集市有一个舞台、彩旗，还有一群见过所有把戏的农夫。村里的学徒们盘腿坐在最前面，还没开始就一脸不以为然。征服他们，全村会念叨你一年；砸了，全村念叨的就是这个。\n# en: A small stage at a village fair under bunting, a student conjuring coloured lights over a crowd of apprentices and farmers.\nscene [ce56eb] = 村集市彩旗下的一个小舞台，一个学生在一群学徒和农夫头顶变出彩色的光。\n# en: Put on a real show\nopt.left.label [a47725] = 来一场真正的表演\n# en: Lights, a dancing hat, a dove that turns into a shower of petals. The apprentices whoop. The farmers pay for the hat trick twice. The village talks about it until harvest.\nopt.left.say.1 [90cf71] = 彩光、一顶会跳舞的帽子、一只变成花瓣雨的鸽子。学徒们欢呼起来。农夫们为帽子戏法付了两次钱。全村一直谈论到收获季。\n# en: The dove refuses to turn into anything and sits on your head. The apprentices love it; the farmers don't pay.\nopt.left.say.2 [5b1927] = 鸽子拒绝变成任何东西，就蹲在你头上。学徒们很喜欢；农夫们没付钱。\n# en: Do card tricks for the front row\nopt.right.label [7a00f1] = 给前排变纸牌戏法\n# en: You sit on the edge of the stage and do card tricks for the apprentices until your voice gives out. No fame to speak of. Half of them walk you to the gate.\nopt.right.say.1 [45b3b7] = 你坐在舞台边上给学徒们变纸牌戏法，一直变到嗓子哑了。谈不上什么名声。有一半人一路送你到门口。\n# en: Lift the mayor's watch for the finale\nopt.up.label [0ce231] = 压轴时摸走镇长的怀表\n# en: For the finale you lift the mayor's watch, his spectacles and, for one bad moment, his purse. You give back the watch and the spectacles, to applause. The purse you forget to mention.\nopt.up.say.1 [2d61e4] = 压轴时你摸走了镇长的怀表、他的眼镜，还有——有那么糟糕的一刻——他的钱袋。你在掌声中还回了怀表和眼镜。钱袋你忘了提。\n\n@event renown_ice_rescue\n# en: A First-Year on the Ice\ntitle [36cb5d] = 冰上的一年级生\n# en: A first-year has gone through the ice on the lake. A crowd is shouting on the shore, and nobody is moving. You're nearest.\ntext [89d72c] = 一个一年级生掉进了湖里的冰窟窿。岸上一群人在喊，没人动。你离得最近。\n# en: A small figure clinging to the edge of a broken patch of ice in the middle of a frozen lake, students shouting on the shore, a student running out across the ice.\nscene [cb70d0] = 结冰的湖中央，一个小小的身影扒着一块碎冰的边缘，岸上的学生在喊，一个学生正冲上冰面。\n# en: Go out on the ice\nopt.left.label [4c1b80] = 到冰上去\n# en: You crawl out flat on the ice and pull them free. By supper the whole school knows your name, and the first-year's mother writes you a letter you keep for ever.\nopt.left.say.1 [e4cca1] = 你平趴在冰面上爬过去，把 TA 拉了出来。到晚饭时全校都知道了你的名字，那个一年级生的母亲给你写了一封信，你一直留着。\n# en: The ice gives under you too. The prefects pull you both out with a ladder. You spend a week in the infirmary, a little famous, mostly frozen.\nopt.left.say.2 [8de0f6] = 冰在你身下也裂了。级长们用梯子把你们俩拖了上来。你在医务室躺了一个星期，小有名气，主要是冻坏了。\n# en: Run for the prefects\nopt.right.label [770692] = 跑去找级长\n# en: You run for help, and the prefects get there with ladders in time. Someone else is the hero. You were right, and you feel wrong about it all winter.\nopt.right.say.1 [2c0c95] = 你跑去求救，级长们及时带着梯子赶到。英雄是别人。你做得对，可你一整个冬天都觉得不对劲。\n\n@event renown_portrait\n# en: A Portrait for the Hall\ntitle [316b46] = 礼堂里的肖像\n# en: The academy hangs a portrait of its most talked-about students in the long gallery each year, and someone has suggested you. The painter's fee is yours to pay, as is the custom. The other portraits are already watching you.\ntext [1c2fbd] = 学院每年会把最受热议的学生的肖像挂在长廊里，有人提名了你。按惯例，画师的酬金由你来付。其他肖像已经在看着你了。\n# en: A painter's easel in a sunlit corridor, a half-finished portrait of a student in formal robes, the sitter trying very hard not to scratch their nose.\nscene [136a0d] = 阳光明媚的走廊里立着一个画架，上面是一幅画了一半的穿礼袍的学生肖像，坐着的人正拼命忍住不去挠鼻子。\n# en: Sit for it\nopt.left.label [e61125] = 去当模特\n# en: Six sittings, an itchy collar and a painter who hums. The portrait is good, if a little more heroic than you are. Passing it, first-years straighten their backs.\nopt.left.say.1 [41686f] = 画了六次，领子扎人，画师一直在哼歌。肖像画得很好，只是比你本人英勇了一点。一年级生从它面前经过时，都会挺直腰板。\n# en: Decline politely\nopt.right.label [c5820d] = 礼貌地谢绝\n# en: You decline. Your purse is grateful. The portrait that goes up instead is of someone who looks very pleased with themselves, and you find you don't mind at all.\nopt.right.say.1 [1dcbbd] = 你谢绝了。你的钱袋很感激。挂上去的是另一个人的肖像，看起来对自己非常满意，而你发现自己一点也不介意。\n\n@event renown_rumour\n# en: A Rumour About You\ntitle [e03f40] = 关于你的流言\n# en: Being known has a price: there's a rumour about you going round the castle, untrue and a little bit funny, and it's in Nessa's gossip column by Friday. People stop talking when you walk in.\ntext [d8f51f] = 出名是有代价的：一则关于你的流言在城堡里传开了，不是真的，还有点好笑，星期五之前就上了内莎的八卦专栏。你一走进来，大家就不说话了。\n# en: Two students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, a third walking past them with a stiff back, a crumpled gossip sheet on the floor.\nscene [c72b51] = 走廊里两个学生捂着嘴窃窃私语，第三个学生挺直脊背从他们身边走过，地上丢着一张揉皱的八卦小报。\n# en: Answer it in public\nopt.left.label [2a7890] = 公开回应\n# en: You laugh it off at supper, loudly and well, with a better story of your own. By Monday everyone's repeating yours instead.\nopt.left.say.1 [6fad96] = 晚饭时你一笑置之，笑得响亮又漂亮，还讲了个更好的故事。到星期一，大家传的都是你那个了。\n# en: You deny it too hard, and it sounds true. The rumour grows a second chapter. It'll pass. It takes a month.\nopt.left.say.2 [de5cf2] = 你否认得太用力，听起来倒像真的。流言又长出了第二章。它会过去的。过了一个月。\n# en: Ignore it\nopt.right.label [e2ae0c] = 不理它\n# en: You say nothing. It burns for a fortnight and then dies of boredom. You learn who repeated it, and remember.\nopt.right.say.1 [e7e80e] = 你什么也没说。它热闹了两个星期，然后无聊死了。你知道了是谁在传，并记住了。\n\n@event renown_patron_dinner\n# en: Dinner With a Patron\ntitle [939524] = 与资助人共进晚餐\n# en: A wealthy patron of the academy has asked to meet \"the students everyone's talking about\", and you're one of them. Dinner is nine courses. The patron likes clever talk and has been known to fund a promising student's whole final year.\ntext [773d2a] = 学院的一位富有的资助人想见见“大家都在谈论的学生”，你就是其中之一。晚餐有九道菜。这位资助人喜欢机智的谈话，据说曾资助过一个有前途的学生整个最后一年的学业。\n# en: A long candlelit dining table in a grand townhouse, a student in borrowed formal robes seated between a wealthy patron and a portrait of the patron.\nscene [de7bac] = 一座豪华宅邸里长长的烛光餐桌，一个穿着借来的礼袍的学生坐在一位富有的资助人和资助人的画像之间。\n# en: Be dazzling\nopt.left.label [166f5f] = 光彩照人\n# en: You're witty through eight courses and wise on the ninth. The patron sends you home with a purse \"for books\" and an invitation to come back next year.\nopt.left.say.1 [00008d] = 前八道菜你妙语连珠，第九道时又显得睿智。资助人送你回去时塞给你一袋钱“买书用”，还邀请你明年再来。\n# en: You use the wrong fork, then the wrong joke. The patron is kind and bored. You're not asked back.\nopt.left.say.2 [0f38a6] = 你用错了叉子，然后讲错了笑话。资助人很和善，也很无聊。你没有再被邀请。\n# en: Be honest about your struggles\nopt.right.label [8dbca1] = 坦诚说出你的难处\n# en: You talk plainly about money and work and exams. The patron listens, the only one at the table who does. A small purse arrives the next week with no letter, just a crest.\nopt.right.say.1 [11822a] = 你坦白地谈起钱、工作和考试。资助人在听，是整张桌上唯一在听的人。下个星期寄来了一小袋钱，没有信，只有一枚纹章。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/spell_lessons.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/spell_lessons.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lesson_flame\n# en: Putting Fire Out\ntitle [dc5132] = 把火灭掉\n# en: Flame is yours now. Professor Thorne gathers the year's new Flame casters in the stone practice room and lights a brazier. \"Any fool can start a fire,\" he says. \"You are here to learn to stop one.\" There is a bucket of water at his feet, and he forbids you to touch it.\ntext [490b3a] = 火焰咒如今归你了。索恩教授把这一年新学会火焰咒的人召集到石头练习室，点燃一只火盆。“任何蠢货都能生火，”他说，“你们来这儿，是学怎么把火停下。”他脚边有一桶水，他不许你碰。\n# en: A stone practice room with scorched walls, a stern grey-bearded professor beside a roaring brazier, a bucket of water at his feet.\nscene [dd2300] = 四壁焦黑的石头练习室，一位严厉的灰胡子教授站在熊熊燃烧的火盆旁，脚边一桶水。\n# en: Pull the fire back into your hand\nopt.left.label [4451c4] = 把火收回手里\n# en: You call the flame back like a sleeve pulled over a wrist. It stings, and the brazier flares and sinks. \"Again,\" says Thorne. He says it eleven times.\nopt.left.say.1 [c20bd6] = 你把火焰召回，就像把袖子拉回手腕。手被灼得生疼，火盆窜起又落下。“再来，”索恩说。他说了十一遍。\n# en: Ask what the bucket is for\nopt.right.label [f6e8aa] = 问那桶水是干什么的\n# en: \"For the student who doesn't ask,\" says Thorne, and empties it over the brazier of the boy beside you. Then he explains, for an hour, how every great fire he has known began.\nopt.right.say.1 [933d3a] = “给不提问的学生用的，”索恩说着，把整桶水浇在你旁边那个男生的火盆上。然后他花了一个钟头，讲他见过的每一场大火是怎么烧起来的。\n\n@event lesson_tide\n# en: Waist-Deep at Dawn\ntitle [82cbf9] = 黎明时齐腰深\n# en: Professor Marisol teaches Tide where it lives: waist-deep in the lake at dawn. \"Cold water,\" she says happily, \"is good for everyone.\" She recites a poem about waves and stops before the last line, frowning. The lake waits for you.\ntext [7c2f23] = 玛丽索尔教授在潮涌咒住的地方教它：黎明时，湖里齐腰深的水中。“冷水，”她高兴地说，“对谁都有好处。”她背了一首关于海浪的诗，在最后一行前停住，皱起眉头。湖水在等你。\n# en: A misty lake at dawn, a professor standing waist-deep in the water with arms raised, students hesitating on the pebbled shore.\nscene [93a9d4] = 黎明时雾气蒙蒙的湖，一位教授站在齐腰深的水里高举双臂，学生们在卵石岸边犹豫不前。\n# en: Wade in and call a wave\nopt.left.label [103d24] = 蹚进水里唤一道浪\n# en: The water is so cold it feels like an opinion. Your wave rises knee-high and slaps Marisol full in the chest, and she whoops. \"The last line!\" she shouts. \"It was about this!\"\nopt.left.say.1 [10fb6d] = 水冷得像一种观点。你唤起的浪只有膝盖高，正正拍在玛丽索尔胸口，她欢呼起来。“最后一行！”她喊，“写的就是这个！”\n# en: Call it from the shore\nopt.right.label [a49270] = 在岸上施咒\n# en: You work the spell from the dry pebbles and raise a neat little swell. Marisol looks at your dry boots with real sorrow, as if at a poem with no ending.\nopt.right.say.1 [0ca97b] = 你站在干燥的卵石上施咒，掀起一道整整齐齐的小浪。玛丽索尔看着你干爽的靴子，真心实意地难过，像在看一首没有结尾的诗。\n\n@event lesson_foresight\n# en: The Bottom of the Cup\ntitle [d6ff5b] = 杯底\n# en: The astromancy lecturer, who wears three scarves in every season, sits the class at the observatory with cups of tea and no instructions. Foresight, it turns out, begins at the bottom of the cup: what you think you see, and what you want to.\ntext [4e84c1] = 那位一年四季围三条围巾的星象讲师，让全班坐在观星台里，每人一杯茶，什么也不讲。原来，预见始于杯底：你以为自己看见了什么，和你想看见什么。\n# en: An observatory room with tea cups on every desk, a lecturer in three scarves peering into a student's cup with a magnifying glass.\nscene [abb57c] = 观星台的房间里每张桌上都放着茶杯，一位围着三条围巾的讲师拿放大镜窥看学生的杯子。\n# en: Read your own cup\nopt.left.label [130c28] = 读你自己的杯子\n# en: The leaves say a hard week is coming. It does, from Tuesday. The lecturer says that's the trouble with reading your own: you start making it true.\nopt.left.say.1 [fdc255] = 茶叶说，艰难的一周就要来了。从周二开始，果然来了。讲师说，读自己的杯子麻烦就在这儿：你会开始让它成真。\n# en: Read your neighbour's\nopt.right.label [661301] = 读邻座的杯子\n# en: You tell your neighbour a letter is on its way. A letter arrives at supper. Now half the dormitory wants their leaves read, and you have to learn to say \"unclear\" kindly.\nopt.right.say.1 [15b70b] = 你告诉邻座，有封信在路上。晚饭时信就到了。现在半个宿舍都想让你读茶叶，你只好学会温和地说“看不清”。\n\n@event lesson_starlight\n# en: A Jar on the Roof\ntitle [346e45] = 屋顶上的罐子\n# en: Starlight is practised after midnight on the observatory roof, where your Starcalling tutor hands you a glass jar and tells you to hold it up \"until something falls in\". The stars are very far away. The roof is very cold.\ntext [a2c014] = 星光咒是午夜后在观星台屋顶上练的。你的唤星术导师递给你一只玻璃罐，让你举着，“直到有东西掉进去”。星星非常远。屋顶非常冷。\n# en: A flat observatory roof at night, a student holding up a glass jar toward a sky crowded with stars, breath steaming.\nscene [64cb32] = 夜里平坦的观星台屋顶，一个学生朝满天繁星举起玻璃罐，呼出的气冒着白雾。\n# en: Hold the jar until it fills\nopt.left.label [72681d] = 举着罐子，直到装满\n# en: At three in the morning a thread of light settles in the jar like milk in tea. You carry it down the stairs in both hands. You can't feel your fingers until breakfast.\nopt.left.say.1 [12b784] = 凌晨三点，一缕光沉进罐子里，像牛奶落进茶里。你双手捧着它下楼。直到早饭时，你的手指都没有知觉。\n# en: Lie back and watch the sky\nopt.right.label [3f4130] = 躺下看星空\n# en: You lie on the roof and name the constellations with whoever is beside you. Your jar stays empty. The lecturer, passing, says that's the first lesson too, and the one most people skip.\nopt.right.say.1 [ad51ae] = 你躺在屋顶上，和身边的人一起数星座。你的罐子一直空着。讲师路过时说，这也是第一课，而且是大多数人跳过的那一课。\n\n@event lesson_mend\n# en: Teacups First\ntitle [606e4e] = 先修茶杯\n# en: Sister Amaranth teaches Mend with teacups. She drops one on the infirmary floor without looking. \"Mend it.\" When it's whole she drops another. \"Bones are harder,\" she says, rolling bandages. \"Cups first.\"\ntext [bd4c39] = 阿玛兰斯修女用茶杯教愈合咒。她看都不看就把一只茶杯摔在医务室地上。“修好它。”修好了，她又摔一只。“骨头更难，”她一边卷绷带一边说，“先修杯子。”\n# en: An infirmary storeroom, a stern healer dropping a teacup to the stone floor while a student kneels over the pieces with a wand.\nscene [32aa56] = 医务室的储藏间，一位严厉的医者把茶杯摔在石地上，一个学生拿着魔杖跪在碎片旁。\n# en: Mend cup after cup\nopt.left.label [0314eb] = 一只接一只地修\n# en: By the twentieth cup you can hear the crack close before you see it. Amaranth says \"Keep doing that,\" and drops a teapot.\nopt.left.say.1 [0628fd] = 修到第二十只，你还没看见裂缝合上，就先听见了。阿玛兰斯说“继续这样做”，然后摔了一只茶壶。\n# en: Ask her about the bones\nopt.right.label [4f800c] = 问她骨头的事\n# en: She tells you, still rolling bandages, about the worst break she ever set. It was a student's; that student is a professor now. You go a little pale, and listen to every word.\nopt.right.say.1 [37dcc5] = 她一边卷绷带，一边给你讲她接过的最糟的一次骨折。那是个学生的；那个学生现在当了教授。你脸色有点发白，但一字不落地听完了。\n\n@event lesson_purify\n# en: Make It Stronger\ntitle [fcd954] = 让它更浓\n# en: Sister Amaranth has heard that Pell taught you to Distill. She sets a cauldron of something green in front of you that she will not name. \"Pell teaches it fast,\" she says. \"I teach it so you're still standing afterwards. Make it stronger.\" Then she goes back to her own work.\ntext [4c9d1e] = 阿玛兰斯修女听说佩尔教会了你萃取。她把一锅不肯说名字的绿色东西放在你面前。“佩尔教得快，”她说，“我教的是让你萃完还站得住。让它更浓。”说完她回去忙自己的了。\n# en: An alchemy lab bench with a cauldron of thick green liquid, a healer standing over a student who holds a wand above it.\nscene [77f705] = 炼金实验室的工作台上一锅浓稠的绿色液体，一位医者站在举着魔杖的学生身边。\n# en: Distill it drop by drop\nopt.left.label [a3f178] = 一滴一滴地萃取\n# en: Drop by drop, the green comes down to one dark spoonful, and each drop costs you a little. You sit down halfway, as she said you would. Amaranth tastes the result, nods and puts it on the high shelf.\nopt.left.say.1 [9cf12d] = 一滴又一滴，那锅绿色浓缩成深色的一勺，每一滴都从你身上拿走一点。萃到一半你坐了下来，正如她说的那样。阿玛兰斯尝了尝成果，点点头，把它放上了高架。\n# en: Ask what it was\nopt.right.label [210c34] = 问那是什么\n# en: \"Last term's soup,\" she says. You decide not to believe her, then decide you do. She shows you where to stop before it takes too much from you, and you distill half a cup, badly.\nopt.right.say.1 [b138c3] = “上学期的汤，”她说。你决定不信，然后又决定信。她教你在它从你身上拿走太多之前停手，你萃了半杯，萃得很糟。\n\n@event lesson_glamour\n# en: Pick a Card\ntitle [760613] = 抽一张牌\n# en: Professor Vey's glamour lesson is a card game. Each student glamours a plain card to look like the queen of stars, and Vey picks out the true queen from the pile. Nobody, she says, has ever made her pick wrong. \"Fool me, and you may skip the essay.\"\ntext [952f53] = 维伊教授的魅影课是一场纸牌游戏。每个学生把一张普通的牌伪装成星辰皇后，维伊从一堆牌里挑出真正的皇后。她说，从来没人让她挑错过。“骗过我，就可以免交论文。”\n# en: A velvet-curtained classroom, a professor in a violet coat fanning out a hand of playing cards toward a row of students.\nscene [dce887] = 挂着天鹅绒帘子的教室，一位穿紫罗兰色外套的教授把一手扑克牌扇形展开，朝向一排学生。\n# en: Glamour your card\nopt.left.label [0c0fb9] = 给你的牌施魅影\n# en: Vey picks your card, turns it over and finds a plain two. She laughs out loud, a real laugh, and the class goes silent. \"Well,\" she says. \"Well, well. No essay for you.\" By supper the whole tower knows, and Vey has started calling you \"my card sharp\".\nopt.left.say.1 [3f0a00] = 维伊挑中你的牌，翻过来，是一张普通的二。她放声大笑，真心的笑，全班鸦雀无声。“好，”她说，“好，好啊。你不用交论文了。”到晚饭时全塔都知道了，维伊开始叫你“我的老千”。\n# en: Vey passes over your card without a glance. \"The queen of stars,\" she says kindly, \"does not blush.\" But you saw how she chose: by the hands, not the cards.\nopt.left.say.2 [8f34d2] = 维伊看都没看就略过了你的牌。“星辰皇后，”她和气地说，“是不会脸红的。”可你看清了她是怎么挑的：看手，不看牌。\n# en: Watch how she catches them\nopt.right.label [be78bf] = 看她怎么识破\n# en: You watch her eyes instead of the cards. She never looks at the faces, only at the hands holding them. You write that down, and she sees you write it, and smiles.\nopt.right.say.1 [1511f9] = 你不看牌，看她的眼睛。她从不看牌面，只看拿牌的手。你把这点记下来，她看见你在记，笑了。\n\n@event lesson_mirror\n# en: Gently\ntitle [99b2bc] = 温柔些\n# en: Professor Vey teaches Mirror in an empty classroom with the curtains drawn, which is not like her. \"A mirror charm shows people themselves,\" she says. \"Do it gently. Most people have not asked to see.\" She stands to one side of every glass in the room.\ntext [0cc1d8] = 维伊教授在一间拉上窗帘的空教室里教镜像咒，这不像她。“镜像咒让人看见自己，”她说，“要温柔些。大多数人没要求看。”教室里每一面镜子，她都站在旁边避开。\n# en: A dim classroom with the curtains drawn, a hand mirror on every desk, a professor standing well to one side of the glass.\nscene [927d45] = 拉上窗帘的昏暗教室，每张桌上一面手镜，一位教授远远地站在镜子侧面。\n# en: Practise on yourself first\nopt.left.label [8a2034] = 先在自己身上练\n# en: The charm shows you yourself a moment too honestly. You practise until you can look at it without flinching. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [f32110] = 咒语让你看见的自己，诚实得过了一点。你一直练到能看着它不躲闪。差不多吧。\n# en: Ask why she avoids the glass\nopt.right.label [42aa1b] = 问她为什么躲开镜子\n# en: \"Vanity,\" says Vey, with a magnificent smile that answers nothing at all. But she lets you stay after the others leave, and shows you how to soften the charm's edges.\nopt.right.say.1 [ffe355] = “虚荣，”维伊说，露出一个华丽的笑容，什么也没回答。不过别人走后她让你留下，教你怎么把咒语的锋芒磨软一些。\n\n@event lesson_haste\n# en: Race the Hippogriff\ntitle [8af651] = 和鹰头马身有翼兽赛跑\n# en: Professor Brask teaches Haste on the flight field, with a stopwatch and a hippogriff that is faster than the spell. \"Beat her,\" she says to you. To the hippogriff she explains, at length and politely, that she is not to bite you if you win.\ntext [165778] = 布拉斯克教授在飞行场教疾速咒，拿着一块秒表，还有一头比咒语还快的鹰头马身有翼兽。“赢过她，”她对你说。然后她又对那头兽客客气气、详详细细地解释：你要是赢了，不许咬你。\n# en: A windy flight field marked with flags, a grey-haired professor holding a stopwatch, a student sprinting beside a hippogriff.\nscene [57e5ef] = 插着旗子的飞行场上风很大，一位灰发教授拿着秒表，一个学生在鹰头马身有翼兽旁边飞奔。\n# en: Race the hippogriff\nopt.left.label [691071] = 和它赛跑\n# en: You cross the flag a beak's length ahead. The hippogriff looks at Brask; Brask looks at the stopwatch, then at you. \"Hm,\" she says. From her, it's a medal.\nopt.left.say.1 [d51c8e] = 你领先一个喙的长度冲过旗子。那头兽看着布拉斯克；布拉斯克看着秒表，又看看你。“嗯，”她说。从她嘴里出来，这就是一枚奖章。\n# en: The hippogriff wins by a length and takes your hat as her prize. Brask tells a story about a student who lost to a goose. It ends with a bite. \"Moral: faster.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [4beef4] = 那头兽领先一个身位赢了，还把你的帽子叼走当奖品。布拉斯克讲了个学生输给一只鹅的故事。结尾是被咬了一口。“寓意：快点。”\n# en: Time the others first\nopt.right.label [f5b3cc] = 先给别人计时\n# en: You hold the stopwatch and watch the fast ones. They aren't running harder; they start the spell a step before they need it. Brask sees you notice, and hands you the next race.\nopt.right.say.1 [38d0ad] = 你拿着秒表看跑得快的人。他们并没有跑得更用力；他们在需要之前一步就起咒了。布拉斯克看出你注意到了，把下一场比赛交给了你。\n# en: Afterwards you ask her how to start the spell a step early yourself. She shows you, and remembers that you asked.\nopt.right.say.2 [dcbb36] = 之后你问她，自己该怎样提前一步起咒。她教了你，并且记住了你开口问过。\n\n@event lesson_beast_speech\n# en: The Mule Has Views\ntitle [ec4453] = 骡子有看法\n# en: Beast-speech, says Professor Brask, is mostly listening. She sits you in a stall beside an old mule who has, she says, \"views\". You are to hear them. The mule looks at you as if you're the one being taught.\ntext [460ee4] = 布拉斯克教授说，兽语大部分是倾听。她让你坐在一个畜栏里，旁边是一头老骡子，她说它“有看法”。你得听出来。骡子看着你，好像你才是来受教的那个。\n# en: A warm stall with straw on the floor, a student sitting cross-legged opposite a grey old mule who stares back without blinking.\nscene [13f579] = 铺着稻草的温暖畜栏，一个学生盘腿坐着，对面一头灰色老骡子一眨不眨地回瞪。\n# en: Sit with him all afternoon\nopt.left.label [32ae78] = 陪它坐一下午\n# en: In the fourth hour the mule's thoughts come through: mostly apples, and one long grievance about a farrier. Brask says \"Good,\" to the mule, not to you.\nopt.left.say.1 [b8cda1] = 第四个钟头，骡子的想法传了过来：大多是苹果，还有一大段对某个钉马掌的人的抱怨。布拉斯克说“好”，是对骡子说的，不是对你。\n# en: Try the spell straight away\nopt.right.label [59b22b] = 直接施咒\n# en: You cast before you've listened and get one word back, very clearly: \"Rude.\" Brask laughs once, loudly. It's the laugh of the term, and everyone in the stables heard it.\nopt.right.say.1 [9b7054] = 你还没听就施了咒，清清楚楚地收到一个词：“无礼。”布拉斯克大笑了一声。这是本学期的那一声笑，整个马厩都听见了。\n\n@event lesson_ward\n# en: Chalk\ntitle [e38b98] = 粉笔\n# en: At the top of the lecture tower, where the lamps burn dim on purpose, the umbral lecturer drills first-years in their Ward by throwing chalk. Hard, and without warning. \"A ward that only works when you're ready,\" says the lecturer, \"is a decoration.\"\ntext [0d49cc] = 讲堂塔顶上，灯故意点得很暗，暗影讲师用扔粉笔的方法训练新生的护界。扔得很狠，毫无预兆。“只在你准备好时才管用的护界，”讲师说，“是装饰品。”\n# en: A dim room at the top of a tower, low lamps, a lecturer mid-throw with a piece of chalk while students hold up shimmering wards.\nscene [066bb6] = 塔顶昏暗的房间，灯光低垂，一位讲师正扔出一截粉笔，学生们举着微微发光的护界。\n# en: Hold the ward all hour\nopt.left.label [2d1be2] = 撑住护界一整节课\n# en: The first chalk hits your forehead. The fifth bounces off the ward with a sound like a bell. By the end of the hour your ward is up before you know you've raised it.\nopt.left.say.1 [c53b86] = 第一截粉笔砸中你的额头。第五截从护界上弹开，响得像一声钟。到下课时，你的护界在你意识到之前就已经立起来了。\n# en: Watch the lecturer's wrist\nopt.right.label [965178] = 盯着讲师的手腕\n# en: You let the ward drop and watch how the throws begin. Three pieces of chalk find your forehead while you do. But you see it now: the shoulder moves first.\nopt.right.say.1 [2c86ff] = 你放下护界，观察每一次投掷是怎么开始的。这期间有三截粉笔砸中了你的额头。但你看出来了：先动的是肩膀。\n\n@event lesson_shadowstep\n# en: Where the Voice Was\ntitle [9b35f0] = 声音在的地方\n# en: Shadowstep is taught in the dark. The umbral lecturer puts out the last dim lamp and calls your name from somewhere else in the room. You have to arrive where the voice was before it moves. Somewhere, a chair is waiting for your shins.\ntext [05e14f] = 影步是在黑暗里教的。暗影讲师熄灭最后一盏暗灯，从房间别处叫你的名字。你得在声音移开之前，到达它刚才所在的地方。某处有一把椅子，正等着你的小腿。\n# en: A pitch-dark tower room with one dim lamp guttering out, the outline of a student stepping between deep shadows.\nscene [877abd] = 漆黑的塔中房间，一盏暗灯即将熄灭，一个学生的轮廓正踏入深深的阴影之间。\n# en: Step into the dark\nopt.left.label [2680ac] = 踏进黑暗\n# en: You step, and the dark folds, and you're across the room with a bruise from the chair. The voice is right beside you. \"Better,\" it says, and moves away again.\nopt.left.say.1 [25eb2c] = 你迈出一步，黑暗折叠起来，你已到了房间另一头，腿上被椅子撞出一块青。声音就在你身边。“好一点了，”它说，又移开了。\n# en: Wait for your eyes to adjust\nopt.right.label [565cb0] = 等眼睛适应\n# en: You stand still until the dark has edges. The lecturer, somewhere, says that's cheating, and that cheating is half of umbral magic. You step only once, but you step exactly.\nopt.right.say.1 [98d714] = 你一动不动，直到黑暗有了边缘。讲师在某处说这是作弊，而作弊是暗影魔法的一半。你只迈了一步，但这一步分毫不差。\n\n@event lesson_animate\n# en: The Chair That Follows\ntitle [d19868] = 跟着走的椅子\n# en: The Artifice homework is to animate something and live with it for a day. You pick a chair, which follows you to every lecture. The teapot is the other option. It has opinions about being carried.\ntext [d289ff] = 奇械术的作业是活化一样东西，和它一起过一天。你挑了一把椅子，它跟着你去上每一节课。另一个选项是茶壶，它对被人提着走颇有意见。\n# en: A workshop corridor, a wooden chair trotting after a student like a dog, a teapot waddling behind it.\nscene [ffbfad] = 工坊走廊里，一把木椅像小狗一样小跑着跟在一个学生身后，后面还摇摇摆摆跟着一只茶壶。\n# en: Live with the chair\nopt.left.label [76d3db] = 和椅子过一天\n# en: The chair follows you everywhere and offers itself whenever you stop, including on the stairs, at speed. By evening you have bruised shins and a very loyal chair.\nopt.left.say.1 [8427ee] = 椅子走到哪儿跟到哪儿，你一停下它就凑上来让你坐，包括在楼梯上、全速的时候。到傍晚，你的小腿青一块紫一块，换来一把非常忠心的椅子。\n# en: Animate the teapot instead\nopt.right.label [4d6ebe] = 改为活化茶壶\n# en: The teapot waddles after you all day, pouring when you sit. It takes more out of you than the chair would, but your friends have never been so well looked after.\nopt.right.say.1 [fd76ab] = 茶壶摇摇摆摆地跟了你一整天，你一坐下它就倒茶。它比椅子更耗你的力气，但你的朋友们从没被照顾得这么周到过。\n\n@event lesson_seal\n# en: Only for the Addressee\ntitle [372311] = 只给收信人\n# en: Today's Hollow Studies exercise: seal a letter so that only the person it's addressed to can open it. The headmistress will try to open it first. \"If I can,\" she says mildly, \"you haven't.\"\ntext [f31824] = 今天空洞研究的练习：封好一封信，让只有收信人才能打开。院长会先试着打开。“要是我打得开，”她温和地说，“你就没封住。”\n# en: A windowless room, a student pressing their thumb to a folded letter while a headmistress watches with her hands folded.\nscene [7faf91] = 一间没有窗户的房间，一个学生把拇指按在一封折好的信上，一位院长双手交叠地看着。\n# en: Seal it against her\nopt.left.label [8b087d] = 封住，防着她\n# en: She tries the letter for a long minute with one finger, then hands it back unopened. \"Better than I was at your age,\" she says, which doesn't sound like a compliment. You feel hollowed out till supper.\nopt.left.say.1 [42dc8e] = 她用一根手指试了足足一分钟，然后把信原封不动地还给你。“比我在你这个年纪时强，”她说，听上去不像夸奖。直到晚饭你都觉得被掏空了。\n# en: Ask how she'd break it\nopt.right.label [902617] = 问她会怎么破解\n# en: She shows you, kindly and very quickly, how she'd break yours. You leave the room knowing a great deal more about seals and rather less sure of your own.\nopt.right.say.1 [6b2748] = 她和气地、飞快地给你演示了她会怎么破你的封。离开时你对封缄懂得多了，对自己的封却没那么有把握了。\n\n@event lesson_wildfire\n# en: Longer Each Night\ntitle [8c3b43] = 一夜长过一夜\n# en: Lyra wants you in the duel hall after hours. \"You let it go too early,\" she says, and lights her own. \"Hold it. Longer each night. It will bite you; let it.\"\ntext [4e22f7] = 莱拉要你下课后来决斗厅。“你松手松得太早了，”她说着点起自己的火，“握住它。一夜比一夜久。它会咬你；让它咬。”\n# en: An empty duel hall at night, two students each holding a growing fire, one watching the other's with narrowed eyes.\nscene [1f034f] = 夜里空荡荡的决斗厅，两个学生各自握着一团不断长大的火，其中一人眯着眼盯着另一人的火。\n# en: Hold it until it bites\nopt.left.label [1580f1] = 握到它咬人\n# en: You hold it until the heat is in your teeth and then a breath longer. When you let it go it runs to the rafters. Lyra looks at your blistered palm and says nothing, which is approval.\nopt.left.say.1 [37eafe] = 你一直握到热气钻进牙缝，然后再多握一口气。一松手，它直奔房梁。莱拉看着你起泡的掌心，什么也没说，这就是认可。\n# en: Ask her how she stands it\nopt.right.label [be5a97] = 问她怎么受得了\n# en: She doesn't answer at first. Then she shows you her palms, scarred and old, and tells you about the first time, and her mother. You don't practise much. It's still the right night.\nopt.right.say.1 [d54a01] = 她起初没回答。然后她给你看了她的掌心，伤痕累累，是旧伤，讲了第一次的事，还有她的母亲。你没怎么练。这一晚依然是对的。\n\n@event lesson_heartwood\n# en: A Tree by the Window\ntitle [2d71f6] = 窗边的一棵树\n# en: Tobias wants you to grow a heartwood every evening for a week, \"so your hands remember\". Today he suggests the courtyard under your dormitory window, where nothing has grown in years.\ntext [ad5103] = 托比亚斯要你连续一周每晚都种一棵心木，“好让你的手记住”。今天他提议种在你宿舍窗下的庭院里，那里好几年没长过东西了。\n# en: A dormitory courtyard at dusk, a young tree that wasn't there that morning, two students sitting under it looking pleased with themselves.\nscene [c8ec59] = 黄昏的宿舍庭院，一棵早上还不存在的小树，两个学生坐在树下，一脸得意。\n# en: Grow it properly, roots and all\nopt.left.label [c091b7] = 好好种，连根带枝\n# en: It takes everything you've got and a blister. By dusk there's a young tree under your window, and your back aches like a harvest. Tobias says it's the best one he's seen, and apologises for saying so.\nopt.left.say.1 [8496a0] = 这用尽了你所有的力气，还磨出一个水泡。到黄昏，你窗下立着一棵小树，你的背酸得像收了一季麦子。托比亚斯说这是他见过最好的一棵，又为这么说道了歉。\n# en: Sit under his instead\nopt.right.label [709e57] = 改坐在他的树下\n# en: You let him grow it and sit under it with him, talking about nothing. Your hands don't remember much. The rest of you does.\nopt.right.say.1 [a1dc24] = 你让他来种，和他一起坐在树下闲聊。你的手没记住多少。你其余的部分记住了。\n\n@event lesson_omen\n# en: The Spoon Will Fall\ntitle [67cae0] = 勺子会掉\n# en: At breakfast Seraphine says, without looking up, \"Practise on small things. Spoons. Doors. The weather. Never people, not yet.\" Then she looks at you, quite clearly, for once.\ntext [55b703] = 早饭时，瑟拉芬头也不抬地说：“在小事上练。勺子。门。天气。别用在人身上，还不行。”然后她看着你，难得地清清楚楚。\n# en: A refectory table at breakfast, a pale student pointing at a spoon balanced on the edge of a bowl, a second student staring at it.\nscene [0de841] = 早饭时膳堂的长桌，一个苍白的学生指着一把搭在碗沿上的勺子，另一个学生盯着它看。\n# en: Foretell small things all day\nopt.left.label [5b12d1] = 一整天预言小事\n# en: The spoon falls. The door sticks. It rains at four. By evening it unnerves you how well it works, and you catch yourself foretelling the soup.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a1997] = 勺子掉了。门卡住了。四点下雨了。到傍晚，它灵验得让你发毛，你发现自己在预言汤。\n# en: Ask what \"not yet\" means\nopt.right.label [daf7eb] = 问“还不行”是什么意思\n# en: \"Later,\" she says, then, \"I mean that exactly.\" She doesn't explain. She does pass you the salt before you've asked for it.\nopt.right.say.1 [9395cc] = “以后，”她说，接着说，“我就是字面意思。”她没解释。不过你还没开口，她就把盐递了过来。\n\n@event lesson_sleight\n# en: Pockets and Decks\ntitle [d97053] = 口袋与牌堆\n# en: Corvin is teaching you to keep your hand in. \"Decks are practice,\" he says, shuffling. \"Pockets are performance.\" Bram is asleep in an armchair nearby, his whistle on a ribbon round his neck.\ntext [e4450d] = 科尔文在教你保持手感。“牌堆是练习，”他洗着牌说，“口袋是表演。”附近一张扶手椅上，布拉姆睡着了，他的哨子用缎带挂在脖子上。\n# en: A dim corner of a common room, a grinning student fanning a deck of cards while another student palms a coin under the table.\nscene [f184cd] = 公共休息室昏暗的角落，一个咧嘴笑的学生展开一副牌，另一个学生在桌下藏起一枚硬币。\n# en: Practise on the deck\nopt.left.label [c7e5c3] = 拿牌堆练\n# en: Two hours of coins and cards until your fingers cramp. It's dull, and by the end the coin vanishes so cleanly even Corvin blinks. \"Saint,\" he says, \"with very quick hands.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [d449a1] = 两个钟头的硬币和纸牌，练到手指抽筋。很枯燥，但到最后硬币消失得干净利落，连科尔文都眨了眨眼。“圣人，”他说，“手很快的那种。”\n# en: Lift Bram's whistle and return it\nopt.right.label [66b534] = 摸走布拉姆的哨子再还回去\n# en: The whistle comes off its ribbon without a sound and goes back on it the same way. Bram snores through all of it. Corvin is delighted and slightly worried about you.\nopt.right.say.1 [e0a626] = 哨子无声无息地离开缎带，又同样无声无息地回去。布拉姆从头打鼾到尾。科尔文乐坏了，又有点替你担心。\n\n@event lesson_contraption\n# en: The Breakfast Engine\ntitle [42c819] = 早餐引擎\n# en: Mir slides a breakfast tray across to you. \"Practice! Make it do breakfast. Toast, butter, tea. It's the hardest machine there is, probably.\" Half the refectory has already turned to watch.\ntext [d21132] = 米尔把一只早餐托盘推到你面前。“练习！让它做早餐。吐司、黄油、茶。这是世上最难的机器，大概吧。”半个膳堂已经转过头来看了。\n# en: A breakfast table where a clattering machine made of a toast rack, a spoon and a teapot is buttering toast at alarming speed.\nscene [be285b] = 早餐桌上，一台由吐司架、勺子和茶壶拼成的机器正咔嗒作响，以惊人的速度往吐司上抹黄油。\n# en: Build the breakfast engine\nopt.left.label [9d4c5d] = 造早餐引擎\n# en: Your engine butters four slices, pours one cup and singes your eyebrow before it falls apart in a shower of crumbs. The refectory applauds. Mir writes something down, beaming.\nopt.left.say.1 [cf3c7b] = 你的引擎抹了四片黄油，倒了一杯茶，燎了你一根眉毛，然后在一阵面包屑雨中散了架。膳堂鼓起掌来。米尔笑容满面地记下了什么。\n# en: Build it with Mir, slowly\nopt.right.label [500d56] = 和米尔慢慢造\n# en: You build it together, Mir explaining every joint. It makes one perfect slice of toast and then retires. Mir says it's the best machine of the year, probably.\nopt.right.say.1 [9461a7] = 你们一起造，米尔讲解每一个接头。它做出了一片完美的吐司，然后退休了。米尔说这是今年最好的机器，大概吧。\n\n@event lesson_hoarfrost\n# en: Putting Out Fires\ntitle [3a4863] = 灭火\n# en: \"It's for putting fires out,\" Kael says again, and nods across the duel hall to where Lyra is practising, very loudly, with a very large flame. He waits to see whether you'll take the hint.\ntext [a79905] = “这是用来灭火的，”凯尔又说了一遍，朝决斗厅另一头点点头，莱拉正在那儿非常响亮地练着一团非常大的火。他等着看你会不会领会。\n# en: A duel hall where one student's flame is frosting over and going out, its caster glaring, a quiet student standing nearby.\nscene [812686] = 一座决斗厅，一个学生的火焰正在结霜熄灭，施法者怒目而视，一个安静的学生站在旁边。\n# en: Put out Lyra's flame\nopt.left.label [d5a941] = 冻熄莱拉的火\n# en: Your frost meets her flame and they cancel with a hiss and a cloud of steam. Lyra's face, when it clears, is magnificent. Kael makes a sound you think is a laugh.\nopt.left.say.1 [6a4da5] = 你的霜迎上她的火，两者嘶的一声在一团蒸汽中抵消了。蒸汽散去时莱拉的表情精彩极了。凯尔发出一个你觉得是笑的声音。\n# en: Practise on the braziers instead\nopt.right.label [61fdba] = 改拿火盆练\n# en: You frost the braziers one by one. It's useful, and dull, and Kael stays with you for all of it without saying anything. You suspect that was the point.\nopt.right.say.1 [e38212] = 你一个个给火盆结霜。有用，也枯燥，凯尔一直陪着你，一句话没说。你怀疑这才是重点。\n\n@event lesson_candour\n# en: Questions People Want to Answer\ntitle [a15e8c] = 别人想回答的问题\n# en: Nessa hands you a notebook in the refectory. \"Three people, three true answers, by the end of lunch,\" she says. \"Ask the question they want to answer. Use the spell only when you have to.\"\ntext [d16f34] = 内莎在膳堂递给你一个笔记本。“三个人，三个真话，午饭结束前，”她说，“问他们想回答的问题。只有不得已时才用咒语。”\n# en: A busy refectory, a bossy editor pointing out students at random tables to another student holding a notebook.\nscene [487f75] = 热闹的膳堂，一位颐指气使的编辑随手指着各桌的学生，另一个学生拿着笔记本。\n# en: Use the spell on all three\nopt.left.label [38e831] = 三个人都用咒语\n# en: Three truths by the pudding course, and one of them is about you, and not kind. Nessa reads your notes and says, \"Now you know why I don't use it much.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [5231a1] = 布丁上桌时你已经拿到三句真话，其中一句是关于你的，而且不太客气。内莎读完你的笔记说：“现在你知道我为什么不常用了。”\n# en: Ask good questions instead\nopt.right.label [30a254] = 只凭问得好\n# en: You only manage two truths without the spell, but they're good ones, and nobody minded telling you. Nessa says that's the better trick, and that she won't print either.\nopt.right.say.1 [e030fc] = 不用咒语你只拿到两句真话，但都很好，而且没人介意告诉你。内莎说这是更好的本事，还说两句都不会登。\n\n@event lesson_brace\n# en: Planted\ntitle [5ea443] = 站稳\n# en: The eyepatched dummy is empty now, but it still throws a hex or two out of habit. The Circle uses it to practise bracing. Today the seniors are taking bets on how long you'll stand.\ntext [f44646] = 那个戴眼罩的假人现在空了，但出于习惯还会扔出一两道恶咒。决斗社用它练坚守。今天高年级生们在打赌你能站多久。\n# en: A duel hall's practice corner, an eyepatched dummy firing weak hexes at a student who stands planted and unmoving.\nscene [c00534] = 决斗厅的练习角落，一个戴眼罩的假人朝一个学生扔出软弱的恶咒，学生站得稳稳的，一动不动。\n# en: Stand until it gives up\nopt.left.label [727463] = 站到它放弃\n# en: You stand there for an hour while the dummy throws everything it has left. The seniors pay out to whoever bet longest. Your shins are a mess and you are unmovable.\nopt.left.say.1 [835744] = 你在那儿站了一个小时，假人把剩下的一切都扔了过来。高年级生们赔钱给押时间最长的人。你的小腿一塌糊涂，人却纹丝不动。\n# en: Coach the first-years instead\nopt.right.label [6d90f5] = 改去指导新生\n# en: You stand behind a row of first-years and fix their feet, one by one, while they get hexed. They're grateful. You're bored. The dummy throws one at you on the way out, out of spite.\nopt.right.say.1 [082447] = 你站在一排新生身后，一个个纠正他们的站姿，他们挨着恶咒。他们很感激。你很无聊。你出门时假人朝你扔了一道，纯属泄愤。\n\n@event lesson_quickquill\n# en: Lecture Notes\ntitle [c0b410] = 课堂笔记\n# en: Your quill wants to take your lecture notes for you. It writes everything down, word for word, faster than the lecturer can talk. It also writes what it thinks of the lecturer, in the margins.\ntext [bbcb65] = 你的羽毛笔想替你记课堂笔记。它一字不落，比讲师说得还快。它还在页边写下了它对讲师的看法。\n# en: A lecture hall, a quill writing busily by itself in a notebook while its owner sits back with folded arms, the lecturer squinting at them.\nscene [a03909] = 一间讲堂，一支羽毛笔在笔记本上自己忙着写，主人抱着胳膊往后靠，讲师眯眼看着他们。\n# en: Let it take the notes\nopt.left.label [468c14] = 让它记笔记\n# en: You have perfect notes, every word, and a margin full of the quill's views on the lecturer's waistcoat. You spend the evening inking them out before anyone sees.\nopt.left.say.1 [52f4b1] = 你有了完美的笔记，一字不差，还有满满一页边羽毛笔对讲师马甲的高见。你花了一晚上用墨涂掉它们，免得被人看见。\n# en: Make it take only what you say\nopt.right.label [db7273] = 让它只记你说的\n# en: It sulks, and then takes down exactly what you tell it, no more. It's work keeping it honest. You come out with half the notes and the full trust of your quill.\nopt.right.say.1 [847ea4] = 它闹了会儿脾气，然后一字不多地记下你让它记的。管住它不说谎可费劲。你只得到一半的笔记，却得到了羽毛笔全部的信任。\n\n@event lesson_bramble\n# en: A Hedge on Demand\ntitle [680c51] = 随叫随到的树篱\n# en: Old Maud has heard about your hedge. \"There's a gap in my fence the rabbits use, dearie,\" she says. \"Grow me one there. Thorns on the outside, mind.\" The rabbits are watching from the cabbages.\ntext [729710] = 老莫德听说了你的树篱。“我篱笆上有个缺口，兔子老从那儿钻，亲爱的，”她说，“给我种一道。刺朝外，记着。”兔子们在卷心菜地里看着。\n# en: Old Maud's cottage garden, a groundskeeper pointing at a gap in her fence while a student coaxes a thorny hedge up out of the ground to fill it.\nscene [dea0bc] = 老莫德小屋的花园，一位园丁指着篱笆上的缺口，一个学生正哄着一道带刺的树篱从地里长出来填补它。\n# en: Grow it thorns and all\nopt.left.label [3d5051] = 连刺一起种\n# en: The hedge comes up thick and thorny, and takes a strip off your knuckles on the way. Maud inspects it and says it'll do. The rabbits look betrayed.\nopt.left.say.1 [67c5a8] = 树篱长得又密又多刺，冒出来时还刮掉了你指节上一层皮。莫德检查了一番，说行。兔子们一脸被背叛。\n# en: Grow it gently, for the cabbages\nopt.right.label [529a7c] = 种得温和些，为了卷心菜\n# en: You grow it soft and plant a row of cabbages beyond it for the rabbits. Maud laughs until she has to sit down. \"You'll understand that in two weeks,\" she says.\nopt.right.say.1 [2c37a8] = 你把它种得很软，又在外面给兔子们种了一排卷心菜。莫德笑得不得不坐下。“过两个星期你就明白了，”她说。\n\n@event lesson_index\n# en: The Right Page\ntitle [8bae11] = 对的那一页\n# en: Every book you touch falls open at the page you need, now. The trouble is the library knows it, and books are leaving their shelves to come and find you. Footnote is sitting on the biggest.\ntext [7d6f48] = 现在你碰到的每本书都会翻到你需要的那一页。麻烦在于图书馆知道了，书纷纷离开书架来找你。脚注坐在最大的那本上。\n# en: A library table piled with heavy books, every one falling open at a different marked page as a student touches it, a cat watching from the top of the pile.\nscene [40e94c] = 一张堆满厚书的图书馆长桌，学生碰到哪本，哪本就翻开到不同的标记页，一只猫在书堆顶上看着。\n# en: Read every page they offer\nopt.left.label [019527] = 读完它们送上的每一页\n# en: You read every page that offers itself until the lamps go out. You learn a great deal, most of it useful, and none of it what you came in for.\nopt.left.say.1 [1c13c9] = 你读每一页送上门来的书，直到灯熄。你学到很多，大部分有用，没有一样是你来找的。\n# en: Find one thing for your exams\nopt.right.label [f27744] = 只找一样考试要的\n# en: You ask for one thing, firmly. Twelve books bring it. You take your page and leave the others, who sulk on the table until a clerk shelves them.\nopt.right.say.1 [31f3f8] = 你坚定地只要一样东西。十二本书都送来了。你拿了你那一页就走，其余的在桌上生闷气，直到一位职员把它们放回去。\n\n@event lesson_rapport\n# en: A Room That Was Turning\ntitle [660228] = 一屋子正要翻脸的人\n# en: Two tables in the refectory are on the edge of a food fight, and every head is turning. It's the Newt all over again, with worse gravy.\ntext [9b4121] = 膳堂里两张桌子眼看要打一场食物大战，所有人都转过头来。这简直是蝾螈酒馆重演，只是肉汁更难吃。\n# en: A refectory at supper where two tables are on the edge of an argument, a student stepping between them with a raised mug and a grin.\nscene [078221] = 晚饭时的膳堂，两张桌子眼看要吵起来，一个学生举着杯子笑着走到中间。\n# en: Step in and turn it\nopt.left.label [fbcbbf] = 上前扭转局面\n# en: One joke about the gravy, at exactly the right moment, and both tables laugh instead of throwing it. The prefects look relieved and slightly suspicious. Your heart doesn't slow down until bed.\nopt.left.say.1 [5bef0c] = 在恰好的时刻说一句关于肉汁的笑话，两桌人笑了，没有扔。级长们松了口气，又有点起疑。你的心跳直到睡前才慢下来。\n# en: Let it happen\nopt.right.label [e54ca5] = 随它去\n# en: You let it go, and the food fight is magnificent. You watch from under the table, laughing. Nobody remembers it was nearly stopped.\nopt.right.say.1 [6bc4ce] = 你随它去，这场食物大战壮观极了。你躲在桌子底下笑着看。没人记得它差点被拦下。\n\n@event lesson_tailwind\n# en: Never Stop\ntitle [fd9083] = 永不停下\n# en: The fisherman's rule for the wind is \"never stop\". You take the long way round the lake on your free afternoon to practise it, and the wind gets behind you as soon as you're off the jetty.\ntext [c7f206] = 渔夫关于风的规矩是“永不停下”。你在空闲的下午绕湖远飞去练，刚离开码头，风就绕到了你身后。\n# en: A student on a broom skimming low over a lake at tremendous speed, a wake of spray behind them and a fisherman waving from a boat.\nscene [5023b4] = 一个学生骑扫帚贴着湖面极速掠过，身后溅起一道水花，一个渔夫在船上挥手。\n# en: Never stop\nopt.left.label [dd27a3] = 永不停下\n# en: You go round the lake three times without stopping, faster each lap, until your hands are numb on the handle. The fisherman waves every lap now.\nopt.left.say.1 [dedab4] = 你绕湖三圈，一刻不停，一圈比一圈快，直到握扫帚柄的手冻麻了。现在渔夫每一圈都挥手。\n# en: Land and talk to him\nopt.right.label [2bd58c] = 落下来和他聊聊\n# en: You land by his boat and tell him about the storm run. He listens, nods, and says, \"Never stop,\" again, and hands you a fish to take home.\nopt.right.say.1 [dc479a] = 你落在他的船边，给他讲了风暴那趟。他听着，点点头，又说了一遍“永不停下”，递给你一条鱼带回家。\n\n@event lesson_lodestar\n# en: The Lighthouse Light\ntitle [20cc48] = 灯塔之光\n# en: The light gathers round your shoulders in Grimsby's shop without your meaning it to, and the old man puts down his pen. \"Lodestar,\" he says softly. \"A lighthouse keeper's spell. She used it to walk the lamp-stair in storms.\" He doesn't say who she was. You think you know.\ntext [375d35] = 在格林斯比的店里，光不由自主地聚到了你肩头，老人放下了笔。“引星，”他轻声说。“灯塔看守人的咒语。她在暴风雨里靠它走灯塔的楼梯。”他没说她是谁。你觉得你知道。\n# en: An old bookseller in a dark shop watching a soft guiding light gather around a student's shoulders, his spectacles reflecting it.\nscene [7fe392] = 昏暗的书店里，一位老书商看着一团柔和的引路之光聚在学生的肩头，眼镜上映着那光。\n# en: Ask him to watch you cast it\nopt.left.label [ee990d] = 请他看你施咒\n# en: You let the light build, breath by breath, until it stands round you like a lantern. Grimsby nods along to your breathing, as if to a tune he knows. \"Slower,\" he says. \"She was always slower.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [90c2df] = 你让光一口气一口气地聚起来，直到它像一盏灯笼一样围着你。格林斯比随着你的呼吸点头，像在跟一支他熟悉的曲子。“慢一点，”他说。“她总是更慢一些。”\n# en: Ask about the lighthouse\nopt.right.label [d77ac4] = 问问灯塔的事\n# en: He tells you about the lamp, the stair, the keeper who wanted to see a long way, and the books she bought from him for forty years. The light round your shoulders stays lit the whole time, listening.\nopt.right.say.1 [824783] = 他跟你讲那盏灯，那道楼梯，那个想看得很远的看守人，还有她四十年来从他这儿买的书。你肩头的光一直亮着，一直在听。\n\n@event lesson_moonwell\n# en: Sitting Still\ntitle [41cd49] = 静坐\n# en: Old Maud finds you on her garden bench at dusk, trying to make Moonwell go faster. She sits down beside you. \"That's your trouble,\" she says. \"It's a sitting-still spell, dearie. You can't hurry a basin.\"\ntext [b0f0a7] = 黄昏时，老莫德发现你坐在她花园的长凳上，想让月泉来得快一点。她在你身边坐下。“这就是你的毛病，”她说。“这是个静坐的咒语，亲爱的。石盆是催不来的。”\n# en: An old woman and a student sitting on a garden bench under the moon, eyes closed, a faint silver light pooling in the student's cupped hands.\nscene [8c5c9d] = 月光下，一位老妇人和一个学生坐在花园长凳上，闭着眼，学生捧着的双手里积着一点淡淡的银光。\n# en: Sit still with her\nopt.left.label [54e6c0] = 陪她静坐\n# en: You sit until the moon is up and your feet are cold. Somewhere in the second hour, the light starts pooling, and you stop trying, and it fills. Maud is asleep. You don't wake her.\nopt.left.say.1 [085c4a] = 你一直坐到月亮升起，脚都冷了。第二个小时里的某一刻，光开始积聚，你不再用力，它就满了。莫德睡着了。你没叫醒她。\n# en: Ask how she knows the spell\nopt.right.label [be9857] = 问她怎么会这个咒语\n# en: \"Learned it in the wood,\" says Maud, \"same as you, sixty years back. Everybody who goes deep enough learns it eventually. The ones who come back, anyway.\" She pats your knee as if that were reassuring.\nopt.right.say.1 [0e8952] = “在林子里学的，”莫德说，“跟你一样，六十年前。走得够深的人迟早都会学会。至少是那些回来了的人。”她拍拍你的膝盖，好像这话很让人安心。\n\n@event lesson_reflect\n# en: Hall of Mirrors\ntitle [785523] = 镜廊\n# en: Professor Vey hears you've learned Reflect and appears in the mirror corridor with a bag of very small, very harmless hexes. \"Show me,\" Vey says, and throws one at your head.\ntext [64d864] = 维伊教授听说你学会了反照，就带着一袋非常小、非常无害的恶咒出现在镜廊里。“露一手，”维伊说，然后朝你脑袋扔了一个。\n# en: A theatrical professor in a corridor of mirrors throwing a small spell at a student, who sends it bouncing back down the corridor.\nscene [08a5e9] = 镜子走廊里，一位很有舞台感的教授朝一个学生扔出一个小咒语，学生把它沿着走廊弹了回去。\n# en: Send it back\nopt.left.label [aed0d3] = 弹回去\n# en: You catch the hex and return it. Vey ducks, delighted, and throws three. You return two. The third gives you rabbit ears for an hour, which Vey says is the lesson.\nopt.left.say.1 [7f4a36] = 你接住恶咒，弹了回去。维伊高兴地一躲，扔了三个。你弹回去两个。第三个让你长了一小时的兔耳朵，维伊说这就是这一课。\n# en: Send it somewhere else\nopt.right.label [92bf64] = 弹到别处去\n# en: You catch the hex and bounce it off a mirror into a suit of armour, which sneezes. Vey laughs so hard the mirrors ring. \"Oh, you're going to be trouble.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [50f320] = 你接住恶咒，让它在一面镜子上弹了一下，飞进一副盔甲里，盔甲打了个喷嚏。维伊笑得镜子都在响。“噢，你以后会是个大麻烦。”\n\n@event lesson_rime\n# en: Cold Enough\ntitle [207a71] = 够冷了\n# en: Kael sees the frost at your collar and goes still. \"Rime,\" he says. \"Where did you... no. Don't tell me.\" He looks at {season == \"winter\": the snow|the courtyard, and frost runs out from his boots across the flagstones}. \"In the North we learn it as children. You don't feel the cold less. You just stop letting it decide things.\"\ntext [2f332e] = 凯尔看见你衣领上的霜，一下子僵住了。“雾凇，”他说。“你从哪儿……不。别告诉我。”他看着{season == \"winter\": 雪|庭院，霜从他的靴底蔓延出去，铺满了石板}。“在北方，我们小时候就学这个。你并不会觉得没那么冷。你只是不再让冷来替你做决定。”\n# en: A pale Northern student and another student standing in a frost-whitened courtyard, frost spreading from their collars, breath steaming.\nscene [895b91] = 结满白霜的庭院里，一个苍白的北方学生和另一个学生站着，霜从他们的衣领蔓延开，呼吸冒着白气。\n# en: Let him show you\nopt.left.label [a35ef6] = 让他教你\n# en: He makes you stand in {season == \"winter\": the snow|the cold he's laid on the flagstones} until it hurts, and then until it doesn't, and then a while longer. When you go in, he's almost smiling. \"Now it's yours,\" he says.\nopt.left.say.1 [e6b6d8] = 他让你站在{season == \"winter\": 雪里|他铺在石板上的寒气里}，一直站到疼，再站到不疼，然后再多站一会儿。你们进屋时，他几乎在笑。“现在它是你的了，”他说。\n# en: Ask what else they learn as children\nopt.right.label [4921a8] = 问他们小时候还学什么\n# en: \"Not to want things,\" he says, and then looks as if he wishes he hadn't. He leaves before you can ask anything else. The frost at your collar feels colder.\nopt.right.say.1 [d5279c] = “不去想要东西，”他说，然后一副后悔说出口的样子。你还没来得及再问，他就走了。衣领上的霜感觉更冷了。\n\n@event lesson_siphon\n# en: The Fern on the Sill\ntitle [82797f] = 窗台上的蕨\n# en: You're tired, and there are lectures at eight, and there's a fern on your windowsill, green and full of life it isn't using. Siphon is right there in your hands. Nobody would ever know but the fern.\ntext [73569c] = 你很累，明早八点还有课，窗台上有一盆蕨，绿油油的，满是它用不上的生命。虹吸就在你手里。除了那盆蕨，没人会知道。\n# en: A student at a dormitory window at night with a hand hovering over a potted fern, the fern's leaves beginning to curl.\nscene [2f67ab] = 夜里，一个学生站在宿舍窗前，手悬在一盆蕨上方，蕨叶开始卷曲。\n# en: Just a little\nopt.left.label [ee6350] = 就一点点\n# en: The fern greys from the tips inward. You feel wonderful. You throw the fern out in the morning, before anyone sees it.\nopt.left.say.1 [d7ac4f] = 蕨从叶尖开始往里变灰。你感觉好极了。早上，你赶在别人看见之前把那盆蕨扔了。\n# en: Go to bed tired\nopt.right.label [6f4bc5] = 累着去睡觉\n# en: You go to bed tired, and wake tired, and the fern is green. You water it, which feels like an apology to something.\nopt.right.say.1 [f67a10] = 你累着上了床，累着醒来，那盆蕨是绿的。你给它浇了水，感觉像是在向什么东西道歉。\n\n@event lesson_compel\n# en: \"Again\"\ntitle [357cd7] = “再来”\n# en: A first-year drops a stack of books in the corridor, and laughs at themselves, and you could say the word. \"Again.\" They'd drop them again, and again, and never know why. It would be funny. That's the worst part: it would be funny.\ntext [18a208] = 一个一年级生在走廊里把一摞书掉在了地上，还自嘲地笑了，而你可以说出那个词。“再来。”他们会把书再掉一次，一次又一次，永远不知道为什么。那会很好笑。这才是最糟的：那会很好笑。\n# en: A student in a castle corridor watching another student drop a stack of books, a word forming on their lips.\nscene [84d873] = 城堡走廊里，一个学生看着另一个学生掉了一摞书，嘴唇正在组成一个词。\n# en: Say it, once\nopt.left.label [d536d0] = 说一次\n# en: They drop the books again, and stare at their hands. You walk on quickly. It wasn't funny.\nopt.left.say.1 [b16160] = 他们又把书掉了，盯着自己的手。你快步走开了。一点也不好笑。\n# en: Help pick them up\nopt.right.label [aa8624] = 帮忙捡起来\n# en: You pick up half the books and the first-year thanks you twice. The word stays in your mouth, unused, like a stone you could throw.\nopt.right.say.1 [814c2f] = 你捡起了一半的书，那个一年级生谢了你两次。那个词留在你嘴里，没用出去，像一块你本可以扔出去的石头。\n\n@event lesson_torment\n# en: The Shape of It\ntitle [0e26e7] = 它的形状\n# en: Sparring in the duelling hall, you feel Torment rise in your hand like a reflex. You stop it. Professor Thorne, across the hall, saw something: he's looking at you the way he looks at a fire in the wrong place.\ntext [a99348] = 在决斗厅里对练时，你感到折磨像一种本能一样在你手里升起。你止住了它。决斗厅那头的索恩教授看见了什么：他看你的眼神，像在看一团烧错了地方的火。\n# en: A stern grey-bearded professor in a duelling hall looking hard at a student whose wand hand has frozen mid-cast.\nscene [dfc983] = 决斗厅里，一位严厉的灰胡子教授紧盯着一个学生，学生的持杖手僵在施咒的半途。\n# en: Tell him\nopt.left.label [bcfe3c] = 告诉他\n# en: You tell him. He listens without a word, then says, \"Then you know what it costs. Most people learn that afterwards.\" He gives you extra drills for a month, which you suspect is kindness.\nopt.left.say.1 [48273a] = 你告诉了他。他一言不发地听着，然后说：“那你就知道它的代价了。大多数人是事后才知道的。”他给你加了一个月的训练，你怀疑这是一种好意。\n# en: As you leave he adds that he lost to the storm because he didn't stop in time. You knew already. You've never heard him say it like that.\nopt.left.say.2 [79fd1d] = 你要走时他补了一句：他输给那场风暴，是因为没有及时停手。你早就知道了。但你从没听他这样说过。\n# en: Say it was nothing\nopt.right.label [4f60bd] = 说没什么\n# en: \"Nothing, sir.\" He doesn't believe you. He doesn't say so. He watches you spar for the rest of the term.\nopt.right.say.1 [596353] = “没什么，先生。”他不信。他没说出来。那一学期剩下的时间里，他一直看着你对练。\n\n@event lesson_extraction\n# en: Someone Else's Spark\ntitle [3e3d67] = 别人的火花\n# en: In a study group, the student beside you is casting a little light to read by, and you can feel their magic like warmth from a stove. Extraction would take it. They'd feel tired, that's all. You've got so much to do.\ntext [36f616] = 在学习小组里，你旁边的学生点着一点光照着读书，你能感觉到他们的魔力，像炉子的热气。汲取能把它拿走。他们只会觉得累，仅此而已。而你还有那么多事要做。\n# en: A study group around a library table at night, one student's quill glowing with magic, another student staring at it hungrily.\nscene [8b35f2] = 夜里图书馆桌旁的学习小组，一个学生的羽毛笔闪着魔光，另一个学生饥渴地盯着它。\n# en: Take a little\nopt.left.label [5e0faf] = 拿一点点\n# en: Their light gutters. They yawn, rub their eyes, and go to bed early. You study until dawn on someone else's magic, and remember none of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [2b4d40] = 他们的光暗了下去。他们打了个哈欠，揉揉眼睛，早早去睡了。你靠别人的魔力学到了天亮，什么都没记住。\n# en: Lend them yours instead\nopt.right.label [c52238] = 把你的借给他们\n# en: You light the table yourself so they can rest their hand. They grin at you. The hunger goes quiet, for now.\nopt.right.say.1 [fdc096] = 你自己把桌子照亮，好让他们歇歇手。他们冲你咧嘴一笑。那份饥渴安静了下来，暂时。\n\n@event lesson_eclipse\n# en: The Burn\ntitle [ef83b1] = 烫伤\n# en: You burn your hand on a cauldron in the lab, badly. And Eclipse wakes up: the pain could be power, it says, all of it, held and ready. You'd still feel the burn. You'd just have something to show for it.\ntext [c2aa09] = 你在实验室里被坩埚烫伤了手，烫得很厉害。然后蚀醒了：它说，这疼痛可以变成力量，全部，攒着，随时可用。你还是会感觉到烫伤。你只是会有点东西可以拿出来。\n# en: A student in an alchemy lab clutching a burnt hand, a faint dark glow gathering around the injury.\nscene [b92a5d] = 炼金实验室里，一个学生捂着被烫伤的手，伤处周围聚起一圈淡淡的暗光。\n# en: Hold the pain\nopt.left.label [dcf061] = 攒住这份疼\n# en: You let the burn become power and carry it all day like a coal in your pocket. It's very strong. It still hurts. Both of those stay true.\nopt.left.say.1 [e20e0d] = 你让烫伤变成力量，一整天都揣着它，像兜里揣着一块炭。它非常强。它也还是疼。两件事都一直是真的。\n# en: Go to the infirmary\nopt.right.label [f09b9c] = 去医务室\n# en: Sister Amaranth dresses it and tells you off for daydreaming. The power you didn't take aches a little, like a tooth.\nopt.right.say.1 [5193c9] = 阿玛兰斯修女给你包扎好，数落你走神。那份你没拿的力量隐隐作痛，像一颗牙。\n\n@event lesson_heartprice\n# en: Paying in Yourself\ntitle [716107] = 用自己付账\n# en: It's late, {mana < 15: your Mana is spent|your Mana is low and tomorrow has a practical}, and the essay isn't done. Heartprice says you don't need Mana. You have yourself. You could pay in that, for as long as you can stand it.\ntext [dea4cb] = 夜深了，{mana < 15: 你的魔力用光了|你的魔力所剩无几，明天还有实践课}，论文还没写完。心价说你不需要魔力。你有你自己。你可以用它来付，付到你撑不住为止。\n# en: An exhausted student at a desk late at night, a faint red glow in their chest feeding a spell, candles burnt down.\nscene [530e5f] = 深夜里，一个筋疲力尽的学生坐在桌前，胸口一点淡淡的红光在为一个咒语供能，蜡烛已经燃尽。\n# en: Pay in yourself\nopt.left.label [202fcd] = 用自己付\n# en: You finish the essay on your own strength. In the morning your hands shake and the essay is very good. You don't feel proud of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [56beb6] = 你靠自己的力气写完了论文。早上你的手在抖，论文写得非常好。你并不为它感到骄傲。\n# en: Leave it for tomorrow\nopt.right.label [69476d] = 留到明天\n# en: You go to bed with the essay unfinished. It's worse than it would have been. You're better than you would have been.\nopt.right.say.1 [6e9f49] = 你带着没写完的论文上了床。它会比本来的样子差一些。你会比本来的样子好一些。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/statuses.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/statuses.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event hungry_leftovers\n# en: The Refectory's Leftovers\ntitle [dbc5bf] = 膳堂的剩饭\n# en: {The refectory is closing, and the kitchens are scraping the pots. A cook sees you hovering in the doorway and holds up a ladle, eyebrows raised.|The same cook, the same ladle. She doesn't ask any more; she just raises her eyebrows.|You know the kitchens' closing time to the minute now.} {A prefect is still at the end table, finishing an essay.|Two first-years are sweeping, and watching.|The cook is humming, which means there's plenty.}\ntext [8bcb19] = {膳堂快关门了，厨房在刮锅底。一个厨娘看见你在门口徘徊，举起汤勺，扬了扬眉毛。|还是那个厨娘，还是那把汤勺。她不再问了，只扬扬眉毛。|如今你对厨房关门的时间精确到分钟。}{尽头的桌子边还坐着一个级长，在赶论文。|两个一年级生在扫地，一边扫一边看。|厨娘在哼歌，这说明还剩不少。}\n# en: A refectory kitchen door at closing time, a cook scraping the last of a stew into a bowl for a student waiting in the doorway.\nscene [8b6728] = 关门时分膳堂的厨房门口，厨娘把最后一点炖菜刮进碗里，递给等在门边的学生。\n# en: Take the bowl\nopt.left.label [629c3f] = 接过那碗\n# en: {It's the burnt bit at the bottom of the stew and it is the best thing you've ever eaten. The prefect looks up from her essay and remembers your face.|The cook fills the bowl to the brim. The first-years see, and by breakfast half your year knows why.|You know the closing time to the minute, and so does the cook. She fills the bowl before you're through the door, humming.}\nopt.left.say.1 [043db7] = {是炖菜锅底烧焦的那层，却是你吃过最好吃的东西。级长从论文上抬起头，记住了你的脸。|厨娘把碗盛得满满的。一年级生们看见了，到早饭时，你们年级一半人都知道为什么。|关门时间你算得分秒不差，厨娘也是。你还没进门，她就哼着歌把碗盛满了。}\n# en: Shake your head and leave\nopt.right.label [d3d37c] = 摇摇头走开\n# en: {\"Not hungry,\" you say, to nobody in particular, and go upstairs on an empty stomach that disagrees.|You walk out with your pride and nothing else. Your stomach complains all night.|You know the closing time, and leave a minute before it. The humming follows you up the stairs, and so does the smell.}\nopt.right.say.1 [013365] = {“不饿，”你对着空气说，空着肚子上了楼，肚子不同意。|你带着自尊离开，别的什么也没带。你的肚子抱怨了一整夜。|你知道关门时间，提前一分钟走了。哼歌声跟着你上了楼梯，香味也是。}\n\n@event hungry_pocketed_rolls\n# en: Rolls for Later\ntitle [e2a96a] = 留着以后吃的面包\n# en: {The refectory's bread rolls are piled high. Board-payers only, says the sign; it doesn't say how many.|The rolls again, and the sign again, and your empty pockets.|Another meal. You've got good at looking as if you've paid.} {The prefect on duty is reading a letter.|The prefect on duty is looking straight at the bread.|The prefect on duty has gone for more tea.}\ntext [12b52e] = {食堂的面包卷堆得高高的。告示写着只供交了食宿费的人；没写能拿几个。|又是面包卷，又是那块告示，又是你空空的口袋。|又一顿饭。你已经很会装作交过钱的样子了。}{值班的级长在读信。|值班的级长正盯着面包。|值班的级长去续茶了。}\n# en: A breakfast table heaped with bread rolls, a student's hand hovering near a pocket while a prefect patrols the aisle.\nscene [5e16e9] = 堆满面包卷的早餐桌，一个学生的手在口袋边犹豫，一个级长在过道里巡视。\n# en: Pocket a few for later\nopt.left.label [daa943] = 揣几个留着\n# en: {Three rolls in your robe, still warm. They last a day, and so does the guilt.|You fill your pockets and walk out at an even pace, crumbs and all.|Two rolls and an apple. Nobody stops you. You eat them on the stairs, a little ashamed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b89f46] = {三个面包卷揣进袍子里，还是热的。它们撑了一整天，内疚也是。|你塞满口袋，不紧不慢地走出去，连面包屑一起。|两个面包卷加一个苹果。没人拦你。你坐在楼梯上吃完，有点羞愧。}\n# en: Eat what's on your plate\nopt.right.label [7782d7] = 只吃盘里的\n# en: {One roll, eaten slowly, and nothing taken that isn't yours. By the next meal you're light-headed and honest.|You eat what you're given, and the hunger keeps you company for hours.|One roll, slowly, with the look of someone who has paid. The hunger knows better.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4f010d] = {一个面包卷，慢慢吃，不拿不属于你的东西。到下一顿饭时你头晕眼花，但问心无愧。|给什么吃什么，饥饿陪了你好几个钟头。|一个面包卷，慢慢吃，一副付过钱的样子。饥饿可不这么想。}\n\n@event hungry_maud_soup\n# en: Soup at the Cottage\ntitle [b46ed5] = 小屋里的汤\n# en: {Old Maud catches you on the path by her cottage. \"You've a hungry look, dearie. Come and eat.\" She has a pot on already, as if she knew.|Maud's pot is on again. She doesn't ask, and you don't explain.} {The soup is leek and something she won't name.|A bee sits on the windowsill, waiting for its share.}\ntext [019fd3] = {老莫德在她小屋旁的小路上拦住你。“你一脸饿相，亲爱的。进来吃点。”她的锅已经炖上了，好像早就知道。|莫德的锅又炖上了。她不问，你也不解释。}{汤里有韭葱，还有一样她不肯说的东西。|窗台上落着一只蜜蜂，等着分一份。}\n# en: A crooked cottage kitchen full of hanging herbs, an old woman ladling soup from a black pot into a chipped bowl.\nscene [65b863] = 歪歪扭扭的小屋厨房里挂满草药，一位老妇人从黑锅里舀汤倒进一只缺口的碗。\n# en: Stay for soup and a story\nopt.left.label [16b691] = 留下喝汤听故事\n# en: {Two bowls, bread, and a sad story about her sister told like a recipe. Your reading waits until morning, and it'll be late.|She talks about the winter the lake froze twice while you eat. The essay you meant to write tonight stays unwritten.}\nopt.left.say.1 [309b82] = {两碗汤、面包，还有一个关于她姐姐的伤心故事，讲得像菜谱一样。你的阅读只好等到明早，肯定要迟交了。|你吃饭时，她讲起湖结了两次冰的那个冬天。你本打算今晚写的论文，一个字也没写。}\n# en: Take a jar back to your room\nopt.right.label [6ec9c2] = 带一罐回房间\n# en: {She fills a jar and wraps it in a cloth. \"Bring the jar back,\" she says. \"That's the price.\" You eat at your desk, studying.|You carry the warm jar up the hill and eat it over your books. Maud waves from the door, a little disappointed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f489b9] = {她装了一罐，用布包好。“罐子要还回来，”她说，“这就是价钱。”你坐在书桌前边吃边学。|你捧着温热的罐子走上山，对着书本吃完。莫德在门口挥手，有点失望。}\n\n@event detention_cauldrons\n# en: Cauldron Duty\ntitle [f28745] = 刷坩埚\n# en: {Detention, and a row of crusted cauldrons and a prefect by the door with a book.|Detention again. The cauldrons are the same; the crust is worse.|You know these cauldrons by name now. One of them, you suspect, knows you.} {Something in the third one is still bubbling.|The prefect is pretending not to be bored.|The prefect has brought a second book this time.}\ntext [fb7d05] = {禁闭，一排结满污垢的坩埚，门口一个拿着书的级长。|又是禁闭。坩埚还是那些，污垢更厚了。|这些坩埚你都叫得出名字了。你怀疑其中一只也认得你。}{第三只里还有东西在冒泡。|级长在假装不无聊。|这回级长带了第二本书。}\n# en: A dim potions storeroom, a student up to the elbows in a crusted cauldron while a prefect reads by a lamp at the door.\nscene [942c95] = 昏暗的魔药储藏室，一个学生胳膊伸进结垢的坩埚里直到手肘，门边一个级长借着灯光看书。\n# en: Scrub them properly\nopt.left.label [1ea45b] = 好好刷干净\n# en: {You scrub until your knuckles are raw, and learn which residues bite back and which only sulk.|By the last cauldron you can tell a week-old draught from a month-old one by the smell alone. Your hands sting for days.|You scrub your old acquaintances one by one. The one that knows you gives up its crust almost gently.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0cda46] = {你刷到指节发红，学会了哪些残渣会反咬一口，哪些只会闹脾气。|刷到最后一只时，你光凭气味就能分辨放了一周和放了一个月的药剂。你的手刺痛了好几天。|你把这些老相识一只只刷过去。认得你的那只几乎是温顺地交出了锅垢。}\n# en: Talk your way out early\nopt.right.label [7a4722] = 说服级长早点放人\n# en: {You talk about the prefect's book until the prefect is talking about it too, and an hour early they wave you off. \"Same time next week. Possibly.\"|You find out you're both from the same county. The cauldrons can wait for someone less charming.|The prefect is on the second book and glad of the interruption. You talk until they send you off early, and the cauldrons stay crusted another week.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6d3ec4] = {你跟级长聊起他手里那本书，聊到他自己也聊开了，提前一个钟头就挥手放你走。“下周同一时间。大概吧。”|你们发现彼此是同乡。坩埚可以留给不那么讨人喜欢的人。|级长看到了第二本书，很乐意被打断。你们一直聊到他提前打发你走，坩埚又结了一星期的垢。}\n# en: {\"Nice try,\" says the prefect, and hands you the worst cauldron in the row.|The prefect hears you out, then points at the third cauldron, the bubbling one. \"For the speech.\"|The prefect doesn't look up from the second book. \"The one that knows you,\" they say. \"Start there.\"}\nopt.right.say.2 [c6834e] = {“想得美，”级长说，把那一排里最糟的坩埚递给了你。|级长听你说完，指了指第三口坩埚，还在冒泡的那口。“奖励你的演讲。”|级长头也没从第二本书上抬起来。“认得你的那只，”级长说，“从它开始。”}\n\n@event detention_lines\n# en: Lines, With Company\ntitle [e410cf] = 有人作伴的罚抄\n# en: Detention means lines, a hundred of them, and {other} is at the next desk doing the same. {~The sentence is \"I will not bring livestock into the library.\"|The sentence is \"Curfew is not a suggestion.\"|The sentence is \"Stairs are for walking.\"} An enchanted quill at the front of the room writes down everything anyone says.\ntext [10752a] = 禁闭就是罚抄，一百遍，{other}就在隔壁桌抄同样的东西。{~句子是“我不会把牲口带进图书馆。”|句子是“宵禁不是建议。”|句子是“楼梯是用来走的。”}教室前面有一支施了魔法的羽毛笔，会把任何人说的每句话都记下来。\n# en: An empty classroom at dusk, two students at separate desks writing the same sentence over and over, a quill scratching on its own at the front.\nscene [e7f96f] = 黄昏时空荡荡的教室，两个学生坐在不同的桌前一遍遍抄同一句话，前面一支羽毛笔自己沙沙地写着。\n# en: Do your homework under the desk\nopt.left.label [9b4823] = 在桌子底下写作业\n# en: {~A hundred lines and an evening's revision for every course. The quill at the front records nothing but scratching.|You alternate a line and a page of notes. Nobody checks. Your whole week's reading is the better for it.|You write \"Stairs are for walking\" with one hand and a week's notes with the other. The quill at the front records only the stairs.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c03b4d] = {~一百遍罚抄，外加把每门课都复习了一晚上。前面的羽毛笔除了沙沙声什么也没记下。|你抄一行，看一页笔记，交替进行。没人检查。你这一整周的功课都因此更扎实了。|你一只手抄“楼梯是用来走的”，另一只手写了一星期的笔记。前面的羽毛笔只记下了楼梯。}\n# en: {~The prefect lifts your lines, finds your notes underneath and confiscates them until the end of term.|Your notes fall on the floor at exactly the wrong moment. The prefect keeps them, and makes you start the lines again.|Between the stairs and the notes you lose track. The prefect finds forty lines of notes among the stairs, and you start again.}\nopt.left.say.2 [8df37d] = {~级长掀起你的罚抄纸，发现底下的笔记，一直没收到学期结束。|你的笔记在最不该掉的时候掉到了地上。级长收走了它们，还让你从头再抄。|楼梯和笔记一交替，你就乱了。级长在楼梯中间发现了四十行笔记，你只好从头再抄。}\n# en: Talk to {other}\nopt.right.label [730ff0] = 和{other}聊天\n# en: {~By the fortieth line you know why {other} is here, and it's a better story than yours. The quill at the front writes it all down, word for word.|You compare crimes. {other} laughs so hard the quill at the front writes \"LAUGHTER\" in capitals.|{other} tells you which staircase is to blame. You laugh until the quill at the front writes \"STAIRS?\" in the margin.}\nopt.right.say.1 [339d75] = {~抄到第四十遍时，你已经知道{other}为什么在这儿了，比你的故事精彩。前面的羽毛笔一字不差地全记了下来。|你们比较各自的罪行。{other}笑得太厉害，前面的羽毛笔用大写写下了“笑声”。|{other}告诉你该怪哪段楼梯。你笑到前面的羽毛笔在页边写下了“楼梯？”。}\n\n@event probation_advisor\n# en: A Word With Your Advisor\ntitle [98682b] = 和导师谈谈\n# en: {A summons to your advisor's office. Your marks lie on the desk between you, upside down for you, right way up for them.|Your advisor again, and the same marks, a little better or a little worse.} \"Two terms under the line and the Disciplinary Board sits,\" they say. \"Shall we make sure it doesn't?\"\ntext [00903e] = {你被叫到导师办公室。你的成绩单摆在你们中间的桌上，对你是倒着的，对他们是正着的。|又是导师，又是那份成绩，好了一点或者差了一点。}“连续两个学期低于及格线，纪律委员会就要开会了，”他们说，“我们一起确保这不会发生，好吗？”\n# en: A cluttered faculty office with a ticking clock, an advisor and a student either side of a desk with a marks list between them.\nscene [edee64] = 杂乱的教职员办公室里时钟滴答作响，导师和学生隔着书桌坐着，中间放着成绩单。\n# en: Make a study plan together\nopt.left.label [c5684e] = 一起订个学习计划\n# en: {An hour later you have a timetable that colours every evening grey, and you start on the first evening of it that night. It is a very good timetable. You hate it.|The plan has a column for sleep, and it is narrow. You work through the first week of it with your advisor checking in.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e6ba58] = {一小时后，你得到一张把每个晚上都涂成灰色的时间表，当晚就照着它开始了第一晚。这是一张非常好的时间表。你恨它。|计划里有一栏是睡觉，那一栏很窄。你按它熬过了第一周，导师时不时过问一下。}\n# en: Talk about what's going wrong\nopt.right.label [d074c1] = 聊聊出了什么问题\n# en: {You say aloud the thing you hadn't said to anyone. Your advisor listens and makes no plan at all. You leave lighter, and no better prepared.|It turns out your advisor failed a year, once. You talk about that instead of your marks.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0f21a6] = {你把从没对人说过的话说出了口。导师听着，一点计划也没订。你离开时轻松了些，准备却一点没多。|原来你的导师也曾留过一级。你们聊的是这个，而不是你的成绩。}\n\n@event probation_letter\n# en: The Warning Letter\ntitle [a7ab28] = 警告信\n# en: A letter from the registrar, in the stiff envelope everyone dreads: Academic Probation, your name spelled correctly for once, and a line about \"consequences\" underlined twice. A copy, it says, has gone to your family.\ntext [29a24a] = 教务处来信，装在人人害怕的那种硬邦邦的信封里：学业察看，你的名字难得拼对了，“后果”一词下面画了两道线。信上说，副本已寄给你的家人。\n# en: A stiff envelope with the academy's seal lying open on a desk, a student holding the letter at arm's length.\nscene [f197c9] = 书桌上一个盖着学院火漆的硬信封已经拆开，一个学生把信拿得远远的。\n# en: Pin it above your desk\nopt.left.label [94c02c] = 钉在书桌上方\n# en: The letter glares at you every time you look up. It works, in the way a headache works: you study, and you don't enjoy a minute of it.\nopt.left.say.1 [2e8283] = 你每次抬头，那封信都瞪着你。它管用，就像头疼管用一样：你学了，但没有一分钟是享受的。\n# en: Put it in a drawer\nopt.right.label [73100a] = 放进抽屉\n# en: Out of sight, mostly out of mind. Mostly. Some nights you open the drawer, look at it and close the drawer again.\nopt.right.say.1 [9a09b4] = 眼不见，心大致不烦。大致。有些晚上你拉开抽屉，看它一眼，又关上。\n\n@event sick_in_the_infirmary\n# en: A Bed by the Window\ntitle [d71099] = 窗边的病床\n# en: {Lake fever. Your nose glows faintly blue, and Sister Amaranth says \"Bed,\" in the voice that means it.|The fever again, and the same bed by the window.} {Lectures are going on without you.|Someone has left a stack of notes on the chair.}\ntext [3abe07] = {湖热病。你的鼻尖泛着淡蓝的光，阿玛兰斯修女说了声“躺下”，用的是不容商量的语气。|又发烧了，还是窗边那张床。}{课在没有你的情况下照常进行。|有人在椅子上留了一沓笔记。}\n# en: A row of white infirmary beds, a student wrapped in blankets with a faintly glowing blue nose and a steaming mug beside them.\nscene [0cb8c2] = 一排白色的医务室病床，一个学生裹着毯子，鼻尖发着淡淡的蓝光，身边放着一杯冒热气的饮品。\n# en: Stay in bed all week\nopt.left.label [1adbad] = 卧床一整周\n# en: {You sleep, drink something bitter and sleep again. When you go back, the class is two chapters ahead.|A week of blankets and broth. You feel much better and know much less.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7b6e03] = {你睡觉，喝点苦东西，再睡。等你回去，班上已经讲到两章以后了。|一周的毯子和肉汤。你感觉好多了，知道的却少多了。}\n# en: Go to lectures in a scarf\nopt.right.label [f539a4] = 围着围巾去上课\n# en: {You sniffle through Arcana in three scarves. The notes are good; the coughing fit in the middle is not.|You make every lecture, and every lecture makes you worse. Sister Amaranth sees you on the stairs and says nothing, very loudly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fa9f22] = {你围着三条围巾吸着鼻子上完了奥术课。笔记记得很好，中途那阵咳嗽就不好了。|每节课你都去了，每节课都让你病得更重。阿玛兰斯修女在楼梯上看见你，什么也没说，说得很大声。}\n\n@event injured_on_the_stairs\n# en: The Moving Stairs, Limping\ntitle [442cc2] = 瘸着腿走会动的楼梯\n# en: {Your ankle is bandaged and the stairs are moving, as stairs here do. The lecture is three landings up and starts in five minutes.|The stairs again, the ankle again. The lecture, as ever, is at the top.}\ntext [c197eb] = {你的脚踝缠着绷带，而楼梯在动，这里的楼梯就是这样。课在三层楼梯之上，五分钟后开始。|又是楼梯，又是脚踝。课照旧在最上面。}\n# en: A grand staircase swinging slowly between landings, a student with a bandaged ankle clinging to the banister as it moves.\nscene [6c9dff] = 一道宏伟的楼梯在两个平台之间缓缓摆动，一个脚踝缠着绷带的学生紧抓着移动的扶手。\n# en: Take the long way, slowly\nopt.left.label [c434dc] = 绕远路，慢慢走\n# en: {You limp round by the back corridor and arrive as everyone is packing up. The ankle thanks you; your notes don't.|Slow, careful and very late. The ankle mends a little faster for it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [27a6aa] = {你一瘸一拐地绕过后走廊，到的时候大家正在收拾东西。脚踝谢谢你；你的笔记不谢。|慢、小心，迟到很久。脚踝因此好得快了一点。}\n# en: Hop the gap as it swings\nopt.right.label [eee215] = 趁它摆过来时跳过去\n# en: {You time it perfectly and land on one foot. Somebody on the landing applauds.|One hop, one wobble, one bow. You're on time and briefly famous.}\nopt.right.say.1 [25eaae] = {你把握好时机，单脚落地。平台上有人鼓掌。|一跳，一晃，一鞠躬。你准时到了，还短暂地出了名。}\n# en: {You land on the wrong foot, the bad one. The stairs swing on without you, and you sit on the step for a while.|The gap is wider than it looked. You arrive on time and limping twice as badly.}\nopt.right.say.2 [87c7cb] = {你落在了错的那只脚上，伤的那只。楼梯丢下你摆走了，你在台阶上坐了好一会儿。|缝隙比看上去宽。你准时到了，瘸得比原来厉害一倍。}\n\n@event purse_and_the_board\n# en: A Light Purse\ntitle [a59033] = 钱袋空空\n# en: {You tip out your purse on the bed. Three coins and a button.|The purse again. The button is still there; the coins are fewer.|The purse, the button, and fewer coins than ever. You've stopped counting the button.} Board is due at the start of every month. {On the way to breakfast you passed the Job Board in the entrance hall, and someone was taking down a notice.|Someone in your year says the Newt is hiring again.|There's a hardship notice by the bursar's door, someone says, and professors who pay for errands.}\ntext [c10029] = {你把钱袋倒在床上。三枚硬币，一颗纽扣。|又是这个钱袋。纽扣还在，硬币更少了。|钱袋，纽扣，硬币比哪次都少。你已经不再把纽扣算进去了。}每个月初都要交食宿费。{去吃早饭的路上，你经过门厅的招工告示板，有人正在揭下一张告示。|你们年级有人说，蝾螈酒馆又在招人了。|有人说，财务官门边贴着困难补助的告示，还有教授会花钱请人跑腿。}\n# en: A student emptying a coin purse on a dormitory bed, three coins and a button rolling out across the blanket.\nscene [edb439] = 一个学生把钱袋倒在宿舍床上，三枚硬币和一颗纽扣滚过毯子。\n# en: Sell your second-best cloak\nopt.left.label [da0dc9] = 卖掉你第二好的斗篷\n# en: {A second-hand stall in the village gives you a fair price and an unfair look. The castle is draughty without it.|You sell the cloak. It was your grandmother's. You're nearly sure she'd understand.|The second-hand stall knows you by now. The price is fair, and the look is kinder than last time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6ad26e] = {山下小镇的二手摊给了你一个公道的价钱，和一个不太公道的眼神。没了它，城堡里冷飕飕的。|你卖掉了斗篷。那是你祖母的。你几乎可以肯定她会理解。|二手摊已经认得你了。价钱公道，眼神也比上次和善。}\n# en: Look at the Job Board later\nopt.right.label [e5acc5] = 回头去看看招工告示板\n# en: {You make a note. The Job Board, entrance hall, morning to evening. One job at a time, paid by the shift.|You'll go and read the notices. Probably. Board is due either way.|You'll look at the Job Board, and at the hardship notice by the bursar's door. Board is due either way.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f564c8] = {你记下：招工告示板，门厅，早上到傍晚。一次一份工，按班次付钱。|你会去看看那些告示的。大概吧。反正食宿费总是要交的。|你会去看招工告示板，还有财务室门口的困难补助告示。反正食宿费总是要交的。}\n\n@event ember_on_the_essay\n# en: A Fox on the Essay\ntitle [342c5e] = 论文上的狐狸\n# en: {Ember has chosen your week's coursework to sleep on, and her tail is smouldering, as it does when she dreams.|Ember is asleep on your notes again. The corner of every page is already brown.|Ember has a new favourite sleeping place, and all of it is due on Monday.}\ntext [365726] = {余烬选中你这一周的功课当床，尾巴冒着烟，她做梦时总是这样。|余烬又在你的笔记上睡着了。每一页的纸角都已经焦黄。|余烬有了新的心头好睡觉地点，而且这些全都周一要交。}\n# en: A fox-spirit curled asleep on a student's half-written essay, her tail smouldering gently at one corner of the page.\nscene [0a42c9] = 一只狐灵蜷在学生写了一半的论文上睡觉，尾巴在纸角轻轻冒烟。\n# en: Rescue the essay\nopt.left.label [e5bb3c] = 抢救论文\n# en: {You slide the pile out from under her. She opens one eye, says something rude in fox and sulks under the bed until supper.|You rescue the pages and lose a fox: she spends the evening on your pillow with her back to you.|You lift her off Monday's work in one piece. She lets you, which is worse: she's planning something.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cda348] = {你把那摞纸从她身下抽出来。她睁开一只眼，用狐狸语说了句难听的话，然后躲到床底下生闷气，一直到晚饭。|你救下了那几页纸，却失去了一只狐狸：她整晚背对着你趴在你的枕头上。|你把她从周一要交的作业上整个抱了下来。她由着你，这更糟：她在打什么主意。}\n# en: Let her sleep and write around her\nopt.right.label [775316] = 让她睡，绕着她写\n# en: {You write the conclusion in the margin, round a sleeping fox. Your essays come back scorched at one corner and marked down for it.|She's warm, she's purring in her own way, and your notes have a hole in them now. It's worth it.|You write Monday's work round her, a paragraph at a time, while her tail smoulders in the margin. It smells of smoke, and it's marked down for it, and it's in.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5217d0] = {你绕着一只熟睡的狐狸，把结论写在了页边。论文发回来时一角烧焦了，还因此被扣了分。|她暖暖的，以她自己的方式打着呼噜，你的笔记现在多了个洞。值得。|你绕着她写周一要交的作业，一次一段，她的尾巴在页边冒着烟。作业有股烟味，因此被扣了分，但总算交上了。}\n\n@event ember_night_hunt\n# en: Ember Goes Hunting\ntitle [093af9] = 余烬去打猎\n# en: {Ember tugs at your blanket. Then at your sleeve. Then at your ear. She wants you to come.|Ember is at the door again, tail glowing, waiting.} {Somewhere below, something is scratching.|The corridors are cold and very quiet.}\ntext [184d85] = {余烬拽你的毯子。然后拽你的袖子。然后拽你的耳朵。她想让你跟着来。|余烬又守在门口，尾巴发着光，等着。}{楼下某处，有什么东西在抓挠。|走廊又冷又静。}\n# en: A dark castle corridor lit only by the glow of a fox's tail, a student in a nightgown following at a distance.\nscene [050b20] = 只有狐狸尾巴的微光照亮的昏暗城堡走廊，一个穿睡袍的学生远远跟在后面。\n# en: Follow her through the castle\nopt.left.label [8c04fb] = 跟着她穿过城堡\n# en: {She leads you down three staircases to a loose stone, and sits by it, very pleased. Behind it, nothing but a draught. You're back at dawn, frozen.|A mouse, two corridors and a door you'd never noticed. You get back to bed cold and much better at walking quietly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cc981e] = {她领你走下三段楼梯，来到一块松动的石头前，坐在旁边，得意极了。石头后面：除了一股穿堂风什么也没有。你天亮才回去，冻得透透的。|一只老鼠，两条走廊，一扇你从没注意过的门。你回到床上时浑身冰凉，走路却轻了许多。}\n# en: Pull the blanket over your head\nopt.right.label [352adf] = 把毯子蒙过头\n# en: {You sleep. Ember goes alone and comes back at dawn smelling of the kitchens, enormously smug.|She sighs at you, a fox's sigh, and goes without you. You sleep like a stone.}\nopt.right.say.1 [55c90a] = {你睡了。余烬独自出门，天亮回来时一身厨房的味道，得意得不得了。|她冲你叹了口气，狐狸式的叹气，自己去了。你睡得像块石头。}\n\n@event ember_and_the_sandwich\n# en: The Prefect's Sandwich\ntitle [4bfec6] = 级长的三明治\n# en: Ember trots through the refectory with a prefect's sandwich in her jaws, past forty witnesses. The prefect looks at the empty plate, then at the fox, then at the room. \"Whose,\" the prefect says, \"is that?\"\ntext [3b22cb] = 余烬叼着一个级长的三明治，从四十个目击者面前小跑着穿过膳堂。级长看看空盘子，看看狐狸，又看看满屋子的人。“这是谁的，”级长说，“那只？”\n# en: A crowded refectory, a small fire-tailed fox trotting off with a sandwich while a prefect stares at an empty plate.\nscene [928f92] = 拥挤的膳堂里，一只火尾小狐狸叼着三明治小跑而去，一个级长瞪着空盘子。\n# en: Own up and buy a new one\nopt.left.label [66e428] = 认了，再买一个\n# en: You raise your hand. The room laughs; you pay for a sandwich at the counter and hand it over. The prefect accepts it with dignity. Ember eats the first one under your chair.\nopt.left.say.1 [27502f] = 你举起手。满屋子的人都笑了；你在柜台买了个三明治递过去。级长庄重地收下了。余烬在你椅子底下吃掉了第一个。\n# en: Look as puzzled as everyone else\nopt.right.label [c5a60c] = 跟大家一样一脸茫然\n# en: You look around the room, as baffled as anybody. Ember sits on your foot under the table, chewing. The prefect never finds out, and Ember never lets you forget it.\nopt.right.say.1 [cbaa51] = 你环顾四周，和所有人一样困惑。余烬坐在桌下你的脚上嚼着。级长始终没查出来，而余烬始终不让你忘了这件事。\n\n@event owl_six_oclock\n# en: \"HOMEWORK\"\ntitle [a40fe3] = “作业”\n# en: {Bedtime. \"HOMEWORK,\" says the clockwork owl, and then, because you haven't moved, \"HOMEWORK. DUE. TOMORROW.\"|\"HOMEWORK,\" says the owl, at bedtime, as ever. This time it's right. A week's exercises are due tomorrow, in every course, and you'd forgotten.|The owl's bedtime hoot has a new word in it: \"LATE.\"}\ntext [3d755a] = {睡觉时间。“作业，”发条猫头鹰说，见你没动，又说：“作业。明天。要交。”|“作业，”猫头鹰照例在睡前说。这次它说对了。每门课都有一周的练习明天要交，你给忘了。|猫头鹰睡前的报时里多了一个新词：“迟了。”}\n# en: A brass clockwork owl on a bedpost at dawn, its amber eyes glowing, a student groaning under a pillow.\nscene [d3576f] = 黎明时床柱上站着一只黄铜发条猫头鹰，琥珀色眼睛发着光，一个学生在枕头底下呻吟。\n# en: Stay up and write it now\nopt.left.label [e04890] = 熬夜马上写\n# en: {You write it all by candlelight with your eyes half shut. It's not your best work, but it's in. The owl says \"GOOD,\" which you didn't know it could.|Two hours of frantic quill-work by the last of the candle. You hand it in on time and nod off in the next lecture.|You write until the owl stops saying \"LATE\" and says nothing at all. It goes in on time, just.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8a7dcf] = {你半睁着眼在烛光下全部写完。不是你最好的作品，但交上了。猫头鹰说“好”，你都不知道它会说这个。|就着最后一截蜡烛奋笔疾书了两个钟头。你按时交了，下一节课却打起了瞌睡。|你一直写到猫头鹰不再说“迟了”，什么也不说了。作业勉强按时交上。}\n# en: Hand it in late\nopt.right.label [6db6d8] = 迟交\n# en: {You go to sleep. The owl says \"DISAPPOINTED\" twice and goes quiet. Your work goes in a day late and is marked accordingly.|You sleep until eight. The owl doesn't speak to you until lunch, and then only to say \"LATE.\"|\"LATE,\" says the owl, and it is. You hand the work in a day after, and the owl says it again at breakfast.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8b8cee] = {你去睡了。猫头鹰说了两遍“失望”，然后不吭声了。你的作业晚交了一天，分数也相应打了折。|你一觉睡到八点。猫头鹰直到午饭都不理你，开口也只说“迟了”。|“迟了，”猫头鹰说，确实迟了。你晚一天交了作业，猫头鹰早饭时又说了一遍。}\n\n@event owl_birthday\n# en: \"BIRTHDAY\"\ntitle [8d676a] = “生日”\n# en: \"BIRTHDAY,\" says the owl, flapping in front of your face, and then, louder, \"BIRTHDAY.\" It turns its head toward the window, toward the dormitory where {friend} lives. Mir built it to remember birthdays. You had not.\ntext [2c9379] = “生日，”猫头鹰扑棱着翅膀在你面前说，然后更大声：“生日。”它把头转向窗外，转向{friend}住的那栋宿舍。米尔造它时就让它记生日。你可没记。\n# en: A clockwork owl fluttering in front of a student, its beak open mid-hoot, a calendar on the wall behind.\nscene [65c33f] = 一只发条猫头鹰在学生面前扑腾，喙张着正在叫，身后墙上挂着日历。\n# en: Buy a proper present\nopt.left.label [c20c31] = 买份像样的礼物\n# en: You run down to the village before the shops close and come back with something {friend} actually wanted. {They} look{s} at you as if you've always remembered.\nopt.left.say.1 [6c6795] = 你赶在商店关门前跑下山，带回一样{friend}真正想要的东西。{They}看着你，好像你一直都记得。\n# en: Write a card, quickly\nopt.right.label [74c735] = 赶紧写张卡片\n# en: A card, a drawing of the owl and \"Happy birthday\" in your best hand. {friend} pins it up anyway, and asks how you knew.\nopt.right.say.1 [5972f0] = 一张卡片，画了那只猫头鹰，用你最好的字写着“生日快乐”。{friend}还是把它钉了起来，问你是怎么知道的。\n\n@event owl_wound_down\n# en: The Owl Winds Down\ntitle [cca46b] = 猫头鹰没电了\n# en: The clockwork owl has started saying \"HOME… WORK…\" very slowly, like a music box running down. One wing droops. There is a tiny panel in its chest, and a tinier key, and absolutely no instructions.\ntext [20f376] = 发条猫头鹰开始很慢很慢地说“作……业……”，像一只快走完的八音盒。一边翅膀耷拉着。它胸口有个小小的盖板，一把更小的钥匙，没有任何说明。\n# en: A brass owl slumped on a desk with one wing drooping, a student peering into a small open panel in its chest with a magnifying glass.\nscene [c0c42f] = 一只黄铜猫头鹰瘫在桌上，一边翅膀下垂，一个学生拿着放大镜往它胸口打开的小盖板里看。\n# en: Take it back to Mir\nopt.left.label [e10bd7] = 带回去给米尔\n# en: Mir lights up and spends the whole evening inside the owl with tweezers, explaining every spring. You miss your reading entirely. The owl comes back saying \"HOMEWORK\" faster than ever.\nopt.left.say.1 [7214a4] = 米尔眼睛一亮，整个晚上都拿着镊子钻在猫头鹰肚子里，一根根发条地讲解。你的阅读完全泡汤了。猫头鹰回来后说“作业”说得比以前还快。\n# en: Fix it yourself\nopt.right.label [bf3e8e] = 自己修\n# en: A wound spring, a drop of oil and a charm to keep it turning. The owl shakes itself and says \"THANK YOU,\" which it has never said before.\nopt.right.say.1 [1c89db] = 上紧一根发条，滴一滴油，再加一道让它转下去的咒语。猫头鹰抖了抖身子，说了声“谢谢”，它以前从没说过。\n# en: You fix it. Now it hoots on the half hour instead of the hour, all night, and says \"HOMEWORK\" in a voice like a kettle.\nopt.right.say.2 [ca3ce4] = 你修好了。现在它不是整点报时，而是每半点报一次，整夜都报，说“作业”的声音像烧开的水壶。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/upper_ranks.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/upper_ranks.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event duelling_captain_challenge\n# en: The Captain's Challenge\ntitle [3e0fbf] = 队长的挑战\n# en: {A visiting champion has challenged the Circle's captain, by name, in front of everyone. The captain is you.|Another challenger at the door, asking for the captain.|They come for the captain now, from three valleys away.} {The hall is packed to the rafters.|{around(lyra): Lyra is in the front row with her arms folded.|The old captains are in the front row with their arms folded.}|Somebody is taking bets in the corridor.}\ntext [616d0c] = {一位来访的冠军当着所有人的面，点名挑战决斗社的队长。队长就是你。|门口又来了一个挑战者，指名要找队长。|如今有人从三个山谷之外赶来挑战队长。}{大厅里挤得水泄不通。|{around(lyra): 莱拉抱着胳膊坐在第一排。|几位前任队长抱着胳膊坐在第一排。}|有人在走廊里坐庄下注。}\n# en: A captain of a duelling circle in a chalk ring facing a visiting champion, the whole hall packed to the rafters.\nscene [f26cae] = 决斗社的队长站在粉笔圈里，对面是来访的冠军，整个大厅挤得水泄不通。\n# en: Accept in front of everyone\nopt.left.label [c550e1] = 当着所有人的面应战\n# en: {You win the third exchange and the hall comes apart. The champion bows, properly. The Circle will talk about it for a year.|A long, hard bout, and yours at the end. Your name goes on the board in gold paint.|You accept, with bets being taken in the corridor. You win, and somebody in the corridor wins a great deal more.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a1e423] = {你在第三回合取胜，整个大厅炸开了锅。冠军郑重地鞠了一躬。决斗社会把这件事谈论一整年。|一场漫长而艰苦的较量，最后是你赢了。你的名字被用金漆写上了荣誉榜。|走廊里有人在下注，你接受了挑战。你赢了，走廊里有人赢得比你多得多。}\n# en: {You lose, in front of everyone, by a breath. The Circle carries you to the infirmary anyway, which is its own verdict.|Beaten, cleanly. The champion is gracious. That's the worst part.|You lose to someone from three valleys away, and the corridor pays out. The Circle buys you a drink with the winnings it didn't make.}\nopt.left.say.2 [fb3105] = {你当着所有人的面输了，只差一口气。决斗社还是把你抬去了医务室，这本身就是一种评价。|干脆利落地输了。冠军很有风度。这才是最难受的。|你输给了一个从三个山谷外来的人，走廊里的赌局开始赔钱。决斗社用它没赢到的钱请你喝了一杯。}\n# en: Send your best officer instead\nopt.right.label [50ddb4] = 派你最好的干事上场\n# en: {Your officer wins, narrowly, and the hall cheers for the Circle. A few people look at you and wonder why you didn't step up.|You coach from the edge. The officer loses but fights well. It was the right call, you think, mostly.|You send your officer. They win, and the corridor's bookmaker grumbles that the captain was the better bet.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fbcd3e] = {你的干事险胜，大厅为决斗社欢呼。有几个人看着你，纳闷你为什么没有亲自上场。|你在场边指导。干事输了，但打得很好。这是正确的决定，你想，大体上是。|你派出了干事。干事赢了，走廊里坐庄的人抱怨说押队长才稳。}\n\n@event duelling_captain_injury\n# en: The Captain's Call\ntitle [65921a] = 队长的决定\n# en: {A first-year in the Circle has twisted a wrist in the final of the house cup, and insists on finishing.|Another final, another stubborn first-year with an injury.|The house cup final again, and again someone hurt who won't stop.} {The house's honour is riding on it.|Their parents are in the gallery.|The referee looks at you: the captain decides.}\ntext [d60d62] = {决斗社的一个一年级生在院际杯决赛中扭伤了手腕，却坚持要打完。|又一场决赛，又一个受了伤还不肯下场的一年级生。|又是院际杯决赛，又有人受了伤却不肯停。}{学院的荣誉全押在这一场上。|他们的父母就在看台上。|裁判看向你：由队长决定。}\n# en: A duellist kneeling in a chalk circle clutching a wrist, a captain crouching beside them, a referee waiting with a whistle.\nscene [89c135] = 一个决斗者跪在粉笔圈里捂着手腕，队长蹲在一旁，裁判拿着哨子等待。\n# en: Let them finish\nopt.left.label [726d27] = 让他们打完\n# en: {They finish, and win, with one hand. The gallery stands. You stood beside them, and you'll remember the fear more than the win.|They finish and win. Amaranth bandages them afterwards and gives you a look that could strip paint.|The referee looks at you, and you nod. They finish, and win, and you hold your breath through every exchange.}\nopt.left.say.1 [54b4bf] = {他们用一只手打完了，还赢了。看台上的人全站了起来。你一直站在他们身旁，你记住的会是恐惧，而不是胜利。|他们打完了，赢了。阿玛兰斯修女事后给他们包扎，看你的眼神能刮掉一层漆。|裁判看向你，你点了头。他们打完了，赢了，每一回合你都屏着气。}\n# en: {They finish and lose, and the wrist is worse. Sister Amaranth sends for you specifically, to explain.|The bout ends with a second injury. You let it happen. You don't sleep that night.|The referee looks at you, you nod, and you're wrong. The wrist goes. You walk them to the infirmary yourself.}\nopt.left.say.2 [2b62e7] = {他们打完了，输了，手腕伤得更重了。阿玛兰斯修女特地派人找你去解释。|这场比赛以第二次受伤告终。是你让它发生的。那天夜里你没有睡着。|裁判看向你，你点了头，可你错了。手腕彻底伤了。你亲自把他们送去了医务室。}\n# en: Stop the bout\nopt.right.label [484d08] = 叫停比赛\n# en: {You call it. The house loses the cup and the first-year cries and calls you a coward. A week later, in the corridor, they thank you.|You stop it. The gallery boos. {around(lyra): Lyra, in the front row, nods once.|The referee, pocketing the whistle, nods once.}|The referee looks at you, and you stop it. The first-year doesn't speak to you for a week, then asks you to coach them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [43f5f4] = {你叫停了。学院输掉了奖杯，那个一年级生哭着骂你是懦夫。一个星期后，在走廊里，他们向你道了谢。|你叫停了。看台上一片嘘声。{around(lyra): 第一排的莱拉点了一下头。|裁判把哨子揣回兜里，点了一下头。}|裁判看向你，你叫停了。那个一年级生一个星期没理你，然后来请你当他们的教练。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_editor_scoop\n# en: The Story That Could Close Us\ntitle [202765] = 可能让报社关门的报道\n# en: {A reporter brings you a story about a governor of the academy, apparently true and well sourced, and dangerous.|Another story with teeth, on your desk with a dawn deadline.|Nessa calls it \"the kind of story that closes papers\". She says it admiringly.} {The evidence is a stack of letters.|The source wants to stay unnamed.|The printer needs the pages by dawn.}\ntext [b5834d] = {一个记者给你带来一篇关于学院一位董事的报道，看上去属实，来源可靠，也很危险。|又一篇带刺的报道摆在你的桌上，天亮前就得付印。|内莎说这是“能让报社关门的那种报道”。她说这话时带着钦佩。}{证据是一叠信件。|消息来源要求匿名。|印刷工天亮前就要版面。}\n# en: A cramped newspaper office at night, a student editor reading a draft under a lamp, a stack of evidence beside it and the clock showing late.\nscene [4d4f9d] = 夜里一间狭小的报社办公室，一个学生主编在灯下读着稿子，旁边摞着证据，钟表显示已经很晚了。\n# en: Run it on the front page\nopt.left.label [b4bc4b] = 登上头版\n# en: {Every fact holds. The Ledger sells out by breakfast; the governor resigns by supper. Nessa frames the front page.|It runs, it holds, and the castle talks of nothing else for a week.|You run it with the printer waiting, and every fact holds. Nessa says it's the story that closes papers, and it closes someone else's career instead.}\nopt.left.say.1 [71ac0c] = {每一个事实都站得住。《流明纪事报》早饭前就卖光了；那位董事晚饭前就辞了职。内莎把那期头版裱了起来。|报道刊出，经得住推敲，整个城堡一个星期都在谈论这件事。|你在印刷工的等待中把它发了出去，每个事实都站得住。内莎说这是能让报纸关门的稿子，结果关门的是别人的前程。}\n# en: {One fact was wrong, and it's the one they sue over. The Ledger prints a retraction; you write it. Detention, too, for good measure.|It runs, with a mistake in it. The governor's lawyers are very polite, and very thorough.|You run it by dawn, and the dawn is too soon. One fact needed another day. The retraction is yours to write.}\nopt.left.say.2 [acb720] = {有一个事实错了，偏偏就是他们起诉的那一条。《流明纪事报》刊登了更正声明，由你执笔。顺便还有一次禁闭。|报道刊出了，里面有个错误。那位董事的律师们非常有礼貌，也非常彻底。|你赶在黎明前发了稿，可黎明来得太早了。有一个事实还需要多核实一天。更正由你来写。}\n# en: Hold it until it's airtight\nopt.right.label [145ca0] = 压到万无一失再说\n# en: {You hold it. Another paper in the village runs a thinner version first. Nessa doesn't speak to you for two days, then admits you were right.|You hold it for a month and check every line. When it finally runs, it's bulletproof, and old news.|You hold the presses until it's airtight. The printer charges you for the dawn, and Nessa pays it, grumbling.}\nopt.right.say.1 [af6116] = {你压下了稿子。村里另一家报纸先登了一个单薄的版本。内莎两天没跟你说话，然后承认你是对的。|你压了一个月，每一行都核实过。等它终于刊出，已经无懈可击，也已经是旧闻了。|你压住印刷机，直到稿子无懈可击。印刷工为这个黎明收了你的钱，内莎嘟囔着付了。}\n\n@event lumen_ledger_editor_threat\n# en: A Letter to the Editor\ntitle [2a57e7] = 致编辑的信\n# en: {A letter to the editor, unsigned, about a story you ran. It knows where you sleep.|Another unsigned letter, the same careful hand.|The letters have stopped being polite.} {It smells of expensive ink.|The seal has been scraped off.|It's been slipped under the office door, not posted.}\ntext [e93e25] = {一封致编辑的匿名信，针对你刊出的一篇报道。它知道你睡在哪儿。|又一封匿名信，同样工整的笔迹。|这些信已经不再客气了。}{它闻起来是昂贵墨水的味道。|火漆印被刮掉了。|它是从编辑部门缝底下塞进来的，不是寄来的。}\n# en: A black-edged letter open on a newspaper desk, a student editor reading it with a cold face, the window behind them dark.\nscene [34ca74] = 一封黑边信摊在报社的桌上，一个学生主编面色冰冷地读着，身后的窗户一片漆黑。\n# en: Print it, with a reply\nopt.left.label [0cd1a2] = 登出来，附上回应\n# en: {You print it in full on page two, with three lines of your own beneath. The castle loves it. You check your door twice every night for a month.|The letter goes in the paper. The next one takes a month to come. The waiting for it is worse.|You print the impolite one, impolitely answered. The letters stop. You don't know whether that's good.}\nopt.left.say.1 [185ef7] = {你把它全文登在第二版，下面附上你自己的三行字。全城堡都爱看。接下来一个月，你每晚都要检查两遍门。|信登在了报纸上。下一封隔了一个月才来。等它的滋味更难受。|你把那封不客气的信登了出来，也不客气地回了。信不再来了。你不知道这是好是坏。}\n# en: Take it to the headmistress\nopt.right.label [069089] = 交给校长\n# en: {She reads it once and puts it in a drawer. \"Leave it with me.\" For a long while, you don't get another. Nessa says that's censorship. It's also sleep.|The headmistress handles it. You don't ask how. The Ledger is quieter for a while.|The headmistress reads the one slipped under the door and asks who has a key to the office. The locks are changed by evening.}\nopt.right.say.1 [24b851] = {她读了一遍就把信放进了抽屉。“交给我吧。”很长一段时间里，你没再收到过。内莎说这是审查。这也是能睡个好觉。|校长处理了这件事。你没问她怎么处理的。《流明纪事报》安静了一阵子。|院长读了那封塞在门下的信，问谁有编辑部的钥匙。傍晚之前锁就换了。}\n\n@event verdant_head_gardener_blight\n# en: The Blight\ntitle [b7ffa6] = 枯萎病\n# en: {Blight in the greenhouses, black spots spreading by the hour. The Head Gardener decides what to burn.|The blight is back, in a different house.|Black spots again. You know the smell of it now.} {{around(tobias): Tobias is close to tears.|The Circle's youngest member is close to tears.}|The rarest plant in the collection is at the edge of it.|Maud has come up from the cottage to look.}\ntext [a2f41a] = {温室里闹起了枯萎病，黑斑一小时一小时地蔓延。首席园丁决定烧掉什么。|枯萎病又来了，在另一间温室。|又是黑斑。你现在已经熟悉它的气味了。}{{around(tobias): 托比亚斯快哭了。|社里年纪最小的成员快哭了。}|收藏里最稀有的那株植物就在病区边上。|莫德从小屋上来看了。}\n# en: A greenhouse full of wilting plants, black spots on every leaf, a head gardener holding a sickly seedling up to the light.\nscene [5e45ab] = 一间满是枯萎植物的温室，每片叶子上都有黑斑，一个首席园丁把一株病恹恹的幼苗举到光下。\n# en: Try an untested cure on everything\nopt.left.label [ee10e0] = 在所有植物上试一种未经检验的疗法\n# en: {The cure works, overnight. Every plant lives, even the rare one. Maud says she's never seen it done, and asks for the recipe.|By morning the spots are fading. The Circle sings, badly, in the greenhouse.|Maud watches you try the cure and says nothing until it works. Then she asks for the recipe, and you give it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3b609d] = {疗法一夜见效。每一株都活了下来，连那株稀有的也是。莫德说她从没见过有人做到，还向你要了配方。|到了早上，黑斑渐渐褪去。青翠社的人在温室里唱起了歌，唱得很难听。|莫德看着你试那个疗法，一直没说话，直到它见效。然后她向你要配方，你给了。}\n# en: {The cure kills what the blight hadn't. You burn the whole house. {around(tobias): Tobias helps|The Circle helps}, and nobody blames you, which is worse.|It fails. You lose half the collection, and all of your confidence for a week.|Maud watches the cure fail and helps you burn what's left. \"Everyone loses a house once,\" she says. \"Once.\"}\nopt.left.say.2 [f0aeea] = {疗法杀死了枯萎病没杀死的东西。你烧掉了整间温室。{around(tobias): 托比亚斯来帮忙|社员们来帮忙}，谁也没有怪你，这更难受。|失败了。你失去了一半的收藏，还有整整一个星期的自信。|莫德看着疗法失败，帮你把剩下的烧了。“谁都会丢一间温室，”她说，“一次。”}\n# en: Burn the sick plants now\nopt.right.label [90fc07] = 现在就烧掉病株\n# en: {You burn a third of the house and save the rest. It's the right call and it smells of grief.|You burn the sick ones before it spreads. The rare plant is among them.|Maud nods at what you burn and points at one plant you missed. You burn that too. She was right.}\nopt.right.say.1 [051d3a] = {你烧掉了三分之一的温室，保住了其余的。这是正确的决定，闻起来却像悲伤。|你在它蔓延之前烧掉了病株。那株稀有的也在其中。|莫德对你烧掉的那些点点头，又指了指一株你漏掉的。你把那株也烧了。她是对的。}\n\n@event verdant_head_gardener_show\n# en: The Valley Fair\ntitle [a34a57] = 山谷集市\n# en: {The valley's flower show, and the Verdant Circle's entry is yours to choose.|The flower show again; the judges remember you.|The show tent smells of crushed grass and rivalry.} {A farmer's pumpkin is the size of a cart.|Thornwick has sent a team.|The judges are three old women who have seen everything.}\ntext [6fe4b1] = {山谷里的花展到了，青翠社的参展作品由你来选。|又是花展；评委们还记得你。|展棚里弥漫着压碎的青草味和较劲的味道。}{一个农夫的南瓜有一辆车那么大。|荆木学院派了一支队伍来。|评委是三位见多识广的老太太。}\n# en: A crowded show tent in a village field, prize vegetables on trestle tables, a student head gardener carrying an enormous potted plant toward the judges.\nscene [1ae86c] = 村里田野上一座拥挤的展棚，搁板桌上摆着获奖的蔬菜，一个学生首席园丁捧着一盆巨大的植物走向评委。\n# en: Enter the night-blooming lily\nopt.left.label [0b838e] = 送夜开百合参展\n# en: {It opens at dusk in front of the judges, as if on cue. First prize, and a purse, and a drawing in the village paper.|The lily wins, narrowly, over a cabbage. Nobody will forget the cabbage's grower's face.|The three old judges watch the lily open and say nothing. Then all three write \"first\" at once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [3e17d0] = {黄昏时它在评委面前绽放，仿佛算准了时机。头奖，一袋奖金，还有村报上的一幅插画。|百合以微弱优势胜过了一颗卷心菜。没人会忘记那位卷心菜种植者的表情。|三位老评委看着百合绽放，一言不发。然后三个人同时写下了“第一”。}\n# en: {It refuses to open. The cabbage wins. The lily blooms at midnight, for nobody, in your arms.|Second place, to Thornwick. {around(tobias): Tobias says|The Circle says} second is good. It isn't.|The three old judges have seen night-blooming lilies before. They give it a polite second and the cabbage a ribbon.}\nopt.left.say.2 [add267] = {它不肯开。卷心菜赢了。百合在午夜绽放，只为你一个人，在你怀里。|第二名，输给了荆木学院。{around(tobias): 托比亚斯说|社员们说}第二名也很好。并不好。|那三位老评委以前就见过夜开的百合。她们礼貌地给了它第二名，给了卷心菜一条绶带。}\n# en: Enter the reliable roses\nopt.right.label [3ae698] = 送稳妥的玫瑰参展\n# en: {Third prize for the roses. Solid, respectable, forgettable.|The roses place. The Circle is pleased; the Circle is also bored.|The three old judges approve of roses. Second prize, and a long talk about pruning you'll actually use.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3995c0] = {玫瑰拿了第三名。扎实，体面，转眼就忘。|玫瑰得了名次。青翠社很满意；青翠社也很无聊。|那三位老评委喜欢玫瑰。第二名，外加一席关于修剪的长谈，你还真用得上。}\n\n@event library_senior_banned_book\n# en: A Request for a Banned Book\ntitle [5026f0] = 借阅禁书的请求\n# en: {A first-year brings you a request slip for a chained book. Senior clerks hold the keys, and the rules say no.|Another request for a chained book, from a student who looks desperate.|They've heard you hold the keys. The slips keep coming.} {The first-year's essay depends on it.|The slip is signed by a professor, perhaps.|The book in question bites.}\ntext [775ce5] = {一个一年级生递来一张借书单，要借一本上了锁链的书。钥匙在资深馆员手里，规矩说不行。|又一张借锁链书的单子，借书的学生看起来走投无路。|他们听说钥匙在你手里。借书单一张接一张地来。}{那个一年级生的作业全靠这本书了。|借书单上有一位教授的签名，大概吧。|那本书会咬人。}\n# en: A library desk at closing time, a nervous first-year holding a request slip for a chained book, a senior clerk with the keys looking down at it.\nscene [c4f362] = 闭馆时分的图书馆服务台，一个紧张的一年级生拿着借锁链书的单子，一个拿着钥匙的资深馆员低头看着它。\n# en: Fetch it, against the rules\nopt.left.label [b72eb7] = 违规去取\n# en: {You fetch it, watch them read it, and chain it back before closing. Nobody knows. The first-year passes with honours and leaves you a cake with crowns folded under it.|The book goes out and comes back, with a grateful envelope. The rules bent, and didn't break.|You fetch it in gloves. It bites the glove and not the reader. The essay is excellent, and the glove is a loss.}\nopt.left.say.1 [527293] = {你取来书，看着他们读完，闭馆前再锁回去。没人知道。那个一年级生以优异成绩通过，给你留了一块蛋糕，底下压着几枚克朗。|书借出去又还回来，附带一个表示感谢的信封。规矩弯了，没断。|你戴着手套去取。它咬了手套，没咬读者。论文写得很出色，手套报废了。}\n# en: {The head librarian finds the chain unlocked. Half your pay docked and a long, quiet talk.|Caught, with the key in your hand, and docked for it. You're still Senior Clerk; nobody quite knows how.|It bites the reader, and the head librarian sees the bandage. Your pay is docked, and the reader's thanks are muffled.}\nopt.left.say.2 [289a6c] = {总馆员发现锁链开着。扣了一半的工钱，外加一场长长的、安静的谈话。|被抓了个正着，钥匙还在你手里，工钱也因此被扣。你仍然是资深馆员；没人说得清这是怎么回事。|它咬了读者，总馆员看见了绷带。你的工钱被扣了，读者的道谢也说得含含糊糊。}\n# en: Refuse\nopt.right.label [cada35] = 拒绝\n# en: {You say no. The first-year leaves in tears. The head librarian, who heard, nods.|The rules hold. So does your conscience, more or less.|You say no, and show them the book from a safe distance. They see it snap at the air and stop asking.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d33e92] = {你说了不。那个一年级生哭着走了。听到这番话的总馆员点了点头。|规矩守住了。你的良心也大致守住了。|你说了不，然后隔着安全距离让他们看了看那本书。他们看见它对着空气咬了一口，就不再求了。}\n\n@event library_senior_night_flood\n# en: Water in the Stacks\ntitle [ccea75] = 书库进水\n# en: {A pipe bursts in the lower stacks at the end of your shift, and the senior clerk on duty is you.|Water in the stacks again. The pipes are older than the books.|The lower level floods every spring now.} {The rarest books are on the bottom shelf.|The water is rising.|Someone is shouting for help two floors up.}\ntext [1c6040] = {你的班快结束时，下层书库的一根水管爆了，当值的资深馆员就是你。|书库又进水了。这些水管比书还老。|如今下层每年春天都要淹。}{最稀有的书就在最底层的书架上。|水在上涨。|楼上两层有人在喊救命。}\n# en: A flooded lower library level at night, water lapping at the bottom shelves, a senior clerk wading with an armful of books.\nscene [58c52c] = 夜里被淹的图书馆下层，水拍打着最底层的书架，一个资深馆员抱着一摞书蹚水而行。\n# en: Save the rare books first\nopt.left.label [e4b589] = 先抢救稀有的书\n# en: {You carry armful after armful up the stair in freezing water until long after closing. Every rare book saved. Your boots are not.|You save the rarest and lose the common ones, and the head librarian, arriving at last, hugs you, which nobody has ever seen.|You save the rare books and then go up for the shouting. It's a first-year stuck on a ladder. You save them too.}\nopt.left.say.1 [02e465] = {你在冰冷的水里一趟又一趟地把书抱上楼梯，一直忙到闭馆后很久。每一本稀有的书都保住了。你的靴子没保住。|你救下了最稀有的，丢掉了普通的，总馆员终于赶到，抱了抱你，这是谁都没见过的事。|你救下了稀有的书，又上楼去找那个喊叫的人。是个困在梯子上的一年级生。你把对方也救了下来。}\n# en: Run for the head librarian\nopt.right.label [ffaf08] = 跑去找总馆员\n# en: {By the time she comes, a shelf is lost. \"You did right to fetch me,\" she says, and looks at the shelf.|She takes charge. You carry what she points at.|You run for the head librarian, past the shouting two floors up. She sends you back for the shouter first. She was right.}\nopt.right.say.1 [100588] = {等她赶到时，一整架书已经毁了。“你去找我是对的，”她说，看着那架书。|她接手指挥。她指什么，你就搬什么。|你跑去找总馆员，路过两层楼上的呼喊声。她让你先回去救喊叫的人。她是对的。}\n\n@event tavern_manager_inspector\n# en: The Inspector Calls\ntitle [663f74] = 检查员来访\n# en: {The licensing inspector is here, unannounced, and the landlady is away. The manager is you.|The inspector again, with the same long coat and longer list.|The inspector nods at you like an old enemy.} {The cellar has a leak.|The ledger has a page you'd rather they didn't read.|There's a goblin asleep in the back booth.}\ntext [69f885] = {执照检查员不打招呼就来了，老板娘不在。经理就是你。|又是那位检查员，同样的长大衣，更长的清单。|检查员像对老冤家一样冲你点点头。}{地窖漏水。|账簿里有一页你宁愿他们别看。|后面的卡座里睡着一个地精。}\n# en: A village tavern in the morning, a stern inspector in a long coat running a finger along a bar, a student manager following with a ledger.\nscene [3c5a55] = 早上的村酒馆，一个穿长大衣的严厉检查员用手指抹过吧台，一个学生经理拿着账簿跟在后面。\n# en: Show everything, honestly\nopt.left.label [cf598d] = 老老实实地全部展示\n# en: {The inspector finds the leak, notes it and passes the Newt with a warning. The landlady, back, gives you a bonus and her late mother's tankard.|A clean bill, almost. The inspector shakes your hand.|The inspector finds the goblin in the back booth, who turns out to be licensed. A clean bill, and a very long afternoon.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c08744] = {检查员发现了漏水，记了下来，给了蝾螈酒馆一个警告就放行了。老板娘回来后给了你一笔奖金，还有她已故母亲的那只大酒杯。|几乎干干净净。检查员和你握了握手。|检查员在后面的卡座里发现了那个地精，结果对方有执照。检查通过，下午也过得很漫长。}\n# en: {The fine is steep, the landlady takes a share out of your wages, and her face is steeper.|Three infractions. The till covers most of the fine. Your own wages cover the rest.|The inspector wakes the goblin in the back booth, who has opinions about inspectors. The fine is for the opinions.}\nopt.left.say.2 [7b7778] = {罚款很重，老板娘从你的工钱里扣了一份，她的脸色更重。|三项违规。钱柜付了大部分罚款，剩下的由你的工钱来付。|检查员吵醒了后面卡座里的地精，对方对检查员很有意见。罚款罚的就是这些意见。}\n# en: Steer them away from the cellar\nopt.right.label [666330] = 引他们避开地窖\n# en: {The inspector never goes downstairs. You fix the leak that night with your own hands, guiltily.|A clean bill, by omission. You don't tell the landlady how.|You steer the inspector past the back booth and the goblin sleeps on. A clean bill, by the narrowest of margins.}\nopt.right.say.1 [aae7bf] = {检查员始终没下楼。那天夜里你亲手修好了漏水，心里有愧。|靠隐瞒换来的干净。你没告诉老板娘是怎么做到的。|你引着检查员绕过后面的卡座，地精继续睡。检查通过，险之又险。}\n# en: {The inspector goes straight to the cellar. The fine doubles for \"obstruction\". Bram hears of it somehow.|Caught steering. The inspector writes your name down in a separate book.|The goblin in the back booth wakes and gives the inspector a tour. Of the cellar.}\nopt.right.say.2 [68e7ad] = {检查员径直走向地窖。罚款因为“妨碍检查”翻了一倍。布拉姆不知怎么听说了。|你引路被识破了。检查员把你的名字记在另一个本子上。|后面卡座里的地精醒了，热情地带检查员参观。参观的是地窖。}\n\n@event tavern_manager_grisk_offer\n# en: An Investor\ntitle [49ae9a] = 一位投资人\n# en: {Grisk would like to invest in the Newt, with new chairs and a second fire, \"and a little room upstairs for my associates\".|Grisk again, with a bigger bag of coin.|\"My offer stands,\" says Grisk, \"and grows.\"} {The chairs really are falling apart.|The landlady is away.|The bag of coin is very heavy.}\ntext [329733] = {格里斯克想投资蝾螈酒馆，新椅子，再加一个壁炉，“还有楼上给我的合伙人留个小房间”。|又是格里斯克，钱袋更大了。|“我的提议依然有效，”格里斯克说，“而且还在加码。”}{那些椅子确实快散架了。|老板娘不在。|那袋钱非常沉。}\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting at a tavern's best table, a bag of coin and a contract in front of him, a student manager standing opposite.\nscene [dbb834] = 一个穿天鹅绒马甲的地精坐在酒馆最好的桌子旁，面前放着一袋钱和一份合同，一个学生经理站在对面。\n# en: Take his money for the chairs\nopt.left.label [e60668] = 收下他的钱换椅子\n# en: {New chairs, a second fire, a fat \"manager's commission\" in your pocket, and Grisk's associates upstairs on Thursdays. Trade is up. So is the noise, at midnight.|Grisk pays you a finder's cut in a sealed envelope. The Newt has never looked better. The upstairs room has never been busier, and you don't go up there.|You take the heavy bag. Grisk's offer grows into the upstairs, then the cellar, then your evenings. The commission is excellent.}\nopt.left.say.1 [797c9a] = {新椅子，第二个壁炉，你口袋里一笔丰厚的“经理佣金”，每周四楼上坐着格里斯克的合伙人。生意好了。午夜的吵闹也多了。|格里斯克用一个封好的信封付了你一笔介绍费。蝾螈酒馆从没这么气派过。楼上的房间从没这么热闹过，而你从不上去。|你收下了那只沉甸甸的钱袋。格里斯克的投资从楼上扩展到地窖，再到你的晚上。佣金非常可观。}\n# en: Turn him down\nopt.right.label [b56858] = 拒绝他\n# en: {\"Your loss,\" says Grisk, and means it as a threat. The chairs stay wobbly.|You say no. Grisk tips his hat and drinks somewhere else for a month, which hurts trade.|You turn the heavier bag down too. Grisk laughs, and says he likes a manager with standards. He'll be back.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d2cba7] = {“你的损失，”格里斯克说，这话是威胁。椅子还是摇摇晃晃。|你说了不。格里斯克抬了抬帽子，去别处喝了一个月，生意受了影响。|你连更沉的那只钱袋也拒绝了。格里斯克笑了，说他喜欢有原则的经理。他还会再来。}\n\n@event apothecary_assistant_poison\n# en: A Dangerous Prescription\ntitle [08b2dc] = 危险的处方\n# en: {A prescription comes in from a village healer with a dose ten times too strong. Pell is out. Assistant apothecaries decide.|Another slip with a strange dose. You've learned to read the healer's handwriting, which doesn't help.|A dose that would fell a horse, for an old shepherd.} {The patient's grown son is waiting.|The healer is a day's ride away.|It might be a slip of the pen. It might not.}\ntext [89141c] = {村里一位治疗师送来一张处方，剂量重了十倍。佩尔不在。由助理药师决定。|又一张剂量古怪的单子。你已经认得那位治疗师的字迹了，可这没用。|能放倒一匹马的剂量，开给一个老牧羊人。}{病人已成年的儿子在等着。|治疗师在一天的路程之外。|可能是笔误。也可能不是。}\n# en: An apothecary counter, a prescription slip with a dose underlined in red, a young assistant alone behind the counter frowning at it.\nscene [ac1da0] = 药铺柜台上一张处方，剂量下面画着红线，一位年轻的助理独自站在柜台后，皱眉看着它。\n# en: Mix it at a safe dose\nopt.left.label [bc845e] = 按安全剂量调配\n# en: {You work out what the healer meant and mix that. The patient recovers. Pell, back, reads the slip and goes white, then pats your shoulder for a long time.|Right dose, right remedy. The healer writes later to thank you, embarrassed.|You mix the shepherd a dose that wouldn't fell a sparrow. It works. The healer, told, sends Pell a very apologetic letter.}\nopt.left.say.1 [98b4d0] = {你推断出治疗师的本意，按那个剂量调了药。病人好了。佩尔回来看了那张单子，脸都白了，然后拍了你的肩膀很久。|剂量对，药也对。治疗师后来写信道谢，很是难为情。|你给那位牧羊人调了一剂连麻雀都撂不倒的药。管用了。治疗师听说后，给佩尔写了一封非常抱歉的信。}\n# en: {Your safe dose is too weak. The patient recovers slowly, and the son tells the whole village.|You get it nearly right. Nearly is a word you stop using for a while.|You cut the dose so far that it does nothing. The shepherd comes back, unwell, and Pell has to sort it out.}\nopt.left.say.2 [2abb4d] = {你的安全剂量太弱了。病人好得很慢，他儿子告诉了全村。|你差不多调对了。有一阵子你不再用“差不多”这个词。|你把剂量减得太多，毫无作用。牧羊人又回来了，还是不舒服，佩尔只好亲自处理。}\n# en: Refuse until the healer confirms\nopt.right.label [14e4f0] = 等治疗师确认后再说\n# en: {A day's wait. The patient's son shouts at you. The healer confirms the slip was wrong. You were right, and it was a long day.|You send a rider. The answer comes at midnight. You stay up for it.|It was a slip of the pen. The healer confirms by midnight, and you mix the right dose by one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b2d046] = {等了一天。病人的儿子冲你大喊大叫。治疗师确认单子写错了。你是对的，那是漫长的一天。|你派人骑马去问。答复半夜才到。你一直等着。|那是笔误。治疗师午夜前确认了，你一点前调好了正确的剂量。}\n\n@event apothecary_assistant_rival_shop\n# en: A Shop Across the Square\ntitle [3596c9] = 广场对面的铺子\n# en: {A new apothecary has opened across the square, all brass and bright bottles and half Pell's prices.|The shop across the square has a queue now.|Pell has started calling the new shop \"that brass insult\".} {Their remedies are mostly coloured water.|They've hired a singer for the doorway.|Pell's daughters have written to ask what's wrong.}\ntext [e6dd15] = {广场对面新开了一家药铺，满眼铜器和亮闪闪的瓶子，价钱只有佩尔的一半。|广场对面的铺子现在排起了队。|佩尔开始管那家新铺子叫“那个铜皮侮辱”。}{他们的药大多是染了色的水。|他们雇了个歌手站在门口。|佩尔的女儿们写信来问出了什么事。}\n# en: A gleaming new apothecary shop across a village square, its window full of bright bottles, an old apothecary peering at it from his own doorway.\nscene [1729f3] = 村广场对面一家闪亮的新药铺，橱窗里满是亮闪闪的瓶子，一位老药师从自家门口朝那边张望。\n# en: Expose their coloured water\nopt.left.label [4016d1] = 揭穿他们的染色水\n# en: {You buy a bottle of everything and test them in Pell's back room. The results go on a board in the window. The brass shop closes by the season's end.|Your tests are fair and public. The village comes back to Pell, a little sheepish.|You write to Pell's daughters with your test results. They come home for a week and help you nail the board to the window.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a4b64a] = {你把他们的每样药都买了一瓶，在佩尔的后屋里检验。结果贴在橱窗的告示板上。那家铜器铺子没到季末就关门了。|你的检验公平又公开。村里人回到了佩尔这儿，有点不好意思。|你把检验结果写信告诉了佩尔的女儿们。她们回来住了一个星期，帮你把告示板钉到了橱窗上。}\n# en: {Their remedies turn out to work, annoyingly. You look petty. Pell says, \"Well, now we know.\"|Inconclusive, and everyone saw you try.|Pell's daughters write back that the brass shop's remedies are fine and that you should worry about Pell's. You do.}\nopt.left.say.2 [a8d769] = {偏偏他们的药是管用的。你显得小家子气。佩尔说：“好吧，现在我们知道了。”|没有定论，而且所有人都看见你试过了。|佩尔的女儿们回信说铜器铺子的药没问题，你该担心的是佩尔的药。你开始担心了。}\n# en: Lower Pell's prices\nopt.right.label [8b354b] = 降低佩尔的价钱\n# en: {Cheaper remedies, longer hours, a full shop. Pell grumbles and is secretly delighted.|You match them on price and beat them on care. It's slow, and it works.|You lower the prices and write to the daughters that all is well. It is, nearly. Pell reads your letter over your shoulder and doesn't object.}\nopt.right.say.1 [fd517c] = {更便宜的药，更长的营业时间，满满一铺子人。佩尔嘴上抱怨，心里暗暗高兴。|你在价钱上追平他们，在用心上胜过他们。很慢，但管用。|你降了价，写信告诉女儿们一切都好。差不多算好。佩尔在你肩后读了信，没有反对。}\n\n@event courier_chief_urgent_crossing\n# en: The Urgent Crossing\ntitle [50b65f] = 紧急穿越\n# en: {A red-sealed package for the far side of the mountains, by tomorrow, and only the chief courier flies red seals.|Another red seal, another gale over the pass.|The red seals come to you by name now.} {The pass is in cloud.|A storm is coming in from the west.|The package is humming.}\ntext [d958a7] = {一个盖着红色火漆的包裹，明天要送到山那边，只有首席信使能送红漆件。|又一个红漆件，山口上又是一阵大风。|如今红漆件都指名给你。}{山口被云遮住了。|一场风暴正从西边过来。|包裹在嗡嗡作响。}\n# en: A courier on a broom flying through a mountain pass in a gale, a red-sealed package clutched to their chest, lightning on the peaks.\nscene [75ad0b] = 一个骑着飞帚的信使在大风中穿过山口，怀里紧抱着一个红漆包裹，山峰上电闪雷鸣。\n# en: Fly the pass tonight\nopt.left.label [8a3c7b] = 今晚就飞过山口\n# en: {Through the pass in the dark, lightning on both sides. You deliver at dawn to a pale woman who breaks the seal and weeps with relief. The bonus is the biggest of your life.|You make it, frozen, at dawn. The recipient signs with shaking hands.|The humming package hums louder in the pass and the wind stays off you. You deliver at dawn and don't ask what it was.}\nopt.left.say.1 [15256d] = {你在黑暗中穿过山口，两侧都是闪电。天亮时你把包裹交给一个脸色苍白的女人，她拆开火漆，如释重负地哭了。这是你一生中最大的一笔奖金。|你冻僵了，天亮时赶到。收件人签字时双手发抖。|那个嗡嗡作响的包裹在山口里响得更大声，风一直没扑到你身上。你天亮时送到了，没问那是什么。}\n# en: {The gale takes you into a snowfield. You walk the last mile with the package and a broken wrist, and arrive late.|You crash short of the pass and are found by a goatherd. The package arrives a day late, via goat.|The humming package hums a note the lightning likes. You land hard and walk it in, late, with the hair on your arms standing up.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c99d6d] = {大风把你卷进了雪原。你带着包裹和一只断了的手腕走完最后一英里，到得晚了。|你在山口前坠落，被一个牧羊人发现。包裹晚到了一天，是羊驮去的。|那个嗡嗡作响的包裹哼出了一个闪电喜欢的音。你重重落地，走着把它送到，晚了，胳膊上的汗毛全竖着。}\n# en: Wait for the storm to pass\nopt.right.label [fd0d0f] = 等风暴过去\n# en: {You wait out the storm and fly at dawn. A day late. The recipient is gracious, and the loft-master isn't.|Late but whole. You'll always wonder what was in it.|You wait out the storm with the humming package on the table. It hums all night. You don't sleep.}\nopt.right.say.1 [676ccb] = {你等风暴过去，天亮才飞。晚了一天。收件人很宽厚，阁楼主管可不。|晚了，但人是完整的。你会一直好奇里面装的是什么。|你等着暴风雨过去，那个嗡嗡作响的包裹就放在桌上。它哼了一整夜。你没睡着。}\n\n@event courier_chief_new_runner\n# en: The New Runner\ntitle [c54f90] = 新来的跑腿\n# en: {The loft has a new runner, younger than you were, who holds the broom like a mop.|Another new runner, another bad grip.|The new runners all ask for you now.} {Their first route is across the lake.|They've never flown in rain.|Their parents are watching from the gate.}\ntext [fcaa4e] = {阁楼来了个新跑腿，比你当年还小，握飞帚像握拖把。|又一个新跑腿，又一个错误的握法。|如今新跑腿们都点名要你带。}{他们的第一条路线要飞过湖。|他们从没在雨里飞过。|他们的父母在门口看着。}\n# en: A courier loft at dawn, a nervous young runner holding a broom the wrong way round, a chief courier showing them the grip.\nscene [7eddf7] = 清晨的信使阁楼，一个紧张的年轻跑腿把飞帚拿反了，一位首席信使在教他们怎么握。\n# en: Fly their first route with them\nopt.left.label [03c8ff] = 陪他们飞第一条路线\n# en: {You fly beside them across the lake, calling the wind. They land shaking and grinning. They'll name a broom after you, they say.|A slow route, flown twice. They'll be good, one day.|You fly their first route with their parents watching from the gate. They land perfectly and pretend it was nothing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6979fa] = {你在他们身边飞过湖面，替他们喊出风向。他们落地时发着抖，咧着嘴笑。他们说要用你的名字给飞帚命名。|一条慢悠悠的路线，飞了两遍。他们总有一天会飞得很好。|他们的父母在门口看着，你陪他们飞了第一条路线。他们落得完美无缺，还装作若无其事。}\n# en: Let them learn on their own\nopt.right.label [4c5eb3] = 让他们自己学\n# en: {They come back soaked and late, with every parcel. You were the same.|They learn the hard way. So did you.|You let them go alone with their parents watching. They wobble, recover, and wave to the gate. You breathe again.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8df8f9] = {他们回来时浑身湿透，晚了，但每个包裹都送到了。你当年也一样。|他们吃了苦头才学会。你也是。|你让他们在父母的注视下独自出发。他们晃了晃，稳住了，朝门口挥手。你这才又喘过气来。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/village_home.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/village_home.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event village_barge_crossing\n# en: The Barge Across\ntitle [bac81a] = 渡湖驳船\n# en: {The village barge is slower than walking, if you could walk on water.|The bargeman has started nodding at you like a regular.|The bargeman saves you the dry end of the bench.} {season == \"winter\": Ice ticks against the hull.|{season == \"summer\": Dragonflies ride the bow for free.|{A first-year is being sick, very politely.|Two prefects are pretending not to hold hands.|Someone is reading aloud to a crate of chickens.}}}\ntext [26d4cd] = {去小镇的驳船慢得很——如果你能在水上走路，走过去都比它快。|船夫开始像对待熟客那样冲你点头了。|船夫开始给你留长凳上干的那一头了。} {season == \"winter\": 薄冰一下下轻敲着船身。|{season == \"summer\": 蜻蜓停在船头，免费搭船。|{一个一年级生正趴在船边呕吐，吐得非常有礼貌。|两个级长正假装没在牵手。|有人在给一笼鸡大声朗读。}}}\n# en: A flat-bottomed barge crossing a mirror-still lake, the seven towers shrinking behind it.\nscene [456c6d] = 一艘平底驳船驶过镜面般平静的湖，七座塔在身后渐渐变小。\n# en: Read on the crossing\nopt.left.label [467133] = 渡湖时看书\n# en: {You finish a chapter and miss the towers turning gold behind you.|You read the same paragraph four times; the lake keeps interrupting.|You look up only when the hull bumps the quay, and feel you've skipped something.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7d78ba] = {你读完一章，却错过了身后的塔楼被夕阳染成金色。|同一段你读了四遍；湖光总来打岔。|直到船身撞上码头你才抬头，总觉得错过了什么。}\n# en: Chat with the bargeman\nopt.right.label [89b231] = 跟船夫聊天\n# en: {He tells you the lake was rougher in his father's day, and expects a tip for the history.|He has opinions about every tower and charges for none of them, except in tips.|He asks after your exams as if they were a sick relative. You tip him for the sympathy.}\nopt.right.say.1 [886987] = {他说他父亲那时候湖上风浪大得多，并且指望你为这段历史付点小费。|他对每座塔都有看法，一概免费——小费除外。|他问起你的考试，口气像在问候一位生病的亲戚。你为这份同情付了小费。}\n\n@event village_square_errand\n# en: One Thing in the Square\ntitle [e25a84] = 广场上的“就一件事”\n# en: {friend} has come down to the village with you \"for one thing\", which turns out to be several. {First, a hat.|You've learned that \"one thing\" means at least three.|You've stopped asking what the one thing is.} {A stallholder is selling candied chestnuts in paper twists.|A tinker is mending a cauldron with a great deal of swearing.|The market cat has chosen {friend} and will not be moved.}\ntext [f59a25] = {friend}陪你下山到小镇，说是“就办一件事”，结果是好几件。{先是一顶帽子。|你已经明白，“一件事”至少意味着三件。|你已经不再问那“一件事”是什么了。}{一个摊贩在卖纸包的糖炒栗子。|一个补锅匠正骂骂咧咧地修一口坩埚。|集市上的猫选中了{friend}，怎么也赶不走。}\n# en: A crooked village market square, two students weaving between stalls, one already carrying too many parcels.\nscene [52254a] = 歪歪扭扭的小镇集市广场，两个学生在摊位间穿行，其中一个已经抱了太多包裹。\n# en: Carry the parcels\nopt.left.label [faeb8d] = 帮忙拎包裹\n# en: {You come back up the hill with an armful of string-tied parcels and sore wrists.|{friend} buys you a chestnut as payment. It is not enough payment.|One parcel clinks suspiciously. You decide not to ask.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c7a573] = {你抱着一摞系着绳子的包裹爬回山上，手腕酸痛。|{friend}买了颗栗子当报酬。这报酬远远不够。|有个包裹叮当作响，十分可疑。你决定不问。}\n# en: Talk {them} out of it\nopt.right.label [2a8f41] = 劝{them}别买了\n# en: {You talk {friend} out of the third thing. {They} sulk{s} the whole way back to the barge.|You win. {friend} says you'd make a terrible shopkeeper and a worse friend, then takes your arm anyway.|You talk {friend} out of the third thing. The market cat follows {them} home anyway, which is a sort of purchase.}\nopt.right.say.1 [41937f] = {你劝{friend}放弃了第三样东西。{They}一路生着闷气回到驳船上。|你赢了。{friend}说你会是个糟糕的店主、更糟糕的朋友，然后照样挽起你的胳膊。|你劝{friend}放弃了第三样东西。可市场的猫还是跟着{them}回了家，这也算买了点什么。}\n# en: Your argument is excellent. {friend} buys the thing anyway, and now you've been wrong in public.\nopt.right.say.2 [c1ae81] = 你的论证无懈可击。{friend}还是买了，于是你当众输了一回。\n\n@event village_last_barge\n# en: The Last Barge\ntitle [d7085d] = 末班驳船\n# en: {The last barge back leaves at the bell, and the bell is ringing.|The last-barge bell, and you're nowhere near the quay. The bargeman knows your running by now.|The bell. Of course the bell.} {season == \"winter\": Snow is coming down thick over the square.|{The inn has started a song with too many verses.|The lamps are only half lit.|The lake has gone the colour of pewter.}} {final_year: Not many evenings like this are left.|You could run. You could stay.}\ntext [52b86c] = {回城堡的末班驳船随钟声开船，而钟声正在响。|末班船的钟声响了，你离码头还远着呢。船夫早就熟悉你奔跑的样子了。|钟声。当然是钟声。} {season == \"winter\": 大雪正纷纷扬扬落在广场上。|{酒馆里有人唱起一首段落多得没完的歌。|街灯才点亮一半。|湖水变成了锡镴般的灰色。}} {final_year: 这样的夜晚剩下的不多了。|你可以跑。你也可以留下。}\n# en: A lamplit village quay at dusk, the last barge pulling at its rope, a student hesitating at the edge of the square.\nscene [aa10dd] = 黄昏时灯火点点的小镇码头，末班驳船拽着缆绳，一个学生在广场边犹豫。\n# en: Run for it\nopt.left.label [38d292] = 跑去赶船\n# en: {You make it with a leap and one wet boot.|You make it; your scarf does not.|The bargeman holds the rope a heartbeat longer than he should. You owe him.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e72148] = {你纵身一跃赶上了，湿了一只靴子。|你赶上了；你的围巾没赶上。|船夫多攥了一会儿缆绳，比他该攥的久一点。你欠他个人情。}\n# en: Stay a little longer\nopt.right.label [4a025a] = 再待一会儿\n# en: {A fisherman rows you back for a ruinous fare and a story you didn't ask for.|You pay the night ferryman far too much. He rows like he has somewhere better to be.|The fisherman who rows you back knows you by now and charges you the regular's rate, which is still a great deal.}\nopt.right.say.1 [238628] = {一个渔夫划船送你回去，要价高得离谱，还附赠一个你没想听的故事。|你付给夜间摆渡人太多钱了。他划船的样子，像是有更要紧的地方要去。|划船送你回去的渔夫现在认得你了，给你算的是熟客价，那也还是很贵。}\n# en: You have no fare for the night ferryman, so you sleep on a bench under the Newt's eaves and wake with a cat on your feet.\nopt.right.say.2 [2c3fa2] = 你付不起夜里船夫的船钱，只好在蝾螈酒馆的屋檐下睡了一夜长凳，醒来时脚上趴着一只猫。\n# en: {You sleep on a bench under the Newt's eaves and wake up with a cat on your feet.|The first barge of morning finds you yawning on the quay.|You sleep under the Newt's eaves again. The cat has started saving you the warm end of the bench.}\nopt.right.say.3 [dab239] = {你在蝾螈酒馆的屋檐下睡了一夜长凳，醒来时脚上趴着一只猫。|清晨的头班驳船发现你正在码头上打哈欠。|你又在蝾螈酒馆的屋檐下睡了一夜。那只猫开始给你留长凳暖和的那一头了。}\n\n@event gilded_newt_fireside\n# en: By the Newt's Fire\ntitle [5b6147] = 蝾螈的炉火边\n# en: {The Gilded Newt's fire has never gone out, and the landlady says it never will while she lives.|The landlady chalks your name on her slate before you've ordered.|Your corner by the fire is free. The regulars leave it for you now.} {season == \"winter\": Snow hisses off the boots of everyone who comes in.|{A dog is asleep with its paws in the hearth.|Someone has hung wet socks on the fire-irons.|A farmer is explaining turnips to nobody.}}\ntext [988636] = {镀金蝾螈酒馆的炉火从没熄过，老板娘说只要她还活着，就永远不会熄。|你还没点单，老板娘就已经在石板上写好了你的名字。|炉边你的那个角落空着。常客们现在都把它留给你。} {season == \"winter\": 每个进门的人靴子上的雪都在嘶嘶作响。|{一条狗把爪子搭在炉边睡着了。|有人把湿袜子挂在了火钳上。|一个农夫正对着空气讲解芜菁。}}\n# en: A low-beamed tavern with a roaring hearth, a student tucked into the corner seat beside it with a steaming cup.\nscene [886f83] = 低矮房梁的酒馆里炉火熊熊，一个学生窝在炉边的角落座位，捧着一杯冒热气的饮料。\n# en: Order a cider and stay\nopt.left.label [fc74cf] = 点杯苹果酒坐着\n# en: {The cider is warm and spiced and costs exactly what the landlady says it does.|You stay until your cheeks are red and your worries are smaller.|The landlady brings the cider without being asked, and charges for it all the same.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d21078] = {苹果酒温热、带着香料味，价钱分毫不差，正如老板娘所说。|你一直坐到脸颊发红，烦恼也变小了。|老板娘没等你开口就端来了苹果酒，钱照收不误。}\n# en: Work by the firelight\nopt.right.label [01152e] = 借着炉光干活\n# en: {You get two pages done between three toasts and a fiddle.|The noise is terrible, and somehow you concentrate better than in the library.|A regular reads over your shoulder and corrects your spelling of \"wyvern\".}\nopt.right.say.1 [ba7f3e] = {在三轮祝酒和一段提琴之间，你写完了两页。|吵得要命，可不知为何你比在图书馆里还专心。|一位常客从你肩后看过来，纠正了你“双足飞龙”的拼写。}\n\n@event gilded_newt_card_game\n# en: Cards at the Newt\ntitle [cae7f0] = 蝾螈的牌局\n# en: {There's a card game in the back room of the Gilded Newt, and an empty chair.|The empty chair in the back room has a cushion on it now, as if it's been waiting.|{flag(newt_cards_played): The card players greet you by the name of your worst hand.|The card players nod you towards the chair as if you were a regular.}} {Tonight it's a tinker, a widow and a man who shuffles like a conjuror.|Tonight the stakes are copper and pride.|Tonight a second-year from Duskgate is losing very cheerfully.}\ntext [9a54d0] = {镀金蝾螈的后屋有一局牌，还有一把空椅子。|后屋那把空椅子上如今多了个坐垫，像是一直在等你。|{flag(newt_cards_played): 牌友们用你最烂的那手牌给你起了外号来打招呼。|牌友们朝那把椅子努努嘴，好像你是常客。}} {今晚是一个补锅匠、一个寡妇，还有一个洗牌像变戏法的男人。|今晚的赌注是铜板和面子。|今晚一个暮门的二年级生正输得兴高采烈。}\n# en: A smoky back room in a tavern, a card game around a scarred table and one empty chair.\nscene [939cdf] = 烟雾缭绕的酒馆后屋，一张满是划痕的桌上正在打牌，还有一把空椅子。\n# en: Take the empty chair\nopt.left.label [2c28bd] = 坐上那把空椅子\n# en: The stake is a few crowns a hand.\nopt.left.hint [dc8887] = 每手的赌注是几枚克朗。\n# en: {You win a handful of crowns and a reputation for an honest face.|You win, and the widow asks where you learned to bluff like that.|The Duskgate second-year loses to you as cheerfully as to everyone. You win a few crowns and an invitation to lose them back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6273ff] = {你赢了一把克朗，还落了个“老实面相”的名声。|你赢了，寡妇问你从哪儿学的这手虚张声势。|那个暮门的二年级生输给你时和输给别人一样开心。你赢了几枚克朗，外加一份把它们输回去的邀请。}\n# en: {You lose your crowns slowly, and then all at once.|The tinker takes your last coin and buys you a drink with it, which somehow makes it worse.|The Duskgate second-year wins for once, delighted. You lose your stake and keep your good humour, mostly.}\nopt.left.say.2 [498b22] = {你的克朗先是慢慢地输，然后一下子全没了。|补锅匠赢走你最后一枚硬币，又用它请你喝了一杯，不知怎的更难受了。|那个暮门的二年级生难得赢了一回，乐坏了。你输了赌注，好心情大体保住了。}\n# en: Watch for the tells\nopt.right.label [f1d424] = 观察他们的破绽\n# en: {You spend the evening watching. The man who shuffles like a conjuror scratches his ear when he lies.|You learn a great deal about bluffing and nothing about having fun.|Someone asks if you're playing or haunting. You leave before you have to answer.}\nopt.right.say.1 [879773] = {你看了一整晚。那个洗牌像变戏法的男人，撒谎时会挠耳朵。|你学到了很多虚张声势的门道，却一点乐子也没有。|有人问你是来打牌的还是来闹鬼的。你没等回答就走了。}\n\n@event gilded_newt_corner_table\n# en: The Corner Table\ntitle [30043d] = 角落那张桌\n# en: {here} is at the corner table with {a jug of cider and no plans to leave.|the house jug, which the landlady refills for whoever sits there.|the table's jug. The landlady nods at you as you come in, as if you're expected.} \"{Sit down, you're blocking the fire.|You look like you need a drink. I look like I need company.|Don't tell anyone you saw me here.}\" {season == \"winter\": Snow is piling up against the window.|The window is open to the night.}\ntext [3523fb] = {here}坐在角落那张桌旁，{面前一壶苹果酒，完全没有走的意思。|面前是店里那只专属酒壶，谁坐在那儿，老板娘就给谁续满。|面前是那张桌的酒壶。你进门时老板娘冲你点点头，好像早料到你会来。}“{坐下吧，你挡着火了。|你看上去需要喝一杯。我看上去需要个伴。|别告诉别人你在这儿见过我。}”{season == \"winter\": 雪正堆在窗台上。|窗户朝着夜色敞开。}\n# en: A tavern corner table with a half-empty jug of cider, someone familiar waving a student over.\nscene [5c8294] = 酒馆角落的桌上放着半壶苹果酒，一个熟面孔正招手叫学生过去。\n# en: Sit down for one drink\nopt.left.label [e16ef4] = 坐下喝一杯\n# en: {One drink becomes three stories and a long walk to the barge.|{here} tells you something true halfway down the jug, then pretends not to have.|You stay later than you meant to. It's worth the morning.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2314b6] = {一杯变成了三个故事，外加一段走回驳船的长路。|喝到半壶时{here}跟你说了句真心话，然后装作没说过。|你待得比打算的晚。明早难熬，但值得。}\n# en: Wave and keep walking\nopt.right.label [11b59d] = 挥挥手继续走\n# en: {You wave and get back to your book. {here} toasts your back, a little hurt.|{term: You plead an early lecture. It's even true, and you read for it before bed.|You plead an early start, and read in your room instead.}|You wave. {here} raises the jug at you as if you'll be back. You will.}\nopt.right.say.1 [807665] = {你挥挥手，回去看你的书。{here}冲你的背影举杯，有点受伤。|{term: 你推说明早有课。这居然是真的，睡前你还为它预习了。|你推说明天要早起，回房间看书去了。}|你挥挥手。{here}朝你举起酒壶，好像笃定你会回来。你会的。}\n\n@event mabels_the_door\n# en: Mabel Reads the Door\ntitle [d9674c] = 梅布尔看门识人\n# en: {Mabel looks up as you open the door and is already reaching for a tin.|Mabel watches you open the door the way a doctor watches a cough.|You've tried opening Mabel's door every way you can think of. She still knows.} {The bell over the door rings a little flat today.|There's a queue of two, which for Mabel's is a crowd.|A cake is cooling on the counter, out of reach and smug about it.}\ntext [3862f4] = {你一推门，梅布尔抬起头，手已经伸向了一个茶罐。|梅布尔看你推门的样子，就像医生听人咳嗽。|你试过用各种方式推梅布尔的门。她照样看得出来。} {今天门上的铃铛响得有点走调。|店里排着两个人的队，对梅布尔茶馆来说这已经算拥挤了。|柜台上晾着一块蛋糕，够不着，还一脸得意。}\n# en: A tiny tea shop crammed with tins, the shop-bell over the door still swinging, a shrewd woman behind the counter already reaching for a tin.\nscene [8b72a6] = 塞满茶罐的小茶馆，门上的铃铛还在摇晃，柜台后一位精明的妇人已经伸手去拿茶罐。\n# en: Let Mabel choose your tea\nopt.left.label [d2a63d] = 让梅布尔替你选\n# en: {She brings you something smoky and says, \"For the week you've had.\" You hadn't told her about the week.|She brings you something with rose in it and won't explain why. It helps.|It's mint. It's always mint when you've been lying to yourself, apparently.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f17eac] = {她端来一杯带烟熏味的茶，说：“给你这一周喝的。”你可没跟她提过这一周。|她端来一杯有玫瑰味的茶，也不解释为什么。挺管用。|是薄荷茶。据说你自欺欺人的时候，永远是薄荷茶。}\n# en: Fake a cheerful entrance\nopt.right.label [35b845] = 装出高兴的样子进门\n# en: {She brings you the cheerful tea and waves your coins away, \"for the performance\". It's so good you actually cheer up, which may have been her plan all along.|Mabel looks fooled, and doesn't charge you. Then she adds a biscuit, which means she wasn't.|You've practised the door. Mabel gives you the cheerful tea and a long look, and charges you for neither.}\nopt.right.say.1 [bf7468] = {她端来了“开心的茶”，摆摆手不收你的钱，说是“看在表演的分上”。好喝得你真的开心起来——说不定这本来就是她的打算。|梅布尔一副被骗过去的样子，没收你的钱。然后她多给了你一块饼干，说明她根本没被骗。|你练过怎么开门了。梅布尔给了你开心的茶，又久久地看了你一眼，两样都没收钱。}\n# en: {She brings you a pot labelled, in her own hand, \"for people pretending\". She doesn't charge for it, which is somehow worse.|\"Nice try, dear,\" says Mabel, and brings the strong one, on the house.|Mabel watches your cheerful entrance the way a doctor watches a cough, and brings the strong one, free.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ec9730] = {她端来一壶茶，壶上是她亲手写的标签：“给装样子的人”。她没收钱，这反而更糟。|“想得美，亲爱的。”梅布尔说完，端来了浓的那种，算店里请的。|梅布尔像医生看咳嗽一样看着你开心地进门，然后端来了浓的那种，不收钱。}\n\n@event mabels_window_seat\n# en: The Window Seat\ntitle [15c249] = 靠窗的座位\n# en: {Mabel's window seat looks out over the lake to the castle, and it's free.|Mabel has stopped asking if you're waiting for someone at the window seat.|Your window seat. Mabel puts a cushion on it when she sees you on the quay.} {season == \"winter\": Frost has drawn ferns on the glass.|{season == \"spring\": Blossom keeps landing on the sill.|{The towers look small from here, almost friendly.|Rain streaks the glass and blurs the towers.|Below, the barges come and go, slow as thoughts.}}}\ntext [1bd9bd] = {梅布尔茶馆的靠窗座位能望见湖对岸的城堡，而且正空着。|你坐在靠窗座位时，梅布尔已经不再问你是不是在等人了。|你的靠窗座位。梅布尔一看见你在码头，就往上放个软垫。} {season == \"winter\": 霜在玻璃上画出了蕨叶。|{season == \"spring\": 花瓣不停落在窗台上。|{从这儿看，塔楼显得小小的，几乎有点亲切。|雨水顺着玻璃流下，把塔楼晕成一片。|下面，驳船来来往往，慢得像思绪。}}}\n# en: A window seat in a tea shop looking across a still lake to seven distant towers, a teapot and open notes on the sill.\nscene [36406e] = 茶馆的靠窗座位，隔着平静的湖望见远处七座塔，窗台上放着茶壶和摊开的笔记。\n# en: Make one pot last all day\nopt.left.label [abe18d] = 一壶茶坐一整天\n# en: {You get through a whole chapter on the one free pot Mabel gives window-seat readers, under her increasingly pointed looks.|By the third hour the tea is cold and Mabel is dusting very close to your table.|You finish your notes. Mabel says nothing, which is worse.}\nopt.left.say.1 [aed9c1] = {梅布尔给坐窗边看书的人免费续一壶茶，你靠这一壶读完了整整一章，她的眼神越来越意味深长。|到第三个钟头，茶凉了，梅布尔在离你桌子很近的地方掸灰。|你写完了笔记。梅布尔一句话也没说，这更可怕。}\n# en: Order cake with it\nopt.right.label [8d317c] = 再配块蛋糕\n# en: {flag(mabel_lemon): {Mabel has started cutting the lemon cake before you sit down.|Mabel brings the lemon without asking. You eat it slowly while the barges come and go.|The lemon cake, the window, the lake. The work can wait.}|Mabel has three cakes. You pick the lemon, and it's worth every crown.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9d5597] = {flag(mabel_lemon): {梅布尔开始在你坐下之前就切好柠檬蛋糕了。|梅布尔没等你开口就端来了柠檬蛋糕。你慢慢吃着，看驳船来来去去。|柠檬蛋糕、窗户、湖。功课可以等一等。}|梅布尔有三种蛋糕。你挑了柠檬味的，每个克朗都花得值。}\n\n@event mabels_forty_teas\n# en: Forty Kinds of Tea\ntitle [e3533b] = 四十种茶\n# en: {friend} wants to try one of Mabel's forty teas, and Mabel insists on choosing. {Today she starts a chart of who has tried what.|Mabel's chart of your friends' teas is pinned behind the counter now; {friend}'s column gets a tick.|Mabel's chart runs to a second sheet. {friend}'s column is the shortest, and Mabel looks at it pointedly.} {Mabel pretends not to be interested.|Mabel has opinions about the order.|Mabel insists on the order, and on the chart.}\ntext [beab52] = {friend}想尝尝梅布尔的四十种茶之一，而梅布尔坚持由她来选。{今天她开始画一张表，记下谁喝过什么。|梅布尔那张记录你朋友们喝茶的表，如今钉在了柜台后面；{friend}那一栏添了一个勾。|梅布尔的表已经画到第二页了。{friend}那一栏最短，梅布尔意味深长地盯着它。}{梅布尔假装不感兴趣。|梅布尔对喝的顺序很有意见。|梅布尔坚持顺序，也坚持那张表。}\n# en: Two students at a small tea-shop table crowded with mismatched teapots, one writing in a notebook.\nscene [b50dbf] = 两个学生坐在茶馆的小桌旁，桌上挤满了不成套的茶壶，其中一人在本子上写着什么。\n# en: Pay for both pots\nopt.left.label [76b503] = 两壶都我请\n# en: {{friend} pronounces today's tea \"bracing\", which means awful. You drink yours anyway.|You pay. Mabel marks you on the chart as a patron of the arts.|{friend} declares it the best tea in the village, then sniffs yours to be sure.}\nopt.left.say.1 [77f68a] = {{friend}宣布今天的茶“提神”，意思是难喝。你还是把自己那杯喝完了。|你付了钱。梅布尔在表上把你记作“艺术赞助人”。|{friend}宣布这是全镇最好的茶，然后凑过来闻了闻你那杯，以防万一。}\n# en: Let {friend} pay this time\nopt.right.label [586795] = 这次让{friend}请客\n# en: {{friend} pays, and calls you unadventurous for ordering your usual. Your tea is perfect, thank you.|\"Coward,\" says {friend} fondly, paying for your usual and their something purple.|{friend} pays, grumbling, and drinks something that smells of bonfires and looks betrayed by it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2a1c2b] = {{friend}付了钱，还说你点老样子太不爱冒险。你的茶好得很，谢谢。|“胆小鬼，”{friend}亲昵地说，一边替你的老样子和自己那杯紫色的东西付了钱。|{friend}嘟囔着付了钱，喝了一杯有篝火味的茶，一脸被背叛的表情。}\n\n@event grimsbys_wrong_book\n# en: Not the Book You Wanted\ntitle [480464] = 不是你要的那本\n# en: {You ask Mr Grimsby for a textbook. He vanishes into the shelves and comes back with something else entirely.|You ask Mr Grimsby for one thing. He brings you another, with great confidence.|Mr Grimsby sees you coming and is already halfway up a ladder, looking for the wrong book.} {Today's is a cookery book with a whole chapter on eels.|Today's is a history of a town that may not exist.|Today's is a guide to the moods of weather.}\ntext [9e8037] = {你向格林斯比先生要一本教科书。他消失在书架间，拿回来的完全是另一本。|你向格林斯比先生要一样东西。他信心十足地拿来另一样。|格林斯比先生一看见你进门，就已经爬上半截梯子，去找那本错的书了。} {今天这本是菜谱，有整整一章讲鳗鱼。|今天这本是一座可能并不存在的小镇的历史。|今天这本是一本讲天气脾性的指南。}\n# en: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean together, an elderly bookseller on a ladder triumphantly holding up the wrong book.\nscene [ea591e] = 挤得书架互相倚靠的书店里，一位老书商站在梯子上，得意地举着一本错的书。\n# en: Buy what he found\nopt.left.label [8212ca] = 买下他找到的书\n# en: {It's useless and wonderful. You read half of it on the barge.|You learn more than you meant to about something you never meant to learn.|There's a pressed flower in it, and a name you don't know on the flyleaf.}\nopt.left.say.1 [012e04] = {它毫无用处，又妙不可言。你在驳船上就读了一半。|你学到了比预想更多的东西，关于一件你从没打算学的事。|书里夹着一朵压干的花，扉页上写着一个你不认识的名字。}\n# en: Search the shelves yourself\nopt.right.label [68f103] = 自己去书架上找\n# en: {You find it behind a stack of almanacs, exactly where he said it wasn't, and read the chapter you need at the counter.|You find it. Mr Grimsby looks wounded and lets you read it at the counter for free, since you found it yourself.|You find it yourself, above the weather guides. Mr Grimsby insists it's a weather guide too, in a way.}\nopt.right.say.1 [81b56d] = {你在一摞年鉴后面找到了它，正是他说不在的地方，就在柜台边读完了你要的那一章。|你找到了。格林斯比先生一脸受伤，因为是你自己找到的，就让你在柜台边免费读了。|你在天气指南上面一层自己找到了它。格林斯比先生坚持说，某种意义上它也算天气指南。}\n# en: {A shelf leans on you for support. You hold it up for a long time.|An afternoon, a sneezing fit, and no book.|You search and find only more guides to the moods of weather. The weather today is a sulk.}\nopt.right.say.2 [8c1e75] = {一个书架靠在你身上寻求支撑。你扶了它很久。|一个下午，一阵喷嚏，没有书。|你找了半天，只找到更多关于天气脾性的指南。今天的天气是在赌气。}\n\n@event grimsbys_damp_barrow\n# en: The Damp Barrow\ntitle [b887f1] = 受潮的书车\n# en: {Outside Grimsby's there's a barrow of secondhand books, a coin apiece, slightly damp.|The barrow has a new sign: \"NO READING WITHOUT BUYING\". Nobody obeys it.|The barrow. You know its smell now: rain, glue and other people's thoughts.} {season == \"winter\": Someone has thrown a sack over it against the sleet.|{The top book is about knots.|A cat is asleep on the poetry.|A card in the corner says \"NOT RUDE, JUST OLD\".}}\ntext [491f09] = {格林斯比书店门外有一车二手书，一枚硬币一本，略微受潮。|书车上多了块新牌子：“不买不许看。”没人理会。|那辆书车。你现在认得它的味道了：雨水、胶水，还有别人的思绪。} {season == \"winter\": 有人往上面盖了只麻袋挡雨雪。|{最上面那本讲的是打绳结。|一只猫睡在诗集上。|角落里一张卡片写着：“不是没礼貌，只是旧了。”}}\n# en: A wooden barrow of damp secondhand books outside a crooked bookshop, a student reading one in the drizzle.\nscene [908d11] = 歪斜的书店外，一辆木推车装满受潮的二手书，一个学生在细雨中读着其中一本。\n# en: Buy a damp book\nopt.left.label [428d93] = 买本受潮的书\n# en: {It dries out wavy and full of someone else's underlinings.|The margins are better than the book. Someone argued with every page.|The last page is a shopping list in faded ink. You find it oddly moving.}\nopt.left.say.1 [94105c] = {书晾干后皱巴巴的，满是别人画的线。|页边批注比正文还精彩。有人跟每一页都吵过架。|最后一页是一张褪色的购物清单。你莫名有些感动。}\n# en: Read it standing in the rain\nopt.right.label [e908fc] = 站在雨里读\n# en: {You read until your feet are cold and Mr Grimsby taps on the window.|You read a whole chapter in the drizzle and don't buy it. You feel like a thief and a scholar at once.|A previous owner has written \"Don't believe this bit\" in the margin. You don't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5daa57] = {你一直读到脚冻僵，格林斯比先生敲了敲窗户。|你在细雨里读完一整章，却没买。你觉得自己既像小偷又像学者。|一位前主人在页边写着“这段别信”。你没信。}\n\n@event grimsbys_last_copy\n# en: The Last Copy\ntitle [370a21] = 最后一本\n# en: There's one copy left of the book you need, and {friend} has {their} hand on it too. {You both laugh. Neither of you lets go.|This keeps happening to you at Grimsby's. You're starting to suspect the shop.|Mr Grimsby swears he ordered two. It never arrives as two.} {\"I saw it first,\" {they} say{s}.|\"We could share,\" {they} offer{s}, unconvincingly.|Mr Grimsby says there might be another in the cellar. There won't.}\ntext [8d6c4c] = 你要的那本书只剩一本了，而{friend}的手也搭在上面。{你们都笑了。谁也没松手。|在格林斯比书店，这种事总落到你头上。你开始怀疑是这家店在搞鬼。|格林斯比先生发誓他订了两本。到货时从来都只有一本。}{“我先看到的。”{they}说。|“我们可以合着看。”{they}提议，毫无说服力。|格林斯比先生说地窖里也许还有一本。不会有的。}\n# en: Two students in a cramped bookshop, both holding the same book, neither letting go.\nscene [ec8868] = 拥挤的书店里，两个学生握着同一本书，谁也不肯放手。\n# en: Let {them} have it\nopt.left.label [e68f07] = 让给{them}\n# en: {{friend} promises to lend it to you \"very soon\". You know what that means.|You let go. {friend} buys you a bun on the way back, which nearly makes up for it.|{friend} reads it on the barge and tells you all the best parts, in the wrong order.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f54922] = {{friend}答应“很快”就借给你。你知道那是什么意思。|你松了手。{friend}回去路上请你吃了个面包，差不多算扯平了。|{friend}在驳船上就读了，把最精彩的部分讲给你听，顺序全是乱的。}\n# en: Haggle for it\nopt.right.label [3e6001] = 讨价还价抢下它\n# en: {Mr Grimsby takes your side for reasons of his own. {friend} won't speak to you until the quay.|You win it fair and square, and {friend} says so in a voice that means the opposite.|You haggle, and win. Mr Grimsby promises {friend} the second copy he ordered. You both know it won't come.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5e588d] = {格林斯比先生出于自己的理由站在你这边。{friend}一直到码头都没理你。|你光明正大地赢了，{friend}也这么说，只是语气意思正相反。|你讨价还价，赢了。格林斯比先生答应把他订的第二本给{friend}。你们俩都知道那本不会来。}\n# en: {{friend} outbids you, and Mr Grimsby hands your crowns back with a sympathetic look.|You argue so long that a third-year buys it while you're both talking. Mr Grimsby gives your crowns back.|{friend} wins, and Mr Grimsby promises you the second copy. It never arrives as two.}\nopt.right.say.2 [05bcc7] = {{friend}出价比你高，格林斯比先生带着同情的眼神把你的克朗退了回来。|你们争了太久，一个三年级生趁你们说话时把书买走了。格林斯比先生把你的克朗退了回来。|{friend}赢了，格林斯比先生答应把第二本给你。它从来不会成双到货。}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_drawers\n# en: A Thousand Drawers\ntitle [e5d6dc] = 一千个抽屉\n# en: {Pell & Daughters has a thousand drawers, each labelled in a different hand.|You find a drawer you'd swear wasn't there last time.|You've read half the labels in the shop. The other half seem to be reading you.} Today it's {\"Moonwort. Do not hum near.\"|\"Dried courage, low quality.\"|\"Owl pellets (sorted).\"} {season == \"winter\": The shop smells of camphor and wet wool.|The shop smells of mint and something burnt.}\ntext [c1af29] = {佩尔父女药铺有一千个抽屉，每个标签的笔迹都不一样。|你找到一个抽屉，敢发誓上次还不在。|店里的标签你读了一半。另一半好像在读你。}今天是{“月草。附近勿哼歌。”|“晒干的勇气，品质较次。”|“猫头鹰食丸（已分拣）。”}{season == \"winter\": 店里一股樟脑和湿羊毛的味道。|店里一股薄荷和什么东西烧焦的味道。}\n# en: An apothecary wall of a thousand tiny labelled drawers, brass handles gleaming, a student reading the labels on tiptoe.\nscene [2f6345] = 药铺的一整面墙是上千个贴着标签的小抽屉，黄铜把手闪闪发亮，一个学生踮着脚读标签。\n# en: Buy a pinch of it\nopt.left.label [a72b3a] = 买一小撮\n# en: {One of the daughters weighs it on scales finer than an eyelash and tells you what it's really for.|It smells of the shop all the way back across the lake.|Old Pell counts the grains himself and warns you about three things you hadn't planned to do.}\nopt.left.say.1 [673e61] = {一个女儿用比睫毛还精细的秤给你称好，告诉你它真正的用途。|回去渡湖的一路上，它都散发着药铺的气味。|老佩尔亲自数了颗粒，还提醒你三件你本来没打算做的事。}\n# en: Just read the labels\nopt.right.label [82b51d] = 只看看标签\n# en: {Old Pell watches you over his spectacles the whole time, as if labels could be stolen.|You copy a dozen labels into your notebook. A daughter asks if you're planning to open a rival shop.|You learn a great deal. Old Pell sighs every time you touch a handle.}\nopt.right.say.1 [73de50] = {老佩尔一直从眼镜上方盯着你，好像标签也能被偷走。|你把十几个标签抄进本子。一个女儿问你是不是打算开家对手店。|你学到了很多。你每碰一下把手，老佩尔就叹一口气。}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_queue\n# en: Symptoms in the Queue\ntitle [ab0a8c] = 排队的病症\n# en: {The queue at Pell & Daughters notices your academy robes. A woman shows you her rash.|Word has got round the queue at Pell's that you're \"the young doctor\".|The queue parts for you as if you were a physician. You are not a physician.} {A farmer has a cough like a door hinge.|A boy has a frog stuck to his hand, and the frog seems content.|An old man wants something for \"the usual\", and won't say what the usual is.}\ntext [12e73c] = {佩尔药铺排队的人注意到了你的学院袍。一位妇人给你看她的疹子。|佩尔药铺的队伍里传开了，大家都叫你“小大夫”。|队伍为你让开一条路，好像你是医师。你不是医师。} {一个农夫咳得像吱呀作响的门轴。|一个男孩手上粘着一只青蛙，青蛙看起来挺满足。|一位老人要买治“老毛病”的药，死活不说是什么毛病。}\n# en: A queue of villagers at an apothecary counter, all turning to a student in academy robes.\nscene [87f566] = 药铺柜台前排着一队镇民，全都转向一个身穿学院袍的学生。\n# en: Offer some advice\nopt.left.label [55dc45] = 给点建议\n# en: {Your advice is sound. One of the daughters nods, which in this shop is a medal.|The farmer tries your remedy and comes back to thank you. Old Pell grumbles about lost trade.|You guess the old man's \"usual\" from the way he walks. Old Pell grunts, which is agreement.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fe74ee] = {你的建议很靠谱。一个女儿点了点头，在这家店里这就是勋章。|农夫试了你的方子，回来向你道谢。老佩尔抱怨生意被抢了。|你从老人走路的样子猜出了他的“老毛病”。老佩尔哼了一声，那是同意。}\n# en: {Old Pell corrects you in front of the whole queue, kindly, which is worse.|Your advice would have worked on a horse. A daughter says so.|You guess the old man's \"usual\" wrong, out loud. He tells the queue what it actually is. Everyone wishes he hadn't.}\nopt.left.say.2 [74e07a] = {老佩尔当着全队人的面纠正了你，语气很和善，这更糟。|你的建议拿去治马倒是管用。一个女儿这么说。|你大声猜错了老人的“老毛病”。他把真正的毛病告诉了整个队伍。大家都希望他没说。}\n# en: Study a drawer intently\nopt.right.label [37493b] = 专心研究抽屉\n# en: {You pretend to be fascinated by a drawer of buttons until the queue moves on.|You say \"Hm\" like Professor Thorne and leave quickly.|You become very interested in the floor. The woman with the rash tells her friend you were rude.}\nopt.right.say.1 [4ab101] = {你假装对一抽屉纽扣着了迷，直到队伍往前挪走。|你学索恩教授说了声“嗯。”，然后迅速离开。|你突然对地板产生了浓厚兴趣。长疹子的妇人跟朋友说你没礼貌。}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_tonic\n# en: The Daughters' Tonic\ntitle [7c5d2b] = 女儿们的补药\n# en: {One of Pell's daughters has a new tonic and needs someone to try it. Her father must not know.|The daughters have another tonic, and a notebook with your name at the top of a column.|The daughters see you and reach under the counter before you've said hello.} {This one is green and fizzes.|This one smells of pears and thunder.|This one is clear, which they say is the worrying kind.}\ntext [9724e2] = {佩尔的一个女儿调了一种新补药，需要有人试喝。绝不能让她父亲知道。|女儿们又调了一种补药，还有个本子，其中一栏最上面写着你的名字。|女儿们一看见你，没等你打招呼就把手伸到了柜台底下。} {这一瓶是绿色的，还冒泡。|这一瓶闻起来像梨和雷雨。|这一瓶是透明的，她们说这是最让人担心的那种。}\n# en: Two apothecary's daughters leaning over a counter, offering a student a small bottle of something that fizzes.\nscene [ecaffe] = 药铺的两个女儿趴在柜台上，递给学生一小瓶冒着泡的东西。\n# en: Drink it down\nopt.left.label [65d992] = 一口喝下\n# en: {You feel wonderful, and briefly taller. The daughters take notes.|Your cold vanishes. So, for an hour, does your sense of smell.|The clear one does nothing at all, then everything at once. You feel marvellous. The daughters look nervous.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c0f9ae] = {你感觉棒极了，还短暂地长高了一点。女儿们记着笔记。|你的感冒消失了。你的嗅觉也消失了一个钟头。|那瓶透明的先是毫无作用，然后一下子全来了。你感觉好极了。女儿们看上去很紧张。}\n# en: {You hiccup bubbles all the way back to the barge.|Your ears go warm and stay warm. The daughters take very interested notes.|The clear one turns your tongue blue. The daughters write \"clear: not clear\" in the notebook.}\nopt.left.say.2 [1be2f5] = {你一路打着冒泡的嗝回到驳船。|你的耳朵发热，而且一直热着。女儿们兴致勃勃地记着笔记。|那瓶透明的把你的舌头染成了蓝色。女儿们在本子上写下“透明：并不透明”。}\n# en: Buy a proper remedy\nopt.right.label [8493b6] = 买副正经药\n# en: {Old Pell sells you something brown and reliable. The daughters look disappointed in you.|You buy the dull, tested thing. It works, dully.|A daughter wraps it and murmurs that you'll be sorry you missed the green one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c1eb98] = {老佩尔卖给你一种褐色的、靠得住的药。女儿们对你很失望。|你买了那种无聊又经过检验的药。它管用，无聊地管用。|一个女儿一边包药一边嘀咕，说你错过那瓶绿的会后悔的。}\n\n@event bakery_bun_queue\n# en: The Bun Queue\ntitle [b02932] = 买面包的队伍\n# en: {There's a queue outside the bakery for the afternoon batch, and it smells like a promise.|The baker's son counts heads in the bun queue and knows you by your scarf.|The bun queue. You know everyone in it by their order.} {season == \"winter\": Breath steams in the cold.|{season == \"autumn\": Leaves skitter round everyone's ankles.|{The baker's son is singing off-key.|A goat has joined the queue and nobody has the heart to send it away.|The queue is swapping recipes it will never use.}}}\ntext [207118] = {面包房门外为下午那一炉排起了队，空气里的香味像一个承诺。|面包师的儿子数着排队的人头，认得你的围巾。|买面包的队伍。队里每个人你都能按他们点的东西认出来。} {season == \"winter\": 呼出的气在冷空气里冒着白雾。|{season == \"autumn\": 落叶在每个人的脚踝边打转。|{面包师的儿子在跑调地唱歌。|一只山羊也排进了队，谁都不忍心把它赶走。|队伍里的人在交换一些永远不会用的菜谱。}}}\n# en: A queue of villagers and students outside a little bakery at dawn, steam and the smell of cinnamon pouring out of the door.\nscene [09ed66] = 黎明时一家小面包房外排着镇民和学生，热气和肉桂香从门里涌出来。\n# en: Buy a bag for your corridor\nopt.left.label [fed2ca] = 给走廊的人买一袋\n# en: {You come back with a warm bag and are, for one evening, the most popular person in your house.|The buns are gone before you've taken your coat off. Someone leaves you a thank-you note on a napkin.|A prefect takes one \"to check it for contraband\". It passes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [49a9cb] = {你带回一袋热面包，整整一个晚上成了学院里最受欢迎的人。|你还没脱外套，面包就被抢光了。有人在餐巾上给你留了张感谢条。|一个级长拿走一个，说要“检查有没有违禁品”。检查通过。}\n# en: Take a broken bun from the free tray\nopt.right.label [355ae4] = 从免费盘里拿个碎面包\n# en: {The baker's son gives you one that came out lopsided. You eat it on the quay while it's still too hot. Worth every burnt finger.|A broken bun, free: butter, cinnamon and the lake in the afternoon. Nothing has ever been better.|The free tray has one left. You eat it slowly and think about nothing at all.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2aad9a] = {面包师的儿子给了你一个烤歪了的。你在码头上趁烫就吃了。烫了手指也值。|一个碎掉的面包，免费的：黄油、肉桂，还有午后的湖。再没有比这更好的了。|免费盘里还剩最后一个。你慢慢吃着，什么也不想。}\n\n@event bakery_oldest_baker\n# en: The Oldest Baker\ntitle [efae3a] = 最老的面包师\n# en: The oldest of the bakers sits by the ovens, too old to knead and too stubborn to leave. {She asks, as she asks every student, whether the post at the castle is reliable these days.|She asks about the castle post. Her daughter gives you a look that says: just answer.|\"Is the post reliable?\" she asks. You've learned the answer she likes.} {The ovens tick as they heat.|Flour hangs in the light.|The bell over the door rings, and she looks up, every time.}\ntext [a4c10c] = 面包师里最年长的那位坐在烤炉边，老得揉不动面，又倔得不肯走。{她问你——就像她问每个学生那样——城堡如今的邮递是否靠得住。|她问起城堡的邮递。她女儿给了你一个眼神：回答就是了。|“邮递靠得住吗？”她问。你已经学会了她爱听的答案。}{烤炉升温时发出轻轻的嗒嗒声。|面粉悬在光里。|门上的铃铛一响，她就抬头，每次都是。}\n# en: A very old woman in a flour-dusted apron sitting by warm bread ovens, looking up hopefully as the shop door opens.\nscene [3f911a] = 一位系着沾满面粉围裙的老妇人坐在温热的烤炉边，门一开，她便满怀期待地抬起头。\n# en: Sit with her a while\nopt.left.label [4b2f48] = 陪她坐一会儿\n# en: {She tells you about her grandmother's grandmother, who waited for a letter. She doesn't say how it ended.|She tells you the family has baked here for two hundred years. She makes it sound like a promise kept.|She falls asleep mid-story. Her daughter tucks a bun into your pocket and says thank you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bafc08] = {她讲起她祖母的祖母，那人一直在等一封信。她没说结局。|她说她家在这里烤了两百年面包。她说得像一个守住了的承诺。|她讲着讲着睡着了。她女儿往你口袋里塞了个面包，说了声谢谢。}\n# en: Help knead the dough\nopt.right.label [d60b9f] = 帮忙揉面\n# en: {The dough fights back. Your arms ache by the time the trays go in.|The baker shows you the turn of the wrist. You don't have it yet.|You knead until your shoulders burn. The first loaf out has your thumbprint on it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b8cff0] = {面团会反抗。等烤盘送进炉子时，你的胳膊已经酸了。|面包师教你手腕那一转。你还没学会。|你揉到肩膀发烫。出炉的第一个面包上留着你的拇指印。}\n\n@event bakery_cooling_rack\n# en: The Cooling Rack\ntitle [5733b6] = 晾面包的窗台\n# en: {Behind the bakery a window stands open, and on the sill a tray of buns is cooling, unwatched.|The cooling tray is back on the sill, nearer the edge this time. The bakers either trust everyone or are testing you.|The tray, the sill, the open window. It's practically an invitation.} {A crow is eyeing it too.|Two first-years are pretending not to look at it.|Steam curls off the tray into the cold air.}\ntext [10f14f] = {面包房后面有扇窗开着，窗台上晾着一盘面包，没人看着。|那盘面包又晾在窗台上了，这回离边缘更近。面包师们要么谁都信任，要么是在考验你。|托盘、窗台、开着的窗。简直就是邀请。} {一只乌鸦也盯着它。|两个一年级生正假装没在看它。|热气从托盘上袅袅升进冷空气里。}\n# en: A tray of steaming buns cooling on the sill of an open bakery window, a crow and a student both eyeing it.\nscene [22dd92] = 面包房敞开的窗台上晾着一盘冒热气的面包，一只乌鸦和一个学生都盯着它。\n# en: Lift one off the sill\nopt.left.label [9b35c2] = 从窗台顺一个\n# en: {You get away with it. It tastes slightly of guilt and mostly of cinnamon.|Clean away. The crow watches you with professional respect.|You lift one through the steam. Nobody sees except the crow, who takes the next.}\nopt.left.say.1 [38fb17] = {你得手了。吃起来有一点点内疚味，主要是肉桂味。|干净利落。乌鸦用同行的敬意看着你。|你隔着热气拿了一个。除了乌鸦没人看见，乌鸦拿走了下一个。}\n# en: {A floury hand appears from the window, palm up. You pay double.|Caught by the baker's son, who makes you pay double and tells everyone in the queue.|The steam gives you away. A floury hand takes your coins, twice over.}\nopt.left.say.2 [84e187] = {一只沾满面粉的手从窗里伸出来，掌心朝上。你付了双倍。|被面包师的儿子逮个正着，他让你付了双倍，还告诉了队伍里的每一个人。|热气出卖了你。一只沾满面粉的手收了你的钱，收了双份。}\n# en: A floury hand appears from the window, palm up. Your pockets are empty, so you spend an hour scrubbing trays instead.\nopt.left.say.3 [af2de5] = 一只沾满面粉的手从窗里伸出来，掌心朝上。你口袋空空，只好去刷了一个钟头的烤盘。\n# en: Ask for yesterday's\nopt.right.label [eb962f] = 讨点昨天的\n# en: {The baker gives you a bag of yesterday's for nothing. They're as hard as roof slates.|You get the broken ones free. You eat one and lose a little faith in bread.|\"For the student who asks,\" says the baker, and hands you a stale one with a wink.}\nopt.right.say.1 [694f85] = {面包师白送你一袋昨天的面包。硬得像屋顶的石板。|你免费拿到了碎掉的那些。吃了一个，你对面包的信仰动摇了一点。|“给肯开口问的学生。”面包师说着，眨眨眼递给你一个干硬的。}\n\n@event home_cottage_old_room\n# en: Your Old Room\ntitle [8a919e] = 你的旧房间\n# en: {Your old room is exactly as you left it, except smaller.|Someone has been storing apples in your old room.|Your old room has started to feel like a guest room, and you like it less for that.} {season == \"winter\": There's frost on the inside of the window, as always.|The window won't shut properly in the heat, as always.} Downstairs, the house is busy without you.\ntext [ad7a94] = {你的旧房间和你离开时一模一样，只是变小了。|有人在你的旧房间里存了苹果。|你的旧房间开始像一间客房了，你因此不那么喜欢它了。} {season == \"winter\": 窗户内侧结着霜，一如往常。|天热时窗户总关不严，一如往常。}楼下，一家人没等你就忙开了。\n# en: A small childhood bedroom under the eaves, a too-short bed, a window looking out over fields.\nscene [0981a6] = 屋檐下一间小小的儿时卧室，一张太短的床，窗外是田野。\n# en: Nap the afternoon away\nopt.left.label [f2dad7] = 睡掉一个下午\n# en: {You sleep like a stone. Downstairs, someone has done all your chores, loudly.|You wake to the smell of supper and a pointed silence.|You sleep through the afternoon. You feel human, and slightly ashamed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [00f0c9] = {你睡得像块石头。楼下有人把你的家务全干了，动静很大。|你在晚饭的香味和意味深长的沉默中醒来。|你睡了整整一下午。感觉活过来了，也有点惭愧。}\n# en: Go down and help\nopt.right.label [5819cf] = 下楼帮忙\n# en: {final_year: {You go down and realise there won't be many more afternoons like this.|You memorise the kitchen without meaning to.|You go down and help, and try to learn how every sound in the house goes, for later.}|{You help with the fire. Nobody says much. Nobody needs to.|The creaky stair announces you. Someone hands you a cup before you've asked.|You go down and are handed the job you always had. Your hands know it before you do.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [bb5985] = {final_year: {你下楼时忽然意识到，这样的下午不会再有很多了。|你不知不觉把厨房的样子记在了心里。|你下楼帮忙，努力记住屋里每一种声音，留着以后想。}|{你帮着生火。谁都没多说话。也不需要。|吱呀的楼梯报告了你的到来。你还没开口，就有人递给你一杯茶。|你下楼，被分到了你一直干的那份活。你的手比你先想起来。}}\n\n@event home_cottage_list\n# en: The List on the Table\ntitle [e8c27a] = 桌上的清单\n# en: {&Home has remembered that you know where everything is.|A day home, and already a list.|The list. Your name, underlined.} {has(origin_noble): {&The steward wants the ledgers checked.|The steward wants the tenants' letters answered.|The steward wants you to sit in on a dispute about a fence.}|{has(origin_merchant): {&The shop's stock needs counting.|The shop sign needs repainting.|The shop's ledger has a column nobody can explain.}|{has(origin_hedge): {&Your grandmother wants herbs hung to dry.|Your grandmother wants every jar labelled.|Your grandmother wants the bees told the news.}|{&The woodpile needs splitting.|The hen house door argues.|The well rope has frayed.}}}}\ntext [3e4b7b] = {&家里没忘，东西放在哪儿你最清楚。|才回家一天，就有了一张清单。|那张清单。你的名字，下面画着线。} {has(origin_noble): {&管家想让你核一核账本。|管家想让你给佃户们回信。|管家想让你旁听一桩关于篱笆的纠纷。}|{has(origin_merchant): {&店里的存货要清点。|店门口的招牌要重新上漆。|店里的账本上有一栏谁也说不清。}|{has(origin_hedge): {&你外婆要你把草药挂起来晾干。|你外婆要你给每个罐子都贴好标签。|你外婆要你去把近来的消息告诉蜜蜂。}|{&柴堆要劈。|鸡舍的门总在跟人较劲。|井绳磨毛了。}}}}\n# en: A kitchen table with a long handwritten list of chores weighted down by a teapot, a student in academy robes reading it with dismay.\nscene [a8ef94] = 厨房桌上压着茶壶的一张长长的家务清单，一个穿学院袍的学生沮丧地读着它。\n# en: Work through the whole list\nopt.left.label [6b16eb] = 把清单全干完\n# en: {&By supper the list is done, and someone you love says \"Well.\" It's the highest praise in the house.|It takes all day. You sleep better than you have all term.|You finish, and the list grows a second page in a different hand.}\nopt.left.say.1 [32257d] = {&到晚饭时清单全做完了，一位你爱的人说了声“嗯，行。”这是家里的最高赞誉。|干了一整天。你睡得比整个学期都好。|你干完了，清单上又多出了第二页，笔迹不一样。}\n# en: Do it with magic\nopt.right.label [155e10] = 用魔法干\n# en: {&The work does itself while you supervise from a chair. The family is impressed and slightly unnerved.|It works. Your mother says it's cheating, then asks if you can do the gutters.|It works so well that a neighbour asks to borrow you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [403d61] = {&活儿自己干完了，你坐在椅子上监工。家人既佩服又有点发毛。|成功了。你母亲说这是作弊，然后问你能不能把檐沟也弄了。|效果太好了，一个邻居问能不能把你借去用用。}\n# en: {&The spell goes slightly wrong. Something is now upside down that wasn't before.|Soot. Everywhere. Someone laughs until they cry, then hands you a broom.|The spell does the list twice, including the things that were already done.}\nopt.right.say.2 [cce0ce] = {&咒语出了点偏差。有样东西现在是倒着的，原来不是。|煤灰。到处都是。有人笑到流泪，然后递给你一把扫帚。|咒语把清单做了两遍，连已经做完的也重做了一遍。}\n\n@event home_cottage_after_supper\n# en: The Same Question\ntitle [2bfa76] = 同一个问题\n# en: After supper, when the dishes are done, someone always asks it. {\"So what will you do, after?\"|\"Still don't know what you'll do after?\" An aunt, this time.|By now you could ask it yourself, in any of their voices, and someone does.} {final_year: This time the answer is close enough to touch.|{The fire pops.|Nobody pretends not to be listening.|Someone pours more tea to give you time.}}\ntext [78cdad] = 晚饭后，碗碟洗完，总有人会问那个问题。{“那你以后打算做什么？”|“还不知道以后做什么吗？”这回是一位姑妈。|如今你自己都能用他们每个人的腔调问出这句话了——而总有人真的问了。}{final_year: 这一次，答案已经近在眼前。|{炉火噼啪一响。|没人假装不在听。|有人又倒了些茶，给你留点时间。}}\n# en: A family kitchen after supper by candlelight, dishes drying, everyone turned towards one student.\nscene [ff9628] = 晚饭后烛光下的家庭厨房，碗碟正在晾干，所有人都转向一个学生。\n# en: Tell them the truth\nopt.left.label [f3042a] = 说实话\n# en: {You say you don't know. It's quiet for a moment, then someone says that's allowed.|You tell them what you're afraid of. It sounds smaller said aloud at this table.|The truth makes your mother go quiet, and then hug you, which you weren't ready for.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f3962b] = {你说你不知道。安静了一会儿，然后有人说，这也没关系。|你把你害怕的事告诉了他们。在这张桌边说出来，它显得小了些。|实话让你母亲沉默了一下，然后抱住了你，你完全没准备好。}\n# en: Give the polished answer\nopt.right.label [fcba81] = 给个体面的答案\n# en: {You give the answer you'd give a professor. They're proud. It sits oddly in your chest all night.|You make it sound like a plan. Everyone relaxes except you.|\"Very sensible,\" says an aunt. You feel as if you've told her a story.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b251a7] = {你给出了对教授说的那种答案。他们很骄傲。整晚它都在你心里硌着。|你说得像个计划。所有人都松了口气，除了你。|“很稳妥。”一位姑妈说。你觉得自己像给她讲了个故事。}\n\n@event home_fields_long_rows\n# en: The Long Rows\ntitle [e5b4eb] = 长长的田垄\n# en: {You walk the long rows to the field's end, where you used to imagine towers.|You've seen the towers now. The horizon at the end of the rows still pulls.|The walk to the field's end is a habit now. Your feet know it by heart.} {season == \"winter\": The furrows crunch with frost.|The wheat is high and loud with insects.} {A hare bolts.|A crow follows you at a respectful distance.|Smoke rises from a chimney you can't see.}\ntext [15efc7] = {你沿着长长的田垄走到田地尽头，从前你就在那里想象高塔。|你如今见过那些塔了。田垄尽头的地平线依然在召唤。|走到田地尽头已成了习惯。你的脚比你的脑子更熟悉这条路。} {season == \"winter\": 垄沟结着霜，踩上去嘎吱作响。|麦子长得老高，虫鸣一片。} {一只野兔窜了出去。|一只乌鸦隔着得体的距离跟着你。|远处看不见的烟囱里升起一缕烟。}\n# en: A student walking alone between long furrowed rows towards a low wall at the edge of the fields, the horizon wide and empty.\nscene [edb3f6] = 一个学生独自走在长长的田垄间，走向田地边缘的矮墙，地平线辽阔而空旷。\n# en: Walk the whole boundary\nopt.left.label [259a1e] = 沿田界走一整圈\n# en: {You come back muddy to the knees and hungry as a first-year.|You walk until your legs ache and your head is finally quiet.|You sit on the low wall where you always sat, and the wind does the thinking.}\nopt.left.say.1 [622c11] = {你回来时泥到膝盖，饿得像个一年级生。|你一直走到腿酸，脑子终于安静了。|你坐在一直坐的那段矮墙上，让风替你思考。}\n# en: Practise where no one watches\nopt.right.label [b2a1d1] = 没人时练练法术\n# en: {You set a scarecrow's hat spinning. Nobody sees. It's glorious.|You practise until your hands tingle and the crows give you a wide berth.|You try the spell you keep failing at school. Out here, with nobody watching, it nearly works.}\nopt.right.say.1 [741772] = {你让稻草人的帽子转了起来。没人看见。太棒了。|你练到双手发麻，乌鸦都绕着你飞。|你试了那个在学校总失败的咒语。在这儿，没人看着，它差点就成了。}\n\n@event home_fields_neighbours\n# en: Neighbours' Work\ntitle [332e5b] = 邻居们的活计\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The neighbours are hauling wood before the next snow.|The neighbours are bringing in the hay before the weather turns.} {They wave you over without asking if you're busy.|This time somebody has saved you a pair of gloves.|They've stopped waving. They just hand you the gloves.} {The neighbour's boy wants to be turned into a frog.|A farmer calls you \"the scholar\", mostly kindly.|Someone has brought a jug of mostly cider.}\ntext [dd78c9] = {season == \"winter\": 邻居们赶在下一场雪前运柴。|邻居们赶在变天前收干草。} {他们招手叫你过去，也不问你忙不忙。|这回有人给你留了一副手套。|他们不再招手了，直接把手套递给你。} {邻居家的男孩想知道你能不能把他变成青蛙。|一个农夫叫你“读书人”，大体是善意的。|有人带来一壶基本算是苹果酒的东西。}\n# en: Neighbours working together at the edge of a field, one waving a student in academy robes over to help.\nscene [962599] = 邻居们在田边一起干活，其中一人招手叫一个穿学院袍的学生过来帮忙。\n# en: Lend a hand\nopt.left.label [d5e12c] = 搭把手\n# en: {Blisters by noon, and a supper invitation from three households.|You work till dusk. Nobody mentions the academy once, which is restful.|Your back reminds you that books are lighter than anything out here.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d60208] = {中午就起了水泡，还收到三户人家的晚饭邀请。|你一直干到黄昏。没人提起学院一个字，这让人很放松。|你的后背提醒你，书比这儿的任何东西都轻。}\n# en: Bring them all a drink\nopt.right.label [a87c41] = 给大家带点喝的\n# en: {You come back from the market with a basket and become the most popular person in the field.|They drink to the academy, then to you, then to the weather.|The neighbour's boy asks for a frog again. You give him a biscuit; it's a compromise.}\nopt.right.say.1 [19cd62] = {你从集市提回一篮子东西，成了田里最受欢迎的人。|他们为学院干杯，再为你干杯，再为天气干杯。|邻居家的男孩又要变青蛙。你给了他一块饼干，算是折中。}\n\n@event home_fields_pond\n# en: The Old Pond\ntitle [ed9a38] = 老池塘\n# en: {season == \"winter\": The pond at the bottom of the fields has frozen over, and the young farmhands are daring each other onto the ice.|The pond at the bottom of the fields is warm and green, and the young farmhands are daring each other off the willow.} {You used to be one of them.|This time they dare you too.|They've started daring you first.} {A dog barks at the whole business.|The blacksmith's apprentice is keeping score.|Your old initials are still carved in the willow.}\ntext [567520] = {season == \"winter\": 田地尽头的池塘结了冰，年轻的农工们正互相激将着上冰。|田地尽头的池塘温暖碧绿，年轻的农工们正互相激将着从柳树上跳下去。} {你从前也是他们中的一个。|这回他们也激你了。|他们现在先激你。} {一条狗冲着这一切汪汪叫。|铁匠的学徒在记分。|你小时候刻的名字缩写还在柳树上。}\n# en: A pond at the bottom of a field under an old willow, local youngsters daring each other at its edge.\nscene [6031f0] = 田边一棵老柳树下的池塘，镇上的年轻人在岸边互相激将。\n# en: Take the dare\nopt.left.label [6cb2fd] = 接受挑战\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {You skate across and back to roaring applause from three farmhands and a dog.|You slide the whole length on your back. It counts.|You skate past your old initials frozen into the willow's roots. The apprentice gives you full marks for nostalgia.}|{You drop off the willow into the green. It's colder than memory, and better.|A perfect splash. The scorekeeper gives it top marks.|You drop off the branch beside your old initials. The score is one you'll be proud of for a week.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [e24a97] = {season == \"winter\": {你滑过去又滑回来，赢得三个农工和一条狗的欢呼。|你仰面滑完了全程。这也算数。|你滑过冻在柳树根上的旧缩写。学徒为你的怀旧给了满分。}|{你从柳树上跳进碧水里。比记忆中更冷，也更好。|完美的水花。记分的人给了最高分。|你从刻着你旧缩写的那根枝上跳了下去。那个分数够你得意一星期。}}\n# en: {season == \"winter\": {You go through the ice up to your knees and limp home to a lecture and a fire.|The ice cracks under you and you scramble back, soaked and laughed at.|You stop to look at your initials, and the ice stops holding you.}|{You belly-flop. The sound carries across three fields. Everyone heard.|You slip off the willow early and land in the reeds, muddy to the ears.|You climb to your old initials and slip on them. The splash is not dignified.}}\nopt.left.say.2 [dbdae5] = {season == \"winter\": {你踩破冰面，水没到膝盖，一瘸一拐地回家，迎接你的是一顿数落和一炉火。|冰在你脚下裂开，你手脚并用爬回岸上，浑身湿透，被笑了个够。|你停下来看你的缩写，冰就不再托着你了。}|{你肚皮拍水。那声响传遍了三块田。所有人都听见了。|你还没站稳就从柳树上滑了下去，一头栽进芦苇丛，泥糊到耳朵。|你爬到你的旧缩写那儿，在上面滑了一跤。水花一点也不体面。}}\n# en: Hold the coats\nopt.right.label [aecc6f] = 帮大家看外套\n# en: {You hold the coats and referee the arguments. Someone has to be the grown-up now.|You keep score and are accused of bias by every side.|Holding the coats, you feel very old and quite content.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e71ba6] = {你看着外套，调解争吵。现在总得有人当大人了。|你负责记分，结果每一方都说你偏心。|抱着一堆外套，你觉得自己老了，也挺知足。}\n\n@event home_market_inn_crowd\n# en: Outside the Inn\ntitle [d68f78] = 客栈门外\n# en: {The old crowd is outside the inn, exactly where you left them. \"Look who it is,\" someone says. \"The wizard.\"|The old crowd have been rehearsing the wizard joke. It has a second part now.|The old crowd outside the inn. Fewer of them every break: somebody married, somebody moved away.} {season == \"winter\": They're stamping their feet against the cold.|They're sitting on the inn steps in the long light.}\ntext [9c5c3d] = {老伙计们聚在客栈门外，跟你离开时一样。“看看这是谁，”有人说，“大巫师回来啦。”|老伙计们排练过“巫师”那个笑话了。现在还多了第二段。|客栈门外的老伙计们。每个假期都少几个人：有人成了家，有人搬走了。} {season == \"winter\": 他们在寒风里跺着脚。|他们坐在客栈台阶上，沐着长长的日光。}\n# en: A knot of young locals outside a country inn, raising their cups as a student in academy robes walks up.\nscene [a67c59] = 乡间客栈外聚着一群年轻的本地人，看见一个穿学院袍的学生走来，纷纷举起杯子。\n# en: Buy a round\nopt.left.label [ac2c88] = 请大家喝一轮\n# en: {They drink your health and ask if you can make the beer stronger. You can't. You're asked again.|For an hour it's as if you never left.|Someone tells the story of the time you fell in the duck pond. It has grown since you left.}\nopt.left.say.1 [10bb4f] = {他们为你的健康干杯，问你能不能把啤酒变得更烈。你不能。他们又问了一遍。|有那么一个钟头，仿佛你从未离开。|有人讲起你掉进鸭塘的那件事。你走后，这故事又长了不少。}\n# en: Stay for the gossip only\nopt.right.label [b278e5] = 只听八卦\n# en: {You learn who's courting, who's feuding and whose goat ate whose washing. Somebody mutters that academy folk are tight with a coin.|You keep your crowns in your pocket and get very well informed.|They notice you didn't buy a round. They notice everything.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ed0ec0] = {你知道了谁在恋爱、谁在结仇、谁家的山羊吃了谁家晾的衣服。有人嘀咕学院的人真抠门。|你把克朗留在兜里，消息却灵通得很。|他们注意到你没请客。他们什么都注意得到。}\n\n@event home_market_charm_seller\n# en: The Charm Seller\ntitle [dc4b46] = 卖护身符的人\n# en: {A stall in the market is selling \"genuine Lumenhold charms, blessed in the seven towers\".|The charm seller has new charms and a bigger sign. The claims have grown to match.|The charm seller sees your robes and starts packing up, then thinks better of it.} {has(origin_merchant): You know that markup. You were raised on it.|{A grandmother is buying one for luck.|The charms are bent nails tied with ribbon.|One of them is, worryingly, glowing.}}\ntext [5cfb18] = {集市上有个摊子在卖“正宗流明堡护身符，七塔加持”。|卖护身符的人换了新货，招牌也更大了。说辞跟着一起膨胀。|卖护身符的人看见你的学院袍，开始收摊，又改了主意。} {has(origin_merchant): 你认得那种加价。你就是在这种加价里长大的。|{一位老奶奶正买一个求好运。|那些护身符是系着丝带的弯钉子。|其中一个居然在发光，叫人不安。}}\n# en: A market stall hung with ribbons and trinkets under a hand-painted sign about academy blessings, a grandmother reaching for her purse.\nscene [2e47e6] = 挂满丝带和小饰物的集市摊位，手绘招牌上吹嘘着学院加持，一位老奶奶正伸手去掏钱袋。\n# en: Call out the fakes\nopt.left.label [2cfc0c] = 揭穿假货\n# en: {The crowd turns on him. A grandmother gets her coin back and gives you a cake. The seller gives you a look.|He folds, and offers you a share of the trade. You decline. He seems genuinely surprised.|You point out the glowing one. The seller goes pale and hands it to you to take away, free. The crowd cheers you anyway.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cbbdbd] = {人群倒向了你这边。一位老奶奶拿回了钱，送你一块蛋糕。摊主瞪了你一眼。|他认输了，提出分你一份生意。你拒绝了。他似乎真心感到意外。|你指出那个发光的。摊主脸色发白，把它白送给你让你拿走。人群还是为你欢呼。}\n# en: {The crowd takes his side. \"Academy snob,\" someone says, buying two.|He out-talks you in front of everyone. You'd admire it if it were happening to someone else.|You call out the glowing one, and it glows brighter, as if insulted. The crowd decides it's genuine and buys the lot.}\nopt.left.say.2 [e5327e] = {人群站在他那边。“学院来的势利眼。”有人说着，买了两个。|他当着所有人的面把你说得哑口无言。要是发生在别人身上，你会佩服的。|你点出那个发光的，它亮得更厉害了，像是被冒犯了。人群认定它是真货，全买光了。}\n# en: Buy one for an aunt\nopt.right.label [7ab465] = 给姑妈买一个\n# en: {Your aunt will love it. It's a bent nail. She will love it anyway.|You buy one. It's useless, and the old woman beside you beams at you with approval.|You buy one that glows a little. You decide not to think about why.}\nopt.right.say.1 [56b1f8] = {你姑妈会喜欢的。那是根弯钉子。她照样会喜欢。|你买了一个。毫无用处，但旁边的老妇人对你赞许地笑了。|你买了一个微微发光的。你决定不去想它为什么发光。}\n\n@event home_market_post_office\n# en: Letters to the Castle\ntitle [b5b8cc] = 寄往城堡的信\n# en: {The town's post office is the size of a cupboard, and its clerk has never posted anything to a magic academy.|The clerk has started weighing your letters with ceremony.|The clerk reaches for the castle stamp before you speak.} {season == \"winter\": The window is fogged; it smells of sealing wax.|Flies wander in to read the notices.} {final_year: Soon you'll write to the castle from elsewhere.|You think about who at the castle you miss.}\ntext [69c4a9] = {镇上的邮局只有柜子那么大，邮务员从没往魔法学院寄过东西。|邮务员开始郑重其事地称你的信了，像举行仪式。|你还没开口，邮务员就去拿城堡邮戳了。} {season == \"winter\": 窗户上蒙着雾气，有股封蜡的味道。|苍蝇飞进来读告示。} {final_year: 很快，你就要从别处给城堡写信了。|你想着城堡里那些你想念的人。}\n# en: A post office the size of a cupboard, a clerk ceremoniously weighing a stack of letters addressed to a castle on a lake.\nscene [8ee65f] = 柜子大小的邮局里，邮务员郑重地称着一摞寄往湖上城堡的信。\n# en: Write to everyone\nopt.left.label [0c3aa2] = 给每个人写信\n# en: {You write until your hand cramps. The clerk sighs at the stack.|You draw the cottage for someone who has never seen one.|You start one letter with \"I miss\" and decide to leave it that way.}\nopt.left.say.1 [236fa8] = {你写到手抽筋。邮务员对着那一摞叹气。|你给一个从没见过乡下小屋的人画了一幅你家的小屋。|你一封信开头写了“我想念”，决定就这样留着。}\n# en: Write the essay you owe\nopt.right.label [4e919a] = 写欠下的论文\n# en: {You write the essay on the clerk's counter, between parcels. It's not bad.|You post an essay to a professor. The clerk asks if it'll get you into trouble. Probably.|You finish it and post it. It's the dullest thing the clerk has ever weighed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1080f8] = {你在邮局柜台上、包裹之间写完了论文。还不赖。|你把论文寄给一位教授。邮务员问这会不会给你惹麻烦。大概会。|你写完寄出。这是邮务员称过的最无聊的东西。}\n\n@event village_errand_parcel\n# en: The Parcel That Clinked\ntitle [e7dea6] = 叮当响的包裹\n# en: {npc} turns up at your door with one of the parcels you carried up the hill: the one that clinked. \"This one was for you,\" {they} say{s}. \"I needed someone to carry it without guessing.\" Inside is a jar of village honey and a note: For the porter.\ntext [995baa] = {npc}拿着一个你帮忙扛上山的包裹出现在你门口：就是那个叮当响的。“这个是给你的，”{they}说，“我需要一个不会乱猜的人来扛。”里面是一罐小镇的蜂蜜，附一张字条：给门房。\n# en: A student unwrapping a string-tied parcel on a dormitory bed, a small glass jar of honey and a note inside.\nscene [f44ded] = 一个学生在宿舍床上拆开一个系着绳子的包裹，里面是一小罐蜂蜜和一张字条。\n# en: Open the honey now and share it\nopt.left.label [5fc1c8] = 现在就打开一起吃\n# en: You eat half the jar with {npc} on the dormitory floor, with spoons borrowed from the kitchens, and talk until your reading is a lost cause.\nopt.left.say.1 [55abe3] = 你和{npc}坐在宿舍地板上，用从厨房借来的勺子吃掉了半罐，一直聊到你的阅读彻底没救。\n# en: Save it for exam month\nopt.right.label [484c1f] = 留到考试月\n# en: You put it on the shelf, labelled. In exam month, a spoonful at midnight gets you through two chapters. {npc} asks, later, if you liked it. You did.\nopt.right.say.1 [6b66a3] = 你把它放上架子，贴好标签。到了考试月，半夜一勺，撑你读完两章。后来{npc}问你喜不喜欢。你喜欢。\n\n@event village_errand_third_thing\n# en: The Third Thing, After All\ntitle [625bb2] = 第三样东西，终究还是买了\n# en: {npc} has gone back down to the village for the third thing, the one you talked {them} out of, and has come back with it. \"You were right,\" {they} say{s}. \"I bought it anyway. Now I need you to say it suits me.\"\ntext [e9d06f] = {npc}又下山去买了第三样东西，就是你劝{them}别买的那样，还带了回来。“你说得对，”{they}说，“可我还是买了。现在我需要你说它适合我。”\n# en: A village stall with a single extravagant hat on a stand, two students looking at it, one with a purse already open.\nscene [c42650] = 山下小镇的一个摊位上，架子上摆着一顶夸张的帽子，两个学生看着它，其中一个已经打开了钱袋。\n# en: Say it suits {them}\nopt.left.label [a89beb] = 说它很适合{them}\n# en: \"It suits you,\" you say. It doesn't, at all. {npc} wears it to breakfast every day for a week, beaming.\nopt.left.say.1 [57cf74] = “很适合你，”你说。其实一点也不适合。{npc}连续一周每天戴着它去吃早饭，笑容满面。\n# en: Tell {them} the truth, kindly\nopt.right.label [c2201f] = 委婉地说实话\n# en: You say it's a brave choice. {npc} hears what you mean, laughs and gives it to the porter, who wears it all winter with great dignity.\nopt.right.say.1 [bfd2a8] = 你说这是个大胆的选择。{npc}听出了你的意思，笑了，把帽子送给了门房，门房整个冬天都庄重地戴着它。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/village_more.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/village_more.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event gilded_newt_ballad\n# en: The Newt's Ballad\ntitle [13f4ec] = 蝾螈之歌\n# en: {An old regular is standing on a bench singing the ballad of the Newt, about the fire that never goes out. It has forty verses. He knows most of them.|The ballad again. Tonight it has forty-one verses; the new one is about a student who fell into the cellar.|The ballad has a verse about the castle now, and the regulars look at you when they reach it.} {The landlady pretends to hate it.|The whole room bangs time on the tables.|{season == \"winter\": Snow is coming down outside, and nobody is going anywhere.|The fire flares at the chorus, every time.}}\ntext [ff2612] = {一位老常客站在长凳上唱蝾螈酒馆的歌谣，唱那团永不熄灭的炉火。一共四十段。他记得大部分。|又是那首歌谣。今晚它有四十一段了；新的一段讲一个掉进地窖的学生。|歌谣里如今有一段唱城堡，唱到那里时常客们都看着你。} {老板娘假装讨厌这首歌。|满屋子人拍着桌子打拍子。|{season == \"winter\": 窗外正下着雪，谁也不打算走。|每到副歌，炉火就蹿高一下。}}\n# en: A crowded low-beamed tavern, an old regular standing on a bench by the fire with a tankard raised, singing, while the room bangs time on the tables.\nscene [c870d8] = 拥挤的矮梁酒馆里，一位老常客站在炉边长凳上举着酒杯高歌，满屋子人拍着桌子打拍子。\n# en: Stand up and add a verse\nopt.left.label [db3294] = 站起来添一段\n# en: {You make up a verse about the landlady's slate and the room roars. She pours you a cider on the house, scowling.|You rhyme \"Newt\" with \"absolute\" and the whole tavern sings it back. You'll hear it for weeks.|Your verse about the castle gets a chorus of its own. The old regular claps you on the back and says you'll go in the ballad.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c9e6ad] = {你即兴编了一段唱老板娘的记账石板，满屋哄堂。她板着脸请你喝了一杯免费的苹果酒。|你用“蝾螈”押上了“圆满”，整个酒馆跟着唱回来。你会听上好几个星期。|你那段唱城堡的歌词有了自己的副歌。老常客拍着你的背说，你会被写进歌谣里。}\n# en: {You stand up, open your mouth and forget every word in the language. The old regular finishes your verse for you, kindly, and badly.|Your rhyme for \"Newt\" is \"Newt\". The room is silent, then merciless.|You sing your verse to the wrong tune. Half the room follows you and half the right one, and it ends in a brawl of harmonies.}\nopt.left.say.2 [99e082] = {你站起来，张开嘴，把这门语言里的每个词都忘了。老常客好心地替你唱完了那段，唱得很糟。|你给“蝾螈”押的韵是“蝾螈”。满屋先是一片寂静，然后毫不留情。|你用错了调子。半屋子人跟着你，半屋子跟着正调，最后成了一场和声斗殴。}\n# en: Buy the singer a drink\nopt.right.label [7d8d29] = 请歌手喝一杯\n# en: {You buy the old regular a cider, and he tells you the verses that aren't allowed in the ballad any more. They're the best ones.|You buy him a drink between verses. He toasts you in verse forty-two, which is new.|You buy the singer his usual. He tells you which verses are true. It's fewer than you'd hoped.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d7359f] = {你请老常客喝了杯苹果酒，他告诉你那些如今不许唱的段落。那几段最好听。|你在两段之间请他喝了一杯。他在第四十二段里向你敬酒，这段是新的。|你请歌手喝了他常喝的那种。他告诉你哪些段落是真事。比你希望的少。}\n\n@event mabels_fourth_cake\n# en: The Fourth Cake\ntitle [8c74a8] = 第四种蛋糕\n# en: {Mabel's has three kinds of cake. Today, on a plate beside them, there is a fourth. \"Experimental,\" says Mabel. \"Free, if you're brave, petal.\"|Another fourth cake. Mabel has started a little card for each one: name, date, survivors.|The fourth cake. Mabel slides it across before you've asked, in case today is the day you're brave.} {This one is blue.|This one is suspiciously plain.|{season == \"winter\": It smells of cloves and something like snow.|It's still warm and very slightly humming.}}\ntext [e95740] = {梅布尔茶馆有三种蛋糕。今天，它们旁边的盘子里有第四种。“试验品，”梅布尔说，“胆子大就免费，小宝贝。”|又一个第四种蛋糕。梅布尔开始给每一个做小卡片了：名字、日期、幸存者。|第四种蛋糕。你还没开口梅布尔就推了过来，以防今天正是你胆大的日子。} {这块是蓝色的。|这块朴素得可疑。|{season == \"winter\": 它闻起来有丁香和某种像雪的味道。|它还温着，还在轻轻哼鸣。}}\n# en: A tea-shop counter with three familiar cakes under glass domes and a fourth, mysterious one on a plate beside them, a woman in an apron watching expectantly.\nscene [57baf8] = 茶馆柜台上，三种熟悉的蛋糕罩在玻璃罩下，旁边一只盘子里放着神秘的第四种，一位系围裙的女士满怀期待地看着。\n# en: Be brave and try it\nopt.left.label [42f3bf] = 大胆尝一口\n# en: {It tastes exactly like the first warm day of spring. Mabel writes \"a triumph\" on her card.|It tastes of your grandmother's kitchen, or a kitchen you wish had been hers. Mabel watches your face and nods, satisfied.|It tastes like good news. Mabel says this one's going on the menu, and names it after the face you made.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4a85e8] = {它尝起来恰似春天第一个暖和的日子。梅布尔在卡片上写下“大获成功”。|它尝起来像你祖母的厨房，或者你希望是她的那个厨房。梅布尔看着你的表情，满意地点点头。|它尝起来像好消息。梅布尔说这款要上菜单，还要用你当时的表情给它命名。}\n# en: {It tastes of a memory of being scolded. Mabel takes it back, apologising, and writes \"no\" on her card.|It tastes of nothing at all, so completely that it's alarming. Mabel looks crestfallen.|It tastes of rainy afternoons in a waiting room. You can't explain it and never want to again.}\nopt.left.say.2 [874caa] = {它尝起来像被训斥的记忆。梅布尔连声道歉地收回去，在卡片上写了个“不”。|它什么味道都没有，没有得如此彻底，令人心惊。梅布尔很是沮丧。|它尝起来像下雨天坐在候诊室的下午。你解释不了，也再也不想尝了。}\n# en: Stick with your usual\nopt.right.label [bbd386] = 还是要老样子\n# en: {You order your usual. Mabel sighs, and cuts it a little larger than usual anyway, as if to make up for your cowardice.|You stick with the lemon slice. Mabel feeds the experiment to a delighted first-year, who turns briefly blue.|\"Your usual, petal?\" Mabel already has it on a plate. The fourth cake waits for someone braver.}\nopt.right.say.1 [901b0f] = {你点了老样子。梅布尔叹口气，照样切得比平常大一点，像在弥补你的胆小。|你还是要柠檬切片。梅布尔把试验品喂给一个兴高采烈的一年级生，那孩子短暂地变蓝了。|“老样子，小宝贝？”梅布尔已经装好盘了。第四种蛋糕留给更勇敢的人。}\n\n@event mabels_regulars_table\n# en: The Regulars' Table\ntitle [3625ea] = 常客的圆桌\n# en: {The big round table at Mabel's belongs to the village regulars, four old friends, a box of dominoes and fifty years of arguments. They wave you over. \"Settle something for us.\"|The regulars wave you over again. Today's dispute is about the academy in their day, when, they say, the stairs moved twice as often.|The regulars have saved you a chair. They've started calling you \"the referee\".} {The dominoes are clicking.|One of them was a student at the castle sixty years ago, and won't let anyone forget it.|{season == \"winter\": The window behind them is fogged from the teapots.|Mabel brings them more tea without being asked.}}\ntext [81592e] = {梅布尔茶馆那张大圆桌属于镇上的常客：四位老朋友、一盒多米诺骨牌和五十年的争吵。他们招手叫你过去。“给我们评评理。”|常客们又招手叫你了。今天争的是他们那时候的学院，他们说，那时楼梯挪动得比现在勤一倍。|常客们给你留了把椅子。他们开始叫你“裁判”了。} {多米诺骨牌哗啦作响。|其中一位六十年前在城堡念过书，谁都别想让他忘了提。|{season == \"winter\": 他们身后的窗户被茶壶熏得起了雾。|梅布尔不等吩咐就给他们添茶。}}\n# en: A big round table in a tea shop crowded with elderly villagers playing dominoes and arguing, one of them waving a student over.\nscene [f38683] = 茶馆里一张大圆桌旁挤满了玩多米诺骨牌、争吵不休的老镇民，其中一位正招手叫一个学生过去。\n# en: Join the domino game\nopt.left.label [c5541c] = 加入骨牌局\n# en: {You keep count of every tile played and win a whole crown off a retired miller who swears he let you. He didn't.|You win, and the old student declares that the academy has gone soft and been lucky. You'll take it.|You win, and the regulars demand a rematch next time. \"The referee's been practising.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [a03087] = {你记下了打出的每一张骨牌，从一位退休磨坊主那儿赢了整整一个克朗，他发誓是让着你。他没有。|你赢了，那位老校友宣布学院如今变软了，你是运气好。你照单全收。|你赢了，常客们要求下次再战。“裁判练过了。”}\n# en: {The retired miller cleans you out with a smile like a closing door.|You lose to a woman of ninety who plays with her eyes shut.|You lose, and the regulars toast your generosity. Mabel brings you a consolation biscuit.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f660fb] = {退休磨坊主带着关门似的笑容把你赢了个精光。|你输给了一位闭着眼打牌的九十岁老太太。|你输了，常客们举杯感谢你的慷慨。梅布尔给你端来一块安慰饼干。}\n# en: Listen to their stories\nopt.right.label [3d7422] = 听他们讲故事\n# en: {You hear about the year a professor turned the refectory into a pond by accident, and the year someone taught the stairs to waltz. Half of it might be true.|The old student describes the castle as it was: the same stairs, different shoes. It's strange to hear your life told back sixty years early.|You referee three arguments and hear four stories. Your tea goes cold. It's worth it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b5857d] = {你听说有一年一位教授不小心把膳堂变成了池塘，还有一年有人教会了楼梯跳华尔兹。大概一半是真的。|老校友描述城堡当年的样子：同样的楼梯，不同的鞋。听你的生活提前六十年被讲回来，感觉很奇妙。|你裁决了三场争论，听了四个故事。你的茶凉了。值得。}\n\n@event grimsbys_by_the_nose\n# en: Chosen by the Nose\ntitle [9eeed6] = 用鼻子挑书\n# en: {Mr Grimsby holds three books under your nose, one after another, eyes closed. \"Don't look. Smell. The right book smells right.\"|Mr Grimsby greets you with a book already held out at nose height. \"Vanilla and rain,\" he says. \"Trust me.\"|Mr Grimsby hands you a book by its smell before you've closed the door. He hasn't asked what you came in for. He never does.} {One smells of pipe smoke and the sea.|One smells of cut grass and arguments.|{season == \"winter\": One smells of woodsmoke, and the shop is freezing.|One smells, faintly, of you.}}\ntext [8407f2] = {格林斯比先生闭着眼，把三本书依次凑到你鼻子底下。“别看。闻。对的书闻起来就对。”|格林斯比先生迎接你时，手里已经举着一本书，高度正好在鼻子前。“香草和雨，”他说，“相信我。”|你还没关上门，格林斯比先生就凭气味递给你一本书。他没问你来买什么。他从来不问。} {有一本闻起来是烟斗和海。|有一本闻起来是青草和争吵。|{season == \"winter\": 有一本闻起来是柴烟，而店里冷得要命。|有一本隐约闻起来像你。}}\n# en: An elderly bookseller holding closed books under a student's nose one at a time, eyes shut in rapture, shelves leaning overhead.\nscene [60b8fc] = 一位年迈的书商把合着的书一本本凑到学生鼻子下，闭着眼一脸陶醉，头顶的书架东倒西歪。\n# en: Buy the one that smells right\nopt.left.label [0dd259] = 买闻起来对的那本\n# en: {You buy the one that smells of the sea. It's a book of storms, beautifully written and of no use whatsoever. You love it.|Vanilla and rain is a slim book of poems about lighthouses. Mr Grimsby wraps it like a gift and takes your coin as an afterthought.|You buy the one that smells like you. It's a novel about someone your age on the night before they leave home. You read it on the barge, slowly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d90229] = {你买了闻起来像海的那本。是一本写风暴的书，文笔优美，毫无用处。你爱它。|“香草和雨”是一本写灯塔的薄诗集。格林斯比先生像包礼物一样包好，顺手才想起收你的钱。|你买了闻起来像你的那本。是一部小说，写一个和你同龄的人离家前的那个夜晚。你在驳船上慢慢读完了。}\n# en: Insist on the one you came for\nopt.right.label [3058bb] = 坚持要你来买的那本\n# en: {You describe the book four times. On the fifth, Mr Grimsby lights up and produces it from under a cat-sized stack. He seems a little disappointed in you.|You stand firm. Mr Grimsby sighs, sniffs the air and goes straight to the right shelf. He could do this all along.|You've learned to describe a book by its smell. Mr Grimsby finds yours in a minute, beaming at your progress.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b5eee2] = {你把那本书描述了四遍。第五遍时，格林斯比先生眼睛一亮，从一摞猫那么高的书底下把它抽了出来。他似乎对你有点失望。|你寸步不让。格林斯比先生叹口气，嗅了嗅空气，径直走到对的书架前。他一直都会。|你学会了用气味描述一本书。格林斯比先生一分钟就找到了，为你的进步笑逐颜开。}\n# en: {You ask for a textbook and leave, somehow, with nothing at all, after an hour's lecture on the smell of old maps.|Mr Grimsby goes into the shelves for your book and is gone so long you leave a note.|Mr Grimsby brings you a cookbook, with great confidence, and is so hurt when you decline it that you stay an hour to make up for it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [bbfa6b] = {你来买一本教科书，结果听了一小时关于旧地图气味的讲座，不知怎的空手而归。|格林斯比先生钻进书架去找你的书，一去不返，你只好留了张字条。|格林斯比先生信心满满地给你拿来一本菜谱，你婉拒时他伤心极了，你只好多待一小时来弥补。}\n\n@event grimsbys_upper_floor\n# en: The Upper Floor\ntitle [7f6c6e] = 楼上\n# en: {A ladder at the back of Grimsby's climbs through a hatch to an upper floor nobody mentions. \"The remainders,\" says Mr Grimsby vaguely. \"Mind the third rung. And the fourth.\"|The ladder to the upper floor. Someone has tied a ribbon round the third rung, as a warning or a memorial.|The upper floor. You know its geography now: poetry slumps against history, and history has given up.} {Dust swirls in the light from the hatch.|Somewhere up there, something slides and thumps.|{season == \"summer\": It's stifling under the roof.|Rain drums on the roof right above your head.}}\ntext [b75cd6] = {格林斯比书店后头有架梯子，穿过一个活板门通到一层没人提起的楼上。“清仓书，”格林斯比先生含糊地说，“小心第三级。还有第四级。”|通往楼上的梯子。有人在第三级上系了根丝带，不知是警告还是纪念。|楼上。你如今熟悉它的地形了：诗歌瘫靠在历史上，历史已经放弃了。} {活板门透进的光里尘埃飞旋。|楼上某处，有什么东西滑下来，咚的一声。|{season == \"summer\": 屋顶底下闷得透不过气。|雨点就在你头顶上方的屋顶上咚咚作响。}}\n# en: A rickety ladder rising through a hatch in a bookshop ceiling into a dim upper room where shelves lean at alarming angles.\nscene [cecdc6] = 书店天花板的活板门里伸出一架摇摇晃晃的梯子，通向一间昏暗的阁楼，书架倾斜得令人心惊。\n# en: Climb up and rummage\nopt.left.label [60f324] = 爬上去翻找\n# en: {You find a first edition priced at a coin, with a pressed love letter inside. Mr Grimsby buys the book back at twice the price without noticing; the letter you keep.|You dig out a box of old exam papers from the castle, decades old. Mr Grimsby gives them to you for nothing and then pays you to take the box away.|You find a book of spells nobody casts any more, wedged under a shelf to stop it falling. Mr Grimsby gives you a crown for straightening the shelf.}\nopt.left.say.1 [737c5d] = {你找到一本标价一枚硬币的初版书，里面夹着一封压平的情书。格林斯比先生没留意，用两倍价钱把书买了回去；情书你留下了。|你翻出一箱几十年前的城堡旧考卷。格林斯比先生白送给你，又付钱请你把箱子搬走。|你找到一本如今没人施的咒语书，被塞在书架底下防它倒。格林斯比先生给了你一个克朗，谢你把书架扶正。}\n# en: {The third rung breaks, as advertised. You land in a heap of atlases and see stars, and some constellations.|A shelf leans on you, gently, and then less gently. You climb down under a small avalanche of poetry.|The fourth rung, this time. Mr Grimsby looks up from his counter, says \"Ah,\" and goes back to his reading.}\nopt.left.say.2 [ff81d3] = {第三级断了，正如预告。你摔进一堆地图集里，眼冒金星，还看见了几个星座。|一排书架轻轻靠在你身上，然后不那么轻了。你在一场小小的诗歌雪崩中爬了下来。|这回是第四级。格林斯比先生从柜台后抬头，说了声“啊”，又接着看书。}\n# en: Browse the window display\nopt.right.label [56d386] = 浏览橱窗陈列\n# en: {The window display is arranged by colour, not subject. You find three books you didn't know you wanted, and want none of them enough to buy.|You browse the window, where the books have faded through thirty summers. It's a lovely, useless hour.|You browse the window and watch the village go by through the gaps between the spines.}\nopt.right.say.1 [16dc6b] = {橱窗按颜色而非主题陈列。你发现了三本你不知道自己想要的书，却没有一本想要到要买。|你浏览着被三十个夏天晒褪了色的橱窗。美好又无用的一小时。|你浏览橱窗，透过书脊间的缝隙看小镇人来人往。}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_back_door\n# en: Foragers at the Back Door\ntitle [7c4537] = 后门的采集人\n# en: {At Pell's back door a forager in muddy boots is waiting to sell the morning's haul from the Whisperwood. She catches your eye. \"Cheaper than the counter, love. No questions.\"|The forager at the back door again, basket heavier, prices lower. She remembers you.|The back-door forager nods at you like an old business partner. Today's basket has something in it that's still moving.} {The basket smells of moss and rain.|There's moonpetal in it, wrapped in a dock leaf.|{season == \"winter\": Her fingers are blue with cold.|Old Pell can be heard counting aloud inside.}}\ntext [329520] = {佩尔药铺的后门口，一个穿着泥靴的采集人等着卖掉早上从低语森林采来的货。她朝你使了个眼色。“比柜台便宜，亲爱的。不问来路。”|后门的采集人又来了，篮子更沉，价钱更低。她记得你。|后门的采集人像对老生意伙伴一样朝你点头。今天的篮子里有样东西还在动。} {篮子里有青苔和雨水的气味。|里面有月瓣花，用酸模叶包着。|{season == \"winter\": 她的手指冻得发青。|里头传来老佩尔大声数数的声音。}}\n# en: A muddy forager with a basket of wild roots and mushrooms lingering at the back door of an apothecary, beckoning to a student in academy robes.\nscene [3ebb85] = 一个满身泥泞的采集人提着一篮野根和蘑菇，在药铺后门徘徊，朝一个穿学院长袍的学生招手。\n# en: Buy straight from her basket\nopt.left.label [d7ed6c] = 直接从篮子里买\n# en: {You buy a bundle of roots for half the counter price and spend the evening sorting the useful from the merely muddy.|You buy the moonpetal, dock leaf and all. It's the real thing; you can tell by the way it doesn't wilt.|You buy the moving thing, which turns out to be a root that wriggles toward water. You learn a great deal keeping it alive.}\nopt.left.say.1 [025ffc] = {你用柜台一半的价钱买了一捆根，晚上花了一整晚把有用的和纯粹沾泥的分开。|你买下了月瓣花，连酸模叶一起。是真货；从它不肯蔫就看得出来。|你买下了那个会动的东西，原来是一条会朝水扭动的根。为了让它活下去，你学到了很多。}\n# en: Ask where she found it all\nopt.right.label [a40297] = 问她都在哪儿采的\n# en: {She tells you about a clearing west of the stream where the moss glows. You write it down and do not go there, yet.|She tells you which mushrooms lie about being safe. It's an education, and it's free.|She tells you, lowering her voice, about a stream that runs uphill on nights of the full moon. Then she laughs and says it's probably nothing.}\nopt.right.say.1 [98f86d] = {她告诉你溪水西边有片空地，那里的青苔会发光。你记下来，暂时没去。|她告诉你哪些蘑菇会谎称自己无毒。这是一堂课，还是免费的。|她压低声音，说有条小溪在满月夜往山上流。然后笑着说大概没什么。}\n\n@event pell_apothecary_humming_drawer\n# en: The Drawer That Hums\ntitle [4c27a9] = 会哼鸣的抽屉\n# en: {One of Pell's thousand drawers is humming, low and steady, like a kettle thinking about it. Its label has worn away.|A humming drawer again. Somebody has taped a fresh label to it: ASK FIRST.|The humming drawer has a new label in Old Pell's precise hand: NOT TODAY. It's a different drawer from last time.} {Both daughters are busy with a queue.|Old Pell is counting aloud in the back, loudly.|{season == \"winter\": The shop is cold, and the drawer is the only warm thing in it.|The bell over the door has just rung, and nobody has come in.}}\ntext [d45c34] = {佩尔家一千个抽屉里有一个在哼鸣，低沉而平稳，像一把正在考虑要不要烧开的水壶。它的标签已经磨没了。|又是一个会哼鸣的抽屉。有人在上面贴了张新标签：“先问”。|会哼鸣的抽屉上有了老佩尔一丝不苟的笔迹写的新标签：“今天不行”。不是上回那个抽屉了。} {两个女儿都在忙着应付排队的人。|老佩尔在后头大声数着数。|{season == \"winter\": 店里很冷，只有这个抽屉是暖的。|门上的铃刚响过，却没有人进来。}}\n# en: A wall of a thousand small labelled drawers in an apothecary, one of them faintly rattling and humming, a student's hand hovering near its brass knob.\nscene [5a1383] = 药铺里一整面墙的小标签抽屉，其中一个微微晃动、哼鸣着，一个学生的手悬在它的黄铜把手旁。\n# en: Open it, just a crack\nopt.left.label [9ad18d] = 拉开一条缝\n# en: {Inside is a nest of dried bee-balm, humming with old summer. The smell follows you all day, and you know it now by heart.|Inside, a single seed pod is vibrating like a plucked string. You close it gently and remember its name.|Inside is a bottle of something that sings when the cork is loose. You tighten the cork and the shop goes quiet with relief.}\nopt.left.say.1 [074cb0] = {里面是一窝干蜂香草，带着旧夏天嗡嗡作响。那股香味跟了你一整天，如今你已烂熟于心。|里面，一颗种荚像被拨动的弦一样震颤。你轻轻关上，记住了它的名字。|里面是一瓶软木塞一松就唱歌的东西。你拧紧瓶塞，整间铺子如释重负地安静下来。}\n# en: {It's sneezing powder, enchanted to share itself. You sneeze until the queue applauds.|A puff of sleep-dust comes out. You wake on the floor with a daughter fanning you and Old Pell counting your breaths, aloud.|It's itching-seed, and it's in your sleeves. A daughter hands you a jar of ointment and a look.}\nopt.left.say.2 [639943] = {是会自我分享的喷嚏粉。你打喷嚏打到排队的人鼓掌。|一股睡粉喷了出来。你醒来时躺在地上，一个女儿在给你扇风，老佩尔在大声数你的呼吸。|是痒痒籽，钻进了你的袖子。一个女儿递给你一罐药膏，外加一个眼神。}\n# en: Ask a daughter what it is\nopt.right.label [91adb4] = 问女儿里面是什么\n# en: {\"Bee-balm from a summer that didn't end,\" says the elder daughter, not looking up. \"Leave it.\" You look it up later. She's right, and it's fascinating.|The younger daughter tells you it's a seed pod that hums before rain, and has never been wrong. You check the sky. She's right.|The daughters argue over what's in it for ten minutes. You learn more from the argument than from either answer.}\nopt.right.say.1 [505b5f] = {“一个没结束的夏天的蜂香草，”大女儿头也不抬，“别碰。”后来你查了查。她说得对，而且很有意思。|小女儿告诉你那是一颗下雨前会哼鸣的种荚，从没错过。你看了看天。她说得对。|两个女儿为里面是什么争了十分钟。你从争论里学到的比从任何一个答案里都多。}\n\n@event home_cottage_neighbour_calls\n# en: A Neighbour Calls\ntitle [1292c6] = 邻居来访\n# en: {A neighbour is at the kitchen door with a stopped clock, a hopeful face and the air of someone who has been saving it for you since harvest.|Another neighbour, another hopeful face. This one has a hen that's laying eggs the wrong colour.|The neighbours have a list now, you suspect. Today, a pie that won't rise and won't explain why.} {Your family pretends not to listen.|Someone at the table says, \"Go on, then,\" with pride.|{season == \"winter\": There's snow on the neighbour's hat; they've walked a long way.|The neighbour has brought a basket of plums, as payment in advance.}}\ntext [86aba7] = {一位邻居站在厨房门口，拿着一座停了的钟，满脸期待，看样子从秋收起就留着等你回来。|又一位邻居，又一张期待的脸。这位的母鸡下的蛋颜色不对。|你怀疑邻居们如今有张清单了。今天：一个不肯发起来、也不肯解释为什么的馅饼。} {你的家人假装没在听。|桌边有人说：“去吧，露一手。”语气里透着骄傲。|{season == \"winter\": 邻居的帽子上落满了雪；他们走了很远的路。|邻居带来一篮李子，算是预付的报酬。}}\n# en: A neighbour in a muddy coat standing at a cottage kitchen door holding a stopped clock, while the family pretends not to listen from the table.\nscene [52625c] = 一位穿着沾泥外套的邻居站在小屋厨房门口，抱着一座停了的钟，家人在桌边假装没在听。\n# en: Have a go at it\nopt.left.label [f3be98] = 试试看\n# en: {You coax the clock's spring awake with a whisper. It chimes, a bit sheepishly. The neighbour tells the whole lane.|You charm the hen's eggs back to brown, more or less. The neighbour insists on giving you a dozen. They're mostly brown.|You find the trouble with the pie: it was sulking. A warm word and it rises. The neighbour's face rises with it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [acbe36] = {你低声一句，把钟的发条哄醒了。它有点不好意思地敲响了。邻居告诉了整条巷子。|你把母鸡的蛋施咒变回了棕色，大致上。邻居坚持送你一打。大部分是棕色的。|你找到了馅饼的毛病：它在闹脾气。一句暖心话，它就发了起来。邻居的脸也跟着亮了起来。}\n# en: {The clock starts running backwards, cheerfully. The neighbour leaves delighted, which is somehow worse, and tells the whole lane.|The eggs come out tartan. The neighbour is polite about it, and tells everyone.|The pie rises, and keeps rising, and leaves by the chimney. The whole lane will talk about it for years.}\nopt.left.say.2 [894b94] = {钟开始欢快地倒着走。邻居心满意足地走了，这不知怎的更糟，还告诉了整条巷子。|蛋变成了格子花纹。邻居客客气气的，然后告诉了所有人。|馅饼发了起来，一直发，最后从烟囱里出去了。整条巷子会念叨好些年。}\n# en: Say you're on holiday\nopt.right.label [ebe5f2] = 说自己在放假\n# en: {You smile and say magic is for term time. The neighbour goes away disappointed, and you have the afternoon to yourself by the fire.|You send them to the tinker, who will charge them, but kindly. You put your feet up.|You say no, gently. Your family exchange a look. You spend the afternoon asleep in your old chair, guilty and rested.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e2387c] = {你笑着说魔法是学期里的事。邻居失望地走了，你独自守着炉火过了一下午。|你把他们打发去找补锅匠，他会收钱，但心肠好。你把脚翘了起来。|你温和地拒绝了。家人交换了一个眼神。你在自己的旧椅子里睡了一下午，又内疚又歇足了。}\n\n@event home_market_schoolmistress\n# en: The Old Schoolmistress\ntitle [364f2d] = 老女教师\n# en: {In the market you're stopped by the old schoolmistress who taught half the town its letters. \"Come and tell my little ones about the academy,\" she says. \"They won't believe me.\"|The schoolmistress again, with her satchel of slates. \"The offer stands. They still don't believe me.\"|The schoolmistress waves from across the square. The children at her heels are wearing paper wizard hats, badly folded.} {She looks smaller than she used to.|She smells of chalk, as she always did.|{season == \"winter\": Her breath steams; she's been out in the cold looking for you.|A market dog is following her for the biscuits in her pocket.}}\ntext [58b194] = {你在集市上被那位教会半个镇子识字的老女教师拦住了。“来给我的小家伙们讲讲学院吧，”她说，“他们不信我。”|又是那位女教师，背着一袋石板。“邀请还算数。他们还是不信我。”|女教师在广场那头冲你挥手。跟在她身后的孩子们戴着折得歪歪扭扭的纸巫师帽。} {她看上去比从前矮小了。|她身上有粉笔味，一如既往。|{season == \"winter\": 她呵着白气；她一直在寒风里找你。|一条集市上的狗跟着她，惦记着她口袋里的饼干。}}\n# en: A small elderly woman with a satchel of slates stopping a student in academy robes in the middle of a busy market street.\nscene [adcda5] = 熙攘的集市街上，一位背着一袋石板的矮小老妇人拦住一个穿学院长袍的学生。\n# en: Visit the schoolroom\nopt.left.label [ddfa76] = 去教室看看\n# en: {You tell twenty children about the moving stairs and the floating candles. One asks if you've met a dragon. You lie a little. They swarm you like bees.|You show them a single small flame in your palm. Twenty faces light up. You're exhausted after an hour, the good way.|You answer questions until your voice gives out. One of them wants to go to the academy now, very badly. It's your fault, and you're glad.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e2151d] = {你给二十个孩子讲会动的楼梯和漂浮的蜡烛。有个孩子问你见没见过龙。你撒了个小谎。他们像蜜蜂一样把你围住。|你在掌心托起一小簇火苗。二十张脸亮了起来。一小时后你累坏了，是好的那种累。|你回答问题直到嗓子哑了。有个孩子现在非常、非常想去学院。都怪你，而你很高兴。}\n# en: Buy her tea and listen\nopt.right.label [1f66da] = 请她喝茶听她说\n# en: {You buy her a pot of tea and she tells you which of your old classmates married whom, and which ran off to sea. You'd forgotten you missed them.|She asks what you're studying and actually listens. Then she corrects your grammar, out of habit, and you let her.|You buy her tea and she tells you what you were like at seven. It's alarming how little you've changed.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a24874] = {你请她喝了一壶茶，她告诉你哪个老同学娶了谁、嫁了谁，哪个跑去出了海。你都忘了自己想念他们。|她问你在学什么，而且真的在听。然后她出于习惯纠正了你的语法，你由她去了。|你请她喝茶，她告诉你七岁时的你是什么样子。你变化之小令人吃惊。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/pools/world_more.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/pools/world_more.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event world_stairs_ate_a_shoe\n# en: The Stairs Ate a Shoe\ntitle [b34164] = 楼梯吃了一只鞋\n# en: {Halfway down the east stair the steps shift, and when they settle you are one shoe lighter. Everyone knows where the stairs put shoes, in a heap under the east wing.|The stairs have taken another shoe. You suspect they like the taste of yours.|Shoe gone again. You've come to think of the heap under the east wing as a kind of shoe library.} {You have somewhere to be.|It's raining, and the castle floors are cold.|{season == \"winter\": Your sock is soaked through in a minute.|The stairs grind smugly behind you.}}\ntext [94ce4b] = {东楼梯走到一半，台阶挪了一下，等它们停稳，你少了一只鞋。谁都知道楼梯把鞋放在哪儿：东翼底下的一大堆。|楼梯又吞了一只鞋。你怀疑它们就爱吃你的。|鞋又没了。你已经把东翼底下那堆当成某种鞋子图书馆了。} {你还有地方要去。|下着雨，城堡的地板冰凉。|{season == \"winter\": 一分钟你的袜子就湿透了。|楼梯在你身后得意地嘎吱作响。}}\n# en: A great pile of odd shoes heaped in a dusty alcove beneath a castle staircase, a student in one sock digging through it.\nscene [8aaa0e] = 城堡楼梯下一处积灰的凹室里堆着一大堆不成对的鞋，一个只穿着一只袜子的学生在里面翻找。\n# en: Go and dig through the heap\nopt.left.label [e27aa6] = 去鞋堆里翻\n# en: {You dig through three centuries of shoes and find yours under a buckled pair that must be older than the towers. You're filthy, late and oddly triumphant.|You find your shoe, and a slipper with a name sewn into it from fifty years ago. You leave the slipper on top, in case.|You know the heap's layers now, like a geologist. Your shoe is on top, almost politely. You stub your toe on the way out anyway.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bfb9eb] = {你翻遍三个世纪的鞋，在一双大概比塔楼还老的搭扣鞋底下找到了你的那只。你满身灰、迟到了，却莫名得意。|你找到了自己的鞋，还有一只五十年前缝着名字的拖鞋。你把拖鞋放在最上面，以防万一。|你如今像个地质学家一样熟悉鞋堆的分层。你的鞋就在最上面，几乎可以说彬彬有礼。出来时你还是踢到了脚趾。}\n# en: Hop on, one shoe down\nopt.right.label [e47056] = 单脚跳着走\n# en: {You hop to your next lecture and spend it with one foot in your bag. You take excellent notes, out of spite.|You go about your day in one shoe and one sock. People stare; you make the most of it by reading on the move.|You wear one shoe all day and read between lectures to keep your mind off the cold foot. A first-year asks if it's a fashion. You say yes.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d62ad1] = {你单脚跳去上下一节课，整节课都把一只脚塞在书包里。出于赌气，你的笔记记得好极了。|你穿着一只鞋一只袜子过了一天。人们盯着看；你趁机边走边读书。|你一整天都只穿一只鞋，课间读书，好让自己忘了那只冷脚。一个一年级生问这是不是时尚。你说是。}\n\n@event world_duchess_has_news\n# en: The Duchess Has News\ntitle [f8bb00] = 公爵夫人有消息\n# en: {The Duchess of Ambry, who refuses to stay in her own frame, has squeezed into a pastoral scene by your corridor and is beckoning to you with her fan.|The Duchess again, this time in a portrait of a stern admiral, who looks resigned. \"Darling. News.\"|The Duchess has taken to waiting for you in whatever frame is nearest your door. Today she's sharing a still life with a pear.} {A painted cow is chewing at her elbow.|Her wig is slightly askew from travel.|{season == \"winter\": She's wearing a painted fur borrowed from a neighbouring frame.|She's fanning herself vigorously.}}\ntext [d4ab17] = {不肯待在自己画框里的安布里公爵夫人挤进了你走廊边的一幅田园画，正用扇子朝你招手。|又是公爵夫人，这回在一幅严肃海军上将的肖像里，上将一脸认命。“亲爱的。有消息。”|公爵夫人如今总在离你门口最近的画框里等你。今天她和一只梨共享一幅静物画。} {一头画里的奶牛在她手肘边嚼着草。|她的假发因一路奔波有点歪。|{season == \"winter\": 她披着从隔壁画框借来的一件画中毛皮。|她使劲扇着扇子。}}\n# en: An ornate gilt frame in a castle corridor where a powdered portrait duchess has squeezed in beside a placid painted cow, leaning out to whisper.\nscene [54301c] = 城堡走廊里一只华丽的镀金画框中，一位扑了粉的肖像公爵夫人挤在一头安详的画中奶牛旁边，探出身来说悄悄话。\n# en: Listen to her gossip\nopt.left.label [f91911] = 听她讲八卦\n# en: {She tells you which professor has a secret allergy to cats and which prefect cries at the opera. You'll never look at either the same way.|She whispers who is courting whom on the third floor, in scandalous detail. You feel like a spy and enjoy it more than you should.|She hands you the castle's secrets like sweets. Some of them are about people you like. You take them anyway.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1013a0] = {她告诉你哪位教授暗地里对猫过敏，哪位级长听歌剧会哭。你再也没法用原来的眼光看他们俩了。|她低声讲三楼谁在追求谁，细节耸人听闻。你觉得自己像个间谍，而且乐在其中，有点过了头。|她像发糖一样把城堡的秘密递给你。有些是关于你喜欢的人的。你还是收下了。}\n# en: Offer her some news instead\nopt.right.label [1d00db] = 反过来给她点消息\n# en: {You tell her about your week, a little improved. By supper every portrait in the castle knows a much grander version, and so does half your house, not all of it flattering.|You give her a harmless story about yourself. It comes back to you three days later as a romance, a duel and a minor tragedy, and people keep asking which bit is true.|You feed her an anecdote; she gasps in all the right places. By morning it's a legend with you in it, not entirely flatteringly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9bb9b1] = {你把这周的事稍加润色讲给她听。到晚饭时城堡里每幅肖像都知道了一个宏大得多的版本，你们学院一半的人也知道了，并不全是好话。|你给了她一个关于自己的无害小故事。三天后它回到你耳边，成了一段罗曼史、一场决斗和一出小悲剧，人们不停地问你哪段是真的。|你喂给她一则轶事；她在每个该惊呼的地方惊呼。到第二天早上它已成了传说，你在里面，形象不怎么光彩。}\n\n@event world_laundry_day\n# en: Laundry Day\ntitle [d89180] = 洗衣日\n# en: {The castle laundry has charmed tubs that wash by themselves, mostly. Your robes are due.|Laundry day. Last month the tubs turned a whole corridor's robes a gentle pink, and nobody has let the tubs forget it.|The laundry. You know which tub shrinks things and which one sings while it spins.} {Someone's socks are trying to escape over the edge.|The steam is so thick you can barely see the lines.|{season == \"winter\": Everything on the lines is frozen stiff as boards.|The windows are open, and the shirts are trying to fly.}}\ntext [413f7d] = {城堡洗衣房有施了咒的洗衣桶，会自己洗，大致上。你的长袍该洗了。|洗衣日。上个月洗衣桶把整条走廊的长袍都洗成了淡粉色，至今没人让洗衣桶忘掉这事。|洗衣房。你知道哪个桶会缩水，哪个桶甩干时会唱歌。} {有人的袜子正想翻过桶沿逃走。|蒸汽浓得几乎看不见晾衣绳。|{season == \"winter\": 绳上的衣服全冻得硬邦邦像木板。|窗户开着，衬衫们正想飞走。}}\n# en: Enchanted wash-tubs churning by themselves in a steamy castle laundry, clothes flapping on lines overhead, a student holding up a robe that has turned an alarming colour.\nscene [e2a1d3] = 热气腾腾的城堡洗衣房里，施了咒的洗衣桶自己翻搅着，头顶晾衣绳上的衣服随风扑打，一个学生举着一件变了吓人颜色的长袍。\n# en: Charm the tub yourself\nopt.left.label [a03b19] = 自己给桶施咒\n# en: {You coax the tub into a perfect wash. Your robes come out clean, warm and smelling faintly of thunderstorms.|Your robes come out their own colour, which, after the pink month, feels like a victory parade.|You charm the singing tub, and it sings your favourite song while it spins. Everything comes out spotless.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1004fa] = {你哄洗衣桶洗得完美。长袍出来干净、温暖，隐约带着雷雨的气息。|你的长袍出来时是它原本的颜色，经历过那个粉色的月份，这感觉像一场凯旋游行。|你给会唱歌的桶施了咒，它甩干时唱着你最喜欢的歌。每件都洗得一尘不染。}\n# en: {Your robes come out three sizes smaller. You buy a cheap replacement from the village tailor.|Your robes come out pink, like everyone's last month. The tubs have a sense of humour. You pay for a dye to put it right.|The tub spins so hard your robes leave by the window. You find most of them. The rest cost you a crown at the tailor's.}\nopt.left.say.2 [96e530] = {你的长袍出来小了三号。你在镇上裁缝那儿买了件便宜的替换。|你的长袍出来成了粉色，就像上个月大家的那样。洗衣桶很有幽默感。你花钱买了染料补救。|洗衣桶甩得太猛，你的长袍从窗户飞了出去。大部分你找回来了。剩下的在裁缝那儿花了你一个克朗。}\n# en: Wash them by hand\nopt.right.label [9fc6da] = 用手洗\n# en: {You scrub everything at the sink till your knuckles are raw. Clean, at least, and still the right size.|You wash by hand, wring by hand, hang by hand. Your arms ache; your robes stay black.|You wash by hand again. It's slow and cold, and you've come to find it soothing, in a chapped sort of way.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d2d39e] = {你在水槽边把所有衣服搓到指节发红。至少干净了，尺码也没变。|手洗、手拧、手晾。你的胳膊酸了；你的长袍还是黑的。|你又用手洗了。又慢又冷，你却渐渐觉得它能让人平静，以一种皲裂的方式。}\n\n@event world_bridge_in_a_gale\n# en: The Bridge in a Gale\ntitle [2dae26] = 狂风中的桥\n# en: {The wind is up, and the bridge of light between the towers is swaying like a hammock. The long way round is ten minutes and four staircases.|Gale day. The bridges of light sway and ring faintly, like glass in a cupboard.|The bridge in the wind. You know its rhythm now: the sway, the pause, the shudder.} {Two first-years are halfway across, frozen.|A prefect stands at the end shouting safety rules nobody can hear.|{season == \"winter\": Sleet is coming sideways off the lake.|Leaves are blowing straight through the bridge, as if it isn't there.}}\ntext [4997b6] = {起风了，塔楼之间的光之桥像吊床一样摇晃。绕远路要十分钟，外加四段楼梯。|大风天。光之桥摇晃着，发出轻微的叮当声，像橱柜里的玻璃杯。|风中的桥。你如今熟悉它的节奏：摇、停、颤。} {两个一年级生走到一半，僵住了。|一位级长站在桥头大喊没人听得见的安全守则。|{season == \"winter\": 冻雨从湖面横着扫过来。|落叶径直穿过桥身，仿佛桥不存在。}}\n# en: A shimmering bridge of light slung between two castle towers, swaying in a howling wind, a student gripping the rail halfway across.\nscene [cafd26] = 一座闪烁的光之桥悬在两座城堡塔楼之间，在呼啸的大风中摇摆，一个学生走到桥中间紧握栏杆。\n# en: Cross it anyway\nopt.left.label [513eed] = 照样过桥\n# en: {You walk the swaying bridge like a sailor on a deck and step off the far side with your heart singing.|You cross in the pauses between gusts. The bridge hums under your feet like something alive.|You cross on the rhythm, sway, pause, shudder, step, and arrive before anyone else. The wind claps you on the back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [87d9ab] = {你像水手走甲板一样走过摇晃的桥，踏上对岸时心在歌唱。|你趁阵风间隙过桥。桥在你脚下嗡嗡作响，像个活物。|你踩着节奏过桥，摇、停、颤、迈步，比谁都先到。风拍了拍你的背。}\n# en: {A gust drops you to your knees halfway across. You crawl the rest, which nobody who saw will ever let you forget.|You freeze in the middle like the first-years and stand there for a long, windy minute. You make it, bruised and shaken.|You misjudge the shudder and the rail catches you in the ribs. You arrive, eventually, aching.}\nopt.left.say.2 [fa9547] = {走到一半，一阵狂风把你吹得跪倒。剩下的路你是爬过去的，看见的人永远不会让你忘了这事。|你像那两个一年级生一样僵在中间，在风里站了漫长的一分钟。你过去了，带着淤青，惊魂未定。|你没算准那一颤，栏杆撞上了你的肋骨。你最终到了，浑身酸痛。}\n# en: Take the long way round\nopt.right.label [d7b80a] = 绕远路\n# en: {You take the four staircases and read on the way. You arrive late and better informed.|You take the long way and find a corridor you'd never noticed, with a portrait that winks. You arrive late and curious.|The long way round has become a habit on windy days. You've read most of a book on those four staircases.}\nopt.right.say.1 [17d144] = {你走那四段楼梯，一路看书。你迟到了，懂得更多了。|你绕远路，发现了一条从没注意过的走廊，里面有幅会眨眼的肖像。你迟到了，充满好奇。|大风天绕远路已成了你的习惯。在那四段楼梯上，你几乎读完了一整本书。}\n\n@event world_common_room_kettle\n# en: The Common-Room Kettle\ntitle [550546] = 公共休息室的水壶\n# en: {Your house common room has one kettle, older than anyone in the room, and whoever makes the tea hears everything.|The common-room kettle is whistling. Somebody shouts \"Whoever's nearest!\" and it's you.|The kettle. You know its temper now: two taps on the lid and it behaves.} {The fire is roaring and people are in their dressing gowns.|Someone has a letter from home and is reading it aloud.|{season == \"winter\": Snow has drifted against the windows.|The windows are open to the warm night.}}\ntext [f442c1] = {你们学院的公共休息室只有一把水壶，比屋里任何人都老，谁沏茶谁就什么都听得见。|公共休息室的水壶在叫。有人喊“谁离得近谁去！”，是你。|那把水壶。你如今摸透了它的脾气：敲两下壶盖，它就老实了。} {炉火正旺，大家都穿着睡袍。|有人收到家信，正大声念出来。|{season == \"winter\": 雪堆到了窗前。|窗户朝温暖的夜敞着。}}\n# en: A crowded house common room late in the evening, students in dressing gowns round a fire, a battered kettle starting to whistle on the hob.\nscene [19ca1e] = 傍晚时分拥挤的学院公共休息室，穿睡袍的学生们围着炉火，一把破旧的水壶在炉上开始鸣叫。\n# en: Make tea for everyone\nopt.left.label [a0aa5b] = 给大家沏茶\n# en: {You make a pot for twelve with the last of your good leaves. In return you hear who's courting whom, who's failing what, and one secret you'll keep.|You make the tea from your own tin, and everyone moves up to make room for you by the fire. It's the warmest you've been all week.|You make it the way the room likes it now, with the good leaves you bought. Someone says \"Thank you\" exactly the way your family says it, and your throat goes tight.}\nopt.left.say.1 [362f9c] = {你用最后一点好茶叶沏了一壶十二人份的茶。作为回报，你听到了谁在追谁、谁哪门课要挂，还有一个你会守住的秘密。|你用自己罐里的茶叶沏好茶，大家挪了挪，给你在炉边让出位置。这是你一周来最暖和的时候。|你如今照着这屋人喜欢的方式沏，用的是你买的好茶叶。有人说“谢谢”的语气和你家里人一模一样，你的喉咙一紧。}\n# en: Take your cup up to bed\nopt.right.label [e9fbac] = 端着杯子上床\n# en: {You take your tea up to bed and fall asleep before it's cold. The common room laughs below you half the night.|You slip away early with your cup. You miss the best story of the term, you hear in the morning.|You go up early, as you've started to. The noise from below is a nice thing to fall asleep to.}\nopt.right.say.1 [01a50c] = {你端着茶上床，茶还没凉就睡着了。公共休息室在你楼下笑了大半夜。|你早早端着杯子溜了。第二天早上你听说错过了本学期最精彩的故事。|你早早上楼，这已成了习惯。楼下的喧闹是很好的催眠曲。}\n\n@event world_the_mirror_asks\n# en: The Mirror Asks Again\ntitle [84bb1b] = 镜子又问了\n# en: {Once, on your first day, the Sorting Mirror asked who you were before you came. Today, as you cross the entrance hall, it ripples and asks the follow-up, in its voice like cold water. \"And now?\"|The Sorting Mirror asks its follow-up again: \"And now?\" It seems to expect a different answer each time.|The Mirror catches you mid-stride. \"And now?\" You've answered it so often that the question has started to sound like a greeting.} {year == 1: In it, you look younger than you feel.|{final_year: In it, you look like someone about to leave.|In it, your reflection waits for an answer too.}}\ntext [f13209] = {入学第一天，分院镜问过你来这里之前是谁。今天你穿过门厅时，它泛起涟漪，用冷水般的声音问出了下一个问题：“那么现在呢？”|分院镜又问了它的追问：“那么现在呢？”它似乎每次都期待一个不同的答案。|镜子在你迈步途中叫住了你。“那么现在呢？”你回答过太多次，这个问题听起来已经像一声问候。} {year == 1: 镜中的你看起来比你感觉的年轻。|{final_year: 镜中的你看起来像一个即将离开的人。|镜中的倒影也在等你回答。}}\n# en: A tall enchanted mirror in a stone entrance hall, its surface rippling like cold water, a student pausing before it with a bag on one shoulder.\nscene [729b6d] = 石砌门厅里一面高大的魔镜，镜面像冷水一样泛着涟漪，一个单肩背包的学生在镜前停下脚步。\n# en: Answer it honestly\nopt.left.label [76032c] = 诚实地回答\n# en: {You tell it plainly what's changed since your first day. The surface stills, and you walk on lighter, as if you'd set something down.|You give it a true answer, with one new thing in it that wasn't there on your first day. The Mirror seems to notice the new thing.|You answer without stopping, plainly, the way you'd answer a friend. It steadies you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6fe304] = {你朴实地告诉它，从入学第一天到现在有什么变了。镜面平静下来，你走开时轻松了，像放下了什么东西。|你给它一个真实的回答，里面有一件入学第一天还没有的新事。镜子似乎注意到了那件新事。|你脚步不停地回答，朴朴实实，就像回答一个朋友。这让你踏实。}\n# en: Tell it a pretty lie\nopt.right.label [1dd6a6] = 编个漂亮的谎\n# en: {You tell it you're secretly a prince in exile. The Mirror ripples, says nothing, and the silence follows you down the corridor.|You tell it something grander than the truth. Your reflection, you'd swear, rolls its eyes.|You lie to it, easily. It doesn't argue. It never argues. That's the worst part.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b63b81] = {你告诉它你其实是一位流亡的王子。镜子泛起涟漪，什么也没说，那份沉默一路跟着你走过走廊。|你告诉它一个比真相更宏大的版本。你敢发誓，你的倒影翻了个白眼。|你对它撒了谎，轻而易举。它不争辩。它从不争辩。这才是最糟的。}\n\n@event world_something_in_your_bag\n# en: A Stowaway in Your Satchel\ntitle [cfe073] = 书包里的偷渡客\n# en: {~A hedge-sprite has moved into your bag and is lining it with your notes.|A small cave newt, glowing faintly orange, is asleep in your inkwell.|A baby owl, still mostly fluff, has climbed into your hood and refuses to leave.} {~Unregistered creatures are tolerated, so long as nobody notices them.|It looks at you as though this was your idea.|It has clearly been there since breakfast.}\ntext [427902] = {~一只树篱精灵搬进了你的书包，正用你的笔记铺窝。|一只泛着淡橙光的小洞穴蝾螈在你的墨水瓶里睡着了。|一只还几乎全是绒毛的小猫头鹰爬进了你的兜帽，赖着不走。} {~未登记的生灵是被容忍的，只要没人注意到。|它看着你，好像这都是你的主意。|它显然从早饭时就在那儿了。}\n# en: A student opening a satchel to find a small magical creature curled up among the books, blinking up at them.\nscene [986d38] = 一个学生打开书包，发现一只小小的魔法生灵蜷在书本中间，正眨巴着眼睛抬头看。\n# en: Keep it quiet a while\nopt.left.label [8f339b] = 悄悄留它一阵\n# en: {~You feed it crumbs and hide it from the prefects all day. By evening it's built a nest out of your essay, and you don't mind.|You keep it warm in your pocket all day. Nobody notices; it glows when you laugh.|You carry it about in your hood all day. It hoots once in the middle of a lecture, and you cough to cover it, heroically.}\nopt.left.say.1 [46340c] = {~你喂它面包屑，一整天都把它藏着不让级长发现。到傍晚它用你的论文搭了个窝，你也不在意。|你一整天把它揣在口袋里暖着。没人发现；你一笑它就发光。|你一整天把它放在兜帽里带着。它在课上叫了一声，你英勇地咳嗽掩护。}\n# en: Take it to the stables\nopt.right.label [262d9f] = 送它去马厩\n# en: {~You walk it down to the stables. Professor Brask tells it, very kindly, where hedge-sprites belong, and tells you, less kindly, to check your bag.|Professor Brask takes the newt in her palm and tells you three things about cave newts you'll never forget. You miss it a bit on the walk back.|Professor Brask tucks the owlet into a nest box and tells you where its mother is likely to be. You find yourself checking your hood all week.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f955d3] = {~你把它送到马厩。布拉斯克教授非常温柔地告诉它树篱精灵该待在哪儿，又不那么温柔地叫你检查书包。|布拉斯克教授把蝾螈托在掌心，告诉你三件你永远忘不了的洞穴蝾螈知识。回来的路上你有点想它。|布拉斯克教授把小猫头鹰塞进一个巢箱，告诉你它妈妈大概在哪儿。你一整周都忍不住摸自己的兜帽。}\n\n@event world_study_group\n# en: The Study Group\ntitle [e635da] = 学习小组\n# en: {{friend} has a study group going in an empty classroom and a spare seat. \"We're doing the hard chapter. Bring your brain, and biscuits.\"|{friend}'s study group is still going, and still short of biscuits. The chalkboard is a battlefield.|{friend} waves you into the study group. Someone has started a list of who brings what; you're down for biscuits.} {Someone is drawing diagrams in the air with a glowing wand-tip.|Two of them are arguing about a footnote.|{season == \"winter\": It's freezing; everyone's in scarves.|The window's open, and moths are attending too.}}\ntext [22a3b8] = {{friend}在一间空教室办了个学习小组，还空着一个座位。“我们在啃最难的那章。带上脑子，还有饼干。”|{friend}的学习小组还在继续，饼干还是不够。黑板成了战场。|{friend}招手叫你进学习小组。有人列了张谁带什么的清单；你负责饼干。} {有人用发光的魔杖尖在空中画示意图。|其中两个人在为一条脚注争论。|{season == \"winter\": 冷得要命；人人都裹着围巾。|窗开着，飞蛾也来旁听了。}}\n# en: A huddle of students round a table piled with books and cold mugs, one drawing diagrams in the air with a glowing wand-tip, late at night.\nscene [af6bf6] = 深夜里一群学生挤在堆满书和冷掉马克杯的桌旁，其中一个用发光的魔杖尖在空中画示意图。\n# en: Join and work late\nopt.left.label [8e18df] = 加入，熬夜学\n# en: {You work through the hard chapter together until midnight. It makes sense in the end, mostly because {friend} drew it as a sandwich. You're shattered.|You argue the footnote to a draw and finish the chapter. {friend} walks you back across the bridges, yawning, long past midnight.|You bring the biscuits and an answer. {friend} says you're the group's secret weapon. You go to bed far too late.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dc2da6] = {你们一起啃那章啃到半夜。最后终于懂了，主要是因为{friend}把它画成了一个三明治。你累垮了。|你们把脚注争成了平局，也啃完了那章。早过了午夜，{friend}打着哈欠陪你走过光之桥回去。|你带来了饼干和一个答案。{friend}说你是小组的秘密武器。你睡得太晚了。}\n# en: Go to bed instead\nopt.right.label [fa0361] = 还是去睡觉\n# en: {You go to bed early. {friend} tells you at breakfast how good the group was, at length.|You turn in. Through the wall you hear the study group laughing until one, and sleep anyway.|You skip it. {friend} saves you a biscuit, which is somehow worse than a reproach.}\nopt.right.say.1 [b7680f] = {你早早睡了。早饭时{friend}跟你讲小组有多好，讲了很久。|你上床了。隔着墙你听见学习小组笑到一点，照样睡着了。|你没去。{friend}给你留了块饼干，这不知怎的比责备还难受。}\n\n@event world_weather_following_you\n# en: Weather Following You\ntitle [d1238f] = 跟着你的天气\n# en: {~Somebody's weather charm has escaped and attached itself to you. A small rain cloud is following you at head height, drizzling.|A runaway weather charm has found you. A tiny snow flurry is falling on you, and only you.|An escaped weather charm has chosen you. A single warm sunbeam follows you everywhere, even indoors.} {~People step politely out of your way.|A first-year is following you around to see what happens.|It seems pleased with itself.}\ntext [c93a52] = {~有人的天气咒跑出来，缠上了你。一小朵雨云在你头顶跟着你，淅淅沥沥。|一个失控的天气咒找上了你。一小阵雪只落在你身上。|一个逃逸的天气咒选中了你。一道温暖的阳光走到哪儿跟到哪儿，连室内也不例外。} {~人们客气地给你让路。|一个一年级生跟着你到处走，想看会发生什么。|它似乎很得意。}\n# en: A student walking down a castle corridor with a small personal weather charm hovering over their head while everyone else keeps clear.\nscene [2d43c6] = 一个学生走在城堡走廊上，头顶悬着一团只属于自己的小小天气咒，其他人都躲得远远的。\n# en: Try to dispel it\nopt.left.label [5c89d9] = 试着驱散它\n# en: {~You unpick the charm thread by thread and the cloud pops like a soap bubble, leaving a small rainbow.|You dispel it; the last snowflake lands on your nose like a goodbye.|You unpick the sunbeam's charm and it fades, regretfully. You feel the cold at once.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f01080] = {~你一丝一丝拆开咒语，雨云像肥皂泡一样破了，留下一道小彩虹。|你驱散了它；最后一片雪花落在你鼻尖上，像一声道别。|你拆开阳光的咒语，它依依不舍地淡去了。你立刻觉得冷了。}\n# en: {~Your spell makes it a thundercloud. It follows you for the rest of the day, rumbling at anyone who looks at you, and soaks you to the skin.|Your spell doubles it. You spend the day in a small private blizzard and catch a chill.|Your spell turns the sunbeam into a heatwave. You spend the afternoon sweating in a cold castle, red as a lobster.}\nopt.left.say.2 [3a6858] = {~你的咒语把它变成了雷雨云。它一整天跟着你，对每个看你的人轰隆作响，还把你浇了个透。|你的咒语让它翻了倍。你在一场私人小暴雪里过了一天，着了凉。|你的咒语把阳光变成了热浪。你在冰冷的城堡里汗流浃背地过了一下午，红得像只龙虾。}\n# en: Let it be, and enjoy it\nopt.right.label [63b153] = 随它去，享受吧\n# en: {~You go about your day in your own private drizzle. By evening it's a castle joke, and you're in on it.|You let it snow on you all day and catch flakes on your tongue in lectures. People talk about it.|You go about your day sunlit and warm. People keep finding reasons to stand next to you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [01d68a] = {~你顶着自己的私人细雨过了一天。到傍晚这成了城堡笑话，而你也乐在其中。|你让雪在身上下了一整天，课上还伸舌头接雪花。大家都在议论。|你阳光普照、暖洋洋地过了一天。人们总找借口站到你旁边。}\n\n@event world_lamplighters_round\n# en: The Lamplighter's Round\ntitle [8bc53b] = 点灯人的巡夜\n# en: {At dusk the old porter makes his round with a long brass pole, lighting the corridor lamps one by one. He's slow these days, and the west corridor is long.|The lamplighter again, at dusk. He nods at the spare pole leaning by the door, as if it had been left there for someone.|The lamplighter's round. The spare pole has a notch carved in it now, about where your hand goes.} {He hums an old song as he goes.|His knees click on every stair.|{season == \"winter\": It's been dark for an hour already, and every lamp matters.|The evening is long and gold, and he's in no hurry.}}\ntext [74dd97] = {黄昏时，老门房扛着一根长长的黄铜长杆巡游，把走廊的灯一盏盏点亮。他如今走得慢了，西走廊又很长。|又是黄昏的点灯人。他朝门边靠着的那根备用长杆点点头，好像是特意给谁留的。|点灯人的巡夜。备用长杆上如今刻了个凹口，正好在你手握的位置。} {他边走边哼一支老歌。|他每上一级台阶膝盖都咔哒一响。|{season == \"winter\": 天已经黑了一个钟头，每一盏灯都很要紧。|傍晚漫长而金黄，他一点也不着急。}}\n# en: An old castle porter with a long brass lighting pole lighting the corridor lamps one by one at dusk, a student walking beside him carrying a second pole.\nscene [89a471] = 黄昏时分，一位扛着黄铜长杆的老城堡门房把走廊的灯一盏盏点亮，一个学生扛着第二根长杆走在他身旁。\n# en: Light the west corridor for him\nopt.left.label [d64dc6] = 替他点亮西走廊\n# en: {You light them with a flick of fire, one after another, all the way to the end, and feel each one in your fingertips. The porter says the corridor has never looked so cheerful, and he's been doing it forty years.|You take the far end with the spare pole and a little flame. He takes the near end and tells you about the castle when he was a boy. You're drained, and lit up.|You light the west corridor in a single walk now, a flame at every lamp, and you're light-headed by the end. The porter lights his pipe off the last one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [569737] = {你弹指生火，一盏接一盏一直点到尽头，每一盏都在你指尖留下一丝灼意。门房说走廊从没这么喜气过，而他干这活四十年了。|你拿着备用长杆和一小簇火负责远端。他负责近端，给你讲他小时候的城堡。你被掏空了，也被点亮了。|如今你一趟就能点亮整条西走廊，每盏灯一簇火，走到头时你有些头晕。门房就着最后一盏点着了烟斗。}\n# en: Walk with him and listen\nopt.right.label [5ab4ae] = 陪他走，听他讲\n# en: {He tells you which lamps never stay lit and why, and which ghost likes the dark. You walk the whole round and forget you had anywhere to be.|He tells you about a headmistress, long ago, who lit every lamp in the castle with one snap of her fingers. \"Showing off,\" he says fondly.|He tells you a story for every lamp. You've heard some before. You don't mind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ae9f40] = {他告诉你哪些灯总也点不亮、为什么，哪个鬼魂喜欢黑暗。你陪他走完全程，忘了自己还有地方要去。|他讲起很久以前有位院长，打一个响指就点亮了城堡里所有的灯。“爱显摆。”他慈爱地说。|他每盏灯讲一个故事。有些你听过了。你不介意。}\n\n@event world_the_warm_flue\n# en: The Warm Flue\ntitle [8e17cb] = 暖烟道\n# en: {Behind the kitchens there's a window niche where the chimney flue runs warm through the stone. It's the best nap in the castle, and it's free.|The warm niche by the flue is free again. Someone has left a cushion, and a note: \"FIRST COME\".|The niche by the flue. You've learned when the kitchens fire the ovens, and when it's warmest.} {The corridor is quiet.|A cat is already asleep in one corner.|{season == \"winter\": Frost is on the window; the stone is toasty.|The window is open, and a breeze plays over it.}}\ntext [edf433] = {厨房后面有个窗龛，烟道从石墙里穿过，暖烘烘的。那是城堡里最好的午睡处，还不要钱。|烟道边的暖窗龛又空着。有人留了个坐垫，还有张字条：“先到先得”。|烟道边的窗龛。你摸清了厨房什么时候烧烤炉，什么时候最暖。} {走廊里很安静。|一只猫已经睡在一个角落里了。|{season == \"winter\": 窗上结着霜；石头却暖烘烘的。|窗开着，微风拂过。}}\n# en: A sunny stone window niche in a castle corridor beside a warm chimney flue, a student curled up asleep in it with a book over their face.\nscene [6f162f] = 城堡走廊里一处洒满阳光的石砌窗龛，紧挨着温暖的烟道，一个学生蜷在里面睡着了，脸上盖着一本书。\n# en: Curl up for an hour\nopt.left.label [71f6b4] = 蜷起来睡一小时\n# en: {You sleep like a loaf in an oven. You wake to find you've missed the reading you meant to do, and don't regret it as much as you should.|You nap on the cushion and wake an hour later, rosy and behind on everything.|You nap through the oven-firing, the warmest hour. Your notes stay shut; your soul is restored.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9331e4] = {你睡得像炉子里的面包。醒来发现错过了本打算读的书，却没有应有的那么后悔。|你枕着坐垫打了个盹，一小时后醒来，脸色红润，什么都落下了。|你一觉睡过了烧炉的时辰，最暖的那一小时。笔记一页没翻；灵魂得到了修复。}\n# en: Read in it instead\nopt.right.label [97e203] = 改在里面看书\n# en: {You read in the warm with your back to the flue and finish a whole chapter without once nodding off. Nearly.|You read with the cushion behind you and the warmth seeping in. It's the most comfortable studying you've ever done.|You read through the warmest hour and manage not to sleep. It's an act of will, and a pleasant one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7ffb2e] = {你背靠烟道在暖意里看书，读完一整章，一次也没打瞌睡。差一点。|你倚着坐垫看书，暖意慢慢渗进来。这是你做过最舒服的功课。|你熬过最暖的那一小时愣是没睡着。这需要意志力，也是一种享受。}\n\n@event world_margin_argument\n# en: The Argument in the Margins\ntitle [da21ee] = 页边的争论\n# en: {Your second-hand textbook came with an argument in the margins. Two former owners, decades apart, have been disagreeing about chapter four in ink.|You've reached chapter six, and the margin argument has followed. A third hand has joined in, in green, mostly to tell them both to calm down.|The margins again. You know the three hands now: the furious one, the patient one, the green one who draws small owls.} {Today it's about whether a spell can be \"polite\".|Today one of them has written \"SEE ME AFTER\" to the other, forty years late.|{final_year: You wonder who'll own this book after you.|Someone has pressed a flower at the page where they finally agree.}}\ntext [d88df4] = {你的二手课本附赠了一场页边争论。两位相隔几十年的前主人，一直在用墨水争论第四章。|你读到第六章了，页边的争论一路跟了过来。第三种笔迹加入进来，用绿墨水，主要是叫那两位都冷静点。|又是页边。你如今认得三种笔迹了：暴躁的那位、耐心的那位，还有画小猫头鹰的绿墨水那位。} {今天争的是一个咒语能不能算“有礼貌”。|今天其中一位给另一位写了句“下课来找我”，迟了四十年。|{final_year: 你不禁想，你之后这本书会归谁。|在他们终于达成一致的那一页，有人夹了一朵压花。}}\n# en: An open second-hand textbook covered in handwritten margin notes in three different inks, arrows and crossings-out running between them, a student's pencil hovering.\nscene [51688a] = 一本摊开的二手课本，页边写满了三种墨色的手写批注，箭头和涂改在其间穿梭，一个学生的铅笔悬在上方。\n# en: Add your own note to the argument\nopt.left.label [cce37c] = 在争论里添一笔\n# en: {You write a careful note siding with the patient one and citing the page. It feels like joining a very old, very slow conversation.|You add a fourth hand, in pencil, and settle the question of polite spells for good. You're sure someone will disagree in thirty years.|You write a small note to the green one, thanking them for the owls. The book feels more yours for it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6ef223] = {你写下一条仔细的批注，站在耐心的那位一边，还注明了页码。感觉像加入了一场非常古老、非常缓慢的对话。|你用铅笔添上第四种笔迹，一劳永逸地解决了礼貌咒语的问题。你确信三十年后会有人不同意。|你给绿墨水那位写了张小纸条，谢谢那些猫头鹰。这本书因此更像你的了。}\n# en: Ignore them and read the text\nopt.right.label [c6a5d0] = 不理他们，只读正文\n# en: {You cover the margins with your hand and read the actual chapter. It's duller than the argument, and you remember more of it.|You read the printed text and resolutely ignore three people shouting in ink. You lose the thread twice anyway.|You read straight through without once looking at the margins, which takes willpower. The chapter goes in; the fun stays out.}\nopt.right.say.1 [541b08] = {你用手挡住页边，读真正的章节。比争论无聊，你却记住得更多。|你读印刷的正文，坚决无视三个用墨水吵架的人。你还是走神了两次。|你一眼都没看页边，一口气读完，这需要意志力。章节读进去了；乐趣留在了外面。}\n\n@event world_door_that_wasnt_there\n# en: A Door That Wasn't There\ntitle [f3b1d3] = 昨天还没有的门\n# en: {~In a corridor you walk every day there is a door that wasn't there yesterday, small and arched, with a brass handle worn by hands that aren't yours.|A door has appeared in the bare wall by the stairs, painted a cheerful blue, with no keyhole.|There's a new door at the end of a corridor that used to just end. Behind it, something is ticking.} {~The castle does this. It always puts them back.|Nobody else seems to notice it.|It's slightly ajar.}\ntext [e5643c] = {~在你每天都走的一条走廊里，出现了一扇昨天还没有的门，小小的拱门，黄铜把手被不是你的手磨得发亮。|楼梯边光秃秃的墙上出现了一扇门，漆成欢快的蓝色，没有钥匙孔。|一条原本是死胡同的走廊尽头多了一扇新门。门后有东西在滴答作响。} {~城堡常干这种事。它总会把门收回去。|别人似乎都没注意到它。|它虚掩着。}\n# en: A small arched wooden door set into a castle corridor wall where there was only bare stone yesterday, its brass handle faintly warm.\nscene [9a4be7] = 城堡走廊的墙上嵌着一扇小小的木拱门，昨天那里还只有光秃秃的石头，它的黄铜把手微微温热。\n# en: Open it and look inside\nopt.left.label [e83222] = 打开往里看\n# en: {~Behind it is a tiny library of books about doors, all open at the same page. You read for an hour, and when you come out, it's gone.|Behind it is a cupboard full of preserved pears, labelled in a hand from two centuries ago. You take one. It's delicious, and the door is gone by supper.|Behind it is a room full of clocks, every one of them right. You set your watch by them and leave, and the door seals behind you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [73f726] = {~门后是一座小小的图书馆，全是关于门的书，都翻在同一页。你读了一小时，出来时它不见了。|门后是一个装满蜜饯梨的橱柜，标签是两个世纪前的笔迹。你拿了一个。很好吃，门到晚饭时就不见了。|门后是一间满是钟的屋子，每一座都准。你照着它们对好表离开，门在你身后封上了。}\n# en: {~Behind it is a spiral stair up to nowhere. Halfway, the steps get narrower than your foot, and you come down with a turned ankle and the door gone.|Behind it is a broom cupboard, and the brooms are not pleased to see you. You leave with bruises.|Behind it is a room full of clocks, all of them striking at once, right in your ears. You stagger out with a headache that lasts till bedtime.}\nopt.left.say.2 [68dd49] = {~门后是一道通往虚无的螺旋楼梯。走到一半，台阶窄得放不下你的脚，你崴了脚踝下来，门已经不见了。|门后是一个扫帚间，扫帚们见到你很不高兴。你带着一身淤青离开。|门后是一间满是钟的屋子，所有钟同时在你耳边敲响。你踉踉跄跄地出来，头疼到睡觉。}\n# en: Chalk a cross on it, walk on\nopt.right.label [d903e5] = 画个叉，继续走\n# en: {~You chalk a neat cross on it, like the old marks you've seen on other walls. By tomorrow the door is gone, but your cross is still there.|You mark it and keep walking. Some doors are invitations; some are tests. You don't feel like being tested today.|You leave your mark and a note of the date. You're keeping a map of where the doors appear. The castle, you suspect, is keeping one of you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0929e9] = {~你在门上用粉笔画了个整齐的叉，就像你在别的墙上见过的那些旧记号。第二天门不见了，你的叉还在。|你画好记号继续走。有些门是邀请；有些是考验。你今天不想被考验。|你留下记号和日期。你在绘制一张门出现位置的地图。你怀疑城堡也在绘制一张你的地图。}\n\n@event world_paper_bird\n# en: The Paper-Bird Jam\ntitle [daaef2] = 纸鸟大堵塞\n# en: {Somebody's routing charm has gone wrong, and every paper bird in your tower is turning in at your window instead of its own. There are eleven on the sill, and more coming.|The paper-bird jam again. Someone has pinned a sign to your door that says SORTING OFFICE.|The birds know your window now. They queue on the sill like regulars at a counter.} {One is pecking at the glass with real urgency.|Two of them are fighting over the latch.|{season == \"winter\": They're dusted with frost and shivering.|Rain has smudged half the names on their wings.}}\ntext [6e37b0] = {有人的寻路咒出了岔子，你们塔里每一只纸鸟都拐进了你的窗户，而不是它们自己的。窗台上已经有十一只，还有更多正在飞来。|纸鸟又堵车了。有人在你门上钉了块牌子，写着“分拣处”。|纸鸟们如今认得你的窗户了。它们在窗台上排着队，像柜台前的老主顾。} {有一只正十万火急地啄着玻璃。|有两只在为窗闩打架。|{season == \"winter\": 它们落满了霜，瑟瑟发抖。|雨水把一半翅膀上的名字洇开了。}}\n# en: Dozens of folded paper birds crowding and flapping against a dormitory window, a student trying to catch them in both hands.\nscene [3caa48] = 几十只折好的纸鸟挤在宿舍窗户上扑腾，一个学生正试图用双手把它们捧住。\n# en: Sort them and send them on\nopt.left.label [a8d569] = 分拣好再送走\n# en: {You read wings, untangle a knot of six and send every bird to its proper window. An hour gone, and your eyes ache from the handwriting.|You sort them by tower and floor on your bed and launch them in order. It's dull work, and every one of them arrives.|You sort the queue at speed now, like a postmaster. It's still an hour you meant to spend on something else.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a025fd] = {你辨认翅膀上的字，解开一团六只缠在一起的，把每只鸟都送往正确的窗口。一小时过去了，那些字迹看得你眼睛发酸。|你在床上按塔楼和楼层分好，依次放飞。活儿很枯燥，每一只都送到了。|如今你分拣得像个邮政局长一样快。可这依然是你本打算花在别处的一小时。}\n# en: Open the window and shoo them\nopt.right.label [501bea] = 开窗把它们轰走\n# en: {You fling the window wide and wave your arms. The birds scatter like startled pigeons, and half of them land at the wrong doors. By supper it's been traced to you.|You shoo them out in a great flapping cloud. It's glorious. Two notes end up in the headmistress's tower, and people know whose window they came from.|You open the window and let the flock go where it likes. Wrong doors all over the tower, and a reputation as the leak.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2d6a11] = {你把窗户大开，挥舞双臂。纸鸟像受惊的鸽子一样四散，一半落在了错误的门口。到晚饭时，源头查到了你头上。|你把它们轰成一大片扑腾的云。壮观极了。有两张字条落进了院长塔，大家都知道是从谁的窗户飞出来的。|你打开窗，任鸟群爱去哪去哪。整座塔到处是送错的门，你落了个“漏洞”的名声。}\n\n@event world_practice_next_door\n# en: The Practice Next Door\ntitle [3cc08e] = 隔壁的练习\n# en: {Someone in the room next door is learning a piece on a charmed fiddle. They play the same eight bars over and over, and the fiddle sighs at every wrong note.|The fiddle next door has got as far as the sixteenth bar. It still sighs, but less.|The piece next door is almost a piece now. You know it as well as the player does, wrong notes included.} {You were trying to read.|It's late, and the wall is thin.|{season == \"winter\": The radiator carries it into your room like a second instrument.|The windows are open, and half the corridor can hear it.}}\ntext [bf4ce1] = {隔壁房间有人在用一把施了咒的小提琴练一首曲子。他们翻来覆去拉同样的八小节，每拉错一个音，琴就叹一口气。|隔壁的小提琴已经拉到第十六小节了。还在叹气，但少了些。|隔壁那首曲子差不多像首曲子了。你和拉琴的人一样熟悉它，连错音都熟悉。} {你本来想看书的。|夜深了，墙很薄。|{season == \"winter\": 暖气片把琴声送进你屋里，像第二件乐器。|窗户开着，半条走廊都听得见。}}\n# en: A student at a desk with their head in their hands, a thin wall between them and an unseen musician, music drifting under the door.\nscene [458ac3] = 一个学生坐在桌前双手抱头，一堵薄墙另一侧是看不见的乐手，琴声从门缝下飘进来。\n# en: Knock and offer to help\nopt.left.label [e79c34] = 敲门去帮忙\n# en: {You knock. The player is a mortified first-year who has never had a lesson. You hum the eight bars properly until they have them, which is well past midnight.|You knock, and stay to count the beat for an hour. The sixteenth bar comes right for the first time; you both cheer, and the neighbours bang on the wall.|The door's already open when you knock. You tap out the tricky bit on the tabletop while they follow. Bed is very late.}\nopt.left.say.1 [18b2d5] = {你敲了门。拉琴的是个窘得要命、从没上过课的一年级生。你把那八小节正确地哼给 TA 听，直到 TA 学会，那时早过了午夜。|你敲门进去，留下来帮着打了一小时拍子。第十六小节头一回拉对了；你们俩一起欢呼，邻居们捶起了墙。|你敲门时门已经开着。你在桌面上用手指敲出难的那段，TA 跟着拉。睡觉时已经很晚了。}\n# en: Pillow over your head\nopt.right.label [4b2d41] = 用枕头捂住头\n# en: {You read with a pillow pressed to one ear. You get through the chapter; the eight bars get into your dreams.|You work through it while the fiddle sighs at the sixteenth bar every four minutes, regular as a clock. You finish your reading through gritted teeth.|You study to the sound of it, grudgingly. Annoyingly, it helps; you'll be humming it in the exam.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e42471] = {你把枕头压在一只耳朵上看书。这一章读完了；那八小节钻进了你的梦里。|你埋头苦读，小提琴每隔四分钟就在第十六小节叹一口气，准得像钟。你咬着牙读完了。|你不情不愿地伴着琴声学习。可气的是，这居然有用；考试时你会哼起它来。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/romance/kael_nessa.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/romance/kael_nessa.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event kael_warm_enough\n# en: Warm Enough\ntitle [5fcb64] = 够暖了\n# en: Kael sits down beside you on the cold bench outside the library. {~Frost creeps from his sleeves onto the stone.|Your tea steams; his breath doesn't.} He holds his bare hands near your mug, not quite touching it, the way you'd hold them near a fire you weren't sure you were allowed to use.\ntext [f2311f] = 凯尔在图书馆外冰冷的长凳上挨着你坐下。{~霜从他的袖口爬到石头上。|你的茶冒着热气，他呼出的气却没有。}他把光着的双手凑近你的杯子，没有真正碰到，就像人把手凑近一堆不确定自己能不能用的火。\n# en: Two students on a cold stone bench under a lamp, one with frost on his sleeves holding his bare hands near the other's mug of tea.\nscene [502b9d] = 灯下冰冷的石凳上坐着两个学生，一个袖子上结着霜，把光着的双手凑近另一个人的茶杯。\n# en: Wrap his hands round the mug\nopt.left.label [748f2b] = 让他的手捧住杯子\n# en: {~You fold his fingers round the mug, your hands over his. The tea goes cold in a minute. He doesn't let go. \"Warm enough,\" he says, as if it's a thing he's never said.|You give him the mug and keep your hands on his. Your fingers ache with cold. His, slowly, don't. \"Warm enough,\" he says, testing the words.}\nopt.left.say.1 [45625e] = {~你把他的手指拢在杯子上，你的手覆在他手上。一分钟后茶就凉了。他没有松手。“够暖了。”他说，好像这句话他从没说过。|你把杯子给他，手留在他手上。你的手指冻得发疼。他的手指，慢慢地，不疼了。“够暖了。”他说，试着念这几个字。}\n# en: Fetch him a second cup\nopt.right.label [1598b6] = 再给他端一杯\n# en: {~You bring him a cup of his own. It freezes at the rim. He drinks it anyway, solemnly, as if it were a ceremony.|You fetch him a cup from the kitchens. He holds it like something alive. \"Nobody brings me hot things,\" he says. \"They assume.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [d8e9b9] = {~你给他端来一杯他自己的。杯沿结了冰。他还是喝了，郑重其事，像在行一场仪式。|你从厨房给他端来一杯。他捧着它，像捧着一个活物。“从没人给我端热东西，”他说，“他们都以为。”}\n\n@event kael_frost_drawings\n# en: Frost Drawings\ntitle [f0db48] = 霜画\n# en: The window by your seat in the lecture tower is frosted over, in a lecture hall that's too warm. Looked at closely, the frost-ferns make a face. Yours. Kael, two rows back, has gone very red, which on him is a faint pink.\ntext [77868c] = 讲堂塔里你座位旁的窗子结了霜，可这间教室明明太热了。凑近细看，那些霜蕨拼成了一张脸。是你的脸。坐在后面两排的凯尔脸红透了——在他脸上，那只是一层淡淡的粉。\n# en: A tall classroom window covered in delicate frost-ferns that, looked at closely, make the shape of a face; a pale boy wiping at it with his sleeve.\nscene [76ebc1] = 一扇高大的教室窗户上覆满了精致的霜蕨，凑近看是一张脸的形状；一个苍白的男孩正用袖子擦它。\n# en: Breathe on the glass and draw one back\nopt.left.label [4b3de8] = 呵气在玻璃上回画一张\n# en: You breathe on the glass and draw, badly, a boy in a fur coat with a speech bubble that says \"...\". By the end of the lecture the frost has added a smile to your drawing. Neither of you mentions it afterwards.\nopt.left.say.1 [a9f754] = 你在玻璃上呵了口气，歪歪扭扭地画了个穿毛皮大衣的男孩，旁边一个对话框写着“……”。下课时，霜给你的画添了一个笑容。之后你们谁都没提起。\n# en: Let him wipe it off\nopt.right.label [6251d5] = 让他擦掉\n# en: He leans over and wipes it away with his sleeve, mortified. \"It does that,\" he says. \"When I'm not paying attention.\" He paid attention to the rest of the lecture very hard.\nopt.right.say.1 [20f64e] = 他探过身用袖子把它擦掉，窘得要命。“它会这样，”他说，“在我不留神的时候。”后半节课他非常非常留神。\n\n@event kael_until_the_thaw\n# en: Until the Thaw\ntitle [5afadc] = 等到解冻\n# en: At the foot of your staircase, Kael stops. \"In the North we don't say goodbye,\" he says. \"It's unlucky. You say 'until the thaw', and touch foreheads, so the cold knows you're coming back.\" He hasn't said it to anyone here. He's waiting to see if you'll laugh.\ntext [c237db] = 在你的楼梯口，凯尔停下脚步。“在北境我们不说再见，”他说，“不吉利。要说‘等到解冻’，再碰一下额头，好让寒冷知道你会回来。”在这里他还没对任何人说过。他在等着看你会不会笑。\n# en: Two students parting at the foot of a spiral staircase at night, one of them touching his forehead briefly to the other's.\nscene [8be6cc] = 夜里，两个学生在螺旋楼梯脚下道别，其中一个把额头轻轻抵在另一个人的额头上。\n# en: Touch foreheads\nopt.left.label [167ea9] = 碰一下额头\n# en: You bend your head to his. His forehead is cold as a windowpane. \"Until the thaw,\" you say. He stands there after you've gone up. You can hear him not moving.\nopt.left.say.1 [167df8] = 你低下头抵住他的额头。他的额头凉得像一块窗玻璃。“等到解冻。”你说。你上楼之后他还站在那里。你听得见他一动不动。\n# en: Say it back, carefully\nopt.right.label [0d9e4f] = 小心地回他一句\n# en: \"Until the thaw,\" you say, getting the vowels wrong. He corrects them, gently, three times, until you sound like you were born to it. He looks absurdly pleased.\nopt.right.say.1 [d638ba] = “等到解冻。”你说，元音念错了。他温和地纠正了三遍，直到你说得像土生土长的北境人。他看上去高兴得有点过分。\n\n@event kael_bakery_window\n# en: Wanting\ntitle [398169] = 想要\n# en: You find Kael standing outside the bakery window in the village, looking at the iced buns. He's been there, by the {season == \"winter\": snow on his shoulders|rain on his collar}, a while. \"I'm not hungry,\" he says, before you ask. He doesn't move.\ntext [4e9a5b] = 你发现凯尔站在镇上面包房的橱窗外，看着那些糖霜小圆面包。看他{season == \"winter\": 肩上的雪|领口的雨}，已经站了好一会儿了。“我不饿。”没等你问他就说。他没有挪步。\n# en: A pale boy standing outside a village bakery window in the cold, looking at a tray of iced buns, hands in his pockets.\nscene [108d53] = 寒风里，一个苍白的男孩双手插兜站在镇上面包房的橱窗外，看着一盘糖霜小圆面包。\n# en: Buy him one\nopt.left.label [4208f1] = 给他买一个\n# en: {~You buy two. He holds his like an unexploded charm. \"I wasn't going to,\" he says. \"We weren't — I wasn't supposed to want things.\" Then he eats it in four bites and looks at you as if you've done something dangerous.|You put a bun in his hands. He looks at it, and at you, and says nothing for a long time. Then: \"Thank you for not asking.\" He eats it slowly, as if someone might take it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d26438] = {~你买了两个。他捧着自己那个，像捧着一枚没爆的符咒。“我本来没打算……”他说，“我们不——我不该想要东西的。”然后他四口吃完，看着你，好像你做了件危险的事。|你把一个面包放进他手里。他看看面包，又看看你，很久没说话。然后：“谢谢你没问。”他吃得很慢，好像会有人来抢。}\n# en: Ask which one he'd pick\nopt.right.label [5ec6fe] = 问他会挑哪个\n# en: {~\"The one with the cherry,\" he says, at once, and then looks shocked at himself. You don't buy it. You just let him have said it, which seems to matter more.|He thinks about it seriously. \"None,\" he says. \"All of them.\" Then, quietly: \"Is that allowed?\" You tell him it is.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6f27a7] = {~“带樱桃的那个。”他立刻说，然后被自己吓了一跳。你没买。你只是让他把这句话说出口，这似乎更要紧。|他认真地想了想。“一个都不要，”他说，“全都要。”然后，很轻地：“可以吗？”你告诉他可以。}\n\n@event kael_lets_himself_want\n# en: One Thing a Month\ntitle [853bba] = 每月一件事\n# en: Kael has a practice now: once a month, he tells you one thing he wants, out loud. {~This month he's been working up to it for days.|He has it ready. He's written it down so he won't back out.} \"I want,\" he says, and stops, and starts again. \"I want to see the sea. With you. Not for a reason.\"\ntext [0dae8b] = 凯尔现在有个练习：每个月，他把一件自己想要的东西说出声告诉你。{~这个月他已经酝酿了好几天。|他准备好了。还写了下来，免得临阵退缩。}“我想要，”他说，停住，又重新开口，“我想去看海。和你一起。不为什么。”\n# en: A boy and his partner on the castle roof at dusk, him counting something on his cold fingers, very seriously.\nscene [c7d2ba] = 黄昏时的城堡屋顶上，一个男孩和他的恋人，他非常认真地掰着冰冷的手指数着什么。\n# en: \"Then we'll go.\"\nopt.left.label [1057eb] = “那我们就去。”\n# en: {~\"Just like that?\" he says. \"Just like that,\" you say. He laughs, out loud, and frost blooms on every slate of the roof at once.|He nods slowly, as if you've signed something. \"Next month,\" he says, \"I'll want something bigger.\" You find you're looking forward to it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a85ca0] = {~“就这样？”他说。“就这样。”你说。他笑出了声，屋顶上每一片石板一下子都开满了霜花。|他慢慢点头，好像你签了什么约。“下个月，”他说，“我要想要一个更大的。”你发现自己很期待。}\n# en: Tell him one of yours\nopt.right.label [f437a1] = 告诉他你想要的\n# en: {~You tell him something you want. He listens like it's a lesson, and then says, \"Yes. That too. I want that for you,\" and seems surprised that he can.|You trade wants with him until the stars come out. His are small and precise. Yours make him laugh. It's the best lesson either of you has had.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d8c9c9] = {~你告诉他一件你想要的东西。他听得像在上课，然后说：“好。这个也要。我想让你得到它。”他似乎惊讶于自己能这样想。|你们交换着各自想要的东西，直到星星出来。他的都很小，很精确。你的逗得他直笑。这是你们俩上过最好的一课。}\n\n@event kael_dont_protect_me\n# en: Don't Protect Me\ntitle [e960b8] = 别护着我\n# en: An older student says something sharp to you in the corridor, and the floor under his boots freezes solid. He goes down hard. Kael steps between you, frost in the air around him. \"Where I'm from,\" he says, very quietly, \"you guard what's yours.\"\ntext [6bd28d] = 一个高年级生在走廊里对你说了句刻薄话，他靴子下的地板立刻冻得结结实实。他重重摔倒。凯尔挡到你们中间，周身的空气都结了霜。“在我长大的地方，”他非常轻地说，“你得守住属于你的东西。”\n# en: A corridor floor glazed with ice around another student's boots, a pale boy standing between them and his partner, frost in the air.\nscene [f740d4] = 走廊地面在另一个学生的靴子周围结了一层亮冰，一个苍白的男孩站在那人和他的恋人之间，空气里飘着霜。\n# en: \"I'm not yours to guard.\"\nopt.left.label [e10093] = “我不归你守。”\n# en: The frost stops. He stares at you. \"I didn't — \" he says, and then, slowly, \"No. You're not.\" He helps the other boy up himself. Later he says, \"Tell me when I do it again,\" and you do, twice, and then he stops.\nopt.left.say.1 [519022] = 霜停了。他盯着你。“我不是——”他说，然后慢慢地：“对。你不归我守。”他亲手把那个男孩扶了起来。后来他说：“我再这样就告诉我。”你说了两次，之后他就不再这样了。\n# en: Let him, this once\nopt.right.label [20292d] = 这一次，由他去\n# en: The corridor clears around you both. People step aside for you for a week. Kael walks you to every lesson, and you start to understand that he doesn't know another way to say it.\nopt.right.say.1 [783589] = 你们俩周围的走廊空了。整整一周，人们都给你让路。凯尔每节课都陪你走过去，你渐渐明白，他不知道还能用别的什么方式来表达。\n\n@event kael_proud_of_you\n# en: Proud of You\ntitle [18f975] = 为你骄傲\n# en: Kael has done something hard, a practical he wasn't sure he'd pass, and passed it well. There's a letter from home on his bed. He hasn't opened it. \"It won't say it,\" he tells you. \"It never does. I don't know why I keep hoping.\"\ntext [052e37] = 凯尔做成了一件难事：一场他没把握能过的实践考，他考得很好。他床上放着一封家信，还没拆。“它不会说的，”他告诉你，“从来不说。我不知道自己为什么还抱着希望。”\n# en: A pale boy on the edge of a bed with an unopened letter in Northern script, his partner sitting down beside him.\nscene [90379f] = 一个苍白的男孩坐在床沿，手里一封北境字迹的信没有拆开，他的恋人在他身边坐下。\n# en: Say it for her\nopt.left.label [2b0ddd] = 替她说出来\n# en: \"I'm proud of you,\" you say. He looks at you the way he did his first night here, at a piece of bread nobody had to offer. Then he puts the letter in a drawer, unopened, and doesn't take it out again.\nopt.left.say.1 [6a4d6a] = “我为你骄傲。”你说。他看着你的样子，就像他来的第一晚看着那块没人非给不可的面包。然后他把信放进抽屉，没拆，再也没拿出来过。\n# en: \"I'm proud of you,\" you say. He doesn't move for a long time. Then he puts the letter in a drawer, unopened, and asks you to say it again, just once, so he'll know he didn't imagine it.\nopt.left.say.2 [a117e4] = “我为你骄傲。”你说。他很久没有动。然后他把信放进抽屉，没拆，请你再说一遍，就一遍，好让他知道不是自己想象出来的。\n# en: Open it together\nopt.right.label [b08f88] = 一起拆开\n# en: You open it together. It's about the weather and his uncle's knee. At the bottom, after the signature, in a different, hurried hand: \"Well done.\" He reads it eleven times. It's more than he's ever had.\nopt.right.say.1 [5b6ec7] = 你们一起拆开。信里说的是天气和他叔叔的膝盖。在最底下，署名之后，换了一种匆忙的笔迹：“做得好。”他读了十一遍。这比他以往得到的都多。\n\n@event kael_ex\n# en: Frost Recedes\ntitle [317cae] = 霜退\n# en: You notice it on a cold morning: the corridor windows are frosted over, every one, except the panes you walk past. Kael is at the far end. When he sees you, the frost on his side of the hall draws back, carefully, the way you'd step out of someone's road.\ntext [931623] = 一个寒冷的早晨你注意到了：走廊的窗子全都结了霜，唯独你经过的那几块玻璃没有。凯尔在走廊尽头。看见你时，他那一侧的霜小心翼翼地退开了，就像人给别人让路。\n# en: A long corridor of tall windows, frost on every pane except the ones nearest a student walking past alone.\nscene [269d68] = 一条长长的走廊排着高窗，每一块玻璃都结着霜，只有一个独自走过的学生身旁那几块没有。\n# en: Walk on\nopt.left.label [383259] = 继续走\n# en: You walk on. The frost closes behind you. It's a kindness, you think, of a kind: he's keeping out of your way so precisely that it must take all his attention.\nopt.left.say.1 [460841] = 你继续往前走。霜在你身后合拢。你想，这也算一种体贴：他避开你避得这样精确，一定耗尽了他全部的注意力。\n# en: Walk up to him\nopt.right.label [d512ec] = 走到他面前\n# en: You say his name. He flinches, and then doesn't. \"Until the thaw?\" you ask, and he almost smiles. \"Until the thaw,\" he says. The frost comes back across the windows, slowly, all of them, yours too.\nopt.right.say.1 [a9f338] = 你叫他的名字。他瑟缩了一下，又没有。“等到解冻？”你问。他几乎笑了。“等到解冻。”他说。霜慢慢地爬回所有窗子上，你身旁的也一样。\n# en: \"Don't,\" he says, very gently. \"Please. I'm not good at this part. I wasn't taught it.\" He goes. The windows stay clear where you stand.\nopt.right.say.2 [89c09e] = “别，”他非常温和地说，“求你了。这部分我做不好。没人教过我。”他走了。你站着的地方，窗子依然透亮。\n\n@event kael_gift_given\n# en: A Snowflake Under Glass\ntitle [57be9e] = 玻璃罩里的雪花\n# en: There's a parcel in your pigeonhole, cold to the touch. Inside, wrapped in a scrap of fur, is a tiny glass dome with a single snowflake in it. It hasn't melted. A note in sharp Northern handwriting: \"It won't. I asked it not to.\"\ntext [ee4555] = 你的信格里有个包裹，摸上去冰凉。里面裹着一块毛皮，毛皮里是一个小小的玻璃罩，罩着一片雪花。它没有融化。一张字条，笔迹是锋利的北境字体：“它不会化的。我请它别化。”\n# en: A tiny glass dome in a student's palm, a single perfect snowflake inside it that has not melted.\nscene [3cb672] = 一个学生的掌心里托着一个小玻璃罩，里面一片完美的雪花，没有融化。\n# en: Keep it on your windowsill\nopt.left.label [99b65b] = 摆在窗台上\n# en: It catches the light every morning. On the hottest day of the year you check it, worried. The snowflake is still there, perfect. So, you think, is he: careful, and very stubborn about what lasts.\nopt.left.say.1 [edb04f] = 每天早上它都映着光。一年里最热的那天，你担心地去看。雪花还在，完好无缺。你想，他也是这样：小心翼翼，又对什么该长久这件事非常固执。\n# en: Give him something warm back\nopt.right.label [d2eec6] = 回送他点暖和的\n# en: You find him a scarf, thick and red and absurd. He wears it at once, even indoors. Frost forms on it and he picks it off, flake by flake, as if he's protecting it.\nopt.right.say.1 [7b10bc] = 你给他找了一条围巾，又厚又红，滑稽极了。他当场就围上了，在屋里也不摘。围巾上结了霜，他一片一片地摘掉，好像在保护它。\n\n@event nessa_the_one_phrase\n# en: The One Phrase\ntitle [1c14a5] = 那一句话\n# en: You tell Nessa something about your day and her quill writes it down. {~It always does.|You're used to it by now.} \"Sorry,\" she says. \"It takes everything. There's one phrase that stops it, you know. I've never used it.\" She doesn't say what it is. She looks at you as if she's deciding.\ntext [f0280b] = 你跟内莎说起你这一天，她的羽毛笔把它记了下来。{~它总是这样。|你已经习惯了。}“抱歉，”她说，“它什么都记。你知道吗，有一句话能让它停下。我从来没用过。”她没说是哪句。她看着你，像在做决定。\n# en: A girl at a cluttered desk with a self-writing quill scratching beside her, glaring at it, while another student leans on the desk.\nscene [74d756] = 一个女孩坐在杂乱的书桌前，一支自动书写的羽毛笔在她身旁沙沙作响，她瞪着它，另一个学生倚在桌边。\n# en: Don't ask; let her decide\nopt.left.label [3f66a5] = 不问，让她自己决定\n# en: {~You say nothing. She keeps looking, and the quill keeps writing, and then she tells you what the phrase is, which is not the same as using it. \"Off the record,\" she says. \"Not yet.\" The quill writes that down too.|You wait. \"Off the record,\" she says at last, as a definition, not a command. \"That's the phrase. Someday I'll say it to somebody.\" The quill underlines \"someday\" twice.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e6301c] = {~你什么都没说。她一直看着你，羽毛笔一直在写，然后她告诉了你那句话是什么——告诉你，和真正对它说，不是一回事。“不作记录，”她说，“还不到时候。”羽毛笔把这句也记下了。|你等着。“不作记录，”她终于说，像在下定义，而不是在下命令，“就是这句话。总有一天我会对某个人说。”羽毛笔在“总有一天”下面画了两道线。}\n# en: Guess the phrase\nopt.right.label [33207a] = 猜那句话\n# en: {~You guess \"please stop\". The quill writes \"PLEASE STOP\" in capitals and keeps going. Nessa laughs so hard she has to sit down.|You guess \"the end\". The quill writes \"THE END\" and then, to make a point, a sequel. Nessa gives you a mark out of ten. It's a four.}\nopt.right.say.1 [12dd59] = {~你猜“请停下”。羽毛笔用大写字母写下“请停下”，然后继续写。内莎笑得不得不坐下。|你猜“全文完”。羽毛笔写下“全文完”，然后为了表明立场，又写了一篇续集。内莎给你打了个分：十分里的四分。}\n\n@event nessa_quill_draft\n# en: The Quill's Draft\ntitle [6ad1a4] = 羽毛笔的草稿\n# en: There's a draft on Nessa's desk in the quill's handwriting, headed \"EXCLUSIVE: THE EDITOR AND THE SOURCE.\" It's about you. And her. And a great many moonlit walks that never happened. Nessa comes in, sees you reading it, and turns the colour of red ink.\ntext [4dda1e] = 内莎桌上有一份草稿，是羽毛笔的笔迹，标题是“独家：主编与线人”。写的是你。和她。还有许许多多根本没发生过的月下漫步。内莎走进来，看见你在读，脸红得像红墨水。\n# en: A sheet of newsprint on a desk with an over-written headline about two students, a mortified girl snatching for it.\nscene [315008] = 桌上一张新闻稿，标题夸张地写着两个学生的事，一个窘迫的女孩伸手去抢。\n# en: Hand it back unread\nopt.left.label [3ab727] = 不读，还给她\n# en: You fold it and hand it back. \"I didn't read past the headline,\" you lie. \"It invents things,\" she says, too fast. \"Left alone. It makes up the news.\" She folds it smaller. She doesn't throw it away.\nopt.left.say.1 [38a989] = 你把它折好还给她。“我只看了标题。”你撒谎。“它会编东西，”她说得太快，“没人看着就编。它会编造新闻。”她把纸折得更小。她没扔掉。\n# en: Read the best line aloud\nopt.right.label [6164c1] = 把最好的一句念出来\n# en: You read out \"their eyes met over the proofs like two scandals\". Nessa puts her face in her hands. \"It makes things up,\" she says, through her fingers. \"When I'm not watching it.\" Then, muffled: \"That line's not even good.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [f9e309] = 你念出“他们的目光在校样上相遇，像两桩丑闻”。内莎把脸埋进手里。“它编的，”她从指缝里说，“我不看着它就编。”然后闷闷地：“那句话写得都不好。”\n\n@event nessa_deadline_night\n# en: Deadline Night\ntitle [d06cb7] = 截稿夜\n# en: The Ledger goes to the printer at midnight and Nessa is three columns short. {~Ink on her face, ink in her hair.|The press is waiting under its dust sheet.} \"I don't need help,\" she says. \"I need a miracle, or a typesetter who doesn't sleep.\"\ntext [2b17ae] = 《流明纪事报》午夜就要付印，内莎还缺三栏。{~脸上是墨，头发上也是墨。|印刷机在防尘布下等着。}“我不需要帮忙，”她说，“我需要一个奇迹，或者一个不睡觉的排字工。”\n# en: A newspaper office at night, proofs everywhere, a girl with ink on her face, a printing press waiting under a dust sheet.\nscene [5ec241] = 夜里的报社编辑部，校样到处都是，一个女孩脸上沾着墨，一台印刷机盖着防尘布等在一旁。\n# en: Set type until midnight\nopt.left.label [112e43] = 陪她排字到午夜\n# en: {~You set type with her until your fingers are black. At two minutes to midnight she slots in the last line, and kisses the press, and then, quickly, as if it were the same thing, your cheek.|You read proofs aloud while she sets them. You catch four errors. She catches you yawning, and calls it the fifth. The paper goes out on time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4ff21e] = {~你陪她排字，排到手指发黑。午夜前两分钟，她塞进最后一行，亲了一下印刷机，然后飞快地，好像是同一件事似的，亲了一下你的脸颊。|她排版，你把校样念出来。你挑出四处错误。她抓到你打哈欠，说这算第五处。报纸按时付印了。}\n# en: Write the third column yourself\nopt.right.label [31465d] = 自己写第三栏\n# en: {~You write it in an hour. She cuts a third and calls it \"almost good\", which from Nessa is a medal. It runs under \"Staff\".|Your column runs. Nessa edits it so hard it bleeds, and then pins the proof on her wall.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a53b7e] = {~你一个钟头就写完了。她删掉三分之一，说“差不多能看”——从内莎嘴里说出来，这是一枚勋章。它署名“本报”刊出。|你的专栏刊出了。内莎改得它鲜血淋漓，然后把校样钉在了她的墙上。}\n# en: {~She reads it, says \"no\", kindly, and writes the column herself in twenty minutes. She keeps your draft anyway.|It's not good. She tells you so, and why, for ten minutes, and you learn more about writing than in a year of essays.}\nopt.right.say.2 [375de3] = {~她读完，温和地说了声“不行”，然后二十分钟自己写完了那一栏。你的草稿她还是留着。|写得不好。她告诉你不好，还说了为什么，说了十分钟，你从中学到的写作比一整年的论文都多。}\n\n@event nessa_shared_byline\n# en: A Shared Byline\ntitle [bff3c4] = 联合署名\n# en: Nessa shows you the proof of next week's lead story. You helped with it, a little: one interview, one fact you checked. Under the headline it says \"by Nessa Quill and\" — and then your name. \"It's accurate,\" she says, too casually. \"I only print what's accurate.\"\ntext [3f322e] = 内莎给你看下周头条的校样。你帮过一点忙：一次采访，一个核实过的事实。标题下写着“内莎·奎尔与”——然后是你的名字。“这是准确的，”她说，故作随意，“我只刊登准确的东西。”\n# en: A newspaper proof with two names printed side by side under a headline, one student pointing at them, the other pretending not to care.\nscene [89447f] = 一张报纸校样，标题下并排印着两个名字，一个学生指着它们，另一个装作不在乎。\n# en: Keep your name on it\nopt.left.label [7f6068] = 保留你的名字\n# en: The story runs with both your names. People stop you in the corridor about it. Nessa cuts the byline out of the proof copy and keeps it in her notebook, where you're not supposed to see.\nopt.left.say.1 [b44fb4] = 报道署着你们俩的名字刊出了。人们在走廊里拦住你聊这篇文章。内莎把校样上的署名剪下来，夹在她的笔记本里——你本不该看见的地方。\n# en: \"It's your story. Take it.\"\nopt.right.label [e07f8a] = “这是你的报道。你署名。”\n# en: She looks almost hurt, then very much not. \"You're the first person who's ever tried to give me credit back,\" she says. \"Fine. But you're in the acknowledgements. In large type.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [3805d1] = 她看上去几乎受伤了，随即又完全不是。“你是第一个想把功劳还给我的人，”她说，“行。但你得上致谢。大号字。”\n\n@event nessa_corrections_column\n# en: Corrections\ntitle [2a1126] = 更正\n# en: Every week the Ledger's corrections column has a paragraph only you can read. {~This week: \"Correction: last Tuesday we reported the weather as fair. It was, in fact, lovely, for reasons the editor declines to give.\"|This week: \"Correction: the editor did not, as reported, sleep at her desk. She slept on someone's shoulder, which is not a desk.\"}\ntext [0f8d75] = 每周，《流明纪事报》的更正栏里都有一段只有你看得懂。{~本周：“更正：上周二本报称天气晴好。实际上是美好，原因主编拒绝透露。”|本周：“更正：主编并未如报道所称在办公桌上睡着。她是靠在某人肩上睡着的，那不是办公桌。”}\n# en: A student reading the back page of a school newspaper over breakfast, smiling at a tiny paragraph in the corrections box.\nscene [03b18d] = 早餐时，一个学生读着校报的最后一版，对着更正栏里一小段文字微笑。\n# en: Write one back\nopt.left.label [ce6383] = 回她一条\n# en: {~You slip a correction under her door: \"Correction: the reader was not 'mildly pleased'. The reader was delighted.\" It runs the next week, verbatim, in a box with a border.|You send in a correction of the correction. She prints it with a correction of that. It goes on for a month. The rest of the school thinks the Ledger's lost its mind.}\nopt.left.say.1 [947c2a] = {~你把一条更正塞到她门下：“更正：读者并非‘略感愉快’。读者是欣喜。”下周它原封不动地刊出了，还加了个带边框的方框。|你寄去一条对更正的更正。她刊出了对那条的更正。来来回回持续了一个月。全校都以为《纪事报》疯了。}\n# en: Read it aloud at breakfast\nopt.right.label [0c0ce7] = 早餐时念出来\n# en: {~You read it aloud. Nobody else understands it. Nessa, three tables away, puts her head down on her toast.|You read it aloud with feeling. Half the table works it out. Nessa runs no corrections the following week, which is its own sort of correction.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e0f7cb] = {~你念了出来。没人听得懂。隔着三张桌子的内莎把头埋进了吐司里。|你声情并茂地念了出来。半桌人听懂了。下一周内莎一条更正都没登，这本身也算一种更正。}\n\n@event nessa_not_a_story\n# en: Not a Story\ntitle [79b584] = 不是一篇报道\n# en: Nessa has written a feature. It's beautiful. It's about you: where you came from, what you told her late at night, what you're afraid of. Names changed. \"Nobody would know,\" she says. \"It's the best thing I've ever written.\"\ntext [a50478] = 内莎写了一篇特稿。写得很美。写的是你：你从哪里来，深夜里你对她说过的话，你害怕什么。名字都改过了。“没人会知道的，”她说，“这是我写过最好的东西。”\n# en: A girl holding out a typed draft to her partner across a desk, eager, the partner not taking it, the quill between them frozen mid-word.\nscene [f4dba8] = 一个女孩隔着书桌把一份打好的稿子递给她的恋人，满怀期待，对方没有接，两人之间的羽毛笔停在半个字上。\n# en: \"I'm not a story.\"\nopt.left.label [8971b7] = “我不是一篇报道。”\n# en: She means it kindly; make her hear you.\nopt.left.hint [7fdcb8] = 她是好意；让她听见你。\n# en: It takes an hour. She argues like an editor; you argue like someone who's been edited. At the end she stops, and looks at the draft, and puts it in the drawer with the quill. \"Off the record,\" she says. \"All of it. Always. I'm sorry.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [c98023] = 说了一个钟头。她像编辑一样争，你像被编辑过的人一样争。最后她停下来，看着那份稿子，把它和羽毛笔一起放进了抽屉。“不作记录，”她说，“全部。永远。对不起。”\n# en: She doesn't print it. She doesn't understand why, either, and she says so, at length, and you both go to bed angry. In the morning the draft is gone from her desk. You don't know where.\nopt.left.say.2 [340450] = 她没登。她也不明白为什么不能登，还滔滔不绝地这么说了，你们俩带着气上了床。第二天早上，草稿从她桌上不见了。你不知道去了哪里。\n# en: Let her print it\nopt.right.label [24f82f] = 让她登出来\n# en: It runs. People cry over it in the refectory. Nobody guesses it's you, quite. Nessa is radiant all week, and you feel, oddly, like a photograph of yourself.\nopt.right.say.1 [d53d6b] = 文章刊出了。膳堂里有人读着读着哭了。没人完全猜出是你。内莎一整周容光焕发，而你奇怪地觉得自己像一张自己的照片。\n\n@event nessa_press_breaks\n# en: The Press Breaks\ntitle [348b25] = 印刷机坏了\n# en: The Ledger's press has cracked down the middle, a hundred years old and done. Nessa is sitting on the floor beside it. \"Twenty years we haven't missed an issue,\" she says, very steady. \"I'll copy it out by hand. Every copy. I'll chain myself to it if I have to.\" She isn't joking.\ntext [b76845] = 《纪事报》的印刷机从中间裂开了，一百岁了，彻底完了。内莎坐在它旁边的地上。“二十年来我们一期都没落下过，”她说得很平稳，“我会手抄。每一份。必要的话我就把自己锁在它上面。”她不是在开玩笑。\n# en: An old iron printing press with a cracked frame, a girl sitting on the floor beside it with her knees drawn up, ink on her hands.\nscene [6b9dfc] = 一台机架裂开的旧铁印刷机，一个女孩抱着膝坐在它旁边的地上，手上沾着墨。\n# en: Copy it out with her, all night\nopt.left.label [0d8f85] = 陪她抄一整夜\n# en: You copy out three hundred Ledgers by hand, by candlelight, with half the club. At dawn the last one dries. Nessa falls asleep on the stack, ink on her nose, and the issue goes out on time, every copy in a different hand.\nopt.left.say.1 [1f82b0] = 你和半个报社的人一起，借着烛光手抄了三百份《纪事报》。黎明时最后一份晾干了。内莎趴在那摞报纸上睡着了，鼻尖沾着墨。这一期按时发出，每一份的笔迹都不一样。\n# en: Find Mir\nopt.right.label [4511c6] = 去找米尔\n# en: Mir looks at the press for a long minute, says \"oh, you poor old thing\", and spends three nights with a welding charm. It prints again, louder than before. Nessa hugs Mir so hard their goggles come off.\nopt.right.say.1 [55b7f3] = 米尔盯着印刷机看了好一会儿，说了句“哦，可怜的老家伙”，然后拿着焊接咒忙了三个晚上。它又能印了，比以前还响。内莎紧紧抱住米尔，把TA的护目镜都抱掉了。\n# en: You don't know anyone who can mend it, so you hire someone from the village. It costs. It prints. Nessa says she'll pay you back; you both know she'll pay you back in column inches.\nopt.right.say.2 [a6e36f] = 你不认识能修它的人，就从镇上雇了个师傅。花了钱。它能印了。内莎说她会还你钱；你们俩都知道，她会用专栏篇幅来还。\n\n@event nessa_ex\n# en: Retraction\ntitle [ec6898] = 撤回声明\n# en: In this week's Ledger, at the very bottom of the back page, in the smallest type the press has: \"Retraction. The editor regrets an earlier statement, made off the record, that she was certain. She was not. She is sorry.\" Nessa is in the doorway, watching you read it.\ntext [0f1f4d] = 本周的《纪事报》，最后一版的最底下，用印刷机能排出的最小号字写着：“撤回声明。主编对此前一则不作记录的声明表示遗憾——她当时说她很确定。她并不确定。她很抱歉。”内莎站在门口，看着你读。\n# en: A tiny boxed paragraph on the back page of a school newspaper, a student reading it alone in a window seat.\nscene [06fdee] = 校报最后一版上一个小小的方框段落，一个学生独自坐在窗台上读着它。\n# en: Fold the paper and go\nopt.left.label [4f92dc] = 折起报纸离开\n# en: You fold the Ledger and put it in your bag. You don't look back. Weeks later you find it again, the little paragraph worn soft where you've folded it, over and over.\nopt.left.say.1 [38d5fa] = 你把《纪事报》折好放进书包。你没回头。几周后你又翻出它来，那一小段被你折了又折，已经磨软了。\n# en: Go and talk to her\nopt.right.label [2c043a] = 去和她谈谈\n# en: \"On the record,\" you say, \"I'd like to be friends.\" Her quill, at her shoulder, writes it down, and she lets it. \"Printed,\" she says. \"No retractions.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [7d63de] = “作记录，”你说，“我想和你做朋友。”她肩头的羽毛笔把这句记了下来，她由着它记。“已付印，”她说，“不撤回。”\n# en: She starts to say something, and then her quill starts writing, and she looks at it, and at you, and puts it away. \"Not yet,\" she says. \"I'm still drafting.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [5f1724] = 她刚要开口，羽毛笔就开始写了，她看看它，又看看你，把它收了起来。“还不到时候，”她说，“我还在打草稿。”\n\n@event nessa_argument\n# en: On the Record\ntitle [6a1830] = 作记录\n# en: You and Nessa are arguing about something small, and it's getting less small, and halfway through you realise her quill is taking it down. Verbatim. With stage directions. \"Ignore it,\" she says. \"Go on. You were saying I'm 'impossible'?\"\ntext [5dde21] = 你和内莎为一件小事争了起来，事情越吵越不小，吵到一半你才发现她的羽毛笔在记录。一字不差。还带舞台提示。“别管它，”她说，“继续。你刚才说我‘不可理喻’？”\n# en: Two students arguing in a corridor, one of them with a notebook out, a quill floating at her shoulder taking it all down.\nscene [298aab] = 两个学生在走廊里争吵，其中一个掏出了笔记本，一支羽毛笔悬在她肩头记录着一切。\n# en: Make her put the quill away\nopt.left.label [086bf7] = 让她把笔收起来\n# en: \"Not with that thing listening.\" She glares, and then, astonishingly, puts it in her pocket. The argument dies without an audience. \"You're right,\" she says. \"Don't quote me.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [3bd92c] = “那东西听着我可不吵。”她瞪着你，然后，出人意料地，把它收进了口袋。没了听众，争吵自己就熄了。“你说得对，”她说，“别引用我。”\n# en: Argue for the record\nopt.right.label [fb2aca] = 当着记录吵\n# en: You argue it out for the record, both of you playing to the quill, until you're laughing too hard to finish. The transcript is pinned on her wall under \"best interview of the year\".\nopt.right.say.1 [8accc0] = 你们当着记录吵到底，两个人都在冲着羽毛笔表演，最后笑得吵不下去。那份记录被钉在她的墙上，标题是“年度最佳采访”。\n# en: You argue for the record. She wins on points, and prints a column the next week about \"people who say 'impossible' when they mean 'right'\". You aren't named. Everyone knows.\nopt.right.say.2 [bddded] = 你们当着记录吵。她以点数取胜，下一周登了篇专栏，讲“那些说‘不可理喻’其实意思是‘你说得对’的人”。没点你的名。大家都知道。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/romance/lyra_tobias.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/romance/lyra_tobias.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event lyra_second_place\n# en: Second Place, Again\ntitle [66b03a] = 又是第二\n# en: The rankings are up. {~Your name is above hers, by one mark. Lyra stands in front of the list with her arms folded. \"One mark,\" she says. \"I'll have it back by Frostcall.\"|Her name is above yours, by one mark, and she's not looking at it. She's looking at you. \"One mark,\" she says. \"I'll make it two by Frostcall.\"|Your names are level. The registrar has had to put you side by side, alphabetically, and she hates it. \"A tie,\" she says, arms folded. \"I'll break it by Frostcall.\"}\ntext [1e371d] = 排名贴出来了。{~你的名字在她上面，只差一分。莱拉抱着胳膊站在榜前。“一分，”她说，“霜唤月之前我会拿回来。”|她的名字在你上面，只差一分，可她没在看榜。她在看你。“一分，”她说，“霜唤月之前我会让它变成两分。”|你们俩分数相同。教务只好按字母顺序把你们并排列出，她恨透了这一点。“平手，”她抱着胳膊说，“霜唤月之前我会打破它。”}\n# en: A posted list of class rankings on a stone wall, two names at the top, a red-haired girl staring at the second one.\nscene [4d01ab] = 石墙上贴着一张班级排名，榜首两个名字，一个红发女孩盯着第二个。\n# en: \"You can try.\"\nopt.left.label [a7c127] = “你可以试试。”\n# en: {~Her eyes narrow, and something in them lights. \"Oh, I will,\" she says, and walks off already planning. You find yourself studying harder too, which you suspect was the point.|\"Good,\" she says, with relish. \"I'd hate to win against someone who wasn't trying.\" She's almost smiling.|\"Try,\" she repeats, as if tasting the word. She's in the library before you the next morning. So are you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5775c1] = {~她眯起眼睛，眼底有什么亮了起来。“我会的。”她说着走开，已经在盘算了。你发现自己也更用功了——你怀疑这正是她的目的。|“很好，”她津津有味地说，“赢一个不尽力的人，我会觉得没意思。”她几乎在笑。|“试试，”她把这个词重复了一遍，像在品味它。第二天一早她比你先到图书馆。你也到了。}\n# en: \"It's just a list.\"\nopt.right.label [eddc15] = “不过是张榜。”\n# en: {~\"It's never just a list,\" she says, and for a second she sounds about twelve. Then the Vaneshade face comes back. \"But you wouldn't know that.\"|She looks at you as though you'd said the sun was optional. Then she walks away to study.|\"Lucky you,\" she says, and means something you don't catch until later.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5c2433] = {~“从来都不只是张榜。”她说，有那么一瞬间，她听上去像个十二岁的孩子。随后凡妮希德家的神情又回来了。“不过你不会懂的。”|她看着你，仿佛你刚说太阳可有可无。然后她走去自习了。|“你真幸运。”她说。这句话的意思，你很久以后才明白。}\n\n@event lyra_glove\n# en: The Glove\ntitle [822695] = 手套\n# en: Lyra drops a glove at your feet in an empty corridor. An actual glove. \"Vaneshades settle things properly,\" she says. \"You've been in my head all term. It's insufferable. Dawn, the practice circle. If I win, you stop.\" She doesn't say stop what. She's gone red to the ears.\ntext [33af1c] = 在空无一人的走廊里，莱拉把一只手套丢在你脚边。一只真正的手套。“凡妮希德家用正式的方式解决问题，”她说，“你整个学期都在我脑子里，烦死了。黎明，练习场。我赢了，你就停下。”她没说停下什么。她连耳朵都红了。\n# en: A red-haired girl dropping a leather duelling glove at a student's feet in an empty corridor, her chin very high.\nscene [0c6174] = 空走廊里，红发女孩把一只皮质决斗手套丢在一个学生脚边，下巴扬得很高。\n# en: Pick it up\nopt.left.label [b90f6a] = 捡起来\n# en: You meet her at dawn. Neither of you casts a single spell. You stand in the frost and talk for an hour instead, about nothing, and she forgets to win, and so do you.\nopt.left.say.1 [385f4a] = 黎明时你去见她。你们谁都没施一个咒。你们站在霜里聊了一个钟头，什么都聊，她忘了要赢，你也忘了。\n# en: Hand it back, politely\nopt.right.label [b4d5ae] = 礼貌地还给她\n# en: \"You dropped this.\" Her face does something complicated and then goes perfectly smooth. \"So I did,\" she says, and puts it on, and that's the end of it. Almost.\nopt.right.say.1 [a3d9ff] = “你掉了这个。”她的表情复杂地变了几变，然后变得一丝不乱。“是啊，”她说着戴上手套，事情就这样结束了。差不多结束了。\n\n@event lyra_after_hours\n# en: After the Torches\ntitle [fa1628] = 火把熄灭之后\n# en: {~The duelling hall's torches go out at ten. Lyra lights one flame in her palm and keeps practising.|Lyra is still in the practice hall after the torches are out, working one form over and over.|The torches are out again. Lyra's palm flame is the only light, and she's waiting, as if she knew you'd come.} \"You're late,\" she says. You didn't say you were coming.\ntext [43d720] = {~决斗厅的火把十点就熄了。莱拉在掌心点起一簇火，继续练。|火把熄了，莱拉还在练习厅里，一遍又一遍地练同一个招式。|火把又熄了。莱拉掌心的火是唯一的光，她在等，好像早知道你会来。}“你迟到了。”她说。你可没说过要来。\n# en: An empty practice hall at night, the torches out, a red-haired girl lighting a single flame in her palm so two people can see.\nscene [012bc5] = 夜里空荡的练习厅，火把已熄，一个红发女孩在掌心点起一簇火，好让两个人都看得见。\n# en: Spar with her\nopt.left.label [ca472c] = 陪她对练\n# en: {~You spar until your arms shake. She says nothing nice, but she shows you, without explaining, how she shapes the turn of a flame. Then she blows it out, and you walk back in the dark, close enough to bump shoulders.|You spar in the light of her hand. She's better than you. She tells you exactly how, and stays until you've fixed it.|You fight until you're both laughing at a missed block. Lyra doesn't laugh, usually. She stops, surprised at herself.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4940bb] = {~你们一直练到你胳膊发抖。她一句好话没说，却不加解释地演示给你看，她是怎样拧转火焰的。然后她吹灭了火，你们在黑暗里走回去，近得肩膀相碰。|你们就着她掌心的光对练。她比你强。她一五一十地告诉你强在哪里，一直留到你改过来。|你们打到两个人都为一次漏挡笑出声。莱拉平时不笑。她停下来，自己也吃了一惊。}\n# en: Make her stop\nopt.right.label [275e64] = 让她停下\n# en: {~\"Enough,\" you say, and take her wrist. The flame goes out. \"You'll be useless tomorrow.\" She glares at you in the dark, and then lets you walk her back, which she almost never does.|You sit on the bench and refuse to move until she does. It takes an hour. Your own reading doesn't get done.|You make her eat something. She eats it as if it's an insult, and then eats yours.}\nopt.right.say.1 [09f8aa] = {~“够了。”你说着握住她的手腕。火灭了。“你明天会废掉的。”她在黑暗里瞪着你，然后让你送她回去——她几乎从不这样。|你在长凳上坐下，她不走你就不走。耗了一个钟头。你自己的书一页没看。|你逼她吃点东西。她吃得像受了侮辱，然后把你那份也吃了。}\n\n@event lyra_partner_watch\n# en: Burnout Watch\ntitle [a16a07] = 守着她别累垮\n# en: You've an arrangement now, the two of you: at midnight, you go and find her, wherever she is, and make her stop. Tonight it's the library. She's on chapter nine of a book with eleven. {~\"Two more,\" she says, without looking up.|\"Ten minutes,\" she says, which means two hours.}\ntext [83d550] = 你们俩现在有个约定：午夜时，无论她在哪儿，你都去找到她，让她停下。今晚是图书馆。她在读一本十一章的书的第九章。{~“还有两章。”她头也不抬地说。|“十分钟。”她说，意思是两个钟头。}\n# en: A candlelit desk buried in books, a red-haired girl asleep on her notes, someone gently taking the quill out of her hand.\nscene [f81f92] = 烛光下堆满书的书桌，一个红发女孩趴在笔记上睡着了，有人轻轻从她手里抽走羽毛笔。\n# en: Make her stop\nopt.left.label [275e64] = 让她停下\n# en: {~You close the book on her fingers, gently. She lets you. On the way back she falls asleep against your shoulder, standing up, which you didn't know was possible.|You take the quill. She says something cutting. Then she says thank you, very quietly, as if it costs her a mark.}\nopt.left.say.1 [17b628] = {~你轻轻合上书，压住她的手指。她由着你。回去的路上，她站着就靠在你肩上睡着了，你都不知道人还能这样。|你拿走她的羽毛笔。她说了句刻薄话。然后很轻地说了声谢谢，好像这要扣她一分。}\n# en: Stay and finish it with her\nopt.right.label [908278] = 留下陪她读完\n# en: {~You pull up a chair and read chapter ten aloud while she takes notes. By two you're both asleep on the table, and the librarian covers you with a cloak.|You stay. She finishes. She says finishing a book with someone there is a different thing entirely.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7aac9d] = {~你拉过一把椅子，把第十章念出来，她记笔记。到两点时，你们俩都趴在桌上睡着了，图书管理员给你们盖上一件斗篷。|你留下了。她读完了。她说，有人在旁边时读完一本书，完全是另一回事。}\n\n@event lyra_not_second\n# en: Not Second to Anyone\ntitle [a1c79b] = 不做任何人的第二\n# en: Lyra has found out you skipped her match for {~a study group|a club meeting|a friend's crisis}. She is very calm, which is worse than shouting. \"I have been second to my mother's ambitions my whole life,\" she says. {~\"I won't be second to a study group.\"|\"I won't be second to a club.\"|\"I know it was a crisis. I know. I still wanted you there.\"}\ntext [26f9bd] = 莱拉发现你为了{~一个学习小组|一次社团活动|一个朋友的急事}错过了她的比赛。她非常平静，这比大吼更糟。“我这辈子一直排在我母亲的野心后面，”她说。{~“我不会排在一个学习小组后面。”|“我不会排在一个社团后面。”|“我知道那是急事。我知道。我还是想要你在场。”}\n# en: A red-haired girl standing very straight in a corridor, eyes bright with anger, a crumpled tournament list in her fist.\nscene [18356e] = 一个红发女孩笔直地站在走廊里，眼里燃着怒火，手里攥着一张揉皱的比赛名单。\n# en: Talk it through, properly\nopt.left.label [43c9e0] = 好好把话说开\n# en: She wants to be argued with, not soothed.\nopt.left.hint [10bed1] = 她要的是争辩，不是安抚。\n# en: You don't apologise first; you argue. You tell her she's not second, she's the one you'd have fought for a seat beside. She listens. Then she kisses you, angrily, and says next time you'll be in the front row.\nopt.left.say.1 [439371] = 你没有先道歉，而是和她争。你告诉她，她不是第二，她是你会为了坐在她身边去争一个位子的人。她听着。然后她吻了你，带着火气，说下次你得坐第一排。\n# en: {~Everything you say sounds like an excuse, because it is.|Everything you say sounds like an excuse, because it is.|You were right to go, and you both know it, and somehow that makes it harder to say.} She nods, very correctly, and walks away. She's at your next lecture. She doesn't sit next to you.\nopt.left.say.2 [d710db] = {~你说的每句话听上去都像借口，因为它们就是借口。|你说的每句话听上去都像借口，因为它们就是借口。|你去是对的，你们俩都知道，可不知怎么的，这反而更难说出口。}她非常得体地点点头，走了。你下一堂课她也在。她没坐在你旁边。\n# en: Apologise, and mean it\nopt.right.label [51060b] = 道歉，真心的\n# en: \"I'm sorry.\" She looks at you a long time. \"Vaneshades don't accept apologies,\" she says. \"But I'm not a Vaneshade with you.\" It's forgiveness. It still stings, for both of you.\nopt.right.say.1 [42a843] = “对不起。”她看了你很久。“凡妮希德家不接受道歉，”她说，“可在你面前，我不是凡妮希德家的人。”这就是原谅了。可你们俩都还隐隐作痛。\n\n@event lyra_empty_chair\n# en: Her Mother's Empty Chair\ntitle [d498d5] = 她母亲的空椅子\n# en: Lyra's final bout of the year. In the front row of the gallery is a velvet chair with a card on it: \"Reserved — Lady Vaneshade.\" It stays empty. Lyra looks at it once, just before she bows, and then at you.\ntext [41e532] = 莱拉这一年的最后一场比赛。看台前排有一把天鹅绒椅子，上面放着一张卡片：“预留——凡妮希德夫人。”椅子一直空着。莱拉在行礼前看了它一眼，然后看向你。\n# en: A crowded duelling gallery with one empty velvet chair in the front row, a red-haired duellist glancing at it before her bout.\nscene [199722] = 坐满人的决斗看台，前排一把空着的天鹅绒椅子，一个红发决斗者在上场前瞥了它一眼。\n# en: Sit in the chair\nopt.left.label [fad2cc] = 坐到那把椅子上\n# en: You walk down to the front and sit in Lady Vaneshade's chair. People stare. Lyra sees, and something in her shoulders lets go. She wins in under a minute. Afterwards she says, \"That was a terrible breach of etiquette,\" and doesn't let go of your hand.\nopt.left.say.1 [2ceaf3] = 你走到前排，在凡妮希德夫人的椅子上坐下。人们盯着你看。莱拉看见了，肩膀里有什么松开了。她不到一分钟就赢了。事后她说：“那是严重的失礼。”然后一直没松开你的手。\n# en: Cheer louder than anyone\nopt.right.label [f04bda] = 喊得比谁都响\n# en: You shout her name until the gallery joins in. She doesn't look at the chair again. She wins, and turns, and looks for you, only you, the way she used to look for the scoreboard.\nopt.right.say.1 [9b7903] = 你喊她的名字，喊到整个看台都跟着喊。她再没看那把椅子一眼。她赢了，转过身来找你，只找你，就像她从前找记分板那样。\n\n@event lyra_ex\n# en: Cold Hearth\ntitle [f78029] = 冷炉\n# en: Lyra passes you in the corridor with her chin high and her eyes forward. She's perfectly polite now, which is how you know. A Vaneshade's coldest weapon is good manners.\ntext [4a4554] = 莱拉在走廊里与你擦肩而过，下巴高扬，目不斜视。她现在对你礼貌周全，你就是从这一点看出来的。凡妮希德家最冷的武器是好教养。\n# en: Two people passing in a corridor, one a red-haired girl who keeps her chin high and her eyes forward.\nscene [7a80bf] = 两个人在走廊里擦肩而过，其中一个红发女孩下巴高扬，目不斜视。\n# en: Let her pass\nopt.left.label [bf55e2] = 让她走\n# en: She passes. You both keep walking. Behind you, a torch on the wall flares, briefly, as if someone's temper had slipped.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a7cd6] = 她走了过去。你们都没停步。你身后，墙上一支火把忽地蹿高了一下，像是谁的脾气没压住。\n# en: Say her name\nopt.right.label [7e575e] = 叫她的名字\n# en: She stops. Turns. \"What,\" she says. You say you miss her as a rival, at least. She almost laughs. \"Library,\" she says. \"Thursday. I'll destroy you.\" It's an olive branch, the Vaneshade way.\nopt.right.say.1 [1b7453] = 她停下，转身。“什么事。”她说。你说你想念她这个对手，至少这个。她差点笑出来。“图书馆，”她说，“周四。我会把你打得落花流水。”这是一根橄榄枝，凡妮希德式的。\n# en: \"Yes?\" she says, and waits, and you don't have the words. \"I thought not,\" she says, not unkindly, and goes.\nopt.right.say.2 [4ee7c1] = “嗯？”她说，等着，而你找不到话。“我就知道。”她说，并无恶意，然后走了。\n\n@event lyra_partner_neglect\n# en: \"I Don't Wait\"\ntitle [afe1bb] = “我不等人”\n# en: Lyra is in your doorway with her arms folded. \"I don't wait for people,\" she says. \"I've waited for you three times this month. I counted. I'm a Vaneshade; I count everything.\" She doesn't say she missed you. She doesn't need to.\ntext [49941d] = 莱拉抱着胳膊站在你门口。“我不等人，”她说，“这个月我等了你三次。我数过。我是凡妮希德家的人，什么都数。”她没说她想你。她不必说。\n# en: A red-haired girl in a duelling coat standing in a doorway with her arms folded, two cups of cold tea on the table behind her.\nscene [bc6b4e] = 一个穿决斗外套的红发女孩抱着胳膊站在门口，身后桌上放着两杯凉掉的茶。\n# en: \"Tomorrow. Dawn. I'll be there first.\"\nopt.left.label [4d7d51] = “明天黎明，我先到。”\n# en: You're there first, at dawn, with two cups of tea that are actually hot. She takes one and says nothing, which from her is an embrace.\nopt.left.say.1 [834636] = 黎明时你先到了，带着两杯真正热的茶。她接过一杯，什么都没说——对她来说，这就是拥抱。\n# en: \"You know what term's like.\"\nopt.right.label [b67bf0] = “你知道学期里是什么样。”\n# en: \"I do,\" she says. \"I manage.\" She walks away, perfectly upright. It's the most hurt you've ever seen her look.\nopt.right.say.1 [afc442] = “我知道，”她说，“我应付得来。”她走开了，背挺得笔直。你从没见过她这么受伤的样子。\n\n@event lyra_lends\n# en: A Loan, Vaneshade Style\ntitle [783102] = 凡妮希德式借钱\n# en: Lyra would sooner die than borrow. She has noticed, though, that your purse is thin, and she is holding out hers at arm's length without looking at it. \"Take what you need,\" she says. \"Don't thank me. Don't ever mention it.\"\ntext [ffbd52] = 莱拉宁死也不会向人借钱。不过她注意到你的钱袋瘪了，此刻正把她自己的钱袋伸直胳膊递过来，眼睛不看它。“需要多少拿多少，”她说，“别谢我。永远别提这事。”\n# en: A red-haired girl holding out a heavy purse at arm's length, not looking at it, as if it were beneath her notice.\nscene [46b429] = 一个红发女孩伸直胳膊递出一只沉甸甸的钱袋，眼睛不看它，好像它不值得她瞧一眼。\n# en: Take a little\nopt.left.label [5e0faf] = 拿一点\n# en: You take a few crowns. She never mentions it again. Neither do you, though you pay her back in study notes she pretends not to read.\nopt.left.say.1 [f889cb] = 你拿了几个克朗。她再也没提过。你也没有，不过你用学习笔记还了她，她假装不看。\n# en: Refuse, kindly\nopt.right.label [3868f0] = 客气地拒绝\n# en: \"I'm fine.\" She puts the purse away. \"Of course you are,\" she says, and the next day there's a meal paid for at the village inn in your name.\nopt.right.say.1 [7f8f43] = “我没事。”她收起钱袋。“你当然没事。”她说。第二天，镇上的客栈里有一顿饭记在了你的名下，已经付过了。\n\n@event tobias_gerald_approves\n# en: Gerald Approves\ntitle [d674c6] = 杰拉尔德认可了\n# en: Tobias holds Gerald up to your face. Gerald is wearing {~a new hat|his winter hat}. \"He's deciding about you,\" Tobias says seriously. \"He doesn't like everyone. He bit the Verdant Circle's treasurer.\" Gerald considers you with his small dark eyes.\ntext [1a7d23] = 托比亚斯把杰拉尔德举到你面前。杰拉尔德戴着{~一顶新帽子|他的冬帽}。“他在考虑你，”托比亚斯严肃地说，“他不是谁都喜欢。他咬过青翠社的司库。”杰拉尔德用一双小小的黑眼睛打量着你。\n# en: A large gentle student holding a potted mandrake in a tiny knitted hat up to another student's face, both very solemn.\nscene [52fade] = 一个高大温和的学生把一盆戴着小毛线帽的曼德拉草举到另一个学生面前，两人都十分庄重。\n# en: Bow to Gerald\nopt.left.label [eec4bd] = 向杰拉尔德鞠躬\n# en: {~You bow. Gerald screams very softly, which Tobias says is approval. Tobias looks at you as if you'd done something wonderful. You think maybe you have.|You bow, and Gerald pats your nose with a leaf. Tobias goes pink. \"He hardly ever does that,\" he says. \"Not even to me.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [b0af47] = {~你鞠了一躬。杰拉尔德非常轻地尖叫了一声，托比亚斯说这表示认可。他看着你，好像你做了件了不起的事。也许你确实做了。|你鞠了一躬，杰拉尔德用一片叶子拍了拍你的鼻子。托比亚斯脸红了。“他很少这样，”他说，“对我都很少。”}\n# en: Offer Gerald a finger\nopt.right.label [4a5386] = 伸一根手指给他\n# en: {~Gerald bites it. Tobias is mortified and laughs so hard he has to sit down. You're both still laughing when the greenhouse bell goes.|Gerald bites it, then lets go and looks sorry. \"He's shy,\" says Tobias. Your finger disagrees.}\nopt.right.say.1 [73864d] = {~杰拉尔德咬了上去。托比亚斯窘得要命，又笑得不得不坐下。温室的钟响时，你们俩还在笑。|杰拉尔德咬了一口，又松开，一副很抱歉的样子。“他害羞。”托比亚斯说。你的手指不同意。}\n\n@event tobias_letter_aloud\n# en: Read It to Me\ntitle [e78a93] = 念给我听\n# en: Tobias has a letter from home and he hasn't opened it. \"My eyes are tired,\" he says, which isn't true. \"Would you read it? Out loud?\" It's from his mother. She writes the way people talk on farms: weather first, goats second, love last and briefly.\ntext [5569ff] = 托比亚斯收到一封家信，还没拆。“我眼睛累了，”他说，这不是真的，“你能念吗？念出声？”信是他母亲写的。她写信的方式就像农场上的人说话：先说天气，再说山羊，爱放在最后，只一句。\n# en: A large student sitting on a bench handing another a letter on farm paper, his eyes red-rimmed and tired.\nscene [73d0bd] = 一个高大的学生坐在长凳上，把一封写在农场信纸上的信递给另一个人，眼圈发红，满脸疲惫。\n# en: Read it, voices and all\nopt.left.label [6c58df] = 念，连语气一起\n# en: You read it in his mother's voice, as near as you can guess. He laughs at the goats and goes quiet at the end. \"She says she's proud,\" he says. \"She never says that.\" You read that line twice, because he asks.\nopt.left.say.1 [d337d5] = 你尽量猜着他母亲的口气念。念到山羊他笑了，念到结尾他安静了。“她说她为我骄傲，”他说，“她从来不说这个。”那一句你念了两遍，因为他要你念。\n# en: Read it plainly, then talk\nopt.right.label [3ea1f4] = 平平地念，再聊聊\n# en: You read it plainly. Then he talks about home for an hour, the farm, the frost, his mother alone at harvest, and you listen. It's a lot to carry. You carry some of it back with you.\nopt.right.say.1 [bf92db] = 你平平地念完了。然后他讲了一个钟头的家：农场、霜、他母亲独自收割。你听着。这些很沉。你帮他带回了一些。\n# en: When he gets to coming home after graduation, you ask what he wants, not what was planned. He doesn't know yet. He says nobody has asked him before.\nopt.right.say.2 [f4871b] = 读到毕业后回家那一段，你问他想要什么，而不是原本计划好的是什么。他还不知道。他说以前从没有人问过他。\n\n@event tobias_umbrella\n# en: One Umbrella\ntitle [e864cd] = 一把伞\n# en: It's pouring, and Tobias has the biggest umbrella you've ever seen: patched, green, and older than both of you. {~\"Ma's,\" he says. \"It's seen worse.\"|\"Room for two,\" he says, \"or four, if they're small.\"}\ntext [44b417] = 大雨倾盆，托比亚斯有一把你见过的最大的伞：打着补丁，绿色的，比你们俩加起来还老。{~“我妈的，”他说，“它见过更糟的。”|“能挤两个人，”他说，“要是个子小，能挤四个。”}\n# en: Two students walking close under one enormous patched umbrella across a rainy castle courtyard, one much bigger than the other.\nscene [68b8fa] = 两个学生挤在一把巨大的补丁伞下穿过下雨的城堡庭院，一个比另一个高大得多。\n# en: Walk close under it\nopt.left.label [351a83] = 挤在伞下走\n# en: {~You walk back under it, very close, because the umbrella insists, and your outside shoulder is soaked through by the door anyway. He tells you a long story about a goat that fell in a well, and you don't hear the end of it, and don't mind.|Halfway across the courtyard he stops talking. So do you. The rain does the talking, and runs down the back of your neck.}\nopt.left.say.1 [384881] = {~你们挤在伞下走回去，挨得很近，因为伞执意如此，可到门口时你靠外的那边肩膀还是湿透了。他讲了一个山羊掉进井里的长故事，结尾你没听见，也不在乎。|走到庭院一半他不说话了。你也没说。雨替你们说，还顺着你的后颈往下淌。}\n# en: Race him to the door\nopt.right.label [d816e0] = 跟他赛跑到门口\n# en: {~You run. He runs after you with the umbrella and arrives soaked anyway, laughing from the belly.|You make it first, drenched. He arrives dry and very pleased with himself.}\nopt.right.say.1 [020d6d] = {~你跑了起来。他打着伞追你，到了还是湿透了，笑得肚子直颤。|你先到了，浑身湿透。他到的时候一身干爽，得意极了。}\n\n@event tobias_seed_month\n# en: A Seed a Month\ntitle [f09a24] = 每月一颗种子\n# en: Tobias gives you one seed a month, every month, in a twist of paper with its name on it. Your windowsill is crowded now. {~This month's is a sunflower. \"For when it's grey,\" he says.|This month's is a herb you've never heard of. \"For your cough,\" he says. You don't have a cough. \"Yet,\" he says.}\ntext [a221eb] = 托比亚斯每个月给你一颗种子，每个月都给，包在一个写着名字的纸卷里。你的窗台已经挤满了。{~这个月是向日葵。“阴天的时候用。”他说。|这个月是一种你从没听说过的草药。“治你的咳嗽。”他说。你没咳嗽。“还没。”他说。}\n# en: A large student pressing a single seed into a pot on a windowsill crowded with sprouting pots, another student watching.\nscene [b60a91] = 一个高大的学生把一颗种子按进窗台上的花盆里，窗台挤满了发芽的小盆，另一个学生在旁边看着。\n# en: Plant it together\nopt.left.label [e4538b] = 一起种下\n# en: {~You plant it together, his big hands over yours, pressing the soil down gently. He waters it. You'll remember this month by it.|He shows you how deep, how much water, where the light falls. The windowsill is a calendar now.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6a75bb] = {~你们一起种下它，他的大手覆在你手上，轻轻压实土。他浇了水。你会靠它记住这个月。|他教你种多深、浇多少水、光从哪儿照进来。窗台现在是一本日历了。}\n# en: Give him one back\nopt.right.label [354d55] = 回送他一颗\n# en: {~You buy a seed in the village and give it to him in a twist of paper. He holds it as if it were a jewel, and plants it that day.|You give him a seed back. He laughs from the belly and plants it in his best pot.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9f214f] = {~你在镇上买了一颗种子，包在纸卷里送他。他捧着它像捧着一颗宝石，当天就种下了。|你回送他一颗种子。他笑得肚子直颤，把它种进了他最好的花盆里。}\n\n@event tobias_home_or_here\n# en: Home or Here\ntitle [1957bb] = 回家，还是留下\n# en: Tobias has been quiet all week. Finally: \"Ma wants me home after. The farm. It's always been the plan.\" He doesn't look at you. \"I always thought I wanted that. I'm sorry. I'm sorry for saying it like this. I'm sorry.\"\ntext [5855ce] = 托比亚斯一整个星期都很安静。终于他开口：“我妈想让我毕业后回家。回农场。一直都是这么打算的。”他不看你。“我一直以为我想要那样。对不起。对不起，这么说出来。对不起。”\n# en: A large student sitting on a farm gate in a dream-like vision, then on a castle wall, torn between the two.\nscene [4ca207] = 一个高大的学生在梦境般的画面里坐在农场的栅栏门上，又坐在城堡的墙头，在两者之间左右为难。\n# en: \"Then I'll come and see the goats.\"\nopt.left.label [ba5ecb] = “那我去看山羊。”\n# en: He looks up so fast he nearly falls off the wall. \"You'd like the goats,\" he says. \"They'd like you.\" Neither of you knows what comes after. It's enough, for now, that you'd come.\nopt.left.say.1 [e92bfa] = 他猛地抬头，差点从墙上掉下去。“你会喜欢那些山羊的，”他说，“它们也会喜欢你。”你们谁都不知道之后会怎样。眼下，你愿意去，这就够了。\n# en: \"Do you still want it?\"\nopt.right.label [a987cf] = “你现在还想要吗？”\n# en: He thinks for a long time, the way he thinks about seeds. \"I don't know,\" he says at last. \"I didn't know I was allowed not to know.\" You sit with him until he stops apologising.\nopt.right.say.1 [36a49b] = 他想了很久，就像他琢磨种子那样。“我不知道，”他终于说，“我不知道我可以不知道。”你陪他坐着，直到他不再道歉。\n\n@event tobias_gerald_wilts\n# en: Gerald Is Ill\ntitle [79d6f3] = 杰拉尔德病了\n# en: Gerald is wilting. His hat has slipped, his leaves are grey, and he hasn't screamed in two days. Tobias is sitting on the greenhouse floor holding the pot, and he isn't apologising, which is how you know it's bad.\ntext [0a20fa] = 杰拉尔德蔫了。帽子滑到一边，叶子发灰，两天没叫一声。托比亚斯坐在温室地上抱着花盆，没有道歉——你就是从这一点看出事情严重了。\n# en: A large student cradling a drooping potted mandrake in a knitted hat, sitting on a greenhouse floor, another student kneeling beside him.\nscene [c1883c] = 一个高大的学生坐在温室地上，怀里抱着一盆戴毛线帽、垂头丧气的曼德拉草，另一个学生跪在他身边。\n# en: Brew him a tonic\nopt.left.label [edae25] = 给他熬一剂补药\n# en: You brew all night. At dawn Gerald drinks it, shudders, and screams, loudly, furiously, beautifully. Tobias laughs and cries at once and holds you so hard your ribs creak.\nopt.left.say.1 [2cab30] = 你熬了一整夜。黎明时杰拉尔德喝下去，打了个哆嗦，然后尖叫起来——响亮、愤怒、美妙极了。托比亚斯又哭又笑，把你抱得肋骨咯吱作响。\n# en: The tonic does nothing. But in the morning Gerald is a little greener anyway, maybe because Tobias didn't sleep and neither did you. \"It's not your fault,\" Tobias says. \"It's nobody's. You stayed.\"\nopt.left.say.2 [f78e5e] = 补药没起作用。可第二天早上杰拉尔德还是绿了一点，也许是因为托比亚斯没睡，你也没睡。“不是你的错，”托比亚斯说，“谁的错都不是。你留下来了。”\n# en: Sit with him all night\nopt.right.label [cb261a] = 陪他坐一整夜\n# en: You sit on the greenhouse floor all night, Tobias holding Gerald, you holding Tobias. At dawn Gerald stirs, and screams, very softly. It's the best sound you've ever heard.\nopt.right.say.1 [605705] = 你在温室地上坐了一整夜，托比亚斯抱着杰拉尔德，你抱着托比亚斯。黎明时杰拉尔德动了动，非常轻地尖叫了一声。那是你听过最好听的声音。\n\n@event tobias_ex\n# en: He Still Waters Your Plant\ntitle [9d492e] = 他还在给你的花浇水\n# en: You find Tobias at your window, watering the plant on your sill. He jumps. \"Sorry,\" he says. \"Sorry. It looked dry. I'll go. I'm sorry.\"\ntext [da1753] = 你发现托比亚斯在你窗前，给你窗台上的植物浇水。他吓了一跳。“对不起，”他说，“对不起。它看着有点干。我这就走。对不起。”\n# en: A large student watering a single pot on a windowsill that isn't his, glancing guiltily at the door.\nscene [183bc5] = 一个高大的学生在不属于他的窗台前给一盆花浇水，心虚地瞥向门口。\n# en: Let him go\nopt.left.label [75e63a] = 让他走\n# en: He goes. The plant is very well watered. You don't know whether to be grateful or sad, so you're both.\nopt.left.say.1 [525790] = 他走了。那盆植物被浇得足足的。你不知道该感激还是难过，于是两样都有。\n# en: Ask him to stay a minute\nopt.right.label [07b9a3] = 请他再待一会儿\n# en: He stays. You talk about the plant, and then about goats, and it's almost easy. \"Friends,\" he says, at the door, testing the word, \"is a good word. I like it.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [31388f] = 他留下了。你们聊那盆植物，又聊山羊，几乎是轻松的。“朋友，”他在门口说，试着念这个词，“是个好词。我喜欢。”\n# en: He stays, and stands, and apologises four times, and goes. The plant turns toward the door after him.\nopt.right.say.2 [b5bd8a] = 他留下了，站着，道了四次歉，走了。那盆植物朝着门口转过去，跟着他。\n\n@event tobias_dance_specialized\n# en: The Broom Waltz, Again\ntitle [be6251] = 又是扫帚华尔兹\n# en: Tobias has been practising again, he admits, with the broom. \"It complained,\" he says. \"But I'm better now. I think. Will you?\" He holds out a hand the size of a spade, very carefully.\ntext [1aa5ca] = 托比亚斯承认，他又拿扫帚练过了。“它抱怨了，”他说，“不过我现在好些了。我想是吧。你愿意吗？”他伸出一只铁锹那么大的手，非常小心。\n# en: A large student counting steps under his breath in a moonlit greenhouse, guiding another student between rows of plants.\nscene [83793a] = 月光下的温室里，一个高大的学生低声数着舞步，引着另一个学生在一排排植物间穿行。\n# en: Dance with him\nopt.left.label [faa28e] = 和他跳舞\n# en: He's better. He steps on your foot only twice, and apologises five times, and between the apologies he's a wonderful dancer, slow and sure, like a tide.\nopt.left.say.1 [687404] = 他确实好些了。只踩了你的脚两次，道了五次歉，而在道歉之间，他是个很棒的舞伴，缓慢而稳当，像潮水。\n# en: Teach him a new step\nopt.right.label [ea5169] = 教他一个新舞步\n# en: You teach him a step from the Midwinter Ball. He gets it on the ninth try and is so proud he dances it alone, between the tomato plants, until the gardener throws him out.\nopt.right.say.1 [59867f] = 你教他一个仲冬舞会上的舞步。他第九次学会了，得意得在番茄架之间独自跳起来，直到园丁把他赶出去。\n\n@event tobias_friend_sick\n# en: Too Big for the Bed\ntitle [e13df4] = 床太小了\n# en: Tobias has Lake Fever, and the infirmary bed is a foot too short for him. His feet stick out at the end under a blanket. He isn't worried about himself. \"Gerald,\" he croaks. \"Who's watering Gerald?\"\ntext [44a27e] = 托比亚斯得了湖热病，医务室的床比他短一英尺。他的脚从毯子底下伸出床尾。他不担心自己。“杰拉尔德，”他哑着嗓子说，“谁给杰拉尔德浇水？”\n# en: A very large student squeezed into a small infirmary bed, feet hanging off the end, a potted mandrake on the bedside table.\nscene [341399] = 一个非常高大的学生挤在医务室的小床上，脚伸出床尾，床头柜上放着一盆曼德拉草。\n# en: Send a card, and a promise\nopt.left.label [44b1dd] = 寄张卡片，许个承诺\n# en: You send a card with a drawing of Gerald in a sickbed hat. Tobias keeps it propped against the water jug and shows it to Sister Amaranth twice a day.\nopt.left.say.1 [f92992] = 你寄了张卡片，上面画着戴病号帽的杰拉尔德。托比亚斯把它靠在水壶上，一天给阿玛兰斯修女看两遍。\n# en: Water Gerald, and sit with him\nopt.right.label [3b6f2c] = 给杰拉尔德浇水，陪着他\n# en: You water Gerald, then sit with Tobias every evening until he's better. Then you're in the next bed. Tobias waters Gerald for both of you, apologising to everyone.\nopt.right.say.1 [d3220c] = 你给杰拉尔德浇水，然后每晚陪着托比亚斯，直到他好起来。然后你躺进了隔壁那张床。托比亚斯替你们俩给杰拉尔德浇水，见谁都道歉。\n# en: {~You bring Gerald to the infirmary in his pot. Amaranth objects. Gerald screams at her. Tobias recovers mostly from laughing.|You water Gerald every morning and report on him every evening, leaf by leaf. Tobias gets better, he says, out of relief.|You read him farming almanacs aloud until he falls asleep, which takes four pages. He recovers in three days.}\nopt.right.say.2 [3e178f] = {~你把杰拉尔德连盆带到医务室。阿玛兰斯反对。杰拉尔德冲她尖叫。托比亚斯主要是笑好的。|你每天早上给杰拉尔德浇水，每晚汇报它的情况，一片叶子一片叶子地讲。托比亚斯说他是放下心来才好的。|你给他念农事历书，念到第四页他就睡着了。三天后他就好了。}\n\n@event lyra_teach_spell\n# en: A Trick Worth Learning\ntitle [8a5e67] = 值得一学的小把戏\n# en: Lyra doesn't invent little spells. \"Half-useful is useless,\" she says. But she'll teach you the flourish her family has used to open a duel for two hundred years, if you swear not to tell anyone she taught it to someone outside the family. She looks as if she might regret the offer.\ntext [9d32e0] = 莱拉不发明小咒语。“半有用就是没用。”她说。不过她愿意教你她家两百年来决斗开场用的起手式，前提是你发誓不告诉任何人她教给了外人。她看上去随时可能后悔。\n# en: A duelling hall after hours, a red-haired girl correcting another student's wand arm with two fingers, a scorched practice target behind them.\nscene [fb3422] = 下课后的决斗厅，一个红发女孩用两根手指纠正另一个学生持杖的手臂，身后是一个烧焦的练习靶。\n# en: Learn it her way\nopt.left.label [0555fe] = 照她的方法学\n# en: She corrects your wrist eleven times. On the twelfth the flourish leaves a ribbon of flame in the air, exactly where it should be. Lyra nods once. \"Adequate,\" she says, which from her is a medal.\nopt.left.say.1 [5c3f46] = 她纠正了你的手腕十一次。第十二次，起手式在空中留下一道火焰缎带，分毫不差。莱拉点了一下头。“还行。”她说——从她嘴里说出来，这是一枚勋章。\n# en: The ribbon of flame goes where it shouldn't, which is your sleeve. Lyra puts it out with a flick and doesn't laugh, which is worse. \"Again,\" she says. The second time it works.\nopt.left.say.2 [0d5525] = 火焰缎带飞到了不该去的地方，也就是你的袖子上。莱拉一弹指把火灭了，没笑，这更糟。“再来。”她说。第二次成功了。\n# en: Teach her to make toast\nopt.right.label [7c8623] = 教她烤面包\n# en: You hand her a slice of bread and a toasting fork. She looks at it the way she looks at a duelling opponent. Four slices are cinders. The fifth is golden. She eats it standing up, in silence, and then says, \"Don't tell anyone that either.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [a12bca] = 你递给她一片面包和一把烤叉。她看着它，就像看一个决斗对手。四片烤成了炭。第五片金黄。她站着默默吃完，然后说：“这件事也别告诉任何人。”\n# en: You offer to trade: her flourish for something useful, like toast. She goes very still. \"I can incinerate a target at forty paces,\" she says. \"I cannot make toast.\" By the fifth slice she can. She makes you swear about that too.\nopt.right.say.2 [171e99] = 你提议交换：她的起手式换个有用的东西，比如烤面包。她一下子僵住了。“我能在四十步外把靶子烧成灰，”她说，“我不会烤面包。”到第五片她会了。她让你也为这件事发了誓。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/romance/seraphine_corvin_mir.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/romance/seraphine_corvin_mir.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event seraphine_stand_next\n# en: Stand Next to Me\ntitle [da37c9] = 站在我旁边\n# en: Seraphine pulls you in front of the long mirror in the east corridor. \"Stand there,\" she says. \"No, closer.\" {~She stares at her own reflection, as if at a stranger.|She looks at herself, then at you, then at herself, and lets out a breath.} \"Seers can't see themselves,\" she says. \"Not ahead. Not unless someone's standing next to them.\"\ntext [fa12e5] = 瑟拉芬把你拉到东走廊那面长镜子前。“站那儿，”她说，“不对，再近点。”{~她盯着镜中的自己，像在看一个陌生人。|她看看自己，又看看你，再看看自己，长长地舒了口气。}“预言者看不见自己，”她说，“看不见往后的自己。除非有人站在旁边。”\n# en: A girl in a paint-stained smock standing before a tall mirror, pulling another student by the sleeve to stand beside her.\nscene [e9ba0f] = 一个穿着沾满颜料罩衫的女孩站在高大的镜子前，拽着另一个学生的袖子让对方站到自己身边。\n# en: Stay as long as she needs\nopt.left.label [760ac1] = 她要多久就陪多久\n# en: {~You stand there until the candles gutter. She looks at herself the whole time, very seriously, and when you finally go, she says, \"Thank you. I was in it. A long way on, and I was in it.\"|You stand beside her a long time. At the end she reaches out and touches the mirror where your shoulders almost meet. \"There,\" she says. \"That's where I'll be.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [51b24b] = {~你一直站到蜡烛快燃尽。她始终很认真地看着自己，等你终于要走时，她说：“谢谢。我在里面。很远很远以后，我在里面。”|你在她身边站了很久。最后她伸出手，摸了摸镜面上你们肩膀几乎相碰的地方。“就是这儿，”她说，“我以后会在这儿。”}\n# en: Ask what she sees\nopt.right.label [229a32] = 问她看见了什么\n# en: {~\"A girl,\" she says. \"Next to someone.\" She doesn't elaborate. She lets go of your sleeve.|\"Half a sentence,\" she says, and laughs, and won't finish it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d164ef] = {~“一个女孩，”她说，“站在某人旁边。”她没有多说，松开了你的袖子。|“半句话。”她说着笑了，却不肯说完。}\n\n@event seraphine_tomorrow_answer\n# en: Tomorrow's Answer\ntitle [6d976a] = 明天的答案\n# en: \"Yes,\" Seraphine says, as you pass her in the corridor. You haven't said anything. \"Oh,\" she says. \"You haven't asked yet. Tomorrow, then.\" She looks embarrassed, which is rare. \"I slip, sometimes. A day ahead. Someone has to tell me what today is.\"\ntext [846be6] = 你在走廊里经过瑟拉芬时，她说：“好。”你什么都还没说。“哦，”她说，“你还没问呢。那就明天吧。”她露出难得的窘态。“我有时会滑出去。提前一天。得有人告诉我今天是哪天。”\n# en: A seer answering a question nobody has asked yet, in a corridor, the other student frozen mid-word.\nscene [a01256] = 走廊里，一个预言者回答了一个还没人问的问题，另一个学生话说到一半愣住了。\n# en: Tell her what today is\nopt.left.label [aad3f8] = 告诉她今天是哪天\n# en: You tell her: the date, the weather, what's for lunch, what she did yesterday. She listens like it's poetry. \"Thursday,\" she says. \"I like Thursday. I'll stay.\" You wonder what you're going to ask her tomorrow.\nopt.left.say.1 [ece441] = 你告诉她：日期、天气、午饭吃什么、她昨天做了什么。她听得像在听诗。“星期四，”她说，“我喜欢星期四。我留下。”你开始好奇自己明天会问她什么。\n# en: Ask what you'll ask\nopt.right.label [136bb9] = 问你会问什么\n# en: \"If I tell you,\" she says, \"you won't ask it, and then I'll have answered nothing.\" She drifts off down the corridor, a day ahead of everyone.\nopt.right.say.1 [fe8c77] = “我要是告诉你，”她说，“你就不会问了，那我就什么都没回答。”她沿着走廊飘走了，比所有人都早一天。\n\n@event seraphine_dark_room\n# en: The Dark Room\ntitle [d689fb] = 暗室\n# en: The storeroom is a habit now. On loud days Seraphine goes there, and lately she's moved her paints in. You find her in the dark, sorting colours by touch. \"Loud again,\" she says. \"Yours is still quiet. Help? You hand me one, I guess it.\"\ntext [ab6efd] = 那间储藏室成了习惯。吵的日子里瑟拉芬就去那儿，最近她把颜料也搬了进去。你发现她在黑暗里凭手感给颜色分类。“又吵了，”她说，“你的还是很安静。帮个忙？你递我一罐，我来猜。”\n# en: A storeroom with no windows, a girl sitting on the floor in the dark among pots of paint, a crack of light from the door falling across her face.\nscene [3e522f] = 没有窗户的储藏室，一个女孩坐在黑暗的地板上，周围是一罐罐颜料，门缝透进的一线光落在她脸上。\n# en: Sort paints in the dark\nopt.left.label [6c0a12] = 在黑暗里分颜料\n# en: {~You hand her pots in the dark for an hour. She names each by its smell: ochre, viridian, a blue she says tastes of Tuesdays. She gets eleven of twelve. \"You're the quietest person I know,\" she says. It sounds like the best compliment she has.|You sort paints by feel until the bell. Your notes don't get written. At some point she leans her head on your shoulder, and the pot in her hand is the right colour, and she doesn't explain.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5175ee] = {~你在黑暗里一罐一罐递给她，递了一个钟头。她凭气味叫出每一种：赭石、铬绿、一种她说尝起来像星期二的蓝。十二罐她猜中了十一罐。“你是我认识的最安静的人，”她说。听上去这是她能给的最高赞美。|你们凭手感分颜料，一直分到钟响。笔记没写成。不知什么时候她把头靠在了你肩上，她手里那罐恰好是对的颜色，她也没解释。}\n# en: Bring her a candle\nopt.right.label [835924] = 给她拿支蜡烛\n# en: {~You bring a single candle and set it by the door. \"That's better,\" she says. \"One small light. It doesn't shout.\"|You bring a candle. She watches it as if it were a friend, and the noise goes away, she says, a little.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e48328] = {~你拿来一支蜡烛，放在门边。“好多了，”她说，“一小点光。它不嚷嚷。”|你拿来一支蜡烛。她看着它，像看着一个朋友。她说，那些噪声退去了，一点点。}\n\n@event seraphine_already_happened\n# en: She Tells You the Ending\ntitle [8077e4] = 她把结局告诉你\n# en: You read together now, the two of you, one book between you in the window seat. Seraphine always knows the ending. {~Today she's trying very hard not to say it.|Today she's covering the last page with her hand.} \"Don't look ahead,\" she says. \"It's better not knowing. I'm told.\"\ntext [c3342c] = 现在你们俩一起读书，窗台上，一本书摊在两人中间。瑟拉芬总是知道结局。{~今天她在拼命忍着不说。|今天她用手捂着最后一页。}“别往后看，”她说，“不知道更好。别人是这么告诉我的。”\n# en: Two people curled in a window seat with one book between them, one of them covering the page with her hand.\nscene [8e2b8f] = 两个人蜷在窗台上，中间一本书，其中一个用手捂着书页。\n# en: Read it slowly, together\nopt.left.label [aa3f5e] = 一起慢慢读\n# en: {~You read aloud, slowly, one page at a time. She doesn't say the ending. At the end she says, \"I was wrong. It ends happier than I saw.\" She is always glad to be wrong about endings.|You read and she listens, and twice she starts to say something and stops. When you finish, she's smiling. \"I didn't know,\" she says. \"I didn't let myself.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [0430d9] = {~你出声慢慢地读，一次一页。她没说结局。读完时她说：“我错了。结局比我看见的要快乐。”她总是乐于在结局上猜错。|你读，她听，有两次她想说什么又停住了。读完时她在微笑。“我不知道，”她说，“我没让自己知道。”}\n# en: Ask her to tell you\nopt.right.label [fb782a] = 让她告诉你\n# en: {~She tells you the ending, delighted, in half a sentence. You finish the other half together, wrong, and laugh.|She tells you. It's terrible. You read it anyway, and she was right, and you both throw the book across the room.}\nopt.right.say.1 [11b507] = {~她兴高采烈地用半句话告诉你结局。另外半句你们一起补完，补错了，笑成一团。|她告诉你了。结局很糟。你们还是读了，她说得没错，你们俩一起把书扔到了房间另一头。}\n\n@event seraphine_dont_look\n# en: Don't Look Ahead\ntitle [0be495] = 别往前看\n# en: You find a painting under Seraphine's bed: the two of you, older, somewhere you've never been. \"I didn't mean to look,\" she says. \"It just came. I'm sorry. I know you didn't want to know.\"\ntext [768895] = 你在瑟拉芬床底下发现一幅画：你们俩，年纪大些，在一个你从没去过的地方。“我不是故意看的，”她说，“它自己来的。对不起。我知道你不想知道。”\n# en: A seer with paint on her hands looking guilty, another student holding up a canvas that shows them both, older.\nscene [9e1e95] = 一个手上沾着颜料的预言者一脸内疚，另一个学生举着一幅画布，画的是年纪大些的她们俩。\n# en: \"Tell me about it, then.\"\nopt.left.label [a10cc9] = “那就讲给我听。”\n# en: She tells you, half a sentence at a time: a house by water, a dog with one ear up, a garden that does badly. \"That's all,\" she says. \"That's all I let myself see.\" You hang the painting where you'll both see it.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f1dee] = 她一次半句地讲给你听：水边的一座房子，一只一边耳朵竖着的狗，一个打理得不太好的花园。“就这些，”她说，“我只让自己看了这些。”你把画挂在你们俩都看得见的地方。\n# en: \"Don't look again. Promise.\"\nopt.right.label [f7844b] = “别再看了。答应我。”\n# en: She promises. You can see what it costs her: it's like asking someone not to hear. But she keeps it. The painting goes back under the bed, face down.\nopt.right.say.1 [d23781] = 她答应了。你看得出这让她付出了什么：就像让一个人别去听。可她守住了承诺。画回到了床底下，正面朝下。\n\n@event seraphine_vision_hurt\n# en: She Won't Let You Go\ntitle [22a3ce] = 她不让你走\n# en: Seraphine catches your wrist in the doorway and won't let go. Her face is white. \"Not today,\" she says. \"Don't go out today. I saw it. I saw you hurt.\" She can't say how, or where. Her hands are shaking.\ntext [b5e766] = 瑟拉芬在门口抓住你的手腕，不肯放开。她脸色煞白。“今天不行，”她说，“今天别出去。我看见了。我看见你受伤。”她说不出是怎么受的伤，在哪里。她的手在发抖。\n# en: A girl in a paint-stained smock gripping another student's wrist in a doorway, her face white, refusing to let go.\nscene [e5885e] = 门口，一个穿着沾满颜料罩衫的女孩紧紧抓着另一个学生的手腕，脸色煞白，不肯放手。\n# en: Stay in, for her\nopt.left.label [8c1731] = 为了她，待在屋里\n# en: You stay in. Nothing happens, all day. At sunset she lets go of your wrist and sits down, very suddenly, and cries, and doesn't explain whether it was because you stayed or because it was never going to happen. You'll never know. She says that's the point.\nopt.left.say.1 [7ace93] = 你待在屋里。一整天什么都没发生。日落时她松开你的手腕，忽然坐下哭了，也没解释是因为你留下了，还是因为那件事本来就不会发生。你永远不会知道。她说，这正是关键。\n# en: Go, and come back to prove it\nopt.right.label [05abcf] = 出门，再回来证明给她看\n# en: You go. You come back with a twisted ankle from a stair that wasn't where it should be. Seraphine looks at it and laughs, horribly relieved. \"That's all?\" she says. \"That's all it was?\" She bandages it herself, shaking.\nopt.right.say.1 [edcaf2] = 你出去了。回来时扭了脚踝——一级台阶不在它该在的地方。瑟拉芬看着伤处笑了，如释重负得可怕。“就这样？”她说，“就只是这样？”她亲手给你包扎，手还在抖。\n\n@event seraphine_ex\n# en: She Painted the Ending\ntitle [9ad74a] = 她画下了结局\n# en: Seraphine stops you in the corridor and gives you a small canvas. It's the two of you, walking away from each other down a long hall, both looking back. \"I painted it last spring,\" she says. \"Before. I'm sorry. I knew.\"\ntext [fa8132] = 瑟拉芬在走廊里拦住你，递给你一幅小画布。画的是你们俩，在一条长廊里背向而行，都在回头。“我去年春天画的，”她说，“在那之前。对不起。我早就知道。”\n# en: A girl in a paint-stained smock handing a small canvas to a former lover in a corridor, both careful not to touch hands.\nscene [f19952] = 走廊里，一个穿着沾满颜料罩衫的女孩把一幅小画递给昔日的恋人，两人都小心地不碰到对方的手。\n# en: Take it, and go\nopt.left.label [b89852] = 收下，离开\n# en: You take it. You don't hang it. You don't throw it away, either. Some nights you look at it, the two figures looking back.\nopt.left.say.1 [cf90d0] = 你收下了。你没挂起来，也没扔掉。有些夜里你会看着它，看那两个回头的人影。\n# en: Ask if she painted after\nopt.right.label [27164c] = 问她有没有画之后\n# en: \"After?\" She smiles, for the first time in a while. \"Two people having tea. Arguing about a book. I don't know when.\" She tucks the painting under your arm. \"Friends, I think. Yes.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [b9db22] = “之后？”她笑了，这是好一阵子以来的第一次。“两个人在喝茶。为一本书争论。我不知道是什么时候。”她把画塞到你胳膊底下。“朋友吧，我想。嗯。”\n# en: \"I don't look after,\" she says. \"Not with you. Not anymore.\" She walks away, a day ahead, already gone.\nopt.right.say.2 [3d0906] = “我不看之后，”她说，“对你不看。再也不看了。”她走了，早你一天，已经不在了。\n\n@event seraphine_partner_neglect\n# en: You Were in a Different Week\ntitle [98faba] = 你在另一周\n# en: Seraphine is on the window seat with two cold cups of tea. \"I waited,\" she says. \"I thought you were coming on Tuesday. Then I thought I'd slipped a day. Then I thought you'd slipped a week.\" She almost smiles. \"It's hard to tell, with you, lately.\"\ntext [abce4e] = 瑟拉芬坐在窗台上，身边两杯凉掉的茶。“我等了，”她说，“我以为你星期二会来。后来我以为是我滑出去了一天。再后来我以为是你滑出去了一周。”她几乎在笑。“最近，跟你在一起，很难分清。”\n# en: A seer waiting on a window seat with two cups of tea gone cold, looking up as another student finally arrives.\nscene [179799] = 一个预言者坐在窗台上，身边两杯已经凉了的茶，抬头看着终于到来的另一个学生。\n# en: Sit, and stay all evening\nopt.left.label [ba943e] = 坐下，陪她一整晚\n# en: You sit with her and drink the cold tea. She tells you the date three times, to be sure. You stay until the candle's out. It's Thursday. She's sure of it now.\nopt.left.say.1 [84dbba] = 你坐下陪她喝凉茶。她把日期说了三遍，确认无误。你一直待到蜡烛燃尽。是星期四。她现在确定了。\n# en: \"I'm here now, isn't that enough?\"\nopt.right.label [03be51] = “我现在来了，还不够吗？”\n# en: \"Now,\" she says, as if it were a word in a foreign language. She pours out the cold tea and goes, a day ahead of you.\nopt.right.say.1 [23ce16] = “现在。”她说，仿佛这是一个外语单词。她倒掉凉茶，走了，早你一天。\n\n@event seraphine_confess_generic\n# en: She Already Knew\ntitle [e91972] = 她早就知道\n# en: You've rehearsed this. You start to say it. Seraphine holds up her sketchbook: it's this moment, the two of you, drawn weeks ago. \"I know,\" she says gently. \"I've known since spring. I just didn't know what I'd say.\"\ntext [fb1e68] = 你排练过。你刚开口，瑟拉芬就举起她的速写本：画的正是这一刻，你们俩，几周前画的。“我知道，”她温柔地说，“我从春天就知道了。我只是不知道自己会说什么。”\n# en: A seer listening to a confession with a small patient smile, a sketchbook open on her knee showing the very scene.\nscene [7ab8a7] = 一个预言者带着耐心的浅笑听着表白，膝上摊开的速写本上画的正是这一幕。\n# en: \"Well? What do you say?\"\nopt.left.label [346e14] = “那么？你怎么说？”\n# en: She closes the sketchbook. \"I didn't let myself look,\" she says. \"I wanted to hear it.\" Then she kisses you, and for once, she's as surprised as anyone.\nopt.left.say.1 [9c6c55] = 她合上速写本。“我没让自己去看，”她说，“我想听你说出来。”然后她吻了你，而这一次，她和所有人一样吃惊。\n# en: Let it rest, for now\nopt.right.label [ac46ae] = 先放一放\n# en: \"Then let's not,\" she says, with relief or disappointment, you can't tell. She tears the drawing out and gives it to you. \"For later,\" she says. \"If there's a later.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [f30883] = “那就先不说吧。”她说，是松了口气还是失望，你分辨不出。她把那页画撕下来给你。“留着以后，”她说，“如果有以后。”\n\n@event corvin_lucky_deck\n# en: The Lucky Deck\ntitle [680635] = 幸运牌\n# en: Corvin deals you a hand from an old deck, soft at the corners and smelling of pipe smoke. {~\"Grandfather's,\" he says. \"It really is lucky. Watch.\"|\"Cut,\" he says. \"Any card. It'll be the queen. It's always the queen.\"} You win, which shouldn't be possible against Corvin.\ntext [4cc8e5] = 科尔文用一副旧牌给你发了一手，牌角都磨软了，带着烟斗的味道。{~“我祖父的，”他说，“真的很幸运。你看着。”|“切牌，”他说，“随便一张。会是王后。永远是王后。”}你赢了，跟科尔文打牌，这本不该发生。\n# en: A lanky student shuffling an old, soft-cornered deck of cards with impossible speed, grinning across a dormitory table.\nscene [188bdd] = 一个瘦高的学生以不可思议的速度洗着一副牌角磨软的旧牌，隔着宿舍的桌子咧嘴笑。\n# en: Ask about his grandfather\nopt.left.label [e2d597] = 问起他的祖父\n# en: {~The jokes stop. He tells you about a man who could find a lost thing in any house, and who taught him to shuffle on rainy Sundays. \"He'd have liked you, saint,\" he says. \"He liked people who lost honestly.\"|He talks for an hour, and none of it is a joke. Then he deals again, and cheats outrageously, so you'll know he's fine.}\nopt.left.say.1 [52ef2c] = {~玩笑停了。他给你讲一个能在任何房子里找到丢失物件的人，在下雨的星期天教他洗牌。“他会喜欢你的，圣人，”他说，“他喜欢输得光明磊落的人。”|他讲了一个钟头，没有一句是玩笑。然后他重新发牌，明目张胆地出千，好让你知道他没事。}\n# en: Play for real stakes\nopt.right.label [70fa5b] = 玩真格的\n# en: {~You play for coppers. You win, because the deck likes you. Corvin is delighted and furious and asks for a rematch.|You play until midnight and come out four coppers up. \"Beginner's luck,\" he says. \"It's the deck. It's cheating for you.\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [885ad9] = {~你们赌铜板。你赢了，因为这副牌喜欢你。科尔文又高兴又气恼，要求再来一局。|你们一直玩到午夜，你赢了四个铜板。“新手运，”他说，“是这副牌。它在替你出千。”}\n\n@event corvin_last_warning\n# en: Last Warning\ntitle [433b1d] = 最后警告\n# en: Corvin is sitting on the stairs with a letter with a red seal. For once he isn't grinning. \"Last warning,\" he says. \"One more detention and they send me home.\" He folds it very small. \"Home isn't a good place, saint. Don't ask.\"\ntext [92fea8] = 科尔文坐在楼梯上，手里一封盖着红色火漆的信。他难得没在笑。“最后警告，”他说，“再关一次禁闭，他们就送我回家。”他把信折得很小。“家不是个好地方，圣人。别问。”\n# en: A lanky student sitting on a staircase holding an official letter with a red seal, no grin at all.\nscene [90d790] = 一个瘦高的学生坐在楼梯上，手里拿着一封盖红色火漆的正式信函，脸上一点笑意都没有。\n# en: Promise to keep him out of trouble\nopt.left.label [3b792c] = 答应帮他别惹麻烦\n# en: \"You?\" he says. \"Keep me out of trouble?\" But he looks at you a long time, and the grin comes back, smaller and realer. \"Fine. Deal. You're my conscience now. Terrible job. No pay.\" That night you lie awake counting his detentions.\nopt.left.say.1 [211b91] = “你？”他说，“让我别惹麻烦？”可他看了你很久，那笑又回来了，小一些，真一些。“行。成交。现在你是我的良心了。差事很烂。没工钱。”那天夜里你睁着眼睛，数他的禁闭次数。\n# en: Tell him to stop being stupid\nopt.right.label [3fb53f] = 叫他别犯傻\n# en: \"Wow,\" he says. \"Thanks, saint.\" But he doesn't pick a single lock all month. You know, because you check.\nopt.right.say.1 [9f1512] = “哇，”他说，“谢了，圣人。”可他整整一个月一把锁都没撬。你知道，因为你查过。\n\n@event corvin_route_named\n# en: A Route Named After You\ntitle [468555] = 以你命名的路线\n# en: Corvin unrolls a map of the castle's passages on your floor. One route is in green ink, new. {~\"Found it last week,\" he says. \"Kitchens to the roof, no stairs.\"|\"Library to the lake,\" he says, \"in four minutes, if you don't mind a ladder.\"} At the bottom, he's labelled it with your name. \"Every route needs a name,\" he says, not looking at you.\ntext [14d956] = 科尔文在你的地板上铺开一张城堡密道图。有一条路线是新画的，用的绿墨水。{~“上周发现的，”他说，“厨房到屋顶，不用爬楼梯。”|“图书馆到湖边，”他说，“四分钟，如果你不介意爬梯子。”}底下，他用你的名字给它标了名。“每条路线都得有个名字。”他说，不看你。\n# en: A lanky student unrolling a hand-drawn map of the castle's hidden passages on a dormitory floor, one route inked in a different colour.\nscene [697bca] = 一个瘦高的学生在宿舍地板上铺开一张手绘的城堡密道图，有一条路线用不同颜色的墨水画出。\n# en: Walk it with him tonight\nopt.left.label [6290f4] = 今晚和他走一遍\n# en: {~You walk it at midnight. It comes out on the roof, under more stars than you knew the castle had. Corvin doesn't say anything clever. He doesn't need to.|You walk the whole route, bumping shoulders in the narrow parts. At the end he says, \"Well? Is it yours?\" It is.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f6b2d4] = {~你们午夜去走了。路线通到屋顶，头顶的星星比你知道的城堡上空能有的还多。科尔文没说什么俏皮话。他不需要。|你们走完了整条路线，在窄处肩膀撞着肩膀。走到尽头他说：“怎么样？是你的吗？”是的。}\n# en: Ask why your name\nopt.right.label [7800e2] = 问为什么用你的名字\n# en: {~\"Because,\" he says, and then nothing, which from Corvin is a whole speech. He rolls the map up very carefully.|\"It's a good name for a route,\" he says. \"Doesn't give anything away.\" He means the opposite.}\nopt.right.say.1 [32de09] = {~“因为。”他说，然后就没了——对科尔文来说，这就是一整篇演讲。他非常小心地把地图卷了起来。|“这名字适合当路线名，”他说，“什么都不透露。”他的意思恰恰相反。}\n\n@event corvin_goat_clause\n# en: The Goat Clause\ntitle [c548c8] = 山羊条款\n# en: Corvin needs fifteen crowns. He's drawn up a contract, very official, with a clause at the bottom: \"In the event of non-payment, the lender shall receive one (1) goat.\" He's drawn the goat. It's a good goat.\ntext [1f4d50] = 科尔文需要十五个克朗。他起草了一份合同，非常正式，底下有一条：“如未能偿还，出借人将获得山羊一（1）只。”他把山羊画上了。是只好山羊。\n# en: A grinning student holding out a scrawled contract with a drawing of a goat at the bottom, an empty purse in his other hand.\nscene [891e2e] = 一个咧嘴笑的学生递出一份潦草的合同，底下画着一只山羊，另一只手里是空钱袋。\n# en: Lend it\nopt.left.label [c384a5] = 借给他\n# en: You sign. He swears he'll pay you back in three weeks, which would be a personal record, and tells you the goat story anyway, because you asked about the clause. The trolls still have it. It runs them now.\nopt.left.say.1 [511863] = 你签了字。他发誓三周内还钱，那会是他的个人纪录。因为你问起那条条款，他还是把山羊的故事讲了。山羊还在巨魔那儿。现在是它在管着巨魔。\n# en: Refuse, for the goat's sake\nopt.right.label [aa80d4] = 为了山羊，拒绝\n# en: \"The goat deserves better,\" you say. He laughs so hard he has to sit down. Then he finds the fifteen crowns somewhere else, and you decide not to ask where.\nopt.right.say.1 [87bd8a] = “那只山羊值得更好的。”你说。他笑得不得不坐下。后来他从别处弄到了十五个克朗，你决定不问是哪儿。\n\n@event corvin_lock_left_open\n# en: A Lock Left Open\ntitle [fc8f54] = 留着的一把锁\n# en: You've a tradition now: once a month Corvin leaves a lock open somewhere in the castle, a door that's always shut, and a note on it. {~Tonight it's the clock tower: \"Up. Bring cake.\"|Tonight it's the music room: \"Play me something bad.\"} You have to find it first.\ntext [998967] = 你们现在有个传统：每个月，科尔文在城堡某处留一把锁开着——一扇总是锁着的门——门上留张字条。{~今晚是钟楼：“上来。带蛋糕。”|今晚是音乐室：“给我弹点难听的。”}你得先找到它。\n# en: A door in a castle corridor standing slightly ajar at midnight, a note pinned to it in a messy hand.\nscene [3f6b5e] = 午夜的城堡走廊里，一扇门虚掩着，门上钉着一张字迹潦草的字条。\n# en: Find it, follow it\nopt.left.label [1f3c53] = 找到它，跟上去\n# en: {~You find it at midnight. He's waiting at the top with a blanket and a cake he definitely didn't buy. You eat it watching the clock's hands move from the wrong side.|You find the door. He's inside, in the dark, playing a terrible tune on a borrowed piano, just for you.}\nopt.left.say.1 [052765] = {~午夜你找到了。他在顶上等着，带着一条毯子和一个肯定不是买来的蛋糕。你们边吃边从背面看着钟的指针走动。|你找到那扇门。他在里面，在黑暗中，用一架借来的钢琴弹一支难听的曲子，只弹给你听。}\n# en: Leave a lock open for him\nopt.right.label [57ef7b] = 也给他留一把锁\n# en: {~You pick a lock, badly, and leave a note: \"Your turn.\" He finds it in ten minutes and is so proud of you he can't speak.|You leave the observatory door open with a note. He finds it, and you, and says you're a terrible influence.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2c4e39] = {~你撬了一把锁，撬得很笨，留了张字条：“轮到你了。”他十分钟就找到了，骄傲得说不出话。|你把观星台的门留着，附上一张字条。他找到了门，也找到了你，说你是个坏影响。}\n\n@event corvin_honest_once\n# en: Honest for Once\ntitle [ee4b92] = 难得坦白\n# en: Corvin has been joking all evening, and it's getting thin. Something's wrong. Every time you ask, he makes another joke, quicker. \"It's nothing, saint,\" he says. \"Nothing's ever anything. That's the trick.\"\ntext [26697f] = 科尔文整晚都在开玩笑，玩笑越来越薄。有事不对劲。你每问一次，他就更快地再抛出一个笑话。“没什么，圣人，”他说，“什么事都不算事。诀窍就在这儿。”\n# en: A lanky student turning away from another, joking with his back turned, his shoulders tense.\nscene [1afbc7] = 一个瘦高的学生转过身去，背对着另一个人开着玩笑，肩膀紧绷。\n# en: Make him tell you\nopt.left.label [9c7bb7] = 让他说出来\n# en: He deflects; get past the jokes.\nopt.left.hint [278d90] = 他在打岔；越过那些玩笑。\n# en: You don't let him off. Eventually the jokes run out, and he tells you: his brother wrote. Wants money. Threatened to come. You sit with him while he says it all, plainly, no jokes. {seen(corvin_brother) > 0: \"You knew already,\" he says. \"I still had to say it out loud.\"|\"That's the first time I've said that to anyone,\" he says.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1f2d56] = 你不放过他。玩笑终于用完了，他告诉你：他哥哥写信来了。要钱。威胁说要来。你陪着他把话说完，平平实实，没有玩笑。{seen(corvin_brother) > 0: “你早就知道了，”他说，“可我还是得亲口说出来。”|“这是我第一次跟别人说这些。”他说。}\n# en: Every question gets a better joke. You end up laughing despite yourself, and he looks relieved and sad at once, and you know you lost.\nopt.left.say.2 [9ea63f] = 每个问题都换来一个更好的笑话。最后你自己也忍不住笑了，他看上去既松了口气又很难过，你知道你输了。\n# en: Let him joke\nopt.right.label [9fc7f3] = 由他开玩笑\n# en: You let him joke. You laugh at the good ones. At the end of the night he says, very quietly, \"Thanks for not making me.\" It's honest. For once.\nopt.right.say.1 [3ab341] = 你由着他开玩笑，好笑的你就笑。夜深时他很轻地说：“谢谢你没逼我。”这是句实话。难得一次。\n\n@event corvin_brother_again\n# en: The Brother, Again\ntitle [e3f2c6] = 又是他哥哥\n# en: Corvin's brother is at the castle gate again. This time he isn't asking Corvin for money. He's looking at you. \"So this is who you've been spending it on,\" he says. Corvin steps in front of you without a joke anywhere in him.\ntext [22ef2b] = 科尔文的哥哥又出现在城堡大门口。这次他不是来找科尔文要钱的。他在看你。“原来钱都花在这人身上了。”他说。科尔文挡到你前面，浑身上下没有一丝玩笑。\n# en: A tough older man in a worn coat looming at the castle gate, a lanky student standing in front of another student protectively.\nscene [725064] = 一个穿旧外套的强壮男人堵在城堡大门口，一个瘦高的学生护在另一个学生身前。\n# en: Stand beside Corvin, not behind\nopt.left.label [9ffa73] = 站到科尔文身边，不是身后\n# en: You step up beside him. The brother looks at the two of you a long time, then spits, and goes. Corvin shakes for an hour afterwards. {chosen(corvin_brother, left): \"Twice now,\" he says. \"You keep standing next to me.\"|\"Nobody's ever done that,\" he says. \"Stood next to me.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [351b85] = 你走上前，站到他身边。那个哥哥盯着你们俩看了很久，啐了一口，走了。科尔文之后抖了一个钟头。{chosen(corvin_brother, left): “第二次了，”他说，“你总是站在我旁边。”|“从没有人这样做过，”他说，“站在我旁边。”}\n# en: Pay him to go\nopt.right.label [af717a] = 给钱让他走\n# en: You pay the brother what he asks, which is a great deal. He goes. Corvin is furious with you, and grateful, and furious about being grateful. \"Never again,\" he says. \"Promise me.\" You promise. You'd do it again.\nopt.right.say.1 [4f2cb2] = 你把那个哥哥要的钱给了他，数目很大。他走了。科尔文对你又气又感激，又因为感激而更气。“下不为例，”他说，“答应我。”你答应了。你还会再这么做。\n\n@event corvin_ex\n# en: New Locks\ntitle [6ad24d] = 新锁\n# en: Corvin is changing the lock on his door. It's a very good lock. \"Don't take it personally, saint,\" he says, with a grin that stops before his eyes. \"Old habits. Somebody once had a key.\"\ntext [57a77b] = 科尔文在换他门上的锁。是把非常好的锁。“别往心里去，圣人，”他说，笑意没到眼睛，“老习惯了。以前有人有钥匙。”\n# en: A lanky student kneeling at a dormitory door fitting a new lock, looking up at his ex with a grin that doesn't reach his eyes.\nscene [704f16] = 一个瘦高的学生跪在宿舍门前装一把新锁，抬头看着他的前任，笑意没到眼睛。\n# en: Leave him to it\nopt.left.label [b84f2c] = 由他去\n# en: You leave him to it. You hear the lock click behind you, very final, and very well made.\nopt.left.say.1 [28dabb] = 你由他去了。你听见身后锁咔嗒一声，非常决绝，做工也非常好。\n# en: \"It's a good lock.\"\nopt.right.label [ad0899] = “这锁不错。”\n# en: He looks up. Then he laughs, a real one. \"It's the best lock in the castle,\" he says. \"I'll show you how it works. You'll never get in.\" It's an invitation, of a kind.\nopt.right.say.1 [dcf11f] = 他抬起头，然后笑了，是真的笑。“这是城堡里最好的锁，”他说，“我教你它怎么用。你永远进不来。”这算是一种邀请。\n# en: \"It is,\" he says. \"Best I've got.\" He turns back to it. That's the end of the conversation.\nopt.right.say.2 [22b0a6] = “是不错，”他说，“我手头最好的。”他转回去继续干活。谈话到此为止。\n\n@event corvin_partner_date\n# en: Supper on Someone Else's Roof\ntitle [7340bc] = 别人屋顶上的晚餐\n# en: Corvin's idea of a date is supper on a roof you're not allowed on, made of food from a kitchen you're not allowed in. \"It's not stealing,\" he says. \"It's redistribution. The cook knows. She likes me.\"\ntext [108212] = 科尔文心目中的约会，是在一个你们不准上的屋顶上，吃一顿从一个你们不准进的厨房里弄来的晚餐。“这不叫偷，”他说，“这叫再分配。厨娘知道。她喜欢我。”\n# en: Two people sitting on a high castle roof with a stolen picnic between them, the kitchens' chimney smoking behind them.\nscene [6d270e] = 两个人坐在城堡高处的屋顶上，中间摆着偷来的野餐，身后厨房的烟囱冒着烟。\n# en: Climb up\nopt.left.label [2cbf33] = 爬上去\n# en: You eat stolen pie on the roof and watch the lake go dark. He points out every window he's ever climbed through. There are a lot.\nopt.left.say.1 [9810a0] = 你们在屋顶上吃偷来的馅饼，看湖面一点点暗下去。他把他爬过的每一扇窗户都指给你看。有很多。\n# en: Bring the cook along\nopt.right.label [77b691] = 把厨娘也叫上\n# en: You talk the cook into joining you. She climbs the roof better than either of you and brings better pie. Corvin is thrilled and slightly outclassed.\nopt.right.say.1 [6a0426] = 你说动厨娘一起来。她爬屋顶比你们俩都利索，带来的馅饼也更好。科尔文兴奋极了，又有点被比下去。\n\n@event corvin_saint_jibe\n# en: \"Saint\"\ntitle [136318] = “圣人”\n# en: Corvin leans across the doorway with one arm. \"Here comes the saint,\" he says, loud enough for the corridor, and then, lower, grinning: \"Does it get heavy? Being better than me? Asking for a friend. The friend is me.\"\ntext [c4a1d8] = 科尔文用一只胳膊横在门口。“圣人来了，”他说，声音大得整条走廊都听得见，然后压低声音，咧嘴笑着，“累不累啊？比我好？替一个朋友问的。那个朋友就是我。”\n# en: A lanky student leaning in a doorway with one arm across it, grinning, while another student stands in front of him with an armful of books.\nscene [06d8b7] = 一个瘦高的学生倚在门口，一只胳膊横挡着，咧嘴笑着，另一个学生抱着一摞书站在他面前。\n# en: \"It does, actually.\"\nopt.left.label [cb6e44] = “其实挺累的。”\n# en: The grin goes. He looks at you properly for a second, then takes half your books without asking and carries them to the library. \"There,\" he says. \"Lighter.\" He doesn't call you saint again that week.\nopt.left.say.1 [5c43d3] = 笑容没了。他认真地看了你一秒，然后二话不说接过你一半的书，帮你搬到图书馆。“喏，”他说，“轻点了。”那一整周他都没再叫你圣人。\n# en: \"You'd know, sinner.\"\nopt.right.label [9411cd] = “你最清楚了，罪人。”\n# en: He laughs so hard he has to let go of the doorway. \"Sinner,\" he repeats, delighted, as if you've given him a title. By dinner he's signing his notes with it.\nopt.right.say.1 [9cfc1e] = 他笑得扶不住门框。“罪人，”他高兴地重复着，好像你封了他一个头衔。到晚饭时，他的字条都用这个署名了。\n\n@event mir_owl_remembers\n# en: The Owl Remembers\ntitle [14b806] = 猫头鹰记得\n# en: Mir's clockwork owl lands on your shoulder and hoots. {~\"Reminder,\" it says, in Mir's voice. \"Birthday. Yours. In nine days. Do not tell them I remembered.\"|\"Reminder,\" it says. \"Lunch. With them. Wear the green coat.\" Mir arrives, scarlet, and grabs it.} Mir is mortified. \"It's not supposed to say the part in brackets,\" they say. \"Probably. It's a feature.\"\ntext [e6300d] = 米尔的发条猫头鹰落在你肩上，咕咕叫了一声。{~“提醒，”它用米尔的声音说，“生日。你的。九天后。别告诉TA我记得。”|“提醒，”它说，“午饭。和TA一起。穿那件绿外套。”米尔满脸通红地冲过来一把抓住它。}米尔窘得要命。“它不该把括号里的话说出来的，”TA说，“大概吧。这是个功能。”\n# en: A clockwork owl landing on a student's shoulder and hooting a reminder, its creator running after it in goggles.\nscene [daba38] = 一只发条猫头鹰落在一个学生肩上咕咕地报着提醒，它的制造者戴着护目镜追在后面。\n# en: Ask what else it remembers\nopt.left.label [a351d6] = 问它还记得什么\n# en: {~Mir tells you, very fast: deadlines, birthdays, and, slightly, grudges. \"And you,\" the owl adds, unhelpfully. Mir puts it in a bag. The bag hoots your name.|\"Everything important,\" says Mir, and then hears themself, and goes red to the goggles.}\nopt.left.say.1 [15dbdf] = {~米尔飞快地告诉你：截止日期、生日，还有一点点，记仇。“还有你。”猫头鹰帮倒忙地补充。米尔把它塞进袋子。袋子里咕咕地叫着你的名字。|“所有重要的事。”米尔说，然后听见了自己说了什么，脸一直红到护目镜。}\n# en: Offer to oil its wings\nopt.right.label [d62e2c] = 帮它给翅膀上油\n# en: {~You oil its wings while Mir explains every gear, with exclamation marks. By the end the owl prefers you, which Mir says is a betrayal and a compliment.|You oil the wings and the owl hoots a perfect C. Mir writes it down: \"they fixed the pitch!!!\"}\nopt.right.say.1 [dde1ad] = {~你给它的翅膀上油，米尔把每个齿轮都讲了一遍，句句带感叹号。到最后猫头鹰更喜欢你了，米尔说这既是背叛也是夸奖。|你给翅膀上了油，猫头鹰咕地叫出一个标准的C音。米尔记下来：“TA把音准修好了！！！”}\n\n@event mir_notebooks\n# en: The Notebooks\ntitle [b73d42] = 笔记本\n# en: Mir holds out a notebook, one of a stack, battered and bristling with slips of paper. \"Three years,\" they say. \"Every idea. Some of them work. Probably. Nobody's read them. You can, if you want. Carefully. Very carefully. With clean hands.\"\ntext [261868] = 米尔递出一本笔记本，是一摞里的一本，破旧，夹满了纸条。“三年，”TA说，“每一个点子。有些能用。大概吧。没人读过。你想读的话可以读。小心点。非常小心。手要干净。”\n# en: An artificer holding out a battered notebook bristling with paper slips, hesitating, to another student in a workshop.\nscene [80f382] = 工坊里，一个奇械师犹豫着把一本夹满纸条的破旧笔记本递给另一个学生。\n# en: Read it with them\nopt.left.label [425240] = 和TA一起读\n# en: You read it at the workbench with Mir hovering, explaining every page before you get to it. Halfway through there's a sketch of you, labelled \"test subject (willing?)\". Mir pretends it isn't there. You pretend too, badly.\nopt.left.say.1 [153ea8] = 你在工作台前读，米尔在旁边转来转去，每一页都抢在你读到之前先解释。读到一半有一张你的素描，标注着“实验对象（自愿？）”。米尔假装它不存在。你也假装，装得很差。\n# en: Ask which idea is their favourite\nopt.right.label [278e49] = 问TA最喜欢哪个点子\n# en: They flip to a page with a drawing of a tiny lantern that remembers who lit it. \"This one,\" they say. \"It doesn't work yet. It will.\" They don't let go of the notebook for the rest of the evening.\nopt.right.say.1 [76cb8f] = TA翻到一页，上面画着一盏能记住是谁点亮它的小灯。“这个，”TA说，“它现在还不能用。会能用的。”那一整晚TA都没放下那本笔记本。\n\n@event mir_blueprints\n# en: Blueprints\ntitle [bbdb09] = 蓝图\n# en: Mir has drawn a blueprint for something for you. {~\"A lamp that turns itself off when you fall asleep reading,\" they say. \"Probably.\"|\"A pocket that's bigger inside,\" they say. \"In theory. In practice, it eats keys.\"} They don't say why they keep making things for you. They hold the blueprint like a confession.\ntext [74e193] = 米尔给你画了一张蓝图。{~“一盏你看书睡着时会自己熄灭的灯，”TA说，“大概吧。”|“一个里面比外面大的口袋，”TA说，“理论上。实际上，它会吃钥匙。”}TA不说为什么总给你做东西。TA捧着蓝图，像捧着一份告白。\n# en: An artificer pinning a blueprint to a workshop wall covered in drawings, all of them labelled with another student's name.\nscene [a1c3b7] = 一个奇械师把一张蓝图钉到工坊墙上，墙上贴满的图纸都标着另一个学生的名字。\n# en: Help build it\nopt.left.label [ff55ff] = 帮忙造出来\n# en: {~You build it together, Mir talking the whole time, both of you singed at the fingertips. It works. Mostly. Mir hugs it, then you, then looks horrified at themself.|You build it until midnight. It doesn't work. Mir says that's the best part: now you have to build it again, together.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9bd0bd] = {~你们一起把它造出来，米尔全程说个不停，两人的指尖都被燎了。它能用。基本能用。米尔抱了抱它，又抱了抱你，然后被自己吓了一跳。|你们一直造到午夜。它不能用。米尔说这才是最好的部分：现在你们得一起再造一次。}\n# en: Pin it up, for later\nopt.right.label [4d2c5f] = 先钉起来，以后再说\n# en: {~You pin it on your wall, next to your timetable, and go back to the essay it interrupted. Mir sees it there the next time they visit and goes very quiet and very pleased.|\"Later,\" you say, and go back to your revision. Mir nods fast. \"Later is good. Later is a plan.\" They say \"later\" several times on the way out.}\nopt.right.say.1 [59716d] = {~你把它钉在墙上，就在课表旁边，然后回去写被它打断的论文。米尔下次来时看见了，变得非常安静，也非常高兴。|“以后吧。”你说着回去复习。米尔飞快地点头。“以后很好。以后是个计划。”出门的路上TA把“以后”说了好几遍。}\n\n@event mir_tinkering_evenings\n# en: Tinkering Evenings\ntitle [152cc3] = 修修补补的夜晚\n# en: Thursdays are tinkering evenings now. Mir works; you sit on the bench with your feet up, reading or helping or just there. {~Tonight it's a music box that plays backwards.|Tonight it's a kettle that refuses to boil unless asked nicely.} The owl is asleep on a pile of bolts.\ntext [3cfe9f] = 现在星期四是修修补补的夜晚。米尔干活，你把脚搁在长凳上，看书、帮忙，或者只是待着。{~今晚是一个倒着放的音乐盒。|今晚是一把不客气地问它就不肯烧开的水壶。}猫头鹰在一堆螺栓上睡着了。\n# en: Two people at a crowded workbench late at night, one soldering, one reading aloud, a clockwork owl asleep between them.\nscene [9f7a0e] = 深夜里，两个人挤在堆满东西的工作台前，一个在焊接，一个在念书，一只发条猫头鹰睡在两人中间。\n# en: Hand them the tools\nopt.left.label [c9fb16] = 给TA递工具\n# en: {~You fetch, hold, steady. The music box plays a waltz backwards and you dance to it, badly, among the bolts.|You hand over each tool before they ask. By midnight you know what they need before they do. \"You're the best assistant,\" Mir says, \"and the worst distraction.\" They mean both kindly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [42488e] = {~你递东西、扶着、稳住。音乐盒倒着放起一支华尔兹，你们在螺栓堆里跳了一曲，跳得很糟。|TA还没开口，你就把每样工具递了过去。到午夜，你比TA自己还先知道TA要什么。“你是最好的助手，”米尔说，“也是最大的干扰。”两句都是好意。}\n# en: Read aloud while they work\nopt.right.label [f36138] = TA干活时你念书\n# en: {~You read aloud. Mir works slower, listening, and doesn't seem to mind.|You read them a terrible novel. They laugh at the wrong parts and burn a finger. The kettle boils, eventually, because you asked it nicely.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c75285] = {~你念出声。米尔干得慢了，在听，看样子并不介意。|你给TA念一本糟糕的小说。TA在不该笑的地方笑，还烫了一根手指。水壶终于烧开了，因为你客气地问了它。}\n\n@event mir_not_a_hobby\n# en: Not a Hobby\ntitle [249789] = 不是爱好\n# en: You said it lightly: \"your hobby\". Mir has gone very still. \"My family calls it that,\" they say. \"A hobby. Something to grow out of. I didn't think you would.\" They're packing their tools away, carefully, which is how you know they're furious.\ntext [f44f4d] = 你只是随口一说：“你的爱好”。米尔一下子僵住了。“我家里人就这么叫它，”TA说，“爱好。长大了就会丢掉的东西。我没想到你也会。”TA在收拾工具，非常仔细——你就是从这一点看出TA有多生气。\n# en: An artificer furiously packing tools into a case in a workshop, another student standing in the doorway.\nscene [334dc0] = 工坊里，一个奇械师愤怒地把工具收进箱子，另一个学生站在门口。\n# en: Take it back, properly\nopt.left.label [464f6b] = 收回那句话，好好地\n# en: You take it back, not with \"sorry\" but with every thing they've ever made, one by one, and what each one means. By the fifth they've stopped packing. By the tenth they're crying into their goggles. \"It's not a hobby,\" they say. \"It's not,\" you agree.\nopt.left.say.1 [f6c4e6] = 你收回了那句话，不是靠“对不起”，而是把TA做过的每一样东西一件件说出来，说清每一件意味着什么。说到第五件，TA不收拾了。说到第十件，TA的眼泪流进了护目镜。“这不是爱好。”TA说。“不是。”你同意。\n# en: Ask them to show you what it is, then\nopt.right.label [867319] = 那就请TA给你看看它是什么\n# en: They stop packing. Then they show you, for three hours, everything, and it's not a hobby. It's a whole life. You never use the word again.\nopt.right.say.1 [93b1c4] = TA停下不收拾了。然后TA给你看了三个钟头，所有的一切，它不是爱好。它是一整个人生。你再也没用过那个词。\n\n@event mir_family_summons\n# en: The Family Wants to Meet You\ntitle [1d5594] = 家里想见见你\n# en: A letter from Mir's family, on company paper. They have heard there is someone. They would like to meet them, at the clockworks, over the next break. Mir is sitting on the workshop floor, very small among their machines. \"You don't have to,\" they say. \"They'll ask about your prospects. And your hobbies.\"\ntext [63d581] = 米尔家里来了封信，用的是公司信纸。他们听说有那么一个人了。他们想见见，在钟表作坊，下个假期。米尔坐在工坊地上，在一堆机器中间显得很小。“你不用去的，”TA说，“他们会问你的前途。还有你的爱好。”\n# en: An artificer holding a formal letter on clockworks-company paper, sitting on the floor of a workshop among half-finished machines, looking up at someone.\nscene [c52a31] = 一个奇械师拿着一封钟表公司信纸写的正式信函，坐在工坊地上，四周是做了一半的机器，抬头看着某个人。\n# en: Go and meet them\nopt.left.label [4ccb85] = 去见他们\n# en: You go. Mir's mother asks about your prospects, twice. Their father shows you every clock in the building, and you ask about every one. On the barge back Mir holds your hand the whole way. \"He showed you the escapement,\" they say. \"He never shows anyone the escapement.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [91bb26] = 你去了。米尔的母亲问了你的前途，问了两遍。TA父亲领你看了楼里每一只钟，你每一只都问了。回程的驳船上，米尔一路握着你的手。“他给你看了擒纵机构，”TA说，“他从不给任何人看擒纵机构。”\n# en: Write to them first\nopt.right.label [530ec3] = 先给他们写信\n# en: \"Not yet,\" you say. \"Let me write first.\" You write a careful letter to two people you've never met. Mir reads it over your shoulder and doesn't correct a single thing, which, for Mir, is a declaration.\nopt.right.say.1 [36bdb4] = “还不是时候，”你说，“让我先写封信。”你给两个从没见过的人写了一封很用心的信。米尔在你肩后读完，一个字都没改，对米尔来说，这就是一种表白。\n\n@event mir_ex\n# en: The Owl Holds a Grudge\ntitle [eb0a21] = 猫头鹰记仇\n# en: Mir's clockwork owl sees you across the courtyard, lands on a gargoyle and glares. \"Grudge,\" it says, in Mir's voice. \"Ongoing.\" Mir, chasing it, sees you, and stops. \"It's not me,\" they say. \"It's the owl. Probably.\"\ntext [4a69cf] = 米尔的发条猫头鹰隔着庭院看见了你，落到一座石像鬼上瞪着你。“记仇，”它用米尔的声音说，“进行中。”追过来的米尔看见你，停住了。“不是我，”TA说，“是猫头鹰。大概吧。”\n# en: A clockwork owl glaring from a high shelf at a former partner of its maker, ruffling its brass feathers.\nscene [c9edb4] = 一只发条猫头鹰在高高的架子上瞪着它主人的前任，抖着黄铜羽毛。\n# en: Leave them to the owl\nopt.left.label [613796] = 把TA留给猫头鹰\n# en: You go. The owl follows you to the library, hooting \"grudge\" at intervals, until Mir catches it.\nopt.left.say.1 [7cbac4] = 你走了。猫头鹰一路跟你到图书馆，隔一会儿就咕一声“记仇”，直到米尔把它抓住。\n# en: Offer the owl a gear\nopt.right.label [3d230a] = 给猫头鹰一个齿轮\n# en: The owl takes the gear and considers it. Then it hoots, quite differently. Mir laughs, surprised. \"That's 'acquaintance, improving',\" they say. \"I didn't know it had that one. I didn't build it in.\" Maybe it built itself.\nopt.right.say.1 [c8b134] = 猫头鹰接过齿轮，琢磨了一下。然后它叫了一声，跟之前完全不同。米尔惊讶地笑了。“那是‘熟人，关系改善中’，”TA说，“我不知道它还有这个。我没装这个。”也许是它自己长出来的。\n# en: The owl takes the gear and drops it on your head. \"Grudge,\" it says. \"Ongoing.\" Mir apologises and doesn't.\nopt.right.say.2 [495441] = 猫头鹰接过齿轮，丢在你头上。“记仇，”它说，“进行中。”米尔道了歉，又没怎么道歉。\n\n@event mir_gift_given\n# en: A Gift With Gears\ntitle [b31fc6] = 带齿轮的礼物\n# en: You give Mir a gift. They turn it over three times, then hold it to their ear, then take their goggles off to look at it properly. \"It doesn't do anything,\" they say, wondering. \"It's just... for me?\"\ntext [62e881] = 你送了米尔一件礼物。TA翻来覆去看了三遍，又凑到耳边听，最后摘下护目镜好好端详。“它什么都不做，”TA惊奇地说，“它只是……给我的？”\n# en: An artificer turning a small gift over and over in their hands, peering at it through goggles as if checking for gears.\nscene [cab933] = 一个奇械师把一件小礼物在手里翻来覆去，透过护目镜仔细查看，像在找里面的齿轮。\n# en: \"It's just for you.\"\nopt.left.label [800918] = “只是给你的。”\n# en: They hold it very carefully for the rest of the day, as if it might break, or tick. The next morning there's a small brass gear on your pillow. It doesn't do anything either.\nopt.left.say.1 [fca876] = 那一整天TA都捧得小心翼翼，好像它会碎，或者会滴答作响。第二天早上，你枕头上有一个小小的黄铜齿轮。它也什么都不做。\n# en: \"It could do something, if you made it.\"\nopt.right.label [5a9298] = “你要是改一改，它能做点什么。”\n# en: Their eyes light. By Friday it hums, glows, and plays one note when you're nearby. It's not what you gave them. It's better.\nopt.right.say.1 [63c8d4] = TA眼睛亮了。到星期五，它会嗡嗡响、会发光，你一靠近就奏一个音。它已经不是你送的那样东西了。它更好。\n\n@event mir_birthday\n# en: Not a Birthday\ntitle [820036] = 不是生日\n# en: You find out it's Mir's birthday, and turn up at the workshop with a cake. Mir looks at the candle, puzzled. \"We don't do birthdays at home,\" they say. \"We do name days. Nobody does mine, but that's the one we do.\"\ntext [920d17] = 你得知今天是米尔的生日，带着蛋糕来到工坊。米尔看着蜡烛，一脸困惑。“我们家不过生日，”TA说，“我们过命名日。没人给我过，但我们家过的是那个。”\n# en: An artificer at a cluttered workbench, goggles pushed up, looking blankly at a small cake with a single candle set among the springs.\nscene [97ea64] = 一个奇械师坐在杂乱的工作台前，护目镜推到额头上，茫然地看着弹簧堆里一块插着一支蜡烛的小蛋糕。\n# en: Light the candle anyway\nopt.left.label [9c4c32] = 还是点上蜡烛\n# en: \"Two in a year,\" Mir says, as you light it. \"That's excessive.\" They blow it out, very carefully, as if it might be a fuse, and then cut the cake with a pair of calipers into exactly equal slices.\nopt.left.say.1 [12fddf] = “一年两次，”你点蜡烛时米尔说，“太过分了。”TA小心翼翼地吹灭它，好像那是根引信，然后用卡尺把蛋糕切成分毫不差的几块。\n# en: You light it anyway. Mir watches the flame for a while, then blows it out with great precision. \"It's a stupid custom,\" they say, and eat three slices, and save the candle in a drawer of springs.\nopt.left.say.2 [86c13f] = 你还是点上了。米尔盯着火苗看了一会儿，然后极其精准地吹灭了它。“这风俗真蠢。”TA说，吃了三块，把蜡烛收进了一抽屉弹簧里。\n# en: Ask when the name day is\nopt.right.label [806bca] = 问TA命名日是哪天\n# en: They tell you, then make you write it down, then check you wrote it down correctly. \"You won't remember,\" they say. \"People don't.\" They say it lightly. They watch you put the note in your pocket.\nopt.right.say.1 [288f0e] = TA告诉你，然后让你写下来，再检查你写得对不对。“你不会记得的，”TA说，“没人记得。”TA说得很轻巧。TA看着你把字条放进口袋。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/scandals.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/scandals.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event scandal_ledger_lie\n# en: The Dragon, Checked\ntitle [06d544] = 那条龙，查证了\n# en: Somebody has checked the Ledger's profile of you, the one with the better version of yourself in it. A boxed item on page two lists, very politely, the four things in it that never happened, with dates. The refectory has read it. Everybody is waiting to see what you'll say.\ntext [f17335] = 有人查证了《纪事报》对你的那篇专访，就是那篇写了一个更好的你的。第二版上一个方框里，非常客气地列出了其中四件从没发生过的事，还附上了日期。膳堂里的人都读过了。大家都在等你怎么说。\n# en: A newspaper spread out on a refectory table, a small boxed headline circled in red ink, a ring of students reading over each other's shoulders.\nscene [93ba95] = 膳堂桌上摊开一份报纸，一个小方框标题被红墨水圈了出来，一圈学生你挤我我挤你地凑着看。\n# en: Own every word of it\nopt.own.label [5b53d2] = 每个字都认下来\n# en: You write to the Ledger: yes, you embellished; yes, it was vanity; no, there was no dragon. Nessa prints it in full. People are unkind about it for a week, and then, slowly, they start to trust what you say again.\nopt.own.say.1 [6364ba] = 你写信给《纪事报》：是的，你添油加醋了；是的，那是虚荣；不，根本没有龙。内莎全文照登。人们刻薄了一个星期，然后，慢慢地，又开始相信你说的话了。\n# en: Insist it was all true\nopt.deny.label [118a9c] = 坚称全是真的\n# en: You insist, calmly and in detail, that every word was true, and you tell it so well that the boxed item starts to look petty. By Friday people have decided the checker had a grudge. You don't feel as good about it as you'd hoped.\nopt.deny.say.1 [d7af2d] = 你冷静而详细地坚称每个字都是真的，而且讲得那么好，让那个方框显得小家子气。到周五，人们认定查证的人是在报私仇。你并没有想象中那么痛快。\n# en: You insist it was all true, and the checker produces a fifth thing, the one you'd forgotten. It's in the next issue, with your denial printed beside it. Nessa doesn't speak to you for a fortnight.\nopt.deny.say.2 [4c04e9] = 你坚称全是真的，查证的人又拿出了第五件事，那件你自己都忘了的。它登在了下一期，旁边印着你的否认。内莎两个星期没跟你说话。\n# en: Say Nessa twisted your words\nopt.spin.label [acfa9a] = 说内莎歪曲了你的话\n# en: You tell people the Ledger must have muddled your interview. It works, mostly: the story becomes one about sloppy journalism. Nessa reads it in silence, folds the paper very small, and doesn't ask you for another interview.\nopt.spin.say.1 [d72057] = 你告诉别人，肯定是《纪事报》把你的采访弄混了。这招大体管用：故事变成了一个关于草率新闻的故事。内莎默默读完，把报纸折得很小，再也没请你做过采访。\n\n@event scandal_candle_smoke\n# en: The Study Candle Fire\ntitle [db611e] = 学习蜡烛失火\n# en: One of the candles with your face on it set a dormitory curtain smouldering last night. Nobody was hurt, but the whole tower was woken and stood in the courtyard in their nightclothes for an hour. By breakfast the castle is calling them \"the {player} candles\", and it isn't meant kindly.\ntext [1f93a0] = 昨晚，一支印着你的脸的蜡烛把宿舍的窗帘烧得冒了烟。没人受伤，可整座塔的人都被叫醒，穿着睡衣在庭院里站了一个小时。到早饭时，城堡里都管它们叫“{player}蜡烛”，可不是什么好话。\n# en: A dormitory window with a scorched curtain, a smoking candle stub on the sill with a little painted label of a student's face, a prefect writing in a notebook.\nscene [7b894a] = 宿舍的窗户上挂着烧焦的窗帘，窗台上放着一截冒烟的蜡烛头，上面贴着一张画有学生面孔的小标签，一位级长在本子上记录。\n# en: Pay for the damage and stop the sales\nopt.own.label [50417a] = 赔偿损失，停止售卖\n# en: You go to the chandler and tell him to stop selling them, and you pay for the curtain out of your own cut. It costs you most of what you made. The tower grumbles, then forgives you, and someone leaves a fire bucket outside your door as a joke.\nopt.own.say.1 [d37f7a] = 你去找蜡烛匠，让他别再卖了，又用自己的分成赔了窗帘。这花掉了你挣的大部分钱。整座塔的人抱怨了一阵，然后原谅了你，还有人开玩笑地在你门口放了一桶灭火的水。\n# en: Prove the candle wasn't the cause\nopt.deny.label [1acdf3] = 证明不是蜡烛的问题\n# en: You take the stub to the alchemy laboratory and prove, with a great deal of smoke, that it was knocked over by a cat, not faulty. The cat belongs to a prefect. The story changes very fast.\nopt.deny.say.1 [d73bd0] = 你把蜡烛头拿到炼金实验室，冒着一大股烟证明了它是被一只猫碰倒的，而不是蜡烛本身有毛病。那只猫是一位级长的。故事一下子就变了。\n# en: You set out to prove the candle wasn't to blame, and your test sets a second curtain alight, in the laboratory, in front of the lecturer. The story is now much better than it was. You are not in it as the hero.\nopt.deny.say.2 [39da70] = 你想证明不是蜡烛的错，结果你的实验在实验室里、当着讲师的面又点着了第二块窗帘。这下故事比原来精彩多了。而你在里面可不是英雄。\n# en: Blame the chandler's wicks\nopt.spin.label [236a27] = 怪蜡烛匠的灯芯\n# en: You say, loudly and often, that the chandler must have changed his wicks without telling you. The castle turns on the chandler, who loses his academy trade for a term. You keep your name. You don't buy candles in the village for a long while.\nopt.spin.say.1 [84e29c] = 你逢人便大声说，一定是蜡烛匠偷偷换了灯芯没告诉你。整个城堡都转而攻击蜡烛匠，他丢了学院一个学期的生意。你保住了名声。很长一段时间里，你都没再去村里买蜡烛。\n\n@event scandal_memoir_book\n# en: Chapter Nine\ntitle [9d88a6] = 第九章\n# en: The memoir has come out in full, and chapter nine, which you never read, says that you once copied a whole essay from a friend in your first year. It isn't true. It's very well written. Half the castle has a copy, and the other half is borrowing one.\ntext [7670e7] = 回忆录全本出版了，而你从没读过的第九章写道，你一年级时曾经从一个朋友那里抄了一整篇论文。这不是真的。可写得非常好。半个城堡的人都有一本，另一半正在借。\n# en: A thin, glossy book lying open on a library table, one page heavily underlined, a group of students crowded around it whispering.\nscene [55927d] = 图书馆桌上摊着一本薄薄的、光鲜的书，有一页被画满了线，一群学生围在旁边窃窃私语。\n# en: Own that you never read it\nopt.own.label [a988b0] = 承认你从没读过它\n# en: You admit, publicly, that you signed a book you never read, and that chapter nine is invented. People laugh at you for it, and then believe you. The publisher's next printing quietly leaves the chapter out.\nopt.own.say.1 [5263b1] = 你公开承认，你签了一本自己从没读过的书，第九章是编造的。人们为此笑话你，然后相信了你。出版社重印时悄悄删掉了那一章。\n# en: Make the publisher retract it\nopt.deny.label [04ef46] = 让出版社撤回\n# en: You write to the publisher, then go to the city and sit in his office until he agrees to print a correction and pay you for the trouble. The correction is small. The money is not. The castle decides you were wronged.\nopt.deny.say.1 [a2f725] = 你写信给出版社，又去了城里，坐在他的办公室里不走，直到他同意刊登更正并赔偿你的麻烦。更正很小。赔偿可不小。城堡里的人认定你受了冤枉。\n# en: The publisher points to the contract you signed, the clause about \"creative licence\", and offers you a free copy. You pay for the journey home yourself. The chapter stays in.\nopt.deny.say.2 [216192] = 出版人指了指你签过的合同里那一条“创作自由”的条款，送了你一本免费的书。回家的路费是你自己掏的。那一章留了下来。\n# en: Hint that someone else copied\nopt.spin.label [c42b7e] = 暗示抄袭的另有其人\n# en: You say, with a sad look, that it must have been confused with somebody else's story. Within a day, the castle has decided whose. It wasn't them either. They don't speak to you again.\nopt.spin.say.1 [c3615e] = 你带着一脸遗憾说，那一定是和别人的故事弄混了。不到一天，城堡里就认定了是谁。那也不是他们干的。他们再也没跟你说过话。\n\n@event scandal_flourish_hurt\n# en: The {player}, Done Badly\ntitle [16868f] = “{player}式”，做砸了\n# en: A first-year doing \"the {player}\", the flourish that does nothing, did it so hard that the spell went off sideways. Nobody is badly hurt: the first-year has lost both eyebrows and is enjoying the attention. But a professor has written to the headmistress, and the word going round is \"reckless\".\ntext [39907b] = 一个一年级生做“{player}式”，就是那个毫无用处的花式，用力过猛，法术朝旁边炸了出去。没人伤得严重：那个一年级生的两道眉毛没了，正享受着大家的关注。可一位教授给院长写了信，到处都在传“鲁莽”这个词。\n# en: An infirmary bed with a first-year sitting up, both eyebrows missing and one sleeve singed, a healer in a grey apron frowning, a crowd of first-years peering round the door.\nscene [0e53d4] = 医务室的病床上坐着一个一年级生，两道眉毛都没了，一只袖子烧焦了，一位系着灰围裙的医者皱着眉，一群一年级生从门边探头张望。\n# en: Visit them and teach the real one\nopt.own.label [b14aa7] = 去看望，教真正的花式\n# en: You go to the infirmary and sit with the first-year, then teach the whole yard the real flourish, the one that steadies a cast, slowly, for a week. The eyebrows grow back. The professor's letter, you hear, ends with \"and the student took it in hand.\"\nopt.own.say.1 [2d6e0d] = 你去医务室陪那个一年级生坐了一会儿，然后花了一个星期，慢慢地教整个练习场那个真正的、能让施法更稳的花式。眉毛长回来了。你听说，那位教授的信最后写的是：“那个学生把事情管起来了。”\n# en: Say it was never your flourish\nopt.deny.label [41954f] = 说那从来不是你的花式\n# en: You point out, reasonably, that you have never once done the flourish and that it isn't yours to teach or stop. People see the sense in it. The first-year, eyebrowless, agrees cheerfully that it was \"more of a tribute\".\nopt.deny.say.1 [c76a80] = 你合情合理地指出，你从没做过那个花式，那不是你能教或能禁的。大家觉得有道理。那个没了眉毛的一年级生高高兴兴地同意，那“更像是一种致敬”。\n# en: You say it was never your flourish, and somebody produces a sketch of you doing it, solemnly, in the yard, on the day the whole yard cheered. You had forgotten that. Nobody else has.\nopt.deny.say.2 [7fa6df] = 你说那从来不是你的花式，结果有人拿出一张速写，画的正是你在练习场上郑重其事地做那个花式，就是整个练习场欢呼的那天。你早忘了。别人可没忘。\n# en: Blame the lecturer who never stopped it\nopt.spin.label [3e01bf] = 怪从没制止的讲师\n# en: You ask, in the right ears, why the practice-yard lecturer let a dangerous flourish go on for a month. The lecturer is spoken to. The flourish is banned. You were never mentioned, and you never quite look the lecturer in the eye again.\nopt.spin.say.1 [527e8f] = 你在该说的人耳边问了一句：为什么练习场的讲师让一个危险的花式流行了一个月？讲师被约谈了。花式被禁了。从头到尾没人提到你，而你再也没能正眼看那位讲师。\n\n@event scandal_wrong_notes\n# en: The {player} Method, Failed\ntitle [2e7e39] = “{player}学习法”，失败了\n# en: The copies of your notes that were sold in the common room had a mistake in them, in a page you never corrected, and a first-year who learned it word for word has failed a paper on it. The first-year's friends are telling everyone that \"the {player} method\" is a fraud, and that you took money for it.\ntext [fe537c] = 公共休息室里卖的那些你的笔记抄本有个错误，就在你从没改过的一页里，一个一年级生一字不差地背下来，结果一门考试没过。那个一年级生的朋友们逢人就说“{player}学习法”是骗人的，还说你收了钱。\n# en: A results list pinned to a board, a first-year in tears beside it clutching a sheaf of copied notes, older students reading the notes and shaking their heads.\nscene [cd260a] = 布告板上钉着成绩单，一个一年级生攥着一沓抄来的笔记在旁边哭，几个年长的学生读着那些笔记直摇头。\n# en: Refund it and tutor the first-year\nopt.own.label [d4d20a] = 退钱，辅导那个一年级生\n# en: You give back every crown you made, and spend three evenings with the first-year going over the paper properly. They pass the resit. The first-year's friends, grudgingly, stop telling the story, and start telling a different one.\nopt.own.say.1 [d46b3d] = 你把挣来的每一个克朗都退了回去，又花了三个晚上和那个一年级生好好过了一遍那门课。对方补考过了。那个一年级生的朋友们不情不愿地不再讲那个故事，开始讲另一个了。\n# en: Point out the page wasn't yours\nopt.deny.label [4dd309] = 指出那一页不是你写的\n# en: You find the original and show, page by page, that the mistake was added by the copyist. It's true, and it's convincing. The seller has to refund everyone. You still feel bad about the first-year.\nopt.deny.say.1 [c89be3] = 你找出原稿，一页一页地证明那个错误是抄写的人加上去的。这是真的，也很有说服力。卖家只好给所有人退钱。你还是为那个一年级生感到难过。\n# en: You go looking for your original, and find the mistake in your own hand. There's no arguing with it. The story gets worse for the week you spent pretending it wasn't there.\nopt.deny.say.2 [cd42bc] = 你去找你的原稿，结果在你自己的笔迹里找到了那个错误。这没法争辩。你装作没这回事的那个星期，让故事变得更糟了。\n# en: Blame the seller\nopt.spin.label [70953e] = 怪卖家\n# en: You tell everyone the third-year with the cash tin had been altering the notes for profit. The third-year is hauled in front of a prefect and loses their place in two clubs. It isn't quite true. It's close enough that you sleep, most nights.\nopt.spin.say.1 [887c53] = 你告诉所有人，那个捧着钱匣的三年级生为了赚钱一直在篡改笔记。那个三年级生被拉到级长面前，丢了两个社团的位置。这不完全是真的。但也差不多，所以大多数夜里你睡得着。\n\n@event scandal_thornwick_cheat\n# en: Thornwick Cries Foul\ntitle [3ad11a] = 荆木喊犯规\n# en: Thornwick's student paper says you cheated at the boundary stones: that the last spell, the one that won it, used a ward Thornwick rules forbid. It's half true. Lumenhold's rules allow it; Thornwick's don't, and nobody agreed whose rules it was. The cartoon on their front page is very good.\ntext [0c385c] = 荆木的校报说你在界石旁作弊了：说最后那个赢下比赛的法术用了荆木规则禁止的结界。这有一半是真的。流明堡的规则允许；荆木的不允许，而当时谁也没说好用谁的规则。他们头版上的漫画画得非常好。\n# en: A rival school's newspaper held up in a courtyard, its front page showing a cartoon of a student cheating at a stone circle, a crowd jeering and laughing.\nscene [153841] = 庭院里有人举着一份对手学校的报纸，头版漫画画的是一个学生在石阵边作弊，一群人起哄大笑。\n# en: Offer them a rematch, their rules\nopt.own.label [53b7ab] = 提出重赛，按他们的规则\n# en: You write and offer a rematch under Thornwick rules, and win that too, by a hair. Thornwick's paper prints a short, sour correction. The cartoon goes up on the Lumenhold common-room wall, framed, with a new caption.\nopt.own.say.1 [3b21ad] = 你写信提出按荆木的规则重赛，结果又赢了，险胜。荆木的校报登了一则简短而酸溜溜的更正。那幅漫画被裱了起来，挂在流明堡公共休息室的墙上，配上了新的说明文字。\n# en: You offer a rematch under Thornwick rules, and lose it, fairly. It hurts. But the story Thornwick tells now is about a Lumenhold student who came back to lose properly, and it isn't an unkind one.\nopt.own.say.2 [e0fcb6] = 你提出按荆木的规则重赛，然后公平地输了。很痛。可荆木如今讲的故事，是一个流明堡学生回来堂堂正正地输了一场，而这并不是个刻薄的故事。\n# en: Say the rules were always Lumenhold's\nopt.deny.label [9ba7ec] = 说规则一直是流明堡的\n# en: You say flatly that the contest was at Lumenhold's boundary and under Lumenhold's rules. It's a fair point. It makes no difference to anybody at Thornwick, and it makes you sound, even to yourself, like someone who won on a technicality.\nopt.deny.say.1 [6456b7] = 你断然表示，比赛在流明堡的地界上，按流明堡的规则进行。这有道理。可对荆木的任何人都毫无影响，而且连你自己听着都像个靠钻空子赢的人。\n# en: Leak a Thornwick scandal of your own\nopt.spin.label [a534b8] = 放出荆木自己的丑闻\n# en: You find out something unflattering about the Thornwick student who sent the challenge, and make sure it reaches their paper's rival. Within a week, nobody at Thornwick is talking about the boundary stones. You won. It doesn't taste of much.\nopt.spin.say.1 [bfe070] = 你打听到了那个发来挑战的荆木学生的一件不光彩的事，确保它传到了他们校报的对头那里。不到一个星期，荆木就没人再谈界石的事了。你赢了。却没什么滋味。\n\n@event scandal_society_dare\n# en: In Your Name\ntitle [c7a5bc] = 以你之名\n# en: The {player} Society, the club of first-years with ribbons, held an initiation last night, and the dare was to climb to the third gargoyle \"like {player} would\". One of them got stuck there till dawn. The castle is saying the society is dangerous, and that you never told them to stop.\ntext [76d41e] = {player}协会，就是那群系着缎带的一年级生，昨晚办了一场入会仪式，挑战是“像{player}那样”爬到第三座石像鬼那儿。其中一个困在那里直到天亮。城堡里都说这个协会很危险，说你从没让他们停下。\n# en: A castle rooftop at dawn, a small first-year clinging to a gargoyle, a crowd of prefects and a professor staring up from the courtyard below.\nscene [4dfcf3] = 黎明时分的城堡屋顶，一个小个子一年级生紧抱着一座石像鬼，一群级长和一位教授在下面的庭院里仰头望着。\n# en: Take responsibility for the society\nopt.own.label [517f8e] = 为协会负起责任\n# en: You go to the headmistress yourself and say it was your name, so it's your society, and you should have been paying attention. You write the society some rules. They are thrilled to have rules. Nobody climbs anything again.\nopt.own.say.1 [951163] = 你亲自去见院长，说那是你的名字，所以那就是你的协会，你本该多留点心。你给协会定了几条规矩。他们有了规矩，高兴坏了。再也没人爬过任何东西。\n# en: Say you never asked for a society\nopt.deny.label [553d7b] = 说你从没要过什么协会\n# en: You point out, gently, that you never asked for a society and have been to exactly one meeting. People agree that that's fair. The first-years are mortified. The first-year from the gargoyle sends you a note: \"SORRY. Still worth it.\"\nopt.deny.say.1 [fef747] = 你温和地指出，你从没要过什么协会，总共只去过一次聚会。大家都认为这说得公道。一年级生们羞愧难当。那个困在石像鬼上的一年级生给你送来一张字条：“对不起。还是值得。”\n# en: You say you had nothing to do with it, and a first-year, very upset, shows everyone the minutes from the meeting you attended, with your signature. \"We thought you'd want us to be brave,\" the minutes say. It's in the Ledger by Friday.\nopt.deny.say.2 [ea3080] = 你说你和这事毫无关系，一个非常难过的一年级生给所有人看了你参加过的那次聚会的记录，上面有你的签名。“我们以为你会希望我们勇敢，”记录上写着。周五就上了《纪事报》。\n# en: Blame the society's president\nopt.spin.label [5aac6f] = 怪协会的会长\n# en: You let it be known that the president had been running the society badly for months. The president resigns in tears. The society carries on without them. You don't go to the next meeting, or any of them.\nopt.spin.say.1 [0085a7] = 你让人们知道，会长几个月来一直把协会管得一团糟。会长哭着辞了职。协会没有会长照样办了下去。你没去下一次聚会，以后也一次都没去。\n\n@event scandal_leaked_letter\n# en: Private Correspondence\ntitle [bef85e] = 私人信件\n# en: A private letter of yours, a long, frank one written to someone back home, has been printed in a gossip sheet. It isn't the Ledger: it's one of those single pages that appear on the boards overnight. In it you say what you really think of several people. They have all read it.\ntext [f870ef] = 你的一封私人信件，一封写给家乡某人的、长长的、毫无保留的信，被登在了一份八卦小报上。不是《纪事报》：是那种一夜之间出现在布告板上的单页小报。信里你说了你对好几个人的真实看法。他们全都读过了。\n# en: A gossip sheet with a letter printed across its centre, a student snatching a copy from a crowd, another reading it aloud on a bench.\nscene [d1b4ff] = 一份八卦小报的正中间印着一封信，一个学生从人群里一把抢过一份，另一个坐在长凳上大声念着。\n# en: Apologise to each of them, in person\nopt.own.label [ba95e1] = 当面挨个道歉\n# en: You go round, one by one, and apologise, and say the letter was unkind and you were tired and homesick when you wrote it. Most of them accept it. One of them says you were right about their singing. It takes a week, and it's the worst week of the term.\nopt.own.say.1 [6a9198] = 你一个一个地去道歉，说那封信写得刻薄，写的时候你又累又想家。大多数人接受了。其中一个说你对自己唱歌的评价是对的。这花了一个星期，是这学期最难熬的一个星期。\n# en: Find out who stole it\nopt.deny.label [f1d3fa] = 查出是谁偷的\n# en: You trace the paper and the ink back to a second-year with a grudge and a key to the post room. Once it's known the letter was stolen, the story becomes the thief's. People forgive you faster than you expected.\nopt.deny.say.1 [3554a4] = 你顺着纸张和墨水追查到一个怀恨在心、又有收发室钥匙的二年级生。大家一知道信是被偷的，故事就成了小偷的故事。人们原谅你比你预想的快。\n# en: You spend a week hunting the thief and find nothing, and meanwhile three more pages of the letter appear. Everybody has noticed that you never said it wasn't true.\nopt.deny.say.2 [c486dc] = 你花了一个星期找小偷，什么也没找到，与此同时，那封信又冒出了三页。所有人都注意到，你从没说过信里的话不是真的。\n# en: Say it was forged\nopt.spin.label [204199] = 说信是伪造的\n# en: You tell everyone the letter was forged, by someone who hates you, and you have a guess who. It works. Somebody else is now the most disliked student in the castle. You can't quite meet the eyes of the people in the letter.\nopt.spin.say.1 [9d4695] = 你告诉所有人那封信是伪造的，出自某个恨你的人之手，而且你猜得到是谁。这招奏效了。如今城堡里最招人厌的学生换了别人。你没法直视信里提到的那些人的眼睛。\n\n@event scandal_cheating_rumour\n# en: Nobody Gets Marks Like That\ntitle [aefd29] = 没人能考出那种分数\n# en: Somebody has started saying that nobody gets marks like yours honestly, and that someone famous could easily get hold of the papers. It's nonsense. But the examiners have heard it, and you've been asked, very politely, to come and answer some questions on Thursday.\ntext [851417] = 有人开始说，没人能老老实实考出你那样的分数，一个名人要弄到考卷简直易如反掌。这是胡说。可考官们听到了，你被非常客气地请去周四回答几个问题。\n# en: A corridor outside an examiners' office, students muttering behind their hands as one student passes with a results slip.\nscene [071f03] = 考官办公室外的走廊里，一个学生拿着成绩单经过，旁边的学生们捂着嘴嘀嘀咕咕。\n# en: Ask them to examine you again\nopt.own.label [3041a5] = 请他们重新考你\n# en: You ask the examiners to set you a fresh paper, there and then, with a proctor. They do. You sit it in a cold room with an hourglass and a lecturer watching, and you do well. The rumour dies in a week, and nobody apologises.\nopt.own.say.1 [3ca702] = 你请考官们当场给你出一套新卷子，派人监考。他们照办了。你在一间冷冰冰的屋子里，对着沙漏、在一位讲师的注视下答完了卷子，考得很好。一个星期后谣言平息了，没人道歉。\n# en: Answer their questions, and theirs alone\nopt.deny.label [42580b] = 只回答他们的问题\n# en: You answer every question on Thursday plainly and well, and the examiners thank you for your time. One of them says, at the door, that they'd never really doubted it. By the next week the rumour has moved on.\nopt.deny.say.1 [869204] = 周四你把每个问题都答得平实而漂亮，考官们感谢你抽空前来。其中一位在门口说，他们其实从没真的怀疑过。到下一周，谣言就转到别处去了。\n# en: You get flustered on Thursday, and the examiners, who had only half believed the story, start to believe it a little more. Nothing comes of it in the end. But it takes the whole term to come to nothing.\nopt.deny.say.2 [4067e7] = 周四你慌了神，原本只信了一半的考官们开始多信了一点。最后什么也没查出来。可这件事花了整整一个学期才不了了之。\n# en: Say who really had the papers\nopt.spin.label [130194] = 说出真正拿到考卷的人\n# en: You let slip a name, someone you once saw near the examiners' office. The rumour moves on to them at once. They are questioned for a week and cleared. They know where it came from. So do you.\nopt.spin.say.1 [1ae636] = 你“不小心”说出了一个名字，一个你曾经在考官办公室附近见过的人。谣言立刻转到了对方身上。对方被问了一个星期的话，最后洗清了嫌疑。对方知道是谁起的头。你也知道。\n\n@event scandal_ex_tells_all\n# en: Their Side of It\ntitle [f0d274] = 对方的说法\n# en: {x} has been telling the story of the two of you to anyone who'll listen: the way it started, the way it ended, and a great many things in between that you'd thought were private. The common room goes quiet when you come in. Somebody coughs.\ntext [25ca30] = {x}一直在跟所有愿意听的人讲你们俩的故事：怎么开始的，怎么结束的，还有中间许多你以为是私事的事。你走进公共休息室时，里面安静了下来。有人咳嗽了一声。\n# en: A crowded common room, a student holding court with a cup in hand, telling a story to a rapt circle, another student frozen in the doorway.\nscene [e838be] = 拥挤的公共休息室里，一个学生端着杯子被一圈人围着，讲得眉飞色舞，另一个学生僵在门口。\n# en: Tell your own side, kindly\nopt.own.label [b968f1] = 和气地讲讲你的说法\n# en: You don't argue. You say that it ended badly and you're sorry for your part in it, and that {x} isn't wrong about everything. It takes the air out of the story. A week later {x} stops you in a corridor and says, stiffly, that {they} had gone too far.\nopt.own.say.1 [022604] = 你没有争辩。你说这段感情结束得很糟，你为自己那部分感到抱歉，而{x}也不是全都说错了。故事一下子泄了气。一个星期后，{x}在走廊里拦住你，生硬地说{they}说得过分了。\n# en: Say it's all made up\nopt.deny.label [5a0a8e] = 说全是编的\n# en: You laugh it off and say {x} always did have an imagination. People decide to believe you, mostly. {x} hears about it and goes very quiet, and the story stops, and so does anything else between you.\nopt.deny.say.1 [e31acb] = 你一笑置之，说{x}向来想象力丰富。人们大体上决定相信你。{x}听说后变得非常沉默，故事停了，你们之间的一切也停了。\n# en: You say it's all made up, and {x} produces your letters. All of them. The common room reads them aloud, with feeling. You have to leave by the window.\nopt.deny.say.2 [c53b03] = 你说全是编的，{x}拿出了你写的信。全部。公共休息室里的人声情并茂地念了出来。你只好从窗户溜走。\n# en: Tell a worse story about {x}\nopt.spin.label [23beff] = 讲一个关于{x}的更糟的故事\n# en: You tell a story about {x}, a worse one, and some of it is true. The common room switches sides by supper. {x} doesn't come down to meals for a week. You win, in the sense that nobody's talking about you.\nopt.spin.say.1 [81d5f3] = 你讲了一个关于{x}的故事，更糟糕的一个，其中有些是真的。到晚饭时，公共休息室里的人都倒向了你这边。{x}一个星期没下来吃饭。你赢了，就是说，没人再谈论你了。\n\n@event scandal_curfew_column\n# en: Seen Where You Shouldn't Be\ntitle [b2f95b] = 出现在不该出现的地方\n# en: The gossip column has a paragraph about a \"certain famous student\" seen creeping back past the night porter at two in the morning, \"from the direction of the boathouse\". Nobody needs to guess who. A prefect has torn it down twice. It keeps going back up.\ntext [9ff71a] = 八卦专栏里有一段写的是一位“某著名学生”凌晨两点“从船屋的方向”偷偷溜过守夜人身边。不用猜是谁。一位级长已经撕掉它两次了。它总是又被贴回去。\n# en: A gossip column pinned to a noticeboard with one paragraph circled, a sketch beside it of a student creeping past a lantern at night.\nscene [3b15a1] = 布告板上钉着一份八卦专栏，其中一段被圈了出来，旁边画着一个学生在夜里偷偷溜过一盏灯笼的速写。\n# en: Own up to the prefects\nopt.own.label [fdd226] = 向级长坦白\n# en: You go to the prefects' room and say yes, it was you, and you were out walking because you couldn't sleep. They give you a week of detention polishing the trophy cabinet. It's dull. The column moves on, because there's nothing left to whisper.\nopt.own.say.1 [8df111] = 你去级长室说，是的，是你，你睡不着，出去走走。他们罚你关一个星期禁闭擦奖杯柜。很无聊。专栏转向了别的话题，因为已经没什么可嘀咕的了。\n# en: Prove you were somewhere else\nopt.deny.label [40d6e9] = 证明你在别处\n# en: You produce three friends who swear you were playing cards in the common room till one. The column prints a short, sniffy correction. Your friends make you buy them supper for a month.\nopt.deny.say.1 [f96abc] = 你找来三个朋友，发誓说你一直在公共休息室打牌打到一点。专栏登了一则简短而傲慢的更正。你的朋友们让你请了一个月的晚饭。\n# en: Your alibi lasts until somebody points out that the common room was locked at midnight. The next column runs the whole story, with the bit about the alibi. The prefects are not amused.\nopt.deny.say.2 [fca442] = 你的不在场证明一直撑到有人指出，公共休息室午夜就锁门了。下一期专栏把整件事都登了出来，连带不在场证明那一段。级长们一点也不觉得好笑。\n# en: Hint it was someone in a borrowed coat\nopt.spin.label [a1c7a7] = 暗示那人穿着借来的外套\n# en: You let it be known you'd lent your coat that night to a friend. It's half true. The column hunts for the friend, who doesn't thank you for it, and the story slowly turns into one about the coat.\nopt.spin.say.1 [9b6602] = 你放出话说，那天晚上你把外套借给了一个朋友。这有一半是真的。专栏开始追查那个朋友，对方可一点也不感激你，故事慢慢变成了一件外套的故事。\n\n@event scandal_snubbed\n# en: The Snub\ntitle [8360aa] = 冷落\n# en: The story is that a first-year waited two hours in the rain outside your lecture to ask you something, and you walked straight past without a word. You don't remember it. You might have been thinking about something else. The first-year cried, apparently, and the first-years have decided you're a monster.\ntext [f57c34] = 传说一个一年级生冒着雨在你的课室外等了两个小时，想问你一件事，而你一言不发地径直走了过去。你不记得这回事。你可能当时在想别的事。据说那个一年级生哭了，一年级生们已经认定你是个怪物。\n# en: A first-year sitting alone on a stone bench, face in hands, a group of first-years glaring across a courtyard at an older student walking away.\nscene [18b961] = 一个一年级生独自坐在石凳上双手捂着脸，一群一年级生隔着庭院怒视着一个正在走开的年长学生。\n# en: Find the first-year and apologise\nopt.own.label [ea83ad] = 找到那个一年级生道歉\n# en: You find the first-year and apologise, properly, and ask what they'd wanted to ask. It was about the library. You take them there. By the end of the week, the first-years' monster has become the first-years' favourite again, mostly.\nopt.own.say.1 [3bcdbd] = 你找到那个一年级生，好好地道了歉，问对方当时想问什么。是关于图书馆的。你带对方去了。到周末，一年级生眼里的怪物又差不多变回了一年级生最喜欢的人。\n# en: Say you were never there that day\nopt.deny.label [f22d27] = 说那天你根本不在\n# en: You say you were in the infirmary that afternoon with a cold, and Sister Amaranth, when asked, can't say you weren't. Neither can you, quite. The story decides the first-year must have mistaken somebody else for you.\nopt.deny.say.1 [915afa] = 你说那天下午你感冒了，在医务室，阿玛兰斯修女被问到时也说不准你不在。你自己也说不太准。大家便认定，那个一年级生一定是把别人错认成了你。\n# en: You say you were never there, and three first-years say they saw you. The first-years are now sure you're a monster and a liar. It takes the rest of the term.\nopt.deny.say.2 [467975] = 你说你根本不在，三个一年级生说他们看见你了。现在一年级生们确信你既是怪物又是骗子。这花了整个学期。\n# en: Say the first-year has been stalking you\nopt.spin.label [185821] = 说那个一年级生一直跟踪你\n# en: You tell people, sadly, that the first-year has been following you everywhere, and you didn't know what to do. People are sympathetic. The first-year stops coming to meals for a while. You never find out what they'd wanted to ask.\nopt.spin.say.1 [8089fd] = 你难过地告诉别人，那个一年级生一直到处跟着你，你不知道该怎么办。人们很同情你。那个一年级生有一阵子不来吃饭了。你始终不知道对方当时想问什么。\n\n@event scandal_thornwick_defection\n# en: Off to Thornwick?\ntitle [cfa5d1] = 要去荆木了？\n# en: Thornwick's student paper has run a story that you're leaving Lumenhold for Thornwick next year, with a quote, apparently from you, about Lumenhold's \"draughty towers and draughtier lecturers\". You've never said it. Half the castle believes it, and the other half is hurt that you'd joke about it.\ntext [f5a743] = 荆木的校报登了一篇报道，说你明年要离开流明堡去荆木，还附了一句据说是你说的话，说流明堡“塔楼漏风，讲师更漏风”。你从没说过。半个城堡的人信了，另一半为你竟然拿这个开玩笑而难过。\n# en: A newspaper from a rival school pinned up in a courtyard, the headline in large red letters, Lumenhold students tearing it down while others stare.\nscene [f61a99] = 庭院里钉着一份对手学校的报纸，标题是大大的红字，几个流明堡学生在撕它，其他人盯着看。\n# en: Say you'd never leave\nopt.own.label [62955d] = 说你永远不会走\n# en: You stand up at supper and say, plainly, that you've never been asked and you'd never go, and that the towers are draughty but they're yours. The Great Hall bangs the tables. It's embarrassing. It works.\nopt.own.say.1 [a520e1] = 你在晚餐时站起来，平平实实地说，从没人邀请过你，你也永远不会去，塔楼是漏风，但那是你的塔楼。大礼堂里的人拍起了桌子。很难为情。很管用。\n# en: Prove the quote is fake\nopt.deny.label [d11047] = 证明那句话是假的\n# en: You find that the \"quote\" is lifted, word for word, from a Thornwick student's own letter home, which their paper printed last year. Lumenhold reprints both side by side. Thornwick's paper is very quiet for a month.\nopt.deny.say.1 [9de418] = 你发现那句“引语”是一字不差地从一个荆木学生自己的家信里搬来的，那封信去年就登在他们的校报上。流明堡把两者并排重印了出来。荆木的校报安静了一个月。\n# en: You try to prove the quote was invented, and can't, and the more you protest, the more people suspect you did say it, once, in a bad mood, somewhere. You probably did. You can't remember.\nopt.deny.say.2 [e24aa6] = 你想证明那句话是编的，却证明不了，你越是抗议，人们越怀疑你确实说过，在某次心情不好的时候，在某个地方。你多半说过。你记不得了。\n# en: Hint you've had a better offer\nopt.spin.label [693c5d] = 暗示你有更好的去处\n# en: You let it be known you've had offers from all sorts of places, and Thornwick isn't the best of them. People are impressed. They are also, now, a little bit wary of you, and stop telling you things.\nopt.spin.say.1 [2e8264] = 你放出话说，你收到过各种地方的邀请，荆木还算不上最好的。人们很佩服。他们现在也对你有点戒心了，不再什么都告诉你。\n\n@event scandal_forged_love_letter\n# en: A Letter You Didn't Write\ntitle [c36010] = 一封你没写过的信\n# en: A love letter is going round the library, in what looks exactly like your handwriting, addressed to {y}. It is very passionate and very badly spelled. You didn't write it. {y} has read it, and is now going very carefully out of every room you come into.\ntext [a16a54] = 图书馆里正传着一封情书，笔迹看上去和你的一模一样，写给{y}。它非常热烈，拼写非常糟糕。你没写过。{y}读过了，现在只要你走进哪个房间，{they}就小心翼翼地从哪个房间出去。\n# en: A pink letter being passed hand to hand along a library table, a student at one end turning bright red, another at the far end staring in horror.\nscene [66e9c1] = 一封粉红色的信在图书馆的长桌上一个接一个地传着，桌子一头的学生满脸通红，另一头的学生惊恐地瞪着眼。\n# en: Go and talk to {y} first\nopt.own.label [f06f9c] = 先去找{y}谈谈\n# en: You find {y} before anybody else can, and say you didn't write it and you're sorry {they} had to read it. {y} laughs, from relief. \"I did wonder about the spelling.\" The story fizzles, and you and {y} are, oddly, closer for it.\nopt.own.say.1 [8a6abd] = 你赶在别人之前找到{y}，说那不是你写的，很抱歉让{they}读到了那封信。{y}松了口气，笑了。“我确实怀疑过那拼写。”故事就这么没了下文，而你和{y}反倒因此更亲近了些。\n# en: Find the forger\nopt.deny.label [3df4bc] = 找出伪造者\n# en: You compare the hand with every exercise book in your year, and find the forger: a second-year who thought it was the funniest thing ever done. The library agrees, a little. The second-year apologises, mostly to {y}.\nopt.deny.say.1 [6d0f64] = 你把那笔迹和你们年级每一本练习簿比对，找到了伪造者：一个觉得这是史上最好笑的事的二年级生。图书馆里的人也有点同意。那个二年级生道了歉，主要是向{y}道歉。\n# en: You can't find the forger, and the longer you look, the more people think you're covering for yourself. {y} starts taking the long way round the library. It's a month before you can laugh about it.\nopt.deny.say.2 [847953] = 你找不到伪造者，找得越久，人们越觉得你在替自己打掩护。{y}开始绕远路去图书馆。过了一个月，你才能笑着提起这件事。\n# en: Say it was meant for someone else\nopt.spin.label [27d155] = 说那是写给别人的\n# en: You say, with a wink, that it was meant for somebody else entirely, and wasn't it obvious? The castle spends a week guessing who. {y} is relieved, and then, for some reason, hurt, and you never quite work out why.\nopt.spin.say.1 [2b87f9] = 你眨眨眼说，那封信其实是写给另一个人的，难道不明显吗？整个城堡猜了一个星期是谁。{y}松了口气，接着，不知为什么，又觉得受了伤，而你始终没弄明白为什么。\n\n@event scandal_credit_taken\n# en: An Idea Over Supper\ntitle [b9ec60] = 晚饭时的一个点子\n# en: The idea everyone's praising you for, the one the lecturer called \"the best thing this year\", started as something {f} said to you over supper. You'd forgotten. {f} hadn't. Now {f}'s friends are telling everyone that the famous {player} steals ideas from people who aren't famous.\ntext [08215b] = 大家都在为之称赞你的那个点子，讲师说的“今年最好的东西”，最初是{f}在晚饭时跟你说的。你忘了。{f}可没忘。现在{f}的朋友们逢人就说，著名的{player}偷不出名的人的点子。\n# en: A lecture hall with a diagram on the board, a professor pointing to one student, another student in the row behind sitting with arms folded and jaw set.\nscene [586a85] = 阶梯教室的黑板上画着一张图，一位教授指着一个学生，后一排的另一个学生抱着胳膊，咬紧了牙。\n# en: Give {f} the credit, out loud\nopt.own.label [157334] = 大声把功劳归给{f}\n# en: You go to the lecturer, with {f}, and say whose idea it was first. The lecturer puts both names on the board. {f} goes red and says it wasn't much of an idea, really. It was. People notice that you said so.\nopt.own.say.1 [87ded9] = 你带着{f}去找讲师，说这个点子最先是谁想的。讲师把两个人的名字都写在了黑板上。{f}脸红了，说其实也算不上什么好点子。它是。人们注意到你说了实话。\n# en: Say you'd had it too\nopt.deny.label [0e8364] = 说你也想到过\n# en: You say you'd been thinking along the same lines for weeks, and you have notes to prove it, nearly. People accept it. {f} doesn't, and things between you are cool for a long while.\nopt.deny.say.1 [c6a5b1] = 你说你几个星期来一直在往这个方向想，还有笔记为证，差不多吧。人们接受了。{f}没有，你们之间冷了很长一段时间。\n# en: You say you'd had the idea too, and {f} produces the napkin from supper with the idea sketched on it, and the date. The story is now much worse, and it has a napkin in it.\nopt.deny.say.2 [b30f73] = 你说你也想到过，{f}拿出了那天晚饭时的餐巾，上面画着那个点子，还写着日期。现在故事糟糕多了，里面还有一张餐巾。\n# en: Say {f} is jealous\nopt.spin.label [c2f09d] = 说{f}是嫉妒\n# en: You say, sorrowfully, that {f} has always been a little jealous of you. People believe it, because you're famous and {f} isn't. {f} stops sitting with you at supper, and you start to wonder whose ideas you're going to have now.\nopt.spin.say.1 [4099d5] = 你沉痛地说，{f}一直有点嫉妒你。人们信了，因为你有名，而{f}没有。{f}不再和你一起吃晚饭了，你开始想，以后你的点子要从哪儿来。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/slice_2a.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/slice_2a.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event slice_ward_lesson\n# en: The Safety Talk\ntitle [34bb10] = 安全讲座\n# en: First week, the Great Hall: the safety talk. Sister Amaranth gives it while re-bandaging a prefect. \"You will be hexed,\" she says, \"by accident and on purpose. One spell before any other: a ward. It stops one blow.\" She looks up. \"Practise in pairs. Aim low.\"\ntext [f59553] = 入学第一周，大礼堂：安全讲座。阿玛兰斯修女一边给一位级长重新缠绷带一边讲。“你们会中恶咒，”她说，“有意的，无意的。先学一道咒，别的都往后放：护界。它能挡一击。”她抬起头。“两人一组练习。往低处打。”\n# en: The Great Hall in the first week of term, rows of first-years on benches holding up wobbling pale shells, a healer walking the aisle with a roll of bandages.\nscene [d87a54] = 学期第一周的大礼堂，一排排新生坐在长凳上举着摇摇晃晃的苍白护壳，一位医师拿着一卷绷带沿过道走着。\n# en: Pair up and take the hexes\nopt.left.label [4850bc] = 两人一组挨恶咒\n# en: Your partner's practice hex stings your knuckles twice before your shell holds. The third one splashes off it like rain off glass. Amaranth, passing, says \"Keep doing that,\" and keeps walking.\nopt.left.say.1 [1606a6] = 你的搭档的练习恶咒蛰了你的指关节两次，你的护壳才撑住。第三下像雨水溅在玻璃上一样弹开了。阿玛兰斯经过时说了句“保持住”，脚步没停。\n# en: Copy her notes and practise alone\nopt.right.label [f18ae3] = 抄她的笔记，自己练\n# en: You take down every word and practise in your room that night. The shell comes on the eleventh try, lopsided, while your roommate watches. It holds, which is the part that counts.\nopt.right.say.1 [baf37c] = 你一字不落地记下，当晚在房间里练。第十一次，护壳歪歪扭扭地出来了，你的室友在一旁看着。它撑住了，这才是要紧的。\n\n@event slice_candle_flame\n# en: Borrowed Fire\ntitle [f667df] = 借来的火\n# en: Lyra watches you fail to light a candle for the third time. \"You're asking it,\" she says. \"Fire doesn't want to be asked. {rel(lyra) >= 12: Here — watch my hand.|Watch, if you can keep up.}\"\ntext [8ed13c] = 莱拉看着你第三次没能点燃蜡烛。“你在请求它，”她说，“火不喜欢被请求。{rel(lyra) >= 12: 来——看我的手。|看好了，如果你跟得上的话。}”\n# en: A crimson-haired student balancing a flame on one fingertip while another student squints at a sputtering candle.\nscene [7237b8] = 一个绯红头发的学生指尖托着一簇火苗，另一个学生眯眼盯着一根噼啪作响的蜡烛。\n# en: Watch her hand\nopt.left.label [8f7d80] = 看她的手\n# en: She doesn't explain. She just does it, slowly, twice. On the third try your candle catches, and so, very briefly, does your sleeve.\nopt.left.say.1 [59ceef] = 她不解释，只是慢慢地做了两遍。第三次，你的蜡烛点着了——你的袖子也短暂地着了一下。\n# en: Challenge her to teach you properly\nopt.right.label [02abfd] = 激她好好教你\n# en: You hold your own long enough that she stops and actually explains. \"Fine,\" she says. \"You'll do.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [24afbf] = 你撑得够久，久到她停下来真的开始讲解。“行吧，”她说，“你还凑合。”\n# en: She wins, and you hit the floor hard enough to feel it tomorrow, but she shows you the trick on the way out, as if it were nothing.\nopt.right.say.2 [cfe391] = 她赢了，你重重摔在地上，明天还会疼，但离开时她顺手把诀窍演示给你看，仿佛那不值一提。\n# en: You end up on the floor with singed eyebrows. Lyra steps over you. \"Ask better,\" she says.\nopt.right.say.3 [a982db] = 你最后躺在地上，眉毛被燎焦了。莱拉从你身上跨过去。“下次问得好一点，”她说。\n\n@event slice_stray_cold\n# en: The Drift\ntitle [b9d28f] = 寒流\n# en: Cold drifts down the corridor like a tide coming in, and the torches lean away from it. {spell_level(ward) > 0: Your ward prickles at your fingertips.|You have nothing to hold it back.}\ntext [74bc38] = 寒意像涨潮一样漫过走廊，火把纷纷向一旁倾斜。{spell_level(ward) > 0: 你的护界在指尖隐隐刺痛。|你没有任何东西能挡住它。}\n# en: A corridor at night where frost spreads across the flagstones toward a student holding a candle.\nscene [e9d25c] = 夜里的一条走廊，霜正沿着石板向一个举着蜡烛的学生蔓延。\n# en: Run for the stairs\nopt.left.label [3e7200] = 往楼梯跑\n# en: You make the stairs with frost in your hair. Behind you, the corridor sighs and is just a corridor again.\nopt.left.say.1 [498ddb] = 你冲上楼梯时，头发里结满了霜。身后的走廊叹了口气，又变回了普通的走廊。\n# en: Stand and study it\nopt.right.label [0a0a0c] = 站住，研究它\n# en: It isn't weather. It feels like being looked at, from somewhere far below. You write down everything before your hands warm up, and none of it makes sense yet.\nopt.right.say.1 [791d24] = 这不是天气。更像是被注视着，从很深很深的下面。你趁双手还没回暖，把一切都记了下来，可眼下还一点也看不明白。\n# en: You learn exactly one thing, which is how cold cold can be. Sister Amaranth wraps you in four blankets.\nopt.right.say.2 [46b7a7] = 你只学到了一件事：冷能冷到什么地步。阿玛兰斯修女给你裹了四层毯子。\n# en: Raise a ward\nopt.up.label [d5a702] = 撑起护界\n# en: Your ward flares, pale and steady. The cold breaks against it and slides away, the way a wave gives up on a rock.\nopt.up.say.1 [e39cb8] = 你的护界亮起，苍白而稳定。寒流撞上它，滑开了，像浪头对一块礁石放弃了。\n\n@event slice_late_lamps\n# en: The Last Lamp\ntitle [5ba6ee] = 最后一盏灯\n# en: {~The library is emptying.|Rain on the high windows, and the library is emptying early.|Exam fever has filled the library all day; now, finally, it is emptying.} {~Footnote, the library cat, is asleep on the book you need|The book you need is on the top shelf, and the ladder squeaks|Someone has left a half-finished essay on your table, and a biscuit}, and the last lamp is yours if you want it.\ntext [d83f80] = {~图书馆渐渐空了。|雨打在高窗上，图书馆早早就空了。|考试热潮挤满了图书馆一整天；现在，终于，人渐渐散了。}{~图书馆的猫“脚注”趴在你要的那本书上睡着了|你要的那本书在最高一层，梯子吱呀作响|有人在你桌上留了一篇写了一半的论文，还有一块饼干}，最后一盏灯，你想要就是你的。\n# en: The library at closing time, one desk still lit, a cat asleep on an open book.\nscene [6a380e] = 闭馆时的图书馆，只剩一张书桌还亮着，一只猫睡在摊开的书上。\n# en: Keep the lamp\nopt.left.label [b4ddff] = 守着这盏灯\n# en: {~You read until Footnote leaves in protest.|You read until the words swim.|You read until the night porter coughs pointedly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [9dbe5e] = {~你读到“脚注”愤然离席。|你读到字都开始游动。|你读到守夜人意味深长地咳嗽。}\n# en: Go to bed\nopt.right.label [2fa817] = 回去睡觉\n# en: {~You sleep like a stone. The book will still be there. So, probably, will Footnote.|You fall asleep before your head finds the pillow. You wake up knowing one thing you didn't, somehow.|You sleep well and dream of shelves. The lamp goes to someone who needed it more.}\nopt.right.say.1 [edb97a] = {~你睡得像块石头。书还会在那里。“脚注”大概也是。|你头还没沾到枕头就睡着了。醒来时，不知怎么，你多懂了一件事。|你睡得很好，梦里全是书架。那盏灯留给了更需要它的人。}\n\n@event slice_quarrel\n# en: A Quarrel in the Courtyard\ntitle [cdc8fb] = 庭院里的争执\n# en: Kael and Lyra are facing each other in the courtyard, and the {season == \"winter\": snow|grass} between them is half {season == \"winter\": melted|scorched}, half frozen. \"Say it again,\" Lyra says. Kael doesn't. He just looks at you.\ntext [d5c1cc] = 凯尔和莱拉在庭院里对峙，两人之间的{season == \"winter\": 雪|草地}一半{season == \"winter\": 融化|焦枯}、一半冻结。“再说一遍，”莱拉说。凯尔没有说。他只是看向你。\n# en: Two students facing each other in a snowy courtyard, one breathing frost, the other with sparks at her fingertips.\nscene [38c0d1] = 积雪的庭院里，两个学生面对面站着，一个呼出寒霜，另一个指尖迸着火星。\n# en: Step between them\nopt.left.label [f242e3] = 站到他们中间\n# en: Lyra's sparks catch your sleeve, Kael's frost puts it out. They both stare at you. The fight, somehow, is over.\nopt.left.say.1 [5ee694] = 莱拉的火星燎到你的袖子，凯尔的霜又把它扑灭。两人都盯着你。不知怎么，这场架就这么结束了。\n# en: Walk past\nopt.right.label [d5359c] = 径直走过\n# en: Behind you, something cracks: ice, or temper. At dinner they sit at opposite ends of the hall.\nopt.right.say.1 [d025e8] = 身后有什么裂开了：是冰，还是脾气。晚饭时，他们坐在大厅的两头。\n\n@event slice_burned_letter\n# en: Ashes in the Grate\ntitle [4b34a6] = 壁炉里的灰\n# en: Lyra is feeding a letter into the common-room fire, a corner at a time. Black seal. She doesn't look round. \"It says 'well done',\" she says. \"It doesn't. I'm checking whether it burns better if I pretend.\"\ntext [847047] = 莱拉正把一封信一角一角地喂进公共休息室的壁炉。黑色封蜡。她没有回头。“信上写着‘做得好’，”她说，“其实没有。我在试试，假装有的话它会不会烧得更旺。”\n# en: A fireplace where the corner of a letter with a black seal is curling into flame.\nscene [e9282e] = 一座壁炉，一封盖着黑色封蜡的信的一角正卷进火焰里。\n# en: Sit with her\nopt.left.label [654e0a] = 陪她坐着\n# en: You watch it burn together. \"She's never said it,\" Lyra says, to the fire. \"Not once.\" You don't say anything, and that seems to be right.\nopt.left.say.1 [437064] = 你们一起看着它烧完。“她从没说过，”莱拉对着火说，“一次都没有。”你什么也没说，而这似乎正是对的。\n# en: Make a joke\nopt.right.label [5f1ec3] = 开个玩笑\n# en: She almost smiles. Then she puts the rest of the letter in at once and leaves you with the smoke.\nopt.right.say.1 [282f1d] = 她差点笑了。然后她把剩下的信一股脑儿丢进火里，留你和烟待在一起。\n\n@event slice_hollow_whisper\n# en: A Voice in the Water\ntitle [b31d19] = 水中的声音\n# en: At the lakeshore your reflection speaks before you do. \"You're running low,\" it says, kindly. \"{mana < 15: I could fill you up.|I could make you stronger.} Just say yes. Saying yes costs nothing.\"\ntext [f94671] = 在湖岸边，你的倒影先于你开口。“你快见底了，”它温和地说，“{mana < 15: 我可以把你填满。|我可以让你更强。}只要说‘好’。说‘好’什么都不花。”\n# en: A student kneeling at the edge of a still lake at night, their reflection smiling when they are not.\nscene [4bb1f8] = 夜里，一个学生跪在平静的湖边，水中的倒影在笑，而他自己并没有笑。\n# en: Say yes\nopt.left.label [c73ccb] = 说“好”\n# en: Cold pours into you, generous and endless. When you stand, your reflection is a little slower to stand with you.\nopt.left.say.1 [3ad748] = 寒意涌进你的身体，慷慨而无穷无尽。你站起来时，倒影慢了一拍才跟着站起来。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.right.label [96da1a] = 转身离开\n# en: The reflection's smile stays on the water after you've gone. You don't look back. You can feel it not minding.\nopt.right.say.1 [8bbf02] = 你走后，倒影的笑容还留在水面上。你没有回头。你能感觉到它并不在意。\n\n@event slice_market_haggle\n# en: The Spice Stall\ntitle [8682c8] = 香料摊\n# en: The stall-holder in the village market wants thirty crowns for {~a jar of starpepper|a twist of dragon-chilli|a pot of cliff-bee honey}. Mabel swears by it for colds. The stall-holder swears by his prices.\ntext [ed6115] = 小镇集市上的摊主要三十克朗卖{~一罐星辰胡椒|一束龙椒|一罐崖蜂蜜}。梅布尔发誓它治感冒有奇效。摊主则发誓他的价钱童叟无欺。\n# en: A crowded village market stall heaped with jars of glowing spices and a stall-holder with an enormous moustache.\nscene [ba55f9] = 拥挤的小镇集市摊位，堆满发光的香料罐，摊主留着一大把胡子。\n# en: Haggle\nopt.left.label [90048b] = 讨价还价\n# en: Eight crowns and a compliment on your persistence. Mabel says you've been robbed, but only slightly.\nopt.left.say.1 [ec3b10] = 八克朗，外加一句夸你有毅力。梅布尔说你被坑了，不过只坑了一点点。\n# en: Fifteen crowns. He throws in a free sneeze's worth of pepper, which you did not want.\nopt.left.say.2 [93bde1] = 十五克朗。他还白送你一个喷嚏的胡椒量——你并不想要。\n# en: You pay full price and somehow promise to come back for more. He waves you off like a nephew.\nopt.left.say.3 [3758d8] = 你付了全价，还不知怎么答应了下次再来买。他像送别侄子一样挥手送你走。\n# en: Leave it\nopt.right.label [3e2fed] = 算了\n# en: You walk on. You catch a cold the next week, out of spite.\nopt.right.say.1 [128e73] = 你走开了。下周你就感冒了，纯属赌气。\n\n@event slice_wager\n# en: The Tower Wager\ntitle [013b2f] = 钟楼赌约\n# en: Half the common room is betting on whether you can climb the clock tower before the bell. Corvin is holding the purse, which is either reassuring or not.\ntext [3371c6] = 半个公共休息室的人在赌你能不能在钟响前爬上钟楼。钱袋在科尔文手里——这让人放心，也可能不。\n# en: A knot of students around a table where coins and a pocket watch are being stacked as bets.\nscene [217f9f] = 一群学生围着一张桌子，桌上叠着当赌注的硬币和一块怀表。\n# en: Climb\nopt.left.label [2c7b2a] = 爬\n# en: {~You make it with a heartbeat to spare.|The bell rings as your hand touches the top.} They carry you round the common room on a chair.\nopt.left.say.1 [91cad2] = {~你提前一个心跳的工夫登了顶。|你的手碰到塔顶的那一刻，钟声响了。}他们把你放在椅子上，抬着绕公共休息室转了一圈。\n# en: The bell rings when you're two-thirds up, and the caretaker has to talk you down with a ladder. They'll tell it for years.\nopt.left.say.2 [e4a38c] = 你才爬了三分之二，钟就响了，最后是管理员架着梯子把你劝下来的。他们会把这事讲上好几年。\n# en: Decline politely\nopt.right.label [c5820d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: The crowd groans. Corvin pockets the purse thoughtfully and you decide not to ask.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f87ed] = 人群一片哀叹。科尔文若有所思地把钱袋揣进兜里，你决定不问。\n\n@event slice_frost_duel\n# en: Frost Against Fire\ntitle [63f7ba] = 霜与火\n# en: Kael asks for a practice duel, which for him is an astonishing number of words. \"Not to win,\" he adds. \"I want to see what you do when it's cold.\"\ntext [4a4fd1] = 凯尔请你和他练一场决斗——对他来说，这已经是惊人的字数。“不是为了赢，”他补充道，“我想看看天冷的时候你会怎么做。”\n# en: A duelling ring at dawn, frost spreading from one side and a faint warmth glowing from the other.\nscene [8d420a] = 黎明时分的决斗场，一边蔓延着寒霜，另一边泛着微弱的暖光。\n# en: Duel him straight\nopt.left.label [a7dfd1] = 正面和他决斗\n# en: You win by a whisker, breathing steam. Kael bows, and his hands, for once, are warm when he shakes yours. Half the duelling club, it turns out, was watching from the doorway.\nopt.left.say.1 [07b3d4] = 你以毫厘之差获胜，呼出白汽。凯尔鞠了一躬，和你握手时，他的手难得是暖的。原来半个决斗社都在门口看着。\n# en: A draw, of sorts: you're both on the ground. He laughs, a short surprised sound, and helps you up.\nopt.left.say.2 [a850c0] = 算是平局：你们俩都倒在地上。他笑了，短促而意外的一声，然后把你拉了起来。\n# en: He wins, gently. \"You stopped when it got cold,\" he says. \"Don't.\"\nopt.left.say.3 [9548e4] = 他赢了，赢得很温和。“天一冷，你就停下了，”他说，“别停。”\n# en: Decline; offer him tea instead\nopt.right.label [3c2e56] = 婉拒，改请他喝茶\n# en: He thinks about this for a long time. \"Tea,\" he says. It freezes in his cup. You make him another.\nopt.right.say.1 [c0affc] = 他想了很久。“茶，”他说。茶在他杯子里冻住了。你又给他泡了一杯。\n# en: Answer with fire\nopt.up.label [589d8f] = 以火回应\n# en: Your flame meets his frost in a column of steam that fills the ring and brings half the corridor running. When it clears, Kael is smiling at you, properly, for the first time.\nopt.up.say.1 [f78aa4] = 你的火焰迎上他的寒霜，一柱蒸汽填满了整个决斗场，引得半条走廊的人都跑了过来。散去时，凯尔正对你微笑——第一次真正地微笑。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/slice_2b.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/slice_2b.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event evo_first_spark\n# en: The First Spark\ntitle [5b0523] = 第一缕火花\n# en: Evocation I begins with a single instruction from the lectern: \"Make heat. Not fire. Heat.\" Half the class makes nothing. One student makes a small, apologetic fire.\ntext [db1b7b] = 塑能术 I 以讲台上的一句指令开场：“造热。不是火。是热。”半个班什么都没造出来。有一个学生造出了一小团满怀歉意的火。\n# en: A lecture hall with scorch marks on every desk, students holding their palms up as if testing for rain.\nscene [47f33f] = 每张课桌上都有焦痕的讲堂，学生们摊开手掌，像在试探下没下雨。\n# en: Aim for heat, carefully\nopt.left.label [ac4d80] = 小心地只求热\n# en: Your palm warms like a stone left in the sun. The lecturer nods, which is better than most get.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe739e] = 你的掌心暖得像晒过太阳的石头。讲师点了点头，这已经胜过大多数人了。\n# en: Aim for fire, boldly\nopt.right.label [639beb] = 大胆地求火\n# en: A neat little flame, exactly where you meant it. The lecturer writes your name down. Approvingly, you hope.\nopt.right.say.1 [013ce4] = 一小簇利落的火苗，正好出现在你想要的位置。讲师记下了你的名字。你希望是带着赞许的。\n# en: Your sleeve learns more about fire than you do. The lecture continues around the smell.\nopt.right.say.2 [090858] = 你的袖子比你更懂火了。课在这股焦味里继续。\n\n@event evo_puddle_drill\n# en: Puddle Drill\ntitle [3a9384] = 水洼操练\n# en: Tidecalling half of the course: a puddle each, and the task of making it stand up and stay up. Yours trembles, considers it, and lies back down.\ntext [4ca6c9] = 课程的唤潮术部分：每人一个水洼，任务是让它站起来并保持站立。你的那个颤了颤，考虑了一下，又躺了回去。\n# en: Students crouched over puddles in a stone courtyard, trying to make the water stand up.\nscene [83fa0c] = 学生们蹲在石砌庭院的水洼前，试图让水站起来。\n# en: Practise until it stands\nopt.left.label [1dfa4e] = 练到它站起来\n# en: By the end of the hour your puddle stands like a sulky child. It counts.\nopt.left.say.1 [106f8b] = 一节课快结束时，你的水洼像个赌气的孩子一样站着。算数。\n# en: Watch the student next to you\nopt.right.label [b884e9] = 观察旁边同学的做法\n# en: You learn their trick, and also that they hum while they cast. You hum too. It works, inexplicably.\nopt.right.say.1 [c0e062] = 你学到了对方的诀窍，还发现他们施法时会哼歌。你也哼了。莫名其妙地，管用。\n\n@event evo_misfire\n# en: Misfire\ntitle [3350b4] = 失手\n# en: Someone's tidecalling meets someone else's flame in the middle of the room. The hall fills with scalding steam and twenty students who all have opinions.\ntext [e661e8] = 某人的唤潮术在房间正中撞上了另一个人的火焰。讲堂里灌满了滚烫的蒸汽，还有二十个各有意见的学生。\n# en: A lecture hall filling with steam after a spell meets a bucket of water at the wrong moment.\nscene [ffbe1f] = 一道咒语在错误的时刻撞上一桶水，讲堂里蒸汽弥漫。\n# en: Shield the front row\nopt.left.label [1e4bc0] = 护住前排\n# en: You take the worst of it on your sleeves. The front row looks at you the way people look at lifeboats.\nopt.left.say.1 [d4298c] = 最烫的那一下你用袖子挡了。前排的人看你的眼神，就像看救生艇。\n# en: Get out and open the windows\nopt.right.label [eb7ddc] = 出去开窗\n# en: The steam pours out into the courtyard and becomes, briefly, a very small cloud with a grudge.\nopt.right.say.1 [3f970d] = 蒸汽涌进庭院，短暂地变成了一朵很小、很记仇的云。\n\n@event evo_tide_wall\n# en: The Tide Wall\ntitle [f1844c] = 潮墙\n# en: Evocation II takes the class to the lakeshore. The exercise: raise a wall of water and hold it while a classmate walks past behind it. Your classmate looks nervous. So do you.\ntext [5eb5bd] = 塑能术 II 把全班带到湖岸。练习：升起一道水墙并撑住，让一位同学从墙后走过。那位同学看起来很紧张。你也是。\n# en: A student holding back a wall of lake water on the shore while classmates watch from behind them.\nscene [23ccc0] = 一名学生在岸边挡住一道湖水筑成的墙，同学们在后面看着。\n# en: Hold it steady\nopt.left.label [d6f0e9] = 稳稳撑住\n# en: The wall holds. Your classmate walks past dry and amazed. You let it down gently, which is the hard part.\nopt.left.say.1 [240cd4] = 水墙撑住了。同学干干爽爽地走过去，满脸惊叹。你把墙轻轻放下——这才是最难的部分。\n# en: The wall holds until it doesn't, and comes down on both of you. Your classmate is very wet and very gracious about it. You shiver until dinner.\nopt.left.say.2 [51a46b] = 水墙撑住了，直到撑不住，哗地砸在你们俩身上。同学浑身湿透，却对此十分大度。你一直哆嗦到晚饭。\n# en: Ask to go second\nopt.right.label [208e6d] = 请求第二个上\n# en: You watch three people do it first and learn from all of their mistakes.\nopt.right.say.1 [2b89b8] = 你看着三个人先做，把他们的错误统统学了个遍。\n\n@event evo_lattice\n# en: The Flame Lattice\ntitle [7d30cd] = 火焰格网\n# en: Fire woven into a lattice, each strand holding the others up. The lecturer says the founders built wards like this. The lecturer also says you have twenty minutes.\ntext [954a4b] = 火被编成格网，每一股都撑着其他几股。讲师说创始者当年就是这样筑护界的。讲师还说你有二十分钟。\n# en: Interlocking threads of fire hanging in the air of a lecture hall like a glowing net.\nscene [827db1] = 交错的火线悬在讲堂半空，像一张发光的网。\n# en: Build it slowly\nopt.left.label [03433a] = 慢慢搭\n# en: Your lattice is small and ugly and holds perfectly. The lecturer says \"Founder-ish,\" which you choose to take as praise.\nopt.left.say.1 [068b5a] = 你的格网又小又丑，却撑得完美无缺。讲师说“有点创始者的味道”，你决定把它当成表扬。\n# en: Build it big\nopt.right.label [33df52] = 搭个大的\n# en: {~It's magnificent for nine seconds.|It holds just long enough for everyone to see it.} Then the strands find each other and the whole thing sighs into sparks, and you understand exactly why.\nopt.right.say.1 [f9a733] = {~它壮丽了整整九秒。|它撑得刚好够每个人看清。}然后火线彼此找到对方，整个东西叹息一声，化作火星，而你完全明白了原因。\n# en: The strands find each other in the first second, and the whole thing goes up in your face. You spend the rest of the lesson picking sparks out of your hair.\nopt.right.say.2 [aa5b3b] = 第一秒火线就缠到了一起，整个东西在你脸前炸开。那节课剩下的时间，你都在从头发里挑火星。\n\n@event evo_storm_pairing\n# en: Storm Pairing\ntitle [6e518e] = 风暴搭档\n# en: Advanced Evocation pairs students for a storm: one calls the rain, the other the lightning, and neither is allowed to let go first. You're paired with {partner}.\ntext [bcffa7] = 高等塑能术让学生两两搭档召唤风暴：一人唤雨，一人引雷，谁都不许先松手。你的搭档是{partner}。\n# en: Two students on a windswept tower top, one calling rain, the other calling lightning, the sky between them churning.\nscene [0f5fd7] = 狂风中的塔顶上，两名学生一个唤雨一个引雷，两人之间的天空翻涌不息。\n# en: Call the lightning\nopt.left.label [26bb28] = 引雷\n# en: The storm answers both of you at once. {partner} laughs into the wind, soaked and astonished.\nopt.left.say.1 [2fb5cc] = 风暴同时回应了你们俩。{partner}迎着风大笑，浑身湿透，满脸惊愕。\n# en: The storm answers both of you at once. Lyra looks at you as though you've finally said something worth hearing.\nopt.left.say.1@lyra [e5dc7b] = 风暴同时回应了你们俩。莱拉看着你，仿佛你终于说了句值得一听的话。\n# en: The lightning goes where it likes. You both spend an afternoon in the infirmary, comparing burns.\nopt.left.say.2 [f7819a] = 闪电想去哪儿就去哪儿。你们俩在医务室待了一下午，互相比较烧伤。\n# en: Call the rain, and let them lead\nopt.right.label [f3854c] = 唤雨，让对方主导\n# en: You hold the rain steady while {partner} draws the lightning down. Afterwards {they} thank{s} you for not trying to steal it.\nopt.right.say.1 [be557c] = 你稳住雨幕，{partner}把闪电引了下来。之后{they}谢谢你没有抢风头。\n\n@event evo_final_practical\n# en: The Final Practical\ntitle [035f37] = 期末实践考\n# en: The last practical of Advanced Evocation: melt an ice dummy without touching the floor around it. Professor Thorne is on the panel, and he is not blinking.\ntext [9b1d08] = 高等塑能术的最后一场实践考：融化一个冰人偶，不许碰到周围的地面。索恩教授在考官席上，眼都不眨。\n# en: An examination hall cleared of desks, a single student facing three examiners and a target dummy made of ice.\nscene [49ac8b] = 清空了课桌的考场，一名学生面对三位考官和一个冰做的靶子人偶。\n# en: Fire, precisely\nopt.left.label [c25e11] = 用火，精准地\n# en: The ice runs off the dummy and not a drop touches the floor. Thorne says \"Hm,\" and writes something underlined.\nopt.left.say.1 [0fdd37] = 冰从人偶上流下，没有一滴碰到地面。索恩说了声“嗯”，写下一行带下划线的字。\n# en: The dummy melts, and so does a fair part of the floor. Thorne writes something with a question mark.\nopt.left.say.2 [20fe15] = 人偶融了，地板也融了不少。索恩写下一行带问号的字。\n# en: Water, patiently\nopt.right.label [21c7ce] = 用水，耐心地\n# en: You draw the cold out of the ice with water instead of fire. It takes twice as long. The panel watches every minute.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c406a] = 你用水而不是火把冰里的寒意抽了出来。花了两倍的时间。考官们一分钟都没错过。\n\n@event duel_first_practice\n# en: Footwork\ntitle [86c7d4] = 步法\n# en: Lyra runs practice like a general. \"Footwork,\" she says. \"If your feet are wrong, your spell is a rumour.\" She pairs you with a dummy called Gerald the Second.\ntext [ef78a6] = 莱拉像将军一样主持练习。“步法，”她说，“步法错了，你的咒语就只是个传闻。”她给你配了一个叫“杰拉尔德二世”的假人。\n# en: A duelling hall with chalk circles on the floor and practice dummies with names painted on their chests.\nscene [f28b25] = 地上画着粉笔圈的决斗厅，练习假人胸口上漆着名字。\n# en: Drill footwork\nopt.left.label [7dee9f] = 练步法\n# en: Two hours of stepping in circles. Gerald the Second wins every round. Your feet, however, are now correct.\nopt.left.say.1 [9d28ca] = 绕圈走了两个小时。杰拉尔德二世赢了每一回合。不过你的脚，现在是对的了。\n# en: Spar for real\nopt.right.label [9e070e] = 真刀真枪对练\n# en: You land a clean disarm on your partner. Across the hall, Lyra raises an eyebrow, which is her version of a standing ovation.\nopt.right.say.1 [71d335] = 你干净利落地缴了对手的械。大厅另一头，莱拉挑了挑眉——这是她版本的起立鼓掌。\n# en: You spend the evening relearning which way is up. Lyra says that's the first lesson anyway.\nopt.right.say.2 [99acac] = 你一晚上都在重新学习哪边是上。莱拉说反正这本来就是第一课。\n\n@event duel_footwork_feud\n# en: The Footwork Feud\ntitle [cf293b] = 步法之争\n# en: Bram says the Circle's footwork is \"Emberhall nonsense\" and Lyra says Bram's footwork is \"a hazard to shipping\". Both of them look at you to settle it.\ntext [8b7ae0] = 布拉姆说决斗社的步法是“烬厅的胡闹”，莱拉说布拉姆的步法是“航运公害”。两人都看着你，等你裁决。\n# en: Two students arguing in the middle of a duelling circle while the rest of the club pretends not to listen.\nscene [a9dbb3] = 两名学生在决斗圈中央争吵，社里其他人假装没在听。\n# en: Side with Lyra\nopt.left.label [451590] = 站莱拉这边\n# en: Lyra nods as if this were obvious. Bram takes a demerit book out of his pocket, looks at it, and puts it back. Progress.\nopt.left.say.1 [dfa265] = 莱拉点点头，好像这是理所当然。布拉姆从口袋里掏出记过本，看了看，又放了回去。有进步。\n# en: Side with Bram\nopt.right.label [1bec38] = 站布拉姆这边\n# en: Bram looks startled to be agreed with. Lyra looks at you like a puzzle she will solve later, with fire.\nopt.right.say.1 [4720c9] = 有人赞同自己，布拉姆显得很吃惊。莱拉看你的样子，像在看一道她以后会用火来解的谜题。\n\n@event duel_fixture\n# en: Fixture Against Thornwick\ntitle [d8c992] = 对阵荆木\n# en: Thornwick Academy's duelling team has crossed the hills for a fixture. The Circle needs one more duellist in the line-up, and the captain is looking at you.\ntext [7ff7a2] = 荆木学院的决斗队翻山过来比赛。决斗社的阵容还差一名决斗者，社长正看着你。\n# en: A duelling green with rival banners, students from both academies packed onto benches.\nscene [0b0deb] = 挂着两校旗帜的决斗场，两所学院的学生挤满长凳。\n# en: Duel for the Circle\nopt.left.label [4b23b1] = 为决斗社出战\n# en: You win your bout, and the Circle wins the fixture by a whisker. Someone carries you a few steps before remembering they don't like you.\nopt.left.say.1 [8c53d7] = 你赢了自己那场，决斗社以毫厘之差赢下比赛。有人把你扛起来走了几步，才想起他们并不喜欢你。\n# en: A draw. The Circle loses by one bout, but nobody blames you, and {around(lyra): Lyra|the captain} says \"acceptable\" twice.\nopt.left.say.2 [ec8511] = 平局。决斗社输了一场，但没人怪你，{around(lyra): 莱拉|队长}说了两遍“还行”。\n# en: The Thornwick duellist is very good and very polite about it, and every bruise you collect is a polite one. You hate both.\nopt.left.say.3 [1736bb] = 荆木的决斗者非常厉害，而且对此非常有礼貌，你身上的每一处淤青都挨得很有礼貌。两样你都讨厌。\n# en: Offer to be the reserve\nopt.right.label [44cb08] = 主动当替补\n# en: You hold coats and bring water. It turns out reserves see everything, including how Thornwick cheats.\nopt.right.say.1 [eac3f5] = 你帮人拿外套、递水。原来替补什么都看得见，包括荆木是怎么作弊的。\n# en: Let the fire run\nopt.up.label [8041ce] = 放开野火\n# en: You hold the fire until it bites, and then let it go. It runs across the hall and through the Thornwick duellist's wards like paper. The Circle goes mad. Your sleeve is on fire, which you notice afterwards.\nopt.up.say.1 [834712] = 你握着火直到它咬人，然后松手。它奔过整个大厅，像穿纸一样穿过荆木学院决斗者的护界。决斗社疯了。你的袖子着了火，你是事后才发现的。\n\n@event duel_captain_call\n# en: The Captain's Question\ntitle [dfe526] = 社长的问题\n# en: After practice, the captain sits beside you. \"When I graduate, someone has to run this place,\" she says. \"Someone who turns up. You turn up.\"\ntext [3fd3c8] = 练习结束后，社长在你身边坐下。“我毕业以后，总得有人管这地方，”她说，“得是个会准时出现的人。你会。”\n# en: An empty duelling hall at night, two students sitting on the edge of a chalk circle.\nscene [0d20d7] = 夜里空荡荡的决斗厅，两名学生坐在粉笔圈边上。\n# en: Say you'll think about it\nopt.left.label [b4769e] = 说你会考虑\n# en: \"Think fast,\" she says. \"Feet first, then spells.\" You're fairly sure that's a yes.\nopt.left.say.1 [a70c37] = “考虑快点，”她说，“先动脚，再施法。”你很确定这算是答应了。\n# en: Say you're not the type\nopt.right.label [9fe8f9] = 说你不是那块料\n# en: \"Nobody's the type,\" she says. \"That's why it has to be someone who turns up.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [cd23ec] = “没人是那块料，”她说，“所以才得是个会准时出现的人。”\n\n@event lib_shelving_rush\n# en: The Shelving Rush\ntitle [5625e2] = 上架高峰\n# en: Exam week, and every book in the library has been borrowed, read and returned at once. The cart is taller than you are. Footnote is sitting on top of it.\ntext [6fa1df] = 考试周，图书馆里的每本书都同时被借走、读完又还回来。书车比你还高。脚注正坐在最顶上。\n# en: A library cart overflowing with returned books, a student pushing it at a run between towering shelves.\nscene [7fe4c3] = 一辆堆满还书的图书车，一名学生推着它在高耸的书架间小跑。\n# en: Shelve properly\nopt.left.label [bac2cb] = 规规矩矩地上架\n# en: Every book in its place by closing. Footnote has moved to the top shelf to supervise.\nopt.left.say.1 [3b9e2e] = 闭馆前每本书都归了位。脚注已经挪到最高一层书架上监工了。\n# en: Shelve quickly\nopt.right.label [9d5012] = 快速上架\n# en: The books are on shelves. Most of them are even on the right shelves. Someone will find out in a decade.\nopt.right.say.1 [79d70e] = 书都在架子上了。大部分甚至在对的架子上。十年后会有人发现的。\n# en: Animate the cart\nopt.up.label [077b83] = 活化书车\n# en: The cart trundles off by itself, putting books back in roughly the right places, and you follow behind it fixing the rough parts. It takes everything you've got to keep it from shelving Footnote.\nopt.up.say.1 [765f8d] = 书车自己咕噜咕噜地走了，把书放回大致对的位置，你跟在后面修正那些“大致”。你得用尽全力，才能拦住它把脚注也上了架。\n\n@event lib_overdue_mage\n# en: The Overdue Mage\ntitle [99bf32] = 逾期的法师\n# en: An old mage returns a book sixty years overdue and asks, with great dignity, what the fine is. The ledger says the fine is more than the library.\ntext [6a40d1] = 一位老法师归还一本逾期六十年的书，并十分庄重地询问罚金是多少。账本上的罚金比图书馆还值钱。\n# en: An elderly mage at the library desk clutching a book that is sixty years overdue.\nscene [b70648] = 一位年迈的法师站在图书馆柜台前，紧攥着一本逾期六十年的书。\n# en: Waive it\nopt.left.label [899866] = 免了吧\n# en: The old mage thanks you and leaves a sweet on the desk. Later you find out he taught Professor Thorne.\nopt.left.say.1 [ef4397] = 老法师谢过你，在柜台上留下一颗糖。后来你才知道，他教过索恩教授。\n# en: Charge it, by the book\nopt.right.label [396965] = 照章收费\n# en: The mage pays in coins so old the bank has to look them up. The head librarian is delighted, and gives you the collector's share of the fine, as is tradition.\nopt.right.say.1 [9de00f] = 法师付的硬币老到银行都得去查。馆长高兴坏了，还按惯例分给你一份收罚款的提成。\n\n@event lib_footnote_strike\n# en: Footnote Goes on Strike\ntitle [ec48a4] = 脚注罢工\n# en: Footnote has lain down across the reference desk and will not move. Students are queueing. The cat has made its demands clear: none.\ntext [8627c8] = 脚注横躺在参考咨询台上，一动不动。学生们在排队。这只猫已经把它的诉求表达得很清楚了：没有诉求。\n# en: A large library cat lying across a reference desk, refusing to move, while students queue in front of it.\nscene [bcfc48] = 一只大个儿的图书馆猫横躺在参考咨询台上不肯挪窝，学生们在它面前排起了队。\n# en: Bribe him with fish\nopt.left.label [ca7565] = 用鱼贿赂它\n# en: Footnote accepts the fish, the apology and the chair you bring him. The queue resumes.\nopt.left.say.1 [d9ebb3] = 脚注接受了鱼、道歉，还有你搬来的椅子。队伍恢复了流动。\n# en: Work around him\nopt.right.label [ea0c37] = 绕开它干活\n# en: You stamp books on the far end of the desk for an hour. Footnote watches with the calm of a cat who has won.\nopt.right.say.1 [40e4a9] = 你在柜台另一头盖了一小时的章。脚注以胜利者的平静注视着你。\n\n@event court_fountain_freeze\n# en: The Frozen Fountain\ntitle [f87a8e] = 冻住的喷泉\n# en: The courtyard fountain has frozen in the middle of a splash. The water hangs in the air like glass. A first-year is trying to lick it.\ntext [abc188] = 庭院的喷泉在水花溅起的一瞬冻住了。水像玻璃一样悬在半空。一个一年级生正想去舔它。\n# en: A courtyard fountain frozen mid-splash, the water hanging in the air like glass.\nscene [8a0a42] = 庭院喷泉冻在了水花飞溅的瞬间，水像玻璃一样悬在空中。\n# en: Stop the first-year\nopt.left.label [d24736] = 拦住那个一年级生\n# en: You pull the first-year away just as the ice cracks. For a moment you'd swear you saw a black star reflected in it.\nopt.left.say.1 [267623] = 冰裂开的那一刻，你正好把一年级生拉开。有一瞬间，你发誓看见冰里映着一颗黑色的星。\n# en: Study the ice\nopt.right.label [e6dcae] = 研究这块冰\n# en: The ice is colder than ice should be. Your notes say \"colder than ice should be\" three times.\nopt.right.say.1 [3744c0] = 这冰比冰该有的还冷。你的笔记里把“比冰该有的还冷”写了三遍。\n# en: Freeze the first-year free\nopt.up.label [f11dbb] = 把那个新生冻解开\n# en: You put a hand on the ice and pull the cold back into yourself, just round the first-year's tongue. They come free with a pop and a look of betrayal at the fountain. Your hand aches with cold all afternoon.\nopt.up.say.1 [f4b007] = 你把手按在冰上，把寒气收回自己身体里，只收那个新生舌头周围的一圈。他啵的一声脱开了，朝喷泉投去被背叛的眼神。你的手冻得疼了一下午。\n\n@event court_rumour_mill\n# en: The Rumour Mill\ntitle [b2db14] = 流言工坊\n# en: {gossip} has news, which is to say gossip, which is to say three different stories about the same broken window. \"You'll want to hear this,\" {they} say{s}.\ntext [ba7e30] = {gossip}有新闻——也就是八卦——也就是关于同一扇破窗户的三个不同版本。“你会想听的，”{they}说。\n# en: Students huddled around the courtyard fountain trading whispers, one of them clearly enjoying it most.\nscene [bc5c84] = 学生们围在庭院喷泉边交换耳语，其中一个显然最乐在其中。\n# en: Listen to all of it\nopt.left.label [71c08a] = 全都听一遍\n# en: You hear all three stories. Two are true. One is about you.\nopt.left.say.1 [1839fa] = 三个版本你都听了。两个是真的。一个是关于你的。\n# en: You hear all three stories. Nessa is already writing the fourth.\nopt.left.say.1@nessa [198fa7] = 三个版本你都听了。内莎已经在写第四个了。\n# en: Make up a better one\nopt.right.label [ed234b] = 编个更好的\n# en: Your story is better than all three. By dinner it's the official version.\nopt.right.say.1 [2aa741] = 你的版本比那三个都好。到晚饭时，它已经成了官方说法。\n# en: Ask what really happened\nopt.up.label [00fcc8] = 问问到底怎么回事\n# en: You ask the right question and {gossip} tells you the truth, startled: the window broke because someone sneezed during a levitation. It is the least interesting story of the four. {They} look{s} at you as if you'd cheated.\nopt.up.say.1 [33bd26] = 你问对了问题，{gossip}吃了一惊，说了实话：窗户破了，是因为有人在悬浮术练习时打了个喷嚏。这是四个版本里最无聊的一个。{they}看着你，好像你作了弊。\n\n@event lake_skimming\n# en: Skimming Stones\ntitle [a14e0e] = 打水漂\n# en: {friend} is skimming stones on the Mirror Lake. The water is so still each stone leaves rings that go on forever. \"Seven,\" {they} say{s}. \"Beat that.\"\ntext [90f788] = {friend}在镜湖上打水漂。湖面静得出奇，每块石头留下的涟漪都仿佛永远不会散。“七下，”{they}说，“来比比。”\n# en: Two students skimming stones across a perfectly still lake at dusk.\nscene [d7fdb5] = 黄昏时分，两名学生在一片静止如镜的湖面上打水漂。\n# en: Try to beat it\nopt.left.label [1284ba] = 试着赢过去\n# en: Nine skips. {friend} demands a rematch and gets one. The afternoon disappears into stones.\nopt.left.say.1 [1f9fa1] = 九下。{friend}要求再来一局，也如愿了。整个下午都消失在石子里。\n# en: Your stone sinks with great dignity. {friend} laughs until {they} have to sit down.\nopt.left.say.2 [533d65] = 你的石头带着极大的尊严沉了下去。{friend}笑得只好坐下来。\n# en: Just watch the rings\nopt.right.label [39fffc] = 就看涟漪\n# en: You sit and watch the rings spread until the light goes. Neither of you says much. It's one of the good afternoons.\nopt.right.say.1 [2ebf1f] = 你坐着看涟漪扩散，直到天光散尽。你们俩都没说什么。这是那种美好的下午之一。\n\n@event lake_reflection_bet\n# en: The Reflection Bet\ntitle [7b2fea] = 倒影的赌约\n# en: Your reflection in the Mirror Lake is holding a coin you don't have. It flips it. It catches it. It offers it to you.\ntext [ad0e65] = 你在镜湖里的倒影拿着一枚你并没有的硬币。它抛起，接住，然后递向你。\n# en: A student staring into a still lake where their reflection appears to be holding a coin.\nscene [407b4a] = 一名学生凝视着平静的湖面，水中的倒影似乎握着一枚硬币。\n# en: Take the coin\nopt.left.label [4cb026] = 接过硬币\n# en: The coin is real, and cold, and you can't remember putting it in your pocket.\nopt.left.say.1 [67628e] = 硬币是真的，冰凉，而你想不起自己是什么时候把它放进口袋的。\n# en: Shake your head\nopt.right.label [5e0346] = 摇头\n# en: Your reflection shrugs, pockets the coin and walks out of the frame before you do.\nopt.right.say.1 [3c806f] = 你的倒影耸耸肩，把硬币揣进兜里，先你一步走出了画面。\n# en: Lift it before it offers\nopt.up.label [aff1dc] = 趁它递来之前摸走\n# en: You lift the coin from your reflection's hand before it can offer it. The reflection looks at its empty hand, then at you, deeply affronted. For a week you can't quite meet your own eye in the washroom mirror.\nopt.up.say.1 [1761bb] = 趁倒影还没递过来，你就从它手里摸走了那枚硬币。倒影看看自己空空的手，又看看你，深感冒犯。接下来一周，你在盥洗室的镜子前总不太敢直视自己的眼睛。\n\n@event ref_food_fight\n# en: Pudding Diplomacy\ntitle [f97acc] = 布丁外交\n# en: A pudding sails across the refectory and lands in Bram's lap. The hall goes silent. Every eye turns to the table it came from, which is yours.\ntext [e5a752] = 一块布丁飞过膳堂，落在布拉姆腿上。大厅一片死寂。所有目光都转向它飞来的那张桌子——你的那张。\n# en: A refectory mid-food-fight, puddings in the air, a prefect rising slowly from behind a table.\nscene [b7e5a2] = 膳堂里一场食物大战正酣，布丁在空中飞，一位级长从桌子后面缓缓站起。\n# en: Own up\nopt.left.label [3a0128] = 承认是我\n# en: Bram writes you a demerit, then pauses. \"Good throw,\" he says, almost without wanting to.\nopt.left.say.1 [d32724] = 布拉姆给你记了一次过，然后顿了顿。“扔得好，”他说，几乎是不情愿的。\n# en: Throw another\nopt.right.label [7417cc] = 再扔一块\n# en: The second pudding starts a war that the refectory will talk about for years. Historians will argue about who won. You know.\nopt.right.say.1 [2e4f89] = 第二块布丁引发了一场膳堂会议论好几年的战争。史学家会争论谁赢了。你知道。\n# en: Turn the hall with a joke\nopt.up.label [f4f416] = 一句笑话扭转全场\n# en: You stand up, bow to Bram, and say the pudding was meant as a tribute. The hall laughs, and so, against his will, does Bram. He fishes the cherry out of his lap and eats it.\nopt.up.say.1 [1c8395] = 你站起来，朝布拉姆鞠了一躬，说那块布丁本意是致敬。整个大厅都笑了，布拉姆也身不由己地笑了。他从腿上捡起那颗樱桃，吃了。\n\n@event ref_cook_secret\n# en: The Cook's Secret\ntitle [c0a5b6] = 厨娘的秘密\n# en: The cook catches you lingering by the kitchen door. \"You look like someone who misses proper soup,\" she says, and hands you a bowl before you can deny it.\ntext [1cf0fd] = 厨娘逮到你在厨房门口徘徊。“你看着就像个想念正经汤的人，”她说，不等你否认就递来一碗。\n# en: A huge kitchen behind the refectory, an enormous cook stirring a pot with a spoon the size of an oar.\nscene [ea98ca] = 膳堂后面的大厨房，一位魁梧的厨娘用船桨那么大的勺子搅着锅。\n# en: Eat it slowly\nopt.left.label [3da080] = 慢慢地喝\n# en: It tastes like the kitchen at home. You don't say so. She can tell anyway.\nopt.left.say.1 [d6f79e] = 这味道像家里的厨房。你没说出口。她反正看得出来。\n# en: Ask for the recipe\nopt.right.label [604f64] = 讨要配方\n# en: \"Ha,\" says the cook, which means no, which means you'll have to keep coming back.\nopt.right.say.1 [fdb0a2] = “哈，”厨娘说。意思是不行，也就是说你得一直回来。\n# en: Ask for the honest recipe\nopt.up.label [abc109] = 问出老实的配方\n# en: The cook tells you the truth before she can stop herself: the secret is a spoon of the faculty's good sherry. She laughs, then makes you promise. You promise, and get a second bowl.\nopt.up.say.1 [1d090e] = 厨娘还没来得及收住嘴就说了实话：秘诀是一勺教职员的好雪利酒。她笑了，然后让你发誓保密。你发了誓，还得了第二碗。\n\n@event obs_comet_watch\n# en: The Comet Watch\ntitle [d479ab] = 守望彗星\n# en: A comet is due tonight and the observatory is full of students pretending they came for the science. The telescope has a queue. The roof has a view.\ntext [20d293] = 今晚有彗星经过，观星台里挤满了假装是为科学而来的学生。望远镜前排着队。屋顶上有好风景。\n# en: The brass dome of an observatory open to the night, a student at the eyepiece, another beside them with a notebook.\nscene [3227ec] = 向夜空敞开的黄铜穹顶观星台，一名学生凑在目镜前，另一人拿着笔记本站在旁边。\n# en: Queue for the telescope\nopt.left.label [1a7ef0] = 排队用望远镜\n# en: Your turn at the eyepiece lasts thirty seconds. The comet's tail is green. You'll remember it longer than most lectures.\nopt.left.say.1 [54f31c] = 轮到你看目镜只有三十秒。彗尾是绿色的。你会记得它比大多数课都久。\n# en: Watch from the roof\nopt.right.label [5e0cfb] = 去屋顶看\n# en: The comet crosses the sky slowly, like it's looking for something. Kael is already on the roof. He moves over to make room.\nopt.right.say.1 [344326] = 彗星慢慢划过天空，像在找什么东西。凯尔已经在屋顶上了。他挪了挪，给你让出位置。\n# en: Foresee where it will cross\nopt.up.label [0bcb22] = 预见它从哪儿划过\n# en: You look a moment ahead and point at an empty patch of sky. Everyone on the roof looks, and the comet crosses exactly there. Half the roof claps. The other half accuses you of reading the charts.\nopt.up.say.1 [a5635e] = 你朝前看了一眼，指向一片空空的天空。屋顶上所有人都望过去，彗星正好从那儿划过。一半人鼓掌，另一半人指责你偷看了星图。\n\n@event obs_star_chart\n# en: The Missing Star\ntitle [f95144] = 缺失的星\n# en: An old star chart in the observatory archive has a circle drawn where a star should be, with a note in faded ink: \"It eats the light. Do not chart it.\"\ntext [f9ab12] = 观星台档案里一张旧星图上，本该是一颗星的地方画着一个圈，旁边用褪色的墨水写着：“它吞噬光。勿绘。”\n# en: An old star chart spread on a desk, one constellation with a careful empty circle drawn where a star should be.\nscene [8cbcfa] = 桌上摊开一张旧星图，某个星座里本该有星的位置被仔细地画了一个空圈。\n# en: Copy the chart\nopt.left.label [ddb5f3] = 抄下星图\n# en: You copy it carefully. That night you dream of a circle with nothing inside it, waiting.\nopt.left.say.1 [c900a1] = 你仔细地抄了下来。那晚你梦见一个空无一物的圈，在等待。\n# en: Put it back\nopt.right.label [862ed2] = 放回原处\n# en: You return it to the drawer. The drawer feels colder when you close it.\nopt.right.say.1 [ca8bc6] = 你把它放回抽屉。合上时，抽屉似乎冷了些。\n\n@event green_mandrake_choir\n# en: The Mandrake Choir\ntitle [a16982] = 曼德拉草合唱团\n# en: Tobias has taught the greenhouse mandrakes to scream in harmony. He would like your opinion. The mandrakes would like an audience.\ntext [26476f] = 托比亚斯教会了温室里的曼德拉草用和声尖叫。他想听听你的意见。曼德拉草想要观众。\n# en: A greenhouse bench of potted mandrakes wearing tiny earmuffs, a student conducting them with a pencil.\nscene [556372] = 温室长凳上一排戴着小耳罩的盆栽曼德拉草，一名学生拿铅笔指挥它们。\n# en: Conduct a verse\nopt.left.label [8d7d71] = 指挥一段\n# en: You conduct one verse. Your ears ring for a day. Tobias says it's the best performance they've given.\nopt.left.say.1 [fe14aa] = 你指挥了一段。耳朵嗡嗡响了一整天。托比亚斯说这是它们最好的一场演出。\n# en: Wear earmuffs and listen\nopt.right.label [fa6dc3] = 戴上耳罩听\n# en: Through the earmuffs it sounds almost beautiful. Tobias is so proud he cries a little.\nopt.right.say.1 [07ed14] = 隔着耳罩，听起来几乎很美。托比亚斯骄傲得哭了一小会儿。\n\n@event green_moonflower_sprout\n# en: A Moonflower Seedling\ntitle [e51a03] = 月光花幼苗\n# en: In a forgotten corner of the greenhouse, a moonflower seed has sprouted on its own, eleven years early. It glows faintly when you lean over it.\ntext [b895be] = 温室一个被遗忘的角落里，一粒月光花种子自己发了芽，早了整整十一年。你俯身靠近时，它发出微弱的光。\n# en: A tiny silver-leaved seedling in a clay pot, glowing faintly in a dark greenhouse.\nscene [1fd66d] = 陶盆里一株银叶小苗，在黑暗的温室中幽幽发光。\n# en: Pot it and look after it\nopt.left.label [aa4dfe] = 移进盆里好好照料\n# en: You give it a pot, a label and a name. It glows a little brighter when you visit.\nopt.left.say.1 [3976bb] = 你给了它一个盆、一张标签和一个名字。你来看它时，它会亮一点。\n# en: Tell Tobias\nopt.right.label [fe6832] = 告诉托比亚斯\n# en: Tobias is speechless, then very loud. The seedling gets a sign, a fence and a rota.\nopt.right.say.1 [69881e] = 托比亚斯先是说不出话，然后非常大声。幼苗得到了一块牌子、一圈篱笆和一张值班表。\n# en: Entangle a hedge round it\nopt.up.label [1f8a5c] = 长道树篱围住它\n# en: A ring of thorns comes up round the seedling, knee-high and very sure of itself. Nobody is treading on this one by accident. The seedling glows a little brighter, or you imagine it does.\nopt.up.say.1 [8d2465] = 一圈荆棘在幼苗周围长了起来，齐膝高，非常笃定。没人会再不小心踩到它了。幼苗似乎更亮了一点，也许是你的想象。\n\n@event castle_empty_halls\n# en: Empty Halls\ntitle [468042] = 空荡的走廊\n# en: The castle in the holidays is enormous and quiet. Your footsteps echo down corridors you've never been alone in. A door you've never noticed is standing open.\ntext [30fae9] = 假期里的城堡巨大而安静。你的脚步声在从没独自走过的走廊里回荡。一扇你从未注意过的门敞开着。\n# en: A long castle corridor in the holidays, dust in sunbeams, one student walking alone.\nscene [c48035] = 假期里一条长长的城堡走廊，阳光里浮着灰尘，一名学生独自走着。\n# en: Go through the door\nopt.left.label [974ffb] = 穿过那扇门\n# en: It leads to a stair that leads to a locked door that leads, you're almost sure, to the seventh tower.\nopt.left.say.1 [6544d0] = 门后是一段楼梯，楼梯通向一扇锁着的门，而那扇门——你几乎可以肯定——通向第七塔。\n# en: Read in the window seat\nopt.right.label [a83ff6] = 在窗边座位上读书\n# en: You read all day in a window seat with the whole lake below you. The best kind of nothing.\nopt.right.say.1 [19769a] = 你在窗边座位上读了一整天书，整片湖就在脚下。最好的那种无所事事。\n\n@event castle_skeleton_crew\n# en: The Skeleton Crew\ntitle [6ec6d4] = 留守的人们\n# en: Supper in the holidays is one table in the Great Hall: you, three other students, the cook, two prefects and Old Maud, who has brought her goat.\ntext [a9be7b] = 假期的晚饭只在大礼堂摆一张桌子：你、另外三个学生、厨娘、两位级长，还有带着山羊来的老莫德。\n# en: A handful of staff and students sharing a long table in a nearly empty great hall.\nscene [4c6b8f] = 几乎空荡的大礼堂里，寥寥几名职员和学生共用一张长桌。\n# en: Sit next to Maud\nopt.left.label [d82b60] = 坐在莫德旁边\n# en: Maud tells a story about the frozen spring that she's never told the term-time students. Bartholomew eats your napkin.\nopt.left.say.1 [2c57bf] = 莫德讲了一个关于冰封之春的故事，那是她从没对学期里的学生讲过的。巴塞洛缪吃掉了你的餐巾。\n# en: Play cards with the prefects\nopt.right.label [a22285] = 和级长们打牌\n# en: The prefects play cards like they enforce rules: badly and with great confidence. You win dessert and most of their pocket money.\nopt.right.say.1 [a7aad7] = 级长们打牌就像执行校规：水平很烂，信心十足。你赢了甜点，还有他们大半的零花钱。\n\n@event home_family_supper\n# en: Supper at Home\ntitle [1f26c6] = 家里的晚饭\n# en: Supper at home. Everyone wants to know what you've learned. You try to explain evocation to an aunt who thinks it's a kind of cheese.\ntext [8ab73f] = 家里的晚饭。每个人都想知道你学了什么。你试着向一位以为塑能术是某种奶酪的姑妈解释它。\n# en: A crowded kitchen table, a family passing dishes, a student back from the academy being asked too many questions.\nscene [ec2a5f] = 挤满人的餐桌，一家人传着菜，一个从学院回来的学生被问了太多问题。\n# en: Show them a spell\nopt.left.label [803b4f] = 给他们露一手\n# en: You light every candle in the room at once. Your aunt applauds and asks if you can do it with cheese.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d4fe6] = 你一下点亮了屋里所有的蜡烛。姑妈鼓掌，问你能不能对奶酪也来一下。\n# en: Just listen\nopt.right.label [6a9f3e] = 就听着\n# en: You let them talk. You'd forgotten how loud and how good it is.\nopt.right.say.1 [738cc0] = 你让他们说。你都忘了这有多吵，又有多好。\n\n@event home_old_friend\n# en: An Old Friend\ntitle [8809c5] = 老朋友\n# en: Your oldest friend is sitting on the wall at the edge of the fields, exactly where you left them. \"So,\" they say. \"Are you a wizard now, or what?\"\ntext [e85e1a] = 你最老的朋友坐在田边的矮墙上，就在你离开时的那个位置。“所以，”对方说，“你现在算巫师了，还是怎样？”\n# en: Two old friends sitting on a field wall at sunset, one in academy robes, the other in work clothes.\nscene [82fd45] = 夕阳下两位老朋友坐在田边矮墙上，一个穿着学院长袍，另一个穿着干活的衣服。\n# en: Tell them everything\nopt.left.label [09f8b1] = 把一切都告诉对方\n# en: You talk until the stars come out. They want to hear about the lake most of all.\nopt.left.say.1 [5d2429] = 你们一直聊到星星出来。对方最想听的是那片湖。\n# en: Ask about their life instead\nopt.right.label [24a3a1] = 反过来问问对方的生活\n# en: They've been busy too: a farm, a fiancé, a goat that bites. Your life isn't the only one that grew.\nopt.right.say.1 [91a591] = 对方也很忙：一座农场、一位未婚夫、一只咬人的山羊。长大的不只是你的生活。\n\n@event home_market_day\n# en: Market Day\ntitle [0bcdaf] = 赶集日\n# en: Market day in town. A stallholder recognises your academy robes and asks if you can do anything about his leaky roof. For a price.\ntext [ebfea1] = 镇上的赶集日。一个摊主认出了你的学院长袍，问你能不能帮他修修漏雨的屋顶。有偿的。\n# en: A small country market, a student in academy robes being stared at by stallholders.\nscene [a7a658] = 一个乡间小集市，一名穿着学院长袍的学生被摊主们盯着看。\n# en: Fix it with magic\nopt.left.label [77d22c] = 用魔法修好它\n# en: The roof is dry, the stallholder is thrilled and you are slightly scorched. He pays in apples and coins.\nopt.left.say.1 [f09cd8] = 屋顶干了，摊主乐坏了，你被轻微烤焦了。他用苹果和硬币付账。\n# en: Just buy sweets\nopt.right.label [bbf438] = 只买糖\n# en: The sweet shop still sells your favourites. You eat them on the bridge like you used to.\nopt.right.say.1 [ea94aa] = 糖果店还卖你最爱的那几样。你像从前一样坐在桥上吃。\n\n@event work_double_shift\n# en: The Double Shift\ntitle [6df679] = 连轴转\n# en: Holiday shifts pay double and feel triple. It's your second shift today and the evening rush hasn't started.\ntext [60fdf7] = 假期的班工钱翻倍，感觉翻三倍。这是你今天的第二个班，晚高峰还没开始。\n# en: A student asleep on their feet behind a counter, a clock on the wall showing a very late hour.\nscene [6bbbbe] = 一名学生站在柜台后面睡着了，墙上的钟指着一个很晚的时间。\n# en: Push through\nopt.left.label [f6ed7c] = 咬牙撑过去\n# en: You push through. The coins are heavy in your pocket and your feet are heavy everywhere else.\nopt.left.say.1 [900c8d] = 你撑过去了。口袋里的硬币沉甸甸的，身上其他地方也沉甸甸的。\n# en: Swap the shift\nopt.right.label [dcf3f9] = 找人换班\n# en: A colleague takes it for a favour you'll owe later. You sleep for eleven hours.\nopt.right.say.1 [6106e9] = 一位同事替了你，换你以后欠一个人情。你睡了十一个小时。\n\n@event intern_first_assignment\n# en: The First Assignment\ntitle [12864b] = 第一项任务\n# en: Your supervisor at the Royal Library hands you a list of forty uncatalogued boxes. \"Find out what's in them,\" she says. \"Carefully. One of them is probably alive.\"\ntext [106ac0] = 你在皇家图书馆的导师递给你一张清单，上面是四十个未编目的箱子。“查清里面有什么，”她说，“小心点。其中有一个大概是活的。”\n# en: A vast underground archive, a supervisor handing a student a list longer than their arm.\nscene [cf395e] = 一座巨大的地下档案馆，导师递给学生一张比胳膊还长的清单。\n# en: Work through the boxes\nopt.left.label [af7cdf] = 逐个整理箱子\n# en: Box thirty-one is alive. It's a very small, very old dictionary that bites. You catalogue it.\nopt.left.say.1 [f9f573] = 第三十一号箱是活的。里面是一本很小、很老、会咬人的词典。你把它编了目。\n# en: Ask the other interns\nopt.right.label [d7f73e] = 问问其他实习生\n# en: The other interns have a system, a rota and a song. You learn all three.\nopt.right.say.1 [b61185] = 其他实习生有一套系统、一张值班表和一首歌。三样你都学会了。\n\n@event intern_index_maze\n# en: The Index Maze\ntitle [708be7] = 索引迷宫\n# en: A cross-reference in the archive leads to another, and another, and another, deeper under the city. The last card is about the founders of Lumenhold.\ntext [6ca7e0] = 档案里的一条交叉引用指向另一条，又一条，再一条，越来越深地钻进城市地下。最后一张卡片讲的是流明堡的创始者。\n# en: A student lost between endless shelves underground, following an index card trail by lamplight.\nscene [37ed2d] = 一名学生迷失在地下无尽的书架间，借着灯光追寻一串索引卡的线索。\n# en: Follow it to the end\nopt.left.label [b0b834] = 追到底\n# en: At the end is a letter from a founder about \"the seventh lock\". You copy it with shaking hands.\nopt.left.say.1 [89da61] = 尽头是一位创始者写的信，谈到“第七把锁”。你手发着抖把它抄了下来。\n# en: The trail ends at a missing card. Somebody took it. Recently.\nopt.left.say.2 [a4b290] = 线索断在一张缺失的卡片上。有人拿走了它。就在最近。\n# en: Report it to your supervisor\nopt.right.label [10c4be] = 报告给导师\n# en: Your supervisor reads the card, goes pale, thanks you for your care, and writes a letter to the headmistress of Lumenhold.\nopt.right.say.1 [ecfad6] = 导师读了卡片，脸色发白，谢过你的细心，提笔给流明堡的院长写了封信。\n\n@event thesis_proposal\n# en: The Proposal\ntitle [0d98d2] = 开题\n# en: Your thesis proposal is due. Professor Thorne wants one sentence that says what you'll find out. You have eleven sentences and none of them is it.\ntext [6735b0] = 论文开题报告该交了。索恩教授要一句话，说清你要查明什么。你有十一句话，没有一句是。\n# en: A desk covered in drafts, one page with a single sentence circled many times.\nscene [19addc] = 一张铺满草稿的桌子，有一页上只有一句话，被圈了很多遍。\n# en: Rewrite it all night\nopt.left.label [2fa9ed] = 通宵重写\n# en: At dawn you have one sentence. Thorne reads it twice. \"Yes,\" he says. \"That.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [348567] = 黎明时你有了一句话。索恩读了两遍。“对，”他说，“就是这个。”\n# en: Hand in what you have\nopt.right.label [648c9a] = 交上手头的\n# en: Thorne reads all eleven sentences and circles the fourth. \"That one,\" he says. \"Next time, only that one.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [8b0a3c] = 索恩读完十一句话，圈出第四句。“这一句，”他说，“下次，只要这一句。”\n\n@event thesis_lab_night\n# en: Late in the Lab\ntitle [d2c7a0] = 实验室深夜\n# en: The thesis lab, long after everyone else has gone home: and your argument has a hole in it the size of the seventh tower.\ntext [676a0d] = 论文实验室，其他人早都回去了：而你的论证里有一个第七塔那么大的漏洞。\n# en: A thesis laboratory at midnight, one carrel lit, a student surrounded by open books.\nscene [9ec7f2] = 午夜的论文实验室，只有一个隔间亮着灯，一名学生被摊开的书围着。\n# en: Find the hole\nopt.left.label [35b489] = 找到漏洞\n# en: You find it, fill it and find two more. That's progress, apparently.\nopt.left.say.1 [e36cbd] = 你找到了，补上了，又发现了两个。据说这就叫进展。\n# en: Go to bed\nopt.right.label [2fa817] = 去睡觉\n# en: You sleep. In the morning the hole is still there, but you can see the edges of it.\nopt.right.say.1 [0eb578] = 你睡了。早上漏洞还在，但你能看清它的边了。\n\n@event bell_first_ring\n# en: A Bell Under the Water\ntitle [1289ef] = 水下的钟声\n# en: In the middle of the night you hear a bell, low and slow, from the middle of the lake. Nobody else in your dormitory wakes up.\ntext [cfa605] = 半夜里你听见钟声，低沉而缓慢，从湖心传来。宿舍里没有别人醒来。\n# en: A dark lakeshore at night, ripples spreading from the middle of the lake though nothing has fallen in.\nscene [38d9e1] = 夜里漆黑的湖岸，涟漪从湖心扩散开来，尽管什么都没有落进水里。\n# en: Go down to the shore\nopt.left.label [c0c878] = 下到湖边去\n# en: The water is perfectly still. Then, once, a ring spreads out from the centre, as if the lake had been struck from below.\nopt.left.say.1 [baf151] = 湖面纹丝不动。然后，只一次，一圈涟漪从中心荡开，仿佛湖被人从下面敲了一下。\n# en: Pull the blanket over your head\nopt.right.label [352adf] = 把毯子蒙过头\n# en: It rings seven times. You count every one. In the morning you are the only one who remembers.\nopt.right.say.1 [e7bc35] = 钟响了七下。你每一下都数了。到了早上，只有你一个人还记得。\n\n@event bell_maud_rope\n# en: Maud's Rope\ntitle [852d20] = 莫德的绳子\n# en: {keeper} shows you a rope, frayed and black with lake water, that she's kept in her shed for sixty years. \"From the bell,\" she says. \"It came up the night the lake froze. Someone cut it.\"\ntext [090813] = {keeper}给你看一根绳子，磨损了，被湖水浸得发黑，她在棚子里留了六十年。“钟上的，”她说，“湖冻上那晚它浮了上来。有人割断了它。”\n# en: An old woman in a garden shed holding up a frayed, waterlogged bell-rope.\nscene [609899] = 花园棚屋里，一位老妇人举着一根磨损、浸透了水的钟绳。\n# en: Ask who cut it\nopt.left.label [a6108f] = 问是谁割断的\n# en: \"That,\" says {keeper}, \"is the question I've been asking for sixty years.\" She promises to tell you what she knows, when she's ready. \"Come by the cottage, dearie. Not tonight.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [9ea41a] = “这个嘛，”{keeper}说，“我问了六十年了。”她答应等她准备好，就把知道的告诉你。“来小屋找我吧，亲爱的。别今晚。”\n# en: Leave her to her memories\nopt.right.label [9f94d2] = 让她独自回忆\n# en: You leave her with the rope in her lap. She doesn't notice you go.\nopt.right.say.1 [10a7b7] = 你留她抱着膝上的绳子。她没注意到你离开。\n\n@event bell_answer\n# en: What Maud Remembers\ntitle [7f91ce] = 莫德记得的事\n# en: {npc} has the kettle on before you knock. \"The rope,\" she says, once the tea is poured. \"Edmund cut it. To stop the bell ringing the thing awake. It went on ringing anyway.\"\ntext [62a789] = 你还没敲门，{npc}就已经把水壶坐上了。“那根绳子，”茶倒好后她说，“是埃德蒙割的。为了不让钟声把那东西敲醒。可钟还是一直在响。”\n# en: An old woman and a student sitting by a cottage fire, a waterlogged rope coiled between them.\nscene [6145b3] = 小屋炉火边坐着一位老妇人和一名学生，两人之间盘着一根浸透水的绳子。\n# en: Listen\nopt.ok.label [03cec6] = 听着\n# en: She talks until the light goes. When she's finished, she seems lighter, and you seem to be carrying something.\nopt.ok.say.1 [de8d6c] = 她一直讲到天光散尽。讲完时她似乎轻松了，而你似乎背上了什么。\n\n@event bell_drowned\n# en: The Diving Bell\ntitle [980982] = 潜水钟\n# en: The bell has rung every night for a week. The boathouse has an old diving helmet and a rope. The lake is very dark and very still.\ntext [62e2e6] = 钟已经连着响了一个星期。船屋里有一个旧潜水头盔和一根绳子。湖水非常黑，非常静。\n# en: A student in a borrowed diving helmet standing at the end of a jetty over black water.\nscene [f56404] = 一名学生戴着借来的潜水头盔，站在黑水之上的栈桥尽头。\n# en: Dive\nopt.left.label [ad77f7] = 潜下去\n# en: Far down, the bell lies on its side, and around its rim, letters: a name you don't know. You surface gasping, and you keep the name.\nopt.left.say.1 [f03244] = 在很深的地方，钟侧躺着，钟沿上刻着字：一个你不认识的名字。你喘着气浮出水面，把那个名字记在了心里。\n# en: The cold drives you back up before you see anything. Old Maud is waiting on the jetty with a blanket and a look. The helmet stays in the boathouse, if you want to try again.\nopt.left.say.2 [0a29d9] = 你还没看见什么，寒冷就把你逼了回去。老莫德拿着毯子和一个眼神等在栈桥上。头盔就留在船屋里，你想再试的话。\n# en: Wait for the bell to stop\nopt.right.label [d2920e] = 等钟声停下\n# en: You wait for a week, listening from the shore, until you know its rhythm by heart. It doesn't stop. Something in you decides it will have to be you, next time: the helmet is still in the boathouse.\nopt.right.say.1 [445e1b] = 你在岸边听了一个星期，直到把它的节奏背得滚瓜烂熟。它没有停。你心里有什么东西决定了：下一次，得是你去——头盔还在船屋里。\n\n@event bell_silence\n# en: The Bell Calls\ntitle [cdbef4] = 钟声召唤\n# en: The bell is ringing every night now, louder, and tonight it rings your way: you can feel it in your teeth. {clue(bell_name): You have the name from its rim.|You have Maud's rope.} You know, the way you know your own handwriting, that it wants you at the lakeshore after dark.\ntext [b84c67] = 现在钟每夜都响，越来越响，今晚它朝着你响：你的牙齿都能感觉到。{clue(bell_name): 你记着钟沿上的那个名字。|你有莫德的绳子。}你就像认得自己的笔迹一样清楚，它要你天黑后去湖岸。\n# en: A dormitory window at night, the Mirror Lake beyond it silver under the moon, rings spreading across the water.\nscene [844d87] = 夜里的宿舍窗户，窗外的镜湖在月光下泛着银光，一圈圈涟漪在水面散开。\n# en: Tonight, then\nopt.ok.label [c74f69] = 那就今晚吧\n# en: You lie awake counting the rings. Seven, always seven. Whenever you go down to the shore after dark, it will be waiting.\nopt.ok.say.1 [4b1224] = 你醒着躺在床上数钟声。七下，总是七下。不管哪天天黑后你去湖岸，它都会在那里等着。\n\n@event bell_laid_to_rest\n# en: The Bell Falls Silent\ntitle [6d5cb4] = 钟声沉寂\n# en: The shore is empty and the lake is glass. Then the bell begins, slow and low, from the very middle. {clue(bell_name) and clue(bell_rope): You have the name from its rim and Maud's rope in your hands.|{clue(bell_name): You have the name from its rim.|You have Maud's rope, cut sixty years ago.}}\ntext [47584d] = 湖岸空无一人，湖面如镜。然后钟声响起，又慢又低，来自湖的正中央。{clue(bell_name) and clue(bell_rope): 你记着钟沿上的名字，手里握着莫德的绳子。|{clue(bell_name): 你记着钟沿上的那个名字。|你手里握着莫德的绳子，六十年前被割断的那根。}}\n# en: The Mirror Lake at dusk, perfectly still, a student standing at the water's edge with a lantern.\nscene [78f3ef] = 黄昏的镜湖，平静如镜，一名学生提着灯站在水边。\n# en: Say the name over the water\nopt.name.label [ddcf78] = 对着湖念出名字\n# en: You say the name from the bell's rim out loud. The bell rings once more, as if answering to it, and then the lake is just a lake. Somewhere in her cottage, you think, Maud sleeps through the night.\nopt.name.say.1 [c94703] = 你大声念出钟沿上的名字。钟又响了一下，像是在应答，然后湖就只是一片湖了。你想，此刻在小屋里，莫德一定能一觉睡到天亮。\n# en: Give the rope back to the lake\nopt.rope.label [3b03b9] = 把绳子还给湖\n# en: You wade in to your knees and let the rope sink. It goes down straight, as if something below were pulling it home. The bell rings one last time, muffled, and stops. The cold stays in your legs all night.\nopt.rope.say.1 [7139e3] = 你蹚到齐膝深的水里，让绳子沉下去。它笔直地往下沉，好像底下有什么在把它拉回家。钟最后闷闷地响了一声，停了。那股寒意在你腿里待了一整夜。\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.wait.label [4bf72b] = 今晚算了\n# en: You stand at the shore until the ringing stops on its own, and go back up the lawn. It will ring again tomorrow.\nopt.wait.say.1 [1d0f14] = 你站在岸边，直到钟声自己停下，然后走回草坪上。明天它还会再响。\n\n@event bell_dive_again\n# en: The Helmet in the Boathouse\ntitle [237b11] = 船屋里的头盔\n# en: The old diving helmet is where you left it, on the workbench by the doors. Somebody has oiled the rope since last time. The lake is still, and under it the bell is waiting.\ntext [51fef3] = 那顶旧潜水头盔还在你上次放的地方，门边的工作台上。从那以后有人给绳子上过油。湖面平静，湖底，那口钟在等着。\n# en: An old brass diving helmet on a boathouse workbench beside a coil of rope, the lake glinting through the open doors.\nscene [ae496f] = 船屋工作台上放着一顶旧黄铜潜水头盔，旁边是一卷绳子，敞开的门外湖光闪烁。\n# en: Dive again\nopt.left.label [986ddf] = 再潜一次\n# en: You know the cold now.\nopt.left.hint [aa31ec] = 你已经熟悉那种冷了。\n# en: You go down slower this time, and further. The bell lies on its side, and around its rim, letters: a name you don't know. You surface gasping, and you keep the name.\nopt.left.say.1 [376aec] = 这次你下得更慢，也更深。那口钟侧躺着，钟沿上一圈字母：一个你不认识的名字。你喘着气浮出水面，把那个名字留在了心里。\n# en: The cold finds you again, halfway down. You come up blue and shaking, with nothing but the certainty that it's down there.\nopt.left.say.2 [74a681] = 下到一半，寒冷又找上了你。你浮上来时嘴唇发紫、浑身发抖，只确定一件事：它就在下面。\n# en: Not today\nopt.right.label [479f6f] = 今天算了\n# en: You put the helmet back on the bench. The bell can wait one more day. It has waited sixty years.\nopt.right.say.1 [ba32f8] = 你把头盔放回工作台。钟可以再等一天。它已经等了六十年。\n\n@event bell_echo_lakeshore\n# en: Rings on Still Water\ntitle [838278] = 静水上的涟漪\n# en: The lake is flat calm, and still rings spread from its middle, slow and even, like the ripples from a bell that nobody hears. A heron on the shallows lifts its head at each one.\ntext [335d77] = 湖面风平浪静，可一圈圈涟漪仍从湖心散开，缓慢而均匀，像一口没人听见的钟荡开的波纹。浅滩上的一只苍鹭，每来一圈就抬一下头。\n# en: A calm lake at dusk, perfect rings spreading from its centre although nothing has fallen in, a student crouched at the water's edge.\nscene [874c57] = 黄昏平静的湖面，完美的涟漪从湖心散开，却什么也没落进水里，一个学生蹲在水边。\n# en: Count the rings\nopt.left.label [da2550] = 数涟漪\n# en: Seven, then a pause, then seven. The heron counts them too, you'd swear. You walk back up the lawn with the number stuck in your head.\nopt.left.say.1 [94cdf4] = 七圈，停一下，又是七圈。你敢发誓那只苍鹭也在数。你走回草坪时，那个数字一直在脑子里打转。\n# en: Skim a stone across them\nopt.right.label [1cacee] = 打个水漂穿过去\n# en: Your stone skips four times and sinks in the middle of a ring. For a moment the rings stop. Then they start again, patient as anything.\nopt.right.say.1 [273dff] = 你的石子跳了四下，沉在一圈涟漪正中。有那么一会儿，涟漪停了。然后又开始了，耐心得不得了。\n\n@event bell_echo_boathouse\n# en: The Boats Keep Time\ntitle [9afc35] = 船在打拍子\n# en: Inside the boathouse every boat is knocking against its post in the same slow rhythm, though there's no wind and the water outside is still. Knock, knock, knock: seven, then a pause.\ntext [8b40ee] = 船屋里，每条船都以同样缓慢的节奏撞着桩子，可外面没有风，水面也一动不动。咚，咚，咚：七下，然后停一下。\n# en: Rowing boats tied in an old boathouse, all knocking gently against their posts in the same rhythm, a student listening in the doorway.\nscene [edb22d] = 旧船屋里拴着的划艇都以同样的节奏轻撞着桩子，一个学生站在门口听。\n# en: Steady the boats\nopt.left.label [621b6f] = 把船稳住\n# en: You go from boat to boat with wet sleeves, holding each one still. As soon as you let go, they take up the rhythm again.\nopt.left.say.1 [58f606] = 你湿着袖子一条船一条船地扶稳。可你一松手，它们又跟上了那个节奏。\n# en: Sit and listen\nopt.right.label [1a3cf5] = 坐下来听\n# en: You sit on an upturned hull and listen until you can hear the bell under the knocking. It is very far down, and very patient.\nopt.right.say.1 [049ba8] = 你坐在一条倒扣的船身上听，直到在撞击声底下听见了那口钟。它在很深很深的地方，非常有耐心。\n\n@event bell_echo_cottage\n# en: Maud's Teacups\ntitle [f4e077] = 莫德的茶杯\n# en: In Maud's cottage the teacups on the dresser are chiming against each other, all together, though nobody has touched them. Maud pours your tea as if she can't hear a thing. \"Biscuit, dearie?\"\ntext [0e7e4a] = 莫德小屋里，碗柜上的茶杯一齐互相碰得叮当响，可谁也没碰过它们。莫德给你倒茶，好像什么也没听见。“来块饼干吗，亲爱的？”\n# en: A cottage dresser full of mismatched teacups, all trembling and chiming together, an old woman pretending not to notice.\nscene [237588] = 小屋碗柜上摆满不成套的茶杯，全都在颤抖着一齐叮当作响，一位老妇人假装没注意到。\n# en: Ask her about the cups\nopt.left.label [43049c] = 问她茶杯的事\n# en: \"Old cups,\" says Maud. \"Old lake.\" She looks at the window a long time. \"They've done that since I was a girl, on the nights it rings. You get used to it. You don't.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [4bd3fc] = “老杯子，”莫德说，“老湖。”她望着窗外看了很久。“从我还是个小姑娘起，每到它响的夜里，它们就这样。你会习惯的。其实不会。”\n# en: Help her wrap them in cloth\nopt.right.label [d143f6] = 帮她用布包好\n# en: You wrap every cup in an old tea towel. The chiming stops, and Maud's shoulders come down an inch. \"There,\" she says. \"Now we can hear ourselves think.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [1b0b76] = 你用旧茶巾把每只杯子都包好。叮当声停了，莫德的肩膀松下来一点。“好了，”她说，“这下我们能听见自己想事情了。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/social.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/social.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event soc_study_session\n# en: Study Session\ntitle [c13d4e] = 一起自习\n# en: {~{npc} drops into the seat across from you with an armful of books. \"Exams are coming for us all. Misery loves company. Study with me?\"|{npc} slides a stack of notes onto your table. \"I've read this chapter four times and it's still in a foreign language. Help?\"|{npc} finds you in the library and sits down without asking. \"If I study alone I'll just reorganise my quills. Keep me honest?\"}\ntext [4f74e2] = {~{npc}抱着一摞书一屁股坐到你对面。“考试要来收拾我们所有人了。同病相怜嘛。一起学？”|{npc}把一叠笔记推到你桌上。“这一章我读了四遍，还是像外语。帮帮我？”|{npc}在图书馆找到你，不问一声就坐下了。“我一个人学只会去整理羽毛笔。你帮我盯着点？”}\n# en: Two students at a candlelit table piled with books, one explaining something with wild hand gestures.\nscene [cb85c7] = 两个学生坐在堆满书的烛光桌前，其中一个正手舞足蹈地讲解着什么。\n# en: You work better alone\nopt.left.label [1f18da] = 你一个人效率更高\n# en: You get a lot done. {~{npc} sits at the next table and sighs loudly every few minutes.|{npc} finds someone else, and their laughter carries across the library all evening.|{npc} leaves you a biscuit on the way out, which somehow makes it worse.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fb167d] = 你完成了很多。{~{npc}坐在隔壁桌，每隔几分钟就大声叹一口气。|{npc}找了别人一起学，他们的笑声整晚都在图书馆里回荡。|{npc}走的时候给你留了一块饼干，不知怎么反而更难受。}\n# en: Pull up a chair\nopt.right.label [d0a229] = 拉过一把椅子\n# en: You get half as much done and remember twice as much of it. {~{npc} explains the Third Law with a biscuit diagram.|You quiz each other until the candle gutters.|{npc} makes up a rude rhyme for every date, and you will never forget a single one.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2f03ab] = 你只完成了一半，却记住了两倍的内容。{~{npc}用饼干摆图讲解了第三定律。|你们互相提问，直到蜡烛快燃尽。|{npc}给每个日期都编了一句粗俗的顺口溜，你一个也忘不掉了。}\n\n@event soc_borrow_money\n# en: A Small Loan\ntitle [cd870f] = 一笔小借款\n# en: {npc} turns out an empty purse. \"I'm fifteen crowns short {~for rent|for the term's ink and parchment|for the wand-mender, and it's my only wand}. I'll pay you back. I swear on my wand.\"\ntext [ddf34a] = {npc}把空空的钱袋翻了过来。“我还差十五克朗{~交房租|买这学期的墨水和羊皮纸|付修魔杖的钱，我就这一根魔杖}。我会还你的。我以我的魔杖发誓。”\n# en: A student holding out an empty purse upside down with a sheepish grin.\nscene [d2da5b] = 一个学生把空钱袋倒过来递给你看，脸上挂着不好意思的笑。\n# en: Sorry, you're tight too\nopt.left.label [d94071] = 抱歉，你也手头紧\n# en: \"No, of course, I understand.\" {They} {are} quiet at dinner.\nopt.left.say.1 [e86862] = “不不，当然，我理解。”晚饭时{they}一直没怎么说话。\n# en: Lend fifteen crowns\nopt.right.label [0326cf] = 借十五克朗\n# en: {npc} hugs you with the empty purse still in {their} hand.\nopt.right.say.1 [995967] = {npc}抱住了你，手里还攥着那只空钱袋。\n# en: {npc} hugs you. You suspect you will never see those fifteen crowns again.\nopt.right.say.2 [506678] = {npc}抱住了你。你怀疑那十五克朗再也回不来了。\n\n@event soc_loan_repaid\n# en: Paid Back\ntitle [ed4502] = 还钱\n# en: {~{npc} finds you after class and presses a little stack of coins into your hand, plus a sticky bun. \"Fifteen crowns. And interest.\" The interest is the bun.|{npc} turns up at breakfast with a small purse and a very straight face. \"Fifteen crowns. I said I would.\"|{npc} slides fifteen crowns across the library table, one coin at a time. The last one has a note under it: \"Thank you. Really.\"}\ntext [d1878a] = {~下课后{npc}找到你，往你手里塞了一小摞硬币，外加一个黏糊糊的小面包。“十五克朗。还有利息。”利息就是那个面包。|{npc}在早餐时出现，拿着一个小钱袋，一脸严肃。“十五克朗。我说过会还的。”|{npc}在图书馆的桌子上把十五克朗一枚一枚推到你面前。最后一枚下面压着一张字条：“谢谢你。真的。”}\n# en: A small stack of coins and a sweet bun left on a desk with a thank-you note.\nscene [a79734] = 桌上放着一小摞硬币和一个甜面包，旁边还有一张感谢字条。\n# en: Count it\nopt.left.label [5fce81] = 数一数\n# en: You count it, because {they}'d be offended if you didn't. Fifteen exactly. {npc} nods, satisfied, as if a debt of honour has been settled.\nopt.left.say.1 [3ec7dd] = 你数了一遍，因为不数的话{they}会觉得被冒犯。正好十五。{npc}满意地点点头，好像一笔关乎荣誉的债终于了结了。\n# en: Pocket it without counting\nopt.right.label [4b0d5d] = 不数就收下\n# en: You put it away uncounted. {npc} looks quietly pleased to be trusted, which was worth more than the crowns.\nopt.right.say.1 [997bc1] = 你没数就收了起来。{npc}看起来暗自高兴被你信任——这比那些克朗值钱多了。\n\n@event soc_become_friends\n# en: Something Like Friendship\ntitle [68b938] = 类似友谊的东西\n# en: {npc} saves you a seat without asking now, and you save {them} one. You trade notes, complaints and the last biscuit without keeping score. Somewhere along the way, this became a friendship.\ntext [013159] = 现在{npc}会不声不响地给你占座，你也给{them}占。你们交换笔记、牢骚和最后一块饼干，从不记账。不知从何时起，这已经成了一段友谊。\n# en: Two students walking across the lawn at sunset, laughing at the same joke, shadows long behind them.\nscene [ea4144] = 夕阳下两个学生穿过草坪，为同一个笑话大笑，身后拖着长长的影子。\n# en: Keep a little distance\nopt.left.label [37e0f8] = 保持一点距离\n# en: You stay friendly. Just not too close. Some walls are load-bearing.\nopt.left.say.1 [4470d5] = 你们保持友好。只是不太亲近。有些墙是承重墙。\n# en: Call it what it is\nopt.right.label [9f3cf3] = 挑明了说\n# en: \"So we're friends now,\" {npc} says. \"Officially.\" You shake on it, solemnly, then ruin it by laughing.\nopt.right.say.1 [eee457] = “那我们现在是朋友了，”{npc}说，“正式的。”你们郑重地握手，然后笑场毁掉了这份庄重。\n\n@event soc_become_close\n# en: The Kind of Friend You Call at 3am\ntitle [e891f8] = 凌晨三点可以去敲门的那种朋友\n# en: It's 3am. You can't sleep, and you've walked to {npc}'s door before you've even decided to. {They} open{s} it before you knock.\ntext [216ec9] = 凌晨三点。你睡不着，还没想好就已经走到了{npc}的门前。你还没敲门，{they}就把门打开了。\n# en: Two friends sitting on a dormitory floor at 3am wrapped in one blanket, empty mugs between them.\nscene [9f5741] = 凌晨三点，两个朋友裹着同一条毯子坐在宿舍地板上，中间摆着空杯子。\n# en: Apologise and go back to bed\nopt.left.label [f9def6] = 道个歉，回去睡觉\n# en: \"Don't be daft,\" {npc} says, but lets you go. In the morning there's tea outside your door.\nopt.left.say.1 [5078b4] = “别傻了。”{npc}说，但还是让你走了。早上，你门外放着一杯茶。\n# en: Talk until sunrise\nopt.right.label [f5afa4] = 聊到日出\n# en: You tell {them} things you haven't told anyone. {They} tell you things back. The sun comes up and neither of you notices.\nopt.right.say.1 [c821d1] = 你告诉了{them}从没告诉过任何人的事。{they}也告诉了你{their}心事。太阳升起来了，你们谁都没注意到。\n\n@event soc_secret\n# en: A Secret\ntitle [880a88] = 一个秘密\n# en: {~{npc} pulls you under the stairs. \"I need to tell someone or I'll burst. Swear you won't tell anyone. Not even the portraits.\"|{npc} closes the dormitory door, checks it, and checks it again. \"I have to tell someone. Swear you'll keep it.\"|{npc} walks you to the far end of the lake path, where nobody goes. \"Can you keep something? Really keep it?\"}\ntext [620902] = {~{npc}把你拉到楼梯底下。“我得告诉一个人，不然我会憋炸的。发誓你谁也不说。连肖像画都不说。”|{npc}关上宿舍门，检查了一遍，又检查了一遍。“我得告诉一个人。发誓你会保密。”|{npc}把你带到湖边小路没人去的尽头。“你能守住一件事吗？真的守住？”}\n# en: Two students huddled under a staircase, one whispering into the other's ear, a finger to their lips.\nscene [e33a93] = 两个学生挤在楼梯下，一个凑在另一个耳边低语，手指竖在唇边。\n# en: \"I'd rather not carry it.\"\nopt.left.label [a4f5da] = “我宁愿不背这个包袱。”\n# en: {npc} looks hurt, then relieved. \"Maybe that's better.\" You'll never know.\nopt.left.say.1 [8b5a46] = {npc}先是一脸受伤，然后松了口气。“也许这样更好。”你永远不会知道了。\n# en: \"I swear.\"\nopt.right.label [381a0e] = “我发誓。”\n# en: {~It's not a big secret. It's the kind that's only big to the person holding it. You hold it with {them}.|It's about a letter from home, and it's heavier than {they} let on. You carry half of it now.|It's ridiculous, and {they} know it's ridiculous, and {they} needed someone else to know anyway. You do.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5f42d1] = {~不是什么大秘密。是那种只对守着它的人来说很大的秘密。你陪{them}一起守着。|是关于一封家书的事，比{they}表现出来的沉重得多。现在你替{them}分担了一半。|很荒唐，{they}自己也知道很荒唐，但{they}还是需要另一个人知道。现在你知道了。}\n\n@event soc_argument\n# en: Words Exchanged\ntitle [260001] = 口角\n# en: It starts with something small — {~a borrowed book, a joke that landed wrong|whose turn it was to fetch the tea|a seat someone saved and someone else took} — and suddenly you and {npc} are arguing {~in the corridor|on the stairs|in the middle of the refectory}, louder than either of you intended.\ntext [838628] = 起因是件小事——{~一本借走的书，一个说错时机的玩笑|该谁去打茶|有人占的座位被别人坐了}——突然间你和{npc}就{~在走廊里|在楼梯上|在食堂正中间}吵了起来，嗓门比你们俩想的都大。\n# en: Two students facing off in an empty corridor, one with arms crossed, the other mid-gesture, both flushed.\nscene [935b50] = 两个学生在空荡的走廊里对峙，一个抱着双臂，另一个正比划着，两人都涨红了脸。\n# en: Apologise first\nopt.left.label [9f3966] = 先道歉\n# en: {~\"I'm sorry.\" {npc} deflates. \"...Me too.\" It's over. It feels better than winning would have.|You say sorry first. {npc} looks almost disappointed not to get to say it. You share a bun about it later.|\"I'm sorry.\" {npc} blinks, then laughs. \"We were both being absurd.\" You were.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d0711f] = {~“对不起。”{npc}一下子泄了气。“……我也是。”结束了。这比吵赢了感觉更好。|你先道了歉。{npc}看上去几乎有点失望，没轮到自己说。后来你们为这事分吃了一个面包。|“对不起。”{npc}眨眨眼，然后笑了。“我们俩都太荒唐了。”确实。}\n# en: Stand your ground\nopt.right.label [3881f5] = 坚持立场\n# en: {npc} storms off. You were right. It doesn't feel as good as you'd hoped.\nopt.right.say.1 [23d1ff] = {npc}气冲冲地走了。你是对的。可感觉并没有你希望的那么好。\n# en: You argue it out until you're both laughing at how ridiculous it got. Somehow you're closer after.\nopt.right.say.2 [16b2d4] = 你们一直吵到两个人都笑了出来，笑这事有多荒唐。不知怎么的，吵完反而更亲近了。\n\n@event soc_village_trip\n# en: A Day in the Village\ntitle [1afb52] = 镇上一日\n# en: \"Barge to the village? Tea at Mabel's, a wander round the bookshops, back before curfew?\" {npc} is already wearing {their} coat.\ntext [07e032] = “坐驳船去镇上吗？去梅布尔茶馆喝茶，逛逛书店，宵禁前回来？”{npc}已经穿好外套了。\n# en: A cobbled village street with crooked shopfronts, two students sharing a paper cone of roasted nuts.\nscene [743ea4] = 鹅卵石铺就的小镇街道，歪歪扭扭的店面，两个学生分享着一纸筒烤坚果。\n# en: Not this week\nopt.left.label [0fe3ce] = 这周不行\n# en: {~{They} bring{s} you back a cinnamon bun anyway.|{They} bring{s} you back a postcard of the village, which is three streets long.|{They} come{s} back with a story about a goat and tell{s} it badly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b6d43c] = {~{they}还是给你带回来一个肉桂卷。|{they}给你带回来一张小镇明信片，那小镇总共只有三条街。|{they}回来时带着一个关于山羊的故事，讲得很烂。}\n# en: Grab your coat\nopt.right.label [b14325] = 抓起外套\n# en: {~Tea, buns, a bookshop cat that sits on {npc}'s feet. You almost miss the last barge, laughing.|Mabel's, then the market, then a long argument about which bun is best. You get the last barge home with sticky fingers.|Rain, so you spend the whole afternoon in the bookshop. {npc} buys a book about knots and explains every one on the barge home.}\nopt.right.say.1 [64f247] = {~喝茶，吃面包，书店的猫趴在{npc}脚上。你们笑着差点错过最后一班驳船。|先去梅布尔茶馆，再逛集市，然后为哪种面包最好吵了半天。你们带着黏糊糊的手指赶上了最后一班驳船。|下雨了，你们一下午都待在书店里。{npc}买了一本讲绳结的书，回程的驳船上把每个结都讲了一遍。}\n\n@event soc_friend_sick\n# en: Sick Friend\ntitle [ac406d] = 生病的朋友\n# en: {npc} has Lake Fever and is miserable in the infirmary. Sister Amaranth says visitors are allowed, but she won't be responsible.\ntext [0a1413] = {npc}得了湖热病，在医务室里难受得要命。阿玛兰斯修女说可以探视，但出了事她不负责。\n# en: A student tucked into an infirmary bed under a mountain of blankets, a friend sitting beside them with a bowl of soup.\nscene [43529f] = 一个学生被埋在医务室病床的一堆毯子底下，一位朋友端着一碗汤坐在床边。\n# en: Send a card\nopt.left.label [72cab2] = 送张卡片\n# en: You send a card with a drawing of Sister Amaranth as a dragon. {npc} keeps it on {their} bedside table.\nopt.left.say.1 [eae564] = 你送了一张卡片，上面画着变成了龙的阿玛兰斯修女。{npc}把它摆在床头柜上。\n# en: Sit with them every evening\nopt.right.label [09fda2] = 每晚都去陪着\n# en: You keep {them} company for a week. Then you're in the next bed. You both find this hilarious.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c2cc5] = 你陪了{them}一个星期。然后你住进了隔壁床。你们俩都觉得这太好笑了。\n# en: {~You read aloud, badly, doing voices. {npc} recovers and claims it was the voices.|You teach {them} every card game you know and lose all of them. {npc} recovers, insufferably.|You bring soup from the kitchens every evening and tell {them} all the gossip. {npc} recovers mostly out of curiosity.}\nopt.right.say.2 [0b98ac] = {~你给{them}念书，念得很烂，还学各种腔调。{npc}康复后坚称是那些腔调治好了{them}。|你把会的纸牌游戏全教给了{them}，结果每一局都输了。{npc}康复了，得意得叫人受不了。|你每晚从厨房端汤过来，把所有八卦都讲给{them}听。{npc}康复，主要是出于好奇。}\n# en: Bring a spoon of Maud's honey\nopt.up.label [e2a568] = 带一勺莫德的蜂蜜\n# en: {~You stir silver honey into {npc}'s tea. {They} sit{s} up, sniff, and ask for more. Sister Amaranth pretends not to see.|{npc} takes one spoonful of the honey and colour comes back into {their} face. Amaranth asks where you got it, then asks for some.|The honey tastes of summer. {npc} is up and complaining by the end of the week, which Amaranth calls a full recovery.}\nopt.up.say.1 [8e70b0] = {~你把银色的蜂蜜搅进{npc}的茶里。{they}坐起来，闻了闻，还要。阿玛兰斯修女假装没看见。|{npc}吃了一勺蜂蜜，脸上恢复了血色。阿玛兰斯问你从哪儿弄来的，然后也要了一点。|蜂蜜有夏天的味道。不到一周{npc}就下床抱怨了，阿玛兰斯称之为完全康复。}\n\n@event soc_gift_given\n# en: A Small Gift\ntitle [9dafb3] = 一件小礼物\n# en: There's a small parcel wedged into your pigeonhole, tied with string. A note in {npc}'s handwriting: \"Saw this and thought of you. Don't make it weird.\"\ntext [ee4873] = 你的信箱格里塞着一个用绳子捆着的小包裹。附着一张{npc}字迹的字条：“看见这个就想到了你。别想太多。”\n# en: A small brown-paper parcel tied with string, wedged into a student's pigeonhole in the porters' lodge.\nscene [4c823d] = 门房的信箱格里塞着一个牛皮纸小包裹，用细绳捆着。\n# en: Thank {them} later\nopt.left.label [a0fa69] = 回头再谢{them}\n# en: {~It's a tiny brass compass that always points to the nearest teapot. You use it constantly.|It's a bookmark that hums when you stop reading. Annoying. You love it.|It's a pair of gloves that stay warm all winter. You wear them until they're threadbare.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e85db0] = {~是一个小小的黄铜罗盘，永远指向最近的茶壶。你天天都在用。|是一枚书签，你一停下不读它就哼哼。烦人。你爱死它了。|是一副整个冬天都暖和的手套。你一直戴到磨破。}\n# en: Get {them} something back (8 gold)\nopt.right.label [42a27b] = 回赠一份礼物（8 金币）\n# en: You find {them} {~a book {they}'d wanted for months|a tin of the good biscuits from the village|a sketchbook with good paper}. {npc} grins and calls you a menace, which is worth twice the price.\nopt.right.say.1 [f8c672] = 你给{them}找到了{~一本{they}想要了好几个月的书|一罐村里买的好饼干|一本纸张很好的速写本}。{npc}咧嘴一笑，说你是个祸害——这一句值两倍的价钱。\n\n@event soc_drift\n# en: Drifting Apart\ntitle [73c51a] = 渐行渐远\n# en: You realise you haven't really talked to {npc} in weeks. You see {them} {~across the courtyard|at the far end of the refectory|on the library steps}, laughing with people you don't know.\ntext [27eced] = 你发现自己已经好几周没好好跟{npc}说过话了。你看见{them}{~在庭院那头|在食堂另一端|在图书馆台阶上}，和一群你不认识的人说笑。\n# en: A student glancing back across a busy courtyard at another who hasn't seen them, the space between them full of strangers.\nscene [e8800d] = 一个学生回头望向熙攘庭院另一端没看见自己的人，两人之间隔满了陌生人。\n# en: Let it be\nopt.left.label [71cc4c] = 顺其自然\n# en: People drift. It's nobody's fault. You still smile when you pass in the halls.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a48fa] = 人总会渐行渐远。不是谁的错。在走廊里擦肩而过时，你们仍会微笑。\n# en: Go over and say hello\nopt.right.label [cdae69] = 走过去打个招呼\n# en: {~\"There you are!\" {npc} says, as if you'd been the one missing. Maybe you had.|{npc} introduces you to everyone as \"the one I told you about\". You wonder what {they} told them. It seems to have been good.|{npc} shifts over to make room on the bench without a word, as if your seat had been waiting.}\nopt.right.say.1 [edea7c] = {~“你在这儿呀！”{npc}说，好像失踪的人是你。也许确实是。|{npc}向所有人介绍你：“就是我跟你们说过的那个。”你很好奇{they}说了什么。听起来是好话。|{npc}一言不发地挪了挪，在长凳上给你腾出位置，好像你的座位一直在等你。}\n# en: You go over. It is friendly, and short, and you both say \"we should catch up\" in a way that means you won't.\nopt.right.say.2 [46fd68] = 你走过去。气氛很友好，也很短，你们都说了句“改天聚聚”，那语气意味着不会有改天。\n\n@event soc_rumour\n# en: Rumours About {npc}\ntitle [e3d846] = 关于{npc}的流言\n# en: A nasty rumour about {npc} is going round {~the common room|the refectory|the library steps}: {~that {they} cheated on an exam|that {they} {are} leaving at the end of term in disgrace|that {they} said something cruel about a friend}. It's not true. You know it's not true. People are laughing.\ntext [fdfb88] = {~公共休息室里|食堂里|图书馆台阶上}流传着一条关于{npc}的恶毒流言：{~说{they}考试作弊|说{they}学期末要灰溜溜地退学|说{they}对朋友说了很刻薄的话}。不是真的。你知道不是真的。大家在笑。\n# en: A cluster of students whispering behind their hands in a corridor, glancing toward someone just out of view.\nscene [78a3d1] = 一群学生在走廊里捂着嘴窃窃私语，朝视线外的某个人瞟去。\n# en: Let it blow over\nopt.left.label [962dcd] = 等它自己平息\n# en: It blows over. {npc} never finds out you heard it. You find out you don't like yourself for it, a little.\nopt.left.say.1 [95d2f0] = 它平息了。{npc}永远不会知道你听到过。可你发现，你有点不喜欢这样的自己。\n# en: Shut it down\nopt.right.label [e3e364] = 当场制止\n# en: You make the rumour-monger look so foolish that the story dies on the spot. {npc} hears about it later and just squeezes your arm.\nopt.right.say.1 [78c724] = 你让造谣的人出尽洋相，流言当场就死了。{npc}后来听说了，只是轻轻捏了捏你的胳膊。\n# en: You make a scene and now there are rumours about you too. {npc} thinks you're an idiot. A loyal idiot.\nopt.right.say.2 [ea7565] = 你大闹了一场，现在也有关于你的流言了。{npc}觉得你是个傻瓜。一个忠诚的傻瓜。\n\n@event soc_homework_help\n# en: Desperate Homework\ntitle [b925b8] = 作业告急\n# en: {npc} waves a half-finished {~essay|lab report|star chart} at you. \"It's due in an hour and I've written the title twice. Help.\"\ntext [eb5473] = {npc}朝你挥着一份写了一半的{~论文|实验报告|星图}。“一小时后就要交了，我只把标题写了两遍。救命。”\n# en: A student with ink on their face clutching a half-finished essay, another student leaning over to read it.\nscene [d955d2] = 一个脸上沾着墨水的学生攥着一篇半成品论文，另一个学生凑过来看。\n# en: Explain the concept\nopt.left.label [d8e8f3] = 讲解知识点\n# en: You explain it so clearly that {npc} finishes the essay with time to spare. {They} insist{s} you're a genius.\nopt.left.say.1 [cf80f6] = 你讲得清清楚楚，{npc}提前写完了论文。{they}坚称你是个天才。\n# en: You explain it so confusingly that you both end up more lost. You hand it in together, two confused people against the world.\nopt.left.say.2 [a0a001] = 你讲得一团乱麻，两个人都更糊涂了。你们一起交了上去——两个糊涂人对抗全世界。\n# en: \"Copy mine.\"\nopt.right.label [b2d7d3] = “抄我的吧。”\n# en: The professor notices two identical pieces of work. You both get detention, a lecture, and a note on your record that every one of your professors reads.\nopt.right.say.1 [d3d733] = 教授发现了两份一模一样的作业。你们俩都被关了禁闭，挨了一顿训，档案上还记了一笔，你的每位教授都会看到。\n# en: {npc} hands it in and gets a better mark than you did. Unbelievable.\nopt.right.say.2 [c22ac9] = {npc}交了上去，分数比你还高。难以置信。\n\n@event soc_faculty_advice\n# en: A Word of Advice\ntitle [594b8b] = 一句忠告\n# en: {npc} catches you after a lecture. \"You've been working hard. Or hardly working. I can never tell with your year. Some advice, if you'll take it?\"\ntext [890d50] = 讲座结束后{npc}叫住了你。“你一直很用功。或者说一直很用‘空’。你们这一届我总是分不清。给你点建议，听不听？”\n# en: A professor walking with a student along a covered walkway, pointing at something in an open book.\nscene [41385c] = 一位教授和一名学生沿着有顶棚的步道走着，指着一本摊开的书里的某处。\n# en: Politely decline\nopt.left.label [8a601d] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: \"Suit yourself.\" {They} walk{s} off. Faculty have long memories.\nopt.left.say.1 [19801b] = “随你。”{they}走开了。教职员的记性可都很好。\n# en: Listen carefully\nopt.right.label [1b6b4d] = 认真听\n# en: It's good advice about how to study, {~annoyingly good|delivered at great length|illustrated with a story from {their} own disastrous first year}. Every piece of work you hand in that month is better for it.\nopt.right.say.1 [3db8c9] = 是关于怎么学习的好建议，{~好得让人恼火|讲得极其冗长|还配了一个{their}自己一年级时惨痛经历的故事}。那个月你交的每一份作业都因此进步了。\n\n@event soc_spark\n# en: A Spark\ntitle [21e629] = 火花\n# en: You and {npc} reach for {&the same quill|the last biscuit on the plate|the same library book} at the same moment. Your hands touch. Neither of you lets go immediately. {&It's a perfectly ordinary quill.|It's a perfectly ordinary biscuit.|It's a perfectly ordinary book.}\ntext [8c5ce5] = 你和{npc}同时伸手去拿{&同一支羽毛笔|盘子里最后一块饼干|同一本图书馆的书}。你们的手碰到了。谁也没有马上松开。{&那只是一支再普通不过的羽毛笔。|那只是一块再普通不过的饼干。|那只是一本再普通不过的书。}\n# en: Two hands brushing as they reach for the same quill, both students freezing for a heartbeat.\nscene [d87696] = 两只手在伸向同一支羽毛笔时轻轻相触，两个学生都愣了一拍。\n# en: Laugh it off\nopt.left.label [29c02a] = 一笑了之\n# en: \"All yours,\" you say. {npc} laughs. The moment passes. Mostly.\nopt.left.say.1 [134287] = “你用吧。”你说。{npc}笑了。那一刻过去了。大体上。\n# en: Hold on a moment longer\nopt.right.label [04d88b] = 多握一会儿\n# en: {npc} looks up. Something in {their} face shifts. Then {they} let{s} go, a little pink, and {&the quill rolls under the desk|the biscuit breaks neatly in half|the book falls open on the floor between you}.\nopt.right.say.1 [c7d64d] = {npc}抬起头。{their}脸上有什么东西变了。然后{they}松开了手，脸有点红，{&羽毛笔滚到了桌子底下|饼干整整齐齐地断成了两半|书摊开掉在你们中间的地上}。\n# en: {npc} pulls back as if burned, and the rest of the {&lesson|meal|afternoon} is very, very awkward.\nopt.right.say.2 [671934] = {npc}像被烫到一样缩回了手，那{&节课|顿饭|个下午}剩下的时间尴尬得要命。\n\n@event soc_date\n# en: An Invitation\ntitle [aba1c1] = 一次邀约\n# en: You could ask {npc} to {&the lantern show in the village|the travelling players at the Newt|the music on the quay} this weekend. As friends. Or, you know. Not as friends.\ntext [c7f340] = 你可以邀请{npc}这个周末去{&镇上看灯笼表演|镀金蝾螈酒馆看巡演剧团|码头听音乐}。以朋友的身份。或者，你懂的。不只是朋友。\n# en: A student nervously holding out a hand-drawn ticket to the village's floating lantern show.\nscene [57c131] = 一个学生紧张地递出一张手绘的小镇浮灯表演门票。\n# en: Ask as friends\nopt.left.label [bd7c43] = 以朋友身份邀请\n# en: You pay for both tickets and the hot cider. It's a lovely evening. You're both very careful about elbows.\nopt.left.say.1 [0135a9] = 两张票和热苹果酒都是你付的。那是个美好的夜晚。你们俩都非常小心地不碰到对方的胳膊肘。\n# en: Ask as more than friends\nopt.right.label [e73a1b] = 以不只是朋友的身份邀请\n# en: {npc} says yes before you finish the sentence. You hold hands through the whole {&show and miss every lantern|play and miss every line|evening and miss every song}.\nopt.right.say.1 [fb9a21] = 你话还没说完{npc}就答应了。{&整场表演你们都牵着手，一盏灯笼也没看见|整场戏你们都牵着手，一句台词也没听见|一整晚你们都牵着手，一首歌也没听进去}。\n# en: {npc} hesitates, then says {they}'d rather go as friends. It's a nice evening. It's a long evening.\nopt.right.say.2 [097e94] = {npc}犹豫了一下，说还是以朋友的身份去吧。那是个不错的夜晚。也是个漫长的夜晚。\n# en: {npc} says yes before you finish the sentence. You hold hands through the whole {&show and miss every lantern|play and miss every line|evening and miss every song}.\nopt.right.say.3 [fb9a21] = 你话还没说完{npc}就答应了。{&整场表演你们都牵着手，一盏灯笼也没看见|整场戏你们都牵着手，一句台词也没听见|一整晚你们都牵着手，一首歌也没听进去}。\n# en: {npc} hesitates, then says {they}'d rather go as friends. It's a nice evening. It's a long evening.\nopt.right.say.4 [097e94] = {npc}犹豫了一下，说还是以朋友的身份去吧。那是个不错的夜晚。也是个漫长的夜晚。\n\n@event soc_confess_generic\n# en: Feelings, Spoken\ntitle [0fdaae] = 说出口的心意\n# en: On the bridge between the towers, the wind snatching at your robes, you realise you can't keep it in anymore. {npc} is looking at you like {they} know what you're about to say.\ntext [0fb936] = 在塔楼之间的桥上，风扯着你的长袍，你意识到自己再也藏不住了。{npc}看着你，好像已经知道你要说什么。\n# en: Two students standing very close on a windswept bridge between towers, one with a hand over their heart.\nscene [80f257] = 两个学生紧挨着站在塔楼之间风大的桥上，其中一人手按在心口。\n# en: Say nothing\nopt.left.label [d1151d] = 什么也不说\n# en: The moment passes. You walk back together, a careful inch apart.\nopt.left.say.1 [8f2183] = 那一刻过去了。你们一起往回走，小心地隔着一寸距离。\n# en: Tell {them}\nopt.right.label [3c60a0] = 告诉{them}\n# en: \"Finally,\" {npc} says, and kisses you, and the wind steals your breath anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [bdde21] = “终于说了。”{npc}说着吻了你，而风依旧夺走了你的呼吸。\n# en: {npc} takes a long breath. \"I care about you,\" {they} say{s}. \"Just not like that. I'm sorry.\" The wind is very cold.\nopt.right.say.2 [fe43d9] = {npc}深吸了一口气。“我在乎你，”{they}说，“只是不是那种在乎。对不起。”风很冷。\n\n@event soc_partner_date\n# en: Date Night\ntitle [b6d8e3] = 约会之夜\n# en: {npc} has booked the window table at Mabel's. Candles. {season == \"winter\": Snow outside.|The lake going gold outside.} \"We're splitting the bill,\" {they} say{s}. \"Don't argue.\"\ntext [ae9174] = {npc}订了梅布尔茶馆靠窗的位子。蜡烛。{season == \"winter\": 窗外飘着雪。|窗外的湖正染成金色。}“这顿我们平摊，”{they}说，“别跟我争。”\n# en: Two people at a tiny candlelit table in a window alcove of a village tea shop, snow falling outside.\nscene [7b9157] = 小镇茶馆窗边凹室里一张小小的烛光桌，两个人对坐，窗外飘着雪。\n# en: Talk about the future\nopt.left.label [4d8f04] = 聊聊未来\n# en: {&You talk about where you'll be in ten years. In every version, you're both there.|You talk about after graduation, carefully, then not carefully at all.|You make a list of places you'll go together. It is already too long.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c9de6d] = {&你们聊起十年后会在哪里。每一个版本里，你们俩都在。|你们聊起毕业以后的事，起初小心翼翼，后来一点也不小心了。|你们列了一张以后要一起去的地方的清单。已经太长了。}\n# en: Talk about absolutely nothing\nopt.right.label [75d2d9] = 聊些完全没用的事\n# en: You argue for an hour about {&whether soup is a drink|whether owls are smart or just look it|which professor would win in a duel}, and {npc} pays the bill while you're making your closing argument. {&It is the best date of your life.|It might be better than the last one.|You lose the argument and don't mind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [cc5078] = 你们争论了一个小时{&汤到底算不算饮料|猫头鹰是真聪明还是只是看起来聪明|哪位教授决斗会赢}，你还在做总结陈词时，{npc}已经把账结了。{&这是你这辈子最棒的一次约会。|也许比上一次还要好。|你辩输了，却一点也不在意。}\n\n@event soc_partner_neglect\n# en: \"You're Always Busy\"\ntitle [46e97c] = “你总是很忙”\n# en: {npc} is waiting outside your door. \"I feel like I only see you in corridors,\" {they} say{s}. \"Do you even want this?\"\ntext [0e3f61] = {npc}等在你门外。“我觉得我只能在走廊里见到你，”{they}说，“你到底还想不想要这段感情？”\n# en: A person waiting alone at a table set for two, a candle burned low, the other chair empty.\nscene [b603a7] = 一个人独自坐在摆好两人份餐具的桌前，蜡烛快燃尽了，另一把椅子空着。\n# en: \"I'm sorry. I'll make time.\"\nopt.left.label [b59fcc] = “对不起。我会挤出时间的。”\n# en: You skip a study session for a long walk by the lake. It's the right call.\nopt.left.say.1 [8a05e5] = 你翘掉一次自习，陪{them}在湖边走了很久。这个决定是对的。\n# en: \"You know how busy I am.\"\nopt.right.label [a60981] = “你知道我有多忙。”\n# en: {npc} nods slowly. Something between you cools a degree.\nopt.right.say.1 [2bc62a] = {npc}缓缓点了点头。你们之间的什么东西，冷了一度。\n\n@event soc_breakup\n# en: The End of Something\ntitle [179272] = 某件事的结束\n# en: {npc} sits down at the far end of the bench. \"I think we both know,\" {they} say{s} quietly. \"This isn't working anymore.\"\ntext [c059c0] = {npc}在长椅的另一头坐下。“我想我们都心知肚明，”{they}轻声说，“这已经行不通了。”\n# en: Two people sitting on opposite ends of a bench in a grey garden, not looking at each other, rain beginning.\nscene [4b79cd] = 灰蒙蒙的花园里，两个人坐在长椅两端，谁也不看谁，开始下雨了。\n# en: Fight for it\nopt.left.label [7ed077] = 争取一下\n# en: You talk for hours. It's raw and honest and it hurts. And at the end, {npc} takes your hand again.\nopt.left.say.1 [207908] = 你们谈了几个小时。赤裸、坦诚，而且很痛。最后，{npc}又握住了你的手。\n# en: You try. It only makes it harder. In the end you're both crying, and it's over anyway.\nopt.left.say.2 [2de21e] = 你努力了。却只让一切更难。最后你们都哭了，而它终究还是结束了。\n# en: Let {them} go\nopt.right.label [185a66] = 放{them}走\n# en: You agree. It's terrible and gentle and right. {npc} kisses your forehead once before {they} leave{s}.\nopt.right.say.1 [e0897b] = 你同意了。这很糟糕，很温柔，也很对。{npc}离开前在你额头上吻了一下。\n\n@event soc_two_timing\n# en: Found Out\ntitle [f1af67] = 东窗事发\n# en: {npc} is holding two letters. One is to {them}. The other isn't. \"I found it in your bag,\" {they} say{s}, very quietly. Half the corridor has stopped to watch.\ntext [b63ccd] = {npc}手里拿着两封信。一封是写给{them}的。另一封不是。“我在你包里找到的，”{they}说，声音很轻。半条走廊的人都停下来看着。\n# en: A person holding up two crumpled love letters in a crowded corridor, the crowd parting around them.\nscene [28e97e] = 拥挤的走廊里，一个人举着两封揉皱的情书，人群在他们周围分开。\n# en: Beg forgiveness\nopt.left.label [b42d94] = 乞求原谅\n# en: {npc} doesn't forgive you. But {they} don't leave. Not yet. You're on borrowed time.\nopt.left.say.1 [27d6d1] = {npc}没有原谅你。但{they}也没有离开。暂时没有。你在借来的时间里过日子。\n# en: {npc} tears both letters in half and walks away. Everyone knows by dinner.\nopt.left.say.2 [3f568e] = {npc}把两封信都撕成两半，转身离开。晚饭时所有人都知道了。\n# en: Say nothing\nopt.right.label [d1151d] = 一言不发\n# en: Your silence is an answer. {npc} drops the letters at your feet and leaves. The story is all over the academy.\nopt.right.say.1 [855b96] = 你的沉默就是答案。{npc}把信扔在你脚边，走了。这件事传遍了整个学院。\n\n@event soc_ex_encounter\n# en: Awkward\ntitle [8ea9c0] = 尴尬\n# en: {~You turn into the library aisle and there's {npc}. Your ex. Holding the same book you came for.|The only free seat in the refectory is opposite {npc}. Your ex. {They} look{s} up at the same moment you do.|You open the door to the owlery and there's {npc}. Your ex. Holding a letter, halfway to an owl.} Neither of you moves.\ntext [d14bf3] = {~你拐进图书馆的一条过道，{npc}就在那儿。你的前任。手里拿着你正要找的那本书。|食堂里唯一的空位在{npc}对面。你的前任。{they}和你同时抬起了头。|你推开猫头鹰塔的门，{npc}就在那儿。你的前任。手里拿着一封信，正要交给猫头鹰。}你们谁也没动。\n# en: Two people coming face to face in a narrow library aisle, both holding books, neither moving.\nscene [0292af] = 两个人在图书馆狭窄的过道里迎面相遇，手里都拿着书，谁也没动。\n# en: Turn around and leave\nopt.left.label [a746c5] = 转身离开\n# en: You find a different book in a different aisle and read the same paragraph six times.\nopt.left.say.1 [a75b45] = 你在另一条过道找了另一本书，把同一段读了六遍。\n# en: Say hello like a person\nopt.right.label [06a265] = 像个正常人一样打招呼\n# en: It's awkward for one minute, then it isn't. You end up laughing about the worst of the dates you went on. Friends, maybe. Really.\nopt.right.say.1 [38140b] = 尴尬了一分钟，然后就不尴尬了。你们最后笑着聊起约会时最糟的那一次。也许能做朋友。真的。\n# en: \"Hello.\" \"Hello.\" You both stand there. Then you both leave. It's a start.\nopt.right.say.2 [f4910b] = “你好。”“你好。”你们俩站在那儿。然后各自离开。算是个开始。\n\n@event soc_rival_taunt\n# en: A Public Taunt\ntitle [0b1cd5] = 当众嘲讽\n# en: In the middle of a crowded {~meal|common room|library queue}, {npc} loudly recounts {~your most embarrassing moment of the term|the time you walked into a door that was a painting|your worst spell in front of the whole class}. People laugh. {They} look{s} very pleased.\ntext [86e910] = 在挤满人的{~饭桌上|公共休息室里|图书馆排队时}，{npc}大声讲起{~你这学期最丢人的一幕|你一头撞上一扇其实是画的门那次|你当着全班的面施出的最烂的咒语}。大家都笑了。{they}看起来非常得意。\n# en: A smirking student surrounded by a small audience, pointing at someone across a crowded dining hall.\nscene [fc4e76] = 一个得意洋洋的学生被一小群观众围着，隔着拥挤的餐厅指向某人。\n# en: Ignore {them}\nopt.left.label [c17487] = 不理{them}\n# en: The laughter dies down. {~You carry on with as much dignity as you can find.|You wait it out, and stare at a fixed point on the wall.|Someone nearby passes you a biscuit in silent solidarity.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b738e5] = 笑声渐渐平息。{~你尽可能体面地继续做自己的事。|你熬过去，眼睛一直盯着墙上的某一点。|旁边有人默默递给你一块饼干，以示声援。}\n# en: Fire back\nopt.right.label [edc214] = 反唇相讥\n# en: {~Your comeback is so good that someone at the far table applauds.|Your comeback lands so well that people repeat it for a week.|Your comeback is quiet, exact and devastating.} {npc} goes scarlet.\nopt.right.say.1 [6c86a5] = {~你的回击太漂亮了，远处那桌有人鼓起掌来。|你的回击一语中的，大家足足学了一个星期。|你的回击轻声、精准、致命。}{npc}满脸通红。\n# en: Your comeback comes out as a squeak. The laughter doubles.\nopt.right.say.2 [992f30] = 你的回击变成了一声尖叫。笑声翻了一倍。\n\n@event soc_make_peace\n# en: Burying the Hatchet\ntitle [91164f] = 握手言和\n# en: {npc} finds you after class, scuffing {their} shoes. \"This whole rivalry thing. It's getting tiring. Truce?\"\ntext [d5620e] = 下课后{npc}找到你，鞋尖蹭着地面。“这整个当对手的事儿。有点累了。休战？”\n# en: Two former rivals shaking hands on a dueling green at sunset, wands tucked away.\nscene [f9c463] = 夕阳下的决斗草坪，两个昔日对手收起魔杖握手。\n# en: Keep the edge\nopt.left.label [a15600] = 保持锋芒\n# en: \"Thought so,\" {npc} says, half-smiling. The game goes on.\nopt.left.say.1 [4e5e1c] = “我就知道。”{npc}半笑着说。较量继续。\n# en: Shake on it\nopt.right.label [982713] = 握手为定\n# en: You shake. It's strange — you know {them} better than most of your friends. Maybe that's the point.\nopt.right.say.1 [e1ff36] = 你们握了手。说来奇怪——你对{them}的了解，比对大多数朋友都深。也许这正是意义所在。\n\n@event soc_rival_bet\n# en: The Wager\ntitle [5a804e] = 赌约\n# en: \"Eight crowns says I {~outscore you in the next practical|light more candles in one breath than you|hold a ward longer than you},\" {npc} says, slapping coins on the table. A crowd forms.\ntext [ff384f] = “八克朗，赌我{~下次实践课分数比你高|一口气点的蜡烛比你多|护界撑得比你久}。”{npc}把硬币拍在桌上。一群人围了过来。\n# en: Two students slapping coins onto a table between them, a crowd gathering, a chalked tally on the wall.\nscene [8dd838] = 两个学生把硬币拍在中间的桌上，人群聚拢，墙上用粉笔记着比分。\n# en: Decline the bet\nopt.left.label [99b979] = 不赌\n# en: \"Thought so.\" {npc} pockets the coins. You decide not to care. You care.\nopt.left.say.1 [556d0b] = “我就知道。”{npc}收起了硬币。你决定不在乎。你很在乎。\n# en: Take the bet\nopt.right.label [8a4d52] = 接下赌约\n# en: You win by a hair. {npc} pays up with a theatrical bow. \"Next time.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [e1aac3] = 你以毫厘之差赢了。{npc}夸张地鞠了一躬，付了钱。“下次再说。”\n# en: You lose by a hair. \"Next time,\" you say. {npc} grins. \"Next time.\"\nopt.right.say.2 [8023a9] = 你以毫厘之差输了。“下次再说。”你说。{npc}咧嘴一笑。“下次再说。”\n\n@event spark_cooldown\n# en: Something Between You\ntitle [e1d3d8] = 你们之间的什么\n# en: Something has shifted between you and {npc}. {~They hold your gaze a moment too long.|They've started saving you a seat, and blushing about it.|Your names keep turning up next to each other in other people's gossip.} You could let it grow, or gently let it cool.\ntext [042740] = 你和{npc}之间有什么变了。{~对方看着你的时间长了那么一瞬。|对方开始给你留座位，还为此脸红。|你们的名字总在别人的闲话里挨在一起。}你可以任它生长，也可以轻轻让它冷下来。\n# en: Two students on a bench a little closer than friends usually sit, both pretending to read the same page.\nscene [dd0df1] = 两个学生坐在长椅上，比朋友通常坐得近一点，都假装在读同一页书。\n# en: Let it grow\nopt.left.label [8ba126] = 任它生长\n# en: {~You don't say anything. You don't move away either.|You hold their gaze back, for exactly as long.|Let them gossip. You sit beside them anyway, in front of everyone.}\nopt.left.say.1 [82adf3] = {~你什么也没说。也没有挪开。|你回望对方，时间不多不少，正好一样长。|让他们去说吧。你还是当着所有人的面坐到了对方身边。}\n# en: Let it cool, kindly\nopt.right.label [6bd6f5] = 温和地让它冷却\n# en: {~You find a gentle way to say you value them as a friend. They look relieved, or almost.|You keep it light and friendly, and the moment passes. The friendship stays.|You give the gossip nothing to work with, gently, and in a week it moves on. The friendship doesn't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [028f08] = {~你找了个温和的方式，说你珍惜这份友情。对方看起来松了口气，或者差不多。|你把气氛保持得轻松友好，那一刻过去了。友情还在。|你温和地不给流言任何把柄，一个星期后它就转向了别处。友情没有。}\n\n@event jealousy_two_invitations\n# en: Two Invitations\ntitle [b97cfd] = 两份邀请\n# en: Two notes in one morning. {suitor} asks if you're free on Friday evening, in neat handwriting, crossed out once. {other} asks the same, for the same evening, in a hurry. They both know the other asked. The whole corridor seems to know.\ntext [cbc824] = 一个早上收到两张字条。{suitor}问你周五晚上有没有空，字迹工整，划掉过一处。{other}问了同样的事，同一个晚上，写得很匆忙。他们都知道对方也问了。整条走廊似乎都知道。\n# en: A student holding two folded notes, one in each hand, in a corridor where two people wait at opposite ends.\nscene [657e49] = 一个学生一手拿着一张折好的字条，站在走廊里，走廊两头各等着一个人。\n# en: Say yes to {suitor}\nopt.left.label [686714] = 答应{suitor}\n# en: Friday with {suitor} is everything the note promised. On Saturday {other} passes you on the stairs with a smile that stops a little short of the eyes.\nopt.left.say.1 [396855] = 和{suitor}的周五，和字条上许诺的一样好。周六，{other}在楼梯上与你擦肩而过，笑容还没到眼睛就停住了。\n# en: Say yes to {other}\nopt.right.label [88b7a4] = 答应{other}\n# en: Friday with {other} runs late and warm. On Saturday {suitor} is very polite to you at breakfast, which is worse than if they'd been rude.\nopt.right.say.1 [4809fb] = 和{other}的周五很晚才散，暖洋洋的。周六早餐时，{suitor}对你客客气气，比翻脸还难受。\n\n@event jealousy_seen_together\n# en: \"Who Was That?\"\ntitle [8e9dfe] = “那是谁？”\n# en: {suitor} saw you laughing with {other} in the courtyard yesterday, and is now asking about it too casually, in the voice people use when they've rehearsed. \"You two looked… close. Not that it's any of my business.\"\ntext [f83a3f] = {suitor}昨天看见你在庭院里和{other}说说笑笑，现在正装作随口问起，用的是那种事先排练过的语气。“你们俩看起来……挺亲近的。当然，这不关我的事。”\n# en: Two students talking by a window in a crowded common room, one of them asking a question too casually while looking at someone across the room.\nscene [3f099c] = 拥挤的公共休息室里，两个学生在窗边说话，其中一个装作随口一问，眼睛却瞟着屋子另一头的人。\n# en: Tell the truth: you like {other}\nopt.left.label [093300] = 说实话：你喜欢{other}\n# en: It lands like a dropped plate. {suitor} nods, and swallows, and thanks you for not pretending. It's the kindest thing you've done all week, and it doesn't feel kind at all.\nopt.left.say.1 [9b3e8a] = 这话像一只摔碎的盘子。{suitor}点点头，咽了一下，谢谢你没有装傻。这是你这周做过最善良的事，却一点也不觉得善良。\n# en: \"Oh, we're just friends\"\nopt.right.label [8fedc9] = “哦，我们只是朋友”\n# en: {suitor}'s shoulders come down an inch. You spend the rest of the evening together, and nobody mentions the courtyard again.\nopt.right.say.1 [b90ca0] = {suitor}的肩膀放松了一寸。那天晚上剩下的时间你们一直在一起，谁也没再提庭院的事。\n# en: It goes well, until word gets back to {other}, who hears \"just friends\" and decides to believe it.\nopt.right.say.2 [0917cb] = 一切都好，直到话传到了{other}耳朵里。TA听到了“只是朋友”，决定当真。\n\n@event jealousy_cold_supper\n# en: A Quiet Supper\ntitle [c2a4e7] = 沉默的晚饭\n# en: {suitor} has gone quiet at supper, and answers in single words. From the far end of the hall, {other} is waving you over to an empty seat. You know exactly why {suitor} is quiet. So, by the look of it, does {other}.\ntext [c7f87d] = {suitor}晚饭时一言不发，问什么都只答一两个字。大厅另一头，{other}正招手让你过去，旁边有个空位。你很清楚{suitor}为什么沉默。看样子，{other}也很清楚。\n# en: A long refectory table at supper, one student eating in pointed silence beside another, a third person waving from the far end of the hall.\nscene [6a1477] = 晚饭时长长的膳堂餐桌，一个学生在另一个人身边刻意沉默地吃饭，大厅另一头有人在招手。\n# en: Stay and talk it through\nopt.left.label [5621eb] = 留下来把话说开\n# en: A long walk after supper and a careful, clumsy conversation. {suitor} laughs at the end of it, which is something. At the far end of the hall, the empty seat stays empty.\nopt.left.say.1 [16900d] = 晚饭后长长的散步，一场小心又笨拙的谈话。最后{suitor}笑了，总算有点什么。大厅那头的空位，一直空着。\n# en: Take the seat {other} saved\nopt.right.label [9c1575] = 坐到{other}留的位子上\n# en: The seat is warm and so is {other}. Behind you, {suitor} finishes supper alone and leaves without the pudding, which everyone notices.\nopt.right.say.1 [58b5f7] = 位子是暖的，{other}也是。你身后，{suitor}一个人吃完晚饭，连甜点都没吃就走了，所有人都看见了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/society.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/society.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@thread the_season\n# en: The Season\nname [eb00c9] = 社交季\n# en: The patrons had noticed you. Their round of suppers and their ball had a place laid for you, if you wanted it.\npremise [16d723] = 赞助人们注意到了你。他们的一轮晚宴和舞会上，为你留了一个位置，只要你想要。\n# en: An invitation came with a crest on it: the patrons' Season had a place laid for you.\njournal.1 [4d819c] = 一张带纹章的请柬寄来了：赞助人们的社交季为你留了一个位置。\n# en: At the first supper you were the talk of the table.\njournal.2 [c2424b] = 第一场晚宴上，你成了全桌的话题。\n# en: At the first supper you were plainly yourself, and some of the table liked it.\njournal.3 [02390b] = 第一场晚宴上，你朴朴实实地做你自己，桌上有些人喜欢这样。\n# en: Lady Vaneshade offered to sponsor you, and you let her.\njournal.4 [4e190f] = 凡妮希德夫人提出要做你的引荐人，你答应了。\n# en: Lady Vaneshade offered to sponsor you, and you thanked her and said no.\njournal.5 [1d0bd4] = 凡妮希德夫人提出要做你的引荐人，你谢过她，婉拒了。\n# en: At the patrons' ball, you outshone the Season's other darling.\njournal.6 [ba7e67] = 赞助人舞会上，你的风头盖过了社交季的另一位宠儿。\n# en: At the patrons' ball, you shared the floor with the Season's other darling.\njournal.7 [a12dec] = 赞助人舞会上，你和社交季的另一位宠儿共享了舞池。\n# en: A patron asked a favour in return, and you did it.\njournal.8 [4a9a2f] = 一位赞助人要你回报一个人情，你照办了。\n# en: A patron asked a favour in return, and you refused.\njournal.9 [c95cf3] = 一位赞助人要你回报一个人情，你拒绝了。\n# en: The Season ended with a standing invitation. You belong among them now, a little.\njournal.10 [8b9820] = 社交季结束时你收到了一份长期邀请。如今你多少算是他们中的一员了。\n# en: The Season ended, and you went back up the hill and didn't look back.\njournal.11 [1fa682] = 社交季结束了，你回到山上，再也没有回头。\n\n@event season_invitation\n# en: A Card With a Crest\ntitle [505a5a] = 一张带纹章的请柬\n# en: The card is heavy, cream and gilt-edged, and it has your name on it in a hand that has written a great many names. The patrons of the academy, it says, would be delighted to see you at the Season: a round of suppers in the town houses below the hill, and a ball at the end of it. People who go to the Season come back different. People say so as if it were a warning.\ntext [d50b17] = 这张请柬厚重，奶油色，镶着金边，上面写着你的名字，那笔迹写过许许多多的名字。请柬上说，学院的赞助人们很乐意在社交季见到你：在山下的宅邸里办一轮晚宴，最后以一场舞会收尾。去过社交季的人回来都变了个样。人们这么说的时候，像是在提醒什么。\n# en: A heavy cream card with a gilt crest lying on a dormitory bed beside a pile of textbooks, a student holding it up to the light.\nscene [8147ac] = 宿舍床上，一张带镀金纹章的厚重奶油色请柬放在一堆课本旁边，一个学生把它举到灯下看。\n# en: Accept\nopt.left.label [b26149] = 接受\n# en: You write back in your best hand and spend more than you meant on a decent coat. Word gets round the castle that you're going. Your friends are impressed and a little wary. \"Don't come back talking about wine,\" one of them says.\nopt.left.say.1 [429a69] = 你用最好的字迹写了回信，还在一件像样的外套上花了比预想多的钱。你要去的消息传遍了城堡。你的朋友们既佩服，又有点提防。“回来可别满嘴都是葡萄酒，”其中一个说。\n# en: Send your regrets\nopt.right.label [c24080] = 婉言谢绝\n# en: You send a polite note saying you're afraid your studies won't allow it. It's half true. The card goes in a drawer, and now and then you take it out and look at the crest.\nopt.right.say.1 [79acc5] = 你写了张客气的便条，说恐怕学业不允许。这有一半是真的。请柬进了抽屉，你时不时拿出来看看那枚纹章。\n\n@event season_first_supper\n# en: The First Supper\ntitle [877e01] = 第一场晚宴\n# en: The first supper is twelve courses in a town house with a ceiling painted with clouds. You're seated between a retired magistrate and a woman who owns half the orchards in the valley. Both of them have read about you. Both of them want to see whether you're as interesting as the Ledger said. The magistrate has an opinion on every course, and is plainly waiting for somebody to disagree with him.\ntext [c4f8ef] = 第一场晚宴有十二道菜，在一座天花板上画着云的宅邸里。你坐在一位退休的地方法官和一位拥有山谷里一半果园的女士中间。他们俩都读过关于你的报道。他们俩都想看看你是不是像《纪事报》说的那么有意思。那位地方法官对每道菜都有意见，显然正等着有人跟他唱反调。\n# en: A long candlelit table in a townhouse dining room, silver and crystal everywhere, a student in a new coat seated between two elderly patrons in jewels.\nscene [ee05b8] = 宅邸餐厅里一张点着蜡烛的长桌，到处是银器和水晶，一个穿着新外套的学生坐在两位珠光宝气的年长赞助人中间。\n# en: Argue with the magistrate\nopt.left.label [1b006f] = 跟地方法官争一争\n# en: You tell the magistrate he's wrong about the soup, and then about a famous old trial, and he's delighted: nobody has disagreed with him since he retired. By the ninth course the whole table is listening to the two of you. Someone at the far end asks your name twice, to remember it.\nopt.left.say.1 [7dfb29] = 你告诉地方法官他对那道汤的看法不对，接着又说他对一场著名旧审判的看法也不对，他高兴极了：自打他退休，就再没人跟他唱过反调。到第九道菜，整张桌子都在听你们俩争。远处有人问了你两遍名字，好记住它。\n# en: You take the magistrate on over an old case, and turn out to have the facts backwards, in front of the man who heard it. The orchard woman changes the subject, kindly, which is worse. You spend the last three courses very interested in your soup.\nopt.left.say.2 [aabc3a] = 你拿一桩旧案跟地方法官较劲，结果发现自己把案情记反了，而且是当着审过那桩案子的人的面。那位果园女士和气地岔开了话题，这更糟。最后三道菜，你对你的汤表现出了浓厚的兴趣。\n# en: Ask about her orchards\nopt.right.label [9d9b2c] = 问问她的果园\n# en: You leave the magistrate to the soup. You ask the orchard woman about her orchards, and she talks for an hour, delighted, about apple blight. The table thinks you're a little dull. She thinks you're the best guest she's had in years.\nopt.right.say.1 [d42897] = 你让地方法官去对付他的汤。你向那位果园女士问起她的果园，她高高兴兴地讲了一个小时的苹果枯萎病。桌上的人觉得你有点无趣。她觉得你是她这些年来最好的客人。\n\n@event season_lady_vaneshade\n# en: Lady Vaneshade's Offer\ntitle [4e8a64] = 凡妮希德夫人的提议\n# en: At the second supper, Lady Vaneshade{met(lyra): , Lyra's mother,|} takes you aside into a drawing room. \"You'll want a sponsor,\" she says. \"Someone to tell you which fork, and which people. I could be that. In return, you'll be mine: seen with my family, and not with anyone I'd rather you weren't.\" She doesn't say who that is. She doesn't have to.\ntext [b42cd2] = 第二场晚宴上，凡妮希德夫人{met(lyra): ，莱拉的母亲，|}把你带到一间客厅里。“你会需要一位引荐人，”她说，“告诉你该用哪把叉子，该见哪些人。我可以做这个人。作为回报，你得是我的人：和我的家族一起露面，不和任何我不愿看到的人在一起。”她没说那是谁。她也用不着说。\n# en: A tall woman in crimson and black in a drawing room by a fire, holding a glass she isn't drinking from, a student standing very straight before her.\nscene [744151] = 炉火边的客厅里，一位穿深红和黑色衣裳的高个女人端着一杯没喝的酒，一个学生笔直地站在她面前。\n# en: Accept her sponsorship\nopt.left.label [17b382] = 接受她的引荐\n# en: \"Good,\" she says, and from then on doors open before you reach them. She tells you which forks. She also tells you, once, very quietly, who not to be seen with, and you find that you've done as she says before you've thought about it.\nopt.left.say.1 [b413fa] = “很好，”她说，从那以后，门总在你走到之前就打开了。她告诉你该用哪把叉子。有一次她也非常小声地告诉你不该和谁一起露面，而你发现，你还没想清楚，就已经照她说的做了。\n# en: Thank her, and say no\nopt.right.label [ecf60d] = 谢过她，然后拒绝\n# en: \"I'd rather find my own forks,\" you say. Lady Vaneshade looks at you for a long moment, then smiles, not warmly. \"Most people do,\" she says. \"Most people find the wrong ones.\" The doors stay shut a little longer after that.\nopt.right.say.1 [86902a] = “我宁可自己找叉子，”你说。凡妮希德夫人看了你好一会儿，然后笑了，笑得并不温暖。“大多数人都这么想，”她说，“大多数人找错了。”从那以后，那些门开得慢了些。\n\n@event season_rival\n# en: The Season's Other Darling\ntitle [8829ce] = 社交季的另一位宠儿\n# en: The Season always has a darling, and this one had one before you came: a young duelling champion from the city, handsome, charming and very used to being the most interesting person in the room. At the third supper they find you by the piano. \"So you're the student,\" they say. \"Shall we find out which of us they'll remember?\"\ntext [1341dd] = 社交季总有一位宠儿，这一季在你来之前就有了：一位来自城里的年轻决斗冠军，英俊，迷人，早已习惯做全场最有意思的人。第三场晚宴上，对方在钢琴边找到了你。“原来你就是那个学生，”对方说，“我们来看看，他们会记住谁？”\n# en: A crowded salon, a dashing young duelling champion holding court by the piano, a ring of admirers, a student watching from the doorway.\nscene [966142] = 拥挤的沙龙里，一位潇洒的年轻决斗冠军在钢琴边被一圈仰慕者围着，一个学生在门口看着。\n# en: Take them on\nopt.left.label [285bb5] = 接受挑战\n# en: You don't compete. You just tell the room a better story than they do, and the room turns, slowly, like a ship. The champion laughs, and bows, and says, \"Well. Until the ball, then.\" The whole Season hears about it by morning.\nopt.left.say.1 [b3c0cf] = 你没有去比。你只是给满屋子的人讲了一个比对方更好的故事，整个房间慢慢转了过来，像一艘船。冠军笑了，鞠了一躬，说：“好吧。舞会上见。”到早上，整个社交季都听说了。\n# en: You try, and the champion is better at this than you, much better, and the room stays with them. Afterwards they find you on the terrace. \"You were good,\" they say. \"I've just had more practice.\" It's oddly comforting.\nopt.left.say.2 [5fcdf5] = 你试了，可冠军比你擅长得多，满屋子的人一直围着对方。后来对方在露台上找到你。“你很好，”对方说，“我只是练得多些。”这话莫名地让人宽慰。\n# en: Make a friend of them\nopt.right.label [ad4a5d] = 和对方交个朋友\n# en: \"Why don't we not?\" you say, and the champion looks surprised, then delighted. You spend the rest of the evening on the terrace, laughing at everyone else. The Season is a little disappointed. You've never had a better supper.\nopt.right.say.1 [28e821] = “我们干脆别比了吧？”你说，冠军先是吃了一惊，接着高兴起来。那个晚上剩下的时间，你们俩在露台上取笑别人。社交季有点失望。你从没吃过比这更好的晚宴。\n\n@event season_ball\n# en: The Patrons' Ball\ntitle [eea43b] = 赞助人舞会\n# en: The Season ends with the patrons' ball. {flag(season_ball_star): Everyone knows by now that you're the one to watch, and the room turns when you come down the stairs.|You and the champion come down the stairs together, by arrangement, and the room can't decide which of you to look at.} The patrons are watching who you dance with, who you talk to, and how long you stay.\ntext [cd7c48] = 社交季以赞助人舞会收尾。{flag(season_ball_star): 如今人人都知道你是最值得看的那个，你走下楼梯时，整个大厅都转了过来。|你和冠军按事先说好的一起走下楼梯，整个大厅都拿不定主意该看谁。}赞助人们在看你和谁跳舞，和谁说话，待了多久。\n# en: A grand townhouse ballroom, chandeliers blazing, couples whirling, a student pausing at the top of a staircase as the room turns to look.\nscene [ce43eb] = 宅邸里富丽堂皇的舞厅，枝形吊灯通明，一对对舞伴旋转着，一个学生停在楼梯顶端，整个大厅转头看来。\n# en: Stay till dawn, and dance with everyone\nopt.left.label [78426a] = 待到天亮，和每个人跳舞\n# en: You dance with the magistrate, the orchard woman, the champion and a very old duke who treads on your feet with great dignity. You leave at dawn with your shoes in your hand. By noon every patron in the valley knows your name.\nopt.left.say.1 [9685df] = 你和地方法官跳了，和果园女士跳了，和冠军跳了，还和一位郑重其事地踩你脚的老公爵跳了。天亮时你拎着鞋离开。到中午，山谷里每一位赞助人都知道了你的名字。\n# en: Leave at midnight, while it's still lovely\nopt.right.label [b908e8] = 午夜离开，趁一切还美好\n# en: You dance three dances, eat one perfect pastry and slip out at midnight into the cold. Walking up the hill under the stars, you think it was the loveliest night of your life, and you're glad you didn't stay long enough to spoil it.\nopt.right.say.1 [d9c1ba] = 你跳了三支舞，吃了一块完美的酥饼，午夜时溜进了寒夜。星光下走回山上时，你觉得这是你一生中最美的夜晚，很庆幸没有待到把它弄糟。\n\n@event season_friends\n# en: Back Up the Hill\ntitle [879a43] = 回到山上\n# en: You come back to the common room after the Season, still in the good coat, and {f} and the others are in the middle of a joke you don't understand, about something that happened while you were away. {f} looks up. \"Oh,\" {they} say{s}. \"You're back. How were the patrons?\" It's not unkind. It's not quite warm either.\ntext [75f645] = 社交季过后，你回到公共休息室，还穿着那件好外套，{f}和其他人正说到一个你听不懂的笑话，讲的是你不在时发生的事。{f}抬起头。“哦，”{they}说，“你回来了。赞助人们怎么样？”这话并不刻薄。只是也不太热络。\n# en: A common room on an ordinary evening, a group of friends around a table mid-laugh, one student standing in the doorway still in a good coat.\nscene [213ab2] = 一个平常夜晚的公共休息室，一群朋友围着桌子笑得正欢，一个学生站在门口，还穿着一件好外套。\n# en: Tell them everything\nopt.left.label [09f8b1] = 全都讲给他们听\n# en: You tell them about the ceiling with the clouds and the duke who trod on your feet. They listen, politely. Somewhere in the middle, you notice {f} looking at the coat. Later, you hear the joke you missed. It was very good. You weren't there.\nopt.left.say.1 [537548] = 你给他们讲画着云的天花板，讲踩你脚的公爵。他们礼貌地听着。讲到一半，你注意到{f}在看那件外套。后来，你听到了那个你错过的笑话。非常好笑。你当时不在。\n# en: Take off the coat and sit down\nopt.right.label [7e306e] = 脱下外套，坐下来\n# en: You hang the coat on the back of the door and sit down and ask what the joke was. It takes three people to explain it and by the end you're all crying with laughter. {f} passes you the last of the biscuits, as if you'd never gone.\nopt.right.say.1 [c9dac7] = 你把外套挂在门后，坐下来问那个笑话是什么。三个人才把它解释清楚，讲完时你们都笑出了眼泪。{f}把最后一块饼干递给你，好像你从没离开过。\n\n@event season_price\n# en: One Word From You\ntitle [dd6928] = 你的一句话\n# en: A letter from one of the patrons, very warm, very grateful for your company at the Season. And a small favour, between friends: the patron's grandson is applying to the Duelling Circle, and a word from you would settle it. You've seen the grandson duel. He's dreadful, and someone better will lose the place.\ntext [5d5b97] = 一位赞助人来信，非常热情，非常感谢你在社交季的陪伴。还有一个朋友之间的小人情：这位赞助人的孙子在申请加入决斗社，你一句话就能定下来。你见过那个孙子决斗。糟透了，会有更好的人因此失去名额。\n# en: A letter with a crest open on a desk beside a quill and an inkpot, a student sitting back with arms crossed, frowning at it.\nscene [a36395] = 书桌上摊着一封带纹章的信，旁边是羽毛笔和墨水瓶，一个学生抱着胳膊往后靠，皱眉看着它。\n# en: Write the word\nopt.left.label [a5525d] = 写下那句话\n# en: You write two warm sentences and seal them. The grandson gets his place. A purse arrives with the patron's thanks. A second-year who should have had the place stops you in the corridor to ask if you know why they didn't. You say no.\nopt.left.say.1 [b34686] = 你写了两句热情的话，封了口。那个孙子拿到了名额。一袋钱随着赞助人的谢意寄来了。一个本该拿到名额的二年级生在走廊里拦住你，问你知不知道自己为什么没被选上。你说不知道。\n# en: Refuse, politely\nopt.right.label [a4b1b7] = 礼貌地拒绝\n# en: You write back that the Circle chooses its own, and you wouldn't presume. The patron's reply is short and cool. The second-year gets the place, and never knows you had anything to do with it. That seems right.\nopt.right.say.1 [bb903c] = 你回信说决斗社自己挑人，你不敢僭越。赞助人的回信简短而冷淡。那个二年级生拿到了名额，永远不知道这件事和你有关。这样似乎才对。\n\n@event season_end\n# en: The Season Ends\ntitle [da0371] = 社交季落幕\n# en: Another card with a crest: the patrons hope you'll come again next Season, and the one after. {flag(season_sponsored): Lady Vaneshade has added a line in her own hand: \"You did well. Do better.\"|flag(season_price_refused): One patron's name, you notice, is missing from the bottom.|It is signed by every patron in the valley.} You could have a place among them, if you wanted one, for good.\ntext [4f6700] = 又一张带纹章的请柬：赞助人们希望你下个社交季再来，还有再下一个。{flag(season_sponsored): 凡妮希德夫人亲笔添了一行：“你做得不错。还要更好。”|flag(season_price_refused): 你注意到，落款里少了一位赞助人的名字。|山谷里每一位赞助人都签了名。}只要你愿意，你可以在他们中间永远有一席之地。\n# en: A student at a window with a heavy cream card in one hand, looking down at the lights of the town houses below the hill.\nscene [bc4d99] = 一个学生站在窗边，一只手拿着厚重的奶油色请柬，俯瞰着山下宅邸的灯火。\n# en: Take your place among them\nopt.left.label [673de4] = 在他们中间占一席之地\n# en: You write back that you'd be honoured. It's a door you can walk through now whenever you like, and you suspect you'll walk through it for the rest of your life. You're not sure, some evenings, whether that's a good thing.\nopt.left.say.1 [9d55c1] = 你回信说你深感荣幸。如今这是一扇你随时可以走进去的门，你猜你这辈子都会走进去。有些夜晚，你不确定这是不是件好事。\n# en: Let the Season go\nopt.right.label [c6b7dc] = 让社交季过去吧\n# en: You write back, kindly, that you've loved it and won't come again. Then you put the card in the drawer with the first one, and go down to the common room, where somebody has saved you a seat by the fire.\nopt.right.say.1 [e48ccc] = 你和气地回信说，你很喜欢，但不会再来了。然后你把请柬和第一张放进同一个抽屉，下楼去公共休息室，那里有人在炉火边给你留了个座位。\n\n@event fame_rivals_for_you\n# en: Both Want You Tonight\ntitle [6b8a12] = 今晚都想要你\n# en: {a} and {b} can't stand each other, and both of them have asked you to the Newt tonight, separately, each making very sure the other heard. It isn't really about the Newt. It's about who gets to be seen walking in with you. They're both waiting for your answer, in front of half the courtyard.\ntext [e2e6b6] = {a}和{b}互相看不顺眼，而两人都约你今晚去蝾螈酒馆，分头约的，还都确保对方听见了。这其实和酒馆无关。而是关于谁能被人看见和你一起走进去。两人都当着半个庭院的人等你的回答。\n# en: A courtyard at dusk, two students on either side of another, each holding an invitation and pointedly not looking at the other.\nscene [c5c915] = 黄昏时的庭院，两个学生站在另一个学生的两边，各自拿着一份邀请，刻意不看对方。\n# en: Go with {a}\nopt.left.label [a0bfbe] = 和{a}去\n# en: You go with {a}. {a} walks into the Newt a little taller than usual. {b} hears about it by breakfast, and greets you all week with a small, bright, terrible smile.\nopt.left.say.1 [acadc4] = 你和{a}去了。{a}走进蝾螈酒馆时比平时挺拔了些。{b}早饭时就听说了，接下来一整个星期见到你都挂着一个小小的、明亮的、可怕的微笑。\n# en: Turn both down, kindly\nopt.right.label [609976] = 和气地都拒绝了\n# en: You tell them both you're staying in, and do, and read in the common room till late. Each of them assumes you've gone with the other. By the time they work out you haven't, they've both forgiven you.\nopt.right.say.1 [12fb49] = 你告诉他们俩你要待在宿舍，你也确实待在公共休息室里读书读到很晚。他们俩都以为你和另一个人去了。等他们发现你谁也没跟，两人都已经原谅你了。\n\n@event fame_wedge\n# en: Using You\ntitle [5a97fc] = 在利用你\n# en: {a} takes you aside. \"I don't like how {b} is with you lately,\" {they} say{s}. \"{b} never sat with us before you were famous. Now {b}'s everywhere you are, telling people you're close.\" {a} and {b} have been friends for years. You can see it costing {a} to say it.\ntext [d9c243] = {a}把你拉到一边。“我不喜欢{b}最近对你的样子，”{they}说，“你出名之前{b}从不和我们坐一起。现在你在哪儿{b}就在哪儿，到处跟人说你们很亲近。”{a}和{b}是多年的朋友。你看得出{a}说这话付出了代价。\n# en: Two friends arguing in low voices in an alcove of a corridor, one of them gesturing back the way they came, where another student is waiting.\nscene [53d19d] = 走廊的壁龛里，两个朋友压低声音争论，其中一个朝来的方向比画，另一个学生正在那边等着。\n# en: Tell {a} {b} is a friend\nopt.left.label [431708] = 告诉{a}，{b}是朋友\n# en: \"{b}'s always been kind to me,\" you say. {a} looks at you for a moment, then nods, stiffly. A week later you see {a} and {b} at opposite ends of the refectory. You're fairly sure that was you.\nopt.left.say.1 [d23a6e] = “{b}一直对我很好，”你说。{a}看了你一会儿，然后生硬地点了点头。一个星期后，你看见{a}和{b}坐在膳堂的两头。你很确定那是因为你。\n# en: Thank {a}, and watch\nopt.right.label [c53ff6] = 谢谢{a}，然后留心看着\n# en: You thank {a}, and say you'll keep an eye out, and you do. {b} is, you notice, a lot more interested in where you're going than in you. You start sitting with {a} again. {a} doesn't say \"I told you so\", which is generous.\nopt.right.say.1 [e620e4] = 你谢过{a}，说你会留意，你也确实留意了。你注意到，{b}对你要去哪儿比对你本人感兴趣得多。你又开始和{a}坐在一起了。{a}没说“我早告诉过你”，真是大度。\n\n@event fame_lend_your_name\n# en: Your Name on It\ntitle [27256b] = 署上你的名字\n# en: {f} is running a stall at the next fair, selling enchanted bookmarks to pay for next year's books, and has had an idea: what if the sign said \"as used by {player}\"? You have, in fact, used one. Once. \"It'd sell out in an hour,\" {f} says. \"Go on. I can't afford the set texts otherwise.\"\ntext [48668d] = {f}要在下一次集市上摆个摊，卖施了魔法的书签，好凑齐明年的课本钱，还想出了个主意：要是招牌上写“{player}同款”呢？你确实用过一个。就一次。“一个小时就能卖光，”{f}说，“答应吧。不然我买不起指定教材。”\n# en: A student holding a hand-painted sign for a stall at a fair, a pot of paint and a brush ready, looking hopefully at a friend.\nscene [b27a2e] = 一个学生举着一块手绘的集市摊位招牌，颜料罐和刷子都备好了，满怀期待地看着一位朋友。\n# en: Let {f} use your name\nopt.left.label [753383] = 让{f}用你的名字\n# en: The stall sells out in forty minutes, and {f} can buy every book on next year's list, with enough over for a new quill. {f} is giddy all evening. You're asked to sign three bookmarks, and you do, and one of them ends up in the library, where it marks a page for years.\nopt.left.say.1 [8358ba] = 摊位四十分钟就卖光了，{f}买齐了明年书单上的每一本书，还剩下钱买了支新羽毛笔。{f}一整晚都乐得飘飘然。有人请你在三个书签上签名，你签了，其中一个最后进了图书馆，在一页书里夹了好多年。\n# en: The sign draws a crowd, and then the bookmarks start marking the wrong pages, all of them, at once. People bring them back in handfuls, saying your name in a tone you don't like. {f} is mortified. You help pack up the stall.\nopt.left.say.2 [4e312a] = 招牌引来了一大群人，接着书签开始夹错页，所有书签，同时。人们一把一把地把它们拿回来，说起你的名字时那语气你可不喜欢。{f}羞愧难当。你帮着收了摊。\n# en: Help at the stall instead\nopt.right.label [95412c] = 改去摊位帮忙\n# en: You say your name stays off the sign, but you'll spend the afternoon behind the stall. You sell twenty bookmarks, badly, and {f} sells two hundred, very well. It's one of the nicest afternoons of the term.\nopt.right.say.1 [a95c00] = 你说招牌上不写你的名字，但你可以在摊位后面待一下午。你卖出了二十个书签，卖得很笨拙，{f}卖出了两百个，卖得非常好。那是这学期最愉快的下午之一。\n\n@event fame_best_friend_claims\n# en: Your Best Friend, Twice\ntitle [db400c] = 你的挚友，两个\n# en: The gossip column this week has two letters. In one, {a} writes that {a} is \"{player}'s closest friend, as everyone knows\". In the other, {b} writes exactly the same thing about {b}. They can't stand each other. Everyone has read both, and everyone is waiting to see what you'll say.\ntext [44565b] = 这周的八卦专栏有两封来信。一封里，{a}写道{a}是“{player}最亲密的朋友，人尽皆知”。另一封里，{b}写{b}的话一模一样。他们俩互相看不顺眼。大家都读了两封，都在等着看你会怎么说。\n# en: A gossip column on a corridor board with two paragraphs circled in different inks, two students on opposite sides of the corridor, arms folded.\nscene [5a4aa0] = 走廊布告板上的八卦专栏里，两段话被用不同颜色的墨水圈了出来，两个学生站在走廊两边，抱着胳膊。\n# en: Write to the column yourself\nopt.left.label [750561] = 亲自给专栏写信\n# en: You write one line: that your closest friends are the two people who wrote in, and that they should compare notes, since they have so much in common. The column prints it. {a} and {b} are furious with you, together, which is a start.\nopt.left.say.1 [e2ab37] = 你只写了一句：你最亲密的朋友就是写信来的那两位，他们俩该好好交流一下心得，毕竟有那么多共同点。专栏登了出来。{a}和{b}都气坏了，冲着你，一起，这也算是个开始。\n# en: Your letter is meant to be funny and reads as if you were choosing. Each of them decides you meant the other. The column runs a follow-up, with diagrams.\nopt.left.say.2 [572707] = 你的信本想写得风趣，读起来却像是在二选一。两人都认定你说的是对方。专栏又出了一篇续文，还配了示意图。\n# en: Say nothing at all\nopt.right.label [0e1895] = 一言不发\n# en: You say nothing, to anyone. The column decides your closest friend must be someone else entirely, and spends a fortnight guessing who. {a} and {b}, united at last, both think it's very unfair.\nopt.right.say.1 [c048f6] = 你对谁都一言不发。专栏断定你最亲密的朋友一定另有其人，猜了两个星期是谁。{a}和{b}终于站到了一起，都觉得这非常不公平。\n\n@event fame_after_you\n# en: What They Found Instead\ntitle [147c59] = 他们找到了别的\n# en: When you were famous, {a} and {b} used to argue over who got to walk with you. Now nobody fights over you, and you see the two of them on the lakeshore path in the evening, walking together, very close, laughing at something. They haven't noticed you on the bench. They don't need to.\ntext [8f8d30] = 你出名的时候，{a}和{b}常常争着要和你一起散步。如今没人为你争了，你在傍晚的湖岸小路上看见他们俩一起散步，挨得很近，为什么事笑着。他们没注意到长凳上的你。他们也不需要注意。\n# en: A quiet lakeshore path in the evening, two students walking close together ahead, laughing, a third student watching them from a bench.\nscene [9a57d1] = 傍晚安静的湖岸小路上，两个学生挨得很近地走在前面，笑着，第三个学生坐在长凳上看着他们。\n# en: Call out to them\nopt.left.label [b99090] = 叫住他们\n# en: You call out, and they turn, and wave, and come over, and the three of you sit on the bench until it's dark. They're kind. You can see, though, that they'd have been just as happy walking on.\nopt.left.say.1 [704a75] = 你喊了一声，他们转过身，挥挥手，走过来，三个人在长凳上一直坐到天黑。他们很友善。不过你看得出，他们就这么接着走下去也一样开心。\n# en: Let them walk on\nopt.right.label [1172b6] = 让他们走吧\n# en: You let them walk on, and watch them go round the curve of the shore until you can't see them. It's a strange feeling, being the reason two people met, and not being needed any more. Mostly it's a good one.\nopt.right.say.1 [d70e8a] = 你让他们走了，看着他们绕过湖岸的弯道，直到看不见。成了两个人相遇的原因，却不再被需要，这感觉很奇怪。大部分时候，是种好的感觉。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/spell_sources.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/spell_sources.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event evo_first_flame\n# en: Flame, Properly\ntitle [1ee0a5] = 火焰，正式的\n# en: {level(evocation) == 0: Professor Thorne writes one word on the board: FLAME. \"Every duellist you will ever face has this ready before their wand is out,\" he says. \"So will you. By Friday.\"|{level(evocation) == 1: Evocation II meets at the duel hall, not the lectern. Thorne has set a straw target forty paces off. \"Last year you lit candles. This year the fire goes where you send it, and arrives.\"|Advanced Evocation opens with Thorne standing in a ring of fire he did not light. \"This is what a duel looks like when it goes wrong,\" he says. \"Your flame must hold while you are hit.\" He has hired a senior to hit you.}}\ntext [d12e8e] = {level(evocation) == 0: 索恩教授在黑板上写下一个词：火焰。“你们将来遇到的每个决斗者，魔杖还没拔出来就已经备好了这个，”他说，“你们也会。周五之前。”|{level(evocation) == 1: 塑能术 II 不在讲台前上，而在决斗厅。索恩在四十步外立了一个草靶。“去年你们点蜡烛。今年，火要去你让它去的地方，并且到达。”|高等塑能术的第一课，索恩站在一圈不是他点的火里。“决斗出岔子时就是这个样子，”他说，“你的火焰在挨打时也必须不灭。”他雇了一个高年级生来打你。}}\n# en: A lecture hall with one word chalked on the board, students holding small flames on their palms, a grey-bearded professor watching from the lectern.\nscene [756ae5] = 一间讲堂，黑板上用粉笔写着一个词，学生们掌心托着小小的火焰，一位灰胡子教授在讲台上看着。\n# en: Drill it until it comes\nopt.left.label [0dbd59] = 练到它听话为止\n# en: {level(evocation) == 0: By Friday the flame comes when you call it, and your fingertips are pink and tender. Thorne looks at your palm for a long moment. \"Hm,\" he says.|{level(evocation) == 1: You throw until your sleeves smoke. On the sixtieth try the straw target goes up with a satisfying whump. Thorne writes something down, without comment.|The senior hits you nine times. On the tenth your flame stays lit through it, and the senior, singed, asks for a rest. Thorne says \"Better,\" which is new.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [ea1481] = {level(evocation) == 0: 到周五，火焰一唤就来，你的指尖又红又嫩。索恩盯着你的手掌看了很久。“嗯，”他说。|{level(evocation) == 1: 你扔到袖子冒烟。第六十次，草靶轰地烧了起来，听着真痛快。索恩记下了什么，不置一词。|那个高年级生打了你九下。第十下时你的火焰撑住了没灭，被燎了一下的高年级生要求歇会儿。索恩说了声“好些了”，这是头一回。}}\n# en: Learn the shape of it first\nopt.right.label [4ff4f7] = 先摸清它的形状\n# en: {level(evocation) == 0: You work it out on paper while the room fills with small fires. Yours comes last, on Thursday night, alone in your room. It's a very correct flame.|{level(evocation) == 1: You chart the target's distance and the arc a flame takes. Your fire lands late but dead centre. Nobody saw, because they'd gone to lunch.|You watch how Thorne's ring keeps burning when the senior hits him. It takes a week of evenings to copy it, and the class teases you for every one.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [c7a379] = {level(evocation) == 0: 满屋子都是小火苗的时候，你还在纸上推演。你的火最后才来，在周四夜里，你一个人在房间里。那是一团非常标准的火焰。|{level(evocation) == 1: 你算出靶子的距离和火焰飞行的弧线。你的火来得晚，却正中靶心。没人看见，大家都去吃午饭了。|你观察索恩那圈火在被击中时如何不灭。花了一周的晚上才学到手，全班每晚都在笑话你。}}\n\n@event astro_first_foresight\n# en: A Moment Ahead\ntitle [bcae99] = 提前一刻\n# en: {level(astromancy) == 0: Astromancy I begins with a coin. The lecturer spins it on the brass desk and asks which way it will fall. \"Don't guess,\" the lecturer says. \"Look a moment ahead and read it.\"|{level(astromancy) == 1: Astromancy II, and Foresight must reach further. The lecturer deals star cards face down. \"The next three, in order, before I turn them.\"|Advanced Astromancy: the lecturer sits opposite you and says nothing at all. \"Foresee what I am about to say,\" the lecturer says at last, \"and say it first.\"}}\ntext [904f55] = {level(astromancy) == 0: 星象 I 从一枚硬币开始。讲师在黄铜桌上转起硬币，问它会落成哪面。“别猜，”讲师说，“往前看一刻，读出来。”|{level(astromancy) == 1: 星象 II，预见必须看得更远。讲师把星牌背面朝上发下来。“接下来三张，按顺序，在我翻开之前说出来。”|高等星象：讲师坐在你对面，一言不发。“预见我要说的话，”讲师终于开口，“然后抢先说出来。”}}\n# en: An observatory classroom by day, a coin spinning on a brass desk, students leaning in with their eyes half-closed.\nscene [175cf7] = 白天的观星台教室，一枚硬币在黄铜桌上旋转，学生们半眯着眼凑过去。\n# en: Look ahead until you see it\nopt.left.label [8b5160] = 往前看直到看见\n# en: {level(astromancy) == 0: The coin falls heads eleven times before you see it fall heads first. Then you see it every time, and your head aches with how far you're leaning.|{level(astromancy) == 1: You see the third card before the first, which is the wrong order and still counts as a start. By the bell you read all three, drained to the soles of your feet.|You say \"Tuesday\" a heartbeat before the lecturer does. It costs you an afternoon of feeling hollowed out, and a small, rare nod.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [b0e502] = {level(astromancy) == 0: 硬币落了十一次正面，你才在它落下之前看见它落成正面。之后你次次都看得见，脑袋因为往前探得太远而发疼。|{level(astromancy) == 1: 你先看见了第三张，后看见第一张，顺序错了，但也算开了个头。到下课时你三张都读出来了，累得脚底板都发空。|你比讲师早一口气说出了“星期二”。代价是一下午被掏空的感觉，外加一个难得的、小小的点头。}}\n# en: Ask how far ahead to look\nopt.right.label [08ab20] = 问该往前看多远\n# en: {level(astromancy) == 0: \"Less far than you think,\" says the lecturer, and makes you read the rules of it twice. You get the coin right on the last try of the hour, with the class watching.|{level(astromancy) == 1: The answer is a chart, and the chart is long. You copy it all and read the cards the next week, after everyone else.|\"A breath,\" says the lecturer. \"Anything past a breath is fortune-telling.\" You learn the breath slowly, and are the last in the room to get it.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [9ad7b0] = {level(astromancy) == 0: “比你以为的近，”讲师说，让你把规则读了两遍。你在这节课的最后一次尝试时才猜对了硬币，全班都看着。|{level(astromancy) == 1: 答案是一张图表，图表很长。你全抄了下来，下一周才读出那几张牌，比所有人都晚。|“一口气，”讲师说，“超过一口气的就是算命了。”你慢慢学会了那一口气，是屋里最后一个学会的。}}\n\n@event alch_first_mend\n# en: The Mending Practical\ntitle [c89d8b] = 愈合实践课\n# en: {level(alchemy) == 0: Alchemy I's first practical is a cracked teacup on every bench. Sister Amaranth sets them down without looking up. \"Mend it,\" she says. \"Then I'll let you near a person.\"|{level(alchemy) == 1: Alchemy II moves into the infirmary. Sister Amaranth hands you a first-year with a split lip from the Duelling Circle. \"Skin this time,\" she says. \"It argues more than china.\"|Advanced Alchemy: Mend for what's deep. Sister Amaranth gives you a sprained wrist, its unhappy owner and her own pocket watch. \"Bone takes patience,\" she says. \"Patients don't.\"}}\ntext [450246] = {level(alchemy) == 0: 炼金 I 的第一堂实践课，每张实验台上都放着一只裂了的茶杯。阿玛兰斯修女头也不抬地摆好。“把它补好，”她说，“补好了，我才让你们给活人治伤。”|{level(alchemy) == 1: 炼金 II 搬进了医务室。阿玛兰斯修女交给你一个在决斗社磕破了嘴唇的新生。“这回是皮肉，”她说，“比瓷器爱顶嘴。”|高等炼金：治深处的伤。阿玛兰斯修女给了你一只扭伤的手腕、手腕那位不高兴的主人，还有她自己的怀表。“骨头需要耐心，”她说，“病人可没有。”}}\n# en: An alchemy bench with a row of cracked teacups, a healer setting down the last one, students holding their hands above them.\nscene [309cce] = 一张炼金实验台上摆着一排裂了的茶杯，一位医师放下最后一只，学生们把手悬在杯子上方。\n# en: Mend it until it holds\nopt.left.label [8a7bb1] = 补到它撑得住\n# en: {level(alchemy) == 0: Your cup holds tea on the fifth try and leaks on the sixth, and you've spent everything getting there. Sister Amaranth pours some anyway and drinks it. \"Keep doing that,\" she says.|{level(alchemy) == 1: The lip closes under your hand, slowly, and you feel every bit of it leave you. The first-year says it tickles. Sister Amaranth says that means it worked.|The sprain knits with the watch ticking beside you. You're wrung out by the end, and the wrist bends like a wrist. Sister Amaranth puts the watch away. \"Again tomorrow,\" she says, which is praise.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [750ddd] = {level(alchemy) == 0: 你的茶杯第五次能盛住茶，第六次又漏了，你为此耗光了力气。阿玛兰斯修女还是倒了茶喝了。“保持住，”她说。|{level(alchemy) == 1: 嘴唇在你手下慢慢合上，你感觉到每一分都从你身上流走。新生说有点痒。阿玛兰斯修女说那就是成了。|扭伤在怀表的滴答声里愈合。到最后你精疲力竭，那只手腕弯起来就是一只手腕。阿玛兰斯修女收起怀表。“明天再来，”她说，这是夸奖。}}\n# en: Watch her do one first\nopt.right.label [8e2d6e] = 先看她做一遍\n# en: {level(alchemy) == 0: She mends hers without stopping her conversation with the next bench. You copy her hands. Yours takes the whole hour, and the bench beside you finishes first.|{level(alchemy) == 1: You watch her close a cut from the inside out. When your turn comes it's slower than hers by a mile, and the first-year sighs the whole time.|You watch her set a wrist and mend it in one quiet minute. Yours takes twenty, and the patient tells you about every one.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [e8a595] = {level(alchemy) == 0: 她一边补自己的杯子，一边照旧和邻桌的人聊着天。你模仿她的手法。你的那只用了一整节课，邻桌的同学都比你先补好了。|{level(alchemy) == 1: 你看她由里向外合上一道伤口。轮到你时，比她慢了一大截，那个新生一直在叹气。|你看她用安静的一分钟接好并治好一只手腕。你的用了二十分钟，病人把每一分钟都讲给你听。}}\n\n@event illu_first_glamour\n# en: Lighting, Not Lying\ntitle [9ae47b] = 是打光，不是撒谎\n# en: {level(illusion) == 0: Illusion I begins where Professor Vey always begins: every student glamours their own face first, one small change held for a minute. Then she sits on her desk. \"Now make me like you more, without a word. Glamour is not lying. It's lighting.\"|{level(illusion) == 1: Illusion II: glamour that lasts past the door. Vey sends you to the refectory at lunch with one rule. \"Be remembered for something you didn't do.\"|Advanced Illusion opens with Vey wearing your face. She teaches the first ten minutes in your voice, quite well. \"Now take it back,\" she says.}}\ntext [d976e0] = {level(illusion) == 0: 幻术 I 的开头和维伊教授每次一样：每个学生先给自己的脸施魅影，做一处小小的改变，维持一分钟。然后她坐到讲桌上。“现在一句话也不说，让我更喜欢你们。魅影不是撒谎，是打光。”|{level(illusion) == 1: 幻术 II：出了门还不散的魅影。维伊让你午饭时去膳堂，只有一条规矩：“让人记住你没做过的一件事。”|高等幻术第一课，维伊顶着你的脸。她用你的声音讲了前十分钟，讲得还挺好。“现在把它拿回去，”她说。}}\n# en: A bright classroom where a smiling professor in a violet scarf sits on her own desk, students across from her trying very hard to look likeable.\nscene [9ff3bc] = 一间明亮的教室，一位围着紫色围巾、面带微笑的教授坐在自己的讲桌上，对面的学生们拼命想显得讨人喜欢。\n# en: Try it on Vey herself\nopt.left.label [08df65] = 直接对维伊试\n# en: {level(illusion) == 0: Vey likes you a great deal for almost a minute, then shakes it off and applauds. You feel wrung out, as if you'd smiled all day.|{level(illusion) == 1: By supper three people remember you saving the cook from a falling ladle. There was no ladle. You're too tired to enjoy it.|You take your face back from her a feature at a time. It costs you everything you had. Vey hands over the last one, your frown, with regret.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [9392fc] = {level(illusion) == 0: 维伊非常喜欢了你将近一分钟，然后甩开了它，鼓起掌来。你累得像笑了一整天。|{level(illusion) == 1: 到晚饭时，有三个人记得你从掉落的长柄勺下救了厨师。根本没有什么勺子。你累得高兴不起来。|你一个五官一个五官地把脸从她那儿拿回来。这用光了你所有的力气。维伊把最后一样——你的皱眉——依依不舍地还给了你。}}\n# en: Practise on a mirror first\nopt.right.label [380b83] = 先对着镜子练\n# en: {level(illusion) == 0: You glamour your reflection, which likes you and is no help at all. Vey catches you at it. \"Start with a harder audience,\" she says, and the class laughs.|{level(illusion) == 1: You rehearse in the washroom mirror until a prefect walks in on you being charming at nobody. The glamour works on the second day.|You practise your own face for a week before you try to take it back. By then everyone has met Vey-as-you, and they liked her better.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [82604a] = {level(illusion) == 0: 你对自己的倒影施了魅影，它很喜欢你，可一点用也没有。维伊抓到你在干这个。“找个难对付点的观众，”她说，全班都笑了。|{level(illusion) == 1: 你在盥洗室镜子前排练，直到一位级长撞见你在对着空气卖弄魅力。第二天魅影才奏效。|你对着自己的脸练了一周才去把它拿回来。到那时人人都见过“维伊版的你”，而且他们更喜欢她。}}\n\n@event beast_first_haste\n# en: Borrowed Speed\ntitle [b659ee] = 借来的速度\n# en: {level(beastcraft) == 0: Beastcraft I's first spell is taught at a run. Professor Brask points at a hare in the paddock. \"Catch it. You won't, on your legs.\" She teaches you Haste in four words and lets the hare go.|{level(beastcraft) == 1: Beastcraft II: Haste in the air. Brask has a hawk on her glove and a broom against the fence. \"Keep up with her,\" she says, \"to the far tower and back.\"|Advanced Beastcraft: Brask's test is to keep pace with a hippogriff at full gallop, the length of the flight field, and still be able to talk at the end.}}\ntext [9701da] = {level(beastcraft) == 0: 驭兽 I 的第一道咒是跑着教的。布拉斯克教授指着围场里的一只野兔。“抓住它。靠你的腿抓不住。”她用四个词教会了你疾速，然后把野兔放了。|{level(beastcraft) == 1: 驭兽 II：空中的疾速。布拉斯克手套上停着一只鹰，篱笆边靠着一把扫帚。“跟上她，”她说，“到远塔，再回来。”|高等驭兽：布拉斯克的考验是跟一头全速奔跑的鹰头马身有翼兽并驾齐驱，跑完整个飞行场，到终点还能开口说话。}}\n# en: A muddy paddock at the stables, a weathered professor pointing at a hare while a student crouches to sprint.\nscene [e7a94c] = 马厩边泥泞的围场，一位饱经风霜的教授指着一只野兔，一个学生蹲下准备冲刺。\n# en: Run it down\nopt.left.label [69bc99] = 追上它\n# en: {level(beastcraft) == 0: You catch the hare on the fourth go, briefly, and it bites you. You sleep eleven hours. Brask says that's the price, and the hare agrees.|{level(beastcraft) == 1: You reach the far tower a wingbeat behind the hawk, and the hawk looks smug about it. Your legs shake all through supper.|You run the field at the hippogriff's shoulder and say \"good afternoon\" at the end, gasping. Brask laughs once. You're stiff for three days.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [2b8b3f] = {level(beastcraft) == 0: 第四次你抓住了野兔，抓了一小会儿，它咬了你一口。你睡了十一个小时。布拉斯克说这就是代价，野兔也同意。|{level(beastcraft) == 1: 你比鹰晚一个振翅到达远塔，鹰看起来很得意。整个晚饭你的腿都在抖。|你在鹰头马身有翼兽肩旁跑完全场，喘着气在终点说了声“下午好”。布拉斯克大笑了一声。你僵了三天。}}\n# en: Study how it moves first\nopt.right.label [b867e5] = 先观察它怎么移动\n# en: {level(beastcraft) == 0: You watch the hare for an hour, then borrow its speed properly, once, at the end. Everyone else is already muddy and proud of it.|{level(beastcraft) == 1: You watch the hawk's turns from the fence and only fly the course at dusk, alone. It's tidy. Nobody sees.|You study the gallop from the rail until you can feel it in your knees. You run it last, slower than the rest, and very sure.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [f197d8] = {level(beastcraft) == 0: 你观察了野兔一个小时，最后才好好地借来了它的速度，只一次。其他人早已满身泥巴、得意洋洋。|{level(beastcraft) == 1: 你在篱笆边看鹰怎么转弯，直到黄昏才独自飞完全程。飞得很干净。没人看见。|你在栏杆边研究它的奔跑，直到膝盖里都能感觉到。你最后一个跑，比别人慢，却非常稳。}}\n\n@event umbral_first_shadowstep\n# en: The Shadows in Between\ntitle [d0d838] = 中间的阴影\n# en: {level(umbral) == 0: Umbral Theory I. The lecturer snuffs every lamp but two and stands in the dark between them. \"Shadowstep,\" says the voice. \"In at one shadow, out at another. Don't stop in the middle.\"|{level(umbral) == 1: Umbral Theory II: Shadowstep under watch. The lecturer sits a prefect in the doorway with a lantern. \"Out of the room,\" says the lecturer, \"without being seen leaving it.\"|Advanced Umbral Theory: step, and keep stepping. At dusk the lecturer lays a path of shadows across the tower roof, chimney to chimney. \"To the far side. Without the floor.\"}}\ntext [a4aaba] = {level(umbral) == 0: 暗影理论 I。讲师熄灭了除两盏以外的所有灯，站在两盏灯之间的黑暗里。“影步，”那个声音说，“从一片阴影进去，从另一片出来。别在中间停下。”|{level(umbral) == 1: 暗影理论 II：被人盯着时的影步。讲师让一位级长提着灯笼坐在门口。“出这个房间，”讲师说，“别让人看见你离开。”|高等暗影理论：迈步，接着迈步。黄昏时讲师在塔顶铺出一条阴影之路，从烟囱到烟囱。“到另一头去。别碰地面。”}}\n# en: The top of the lecture tower in near darkness, two pools of lamplight on the floor and a long shadow between them, a student stepping into it.\nscene [c1c385] = 讲堂塔顶几乎一片漆黑，地上两团灯光，中间一道长长的影子，一个学生正迈进去。\n# en: Step through and trust it\nopt.left.label [26f36a] = 迈进去，相信它\n# en: {level(umbral) == 0: You step in, and the dark is cold, and you come out against a desk with your shin. It counts. The lecturer's voice says, \"Again,\" from somewhere new.|{level(umbral) == 1: You leave by the prefect's own shadow and arrive in the corridor with your elbow in a coat rack. The prefect is still watching the door.|Chimney to chimney, six steps, and on the fifth the shadow is thinner than you thought. You land on the far side with grazed palms and a grin.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [422fa6] = {level(umbral) == 0: 你迈了进去，黑暗冰冷，你出来时小腿撞上了一张桌子。这也算数。讲师的声音从某个新的方向传来：“再来。”|{level(umbral) == 1: 你从级长自己的影子里离开，出现在走廊上时胳膊肘撞进了衣帽架。级长还在盯着门。|烟囱到烟囱，六步，第五步的影子比你以为的薄。你掌心擦破，咧着嘴落在了另一头。}}\n# en: Map the shadows first\nopt.right.label [9d6755] = 先把阴影画下来\n# en: {level(umbral) == 0: You sketch where every shadow falls before you trust one. The class has stepped twice by then and is bored of watching you. Your step, when it comes, is clean.|{level(umbral) == 1: You learn where the lantern can't reach before you move, and the bell goes first. You get out the next day, unseen and unhurried.|You chart the roof's shadows as the sun goes down and miss the light for it. You cross at full dark, alone, to no applause.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [ed64e3] = {level(umbral) == 0: 你先画下每一片阴影落在哪儿，才敢信任其中一片。那时全班已经迈了两次，看你看得都腻了。你那一步迈得很干净。|{level(umbral) == 1: 你先弄清了灯笼照不到哪里才动，结果下课铃先响了。第二天你出去了，没人看见，不慌不忙。|你趁太阳落山画下塔顶的阴影，也因此错过了天光。你在全黑时独自穿过，没有掌声。}}\n\n@event art_first_animate\n# en: Make It Want One Thing\ntitle [715246] = 只让它想要一件事\n# en: Artifice begins with a broom. The lecturer says one word and the broom sweeps the workshop by itself, badly and with enthusiasm. \"Animate,\" the lecturer says. \"Give a made thing one want, and only one. Yours next.\"\ntext [2c8bb0] = 奇械术的第一课是一把扫帚。讲师说了一个词，扫帚就自己扫起了工坊，扫得很糟，热情却很高。“活化，”讲师说，“给一件造出来的东西一个念想，只给一个。下一个是你的。”\n# en: A cluttered workshop with a broom sweeping the floor by itself in uneven circles, a student dodging it, brass gears on every bench.\nscene [707033] = 一间杂乱的工坊，一把扫帚自己绕着不规则的圈扫地，一个学生在躲它，每张工作台上都是黄铜齿轮。\n# en: Give your broom its want\nopt.left.label [785d7e] = 给你的扫帚一个念想\n# en: Your broom wants to sweep, and sweeps. It also wants to sweep you, and does, out of the door and halfway down the stairs. The lecturer marks it a pass.\nopt.left.say.1 [44b2fc] = 你的扫帚想扫地，于是扫了起来。它也想扫你，于是把你扫出门，一路扫下了半段楼梯。讲师给了及格。\n# en: Read how the old ones were made\nopt.right.label [d19458] = 读读老东西是怎么造的\n# en: You read the workshop's oldest notes on wanting things until your broom is the last one still lying down. When it rises, it sweeps one perfect circle and stops, satisfied.\nopt.right.say.1 [8299d2] = 你翻读工坊里最老的、关于“让东西想要什么”的笔记，读到全班只剩你的扫帚还躺着。它站起来时，扫了一个完美的圆，然后满意地停下了。\n\n@event star_first_starlight\n# en: A Name to Call\ntitle [ea00b4] = 一个可以呼唤的名字\n# en: The first appointment is under the open dome, past midnight. Your tutor says a star's name aloud, the old name, the one on no chart, and points. \"Ask it for a thread of its light,\" the tutor says. \"Ask properly. It has been asked rudely before.\"\ntext [0f823e] = 第一次约课在敞开的穹顶下，过了午夜。你的导师大声说出一颗星的名字——那个古老的、没有记在任何星图上的名字——然后指向它。“向它要一缕光，”导师说，“好好要。从前有人对它很无礼。”\n# en: The observatory dome open to a black sky, a tutor pointing out one faint star to a student whose hand is raised toward it.\nscene [3ad912] = 观星台的穹顶向漆黑的天空敞开，一位导师向一个学生指出一颗暗淡的星，学生正朝它举起手。\n# en: Call until it answers\nopt.left.label [012011] = 呼唤直到它回应\n# en: You call until your voice goes and your hands are numb on the rail. At four the thread comes down, no thicker than a hair, and rests in your palm, warm. You don't feel the cold again until breakfast.\nopt.left.say.1 [17b053] = 你一直呼唤，直到嗓子哑了，手冻僵在栏杆上。四点钟，那缕光落了下来，不比一根头发粗，温暖地停在你掌心。直到早饭你都没再觉得冷。\n# en: Learn whose name it is first\nopt.right.label [45ad71] = 先弄清这是谁的名字\n# en: You ask about the name instead, and get an hour of history you'll need later. The thread comes for you at dawn, the last in the dome, with the tutor already yawning.\nopt.right.say.1 [6d82d4] = 你转而问起这个名字，得到了一小时你日后会用上的历史。那缕光在黎明时才来找你，你是穹顶下最后一个，导师早已哈欠连天。\n\n@event hollow_first_seal\n# en: Closed, Even to Me\ntitle [0197ec] = 封住，连我也打不开\n# en: The headmistress sets a small wooden box on the table and opens it. It is empty. \"Close it,\" she says, \"so that it stays closed. To anyone. Even to me.\" She sits down across from you with her hands folded. \"That is Seal.\"\ntext [f8bf9e] = 院长把一只小木盒放在桌上，打开。里面是空的。“把它合上，”她说，“让它一直合着。对任何人都是。连对我也是。”她在你对面坐下，双手交叠。“这就是封缄。”\n# en: A room without windows lined with black-bound books, a headmistress setting an open, empty wooden box on the table in front of a student.\nscene [7d3523] = 一间没有窗户、摆满黑皮书的房间，一位院长把一只敞开的空木盒放在一个学生面前的桌上。\n# en: Seal it as tight as you can\nopt.left.label [e17684] = 尽全力封紧\n# en: It takes everything you have and the rest of the hour. The headmistress tries the lid with one finger, then two, then not at all. \"Good,\" she says. You feel scraped hollow until supper.\nopt.left.say.1 [0017f8] = 这用掉了你所有的力气和剩下的整节课。院长用一根手指试了试盒盖，又用两根，然后不试了。“好，”她说。直到晚饭你都觉得自己被刮空了。\n# en: Ask what she keeps sealed\nopt.right.label [6b3304] = 问她封着些什么\n# en: \"Many things,\" she says, \"for good reasons.\" She doesn't say more, and you seal the box with that sitting in your stomach. It holds. She doesn't try it.\nopt.right.say.1 [bbf578] = “很多东西，”她说，“理由都很充分。”她没再多说，你带着这句话压在心口封上了盒子。它撑住了。她没去试。\n\n@event lyra_teaches_wildfire\n# en: Let It Run\ntitle [a021c6] = 放它去跑\n# en: Lyra lets you into the duel hall after it has closed. She cups her hands and the fire in them grows, and grows, until it lights the rafters. \"They taught me to keep it small,\" she says. \"This is what it does when I stop. {I've never shown anyone.\"|You've seen it once already. The offer's still open.\"}\ntext [4b1b74] = 决斗厅关门后，莱拉放你进去。她捧起双手，手里的火一点点长大，长到照亮了房梁。“他们教我把它压小，”她说，“我不压的时候，它就是这样。{我从没给任何人看过。”|你已经看过一次了。这个提议还算数。”}\n# en: An empty duel hall at night, a crimson-haired student holding a fire that keeps growing in her cupped hands, its light up to the rafters.\nscene [fdf277] = 夜里空荡荡的决斗厅，一个深红头发的学生捧着一团不断长大的火，火光一直照到房梁。\n# en: Ask her to teach you\nopt.left.label [431f80] = 请她教你\n# en: She teaches it the way she does everything, without explaining, twice. Your fire runs on the third try and bites your wrist on the way out. \"It does that,\" she says. \"You stop apologising for it eventually.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [47f413] = 她像做任何事一样教它：不解释，示范两遍。第三次你的火奔了出去，出去时咬了你的手腕一口。“它会这样的，”她说，“总有一天你会不再为它道歉。”\n# en: Just watch her let it run\nopt.right.label [ec745e] = 只看她放火奔跑\n# en: You sit on the bench and watch her let it run to the rafters and back. She doesn't offer again tonight. On the way out she says, \"Another time,\" which from Lyra is a promise.\nopt.right.say.1 [b02520] = 你坐在长凳上，看她让火奔向房梁又奔回来。今晚她没再提。出门时她说“改天吧”，从莱拉嘴里出来，这就是承诺。\n\n@event tobias_teaches_heartwood\n# en: Something to Stand Under\ntitle [d33246] = 可以站在下面的东西\n# en: Tobias kneels in an empty bed at the back of the greenhouse. \"Ma used to say a farm's only as good as its shelter,\" he says, and presses his hands into the soil. A sapling comes up between you, then a trunk, then branches overhead. \"Took me a year. I'll show you in an evening, if you like.\"\ntext [c7446d] = 托比亚斯跪在温室最里面一块空苗床前。“我妈常说，一个农场好不好，要看它的遮蔽，”他说着，把手按进土里。一棵树苗在你们之间冒了出来，然后是树干，然后枝条撑在头顶。“我花了一年。你要愿意，我一个晚上教会你。”\n# en: A greenhouse at dusk, a broad young man kneeling over bare soil as a sapling shoots up into a sheltering tree between two students.\nscene [9c4be4] = 黄昏的温室，一个壮实的年轻人跪在光秃秃的泥土上，一株树苗在两个学生之间蹿成一棵遮蔽的树。\n# en: Kneel down beside him\nopt.left.label [9f3a4e] = 跪在他旁边\n# en: You kneel in the cold soil until your knees ache and your back complains. Near midnight a little tree stands up out of your bed, wobbling, and spreads one branch over you. Tobias laughs from the belly and apologises for the mud.\nopt.left.say.1 [9dba49] = 你跪在冰冷的泥土里，直到膝盖酸痛、腰也抗议。快到午夜时，一棵小树摇摇晃晃地从你的苗床里站了起来，在你头顶撑开一根枝。托比亚斯从肚子里笑出来，又为满地的泥道了歉。\n# en: Sit under his tree instead\nopt.right.label [0c9cbd] = 改坐在他的树下\n# en: You sit under his tree and he tells you a long story about a goat. It is very peaceful. \"Another evening, then,\" he says, and means it.\nopt.right.say.1 [bca719] = 你坐在他的树下，他给你讲了一个关于山羊的长故事。非常安宁。“那就改个晚上，”他说，是真心的。\n\n@event seraphine_teaches_omen\n# en: Say It So It Lands\ntitle [60f6f8] = 说出来，让它应验\n# en: Seraphine stops halfway through a sentence and looks at the candle across the observatory. \"That will go out,\" she says, quite clearly, \"when I've finished the next word.\" It does. \"Saying it is the spell. Seeing it is just… I can teach you the saying part.\"\ntext [918803] = 瑟拉芬话说到一半停下，看着观星台另一头的蜡烛。“说完下一个词，”她清清楚楚地说，“它就会灭。”它灭了。“说出来就是咒语。看见只是……说出来那部分，我可以教你。”\n# en: The observatory at night, a pale student with distant eyes speaking softly while a candle across the room gutters out exactly as she finishes.\nscene [6a8ae6] = 夜里的观星台，一个眼神飘远的苍白学生轻声说话，房间另一头的蜡烛恰好在她说完时熄灭。\n# en: Learn to say it out loud\nopt.left.label [998886] = 学着把它说出来\n# en: It takes the whole night, and it's unsettling to hear your own voice go flat and certain. Near dawn you foretell a dropped spoon and the spoon drops. Seraphine smiles at something next week.\nopt.left.say.1 [73a48a] = 学了一整夜，听自己的声音变得平板而笃定，让人发毛。快天亮时你预言一把勺子会掉，勺子就掉了。瑟拉芬对着下周的某件事笑了。\n# en: Ask what she sees for you\nopt.right.label [49945e] = 问她看见了你的什么\n# en: \"Quiet,\" she says. \"Yours is always quiet.\" She doesn't teach you tonight. She says you'll ask again, and you know she's right, which is the strange part.\nopt.right.say.1 [236cfa] = “安静，”她说，“你的总是很安静。”今晚她没有教你。她说你会再来问的，你知道她说得对，这才是奇怪的地方。\n\n@event corvin_teaches_sleight\n# en: Watch the Other Hand\ntitle [baef32] = 看另一只手\n# en: At the Gilded Newt, Corvin rolls a coin across his knuckles, and then it's in your pocket, and then it's back in his. \"Grandfather's trick,\" he says. \"I'd teach it to a saint, if the saint promised to only use it on people who deserve it.\" The jokes stop. \"Do you promise?\"\ntext [39419c] = 在镀金蝾螈酒馆，科尔文让一枚硬币在指节间滚动，然后它到了你口袋里，又回到了他的口袋。“我爷爷的把戏，”他说，“我愿意教给一个圣人，只要圣人保证只用在活该的人身上。”玩笑停了。“你保证吗？”\n# en: A corner table at a tavern in the evening, a grinning student holding up a coin that is clearly not where it was a moment ago.\nscene [123700] = 傍晚酒馆角落的一张桌子，一个咧嘴笑的学生举起一枚硬币，它显然已经不在刚才的位置了。\n# en: Promise, and learn it\nopt.left.label [716332] = 保证，然后学\n# en: You promise. He teaches you over three drinks you don't pay for, and by the last one you lift his coin without him feeling it. He looks at you for a long moment. \"Don't make me sorry,\" he says, quietly.\nopt.left.say.1 [74d1d6] = 你保证了。他用三杯你没付钱的酒的工夫教会了你，到最后一杯时，你摸走他的硬币，他毫无察觉。他盯着你看了很久。“别让我后悔，”他轻声说。\n# en: \"Not that one, Corvin.\"\nopt.right.label [d8caaa] = “这个就算了，科尔文。”\n# en: \"Saint,\" he says, fondly, and the coin goes back in his own pocket. He buys the next round anyway. You suspect he's relieved.\nopt.right.say.1 [2407ea] = “圣人，”他说，语气亲昵，硬币回到了他自己的口袋。他还是请了下一轮。你怀疑他松了口气。\n\n@event mir_teaches_contraption\n# en: It Probably Works\ntitle [771dd5] = 大概能用\n# en: Mir sweeps a bench clear with one arm. \"Watch!\" A kettle, a mantel clock and a spoon jump together into a whirring thing that pours tea, mostly into the cup. \"No plans, no drawings, just ask the bits what they want to be! It's my own. It probably won't explode. Want it?\"\ntext [453e90] = 米尔一挥胳膊把工作台清空。“看！”一把水壶、一座壁钟和一把勺子跳到一起，变成一个嗡嗡作响、会倒茶的东西，茶大部分倒进了杯子。“不用图纸，不用设计，只要问零件想变成什么！这是我自己的。大概不会爆炸。想要吗？”\n# en: A cluttered workshop, an artificer flinging together a whirring machine from a kettle, a clock and a spoon while another student ducks.\nscene [1876f4] = 一间杂乱的工坊，一位机械师用水壶、时钟和勺子拼出一台呼呼转的机器，另一个学生赶紧低头躲。\n# en: Yes, show me how\nopt.left.label [e7175f] = 要，教我怎么做\n# en: Your first one is a teapot that walks and the second one sparks and singes your eyebrows. The third pours tea. Mir writes your name in a notebook, which you understand is the highest honour Mir has.\nopt.left.say.1 [cf4b5e] = 你的第一台是一只会走路的茶壶，第二台冒火花燎了你的眉毛。第三台会倒茶。米尔把你的名字写进了笔记本，你明白这是米尔能给的最高荣誉。\n# en: Help them clean up instead\nopt.right.label [d457a0] = 改帮米尔收拾\n# en: You help sort the bits back into their drawers while Mir explains every one. \"Next time,\" Mir says, \"you're building. Probably.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [e1e41b] = 你帮着把零件分回抽屉，米尔把每一样都讲给你听。“下回，”米尔说，“你来造。大概吧。”\n\n@event kael_teaches_hoarfrost\n# en: Cold Hands\ntitle [a4a2d1] = 冰冷的手\n# en: Kael holds his hand over a brazier in the courtyard, and the coals grey over with frost until the fire gives up. \"Where I come from, everyone learns this before they learn to read,\" he says. A pause. \"It's for putting fires out. Mostly. I could teach you, if you wanted it.\"\ntext [aec993] = 凯尔把手悬在庭院的火盆上，炭火蒙上一层白霜，直到火认了输。“在我们那儿，人人识字之前就先学这个，”他说。停顿。“是用来灭火的。大多数时候。你要是想要，我可以教你。”\n# en: A frosty courtyard at night, a quiet student holding a hand over a brazier as rime creeps across its coals and the fire dies down.\nscene [439e75] = 结霜的夜间庭院，一个安静的学生把手悬在火盆上，白霜爬过炭块，火渐渐熄了。\n# en: \"I want it.\"\nopt.left.label [b2d92f] = “我想要。”\n# en: He seems startled that you said it out loud. He takes your hands to show you, and his are as cold as ever. By the end your fingers ache and the brazier is frosted grey. \"Good,\" he says, and doesn't let go at once.\nopt.left.say.1 [d39fb4] = 你把这话说出口，他似乎吃了一惊。他握住你的手示范，他的手还是那么冷。到最后你的手指发疼，火盆结了一层灰白的霜。“好，”他说，没有立刻松手。\n# en: Warm his hands instead\nopt.right.label [c09ca8] = 改去暖他的手\n# en: You take his hands and hold them over the last of the fire. He doesn't say anything for a long time. \"Another night,\" he says, eventually, about the spell or the hands.\nopt.right.say.1 [138704] = 你握住他的手，放在最后一点火上。他很久没说话。“改个晚上吧，”他终于说——说的是咒语，还是手，你不确定。\n\n@event nessa_teaches_candour\n# en: Off the Record\ntitle [a5d3b6] = 不予记录\n# en: Nessa puts her quill down, says \"off the record\", and it goes still. \"Here's the real trick of interviews,\" she says. \"Ask the question they want to answer. And then this.\" She says a word, and you find yourself telling her the truth about your exams. \"That's Candour. Want it?\"\ntext [4579a4] = 内莎放下羽毛笔，说了句“不予记录”，笔就不动了。“采访真正的诀窍是，”她说，“问对方想回答的问题。然后是这个。”她说了一个词，你发现自己把考试的实情全告诉了她。“这就是坦言。想要吗？”\n# en: A newspaper office after midnight, an editor setting down her quill which goes still, two students talking quietly over cold tea.\nscene [e4fe83] = 午夜后的报社，一位编辑放下羽毛笔，笔静止不动，两个学生在凉掉的茶旁低声交谈。\n# en: Learn it from her\nopt.left.label [f81f8d] = 跟她学\n# en: She practises on you until you've told her three embarrassing things, then lets you practise on her. She tells you one true thing, quietly, and asks you never to print it. You won't.\nopt.left.say.1 [2f71f3] = 她拿你练，直到你说出三件丢脸的事，然后让你拿她练。她轻声告诉你一件真事，请你永远别登出来。你不会的。\n# en: Ask it on the record\nopt.right.label [66005c] = 要求“予以记录”\n# en: She laughs, picks up the quill and interviews you about why you won't learn it. It runs on page four. \"Another night,\" she says. \"Off the record.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [115745] = 她笑了，拿起羽毛笔，采访你为什么不肯学。报道登在第四版。“改个晚上吧，”她说，“不予记录。”\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/spell_uses.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/spell_uses.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event mabels_cold_range\n# en: The Cold Range\ntitle [e9bf9f] = 冷掉的炉灶\n# en: {No steam on Mabel's windows, and no smell of toast. The range behind the counter has gone out, and a queue of students in scarves is stamping its feet. \"No tea till it's lit, petal,\" says Mabel, rattling the grate.|The range is out again: the flue sulks in a north wind. Mabel looks at the queue, then at you, then at the coal bucket.|Mabel's range, cold again, and Mabel in front of it with a twist of paper. \"It knows you now,\" she says. \"Talk to it.\"}\ntext [a59fcf] = {梅布尔茶馆的窗上没有水汽，也闻不到烤面包的香味。柜台后的炉灶熄了，围着围巾的学生排成一队，直跺脚。“炉子不点着就没有茶，小宝贝。”梅布尔一边说，一边哐哐地摇炉栅。|炉灶又熄了：北风一刮，烟道就闹脾气。梅布尔看看队伍，看看你，又看看煤桶。|梅布尔的炉灶又冷了，梅布尔捏着一卷纸捻站在它跟前。“它现在认得你了，”她说，“跟它说说话。”}\n# en: A village tea shop on a cold day, its iron range out, a queue of students in scarves, a woman on her knees at the grate.\nscene [9ca5e2] = 一间山下小镇的茶馆，天气寒冷，铁炉灶熄了火，围着围巾的学生排着队，一个女人跪在炉栅前。\n# en: Fetch coal from the yard\nopt.left.label [503113] = 去院子里搬煤\n# en: {You haul two heavy buckets in from the coal yard. When the kettle finally sings, Mabel pours yours first and waves your coins away.|Two buckets, one dropped on your foot. The range catches on the third try, and Mabel puts a scone by your cup, \"for the foot\".|You fetch the coal without being asked. Mabel tells the queue you're \"staff, near enough\", and makes you carry the milk.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dcd2ff] = {你从煤场拎回两桶沉甸甸的煤。水壶终于响起来时，梅布尔先给你倒了一杯，摆摆手不收你的钱。|两桶煤，一桶砸在了你脚上。炉子试到第三次才着，梅布尔在你杯子旁放了一块司康饼，“给你的脚”。|没人开口，你就去搬了煤。梅布尔跟排队的人说你“差不多算店里的人了”，然后让你去端牛奶。}\n# en: Read in the cold till it's lit\nopt.right.label [24c390] = 冻着读书，等它点着\n# en: {You read a chapter in your coat while the queue grumbles. By the time the tea comes your fingers can't turn the page.|You read by the cold window. The chapter is good; the draught under the door is better at its job.|Coat on, book open, breath fogging. You learn a great deal and feel your toes rather less.}\nopt.right.say.1 [afafa8] = {你裹着外套读完一章，队伍在旁边抱怨。等茶端上来，你的手指已经冻得翻不动书页了。|你靠着冰冷的窗户读书。这一章写得很好；门缝底下的穿堂风干活更卖力。|外套穿着，书摊着，呵出白气。你学到了很多，也渐渐感觉不到脚趾了。}\n# en: Light it with a finger\nopt.up.label [2bfc60] = 用手指点着它\n# en: {You lean over the counter and touch the coals. The range roars, the queue cheers, and Mabel calls you \"a useful sort of petal\" for the rest of the term.|A flick, and the flue draws like a chimney in a storybook. Mabel pours your cup first and doesn't charge, \"for services to tea\".|{spell_level(flame) >= 2: You light the range and the lamps and the kettle's underside, one, two, three. Mabel applauds with the tea towel.|You light it before Mabel has finished asking. She gives you a look that's half thanks, half \"show-off\".}}\nopt.up.say.1 [70ef09] = {你探身越过柜台，碰了碰煤块。炉火轰地一声烧起来，排队的人一片欢呼，梅布尔这一整个学期都叫你“有用的小宝贝”。|手指一弹，烟道就像故事书里的烟囱一样呼呼抽起风来。梅布尔先给你倒茶，不收钱，“算你对茶事有功”。|{spell_level(flame) >= 2: 你点着了炉灶，又点着了灯，再点着水壶底下，一、二、三。梅布尔挥着茶巾给你鼓掌。|梅布尔还没开口，你就把它点着了。她看你的眼神一半是谢意，一半是“显摆”。}}\n\n@event boathouse_damp_stove\n# en: The Damp Stove\ntitle [c554df] = 受潮的炉子\n# en: {A rowing crew comes in off the lake soaked to the skin, laughing through chattering teeth. The boathouse stove is cold, and the kindling basket is wet through.|The crew again, wetter than last time: someone caught a crab and took the boat over. The kindling has been left out in the rain, as usual.|Another soaking. The crew have started looking at you when they come in, and then at the stove.}\ntext [27f52a] = {一支划船队从湖上回来，浑身湿透，牙齿打着颤还在笑。船屋的炉子是冷的，引火柴的篮子也湿透了。|又是那支船队，比上次还湿：有人一桨没划好，把船弄翻了。引火柴照例被扔在外头淋了雨。|又一次落汤鸡。船队现在一进门就先看你，再看炉子。}\n# en: A wooden boathouse at dusk, a rowing crew dripping and shivering around a cold iron stove, wet kindling steaming in a basket.\nscene [f9bbd4] = 黄昏时的木船屋，划船队的人滴着水，围着冰冷的铁炉子发抖，篮子里的湿引火柴冒着潮气。\n# en: Run to the boatman's loft for dry wood\nopt.left.label [536a8a] = 跑去船夫的阁楼拿干柴\n# en: {You run up to the loft and back with an armful of dry wood. By the time the stove's going you're as wet as they are, from the sweat.|The loft ladder, in the dark, with an armful of wood. You make it. The crew make room for you on the bench.|The loft ladder is the worst part. The crew cheer you down it like a returning hero.}\nopt.left.say.1 [04ef44] = {你跑上阁楼，抱着一捆干柴跑回来。等炉子烧旺了，你跟他们一样湿，是汗湿的。|黑灯瞎火的阁楼梯子，怀里抱着一捆柴。你成功了。船队在长凳上给你挪出个位子。|最难的是阁楼那架梯子。你从梯子上下来时，船队像迎接凯旋的英雄一样欢呼。}\n# en: Lend your scarf and leave them to it\nopt.right.label [5d5e5c] = 借出围巾，让他们自己想办法\n# en: {You give your scarf to the one with blue lips and walk back up to the castle with a cold neck. You'll get the scarf back. Probably.|You hand round your scarf and your sympathy and leave them to it. The scarf comes back a week later, smelling of lake.|Scarf, a joke, and goodnight. They wave you off, still shivering, still laughing.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7d77ce] = {你把围巾给了嘴唇发紫的那个，自己缩着冷脖子走回城堡。围巾会还回来的。大概吧。|你把围巾和同情都分了出去，让他们自己想办法。一周后围巾还回来了，一股湖水味。|一条围巾，一句玩笑，一声晚安。他们挥手送你，还在发抖，还在笑。}\n# en: Dry the kindling with a flame\nopt.up.label [ebf123] = 用火烘干引火柴\n# en: {You hold a flame under the wet kindling until it steams, then catches. The crew crowd the stove and somebody's lake-cold hand finds your shoulder in thanks.|A flame, coaxed small and patient, and the kindling catches in a fizz of steam. The crew name the stove after you. It's not a compliment, exactly.|You dry the whole basket in one long breath of fire. The crew offer you a seat in the boat next week. You decline, warmly.}\nopt.up.say.1 [fd1004] = {你在湿柴底下托着一团火，直到它冒出白汽，然后着了。船队挤到炉子边，不知谁湖水般冰凉的手搭上你的肩头，算是道谢。|一团火，耐着性子哄得小小的，引火柴嘶的一声冒着蒸汽燃了起来。船队用你的名字给炉子命了名。严格说来，这不太算是夸奖。|你长长呼出一口火，把整篮柴都烘干了。船队邀你下周坐他们的船。你热情地婉拒了。}\n\n@event secret_garden_late_frost\n# en: A Hard Frost\ntitle [73a497] = 一场重霜\n# en: {A hard frost is coming down out of a clear sky. Old Maud is going along the row of clay smudge pots under the humming pear with a tinderbox that won't strike. \"Every one, dearie,\" she says, \"or the old pear sulks for a month.\"|Frost again. The pear has gone quiet, which Maud says is how it shivers.|The smudge pots are set out before you arrive. Maud has left a second tinderbox on the wall, for you.}\ntext [93be83] = {晴空万里，一场重霜正降下来。老莫德拿着一只怎么也打不着的火绒盒，沿着嗡鸣梨树下那排陶制熏烟罐挨个走。“每一个都要点，亲爱的，”她说，“不然这棵老梨树要闹一个月脾气。”|又下霜了。梨树不哼了，莫德说，这是它在打哆嗦。|你到的时候熏烟罐已经摆好了。莫德在墙头给你留了第二只火绒盒。}\n# en: A hidden garden on a clear, freezing evening, the humming pear tree, a row of unlit clay pots and an old woman with a tinderbox.\nscene [70d1a8] = 一个隐秘的花园，晴朗而冰冷的傍晚，嗡鸣的梨树，一排没点着的陶罐，一位拿着火绒盒的老妇人。\n# en: Light the pots with a tinderbox\nopt.left.label [54b74c] = 用火绒盒点罐子\n# en: {Forty pots, a tinderbox and numb fingers. The last one catches as the frost comes down, and the pear starts humming again, low and grateful.|You light pot after pot on your knees in the frost. Maud brings you something hot in a chipped cup and doesn't say what it is.|You light your half of the row before Maud lights hers, and she pretends to be cross about it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e51b90] = {四十个罐子，一只火绒盒，冻僵的手指。霜降下来时最后一个罐子着了，梨树又哼了起来，低低的，满怀感激。|你跪在霜地里一个接一个地点罐子。莫德用一只缺了口的杯子给你端来一杯热的东西，没说那是什么。|你抢在莫德之前点完了你那半排，她装作生气。}\n# en: Wrap the low branches in sacking\nopt.right.label [d4c741] = 用麻袋裹住低处的树枝\n# en: {You climb into the lower branches and wrap what you can reach. It isn't all of it. Maud says some is a lot more than none.|Sacking, string and a ladder that leans the wrong way. You save the low branches; the top will have to take its chances.|You wrap the branches like presents. Maud says the pear will remember, and she says it as if pears do.}\nopt.right.say.1 [292830] = {你爬上低处的枝杈，够得着的地方都裹上了。不是全部。莫德说，有一些可比什么都没有强多了。|麻袋、绳子，还有一架歪向错误方向的梯子。你保住了低处的枝条，高处的只好听天由命。|你像包礼物一样把树枝裹好。莫德说梨树会记得的，说得好像梨树真会记事。}\n# en: Light every pot at once\nopt.up.label [134f29] = 一口气点亮所有罐子\n# en: {You hold out your hand and every pot in the row blooms at once, a line of small suns in the frost. Maud laughs, which you've never heard her do so loudly.|One breath, forty flames. The pear hums so loudly the bees come out to see. \"Well,\" says Maud. \"Aren't you handy.\"|{spell_level(flame) >= 3: You light the pots and hold the warmth over the whole tree like a hand. Not one bud browns. Maud says she'll name a pear after you, and does.|You light the row in two breaths. Maud says it took her forty years to learn to do it with a tinderbox, and she isn't sure which of you to be proud of.}}\nopt.up.say.1 [47e39d] = {你伸出手，整排罐子一齐绽开火光，霜夜里排起一列小小的太阳。莫德笑出了声，你从没听她笑得这么响。|一口气，四十团火。梨树哼得那么响，连蜜蜂都飞出来看。“好嘛，”莫德说，“你还真顶用。”|{spell_level(flame) >= 3: 你点着罐子，又把暖意像一只手似的罩住整棵树。一个花苞都没冻焦。莫德说要用你的名字给一只梨命名，后来真这么做了。|你两口气点完了一整排。莫德说她花了四十年才学会用火绒盒干这活，现在都不知道该为谁骄傲。}}\n\n@event refectory_tipping_tray\n# en: The Tipping Tray\ntitle [636073] = 要翻的托盘\n# en: {A first-year is coming down the aisle with a tray of eight soup bowls stacked like a tower, and a bench leg is sticking out right where they'll step.|The same first-year, a bigger tray, a different bench leg. Some people never learn; some benches don't either.|The tray, the first-year, the bench. You can almost hear the crash before it happens.}\ntext [e806f5] = {一个一年级生端着八碗汤叠成的高塔沿过道走来，而一条长凳腿正好伸在TA要落脚的地方。|还是那个一年级生，更大的托盘，换了一条凳子腿。有些人永远学不乖；有些凳子也是。|托盘、一年级生、长凳。你几乎能提前听见那声巨响。}\n# en: A crowded dining hall, a first-year carrying a towering tray of soup bowls between long benches, one bench leg sticking out into the aisle.\nscene [6bd0cc] = 拥挤的膳堂，一个一年级生端着高高一摞汤碗走在长凳之间，一条凳子腿伸进了过道。\n# en: Lunge for the tray\nopt.left.label [0089d8] = 扑过去接托盘\n# en: {You catch the tray an inch from disaster with soup up to your wrists. The first-year looks at you as if you'd stopped the sun.|You get a hand under it and one bowl of eight goes over. The first-year counts it as a miracle.|Tray, bowls, first-year, all upright, and not a drop on your sleeve. The table nearest applauds with spoons.}\nopt.left.say.1 [dcacbf] = {你在离灾难一寸的地方接住托盘，汤一直没到手腕。那个一年级生看你的样子，像是你让太阳停住了。|你一只手托住了它，八碗只洒了一碗。那个一年级生认为这是奇迹。|托盘、汤碗、一年级生，全都稳稳当当，你的袖子上一滴汤都没沾。最近那一桌用勺子敲着桌子鼓掌。}\n# en: {You get there just in time to wear the soup. All eight bowls. It's leek.|You catch the first-year and miss the tray. The crash is heard in the kitchens; the cook comes out to see.|You lunge, the bench catches you too, and you both go down in a lake of soup.}\nopt.left.say.2 [780c9d] = {你赶到得正是时候——刚好接住了一身汤。八碗全中。是韭葱汤。|你接住了一年级生，没接住托盘。那一声连厨房都听见了，厨娘出来看。|你扑过去，长凳把你也绊倒了，你们俩一起摔进一摊汤里。}\n# en: Move your own bowl out of range\nopt.right.label [b79db7] = 把自己的碗挪远\n# en: {You lift your bowl to safety and watch it happen. The leg, the trip, the crash. Nobody blames you. You blame you, a little.|You move your bowl. The first-year doesn't move theirs. Somebody else catches the tray, and the first-year thanks them with shining eyes.|Out of range, and dry. The crash is spectacular. You help pick up the bowls afterwards, which is something.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8c3194] = {你把碗端到安全的地方，看着事情发生。凳子腿，一绊，一声巨响。没人怪你。你自己有点怪自己。|你挪开了自己的碗。那个一年级生没能挪开TA的。别人接住了托盘，一年级生两眼放光地谢那个人。|躲开了，滴汤未沾。那一下摔得蔚为壮观。事后你帮着捡碗，总算做了点什么。}\n# en: Kick the bench leg in first\nopt.up.label [cb034f] = 先把凳子腿踢回去\n# en: {You see it a heartbeat early, the leg and the step and the tray, and nudge the bench in with your heel. The first-year sails past and never knows.|A glimpse ahead and a small shove. The tray arrives whole. The first-year doesn't notice; you do, and it's lovely.|You move the bench before the first-year has even turned into the aisle. The cook, watching from the hatch, raises her ladle at you like a salute.}\nopt.up.say.1 [872817] = {你提前一个心跳看见了——凳子腿、那一步、那托盘——用脚跟把长凳轻轻一推。那个一年级生顺顺当当地走了过去，一无所知。|向前一瞥，轻轻一推。托盘完好无损地送到了。一年级生没察觉；你察觉了，这感觉真好。|那个一年级生还没拐进过道，你就把长凳挪好了。从窗口看着的厨娘冲你举了举汤勺，像在敬礼。}\n\n@event library_crumbs\n# en: Crumbs\ntitle [36bd2d] = 饼干屑\n# en: {You're three floors up in the library with a paper bag of ginger biscuits from the village, which is against every rule painted on the stairs. Somewhere in the stacks the head librarian is doing her rounds, and her rounds make no sound at all.|Biscuits again, and the head librarian again, somewhere in the shelves. Footnote has smelt the ginger and is watching you from the next desk with enormous patience.|The bag rustles. Two stacks away, a floorboard doesn't.}\ntext [783884] = {你在图书馆四楼，揣着一纸袋山下小镇买的姜饼，这违反了楼梯上写着的每一条规矩。书架深处，总馆员正在巡视，而她巡视时一点声音都没有。|又是饼干，又是总馆员，在书架间某处。脚注闻到了姜味，正从隔壁桌极有耐心地盯着你。|纸袋沙沙一响。隔着两排书架，一块地板没有响。}\n# en: A quiet library stack three floors up, a student hunched over notes with a paper bag of biscuits, a grey cat watching, a lamp moving somewhere among the shelves.\nscene [426dd7] = 图书馆高处一排安静的书架间，一个学生伏在笔记上，手边一纸袋饼干，一只灰猫在一旁看着，书架间某处有一盏灯在移动。\n# en: Put the bag away and work hungry\nopt.left.label [d85f04] = 收起纸袋，饿着干活\n# en: {You fold the bag into your satchel and work on an empty stomach. You get a great deal done, all of it about food.|The bag goes away. Your stomach complains loudly enough to echo down the stacks.|You work hungry. When the head librarian passes, she nods at your empty desk, as if she knows exactly what's in the satchel.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0c3288] = {你把纸袋折好塞进书包，空着肚子干活。你完成了很多，满脑子都是吃的。|纸袋收起来了。你的肚子叫得那么响，声音顺着书架一路传了过去。|你饿着肚子干活。总馆员经过时，朝你空空的桌面点了点头，好像她清楚书包里装着什么。}\n# en: Eat them and risk it\nopt.right.label [9e6694] = 吃了，碰碰运气\n# en: {You eat the whole bag a crumb at a time. Nobody comes. It's the best chapter you've ever read.|You and Footnote share the bag, one for you, one for the cat. The rounds pass two stacks away and keep going.|You eat them all and brush the crumbs into your sleeve. Nobody ever knows except the cat, who is not telling.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2d4585] = {你一点一点地吃完了整袋。谁也没来。这是你读过的最好的一章。|你和脚注分着吃，你一块，猫一块。巡视的脚步在两排书架外经过，没有停。|你全吃完了，把碎屑拂进袖子里。除了猫谁也不知道，而猫不会说。}\n# en: {The head librarian is behind you, holding out her hand for the bag. She takes it without a word. You hear her eating them, later, at the desk.|A crunch, and the lamp is suddenly at the end of your row. Your name goes in a book with a black cover.|Footnote knocks the bag off the desk at the worst possible moment. The head librarian arrives as the biscuits finish rolling.}\nopt.right.say.2 [d24c7b] = {总馆员就站在你身后，伸手要那个纸袋。她一言不发地拿走了。后来你听见她在服务台那边吃。|咔嚓一声，灯光忽然就到了你这排的尽头。你的名字被记进了一本黑封皮的本子。|脚注在最要命的时刻把纸袋拍下了桌。总馆员赶到时，饼干刚刚滚停。}\n# en: See her coming\nopt.up.label [dbc48a] = 预见她过来\n# en: {You glimpse the lamp turning into your row a breath before it does, and when it arrives there is nothing on your desk but notes and a very innocent face.|A moment ahead, every time. The bag goes under the book, the book comes out of the bag, and the head librarian passes and never knows.|You see her coming, and seeing her coming, offer her a biscuit. She looks at you for a long time. Then she takes one.}\nopt.up.say.1 [b3745e] = {你提前一口气瞥见灯光拐进你这一排，等它真到了，你桌上只有笔记和一张无比无辜的脸。|每一次都提前一刻。纸袋塞到书底下，书从纸袋里抽出来，总馆员走过，什么都不知道。|你预见她要来，既然预见了，就递给她一块饼干。她盯着你看了很久。然后拿了一块。}\n\n@event gilded_newt_knucklebones\n# en: Knucklebones\ntitle [972621] = 抓羊拐\n# en: {At the fireside table the regulars are throwing knucklebones for coppers. There's a space on the bench, and a copper on the table with your name on it, if you want it.|Knucklebones again. The old carter who always wins is winning. He pats the bench beside him.|The regulars wave you over before you've shut the door. The landlady says the game's been dull without you, and charges you for it.}\ntext [eeb472] = {壁炉边那张桌上，常客们在用羊拐赌铜板。长凳上空着一个位子，桌上还有一枚写着你名字的铜板，你要的话。|又在抓羊拐。那个总是赢的老车夫正在赢。他拍拍身边的长凳。|你还没关上门，常客们就招呼你过去。老板娘说你不在的时候这游戏没劲透了，还为此收了你的钱。}\n# en: A low-beamed village tavern, regulars round a table throwing knucklebones for copper stakes by the fire.\nscene [76bbca] = 一间房梁低矮的小镇酒馆，常客们围坐在炉火边的桌旁，用羊拐赌铜板。\n# en: Play it straight\nopt.left.label [324ce2] = 老老实实地玩\n# en: {The bones fall your way three throws running. The carter says it's beginner's luck, then that it's been going on too long for that.|You win the pot, a handful of coppers and a lot of goodwill. The landlady takes a cut for the fire.|You play badly and win anyway. The table says that's the worst kind of player.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2556e6] = {羊拐连着三把都落得合你心意。车夫说这是新手运，过一会儿又说，这运气未免也太长了。|你赢下了彩池，一把铜板外加满桌的好感。老板娘抽了一成，说是柴火钱。|你玩得很烂，却还是赢了。桌上的人说，这是最气人的那种玩家。}\n# en: {The bones roll every way but yours. The carter buys you a cider out of your own losses, with great ceremony.|You lose, cheerfully and steadily. The regulars like you better for it.|Down a few coppers by the end. The carter says you've got the hands for it, just not the luck.}\nopt.left.say.2 [08d0b1] = {羊拐往哪儿滚都不往你这边滚。车夫用你输掉的钱给你买了一杯苹果酒，还郑重其事。|你输得开开心心、稳稳当当。常客们因此更喜欢你了。|到最后输了几个铜板。车夫说你手是好手，就是没那个运。}\n# en: Buy the round and just watch\nopt.right.label [6ba84e] = 请一轮酒，光看着\n# en: {You buy the round and watch. By the end you know everyone's name and the carter's whole family tree.|A round for the table, and a seat by the fire with no stakes at all. It's the best evening you've had all month.|You buy the round. The landlady writes it on your tab, to the penny, and the table drinks your health.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f7de95] = {你请了一轮，坐着看。到散场时，你已经认得每个人，还知道了车夫的整张家谱。|给全桌请一轮，在炉火边占个座，一个铜板都不押。这是你这个月过得最好的一晚。|你请了这一轮。老板娘把账一分不差地记在你名下，满桌人举杯祝你健康。}\n# en: See how the bones will fall\nopt.up.label [6c0f43] = 预见羊拐怎么落\n# en: {You glimpse each throw before it leaves the hand and bet on what you've seen. You win, and win, and the carter starts to look at you with great interest.|A moment ahead every time. You win the pot and buy the next round with it, which helps your conscience a little.|You see the bones fall before they're thrown. You take the pot, and leave before the carter works out how, which he is getting close to.}\nopt.up.say.1 [b1a186] = {每一把还没出手，你就瞥见了结果，照着看到的下注。你赢了又赢，车夫开始饶有兴味地打量你。|每次都提前一刻。你赢下彩池，又拿它请了下一轮，良心多少好受了点。|羊拐还没抛出，你就看见了它们怎么落。你拿走彩池，赶在车夫想明白之前离开——他就快想明白了。}\n\n@event workshop_cracked_lens\n# en: The Cracked Lens\ntitle [971ae9] = 裂了的镜片\n# en: {Mir is holding their best lens up to the window, and there's a crack across it like a hair. \"Demonstration's tomorrow,\" they say. \"For the faculty. It's probably fine. It's not fine.\"|Another lens, another crack. Mir has started keeping the good ones in a sock.|Mir doesn't say anything; they just hold up the lens. The crack is worse than last time.}\ntext [15f32d] = {米尔把TA最好的镜片举到窗前，上面横着一道发丝般的裂纹。“明天就要演示了，”TA说，“给教职员看。大概没事。肯定有事。”|又一片镜片，又一道裂纹。米尔开始把好镜片装在袜子里了。|米尔什么也没说，只是把镜片举起来。裂纹比上次还长。}\n# en: A cluttered workshop, an artificer holding up a large glass lens with a crack running across it, a half-built machine waiting on the bench.\nscene [13b4ac] = 杂乱的工坊，一位奇械师举着一片横贯裂纹的大玻璃镜片，工作台上一台造到一半的机器等着它。\n# en: Grind a spare by hand\nopt.left.label [d9d80b] = 手工磨一片备用的\n# en: {You grind a spare lens with Mir until midnight. It's slightly wrong in a way only Mir can see, and they say it's the best wrong lens they've ever had.|Two hours of grit and polish. The new lens works. Mir writes your name on its rim in tiny letters.|Mir grinds one side, you the other. The lens is done by ten; Mir makes tea that tastes of solder.}\nopt.left.say.1 [863fca] = {你和米尔一起磨一片备用镜片，磨到半夜。它有点不对劲，不对得只有米尔看得出来，TA说这是TA用过的最好的一片不对劲的镜片。|两个钟头的砂和抛光。新镜片能用了。米尔用极小的字把你的名字刻在镜框边上。|米尔磨一面，你磨另一面。十点就磨好了；米尔泡的茶有股焊锡味。}\n# en: Tape it and hope\nopt.right.label [ca862f] = 粘上胶带，听天由命\n# en: {You tape the crack and tell Mir it'll hold. It holds for the demonstration and splits in half at the applause, which Mir calls \"dramatic timing\".|Tape, and a promise to come back. Mir says \"probably\" four times in a row, which is a lot even for Mir.|You tape it. Mir looks at the tape for a long time, then at you, then says it's fine in a voice that means it isn't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7b1a83] = {你用胶带粘住裂缝，告诉米尔撑得住。它撑过了演示，在掌声响起时裂成两半，米尔管这叫“戏剧性的时机”。|一条胶带，一句会回来的承诺。米尔一连说了四遍“大概”，就算对米尔来说也够多了。|你粘好了。米尔盯着胶带看了很久，又看看你，然后用一种明显不是没事的语气说没事。}\n# en: Mend it, seamless\nopt.up.label [3f49a9] = 修好它，天衣无缝\n# en: {You close the crack with a breath of Mend, and the glass runs together like water. Mir holds it to the light and can't find where it was. \"Probably magic,\" they say, and hug you.|The lens mends clear as the day it was poured. Mir tests it on a moth, which is now the best-studied moth in the castle.|{spell_level(mend) >= 2: You mend the lens and the hairline crack in its brass ring. Mir makes you promise to come to the demonstration, \"in case\".|You mend it on the second try. Mir says the first try was \"very educational\", and keeps the slightly wobbly result for a different machine.}}\nopt.up.say.1 [63ef8b] = {你呼出一口愈合咒，玻璃像水一样流到一起。米尔把它对着光看，找不到裂过的地方。“大概是魔法吧。”TA说着，抱了你一下。|镜片愈合得像刚浇铸那天一样清亮。米尔拿一只飞蛾来试，如今那是城堡里被研究得最透彻的飞蛾。|{spell_level(mend) >= 2: 你修好了镜片，顺手把黄铜镜框上的细纹也修了。米尔要你保证去看演示，“以防万一”。|你第二次才修好。米尔说第一次“很有教育意义”，把那片有点歪的留下来，给另一台机器用。}}\n\n@event lakeshore_torn_net\n# en: The Torn Net\ntitle [97490e] = 破了的渔网\n# en: {An old fisher is sitting on an upturned boat, mending a net with a hole in it the size of a door. \"The old pike,\" they say, not looking up. \"Bigger every year.\"|The fisher again, the net again, a different hole. \"Same pike,\" they say.|The fisher has kept a space on the boat for you, and the net has kept a hole.}\ntext [64bcf0] = {一位老渔人坐在一条倒扣的船上补网，网上的洞有门那么大。“那条老梭子鱼，”TA头也不抬地说，“一年比一年大。”|又是那位渔人，又是那张网，换了个洞。“还是那条梭子鱼。”TA说。|渔人在船上给你留了个位子，网上也给你留了个洞。}\n# en: An old fisher sitting on an upturned boat on a still lakeshore, a long net across their knees with a ragged hole torn through it.\nscene [ee73c6] = 平静的湖岸，一位老渔人坐在倒扣的船上，膝上摊着一张长长的渔网，网上破了个参差不齐的大洞。\n# en: Help knot the net\nopt.left.label [5f4fe4] = 帮忙结网\n# en: {You knot until your fingers go white in the cold. The fisher tells you about every fish they ever lost, and none they caught.|An hour of knots, badly, then less badly. The fisher gives you a fish wrapped in a dock leaf, still flapping.|The knot comes to your fingers more easily than you expected. The fisher says you'd have made a fisher, in a tone that means it's the highest thing they can say.}\nopt.left.say.1 [884747] = {你一直结到手指冻得发白。渔人跟你讲了TA丢掉的每一条鱼，抓到的一条没提。|一个钟头的结，先是结得很糟，后来没那么糟了。渔人送你一条用酸模叶包着的鱼，还在扑腾。|那个结在你指间打得比你预想的顺手。渔人说你本来能当个渔人，那语气意味着这是TA能给的最高评价。}\n# en: Buy a fish and leave them to it\nopt.right.label [be5464] = 买条鱼，让TA自己补\n# en: {You buy the best of the morning's catch. The fisher wraps it in yesterday's Ledger and doesn't look up from the net.|A fish, a nod, a coin. You eat it grilled on the shore, and the net doesn't get any smaller.|You buy a fish. The fisher throws in a second, small and ugly, \"for luck\". It's the tastier of the two.}\nopt.right.say.1 [8fba71] = {你买下早上捕到的最好的一条。渔人用昨天的《流明纪事报》把鱼包好，眼睛没离开渔网。|一条鱼，一个点头，一枚硬币。你在岸边把它烤着吃了，那张网一点也没变小。|你买了一条鱼。渔人又白送一条，又小又丑，“图个吉利”。结果这条更好吃。}\n# en: Mend the whole net\nopt.up.label [ca04f2] = 把整张网补好\n# en: {You lay a hand on the net and the hole knits shut, knot by knot, from the edges in. The fisher stares at it, then at you, then gives you their lunch.|The net mends in one breath. The fisher says nothing for a long time, then tells you about the pike, which by the end of the story is the length of a rowing boat.|You mend the net and two old patches you hadn't noticed. The fisher says the pike will be furious, and grins with three teeth.}\nopt.up.say.1 [c7b777] = {你把手按在网上，那个洞一结一结地从边缘往里合拢。渔人盯着网，又盯着你，然后把午饭给了你。|一口气，网就补好了。渔人很久没说话，然后给你讲起那条梭子鱼，讲到最后，它已经有一条划艇那么长了。|你补好了网，还顺手补了两块你都没注意到的旧补丁。渔人说那条梭子鱼要气坏了，咧嘴一笑，露出三颗牙。}\n\n@event great_hall_cracked_sky\n# en: A Crack in Tomorrow\ntitle [a0c68c] = 明天裂了道缝\n# en: {The great hall's ceiling shows tomorrow, and tomorrow, it seems, is wet. There's a crack across the sky above the end of the long table, and tomorrow's rain is dripping through it into somebody's soup.|The crack is back, over a different table. Tomorrow is sleet.|A crack again, and a first-year holding a bowl under it with the air of someone who's been there a while.}\ntext [9a6765] = {大礼堂的天花板显示的是明天，而明天看来要下雨。长桌尽头上方的天空裂了一道缝，明天的雨正从缝里滴进某人的汤碗。|裂缝又出现了，换到了另一张桌子上方。明天是雨夹雪。|又一道裂缝，一个一年级生捧着碗接在底下，看样子已经接了好一阵子。}\n# en: A vaulted hall whose ceiling shows tomorrow's weather, a thin crack across the painted sky, rain dripping through it onto the end of a long table.\nscene [6901ed] = 拱顶大厅，天花板映着明天的天气，画一般的天空上有一道细裂缝，雨水从中滴落在一张长桌的尽头。\n# en: Fetch buckets from the kitchens\nopt.left.label [55c1a8] = 去厨房拿水桶\n# en: {You fetch two buckets and a mop. By supper there are buckets all down the table, and the hall sounds like a clock shop.|You set out buckets under the drips. They ring all through supper in different notes, and somebody starts conducting.|Buckets, and the mop, and the first-year freed from duty. They follow you about for a week.}\nopt.left.say.1 [188112] = {你拿来两只水桶和一把拖把。到晚饭时，整张长桌下摆满了水桶，礼堂里滴答声响成一片，像钟表铺。|你在滴水处摆好水桶。晚饭时它们一直在响，音高各不相同，有人开始打起了拍子指挥。|水桶、拖把，还有那个终于解放了的一年级生。TA跟了你整整一个星期。}\n# en: Move to a dry bench and eat\nopt.right.label [a3c7f6] = 换张干凳子吃饭\n# en: {You move up the table and eat with tomorrow dripping steadily behind you. Someone else will fix it. Nobody does until Thursday.|You move. The first-year with the bowl watches you go with the look of a soldier left at the gate.|A dry bench, and supper. The drip keeps time with your spoon all evening, which is somehow worse.}\nopt.right.say.1 [da1718] = {你挪到长桌另一头吃饭，身后明天一滴一滴地落个不停。会有别人来修的。一直到星期四都没人修。|你挪走了。捧碗的一年级生看着你离开，眼神像被留在城门口的哨兵。|一张干凳子，一顿晚饭。那滴水一整晚都跟着你的勺子打拍子，不知怎的更糟了。}\n# en: Mend the sky\nopt.up.label [788f2f] = 补好天空\n# en: {You reach up with Mend and the crack in tomorrow knits shut, cloud to cloud. The drip stops mid-fall. Half the hall claps; the other half didn't notice, which is the better compliment.|The crack draws together from both ends and meets in the middle with a small click. Tomorrow's rain will have to wait for tomorrow, as it should.|You mend the sky over the long table. The first-year with the bowl pours it, solemnly, into a plant pot.}\nopt.up.say.1 [5d9692] = {你举手施展愈合咒，明天的裂缝一朵云接一朵云地合上了。那滴水停在半空。礼堂里一半人鼓掌；另一半人根本没注意到，这才是更高的夸奖。|裂缝从两头往中间收拢，合上时轻轻咔哒一声。明天的雨只好等到明天再下，这才对。|你把长桌上方的天空补好了。捧碗的一年级生郑重其事地把碗里的水倒进了一个花盆。}\n\n@event boathouse_good_boat\n# en: The Good Boat\ntitle [dc7c09] = 那条好船\n# en: {The boathouse has six boats that leak and one, the blue one, that doesn't. The boatman keeps it for himself and sits on it to make the point.|The blue boat, the boatman, the folded arms. You've started to think of them as one animal.|The boatman sees you coming and settles more firmly onto the blue boat. \"No,\" he says, before you've asked.}\ntext [4320a2] = {船屋里有六条漏水的船，还有一条不漏的，那条蓝色的。船夫把它留给自己，还坐在上面，以示立场。|蓝船、船夫、抱着的胳膊。你已经开始把它们看作同一只动物了。|船夫看见你过来，在蓝船上坐得更稳了。“不行。”你还没开口，他就说了。}\n# en: A boathouse jetty, a row of patched rowing boats and one sound blue one, a weathered boatman sitting on it with his arms folded.\nscene [44416f] = 船屋的栈桥，一排打满补丁的划艇和一条完好的蓝船，一个饱经风霜的船夫抱着胳膊坐在蓝船上。\n# en: Pay his price for an hour\nopt.left.label [279614] = 照他的价租一个钟头\n# en: {He takes your crowns, counts them twice and lets you have the blue boat for exactly an hour. It's the stillest hour you've spent all term.|An hour on the lake in a boat that doesn't sink. You row out to the middle and lie back and watch the towers upside down.|You pay. He watches you the whole hour, from the jetty, as if you'd borrowed his dog.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d5635f] = {他收下你的克朗，数了两遍，把蓝船借给你，不多不少一个钟头。这是你这学期度过的最安静的一个钟头。|在一条不会沉的船上待一个钟头。你划到湖心，向后一躺，看倒映的塔楼。|你付了钱。他从栈桥上盯了你整整一个钟头，好像你借走的是他的狗。}\n# en: Take the green one, bailing\nopt.right.label [5f83ca] = 划那条绿的，边划边舀水\n# en: {You take the green boat and a bailer. You row half the time and bail the other half. It's still the lake, and still lovely.|The green boat, which takes on water like a sponge. You come back with wet boots and a better opinion of yourself.|You bail all the way out and all the way back. The boatman says you've got a rower's arms now, and charges you nothing for the joke.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d1fb9d] = {你划了那条绿船，带着一只舀水勺。一半时间划船，一半时间舀水。湖还是那个湖，还是那么美。|那条绿船，吃水像块海绵。你回来时靴子湿透，对自己的评价却高了一截。|你一路舀着水划出去，又一路舀着水划回来。船夫说你现在有了一双划船手，这个笑话不收你钱。}\n# en: Charm him off the blue boat\nopt.up.label [bbf0d2] = 用魅影咒哄他让出蓝船\n# en: {You turn on a little more charm than you have, and he finds himself telling you about his late wife, and then lending you the blue boat, and then wondering why.|A smile with Glamour in it. The boatman gets up, holds the boat steady for you, and calls you \"young sir or madam\" like a courtier.|He remembers liking you and not quite why. You get the blue boat for the rest of the day and a cup of his terrible tea after.}\nopt.up.say.1 [8b4fbe] = {你多施展了一点你本没有的魅力，他不知不觉跟你讲起了他过世的妻子，接着把蓝船借给了你，然后才纳闷自己为什么这么做。|带着魅影咒的一个微笑。船夫站起身，替你扶稳船，像个侍臣一样称你“年轻的先生或女士”。|他记得自己喜欢你，却不太记得为什么。那天余下的时间蓝船都归你，回来后还有一杯他泡的难喝的茶。}\n\n@event courtyard_prefects_notebook\n# en: The Prefect's Notebook\ntitle [5f43aa] = 级长的本子\n# en: {You have, it turns out, been walking on the lawn, which is for looking at. Bram Hollis has his notebook open and his pencil licked. \"Rule nine,\" he says. \"Demerit.\"|Bram again, and the notebook again, and the lawn again. \"Rule nine,\" he says, with real joy.|Bram's pencil is already moving when you look up. He has a page with your name at the top now.}\ntext [464bab] = {原来你一直踩在草坪上，而那片草坪是用来看的。布拉姆·霍利斯翻开本子，舔了舔铅笔。“第九条，”他说，“记过。”|又是布拉姆，又是本子，又是草坪。“第九条，”他说，满心欢喜。|你一抬头，布拉姆的铅笔已经在动了。他现在有一页专门以你的名字打头。}\n# en: A sunny castle courtyard, a red-sashed prefect with a notebook open and pencil poised, a student standing on a forbidden patch of lawn.\nscene [59f37c] = 阳光明媚的城堡庭院，一位佩着红色绶带的级长翻开本子、举着铅笔，一个学生站在一块禁止踩踏的草坪上。\n# en: Argue the rule\nopt.left.label [ef485c] = 跟他争这条规矩\n# en: {You point out that rule nine was repealed in a year when the lawn caught fire. Bram checks. It was. He closes the notebook with great dignity.|You argue that you were looking at the lawn, very closely. Bram can't find the rule against that. He'll have one by Thursday.|You quote rule nine back at him, with the bit he left out. Bram goes pink and lets you off \"this once, as a courtesy\".}\nopt.left.say.1 [2e228e] = {你指出，第九条在草坪着火的那一年就废除了。布拉姆查了查。确实如此。他极有尊严地合上了本子。|你争辩说你是在看草坪，看得很仔细。布拉姆找不到禁止这样做的条款。到星期四他就会有一条了。|你把第九条连同他漏掉的那半句一起背给他听。布拉姆脸红了，放你一马，“仅此一次，算是给你面子”。}\n# en: {Bram reads rule nine out loud to the whole courtyard, twice, and writes your name in capitals.|You argue; Bram quotes rule ten, which is about arguing. Two demerits.|Bram listens to your whole case, then writes it down as a separate offence.}\nopt.left.say.2 [efbe56] = {布拉姆当着整个庭院把第九条大声念了两遍，然后用大写字母写下你的名字。|你争辩；布拉姆引用第十条，那条是关于争辩的。记过两次。|布拉姆听完了你的全部陈词，然后把它记成了另一项过失。}\n# en: Take the demerit and move on\nopt.right.label [008a36] = 认了记过，走人\n# en: {You take it. Bram writes slowly, so everyone can see. \"Next time,\" he says, and means it kindly, in his way.|Demerit. You walk off the lawn with your hands in your pockets. A first-year on a bench gives you a sympathetic look.|You take the demerit with a bow. Bram doesn't know what to do with a bow, and writes that down too.}\nopt.right.say.1 [7697f1] = {你认了。布拉姆写得很慢，好让大家都看见。“下次注意。”他说，以他的方式，带着善意。|记过一次。你双手插兜走下草坪。长凳上一个一年级生向你投来同情的目光。|你鞠了一躬，认下了记过。布拉姆不知道该拿一个鞠躬怎么办，于是把这个也记了下来。}\n# en: Remind him he likes you\nopt.up.label [9d7ca6] = 提醒他，他喜欢你\n# en: {You say his name the way people say a friend's, with a little Glamour in it. Bram's pencil stops. He forgets, quite visibly, what he was writing.|A smile with a spell behind it. Bram closes the notebook, tells you to \"watch the edges\", and walks off frowning at himself.|Bram remembers liking you and not why. He lets you off, then follows you to the cloister to explain rule nine properly, which is its own punishment.}\nopt.up.say.1 [05c57d] = {你像叫朋友那样叫他的名字，里面掺了一点魅影咒。布拉姆的铅笔停住了。他明显忘了自己在写什么。|一个背后藏着咒语的微笑。布拉姆合上本子，叮嘱你“注意边上”，然后皱着眉对自己生着闷气走开了。|布拉姆记得自己喜欢你，却不记得为什么。他放过了你，然后跟着你走到回廊，把第九条从头到尾给你讲清楚，这本身就是一种惩罚。}\n\n@event grimsbys_book_behind_counter\n# en: The Book Behind the Counter\ntitle [70cf88] = 柜台后的书\n# en: {Behind Mr Grimsby's counter there's a book wrapped in brown paper, kept apart. \"For someone who'll need it,\" he says vaguely. \"Not you. Or not yet.\"|The wrapped book is still there. Mr Grimsby moves it a little further from you when you come in.|The brown-paper book has a name pencilled on it now, but he keeps his thumb over the name.}\ntext [65738b] = {格林斯比先生的柜台后面放着一本牛皮纸包着的书，单独搁着。“给一个会需要它的人，”他含糊地说，“不是你。或者说，还不是。”|那本包着的书还在。你一进门，格林斯比先生就把它往离你远一点的地方挪了挪。|牛皮纸包着的书上现在用铅笔写了个名字，可他总用拇指压着那个名字。}\n# en: A cramped bookshop, an elderly bookseller behind his counter with one wrapped book kept apart from the rest, a student leaning in hopefully.\nscene [afdcfb] = 一间挤得满满的书店，一位年迈的书商站在柜台后，身边单独放着一本包好的书，一个学生满怀希望地探过身去。\n# en: Wait to be the someone\nopt.left.label [92aa81] = 等着成为那个人\n# en: {You browse for an hour in plain sight, being the sort of person who'd need a book. Mr Grimsby sells you a history of lighthouses instead.|You come back twice in the week. On the second visit he lets you hold it, then takes it back. \"Soon,\" he says.|You don't ask. Mr Grimsby notices you not asking, and approves, and doesn't give you the book.}\nopt.left.say.1 [7a36e6] = {你在他眼皮底下逛了一个钟头，努力做一个会需要一本书的人。格林斯比先生最后卖给你一本灯塔史。|这个星期你又来了两次。第二次他让你拿了拿那本书，又收了回去。“快了。”他说。|你没开口问。格林斯比先生注意到你没问，颇为赞许，然后还是没把书给你。}\n# en: Buy what he recommends instead\nopt.right.label [661be7] = 买他推荐的那本\n# en: {He hands you something about tides that smells of seaweed. Chapter two is exactly what your essay needed, as usual, annoyingly.|You buy his recommendation without arguing. He looks almost disappointed you didn't fight for the other one.|A slim book on candle-making, for reasons he won't explain. You'll need it on Thursday.}\nopt.right.say.1 [565866] = {他递给你一本讲潮汐的书，闻着有海藻味。第二章恰好是你的论文需要的，一如既往，叫人恼火。|你二话不说买下他推荐的书。他看上去几乎有点失望，你没为另一本争一争。|一本讲做蜡烛的薄书，原因他不肯说。星期四你就会用上它。}\n# en: Charm the parcel out of him\nopt.up.label [846107] = 用魅影咒把书哄到手\n# en: {You smile at him with a little more charm than you own, and he finds himself handing you the parcel. \"Ah,\" he says, puzzled. \"It was you all along. Was it?\"|Glamour, and a compliment about his shelving. He unwraps the book for you himself. It's the one you came in for last month and couldn't find.|He beams, decides you've been a customer for years, and wraps the parcel again for you, more nicely, with string.}\nopt.up.say.1 [e788b1] = {你朝他笑了笑，那份魅力比你本有的多一点，他不知不觉就把包裹递给了你。“啊，”他困惑地说，“原来一直是你。是吗？”|一点魅影咒，再夸一句他的书架。他亲手为你拆开了包装。正是你上个月来找却没找到的那本。|他笑逐颜开，认定你是多年的老主顾，又替你把包裹重新包了一遍，包得更漂亮，还系上了绳子。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_far_bell\n# en: The Far Bell\ntitle [32b356] = 远处的钟\n# en: {The bell for your lecture is ringing in the other tower, and you are on the wrong side of the bridge with all your books.|The bell again, and you on the wrong end of the bridge again. You're starting to suspect the bridge.|You can hear the bell before you see the bridge. Seven strokes; it's on the third.}\ntext [09eaf4] = {你那堂课的钟声在另一座塔里敲响了，而你抱着所有的书，站在桥的这一头。|又是钟声，你又站在桥的另一头。你开始怀疑这座桥了。|还没看见桥，你就听见了钟声。一共七下，现在是第三下。}\n# en: A bridge of light between two towers, a student running across it with an armful of books while a bell rings on the far side.\nscene [bac538] = 两座塔之间的一道光桥，一个学生抱着一摞书在桥上奔跑，桥那头的钟正在敲响。\n# en: Run for it\nopt.left.label [38d292] = 拼命跑过去\n# en: {You slide into your seat on the last stroke with your books in a heap. The lecturer looks at the clock, then at you, then lets it go.|You make it, red-faced and triumphant. Your neighbour pushes a quill at you without a word.|You know the run now: left foot on the second plank, duck the lamp. In with a stroke to spare.}\nopt.left.say.1 [092118] = {最后一声钟响时，你滑进座位，书堆成一团。讲师看看钟，又看看你，没跟你计较。|你赶上了，满脸通红，得意洋洋。邻座一言不发地把一支羽毛笔推了过来。|这条路你跑熟了：左脚踩第二块板，低头躲过灯。还剩一声钟响，你就进了门。}\n# en: {You drop your books halfway across the bridge and arrive five minutes late with half of them.|You arrive as the door closes, and spend the hour listening through it. It's not the same.|You trip on the top step and the whole lecture turns to watch you pick up your inkwell.}\nopt.left.say.2 [c512e6] = {你在桥中间把书掉了一地，迟到五分钟，只抱着一半的书赶到。|你赶到时门正好关上，于是你隔着门听了一个钟头。这不一样。|你在最上面一级台阶绊了一跤，整堂课的人都转过头来看你捡墨水瓶。}\n# en: Arrive late, quietly\nopt.right.label [8cc63d] = 安安静静地迟到\n# en: {You walk, unhurried, and slip in at the back ten minutes late, breathing easy. You miss the one thing that'll be on the exam.|You arrive late and sit on the end of the back row, where the notes from the front arrive late too.|Late, and calm, and behind for the rest of the hour. Someone lends you their notes afterwards, in a hand you can't read.}\nopt.right.say.1 [83f7ce] = {你不慌不忙地走过去，晚了十分钟从后门溜进去，呼吸平稳。你错过了考试唯一会考的那一点。|你迟到了，坐在最后一排的边上，前排传下来的笔记也传得最晚。|迟到了，心平气和，这一个钟头都跟不上。下课后有人把笔记借给你，字迹你一个也认不出。}\n# en: Borrow a hare's legs\nopt.up.label [19664e] = 借一双野兔的腿\n# en: {You cross the bridge in four strides with the wind whistling, and sit down before the bell has finished. Your books arrive a moment later, in your arms.|Haste, and the bridge goes by in a blur of light. You're in your seat, notes open, looking as if you've been there an hour.|{spell_level(haste) >= 2: You're early. You've time to sharpen a quill and help a first-year find the room.|You arrive breathless but on the stroke. The lecturer says \"Prompt,\" which from them is warm.}}\nopt.up.say.1 [66762c] = {你四步跨过了桥，耳边风声呼啸，钟声还没敲完你就坐下了。你的书过了一会儿才到——在你怀里。|疾速咒一施，光桥化作一道模糊的光影掠过。你已经坐在座位上，笔记摊开，好像来了一个钟头似的。|{spell_level(haste) >= 2: 你来早了。还有时间削一支羽毛笔，再帮一个一年级生找到教室。|你赶在钟声上到了，气喘吁吁。讲师说了句“准时”，这对TA来说算是热情了。}}\n\n@event courtyard_loose_pages\n# en: Loose Pages\ntitle [115f61] = 散落的纸页\n# en: {A gust comes over the wall and takes a first-year's essay out of their hands, every page of it, and scatters it across the courtyard towards the fountain. They stand there holding the empty folder.|Wind, a folder, a first-year, and pages everywhere. The fountain looks hungry.|The pages are already in the air when you come through the arch. You know exactly where the fountain is.}\ntext [3751e2] = {一阵风翻过墙头，把一个一年级生手里的论文卷走了，一页不剩，朝着喷泉撒了一院子。TA站在那儿，手里只剩一个空文件夹。|风、文件夹、一年级生，满地纸页。喷泉看起来很饿。|你穿过拱门时，纸页已经飞在空中了。你很清楚喷泉在哪儿。}\n# en: A castle courtyard on a windy day, a first-year clutching an empty folder while the pages of an essay blow across the flagstones toward a fountain.\nscene [1d334d] = 刮风天的城堡庭院，一个一年级生紧抓着空文件夹，论文的纸页正被风卷过石板地，飞向一座喷泉。\n# en: Chase them down\nopt.left.label [e61193] = 追回来\n# en: {You run them down one by one, off the flagstones, out of a hedge, one off a prefect's back. Every page, and only one of them damp.|You stamp on three, catch two and dive for the last at the fountain's rim. The first-year hugs the folder like a lost dog.|You read the wind and get to the fountain before the pages do, and catch them as they come.}\nopt.left.say.1 [85b0a3] = {你一张一张地追：从石板上、从树篱里，还有一张从一位级长背上揭下来。一页不少，只有一页湿了。|你踩住三张，接住两张，在喷泉边上扑住了最后一张。那个一年级生抱着文件夹，像抱着失而复得的小狗。|你看准了风向，赶在纸页之前到了喷泉边，一张张接住。}\n# en: {You get most of them. The last three go into the fountain, and you go in after the last one, and it's a long cold walk to the dormitory.|You trip on the fountain step and the pages sail on without you. The first-year helps you up, which isn't how it was meant to go.|The wind wins. You bring back half an essay and a leaf.}\nopt.left.say.2 [f58186] = {大部分都追回来了。最后三张掉进了喷泉，你追着最后一张也进去了，回宿舍的路又长又冷。|你在喷泉台阶上绊了一跤，纸页撇下你继续飞。那个一年级生把你扶了起来，这跟原本的剧本可不一样。|风赢了。你只带回来半篇论文和一片树叶。}\n# en: Lend them your own notes to start again\nopt.right.label [d60985] = 借出笔记，让TA重写\n# en: {You sit with them on the cloister step while they work it up again from your notes. It comes out shorter and, honestly, better.|You lend them your notes and your afternoon. It takes all of it, and they tell their whole year you did.|You lend them your quill and your evening, and they hand it in on time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2c4043] = {你陪TA坐在回廊台阶上，看TA照着你的笔记重新写。写出来的短了些，而且说实话，更好了。|你借出了笔记，也搭上了一下午。整个下午都搭进去了，TA跟全年级的人都说了是你帮的忙。|你借出羽毛笔，搭上一晚上，TA按时交上了论文。}\n# en: Gather every page before it lands\nopt.up.label [a7a43a] = 赶在落地前收齐每一页\n# en: {Haste, and the courtyard slows around you. You pluck every page out of the air before the gust has finished, and hand back the folder neatly stacked.|You go round the courtyard like a whirl of wind yourself, and come back with the essay in order. The first-year counts the pages twice, then stares at you.|{spell_level(haste) >= 2: You catch the pages and the first-year's hat, which they hadn't noticed had gone.|You get every page but one, and that one you snatch off the fountain's surface, barely wet.}}\nopt.up.say.1 [14a5d0] = {疾速咒一施，庭院在你周围慢了下来。风还没停，你就从空中摘下了每一页，把文件夹整整齐齐地递了回去。|你自己也像一阵旋风似的绕着庭院转了一圈，回来时论文已经按顺序排好。那个一年级生数了两遍，然后呆呆地看着你。|{spell_level(haste) >= 2: 你接住了纸页，还接住了那个一年级生的帽子——TA都没发现帽子飞了。|你接住了除一页以外的所有纸页，那一页你是从喷泉水面上抢回来的，只湿了一点点。}}\n\n@event refectory_supper_hatch\n# en: The Supper Hatch\ntitle [32f841] = 晚饭窗口\n# en: {The kitchen hatch shuts at the bell, and the bell is going, and you are at the wrong end of the longest hall in the castle.|The hatch is coming down again. The cook leaves it open an inch less for you every week, on principle.|You hear the hatch rattle before you're through the door. Stew night, too.}\ntext [cbc127] = {厨房的窗口钟一响就关，钟已经在响了，而你在城堡里最长的大厅的另一头。|窗口又要落下来了。厨娘每周给你留的缝都少一寸，原则问题。|你还没进门就听见窗口哗啦作响。偏偏还是炖菜之夜。}\n# en: A long dining hall at evening, the kitchen hatch at the far end with its shutter starting to come down, a student at the wrong end of the hall.\nscene [1a5617] = 傍晚的长条膳堂，尽头厨房窗口的挡板正开始落下，一个学生站在大厅的另一头。\n# en: Run the length of the hall\nopt.left.label [481e9e] = 跑过整个大厅\n# en: {You get a bowl under the hatch as it comes down. The cook gives you the last of the stew and a look of grudging respect.|You make it with a slide that would win prizes. The hatch stops for you. The cook looks almost impressed.|In under the shutter by a hand's breadth. The cook says, \"You again,\" and adds a dumpling, as a prize.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e8b740] = {你在窗口落下时把碗伸了进去。厨娘给了你最后一勺炖菜，外加一个勉强服气的眼神。|你一个滑步冲过去，这一下能拿奖。窗口为你停住了。厨娘看起来几乎有点佩服。|你从挡板底下以一掌之隔钻了进去。厨娘说：“又是你。”然后加了一个面团子，算是奖品。}\n# en: {The hatch closes in your face, gently, with finality. Bread and cheese it is.|You slip on the flagstones halfway and arrive on your back. The cook leans over the hatch to check you're alive, and closes it.|Too slow. You hear the stew being scraped into a bucket for the pigs, which is a sound that stays with you.}\nopt.left.say.2 [d9ae89] = {窗口当着你的面关上了，轻轻地，不容商量。那就面包和奶酪吧。|你跑到一半在石板上滑倒，仰面朝天到了窗口。厨娘探出身确认你还活着，然后关上了窗口。|太慢了。你听见炖菜被刮进喂猪的桶里，那声音你会记很久。}\n# en: Take bread and cheese from the board\nopt.right.label [5d4d5d] = 从板子上拿面包和奶酪\n# en: {You take bread and cheese from the board by the door and eat them on the steps. It's fine. It isn't stew.|Bread, cheese and an apple, eaten walking. You catch the smell of the stew all the way upstairs.|You take the board's bread and somebody's abandoned pudding. You're not proud. You're full.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9c3a59] = {你从门边的板子上拿了面包和奶酪，坐在台阶上吃。还行。不是炖菜。|面包、奶酪和一个苹果，边走边吃。一路上楼你都闻得到炖菜的香味。|你拿了板子上的面包，还有不知谁剩下的布丁。你不骄傲。你吃饱了。}\n# en: Cross the hall in a heartbeat\nopt.up.label [14d195] = 一个心跳间穿过大厅\n# en: {You're at the hatch before the bell's first echo. The cook stares at the door, then at you, then ladles. \"Well,\" she says. \"Somebody's hungry.\"|Haste down the long aisle, between the benches, round a prefect, and your bowl is under the hatch with time to spare.|You arrive so fast you have to wait for the hatch to finish opening. The cook gives you seconds out of sheer surprise.}\nopt.up.say.1 [fec03d] = {钟声的第一道回音还没落，你已经站在窗口前了。厨娘看看门，看看你，然后舀了一勺。“好家伙，”她说，“有人饿坏了。”|疾速咒一施，你沿着长长的过道，穿过长凳，绕过一位级长，碗已经伸进窗口，时间绰绰有余。|你到得太快，只好等窗口完全打开。厨娘惊得给你多盛了一份。}\n\n@event lecture_tower_volunteer\n# en: A Volunteer\ntitle [9dfa7e] = 一名志愿者\n# en: {\"I need a volunteer,\" says the lecturer, holding up a charm that crackles, \"for a hex that is only mildly unpleasant.\" Their eyes are travelling along the back row towards you.|The volunteer again. The back row has learned to look at its shoes; the lecturer has learned to look at the back row.|\"Volunteer,\" says the lecturer, and you can feel the word coming for you like weather.}\ntext [51aede] = {“我需要一名志愿者，”讲师举着一道噼啪作响的咒符说，“来试一个只是有点不舒服的恶咒。”TA的目光正沿着最后一排朝你移过来。|又要志愿者。最后一排已经学会了盯着自己的鞋；讲师也学会了盯着最后一排。|“志愿者。”讲师说，你能感觉到这个词像天气一样朝你压过来。}\n# en: A tiered lecture hall, a lecturer at the front with a crackling demonstration charm, scanning the back row for a volunteer.\nscene [f7ebbc] = 阶梯讲堂，讲师在前面举着一道噼啪作响的示范咒符，扫视最后一排寻找志愿者。\n# en: Put your hand up\nopt.left.label [fef1af] = 举手\n# en: {You volunteer. The hex is exactly as mildly unpleasant as promised, and your hair stays upright until supper. You learn more than anyone else in the room.|You go down and take it on the chin, literally. The class applauds; your chin doesn't.|You volunteer before they ask. The lecturer, delighted, gives you the slightly worse hex, \"as a treat\".}\nopt.left.say.1 [e18cc1] = {你自告奋勇。那个恶咒果然只是有点不舒服，你的头发一直竖到晚饭。你比教室里任何人学到的都多。|你走下去，硬生生地扛了下来——字面意义上，用下巴扛的。全班鼓掌；你的下巴没鼓。|讲师还没开口你就举了手。讲师很高兴，给了你一个稍微更难受的恶咒，“当作奖励”。}\n# en: Sink into your seat\nopt.right.label [89c92c] = 缩进座位里\n# en: {You sink. The lecturer picks the student beside you, who gives you a look you'll be paying for until the end of term.|You become very interested in your notes. It works; the person in front is less lucky and less interested in theirs.|You sink until you're more desk than student. The lecturer says, \"I can see you,\" and picks someone else anyway.}\nopt.right.say.1 [67d79b] = {你缩了下去。讲师点了你旁边的同学，那人看你的眼神，你得一直还到学期末。|你忽然对自己的笔记产生了浓厚的兴趣。这招管用；前排那个人运气差一点，对笔记的兴趣也少一点。|你缩得几乎跟桌子融为一体。讲师说：“我看得见你。”然后还是点了别人。}\n# en: Step into the pillar's shadow\nopt.up.label [bb49f2] = 踏进柱子的阴影\n# en: {You step into the shadow of the pillar and out of the shadow by the door, and spend the demonstration watching from the corridor, very innocently.|A step into the dark under your desk and out under the window. When the lecturer's eyes reach your seat, your seat is empty, and they look puzzled for the rest of the hour.|You're gone before the word \"volunteer\" lands. From the doorway you watch someone else's hair stand on end, and feel only slightly guilty.}\nopt.up.say.1 [bf3e27] = {你踏进柱子的阴影，又从门边的阴影里踏出来，整个示范你都在走廊里观看，一脸无辜。|一步踏进桌下的暗处，再从窗下踏出。讲师的目光扫到你的座位时，座位是空的，TA困惑了整整一个钟头。|“志愿者”这个词还没落地，你就不见了。你从门口看着别人的头发竖起来，只觉得有那么一点点愧疚。}\n\n@event observatory_shutter_rope\n# en: The Shutter Rope\ntitle [8ec21b] = 天窗的绳子\n# en: {The stars are coming out, and the dome is still shut. The crank rope has jumped its pulley and is hanging up in the dark of the ribs, well out of anyone's reach. A dozen students with star charts look up at it.|The shutter rope has jumped its pulley again. The caretaker says it does it on clear nights only, out of spite.|Up in the ribs of the dome, in the dark, the rope again. Somebody has chalked an arrow on the floor pointing at it.}\ntext [86a967] = {星星出来了，圆顶却还关着。摇柄的绳子跳出了滑轮，挂在穹肋间的暗处，谁也够不着。十几个拿着星图的学生仰头望着它。|天窗的绳子又跳出了滑轮。看守说它只在晴朗的夜里这么干，纯属故意。|圆顶的穹肋间，暗处，又是那根绳子。有人在地板上用粉笔画了个箭头指着它。}\n# en: A brass observatory dome at dusk, its shutter still closed, a crank rope dangling out of reach up in the dark of the dome's ribs, students with star charts waiting below.\nscene [c70f94] = 黄昏时的黄铜观星圆顶，天窗仍然紧闭，一根摇柄绳挂在穹肋间的暗处够不着，拿着星图的学生在下面等着。\n# en: Climb the brass ribs\nopt.left.label [004882] = 爬上黄铜穹肋\n# en: {You climb the ribs like a ladder and hook the rope with one foot. The shutter grinds open on a sky full of stars, and the room cheers.|Up the ribs, out along the curve, a grab. The rope comes free and the dome opens with a groan like a waking giant.|The handholds are just where you'd hope. Up, grab, down, and the sky is open before the charts are unrolled.}\nopt.left.say.1 [20949c] = {你像爬梯子一样爬上穹肋，用一只脚勾住了绳子。天窗嘎吱嘎吱地打开，满天星斗，屋里一片欢呼。|爬上穹肋，沿着弧顶往外，一把抓住。绳子松了下来，圆顶像巨人醒来似的呻吟着打开了。|抓手正好都在你希望的地方。上去，一抓，下来，星图还没展开，天空已经敞开了。}\n# en: {Halfway up a rib turns slippery with dew and you come down a lot faster than you went up. The rope stays where it is.|You get a hand to the rope and the pulley bites your fingers. You let go of both.|You climb, reach, miss and hang there by your elbows while someone runs for the caretaker.}\nopt.left.say.2 [583080] = {爬到一半，一根穹肋沾了露水变得滑溜，你下来的速度比上去快得多。绳子纹丝未动。|你一只手碰到了绳子，滑轮夹了你的手指。你两样都松开了。|你爬上去，伸手，没够着，用胳膊肘挂在那儿，有人跑去叫看守了。}\n# en: Read the charts by lamplight instead\nopt.right.label [d68028] = 借着灯光看星图\n# en: {The caretaker comes up an hour later with a hook on a pole. By then you've learned the charts by heart, and the best of the sky has gone behind the hill.|You spread the charts on the floor and plot tonight's route across them instead. It isn't the same; it's not nothing.|You read by lamplight under the shut dome. When it finally opens, the moon is up and drowning half of what you came for.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e88039] = {一个钟头后，看守拿着一根带钩的长杆上来了。那时你已经把星图背得滚瓜烂熟，最好的那片天空也落到山后去了。|你把星图摊在地板上，在上面规划今晚的观测路线。这不一样；但也不是什么都没有。|你在关着的圆顶下借着灯光读图。等它终于打开，月亮已经升起，你想看的有一半都被月光淹没了。}\n# en: Step into the dark under the dome\nopt.up.label [f0f458] = 踏进圆顶下的黑暗\n# en: {You step into the shadow by the door and out of the shadow in the ribs, grab the rope, and step back down. The dome opens. A dozen star charts lower slowly as their owners stare at you.|Shadow to shadow, up into the dark of the dome and back. The rope slots onto its pulley with a clunk. Someone asks if you can do that for the bell tower.|You don't even put your charts down. A step into the dark, a tug, and the shutter rolls back on the whole sky.}\nopt.up.say.1 [6430d2] = {你踏进门边的阴影，从穹肋间的阴影里踏出，抓住绳子，再一步踏回地面。圆顶打开了。十几张星图慢慢放了下来，它们的主人都盯着你。|影子到影子，上到圆顶的黑暗里又回来。绳子咔哒一声落回滑轮。有人问你能不能也去钟楼帮个忙。|你连星图都没放下。一步踏进黑暗，一拉，天窗向后卷开，露出整片天空。}\n\n@event infirmary_slip_out\n# en: One More Night\ntitle [47564e] = 再住一晚\n# en: {Sister Amaranth wants you in for \"one more night, for observation\". You feel completely fine and have three things due tomorrow.|One more night again. Sister Amaranth has started leaving your boots on the far side of the ward.|The bed by the window has your name on the slate in ink. \"One more night,\" says Sister Amaranth, not as a question.}\ntext [a9f95a] = {阿玛兰斯修女要你“再住一晚，观察观察”。你觉得自己好得很，明天还有三样东西要交。|又是再住一晚。阿玛兰斯修女开始把你的靴子放到病房另一头去了。|靠窗那张床的石板上用墨水写着你的名字。“再住一晚。”阿玛兰斯修女说，不是在商量。}\n# en: An infirmary ward at night, a student sitting up in a white bed fully dressed, a healer's lamp moving between beds at the far end.\nscene [c74b0b] = 夜晚的医务室病房，一个学生穿戴整齐地坐在白色病床上，远处一盏医师的灯在病床间移动。\n# en: Stay the night\nopt.left.label [b1754d] = 住下\n# en: {You stay. You sleep through two bells and the breakfast trolley and wake up much better than \"completely fine\" turned out to be. Your three things are now late.|You stay. She was right, irritatingly; you sleep like a dropped stone. Your essay, unwritten, waits.|You stay without arguing. Sister Amaranth notices, and gives you the good tonic, and a look that might be respect.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e2cb3c] = {你住下了。你一觉睡过了两次钟响和早饭推车，醒来后发现，比起所谓的“好得很”，你现在好多了。那三样东西现在都迟交了。|你住下了。她是对的，真让人恼火；你睡得像块掉进井里的石头。你的论文还没写，就那么等着。|你没争辩就住下了。阿玛兰斯修女注意到了，给了你那瓶好药水，还有一个可能是尊重的眼神。}\n# en: Argue your case\nopt.right.label [c2ad84] = 为自己争取\n# en: {You make a short, true, convincing case. She looks at you over her spectacles. \"Go,\" she says. \"Come back if it swells. Don't come back if it doesn't.\"|You tell her the true version of how fine you are, with the limp left in. She respects that and lets you go.|She lets you go with a tonic in your pocket and instructions you'll follow later, when you understand them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f4e4d3] = {你的陈述简短、真实、令人信服。她从眼镜上方看着你。“走吧，”她说，“肿了就回来。没肿就别回来。”|你把自己有多好的真实版本告诉了她，连那点跛都没瞒。她认可这一点，放你走了。|她放你走了，往你兜里塞了一瓶药水，还有几句叮嘱，你要过些日子才会明白、才会照做。}\n# en: {She listens to every word, then points at the bed. You lie down. You don't remember falling asleep.|\"No,\" says Sister Amaranth, which was always going to be the answer, and she says it while doing something else.|She lets you argue to the end, then writes \"argued\" on your slate, under your name.}\nopt.right.say.2 [c4b33e] = {她听完了每一个字，然后指了指床。你躺下了。你不记得自己是怎么睡着的。|“不行。”阿玛兰斯修女说，答案本来就只会是这个，她说的时候手上还在忙别的。|她让你一直争到底，然后在你的石板上、名字下面写了“争辩过”。}\n# en: Step out through the dark\nopt.up.label [939587] = 从暗处溜出去\n# en: {When her lamp turns, you step into the shadow behind the screen and out of the shadow by the door. In the morning there's a note on your desk in square handwriting. It says \"Next time, ask.\"|You step out of the ward and into your own room's corner in one breath. You get your three things done. Sister Amaranth, of course, knows.|Shadow to shadow, past the beds, out. Your essay is finished by midnight. At breakfast Sister Amaranth puts a tonic by your plate without a word.}\nopt.up.say.1 [b69da4] = {她的灯一转开，你就踏进屏风后的阴影，从门边的阴影里踏出来。第二天早上，你桌上有一张字迹方正的纸条。上面写着：“下次，先问。”|一口气，你从病房踏进了自己房间的角落。三样东西都完成了。阿玛兰斯修女当然知道。|影子到影子，穿过病床，出去了。半夜前你写完了论文。早饭时，阿玛兰斯修女一言不发地在你盘子旁放了一瓶药水。}\n\n@event alchemy_lab_do_not_drop\n# en: Do Not Drop\ntitle [d4aebe] = 切勿掉落\n# en: {A first-year at the top of the shelf ladder is reaching for a bottle labelled, in large letters, DO NOT DROP. It's sliding off their fingertips, over a bench crowded with people.|The same shelf, a different first-year, the same bottle. Someone has added AGAIN to the label.|The bottle is tilting before you've put your bag down. You'd swear it waits for you.}\ntext [d27a2b] = {一个一年级生站在架子梯的最高处，去够一个标签上用大字写着“切勿掉落”的瓶子。瓶子正从TA指尖滑出去，下面是挤满了人的工作台。|同一个架子，换了个一年级生，同一个瓶子。有人在标签上加了“又来”两个字。|你还没放下书包，瓶子就已经歪了。你敢发誓它是在等你。}\n# en: A laboratory with tall shelves, a first-year on a ladder reaching for a bottle labelled DO NOT DROP, the bottle tilting off their fingertips over a crowded bench.\nscene [f5abbb] = 一间立着高架子的实验室，一个一年级生站在梯子上去够一个标着“切勿掉落”的瓶子，瓶子正从指尖滑落，下面是拥挤的工作台。\n# en: Dive and catch it\nopt.left.label [af6772] = 扑过去接住\n# en: {You catch it an inch above the bench. Everyone breathes out at once. The first-year comes down the ladder very carefully and shakes your hand for a long time.|Caught, with both hands, against your chest. You put it back on the shelf yourself, at the bottom.|You catch it one-handed without looking, which surprises you more than anyone. Somebody starts a sweepstake on next time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cd5c0f] = {你在离工作台一寸的地方接住了它。所有人同时松了一口气。那个一年级生小心翼翼地爬下梯子，握着你的手久久不放。|接住了，用两只手，抱在胸前。你亲手把它放回架子——放在最底层。|你看都没看，单手就接住了，最吃惊的是你自己。有人开始押注下一次。}\n# en: {You touch it and it skips off your fingers and bursts on the floor. Everyone's eyebrows go blue, yours most.|You get a hand to it and slow it down. It still breaks, but only on your boots, which smoke politely for an hour.|You catch the first-year instead of the bottle. Both of you are blue until Thursday.}\nopt.left.say.2 [20c5bb] = {你碰到了它，它从你指尖弹开，在地上炸开了。所有人的眉毛都变蓝了，你的最蓝。|你一只手碰到了它，减缓了下落。它还是碎了，不过只碎在你的靴子上，靴子礼貌地冒了一个钟头的烟。|你接住的是那个一年级生，不是瓶子。你们俩一直蓝到星期四。}\n# en: Shout everyone back from the bench\nopt.right.label [d3558b] = 喊大家离开工作台\n# en: {\"Back!\" Everyone backs. The bottle hits the bench and the cloud goes up blue and harmless and smelling of pear drops. The bench is ruined; nobody is.|You clear the bench in a word. The bottle breaks; the first-year cries; the lecturer says it was the right call, in a voice that says it was an expensive one.|You shout before it's fallen. The bench clears; the bottle, perversely, lands upright.}\nopt.right.say.1 [504c6a] = {“退后！”所有人都退了。瓶子砸在工作台上，腾起一团无害的蓝雾，一股水果硬糖味。工作台毁了；人都没事。|你一句话就清空了工作台。瓶子碎了；一年级生哭了；讲师说你做得对，那语气在说这代价可不小。|瓶子还没落，你就喊了出来。工作台空了；瓶子偏偏正着落地，一点事没有。}\n# en: Throw a ward over the bench\nopt.up.label [2a5b0d] = 在工作台上撑起护界\n# en: {The bottle bursts on a pale shell an arm's length above everyone's heads. The cloud rolls off the ward like rain off a window. The first-year, still on the ladder, starts to clap.|A ward, flat over the bench like a tabletop. The bottle breaks on it and the cloud slides off into the sink. The lecturer writes your name down, in the good column.|{spell_level(ward) >= 2: You ward the bench and the first-year at once. Nothing touches anyone. Somebody asks you to teach them; you do, after.|You ward the bench, a little late; the cloud catches your sleeve. Your sleeve is blue. Nobody else is.}}\nopt.up.say.1 [f0e91c] = {瓶子在众人头顶一臂高的一层淡色护壳上炸开。雾气从护界上滚落，像雨水从窗玻璃上滑下。梯子上的一年级生开始鼓掌。|一道护界，像桌面一样平铺在工作台上方。瓶子在上面摔碎，雾气滑进了水槽。讲师把你的名字记了下来，记在好的那一栏。|{spell_level(ward) >= 2: 你同时护住了工作台和那个一年级生。什么都没碰到任何人。有人请你教TA；事后你教了。|你护住了工作台，只是稍晚了一点；雾气沾上了你的袖子。你的袖子蓝了。别人都没事。}}\n\n@event courtyard_hail\n# en: Hail in the Courtyard\ntitle [178de2] = 庭院里的冰雹\n# en: {Hail comes out of a clear sky, stones the size of peas, then the size of marbles. A knot of first-years is caught in the middle of the courtyard with their books over their heads.|Hail again, the courtyard's speciality. The first-years know to run now; they just don't know where.|The sky goes white and the hail comes down like a dropped tray of beads. {season == \"summer\": In summer, which is insulting.|The fountain rings with it.}}\ntext [7ccd74] = {晴空里落下冰雹，起先像豌豆那么大，后来像弹珠那么大。一群一年级生被困在庭院中央，把书顶在头上。|又下冰雹了，这是庭院的特产。一年级生现在知道要跑了；只是不知道往哪儿跑。|天空一白，冰雹像打翻了一盘珠子似的砸下来。{season == \"summer\": 还是在夏天，简直是侮辱。|喷泉被砸得叮当作响。}}\n# en: A castle courtyard in a sudden hailstorm, first-years caught in the open with their arms over their heads, white stones bouncing off the flagstones.\nscene [23c11c] = 突降冰雹的城堡庭院，一年级生们被困在空地上，双臂护着头，白色的冰粒在石板上弹跳。\n# en: Herd them into the cloister\nopt.left.label [985672] = 把他们赶进回廊\n# en: {You wave them into the cloister and take the last of the hail on your own back. One of them gives you a sugar mouse from their pocket, warm and slightly squashed.|You get them all in and count them twice. Your ears ring for an hour from the stones.|You herd them like ducks into the cloister, the stones bouncing off your shoulders. One of them says thank you in a very small voice.}\nopt.left.say.1 [56a32a] = {你挥手把他们赶进回廊，最后一阵冰雹全砸在你自己背上。其中一个从口袋里掏出一只糖老鼠给你，温温的，有点压扁了。|你把他们全带了进去，还数了两遍。冰雹砸得你耳朵嗡嗡响了一个钟头。|你像赶鸭子一样把他们赶进回廊，冰雹从你肩头弹开。其中一个用很小很小的声音说了声谢谢。}\n# en: Run for cover yourself\nopt.right.label [d4ce6f] = 自己先找地方躲\n# en: {You make the cloister in six strides and watch them work it out for themselves. They do, eventually, damply.|You run. A prefect gets them in. You feel you were quicker than you should have been.|Cover first. From the cloister you shout directions, which helps a little and makes you feel better a lot.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d40d8d] = {你六步跨进回廊，看着他们自己想办法。他们最后想出来了，湿漉漉的。|你跑了。一位级长把他们带了进去。你觉得自己跑得比应该的快。|先顾自己。你在回廊里朝外面喊着指路，帮了一点小忙，让自己心里好受了许多。}\n# en: Raise a ward over them\nopt.up.label [e586fe] = 在他们头上撑起护界\n# en: {You throw up a ward like a roof and the hail bounces off it in a white roar. The first-years stand under it, dry, looking up at you as if you'd hung the sky.|A pale shell over the knot of them. The hail drums on it like rain on a tent. Somebody under it starts to sing.|You walk them across the courtyard under a ward, slowly, like a procession. Half the castle is watching from the windows. You don't hurry.}\nopt.up.say.1 [d4383f] = {你撑起一道像屋顶一样的护界，冰雹在上面弹开，一片白茫茫的轰响。一年级生们站在下面，干干爽爽，抬头看你，好像天是你挂上去的。|一层淡色的护壳罩住了这群人。冰雹敲在上面，像雨点打在帐篷上。下面有人唱起了歌。|你撑着护界，领着他们慢慢穿过庭院，像一支游行队伍。半座城堡都在窗口看着。你一点也不急。}\n\n@event lakeshore_swan\n# en: The Swan\ntitle [f3d181] = 天鹅\n# en: {A first-year has wandered into the reeds too close to the swans' nest. The cob is coming out of the water with his wings up, hissing like a kettle.|The swans are nesting again. The cob has grown, or your memory of him has, and there's a first-year in the reeds.|The cob sees you from across the lake and starts hissing in advance.}\ntext [53f410] = {一个一年级生钻进芦苇丛，离天鹅窝太近了。那只雄天鹅张着翅膀从水里冲出来，嘶嘶叫得像一把水壶。|天鹅又在筑巢了。那只雄天鹅长大了，或者是你记忆里的它长大了，而芦苇丛里又有一个一年级生。|雄天鹅隔着湖就看见了你，提前嘶嘶叫起来。}\n# en: A reedy lakeshore, a huge white swan with wings raised and neck lowered, hissing at a first-year who has wandered too close to its nest.\nscene [eae669] = 长满芦苇的湖岸，一只巨大的白天鹅张开翅膀、压低脖子，冲着一个离窝太近的一年级生嘶叫。\n# en: Step between them\nopt.left.label [f242e3] = 挡在他们中间\n# en: {You step between them with your arms out wide and hiss back. The cob considers this, and decides you are the bigger swan. The first-year runs.|You stand your ground, slowly, talking nonsense in a low voice. He lowers his wings an inch, then another, then goes back to the nest.|He knows you by now, and you know him. You both posture, politely, until the first-year is clear.}\nopt.left.say.1 [39f757] = {你张开双臂挡在中间，也冲它嘶嘶叫。雄天鹅琢磨了一下，认定你是更大的那只天鹅。一年级生跑了。|你慢慢地站稳脚跟，低声说着胡话。它把翅膀放低了一寸，又一寸，然后回窝去了。|它现在认得你了，你也认得它。你们俩客客气气地摆着架势，直到一年级生脱身。}\n# en: {He hits you with a wing like a flung door. The first-year gets away; you get a bruise the size of a plate.|You step in and the cob takes it personally. You retreat across the reeds at speed, honked at.|You take one wing and then a bite on the ankle. The first-year helps you limp home, which seems backwards.}\nopt.left.say.2 [799431] = {它一翅膀扇过来，像一扇甩过来的门。一年级生逃掉了；你得到一块盘子大的淤青。|你一挡上去，雄天鹅就当成了私人恩怨。你飞快地撤过芦苇丛，身后一路被它冲着叫。|你挨了一翅膀，脚踝又被咬了一口。最后是那个一年级生扶着你一瘸一拐地回去，这好像反过来了。}\n# en: Shout for them to run\nopt.right.label [62f02f] = 喊他们快跑\n# en: {\"Run!\" The first-year runs, the cob chases them twenty yards, and everyone ends up in the lake except you.|You shout, they run, the cob gets a mouthful of their scarf. They don't get the scarf back.|You shout from a safe distance. It works, mostly. The first-year limps into the infirmary with a story they'll tell for years.}\nopt.right.say.1 [005fd0] = {“快跑！”一年级生跑了，雄天鹅追了二十码，最后除了你，所有人都掉进了湖里。|你一喊，TA就跑，雄天鹅叼住了TA的围巾。围巾再也没要回来。|你在安全距离外喊话。基本管用。那个一年级生一瘸一拐地进了医务室，带着一个能讲好多年的故事。}\n# en: Put a ward between them\nopt.up.label [5e969d] = 在他们之间撑起护界\n# en: {A pale shell springs up between the cob and the first-year. He beats his wings on it twice, offended, then sails back to his nest with enormous dignity.|You ward the first-year and walk them out of the reeds inside it. The cob hisses at the ward all the way, and the ward doesn't mind.|A ward, and a slow walk, and the swans left in peace. The cob watches you go, and you'd swear he nods.}\nopt.up.say.1 [bb7207] = {一层淡色的护壳在雄天鹅和一年级生之间撑了起来。它气呼呼地扑打了两下，然后无比庄严地游回了窝里。|你用护界罩住那个一年级生，带着TA从芦苇丛里走出来。雄天鹅一路冲着护界嘶叫，护界毫不在意。|一道护界，一段慢慢的路，天鹅们得以安宁。雄天鹅目送你离开，你敢发誓它点了点头。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/spotlight_famous.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/spotlight_famous.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event spot_endorsement\n# en: Your Face on a Candle\ntitle [d6592c] = 蜡烛上的你\n# en: The village chandler wants to sell \"{player}'s study candles: they burn all night, like genius\". Your face on every label, a crown or two from every box. He's already had the labels painted. They look almost like you.\ntext [e68ed8] = 村里的蜡烛匠想卖“{player}牌学习蜡烛：通宵长明，如天才一般”。每张标签上都是你的脸，每卖一盒你就能分一两个克朗。标签他已经请人画好了。看上去几乎像你。\n# en: A village chandler's shop window full of candles, each with a little painted label of a student's face, the chandler beaming in the doorway.\nscene [24c4ba] = 村里一家蜡烛铺的橱窗摆满了蜡烛，每支上都贴着一张画有学生面孔的小标签，蜡烛匠站在门口笑容满面。\n# en: Lend your name\nopt.left.label [1df9b2] = 借出你的名字\n# en: The candles burn all night, as promised, and give off a smell like a wet dog. Half the dormitory sleeps with the windows open. Someone writes \"GENIUS\" under your painted face, with an arrow, in soot.\nopt.left.say.1 [2a7bbf] = 蜡烛如约通宵长明，同时散发出一股湿狗的味道。半个宿舍的人只好开着窗睡觉。有人用烟灰在你那张画出来的脸下面写了“天才”，还画了个箭头。\n# en: The candles sell out twice. They're perfectly ordinary candles, but people study by them with great seriousness, and the chandler sends a box up to you every month, with your own face looking back from the label.\nopt.left.say.2 [205bc3] = 蜡烛卖断了两次货。它们只是再普通不过的蜡烛，可人们点着它们读书时都郑重其事，蜡烛匠每个月都给你送上来一盒，标签上你自己的脸回望着你。\n# en: Turn him down\nopt.right.label [b56858] = 拒绝他\n# en: You tell him, kindly, that your face isn't for sale. He sighs and paints over the labels. The next week they say \"a famous student's study candles\", and sell almost as well.\nopt.right.say.1 [dbd2a1] = 你和气地告诉他，你的脸不卖。他叹了口气，把标签涂掉了。第二周，标签上写的是“某位名学生的学习蜡烛”，卖得几乎一样好。\n\n@event spot_memoir\n# en: As Told To\ntitle [79a8c3] = 口述实录\n# en: A man from a city publisher has come all the way up the hill to offer you a sum for your memoir. You won't have to write a word: \"Our writer does all that. You just tell her a few stories, and sign.\" He has a draft title already. It has an exclamation mark.\ntext [5e77ae] = 一位城里出版社的人一路爬上山来，出一笔钱要买你的回忆录。你一个字都不用写：“我们的写手全包了。你只要给她讲几个故事，然后签字。”他连书名都拟好了。还带着一个感叹号。\n# en: A smart stranger in a travelling coat at a refectory table, a contract and a quill laid out in front of a student.\nscene [477ef8] = 膳堂的桌边坐着一位穿旅行外套的体面陌生人，一份合同和一支羽毛笔摆在一个学生面前。\n# en: Sign\nopt.left.label [6340c4] = 签字\n# en: The first chapter is printed in the Ledger as a teaser. In it you wrestle a lake serpent, win a duel blindfold and kiss three people on one page. You have done none of these things. The whole castle reads it aloud at breakfast, in voices.\nopt.left.say.1 [f0b59d] = 第一章作为预告登在了《纪事报》上。书里你和湖中巨蛇搏斗，蒙着眼睛赢了一场决斗，还在一页之内亲了三个人。这些事你一件都没做过。全城堡的人在早餐时把它大声念出来，还配上了腔调。\n# en: The book is short and breathless and only slightly made up. It sells in the city, and copies come back up the hill, where first-years read it under the desks. You sign one, once, and then hide.\nopt.left.say.2 [ab0a00] = 这本书篇幅不长，读来令人屏息，只稍微编造了一点。它在城里卖得不错，几本又流回了山上，一年级生把它藏在课桌底下读。你签过一本，就一次，然后就躲起来了。\n# en: Decline\nopt.right.label [eaef39] = 婉拒\n# en: You tell him you're still in the middle of it, and you'd like to find out how it ends first. He laughs, leaves his card, and takes the long way down the hill.\nopt.right.say.1 [018928] = 你告诉他你还在故事的中间，想先看看结局如何。他笑了，留下名片，绕远路下了山。\n\n@event spot_impostor\n# en: The Other {player}\ntitle [eb5455] = 另一个{player}\n# en: The baker asks, very politely, when you're going to settle your account. You haven't got one. It turns out somebody in an academy scarf has been eating very well in the village for a month and signing your name, with a flourish, at the bakery, the tearoom and the Newt.\ntext [1169cd] = 面包师非常客气地问你，什么时候结清你的账。你根本没有账。原来有人围着学院颜色的围巾，在村里吃香喝辣了一个月，在面包房、茶室和蝾螈酒馆都签了你的名字，还签得龙飞凤舞。\n# en: A village bakery counter, a slip of paper pinned up reading \"on account\", a hooded figure in academy-coloured scarf ducking out of the back door.\nscene [591e45] = 村里面包房的柜台上钉着一张写着“记账”的纸条，一个围着学院颜色围巾的兜帽身影正从后门溜出去。\n# en: Catch them in the act\nopt.left.label [59255a] = 当场抓住对方\n# en: You catch the impostor at the bakery on market day, in front of a crowd. The impostor is a thin first-year who hadn't eaten properly in weeks. The crowd looks at you as if you'd kicked a puppy. You pay the account. It doesn't help much.\nopt.left.say.1 [db8704] = 赶集那天你在面包房当着一群人的面抓住了冒名者。那是个瘦弱的一年级生，已经好几个星期没好好吃过饭了。人群看你的眼神就像你踢了一只小狗。你结清了账。这没多大用。\n# en: You catch the impostor in the act at the Newt: a grinning fourth-year who's done it for a bet. The landlady makes them pay every crown back, and you get a free supper and a story that does the rounds for weeks.\nopt.left.say.2 [a3163d] = 你在蝾螈酒馆当场抓住了冒名者：一个咧嘴笑的四年级生，为了打赌才这么干。老板娘让对方一个克朗不少地全还了回来，你得了一顿免费晚餐，还有一个流传了好几个星期的故事。\n# en: Pay the bills, and leave a note\nopt.right.label [197c5f] = 付清账单，留张字条\n# en: You pay what's owed and leave a note with the baker: \"If they come back, feed them, and tell them to come and see me.\" A week later, a thin first-year turns up at your door, and you find them a job at the Newt.\nopt.right.say.1 [759c66] = 你付清了欠账，给面包师留了张字条：“如果对方再来，就给饭吃，再让对方来找我。”一个星期后，一个瘦弱的一年级生出现在你门口，你给对方在蝾螈酒馆找了份活。\n\n@event spot_fan_circle\n# en: The {player} Society\ntitle [2a546d] = {player}协会\n# en: There is a society. It meets on Tuesdays in the disused classroom at the top of the lecture tower. It has a banner, ribbons, minutes and a portrait of you, done in crayon. Its president has written asking whether you would honour them with a visit.\ntext [af7082] = 有一个协会。每周二在讲堂塔顶那间废弃的教室聚会。它有横幅、缎带、会议记录，还有一幅用蜡笔画的你的肖像。会长写信来问，你是否愿意赏光来访。\n# en: A disused classroom with a hand-painted banner, a dozen younger students in matching ribbons sitting in a circle, a portrait of one student propped on a chair.\nscene [4ce436] = 一间废弃的教室挂着手绘的横幅，十来个系着同样缎带的年轻学生围坐成一圈，一幅学生的画像靠在一把椅子上。\n# en: Visit, and make a fuss of them\nopt.left.label [27476c] = 去看看，好好捧捧他们\n# en: The society is thrilled, and sets out to honour you properly. The next morning your name is written across the observatory dome in luminous paint, misspelled. The headmistress asks to see you. So does the observatory.\nopt.left.say.1 [32618d] = 协会激动万分，决定好好向你致敬。第二天早上，你的名字被人用夜光漆写在了观星台的穹顶上，还拼错了。院长要见你。观星台那边也要。\n# en: You visit, sign the minutes, admire the portrait, and answer their questions for an hour. The society is beside itself. You have, from then on, a dozen first-years who would walk into the lake for you, and who very kindly don't.\nopt.left.say.2 [f814ff] = 你去了，在会议记录上签了名，夸了那幅肖像，回答了他们一个小时的问题。协会的人高兴得不得了。从那以后，你有了十几个愿意为你跳进湖里的一年级生，而他们很体贴地没有真跳。\n# en: Ask them to do something useful\nopt.right.label [c974ac] = 请他们做点有用的事\n# en: You visit, and suggest that a society in your name might run a study table for first-years who are struggling. They look crestfallen, and then very determined. By spring, the study table is the best thing about Tuesdays.\nopt.right.say.1 [5a3650] = 你去了，建议一个以你命名的协会不妨给学得吃力的一年级生办个学习桌。他们先是垂头丧气，接着变得非常坚决。到了春天，那张学习桌成了每周二最好的事。\n\n@event spot_thornwick_challenge\n# en: A Challenge From Across the Hills\ntitle [bc6a2f] = 来自山那边的挑战\n# en: A Thornwick student has sent a challenge, very publicly, by way of a notice in a red seal on the courtyard board: \"We hear Lumenhold's famous {player} is the best you've got. A contest of spells at the boundary stones, at the month's end. Bring a crowd.\" Half the castle has already promised to come.\ntext [572c91] = 一个荆木学院的学生大张旗鼓地发来挑战，用一张盖着红色荆木印章的告示钉在庭院的布告板上：“听说流明堡著名的{player}是你们最好的人。月底，界石旁，比一场法术。多带些观众来。”半个城堡的人已经说好要去了。\n# en: A pinned notice on the courtyard board with a red Thornwick seal, a crowd of students reading it and turning to look at one student.\nscene [ebd32d] = 庭院的布告板上钉着一张盖着红色荆木印章的告示，一群学生读着告示，转头看向一个学生。\n# en: Accept\nopt.left.label [b26149] = 接受\n# en: The contest at the boundary stones lasts four minutes. You win it on the last spell, with the whole of Lumenhold roaring behind you. The Thornwick student shakes your hand, says \"next year\", and means it.\nopt.left.say.1 [803e08] = 界石旁的比试只持续了四分钟。你在最后一个法术上赢了，身后整个流明堡都在欢呼。荆木的学生和你握了握手，说了句“明年见”，而且是认真的。\n# en: You lose, fairly and very publicly, to a Thornwick student who is simply better on the day. The walk back up the hill is long and quiet. Thornwick's paper prints the result in very large letters.\nopt.left.say.2 [453cf8] = 你输了，输得公平，也输得人尽皆知，对手是个那天就是比你强的荆木学生。回山上的路又长又安静。荆木的校报用非常大的字登出了结果。\n# en: Answer with a letter\nopt.right.label [fc938e] = 写信回应\n# en: You pin a reply under the challenge: \"Lumenhold doesn't perform on request. Do come for the Spring Tournament.\" The castle is a little disappointed, and a little proud. Thornwick's reply, if there is one, never arrives.\nopt.right.say.1 [bce8e8] = 你在挑战书下面钉了一张回信：“流明堡不应邀表演。春季锦标赛欢迎光临。”城堡里的人有点失望，也有点自豪。荆木的回信，如果有的话，始终没有到。\n\n@event spot_settle_dispute\n# en: Your Word on It\ntitle [102bf0] = 听你一句话\n# en: The Duelling Circle and the Skyrace Squad have both booked the flight field for the same Saturday, and it has come close to blows. Somebody suggests they let you decide, since everybody listens to you. Both captains agree. Both are glaring at you, and both are certain you'll pick them.\ntext [8285a1] = 决斗社和天空竞速队都订了同一个周六的飞行场，差点动起手来。有人提议让你来定，因为大家都听你的。两位队长都同意了。两人都瞪着你，都确信你会选自己。\n# en: Two groups of students in different club colours glaring at each other across a courtyard, one student standing between them with a hand raised.\nscene [c27127] = 两群穿着不同社团颜色的学生隔着庭院怒目相对，一个学生举着手站在他们中间。\n# en: Decide it, there and then\nopt.left.label [c326e4] = 当场裁决\n# en: You give the field to the Squad, who had booked first. By supper the Circle is telling everyone you were bought, and someone remembers you once had a drink with the Squad's captain. It's nonsense. It sticks for a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [97028f] = 你把场地判给了先订的竞速队。到晚饭时，决斗社到处说你被收买了，还有人记起你曾经和竞速队队长喝过一杯。纯属胡说。可这话传了一个月。\n# en: You give the field to the Squad, who had booked first, and the Circle the Sunday, with the good side of the hall for practice. Both captains grumble, and both shake on it. People start bringing you their arguments.\nopt.left.say.2 [c7e2c0] = 你把场地判给了先订的竞速队，周日给决斗社，外加礼堂好的那一边供他们练习。两位队长都嘟囔了几句，也都握手同意了。人们开始把他们的争执带来找你。\n# en: Make them settle it themselves\nopt.right.label [8e7b51] = 让他们自己解决\n# en: You sit both captains down with a pot of tea and refuse to leave until they've worked it out. It takes three hours and the whole pot. They share the field, half the day each, and claim it was their idea.\nopt.right.say.1 [ebec62] = 你让两位队长坐下，泡了一壶茶，说不谈出结果你就不走。这花了三个小时，喝光了整壶茶。他们平分了场地，一人半天，还说这是他们自己的主意。\n# en: You sit them down, and they argue for three hours, and in the end neither gets the field, because the groundskeeper has booked it for mowing. Both clubs blame you, slightly, forever.\nopt.right.say.2 [a6adfb] = 你让他们坐下，他们吵了三个小时，最后谁也没拿到场地，因为场地管理员早就订好了那天割草。两个社团都怪你，有一点点，永远。\n\n@event spot_charity_auction\n# en: An Afternoon With {player}\ntitle [238021] = 与{player}共度一个下午\n# en: The infirmary fund is holding an auction, and the top lot, the committee tells you, has to be \"an afternoon with {player}\": tea, a walk, whatever the winner likes. They think it will raise more than everything else put together. They are probably right.\ntext [f159c1] = 医务室基金要办一场拍卖会，委员会告诉你，压轴拍品非得是“与{player}共度一个下午”不可：喝茶、散步，随中标者的意。他们认为这一件拍出的钱会比其他所有拍品加起来还多。他们多半是对的。\n# en: The Great Hall set out for an auction, a student on a stage holding up a card, a forest of raised hands, an auctioneer with a gavel.\nscene [5fedd7] = 大礼堂布置成了拍卖会场，一个学生站在台上举着一张卡片，台下举手如林，拍卖师握着一把木槌。\n# en: Agree to be auctioned\nopt.left.label [13a1c1] = 同意被拍卖\n# en: It raises a fortune. The winner turns out to be the Ledger's worst gossip, who spends the afternoon asking about your love life and prints every word. The infirmary gets new beds. You get a column you'd rather not have read.\nopt.left.say.1 [1afe5c] = 拍出了一大笔钱。中标者原来是《纪事报》最爱嚼舌根的人，整个下午都在打听你的感情生活，还把每个字都登了出来。医务室添了新床。你得到了一篇你宁愿没读过的专栏。\n# en: It raises a fortune. The winner is an old bookbinder from the village, who has saved for a year and only wants to walk round the lake with somebody famous and tell them about his garden. It's one of the best afternoons you have.\nopt.left.say.2 [d36dc1] = 拍出了一大笔钱。中标者是村里一位年迈的装订匠，攒了一年的钱，只想和一个名人绕着湖走一圈，跟对方讲讲自己的花园。那是你度过的最好的下午之一。\n# en: Give the fund a donation instead\nopt.right.label [a4427f] = 改为向基金捐款\n# en: You give the committee what you can, and ask them to auction something more useful than you. They auction the headmistress's old hat, which raises almost as much and is far less trouble.\nopt.right.say.1 [66ec74] = 你尽自己所能捐了一笔，请委员会拍卖点比你更有用的东西。他们拍卖了院长的旧帽子，拍出的钱差不多一样多，麻烦却少得多。\n\n@event spot_newt_drink\n# en: Whatever You're Having\ntitle [f16c9d] = 照你的来\n# en: Whatever you order at the Gilded Newt, half the bar orders next, and the landlady has noticed. She leans over the counter. \"Do me a favour, love. Order the elderflower fizz. I've forty bottles of it in the cellar and nobody's touched one since midsummer. There's a cut in it for you.\" The whole bar is waiting to see what you'll have.\ntext [594688] = 你在镀金蝾螈酒馆点什么，半个酒馆的人就跟着点什么，老板娘早注意到了。她从柜台后探过身来。“帮个忙，亲爱的。点一杯接骨木花汽酒吧。我地窖里有四十瓶，从仲夏到现在一瓶都没人碰过。少不了你的分成。”整个酒馆都在等着看你点什么。\n# en: A tavern bar, a landlady leaning over the counter with a dusty bottle of pale fizz, the whole room turned to watch one student order.\nscene [dcd7a2] = 酒馆吧台边，老板娘探身越过柜台，手里拿着一瓶落满灰的淡色汽酒，满屋子的人都转过头来看一个学生点单。\n# en: \"One elderflower fizz, please.\"\nopt.left.label [153ff7] = “来一杯接骨木花汽酒。”\n# en: The fizz has been in the cellar since midsummer for a reason. Half the bar orders one after you, and half the bar spends the rest of the evening outside. The landlady gives you your cut anyway. By morning everyone knows whose idea it was.\nopt.left.say.1 [dab963] = 那瓶汽酒从仲夏起就待在地窖里，是有原因的。半个酒馆的人跟着你点了一杯，然后半个酒馆的人整晚都待在外面。老板娘照样给了你分成。到第二天早上，人人都知道这是谁的主意了。\n# en: You raise a glass of fizz to the bar, and the bar raises forty back. It is far too sweet. For the rest of the term it's what people drink at the Newt, and every few weeks the landlady presses a small purse of coppers into your hand.\nopt.left.say.2 [618a91] = 你举起一杯汽酒向整个酒馆致意，酒馆里举起四十杯回敬你。它甜得过分。这个学期剩下的日子里，大家在蝾螈酒馆都喝这个，每隔几个星期，老板娘就往你手里塞一小袋铜板。\n# en: Order what you always have\nopt.right.label [3355fd] = 照常点你平时喝的\n# en: You order your usual. So does half the bar. The landlady sighs, says \"Traitor\" quite fondly, and goes down to the cellar for more of it. The fizz stays where it is.\nopt.right.say.1 [bbd26a] = 你点了你平时喝的。半个酒馆的人也点了一样的。老板娘叹了口气，相当亲昵地骂了声“叛徒”，下到地窖里去多拿几瓶。那些汽酒原封不动。\n\n@event spot_lecture_crowds\n# en: Standing Room Only\ntitle [eaed8a] = 只剩站票\n# en: Your lectures have filled up. Not with people who take the course: with people who want to sit near you. They sit in the aisles. They sit on the windowsills. After class, the lecturer asks you to stay behind, and says, without warmth, \"This is a lecture, not a menagerie. Do something about it.\"\ntext [d2af75] = 你的课坐满了人。不是选这门课的人：是想坐在你附近的人。他们坐在过道里。他们坐在窗台上。下课后，讲师让你留下，毫无暖意地说：“这是讲课，不是动物园。想想办法。”\n# en: A lecture hall crammed with students sitting in the aisles and on the windowsills, a lecturer at the front glaring over spectacles at one student in the third row.\nscene [e8c2f7] = 阶梯教室里挤满了学生，过道和窗台上都坐着人，讲台上的讲师从眼镜上方瞪着第三排的一个学生。\n# en: Hold a question time after class\nopt.left.label [7f0d3c] = 课后设个答疑时间\n# en: You offer a half-hour of questions after class, for anyone. The first question is about your love life. The second is about your hair. The lecturer comes back for a book, sees the circus, and gives you an essay to write on decorum.\nopt.left.say.1 [8726be] = 你在课后开了半小时答疑，谁都可以来。第一个问题是关于你的感情生活。第二个是关于你的头发。讲师回来拿书，看见这副马戏团的场面，罚你写一篇关于礼仪的论文。\n# en: You offer a half-hour of questions after class, and to your surprise most of them are about the lecture. By the end of the month you know the course better than ever, and the aisles are clear: people come to your half-hour instead.\nopt.left.say.2 [966afa] = 你在课后开了半小时答疑，出乎意料的是，大部分问题都是关于这门课的。到月底，你对这门课比以往任何时候都熟，过道也空了：人们改来参加你的半小时。\n# en: Sit at the back, in a hood\nopt.right.label [49051c] = 坐到后排，戴上兜帽\n# en: You sit at the back with your hood up, take careful notes and leave by the side door. The crowds drift away to look at something else. The lecturer gives you, at the end of term, the very smallest nod.\nopt.right.say.1 [5569b9] = 你戴着兜帽坐在后排，认真记笔记，从侧门离开。人群渐渐散去，去看别的热闹了。学期末，讲师朝你点了点头，幅度小得不能再小。\n\n@event spot_luck_sleeve\n# en: For Luck\ntitle [83cd21] = 图个好运\n# en: Somebody has decided that touching your sleeve before an exam brings luck. The queue outside the exam hall reaches out as you pass, one after another, very solemnly. A first-year asks if you'd mind breathing on their quill.\ntext [1e9490] = 有人认定考试前摸一摸你的袖子能带来好运。考场外排队的人在你经过时一个接一个地伸出手，神情非常庄重。一个一年级生问你介不介意朝那支羽毛笔哈口气。\n# en: A corridor outside an exam hall, a nervous queue of students each reaching out to touch one student's sleeve as they pass.\nscene [4a603b] = 考场外的走廊里，一排紧张的学生在一个学生经过时，一个接一个地伸手去碰这个学生的袖子。\n# en: Bless them all, grandly\nopt.left.label [45c92a] = 隆重地为所有人祝福\n# en: You bless every quill and sleeve with great ceremony. When the results come out, a first-year who failed writes to the Ledger to say that your luck \"is a fraud, and so are you\". The letter is printed. Several people agree with it loudly.\nopt.left.say.1 [2332c7] = 你郑重其事地为每一支羽毛笔、每一只袖子祝了福。成绩出来后，一个没考过的一年级生写信给《纪事报》，说你的好运“是个骗局，你也是”。信登了出来。好几个人大声表示赞同。\n# en: You bless every quill with great ceremony, and the queue goes in laughing, which is better for them than any luck. Afterwards three of them find you to say they passed. You don't point out that most people do.\nopt.left.say.2 [6f0716] = 你郑重其事地为每一支羽毛笔祝了福，排队的人笑着走进考场，这比任何好运都对他们更有用。事后有三个人来找你，说他们考过了。你没有指出大多数人都会考过。\n# en: Tell them to trust their work\nopt.right.label [ba0698] = 让他们相信自己的努力\n# en: You tell the queue, kindly, that your sleeve has never passed an exam, and that they've done the work. Some of them look let down. One of them, a first-year with very cold hands, says thank you and goes in steadier.\nopt.right.say.1 [a9e9c3] = 你和气地告诉排队的人，你的袖子从没考过一场试，而他们已经下过功夫了。有些人显得有点失望。其中一个手冰凉的一年级生道了声谢，走进去时稳当了些。\n\n@event spot_routine_known\n# en: Where to Find {player}\ntitle [c04713] = 去哪儿找{player}\n# en: Someone has printed your whole week in a gossip sheet: which mornings you're in the library, where you sit for lunch, which way you walk back. By Tuesday there's a crowd on the library steps at nine o'clock, waiting, and the librarians are not pleased.\ntext [b942ab] = 有人把你一整周的行程登在了一份八卦小报上：哪几个早上你在图书馆，午饭坐在哪儿，回去走哪条路。到了周二，九点钟的图书馆台阶上已经聚了一群人在等，图书管理员们很不高兴。\n# en: A gossip sheet pinned to a board with a timetable printed on it, a crowd gathering on the library steps, a student watching from a corner with books held to their chest.\nscene [192163] = 布告板上钉着一份印着时间表的八卦小报，一群人聚在图书馆台阶上，一个学生把书抱在胸前，从角落里看着。\n# en: Hold court on the steps\nopt.left.label [dfa614] = 在台阶上接见众人\n# en: You stop on the steps and chat, and the crowd grows, and grows, until it blocks the library door. The librarians ban you, very politely, for a week. The crowd moves on to the Newt. You fall behind on everything.\nopt.left.say.1 [4a9974] = 你在台阶上停下来聊天，人越聚越多，直到堵住了图书馆的门。图书管理员们非常客气地禁止你入馆一周。人群转移到了蝾螈酒馆。你什么都落下了。\n# en: You stop on the steps each morning for ten minutes, answer whatever people ask, and then go inside. The crowd shrinks to a friendly handful who bring you tea. The librarians decide they can live with it.\nopt.left.say.2 [0d28da] = 你每天早上在台阶上停留十分钟，有问必答，然后进去。人群渐渐缩成一小撮友好的人，还给你带茶。图书管理员们觉得这还能忍。\n# en: Change everything\nopt.right.label [c6188a] = 全部改掉\n# en: You change your mornings, your lunch table, your way back. It takes a week to get it right and you keep getting lost. But you find a window seat in a disused stair where nobody comes, and you work better there than you have all year.\nopt.right.say.1 [e3f522] = 你改了早上的安排、午饭的桌子、回去的路。花了一个星期才调整好，你还总是迷路。不过你找到了一处没人去的废弃楼梯间的窗台，在那儿学习的效率比这一年里任何时候都高。\n\n@event spot_guest_flyer\n# en: A Guest Lap\ntitle [1381e1] = 表演一圈\n# en: The Skyrace Squad wants you to fly a guest lap at the season's opening race: just once round the course, before the real race starts, for the crowd. \"It'll double the gate,\" the captain says. \"Nobody's going to watch you race. They'll just watch you fly.\" The course goes out over the lake.\ntext [1e8cc3] = 天空竞速队想请你在赛季揭幕赛上飞一圈表演：在正式比赛开始前，绕赛道飞一圈就好，给观众看。“门票能翻倍，”队长说，“没人要看你比赛。他们只想看你飞。”赛道要飞越湖面。\n# en: A flight field ringed by a packed crowd, banners snapping, one student on a borrowed broom at the starting line, the lake glittering beyond.\nscene [37fe5f] = 飞行场四周挤满了观众，旗帜猎猎作响，一个学生骑着借来的飞帚停在起跑线上，远处的湖面波光粼粼。\n# en: Fly it\nopt.left.label [21fb6b] = 飞\n# en: You fly the lap clean and a little faster than you should, and the crowd stands up to roar as you come round the last marker. The Squad captain is grinning. For the rest of the season, small children run about the field with brooms, being you.\nopt.left.say.1 [3bd6c9] = 你干净利落地飞完了一圈，还稍微快了点，绕过最后一个标志时观众站起来欢呼。竞速队队长咧着嘴笑。那个赛季剩下的日子里，小孩子们骑着扫帚在场上跑来跑去，扮演你。\n# en: You fly it beautifully, right up to the last marker over the lake, where you misjudge the wind and go into the water in front of the whole academy. The crowd's gasp is louder than any cheer. Your own laughter, when you surface, saves a little of it.\nopt.left.say.2 [ce5c4c] = 你飞得很漂亮，一直到湖面上的最后一个标志，你算错了风向，当着整个学院的面掉进了水里。观众的惊呼声比任何欢呼都响。你浮出水面时自己的笑声，多少挽回了一点。\n# en: Present the trophy instead\nopt.right.label [018ab9] = 改为颁奖\n# en: You stay on the ground and present the trophy at the end, which takes a speech and no risk at all. The crowd is perfectly happy. The captain looks at your boots, and then the sky, and doesn't say anything.\nopt.right.say.1 [801db5] = 你留在地面上，在最后颁发奖杯，只需讲几句话，毫无风险。观众完全满意。队长看了看你的靴子，又看了看天空，什么也没说。\n\n@event spot_petition\n# en: Your Name at the Top\ntitle [a1bf06] = 你的名字签在最上面\n# en: A petition is going round to keep the library open all night in exam weeks. It has a hundred and twelve names and a blank line at the top, which the second-year carrying it would like you to fill. \"If your name's on it, they'll have to listen.\" The scroll goes on for quite a long way below the main request.\ntext [04041a] = 一份请愿书正在传阅，要求考试周图书馆通宵开放。上面已经有一百一十二个名字，最上面留着一行空白，拿着它的二年级生希望你来填。“只要你的名字在上面，他们就不得不听。”主要诉求下面，卷轴还拖得相当长。\n# en: A student holding out a long scroll petition with dozens of signatures, a quill in the other hand, the top line left blank.\nscene [4c3d2b] = 一个学生一手举着长长的请愿卷轴，上面有几十个签名，另一只手拿着羽毛笔，最上面一行空着。\n# en: Sign at the top\nopt.left.label [64397d] = 签在最上面\n# en: The headmistress reads it out at supper, all of it, including clause nine (puddings twice a day) and clause twelve (the abolition of Thursdays), \"proposed by {player}\". The hall laughs for a full minute. The library stays shut.\nopt.left.say.1 [a12d08] = 院长在晚餐时把它从头到尾念了出来，包括第九条（一天两顿布丁）和第十二条（废除星期四），“由{player}提议”。整个大厅笑了足足一分钟。图书馆照旧关门。\n# en: The library stays open all night in exam weeks. The petition is framed and hung by the door, with your name at the top, and in the small hours there are students at every desk, and someone always brings you a cup of tea.\nopt.left.say.2 [51c881] = 考试周图书馆通宵开放了。请愿书被装进画框挂在门边，你的名字在最上面，深夜里每张桌子边都坐着学生，总有人给你端来一杯茶。\n# en: Read it all first\nopt.right.label [4c4f9b] = 先从头读一遍\n# en: You read it all, which takes a while. Clause nine asks for puddings twice a day. Clause twelve abolishes Thursdays. You hand it back with a note on how to make it shorter. The second-year looks very let down, and then, the next week, comes back with one clause, which you sign.\nopt.right.say.1 [a1269c] = 你从头到尾读了一遍，花了不少时间。第九条要求一天两顿布丁。第十二条要废除星期四。你把它还了回去，附了张字条，教对方怎么改短。那个二年级生显得很失望，然后，第二周，拿着只剩一条的请愿书回来了，你签了。\n\n@event spot_party_host\n# en: The Party, If You Come\ntitle [5927e0] = 只要你来\n# en: A second-year is throwing a party in the old music room on Saturday, and says frankly that it'll either be the party of the term or a sad evening with twelve people and a fiddle, depending entirely on whether you come. Everybody knows already that you've been asked. Everybody is waiting to see.\ntext [2a8396] = 一个二年级生周六要在旧音乐室开派对，坦白说，这场派对要么是本学期最棒的，要么是十二个人加一把小提琴的冷清夜晚，全看你来不来。大家都已经知道你被邀请了。大家都在等着看。\n# en: A student at a dormitory door holding out a handwritten invitation, behind them a room strung with lanterns and stacked with crates.\nscene [0a5669] = 一个学生站在宿舍门口递出一张手写的请柬，身后的房间里挂着灯笼，堆着板条箱。\n# en: Go\nopt.left.label [d0c56b] = 去\n# en: Everyone comes. Everyone. At one o'clock the prefects come too, and the report that goes to the headmistress names one guest by name, at the top. You spend a week of evenings in detention polishing the trophy cabinet, and the castle talks about it for a month.\nopt.left.say.1 [9684f2] = 大家都来了。所有人。凌晨一点，级长们也来了，交给院长的报告上，最上面点名写着一位客人的名字。你花了一个星期的晚上被关禁闭擦奖杯柜，城堡里议论了一个月。\n# en: It's the party of the term. You dance badly, talk to everyone, and leave before it gets out of hand. On Monday a dozen people you'd never met say hello to you in the corridors, and the second-year is a legend.\nopt.left.say.2 [b0eb4b] = 那是本学期最棒的派对。你跳舞跳得很差，和每个人都聊了天，在场面失控之前离开了。周一，十几个你从没见过的人在走廊里跟你打招呼，那个二年级生成了传奇。\n# en: Stay in, and wish them well\nopt.right.label [4c9d81] = 待在宿舍，祝他们玩得开心\n# en: You send a note saying you can't, and a cake. The party is small and very nice, and the twelve people and the fiddle have a lovely time. The second-year sends back a slice.\nopt.right.say.1 [026bd9] = 你写了张便条说去不了，还送去一个蛋糕。派对规模不大，非常愉快，那十二个人和那把小提琴玩得很开心。那个二年级生给你送回来一块蛋糕。\n\n@event spot_ball_opening\n# en: The First Dance\ntitle [e47601] = 第一支舞\n# en: The Midwinter Ball committee has asked you to open the Ball: the first dance, alone on the floor, in front of the whole school, before anybody else may step out. {bonds(partner) > 0: Your partner has gone very pale and is pretending not to have heard.|You'd need to choose someone, and everyone knows it.}\ntext [5d5899] = 仲冬舞会委员会请你为舞会开场：第一支舞，独自在舞池里，当着全校的面，在你跳之前谁都不能下场。{bonds(partner) > 0: 你的伴侣脸色煞白，正假装没听见。|你得选一个人，大家都知道。}\n# en: The Great Hall turned to ice and starlight, an empty dance floor ringed by the whole school, one couple stepping out alone into the middle.\nscene [0fb66d] = 大礼堂化作冰与星光，空荡荡的舞池四周围着全校师生，一对舞伴独自走向中央。\n# en: Open the Ball\nopt.left.label [157b5b] = 为舞会开场\n# en: The music starts and, for once, your feet know what to do. You turn once round the whole floor under the floating candles, and when the music ends, the hall applauds before it remembers to dance. People talk about it till spring.\nopt.left.say.1 [839283] = 音乐响起，难得一次你的脚知道该怎么走。你在漂浮的蜡烛下绕着整个舞池转了一圈，音乐结束时，大厅先鼓起了掌，才想起来该跳舞了。人们一直谈论到春天。\n# en: You start well. Then the ice-floor, the candles and the whole school watching all happen at once, and you go down in a heap at the exact centre of the hall. You get up and finish the dance. The hall cheers more for that than it would have for the steps.\nopt.left.say.2 [b31d92] = 你开头很好。然后冰面、蜡烛和全校的目光一下子同时袭来，你在大厅正中央摔成了一团。你爬起来跳完了这支舞。大厅为此欢呼得比为舞步欢呼得还响。\n# en: Let someone else open it\nopt.right.label [a69926] = 让别人开场\n# en: You thank the committee and suggest the two first-years who've been practising for weeks in the boathouse. They open the Ball, nervously and beautifully. You dance later, in the crowd, where nobody's watching, and enjoy every minute.\nopt.right.say.1 [63a024] = 你谢过委员会，推荐了那两个在船屋里练了好几个星期的一年级生。他们为舞会开了场，紧张而优美。你后来在人群中跳舞，没人看着，每一分钟都很享受。\n\n@event spot_stable_name\n# en: A Name for the Foal\ntitle [9cc0cb] = 给小马起个名字\n# en: A winged foal was born at the Familiar Stables last night, and Professor Brask, who claims to have no opinion on the matter, says the first-years will only stop arguing about its name if you choose it. She hands you a slate. The foal looks at you and sneezes.\ntext [18e7d7] = 昨夜魔宠马厩里生了一匹带翅膀的小马驹，布拉斯克教授声称自己对此毫无意见，却说只有你来起名，一年级生们才会停止争吵。她递给你一块石板。小马驹看着你，打了个喷嚏。\n# en: A stable stall with a gangly winged foal tottering on long legs, a professor in a leather apron holding out a slate and chalk to a student.\nscene [2543b5] = 马厩隔间里，一匹瘦长的带翅小马驹摇摇晃晃地站在细长的腿上，一位系着皮围裙的教授把石板和粉笔递给一个学生。\n# en: Give it a grand name\nopt.left.label [1489cb] = 起个气派的名字\n# en: You chalk up something grand and ancient and very hard to spell. The first-years approve. The foal grows into it slowly, and in the meantime everybody calls it something else, behind Brask's back, that you suspect is ruder.\nopt.left.say.1 [3fcfdd] = 你用粉笔写了一个气派、古老、非常难拼的名字。一年级生们很满意。小马驹慢慢地才配得上这个名字，在此期间，大家背着布拉斯克管它叫别的名字，你怀疑那个更不雅。\n# en: Let the first-years vote\nopt.right.label [8b1b50] = 让一年级生投票\n# en: You hand the slate back and say the first-years should vote. They vote for \"Biscuit\". Brask writes it in the stable book with the face of a woman enduring something, and the foal, as it turns out, loves it.\nopt.right.say.1 [4dfde0] = 你把石板递了回去，说该让一年级生投票。他们投了“饼干”。布拉斯克把它写进马厩的登记簿，脸上是一副忍耐着什么的女人的表情，而事实证明，小马驹很喜欢这个名字。\n\n@event spot_friend_strain\n# en: Just {player}'s Friend\ntitle [f98f14] = 只是“{player}的朋友”\n# en: {f} sits down beside you on the step and says, without looking at you, \"Someone called me '{player}'s friend' today. Not my name. Just that. It's the fourth time this week.\" {They} laugh{s}, but not much. \"I don't mind. I just wanted you to know.\"\ntext [8098fb] = {f}在你旁边的台阶上坐下，没有看你，说：“今天有人叫我‘{player}的朋友’。不是我的名字。就这么叫。这个星期已经是第四次了。”{They}笑了笑，但笑得不多。“我不介意。我只是想让你知道。”\n# en: Two students sitting on a cold stone step outside the castle at dusk, one with their hands in their pockets, the other turned toward them.\nscene [15f9dc] = 黄昏时分，两个学生坐在城堡外冰冷的石阶上，一个双手插在口袋里，另一个侧身朝着对方。\n# en: Make time for {f}, away from it all\nopt.left.label [1b3f9f] = 抽出时间陪{f}，远离这一切\n# en: You start taking {f} to the places no one goes: the boathouse at dusk, the end of the orchard. You let the invitations wait. One evening {f} says something so funny you fall off a jetty, and nobody else is there to see it, and that's the point.\nopt.left.say.1 [df4ef2] = 你开始带{f}去没人去的地方：黄昏时的船屋，果园的尽头。你让那些邀请等着。一天傍晚，{f}说了句好笑得让你从栈桥上摔下去的话，而旁边没有别人看见，这正是重点。\n# en: Bring {f} into the spotlight with you\nopt.right.label [f1d697] = 把{f}也带进聚光灯下\n# en: You start bringing {f} everywhere with you, and making sure people hear {f.their} name. After a month, people in corridors start saying \"{f} and {player}\", in that order. {f} pretends not to notice, and is pleased.\nopt.right.say.1 [b56e99] = 你开始走到哪儿都带着{f}，确保大家听到{f.their}名字。一个月后，走廊里的人开始说“{f}和{player}”，按这个顺序。{f}假装没注意到，心里很高兴。\n# en: You bring {f} everywhere, and introduce {f.them} to everyone, and {f} goes quieter and quieter. \"I didn't want to be famous,\" {f} says at last. \"I wanted you to notice.\" It takes a while to put right.\nopt.right.say.2 [dc1db0] = 你走到哪儿都带着{f}，把{f.them}介绍给每一个人，而{f}变得越来越沉默。“我不想出名，”{f}终于说，“我想让你注意到我。”这花了好一阵子才弥补过来。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/spotlight_legend.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/spotlight_legend.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event spot_tavern_song\n# en: The Ballad of {player}\ntitle [05748c] = {player}之歌\n# en: The Newt has a new song, and it's about you. It has nine verses and a chorus everybody knows. In verse three you fight a frost giant on the lake. In verse six you marry a mermaid. The fiddler sees you in the doorway, bows, and starts again from the top, louder.\ntext [4519d3] = 蝾螈酒馆有了一首新歌，唱的是你。一共九段，副歌人人会唱。第三段里你在湖上大战冰霜巨人。第六段里你娶了一条美人鱼。拉小提琴的人看见你站在门口，鞠了一躬，从头又唱了一遍，声音更响了。\n# en: A packed tavern, a fiddler standing on a bench, the whole room singing along with raised mugs, one student by the door with their face in their hands.\nscene [9f912b] = 挤满人的酒馆里，一个小提琴手站在长凳上，满屋子的人举着杯子跟着唱，门边一个学生双手捂着脸。\n# en: Teach them the true verses\nopt.left.label [d96461] = 教他们唱真实的段落\n# en: You climb on a bench and sing them what really happened, which rhymes much worse. The room listens politely, then goes back to the mermaid. But the fiddler keeps one of your verses, the true one about the night you got lost, and it's the one that makes people quiet.\nopt.left.say.1 [653fba] = 你爬上长凳，把真实发生的事唱给他们听，押韵差得多。满屋子的人礼貌地听完，又回去唱美人鱼了。不过小提琴手留下了你的一段，那段真实的，关于你迷路的那个夜晚，每次唱到那里，大家都会安静下来。\n# en: Let the legend grow\nopt.right.label [0388a5] = 让传说越传越大\n# en: By the next week the song has a tenth verse, in which you are cruel to a friend, by name. It isn't true. It is very catchy. The friend hears it at breakfast, and you spend a long evening putting it right.\nopt.right.say.1 [ad2b40] = 到下个星期，这首歌多了第十段，唱你对一个朋友很刻薄，还点了名。那不是真的。可它非常上口。那位朋友在早餐时听到了，你花了一整个晚上才把事情弥补过来。\n# en: You buy the fiddler a drink and let it grow. By spring there are fourteen verses, two dances and a version for children. You have never fought a frost giant. Half the village is sure you did.\nopt.right.say.2 [be67bb] = 你请小提琴手喝了一杯，让歌继续长下去。到了春天，它已经有十四段、两支舞，还有一个儿童版。你从没跟冰霜巨人打过架。半个村子都确信你打过。\n\n@event spot_witness_promise\n# en: Stand Witness\ntitle [307e88] = 做个见证\n# en: {a} and {b} find you together, very nervous, holding hands. They've decided to make each other a promise, a proper one, at the lakeshore at dawn, the old way. They'd like a witness. \"It has to be you,\" {a} says. \"If you saw it, everyone will know it happened.\"\ntext [544a98] = {a}和{b}一起来找你，紧张得很，手牵着手。他们决定向彼此许下一个承诺，一个郑重的承诺，按老规矩，在黎明的湖岸边。他们想要一个见证人。“非你不可，”{a}说，“只要你看见了，大家就都会知道这是真的。”\n# en: A lakeshore at dawn, two students holding hands on the shingle, a third student standing before them holding a lantern, mist on the water.\nscene [8d845a] = 黎明时的湖岸，两个学生手牵手站在卵石滩上，第三个学生提着灯站在他们面前，水面上笼着薄雾。\n# en: Make it a ceremony\nopt.left.label [1a90b5] = 办成一场仪式\n# en: You find the right words, short and true, and say them over the water as the sun comes up. {a} cries. {b} pretends not to. By breakfast the whole castle knows, and it's the happiest thing anyone has talked about all term.\nopt.left.say.1 [1709c3] = 你找到了恰当的话，简短而真诚，在太阳升起时对着湖水说了出来。{a}哭了。{b}假装没哭。到早饭时整个城堡都知道了，这是这学期大家谈论过的最开心的事。\n# en: You'd prepared a speech, and halfway through it you lose your place, and a duck lands in the shallows and starts shouting. {a} and {b} laugh so hard they have to hold each other up. It's perfect, they tell you. It is not what you planned.\nopt.left.say.2 [31b580] = 你准备了一篇讲话，讲到一半忘了词，一只鸭子落在浅水里大叫起来。{a}和{b}笑得只能互相扶着。他们告诉你，这很完美。这可不是你计划的样子。\n# en: Keep it quiet and simple\nopt.right.label [6034ba] = 简简单单，悄悄进行\n# en: \"Just us, then,\" you say, and it is: three people and a lantern on the shingle, and the promise said plainly. Nobody else knows for a week. When it comes out, {a} and {b} only smile, because it was theirs first.\nopt.right.say.1 [5aa39b] = “那就我们三个，”你说，事实也是如此：三个人，一盏灯，卵石滩上，承诺说得朴朴实实。一个星期都没别人知道。等消息传开，{a}和{b}只是笑笑，因为它先是属于他们的。\n\n@event spot_flourish_named\n# en: The {player}\ntitle [68b19f] = “{player}式”\n# en: There's a wand flourish going round the practice yards, a sort of showy twist before the cast, and everybody calls it \"the {player}\". You've never done it in your life. It does nothing at all for the spell. Every first-year in the castle is doing it.\ntext [64028f] = 练习场上流行起一种魔杖花式，施法前来一下花哨的转动，大家都管它叫“{player}式”。你这辈子从没这么做过。它对法术毫无用处。城堡里每个一年级生都在这么做。\n# en: A practice yard full of students all flicking their wands in the same showy twist, one older student watching with arms folded.\nscene [36dfc6] = 练习场上满是学生，人人都在用同一个花哨的动作甩着魔杖，一个年长的学生抱着胳膊在一旁看着。\n# en: Teach them a flourish that helps\nopt.left.label [635f42] = 教他们一个有用的花式\n# en: You spend three afternoons in the practice yard showing them a real one, a small turn of the wrist that steadies the cast. It's much less showy. The first-years are disappointed, and then their spells start landing, and they call that one \"the {player}\" instead.\nopt.left.say.1 [9bb90e] = 你花了三个下午在练习场上教他们一个真正的花式，手腕轻轻一转，能让施法更稳。它一点都不花哨。一年级生们很失望，然后他们的法术开始命中了，于是他们改叫这一个“{player}式”。\n# en: Let them have it\nopt.right.label [192387] = 随他们去\n# en: A first-year doing \"the {player}\" too enthusiastically puts the twist through the greenhouse glass. Professor Thorne asks, in a voice like frost, whose name it is. Everybody looks at you.\nopt.right.say.1 [14450f] = 一个一年级生太卖力地做“{player}式”，一下子把温室的玻璃打穿了。索恩教授用冰霜般的声音问，这是谁的名字。所有人都看向你。\n# en: You let them have it. It's harmless, and it makes the practice yards look like a dance. Once, passing, you do it yourself, very solemnly, and the whole yard cheers.\nopt.right.say.2 [e6b0c4] = 你随他们去了。这无伤大雅，还让练习场看起来像在跳舞。有一次路过时，你自己也郑重其事地做了一遍，整个练习场都欢呼起来。\n\n@event spot_named_child\n# en: A Baby Called {player}\ntitle [7d6388] = 一个叫{player}的婴儿\n# en: A letter from the village: the blacksmith's daughter has had a baby, and they've named it after you, and would you, if it's not too much, come to the naming? There'll be cake. The letter is spelled very carefully, as if it was copied out three times.\ntext [308be4] = 村里来了一封信：铁匠的女儿生了个孩子，用你的名字起了名，问你，如果不太麻烦的话，能不能来参加命名礼？会有蛋糕。信上的字写得非常仔细，像是誊抄了三遍。\n# en: A cottage doorway in the village, a young couple holding a swaddled baby, an academy student on the step holding a small wrapped gift.\nscene [b18bad] = 村里一间小屋的门口，一对年轻夫妇抱着襁褓中的婴儿，一个学院学生站在台阶上，手里拿着一份包好的小礼物。\n# en: Go to the naming\nopt.left.label [e03fad] = 去参加命名礼\n# en: You go down the hill with a silver spoon you can't really afford. The baby is red and furious and has your name. You hold it for the whole naming and it falls asleep on you, which the village takes as a very good sign.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f78a2] = 你带着一把自己其实买不起的银勺子下了山。婴儿红通通的，怒气冲冲，名字和你一样。整个命名礼你都抱着它，它在你怀里睡着了，全村人都认为这是个非常好的兆头。\n# en: Send a gift and a letter\nopt.right.label [dae1fc] = 寄一份礼物和一封信\n# en: You can't get away, so you send a silver spoon and a long letter, to be read to the child when it's older, about the hill and the lake and the castle. The blacksmith's daughter writes back to say it's in a drawer, waiting.\nopt.right.say.1 [5f5c8f] = 你走不开，于是寄去了一把银勺子和一封长信，等孩子长大了念给它听，信里写了山丘、湖和城堡。铁匠的女儿回信说，信放在抽屉里，等着。\n\n@event spot_disguise_day\n# en: An Ordinary Afternoon\ntitle [07b5dc] = 一个普通的下午\n# en: You haven't bought a loaf of bread in a month without someone asking you to sign it. Today you borrow a hat, a scarf and somebody's terrible coat, pull them all up, and walk down to the village to see if you can have one ordinary afternoon.\ntext [3db730] = 一个月来，你买一条面包都会有人请你在上面签名。今天你借了一顶帽子、一条围巾和不知谁的一件难看外套，全都拉得严严实实，走下山去村里，看看能不能过一个普通的下午。\n# en: A student in a borrowed hat and a scarf pulled up to the nose walking through a busy village market, glancing sideways at the stalls.\nscene [268506] = 一个戴着借来的帽子、围巾拉到鼻子的学生走过熙熙攘攘的村集，斜眼打量着摊位。\n# en: Try it\nopt.left.label [c589d7] = 试试看\n# en: Nobody looks twice. You buy bread, argue with a stallholder over apples, sit on the quay and eat them, and walk back up the hill unrecognised, humming. It's the best afternoon you've had in a year.\nopt.left.say.1 [e91e06] = 没人多看你一眼。你买了面包，和摊主为苹果讨价还价，坐在码头上吃完，哼着歌走回山上，谁也没认出你。这是你一年来过得最好的一个下午。\n# en: You get as far as the bakery, where a small child points and says, very loudly, \"That's {player} in a silly hat.\" The whole street turns. You sign eleven loaves. The coat, for some reason, becomes fashionable.\nopt.left.say.2 [eba3bb] = 你只走到面包房，一个小孩就指着你大声说：“那是戴着傻帽子的{player}。”整条街都转过头来。你签了十一条面包。那件外套不知为什么流行了起来。\n# en: Stay in the castle\nopt.right.label [0285a8] = 待在城堡里\n# en: You stay in, and read, and look out of the window at the village, very small and far away. Somebody brings you bread from it, which is kind of them. It isn't the same.\nopt.right.say.1 [ac94fa] = 你待在城堡里读书，望着窗外的村子，那么小，那么远。有人从那里给你带了面包，真好心。可那不一样。\n\n@event spot_higher_bar\n# en: Because It's You\ntitle [aa5435] = 因为是你\n# en: Your essay comes back covered in red, with a mark lower than your neighbour's, though your neighbour's essay was, honestly, not as good. At the bottom the lecturer has written: \"From anyone else, excellent. From you, I expect more. Everybody is watching what you do.\"\ntext [017810] = 你的论文发回来时满是红字，分数比你邻座的还低，可老实说，邻座的论文没你的好。讲师在最后写道：“换了别人，这是优秀。换了你，我期待更多。大家都在看你做什么。”\n# en: A returned essay on a desk covered in red ink, a lecturer's note at the bottom, a student staring at it in disbelief beside a classmate's cleaner page.\nscene [e6932c] = 书桌上摊着一篇发回来的论文，满是红墨水，末尾有讲师的批语，一个学生难以置信地盯着它，旁边是同学那页干净得多的卷子。\n# en: Rise to it\nopt.left.label [46df16] = 迎难而上\n# en: You rewrite it from the first line, then rewrite the next one before it's due. It is the hardest you've worked all year. The lecturer's next note is one word, \"Better\", and you find you care about it more than any cheer in the Great Hall.\nopt.left.say.1 [26b607] = 你从第一行开始重写，交下一篇之前又重写了一遍。这是你这一年里最拼命的一次。讲师的下一条批语只有一个词：“更好”，你发现自己在乎它，胜过大礼堂里的任何欢呼。\n# en: Ask to be marked like anyone else\nopt.right.label [3e7730] = 要求和别人一样评分\n# en: You go to the lecturer's office and say, politely, that you'd like to be marked like everyone else. The lecturer looks at you over the spectacles, and then laughs, and changes the mark. \"Fair,\" the lecturer says. \"But I'll still be reading closely.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [f24362] = 你去讲师办公室，礼貌地说你希望和大家一样评分。讲师从眼镜上方看着你，然后笑了，改了分数。“说得公道，”讲师说，“不过我还是会仔细读的。”\n# en: You make your case. The lecturer hears you out and says that's exactly what a famous student would say. The mark stands. Worse, the story gets out, and for a week people call you \"the one who argued about the mark\".\nopt.right.say.2 [615a06] = 你据理力争。讲师听完，说这正是一个出名的学生会说的话。分数不变。更糟的是，这件事传了出去，整整一个星期，人们都叫你“那个为分数争论的人”。\n\n@event spot_history_essay\n# en: A Living Figure\ntitle [19bccb] = 一位在世人物\n# en: A lecturer has set the second-years an essay on \"a living figure in the academy's history\", and forty of them have chosen you. They want interviews. There is a queue outside your door, with notebooks, and one of them has brought a list of a hundred questions.\ntext [d0b5b7] = 一位讲师给二年级生布置了一篇论文，题目是“学院历史上的一位在世人物”，其中四十个人选了你。他们想采访你。你门外排起了队，人人拿着笔记本，其中一个带来了一张有一百个问题的清单。\n# en: A corridor outside a lecture hall, a queue of students holding notebooks and quills, one student at the front of it looking trapped.\nscene [f0059f] = 阶梯教室外的走廊里，一排学生拿着笔记本和羽毛笔，排在最前面的那个学生一脸被困住的样子。\n# en: Answer them all\nopt.left.label [7d9608] = 每个问题都回答\n# en: It takes a week of evenings. You answer every question, including the one about your favourite pudding and the one about whether you're afraid of dying. The essays are pinned up in the corridor. Two of them are better than anything you've written.\nopt.left.say.1 [ba66b5] = 这花了一个星期的晚上。你回答了每一个问题，包括你最喜欢的布丁是什么，以及你怕不怕死。论文被钉在了走廊上。其中有两篇比你写过的任何东西都好。\n# en: \"I'm not history yet\"\nopt.right.label [b49fb7] = “我还不是历史”\n# en: You tell them, cheerfully, that you're not history yet and might still do something stupid, and send them to the library to write about someone safely dead. Most of them laugh. The one with the hundred questions writes about you anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [b90439] = 你高高兴兴地告诉他们，你还不是历史，说不定还会干出什么蠢事，让他们去图书馆写某位已经稳稳当当去世的人。大多数人都笑了。那个带着一百个问题的人还是写了你。\n\n@event spot_villain_play\n# en: The Villain of Thornwick\ntitle [c0387f] = 荆木的反派\n# en: Some visiting Thornwick students are putting on a play in the village hall, and it's about you. In their version you are the villain: you cheat at duels, steal Thornwick's glory and have a moustache. The actor playing you hisses very well. A Lumenhold crowd has come along to boo.\ntext [f5302f] = 几个来访的荆木学生在村礼堂里演一出戏，讲的是你。在他们的版本里你是反派：决斗作弊，抢走荆木的荣光，还留着小胡子。演你的那个演员嘶嘶声做得非常好。一群流明堡的人专程去喝倒彩。\n# en: A makeshift stage in a village hall, a student actor in a black cape and a cardboard crown hissing at the audience, another student in the back row laughing into a scarf.\nscene [34afa2] = 村礼堂里搭起的简易舞台上，一个披黑斗篷、戴纸板王冠的学生演员朝观众嘶嘶作声，后排另一个学生把脸埋进围巾里笑。\n# en: Go, and laugh loudest\nopt.left.label [6c7ce1] = 去，笑得最响\n# en: You laugh loudest, and the actor, spotting you, improvises a whole scene about your real lost duels, which turns out to be well researched. The village laughs at you, not with you. Thornwick's paper prints the best lines.\nopt.left.say.1 [2d4566] = 你笑得最响，那个演员发现了你，即兴加了一整场关于你真实输掉的决斗的戏，看得出功课做得很足。全村人都在笑你，而不是和你一起笑。荆木的校报登出了最妙的台词。\n# en: You sit in the front row and laugh loudest, and at the curtain the actor bows to you, and you bow back, and the hall cheers both of you. Afterwards you buy the cast supper. The moustache, you admit, is a fair cop.\nopt.left.say.2 [a3c558] = 你坐在第一排，笑得最响，谢幕时演员向你鞠躬，你也回了一躬，全场为你们俩欢呼。散场后你请全体演员吃了晚饭。那撇小胡子，你承认，演得没毛病。\n# en: Stay away\nopt.right.label [91c94a] = 不去\n# en: You stay away, which turns out to be the right thing, because the play without you is merely silly. Your friends come back with a programme and a very good impression of your hiss, which you have never once made.\nopt.right.say.1 [dc62d7] = 你没去，结果这是对的，因为没有你在场，这出戏只是傻气而已。你的朋友们带回来一份节目单，还把你的嘶嘶声学得惟妙惟肖，而你一次都没那样嘶过。\n\n@event spot_rival_lucky\n# en: Just Lucky\ntitle [4948f3] = 只是运气好\n# en: {r} stops you in the corridor. \"Everyone says you're a legend,\" {they} say{s}. \"I think you're lucky. You were in the right place, at the right time, a lot. Someone should say it to your face.\" {r} doesn't look angry. {They} look{s} as if {they} had been waiting a long time to say it.\ntext [7e95e9] = {r}在走廊里拦住了你。“大家都说你是传奇，”{they}说，“我觉得你只是运气好。你很多次都恰好在对的时间出现在对的地方。总该有人当面告诉你。”{r}看起来并不生气。{They}看起来像是等着说这句话已经等了很久。\n# en: A quiet corridor late in the day, two students facing each other, one leaning against the wall with arms folded, the other holding a stack of books.\nscene [61f0d8] = 傍晚安静的走廊里，两个学生面对面，一个抱着胳膊靠在墙上，另一个抱着一摞书。\n# en: Agree that a lot of it was luck\nopt.left.label [eb16a8] = 承认很多都是运气\n# en: \"A lot of it was,\" you say. {r} blinks. You tell {r.them} which bits, honestly. By the end {r} is half laughing. \"That's worse,\" {they} say{s}. \"That's so much worse.\" It's the start of something like respect, on both sides.\nopt.left.say.1 [bfec34] = “很多确实是，”你说。{r}眨了眨眼。你老老实实地告诉{r.them}是哪些部分。到最后{r}都快笑出来了。“那更糟，”{they}说，“糟多了。”这是一种类似敬意的东西的开始，双方都是。\n# en: Tell {r} to try being lucky\nopt.right.label [642855] = 让{r}也试试好运气\n# en: \"Try it,\" you say. \"See how lucky it feels.\" {r} looks at you for a long moment and walks off. You win the argument. It doesn't feel like it, for the rest of the day.\nopt.right.say.1 [141300] = “你试试，”你说，“看看这运气是什么滋味。”{r}看了你好一会儿，走开了。你赢了这场争论。那一整天，却没有赢了的感觉。\n\n@event spot_guest_lecture\n# en: Take the Class\ntitle [5a6785] = 你来上这堂课\n# en: A lecturer has asked you to take the first-years' class for an hour: \"They listen to you. They certainly don't listen to me.\" Sixty first-years, the chalk, the lectern. The lecturer will be in the back row, taking notes on you.\ntext [c04288] = 一位讲师请你给一年级生上一小时的课：“他们听你的。他们肯定不听我的。”六十个一年级生，粉笔，讲台。讲师会坐在最后一排，记你的笔记。\n# en: A lecture hall full of first-years in rows, an older student at the lectern holding chalk, a professor sitting in the back row with a notebook.\nscene [0d6928] = 阶梯教室里坐满了一排排一年级生，一个年长的学生拿着粉笔站在讲台边，一位教授坐在最后一排拿着笔记本。\n# en: Teach it properly\nopt.left.label [ef9064] = 好好讲\n# en: You teach them the thing you found hardest, the way you wish someone had taught you, and they get it. All sixty. The lecturer comes down at the end, looks at the board for a long time, and copies it into the notebook.\nopt.left.say.1 [ce2ad2] = 你教了他们你当初觉得最难的东西，用你希望当年有人教你的方式，他们听懂了。六十个人全懂了。最后讲师走下来，盯着黑板看了很久，把它抄进了笔记本。\n# en: You start well, and lose them at the second diagram, and never get them back. By the end the first-years are passing notes about your hair. The lecturer takes the last ten minutes and says, kindly, that teaching is harder than it looks.\nopt.left.say.2 [12af10] = 你开头不错，到第二张图就把他们讲丢了，再也没找回来。到最后，一年级生们在传纸条讨论你的发型。讲师接过了最后十分钟，和气地说，教书比看起来难。\n# en: Tell stories instead\nopt.right.label [5ac9cd] = 改讲故事\n# en: You put the chalk down and tell them stories: the lake, the stairs, the first time a spell went right. They hang on every word. They learn almost nothing about the syllabus. A few of them, you suspect, will stay up late tonight practising.\nopt.right.say.1 [8a1ba3] = 你放下粉笔，给他们讲故事：湖，楼梯，第一次法术成功的那一刻。他们听得入了迷。关于课程大纲，他们几乎什么也没学到。你猜，他们当中有几个今晚会熬夜练习。\n\n@event spot_legend_burden\n# en: A Legend Can't Miss\ntitle [d74310] = 传奇不能失手\n# en: In practice, in front of the class, an easy spell goes wrong in your hands. It just fizzles, the way spells do for everyone, now and then. But the whole room gasps as if the sky had fallen, and you can feel them all waiting to see what you'll do.\ntext [031d02] = 练习课上，当着全班的面，一个简单的法术在你手里出了岔子。只是熄了火，就像每个人的法术偶尔都会的那样。可整个房间倒抽一口凉气，仿佛天塌了下来，你能感觉到他们都在等着看你会怎么做。\n# en: A practice room, a student's spell fizzling into smoke, a whole class turned to stare in shocked silence.\nscene [6caa23] = 练习室里，一个学生的法术化作一缕青烟熄灭，全班转过头来，震惊地一片寂静。\n# en: Laugh, and try again\nopt.left.label [3c9cce] = 笑一笑，再来一次\n# en: You laugh, say \"well, that's a relief\", and try again, and get it. The room laughs with you. Afterwards a first-year says it was the best thing they'd seen all year: that even you miss. You decide that's worth something.\nopt.left.say.1 [223845] = 你笑了，说了句“这下放心了”，又试了一次，成功了。全屋的人跟着你笑了。事后一个一年级生说，这是自己这一年里见过最棒的事：原来连你也会失手。你觉得这还挺有价值的。\n# en: Practise until it never happens again\nopt.right.label [e159f6] = 练到再也不会出错\n# en: You stay behind and cast it a hundred times, until your arm aches and it couldn't fail if it tried. It never happens again, in front of anyone. Sometimes you wish it would, so you could stop being so careful.\nopt.right.say.1 [00b7a2] = 你留下来把它施放了一百遍，直到胳膊发酸，想失败都难。在任何人面前，它再也没出过错。有时你倒希望它出一次错，这样你就不用那么小心翼翼了。\n\n@event renown_fallen_entry\n# en: Yesterday's News\ntitle [7759f8] = 昨日新闻\n# en: You notice it on a Tuesday. You walk the length of the long corridor and nobody turns. In the common room, a copy of the Ledger with your face on the front is being used to light the fire. Not long ago you couldn't buy bread without signing it. Now you're just somebody who used to be somebody.\ntext [a82872] = 你是在一个星期二注意到的。你走完了整条长廊，没有一个人转头。公共休息室里，一份头版印着你的脸的《纪事报》正被拿来生火。不久之前，你买条面包都得签名。现在你只是一个曾经是个人物的人。\n# en: A student walking through a busy corridor that doesn't part for them, a crumpled old copy of the Ledger with their face on it lying in a bin.\nscene [42e691] = 一个学生走过熙熙攘攘的走廊，人群不再为这个学生让路，一份印着这个学生的脸的旧《纪事报》皱巴巴地躺在垃圾桶里。\n# en: Enjoy the quiet\nopt.left.label [293287] = 享受这份清静\n# en: It's wonderful, mostly. You eat your lunch where you like. You walk to the lake without an escort. Now and then somebody squints at you and says \"weren't you...?\", and you say \"no\", and they believe you.\nopt.left.say.1 [3b08a9] = 大部分时候，这感觉好极了。你想在哪儿吃午饭就在哪儿吃。你走去湖边，不再有人簇拥。偶尔有人眯着眼睛看你说“你不是那个……？”，你说“不是”，他们就信了。\n# en: Plan the comeback\nopt.right.label [b51854] = 策划东山再起\n# en: You sit down with a sheet of paper and write a list of everything that made people talk. Some of it you'd do again. Some of it you can't believe you did. You fold the paper very small and keep it in your pocket, just in case.\nopt.right.say.1 [cf426e] = 你坐下来，拿出一张纸，列出所有曾让人们议论的事。有些你会再做一次。有些你简直不敢相信自己做过。你把那张纸折得很小，揣在口袋里，以防万一。\n\n@event fallen_old_fan\n# en: The Fan Who Stayed\ntitle [8193c3] = 留下来的那个粉丝\n# en: Most of the first-years who used to queue for you have found somebody else. But one of them, older now, stops you in the courtyard and says, a bit shyly, \"I still think you were the best one. For what it's worth.\" {chosen(spot_fan_charm, left): You recognise the blue thread on their wrist.|They've still got your name written inside the cover of a book.}\ntext [fec067] = 曾经为你排队的一年级生大多另找了别人。但其中一个，如今大了些，在庭院里拦住你，有点害羞地说：“我还是觉得你是最好的那个。不管这话值多少。”{chosen(spot_fan_charm, left): 你认出了对方手腕上的蓝线。|对方的一本书封皮里还写着你的名字。}\n# en: A first-year holding a frayed charm on a ribbon, standing a little awkwardly in front of an older student in a quiet courtyard.\nscene [a37d87] = 安静的庭院里，一个一年级生手里攥着一个系在缎带上、已经磨毛的护身符，有点局促地站在一个年长的学生面前。\n# en: Ask how they're getting on\nopt.left.label [499c6b] = 问问对方过得怎么样\n# en: You ask about their lectures, their friends, the spell they couldn't get. They tell you everything. It turns out they've been doing very well, and nobody has asked. You walk them to supper and they talk the whole way.\nopt.left.say.1 [721e5a] = 你问起对方的课、朋友，还有那个怎么也学不会的法术。对方什么都告诉了你。原来对方一直过得很好，只是没人问过。你陪对方走去吃晚饭，一路上对方说个不停。\n# en: Thank them, and keep walking\nopt.right.label [d12fa6] = 道声谢，继续走\n# en: \"That's very kind,\" you say, and mean it, and keep walking, because you're late. It's only later that you realise they'd wanted to say more. The next time you look for them, they're with a crowd of their own.\nopt.right.say.1 [59175f] = “你真好，”你说，也是真心的，然后继续走了，因为你要迟到了。直到后来你才意识到，对方还想多说几句。下一次你去找对方时，对方已经和自己的一群朋友在一起了。\n\n@event fallen_where_now\n# en: Where Are They Now?\ntitle [612d31] = 他们现在在哪儿？\n# en: Nessa wants to run a piece: \"Where Are They Now?\", a look back at the academy's brightest stars, starring you. \"It's not unkind,\" she says. \"It's nostalgic. People love a comeback. Or a cautionary tale. You can choose which.\"\ntext [83d76b] = 内莎想做一篇报道：《他们现在在哪儿？》，回顾学院最耀眼的明星，主角是你。“不是刻薄的那种，”她说，“是怀旧的。人们爱看东山再起。或者警世故事。你可以选哪一种。”\n# en: A newspaper office at dusk, an editor with a quill leaning across a desk toward a student who has their arms folded.\nscene [89dba4] = 黄昏时的报社办公室，一位编辑握着羽毛笔隔着桌子探身向一个抱着胳膊的学生。\n# en: Give her the comeback\nopt.left.label [2a8b4b] = 给她一个东山再起的故事\n# en: You give her a new plan and an old smile, and the piece runs on the front page: \"{player}: not done yet\". By the end of the week people are turning in corridors again, cautiously, as if they'd forgotten how.\nopt.left.say.1 [f17c98] = 你给了她一个新计划和一个老笑容，报道登上了头版：《{player}：尚未落幕》。到周末，走廊里的人又开始转头了，小心翼翼的，像是忘了该怎么转。\n# en: You try to sound like someone with a plan. It comes out sounding like someone with a list. The piece runs on page six, between the weather and the crossword, under the heading \"fading gracefully?\"\nopt.left.say.2 [666bee] = 你努力让自己听起来像个有计划的人。结果听起来像个有清单的人。报道登在第六版，夹在天气和填字游戏之间，标题是《体面地淡出？》。\n# en: Ask her to leave you out\nopt.right.label [f31056] = 请她别写你\n# en: \"Leave me out of it,\" you say. \"I like it here, out of the paper.\" Nessa looks at you for a long moment and puts her quill down. \"That,\" she says, \"is the most interesting thing you've ever told me. Fine.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [830cd9] = “别写我了，”你说，“我喜欢待在报纸外面。”内莎看了你好一会儿，放下了羽毛笔。“这个，”她说，“是你告诉过我的最有意思的事。好吧。”\n\n@event fallen_unrecognised\n# en: Just Another Student\ntitle [0b100f] = 又一个普通学生\n# en: In the village, the baker who used to lean out of the window and shout your name sells you a loaf, gives you your change and says \"next, please\" without looking up. Outside, the children who used to follow you are following somebody else, over by the fountain.\ntext [f9f06d] = 在村里，那个曾经从窗口探出身来喊你名字的面包师卖给你一条面包，找了零钱，头也不抬地说“下一位”。外面，以前跟着你的孩子们正跟着另一个人，在喷泉那边。\n# en: A village bakery counter, a baker handing a loaf to a student without a second glance, a short queue behind.\nscene [4502e2] = 村里面包房的柜台前，面包师看都没看就把一条面包递给一个学生，身后排着一小队人。\n# en: Laugh about it\nopt.left.label [850c3c] = 一笑置之\n# en: You laugh, out loud, in the street, and a dog barks at you. You eat the end of the loaf on the quay and watch the boats, and nobody comes to ask you anything at all, and it's lovely.\nopt.left.say.1 [0a059f] = 你在街上笑出了声，一条狗冲你叫了起来。你坐在码头上吃完面包的最后一截，看着船，没有一个人来问你任何事，这真好。\n# en: Remind the baker who you are\nopt.right.label [8f8693] = 提醒面包师你是谁\n# en: \"It's me,\" you say. \"From the Ledger.\" The baker squints, says \"oh, yes\", and gives you a bun, out of pity. You eat it on the way back up the hill, slowly. It tastes of nothing much.\nopt.right.say.1 [c72fab] = “是我啊，”你说，“《纪事报》上那个。”面包师眯着眼睛说“哦，对”，出于同情给了你一个小面包。你在回山上的路上慢慢吃着。没什么味道。\n\n@event fallen_mocked\n# en: Weren't You Somebody?\ntitle [3185a3] = 你以前不是挺有名的吗？\n# en: At lunch, someone at the next table says, just loud enough, \"Weren't you somebody once?\" The table laughs. A couple of people at your own table laugh too, then look as if they wish they hadn't. Everyone waits to see whether you'll answer.\ntext [fda369] = 午饭时，邻桌有人刚好用能让人听见的声音说：“你以前不是挺有名的吗？”那一桌笑了起来。你们这桌也有几个人笑了，随即又一副后悔的样子。所有人都在等着看你会不会回答。\n# en: A crowded refectory, a student at the next table pointing with a fork, a ripple of laughter, one student sitting very straight with their tray.\nscene [32f73e] = 拥挤的膳堂里，邻桌一个学生用叉子指着这边，笑声一阵阵传开，一个学生端着托盘坐得笔直。\n# en: Answer with a joke\nopt.left.label [5a4c5d] = 用玩笑回应\n# en: \"I was,\" you say. \"Then I got hungry.\" You hold up your pudding. The whole refectory laughs, at the right person this time, and somebody at the next table actually applauds.\nopt.left.say.1 [3dcdbb] = “是啊，”你说，“后来我饿了。”你举起你的布丁。整个膳堂都笑了，这一次笑对了人，邻桌甚至有人鼓起了掌。\n# en: Your joke comes out a beat too late and a little too sharp. Nobody laughs. The next table goes back to its lunch, satisfied. You eat yours very quickly.\nopt.left.say.2 [89d4a7] = 你的玩笑慢了半拍，也太尖刻了一点。没人笑。邻桌心满意足地继续吃饭。你飞快地吃完了你的。\n# en: Say nothing, and eat your lunch\nopt.right.label [01cc6f] = 一言不发，吃你的饭\n# en: You say nothing and eat your lunch, and in a minute they've moved on to something else. A friend at your table kicks you gently under it and passes you their pudding. You take it.\nopt.right.say.1 [dd1a35] = 你什么也没说，吃你的午饭，没过一分钟他们就转到别的话题上了。你们这桌的一个朋友在桌子底下轻轻踢了你一下，把自己的布丁递给了你。你收下了。\n\n@event fallen_friends\n# en: The Ones Who Stayed\ntitle [5337c3] = 留下来的人\n# en: The invitations have stopped. The crowd has gone. But on Thursday night, as always, {f} has pulled out the chair in the corner of the common room, and the cards are dealt, and the cocoa is going cold. \"You're late,\" {f} says. \"We waited.\"\ntext [eca721] = 邀请停了。人群散了。可到了星期四晚上，像往常一样，{f}在公共休息室的角落里拉开了那把椅子，牌已经发好，可可正在变凉。“你来晚了，”{f}说，“我们一直在等你。”\n# en: A small group of students around a table in a quiet corner of a common room late at night, cards scattered, cups of cocoa, one chair pulled out for someone arriving.\nscene [4451aa] = 深夜公共休息室安静的角落里，一小群学生围坐在桌旁，纸牌散落，可可杯摆着，一把椅子被拉开，等着某个人到来。\n# en: Sit down and stay late\nopt.left.label [58598f] = 坐下，待到很晚\n# en: You sit down and stay until the fire goes out. Nobody asks you about anything you did. They ask what you think, about everything, and you realise you'd almost forgotten anybody wanted to know.\nopt.left.say.1 [f95abc] = 你坐下来，一直待到炉火熄灭。没人问你做过什么。他们问你怎么看，什么都问，你这才意识到，你几乎忘了还有人想知道你的想法。\n# en: Say you've been a poor friend\nopt.right.label [5ba912] = 说你最近是个差劲的朋友\n# en: \"I've been a poor friend,\" you say. {f} shrugs. \"You were busy being famous.\" Then, more quietly: \"You're back now.\" {f} deals you in, and you lose, as usual, and it's the best evening in months.\nopt.right.say.1 [5034e4] = “我最近是个差劲的朋友，”你说。{f}耸了耸肩。“你忙着出名呢。”然后，声音低了些：“现在你回来了。”{f}给你发了牌，你照旧输了，这是几个月来最好的一个晚上。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/spotlight.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/spotlight.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event renown_famous_entry\n# en: Your Name in Every Mouth\ntitle [b3b06c] = 人人口中的名字\n# en: Somewhere this month it stopped being \"have you heard of {player}\" and became just \"{player}\". Corridors go quiet and then loud as you pass. The Ledger has stopped explaining who you are. You are, it seems, famous: the academy watches what you do now, and remembers.\ntext [62a380] = 这个月的某一天，人们不再说“你听说过{player}吗”，而是直接说“{player}”。你走过时，走廊先是一静，然后又热闹起来。《纪事报》已经不再解释你是谁。看来，你出名了：如今整个学院都在看你做什么，而且都记得。\n# en: A crowded corridor parting around a student, heads turning in a wave, a first-year pointing and being shushed.\nscene [f95646] = 拥挤的走廊在一个学生面前分开，一颗颗脑袋像波浪一样转过来，一个一年级生伸手去指，被人嘘了回去。\n# en: Enjoy it\nopt.left.label [8993d9] = 好好享受\n# en: You let yourself enjoy it, for a week. The seats are better. The queues are shorter. Someone gives you the last slice of pudding without being asked. It is, you admit, very nice.\nopt.left.say.1 [9dcedc] = 你允许自己享受了一个星期。座位更好了，队伍更短了，有人不等你开口就把最后一块布丁让给你。你承认，这感觉非常好。\n# en: Keep your head down\nopt.right.label [b59315] = 保持低调\n# en: You keep to the library and the back row. It doesn't make much difference: people find you anyway. But you get a great deal of reading done, and they come away saying you're modest, which only makes it worse.\nopt.right.say.1 [3e5e17] = 你待在图书馆和最后一排。这没什么用：人们照样找得到你。不过你读了很多书，而他们回去都说你很谦虚，这反倒更糟。\n\n@event renown_legend_entry\n# en: The Academy's Own Story\ntitle [e990cd] = 学院自己的故事\n# en: First-years tell stories about you that you don't recognise. A third-year swears you once did something you have never done, and the version where you did it is better. When you come into the Great Hall, it isn't quiet and it isn't loud: it simply watches. You've become part of what Lumenhold says about itself.\ntext [fbe263] = 一年级生讲着关于你的故事，你自己都认不出来。一个三年级生发誓说你做过一件你从没做过的事，而你做了的那个版本更精彩。你走进大礼堂时，那里既不安静也不喧闹：只是看着你。你已经成了流明堡讲述自己时的一部分。\n# en: A great hall at supper, every head at every table turned toward one student coming in through the doors, as if a story had walked in.\nscene [ef10af] = 晚餐时的大礼堂，每张桌子上的每一颗脑袋都转向刚从门口进来的一个学生，仿佛走进来的是一个故事。\n# en: Live up to it\nopt.left.label [4e38ae] = 不负其名\n# en: You start getting up earlier, working later, being kinder in corridors, because people are watching and you'd like the story to be true. It mostly is. It's exhausting. It's worth it.\nopt.left.say.1 [216c0c] = 你开始起得更早，睡得更晚，在走廊里待人更和气，因为人们在看，而你希望那些故事是真的。大部分确实是。这很累人。也很值得。\n# en: Correct the stories\nopt.right.label [ea70ff] = 纠正那些故事\n# en: You start telling people, cheerfully, which bits never happened. It makes no difference whatever to the stories. It makes a great deal of difference to the people who hear you say it.\nopt.right.say.1 [b42bce] = 你开始高高兴兴地告诉别人，哪些部分从没发生过。这对故事本身毫无影响。对听你这么说的人，影响却很大。\n\n@event spot_ledger_profile\n# en: A Profile in the Ledger\ntitle [5e7e90] = 《纪事报》人物专访\n# en: Nessa wants you for the Ledger: a whole column, \"Who Is {player}?\", with an interview. Her quill is already hovering. \"Tell me everything,\" she says. \"Or tell me something better than everything. Readers can't tell the difference. I can.\"\ntext [3e4f3f] = 内莎想让你上《纪事报》：整整一栏，《{player}是谁？》，还要专访。她的羽毛笔已经悬在半空。“全告诉我，”她说，“或者告诉我比全部更精彩的东西。读者分不出来。我分得出来。”\n# en: A cluttered newspaper office, an editor with a self-writing quill hovering over a notebook, a student in the visitor's chair looking uneasy.\nscene [3ac32d] = 杂乱的报社办公室里，一位编辑的自动羽毛笔悬在笔记本上方，来访者椅子上的学生显得有些不安。\n# en: Be candid\nopt.left.label [af726f] = 实话实说\n# en: You tell her the truth, including the dull parts and one embarrassing one. The column is warm and true and a little bit unflattering. People like you more for it. You wish they'd stop quoting the embarrassing part.\nopt.left.say.1 [bb5e93] = 你把真话告诉了她，包括无聊的部分和一件丢脸的事。那篇专栏温暖、真实，还有一点不那么体面。人们因此更喜欢你了。你只希望他们别再引用那件丢脸的事。\n# en: Polish the story\nopt.right.label [b04a62] = 润色一下\n# en: You give her a better version of yourself: braver, wittier, slightly taller. Nessa writes it all down with a look you can't read. The column is a sensation. Some of it is even true.\nopt.right.say.1 [d8e4c5] = 你给了她一个更好的你：更勇敢，更机智，还稍微高一点。内莎全记了下来，神情让你捉摸不透。专栏轰动一时。其中有些甚至是真的。\n# en: Nessa stops writing halfway through the story about the dragon. \"There was no dragon,\" she says. The column runs anyway, short and faintly amused. \"We checked,\" it says, twice.\nopt.right.say.2 [dbd49e] = 你讲到那条龙的时候，内莎停了笔。“根本没有龙，”她说。专栏照样登了，短短一篇，带着淡淡的笑意。“我们核实过了，”文中写道，写了两遍。\n\n@event spot_judge_exhibition\n# en: A Judge for the First-Years\ntitle [1ac9e2] = 一年级的评委\n# en: The first-years' duelling exhibition needs a judge, and they've asked for you by name. Twelve bouts, a scorecard, a little brass bell. Every first-year in the hall wants to impress you. Every one of their friends is in the gallery.\ntext [0cae44] = 一年级的决斗表演赛需要一位评委，他们点名要你。十二场比试，一张记分卡，一只小铜铃。场上每个一年级生都想给你留下好印象。他们的每一个朋友都坐在看台上。\n# en: A duelling floor ringed by nervous first-years in practice robes, a student at a small table with a scorecard and a bell.\nscene [05425b] = 决斗场地四周围着穿练习袍的紧张一年级生，一个学生坐在小桌后，面前放着记分卡和一只铃。\n# en: Judge it strictly\nopt.left.label [2a93bd] = 严格评判\n# en: You score every bout to the rules, including the one where the favourite lost on a foot fault. The gallery boos. The first-year who won on the foot fault looks at you as if you'd hung the moon.\nopt.left.say.1 [298d53] = 你每一场都按规则打分，包括热门选手因为脚步犯规而输掉的那场。看台上一片嘘声。靠对方脚步犯规赢下来的那个一年级生看着你，就像你为TA摘下了月亮。\n# en: Play to the crowd\nopt.right.label [fc1a82] = 迎合观众\n# en: You ring the bell with a flourish, give a speech between bouts, and somehow every first-year comes away with a prize for something. The hall loves it. Two of the duellists who deserved to win know they were robbed.\nopt.right.say.1 [e6b54b] = 你花哨地摇铃，在比试之间发表演说，结果每个一年级生都因为某件事拿了奖。全场都爱死了。两个本该获胜的决斗者知道自己被抢了。\n\n@event spot_village_recognised\n# en: Is It You?\ntitle [606872] = 是你吗？\n# en: In the village, a baker leans out of the window and says, \"It's you, isn't it? From the Ledger?\" By the time you've said yes, there are children, a dog, and an old man who wants to tell you what the academy was like in his day.\ntext [efb1c8] = 在村子里，一个面包师从窗口探出身来说：“是你吧？《纪事报》上那个？”等你说完“是”，身边已经围了一群孩子、一条狗，还有一位老人，想告诉你他那个年代的学院是什么样子。\n# en: A village square, a baker leaning out of a shop window with a tray of buns, children gathering round a student in academy robes.\nscene [03c34d] = 村子的广场上，面包师端着一盘小圆面包从店窗里探出身，孩子们围着一个穿学院长袍的学生。\n# en: Stay and talk\nopt.left.label [430859] = 留下来聊聊\n# en: You stay the afternoon. You eat a free bun, admire the dog, and hear three versions of the old man's story. You walk back up the hill much later than you meant to, with flour on your sleeve.\nopt.left.say.1 [465f8b] = 你待了一下午。吃了一个免费的面包，夸了那条狗，听了老人的故事的三个版本。你走回山上时比原打算晚了很多，袖子上沾着面粉。\n# en: Smile and slip away\nopt.right.label [1eb8d6] = 笑一笑，悄悄溜走\n# en: You thank the baker, buy a bun like anyone else, and slip away before the crowd thickens. Behind you, the old man is telling the story to the dog.\nopt.right.say.1 [205b2f] = 你谢过面包师，像别人一样买了个面包，在人群变多之前溜走了。身后，那位老人正把故事讲给那条狗听。\n\n@event spot_fan_charm\n# en: A Charm From a Stranger\ntitle [0059fc] = 陌生人的护身符\n# en: A first-year you've never spoken to waits outside your lecture to give you a charm: a little knot of blue thread on a ribbon, clearly made by hand, clearly not very well. \"For luck,\" the first-year says, to the floor. \"I made it. You don't have to wear it.\"\ntext [41e307] = 一个你从没说过话的一年级生在讲堂外等你，要送你一个护身符：一小团蓝线打的结，系在缎带上，一看就是手工做的，一看就做得不太好。“保佑你的，”那个一年级生对着地板说，“我自己做的。你不用戴。”\n# en: A first-year holding out a small knotted charm on a ribbon at arm's length, looking at the floor, an older student bending to take it.\nscene [540def] = 一个一年级生伸直手臂递出一个系在缎带上的小绳结护身符，眼睛盯着地面，一个年长的学生弯腰去接。\n# en: Wear it where people can see\nopt.left.label [ab1b6e] = 戴在显眼的地方\n# en: You tie it to your bag strap. Within a week every first-year in the castle is knotting blue thread, and the one who made yours walks past you every morning, very straight, very proud.\nopt.left.say.1 [37a54b] = 你把它系在书包带上。一个星期之内，城堡里每个一年级生都在打蓝线结，而给你做护身符的那个，每天早上从你身边走过，腰板挺得笔直，骄傲极了。\n# en: Thank them, and keep it in a pocket\nopt.right.label [d25df8] = 道谢，放进口袋\n# en: \"I'll keep it close,\" you say, and you do, in the inside pocket nobody sees. The first-year goes pink and runs. It's still there at the end of the year, a little frayed.\nopt.right.say.1 [a8acb6] = “我会贴身带着，”你说，你也确实这么做了，放在谁也看不见的内袋里。那个一年级生脸一红，跑开了。到学年结束时它还在那儿，有点磨毛了。\n\n@event spot_signature\n# en: Sign This?\ntitle [5957f5] = 能签个名吗？\n# en: A first-year has their warding textbook open at the chapter on circles, and a quill held out. \"Could you sign it? Next to the diagram. Everyone says you got it in your first week.\"\ntext [96ef47] = 一个一年级生把结界课本翻到讲法阵的那一章，递过来一支羽毛笔。“能签个名吗？签在图旁边。大家都说你第一周就弄懂了。”\n# en: A library corridor, a younger student holding out an open textbook and a quill, an older student hesitating over the page.\nscene [e5e7c1] = 图书馆的走廊里，一个年轻学生递出一本摊开的课本和一支羽毛笔，一个年长的学生对着书页犹豫。\n# en: Sign it with a flourish\nopt.left.label [f423ec] = 潇洒地签上\n# en: You sign it, with a little drawn circle for good measure. The textbook goes round the first-year dormitory by supper. By the end of the week three more appear, and a library book, which you decline.\nopt.left.say.1 [585d3b] = 你签了，还顺手画了个小法阵。晚饭前，这本课本已经在一年级宿舍里传了一圈。到周末，又冒出来三本，还有一本图书馆的书，你婉拒了。\n# en: Show them the rune instead\nopt.right.label [1973a5] = 教他们画法阵\n# en: You don't sign it. You take the quill and draw the circle properly in the margin, slowly, and make the first-year draw it back. \"There. Now it's yours.\" The first-year looks disappointed, and then, by the third try, astonished.\nopt.right.say.1 [c29111] = 你没有签名。你接过羽毛笔，在页边慢慢地把法阵好好画了一遍，再让那个一年级生照着画回来。“好了。现在它是你的了。”那个一年级生先是失望，到第三次时，变成了惊讶。\n\n@event spot_house_speech\n# en: A Few Words\ntitle [13224b] = 说几句\n# en: At the end-of-term supper, a final-year at the top of your table taps a glass and says that {player}, who has done the table such credit this term, will now say a few words. Nobody told you about the few words. The whole table turns.\ntext [3bed86] = 学期末的晚宴上，坐在你们这桌上首的一个毕业班学生敲了敲杯子，宣布这学期为全桌争了光的{player}现在要讲几句话。没人跟你提过要讲几句话。整张桌子都转了过来。\n# en: A long table at supper, candles lit, a student halfway to their feet with a cup in hand, every face along the table turned their way.\nscene [161380] = 晚餐时点着蜡烛的长桌边，一个学生手握杯子，刚站起一半，桌边每一张脸都朝着这个学生。\n# en: Make a speech\nopt.left.label [520c02] = 发表演讲\n# en: You say something short and true about the people at this table, and sit down before you can spoil it. They bang the table for you. A third-year you barely know shakes your hand after, hard.\nopt.left.say.1 [46f7a3] = 你简短而真诚地讲了讲这张桌子边的人，在自己搞砸之前坐了下来。他们为你拍桌子。一个你几乎不认识的三年级生事后用力握了握你的手。\n# en: You start well and lose the thread somewhere around \"and in conclusion\". Your table cheers anyway, loyally and a bit too loudly. You eat your pudding very fast.\nopt.left.say.2 [f0ae47] = 你开头不错，到“总而言之”那里就乱了。全桌照样为你欢呼，忠诚而且有点太响了。你飞快地吃完了布丁。\n# en: Raise your cup and sit\nopt.right.label [93b50b] = 举杯，坐下\n# en: You raise your cup, say \"to all of you\", and sit down. It turns out that's all anybody wanted. The table drinks, and goes back to arguing about the pudding.\nopt.right.say.1 [4b2956] = 你举起杯子，说了句“敬大家”，就坐下了。原来大家要的也就是这个。全桌喝了一口，又回去争论布丁了。\n\n@event spot_resentful\n# en: All Anyone Talks About\ntitle [831bec] = 大家都在谈论你\n# en: {o} has been short with you all week. Tonight, when the common room gathers round you for the third time to hear about something you did, {o} shuts {o.their} book hard and says, to nobody, \"Some of us did things this term too.\"\ntext [f548a8] = {o}这一个星期对你都很冷淡。今晚，公共休息室里的人第三次围过来听你讲你做过的事时，{o}啪地合上书，对着空气说：“这学期别人也做了些事。”\n# en: A common room, a student sitting stiffly apart with a book they're not reading, glancing at a crowd gathered around someone else.\nscene [35622c] = 公共休息室里，一个学生僵硬地坐在一旁，手里的书并没有在读，瞥着围在另一个人身边的人群。\n# en: Turn the room to {o}\nopt.left.label [df07a8] = 把大家的目光转向{o}\n# en: \"{o} did, actually,\" you say, and tell them about it, the thing {o} did that nobody noticed. The room turns. {o} goes red, and then, slowly, starts telling it properly. Later {o} says thank you, gruffly.\nopt.left.say.1 [b1ccd0] = “{o}确实做了，”你说，然后讲起那件事，{o}做的、没人注意到的那件事。大家转过身来。{o}脸红了，然后，慢慢地，开始亲自好好讲起来。后来，{o}粗声粗气地向你道了谢。\n# en: Let it pass\nopt.right.label [7680ac] = 随它去\n# en: You let it pass, and the room moves on, back to you. {o} goes up to bed early. It sits between you for a while, like a coat on a chair nobody will move.\nopt.right.say.1 [9c005f] = 你没有接话，房间里的话题又回到了你身上。{o}早早上楼睡觉去了。这件事在你们之间搁了一阵子，像一件没人肯挪开的、搭在椅子上的外套。\n\n@event spot_letters_page\n# en: Letters to the Editor\ntitle [69e221] = 读者来信\n# en: The Ledger's letters page is about you this week. \"Sir, I write to complain about {player}'s conduct in the library,\" says one. \"Sir, I write to defend {player}, who is a credit to us all,\" says another, in what is very clearly the same handwriting. Half the castle is reading it.\ntext [5b7211] = 这周《纪事报》的读者来信版全是关于你的。“先生，我写信投诉{player}在图书馆里的行为，”一封信写道。“先生，我写信为{player}辩护，此人是我们所有人的骄傲，”另一封写道，笔迹明显出自同一人之手。半个城堡都在读。\n# en: A newspaper's letters page pinned to a corridor board, students crowding round to read it, one of them laughing behind their hand.\nscene [6c5017] = 走廊的布告板上钉着一张报纸的读者来信版，学生们挤着看，其中一个用手捂着嘴偷笑。\n# en: Write in yourself\nopt.left.label [83fa80] = 亲自写信\n# en: You write a short, grave letter apologising for your conduct in the library, which you say was \"a sneeze of uncommon violence\". It runs on the front page. The library gives you a round of applause.\nopt.left.say.1 [f73536] = 你写了一封简短而郑重的信，为自己在图书馆里的行为道歉，说那是“一个异常猛烈的喷嚏”。它登上了头版。图书馆为你鼓了掌。\n# en: Your letter tries to be funny and comes out pompous. The next issue has three letters about your letter. You resolve never to write to a newspaper again.\nopt.left.say.2 [b5b52e] = 你的信想写得有趣，结果写得自命不凡。下一期刊登了三封关于你那封信的信。你下定决心再也不给报纸写信。\n# en: Ignore it entirely\nopt.right.label [f9d712] = 完全不理\n# en: You don't read it, which is harder than it sounds when everyone keeps reading it to you. By the next week there's a new scandal: someone has been taking two puddings. You are forgotten, gratefully.\nopt.right.say.1 [9f0046] = 你没有去读，这比听起来难，因为每个人都不停地念给你听。到下周，有了新的丑闻：有人一直在拿两份布丁。你被遗忘了，谢天谢地。\n\n@event spot_saved_seats\n# en: The Seat Problem\ntitle [4889a6] = 座位问题\n# en: {~Three different tables have saved you a seat at lunch, and all three are waving.|You can't sit down in the refectory any more without someone having saved the spot \"for you\", whether you wanted it or not.|Somebody you have never met has saved you a seat, and is offended you haven't taken it.} Your own friends are at the corner table, where they always are, watching to see where you'll go.\ntext [8932a7] = {~午饭时有三张不同的桌子给你留了座位，三张桌子都在冲你招手。|如今你在膳堂里一坐下，总有人已经“为你”占好了位置，不管你想不想要。|一个你从没见过的人给你留了座位，而你没坐过去，对方很生气。}你自己的朋友坐在角落那张桌子，他们一向坐在那儿，正看着你会往哪儿走。\n# en: A refectory at lunch, three different tables each with a student waving at an empty seat, a fourth student standing in the middle with a tray.\nscene [6538ef] = 午饭时的膳堂，三张不同的桌子上各有一个学生朝空座招手，第四个学生端着托盘站在中间。\n# en: Sit with your friends\nopt.left.label [b55f91] = 和朋友坐\n# en: {~You walk past all three waving tables to the corner. Your friends make a great show of not caring, and then shift up to make room.|You sit where you always sit. One table looks hurt. Your friends look smug.|You sit down at the corner. \"Oh, we're good enough for you, are we?\" says someone, delighted.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e33c07] = {~你从三张招手的桌子旁走过，去了角落。你的朋友们装作毫不在意，然后挪了挪给你腾出位置。|你坐在老地方。一张桌子显得很受伤。你的朋友们显得很得意。|你在角落坐下。“哦，我们配得上你啦？”有人说，高兴得很。}\n# en: Take a saved seat\nopt.right.label [1bcba7] = 坐留好的座位\n# en: {~You sit at the nearest table and are asked forty questions. It's flattering. At the corner table, your friends are laughing at something without you.|You take the saved seat, and the table is very kind, and you can't remember any of their names afterwards.|You sit with the stranger, who is thrilled, and tells you about their aunt. Your friends wave from across the hall.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d5867d] = {~你在最近的桌子坐下，被问了四十个问题。这很让人受用。角落那张桌子上，你的朋友们正为什么事笑着，没有你。|你坐到留好的座位上，那桌人都很和善，可事后你一个名字都记不起来。|你和那个陌生人坐在一起，对方激动极了，跟你讲起自己的姨妈。你的朋友们在大厅那头朝你挥手。}\n\n@event spot_shop_window\n# en: The Window Table\ntitle [b8283c] = 靠窗的桌子\n# en: A tearoom in the village has noticed that when you sit in its window, people come in. The owner makes you an offer: your tea is free, for the rest of the term, so long as you drink it in the window, where it can be seen.\ntext [3762e6] = 村里一家茶室发现，只要你坐在它的窗边，就会有人进来。店主给你开了个条件：这学期剩下的日子里你的茶免费，只要你坐在窗边喝，让人看得见。\n# en: A village tearoom window with a reserved card on its best table, a shopkeeper beckoning a student in from the street.\nscene [a1823b] = 村里一家茶室的窗口，最好的那张桌子上摆着“已预订”的卡片，店主正招手把街上的一个学生请进来。\n# en: Take the window\nopt.left.label [8ae02b] = 坐到窗边\n# en: You drink a great deal of free tea in the window, smiling at strangers who press their noses to the glass. The tearoom is full every afternoon. You can never quite read your book.\nopt.left.say.1 [c27556] = 你在窗边喝了很多免费的茶，对着把鼻子贴在玻璃上的陌生人微笑。茶室每天下午都坐满了人。你的书却总也读不进去。\n# en: Pay for your tea\nopt.right.label [d38fb7] = 自己付茶钱\n# en: You thank the owner and pay, and sit at the back, where nobody sees you. You read four chapters in peace. The owner, a little put out, brings you extra biscuits anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [e663b3] = 你谢过店主，付了钱，坐到后面没人看得见的地方。你安安静静地读了四章。店主有点不痛快，还是给你多送了几块饼干。\n\n@event spot_impression\n# en: Somebody Doing You\ntitle [601669] = 有人在演你\n# en: At the end-of-term skits in the common room, a second-year gets up on a chair and does you. The walk, the face you make in lectures, the thing you say when you lose at cards. It is very, very accurate. The whole room is looking at you to see how you'll take it.\ntext [2fb3c9] = 学期末公共休息室里的小品会上，一个二年级生站到椅子上，开始学你。走路的样子，上课时的表情，打牌输了时说的那句话。非常、非常像。整个房间都在看你，看你会是什么反应。\n# en: An end-of-term skit in a common room, a student on a chair doing an exaggerated impression, the room in stitches, one student in the audience watching their own walk being copied.\nscene [43a472] = 学期末公共休息室里的小品表演，一个学生站在椅子上夸张地模仿，满屋子的人笑得前仰后合，观众里有一个学生正看着自己的走路姿势被人学去。\n# en: Get up and do them back\nopt.left.label [572a07] = 上去学回来\n# en: You climb up on the next chair and do them doing you. The room goes wild. The second-year laughs so hard they fall off the chair. It becomes, for a term, a double act.\nopt.left.say.1 [2ee4c9] = 你爬上旁边的椅子，学起了那个学你的人。全场沸腾了。那个二年级生笑得从椅子上掉了下来。这一学期，你们成了一对搭档。\n# en: You climb up, and freeze, and the second-year does you freezing, which gets the biggest laugh of the night. You laugh too, eventually. It takes a while.\nopt.left.say.2 [f4e647] = 你爬了上去，然后僵住了，那个二年级生又学起你僵住的样子，赢得了当晚最大的笑声。最后你也笑了。花了好一会儿。\n# en: Laugh along\nopt.right.label [ccfaef] = 跟着笑\n# en: You laugh, properly, because it is funny. The second-year bows to you at the end. You notice, for the rest of the week, that you've stopped making the face in lectures.\nopt.right.say.1 [83a10d] = 你笑了，真心实意地笑，因为确实好笑。结束时那个二年级生向你鞠了一躬。那个星期剩下的日子里，你发现自己上课时不再做那个表情了。\n\n@event spot_name_dropped\n# en: A Friend of {player}'s\ntitle [b4acce] = {player}的朋友\n# en: You come round a corner and hear {o} telling a little crowd of second-years that {o.they} and {player} are \"very close, actually\", and that you \"always ask {o.their} advice\". You've spoken to {o} perhaps four times. {o} turns, and sees you.\ntext [b7e40d] = 你拐过一个弯，听见{o}正在对一小群二年级生说，{o.they}和{player}“其实很熟”，说你“总是来问{o.their}意见”。你和{o}大概只说过四次话。{o}转过身，看见了你。\n# en: A student talking grandly to a group of younger students in a corridor, an arm flung out, the person they're talking about rounding the corner behind them.\nscene [7b9679] = 走廊里一个学生正挥着手臂对一群年轻学生高谈阔论，他们谈论的那个人正从身后的拐角走过来。\n# en: Play along\nopt.left.label [55ddb6] = 顺着说\n# en: \"We are,\" you say. \"Terrible advice, mostly.\" The second-years laugh. {o} looks at you with such gratitude that you find, over the next month, that you do start asking {o.their} advice. Some of it is quite good.\nopt.left.say.1 [092de5] = “是啊，”你说，“大多是馊主意。”二年级生们笑了。{o}满怀感激地看着你，结果接下来一个月里，你发现自己真的开始问{o.their}意见了。有些还挺不错。\n# en: Set it straight, kindly\nopt.right.label [d6c817] = 委婉地澄清\n# en: \"We've met,\" you say pleasantly, \"a few times.\" {o} goes scarlet. The second-years drift away. {o} avoids you for a while, and then, one day, sits down and talks to you properly, about nothing much.\nopt.right.say.1 [9c97ed] = “我们见过，”你和气地说，“几次。”{o}的脸一下子红透了。二年级生们散开了。{o}躲了你一阵子，后来有一天，坐下来和你好好聊了聊，聊的都是些无关紧要的事。\n\n@event spot_professor_example\n# en: As {player} Showed Us\ntitle [fa8598] = 正如{player}向我们展示的\n# en: In the middle of a lecture, the lecturer says, \"As {player} showed us last week,\" and describes something you did, slightly wrong, as the right way to do it. Forty heads turn. You know, and you're fairly sure the lecturer doesn't, that it only worked by accident.\ntext [d2a212] = 讲课讲到一半，讲师说：“正如{player}上周向我们展示的，”然后描述了你做过的一件事，描述得略有偏差，还把它当成了正确做法。四十颗脑袋转了过来。你知道，而且你很确定讲师不知道，那次能成功纯属意外。\n# en: A lecture hall, a professor gesturing toward one student in the third row, the rest of the class turning to look.\nscene [b50de0] = 阶梯教室里，一位教授朝第三排的一个学生比画，全班都转过头去看。\n# en: Say it was an accident\nopt.left.label [80edf4] = 说那是意外\n# en: You put your hand up and say it was mostly luck. The lecturer looks at you for a long moment, then laughs, and makes the class work out why it shouldn't have worked. It's the best lecture of the term.\nopt.left.say.1 [b20958] = 你举手说那主要是运气。讲师看了你好一会儿，然后笑了，让全班推算为什么那本不该成功。那是这学期最好的一堂课。\n# en: Let it stand\nopt.right.label [d81aa7] = 就让它这样\n# en: You say nothing. The class writes it down. For the rest of the term people ask you to show them how, and you have to work out, in secret and in a hurry, how you did it.\nopt.right.say.1 [cd3b1c] = 你什么也没说。全班都记了下来。那学期剩下的日子里，人们一直请你演示怎么做，你只好偷偷地、匆匆忙忙地弄明白自己当初是怎么做到的。\n\n@event spot_three_invitations\n# en: Three Invitations, One Evening\ntitle [c19b7d] = 一个晚上，三份邀请\n# en: Three invitations for the same evening: a supper at the Gilded Newt with people who matter, a stargazing party on the observatory roof, and your oldest friend here, who just wants to play cards in the common room like you used to.\ntext [c64f9f] = 同一个晚上来了三份邀请：在镀金蝾螈酒馆和要紧人物共进晚餐，在观星台楼顶参加观星派对，还有你在这里最老的朋友，只想像从前那样在公共休息室里打牌。\n# en: A student at a desk with three different invitation cards fanned out in one hand, a calendar with one evening circled three times.\nscene [38c342] = 一个学生坐在书桌前，一只手里扇形展开三张不同的请柬，日历上同一个晚上被圈了三次。\n# en: Go where you'll be seen\nopt.left.label [e96d78] = 去会被人看见的地方\n# en: The Newt supper is loud and full of people who know your name. You make three useful acquaintances and laugh a great deal. Walking back, you think of the card game, and wonder who won.\nopt.left.say.1 [9f1c52] = 蝾螈酒馆的晚宴热闹非凡，满座都是知道你名字的人。你结识了三位有用的人，笑了很多。走回去的路上，你想起那场牌局，不知道最后是谁赢了。\n# en: Play cards with your friend\nopt.right.label [b6cf49] = 和朋友打牌\n# en: You send two polite notes and go down to the common room. Your friend looks up, surprised, and deals you in without a word. You lose every hand. It's the best evening of the month.\nopt.right.say.1 [3ff48d] = 你写了两张客气的便条，下楼去了公共休息室。你的朋友抬起头，有些意外，一言不发地给你发了牌。你每一把都输了。那是这个月最好的一个晚上。\n\n@event spot_family_tour\n# en: Show Them Round\ntitle [164e2a] = 带他们转转\n# en: A note comes down from the Headmistress's Tower: a family is visiting, their eldest starts next autumn, and they've asked, having read about you, whether you might show them round. The eldest is pale with nerves. The parents have a list of questions. The youngest wants to see a dragon.\ntext [0180d6] = 院长塔送来一张便条：有一家人来参观，他们的长子明年秋天入学，因为在报上读到过你，问你能不能带他们四处看看。那位长子紧张得脸色发白。父母列了一张问题清单。最小的孩子想看龙。\n# en: A student leading a nervous family through a castle courtyard, a small child staring up at the towers, a parent clutching a prospectus.\nscene [f2f421] = 一个学生领着一家紧张的人穿过城堡庭院，一个小孩仰头盯着塔楼，一位家长紧紧攥着一本招生简章。\n# en: Show them the polished academy\nopt.left.label [c5c519] = 带他们看光鲜的学院\n# en: You take them the long way: the Great Hall at its brightest, the library from the good end, the observatory with the sun on it. You skip the draughty stairs and the lecture with the smell. The parents are dazzled. The eldest looks more frightened than ever.\nopt.left.say.1 [76a581] = 你带他们绕了远路：最明亮时的大礼堂，从好的那头看过去的图书馆，洒满阳光的观星台。你跳过了漏风的楼梯和那间有怪味的教室。父母看得眼花缭乱。那位长子看上去比之前更害怕了。\n# en: Show them the real one\nopt.right.label [d7f3af] = 带他们看真实的学院\n# en: You show them the draughty stairs, the bench where everyone cries before exams, and the kitchen door where the kind cook leaves buns. The parents look a little doubtful. The eldest, for the first time, smiles. \"So people get lost here too?\" Constantly, you tell them.\nopt.right.say.1 [221580] = 你带他们看了漏风的楼梯，看了大家考试前都会在上面哭的那条长凳，还有厨房后门，好心的厨子会在那儿留面包。父母显得有点将信将疑。那位长子第一次露出了笑容。“这么说，在这儿也会迷路？”你告诉他们，经常。\n\n@event spot_owl_mail\n# en: Letters From Strangers\ntitle [6abfcd] = 陌生人的来信\n# en: {~The owls have started bringing you letters from people you've never met: a farmer's child who wants to come to Lumenhold, a retired professor with opinions, someone who wants to know if you're single.|There are eleven letters on your windowsill this morning, and an owl asleep on top of them, looking put-upon.|Another heap of letters from strangers. One has a pressed flower in it. One is just a drawing of you, with very large hands.}\ntext [aa47fe] = {~猫头鹰开始给你送来陌生人的信：一个想来流明堡的农家孩子，一位很有主见的退休教授，还有人想知道你是不是单身。|今天早上你的窗台上有十一封信，上面还睡着一只猫头鹰，一副受了委屈的样子。|又是一堆陌生人的来信。一封里夹着一朵压干的花。一封只是一幅你的画像，手画得特别大。}\n# en: A dormitory windowsill heaped with letters, a tired delivery owl sitting on the pile, a student reading one with a candle.\nscene [1e289c] = 宿舍的窗台上堆满了信，一只疲惫的送信猫头鹰坐在信堆上，一个学生借着烛光读其中一封。\n# en: Answer every one\nopt.left.label [6e5098] = 每一封都回\n# en: {~You spend the evening writing back, one after another, until your hand aches. The farmer's child writes again within a week, in very careful capitals.|You answer every letter, even the one with the drawing, and thank its artist for the hands.|It takes two evenings. You sleep badly and dream of owls. Somewhere out there, eleven people are very pleased.}\nopt.left.say.1 [875a10] = {~你花了一整个晚上一封接一封地回信，直到手发酸。不到一个星期，那个农家孩子又写来了信，用非常认真的大写字母。|你回了每一封信，连那幅画像也回了，还感谢画家把你的手画得那么大。|这花了两个晚上。你睡得不好，梦里全是猫头鹰。在外面的某个地方，有十一个人非常高兴。}\n# en: Keep them, answer none\nopt.right.label [8a30b2] = 收好，一封也不回\n# en: {~You put them in a box under the bed and get on with your reading. Now and then you take one out and read it again. You never quite manage to write back.|The box under the bed is getting full. The owl gives you a long, reproachful look on its way out.|You read them all, at least. The pressed flower goes in a book. You mean to answer that one. You don't.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6dfe02] = {~你把它们放进床底下的一只盒子里，接着读书。偶尔你会拿出一封再读一遍。你始终没能回信。|床底下的盒子越来越满了。猫头鹰临走时责备地看了你很久。|至少你每一封都读了。那朵压干的花夹进了一本书里。你本想回那一封的。你没有。}\n\n@event spot_notes_for_sale\n# en: Your Notes, For Sale\ntitle [17637d] = 你的笔记，出售中\n# en: Somebody has been copying your lecture notes and selling them in the common room as \"the {player} method\", a few coppers a copy, and there's a queue. The seller, a cheerful third-year with a cash tin, sees you and doesn't even look guilty. \"You could have a cut,\" they offer.\ntext [1e2999] = 有人一直在抄你的课堂笔记，在公共休息室里当作“{player}学习法”出售，几个铜板一份，还排起了队。卖家是个乐呵呵的三年级生，捧着一只钱匣，看见你，连一点心虚的样子都没有。“你可以分一成，”对方提议。\n# en: A common room table covered in copied pages, a student with a cash tin selling sheaves of notes, a queue of younger students waiting.\nscene [d4fa06] = 公共休息室的桌子上铺满了抄写的纸页，一个学生捧着钱匣在卖一沓沓笔记，一群年纪小的学生排着队。\n# en: Take a cut\nopt.left.label [7b3269] = 分一成\n# en: You take your cut and correct a few pages so the copies are at least right. The money is very welcome. A first-year thanks you, earnestly, for the notes they paid for. It sits a little wrong with you all week.\nopt.left.say.1 [e36007] = 你拿了你那一份，还改正了几页，好让抄本至少是对的。这笔钱来得正是时候。一个一年级生诚恳地感谢你，为了那份花钱买来的笔记。这一整个星期，你心里都有点不是滋味。\n# en: Give them away free\nopt.right.label [4ac3ee] = 免费送出去\n# en: You buy the last copy yourself and pin it to the common-room board, free to anyone. The seller sighs and closes the tin. By supper there's a queue at the board instead, and someone has added a note at the bottom: \"thank you\".\nopt.right.say.1 [4f94ca] = 你自己买下最后一份，钉在公共休息室的布告板上，谁都可以看。卖家叹了口气，合上了钱匣。到晚饭时，排队的人换到了布告板前，有人在最下面添了一行字：“谢谢”。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/whisperwood/around.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/whisperwood/around.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event ww_tree_tobias\n# en: The Trees Gossip\ntitle [e401ba] = 树在八卦\n# en: Two old trees have their crowns together, whispering. You catch your own name, and then Tobias's. The trees know him: he talks to plants, and plants, it turns out, talk back about him. Something about a hat.\ntext [054db0] = 两棵老树把树冠凑在一起说悄悄话。你听见了自己的名字，然后是托比亚斯的。树认识他：他跟植物说话，而事实证明，植物也会回过头来议论他。好像跟一顶帽子有关。\n# en: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, as if whispering, a student listening beneath.\nscene [78fe69] = 雾气弥漫的森林里，两棵老树把树冠凑在一起，像在说悄悄话，一个学生在树下偷听。\n# en: Leave them to it; turn back\nopt.left.label [4b6247] = 由它们去说吧；往回走\n# en: You leave the trees to their gossip and go home. Whatever it was about the hat, it can wait.\nopt.left.say.1 [a61e78] = 你让树们继续八卦，自己回家了。不管帽子是怎么回事，都可以等等再说。\n# en: Listen in\nopt.right.label [70ecdd] = 偷听\n# en: Tobias, say the trees, is raising a mandrake called Gerald, who wears a knitted hat and screams softly. They approve of Gerald. They're less sure about the hat.\nopt.right.say.1 [e58cd8] = 树们说，托比亚斯在养一株叫杰拉尔德的曼德拉草，它戴着一顶针织帽，叫起来很轻。树们很认可杰拉尔德。对那顶帽子就没那么确定了。\n\n@event ww_tree_lyra\n# en: The Trees Gossip\ntitle [e401ba] = 树在八卦\n# en: Two old trees have their crowns together, whispering. The name that comes up is Lyra's, and the trees sound amused. One of them has a scorch mark low on its trunk, and it's still a little sore about it.\ntext [58f941] = 两棵老树把树冠凑在一起说悄悄话。提到的名字是莱拉，树们听起来很乐。其中一棵树干下方有一块焦痕，它到现在还有点耿耿于怀。\n# en: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, as if whispering, a student listening beneath.\nscene [78fe69] = 雾气弥漫的森林里，两棵老树把树冠凑在一起，像在说悄悄话，一个学生在树下偷听。\n# en: Leave them to it; turn back\nopt.left.label [4b6247] = 由它们去说吧；往回走\n# en: You leave the trees to their gossip. Some things about Lyra you'd rather hear from Lyra.\nopt.left.say.1 [94c767] = 你让树们继续八卦。有些关于莱拉的事，你宁愿听莱拉自己说。\n# en: Listen in\nopt.right.label [70ecdd] = 偷听\n# en: Lyra Vaneshade, say the trees, can incinerate a target at forty paces and cannot make toast. She tried, out here, alone, at dawn. The scorched tree was the toast.\nopt.right.say.1 [922666] = 树们说，莱拉·凡妮希德能在四十步外把靶子烧成灰，却烤不好一片面包。她试过，就在这儿，独自一人，在黎明时分。那棵烧焦的树，就是那片面包。\n\n@event ww_tree_bram\n# en: The Trees Gossip\ntitle [e401ba] = 树在八卦\n# en: Two old trees have their crowns together, whispering, and the word you catch is \"prefect\", said with relish. They know Bram: a red sash comes through here on patrol, they say, and talks to itself about money.\ntext [379ac3] = 两棵老树把树冠凑在一起说悄悄话，你听到的词是“级长”，说得津津有味。它们认识布拉姆：树们说，有一条红肩带会到这儿来巡逻，还自言自语地念叨钱。\n# en: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, as if whispering, a student listening beneath.\nscene [78fe69] = 雾气弥漫的森林里，两棵老树把树冠凑在一起，像在说悄悄话，一个学生在树下偷听。\n# en: Leave them to it; turn back\nopt.left.label [4b6247] = 由它们去说吧；往回走\n# en: You leave the trees to their gossip. Bram has enough people talking about him.\nopt.left.say.1 [d34255] = 你让树们继续八卦。议论布拉姆的人已经够多了。\n# en: Listen in\nopt.right.label [70ecdd] = 偷听\n# en: Bram, say the trees, once invented a hall tax for the east staircase, and collected it for a week before anyone checked. They think it was very enterprising. They would.\nopt.right.say.1 [808303] = 树们说，布拉姆曾经给东楼梯发明过一种“过厅税”，收了一个星期才有人去查。它们觉得这很有进取心。它们当然会这么觉得。\n\n@event ww_tree_mir\n# en: The Trees Gossip\ntitle [e401ba] = 树在八卦\n# en: Two old trees have their crowns together, whispering about a bird that isn't a bird. It ticks, they say. It flew through here once with Mir chasing it and shouting about a deadline.\ntext [53d7f1] = 两棵老树把树冠凑在一起，悄悄议论一只不是鸟的鸟。它会滴答响，树们说。有一次它从这儿飞过，米尔在后面追着它，嚷嚷着什么截止日期。\n# en: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, as if whispering, a student listening beneath.\nscene [78fe69] = 雾气弥漫的森林里，两棵老树把树冠凑在一起，像在说悄悄话，一个学生在树下偷听。\n# en: Leave them to it; turn back\nopt.left.label [4b6247] = 由它们去说吧；往回走\n# en: You leave the trees to their gossip, and go home listening for ticking.\nopt.left.say.1 [3f72d2] = 你让树们继续八卦，回家的路上一直竖着耳朵听有没有滴答声。\n# en: Listen in\nopt.right.label [70ecdd] = 偷听\n# en: Mir, say the trees, built a clockwork owl that remembers deadlines, birthdays, and grudges, slightly. The owl landed here and told an oak it was overdue. The oak is still upset.\nopt.right.say.1 [ab36b8] = 树们说，米尔造了一只发条猫头鹰，它记得截止日期、生日，还有一点点仇。那只猫头鹰在这儿落过脚，告诉一棵橡树它逾期了。那棵橡树到现在还不痛快。\n\n@event ww_tree_kael\n# en: The Trees Gossip\ntitle [e401ba] = 树在八卦\n# en: Two old trees are whispering, and one side of each trunk is frosted, as if someone leaned there. The Northern boy, they say. He walks out here when he can't sleep, and puts his hands on their bark.\ntext [a211d8] = 两棵老树在说悄悄话，每棵树干的一侧都结着霜，像有人在那儿靠过。北方来的那个男孩，它们说。他睡不着的时候就走到这儿来，把手放在它们的树皮上。\n# en: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, frost on one side of their trunks, a student listening.\nscene [6ed471] = 雾气弥漫的森林里，两棵老树把树冠凑在一起，树干一侧结着霜，一个学生在树下偷听。\n# en: Leave them to it; turn back\nopt.left.label [4b6247] = 由它们去说吧；往回走\n# en: You leave the trees to their whispering. It feels like his to tell.\nopt.left.say.1 [db0d8d] = 你让树们继续低语。这感觉该由他自己来说。\n# en: Listen in\nopt.right.label [70ecdd] = 偷听\n# en: His hands are always cold, the trees say. He puts them on the bark to see if anything is ever warm enough. Nothing has been, yet. The trees sound sorry about it.\nopt.right.say.1 [8434af] = 他的手总是冰的，树们说。他把手放在树皮上，看有没有什么东西能暖得过来。到现在还没有。树们听起来为此感到抱歉。\n\n@event ww_tree_nessa\n# en: The Trees Gossip\ntitle [e401ba] = 树在八卦\n# en: Two old trees are whispering about a quill. Somebody's quill was left out here overnight, they say, and by morning it had written a headline on a birch. The birch would like a retraction.\ntext [66da0d] = 两棵老树在悄悄议论一支羽毛笔。有人的羽毛笔在这儿落了一夜，树们说，到了早上，它已经在一棵白桦上写好了一条头条新闻。那棵白桦希望登报更正。\n# en: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, a quill-scratched message on one trunk, a student listening.\nscene [8876b5] = 雾气弥漫的森林里，两棵老树把树冠凑在一起，其中一棵树干上刻着羽毛笔划出的字，一个学生在偷听。\n# en: Leave them to it; turn back\nopt.left.label [4b6247] = 由它们去说吧；往回走\n# en: You leave the trees to their gossip, which is at least partly about the press.\nopt.left.say.1 [639d37] = 你让树们继续八卦，它们至少有一部分在议论新闻界。\n# en: Listen in\nopt.right.label [70ecdd] = 偷听\n# en: Nessa's quill, say the trees, starts inventing the news if she leaves it unsupervised. The birch's headline is about the birch. It's very unflattering, and, the trees admit, mostly true.\nopt.right.say.1 [b2c334] = 树们说，内莎的羽毛笔要是没人看着，就会开始编新闻。那棵白桦上的头条写的就是那棵白桦。很不客气，而且，树们承认，大体属实。\n\n@event ww_tree_corvin\n# en: The Trees Gossip\ntitle [e401ba] = 树在八卦\n# en: Two old trees are whispering, and laughing, in the creaking way trees laugh. It's about Corvin. It's about a debt. It's about, as far as you can make out, a goat.\ntext [dca147] = 两棵老树在说悄悄话，还在笑，是树那种嘎吱嘎吱的笑法。是关于科尔文的。是关于一笔债的。就你能听明白的部分来说，是关于一只山羊的。\n# en: Two old trees leaning their crowns together in a misty forest, a goat grazing beneath, a student listening.\nscene [4d87f8] = 雾气弥漫的森林里，两棵老树把树冠凑在一起，树下一只山羊在吃草，一个学生在偷听。\n# en: Leave them to it; turn back\nopt.left.label [4b6247] = 由它们去说吧；往回走\n# en: You leave the trees laughing. You'll hear about the goat eventually. Everyone does.\nopt.left.say.1 [0879ea] = 你让树们笑着。你迟早会听说那只山羊的。每个人都会。\n# en: Listen in\nopt.right.label [70ecdd] = 偷听\n# en: Corvin, say the trees, once owed thirty crowns to trolls, and offered to pay them back with a goat. The trolls took the goat. The goat now runs the trolls.\nopt.right.say.1 [345a19] = 树们说，科尔文曾经欠了巨魔三十克朗，提出用一只山羊来还。巨魔收下了山羊。现在那只山羊管着巨魔。\n\n@event ww_tree_tell\n# en: Something the Trees Said\ntitle [b8e74e] = 树说过的事\n# en: {npc} is right there, and you know something about {them} that {they} didn't tell you. The Whisperwood did. It's on the tip of your tongue.\ntext [db8a12] = {npc}就在那儿，而你知道一件关于{them}的事，是{they}没告诉过你的。是低语森林告诉你的。话就在嘴边。\n# en: Two students talking in a castle corridor, one looking surprised and caught out, the other trying not to smile.\nscene [758852] = 城堡走廊里两个学生在说话，一个一脸惊讶、像被抓了个现行，另一个努力忍着不笑。\n# en: Keep it to yourself\nopt.left.label [09de43] = 自己留着\n# en: You keep it. Whatever the trees say, it's {npc}'s to tell. It sits in you like a small warm stone.\nopt.left.say.1 [4fd816] = 你没说。不管树们怎么讲，那都该由{npc}自己来说。它在你心里，像一块温热的小石头。\n# en: Tell {them} you heard it\nopt.right.label [cce0c0] = 告诉{them}你听说了\n# en: {npc_is(tobias): Tobias goes pink to the ears. \"They told you about Gerald?\" Then, proudly: \"Do you want to meet him?\"|{npc_is(mir): \"The owl went to the WOOD?\" Mir is delighted. \"It's famous. It's going to be unbearable.\"|{npc_is(corvin): Corvin laughs until he has to lean on the wall. \"The goat,\" he says. \"Everyone always hears about the goat.\"|{npc_is(kael): Kael is quiet for a long moment. Then he holds out a hand, and you take it. \"Not yet,\" he says. \"But warmer.\"|{npc_is(lyra): Lyra turns the colour of her own fire. \"Trees,\" she says, \"are liars,\" and stalks off, and doesn't speak to you until Thursday.|{npc_is(bram): Bram goes rigid. \"That was a legitimate levy,\" he says, and writes something down that is probably your name.|Nessa narrows her eyes. \"Who's your source? A tree. A TREE.\" She writes it down anyway.}}}}}}\nopt.right.say.1 [95bf60] = {npc_is(tobias): 托比亚斯的耳朵都红了。“它们跟你说了杰拉尔德？”然后，骄傲地：“你想见见他吗？”|{npc_is(mir): “那只猫头鹰去了森林？”米尔高兴坏了。“它出名了。它会变得让人受不了的。”|{npc_is(corvin): 科尔文笑得不得不扶着墙。“那只山羊，”他说。“每个人最后都会听说那只山羊。”|{npc_is(kael): 凯尔沉默了很久。然后他伸出一只手，你握住了。“还没有，”他说。“不过暖和些了。”|{npc_is(lyra): 莱拉的脸红得跟她的火一样。“树，”她说，“都是骗子，”然后大步走开，到星期四之前都没跟你说话。|{npc_is(bram): 布拉姆浑身僵住。“那是合法的征收，”他说，然后写下了什么，多半是你的名字。|内莎眯起眼睛。“你的消息来源是谁？一棵树。一棵树！”她还是把它记下来了。}}}}}}\n\n@event jit_maud_warning\n# en: Maud's Warning\ntitle [5e3c78] = 莫德的忠告\n# en: Old Maud is at the edge of the wood with a basket, and catches your sleeve. \"Off the path, are we? Listen, dearie. Press on as far as you like, turn back whenever you like, and what you bring out, Pell will buy. Fall once and you'll get up. Twice, and someone'll have to come and find you.\"\ntext [04432e] = 老莫德挎着篮子站在林子边上，一把拉住你的袖子。“离开小路了，是吧？听着，亲爱的。想往里走多远都行，想什么时候回头都行，带出来的东西，佩尔都会收。摔一跤，你还能爬起来。摔两跤，就得有人去找你了。”\n# en: An old woman in a shawl with a basket of mushrooms catching a student's sleeve at the edge of a misty forest.\nscene [0bb50e] = 雾气弥漫的森林边缘，一位披着披肩、挎着一篮蘑菇的老妇人拉住一个学生的袖子。\n# en: Thank her\nopt.left.label [c00500] = 谢谢她\n# en: \"And mind the Hush,\" she adds, letting go. \"It keeps the castle's hours. It wakes when you're meant to be asleep.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [81d34f] = “还有，当心寂境，”她松开手时又补了一句。“它守的是城堡的时辰。你该睡觉的时候，它就醒了。”\n# en: Ask about the deep part\nopt.right.label [0e616f] = 问问深处的事\n# en: \"The Hush,\" she says. \"Past the heartwood. It keeps the castle's hours, dearie: it wakes at midnight, when you're meant to be asleep.\" She looks at the wood a long moment. \"Don't go down there without a friend.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [427783] = “寂境，”她说。“在心木林再往里。它守的是城堡的时辰，亲爱的：午夜才醒，正是你该睡觉的时候。”她望着森林看了很久。“别一个人下去。”\n\n@event brask_deep_hours\n# en: Midnight Hours\ntitle [6200fa] = 午夜的时辰\n# en: Professor Brask is feeding the old barn owl in the loft. \"You've been in the wood,\" she says, without turning round. \"By day, I hope. The deep things keep midnight hours. Most of them would rather you didn't.\"\ntext [61eeda] = 布拉斯克教授在阁楼上喂那只老仓鸮。“你去过林子了，”她头也不回地说。“但愿是白天去的。深处的东西守的是午夜的时辰。它们大多不希望你去。”\n# en: A professor in a riding coat feeding a barn owl in a dim stable loft at dusk, the forest dark through the window.\nscene [cf0119] = 黄昏时分，昏暗的马厩阁楼里，一位穿骑装的教授在喂一只仓鸮，窗外的森林一片漆黑。\n# en: Ask her what deep things\nopt.left.label [6c281e] = 问她是什么深处的东西\n# en: \"Old ones,\" says Brask. \"In the Hush, past the heartwood. Wakes at midnight. Go down by day and it's just quiet.\" She feeds the owl another mouse. \"That was a warning, not an invitation.\" It didn't sound like either.\nopt.left.say.1 [d38093] = “老东西，”布拉斯克说。“在寂境里，心木林再往里。午夜才醒。白天下去，那儿只是安静。”她又喂了猫头鹰一只老鼠。“这是警告，不是邀请。”可听起来两样都不像。\n# en: Help with the owl instead\nopt.right.label [8c3f95] = 还是帮忙喂猫头鹰吧\n# en: You hold the bucket and the owl regards you with deep contempt. Brask says nothing more about the wood, and you leave with a peck on the thumb and her good opinion.\nopt.right.say.1 [912a71] = 你拎着桶，猫头鹰用深深的鄙夷打量着你。布拉斯克没再提林子的事，你离开时拇指上挨了一啄，也赢得了她的好感。\n# en: You don't ask which colleague the owl outlived last. She tells you anyway, in four words, and that's all.\nopt.right.say.2 [4bd211] = 你没有问那只猫头鹰最近又熬走了哪位同事。她还是告诉了你，只用了寥寥几个字，就这样。\n\n@event carto_hush_margin\n# en: A Note in the Margin\ntitle [e2dcc2] = 页边的笔记\n# en: The Cartographers' oldest map of the grounds has a margin crowded with notes in a dozen hands. Over the Whisperwood someone has written, very small: \"W. wood: HUSH. Twelfth bell only.\" Corvin taps it. \"Never checked that one,\" he says. \"Nobody's that brave. Or that stupid.\"\ntext [e65e76] = 午夜制图社最老的那张校园地图，页边挤满了十几种笔迹的批注。在低语森林上方，有人写得很小：“西林：寂境。只在第十二声钟响。”科尔文敲了敲那行字。“这条从没查证过，”他说。“没人那么勇敢。或者那么蠢。”\n# en: An old hand-drawn map of a forest spread on a table by candlelight, one margin crowded with tiny ink notes.\nscene [aacac8] = 烛光下，一张手绘的旧森林地图摊在桌上，一侧页边挤满了细小的墨水批注。\n# en: Ink it onto the new map\nopt.left.label [a4f927] = 把它誊到新地图上\n# en: You copy the note onto the new map in your neatest hand, and the Cartographers watch you do it with the particular silence of people placing bets.\nopt.left.say.1 [794e00] = 你用最工整的字迹把这条批注誊到新地图上，制图社的人看着你写，那种安静，像一群正在下注的人。\n# en: Leave the margin alone\nopt.right.label [21309c] = 别动那页边\n# en: You leave the old note where it is. The Cartographers move on to the third floor's missing corridor. The margin stays in your head anyway.\nopt.right.say.1 [9dfcea] = 你把那条旧批注留在原处。制图社转而去研究三楼那条失踪的走廊。可那行页边的字还是留在了你脑子里。\n\n@event grimsbys_field_guide\n# en: A Field Guide\ntitle [515800] = 野外图鉴\n# en: Grimsby has put a field guide to the western woods on the counter, open at moonpetal. There's a long footnote, in tiny print, and someone has circled it. \"I think,\" says Grimsby mildly, \"that you should read the footnote.\"\ntext [e73338] = 格林斯比在柜台上放了一本西部林地的野外图鉴，翻开在月瓣花那一页。那里有一条长长的脚注，字很小，有人把它圈了出来。“我想，”格林斯比温和地说，“你该读读那条脚注。”\n# en: A small battered green field guide open on a bookshop counter, a long footnote circled in pencil.\nscene [96d2d5] = 书店柜台上摊着一本破旧的绿色小图鉴，一条长脚注被铅笔圈了出来。\n# en: Buy the book\nopt.left.label [c7f915] = 买下这本书\n# en: The footnote says moonpetal in the deep wood opens only \"in the Hush, at the twelfth bell\". You buy the book and read the rest on the way home. It's mostly about mushrooms, and very good.\nopt.left.say.1 [888936] = 脚注说，深林里的月瓣花只在“寂境之中、第十二声钟响时”开放。你买下书，在回家路上读完了剩下的部分。大半是讲蘑菇的，写得非常好。\n# en: Read the footnote and go\nopt.right.label [840a0b] = 读完脚注就走\n# en: You read the footnote standing up: moonpetal in the deep wood opens only \"in the Hush, at the twelfth bell\". Grimsby closes the book with a sigh that says browsers are a cross he bears.\nopt.right.say.1 [c4fbb5] = 你站着读完了脚注：深林里的月瓣花只在“寂境之中、第十二声钟响时”开放。格林斯比合上书，叹了口气，意思是光看不买的人是他背负的十字架。\n\n@event maud_tuppence\n# en: My Girl in the Hush\ntitle [1605d7] = 我那住在寂境的姑娘\n# en: Over tea, Maud nods at a drawing pinned over the stove: a spider, no bigger than a hand. \"My Tuppence,\" she says. \"Found her in the greenhouses, my first winter as groundskeeper. Kept her in the potting shed till she got too big for it. She's in the Hush now, the deep part of the wood. Keeps the worse things out.\" She sips. \"Only wakes at midnight, the Hush.\"\ntext [605cc2] = 喝茶的时候，莫德朝炉子上方钉着的一张画点了点头：一只蜘蛛，不比一只手大。“我的塔彭丝，”她说。“在温室里捡到的，我当园丁的头一个冬天。养在育苗棚里，直到她长得装不下。现在她在寂境，林子最深的地方。把更糟的东西挡在外面。”她抿了一口茶。“寂境只在午夜才醒。”\n# en: An old woman pouring tea in a cluttered cottage kitchen, an old drawing of a tiny spider pinned above the stove.\nscene [6a84e2] = 杂乱的小屋厨房里，一位老妇人在倒茶，炉子上方钉着一张小蜘蛛的旧画。\n# en: Ask how big she is now\nopt.left.label [a2483c] = 问她现在有多大\n# en: \"Oh, carriage-sized,\" says Maud fondly. \"Bit more.\" She pours more tea. You find you don't want any.\nopt.left.say.1 [8d58a0] = “噢，马车那么大，”莫德慈爱地说。“还大一点。”她又倒了些茶。你发现自己一口也不想喝了。\n# en: Ask if she misses her\nopt.right.label [6e0e36] = 问她想不想她\n# en: \"Every day,\" says Maud. \"But you can't keep a thing in a potting shed because you'd miss it. That's not love, that's a jar.\" She looks at you over the cup. \"Tell her I said hello, if you're ever that foolish.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [8b7f57] = “天天想，”莫德说。“可你不能因为舍不得，就把一样东西关在育苗棚里。那不叫爱，那叫罐子。”她从杯沿上方看着你。“要是你哪天真蠢到那份上，替我跟她问声好。”\n\n@event maud_worse_out\n# en: She Kept Worse Out\ntitle [b71f8b] = 她把更糟的挡在外面\n# en: Maud knows. Of course she knows: the bees told her, or the wood did. \"You beat my Tuppence,\" she says, and she's proud, you can hear it, and frightened too. \"Good for you, dearie. Only she kept worse things out. Now she's let one in. Sits by a lantern, I hear. Polite.\" She doesn't say his name.\ntext [90a84e] = 莫德知道了。她当然知道：是蜜蜂告诉她的，或者是林子。“你打赢了我的塔彭丝，”她说，听得出她很骄傲，也很害怕。“好样的，亲爱的。只是她一直把更糟的东西挡在外面。现在她放进来了一个。听说坐在一盏灯笼旁边。很有礼貌。”她没说他的名字。\n# en: An old woman standing at her cottage door at dusk, looking toward a dark forest with an expression half proud, half afraid.\nscene [4ca456] = 黄昏时分，一位老妇人站在小屋门口，望着漆黑的森林，神情一半骄傲，一半害怕。\n# en: Promise to be careful\nopt.left.label [73c3d8] = 保证会小心\n# en: \"Careful,\" says Maud, \"is what people say instead of 'not going'.\" But she pats your hand, and gives you a jar of honey for the road you haven't said you'll take.\nopt.left.say.1 [e8d43d] = “小心，”莫德说，“是人们不想说‘不去’时才说的话。”可她还是拍了拍你的手，给了你一罐蜂蜜，让你带着上那条你还没说要走的路。\n# en: Ask who he is\nopt.right.label [15bdc3] = 问他是谁\n# en: \"Somebody they sent away,\" says Maud, \"before my time, almost. He was clever. They always are.\" She shuts the door very firmly, as if on a draught.\nopt.right.say.1 [908be9] = “一个被他们送走的人，”莫德说，“差不多在我来之前。他很聪明。他们总是很聪明。”她把门关得很紧，像在挡穿堂风。\n\n@event ww_mastery_map\n# en: Maud's Old Map\ntitle [cc4ecb] = 莫德的旧地图\n# en: Maud slides a creased old map across the table. The Whisperwood, drawn in her young hand, every carved bee marked, every path to the heartwood, and a careful line down into the Hush. \"Mine,\" she says, \"from when I used to go down to visit. My knees won't take it now. Yours will.\"\ntext [661d71] = 莫德把一张皱巴巴的旧地图推过桌面。低语森林，出自她年轻时的手笔，每一只刻着的蜜蜂都标了出来，每一条通往心木林的路，还有一条小心画下的线，一直通到寂境里。“我的，”她说，“以前我常下去看她。现在我的膝盖受不了了。你的还行。”\n# en: An old woman handing a creased, hand-drawn map of a forest to a student across a cottage table.\nscene [0a0925] = 小屋的桌旁，一位老妇人把一张皱巴巴的手绘森林地图递给一个学生。\n# en: Take it, gratefully\nopt.left.label [6ab2d2] = 心怀感激地收下\n# en: You fold it into your coat. From now on, at midnight, you can go straight down past the heartwood. \"Bring it back,\" says Maud, \"when you're old.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [c55e9e] = 你把它折好放进外套里。从今往后，午夜时你可以直接下到心木林以下。“等你老了，”莫德说，“再还给我。”\n# en: Ask her to come with you, once\nopt.right.label [da9c48] = 请她陪你去一次\n# en: She laughs till she coughs. \"Once,\" she says. And one night she does, slowly, on your arm, as far as the heartwood, and stands there smiling at the dark. Then she gives you the map.\nopt.right.say.1 [897f31] = 她笑得咳嗽起来。“就一次，”她说。有一天晚上她真的去了，慢慢地，挽着你的胳膊，一直走到心木林，站在那儿对着黑暗微笑。然后她把地图给了你。\n\n@event offer_heartwood_wand\n# en: Mir Carves the Heartwood\ntitle [ed25cb] = 米尔雕心木\n# en: Mir is at the market stall, mending a clockwork hen, when you put the heartwood splinter on the counter. They stop. They pick it up as if it might bite, then as if it might sing. \"Where,\" they say, \"did you get THIS?\" Then: \"I can carve it. It's work. Not much money.\"\ntext [3ad44d] = 米尔正在集市摊子上修一只发条母鸡，你把心木木片放在了柜台上。TA停住了。TA把它拿起来，先像怕它咬人，又像怕它唱歌。“你从哪儿，”TA说，“弄到这个的？”然后：“我能雕。是个活儿。不怎么收钱。”\n# en: An artificer at a market stall turning a red-gold splinter of wood in their fingers, a carving knife ready.\nscene [eafbbe] = 集市摊子前，一位工匠用手指转动着一片红金色的木片，雕刻刀已经备好。\n# en: Not yet\nopt.left.label [8dd27c] = 先不用\n# en: Mir hands it back with real reluctance. \"Don't lose it,\" they say. \"Don't use it to stir anything. Don't...\" You leave while they're still listing.\nopt.left.say.1 [26a67c] = 米尔极不情愿地把它还给你。“别弄丢了，”TA说。“别拿它搅东西。别……”TA还在列的时候，你就走了。\n# en: Have Mir carve it\nopt.right.label [3e2515] = 请米尔雕它\n# en: A wand for fights: more Mana.\nopt.right.hint [74763c] = 一根战斗用的魔杖：更多魔力。\n# en: Mir carves it in an evening, talking to it the whole time. The grain moves under their knife as if it's helping. When they hand it over, it's warm, and so are their hands. \"Don't tell anyone I made a thing that isn't a machine,\" they say.\nopt.right.say.1 [7a8145] = 米尔花了一个晚上雕它，一边雕一边跟它说话。木纹在刀下游动，好像在帮忙。TA递给你的时候，它是温的，TA的手也是。“别告诉别人我做了个不是机器的东西，”TA说。\n\n@event offer_spinner_silk_robe\n# en: Spinner Silk\ntitle [8231f7] = 织网者的丝\n# en: The village tailor unrolls the Spinner's silk across her table and goes very quiet. She holds it to the window. Light goes into it and doesn't come out. \"I can make you a robe of this,\" she says at last. \"It will cost you. Not for the silk. For my nerves.\"\ntext [d43853] = 村里的裁缝把织网者的丝在桌上展开，一下子安静了。她把它举到窗前。光照进去，就没再出来。“我能用这个给你做件长袍，”她终于说。“会很贵。不是贵在丝。是贵在我的神经。”\n# en: A village tailor holding up a length of shimmering grey silk to the window light, her mouth open in astonishment.\nscene [48cb36] = 一位村里的裁缝把一段闪着微光的灰色丝绸举到窗前的光里，惊讶得合不拢嘴。\n# en: Keep the silk for now\nopt.left.label [e0ca3f] = 丝先留着\n# en: She rolls it up again, very carefully, as if it might roll her up. \"Come back,\" she says. \"Please come back. I want to have made it.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [043b06] = 她又把它卷起来，非常小心，好像怕它反过来把她卷起来。“回来，”她说。“请一定回来。我想做过这件东西。”\n# en: Have the robe made\nopt.right.label [d6d213] = 请她做长袍\n# en: A robe for fights: much more Health, quicker steps.\nopt.right.hint [d0d7c1] = 一件战斗用的长袍：多得多的生命，步子更快。\n# en: It takes her a week and two sleepless nights. When you put it on, you move like a rumour. The tailor sits down. \"I'll never make anything like it again,\" she says, happily.\nopt.right.say.1 [1a0874] = 她花了一个星期，熬了两个通宵。你穿上它，走起路来像一则传闻。裁缝坐了下来。“我再也做不出这样的东西了，”她开心地说。\n\n@event item_quill_brask\n# en: Brask Knows That Owl\ntitle [247d21] = 布拉斯克认得那只猫头鹰\n# en: Professor Brask sees your quill and takes it out of your hand without asking. She turns it to the light. Above you, in the rafters, the old barn owl shifts. \"That's hers,\" says Brask. \"From the winter she moulted in the wood. I looked for that feather for a month.\"\ntext [8e5496] = 布拉斯克教授看见你的羽毛笔，不由分说就从你手里拿了过去。她把它转向光。在你头顶的椽子上，那只老仓鸮挪了挪身子。“这是她的，”布拉斯克说。“她在林子里换羽的那个冬天掉的。那根羽毛我找了一个月。”\n# en: A professor in a riding coat holding up a white owl-feather quill to the light, an old barn owl in the rafters above.\nscene [e59717] = 一位穿骑装的教授把一支白色猫头鹰羽毛笔举向光亮，上方椽子上蹲着一只老仓鸮。\n# en: Offer it back\nopt.left.label [57406d] = 把它还给她\n# en: Brask looks at the quill a long time, then hands it back. \"Keep it. She'd want it used.\" Then, gruffly, she tells you how old the owl is, and how many colleagues it has outlived, and doesn't say which loss hurt most.\nopt.left.say.1 [476bbf] = 布拉斯克看着那支笔看了很久，然后还给了你。“留着吧。她会希望它被用上。”然后，她粗声粗气地告诉你那只猫头鹰有多老，送走了多少同事，却没说哪一次离别最痛。\n# en: Ask her about the owl\nopt.right.label [cf6e1d] = 问她那只猫头鹰的事\n# en: \"Older than the beastcraft syllabus,\" says Brask. \"Outlived three professors.\" She hands back the quill. \"Wouldn't say which one I miss most,\" she says, and then doesn't.\nopt.right.say.1 [3672e3] = “比魔兽学的教学大纲还老，”布拉斯克说。“送走了三位教授。”她把笔还给你。“不会告诉你我最想念哪一个，”她说，然后果然没说。\n\n@event item_quill_nessa\n# en: The Quill Writes Back\ntitle [189326] = 羽毛笔回嘴了\n# en: You leave your owl quill on Nessa's desk for one minute. When you come back, her enchanted quill and yours are writing at each other on a sheet of foolscap: hers in headlines, yours in very tidy footnotes. It is, as far as you can tell, an argument about accuracy.\ntext [9200ac] = 你把猫头鹰羽毛笔在内莎桌上放了一分钟。等你回来，她那支施了魔法的羽毛笔正和你的笔在一张大页纸上对着写：她的写标题，你的写非常工整的脚注。就你能看出来的，这是一场关于准确性的争论。\n# en: Two quills scratching furiously at each other on a sheet of paper in a cluttered newspaper office, a student and an editor watching aghast.\nscene [83cbae] = 杂乱的报社办公室里，两支羽毛笔在一张纸上激烈地互相书写，一个学生和一个编辑目瞪口呆地看着。\n# en: Let them finish\nopt.left.label [726d27] = 让它们写完\n# en: Nessa reads the finished sheet aloud. Her quill invented a scandal; yours corrected it, line by line. \"I'm printing this,\" says Nessa, \"as a warning to my quill.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [bfb506] = 内莎把写完的那张纸大声念了出来。她的笔编了一桩丑闻；你的笔逐行把它更正了。“我要把这个登出来，”内莎说，“给我的笔一个警告。”\n# en: Separate them\nopt.right.label [b77b0a] = 把它们分开\n# en: You pull your quill away mid-footnote. Nessa's quill sulks for a week and invents nothing, which Nessa says is worse.\nopt.right.say.1 [8c3a4d] = 你在脚注写到一半时把笔拿开了。内莎的笔赌气赌了一个星期，什么都没编，内莎说这更糟。\n\n@event item_lodestone_kael\n# en: North, Always North\ntitle [1983ce] = 北方，永远是北方\n# en: Kael notices the lodestone turning on its cord, slowly, to face north. He watches it for a long moment. \"It points home,\" he says. \"My home.\" He doesn't sound as if he's decided how he feels about that.\ntext [12c914] = 凯尔注意到磁石在绳子上慢慢转动，转向北方。他看了它很久。“它指着家，”他说。“我的家。”听起来他还没决定自己对此是什么感觉。\n# en: A pale Northern student looking at a dark pendant turning slowly on a cord, his frosted hand held out beneath it.\nscene [3eb168] = 一个苍白的北方学生看着一只深色吊坠在绳子上缓缓转动，他结着霜的手托在下面。\n# en: Let him hold it\nopt.left.label [c4aede] = 让他拿着\n# en: He holds it in his cold palm and the stone keeps turning north, steady as a promise. \"It doesn't know I left,\" he says, and gives it back.\nopt.left.say.1 [6d883b] = 他把它托在冰冷的掌心里，石头依然转向北方，稳得像一个承诺。“它不知道我已经离开了，”他说，把它还给了你。\n# en: Ask about the North\nopt.right.label [2b1b3f] = 问问北方的事\n# en: \"Cold,\" says Kael, which is the whole answer, and the subject is closed. The pendant keeps pointing at it.\nopt.right.say.1 [4de6c2] = “冷，”凯尔说，这就是全部的回答，话题到此为止。吊坠还在指着那个方向。\n\n@event item_lodestone_stacks\n# en: Lost in the Stacks\ntitle [8eca0e] = 迷失在书库\n# en: In the deep stacks the lodestone stops pointing north and starts pointing at a shelf. Then at a book, misshelved forty years ago between two treatises on drainage. It's very old, and very interesting, and nobody has missed it.\ntext [2a2dd2] = 在书库深处，磁石不再指向北方，而是开始指向一个书架。然后指向一本书，四十年前被放错了地方，夹在两本排水论著中间。它很老，很有意思，而且没有人想念过它。\n# en: A student following a turning pendant between tall dim library shelves, a misshelved book glowing faintly at the end of a row.\nscene [15a96d] = 一个学生跟着一只转动的吊坠穿行在高大昏暗的图书馆书架间，一排尽头一本放错位置的书微微发着光。\n# en: Read it here and now\nopt.left.label [5057ed] = 就在这儿读\n# en: You read it on the floor of the stacks until the lamps are put out. It's about the wood. Most of it you already knew, the hard way.\nopt.left.say.1 [093dc1] = 你坐在书库地板上读，直到灯被熄掉。书是讲林子的。大部分你已经知道了，是吃过苦头才知道的。\n# en: Return it to its shelf\nopt.right.label [36b1d3] = 把它放回原位\n# en: You find where it belongs and put it there. Grimsby, when you tell him, is so pleased he gives you tea.\nopt.right.say.1 [ab4739] = 你找到它该在的地方，把它放了回去。你告诉格林斯比的时候，他高兴得请你喝了茶。\n\n@event item_tine_brask\n# en: Brask Bows to It\ntitle [fde4ad] = 布拉斯克向它鞠躬\n# en: Professor Brask sees the stag's tine at your belt and stops in the middle of a sentence. Then she bows to it, deeply, the way she bows in the bracken. Every horse in the stable goes quiet.\ntext [3d87cf] = 布拉斯克教授看见你腰间的鹿角尖，话说到一半停住了。然后她向它深深鞠了一躬，就像她在蕨丛里鞠躬那样。马厩里的每一匹马都安静了下来。\n# en: A sturdy professor bowing low to a dark antler tine held by a student, horses watching from their stalls.\nscene [86e7ce] = 一位结实的教授向一个学生手里的深色鹿角尖深深鞠躬，马儿们从隔栏里看着。\n# en: Bow back\nopt.left.label [9e67be] = 也鞠一躬\n# en: You bow to the tine too, which feels silly, and then doesn't. Brask straightens and nods. \"You'll do,\" she says, which from her is a sonnet.\nopt.left.say.1 [267bf8] = 你也向鹿角尖鞠了一躬，感觉有点傻，然后就不觉得了。布拉斯克直起身，点点头。“你还行，”她说，从她嘴里出来，这就是一首十四行诗了。\n# en: Ask why she bowed\nopt.right.label [ffe803] = 问她为什么鞠躬\n# en: \"It's not yours,\" she says. \"It's lent. The stag lends it to people it means to see again.\" She looks at you hard. \"Don't make it regret that.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [1d1c49] = “它不是你的，”她说。“是借给你的。影鹿把它借给它想再见到的人。”她狠狠地看着你。“别让它后悔。”\n\n@event item_heartwood_tobias\n# en: It Hums\ntitle [3bb277] = 它在嗡嗡响\n# en: You walk into the greenhouses with the heartwood wand, and every plant in the place leans toward it. Tobias looks up from his potting. \"Oh,\" he says. \"Oh, they like that. It's humming. Can you hear it? It's humming to them.\"\ntext [5376b2] = 你带着心木魔杖走进温室，里面每一株植物都朝它倾过去。托比亚斯从花盆前抬起头。“噢，”他说。“噢，它们喜欢这个。它在嗡嗡响。你听得见吗？它在对它们哼歌。”\n# en: A large gentle student in a greenhouse surrounded by potted plants all leaning toward a red-gold wand in another student's hand.\nscene [b594ad] = 温室里，一个高大温和的学生被盆栽围着，所有植物都朝另一个学生手里的红金色魔杖倾斜。\n# en: Let him listen\nopt.left.label [ab5757] = 让他听\n# en: Tobias holds the wand to his ear with his eyes shut and smiles. \"It says it's far from home,\" he says. \"But it doesn't mind.\" The plants agree. You almost hear it.\nopt.left.say.1 [9a3831] = 托比亚斯闭着眼把魔杖贴在耳边，笑了。“它说它离家很远，”他说。“不过它不在意。”植物们也同意。你几乎也听见了。\n# en: Get on with your work\nopt.right.label [ccacf4] = 干你自己的活\n# en: You put the wand away and get on with your herbology. The plants lean back to their own business, disappointed, and so does Tobias.\nopt.right.say.1 [4b0677] = 你把魔杖收起来，继续做你的草药课作业。植物们失望地回到自己的事情上去，托比亚斯也是。\n\n@event item_thorn_wants\n# en: It Wants to Be Used\ntitle [9b19f6] = 它想被用\n# en: You wake at night with the thorn wand in your hand and no memory of picking it up. It's warm. It's telling you, in the way wands tell you things, about the student who laughed at you at supper, asleep two doors down, and how much life a sleeper doesn't need until morning.\ntext [f620b0] = 你半夜醒来，手里握着荆棘魔杖，却不记得自己拿起过它。它是温的。它在告诉你——魔杖就是这样告诉你事情的——晚饭时嘲笑你的那个学生，就睡在隔两间的房里；一个熟睡的人，直到早上都用不着多少生命力。\n# en: A blackthorn wand held in a student's hand in a dark dormitory, faintly green at its heart, its thorns bristling toward the wall and the room beyond it.\nscene [ec1a04] = 黑暗的宿舍里，一个学生手里握着一根黑刺李魔杖，杖心泛着淡淡的绿，尖刺朝着墙壁和墙那边的房间竖起。\n# en: Put it away in a drawer\nopt.left.label [cb2b9e] = 把它锁进抽屉\n# en: You shut it in the drawer and lie awake listening to it not be quiet. At breakfast the student who laughed is fine, and as rude as ever. You're not, quite.\nopt.left.say.1 [93f6a4] = 你把它关进抽屉，躺着听它不肯安静。早饭时，那个嘲笑你的学生好好的，还是一样无礼。你却不太好。\n# en: Just a little of theirs\nopt.right.label [b5084a] = 就取一点点TA的\n# en: You point it at the wall between you, and something comes through, warm and green. You feel wonderful. At breakfast they're yawning into their porridge, puzzled, and you watch them yawn. The thorn wand is cool again, satisfied, for now.\nopt.right.say.1 [5e8830] = 你把它对准你们之间的那堵墙，有什么东西穿了过来，温暖而碧绿。你感觉好极了。早饭时TA对着粥直打哈欠，一脸困惑，你看着TA打哈欠。荆棘魔杖又凉了下来，满足了，暂时。\n\n@event item_thorn_brask\n# en: Brask Smells It\ntitle [9ba827] = 布拉斯克闻出来了\n# en: Professor Brask stops beside you and sniffs, like a horse. \"Thorn,\" she says. \"Blackthorn. Where did you get it?\" She holds out her hand. \"I know that wand. I know the woman who cuts them. Give it to me, and I'll snap it, and we'll say no more.\"\ntext [adbe8b] = 布拉斯克教授在你身边停下，像马一样嗅了嗅。“荆棘，”她说。“黑刺李。你从哪儿弄来的？”她伸出手。“我认得那种魔杖。我认得削它的那个女人。给我，我把它折了，这事就不提了。”\n# en: A stern professor in a stable holding out her hand for a blackthorn wand, a student hesitating.\nscene [a12a93] = 马厩里，一位严厉的教授伸手要一根黑刺李魔杖，一个学生犹豫着。\n# en: Hand it over\nopt.left.label [5e3c6d] = 交给她\n# en: Brask snaps it over her knee. It screams, very faintly. \"Good,\" she says, and drops the pieces in the stove, and gives you a hippogriff to muck out, as a kindness.\nopt.left.say.1 [c6d46d] = 布拉斯克把它在膝盖上一折。它尖叫了，很轻很轻。“好，”她说，把碎片扔进炉子，然后派给你一只鹰头马身有翼兽去清理马粪，这算是一种好意。\n# en: Argue you can handle it\nopt.right.label [7ee39b] = 争辩说你能驾驭它\n# en: \"Can you,\" says Brask flatly. She doesn't take it. She doesn't say anything else, either, and she doesn't need to.\nopt.right.say.1 [653dcc] = “你能吗，”布拉斯克平平地说。她没拿走它。她也没再说别的，她也不需要说。\n\n@event item_thorn_snap\n# en: Snap It\ntitle [d6e9fa] = 折断它\n# en: Alone on the ramparts, at midnight, you take out the thorn wand and look at it. It knows what you're thinking. It's very still, the way a cat is still.\ntext [75c0d5] = 午夜，你独自站在城墙上，拿出荆棘魔杖看着它。它知道你在想什么。它非常安静，像猫那样安静。\n# en: A student alone on a castle rampart at midnight, holding a blackthorn wand bent nearly to breaking.\nscene [faf4c6] = 午夜，一个学生独自站在城堡城墙上，手里的黑刺李魔杖弯得几乎要断了。\n# en: Snap it\nopt.left.label [87381a] = 折断它\n# en: It takes both hands. It doesn't want to go, and then it goes, with a crack like ice. You drop the pieces over the parapet and feel lighter than you have in months.\nopt.left.say.1 [9c6449] = 要用两只手。它不想走，然后它走了，咔嚓一声，像冰裂开。你把碎片扔下城墙，几个月来从没觉得这么轻松。\n# en: Not yet\nopt.right.label [8dd27c] = 还不是时候\n# en: You put it away. It's warm against your side all the way back to bed, purring, if wands purr.\nopt.right.say.1 [d3cb4f] = 你把它收了起来。回床的一路上它都暖暖地贴着你，在打呼噜——如果魔杖会打呼噜的话。\n\n@event item_locket_midnight\n# en: The Locket Opens\ntitle [f63677] = 吊盒打开了\n# en: At the twelfth bell the moonpetal locket opens in your hand on its own. The pressed petal inside glows, very faintly, and for a moment you smell the Hush: cold stone, moonlight, silence.\ntext [029816] = 第十二声钟响时，月瓣吊盒在你手里自己打开了。里面压干的花瓣发着光，非常微弱，有那么一刻你闻到了寂境的气味：冰冷的石头，月光，寂静。\n# en: A plain silver locket opening on its own in a student's palm at midnight, a pressed silver flower glowing inside.\nscene [e4f70c] = 午夜，一只朴素的银吊盒在学生掌心里自己打开，里面一朵压干的银色花朵发着光。\n# en: Close it gently\nopt.left.label [21892d] = 轻轻合上\n# en: You close it. The glow stays on your fingers for a while, like pollen.\nopt.left.say.1 [f8ec41] = 你把它合上了。那光在你指尖上留了一会儿，像花粉。\n# en: Let it glow\nopt.right.label [a8f060] = 让它亮着\n# en: You hold it open until the glow fades, and you're full of quiet, and very cold, and you sleep badly and well.\nopt.right.say.1 [ac803e] = 你一直开着它，直到光芒褪去，你满心安静，浑身冰冷，睡得既糟糕又安稳。\n\n@event item_silk_corvin\n# en: Corvin Is Jealous\ntitle [7d6f3d] = 科尔文嫉妒了\n# en: Corvin walks round you twice. \"That,\" he says, \"is Spinner silk. You can't buy Spinner silk. You have to...\" He stops. \"You didn't.\" He looks at you with a whole new kind of respect, and also as if calculating what the robe would fetch.\ntext [00284e] = 科尔文绕着你走了两圈。“那个，”他说，“是织网者的丝。织网者的丝是买不到的。你得……”他停住了。“你不会吧。”他看你的眼神里多了一种全新的敬意，也像在盘算这件长袍能卖多少钱。\n# en: A lanky student in a patched coat circling another student in a shimmering grey silk robe, looking deeply envious.\nscene [ef88b7] = 一个穿着打补丁外套的瘦高学生绕着另一个穿闪光灰色丝袍的学生打转，满脸羡慕。\n# en: Let him touch the sleeve\nopt.left.label [c7fa68] = 让他摸摸袖子\n# en: He touches the sleeve with one finger, reverently. \"I would sell my grandfather's deck for this,\" he says. \"Don't tell him I said that. He's dead. He'd still mind.\"\nopt.left.say.1 [5f91ff] = 他用一根手指碰了碰袖子，满怀敬畏。“为了这个，我愿意卖掉我爷爷的牌，”他说。“别告诉他我说过这话。他已经死了。他还是会介意的。”\n# en: Keep it out of his reach\nopt.right.label [3dfc7d] = 别让他够着\n# en: You step back. Corvin grins. \"Smart,\" he says. \"I'd have had it off you by Thursday.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [9bf958] = 你退后一步。科尔文咧嘴一笑。“聪明，”他说。“不然星期四之前我就把它从你身上弄走了。”\n\n@event item_grimoire_isolde\n# en: The Headmistress Asks\ntitle [298912] = 院长问起\n# en: The headmistress stops you on the stair. She doesn't look at you. She looks at the black grimoire. \"Absalom Vrell,\" she says. \"Expelled long before my time. His name is still in the ledger, crossed out. I wondered when he'd find a way to give that away.\" Now she looks at you. \"What do you mean to do with it?\"\ntext [0f7a77] = 院长在楼梯上拦住了你。她没看你。她看着那本黑色魔典。“阿布萨隆·弗雷尔，”她说。“早在我来之前就被开除了。他的名字还在名册上，被划掉了。我一直在想，他什么时候会找到办法把那东西送出去。”现在她看向你。“你打算拿它做什么？”\n# en: A silver-haired headmistress on a castle stair, looking at a black-bound book in a student's hands, her face very still.\nscene [157bc3] = 城堡楼梯上，一位银发的院长看着学生手里一本黑色封皮的书，脸上一动不动。\n# en: Offer it to her\nopt.left.label [48f690] = 把它交给她\n# en: She takes it without touching the pages and locks it in a drawer. \"Thank you,\" she says. \"Some things are safest where people can see them not being used.\" She means it as praise. You think.\nopt.left.say.1 [51a8b5] = 她接过去，没碰书页，把它锁进了抽屉。“谢谢你，”她说。“有些东西，放在人们能看见它没被使用的地方，才最安全。”她这是在夸你。你想。\n# en: Keep it, and say so\nopt.right.label [03f1dd] = 留着它，并且直说\n# en: She looks at you for a long time. \"Then use it as he never did,\" she says, \"carefully.\" She doesn't ask for it again. That's somehow worse.\nopt.right.say.1 [8dc34a] = 她看了你很久。“那就用它做他从没做过的事，”她说，“小心地用。”她没再要过它。这反而更糟。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/whisperwood/delve.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/whisperwood/delve.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event ww_edge\n# en: Off the Path\ntitle [8c4f58] = 离开小路\n# en: {ww_deepest == 0: The path through the Whisperwood is safe, everyone says. Off it, the wood goes down and down, and things grow there that Pell pays good money for. So do other things.|{ww_deepest >= 10: The wood knows you now. The path bends politely toward the deep places, as if to save you the trouble.|{~The path, and off it, the deep green dark you've been into before.|Fog between the trunks, and the deep wood breathing behind it.|The wood is quiet at the edge. Further in, it never is.}}} {flag(ww_stag_friend): The shadow stag will meet you halfway now.|{flag(ww_trail): You know where the bee-marked trail begins.|}}\ntext [b09fc1] = {ww_deepest == 0: 大家都说，穿过低语森林的小路是安全的。离开小路，林子就一路往下、往下，那里长着佩尔肯出好价钱收的东西。也长着别的东西。|{ww_deepest >= 10: 林子现在认得你了。小路客客气气地朝深处拐过去，像是要替你省点事。|{~小路，和小路之外，那片你进去过的深绿色的黑暗。|树干之间起了雾，深林在雾后面呼吸。|林子边上很安静。再往里，就从来不安静了。}}} {flag(ww_stag_friend): 影鹿现在会在半路接你。|{flag(ww_trail): 你知道刻着蜜蜂的那条小径从哪儿开始。|}}\n# en: A narrow forest path at the edge of an old wood, fog between the trunks, a student looking off the path into the green dark.\nscene [5779de] = 一片古老森林边缘的狭窄小路，树干间弥漫着雾气，一个学生望着小路外那片绿色的黑暗。\n# en: Stay on the path\nopt.left.label [58d099] = 留在小路上\n# en: {~You keep to the path, where the wood only mutters.|Not today. The path is quite interesting enough.|You stay on the path. The dark between the trees watches you not go in.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fbe5e9] = {~你守着小路走，林子在这里只是嘟囔几句。|今天不了。小路就已经够有意思了。|你留在小路上。树间的黑暗看着你没有进去。}\n# en: Step off the path\nopt.right.label [30b7a0] = 离开小路\n# en: A delve: press on card by card, turn back any time.\nopt.right.hint [e6594e] = 一次深入：一张张卡往里走，随时可以回头。\n# en: {flag(ww_stag_friend): A shadow moves between the trunks and waits. You follow it down past the easy places, into the heartwood.|{flag(ww_trail): You find the first little carved bee on a birch, and follow the trail straight into the thicket.|{bonds_around(partner) + bonds_around(close) > 0: You step off the path, and someone you trust steps off after you.|You step off the path. The mutter of the wood drops to a whisper.}}}\nopt.right.say.1 [1a1ca4] = {flag(ww_stag_friend): 一道影子在树干间移动，停下来等着。你跟着它往下，越过那些容易的地方，进入心木林。|{flag(ww_trail): 你在一棵白桦上找到第一只刻着的小蜜蜂，顺着小径一路走进了灌木林。|{bonds_around(partner) + bonds_around(close) > 0: 你离开了小路，一个你信任的人也跟着你离开了小路。|你离开了小路。林子的嘟囔声低成了耳语。}}}\n\n@event ww_edge_night\n# en: The Hush Is Awake\ntitle [d4db23] = 寂境醒了\n# en: {flag(ww_hush_seen): The wood at midnight, and deep in it the Hush, awake and listening.|Midnight in the Whisperwood. You were told the deep part keeps the castle's hours. Somewhere ahead, the forest has gone quiet in a way it never is by day.} {flag(ww_mastery): Maud's old map is in your pocket.|}\ntext [4af414] = {flag(ww_hush_seen): 午夜的林子，在它深处，寂境醒着，正在倾听。|午夜的低语森林。有人告诉过你，深处守的是城堡的时辰。前方某处，森林安静下来，那种安静白天从来没有过。} {flag(ww_mastery): 莫德的旧地图就在你口袋里。|}\n# en: A forest at midnight under a pale moon, the trees silvered, a deep silence pooling between them like water.\nscene [d00e2e] = 苍白月光下的午夜森林，树木泛着银光，深沉的寂静像水一样积在树与树之间。\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.left.label [4bf72b] = 今晚不了\n# en: {~You stand at the edge until the cold gets in, and go no further.|You listen to the silence for a while, and keep to the path.|Not tonight. The Hush can wait; it's good at it.}\nopt.left.say.1 [459f80] = {~你在林子边站到寒气渗进身子，没再往前走。|你听了一会儿寂静，然后守着小路走。|今晚不了。寂境等得起；它最擅长等。}\n# en: Go down into the dark\nopt.right.label [65e5f6] = 下到黑暗里去\n# en: A midnight delve: the Hush lies past the heartwood.\nopt.right.hint [1bd15e] = 一次午夜深入：寂境在心木林之后。\n# en: {flag(ww_mastery): You follow Maud's map down by moonlight, past everything that would have stopped you once.|{flag(ww_stag_friend): The shadow stag is waiting by the path, darker than the dark. It leads you down.|You step off the path. At midnight the wood is a different wood, and it knows you came.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [713e40] = {flag(ww_mastery): 你借着月光照莫德的地图往下走，越过了所有曾经会拦住你的东西。|{flag(ww_stag_friend): 影鹿在路边等着，比黑暗还要黑。它领着你往下走。|你离开了小路。午夜的林子是另一片林子，它知道你来了。}}\n\n@event ww_night_first\n# en: The Wood After Midnight\ntitle [d80a5d] = 午夜之后的森林\n# en: The Whisperwood at midnight is not the wood you know. The path is the same, pale under the moon, but past the first trees the usual mutter of leaves and small feet stops, as if someone had shut a door on it. Deep in, the silence is listening.\ntext [edad22] = 午夜的低语森林不是你认识的那片林子。小路还是那条，在月光下泛白，可越过最前面几棵树，平常树叶的低语和小爪子的窸窣都停了，像有人关上了一扇门。深处，寂静在聆听。\n# en: The edge of a forest at midnight, the path pale under the moon, and beyond the first trees a darkness so still it looks solid.\nscene [ef6983] = 午夜的林缘，小路在月光下泛白，最前面几棵树后是一片静得像实体的黑暗。\n# en: Go back to bed\nopt.left.label [ef6924] = 回去睡觉\n# en: {~You back away from the edge, and the night noises start again behind you, all at once, as if released. You'll come back when you know what keeps those hours.|You don't go in. On the walk back the castle windows look very warm, and the silence behind you feels like something that will wait.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a91565] = {~你从林缘退开，身后夜里的声响一下子全回来了，像被放了出来。等你知道是什么守着这样的时辰，再回来吧。|你没有进去。回去的路上，城堡的窗户看起来格外温暖，而身后的寂静像是会一直等下去。}\n# en: Go towards the silence\nopt.right.label [b7e0db] = 朝寂静走去\n# en: A midnight delve: the deep wood keeps the castle's hours.\nopt.right.hint [6268be] = 午夜深入：森林深处守着城堡的时辰。\n# en: {~You walk until the silence presses on your ears. This is what Maud meant, or would have, if you'd asked: the deep wood keeps the castle's hours.|You follow the path to where the quiet starts. It isn't empty. It's awake.}\nopt.right.say.1 [809189] = {~你一直走到寂静压得耳朵发胀。这就是莫德的意思——假如你问过她的话：森林深处守着城堡的时辰。|你沿着小路走到安静开始的地方。那里不是空的。它醒着。}\n\n@event ww_way_home\n# en: The Way Home\ntitle [e16c4a] = 回家的路\n# en: {ww_haul == 0: You come out of the trees with nothing but burrs and the feeling you were watched.|{ww_haul <= 2: You come out of the trees with a light bag: a few good mushrooms, a root Pell will want.|{ww_haul <= 4: You come out of the trees with a bag that clinks and rustles: roots, caps, a shed thing or two.|{ww_haul <= 6: You come out of the trees bent under a heavy bag. Pell will have to sit down.|You come out of the trees with more than you can carry, and carry it anyway.}}}} {ww_depth >= 8: The moon is low.|{ww_depth >= 5: Your hands still smell of heartwood.|}}\ntext [f2f303] = {ww_haul == 0: 你从树林里出来，除了一身苍耳，就只有被盯着看的感觉。|{ww_haul <= 2: 你从树林里出来，包里轻飘飘的：几朵好蘑菇，一截佩尔会要的根。|{ww_haul <= 4: 你从树林里出来，包里叮当作响、窸窣有声：根、菌盖，还有一两样蜕下来的东西。|{ww_haul <= 6: 你从树林里出来，被沉甸甸的包压弯了腰。佩尔得坐下来慢慢看了。|你从树林里出来，带的东西多得拿不动，可你还是拿着。}}}} {ww_depth >= 8: 月亮已经很低了。|{ww_depth >= 5: 你手上还有心木的气味。|}}\n# en: A tired student coming out of a dark forest into evening light, a lumpy foraging bag over one shoulder.\nscene [a8e88e] = 一个疲惫的学生背着一只鼓鼓囊囊的采集袋，从漆黑的森林走进傍晚的光里。\n# en: Sell it to Pell\nopt.left.label [4f6722] = 卖给佩尔\n# en: Crowns, by what you carry.\nopt.left.hint [0ece3c] = 克朗，按你带出来的多少算。\n# en: {ww_haul == 0: Pell looks at your empty bag and offers you a biscuit, which is worse than nothing.|{~Pell weighs it all on the brass scale and pays without haggling, which means you should have asked for more.|Pell's daughters sort your bag in a minute and argue about it for ten. The crowns are fair.|Pell holds a cap up to the lamp, sniffs it, and pays you double for that one alone.}}\nopt.left.say.1 [7bb95e] = {ww_haul == 0: 佩尔看了看你的空袋子，递给你一块饼干，这比什么都没有还糟。|{~佩尔把东西全放在铜秤上称了，没讨价还价就付了钱，这说明你该多要些。|佩尔的女儿们一分钟就分好了你的袋子，又为此争了十分钟。价钱很公道。|佩尔把一朵菌盖举到灯下闻了闻，单为这一朵就付了你双倍。}}\n# en: Keep it for the lab\nopt.right.label [bfc97e] = 留给实验室\n# en: Alchemy and lore, by what you carry.\nopt.right.hint [52aee6] = 炼金和学识，按你带出来的多少算。\n# en: {ww_haul == 0: There's nothing to keep but the walk, and you keep that.|{~You spread it all out on a lab bench and spend the evening learning what each thing is, and what it's for, and what it does to your fingers.|You press, dry, label and brew until midnight. Half of it was worth more to Pell. The other half you'd never have understood.|You brew from the wood's own stock and the lab smells of forest for a week.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [c3bd86] = {ww_haul == 0: 没什么可留的，只有这趟路，你把它留下了。|{~你把东西全摊在实验台上，花了一晚上弄清楚每样是什么、有什么用、会对你的手指做什么。|你压、晒、贴标签、熬制，一直弄到午夜。一半东西卖给佩尔会更值钱。另一半你本来永远不会明白。|你用林子自己的存货熬药，实验室里一个星期都是森林的味道。}}\n\n@event ww_stumble\n# en: Back on Your Feet\ntitle [8218be] = 重新站起来\n# en: {~You go down hard, and something spills out of your bag into the dark. When you get up, the wood is waiting to see what you do.|You come to your senses in the bracken, bruised and short of breath and short of something from your bag.|A hand, or a branch, or luck, catches you before the worst. You lose a little of what you carried getting free.} {ww_grace > 0: You've got one more fall in you, maybe.|That was your last easy fall.}\ntext [897110] = {~你重重摔倒，包里有东西滚进了黑暗里。等你爬起来，林子正等着看你怎么办。|你在蕨丛里回过神来，浑身淤青，喘不上气，包里也少了点什么。|一只手，或一根树枝，或是运气，在最坏的事发生前拉住了你。挣脱的时候，你丢了一点带着的东西。} {ww_grace > 0: 你或许还能再摔一次。|那是你最后一次轻轻摔倒了。}\n# en: A student sitting in wet bracken in a dark forest, catching their breath, a spilled foraging bag beside them.\nscene [c4a5dd] = 漆黑的森林里，一个学生坐在湿漉漉的蕨丛中喘气，身边是撒了一地的采集袋。\n# en: That's enough for today\nopt.left.label [3d2940] = 今天就到这儿吧\n# en: {~You pick up what's left and limp back toward the light.|You decide the wood has made its point.|You turn back, and the wood lets you, this time.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c96949] = {~你捡起剩下的东西，一瘸一拐地朝亮光走回去。|你认定林子已经把话说明白了。|你往回走，林子这次放过了你。}\n# en: Get up and go on\nopt.right.label [813dfe] = 站起来继续走\n# en: Deeper: the next fall may not be kind.\nopt.right.hint [cae0c4] = 更深处：下一次摔倒可能就没这么客气了。\n# en: {~You brush off the leaves and go on, a little slower.|You get up. The wood seems surprised, and then interested.|You go on. Somewhere behind you, something stops following.}\nopt.right.say.1 [944a56] = {~你拍掉身上的落叶，继续往前，慢了一点。|你站了起来。林子似乎很意外，然后来了兴趣。|你继续往前。身后某处，有什么东西不再跟着了。}\n\n@event ww_lost\n# en: Found at Dawn\ntitle [ad3a7a] = 黎明时被找到\n# en: {ww_hush == 1: You don't remember the rest of the night. At grey dawn a lantern bobs between the trees, and Old Maud is there with a blanket and a flask of something hot. \"There you are, dearie,\" she says, as if you'd been late for tea.|You don't remember the rest of the day. At dusk Professor Brask finds you in the ferns, cold and scratched and missing your bag. She looks you over the way she looks over a hurt foal.}\ntext [16afc3] = {ww_hush == 1: 那一夜剩下的事你都不记得了。灰蒙蒙的黎明时分，一盏灯笼在树间晃动，老莫德来了，带着一条毯子和一壶热乎乎的东西。“你在这儿啊，亲爱的，”她说，好像你只是喝茶迟到了。|那一天剩下的事你都不记得了。黄昏时，布拉斯克教授在蕨丛里找到了你，冻得发抖，浑身划伤，袋子也没了。她打量你的样子，就像在打量一匹受伤的小马。}\n# en: A grey dawn in a forest, an old woman with a lantern helping a muddy, shivering student to their feet.\nscene [c7b5e3] = 灰蒙蒙的黎明，森林里，一位提着灯笼的老妇人扶起一个浑身泥泞、瑟瑟发抖的学生。\n# en: Thank her and go quietly\nopt.left.label [0b09fa] = 谢过她，安静地走\n# en: {ww_hush == 1: Maud walks you back to her cottage and doesn't ask a single question, which is worse than a lecture.|Brask walks you to the stables in silence. At the door she says, \"The wood doesn't hate you. It just doesn't notice you yet.\" It's the kindest thing she's ever said.}\nopt.left.say.1 [48ed6f] = {ww_hush == 1: 莫德把你送回她的小屋，一个问题也没问，这比训一顿还难受。|布拉斯克一言不发地把你送到马厩。到了门口，她说：“林子不恨你。它只是还没注意到你。”这是她说过的最温柔的话。}\n# en: Ask how she knew to look\nopt.right.label [9b048f] = 问她怎么知道来找\n# en: {ww_hush == 1: \"The bees were cross,\" says Maud. \"They're always cross when someone's lost.\" She doesn't seem to be joking.|\"The owls were talking about you,\" says Brask. \"Unflatteringly.\" She doesn't seem to be joking.}\nopt.right.say.1 [41d9f4] = {ww_hush == 1: “蜜蜂不高兴了，”莫德说。“有人迷路的时候它们总是不高兴。”她看起来不像在开玩笑。|“猫头鹰在议论你，”布拉斯克说。“说得不太好听。”她看起来不像在开玩笑。}\n\n@thread ww_delve\n# en: The Whisperwood\nname [0cc2ff] = 低语森林\n# en: The wood past the village goes deeper every time you go in.\npremise [a4c847] = 村子那头的林子，你每进去一次，它就更深一些。\n# en: You followed little carved bees a mile into the Whisperwood. You know whose bees they are.\njournal.1 [55275b] = 你跟着树上刻的小蜜蜂走进低语森林一英里深。你知道那是谁的蜜蜂。\n# en: You bowed in the bracken beside Professor Brask. Now the wood knows your face.\njournal.2 [bf7703] = 你在蕨丛里陪布拉斯克教授一起鞠了躬。现在林子认得你的脸了。\n# en: Ember's kin decided you'd do. A small smouldering tail walks with you off the path now.\njournal.3 [30ca5e] = 余烬的族人认定你还行。现在你离开小路时，总有一条冒烟的小尾巴陪着你。\n# en: The Shadow Stag lowered its great head to you.\njournal.4 [c274ce] = 影鹿向你低下了它巨大的头。\n# en: You asked the Elder Oak for its heartwood.\njournal.5 [0427c0] = 你向古橡讨了一块心木。\n# en: Deep in the wood there's a clearing where it's always midnight, its moonpetal waiting.\njournal.6 [091611] = 林子深处有一片永远是午夜的空地，那里的月瓣花在等待。\n# en: At midnight the silence opened, and you saw the Hush.\njournal.7 [f8b4e9] = 午夜时分，寂静开启，你看见了寂境。\n# en: You fought the Spinner, queen of the Hush, and won.\njournal.8 [f45d1a] = 你与寂境的女王织网者交了手，并且赢了。\n# en: You faced Absalom Vrell by his lantern, and walked away.\njournal.9 [ce3db1] = 你在灯笼旁面对了阿布萨隆·弗雷尔，然后走了出来。\n# en: You reached the heart of the Hush. The wood is yours, as much as a wood is ever anyone's.\njournal.10 [19a6ee] = 你走到了寂境的中心。这片林子是你的了，就像林子能属于任何人的那种程度。\n\n@event ww1_forage\n# en: The Mushroom Ring\ntitle [7d1f98] = 蘑菇圈\n# en: {season == \"autumn\": A fairy ring in full flush, caps as big as saucers, more than you could carry.|A ring of pale caps in the moss, and among them the frilled ones Pell pays for.} Never step inside a ring, Maud says. She never says why.\ntext [6c7987] = {season == \"autumn\": 一圈仙女环正冒得旺，菌盖大得像碟子，多得你拿不完。|苔藓里一圈苍白的菌盖，中间夹着佩尔肯花钱收的那种带褶边的。} 莫德说，永远别踏进圈里去。她从没说为什么。\n# en: A perfect ring of pale mushrooms in a mossy forest clearing, a student kneeling with a knife and a basket.\nscene [923abf] = 长满苔藓的林间空地上，一圈完美的苍白蘑菇，一个学生拿着小刀和篮子跪在旁边。\n# en: Leave it and turn back\nopt.left.label [d1faaa] = 不动它，往回走\n# en: {~You leave the ring to whoever made it and head back to the path.|You count the caps, don't take any, and go home feeling oddly polite.}\nopt.left.say.1 [17baa8] = {~你把这个圈留给造出它的人，回到小路上去了。|你数了数菌盖，一朵没拿，回家的路上莫名觉得自己很有礼貌。}\n# en: Fill your bag from outside\nopt.right.label [ca3148] = 从圈外装满袋子\n# en: {~You pick from the outside, kneeling in cold moss. Your knees are soaked and your bag is heavier.|You reach in for the best cap without stepping in. Something in the ring sighs. You move on, briskly.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0871b0] = {~你跪在冰冷的苔藓上从圈外采。膝盖湿透了，袋子沉了。|你没踏进去，伸手够到了最好的那朵。圈里有什么叹了口气。你赶紧走了。}\n\n@event ww1_bramble_ferret\n# en: Something in Your Bag\ntitle [31c243] = 包里有东西\n# en: A bramble ferret has got into your bag, and is eating {ww_haul > 0: your best find|your lunch}. It's all bristles and bad temper, and it fights like a pin cushion that has opinions.\ntext [135671] = 一只荆棘雪貂钻进了你的包里，正在吃{ww_haul > 0: 你最好的收获|你的午饭}。它浑身是刺、脾气很坏，打起架来像一个有主见的针插。\n# en: A small bristly ferret with brambles tangled in its fur, half inside a student's foraging bag, glaring out.\nscene [a477e5] = 一只毛里缠着荆棘的小雪貂，半个身子钻在学生的采集袋里，正怒目而视。\n# en: Let it have it; turn back\nopt.left.label [af6fcb] = 让给它；往回走\n# en: {~You tip the bag out and let it keep what it wanted. You go home lighter and wiser.|You leave it to its meal and walk back, picking bristles out of your sleeve.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4620ff] = {~你把袋子倒空，让它留下它想要的。你回家时轻了些，也聪明了些。|你让它吃它的，一边往回走一边从袖子上拔刺。}\n# en: Fight it for the bag\nopt.right.label [f29a2c] = 跟它抢袋子\n# en: {~It bolts, dropping what it stole and something shiny it stole earlier. You take both.|It gives up with a furious chitter and leaves behind its own hoard of nuts and one silver button.}\nopt.right.say.1 [84dcdb] = {~它逃了，丢下了偷走的东西，还有一样它早先偷来的亮闪闪的东西。你两样都拿了。|它气急败坏地吱吱叫着放弃了，留下了它自己藏的一堆坚果和一枚银纽扣。}\n# en: {~It nips, dodges, nips, and is gone with your bag's best corner. You're bleeding from places you didn't know could.|You never land a thing on it. It leaves you in the bracken, panting and full of small holes.}\nopt.right.say.2 [44d3c3] = {~它咬一口、躲一下、再咬一口，叼着你袋子里最好的那一角跑了。你身上流血的地方，你都不知道还能流血。|你一下都没打着它。它把你丢在蕨丛里，气喘吁吁，浑身都是小窟窿。}\n# en: Bribe it with honey\nopt.up.label [272ad1] = 用蜂蜜贿赂它\n# en: {~You open Maud's honey tin and hold out a fingerful. The ferret forgets the bag entirely, eats the honey, and follows you a mile, hoping.|One sniff of the silver-bee honey and the ferret is your friend for life, or at least until the honey runs out.}\nopt.up.say.1 [0a99fc] = {~你打开莫德的蜂蜜罐，伸出一指头蜂蜜。雪貂把袋子忘得一干二净，吃完蜂蜜，满怀希望地跟了你一英里。|闻了一下银蜂蜜，雪貂就成了你一辈子的朋友，至少在蜂蜜吃完之前是这样。}\n\n@event ww1_bristleback\n# en: The Bristleback\ntitle [2f4350] = 刚鬃熊\n# en: A bristleback rears up out of the blackberries: a bear with a ridge of quills down its spine and a voice like a cellar door. It isn't hungry. It's offended. It swings slowly, and you can see each blow coming a long way off.\ntext [1d3a3a] = 一头刚鬃熊从黑莓丛里人立起来：一头脊背上长着一排硬刺的熊，嗓音像地窖门。它不饿。它是被冒犯了。它挥掌很慢，每一击你都能老远看见它过来。\n# en: A huge shaggy bear-like creature with a ridge of bristles along its back rising on its hind legs in a forest clearing.\nscene [7a96d7] = 林间空地上，一头背上长着一排硬鬃的巨大毛熊用后腿站了起来。\n# en: Back away slowly\nopt.left.label [b7e862] = 慢慢退开\n# en: {~You back away the way Brask taught you: slowly, eyes down, apologising. It lets you go and goes back to its berries.|You leave the clearing to the bristleback, who seems to think that's the least you could do.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1f60cd] = {~你按布拉斯克教的那样往后退：慢慢地，低着眼，一边道歉。它放你走了，回去吃它的莓子。|你把空地让给了刚鬃熊，它似乎觉得这是你起码该做的。}\n# en: Stand your ground\nopt.right.label [3881f5] = 寸步不让\n# en: It telegraphs its blows: brace on the swing.\nopt.right.hint [e750b5] = 它出手前有预兆：在它挥掌时坚守。\n# en: {~It decides you're not worth the trouble and lumbers off with great dignity. Word gets round the foragers that you saw off a bristleback.|You weather three swings and land enough to make it think again. It goes, grumbling, and you go on, shaking.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0e1724] = {~它认定你不值得费事，非常有尊严地慢吞吞走开了。采集者们都传开了，说你吓退了一头刚鬃熊。|你扛住了三掌，又打中了几下，让它重新考虑了一番。它嘟囔着走了，你也继续往前，腿还在抖。}\n# en: {~You see the swing coming the whole way and still don't move fast enough.|It knocks you into the blackberries, which is somehow the worst part.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1ff345] = {~那一掌你从头看到尾，还是没躲开。|它把你拍进了黑莓丛，不知怎么的这才是最糟的部分。}\n# en: Wave fire at it\nopt.up.label [943f52] = 冲它挥火\n# en: {~One flare of flame and the bristleback remembers an appointment elsewhere.|You light the air between you. It snorts at the smoke, deeply offended, and leaves.}\nopt.up.say.1 [e291c6] = {~火光一闪，刚鬃熊就想起来它在别处还有约。|你在你们之间点燃了空气。它冲着烟喷了口鼻息，深感被冒犯，走了。}\n\n@event ww1_bog\n# en: The Sucking Bog\ntitle [63cbf2] = 吸人的沼泽\n# en: The moss looks solid until you're on it. Then you're knee-deep in black water that pulls, gently and with great patience, like a grandmother who wants you to stay for supper.\ntext [95dc55] = 苔藓看起来很结实，直到你踩上去。然后你就站在没膝的黑水里了，那水在往下拉，轻轻地，极有耐心，像一个想留你吃晚饭的老奶奶。\n# en: A student knee-deep in black bog water among reeds, reaching for a low branch, frogs watching from a log.\nscene [82b9ad] = 芦苇丛中，一个学生站在没膝的黑色沼泽水里，伸手去够一根低垂的树枝，青蛙们在一根木头上看着。\n# en: Haul yourself back out\nopt.left.label [c1e40b] = 把自己拖出来\n# en: {~You haul yourself back the way you came, losing a boot and gaining a smell.|You get out backwards and go home with the bog all the way up to your knees.}\nopt.left.say.1 [77e3c0] = {~你沿着来路把自己拖了出来，丢了一只靴子，得了一身味儿。|你倒退着出来，带着一直到膝盖的沼泽回了家。}\n# en: Wade on across\nopt.right.label [46fe8c] = 蹚过去\n# en: {~You wade, haul, wade and come out the far side, black to the thighs and still going.|You find the tussocks under the water by feel, and cross like a heron. A muddy heron.}\nopt.right.say.1 [e27463] = {~你蹚着、拽着、蹚着，从对岸出来了，大腿以下全黑了，可还在往前走。|你摸索着找到水下的草墩，像一只苍鹭一样走了过去。一只满身泥的苍鹭。}\n# en: {~The bog takes you to the waist, then to the chest. You get out, eventually, much colder and much humbler.|You lose your footing and go under. You come up spitting moss.}\nopt.right.say.2 [2206fc] = {~沼泽没到了你的腰，然后是胸口。你最后还是出来了，冷多了，也谦卑多了。|你脚下一滑，沉了下去。浮上来的时候嘴里全是苔藓。}\n# en: Skim across\nopt.up.label [1dd5b6] = 掠过去\n# en: {~A gust under your feet and you skim the bog like a skipped stone. The frogs are very impressed.|You call a wind and run on it. Your boots barely get damp.}\nopt.up.say.1 [c3d407] = {~脚下一阵风，你像打水漂的石子一样掠过沼泽。青蛙们非常佩服。|你唤来一阵风，踩着它跑了过去。靴子几乎没湿。}\n\n@event ww1_pixie_toll\n# en: The Toll Stone\ntitle [95852c] = 收费石\n# en: Three pixies sit on a mossy stone across the path. \"Toll,\" says the smallest. \"Toll,\" agrees the middle one. The third is eating a beetle and nods. Nobody has ever established what the toll is, only that it isn't what you offer.\ntext [d835c1] = 三只小精灵坐在横在路上的一块长满苔藓的石头上。“过路费，”最小的那只说。“过路费，”中间那只附和。第三只在吃一只甲虫，点了点头。从来没人弄清楚过路费到底是什么，只知道不是你给的那样东西。\n# en: Several tiny winged pixies perched on a mossy stone across a forest path, one holding out a tiny hand for payment.\nscene [bdc654] = 几只长着翅膀的小精灵蹲在森林小路上一块长满苔藓的石头上，其中一只伸出小手要钱。\n# en: Turn round\nopt.left.label [ee8dd6] = 掉头\n# en: {~You turn back. The pixies jeer, delighted, in tiny voices.|You don't have time to be fleeced by beetles' cousins. You go home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [756f15] = {~你往回走。小精灵们用尖细的声音开心地起哄。|你没工夫被甲虫的表亲敲竹杠。你回家了。}\n# en: Haggle your way past\nopt.right.label [fd8b47] = 讨价还价地过去\n# en: {~You argue them down to a compliment and a button, and they're so pleased they throw in a dropped cap from the ring.|You convince them that you are the toll, coming the other way. They pay you, confused, and wave you on.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2cee55] = {~你把价砍到了一句恭维和一枚纽扣，它们高兴得还送了你一朵从蘑菇圈里掉下来的菌盖。|你说服它们，你就是过路费，只不过是从另一头来的。它们糊里糊涂地付了你钱，挥手让你过去。}\n# en: {~They take your offer, your next offer, and your bootlaces. Then they trip you anyway, on principle.|They don't care for your terms. They express this with pinecones, at speed.}\nopt.right.say.2 [55d564] = {~它们收下了你的出价，你的下一个出价，还有你的鞋带。然后照样绊了你一跤，出于原则。|它们不喜欢你的条件。它们用松果表达了这一点，速度很快。}\n# en: Pay in fairy gold\nopt.up.label [d2e9cd] = 用仙子金付账\n# en: {~You pay in coins that will be leaves by morning. They bite them, nod, and let you by. You feel a little guilty, and a little proud.|You glamour a pebble into a crown. The smallest pixie looks at you for a long moment, then pockets it. Professional courtesy.}\nopt.up.say.1 [a73345] = {~你付的硬币到早上就会变成树叶。它们咬了咬，点点头，让你过去了。你有点内疚，也有点得意。|你把一颗小石子幻化成一枚克朗。最小的那只盯着你看了很久，然后把它揣进了兜里。同行之间的礼貌。}\n\n@event ww1_lost_firstyear\n# en: A First-Year in the Ferns\ntitle [5abce6] = 蕨丛里的一年级生\n# en: A first-year is sitting in the ferns, holding a library book on edible fungi and trying very hard not to cry. They've been lost for an hour. They have eaten nothing from the book, which is the first sensible thing they've done.\ntext [7e631b] = 一个一年级生坐在蕨丛里，抱着一本讲可食用菌类的图书馆藏书，拼命忍着不哭。他们已经迷路一个小时了。书里的东西他们一样都没吃，这是他们做过的第一件明智的事。\n# en: A small tearful first-year student sitting in tall ferns in a forest, clutching a library book, looking up hopefully.\nscene [16edb4] = 森林高高的蕨丛里坐着一个哭鼻子的小个子一年级生，抱着一本图书馆的书，满怀希望地抬头看。\n# en: Walk them back out\nopt.left.label [9e6c31] = 把他们送出去\n# en: {~You walk them all the way back to the path and point them at the castle. They thank you four times. Your delve is over, and you don't mind much.|You take them home. They ask forty questions on the way, and you answer most of them honestly.}\nopt.left.say.1 [fb8d90] = {~你一路把他们送回小路，指给他们城堡的方向。他们谢了你四次。你的深入结束了，你也不怎么在意。|你把他们送回了家。一路上他们问了四十个问题，你大多老老实实地回答了。}\n# en: Point the way and press on\nopt.right.label [b51fac] = 指了路，继续往前\n# en: {~You point them back toward the path and go on. You look back once. They're walking the right way. Probably.|You draw them a map on the flyleaf of the library book and go on. It's a good map. You keep thinking about it anyway.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1f4ab3] = {~你给他们指了回小路的方向，自己继续往前。你回头看了一眼。他们走的方向是对的。大概吧。|你在那本图书馆藏书的扉页上给他们画了张地图，然后继续往前。是张好地图。可你还是一直惦记着。}\n\n@event ww1_hollow_whisper\n# en: The Riddling Hollow\ntitle [0752a8] = 出谜语的树洞\n# en: A hollow oak whispers as you pass. It's asking a riddle, the same one over and over, and it sounds as if it has been waiting a long time for anyone to try. The answer is not \"a tree\". You can tell that from the tone.\ntext [693f71] = 你经过时，一棵空心橡树在低语。它在出一个谜语，一遍又一遍，听起来像是等了很久，等有人来试一试。答案不是“一棵树”。从语气你就听得出来。\n# en: A hollow in an ancient oak trunk with a faint glow inside, a student leaning close to listen.\nscene [50af6a] = 一棵古老橡树的树干上有个树洞，里面透着微光，一个学生凑近了在听。\n# en: Leave it riddling; turn back\nopt.left.label [11b91d] = 让它接着出谜；往回走\n# en: {~You leave the riddle unanswered. It follows you all the way to the path.|You go home. Halfway there you think of the answer, and it's too late, and it's probably wrong.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f090db] = {~你没回答那个谜语。它一路跟着你到了小路上。|你回家了。走到一半你想出了答案，可已经太迟了，而且多半是错的。}\n# en: Try an answer\nopt.right.label [6d4af1] = 试着回答\n# en: {~You answer, and argue the point when it quibbles. The oak goes quiet, then tells you something about the wood that you'll understand in a fortnight.|\"Close enough,\" it sighs, and opens a path you hadn't seen.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0e4d7c] = {~你回答了，它挑刺时你还跟它争了几句。橡树安静下来，然后告诉了你一件关于林子的事，你要过两个星期才会明白。|“差不多吧，”它叹了口气，打开了一条你没见过的路。}\n# en: {~Wrong, says the oak, and the path rearranges itself so that you walk into a ditch.|It laughs, a terrible creaking laugh, and you find you've been walking in circles for an hour.}\nopt.right.say.2 [adadeb] = {~错了，橡树说，小路重新排列了一番，让你一脚踩进了沟里。|它笑了，一种可怕的嘎吱嘎吱的笑，你发现自己已经绕着圈走了一个小时。}\n\n@event ww1_quiet_path\n# en: The Path Runs On\ntitle [e98ce8] = 小路一直延伸\n# en: {~A deer path, winding and quiet, with the castle bells faint behind you.|Birdsong, and moss, and nothing at all trying to eat you.|A stream to cross on stepping stones, and wild garlic along the bank.} For a while the wood is only a wood.\ntext [023f9b] = {~一条鹿径，蜿蜒而安静，城堡的钟声在身后隐约可闻。|鸟鸣，苔藓，而且没有任何东西想吃掉你。|一条要踩着垫脚石过的小溪，岸边长着野蒜。} 有一阵子，林子就只是一片林子。\n# en: A deer path winding between moss-covered trees, dappled light, a student walking alone with a foraging bag.\nscene [67b091] = 长满苔藓的树木之间蜿蜒着一条鹿径，光影斑驳，一个学生背着采集袋独自走着。\n# en: Turn back while it's easy\nopt.left.label [d572f3] = 趁还轻松往回走\n# en: {~You turn round while the going is good, and are home before the light changes.|You take the quiet as a hint and head back.|You turn back at the stream, with dry feet and a clear head.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bb3195] = {~你趁着好走往回走，天色还没变就到家了。|你把这份安静当作暗示，往回走了。|你在小溪边回头，脚是干的，脑子是清醒的。}\n# en: Go on\nopt.right.label [8d20dc] = 继续走\n# en: {~You walk on, deeper, and the birdsong thins.|You go on, deeper, and the wood stops being only a wood.|You go on. The bells behind you stop being audible somewhere around the stream.}\nopt.right.say.1 [722261] = {~你继续往深处走，鸟鸣渐渐稀了。|你继续往深处走，林子不再只是一片林子了。|你继续走。身后的钟声在小溪附近的某个地方听不见了。}\n\n@event ww1_maud_blaze\n# en: The Little Carved Bees\ntitle [23be92] = 刻着的小蜜蜂\n# en: On a birch, at knee height, someone has carved a tiny bee. There's another a few trees on, and another. They're old, smoothed by sixty years of bark growing round them, and they lead somewhere on purpose.\ntext [a3f8bb] = 一棵白桦上，齐膝高的地方，有人刻了一只小蜜蜂。往前几棵树又有一只，再往前又有一只。它们很老了，六十年来树皮绕着它们生长，已经被磨得很光滑，而且它们是有意指向某个地方的。\n# en: A tiny bee carved into the bark of a birch tree, worn smooth by decades, a student tracing it with a finger.\nscene [524459] = 一棵白桦的树皮上刻着一只小蜜蜂，几十年下来已被磨平，一个学生用手指描着它。\n# en: Note it and turn back\nopt.left.label [cebf5d] = 记下来，往回走\n# en: {~You remember the birch and go home. The bees will still be there.|You touch the first bee for luck and head back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f246a0] = {~你记住了那棵白桦，回家了。蜜蜂们还会在那儿的。|你摸了摸第一只蜜蜂图个吉利，往回走了。}\n# en: Follow the bees\nopt.right.label [924160] = 跟着蜜蜂走\n# en: {~Bee to bee, a mile in, the trail drops straight past the fringe to the thicket's edge. Next time you can start here. You know exactly whose bees these are.|You lose the trail twice and find it twice. By the end you could walk it blind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2d3a6a] = {~一只蜜蜂接一只蜜蜂，走进去一英里，小径径直越过林缘，下到灌木林的边上。下次你可以从这里出发。你很清楚这些是谁的蜜蜂。|你两次跟丢了小径，又两次找了回来。到最后你闭着眼都能走。}\n# en: {~You lose the bees in a hollow full of brambles and spend an hour getting out.|The trail runs out at a fallen tree. Climbing round it, you fall in a ditch.}\nopt.right.say.2 [f25551] = {~你在一个长满荆棘的坑里跟丢了蜜蜂，花了一个小时才出来。|小径在一棵倒下的树那儿断了。你绕过去的时候掉进了沟里。}\n\n@event ww1_owl_feather\n# en: The Barn Owl's Tree\ntitle [be0cab] = 仓鸮的树\n# en: High in a dead oak, a single barn-owl feather is caught on a twig, white as paper and long as your hand. Grimsby once told you an owl's flight feather makes the best quill there is. He didn't say anything about the climb.\ntext [bb1cb1] = 一棵枯死的橡树高处，一根仓鸮羽毛挂在细枝上，白得像纸，长得像你的手。格林斯比有一次告诉你，猫头鹰的飞羽能做成最好的羽毛笔。他可没说要爬上去。\n# en: A tall dead oak with a single white barn-owl feather caught on a high branch, a student looking up at it.\nscene [10c759] = 一棵高大的枯橡树，高处的树枝上挂着一根白色的仓鸮羽毛，一个学生仰头看着它。\n# en: Leave it to the wind; turn back\nopt.left.label [6ac3a4] = 留给风吧；往回走\n# en: {~You leave the feather where it is. When you look back, it's gone.|It's a long way up and a long way home. You choose home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [d66f23] = {~你把羽毛留在原处。你回头看时，它已经不见了。|往上很远，回家也很远。你选了回家。}\n# en: Climb for it\nopt.right.label [5d1352] = 爬上去拿\n# en: {~You climb, reach, nearly fall, reach again. You come down with the feather and a fine view of the castle. Cut to a nib, it will make a quill worth having.|The branch breaks just as your fingers close. You land in leaves, feather intact.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ae3477] = {~你爬，伸手，差点掉下去，再伸手。你拿着羽毛下来了，还顺带看到了城堡的好风景。削成笔尖，它会是一支值得拥有的羽毛笔。|你的手指刚握住，树枝就断了。你落在落叶堆里，羽毛完好无损。}\n# en: {~The dead wood gives under your boot halfway up. You come down a good deal faster than you went up.|You get high enough to see the feather is just out of reach. Then the branch has opinions.}\nopt.right.say.2 [237e38] = {~爬到一半，枯木在你靴子底下塌了。你下来的速度比上去快得多。|你爬到了能看见羽毛刚好够不着的高度。然后树枝有了自己的想法。}\n\n@event ww1_brask_bows\n# en: Brask in the Bracken\ntitle [c03604] = 蕨丛里的布拉斯克\n# en: Professor Brask is standing in the bracken, bowing deeply to nothing you can see. \"Don't stare,\" she says without straightening. \"Bow. It's watching you.\" You can't see what it is. You're fairly sure that's the point.\ntext [61a48a] = 布拉斯克教授站在蕨丛里，正向一个你看不见的东西深深鞠躬。“别盯着看，”她没直起身就说。“鞠躬。它在看着你。”你看不见那是什么。你很确定，这正是关键所在。\n# en: A sturdy older professor in a riding coat bowing low to something unseen in tall bracken, in a misty forest.\nscene [51bfe0] = 雾气弥漫的森林里，一位穿骑装的结实的年长教授在高高的蕨丛中向某个看不见的东西深深鞠躬。\n# en: Back away and go home\nopt.left.label [ed269c] = 退开，回家\n# en: {~You back away. Brask doesn't watch you go, but the bracken does.|You retreat to the path. Next lesson, Brask gives you a look that says she noticed.}\nopt.left.say.1 [c9b9ac] = {~你往后退。布拉斯克没有看你走，可蕨丛在看。|你退回了小路。下一堂课上，布拉斯克看你的眼神说明她注意到了。}\n# en: Bow with her\nopt.right.label [e0a866] = 跟她一起鞠躬\n# en: {~You bow, low, and hold it until Brask straightens. Something in the bracken moves away, satisfied. \"Good,\" says Brask. \"Do that to everything down there. Most of it's older than manners.\"|You bow. Brask nods once. \"Now the wood knows your face,\" she says, and walks off, which is apparently the lesson.}\nopt.right.say.1 [76212f] = {~你深深鞠躬，一直保持到布拉斯克直起身。蕨丛里有什么东西心满意足地走开了。“很好，”布拉斯克说。“对那底下的每样东西都这么做。它们大多比礼节还老。”|你鞠了躬。布拉斯克点了一下头。“现在林子认得你的脸了，”她说完就走了，看来这就是这一课。}\n\n@event ww2_grumpy_oak\n# en: The Oak You Kicked\ntitle [4ae4e1] = 被你踢了的橡树\n# en: You stub your toe on a root, and say something about it. The root's oak takes this badly. It has a face in its bark now, and the face is scowling, and the path ahead is suddenly a great deal of oak.\ntext [f62f48] = 你在一条树根上踢到了脚趾，还骂了一句。那条树根的橡树很不高兴。它的树皮上现在有了一张脸，脸上满是怒容，而前面的路忽然变成了一大片橡树。\n# en: A gnarled oak with a scowling face in its bark leaning over a forest path, a student holding a stubbed foot.\nscene [98aa1f] = 一棵树皮上长着怒容的盘曲橡树俯在森林小路上方，一个学生捂着踢痛的脚。\n# en: Retreat with dignity\nopt.left.label [d3ae4a] = 体面地撤退\n# en: {~You back away, apologising to the general direction of the oak, and limp home.|You decide the oak has won this one and turn back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e009a3] = {~你一边往后退，一边朝橡树的大致方向道歉，一瘸一拐地回家了。|你认定这一回合橡树赢了，往回走了。}\n# en: Apologise properly\nopt.right.label [8c7789] = 好好道歉\n# en: {~You apologise to the oak at length, with references. It harrumphs, drops you an acorn the size of a fist as a mark of favour, and lets you by.|Your apology is so formal that the oak is moved. It lifts its root for you, and a hoard of truffles under it.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ef3e7b] = {~你向橡树长篇大论地道歉，还引经据典。它哼了一声，丢给你一颗拳头大的橡果以示恩宠，让你过去了。|你的道歉如此正式，橡树被打动了。它为你抬起树根，底下藏着一窝松露。}\n# en: {~The apology goes badly. It turns out you also stood on its cousin.|The oak hears you out and then creaks something that is very clearly \"no\".}\nopt.right.say.2 [37ea04] = {~道歉搞砸了。原来你还踩了它的表亲。|橡树听你说完，然后嘎吱出一个非常清楚的“不”。}\n# en: Show the heartwood\nopt.up.label [fefd62] = 亮出心木\n# en: {~You hold up the heartwood wand. The oak's scowl softens into something almost grandfatherly, and it lifts a root to let you by.|The oak sees the heartwood and goes quiet. Then it bows, creaking, and steps aside.}\nopt.up.say.1 [3b3c4e] = {~你举起心木魔杖。橡树的怒容柔和下来，几乎像个慈祥的老祖父，抬起一条树根让你过去。|橡树看见心木，安静了下来。然后它嘎吱嘎吱地鞠了一躬，让到一边。}\n\n@event ww2_grumpy_oak_fight\n# en: The Oak Swings\ntitle [46a2f8] = 橡树抡枝\n# en: The oak's roots come up out of the path and close round your ankles, and a bough the size of a boat lifts overhead. Oaks are slow. This one has been practising.\ntext [50d812] = 橡树的根从小路下钻出来，缠住了你的脚踝，一根船那么大的树枝在头顶举了起来。橡树很慢。这一棵一直在练。\n# en: An angry oak tree swinging a great bough down at a student, roots erupting from the ground around their feet.\nscene [1eaf94] = 一棵愤怒的橡树朝一个学生抡下一根巨大的树枝，树根从学生脚边的地里钻出来。\n# en: Tear free and run\nopt.left.label [c81721] = 挣脱，快跑\n# en: {~You leave a boot in the roots and run. The oak keeps the boot.|You wrench free and run, shedding half your bag. The oak looks satisfied.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a884c0] = {~你把一只靴子留在树根里，跑了。橡树留下了靴子。|你用力挣脱，跑了，包里的东西撒了一半。橡树看起来很满意。}\n# en: Fight it\nopt.right.label [533b96] = 跟它打\n# en: It roots you, then swings: guard early.\nopt.right.hint [206cda] = 它先缠住你，再抡枝：早点防御。\n# en: {~You burn, hack and dodge until the oak decides it's had enough exercise for one century, and goes back to scowling at nothing.|The bough comes down where you were. The oak stops, as if surprised that you're still here, and lets you go on.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1493ef] = {~你烧、砍、躲，直到橡树认定这个世纪的运动量已经够了，又回去冲着空气生闷气。|树枝砸在你刚才站的地方。橡树停了下来，好像很惊讶你还在，然后让你继续走了。}\n# en: {~The roots hold, the bough falls, and the oak goes back to being a tree, slightly smug.|You never get your feet free. The bough finds you twice.}\nopt.right.say.2 [6f950c] = {~树根缠住了，树枝落下了，橡树又变回了一棵树，有点得意。|你始终没能把脚挣出来。树枝两次找到了你。}\n\n@event ww2_briar_maze\n# en: The Thorn Maze\ntitle [2df3dc] = 荆棘迷宫\n# en: The briars here have grown into walls taller than you, with corridors between them that turn and fork and double back. Somebody planted this, a long time ago, on purpose, and they weren't fond of visitors.\ntext [4893d8] = 这里的荆棘长成了比你还高的墙，墙间的通道拐来拐去、分岔又折回。很久以前，有人故意种下了它们，而且那人不怎么欢迎访客。\n# en: A maze of tall thorny briars in a dim forest, narrow green corridors branching off in every direction.\nscene [396c12] = 昏暗的森林里，一座由高大的带刺荆棘组成的迷宫，狭窄的绿色通道向四面八方分岔。\n# en: Back out the way you came\nopt.left.label [02e531] = 原路退出\n# en: {~You retrace your steps while you still can, and lose only your sleeve.|You find the way out, and decide that's an achievement in itself.}\nopt.left.say.1 [26e59b] = {~你趁还来得及原路退回，只丢了一只袖子。|你找到了出口，认定这本身就是一项成就。}\n# en: Find the way through\nopt.right.label [51285b] = 找路穿过去\n# en: {~Left at every fork, then right at the one with the dead rose: the old gardener's rule, and now you know it. You come out the far side scratched but triumphant.|You notice that the moss grows on the side the old gardener wanted you to see, and follow it through.}\nopt.right.say.1 [addd95] = {~每个岔口都往左，到长着一朵枯玫瑰的那个岔口往右：这是老园丁的规矩，现在你也知道了。你从另一头出来，满身划痕，但大获全胜。|你注意到苔藓长在老园丁想让你看见的那一面，顺着它走了出去。}\n# en: {~The corridors turn back on themselves until you're dizzy. You come out bleeding from a hundred tiny cuts.|You take a wrong turn into a dead end so full of thorns it has its own weather.}\nopt.right.say.2 [114cd8] = {~通道绕来绕去，绕得你头晕。你出来的时候身上有上百道小口子在流血。|你拐错了弯，走进一个满是荆棘的死胡同，那里刺多得自成一种天气。}\n# en: Follow the lodestone\nopt.up.label [7f0f70] = 跟着磁石走\n# en: {~The lodestone turns, steady and patient, and you follow it through every fork without a scratch.|You let the pendant lead. It knows north, and north is through.}\nopt.up.say.1 [9f23d2] = {~磁石转动着，稳定而耐心，你跟着它穿过每一个岔口，一道划痕都没有。|你让吊坠带路。它认得北方，而北方就是出口。}\n\n@event ww2_stag_beetle\n# en: The Stag Beetle\ntitle [586e32] = 锹形甲虫\n# en: A stag beetle the size of a pony is guarding a fallen log. Its jaws are as long as your arm and its shell would turn a sword. It lowers its head and starts to back up, which, you realise, is a run-up.\ntext [8c05cc] = 一只小马那么大的锹形甲虫守着一根倒木。它的颚有你的胳膊那么长，它的壳能把剑弹开。它低下头，开始往后退，你意识到，这是在助跑。\n# en: An enormous armoured stag beetle the size of a pony lowering its antler-like jaws in a forest clearing.\nscene [1baddd] = 林间空地上，一只小马大小、披着铠甲的巨型锹形甲虫压低了鹿角般的大颚。\n# en: Leave it the log; turn back\nopt.left.label [30465f] = 把木头让给它；往回走\n# en: {~You leave the beetle its log. It watches you all the way out of the clearing, revving.|You go home. Some fights aren't yours, and that one was very much the beetle's.}\nopt.left.say.1 [bc1ffd] = {~你把木头让给了甲虫。它一路盯着你走出空地，一边还在蓄劲。|你回家了。有些仗不是你的，那一仗完完全全是甲虫的。}\n# en: Take it on\nopt.right.label [69ccb8] = 迎战\n# en: It charges in two beats: step aside on the second.\nopt.right.hint [bda719] = 它分两拍冲锋：第二拍时闪开。\n# en: {~It charges, misses, charges, misses, and gives up in disgust, shedding a wing-case like polished jet. Pell will weep.|You catch it mid-charge. It retreats under its log, leaving a wing-case and some dignity behind.}\nopt.right.say.1 [1a5750] = {~它冲，扑空，再冲，再扑空，然后厌烦地放弃了，蜕下一片像抛光黑玉一样的鞘翅。佩尔会感动得落泪。|你在它冲到一半时截住了它。它退回了倒木底下，留下一片鞘翅和一点尊严。}\n# en: {~The second charge catches you square. You fly further than you ever have on a broom.|It charges. You dodge the wrong way. You see a great many leaves, very fast.}\nopt.right.say.2 [6f472a] = {~第二次冲锋正中了你。你飞出去的距离比你骑扫帚飞过的都远。|它冲过来。你躲错了方向。你极快地看到了非常多的树叶。}\n# en: Step out of its way\nopt.up.label [54f1ed] = 闪开它的路\n# en: {~You step sideways into shadow just as it charges. It thunders past into a tree and stays there, thinking about it.|You blink out of its path and it charges straight through where you were. You leave while it's still confused.}\nopt.up.say.1 [32b4e6] = {~就在它冲过来的一刻，你侧身踏进了影子里。它轰隆隆地冲过去撞上一棵树，就那么待在那儿，若有所思。|你一眨眼从它的路线上消失，它径直冲过你刚才站的地方。你趁它还糊涂着离开了。}\n\n@event ww2_fog_wall\n# en: The Fog Wall\ntitle [7e9d08] = 雾墙\n# en: The fog in front of you isn't drifting. It's standing, like a wall, and when you put out a hand it's cold and faintly springy. Behind it the wood goes on. Through it, perhaps, if you can talk it into letting you.\ntext [0dfe25] = 你面前的雾不是在飘。它立着，像一堵墙，你伸出手去，它是冷的，还微微有弹性。墙后面林子还在继续。或许可以穿过去，如果你能说服它让你过的话。\n# en: A solid-looking wall of white fog blocking a forest path, tree branches vanishing into it, a student holding out a hand.\nscene [ee90a6] = 一堵看起来很结实的白雾墙挡住了森林小路，树枝消失在雾里，一个学生伸出了手。\n# en: Turn back\nopt.left.label [2f9f2d] = 往回走\n# en: {~You don't argue with fog. You go home.|You leave the wall to its standing and walk back toward the light.}\nopt.left.say.1 [cf2571] = {~你不跟雾争。你回家了。|你让那堵墙继续立着，朝亮光走了回去。}\n# en: Part it with a spell\nopt.right.label [b0d16a] = 用咒语分开它\n# en: {~You push, and the fog parts like a curtain, sulkily, and lets you through. You'll remember how it gave.|You find the knot at the heart of the fog and pick it. The wall unravels into ordinary mist.}\nopt.right.say.1 [128c2f] = {~你一推，雾就像帘子一样不情不愿地分开，让你过去了。你会记住它是怎么让步的。|你找到了雾中心的那个结，把它挑开了。那堵墙散成了普通的雾气。}\n# en: {~The fog swallows you, turns you round three times and puts you out at the edge of the wood, exactly where you started. It wasn't unkind about it.|You walk into the fog and out of it again, a mile back toward the castle. The wood is done with you for today.}\nopt.right.say.2 [dc32ad] = {~雾吞没了你，把你转了三圈，放到了林子边上，正好是你出发的地方。它倒没什么恶意。|你走进雾里又走出来，已经往城堡方向退回去一英里了。林子今天跟你没话说了。}\n# en: Raise the tine\nopt.up.label [9b742a] = 举起鹿角尖\n# en: {~You hold up the stag's tine and the fog draws back from it as from a lantern.|The fog sees the tine and parts, politely, the way a crowd parts for a duchess.}\nopt.up.say.1 [9b7a8a] = {~你举起影鹿的角尖，雾像躲开一盏灯笼那样退开了。|雾看见了角尖，客客气气地分开，像人群给一位公爵夫人让路。}\n\n@event ww2_waystone\n# en: The Waystone\ntitle [17d710] = 路石\n# en: A standing stone, worn smooth, with offerings heaped at its foot: hazelnuts, a ribbon, a copper crown older than the castle. Foragers leave something here on the way in. Nobody you've asked will say what for.\ntext [78f498] = 一块被磨得光滑的立石，脚下堆着供品：榛子，一根丝带，一枚比城堡还老的铜克朗。采集者们进林子时会在这里留下点东西。你问过的人里，没有一个肯说是为了什么。\n# en: A weathered standing stone in a forest, little offerings of nuts, coins and ribbons piled at its base.\nscene [212383] = 森林里一块饱经风霜的立石，脚下堆着坚果、硬币和丝带之类的小供品。\n# en: Turn back here\nopt.left.label [3fbec1] = 就在这儿回头\n# en: {~You leave the stone and its offerings alone and turn back.|You decide the stone is a sign, and the sign says home.}\nopt.left.say.1 [482d02] = {~你没碰那块石头和它的供品，往回走了。|你认定那块石头是个指示，而指示说的是回家。}\n# en: Leave an offering\nopt.right.label [fa87c3] = 留下一件供品\n# en: {ww_haul > 0: Something from your bag, for a little luck.|Your bag is empty: a coin from your purse, then.}\nopt.right.hint [531c0f] = {ww_haul > 0: 从包里拿一样东西，换一点运气。|包是空的：那就从钱袋里拿一枚硬币吧。}\n# en: {ww_haul > 0: {~You leave the best thing in your bag. Walking on, you feel watched over, which is a nicer feeling than watched.|You set down your offering with the others. A wind goes through the trees, approving.}|{~Your bag is empty, so you leave a crown from your purse. Walking on, you feel watched over, which is a nicer feeling than watched.|You set a coin down with the others, warm from your pocket. A wind goes through the trees, approving.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [8d2d7c] = {ww_haul > 0: {~你留下了包里最好的东西。继续往前走时，你觉得自己被守护着，这比被盯着的感觉好多了。|你把供品和其他的放在一起。一阵风穿过树林，像是在赞许。}|{~包是空的，你便从钱袋里留下一枚克朗。继续往前走时，你觉得自己被守护着，这比被盯着的感觉好多了。|你把一枚还带着口袋余温的硬币和其他供品放在一起。一阵风穿过树林，像是在赞许。}}\n# en: Palm a few offerings\nopt.up.label [393a73] = 顺走几件供品\n# en: {~Nobody's looking. A copper, a ribbon, a handful of nuts slip into your bag. The wood goes very quiet as you walk on.|You palm the old crown. It's cold in your pocket for the rest of the day, and something keeps pace with you, just out of sight.}\nopt.up.say.1 [8ce414] = {~没人在看。一枚铜币，一根丝带，一把坚果，溜进了你的包里。你继续往前走时，林子变得非常安静。|你顺走了那枚老克朗。那天剩下的时间里，它在你口袋里一直是冰的，有什么东西一直跟你并肩走着，就在视线之外。}\n\n@event ww2_lodestone\n# en: The Iron Hollow\ntitle [d6ad0d] = 铁石坑\n# en: In this hollow every fallen leaf lies pointing the same way, and your boots feel heavier. At the bottom, half-buried in roots, is a dark stone the size of an egg, and every leaf points at it.\ntext [a9b404] = 在这个坑里，每一片落叶都朝着同一个方向，你的靴子也觉得沉了。坑底半埋在树根里的，是一块鸡蛋大小的深色石头，每一片叶子都指着它。\n# en: A forest hollow where every fallen leaf points the same direction, a dark stone half-buried at its centre.\nscene [670394] = 森林里的一个坑，每一片落叶都朝着同一个方向，中央半埋着一块深色的石头。\n# en: Leave it buried\nopt.left.label [50a426] = 让它埋着\n# en: {~Some things are buried for a reason. You go home.|You leave the stone to its leaves and turn back.}\nopt.left.say.1 [eec1a3] = {~有些东西埋着是有原因的。你回家了。|你把石头留给它的叶子，往回走了。}\n# en: Dig it out\nopt.right.label [c0d1e0] = 把它挖出来\n# en: {~You dig until your nails are black and work it free. It turns, slowly, in your palm, to face north. A bit of cord and it's a pendant.|It comes up with a sucking sound and a lot of roots. In your hand it turns, gently, like a compass waking.}\nopt.right.say.1 [98b9b2] = {~你挖到指甲缝都黑了，终于把它弄了出来。它在你掌心里慢慢转动，转向北方。加一截绳子，就是一只吊坠。|它被挖出来时发出一声吸吮般的响声，带出一大堆树根。在你手里，它轻轻地转着，像一只苏醒的罗盘。}\n# en: {~The roots won't let go, and neither will you, until the hollow wall comes down on you.|You dig too deep and the hollow fills with black water.}\nopt.right.say.2 [2382ab] = {~树根不肯松手，你也不肯，直到坑壁塌下来压住了你。|你挖得太深，坑里涌满了黑水。}\n\n@event ww2_stone_words\n# en: Words on a Stone\ntitle [9583a1] = 石上的字\n# en: A flat stone, mossed over, with letters carved deep in it. When you scrape the moss away, the letters are a spell: a way to gather light around you, breath by breath, until it stands between you and harm. Whoever left it here wanted it found by someone patient.\ntext [eca37d] = 一块长满苔藓的平石，上面深深地刻着字。你把苔藓刮掉，那些字是一条咒语：一种把光聚集在身边的方法，一口气接一口气，直到它挡在你和伤害之间。把它留在这里的人，希望它被一个有耐心的人发现。\n# en: A moss-covered flat stone in a forest clearing carved with faint glowing letters, a student kneeling to read.\nscene [31c2d6] = 林间空地上，一块长满苔藓的平石上刻着微微发光的字，一个学生跪下来阅读。\n# en: Copy nothing; go home\nopt.left.label [2cf477] = 什么都不抄；回家\n# en: {~You cover the words again with moss and go home. They'll keep.|You leave the stone its secret.}\nopt.left.say.1 [838aa9] = {~你又用苔藓把字盖好，回家了。它们会一直在那儿。|你把石头的秘密留给了它自己。}\n# en: Work out the spell\nopt.right.label [364101] = 弄懂这条咒语\n# en: {~You read it three times and the light begins to gather at your shoulders. Lodestar. It feels like being walked home.|It takes an hour on your knees in the moss. By the end, light gathers when you breathe in, and stays.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0edec0] = {~你读了三遍，光开始在你肩头聚集。引星。感觉就像有人送你回家。|你在苔藓上跪了一个小时。到最后，你一吸气，光就聚过来，而且留下了。}\n# en: {~You get the first line and the second line, and the third line is wrong in a way that knocks you flat.|The light gathers, then goes off like a lamp dropped on stone.}\nopt.right.say.2 [074b65] = {~第一行你懂了，第二行你也懂了，第三行错得把你掀翻在地。|光聚了起来，然后像一盏摔在石头上的灯一样炸开了。}\n\n@event ww2_thornwitch\n# en: The Woman Among the Thorns\ntitle [8e4ed1] = 荆棘中的女人\n# en: A thin woman in a cloak sewn with thorns has her palm on a birch, and the birch is dying: its leaves curl and fall as you watch, and the woman looks younger with every leaf. She sees you. She doesn't stop. \"Siphon,\" she says. \"They'd never teach it to you up there.\"\ntext [73098a] = 一个披着缝满荆棘的斗篷的瘦削女人把手掌按在一棵白桦上，而那棵白桦正在死去：你看着它的叶子卷曲、掉落，那个女人每掉一片叶子就年轻一分。她看见了你。她没有停。“虹吸，”她说。“上面那些人永远不会教你这个。”\n# en: A thin woman in a thorn-trimmed cloak with her hand on a withering birch tree, a sickly green light flowing from the tree into her palm.\nscene [206a87] = 一个斗篷上镶着荆棘的瘦削女人把手按在一棵枯萎的白桦上，一道病恹恹的绿光从树里流进她的掌心。\n# en: Leave her to it\nopt.left.label [d94459] = 由她去吧\n# en: {~You back away and go home, and the dying birch stays in your head all evening.|You leave. She laughs, softly, without turning round.}\nopt.left.say.1 [4e3a77] = {~你退开，回家了，那棵将死的白桦在你脑子里留了一整晚。|你走了。她轻声笑着，没有回头。}\n# en: Talk to her\nopt.right.label [47f973] = 跟她谈谈\n# en: {~You talk. She listens, and stops, and takes her hand off the birch to look at you properly.|She's lonely, it turns out, and proud, and she likes being argued with. The birch gets a reprieve.}\nopt.right.say.1 [807be8] = {~你开口说话。她听着，停了下来，把手从白桦上拿开，好好地打量你。|原来她很孤独，也很骄傲，而且喜欢有人跟她争论。白桦得到了缓刑。}\n# en: {~She tires of you halfway through a sentence and turns her hand toward you instead.|\"Enough,\" she says, and the thorns on her cloak lift like hackles.}\nopt.right.say.2 [55086d] = {~你一句话说到一半，她就不耐烦了，把手转向了你。|“够了，”她说，斗篷上的荆棘像颈毛一样竖了起来。}\n# en: Stop her\nopt.up.label [cba466] = 阻止她\n# en: {~You drive her off the birch and into the thorns. She goes, cursing, and the birch puts out one green leaf. You'll tell Brask.|She runs. The birch is too far gone, but the next one won't be. Brask will want to know.}\nopt.up.say.1 [b98d64] = {~你把她从白桦边赶进了荆棘丛。她骂骂咧咧地走了，白桦长出了一片绿叶。你会告诉布拉斯克的。|她跑了。这棵白桦已经救不回来了，但下一棵能。布拉斯克会想知道的。}\n# en: {~She turns your fire on you, and then turns to you, and then you don't remember.|She takes from you what she was taking from the birch. You wake much later, very cold.}\nopt.up.say.2 [f927c7] = {~她把你的火转向了你，然后转向了你本人，然后你就不记得了。|她从你身上拿走了她从白桦身上拿走的东西。你很久以后才醒，冷得要命。}\n\n@event ww2_thornwitch_fight\n# en: Thorns\ntitle [468523] = 荆棘\n# en: Brambles come up out of the ground at her gesture. She's going to try Siphon on you next, and you have seen what it does to a birch. ⚠ Down here, nobody comes to catch you.\ntext [19f832] = 她一挥手，荆棘就从地里钻了出来。她接下来要对你用虹吸，而你见过它对一棵白桦做了什么。⚠ 在这底下，没人会来接住你。\n# en: A woman in a thorn cloak raising both hands as brambles burst from the forest floor toward a student.\nscene [d20b6e] = 一个披着荆棘斗篷的女人举起双手，荆棘从林地里迸出，朝一个学生扑去。\n# en: Run for it\nopt.left.label [38d292] = 快跑\n# en: {~You run, and the brambles tear at you all the way to the path. She doesn't follow. She doesn't need to.|You run. Her laugh follows you further than she does.}\nopt.left.say.1 [51e396] = {~你跑了，荆棘一路撕扯着你直到小路。她没追。她用不着追。|你跑了。她的笑声比她本人追得更远。}\n# en: Fight her\nopt.right.label [d6ea52] = 跟她打\n# en: She heals by holding Siphon: break the hold.\nopt.right.hint [d943c8] = 她靠维持虹吸来疗伤：打断它。\n# en: {~You break her hold and she breaks, fleeing into the thorns. The birch behind her is grey, but you'll tell Brask, and it won't happen here again.|She goes down into the brambles and doesn't come up. When you look, she's gone, and so are the thorns.}\nopt.right.say.1 [0cdec4] = {~你打断了她的维持，她也垮了，逃进了荆棘丛。她身后的白桦已经灰了，但你会告诉布拉斯克，这里不会再发生这种事。|她倒进荆棘丛里，再没起来。你再看时，她不见了，荆棘也不见了。}\n# en: {~Her hand finds your arm, and the cold comes in, and you don't remember the rest.|She drinks from you until you can't stand. Then she leaves you, generously, alive.}\nopt.right.say.2 [ecb41c] = {~她的手抓住了你的胳膊，寒意涌了进来，剩下的你都不记得了。|她吸你吸到你站不住。然后她慷慨地留了你一命。}\n\n@event ww2_thornwitch_offer\n# en: Her Lesson\ntitle [150c88] = 她的一课\n# en: \"You have nerve,\" the thornwitch says. \"I could use an apprentice with nerve.\" She holds out a wand of blackthorn, still bristling. \"I'll teach you Siphon. It's only life. There's so much of it about.\" Behind her the birch is grey to the root.\ntext [b28689] = “你有胆量，”荆棘女巫说。“我正需要一个有胆量的学徒。”她递出一根黑刺李魔杖，上面的刺还竖着。“我教你虹吸。不过是生命而已。到处都是。”在她身后，那棵白桦已经灰到了根。\n# en: A thin woman in a thorn cloak holding out a blackthorn wand to a student, a grey birch behind her.\nscene [f5f7da] = 一个披着荆棘斗篷的瘦削女人向一个学生递出一根黑刺李魔杖，她身后是一棵灰白的白桦。\n# en: Tell her to go\nopt.left.label [c53d24] = 叫她走\n# en: {~\"Go,\" you say, \"and don't come back to this wood.\" She shrugs, as if you'd refused a biscuit, and walks off into the thorns. You'll tell Brask.|She looks at you for a long time. Then she goes, and the thorns go with her.}\nopt.left.say.1 [843cbc] = {~“走，”你说，“别再回这片林子。”她耸耸肩，好像你只是拒绝了一块饼干，走进了荆棘丛。你会告诉布拉斯克的。|她看了你很久。然后她走了，荆棘也跟着她走了。}\n# en: Take the lesson\nopt.right.label [187b3f] = 接受这一课\n# en: A forbidden spell, and a wand that wants using.\nopt.right.hint [3f2473] = 一条禁咒，和一根想被使用的魔杖。\n# en: {~She shows you. It's easy, which is the worst part. You walk on with the thorn wand in your belt and a dead birch behind you.|You learn it on a fern, which curls and greys. \"There,\" she says. \"Now you know something they don't.\" The thorn wand pricks your palm.}\nopt.right.say.1 [67f5a9] = {~她教了你。很容易，这才是最糟的。你腰里插着荆棘魔杖继续往前走，身后是一棵死去的白桦。|你在一株蕨上学会了，它卷曲、变灰。“好了，”她说。“现在你知道一些他们不知道的事了。”荆棘魔杖刺了一下你的掌心。}\n\n@event ww2_embers_kin\n# en: Ember's Kin\ntitle [b66af0] = 余烬的族人\n# en: A ring of small foxes is sitting in the dark, tails glowing like banked coals. {has(familiar_fox): Your own fox goes forward and touches noses with the eldest.|The eldest looks at you as if it has heard about you.} Foxes like these, Maud says, will walk anyone through the wood who can hold a conversation.\ntext [6812d3] = 一圈小狐狸坐在黑暗里，尾巴像封着的炭火一样发着光。{has(familiar_fox): 你自己的狐狸走上前，跟最年长的那只碰了碰鼻子。|最年长的那只看着你，好像听说过你。} 莫德说，这样的狐狸会领任何能好好聊天的人穿过林子。\n# en: Several small foxes with glowing ember-like tails sitting in a ring in a dark forest, watching a student.\nscene [002132] = 漆黑的森林里，几只尾巴像余烬一样发光的小狐狸围坐成一圈，看着一个学生。\n# en: Bow and turn back\nopt.left.label [474bd8] = 鞠个躬，往回走\n# en: {~You bow to the foxes and go home. Their tails glow in the dark behind you all the way.|You leave them their ring. One follows you as far as the path, then vanishes.}\nopt.left.say.1 [1e8d33] = {~你向狐狸们鞠了一躬，回家了。一路上它们的尾巴都在你身后的黑暗里发着光。|你把那个圈留给了它们。有一只一直跟你到小路上，然后消失了。}\n# en: Hold a conversation\nopt.right.label [2c5dda] = 跟它们聊聊\n# en: {~You talk, they yip, and the eldest decides you'll do. From now on, a small smouldering tail will turn up whenever you step off the path, and steer you out of the worst of it.|You trade news for news. The foxes are terrible gossips. One of them will walk with you from now on.}\nopt.right.say.1 [18be86] = {~你说话，它们尖声叫，最年长的那只认定你还行。从今往后，只要你离开小路，就会有一条冒着烟的小尾巴出现，把你从最糟的地方引开。|你们用消息换消息。狐狸们是可怕的八卦精。从今往后，会有一只跟你一起走。}\n# en: {~You say the wrong thing, whatever it was. The foxes vanish, and so does the path.|The eldest yawns at you. A tail flicks, and you're lost.}\nopt.right.say.2 [1a6e11] = {~你说错了话，不管是哪句。狐狸们消失了，小路也消失了。|最年长的那只冲你打了个哈欠。一条尾巴一甩，你就迷路了。}\n# en: Meet them as equals\nopt.up.label [5c41fd] = 平起平坐地相待\n# en: {~You sit down in their ring as if you'd been invited, and talk to them like old friends. They're delighted. From now on, one of them walks with you whenever you step off the path.|The eldest laughs, a fox's laugh. \"Manners,\" it says. \"At last.\" One of them will walk with you now.}\nopt.up.say.1 [5a70c9] = {~你在它们的圈里坐下，好像本来就受了邀请，像老朋友一样跟它们说话。它们高兴极了。从今往后，只要你离开小路，就会有一只陪你走。|最年长的那只笑了，狐狸的那种笑。“礼貌，”它说。“终于来了。”从现在起，会有一只跟你一起走。}\n\n@event ww2_stag_tine\n# en: The Shed Tine\ntitle [36ab58] = 脱落的角尖\n# en: In a ring of stones lies a single tine from the shadow stag's antlers, shed and left as if for someone. It's heavy, and darker than its shadow. You bowed once, in the bracken. Perhaps that was noticed.\ntext [c32cee] = 一圈石头里躺着一截影鹿的角尖，像是脱落后特意留给某个人的。它很沉，比它的影子还黑。你曾在蕨丛里鞠过一次躬。也许那被注意到了。\n# en: A single dark antler tine lying on moss inside a ring of stones, faint shadow curling off it like smoke.\nscene [22aa9e] = 一圈石头里，苔藓上躺着一截深色的鹿角尖，淡淡的影子像烟一样从它上面升起。\n# en: Leave it be\nopt.left.label [1c761e] = 别动它\n# en: {~You bow to the tine, and leave it, and go home. That feels right too.|You don't touch it. The ring of stones seems to approve.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6eb519] = {~你向角尖鞠了一躬，没动它，回家了。这样也感觉对。|你没碰它。那圈石头似乎表示赞许。}\n# en: Lift it\nopt.right.label [4709a9] = 把它举起来\n# en: {~It weighs as much as a sack of flour and as little as a feather, depending on how you hold it. You find the right way. Wild things will look at it before they look at you.|You lift it. Somewhere in the wood, something very large breathes out, satisfied.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d9f4a9] = {~它有时重得像一袋面粉，有时轻得像一根羽毛，取决于你怎么拿。你找到了正确的拿法。野生的东西会先看它，再看你。|你把它举了起来。林子某处，一个非常大的东西满足地呼出一口气。}\n# en: {~It's too heavy, and then suddenly not, and you fall backwards over the ring of stones.|You lift it and it lifts you, and sets you down a long way off, bruised.}\nopt.right.say.2 [3d4a83] = {~它太重了，然后忽然又不重了，你向后摔倒在那圈石头上。|你举起了它，它也举起了你，把你放到了很远的地方，摔得青一块紫一块。}\n\n@event ww2_deer_path\n# en: Deeper\ntitle [70f044] = 更深处\n# en: {~The thicket closes overhead and the daylight goes green, then grey.|Pale fungus glows on the trunks in rings, like lamps somebody keeps lit.|A path of old flagstones, sunk in moss, going down.} The wood is quieter here, and it's listening.\ntext [a25604] = {~灌木在头顶合拢，日光变绿，又变灰。|树干上一圈圈苍白的菌子在发光，像有人一直点着的灯。|一条陷在苔藓里的老石板路，一路向下。} 这里的林子更安静了，它在听。\n# en: A dim forest thicket with dense old trees, a narrow path between them, pale fungus glowing faintly on the trunks.\nscene [bbcca6] = 昏暗的森林灌木林里，古树密布，中间一条窄路，树干上的苍白菌子发着微光。\n# en: Turn back\nopt.left.label [2f9f2d] = 往回走\n# en: {~You turn round. The light comes back a shade at a time.|You go home while you still remember the way.|You climb back up the flagstones toward the day.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f2b60d] = {~你转身往回走。光一点一点地回来了。|你趁还记得路回家了。|你沿着石板路往上爬，回到白天里去。}\n# en: Go deeper\nopt.right.label [a3b500] = 往深处走\n# en: {~You pick glowing caps from the trunks as you go, and your fingers glow for an hour.|You gather pale fungus from the lamp-rings, and the trees let you.|Down the flagstones, gathering what grows between them.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3ab371] = {~你一边走一边从树干上摘发光的菌盖，手指亮了一个小时。|你从那些灯环上采下苍白的菌子，树们由着你。|顺着石板路往下，采着石缝里长的东西。}\n\n@event ww3_shadow_stag\n# en: The Shadow Stag\ntitle [74389f] = 影鹿\n# en: The shadow stag stands across the way, a stag-shaped hole in the light with antlers that go up into the canopy and don't stop. It doesn't move. It's waiting to see what you are.\ntext [17ce0b] = 影鹿横在路上，像光里一个鹿形的洞，鹿角一直伸进树冠里，没有尽头。它一动不动。它在等着看你是什么。\n# en: A huge stag made of living shadow standing between ancient trees, its antlers lost in the dark canopy.\nscene [d28005] = 古树之间站着一头由活生生的影子构成的巨鹿，它的角隐没在漆黑的树冠里。\n# en: Back away\nopt.left.label [be6d04] = 退开\n# en: {~You back away, step by step. It watches until you're out of sight, and then a while longer.|You leave the heartwood to the stag. It seems to think that's correct.}\nopt.left.say.1 [6d9d03] = {~你一步一步往后退。它一直看着你，直到你走出视线，又多看了一会儿。|你把心木林让给了影鹿。它似乎觉得这样才对。}\n# en: Bow, and hold your nerve\nopt.right.label [fa47e8] = 鞠躬，稳住胆量\n# en: {flag(ww_bowed): You learned to bow from Brask.|A test of nerve.}\nopt.right.hint [8476f6] = {flag(ww_bowed): 你跟布拉斯克学过鞠躬。|一场胆量的考验。}\n# en: {flag(ww_stag_friend): You bow and don't look away, and the stag lowers its great head. From now on it'll meet you at the edge and walk you down past the easy places.|You bow and hold its gaze too long. The stag lowers its antlers, not in greeting.}\nopt.right.say.1 [d36e7a] = {flag(ww_stag_friend): 你鞠躬，目光没有移开，影鹿低下了它巨大的头。从今往后，它会在林子边接你，领你越过那些容易的地方往下走。|你鞠了躬，却盯着它的眼睛看得太久了。影鹿压低了鹿角，不是在打招呼。}\n\n@event ww3_shadow_stag_fight\n# en: Antlers\ntitle [65f85e] = 鹿角\n# en: The stag lowers its antlers. Brask would tell you to run. Brask would also tell you, afterwards, that running from a shadow stag is how you teach it to chase.\ntext [0caac8] = 影鹿压低了鹿角。布拉斯克会叫你快跑。布拉斯克事后也会告诉你，从影鹿面前逃跑，就是在教它追。\n# en: A shadow stag charging between dark trees, antlers lowered, a student raising a wand.\nscene [ab4d78] = 一头影鹿在黑暗的树木间低角冲锋，一个学生举起了魔杖。\n# en: Run for the path\nopt.left.label [48bc35] = 朝小路跑\n# en: {~You run. It doesn't chase. Somehow that's worse.|You run until the trees thin. When you look back, there's nothing but shadow.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e14f77] = {~你跑了。它没追。不知怎么的，这更糟。|你一直跑到树木稀疏的地方。你回头看时，只剩下影子。}\n# en: Stand and fight\nopt.right.label [300910] = 站住，迎战\n# en: {~It turns away at last, wounded more in its pride than its shadow. You go on, but the wood feels colder, as if you'd broken something it liked.|You drive it off. Nothing about that feels like winning.}\nopt.right.say.1 [c02941] = {~它终于转身离开了，伤到的更多是骄傲而不是影子。你继续往前走，可林子感觉更冷了，好像你打碎了它喜欢的什么东西。|你把它赶走了。这件事里没有一点赢了的感觉。}\n# en: {~The antlers lift you and set you down a long way off, almost gently.|It charges through you like a cold wind. You lie in the leaves for a long time.}\nopt.right.say.2 [c08277] = {~鹿角把你挑起来，放到很远的地方，几乎是轻轻地。|它像一阵冷风一样冲过你的身体。你在落叶里躺了很久。}\n\n@event ww3_elder_oak\n# en: The Elder Oak\ntitle [2df545] = 古橡\n# en: The elder oak is so old the other trees grow round it like courtiers. Its bark is a face with its eyes shut. You ask, politely, how old it is, and it opens a split in its trunk to show you its rings. Sixty rings in from the bark, one ring is pale as frost. \"The cold spring,\" it says, and nothing more.\ntext [8a9d76] = 古橡太老了，其他的树像朝臣一样围着它生长。它的树皮是一张闭着眼睛的脸。你礼貌地问它多大年纪，它在树干上裂开一道缝，给你看它的年轮。从树皮往里数六十圈，有一圈年轮白得像霜。“寒冷的春天，”它说，再没别的了。\n# en: An immense ancient oak in a forest clearing, its bark like a face with closed eyes, a student standing small before it.\nscene [e7864b] = 林间空地上一棵巨大的古橡，树皮像一张闭着眼睛的脸，一个学生小小地站在它面前。\n# en: Bow out and go home\nopt.left.label [ca5623] = 鞠躬告退，回家\n# en: {~You bow, and leave the old thing to its long thoughts.|You go home thinking about one pale ring among a thousand brown ones.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8a652a] = {~你鞠了一躬，让这位老者继续它漫长的思考。|你回家的路上一直想着上千圈褐色年轮里的那一圈苍白。}\n# en: Ask it for heartwood\nopt.right.label [c208e5] = 向它求一片心木\n# en: A splinter of it would make a wand.\nopt.right.hint [4fad57] = 它的一片碎木能做成一根魔杖。\n# en: {~It considers you for so long you think it's fallen asleep. Then a splinter of heartwood, red-gold and warm, drops into your hand. Mir could carve this.|\"Use it well,\" it creaks, \"or not at all.\" The splinter in your palm is warm as a living thing.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a830d8] = {~它端详你的时间长得你以为它睡着了。然后一片心木，红金色的，温热的，落进了你手里。米尔能雕这个。|“好好用它，”它嘎吱着说，“或者干脆别用。”你掌心里的碎木温热得像一个活物。}\n# en: {~It closes its split, and its eyes open instead.|\"Take,\" it says, \"is not a word for trees.\" Its roots begin to move.}\nopt.right.say.2 [dd5e9c] = {~它合上了那道缝，睁开的是它的眼睛。|“拿，”它说，“不是树的词。”它的根开始动了。}\n\n@event ww3_elder_oak_fight\n# en: The Slow Fall\ntitle [ec7d5a] = 缓坠\n# en: The elder oak doesn't hurry. It lifts one great bough, slowly, and you have all the time in the world to watch it come down. Its roots are moving too. The bough will take three breaths to land.\ntext [bcd7cb] = 古橡不着急。它慢慢举起一根巨大的树枝，你有世上所有的时间看着它落下来。它的根也在动。那根树枝要三次呼吸才会落地。\n# en: A colossal oak bough slowly descending toward a student in a forest clearing, roots coiling across the ground.\nscene [1e0c64] = 林间空地上，一根巨大的橡树枝正缓缓压向一个学生，树根在地面上盘绕。\n# en: Get out from under it\nopt.left.label [b8852c] = 从底下逃出来\n# en: {~You run out from under the falling bough and keep running. The ground shakes behind you like a door slammed in a cellar.|You leave. The oak goes back to sleep before the bough has finished falling.}\nopt.left.say.1 [631a02] = {~你从落下的树枝底下跑出来，一直跑。身后的地面在震，像地窖里一扇被摔上的门。|你走了。树枝还没落完，古橡就又睡着了。}\n# en: Hold your ground\nopt.right.label [52ce0a] = 寸步不让\n# en: Answer each slow fall; its roots chip at you all along.\nopt.right.hint [7b5f74] = 应对每一次缓坠；它的根会一直啃着你。\n# en: {~You weather every fall, and the oak stops, surprised. It doesn't give you any heartwood. It doesn't stop you going on, either.|The oak lets the last bough rest. You've won nothing but the right to pass.}\nopt.right.say.1 [44bf55] = {~你扛过了每一次坠落，古橡停了下来，很惊讶。它没给你心木。它也没拦着你继续往前走。|古橡让最后一根树枝停了下来。你赢得的只有通行的权利。}\n# en: {~The third fall finds you. The roots carry you, gently, to the edge of the clearing and set you down.|You misjudge the third breath.}\nopt.right.say.2 [7b9721] = {~第三次坠落找到了你。树根轻轻地把你抬到空地边上，放下。|你算错了第三次呼吸。}\n\n@event ww3_masked_circle\n# en: The Masked Circle\ntitle [65f497] = 假面法阵会\n# en: Three figures in plain white masks stand in a chalk circle, practising on a hare. One says \"again,\" and the hare does what it just did, again, against its will. The chalk marks round the circle are copied from somewhere, badly. They haven't seen you yet.\ntext [1430ff] = 三个戴着素白面具的人站在一个粉笔圈里，在一只野兔身上练习。其中一个说“再来”，野兔就违背自己的意愿，把刚才做过的事又做了一遍。圈周围的粉笔符号是从哪儿抄来的，抄得很糟。他们还没看见你。\n# en: Three figures in plain white masks standing in a chalk circle in a dark forest clearing, one pointing at a cringing hare.\nscene [f48c6f] = 漆黑的林间空地上，三个戴着素白面具的人站在粉笔圈里，其中一个指着一只瑟缩的野兔。\n# en: Slip away\nopt.left.label [b06a4f] = 悄悄溜走\n# en: {~You slip back into the dark before they turn. The hare's eyes stay with you.|You leave. Behind you, someone says \"again\", and you walk faster.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8de5e0] = {~你在他们转身前退回了黑暗里。野兔的眼睛一直留在你心里。|你走了。身后有人说“再来”，你走得更快了。}\n# en: Walk in as one of them\nopt.right.label [7b8c58] = 装作他们的人走进去\n# en: {~You stride in as if you were late. They're amateurs: they make room for you at once.|\"You're late,\" says one. \"Sorry,\" you say, and they believe you.}\nopt.right.say.1 [9070a4] = {~你大步走进去，好像只是迟到了。他们是外行：立刻给你让了位置。|“你迟到了，”其中一个说。“抱歉，”你说，他们信了。}\n# en: {~\"That's not one of us,\" says the tallest, and says the word at you instead.|They know their own. Three wands turn your way, and a voice says \"again\", and again.}\nopt.right.say.2 [c521ec] = {~“那不是我们的人，”最高的那个说，然后把那个词冲你说了出来。|他们认得自己人。三根魔杖转向你，一个声音说“再来”，一遍又一遍。}\n# en: Break up the circle\nopt.up.label [5f01a6] = 冲散这个圈子\n# en: {~You scatter them. They run, masks askew, into the trees. The hare is gone before you can look. You'll tell the headmistress.|One loses a mask as they run. You don't see the face. You do see the chalk, and scuff it out.}\nopt.up.say.1 [6b1e5a] = {~你把他们冲散了。他们面具歪斜地逃进了树林。你还没来得及看，野兔就不见了。你会告诉院长的。|其中一个逃跑时掉了面具。你没看到那张脸。你看到了粉笔圈，把它踢花了。}\n# en: {~They make you do it again: whatever you did last, until you can't stand. Then they leave you.|Three against one, and they've practised this. You don't remember the end.}\nopt.up.say.2 [34dfda] = {~他们让你再来一遍：不管你上一步做了什么，一直做到你站不住。然后他们丢下了你。|三对一，而且他们练过。你不记得结局了。}\n\n@event ww3_masked_offer\n# en: \"Again\"\ntitle [357cd7] = “再来”\n# en: Inside the circle, the masked three are friendly, which is worse. \"It's called Compel,\" the tallest says. \"Say the word and they do it again. Anyone. Anything.\" They hold out a spare mask. The hare in the middle is shaking.\ntext [05f223] = 在圈里，三个戴面具的人很友好，这更糟。“这叫强令，”最高的那个说。“说出那个词，他们就会再做一遍。任何人。任何事。”他们递来一张备用的面具。中间的野兔在发抖。\n# en: Three masked figures in a chalk circle offering a white mask to a student, a hare frozen in the middle.\nscene [c73711] = 粉笔圈里，三个戴面具的人向一个学生递出一张白面具，一只野兔僵在中间。\n# en: Let the hare go and leave\nopt.left.label [d80fff] = 放走野兔，离开\n# en: {~You scoop up the hare on your way out of the circle, and nobody stops you, because you walk like you're allowed. Behind you they argue about who you were.|You let the hare go and walk on. They don't follow. They don't know who you are, which is the only thing they've got right.}\nopt.left.say.1 [0a88a4] = {~你走出圈子时顺手抱起了野兔，没人拦你，因为你走路的样子像是本来就被允许的。身后他们在争论你是谁。|你放走了野兔，继续往前。他们没跟上来。他们不知道你是谁，这是他们唯一做对的事。}\n# en: Learn the word\nopt.right.label [c592ba] = 学会那个词\n# en: A forbidden spell.\nopt.right.hint [1ffa0f] = 一条禁咒。\n# en: {~You learn it on the hare. It works the first time. You walk on with the word in your mouth like a stone.|\"Again,\" you say, and the hare does. The masks nod. You leave before they can see your face, or you theirs.}\nopt.right.say.1 [a10a69] = {~你在野兔身上学会了它。第一次就成了。你继续往前走，那个词含在嘴里，像一块石头。|“再来，”你说，野兔照做了。面具们点了点头。在他们看见你的脸、或你看见他们的脸之前，你离开了。}\n\n@event ww3_always_midnight\n# en: Where It's Always Midnight\ntitle [2017b8] = 永远是午夜的地方\n# en: You step into a clearing and it's midnight: stars overhead, though it's day behind you, and moonlight pooling in a stone basin in the middle. The moonpetal round the edge is closed. It's waiting, you'd swear, for a real midnight.\ntext [0e2607] = 你踏进一片空地，这里是午夜：头顶有星星，尽管你身后还是白天，月光积在中央一个石盆里。边上的月瓣花都合着。你敢发誓，它们在等一个真正的午夜。\n# en: A forest clearing under a night sky in broad daytime, moonlight pooling in a stone basin full of still water.\nscene [218eac] = 大白天里，一片林间空地却笼罩在夜空之下，月光积在一个盛满静水的石盆里。\n# en: Step back into the day\nopt.left.label [3602d9] = 退回白天里\n# en: {~You step back and it's afternoon again. You note the place, and the closed flowers, and the hour they're waiting for.|You leave the clearing to its private night, and remember the moonpetal waiting.}\nopt.left.say.1 [32ed0b] = {~你退后一步，又是下午了。你记下了这个地方，那些合着的花，还有它们在等的那个时辰。|你把这片空地留给它私有的夜晚，记住了那些等待着的月瓣花。}\n# en: Drink from the basin\nopt.right.label [b9d769] = 从石盆里喝\n# en: It wants a careful hand; a brewer's.\nopt.right.hint [666c88] = 它要一双小心的手；一双熬药人的手。\n# en: {~You skim the moonlight off the water like cream, the way you'd skim a potion. It settles in you, slow and free. Moonwell. The flowers stay shut: not their hour. Midnight, they seem to say. Come back at midnight.|You drink the light, not the water. It fills you slowly, like a basin. Around you the moonpetal waits for midnight.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2c8888] = {~你像撇药汤一样，把月光从水面上撇了下来，像撇奶油。它在你体内沉下来，缓慢，而且不要代价。月泉。花还是合着：不是它们的时辰。午夜，它们像在说。午夜再来。|你喝的是光，不是水。它慢慢地把你注满，像注满一只石盆。你周围的月瓣花在等午夜。}\n# en: {~You drink the water, not the light. It's very cold, and so are you, for the rest of the day.|The basin tips. Moonlight goes everywhere, and you go down in it.}\nopt.right.say.2 [5c9d30] = {~你喝的是水，不是光。水很冷，那天剩下的时间你也很冷。|石盆翻了。月光洒得到处都是，你也跟着倒在了里面。}\n\n@event ww3_other_sky\n# en: The Other Sky\ntitle [cc2dd3] = 另一片天空\n# en: A pool so still it's a mirror, except that it reflects the wrong sky: another season's stars at noon, as if the pool is looking somewhere else. When you lean over, your reflection is a moment late. Then it's a moment early.\ntext [8c7856] = 一潭静得像镜子的水，只是它映出的天空不对：正午时分映着另一个季节的星星，好像这潭水在看着别处。你俯身去看，你的倒影慢了一拍。然后又快了一拍。\n# en: A still forest pool reflecting a completely different sky from the one above, autumn constellations in a summer noon.\nscene [bf20a6] = 一潭静止的林中池水映出与头顶完全不同的天空，夏日正午里映着秋天的星座。\n# en: Look away and go home\nopt.left.label [29a548] = 移开目光，回家\n# en: {~You look away first. All the way home your reflection in puddles is very slightly late.|You leave the pool to its other sky.}\nopt.left.say.1 [db183a] = {~你先移开了目光。回家的一路上，你在水洼里的倒影都慢了那么一点点。|你把这潭水留给它的另一片天空。}\n# en: Work out how it does that\nopt.right.label [e9286d] = 弄清楚它是怎么做到的\n# en: {~It's throwing back what's cast at it, all of it, from wherever it comes. You can't do it yourself, but you understand it, and you fill a jar with the water for Pell.|You watch until your reflection catches up. Then you understand: it gives everything back. You take a jar of the pool with you, carefully.}\nopt.right.say.1 [2dd1f3] = {~它把投向它的东西全部弹回去，不管是从哪儿来的。你自己做不到，但你明白了其中的道理，还给佩尔装了一罐水。|你一直看到倒影追上你。然后你明白了：它把一切都还回去。你小心翼翼地带走了一罐潭水。}\n# en: {~You lean too far, and your reflection leans further, and you're in the water and very cold.|The pool throws your own curiosity back at you, hard.}\nopt.right.say.2 [0e048d] = {~你俯得太低，你的倒影俯得更低，然后你就掉进了水里，冷得要命。|那潭水把你的好奇心狠狠地砸了回来。}\n# en: Answer it with starlight\nopt.up.label [072204] = 用星光回应它\n# en: {~You cast starlight at the pool and it throws it back, all of it, and you catch it, and a little of the other sky with it, in a jar.|The pool returns your starlight with interest. You learn something about your own light by being on the wrong end of it, and bottle a little of the pool.}\nopt.up.say.1 [67804a] = {~你朝水潭施放星光，它把星光全部弹了回来，你接住了，连同一点别处的天空，装进了罐子。|水潭连本带利地还回了你的星光。你是在挨打的那一头，才对自己的光多懂了一点，还装走了一点潭水。}\n# en: {~The pool returns your starlight, all of it, and you weren't ready.|It throws the light back. You see stars for some time.}\nopt.up.say.2 [b4bc93] = {~水潭把你的星光全部还了回来，而你没准备好。|它把光弹了回来。你眼冒金星了好一阵。}\n\n@event ww3_spiderlings\n# en: Spiderlings\ntitle [b9ae0d] = 小蜘蛛\n# en: The branches overhead are thick with grey silk, and the silk is moving. Spiderlings, hundreds, pouring down their threads toward you, each the size of a coin. Somewhere deeper, something laid all these. You'd rather not meet it today.\ntext [45d0e0] = 头顶的树枝上结满了灰色的蛛丝，而蛛丝在动。小蜘蛛，成百上千，顺着丝线朝你倾泻下来，每只都有硬币那么大。更深的地方，有什么东西产下了这一切。今天你可不想遇见它。\n# en: Hundreds of small grey spiders pouring down silk threads from dark branches toward a startled student.\nscene [680b51] = 成百上千只灰色小蜘蛛顺着蛛丝从漆黑的树枝上倾泻而下，扑向一个受惊的学生。\n# en: Run back the way you came\nopt.left.label [68dc74] = 原路跑回去\n# en: {~You run, brushing at your hair for the next three days.|You go home and shake out every piece of clothing you own.}\nopt.left.say.1 [162e85] = {~你跑了，之后三天一直在拍头发。|你回了家，把每一件衣服都抖了一遍。}\n# en: Fight through them\nopt.right.label [c67eea] = 杀出一条路\n# en: Many small bites: a single brace won't stop them.\nopt.right.hint [d2bfbf] = 许多小口的咬：一次坚守挡不住。\n# en: {~You fight through, wand flailing, until the silk thins and the bites stop. You come out with a bag full of silk-wrapped seed-cases Pell will pay for.|They scatter. You gather a handful of their silk, which is stronger than rope and lighter than breath.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6a14dc] = {~你挥舞着魔杖杀了过去，直到蛛丝变稀、咬啮停止。你出来时带了一袋蛛丝裹着的种荚，佩尔会买的。|它们四散了。你收集了一把它们的丝，比绳子还结实，比呼吸还轻。}\n# en: {~They're everywhere at once. You go down in a grey storm of tiny bites.|You stop a dozen and a hundred get through.}\nopt.right.say.2 [e01719] = {~它们无处不在。你倒在一场灰色的小咬风暴里。|你挡住了十几只，却有上百只冲了过来。}\n# en: Burn the webs\nopt.up.label [1ca3da] = 烧掉蛛网\n# en: {~The silk goes up like paper and the spiderlings flee in every direction. The path ahead is clear, and smells of burnt sugar.|You burn a road through the silk. Something deeper in the wood notices the smoke.}\nopt.up.say.1 [01e9b2] = {~蛛丝像纸一样烧了起来，小蜘蛛们四散奔逃。前方的路清出来了，闻起来像焦糖。|你在蛛丝里烧出一条路。林子更深处，有什么注意到了烟。}\n\n@event ww3_wisps\n# en: Wisps\ntitle [d6b4b7] = 鬼火\n# en: Wisps: little blue lights that bob between the trees and ask riddles, and lead you into bogs if you answer wrong. They know where the best things grow. When something startles them, they scatter every way but one: toward the lake.\ntext [6a475c] = 鬼火：在树间上下浮动的蓝色小光点，会出谜语，要是你答错了，就把你引进沼泽。它们知道最好的东西长在哪儿。受惊的时候，它们会朝每一个方向四散，只有一个方向除外：湖那边。\n# en: Several small floating blue lights drifting between dark trees, one hovering close to a student's face.\nscene [af1e7b] = 几点漂浮的蓝色小光在漆黑的树木间飘荡，其中一点悬停在一个学生的脸旁。\n# en: Ignore them; turn back\nopt.left.label [b01f9e] = 别理它们；往回走\n# en: {~You ignore them, which wisps find very rude. They follow you to the edge, sulking.|You turn back. The wisps scatter, and you notice, again, the direction they don't go.}\nopt.left.say.1 [2d82a4] = {~你没理它们，鬼火觉得这非常失礼。它们赌着气跟你到了林子边上。|你往回走。鬼火四散了，你又一次注意到了它们不去的那个方向。}\n# en: Play their game\nopt.right.label [914853] = 陪它们玩\n# en: {~You out-riddle the riddlers. Furious and delighted, they lead you to a hollow full of things Pell has only read about.|You argue every answer and they love it. They lead you somewhere good, and then somewhere better.}\nopt.right.say.1 [63bf30] = {~你比出谜的更会出谜。它们又气又乐，把你领到一个坑里，里面的东西佩尔只在书上读到过。|你每个答案都跟它们争，它们爱死了。它们把你领到一个好地方，然后是一个更好的地方。}\n# en: {~They lead you, laughing, into a bog. A deep one.|You answer wrong. They go out like candles, and you're alone in the dark.}\nopt.right.say.2 [59a14f] = {~它们笑着把你领进了沼泽。很深的那种。|你答错了。它们像蜡烛一样熄灭了，你独自待在黑暗里。}\n\n@event ww3_sinkhole\n# en: The Sinkhole\ntitle [648169] = 天坑\n# en: The ground opens. A sinkhole the size of a cottage, roots hanging down into a dark that doesn't end, and the edge crumbling under your boots. ⚠ Nobody comes to catch you down here.\ntext [df721a] = 地面裂开了。一个小屋那么大的天坑，树根垂进没有尽头的黑暗里，坑边在你靴子底下崩塌。⚠ 在这底下，没人会来接住你。\n# en: The edge of a collapsing sinkhole in a dark forest floor, roots dangling into darkness, a student leaping.\nscene [1924d1] = 漆黑的林地上一个正在塌陷的天坑边缘，树根垂进黑暗里，一个学生纵身跃起。\n# en: Scramble back\nopt.left.label [990249] = 爬回去\n# en: {~You throw yourself backwards and the edge goes without you. You go home very carefully.|You scramble back from the edge and decide you've seen enough of the wood today.}\nopt.left.say.1 [b38c0e] = {~你往后一扑，坑边没带上你就塌了。你非常小心地回了家。|你从坑边爬了回来，认定今天的林子已经看够了。}\n# en: Jump for the far side\nopt.right.label [740d7f] = 跳到对面去\n# en: {~You jump. For a moment there's nothing under you at all. Then there's moss, and you're through.|You land on the far edge as it crumbles, and run. Behind you the hole sighs.}\nopt.right.say.1 [838b94] = {~你跳了。有那么一刻，你脚下什么都没有。然后是苔藓，你过来了。|你落在对面正在崩塌的边上，拔腿就跑。身后，那个坑叹了口气。}\n# en: {~You don't make it. Roots catch you, and hold you, and you hang there for a long time.|The far edge gives under you.}\nopt.right.say.2 [96a3b8] = {~你没跳过去。树根接住了你，抓着你，你在那儿吊了很久。|对面的边在你脚下塌了。}\n# en: Ride a gust over\nopt.up.label [f80013] = 乘一阵风过去\n# en: {~A gust lifts you over the hole like a leaf, and sets you down on the far side, dizzy and laughing.|You float across. Something at the bottom watches you go.}\nopt.up.say.1 [45a86b] = {~一阵风像托起一片叶子那样把你托过天坑，放在对面，你头晕目眩，哈哈大笑。|你飘了过去。坑底有什么东西看着你离开。}\n\n@event ww3_heartwood_hush\n# en: The Heartwood\ntitle [ffa94d] = 心木林\n# en: {~The heartwood: trees so old their roots are walls, and the light comes down in single shafts like rope.|The trees here are red-barked and warm to the touch, as if they had blood.|Silence, and the slow creak of trees that were old when the castle was a quarry.} You're deeper than most foragers ever go.\ntext [f6022a] = {~心木林：树老得根都成了墙，光一束一束地垂下来，像绳子。|这里的树皮是红的，摸上去是温的，像有血。|寂静，还有那些在城堡还是采石场时就已经很老的树缓慢的嘎吱声。} 你已经比大多数采集者走得都深了。\n# en: Enormous red-barked trees in a dim forest heart, their roots like walls, a single shaft of light reaching the floor.\nscene [be3ed9] = 昏暗的森林中心，巨大的红皮树木的根像墙一样，一束光照到了地面。\n# en: Turn back\nopt.left.label [2f9f2d] = 往回走\n# en: {~You turn round. It's a long way back up.|You head home while you still have legs.|You turn back, and the warm red trees watch you go.}\nopt.left.say.1 [e00c55] = {~你转身往回走。回上去的路很长。|你趁腿还能走回家了。|你往回走，温暖的红树们看着你离开。}\n# en: Gather and go on\nopt.right.label [047d5c] = 边采边往前\n# en: {~You gather red bark and root-caps in the long light, and go on, slower.|You fill your bag with warm red bark, and go on.|You climb between the roots, gathering as you go, and come out lower and more tired.}\nopt.right.say.1 [088038] = {~你在长长的光束里采红树皮和根菌，继续往前走，慢了一些。|你在包里装满温热的红树皮，继续往前。|你在树根间攀爬，一边走一边采，出来时更低了，也更累了。}\n\n@event ww_dusk\n# en: The Silence Ahead\ntitle [dce7ce] = 前方的寂静\n# en: The path runs on into a silence so thick it presses on your ears. No birds, no wind, no mutter of trees. At its edge a single moonpetal is closed tight, waiting for something. {flag(ww_midnight_known): Midnight. You know that now.|Midnight, by the look of the moon.}\ntext [29ab1b] = 小路一直延伸进一片寂静里，那寂静厚得压着你的耳朵。没有鸟，没有风，没有树的嘟囔。在它的边上，一朵月瓣花紧紧合着，在等什么。{flag(ww_midnight_known): 午夜。现在你知道了。|看月亮的样子，是午夜。}\n# en: The end of a forest path where the trees go silent and dark, a single closed silver flower at the edge of the gloom.\nscene [8ed25e] = 森林小路的尽头，树木变得寂静而幽暗，阴影边缘有一朵合拢的银色花朵。\n# en: Turn back\nopt.left.label [2f9f2d] = 往回走\n# en: {~You turn back, and the birds start again behind you, as if released.|You leave the silence to itself, and to whatever keeps the castle's hours down there.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8af391] = {~你往回走，身后的鸟又叫了起来，像被放了出来。|你把寂静留给它自己，留给那底下守着城堡时辰的东西。}\n# en: Listen a while\nopt.right.label [7fea7c] = 听一会儿\n# en: {~You stand at the edge of the silence and listen to nothing for a long time. It listens back. You go home before it's dark.|You listen until you can hear your own blood. Somewhere in the silence, very far down, something turns over in its sleep.}\nopt.right.say.1 [535168] = {~你站在寂静的边上，听了很久的虚无。它也在听你。你在天黑前回了家。|你一直听到能听见自己的血流声。在寂静里，很深很深的地方，有什么在睡梦中翻了个身。}\n\n@event ww_hush_gate\n# en: The Hush\ntitle [510138] = 寂境\n# en: At midnight the silence has opened. Every moonpetal along the edge is wide and shining, and the path goes on between them into the Hush, where the wood is so quiet you can hear the moon. {flag(ww_hush_seen): You've been here before. The Hush remembers.|Nobody told you it would be beautiful.}\ntext [3878b7] = 到了午夜，那片寂静打开了。边上每一朵月瓣花都开得大大的，闪着光，小路从它们中间一直延伸进寂境，那里的林子安静得你能听见月亮。{flag(ww_hush_seen): 你来过这里。寂境记得。|没人告诉过你，这里会这么美。}\n# en: A silent moonlit forest where every silver flower has opened at once, the trees perfectly still, a student at the threshold.\nscene [a5d9d4] = 寂静的月光森林里，所有银色的花同时开放，树木纹丝不动，一个学生站在入口。\n# en: Turn back\nopt.left.label [2f9f2d] = 往回走\n# en: {~You look, and remember, and turn back while you can.|You turn back. The moonpetal close behind you one by one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [50d27b] = {~你看着，记住，趁还能回头时回头了。|你往回走。身后的月瓣花一朵接一朵地合上了。}\n# en: Step into the Hush\nopt.right.label [7ef77c] = 踏进寂境\n# en: Deep magic: every check here is hard.\nopt.right.hint [a597d2] = 深层魔法：这里的每一次检定都很难。\n# en: {~You step in. The sound of your own boots goes away.|You walk into the Hush. It doesn't welcome you. It doesn't stop you, either.}\nopt.right.say.1 [86b806] = {~你走了进去。你自己的脚步声消失了。|你走进了寂境。它不欢迎你。它也不拦你。}\n\n@event ww4_silence\n# en: The Silence\ntitle [7483c7] = 寂静\n# en: The silence here is a thing. It presses, it leans, it wants you to stop: breathing, thinking, being. It feels like the stillness over the lake on exam mornings. Under it, very faintly, something hums one low note. ⚠ Nobody comes to catch you down here.\ntext [068bc5] = 这里的寂静是一样东西。它压着，倚着，想让你停下：停止呼吸，停止思考，停止存在。它感觉就像考试那天早上湖面上的那种静。在它下面，非常微弱地，有什么在哼着一个低音。⚠ 在这底下，没人会来接住你。\n# en: A student standing in a perfectly still moonlit forest, hands over their ears, frost forming on the leaves around them.\nscene [bc5668] = 一个学生站在纹丝不动的月光森林里，双手捂着耳朵，周围的叶子上正在结霜。\n# en: Back out of it\nopt.left.label [d58d08] = 从里面退出来\n# en: {~You back out of the silence and it lets you go, reluctantly, like a hand.|You turn back. It takes the whole walk home for your ears to stop ringing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [46b437] = {~你从寂静里退了出来，它不情愿地放开了你，像一只手。|你往回走。一路走到家，耳朵才停止嗡鸣。}\n# en: Hold your own sound\nopt.right.label [da37d0] = 守住你自己的声音\n# en: {~You hold a small spell in your mouth like a lozenge, and the silence can't get in. You walk through it humming.|You keep one spark lit inside you the whole way through. The silence presses and finds nothing to press.}\nopt.right.say.1 [479da6] = {~你把一个小咒语含在嘴里，像含一颗润喉糖，寂静进不来。你哼着歌穿了过去。|你一路在心里点着一点火花。寂静压过来，却找不到可压的东西。}\n# en: {~The silence gets in. You stop, and stand, and the next thing you know is dawn.|You can't hear your own thoughts. Then you can't have them.}\nopt.right.say.2 [30c854] = {~寂静钻了进来。你停下，站着，再知道事情时已经是黎明了。|你听不见自己的想法了。然后你连想法都没有了。}\n\n@event ww4_rime_hollow\n# en: The Rime Hollow\ntitle [09626a] = 雾凇坑\n# en: A hollow where it's always winter. Frost thick as fur on every twig, and in the middle, hanging from nothing, a single sliver of ice that glows. It's a spell, or the shape of one: the blow that should fell you finds you frozen instead.\ntext [3d6ac0] = 一个永远是冬天的坑。每根细枝上都结着厚得像毛皮的霜，正中间，悬在虚空里，是一片发着光的冰。那是一条咒语，或者说是咒语的形状：本该把你击倒的那一击，会发现你已冻住了。\n# en: A hollow in a moonlit forest where everything is coated in thick white frost, a single icicle hanging from a branch, glowing.\nscene [4ee8bf] = 月光森林里的一个坑，一切都裹着厚厚的白霜，一根冰柱挂在树枝上，发着光。\n# en: Leave the winter here\nopt.left.label [d95b56] = 把冬天留在这里\n# en: {~You leave the hollow to its winter, and go home with frost in your eyelashes.|You turn back. Your breath steams all the way out of the wood.}\nopt.left.say.1 [55267d] = {~你把这个坑留给它的冬天，睫毛上带着霜回了家。|你往回走。你的呼吸一路冒着白气，直到走出林子。}\n# en: Take the sliver\nopt.right.label [d7b0b0] = 取走那片冰\n# en: {~You take it in your bare hand. It doesn't melt; it goes in. Rime. You'll never quite be warm again, and you'll never quite be felled.|The sliver goes into your collar like a needle and stays there. Rime. The hollow thaws a little, as if relieved.}\nopt.right.say.1 [222b86] = {~你赤手拿起它。它没有融化；它钻了进去。雾凇。你再也不会真正暖和了，也再也不会真正被击倒。|那片冰像针一样钻进你的衣领，留在了那里。雾凇。那个坑稍稍解冻了一点，像是松了口气。}\n# en: {~The sliver bites. The cold goes all the way in, and you go down.|Frost climbs your arm faster than you can let go.}\nopt.right.say.2 [464d92] = {~那片冰咬了你一口。寒意一直钻到最深处，你倒下了。|霜顺着你的胳膊往上爬，比你松手还快。}\n\n@event ww4_web_halls\n# en: The Web Halls\ntitle [29b5c6] = 蛛网大厅\n# en: Webs strung between the trees like halls, silver in the moon, each thread as thick as a finger. Hanging in one, wrapped to the neck, is a hippogriff from the stables, its eyes white with fright. It's Brask's. You'd know that grey anywhere.\ntext [378aa8] = 树与树之间张着一张张网，像一座座大厅，在月光下泛着银光，每根丝都有手指那么粗。其中一张网里挂着一只从马厩来的鹰头马身有翼兽，被缠到了脖子，眼睛吓得发白。是布拉斯克的。那身灰色你在哪儿都认得出。\n# en: Vast silver webs strung between moonlit trees like cathedral halls, a cocooned hippogriff struggling in one of them.\nscene [f40817] = 月光下的树木之间张着大教堂般宏伟的银色蛛网，其中一张网里缠着一只挣扎的鹰头马身有翼兽。\n# en: Leave it; turn back\nopt.left.label [0a4520] = 不管它；往回走\n# en: {~You can't. You do. You go home and can't look at the stables for a week.|You turn back. Its eyes follow you, and then you can't see them.}\nopt.left.say.1 [da2980] = {~你不能。你还是走了。你回了家，一个星期都没法正眼看马厩。|你往回走。它的眼睛一直跟着你，然后你就看不见了。}\n# en: Cut it free\nopt.right.label [15023a] = 把它割下来\n# en: {~You saw through the silk thread by thread and the hippogriff bolts, free, for home. Brask will find it at dawn on the stable roof. Something deeper in the Hush has noticed you took it.|It comes free all at once and kicks you, gratefully, and flies. Far off, a web shivers.}\nopt.right.say.1 [5ded16] = {~你一根一根地锯断蛛丝，鹰头马身有翼兽挣脱了，朝家飞奔而去。布拉斯克会在黎明时在马厩顶上找到它。寂境更深处，有什么注意到你把它带走了。|它一下子挣脱了，感激地踢了你一脚，飞走了。远处，一张网颤了颤。}\n# en: {~The silk takes your knife, then your sleeve, then you.|You get it half free and the web gets you the rest of the way.}\nopt.right.say.2 [7ac11e] = {~蛛丝先吞了你的刀，然后是你的袖子，然后是你。|你把它弄出来一半，剩下的路由网替你走完了。}\n# en: Burn it loose\nopt.up.label [c4775b] = 把它烧下来\n# en: {~You burn the silk away from its wings and it bolts for home. The whole web hall shudders. Something deeper is very, very awake now.|Fire goes up the silk like a fuse. The hippogriff drops, rights itself, and flies for the castle.}\nopt.up.say.1 [403cf5] = {~你烧掉它翅膀上的丝，它朝家飞奔而去。整座蛛网大厅都在颤抖。更深处的什么东西，现在非常、非常清醒了。|火顺着蛛丝烧上去，像一根引信。鹰头马身有翼兽掉下来，翻正身子，朝城堡飞去。}\n\n@event ww4_last_witch\n# en: The Last Witch of the Wood\ntitle [33074c] = 林中最后的女巫\n# en: A ghost sits on a fallen log, knitting something that isn't there. She was the wood's witch, she says, before the castle, before the lake was a lake. She left when the Spinner came, and still can't decide if that was cowardice or manners. Then she asks what you want.\ntext [8eec38] = 一个鬼魂坐在一根倒木上，织着一件不存在的东西。她说，她是这片林子的女巫，在城堡之前，在湖还不是湖之前。织网者来的时候她离开了，到现在也说不清那是懦弱还是礼貌。然后她问你想要什么。\n# en: The faint silver ghost of an old woman in a shawl sitting on a fallen log in a moonlit forest, knitting.\nscene [2a5969] = 月光森林里，一个披着披肩的老妇人的淡银色鬼魂坐在一根倒木上织东西。\n# en: Nothing; turn back\nopt.left.label [ad3101] = 什么都不要；往回走\n# en: {~\"Wise,\" says the ghost, and goes on knitting the nothing.|You leave her on her log, still asking her question of the dark.}\nopt.left.say.1 [f79318] = {~“明智，”鬼魂说，继续织她那件虚无。|你把她留在她的木头上，她还在向黑暗提着她的问题。}\n# en: Ask about the Spinner\nopt.right.label [f6c641] = 问织网者的事\n# en: {~\"She has a blind side,\" the ghost says. \"The left. Something took an eye a long time ago, and she's never let anyone see. Stand on her left and talk to her like a lady, and you'll have a fair fight.\"|She tells you about the Spinner's left side, and laughs about it for a long time.}\nopt.right.say.1 [3193ef] = {~“她有个盲侧，”鬼魂说。“左边。很久以前有什么东西夺走了一只眼睛，她从没让任何人看见过。站在她左边，像对一位贵妇那样跟她说话，你就能有一场公平的较量。”|她跟你讲织网者的左侧，讲完了还笑了很久。}\n# en: {~\"No,\" says the ghost, and she isn't there, and neither is the path.|She doesn't like your question. The cold comes down like a curtain.}\nopt.right.say.2 [809a92] = {~“不，”鬼魂说，然后她不在了，小路也不在了。|她不喜欢你的问题。寒意像帷幕一样落了下来。}\n\n@event ww4_moonpetal_bloom\n# en: Moonpetal in Bloom\ntitle [cd9d18] = 盛开的月瓣花\n# en: A whole clearing of moonpetal, open to the moon, shining like a lake. You've only ever seen one bloom at a time. {has_item(moonpetal_locket): Your locket opens on its own.|{flag(ww_locket_found): You look for the root where the locket was, and can't find it.|In the middle, caught on a root, something glints.}}\ntext [718420] = 一整片空地的月瓣花，朝着月亮盛开，亮得像一片湖。你以前只见过一次开一朵。{has_item(moonpetal_locket): 你的吊盒自己打开了。|{flag(ww_locket_found): 你去找当初挂着吊盒的那条树根，却找不到了。|正中间，一条树根上挂着什么东西，在闪光。}}\n# en: A moonlit clearing carpeted with glowing silver flowers in full bloom, a student kneeling among them.\nscene [a5bb09] = 月光下的空地上铺满了盛开的发光银花，一个学生跪在花丛中。\n# en: Leave them be\nopt.left.label [8e7a0b] = 让它们开着\n# en: {~You sit and look until the moon moves, and pick nothing, and go home.|You leave them all to bloom. You'll remember this one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [accd1f] = {~你坐着看，直到月亮移了位，一朵也没摘，回家了。|你让它们全都开着。这一片你会记住的。}\n# en: Gather a few\nopt.right.label [7e629f] = 采几朵\n# en: You gather moonpetal by the armful, and the clearing hardly notices. Caught on a root you find a silver locket, old and plain. When you open it, there's a pressed moonpetal inside already.\nopt.right.say.1 [688db7] = 你一抱一抱地采月瓣花，空地几乎没察觉。在一条树根上，你发现了一只银吊盒，又旧又朴素。你打开它，里面已经夹着一片压干的月瓣花。\n# en: {~You fill your bag with moonlight and go on.|You gather by the armful, and the clearing hardly notices.}\nopt.right.say.2 [3fb83e] = {~你在包里装满月光，继续往前。|你一抱一抱地采，空地几乎没察觉。}\n\n@event ww4_spinner_court\n# en: An Audience\ntitle [6e1e78] = 觐见\n# en: The Spinner receives you in her halls, on the left side as is proper. {knows(maud.tuppence): \"You smell of her bees,\" she says, as she always does. |}\"The trees grow away from the water,\" she says. \"So do I. I've never liked what's down there.\" She waits to see if you've brought her anything.\ntext [c26d14] = 织网者在她的大厅里接见你，按规矩是在左侧。{knows(maud.tuppence): “你身上有她那些蜜蜂的味道，”她说，她每次都这么说。|}“树都背着水长，”她说。“我也是。我从来不喜欢那底下的东西。”她等着看你有没有给她带什么来。\n# en: A great grey spider the size of a carriage resting in a silver web canopy, bowing her forelegs to a student below.\nscene [572426] = 一只马车大小的灰色巨蛛栖在银色的蛛网穹顶里，向下方的学生屈下前腿。\n# en: Bow and take your leave\nopt.left.label [20d10b] = 鞠躬告辞\n# en: {~You bow. She bows her forelegs back. You go home with the strange warm feeling of having been to court.|\"Mind the left,\" she says, as you leave. It may be a joke.}\nopt.left.say.1 [23667b] = {~你鞠了一躬。她也屈下前腿回礼。你带着一种奇异而温暖的、刚刚觐见过宫廷的感觉回了家。|“当心左边，”你离开时她说。可能是个玩笑。}\n# en: Sit with her a while\nopt.right.label [4b2f48] = 陪她坐一会儿\n# en: {~You sit with her and she tells you about the wood before the castle. She gives you a bolt of silk as you go, for your trouble.|She shows you her oldest webs, which are a kind of map. You leave with silk and a better sense of the Hush.}\nopt.right.say.1 [6f5c49] = {~你陪她坐着，她给你讲城堡之前的林子。你走的时候，她送了你一卷丝，算是辛苦费。|她给你看她最老的网，那是一种地图。你离开时带着丝，也对寂境多了几分了解。}\n\n@event ww_hush_heart\n# en: The Heart of the Hush\ntitle [100ca2] = 寂境之心\n# en: The heart of the Hush is a ring of silvered trees round a pool that reflects nothing but the moon. Nobody is waiting here any more. The wood is yours, as much as a wood is ever anyone's.\ntext [1d374d] = 寂境之心是一圈泛着银光的树，围着一潭只映出月亮的水。再也没有人在这里等着了。林子是你的了，就像一片林子能属于任何人那样。\n# en: The silent moonlit heart of a forest, a ring of silver trees around a still pool, a student alone and unafraid.\nscene [574f8e] = 寂静的月光森林中心，一圈银色的树围着一潭静水，一个学生独自站着，毫无惧色。\n# en: Take a little, and go home\nopt.ok.label [d1c769] = 拿一点，回家\n# en: {~You fill your bag at the edge of the pool and walk home under the moon, unhurried, unafraid.|You sit by the pool until the moon moves, and take a little, and go home.}\nopt.ok.say.1 [dac078] = {~你在水边装满了包，在月光下走回家，不慌不忙，无所畏惧。|你在水边坐到月亮移了位，拿了一点，回家了。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/events/whisperwood/hush.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/events/whisperwood/hush.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@event ww_spinner_meet\n# en: The Spinner\ntitle [2903b9] = 织网者\n# en: Silk across the path, then silk everywhere, and then the Spinner comes down her thread: grey, vast, queen of the Hush, with more eyes than you'd like and one of them milky. {knows(maud.tuppence): She lowers her head to you. \"You smell of her bees,\" she says.|\"A student,\" she says, in a voice like a cello. \"It's been a while.\"} {flag(ww_spinner_angry): \"You took my hippogriff.\"|}\ntext [150e54] = 先是横在路上的蛛丝，然后到处都是蛛丝，接着织网者顺着丝线降了下来：灰色，庞大，寂境的女王，眼睛多得让人不舒服，其中一只蒙着乳白的翳。{knows(maud.tuppence): 她朝你低下头来。“你身上有她那些蜜蜂的味道，”她说。|“一个学生，”她说，嗓音像大提琴。“好久没见过了。”} {flag(ww_spinner_angry): “你偷走了我的鹰头马身有翼兽。”|}\n# en: A great grey spider the size of a carriage descending on a silver thread in a moonlit forest, many eyes gleaming.\nscene [c84908] = 一只马车大小的灰色巨蛛顺着银丝降落在月光下的森林里，许多只眼睛闪着光。\n# en: Bow and back away\nopt.left.label [224649] = 鞠躬，慢慢退开\n# en: {~You bow, very low, and back away. She lets you go. \"Another night,\" she says. It sounds like a promise.|You bow and retreat. Her eyes follow you a long way, all but the milky one.}\nopt.left.say.1 [8b406b] = {~你深深鞠了一躬，往后退。她放你走了。“改天晚上，”她说。听起来像一个承诺。|你鞠躬后撤。她的眼睛一路跟着你，除了那只蒙翳的。}\n# en: Ask for a fair fight\nopt.right.label [9d1c3a] = 要求一场公平的较量\n# en: {flag(ww_spinner_secret): You know her blind side.|She's proud: bargain well and she'll fight fair.}\nopt.right.hint [054d10] = {flag(ww_spinner_secret): 你知道她的盲侧。|她很骄傲：谈得好，她就会公平地打。}\n# en: {flag(ww_spinner_weak): You step to her left and speak to her like a lady. She hesitates, then laughs. \"Fair, then,\" she says, and sends her brood away.|{flag(ww_spinner_angry): \"You stole from my larder,\" she says. \"There's no bargaining after that.\"|She doesn't care for your terms. Her brood comes down the threads behind her.}}\nopt.right.say.1 [69fa47] = {flag(ww_spinner_weak): 你站到她的左侧，像对一位贵妇那样跟她说话。她迟疑了一下，然后笑了。“那就公平地来，”她说，把幼蛛们打发走了。|{flag(ww_spinner_angry): “你偷了我的储粮，”她说。“那之后就没什么好谈的了。”|她不喜欢你的条件。她身后，幼蛛们顺着丝线爬了下来。}}\n\n@event ww_spinner_fight\n# en: The Long Wait\ntitle [0171eb] = 漫长的等待\n# en: She comes down at you with all her brood. Venom, then web, then the long wait: she'll bind you, and then she'll strike when you can't move. ⚠ Nobody comes to catch you down here. {flag(ww_spinner_offended): She won't let you leave now.|}\ntext [369e1a] = 她带着所有幼蛛朝你扑下来。先是毒，然后是网，然后是漫长的等待：她会缚住你，等你动弹不得时再出手。⚠ 在这底下，没人会来接住你。{flag(ww_spinner_offended): 现在她不会让你走了。|}\n# en: A giant grey spider rearing in a moonlit web hall, venom glistening, a student braced below with a wand raised.\nscene [aeb15c] = 月光下的蛛网大厅里，一只灰色巨蛛人立而起，毒液闪亮，下方一个学生举着魔杖严阵以待。\n# en: Yield\nopt.left.label [0bde4e] = 认输\n# en: {~You lower your wand. She takes her toll from your bag and lets you go. \"Another night,\" she says.|You yield. She considers this, and takes her share of your haul, and lets you walk away.}\nopt.left.say.1 [5953d2] = {~你放下魔杖。她从你包里取走她的过路费，放你走了。“改天晚上，”她说。|你认输了。她想了想，从你的收获里拿走了她那一份，让你离开。}\n# en: Fight her\nopt.right.label [d6ea52] = 跟她打\n# en: She binds, then strikes: keep a way out.\nopt.right.hint [d885a4] = 她先缚后击：给自己留条退路。\n# en: {~She falls back, legs folded, and bows her head to the ground. \"Well,\" she says. \"Well. It's been a long time since anyone did that.\"|Her last strike misses. She stops, and lowers herself, and the web halls go still.}\nopt.right.say.1 [f937de] = {~她退开了，腿收拢起来，头低到地上。“好，”她说。“好啊。很久没人做到过了。”|她最后一击落空了。她停下来，伏低身子，蛛网大厅静了下来。}\n# en: {~The web holds you. The long wait ends. Silk, everywhere, warm and tight, and then nothing.|She binds you and you can't answer the blow. The silk comes up over your eyes.}\nopt.right.say.2 [2c21f7] = {~蛛网缠住了你。漫长的等待结束了。到处都是蛛丝，温暖而紧，然后什么都没有了。|她缚住了你，你接不住那一击。蛛丝漫过了你的眼睛。}\n\n@event ww_spinner_fight_weak\n# en: A Fair Fight\ntitle [bf4310] = 公平的较量\n# en: She's sent the brood away. You stand on her left, where the milky eye can't see you, and she knows that you know. She's still the biggest thing in the wood. ⚠ Nobody comes to catch you down here.\ntext [589cea] = 她把幼蛛打发走了。你站在她的左侧，那只蒙翳的眼睛看不见你，而她知道你知道。她依然是这片森林里最大的东西。⚠ 在这底下，没人会来接住你。\n# en: A giant grey spider facing a student alone in a moonlit clearing, the student standing on her blind left side.\nscene [d0fbd6] = 月光下的林间空地上，一只灰色巨蛛独自面对一个学生，学生站在她看不见的左侧。\n# en: Yield\nopt.left.label [0bde4e] = 认输\n# en: {~You lower your wand. \"Sensible,\" she says, and takes her toll from your bag, and lets you go.|You yield. She bows, a little mocking, and takes a fee from your bag.}\nopt.left.say.1 [874e6a] = {~你放下魔杖。“明智，”她说，从你包里取走过路费，放你走了。|你认输了。她略带嘲弄地欠了欠身，从你包里拿走一份报酬。}\n# en: Fight her\nopt.right.label [d6ea52] = 跟她打\n# en: Stay on her blind side.\nopt.right.hint [7898a2] = 一直待在她的盲侧。\n# en: {~You stay on her blind side and she can't find you in time. At last she folds her legs and bows. \"Fairly done,\" she says.|She turns and turns, and you're always on her left. In the end she laughs and stops.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ffc3ad] = {~你一直待在她的盲侧，她总是来不及找到你。最后她收拢腿，低头致意。“打得公道，”她说。|她转了又转，而你总在她左边。最后她笑了，停了下来。}\n# en: {~Even half-blind, she's faster. The silk comes up warm and tight.|She finds you by your breathing. The web closes.}\nopt.right.say.2 [d2bf6e] = {~就算瞎了半边，她还是更快。蛛丝温暖而紧地漫了上来。|她听着你的呼吸找到了你。蛛网合拢了。}\n\n@event ww_spinner_hoard\n# en: The Spinner's Hoard\ntitle [6b5f51] = 织网者的宝藏\n# en: She bows her forelegs to you, the way the old courts did. Behind her, in the oldest web, is a hoard: lost coins, a christening spoon, sixty years of things that fell out of foragers' pockets, and bolts of her own grey silk, which a tailor could make into anything. \"Take what you like,\" she says. \"You've earned the left side.\"\ntext [0d40e0] = 她向你屈下前腿，像古时的宫廷那样行礼。在她身后最古老的那张网里，有一堆宝藏：遗失的硬币，一把洗礼银勺，六十年来从采集者口袋里掉出来的东西，还有一卷卷她自己的灰色蛛丝，裁缝能用它做出任何东西。“喜欢什么就拿吧，”她说。“左侧是你赢来的。”\n# en: A great grey spider bowing her forelegs to a student amid a hoard of glittering trinkets and bolts of silver silk.\nscene [974f18] = 一只灰色巨蛛在一堆闪亮的小玩意儿和一卷卷银色蛛丝中间，向一个学生屈下前腿。\n# en: Take silk and silver\nopt.ok.label [ac4ea7] = 拿走蛛丝和银子\n# en: You take a bolt of silk and as much silver as you can carry. She watches you go. \"Tell the girl I'm well,\" she says. {knows(maud.tuppence): She means Maud, who raised her in the potting shed, and you'll tell her.|You don't yet know who she means.} Behind you, the Hush feels emptier, and something else in it shifts, interested.\nopt.ok.say.1 [1f7802] = 你拿了一卷蛛丝，还有能带走的所有银子。她看着你离开。“告诉那姑娘我很好，”她说。{knows(maud.tuppence): 她说的是老莫德，在盆栽棚里把她养大的人，你会转告她的。|你还不知道她指的是谁。}在你身后，寂境显得更空了，而里面另有什么东西动了动，来了兴趣。\n\n@event ww_cocooned\n# en: Cut Free\ntitle [834867] = 割开蛛茧\n# en: Silk, warm and tight and everywhere. Then a voice outside it: \"Tuppence. Let go, dearie. That one's ours.\" A pruning knife slits the silk, and Old Maud's face is there, lit by a lantern, very cross. Above her the Spinner is waiting, quite still, as if she'd been told off.\ntext [b69b18] = 蛛丝，温暖，紧密，无处不在。然后外面传来一个声音：“塔彭丝。松开，亲爱的。那个是我们的人。”一把修枝刀划开了蛛丝，老莫德的脸出现了，被灯笼照着，非常生气。在她上方，织网者一动不动地等着，像是挨了训。\n# en: An old woman with a pruning knife cutting a silk cocoon open in a moonlit forest, a giant spider watching quietly above.\nscene [4eb6a2] = 月光下的森林里，一位老妇人用修枝刀割开一个蛛茧，一只巨蛛在上方安静地看着。\n# en: Let Maud take you home\nopt.left.label [64f10a] = 让莫德送你回家\n# en: Maud walks you all the way home without a word. At the door she says, \"She was the size of my hand, once. I kept her in the potting shed.\" Then she goes back into the dark, toward the wood.\nopt.left.say.1 [2a7107] = 莫德一路把你送回家，一句话也没说。到了门口，她说：“她以前只有我的手那么大。我把她养在育苗棚里。”然后她又走回黑暗里，朝森林去了。\n# en: Ask her who Tuppence is\nopt.right.label [7d3de9] = 问她塔彭丝是谁\n# en: \"Mine,\" says Maud. \"Once. She outgrew the potting shed, and I let her go.\" She looks back at the trees. \"She keeps worse things out, you know. Don't you go thinking she's the worst thing down there.\"\nopt.right.say.1 [ed89bb] = “我的，”莫德说。“以前是。她长得育苗棚装不下了，我就放她走了。”她回头望着树林。“她把更糟的东西挡在外面，你知道吗。可别以为她是那底下最糟的东西。”\n\n@event ww_outcast_meet\n# en: A Lantern Left Burning\ntitle [89529a] = 一盏留着的灯\n# en: Where the Spinner's webs were, there's a ring of silvered trees and a lantern left burning. An old man in a frayed academy robe, decades out of date, is sitting on a root beside it. \"Absalom Vrell,\" he says. \"Expelled, before your parents were born. I've been waiting for her to fall. The lake hums at night, down here. I don't ask what about. Nobody sensible does.\"\ntext [f5e08a] = 织网者的网原来所在的地方，现在是一圈泛着银光的树，和一盏留着没灭的灯笼。一个穿着磨旧了的学院长袍的老人坐在灯旁的树根上，那长袍的式样已经过时几十年了。“阿布萨隆·弗雷尔，”他说。“被开除了，在你父母出生之前。我一直在等她倒下。夜里，这底下的湖会嗡嗡作响。我不问它在响什么。明白人都不问。”\n# en: A gaunt elegant old man in a frayed academy robe sitting on a tree root beside a lantern in a ring of silver trees.\nscene [318be1] = 一圈银色的树中间，一个清瘦优雅、穿着磨旧学院长袍的老人坐在灯笼旁的树根上。\n# en: Not tonight\nopt.left.label [4bf72b] = 今晚不了\n# en: {~\"Another night, then,\" he says, amused, and turns the lantern down.|He waves you off without getting up. \"I'll be here. I'm always here.\"}\nopt.left.say.1 [035786] = {~“那就改天晚上，”他说，觉得挺有意思，把灯笼调暗了。|他坐着没起身，挥手让你走。“我会在这儿。我一直都在这儿。”}\n# en: Sit down with him\nopt.right.label [c106ad] = 在他对面坐下\n# en: {~You sit on the other side of the lantern. He looks pleased, and then hungry, and then polite again.|You sit. He offers you tea from a flask. It's very good, and you drink it carefully.}\nopt.right.say.1 [034c10] = {~你在灯笼另一边坐下。他看起来很高兴，然后很饥渴，然后又恢复了礼貌。|你坐下了。他从水壶里给你倒了杯茶。茶很好，你喝得很小心。}\n\n@event ww_outcast_table\n# en: Absalom Vrell\ntitle [e09229] = 阿布萨隆·弗雷尔\n# en: \"I taught the thornwitch,\" he says. \"And those children in masks, after a fashion, though they never paid attention. I know every spell they burned me for. I'd teach you one. Or,\" and his eyes light, \"you could try me. Nobody has in forty years. Nothing would be at stake but my pride.\"\ntext [249da2] = “荆棘女巫是我教的，”他说。“那些戴面具的孩子，也算是吧，虽然他们从来不认真听。他们为之把我烧出去的每一条咒语，我都会。我可以教你一条。或者，”他眼睛亮了起来，“你可以跟我比一场。四十年没人试过了。除了我的面子，什么都不赌。”\n# en: An old man and a student sitting across a lantern in a moonlit forest, a black book open on his knee.\nscene [c35fdc] = 月光下的森林里，一个老人和一个学生隔着灯笼相对而坐，他膝上摊着一本黑色的书。\n# en: Bargain for a lesson\nopt.left.label [27795b] = 讨价还价，换一课\n# en: Forbidden spells; he drives a hard bargain.\nopt.left.hint [f209d6] = 禁咒；他很会讨价还价。\n# en: {~He teaches you, there by the lantern, with a patience that's almost kind. It's a terrible thing, beautifully explained. You go home with it under your tongue.|\"Good,\" he says. \"You argue like we used to.\" He teaches you one thing, and makes you promise nothing.}\nopt.left.say.1 [09a114] = {~他就在灯旁教了你，耐心得近乎温柔。一件可怕的东西，讲解得很美。你把它含在舌下带回了家。|“很好，”他说。“你吵架的样子像我们那时候。”他教了你一样东西，没让你承诺任何事。}\n# en: {~\"Almost,\" he says, delighted. \"Another night.\" He turns the lantern down.|He laughs and shakes his head. \"Not tonight. But you're close.\"}\nopt.left.say.2 [4c7098] = {~“差一点，”他说，很开心。“改天晚上。”他把灯笼调暗了。|他笑着摇头。“今晚不行。不过你很接近了。”}\n# en: {~He shrugs. \"Another night.\" He doesn't seem disappointed. He has a great many nights.|He listens to your terms, and then to the Hush, and then says no, kindly.}\nopt.left.say.3 [f387d9] = {~他耸耸肩。“改天晚上。”他看起来并不失望。他有很多很多个晚上。|他听完你的条件，又听了听寂境，然后和气地说了不。}\n# en: Challenge him to a duel\nopt.right.label [617dec] = 向他提出决斗\n# en: Nothing at stake but pride.\nopt.right.hint [6a06fe] = 除了面子，什么都不赌。\n# en: {~He stands, and his old robe straightens itself. \"Oh,\" he says. \"Oh, lovely.\"|He puts down his tea. For the first time, he looks young.}\nopt.right.say.1 [ec84a5] = {~他站起身，那件旧长袍自己抚平了。“噢，”他说。“噢，太好了。”|他放下茶杯。第一次，他看起来年轻了。}\n\n@event ww_outcast_fight\n# en: Every Forbidden Thing\ntitle [9640d5] = 每一样禁忌\n# en: He fights with everything they expelled him for: Heartprice and Siphon, Compel and Eclipse, all at once, at a grandmaster's speed, and he's laughing. There's nothing at stake. He promised. It doesn't feel like nothing.\ntext [071d3f] = 他用他们开除他的所有理由来跟你打：心价与虹吸，强令与蚀，全都一起上，以大宗师的速度，而且他在笑。什么都不赌。他保证过。可感觉并不像什么都不赌。\n# en: An old wizard in a frayed robe casting dark spells in a ring of silver trees, a student dodging, a lantern on the ground between them.\nscene [1d35c8] = 一圈银色的树中间，一个穿着磨旧长袍的老巫师施放着黑暗的咒语，一个学生在闪躲，两人之间的地上放着一盏灯笼。\n# en: Walk away\nopt.left.label [96da1a] = 走开\n# en: {~You lower your wand. He bows. \"Another night,\" he says. \"Come back when you're bored of winning.\"|You step back out of the ring. He's not offended. He's already pouring more tea.}\nopt.left.say.1 [a15818] = {~你放下魔杖。他欠身行礼。“改天晚上，”他说。“等你赢腻了再来。”|你退出了那个圈子。他并不介意。他已经在倒第二杯茶了。}\n# en: Fight him\nopt.right.label [b8f327] = 跟他打\n# en: A grandmaster; you lose nothing by losing.\nopt.right.hint [25c197] = 一位大宗师；输了你也什么都不会失去。\n# en: {~His last spell fizzles. He looks at his hand, then at you, and laughs until he has to sit down.|You catch him between Heartprice and Siphon, with nothing left to pay. He bows, very low.}\nopt.right.say.1 [db3835] = {~他最后一个咒语哧地灭了。他看看自己的手，又看看你，笑得不得不坐下。|你在心价和虹吸之间抓住了他，他已经没什么可付了。他深深地鞠了一躬。}\n# en: {~You give him the fight of his life, nearly. He stops you, gently, a breath from winning.|You come close. He knows it. So do you.}\nopt.right.say.2 [73bf60] = {~你差一点就给了他这辈子最好的一场。他轻轻拦住了你，离赢只差一口气。|你很接近了。他知道。你也知道。}\n# en: {~He takes you apart with great courtesy. Then he helps you up.|You don't touch him. He doesn't hurt you either, much.}\nopt.right.say.3 [1f4b63] = {~他彬彬有礼地把你拆了个七零八落。然后扶你起来。|你连碰都没碰到他。他也没怎么伤你。}\n\n@event ww_outcast_victory\n# en: The Hush Is Yours\ntitle [23e658] = 寂境归你了\n# en: Absalom Vrell bows to you as deep as his old back allows. \"Forty years,\" he says. \"Well. The Hush is yours, then. Mind the lake.\" He sets his black grimoire on the root, and laughs, and is gone in a breath of silver frost. The lantern goes out on its own.\ntext [d0ff0b] = 阿布萨隆·弗雷尔向你鞠躬，深到他那把老骨头所能及。“四十年，”他说。“好吧。那寂境就归你了。当心那片湖。”他把那本黑色魔典放在树根上，笑了一声，化作一口银霜的气息不见了。灯笼自己熄了。\n# en: An old wizard bowing deeply to a student in a moonlit forest, then dissolving into silver frost, a black book left on the root.\nscene [91e1c7] = 月光下的森林里，一个老巫师向一个学生深深鞠躬，随即化作银霜消散，一本黑色的书留在树根上。\n# en: Take the grimoire\nopt.ok.label [1c76c9] = 拿起魔典\n# en: You pick up the grimoire. It's cold, and some of its pages are blank, and they won't stay blank. Somewhere in the castle, someone will hear of this by breakfast. The headmistress, probably.\nopt.ok.say.1 [bef35b] = 你拿起了魔典。它很冷，有些书页是空白的，而它们不会一直空白。城堡里某个人，早饭前就会听说这件事。多半是院长。\n\n@event ww_outcast_loss\n# en: Another Night\ntitle [5f8bc2] = 改天晚上\n# en: Absalom helps you up, brushes the leaves off your shoulder, and pours you a cup of tea from his flask. \"Come back when you're bored of winning,\" he says. \"I'm not going anywhere. That's rather the point of me.\"\ntext [29eefb] = 阿布萨隆扶你起来，拍掉你肩上的落叶，从水壶里给你倒了一杯茶。“等你赢腻了再来，”他说。“我哪儿也不去。这正是我存在的意义。”\n# en: An old wizard helping a student up off the forest floor beside a lantern, both smiling.\nscene [f57fe8] = 灯笼旁，一个老巫师把一个学生从林地上扶起来，两人都在笑。\n# en: Drink the tea, and go home\nopt.ok.label [66e590] = 喝完茶，回家\n# en: {~The tea is very good. You walk home under the moon, beaten and oddly cheerful.|You drink it, thank him, and go home. Next time, you think.}\nopt.ok.say.1 [a5cc0d] = {~茶很好。你在月光下走回家，输了，却莫名地高兴。|你喝了茶，谢过他，回家了。下次吧，你想。}\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/states/contraband.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/states/contraband.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@state brightwater_bottle\n# en: A Bottle of Brightwater\nname [b09be2] = 一瓶亮泉水\n# en: Hidden in your trunk, wrapped in a sock: one dose of a banned elixir, cloudy gold.\ndesc [876716] = 藏在你的箱子里，裹在一只袜子里：一剂违禁的药水，浑浊的金色。\n\n@state owls_ink_bottle\n# en: A Bottle of Owl's Ink\nname [e00ecd] = 一瓶枭墨\n# en: Hidden in your trunk, wrapped in a sock: one dose of a banned elixir, black and bitter.\ndesc [4eca50] = 藏在你的箱子里，裹在一只袜子里：一剂违禁的药水，又黑又苦。\n\n@state brightwater\n# en: Brightwater\nname [9cc570] = 亮泉水\n# en: You sleep like a stone and wake like a bell. It's lovely. It's banned.\ndesc [5a4141] = 你睡得像块石头，醒来像口钟。美妙极了。也是违禁的。\n\n@state owls_ink\n# en: Owl's Ink\nname [7ccd47] = 枭墨\n# en: Your mind is a new nib and your magic a full well. Exams look small from up here.\ndesc [22fe52] = 你的头脑像一支新笔尖，魔力像一口满井。从这儿往下看，考试都显得很小。\n\n@state brightwater_want\n# en: Wanting Brightwater\nname [642fee] = 想要亮泉水\n# en: The world has gone a shade greyer since the Brightwater wore off, and you know exactly what would fix it. Sister Amaranth, they say, has seen this before.\ndesc [8de9cc] = 亮泉水的劲儿过去以后，世界灰了一层，而你很清楚什么能让它变回来。听说阿玛兰斯修女以前见过这种情形。\n\n@state owls_ink_want\n# en: Wanting Owl's Ink\nname [6de299] = 想要枭墨\n# en: Since the ink wore off your magic comes slow and the page swims, and you know exactly what would fix it. Sister Amaranth, they say, has seen this before.\ndesc [4971af] = 墨的劲儿过去以后，你的魔力来得很慢，书页上的字也在游动，而你很清楚什么能让它变回来。听说阿玛兰斯修女以前见过这种情形。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/states/states.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/states/states.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@state origin_noble\n# en: Minor Nobility\nname [ccd408] = 小贵族出身\n# en: A family name that opens doors, and letters from home that bring money and expectations in the same envelope.\ndesc [1593e8] = 一个能敲开大门的姓氏，还有家书：钱和期望装在同一个信封里。\n# en: Comfortable: 60 crowns from home to start, and 38 from the family before each later term, now and then with a gift. Every letter weighs on you a little. Tuition is yours to pay.\nmoney [72c925] = 宽裕：起步时家里给 60 克朗，此后每学期开学前家里寄来 38 克朗，偶尔还有一份礼物。每封信都让你心里沉一沉。学费自己付。\n\n@state origin_scholar\n# en: Scholarship Student\nname [036234] = 奖学金学生\n# en: The Crown pays for clever students, for as long as they stay clever.\ndesc [a89deb] = 王室资助聪明的学生——只要他们一直聪明。\n# en: Tight: 30 crowns to start. Before each later term, 38 crowns if last term's GPA was 3.0 or better, 46 at 3.5 or better, and nothing below. Tuition is yours to pay.\nmoney [5d6aae] = 拮据：起步 30 克朗。此后每学期开学前，若上学期 GPA 达到 3.0，发 38 克朗；达到 3.5，发 46 克朗；低于 3.0 则分文没有。学费自己付。\n\n@state origin_hedge\n# en: Hedge-Witch's Grandchild\nname [b36bba] = 乡野女巫的孙辈\n# en: Raised among drying herbs and muttered charms: a practical hand with potions, and not much patience for theory.\ndesc [e66cc4] = 在晾晒的草药和念叨的咒语间长大：调药是一把好手，对理论却没什么耐心。\n# en: Poor: 15 crowns and no money from home. You will need a job from the first term.\nmoney [fa3a9c] = 贫穷：15 克朗，家里不会寄钱来。你从第一学期起就需要打工。\n\n@state origin_street\n# en: Street-Raised\nname [9b8f09] = 街头长大\n# en: You learned to read crowds before you learned to read runes.\ndesc [8551b3] = 你学会读懂人群，比学会读懂符文还早。\n# en: Poor: 8 crowns and no one to send more. You will need a job from the first term.\nmoney [224c03] = 贫穷：8 克朗，也没人会再寄钱来。你从第一学期起就需要打工。\n\n@state origin_merchant\n# en: Merchant's Child\nname [a051a5] = 商人之子\n# en: Everything has a price, and you were taught to haggle it down.\ndesc [c5b5c4] = 万物皆有价，而你从小就学会了砍价。\n# en: Rich but unsteady: 120 crowns to start, and 78 from the company before each later term when trade goes well, about two terms in three; nothing when it doesn't. Tuition is yours to pay.\nmoney [ae479e] = 富有但不稳定：起步 120 克朗；此后每学期开学前，生意好时商行寄来 78 克朗，大约三个学期有两个；生意不好就分文没有。学费自己付。\n\n@state class_pyromancy\n# en: Elective: Pyromancy\nname [bc2cb8] = 选修：火焰术\n# en: Fire answers to will, not wishes. Singed eyebrows are considered a rite of passage.\ndesc [f7cdfd] = 火焰回应意志，而非愿望。烧焦眉毛被视为一种成人礼。\n\n@state class_tidecalling\n# en: Elective: Tidecalling\nname [25e58c] = 选修：唤潮术\n# en: Lessons on the lake shore, bending water and patience in equal measure.\ndesc [550653] = 在湖岸上课，操纵湖水与磨练耐心，二者同等重要。\n\n@state class_illusion\n# en: Elective: Illusion & Glamour\nname [b30964] = 选修：幻术与魅影\n# en: The art of making people see what you want. Professor Vey insists it is not lying.\ndesc [7aa865] = 让人看见你想让他们看见的东西的艺术。维伊教授坚称这不算撒谎。\n\n@state class_alchemy\n# en: Elective: Practical Alchemy\nname [8a43a8] = 选修：实用炼金\n# en: Brewing, distilling and the occasional controlled explosion.\ndesc [4cfafb] = 熬煮、蒸馏，以及偶尔一场受控的爆炸。\n\n@state class_divination\n# en: Elective: Divination\nname [14ec75] = 选修：占卜学\n# en: Tea leaves, star charts and the Mirror of Next Tuesday.\ndesc [531e06] = 茶叶、星图，还有“下周二之镜”。\n\n@state class_artifice\n# en: Elective: Artifice\nname [20fedd] = 选修：奇械术\n# en: Enchanting objects to do what they were never meant to do.\ndesc [1d11f0] = 给物品施加魔法，让它们做本不该做的事。\n\n@state class_beastcraft\n# en: Elective: Beastcraft\nname [664aeb] = 选修：驭兽学\n# en: Familiars, griffon husbandry and dodging the manticore pen.\ndesc [d23187] = 魔宠、狮鹫饲养，以及绕开蝎尾狮的围栏。\n\n@state class_astromancy\n# en: Elective: Astromancy\nname [58b65d] = 选修：星象学\n# en: Midnight lectures on the observatory roof. You will learn the stars; you will lose sleep.\ndesc [9aeff9] = 天文台屋顶上的午夜讲座。你会认识星星，也会失去睡眠。\n\n@state class_healing\n# en: Elective: Healing Arts\nname [9056e4] = 选修：治愈术\n# en: Mending flesh with light and needle. Sister Amaranth expects perfection.\ndesc [1f8e64] = 以光与针缝合血肉。阿玛兰斯修女要求完美。\n\n@state class_umbral\n# en: Elective: Umbral Theory\nname [44e968] = 选修：暗影理论\n# en: Restricted. The study of shadow, death and what lies behind both. Strictly theoretical. Mostly.\ndesc [ddfba6] = 受限课程。研究阴影、死亡，以及二者背后的东西。纯属理论。大体上是。\n\n@state club_dueling\n# en: Club: Dueling Circle\nname [ba1685] = 社团：决斗社\n# en: Bruises, glory and the Spring Tournament.\ndesc [284ecb] = 淤青、荣耀，还有春季锦标赛。\n\n@state club_choir\n# en: Club: Choir of the Spheres\nname [7a5e02] = 社团：天球合唱团\n# en: Harmonies that make the candles flicker in time.\ndesc [f7c729] = 和声能让烛火随着节拍摇曳。\n\n@state club_skyrace\n# en: Club: Skyrace Squad\nname [ffb40f] = 社团：天空竞速队\n# en: Brooms, hoops and screaming crowds.\ndesc [727b30] = 飞帚、光环和尖叫的人群。\n\n@state club_ledger\n# en: Club: The Lumen Ledger\nname [c5d754] = 社团：《流明纪事报》社\n# en: The student paper. Deadlines, scoops and enemies.\ndesc [0bbdce] = 学生报纸。截稿日、独家新闻和仇家。\n\n@state club_garden\n# en: Club: Verdant Circle\nname [b7b687] = 社团：青翠社\n# en: Greenhouse afternoons with mandrakes that sing off-key.\ndesc [443e64] = 温室里的午后时光，还有唱歌跑调的曼德拉草。\n\n@state club_stargazers\n# en: Club: Stargazers\nname [82b69d] = 社团：观星社\n# en: Telescopes, cocoa and constellations nobody else can name.\ndesc [7d729f] = 望远镜、热可可，以及谁也叫不出名字的星座。\n\n@state club_cartographers\n# en: Club: Midnight Cartographers\nname [6737ac] = 社团：午夜制图社\n# en: A club that officially does not exist, mapping corridors that officially do not either.\ndesc [e99289] = 一个官方上并不存在的社团，绘制着官方上同样不存在的走廊。\n\n@state job_library\n# en: Job: Library Assistant\nname [79ff99] = 工作：图书馆助理\n# en: Reshelving books that bite. Quiet hours, modest pay.\ndesc [c1eafc] = 把会咬人的书放回书架。工时安静，薪水微薄。\n\n@state job_tavern\n# en: Job: The Gilded Newt\nname [73eefc] = 工作：镀金蝾螈酒馆\n# en: Serving cider in the village tavern. Good tips, sore feet.\ndesc [598a90] = 在镇上的酒馆端苹果酒。小费不错，脚很酸。\n\n@state job_apothecary\n# en: Job: Apothecary Clerk\nname [1e38cf] = 工作：药铺店员\n# en: Labelling jars at Pell & Daughters. Do not lick anything.\ndesc [ddcc95] = 在佩尔父女药铺给瓶瓶罐罐贴标签。什么都别舔。\n\n@state job_stables\n# en: Job: Familiar Stables\nname [ecb6b5] = 工作：魔宠马厩\n# en: Feeding hippogriffs and mucking out things that breathe smoke.\ndesc [1c9431] = 喂鹰头马身有翼兽，清扫会喷烟的东西。\n\n@state job_tutor\n# en: Job: First-Year Tutor\nname [c57f7a] = 工作：一年级辅导员\n# en: Explaining the difference between a hex and a jinx, again.\ndesc [0f630b] = 再一次解释“恶咒”和“小咒”的区别。\n\n@state job_courier\n# en: Job: Broom Courier\nname [c82174] = 工作：飞帚信使\n# en: Parcels across the lake at full speed. Pays well. Hurts often.\ndesc [216f36] = 全速飞越湖面送包裹。报酬丰厚。经常受伤。\n\n@state hungry\n# en: Hungry\nname [0ae59c] = 饥饿\n# en: The board went unpaid this month. Bread rolls pocketed from the refectory only go so far.\ndesc [67e035] = 这个月的伙食费没交上。从膳堂顺来的面包卷也只能撑这么久。\n# en: {~Your stomach growls through every lecture.|Bread rolls in your pockets, and not enough of them.|You learn exactly how long a bowl of porridge lasts.}\ntick.say.1 [169a81] = {~每节课上你的肚子都在叫。|兜里揣着面包，可惜不够多。|你精确地知道了一碗粥能撑多久。}\n\n@state sick\n# en: Lake Fever\nname [977d44] = 湖热病\n# en: Shivers, sniffles and a nose that glows faintly blue.\ndesc [ca2430] = 发抖、流鼻涕，鼻头还泛着淡淡的蓝光。\n# en: {~You sniffle through the month, nose glowing faintly blue.|Shivers, tea and far too many handkerchiefs.}\ntick.say.1 [f0b7d2] = {~你吸着鼻子过了一整个月，鼻尖泛着淡淡的蓝光。|发抖、喝茶，还有多得离谱的手帕。}\n\n@state injured\n# en: Injured\nname [624ada] = 受伤\n# en: Something is bruised, sprained or bent the wrong way.\ndesc [316db4] = 身上有什么地方青了、扭了，或者弯错了方向。\n# en: {~Every staircase reminds you where it hurts.|You limp, and the moving stairs show no sympathy.}\ntick.say.1 [c69fb6] = {~每一段楼梯都在提醒你哪里疼。|你一瘸一拐，会动的楼梯毫不同情。}\n\n@state exhausted\n# en: Exhausted\nname [34cdec] = 精疲力竭\n# en: You could sleep standing up. You might have.\ndesc [6c252f] = 你站着都能睡着。说不定已经睡过了。\n# en: {~You yawn through a whole lecture without ever closing your mouth.|You could sleep standing up, and once you do.}\ntick.say.1 [1a54ed] = {~你打了一整节课的哈欠，嘴就没合上过。|你站着都能睡着，而且真睡着过一次。}\n\n@state inspired\n# en: Inspired\nname [b91753] = 灵感迸发\n# en: Ideas arrive faster than you can write them down.\ndesc [ba8e50] = 想法涌来的速度比你记下来还快。\n# en: {~Ideas arrive faster than you can write them down.|You fill a notebook and start another.}\ntick.say.1 [d826d8] = {~灵感来得比你记下的还快。|你写满一本笔记，又开了一本。}\n\n@state heartbroken\n# en: Heartbroken\nname [a560a2] = 心碎\n# en: Songs are suddenly about you. All of them.\ndesc [dd2311] = 突然之间，所有的歌唱的都是你。每一首。\n# en: {~Every song in the common room is about you.|You walk the long way round, to miss a certain corridor.}\ntick.say.1 [f73fc5] = {~公共休息室里的每首歌都在唱你。|你绕远路走，只为避开某条走廊。}\n\n@state slacking\n# en: Skipping Classes\nname [169826] = 逃课中\n# en: Freedom! Also: falling behind.\ndesc [e0a116] = 自由！以及：进度落后。\n# en: {~A free morning, a lie-in, a twinge of guilt.|You skip a lecture, and nobody seems to mind yet.}\ntick.say.1 [2416b1] = {~一个自由的早晨，一场懒觉，一丝内疚。|你逃了一节课，暂时好像没人在意。}\n\n@state detention\n# en: Detention\nname [49189b] = 禁闭\n# en: Your afternoons and evenings belong to a prefect, a bucket and a long list of chores.\ndesc [51150d] = 你的下午和傍晚都归一个级长、一只水桶和一长串杂活所有。\n\n@state probation\n# en: Academic Probation\nname [e41d9e] = 留校察看\n# en: Your marks are below the line. Two terms under a 1.0 and the Disciplinary Board sits. A GPA of 2.0 clears it.\ndesc [9f9702] = 你的成绩跌破了底线。连续两个学期GPA 低于 1.0，纪律委员会就要开会了。GPA 回到 2.0 即可解除。\n# en: {~Probation sits on your shoulder through every lecture.|Your name is on a list you never wanted to be on.}\ntick.say.1 [95cc4f] = {~每节课上，察看处分都压在你肩头。|你的名字上了一张你从不想上的名单。}\n\n@state hardship_grant\n# en: Hardship Grant\nname [db03a0] = 困难补助\n# en: The bursar's hardship fund pays 8 crowns a month for a term. The paperwork asked how poor you were, in detail.\ndesc [b1f685] = 财务官的困难基金每月发 8 克朗，为期一个学期。申请表把你有多穷问得清清楚楚。\n# en: {~The hardship grant arrives in a brown envelope with your name spelled wrong.|Eight crowns from the fund, and a form to say you received them.}\ntick.say.1 [6386b4] = {~困难补助装在一个牛皮纸信封里送来，你的名字拼错了。|基金寄来八克朗，外加一张让你签收的表格。}\n\n@state bursar_loan\n# en: Bursar's Loan\nname [9b1ce0] = 财务官的贷款\n# en: The academy lent you sixty crowns. It takes 8 a month when you can pay, seven times, then a last 4, with no interest and no collectors: only a very patient ledger.\ndesc [2599b5] = 学院借给你六十克朗。等你付得起时每月还 8 克朗，共七次，最后一次还 4 克朗，不收利息，也没有催债人：只有一本非常有耐心的账簿。\n# en: {~No loan payment this month. The bursar notes it without comment, which is its own comment.|The loan waits. The ledger is patient; you are less so.}\ntick.say.1 [863378] = {~这个月没还贷款。财务官记下了，一言不发，这本身就是一种表态。|贷款在等着。账簿很有耐心，你就没那么有耐心了。}\n# en: The bursar's loan is paid off. She stamps the ledger with something like a smile.\ntick.say.2 [f6df86] = 财务官的贷款还清了。她在账簿上盖了个章，脸上的表情近乎微笑。\n\n@state in_debt\n# en: Debt to Grisk\nname [d38823] = 欠格里斯克的债\n# en: The goblin moneylender takes 12 crowns a month until the debt is cleared. Ignore a missed payment and he adds another.\ndesc [37f244] = 地精放贷人每月拿走 12 克朗，直到债务还清。漏还一笔却置之不理，他就再加一个月。\n\n@state famous\n# en: Talk of the Academy\nname [a60529] = 学院热议人物\n# en: Strangers know your name. Some of them even say it nicely.\ndesc [e6822e] = 陌生人都知道你的名字。其中一些人提起时甚至还挺客气。\n\n@state hiccup_hex\n# en: Hiccup Hex\nname [c18908] = 打嗝恶咒\n# en: Every hiccup releases a small, harmless, extremely embarrassing firework.\ndesc [14430d] = 每打一个嗝，就会冒出一朵小小的、无害的、极其丢人的烟花。\n\n@state blessed\n# en: Maud's Blessing\nname [d2410d] = 莫德的祝福\n# en: A sprig of rosemary in your pocket that never wilts.\ndesc [7af4e4] = 口袋里一枝永不枯萎的迷迭香。\n\n@state familiar_fox\n# en: Familiar: Ember\nname [7343af] = 魔宠：余烬\n# en: A fox-spirit with a smouldering tail who has decided you are hers.\ndesc [8b3cfa] = 一只尾巴冒着余火的狐灵，她已经认定你是她的人了。\n\n@state familiar_owl\n# en: Familiar: Clockwork Owl\nname [e993d5] = 魔宠：发条猫头鹰\n# en: Mir's gift. It hoots the hour, mostly correctly, and remembers your homework.\ndesc [863cd5] = 米尔的礼物。它会报时（大多准确），还会记得你的作业。\n\n@state training\n# en: Tournament Training\nname [5aecec] = 锦标赛特训\n# en: Dawn drills on the dueling green.\ndesc [ba8ab4] = 在决斗草坪上的黎明操练。\n\n@state wakeful\n# en: Draught of Wakefulness\nname [e9272f] = 清醒药剂\n# en: You haven't blinked in eleven hours. Your notes are immaculate.\ndesc [8764b7] = 你已经十一个小时没眨眼了。笔记无可挑剔。\n\n@state haunted\n# en: Haunted Dormitory\nname [42efc1] = 闹鬼的宿舍\n# en: Something rearranges your socks at night.\ndesc [88b052] = 夜里有什么东西在重新整理你的袜子。\n\n@state watched\n# en: Under Bram's Eye\nname [53b10a] = 被布拉姆盯上\n# en: The prefect is watching you. Specifically, and with relish.\ndesc [03458f] = 那位级长正盯着你。专门盯着，而且乐在其中。\n\n@state umbral_curse\n# en: Umbral Curse\nname [11a418] = 暗影诅咒\n# en: A cold that lives under your skin. Something in the restricted section followed you home.\ndesc [3ffb2e] = 一股寒意住在你的皮肤下。禁书区里有什么东西跟着你回来了。\n\n@state hollow_dreams\n# en: Hollow Dreams\nname [b977da] = 空洞之梦\n# en: Every night: a black star, a door beneath the lake, a voice that knows your name.\ndesc [b0df12] = 每一夜：一颗黑色的星，一扇湖底之门，一个知道你名字的声音。\n\n@state star_mark\n# en: The Star-Mark\nname [b7b256] = 星痕\n# en: A pale, star-shaped mark on your palm that hums near old magic.\ndesc [c1678d] = 掌心一枚苍白的星形印记，靠近古老魔法时会嗡嗡作响。\n\n@state star_crowned\n# en: The Star's Crown\nname [0aeb75] = 星之冠\n# en: The star on your palm has grown a crown of points. Spells come at a thought, and a voice at the back of your mind agrees with you a little too often.\ndesc [3a1131] = 你掌心的星痕长出了一圈冠冕般的尖角。咒语心念一动便成，脑海深处有个声音，总是过分地赞同你。\n\n@state star_taste\n# en: A Taste of the Star\nname [751069] = 星辰的滋味\n# en: Since the man in grey showed you, everything comes easier, and every night you dream of cold water.\ndesc [87451c] = 自从灰衣人让你看过之后，一切都变得容易，而每天夜里你都梦见冰冷的水。\n# en: {~Spells come easier, and the dreams come colder.|You wake with frost on the inside of the window, and the day's work goes like a song.}\ntick.say.1 [477cc7] = {~咒语来得更容易了，梦也更冷了。|你醒来时窗户内侧结了霜，而这一天的功课顺得像一首歌。}\n\n@state grey_watching\n# en: He Remembers\nname [a9b41a] = 他记得\n# en: You told the man in grey to get out. Candles burn blue when you're alone in the library, and you've learned to look over your shoulder.\ndesc [e4400f] = 你叫灰衣人滚出去。你独自待在图书馆时，蜡烛会烧成蓝色，你也学会了时不时回头看一眼。\n\n@state summer_away\n# en: Summer Away\nname [77bf0e] = 离校度暑\n# en: Away from the academy for the summer recess. No rent, no classes.\ndesc [abac1f] = 暑假期间离开学院。不交房租，不用上课。\n\n@state summer_work\n# en: Summer Work\nname [2d8ae9] = 暑期打工\n# en: A summer of honest (and occasionally dishonest) labour.\ndesc [4dd36c] = 一个诚实（偶尔不太诚实）劳动的夏天。\n\n@state frog_voice\n# en: Croaking\nname [5daa73] = 呱呱叫\n# en: A botched transfiguration left you with the voice of a bullfrog.\ndesc [02c975] = 一次失败的变形术，让你的嗓音变成了牛蛙。\n\n@state garden_plot\n# en: Garden Plot\nname [7ba473] = 一方菜园\n# en: A little square of earth behind the greenhouses that is yours. Things grow; so do you.\ndesc [3a1dd5] = 温室后面一小块属于你的泥土。东西在长，你也在长。\n\n@state pen_pal\n# en: Pen Pal in Thornwick\nname [7e85f7] = 荆木学院的笔友\n# en: A student at the rival academy who writes you long, funny letters full of terrible gossip.\ndesc [b0b239] = 对手学院的一名学生，常给你写又长又好笑、满是糟糕八卦的信。\n\n@state frost_ward\n# en: Ilse's Frost-Ward\nname [302a95] = 伊尔泽的霜之护符\n# en: A sliver of Northern winter that Ilse sewed into your collar. Cold things think twice about you.\ndesc [ca24da] = 伊尔泽缝进你衣领里的一缕北境寒冬。冰冷的东西靠近你之前都会犹豫一下。\n\n@state plan_castle\n# en: Break: at the castle\nname [961ce0] = 假期：留在城堡\n# en: A skeleton crew, empty corridors and secrets. Board costs 6 crowns a month.\ndesc [c6616d] = 只剩少数留守的职员、空荡的走廊和秘密。每月食宿 6 克朗。\n\n@state plan_home\n# en: Break: at home\nname [5a492e] = 假期：回家\n# en: Family, old friends and free board. The days are slower here.\ndesc [8e2284] = 家人、老朋友和免费食宿。这里的日子过得慢些。\n\n@state gig_grounds\n# en: Holiday gig: the grounds\nname [1908bb] = 假期打工：庭园\n# en: Working the grounds for Old Maud this break. Good pay, sore hands.\ndesc [c0cf6b] = 这个假期跟老莫德打理庭园。工钱不错，手会酸。\n\n@state gig_kitchens\n# en: Holiday gig: the kitchens\nname [78fcf0] = 假期打工：后厨\n# en: Working the Refectory kitchens this break. Good pay, short temper.\ndesc [b6d78e] = 这个假期在膳堂后厨帮工。工钱不错，脾气会变短。\n\n@state gig_library\n# en: Holiday gig: the stocktake\nname [4d1632] = 假期零工：图书盘点\n# en: The library's holiday stocktake, for its senior clerks. Every book counted, every bite noted. Better pay than the baseline gigs.\ndesc [ffcda9] = 图书馆的假期盘点，给资深馆员的活。每本书都要数，每次被咬都要记。比基础零工报酬更高。\n\n@state gig_newt\n# en: Holiday gig: the Newt's holiday rush\nname [d06f0d] = 假期零工：蝾螈酒馆的节日高峰\n# en: Running the Gilded Newt through the holiday rush, for its managers. The best tips of the year, and the longest nights.\ndesc [1bfad0] = 替镀金蝾螈酒馆撑过节日高峰，给经理的活。一年里最好的小费，也是最长的夜。\n\n@state gig_pell\n# en: Holiday gig: the remedy rush\nname [cbd921] = 假期零工：药剂旺季\n# en: Mixing the season's remedies at Pell & Daughters, for its assistant apothecaries. Good pay, and the village depends on it.\ndesc [13afb3] = 在佩尔父女药铺调配应季药剂，给助理药师的活。报酬不错，全村都指望着你。\n\n@state gig_post\n# en: Holiday gig: the holiday post\nname [715a4f] = 假期零工：节日邮件\n# en: Flying the holiday post for the courier loft, for its chief couriers. Every parcel in the valley, and the weather against you.\ndesc [efbb90] = 替信使阁楼飞送节日邮件，给首席信使的活。山谷里的每一个包裹，还有跟你作对的天气。\n\n@state plan_work\n# en: Break: working full-time\nname [3237e0] = 假期：全职打工\n# en: Working on campus through the break: a holiday gig, and board as usual. Tiring.\ndesc [91b8eb] = 整个假期在校内打工：一份假期零工，食宿照常付。很累。\n\n@state plan_internship\n# en: Break: internship\nname [4871d8] = 假期：实习\n# en: A research placement away from the castle, with a stipend and a supervisor.\ndesc [f66cf3] = 离开城堡的研究实习，有津贴，也有导师。\n\n@state locked_out\n# en: Confined After Dark\nname [3f39dd] = 入夜禁足\n# en: Caught once too often. Your door is checked at midnight, and the night is closed to you.\ndesc [a2bb11] = 被抓得太多次了。午夜有人查你的门，夜晚对你关上了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/states/tonics.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/states/tonics.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@state courage_blend\n# en: Courage Blend\nname [cf28e0] = 勇气茶\n# en: Mabel's courage blend: ginger, honey and something she won't name. Asking for things and getting up in front of people come a little easier.\ndesc [8fa344] = 梅布尔的勇气茶：姜、蜂蜜，还有一样她不肯说的东西。开口求人、当众登台，都会容易一点。\n\n@state calm_blend\n# en: Calm Blend\nname [2539d9] = 安神茶\n# en: Chamomile, lavender and a pinch of moonwort, in a tin Mabel labelled \"for nerves\". Exam halls and frightened animals both seem quieter.\ndesc [e8cc6f] = 洋甘菊、薰衣草和一小撮月草，装在梅布尔贴着“安神用”的罐子里。考场和受惊的动物，好像都安静了些。\n\n@state steadying_draught\n# en: Steadying Draught\nname [730eb4] = 定神药剂\n# en: Pell's steadying draught, measured to the drop. Your hands stop shaking over the fine work, and your eyes stop skipping lines.\ndesc [2629a4] = 佩尔的定神药剂，一滴不差。做精细活时手不再抖，读书时眼睛也不再跳行。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/ui.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) interface strings.\n# Keys come from UI_EN in src/i18n.js. Keep {placeholders}; \"_html\" keys may contain <b> tags.\n# pronoun.* keys fill {they} {them} {their} {theirs} {are} {s} in content text (empty is allowed).\n\n@ui\n# en: English\nlang.name = 简体中文\n# en: Your name\ntitle.yourName = 你的名字\n# en: Seed (optional)\ntitle.seed = 随机种子（可选）\n# en: random\ntitle.seedPlaceholder = 随机\n# en: Begin your first year\ntitle.begin = 开始你的第一学年\n# en: Continue · {info}\ntitle.continue = 继续 · {info}\n# en: {name} — {month}, Year {year}\ntitle.saveInfo = {name} — 第 {year} 年{month}\n# en: <b>Swipe</b> each card left or right (or press ← / →) to choose. Some moments open a <b>menu</b> with more options. Keep your <b>Health, Mood and Crowns</b> above zero for four years — 48 months — and see who you become. Glowing dots above a meter show what a choice will touch.\ntitle.howto_html = 左右<b>滑动</b>卡牌（或按 ← / →）做出选择。某些时刻会弹出<b>菜单</b>，提供更多选项。在四年——共 48 个月——里，让你的<b>健康、心情和金币</b>都保持在零以上，看看你会成为怎样的人。属性上方亮起的光点，提示这个选择会影响什么。\n# en: Elowen, Isla, Maren, Briony, Clemency, Delphine, Eira, Fenella, Greer, Hesper, Imogen, Iona, Linnea, Mae, Morwenna, Sabine, Philippa, Rosalind, Saoirse, Tegan, Wilhelmina, Yseult, Aveline, Beatrix, Cordelia, Edith, Esme, Flora, Gwendolyn, Hazel, Ivy, Lettice, Lilias, Mirabel, Nell, Opal, Petra, Rhiannon, Rosamund, Thea, Verity, Winifred, Agnes, Dulcie, Effie, Honor, Leda, Clover, Bettany, Sidony\ntitle.names.she = 埃洛温, 艾拉, 玛伦, 布里奥妮, 克莱门西, 德尔菲娜, 艾拉妮, 费内拉, 格丽尔, 赫丝珀, 伊莫金, 艾奥娜, 林奈娅, 梅伊, 莫温娜, 萨宾, 菲利帕, 罗莎琳德, 瑟莎, 蒂根, 威廉明娜, 伊瑟特, 艾芙琳, 比阿特丽克丝, 科迪莉亚, 伊迪丝, 埃丝梅, 弗洛拉, 格温德琳, 黑兹尔, 艾薇, 莱蒂丝, 莉莉亚斯, 米拉贝尔, 内尔, 奥帕尔, 佩特拉, 里安农, 罗莎蒙德, 西娅, 维里蒂, 温妮弗雷德, 艾格尼丝, 达尔茜, 埃菲, 霍诺尔, 丽达, 克洛弗, 贝塔妮, 西多妮\n# en: Theo, Ezra, Alaric, Ambrose, Barnaby, Caspian, Cedric, Cormac, Crispin, Declan, Rupert, Emrys, Evander, Felix, Gideon, Godric, Hamish, Ignatius, Jasper, Lachlan, Lucan, Magnus, Malachy, Merrick, Oisin, Osric, Percival, Peregrine, Piers, Quentin, Rafferty, Roderick, Rufus, Silas, Tristan, Ulric, Valentine, Wystan, Augustin, Desmond, Fergus, Gareth, Leopold, Matthias, Niall, Anselm, Benedict, Hugo, Lorcan, Dorian\ntitle.names.he = 西奥, 以斯拉, 阿拉里克, 安布罗斯, 巴纳比, 卡斯皮安, 塞德里克, 科马克, 克里斯平, 德克兰, 鲁珀特, 埃姆里斯, 埃文德, 菲利克斯, 基甸, 戈德里克, 哈米什, 伊格内修斯, 贾斯珀, 拉克兰, 卢坎, 马格努斯, 马拉奇, 梅里克, 奥辛, 奥斯里克, 珀西瓦尔, 佩里格林, 皮尔斯, 昆汀, 拉弗蒂, 罗德里克, 鲁弗斯, 赛拉斯, 特里斯坦, 乌尔里克, 瓦伦丁, 威斯坦, 奥古斯丁, 德斯蒙德, 弗格斯, 加雷斯, 利奥波德, 马蒂亚斯, 尼尔, 安塞尔姆, 本尼迪克特, 雨果, 洛坎, 多里安\n# en: Wren, Ash, Rowan, Sable, Marlo, Juniper, Avery, Bryn, Ellis, Emery, Finch, Indigo, Jude, Kit, Linden, Morgan, Quinn, Robin, Sage, Shay, Sparrow, Tarn, Vale, Wynn, Arden, Blair, Briar, Cass, Darcy, Aubrey, Lark, Sorrel, Teagan, Rumer, Perry, Ainsley, Merritt, Remy, Tansy, Reese\ntitle.names.they = 雷恩, 艾什, 罗文, 塞布尔, 马洛, 朱妮珀, 艾弗里, 布林, 埃利斯, 埃默里, 芬奇, 因迪戈, 裘德, 基特, 林登, 摩根, 奎因, 罗宾, 塞奇, 谢伊, 斯帕罗, 塔恩, 维尔, 温恩, 阿登, 布莱尔, 布赖尔, 卡斯, 达西, 奥布里, 拉克, 索雷尔, 提根, 鲁默, 佩里, 安斯利, 梅里特, 雷米, 坦西, 里斯\n# en: Adaeze, Amaya, Anahí, Chiyo, Esi, Farida, Haeun, Imani, Itzel, Leilani, Mei, Mirela, Parisa, Priya, Saanvi, Sakura, Shirin, Sigrún, Solveig, Tala, Thandiwe, Yara, Zainab, Zarina, Ximena, Aigerim, Nilufar, Ngaio, Aroha, Malia\ntitle.names.world_she = 阿达埃泽, 阿玛娅, 阿娜希, 千代, 埃西, 法丽达, 夏恩, 伊玛尼, 伊采尔, 蕾拉妮, 美, 米蕾拉, 帕丽莎, 普丽娅, 桑薇, 樱, 希琳, 西格伦, 索尔薇格, 塔拉, 坦迪薇, 雅拉, 扎伊娜布, 扎丽娜, 希梅娜, 艾格琳, 尼卢法尔, 恩盖奥, 阿萝哈, 玛莉亚\n# en: Arjun, Bao, Chidi, Dawit, Emeka, Haruto, Ilyas, Inti, Kwame, Kofi, Mateo, Nikolai, Oren, Rustam, Santiago, Tariq, Yusuf, Zoltan, Ruslan, Ikaika, Kaito, Anwar, Bayani, Matiu, Temir, Hemi, Bohdan, Farhan, Jun, Thabo\ntitle.names.world_he = 阿琼, 宝, 奇迪, 达维特, 埃梅卡, 阳翔, 伊利亚斯, 印蒂, 夸梅, 科菲, 马特奥, 尼古拉, 奥伦, 鲁斯塔姆, 圣地亚哥, 塔里克, 优素福, 佐尔坦, 鲁斯兰, 伊凯卡, 海斗, 安瓦尔, 巴亚尼, 马蒂乌, 铁木尔, 赫米, 博赫丹, 法尔汉, 俊, 塔博\n# en: Akira, Hikaru, Rei, Yuki, Noor, Kalani, Tenzin, Ayodele, Nima, Dara, Lumi, Rumi, Sami, Amani, Chidera, Eun, Sasha, Ariya, Sefa, Ola\ntitle.names.world_they = 晶, 光, 玲, 雪, 努尔, 卡拉妮, 丹增, 阿约德勒, 尼玛, 达拉, 卢米, 鲁米, 萨米, 阿玛尼, 奇德拉, 恩, 萨沙, 阿丽雅, 塞法, 奥拉\n# en: Random name\ntitle.randomName = 随机名字\n# en: Pronouns\ntitle.pronouns = 代词\n# en: she/her\ntitle.pronoun.she = 她\n# en: he/him\ntitle.pronoun.he = 他\n# en: they/them\ntitle.pronoun.they = TA\n# en: Wren\ntitle.defaultName = 雷恩\n# en: Very much a work in progress: content, balance and art are all still changing, and saves from older versions may stop loading. Tell us what you think with the feedback bar in the lower-left corner while you play: rate a card 👍 or 👎, or press F (✎) to comment on a card or on the game in general.\ntitle.wip = 本作仍处于非常早期的开发阶段：内容、平衡与美术都还在不断变化，旧版本的存档可能无法继续读取。游戏中请用左下角的反馈栏告诉我们你的想法：为卡牌点 👍 或 👎，或按 F（✎）对某张卡牌或整个游戏留言。\n# en: Character & people\ntop.people = 角色与人物\n# en: Save & return to title\ntop.quit = 保存并返回标题\n# en: Map\ntop.map = 地图\n# en: Tap a place to see what it is.\nmap.howToLook = 点一个地点，看看那是什么地方。\n# en: Close\nmap.close = 关闭\n# en: Home\nregion.home = 老家\n# en: The castle\nregion.castle = 城堡\n# en: The grounds\nregion.grounds = 校园外场\n# en: The village\nregion.village = 山下小镇\n# en: The wilds\nregion.wilds = 荒野\n# en: On the road\nregion.travel = 旅途中\n# en: Internship\nregion.internship = 实习\n# en: Language\ntop.lang = 语言\n# en: YEAR {year} · MONTH {turn} OF {max}\ndate.sub = 第 {year} 年 · 第 {turn} / {max} 月\n# en: You\ntab.you = 你\n# en: Kit\ntab.kit = 行装\n# en: One wand, one robe and one trinket. What you wear matters only in fights; what you own opens ways through cards. Tap an item to wear it.\nkit.intro = 一根魔杖、一件长袍、一件饰物。穿戴的东西只在战斗中起作用；拥有的东西能在卡牌里开出新路。点一件物品即可穿戴。\n# en: Your kit is locked until the fight is over.\nkit.locked = 战斗结束前，行装无法更换。\n# en: Wand\nkit.slot.wand = 魔杖\n# en: Robe\nkit.slot.robe = 长袍\n# en: Trinket\nkit.slot.trinket = 饰物\n# en: Nothing yet.\nkit.none = 还没有。\n# en: Wear\nkit.wear = 穿戴\n# en: Wearing\nkit.wearing = 已穿戴\n# en: In fights: {what}\nkit.inFights = 战斗中：{what}\n# en: Health +{n}\nkit.hp = 生命 +{n}\n# en: Mana +{n}\nkit.mana = 法力 +{n}\n# en: {spell} costs {n} less Mana\nkit.cost = {spell}少耗 {n} 点法力\n# en: each {spell} cast also deals {n} spell damage\nkit.onCast = 每次施放{spell}，额外造成 {n} 点法术伤害\n# en: a foe who guards, wards or braces takes {n} damage\nkit.onDefend = 对手格挡、护界或坚守时受到 {n} 点伤害\n# en: {n} less damage from {from}\nkit.taken = 受到{from}的伤害减少 {n} 点\n# en: forbidden spells\nkit.group.forbidden = 禁咒\n# en: umbral spells\nkit.group.umbral = 幽影咒语\n# en: hidden spells\nkit.group.hidden = 秘传咒语\n# en: creature moves\nkit.group.creature = 生物招式\n# en: creatures\nkit.group.nonhuman = 生物\n# en: any spell\nkit.group.any = 任何咒语\n# en: Use · {item}\nitem.button = 使用 · {item}\n# en: New in your kit: {item}\nnote.itemGained = 行装新增：{item}\n# en: New in your kit, and worn: {item}\nnote.itemWorn = 行装新增，并已穿戴：{item}\n# en: Gone from your kit: {item}\nnote.itemLost = 行装中失去：{item}\n# en: People\ntab.people = 人物\n# en: Clues\ntab.clues = 线索\n# en: Nothing to tell yet. The stories you live, and the odd things you notice, gather here.\nside.noClues = 还没有什么可讲的。你经历的故事、你注意到的怪事，都会汇集在这里。\n# en: Followed up\njournal.finished = 已追查\n# en: Journal\ntab.journal = 日志\n# en: Conditions & circumstances\nside.conditions = 状态与境况\n# en: Nothing yet.\nside.nothing = 暂时还没有。\n# en: Close\nside.close = 关闭\n# en: {n} mo\nside.monthsLeft = 剩 {n} 月\n# en: You haven't really met anyone yet.\nside.noPeople = 你还没真正认识谁。\n# en: Relationship {n}\nside.relationship = 关系 {n}\n# en: Spark {n}\nside.romance = 心动 {n}\n# en: Spells\nside.spells = 咒语\n# en: {n} mana\nside.spellCost = {n} 法力\n# en: Scales with {skill}\nspell.scales = 随{skill}增强\n# en: Doesn’t scale with a skill\nspell.flat = 不随技能增强\n# en: Your story is yet unwritten.\nside.emptyJournal = 你的故事尚未落笔。\n# en: {month} · Y{year}\njournal.when = 第 {year} 年 · {month}\n# en: Act {n} of {of} · {name}\njournal.act = 第 {n} 幕（共 {of} 幕）· {name}\n# en: Clues\njournal.clues = 线索\n# en: Leads\njournal.leads = 头绪\n# en: followed\njournal.followed = 已追查\n# en: a key clue: needed beneath the lake\njournal.keyClue = 关键线索：湖底用得上\n# en: The main story\njournal.main = 主线\n# en: Stories\njournal.stories = 故事\n# en: Leads to follow\njournal.openLeads = 待追查的头绪\n# en: Diary\njournal.diary = 日记\n# en: ongoing\njournal.status.ongoing = 进行中\n# en: resolved\njournal.status.resolved = 已了结\n# en: let go\njournal.status.let_go = 已放下\n# en: missed\njournal.status.missed = 已错过\n# en: over\njournal.status.closed = 已落幕\n# en: Known to all\nfacts.surface = 众所周知\n# en: Between you\nfacts.personal = 你们之间\n# en: Secrets\nfacts.secret = 秘密\n# en: · still to hear: {n} ·\nfacts.locked.surface = · 还有 {n} 件事待听说 ·\n# en: · still to learn: {n} ·\nfacts.locked.personal = · 还有 {n} 件事待了解 ·\n# en: · still hidden: {n} ·\nfacts.locked.secret = · 还有 {n} 个秘密 ·\n# en: {n}/{of}\nfacts.count = {n}/{of}\n# en: What you know of them\nfacts.countTip = 你对TA的了解\n# en: Something new you know about them\nfacts.new = 你对TA有了新的了解\n# en: Gets on with {name}\npeople.likes = 和{name}合得来\n# en: Can't stand {name}\npeople.dislikes = 受不了{name}\n# en: {bond} since {month}, Y{year}\npeople.since = 第 {year} 年{month}起成为{bond}\n# en: {name}: {tier}\nnote.tier = {name}：{tier}\n# en: friend\nbond.friend = 朋友\n# en: close\nbond.close = 挚友\n# en: rival\nbond.rival = 对手\n# en: nemesis\nbond.nemesis = 宿敌\n# en: partner\nbond.partner = 恋人\n# en: ex\nbond.ex = 前任\n# en: Class\ntag.class = 课程\n# en: Club\ntag.club = 社团\n# en: Work\ntag.job = 打工\n# en: Social\ntag.social = 社交\n# en: Romance\ntag.romance = 恋爱\n# en: Study\ntag.study = 学业\n# en: Mystery\ntag.mystery = 谜团\n# en: Danger\ntag.danger = 危险\n# en: Crisis\ntag.crisis = 危机\n# en: Festival\ntag.festival = 节庆\n# en: Coin\ntag.money = 钱财\n# en: Grounds\ntag.nature = 校园\n# en: Home\ntag.home = 日常\n# en: Rivalry\ntag.rival = 较量\n# en: Exam\ntag.exam = 考试\n# en: Chronicle\ntag.story = 编年\n# en: Magic\ntag.magic = 魔法\n# en: Academy life\ntag.default = 学院生活\n# en: consequence\ncard.consequence = 后果\n# en: Not available\ncard.notAvailable = 无法选择\n# en: Not enough crowns ({cost} needed)\ncard.cantAfford = 克朗不够（需要 {cost}）\n# en: {skill}\ncheck.button = {skill}\n# en: max\nchip.max = 已满\n# en: {name} {delta}\nchip.rel = {name} {delta}\n# en: {track} performance {delta}\nchip.perf = {track}表现 {delta}\n# en: {track} progress {delta}\nchip.progress = {track}进度 {delta}\n# en: {track} study {delta}\nchip.study = {track}备考 {delta}\n# en: Exam preparation, {n} courses\nchip.studyAll = 备考（{n}门课）\n# en: {name} (spark) {delta}\nchip.love = {name}（心动）{delta}\n# en: You met {name}.\nnote.meet = 你认识了{name}。\n# en: {name}: {status}\nnote.bond = {name}：{status}\n# en: + {state}\nnote.gain = + {state}\n# en: − {state}\nnote.lose = − {state}\n# en: New spell: {spell} {level}\nnote.spell = 新咒语：{spell} {level}\n# en: New spell: {spell} {level} ({bonus})\nnote.spellBonus = 新咒语：{spell} {level}（{bonus}）\n# en: You learned something about {name}.\nnote.fact = 你对{name}多了一分了解。\n# en: {kind} ({p}%) · {result}\ncheck.challengeChip = {kind}（{p}%）· {result}\n# en: Combat · {p}% · −{cost} mana\ncheck.combatButton = 战斗 · {p}% · 法力 −{cost}\n# en: Combat\nchallenge.combat = 战斗\n# en: Tests {skill}\ncourse.tests = 考查{skill}\n# en: {tier} exam, {p}% pass today\ncourse.exam = {tier}考试，按现在算及格率 {p}%\n# en: teaches {spells}\ncourse.teaches = 教授{spells}\n# en: About {gold} crowns a day · {n} days of work a term\njob.outlook = 每天约 {gold} 克朗 · 每学期工作 {n} 天\n# en: Leaves {from}: your {rank} rank and {perf} performance; you start here as new\ntrack.switch = 将离开{from}：你的{rank}职位和 {perf} 表现；在这里从新人做起\n# en: Easy\ntier.easy = 简单\n# en: Standard\ntier.standard = 标准\n# en: Medium\ntier.medium = 中等\n# en: Hard\ntier.hard = 困难\n# en: Heroic\ntier.heroic = 英雄级\n# en: Duel\nchallenge.duel = 决斗\n# en: Negotiation\nchallenge.negotiate = 交涉\n# en: Deception\nchallenge.deceive = 欺瞒\n# en: Exam\nchallenge.exam = 考试\n# en: Research\nchallenge.research = 研究\n# en: Brewing\nchallenge.brew = 调制\n# en: Enchanting\nchallenge.enchant = 附魔\n# en: Feat\nchallenge.feat = 体能\n# en: Spellwork\nchallenge.spell = 施法\n# en: win\nchallenge.win = 胜\n# en: partial\nchallenge.partial = 小胜\n# en: lose\nchallenge.lose = 负\n# en: {spell} {level} · {cost} mana\nspell.button = {spell} {level} · {cost} 法力\n# en: Not enough mana\nspell.short = 法力不足\n# en: More follows later\npreview.followUp = 之后还有后续\n# en: This will be remembered\npreview.remembered = 这会被记住\n# en: Your story ends here\npreview.ends = 你的故事将在此结束\n# en: Your saved game was from an older version of the game and can no longer be loaded. Its run log was kept.\ntitle.oldSave = 你的存档来自旧版本游戏，已无法读取。它的游戏记录已被保留。\n# en: From an older version; can’t be loaded\nslots.tooOld = 旧版本存档，无法读取\n# en: tap or swipe to continue\noutcome.tap = 轻点或滑动继续\n# en: Continue\noutcome.continue = 继续\n# en: Year {year} · Month {turn} of {max}\nmonth.header = 第 {year} 年 · 第 {turn} / {max} 月\n# en: This month\nmonth.thisMonth = 本月\n# en: Fading sparks\nmonth.fading = 渐淡的火花\n# en: No romantic moment last month: the spark with {names} cools a little.\nmonth.fadingSay = 上个月没有浪漫时刻：你与{names}之间的火花淡了一些。\n# en: Apart for now, the spark with {names} cools, more slowly than it would on campus.\nmonth.fadingAway = 暂时分隔两地，你与{names}之间的火花在变淡，只是比在校时慢些。\n# en: The spark with {name} has gone out, and the partnership with it.\nmonth.partnerEnded = 你与{name}之间的火花熄灭了，恋人关系也随之结束。\n# en: {a} and {b}\nlist.and = {a}和{b}\n# en: , \nlist.sep = 、\n# en: Begin {month}\nmonth.begin = 开始{month}\n# en: Continue\nmonth.continue = 继续\n# en: The leaves turn. A new term begins.\nseason.1 = 落叶纷飞，新学期开始了。\n# en: Lanterns over the lake.\nseason.2 = 灯笼飘过湖面。\n# en: Fog creeps up the stairwells.\nseason.3 = 雾气爬上了楼梯间。\n# en: Frost on the windows; exams in the air.\nseason.4 = 窗上结霜，考试的气息弥漫开来。\n# en: Deep winter. The Midwinter Ball approaches.\nseason.5 = 隆冬时节。仲冬舞会近了。\n# en: The thaw, and a new term.\nseason.6 = 冰雪消融，新学期又至。\n# en: Storms roll in off the mountains.\nseason.7 = 风暴从山那边滚滚而来。\n# en: The lawns are green again. Tournament season.\nseason.8 = 草坪又绿了。锦标赛的季节到了。\n# en: Blossom on every bough.\nseason.9 = 枝头开满了花。\n# en: High summer heat — and the Grand Examinations.\nseason.10 = 盛夏酷暑——还有年度大考。\n# en: Summer recess. The towers empty.\nseason.11 = 暑假到了。塔楼空了下来。\n# en: The last weeks of summer. Stars begin to fall.\nseason.12 = 夏天的最后几周。星星开始坠落。\n# en: Your story ends early · {month}, Year {year}\nending.early = 你的故事提前落幕 · 第 {year} 年{month}\n# en: Graduation · Ending\nending.graduation = 毕业 · 结局\n# en: The people who mattered\nending.people = 那些重要的人\n# en: Begin anew\nending.again = 重新开始\n# en: Read your journal\nending.journal = 翻阅你的日志\n# en: Back to the ending\nending.back = 回到结局\n# en: fond\nending.fond = 亲近\n# en: cold\nending.cold = 冷淡\n# en: Export this run\nending.export = 导出本局记录\n# en: The Hollow Star\nending.slot.mystery = 空洞之星\n# en: Bonds\nending.slot.relationships = 羁绊\n# en: Remembered as\nending.slot.personal = 人们记得你是\n# en: This card\nfb.this = 当前卡\n# en: Last card\nfb.last = 刚才那张\n# en: Previous\nfb.prev = 更早一张\n# en: Ending\nfb.ending = 结局\n# en: Good\nfb.good = 好\n# en: Bad\nfb.bad = 不好\n# en: Rate & comment (F)\nfb.open = 评分与评论（F）\n# en: Feedback\nfb.heading = 反馈\n# en: Rate cards and leave notes for the writers. Everything is saved with this run’s log.\nfb.intro = 为卡牌评分并给作者留言。所有内容都会随本局记录一起保存。\n# en: You chose: {choice}\nfb.chose = 你的选择：{choice}\n# en: What the card said\nfb.cardText = 卡牌原文\n# en: What worked, what didn’t? Tone, typos, balance, a confusing choice… or anything about the game in general.\nfb.placeholder = 哪里好，哪里不好？语气、错字、数值平衡、令人困惑的选项……或者关于整个游戏的任何想法。\n# en: Save\nfb.save = 保存\n# en: Cancel\nfb.cancel = 取消\n# en: Nothing to rate yet.\nfb.none = 还没有可以评分的内容。\n# en: {month} · Y{year}\nfb.when = 第 {year} 年 · {month}\n# en: Playtest log: {runs} runs · {notes} notes\nfb.log = 试玩记录：{runs} 局 · {notes} 条反馈\n# en: Export\nfb.export = 导出\n# en: Morning\nslot.morning = 清晨\n# en: Noon\nslot.noon = 正午\n# en: Afternoon\nslot.afternoon = 午后\n# en: Evening\nslot.evening = 傍晚\n# en: Night\nslot.night = 夜晚\n# en: Midnight\nslot.midnight = 午夜\n# en: Term\nphase.term = 学期中\n# en: Break\nphase.break = 假期\n# en: Wrap-up\nphase.wrapup = 收尾\n# en: {month} · Year {year} · {phase}\nmap.title = 第 {year} 年 · {month} · {phase}\n# en: Where will you spend the afternoon and the evening?\nmap.question2 = 这个下午和傍晚你要去哪儿？\n# en: Where will you spend the afternoon?\nmap.q.afternoon = 这个下午你要去哪儿？\n# en: Where will you spend the evening?\nmap.q.evening = 这个傍晚你要去哪儿？\n# en: Where will you go tonight?\nmap.q.midnight = 今晚你要去哪儿？\n# en: visit {n} of {of}\nmap.visitOf = 第 {n} / {of} 次拜访\n# en: Stay in\nmap.stayIn = 待在住处\n# en: Go to {place}\nmap.goTo = 前往{place}\n# en: Tap a place to pick it; tap it again to go.\nmap.howToPick = 点一个地点选中它；再点一次即出发。\n# en: Pick a place for each part of the day first.\nmap.notReady = 请先为一天中的每个时段选好地点。\n# en: Tap {place} again to go there, or press the button.\nmap.again = 再点一次{place}即可前往，或点下方按钮。\n# en: {event}: at {place}, this {slot}\nmap.festivalHere = {event}：这个{slot}在{place}\n# en: choose…\nmap.choose = 选择……\n# en: Begin the day\nmap.go = 开始这一天\n# en: List view\nmap.showList = 列表视图\n# en: Map view\nmap.showMap = 地图视图\n# en: Somewhere you haven’t found yet.\nmap.undiscovered = 一个你还没发现的地方。\n# en: Both visits are picked; tap one to change it.\nmap.full = 两次拜访都选好了；点其中一个可以更换。\n# en: {name} isn’t open then (open: {hours}).\nmap.closed = {name}那时不开放（开放时间：{hours}）。\n# en: closed now\nmap.closedNow = 现在关闭\n# en: open\nmap.open = 开放\n# en: not yet found\nmap.hinted = 尚未发现\n# en: festival this {slot}\nmap.festival = 这个{slot}有节庆\n# en: here: {names}\nmap.here = 在这里：{names}\n# en: something new\nmap.new = 有新鲜事\n# en: Work · {n} left\nmap.shiftsLeft = 工作 · 还剩 {n} 次\n# en: Work · done this term\nmap.shiftsDone = 工作 · 本学期已完成\n# en: visited today\nmap.visitedNow = 今天去过了\n# en: You’ve already been to {name} today.\nmap.visitedToday = 你今天已经去过{name}了。\n# en: getting ready for the festival this {slot}\nmap.festivalSoon = 正在为{slot}的节庆做准备\n# en: {name} is closed while it gets ready for the festival this {slot}.\nmap.festivalLater = {name}正在为{slot}的节庆做准备，暂不开放。\n# en: Session · {n} left\nmap.sessionsLeft = 课时 · 还剩 {n} 次\n# en: Confirm ({n}/{max})\nmenu.confirm = 确认（{n}/{max}）\n# en: Confirm\nmenu.confirmOne = 确认\n# en: Tap again to confirm\nmenu.again = 再点一次确认\n# en: {delta} next month\ndue.next = 下月 {delta}\n# en: Next month:\ndue.heading = 下个月：\n# en: Other\ndue.other = 其他\n# en: A choice\nmenu.tagline = 抉择\n# en: Make your choice\nmenu.lead = 做出选择\n# en: Choose up to {n}\nmenu.leadMany = 最多选 {n} 项\n# en: Choose one: pick it, then tap it again or confirm.\nmenu.prompt = 选一项：选中后再点一次，或点确认。\n# en: Choose up to {n}, then tap your last pick again or confirm.\nmenu.promptMany = 最多选 {n} 项，然后再点一次最后选的那项，或点确认。\n# en: Signs (+ ++ +++)\namounts.marks = 符号（+ ++ +++）\n# en: Numbers\namounts.numbers = 数字\n# en: Icons only\namounts.icons = 仅图标\n# en: Settings\nsettings.heading = 设置\n# en: Amounts in previews\nsettings.amounts = 预览中的数值\n# en: How a choice's effects show before you make it. Afterwards the outcome always shows the numbers; Crowns and Mana are always exact.\nsettings.amountsHint = 选择之前，效果如何显示。选择之后，结果总会显示具体数字；克朗和魔力始终显示确切数值。\n# en: Scene descriptions\nsettings.notes = 场景描写\n# en: A line in italics under each card that sets the scene.\nsettings.notesHint = 每张卡片下方的一行斜体文字，交代场景。\n# en: Show\nsettings.on = 显示\n# en: Hide\nsettings.off = 隐藏\n# en: Portrait art\nsettings.portraits = 头像画风\n# en: How the people you meet are drawn.\nsettings.portraitsHint = 你遇到的人物以何种风格绘制。\n# en: Minimal\nportraits.minimal = 简约\n# en: Original\nportraits.original = 原版\n# en: Motion\nsettings.motion = 动效\n# en: Animations and the moving sky. Reduced keeps the screen still, for anyone who finds motion uncomfortable.\nsettings.motionHint = 动画与流动的天空。“减少”会让画面保持静止，适合对动态效果感到不适的玩家。\n# en: Full\nmotion.full = 完整\n# en: Reduced\nmotion.reduced = 减少\n# en: Fights\nsettings.fight = 战斗\n# en: How your opponents choose their moves. It changes only their decisions, never the numbers: their level, Health, Mana and spells stay the same. Engine takes the best move it can find every turn; the lower settings play more naturally, drifting from the best move and leaning on the habits that fit who they are.\nsettings.fightHint = 对手如何选择招式。它只影响对手的决策，不改变任何数值：等级、生命、法力和法术都保持不变。“引擎”每回合都选它能找到的最佳招式；较低的难度出招更自然，会偏离最佳招式，更依赖符合其性格的习惯。\n# en: Engine analysis\nsettings.fightEngine = 引擎分析\n# en: Cheating: during a fight, a toggle shows the engine’s analysis of your turn: how good each move is, the best one, and the lines it expects.\nsettings.fightEngineHint = 作弊：战斗中可用开关显示引擎对你这一回合的分析：每个招式的优劣、最佳招式，以及它预计的走向。\n# en: Off\nsettings.engineOff = 关闭\n# en: On (cheating)\nsettings.engineOn = 开启（作弊）\n# en: Easy\ndifficulty.easy = 简单\n# en: Medium\ndifficulty.medium = 中等\n# en: Hard\ndifficulty.hard = 困难\n# en: Engine\ndifficulty.engine = 引擎\n# en: Free to use: WTFPL\ncredits.license = 可自由使用：WTFPL 许可\n# en: Source on GitHub\ncredits.source = GitHub 源代码\n# en: Report a problem\ncredits.issues = 反馈问题\n# en: Fight\ncheck.fight = 战斗\n# en: Fight · {foe} · level {level}\ncheck.fightButton = 战斗 · {foe} · {level} 级\n# en: Fight: {result} · {reason}\ncheck.fightChip = 战斗：{result} · {reason}\n# en: Negotiation · {skill}\ncheck.parleyButton = 交涉 · {skill}\n# en: Negotiation ({p}%) · {result}\ncheck.parleyChip = 交涉（{p}%）· {result}\n# en: Settled by charm, lore and guile until the negotiation game arrives.\ncheck.parleyStub = 交涉小游戏上线之前，由魅力、学识和诡计决定。\n# en: More options\ntitle.options = 更多选项\n# en: Skip the introductions (you know the academy already)\ntitle.skipIntros = 跳过入门介绍（你已经熟悉学院了）\n# en: Combat bench\ntitle.bench = 战斗试验台\n# en: Practice fights with any loadout, foe and level, and debug the combat rules\ntitle.benchHint = 用任意配装、对手和等级练习战斗，并调试战斗规则\n# en: The combat bench needs the game's server: run npm start and open the game from it.\ntitle.benchNoServer = 战斗试验台需要游戏服务器：运行 npm start，再从服务器打开游戏。\n# en: Introductions\nsettings.intros = 入门介绍\n# en: The cards that explain a system the first time you meet it. Off for players who know the academy. The orientation at the start is separate: the first card of a new game lets you skip it.\nsettings.introsHint = 第一次遇到某个系统时解释它的卡牌。熟悉学院的玩家可以关掉。开局的入学引导是分开的：新游戏的第一张卡就能让你跳过它。\n# en: Thorne:\nfight.coachTitle = 索恩教授：\n# en: Skip the lesson\nfight.skipLesson = 跳过这一课\n# en: Walk away\nfight.walkAway = 转身离开\n# en: Your kit\nfight.kit = 你的行装\n# en: Idle\nfight.idle = 原地不动\n# en: \"It's straw, and it won't do a thing this time. Hit it. Get its Health to nothing and you've won.\"\nfight.coach.idle = “它是草扎的，这回什么都不会做。打它。把它的生命打到零，你就赢了。”\n# en: \"See what it's committed to? You often will. It means to swing at you. Parry.\"\nfight.coach.strike = “看见它出的什么招了？你常常能看见。它要朝你挥过来。招架。”\n# en: \"Fire, this time. A Parry takes only half a spell; a Ward stops all of it, but only half a blow. Pick your shield for the hit that's coming.\"\nfight.coach.spell = “这回是火。招架只能挡下一半咒语；护界能挡下整道咒语，可冲击只能挡一半。看清要来的是什么，再选你的盾。”\n# en: \"It's hiding behind its parry. The Mana you spend turns to flux; Refocus turns it back. Do it when nothing can hurt you. Like now.\"\nfight.coach.guard = “它躲在招架后面。你花掉的法力会变成游离法力；凝神能把它变回来。挑没东西能伤到你的时候做。比如现在。”\n# en: \"Now it's refocusing, and while it does, it's open. Make it pay.\"\nfight.coach.reclaim = “现在它在凝神，凝神的时候它是敞开的。让它付出代价。”\n# en: \"From here you take turns. This time you commit first and it sees your move; next time you'll see its. Think what it will do with what it sees.\"\nfight.coach.commit = “从这里起轮流先出招。这回你先出，它能看见你的招；下回你能看见它的。想想它看见之后会怎么做。”\n# en: \"That's a duel. Ward its fire, hit it while it refocuses, and when it swings, trade blows: you can take more than it can. Finish it.\"\nfight.coach.finish = “这就是决斗。用护界挡它的火，趁它凝神时出手；它挥过来时，就跟它对换一击——你比它扛得住。收尾吧。”\n# en: The mannequin moves first and shows you its move: read it before you pick yours.\nfight.coach.lead = 木人先出招，并亮出它的招式：看清了再选你的。\n# en: Each button shows what it does and what it costs. Impulse is free; spells cost Mana.\nfight.coach.read = 每个按钮都写着效果和消耗。冲击不花钱；咒语要耗法力。\n# en: A ward stops a spell and halves a blow. Raise it when something is coming.\nfight.coach.ward = 护界能挡下一道咒语、减半一次冲击。有东西要来时就举起它。\n# en: Spent Mana becomes loose magic (the ↻ on your bar): Refocus gathers it back.\nfight.coach.mana = 花掉的法力会化作游离魔力（法力条上的 ↻）：凝神能把它收回来。\n# en: The fight is starting…\nfight.loading = 战斗即将开始……\n# en: Engine analysis\nfight.engine = 引擎分析\n# en: The engine’s evaluation for you, in Health\nfight.evalBar = 引擎对你局势的评估（以生命计）\n# en: Engine · depth {depth} · {nodes}k positions · {s} s\nfight.engineHead = 引擎 · 深度 {depth} · {nodes}k 个局面 · {s} 秒\n# en: searching deeper…\nfight.engineDeeper = 继续深入搜索……\n# en: Engine · thinking…\nfight.engineThinking = 引擎 · 思考中……\n# en: Engine · waits for your turn\nfight.engineWaits = 引擎 · 等待你的回合\n# en: Engine time per turn\nfight.engineTime = 引擎每回合用时\n# en: {n} s\nfight.seconds = {n} 秒\n# en: Values are in Health from your side: + favours you. Each line is both sides’ best play as the engine sees it: gold chips are your moves, blue chips your opponent’s, and | separates exchanges.\nfight.engineNote = 数值以生命计，从你的角度看：+ 对你有利。每一行是引擎眼中双方的最佳应对：金色是你的招式，蓝色是对手的，| 分隔每次交锋。\n# en: KO · you win\nfight.lineWin = 击倒 · 你获胜\n# en: KO · you lose\nfight.lineLoss = 击倒 · 你落败\n# en: draw\nfight.lineDraw = 平局\n# en: The fight couldn’t load. Reload the page; if you play on a local server, restart it.\nfight.noEngine = 战斗无法加载。请刷新页面；如果你在本地服务器上游玩，请重启服务器。\n# en: That choice couldn’t go through, so nothing changed. Try again, or pick another option.\nerror.choice = 这个选择没能生效，一切保持原样。请再试一次，或换一个选项。\n# en: You\nfight.you = 你\n# en: Lv {n}\nfight.lv = {n} 级\n# en: Level {n}\nfight.spellLevel = {n} 级\n# en: Look at your opponent\nfight.inspect = 查看对手\n# en: {name} is choosing…\nfight.choosing = {name}正在思考……\n# en: Their move appears when they commit.\nfight.choosingHint = 对方出招后才会显示。\n# en: What their move does\nfight.theirMove = 对方这一招的效果\n# en: They commit:\nfight.theyCommit = 对方出招：\n# en: Compelled:\nfight.compelled = 被迫：\n# en: You commit first\nfight.youFirst = 你先出招\n# en: Their reply stays hidden until you do.\nfight.youFirstHint = 在你出招之前，对方的应对保持隐藏。\n# en: they commit first\nfight.theyFirst = 对方先出招\n# en: Pick an action to see what it does. Pick it again to act.\nfight.pickHint = 选择一个行动查看效果，再点一次即可出招。\n# en: Waiting for your opponent…\nfight.waiting = 等待对手……\n# en: Commit\nfight.commit = 出招\n# en: Cast\nfight.cast = 施放\n# en: Free\nfight.free = 免费\n# en: {n} Mana\nfight.mana = {n} 法力\n# en: {n} HP\nfight.hp = {n} 生命\n# en: HP {v} / {max}\nfight.hpBar = 生命 {v} / {max}\n# en: Mana {v} / {max} · flux {flux}: Refocus turns flux back into Mana\nfight.manaBar = 法力 {v} / {max} · 游离 {flux}：凝神可把游离法力变回法力\n# en: Flux: Refocus turns it back into Mana\nfight.flux = 游离法力：凝神可把它变回法力\n# en: Shield: absorbs damage before HP\nfight.shield = 护盾：先于生命吸收伤害\n# en: {name}: {left} of {of} HP left, until exchange {until}\nfight.construct = {name}：还剩 {left} / {of} 点生命，持续到第 {until} 回合\n# en: Frozen · you thaw after this exchange\nfight.frozen = 冰封 · 本回合结束后解冻\n# en: {spell} · Hold or Release\nfight.channel = {spell} · 蓄持或释放\n# en: Haste · a spell without damage of its own also throws an Impulse; any other action also parries\nfight.haste = 疾速 · 本身不造成伤害的法术也会附带一次冲击；其他行动都会同时招架\n# en: Bound · Breaking Free is your only move\nfight.bound = 被缚 · 挣脱是你唯一的行动\n# en: Compelled · you must repeat {action}\nfight.compelledYou = 被迫 · 你必须重复{action}\n# en: Compelled · you can’t play {action} again, so this exchange is lost\nfight.compelledLost = 被迫 · 你无法再做出{action}，本回合作废\n# en: Compelled · you have no move to repeat, so this exchange is lost\nfight.compelledNone = 被迫 · 你没有可重复的行动，本回合作废\n# en: They cast Compel: the move you pick now you must repeat for the next three exchanges, unless Ward or Shadowstep keeps it off\nfight.compelSeize = 对方施放了强令：你现在选的这一招，接下来三个回合都必须重复，除非护界或影步把它挡下\n# en: Frozen\nfight.frozenName = 冰封\n# en: Rime ready\nfight.rimeReady = 雾凇就绪\n# en: next burn {n}\nfight.nextBurn = 下一次灼烧 {n}\n# en: Impact\nfight.impactNote = 冲击伤害\n# en: spell\nfight.spellNote = 法术伤害\n# en: {move} lands this exchange: {n} damage before defences.\nfight.incomingNow = {move}在本回合落下：防御前 {n} 点伤害。\n# en: {move} lands {when} (exchange {ex}): {n} damage before defences.\nfight.incomingLater = {move}将在{when}（第 {ex} 回合）落下：防御前 {n} 点伤害。\n# en: ({parts})\nfight.incomingSplit = （{parts}）\n# en: next exchange\nfight.whenNext = 下一回合\n# en: in {k} exchanges\nfight.whenIn = {k} 回合后\n# en: matures: all damage negated, then heals {n}\nfight.lodestarUp = 升至顶点：所有伤害均被化解，随后治疗 {n}\n# en: chain {n}\nfight.tailwindChain = 连击 {n}\n# en: held {n}×\nfight.holds = 已蓄持 {n} 次\n# en: {n} banked\nfight.banked = 已存 {n}\n# en: {left} of {of} HP\nfight.hpOf = {left} / {of} 生命\n# en: Exchange {n}\nfight.exchange = 第 {n} 回合\n# en: Exchange {n}: show it in full\nfight.exchangeShow = 第 {n} 回合：查看详情\n# en: you led\nfight.youLed = 你先手\n# en: they led\nfight.theyLed = 对方先手\n# en: you lead\nfight.youLead = 你先手\n# en: they lead\nfight.theyLead = 对方先手\n# en: committed {action}\nfight.committed = 原本出招：{action}\n# en: Back to the latest\nfight.latest = 回到最新\n# en: 1st\nfight.first = 先\n# en: Your move this exchange\nfight.yourMove = 你本回合的行动\n# en: until exchange {n}\nfight.until = 持续到第 {n} 回合\n# en: Exchanges: the past, this one and the next ones\nfight.trackLabel = 回合：过去、当前与接下来\n# en: Exchange {n}: {who} frozen\nfight.slotFrozen = 第 {n} 回合：{who}被冰封\n# en: Exchange {n}: compelled to repeat {action}\nfight.slotCompelled = 第 {n} 回合：被迫重复{action}\n# en: Exchange {n}: compelled to repeat {action}, which can’t be paid now: a lost turn\nfight.slotCompelledLost = 第 {n} 回合：被迫重复{action}，但目前无法支付：回合作废\n# en: Exchange {n}: they commit first, so you see their move\nfight.slotTheyLead = 第 {n} 回合：对方先出招，你能看到对方的行动\n# en: Exchange {n}: you commit first; their move stays hidden\nfight.slotYouLead = 第 {n} 回合：你先出招；对方的行动保持隐藏\n# en: What they can do\nfight.canDo = 对方能做什么\n# en: What they can do now\nfight.canDoNow = 对方现在能做什么\n# en: Their last moves\nfight.lastMoves = 对方最近的招式\n# en: Everything they can do →\nfight.everything = 对方能做的一切 →\n# en: Close\nfight.close = 关闭\n# en: Level {level} · HP {hp}/{maxHp} · Mana {mana}/{maxMana}\nfight.sheetLine = {level} 级 · 生命 {hp}/{maxHp} · 法力 {mana}/{maxMana}\n# en: (+{n} flux)\nfight.plusFlux = （+{n} 游离）\n# en: You come in hurt (Health {n}, under {full}): your HP in the fight starts low.\nfight.unfitHealth = 你带着伤上场（健康 {n}，低于 {full}）：战斗中的生命起点较低。\n# en: Practice duel\nfight.context.practice_duel = 练习对决\n# en: A real fight\nfight.context.real_conflict = 真正的打斗\n# en: Public duel\nfight.context.public_duel = 公开对决\n# en: Rehearsal\nfight.context.rehearsal = 排练\n# en: Ring bout\nfight.context.ring_bout = 擂台赛\n# en: A lesson\nfight.lessonTitle = 一堂课\n# en: Thorne coaches every exchange; only your mood rides on it\nfight.lessonStakes = 索恩教授每个回合都会指点你；只有你的心情系于此\n# en: Nothing at stake\nfight.freeTitle = 无关输赢\n# en: win or lose, nothing is lost and nothing changes afterwards\nfight.nothingAtStake = 不论输赢，什么都不会失去，之后也什么都不会改变\n# en: Health is lost only if you end below {n} HP\nfight.healthBelow = 结束时生命低于 {n} 才会损失健康\n# en: {pct}% of the Health and Mana lost comes back\nfight.rebateBoth = 损失的健康与法力返还 {pct}%\n# en: {pct}% of the Health lost comes back\nfight.rebateHealth = 损失的健康返还 {pct}%\n# en: {pct}% of the Mana spent comes back\nfight.rebateMana = 消耗的法力返还 {pct}%\n# en: A crowd is watching: Renown is won and lost ({pct}% of a loss comes back)\nfight.watched = 有人群围观：声望有得有失（损失返还 {pct}%）\n# en: No crowd: Renown isn’t at stake\nfight.unwatched = 无人围观：声望不受影响\n# en: Someone here would report a forbidden spell\nfight.witnessed = 这里有人会举报禁咒\n# en: No one here would report a forbidden spell\nfight.unwitnessed = 这里没人会举报禁咒\n# en: {name} fights beside you and throws in {spell} now and then\nfight.allyBeside = {name}与你并肩作战，不时施放{spell}\n# en: Your Mana isn't full ({n} of {max}): the rest starts as flux, which takes a Reclaim.\nfight.unfitMana = 你的法力不满（{n} / {max}）：差额以游离法力开场，需要回收。\n# en: Victory\nfight.win = 胜利\n# en: Defeat\nfight.lose = 落败\n# en: Draw\nfight.draw = 平局\n# en: Continue\nfight.continue = 继续\n# en: by knockout\nfight.reason.knockout = 击倒\n# en: on points\nfight.reason.decision = 点数判定\n# en: neither can finish it\nfight.reason.stalemate = 双方都无法取胜\n# en: by concession\nfight.reason.conceded = 认输\n# en: tiny\nsize.tiny = 极小\n# en: small\nsize.small = 小\n# en: medium\nsize.medium = 中\n# en: large\nsize.large = 大\n# en: huge\nsize.huge = 极大\n# en: {track}: {grade}\nnote.grade = {track}：{grade}\n# en: {track}: {from} → {grade} on appeal\nnote.regrade = {track}：申诉后 {from} → {grade}\n# en: {track}: {grade} ({skill} {value} against an exam bar of {bar}; pass chance {p}%)\nnote.gradeWhy = {track}：{grade}（{skill} {value}，考试门槛 {bar}；及格几率 {p}%）\n# en: Enrolled: {track}\nnote.enrol = 已选课：{track}\n# en: Joined: {track}\nnote.join = 已加入：{track}\n# en: Left: {track}\nnote.leave = 已退出：{track}\n# en: {track}: done\nnote.thesisDone = {track}：已完成\n# en: Promoted in {track}\nnote.promote = 在{track}获得晋升\n# en: Dropped from {track}\nnote.dropped = 被{track}除名\n# en: {track}: missed {n}\nnote.missed = {track}：缺席 {n} 次\n# en: {track}: you never showed up this term\nnote.missedClub = {track}：你这学期一次都没来\n# en: {track}: {n} more this term\nnote.attend = {track}：本学期还要 {n} 次\n# en: {track}: your wages, {gold} crowns\nnote.wage = {track}：本次工钱 {gold} 克朗\n# en: {track}: an extra visit (breaks don't count toward the term)\nnote.attendBreak = {track}：额外的一次（假期不计入学期要求）\n# en: New place: {place}\nnote.discover = 新地点：{place}\n# en: A clue found\nnote.clue = 发现了一条线索\n# en: Studies & commitments\nside.studies = 学业与职责\n# en: GPA {gpa}\nside.gpa = GPA {gpa}\n# en: Preparation {n}/{cap}\nside.prep = 备考 {n}/{cap}\n# en: exam in {n} months\nside.examIn = {n} 个月后考试\n# en: exam next month\nside.examNext = 下个月考试\n# en: exam this month\nside.examNow = 本月考试\n# en: Pass {p}%\nside.passToday = 及格 {p}%\n# en: If the exam were today: pass {pass}%, A or B {top}%. Lectures and study choices add preparation (up to the cap); your skill keeps growing until the exam.\nside.oddsTip = 如果今天考试：及格 {pass}%，A 或 B {top}%。上课和学习选择会增加备考（有上限）；考试前你的技能还会继续成长。\n# en: Grades: {grades}\nside.grades = 成绩：{grades}\n# en: {rank} · {due} days of work left\nside.rankLine = {rank} · 还需工作 {due} 天\n# en: {rank}\nside.clubLine = {rank}\n# en: {rank} · work done this term\nside.shiftsDone = {rank} · 本学期的工作已完成\n# en: {rank} · no work over the break\nside.noShifts = {rank} · 假期不用工作\n# en: {n}%\nside.perf = {n}%\n# en: Progress {n}%\nside.progress = 进度 {n}%\n# en: a society: no dues, no ranks\nside.society = 秘密社团：无出勤要求，无职级\n# en: Applied\nside.applied = 已申请\n# en: Saved games\nslots.heading = 存档\n# en: Autosave\nslots.auto = 自动存档\n# en: Slot {n}\nslots.slot = 存档位 {n}\n# en: Empty\nslots.empty = 空\n# en: {name} — {month}, Year {year}\nslots.info = {name} — 第 {year} 年 · {month}\n# en: Save here\nslots.saveHere = 存到这里\n# en: Load\nslots.load = 读取\n# en: Saved\nslots.saved = 已保存\n# en: Close\nslots.close = 关闭\n# en: Stored on the server. Loading a slot continues it as a new run.\nslots.whereServer = 存档保存在服务器上。读取存档位后，会作为一局新的游戏继续。\n# en: Stored in this browser. Loading a slot continues it as a new run.\nslots.whereLocal = 存档保存在本浏览器中。读取存档位后，会作为一局新的游戏继续。\n# en: Connected: runs and feedback are saved on the server.\nnet.connected = 已连接：游戏记录与反馈会保存到服务器。\n# en: Admin\nnet.admin = 管理\n# en: This server needs an invite link to save online. Playing in this browser only.\nnet.needInvite = 此服务器需要邀请链接才能在线存档。目前仅在本浏览器中游玩。\n# en: That sync link didn’t work. Playing in this browser only.\nnet.badSync = 该同步链接无效。目前仅在本浏览器中游玩。\n# en: Play on another device\nsync.heading = 在其他设备上继续\n# en: This code is the only key to your online saves. Open the link (or enter the code) on another device to continue there.\nsync.hint = 这串代码是你在线存档的唯一钥匙。在其他设备上打开链接（或输入代码），即可在那里继续。\n# en: Show code\nsync.show = 显示代码\n# en: Copy link\nsync.copyLink = 复制链接\n# en: Link copied.\nsync.copied = 链接已复制。\n# en: Use a code\nsync.use = 使用代码\n# en: Switch to that code’s saves? The game in this browser is set aside (its log and feedback are kept).\nsync.confirm = 切换到该代码的存档？本浏览器中的游戏会被搁置（其记录与反馈会保留）。\n# en: That code wasn’t found.\nsync.bad = 找不到该代码。\n# en: she\npronoun.she.they = 她\n# en: her\npronoun.she.them = 她\n# en: her\npronoun.she.their = 她的\n# en: hers\npronoun.she.theirs = 她的\n# en: is\npronoun.she.are = \n# en: s\npronoun.she.s = \n# en: he\npronoun.he.they = 他\n# en: him\npronoun.he.them = 他\n# en: his\npronoun.he.their = 他的\n# en: his\npronoun.he.theirs = 他的\n# en: is\npronoun.he.are = \n# en: s\npronoun.he.s = \n# en: they\npronoun.they.they = TA\n# en: them\npronoun.they.them = TA\n# en: their\npronoun.they.their = TA的\n# en: theirs\npronoun.they.theirs = TA的\n# en: are\npronoun.they.are = \n# en: \npronoun.they.s = \n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/arcs.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/arcs.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@arc hollowstar\n# en: The Hollow Star\nname [e1bf91] = 空洞之星\n# en: Omens, Investigation, Escalation, Convergence\nact_names [aff64b] = 预兆、调查、升级、汇聚\n# en: Something that eats light sleeps beneath the Mirror Lake, behind a door the founders sealed with seven locks. Someone wants it open.\ndesc [30af88] = 有一种吞噬光的东西沉睡在镜湖底下，被创始人用七道锁封在一扇门后。有人想把门打开。\n# en: The Night is over. Whatever you chose beneath the lake, the castle wakes every morning around it now.\nepilogue [b6907b] = 那一夜过去了。无论你在湖底做了什么选择，城堡如今每天早晨都围着它醒来。\n# en: They say the lake breathed on the first frost, and the bell under it rang with no one near.\nact_rumours.1 [d9fda6] = 人们说，初霜那天湖呼吸了一下，湖底的钟在四下无人时响了。\n# en: There's talk of a girl in white furs in the village, and of a man in grey who asks after the Dumonts.\nact_rumours.2 [66a273] = 村里有人在议论一个穿白裘的女孩，还有一个打听杜蒙家的灰衣人。\n# en: A tower went dark. Nobody says the word \"cult\" at supper, which is how you know everyone is thinking it.\nact_rumours.3 [d237ba] = 一座塔暗了下去。晚饭时没人说出“邪教”这个词，你由此知道每个人都在想它。\n# en: The lake has frozen early, and the headmistress has stopped pretending it hasn't.\nact_rumours.4 [dc157f] = 湖面早早封冻了，院长也不再假装它没有。\n\n@arc lyra_and_her_mother\n# en: Second Place\nname [604ed6] = 第二名\n# en: Rivals, Cracks, Choice\nact_names [254274] = 对手、裂痕、抉择\n# en: Lyra Vaneshade has never once heard \"well done\". Her rivalry with you can become respect, friendship or more, and her mother will have something to say about all three.\ndesc [227832] = 莱拉·凡妮希德从未听过一句“做得好”。她与你的竞争可以变成尊重、友谊，甚至更多——而她的母亲对这三者都会有话要说。\n# en: Everyone knows Lyra Vaneshade means to come first. Fewer know who she means to beat.\nact_rumours.1 [98d0a1] = 人人都知道莱拉·凡妮希德要争第一。知道她想赢的是谁的人就少多了。\n# en: Her mother's letters come with a black seal, and Lyra reads them alone.\nact_rumours.2 [8ede18] = 她母亲的信封着黑色火漆，莱拉总是独自读。\n# en: House Vaneshade expects an answer from its heir before she graduates.\nact_rumours.3 [d7da02] = 凡妮希德家族要它的继承人在毕业前给出答复。\n\n@arc kael_thaw\n# en: Thaw\nname [448405] = 解冻\n# en: Frost, Trust, Thaw\nact_names [0abe5d] = 冰封、信任、解冻\n# en: Kael Dumont was sent to open a door and came to stop it. Whether he manages depends on whether anyone is warm enough.\ndesc [4e1d95] = 凯尔·杜蒙被派来打开一扇门，却来阻止它。他能否做到，取决于有没有人足够温暖。\n# en: The transfer from the North doesn't feel the cold, they say, and doesn't sit with anyone.\nact_rumours.1 [eecc9c] = 人们说，那个从北境转来的学生不怕冷，也从不跟谁坐在一起。\n# en: Kael has started sitting somewhere. People have noticed where.\nact_rumours.2 [a3286a] = 凯尔开始固定坐在某个地方了。大家都注意到是哪里。\n# en: Letters come from the North more often now, and Kael answers fewer of them.\nact_rumours.3 [d56b50] = 北境来的信越来越多，凯尔回的却越来越少。\n\n@arc ilse_dumont\n# en: The Girl in White Furs\nname [a99aba] = 白裘少女\n# en: Arrival, The Man in Grey, North\nact_names [b9bace] = 到来、灰衣人、北方\n# en: Kael's sister was raised by the Hollow cult to do what he wouldn't. The man in grey wants her home. She might let him.\ndesc [7d861b] = 凯尔的妹妹被空洞教团养大，去做他不肯做的事。灰衣人想带她回家。她也许会跟他走。\n# en: A girl in white furs paid at the bakery with a coin that froze to the counter.\nact_rumours.1 [cf0177] = 一个穿白裘的女孩在面包房付钱，硬币冻在了柜台上。\n# en: The man in grey has been seen in the lanes after dark, asking after the girl in white.\nact_rumours.2 [33f888] = 有人在天黑后的小巷里见过那个灰衣人，打听那个穿白衣的女孩。\n# en: Letters with a black-star seal keep coming for Ilse.\nact_rumours.3 [cbb554] = 封着黑星火漆的信，一封接一封地寄给伊尔泽。\n\n@arc the_bell\n# en: The Bell Under the Water\nname [f2ca3f] = 水下的钟\n# en: Echo, Rope\nact_names [ee3094] = 回声、绳索\n# en: The drowned bell under the Mirror Lake rings when no one is near it. Someone wants it found.\ndesc [e24896] = 镜湖底那口沉钟会在无人靠近时鸣响。有人想让它被找到。\n# en: Some nights the drowned bell rings, and the fishermen won't say so in daylight.\nact_rumours.1 [cfe516] = 有些夜里，沉在水下的钟会响，渔夫们白天却绝口不提。\n# en: Old Maud has kept something of the bell for sixty years, they say, and she's starting to talk.\nact_rumours.2 [7c13a1] = 人们说，老莫德留着那口钟的某样东西已经六十年了，而她开始松口了。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/cast.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/cast.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@npc lyra\n# en: Lyra Vaneshade\nname [7ccd7a] = 莱拉·凡妮希德\n# en: Heir of House Vaneshade, pyromancy prodigy\ntitle [a4c6ad] = 凡妮希德家族继承人，火焰术天才\n# en: Emberhall\nhouse [6adebf] = 烬厅\n# en: Brilliant, impatient and used to winning. She treats everyone as either a rival or furniture — and is quietly lonely about it.\ndesc [b8c380] = 才华横溢，缺乏耐心，习惯了赢。在她眼里，每个人要么是对手，要么是家具——她为此默默地孤独着。\n\n@npc tobias\n# en: Tobias Fennick\nname [b5c657] = 托比亚斯·芬尼克\n# en: Farmer's son on a turnip scholarship\ntitle [ae6bc4] = 拿着芜菁奖学金的农家子弟\n# en: Rootward\nhouse [dd14d3] = 根庐\n# en: Big hands, bigger heart. Talks to plants and, worryingly, the plants seem to answer.\ndesc [325d88] = 手掌大，心更大。他会跟植物说话，而令人担忧的是，植物似乎会回答。\n\n@npc seraphine\n# en: Seraphine Hale\nname [b9b6ae] = 瑟拉芬·海尔\n# en: Seer who never quite looks at the present\ntitle [a72b9b] = 从不正眼看“现在”的预言者\n# en: Stillwater\nhouse [67e7fc] = 静水\n# en: Speaks in half-sentences, as if finishing the other half in a different week. Often right. Unsettlingly often.\ndesc [abbc16] = 说话总是只说半句，仿佛另一半要留到下个星期再说完。她常常是对的。准得让人发毛。\n\n@npc corvin\n# en: Corvin Ashby\nname [5f13bd] = 科尔文·阿什比\n# en: Charming liability\ntitle [c06ba3] = 迷人的麻烦精\n# en: Duskgate\nhouse [a1e05f] = 暮门\n# en: Knows every locked door in the academy and has opinions about all of them. Owes money to at least three people.\ndesc [3fb5db] = 认识学院里每一扇锁着的门，并对每一扇都有意见。至少欠着三个人的钱。\n\n@npc mir\n# en: Mir Ten\nname [31baa8] = 米尔·滕\n# en: Artificer of questionable inventions\ntitle [dcc640] = 发明可疑的奇械师\n# en: Brasswick\nhouse [6604c0] = 铜芯\n# en: Smells permanently of solder and ozone. Has built a clockwork owl, a self-stirring cauldron and at least one small fire.\ndesc [7b73bc] = 身上永远带着焊锡和臭氧的味道。造过一只发条猫头鹰、一口自动搅拌坩埚，以及至少一场小火灾。\n\n@npc kael\n# en: Kael Dumont\nname [18aead] = 凯尔·杜蒙\n# en: Transfer student from the frozen North\ntitle [80355d] = 来自冰封北境的转学生\n# en: Stillwater\nhouse [67e7fc] = 静水\n# en: Arrived mid-term with no luggage and frost in his eyelashes. Doesn't talk about where he came from. Watches the sky at night.\ndesc [058f2e] = 学期中途到来，没带行李，睫毛上挂着霜。从不谈起自己从哪里来。夜里总在看天。\n\n@npc nessa\n# en: Nessa Quill\nname [3ad688] = 内莎·奎尔\n# en: Editor of the Lumen Ledger\ntitle [c35a40] = 《流明纪事报》主编\n# en: Duskgate\nhouse [a1e05f] = 暮门\n# en: Runs the student paper with a quill that writes on its own. Everyone is a story; some are just not finished yet.\ndesc [2179de] = 用一支会自己写字的羽毛笔经营着学生报纸。每个人都是一个故事；有些只是还没写完。\n\n@npc bram\n# en: Bram Hollis\nname [182554] = 布拉姆·霍利斯\n# en: Prefect, enforcer, menace\ntitle [2cee1b] = 级长、执法者、祸害\n# en: Emberhall\nhouse [6adebf] = 烬厅\n# en: A prefect of your own year, with a ledger of demerits and a fondness for writing your name in it.\ndesc [44da9b] = 和你同年级的级长，揣着一本记过簿，特别喜欢在上面写你的名字。\n\n@npc thorne\n# en: Professor Aldric Thorne\nname [734bbb] = 奥尔德里克·索恩教授\n# en: Professor Thorne\nshort [fff982] = 索恩教授\n# en: Master of Arcana\ntitle [c13eee] = 奥术大师\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Severe, exacting, allergic to flattery. Rumoured to have once out-dueled a storm.\ndesc [a1a584] = 严厉、苛刻，对奉承过敏。传说他曾在决斗中赢过一场风暴。\n\n@npc maud\n# en: Old Maud\nname [9c77f5] = 老莫德\n# en: Old Maud\nshort [9c77f5] = 老莫德\n# en: Groundskeeper and hedge-witch\ntitle [e5de9f] = 园丁兼乡野女巫\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Keeps bees, chickens and a rather large secret garden. Gives advice you only understand two weeks later.\ndesc [8d1e7c] = 养蜂、养鸡，还有一座相当大的秘密花园。她的建议，你往往要过两周才听得懂。\n\n@npc isolde\n# en: Headmistress Isolde Marrow\nname [9e3659] = 伊索尔德·马罗院长\n# en: Headmistress Marrow\nshort [feeebf] = 马罗院长\n# en: Headmistress of Lumenhold\ntitle [b622da] = 流明堡院长\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Tall, pale and impossibly calm. She remembers every student's name — and every student's mistakes.\ndesc [264ae0] = 高挑、苍白，冷静得不可思议。她记得每个学生的名字——也记得每个学生犯过的错。\n\n@npc ilse\n# en: Ilse Dumont\nname [7e9501] = 伊尔泽·杜蒙\n# en: Kael's younger sister, a storm in white furs\ntitle [8c137b] = 凯尔的妹妹，一场裹着白色毛皮的风暴\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: Raised by the Hollow cult to open a door she has never seen. Sharp as an icicle, lonely as a frozen lake, and much younger than she pretends to be.\ndesc [908293] = 被空洞教团养大，只为打开一扇她从未见过的门。锋利如冰锥，孤独如冰封的湖——而且比她装出来的样子年轻得多。\n\n@npc tamsin\n# en: Tamsin Reed\nname [cc4cab] = 塔姆辛·里德\n# en: Tamsin\nshort [4365c9] = 塔姆辛\n# en: Illusionist at Thornwick, your pen pal\ntitle [7ad721] = 荆木学院的幻术学生，你的笔友\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: A Thornwick illusionist who answered a letter to \"a Lumenhold student chosen at random\" and kept writing. Insufferable, by their own account, and the best correspondent you have.\ndesc [54270b] = 荆木学院的一名幻术师，回了一封寄给“随机抽中的流明堡学生”的信，然后一直写了下去。按 TA 自己的说法，让人受不了；也是你最好的通信对象。\n\n@npc amaranth\n# en: Sister Amaranth\nname [f35ea5] = 阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Sister Amaranth\nshort [f35ea5] = 阿玛兰斯修女\n# en: Healer of the infirmary\ntitle [6b950d] = 医务室的治疗师\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Runs the infirmary with warming charms, tonics and no patience for excuses. Teaches the healing half of Alchemy & Healing.\ndesc [8363a5] = 掌管医务室：暖身咒、补剂，以及对借口零容忍。也教炼金与治愈里治愈的那一半。\n\n@npc brask\n# en: Professor Hedda Brask\nname [dafdd1] = 赫达·布拉斯克教授\n# en: Professor Brask\nshort [a513af] = 布拉斯克教授\n# en: Professor of Beastcraft & Flight\ntitle [af9122] = 驭兽与飞行教授\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Teaches Beastcraft & Flight and is kinder to animals than to students, though the gap closes for anyone who mucks out without being asked.\ndesc [34945d] = 教驭兽与飞行，对动物比对学生和气；不过谁要是不等吩咐就去铲马厩，这差距就会缩小。\n\n@npc vey\n# en: Professor Ottilie Vey\nname [0ed5bc] = 奥蒂莉·维伊教授\n# en: Professor Vey\nshort [a16fca] = 维伊教授\n# en: Professor of Illusion & Glamour\ntitle [3b7ca9] = 幻术与魅影教授\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Teaches Illusion & Glamour and grades on whether you fooled her. She is very hard to fool, and very generous to the few who manage it.\ndesc [e5e975] = 教幻术与魅影，按你骗没骗过她来打分。她极难被骗，对少数骗过她的人却极其慷慨。\n\n@npc marisol\n# en: Professor Ines Marisol\nname [36ca1e] = 伊内丝·玛丽索尔教授\n# en: Professor Marisol\nshort [40ede1] = 玛丽索尔教授\n# en: Professor of Tidecalling\ntitle [867c83] = 唤潮术教授\n# en: Faculty\nhouse [078d5f] = 教职员\n# en: Teaches the tidecalling half of Evocation, waist-deep in the lake at dawn. Keeps the lake calm on exam days and reads poetry under a dry patch of air when it rains.\ndesc [9fa4f2] = 教塑能术里唤潮的那一半，黎明时分站在齐腰深的湖水里上课。考试那几天由她让湖面平静；下雨时，她在一小片干燥的空气下读诗。\n\n@npc mabel\n# en: Mabel Thistlewood\nname [472ddc] = 梅布尔·蓟木\n# en: Mabel\nshort [60ac42] = 梅布尔\n# en: Keeper of the tea shop\ntitle [389e2b] = 茶馆老板娘\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: Forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake, and a talent for reading your mood from the way you open her door.\ndesc [5c8604] = 四十种茶，三种蛋糕，还有一样本事：看你怎么推门，就知道你今天心情如何。\n\n@npc grimsby\n# en: Mr Alder Grimsby\nname [be7a57] = 奥尔德·格林斯比先生\n# en: Mr Grimsby\nshort [aa7b23] = 格林斯比先生\n# en: Bookseller\ntitle [c29f43] = 书商\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: Keeps a bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other. He will find anything except what you came in for, which is often what you needed.\ndesc [bf3215] = 他的书店挤得书架互相倚靠。你要什么他都找得到，唯独找不到你进门要的那本——而那往往正是你需要的。\n\n@npc pell\n# en: Old Pell\nname [345189] = 老佩尔\n# en: Old Pell\nshort [345189] = 老佩尔\n# en: Apothecary of Pell & Daughters\ntitle [c6c952] = 佩尔父女药铺的药剂师\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: Taught three generations of the village to count drops. His daughters run the counter now; he runs the drawers, and the drawers are never wrong.\ndesc [5f1bca] = 教会了村里三代人数药滴。如今柜台归他的女儿们管，他管抽屉，而抽屉从不出错。\n\n@npc grisk\n# en: Grisk\nname [9443cd] = 格里斯克\n# en: Grisk\nshort [9443cd] = 格里斯克\n# en: Moneylender, friend to the temporarily embarrassed\ntitle [ad4b4c] = 放贷人，一时手头拮据者的朋友\n# en: none\nhouse [a7afdb] = 无\n# en: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat who lends to students: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six. He keeps a ledger, and sometimes asks for favours instead of coin.\ndesc [cc2c49] = 穿天鹅绒马甲的地精，专借钱给学生：今天六十克朗，之后六个月每月十二。他有一本账簿，偶尔要人情而不要钱。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/clues.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/clues.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@clue lead_lake_breathed\n# en: The lake held its breath\nname [9bf32e] = 湖屏住了呼吸\n# en: In Mistveil the lake froze for one breath and the drowned bell tolled. Whoever keeps the boathouse has heard that shore for years.\ntext [488b1c] = 雾纱月里，湖面冻结了一口气的工夫，沉钟响了一声。守船屋的人在那片岸边听了许多年。\n\n@clue lead_founders_archive\n# en: What the founders wrote\nname [7110fd] = 创始者写下了什么\n# en: The headmistress told the hall it was nothing. The founders' records in the library archive may disagree, and the Restricted Section keeps what the archive won't.\ntext [6e77dc] = 校长对全场说那不算什么。图书馆档案库里创始者的记录也许另有说法，而档案库不肯留的东西，禁书区会留着。\n\n@clue lead_maud_friend\n# en: Maud lost a friend\nname [13ea1d] = 老莫德失去过一个朋友\n# en: Old Maud was a student the last time the lake froze, and lost a friend to it. She might tell you more, if she trusts you.\ntext [9085dd] = 上一次湖面封冻时，老莫德还是这里的学生，她因此失去了一个朋友。如果她信任你，也许会告诉你更多。\n\n@clue lead_man_in_grey\n# en: A man in grey\nname [44c1ae] = 灰衣人\n# en: A man in a grey coat told Kael to open something, or his sister would. Kael knows what. He'll tell someone he trusts.\ntext [6568f1] = 一个穿灰大衣的男人要凯尔打开某样东西，否则就由他妹妹来。凯尔知道是什么。他会告诉他信任的人。\n\n@clue lead_black_star_dream\n# en: A dream of a black star\nname [077898] = 黑星之梦\n# en: You dreamt of the lake from underneath. Seraphine dreams too, and the observatory sees further than any dream.\ntext [310e35] = 你梦见了从下方看到的湖。瑟拉芬也做这样的梦，而观星台看得比任何梦都远。\n\n@clue lead_seventh_tower\n# en: The seventh tower\nname [0db95c] = 第七塔\n# en: Seven towers, seven locks. One tower has been locked for a century, and something is kept at the top of it.\ntext [4f08ee] = 七座塔，七把锁。其中一座塔已锁了一百年，塔顶藏着什么东西。\n\n@clue lead_white_furs\n# en: A girl in white furs\nname [f6670a] = 白裘少女\n# en: A Northern girl has taken a room above the village bakery. Kael went pale when he heard.\ntext [5dc951] = 一个北方姑娘在村里面包房楼上租了间屋子。凯尔听说后脸都白了。\n\n@clue heard_of_starcalling\n# en: A scraped-out word\nname [9bfbf7] = 被刮掉的词\n# en: One word has been scraped out of the observatory's oldest logs. Students who finished Advanced Astromancy were taught it aloud.\ntext [90a830] = 观星台最老的日志里，有一个词被刮掉了。修完高等星象学的学生，都是被人当面口授这个词的。\n\n@clue founders_ledger\n# en: The razored page\nname [68d0a8] = 被割去的一页\n# en: The founding year was cut out of the Founders' Ledger with a razor, but the pen's impression survived on the page beneath.\ntext [dfbf3b] = 《创始者账簿》里建校那一年被人用刀片割掉了，但笔尖的压痕留在了下面那一页上。\n\n@clue knows_founders_seal\n# en: The founders' seal\nname [44bb6a] = 创始者的封印\n# en: Lumenhold was built as a seal: seven towers, seven locks, and a key shaped like a star. Something sleeps beneath the water.\ntext [934b3b] = 流明堡是作为一道封印建起来的：七座塔，七把锁，还有一把星形的钥匙。有什么东西在水底沉睡。\n\n@clue lake_bell\n# en: The bell under the water\nname [e5ecdf] = 水下的钟\n# en: A drowned bell lies under the lake and tolls when the thing below stirs. A tunnel runs down from the boathouse toward it.\ntext [8955ef] = 一口沉钟躺在湖底，下面的东西一动，它就会响。一条隧道从船屋一路通向它。\n\n@clue second_shadow\n# en: A second shadow\nname [ac1374] = 第二道影子\n# en: Something small and dark crossed the sky the wrong way and stopped directly above the lake.\ntext [49214c] = 一个又小又暗的东西逆着方向划过天空，正好停在湖的上方。\n\n@clue seven_locks\n# en: Seven locks\nname [bb2192] = 七把锁\n# en: One lock in each tower; when a tower goes dark, so does its lock. Locks can be mended. They need hands, and a key.\ntext [8ec40d] = 每座塔里一把锁；一座塔熄了灯，它的锁也就沉寂了。锁可以修补，需要人手，也需要一把钥匙。\n\n@clue star_key\n# en: The star key\nname [213cd3] = 星钥\n# en: A key shaped like a star that holds no light, kept at the top of the seventh tower. The seal can't be renewed without it, or without the founders' words.\ntext [82437c] = 一把星形的钥匙，不含一丝光，藏在第七塔塔顶。没有它，或者没有创始者的咒文，封印就无法重铸。\n\n@clue kael_family\n# en: The Dumonts\nname [6f83b6] = 杜蒙家族\n# en: Kael's family serve the thing under the lake. They sent him to open its door. He came to stop them.\ntext [245d59] = 凯尔的家族侍奉着湖底的那个东西。他们派他来打开那扇门。他却是来阻止他们的。\n\n@clue ilse_truth\n# en: What Ilse was raised for\nname [48407c] = 伊尔泽被养大的目的\n# en: The Dumonts raised Kael's sister to open the door if he wouldn't. Nobody ever asked her if she wanted to.\ntext [6eb048] = 杜蒙家把凯尔的妹妹养大，就是为了在凯尔不肯时由她去开门。从来没人问过她愿不愿意。\n\n@clue codex_names\n# en: The Codex of Hollow Names\nname [b71797] = 《空洞名录》\n# en: A book in the Restricted Section lists everyone the Star has noticed. The last name is still being written, and it is yours.\ntext [3e42ac] = 禁书区里有一本书，列着所有被那颗星注意到的人。最后一个名字还在写，那是你的名字。\n\n@clue bell_heard\n# en: A bell where none is\nname [d030c0] = 无人之处的钟声\n# en: The drowned bell rang in the night with no one near it, and nobody else woke.\ntext [233601] = 夜里沉钟响了，附近一个人也没有，而别人都没有醒。\n\n@clue bell_rope\n# en: The cut rope\nname [581c10] = 被割断的绳\n# en: Maud has kept the bell's rope for sixty years. It came up the night the lake froze, cut clean.\ntext [6af71a] = 老莫德把那口钟的绳子留了六十年。湖面封冻的那一夜它浮了上来，断口齐整。\n\n@clue bell_name\n# en: The name on the rim\nname [3ef04f] = 钟沿上的名字\n# en: Letters run around the bell's rim on the lakebed: a name you don't know, and now keep.\ntext [05aeb6] = 湖底那口钟的边沿上刻着一圈字母：一个你不认识的名字，如今你记住了。\n\n@clue lead_maud_answer\n# en: What Maud knows\nname [697576] = 莫德知道的事\n# en: Maud has kept the bell's rope for sixty years, and she has promised to tell you who cut it. Go and see her at her cottage.\ntext [a039ae] = 莫德把那口钟的绳子留了六十年，她答应告诉你是谁割断了它。去她的小屋找她吧。\n\n@clue lead_bell_dive\n# en: The diving helmet\nname [5a9fa6] = 潜水头盔\n# en: There's an old diving helmet in the boathouse. Whatever is written on the bell, you'll only read it from the lakebed.\ntext [ae5827] = 船屋里有一顶旧潜水头盔。钟上不管写着什么，你只能到湖底去读。\n\n@clue lead_bell_shore\n# en: The lakeshore after dark\nname [2ebc9e] = 天黑后的湖岸\n# en: The bell wants you at the lakeshore after dark, in the evening or at midnight, with what you have: the name from its rim, or Maud's rope.\ntext [6391ee] = 那口钟要你在天黑后去湖岸，傍晚或午夜都行，带上你手里有的东西：钟沿上的名字，或者莫德的绳子。\n\n@clue black_seal_letters\n# en: Letters under a black seal\nname [cd05c9] = 黑色火漆的信\n# en: House Vaneshade writes to Lyra under its black seal. Her mother asks who beat her, never how she is.\ntext [9609b0] = 凡妮希德家用黑色火漆给莱拉写信。她母亲只问是谁赢了她，从不问她过得好不好。\n\n@clue lead_moonpetal\n# en: The cold that won't leave\nthread [fcb8af] = 挥之不去的寒意\n# en: Moonpetal\nname [676f04] = 月瓣花\n# en: Old Maud says the cure is a moonpetal from the Whisperwood, which blooms only after dark, and that you have to fetch it yourself.\ntext [7cd738] = 老莫德说，解药是低语森林里的一朵月瓣花，只在天黑后开放，而且必须由你亲手去采。\n\n@clue lead_second_timetable\n# en: The unsigned note\nthread [41bfa1] = 没有署名的字条\n# en: A second timetable\nname [efd783] = 第二张时间表\n# en: Someone slipped a note under your door: the castle has a second timetable, and it starts at midnight. Whoever wrote it knows the passages.\ntext [631b45] = 有人从你门缝塞进一张字条：城堡还有第二张时间表，从午夜开始。写字条的人熟悉那些暗道。\n\n@clue lead_grisk_jetty\n# en: Grisk's parcels\nthread [c5fea9] = 格里斯克的包裹\n# en: The jetty at midnight\nname [8b40af] = 午夜的栈桥\n# en: Grisk's arrangement: a parcel collected at the boathouse jetty at midnight, carried to a cobbler's back door in the village by day.\ntext [6eb586] = 格里斯克的安排：午夜在船屋栈桥取一个包裹，白天送到村里一家鞋匠铺的后门。\n\n@clue lead_grisk_bottles\n# en: Grisk's bottles\nthread [e862de] = 格里斯克的瓶子\n# en: Bottles at the jetty\nname [248d14] = 码头上的瓶子\n# en: Grisk's arrangement: a bottle of Brightwater or Owl's Ink for a favour, at the boathouse jetty at midnight.\ntext [edcb5b] = 格里斯克的安排：午夜在船屋码头，用一个人情换一瓶亮泉水或枭墨。\n\n@clue lead_silver_key\n# en: The silver key\nthread [d5ce1e] = 银钥匙\n# en: The Restricted Section\nname [b1cf8a] = 禁书区\n# en: Odile Varn's notebook came with a silver key to the Restricted Section, the locked door at the back of the library. It's yours to use after hours, in the evening or at midnight.\ntext [b9cf1a] = 奥迪尔·瓦恩的笔记本里夹着一把银钥匙，能打开禁书区——图书馆后面那扇总是锁着的门。闭馆之后，傍晚或午夜，你都可以用它。\n\n@clue lead_restricted_shelves\n# en: The silver key\nthread [d5ce1e] = 银钥匙\n# en: Deeper in the stacks\nname [c89789] = 书架深处\n# en: You've been inside the Restricted Section once, and there are shelves you didn't reach. The key still works after hours.\ntext [35d082] = 你进过一次禁书区，还有好些书架没走到。闭馆之后，钥匙依然管用。\n\n@clue lead_codex_open\n# en: The silver key\nthread [d5ce1e] = 银钥匙\n# en: The Codex, left open\nname [5cf0da] = 摊开的名录\n# en: The Codex of Hollow Names is still lying where it fell in the Restricted Section, open at your name. Sooner or later, someone will notice you've been there.\ntext [9a9116] = 《空洞名录》还躺在禁书区里它掉落的地方，摊开在写着你名字的那一页。迟早会有人发现你去过那里。\n\n@clue lead_newt_upstairs\n# en: The upstairs room\nthread [925e4e] = 楼上的房间\n# en: A regular at the Newt\nname [19371c] = 蝾螈酒馆的熟客\n# en: The landlady of the Gilded Newt's upstairs room keeps a jacket for you now. Any night out at the Newt, there's a party upstairs that pays.\ntext [82f43f] = 镀金蝾螈酒馆楼上房间的老板娘现在给你留着一件外套。哪天夜里去蝾螈酒馆，楼上总有一场付钱的聚会。\n\n@clue lead_sky_championship\n# en: The Skyrace team\nthread [dc4a0d] = 天空竞速队\n# en: The championship\nname [b9ac19] = 锦标赛\n# en: You made the team. The championship against Thornwick is flown at the flight field later in the year.\ntext [bc0a04] = 你进了校队。对荆木学院的锦标赛，会在今年晚些时候于飞行场举行。\n\n@clue lead_brace\n# en: The captain's charm\nthread [7a7935] = 队长的护符\n# en: The dummy that hexes back\nname [f7da43] = 会回击的假人\n# en: An old duelling captain's charm is sewn into the practice dummy, and it throws hexes back. Read the captain's notes, stand Bram's barrage, drill your footwork: then hold firm inside the circle while it empties.\ntext [764c8f] = 一位老决斗队长的护符缝在练习假人里，它会把恶咒弹回来。读队长的笔记，扛过布拉姆的轰炸，练好步法：然后在圈里站稳，直到护符放空。\n\n@clue lead_quickquill\n# en: The quill that talks back\nthread [0707e8] = 会顶嘴的羽毛笔\n# en: A quill of your own\nname [40b795] = 一支属于你的羽毛笔\n# en: A spare quill from the Ledger's drying line finishes your sentences, and then invents them. Teach it, give it proper ink and sit a deadline night with it, before the interview where everyone denies everything.\ntext [675f89] = 纪事报晾稿绳上的一支备用羽毛笔会替你把话写完，然后开始自己编。教它，给它好墨水，陪它熬一个截稿夜，再去做那场人人都矢口否认的采访。\n\n@clue lead_bramble\n# en: The seed in your pocket\nthread [e1a78b] = 口袋里的种子\n# en: A seed that answers back\nname [d31014] = 会回嘴的种子\n# en: A seed from the Verdant Circle's far bed sprouted in your pocket. Plant it, see it through a cold snap, and quiet the mandrakes around it before the night it bolts.\ntext [7f51b9] = 青翠社最里头那块苗床的一颗种子在你口袋里发了芽。把它种下，陪它熬过一场寒潮，让周围的曼德拉草安静下来，然后等它疯长的那一夜。\n\n@clue lead_index\n# en: Footnote's drawer\nthread [f8bf3c] = 脚注的抽屉\n# en: The drawer Footnote guards\nname [6e005b] = 脚注守着的抽屉\n# en: Footnote sits on one catalogue drawer in the library and won't move. Under him is the card for a book eleven years overdue, somewhere on shelves that wander.\ntext [a87346] = 脚注蹲在图书馆的一个目录抽屉上，怎么也不挪窝。它身下是一本逾期十一年的书的卡片，书在某排会游走的书架上。\n\n@clue lead_rapport\n# en: Two taps on the bar\nthread [12c408] = 吧台上敲两下\n# en: How the landlady does it\nname [0fcd25] = 老板娘是怎么做到的\n# en: The Newt's landlady taps the bar twice and a fight turns into a laugh. Learn the regulars' stories, stand them a round and work a double shift, and one festival night the room will be yours to hold.\ntext [5674f8] = 蝾螈酒馆的老板娘在吧台上敲两下，一场架就变成了一阵笑。学会熟客们的故事，请他们喝一轮，上一次双班，总有一个节日夜，整个酒馆会归你镇住。\n\n@clue lead_distil\n# en: Pell's oldest drawer\nthread [16c158] = 佩尔最老的抽屉\n# en: A draught of your own strength\nname [90f116] = 一剂“取自本身之力”的药\n# en: Pell's oldest drawer holds a recipe for a draught \"of your own strength\". Count drops with Pell, buy moonwort, try the daughters' shortcut, and then brew it.\ntext [f815c7] = 佩尔最老的抽屉里有一张配方，是一剂“取自本身之力”的药。跟佩尔数一夜药滴，买月蕨，试试他女儿们的捷径，然后把它熬出来。\n\n@clue lead_tailwind\n# en: The jar of wind\nthread [4a78a8] = 罐中之风\n# en: Wind in a jar\nname [aed1d4] = 装在罐子里的风\n# en: A jar of wind sits on the courier loft's dead-letter shelf, and someone once flew with it. Find out who, race the gulls and fix the broom that pulls left, then fly a storm delivery across the lake.\ntext [92c9fa] = 信使阁楼的死信架上有一罐风，从前有人带着它飞过。找出那人是谁，和海鸥比一场，修好那把总往左偏的扫帚，然后在风暴里飞一趟湖上快递。\n\n@clue lead_the_hush\n# en: The Hush\nthread [510138] = 寂境\n# en: The Hush\nname [510138] = 寂境\n# en: The deepest part of the Whisperwood, past the heartwood, keeps the castle's hours: it wakes only at midnight.\ntext [d4cf9b] = 低语森林最深处，心木林再往里，守的是城堡的时辰：它只在午夜醒来。\n\n@clue lead_heartwood\n# en: The elder oak's gift\nthread [e28277] = 古橡的馈赠\n# en: A splinter of heartwood\nname [539ac3] = 一片心木\n# en: The elder oak gave you a splinter of its heartwood. Mir, at the market stall, could carve it into a wand.\ntext [5f40f3] = 古橡给了你一片它的心木。集市摊子上的米尔能把它雕成一根魔杖。\n\n@clue lead_spinner_silk\n# en: The Spinner's silk\nthread [53ecf8] = 织网者的丝\n# en: A bolt of grey silk\nname [e5dad8] = 一卷灰丝\n# en: The Spinner gave you a bolt of her silk. The village tailor could make a robe of it.\ntext [6fd0e6] = 织网者给了你一卷她的丝。村里的裁缝能用它做一件长袍。\n\n@clue lead_missed_round\n# en: The missed round\nthread [5ab68d] = 漏掉的巡房\n# en: The lamp that went out\nname [618d37] = 熄灭的灯\n# en: Sister Amaranth missed her evening round, the first time anyone can remember. Someone has to keep the infirmary going until she's on her feet, in the evenings, bed by bed.\ntext [ab8c88] = 阿玛兰斯修女漏掉了晚间巡房，这是谁都记不起来的头一回。在她重新站起来之前，总得有人在傍晚撑起医务室，一张床一张床地照看。\n\n@clue lead_red_ink\n# en: Red ink\nthread [77bee0] = 红墨水\n# en: Two sets of marks\nname [2509f5] = 两种批改\n# en: Someone is marking your essays a second time, in red ink and unsigned, and leaving them on the lecture tower stair. They expect the next draft there too.\ntext [e6641c] = 有人在用红墨水重新批改你的论文，不署名，批完就放在讲堂塔的楼梯上。他们等着下一稿也放在那里。\n\n@clue lead_late_bloomer\n# en: The sweet shop's daughter\nthread [056316] = 糖果铺的女儿\n# en: The cold stove\nname [e93267] = 冷灶\n# en: The sweet shop owner's grown daughter boils the sugar on a cold stove when she's upset, and nobody at home knows what to do about it. You're the one from the castle, and she'll be waiting when you're next home.\ntext [81bb90] = 糖果铺店主那个已经成年的女儿一着急，冷灶上的糖就会沸起来，家乡没人知道该拿这事怎么办。你是从城堡回来的人，下次你回家时，她会等着你。\n\n@clue lead_master_map\n# en: The Master Map\nthread [d14ae0] = 总图\n# en: The blanks on the map\nname [b5ace9] = 总图上的空白\n# en: The Cartographers' Master Map has two great blanks: below the Restricted Section's chained shelf, and inside the seventh tower's wall. Inside each, once it's charted, there's a shortcut to find. Two lines on the map, and Corvin will ask you to keep it with him. The club's work turns up at the third gargoyle.\ntext [6903eb] = 制图社的总图上有两大块空白：禁书区锁链书架的下方，和第七塔的墙里面。每一块画出来之后，里面都还有一条近路可找。在总图上画下两条线，科尔文就会请你和他一起保管它。社里的事都在第三座石像鬼那儿出现。\n\n@clue lead_deep_stacks\n# en: The deep stacks\nthread [22aa07] = 深层书库\n# en: The stair under the chained shelf\nname [0cc21c] = 锁链书架下的楼梯\n# en: Below the last chained shelf of the Restricted Section's east stacks, by the book-return hatch, a stair goes down to stacks no lamp reaches, and to the confiscation vault. The Cartographers go at midnight.\ntext [ad6a3b] = 禁书区东区书库最后一排锁链书架的下方，还书口旁边，有一道楼梯通往灯光照不到的书库，还有没收库。制图社的人午夜去。\n\n@clue lead_tower_stair\n# en: The tower's wall stair\nthread [e55550] = 塔墙里的楼梯\n# en: The stair inside the wall\nname [398d40] = 墙里的楼梯\n# en: A servants' stair, one shoulder wide, climbs inside the seventh tower's wall from the cellars to the landing of candles. The Cartographers go at midnight.\ntext [5d0612] = 一道只容一肩的仆人楼梯，在第七塔的墙里从地窖一直通到蜡烛平台。制图社的人午夜去。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/factions.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/factions.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@faction wardens\n# en: The Wardens\nname [2a89ea] = 守望者\n# en: The headmistress's quiet circle, sworn to keep the founders' seal. They recruit students only when the seal grows thin.\ndesc [f1e201] = 院长身边一个安静的小圈子，誓守创始人的封印。只有在封印变薄时，他们才会招募学生。\n\n@faction hollow_cult\n# en: The Hollow Cult\nname [b781ad] = 空洞教团\n# en: Northern families, the Dumonts first among them, who serve the star beneath the lake. Their messenger is the man in grey.\ndesc [25011f] = 侍奉湖底之星的北境家族，杜蒙家首当其冲。他们的信使就是灰衣人。\n\n@faction midnight_cartographers\n# en: The Midnight Cartographers\nname [40c563] = 午夜制图社\n# en: The club that doesn't exist: students who map the castle's secret passages and share them with almost no one.\ndesc [14391b] = 那个不存在的社团：一群绘制城堡秘密通道、却几乎从不与人分享的学生。\n\n@faction faculty\n# en: The Faculty\nname [5b97ac] = 教职员\n# en: The professors and staff of Lumenhold: strict, eccentric, and mostly on your side whether you like it or not.\ndesc [645f6f] = 流明堡的教授与职员：严格、古怪，而且不管你乐不乐意，大多站在你这一边。\n\n@faction conclave\n# en: The Conclave of the Seven Towers\nname [c832a4] = 七塔议会\n# en: The governing body of magic in the realm, headquartered in the capital. Hands out research posts, licences and very long letters.\ndesc [76fd4f] = 王国魔法界的管理机构，总部设在王都。负责分配研究职位、颁发执照，以及寄来非常长的信。\n\n@faction thornwick\n# en: Thornwick Academy\nname [74b242] = 荆木学院\n# en: The rival school across the hills. Famous for illusionists, tournament grudges and spies who think they're subtle.\ndesc [28e5ca] = 山那边的对手学校。以幻术师、锦标赛积怨，以及自以为很隐蔽的间谍闻名。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/amaranth.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/amaranth.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact amaranth.healer\n# en: Runs the infirmary and teaches the healing half of Alchemy & Healing. Believes no excuse.\ntext [63b5e6] = 掌管医务室，教炼金与治愈里治愈的那一半。任何借口她都不信。\n\n@fact amaranth.truth\n# en: She treats the true version of an injury faster than the story you tell her, and she always knows which one you told.\ntext [76020a] = 你说出伤的真实来历，她治得比听你编的故事更快；而你说的是哪一种，她总是知道。\n# en: Tell her how you really got hurt, and see.\nhint [fee182] = 跟她说说你到底是怎么受的伤，看看会怎样。\n\n@fact amaranth.rounds\n# en: She does evening rounds of every bed herself, and has never once missed one.\ntext [8c9554] = 每晚的查房她都亲自走遍每张床，从未落下过一次。\n# en: Watch the infirmary at evening.\nhint [22747d] = 傍晚时留意一下医务室。\n\n@fact amaranth.key\n# en: Only a handful of students have ever been trusted with the key to her tonic cupboard.\ntext [5f5b57] = 被托付过她补剂柜钥匙的学生，屈指可数。\n# en: Some students are trusted with more than tonics.\nhint [1b0506] = 有些学生被托付的，不只是补药。\n\n@fact amaranth.count\n# en: She counts every remedy twice, because the second count is the true one, and keeps a black jar on the top shelf for a curse not seen since her first year.\ntext [ec82ec] = 每一样药她都要数两遍，因为第二遍才是准的；她在最高一层架子上留着一只黑罐子，为的是一种她第一年之后就再没见过的诅咒。\n# en: Help her restock over a break.\nhint [af1a5c] = 假期里帮她补货。\n\n@fact amaranth.cupboard\n# en: She leaves the tonic cupboard open at teatime on purpose, to see who will help themselves, and writes their names in a book.\ntext [1223c9] = 她故意在下午茶时间敞着补药柜，看谁会自己动手，再把他们的名字记进一本簿子里。\n# en: Look in the tonic cupboard at teatime.\nhint [41fc41] = 下午茶时去看看补药柜。\n\n@fact amaranth.book\n# en: She keeps a book of the remedies her students invented, written out in her own square hand.\ntext [3bfc5a] = 她有一本簿子，用她方正的字迹抄录着学生们自创的药方。\n# en: Invent a remedy under her eye.\nhint [3f7758] = 在她眼皮底下发明一种药。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/bram.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/bram.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact bram.prefect\n# en: A prefect of your own year, with a red sash, a ledger of demerits and a fondness for your name.\ntext [6bfca0] = 和你同年级的级长，系着红绶带，揣着一本记过簿，特别喜欢往上写你的名字。\n\n@fact bram.father\n# en: His father was head boy, and has told Bram all his life that he will never amount to anything.\ntext [414950] = 他父亲当年是男生学生会主席，一辈子都在告诉布拉姆他不会有出息。\n# en: Someone's opinion weighs on him more than any rule.\nhint [2f9e41] = 有个人的看法，比任何校规都压得他喘不过气。\n\n@fact bram.hall_tax\n# en: He once invented a \"hall tax\" for the east staircase.\ntext [67e9e9] = 他曾为东侧楼梯发明过一项“走廊税”。\n# en: Ask about the east staircase.\nhint [6d48c6] = 问问东侧楼梯的事。\n\n@fact bram.cheating\n# en: You saw him cheat in an exam. He knows you saw.\ntext [2e1460] = 你看见他考试作弊。他知道你看见了。\n# en: Prefects sit exams too.\nhint [72526b] = 级长也是要考试的。\n\n@fact bram.ally\n# en: Under the sash, he will step between you and trouble. Then he tells you not to make it weird.\ntext [e7ac5a] = 在那条绶带底下，他会挡在你和麻烦之间。然后叫你别把气氛搞得奇怪。\n# en: Under the sash, there may be someone else.\nhint [48a6c4] = 那条级长绶带底下，也许是另一个人。\n\n@fact bram.portrait\n# en: His father's portrait hangs in the corridor of head boys. Every visit, his father stops in front of it, and then looks at Bram.\ntext [f58601] = 他父亲的肖像挂在历届男生代表的走廊里。每次来访，他父亲都会在它面前停下，然后看向布拉姆。\n# en: Walk the corridor of head boys with him.\nhint [afb801] = 陪他走一趟历届男学生会长的画像长廊。\n\n@fact bram.rematch\n# en: When he loses, he practises alone on the duelling green every evening for a month, and then asks for a rematch.\ntext [f1ee7c] = 他一输，就会每天晚上独自在决斗草坪上练一个月，然后要求再比一场。\n# en: Beat him at something he cares about.\nhint [89661e] = 在他在乎的事上赢他一次。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/brask.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/brask.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact brask.beasts\n# en: Teaches Beastcraft & Flight from the stables, and talks to every creature in them more kindly than to students.\ntext [267a74] = 在马厩里教驭兽与飞行，对里面每一只生灵说话都比对学生和气。\n\n@fact brask.stories\n# en: Every one of her stories ends with a bite and a moral. She laughs out loud about once a term.\ntext [cbc9ea] = 她讲的每个故事都以被咬一口和一个道理收尾。她大约一学期大笑一次。\n# en: Let her tell you a story. Wait for the end.\nhint [2fc4b3] = 让她给你讲个故事。等到结尾。\n\n@fact brask.owl\n# en: The old barn owl in the stable loft has outlived three of her colleagues. She would not say which loss hurt most.\ntext [88c4c3] = 马厩阁楼上那只老仓鸮，比她的三位同事都活得长。哪一次失去最让她难过，她不肯说。\n# en: Visit the stable loft at night.\nhint [da15e2] = 夜里去马厩的草料阁楼看看。\n\n@fact brask.courtesies\n# en: She bows to the things that live in the Whisperwood, and believes it's why they have never bitten her. Much.\ntext [ce36dc] = 她会向住在低语森林里的东西鞠躬，并相信正因如此它们从没咬过她。基本没有。\n# en: She has manners for things that live in the wood.\nhint [7a78b9] = 她对林子里的东西自有一套礼数。\n\n@fact brask.feather\n# en: Her barn owl moulted in the Whisperwood one winter, and she looked for the lost flight feather for a month.\ntext [f6d3db] = 有一年冬天，她的仓鸮在低语森林里换羽，她找那根丢失的飞羽找了一个月。\n# en: She lost something in the Whisperwood one winter.\nhint [dcb4f1] = 有一年冬天，她在低语森林里丢了样东西。\n\n@fact brask.blackthorn\n# en: She knows a blackthorn wand by its smell, and she knows the woman in the wood who cuts them.\ntext [47d6a4] = 她闻一下就认得出黑刺李魔杖，也认得林子里削这种魔杖的那个女人。\n# en: She knows wands by more than their look.\nhint [0331d4] = 她认魔杖，靠的不只是眼睛。\n\n@fact brask.asking\n# en: She never thinks less of a student for asking for help. She just remembers that they asked.\ntext [cf0881] = 学生开口求助，她从不会因此看轻对方。她只是会记住对方开过口。\n# en: Ask her for a saddle.\nhint [99d9de] = 向她要一副鞍子。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/corvin.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/corvin.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact corvin.locks\n# en: Knows every locked door in the academy and owes money to at least three people.\ntext [8de015] = 学院里每一扇锁着的门他都知道，而且至少欠着三个人的钱。\n\n@fact corvin.last_warning\n# en: He is on his last warning. One more detention and they send him home, and home is not a good place.\ntext [b33ba6] = 他已经是最后一次警告了。再被罚一次禁闭，就会被送回家——而家，不是个好地方。\n# en: One more detention would cost him more than an evening.\nhint [1d211f] = 再多一次禁闭，他要付出的就不只是一个晚上了。\n\n@fact corvin.brother\n# en: His brother turns up for money and threatens to tell the headmistress where Corvin learned to pick locks.\ntext [bb32a8] = 他哥哥会找上门来要钱，还威胁要告诉院长科尔文是在哪儿学会撬锁的。\n# en: Not all of his family writes kindly.\nhint [80f55b] = 他的家人，并不都写客气的信。\n\n@fact corvin.grandfather\n# en: His grandfather's lucky deck sat in the confiscation vault for decades. It really is lucky.\ntext [26a338] = 他祖父的幸运牌在没收品库里躺了几十年。那副牌是真的幸运。\n# en: The confiscation vault keeps more than contraband.\nhint [3c9872] = 没收库里锁着的，不只是违禁品。\n\n@fact corvin.cartographers\n# en: He runs the Midnight Cartographers, the club that doesn't exist.\ntext [d80fd1] = 他是午夜制图社的头儿——那个不存在的社团。\n# en: Some clubs don't put up posters.\nhint [2083ec] = 有些社团从不贴招新告示。\n\n@fact corvin.trolls\n# en: He once owed thirty crowns to trolls and offered to pay you back with a goat.\ntext [976180] = 他曾欠巨魔三十克朗，还提出用一只山羊来还你钱。\n# en: Ask him about money, if you dare.\nhint [e56845] = 敢的话，问问他钱的事。\n\n@fact corvin.sundays\n# en: His grandfather could find a lost thing in any house, and taught him to shuffle on rainy Sundays. He's the one person Corvin talks about without a joke.\ntext [6974c6] = 他祖父能在任何房子里找到丢失的东西，还在下雨的星期天教他洗牌。那是科尔文唯一不开玩笑地谈起的人。\n# en: There's one person he talks about without a joke.\nhint [1634b5] = 有一个人，他提起时从不开玩笑。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/grimsby.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/grimsby.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact grimsby.books\n# en: Keeps the village bookshop. He will find anything except what you came in for.\ntext [034041] = 开着村里的书店。你要什么他都找得到，唯独找不到你进门要的那本。\n\n@fact grimsby.wrong\n# en: The book he gives you instead is, more often than not, the one you needed.\ntext [2be71b] = 他塞给你的那本“错书”，多半正是你需要的。\n# en: Ask him for a book, and take what he gives you.\nhint [b86dd1] = 向他要一本书，然后收下他给你的那本。\n\n@fact grimsby.parcels\n# en: He posts books to students he thinks need them, on credit, and has never once chased a debt.\ntext [5362f8] = 他会把他觉得学生需要的书赊账寄过去，从没催过一次债。\n# en: Some students get post from the bookshop.\nhint [18c12f] = 有些学生会收到书店寄来的包裹。\n\n@fact grimsby.lighthouses\n# en: He has a weakness for lighthouses, and when he closes the shop, it is usually to go and look at one.\ntext [f1d0ca] = 他对灯塔情有独钟；他关店的时候，通常就是去看某座灯塔了。\n# en: Ask where he goes when the shop is shut.\nhint [a343c5] = 问问他关店以后去哪儿。\n\n@fact grimsby.nose\n# en: He finds a book by its smell. The right book, he says, smells right.\ntext [f5d641] = 他凭气味找书。他说，对的书闻起来就是对的。\n# en: Let him choose for you, eyes shut.\nhint [c84c17] = 闭上眼睛，让他替你挑。\n\n@fact grimsby.upper_floor\n# en: Above the shop is a floor of remainders nobody mentions, up a ladder whose third and fourth rungs break.\ntext [6396e3] = 书店楼上有一层没人提起的滞销书，要爬一架梯子上去，梯子的第三和第四级都会断。\n# en: Climb the ladder at the back of the shop.\nhint [9b3cde] = 爬上店铺后面的梯子。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/grisk.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/grisk.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact grisk.lender\n# en: A goblin moneylender in a velvet waistcoat: sixty crowns today, twelve a month for six months.\ntext [023206] = 穿天鹅绒马甲的地精放贷人：今天六十克朗，之后六个月每月十二。\n\n@fact grisk.ledger\n# en: He calls debts \"friendships\", and keeps all of them in one ledger he never lets out of reach.\ntext [fa5464] = 他把债叫作“交情”，全都记在一本从不离手的账簿里。\n# en: Pass the time of day with him.\nhint [60fc73] = 跟他寒暄几句。\n\n@fact grisk.favours\n# en: He sometimes asks for favours instead of coin, and the favours are for clients he never names.\ntext [a745d9] = 他有时要人情而不要钱，而那些人情是替他从不透露姓名的客户讨的。\n# en: He doesn't always want coin.\nhint [15f768] = 他想要的，并不总是钱。\n\n@fact grisk.newt_table\n# en: He keeps the corner table at the Gilded Newt, and talks about money the way other people talk about weather: which shops are in trouble, and why.\ntext [797581] = 他占着镀金蝾螈酒馆角落的那张桌子，谈起钱来就像别人谈天气：哪些铺子要撑不住了，为什么。\n# en: Sit with him at the Newt more than once.\nhint [64043e] = 在蝾螈酒馆多陪他坐几次。\n\n@fact grisk.collectors\n# en: When a debt runs late, two trolls in waistcoats come to collect. They are polite, and very thorough.\ntext [ee77c7] = 债一拖欠，就会有两个穿马甲的巨魔上门收账。他们很有礼貌，也非常彻底。\n# en: Fall behind on a payment.\nhint [17b5fe] = 拖欠一次还款。\n\n@fact grisk.parcels\n# en: He runs parcels in oilcloth from the boathouse jetty at midnight to a cobbler's back door in the village. Nobody opens them.\ntext [954837] = 他在午夜把油布包着的包裹从船屋栈桥运到村里一个鞋匠的后门。从没人打开过。\n# en: He has work that only happens at midnight.\nhint [780f7e] = 他有些活儿只在午夜干。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/ilse.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/ilse.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact ilse.furs\n# en: Kael's younger sister: white furs, a private snowstorm and coins that freeze to counters.\ntext [e7eab1] = 凯尔的妹妹：一身白裘，一场随身的私人小暴风雪，还有会冻在柜台上的硬币。\n\n@fact ilse.raised\n# en: The Hollow cult raised her to open a door she has never seen.\ntext [a006d7] = 空洞教团把她养大，要她去打开一扇她从没见过的门。\n# en: Earn her trust, and she may tell you what she was raised for.\nhint [c5200e] = 赢得她的信任，她也许会告诉你，她是被养来做什么的。\n\n@fact ilse.frost_craft\n# en: She knows Northern frost-craft better than anything the professors teach.\ntext [ac2fad] = 她对北境霜术的了解，胜过教授们教的任何东西。\n# en: She might teach you, if you trade.\nhint [5d113c] = 如果你肯交换，她也许会教你。\n\n@fact ilse.grey\n# en: The man in grey came to take her home. She didn't call for help; she only looked.\ntext [f8b96d] = 灰衣人来带她回家。她没有呼救，只是看着。\n# en: Someone has come looking for her.\nhint [9efdd6] = 有人来找她了。\n\n@fact ilse.scared\n# en: She admits, to her brother, that she was scared. So was he.\ntext [fe62fb] = 她向哥哥承认，她当时很害怕。他也是。\n# en: Brother and sister still haven't really talked.\nhint [104b9f] = 兄妹俩还没有真正谈过。\n\n@fact ilse.stitch\n# en: The stitch in her frost-wards is her mother's. Ilse learned it from her; Kael knows it at a touch.\ntext [c30e43] = 她霜护符上的针法是她母亲的。伊尔泽跟母亲学的；凯尔一摸就认得出来。\n# en: Look closely at a frost-ward's thread.\nhint [ac66a0] = 仔细看看霜结界的线。\n\n@fact ilse.useless\n# en: She thinks the hardest thing to learn is something that isn't useful. She counts out loud in Northern when she plays.\ntext [174e31] = 她觉得最难学的是那些没用的东西。她玩的时候会用北地话大声数数。\n# en: Ask her to teach you something.\nhint [eb02ae] = 请她教你点什么。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/isolde.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/isolde.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact isolde.names\n# en: She remembers every student's name, and every student's mistakes.\ntext [00ea88] = 她记得每个学生的名字，也记得每个学生犯过的错。\n\n@fact isolde.portrait\n# en: As a student she set the observatory on fire twice. Her portrait from those days is hidden in a forgotten corridor.\ntext [389493] = 她当学生时，两次把观星台点着了。那时的肖像被藏在一条被遗忘的走廊里。\n# en: Look for the corridor nobody uses.\nhint [37657d] = 找找那条没人走的走廊。\n\n@fact isolde.chess\n# en: She plays tower chess by her window and laughs, short and delighted, when she loses.\ntext [29f019] = 她在窗边下水晶塔棋，输了的时候会短促而愉快地笑出声。\n# en: Visit her tower when she's at her window.\nhint [410856] = 趁她在窗边时，去她的塔上拜访。\n\n@fact isolde.thread\n# en: She has been following the same thread you have: the seal beneath the lake is weakening.\ntext [db9906] = 她一直在追查和你同一条线索：湖底的封印正在变弱。\n# en: She is following something too.\nhint [92e5c8] = 她也在追查某件事。\n\n@fact isolde.mercy\n# en: She asks students what they think is just, and sometimes she listens.\ntext [d24034] = 她会问学生，他们认为怎样才算公正——有时，她会听。\n# en: Ask her what she thinks is just.\nhint [e23f74] = 问问她，她认为什么才算公正。\n\n@fact isolde.vrell\n# en: She knows the name Absalom Vrell: expelled long before her time, and still in the ledger, crossed out.\ntext [d5c518] = 她知道阿布萨隆·弗雷尔这个名字：早在她的时代之前就被开除了，名字还留在名册上，被划掉了。\n# en: An old name is crossed out in the academy's ledger.\nhint [8dc7b4] = 学院的名册里，有个旧名字被划掉了。\n\n@fact isolde.really\n# en: She asks how your term is going, and then how it is really going, and waits for the second answer.\ntext [d71be9] = 她会问你这学期过得怎么样，再问你“真正”过得怎么样，然后等着第二个回答。\n# en: Take tea with her in her tower.\nhint [2d4f66] = 到她的塔里和她喝杯茶。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/kael.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/kael.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact kael.north\n# en: A transfer from the frozen North who arrived mid-term with frost in his eyelashes.\ntext [c365b9] = 来自冰封北境的转学生，学期中途抵达，睫毛上挂着霜。\n\n@fact kael.cold_hands\n# en: His hands are always cold. Nobody has ever been warm enough before.\ntext [4b3405] = 他的手总是冰凉。以前从没有人暖到足以让它们回温。\n# en: Notice his hands.\nhint [06c4ef] = 留意他的手。\n\n@fact kael.sky\n# en: He watches a patch of sky with no stars in it: a star that eats light.\ntext [1206d5] = 他凝望着一片没有星星的夜空：一颗吞噬光的星。\n# en: Find him on the roof at night.\nhint [b229e7] = 夜里去屋顶找他。\n\n@fact kael.family\n# en: His family serve something under the lake. They sent him to open its door; he came to stop them.\ntext [cfe636] = 他的家族侍奉着湖底的某样东西。他们派他来打开那扇门；他来，是为了阻止他们。\n# en: Earn his trust, and ask what he's running from.\nhint [568929] = 赢得他的信任，问他在逃避什么。\n\n@fact kael.sister\n# en: He has a younger sister, Ilse, raised to do what he wouldn't.\ntext [8def5c] = 他有一个妹妹，伊尔泽，被培养去做他不肯做的事。\n# en: He has family he doesn't mention.\nhint [e8a9f9] = 他有个从不提起的家人。\n\n@fact kael.mother\n# en: His mother never once said she was proud of him.\ntext [a4117c] = 他的母亲从没说过一次为他骄傲。\n# en: Under the northern lights, he might talk.\nhint [b87b11] = 在北极光下，他也许会开口。\n\n@fact kael.wanting\n# en: He was taught not to want things, because wanting is how they control you.\ntext [34ad8d] = 他被教导不要想要任何东西，因为想要，就是他们控制你的方式。\n# en: Some things he was taught not to say.\nhint [0c7802] = 有些话，他被教导过不能说。\n\n@fact kael.thaw\n# en: In the North nobody says goodbye; it's unlucky. You say \"until the thaw\" and touch foreheads, so the cold knows you're coming back.\ntext [1300cb] = 在北境没人说再见，那不吉利。要说“等到解冻”，再碰一下额头，好让寒冷知道你会回来。\n# en: Ask how the North says goodbye.\nhint [0d1e0f] = 问问北境的人怎么道别。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/lyra.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/lyra.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact lyra.prodigy\n# en: The pyromancy prodigy of House Vaneshade. Everyone knows the name, and she makes sure of it.\ntext [7f3f8a] = 凡妮希德家族的火焰术天才。人人都知道这个名字，她也确保人人都知道。\n\n@fact lyra.toast\n# en: She can incinerate a target at forty paces. She cannot make toast.\ntext [8eef62] = 她能在四十步外把靶子烧成灰。她不会烤面包片。\n# en: Catch her at breakfast.\nhint [cbaba1] = 早饭时逮住她。\n\n@fact lyra.mother\n# en: Her mother has never once told her \"well done\".\ntext [a56886] = 她的母亲从没对她说过一句“做得好”。\n# en: Get closer, and ask about her letters home.\nhint [b0f195] = 跟她再熟一些，问问她寄回家的信。\n\n@fact lyra.second_place\n# en: She came second in the Winter Trials once. Her mother wrote to ask who beat her.\ntext [c586c0] = 她在冬季试炼中得过一次第二名。她母亲写信来问是谁赢了她。\n# en: Ask her about the Winter Trials.\nhint [dc6f35] = 问问她冬季考核的事。\n\n@fact lyra.burnout\n# en: She studies until she collapses, and begs you not to let her mother find out.\ntext [8e25bd] = 她会一直学到昏过去，还求你别让她母亲知道。\n# en: Watch how late her lamp burns.\nhint [a8cd57] = 看看她的灯亮到多晚。\n\n@fact lyra.rival\n# en: You are the only person here who makes her better. She hates it. She doesn't want it to stop.\ntext [049900] = 你是这里唯一能让她变得更好的人。她恨这一点。她不想让它停下。\n# en: Be the rival she can't shake.\nhint [ac2524] = 做她甩不掉的那个对手。\n\n@fact lyra.inheritance\n# en: Her mother threatened her inheritance over you. She set the letter on fire.\ntext [b8b62f] = 她母亲为了你，拿她的继承权来威胁她。她把那封信烧了。\n# en: Her mother may have something to say about you.\nhint [418d0a] = 她母亲也许对你有话要说。\n\n@fact lyra.wildfire\n# en: Vaneshades sleep exactly eight hours; she can't sleep. Her grandmother's spell, Wildfire, was only for people she trusted, and Lyra has never had anyone to teach it to.\ntext [981a53] = 凡妮希德家的人睡足整整八个小时；她却睡不着。她祖母的咒语“野火”只传给信得过的人，而莱拉从来没有可以传授的人。\n# en: Get truly close to her.\nhint [46a4dd] = 和她真正亲近起来。\n\n@fact lyra.flourish\n# en: Her family has opened its duels with the same ribbon-of-flame flourish for two hundred years. Teaching it outside the family is not done.\ntext [4802ff] = 两百年来，她家决斗开场都用同一个火焰缎带起手式。把它教给外人，是不合规矩的。\n# en: Ask her to teach you something of her family's.\nhint [68d362] = 请她教你一点她家传的东西。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/mabel.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/mabel.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact mabel.teashop\n# en: Keeps the tea shop in the village: forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake.\ntext [c5dd16] = 在村里开茶馆：四十种茶，三种蛋糕。\n\n@fact mabel.door\n# en: She reads your mood from the way you open her door, and is rarely wrong.\ntext [33ce2b] = 看你怎么推她的门，就知道你的心情，而且很少看错。\n# en: Open her door in a mood.\nhint [b6afb9] = 带着心事推开她的门。\n\n@fact mabel.tins\n# en: She sends tins of tea up to the castle on the barge for students she's worried about, and refuses payment for them.\ntext [390792] = 她会用驳船把一罐罐茶送上城堡，给她放心不下的学生，而且坚决不收钱。\n# en: Some students get tea on the barge.\nhint [d3daa2] = 有些学生会收到驳船捎来的茶。\n\n@fact mabel.cups\n# en: The cups on the hooks behind her counter belong to her regulars, living and dead. She remembers every one of them.\ntext [3e39fe] = 她柜台后挂钩上的杯子属于她的常客，有活着的，也有已故的。每一位她都记得。\n# en: Ask about the cups behind her counter.\nhint [035f7a] = 问问她柜台后面那些杯子。\n\n@fact mabel.petal\n# en: Calls everyone \"petal\", and means it more for some than others.\ntext [87afbf] = 管谁都叫“小可爱”，对有些人叫得格外真心。\n\n@fact mabel.fourth_cake\n# en: Beside her three cakes there is sometimes a fourth, experimental and free, and she keeps a card on each: its name, the date, and the survivors.\ntext [b6fad2] = 她那三种蛋糕旁边有时会多出第四种，试验品，免费，每一种她都配一张卡片：名字、日期，还有幸存者。\n# en: Be brave about the cake.\nhint [d202b2] = 对那块蛋糕勇敢一点。\n\n@fact mabel.range\n# en: Her range goes out whenever the wind is in the north. Anyone who helps her light it is \"staff, near enough\", and carries the milk.\ntext [8f6117] = 只要刮北风，她的炉灶就会熄。谁帮她点着炉子，谁就“差不多算是店里的人了”，还得帮着搬牛奶。\n# en: Come in on a cold morning.\nhint [a64346] = 在一个寒冷的早上进店。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/marisol.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/marisol.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact marisol.tides\n# en: Teaches the tidecalling half of Evocation, waist-deep in the lake at dawn, and keeps the lake calm on exam days.\ntext [5356ef] = 教塑能术里唤潮的那一半，黎明时站在齐腰深的湖水里上课；考试那几天由她让湖面平静。\n\n@fact marisol.poems\n# en: She reads poems about water in the rain, under a dry patch of air, and never remembers their last lines.\ntext [936059] = 她在雨里、在一小片干燥的空气下读写水的诗，却从来记不住最后一行。\n# en: Walk with her in the rain.\nhint [623b9d] = 下雨时陪她走一走。\n\n@fact marisol.storms\n# en: She goes out into the worst storms of the year on purpose. She says nobody learns tides on a calm day.\ntext [2d8f05] = 她专挑一年里最猛的暴风雨出门。她说风平浪静的日子里，谁也学不会潮汐。\n# en: Find her on the worst day of the year.\nhint [eba8cf] = 在一年中最糟的那天去找她。\n\n@fact marisol.margins\n# en: She wrote her own tidecalling into the margins of a book of poems when she was a student, and still teaches from it.\ntext [2b7267] = 她还是学生时，把自己的唤潮术写在一本诗集的页边，至今仍照着它教。\n# en: Her teaching comes from an old book.\nhint [7c2601] = 她教的东西，出自一本旧书。\n\n@fact marisol.ice\n# en: She stays in the lake when there is ice at the edges, and whoops like a child whenever a student's water rises.\ntext [922a9d] = 湖边结了冰她也照样待在湖里，学生的水一升起来，她就像孩子一样欢呼。\n# en: Pass by when she is in the lake.\nhint [873c5e] = 在她泡在湖里的时候路过。\n\n@fact marisol.spirits\n# en: She says the lake's water spirits always cheat, and that asking them nicely not to is the whole point of tidecalling.\ntext [e49b8e] = 她说湖里的水灵总是作弊，而客客气气地请它们别作弊，正是潮汐术的全部要义。\n# en: Swim her last test.\nhint [e79d1c] = 游完她的最后一场考验。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/maud.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/maud.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact maud.groundskeeper\n# en: Groundskeeper and hedge-witch, keeper of bees, chickens and a goat called Bartholomew.\ntext [54cb93] = 园丁兼乡野女巫，养蜂、养鸡，还有一只叫巴塞洛缪的山羊。\n\n@fact maud.student\n# en: She was a student here sixty years ago, the spring the lake froze.\ntext [5b3f12] = 六十年前她也是这里的学生——就在湖面结冰的那个春天。\n# en: Ask her what the lake was like, long ago.\nhint [371df6] = 问问她，很久以前的湖是什么样子。\n\n@fact maud.edmund\n# en: Edmund went down beneath the lake that night and didn't come back. She kept his silver wand.\ntext [624c6b] = 那天夜里埃德蒙下到湖底，再没回来。她一直留着他的银魔杖。\n# en: She keeps a wand that isn't hers.\nhint [5f8d60] = 她收着一根不属于她的魔杖。\n\n@fact maud.soup\n# en: Her soup cures colds, broken hearts and most curses. You're not allowed to write it down.\ntext [395a7c] = 她的汤能治感冒、心碎和大多数诅咒。不许写下来。\n# en: Ask for her recipe. You won't get it.\nhint [85b303] = 向她讨食谱。你讨不到的。\n\n@fact maud.garden\n# en: Behind the potting shed there is a door to a garden that only exists for people she shows it to.\ntext [408faa] = 盆栽棚后面有一扇门，通往一座只为她领进去的人而存在的花园。\n# en: Look behind the potting shed.\nhint [075962] = 去盆栽棚后面看看。\n\n@fact maud.age\n# en: She is eighty-one, and says she's allowed the occasional chill.\ntext [601a9c] = 她八十一岁了，说自己偶尔着个凉是被允许的。\n# en: Visit when she's under the weather.\nhint [c44d19] = 趁她身子不爽时去看看她。\n\n@fact maud.tuppence\n# en: In her first winter as groundskeeper she raised a hatchling spider called Tuppence in the potting shed. Tuppence is the Spinner of the Hush now.\ntext [238ba6] = 她当园丁的头一个冬天，在育苗棚里养大了一只叫塔彭丝的蜘蛛幼崽。如今塔彭丝就是寂境里的织网者。\n# en: Ask about her first winter as groundskeeper.\nhint [80dec3] = 问问她当园丁的头一个冬天。\n\n@fact maud.blazes\n# en: The little bees carved on the Whisperwood's birches are hers, from when she used to go down to the Hush to visit Tuppence. Her knees won't take it now.\ntext [15bfaf] = 低语森林桦树上刻的那些小蜜蜂是她刻的，那时她常下到寂境去看塔彭丝。如今她的膝盖受不了了。\n# en: Someone marked the old way into the Whisperwood.\nhint [345c20] = 有人在通往低语森林的老路上做过记号。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/mir.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/mir.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact mir.artificer\n# en: An artificer who smells of solder and ozone and has started at least one small fire.\ntext [6f21ba] = 一个浑身焊锡和臭氧味的奇械师，至少引发过一场小火灾。\n\n@fact mir.owl\n# en: Built a clockwork owl that remembers deadlines, birthdays and, slightly, grudges.\ntext [841167] = 造过一只发条猫头鹰，能记住截止日期、生日，还有一点点的仇。\n# en: Ask what their clockwork owl remembers.\nhint [1dd2b1] = 问问那只发条猫头鹰都记得些什么。\n\n@fact mir.notebooks\n# en: Three years of notebooks are the most precious things they own.\ntext [9a3403] = 三年的笔记本是 TA 最珍贵的东西。\n# en: Get close enough to be shown the notebooks.\nhint [11d04f] = 熟到能让他们给你看那些笔记本。\n\n@fact mir.family\n# en: Their family runs a clockworks and thinks invention is a hobby. It is not a hobby.\ntext [91fa32] = TA 家开着一间钟表工坊，觉得发明只是个爱好。这不是爱好。\n# en: Ask about the family business.\nhint [c0a040] = 问问家里的生意。\n\n@fact mir.name_day\n# en: Nobody ever remembered their name day before.\ntext [40158a] = 以前从没有人记得 TA 的命名日。\n# en: Find out when their name day is.\nhint [f96769] = 打听一下他们的命名日是哪天。\n\n@fact mir.workshop\n# en: After the third fire in a term, the faculty padlocked their workshop.\ntext [14af34] = 一个学期里第三次失火之后，教职员给 TA 的工坊上了挂锁。\n# en: Count the fires this term.\nhint [5329ce] = 数数这学期着了几次火。\n\n@fact mir.corridor\n# en: Locked out of a workshop, they will solder on the corridor floor for a week rather than stop. It's character-building, they say.\ntext [be7fd4] = 被锁在工坊外面，TA 宁可在走廊地板上焊一个星期，也不肯停下。TA 说，这能磨炼品格。\n# en: See what they do when the door is locked.\nhint [d51d70] = 看看门锁上时 TA 会怎么做。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/nessa.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/nessa.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact nessa.editor\n# en: Editor of the Lumen Ledger, with a quill that writes on its own.\ntext [e5df5e] = 《流明纪事报》的主编，有一支会自己写字的羽毛笔。\n\n@fact nessa.quill\n# en: Left unsupervised, her quill starts inventing the news.\ntext [e4c012] = 一旦没人看着，她的羽毛笔就会开始编新闻。\n# en: Watch her quill when she isn't.\nhint [3b6655] = 趁她不注意时看看她的羽毛笔。\n\n@fact nessa.interview\n# en: Nobody has interviewed the headmistress in twenty years. She means to be the first.\ntext [0599a6] = 二十年来没有人采访过院长。她打算做第一个。\n# en: Ask her what story she wants most.\nhint [165dd2] = 问问她最想写哪篇报道。\n\n@fact nessa.string\n# en: Her office wall is covered in red string around a sketch of a black star.\ntext [98be58] = 她办公室的墙上缠满了红线，正中间是一颗黑色星星的草图。\n# en: See what's pinned to her office wall.\nhint [119f0a] = 看看她办公室墙上钉着什么。\n\n@fact nessa.press\n# en: She would chain herself to the printing press before letting anyone shut the Ledger.\ntext [c72444] = 谁要是想关掉《流明纪事报》，她宁可把自己锁在印刷机上。\n# en: See what happens if someone threatens the Ledger.\nhint [17f3b4] = 看看要是有人威胁《纪事报》会怎样。\n\n@fact nessa.off_record\n# en: The only time her quill stops is when she tells it the conversation is off the record.\ntext [9ac8f2] = 她的羽毛笔唯一停下的时候，是她告诉它这段对话不作记录。\n# en: There's one phrase that stops her quill.\nhint [d9c900] = 有一句话能让她的羽毛笔停下。\n\n@fact nessa.planted\n# en: She plants one error in every proof she asks you to check, to see whether you'll find it.\ntext [11da12] = 她让你校对的每一份校样里，都埋着一个错误，看你能不能找到。\n# en: Check her proofs for her.\nhint [e3aa57] = 替她校对校样。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/pell.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/pell.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact pell.apothecary\n# en: Runs the drawers at Pell & Daughters. He taught three generations of the village to count drops.\ntext [0b856c] = 管着佩尔父女药铺的抽屉。他教会了村里三代人数药滴。\n\n@fact pell.counting\n# en: He makes every new clerk count aloud. He trusts people who measure twice.\ntext [4c5e9c] = 每个新店员都得被他逼着大声数数。他信得过量两遍的人。\n# en: Count with him.\nhint [8ac258] = 陪他一起数。\n\n@fact pell.daughters\n# en: His daughters run the counter and want the shop to change; he wants it to be fifty years ago. They're all secretly proud of each other.\ntext [4a27be] = 女儿们管柜台，想让铺子变一变；他却希望一切停在五十年前。其实三个人都暗暗以彼此为傲。\n# en: Meet the daughters behind the counter.\nhint [37802b] = 见见柜台后面那两个女儿。\n\n@fact pell.spoon\n# en: His brass measuring spoon hasn't been wrong in forty years. His daughters learned to measure on it, and say it's theirs.\ntext [dc3a46] = 他那把黄铜量勺四十年没出过错。他的女儿们是用它学会量药的，她们说勺子是她们的。\n# en: Ask about the brass spoon.\nhint [b88cb2] = 问问那把黄铜量勺。\n\n@fact pell.oldest_drawer\n# en: His oldest drawer holds a yellowed card for a draught \"of your own strength\", in a hand older than his. He has labelled it \"Not yet.\"\ntext [72e03f] = 他最老的那个抽屉里放着一张发黄的卡片，是一种“以己之力”的药剂，字迹比他的还要古老。他给它贴的标签是“还不行。”\n# en: Restock the bottom of his wall.\nhint [bf9dc7] = 去补他那面墙最底下的货。\n\n@fact pell.humming\n# en: Some of his thousand drawers hum. He relabels them himself, in a precise hand: ASK FIRST, NOT TODAY.\ntext [924948] = 他那上千个抽屉里有些会嗡嗡响。他亲自用一手工整的字给它们重贴标签：先问、今天不行。\n# en: Listen to his drawers.\nhint [e85071] = 听听他的抽屉。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/seraphine.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/seraphine.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact seraphine.seer\n# en: A seer who speaks in half-sentences and is unsettlingly often right.\ntext [8fee9c] = 一个说话总说半句的先知，准得叫人心里发毛。\n\n@fact seraphine.slips\n# en: Sometimes she slips a day ahead and needs someone to tell her what today is.\ntext [6bdae8] = 有时她会滑到明天去，需要有人告诉她今天是哪一天。\n# en: Sometimes she isn't quite in today.\nhint [113880] = 有时候，她不太在“今天”里。\n\n@fact seraphine.oracle\n# en: Her mother is the Oracle of Vell, who wants her home to tell kings what they want to hear.\ntext [e701e0] = 她的母亲是维尔神谕，想让她回家，去对国王们说他们爱听的话。\n# en: Get close, and ask about home.\nhint [51f62b] = 跟她熟起来，问问她的家。\n\n@fact seraphine.self\n# en: Seers can't see themselves, unless someone is standing next to them.\ntext [90e477] = 先知看不见自己——除非有人站在他们身边。\n# en: Stand next to her while she paints.\nhint [46c550] = 她画画时，站在她身边。\n\n@fact seraphine.lake\n# en: The lake calls to her at night. She says it calls to you too.\ntext [37ecd3] = 夜里，湖在呼唤她。她说，它也在呼唤你。\n# en: Ask what she hears at night.\nhint [6ba648] = 问问她夜里听见了什么。\n\n@fact seraphine.too_loud\n# en: When everyone's futures get too loud she hides in the dark. Yours, she says, is quiet.\ntext [9cdeb7] = 当所有人的未来吵得太响时，她会躲进黑暗里。她说，你的未来很安静。\n# en: Find out where she hides.\nhint [a3ca00] = 弄清楚她躲在哪里。\n\n@fact seraphine.paintings\n# en: What she sees ahead comes out in her paintings, sometimes months early. She hides the ones she didn't mean to paint under her bed.\ntext [18b183] = 她预见的东西会出现在她的画里，有时早上好几个月。她不是有意画出的那些，都藏在床底下。\n# en: Look under her bed. Or ask.\nhint [34b5b0] = 看看她床底下。或者直接问。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/tamsin.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/tamsin.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact tamsin.thornwick\n# en: An illusion student at Thornwick, in your year, who writes to you.\ntext [c2878b] = 荆木学院幻术专业的学生，与你同年级，一直给你写信。\n\n@fact tamsin.theatre\n# en: Their grandmother runs a travelling theatre; they learned illusion painting her backdrops.\ntext [93eb25] = TA 的祖母经营一个巡回剧团；TA 是在给她画布景时学会幻术的。\n# en: Keep writing back.\nhint [c47e9f] = 继续给他们回信。\n\n@fact tamsin.assigned\n# en: Thornwick told them to write to a Lumenhold student and report back. They stopped reporting by the third letter.\ntext [3ef61a] = 荆木学院让 TA 写信给一名流明堡学生，并回去汇报。到第三封信时，TA 就不再汇报了。\n# en: Ask why they wrote to you in the first place.\nhint [da8702] = 问问他们当初为什么给你写信。\n\n@fact tamsin.pie\n# en: Their roommate bet them a pie that you'd never write back. They won the pie.\ntext [490b59] = TA的室友跟TA打赌，说你永远不会回信。TA赢了一个馅饼。\n# en: Answer their second letter.\nhint [135c4f] = 回TA的第二封信。\n\n@fact tamsin.sketches\n# en: They fill their letters with sketches of Thornwick's professors in the margins. Their illusion professor is usually a goat.\ntext [6306eb] = TA在信的页边画满了荆木教授们的速写。TA的幻术教授通常是一只山羊。\n# en: Let them tell you the Thornwick gossip.\nhint [85e3d5] = 让TA讲讲荆木的八卦。\n\n@fact tamsin.failed\n# en: They once failed their illusion examination, the one thing they were good at, in the same term their grandmother's theatre was sold.\ntext [8405fc] = TA曾经在幻术考试中失利，那是TA唯一擅长的东西，而就在同一个学期，TA祖母的剧团被卖掉了。\n# en: Read the short letter.\nhint [3743b2] = 读那封很短的信。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/thorne.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/thorne.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact thorne.storm\n# en: Rumoured to have once out-duelled a storm.\ntext [fa9226] = 据说他曾在决斗中打败过一场风暴。\n\n@fact thorne.lost\n# en: He lost to the storm, badly. Losing well, he says, is the only thing worth teaching.\ntext [f094e4] = 他输给了那场风暴，输得很惨。他说，好好地输，是唯一值得教的东西。\n# en: Ask about the storm he fought.\nhint [f30ce3] = 问问他打过的那场风暴。\n\n@fact thorne.roses\n# en: He tends a tiny walled rose garden behind the observatory at dawn and talks to the roses.\ntext [ec7ccc] = 黎明时分，他在观星台后面一座小小的围墙玫瑰园里打理花草，还对玫瑰说话。\n# en: Walk past the observatory at dawn.\nhint [b98b34] = 黎明时从观星台旁走过。\n\n@fact thorne.hands\n# en: His hands have started to shake. Age comes for every storm eventually.\ntext [95c9a9] = 他的手开始发抖了。岁月终究会找上每一场风暴。\n# en: Watch his hands in class.\nhint [b358fd] = 上课时留意他的手。\n\n@fact thorne.rival\n# en: A flamboyant old wizard in purple has wanted a rematch with him for forty years.\ntext [054b17] = 一个穿紫袍、作派浮夸的老巫师，四十年来一直想跟他再比一场。\n# en: Someone has wanted a rematch for forty years.\nhint [950f44] = 有人想跟他再比一场，已经等了四十年。\n\n@fact thorne.letters\n# en: He writes very few letters of recommendation, and the Conclave reads every one.\ntext [067d68] = 他很少写推荐信，而七塔议会每一封都会读。\n# en: Earn his respect. He writes very few letters.\nhint [69cf4b] = 赢得他的尊重。他很少写推荐信。\n\n@fact thorne.accusation\n# en: \"You're better than this\" is, from him, not a compliment. He says so himself: it's an accusation.\ntext [3a7876] = “你比这强”，从他嘴里说出来，并不是夸奖。他自己就这么说过：这是一种指控。\n# en: Disappoint him.\nhint [246694] = 让他失望一次。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/tobias.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/tobias.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact tobias.scholarship\n# en: A farmer's son on a turnip scholarship, built like a barn door and gentle as a lamb.\ntext [2b6d69] = 一个拿芜菁奖学金的农家子弟，块头像谷仓门，性子像小羊羔。\n\n@fact tobias.plants\n# en: He talks to plants. Worryingly, they seem to answer.\ntext [e42ce7] = 他会跟植物说话。令人担心的是，它们似乎会回答。\n\n@fact tobias.ma\n# en: His mother runs the farm alone at harvest. It's the first harvest he has ever missed.\ntext [b4dd4f] = 收获季节，他母亲一个人打理农场。这是他第一次错过收获。\n# en: Ask about harvest at home.\nhint [a2ef64] = 问问他家里的收成。\n\n@fact tobias.gerald\n# en: He is raising a mandrake called Gerald, who wears a knitted hat and screams softly.\ntext [f5ffc4] = 他在养一株叫杰拉尔德的曼德拉草，戴着一顶针织小帽，会轻声尖叫。\n# en: Ask about the mandrake in the hat.\nhint [1205f8] = 问问那棵戴帽子的曼德拉草。\n\n@fact tobias.moonflowers\n# en: The greenhouse moonflowers bloom once every eleven years. He would stand in a storm for them.\ntext [047bca] = 温室里的月光花每十一年才开一次。为了它们，他愿意站在暴风雨里。\n# en: Look in the greenhouse at night.\nhint [7995d0] = 夜里去温室看看。\n\n@fact tobias.blight\n# en: Blight took his family's harvest. Thirty crowns stood between them and a hungry winter.\ntext [15f8ba] = 枯萎病毁了他家的收成。他们和一个挨饿的冬天之间，只隔着三十克朗。\n# en: Home may be having a bad year.\nhint [3fa934] = 家里今年也许过得不好。\n\n@fact tobias.dancing\n# en: He practised waltzing with a broom before the Midwinter Ball. The broom complained.\ntext [3db4ab] = 冬至舞会前，他拿一把扫帚练华尔兹。扫帚提出了抗议。\n# en: Ask him about the Midwinter Ball.\nhint [93f4a0] = 问问他仲冬舞会的事。\n\n@fact tobias.after\n# en: His mother expects him home on the farm after graduation. It was always the plan; he isn't sure any more that it's his.\ntext [850e1d] = 他母亲指望他毕业后回农场。这一直是计划；他已经不确定这是不是他自己的计划了。\n# en: Ask what he'll do after graduation.\nhint [07dbcd] = 问问他毕业以后打算做什么。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/facts/vey.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/facts/vey.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@fact vey.illusion\n# en: Teaches Illusion & Glamour and grades on whether you fooled her.\ntext [c1fb18] = 教幻术与魅影，按你骗没骗过她来打分。\n\n@fact vey.tests\n# en: She tests students in corridors, unannounced, with tea that isn't there. She keeps score.\ntext [60f4e6] = 她会在走廊里突击考学生，用并不存在的茶。她一直在记分。\n# en: Expect a question in a corridor.\nhint [dfcbaf] = 当心在走廊里被提问。\n\n@fact vey.revue\n# en: She produces the faculty revue every year, and has never once let Professor Thorne refuse a part.\ntext [d5edf4] = 她每年都负责排演教职员汇演，从没让索恩教授推掉过一个角色。\n# en: Ask about the faculty revue.\nhint [c5140f] = 问问教职工的年度演出。\n\n@fact vey.mirror\n# en: She doesn't use the mirror charm in public. Once, her reflection didn't come back for a week.\ntext [950435] = 她从不在人前用镜像咒。有一次，她的倒影整整一周没回来。\n# en: Ask why she never uses the mirror charm.\nhint [b56b0d] = 问问她为什么从不用镜像咒。\n\n@fact vey.smell\n# en: She teaches that sight is easy to fool; smell is where liars are caught.\ntext [e9a79d] = 她教导说，骗过眼睛很容易；骗子是在气味上露馅的。\n# en: Bake something false in her class.\nhint [d8bd09] = 在她课上烤点假东西。\n\n@fact vey.own_face\n# en: She has every student glamour their own face first: a glamour you can't hold on yourself, she says, won't hold on anyone.\ntext [fb13dd] = 她让每个学生先给自己的脸施幻术：她说，自己身上都撑不住的幻术，放到谁身上也撑不住。\n# en: Change one small thing about yourself, in her class.\nhint [83587e] = 在她课上，改变自己身上的一个小地方。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/items.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/items.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@item borrowed_wand\n# en: Borrowed Wand\nname [d1a14d] = 借来的魔杖\n# en: The academy's loan to every first-year: scuffed beech, a chipped tip and somebody else's initials on the handle. It works. Mostly.\ndesc [0cbc2c] = 学院借给每个一年级生的：磨花的山毛榉，崩了一角的杖尖，杖柄上刻着别人的姓名缩写。能用。大部分时候。\n\n@item hazel_wand\n# en: Hazelwood Wand\nname [60934a] = 榛木魔杖\n# en: Mir's work: light, springy hazel with a copper band. It warms up before you do.\ndesc [ab427a] = 米尔的手艺：轻巧有弹性的榛木，箍着一圈铜。它比你先热起来。\n\n@item ashwood_wand\n# en: Ashwood Duelling Wand\nname [47421e] = 白蜡木决斗魔杖\n# en: A duellist's wand, dense and unforgiving. Anything that tries to block it gets a shock for its trouble.\ndesc [c99d2d] = 决斗者的魔杖，沉实，毫不留情。谁想挡它，谁就会被狠狠震一下。\n\n@item heartwood_wand\n# en: Heartwood Wand\nname [9cc254] = 心材魔杖\n# en: Carved by Mir from a splinter the elder oak gave you. The grain moves when no one is looking.\ndesc [033f3b] = 米尔用古橡送你的木片雕成。没人看的时候，木纹会动。\n\n@item edmunds_silver_wand\n# en: Edmund's Silver Wand\nname [52050b] = 埃德蒙的银魔杖\n# en: Slender silver, sixty years old, wrapped for decades in a faded scarf. Near the lake it hums one low note.\ndesc [b73a9d] = 纤细的银杖，六十岁了，在一条褪色的围巾里裹了几十年。靠近湖边时，它会哼出一个低音。\n\n@item thorn_wand\n# en: Thorn Wand\nname [881a54] = 荆棘魔杖\n# en: Blackthorn, still bristling, still faintly green at the core. It wants to be used, and it does not much care for what.\ndesc [f9b9f8] = 黑刺李木，尖刺仍在，芯里还泛着一点绿。它想被使用，至于用来做什么，它不太在乎。\n# en: {~The thorn wand pricks your palm when you pick it up, as if it were keeping count.|You dream of brambles growing over a door. The thorn wand is warm in the morning.}\ntick.say.1 [94b5cd] = {~你拿起荆棘魔杖时，它扎了一下你的掌心，像在记数。|你梦见荆棘爬满了一扇门。早上醒来，荆棘魔杖是温的。}\n\n@item school_robes\n# en: School Robes\nname [d3931a] = 校袍\n# en: Regulation black with the house trim, a size too big in the sleeves. Everyone has a pair.\ndesc [1e3de2] = 按规定的黑色，镶着学院边，袖子大了一号。人人都有一套。\n\n@item duelling_coat\n# en: Quilted Duelling Coat\nname [d80576] = 绗缝决斗外套\n# en: Padded, stitched in diamonds, smelling faintly of the duelling hall. Bracing in it feels like leaning on a wall.\ndesc [8a4280] = 夹着棉，缝成菱格，隐约有决斗厅的味道。穿着它坚守，像靠在一堵墙上。\n\n@item ward_cloak\n# en: Ward-Stitched Cloak\nname [3c238e] = 护界绣斗篷\n# en: Amaranth's ward thread runs through the hem in a pattern too fine to see. Fire and frost slide off it like rain.\ndesc [253e8a] = 阿玛兰斯修女的护界线织进了下摆，花纹细得看不见。火与霜从上面滑落，就像雨水。\n\n@item midnight_robes\n# en: Midnight-Blue Robes\nname [1d09a3] = 午夜蓝长袍\n# en: Deep blue wool with silver trim and a lining like the night sky. People stand up a little straighter when you walk in.\ndesc [0219e6] = 深蓝羊毛，镶银边，里衬像夜空。你一走进来，大家都会把腰板挺直一点。\n\n@item spinner_silk_robe\n# en: Spinner-Silk Robe\nname [4b8892] = 织网者丝袍\n# en: Grey silk that drinks the light, sewn by the village tailor from what the Spinner left you. You move in it like a rumour.\ndesc [e3e7a0] = 吸光的灰丝，由小镇裁缝用织网者留给你的丝缝成。穿上它，你走动起来像一则传言。\n\n@item ilse_frost_ward\n# en: Ilse's Frost-Ward\nname [302a95] = 伊尔泽的霜护符\n# en: A sliver of Northern winter that Ilse sewed into your collar. Cold things think twice about you.\ndesc [ca24da] = 伊尔泽缝进你衣领里的一小片北境寒冬。冰冷的东西靠近你之前会再想一想。\n\n@item black_star_coin\n# en: Black-Star Coin\nname [48124c] = 黑星硬币\n# en: Black, cold as ice and heavier than it should be, stamped with a hollow star. You keep meaning to throw it away.\ndesc [710aa7] = 漆黑，冰冷，比它该有的分量更沉，上面压着一颗空心的星。你一直打算把它扔掉。\n\n@item lodestone_pendant\n# en: Lodestone Pendant\nname [08ddaa] = 磁石吊坠\n# en: A dark stone on a leather cord that turns, very slowly, to face north. In the Whisperwood it is never wrong.\ndesc [08b15b] = 皮绳上挂着一块暗色的石头，会非常缓慢地转向北方。在低语森林里，它从不出错。\n\n@item stag_tine\n# en: Stag's Tine\nname [86432e] = 鹿角尖\n# en: A tine shed by the shadow stag, smooth as old ivory. Wild things look at it before they look at you.\ndesc [3c8e61] = 影鹿脱落的一截角尖，光滑得像旧象牙。野生的东西会先看它，再看你。\n\n@item owl_quill\n# en: Owl-Feather Quill\nname [4a647e] = 猫头鹰羽毛笔\n# en: A barn-owl feather cut to a nib. Notes taken with it are tidier than the hand that wrote them.\ndesc [565ab9] = 一根仓鸮羽毛削成的笔。用它记的笔记，比写字的手还要工整。\n\n@item moonpetal_locket\n# en: Moonpetal Locket\nname [9f9cf2] = 月瓣吊盒\n# en: A plain silver locket with one pressed moonpetal inside. It opens on its own at midnight.\ndesc [4c3806] = 一只朴素的银吊盒，里面夹着一片压干的月瓣花。到了午夜，它会自己打开。\n\n@item pocket_almanac\n# en: Pocket Almanac\nname [b50a94] = 袖珍年历\n# en: Tides, moons and weather for fifty years, in print you need a lens for. Grimsby's pencil notes run down every margin.\ndesc [20f75c] = 五十年的潮汐、月相和天气，字小得要用放大镜看。格林斯比的铅笔批注写满了每一页的页边。\n\n@item measuring_spoon\n# en: Pell's Measuring Spoon\nname [64ca3a] = 佩尔的量勺\n# en: A brass spoon worn thin on one side by forty years of exact quarters. Pell's daughters say it is theirs.\ndesc [f96014] = 一把黄铜勺，四十年来精确地量了无数个四分之一，一侧都磨薄了。佩尔的女儿们说这是她们的。\n\n@item honey_tin\n# en: Honey Tin\nname [516547] = 蜂蜜罐\n# en: A dented tin of Maud's silver-bee honey. Shared, it opens doors; the bees seem to know it is yours.\ndesc [812c30] = 一只磕瘪了的罐子，装着老莫德的银蜂蜜。拿出来分享，能叩开许多门；蜜蜂们似乎知道它是你的。\n\n@item outcast_grimoire\n# en: The Outcast's Grimoire\nname [a3c841] = 放逐者的魔典\n# en: A black-bound book of forbidden practice in a cramped, elegant hand. Its pages are cold, and some of them are blank until midnight.\ndesc [747aa5] = 一本黑色封皮的禁术之书，字迹紧凑而优雅。书页冰冷，有些页要到午夜才显出字来。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/places.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/places.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@place courtyard\n# en: The Courtyard\nname [1b8037] = 庭院\n# en: Flagstones worn smooth by three centuries of students, a fountain that freezes when it shouldn't, and the best gossip in the castle.\ndesc [d60535] = 被三百年学生踩得光滑的石板，一座总在不该结冰时结冰的喷泉，以及全城堡最好的八卦。\n\n@place library\n# en: The Library\nname [64e91f] = 图书馆\n# en: Seven floors of shelves that rearrange themselves when nobody is looking. Footnote the cat keeps order.\ndesc [9e9a17] = 七层书架，趁没人注意时自己重新排列。图书馆猫“脚注”负责维持秩序。\n\n@place refectory\n# en: The Refectory\nname [0b96c1] = 膳堂\n# en: Long tables, longer benches, and soup that tastes faintly of whatever you're homesick for.\ndesc [289d61] = 长桌，更长的长凳，还有一种汤——它尝起来隐约像你正在想念的那个地方。\n\n@place lakeshore\n# en: The Lakeshore\nname [6c0614] = 湖岸\n# en: The Mirror Lake lies so still it shows the towers upside down. On some nights it shows other things.\ndesc [733f61] = 镜湖静得能倒映出倒立的塔楼。有些夜晚，它倒映出的是别的东西。\n\n@place village\n# en: The Village Square\nname [a1e00b] = 村庄广场\n# en: A barge ride across the lake: crooked roofs, a market square, a bakery that has fed students for two hundred years and shopkeepers who have seen every kind of student.\ndesc [88b626] = 乘驳船渡过湖面就到：歪歪扭扭的屋顶、一个集市广场、一家给学生们供应了两百年面包的烘焙坊，还有见识过各种学生的店主们。\n\n@place lecture_tower\n# en: The Lecture Tower\nname [d37b7f] = 讲堂塔\n# en: Spiral lecture halls stacked one above another. Evocation on the lower floors, where scorch marks are expected; Umbral Theory at the top, where the lamps burn dim.\ndesc [3bff12] = 一层叠一层的螺旋讲堂。塑能术在底层——那里的焦痕是意料之中的事；暗影理论在顶层，灯光昏暗。\n\n@place observatory\n# en: The Observatory\nname [202edc] = 观星台\n# en: A brass dome at the top of the east tower. The great telescope points wherever it likes; usually that is the right place.\ndesc [4813c0] = 东塔顶上的黄铜穹顶。大望远镜想指向哪儿就指向哪儿——通常，那正是该看的地方。\n\n@place great_hall\n# en: The Great Hall\nname [e97d10] = 大礼堂\n# en: Floating candles, a vaulted ceiling that shows tomorrow's weather, and the stage where the choir rehearses and the Midwinter Ball is held.\ndesc [eb7d92] = 漂浮的蜡烛，能显示明天天气的拱顶，还有合唱团排练、仲冬舞会举办的舞台。\n\n@place infirmary\n# en: The Infirmary\nname [b3b383] = 医务室\n# en: Clean white beds, a cupboard of tonics and Sister Amaranth, who has seen every kind of injury and believes none of the excuses.\ndesc [80a36a] = 洁白的床铺，一柜子补药，还有阿玛兰斯修女——她见过各种伤，却一个借口都不信。\n\n@place alchemy_lab\n# en: The Alchemy Laboratory\nname [333e04] = 炼金实验室\n# en: Long benches, cauldrons in every size, and a ventilation charm that has given up on the third floor.\ndesc [9767f5] = 长长的工作台，大大小小的坩埚，还有一道早就放弃了三楼的通风咒。\n\n@place workshop\n# en: Mir's Workshop\nname [4b161e] = 米尔的工坊\n# en: A former classroom full of springs, gears, half-finished machines and the smell of solder. Fire buckets by the door, on the faculty's orders.\ndesc [fed5e7] = 一间旧教室，堆满弹簧、齿轮、半成品机器和焊锡味。门边按教职员的要求摆着几只灭火桶。\n# en: Something clanks and hums behind the west-wing tapestry.\nhint [507a91] = 西翼挂毯后面，有什么东西在叮当作响、嗡嗡低鸣。\n\n@place job_board\n# en: The Job Board\nname [377c1b] = 招工告示板\n# en: A cork board in the entrance hall layered with notices: jobs, lost familiars, club recruitment and the occasional ransom note for a sock.\ndesc [4e9473] = 门厅里的一块软木板，层层叠叠贴满告示：招工、走失的使魔、社团招新，偶尔还有一封某只袜子的赎金信。\n\n@place ledger_office\n# en: The Lumen Ledger Office\nname [fdd2aa] = 《流明纪事报》编辑部\n# en: A cramped room under the library stairs, walls papered with old front pages. The printing press groans like a dragon on deadline night.\ndesc [40d726] = 图书馆楼梯下一间逼仄的小屋，墙上贴满旧头版。截稿之夜，印刷机呻吟得像一条赶工的龙。\n\n@place headmistress_tower\n# en: The Headmistress's Tower\nname [735fdf] = 院长塔\n# en: Books, tea and a board of crystal towers by the window. You are only here because she wants you here.\ndesc [a5fc77] = 书、茶，还有窗边一盘水晶塔棋。你会在这里，只因为她想让你在这里。\n# en: A spiral stair guarded by a stone owl that only opens its eyes for some students.\nhint [49a3ae] = 一道由石头猫头鹰把守的螺旋楼梯——它只为某些学生睁开眼睛。\n\n@place restricted_section\n# en: The Restricted Section\nname [b1cf8a] = 禁书区\n# en: Black-bound books that breathe in the dark. Some of them read you back.\ndesc [9d32a6] = 在黑暗中呼吸的黑皮书。有些书会反过来读你。\n# en: A door at the back of the library that is always locked, and always slightly warm.\nhint [16cc43] = 图书馆最里面有一扇门，总是锁着，而且总是微微发烫。\n\n@place gargoyle_passage\n# en: The Third Gargoyle\nname [e05e0b] = 第三座石像鬼\n# en: Behind the third gargoyle on the north parapet, a passage that isn't on any map. Except theirs.\ndesc [e1c46f] = 北侧护墙上第三座石像鬼背后，有一条任何地图上都没有的通道。除了他们的地图。\n# en: Corvin keeps glancing at a particular gargoyle.\nhint [b7dd0a] = 科尔文总忍不住瞟向某一座石像鬼。\n\n@place seventh_tower\n# en: The Seventh Tower\nname [44b8dc] = 第七塔\n# en: Locked for a century. At the very top, one room of dust and moonlight, and a star-shaped hollow in the wall.\ndesc [f3b2a5] = 已经锁了一个世纪。塔顶只有一间屋子：灰尘、月光，以及墙上一个星形的凹槽。\n# en: A locked stair that leads up into a tower nobody admits is there.\nhint [5d54eb] = 一道上锁的楼梯，通往一座没人承认存在的塔。\n\n@place thesis_lab\n# en: The Thesis Laboratory\nname [92f1a5] = 论文实验室\n# en: Rows of carrels where final-year students and their advisors argue about footnotes until the candles give out.\ndesc [136592] = 一排排研读隔间，毕业年级的学生和导师在这里为脚注争论，直到蜡烛燃尽。\n\n@place duel_hall\n# en: The Duelling Hall\nname [481ae1] = 决斗厅\n# en: Scorched floorboards and a ceiling that has seen things. The practice dummies have names, and grudges.\ndesc [5bea7b] = 烧焦的地板，和一块见过太多场面的天花板。练习用的假人都有名字，也都记仇。\n# en: Steel rings somewhere above the east stair.\nhint [96a1b0] = 东侧楼梯上方，某处传来金铁交鸣。\n\n@place greenhouses\n# en: The Greenhouses\nname [27cc8e] = 温室\n# en: Glass houses that steam even in winter. The moonflowers bloom once every eleven years; the mandrakes have opinions every day.\ndesc [b1e233] = 冬天也冒着热气的玻璃房。月光花每十一年才开一次；曼德拉草每天都有意见。\n\n@place flight_field\n# en: The Flight Field\nname [ee78ba] = 飞行场\n# en: A long green field with hoops at either end and a wind that cheats. Broom lessons, races and spectacular landings.\ndesc [1c581a] = 一片狭长的草场，两端各立着圆环，风总爱作弊。飞帚课、竞速，以及各种壮观的着陆。\n\n@place stables\n# en: The Familiar Stables\nname [151887] = 使魔马厩\n# en: Hippogriffs, a sulky griffon chick, owls in the rafters and Professor Brask, who talks to all of them more kindly than to students.\ndesc [b3d42f] = 鹰头马身有翼兽、一只闹脾气的狮鹫幼崽、椽子上的猫头鹰，还有布拉斯克教授——她对它们说话比对学生温柔得多。\n\n@place boathouse\n# en: The Boathouse\nname [ba1cb2] = 船屋\n# en: An old wooden boathouse and a jetty out over the water. People come here to say the things they can't say indoors.\ndesc [cf59e4] = 一座旧木船屋，一道伸向水面的栈桥。人们来这里说那些在室内说不出口的话。\n\n@place orchard\n# en: The Orchard\nname [f7870f] = 果园\n# en: Apple and pear trees older than the towers. In spring the blossom falls like snow; once, it was snow.\ndesc [73fd80] = 比塔楼还老的苹果树和梨树。春天花落如雪；有一次，落下的真是雪。\n\n@place maud_cottage\n# en: Old Maud's Cottage\nname [37a3a6] = 老莫德的小屋\n# en: A thatched cottage with chickens on the doorstep, bees in the eaves, a goat called Bartholomew and a kettle that is always nearly boiling. Behind it, apple and pear trees older than the towers.\ndesc [fe0949] = 茅草屋顶的小屋，门阶上有鸡，屋檐下有蜂，一只叫巴塞洛缪的山羊，还有一壶永远快要烧开的水。屋后是比七塔还古老的苹果树和梨树。\n\n@place secret_garden\n# en: The Secret Garden\nname [2193f1] = 秘密花园\n# en: Glowing ferns, silver bees and a pear tree that hums. It only exists for people Maud has shown it to.\ndesc [7a21e5] = 发光的蕨类、银色的蜜蜂，还有一棵会哼歌的梨树。它只为莫德带进来过的人存在。\n# en: A door in the wall behind the potting shed that you keep walking past.\nhint [17ccd0] = 盆栽棚后面的墙上有一扇门，你总是从它旁边走过去。\n\n@place courier_loft\n# en: The Courier Loft\nname [ef540e] = 信使阁楼\n# en: A draughty loft full of parcels, pigeonholes and brooms in various states of repair. The dispatch bell rings at all hours.\ndesc [6cb721] = 一间漏风的阁楼，堆满包裹、信格和各种状态的扫帚。派送铃不分昼夜地响。\n\n@place gilded_newt\n# en: The Gilded Newt\nname [0d7995] = 镀金蝾螈酒馆\n# en: The village tavern: low beams, a fire that never goes out, and a landlady who remembers every student's tab to the penny.\ndesc [5e1794] = 小镇的酒馆：低矮的房梁，一炉从不熄灭的火，还有一位把每个学生的欠账都记到最后一个铜子儿的老板娘。\n\n@place mabels\n# en: Mabel's Tea Shop\nname [d2018a] = 梅布尔茶馆\n# en: Forty kinds of tea, three kinds of cake and Mabel, who reads your mood from the way you open the door.\ndesc [a4c364] = 四十种茶，三种蛋糕，还有梅布尔——她能从你推门的样子读出你的心情。\n\n@place grimsbys_books\n# en: Grimsby's Books\nname [5f12ee] = 格林斯比书店\n# en: A bookshop so crowded the shelves lean on each other for support. Mr Grimsby will find anything except what you came in for.\ndesc [555651] = 一家挤得书架都要互相倚靠的书店。格林斯比先生什么都找得到，除了你进门要找的那本。\n\n@place pell_apothecary\n# en: Pell & Daughters\nname [e047f1] = 佩尔父女药铺\n# en: An apothecary of a thousand labelled drawers. Old Pell taught three generations to count drops; his daughters taught him to smile at customers.\ndesc [4bb1ef] = 一家有上千个贴着标签的抽屉的药铺。老佩尔教会了三代人数药滴；他的女儿们教会了他对客人微笑。\n\n@place bakery\n# en: The Bakery\nname [ddad08] = 面包房\n# en: The same family has baked here for two hundred years. The buns are famous; so, in certain circles, is a letter that arrived very late.\ndesc [7aadb6] = 同一家人在这里烤了两百年面包。小圆面包远近闻名；在某些圈子里，一封迟到了很久的信也是。\n\n@place whisperwood\n# en: The Whisperwood\nname [0cc2ff] = 低语森林\n# en: An old forest that talks in its sleep. Moonpetal grows in its clearings; so do things that are better left there.\ndesc [c682e5] = 一片会说梦话的古老森林。月瓣花长在林间空地上；那里也长着一些最好留在原地的东西。\n# en: Fog sits on the western woods even at noon.\nhint [a946d3] = 西边的树林里，即使正午也笼着雾。\n\n@place royal_library_archives\n# en: The Royal Library Archives\nname [c0d567] = 皇家图书馆档案馆\n# en: Miles of stacks beneath the capital, where the oldest records of the realm are kept in temperature-controlled silence.\ndesc [1c545c] = 王都地下绵延数里的书库，王国最古老的记录在恒温的寂静中保存。\n\n@place conclave_laboratory\n# en: The Conclave Laboratory\nname [858e84] = 七塔议会实验室\n# en: The research laboratory of the Conclave of the Seven Towers, where new spells are tested behind very thick walls.\ndesc [d888bf] = 七塔议会的研究实验室，新咒语在非常非常厚的墙后接受测试。\n\n@place alchemists_works\n# en: The Guild Works\nname [f658d1] = 公会工坊\n# en: The Guild of Alchemists' great works by the river: vats, chimneys and a strict rule about naked flames.\ndesc [041a92] = 炼金师公会在河边的大工坊：大缸、烟囱，以及一条关于明火的严格规定。\n\n@place observatory_camp\n# en: The Expedition Camp\nname [bdc258] = 远征营地\n# en: Tents on a high plateau where the air is thin and the stars are close enough to argue with.\ndesc [aa6f2e] = 高原上的帐篷。空气稀薄，星星近得可以和它们争论。\n\n@place field_station\n# en: The Beastcraft Field Station\nname [93e08b] = 驭兽术野外站\n# en: A cabin on the moors, ringed with paddocks and nesting towers, where injured creatures are mended and wild ones are counted.\ndesc [0090c1] = 荒原上的一座小木屋，四周是围场和筑巢塔。受伤的生灵在这里被治好，野生的则在这里被清点。\n\n@place thornwick_lab\n# en: The Thornwick Exchange Lab\nname [efa297] = 荆木交换实验室\n# en: A guest laboratory at Thornwick Academy, the rival school, where the illusionists are excellent and the hospitality is suspicious.\ndesc [8e0893] = 对手学校荆木学院的一间客座实验室：幻术师们一流，待客之道可疑。\n\n@place unseen_office\n# en: The Office of Unseen Affairs\nname [dcf6f5] = 隐秘事务署\n# en: The Crown's Office of Unseen Affairs. It has no sign, no address, and a very good coffee machine.\ndesc [fb76d0] = 王室隐秘事务署。没有招牌，没有地址，只有一台非常好用的咖啡机。\n\n@place home_cottage\n# en: The Family Cottage\nname [be9171] = 老家小屋\n# en: The kitchen table, the creaky stair, the smell of whatever is in the oven. Everyone asks how school is.\ndesc [e5a1df] = 餐桌、吱呀的楼梯、烤箱里不知什么的香味。每个人都问你学校怎么样。\n\n@place home_fields\n# en: The Fields\nname [50cb01] = 田野\n# en: Long rows, a low wall to sit on, and the same view you used to stare at when you dreamed of towers.\ndesc [5dc6c3] = 长长的田垄，一堵可以坐的矮墙，还有你从前梦想高塔时一直盯着看的那片风景。\n\n@place home_market\n# en: The Market Town\nname [28baa8] = 集镇\n# en: Market stalls, the old crowd outside the inn, and the one shop that still sells your favourite sweets.\ndesc [0cdab5] = 集市摊位、旅店门口的老面孔，还有那家仍在卖你最爱的糖果的小店。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/spells.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/spells.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@spell flame\n# en: Flame\nname [1551a0] = 火焰\n# en: Fire from the fingertips: a candle's worth at first, a bonfire's later. Every duellist you'll ever face has it ready, and so should you.\ndesc [6b7da9] = 指尖生火：起初只有一根蜡烛的分量，后来是一堆篝火。你将来遇到的每个决斗者都会随时备着它，你也该如此。\n\n@spell foresight\n# en: Foresight\nname [1834e4] = 预见\n# en: A glimpse of the next few moments. Enough to know which way the dice will fall, sometimes.\ndesc [d1ae0e] = 瞥见接下来的片刻。有时，足以知道骰子会落在哪一面。\n\n@spell mend\n# en: Mend\nname [c5efbd] = 愈合\n# en: Knits cuts, cracks and broken teacups. Hearts take longer.\ndesc [fff564] = 缝合伤口、裂缝和摔碎的茶杯。心要慢一些。\n\n@spell glamour\n# en: Glamour\nname [ca8c7e] = 魅影\n# en: A little more charm than you really have. People remember liking you and not quite why.\ndesc [844244] = 比你实际拥有的多一点点魅力。人们记得喜欢你，却说不清为什么。\n\n@spell haste\n# en: Haste\nname [2f6010] = 疾速\n# en: Speed borrowed from hares and hawks: a blow slipped in between your spells, a parry in between everything else. It wears off quickly.\ndesc [be10a9] = 从野兔和鹰那里借来的速度：法术之间偷出一击，其他动作之间顺手一挡。效力去得很快。\n\n@spell shadowstep\n# en: Shadowstep\nname [d5b5ab] = 影步\n# en: Step into one shadow and out of another. Short distances only; the shadows in between are not friendly.\ndesc [c7cfde] = 踏入一片影子，从另一片影子里走出。只限短距离；中间的那些影子并不友善。\n\n@spell ward\n# en: Ward\nname [a11053] = 护界\n# en: A pale shell that turns aside the next harm. Every first-year is taught it in the first week, before anything else, and told to keep it close.\ndesc [b468c5] = 一层苍白的护壳，能挡开下一次伤害。每个新生入学第一周、先于一切学的就是它，并被叮嘱要随时备着。\n\n@spell animate\n# en: Animate\nname [8e9850] = 活化\n# en: A made thing gets up and moves by itself: a broom that sweeps, a chair that follows. It does one thing, and keeps doing it.\ndesc [96ff76] = 让一件造出来的东西自己动起来：扫地的扫帚，跟着你走的椅子。它只做一件事，并且一直做下去。\n\n@spell starlight\n# en: Starlight\nname [461e3b] = 星光\n# en: The light of a named star, held in the hand. Darkness that eats light finds it hard to swallow.\ndesc [c90510] = 一颗有名字的星星的光，握在手里。吞噬光的黑暗很难把它咽下去。\n\n@spell seal\n# en: Seal\nname [7ca2c0] = 封缄\n# en: Closes what is open and locks it away: a box, a door, a spell half-spent. What's sealed stays where you put it.\ndesc [67d898] = 把开着的东西合上、锁起来：一只盒子，一扇门，一道施了一半的咒。封住的东西，就待在你放它的地方。\n\n@spell wildfire\n# en: Wildfire\nname [5e3b09] = 野火\n# en: Fire you stop holding small. It grows while you hold it and runs when you let go, and it never quite forgets you're there.\ndesc [b334d5] = 不再被压小的火。你握着它时它会长，你松手时它会奔，而且它从来不会完全忘记你还在那儿。\n\n@spell heartwood\n# en: Heartwood\nname [5a2042] = 心木\n# en: A young tree out of bare ground, grown in a breath to stand over you. It shelters whoever stands under it.\ndesc [abc13a] = 一棵从光秃秃的地里一口气长起来的小树，立在你头顶。谁站在它下面，它就护着谁。\n\n@spell omen\n# en: Omen\nname [a7197e] = 预兆\n# en: Say what will happen, out loud, and it lands. Not soon; but it lands.\ndesc [547689] = 把将要发生的事大声说出来，它就会应验。不会很快；但会应验。\n\n@spell sleight\n# en: Sleight\nname [1251ad] = 妙手\n# en: Palm, swap, pocket. A coin walks out of a purse and into yours, and nobody saw a thing. It is still theft.\ndesc [25e378] = 藏、换、揣。一枚硬币从别人的钱袋里走进你的，谁也没看见。这仍然是偷。\n\n@spell contraption\n# en: Contraption\nname [a65e7e] = 奇械\n# en: A machine out of whatever is in reach, that works at once and mostly as meant. Stand back when it stops.\ndesc [2e81e0] = 用手边随便什么拼成一台机器，当场就能用，大致按你的意思运转。它停下来的时候，站远点。\n\n@spell hoarfrost\n# en: Hoarfrost\nname [e011a7] = 白霜\n# en: Cold called down hard: it freezes, cools and quenches. Set against a flame, the two of them cancel out.\ndesc [74903e] = 猛地唤下的寒意：能冻结、降温、扑灭。迎上一道火焰，两者会彼此抵消。\n\n@spell candour\n# en: Candour\nname [926713] = 坦言\n# en: Ask the right question and get the honest answer, sometimes more honest than anyone wanted.\ndesc [5cbfa6] = 问对问题，就能得到老实的回答，有时比任何人想要的都更老实。\n\n@spell brace\n# en: Brace\nname [3cb458] = 坚守\n# en: Plant your feet and hold. For a breath or two, blows that should fell you land soft.\ndesc [5aed1e] = 站稳脚跟，顶住。一两个呼吸之间，本该把你击倒的攻击都落得很轻。\n\n@spell quickquill\n# en: Quickquill\nname [8ab1bd] = 速记羽\n# en: A quill that takes it all down, word for word, as fast as anyone can talk. It has opinions about your handwriting.\ndesc [1ba45a] = 一支一字不落、能跟上任何人语速的羽毛笔。它对你的字迹颇有看法。\n\n@spell bramble\n# en: Bramble\nname [9f2958] = 荆棘\n# en: A hedge or a vine, now, where there wasn't one. The thorns are not for decoration.\ndesc [a5a3fd] = 一道树篱或一条藤蔓，立刻出现在原本没有的地方。那些刺可不是装饰。\n\n@spell index\n# en: Index\nname [c7ae4b] = 索引\n# en: Find the page. Any book you've held falls open where you need it, and a library starts to make sense.\ndesc [8fbe7d] = 找到那一页。你拿过的任何一本书都会翻到你需要的地方，图书馆也开始变得有道理起来。\n\n@spell rapport\n# en: Rapport\nname [330eef] = 投契\n# en: The right joke at the right moment. A room that was about to turn on you laughs instead.\ndesc [75ac2a] = 在恰好的时刻说出恰好的笑话。一屋子正要冲你发作的人，反倒笑了起来。\n\n@spell purify\n# en: Distill\nname [644c98] = 萃取\n# en: Draws out and concentrates: the strength from a root, the magic from yourself. It costs no Mana; it costs you.\ndesc [4e9fc8] = 抽取并浓缩：从根里取出力气，从你自己身上取出魔力。它不耗法力；它耗的是你。\n\n@spell tailwind\n# en: Tailwind\nname [560d95] = 顺风\n# en: Go fast, then faster. The wind gets behind you and keeps pushing as long as you don't stop.\ndesc [252b08] = 快，再快。风绕到你身后，只要你不停，它就一直推着你。\n\n@spell rime\n# en: Rime\nname [853e7a] = 雾凇\n# en: A sliver of winter sewn into your collar. The blow that should fell you finds you frozen instead, and you thaw.\ndesc [53de38] = 一片缝进衣领里的冬天。本该把你击倒的那一击，只会发现你冻住了，然后你慢慢解冻。\n\n@spell reflect\n# en: Reflect\nname [70544a] = 反照\n# en: What is thrown at you goes back where it came from, all of it.\ndesc [419257] = 朝你扔来的东西，原样回到它来的地方，一点不剩。\n\n@spell moonwell\n# en: Moonwell\nname [229b68] = 月泉\n# en: Sit still and let magic pool in you, like moonlight in a basin. It is slow, and it is free.\ndesc [ebf287] = 静坐，让魔力像月光落进水盆一样在你体内汇聚。很慢，但不要钱。\n\n@spell lodestar\n# en: Lodestar\nname [85b4ab] = 引星\n# en: Gather a guiding light around you, a little more each breath, until it is ready to stand between you and harm.\ndesc [491dd8] = 在身边聚起一团引路的光，每次呼吸多一点，直到它能挡在你和伤害之间。\n\n@spell toll\n# en: Toll\nname [9908fc] = 鸣钟\n# en: One low note, held, like a bell heard under water. Nobody teaches it.\ndesc [784cdd] = 一个低沉的音，被一直撑着，像水下听见的钟声。没有人教这个。\n\n@spell torment\n# en: Torment\nname [099f12] = 折磨\n# en: Every spell your opponent pays for hurts them. It is forbidden for good reason.\ndesc [0ddb58] = 对手每付出法力施一个法术，都会让自己吃痛。它被禁止是有道理的。\n\n@spell extraction\n# en: Extraction\nname [28c060] = 汲取\n# en: Reach into someone and take their magic for yours. Forbidden, and everyone who has felt it remembers.\ndesc [2d4e01] = 探进别人体内，把他的魔力拿来当你的。这是禁术，每个尝过它滋味的人都记得。\n\n@spell siphon\n# en: Siphon\nname [2981cc] = 虹吸\n# en: Draw the life out of someone, slowly, and keep it. Forbidden.\ndesc [8b717f] = 慢慢把别人的生命力抽出来，留给自己。禁术。\n\n@spell compel\n# en: Compel\nname [dd4703] = 强令\n# en: Make someone do what they just did again, and again, whether they want to or not. Forbidden.\ndesc [38771d] = 让人把刚做过的事再做一遍，一遍又一遍，不管他愿不愿意。禁术。\n\n@spell eclipse\n# en: Eclipse\nname [412a9e] = 蚀\n# en: Pain taken becomes power held. You still feel all of it. Forbidden.\ndesc [468617] = 承受的痛化作握在手里的力量。你依然会感受到每一分痛。禁术。\n\n@spell heartprice\n# en: Heartprice\nname [fa13cc] = 心价\n# en: Pay for magic with your own strength instead of Mana, for as long as you can stand it. Forbidden.\ndesc [da319f] = 用你自己的元气而不是法力来为魔法买单，能撑多久撑多久。禁术。\n"},{"path":"locales/zh-CN/world/tracks.lang","text":"# Lumenhold Academy — 简体中文 (zh-CN) translation of content/world/tracks.grim\n# Maintained by `node tools/grim.js i18n zh-CN`. Translate the text after \"=\"; keep {tokens}.\n# \"# en:\" shows the English source. [hash] marks the English version a line was translated from;\n# lines marked STALE need updating — after fixing the text, delete the [hash] and re-run the tool.\n\n\n@track evocation\n# en: Evocation\nname [ade249] = 塑能术\n# en: Evocation I, Evocation II, Advanced Evocation\nlevel_names [bc7426] = 塑能术 I、塑能术 II、高等塑能术\n# en: Fire and water, called up and held. Pyromancy and Tidecalling, taught together because they keep putting each other out.\ndesc [17db46] = 召唤并驾驭火与水。火焰术和唤潮术合在一起教，因为它们总是互相浇灭。\n# en: You sign up for Evocation. The reading list comes with a note about singed eyebrows.\npicked [99ef0b] = 你报了塑能术。书单后面附了一张关于眉毛烧焦的提醒。\n\n@track astromancy\n# en: Astromancy & Divination\nname [f9e4fb] = 星象与占卜\n# en: Astromancy I, Astromancy II, Advanced Astromancy\nlevel_names [9c3a9b] = 星象学 I、星象学 II、高等星象学\n# en: Reading the sky and the time that hasn't happened yet. Tea leaves in the first year; star charts that move in the third.\ndesc [74bd21] = 解读星空，以及尚未发生的时间。一年级看茶叶；三年级的星图会自己动。\n# en: You sign up for Astromancy. Someone hands you a teacup and tells you not to drink from it.\npicked [a1269f] = 你报了星象与占卜。有人递给你一只茶杯，叮嘱你别喝里面的东西。\n\n@track alchemy\n# en: Alchemy & Healing\nname [dd40d4] = 炼金与治愈\n# en: Alchemy I, Alchemy II, Advanced Alchemy\nlevel_names [5ac82d] = 炼金学 I、炼金学 II、高等炼金学\n# en: Potions, remedies and the patience to stir clockwise. Half of it happens in the laboratory and half in the infirmary.\ndesc [88b971] = 药剂、疗方，以及顺时针搅拌的耐心。一半在实验室里学，一半在医务室里学。\n# en: You sign up for Alchemy. Your first lesson is how to stir clockwise, and why.\npicked [f50cd5] = 你报了炼金与治愈。第一课是怎么顺时针搅拌，以及为什么。\n\n@track illusion\n# en: Illusion & Glamour\nname [5cdf61] = 幻术与魅影\n# en: Illusion I, Illusion II, Advanced Illusion\nlevel_names [b3f52d] = 幻术 I、幻术 II、高等幻术\n# en: Making people see what you choose. Professor Vey grades on whether you fooled her, and she is very hard to fool.\ndesc [fcaca3] = 让别人看见你选择的东西。维伊教授的评分标准是你有没有骗过她——而她很难被骗。\n# en: You sign up for Illusion. Professor Vey looks at you as if you might already be one.\npicked [0ae3d9] = 你报了幻术与魅影。维伊教授看你的眼神，好像你本人就可能是个幻象。\n\n@track beastcraft\n# en: Beastcraft & Flight\nname [d8160a] = 驭兽与飞行\n# en: Beastcraft I, Beastcraft II, Advanced Beastcraft\nlevel_names [ff1301] = 驭兽学 I、驭兽学 II、高等驭兽学\n# en: Creatures, familiars and brooms. Professor Brask believes the first two are easier to reason with than the third.\ndesc [4a5da1] = 生灵、使魔与扫帚。布拉斯克教授认为前两者比第三者讲道理。\n# en: You sign up for Beastcraft. Your name goes on the list beside a broom and a hippogriff.\npicked [39f700] = 你报了驭兽与飞行。你的名字写在名单上，旁边是一把扫帚和一只鹰头马身有翼兽。\n\n@track umbral\n# en: Umbral Theory\nname [97bda7] = 暗影理论\n# en: Umbral Theory I, Umbral Theory II, Advanced Umbral Theory\nlevel_names [3a8538] = 暗影理论 I、暗影理论 II、高等暗影理论\n# en: Shadow, wards and the magic of what is hidden. Taught at the top of the tower, where the lamps burn dim on purpose.\ndesc [418538] = 影、结界，以及隐藏之物的魔法。在塔顶讲授，那里的灯故意点得很暗。\n# en: You sign up for Umbral Theory. The lamps at the top of the tower are dim on purpose.\npicked [fe0dba] = 你报了暗影理论。塔顶的灯光是故意调暗的。\n\n@track artifice\n# en: Artifice\nname [de3328] = 奇械术\n# en: Artifice\nlevel_names [de3328] = 奇械术\n# en: Enchanted machinery: clockwork that thinks, cauldrons that stir themselves. Offered to students who already know how to make things and how to make them burn.\ndesc [7cb472] = 附魔机械：会思考的发条装置，会自己搅拌的坩埚。只开放给已经懂得如何造东西、也懂得东西如何着火的学生。\n# en: You take the place in Artifice. Someone shows you where the fire buckets are before anything else.\npicked [8435e9] = 你接下了奇械术的名额。在一切开始之前，有人先告诉你灭火桶放在哪儿。\n\n@track starcalling\n# en: Starcalling\nname [7a102b] = 唤星术\n# en: Starcalling\nlevel_names [7a102b] = 唤星术\n# en: Calling down the light of a named star. Not on any syllabus; taught by appointment, at night.\ndesc [73276b] = 召唤一颗有名字的星星的光。不在任何课表上；需预约，夜间授课。\n# en: You accept the appointment for Starcalling. The first one is tonight.\npicked [d972c5] = 你接受了唤星术的约见。第一次就在今晚。\n\n@track hollow_studies\n# en: Hollow Studies\nname [1138df] = 空洞研究\n# en: Hollow Studies\nlevel_names [1138df] = 空洞研究\n# en: What the founders sealed, and how. The headmistress teaches it herself, to very few students, in a room without windows.\ndesc [e6cd54] = 创始人封印了什么，又是如何封印的。院长亲自讲授，学生极少，在一间没有窗的屋子里。\n# en: You accept a seat in Hollow Studies. The headmistress writes your name in a book with a black cover.\npicked [10801c] = 你接受了空洞研究的席位。院长把你的名字写进一本黑色封皮的书里。\n\n@track duelling\n# en: Duelling Circle\nname [d0fcd5] = 决斗社\n# en: Member, Officer, Captain\nranks [b9c401] = 社员、干事、队长\n# en: Practice bouts, the Spring Tournament and a great deal of shouting about footwork.\ndesc [cd83f9] = 练习对决、春季锦标赛，还有关于步法的大量吼叫。\n# en: You join the Duelling Circle. Someone is already explaining footwork to you, loudly.\npicked [5318b4] = 你加入了决斗社。已经有人在大声给你讲步法了。\n\n@track lumen_ledger\n# en: The Lumen Ledger\nname [2606ae] = 《流明纪事报》社\n# en: Stringer, Reporter, Editor\nranks [82cebb] = 通讯员、记者、主编\n# en: The student newspaper. Scoops, deadlines, gossip columns and the occasional duty to the truth.\ndesc [ab972d] = 学生报纸。独家新闻、截稿日、八卦专栏，偶尔还有对真相的责任。\n# en: You join the Lumen Ledger. Nessa hands you a notebook and your first deadline.\npicked [2d283c] = 你加入了《流明纪事报》社。内莎递给你一个笔记本和你的第一个截稿日。\n\n@track verdant\n# en: Verdant Circle\nname [7ac3fd] = 青翠社\n# en: Member, Gardener, Head Gardener\nranks [cddc83] = 社员、园丁、首席园丁\n# en: The greenhouse club: moonflowers, mandrakes and long talks with plants that occasionally answer.\ndesc [263288] = 温室社团：月光花、曼德拉草，还有和偶尔会回话的植物之间的长谈。\n# en: You join the Verdant Circle. Tobias introduces you to the plants, one by one.\npicked [dec315] = 你加入了青翠社。托比亚斯把植物一株一株介绍给你。\n\n@track cartographers\n# en: Midnight Cartographers\nname [771e36] = 午夜制图社\n# en: The club that doesn't exist, mapping the passages the castle would prefer to keep. Never offered at the Club Fair; you are invited, or you aren't.\ndesc [f48173] = 那个不存在的社团，专门绘制城堡宁愿藏起来的通道。社团招新会上从不出现；你要么被邀请，要么没有。\n\n@track library_assistant\n# en: Library Assistant\nname [111c30] = 图书馆助理\n# en: Apprentice, Clerk, Senior Clerk\nranks [e4d902] = 学徒、馆员、资深馆员\n# en: Shelving books that don't want to be shelved, and keeping Footnote off the reference desk.\ndesc [dd4493] = 把不想被上架的书上架，并且别让“脚注”坐上参考咨询台。\n# en: You sign on at the library. Your first shift is next week, and Footnote is already watching you.\npicked [a968ac] = 你当上了图书馆助理。第一个班在下周，脚注已经在盯着你了。\n\n@track tavern\n# en: Server at the Gilded Newt\nname [e530c9] = 镀金蝾螈酒馆侍应\n# en: Server, Barkeep, Manager\nranks [a43a61] = 侍应、吧台手、经理\n# en: Evening shifts behind the bar. Tips depend on charm; survival depends on remembering who ordered the spiced cider.\ndesc [173a67] = 吧台后的晚班。小费取决于魅力；能否活下来，取决于你记不记得是谁点了香料苹果酒。\n# en: You sign on at the Gilded Newt. The landlady shows you the cellar and the back door.\npicked [7077f7] = 你在镀金蝾螈酒馆当上了侍应。老板娘带你看了酒窖和后门。\n\n@track apothecary_clerk\n# en: Apothecary Clerk\nname [7d9f6c] = 药铺店员\n# en: Clerk, Dispenser, Assistant Apothecary\nranks [53ed21] = 店员、配药员、助理药师\n# en: Counting drops and reading labels at Pell & Daughters. The wrong drawer is never just the wrong drawer.\ndesc [b1db1f] = 在佩尔父女药铺数药滴、认标签。拿错的抽屉，从来不只是拿错了抽屉。\n# en: You sign on at Pell & Daughters. Pell shows you which drawers never to open.\npicked [23e86f] = 你在佩尔父女药铺当上了店员。佩尔告诉你哪些抽屉永远别打开。\n\n@track broom_courier\n# en: Broom Courier\nname [8250e5] = 飞帚信使\n# en: Runner, Courier, Chief Courier\nranks [1d2734] = 跑腿、信使、首席信使\n# en: Parcels, letters and the occasional live delivery, flown across the lake in all weathers.\ndesc [2e4f08] = 包裹、信件，偶尔还有活物，风雨无阻地飞越湖面送达。\n# en: You sign on at the courier loft. They give you a satchel and a broom that pulls left.\npicked [48f0a6] = 你当上了飞帚信使。他们给了你一个挎包和一把往左偏的扫帚。\n\n@track intern_royal_library\n# en: Royal Library Archives\nname [df141e] = 皇家图书馆档案馆\n# en: Cataloguing and research in the oldest archives of the realm.\ndesc [fa724c] = 在王国最古老的档案馆里编目、做研究。\n\n@track intern_conclave\n# en: Conclave Laboratory\nname [132d7b] = 七塔议会实验室\n# en: Spell research with the Conclave of the Seven Towers. Prestigious; a faculty recommendation is expected.\ndesc [256192] = 在七塔议会从事咒语研究。名额珍贵；通常需要教职员的推荐信。\n\n@track intern_alchemists\n# en: Guild of Alchemists' Works\nname [6d98e1] = 炼金师公会工坊\n# en: Industrial alchemy at the Guild's great works.\ndesc [56dec3] = 在公会的大工坊里做工业炼金。\n\n@track intern_expedition\n# en: Observatory Expedition\nname [fe57ea] = 观星远征\n# en: A summer of sky charts on a high plateau.\ndesc [03b2f6] = 在高原上与星图共度一个夏天。\n\n@track intern_field_station\n# en: Beastcraft Field Station\nname [939e0e] = 驭兽术野外站\n# en: Tracking, tending and counting creatures on the moors.\ndesc [7564b3] = 在荒原上追踪、照料、清点各种生灵。\n\n@track intern_thornwick\n# en: Thornwick Exchange Lab\nname [28923e] = 荆木交换实验室\n# en: An exchange placement at the rival academy's illusion laboratory.\ndesc [51032c] = 前往对手学院的幻术实验室交换实习。\n\n@track intern_unseen_office\n# en: Office of Unseen Affairs\nname [c20130] = 隐秘事务署\n# en: By invitation only. Nobody applies to the Office of Unseen Affairs.\ndesc [55df65] = 只凭邀请。没有人会申请隐秘事务署。\n\n@track thesis\n# en: Thesis\nname [187afd] = 毕业论文\n# en: A year-long research project with a faculty advisor, ending in a defence before a committee of three.\ndesc [a30062] = 与一位教职员导师合作、为期一年的研究课题，最后在三人委员会面前答辩。\n"}]};
